ves in the system a tension among vengeance, preservation, and destruction.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[]},{"id":"CH_MHON_T_15:MRT7","text":"The authority of Anaxagoras and of ancient philosophers confirms the operation testimonialy, not through internal proof of its efficacy.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[]},{"id":"CH_MHON_T_15:MRT8","text":"The reconstruction preserves the true movement of the text, but the exact relation between the signs of the accompanying figures and all doctrinal elements is not wholly determined from the exposition alone.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[]}],"original_name":"Minimal Reconstruction Test"}],"revision":"CH_MHON_T_15@R001","source_variant_id":"canonical:CH_MHON_T_15:v1","source_language_code":"la"}],"identity":{"layer":"CH","kicker":"Chapter 19","title":"Theor. XVIII.","context":"Chapter 19","original_title":"Theor. Xviii.","original_context":"Chapter 19"},"original_title":"Theor. Xviii.","title_language_code":"en"},{"id":"CH_MHON_THER","layer":"CH","title":"T H E Ò R. X I X.","order":20,"parents":["RAW_MHON_THER"],"revision":"CH_MHON_THER@R001","sha256":"b5cd5a09fc0255c21ea4b42e4e4f548c32ce98e907cc5f81c914c393be3778da","sections":[{"number":"1","name":"Argumental Spine","items":[{"id":"CH_MHON_THER:AS1","text":"Quaestio est quomodo corpora Elementata inferiora vires suas corporales accipiant.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[]},{"id":"CH_MHON_THER:AS2","text":"Nova quaestionis constitutio ponit Solem et Lunam, non cetera Planeta, principales influxus corporales esse.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[]},{"id":"CH_MHON_THER:AS3","text":"Fundamentum est quod *Omnium rerum Corporatarum Analysis Pyronomica* hanc differentiam effectu demonstrat.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[]},{"id":"CH_MHON_THER:AS4","text":"Probatio distribuit influxum Lunae in Humorem Aqueum et influxum Solis in Liquorem Igneum.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[]},{"id":"CH_MHON_THER:AS5","text":"Motus argumentationis procedit ab inaequali vi caelesti ad constitutionem corporeitatis terrestris mortalium.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[]}],"original_name":"Argumental Spine"},{"number":"2","name":"Load-Bearing Axioms","items":[{"id":"CH_MHON_THER:A1","text":"Sol et Luna omnibus ceteris Planetis fortiores sunt in inferiora Elementata Corpora.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[]},{"id":"CH_MHON_THER:A2","text":"Corpora caelestia suas vires corporales in corpora inferiora infundunt.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[]},{"id":"CH_MHON_THER:A3","text":"*Analysis Pyronomica* effectibus rerum corporatarum influxus corporales demonstrat.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[]},{"id":"CH_MHON_THER:A4","text":"Luna Humorem Aqueum refundit.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[]},{"id":"CH_MHON_THER:A5","text":"Sol Liquorem Igneum refundit.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[]},{"id":"CH_MHON_THER:A6","text":"Humore Aqueo et Liquore Igneo sustentatur corporeitas terrestris rerum mortalium.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[]},{"id":"CH_MHON_THER:A7","text":"Effectus caelestes in corporibus inferioribus per formas materiales determinatas manifestantur.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[]},{"id":"CH_MHON_THER:A8","text":"Corporeitas rerum mortalium terrestrium a coniunctione influxuum solaris et lunaris dependet.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[]}],"original_name":"Load-Bearing Axioms"},{"number":"3","name":"Apophatic Structure","items":[{"id":"CH_MHON_THER:NX1","text":"Influxus corporalis Solis et Lunae non aequalis influxui omnium ceterorum Planetarum ponitur.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[]},{"id":"CH_MHON_THER:NX2","text":"Corpora inferiora non tamquam a seipsis sufficientia describuntur.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[]},{"id":"CH_MHON_THER:NX3","text":"Humores et liquores terrestres non sine origine caelesti explicantur.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[]},{"id":"CH_MHON_THER:NX4","text":"*Analysis Pyronomica* non mera descriptio corporum, sed demonstratio effectuum corporalis habetur.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[]},{"id":"CH_MHON_THER:NX5","text":"Sustentatio rerum mortalium non ad unam causam caelestem reducitur.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[]}],"original_name":"Apophatic Structure"},{"number":"4","name":"Derivative Propositions","items":[{"id":"CH_MHON_THER:D1","text":"Sol et Luna structuram materialem corporum inferiorum causaliter participant.","refs":["CH_MHON_THER:A1","CH_MHON_THER:A2"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"From","passages":[]},{"id":"CH_MHON_THER:D2","text":"Lunae influxus ad dimensionem aqueam corporeitatis refertur.","refs":["CH_MHON_THER:A2","CH_MHON_THER:A4"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"From","passages":[]},{"id":"CH_MHON_THER:D3","text":"Solis influxus ad dimensionem igneam corporeitatis refertur.","refs":["CH_MHON_THER:A2","CH_MHON_THER:A5"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"From","passages":[]},{"id":"CH_MHON_THER:D4","text":"Corporeitas terrestris ex differentiata distributione elementorum materialium constituitur.","refs":["CH_MHON_THER:A4","CH_MHON_THER:A5","CH_MHON_THER:A6"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"From","passages":[]},{"id":"CH_MHON_THER:D5","text":"Mortalia corpora sunt effectus sustentati, non simpliciter data, influxuum caelestium.","refs":["CH_MHON_THER:A2","CH_MHON_THER:A6"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"From","passages":[]}],"original_name":"Derivative Propositions"},{"number":"5","name":"Super-Derivatives","items":[{"id":"CH_MHON_THER:SD1","text":"Ordo corporeus terrestris in hierarchia caelesti fundatur, quia vis corporalis superiorum corporum conditionem inferiorum determinat.","refs":["CH_MHON_THER:A1","CH_MHON_THER:A2","CH_MHON_THER:A6"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"From","passages":[]},{"id":"CH_MHON_THER:SD2","text":"Distinctio Solis et Lunae efficit ut generatio corporeitatis non una sed bipolaris intelligatur.","refs":["CH_MHON_THER:A4","CH_MHON_THER:A5","CH_MHON_THER:A6"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"From","passages":[]},{"id":"CH_MHON_THER:SD3","text":"*Analysis Pyronomica* fit nexus inter observationem effectuum et hypothesim cosmologicam de causis materialibus.","refs":["CH_MHON_THER:A3","CH_MHON_THER:A7"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"From","passages":[]},{"id":"CH_MHON_THER:SD4","text":"Corpora mortalia ontologice dependent ab circulatione virium inter corpora caelestia et elementa inferiora.","refs":["CH_MHON_THER:A2","CH_MHON_THER:A6","CH_MHON_THER:A8"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"From","passages":[]}],"original_name":"Super-Derivatives"},{"number":"6","name":"Deep Apophatics","items":[{"id":"CH_MHON_THER:DNX1","text":"Falsum esse debet corpora terrestria clausam et autonomam materiam constituere.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[]},{"id":"CH_MHON_THER:DNX2","text":"Falsum esse debet influxum caelestem ad metaphoram vel significationem non-materialem reduci.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[]},{"id":"CH_MHON_THER:DNX3","text":"Falsum esse debet omnes Planetas eandem vim in corpora inferiora exercere.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[]},{"id":"CH_MHON_THER:DNX4","text":"Falsum esse debet corporeitatem mortalium explicari posse sine relatione inter Humorem Aqueum et Liquorem Igneum.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[]},{"id":"CH_MHON_THER:DNX5","text":"Falsum esse debet effectus corporales a principiis cosmologicis separari posse.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[]}],"original_name":"Deep Apophatics"},{"number":"7","name":"Super-Apophatics","items":[{"id":"CH_MHON_THER:SNX1","text":"Textus excludit mundum materialem sine mediatione caelesti sufficienter intelligibilem.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[]},{"id":"CH_MHON_THER:SNX2","text":"Textus praecludit aequalitatem causalem inter corpora caelestia.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[]},{"id":"CH_MHON_THER:SNX3","text":"Textus circumit sed non explicite resolvit quomodo influxus caelestes in substantiam terrestrem transmutentur.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[]},{"id":"CH_MHON_THER:SNX4","text":"Textus inadmissibilem reddit notionem vitae mortalis a sustentatione cosmica abstractae.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[]}],"original_name":"Super-Apophatics"},{"number":"8","name":"Formal Generative Schema","items":[{"id":"CH_MHON_THER:FGS1","text":"Inaequalitas virium caelestium → praecipua potestas Solis et Lunae → influxus corporalis in Elementata Corpora.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[]},{"id":"CH_MHON_THER:FGS2","text":"Influxus Lunae → Humoris Aquei refunditio; influxus Solis → Liquoris Ignei refunditio.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[]},{"id":"CH_MHON_THER:FGS3","text":"Humor Aqueus + Liquor Igneus → sustentatio Corpulentiae Terrestris → conservatio rerum mortalium.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[]},{"id":"CH_MHON_THER:FGS4","text":"Effectus corporales observati → Analysis Pyronomica → confirmatio ordinis causalitatis caelestis.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[]}],"original_name":"Formal Generative Schema"},{"number":"9","name":"Argument Flow Map","items":[{"id":"CH_MHON_THER:AF1","text":"Corporum mortalium sustentatio → investigatio causarum materialium → comparatio virium Planetarum → privilegium Solis et Lunae → distinctio influxuum aquei et ignei → constitutio corporeitatis terrestris.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[]}],"original_name":"Argument Flow Map"},{"number":"10","name":"Map-Effects","items":[{"id":"CH_MHON_THER:ME1","text":"Corpora inferiora ex obiectis passivis in nodos relationis inter influxus caelestes et materias elementares reclassificantur.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[]},{"id":"CH_MHON_THER:ME2","text":"Sol et Luna ex corporibus caelestibus inter alia enumeratis in fontes materiales distinctos elevantur.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[]},{"id":"CH_MHON_THER:ME3","text":"Oppositio inter causam caelestem et materiam terrestrem dissolvitur, quia corporeitas terrestris per influxum caelestem describitur.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[]},{"id":"CH_MHON_THER:ME4","text":"Humidum et igneum fiunt principia coordinata, quibus ager corporeitatis mortalium ordinatur.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[]}],"original_name":"Map-Effects"},{"number":"11","name":"Altitude Levels","items":[{"id":"CH_MHON_THER:ALT1","text":"Surface — Sol et Luna vires corporales in corpora inferiora infundunt; Luna Humorem Aqueum, Sol Liquorem Igneum refundit.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[]},{"id":"CH_MHON_THER:ALT2","text":"Structural — Corporeitas terrestris ex influxuum caelestium distributione materiali sustentatur.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[]},{"id":"CH_MHON_THER:ALT3","text":"Metastrategic — Effectus observabiles per *Analysim Pyronomicam* convertuntur in probationem ordinis causalitatis cosmici.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[]},{"id":"CH_MHON_THER:ALT4","text":"Ontological/Temporal — Res mortales dependent ab continuo nexu inter corpora caelestia, elementa inferiora et sustentationem corporeitatis.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[]}],"original_name":"Altitude Levels"},{"number":"12","name":"Minimal Reconstruction Test","items":[{"id":"CH_MHON_THER:MRT1","text":"Axiomata sufficiunt ad restituendam catenam a superioritate Solis et Lunae ad sustentationem corporeitatis terrestris.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[]},{"id":"CH_MHON_THER:MRT2","text":"Distinctio inter influxum Lunae aqueum et influxum Solis igneum est necessaria ad structuram argumenti.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[]},{"id":"CH_MHON_THER:MRT3","text":"Mechanismus quo Humor Aqueus et Liquor Igneus corporeitatem sustentant nondum satis determinatur.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[]},{"id":"CH_MHON_THER:MRT4","text":"Vis argumenti firmissima est in distributione bipartita influxuum et in conversione effectus in probationem.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[]},{"id":"CH_MHON_THER:MRT5","text":"Systema nititur praesuppositione quod *Analysis Pyronomica* revera influxus caelestes demonstrat, quamquam modus demonstrationis hic non explicatur.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[]},{"id":"CH_MHON_THER:MRT6","text":"Restitutio servat motum fontis: effectus corporales → demonstratio pyronomica → privilegium Solis et Lunae → formas materiales → corporeitas terrestris.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[]}],"original_name":"Minimal Reconstruction Test"}],"language_code":"la","language_name":"Latina","translations":[{"id":"CAREVAR_20261006T222307264674Z_dcd3b679","language_code":"en","language_name":"English","title":"T H E O R. X I X.","source_sha256":"b5cd5a09fc0255c21ea4b42e4e4f548c32ce98e907cc5f81c914c393be3778da","sha256":"6c6ae9b9e0b89777cd0e8c7e5b5dfa6f0a05259a24064bc926a46c31e880e574","rendition_type":"machine","review_status":"machine","model":"gpt-5.6-terra","created":"2026-10-06T22:23:07.267214+00:00","sections":[{"number":"1","name":"Argumental Spine","items":[{"id":"CH_MHON_THER:AS1","text":"The question is how lower Elementated bodies receive their corporeal powers.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[]},{"id":"CH_MHON_THER:AS2","text":"The new formulation of the question posits that the Sun and Moon, not the other Planets, are the principal corporeal influences.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[]},{"id":"CH_MHON_THER:AS3","text":"The foundation is that the *Pyronomic Analysis of All Corporeal Things* demonstrates this difference by effect.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[]},{"id":"CH_MHON_THER:AS4","text":"The proof distributes the Moon's influence into Aqueous Moisture and the Sun's influence into Fiery Liquid.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[]},{"id":"CH_MHON_THER:AS5","text":"The movement of the argument proceeds from unequal celestial power to the constitution of the terrestrial corporeity of mortal things.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[]}],"original_name":"Argumental Spine"},{"number":"2","name":"Load-Bearing Axioms","items":[{"id":"CH_MHON_THER:A1","text":"The Sun and Moon are stronger than all the other Planets upon lower Elementated Bodies.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[]},{"id":"CH_MHON_THER:A2","text":"Celestial bodies pour their corporeal powers into lower bodies.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[]},{"id":"CH_MHON_THER:A3","text":"*Pyronomic Analysis* demonstrates corporeal influences through the effects of corporeal things.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[]},{"id":"CH_MHON_THER:A4","text":"The Moon pours forth Aqueous Moisture.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[]},{"id":"CH_MHON_THER:A5","text":"The Sun pours forth Fiery Liquid.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[]},{"id":"CH_MHON_THER:A6","text":"The terrestrial corporeity of mortal things is sustained by Aqueous Moisture and Fiery Liquid.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[]},{"id":"CH_MHON_THER:A7","text":"Celestial effects are manifested in lower bodies through determinate material forms.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[]},{"id":"CH_MHON_THER:A8","text":"The corporeity of terrestrial mortal things depends upon the conjunction of solar and lunar influences.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[]}],"original_name":"Load-Bearing Axioms"},{"number":"3","name":"Apophatic Structure","items":[{"id":"CH_MHON_THER:NX1","text":"The corporeal influence of the Sun and Moon is not posited as equal to the influence of all the other Planets.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[]},{"id":"CH_MHON_THER:NX2","text":"Lower bodies are not described as self-sufficient in themselves.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[]},{"id":"CH_MHON_THER:NX3","text":"Terrestrial moistures and liquids are not explained without a celestial origin.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[]},{"id":"CH_MHON_THER:NX4","text":"*Pyronomic Analysis* is regarded not as a mere description of bodies, but as a demonstration of corporeal effects.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[]},{"id":"CH_MHON_THER:NX5","text":"The sustenance of mortal things is not reduced to one celestial cause.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[]}],"original_name":"Apophatic Structure"},{"number":"4","name":"Derivative Propositions","items":[{"id":"CH_MHON_THER:D1","text":"The Sun and Moon causally participate in the material structure of lower bodies.","refs":["CH_MHON_THER:A1","CH_MHON_THER:A2"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"From","passages":[]},{"id":"CH_MHON_THER:D2","text":"The Moon's influence is referred to the aqueous dimension of corporeity.","refs":["CH_MHON_THER:A2","CH_MHON_THER:A4"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"From","passages":[]},{"id":"CH_MHON_THER:D3","text":"The Sun's influence is referred to the fiery dimension of corporeity.","refs":["CH_MHON_THER:A2","CH_MHON_THER:A5"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"From","passages":[]},{"id":"CH_MHON_THER:D4","text":"Terrestrial corporeity is constituted from the differentiated distribution of material elements.","refs":["CH_MHON_THER:A4","CH_MHON_THER:A5","CH_MHON_THER:A6"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"From","passages":[]},{"id":"CH_MHON_THER:D5","text":"Mortal bodies are sustained effects, not simply given, of celestial influences.","refs":["CH_MHON_THER:A2","CH_MHON_THER:A6"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"From","passages":[]}],"original_name":"Derivative Propositions"},{"number":"5","name":"Super-Derivatives","items":[{"id":"CH_MHON_THER:SD1","text":"The terrestrial corporeal order is founded in a celestial hierarchy, because the corporeal power of higher bodies determines the condition of lower ones.","refs":["CH_MHON_THER:A1","CH_MHON_THER:A2","CH_MHON_THER:A6"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"From","passages":[]},{"id":"CH_MHON_THER:SD2","text":"The distinction of Sun and Moon causes the generation of corporeity to be understood as not one but bipolar.","refs":["CH_MHON_THER:A4","CH_MHON_THER:A5","CH_MHON_THER:A6"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"From","passages":[]},{"id":"CH_MHON_THER:SD3","text":"*Pyronomic Analysis* becomes the nexus between the observation of effects and the cosmological hypothesis concerning material causes.","refs":["CH_MHON_THER:A3","CH_MHON_THER:A7"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"From","passages":[]},{"id":"CH_MHON_THER:SD4","text":"Mortal bodies depend ontologically on the circulation of powers between celestial bodies and lower elements.","refs":["CH_MHON_THER:A2","CH_MHON_THER:A6","CH_MHON_THER:A8"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"From","passages":[]}],"original_name":"Super-Derivatives"},{"number":"6","name":"Deep Apophatics","items":[{"id":"CH_MHON_THER:DNX1","text":"It must be false that terrestrial bodies constitute closed and autonomous matter.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[]},{"id":"CH_MHON_THER:DNX2","text":"It must be false that celestial influence is reduced to metaphor or non-material signification.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[]},{"id":"CH_MHON_THER:DNX3","text":"It must be false that all Planets exert the same power upon lower bodies.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[]},{"id":"CH_MHON_THER:DNX4","text":"It must be false that the corporeity of mortals can be explained without a relation between Aqueous Moisture and Fiery Liquid.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[]},{"id":"CH_MHON_THER:DNX5","text":"It must be false that corporeal effects can be separated from cosmological principles.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[]}],"original_name":"Deep Apophatics"},{"number":"7","name":"Super-Apophatics","items":[{"id":"CH_MHON_THER:SNX1","text":"The text excludes a material world sufficiently intelligible without celestial mediation.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[]},{"id":"CH_MHON_THER:SNX2","text":"The text precludes causal equality among celestial bodies.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[]},{"id":"CH_MHON_THER:SNX3","text":"The text skirts but does not explicitly resolve how celestial influences are transformed into terrestrial substance.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[]},{"id":"CH_MHON_THER:SNX4","text":"The text renders inadmissible the notion of mortal life abstracted from cosmic sustenance.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[]}],"original_name":"Super-Apophatics"},{"number":"8","name":"Formal Generative Schema","items":[{"id":"CH_MHON_THER:FGS1","text":"Inequality of celestial powers → preeminent power of the Sun and Moon → corporeal influence into Elementated Bodies.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[]},{"id":"CH_MHON_THER:FGS2","text":"Influence of the Moon → pouring forth of Aqueous Moisture; influence of the Sun → pouring forth of Fiery Liquid.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[]},{"id":"CH_MHON_THER:FGS3","text":"Aqueous Moisture + Fiery Liquid → sustenance of Terrestrial Corporeity → preservation of mortal things.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[]},{"id":"CH_MHON_THER:FGS4","text":"Observed corporeal effects → Pyronomic Analysis → confirmation of the order of celestial causality.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[]}],"original_name":"Formal Generative Schema"},{"number":"9","name":"Argument Flow Map","items":[{"id":"CH_MHON_THER:AF1","text":"Sustenance of mortal bodies → investigation of material causes → comparison of the powers of the Planets → privilege of the Sun and Moon → distinction of aqueous and fiery influences → constitution of terrestrial corporeity.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[]}],"original_name":"Argument Flow Map"},{"number":"10","name":"Map-Effects","items":[{"id":"CH_MHON_THER:ME1","text":"Lower bodies are reclassified from passive objects into nodes of relation between celestial influences and elemental materials.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[]},{"id":"CH_MHON_THER:ME2","text":"The Sun and Moon are elevated from celestial bodies enumerated among others into distinct material sources.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[]},{"id":"CH_MHON_THER:ME3","text":"The opposition between celestial cause and terrestrial matter is dissolved, because terrestrial corporeity is described through celestial influence.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[]},{"id":"CH_MHON_THER:ME4","text":"The moist and the fiery become coordinated principles by which the field of mortal corporeity is ordered.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[]}],"original_name":"Map-Effects"},{"number":"11","name":"Altitude Levels","items":[{"id":"CH_MHON_THER:ALT1","text":"Surface — The Sun and Moon pour corporeal powers into lower bodies; the Moon pours forth Aqueous Moisture, the Sun Fiery Liquid.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[]},{"id":"CH_MHON_THER:ALT2","text":"Structural — Terrestrial corporeity is sustained by the material distribution of celestial influences.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[]},{"id":"CH_MHON_THER:ALT3","text":"Metastrategic — Observable effects are converted through *Pyronomic Analysis* into proof of the order of cosmic causality.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[]},{"id":"CH_MHON_THER:ALT4","text":"Ontological/Temporal — Mortal things depend upon a continuous nexus between celestial bodies, lower elements, and the sustenance of corporeity.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[]}],"original_name":"Altitude Levels"},{"number":"12","name":"Minimal Reconstruction Test","items":[{"id":"CH_MHON_THER:MRT1","text":"The axioms suffice to restore the chain from the superiority of the Sun and Moon to the sustenance of terrestrial corporeity.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[]},{"id":"CH_MHON_THER:MRT2","text":"The distinction between the Moon's aqueous influence and the Sun's fiery influence is necessary to the structure of the argument.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[]},{"id":"CH_MHON_THER:MRT3","text":"The mechanism by which Aqueous Moisture and Fiery Liquid sustain corporeity is not yet sufficiently determined.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[]},{"id":"CH_MHON_THER:MRT4","text":"The argument's greatest force lies in the bipartite distribution of influences and in the conversion of effect into proof.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[]},{"id":"CH_MHON_THER:MRT5","text":"The system relies upon the presupposition that *Pyronomic Analysis* truly demonstrates celestial influences, although the mode of demonstration is not explained here.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[]},{"id":"CH_MHON_THER:MRT6","text":"The reconstruction preserves the movement of the source: corporeal effects → pyronomic demonstration → privilege of the Sun and Moon → material forms → terrestrial corporeity.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[]}],"original_name":"Minimal Reconstruction Test"}],"revision":"CH_MHON_THER@R001","source_variant_id":"canonical:CH_MHON_THER:v1","source_language_code":"la"}],"identity":{"layer":"CH","kicker":"Chapter 20","title":"T H E Ò R. X I X.","context":"Chapter 20","original_title":"T H E Ò R. X I X.","original_context":"Chapter 20"},"original_title":"T H E Ò R. X I X.","title_language_code":"en"},{"id":"CH_MHON_T_16","layer":"CH","title":"T H E O R. X X.","order":21,"parents":["RAW_MHON_T_16"],"revision":"CH_MHON_T_16@R001","sha256":"a391f37bee73f7bb1cd733017e6162a8d1d480112864cdddd60e633a3d854407","sections":[{"number":"1","name":"Argumental Spine","items":[{"id":"CH_MHON_T_16:AS1","text":"Quaestio est utrum punctum centrale crucis sit necessarium ternario ex binario constituto, an sine detrimento removeri possit.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[]},{"id":"CH_MHON_T_16:AS2","text":"Argumentatio demonstrativa et mystagogica absentiam puncti redigit ad dissolutionem unitatis linearum atque ad mutationem binarii in quaternarium.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[]},{"id":"CH_MHON_T_16:AS3","text":"Punctum ternarii ut necessarium statuitur, dum eiusdem puncti in quaternario remotio ut purgatio superfluitatis exhibetur.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[]},{"id":"CH_MHON_T_16:AS4","text":"Eadem figura geometrica in distinctionem ontologicam convertitur: punctum ternarii est principium copulativum, punctum quaternarii est impedimentum confusionis.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[]},{"id":"CH_MHON_T_16:AS5","text":"Monas per binarium et ternarium ad quaternarium purificatum procedere dicitur, ut proportione aequalitatis ad unitatem et materiam propriam redeat.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[]}],"original_name":"Argumental Spine"},{"number":"2","name":"Load-Bearing Axioms","items":[{"id":"CH_MHON_T_16:A1","text":"Lineae rectae elementa significant.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[]},{"id":"CH_MHON_T_16:A2","text":"Binarium linearum unitatem praesupponit quae puncto communi servatur.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[]},{"id":"CH_MHON_T_16:A3","text":"Sublato puncto communi, unitas linearum discontinuatur et binarium in quaternarium emergit.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[]},{"id":"CH_MHON_T_16:A4","text":"Punctum quod binario adest ad ternarium constituendum necessario adesse debet et substitui non potest.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[]},{"id":"CH_MHON_T_16:A5","text":"Punctum centrale non est pars binarii, quia partes linearum lineae sunt, punctum autem linea non est.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[]},{"id":"CH_MHON_T_16:A6","text":"Punctum propriam hypostasim habet, quamvis in longitudinibus linearibus binarii contineatur.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[]},{"id":"CH_MHON_T_16:A7","text":"Quaternarium ex puncto remoto non substantiam linearum amittit, sed distinctionem et claritatem consequitur.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[]},{"id":"CH_MHON_T_16:A8","text":"In ternario punctum nullatenus superfluum est; in regno quattuor elementorum idem punctum superfluum, confusivum et corruptibile censetur.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[]},{"id":"CH_MHON_T_16:A9","text":"Monas nullas unitates aut numeros externos admittit, quia suis numeris sibi sufficienter absoluta est.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[]},{"id":"CH_MHON_T_16:A10","text":"Reditus Monae ad materiam propriam requirit retentionem proportionis genuinae et resectionem eorum quae ad eam non pertinent.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[]}],"original_name":"Load-Bearing Axioms"},{"number":"3","name":"Apophatic Structure","items":[{"id":"CH_MHON_T_16:NX1","text":"Punctum ternarii non potest abesse, quia absentia eius binarium destrueret.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[]},{"id":"CH_MHON_T_16:NX2","text":"Punctum necessarium non est pars linearis neque proprietas hypostatica binarii.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[]},{"id":"CH_MHON_T_16:NX3","text":"Quaternarium purificatum non retinet punctum centrale, quia illud ibi non est principium unitatis substantialis.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[]},{"id":"CH_MHON_T_16:NX4","text":"Claritas quaternarii non per additionem obtinetur, sed per remotionem puncti superflui et confusionis.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[]},{"id":"CH_MHON_T_16:NX5","text":"Via Monae non recipit numeros vel unitates extrinsecas.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[]},{"id":"CH_MHON_T_16:NX6","text":"Materia propria Monae non includit elementa quae eius proportionem hereditariam non servant.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[]}],"original_name":"Apophatic Structure"},{"number":"4","name":"Derivative Propositions","items":[{"id":"CH_MHON_T_16:D1","text":"Ternarium non est mera aggregatio binarii et puncti, sed unitas cuius punctum est conditio constitutiva.","refs":["CH_MHON_T_16:A2","CH_MHON_T_16:A3","CH_MHON_T_16:A4"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"From","passages":[]},{"id":"CH_MHON_T_16:D2","text":"Punctum idem secundum situm numeralem contrarias functiones suscipit: ternarium copulat, quaternarium separat.","refs":["CH_MHON_T_16:A4","CH_MHON_T_16:A7","CH_MHON_T_16:A8"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"From","passages":[]},{"id":"CH_MHON_T_16:D3","text":"Puncti remotio a quaternario non imminutio est, sed manifestatio linearum distinctarum sine detrimento ipsarum.","refs":["CH_MHON_T_16:A7","CH_MHON_T_16:A8"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"From","passages":[]},{"id":"CH_MHON_T_16:D4","text":"Purificatio definitur ut exclusio eius quod structuram non constituit sed proportionem obscurat.","refs":["CH_MHON_T_16:A7","CH_MHON_T_16:A8","CH_MHON_T_16:A10"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"From","passages":[]},{"id":"CH_MHON_T_16:D5","text":"Aequalitas proportionis permitit Monae, per formas suas internas transitum facienti, ad unitatem propriam restitui.","refs":["CH_MHON_T_16:A9","CH_MHON_T_16:A10"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"From","passages":[]}],"original_name":"Derivative Propositions"},{"number":"5","name":"Super-Derivatives","items":[{"id":"CH_MHON_T_16:SD1","text":"Ordo numeralis non est scalaris: idem elementum fit necessarium aut noxium prout unitatem constituit aut eam iam constitutam confundit.","refs":["CH_MHON_T_16:A4","CH_MHON_T_16:A7","CH_MHON_T_16:A8"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"From","passages":[]},{"id":"CH_MHON_T_16:SD2","text":"Integritas non per conservationem omnium partium definitur, sed per conservationem solius proportionis propriae; purgatio igitur forma restitutionis est.","refs":["CH_MHON_T_16:A7","CH_MHON_T_16:A10"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"From","passages":[]},{"id":"CH_MHON_T_16:SD3","text":"Monas systema clausum est quod internis numeris expanditur, artificio transformatur, et tamen ad primam materiam suam redit.","refs":["CH_MHON_T_16:A9","CH_MHON_T_16:A10"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"From","passages":[]},{"id":"CH_MHON_T_16:SD4","text":"Valorizatio luminis et tenebrarum super operationem geometricam imponitur, ita ut puritas formalis in ordinem spiritualem transferatur.","refs":["CH_MHON_T_16:A8"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"From","passages":[]}],"original_name":"Super-Derivatives"},{"number":"6","name":"Deep Apophatics","items":[{"id":"CH_MHON_T_16:DNX1","text":"Falsum esse debet punctum semper eandem essentiam functionemque servare, quia punctum ternarii et punctum quaternarii oppositis valoribus ordinantur.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[]},{"id":"CH_MHON_T_16:DNX2","text":"Falsa est identitas inter contineri in lineis et esse pars linearum; punctum continetur sine compositione lineari.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[]},{"id":"CH_MHON_T_16:DNX3","text":"Falsum esse debet quod imminutio numerica necessario sit imminutio substantialis, quia sublatio puncti quaternarium clarius distinguit.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[]},{"id":"CH_MHON_T_16:DNX4","text":"Externa additio non potest Monae perfectionem conferre, quia eius sufficientia et restitutio ex numeris propriis pendent.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[]}],"original_name":"Deep Apophatics"},{"number":"7","name":"Super-Apophatics","items":[{"id":"CH_MHON_T_16:SNX1","text":"Argumentatio excludit omnem unitatem quae sine medio copulativo immediate inter lineas ponitur.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[]},{"id":"CH_MHON_T_16:SNX2","text":"Argumentatio excludit plenitudinem indiscriminatam, in qua retinere omne datum praeferatur distinctioni proportionatae.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[]},{"id":"CH_MHON_T_16:SNX3","text":"Systema non admittit principium extrinsecum quod Monae ordinem fundet, quia Monas ad se ipsam et ad materiam suam propriam reducitur.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[]}],"original_name":"Super-Apophatics"},{"number":"8","name":"Formal Generative Schema","items":[{"id":"CH_MHON_T_16:FGS1","text":"Binarium linearum + punctum commune → ternarium unitum; remotio puncti → discontinuatio unitatis → quaternarium.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[]},{"id":"CH_MHON_T_16:FGS2","text":"Examen situs puncti → distinctio puncti necessarii et puncti superflui → conservatio primi, reiectio secundi.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[]},{"id":"CH_MHON_T_16:FGS3","text":"Quaternarium purificatum + proportio aequalitatis → restitutio Monae ad unitatem et materiam propriam.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[]},{"id":"CH_MHON_T_16:FGS4","text":"Exclusio externorum numerorum → autarkia numeralis → expansio interna et reditus proportionalis.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[]}],"original_name":"Formal Generative Schema"},{"number":"9","name":"Argument Flow Map","items":[{"id":"CH_MHON_T_16:AF1","text":"Dubium de absentia puncti → suppositio subtractionis → binarii conversio in quaternarium → impossibilitas binarii simul remanentis et dissoluti.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[]},{"id":"CH_MHON_T_16:AF2","text":"Necessitas puncti ternarii → negatio partis linearis → affirmatio hypostasis propriae intra binarium contentae.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[]},{"id":"CH_MHON_T_16:AF3","text":"Transitus ad crucem quaternariam → remotio mathematice possibilis → quaternarii distinctio et purificatio.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[]},{"id":"CH_MHON_T_16:AF4","text":"Figura A punctum necessarium exhibens / figura B vacuitatem puncti remoti exhibens → Monas per ternarium ad quaternarium purificatum restituta.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[]}],"original_name":"Argument Flow Map"},{"number":"10","name":"Map-Effects","items":[{"id":"CH_MHON_T_16:ME1","text":"Punctum reclassificatur non ut res simpliciter data, sed ut operator cuius necessitas a configuratione numerali pendet.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[]},{"id":"CH_MHON_T_16:ME2","text":"Oppositio inter additionem et subtractionem dissolvitur: additio puncti potest unitatem constituere, subtractio puncti potest claritatem producere.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[]},{"id":"CH_MHON_T_16:ME3","text":"Quaternarium non describitur ut mera multiplicatio binarii, sed ut effectus discontinuatae unitatis et locus purificationis.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[]},{"id":"CH_MHON_T_16:ME4","text":"Proportio transfertur a relatione quantitativa ad criterium inclusionis, purificationis, identitatis et reditus.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[]}],"original_name":"Map-Effects"},{"number":"11","name":"Altitude Levels","items":[{"id":"CH_MHON_T_16:ALT1","text":"Surface — Cruces duarum linearum et punctum earum commune vel remotum examinantur.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[]},{"id":"CH_MHON_T_16:ALT2","text":"Structural — Unitas, discontinuatio, pars, hypostasis, proportio et purificatio relationem inter binarium, ternarium et quaternarium ordinant.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[]},{"id":"CH_MHON_T_16:ALT3","text":"Metastrategic — Demonstratio mathematica adhibetur ut discriminet quid intra figuram retinendum sit et quid tanquam superfluum reiciendum.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[]},{"id":"CH_MHON_T_16:ALT4","text":"Ontological/Temporal — Monas ex numero proprio evolvitur, in amplitudinem diffunditur, et per resectionem ad materiam primam propriam restituitur.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[]}],"original_name":"Altitude Levels"},{"number":"12","name":"Minimal Reconstruction Test","items":[{"id":"CH_MHON_T_16:MRT1","text":"Axiomata sufficiunt ad restituendam necessitatem puncti in ternario, conversionem eius subtractione in quaternarium, et purificationem quaternarii.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[]},{"id":"CH_MHON_T_16:MRT2","text":"Commissura principalis manet in transitu a demonstratione geometrica ad iudicium spirituale, quia corruptibilitas et tenebrositas puncti quaternarii geometrice non inferuntur.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[]},{"id":"CH_MHON_T_16:MRT3","text":"Systema cohaeret dum differentia inter punctum constitutivum et punctum confusivum situ numerali regitur; criterium huius mutationis ultra ipsam configurationem non plene determinatur.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[]},{"id":"CH_MHON_T_16:MRT4","text":"Tensionem efficit quod punctum propriam hypostasim habet et tamen in longitudinibus linearibus contineri dicitur, sine expositione modi huius continentiae.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[]},{"id":"CH_MHON_T_16:MRT5","text":"Tensionem secundariam efficit quod Monas sibi sufficiens dicitur, sed simul artificis industria ad expansionem et restitutionem indigere describitur.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[]}],"original_name":"Minimal Reconstruction Test"}],"language_code":"la","language_name":"Latina","translations":[{"id":"CAREVAR_20261006T222901545875Z_942f09e7","language_code":"en","language_name":"English","title":"T H E O R. X X.","source_sha256":"a391f37bee73f7bb1cd733017e6162a8d1d480112864cdddd60e633a3d854407","sha256":"84bb3331851293af1c8c78ad9326fb8b7aec9185662d5284dbf6a6c73b42c0a0","rendition_type":"machine","review_status":"machine","model":"gpt-5.6-terra","created":"2026-10-06T22:29:01.548427+00:00","sections":[{"number":"1","name":"Argumental Spine","items":[{"id":"CH_MHON_T_16:AS1","text":"The question is whether the central point of the cross is necessary to the ternary constituted from the binary, or whether it can be removed without loss.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[]},{"id":"CH_MHON_T_16:AS2","text":"Demonstrative and mystagogical argumentation reduces the absence of the point to the dissolution of the unity of the lines and to the transformation of the binary into the quaternary.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[]},{"id":"CH_MHON_T_16:AS3","text":"The point of the ternary is established as necessary, while the removal of that same point in the quaternary is presented as a purgation of superfluity.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[]},{"id":"CH_MHON_T_16:AS4","text":"The same geometric figure is converted into an ontological distinction: the point of the ternary is a copulative principle, the point of the quaternary is an impediment of confusion.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[]},{"id":"CH_MHON_T_16:AS5","text":"The Monad is said to proceed through the binary and the ternary to the purified quaternary, so that by the proportion of equality it may return to unity and to its own matter.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[]}],"original_name":"Argumental Spine"},{"number":"2","name":"Load-Bearing Axioms","items":[{"id":"CH_MHON_T_16:A1","text":"Straight lines signify elements.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[]},{"id":"CH_MHON_T_16:A2","text":"The binary of lines presupposes a unity that is preserved by a common point.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[]},{"id":"CH_MHON_T_16:A3","text":"When the common point is removed, the unity of the lines is interrupted and a quaternary emerges from the binary.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[]},{"id":"CH_MHON_T_16:A4","text":"The point that is present to the binary in order to constitute the ternary must necessarily be present and cannot be substituted.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[]},{"id":"CH_MHON_T_16:A5","text":"The central point is not a part of the binary, because the parts of lines are lines, whereas a point is not a line.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[]},{"id":"CH_MHON_T_16:A6","text":"The point has its own hypostasis, although it is contained within the linear extensions of the binary.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[]},{"id":"CH_MHON_T_16:A7","text":"The quaternary resulting from the removed point does not lose the substance of the lines, but attains distinction and clarity.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[]},{"id":"CH_MHON_T_16:A8","text":"In the ternary the point is by no means superfluous; in the realm of the four elements, the same point is considered superfluous, confusive, and corruptible.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[]},{"id":"CH_MHON_T_16:A9","text":"The Monad admits no external units or numbers, because it is sufficiently absolute in itself through its own numbers.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[]},{"id":"CH_MHON_T_16:A10","text":"The return of the Monad to its own matter requires the retention of genuine proportion and the cutting away of those things that do not pertain to it.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[]}],"original_name":"Load-Bearing Axioms"},{"number":"3","name":"Apophatic Structure","items":[{"id":"CH_MHON_T_16:NX1","text":"The point of the ternary cannot be absent, because its absence would destroy the binary.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[]},{"id":"CH_MHON_T_16:NX2","text":"The necessary point is neither a linear part nor a hypostatic property of the binary.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[]},{"id":"CH_MHON_T_16:NX3","text":"The purified quaternary does not retain the central point, because there it is not a principle of substantial unity.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[]},{"id":"CH_MHON_T_16:NX4","text":"The clarity of the quaternary is not attained through addition, but through the removal of the superfluous point and of confusion.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[]},{"id":"CH_MHON_T_16:NX5","text":"The path of the Monad admits no extrinsic numbers or units.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[]},{"id":"CH_MHON_T_16:NX6","text":"The proper matter of the Monad does not include elements that do not preserve its inherited proportion.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[]}],"original_name":"Apophatic Structure"},{"number":"4","name":"Derivative Propositions","items":[{"id":"CH_MHON_T_16:D1","text":"The ternary is not a mere aggregation of the binary and the point, but a unity of which the point is a constitutive condition.","refs":["CH_MHON_T_16:A2","CH_MHON_T_16:A3","CH_MHON_T_16:A4"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"From","passages":[]},{"id":"CH_MHON_T_16:D2","text":"The same point, according to its numerical position, assumes contrary functions: it couples the ternary and separates the quaternary.","refs":["CH_MHON_T_16:A4","CH_MHON_T_16:A7","CH_MHON_T_16:A8"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"From","passages":[]},{"id":"CH_MHON_T_16:D3","text":"The removal of the point from the quaternary is not a diminution, but a manifestation of distinct lines without loss of the lines themselves.","refs":["CH_MHON_T_16:A7","CH_MHON_T_16:A8"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"From","passages":[]},{"id":"CH_MHON_T_16:D4","text":"Purgation is defined as the exclusion of that which does not constitute the structure but obscures proportion.","refs":["CH_MHON_T_16:A7","CH_MHON_T_16:A8","CH_MHON_T_16:A10"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"From","passages":[]},{"id":"CH_MHON_T_16:D5","text":"Equality of proportion permits the Monad, passing through its internal forms, to be restored to its own unity.","refs":["CH_MHON_T_16:A9","CH_MHON_T_16:A10"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"From","passages":[]}],"original_name":"Derivative Propositions"},{"number":"5","name":"Super-Derivatives","items":[{"id":"CH_MHON_T_16:SD1","text":"Numerical order is not scalar: the same element becomes necessary or harmful according as it constitutes unity or confuses unity already constituted.","refs":["CH_MHON_T_16:A4","CH_MHON_T_16:A7","CH_MHON_T_16:A8"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"From","passages":[]},{"id":"CH_MHON_T_16:SD2","text":"Integrity is defined not through the preservation of all parts, but through the preservation of proportion alone; purgation is therefore a form of restoration.","refs":["CH_MHON_T_16:A7","CH_MHON_T_16:A10"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"From","passages":[]},{"id":"CH_MHON_T_16:SD3","text":"The Monad is a closed system that expands through internal numbers, is transformed by artifice, and nevertheless returns to its first matter.","refs":["CH_MHON_T_16:A9","CH_MHON_T_16:A10"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"From","passages":[]},{"id":"CH_MHON_T_16:SD4","text":"A valuation of light and darkness is imposed upon the geometric operation, so that formal purity is transferred into a spiritual order.","refs":["CH_MHON_T_16:A8"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"From","passages":[]}],"original_name":"Super-Derivatives"},{"number":"6","name":"Deep Apophatics","items":[{"id":"CH_MHON_T_16:DNX1","text":"It must be false that the point always preserves the same essence and function, because the point of the ternary and the point of the quaternary are ordered by opposed values.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[]},{"id":"CH_MHON_T_16:DNX2","text":"The identity between being contained in lines and being a part of lines is false; the point is contained without linear composition.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[]},{"id":"CH_MHON_T_16:DNX3","text":"It must be false that numerical diminution is necessarily substantial diminution, because the removal of the point distinguishes the quaternary more clearly.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[]},{"id":"CH_MHON_T_16:DNX4","text":"External addition cannot confer perfection upon the Monad, because its sufficiency and restoration depend upon its own numbers.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[]}],"original_name":"Deep Apophatics"},{"number":"7","name":"Super-Apophatics","items":[{"id":"CH_MHON_T_16:SNX1","text":"The argument excludes every unity that is immediately posited among lines without a copulative medium.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[]},{"id":"CH_MHON_T_16:SNX2","text":"The argument excludes indiscriminate fullness, in which retaining everything given is preferred to proportionate distinction.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[]},{"id":"CH_MHON_T_16:SNX3","text":"The system admits no extrinsic principle that grounds the order of the Monad, because the Monad is reduced to itself and to its own proper matter.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[]}],"original_name":"Super-Apophatics"},{"number":"8","name":"Formal Generative Schema","items":[{"id":"CH_MHON_T_16:FGS1","text":"Binary of lines + common point → united ternary; removal of the point → interruption of unity → quaternary.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[]},{"id":"CH_MHON_T_16:FGS2","text":"Examination of the point's position → distinction between necessary point and superfluous point → preservation of the former, rejection of the latter.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[]},{"id":"CH_MHON_T_16:FGS3","text":"Purified quaternary + proportion of equality → restoration of the Monad to unity and its own matter.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[]},{"id":"CH_MHON_T_16:FGS4","text":"Exclusion of external numbers → numerical autarky → internal expansion and proportional return.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[]}],"original_name":"Formal Generative Schema"},{"number":"9","name":"Argument Flow Map","items":[{"id":"CH_MHON_T_16:AF1","text":"Doubt concerning the absence of the point → supposition of subtraction → conversion of the binary into the quaternary → impossibility of the binary remaining and being dissolved at once.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[]},{"id":"CH_MHON_T_16:AF2","text":"Necessity of the point of the ternary → negation of linear part → affirmation of a proper hypostasis contained within the binary.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[]},{"id":"CH_MHON_T_16:AF3","text":"Transition to the quaternary cross → mathematically possible removal → distinction and purification of the quaternary.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[]},{"id":"CH_MHON_T_16:AF4","text":"Figure A displaying the necessary point / figure B displaying the emptiness of the removed point → the Monad restored through the ternary to the purified quaternary.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[]}],"original_name":"Argument Flow Map"},{"number":"10","name":"Map-Effects","items":[{"id":"CH_MHON_T_16:ME1","text":"The point is reclassified not as a thing simply given, but as an operator whose necessity depends on numerical configuration.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[]},{"id":"CH_MHON_T_16:ME2","text":"The opposition between addition and subtraction is dissolved: addition of the point can constitute unity, removal of the point can produce clarity.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[]},{"id":"CH_MHON_T_16:ME3","text":"The quaternary is described not as a mere multiplication of the binary, but as the effect of interrupted unity and as a site of purification.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[]},{"id":"CH_MHON_T_16:ME4","text":"Proportion is transferred from a quantitative relation to a criterion of inclusion, purification, identity, and return.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[]}],"original_name":"Map-Effects"},{"number":"11","name":"Altitude Levels","items":[{"id":"CH_MHON_T_16:ALT1","text":"Surface — Crosses of two lines and their common or removed point are examined.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[]},{"id":"CH_MHON_T_16:ALT2","text":"Structural — Unity, interruption, part, hypostasis, proportion, and purification order the relation among the binary, ternary, and quaternary.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[]},{"id":"CH_MHON_T_16:ALT3","text":"Metastrategic — Mathematical demonstration is employed to discriminate what is to be retained within the figure and what is to be rejected as superfluous.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[]},{"id":"CH_MHON_T_16:ALT4","text":"Ontological/Temporal — The Monad evolves from its own number, spreads into extension, and through cutting away is restored to its own first matter.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[]}],"original_name":"Altitude Levels"},{"number":"12","name":"Minimal Reconstruction Test","items":[{"id":"CH_MHON_T_16:MRT1","text":"The axioms suffice to reconstruct the necessity of the point in the ternary, its conversion by subtraction into the quaternary, and the purification of the quaternary.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[]},{"id":"CH_MHON_T_16:MRT2","text":"The principal juncture remains in the transition from geometric demonstration to spiritual judgment, because the corruptibility and darkness of the point of the quaternary are not geometrically inferred.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[]},{"id":"CH_MHON_T_16:MRT3","text":"The system coheres so long as the difference between the constitutive point and the confusive point is governed by numerical position; the criterion of this change beyond the configuration itself is not fully determined.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[]},{"id":"CH_MHON_T_16:MRT4","text":"A tension is produced by the fact that the point has its own hypostasis and yet is said to be contained within the linear extensions, without an exposition of the mode of this containment.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[]},{"id":"CH_MHON_T_16:MRT5","text":"A secondary tension is produced by the fact that the Monad is said to be self-sufficient, yet at the same time is described as requiring the craftsman's industry for expansion and restoration.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[]}],"original_name":"Minimal Reconstruction Test"}],"revision":"CH_MHON_T_16@R001","source_variant_id":"canonical:CH_MHON_T_16:v1","source_language_code":"la","original_title":"T H E O R. X X."}],"identity":{"layer":"CH","kicker":"Chapter 21","title":"T H E O R. X X.","context":"Chapter 21","original_title":"T H E O R. X X.","original_context":"Chapter 21"},"original_title":"T H E O R. X X.","title_language_code":"en"},{"id":"CH_MHON_T_17","layer":"CH","title":"Theor. XXI.","order":22,"parents":["RAW_MHON_T_17"],"revision":"CH_MHON_T_17@R001","sha256":"f231dd8f05f857adea90b20ecebf63c76f61d80e01b5570c9397f82a82403ba7","sections":[{"number":"1","name":"Argumental Spine","items":[{"id":"CH_MHON_T_17:AS1","text":"Quaeritur quid nova Monadis mysticae commutatio localis revelet, cum corpus eius in membra B, D, C dissecare liceat.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[]},{"id":"CH_MHON_T_17:AS2","text":"Argumentatio demonstrativa et hermetico-hieroglyphica est: situs, figura, magnitudo et connexio partium pro notis essentialibus accipiuntur.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[]},{"id":"CH_MHON_T_17:AS3","text":"D et C, ad terram versa et inter se notis figuris manifesta, essentias inferiores, stabiles ac fixas indicant; B autem tertium membrum obscurius et minus determinatum manet.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[]},{"id":"CH_MHON_T_17:AS4","text":"Natura B per lunam duplicem et crucem elementorum describitur ut gradus existendi et vivendi, elementari artificio tractandus, non ut solaris plenitudo iam naturaliter perfecta.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[]},{"id":"CH_MHON_T_17:AS5","text":"Ex semicirculis B separatis solaris circulus effici potest, sed effectus est artificialis; ipsa figura B naturaliter laxa, fluxilis et incompleta manet.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[]},{"id":"CH_MHON_T_17:AS6","text":"Dissectio unius corporis in ternarium non destruit unitatem: membra, ex eadem Monade orta, sympathiam et unionem monadicam mutuam recipere dicuntur.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[]}],"original_name":"Argumental Spine"},{"number":"2","name":"Load-Bearing Axioms","items":[{"id":"CH_MHON_T_17:A1","text":"Partium Monadis situs localis significationem hieroglyphicam et philosophicam mutat.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[]},{"id":"CH_MHON_T_17:A2","text":"Figurae D et C a figura B essentialiter distinguuntur.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[]},{"id":"CH_MHON_T_17:A3","text":"Directio inferior ad terram, in hac arte, stabilitatem et fixationem significat.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[]},{"id":"CH_MHON_T_17:A4","text":"Semicirculus lunae et circulus integer solis hieroglyphica sunt signa.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[]},{"id":"CH_MHON_T_17:A5","text":"Duplex figura lunaris B duos primos gradus naturarum creatarum, esse et vivere, designare potest.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[]},{"id":"CH_MHON_T_17:A6","text":"Crux B adiuncta elementorum artificium requirit.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[]},{"id":"CH_MHON_T_17:A7","text":"Semicirculi separati, arte connexi, circulum solaris plenitudinis repraesentare possunt.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[]},{"id":"CH_MHON_T_17:A8","text":"Quod arte ex B componitur non propterea naturaliter in B solidum aut perfectum est.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[]},{"id":"CH_MHON_T_17:A9","text":"Minor mensura semicirculorum B disproportionalitatem eius ad D et C indicat.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[]},{"id":"CH_MHON_T_17:A10","text":"Membra ex uno corpore Monadis anatomice educta originem communem servant.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[]},{"id":"CH_MHON_T_17:A11","text":"Origo communis membrorum sympathiam, attractionem et unionem monadicam fundat.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[]}],"original_name":"Load-Bearing Axioms"},{"number":"3","name":"Apophatic Structure","items":[{"id":"CH_MHON_T_17:NX1","text":"B non est figura tam manifesta neque tam vulgariter cognoscibilis quam D et C.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[]},{"id":"CH_MHON_T_17:NX2","text":"B non est naturaliter circulus solaris integer, quia semicirculi eius separati, laxi et dissoluti sunt.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[]},{"id":"CH_MHON_T_17:NX3","text":"Plenitudo solaris ex B non datur immediate, sed per artem connexionis producitur.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[]},{"id":"CH_MHON_T_17:NX4","text":"Ex B verum D sperari non potest.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[]},{"id":"CH_MHON_T_17:NX5","text":"Vera C non fuit primum in natura B; ideo B non est vera luna viva.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[]},{"id":"CH_MHON_T_17:NX6","text":"Vita et motus B non proprie et naturaliter, sed saltem analogice, attribuuntur.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[]},{"id":"CH_MHON_T_17:NX7","text":"Theoria non exhaurit mysteria Monadis, sed iter ad ulteriora tantum indicat.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[]}],"original_name":"Apophatic Structure"},{"number":"4","name":"Derivative Propositions","items":[{"id":"CH_MHON_T_17:D1","text":"D et C, quia ad inferiora magis vergunt et terram hieroglyphice participant, stabilitatem et fixationem magis quam B significant.","refs":["CH_MHON_T_17:A2","CH_MHON_T_17:A3"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"From","passages":[]},{"id":"CH_MHON_T_17:D2","text":"B significat existentiam viventem sub conditione operationis elementaris, non perfectionem solarem iam possessam.","refs":["CH_MHON_T_17:A4","CH_MHON_T_17:A5","CH_MHON_T_17:A6"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"From","passages":[]},{"id":"CH_MHON_T_17:D3","text":"Solaris circulus est terminus possibilis transformationis B, quia semicirculi eius ad punctum commune iam referuntur, licet nondum cohaereant.","refs":["CH_MHON_T_17:A4","CH_MHON_T_17:A7"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"From","passages":[]},{"id":"CH_MHON_T_17:D4","text":"Productio figurae E ex B confirmat transformationem eius in characterem Veneris, non in D aut C verum.","refs":["CH_MHON_T_17:A7","CH_MHON_T_17:A8","CH_MHON_T_17:A9"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"From","passages":[]},{"id":"CH_MHON_T_17:D5","text":"Praedicata de B transferenda sunt analogice, dum praedicata propria eius ad figuram arietinam inversam et ad signum elementorum referuntur.","refs":["CH_MHON_T_17:A5","CH_MHON_T_17:A6","CH_MHON_T_17:A8"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"From","passages":[]},{"id":"CH_MHON_T_17:D6","text":"Ternarius B–D–C est articulatio interna unius Monadis, non aggregatum trium rerum extrinsecarum.","refs":["CH_MHON_T_17:A10","CH_MHON_T_17:A11"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"From","passages":[]}],"original_name":"Derivative Propositions"},{"number":"5","name":"Super-Derivatives","items":[{"id":"CH_MHON_T_17:SD1","text":"Systema perfectionem non ut datum originarium, sed ut effectum artis ordinantis constituit: natura B potentiam praebet, ars plenitudinem formalem efficit.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[]},{"id":"CH_MHON_T_17:SD2","text":"Ordo ontologicus graduum naturae et ordo figuralis transformationum non coincidunt: signum vivum-lunare potest structuram solarem simul potentia continere et actu carere.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[]},{"id":"CH_MHON_T_17:SD3","text":"Inferioritas localis D et C non degradationem simplicem, sed maiorem determinationem, stabilitatem et essentialem manifestiam importat.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[]},{"id":"CH_MHON_T_17:SD4","text":"Unitas monadica non est indistinctio partium, sed vis quae differentias anatomice explicatas in sympathiam reciprocumque nexum reconducit.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[]}],"original_name":"Super-Derivatives"},{"number":"6","name":"Deep Apophatics","items":[{"id":"CH_MHON_T_17:DNX1","text":"Falsum esse debet quod figurae similes eandem essentiam aut eundem gradum perfectionis necessario habeant.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[]},{"id":"CH_MHON_T_17:DNX2","text":"Falsum esse debet quod possibilitas geometrica connexionis iam praesentem unitatem substantialem constituat.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[]},{"id":"CH_MHON_T_17:DNX3","text":"Falsum esse debet quod vita, motus et lunaritas in B proprie praedicentur ex sola specie symbolica.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[]},{"id":"CH_MHON_T_17:DNX4","text":"Falsum esse debet quod dissectio analytica Monadem in res independentes dissolvat.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[]}],"original_name":"Deep Apophatics"},{"number":"7","name":"Super-Apophatics","items":[{"id":"CH_MHON_T_17:SNX1","text":"Systema excludit perfectionem naturalem sine operatione artificiosa: solaritas plena non est status ad manum, sed compositio facienda.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[]},{"id":"CH_MHON_T_17:SNX2","text":"Systema excludit interpretationem omnino exhaustivam: figura est fons mysteriorum indefinite ulteriorum, non obiectum semel clausum.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[]},{"id":"CH_MHON_T_17:SNX3","text":"Systema excludit identitatem immediatam inter visibilem formam et veritatem essentialem, cum B apparere possit lunaris et vitalis sine vera lunaritate vivente.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[]}],"original_name":"Super-Apophatics"},{"number":"8","name":"Formal Generative Schema","items":[{"id":"CH_MHON_T_17:FGS1","text":"Commutatio situs Monadis → distributio anatomica in B, D, C → lectura orientationis, figurae et situs.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[]},{"id":"CH_MHON_T_17:FGS2","text":"D et C terram versus ordinata → stabilitas et fixio → essentiae inferiores magis manifeste determinatae.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[]},{"id":"CH_MHON_T_17:FGS3","text":"B cum duplici luna et cruce → esse + vivere + elementorum magisterium → potentia transmutationis.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[]},{"id":"CH_MHON_T_17:FGS4","text":"Semicirculi B separati → connexio artificialis → circulus solaris repraesentatus → character Veneris, non solaritas naturaliter possessa.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[]},{"id":"CH_MHON_T_17:FGS5","text":"Defectus naturalis B → restrictio praedicatorum ad analogiam → distinctio inter proprium B et translata de D ac C.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[]},{"id":"CH_MHON_T_17:FGS6","text":"Unum corpus dissectum → origo communis membrorum → sympathia magnetica → unio monadica absolutissima.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[]}],"original_name":"Formal Generative Schema"},{"number":"9","name":"Argument Flow Map","items":[{"id":"CH_MHON_T_17:AF1","text":"Commutatio localis Monadis → ternarius B–D–C → differentia essentialis D/C et B → analysis naturae B.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[]},{"id":"CH_MHON_T_17:AF2","text":"Luna duplex + crux elementorum → existentia viva elementis tractanda → semicirculi separati → plenitudo solaris artificialiter componenda.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[]},{"id":"CH_MHON_T_17:AF3","text":"Inadequatio B ad D et C → negatio verae lunae vivae et solaritatis naturalis → praedicatio analogica → restitutio unitatis per sympathiam monadicam.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[]}],"original_name":"Argument Flow Map"},{"number":"10","name":"Map-Effects","items":[{"id":"CH_MHON_T_17:ME1","text":"Monas non iam figura simplex videtur, sed corpus resolubile cuius partes diversa munera, gradus et modos perfectionis exhibent.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[]},{"id":"CH_MHON_T_17:ME2","text":"Oppositio naturae et artis reordinatur: ars non creat ex nihilo, sed potentiam incompositam B in formam solarem apparentem disponit.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[]},{"id":"CH_MHON_T_17:ME3","text":"Solaris, lunaris, elementaris, terrestris et Venerius characteres non species separatae manent, sed effectus positionis, compositionis et mensurae partium fiunt.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[]},{"id":"CH_MHON_T_17:ME4","text":"Unitas et distinctio desinunt esse contraria: discretio anatomica fit instrumentum manifestandae unitatis magneticae.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[]}],"original_name":"Map-Effects"},{"number":"11","name":"Altitude Levels","items":[{"id":"CH_MHON_T_17:ALT1","text":"Surface — Monas relocata in membra B, D et C dividitur; B per semicirculos et crucem, D per circulum punctatum, C per cornua lunaria legitur.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[]},{"id":"CH_MHON_T_17:ALT2","text":"Structural — Figura, directio, proportio et nexus partium constituunt regulas quibus status naturalis, analogicus vel artificialis discernitur.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[]},{"id":"CH_MHON_T_17:ALT3","text":"Metastrategic — Textus interpretationem hieroglyphicam a recognitione visiva ad operationem disciplinatam transfert: videre figuram non sufficit; oportet scire quid arte effici, quid natura possideri possit.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[]},{"id":"CH_MHON_T_17:ALT4","text":"Ontological/Temporal — Esse et vivere sunt gradus initiales; plenitudo solaris est terminus nondum naturaliter adveniens, quem transformatio artificialis temporaliter interponit.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[]}],"original_name":"Altitude Levels"},{"number":"12","name":"Minimal Reconstruction Test","items":[{"id":"CH_MHON_T_17:MRT1","text":"Axiomata sufficiunt ad restituendam conclusionem principalem: B est figura elementaris et lunaris potentiae, solaritatem autem non nisi arte compositam exhibet.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[]},{"id":"CH_MHON_T_17:MRT2","text":"Systema maxime cohaeret ubi figurae, situs inferiores, proportiones et distinctionem naturae ab arte in unam catenam inferentialem coniungit.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[]},{"id":"CH_MHON_T_17:MRT3","text":"Commissura fragilis manet in transitu a proprietatibus graphicis ad essentias, stabilitatem, vitam et veritatem figurarum, quia regula generalis huius transitus hic non demonstratur.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[]},{"id":"CH_MHON_T_17:MRT4","text":"Tensionem constituit quod B dicitur potentiam solaris plenitudinis habere, simul vero nec veram lunam vivam nec verum D in se naturaliter continere.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[]},{"id":"CH_MHON_T_17:MRT5","text":"Unio monadica post dissectionem assertio structuram claudit, sed mechanismus quo sympathia magnetica ex sola origine communi necessario sequatur non plene determinatur.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[]},{"id":"CH_MHON_T_17:MRT6","text":"Restitutio motum textus servat, quia ab relocatione figurali per distinctionem naturalis et artificialis ad unionem finalem procedit, atque mysteriorum ulteriorem indagationem apertam relinquit.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[]}],"original_name":"Minimal Reconstruction Test"}],"language_code":"la","language_name":"Latina","translations":[{"id":"CAREVAR_20261006T222924013488Z_e33f69b1","language_code":"en","language_name":"English","title":"THEOR. XXI.","source_sha256":"f231dd8f05f857adea90b20ecebf63c76f61d80e01b5570c9397f82a82403ba7","sha256":"5203f5bc8860f26fb69602e0508f3919f94c4591f2823179a1d2c1acf92c5567","rendition_type":"machine","review_status":"machine","model":"gpt-5.6-terra","created":"2026-10-06T22:29:24.015639+00:00","sections":[{"number":"1","name":"Argumental Spine","items":[{"id":"CH_MHON_T_17:AS1","text":"It is asked what the new local transformation of the mystical Monad reveals, when its body may be dissected into the members B, D, C.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[]},{"id":"CH_MHON_T_17:AS2","text":"The argumentation is demonstrative and hermetic-hieroglyphic: position, figure, magnitude, and connection of the parts are taken as essential marks.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[]},{"id":"CH_MHON_T_17:AS3","text":"D and C, turned toward the earth and manifest to one another by their figures, indicate lower, stable, and fixed essences; B, however, remains a third member more obscure and less determinate.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[]},{"id":"CH_MHON_T_17:AS4","text":"The nature of B is described through the double moon and the cross of the elements as a degree of existing and living, to be treated by elemental artifice, not as a solar fullness already naturally perfected.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[]},{"id":"CH_MHON_T_17:AS5","text":"From the separated semicircles of B a solar circle can be made, but the effect is artificial; the figure of B itself remains naturally loose, fluid, and incomplete.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[]},{"id":"CH_MHON_T_17:AS6","text":"The dissection of one body into a ternary does not destroy unity: the members, arising from the same Monad, are said to receive mutual sympathy and monadic union.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[]}],"original_name":"Argumental Spine"},{"number":"2","name":"Load-Bearing Axioms","items":[{"id":"CH_MHON_T_17:A1","text":"The local position of the Monad's parts changes their hieroglyphic and philosophical signification.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[]},{"id":"CH_MHON_T_17:A2","text":"The figures of D and C are essentially distinct from the figure of B.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[]},{"id":"CH_MHON_T_17:A3","text":"Direction downward toward the earth, in this art, signifies stability and fixation.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[]},{"id":"CH_MHON_T_17:A4","text":"The lunar semicircle and the complete solar circle are hieroglyphic signs.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[]},{"id":"CH_MHON_T_17:A5","text":"The double lunar figure of B can designate the first two degrees of created natures, being and living.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[]},{"id":"CH_MHON_T_17:A6","text":"The cross appended to B requires the artifice of the elements.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[]},{"id":"CH_MHON_T_17:A7","text":"Separated semicircles, connected by art, can represent the circle of solar fullness.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[]},{"id":"CH_MHON_T_17:A8","text":"What is composed from B by art is not thereby naturally solid or perfect in B.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[]},{"id":"CH_MHON_T_17:A9","text":"The lesser size of B's semicircles indicates its disproportion to D and C.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[]},{"id":"CH_MHON_T_17:A10","text":"Members anatomically drawn forth from one body of the Monad retain a common origin.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[]},{"id":"CH_MHON_T_17:A11","text":"The members' common origin grounds sympathy, attraction, and monadic union.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[]}],"original_name":"Load-Bearing Axioms"},{"number":"3","name":"Apophatic Structure","items":[{"id":"CH_MHON_T_17:NX1","text":"B is not a figure as manifest or as commonly recognizable as D and C.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[]},{"id":"CH_MHON_T_17:NX2","text":"B is not naturally a complete solar circle, because its semicircles are separated, loose, and dissolved.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[]},{"id":"CH_MHON_T_17:NX3","text":"Solar fullness is not given from B immediately, but is produced through the art of connection.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[]},{"id":"CH_MHON_T_17:NX4","text":"From B, a true D cannot be hoped for.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[]},{"id":"CH_MHON_T_17:NX5","text":"The true C was not originally in the nature of B; therefore B is not a true living moon.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[]},{"id":"CH_MHON_T_17:NX6","text":"Life and motion are attributed to B not properly and naturally, but at least analogically.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[]},{"id":"CH_MHON_T_17:NX7","text":"The theory does not exhaust the mysteries of the Monad, but only indicates a path toward further matters.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[]}],"original_name":"Apophatic Structure"},{"number":"4","name":"Derivative Propositions","items":[{"id":"CH_MHON_T_17:D1","text":"D and C, because they incline more toward lower things and hieroglyphically partake of earth, signify stability and fixation more than B.","refs":["CH_MHON_T_17:A2","CH_MHON_T_17:A3"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"From","passages":[]},{"id":"CH_MHON_T_17:D2","text":"B signifies living existence under the condition of elemental operation, not solar perfection already possessed.","refs":["CH_MHON_T_17:A4","CH_MHON_T_17:A5","CH_MHON_T_17:A6"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"From","passages":[]},{"id":"CH_MHON_T_17:D3","text":"The solar circle is a possible terminus of B's transformation, because its semicircles already refer to a common point, although they do not yet cohere.","refs":["CH_MHON_T_17:A4","CH_MHON_T_17:A7"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"From","passages":[]},{"id":"CH_MHON_T_17:D4","text":"The production of figure E from B confirms its transformation into the character of Venus, not into a true D or C.","refs":["CH_MHON_T_17:A7","CH_MHON_T_17:A8","CH_MHON_T_17:A9"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"From","passages":[]},{"id":"CH_MHON_T_17:D5","text":"Predicates concerning B are to be transferred analogically, while its proper predicates are referred to the inverted ram-like figure and to the sign of the elements.","refs":["CH_MHON_T_17:A5","CH_MHON_T_17:A6","CH_MHON_T_17:A8"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"From","passages":[]},{"id":"CH_MHON_T_17:D6","text":"The ternary B–D–C is an internal articulation of one Monad, not an aggregate of three extrinsic things.","refs":["CH_MHON_T_17:A10","CH_MHON_T_17:A11"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"From","passages":[]}],"original_name":"Derivative Propositions"},{"number":"5","name":"Super-Derivatives","items":[{"id":"CH_MHON_T_17:SD1","text":"The system establishes perfection not as an original given, but as an effect of ordering art: the nature of B supplies potency, art produces formal fullness.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[]},{"id":"CH_MHON_T_17:SD2","text":"The ontological order of the degrees of nature and the figural order of transformations do not coincide: the living-lunar sign can at once contain a solar structure in potency and lack it in act.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[]},{"id":"CH_MHON_T_17:SD3","text":"The local inferiority of D and C imports not simple degradation, but greater determination, stability, and essential manifestness.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[]},{"id":"CH_MHON_T_17:SD4","text":"Monadic unity is not the indistinction of parts, but a force that leads differences anatomically unfolded back into sympathy and reciprocal connection.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[]}],"original_name":"Super-Derivatives"},{"number":"6","name":"Deep Apophatics","items":[{"id":"CH_MHON_T_17:DNX1","text":"It must be false that similar figures necessarily have the same essence or the same degree of perfection.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[]},{"id":"CH_MHON_T_17:DNX2","text":"It must be false that the geometrical possibility of connection already constitutes a present substantial unity.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[]},{"id":"CH_MHON_T_17:DNX3","text":"It must be false that life, motion, and lunarity are properly predicated of B from symbolic appearance alone.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[]},{"id":"CH_MHON_T_17:DNX4","text":"It must be false that analytical dissection dissolves the Monad into independent things.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[]}],"original_name":"Deep Apophatics"},{"number":"7","name":"Super-Apophatics","items":[{"id":"CH_MHON_T_17:SNX1","text":"The system excludes natural perfection without artificial operation: full solarity is not a state at hand, but a composition to be made.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[]},{"id":"CH_MHON_T_17:SNX2","text":"The system excludes a wholly exhaustive interpretation: the figure is a source of indefinitely further mysteries, not an object closed once and for all.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[]},{"id":"CH_MHON_T_17:SNX3","text":"The system excludes immediate identity between visible form and essential truth, since B can appear lunar and vital without true living lunarity.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[]}],"original_name":"Super-Apophatics"},{"number":"8","name":"Formal Generative Schema","items":[{"id":"CH_MHON_T_17:FGS1","text":"Transformation of the Monad's position → anatomical distribution into B, D, C → reading of orientation, figure, and position.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[]},{"id":"CH_MHON_T_17:FGS2","text":"D and C ordered toward the earth → stability and fixation → lower essences more manifestly determined.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[]},{"id":"CH_MHON_T_17:FGS3","text":"B with double moon and cross → being + living + mastery of the elements → potentiality of transmutation.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[]},{"id":"CH_MHON_T_17:FGS4","text":"Separated semicircles of B → artificial connection → solar circle represented → character of Venus, not naturally possessed solarity.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[]},{"id":"CH_MHON_T_17:FGS5","text":"Natural defect of B → restriction of predicates to analogy → distinction between what is proper to B and matters transferred from D and C.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[]},{"id":"CH_MHON_T_17:FGS6","text":"One dissected body → common origin of the members → magnetic sympathy → most absolute monadic union.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[]}],"original_name":"Formal Generative Schema"},{"number":"9","name":"Argument Flow Map","items":[{"id":"CH_MHON_T_17:AF1","text":"Local transformation of the Monad → ternary B–D–C → essential difference of D/C and B → analysis of B's nature.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[]},{"id":"CH_MHON_T_17:AF2","text":"Double moon + cross of the elements → living existence to be treated through the elements → separated semicircles → solar fullness to be artificially composed.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[]},{"id":"CH_MHON_T_17:AF3","text":"Inadequacy of B to D and C → negation of a true living moon and natural solarity → analogical predication → restoration of unity through monadic sympathy.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[]}],"original_name":"Argument Flow Map"},{"number":"10","name":"Map-Effects","items":[{"id":"CH_MHON_T_17:ME1","text":"The Monad no longer appears as a simple figure, but as a resolvable body whose parts display diverse functions, degrees, and modes of perfection.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[]},{"id":"CH_MHON_T_17:ME2","text":"The opposition of nature and art is reordered: art does not create from nothing, but disposes B's uncomposed potency into an apparent solar form.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[]},{"id":"CH_MHON_T_17:ME3","text":"Solar, lunar, elemental, terrestrial, and Venusian characters do not remain separate species, but become effects of the position, composition, and measure of the parts.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[]},{"id":"CH_MHON_T_17:ME4","text":"Unity and distinction cease to be contraries: anatomical separation becomes an instrument for manifesting magnetic unity.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[]}],"original_name":"Map-Effects"},{"number":"11","name":"Altitude Levels","items":[{"id":"CH_MHON_T_17:ALT1","text":"Surface — The relocated Monad is divided into members B, D, and C; B is read through semicircles and a cross, D through a dotted circle, C through lunar horns.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[]},{"id":"CH_MHON_T_17:ALT2","text":"Structural — Figure, direction, proportion, and connection of the parts establish the rules by which natural, analogical, or artificial status is discerned.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[]},{"id":"CH_MHON_T_17:ALT3","text":"Metastrategic — The text transfers hieroglyphic interpretation from visual recognition to disciplined operation: seeing a figure is not sufficient; one must know what can be effected by art and what can be possessed by nature.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[]},{"id":"CH_MHON_T_17:ALT4","text":"Ontological/Temporal — Being and living are initial degrees; solar fullness is a terminus not yet naturally attained, which artificial transformation temporally interposes.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[]}],"original_name":"Altitude Levels"},{"number":"12","name":"Minimal Reconstruction Test","items":[{"id":"CH_MHON_T_17:MRT1","text":"The axioms suffice to reconstruct the principal conclusion: B is an elemental and lunar figure of potency, but displays solarity only as composed by art.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[]},{"id":"CH_MHON_T_17:MRT2","text":"The system is most coherent where it joins figures, lower positions, proportions, and the distinction of nature from art into one inferential chain.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[]},{"id":"CH_MHON_T_17:MRT3","text":"A fragile juncture remains in the transition from graphic properties to essences, stability, life, and the truth of figures, because the general rule of this transition is not demonstrated here.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[]},{"id":"CH_MHON_T_17:MRT4","text":"It constitutes a tension that B is said to have the potency of solar fullness, while at the same time naturally containing within itself neither a true living moon nor a true D.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[]},{"id":"CH_MHON_T_17:MRT5","text":"Monadic union after dissection, as an assertion, closes the structure, but the mechanism by which magnetic sympathy necessarily follows from common origin alone is not fully determined.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[]},{"id":"CH_MHON_T_17:MRT6","text":"The reconstruction preserves the movement of the text, because it proceeds from figural relocation through the distinction of natural and artificial to final union, and leaves open the further investigation of mysteries.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[]}],"original_name":"Minimal Reconstruction Test"}],"revision":"CH_MHON_T_17@R001","source_variant_id":"canonical:CH_MHON_T_17:v1","source_language_code":"la","original_title":"THEOR. XXI."}],"identity":{"layer":"CH","kicker":"Chapter 22","title":"Theor. XXI.","context":"Chapter 22","original_title":"Theor. Xxi.","original_context":"Chapter 22"},"original_title":"Theor. Xxi.","title_language_code":"en"},{"id":"CH_MHON_T_18","layer":"CH","title":"Theor. XXII.","order":23,"parents":["RAW_MHON_T_18"],"revision":"CH_MHON_T_18@R001","sha256":"dcda7b9cdebdf8fd0365b8407701e2e996ef97c3779c0f62a4ad93ac5413bc99","sections":[{"number":"1","name":"Argumental Spine","items":[{"id":"CH_MHON_T_18:AS1","text":"Theorema quaerit utrum mysteria MONADIS iam exhausta sint et quomodo ex ea adhuc instrumenta artis sacrae deduci possint.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[]},{"id":"CH_MHON_T_18:AS2","text":"Quaestio in quaestionem novam vertitur: non res extraneae quaerendae sunt, sed nexus MONADIS dissolvendi et partes eius notis distinctae.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[]},{"id":"CH_MHON_T_18:AS3","text":"Fundamentum argumentationis est compositio formarum, litterarum, vasorum, materiarum et operationum intra idem signorum systema.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[]},{"id":"CH_MHON_T_18:AS4","text":"Probatio petitur ex symmetria mystica, metathesi locali, homologis geometricis, functionibus vasorum et effectibus artis.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[]},{"id":"CH_MHON_T_18:AS5","text":"Motus procedit a revelatione selectiva ad decompositionem symbolorum, deinde ad usum operativum et ad interpretationem mysticam.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[]},{"id":"CH_MHON_T_18:AS6","text":"Argumentatio est reconstructiva, mystagogica et symbolico-operativa, cum implicatione cosmologica.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[]}],"original_name":"Argumental Spine"},{"number":"2","name":"Load-Bearing Axioms","items":[{"id":"CH_MHON_T_18:A1","text":"Mysteria MONADIS nondum exhausta sunt.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[]},{"id":"CH_MHON_T_18:A2","text":"Vasa secretiora artis sacrae ex officina MONADIS caute deduci et solis initiatis revelari possunt.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[]},{"id":"CH_MHON_T_18:A3","text":"Omnis nexus MONADIS sapienter dissolvi potest, ut singulae partes distinctis notis designentur.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[]},{"id":"CH_MHON_T_18:A4","text":"Vas a artificiale ex A et B componitur, utrique commune, cum semidiametro M.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[]},{"id":"CH_MHON_T_18:A5","text":"Vas a a prima littera alphabeti Graeci α sola partium metathesi locali differt.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[]},{"id":"CH_MHON_T_18:A6","text":"Recta, circulus et semicirculus veram symmetriam mysticam constituunt, quam etiam circulus cum semicirculo ad idem propositum praestare potest.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[]},{"id":"CH_MHON_T_18:A7","text":"λ et δ quasi imagines aliorum vasorum sunt, λ vitreum et δ terreum.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[]},{"id":"CH_MHON_T_18:A8","text":"λ et δ etiam pistillum ac mortarium significant, quibus lapides artificiales et alia pretiosa in subtilissimos pulveres conteruntur.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[]},{"id":"CH_MHON_T_18:A9","text":"ω vas mysteriorum plenissimum est, ex duobus semicirculis constans et a postrema Graeci alphabeti littera sola partium metathesi differens.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[]},{"id":"CH_MHON_T_18:A10","text":"α ex artificio spiraculi secretissimo et brevissimo specimen primordiale tyronibus elicit, dum via ad subtiliora praeparanda discitur.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[]},{"id":"CH_MHON_T_18:A11","text":"Aer externus et ventus λ in praecipua eius functione magnum damnum afferunt.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[]},{"id":"CH_MHON_T_18:A12","text":"ω OMNIUM HORARUM HOMO est.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[]},{"id":"CH_MHON_T_18:A13","text":"Fructus artis sacrae ex mysterio duarum litterarum oriuntur, nihil extra MONADEM in medium adducto.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[]},{"id":"CH_MHON_T_18:A14","text":"Recta linea quae in α apparet homologiam habet cum parte crucis postremae anatomiae, quae littera M notatur.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[]},{"id":"CH_MHON_T_18:A15","text":"Signa haec non solum aphormas sed etiam apodixes praebent iis quorum vigor igneus et origo caelestis est.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[]},{"id":"CH_MHON_T_18:A16","text":"Dogma non Μυσικὸν sed Μυστικὸν et Arcanum est; mundus λόγῳ δημιουργῷ methodizatur et homo contemplativus per rectam operationem λόγον mysticum discit.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[]}],"original_name":"Load-Bearing Axioms"},{"number":"3","name":"Apophatic Structure","items":[{"id":"CH_MHON_T_18:NX1","text":"MONAS non est corpus mysteriorum iam perfecte exhaustum.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[]},{"id":"CH_MHON_T_18:NX2","text":"Ars sacra non est doctrina omnibus indiscriminatim revelata, sed ad initiatos restringitur.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[]},{"id":"CH_MHON_T_18:NX3","text":"Formae et materiae vasorum vulgarium in hoc loco explicandae non sunt.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[]},{"id":"CH_MHON_T_18:NX4","text":"Explicatio nihil extra MONADEM ad argumentum admittit.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[]},{"id":"CH_MHON_T_18:NX5","text":"Dogma hoc non est Μυσικὸν, id est ad musicam pertinens, sed Μυστικὸν et Arcanum.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[]},{"id":"CH_MHON_T_18:NX6","text":"Functio praecipua λ sine exclusione aeris externi et venti stabilis non est.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[]},{"id":"CH_MHON_T_18:NX7","text":"Discrimen inter constructiones geometricas ad finem mysticum usque ad punctum evanescit, cum ad idem propositum recidant.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[]}],"original_name":"Apophatic Structure"},{"number":"4","name":"Derivative Propositions","items":[{"id":"CH_MHON_T_18:D1","text":"Dissolutio nexus et notatio partium transformant MONADEM in inventarium symbolorum ex quo vasa distincta componi possunt.","refs":["CH_MHON_T_18:A3","CH_MHON_T_18:A4","CH_MHON_T_18:A5"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"From","passages":[]},{"id":"CH_MHON_T_18:D2","text":"Metathesis localis non est mutatio merae positionis, sed medium quo eadem forma diversa munera mystica et operativa accipit.","refs":["CH_MHON_T_18:A5","CH_MHON_T_18:A9"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"From","passages":[]},{"id":"CH_MHON_T_18:D3","text":"Geometria signorum simul est descriptio vasorum, imago instrumentorum et schema operationis materialis.","refs":["CH_MHON_T_18:A6","CH_MHON_T_18:A7","CH_MHON_T_18:A8"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"From","passages":[]},{"id":"CH_MHON_T_18:D4","text":"Ars initiatoria gradatim procedit a specimine primordiali tyronum ad praeparationes subtiliores.","refs":["CH_MHON_T_18:A2","CH_MHON_T_18:A10"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"From","passages":[]},{"id":"CH_MHON_T_18:D5","text":"Efficacia artis non separatur a condicionibus materialibus, quia functio λ per aerem et ventum perturbatur.","refs":["CH_MHON_T_18:A8","CH_MHON_T_18:A11"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"From","passages":[]},{"id":"CH_MHON_T_18:D6","text":"Correspondentia inter litteram, figuram, crucem et anatomiam efficit ut signa MONADIS originem internam partium demonstrent.","refs":["CH_MHON_T_18:A3","CH_MHON_T_18:A13","CH_MHON_T_18:A14"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"From","passages":[]},{"id":"CH_MHON_T_18:D7","text":"Status ω tam temporalem quam anthropologicam dimensionem signo tribuit, quia vas simul «omnium horarum homo» appellatur.","refs":["CH_MHON_T_18:A9","CH_MHON_T_18:A12"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"From","passages":[]},{"id":"CH_MHON_T_18:D8","text":"Signa geometrica, si a subiecto initiato intellecta et operata sint, ex indicibus possibilitatis in testimonia demonstrativa convertuntur.","refs":["CH_MHON_T_18:A15","CH_MHON_T_18:A16"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"From","passages":[]}],"original_name":"Derivative Propositions"},{"number":"5","name":"Super-Derivatives","items":[{"id":"CH_MHON_T_18:SD1","text":"MONAS fit microcosmus generativus in quo litterae, geometria, materia, instrumenta, corpus, initiatio et cosmologia ad idem principium referuntur.","refs":["CH_MHON_T_18:A3","CH_MHON_T_18:A6","CH_MHON_T_18:A8","CH_MHON_T_18:A13","CH_MHON_T_18:A14","CH_MHON_T_18:A16"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"From","passages":[]},{"id":"CH_MHON_T_18:SD2","text":"Cognitio mystica hic non est contemplatio passiva, sed operatio recta qua signum, apparatus et intellectus simul transformantur.","refs":["CH_MHON_T_18:A8","CH_MHON_T_18:A10","CH_MHON_T_18:A16"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"From","passages":[]},{"id":"CH_MHON_T_18:SD3","text":"Systema unitatem MONADIS per analysim obtinet: nexus dissolvuntur ut partes discernantur, sed omnes differentiae ad idem propositum mysticum redeunt.","refs":["CH_MHON_T_18:A3","CH_MHON_T_18:A6","CH_MHON_T_18:A13"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"From","passages":[]},{"id":"CH_MHON_T_18:SD4","text":"Secreta distributio cognitionis est pars mechanismi argumenti, quia specimen tyronibus, subtiliora initiatis et apodixis subiecto praeparato reservantur.","refs":["CH_MHON_T_18:A2","CH_MHON_T_18:A10","CH_MHON_T_18:A15"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"From","passages":[]},{"id":"CH_MHON_T_18:SD5","text":"Tempus et ambitus physicus non sunt adiuncta externa, sed condiciones quae statum et munus signorum determinant.","refs":["CH_MHON_T_18:A11","CH_MHON_T_18:A12"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"From","passages":[]},{"id":"CH_MHON_T_18:SD6","text":"Auctoritas probationis transfertur a demonstratione communiter verificabili ad aptitudinem initiati, cui signa apodicticam vim praebere dicuntur.","refs":["CH_MHON_T_18:A2","CH_MHON_T_18:A15","CH_MHON_T_18:A16"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"From","passages":[]}],"original_name":"Super-Derivatives"},{"number":"6","name":"Deep Apophatics","items":[{"id":"CH_MHON_T_18:DNX1","text":"Systema excludit realitatem autonomam quae extra MONADEM causam, materiam vel finem praebeat.","refs":["CH_MHON_T_18:A13"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"From","passages":[]},{"id":"CH_MHON_T_18:DNX2","text":"Litterae non sunt signa neutra vel mere alphabetica, sed structurae operativae quae vasa et processus generant.","refs":["CH_MHON_T_18:A4","CH_MHON_T_18:A5","CH_MHON_T_18:A8"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"From","passages":[]},{"id":"CH_MHON_T_18:DNX3","text":"Scientia non est universaliter communicabilis, quia intellectus eius dependet ab initiatione, dispositione et gradu artis.","refs":["CH_MHON_T_18:A2","CH_MHON_T_18:A10","CH_MHON_T_18:A15"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"From","passages":[]},{"id":"CH_MHON_T_18:DNX4","text":"Separatio inter formam geometricam, materiam, instrumentum, corpus et mundum non admittitur ut divisio ultima.","refs":["CH_MHON_T_18:A6","CH_MHON_T_18:A8","CH_MHON_T_18:A14","CH_MHON_T_18:A16"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"From","passages":[]},{"id":"CH_MHON_T_18:DNX5","text":"MONAS non claudit seriem in finem exhaustivum, quamvis singulas figuras et functiones definiat.","refs":["CH_MHON_T_18:A1","CH_MHON_T_18:A13"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"From","passages":[]},{"id":"CH_MHON_T_18:DNX6","text":"Intellectus mysticus a recta operatione seiunctus non sufficit.","refs":["CH_MHON_T_18:A10","CH_MHON_T_18:A16"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"From","passages":[]}],"original_name":"Deep Apophatics"},{"number":"7","name":"Super-Apophatics","items":[{"id":"CH_MHON_T_18:SNX1","text":"Argumentatio praecludit probationem ab observatore extrinseco qui initiationem, dispositionem et artem non participat.","refs":["CH_MHON_T_18:A2","CH_MHON_T_18:A15"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"From","passages":[]},{"id":"CH_MHON_T_18:SNX2","text":"Possibilitas signum a re significata absolute separandi excluditur, quia figura ipsa munus, materiam et effectum continere debet.","refs":["CH_MHON_T_18:A7","CH_MHON_T_18:A8","CH_MHON_T_18:A14"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"From","passages":[]},{"id":"CH_MHON_T_18:SNX3","text":"Materia indifferens et ambitus physicus neuter esse non possunt, cum λ ab aere et vento laedatur.","refs":["CH_MHON_T_18:A7","CH_MHON_T_18:A11"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"From","passages":[]},{"id":"CH_MHON_T_18:SNX4","text":"Conclusio definitiva quae omnia mysteria MONADIS clauderet structuram destruere deberet, quia inexhaustio eius principium est.","refs":["CH_MHON_T_18:A1","CH_MHON_T_18:A13"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"From","passages":[]},{"id":"CH_MHON_T_18:SNX5","text":"Publica aequabilitas cognitionis praeclusa est; ordo cognitionis per gradus occultationis et revelationis constituitur.","refs":["CH_MHON_T_18:A2","CH_MHON_T_18:A10","CH_MHON_T_18:A15"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"From","passages":[]}],"original_name":"Super-Apophatics"},{"number":"8","name":"Formal Generative Schema","items":[{"id":"CH_MHON_T_18:FGS1","text":"MONAS inexhausta → nexus universi dissolvuntur → partes litteraliter notantur.","refs":["CH_MHON_T_18:A1","CH_MHON_T_18:A3"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"From","passages":[]},{"id":"CH_MHON_T_18:FGS2","text":"Partes notatae → compositio A et B → vas a cum semidiametro M.","refs":["CH_MHON_T_18:A4"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"From","passages":[]},{"id":"CH_MHON_T_18:FGS3","text":"Metathesis localis → distinctio α, λ, δ et ω → distributio formarum et munerum.","refs":["CH_MHON_T_18:A5","CH_MHON_T_18:A7","CH_MHON_T_18:A9"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"From","passages":[]},{"id":"CH_MHON_T_18:FGS4","text":"Recta, circulus, semicirculus → symmetria mystica → translatio inter figuram, vas et instrumentum.","refs":["CH_MHON_T_18:A6","CH_MHON_T_18:A8"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"From","passages":[]},{"id":"CH_MHON_T_18:FGS5","text":"Instrumentum et materia → conterere lapides artificiales → praeparatio operativa.","refs":["CH_MHON_T_18:A8"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"From","passages":[]},{"id":"CH_MHON_T_18:FGS6","text":"Artificium secretum → specimen tyronum → cognitio subtiliorum viarum.","refs":["CH_MHON_T_18:A2","CH_MHON_T_18:A10"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"From","passages":[]},{"id":"CH_MHON_T_18:FGS7","text":"Munus λ → exclusio aeris et venti; munus ω → generalitas temporalium horarum.","refs":["CH_MHON_T_18:A11","CH_MHON_T_18:A12"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"From","passages":[]},{"id":"CH_MHON_T_18:FGS8","text":"Correspondentiae internae → apodixis initiato → doctrina de homine, mundo et λόγῳ mystico.","refs":["CH_MHON_T_18:A13","CH_MHON_T_18:A14","CH_MHON_T_18:A15","CH_MHON_T_18:A16"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"From","passages":[]}],"original_name":"Formal Generative Schema"},{"number":"9","name":"Argument Flow Map","items":[{"id":"CH_MHON_T_18:AF1","text":"Mysteria MONADIS inexhausta → revelatio vasorum secretorum → dissolutio nexus → notatio partium.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[]},{"id":"CH_MHON_T_18:AF2","text":"Notatio partium → compositio et metathesis litterarum → distinctio vasorum, formarum et materiarum.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[]},{"id":"CH_MHON_T_18:AF3","text":"Distinctio symbolica → functio instrumentalis → gradus initiationis et praeparationis.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[]},{"id":"CH_MHON_T_18:AF4","text":"Functio materialis et temporalis → correspondentiae anatomicae et cosmologicae → signa ut apodixes doctrinae mysticae.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[]}],"original_name":"Argument Flow Map"},{"number":"10","name":"Map-Effects","items":[{"id":"CH_MHON_T_18:ME1","text":"Primum visibile fit non signum isolatum, sed nexus internus ex quo formae, vasa et functiones generantur.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[]},{"id":"CH_MHON_T_18:ME2","text":"Litterae denuo classificatae sunt ut figurae geometricæ, vasa artificialia, instrumenta, materiae et indices anatomici.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[]},{"id":"CH_MHON_T_18:ME3","text":"Oppositio inter mysticum et practicum cadit, quia operatio materialis ipsa fit medium cognitionis mysticae.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[]},{"id":"CH_MHON_T_18:ME4","text":"Ager strategicus cognitionis describitur ut scala initiatoria: occultatio, specimen, praeparatio subtilior, apodixis.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[]},{"id":"CH_MHON_T_18:ME5","text":"Tempus et ambitus physicus in tabulam conceptualem inseruntur ut condiciones constitutivae, non circumstantiae externae.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[]},{"id":"CH_MHON_T_18:ME6","text":"Oppositio inter MONADEM et mundum externum reordinatur, quia mundus, corpus et ars intra productionem MONADIS comprehenduntur.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[]}],"original_name":"Map-Effects"},{"number":"11","name":"Altitude Levels","items":[{"id":"CH_MHON_T_18:ALT1","text":"Surface: Litterae α, λ, δ et ω ut figurae et vasa cum materiis ac functionibus propriis definiuntur.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[]},{"id":"CH_MHON_T_18:ALT2","text":"Structural: Dissolutio nexus, notatio partium et metathesis localis mechanismum differentiationis ac recompositionis constituunt.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[]},{"id":"CH_MHON_T_18:ALT3","text":"Metastrategic: Revelatio gradatim ordinata determinat quis videre, operari et apodixin accipere possit.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[]},{"id":"CH_MHON_T_18:ALT4","text":"Ontological/Temporal: MONAS ut campus realitatis internus ponitur, in quo forma, materia, corpus, mundus et tempus per symbola connectuntur; ω horarum totalitatem et personam humanam coniungit.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[]}],"original_name":"Altitude Levels"},{"number":"12","name":"Minimal Reconstruction Test","items":[{"id":"CH_MHON_T_18:MRT1","text":"Axiomata sufficiunt ad restituendam seriem fundamentalem: inexhaustio MONADIS, dissolutio partium, compositio vasorum, functio materialis et transitus ad doctrinam mysticam.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[]},{"id":"CH_MHON_T_18:MRT2","text":"Firmitas maxima est in nexu inter decompositionem symbolicam, metathesin localem, symmetriam et correspondentias vasorum.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[]},{"id":"CH_MHON_T_18:MRT3","text":"Exacta relatio graphica partium et omnium formarum e figuris adiunctis tantum ex parte determinatur; schema non restituit omnem geometriam.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[]},{"id":"CH_MHON_T_18:MRT4","text":"Locus fragilis est transitus a similitudine figurae ad efficaciam materialem et inde ad vim apodicticam.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[]},{"id":"CH_MHON_T_18:MRT5","text":"Tensio manet inter secretum initiatorum et exhibitionem publicam, inter analysim partium et unitatem MONADIS, inter inexhaustionem et assertionem demonstrativam.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[]},{"id":"CH_MHON_T_18:MRT6","text":"Passus Hebraicus et nonnulla vocabula in fonte illegibilia vel ancipitia manent, unde relatio inter artificium secretum et specimen primordiale plene definiri non potest.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[]},{"id":"CH_MHON_T_18:MRT7","text":"Restitutio motum verum argumentationis servat, sed non potest supplere proceduras occultas quas textus solis initiatis reservat.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[]}],"original_name":"Minimal Reconstruction Test"}],"language_code":"la","language_name":"Latina","translations":[{"id":"CAREVAR_20261006T222949330865Z_23e88df7","language_code":"en","language_name":"English","title":"THEOREM XXII.","source_sha256":"dcda7b9cdebdf8fd0365b8407701e2e996ef97c3779c0f62a4ad93ac5413bc99","sha256":"e9c470b9321ee288f0599275550239c5fa946160a553704bd2408884882c0889","rendition_type":"machine","review_status":"machine","model":"gpt-5.6-terra","created":"2026-10-06T22:29:49.332620+00:00","sections":[{"number":"1","name":"Argumental Spine","items":[{"id":"CH_MHON_T_18:AS1","text":"The theorem asks whether the mysteries of the MONAD have already been exhausted and how instruments of the sacred art may still be derived from it.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[]},{"id":"CH_MHON_T_18:AS2","text":"The question turns into a new question: not extraneous things are to be sought, but the connections of the MONAD are to be dissolved and its parts distinguished by signs.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[]},{"id":"CH_MHON_T_18:AS3","text":"The foundation of the argument is the composition of forms, letters, vessels, materials, and operations within the same system of signs.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[]},{"id":"CH_MHON_T_18:AS4","text":"Proof is sought from mystical symmetry, local metathesis, geometric homologues, functions of vessels, and effects of the art.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[]},{"id":"CH_MHON_T_18:AS5","text":"The movement proceeds from selective revelation to the decomposition of symbols, then to operative use and mystical interpretation.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[]},{"id":"CH_MHON_T_18:AS6","text":"The argumentation is reconstructive, mystagogical, and symbolic-operative, with cosmological implication.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[]}],"original_name":"Argumental Spine"},{"number":"2","name":"Load-Bearing Axioms","items":[{"id":"CH_MHON_T_18:A1","text":"The mysteries of the MONAD have not yet been exhausted.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[]},{"id":"CH_MHON_T_18:A2","text":"The more secret vessels of the sacred art may be cautiously derived from the workshop of the MONAD and revealed only to initiates.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[]},{"id":"CH_MHON_T_18:A3","text":"Every connection of the MONAD can be wisely dissolved, so that its individual parts may be designated by distinct signs.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[]},{"id":"CH_MHON_T_18:A4","text":"The artificial vessel a is composed from A and B, common to both, with semidiameter M.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[]},{"id":"CH_MHON_T_18:A5","text":"The vessel a differs from the first letter of the Greek alphabet α solely by a local metathesis of parts.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[]},{"id":"CH_MHON_T_18:A6","text":"The straight line, circle, and semicircle constitute true mystical symmetry, which the circle together with the semicircle can also provide for the same purpose.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[]},{"id":"CH_MHON_T_18:A7","text":"λ and δ are, as it were, images of other vessels, λ glass and δ earthen.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[]},{"id":"CH_MHON_T_18:A8","text":"λ and δ also signify pestle and mortar, by which artificial stones and other precious things are ground into the finest powders.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[]},{"id":"CH_MHON_T_18:A9","text":"ω is the vessel most full of mysteries, consisting of two semicircles and differing from the last letter of the Greek alphabet solely by a metathesis of parts.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[]},{"id":"CH_MHON_T_18:A10","text":"α draws forth for novices a primordial specimen from the most secret and shortest artifice of the vent, while the way of preparing subtler things is learned.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[]},{"id":"CH_MHON_T_18:A11","text":"External air and the wind λ inflict great damage upon its principal function.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[]},{"id":"CH_MHON_T_18:A12","text":"ω is OMNIUM HORARUM HOMO.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[]},{"id":"CH_MHON_T_18:A13","text":"The fruits of the sacred art arise from the mystery of two letters, with nothing outside the MONAD brought into consideration.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[]},{"id":"CH_MHON_T_18:A14","text":"The straight line that appears in α has a homology with the part of the final cross of anatomy, which is marked by the letter M.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[]},{"id":"CH_MHON_T_18:A15","text":"These signs provide not only aphormae but also apodixes for those whose force is fiery and whose origin is celestial.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[]},{"id":"CH_MHON_T_18:A16","text":"The doctrine is not Μυσικὸν but Μυστικὸν and Arcanum; the world is methodized by λόγῳ δημιουργῷ, and the contemplative man learns the mystical λόγον through right operation.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[]}],"original_name":"Load-Bearing Axioms"},{"number":"3","name":"Apophatic Structure","items":[{"id":"CH_MHON_T_18:NX1","text":"The MONAD is not a body of mysteries already perfectly exhausted.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[]},{"id":"CH_MHON_T_18:NX2","text":"The sacred art is not a doctrine revealed indiscriminately to all, but is restricted to initiates.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[]},{"id":"CH_MHON_T_18:NX3","text":"The forms and materials of common vessels are not to be explained here.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[]},{"id":"CH_MHON_T_18:NX4","text":"The explanation admits nothing outside the MONAD into the argument.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[]},{"id":"CH_MHON_T_18:NX5","text":"This doctrine is not Μυσικὸν, that is, pertaining to music, but Μυστικὸν and Arcanum.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[]},{"id":"CH_MHON_T_18:NX6","text":"The principal function of λ is not without the exclusion of external air and wind.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[]},{"id":"CH_MHON_T_18:NX7","text":"The difference between geometric constructions vanishes, up to a point, with respect to the mystical end, since they return to the same purpose.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[]}],"original_name":"Apophatic Structure"},{"number":"4","name":"Derivative Propositions","items":[{"id":"CH_MHON_T_18:D1","text":"The dissolution of connections and notation of parts transform the MONAD into an inventory of symbols from which distinct vessels may be composed.","refs":["CH_MHON_T_18:A3","CH_MHON_T_18:A4","CH_MHON_T_18:A5"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"From","passages":[]},{"id":"CH_MHON_T_18:D2","text":"Local metathesis is not a mere change of position, but the means by which the same form assumes diverse mystical and operative functions.","refs":["CH_MHON_T_18:A5","CH_MHON_T_18:A9"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"From","passages":[]},{"id":"CH_MHON_T_18:D3","text":"The geometry of signs is at once a description of vessels, an image of instruments, and a schema of material operation.","refs":["CH_MHON_T_18:A6","CH_MHON_T_18:A7","CH_MHON_T_18:A8"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"From","passages":[]},{"id":"CH_MHON_T_18:D4","text":"Initiatory art proceeds by stages from the primordial specimen for novices to subtler preparations.","refs":["CH_MHON_T_18:A2","CH_MHON_T_18:A10"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"From","passages":[]},{"id":"CH_MHON_T_18:D5","text":"The efficacy of the art is not separable from material conditions, because the function of λ is disturbed by air and wind.","refs":["CH_MHON_T_18:A8","CH_MHON_T_18:A11"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"From","passages":[]},{"id":"CH_MHON_T_18:D6","text":"The correspondence among letter, figure, cross, and anatomy causes the signs of the MONAD to demonstrate the internal origin of the parts.","refs":["CH_MHON_T_18:A3","CH_MHON_T_18:A13","CH_MHON_T_18:A14"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"From","passages":[]},{"id":"CH_MHON_T_18:D7","text":"The status of ω attributes both a temporal and an anthropological dimension to the sign, because the vessel is at once called «man of all hours».","refs":["CH_MHON_T_18:A9","CH_MHON_T_18:A12"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"From","passages":[]},{"id":"CH_MHON_T_18:D8","text":"Geometric signs, if understood and operated by an initiated subject, are converted from indications of possibility into demonstrative testimonies.","refs":["CH_MHON_T_18:A15","CH_MHON_T_18:A16"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"From","passages":[]}],"original_name":"Derivative Propositions"},{"number":"5","name":"Super-Derivatives","items":[{"id":"CH_MHON_T_18:SD1","text":"The MONAD becomes a generative microcosm in which letters, geometry, matter, instruments, body, initiation, and cosmology are referred to the same principle.","refs":["CH_MHON_T_18:A3","CH_MHON_T_18:A6","CH_MHON_T_18:A8","CH_MHON_T_18:A13","CH_MHON_T_18:A14","CH_MHON_T_18:A16"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"From","passages":[]},{"id":"CH_MHON_T_18:SD2","text":"Mystical knowledge here is not passive contemplation, but right operation through which sign, apparatus, and intellect are transformed together.","refs":["CH_MHON_T_18:A8","CH_MHON_T_18:A10","CH_MHON_T_18:A16"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"From","passages":[]},{"id":"CH_MHON_T_18:SD3","text":"The system obtains the unity of the MONAD through analysis: connections are dissolved so that parts may be discerned, but all differences return to the same mystical purpose.","refs":["CH_MHON_T_18:A3","CH_MHON_T_18:A6","CH_MHON_T_18:A13"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"From","passages":[]},{"id":"CH_MHON_T_18:SD4","text":"The secret distribution of knowledge is part of the mechanism of the argument, because the specimen is reserved for novices, subtler matters for initiates, and apodixis for the prepared subject.","refs":["CH_MHON_T_18:A2","CH_MHON_T_18:A10","CH_MHON_T_18:A15"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"From","passages":[]},{"id":"CH_MHON_T_18:SD5","text":"Time and the physical environment are not external adjuncts, but conditions that determine the status and function of signs.","refs":["CH_MHON_T_18:A11","CH_MHON_T_18:A12"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"From","passages":[]},{"id":"CH_MHON_T_18:SD6","text":"The authority of proof is transferred from demonstration verifiable in common to the aptitude of the initiate, to whom signs are said to furnish apodictic force.","refs":["CH_MHON_T_18:A2","CH_MHON_T_18:A15","CH_MHON_T_18:A16"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"From","passages":[]}],"original_name":"Super-Derivatives"},{"number":"6","name":"Deep Apophatics","items":[{"id":"CH_MHON_T_18:DNX1","text":"The system excludes an autonomous reality that would provide cause, matter, or end outside the MONAD.","refs":["CH_MHON_T_18:A13"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"From","passages":[]},{"id":"CH_MHON_T_18:DNX2","text":"Letters are not neutral or merely alphabetical signs, but operative structures that generate vessels and processes.","refs":["CH_MHON_T_18:A4","CH_MHON_T_18:A5","CH_MHON_T_18:A8"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"From","passages":[]},{"id":"CH_MHON_T_18:DNX3","text":"Knowledge is not universally communicable, because its understanding depends upon initiation, disposition, and degree of art.","refs":["CH_MHON_T_18:A2","CH_MHON_T_18:A10","CH_MHON_T_18:A15"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"From","passages":[]},{"id":"CH_MHON_T_18:DNX4","text":"The separation between geometric form, matter, instrument, body, and world is not admitted as an ultimate division.","refs":["CH_MHON_T_18:A6","CH_MHON_T_18:A8","CH_MHON_T_18:A14","CH_MHON_T_18:A16"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"From","passages":[]},{"id":"CH_MHON_T_18:DNX5","text":"The MONAD does not close the series in an exhaustive end, although it defines individual figures and functions.","refs":["CH_MHON_T_18:A1","CH_MHON_T_18:A13"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"From","passages":[]},{"id":"CH_MHON_T_18:DNX6","text":"Mystical understanding separated from right operation is insufficient.","refs":["CH_MHON_T_18:A10","CH_MHON_T_18:A16"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"From","passages":[]}],"original_name":"Deep Apophatics"},{"number":"7","name":"Super-Apophatics","items":[{"id":"CH_MHON_T_18:SNX1","text":"The argument precludes proof by an extrinsic observer who does not participate in initiation, disposition, and art.","refs":["CH_MHON_T_18:A2","CH_MHON_T_18:A15"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"From","passages":[]},{"id":"CH_MHON_T_18:SNX2","text":"The possibility of absolutely separating a sign from the thing signified is excluded, because the figure itself must contain function, matter, and effect.","refs":["CH_MHON_T_18:A7","CH_MHON_T_18:A8","CH_MHON_T_18:A14"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"From","passages":[]},{"id":"CH_MHON_T_18:SNX3","text":"Indifferent matter and a neutral physical environment cannot exist, since λ is harmed by air and wind.","refs":["CH_MHON_T_18:A7","CH_MHON_T_18:A11"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"From","passages":[]},{"id":"CH_MHON_T_18:SNX4","text":"A definitive conclusion that would close all the mysteries of the MONAD would have to destroy the structure, because its inexhaustibility is a principle.","refs":["CH_MHON_T_18:A1","CH_MHON_T_18:A13"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"From","passages":[]},{"id":"CH_MHON_T_18:SNX5","text":"Public equality of knowledge is precluded; the order of knowledge is constituted through degrees of concealment and revelation.","refs":["CH_MHON_T_18:A2","CH_MHON_T_18:A10","CH_MHON_T_18:A15"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"From","passages":[]}],"original_name":"Super-Apophatics"},{"number":"8","name":"Formal Generative Schema","items":[{"id":"CH_MHON_T_18:FGS1","text":"Inexhausted MONAD → connections of the whole are dissolved → parts are marked literally.","refs":["CH_MHON_T_18:A1","CH_MHON_T_18:A3"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"From","passages":[]},{"id":"CH_MHON_T_18:FGS2","text":"Marked parts → composition of A and B → vessel a with semidiameter M.","refs":["CH_MHON_T_18:A4"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"From","passages":[]},{"id":"CH_MHON_T_18:FGS3","text":"Local metathesis → distinction of α, λ, δ, and ω → distribution of forms and functions.","refs":["CH_MHON_T_18:A5","CH_MHON_T_18:A7","CH_MHON_T_18:A9"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"From","passages":[]},{"id":"CH_MHON_T_18:FGS4","text":"Straight line, circle, semicircle → mystical symmetry → translation among figure, vessel, and instrument.","refs":["CH_MHON_T_18:A6","CH_MHON_T_18:A8"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"From","passages":[]},{"id":"CH_MHON_T_18:FGS5","text":"Instrument and matter → grind artificial stones → operative preparation.","refs":["CH_MHON_T_18:A8"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"From","passages":[]},{"id":"CH_MHON_T_18:FGS6","text":"Secret artifice → specimen for novices → knowledge of subtler ways.","refs":["CH_MHON_T_18:A2","CH_MHON_T_18:A10"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"From","passages":[]},{"id":"CH_MHON_T_18:FGS7","text":"Function of λ → exclusion of air and wind; function of ω → generality of temporal hours.","refs":["CH_MHON_T_18:A11","CH_MHON_T_18:A12"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"From","passages":[]},{"id":"CH_MHON_T_18:FGS8","text":"Internal correspondences → apodixis to the initiate → doctrine concerning man, world, and mystical λόγῳ.","refs":["CH_MHON_T_18:A13","CH_MHON_T_18:A14","CH_MHON_T_18:A15","CH_MHON_T_18:A16"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"From","passages":[]}],"original_name":"Formal Generative Schema"},{"number":"9","name":"Argument Flow Map","items":[{"id":"CH_MHON_T_18:AF1","text":"Inexhausted mysteries of the MONAD → revelation of secret vessels → dissolution of connections → notation of parts.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[]},{"id":"CH_MHON_T_18:AF2","text":"Notation of parts → composition and metathesis of letters → distinction of vessels, forms, and materials.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[]},{"id":"CH_MHON_T_18:AF3","text":"Symbolic distinction → instrumental function → degrees of initiation and preparation.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[]},{"id":"CH_MHON_T_18:AF4","text":"Material and temporal function → anatomical and cosmological correspondences → signs as apodixes of mystical doctrine.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[]}],"original_name":"Argument Flow Map"},{"number":"10","name":"Map-Effects","items":[{"id":"CH_MHON_T_18:ME1","text":"What first becomes visible is not an isolated sign, but the internal connection from which forms, vessels, and functions are generated.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[]},{"id":"CH_MHON_T_18:ME2","text":"Letters are classified anew as geometric figures, artificial vessels, instruments, materials, and anatomical indices.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[]},{"id":"CH_MHON_T_18:ME3","text":"The opposition between mystical and practical falls away, because material operation itself becomes a means of mystical knowledge.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[]},{"id":"CH_MHON_T_18:ME4","text":"The strategic field of knowledge is described as an initiatory scale: concealment, specimen, subtler preparation, apodixis.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[]},{"id":"CH_MHON_T_18:ME5","text":"Time and the physical environment are inserted into the conceptual map as constitutive conditions, not external circumstances.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[]},{"id":"CH_MHON_T_18:ME6","text":"The opposition between the MONAD and the external world is reordered, because world, body, and art are comprehended within the production of the MONAD.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[]}],"original_name":"Map-Effects"},{"number":"11","name":"Altitude Levels","items":[{"id":"CH_MHON_T_18:ALT1","text":"Surface: The letters α, λ, δ, and ω are defined as figures and vessels with their own materials and functions.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[]},{"id":"CH_MHON_T_18:ALT2","text":"Structural: The dissolution of connections, notation of parts, and local metathesis constitute the mechanism of differentiation and recomposition.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[]},{"id":"CH_MHON_T_18:ALT3","text":"Metastrategic: Gradually ordered revelation determines who may see, operate, and receive apodixis.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[]},{"id":"CH_MHON_T_18:ALT4","text":"Ontological/Temporal: The MONAD is posited as an internal field of reality, in which form, matter, body, world, and time are connected through symbols; ω joins the totality of hours and the human person.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[]}],"original_name":"Altitude Levels"},{"number":"12","name":"Minimal Reconstruction Test","items":[{"id":"CH_MHON_T_18:MRT1","text":"The axioms suffice to restore the fundamental sequence: the inexhaustibility of the MONAD, dissolution of parts, composition of vessels, material function, and transition to mystical doctrine.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[]},{"id":"CH_MHON_T_18:MRT2","text":"The greatest firmness lies in the connection among symbolic decomposition, local metathesis, symmetry, and correspondences of vessels.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[]},{"id":"CH_MHON_T_18:MRT3","text":"The exact graphic relation of the parts and of all forms is only partially determined from the attached figures; the schema does not restore all geometry.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[]},{"id":"CH_MHON_T_18:MRT4","text":"The fragile point is the transition from resemblance of figure to material efficacy and thence to apodictic force.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[]},{"id":"CH_MHON_T_18:MRT5","text":"A tension remains between the secrecy of initiates and public display, between analysis of parts and the unity of the MONAD, between inexhaustibility and demonstrative assertion.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[]},{"id":"CH_MHON_T_18:MRT6","text":"The Hebrew passage and certain words in the source remain illegible or ambiguous, whence the relation between the secret artifice and the primordial specimen cannot be fully defined.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[]},{"id":"CH_MHON_T_18:MRT7","text":"The reconstruction preserves the true movement of the argumentation, but cannot supply the hidden procedures that the text reserves for initiates alone.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[]}],"original_name":"Minimal Reconstruction Test"}],"revision":"CH_MHON_T_18@R001","source_variant_id":"canonical:CH_MHON_T_18:v1","source_language_code":"la"}],"identity":{"layer":"CH","kicker":"Chapter 23","title":"Theor. XXII.","context":"Chapter 23","original_title":"Theor. Xxii.","original_context":"Chapter 23"},"original_title":"Theor. Xxii.","title_language_code":"en"},{"id":"CH_MHON_T_19","layer":"CH","title":"T H E O R. XXIII.","order":24,"parents":["RAW_MHON_T_19"],"revision":"CH_MHON_T_19@R001","sha256":"b27dfc95e481a91bc5d42578250c1d1fcad14556609b39bb6928015f4085a908","sections":[{"number":"1","name":"Argumental Spine","items":[{"id":"CH_MHON_T_19:AS1","text":"Textus quaerit quibus commensurationibus figura hieroglyphica Monadis, ex cruce elementari derivata, necessarie constituatur.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[]},{"id":"CH_MHON_T_19:AS2","text":"Argumentatio constructiva et numerologico-metaphysica est: elementa, proportiones lineares, figurae circulares et operationes numerales in unum canonem rediguntur.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[]},{"id":"CH_MHON_T_19:AS3","text":"Crux elementaris ponitur ut medium quo inaequalitas naturalis elementorum arte in symmetriam ordinatam convertitur.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[]},{"id":"CH_MHON_T_19:AS4","text":"Constructio geometrica ex mensura fundamentali AB producit quaternarium linearum, cuius divisiones mysticae numerorum doctrinam fundant.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[]},{"id":"CH_MHON_T_19:AS5","text":"Quaternarius et denarius non merae numerationis terminationes sunt, sed fines processuum quos Monas physice restituta intra ordines creatos patitur.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[]},{"id":"CH_MHON_T_19:AS6","text":"Motus argumentationis est: elementa inaequalia → reductio artificialis ad symmetriam → constructio crucis → quaternarius numericus → canon permutationis et mensurae → limes cosmologicus et theologica clausura.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[]}],"original_name":"Argumental Spine"},{"number":"2","name":"Load-Bearing Axioms","items":[{"id":"CH_MHON_T_19:A1","text":"Quidquid sub caelo Lunae generationem capit aut ex quattuor elementis coagmentatur aut essentia elementaris est.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[]},{"id":"CH_MHON_T_19:A2","text":"Elementa in nulla re creata naturaliter aequali proportione vel virtute insunt.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[]},{"id":"CH_MHON_T_19:A3","text":"Ars potest in rebus quibusdam elementorum inaequalitatem ad aequalitatem reducere.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[]},{"id":"CH_MHON_T_19:A4","text":"Crux elementaris partes simul aequales et inaequales constituit; haec duplex partitio relationem unius ad plura et eiusdem ad diversum exprimit.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[]},{"id":"CH_MHON_T_19:A5","text":"Longitudo AB est mensura communis ex qua proportiones principales crucis derivantur.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[]},{"id":"CH_MHON_T_19:A6","text":"Lineae AC, AD et AE secundum rationes triplam et duplices ipsius AB crucem elementarem constituunt, ita ut DE sit quadrupla AB.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[]},{"id":"CH_MHON_T_19:A7","text":"Symmetriae mysticae figurae sunt invariantes necessariae, cum ornamenta, litterae additae et modificationes superficiales arbitrio artificis relinquantur.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[]},{"id":"CH_MHON_T_19:A8","text":"Productum multiplicationis continuae numerorum a prima Monade ad ultimum numerum descriptum omnem metathesin possibilem illius multitudinis determinat.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[]},{"id":"CH_MHON_T_19:A9","text":"Quaternarius simul ordinem permutativum, summam Pythagoricam et omnes additiones partium ordinat.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[]},{"id":"CH_MHON_T_19:A10","text":"Numeri ex symmetriis Monadis eliciti pondus elementorum, mensuras temporum et gradus potestatis vel virtutis praescribere possunt.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[]},{"id":"CH_MHON_T_19:A11","text":"Monas integre physiceque restituta est unitas plenissima, cui nulla potestas creata ulterioris motus principium ultra quaternam revolutionem supercaelestem imponere potest.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[]},{"id":"CH_MHON_T_19:A12","text":"Terminatio quaternaria vel denaria fundatur in limite potestatis creatae, non in explicatione a maioribus tradita.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[]}],"original_name":"Load-Bearing Axioms"},{"number":"3","name":"Apophatic Structure","items":[{"id":"CH_MHON_T_19:NX1","text":"Aequalitas elementorum non est conditio naturalis rei creatae.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[]},{"id":"CH_MHON_T_19:NX2","text":"Ornamentum figurae non est necessitas mystica neque pars ratio symmetriarum constituens.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[]},{"id":"CH_MHON_T_19:NX3","text":"Centra geometrica visibilia esse non debent, excepto centro solari littera I signato.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[]},{"id":"CH_MHON_T_19:NX4","text":"Demonstratio integra singularum causarum symmetriarum non datur, quia fines propositi transgrederetur.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[]},{"id":"CH_MHON_T_19:NX5","text":"Multiplicatio numerorum ex virtute arithmetica quaternarii sola obscuritatem naturae non necessario illustrat.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[]},{"id":"CH_MHON_T_19:NX6","text":"Ratio maiorum de fine quaternarii aut denarii non habetur absoluta neque exacta.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[]},{"id":"CH_MHON_T_19:NX7","text":"Nulla potestas influentialis creata, sive elementalis sive caelestis sive supercaelestis, Monadem restitutam ultra quater impellere potest.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[]}],"original_name":"Apophatic Structure"},{"number":"4","name":"Derivative Propositions","items":[{"id":"CH_MHON_T_19:D1","text":"Cum mixtio naturalis elementorum sit inaequalis et ars aequalitatem restituere possit, crux non simpliciter imaginem naturae imitatur sed naturam in forma regulata corrigit.","refs":["CH_MHON_T_19:A2","CH_MHON_T_19:A3","CH_MHON_T_19:A4"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"From","passages":[]},{"id":"CH_MHON_T_19:D2","text":"Mensura AB fungitur ut modulus conversionis inter lineas crucis, diametros, semicirculos et totalem figuram.","refs":["CH_MHON_T_19:A5","CH_MHON_T_19:A6"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"From","passages":[]},{"id":"CH_MHON_T_19:D3","text":"Figura potest variari in superficie visibili, dummodo proportiones generatrices non laedantur.","refs":["CH_MHON_T_19:A5","CH_MHON_T_19:A6","CH_MHON_T_19:A7"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"From","passages":[]},{"id":"CH_MHON_T_19:D4","text":"Quaternarius non est tantum numerus quattuor linearum, sed structura partitionis cuius permutationes, summae et additiones diversis operationibus numerantur.","refs":["CH_MHON_T_19:A4","CH_MHON_T_19:A8","CH_MHON_T_19:A9"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"From","passages":[]},{"id":"CH_MHON_T_19:D5","text":"Canon metatheseos extendit rationem combinatoriam a numeris ad res diversas, examinationem naturae et negotia Reipublicae.","refs":["CH_MHON_T_19:A8"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"From","passages":[]},{"id":"CH_MHON_T_19:D6","text":"Quaternarii et denarii finis explicatur ut clausura dynamica ordinis creati: potestas creata, etiamsi plena sit, non producit progressum indefinitum.","refs":["CH_MHON_T_19:A11","CH_MHON_T_19:A12"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"From","passages":[]},{"id":"CH_MHON_T_19:D7","text":"Quattuor agentibus simul philosophantibus effectus ad Monadem relatus exemplum concordiae numericae, cognitionis et potestatis super creaturas exhibet.","refs":["CH_MHON_T_19:A9","CH_MHON_T_19:A11"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"From","passages":[]}],"original_name":"Derivative Propositions"},{"number":"5","name":"Super-Derivatives","items":[{"id":"CH_MHON_T_19:SD1","text":"Geometria hic fit apparatus transpositionis: eadem ratio mensuralis corpus signi, compositionem elementorum, combinatoriam numerorum et ordinem cosmicum in campo uno tractabili componit.","refs":["CH_MHON_T_19:A1","CH_MHON_T_19:A4","CH_MHON_T_19:A5","CH_MHON_T_19:A8","CH_MHON_T_19:A10"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"From","passages":[]},{"id":"CH_MHON_T_19:SD2","text":"Aequalitas non opponitur inaequalitati tamquam eius negatio absoluta, sed est forma artificiose producta quae inaequalitatem naturalem continet et ordinat.","refs":["CH_MHON_T_19:A2","CH_MHON_T_19:A3","CH_MHON_T_19:A4"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"From","passages":[]},{"id":"CH_MHON_T_19:SD3","text":"Numerus fit principium limitativum: quaternarius et denarius non solum res enumerant, sed ambitum mutationis, ordinem potentiarum et terminos progressus definiunt.","refs":["CH_MHON_T_19:A8","CH_MHON_T_19:A10","CH_MHON_T_19:A11","CH_MHON_T_19:A12"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"From","passages":[]},{"id":"CH_MHON_T_19:SD4","text":"Disciplina hieroglyphica postulatur ut scientia transmissibilis, cuius permanentia pendet a conservatione commensurationum potius quam a fidelitate ornamentali.","refs":["CH_MHON_T_19:A6","CH_MHON_T_19:A7"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"From","passages":[]}],"original_name":"Super-Derivatives"},{"number":"6","name":"Deep Apophatics","items":[{"id":"CH_MHON_T_19:DNX1","text":"Figura valida non potest esse compositio libera signorum: eius veritas tacite pendet a proportionibus quae ante omnem ornatum determinant quid figura esse possit.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[]},{"id":"CH_MHON_T_19:DNX2","text":"Natura non sufficit ut mensura absoluta, quia in ipsa rerum creatione elementa inaequaliter distributa supponuntur et ars ad aequalitatem reducens requiritur.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[]},{"id":"CH_MHON_T_19:DNX3","text":"Pluralitas numerorum non est per se incrementum cognitionis; numerus legitimus est tantum qui obscuritatem naturae illuminat.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[]},{"id":"CH_MHON_T_19:DNX4","text":"Motus vel progressus creatus non est indefinitus, quia Monas restituta nullam causam creatam ad ulteriorem impulsionem recipit.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[]},{"id":"CH_MHON_T_19:DNX5","text":"Ratio cosmologica maiorum repudiatur quatenus finem numeralem sine fundamento sufficienti tradit.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[]}],"original_name":"Deep Apophatics"},{"number":"7","name":"Super-Apophatics","items":[{"id":"CH_MHON_T_19:SNX1","text":"Systema ordinatur circa impossibilitatem causalitatis creatae absolutae: potestas creata plenissime dotata terminum suum non transcendit.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[]},{"id":"CH_MHON_T_19:SNX2","text":"Veritas integra symmetriarum non praesentatur ut plane exhausta; disciplina futura manet amplior libello et a temporis processu sub nutu divino manifestanda.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[]},{"id":"CH_MHON_T_19:SNX3","text":"Ratio ultima figurae non residet in visibilitate: centra absconduntur, ornamenta sunt contingenta, et necessitas in relationibus non apparentibus collocatur.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[]}],"original_name":"Super-Apophatics"},{"number":"8","name":"Formal Generative Schema","items":[{"id":"CH_MHON_T_19:FGS1","text":"Generatio sublunaris ex elementis → inaequalitas naturalis elementorum → reductio artificialis ad aequalitatem → crux ut corpus proportionum aequalium et inaequalium.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[]},{"id":"CH_MHON_T_19:FGS2","text":"Modulus AB → proportiones triplae et duplices → quaternarius linearum → circuli, semicirculi et superficies crucis → figura hieroglyphica constructa.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[]},{"id":"CH_MHON_T_19:FGS3","text":"Quaternarius geometricus → partitiones mysticae → permutationes et summae → canon metatheseos → mensura elementorum, temporum et graduum.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[]},{"id":"CH_MHON_T_19:FGS4","text":"Monas physice restituta → unitas plenissima → imminutio causalitatis creatae → limes quaternarius motus → finis quaternarii vel denarii.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[]},{"id":"CH_MHON_T_19:FGS5","text":"Proportiones necessariae conservatae + ornamenta contingenter addita → transmissio figurae sine corruptione disciplinae.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[]}],"original_name":"Formal Generative Schema"},{"number":"9","name":"Argument Flow Map","items":[{"id":"CH_MHON_T_19:AF1","text":"Elementa inaequalia → ars aequans → crux elementaris → proportiones ex AB.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[]},{"id":"CH_MHON_T_19:AF2","text":"Proportiones crucis → quaternarius linearum → divisio mystica → quaternarius Pythagoricus.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[]},{"id":"CH_MHON_T_19:AF3","text":"Quaternarius numericus → metathesis possibilis → applicatio ad naturam, tempora, pondera et gradus virtutis.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[]},{"id":"CH_MHON_T_19:AF4","text":"Monas restituta → impotentia impulsus creati ultra quater → terminatio quaternaria vel denaria → subordinatio finalis ad potentiam divinam.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[]}],"original_name":"Argument Flow Map"},{"number":"10","name":"Map-Effects","items":[{"id":"CH_MHON_T_19:ME1","text":"Crux reclassificatur ex signo religioso vel ornamentali in instrumentum commensurationis elementaris, geometricae et numericae.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[]},{"id":"CH_MHON_T_19:ME2","text":"Oppositio aequalitatis et inaequalitatis reconfiguratur: inaequalitas est status naturalis, aequalitas est effectus artis ordinantis.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[]},{"id":"CH_MHON_T_19:ME3","text":"Quaternarius reclassificatur ex numero simpliciter enumerativo in matricem linearum, divisionum, permutationum et cooperationis causalis.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[]},{"id":"CH_MHON_T_19:ME4","text":"Denarius reclassificatur ex termino conventionis numeralis in effectum limitis quem constitutio Monadis et potestas creata imponunt.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[]},{"id":"CH_MHON_T_19:ME5","text":"Visibile et necessarium separantur: ornamenta et litterae variantur, sed proportiones mysticae et centrum solare structuram determinant.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[]}],"original_name":"Map-Effects"},{"number":"11","name":"Altitude Levels","items":[{"id":"CH_MHON_T_19:ALT1","text":"Surface — Textus describit constructionem figurae ex punctis, rectis, perpendicularibus, circulis, semicirculis et mensura fundamentali AB.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[]},{"id":"CH_MHON_T_19:ALT2","text":"Structural — Figura constituitur per relationes constantes inter inaequalitatem elementorum, aequalitatem artificialem, quaternarium linearum et operationes numerales.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[]},{"id":"CH_MHON_T_19:ALT3","text":"Metastrategic — Scientia hieroglyphica defenditur per distinctionem inter invariantes mysticas conservandas et accidentia ornamentalia mutabilia.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[]},{"id":"CH_MHON_T_19:ALT4","text":"Ontological/Temporal — Res creatae subiciuntur inaequalitati, mensurae et fini motus; veritas plenior disciplinae in tempore, sub nutu Dei, manifestanda differtur.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[]}],"original_name":"Altitude Levels"},{"number":"12","name":"Minimal Reconstruction Test","items":[{"id":"CH_MHON_T_19:MRT1","text":"Systema restitui potest ex nexu inter elementorum inaequalitatem, reductionem artificialem, mensuram AB, quaternarium numericum et limitem causalitatis creatae.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[]},{"id":"CH_MHON_T_19:MRT2","text":"Firmissimum vinculum textus est constructio geometrica: relationes AB, AC, AD, AE, DE et figurae derivatae explicite ordinantur.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[]},{"id":"CH_MHON_T_19:MRT3","text":"Transitus a proportione geometrica ad auctoritatem in ponderibus, temporibus et gradibus rerum asseritur, sed intra hanc theoriam non plene demonstratur.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[]},{"id":"CH_MHON_T_19:MRT4","text":"Tensionem continet quod crux partes aequales et inaequales simul dicitur constituere, dum aequalitas elementorum alibi ut effectus artis contra naturam inaequalem ponitur.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[]},{"id":"CH_MHON_T_19:MRT5","text":"Tensionem continet quod proportiones mysticae necessariae vocantur, sed rationes earum singulae consulto extra limites tractationis relinquuntur.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[]},{"id":"CH_MHON_T_19:MRT6","text":"Clausura de limite quaternario pendet ab identificatione Monadis restitutae cum unitate plena et ab axioma de impotentia ulterioris causae creatae; haec identificatio affirmatur potius quam ex constructione geometrica deducitur.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[]}],"original_name":"Minimal Reconstruction Test"}],"language_code":"la","language_name":"Latina","translations":[{"id":"CAREVAR_20261006T223013853530Z_7ea01490","language_code":"en","language_name":"English","title":"T H E O R E M XXIII.","source_sha256":"b27dfc95e481a91bc5d42578250c1d1fcad14556609b39bb6928015f4085a908","sha256":"4a4f15f280a9a16d12b591ca246be4d22af743028cb628f3e35e5e2022cf0903","rendition_type":"machine","review_status":"machine","model":"gpt-5.6-terra","created":"2026-10-06T22:30:13.855084+00:00","sections":[{"number":"1","name":"Argumental Spine","items":[{"id":"CH_MHON_T_19:AS1","text":"The text asks by what commensurations the hieroglyphic figure of the Monad, derived from the elemental cross, must necessarily be constituted.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[]},{"id":"CH_MHON_T_19:AS2","text":"The argumentation is constructive and numerologico-metaphysical: elements, linear proportions, circular figures, and numerical operations are reduced to a single canon.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[]},{"id":"CH_MHON_T_19:AS3","text":"The elemental cross is posited as the means by which the natural inequality of the elements is, through art, converted into ordered symmetry.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[]},{"id":"CH_MHON_T_19:AS4","text":"The geometric construction from the fundamental measure AB produces a quaternary of lines, whose mystical divisions ground the doctrine of numbers.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[]},{"id":"CH_MHON_T_19:AS5","text":"The quaternary and the denary are not mere termini of enumeration, but the limits of processes which the Monad, physically restored, undergoes within created orders.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[]},{"id":"CH_MHON_T_19:AS6","text":"The movement of the argument is: unequal elements → artificial reduction to symmetry → construction of the cross → numerical quaternary → canon of permutation and measure → cosmological limit and theological closure.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[]}],"original_name":"Argumental Spine"},{"number":"2","name":"Load-Bearing Axioms","items":[{"id":"CH_MHON_T_19:A1","text":"Whatever undergoes generation beneath the sphere of the Moon is either composed of the four elements or is an elemental essence.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[]},{"id":"CH_MHON_T_19:A2","text":"The elements are naturally present in no created thing in equal proportion or power.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[]},{"id":"CH_MHON_T_19:A3","text":"Art can, in certain things, reduce the inequality of the elements to equality.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[]},{"id":"CH_MHON_T_19:A4","text":"The elemental cross constitutes parts at once equal and unequal; this twofold division expresses the relation of one to many and of the same to the different.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[]},{"id":"CH_MHON_T_19:A5","text":"The length AB is the common measure from which the principal proportions of the cross are derived.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[]},{"id":"CH_MHON_T_19:A6","text":"The lines AC, AD, and AE, according to the triple and double ratios of AB itself, constitute the elemental cross, such that DE is fourfold AB.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[]},{"id":"CH_MHON_T_19:A7","text":"The mystical symmetries of the figure are necessary invariants, while ornaments, added letters, and superficial modifications are left to the artisan's discretion.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[]},{"id":"CH_MHON_T_19:A8","text":"The product of the continuous multiplication of the numbers from the first Monad to the last number described determines every possible transposition of that multitude.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[]},{"id":"CH_MHON_T_19:A9","text":"The quaternary simultaneously orders the permutative order, the Pythagorean sum, and all additions of parts.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[]},{"id":"CH_MHON_T_19:A10","text":"Numbers elicited from the symmetries of the Monad can prescribe the weight of the elements, measures of times, and degrees of power or virtue.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[]},{"id":"CH_MHON_T_19:A11","text":"The Monad, wholly and physically restored, is the most complete unity, upon which no created power can impose a principle of further motion beyond the fourfold supercelestial revolution.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[]},{"id":"CH_MHON_T_19:A12","text":"The quaternary or denary termination is founded on the limit of created power, not on an explanation handed down by the ancients.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[]}],"original_name":"Load-Bearing Axioms"},{"number":"3","name":"Apophatic Structure","items":[{"id":"CH_MHON_T_19:NX1","text":"Equality of the elements is not the natural condition of a created thing.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[]},{"id":"CH_MHON_T_19:NX2","text":"The ornament of the figure is not a mystical necessity nor a constituent part of the rationale of the symmetries.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[]},{"id":"CH_MHON_T_19:NX3","text":"Geometrical centres ought not to be visible, except for the solar centre marked by the letter I.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[]},{"id":"CH_MHON_T_19:NX4","text":"A complete demonstration of the individual causes of the symmetries is not given, because it would exceed the limits of the purpose.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[]},{"id":"CH_MHON_T_19:NX5","text":"The multiplication of numbers by the arithmetical power of the quaternary alone does not necessarily illuminate the obscurity of nature.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[]},{"id":"CH_MHON_T_19:NX6","text":"The reasoning of the ancients concerning the limit of the quaternary or denary is not held to be absolute or exact.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[]},{"id":"CH_MHON_T_19:NX7","text":"No created influential power, whether elemental, celestial, or supercelestial, can impel the restored Monad beyond four times.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[]}],"original_name":"Apophatic Structure"},{"number":"4","name":"Derivative Propositions","items":[{"id":"CH_MHON_T_19:D1","text":"Since the natural mixture of the elements is unequal and art can restore equality, the cross does not simply imitate the image of nature but corrects nature in a regulated form.","refs":["CH_MHON_T_19:A2","CH_MHON_T_19:A3","CH_MHON_T_19:A4"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"From","passages":[]},{"id":"CH_MHON_T_19:D2","text":"The measure AB functions as a modulus of conversion among the lines of the cross, diameters, semicircles, and the total figure.","refs":["CH_MHON_T_19:A5","CH_MHON_T_19:A6"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"From","passages":[]},{"id":"CH_MHON_T_19:D3","text":"The figure may vary in its visible surface, provided that its generative proportions are not impaired.","refs":["CH_MHON_T_19:A5","CH_MHON_T_19:A6","CH_MHON_T_19:A7"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"From","passages":[]},{"id":"CH_MHON_T_19:D4","text":"The quaternary is not merely the number of four lines, but a structure of division whose permutations, sums, and additions are reckoned by diverse operations.","refs":["CH_MHON_T_19:A4","CH_MHON_T_19:A8","CH_MHON_T_19:A9"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"From","passages":[]},{"id":"CH_MHON_T_19:D5","text":"The canon of transposition extends combinatorial reasoning from numbers to diverse things, the examination of nature, and the affairs of the Commonwealth.","refs":["CH_MHON_T_19:A8"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"From","passages":[]},{"id":"CH_MHON_T_19:D6","text":"The limit of the quaternary and denary is explained as a dynamic closure of the created order: created power, even if complete, does not produce indefinite progress.","refs":["CH_MHON_T_19:A11","CH_MHON_T_19:A12"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"From","passages":[]},{"id":"CH_MHON_T_19:D7","text":"With four agents philosophizing together, the effect referred to the Monad presents an example of the concord of number, knowledge, and power over creatures.","refs":["CH_MHON_T_19:A9","CH_MHON_T_19:A11"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"From","passages":[]}],"original_name":"Derivative Propositions"},{"number":"5","name":"Super-Derivatives","items":[{"id":"CH_MHON_T_19:SD1","text":"Geometry here becomes an apparatus of transposition: the same mensural ratio brings together, in one tractable field, the body of the sign, the composition of the elements, the combinatorics of numbers, and the cosmic order.","refs":["CH_MHON_T_19:A1","CH_MHON_T_19:A4","CH_MHON_T_19:A5","CH_MHON_T_19:A8","CH_MHON_T_19:A10"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"From","passages":[]},{"id":"CH_MHON_T_19:SD2","text":"Equality is not opposed to inequality as its absolute negation, but is an artificially produced form that contains and orders natural inequality.","refs":["CH_MHON_T_19:A2","CH_MHON_T_19:A3","CH_MHON_T_19:A4"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"From","passages":[]},{"id":"CH_MHON_T_19:SD3","text":"Number becomes a limiting principle: the quaternary and denary do not merely enumerate things, but define the scope of change, the order of powers, and the bounds of progress.","refs":["CH_MHON_T_19:A8","CH_MHON_T_19:A10","CH_MHON_T_19:A11","CH_MHON_T_19:A12"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"From","passages":[]},{"id":"CH_MHON_T_19:SD4","text":"The hieroglyphic discipline is required as transmissible knowledge, whose permanence depends on the preservation of commensurations rather than on ornamental fidelity.","refs":["CH_MHON_T_19:A6","CH_MHON_T_19:A7"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"From","passages":[]}],"original_name":"Super-Derivatives"},{"number":"6","name":"Deep Apophatics","items":[{"id":"CH_MHON_T_19:DNX1","text":"A valid figure cannot be a free composition of signs: its truth tacitly depends upon proportions that, before every ornament, determine what the figure can be.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[]},{"id":"CH_MHON_T_19:DNX2","text":"Nature does not suffice as an absolute measure, because in the very creation of things the elements are presupposed to be unequally distributed and art reducing them to equality is required.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[]},{"id":"CH_MHON_T_19:DNX3","text":"The plurality of numbers is not in itself an increase of knowledge; a number is legitimate only if it illuminates the obscurity of nature.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[]},{"id":"CH_MHON_T_19:DNX4","text":"Created motion or progress is not indefinite, because the restored Monad admits no created cause for further impulsion.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[]},{"id":"CH_MHON_T_19:DNX5","text":"The cosmological reasoning of the ancients is repudiated insofar as it hands down a numerical limit without sufficient foundation.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[]}],"original_name":"Deep Apophatics"},{"number":"7","name":"Super-Apophatics","items":[{"id":"CH_MHON_T_19:SNX1","text":"The system is ordered around the impossibility of absolute created causality: created power endowed most fully does not transcend its limit.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[]},{"id":"CH_MHON_T_19:SNX2","text":"The complete truth of the symmetries is not presented as fully exhausted; the future discipline remains broader than the booklet and is to be manifested through the course of time under divine direction.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[]},{"id":"CH_MHON_T_19:SNX3","text":"The ultimate rationale of the figure does not reside in visibility: the centres are hidden, ornaments are contingent, and necessity is located in non-apparent relations.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[]}],"original_name":"Super-Apophatics"},{"number":"8","name":"Formal Generative Schema","items":[{"id":"CH_MHON_T_19:FGS1","text":"Sublunary generation from the elements → natural inequality of the elements → artificial reduction to equality → cross as the body of equal and unequal proportions.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[]},{"id":"CH_MHON_T_19:FGS2","text":"Modulus AB → triple and double proportions → quaternary of lines → circles, semicircles, and surfaces of the cross → constructed hieroglyphic figure.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[]},{"id":"CH_MHON_T_19:FGS3","text":"Geometrical quaternary → mystical divisions → permutations and sums → canon of transposition → measure of elements, times, and degrees.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[]},{"id":"CH_MHON_T_19:FGS4","text":"Monad physically restored → most complete unity → diminution of created causality → quaternary limit of motion → end of the quaternary or denary.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[]},{"id":"CH_MHON_T_19:FGS5","text":"Necessary proportions preserved + ornaments contingently added → transmission of the figure without corruption of the discipline.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[]}],"original_name":"Formal Generative Schema"},{"number":"9","name":"Argument Flow Map","items":[{"id":"CH_MHON_T_19:AF1","text":"Unequal elements → equalizing art → elemental cross → proportions from AB.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[]},{"id":"CH_MHON_T_19:AF2","text":"Proportions of the cross → quaternary of lines → mystical division → Pythagorean quaternary.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[]},{"id":"CH_MHON_T_19:AF3","text":"Numerical quaternary → possible transposition → application to nature, times, weights, and degrees of virtue.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[]},{"id":"CH_MHON_T_19:AF4","text":"Restored Monad → impotence of created impulsion beyond four times → quaternary or denary termination → final subordination to divine power.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[]}],"original_name":"Argument Flow Map"},{"number":"10","name":"Map-Effects","items":[{"id":"CH_MHON_T_19:ME1","text":"The cross is reclassified from a religious or ornamental sign into an instrument of elemental, geometrical, and numerical commensuration.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[]},{"id":"CH_MHON_T_19:ME2","text":"The opposition of equality and inequality is reconfigured: inequality is the natural state; equality is the effect of ordering art.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[]},{"id":"CH_MHON_T_19:ME3","text":"The quaternary is reclassified from a simply enumerative number into a matrix of lines, divisions, permutations, and causal cooperation.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[]},{"id":"CH_MHON_T_19:ME4","text":"The denary is reclassified from a term of numerical convention into the effect of a limit imposed by the constitution of the Monad and created power.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[]},{"id":"CH_MHON_T_19:ME5","text":"The visible and the necessary are separated: ornaments and letters vary, but mystical proportions and the solar centre determine the structure.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[]}],"original_name":"Map-Effects"},{"number":"11","name":"Altitude Levels","items":[{"id":"CH_MHON_T_19:ALT1","text":"Surface — The text describes the construction of the figure from points, straight lines, perpendiculars, circles, semicircles, and the fundamental measure AB.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[]},{"id":"CH_MHON_T_19:ALT2","text":"Structural — The figure is constituted through constant relations among the inequality of the elements, artificial equality, the quaternary of lines, and numerical operations.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[]},{"id":"CH_MHON_T_19:ALT3","text":"Metastrategic — Hieroglyphic knowledge is defended through the distinction between mystical invariants to be preserved and mutable ornamental accidents.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[]},{"id":"CH_MHON_T_19:ALT4","text":"Ontological/Temporal — Created things are subject to inequality, measure, and the limit of motion; the fuller truth of the discipline is deferred to be manifested in time, under the direction of God.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[]}],"original_name":"Altitude Levels"},{"number":"12","name":"Minimal Reconstruction Test","items":[{"id":"CH_MHON_T_19:MRT1","text":"The system can be reconstructed from the nexus among the inequality of the elements, artificial reduction, measure AB, the numerical quaternary, and the limit of created causality.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[]},{"id":"CH_MHON_T_19:MRT2","text":"The strongest bond of the text is the geometrical construction: the relations AB, AC, AD, AE, DE, and the derived figures are explicitly ordered.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[]},{"id":"CH_MHON_T_19:MRT3","text":"The transition from geometrical proportion to authority in weights, times, and degrees of things is asserted, but is not fully demonstrated within this theory.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[]},{"id":"CH_MHON_T_19:MRT4","text":"There is a tension in that the cross is said to constitute equal and unequal parts simultaneously, while the equality of the elements is elsewhere posited as the effect of art against unequal nature.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[]},{"id":"CH_MHON_T_19:MRT5","text":"There is a tension in that mystical proportions are called necessary, but their individual rationales are deliberately left outside the limits of the treatment.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[]},{"id":"CH_MHON_T_19:MRT6","text":"The closure concerning the quaternary limit depends on the identification of the restored Monad with complete unity and on the axiom of the impotence of any further created cause; this identification is affirmed rather than deduced from the geometrical construction.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[]}],"original_name":"Minimal Reconstruction Test"}],"revision":"CH_MHON_T_19@R001","source_variant_id":"canonical:CH_MHON_T_19:v1","source_language_code":"la"}],"identity":{"layer":"CH","kicker":"Chapter 24","title":"T H E O R. XXIII.","context":"Chapter 24","original_title":"T H E O R. Xxiii.","original_context":"Chapter 24"},"original_title":"T H E O R. Xxiii.","title_language_code":"en"},{"id":"CH_MHON_T_20","layer":"CH","title":"Theor. XXIIII.","order":25,"parents":["RAW_MHON_T_20"],"revision":"CH_MHON_T_20@R001","sha256":"f3f668a9a170c91c7297d137440fae3afba822ad1520f9d9879f17b71d41e2e4","sections":[{"number":"1","name":"Argumental Spine","items":[{"id":"CH_MHON_T_20:AS1","text":"Exordium a Puncto, Recta et Circulo procedit.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[]},{"id":"CH_MHON_T_20:AS2","text":"Punctum monadicum effusionem linearem elementorum generat, quae extrema sua in circulum concludit.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[]},{"id":"CH_MHON_T_20:AS3","text":"Circulus hic cum circuitu aequinoctiali et numero 24 analogice coniungitur.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[]},{"id":"CH_MHON_T_20:AS4","text":"Quaternarius per numerum definitum 24 ad metamorphosin et conclusionem totius theoriae perducitur.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[]},{"id":"CH_MHON_T_20:AS5","text":"Circulus numericus et apocalypticus ultimam ordinationem creaturarum ad voluntatem creatoris refert.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[]},{"id":"CH_MHON_T_20:AS6","text":"Figura finalis et eius interpretatio simul mysterium divinum consignandum et visum vulgarem superans constituunt.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[]}],"original_name":"Argumental Spine"},{"number":"2","name":"Load-Bearing Axioms","items":[{"id":"CH_MHON_T_20:A1","text":"Omnis constructio demonstrata a puncto, recta et circulo incipit.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[]},{"id":"CH_MHON_T_20:A2","text":"Punctum monadicum est principium effusionis linearis elementorum.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[]},{"id":"CH_MHON_T_20:A3","text":"Effusio linearis extrema sua per circumductionem in circulum reducit.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[]},{"id":"CH_MHON_T_20:A4","text":"Circuitus 24 horarum praebet exemplar numericum circulationis completae.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[]},{"id":"CH_MHON_T_20:A5","text":"Numerus 24 quaternarium definit et eius metamorphosin ultimam determinat.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[]},{"id":"CH_MHON_T_20:A6","text":"Quaternarius non tantum numerus est, sed forma transmutationis totius ordinis.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[]},{"id":"CH_MHON_T_20:A7","text":"In visione apocalyptica quattuor animalia et viginti quattuor seniores ordinem circularem divinum repraesentant.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[]},{"id":"CH_MHON_T_20:A8","text":"Omnia creata sunt propter voluntatem Dei et ad eius gloriam, honorem et virtutem referuntur.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[]},{"id":"CH_MHON_T_20:A9","text":"Deus homini voluntatem et habilitatem ad hoc mysterium consignandum tribuit.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[]},{"id":"CH_MHON_T_20:A10","text":"Mysterium, ut litteris et figuris consignetur, simul intellectum exposcit et sensum vulgarem excedit.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[]}],"original_name":"Load-Bearing Axioms"},{"number":"3","name":"Apophatic Structure","items":[{"id":"CH_MHON_T_20:NX1","text":"Circulus finalis non est clausura statica, sed circuitus per quem effusio monadica ordinatur.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[]},{"id":"CH_MHON_T_20:NX2","text":"Numerus 24 non ad solam quantitatem arithmeticam reducitur.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[]},{"id":"CH_MHON_T_20:NX3","text":"Mysterium divinum non est simpliciter perspicuum oculis vulgaribus.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[]},{"id":"CH_MHON_T_20:NX4","text":"Figura et doctrina non separantur in duas res independentes, sed una mysterii consignatio constituuntur.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[]},{"id":"CH_MHON_T_20:NX5","text":"Creaturae non sunt sui ipsius finis, quia textus eas voluntati creatoris subicit.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[]}],"original_name":"Apophatic Structure"},{"number":"4","name":"Derivative Propositions","items":[{"id":"CH_MHON_T_20:D1","text":"Si punctum est principium et circulus finis, tunc genesis et reditus unius processus sunt.","refs":["CH_MHON_T_20:A2","CH_MHON_T_20:A3"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"From","passages":[]},{"id":"CH_MHON_T_20:D2","text":"Si circuitus aequinoctialis 24 horis completur, tunc numerus 24 temporalem et cosmicam completionem simul designat.","refs":["CH_MHON_T_20:A4","CH_MHON_T_20:A5"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"From","passages":[]},{"id":"CH_MHON_T_20:D3","text":"Si quaternarius in 24 metamorphoseos formam accipit, tunc series numerica ipsa fit machina transformationis.","refs":["CH_MHON_T_20:A5","CH_MHON_T_20:A6"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"From","passages":[]},{"id":"CH_MHON_T_20:D4","text":"Si 24 seniores circa thronum adorant et quattuor animalia sine requie laudant, tunc numerus circularis ordinem liturgicum et cosmicum continet.","refs":["CH_MHON_T_20:A7","CH_MHON_T_20:A8"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"From","passages":[]},{"id":"CH_MHON_T_20:D5","text":"Si homo voluntatem et habilitatem ad mysterium scribendum accipit, tunc scriptura ipsa fit actus tradendi ordinem divinum, non mera descriptio eius.","refs":["CH_MHON_T_20:A9","CH_MHON_T_20:A10"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"From","passages":[]},{"id":"CH_MHON_T_20:D6","text":"Si sensus vulgaris hic «CALIGABIT» et «DIFFIDET», tunc veritas figurae ad intellectum specialiter ordinatum transfertur.","refs":["CH_MHON_T_20:A10"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"From","passages":[]}],"original_name":"Derivative Propositions"},{"number":"5","name":"Super-Derivatives","items":[{"id":"CH_MHON_T_20:SD1","text":"Ordo rerum a monade ad lineam, a linea ad circulum et a circulo ad 24 componitur tamquam processus emanationis, completionis et reditus.","refs":["CH_MHON_T_20:A2","CH_MHON_T_20:A3","CH_MHON_T_20:A4","CH_MHON_T_20:A5"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"From","passages":[]},{"id":"CH_MHON_T_20:SD2","text":"Numerus 24 fit nexus inter geometriam, tempus, apocalypsim et liturgicam adorationem.","refs":["CH_MHON_T_20:A4","CH_MHON_T_20:A5","CH_MHON_T_20:A7","CH_MHON_T_20:A8"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"From","passages":[]},{"id":"CH_MHON_T_20:SD3","text":"Auctoritas figurae non ex sola visibilitate, sed ex concordantia inter formam geometricam, numerum et testimonium apocalypticum derivatur.","refs":["CH_MHON_T_20:A1","CH_MHON_T_20:A5","CH_MHON_T_20:A7"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"From","passages":[]},{"id":"CH_MHON_T_20:SD4","text":"Finis theoriae est clausura cosmologica et religiosa simul: systema completur cum omnia ad creatorem et voluntatem eius referuntur.","refs":["CH_MHON_T_20:A6","CH_MHON_T_20:A8"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"From","passages":[]}],"original_name":"Super-Derivatives"},{"number":"6","name":"Deep Apophatics","items":[{"id":"CH_MHON_T_20:DNX1","text":"Systema falsum esse oporteret si initium, fluxus et finis essent ordines omnino disiuncti.","refs":["CH_MHON_T_20:A1","CH_MHON_T_20:A2","CH_MHON_T_20:A3"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"From","passages":[]},{"id":"CH_MHON_T_20:DNX2","text":"Systema falsum esse oporteret si numerus 24 tantum mensura externa esset nec formam internam transformationis contineret.","refs":["CH_MHON_T_20:A4","CH_MHON_T_20:A5","CH_MHON_T_20:A6"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"From","passages":[]},{"id":"CH_MHON_T_20:DNX3","text":"Systema falsum esse oporteret si mysterium ad perceptionem communem vel ad descriptionem literalem sine interpretatione redigi posset.","refs":["CH_MHON_T_20:A9","CH_MHON_T_20:A10"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"From","passages":[]},{"id":"CH_MHON_T_20:DNX4","text":"Systema falsum esse oporteret si creaturae autonomum finem haberent extra voluntatem et creationem divinam.","refs":["CH_MHON_T_20:A7","CH_MHON_T_20:A8"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"From","passages":[]}],"original_name":"Deep Apophatics"},{"number":"7","name":"Super-Apophatics","items":[{"id":"CH_MHON_T_20:SNX1","text":"Textus excludit mundum in quo principium et reditus non ad eandem circularem unitatem referuntur.","refs":["CH_MHON_T_20:DNX1"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"From","passages":[]},{"id":"CH_MHON_T_20:SNX2","text":"Textus excludit cognitionem quae signum separare possit a mysterio, formam a numero vel numerum a tempore.","refs":["CH_MHON_T_20:DNX2","CH_MHON_T_20:DNX3"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"From","passages":[]},{"id":"CH_MHON_T_20:SNX3","text":"Textus praecludit finem rerum extra ordinem creationis, adorationis et divinae voluntatis.","refs":["CH_MHON_T_20:DNX4"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"From","passages":[]}],"original_name":"Super-Apophatics"},{"number":"8","name":"Formal Generative Schema","items":[{"id":"CH_MHON_T_20:FGS1","text":"Punctum monadicum → effusio linearis → extrema elementorum → circumductio circularis.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[]},{"id":"CH_MHON_T_20:FGS2","text":"Circulus geometricus → analogia aequinoctialis → circuitus 24 horarum → definitio numerica completionis.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[]},{"id":"CH_MHON_T_20:FGS3","text":"Quaternarius → 24 → metamorphosis → conclusio vigesimae quartae theoriae.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[]},{"id":"CH_MHON_T_20:FGS4","text":"Circulus numericus → animalia quattuor et seniores 24 → adoratio circularis → relatio ad voluntatem creatoris.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[]},{"id":"CH_MHON_T_20:FGS5","text":"Mysterium divinum → facultas humana consignandi → figura et littera → intellectus supra visum vulgarem.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[]}],"original_name":"Formal Generative Schema"},{"number":"9","name":"Argument Flow Map","items":[{"id":"CH_MHON_T_20:AF1","text":"Punctum principiale → effluxio linearis → circulus clausivus → analogia temporis 24 → quaternarii metamorphosis → apocalyptica totalizatio → theoriae terminatio → mysterii consignatio.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[]},{"id":"CH_MHON_T_20:AF2","text":"Geometria initialis → numerus definitorius → figura apocalyptica → cognitio non-vulgaris → ordinatio omnium ad divinam voluntatem.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[]}],"original_name":"Argument Flow Map"},{"number":"10","name":"Map-Effects","items":[{"id":"CH_MHON_T_20:ME1","text":"Geometria elementaris fit tabula ontologica qua origo, processus et finis unius rei ordinantur.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[]},{"id":"CH_MHON_T_20:ME2","text":"Numerus 24 fit classificator communis temporis, structurae apocalypticae et conclusionis textualis.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[]},{"id":"CH_MHON_T_20:ME3","text":"Oppositio inter formam geometricam et symbolum religiosum corruit, quia utraque eundem ordinem circularem exprimit.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[]},{"id":"CH_MHON_T_20:ME4","text":"Visio vulgaris denuo classificatur ut cognitio insufficiens, dum intellectus figuram ut signum mysterii accipit.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[]},{"id":"CH_MHON_T_20:ME5","text":"Finis operis denuo describitur non ut simplex cessatio, sed ut reditus ad principium sub auctoritate divina.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[]}],"original_name":"Map-Effects"},{"number":"11","name":"Altitude Levels","items":[{"id":"CH_MHON_T_20:ALT1","text":"Surface — Textus explicite coniungit punctum, rectam, circulum, numerum 24 et clausuram vigesimae quartae theoriae.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[]},{"id":"CH_MHON_T_20:ALT2","text":"Structural — Ordo argumenti constituitur per relationem inter monadem, effusionem, circumductionem et completionem numericam.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[]},{"id":"CH_MHON_T_20:ALT3","text":"Metastrategic — Textus lectoris intuitum vulgarem de figura avertit et eum ad lectionem geometricam, numericam et apocalypticam simul impellit.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[]},{"id":"CH_MHON_T_20:ALT4","text":"Ontological/Temporal — Res intelliguntur ut a principio emanantes, per tempus et numerum ordinatae, atque in circularem ad Deum relationem redeuntes.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[]}],"original_name":"Altitude Levels"},{"number":"12","name":"Minimal Reconstruction Test","items":[{"id":"CH_MHON_T_20:MRT1","text":"Axiomata sufficiunt ad motum a geometria initiali ad clausuram religiosam restituendum.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[]},{"id":"CH_MHON_T_20:MRT2","text":"Nexus inter quaternarium et plenam «metathesim» 24 numeri affirmatur, sed mechanismus transformationis singillatim non explicatur.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[]},{"id":"CH_MHON_T_20:MRT3","text":"Concordantia inter circulum geometricum, circuitum temporalem et ordinem apocalypticum est firmissimum systematis vinculum.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[]},{"id":"CH_MHON_T_20:MRT4","text":"Interpretatio oculi vulgaris et intellectus veritatem iudicantis innititur distinctioni inter visibilitatem communem et cognitionem symbolicam.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[]},{"id":"CH_MHON_T_20:MRT5","text":"Tensionalis commissura manet inter analogiam geometricam et auctoritatem scripturalem: textus eas coniungit, sed formalem regulam translationis non praebet.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[]},{"id":"CH_MHON_T_20:MRT6","text":"Restitutio servat verum motum argumentationis: a puncto et effluxione ad circulum, a circulo ad 24, a 24 ad metamorphosin quaternariam et ad ordinationem divinam.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[]}],"original_name":"Minimal Reconstruction Test"}],"language_code":"la","language_name":"Latina","translations":[{"id":"CAREVAR_20261006T223030759880Z_b5411e45","language_code":"en","language_name":"English","title":"THEORY XXIIII.","source_sha256":"f3f668a9a170c91c7297d137440fae3afba822ad1520f9d9879f17b71d41e2e4","sha256":"6d05a90cb2cd64ff4615eace55cb6315385241dd59be062ab741c118ecdd5acb","rendition_type":"machine","review_status":"machine","model":"gpt-5.6-terra","created":"2026-10-06T22:30:30.761711+00:00","sections":[{"number":"1","name":"Argumental Spine","items":[{"id":"CH_MHON_T_20:AS1","text":"The exposition proceeds from the Point, Straight Line, and Circle.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[]},{"id":"CH_MHON_T_20:AS2","text":"The monadic point generates a linear effusion of the elements, which encloses its extremities in a circle.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[]},{"id":"CH_MHON_T_20:AS3","text":"This circle is analogically joined with the equinoctial circuit and the number 24.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[]},{"id":"CH_MHON_T_20:AS4","text":"The quaternary, through the determinate number 24, is brought to the metamorphosis and conclusion of the whole theory.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[]},{"id":"CH_MHON_T_20:AS5","text":"The numerical and apocalyptic circle refers the final ordering of creatures to the will of the creator.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[]},{"id":"CH_MHON_T_20:AS6","text":"The final figure and its interpretation together constitute the recording of the divine mystery and the surpassing of common sight.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[]}],"original_name":"Argumental Spine"},{"number":"2","name":"Load-Bearing Axioms","items":[{"id":"CH_MHON_T_20:A1","text":"Every demonstrated construction begins from the point, straight line, and circle.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[]},{"id":"CH_MHON_T_20:A2","text":"The monadic point is the principle of the linear effusion of the elements.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[]},{"id":"CH_MHON_T_20:A3","text":"The linear effusion returns its extremities into a circle through circumduction.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[]},{"id":"CH_MHON_T_20:A4","text":"The circuit of 24 hours provides the numerical model of complete circulation.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[]},{"id":"CH_MHON_T_20:A5","text":"The number 24 defines the quaternary and determines its ultimate metamorphosis.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[]},{"id":"CH_MHON_T_20:A6","text":"The quaternary is not merely a number, but the form of the transmutation of the entire order.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[]},{"id":"CH_MHON_T_20:A7","text":"In the apocalyptic vision, the four living creatures and the twenty-four elders represent the divine circular order.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[]},{"id":"CH_MHON_T_20:A8","text":"All things were created on account of the will of God and are referred to His glory, honour, and power.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[]},{"id":"CH_MHON_T_20:A9","text":"God grants man the will and ability to record this mystery.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[]},{"id":"CH_MHON_T_20:A10","text":"The mystery, in order to be recorded in letters and figures, simultaneously requires understanding and exceeds common sense.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[]}],"original_name":"Load-Bearing Axioms"},{"number":"3","name":"Apophatic Structure","items":[{"id":"CH_MHON_T_20:NX1","text":"The final circle is not a static closure, but a circuit through which monadic effusion is ordered.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[]},{"id":"CH_MHON_T_20:NX2","text":"The number 24 is not reduced to mere arithmetic quantity.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[]},{"id":"CH_MHON_T_20:NX3","text":"The divine mystery is not simply evident to common eyes.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[]},{"id":"CH_MHON_T_20:NX4","text":"Figure and doctrine are not separated into two independent things, but constitute one recording of the mystery.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[]},{"id":"CH_MHON_T_20:NX5","text":"Creatures are not their own end, because the text subjects them to the will of the creator.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[]}],"original_name":"Apophatic Structure"},{"number":"4","name":"Derivative Propositions","items":[{"id":"CH_MHON_T_20:D1","text":"If the point is the principle and the circle the end, then genesis and return are one process.","refs":["CH_MHON_T_20:A2","CH_MHON_T_20:A3"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"From","passages":[]},{"id":"CH_MHON_T_20:D2","text":"If the equinoctial circuit is completed in 24 hours, then the number 24 simultaneously signifies temporal and cosmic completion.","refs":["CH_MHON_T_20:A4","CH_MHON_T_20:A5"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"From","passages":[]},{"id":"CH_MHON_T_20:D3","text":"If the quaternary takes on the form of metamorphosis in 24, then the numerical series itself becomes an engine of transformation.","refs":["CH_MHON_T_20:A5","CH_MHON_T_20:A6"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"From","passages":[]},{"id":"CH_MHON_T_20:D4","text":"If the 24 elders worship around the throne and the four living creatures praise without rest, then the circular number contains liturgical and cosmic order.","refs":["CH_MHON_T_20:A7","CH_MHON_T_20:A8"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"From","passages":[]},{"id":"CH_MHON_T_20:D5","text":"If man receives the will and ability to write the mystery, then writing itself becomes an act of transmitting divine order, not a mere description of it.","refs":["CH_MHON_T_20:A9","CH_MHON_T_20:A10"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"From","passages":[]},{"id":"CH_MHON_T_20:D6","text":"If common sense here «CALIGABIT» and «DIFFIDET», then the truth of the figure is transferred to an understanding specially ordered.","refs":["CH_MHON_T_20:A10"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"From","passages":[]}],"original_name":"Derivative Propositions"},{"number":"5","name":"Super-Derivatives","items":[{"id":"CH_MHON_T_20:SD1","text":"The order of things is composed from monad to line, from line to circle, and from circle to 24, as a process of emanation, completion, and return.","refs":["CH_MHON_T_20:A2","CH_MHON_T_20:A3","CH_MHON_T_20:A4","CH_MHON_T_20:A5"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"From","passages":[]},{"id":"CH_MHON_T_20:SD2","text":"The number 24 becomes the nexus between geometry, time, apocalypse, and liturgical worship.","refs":["CH_MHON_T_20:A4","CH_MHON_T_20:A5","CH_MHON_T_20:A7","CH_MHON_T_20:A8"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"From","passages":[]},{"id":"CH_MHON_T_20:SD3","text":"The authority of the figure derives not from visibility alone, but from the concordance between geometric form, number, and apocalyptic testimony.","refs":["CH_MHON_T_20:A1","CH_MHON_T_20:A5","CH_MHON_T_20:A7"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"From","passages":[]},{"id":"CH_MHON_T_20:SD4","text":"The end of the theory is simultaneously cosmological and religious closure: the system is completed when all things are referred to the creator and His will.","refs":["CH_MHON_T_20:A6","CH_MHON_T_20:A8"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"From","passages":[]}],"original_name":"Super-Derivatives"},{"number":"6","name":"Deep Apophatics","items":[{"id":"CH_MHON_T_20:DNX1","text":"The system would have to be false if beginning, flux, and end were wholly disjoined orders.","refs":["CH_MHON_T_20:A1","CH_MHON_T_20:A2","CH_MHON_T_20:A3"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"From","passages":[]},{"id":"CH_MHON_T_20:DNX2","text":"The system would have to be false if the number 24 were only an external measure and did not contain the internal form of transformation.","refs":["CH_MHON_T_20:A4","CH_MHON_T_20:A5","CH_MHON_T_20:A6"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"From","passages":[]},{"id":"CH_MHON_T_20:DNX3","text":"The system would have to be false if the mystery could be reduced to common perception or to literal description without interpretation.","refs":["CH_MHON_T_20:A9","CH_MHON_T_20:A10"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"From","passages":[]},{"id":"CH_MHON_T_20:DNX4","text":"The system would have to be false if creatures had an autonomous end outside divine will and creation.","refs":["CH_MHON_T_20:A7","CH_MHON_T_20:A8"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"From","passages":[]}],"original_name":"Deep Apophatics"},{"number":"7","name":"Super-Apophatics","items":[{"id":"CH_MHON_T_20:SNX1","text":"The text excludes a world in which principle and return are not referred to the same circular unity.","refs":["CH_MHON_T_20:DNX1"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"From","passages":[]},{"id":"CH_MHON_T_20:SNX2","text":"The text excludes a cognition that can separate sign from mystery, form from number, or number from time.","refs":["CH_MHON_T_20:DNX2","CH_MHON_T_20:DNX3"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"From","passages":[]},{"id":"CH_MHON_T_20:SNX3","text":"The text precludes an end of things outside the order of creation, worship, and divine will.","refs":["CH_MHON_T_20:DNX4"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"From","passages":[]}],"original_name":"Super-Apophatics"},{"number":"8","name":"Formal Generative Schema","items":[{"id":"CH_MHON_T_20:FGS1","text":"Monadic point → linear effusion → extremities of the elements → circular circumduction.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[]},{"id":"CH_MHON_T_20:FGS2","text":"Geometric circle → equinoctial analogy → circuit of 24 hours → numerical definition of completion.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[]},{"id":"CH_MHON_T_20:FGS3","text":"Quaternary → 24 → metamorphosis → conclusion of the twenty-fourth theory.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[]},{"id":"CH_MHON_T_20:FGS4","text":"Numerical circle → four living creatures and 24 elders → circular worship → relation to the will of the creator.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[]},{"id":"CH_MHON_T_20:FGS5","text":"Divine mystery → human faculty of recording → figure and letter → understanding above common sight.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[]}],"original_name":"Formal Generative Schema"},{"number":"9","name":"Argument Flow Map","items":[{"id":"CH_MHON_T_20:AF1","text":"Principial point → linear efflux → closing circle → analogy of time 24 → metamorphosis of the quaternary → apocalyptic totalization → termination of the theory → recording of the mystery.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[]},{"id":"CH_MHON_T_20:AF2","text":"Initial geometry → defining number → apocalyptic figure → non-common cognition → ordering of all things to divine will.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[]}],"original_name":"Argument Flow Map"},{"number":"10","name":"Map-Effects","items":[{"id":"CH_MHON_T_20:ME1","text":"Elementary geometry becomes an ontological map by which the origin, process, and end of one thing are ordered.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[]},{"id":"CH_MHON_T_20:ME2","text":"The number 24 becomes a common classifier of time, apocalyptic structure, and textual conclusion.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[]},{"id":"CH_MHON_T_20:ME3","text":"The opposition between geometric form and religious symbol collapses, because both express the same circular order.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[]},{"id":"CH_MHON_T_20:ME4","text":"Common vision is again classified as insufficient cognition, while understanding receives the figure as a sign of mystery.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[]},{"id":"CH_MHON_T_20:ME5","text":"The end of the work is again described not as simple cessation, but as return to the principle under divine authority.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[]}],"original_name":"Map-Effects"},{"number":"11","name":"Altitude Levels","items":[{"id":"CH_MHON_T_20:ALT1","text":"Surface — The text explicitly joins the point, straight line, circle, number 24, and the closure of the twenty-fourth theory.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[]},{"id":"CH_MHON_T_20:ALT2","text":"Structural — The order of the argument is constituted through the relation between monad, effusion, circumduction, and numerical completion.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[]},{"id":"CH_MHON_T_20:ALT3","text":"Metastrategic — The text turns the reader's common intuition away from the figure and impels him toward a simultaneous geometric, numerical, and apocalyptic reading.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[]},{"id":"CH_MHON_T_20:ALT4","text":"Ontological/Temporal — Things are understood as emanating from the principle, ordered through time and number, and returning into a circular relation to God.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[]}],"original_name":"Altitude Levels"},{"number":"12","name":"Minimal Reconstruction Test","items":[{"id":"CH_MHON_T_20:MRT1","text":"The axioms suffice to reconstruct the movement from initial geometry to religious closure.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[]},{"id":"CH_MHON_T_20:MRT2","text":"The nexus between the quaternary and the full «metathesim» of the number 24 is affirmed, but the mechanism of transformation is not explained in detail.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[]},{"id":"CH_MHON_T_20:MRT3","text":"The concordance between the geometric circle, temporal circuit, and apocalyptic order is the firmest bond of the system.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[]},{"id":"CH_MHON_T_20:MRT4","text":"The interpretation of the common eye and of understanding judging truth rests upon the distinction between common visibility and symbolic cognition.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[]},{"id":"CH_MHON_T_20:MRT5","text":"A tension-bearing seam remains between geometric analogy and scriptural authority: the text joins them, but does not provide a formal rule of translation.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[]},{"id":"CH_MHON_T_20:MRT6","text":"The reconstruction preserves the true movement of the argumentation: from point and efflux to circle, from circle to 24, from 24 to quaternary metamorphosis and to divine ordering.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[]}],"original_name":"Minimal Reconstruction Test"}],"revision":"CH_MHON_T_20@R001","source_variant_id":"canonical:CH_MHON_T_20:v1","source_language_code":"la"}],"identity":{"layer":"CH","kicker":"Chapter 25","title":"Theor. XXIIII.","context":"Chapter 25","original_title":"Theor. Xxiiii.","original_context":"Chapter 25"},"original_title":"Theor. Xxiiii.","title_language_code":"en"},{"id":"CL_MHON_01","layer":"CL","title":"Chapters 01–03","order":1,"parents":["CH_MHON_FROM","CH_MHON_THEI","CH_MHON_TH_2"],"revision":"CL_MHON_01@R001","sha256":"c1fbcaa2d4795bb1a89e6934491ab2e257f78e5b4ae45ad1083ea19827702dea","sections":[{"number":"1","name":"Book-Scale Argumental Spine","items":[{"id":"CL_MHON_01:BAS-01","text":"Systema quaerit quomodo character formaliter minimus simul compendium sapientiae, principium constructionis rerum et obiectum transmissionis custoditae esse possit.","refs":["CH_MHON_FROM:AS1","CH_MHON_TH_2:AS1"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[]},{"id":"CL_MHON_01:BAS-02","text":"Thesis globalis creationem ut scripturam legibilem constituit, cuius formae geometricae non solum res significant, sed eas ad manifestationem producunt atque in Monade principium habent.","refs":["CH_MHON_FROM:A2","CH_MHON_THEI:A6","CH_MHON_TH_2:AS3"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[]},{"id":"CL_MHON_01:BAS-03","text":"Argumentatio apologetica et reconstructiva fit regressus ontologicus: Monas primum tamquam character totalis proponitur, linea et circulus tamquam formae primae explicantur, deinde utriusque condicio in Punctum et Monadem retrahitur.","refs":["CH_MHON_FROM:AS3","CH_MHON_THEI:AS5","CH_MHON_TH_2:AS5"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[]},{"id":"CL_MHON_01:BAS-04","text":"Motus est recursivus et spiralis, quia signa dispersa in formam simplicem comprimuntur, forma per omnes rerum classes dilatatur, ac dilatatio ad terminum communem Puncti iterum contrahitur.","refs":["CH_MHON_FROM:A6","CH_MHON_THEI:SD4","CH_MHON_TH_2:D4"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[]},{"id":"CL_MHON_01:BAS-05","text":"Probatio coniungit necessitatem constructionis geometricae, interpretationem ontologicam et fecunditatem analogicam, ita ut rigor formae vim transdisciplinarem regat.","refs":["CH_MHON_FROM:A7","CH_MHON_TH_2:AS2","CH_MHON_FROM:A8"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[]},{"id":"CL_MHON_01:BAS-06","text":"Exactitudo publicationis non est appendix externa, quia forma producens et repraesentans doctrinam amittit si Punctum, linea, centrum vel relatio materialiter corrumpitur.","refs":["CH_MHON_FROM:A17","CH_MHON_THEI:D4","CH_MHON_TH_2:A7"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[]},{"id":"CL_MHON_01:BAS-07","text":"Clausura ad invariantiam centri tendit: nulla extensio peripheriae ministerium Puncti tollit, quamvis identitas inter causam geometricam et principium ontologicum nondum demonstrative claudatur.","refs":["CH_MHON_TH_2:A8","CH_MHON_TH_2:MRT2"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[]},{"id":"CL_MHON_01:BAS-08","text":"Universalitas signorum clausuram apertam producit, quia omnis creatura intra campum legibilem cadit, sed recta interpretatio et transmissio ad lectores indiscriminatos non extenduntur.","refs":["CH_MHON_FROM:SD4","CH_MHON_FROM:NX7"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[]}],"original_name":"Book-Scale Argumental Spine"},{"number":"2","name":"Global Load-Bearing Axioms","items":[{"id":"CL_MHON_01:GA-01","text":"[Persistent] Creatio est ordo divinitus fundatus in quo visibilia et invisibilia simul res et signa legibilia sunt.","refs":["CH_MHON_FROM:A1","CH_MHON_FROM:A2"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[]},{"id":"CL_MHON_01:GA-02","text":"[Persistent] Punctum, linea et circulus constituunt repertorium minimum sine quo forma monadica neque componi neque geometrice restitui potest.","refs":["CH_MHON_FROM:A3","CH_MHON_THEI:AS1","CH_MHON_TH_2:A1"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[]},{"id":"CL_MHON_01:GA-03","text":"[Mutating] Forma geometrica ex signo repraesentativo in operationem producendi, manifestandi et repraesentandi mutatur.","refs":["CH_MHON_THEI:A6","CH_MHON_THEI:D4"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[]},{"id":"CL_MHON_01:GA-04","text":"[Mutating] Monas ex charactere exacto et comprehensivo in rationem Puncti, originem rerum atque principium ipsius esse profunditur.","refs":["CH_MHON_FROM:AS2","CH_MHON_TH_2:AS3"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[]},{"id":"CL_MHON_01:GA-05","text":"[Persistent] Unitas pluralitatem ordinatam continet sicut centrum omnem peripheriam constitutive ordinat.","refs":["CH_MHON_FROM:A5","CH_MHON_TH_2:A6"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[]},{"id":"CL_MHON_01:GA-06","text":"[Emergent] Eadem hierarchia dependentiae constructionem figurarum et originem ontologicam regit, ita ut geometria forma localis ontologiae fiat.","refs":["CH_MHON_TH_2:AS5","CH_MHON_TH_2:SD1"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[]},{"id":"CL_MHON_01:GA-07","text":"[Attractor] Existentia, non-existentia et latentia Naturae intra eundem campum manifestationis formalis attrahuntur.","refs":["CH_MHON_THEI:AS2","CH_MHON_THEI:A5"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[]},{"id":"CL_MHON_01:GA-08","text":"[Persistent] Nulla differentia minima formae vel reproductionis semanticam indifferentiam habet.","refs":["CH_MHON_FROM:A4","CH_MHON_FROM:A17"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[]},{"id":"CL_MHON_01:GA-09","text":"[Emergent] Magnitudo et extensio effectuum non minuunt dependentiam a principio unitario et centrali.","refs":["CH_MHON_TH_2:A8","CH_MHON_TH_2:SD3"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[]},{"id":"CL_MHON_01:GA-10","text":"[Persistent] Validitas cognitionis a demonstratione structurae pendet, non ab auctoritate, similitudine nominis aut communitate terminorum.","refs":["CH_MHON_FROM:A14","CH_MHON_FROM:NX6","CH_MHON_TH_2:AS2"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[]},{"id":"CL_MHON_01:GA-11","text":"[Terminal] Transmissio legitima exemplar exacte reproductum cum delectu recipientium coniungit.","refs":["CH_MHON_FROM:D8","CH_MHON_FROM:NX7"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[]},{"id":"CL_MHON_01:GA-12","text":"[Attractor] Disciplinae particulares ad unam structuram monadicam recurrunt, cuius potentiae per analogias diversas iterum explicantur.","refs":["CH_MHON_FROM:A8","CH_MHON_FROM:SD1"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[]}],"original_name":"Global Load-Bearing Axioms"},{"number":"3","name":"Recurrence, Mutation, and Displacement Matrix","items":[{"id":"CL_MHON_01:RMD-01","text":"Linea et circulus identitatem geometricam servant, sed functionem mutant: primo formae simplicissimae sunt, deinde effectus dependentiae a Puncto et Recta fiunt.","refs":["CH_MHON_THEI:A1","CH_MHON_TH_2:A2"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[]},{"id":"CL_MHON_01:RMD-02","text":"Punctum ex ingrediente compositionis litterarum in originem rerum, principium esse et centrum omnis peripheriae amplificatur.","refs":["CH_MHON_FROM:A3","CH_MHON_TH_2:AS3","CH_MHON_TH_2:A7"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[]},{"id":"CL_MHON_01:RMD-03","text":"Monas ex charactere proposito in rationem ontologicam Puncti migrat, unde figurae et esse commune principium accipiunt.","refs":["CH_MHON_FROM:AS2","CH_MHON_TH_2:A3"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[]},{"id":"CL_MHON_01:RMD-04","text":"Repraesentatio vim invertit: non amplius imaginem rei iam constitutae sequitur, sed cum productione rei ad manifestationem coincidit.","refs":["CH_MHON_THEI:A6","CH_MHON_THEI:D4"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[]},{"id":"CL_MHON_01:RMD-05","text":"Occultatio duplicem functionem acquirit: involucrum Naturae per formam superandum est, velamentum doctrinale autem veritatem ab usu indigno custodit.","refs":["CH_MHON_THEI:A4","CH_MHON_FROM:NX9"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[]},{"id":"CL_MHON_01:RMD-06","text":"Universalitas a scriptura totius creationis ad comprehensionem existentium, non-existentium et latentium, deinde ad omnem magnitudinem peripheriae amplificatur.","refs":["CH_MHON_FROM:A9","CH_MHON_THEI:AS2","CH_MHON_TH_2:A8"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[]},{"id":"CL_MHON_01:RMD-07","text":"Raritas desinit esse sola proprietas philosophi et in structuram competentiae, receptionis selectivae atque transmissionis legitimae absorbetur.","refs":["CH_MHON_FROM:A10","CH_MHON_FROM:D8"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[]},{"id":"CL_MHON_01:RMD-08","text":"Tutela regia et corpus typographicum, quamvis theorematibus geometricis non repetantur, permanent ut custodes materiales distinctionum formalium ab illis theorematibus requisitarum.","refs":["CH_MHON_FROM:A18","CH_MHON_FROM:SD3"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[]}],"original_name":"Recurrence, Mutation, and Displacement Matrix"},{"number":"4","name":"Lower-Layer vs Current-Level Distinction","items":[{"id":"CL_MHON_01:CGD-01","text":"Patrocinium regium, dedicatio et administratio typographica mechanismi locales frontis materiae manent; hoc gradu valent tantum quatenus integritatem formae et iudicii protegunt.","refs":["CH_MHON_FROM:A18","CH_MHON_FROM:AS7"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[]},{"id":"CL_MHON_01:CGD-02","text":"Distinctio inter non-existentia et latentia manet determinatio localis incompleta, dum synthesis retinet solum utriusque inclusionem in campo manifestationis.","refs":["CH_MHON_THEI:MRT4","CH_MHON_THEI:AS2"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[]},{"id":"CL_MHON_01:CGD-03","text":"Dependentiae Rectae a Puncto et Circuli a Recta sunt demonstrationes locales; earum amplificatio globalis est principium quo omne compositum ad unitatem constitutivam reducitur.","refs":["CH_MHON_TH_2:A1","CH_MHON_TH_2:A2"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[]},{"id":"CL_MHON_01:CGD-04","text":"Coniunctio inter productionem formalem et exactitudinem typographicam tantum hoc gradu emergit: corruptio exemplaris fit interruptio operationis repraesentativae.","refs":["CH_MHON_FROM:A17","CH_MHON_THEI:A6"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[]},{"id":"CL_MHON_01:CGD-05","text":"Transitus maior fit cum linea et circulus, antea formae primae, retroactive subordinantur Puncto ratione Monadis.","refs":["CH_MHON_THEI:AS1","CH_MHON_TH_2:A3"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[]},{"id":"CL_MHON_01:CGD-06","text":"Polemica de magia in structuram generalem distinctionis inter corpus verum et umbram homonymam absorbetur; nomen disciplinae suam vim classificatoriam amittit.","refs":["CH_MHON_FROM:NX5","CH_MHON_FROM:A12"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[]},{"id":"CL_MHON_01:CGD-07","text":"Enumerationes effectuum disciplinarium nondum ad axiomata globalia singillatim ascendunt; globaliter servatur tantum regula re-expansionis analogicae ex eadem structura.","refs":["CH_MHON_FROM:MRT9","CH_MHON_FROM:A8"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[]}],"original_name":"Lower-Layer vs Current-Level Distinction"},{"number":"5","name":"Global Apophatic Structure","items":[{"id":"CL_MHON_01:GNX-01","text":"Signum monadicum non est institutum conventionaliter rei extrinsecum, sed forma cui vis realis et operativa attribuitur.","refs":["CH_MHON_FROM:DNX2","CH_MHON_FROM:SNX3"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[]},{"id":"CL_MHON_01:GNX-02","text":"Repraesentatio non est imitatio passiva rei iam completae neque operatio a productione separabilis.","refs":["CH_MHON_THEI:NX4","CH_MHON_THEI:D4"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[]},{"id":"CL_MHON_01:GNX-03","text":"Nulla figura geometrica est autonoma: Recta Punctum, Circulus Rectam, peripheria centrum necessario implicant.","refs":["CH_MHON_TH_2:A1","CH_MHON_TH_2:A2","CH_MHON_TH_2:NX3"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[]},{"id":"CL_MHON_01:GNX-04","text":"Pluralitas et extensio non possunt unitatem principii superare aut relationem ad Monadem abolere.","refs":["CH_MHON_TH_2:DNX3","CH_MHON_TH_2:SNX2"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[]},{"id":"CL_MHON_01:GNX-05","text":"Antiquitas, auctoritas et similitudo vocabuli non constituunt probationem intrinsecam rei.","refs":["CH_MHON_FROM:NX4","CH_MHON_FROM:NX6","CH_MHON_FROM:A14"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[]},{"id":"CL_MHON_01:GNX-06","text":"Neque circulatio indiscriminata neque medium typographicum indifferens condicionem legitimam doctrinae constituunt.","refs":["CH_MHON_FROM:NX7","CH_MHON_FROM:NX8"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[]},{"id":"CL_MHON_01:GNX-07","text":"Disciplinae non sunt regiones absolutis limitibus clausae, nec contemplatio sacra, scientia naturalis et operatio technica penitus separari possunt.","refs":["CH_MHON_FROM:DNX1","CH_MHON_FROM:SNX4"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[]}],"original_name":"Global Apophatic Structure"},{"number":"6","name":"Deep Book Apophatics","items":[{"id":"CL_MHON_01:DBN-01","text":"Falsum esse oportet realitatem continere residuum absolute informe, inaccessibile et extra classes existentiae, non-existentiae atque latentiae positum.","refs":["CH_MHON_THEI:SNX1","CH_MHON_THEI:SNX4"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[]},{"id":"CL_MHON_01:DBN-02","text":"Falsum esse oportet ordinem essendi ab ordine significandi et repraesentandi fundamentaliter separari.","refs":["CH_MHON_THEI:DNX2","CH_MHON_THEI:SNX2"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[]},{"id":"CL_MHON_01:DBN-03","text":"Falsum esse oportet extensionem, pluralitatem aut peripheriam se ipsas sine principio indivisibili fundare posse.","refs":["CH_MHON_TH_2:DNX2","CH_MHON_TH_2:DNX4"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[]},{"id":"CL_MHON_01:DBN-04","text":"Falsum esse oportet mutationem materialem puncti, lineae aut schematis sensum doctrinalem intactum relinquere.","refs":["CH_MHON_FROM:DNX6","CH_MHON_FROM:SD3"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[]},{"id":"CL_MHON_01:DBN-05","text":"Falsum esse oportet universalem praesentiam signorum universalem competentiam interpretandi efficere.","refs":["CH_MHON_FROM:SD4","CH_MHON_FROM:A11"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[]},{"id":"CL_MHON_01:DBN-06","text":"Falsum esse oportet abusum, imitationem aut corruptionem artis existentiam corporis doctrinalis veri tollere.","refs":["CH_MHON_FROM:D6","CH_MHON_FROM:DNX8"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[]}],"original_name":"Deep Book Apophatics"},{"number":"7","name":"Super-Apophatics of the Whole","items":[{"id":"CL_MHON_01:BSA-01","text":"Possibilitas mundi sine centro originario, aut peripheriae infinitae a Puncto emancipatae, ontologice inadmissibilis est.","refs":["CH_MHON_TH_2:SNX1","CH_MHON_TH_2:ME3"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[]},{"id":"CL_MHON_01:BSA-02","text":"Systema circum exclusionem puri exterioris ordinatur: nihil admittitur quod neque existens, neque non existens, neque latens, neque formaliter producibile sit.","refs":["CH_MHON_THEI:SNX4","CH_MHON_THEI:AS2"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[]},{"id":"CL_MHON_01:BSA-03","text":"Perspicuitas totalis omnibus indiscriminatim praebita est absentia constitutiva, quia plena publicitas ordinem moralem et epistemicum receptionis dissolveret.","refs":["CH_MHON_FROM:SNX1","CH_MHON_FROM:NX7"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[]},{"id":"CL_MHON_01:BSA-04","text":"Tribunal omnino externum et cognitione rei carens nullam potestatem ultimam obtinere potest.","refs":["CH_MHON_FROM:SNX2","CH_MHON_FROM:A11"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[]},{"id":"CL_MHON_01:BSA-05","text":"Separatio absoluta contemplationis, naturae et operationis manet impossibilitas circa quam universalis potentia Monadis distribuitur.","refs":["CH_MHON_FROM:SNX4","CH_MHON_FROM:A8"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[]}],"original_name":"Super-Apophatics of the Whole"},{"number":"8","name":"Cross-Unit Derivative Propositions","items":[{"id":"CL_MHON_01:XD-01","text":"Monas non solum signa iam data comprimit, sed per Punctum, lineam, centrum et circulum ipsam machinam productionis et repraesentationis constituit.","refs":["CL_MHON_01:GA-02","CL_MHON_01:GA-03","CL_MHON_01:GA-04","CL_MHON_01:GA-06","CH_MHON_FROM:A3","CH_MHON_THEI:A6","CH_MHON_TH_2:AS5"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"Composite","passages":[]},{"id":"CL_MHON_01:XD-02","text":"Quia forma rem ad manifestationem producit, exactitudo typographica fit continuatio causalitatis formalis, non mera fidelitas editorialia.","refs":["CL_MHON_01:GA-03","CL_MHON_01:GA-08","CH_MHON_FROM:A17","CH_MHON_THEI:D4","CH_MHON_TH_2:A7"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"Composite","passages":[]},{"id":"CL_MHON_01:XD-03","text":"Universalitas systematis est intensiva: eadem relatio simplex creationem, omnes classes rerum et quamlibet peripheriae magnitudinem permeat.","refs":["CL_MHON_01:GA-01","CL_MHON_01:GA-05","CL_MHON_01:GA-09","CH_MHON_FROM:A2","CH_MHON_THEI:AS2","CH_MHON_TH_2:A8"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"Composite","passages":[]},{"id":"CL_MHON_01:XD-04","text":"Occultatio bifurcatur in statum naturalem superandum et velamentum doctrinale conservandum; eadem forma igitur manifestat rem et eius accessum restringit.","refs":["CL_MHON_01:GA-07","CL_MHON_01:GA-11","CH_MHON_FROM:NX9","CH_MHON_THEI:NX5"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"Tension","passages":[]},{"id":"CL_MHON_01:XD-05","text":"Necessitas geometrica probationem intrinsecam praebet, dum auctoritas regia solum condiciones tutelae et discretionis externae administrat.","refs":["CL_MHON_01:GA-10","CL_MHON_01:GA-11","CH_MHON_FROM:A14","CH_MHON_FROM:A18","CH_MHON_TH_2:AS2"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"Composite","passages":[]},{"id":"CL_MHON_01:XD-06","text":"Pluralitas admittitur tantum ut distinctio ordinate inclusa et centraliter dependens, non ut aggregatio principiorum autonomorum.","refs":["CL_MHON_01:GA-05","CL_MHON_01:GA-09","CH_MHON_FROM:A5","CH_MHON_TH_2:A6","CH_MHON_TH_2:SD3"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"Composite","passages":[]},{"id":"CL_MHON_01:XD-07","text":"Appellatio lineae et circuli ut formarum primarum fit prioritas relativa manifestationis, quia theorema sequens utramque ad Punctum ratione Monadis retroactive subicit.","refs":["CL_MHON_01:GA-02","CL_MHON_01:GA-04","CL_MHON_01:GA-06","CH_MHON_THEI:AS1","CH_MHON_TH_2:A1","CH_MHON_TH_2:A3"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"Tension","passages":[]},{"id":"CL_MHON_01:XD-08","text":"Inclusio non-existentium in campo formali significat possibilitatem manifestationis ordine formae constitui antequam existentia actualis determinetur.","refs":["CL_MHON_01:GA-03","CL_MHON_01:GA-07","CH_MHON_THEI:A3","CH_MHON_THEI:SD3","CH_MHON_TH_2:A4"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"Tension","passages":[]}],"original_name":"Cross-Unit Derivative Propositions"},{"number":"9","name":"Book-Scale Super-Derivatives","items":[{"id":"CL_MHON_01:BSD-01","text":"Cognoscere rem significat eius dependentias formales reconstruere, non imaginem externam cum obiecto iam completo conferre.","refs":["CL_MHON_01:GA-03","CL_MHON_01:GA-06","CL_MHON_01:GA-10","CH_MHON_TH_2:D4","CH_MHON_THEI:D4"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"From","passages":[]},{"id":"CL_MHON_01:BSD-02","text":"Causalitas, ontologia et repraesentatio in una serie coalescunt: principium simplex figuram constituit, figura rem manifestat, manifestatio eam cognoscibilem reddit.","refs":["CL_MHON_01:GA-03","CL_MHON_01:GA-06","CL_MHON_01:GA-07","CH_MHON_THEI:SD2","CH_MHON_TH_2:SD1"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"From","passages":[]},{"id":"CL_MHON_01:BSD-03","text":"Tempus originis fit recursivum, quia id quod primo tamquam forma primaria apparet postea ex principio adhuc simpliciore derivatur.","refs":["CL_MHON_01:GA-02","CL_MHON_01:GA-04","CH_MHON_THEI:AS1","CH_MHON_TH_2:A3","CH_MHON_TH_2:MRT5"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"From","passages":[]},{"id":"CL_MHON_01:BSD-04","text":"Subiectum cognoscens non est spectator neuter, sed lector et operator cui amplitudo cognitionis, sinceritas et aptitudo receptionis imponuntur.","refs":["CL_MHON_01:GA-07","CL_MHON_01:GA-10","CL_MHON_01:GA-11","CH_MHON_FROM:A11","CH_MHON_FROM:A16","CH_MHON_THEI:A5"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"From","passages":[]},{"id":"CL_MHON_01:BSD-05","text":"Potestas politica cognitionem legitimam tueri et falsas assimilationes reprimere potest, sed probationem formalem producere non potest.","refs":["CL_MHON_01:GA-10","CL_MHON_01:GA-11","CH_MHON_FROM:A14","CH_MHON_FROM:A18","CH_MHON_FROM:D9"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"From","passages":[]},{"id":"CL_MHON_01:BSD-06","text":"Publicatio fit porta duplex: exemplar omnibus materialiter producitur, sed integritas doctrinalis a forma exacta et receptione discriminata simul dependet.","refs":["CL_MHON_01:GA-08","CL_MHON_01:GA-11","CH_MHON_FROM:D8","CH_MHON_FROM:SD4"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"From","passages":[]}],"original_name":"Book-Scale Super-Derivatives"},{"number":"10","name":"Recursive Loops, Escalation Paths, and Conceptual Attractors","items":[{"id":"CL_MHON_01:RLE-01","text":"Compressio signorum in Monadem re-expansionem per disciplinas generat, cuius effectus concordantes ad fecunditatem ipsius Monadis iterum referuntur.","refs":["CH_MHON_FROM:D3","CH_MHON_FROM:A8"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[]},{"id":"CL_MHON_01:RLE-02","text":"Latentia postulat formam manifestantem, sed manifestatio doctrinalis novum velamentum postulat; revelatio et custodia se mutuo reproducunt.","refs":["CH_MHON_THEI:A4","CH_MHON_THEI:A5","CH_MHON_FROM:NX9"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[]},{"id":"CL_MHON_01:RLE-03","text":"Centrum peripheriam constituit, peripheria centrum necessarium reddit, atque invariantia huius relationis per omnes magnitudines primatum Monadis amplificat.","refs":["CH_MHON_TH_2:A6","CH_MHON_TH_2:A8","CH_MHON_TH_2:SD2"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[]},{"id":"CL_MHON_01:RLE-04","text":"Minima corruptio formae mutilationem doctrinae parit, mutilatio errorem receptionis auget, error autem necessitatem exactioris custodiae confirmat.","refs":["CH_MHON_FROM:A4","CH_MHON_FROM:SD3","CH_MHON_FROM:A16"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[]},{"id":"CL_MHON_01:RLE-05","text":"Cognitio amplitudinis mysteriorum condicionem iudicii constituit, sed circulatio selectiva acquisitionem huius cognitionis restringit; competentia fit attractor simul requisitus et custoditus.","refs":["CH_MHON_FROM:A11","CH_MHON_FROM:SD6"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"Tension","passages":[]},{"id":"CL_MHON_01:RLE-06","text":"Punctum-Monas est attractor dominantissimus, quia ad se compositionem characteris, originem esse, centrum figurarum et distinctionem unitatis a pluralitate revocat.","refs":["CH_MHON_TH_2:D4","CH_MHON_TH_2:ME1"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[]},{"id":"CL_MHON_01:RLE-07","text":"Communitas terminorum corpus et umbram confundit, demonstratio usum et structuram distinguit, distinctio autem necessitatem iudicii disciplinati instaurat.","refs":["CH_MHON_FROM:A13","CH_MHON_FROM:D7"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[]}],"original_name":"Recursive Loops, Escalation Paths, and Conceptual Attractors"},{"number":"11","name":"Formal Generative Schema of the Whole Book","items":[{"id":"CL_MHON_01:FGS-B01","text":"Deus → emanatio visibilium et invisibilium → creatio ut scriptura → mundus signorum legibilium.","refs":["CH_MHON_FROM:A1","CH_MHON_FROM:A2"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[]},{"id":"CL_MHON_01:FGS-B02","text":"Signa dispersa → reductio ad Punctum, lineam et circulum → compositio characteris monadici exacti.","refs":["CH_MHON_FROM:A3","CH_MHON_FROM:A6"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[]},{"id":"CL_MHON_01:FGS-B03","text":"Linea et circulus → comprehensio existentium, non-existentium et latentium → productio in lucem = repraesentatio determinata.","refs":["CH_MHON_THEI:AS2","CH_MHON_THEI:A6"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[]},{"id":"CL_MHON_01:FGS-B04","text":"Quaestio constructionis → Recta ad Punctum → Circulus ad Rectam → centrum ad Punctum → Punctum ad Monadem.","refs":["CH_MHON_TH_2:AS2","CH_MHON_TH_2:AS5"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[]},{"id":"CL_MHON_01:FGS-B05","text":"Punctum-Monas → centrum → peripheria cuiuslibet magnitudinis → pluralitas extensa unitati constitutive subordinata.","refs":["CH_MHON_TH_2:A6","CH_MHON_TH_2:A8"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[]},{"id":"CL_MHON_01:FGS-B06","text":"Structura monadica → projectiones analogicae → effectus disciplinarii concordantes → probatio fecunditatis muneris.","refs":["CH_MHON_FROM:A8","CH_MHON_FROM:D4"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[]},{"id":"CL_MHON_01:FGS-B07","text":"Veritas formalis → exemplar exacte impressum → lector discriminatus → transmissio legitima; corruptio vel promiscuitas → error et accusatio.","refs":["CH_MHON_FROM:D8","CH_MHON_FROM:A16"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[]}],"original_name":"Formal Generative Schema of the Whole Book"},{"number":"12","name":"Whole-Book Argument Flow Map","items":[{"id":"CL_MHON_01:AFM-B01","text":"Raritas sapientiae → propositio Monadis → reductio ad lineam et circulum → regressus ad Punctum → constitutio centri → universalitas dependentiae monadicae.","refs":["CH_MHON_FROM:AS4","CH_MHON_THEI:AS1","CH_MHON_TH_2:AS5","CH_MHON_TH_2:AS3"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[]},{"id":"CL_MHON_01:AFM-B02","text":"Involucrum Naturae → forma simplicissima → productio in lucem → repraesentatio activa → cognitio reconstructiva.","refs":["CH_MHON_THEI:A4","CH_MHON_THEI:A5","CH_MHON_THEI:D4"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[]},{"id":"CL_MHON_01:AFM-B03","text":"Communitas nominum et figurarum → confusio corporis cum umbra → demonstratio structuram discernens → impressio exacta → circulatio selectiva.","refs":["CH_MHON_FROM:A13","CH_MHON_FROM:D7","CH_MHON_FROM:D8"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[]}],"original_name":"Whole-Book Argument Flow Map"},{"number":"13","name":"Macro Map-Effects","items":[{"id":"CL_MHON_01:MME-01","text":"Geometria ex instrumento descriptionis in principium ontologicum et generativum reclassificatur.","refs":["CH_MHON_THEI:ME3","CH_MHON_TH_2:ME1"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[]},{"id":"CL_MHON_01:MME-02","text":"Oppositio inter productionem et repraesentationem corruit; significare fit modus rem ad manifestationem constituendi.","refs":["CH_MHON_THEI:ME2","CH_MHON_THEI:D4"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[]},{"id":"CL_MHON_01:MME-03","text":"Non-existentia et latentia ex exterioribus cognitionis in modos formabilitatis nondum manifestatae transferuntur.","refs":["CH_MHON_THEI:A3","CH_MHON_THEI:SD3"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[]},{"id":"CL_MHON_01:MME-04","text":"Oppositio unitatis et pluralitatis substituitur relatione centri et peripheriae, qua extensio variabilis dependentiam invariantem servat.","refs":["CH_MHON_FROM:A5","CH_MHON_TH_2:ME3"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[]},{"id":"CL_MHON_01:MME-05","text":"Liber materialis ex vehiculo externo in partem operationis doctrinalis transit.","refs":["CH_MHON_FROM:SD3","CH_MHON_FROM:NX8"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[]},{"id":"CL_MHON_01:MME-06","text":"Oppositio inter scientiam et magiam cedit distinctioni inter corpus demonstrabile et umbram adulteratam quae terminis communibus utitur.","refs":["CH_MHON_FROM:ME4","CH_MHON_FROM:A12"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[]},{"id":"CL_MHON_01:MME-07","text":"Auctoritas reclassificatur: non est fons veritatis, sed instrumentum protectionis condicionum sub quibus demonstratio recte recipitur.","refs":["CH_MHON_FROM:D9","CH_MHON_FROM:A14"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[]}],"original_name":"Macro Map-Effects"},{"number":"14","name":"Altitude Integration","items":[{"id":"CL_MHON_01:ALT-B01","text":"In gradu superficiei agunt character Monadis, linea, circulus, Punctum, quaestio constructionis et cura exactae publicationis.","refs":["CH_MHON_FROM:AS2","CH_MHON_TH_2:AS1"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[]},{"id":"CL_MHON_01:ALT-B02","text":"In gradu structurali rerum classes et figurae distribuuntur secundum dependentias ordinatas quae a peripheria ad centrum et a composito ad principium simplex reducuntur.","refs":["CH_MHON_TH_2:D3","CH_MHON_THEI:D2"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[]},{"id":"CL_MHON_01:ALT-B03","text":"In gradu metastrategico certamen de legitimitate ex vocabulis controversis ad usum, structuram, finem et demonstrationem internam transfertur.","refs":["CH_MHON_FROM:D7","CH_MHON_FROM:NX6"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[]},{"id":"CL_MHON_01:ALT-B04","text":"In gradu ontologico et temporali realitas est scriptura emanata, latentia est status transitorius, et origo per regressus ad principia simpliciora iterum determinatur.","refs":["CH_MHON_FROM:A1","CH_MHON_THEI:D3","CH_MHON_TH_2:D5"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[]},{"id":"CL_MHON_01:ALT-B05","text":"In gradu methodologico et performativo compressio theorematis imitatur compressionem Monadis, dum exactitudo formae scriptae efficit doctrinam quam de significatione necessaria affirmat.","refs":["CH_MHON_FROM:D3","CH_MHON_FROM:A17","CH_MHON_THEI:A6"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[]}],"original_name":"Altitude Integration"},{"number":"15","name":"Internal Tensions, Mutation Points, and Fault Lines","items":[{"id":"CL_MHON_01:FL-01","text":"Linea et circulus dicuntur formae primae, sed Punctum ratione Monadis postea ut principium utriusque et rerum ipsarum ponitur; prioritas igitur inter gradum manifestationis et originem ontologicam oscillat.","refs":["CH_MHON_THEI:AS1","CH_MHON_TH_2:A3"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"Tension","passages":[]},{"id":"CL_MHON_01:FL-02","text":"Productio et repraesentatio una operatione iunguntur sine criterio quo constitutio rei a constitutione eius apparentiae discernatur.","refs":["CH_MHON_THEI:MRT5","CH_MHON_THEI:SD2"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"Tension","passages":[]},{"id":"CL_MHON_01:FL-03","text":"Universalitas scripturae creationis cum restrictione mysteriorum ad lectores idoneos cohaeret tantum si praesentia signi a facultate interpretandi stricte distinguatur.","refs":["CH_MHON_FROM:D5","CH_MHON_FROM:NX7"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"Tension","passages":[]},{"id":"CL_MHON_01:FL-04","text":"Repudiatio auctoritatis humanae et invocatio tutelae regiae non contradicunt directe, sed finis probativus et finis politicus facile confunduntur.","refs":["CH_MHON_FROM:A14","CH_MHON_FROM:A18"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"Tension","passages":[]},{"id":"CL_MHON_01:FL-05","text":"Non-existentia et latentia sub uno processu formativo colliguntur, quamvis alterum privationem existentiae, alterum occultationem rei iam ordinatae indicare possit.","refs":["CH_MHON_THEI:A3","CH_MHON_THEI:MRT4"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"Tension","passages":[]},{"id":"CL_MHON_01:FL-06","text":"Potentia analogica per disciplinas structuram globalem sustinet, sed effectus particulares adhuc enumeratione et promissione magis quam demonstratione probantur.","refs":["CH_MHON_FROM:A8","CH_MHON_FROM:MRT9"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"Tension","passages":[]},{"id":"CL_MHON_01:MP-01","text":"Repraesentatio mutatur ex relatione posteriore ad rem in operationem quae rem in lucem producit.","refs":["CH_MHON_THEI:A6","CH_MHON_THEI:D4"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"Tension","passages":[]},{"id":"CL_MHON_01:MP-02","text":"Punctum mutatur ex elemento minimo scripturae geometricae in rationem Monadis et principium ontologicum.","refs":["CH_MHON_FROM:A3","CH_MHON_TH_2:AS3"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"Tension","passages":[]},{"id":"CL_MHON_01:MP-03","text":"Occultatio mutatur ex involucro Naturae removendo in velamentum doctrinale deliberate conservandum.","refs":["CH_MHON_THEI:A4","CH_MHON_FROM:NX9"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"Tension","passages":[]},{"id":"CL_MHON_01:MP-04","text":"Terminus “primum” mutatur ex ordine formarum manifestarum in ordinem principii ontologici, unde temporalitas originis fit analogica et stratificata.","refs":["CH_MHON_THEI:AS1","CH_MHON_TH_2:A3","CH_MHON_TH_2:MRT5"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"Tension","passages":[]}],"original_name":"Internal Tensions, Mutation Points, and Fault Lines"},{"number":"16","name":"Hidden System Image","items":[{"id":"CL_MHON_01:HSI-01","text":"Realitas implicite est machina scriptoria centrata, in qua Punctum lineas et circulos explicat, peripheriae autem pluralitatem ad centrum complicant.","refs":["CH_MHON_FROM:A2","CH_MHON_TH_2:AS5","CH_MHON_TH_2:A6"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[]},{"id":"CL_MHON_01:HSI-02","text":"Subiectum fit lector-constructor qui involucrum per formam aperit et corpus verum ab umbra secundum structuram discernit.","refs":["CH_MHON_FROM:A11","CH_MHON_THEI:A5","CH_MHON_FROM:D7"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[]},{"id":"CL_MHON_01:HSI-03","text":"Tempus est regressus generativus: manifestum ad latentiam, compositum ad simplex et primum apparens ad prius ontologicum reducitur.","refs":["CH_MHON_FROM:AS4","CH_MHON_THEI:AS5","CH_MHON_TH_2:MRT5"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[]},{"id":"CL_MHON_01:HSI-04","text":"Certamen possibile non est fuga a centro, sed conservatio canalis exacti inter principium, figuram et lectorem contra umbras, corruptionem et receptionem indiscretam.","refs":["CH_MHON_FROM:A12","CH_MHON_FROM:A16","CH_MHON_FROM:D8"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[]}],"original_name":"Hidden System Image"},{"number":"17","name":"Minimal Whole-Book Reconstruction Test","items":[{"id":"CL_MHON_01:MRT-B01","text":"Axiomata praesentia sufficiunt ad restituendam machinam fundamentalem: scriptura creationis, formae simplices, manifestatio productiva, regressus ad Punctum-Monadem et transmissio custodita.","refs":["CH_MHON_FROM:MRT1","CH_MHON_THEI:MRT1","CH_MHON_TH_2:MRT1"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[]},{"id":"CL_MHON_01:MRT-B02","text":"Nondum satis determinatur quomodo identitas inter constructionem geometricam, originem ontologicam et repraesentationem productivam demonstrative constituatur.","refs":["CH_MHON_TH_2:MRT2","CH_MHON_THEI:MRT5"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[]},{"id":"CL_MHON_01:MRT-B03","text":"Structura firmissima est invariantia dependentiae a Puncto centrali coniuncta cum necessitate integritatis materialis formae.","refs":["CH_MHON_TH_2:A8","CH_MHON_FROM:SD3"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[]},{"id":"CL_MHON_01:MRT-B04","text":"Systema hac magnitudine cohaeret quatenus omnia elementa ad unum transitum inter unitatem, manifestationem et dependentiam formalem reducuntur.","refs":["CH_MHON_FROM:MRT3","CH_MHON_THEI:MRT2"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[]},{"id":"CL_MHON_01:MRT-B05","text":"Cohaerentia fragilior fit ubi competentia praeexigitur ad iudicandum, universalitas signorum affirmatur et accessus doctrinalis simul restringitur.","refs":["CH_MHON_FROM:MRT5","CH_MHON_FROM:MRT6"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[]},{"id":"CL_MHON_01:MRT-B06","text":"Reductioni axiomaticae adhuc resistunt nexus particulares inter formam monadicam et effectus disciplinarios, necnon differentia inter non-existentia et latentia.","refs":["CH_MHON_FROM:MRT9","CH_MHON_THEI:MRT4"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[]},{"id":"CL_MHON_01:MRT-B07","text":"Restitutio verum motum servat, quia ab annuntiatione unitatis per formas manifestantes ad fundamentum centrale regreditur, deinde ad exactam transmissionem redit.","refs":["CH_MHON_FROM:MRT10","CH_MHON_THEI:MRT6","CH_MHON_TH_2:MRT6"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[]}],"original_name":"Minimal Whole-Book Reconstruction Test"}],"language_code":"la","language_name":"Latina","translations":[{"id":"CAREVAR_20261006T223110856438Z_d29ad058","language_code":"en","language_name":"English","title":"Chapters 01–03","source_sha256":"c1fbcaa2d4795bb1a89e6934491ab2e257f78e5b4ae45ad1083ea19827702dea","sha256":"5bc1adce30cb1f212601a1bbe98277a7597c63a2e30ea2bbfb1d46b2c7409bc1","rendition_type":"machine","review_status":"machine","model":"gpt-5.6-terra","created":"2026-10-06T22:31:10.859214+00:00","sections":[{"number":"1","name":"Book-Scale Argumental Spine","items":[{"id":"CL_MHON_01:BAS-01","text":"The system asks how a formally minimal character can simultaneously be a compendium of wisdom, a principle of the construction of things, and an object of safeguarded transmission.","refs":["CH_MHON_FROM:AS1","CH_MHON_TH_2:AS1"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[]},{"id":"CL_MHON_01:BAS-02","text":"The global thesis establishes creation as legible writing, whose geometrical forms not only signify things, but bring them forth into manifestation and have their principle in the Monad.","refs":["CH_MHON_FROM:A2","CH_MHON_THEI:A6","CH_MHON_TH_2:AS3"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[]},{"id":"CL_MHON_01:BAS-03","text":"The apologetic and reconstructive argument becomes an ontological regress: the Monad is first proposed as a total character, line and circle are explained as primary forms, and then the condition of both is traced back to the Point and the Monad.","refs":["CH_MHON_FROM:AS3","CH_MHON_THEI:AS5","CH_MHON_TH_2:AS5"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[]},{"id":"CL_MHON_01:BAS-04","text":"The movement is recursive and spiral, because dispersed signs are compressed into a simple form, the form is expanded through all classes of things, and the expansion is again contracted to the common terminus of the Point.","refs":["CH_MHON_FROM:A6","CH_MHON_THEI:SD4","CH_MHON_TH_2:D4"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[]},{"id":"CL_MHON_01:BAS-05","text":"The proof combines the necessity of geometrical construction, ontological interpretation, and analogical fecundity, so that the rigor of form governs transdisciplinary force.","refs":["CH_MHON_FROM:A7","CH_MHON_TH_2:AS2","CH_MHON_FROM:A8"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[]},{"id":"CL_MHON_01:BAS-06","text":"The exactness of publication is not an external appendix, because the form that produces and represents doctrine loses force if the Point, line, center, or relation is materially corrupted.","refs":["CH_MHON_FROM:A17","CH_MHON_THEI:D4","CH_MHON_TH_2:A7"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[]},{"id":"CL_MHON_01:BAS-07","text":"The closure tends toward the invariance of the center: no extension of the periphery removes the service of the Point, although the identity between geometrical cause and ontological principle is not yet demonstratively closed.","refs":["CH_MHON_TH_2:A8","CH_MHON_TH_2:MRT2"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[]},{"id":"CL_MHON_01:BAS-08","text":"The universality of signs produces an open closure, because every creature falls within the legible field, but correct interpretation and transmission do not extend to indiscriminate readers.","refs":["CH_MHON_FROM:SD4","CH_MHON_FROM:NX7"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[]}],"original_name":"Book-Scale Argumental Spine"},{"number":"2","name":"Global Load-Bearing Axioms","items":[{"id":"CL_MHON_01:GA-01","text":"[Persistent] Creation is a divinely founded order in which visibles and invisibles are at once things and legible signs.","refs":["CH_MHON_FROM:A1","CH_MHON_FROM:A2"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[]},{"id":"CL_MHON_01:GA-02","text":"[Persistent] Point, line, and circle constitute the minimal repertoire without which the monadic form can neither be composed nor geometrically restored.","refs":["CH_MHON_FROM:A3","CH_MHON_THEI:AS1","CH_MHON_TH_2:A1"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[]},{"id":"CL_MHON_01:GA-03","text":"[Mutating] Geometrical form changes from a representative sign into an operation of producing, manifesting, and representing.","refs":["CH_MHON_THEI:A6","CH_MHON_THEI:D4"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[]},{"id":"CL_MHON_01:GA-04","text":"[Mutating] The Monad is deepened from an exact and comprehensive character into the rationale of the Point, the origin of things, and the principle of being itself.","refs":["CH_MHON_FROM:AS2","CH_MHON_TH_2:AS3"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[]},{"id":"CL_MHON_01:GA-05","text":"[Persistent] Unity contains ordered plurality just as the center constitutively orders every periphery.","refs":["CH_MHON_FROM:A5","CH_MHON_TH_2:A6"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[]},{"id":"CL_MHON_01:GA-06","text":"[Emergent] The same hierarchy of dependence governs the construction of figures and ontological origin, so that geometry becomes a local form of ontology.","refs":["CH_MHON_TH_2:AS5","CH_MHON_TH_2:SD1"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[]},{"id":"CL_MHON_01:GA-07","text":"[Attractor] Existence, non-existence, and the latency of Nature are drawn into the same field of formal manifestation.","refs":["CH_MHON_THEI:AS2","CH_MHON_THEI:A5"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[]},{"id":"CL_MHON_01:GA-08","text":"[Persistent] No minimal difference of form or reproduction is semantically indifferent.","refs":["CH_MHON_FROM:A4","CH_MHON_FROM:A17"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[]},{"id":"CL_MHON_01:GA-09","text":"[Emergent] The magnitude and extension of effects do not diminish dependence upon a unitary and central principle.","refs":["CH_MHON_TH_2:A8","CH_MHON_TH_2:SD3"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[]},{"id":"CL_MHON_01:GA-10","text":"[Persistent] The validity of knowledge depends on the demonstration of structure, not on authority, similarity of name, or community of terms.","refs":["CH_MHON_FROM:A14","CH_MHON_FROM:NX6","CH_MHON_TH_2:AS2"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[]},{"id":"CL_MHON_01:GA-11","text":"[Terminal] Legitimate transmission combines an exactly reproduced exemplar with the selection of recipients.","refs":["CH_MHON_FROM:D8","CH_MHON_FROM:NX7"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[]},{"id":"CL_MHON_01:GA-12","text":"[Attractor] Particular disciplines return to one monadic structure, whose powers are again unfolded through diverse analogies.","refs":["CH_MHON_FROM:A8","CH_MHON_FROM:SD1"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[]}],"original_name":"Global Load-Bearing Axioms"},{"number":"3","name":"Recurrence, Mutation, and Displacement Matrix","items":[{"id":"CL_MHON_01:RMD-01","text":"Line and circle preserve their geometrical identity, but change function: first they are the simplest forms, then they become effects of dependence upon the Point and the Straight Line.","refs":["CH_MHON_THEI:A1","CH_MHON_TH_2:A2"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[]},{"id":"CL_MHON_01:RMD-02","text":"The Point is expanded from an element in the composition of letters into the origin of things, the principle of being, and the center of every periphery.","refs":["CH_MHON_FROM:A3","CH_MHON_TH_2:AS3","CH_MHON_TH_2:A7"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[]},{"id":"CL_MHON_01:RMD-03","text":"The Monad migrates from the proposed character into the ontological rationale of the Point, from which figures and being receive a common principle.","refs":["CH_MHON_FROM:AS2","CH_MHON_TH_2:A3"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[]},{"id":"CL_MHON_01:RMD-04","text":"Representation reverses its force: it no longer follows an image of an already constituted thing, but coincides with the production of the thing into manifestation.","refs":["CH_MHON_THEI:A6","CH_MHON_THEI:D4"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[]},{"id":"CL_MHON_01:RMD-05","text":"Concealment acquires a double function: the covering of Nature must be overcome through form, whereas the doctrinal veil safeguards truth from unworthy use.","refs":["CH_MHON_THEI:A4","CH_MHON_FROM:NX9"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[]},{"id":"CL_MHON_01:RMD-06","text":"Universality is expanded from the writing of all creation to the comprehension of existents, non-existents, and latents, and then to every magnitude of the periphery.","refs":["CH_MHON_FROM:A9","CH_MHON_THEI:AS2","CH_MHON_TH_2:A8"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[]},{"id":"CL_MHON_01:RMD-07","text":"Rarity ceases to be the sole property of the philosopher and is absorbed into a structure of competence, selective reception, and legitimate transmission.","refs":["CH_MHON_FROM:A10","CH_MHON_FROM:D8"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[]},{"id":"CL_MHON_01:RMD-08","text":"Royal protection and the typographical corpus, although not repeated by geometrical theorems, remain as material guardians of the formal distinctions required by those theorems.","refs":["CH_MHON_FROM:A18","CH_MHON_FROM:SD3"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[]}],"original_name":"Recurrence, Mutation, and Displacement Matrix"},{"number":"4","name":"Lower-Layer vs Current-Level Distinction","items":[{"id":"CL_MHON_01:CGD-01","text":"Royal patronage, dedication, and typographical administration remain local mechanisms of the front matter; at this level they are valid only insofar as they protect the integrity of form and judgment.","refs":["CH_MHON_FROM:A18","CH_MHON_FROM:AS7"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[]},{"id":"CL_MHON_01:CGD-02","text":"The distinction between non-existents and latents remains an incomplete local determination, while the synthesis retains only the inclusion of both within the field of manifestation.","refs":["CH_MHON_THEI:MRT4","CH_MHON_THEI:AS2"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[]},{"id":"CL_MHON_01:CGD-03","text":"The dependences of the Straight Line upon the Point and of the Circle upon the Straight Line are local demonstrations; their global expansion is the principle by which every composite is reduced to constitutive unity.","refs":["CH_MHON_TH_2:A1","CH_MHON_TH_2:A2"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[]},{"id":"CL_MHON_01:CGD-04","text":"The conjunction of formal production and typographical exactitude emerges only at this level: corruption of the exemplar becomes an interruption of the representative operation.","refs":["CH_MHON_FROM:A17","CH_MHON_THEI:A6"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[]},{"id":"CL_MHON_01:CGD-05","text":"The major transition occurs when line and circle, previously primary forms, are retroactively subordinated to the Point by reason of the Monad.","refs":["CH_MHON_THEI:AS1","CH_MHON_TH_2:A3"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[]},{"id":"CL_MHON_01:CGD-06","text":"The polemic concerning magic is absorbed into the general structure of the distinction between the true body and the homonymous shadow; the name of the discipline loses its classificatory force.","refs":["CH_MHON_FROM:NX5","CH_MHON_FROM:A12"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[]},{"id":"CL_MHON_01:CGD-07","text":"Enumerations of disciplinary effects do not yet rise individually to global axioms; globally, only the rule of analogical re-expansion from the same structure is retained.","refs":["CH_MHON_FROM:MRT9","CH_MHON_FROM:A8"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[]}],"original_name":"Lower-Layer vs Current-Level Distinction"},{"number":"5","name":"Global Apophatic Structure","items":[{"id":"CL_MHON_01:GNX-01","text":"The monadic sign is not a conventionally instituted form extrinsic to the thing, but a form to which real and operative force is attributed.","refs":["CH_MHON_FROM:DNX2","CH_MHON_FROM:SNX3"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[]},{"id":"CL_MHON_01:GNX-02","text":"Representation is not a passive imitation of an already completed thing, nor an operation separable from production.","refs":["CH_MHON_THEI:NX4","CH_MHON_THEI:D4"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[]},{"id":"CL_MHON_01:GNX-03","text":"No geometrical figure is autonomous: the Straight Line necessarily implicates the Point, the Circle the Straight Line, and the periphery the center.","refs":["CH_MHON_TH_2:A1","CH_MHON_TH_2:A2","CH_MHON_TH_2:NX3"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[]},{"id":"CL_MHON_01:GNX-04","text":"Plurality and extension cannot surpass the unity of the principle or abolish relation to the Monad.","refs":["CH_MHON_TH_2:DNX3","CH_MHON_TH_2:SNX2"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[]},{"id":"CL_MHON_01:GNX-05","text":"Antiquity, authority, and similarity of word do not constitute intrinsic proof of a thing.","refs":["CH_MHON_FROM:NX4","CH_MHON_FROM:NX6","CH_MHON_FROM:A14"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[]},{"id":"CL_MHON_01:GNX-06","text":"Neither indiscriminate circulation nor an indifferent typographical medium constitutes a legitimate condition of doctrine.","refs":["CH_MHON_FROM:NX7","CH_MHON_FROM:NX8"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[]},{"id":"CL_MHON_01:GNX-07","text":"Disciplines are not regions closed by absolute boundaries, nor can sacred contemplation, natural science, and technical operation be entirely separated.","refs":["CH_MHON_FROM:DNX1","CH_MHON_FROM:SNX4"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[]}],"original_name":"Global Apophatic Structure"},{"number":"6","name":"Deep Book Apophatics","items":[{"id":"CL_MHON_01:DBN-01","text":"It must be false that reality contains an absolutely formless, inaccessible residue situated outside the classes of existence, non-existence, and latency.","refs":["CH_MHON_THEI:SNX1","CH_MHON_THEI:SNX4"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[]},{"id":"CL_MHON_01:DBN-02","text":"It must be false that the order of being is fundamentally separated from the order of signifying and representing.","refs":["CH_MHON_THEI:DNX2","CH_MHON_THEI:SNX2"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[]},{"id":"CL_MHON_01:DBN-03","text":"It must be false that extension, plurality, or periphery can found themselves without an indivisible principle.","refs":["CH_MHON_TH_2:DNX2","CH_MHON_TH_2:DNX4"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[]},{"id":"CL_MHON_01:DBN-04","text":"It must be false that material alteration of point, line, or diagram leaves doctrinal meaning intact.","refs":["CH_MHON_FROM:DNX6","CH_MHON_FROM:SD3"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[]},{"id":"CL_MHON_01:DBN-05","text":"It must be false that the universal presence of signs produces a universal competence in interpretation.","refs":["CH_MHON_FROM:SD4","CH_MHON_FROM:A11"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[]},{"id":"CL_MHON_01:DBN-06","text":"It must be false that abuse, imitation, or corruption of an art removes the existence of a true doctrinal body.","refs":["CH_MHON_FROM:D6","CH_MHON_FROM:DNX8"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[]}],"original_name":"Deep Book Apophatics"},{"number":"7","name":"Super-Apophatics of the Whole","items":[{"id":"CL_MHON_01:BSA-01","text":"The possibility of a world without an originating center, or of an infinite periphery emancipated from the Point, is ontologically inadmissible.","refs":["CH_MHON_TH_2:SNX1","CH_MHON_TH_2:ME3"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[]},{"id":"CL_MHON_01:BSA-02","text":"The system is ordered around the exclusion of the pure exterior: nothing is admitted that is neither existent, nor non-existent, nor latent, nor formally producible.","refs":["CH_MHON_THEI:SNX4","CH_MHON_THEI:AS2"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[]},{"id":"CL_MHON_01:BSA-03","text":"Total perspicuity offered indiscriminately to all is a constitutive absence, because full publicity would dissolve the moral and epistemic order of reception.","refs":["CH_MHON_FROM:SNX1","CH_MHON_FROM:NX7"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[]},{"id":"CL_MHON_01:BSA-04","text":"A tribunal wholly external and lacking knowledge of the thing cannot obtain any ultimate authority.","refs":["CH_MHON_FROM:SNX2","CH_MHON_FROM:A11"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[]},{"id":"CL_MHON_01:BSA-05","text":"The absolute separation of contemplation, nature, and operation remains the impossibility around which the universal power of the Monad is distributed.","refs":["CH_MHON_FROM:SNX4","CH_MHON_FROM:A8"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[]}],"original_name":"Super-Apophatics of the Whole"},{"number":"8","name":"Cross-Unit Derivative Propositions","items":[{"id":"CL_MHON_01:XD-01","text":"The Monad not only compresses already given signs, but through Point, line, center, and circle constitutes the very machinery of production and representation.","refs":["CL_MHON_01:GA-02","CL_MHON_01:GA-03","CL_MHON_01:GA-04","CL_MHON_01:GA-06","CH_MHON_FROM:A3","CH_MHON_THEI:A6","CH_MHON_TH_2:AS5"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"Composite","passages":[]},{"id":"CL_MHON_01:XD-02","text":"Because form brings the thing forth into manifestation, typographical exactitude becomes a continuation of formal causality, not mere editorial fidelity.","refs":["CL_MHON_01:GA-03","CL_MHON_01:GA-08","CH_MHON_FROM:A17","CH_MHON_THEI:D4","CH_MHON_TH_2:A7"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"Composite","passages":[]},{"id":"CL_MHON_01:XD-03","text":"The universality of the system is intensive: the same simple relation pervades creation, all classes of things, and every magnitude of the periphery.","refs":["CL_MHON_01:GA-01","CL_MHON_01:GA-05","CL_MHON_01:GA-09","CH_MHON_FROM:A2","CH_MHON_THEI:AS2","CH_MHON_TH_2:A8"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"Composite","passages":[]},{"id":"CL_MHON_01:XD-04","text":"Concealment bifurcates into a natural state to be overcome and a doctrinal veil to be preserved; the same form therefore manifests the thing and restricts access to it.","refs":["CL_MHON_01:GA-07","CL_MHON_01:GA-11","CH_MHON_FROM:NX9","CH_MHON_THEI:NX5"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"Tension","passages":[]},{"id":"CL_MHON_01:XD-05","text":"Geometrical necessity provides intrinsic proof, whereas royal authority administers only the conditions of external protection and discretion.","refs":["CL_MHON_01:GA-10","CL_MHON_01:GA-11","CH_MHON_FROM:A14","CH_MHON_FROM:A18","CH_MHON_TH_2:AS2"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"Composite","passages":[]},{"id":"CL_MHON_01:XD-06","text":"Plurality is admitted only as an orderly included and centrally dependent distinction, not as an aggregation of autonomous principles.","refs":["CL_MHON_01:GA-05","CL_MHON_01:GA-09","CH_MHON_FROM:A5","CH_MHON_TH_2:A6","CH_MHON_TH_2:SD3"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"Composite","passages":[]},{"id":"CL_MHON_01:XD-07","text":"The designation of line and circle as primary forms becomes a relative priority of manifestation, because the following theorem retroactively subjects both to the Point by reason of the Monad.","refs":["CL_MHON_01:GA-02","CL_MHON_01:GA-04","CL_MHON_01:GA-06","CH_MHON_THEI:AS1","CH_MHON_TH_2:A1","CH_MHON_TH_2:A3"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"Tension","passages":[]},{"id":"CL_MHON_01:XD-08","text":"The inclusion of non-existents in the formal field means that the possibility of manifestation is constituted in the order of form before actual existence is determined.","refs":["CL_MHON_01:GA-03","CL_MHON_01:GA-07","CH_MHON_THEI:A3","CH_MHON_THEI:SD3","CH_MHON_TH_2:A4"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"Tension","passages":[]}],"original_name":"Cross-Unit Derivative Propositions"},{"number":"9","name":"Book-Scale Super-Derivatives","items":[{"id":"CL_MHON_01:BSD-01","text":"To know a thing means to reconstruct its formal dependencies, not to compare an external image with an already completed object.","refs":["CL_MHON_01:GA-03","CL_MHON_01:GA-06","CL_MHON_01:GA-10","CH_MHON_TH_2:D4","CH_MHON_THEI:D4"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"From","passages":[]},{"id":"CL_MHON_01:BSD-02","text":"Causality, ontology, and representation coalesce in one sequence: a simple principle constitutes a figure, the figure manifests a thing, and manifestation renders it knowable.","refs":["CL_MHON_01:GA-03","CL_MHON_01:GA-06","CL_MHON_01:GA-07","CH_MHON_THEI:SD2","CH_MHON_TH_2:SD1"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"From","passages":[]},{"id":"CL_MHON_01:BSD-03","text":"The time of origin becomes recursive, because what first appears as a primary form is later derived from an even simpler principle.","refs":["CL_MHON_01:GA-02","CL_MHON_01:GA-04","CH_MHON_THEI:AS1","CH_MHON_TH_2:A3","CH_MHON_TH_2:MRT5"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"From","passages":[]},{"id":"CL_MHON_01:BSD-04","text":"The knowing subject is not a neutral spectator, but a reader and operator upon whom breadth of knowledge, sincerity, and fitness for reception are imposed.","refs":["CL_MHON_01:GA-07","CL_MHON_01:GA-10","CL_MHON_01:GA-11","CH_MHON_FROM:A11","CH_MHON_FROM:A16","CH_MHON_THEI:A5"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"From","passages":[]},{"id":"CL_MHON_01:BSD-05","text":"Political power can protect legitimate knowledge and repress false assimilations, but cannot produce formal proof.","refs":["CL_MHON_01:GA-10","CL_MHON_01:GA-11","CH_MHON_FROM:A14","CH_MHON_FROM:A18","CH_MHON_FROM:D9"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"From","passages":[]},{"id":"CL_MHON_01:BSD-06","text":"Publication becomes a double gate: the exemplar is materially produced for all, but doctrinal integrity depends simultaneously upon exact form and discriminating reception.","refs":["CL_MHON_01:GA-08","CL_MHON_01:GA-11","CH_MHON_FROM:D8","CH_MHON_FROM:SD4"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"From","passages":[]}],"original_name":"Book-Scale Super-Derivatives"},{"number":"10","name":"Recursive Loops, Escalation Paths, and Conceptual Attractors","items":[{"id":"CL_MHON_01:RLE-01","text":"The compression of signs into the Monad generates re-expansion through the disciplines, whose concordant effects are again referred to the fecundity of the Monad itself.","refs":["CH_MHON_FROM:D3","CH_MHON_FROM:A8"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[]},{"id":"CL_MHON_01:RLE-02","text":"Latency requires a manifesting form, but doctrinal manifestation requires a new veil; revelation and safeguarding mutually reproduce one another.","refs":["CH_MHON_THEI:A4","CH_MHON_THEI:A5","CH_MHON_FROM:NX9"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[]},{"id":"CL_MHON_01:RLE-03","text":"The center constitutes the periphery, the periphery renders the center necessary, and the invariance of this relation through all magnitudes amplifies the primacy of the Monad.","refs":["CH_MHON_TH_2:A6","CH_MHON_TH_2:A8","CH_MHON_TH_2:SD2"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[]},{"id":"CL_MHON_01:RLE-04","text":"The slightest corruption of form engenders mutilation of doctrine, mutilation increases error of reception, and error confirms the need for more exact safeguarding.","refs":["CH_MHON_FROM:A4","CH_MHON_FROM:SD3","CH_MHON_FROM:A16"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[]},{"id":"CL_MHON_01:RLE-05","text":"Knowledge of the magnitude of mysteries constitutes a condition of judgment, but selective circulation restricts the acquisition of this knowledge; competence becomes an attractor both required and safeguarded.","refs":["CH_MHON_FROM:A11","CH_MHON_FROM:SD6"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"Tension","passages":[]},{"id":"CL_MHON_01:RLE-06","text":"The Point-Monad is the most dominant attractor, because it recalls to itself the composition of the character, the origin of being, the center of figures, and the distinction of unity from plurality.","refs":["CH_MHON_TH_2:D4","CH_MHON_TH_2:ME1"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[]},{"id":"CL_MHON_01:RLE-07","text":"Community of terms confuses body and shadow, demonstration distinguishes use and structure, and distinction restores the need for disciplined judgment.","refs":["CH_MHON_FROM:A13","CH_MHON_FROM:D7"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[]}],"original_name":"Recursive Loops, Escalation Paths, and Conceptual Attractors"},{"number":"11","name":"Formal Generative Schema of the Whole Book","items":[{"id":"CL_MHON_01:FGS-B01","text":"God → emanation of visibles and invisibles → creation as writing → world of legible signs.","refs":["CH_MHON_FROM:A1","CH_MHON_FROM:A2"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[]},{"id":"CL_MHON_01:FGS-B02","text":"Dispersed signs → reduction to Point, line, and circle → composition of the exact monadic character.","refs":["CH_MHON_FROM:A3","CH_MHON_FROM:A6"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[]},{"id":"CL_MHON_01:FGS-B03","text":"Line and circle → comprehension of existents, non-existents, and latents → production into light = determinate representation.","refs":["CH_MHON_THEI:AS2","CH_MHON_THEI:A6"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[]},{"id":"CL_MHON_01:FGS-B04","text":"Question of construction → Straight Line to Point → Circle to Straight Line → center to Point → Point to Monad.","refs":["CH_MHON_TH_2:AS2","CH_MHON_TH_2:AS5"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[]},{"id":"CL_MHON_01:FGS-B05","text":"Point-Monad → center → periphery of any magnitude → extended plurality constitutively subordinated to unity.","refs":["CH_MHON_TH_2:A6","CH_MHON_TH_2:A8"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[]},{"id":"CL_MHON_01:FGS-B06","text":"Monadic structure → analogical projections → concordant disciplinary effects → proof of the fecundity of function.","refs":["CH_MHON_FROM:A8","CH_MHON_FROM:D4"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[]},{"id":"CL_MHON_01:FGS-B07","text":"Formal truth → exactly printed exemplar → discriminating reader → legitimate transmission; corruption or promiscuity → error and accusation.","refs":["CH_MHON_FROM:D8","CH_MHON_FROM:A16"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[]}],"original_name":"Formal Generative Schema of the Whole Book"},{"number":"12","name":"Whole-Book Argument Flow Map","items":[{"id":"CL_MHON_01:AFM-B01","text":"Rarity of wisdom → proposition of the Monad → reduction to line and circle → regress to the Point → constitution of the center → universality of monadic dependence.","refs":["CH_MHON_FROM:AS4","CH_MHON_THEI:AS1","CH_MHON_TH_2:AS5","CH_MHON_TH_2:AS3"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[]},{"id":"CL_MHON_01:AFM-B02","text":"Covering of Nature → simplest form → production into light → active representation → reconstructive knowledge.","refs":["CH_MHON_THEI:A4","CH_MHON_THEI:A5","CH_MHON_THEI:D4"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[]},{"id":"CL_MHON_01:AFM-B03","text":"Community of names and figures → confusion of body with shadow → demonstration discerning structure → exact printing → selective circulation.","refs":["CH_MHON_FROM:A13","CH_MHON_FROM:D7","CH_MHON_FROM:D8"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[]}],"original_name":"Whole-Book Argument Flow Map"},{"number":"13","name":"Macro Map-Effects","items":[{"id":"CL_MHON_01:MME-01","text":"Geometry is reclassified from an instrument of description into an ontological and generative principle.","refs":["CH_MHON_THEI:ME3","CH_MHON_TH_2:ME1"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[]},{"id":"CL_MHON_01:MME-02","text":"The opposition between production and representation collapses; signifying becomes a mode of constituting a thing into manifestation.","refs":["CH_MHON_THEI:ME2","CH_MHON_THEI:D4"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[]},{"id":"CL_MHON_01:MME-03","text":"Non-existence and latency are transferred from the exterior of knowledge into modes of not-yet-manifested formability.","refs":["CH_MHON_THEI:A3","CH_MHON_THEI:SD3"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[]},{"id":"CL_MHON_01:MME-04","text":"The opposition of unity and plurality is replaced by the relation of center and periphery, by which variable extension preserves invariant dependence.","refs":["CH_MHON_FROM:A5","CH_MHON_TH_2:ME3"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[]},{"id":"CL_MHON_01:MME-05","text":"The material book passes from an external vehicle into a part of the doctrinal operation.","refs":["CH_MHON_FROM:SD3","CH_MHON_FROM:NX8"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[]},{"id":"CL_MHON_01:MME-06","text":"The opposition between science and magic gives way to the distinction between a demonstrable body and an adulterated shadow that employs common terms.","refs":["CH_MHON_FROM:ME4","CH_MHON_FROM:A12"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[]},{"id":"CL_MHON_01:MME-07","text":"Authority is reclassified: it is not a source of truth, but an instrument for protecting the conditions under which demonstration is correctly received.","refs":["CH_MHON_FROM:D9","CH_MHON_FROM:A14"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[]}],"original_name":"Macro Map-Effects"},{"number":"14","name":"Altitude Integration","items":[{"id":"CL_MHON_01:ALT-B01","text":"At the level of the surface operate the character of the Monad, line, circle, Point, the question of construction, and concern for exact publication.","refs":["CH_MHON_FROM:AS2","CH_MHON_TH_2:AS1"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[]},{"id":"CL_MHON_01:ALT-B02","text":"At the structural level, classes of things and figures are distributed according to ordered dependencies that are reduced from periphery to center and from composite to simple principle.","refs":["CH_MHON_TH_2:D3","CH_MHON_THEI:D2"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[]},{"id":"CL_MHON_01:ALT-B03","text":"At the metastrategic level, the contest over legitimacy is transferred from controversial terms to use, structure, end, and internal demonstration.","refs":["CH_MHON_FROM:D7","CH_MHON_FROM:NX6"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[]},{"id":"CL_MHON_01:ALT-B04","text":"At the ontological and temporal level, reality is emanated writing, latency is a transitory state, and origin is again determined through regresses to simpler principles.","refs":["CH_MHON_FROM:A1","CH_MHON_THEI:D3","CH_MHON_TH_2:D5"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[]},{"id":"CL_MHON_01:ALT-B05","text":"At the methodological and performative level, the compression of the theorem imitates the compression of the Monad, while the exactness of written form effects the doctrine that it affirms concerning necessary signification.","refs":["CH_MHON_FROM:D3","CH_MHON_FROM:A17","CH_MHON_THEI:A6"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[]}],"original_name":"Altitude Integration"},{"number":"15","name":"Internal Tensions, Mutation Points, and Fault Lines","items":[{"id":"CL_MHON_01:FL-01","text":"Line and circle are called primary forms, but the Point by reason of the Monad is later posited as the principle of both and of things themselves; priority therefore oscillates between the level of manifestation and ontological origin.","refs":["CH_MHON_THEI:AS1","CH_MHON_TH_2:A3"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"Tension","passages":[]},{"id":"CL_MHON_01:FL-02","text":"Production and representation are joined in one operation without a criterion by which the constitution of a thing may be distinguished from the constitution of its appearance.","refs":["CH_MHON_THEI:MRT5","CH_MHON_THEI:SD2"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"Tension","passages":[]},{"id":"CL_MHON_01:FL-03","text":"The universality of the writing of creation coheres with the restriction of mysteries to suitable readers only if the presence of the sign is strictly distinguished from the faculty of interpretation.","refs":["CH_MHON_FROM:D5","CH_MHON_FROM:NX7"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"Tension","passages":[]},{"id":"CL_MHON_01:FL-04","text":"The repudiation of human authority and the invocation of royal protection do not directly contradict one another, but the probative end and the political end are easily confused.","refs":["CH_MHON_FROM:A14","CH_MHON_FROM:A18"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"Tension","passages":[]},{"id":"CL_MHON_01:FL-05","text":"Non-existence and latency are brought together under one formative process, although the former can indicate privation of existence and the latter concealment of an already ordered thing.","refs":["CH_MHON_THEI:A3","CH_MHON_THEI:MRT4"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"Tension","passages":[]},{"id":"CL_MHON_01:FL-06","text":"Analogical power through the disciplines sustains the global structure, but particular effects are still proved more by enumeration and promise than by demonstration.","refs":["CH_MHON_FROM:A8","CH_MHON_FROM:MRT9"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"Tension","passages":[]},{"id":"CL_MHON_01:MP-01","text":"Representation changes from a subsequent relation to the thing into an operation that brings the thing forth into light.","refs":["CH_MHON_THEI:A6","CH_MHON_THEI:D4"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"Tension","passages":[]},{"id":"CL_MHON_01:MP-02","text":"The Point changes from a minimal element of geometrical writing into the rationale of the Monad and an ontological principle.","refs":["CH_MHON_FROM:A3","CH_MHON_TH_2:AS3"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"Tension","passages":[]},{"id":"CL_MHON_01:MP-03","text":"Concealment changes from a covering of Nature to be removed into a doctrinal veil deliberately to be preserved.","refs":["CH_MHON_THEI:A4","CH_MHON_FROM:NX9"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"Tension","passages":[]},{"id":"CL_MHON_01:MP-04","text":"The term “first” changes from the order of manifested forms into the order of ontological principle, whence the temporality of origin becomes analogical and stratified.","refs":["CH_MHON_THEI:AS1","CH_MHON_TH_2:A3","CH_MHON_TH_2:MRT5"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"Tension","passages":[]}],"original_name":"Internal Tensions, Mutation Points, and Fault Lines"},{"number":"16","name":"Hidden System Image","items":[{"id":"CL_MHON_01:HSI-01","text":"Reality is implicitly a centered writing-machine, in which the Point unfolds lines and circles, whereas peripheries fold plurality back into the center.","refs":["CH_MHON_FROM:A2","CH_MHON_TH_2:AS5","CH_MHON_TH_2:A6"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[]},{"id":"CL_MHON_01:HSI-02","text":"The subject becomes a reader-constructor who opens the covering through form and discerns the true body from the shadow according to structure.","refs":["CH_MHON_FROM:A11","CH_MHON_THEI:A5","CH_MHON_FROM:D7"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[]},{"id":"CL_MHON_01:HSI-03","text":"Time is a generative regress: the manifest is reduced to latency, the composite to the simple, and what first appears to ontological priority.","refs":["CH_MHON_FROM:AS4","CH_MHON_THEI:AS5","CH_MHON_TH_2:MRT5"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[]},{"id":"CL_MHON_01:HSI-04","text":"The possible contest is not flight from the center, but preservation of the exact channel between principle, figure, and reader against shadows, corruption, and indiscriminate reception.","refs":["CH_MHON_FROM:A12","CH_MHON_FROM:A16","CH_MHON_FROM:D8"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[]}],"original_name":"Hidden System Image"},{"number":"17","name":"Minimal Whole-Book Reconstruction Test","items":[{"id":"CL_MHON_01:MRT-B01","text":"The present axioms suffice to restore the fundamental machinery: writing of creation, simple forms, productive manifestation, regress to the Point-Monad, and safeguarded transmission.","refs":["CH_MHON_FROM:MRT1","CH_MHON_THEI:MRT1","CH_MHON_TH_2:MRT1"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[]},{"id":"CL_MHON_01:MRT-B02","text":"It is not yet sufficiently determined how the identity between geometrical construction, ontological origin, and productive representation is demonstratively constituted.","refs":["CH_MHON_TH_2:MRT2","CH_MHON_THEI:MRT5"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[]},{"id":"CL_MHON_01:MRT-B03","text":"The firmest structure is the invariance of dependence upon the central Point conjoined with the necessity of the material integrity of form.","refs":["CH_MHON_TH_2:A8","CH_MHON_FROM:SD3"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[]},{"id":"CL_MHON_01:MRT-B04","text":"At this magnitude the system coheres insofar as all elements are reduced to one transition between unity, manifestation, and formal dependence.","refs":["CH_MHON_FROM:MRT3","CH_MHON_THEI:MRT2"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[]},{"id":"CL_MHON_01:MRT-B05","text":"Coherence becomes more fragile where competence is presupposed for judgment, the universality of signs is affirmed, and doctrinal access is simultaneously restricted.","refs":["CH_MHON_FROM:MRT5","CH_MHON_FROM:MRT6"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[]},{"id":"CL_MHON_01:MRT-B06","text":"Particular connections between the monadic form and disciplinary effects, as well as the difference between non-existents and latents, still resist axiomatic reduction.","refs":["CH_MHON_FROM:MRT9","CH_MHON_THEI:MRT4"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[]},{"id":"CL_MHON_01:MRT-B07","text":"The restoration preserves the true movement, because it regresses from the announcement of unity through manifesting forms to the central foundation, then returns to exact transmission.","refs":["CH_MHON_FROM:MRT10","CH_MHON_THEI:MRT6","CH_MHON_TH_2:MRT6"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[]}],"original_name":"Minimal Whole-Book Reconstruction Test"}],"revision":"CL_MHON_01@R001","source_variant_id":"canonical:CL_MHON_01:v1","source_language_code":"la","original_title":"Chapters 01–03"}],"identity":{"layer":"CL","kicker":"Cluster · Monas Hieroglyphica","title":"Chapters 01–03","context":"Front matter · Theorem I. · Theorem Ii.","original_title":"Chapters 01–03","original_context":"Front matter · Theorema I. · Theorema Ii."},"original_title":"Chapters 01–03","title_language_code":"en"},{"id":"CL_MHON_02","layer":"CL","title":"Chapters 04–06","order":2,"parents":["CH_MHON_TH_3","CH_MHON_THEO","CH_MHON_TH_4"],"revision":"CL_MHON_02@R001","sha256":"52f459b805edf387f1ff3cfe8c068f5f5444c8a4a170ecc96d6b57a331fa9d2c","sections":[{"number":"1","name":"Book-Scale Argumental Spine","items":[{"id":"CL_MHON_02:BS-01","text":"Quaestio centralis est quomodo figura cosmica, a centro terrestri per dignitatem Solis et dependentiam Lunæ composita, ordinem temporis generare possit.","refs":["CH_MHON_TH_3:AS1","CH_MHON_TH_4:AS1"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[]},{"id":"CL_MHON_02:BS-02","text":"Thesis globalis est quod centrum, principatus Solaris et partialitas Lunaris non tres res separatas constituunt, sed machinam relationalem quæ ex figuris cosmologicis ordinem temporalem et originem Lucis deducit.","refs":["CH_MHON_TH_3:SD1","CH_MHON_TH_4:SD1"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[]},{"id":"CL_MHON_02:BS-03","text":"Argumentatio fit symbolica, cosmologica et reconstructiva: geometria non tantum describit corpora, sed relationes dignitatis, assimilationis, complementi et productionis ordinis efficit.","refs":["CH_MHON_TH_3:AS5","CH_MHON_TH_4:SD4"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[]},{"id":"CL_MHON_02:BS-04","text":"Motus systematis est cumulativus et recursivus: centrum ordinem disponit, Sol mediatur, Luna mutatur, complementum partialitatem claudit, et clausura temporalis novum principium instituit.","refs":["CH_MHON_TH_3:FGS5","CH_MHON_TH_4:FGS6"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[]},{"id":"CL_MHON_02:BS-05","text":"Clausura non est stabilitas immobilis, sed circulatio generativa in qua disparitio ad reditum et dies ad principium ordinis refertur.","refs":["CH_MHON_THEO:D5","CH_MHON_TH_4:A5"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[]}],"original_name":"Book-Scale Argumental Spine"},{"number":"2","name":"Global Load-Bearing Axioms","items":[{"id":"CL_MHON_02:GA-01","text":"[Persistent] Centrum conspicuum non manet figura geometrica, sed terram refert et ordinem corporum circa eam distribuit.","refs":["CH_MHON_TH_3:A1","CH_MHON_TH_3:A2"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[]},{"id":"CL_MHON_02:GA-02","text":"[Persistent] Sol obtinet dignitatem supremam, quæ eius circulum integrum et functionem mediantem inter centrum et Lunam determinat.","refs":["CH_MHON_TH_3:A3","CH_MHON_TH_3:D2"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[]},{"id":"CL_MHON_02:GA-03","text":"[Mutating] Luna non est forma stabilis, sed corpus cuius visibilitas, figura et lumen per relationem ad Solem variant.","refs":["CH_MHON_TH_3:D4","CH_MHON_THEO:SD2"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[]},{"id":"CL_MHON_02:GA-04","text":"[Emergent] Apparens superioritas Lunæ in dependentiam Solariter ordinatam invertitur; quod quasi prius apparet, causaliter post Solem constituitur.","refs":["CH_MHON_THEO:SD1","CH_MHON_THEO:ME1"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[]},{"id":"CL_MHON_02:GA-05","text":"[Persistent] Sol est mediatrix non solum luminis, sed etiam formæ, vicinitatis, assimilationis et mutationis Lunaris.","refs":["CH_MHON_THEO:SD5","CH_MHON_THEO:ME5"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[]},{"id":"CL_MHON_02:GA-06","text":"[Emergent] Complementum Solare partialitatem Lunarem transformat in unitatem constitutivam, ex qua tempus fit legibile.","refs":["CH_MHON_TH_4:D1","CH_MHON_TH_4:D3"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[]},{"id":"CL_MHON_02:GA-07","text":"[Emergent] Vesper et mane non sunt tempora independentia, sed membra unius diei producti per relationem complementariam.","refs":["CH_MHON_TH_4:A4","CH_MHON_TH_4:SD2"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[]},{"id":"CL_MHON_02:GA-08","text":"[Persistent] Lux est principium ordinis, quia dies primus eam non simpliciter continet, sed per eam ontologice instituitur.","refs":["CH_MHON_TH_4:A6","CH_MHON_TH_4:SD3"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[]},{"id":"CL_MHON_02:GA-09","text":"[Mutating] Disparitio Lunaris est status transformationis, non annihilatio; eius absentia in reditum figuræ corniculatæ transit.","refs":["CH_MHON_THEO:A7","CH_MHON_THEO:NX5"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[]},{"id":"CL_MHON_02:GA-10","text":"[Attractor] Relatio completiva attrahit figuram, lumen et tempus ad unitatem in qua cosmologia fit ordo philosophicus.","refs":["CH_MHON_TH_4:SD4","CH_MHON_TH_4:FGS3"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[]}],"original_name":"Global Load-Bearing Axioms"},{"number":"3","name":"Recurrence, Mutation, and Displacement Matrix","items":[{"id":"CL_MHON_02:RMD-01","text":"Centrum identidem redit, sed munus eius mutatur: primum refert terram, deinde ordinem cosmicum continet, postremo ad principium temporale transfertur.","refs":["CH_MHON_TH_3:ME1","CH_MHON_TH_4:SD1"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[]},{"id":"CL_MHON_02:RMD-02","text":"Dignitas Solaris retinetur, sed a proprietate circulari in auctoritatem relationalem et tandem in complementum ordinis temporalis mutatur.","refs":["CH_MHON_TH_3:ME2","CH_MHON_TH_4:A2"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[]},{"id":"CL_MHON_02:RMD-03","text":"Luna e corpore cælesti quasi separato in formam relationalem, deinde in terminum partialem qui complementum requirit, dislocatur.","refs":["CH_MHON_TH_3:ME3","CH_MHON_TH_4:A1"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[]},{"id":"CL_MHON_02:RMD-04","text":"Apparens superioritas non identitatem simpliciter repetit, sed vim invertit: species priora fit indicium subordinationis ad Solem.","refs":["CH_MHON_THEO:SD1","CH_MHON_THEO:D1"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[]},{"id":"CL_MHON_02:RMD-05","text":"Lumen a proprietate recepta in directionem ad Solem, inde in assimilationem, ac demum in principium productionis diei mutatur.","refs":["CH_MHON_TH_3:A9","CH_MHON_TH_4:SD3"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[]},{"id":"CL_MHON_02:RMD-06","text":"Disparitio primum quasi terminus assimilationis apparet, sed per reditum corniculatum in circuitum periodicum absorbetur.","refs":["CH_MHON_THEO:ME4","CH_MHON_THEO:A8"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[]},{"id":"CL_MHON_02:RMD-07","text":"Vesper et mane, initio opposita, in unum diem absorbentur; oppositio temporalis sic fit forma unitatis.","refs":["CH_MHON_TH_4:SD2","CH_MHON_TH_4:NX1"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[]},{"id":"CL_MHON_02:RMD-08","text":"Geometria non evanescit, sed in cosmologiam, temporalitatem et designationem Philosophorum altius codificatur.","refs":["CH_MHON_TH_3:ALT3","CH_MHON_TH_4:SD4"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[]}],"original_name":"Recurrence, Mutation, and Displacement Matrix"},{"number":"4","name":"Lower-Layer vs Current-Level Distinction","items":[{"id":"CL_MHON_02:CGD-01","text":"Localis manet demonstratio quod centrum punctuale terram referat et quod circulus integer Soli attribuatur; cluster autem ex his relationem generativam inter centrum, dignitatem et motum extrahit.","refs":["CH_MHON_TH_3:NX1","CH_MHON_TH_3:ME1"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[]},{"id":"CL_MHON_02:CGD-02","text":"Localis manet explicatio irradiationis et vicinitatis Lunaris; hoc gradu illa fit machina qua forma, visibilitas et tempus inter se conectuntur.","refs":["CH_MHON_TH_3:A10","CH_MHON_THEO:ME2"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[]},{"id":"CL_MHON_02:CGD-03","text":"Unitates coniunguntur cum apparens superioritas Lunæ ad auctoritatem Solis refertur et cum partialitas Lunaris ad complementum Solare transit.","refs":["CH_MHON_THEO:ME1","CH_MHON_TH_4:NX3"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[]},{"id":"CL_MHON_02:CGD-04","text":"Maior transitus fit cum figura Lunaris non amplius solum astronomicam apparentiam, sed productionem diei et ordinis originarii significat.","refs":["CH_MHON_TH_4:ME3","CH_MHON_TH_4:ME4"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[]},{"id":"CL_MHON_02:CGD-05","text":"Formulae de motu, lumine et forma, quæ localiter separari poterant, hoc gradu in unam relationem Solariter mediatam rediguntur.","refs":["CH_MHON_TH_3:D5","CH_MHON_THEO:SD5"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[]},{"id":"CL_MHON_02:CGD-06","text":"Determinatio astronomica exacta superatur ut finis synthesis; retinetur tamen eius ambiguitas, quia nexus geometricus inter figuram et causationem non plene codificatur.","refs":["CH_MHON_TH_3:MRT5","CH_MHON_TH_3:MRT6"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[]}],"original_name":"Lower-Layer vs Current-Level Distinction"},{"number":"5","name":"Global Apophatic Structure","items":[{"id":"CL_MHON_02:GNX-01","text":"Systema excludit cosmologiam in qua centrum, dignitas, motus et lumen inter se disiunguntur.","refs":["CH_MHON_TH_3:SNX1","CH_MHON_TH_3:DNX6"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[]},{"id":"CL_MHON_02:GNX-02","text":"Excluditur Luna autonoma, cuius forma, lumen et visibilitas sine relatione ad Solem explicari possent.","refs":["CH_MHON_TH_3:NX3","CH_MHON_THEO:SNX5"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[]},{"id":"CL_MHON_02:GNX-03","text":"Excluditur forma fixa: reditus, disparitio et corniculata apparentia ostendunt identitatem per mutationem constitui.","refs":["CH_MHON_TH_3:NX5","CH_MHON_THEO:ME3"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[]},{"id":"CL_MHON_02:GNX-04","text":"Excluditur tempus mere lineare, quia principium diei oritur ex coniunctione contrariorum et in circulationem apparentiarum inseritur.","refs":["CH_MHON_THEO:DNX5","CH_MHON_TH_4:SD2"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[]},{"id":"CL_MHON_02:GNX-05","text":"Excluditur philosophicum tamquam nomen extrinsecum; hic enim designat conversionem ordinis cosmici in ordinem temporalem et lucis.","refs":["CH_MHON_TH_4:SNX4","CH_MHON_TH_4:SD4"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[]}],"original_name":"Global Apophatic Structure"},{"number":"6","name":"Deep Book Apophatics","items":[{"id":"CL_MHON_02:DBN-01","text":"Falsum esse oportet corpora cælestia tamquam entia æqualia, isolata et sine hierarchia relationali constitui.","refs":["CH_MHON_TH_3:DNX2","CH_MHON_TH_3:SNX3"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[]},{"id":"CL_MHON_02:DBN-02","text":"Falsum esse oportet tempus ante relationem figuralem iam plene datum esse; dies producitur, non simpliciter invenitur.","refs":["CH_MHON_TH_4:SNX1","CH_MHON_TH_4:D3"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[]},{"id":"CL_MHON_02:DBN-03","text":"Falsum esse oportet absentiam, completionem et transformationem principia separata esse, quia idem cursus Lunaris hæc momenta in unam machinam colligit.","refs":["CH_MHON_THEO:DNX4","CH_MHON_THEO:ME4"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[]}],"original_name":"Deep Book Apophatics"},{"number":"7","name":"Super-Apophatics of the Whole","items":[{"id":"CL_MHON_02:BSA-01","text":"Systema circumit possibilitatem realitatis uniformis, in qua nullum centrum, nulla dignitas et nulla relatio complementaria ordinem producat.","refs":["CH_MHON_TH_3:SNX3","CH_MHON_TH_4:SNX2"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[]},{"id":"CL_MHON_02:BSA-02","text":"Indisponibilis manet autonomia Lunaris, quia Luna semper per Solem, per vicinitatem, per lumen aut per complementum advenit.","refs":["CH_MHON_THEO:SNX5","CH_MHON_THEO:SD5"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[]},{"id":"CL_MHON_02:BSA-03","text":"Absentia quæ totum systema ordinat est separatio absoluta inter figuram, tempus et lucem: cosmologia non potest esse sine eorum coarticulatione.","refs":["CH_MHON_TH_3:SNX4","CH_MHON_TH_4:SNX4"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[]}],"original_name":"Super-Apophatics of the Whole"},{"number":"8","name":"Cross-Unit Derivative Propositions","items":[{"id":"CL_MHON_02:XD-01","text":"Dignitas Solaris, quæ in una unitate circulum integrum constituit et in altera dominationem, in tertia fit operator complementi; sic auctoritas cosmica productionem temporis incipit.","refs":["CL_MHON_02:GA-02","CL_MHON_02:GA-06","CH_MHON_TH_3:D3","CH_MHON_TH_4:SD1"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"Composite","passages":[]},{"id":"CL_MHON_02:XD-02","text":"Apparens superioritas Lunæ et eius partialitas non sunt duæ anomaliæ, sed duæ facies eiusdem inversionis: Luna quasi superius apparet dum structurally complementum requirit.","refs":["CL_MHON_02:GA-04","CL_MHON_02:GA-06","CH_MHON_THEO:SD1","CH_MHON_TH_4:NX2"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"Tension","passages":[]},{"id":"CL_MHON_02:XD-03","text":"Disparitio per assimilationem ad Solem et constitutio diei per Lucem coniunguntur, quia absentia Lunaris et productio ordinis ex una transformatione luminis oriuntur.","refs":["CL_MHON_02:GA-08","CL_MHON_02:GA-09","CH_MHON_THEO:SD3","CH_MHON_TH_4:SD3"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"Composite","passages":[]},{"id":"CL_MHON_02:XD-04","text":"Centrum terrestre et principium temporale in eodem motu relabuntur: punctum quod primum locum corporibus dat postea locum originarium diei continet.","refs":["CL_MHON_02:GA-01","CL_MHON_02:GA-08","CH_MHON_TH_3:ME1","CH_MHON_TH_4:ME3"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"Composite","passages":[]},{"id":"CL_MHON_02:XD-05","text":"Mutatio figuræ Lunaris fit transitus a cosmologia distributiva ad temporalitatem generativam, quia reditus corniculatus et complementum diei unam clausuram non-staticam efficiunt.","refs":["CL_MHON_02:GA-03","CL_MHON_02:GA-07","CL_MHON_02:GA-09","CH_MHON_TH_3:SD3","CH_MHON_TH_4:FGS3"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"Composite","passages":[]}],"original_name":"Cross-Unit Derivative Propositions"},{"number":"9","name":"Book-Scale Super-Derivatives","items":[{"id":"CL_MHON_02:BSD-01","text":"Systema transformat cosmologiam ex tabula corporum in machinam relationum, ubi ens non per formam isolatam sed per dignitatem, vicinitatem et complementum constituitur.","refs":["CL_MHON_02:GA-01","CL_MHON_02:GA-03","CL_MHON_02:GA-06","CH_MHON_TH_3:SD2","CH_MHON_TH_4:SD1"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"Composite","passages":[]},{"id":"CL_MHON_02:BSD-02","text":"Tempus fit effectus ontologicus relationis: dies non mensurat prius constitutum, sed ex completione figurarum et productione Lucis oritur.","refs":["CL_MHON_02:XD-01","CL_MHON_02:GA-08","CH_MHON_TH_4:ME1","CH_MHON_TH_4:D3"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"Composite","passages":[]},{"id":"CL_MHON_02:BSD-03","text":"Subiectum Lunare non amplius substantia spectans, sed trajectoria relationalis fit, quæ per conversionem, assimilationem, absentiam et reditum se constituit.","refs":["CL_MHON_02:GA-03","CL_MHON_02:GA-09","CH_MHON_THEO:SD2","CH_MHON_THEO:ME3"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"Composite","passages":[]},{"id":"CL_MHON_02:BSD-04","text":"Principium Solaris simul politicum, cosmologicum et temporalem potestatem exercet: dominatio convertitur in mediationem, mediatrix autem in ordinis productionem.","refs":["CL_MHON_02:GA-02","CL_MHON_02:XD-01","CH_MHON_TH_3:SD4","CH_MHON_TH_4:SD3"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"Composite","passages":[]},{"id":"CL_MHON_02:BSD-05","text":"Forma et tempus eodem gradu reordinantur: figura non iam stat contra successionem, sed successionem visibilem et structurally necessariam reddit.","refs":["CL_MHON_02:GA-03","CL_MHON_02:GA-07","CH_MHON_TH_3:ME5","CH_MHON_TH_4:ME1"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"Composite","passages":[]}],"original_name":"Book-Scale Super-Derivatives"},{"number":"10","name":"Recursive Loops, Escalation Paths, and Conceptual Attractors","items":[{"id":"CL_MHON_02:RLE-01","text":"Centrum → dignitas Solaris → dependentia Lunaris → mutatio figuræ → complementum → dies → principium Lucis; principium novum iterum ordinem visibilem disponit.","refs":["CH_MHON_TH_3:FGS5","CH_MHON_TH_4:FGS5"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[]},{"id":"CL_MHON_02:RLE-02","text":"Vicinitas auget similitudinem, similitudo assimilationem, assimilatio disparitionem; disparitio tamen reditum gignit, unde ipsa extrema assimilatio circuitum continuat.","refs":["CH_MHON_THEO:FGS3","CH_MHON_THEO:FGS4"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[]},{"id":"CL_MHON_02:RLE-03","text":"Partialitas → complementum → unitas temporalis → novum principium; hic circuitus repetitio non est, quia singula clausura gradum ontologicum addit.","refs":["CH_MHON_TH_4:FGS1","CH_MHON_TH_4:FGS4"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[]},{"id":"CL_MHON_02:RLE-04","text":"Attractor occultus est relatio completiva: corpora, lumina et tempora ad eam convergunt, dum identitas separata paulatim amittitur.","refs":["CH_MHON_TH_4:SNX2","CH_MHON_THEO:SD5"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[]},{"id":"CL_MHON_02:RLE-05","text":"Terminus unius machinae fit initium alterius: reditus Lunaris clausuram imaginis, dies autem clausuram temporis in novam seriem aperit.","refs":["CH_MHON_THEO:D5","CH_MHON_TH_4:A5"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[]}],"original_name":"Recursive Loops, Escalation Paths, and Conceptual Attractors"},{"number":"11","name":"Formal Generative Schema of the Whole Book","items":[{"id":"CL_MHON_02:FGS-B01","text":"Centrum terrestre + dignitas Solaris → distributio corporum + hierarchia cosmica.","refs":["CH_MHON_TH_3:SD1","CH_MHON_TH_3:D1"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[]},{"id":"CL_MHON_02:FGS-B02","text":"Hierarchia Solaris + conversio Lunaris → mutatio luminis + assimilatio + absentia temporalis.","refs":["CH_MHON_TH_3:FGS3","CH_MHON_THEO:FGS3"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[]},{"id":"CL_MHON_02:FGS-B03","text":"Partialitas Lunaris + complementum Solare → coniunctio vesperis et matutini → dies unus → principium Lucis.","refs":["CH_MHON_TH_4:FGS1","CH_MHON_TH_4:FGS3"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[]},{"id":"CL_MHON_02:FGS-B04","text":"Relationes figurales → ordinis temporalizatio → ontogenia diei → philosophica reclassificatio cosmologiæ.","refs":["CH_MHON_TH_4:ME3","CH_MHON_TH_4:FGS6"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[]}],"original_name":"Formal Generative Schema of the Whole Book"},{"number":"12","name":"Whole-Book Argument Flow Map","items":[{"id":"CL_MHON_02:AFM-B01","text":"Centrum terrestre → ordo concentricus → dignitas Solaris → Luna relationalis → mutatio visibilitatis.","refs":["CH_MHON_TH_3:AF1","CH_MHON_TH_3:ME3"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[]},{"id":"CL_MHON_02:AFM-B02","text":"Apparens superioritas Lunæ → inversio hierarchica → solarisatio → disparitio → reditus corniculatus.","refs":["CH_MHON_THEO:AF1","CH_MHON_THEO:AF2"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[]},{"id":"CL_MHON_02:AFM-B03","text":"Partialitas Lunaris → complementum Solare → coniunctio vesperis et matutini → unus dies.","refs":["CH_MHON_TH_4:AF1","CH_MHON_TH_4:FGS2"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[]},{"id":"CL_MHON_02:AFM-B04","text":"Dies primus → productio Lucis → principium ontologicum → ordo Philosophorum.","refs":["CH_MHON_TH_4:FGS4","CH_MHON_TH_4:FGS6"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[]}],"original_name":"Whole-Book Argument Flow Map"},{"number":"13","name":"Macro Map-Effects","items":[{"id":"CL_MHON_02:MME-01","text":"Centrum reclassificatur ab elemento geometrico ad principium distributionis cosmicae et temporalisationis.","refs":["CH_MHON_TH_3:ME1","CH_MHON_TH_4:ME1"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[]},{"id":"CL_MHON_02:MME-02","text":"Luna reclassificatur ab obiecto cælesti ad indicem relationis, cuius identitas per Solem et per temporalem mutationem constituitur.","refs":["CH_MHON_TH_3:ME3","CH_MHON_TH_3:SD2"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[]},{"id":"CL_MHON_02:MME-03","text":"Oppositio inter apparentem superioritatem et realem dependentiam non tollitur, sed fit instrumentum quo systema ordinem suum invertendo explicat.","refs":["CH_MHON_THEO:ME1","CH_MHON_THEO:ALT3"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[]},{"id":"CL_MHON_02:MME-04","text":"Tempus denuo describitur non ut series mensurabilis, sed ut effectus complementi, lucis et coniunctionis oppositorum.","refs":["CH_MHON_TH_4:ME1","CH_MHON_TH_4:SD2"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[]}],"original_name":"Macro Map-Effects"},{"number":"14","name":"Altitude Integration","items":[{"id":"CL_MHON_02:ALT-B01","text":"Gradus superficiei continet punctum, circulum, hemicyclium, radios, disparitionem, complementum et figuram corniculatam tamquam signa mobilia.","refs":["CH_MHON_TH_3:ALT1","CH_MHON_THEO:ALT1"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[]},{"id":"CL_MHON_02:ALT-B02","text":"Gradus structuralis constituit distributionem concentricam, hierarchiam Solaris, dependentiam Lunarem et mechanismum complementi temporalem.","refs":["CH_MHON_TH_3:ALT2"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[]},{"id":"CL_MHON_02:ALT-B03","text":"Gradus metastrategicus figuram geometricam in instrumentum interpretationis convertit, quo dignitas, dependentia et ordo originarius simul leguntur.","refs":["CH_MHON_TH_3:ALT3","CH_MHON_TH_4:ALT3"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[]},{"id":"CL_MHON_02:ALT-B04","text":"Gradus ontologicus et temporalis corpora ponit ut entia relationalia, tempus autem ut effectum productionis Lucis et completionis figuralis.","refs":["CH_MHON_TH_3:ALT4","CH_MHON_TH_4:ALT4"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[]},{"id":"CL_MHON_02:ALT-B05","text":"Gradus methodologicus performativus efficit quod ipsa compositio hieroglyphica relationes suas producit: figura non solum significat ordinem, sed eum legibilem instituit.","refs":["CH_MHON_TH_3:ALT3","CH_MHON_TH_4:SD4"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[]}],"original_name":"Altitude Integration"},{"number":"15","name":"Internal Tensions, Mutation Points, and Fault Lines","items":[{"id":"CL_MHON_02:FL-01","text":"Prima commissura est inter Lunæ apparentem superioritatem et eius Solariter subordinatam causalitatem; systema hanc contradictionem servat ut mechanismum inversionis.","refs":["CH_MHON_THEO:MRT5","CH_MHON_THEO:SD1"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[]},{"id":"CL_MHON_02:FL-02","text":"Altera commissura est inter transformationem quasi totalem in Solem et reversionem ad figuram corniculatam; disparitio hic simul finis et transitus est.","refs":["CH_MHON_THEO:MRT6","CH_MHON_THEO:NX5"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[]},{"id":"CL_MHON_02:FL-03","text":"Tertia commissura est inter figuram symbolicam et causationem physicam: relatio Solariter instituta est, sed mechanismus astronomicus exactus manet incertus.","refs":["CH_MHON_TH_3:MRT6","CH_MHON_THEO:MRT3"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[]},{"id":"CL_MHON_02:FL-04","text":"Quarta commissura est inter diem tamquam ordinem cosmologicum et designationem Philosophorum, cuius sensus ultimus non plene determinatur.","refs":["CH_MHON_TH_4:MRT3","CH_MHON_TH_4:MRT4"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[]},{"id":"CL_MHON_02:MP-01","text":"Punctum mutat munus cum a referente terrestri in principium ordinis temporalis transfertur.","refs":["CH_MHON_TH_3:ME1","CH_MHON_TH_4:ME3"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[]},{"id":"CL_MHON_02:MP-02","text":"Lumen mutat munus cum ab effectu irradiationis Lunaris in originem ontologicam diei convertitur.","refs":["CH_MHON_TH_3:D5","CH_MHON_TH_4:SD3"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[]},{"id":"CL_MHON_02:MP-03","text":"Disparitio mutat statum cum ab absentia quasi terminali in clausuram periodicam et reditum formæ vertitur.","refs":["CH_MHON_THEO:ME4","CH_MHON_THEO:D5"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[]}],"original_name":"Internal Tensions, Mutation Points, and Fault Lines"},{"number":"16","name":"Hidden System Image","items":[{"id":"CL_MHON_02:HSI-01","text":"Systema implicite imaginatur machinam concentricam et completivam, in qua omne elementum suam vim recipit ex positione ad centrum et ad terminum relationalem.","refs":["CH_MHON_TH_3:SD1","CH_MHON_TH_4:NX3"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[]},{"id":"CL_MHON_02:HSI-02","text":"Ens Lunare intra hanc machinam non est substantia clausa, sed trajectoria quæ inter similitudinem, absentiam et reditum navigat.","refs":["CH_MHON_THEO:SD2","CH_MHON_THEO:ALT4"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[]},{"id":"CL_MHON_02:HSI-03","text":"Tempus apparet ut circulus transformativus, non ut linea: vesper et mane uniuntur, Luna disparit et redit, dies autem novum principium aperit.","refs":["CH_MHON_THEO:SD4","CH_MHON_TH_4:ME2"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[]},{"id":"CL_MHON_02:HSI-04","text":"Potentia agendi in systemate non est autonomia, sed conversio ad relationem dominativam quæ formam, lumen et ordinem generat.","refs":["CH_MHON_THEO:A5","CH_MHON_THEO:ME5"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[]}],"original_name":"Hidden System Image"},{"number":"17","name":"Minimal Whole-Book Reconstruction Test","items":[{"id":"CL_MHON_02:MRT-B01","text":"Axiomata praesentia sufficiunt ad restituendam machinam a centro terrestri per dignitatem Solaris et transformationem Lunarem ad complementum, diem et Lucis principium.","refs":["CH_MHON_TH_3:MRT1","CH_MHON_TH_4:MRT1"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[]},{"id":"CL_MHON_02:MRT-B02","text":"Systema hac magnitudine cohaeret in structura relationali et recursiva; nondum satis determinantur mechanismus astronomicus exactus, sensus Philosophorum et limites causalitatis physicæ.","refs":["CH_MHON_TH_3:MRT5","CH_MHON_TH_4:MRT5"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[]},{"id":"CL_MHON_02:MRT-B03","text":"Restitutio servat verum motum: non a figura fixa ad doctrinam linearem, sed a centro ad mediationem, a mediatione ad transformationem, a transformatione ad complementum et a complemento ad novum principium procedit.","refs":["CH_MHON_TH_3:MRT6","CH_MHON_TH_4:MRT6"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[]}],"original_name":"Minimal Whole-Book Reconstruction Test"}],"language_code":"la","language_name":"Latina","translations":[{"id":"CAREVAR_20261006T223141191575Z_67ce6943","language_code":"en","language_name":"English","title":"Chapters 04–06","source_sha256":"52f459b805edf387f1ff3cfe8c068f5f5444c8a4a170ecc96d6b57a331fa9d2c","sha256":"f693be5de4a91a4f82675aba49d7ba8e01764c860740b1856b87beae4e2c9faf","rendition_type":"machine","review_status":"machine","model":"gpt-5.6-terra","created":"2026-10-06T22:31:41.193347+00:00","sections":[{"number":"1","name":"Book-Scale Argumental Spine","items":[{"id":"CL_MHON_02:BS-01","text":"The central question is how a cosmic figure, composed from the terrestrial center through the dignity of the Sun and the dependence of the Moon, can generate the order of time.","refs":["CH_MHON_TH_3:AS1","CH_MHON_TH_4:AS1"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[]},{"id":"CL_MHON_02:BS-02","text":"The global thesis is that the center, Solar primacy, and Lunar partiality do not constitute three separate things, but a relational machine that derives temporal order and the origin of Light from cosmological figures.","refs":["CH_MHON_TH_3:SD1","CH_MHON_TH_4:SD1"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[]},{"id":"CL_MHON_02:BS-03","text":"The argument proceeds symbolically, cosmologically, and reconstructively: geometry does not merely describe bodies, but effects relations of dignity, assimilation, complement, and the production of order.","refs":["CH_MHON_TH_3:AS5","CH_MHON_TH_4:SD4"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[]},{"id":"CL_MHON_02:BS-04","text":"The movement of the system is cumulative and recursive: the center disposes order, the Sun mediates, the Moon changes, complement closes partiality, and temporal closure establishes a new principle.","refs":["CH_MHON_TH_3:FGS5","CH_MHON_TH_4:FGS6"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[]},{"id":"CL_MHON_02:BS-05","text":"Closure is not immobile stability, but generative circulation in which disappearance is referred to return and days to the principle of order.","refs":["CH_MHON_THEO:D5","CH_MHON_TH_4:A5"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[]}],"original_name":"Book-Scale Argumental Spine"},{"number":"2","name":"Global Load-Bearing Axioms","items":[{"id":"CL_MHON_02:GA-01","text":"[Persistent] The conspicuous center does not remain a geometrical figure, but refers to the earth and distributes the order of bodies around it.","refs":["CH_MHON_TH_3:A1","CH_MHON_TH_3:A2"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[]},{"id":"CL_MHON_02:GA-02","text":"[Persistent] The Sun holds the highest dignity, which determines its complete circle and mediating function between the center and the Moon.","refs":["CH_MHON_TH_3:A3","CH_MHON_TH_3:D2"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[]},{"id":"CL_MHON_02:GA-03","text":"[Mutating] The Moon is not a stable form, but a body whose visibility, figure, and light vary through its relation to the Sun.","refs":["CH_MHON_TH_3:D4","CH_MHON_THEO:SD2"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[]},{"id":"CL_MHON_02:GA-04","text":"[Emergent] The apparent superiority of the Moon is inverted into Solar-ordered dependence; what appears as if prior is causally constituted after the Sun.","refs":["CH_MHON_THEO:SD1","CH_MHON_THEO:ME1"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[]},{"id":"CL_MHON_02:GA-05","text":"[Persistent] The Sun is mediatrix not only of light, but also of Lunar form, proximity, assimilation, and change.","refs":["CH_MHON_THEO:SD5","CH_MHON_THEO:ME5"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[]},{"id":"CL_MHON_02:GA-06","text":"[Emergent] Solar complement transforms Lunar partiality into a constitutive unity from which time becomes legible.","refs":["CH_MHON_TH_4:D1","CH_MHON_TH_4:D3"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[]},{"id":"CL_MHON_02:GA-07","text":"[Emergent] Evening and morning are not independent times, but members of one day produced through a complementary relation.","refs":["CH_MHON_TH_4:A4","CH_MHON_TH_4:SD2"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[]},{"id":"CL_MHON_02:GA-08","text":"[Persistent] Light is the principle of order, because the first day does not merely contain it, but is ontologically established through it.","refs":["CH_MHON_TH_4:A6","CH_MHON_TH_4:SD3"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[]},{"id":"CL_MHON_02:GA-09","text":"[Mutating] Lunar disappearance is a state of transformation, not annihilation; its absence passes into the return of the horned figure.","refs":["CH_MHON_THEO:A7","CH_MHON_THEO:NX5"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[]},{"id":"CL_MHON_02:GA-10","text":"[Attractor] The complementary relation draws figure, light, and time toward a unity in which cosmology becomes philosophical order.","refs":["CH_MHON_TH_4:SD4","CH_MHON_TH_4:FGS3"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[]}],"original_name":"Global Load-Bearing Axioms"},{"number":"3","name":"Recurrence, Mutation, and Displacement Matrix","items":[{"id":"CL_MHON_02:RMD-01","text":"The center repeatedly returns, but its function changes: first it refers to the earth, then it contains cosmic order, and finally it is transferred to the temporal principle.","refs":["CH_MHON_TH_3:ME1","CH_MHON_TH_4:SD1"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[]},{"id":"CL_MHON_02:RMD-02","text":"Solar dignity is retained, but changes from a circular property into relational authority and finally into the complement of temporal order.","refs":["CH_MHON_TH_3:ME2","CH_MHON_TH_4:A2"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[]},{"id":"CL_MHON_02:RMD-03","text":"The Moon is displaced from a quasi-separate celestial body into a relational form, then into a partial term requiring complement.","refs":["CH_MHON_TH_3:ME3","CH_MHON_TH_4:A1"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[]},{"id":"CL_MHON_02:RMD-04","text":"Apparent superiority does not merely repeat identity, but inverts force: a prior appearance becomes an indication of subordination to the Sun.","refs":["CH_MHON_THEO:SD1","CH_MHON_THEO:D1"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[]},{"id":"CL_MHON_02:RMD-05","text":"Light changes from a received property into orientation toward the Sun, thence into assimilation, and finally into the principle of the production of day.","refs":["CH_MHON_TH_3:A9","CH_MHON_TH_4:SD3"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[]},{"id":"CL_MHON_02:RMD-06","text":"Disappearance first appears as if the terminus of assimilation, but through horned return it is absorbed into a periodic circuit.","refs":["CH_MHON_THEO:ME4","CH_MHON_THEO:A8"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[]},{"id":"CL_MHON_02:RMD-07","text":"Evening and morning, initially opposites, are absorbed into one day; temporal opposition thus becomes a form of unity.","refs":["CH_MHON_TH_4:SD2","CH_MHON_TH_4:NX1"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[]},{"id":"CL_MHON_02:RMD-08","text":"Geometry does not vanish, but is more deeply codified into cosmology, temporality, and the designation of the Philosophers.","refs":["CH_MHON_TH_3:ALT3","CH_MHON_TH_4:SD4"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[]}],"original_name":"Recurrence, Mutation, and Displacement Matrix"},{"number":"4","name":"Lower-Layer vs Current-Level Distinction","items":[{"id":"CL_MHON_02:CGD-01","text":"The local demonstration that the point-like center refers to the earth and that the complete circle is attributed to the Sun remains; the cluster, however, derives from these a generative relation among center, dignity, and motion.","refs":["CH_MHON_TH_3:NX1","CH_MHON_TH_3:ME1"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[]},{"id":"CL_MHON_02:CGD-02","text":"The local explanation of Lunar irradiation and proximity remains; at this level it becomes the machine by which form, visibility, and time are interconnected.","refs":["CH_MHON_TH_3:A10","CH_MHON_THEO:ME2"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[]},{"id":"CL_MHON_02:CGD-03","text":"The units are joined when the apparent superiority of the Moon is referred to the authority of the Sun and when Lunar partiality passes to Solar complement.","refs":["CH_MHON_THEO:ME1","CH_MHON_TH_4:NX3"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[]},{"id":"CL_MHON_02:CGD-04","text":"The greater transition occurs when the Lunar figure signifies no longer merely an astronomical appearance, but the production of day and of originary order.","refs":["CH_MHON_TH_4:ME3","CH_MHON_TH_4:ME4"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[]},{"id":"CL_MHON_02:CGD-05","text":"Formulations concerning motion, light, and form, which could locally be separated, are at this level reduced to one Solar-mediated relation.","refs":["CH_MHON_TH_3:D5","CH_MHON_THEO:SD5"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[]},{"id":"CL_MHON_02:CGD-06","text":"Exact astronomical determination is surpassed as the end of the synthesis; yet its ambiguity is retained, because the geometrical nexus between figure and causation is not fully codified.","refs":["CH_MHON_TH_3:MRT5","CH_MHON_TH_3:MRT6"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[]}],"original_name":"Lower-Layer vs Current-Level Distinction"},{"number":"5","name":"Global Apophatic Structure","items":[{"id":"CL_MHON_02:GNX-01","text":"The system excludes a cosmology in which center, dignity, motion, and light are mutually disconnected.","refs":["CH_MHON_TH_3:SNX1","CH_MHON_TH_3:DNX6"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[]},{"id":"CL_MHON_02:GNX-02","text":"An autonomous Moon is excluded, whose form, light, and visibility could be explained without relation to the Sun.","refs":["CH_MHON_TH_3:NX3","CH_MHON_THEO:SNX5"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[]},{"id":"CL_MHON_02:GNX-03","text":"A fixed form is excluded: return, disappearance, and horned appearance show that identity is constituted through change.","refs":["CH_MHON_TH_3:NX5","CH_MHON_THEO:ME3"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[]},{"id":"CL_MHON_02:GNX-04","text":"Merely linear time is excluded, because the principle of day arises from the conjunction of opposites and is inserted into the circulation of appearances.","refs":["CH_MHON_THEO:DNX5","CH_MHON_TH_4:SD2"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[]},{"id":"CL_MHON_02:GNX-05","text":"The philosophical as an extrinsic name is excluded; for here it designates the conversion of cosmic order into temporal order and order of light.","refs":["CH_MHON_TH_4:SNX4","CH_MHON_TH_4:SD4"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[]}],"original_name":"Global Apophatic Structure"},{"number":"6","name":"Deep Book Apophatics","items":[{"id":"CL_MHON_02:DBN-01","text":"It must be false that celestial bodies are constituted as equal, isolated entities without relational hierarchy.","refs":["CH_MHON_TH_3:DNX2","CH_MHON_TH_3:SNX3"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[]},{"id":"CL_MHON_02:DBN-02","text":"It must be false that time is already fully given before figural relation; the day is produced, not merely found.","refs":["CH_MHON_TH_4:SNX1","CH_MHON_TH_4:D3"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[]},{"id":"CL_MHON_02:DBN-03","text":"It must be false that absence, completion, and transformation are separate principles, because the same Lunar course gathers these moments into one machine.","refs":["CH_MHON_THEO:DNX4","CH_MHON_THEO:ME4"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[]}],"original_name":"Deep Book Apophatics"},{"number":"7","name":"Super-Apophatics of the Whole","items":[{"id":"CL_MHON_02:BSA-01","text":"The system circumvents the possibility of a uniform reality in which no center, no dignity, and no complementary relation produces order.","refs":["CH_MHON_TH_3:SNX3","CH_MHON_TH_4:SNX2"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[]},{"id":"CL_MHON_02:BSA-02","text":"Lunar autonomy remains unavailable, because the Moon always comes through the Sun, through proximity, through light, or through complement.","refs":["CH_MHON_THEO:SNX5","CH_MHON_THEO:SD5"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[]},{"id":"CL_MHON_02:BSA-03","text":"The absence ordering the whole system is absolute separation between figure, time, and light: cosmology cannot exist without their co-articulation.","refs":["CH_MHON_TH_3:SNX4","CH_MHON_TH_4:SNX4"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[]}],"original_name":"Super-Apophatics of the Whole"},{"number":"8","name":"Cross-Unit Derivative Propositions","items":[{"id":"CL_MHON_02:XD-01","text":"Solar dignity, which in one unit constitutes the complete circle and in another domination, becomes in a third the operator of complement; thus cosmic authority initiates the production of time.","refs":["CL_MHON_02:GA-02","CL_MHON_02:GA-06","CH_MHON_TH_3:D3","CH_MHON_TH_4:SD1"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"Composite","passages":[]},{"id":"CL_MHON_02:XD-02","text":"The Moon's apparent superiority and its partiality are not two anomalies, but two faces of the same inversion: the Moon appears as if higher while structurally requiring complement.","refs":["CL_MHON_02:GA-04","CL_MHON_02:GA-06","CH_MHON_THEO:SD1","CH_MHON_TH_4:NX2"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"Tension","passages":[]},{"id":"CL_MHON_02:XD-03","text":"Disappearance through assimilation to the Sun and the constitution of day through Light are joined, because Lunar absence and the production of order arise from one transformation of light.","refs":["CL_MHON_02:GA-08","CL_MHON_02:GA-09","CH_MHON_THEO:SD3","CH_MHON_TH_4:SD3"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"Composite","passages":[]},{"id":"CL_MHON_02:XD-04","text":"The terrestrial center and the temporal principle fall back into the same movement: the point that first gives place to bodies later contains the originary place of day.","refs":["CL_MHON_02:GA-01","CL_MHON_02:GA-08","CH_MHON_TH_3:ME1","CH_MHON_TH_4:ME3"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"Composite","passages":[]},{"id":"CL_MHON_02:XD-05","text":"The change of Lunar figure becomes the transition from distributive cosmology to generative temporality, because horned return and the complement of day effect one non-static closure.","refs":["CL_MHON_02:GA-03","CL_MHON_02:GA-07","CL_MHON_02:GA-09","CH_MHON_TH_3:SD3","CH_MHON_TH_4:FGS3"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"Composite","passages":[]}],"original_name":"Cross-Unit Derivative Propositions"},{"number":"9","name":"Book-Scale Super-Derivatives","items":[{"id":"CL_MHON_02:BSD-01","text":"The system transforms cosmology from a table of bodies into a machine of relations, where being is constituted not through isolated form but through dignity, proximity, and complement.","refs":["CL_MHON_02:GA-01","CL_MHON_02:GA-03","CL_MHON_02:GA-06","CH_MHON_TH_3:SD2","CH_MHON_TH_4:SD1"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"Composite","passages":[]},{"id":"CL_MHON_02:BSD-02","text":"Time becomes an ontological effect of relation: the day does not measure what was previously constituted, but arises from the completion of figures and the production of Light.","refs":["CL_MHON_02:XD-01","CL_MHON_02:GA-08","CH_MHON_TH_4:ME1","CH_MHON_TH_4:D3"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"Composite","passages":[]},{"id":"CL_MHON_02:BSD-03","text":"The Lunar subject becomes no longer a beholding substance, but a relational trajectory that constitutes itself through conversion, assimilation, absence, and return.","refs":["CL_MHON_02:GA-03","CL_MHON_02:GA-09","CH_MHON_THEO:SD2","CH_MHON_THEO:ME3"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"Composite","passages":[]},{"id":"CL_MHON_02:BSD-04","text":"The Solar principle exercises at once political, cosmological, and temporal power: domination is converted into mediation, and mediatrix into the production of order.","refs":["CL_MHON_02:GA-02","CL_MHON_02:XD-01","CH_MHON_TH_3:SD4","CH_MHON_TH_4:SD3"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"Composite","passages":[]},{"id":"CL_MHON_02:BSD-05","text":"Form and time are reordered at the same level: figure no longer stands against succession, but makes succession visible and structurally necessary.","refs":["CL_MHON_02:GA-03","CL_MHON_02:GA-07","CH_MHON_TH_3:ME5","CH_MHON_TH_4:ME1"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"Composite","passages":[]}],"original_name":"Book-Scale Super-Derivatives"},{"number":"10","name":"Recursive Loops, Escalation Paths, and Conceptual Attractors","items":[{"id":"CL_MHON_02:RLE-01","text":"Center → Solar dignity → Lunar dependence → change of figure → complement → day → principle of Light; the new principle again disposes visible order.","refs":["CH_MHON_TH_3:FGS5","CH_MHON_TH_4:FGS5"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[]},{"id":"CL_MHON_02:RLE-02","text":"Proximity increases similarity, similarity assimilation, assimilation disappearance; yet disappearance generates return, whence even the utmost assimilation continues the circuit.","refs":["CH_MHON_THEO:FGS3","CH_MHON_THEO:FGS4"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[]},{"id":"CL_MHON_02:RLE-03","text":"Partiality → complement → temporal unity → new principle; this circuit is not repetition, because each closure adds an ontological degree.","refs":["CH_MHON_TH_4:FGS1","CH_MHON_TH_4:FGS4"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[]},{"id":"CL_MHON_02:RLE-04","text":"The hidden attractor is the complementary relation: bodies, lights, and times converge toward it, while separate identity is gradually lost.","refs":["CH_MHON_TH_4:SNX2","CH_MHON_THEO:SD5"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[]},{"id":"CL_MHON_02:RLE-05","text":"The terminus of one machine becomes the beginning of another: Lunar return opens the closure of the image, while day opens the closure of time into a new series.","refs":["CH_MHON_THEO:D5","CH_MHON_TH_4:A5"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[]}],"original_name":"Recursive Loops, Escalation Paths, and Conceptual Attractors"},{"number":"11","name":"Formal Generative Schema of the Whole Book","items":[{"id":"CL_MHON_02:FGS-B01","text":"Terrestrial center + Solar dignity → distribution of bodies + cosmic hierarchy.","refs":["CH_MHON_TH_3:SD1","CH_MHON_TH_3:D1"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[]},{"id":"CL_MHON_02:FGS-B02","text":"Solar hierarchy + Lunar conversion → change of light + assimilation + temporal absence.","refs":["CH_MHON_TH_3:FGS3","CH_MHON_THEO:FGS3"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[]},{"id":"CL_MHON_02:FGS-B03","text":"Lunar partiality + Solar complement → conjunction of evening and morning → one day → principle of Light.","refs":["CH_MHON_TH_4:FGS1","CH_MHON_TH_4:FGS3"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[]},{"id":"CL_MHON_02:FGS-B04","text":"Figural relations → temporalization of order → ontogeny of day → philosophical reclassification of cosmology.","refs":["CH_MHON_TH_4:ME3","CH_MHON_TH_4:FGS6"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[]}],"original_name":"Formal Generative Schema of the Whole Book"},{"number":"12","name":"Whole-Book Argument Flow Map","items":[{"id":"CL_MHON_02:AFM-B01","text":"Terrestrial center → concentric order → Solar dignity → relational Moon → change of visibility.","refs":["CH_MHON_TH_3:AF1","CH_MHON_TH_3:ME3"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[]},{"id":"CL_MHON_02:AFM-B02","text":"Apparent superiority of the Moon → hierarchical inversion → solarization → disappearance → horned return.","refs":["CH_MHON_THEO:AF1","CH_MHON_THEO:AF2"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[]},{"id":"CL_MHON_02:AFM-B03","text":"Lunar partiality → Solar complement → conjunction of evening and morning → one day.","refs":["CH_MHON_TH_4:AF1","CH_MHON_TH_4:FGS2"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[]},{"id":"CL_MHON_02:AFM-B04","text":"First day → production of Light → ontological principle → order of the Philosophers.","refs":["CH_MHON_TH_4:FGS4","CH_MHON_TH_4:FGS6"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[]}],"original_name":"Whole-Book Argument Flow Map"},{"number":"13","name":"Macro Map-Effects","items":[{"id":"CL_MHON_02:MME-01","text":"The center is reclassified from a geometrical element into a principle of cosmic distribution and temporalization.","refs":["CH_MHON_TH_3:ME1","CH_MHON_TH_4:ME1"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[]},{"id":"CL_MHON_02:MME-02","text":"The Moon is reclassified from a celestial object into an index of relation, whose identity is constituted through the Sun and through temporal change.","refs":["CH_MHON_TH_3:ME3","CH_MHON_TH_3:SD2"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[]},{"id":"CL_MHON_02:MME-03","text":"The opposition between apparent superiority and real dependence is not removed, but becomes the instrument by which the system explains its order through inversion.","refs":["CH_MHON_THEO:ME1","CH_MHON_THEO:ALT3"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[]},{"id":"CL_MHON_02:MME-04","text":"Time is redescribed not as a measurable series, but as an effect of complement, light, and the conjunction of opposites.","refs":["CH_MHON_TH_4:ME1","CH_MHON_TH_4:SD2"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[]}],"original_name":"Macro Map-Effects"},{"number":"14","name":"Altitude Integration","items":[{"id":"CL_MHON_02:ALT-B01","text":"The surface level contains the point, circle, semicircle, rays, disappearance, complement, and horned figure as mobile signs.","refs":["CH_MHON_TH_3:ALT1","CH_MHON_THEO:ALT1"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[]},{"id":"CL_MHON_02:ALT-B02","text":"The structural level constitutes concentric distribution, Solar hierarchy, Lunar dependence, and the temporal mechanism of complement.","refs":["CH_MHON_TH_3:ALT2"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[]},{"id":"CL_MHON_02:ALT-B03","text":"The metastrategic level converts geometrical figure into an instrument of interpretation, through which dignity, dependence, and originary order are read together.","refs":["CH_MHON_TH_3:ALT3","CH_MHON_TH_4:ALT3"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[]},{"id":"CL_MHON_02:ALT-B04","text":"The ontological and temporal level posits bodies as relational beings, but time as the effect of the production of Light and figural completion.","refs":["CH_MHON_TH_3:ALT4","CH_MHON_TH_4:ALT4"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[]},{"id":"CL_MHON_02:ALT-B05","text":"The performative methodological level effects that the hieroglyphic composition itself produces its relations: figure does not merely signify order, but establishes it as legible.","refs":["CH_MHON_TH_3:ALT3","CH_MHON_TH_4:SD4"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[]}],"original_name":"Altitude Integration"},{"number":"15","name":"Internal Tensions, Mutation Points, and Fault Lines","items":[{"id":"CL_MHON_02:FL-01","text":"The first juncture is between the Moon's apparent superiority and its Solar-subordinated causality; the system retains this contradiction as a mechanism of inversion.","refs":["CH_MHON_THEO:MRT5","CH_MHON_THEO:SD1"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[]},{"id":"CL_MHON_02:FL-02","text":"The second juncture is between quasi-total transformation into the Sun and reversion to the horned figure; disappearance here is simultaneously end and transition.","refs":["CH_MHON_THEO:MRT6","CH_MHON_THEO:NX5"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[]},{"id":"CL_MHON_02:FL-03","text":"The third juncture is between symbolic figure and physical causation: the relation is Solar-established, but the exact astronomical mechanism remains uncertain.","refs":["CH_MHON_TH_3:MRT6","CH_MHON_THEO:MRT3"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[]},{"id":"CL_MHON_02:FL-04","text":"The fourth juncture is between day as cosmological order and the designation of the Philosophers, whose ultimate sense is not fully determined.","refs":["CH_MHON_TH_4:MRT3","CH_MHON_TH_4:MRT4"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[]},{"id":"CL_MHON_02:MP-01","text":"The point changes function when it is transferred from a terrestrial referent into the principle of temporal order.","refs":["CH_MHON_TH_3:ME1","CH_MHON_TH_4:ME3"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[]},{"id":"CL_MHON_02:MP-02","text":"Light changes function when it is converted from the effect of Lunar irradiation into the ontological origin of day.","refs":["CH_MHON_TH_3:D5","CH_MHON_TH_4:SD3"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[]},{"id":"CL_MHON_02:MP-03","text":"Disappearance changes status when it is turned from a quasi-terminal absence into periodic closure and return of form.","refs":["CH_MHON_THEO:ME4","CH_MHON_THEO:D5"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[]}],"original_name":"Internal Tensions, Mutation Points, and Fault Lines"},{"number":"16","name":"Hidden System Image","items":[{"id":"CL_MHON_02:HSI-01","text":"The system implicitly imagines a concentric and complementary machine, in which every element receives its force from its position relative to the center and to the relational terminus.","refs":["CH_MHON_TH_3:SD1","CH_MHON_TH_4:NX3"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[]},{"id":"CL_MHON_02:HSI-02","text":"The Lunar being within this machine is not a closed substance, but a trajectory that navigates among similarity, absence, and return.","refs":["CH_MHON_THEO:SD2","CH_MHON_THEO:ALT4"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[]},{"id":"CL_MHON_02:HSI-03","text":"Time appears as a transformative circle, not as a line: evening and morning are united, the Moon disappears and returns, while day opens a new principle.","refs":["CH_MHON_THEO:SD4","CH_MHON_TH_4:ME2"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[]},{"id":"CL_MHON_02:HSI-04","text":"The power of acting in the system is not autonomy, but conversion toward a dominant relation that generates form, light, and order.","refs":["CH_MHON_THEO:A5","CH_MHON_THEO:ME5"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[]}],"original_name":"Hidden System Image"},{"number":"17","name":"Minimal Whole-Book Reconstruction Test","items":[{"id":"CL_MHON_02:MRT-B01","text":"The present axioms suffice to restore the machine from the terrestrial center through Solar dignity and Lunar transformation to complement, day, and the principle of Light.","refs":["CH_MHON_TH_3:MRT1","CH_MHON_TH_4:MRT1"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[]},{"id":"CL_MHON_02:MRT-B02","text":"The system coheres at this magnitude in relational and recursive structure; the exact astronomical mechanism, the sense of the Philosophers, and the limits of physical causality are not yet sufficiently determined.","refs":["CH_MHON_TH_3:MRT5","CH_MHON_TH_4:MRT5"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[]},{"id":"CL_MHON_02:MRT-B03","text":"The reconstruction preserves the true movement: it proceeds not from fixed figure to linear doctrine, but from center to mediation, from mediation to transformation, from transformation to complement, and from complement to a new principle.","refs":["CH_MHON_TH_3:MRT6","CH_MHON_TH_4:MRT6"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[]}],"original_name":"Minimal Whole-Book Reconstruction Test"}],"revision":"CL_MHON_02@R001","source_variant_id":"canonical:CL_MHON_02:v1","source_language_code":"la","original_title":"Chapters 04–06"}],"identity":{"layer":"CL","kicker":"Cluster · Monas Hieroglyphica","title":"Chapters 04–06","context":"Theorem Iii. · T H E O R E M Iiii. · T H E O R E M V.","original_title":"Chapters 04–06","original_context":"Theorema Iii. · T H E O R E M A Iiii. · T H E O R E M A V."},"original_title":"Chapters 04–06","title_language_code":"en"},{"id":"CL_MHON_03","layer":"CL","title":"Chapters 07–09","order":3,"parents":["CH_MHON_TH_5","CH_MHON_TH_6","CH_MHON_THEV"],"revision":"CL_MHON_03@R001","sha256":"d6732a7fd87c8c28ced66921a1eada671a90a7596a5ce91c8beaf9042c41990e","sections":[{"number":"1","name":"Book-Scale Argumental Spine","items":[{"id":"CL_MHON_03:SP-01","text":"Quaestio centralis est quomodo figurae rectilineae, puncta, motus et numeri in unum ordinem symbolicum convertantur.","refs":["CH_MHON_TH_5:AS1","CH_MHON_THEV:AS1"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"Composite","passages":[]},{"id":"CL_MHON_03:SP-02","text":"Thesis centralis est geometriam non ut formam staticam, sed ut machinam translationis inter restitutionem naturalem, fluxum physicum, numerationem et significationem magicam agere.","refs":["CH_MHON_TH_6:AS4","CH_MHON_TH_6:SD1"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"Composite","passages":[]},{"id":"CL_MHON_03:SP-03","text":"Argumentatio reconstructivo-symbolica a dislocatione ad reditum, a puncto ad lineam, a quaternario ad denarium et a figura ad locum doctrinalem progreditur.","refs":["CH_MHON_TH_5:AS5","CH_MHON_TH_6:AS7"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"Composite","passages":[]},{"id":"CL_MHON_03:SP-04","text":"Motus systematis est recursivus et cumulativus, quia eadem relatio inter punctum, lineam, numerum et figuram in campos physicos, geometricos et doctrinales migrat.","refs":["CH_MHON_TH_6:SD5","CH_MHON_THEV:SD1"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"Composite","passages":[]},{"id":"CL_MHON_03:SP-05","text":"Quaternarius non terminat motum, sed eum amplificat in denarium, crucem, septenarium et alias ordinationes symbolicas.","refs":["CH_MHON_TH_6:AS6","CH_MHON_THEV:ME1"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"Composite","passages":[]},{"id":"CL_MHON_03:SP-06","text":"Clausura systematis est symbolica potius quam deductiva: formae convergunt in unam seriem, dum quaedam relationes, praesertim octonarii et ternarii ad septenarium, formaliter incompletae manent.","refs":["CH_MHON_TH_5:SNX3","CH_MHON_THEV:MRT3"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"Tension","passages":[]}],"original_name":"Book-Scale Argumental Spine"},{"number":"2","name":"Global Load-Bearing Axioms","items":[{"id":"CL_MHON_03:GA-01","text":"[Persistent] Figura rectilinea potest simul rationem numericam et significationem Hieroglyphicam sustinere.","refs":["CH_MHON_TH_5:A1"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[]},{"id":"CL_MHON_03:GA-02","text":"[Mutating] Punctum et linea non sunt unitates inertes, sed principia relationis et productionis per fluxum.","refs":["CH_MHON_TH_6:A5"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[]},{"id":"CL_MHON_03:GA-03","text":"[Persistent] Dislocatio partium homogenearum ad sedes naturales per rectas lineas restitutionem generat.","refs":["CH_MHON_TH_6:A1"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[]},{"id":"CL_MHON_03:GA-04","text":"[Emergent] Natura restitutiva et Magia mechanica sub eadem ratione fluxus et productionis coniunguntur.","refs":["CH_MHON_TH_6:SD1"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[]},{"id":"CL_MHON_03:GA-05","text":"[Persistent] Quaternarius ex quattuor rectis et angulis constituitur, sed eius vis non in sola quantitate quattuor clauditur.","refs":["CH_MHON_TH_5:A3","CH_MHON_TH_6:NX6"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[]},{"id":"CL_MHON_03:GA-06","text":"[Mutating] Unus, duo, tria et quattuor in denarium coalescunt, ita ut numeratio fiat extensio structuræ, non mera enumeratio.","refs":["CH_MHON_THEV:A2"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[]},{"id":"CL_MHON_03:GA-07","text":"[Emergent] Numerus, figura, littera et locus unam unitatem symbolicam efficiunt cuius identitas distributa est inter plures ordines.","refs":["CH_MHON_THEV:SD1"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[]},{"id":"CL_MHON_03:GA-08","text":"[Attractor] Ternarius et quaternarius in septenario et denario convergunt ut relationes copulationis, separationis et extensionis.","refs":["CH_MHON_TH_5:A7","CH_MHON_THEV:SD2"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[]},{"id":"CL_MHON_03:GA-09","text":"[Terminal] Numerus a relationibus geometricis, physicis et symbolicis separatus intra systema intelligibilis esse non potest.","refs":["CH_MHON_TH_5:SNX4"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[]},{"id":"CL_MHON_03:GA-10","text":"[Persistent] Crux non est figura isolata, sed signum traditione doctrinali et numerali confirmatum.","refs":["CH_MHON_THEV:A6"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[]}],"original_name":"Global Load-Bearing Axioms"},{"number":"3","name":"Recurrence, Mutation, and Displacement Matrix","items":[{"id":"CL_MHON_03:RMD-01","text":"Ternarius e figura geometrica redit ut compositio ontologica Corporis, Spiritus et Animae; idem numerus igitur formam mutat, non identitatem simpliciter repetit.","refs":["CH_MHON_TH_5:A6","CH_MHON_TH_5:D4"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"Composite","passages":[]},{"id":"CL_MHON_03:RMD-02","text":"Quaternarius e configuratione angulari migrat in directiones, summationem et amplificationem ad denarium.","refs":["CH_MHON_TH_6:A4","CH_MHON_TH_6:NX6"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"Composite","passages":[]},{"id":"CL_MHON_03:RMD-03","text":"Punctum ab unitate immobili in originem fluxus et productionis transit.","refs":["CH_MHON_TH_6:ME3"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"Mutating","passages":[]},{"id":"CL_MHON_03:RMD-04","text":"Recta a figura descriptiva in mechanismum reditus et instrumentum compositionis elementaris convertitur.","refs":["CH_MHON_TH_6:ME2"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"Composite","passages":[]},{"id":"CL_MHON_03:RMD-05","text":"Stilla coniungit punctum geometricum cum materia physica, ita ut linea inter utrumque fiat genealogia communis.","refs":["CH_MHON_TH_6:ME4"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"Composite","passages":[]},{"id":"CL_MHON_03:RMD-06","text":"Crux e quaternaria forma geometrica in nexum numeralem, alphabeticum, doctrinalem et locum ordinis convertitur.","refs":["CH_MHON_THEV:ME2","CH_MHON_THEV:ME4"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"Composite","passages":[]},{"id":"CL_MHON_03:RMD-07","text":"Octonarius non fit axioma primarium, sed effectus latentis repetitionis qui post primam configurationem emergit et in maiorem ordinem absorbetur.","refs":["CH_MHON_TH_5:ME6"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"Absorptive","passages":[]}],"original_name":"Recurrence, Mutation, and Displacement Matrix"},{"number":"4","name":"Lower-Layer vs Current-Level Distinction","items":[{"id":"CL_MHON_03:CGD-01","text":"Sol et Luna in Cruce Rectilinea manent exemplum locale orientationis figurae, non axioma globale totius machinae.","refs":["CH_MHON_TH_5:ALT1"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"Direct","passages":[]},{"id":"CL_MHON_03:CGD-02","text":"Identificatio crucis cum littera Romani alphabeti vicesima primae ordinem alphabeticum addit, sed ipsa correspondentiæ mechanismus ad gradum localem restringitur.","refs":["CH_MHON_THEV:A5"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"Direct","passages":[]},{"id":"CL_MHON_03:CGD-03","text":"Stilla et casus continuus explicant nexum physicæ cum Mechanica Magia, sed non solum ipsi constituunt legem generalem productionis.","refs":["CH_MHON_TH_6:AS5"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"Direct","passages":[]},{"id":"CL_MHON_03:CGD-04","text":"Sur sum amplificatur commune schema quo dislocatio, fluxus et figura eandem restitutionis-productionis rationem exhibent.","refs":["CH_MHON_TH_6:SD2","CH_MHON_TH_6:SD3"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"Composite","passages":[]},{"id":"CL_MHON_03:CGD-05","text":"In gradu praesentis synthesis punctum, recta, elementum et numerus non amplius singulae imagines sunt, sed partes unius conversionis generatricis.","refs":["CH_MHON_TH_6:SD5"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"Composite","passages":[]},{"id":"CL_MHON_03:CGD-06","text":"Dubitatio de praedecessoribus Magis et insufficiens demonstratio relationis ternarii ad septenarium localia manent, quamvis ambiguitas ipsa structuram tensionis altiorem alimenteat.","refs":["CH_MHON_TH_5:NX6","CH_MHON_TH_6:MRT3"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"Tension","passages":[]},{"id":"CL_MHON_03:CGD-07","text":"Obsoleta fit imago puncti, lineae vel numeri ut entium a motu, materia et ordine symbolico separatorum.","refs":["CH_MHON_TH_6:NX4","CH_MHON_TH_6:DNX6"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"Composite","passages":[]}],"original_name":"Lower-Layer vs Current-Level Distinction"},{"number":"5","name":"Global Apophatic Structure","items":[{"id":"CL_MHON_03:GNX-01","text":"Systema negat numerum esse mensuram externam cui nulla relatio figurativa vel ontologica respondeat.","refs":["CH_MHON_TH_5:DNX1"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"Negative","passages":[]},{"id":"CL_MHON_03:GNX-02","text":"Systema excludit separationem absolutam inter geometriam et significationem symbolicam.","refs":["CH_MHON_TH_5:DNX2"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"Negative","passages":[]},{"id":"CL_MHON_03:GNX-03","text":"Systema recusat lineam ut formam a puncto, fluxu et productione independentem.","refs":["CH_MHON_TH_6:DNX2"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"Negative","passages":[]},{"id":"CL_MHON_03:GNX-04","text":"Systema non sinit quaternarium in numero quattuor terminari, quia numerus ipse ad denarium, crucem et ordines maiores extenditur.","refs":["CH_MHON_TH_6:NX6"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"Negative","passages":[]},{"id":"CL_MHON_03:GNX-05","text":"Systema respuit signum cuius figura, numerus, littera et locus inter se non respondeant.","refs":["CH_MHON_THEV:SNX1"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"Negative","passages":[]}],"original_name":"Global Apophatic Structure"},{"number":"6","name":"Deep Book Apophatics","items":[{"id":"CL_MHON_03:DBN-01","text":"Falsum esse oportet mundum in quo natura sine reditu, sede naturali aut directione consistat.","refs":["CH_MHON_TH_6:DNX1"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"Negative","passages":[]},{"id":"CL_MHON_03:DBN-02","text":"Falsum esse oportet lineam ut formam mortuam sine origine punctiformi et fluxu continuae productionis.","refs":["CH_MHON_TH_6:NX4"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"Negative","passages":[]},{"id":"CL_MHON_03:DBN-03","text":"Falsum esse oportet numerum ut seriem clausam, a figura, materia et doctrina separatam.","refs":["CH_MHON_TH_6:DNX5"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"Negative","passages":[]},{"id":"CL_MHON_03:DBN-04","text":"Falsum esse oportet physicam, geometriam et Magiam tres ordines disiunctos constituere.","refs":["CH_MHON_TH_6:DNX6"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"Negative","passages":[]}],"original_name":"Deep Book Apophatics"},{"number":"7","name":"Super-Apophatics of the Whole","items":[{"id":"CL_MHON_03:BSA-01","text":"Systema circumagit possibilitatem figurae purae quae nec numerum nec significationem ontologicam generaret.","refs":["CH_MHON_TH_5:SNX1"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"Negative","passages":[]},{"id":"CL_MHON_03:BSA-02","text":"Discretio inter mathematicam formam et magicam interpretationem intra systema fieri non potest quin eius logica dissolvatur.","refs":["CH_MHON_TH_5:SNX2"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"Negative","passages":[]},{"id":"CL_MHON_03:BSA-03","text":"Possibilitas indisponibilis est numerus a relationibus suis geometricis separatus.","refs":["CH_MHON_TH_5:SNX4"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"Negative","passages":[]},{"id":"CL_MHON_03:BSA-04","text":"Absentia regulae completae de repetitione octonarii vim attractivam praebet ordini qui numquam perfecte formalizatur.","refs":["CH_MHON_TH_5:SNX3"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"Tension","passages":[]}],"original_name":"Super-Apophatics of the Whole"},{"number":"8","name":"Cross-Unit Derivative Propositions","items":[{"id":"CL_MHON_03:XD-01","text":"Reditus naturalis, fluxus magicus et constructio symbolica sub una machina generativa coalescunt: origo punctiformis per motum ordinatum ad figuram et signum pervenit.","refs":["CL_MHON_03:GA-02","CL_MHON_03:GA-03","CL_MHON_03:GA-04","CL_MHON_03:GA-07","CH_MHON_TH_6:SD1","CH_MHON_THEV:SD1"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"From","passages":[]},{"id":"CL_MHON_03:XD-02","text":"Quaternarius ex forma quattuor rectarum in operatorium transit qui simul directionem, summationem, crucem et denarium generat.","refs":["CL_MHON_03:GA-05","CL_MHON_03:GA-06","CL_MHON_03:GA-10","CH_MHON_THEV:FGS3","CH_MHON_THEV:FGS1"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"From","passages":[]},{"id":"CL_MHON_03:XD-03","text":"Crux fit punctum convergencee ubi arithmeticum, geometricum, alphabeticum et doctrinale non solum correspondent, sed eundem locum symbolicum instituunt.","refs":["CL_MHON_03:GA-07","CL_MHON_03:GA-08","CL_MHON_03:GA-10","CH_MHON_THEV:FGS7","CH_MHON_THEV:ME4"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"From","passages":[]},{"id":"CL_MHON_03:XD-04","text":"Dislocatio, quae initio perturbatio videtur, fit condicio qua ordo naturalis et ordo symbolicus denuo construuntur.","refs":["CL_MHON_03:GA-03","CL_MHON_03:GA-04","CH_MHON_TH_6:ME1","CH_MHON_TH_6:SD2"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"From","passages":[]},{"id":"CL_MHON_03:XD-05","text":"Punctum, linea, elementum et numerus in seriem recursivam convertuntur, dum numerus ad figuram, litteram et locum revertitur.","refs":["CL_MHON_03:GA-02","CL_MHON_03:GA-06","CL_MHON_03:GA-07","CH_MHON_TH_6:SD5","CH_MHON_THEV:FGS7"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"From","passages":[]},{"id":"CL_MHON_03:XD-06","text":"Natura et Magia non tantum analogice comparantur, sed per communem fluxus logicam in unum campum causalitatis rediguntur.","refs":["CL_MHON_03:GA-03","CL_MHON_03:GA-04","CH_MHON_TH_6:ME7","CH_MHON_TH_6:SD1"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"From","passages":[]},{"id":"CL_MHON_03:XD-07","text":"Eadem machina quae ordines amplificat eos etiam formaliter instabiles relinquit: octonarius et transitus ternarii ad septenarium vim habent sine clausura plena.","refs":["CL_MHON_03:GA-08","CL_MHON_03:GA-09","CH_MHON_TH_5:MRT4","CH_MHON_THEV:MRT3"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"From","passages":[]}],"original_name":"Cross-Unit Derivative Propositions"},{"number":"9","name":"Book-Scale Super-Derivatives","items":[{"id":"CL_MHON_03:BSD-01","text":"Ager conceptualis reordinatur ita ut geometria fiat grammatica generativa, non ornamentum quod post numerum interpretetur.","refs":["CL_MHON_03:GA-01","CL_MHON_03:GA-02","CL_MHON_03:GA-05","CH_MHON_TH_5:ME1","CH_MHON_TH_5:ALT2"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"From","passages":[]},{"id":"CL_MHON_03:BSD-02","text":"Causalitas naturalis et operatio magica sub communi schemate productionis fluxionalis leguntur.","refs":["CL_MHON_03:GA-03","CL_MHON_03:GA-04","CH_MHON_TH_6:SD3","CH_MHON_TH_6:ME7"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"From","passages":[]},{"id":"CL_MHON_03:BSD-03","text":"Subiectum huius systematis non est spectator formarum, sed ens quod per motum, restitutionem, resolutionem et aggregationem constituitur.","refs":["CL_MHON_03:GA-02","CL_MHON_03:GA-03","CL_MHON_03:GA-06","CH_MHON_TH_6:ALT4","CH_MHON_TH_6:SD4"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"From","passages":[]},{"id":"CL_MHON_03:BSD-04","text":"Identitas symbolica fit distributa: nulla figura sola sufficit, quia eius vis inter numerum, litteram, locum et traditionem dividitur.","refs":["CL_MHON_03:GA-07","CL_MHON_03:GA-10","CH_MHON_THEV:SD1","CH_MHON_THEV:SD3"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"From","passages":[]},{"id":"CL_MHON_03:BSD-05","text":"Tempus systematis est duplex: reditus repetit naturalem ordinem, dum repetitio ulterior ordinem latentem postea manifestat.","refs":["CL_MHON_03:GA-03","CL_MHON_03:GA-08","CH_MHON_TH_6:ALT4","CH_MHON_TH_5:SD4"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"From","passages":[]},{"id":"CL_MHON_03:BSD-06","text":"Methodus superior est reconstructiva et analogica: observationem, figuram et traditionem coniungit, sed nexum strictum inter omnes ordines non semper demonstrat.","refs":["CL_MHON_03:GA-07","CL_MHON_03:GA-10","CH_MHON_THEV:AS5","CH_MHON_TH_6:MRT6"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"From","passages":[]}],"original_name":"Book-Scale Super-Derivatives"},{"number":"10","name":"Recursive Loops, Escalation Paths, and Conceptual Attractors","items":[{"id":"CL_MHON_03:RLE-01","text":"Dislocatio → reditus → recta → sedes naturalis; reditus ipse fit exemplar quo omnis perturbatio in ordinem convertitur.","refs":["CH_MHON_TH_6:FGS1"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"Composite","passages":[]},{"id":"CL_MHON_03:RLE-02","text":"Punctum → fluxus → linea → elementum → numerus; numerus → figura → littera → locus; extrema catenae in eandem machinam symbolicam recurrunt.","refs":["CH_MHON_TH_6:SD5","CH_MHON_THEV:FGS7"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"Composite","passages":[]},{"id":"CL_MHON_03:RLE-03","text":"Quaternarius → denarius → crux → septenarius; amplificatio numerica in ordinem figurativum revertitur et novam amplificationem parit.","refs":["CH_MHON_THEV:FGS2","CH_MHON_THEV:FGS6"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"Composite","passages":[]},{"id":"CL_MHON_03:RLE-04","text":"Repetitio configuris latentem producit quae in primo gradu nondum apparet; reditus igitur mutationem status, non redundantiam, significat.","refs":["CH_MHON_TH_5:SD4"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"Mutating","passages":[]},{"id":"CL_MHON_03:RLE-05","text":"Attractor principalis est unitas symbolica ubi numerus, figura, littera et locus in unam configurationem convergunt.","refs":["CH_MHON_THEV:FGS7"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"Attractor","passages":[]},{"id":"CL_MHON_03:RLE-06","text":"Circuitus instabilis oritur ubi analogia doctrinam ad ordinem altiorem trahit, sed transitus eius formaliter demonstrari non potest.","refs":["CH_MHON_TH_6:MRT8","CH_MHON_THEV:MRT3"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"Tension","passages":[]}],"original_name":"Recursive Loops, Escalation Paths, and Conceptual Attractors"},{"number":"11","name":"Formal Generative Schema of the Whole Book","items":[{"id":"CL_MHON_03:FGS-B01","text":"Dislocatio partium → reditus per rectas → restitutio sedium naturalium → figura ordinata.","refs":["CH_MHON_TH_6:FGS1"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"Composite","passages":[]},{"id":"CL_MHON_03:FGS-B02","text":"Punctum → fluxus → linea → quattuor directiones → elementa → compositio numerica.","refs":["CH_MHON_TH_6:FGS2","CH_MHON_TH_6:FGS3"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"Composite","passages":[]},{"id":"CL_MHON_03:FGS-B03","text":"Stilla physica → casus continuus → lineae elementares → Mechanica Magia → causalitas communis.","refs":["CH_MHON_TH_6:FGS4"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"Composite","passages":[]},{"id":"CL_MHON_03:FGS-B04","text":"Unum + duo + tria + quattuor → quaternarius aggregatus → denarius → translatio ad crucem.","refs":["CH_MHON_TH_6:FGS5"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"Composite","passages":[]},{"id":"CL_MHON_03:FGS-B05","text":"Crux → littera → traditio philosophica → signum numerale → locus per Ternarium et Septenarium.","refs":["CH_MHON_THEV:FGS4","CH_MHON_THEV:FGS5"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"Composite","passages":[]},{"id":"CL_MHON_03:FGS-B06","text":"Motus universus: perturbatio → restitutio → fluxus → figura → numerus → littera → locus → clausura symbolica incompleta.","refs":["CH_MHON_TH_6:FGS7","CH_MHON_THEV:FGS7"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"Composite","passages":[]}],"original_name":"Formal Generative Schema of the Whole Book"},{"number":"12","name":"Whole-Book Argument Flow Map","items":[{"id":"CL_MHON_03:AFM-B01","text":"Dislocatio elementorum → quaestio de reditu → recta ut via naturalis → punctum ut origo fluxus → elementa → quaternarius → denarius → crux → littera → locus symbolicus → attractio ad ordinem ternarii et septenarii.","refs":["CH_MHON_TH_6:AF1","CH_MHON_THEV:AF1"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"Composite","passages":[]},{"id":"CL_MHON_03:AFM-B02","text":"Figura geometrica → relatio numerica → translatio physico-magica → amplificatio doctrinalis → unitas symbolica → clausura per analogiam, non per demonstrationem plenam.","refs":["CH_MHON_TH_5:FGS7","CH_MHON_TH_6:MRT8"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"Composite","passages":[]}],"original_name":"Whole-Book Argument Flow Map"},{"number":"13","name":"Macro Map-Effects","items":[{"id":"CL_MHON_03:MME-01","text":"Figura rectilinea fit tabula communis in qua punctum, recta, angulus, motus et repetitio simul leguntur.","refs":["CH_MHON_TH_5:ME1"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"Composite","passages":[]},{"id":"CL_MHON_03:MME-02","text":"Numeri quattuor, tres, septem, octo et decem ex seriebus abstractis in amplitudines transformationis convertuntur.","refs":["CH_MHON_TH_6:ME6"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"Composite","passages":[]},{"id":"CL_MHON_03:MME-03","text":"Dislocatio reclassificatur ut conditio ordinis, punctum ut principium productionis, et recta ut instrumentum restitutionis.","refs":["CH_MHON_TH_6:ME1","CH_MHON_TH_6:ME3"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"Composite","passages":[]},{"id":"CL_MHON_03:MME-04","text":"Quaternarius fit operator inter amplitudines, non terminus numericus: eadem forma ad elementa, denarium, crucem et ordinem doctrinalem ducit.","refs":["CH_MHON_TH_6:ME6","CH_MHON_THEV:ME1"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"Composite","passages":[]},{"id":"CL_MHON_03:MME-05","text":"Methodus lectionis mutatur: figura non amplius describitur, sed per genealogiam fluxus, numeri, traditionis et loci restituitur.","refs":["CH_MHON_TH_5:ALT3","CH_MHON_THEV:ALT3"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"Composite","passages":[]}],"original_name":"Macro Map-Effects"},{"number":"14","name":"Altitude Integration","items":[{"id":"CL_MHON_03:ALT-B01","text":"Gradus superficiei continet Sol et Lunam, partes dislocatas, stillam, numeros et Crucem Rectilineam ut obiecta expressa.","refs":["CH_MHON_TH_5:ALT1","CH_MHON_TH_6:ALT1"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"Composite","passages":[]},{"id":"CL_MHON_03:ALT-B02","text":"Gradus structuralis continet reditum, fluxum, resolutionem, copulationem, separationem et aggregationem ut mechanismos recurrentes.","refs":["CH_MHON_TH_5:ALT2","CH_MHON_TH_6:ALT2"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"Composite","passages":[]},{"id":"CL_MHON_03:ALT-B03","text":"Gradus metastrategicus lectionem transfert a descriptione physica ad ordinationem geometricam, magicam, numericam et doctrinalem.","refs":["CH_MHON_TH_5:ALT3","CH_MHON_TH_6:ALT3"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"Composite","passages":[]},{"id":"CL_MHON_03:ALT-B04","text":"Gradus ontologicus ens concipit ut productum fluxus, casus, resolutionis et aggregationis, dum tempus reditum et latentem manifestationem sustinet.","refs":["CH_MHON_TH_6:ALT4","CH_MHON_TH_5:ALT4"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"Composite","passages":[]},{"id":"CL_MHON_03:ALT-B05","text":"Gradus methodologicus propriam conclusionem perficit eo ipso quod fontes diversos in seriem reconstructivo-symbolicam redigit.","refs":["CH_MHON_THEV:AS5","CH_MHON_TH_5:ALT3"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"Composite","passages":[]}],"original_name":"Altitude Integration"},{"number":"15","name":"Internal Tensions, Mutation Points, and Fault Lines","items":[{"id":"CL_MHON_03:FL-01","text":"Demonstratio stricte deductiva inter quaternarium, ternarium, septenarium et denarium promittitur implicite, sed relationes saepe per correspondentiam tantum sustinentur.","refs":["CH_MHON_TH_6:MRT3","CH_MHON_THEV:MRT3"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"Tension","passages":[]},{"id":"CL_MHON_03:FL-02","text":"Octonarius simul ut effectus repetitionis necessarius apparet et ut ordo cuius regula completa deest.","refs":["CH_MHON_TH_5:MRT4","CH_MHON_TH_5:SNX3"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"Tension","passages":[]},{"id":"CL_MHON_03:FL-03","text":"Transitus a observatione experientiali ad mysterium quattuor elementorum analogiam adhibet ubi probatio stricte deficit.","refs":["CH_MHON_TH_6:MRT6"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"Tension","passages":[]},{"id":"CL_MHON_03:FL-04","text":"Figura geometrica simul materialis, numeralis, alphabetica et magica ponitur, sed status horum ordinum inter se non semper definitur.","refs":["CH_MHON_THEV:A8","CH_MHON_THEV:MRT4"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"Tension","passages":[]},{"id":"CL_MHON_03:MP-01","text":"Punctum mutat cum ab unitate immobili ad principium fluxus et productionis transit.","refs":["CH_MHON_TH_6:ME3"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"Mutating","passages":[]},{"id":"CL_MHON_03:MP-02","text":"Quaternarius mutat cum ab figura quattuor rectarum ad extensionem denariam et signum crucis movetur.","refs":["CH_MHON_THEV:ME1"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"Mutating","passages":[]},{"id":"CL_MHON_03:MP-03","text":"Crux mutat cum a figura geometrica ad litteram, traditionem et locum intra seriem symbolicam convertitur.","refs":["CH_MHON_THEV:D3","CH_MHON_THEV:ME2"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"Mutating","passages":[]},{"id":"CL_MHON_03:MP-04","text":"Repetitio mutat cum effectus occultus ampliorem ordinem generat, ita ut reditus fiat transformatio et non iteratio identica.","refs":["CH_MHON_TH_5:SD4"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"Mutating","passages":[]}],"original_name":"Internal Tensions, Mutation Points, and Fault Lines"},{"number":"16","name":"Hidden System Image","items":[{"id":"CL_MHON_03:HSI-01","text":"Systema implicite imaginem machinae fluxionalis et recursivae praebet, in qua puncta, materiae, numeri et signa per conversiones ordinatas moventur.","refs":["CH_MHON_TH_6:SD5","CH_MHON_TH_6:FGS7"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"Composite","passages":[]},{"id":"CL_MHON_03:HSI-02","text":"Subiectum intra hanc machinam est ens dislocabile quod per reditum, resolutionem et compositionem suam formam acquirit.","refs":["CH_MHON_TH_6:ALT4","CH_MHON_TH_6:ME1"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"Composite","passages":[]},{"id":"CL_MHON_03:HSI-03","text":"Tempus est spiraliter recurrens: perturbatio ad sedem redit, sed repetitio ulterior novum ordinem latentem aperit.","refs":["CH_MHON_TH_6:FGS1","CH_MHON_TH_5:SD4"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"Composite","passages":[]},{"id":"CL_MHON_03:HSI-04","text":"Certamen principale non est inter formas oppositas, sed inter separationem et copulationem, inter formam fixam et fluxum qui eam denuo generat.","refs":["CH_MHON_TH_5:FGS6","CH_MHON_TH_6:FGS7"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"Composite","passages":[]}],"original_name":"Hidden System Image"},{"number":"17","name":"Minimal Whole-Book Reconstruction Test","items":[{"id":"CL_MHON_03:MRT-B01","text":"Axiomata praesentia sufficiunt ad machinam principalem restituendam: dislocatio in reditum, punctum in fluxum, fluxus in lineam, quaternarius in denarium et crux in ordinem symbolicum convertitur.","refs":["CH_MHON_TH_5:MRT1","CH_MHON_TH_6:MRT1"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"Composite","passages":[]},{"id":"CL_MHON_03:MRT-B02","text":"Systema hac magnitudine cohaeret quia geometria, physica, numeratio et Magia per eandem productionis et conversionis logicam ordinantur.","refs":["CH_MHON_TH_6:MRT4","CH_MHON_TH_6:SD3"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"Composite","passages":[]},{"id":"CL_MHON_03:MRT-B03","text":"Firmissima est catena punctum → fluxus → linea → elementum et relatio quaternarii ad figuram; incertissima sunt octonarii clausura, litterae crucis mechanismus et transitus ternarii ad septenarium.","refs":["CH_MHON_TH_6:MRT5","CH_MHON_THEV:MRT2"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"Tension","passages":[]},{"id":"CL_MHON_03:MRT-B04","text":"Restitutio verum motum servat, dummodo correspondentiae inter ordines non pro demonstrationibus absolutis, sed pro machina reconstructivo-symbolica cum tensionibus internis intellegantur.","refs":["CH_MHON_THEV:MRT5"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"Composite","passages":[]}],"original_name":"Minimal Whole-Book Reconstruction Test"}],"language_code":"la","language_name":"Latina","translations":[{"id":"CAREVAR_20261006T223209444239Z_caf84be1","language_code":"en","language_name":"English","title":"Chapters 07–09","source_sha256":"d6732a7fd87c8c28ced66921a1eada671a90a7596a5ce91c8beaf9042c41990e","sha256":"46f99b0db6af6597e75d186cca46299466dbc9320b45ec2848a13825747191ca","rendition_type":"machine","review_status":"machine","model":"gpt-5.6-terra","created":"2026-10-06T22:32:09.445585+00:00","sections":[{"number":"1","name":"Book-Scale Argumental Spine","items":[{"id":"CL_MHON_03:SP-01","text":"The central question is how rectilinear figures, points, motions, and numbers are converted into a single symbolic order.","refs":["CH_MHON_TH_5:AS1","CH_MHON_THEV:AS1"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"Composite","passages":[]},{"id":"CL_MHON_03:SP-02","text":"The central thesis is that geometry acts not as a static form, but as a machine of translation among natural restoration, physical flux, numeration, and magical signification.","refs":["CH_MHON_TH_6:AS4","CH_MHON_TH_6:SD1"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"Composite","passages":[]},{"id":"CL_MHON_03:SP-03","text":"The reconstructive-symbolic argument proceeds from displacement to return, from point to line, from the quaternary to the denary, and from figure to doctrinal place.","refs":["CH_MHON_TH_5:AS5","CH_MHON_TH_6:AS7"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"Composite","passages":[]},{"id":"CL_MHON_03:SP-04","text":"The movement of the system is recursive and cumulative, because the same relation among point, line, number, and figure migrates into physical, geometric, and doctrinal fields.","refs":["CH_MHON_TH_6:SD5","CH_MHON_THEV:SD1"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"Composite","passages":[]},{"id":"CL_MHON_03:SP-05","text":"The quaternary does not terminate motion, but amplifies it into the denary, the cross, the septenary, and other symbolic orderings.","refs":["CH_MHON_TH_6:AS6","CH_MHON_THEV:ME1"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"Composite","passages":[]},{"id":"CL_MHON_03:SP-06","text":"The closure of the system is symbolic rather than deductive: forms converge into a single series, while certain relations, especially those of the octonary and ternary to the septenary, remain formally incomplete.","refs":["CH_MHON_TH_5:SNX3","CH_MHON_THEV:MRT3"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"Tension","passages":[]}],"original_name":"Book-Scale Argumental Spine"},{"number":"2","name":"Global Load-Bearing Axioms","items":[{"id":"CL_MHON_03:GA-01","text":"[Persistent] A rectilinear figure can simultaneously sustain numerical ratio and Hieroglyphic signification.","refs":["CH_MHON_TH_5:A1"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[]},{"id":"CL_MHON_03:GA-02","text":"[Mutating] Point and line are not inert units, but principles of relation and production through flux.","refs":["CH_MHON_TH_6:A5"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[]},{"id":"CL_MHON_03:GA-03","text":"[Persistent] The displacement of homogeneous parts toward natural seats through straight lines generates restoration.","refs":["CH_MHON_TH_6:A1"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[]},{"id":"CL_MHON_03:GA-04","text":"[Emergent] Restorative nature and mechanical Magic are joined under the same rationale of flux and production.","refs":["CH_MHON_TH_6:SD1"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[]},{"id":"CL_MHON_03:GA-05","text":"[Persistent] The quaternary is constituted from four straight lines and angles, but its force is not enclosed in the quantity four alone.","refs":["CH_MHON_TH_5:A3","CH_MHON_TH_6:NX6"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[]},{"id":"CL_MHON_03:GA-06","text":"[Mutating] One, two, three, and four coalesce into the denary, such that numeration becomes an extension of structure, not mere enumeration.","refs":["CH_MHON_THEV:A2"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[]},{"id":"CL_MHON_03:GA-07","text":"[Emergent] Number, figure, letter, and place constitute a single symbolic unity whose identity is distributed among several orders.","refs":["CH_MHON_THEV:SD1"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[]},{"id":"CL_MHON_03:GA-08","text":"[Attractor] The ternary and quaternary converge in the septenary and denary as relations of coupling, separation, and extension.","refs":["CH_MHON_TH_5:A7","CH_MHON_THEV:SD2"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[]},{"id":"CL_MHON_03:GA-09","text":"[Terminal] A number separated from geometric, physical, and symbolic relations cannot be intelligible within the system.","refs":["CH_MHON_TH_5:SNX4"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[]},{"id":"CL_MHON_03:GA-10","text":"[Persistent] The cross is not an isolated figure, but a sign confirmed by doctrinal and numerical tradition.","refs":["CH_MHON_THEV:A6"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[]}],"original_name":"Global Load-Bearing Axioms"},{"number":"3","name":"Recurrence, Mutation, and Displacement Matrix","items":[{"id":"CL_MHON_03:RMD-01","text":"The ternary returns from geometric figure as the ontological composition of Body, Spirit, and Soul; the same number therefore changes form rather than simply repeating identity.","refs":["CH_MHON_TH_5:A6","CH_MHON_TH_5:D4"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"Composite","passages":[]},{"id":"CL_MHON_03:RMD-02","text":"The quaternary migrates from angular configuration into directions, summation, and amplification toward the denary.","refs":["CH_MHON_TH_6:A4","CH_MHON_TH_6:NX6"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"Composite","passages":[]},{"id":"CL_MHON_03:RMD-03","text":"The point passes from an immobile unit into the origin of flux and production.","refs":["CH_MHON_TH_6:ME3"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"Mutating","passages":[]},{"id":"CL_MHON_03:RMD-04","text":"The straight line is converted from a descriptive figure into a mechanism of return and an instrument of elemental composition.","refs":["CH_MHON_TH_6:ME2"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"Composite","passages":[]},{"id":"CL_MHON_03:RMD-05","text":"The drop joins the geometric point with physical matter, so that the line becomes a common genealogy between the two.","refs":["CH_MHON_TH_6:ME4"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"Composite","passages":[]},{"id":"CL_MHON_03:RMD-06","text":"The cross is converted from a quaternary geometric form into a numerical, alphabetical, doctrinal nexus and a place of order.","refs":["CH_MHON_THEV:ME2","CH_MHON_THEV:ME4"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"Composite","passages":[]},{"id":"CL_MHON_03:RMD-07","text":"The octonary does not become a primary axiom, but an effect of latent repetition that emerges after the first configuration and is absorbed into a greater order.","refs":["CH_MHON_TH_5:ME6"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"Absorptive","passages":[]}],"original_name":"Recurrence, Mutation, and Displacement Matrix"},{"number":"4","name":"Lower-Layer vs Current-Level Distinction","items":[{"id":"CL_MHON_03:CGD-01","text":"The Sun and Moon in the Rectilinear Cross remain a local example of the orientation of the figure, not a global axiom of the whole machine.","refs":["CH_MHON_TH_5:ALT1"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"Direct","passages":[]},{"id":"CL_MHON_03:CGD-02","text":"The identification of the cross with the twenty-first letter of the Roman alphabet adds an alphabetical order, but the mechanism of correspondence itself is restricted to the local level.","refs":["CH_MHON_THEV:A5"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"Direct","passages":[]},{"id":"CL_MHON_03:CGD-03","text":"The drop and continuous fall explain the nexus of physics with Mechanical Magic, but they alone do not constitute the general law of production.","refs":["CH_MHON_TH_6:AS5"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"Direct","passages":[]},{"id":"CL_MHON_03:CGD-04","text":"Above, the common schema is amplified, whereby displacement, flux, and figure exhibit the same rationale of restoration-production.","refs":["CH_MHON_TH_6:SD2","CH_MHON_TH_6:SD3"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"Composite","passages":[]},{"id":"CL_MHON_03:CGD-05","text":"At the level of the present synthesis, point, straight line, element, and number are no longer individual images, but parts of a single generative conversion.","refs":["CH_MHON_TH_6:SD5"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"Composite","passages":[]},{"id":"CL_MHON_03:CGD-06","text":"Doubt concerning the predecessors of the Magi and the insufficient demonstration of the relation of the ternary to the septenary remain local, although ambiguity itself nourishes a higher structure of tension.","refs":["CH_MHON_TH_5:NX6","CH_MHON_TH_6:MRT3"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"Tension","passages":[]},{"id":"CL_MHON_03:CGD-07","text":"The image of point, line, or number as entities separated from motion, matter, and symbolic order becomes obsolete.","refs":["CH_MHON_TH_6:NX4","CH_MHON_TH_6:DNX6"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"Composite","passages":[]}],"original_name":"Lower-Layer vs Current-Level Distinction"},{"number":"5","name":"Global Apophatic Structure","items":[{"id":"CL_MHON_03:GNX-01","text":"The system denies that number is an external measure to which no figurative or ontological relation corresponds.","refs":["CH_MHON_TH_5:DNX1"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"Negative","passages":[]},{"id":"CL_MHON_03:GNX-02","text":"The system excludes an absolute separation between geometry and symbolic signification.","refs":["CH_MHON_TH_5:DNX2"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"Negative","passages":[]},{"id":"CL_MHON_03:GNX-03","text":"The system rejects the line as a form independent of point, flux, and production.","refs":["CH_MHON_TH_6:DNX2"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"Negative","passages":[]},{"id":"CL_MHON_03:GNX-04","text":"The system does not permit the quaternary to terminate in the number four, because number itself extends to the denary, the cross, and greater orders.","refs":["CH_MHON_TH_6:NX6"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"Negative","passages":[]},{"id":"CL_MHON_03:GNX-05","text":"The system rejects a sign whose figure, number, letter, and place do not correspond to one another.","refs":["CH_MHON_THEV:SNX1"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"Negative","passages":[]}],"original_name":"Global Apophatic Structure"},{"number":"6","name":"Deep Book Apophatics","items":[{"id":"CL_MHON_03:DBN-01","text":"A world in which nature subsists without return, natural seat, or direction must be false.","refs":["CH_MHON_TH_6:DNX1"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"Negative","passages":[]},{"id":"CL_MHON_03:DBN-02","text":"The line as a dead form without a point-like origin and the flux of continuous production must be false.","refs":["CH_MHON_TH_6:NX4"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"Negative","passages":[]},{"id":"CL_MHON_03:DBN-03","text":"Number as a closed series, separated from figure, matter, and doctrine, must be false.","refs":["CH_MHON_TH_6:DNX5"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"Negative","passages":[]},{"id":"CL_MHON_03:DBN-04","text":"Physics, geometry, and Magic as three disjoined orders must be false.","refs":["CH_MHON_TH_6:DNX6"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"Negative","passages":[]}],"original_name":"Deep Book Apophatics"},{"number":"7","name":"Super-Apophatics of the Whole","items":[{"id":"CL_MHON_03:BSA-01","text":"The system revolves around the possibility of a pure figure that would generate neither number nor ontological signification.","refs":["CH_MHON_TH_5:SNX1"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"Negative","passages":[]},{"id":"CL_MHON_03:BSA-02","text":"A distinction between mathematical form and magical interpretation cannot be made within the system without its logic dissolving.","refs":["CH_MHON_TH_5:SNX2"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"Negative","passages":[]},{"id":"CL_MHON_03:BSA-03","text":"The possibility of number separated from its geometric relations is unavailable.","refs":["CH_MHON_TH_5:SNX4"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"Negative","passages":[]},{"id":"CL_MHON_03:BSA-04","text":"The absence of a complete rule concerning the repetition of the octonary affords attractive force to an order that is never perfectly formalized.","refs":["CH_MHON_TH_5:SNX3"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"Tension","passages":[]}],"original_name":"Super-Apophatics of the Whole"},{"number":"8","name":"Cross-Unit Derivative Propositions","items":[{"id":"CL_MHON_03:XD-01","text":"Natural return, magical flux, and symbolic construction coalesce under a single generative machine: the point-like origin reaches figure and sign through ordered motion.","refs":["CL_MHON_03:GA-02","CL_MHON_03:GA-03","CL_MHON_03:GA-04","CL_MHON_03:GA-07","CH_MHON_TH_6:SD1","CH_MHON_THEV:SD1"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"From","passages":[]},{"id":"CL_MHON_03:XD-02","text":"The quaternary passes from the form of four straight lines into an operator that simultaneously generates direction, summation, cross, and denary.","refs":["CL_MHON_03:GA-05","CL_MHON_03:GA-06","CL_MHON_03:GA-10","CH_MHON_THEV:FGS3","CH_MHON_THEV:FGS1"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"From","passages":[]},{"id":"CL_MHON_03:XD-03","text":"The cross becomes a point of convergence where the arithmetical, geometric, alphabetical, and doctrinal not only correspond, but establish the same symbolic place.","refs":["CL_MHON_03:GA-07","CL_MHON_03:GA-08","CL_MHON_03:GA-10","CH_MHON_THEV:FGS7","CH_MHON_THEV:ME4"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"From","passages":[]},{"id":"CL_MHON_03:XD-04","text":"Displacement, which initially appears a disturbance, becomes the condition by which natural order and symbolic order are reconstructed.","refs":["CL_MHON_03:GA-03","CL_MHON_03:GA-04","CH_MHON_TH_6:ME1","CH_MHON_TH_6:SD2"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"From","passages":[]},{"id":"CL_MHON_03:XD-05","text":"Point, line, element, and number are converted into a recursive series, while number returns to figure, letter, and place.","refs":["CL_MHON_03:GA-02","CL_MHON_03:GA-06","CL_MHON_03:GA-07","CH_MHON_TH_6:SD5","CH_MHON_THEV:FGS7"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"From","passages":[]},{"id":"CL_MHON_03:XD-06","text":"Nature and Magic are not merely compared analogically, but are reduced to a single field of causality through a common logic of flux.","refs":["CL_MHON_03:GA-03","CL_MHON_03:GA-04","CH_MHON_TH_6:ME7","CH_MHON_TH_6:SD1"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"From","passages":[]},{"id":"CL_MHON_03:XD-07","text":"The same machine that amplifies orders also leaves them formally unstable: the octonary and the transition of the ternary to the septenary have force without full closure.","refs":["CL_MHON_03:GA-08","CL_MHON_03:GA-09","CH_MHON_TH_5:MRT4","CH_MHON_THEV:MRT3"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"From","passages":[]}],"original_name":"Cross-Unit Derivative Propositions"},{"number":"9","name":"Book-Scale Super-Derivatives","items":[{"id":"CL_MHON_03:BSD-01","text":"The conceptual field is reordered so that geometry becomes a generative grammar, not an ornament that interprets after number.","refs":["CL_MHON_03:GA-01","CL_MHON_03:GA-02","CL_MHON_03:GA-05","CH_MHON_TH_5:ME1","CH_MHON_TH_5:ALT2"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"From","passages":[]},{"id":"CL_MHON_03:BSD-02","text":"Natural causality and magical operation are read under a common schema of fluxional production.","refs":["CL_MHON_03:GA-03","CL_MHON_03:GA-04","CH_MHON_TH_6:SD3","CH_MHON_TH_6:ME7"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"From","passages":[]},{"id":"CL_MHON_03:BSD-03","text":"The subject of this system is not a spectator of forms, but a being constituted through motion, restoration, resolution, and aggregation.","refs":["CL_MHON_03:GA-02","CL_MHON_03:GA-03","CL_MHON_03:GA-06","CH_MHON_TH_6:ALT4","CH_MHON_TH_6:SD4"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"From","passages":[]},{"id":"CL_MHON_03:BSD-04","text":"Symbolic identity becomes distributed: no figure alone suffices, because its force is divided among number, letter, place, and tradition.","refs":["CL_MHON_03:GA-07","CL_MHON_03:GA-10","CH_MHON_THEV:SD1","CH_MHON_THEV:SD3"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"From","passages":[]},{"id":"CL_MHON_03:BSD-05","text":"The time of the system is double: return repeats natural order, while further repetition subsequently manifests a latent order.","refs":["CL_MHON_03:GA-03","CL_MHON_03:GA-08","CH_MHON_TH_6:ALT4","CH_MHON_TH_5:SD4"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"From","passages":[]},{"id":"CL_MHON_03:BSD-06","text":"The higher method is reconstructive and analogical: it joins observation, figure, and tradition, but does not always demonstrate a strict nexus among all orders.","refs":["CL_MHON_03:GA-07","CL_MHON_03:GA-10","CH_MHON_THEV:AS5","CH_MHON_TH_6:MRT6"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"From","passages":[]}],"original_name":"Book-Scale Super-Derivatives"},{"number":"10","name":"Recursive Loops, Escalation Paths, and Conceptual Attractors","items":[{"id":"CL_MHON_03:RLE-01","text":"Displacement → return → straight line → natural seat; return itself becomes the model by which every disturbance is converted into order.","refs":["CH_MHON_TH_6:FGS1"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"Composite","passages":[]},{"id":"CL_MHON_03:RLE-02","text":"Point → flux → line → element → number; number → figure → letter → place; the extremes of the chain recur in the same symbolic machine.","refs":["CH_MHON_TH_6:SD5","CH_MHON_THEV:FGS7"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"Composite","passages":[]},{"id":"CL_MHON_03:RLE-03","text":"Quaternary → denary → cross → septenary; numerical amplification returns into figurative order and begets new amplification.","refs":["CH_MHON_THEV:FGS2","CH_MHON_THEV:FGS6"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"Composite","passages":[]},{"id":"CL_MHON_03:RLE-04","text":"The repetition of configurations produces a latent order that does not yet appear at the first level; return therefore signifies a change of state, not redundancy.","refs":["CH_MHON_TH_5:SD4"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"Mutating","passages":[]},{"id":"CL_MHON_03:RLE-05","text":"The principal attractor is the symbolic unity in which number, figure, letter, and place converge into one configuration.","refs":["CH_MHON_THEV:FGS7"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"Attractor","passages":[]},{"id":"CL_MHON_03:RLE-06","text":"An unstable circuit arises where analogy draws doctrine toward a higher order, but its transition cannot be formally demonstrated.","refs":["CH_MHON_TH_6:MRT8","CH_MHON_THEV:MRT3"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"Tension","passages":[]}],"original_name":"Recursive Loops, Escalation Paths, and Conceptual Attractors"},{"number":"11","name":"Formal Generative Schema of the Whole Book","items":[{"id":"CL_MHON_03:FGS-B01","text":"Displacement of parts → return through straight lines → restoration of natural seats → ordered figure.","refs":["CH_MHON_TH_6:FGS1"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"Composite","passages":[]},{"id":"CL_MHON_03:FGS-B02","text":"Point → flux → line → four directions → elements → numerical composition.","refs":["CH_MHON_TH_6:FGS2","CH_MHON_TH_6:FGS3"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"Composite","passages":[]},{"id":"CL_MHON_03:FGS-B03","text":"Physical drop → continuous fall → elemental lines → Mechanical Magic → common causality.","refs":["CH_MHON_TH_6:FGS4"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"Composite","passages":[]},{"id":"CL_MHON_03:FGS-B04","text":"One + two + three + four → aggregated quaternary → denary → translation to the cross.","refs":["CH_MHON_TH_6:FGS5"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"Composite","passages":[]},{"id":"CL_MHON_03:FGS-B05","text":"Cross → letter → philosophical tradition → numerical sign → place through the Ternary and Septenary.","refs":["CH_MHON_THEV:FGS4","CH_MHON_THEV:FGS5"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"Composite","passages":[]},{"id":"CL_MHON_03:FGS-B06","text":"Universal movement: disturbance → restoration → flux → figure → number → letter → place → incomplete symbolic closure.","refs":["CH_MHON_TH_6:FGS7","CH_MHON_THEV:FGS7"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"Composite","passages":[]}],"original_name":"Formal Generative Schema of the Whole Book"},{"number":"12","name":"Whole-Book Argument Flow Map","items":[{"id":"CL_MHON_03:AFM-B01","text":"Displacement of elements → question of return → straight line as natural path → point as origin of flux → elements → quaternary → denary → cross → letter → symbolic place → attraction toward the order of the ternary and septenary.","refs":["CH_MHON_TH_6:AF1","CH_MHON_THEV:AF1"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"Composite","passages":[]},{"id":"CL_MHON_03:AFM-B02","text":"Geometric figure → numerical relation → physical-magical translation → doctrinal amplification → symbolic unity → closure through analogy, not through full demonstration.","refs":["CH_MHON_TH_5:FGS7","CH_MHON_TH_6:MRT8"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"Composite","passages":[]}],"original_name":"Whole-Book Argument Flow Map"},{"number":"13","name":"Macro Map-Effects","items":[{"id":"CL_MHON_03:MME-01","text":"The rectilinear figure becomes a common map in which point, straight line, angle, motion, and repetition are read together.","refs":["CH_MHON_TH_5:ME1"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"Composite","passages":[]},{"id":"CL_MHON_03:MME-02","text":"The numbers four, three, seven, eight, and ten are converted from abstract series into dimensions of transformation.","refs":["CH_MHON_TH_6:ME6"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"Composite","passages":[]},{"id":"CL_MHON_03:MME-03","text":"Displacement is reclassified as a condition of order, the point as a principle of production, and the straight line as an instrument of restoration.","refs":["CH_MHON_TH_6:ME1","CH_MHON_TH_6:ME3"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"Composite","passages":[]},{"id":"CL_MHON_03:MME-04","text":"The quaternary becomes an operator among dimensions, not a numerical terminus: the same form leads to elements, the denary, the cross, and doctrinal order.","refs":["CH_MHON_TH_6:ME6","CH_MHON_THEV:ME1"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"Composite","passages":[]},{"id":"CL_MHON_03:MME-05","text":"The method of reading changes: figure is no longer described, but restored through the genealogy of flux, number, tradition, and place.","refs":["CH_MHON_TH_5:ALT3","CH_MHON_THEV:ALT3"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"Composite","passages":[]}],"original_name":"Macro Map-Effects"},{"number":"14","name":"Altitude Integration","items":[{"id":"CL_MHON_03:ALT-B01","text":"The level of surface contains the Sun and Moon, displaced parts, the drop, numbers, and the Rectilinear Cross as explicit objects.","refs":["CH_MHON_TH_5:ALT1","CH_MHON_TH_6:ALT1"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"Composite","passages":[]},{"id":"CL_MHON_03:ALT-B02","text":"The structural level contains return, flux, resolution, coupling, separation, and aggregation as recurrent mechanisms.","refs":["CH_MHON_TH_5:ALT2","CH_MHON_TH_6:ALT2"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"Composite","passages":[]},{"id":"CL_MHON_03:ALT-B03","text":"The metastrategic level transfers reading from physical description to geometric, magical, numerical, and doctrinal ordering.","refs":["CH_MHON_TH_5:ALT3","CH_MHON_TH_6:ALT3"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"Composite","passages":[]},{"id":"CL_MHON_03:ALT-B04","text":"The ontological level conceives being as the product of flux, fall, resolution, and aggregation, while time sustains return and latent manifestation.","refs":["CH_MHON_TH_6:ALT4","CH_MHON_TH_5:ALT4"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"Composite","passages":[]},{"id":"CL_MHON_03:ALT-B05","text":"The methodological level accomplishes its own conclusion precisely by reducing diverse sources to a reconstructive-symbolic series.","refs":["CH_MHON_THEV:AS5","CH_MHON_TH_5:ALT3"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"Composite","passages":[]}],"original_name":"Altitude Integration"},{"number":"15","name":"Internal Tensions, Mutation Points, and Fault Lines","items":[{"id":"CL_MHON_03:FL-01","text":"A strictly deductive demonstration among the quaternary, ternary, septenary, and denary is implicitly promised, but the relations are often sustained only through correspondence.","refs":["CH_MHON_TH_6:MRT3","CH_MHON_THEV:MRT3"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"Tension","passages":[]},{"id":"CL_MHON_03:FL-02","text":"The octonary appears both as a necessary effect of repetition and as an order lacking a complete rule.","refs":["CH_MHON_TH_5:MRT4","CH_MHON_TH_5:SNX3"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"Tension","passages":[]},{"id":"CL_MHON_03:FL-03","text":"The transition from experiential observation to the mystery of the four elements employs analogy where strict proof fails.","refs":["CH_MHON_TH_6:MRT6"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"Tension","passages":[]},{"id":"CL_MHON_03:FL-04","text":"The geometric figure is posited simultaneously as material, numerical, alphabetical, and magical, but the status of these orders in relation to one another is not always defined.","refs":["CH_MHON_THEV:A8","CH_MHON_THEV:MRT4"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"Tension","passages":[]},{"id":"CL_MHON_03:MP-01","text":"The point changes when it passes from an immobile unit to a principle of flux and production.","refs":["CH_MHON_TH_6:ME3"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"Mutating","passages":[]},{"id":"CL_MHON_03:MP-02","text":"The quaternary changes when it moves from the figure of four straight lines to denary extension and the sign of the cross.","refs":["CH_MHON_THEV:ME1"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"Mutating","passages":[]},{"id":"CL_MHON_03:MP-03","text":"The cross changes when it is converted from a geometric figure into letter, tradition, and place within the symbolic series.","refs":["CH_MHON_THEV:D3","CH_MHON_THEV:ME2"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"Mutating","passages":[]},{"id":"CL_MHON_03:MP-04","text":"Repetition changes when a hidden effect generates a greater order, so that return becomes transformation and not identical iteration.","refs":["CH_MHON_TH_5:SD4"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"Mutating","passages":[]}],"original_name":"Internal Tensions, Mutation Points, and Fault Lines"},{"number":"16","name":"Hidden System Image","items":[{"id":"CL_MHON_03:HSI-01","text":"The system implicitly presents the image of a fluxional and recursive machine in which points, materials, numbers, and signs move through ordered conversions.","refs":["CH_MHON_TH_6:SD5","CH_MHON_TH_6:FGS7"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"Composite","passages":[]},{"id":"CL_MHON_03:HSI-02","text":"The subject within this machine is a displaceable being that acquires its form through return, resolution, and composition.","refs":["CH_MHON_TH_6:ALT4","CH_MHON_TH_6:ME1"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"Composite","passages":[]},{"id":"CL_MHON_03:HSI-03","text":"Time recurs spirally: disturbance returns to its seat, but further repetition opens a new latent order.","refs":["CH_MHON_TH_6:FGS1","CH_MHON_TH_5:SD4"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"Composite","passages":[]},{"id":"CL_MHON_03:HSI-04","text":"The principal contest is not between opposed forms, but between separation and coupling, between fixed form and the flux that regenerates it.","refs":["CH_MHON_TH_5:FGS6","CH_MHON_TH_6:FGS7"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"Composite","passages":[]}],"original_name":"Hidden System Image"},{"number":"17","name":"Minimal Whole-Book Reconstruction Test","items":[{"id":"CL_MHON_03:MRT-B01","text":"The present axioms suffice to restore the principal machine: displacement is converted into return, point into flux, flux into line, quaternary into denary, and cross into symbolic order.","refs":["CH_MHON_TH_5:MRT1","CH_MHON_TH_6:MRT1"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"Composite","passages":[]},{"id":"CL_MHON_03:MRT-B02","text":"The system coheres at this magnitude because geometry, physics, numeration, and Magic are ordered through the same logic of production and conversion.","refs":["CH_MHON_TH_6:MRT4","CH_MHON_TH_6:SD3"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"Composite","passages":[]},{"id":"CL_MHON_03:MRT-B03","text":"The strongest are the chain point → flux → line → element and the relation of the quaternary to figure; the most uncertain are the closure of the octonary, the mechanism of the letter of the cross, and the transition of the ternary to the septenary.","refs":["CH_MHON_TH_6:MRT5","CH_MHON_THEV:MRT2"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"Tension","passages":[]},{"id":"CL_MHON_03:MRT-B04","text":"The restoration preserves true movement, provided that correspondences among orders are understood not as absolute demonstrations, but as a reconstructive-symbolic machine with internal tensions.","refs":["CH_MHON_THEV:MRT5"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"Composite","passages":[]}],"original_name":"Minimal Whole-Book Reconstruction Test"}],"revision":"CL_MHON_03@R001","source_variant_id":"canonical:CL_MHON_03:v1","source_language_code":"la","original_title":"Chapters 07–09"}],"identity":{"layer":"CL","kicker":"Cluster · Monas Hieroglyphica","title":"Chapters 07–09","context":"T H E O R E M V I. · T H E O R. V I I. · Theorem Viii.","original_title":"Chapters 07–09","original_context":"T H E O R E M A V I. · T H E O R. V I I. · Theor. Viii."},"original_title":"Chapters 07–09","title_language_code":"en"},{"id":"CL_MHON_04","layer":"CL","title":"Chapters 10–12","order":4,"parents":["CH_MHON_TH_7","CH_MHON_TH_8","CH_MHON_THEX"],"revision":"CL_MHON_04@R001","sha256":"1a7ce88dd7efea4e2f977946da78594f671a6e074cbcf3b45876e89112a4b0f4","sections":[{"number":"1","name":"Book-Scale Argumental Spine","items":[{"id":"CL_MHON_04:BAS-01","text":"Quaestio centralis est quomodo unitas symbolica, geometrica et temporalis in distinctiones ordinatas convertatur sine ruptura relationis initialis.","refs":["CH_MHON_TH_7:AS1","CH_MHON_TH_8:AS1"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[]},{"id":"CL_MHON_04:BAS-02","text":"Thesis globalis est quod MONAS, figura Arietis et ordo nycthemericus unam machinam differentiationis proportionatae constituant.","refs":["CH_MHON_TH_7:AS2","CH_MHON_THEX:SD3"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[]},{"id":"CL_MHON_04:BAS-03","text":"Fundamentum argumentationis est translatio formarum in operationes: Lineae fiunt circuli, ignis fit medium separationis, et figura fit distributio temporis.","refs":["CH_MHON_TH_7:AS3","CH_MHON_TH_8:A8","CH_MHON_THEX:AS5"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"Composite","passages":[]},{"id":"CL_MHON_04:BAS-04","text":"Motus systematis est recursivus et cumulativus: unitas producit distinctionem, distinctio proportionem, proportio novam unitatem legibilem.","refs":["CH_MHON_TH_8:SD1","CH_MHON_TH_8:D6"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"Composite","passages":[]},{"id":"CL_MHON_04:BAS-05","text":"Clausura non est numerica plena, sed attractio ad proportionem occultam quae relationem inter caelum, Terram et symbolum stabilit.","refs":["CH_MHON_TH_7:A9","CH_MHON_THEX:SNX3"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"Tension","passages":[]}],"original_name":"Book-Scale Argumental Spine"},{"number":"2","name":"Global Load-Bearing Axioms","items":[{"id":"CL_MHON_04:GLA-01","text":"[Persistent] Unitas systematis non excludit distinctionem, sed eam tamquam processum internum sustinet.","refs":["CH_MHON_TH_8:SD1"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[]},{"id":"CL_MHON_04:GLA-02","text":"[Persistent] Materia symbolica in partes determinatas separari potest, dummodo ordo proportionis servetur.","refs":["CH_MHON_TH_7:A3"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[]},{"id":"CL_MHON_04:GLA-03","text":"[Persistent] Geometria est medium generale quo differentiae in relationes constructibiles convertuntur.","refs":["CH_MHON_TH_7:A2"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[]},{"id":"CL_MHON_04:GLA-04","text":"[Persistent] Figura hieroglyphica relationes inter signum, partes, operationem et tempus simul continet.","refs":["CH_MHON_TH_8:A9"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[]},{"id":"CL_MHON_04:GLA-05","text":"[Persistent] Proportio dat ordinem communem elementis separatis, quamvis eius mensura numerica ubique non tradatur.","refs":["CH_MHON_TH_7:A8","CH_MHON_THEX:SD4"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[]},{"id":"CL_MHON_04:GLA-06","text":"[Mutating] MONAS mutatur a termino congruentiae in matricem quae separationem, mediationem et reconstitutionem continet.","refs":["CH_MHON_TH_7:A1","CH_MHON_TH_8:A4"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[]},{"id":"CL_MHON_04:GLA-07","text":"[Mutating] Aries mutatur a loco astronomico in principium operativum ignis et exordium transformationis.","refs":["CH_MHON_TH_8:A1","CH_MHON_TH_8:SD5"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[]},{"id":"CL_MHON_04:GLA-08","text":"[Emergent] Tempus nycthemericum fit alterum spatium constructionis, in quo distributio aequinoctialis functionem proportionis suscipit.","refs":["CH_MHON_THEX:A4","CH_MHON_THEX:A6"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[]},{"id":"CL_MHON_04:GLA-09","text":"[Emergent] Signum unum non amplius ad unum campum pertinet, sed transit inter astronomiam, geometriam, praxim et Terram.","refs":["CH_MHON_TH_8:A10","CH_MHON_THEX:A7"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[]},{"id":"CL_MHON_04:GLA-10","text":"[Attractor] Proportio occulta est punctum convergencee ad quod figurae, elementa, tempora et loca terrestria referuntur.","refs":["CH_MHON_THEX:SD4","CH_MHON_THEX:ME4"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[]},{"id":"CL_MHON_04:GLA-11","text":"[Terminal] Systema ad ordinem formalem pervenit, sed clausura eius manet incompleta, quia proportio indicatur potius quam numerice demonstratur.","refs":["CH_MHON_TH_7:MRT5","CH_MHON_THEX:MRT4"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[]}],"original_name":"Global Load-Bearing Axioms"},{"number":"3","name":"Recurrence, Mutation, and Displacement Matrix","items":[{"id":"CL_MHON_04:RMD-01","text":"MONAS identitatem retinet ut nomen unitatis, sed functionem mutat: fit simul continens, processus et effectus differentiationis.","refs":["CH_MHON_TH_8:A4","CH_MHON_TH_8:D6"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"Composite","passages":[]},{"id":"CL_MHON_04:RMD-02","text":"Separatio redit in tribus formis: ut Lineae geometricae, ut divisio elementorum igne effecta, et ut partitio temporis.","refs":["CH_MHON_TH_7:NX1","CH_MHON_TH_8:A6","CH_MHON_THEX:NX3"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"Composite","passages":[]},{"id":"CL_MHON_04:RMD-03","text":"Forma circularis e spatio ad tempus migrat: circulus fit schema distributionis et semicirculi fiunt figura temporis divisibilis.","refs":["CH_MHON_TH_7:A4","CH_MHON_THEX:D1"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"Composite","passages":[]},{"id":"CL_MHON_04:RMD-04","text":"Proportio denaria initio regulam elementorum constituit; postea fit proportio occulta, cuius ambitus extenditur ad Terram et nycthemeram.","refs":["CH_MHON_TH_8:A7","CH_MHON_THEX:A6"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"Composite","passages":[]},{"id":"CL_MHON_04:RMD-05","text":"Aries vim invertit: quod initio signum loci videtur, postea index processus fit.","refs":["CH_MHON_TH_8:ME1","CH_MHON_TH_8:A2"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"Composite","passages":[]},{"id":"CL_MHON_04:RMD-06","text":"Ignis a elemento descriptivo ad mediationem operativam transfertur et separationem tamquam conversionem conservat.","refs":["CH_MHON_TH_8:ME4","CH_MHON_TH_8:D5"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"Composite","passages":[]},{"id":"CL_MHON_04:RMD-07","text":"«Occultum» non permanet absentia pura, sed in ordinem numerabilem absorbetur ut attractor interpretationis.","refs":["CH_MHON_THEX:ME4","CH_MHON_THEX:SD4"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"Negative","passages":[]},{"id":"CL_MHON_04:RMD-08","text":"Lectio signorum singulorum evanescit ut methodus sufficiens, quia figura tota tamquam nexus relationum assumitur.","refs":["CH_MHON_TH_8:DNX5","CH_MHON_TH_8:ME5"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"Composite","passages":[]}],"original_name":"Recurrence, Mutation, and Displacement Matrix"},{"number":"4","name":"Lower-Layer vs Current-Level Distinction","items":[{"id":"CL_MHON_04:CGD-01","text":"Localis manet constructio singularum Linearum, circulorum, semicirculorum et punctorum communium.","refs":["CH_MHON_TH_7:AS4","CH_MHON_THEX:A1"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[]},{"id":"CL_MHON_04:CGD-02","text":"Localis manet etiam assignatio Arietis ad exordium Triplicitatis Igneæ, nisi haec assignatio in processum amplificetur.","refs":["CH_MHON_TH_8:A1","CH_MHON_TH_8:A10"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[]},{"id":"CL_MHON_04:CGD-03","text":"Sursum amplificatur principium quod separatio non est dissolutio, sed gradus ordinis communis.","refs":["CH_MHON_TH_7:NX2","CH_MHON_TH_8:ME3"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[]},{"id":"CL_MHON_04:CGD-04","text":"Unitatem fontium coniungit functio mediationis: geometria, ignis et aequinoctium eandem conversionem inter unitatem et distinctionem exercent.","refs":["CH_MHON_TH_7:SD1","CH_MHON_TH_8:A8","CH_MHON_THEX:ME3"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"Composite","passages":[]},{"id":"CL_MHON_04:CGD-05","text":"Maiorem transitionem efficit translatio a figura ad processum: signum iam non indicat tantum, sed ordinem producit.","refs":["CH_MHON_TH_8:ME1","CH_MHON_THEX:SD1"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"Composite","passages":[]},{"id":"CL_MHON_04:CGD-06","text":"Obsoleta fit oppositio simplex inter unitatem et divisionem, sicut etiam oppositio inter caelum et Terram.","refs":["CH_MHON_TH_8:SNX1","CH_MHON_THEX:SD2"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"Negative","passages":[]},{"id":"CL_MHON_04:CGD-07","text":"Aliter codificatur proportio: non iam tantum forma denaria, sed relatio trans-campalis inter figuram, tempus et locum.","refs":["CH_MHON_THEX:SD3","CH_MHON_THEX:D3"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"Composite","passages":[]}],"original_name":"Lower-Layer vs Current-Level Distinction"},{"number":"5","name":"Global Apophatic Structure","items":[{"id":"CL_MHON_04:GNX-01","text":"Systema excludit congruentiam immediatam quae sine separatione praevia et sine mediis geometricis assereretur.","refs":["CH_MHON_TH_7:DNX1","CH_MHON_TH_7:AS3"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"Negative","passages":[]},{"id":"CL_MHON_04:GNX-02","text":"Systema excludit unitatem immobiliter clausam, quia MONAS tantum per differentiationem operantem intelligitur.","refs":["CH_MHON_TH_8:DNX1","CH_MHON_TH_8:SD1"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"Negative","passages":[]},{"id":"CL_MHON_04:GNX-03","text":"Systema excludit interpretationem signi ut conventionis arbitrariae vel imaginis isolatae.","refs":["CH_MHON_THEX:SNX2","CH_MHON_THEX:DNX4"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"Negative","passages":[]},{"id":"CL_MHON_04:GNX-04","text":"Systema excludit proportionem quae a relatione ad Terram, tempus vel elementa separari possit.","refs":["CH_MHON_THEX:DNX3","CH_MHON_THEX:NX4"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"Negative","passages":[]},{"id":"CL_MHON_04:GNX-05","text":"Systema excludit scientiam puram sine operatione: cognoscibile hic est quod per figuram, partitionem et mediationem efficitur.","refs":["CH_MHON_TH_7:SD1","CH_MHON_TH_8:NX6"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"Composite","passages":[]}],"original_name":"Global Apophatic Structure"},{"number":"6","name":"Deep Book Apophatics","items":[{"id":"CL_MHON_04:DBN-01","text":"Falsum esse oportet quod unitatem et divisionem tamquam principia absolute incompatibilia statuat.","refs":["CH_MHON_TH_8:SNX1"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"Negative","passages":[]},{"id":"CL_MHON_04:DBN-02","text":"Falsum esse oportet quod forma signi sola, extra contextum relationum, sensum eius exhauriat.","refs":["CH_MHON_TH_8:DNX5"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"Negative","passages":[]},{"id":"CL_MHON_04:DBN-03","text":"Falsum esse oportet quod tempus continuum et neutrum sit; nycthemera tantum ut distributio proportionalis operatur.","refs":["CH_MHON_THEX:NX3"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"Negative","passages":[]},{"id":"CL_MHON_04:DBN-04","text":"Falsum esse oportet quod proportio possit poni sine constructione quae eam producit, etiam ubi eius numerus non traditur.","refs":["CH_MHON_TH_7:DNX3","CH_MHON_THEX:SD4"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"Negative","passages":[]}],"original_name":"Deep Book Apophatics"},{"number":"7","name":"Super-Apophatics of the Whole","items":[{"id":"CL_MHON_04:BSA-01","text":"Systema circumit possibilitatem ordinis qui nullam mediationem inter caelum, symbolum, tempus et Terram agnoscat.","refs":["CH_MHON_TH_8:SNX4"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"Negative","passages":[]},{"id":"CL_MHON_04:BSA-02","text":"Indisponibilis manet unitas pura sine differentiatione, quia omnis unitas hic per transitum et separationem manifestatur.","refs":["CH_MHON_TH_8:DNX1"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"Negative","passages":[]},{"id":"CL_MHON_04:BSA-03","text":"Vetita est proportio omnino aperta et exhausta: systema indiget residuo occulto ut interpretatio recursiva permaneat.","refs":["CH_MHON_THEX:SNX3"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"Negative","passages":[]},{"id":"CL_MHON_04:BSA-04","text":"Ontologice inadmissibilis fit schema quod signum, processum et mundum terrestre in regiones independentes dividit.","refs":["CH_MHON_THEX:SD2"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"Negative","passages":[]}],"original_name":"Super-Apophatics of the Whole"},{"number":"8","name":"Cross-Unit Derivative Propositions","items":[{"id":"CL_MHON_04:XD-01","text":"Separatio geometrica et ministerium ignis simul efficiunt ut distinctio non sit exitus dissolutivus, sed transitus ordinatus; distributio temporalis eandem formam ad alium campum transfert.","refs":["CL_MHON_04:GLA-02","CL_MHON_04:GLA-03","CL_MHON_04:GLA-06","CL_MHON_04:GLA-08","CH_MHON_TH_7:AS3","CH_MHON_TH_8:A8","CH_MHON_THEX:FGS3"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"From","passages":[]},{"id":"CL_MHON_04:XD-02","text":"Aries fit nexus inter exordium astronomicum et operationem internam MONADIS, quia ignis in eo simul initium et medium transformationis est.","refs":["CL_MHON_04:GLA-06","CL_MHON_04:GLA-07","CH_MHON_TH_8:AS2","CH_MHON_TH_8:SD5"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"From","passages":[]},{"id":"CL_MHON_04:XD-03","text":"Circulus geometricus et semicirculus mysticus concurrunt ad unam propositionem: forma est distributio relationum, non imago statica.","refs":["CL_MHON_04:GLA-03","CL_MHON_04:GLA-04","CH_MHON_TH_7:A5","CH_MHON_THEX:D1"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"From","passages":[]},{"id":"CL_MHON_04:XD-04","text":"Proportio denaria in elementis et proportio occulta in tempore efficiunt calculum distributum, cuius regula per varias figuras redit sed in nulla forma plene clauditur.","refs":["CL_MHON_04:GLA-05","CL_MHON_04:GLA-08","CL_MHON_04:GLA-10","CH_MHON_TH_8:A7","CH_MHON_THEX:A6","CH_MHON_THEX:SNX3"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"From","passages":[]},{"id":"CL_MHON_04:XD-05","text":"Separationis et coniunctionis oppositio dissolvitur cum tres unitates monstrant easdem operationes in diversis campis exercere.","refs":["CL_MHON_04:GLA-01","CL_MHON_04:GLA-02","CH_MHON_TH_7:ME2","CH_MHON_TH_8:SD1"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"From","passages":[]},{"id":"CL_MHON_04:XD-06","text":"Cum figura ad Terram referatur, caeleste non amplius superius et terrestre inferius manent, sed in una machina proportionaliter copulantur.","refs":["CL_MHON_04:GLA-08","CL_MHON_04:GLA-09","CH_MHON_THEX:A7","CH_MHON_THEX:SD2"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"From","passages":[]}],"original_name":"Cross-Unit Derivative Propositions"},{"number":"9","name":"Book-Scale Super-Derivatives","items":[{"id":"CL_MHON_04:BSD-01","text":"Systema generalem conversionem generat qua unitas, figura et tempus non substantiae clausae, sed modi ordinationis fiunt.","refs":["CH_MHON_TH_8:SD4","CH_MHON_THEX:SD3"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"Composite","passages":[]},{"id":"CL_MHON_04:BSD-02","text":"Astronomia, geometria et praxis alchemica in unam logicam operativam coalescunt, ita ut interpretatio ipsa processum imitari debeat.","refs":["CH_MHON_TH_8:SD3","CH_MHON_TH_7:ALT3"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"Composite","passages":[]},{"id":"CL_MHON_04:BSD-03","text":"Subjectum interpretans a spectatore signorum fit operator qui separationes, analogias et reconstitutiones inter campos persequitur.","refs":["CH_MHON_TH_8:ME5","CH_MHON_TH_7:ME3"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"Composite","passages":[]},{"id":"CL_MHON_04:BSD-04","text":"Tempus producitur tamquam materia formabilis: nycthemera non solum continet horas, sed eas in ordinem occultarum proportionum disponit.","refs":["CH_MHON_THEX:ME2","CH_MHON_THEX:D3"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"Composite","passages":[]},{"id":"CL_MHON_04:BSD-05","text":"Causalitas systematis est medialis: locus producit significationem per ignem, figura per partitionem, partitio per proportionem.","refs":["CH_MHON_TH_8:D2","CH_MHON_TH_8:D5"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"Composite","passages":[]},{"id":"CL_MHON_04:BSD-06","text":"Effectus methodologicus est ut nulla notio valide maneat extra seriem transformationum quae eam generat et reformat.","refs":["CH_MHON_TH_7:SD2","CH_MHON_TH_7:MRT3"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"Composite","passages":[]}],"original_name":"Book-Scale Super-Derivatives"},{"number":"10","name":"Recursive Loops, Escalation Paths, and Conceptual Attractors","items":[{"id":"CL_MHON_04:RLE-01","text":"Circuitus principalis est: unitas → separatio → proportio → coniunctio → unitas iterum lecta tamquam capacitas differentiationis.","refs":["CH_MHON_TH_8:D6","CH_MHON_TH_7:D3"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"Composite","passages":[]},{"id":"CL_MHON_04:RLE-02","text":"Circuitus trans-campalis est: Aries → ignis → figura → temporis divisio → Terra → nova significatio Arietis.","refs":["CH_MHON_TH_8:AF1","CH_MHON_THEX:SD2"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"Composite","passages":[]},{"id":"CL_MHON_04:RLE-03","text":"Amplificatio fit cum eadem functio separationis a spatio ad elementum, ab elemento ad tempus, a tempore ad mundum terrestre transfertur.","refs":["CH_MHON_TH_8:A6","CH_MHON_THEX:FGS5"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"Composite","passages":[]},{"id":"CL_MHON_04:RLE-04","text":"Attractor occultus est proportio: omnia elementa ad eam convergunt, sed ipsa semper novam quaestionem de mensura aperit.","refs":["CH_MHON_THEX:SD4","CH_MHON_TH_7:MRT5"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"Tension","passages":[]},{"id":"CL_MHON_04:RLE-05","text":"Punctum terminale est clausura formalis per Crucem DENARIAM vel proportionem aequinoctialem; punctum idem fit initium novae interpretationis, quia numerus plenus abest.","refs":["CH_MHON_TH_7:A8","CH_MHON_THEX:MRT4"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"Tension","passages":[]},{"id":"CL_MHON_04:RLE-06","text":"Fragmenta dispersa circa figuras, elementa et tempora convergunt ad imaginem unam: unitas non est initium simplex, sed attractor recursivus.","refs":["CH_MHON_TH_8:FGS6","CH_MHON_TH_8:FGS5"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"Composite","passages":[]}],"original_name":"Recursive Loops, Escalation Paths, and Conceptual Attractors"},{"number":"11","name":"Formal Generative Schema of the Whole Book","items":[{"id":"CL_MHON_04:FGS-B01","text":"Unitas initialis → separatio ordinata → conservatio relationis per proportionem.","refs":["CH_MHON_TH_8:A4","CH_MHON_TH_8:A7"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"Composite","passages":[]},{"id":"CL_MHON_04:FGS-B02","text":"Materia elementaris → Lineae et circuli → figura relationalis quae formas distinctas in constructionem unam colligit.","refs":["CH_MHON_TH_7:A2","CH_MHON_TH_7:AS4"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"Composite","passages":[]},{"id":"CL_MHON_04:FGS-B03","text":"Figura astronomica → ministerium ignis → divisio interna MONADIS → hieroglyphum quod unitatem et differentiam simul exhibet.","refs":["CH_MHON_TH_8:A2","CH_MHON_TH_8:D5"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"Composite","passages":[]},{"id":"CL_MHON_04:FGS-B04","text":"Signum connexo tempori → distributio aequinoctialis → proportio occulta → relatio terrestris → reclassificatio signi ut machinae.","refs":["CH_MHON_THEX:FGS2","CH_MHON_THEX:A7"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"Composite","passages":[]}],"original_name":"Formal Generative Schema of the Whole Book"},{"number":"12","name":"Whole-Book Argument Flow Map","items":[{"id":"CL_MHON_04:AFM-B01","text":"Quaestio de congruentia → separatio exacta → geometrica coniunctio → mediatio ignis → proportio denaria → unitas differentiata.","refs":["CH_MHON_TH_7:AS1","CH_MHON_TH_7:SD2"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"Composite","passages":[]},{"id":"CL_MHON_04:AFM-B02","text":"Exordium Arietis → signum operativum → figura composita → divisio nycthemerica → proportio occulta → relatio ad Terram.","refs":["CH_MHON_TH_8:FGS1","CH_MHON_THEX:AF1"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"Composite","passages":[]},{"id":"CL_MHON_04:AFM-B03","text":"Unitas indivisa → differentiationes mediatae → convergentia proportionalis → clausura formalis → residuum occultum quod recursionem renovat.","refs":["CH_MHON_TH_8:SD4","CH_MHON_THEX:SNX3"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"Tension","passages":[]}],"original_name":"Whole-Book Argument Flow Map"},{"number":"13","name":"Macro Map-Effects","items":[{"id":"CL_MHON_04:MME-01","text":"MONAS, SOL et LVNA, sicut Aries et nycthemera, reclassificantur ab rebus separatis ad gradus unius constructionis distributae.","refs":["CH_MHON_TH_7:ME1","CH_MHON_THEX:ME1"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"Composite","passages":[]},{"id":"CL_MHON_04:MME-02","text":"Oppositio inter unitatem et distinctionem corruit; distinctio fit actus quo unitas se legibilem constituit.","refs":["CH_MHON_TH_8:ME3","CH_MHON_TH_8:SD1"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"Composite","passages":[]},{"id":"CL_MHON_04:MME-03","text":"Ager temporalis fit ager constructionis, quia horae, sicut Lineae, per distributionem in proportiones ordinantur.","refs":["CH_MHON_THEX:ME2","CH_MHON_THEX:FGS3"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"Composite","passages":[]},{"id":"CL_MHON_04:MME-04","text":"Occultum reclassificatur ab extra-ordinem ad residuum internum ordinis, quod interpretatio sola per recursionem attingere potest.","refs":["CH_MHON_THEX:ME4","CH_MHON_THEX:FGS4"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"Composite","passages":[]},{"id":"CL_MHON_04:MME-05","text":"Methodus linearis fit insufficiens; requiritur lectio quae figuras, operationes, tempora et loca simul componat.","refs":["CH_MHON_TH_8:ME5","CH_MHON_TH_8:D3"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"Composite","passages":[]}],"original_name":"Macro Map-Effects"},{"number":"14","name":"Altitude Integration","items":[{"id":"CL_MHON_04:ALT-B01","text":"Gradus superficiei continet MONADEM, SOLEM, LVNAM, Arietem, ignem, semicirculos, horas et Terram tamquam signa expressa.","refs":["CH_MHON_TH_7:ALT1","CH_MHON_TH_8:ALT1"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[]},{"id":"CL_MHON_04:ALT-B02","text":"Gradus structuralis ostendit distributionem communem: separatio, mediatio, circulatio et aequinoctium eandem machinam ordinationis exercent.","refs":["CH_MHON_TH_7:ALT2","CH_MHON_THEX:ALT2"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"Composite","passages":[]},{"id":"CL_MHON_04:ALT-B03","text":"Gradus metastrategicus quaestionem transfert a “quid signum significat?” ad “quomodo signum relationes producit?”.","refs":["CH_MHON_TH_7:ALT3","CH_MHON_THEX:ALT3"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"Composite","passages":[]},{"id":"CL_MHON_04:ALT-B04","text":"Gradus ontologico-temporalis constituit realitatem ubi unitas, tempus et locus non praexistunt ut data, sed per operationes formantur.","refs":["CH_MHON_TH_7:ALT4","CH_MHON_THEX:ALT4"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"Composite","passages":[]},{"id":"CL_MHON_04:ALT-B05","text":"Gradus methodologicus et performativus cogit lectorem ipsas conversiones exsequi: signum legere est eius ordinem iterare.","refs":["CH_MHON_TH_8:ME5","CH_MHON_TH_7:ME3"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"Composite","passages":[]}],"original_name":"Altitude Integration"},{"number":"15","name":"Internal Tensions, Mutation Points, and Fault Lines","items":[{"id":"CL_MHON_04:FL-01","text":"Commissura inter coniunctionem circularem et quantificationem proportionis manet fragilis, quia constructio ostenditur sed regula numerica non absolvitur.","refs":["CH_MHON_TH_7:MRT5","CH_MHON_TH_7:MRT6"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"Tension","passages":[]},{"id":"CL_MHON_04:FL-02","text":"Status ignis oscillat inter elementum, signum et ministerium; haec ambiguitas non removetur, quia ipsa mediationem eius sustinet.","refs":["CH_MHON_TH_8:MRT5","CH_MHON_TH_8:NX4"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"Tension","passages":[]},{"id":"CL_MHON_04:FL-03","text":"Analogia inter semicirculos et horas operatur ut fundamentum, quamvis schema quo partes figurae singulis partibus temporis respondeant desit.","refs":["CH_MHON_THEX:MRT3","CH_MHON_THEX:SNX1"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"Tension","passages":[]},{"id":"CL_MHON_04:MP-01","text":"Mutatio prima fit cum Aries desinit esse locus zodiacalis et fit exordium transformationis internae.","refs":["CH_MHON_TH_8:ME1","CH_MHON_TH_8:SD5"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"Tension","passages":[]},{"id":"CL_MHON_04:MP-02","text":"Mutatio secunda fit cum separatio desinit esse disiunctio et fit actus quo unitas formam propriam constituit.","refs":["CH_MHON_TH_8:ME3","CH_MHON_TH_8:SD4"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"Composite","passages":[]},{"id":"CL_MHON_04:MP-03","text":"Mutatio tertia fit cum occultum desinit esse extra ordinem et fit attractor intra distributionem numerabilem.","refs":["CH_MHON_THEX:ME4","CH_MHON_THEX:ME2"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"Composite","passages":[]}],"original_name":"Internal Tensions, Mutation Points, and Fault Lines"},{"number":"16","name":"Hidden System Image","items":[{"id":"CL_MHON_04:HSI-01","text":"Systema implicite imaginem machinae circulatoriae praebet: partes separantur, per media transformantur, et in ordinem communem redeunt.","refs":["CH_MHON_TH_7:SD2","CH_MHON_TH_7:ME2"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"Composite","passages":[]},{"id":"CL_MHON_04:HSI-02","text":"Subiectum huius machinae est unitas operativa quae se non conservat per immobilitatem, sed per repetitionem differentiae.","refs":["CH_MHON_TH_8:D6","CH_MHON_TH_8:SD1"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"Composite","passages":[]},{"id":"CL_MHON_04:HSI-03","text":"Tempus est spirale potius quam lineare: distributio eius ad occultas proportiones redit et eas in novis campis repraesentat.","refs":["CH_MHON_THEX:A6","CH_MHON_THEX:ME4"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"Composite","passages":[]},{"id":"CL_MHON_04:HSI-04","text":"Certamen centrale est inter clausuram formalem et residuum occultum; fuga possibilis manet per novam translationem signi, temporis et loci.","refs":["CH_MHON_THEX:SNX3","CH_MHON_THEX:MRT5"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"Tension","passages":[]}],"original_name":"Hidden System Image"},{"number":"17","name":"Minimal Whole-Book Reconstruction Test","items":[{"id":"CL_MHON_04:MRT-B01","text":"Axiomata praesentia sufficiunt ad restituendam machinam minimam: unitas, separatio, mediatio, proportio, figura et distributio temporalis.","refs":["CH_MHON_TH_7:MRT1","CH_MHON_TH_8:MRT2"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"Composite","passages":[]},{"id":"CL_MHON_04:MRT-B02","text":"Cohaerentia globalis firmissima est in principio quod unitas per distinctionem operatur; minus firma est in translatione figurae ad mensuram exactam.","refs":["CH_MHON_TH_8:MRT3","CH_MHON_TH_7:MRT5"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"Tension","passages":[]},{"id":"CL_MHON_04:MRT-B03","text":"Restitutio servat verum motum, quia reditus notionum non ut repetitio simplex, sed ut mutatio campi et functionis legitur.","refs":["CH_MHON_TH_8:MRT7","CH_MHON_THEX:MRT2"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"Composite","passages":[]},{"id":"CL_MHON_04:MRT-B04","text":"Ad plenam restitutionem adhuc resistunt definitio numerica proportionis, status exactus ignis et schema quo figura tempori partiatur.","refs":["CH_MHON_TH_7:MRT4","CH_MHON_TH_8:MRT4","CH_MHON_THEX:MRT4"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"Tension","passages":[]}],"original_name":"Minimal Whole-Book Reconstruction Test"}],"language_code":"la","language_name":"Latina","translations":[{"id":"CAREVAR_20261006T223235895146Z_08480045","language_code":"en","language_name":"English","title":"Chapters 10–12","source_sha256":"1a7ce88dd7efea4e2f977946da78594f671a6e074cbcf3b45876e89112a4b0f4","sha256":"c3075e8530a4bdfe6da0f98bd6795c827c80078f89ad123be504ab9348c45613","rendition_type":"machine","review_status":"machine","model":"gpt-5.6-terra","created":"2026-10-06T22:32:35.897266+00:00","sections":[{"number":"1","name":"Book-Scale Argumental Spine","items":[{"id":"CL_MHON_04:BAS-01","text":"The central question is how symbolic, geometric, and temporal unity is converted into ordered distinctions without rupture of the initial relation.","refs":["CH_MHON_TH_7:AS1","CH_MHON_TH_8:AS1"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[]},{"id":"CL_MHON_04:BAS-02","text":"The global thesis is that MONAS, the figure of Aries, and the nycthemeral order constitute a single machine of proportionate differentiation.","refs":["CH_MHON_TH_7:AS2","CH_MHON_THEX:SD3"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[]},{"id":"CL_MHON_04:BAS-03","text":"The foundation of the argument is the translation of forms into operations: Lines become circles, fire becomes the medium of separation, and figure becomes the distribution of time.","refs":["CH_MHON_TH_7:AS3","CH_MHON_TH_8:A8","CH_MHON_THEX:AS5"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"Composite","passages":[]},{"id":"CL_MHON_04:BAS-04","text":"The movement of the system is recursive and cumulative: unity produces distinction, distinction proportion, proportion a new readable unity.","refs":["CH_MHON_TH_8:SD1","CH_MHON_TH_8:D6"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"Composite","passages":[]},{"id":"CL_MHON_04:BAS-05","text":"Closure is not numerically complete, but an attraction toward a hidden proportion that stabilizes the relation among heaven, Earth, and symbol.","refs":["CH_MHON_TH_7:A9","CH_MHON_THEX:SNX3"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"Tension","passages":[]}],"original_name":"Book-Scale Argumental Spine"},{"number":"2","name":"Global Load-Bearing Axioms","items":[{"id":"CL_MHON_04:GLA-01","text":"[Persistent] The unity of the system does not exclude distinction, but sustains it as an internal process.","refs":["CH_MHON_TH_8:SD1"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[]},{"id":"CL_MHON_04:GLA-02","text":"[Persistent] Symbolic matter can be separated into determinate parts, provided that the order of proportion is preserved.","refs":["CH_MHON_TH_7:A3"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[]},{"id":"CL_MHON_04:GLA-03","text":"[Persistent] Geometry is the general medium through which differences are converted into constructible relations.","refs":["CH_MHON_TH_7:A2"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[]},{"id":"CL_MHON_04:GLA-04","text":"[Persistent] The hieroglyphic figure simultaneously contains relations among sign, parts, operation, and time.","refs":["CH_MHON_TH_8:A9"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[]},{"id":"CL_MHON_04:GLA-05","text":"[Persistent] Proportion gives a common order to separate elements, although its numerical measure is not everywhere transmitted.","refs":["CH_MHON_TH_7:A8","CH_MHON_THEX:SD4"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[]},{"id":"CL_MHON_04:GLA-06","text":"[Mutating] MONAS changes from a term of congruence into a matrix containing separation, mediation, and reconstitution.","refs":["CH_MHON_TH_7:A1","CH_MHON_TH_8:A4"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[]},{"id":"CL_MHON_04:GLA-07","text":"[Mutating] Aries changes from an astronomical location into an operative principle of fire and the beginning of transformation.","refs":["CH_MHON_TH_8:A1","CH_MHON_TH_8:SD5"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[]},{"id":"CL_MHON_04:GLA-08","text":"[Emergent] Nycthemeral time becomes another space of construction, in which equinoctial distribution assumes the function of proportion.","refs":["CH_MHON_THEX:A4","CH_MHON_THEX:A6"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[]},{"id":"CL_MHON_04:GLA-09","text":"[Emergent] A single sign no longer belongs to one field, but passes among astronomy, geometry, praxis, and Earth.","refs":["CH_MHON_TH_8:A10","CH_MHON_THEX:A7"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[]},{"id":"CL_MHON_04:GLA-10","text":"[Attractor] Hidden proportion is the point of convergence to which figures, elements, times, and terrestrial places are referred.","refs":["CH_MHON_THEX:SD4","CH_MHON_THEX:ME4"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[]},{"id":"CL_MHON_04:GLA-11","text":"[Terminal] The system arrives at formal order, but its closure remains incomplete, because proportion is indicated rather than numerically demonstrated.","refs":["CH_MHON_TH_7:MRT5","CH_MHON_THEX:MRT4"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[]}],"original_name":"Global Load-Bearing Axioms"},{"number":"3","name":"Recurrence, Mutation, and Displacement Matrix","items":[{"id":"CL_MHON_04:RMD-01","text":"MONAS retains its identity as the name of unity, but changes function: it becomes at once container, process, and effect of differentiation.","refs":["CH_MHON_TH_8:A4","CH_MHON_TH_8:D6"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"Composite","passages":[]},{"id":"CL_MHON_04:RMD-02","text":"Separation returns in three forms: as geometric Lines, as the division of elements effected by fire, and as the partition of time.","refs":["CH_MHON_TH_7:NX1","CH_MHON_TH_8:A6","CH_MHON_THEX:NX3"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"Composite","passages":[]},{"id":"CL_MHON_04:RMD-03","text":"Circular form migrates from space to time: the circle becomes a schema of distribution and the semicircles become a figure of divisible time.","refs":["CH_MHON_TH_7:A4","CH_MHON_THEX:D1"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"Composite","passages":[]},{"id":"CL_MHON_04:RMD-04","text":"The denary proportion initially constitutes the rule of the elements; afterward it becomes hidden proportion, whose scope extends to Earth and the nycthemeron.","refs":["CH_MHON_TH_8:A7","CH_MHON_THEX:A6"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"Composite","passages":[]},{"id":"CL_MHON_04:RMD-05","text":"Aries reverses its force: what initially appears to be a sign of place later becomes an index of process.","refs":["CH_MHON_TH_8:ME1","CH_MHON_TH_8:A2"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"Composite","passages":[]},{"id":"CL_MHON_04:RMD-06","text":"Fire is transferred from a descriptive element to operative mediation and preserves separation as conversion.","refs":["CH_MHON_TH_8:ME4","CH_MHON_TH_8:D5"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"Composite","passages":[]},{"id":"CL_MHON_04:RMD-07","text":"“The hidden” does not remain pure absence, but is absorbed into calculable order as an attractor of interpretation.","refs":["CH_MHON_THEX:ME4","CH_MHON_THEX:SD4"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"Negative","passages":[]},{"id":"CL_MHON_04:RMD-08","text":"The reading of individual signs vanishes as a sufficient method, because the whole figure is taken as a nexus of relations.","refs":["CH_MHON_TH_8:DNX5","CH_MHON_TH_8:ME5"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"Composite","passages":[]}],"original_name":"Recurrence, Mutation, and Displacement Matrix"},{"number":"4","name":"Lower-Layer vs Current-Level Distinction","items":[{"id":"CL_MHON_04:CGD-01","text":"The construction of individual Lines, circles, semicircles, and common points remains local.","refs":["CH_MHON_TH_7:AS4","CH_MHON_THEX:A1"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[]},{"id":"CL_MHON_04:CGD-02","text":"The assignment of Aries to the beginning of the Fiery Triplicity also remains local, unless this assignment is amplified into a process.","refs":["CH_MHON_TH_8:A1","CH_MHON_TH_8:A10"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[]},{"id":"CL_MHON_04:CGD-03","text":"The principle that separation is not dissolution, but a stage of common order, is amplified upward.","refs":["CH_MHON_TH_7:NX2","CH_MHON_TH_8:ME3"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[]},{"id":"CL_MHON_04:CGD-04","text":"The function of mediation unites the sources: geometry, fire, and the equinox enact the same conversion between unity and distinction.","refs":["CH_MHON_TH_7:SD1","CH_MHON_TH_8:A8","CH_MHON_THEX:ME3"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"Composite","passages":[]},{"id":"CL_MHON_04:CGD-05","text":"The greater transition is effected by the translation from figure to process: the sign no longer merely indicates, but produces order.","refs":["CH_MHON_TH_8:ME1","CH_MHON_THEX:SD1"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"Composite","passages":[]},{"id":"CL_MHON_04:CGD-06","text":"The simple opposition between unity and division becomes obsolete, as does the opposition between heaven and Earth.","refs":["CH_MHON_TH_8:SNX1","CH_MHON_THEX:SD2"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"Negative","passages":[]},{"id":"CL_MHON_04:CGD-07","text":"Proportion is otherwise encoded: no longer merely as denary form, but as a trans-field relation among figure, time, and place.","refs":["CH_MHON_THEX:SD3","CH_MHON_THEX:D3"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"Composite","passages":[]}],"original_name":"Lower-Layer vs Current-Level Distinction"},{"number":"5","name":"Global Apophatic Structure","items":[{"id":"CL_MHON_04:GNX-01","text":"The system excludes immediate congruence asserted without prior separation and without geometric mediations.","refs":["CH_MHON_TH_7:DNX1","CH_MHON_TH_7:AS3"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"Negative","passages":[]},{"id":"CL_MHON_04:GNX-02","text":"The system excludes immovably closed unity, because MONAS is understood only through operative differentiation.","refs":["CH_MHON_TH_8:DNX1","CH_MHON_TH_8:SD1"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"Negative","passages":[]},{"id":"CL_MHON_04:GNX-03","text":"The system excludes interpretation of the sign as arbitrary convention or isolated image.","refs":["CH_MHON_THEX:SNX2","CH_MHON_THEX:DNX4"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"Negative","passages":[]},{"id":"CL_MHON_04:GNX-04","text":"The system excludes a proportion that can be separated from relation to Earth, time, or the elements.","refs":["CH_MHON_THEX:DNX3","CH_MHON_THEX:NX4"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"Negative","passages":[]},{"id":"CL_MHON_04:GNX-05","text":"The system excludes pure knowledge without operation: what is knowable here is what is effected through figure, partition, and mediation.","refs":["CH_MHON_TH_7:SD1","CH_MHON_TH_8:NX6"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"Composite","passages":[]}],"original_name":"Global Apophatic Structure"},{"number":"6","name":"Deep Book Apophatics","items":[{"id":"CL_MHON_04:DBN-01","text":"That which posits unity and division as absolutely incompatible principles must be false.","refs":["CH_MHON_TH_8:SNX1"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"Negative","passages":[]},{"id":"CL_MHON_04:DBN-02","text":"That the form of the sign alone, outside the context of relations, exhausts its meaning must be false.","refs":["CH_MHON_TH_8:DNX5"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"Negative","passages":[]},{"id":"CL_MHON_04:DBN-03","text":"That time is continuous and neutral must be false; the nycthemeron operates only as proportional distribution.","refs":["CH_MHON_THEX:NX3"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"Negative","passages":[]},{"id":"CL_MHON_04:DBN-04","text":"That proportion can be posited without the construction that produces it, even where its number is not transmitted, must be false.","refs":["CH_MHON_TH_7:DNX3","CH_MHON_THEX:SD4"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"Negative","passages":[]}],"original_name":"Deep Book Apophatics"},{"number":"7","name":"Super-Apophatics of the Whole","items":[{"id":"CL_MHON_04:BSA-01","text":"The system circumvents the possibility of an order that acknowledges no mediation among heaven, symbol, time, and Earth.","refs":["CH_MHON_TH_8:SNX4"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"Negative","passages":[]},{"id":"CL_MHON_04:BSA-02","text":"Pure unity without differentiation remains unavailable, because every unity here is manifested through transition and separation.","refs":["CH_MHON_TH_8:DNX1"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"Negative","passages":[]},{"id":"CL_MHON_04:BSA-03","text":"A wholly open and exhausted proportion is prohibited: the system requires a hidden remainder in order that recursive interpretation may persist.","refs":["CH_MHON_THEX:SNX3"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"Negative","passages":[]},{"id":"CL_MHON_04:BSA-04","text":"A schema that divides sign, process, and the terrestrial world into independent regions becomes ontologically inadmissible.","refs":["CH_MHON_THEX:SD2"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"Negative","passages":[]}],"original_name":"Super-Apophatics of the Whole"},{"number":"8","name":"Cross-Unit Derivative Propositions","items":[{"id":"CL_MHON_04:XD-01","text":"Geometric separation and the ministry of fire together bring it about that distinction is not a dissolutive outcome, but an ordered transition; temporal distribution transfers the same form to another field.","refs":["CL_MHON_04:GLA-02","CL_MHON_04:GLA-03","CL_MHON_04:GLA-06","CL_MHON_04:GLA-08","CH_MHON_TH_7:AS3","CH_MHON_TH_8:A8","CH_MHON_THEX:FGS3"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"From","passages":[]},{"id":"CL_MHON_04:XD-02","text":"Aries becomes the nexus between astronomical beginning and the internal operation of MONAS, because fire in it is simultaneously the beginning and medium of transformation.","refs":["CL_MHON_04:GLA-06","CL_MHON_04:GLA-07","CH_MHON_TH_8:AS2","CH_MHON_TH_8:SD5"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"From","passages":[]},{"id":"CL_MHON_04:XD-03","text":"The geometric circle and mystical semicircle converge upon one proposition: form is a distribution of relations, not a static image.","refs":["CL_MHON_04:GLA-03","CL_MHON_04:GLA-04","CH_MHON_TH_7:A5","CH_MHON_THEX:D1"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"From","passages":[]},{"id":"CL_MHON_04:XD-04","text":"Denary proportion in the elements and hidden proportion in time effect a distributed calculation, whose rule returns through various figures but is fully closed in none.","refs":["CL_MHON_04:GLA-05","CL_MHON_04:GLA-08","CL_MHON_04:GLA-10","CH_MHON_TH_8:A7","CH_MHON_THEX:A6","CH_MHON_THEX:SNX3"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"From","passages":[]},{"id":"CL_MHON_04:XD-05","text":"The opposition of separation and conjunction is dissolved when the three units show that they enact the same operations in different fields.","refs":["CL_MHON_04:GLA-01","CL_MHON_04:GLA-02","CH_MHON_TH_7:ME2","CH_MHON_TH_8:SD1"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"From","passages":[]},{"id":"CL_MHON_04:XD-06","text":"When figure is referred to Earth, the celestial no longer remains higher and the terrestrial lower, but they are proportionally coupled in one machine.","refs":["CL_MHON_04:GLA-08","CL_MHON_04:GLA-09","CH_MHON_THEX:A7","CH_MHON_THEX:SD2"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"From","passages":[]}],"original_name":"Cross-Unit Derivative Propositions"},{"number":"9","name":"Book-Scale Super-Derivatives","items":[{"id":"CL_MHON_04:BSD-01","text":"The system generates a general conversion whereby unity, figure, and time become not closed substances, but modes of ordering.","refs":["CH_MHON_TH_8:SD4","CH_MHON_THEX:SD3"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"Composite","passages":[]},{"id":"CL_MHON_04:BSD-02","text":"Astronomy, geometry, and alchemical praxis coalesce into a single operative logic, such that interpretation itself must imitate the process.","refs":["CH_MHON_TH_8:SD3","CH_MHON_TH_7:ALT3"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"Composite","passages":[]},{"id":"CL_MHON_04:BSD-03","text":"The interpreting subject becomes, from a spectator of signs, an operator who pursues separations, analogies, and reconstitutions among fields.","refs":["CH_MHON_TH_8:ME5","CH_MHON_TH_7:ME3"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"Composite","passages":[]},{"id":"CL_MHON_04:BSD-04","text":"Time is produced as formable matter: the nycthemeron not only contains hours, but disposes them into an order of hidden proportions.","refs":["CH_MHON_THEX:ME2","CH_MHON_THEX:D3"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"Composite","passages":[]},{"id":"CL_MHON_04:BSD-05","text":"The causality of the system is medial: place produces meaning through fire, figure through partition, partition through proportion.","refs":["CH_MHON_TH_8:D2","CH_MHON_TH_8:D5"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"Composite","passages":[]},{"id":"CL_MHON_04:BSD-06","text":"The methodological effect is that no notion remains valid outside the series of transformations that generates and reforms it.","refs":["CH_MHON_TH_7:SD2","CH_MHON_TH_7:MRT3"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"Composite","passages":[]}],"original_name":"Book-Scale Super-Derivatives"},{"number":"10","name":"Recursive Loops, Escalation Paths, and Conceptual Attractors","items":[{"id":"CL_MHON_04:RLE-01","text":"The principal circuit is: unity → separation → proportion → conjunction → unity reread as the capacity for differentiation.","refs":["CH_MHON_TH_8:D6","CH_MHON_TH_7:D3"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"Composite","passages":[]},{"id":"CL_MHON_04:RLE-02","text":"The trans-field circuit is: Aries → fire → figure → division of time → Earth → new meaning of Aries.","refs":["CH_MHON_TH_8:AF1","CH_MHON_THEX:SD2"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"Composite","passages":[]},{"id":"CL_MHON_04:RLE-03","text":"Amplification occurs when the same function of separation is transferred from space to element, from element to time, from time to the terrestrial world.","refs":["CH_MHON_TH_8:A6","CH_MHON_THEX:FGS5"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"Composite","passages":[]},{"id":"CL_MHON_04:RLE-04","text":"The hidden attractor is proportion: all elements converge upon it, but it always opens a new question about measure.","refs":["CH_MHON_THEX:SD4","CH_MHON_TH_7:MRT5"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"Tension","passages":[]},{"id":"CL_MHON_04:RLE-05","text":"The terminal point is formal closure through the DENARY Cross or equinoctial proportion; the same point becomes the beginning of a new interpretation, because the full number is absent.","refs":["CH_MHON_TH_7:A8","CH_MHON_THEX:MRT4"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"Tension","passages":[]},{"id":"CL_MHON_04:RLE-06","text":"Fragments scattered around figures, elements, and times converge upon one image: unity is not a simple beginning, but a recursive attractor.","refs":["CH_MHON_TH_8:FGS6","CH_MHON_TH_8:FGS5"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"Composite","passages":[]}],"original_name":"Recursive Loops, Escalation Paths, and Conceptual Attractors"},{"number":"11","name":"Formal Generative Schema of the Whole Book","items":[{"id":"CL_MHON_04:FGS-B01","text":"Initial unity → ordered separation → preservation of relation through proportion.","refs":["CH_MHON_TH_8:A4","CH_MHON_TH_8:A7"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"Composite","passages":[]},{"id":"CL_MHON_04:FGS-B02","text":"Elementary matter → Lines and circles → relational figure that gathers distinct forms into one construction.","refs":["CH_MHON_TH_7:A2","CH_MHON_TH_7:AS4"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"Composite","passages":[]},{"id":"CL_MHON_04:FGS-B03","text":"Astronomical figure → ministry of fire → internal division of MONAS → hieroglyph that simultaneously displays unity and difference.","refs":["CH_MHON_TH_8:A2","CH_MHON_TH_8:D5"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"Composite","passages":[]},{"id":"CL_MHON_04:FGS-B04","text":"Sign connected to time → equinoctial distribution → hidden proportion → terrestrial relation → reclassification of the sign as a machine.","refs":["CH_MHON_THEX:FGS2","CH_MHON_THEX:A7"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"Composite","passages":[]}],"original_name":"Formal Generative Schema of the Whole Book"},{"number":"12","name":"Whole-Book Argument Flow Map","items":[{"id":"CL_MHON_04:AFM-B01","text":"Question of congruence → exact separation → geometric conjunction → mediation of fire → denary proportion → differentiated unity.","refs":["CH_MHON_TH_7:AS1","CH_MHON_TH_7:SD2"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"Composite","passages":[]},{"id":"CL_MHON_04:AFM-B02","text":"Beginning of Aries → operative sign → composite figure → nycthemeral division → hidden proportion → relation to Earth.","refs":["CH_MHON_TH_8:FGS1","CH_MHON_THEX:AF1"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"Composite","passages":[]},{"id":"CL_MHON_04:AFM-B03","text":"Undivided unity → mediated differentiations → proportional convergence → formal closure → hidden remainder that renews recursion.","refs":["CH_MHON_TH_8:SD4","CH_MHON_THEX:SNX3"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"Tension","passages":[]}],"original_name":"Whole-Book Argument Flow Map"},{"number":"13","name":"Macro Map-Effects","items":[{"id":"CL_MHON_04:MME-01","text":"MONAS, SOL, and LVNA, as also Aries and the nycthemeron, are reclassified from separate things into stages of one distributed construction.","refs":["CH_MHON_TH_7:ME1","CH_MHON_THEX:ME1"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"Composite","passages":[]},{"id":"CL_MHON_04:MME-02","text":"The opposition between unity and distinction collapses; distinction becomes the act by which unity makes itself readable.","refs":["CH_MHON_TH_8:ME3","CH_MHON_TH_8:SD1"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"Composite","passages":[]},{"id":"CL_MHON_04:MME-03","text":"The temporal field becomes a field of construction, because hours, like Lines, are ordered into proportions through distribution.","refs":["CH_MHON_THEX:ME2","CH_MHON_THEX:FGS3"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"Composite","passages":[]},{"id":"CL_MHON_04:MME-04","text":"The hidden is reclassified from outside order into an internal remainder of order, which interpretation alone can reach through recursion.","refs":["CH_MHON_THEX:ME4","CH_MHON_THEX:FGS4"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"Composite","passages":[]},{"id":"CL_MHON_04:MME-05","text":"The linear method becomes insufficient; a reading is required that simultaneously composes figures, operations, times, and places.","refs":["CH_MHON_TH_8:ME5","CH_MHON_TH_8:D3"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"Composite","passages":[]}],"original_name":"Macro Map-Effects"},{"number":"14","name":"Altitude Integration","items":[{"id":"CL_MHON_04:ALT-B01","text":"The surface level contains MONAS, SOL, LVNA, Aries, fire, semicircles, hours, and Earth as explicit signs.","refs":["CH_MHON_TH_7:ALT1","CH_MHON_TH_8:ALT1"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[]},{"id":"CL_MHON_04:ALT-B02","text":"The structural level shows a common distribution: separation, mediation, circulation, and the equinox enact the same machine of ordering.","refs":["CH_MHON_TH_7:ALT2","CH_MHON_THEX:ALT2"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"Composite","passages":[]},{"id":"CL_MHON_04:ALT-B03","text":"The metastrategic level transfers the question from “what does the sign mean?” to “how does the sign produce relations?”.","refs":["CH_MHON_TH_7:ALT3","CH_MHON_THEX:ALT3"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"Composite","passages":[]},{"id":"CL_MHON_04:ALT-B04","text":"The ontological-temporal level constitutes a reality in which unity, time, and place do not preexist as givens, but are formed through operations.","refs":["CH_MHON_TH_7:ALT4","CH_MHON_THEX:ALT4"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"Composite","passages":[]},{"id":"CL_MHON_04:ALT-B05","text":"The methodological and performative level compels the reader to carry out the conversions themselves: to read the sign is to repeat its order.","refs":["CH_MHON_TH_8:ME5","CH_MHON_TH_7:ME3"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"Composite","passages":[]}],"original_name":"Altitude Integration"},{"number":"15","name":"Internal Tensions, Mutation Points, and Fault Lines","items":[{"id":"CL_MHON_04:FL-01","text":"The joint between circular conjunction and the quantification of proportion remains fragile, because the construction is shown but the numerical rule is not completed.","refs":["CH_MHON_TH_7:MRT5","CH_MHON_TH_7:MRT6"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"Tension","passages":[]},{"id":"CL_MHON_04:FL-02","text":"The status of fire oscillates among element, sign, and ministry; this ambiguity is not removed, because it itself sustains its mediation.","refs":["CH_MHON_TH_8:MRT5","CH_MHON_TH_8:NX4"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"Tension","passages":[]},{"id":"CL_MHON_04:FL-03","text":"The analogy between semicircles and hours operates as a foundation, although the schema by which parts of the figure correspond to individual parts of time is lacking.","refs":["CH_MHON_THEX:MRT3","CH_MHON_THEX:SNX1"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"Tension","passages":[]},{"id":"CL_MHON_04:MP-01","text":"The first mutation occurs when Aries ceases to be a zodiacal location and becomes the beginning of internal transformation.","refs":["CH_MHON_TH_8:ME1","CH_MHON_TH_8:SD5"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"Tension","passages":[]},{"id":"CL_MHON_04:MP-02","text":"The second mutation occurs when separation ceases to be disjunction and becomes the act by which unity constitutes its own form.","refs":["CH_MHON_TH_8:ME3","CH_MHON_TH_8:SD4"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"Composite","passages":[]},{"id":"CL_MHON_04:MP-03","text":"The third mutation occurs when the hidden ceases to be outside order and becomes an attractor within calculable distribution.","refs":["CH_MHON_THEX:ME4","CH_MHON_THEX:ME2"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"Composite","passages":[]}],"original_name":"Internal Tensions, Mutation Points, and Fault Lines"},{"number":"16","name":"Hidden System Image","items":[{"id":"CL_MHON_04:HSI-01","text":"The system implicitly presents the image of a circulatory machine: parts are separated, transformed through mediations, and return into a common order.","refs":["CH_MHON_TH_7:SD2","CH_MHON_TH_7:ME2"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"Composite","passages":[]},{"id":"CL_MHON_04:HSI-02","text":"The subject of this machine is an operative unity that preserves itself not through immobility, but through repetition of difference.","refs":["CH_MHON_TH_8:D6","CH_MHON_TH_8:SD1"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"Composite","passages":[]},{"id":"CL_MHON_04:HSI-03","text":"Time is spiral rather than linear: its distribution returns to hidden proportions and represents them in new fields.","refs":["CH_MHON_THEX:A6","CH_MHON_THEX:ME4"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"Composite","passages":[]},{"id":"CL_MHON_04:HSI-04","text":"The central contest is between formal closure and hidden remainder; escape remains possible through a new translation of sign, time, and place.","refs":["CH_MHON_THEX:SNX3","CH_MHON_THEX:MRT5"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"Tension","passages":[]}],"original_name":"Hidden System Image"},{"number":"17","name":"Minimal Whole-Book Reconstruction Test","items":[{"id":"CL_MHON_04:MRT-B01","text":"The present axioms suffice to restore the minimal machine: unity, separation, mediation, proportion, figure, and temporal distribution.","refs":["CH_MHON_TH_7:MRT1","CH_MHON_TH_8:MRT2"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"Composite","passages":[]},{"id":"CL_MHON_04:MRT-B02","text":"Global coherence is strongest in the principle that unity operates through distinction; it is less strong in the translation of figure into exact measure.","refs":["CH_MHON_TH_8:MRT3","CH_MHON_TH_7:MRT5"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"Tension","passages":[]},{"id":"CL_MHON_04:MRT-B03","text":"The reconstruction preserves the true movement, because the return of notions is read not as simple repetition, but as mutation of field and function.","refs":["CH_MHON_TH_8:MRT7","CH_MHON_THEX:MRT2"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"Composite","passages":[]},{"id":"CL_MHON_04:MRT-B04","text":"The numerical definition of proportion, the exact status of fire, and the schema by which figure is partitioned into time still resist full reconstruction.","refs":["CH_MHON_TH_7:MRT4","CH_MHON_TH_8:MRT4","CH_MHON_THEX:MRT4"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"Tension","passages":[]}],"original_name":"Minimal Whole-Book Reconstruction Test"}],"revision":"CL_MHON_04@R001","source_variant_id":"canonical:CL_MHON_04:v1","source_language_code":"la","original_title":"Chapters 10–12"}],"identity":{"layer":"CL","kicker":"Cluster · Monas Hieroglyphica","title":"Chapters 10–12","context":"T H E O R. I X. · T H E O R. X. · Theorem Xi.","original_title":"Chapters 10–12","original_context":"T H E O R. I X. · T H E O R. X. · Theor. Xi."},"original_title":"Chapters 10–12","title_language_code":"en"},{"id":"CL_MHON_05","layer":"CL","title":"Chapters 13–15","order":5,"parents":["CH_MHON_TH_9","CH_MHON_T_10","CH_MHON_T_11"],"revision":"CL_MHON_05@R001","sha256":"f3749d9b19a5521dcf11743d9d2d2b1bfd4d23b4c76e264aa7a9a4cf0e2ece2b","sections":[{"number":"1","name":"Book-Scale Argumental Spine","items":[{"id":"CL_MHON_05:BS-01","text":"Systema quaerit quomodo figurae planetariae ex compositione signorum, successione elementorum et influxu caelesti-terrestri efficiantur.","refs":["CH_MHON_TH_9:AS1","CH_MHON_T_10:AS1","CH_MHON_T_11:AS1"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[]},{"id":"CL_MHON_05:BS-02","text":"Quaestio de signis in quaestionem de productione, transformatione et transmissione convertitur.","refs":["CH_MHON_TH_9:AS2","CH_MHON_T_10:AS2","CH_MHON_T_11:AS2"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[]},{"id":"CL_MHON_05:BS-03","text":"Fundamentum commune est translatio formarum visibilium in ordines operativos quos regulae elementares et cosmologicae gubernant.","refs":["CH_MHON_TH_9:AS3","CH_MHON_T_10:AS3"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[]},{"id":"CL_MHON_05:BS-04","text":"Systema planetarium figuras non tamquam entia separata, sed tamquam gradus unius naturae transformativae componit.","refs":["CH_MHON_TH_9:SD1","CH_MHON_T_10:SD1"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[]},{"id":"CL_MHON_05:BS-05","text":"Motus a Luna et Sole per Mercurium, animam, radios et Terram Lemniam ad effectum materialem et nutritivum procedit.","refs":["CH_MHON_TH_9:SD2","CH_MHON_T_11:D1"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[]},{"id":"CL_MHON_05:BS-06","text":"Genus argumentationis est reconstructivum, analogicum et alchemicum, quia compositiones symbolicas in processum cosmologicum restituit.","refs":["CH_MHON_T_10:AS6","CH_MHON_T_11:SD1"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[]},{"id":"CL_MHON_05:BS-07","text":"Motus non est linearis: per revolutionem, substitutionem, reclassificatio-nem et reditum ad novum medium spiralis fit.","refs":["CH_MHON_TH_9:A5","CH_MHON_T_10:ME4"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[]},{"id":"CL_MHON_05:BS-08","text":"Clausura fit distributiva: figura hieroglyphica in Terra imprimitur, dum Magisterium inter fontes caelestes et receptaculum terrestre circulat.","refs":["CH_MHON_TH_9:A11","CH_MHON_T_11:SD1"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[]}],"original_name":"Book-Scale Argumental Spine"},{"number":"2","name":"Global Load-Bearing Axioms","items":[{"id":"CL_MHON_05:GA-01","text":"[Persistent] Signum planetarium constituitur compositione plurium characterum, non designatione simplici et isolata.","refs":["CH_MHON_TH_9:A2"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[]},{"id":"CL_MHON_05:GA-02","text":"[Persistent] Luna, Sol, Elementa et Aries sunt termini functionales quorum coniunctio figuram determinat.","refs":["CH_MHON_TH_9:A2"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[]},{"id":"CL_MHON_05:GA-03","text":"[Mutating] Natura Lunaris per gradus et revolutiones sub nominibus planetariis varias functiones recipit.","refs":["CH_MHON_TH_9:A5"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[]},{"id":"CL_MHON_05:GA-04","text":"[Emergent] Cosmologia, alchimia et anthropologia in Mercurio tamquam nodo communi coalescunt.","refs":["CH_MHON_T_10:SD2"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[]},{"id":"CL_MHON_05:GA-05","text":"[Mutating] Mercurius transit a quarta revolutione Lunaris ordinis ad Microcosmum et Adam, ita ut planeta fiat forma hominis cosmici.","refs":["CH_MHON_TH_9:A6","CH_MHON_T_10:A7"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[]},{"id":"CL_MHON_05:GA-06","text":"[Attractor] Perfectio magiae lunaris et Solaris ad apparitionem Mercurii conspicui tendit.","refs":["CH_MHON_T_10:A6"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[]},{"id":"CL_MHON_05:GA-07","text":"[Mutating] Anima separata a principio spirituali in vinculum operativum corporum planetariorum convertitur.","refs":["CH_MHON_T_10:A9"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[]},{"id":"CL_MHON_05:GA-08","text":"[Persistent] Sol et Luna principia generativa sunt, Terra Lemnia autem locum nutritionis et continuationis praebet.","refs":["CH_MHON_T_11:A1","CH_MHON_T_11:A4"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[]},{"id":"CL_MHON_05:GA-09","text":"[Emergent] Radii efficiunt medium commune quo principia caelestia in operationem circa terram transeunt.","refs":["CH_MHON_T_11:A5","CH_MHON_T_11:D3"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[]},{"id":"CL_MHON_05:GA-10","text":"[Terminal] Figura hieroglyphica tantum completur cum Spiritus eam in Terram introducit et imprimit.","refs":["CH_MHON_TH_9:A11"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[]},{"id":"CL_MHON_05:GA-11","text":"[Attractor] Anatomia Monadis Hieroglyphicae ordinem planetarium et arcana Theorematum in unam tabulam internam contrahit.","refs":["CH_MHON_T_10:A11","CH_MHON_T_10:ME5"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[]},{"id":"CL_MHON_05:GA-12","text":"[Persistent] Traditio auctoritatem praebet, sed mechanismus operativus eam per compositionem, influxum et transformationem efficit.","refs":["CH_MHON_TH_9:A1","CH_MHON_T_11:A6"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[]}],"original_name":"Global Load-Bearing Axioms"},{"number":"3","name":"Recurrence, Mutation, and Displacement Matrix","items":[{"id":"CL_MHON_05:RMD-01","text":"Compositio signorum redit, sed munus eius mutatur: a descriptione figurae ad genealogiam transformationis transit.","refs":["CH_MHON_TH_9:A12","CH_MHON_T_10:SD1"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[]},{"id":"CL_MHON_05:RMD-02","text":"Luna primum principium interpretativum est, deinde medium substituibile quod Spiritus Magicus sine ruptura operis assumit.","refs":["CH_MHON_TH_9:A8","CH_MHON_TH_9:NX5"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[]},{"id":"CL_MHON_05:RMD-03","text":"Sol a componente hieroglyphico in principium parentale, medium naturale et influxum radialem dislocatur.","refs":["CH_MHON_TH_9:A9","CH_MHON_T_11:SD3"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[]},{"id":"CL_MHON_05:RMD-04","text":"Elementa a signis classificatoriis in Magisterium quod compositionem et regenerationem dirigit mutantur.","refs":["CH_MHON_TH_9:A4","CH_MHON_T_10:A3"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[]},{"id":"CL_MHON_05:RMD-05","text":"Mercurius redit sub formis diversis, sed munus eius semper ad transitum inter ordines spectat.","refs":["CH_MHON_TH_9:A6","CH_MHON_T_10:A7"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[]},{"id":"CL_MHON_05:RMD-06","text":"Terra a superficie receptiva figurarum ad nutrimentum activum Magisterii elevatur.","refs":["CH_MHON_TH_9:A10","CH_MHON_T_11:A4"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[]},{"id":"CL_MHON_05:RMD-07","text":"Spiritus Magicus et anima separata eandem mediationis functionem in diversis ordinibus agunt, alter pro Luna, altera pro corporibus planetariis.","refs":["CH_MHON_TH_9:SD4","CH_MHON_T_10:A9"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[]},{"id":"CL_MHON_05:RMD-08","text":"Genealogia redit duplici forma: ut successio Saturni, Iovis et Mercurii, et ut dispositio patris, matris et terrae.","refs":["CH_MHON_TH_9:D2","CH_MHON_T_11:D4"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[]},{"id":"CL_MHON_05:RMD-09","text":"Septenarius a numeratione corporum in ordinem graduum, substitutionum et transformationum convertitur.","refs":["CH_MHON_T_10:SD4"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[]},{"id":"CL_MHON_05:RMD-10","text":"Reditus formarum non identitatem servat, sed novum statum producit: signum fit processus, processus fit corpus, corpus fit receptaculum.","refs":["CH_MHON_TH_9:SD2","CH_MHON_T_11:SD2"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[]}],"original_name":"Recurrence, Mutation, and Displacement Matrix"},{"number":"4","name":"Lower-Layer vs Current-Level Distinction","items":[{"id":"CL_MHON_05:CGD-01","text":"Singulae unitates inferiores retinent quaestiones locales de numero revolutionum, formis Mercurii et exacta correspondentia anatomica.","refs":["CH_MHON_TH_9:MRT4","CH_MHON_T_10:MRT3"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[]},{"id":"CL_MHON_05:CGD-02","text":"In gradu praesenti hae quaestiones amplificantur in problema communem de identitate quae per transformationem, non per statum fixum, definitur.","refs":["CH_MHON_TH_9:SD3","CH_MHON_T_10:SNX4"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[]},{"id":"CL_MHON_05:CGD-03","text":"Compositio Martis, Veneris et Mercurii cum triade Solis, Lunae et Terrae in unam architecturam genealogicam coniungitur.","refs":["CH_MHON_T_10:SD1","CH_MHON_T_11:D1"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[]},{"id":"CL_MHON_05:CGD-04","text":"Substitutiones Lunae, Solis et Spiritus, quae localiter conditionalia sunt, hic efficiunt regulam generalem medii commutabilis.","refs":["CH_MHON_TH_9:D4","CH_MHON_T_10:NX4"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[]},{"id":"CL_MHON_05:CGD-05","text":"Impressio in Terram et nutritio Terrae Lemniae ad eandem logicam receptionis activae rediguntur.","refs":["CH_MHON_TH_9:D6","CH_MHON_T_11:SD2"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[]},{"id":"CL_MHON_05:CGD-06","text":"Auctoritas Hermetica, localiter testimonium, in hac magnitudine fit pars mechanismi quo genealogia symbolica efficaciam acquirit.","refs":["CH_MHON_T_11:SD4"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[]},{"id":"CL_MHON_05:CGD-07","text":"Obsolescit imago planetarum independentium, quia omnes formae in ordinem communem circulationis et regenerationis absorbentur.","refs":["CH_MHON_T_10:DNX1"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[]},{"id":"CL_MHON_05:CGD-08","text":"Novum obiectum huius gradus est machina quae figuram, genealogiam, animam, influxum et materiam eodem processu distribuit.","refs":["CH_MHON_T_10:ME6","CH_MHON_T_11:SD1"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[]}],"original_name":"Lower-Layer vs Current-Level Distinction"},{"number":"5","name":"Global Apophatic Structure","items":[{"id":"CL_MHON_05:GNX-01","text":"Systema negat signum esse designationem meram quae a compositione et productione separari possit.","refs":["CH_MHON_TH_9:DNX1"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[]},{"id":"CL_MHON_05:GNX-02","text":"Systema negat planetas esse nomina disiuncta sine successione unius Naturae Lunaris.","refs":["CH_MHON_TH_9:DNX2"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[]},{"id":"CL_MHON_05:GNX-03","text":"Systema negat causalitatem univocam, sive caelestem sive terrestrem, quia Magisterium ex influxu et nutritione simul dependet.","refs":["CH_MHON_T_11:DNX2"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[]},{"id":"CL_MHON_05:GNX-04","text":"Systema negat communicationem hieroglyphicam a voce materiali pendere; silentium hic medium operativum fieri potest.","refs":["CH_MHON_TH_9:NX4","CH_MHON_TH_9:ME5"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[]},{"id":"CL_MHON_05:GNX-05","text":"Systema negat Terram esse passivam superficiem, quia recipit, nutrit et figuras producit.","refs":["CH_MHON_TH_9:ME4","CH_MHON_T_11:NX2"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[]},{"id":"CL_MHON_05:GNX-06","text":"Systema negat traditionem auctoritatem sufficientem esse sine mechanismo influxus et transformationis.","refs":["CH_MHON_T_11:DNX5"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[]},{"id":"CL_MHON_05:GNX-07","text":"Systema negat separationem rigidam inter astronomiam, alchemiam, anthropologiam et operationem materialem.","refs":["CH_MHON_T_10:DNX2","CH_MHON_T_10:SD2"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[]}],"original_name":"Global Apophatic Structure"},{"number":"6","name":"Deep Book Apophatics","items":[{"id":"CL_MHON_05:DBN-01","text":"Ut systema consistat, figura non potest esse ornamentum quod a processu formationis seiungitur.","refs":["CH_MHON_TH_9:SNX1"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[]},{"id":"CL_MHON_05:DBN-02","text":"Falsum esse debet mundum in quo anima, elementa et planetae ordines penitus heterogenei manent.","refs":["CH_MHON_T_10:SNX3"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[]},{"id":"CL_MHON_05:DBN-03","text":"Falsum est Magisterium ex uno fonte simplici oriri, quia caeleste principium sine nutritione terrestri deficit.","refs":["CH_MHON_T_11:DNX2","CH_MHON_T_11:DNX4"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[]},{"id":"CL_MHON_05:DBN-04","text":"Falsum est regenerationem eventum naturalem sine arte, magia et mediatione animae esse.","refs":["CH_MHON_T_10:DNX3"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[]},{"id":"CL_MHON_05:DBN-05","text":"Systema circumit regulam exactam qua singulae formae ex successionibus elementatis producuntur, sed sine hac regula eius genealogia dissolveretur.","refs":["CH_MHON_TH_9:SNX4"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[]}],"original_name":"Deep Book Apophatics"},{"number":"7","name":"Super-Apophatics of the Whole","items":[{"id":"CL_MHON_05:BSA-01","text":"Systema circumagit possibilitatem artificii humani autonomi, quia origo et potentia Magisterii extra humanam actionem collocantur.","refs":["CH_MHON_T_11:SNX1"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[]},{"id":"CL_MHON_05:BSA-02","text":"Indisponibilis fit caelum sine efficacia, quia radii non sunt merae imagines sed media influxus.","refs":["CH_MHON_T_11:SNX2"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[]},{"id":"CL_MHON_05:BSA-03","text":"Inadmissibilis est separatio absoluta inter symbolicam genealogiam et operationem naturalem.","refs":["CH_MHON_T_11:SNX3"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[]},{"id":"CL_MHON_05:BSA-04","text":"Inadmissibilis est origo unica quae Magisterium tantum Soli, Lunae aut Terrae attribuat.","refs":["CH_MHON_T_11:SNX4"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[]},{"id":"CL_MHON_05:BSA-05","text":"Systema tacite excludit mundum in quo figura, anima, influxus et materia ordines clausi ac non-transmissibiles sint.","refs":["CH_MHON_TH_9:SNX5"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[]}],"original_name":"Super-Apophatics of the Whole"},{"number":"8","name":"Cross-Unit Derivative Propositions","items":[{"id":"CL_MHON_05:XD-01","text":"Compositio hieroglyphica et triadica causalitas efficiunt ut signum simul originem, medium et receptaculum designet.","refs":["CH_MHON_TH_9:A2","CH_MHON_T_11:D1"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"Composite","passages":[]},{"id":"CL_MHON_05:XD-02","text":"Successio planetaria et Solaris circulatio in Mercurium conveniunt tamquam in attractorem transformationis, non tamquam in nomen ultimum.","refs":["CH_MHON_TH_9:SD1","CH_MHON_T_10:A6"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"Composite","passages":[]},{"id":"CL_MHON_05:XD-03","text":"Mercurius ut Microcosmus et Magisterium ut coniunctio caelestis-terrestris eundem transitum ab ordine cosmico ad ordinem humanum efficiunt.","refs":["CH_MHON_T_10:A7","CH_MHON_T_10:SD2"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"Composite","passages":[]},{"id":"CL_MHON_05:XD-04","text":"Substitutio Lunae per Spiritum et alternatio loci Solis ac Mercurii ostendunt identitatem planetariam esse functionem medii, non substantiam fixam.","refs":["CH_MHON_TH_9:NX5","CH_MHON_T_10:NX4"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"Tension","passages":[]},{"id":"CL_MHON_05:XD-05","text":"Anima separata et radii constituunt duas formas mediationis quae corpus planetarium et Magisterium eandem circulationem participare faciunt.","refs":["CH_MHON_T_10:A9","CH_MHON_T_11:D3"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"Composite","passages":[]},{"id":"CL_MHON_05:XD-06","text":"Terra fit receptaculum commune figurae impressae et influxus nutritivi, ita ut materialitas iam non sit terminus passivus.","refs":["CH_MHON_TH_9:A10","CH_MHON_T_11:A4"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"Composite","passages":[]},{"id":"CL_MHON_05:XD-07","text":"Testimonium Hermeticum et mechanismus radialis auctoritatem ac efficaciam in unam legitimam machinam colligunt.","refs":["CH_MHON_T_11:SD4"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"Composite","passages":[]},{"id":"CL_MHON_05:XD-08","text":"Assertio de correspondentia exacta simul systema firmat et fragile reddit, quia ordinem promittit quem mechanismus plene non explicat.","refs":["CH_MHON_T_10:MRT6","CH_MHON_T_11:MRT5"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"Tension","passages":[]}],"original_name":"Cross-Unit Derivative Propositions"},{"number":"9","name":"Book-Scale Super-Derivatives","items":[{"id":"CL_MHON_05:BSD-01","text":"Hieroglyphicum fit machina distributa quae ordinem cosmologicum in operationem magicam et inscriptionem materialem convertit.","refs":["CH_MHON_TH_9:SD2","CH_MHON_T_10:ME6"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[]},{"id":"CL_MHON_05:BSD-02","text":"Cosmologia, alchimia et anthropologia non simpliciter coniunguntur, sed per eundem processum genealogicum fiunt aspectus unius machinae.","refs":["CH_MHON_T_10:SD2","CH_MHON_T_10:SD1"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[]},{"id":"CL_MHON_05:BSD-03","text":"Causalitas systematis distribuitur in generationem caelestem, receptionem terrestrem, nutritionem et inscriptionem.","refs":["CH_MHON_T_11:SD2","CH_MHON_TH_9:D6"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[]},{"id":"CL_MHON_05:BSD-04","text":"Subiectum humanum non praesupponitur, sed per Mercurium, Adam et animam separatam tamquam nodus operativus producitur.","refs":["CH_MHON_T_10:D5","CH_MHON_T_10:ME3"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[]},{"id":"CL_MHON_05:BSD-05","text":"Tempus fit series revolutionum et graduum transformationis, non linea externa cui processi superponuntur.","refs":["CH_MHON_TH_9:ALT2","CH_MHON_T_10:A5"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[]},{"id":"CL_MHON_05:BSD-06","text":"Lectio figurae fit investigatio mechanismi, quia signum, anatomia et character planetarius processum occultum comprimunt.","refs":["CH_MHON_TH_9:ME1","CH_MHON_T_10:ME5"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[]},{"id":"CL_MHON_05:BSD-07","text":"Origo simplex evanescit et loco eius emergit rete dependentiarum inter principia, media, receptacula et gradus.","refs":["CH_MHON_T_11:SNX4","CH_MHON_T_10:SD4"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[]},{"id":"CL_MHON_05:BSD-08","text":"Effectus totalis est circulatio caelestis-terrestris in qua influxus, forma et materia se invicem producunt et sustinent.","refs":["CH_MHON_T_11:ME5","CH_MHON_T_11:SD1"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[]}],"original_name":"Book-Scale Super-Derivatives"},{"number":"10","name":"Recursive Loops, Escalation Paths, and Conceptual Attractors","items":[{"id":"CL_MHON_05:RLE-01","text":"Compositio producit successionem, successio transformationem, transformatio figuram materialem; figura materialis vicissim ordinem occultum testatur.","refs":["CH_MHON_TH_9:D6","CH_MHON_T_10:ME6"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"Composite","passages":[]},{"id":"CL_MHON_05:RLE-02","text":"Luna in Spiritum transit, Spiritus operationem administrat, et operatio reconstituit unitatem quae Lunae principio assignabatur.","refs":["CH_MHON_TH_9:D4","CH_MHON_TH_9:SD4"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"Composite","passages":[]},{"id":"CL_MHON_05:RLE-03","text":"Sol et Mercurius oscillant inter principium, locum et substitutum; haec instabilitas septenarium ampliat potius quam dissolvit.","refs":["CH_MHON_T_10:NX4","CH_MHON_T_10:ME4"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"Tension","passages":[]},{"id":"CL_MHON_05:RLE-04","text":"Nomina Saturni, Iovis et Mercurii sunt puncta amplificationis quibus una Natura Lunaris gradatim novas functiones recipit.","refs":["CH_MHON_TH_9:A5","CH_MHON_T_10:D3"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"Composite","passages":[]},{"id":"CL_MHON_05:RLE-05","text":"Genealogia caelestis transit in receptaculum terrestre, et receptaculum per radios ad genealogiam initialem refertur.","refs":["CH_MHON_T_11:ME4"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"Composite","passages":[]},{"id":"CL_MHON_05:RLE-06","text":"Mercurius et Monas sunt attractores quibus ordines planetarius, anthropologicus et anatomicus in unam figuram convergunt.","refs":["CH_MHON_T_10:A7","CH_MHON_T_10:ME5"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"Composite","passages":[]},{"id":"CL_MHON_05:RLE-07","text":"Puncta incerta de revolutionibus, formis Mercurii et correspondentia anatomica non sunt margines externi, sed locis ipsis attractoribus vim dant.","refs":["CH_MHON_TH_9:MRT4","CH_MHON_T_10:MRT3"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"Tension","passages":[]}],"original_name":"Recursive Loops, Escalation Paths, and Conceptual Attractors"},{"number":"11","name":"Formal Generative Schema of the Whole Book","items":[{"id":"CL_MHON_05:FGS-B01","text":"Compositio Solis, Lunae, Elementorum aut Arietis → character hieroglyphicus → signum cum origine astrali et determinatione elementari.","refs":["CH_MHON_TH_9:A2","CH_MHON_T_10:D1"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[]},{"id":"CL_MHON_05:FGS-B02","text":"Character hieroglyphicus → Natura Lunaris vel Solaris → revolutio → gradus planetarius → genealogia transformationis.","refs":["CH_MHON_TH_9:A4","CH_MHON_T_10:SD1"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[]},{"id":"CL_MHON_05:FGS-B03","text":"Perfectio magiae lunaris et Solaris → Mercurius conspicuus → Microcosmus et Adam → subiectum cosmologicum.","refs":["CH_MHON_T_10:A6","CH_MHON_T_10:A7"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[]},{"id":"CL_MHON_05:FGS-B04","text":"Mercurius vel Luna → Spiritus Magicus aut anima separata → vinculum corporum et mediatio operativa.","refs":["CH_MHON_TH_9:A8","CH_MHON_T_10:A9"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[]},{"id":"CL_MHON_05:FGS-B05","text":"Sol + Luna → principia generativa; Terra Lemnia → receptio et nutritio; radii → influxus communis.","refs":["CH_MHON_T_11:A1","CH_MHON_T_11:A4","CH_MHON_T_11:A5"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[]},{"id":"CL_MHON_05:FGS-B06","text":"Influxus et mediatio → figura in Terram introducta → impressio materialis → circulus reditivus inter formam et processum.","refs":["CH_MHON_TH_9:A11","CH_MHON_TH_9:D6"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[]}],"original_name":"Formal Generative Schema of the Whole Book"},{"number":"12","name":"Whole-Book Argument Flow Map","items":[{"id":"CL_MHON_05:AFM-B01","text":"Compositio signorum → reclassificatio planetarum → Natura Lunaris et Solaris → Mercurius ut attractor → anima et Spiritus ut media → Terra Lemnia ut receptaculum → influxus radialis → impressio et nutritio.","refs":["CH_MHON_T_10:SD1","CH_MHON_T_10:A7","CH_MHON_T_11:D1"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[]},{"id":"CL_MHON_05:AFM-B02","text":"Forma hieroglyphica → interpretatio processualis → substitutio medii → translatio in corpus, animam et Terram → confirmatio per anatomiam Monadis.","refs":["CH_MHON_TH_9:SD2","CH_MHON_T_10:ME5"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[]},{"id":"CL_MHON_05:AFM-B03","text":"Dependentia caelestis → receptio terrestris → influxus radialis → operatio materialis → reconstitutio unitatis inter caelum et terram.","refs":["CH_MHON_T_11:AF1","CH_MHON_T_11:ME5"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[]}],"original_name":"Whole-Book Argument Flow Map"},{"number":"13","name":"Macro Map-Effects","items":[{"id":"CL_MHON_05:MME-01","text":"Obiectum hieroglyphicum reclassificatur ut nexus inter originem astralem, compositionem elementarem et gradum operationis.","refs":["CH_MHON_TH_9:ME1"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[]},{"id":"CL_MHON_05:MME-02","text":"Oppositio Lunae et Solis dissolvitur in differentias principii, medii, substitutionis et complementi.","refs":["CH_MHON_TH_9:ME2"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[]},{"id":"CL_MHON_05:MME-03","text":"Nomina Saturni, Iovis et Mercurii fiunt stationes transformationis unius Naturae Lunaris, non entia planetaria isolata.","refs":["CH_MHON_TH_9:ME3"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[]},{"id":"CL_MHON_05:MME-04","text":"Terra fit nodus productivus ubi figurae et influxus simul recipiuntur, transformantur et continuantur.","refs":["CH_MHON_TH_9:ME4","CH_MHON_T_11:ME2"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[]},{"id":"CL_MHON_05:MME-05","text":"Silentium verborum reclassificatur ut condicio communicationis hieroglyphicae et non ut eius defectus.","refs":["CH_MHON_TH_9:ME5"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[]},{"id":"CL_MHON_05:MME-06","text":"Anima separata reclassificatur a principio interiori ad instrumentum ligans corpora planetaria et ordinem alchemicum.","refs":["CH_MHON_T_10:ME3"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[]},{"id":"CL_MHON_05:MME-07","text":"Anatomia Monadis fit tabula compressa qua cosmologia, alchimia, anthropologia et ars lectionis in eodem agere possunt.","refs":["CH_MHON_T_10:SD5","CH_MHON_T_10:ME5"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[]}],"original_name":"Macro Map-Effects"},{"number":"14","name":"Altitude Integration","items":[{"id":"CL_MHON_05:ALT-B01","text":"Gradus superficiei exhibet characteres planetarios, signa elementaria, animam, radios et Terram ut vocabula explicita.","refs":["CH_MHON_T_10:ALT1","CH_MHON_T_11:ALT1"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[]},{"id":"CL_MHON_05:ALT-B02","text":"Gradus structuralis distribuit successiones, revolutiones, substitutiones, genealogias et radios tamquam mechanismos mediationis.","refs":["CH_MHON_T_10:ALT2","CH_MHON_T_11:ALT2"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[]},{"id":"CL_MHON_05:ALT-B03","text":"Gradus metastrategicus lectionem nominum et figurarum in restitutionem ordinis productivi mutat.","refs":["CH_MHON_TH_9:ALT3","CH_MHON_T_10:ALT3"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[]},{"id":"CL_MHON_05:ALT-B04","text":"Gradus ontologicus realitatem constituit ut seriem transformationum inter caelum, animam, corpus, elementa et materiam.","refs":["CH_MHON_T_10:ALT4","CH_MHON_T_11:ALT4"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[]},{"id":"CL_MHON_05:ALT-B05","text":"Gradus methodologicus efficit ut figura ipsa probatio sit: quod legitur tamquam signum simul producit ordinem quem significat.","refs":["CH_MHON_TH_9:SNX5","CH_MHON_T_10:ME6"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[]}],"original_name":"Altitude Integration"},{"number":"15","name":"Internal Tensions, Mutation Points, and Fault Lines","items":[{"id":"CL_MHON_05:FL-01","text":"Systema affirmat correspondentiam exactam inter Monadem et arcana, sed probatio mechanismum huius correspondentiae plene non exhibet.","refs":["CH_MHON_T_10:A11","CH_MHON_T_10:MRT6"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"Tension","passages":[]},{"id":"CL_MHON_05:FL-02","text":"Mercurius inter planetam, Microcosmum, Adam et locum substituibilem oscillat; oscillatio ordinem auget, sed identitatem eius instabilem relinquit.","refs":["CH_MHON_T_10:A7","CH_MHON_T_10:MRT3"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"Tension","passages":[]},{"id":"CL_MHON_05:FL-03","text":"Auctoritas Hermetica relationem confirmat, sed transitus ab auctoritate ad efficaciam radialem manet commissura fragilis.","refs":["CH_MHON_T_11:MRT5"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"Tension","passages":[]},{"id":"CL_MHON_05:FL-04","text":"Causalitas dividitur inter principia caelestia et Terram nutritivam, sed influxus radialis utrumque ordinem in unum medium cogit.","refs":["CH_MHON_T_11:D2","CH_MHON_T_11:D3"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"Tension","passages":[]},{"id":"CL_MHON_05:MP-01","text":"Quarta revolutio punctum mutationis est quo Natura Lunaris in Mercurium transit sine ruptura ordinis prioris.","refs":["CH_MHON_TH_9:A6","CH_MHON_TH_9:D3"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"Composite","passages":[]},{"id":"CL_MHON_05:MP-02","text":"Substitutio Lunae per Spiritum Magicum punctum mutationis est quo medium mutatur, finis operis autem persistit.","refs":["CH_MHON_TH_9:D4"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"Composite","passages":[]},{"id":"CL_MHON_05:MP-03","text":"Transitus a figura ad impressionem punctum mutationis est quo hieroglyphicum desinit esse tantum interpretandum et fit agens materiale.","refs":["CH_MHON_TH_9:D6","CH_MHON_T_10:ME6"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"Composite","passages":[]}],"original_name":"Internal Tensions, Mutation Points, and Fault Lines"},{"number":"16","name":"Hidden System Image","items":[{"id":"CL_MHON_05:HSI-01","text":"Systema realitatem implicite fingit ut machinam circulationis in qua principia caelestia, media spiritualia et receptacula terrestria se invicem continuant.","refs":["CH_MHON_T_11:SD1","CH_MHON_T_11:ME5"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[]},{"id":"CL_MHON_05:HSI-02","text":"Subiectum intra hanc machinam fit Mercurius-Anima, ens quod inter corpus, planetas, elementa et Magisterium transit.","refs":["CH_MHON_T_10:SD3","CH_MHON_T_10:D5"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[]},{"id":"CL_MHON_05:HSI-03","text":"Tempus non est successio externa, sed series revolutionum et restitutionum quae eandem naturam in novos gradus transformat.","refs":["CH_MHON_TH_9:ALT2","CH_MHON_T_10:D3"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[]},{"id":"CL_MHON_05:HSI-04","text":"Certamen principale non est inter caelum et terram, sed circa modum quo influxus caelestis in materiam recipiatur et operetur.","refs":["CH_MHON_T_11:ME2","CH_MHON_T_11:FGS3"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[]},{"id":"CL_MHON_05:HSI-05","text":"Fuga systematis consistit in substitutione medii: cum unus terminus instabilis fit, Spiritus, anima, Sol aut Terra functionem eius recipit.","refs":["CH_MHON_TH_9:SNX3","CH_MHON_TH_9:NX5"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[]}],"original_name":"Hidden System Image"},{"number":"17","name":"Minimal Whole-Book Reconstruction Test","items":[{"id":"CL_MHON_05:MRT-B01","text":"Axiomata sufficiunt ad restituendam machinam minimam: compositio signorum, successiones planetariae, Mercurius, media spiritualia, triadica causalitas et receptio terrestris.","refs":["CH_MHON_TH_9:MRT1","CH_MHON_T_11:MRT1"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[]},{"id":"CL_MHON_05:MRT-B02","text":"Systema hac magnitudine cohaeret quia omnes mutationes ad eandem logicam mediationis et circulationis referuntur.","refs":["CH_MHON_TH_9:MRT2","CH_MHON_T_11:MRT2"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[]},{"id":"CL_MHON_05:MRT-B03","text":"Firmitas maxima est in connexione inter compositionem, transformationem, Mercurium et circulationem caelestem-terrestrem.","refs":["CH_MHON_TH_9:MRT3","CH_MHON_T_11:MRT3"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[]},{"id":"CL_MHON_05:MRT-B04","text":"Determinatio minima nondum sufficit circa leges quattuor revolutionum, formas Mercurii, efficaciam radiorum et ontologiam Terrae Lemniae.","refs":["CH_MHON_TH_9:MRT4","CH_MHON_T_11:MRT4"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[]},{"id":"CL_MHON_05:MRT-B05","text":"Restitutio verum motum servat: a signo composito ad processum, a processu ad mediationem, a mediatione ad influxum et effectum terrestre; resistit autem clausura unica, quia systema per substitutiones et tensionem procedit.","refs":["CH_MHON_TH_9:MRT7","CH_MHON_T_11:MRT6"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[]}],"original_name":"Minimal Whole-Book Reconstruction Test"}],"language_code":"la","language_name":"Latina","translations":[{"id":"CAREVAR_20261006T223308396060Z_26b8caf1","language_code":"en","language_name":"English","title":"Chapters 13–15","source_sha256":"f3749d9b19a5521dcf11743d9d2d2b1bfd4d23b4c76e264aa7a9a4cf0e2ece2b","sha256":"3d3b715d4deccfeaa9c18a794aaa2d26147be393bc2462d7cd4314d2dd256f9c","rendition_type":"machine","review_status":"machine","model":"gpt-5.6-terra","created":"2026-10-06T22:33:08.397513+00:00","sections":[{"number":"1","name":"Book-Scale Argumental Spine","items":[{"id":"CL_MHON_05:BS-01","text":"The system asks how planetary figures are produced from the composition of signs, the succession of elements, and celestial-terrestrial influence.","refs":["CH_MHON_TH_9:AS1","CH_MHON_T_10:AS1","CH_MHON_T_11:AS1"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[]},{"id":"CL_MHON_05:BS-02","text":"The question of signs is converted into a question of production, transformation, and transmission.","refs":["CH_MHON_TH_9:AS2","CH_MHON_T_10:AS2","CH_MHON_T_11:AS2"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[]},{"id":"CL_MHON_05:BS-03","text":"The common foundation is the translation of visible forms into operative orders governed by elemental and cosmological rules.","refs":["CH_MHON_TH_9:AS3","CH_MHON_T_10:AS3"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[]},{"id":"CL_MHON_05:BS-04","text":"The planetary system composes figures not as separate entities, but as stages of a single transformative nature.","refs":["CH_MHON_TH_9:SD1","CH_MHON_T_10:SD1"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[]},{"id":"CL_MHON_05:BS-05","text":"The movement proceeds from the Moon and Sun through Mercury, the soul, rays, and Lemnian Earth to material and nutritive effect.","refs":["CH_MHON_TH_9:SD2","CH_MHON_T_11:D1"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[]},{"id":"CL_MHON_05:BS-06","text":"The mode of argumentation is reconstructive, analogical, and alchemical, because it restores symbolic compositions to cosmological process.","refs":["CH_MHON_T_10:AS6","CH_MHON_T_11:SD1"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[]},{"id":"CL_MHON_05:BS-07","text":"The movement is not linear: it occurs spirally through revolution, substitution, reclassification, and return to a new medium.","refs":["CH_MHON_TH_9:A5","CH_MHON_T_10:ME4"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[]},{"id":"CL_MHON_05:BS-08","text":"Closure occurs distributively: the hieroglyphic figure is impressed into Earth, while the Magisterium circulates between celestial sources and the terrestrial receptacle.","refs":["CH_MHON_TH_9:A11","CH_MHON_T_11:SD1"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[]}],"original_name":"Book-Scale Argumental Spine"},{"number":"2","name":"Global Load-Bearing Axioms","items":[{"id":"CL_MHON_05:GA-01","text":"[Persistent] A planetary sign is constituted by the composition of several characters, not by a simple and isolated designation.","refs":["CH_MHON_TH_9:A2"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[]},{"id":"CL_MHON_05:GA-02","text":"[Persistent] Moon, Sun, Elements, and Aries are functional terms whose conjunction determines the figure.","refs":["CH_MHON_TH_9:A2"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[]},{"id":"CL_MHON_05:GA-03","text":"[Mutating] Lunar Nature receives various functions through stages and revolutions under planetary names.","refs":["CH_MHON_TH_9:A5"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[]},{"id":"CL_MHON_05:GA-04","text":"[Emergent] Cosmology, alchemy, and anthropology coalesce in Mercury as a common node.","refs":["CH_MHON_T_10:SD2"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[]},{"id":"CL_MHON_05:GA-05","text":"[Mutating] Mercury passes from the fourth revolution of the Lunar order to the Microcosm and Adam, such that the planet becomes the form of the cosmic human.","refs":["CH_MHON_TH_9:A6","CH_MHON_T_10:A7"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[]},{"id":"CL_MHON_05:GA-06","text":"[Attractor] The perfection of lunar and Solar magic tends toward the appearance of conspicuous Mercury.","refs":["CH_MHON_T_10:A6"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[]},{"id":"CL_MHON_05:GA-07","text":"[Mutating] The soul is converted from a spiritual principle into an operative bond of planetary bodies.","refs":["CH_MHON_T_10:A9"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[]},{"id":"CL_MHON_05:GA-08","text":"[Persistent] Sun and Moon are generative principles, whereas Lemnian Earth provides the place of nourishment and continuation.","refs":["CH_MHON_T_11:A1","CH_MHON_T_11:A4"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[]},{"id":"CL_MHON_05:GA-09","text":"[Emergent] Rays bring about the common medium through which celestial principles pass into operation around the earth.","refs":["CH_MHON_T_11:A5","CH_MHON_T_11:D3"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[]},{"id":"CL_MHON_05:GA-10","text":"[Terminal] The hieroglyphic figure is completed only when Spirit introduces and impresses it into Earth.","refs":["CH_MHON_TH_9:A11"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[]},{"id":"CL_MHON_05:GA-11","text":"[Attractor] The anatomy of the Monas Hieroglyphica contracts the planetary order and the mysteries of the Theorems into a single internal diagram.","refs":["CH_MHON_T_10:A11","CH_MHON_T_10:ME5"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[]},{"id":"CL_MHON_05:GA-12","text":"[Persistent] Tradition provides authority, but the operative mechanism actualizes it through composition, influence, and transformation.","refs":["CH_MHON_TH_9:A1","CH_MHON_T_11:A6"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[]}],"original_name":"Global Load-Bearing Axioms"},{"number":"3","name":"Recurrence, Mutation, and Displacement Matrix","items":[{"id":"CL_MHON_05:RMD-01","text":"The composition of signs recurs, but its function changes: it passes from the description of a figure to the genealogy of transformation.","refs":["CH_MHON_TH_9:A12","CH_MHON_T_10:SD1"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[]},{"id":"CL_MHON_05:RMD-02","text":"The Moon is first an interpretive principle, then a substitutable medium which the Spiritus Magicus assumes without rupture of the work.","refs":["CH_MHON_TH_9:A8","CH_MHON_TH_9:NX5"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[]},{"id":"CL_MHON_05:RMD-03","text":"The Sun is displaced from a hieroglyphic component into a parental principle, a natural medium, and radial influence.","refs":["CH_MHON_TH_9:A9","CH_MHON_T_11:SD3"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[]},{"id":"CL_MHON_05:RMD-04","text":"The Elements are transformed from classificatory signs into the Magisterium that directs composition and regeneration.","refs":["CH_MHON_TH_9:A4","CH_MHON_T_10:A3"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[]},{"id":"CL_MHON_05:RMD-05","text":"Mercury recurs under diverse forms, but its function always concerns passage between orders.","refs":["CH_MHON_TH_9:A6","CH_MHON_T_10:A7"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[]},{"id":"CL_MHON_05:RMD-06","text":"Earth is elevated from a receptive surface of figures to the active nourishment of the Magisterium.","refs":["CH_MHON_TH_9:A10","CH_MHON_T_11:A4"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[]},{"id":"CL_MHON_05:RMD-07","text":"The Spiritus Magicus and the separate soul perform the same mediating function in different orders, the former for the Moon, the latter for planetary bodies.","refs":["CH_MHON_TH_9:SD4","CH_MHON_T_10:A9"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[]},{"id":"CL_MHON_05:RMD-08","text":"Genealogy recurs in a double form: as the succession of Saturn, Jupiter, and Mercury, and as the arrangement of father, mother, and earth.","refs":["CH_MHON_TH_9:D2","CH_MHON_T_11:D4"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[]},{"id":"CL_MHON_05:RMD-09","text":"The sevenfold is converted from the enumeration of bodies into an order of stages, substitutions, and transformations.","refs":["CH_MHON_T_10:SD4"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[]},{"id":"CL_MHON_05:RMD-10","text":"The return of forms does not preserve identity, but produces a new state: the sign becomes process, the process becomes body, the body becomes receptacle.","refs":["CH_MHON_TH_9:SD2","CH_MHON_T_11:SD2"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[]}],"original_name":"Recurrence, Mutation, and Displacement Matrix"},{"number":"4","name":"Lower-Layer vs Current-Level Distinction","items":[{"id":"CL_MHON_05:CGD-01","text":"The individual lower units retain local questions concerning the number of revolutions, the forms of Mercury, and exact anatomical correspondence.","refs":["CH_MHON_TH_9:MRT4","CH_MHON_T_10:MRT3"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[]},{"id":"CL_MHON_05:CGD-02","text":"At the present level, these questions are amplified into a common problem of identity defined through transformation, not through a fixed state.","refs":["CH_MHON_TH_9:SD3","CH_MHON_T_10:SNX4"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[]},{"id":"CL_MHON_05:CGD-03","text":"The composition of Mars, Venus, and Mercury is joined with the triad of Sun, Moon, and Earth in a single genealogical architecture.","refs":["CH_MHON_T_10:SD1","CH_MHON_T_11:D1"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[]},{"id":"CL_MHON_05:CGD-04","text":"The substitutions of Moon, Sun, and Spirit, which are locally conditional, here produce a general rule of the interchangeable medium.","refs":["CH_MHON_TH_9:D4","CH_MHON_T_10:NX4"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[]},{"id":"CL_MHON_05:CGD-05","text":"Impression into Earth and the nourishment of Lemnian Earth are reduced to the same logic of active reception.","refs":["CH_MHON_TH_9:D6","CH_MHON_T_11:SD2"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[]},{"id":"CL_MHON_05:CGD-06","text":"Hermetic authority, locally a testimony, at this scale becomes part of the mechanism by which symbolic genealogy acquires efficacy.","refs":["CH_MHON_T_11:SD4"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[]},{"id":"CL_MHON_05:CGD-07","text":"The image of independent planets becomes obsolete, because all forms are absorbed into a common order of circulation and regeneration.","refs":["CH_MHON_T_10:DNX1"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[]},{"id":"CL_MHON_05:CGD-08","text":"The new object of this level is a machine that distributes figure, genealogy, soul, influence, and matter through the same process.","refs":["CH_MHON_T_10:ME6","CH_MHON_T_11:SD1"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[]}],"original_name":"Lower-Layer vs Current-Level Distinction"},{"number":"5","name":"Global Apophatic Structure","items":[{"id":"CL_MHON_05:GNX-01","text":"The system denies that the sign is a mere designation that can be separated from composition and production.","refs":["CH_MHON_TH_9:DNX1"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[]},{"id":"CL_MHON_05:GNX-02","text":"The system denies that planets are disjoined names without the succession of one Lunar Nature.","refs":["CH_MHON_TH_9:DNX2"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[]},{"id":"CL_MHON_05:GNX-03","text":"The system denies univocal causality, whether celestial or terrestrial, because the Magisterium depends simultaneously on influence and nourishment.","refs":["CH_MHON_T_11:DNX2"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[]},{"id":"CL_MHON_05:GNX-04","text":"The system denies that hieroglyphic communication depends on material voice; silence can here become an operative medium.","refs":["CH_MHON_TH_9:NX4","CH_MHON_TH_9:ME5"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[]},{"id":"CL_MHON_05:GNX-05","text":"The system denies that Earth is a passive surface, because it receives, nourishes, and produces figures.","refs":["CH_MHON_TH_9:ME4","CH_MHON_T_11:NX2"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[]},{"id":"CL_MHON_05:GNX-06","text":"The system denies that tradition is sufficient authority without a mechanism of influence and transformation.","refs":["CH_MHON_T_11:DNX5"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[]},{"id":"CL_MHON_05:GNX-07","text":"The system denies a rigid separation between astronomy, alchemy, anthropology, and material operation.","refs":["CH_MHON_T_10:DNX2","CH_MHON_T_10:SD2"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[]}],"original_name":"Global Apophatic Structure"},{"number":"6","name":"Deep Book Apophatics","items":[{"id":"CL_MHON_05:DBN-01","text":"For the system to hold together, the figure cannot be an ornament separated from the process of formation.","refs":["CH_MHON_TH_9:SNX1"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[]},{"id":"CL_MHON_05:DBN-02","text":"A world in which soul, elements, and planets remain thoroughly heterogeneous orders must be false.","refs":["CH_MHON_T_10:SNX3"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[]},{"id":"CL_MHON_05:DBN-03","text":"It is false that the Magisterium arises from one simple source, because the celestial principle fails without terrestrial nourishment.","refs":["CH_MHON_T_11:DNX2","CH_MHON_T_11:DNX4"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[]},{"id":"CL_MHON_05:DBN-04","text":"It is false that regeneration is a natural event without art, magic, and the mediation of the soul.","refs":["CH_MHON_T_10:DNX3"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[]},{"id":"CL_MHON_05:DBN-05","text":"The system skirts the exact rule by which individual forms are produced from elemental successions, but without this rule its genealogy would dissolve.","refs":["CH_MHON_TH_9:SNX4"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[]}],"original_name":"Deep Book Apophatics"},{"number":"7","name":"Super-Apophatics of the Whole","items":[{"id":"CL_MHON_05:BSA-01","text":"The system circumscribes the possibility of autonomous human artifice, because the origin and power of the Magisterium are placed outside human action.","refs":["CH_MHON_T_11:SNX1"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[]},{"id":"CL_MHON_05:BSA-02","text":"A heaven without efficacy becomes unavailable, because rays are not mere images but media of influence.","refs":["CH_MHON_T_11:SNX2"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[]},{"id":"CL_MHON_05:BSA-03","text":"An absolute separation between symbolic genealogy and natural operation is inadmissible.","refs":["CH_MHON_T_11:SNX3"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[]},{"id":"CL_MHON_05:BSA-04","text":"A single origin that attributes the Magisterium only to Sun, Moon, or Earth is inadmissible.","refs":["CH_MHON_T_11:SNX4"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[]},{"id":"CL_MHON_05:BSA-05","text":"The system tacitly excludes a world in which figure, soul, influence, and matter are closed and non-transmissible orders.","refs":["CH_MHON_TH_9:SNX5"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[]}],"original_name":"Super-Apophatics of the Whole"},{"number":"8","name":"Cross-Unit Derivative Propositions","items":[{"id":"CL_MHON_05:XD-01","text":"Hieroglyphic composition and triadic causality bring it about that the sign designates origin, medium, and receptacle simultaneously.","refs":["CH_MHON_TH_9:A2","CH_MHON_T_11:D1"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"Composite","passages":[]},{"id":"CL_MHON_05:XD-02","text":"Planetary succession and Solar circulation converge in Mercury as an attractor of transformation, not as a final name.","refs":["CH_MHON_TH_9:SD1","CH_MHON_T_10:A6"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"Composite","passages":[]},{"id":"CL_MHON_05:XD-03","text":"Mercury as Microcosm and the Magisterium as celestial-terrestrial conjunction produce the same passage from cosmic order to human order.","refs":["CH_MHON_T_10:A7","CH_MHON_T_10:SD2"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"Composite","passages":[]},{"id":"CL_MHON_05:XD-04","text":"The substitution of the Moon by Spirit and the alternation of the place of Sun and Mercury show that planetary identity is a function of the medium, not a fixed substance.","refs":["CH_MHON_TH_9:NX5","CH_MHON_T_10:NX4"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"Tension","passages":[]},{"id":"CL_MHON_05:XD-05","text":"The separate soul and rays constitute two forms of mediation that cause the planetary body and the Magisterium to participate in the same circulation.","refs":["CH_MHON_T_10:A9","CH_MHON_T_11:D3"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"Composite","passages":[]},{"id":"CL_MHON_05:XD-06","text":"Earth becomes the common receptacle of the impressed figure and nutritive influence, such that materiality is no longer a passive term.","refs":["CH_MHON_TH_9:A10","CH_MHON_T_11:A4"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"Composite","passages":[]},{"id":"CL_MHON_05:XD-07","text":"Hermetic testimony and the radial mechanism gather authority and efficacy into one legitimate machine.","refs":["CH_MHON_T_11:SD4"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"Composite","passages":[]},{"id":"CL_MHON_05:XD-08","text":"The assertion of exact correspondence both strengthens and renders the system fragile, because it promises an order that the mechanism does not fully explain.","refs":["CH_MHON_T_10:MRT6","CH_MHON_T_11:MRT5"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"Tension","passages":[]}],"original_name":"Cross-Unit Derivative Propositions"},{"number":"9","name":"Book-Scale Super-Derivatives","items":[{"id":"CL_MHON_05:BSD-01","text":"The hieroglyphic becomes a distributed machine that converts cosmological order into magical operation and material inscription.","refs":["CH_MHON_TH_9:SD2","CH_MHON_T_10:ME6"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[]},{"id":"CL_MHON_05:BSD-02","text":"Cosmology, alchemy, and anthropology are not simply joined, but become aspects of one machine through the same genealogical process.","refs":["CH_MHON_T_10:SD2","CH_MHON_T_10:SD1"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[]},{"id":"CL_MHON_05:BSD-03","text":"The causality of the system is distributed across celestial generation, terrestrial reception, nourishment, and inscription.","refs":["CH_MHON_T_11:SD2","CH_MHON_TH_9:D6"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[]},{"id":"CL_MHON_05:BSD-04","text":"The human subject is not presupposed, but is produced through Mercury, Adam, and the separate soul as an operative node.","refs":["CH_MHON_T_10:D5","CH_MHON_T_10:ME3"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[]},{"id":"CL_MHON_05:BSD-05","text":"Time becomes a series of revolutions and stages of transformation, not an external line upon which processes are superimposed.","refs":["CH_MHON_TH_9:ALT2","CH_MHON_T_10:A5"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[]},{"id":"CL_MHON_05:BSD-06","text":"Reading the figure becomes an investigation of mechanism, because the sign, anatomy, and planetary character compress the hidden process.","refs":["CH_MHON_TH_9:ME1","CH_MHON_T_10:ME5"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[]},{"id":"CL_MHON_05:BSD-07","text":"Simple origin vanishes, and in its place emerges a network of dependencies among principles, media, receptacles, and stages.","refs":["CH_MHON_T_11:SNX4","CH_MHON_T_10:SD4"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[]},{"id":"CL_MHON_05:BSD-08","text":"The total effect is a celestial-terrestrial circulation in which influence, form, and matter mutually produce and sustain one another.","refs":["CH_MHON_T_11:ME5","CH_MHON_T_11:SD1"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[]}],"original_name":"Book-Scale Super-Derivatives"},{"number":"10","name":"Recursive Loops, Escalation Paths, and Conceptual Attractors","items":[{"id":"CL_MHON_05:RLE-01","text":"Composition produces succession, succession transformation, transformation material figure; the material figure in turn attests the hidden order.","refs":["CH_MHON_TH_9:D6","CH_MHON_T_10:ME6"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"Composite","passages":[]},{"id":"CL_MHON_05:RLE-02","text":"The Moon passes into Spirit, Spirit administers operation, and operation reconstitutes the unity that was assigned to the principle of the Moon.","refs":["CH_MHON_TH_9:D4","CH_MHON_TH_9:SD4"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"Composite","passages":[]},{"id":"CL_MHON_05:RLE-03","text":"Sun and Mercury oscillate between principle, place, and substitute; this instability expands rather than dissolves the sevenfold.","refs":["CH_MHON_T_10:NX4","CH_MHON_T_10:ME4"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"Tension","passages":[]},{"id":"CL_MHON_05:RLE-04","text":"The names of Saturn, Jupiter, and Mercury are points of amplification by which one Lunar Nature gradually receives new functions.","refs":["CH_MHON_TH_9:A5","CH_MHON_T_10:D3"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"Composite","passages":[]},{"id":"CL_MHON_05:RLE-05","text":"Celestial genealogy passes into the terrestrial receptacle, and the receptacle is referred back through rays to the initial genealogy.","refs":["CH_MHON_T_11:ME4"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"Composite","passages":[]},{"id":"CL_MHON_05:RLE-06","text":"Mercury and the Monad are attractors in which the planetary, anthropological, and anatomical orders converge into one figure.","refs":["CH_MHON_T_10:A7","CH_MHON_T_10:ME5"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"Composite","passages":[]},{"id":"CL_MHON_05:RLE-07","text":"Uncertain points concerning revolutions, the forms of Mercury, and anatomical correspondence are not external margins, but lend force to the attractor points themselves.","refs":["CH_MHON_TH_9:MRT4","CH_MHON_T_10:MRT3"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"Tension","passages":[]}],"original_name":"Recursive Loops, Escalation Paths, and Conceptual Attractors"},{"number":"11","name":"Formal Generative Schema of the Whole Book","items":[{"id":"CL_MHON_05:FGS-B01","text":"Composition of Sun, Moon, Elements, or Aries → hieroglyphic character → sign with astral origin and elemental determination.","refs":["CH_MHON_TH_9:A2","CH_MHON_T_10:D1"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[]},{"id":"CL_MHON_05:FGS-B02","text":"Hieroglyphic character → Lunar or Solar Nature → revolution → planetary stage → genealogy of transformation.","refs":["CH_MHON_TH_9:A4","CH_MHON_T_10:SD1"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[]},{"id":"CL_MHON_05:FGS-B03","text":"Perfection of lunar and Solar magic → conspicuous Mercury → Microcosm and Adam → cosmological subject.","refs":["CH_MHON_T_10:A6","CH_MHON_T_10:A7"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[]},{"id":"CL_MHON_05:FGS-B04","text":"Mercury or Moon → Spiritus Magicus or separate soul → bond of bodies and operative mediation.","refs":["CH_MHON_TH_9:A8","CH_MHON_T_10:A9"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[]},{"id":"CL_MHON_05:FGS-B05","text":"Sun + Moon → generative principles; Lemnian Earth → reception and nourishment; rays → common influence.","refs":["CH_MHON_T_11:A1","CH_MHON_T_11:A4","CH_MHON_T_11:A5"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[]},{"id":"CL_MHON_05:FGS-B06","text":"Influence and mediation → figure introduced into Earth → material impression → return circuit between form and process.","refs":["CH_MHON_TH_9:A11","CH_MHON_TH_9:D6"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[]}],"original_name":"Formal Generative Schema of the Whole Book"},{"number":"12","name":"Whole-Book Argument Flow Map","items":[{"id":"CL_MHON_05:AFM-B01","text":"Composition of signs → reclassification of planets → Lunar and Solar Nature → Mercury as attractor → soul and Spirit as media → Lemnian Earth as receptacle → radial influence → impression and nourishment.","refs":["CH_MHON_T_10:SD1","CH_MHON_T_10:A7","CH_MHON_T_11:D1"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[]},{"id":"CL_MHON_05:AFM-B02","text":"Hieroglyphic form → processual interpretation → substitution of the medium → translation into body, soul, and Earth → confirmation through the anatomy of the Monad.","refs":["CH_MHON_TH_9:SD2","CH_MHON_T_10:ME5"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[]},{"id":"CL_MHON_05:AFM-B03","text":"Celestial dependence → terrestrial reception → radial influence → material operation → reconstitution of unity between heaven and earth.","refs":["CH_MHON_T_11:AF1","CH_MHON_T_11:ME5"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[]}],"original_name":"Whole-Book Argument Flow Map"},{"number":"13","name":"Macro Map-Effects","items":[{"id":"CL_MHON_05:MME-01","text":"The hieroglyphic object is reclassified as a nexus between astral origin, elemental composition, and the stage of operation.","refs":["CH_MHON_TH_9:ME1"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[]},{"id":"CL_MHON_05:MME-02","text":"The opposition of Moon and Sun is dissolved into differences of principle, medium, substitution, and complement.","refs":["CH_MHON_TH_9:ME2"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[]},{"id":"CL_MHON_05:MME-03","text":"The names of Saturn, Jupiter, and Mercury become stations of transformation of one Lunar Nature, not isolated planetary entities.","refs":["CH_MHON_TH_9:ME3"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[]},{"id":"CL_MHON_05:MME-04","text":"Earth becomes a productive node where figures and influences are simultaneously received, transformed, and continued.","refs":["CH_MHON_TH_9:ME4","CH_MHON_T_11:ME2"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[]},{"id":"CL_MHON_05:MME-05","text":"The silence of words is reclassified as a condition of hieroglyphic communication and not as its defect.","refs":["CH_MHON_TH_9:ME5"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[]},{"id":"CL_MHON_05:MME-06","text":"The separate soul is reclassified from an interior principle into an instrument binding planetary bodies and the alchemical order.","refs":["CH_MHON_T_10:ME3"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[]},{"id":"CL_MHON_05:MME-07","text":"The anatomy of the Monad becomes a compressed diagram in which cosmology, alchemy, anthropology, and the art of reading can act together.","refs":["CH_MHON_T_10:SD5","CH_MHON_T_10:ME5"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[]}],"original_name":"Macro Map-Effects"},{"number":"14","name":"Altitude Integration","items":[{"id":"CL_MHON_05:ALT-B01","text":"The surface level displays planetary characters, elemental signs, soul, rays, and Earth as explicit terms.","refs":["CH_MHON_T_10:ALT1","CH_MHON_T_11:ALT1"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[]},{"id":"CL_MHON_05:ALT-B02","text":"The structural level distributes successions, revolutions, substitutions, genealogies, and rays as mechanisms of mediation.","refs":["CH_MHON_T_10:ALT2","CH_MHON_T_11:ALT2"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[]},{"id":"CL_MHON_05:ALT-B03","text":"The metastrategic level converts the reading of names and figures into the restoration of productive order.","refs":["CH_MHON_TH_9:ALT3","CH_MHON_T_10:ALT3"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[]},{"id":"CL_MHON_05:ALT-B04","text":"The ontological level constitutes reality as a series of transformations between heaven, soul, body, elements, and matter.","refs":["CH_MHON_T_10:ALT4","CH_MHON_T_11:ALT4"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[]},{"id":"CL_MHON_05:ALT-B05","text":"The methodological level makes the figure itself proof: what is read as a sign simultaneously produces the order that it signifies.","refs":["CH_MHON_TH_9:SNX5","CH_MHON_T_10:ME6"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[]}],"original_name":"Altitude Integration"},{"number":"15","name":"Internal Tensions, Mutation Points, and Fault Lines","items":[{"id":"CL_MHON_05:FL-01","text":"The system affirms an exact correspondence between the Monad and the mysteries, but the proof does not fully exhibit the mechanism of this correspondence.","refs":["CH_MHON_T_10:A11","CH_MHON_T_10:MRT6"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"Tension","passages":[]},{"id":"CL_MHON_05:FL-02","text":"Mercury oscillates among planet, Microcosm, Adam, and substitutable place; the oscillation increases order, but leaves its identity unstable.","refs":["CH_MHON_T_10:A7","CH_MHON_T_10:MRT3"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"Tension","passages":[]},{"id":"CL_MHON_05:FL-03","text":"Hermetic authority confirms the relation, but the passage from authority to radial efficacy remains a fragile seam.","refs":["CH_MHON_T_11:MRT5"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"Tension","passages":[]},{"id":"CL_MHON_05:FL-04","text":"Causality is divided between celestial principles and nutritive Earth, but radial influence compels both orders into one medium.","refs":["CH_MHON_T_11:D2","CH_MHON_T_11:D3"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"Tension","passages":[]},{"id":"CL_MHON_05:MP-01","text":"The fourth revolution is the mutation point at which Lunar Nature passes into Mercury without rupture of the prior order.","refs":["CH_MHON_TH_9:A6","CH_MHON_TH_9:D3"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"Composite","passages":[]},{"id":"CL_MHON_05:MP-02","text":"The substitution of the Moon by the Spiritus Magicus is the mutation point at which the medium changes, while the end of the work persists.","refs":["CH_MHON_TH_9:D4"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"Composite","passages":[]},{"id":"CL_MHON_05:MP-03","text":"The passage from figure to impression is the mutation point at which the hieroglyphic ceases to be merely interpreted and becomes a material agent.","refs":["CH_MHON_TH_9:D6","CH_MHON_T_10:ME6"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"Composite","passages":[]}],"original_name":"Internal Tensions, Mutation Points, and Fault Lines"},{"number":"16","name":"Hidden System Image","items":[{"id":"CL_MHON_05:HSI-01","text":"The system implicitly fashions reality as a machine of circulation in which celestial principles, spiritual media, and terrestrial receptacles mutually continue one another.","refs":["CH_MHON_T_11:SD1","CH_MHON_T_11:ME5"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[]},{"id":"CL_MHON_05:HSI-02","text":"Within this machine, the subject becomes Mercury-Soul, a being that passes between body, planets, elements, and the Magisterium.","refs":["CH_MHON_T_10:SD3","CH_MHON_T_10:D5"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[]},{"id":"CL_MHON_05:HSI-03","text":"Time is not an external succession, but a series of revolutions and restitutions that transforms the same nature into new stages.","refs":["CH_MHON_TH_9:ALT2","CH_MHON_T_10:D3"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[]},{"id":"CL_MHON_05:HSI-04","text":"The principal contest is not between heaven and earth, but concerns the manner in which celestial influence is received and operates in matter.","refs":["CH_MHON_T_11:ME2","CH_MHON_T_11:FGS3"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[]},{"id":"CL_MHON_05:HSI-05","text":"The system's escape consists in the substitution of the medium: when one term becomes unstable, Spirit, soul, Sun, or Earth receives its function.","refs":["CH_MHON_TH_9:SNX3","CH_MHON_TH_9:NX5"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[]}],"original_name":"Hidden System Image"},{"number":"17","name":"Minimal Whole-Book Reconstruction Test","items":[{"id":"CL_MHON_05:MRT-B01","text":"The axioms suffice to restore the minimal machine: composition of signs, planetary successions, Mercury, spiritual media, triadic causality, and terrestrial reception.","refs":["CH_MHON_TH_9:MRT1","CH_MHON_T_11:MRT1"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[]},{"id":"CL_MHON_05:MRT-B02","text":"At this scale, the system coheres because all mutations are referred to the same logic of mediation and circulation.","refs":["CH_MHON_TH_9:MRT2","CH_MHON_T_11:MRT2"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[]},{"id":"CL_MHON_05:MRT-B03","text":"The greatest firmness lies in the connection between composition, transformation, Mercury, and celestial-terrestrial circulation.","refs":["CH_MHON_TH_9:MRT3","CH_MHON_T_11:MRT3"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[]},{"id":"CL_MHON_05:MRT-B04","text":"Minimal determination is not yet sufficient concerning the laws of the four revolutions, the forms of Mercury, the efficacy of rays, and the ontology of Lemnian Earth.","refs":["CH_MHON_TH_9:MRT4","CH_MHON_T_11:MRT4"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[]},{"id":"CL_MHON_05:MRT-B05","text":"The reconstruction preserves the true movement: from composite sign to process, from process to mediation, from mediation to influence and terrestrial effect; yet it resists a single closure, because the system proceeds through substitutions and tension.","refs":["CH_MHON_TH_9:MRT7","CH_MHON_T_11:MRT6"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[]}],"original_name":"Minimal Whole-Book Reconstruction Test"}],"revision":"CL_MHON_05@R001","source_variant_id":"canonical:CL_MHON_05:v1","source_language_code":"la","original_title":"Chapters 13–15"}],"identity":{"layer":"CL","kicker":"Cluster · Monas Hieroglyphica","title":"Chapters 13–15","context":"T H E O R. Xii. · Theorem Xiii. · Theorem Xiii.","original_title":"Chapters 13–15","original_context":"T H E O R. Xii. · Theor. Xiii. · Theorema Xiii."},"original_title":"Chapters 13–15","title_language_code":"en"},{"id":"CL_MHON_06","layer":"CL","title":"Chapters 16–18","order":6,"parents":["CH_MHON_T_12","CH_MHON_T_13","CH_MHON_T_14"],"revision":"CL_MHON_06@R001","sha256":"524f581104e29e5fe30de1e2d04d277b33cac234e5985126f30b259a12e8333b","sections":[{"number":"1","name":"Book-Scale Argumental Spine","items":[{"id":"CL_MHON_06:ABS-01","text":"Systema quaerit quomodo figurae caelestes et geometricae, per situm, partitionem, conversionem et compositionem, in ordines numerales, alphabeticos, elementales atque verbales transire possint.","refs":["CH_MHON_T_12:AS5","CH_MHON_T_13:AS3","CH_MHON_T_14:AS3"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[]},{"id":"CL_MHON_06:ABS-02","text":"Thesis globalis statuit signum hieroglyphicum non rem fixam esse, sed operatorium quod relationes cosmicas et geometricae structuras in numeros, litteras et potentias interpretativas generat.","refs":["CH_MHON_T_12:A8","CH_MHON_T_13:SD1","CH_MHON_T_14:SD1"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[]},{"id":"CL_MHON_06:ABS-03","text":"Argumentatio fit reconstructiva: non a definitione univoca signi incipit, sed ex transformationibus eiusdem figurae necessitatem plurium ordinum significationis colligit.","refs":["CH_MHON_T_12:AS6","CH_MHON_T_13:SD2","CH_MHON_T_14:SD2"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[]},{"id":"CL_MHON_06:ABS-04","text":"Motus a relatione locali ad potentiam compositam progreditur: vicinitas astralis dignitatem producit, divisio geometrica numerum producit, et compositio numerico-litteralis Verbum terminale producit.","refs":["CH_MHON_T_12:A2","CH_MHON_T_13:SD5","CH_MHON_T_14:SD5"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[]},{"id":"CL_MHON_06:ABS-05","text":"Motus systematis est spiralis potius quam linearis, quia eadem figura per conversiones repetitas redit, sed singulis reditibus alium ordinem operativum suscipit.","refs":["CH_MHON_T_12:SD3","CH_MHON_T_13:A7","CH_MHON_T_14:DNX5"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[]},{"id":"CL_MHON_06:ABS-06","text":"Clausura non consistit in immobilitate unius sensus, sed in coordinatione plurium potestatum sub unitate hieroglyphica, quae Lux et Verbum ut effectus conversionis exhibet.","refs":["CH_MHON_T_14:SD4","CH_MHON_T_14:FGS6","CH_MHON_T_14:ALT4"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[]},{"id":"CL_MHON_06:ABS-07","text":"Systema transit ab astrologia relationum ad arithmologiam operationum, deinde ad theologiam verbalem, sine separatione rigida inter naturalem ordinem, figuram et significationem.","refs":["CH_MHON_T_12:SD1","CH_MHON_T_13:SD6","CH_MHON_T_14:SNX2"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[]},{"id":"CL_MHON_06:ABS-08","text":"Argumentum suam vim ex eo accipit quod figura non solum significat, sed ipsa mechanismus fit quo significatio successive producitur, multiplicatur et ordinatur.","refs":["CH_MHON_T_12:SD3","CH_MHON_T_13:SD4","CH_MHON_T_14:ME3"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[]}],"original_name":"Book-Scale Argumental Spine"},{"number":"2","name":"Global Load-Bearing Axioms","items":[{"id":"CL_MHON_06:GA-01","text":"[Persistent] Significatio non in substantia isolata signi, sed in relationibus eius ad situm, divisionem, vicinitatem et conversionem consistit.","refs":["CH_MHON_T_12:NX2","CH_MHON_T_13:NX4","CH_MHON_T_14:NX2"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[]},{"id":"CL_MHON_06:GA-02","text":"[Persistent] Figura hieroglyphica simul est demonstratio et operatorium, quia relationes invisibiles in formas iterabiles et tractabiles transfert.","refs":["CH_MHON_T_12:NX4","CH_MHON_T_13:SD1","CH_MHON_T_14:SD1"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[]},{"id":"CL_MHON_06:GA-03","text":"[Mutating] Unitas figurae non impedit pluralitatem significationum, sed eam per partitionem, geminationem et superpositionem generat.","refs":["CH_MHON_T_13:NX5","CH_MHON_T_14:NX1","CH_MHON_T_14:SD2"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[]},{"id":"CL_MHON_06:GA-04","text":"[Persistent] Geometria, alphabetum et arithmetica non sunt disciplinae separatae, sed ordines conversionis unius machinae hieroglyphicae.","refs":["CH_MHON_T_12:DNX2","CH_MHON_T_13:SD6","CH_MHON_T_14:ME2"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[]},{"id":"CL_MHON_06:GA-05","text":"[Emergent] Dignitas, numerus et Verbum sunt effectus relationum ordinatarum, non qualitates immediate datae rerum aut signorum.","refs":["CH_MHON_T_12:SD2","CH_MHON_T_13:SD5","CH_MHON_T_14:ME6"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[]},{"id":"CL_MHON_06:GA-06","text":"[Mutating] Situs non est conditio exterior figurae, sed variabilis generativa quae eandem formam in aliam virtutem numericam vel semanticam convertit.","refs":["CH_MHON_T_12:SNX5","CH_MHON_T_13:A7","CH_MHON_T_14:A3"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[]},{"id":"CL_MHON_06:GA-07","text":"[Attractor] Quinarietas et denarietas agunt ut nodi compositionales per quos figura transit ab elementis localibus ad potentias superiores.","refs":["CH_MHON_T_13:A8","CH_MHON_T_13:A10","CH_MHON_T_14:A8"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[]},{"id":"CL_MHON_06:GA-08","text":"[Emergent] Multiplicatio non extrinsecus figurae imponitur, sed e partitione, duplicatione, quadratura et comparatione interna procedit.","refs":["CH_MHON_T_13:NX6","CH_MHON_T_13:SD5","CH_MHON_T_13:FGS6"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[]},{"id":"CL_MHON_06:GA-09","text":"[Terminal] Interpretatio numeralis ad se ipsam non clauditur, sed in lucem et Verbum transponitur, ubi pluralitas virium sub unitate coordinata apparet.","refs":["CH_MHON_T_14:NX6","CH_MHON_T_14:D7","CH_MHON_T_14:ALT4"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[]},{"id":"CL_MHON_06:GA-10","text":"[Attractor] Monas intelligitur ut unitas distributiva quae diversos codices non absorbet per reductionem, sed eos conservat in mutua convertibilitate.","refs":["CH_MHON_T_12:SD1","CH_MHON_T_13:SD3","CH_MHON_T_14:SD2"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[]}],"original_name":"Global Load-Bearing Axioms"},{"number":"3","name":"Recurrence, Mutation, and Displacement Matrix","items":[{"id":"CL_MHON_06:RMD-01","text":"Relatio figurae ad numerum recurrit identice ut principium, sed mutatur ab exaltatione astrali ad partitionem crucis et postea ad compositionem verbalem.","refs":["CH_MHON_T_12:SD2","CH_MHON_T_13:SD1","CH_MHON_T_14:SD5"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[]},{"id":"CL_MHON_06:RMD-02","text":"Crux a figura geometrica ad litteram numeraliter operantem mutatur, deinde a littera ad medium productionis Lucis et Verbi transfertur.","refs":["CH_MHON_T_13:D2","CH_MHON_T_14:A6","CH_MHON_T_14:FGS6"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[]},{"id":"CL_MHON_06:RMD-03","text":"Taurus et crux eandem functionem hieroglyphicam retinent, sed Taurus praecipue ordines caelestes et elementales coniungit, crux autem ordines partitionis, numeri et Verbi.","refs":["CH_MHON_T_12:D7","CH_MHON_T_12:ME2","CH_MHON_T_14:ME1"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[]},{"id":"CL_MHON_06:RMD-04","text":"Quinarietas a dignitate figurali localiter significata ad attractorem arithmeticum transit, ex quo quinarium, quinquagenarium, centenarium et 252 componuntur.","refs":["CH_MHON_T_13:A6","CH_MHON_T_13:A9","CH_MHON_T_14:A9"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[]},{"id":"CL_MHON_06:RMD-05","text":"Unitas mutatur ab uno charactere ad potentiam denariam et tandem ad unitatem Verbi, ita ut idem terminus successive numericus, structuralis et theologico-verbaliter coordinatus fiat.","refs":["CH_MHON_T_13:A13","CH_MHON_T_13:D8","CH_MHON_T_14:SD4"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[]},{"id":"CL_MHON_06:RMD-06","text":"Elementum, initio medium inter astrologiam et cabalam, in hoc grege absorbetur in legem generalem conversionis, qua nulla materia inferior extra ordinem significationis manet.","refs":["CH_MHON_T_12:NX5","CH_MHON_T_12:SD4","CH_MHON_T_12:SNX4"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[]}],"original_name":"Recurrence, Mutation, and Displacement Matrix"},{"number":"4","name":"Lower-Layer vs Current-Level Distinction","items":[{"id":"CL_MHON_06:CGD-01","text":"Exaltatio Lunae in Tauro, robur Martis in Ariete et determinatae domus planetariae manent propositiones locales, quia ad dispositionem astralem particularem pertinent.","refs":["CH_MHON_T_12:A3","CH_MHON_T_12:A6","CH_MHON_T_12:A7"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[]},{"id":"CL_MHON_06:CGD-02","text":"Particulares numeri quinarii, quinquagenarii, centenarii et 252 sunt effectus locales, sed machina compositionis et conversionis quae eos producit ad gradum praesentem ascendit.","refs":["CH_MHON_T_13:A8","CH_MHON_T_13:A11","CH_MHON_T_14:D3"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[]},{"id":"CL_MHON_06:CGD-03","text":"Transitus a figura ad litteram in unitatibus inferioribus demonstratio specialis est; in grege fit regula generalis qua signum per mutationem situs vel divisionem novum codicem suscipit.","refs":["CH_MHON_T_12:D6","CH_MHON_T_13:D3","CH_MHON_T_14:D5"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[]},{"id":"CL_MHON_06:CGD-04","text":"Figurae Tauri et crucis non in unam imaginem materialem conflantur, sed in structuram superiorem absorbentur ut diversae instantiationes conversionis hieroglyphicae.","refs":["CH_MHON_T_12:FGS4","CH_MHON_T_13:ME6","CH_MHON_T_14:SD1"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[]},{"id":"CL_MHON_06:CGD-05","text":"Interpretatio initiatica mysteriorum manet limitatio epistemica localis, sed in gradu gregis fit indicium quod significatio plena requirit transitum inter codices et non datur inspectione simplici.","refs":["CH_MHON_T_12:A12","CH_MHON_T_12:NX1","CH_MHON_T_14:SNX3"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[]},{"id":"CL_MHON_06:CGD-06","text":"Clausura in Lux et Verbum est terminus transformativus praesentis gregis, quia numeros non simpliciter cumulat, sed eos in modum coordinationis semanticum redigit.","refs":["CH_MHON_T_14:D6","CH_MHON_T_14:D7","CH_MHON_T_14:ME6"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[]}],"original_name":"Lower-Layer vs Current-Level Distinction"},{"number":"5","name":"Global Apophatic Structure","items":[{"id":"CL_MHON_06:GNX-01","text":"Systema excludit signum quod ad figuram nudam, nomen mythicum, litteram linguisticam aut quantitatem arithmeticam unice reducatur.","refs":["CH_MHON_T_12:NX6","CH_MHON_T_13:NX3","CH_MHON_T_14:NX3"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[]},{"id":"CL_MHON_06:GNX-02","text":"Excluditur stabilitas sensus ab omni situ et conversione independens, quia vicinitas, metathesis, geminatio et distributio vim signi mutant.","refs":["CH_MHON_T_12:SNX5","CH_MHON_T_13:DNX2","CH_MHON_T_14:NX2"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[]},{"id":"CL_MHON_06:GNX-03","text":"Excluditur progressio mere additiva, quoniam dignitas, quadratura et compositio internam transmutationem potius quam successionem quantitatum exigunt.","refs":["CH_MHON_T_12:DNX3","CH_MHON_T_13:DNX5","CH_MHON_T_14:SD3"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[]},{"id":"CL_MHON_06:GNX-04","text":"Excluditur separatio absoluta inter causalitatem naturalem et significationem symbolicam, quia elementum, situs et figura eandem mediationem constituunt.","refs":["CH_MHON_T_12:SNX4","CH_MHON_T_12:ME4","CH_MHON_T_13:ALT4"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[]},{"id":"CL_MHON_06:GNX-05","text":"Excluditur clausura interpretationis in prima derivatione, quia unum signum novas rationes numerales, alphabeticas et verbales successive aperit.","refs":["CH_MHON_T_14:DNX5","CH_MHON_T_14:ME2","CH_MHON_T_14:ME3"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[]}],"original_name":"Global Apophatic Structure"},{"number":"6","name":"Deep Book Apophatics","items":[{"id":"CL_MHON_06:DBN-01","text":"Falsum esse debet mundum in quo figurae sint res passivae, quia systema requirit ut figura ipsa operationes conversionis et multiplicationis agat.","refs":["CH_MHON_T_12:DNX4","CH_MHON_T_13:SD1","CH_MHON_T_14:ME1"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[]},{"id":"CL_MHON_06:DBN-02","text":"Falsum esse debet ordinem disciplinarum autonomarum, quia astrologia, geometria, arithmetica, grammatica et cabala per eundem circuitum translatum sustentantur.","refs":["CH_MHON_T_12:DNX2","CH_MHON_T_12:SNX2","CH_MHON_T_13:ME6"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[]},{"id":"CL_MHON_06:DBN-03","text":"Falsum esse debet signum univocum, quia identitas eius tantum per stratificationem codicum et conversionum constituitur.","refs":["CH_MHON_T_13:SNX1","CH_MHON_T_14:DNX2","CH_MHON_T_14:SNX1"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[]},{"id":"CL_MHON_06:DBN-04","text":"Falsum esse debet numerus a figura separatus, quia omnis progressio numeralis hic a partitione, situ, relatione et comparatione formali pendet.","refs":["CH_MHON_T_13:DNX4","CH_MHON_T_13:FGS5","CH_MHON_T_14:FGS4"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[]},{"id":"CL_MHON_06:DBN-05","text":"Falsum esse debet Verbum immediatum et indemonstratum, quia Verbum terminale tantum ex coordinatione numeralis, litteralis et ternariae relationis emergit.","refs":["CH_MHON_T_14:NX6","CH_MHON_T_14:D7","CH_MHON_T_14:SNX4"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[]}],"original_name":"Deep Book Apophatics"},{"number":"7","name":"Super-Apophatics of the Whole","items":[{"id":"CL_MHON_06:BSA-01","text":"Systema circumagit impossibilitatem sensus pure immediati: nihil plene intellegitur nisi per transitum inter figuras, numeros, litteras et compositiones.","refs":["CH_MHON_T_12:NX1","CH_MHON_T_13:SD6","CH_MHON_T_14:SNX3"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[]},{"id":"CL_MHON_06:BSA-02","text":"Inadmissibilis est realitas composita ex rebus mutuo indifferentibus, quia locus, vicinitas, divisio et consensus omnes sunt conditiones effectivae significationis.","refs":["CH_MHON_T_12:SNX5","CH_MHON_T_13:A5","CH_MHON_T_14:A12"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[]},{"id":"CL_MHON_06:BSA-03","text":"Vetatur clausura finalis quae pluralitatem signi deleat, quia etiam compendium numericum interpretationem non finit sed ad productionem verbalem remittit.","refs":["CH_MHON_T_14:NX5","CH_MHON_T_14:SD3","CH_MHON_T_14:DNX5"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[]},{"id":"CL_MHON_06:BSA-04","text":"Ontologice inadmissibilis est oppositio simplex inter unum et multa, quia unitas operativa tantum per coordinationem partium, potentiarum et conversionum fit.","refs":["CH_MHON_T_13:A13","CH_MHON_T_13:SNX3","CH_MHON_T_14:SD4"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[]}],"original_name":"Super-Apophatics of the Whole"},{"number":"8","name":"Cross-Unit Derivative Propositions","items":[{"id":"CL_MHON_06:XD-01","text":"Relatio astralis et partitionis geometricae eandem legem generativam manifestant: dignitas vel numerus non ex elemento isolato, sed ex dispositione relatorum emergit.","refs":["CL_MHON_06:GA-01","CL_MHON_06:GA-05","CH_MHON_T_12:D1","CH_MHON_T_13:D1","CH_MHON_T_14:D1"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"Composite","passages":[]},{"id":"CL_MHON_06:XD-02","text":"Figura Tauri et crux diversis materiis operantur, sed ambae medium constituunt quo structura cosmica vel geometrica in seriem alphabetico-numericam convertitur.","refs":["CL_MHON_06:GA-02","CL_MHON_06:GA-04","CH_MHON_T_12:D6","CH_MHON_T_13:D2","CH_MHON_T_14:D2"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"Composite","passages":[]},{"id":"CL_MHON_06:XD-03","text":"Conversiones situs non sunt accidentia iconographica, sed mechanismi quibus figura eandem identitatem retinens novas potentias arithmeticas adquirit.","refs":["CL_MHON_06:GA-03","CL_MHON_06:GA-06","CH_MHON_T_12:A10","CH_MHON_T_13:D3","CH_MHON_T_14:NX2"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"Direct","passages":[]},{"id":"CL_MHON_06:XD-04","text":"Quinarietas transit ex indice figurali ad principium compositionis, quo partes crucis in magnitudines superiores et postea in compendium 252 ordinantur.","refs":["CL_MHON_06:GA-07","CL_MHON_06:GA-08","CH_MHON_T_13:A8","CH_MHON_T_13:D5","CH_MHON_T_14:D3"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"Composite","passages":[]},{"id":"CL_MHON_06:XD-05","text":"Unitas non antecedit conversiones ut fundamentum immobile, sed post conversiones redit ut effectus compositus potentiarum numericarum et verbalium.","refs":["CL_MHON_06:GA-03","CL_MHON_06:GA-09","CH_MHON_T_13:D8","CH_MHON_T_13:MRT6","CH_MHON_T_14:SD4"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"Tension","passages":[]},{"id":"CL_MHON_06:XD-06","text":"Reiectio termini neutralis inter figuram, litteram et numerum efficit ut omnis interpretatio plena necessario transdisciplinaris sit.","refs":["CL_MHON_06:GA-04","CL_MHON_06:GA-10","CH_MHON_T_12:SNX2","CH_MHON_T_13:SNX1","CH_MHON_T_14:SNX1"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"Negative","passages":[]}],"original_name":"Cross-Unit Derivative Propositions"},{"number":"9","name":"Book-Scale Super-Derivatives","items":[{"id":"CL_MHON_06:BSD-01","text":"Realitas systematis non componitur ex substantiis fixis, sed ex nodis convertibilibus, quorum identitas in operationibus translativis persistit.","refs":["CH_MHON_T_12:SD1","CH_MHON_T_13:SD2","CH_MHON_T_14:SD2"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[]},{"id":"CL_MHON_06:BSD-02","text":"Causalitas reconfiguratur ut causalitas relationis: vicinitas astralis, sectio geometrica, conversio situs et consensus verbaliter coordinatus omnes effectus generant.","refs":["CH_MHON_T_12:A2","CH_MHON_T_13:A5","CH_MHON_T_14:A12"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[]},{"id":"CL_MHON_06:BSD-03","text":"Tempus systematis est tempus recursionis, in quo reditus figurae non repetitionem inertem, sed ascensum ad novam potentiam significat.","refs":["CH_MHON_T_12:SD3","CH_MHON_T_13:D7","CH_MHON_T_14:SD3"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[]},{"id":"CL_MHON_06:BSD-04","text":"Subiectus cognoscens non spectator simplex est, sed interpres qui figuras dividere, convertere, comparare et componere debet ut earum virtutes actuaret.","refs":["CH_MHON_T_12:A12","CH_MHON_T_13:ALT3","CH_MHON_T_14:ALT3"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[]},{"id":"CL_MHON_06:BSD-05","text":"Verbum non est principium extra figuram positum, sed terminus in quo geometria, numerus et littera suam coordinationem ontologico-semanticam efficiunt.","refs":["CH_MHON_T_14:SD1","CH_MHON_T_14:SD5","CH_MHON_T_14:ALT4"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[]}],"original_name":"Book-Scale Super-Derivatives"},{"number":"10","name":"Recursive Loops, Escalation Paths, and Conceptual Attractors","items":[{"id":"CL_MHON_06:RLE-01","text":"Circuitus principalis est relatio → figura → littera → numerus → compositio → nova relatio, quo figura semper redit sed cum potestate aucta.","refs":["CH_MHON_T_12:ME5","CH_MHON_T_13:MRT8","CH_MHON_T_14:MRT7"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[]},{"id":"CL_MHON_06:RLE-02","text":"Via escalationis numeralis procedit a quinario ad quadraturam, quinquagenarium, centenarium et compendium, sed non in infinitum lineariter, quia circulariter ad unitatem reordinatur.","refs":["CH_MHON_T_13:A8","CH_MHON_T_13:D7","CH_MHON_T_13:ME5"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[]},{"id":"CL_MHON_06:RLE-03","text":"Via escalationis semanticus procedit a signo astrali vel geometrico ad hieroglyphum, ab hieroglypho ad litteram, et a littera ad Lucem atque Verbum.","refs":["CH_MHON_T_12:FGS7","CH_MHON_T_13:ME6","CH_MHON_T_14:AF6"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[]},{"id":"CL_MHON_06:RLE-04","text":"Attractor dominans est Monas ut unitas conversionum: non finis immobilis, sed centrum quo plurimi ordines sine dissolutione mutua conveniunt.","refs":["CH_MHON_T_12:A8","CH_MHON_T_12:SD1","CH_MHON_T_13:SD3"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[]},{"id":"CL_MHON_06:RLE-05","text":"Terminus Verbi machinae redintegratur, quia conclusio verbaliter magistralis non sistit processum sed ostendit quid coordinatio plurium potentiarum generare possit.","refs":["CH_MHON_T_14:D7","CH_MHON_T_14:SD3","CH_MHON_T_14:ME6"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[]}],"original_name":"Recursive Loops, Escalation Paths, and Conceptual Attractors"},{"number":"11","name":"Formal Generative Schema of the Whole Book","items":[{"id":"CL_MHON_06:FGS-B01","text":"Relatio localis astralis vel geometrica → determinatio situs, vicinitatis aut partitionis → productio figurae operationalis.","refs":["CH_MHON_T_12:A2","CH_MHON_T_13:A5","CH_MHON_T_14:A3"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[]},{"id":"CL_MHON_06:FGS-B02","text":"Figura operationalis → conversio hieroglyphica → transmissio relationis in litteram, numerum aut elementum.","refs":["CH_MHON_T_12:D6","CH_MHON_T_13:D2","CH_MHON_T_14:A4"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[]},{"id":"CL_MHON_06:FGS-B03","text":"Littera vel nota → geminatio, inversio, divisio aut compositio → generatio potentiae numericae non additive sed formaliter.","refs":["CH_MHON_T_13:A7","CH_MHON_T_13:D3","CH_MHON_T_14:FGS4"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[]},{"id":"CL_MHON_06:FGS-B04","text":"Potentia numerica → comparatio interna et coordinatio plurium ordinum → restitutio unitatis sicut effectus structurae.","refs":["CH_MHON_T_13:D7","CH_MHON_T_14:D4","CH_MHON_T_14:SD4"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[]},{"id":"CL_MHON_06:FGS-B05","text":"Unitas restituta → Lux et Verbum → relectio retroactiva figurae ut nexus cosmici, numerici et theologico-verbaliter activi.","refs":["CH_MHON_T_14:D6","CH_MHON_T_14:FGS6","CH_MHON_T_14:ALT4"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[]}],"original_name":"Formal Generative Schema of the Whole Book"},{"number":"12","name":"Whole-Book Argument Flow Map","items":[{"id":"CL_MHON_06:AFM-B01","text":"Relatio astralis et geometrica → reiectio proprietatis isolatae → figura ut medium relationis.","refs":["CH_MHON_T_12:NX2","CH_MHON_T_13:A1","CH_MHON_T_14:NX3"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[]},{"id":"CL_MHON_06:AFM-B02","text":"Figura ut medium relationis → situs, sectio et vicinitas → dignitas vel symmetria operativa.","refs":["CH_MHON_T_12:A2","CH_MHON_T_13:D1","CH_MHON_T_14:A3"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[]},{"id":"CL_MHON_06:AFM-B03","text":"Dignitas vel symmetria operativa → nota hieroglyphica → translatio in litteram et numerum.","refs":["CH_MHON_T_12:A8","CH_MHON_T_13:A6","CH_MHON_T_14:A4"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[]},{"id":"CL_MHON_06:AFM-B04","text":"Littera et numerus → geminatio, quadratura et compositio → potentiae multiplicatae et compendia numeralia.","refs":["CH_MHON_T_13:A12","CH_MHON_T_13:D8","CH_MHON_T_14:D3"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[]},{"id":"CL_MHON_06:AFM-B05","text":"Compendium numerale → divisio litteralis et collatio virium → Lux, consensus ternarius et Verbum magistrale.","refs":["CH_MHON_T_14:D5","CH_MHON_T_14:D6","CH_MHON_T_14:D7"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[]}],"original_name":"Whole-Book Argument Flow Map"},{"number":"13","name":"Macro Map-Effects","items":[{"id":"CL_MHON_06:MME-01","text":"Ager conceptualis reordinatur ita ut caelum, figura, littera et numerus non sint obiecta diversa, sed diversa momenta unius processus interpretativi.","refs":["CH_MHON_T_12:ME2","CH_MHON_T_13:ME6","CH_MHON_T_14:ME1"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[]},{"id":"CL_MHON_06:MME-02","text":"Oppositio inter mythum et demonstrationem corruit, quia nomen, figura et numerus in eadem machina probationis et translationis constituuntur.","refs":["CH_MHON_T_12:ME3","CH_MHON_T_13:SD1","CH_MHON_T_14:SD1"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[]},{"id":"CL_MHON_06:MME-03","text":"Oppositio inter unum et multiplex reclassificatur: unum signum potest esse simultanee figura, character, numerus, potestas et terminus verbaliter coordinatus.","refs":["CH_MHON_T_13:SD4","CH_MHON_T_14:A7","CH_MHON_T_14:SD4"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[]},{"id":"CL_MHON_06:MME-04","text":"Numeri desinunt esse quantitates nudae et fiunt indices operationum, quarum formae causales sunt sectio, situs, geminatio, quadratura et consensus.","refs":["CH_MHON_T_13:DNX4","CH_MHON_T_13:ALT2","CH_MHON_T_14:ALT2"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[]},{"id":"CL_MHON_06:MME-05","text":"Lectio necessaria fit diagrammatica et translatrice: interpretari est relationes inter codices sequi, non sensum unius vocabuli vel figurae immobilis extrahere.","refs":["CH_MHON_T_12:SD3","CH_MHON_T_13:ALT3","CH_MHON_T_14:ALT3"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[]}],"original_name":"Macro Map-Effects"},{"number":"14","name":"Altitude Integration","items":[{"id":"CL_MHON_06:ALT-B01","text":"In gradu superficiei, Sol, Luna, Taurus, Aries et crux per dignitates, angulos, partes, notationes et litteras explicite disponuntur.","refs":["CH_MHON_T_12:ALT1","CH_MHON_T_13:ALT1","CH_MHON_T_14:ALT1"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[]},{"id":"CL_MHON_06:ALT-B02","text":"In gradu structurali, relatio, vicinitas, divisio, conversio situs et compositio sunt operationes quae significationem et numerum generant.","refs":["CH_MHON_T_12:ALT2","CH_MHON_T_13:ALT2","CH_MHON_T_14:ALT2"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[]},{"id":"CL_MHON_06:ALT-B03","text":"In gradu metastrategico, interpretatio non quaerit codicem unicum, sed imponit transitum disciplinarum tamquam condicionem cognitionis plenae.","refs":["CH_MHON_T_12:ALT3","CH_MHON_T_13:SD6","CH_MHON_T_14:ALT3"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[]},{"id":"CL_MHON_06:ALT-B04","text":"In gradu ontologico-temporali, realitas est ordo transmutativus in quo relationes producunt dignitates, numeri emergunt ex figuris, et unitas ex pluralitate coordinata redit.","refs":["CH_MHON_T_12:ALT4","CH_MHON_T_13:ALT4","CH_MHON_T_14:ALT4"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[]},{"id":"CL_MHON_06:ALT-B05","text":"In gradu methodologico-performativo, ipsa dispositio argumentorum efficit quod asserit, quia textus figuras successive dividit, convertit, numerat et in unitatem reordinat.","refs":["CH_MHON_T_12:FGS7","CH_MHON_T_13:FGS7","CH_MHON_T_14:FGS6"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[]}],"original_name":"Altitude Integration"},{"number":"15","name":"Internal Tensions, Mutation Points, and Fault Lines","items":[{"id":"CL_MHON_06:FL-01","text":"Aequalitas et inaequalitas partium crucis simul admittuntur, sed demonstratio principalis symmetriam similium partium privilegiat; tensio manet inter pluralitatem distributionum et normam iustitiae.","refs":["CH_MHON_T_13:A2","CH_MHON_T_13:A4","CH_MHON_T_13:MRT5"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[]},{"id":"CL_MHON_06:FL-02","text":"Transitus a geometria ad litteram innititur congruentiis symbolicis et consuetudini antiquae, non ubique necessitate formali plene demonstrata.","refs":["CH_MHON_T_13:DNX6","CH_MHON_T_13:MRT4","CH_MHON_T_14:MRT6"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[]},{"id":"CL_MHON_06:FL-03","text":"Reductio magnitudinum multiplicatarum ad centenarium circularem structuram recursionis postulat, sed ratio formalis huius reditus minus explicata manet.","refs":["CH_MHON_T_13:D7","CH_MHON_T_13:ME5","CH_MHON_T_13:MRT7"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[]},{"id":"CL_MHON_06:FL-04","text":"Clausura in Lux et Verbum plus postulatur quam numerice demonstratur, quia nexus inter divisam crucem, litteras et necessitatem conclusionis magistralis incompletus manet.","refs":["CH_MHON_T_14:D6","CH_MHON_T_14:MRT4","CH_MHON_T_14:MRT7"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[]},{"id":"CL_MHON_06:MP-01","text":"Exaltatio mutatur e vocabulo astrologico in formam generalem transmutationis, qua relatio localis dignitatem interpretativam accipit.","refs":["CH_MHON_T_12:D1","CH_MHON_T_12:SD2","CH_MHON_T_12:ALT2"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[]},{"id":"CL_MHON_06:MP-02","text":"Crux mutatur e signo geometrico in operatorium arithmeticum cum situs, geminatio et quadratura figuras in virtutes numerales convertunt.","refs":["CH_MHON_T_13:D3","CH_MHON_T_13:SD1","CH_MHON_T_13:ME1"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[]},{"id":"CL_MHON_06:MP-03","text":"Unitas mutatur ex charactere singulari in effectum consensualem, cum potestas denaria et pluralitas litterarum ad Verbum coordinantur.","refs":["CH_MHON_T_13:A13","CH_MHON_T_13:D8","CH_MHON_T_14:SD4"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[]}],"original_name":"Internal Tensions, Mutation Points, and Fault Lines"},{"number":"16","name":"Hidden System Image","items":[{"id":"CL_MHON_06:HSI-01","text":"Mundus implicite concipitur ut rete figurarum operantium, in quo positiones, anguli, partes et vicinitates non accidentia sunt, sed canales productionis ontologicae et semanticae.","refs":["CH_MHON_T_12:ME1","CH_MHON_T_13:A5","CH_MHON_T_14:ALT2"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[]},{"id":"CL_MHON_06:HSI-02","text":"Subiectus intra hanc machinam fit interpres-initiatum, cuius actio est conversiones non invenire ex nihilo, sed relationes iam distributas in ordinem legibilem redigere.","refs":["CH_MHON_T_12:A12","CH_MHON_T_12:SD5","CH_MHON_T_13:ALT3"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[]},{"id":"CL_MHON_06:HSI-03","text":"Tempus est tempus transitus et reditus: figura eadem manet satis ut recognoscatur, sed mutatur satis ut novam potentiam acquirat.","refs":["CH_MHON_T_12:ALT4","CH_MHON_T_13:A7","CH_MHON_T_14:SD3"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[]},{"id":"CL_MHON_06:HSI-04","text":"Certamen interpretativum non est inter verum et falsum signum simpliciter, sed inter lectionem quae conversiones sustinet et lectionem quae signum in unum codicem inertem claudit.","refs":["CH_MHON_T_13:DNX1","CH_MHON_T_13:SNX2","CH_MHON_T_14:SNX1"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[]}],"original_name":"Hidden System Image"},{"number":"17","name":"Minimal Whole-Book Reconstruction Test","items":[{"id":"CL_MHON_06:MRT-B01","text":"Axiomata de relatione, figura operationali, conversione codicum et unitate composita sufficiunt ad restituendam machinam principalem huius gregis.","refs":["CH_MHON_T_12:SD1","CH_MHON_T_13:SD3","CH_MHON_T_14:MRT5"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[]},{"id":"CL_MHON_06:MRT-B02","text":"Structura firmissima est ubi vicinitas, situs, divisio et compositio demonstrantur tamquam operationes productrices, non ut ornamenta explanatoria.","refs":["CH_MHON_T_12:ALT2","CH_MHON_T_13:SD1","CH_MHON_T_14:ALT2"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[]},{"id":"CL_MHON_06:MRT-B03","text":"Minus determinata manent necessitas transitus ab analogia geometrica ad litteram et nexus ultimorum calculorum cum conclusione de Luce et Verbo.","refs":["CH_MHON_T_13:MRT4","CH_MHON_T_14:MRT4","CH_MHON_T_14:MRT6"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[]},{"id":"CL_MHON_06:MRT-B04","text":"Cohaerentia gregis non est identitas propositionum localium, sed perseverantia regulae qua idem signum per conversiones diversas ad structuram ampliorem redit.","refs":["CH_MHON_T_12:SD3","CH_MHON_T_13:SD2","CH_MHON_T_14:SD2"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[]},{"id":"CL_MHON_06:MRT-B05","text":"Restitutio verum motum servat si astrologia, geometria, arithmetica et theologia verbalis leguntur ut gradus conversionis, non ut capitula separata aut argumenta parallela.","refs":["CH_MHON_T_13:AS6","CH_MHON_T_13:MRT8","CH_MHON_T_14:MRT7"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[]}],"original_name":"Minimal Whole-Book Reconstruction Test"}],"language_code":"la","language_name":"Latina","translations":[{"id":"CAREVAR_20261006T223338894738Z_4273f4a6","language_code":"en","language_name":"English","title":"Chapters 16–18","source_sha256":"524f581104e29e5fe30de1e2d04d277b33cac234e5985126f30b259a12e8333b","sha256":"e283a9db232f6553167e4df1e14c77a6772d81a261c71988bdf0e61683fecf6e","rendition_type":"machine","review_status":"machine","model":"gpt-5.6-terra","created":"2026-10-06T22:33:38.895736+00:00","sections":[{"number":"1","name":"Book-Scale Argumentative Spine","items":[{"id":"CL_MHON_06:ABS-01","text":"The system asks how celestial and geometrical figures, through position, partition, conversion, and composition, can pass into numerical, alphabetical, elemental, and verbal orders.","refs":["CH_MHON_T_12:AS5","CH_MHON_T_13:AS3","CH_MHON_T_14:AS3"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[]},{"id":"CL_MHON_06:ABS-02","text":"The global thesis establishes that the hieroglyphic sign is not a fixed thing, but an operator that generates cosmic relations and geometrical structures into numbers, letters, and interpretative powers.","refs":["CH_MHON_T_12:A8","CH_MHON_T_13:SD1","CH_MHON_T_14:SD1"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[]},{"id":"CL_MHON_06:ABS-03","text":"The argument proceeds reconstructively: it does not begin from a univocal definition of the sign, but infers from the transformations of the same figure the necessity of multiple orders of meaning.","refs":["CH_MHON_T_12:AS6","CH_MHON_T_13:SD2","CH_MHON_T_14:SD2"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[]},{"id":"CL_MHON_06:ABS-04","text":"The movement advances from local relation to composite power: astral proximity produces dignity, geometrical division produces number, and numerical-literal composition produces the terminal Word.","refs":["CH_MHON_T_12:A2","CH_MHON_T_13:SD5","CH_MHON_T_14:SD5"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[]},{"id":"CL_MHON_06:ABS-05","text":"The movement of the system is spiral rather than linear, because the same figure returns through repeated conversions, yet at each return assumes another operative order.","refs":["CH_MHON_T_12:SD3","CH_MHON_T_13:A7","CH_MHON_T_14:DNX5"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[]},{"id":"CL_MHON_06:ABS-06","text":"Closure does not consist in the immobility of a single sense, but in the coordination of multiple powers under hieroglyphic unity, which displays Light and the Word as effects of conversion.","refs":["CH_MHON_T_14:SD4","CH_MHON_T_14:FGS6","CH_MHON_T_14:ALT4"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[]},{"id":"CL_MHON_06:ABS-07","text":"The system passes from an astrology of relations to an arithmology of operations, then to verbal theology, without rigid separation between natural order, figure, and meaning.","refs":["CH_MHON_T_12:SD1","CH_MHON_T_13:SD6","CH_MHON_T_14:SNX2"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[]},{"id":"CL_MHON_06:ABS-08","text":"The argument receives its force from the fact that the figure not only signifies, but itself becomes the mechanism by which meaning is successively produced, multiplied, and ordered.","refs":["CH_MHON_T_12:SD3","CH_MHON_T_13:SD4","CH_MHON_T_14:ME3"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[]}]},{"number":"2","name":"Global Load-Bearing Axioms","items":[{"id":"CL_MHON_06:GA-01","text":"[Persistent] Meaning consists not in the isolated substance of the sign, but in its relations to position, division, proximity, and conversion.","refs":["CH_MHON_T_12:NX2","CH_MHON_T_13:NX4","CH_MHON_T_14:NX2"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[]},{"id":"CL_MHON_06:GA-02","text":"[Persistent] The hieroglyphic figure is at once demonstration and operator, because it transfers invisible relations into repeatable and tractable forms.","refs":["CH_MHON_T_12:NX4","CH_MHON_T_13:SD1","CH_MHON_T_14:SD1"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[]},{"id":"CL_MHON_06:GA-03","text":"[Mutating] The unity of the figure does not prevent a plurality of meanings, but generates it through partition, doubling, and superposition.","refs":["CH_MHON_T_13:NX5","CH_MHON_T_14:NX1","CH_MHON_T_14:SD2"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[]},{"id":"CL_MHON_06:GA-04","text":"[Persistent] Geometry, alphabet, and arithmetic are not separate disciplines, but orders of conversion of one hieroglyphic machine.","refs":["CH_MHON_T_12:DNX2","CH_MHON_T_13:SD6","CH_MHON_T_14:ME2"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[]},{"id":"CL_MHON_06:GA-05","text":"[Emergent] Dignity, number, and the Word are effects of ordered relations, not immediately given qualities of things or signs.","refs":["CH_MHON_T_12:SD2","CH_MHON_T_13:SD5","CH_MHON_T_14:ME6"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[]},{"id":"CL_MHON_06:GA-06","text":"[Mutating] Position is not an external condition of the figure, but a generative variable that converts the same form into another numerical or semantic power.","refs":["CH_MHON_T_12:SNX5","CH_MHON_T_13:A7","CH_MHON_T_14:A3"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[]},{"id":"CL_MHON_06:GA-07","text":"[Attractor] Quinarity and denarity act as compositional nodes through which the figure passes from local elements to higher powers.","refs":["CH_MHON_T_13:A8","CH_MHON_T_13:A10","CH_MHON_T_14:A8"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[]},{"id":"CL_MHON_06:GA-08","text":"[Emergent] Multiplication is not imposed extrinsically upon the figure, but proceeds from partition, duplication, squaring, and internal comparison.","refs":["CH_MHON_T_13:NX6","CH_MHON_T_13:SD5","CH_MHON_T_13:FGS6"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[]},{"id":"CL_MHON_06:GA-09","text":"[Terminal] Numerical interpretation does not close upon itself, but is transposed into light and the Word, where plurality of powers appears under coordinated unity.","refs":["CH_MHON_T_14:NX6","CH_MHON_T_14:D7","CH_MHON_T_14:ALT4"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[]},{"id":"CL_MHON_06:GA-10","text":"[Attractor] The Monad is understood as a distributive unity that does not absorb diverse codes through reduction, but preserves them in mutual convertibility.","refs":["CH_MHON_T_12:SD1","CH_MHON_T_13:SD3","CH_MHON_T_14:SD2"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[]}],"original_name":"Global Load-Bearing Axioms"},{"number":"3","name":"Recurrence, Mutation, and Displacement Matrix","items":[{"id":"CL_MHON_06:RMD-01","text":"The relation of figure to number recurs identically as a principle, but changes from astral exaltation to the partition of the cross and then to verbal composition.","refs":["CH_MHON_T_12:SD2","CH_MHON_T_13:SD1","CH_MHON_T_14:SD5"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[]},{"id":"CL_MHON_06:RMD-02","text":"The cross changes from a geometrical figure into a numerically operative letter, then is transferred from letter into a means of producing Light and the Word.","refs":["CH_MHON_T_13:D2","CH_MHON_T_14:A6","CH_MHON_T_14:FGS6"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[]},{"id":"CL_MHON_06:RMD-03","text":"Taurus and the cross retain the same hieroglyphic function, but Taurus chiefly joins celestial and elemental orders, whereas the cross joins the orders of partition, number, and the Word.","refs":["CH_MHON_T_12:D7","CH_MHON_T_12:ME2","CH_MHON_T_14:ME1"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[]},{"id":"CL_MHON_06:RMD-04","text":"Quinarity passes from locally signified figural dignity to an arithmetic attractor, from which the quinary, quinquagenary, centenary, and 252 are composed.","refs":["CH_MHON_T_13:A6","CH_MHON_T_13:A9","CH_MHON_T_14:A9"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[]},{"id":"CL_MHON_06:RMD-05","text":"Unity changes from a single character into denary power and finally into the unity of the Word, such that the same term successively becomes numerical, structural, and theologically-verbally coordinated.","refs":["CH_MHON_T_13:A13","CH_MHON_T_13:D8","CH_MHON_T_14:SD4"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[]},{"id":"CL_MHON_06:RMD-06","text":"The element, initially a medium between astrology and cabala, is in this group absorbed into the general law of conversion, by which no lower matter remains outside the order of meaning.","refs":["CH_MHON_T_12:NX5","CH_MHON_T_12:SD4","CH_MHON_T_12:SNX4"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[]}],"original_name":"Recurrence, Mutation, and Displacement Matrix"},{"number":"4","name":"Lower-Layer vs Current-Level Distinction","items":[{"id":"CL_MHON_06:CGD-01","text":"The exaltation of the Moon in Taurus, the strength of Mars in Aries, and determined planetary houses remain local propositions, because they pertain to a particular astral disposition.","refs":["CH_MHON_T_12:A3","CH_MHON_T_12:A6","CH_MHON_T_12:A7"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[]},{"id":"CL_MHON_06:CGD-02","text":"The particular numbers five, fifty, one hundred, and 252 are local effects, but the machine of composition and conversion that produces them rises to the present level.","refs":["CH_MHON_T_13:A8","CH_MHON_T_13:A11","CH_MHON_T_14:D3"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[]},{"id":"CL_MHON_06:CGD-03","text":"The passage from figure to letter is a special demonstration in the lower units; in the group it becomes a general rule by which the sign assumes a new code through change of position or division.","refs":["CH_MHON_T_12:D6","CH_MHON_T_13:D3","CH_MHON_T_14:D5"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[]},{"id":"CL_MHON_06:CGD-04","text":"The figures of Taurus and the cross are not fused into one material image, but are absorbed into a higher structure as diverse instantiations of hieroglyphic conversion.","refs":["CH_MHON_T_12:FGS4","CH_MHON_T_13:ME6","CH_MHON_T_14:SD1"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[]},{"id":"CL_MHON_06:CGD-05","text":"The initiatic interpretation of mysteries remains a local epistemic limitation, but at the level of the group becomes an indication that full meaning requires passage among codes and is not given by simple inspection.","refs":["CH_MHON_T_12:A12","CH_MHON_T_12:NX1","CH_MHON_T_14:SNX3"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[]},{"id":"CL_MHON_06:CGD-06","text":"Closure in Light and the Word is the transformative terminus of the present group, because it does not simply accumulate numbers, but reduces them to a semantic mode of coordination.","refs":["CH_MHON_T_14:D6","CH_MHON_T_14:D7","CH_MHON_T_14:ME6"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[]}],"original_name":"Lower-Layer vs Current-Level Distinction"},{"number":"5","name":"Global Apophatic Structure","items":[{"id":"CL_MHON_06:GNX-01","text":"The system excludes a sign that is uniquely reduced to a bare figure, mythical name, linguistic letter, or arithmetic quantity.","refs":["CH_MHON_T_12:NX6","CH_MHON_T_13:NX3","CH_MHON_T_14:NX3"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[]},{"id":"CL_MHON_06:GNX-02","text":"The stability of meaning independent of every position and conversion is excluded, because proximity, metathesis, doubling, and distribution change the force of the sign.","refs":["CH_MHON_T_12:SNX5","CH_MHON_T_13:DNX2","CH_MHON_T_14:NX2"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[]},{"id":"CL_MHON_06:GNX-03","text":"Merely additive progression is excluded, since dignity, squaring, and composition require internal transmutation rather than a succession of quantities.","refs":["CH_MHON_T_12:DNX3","CH_MHON_T_13:DNX5","CH_MHON_T_14:SD3"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[]},{"id":"CL_MHON_06:GNX-04","text":"Absolute separation between natural causality and symbolic meaning is excluded, because element, position, and figure constitute the same mediation.","refs":["CH_MHON_T_12:SNX4","CH_MHON_T_12:ME4","CH_MHON_T_13:ALT4"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[]},{"id":"CL_MHON_06:GNX-05","text":"The closure of interpretation in the first derivation is excluded, because one sign successively opens new numerical, alphabetical, and verbal relations.","refs":["CH_MHON_T_14:DNX5","CH_MHON_T_14:ME2","CH_MHON_T_14:ME3"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[]}],"original_name":"Global Apophatic Structure"},{"number":"6","name":"Deep Book Apophatics","items":[{"id":"CL_MHON_06:DBN-01","text":"A world in which figures are passive things must be false, because the system requires that the figure itself perform operations of conversion and multiplication.","refs":["CH_MHON_T_12:DNX4","CH_MHON_T_13:SD1","CH_MHON_T_14:ME1"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[]},{"id":"CL_MHON_06:DBN-02","text":"An order of autonomous disciplines must be false, because astrology, geometry, arithmetic, grammar, and cabala are sustained through the same circuit of translation.","refs":["CH_MHON_T_12:DNX2","CH_MHON_T_12:SNX2","CH_MHON_T_13:ME6"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[]},{"id":"CL_MHON_06:DBN-03","text":"A univocal sign must be false, because its identity is constituted only through the stratification of codes and conversions.","refs":["CH_MHON_T_13:SNX1","CH_MHON_T_14:DNX2","CH_MHON_T_14:SNX1"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[]},{"id":"CL_MHON_06:DBN-04","text":"A number separated from figure must be false, because every numerical progression here depends upon partition, position, relation, and formal comparison.","refs":["CH_MHON_T_13:DNX4","CH_MHON_T_13:FGS5","CH_MHON_T_14:FGS4"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[]},{"id":"CL_MHON_06:DBN-05","text":"An immediate and undemonstrated Word must be false, because the terminal Word emerges only from the coordination of numerical, literal, and ternary relation.","refs":["CH_MHON_T_14:NX6","CH_MHON_T_14:D7","CH_MHON_T_14:SNX4"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[]}],"original_name":"Deep Book Apophatics"},{"number":"7","name":"Super-Apophatics of the Whole","items":[{"id":"CL_MHON_06:BSA-01","text":"The system revolves around the impossibility of purely immediate meaning: nothing is fully understood except through passage among figures, numbers, letters, and compositions.","refs":["CH_MHON_T_12:NX1","CH_MHON_T_13:SD6","CH_MHON_T_14:SNX3"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[]},{"id":"CL_MHON_06:BSA-02","text":"A reality composed of mutually indifferent things is inadmissible, because place, proximity, division, and agreement are all effective conditions of meaning.","refs":["CH_MHON_T_12:SNX5","CH_MHON_T_13:A5","CH_MHON_T_14:A12"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[]},{"id":"CL_MHON_06:BSA-03","text":"A final closure that erases the plurality of the sign is prohibited, because even a numerical compendium does not end interpretation but refers it to verbal production.","refs":["CH_MHON_T_14:NX5","CH_MHON_T_14:SD3","CH_MHON_T_14:DNX5"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[]},{"id":"CL_MHON_06:BSA-04","text":"A simple opposition between one and many is ontologically inadmissible, because operative unity comes about only through coordination of parts, powers, and conversions.","refs":["CH_MHON_T_13:A13","CH_MHON_T_13:SNX3","CH_MHON_T_14:SD4"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[]}],"original_name":"Super-Apophatics of the Whole"},{"number":"8","name":"Cross-Unit Derivative Propositions","items":[{"id":"CL_MHON_06:XD-01","text":"Astral relation and geometrical partition manifest the same generative law: dignity or number emerges not from an isolated element, but from the disposition of relata.","refs":["CL_MHON_06:GA-01","CL_MHON_06:GA-05","CH_MHON_T_12:D1","CH_MHON_T_13:D1","CH_MHON_T_14:D1"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"Composite","passages":[]},{"id":"CL_MHON_06:XD-02","text":"The figure of Taurus and the cross operate with different materials, but both constitute a medium by which cosmic or geometrical structure is converted into an alphabetic-numerical series.","refs":["CL_MHON_06:GA-02","CL_MHON_06:GA-04","CH_MHON_T_12:D6","CH_MHON_T_13:D2","CH_MHON_T_14:D2"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"Composite","passages":[]},{"id":"CL_MHON_06:XD-03","text":"Conversions of position are not iconographic accidents, but mechanisms by which a figure, retaining the same identity, acquires new arithmetic powers.","refs":["CL_MHON_06:GA-03","CL_MHON_06:GA-06","CH_MHON_T_12:A10","CH_MHON_T_13:D3","CH_MHON_T_14:NX2"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"Direct","passages":[]},{"id":"CL_MHON_06:XD-04","text":"Quinarity passes from a figural index to a principle of composition, by which the parts of the cross are ordered into higher magnitudes and afterward into the compendium 252.","refs":["CL_MHON_06:GA-07","CL_MHON_06:GA-08","CH_MHON_T_13:A8","CH_MHON_T_13:D5","CH_MHON_T_14:D3"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"Composite","passages":[]},{"id":"CL_MHON_06:XD-05","text":"Unity does not precede conversions as an immobile foundation, but returns after conversions as a composite effect of numerical and verbal powers.","refs":["CL_MHON_06:GA-03","CL_MHON_06:GA-09","CH_MHON_T_13:D8","CH_MHON_T_13:MRT6","CH_MHON_T_14:SD4"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"Tension","passages":[]},{"id":"CL_MHON_06:XD-06","text":"The rejection of a neutral term between figure, letter, and number entails that every full interpretation is necessarily transdisciplinary.","refs":["CL_MHON_06:GA-04","CL_MHON_06:GA-10","CH_MHON_T_12:SNX2","CH_MHON_T_13:SNX1","CH_MHON_T_14:SNX1"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"Negative","passages":[]}],"original_name":"Cross-Unit Derivative Propositions"},{"number":"9","name":"Book-Scale Super-Derivatives","items":[{"id":"CL_MHON_06:BSD-01","text":"The reality of the system is not composed of fixed substances, but of convertible nodes, whose identity persists in translative operations.","refs":["CH_MHON_T_12:SD1","CH_MHON_T_13:SD2","CH_MHON_T_14:SD2"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[]},{"id":"CL_MHON_06:BSD-02","text":"Causality is reconfigured as the causality of relation: astral proximity, geometrical section, conversion of position, and verbally coordinated agreement all generate effects.","refs":["CH_MHON_T_12:A2","CH_MHON_T_13:A5","CH_MHON_T_14:A12"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[]},{"id":"CL_MHON_06:BSD-03","text":"The time of the system is the time of recursion, in which the return of the figure signifies not inert repetition, but ascent to a new power.","refs":["CH_MHON_T_12:SD3","CH_MHON_T_13:D7","CH_MHON_T_14:SD3"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[]},{"id":"CL_MHON_06:BSD-04","text":"The knowing subject is not a simple spectator, but an interpreter who must divide, convert, compare, and compose figures in order to actualize their powers.","refs":["CH_MHON_T_12:A12","CH_MHON_T_13:ALT3","CH_MHON_T_14:ALT3"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[]},{"id":"CL_MHON_06:BSD-05","text":"The Word is not a principle set outside the figure, but the terminus in which geometry, number, and letter effect their ontological-semantic coordination.","refs":["CH_MHON_T_14:SD1","CH_MHON_T_14:SD5","CH_MHON_T_14:ALT4"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[]}],"original_name":"Book-Scale Super-Derivatives"},{"number":"10","name":"Recursive Loops, Escalation Paths, and Conceptual Attractors","items":[{"id":"CL_MHON_06:RLE-01","text":"The principal circuit is relation → figure → letter → number → composition → new relation, by which the figure always returns, but with increased power.","refs":["CH_MHON_T_12:ME5","CH_MHON_T_13:MRT8","CH_MHON_T_14:MRT7"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[]},{"id":"CL_MHON_06:RLE-02","text":"The path of numerical escalation proceeds from the quinary to squaring, the quinquagenary, the centenary, and the compendium, but not linearly to infinity, because it is circularly reordered to unity.","refs":["CH_MHON_T_13:A8","CH_MHON_T_13:D7","CH_MHON_T_13:ME5"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[]},{"id":"CL_MHON_06:RLE-03","text":"The path of semantic escalation proceeds from an astral or geometrical sign to hieroglyph, from hieroglyph to letter, and from letter to Light and the Word.","refs":["CH_MHON_T_12:FGS7","CH_MHON_T_13:ME6","CH_MHON_T_14:AF6"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[]},{"id":"CL_MHON_06:RLE-04","text":"The dominant attractor is the Monad as unity of conversions: not an immobile end, but a center at which multiple orders converge without mutual dissolution.","refs":["CH_MHON_T_12:A8","CH_MHON_T_12:SD1","CH_MHON_T_13:SD3"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[]},{"id":"CL_MHON_06:RLE-05","text":"The terminus of the Word is reintegrated into the machine, because the masterful verbal conclusion does not halt the process but shows what the coordination of multiple powers can generate.","refs":["CH_MHON_T_14:D7","CH_MHON_T_14:SD3","CH_MHON_T_14:ME6"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[]}],"original_name":"Recursive Loops, Escalation Paths, and Conceptual Attractors"},{"number":"11","name":"Formal Generative Schema of the Whole Book","items":[{"id":"CL_MHON_06:FGS-B01","text":"Local astral or geometrical relation → determination of position, proximity, or partition → production of an operational figure.","refs":["CH_MHON_T_12:A2","CH_MHON_T_13:A5","CH_MHON_T_14:A3"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[]},{"id":"CL_MHON_06:FGS-B02","text":"Operational figure → hieroglyphic conversion → transmission of relation into letter, number, or element.","refs":["CH_MHON_T_12:D6","CH_MHON_T_13:D2","CH_MHON_T_14:A4"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[]},{"id":"CL_MHON_06:FGS-B03","text":"Letter or mark → doubling, inversion, division, or composition → generation of numerical power not additively but formally.","refs":["CH_MHON_T_13:A7","CH_MHON_T_13:D3","CH_MHON_T_14:FGS4"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[]},{"id":"CL_MHON_06:FGS-B04","text":"Numerical power → internal comparison and coordination of multiple orders → restoration of unity as an effect of structure.","refs":["CH_MHON_T_13:D7","CH_MHON_T_14:D4","CH_MHON_T_14:SD4"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[]},{"id":"CL_MHON_06:FGS-B05","text":"Restored unity → Light and the Word → retroactive rereading of the figure as a nexus cosmically, numerically, and theologically-verbally active.","refs":["CH_MHON_T_14:D6","CH_MHON_T_14:FGS6","CH_MHON_T_14:ALT4"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[]}],"original_name":"Formal Generative Schema of the Whole Book"},{"number":"12","name":"Whole-Book Argument Flow Map","items":[{"id":"CL_MHON_06:AFM-B01","text":"Astral and geometrical relation → rejection of isolated property → figure as medium of relation.","refs":["CH_MHON_T_12:NX2","CH_MHON_T_13:A1","CH_MHON_T_14:NX3"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[]},{"id":"CL_MHON_06:AFM-B02","text":"Figure as medium of relation → position, section, and proximity → dignity or operative symmetry.","refs":["CH_MHON_T_12:A2","CH_MHON_T_13:D1","CH_MHON_T_14:A3"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[]},{"id":"CL_MHON_06:AFM-B03","text":"Dignity or operative symmetry → hieroglyphic mark → translation into letter and number.","refs":["CH_MHON_T_12:A8","CH_MHON_T_13:A6","CH_MHON_T_14:A4"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[]},{"id":"CL_MHON_06:AFM-B04","text":"Letter and number → doubling, squaring, and composition → multiplied powers and numerical compendia.","refs":["CH_MHON_T_13:A12","CH_MHON_T_13:D8","CH_MHON_T_14:D3"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[]},{"id":"CL_MHON_06:AFM-B05","text":"Numerical compendium → literal division and collation of powers → Light, ternary agreement, and masterful Word.","refs":["CH_MHON_T_14:D5","CH_MHON_T_14:D6","CH_MHON_T_14:D7"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[]}],"original_name":"Whole-Book Argument Flow Map"},{"number":"13","name":"Macro Map-Effects","items":[{"id":"CL_MHON_06:MME-01","text":"The conceptual field is reordered such that heaven, figure, letter, and number are not diverse objects, but diverse moments of one interpretative process.","refs":["CH_MHON_T_12:ME2","CH_MHON_T_13:ME6","CH_MHON_T_14:ME1"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[]},{"id":"CL_MHON_06:MME-02","text":"The opposition between myth and demonstration collapses, because name, figure, and number are constituted in the same machine of proof and translation.","refs":["CH_MHON_T_12:ME3","CH_MHON_T_13:SD1","CH_MHON_T_14:SD1"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[]},{"id":"CL_MHON_06:MME-03","text":"The opposition between one and many is reclassified: one sign can be simultaneously figure, character, number, power, and verbally coordinated terminus.","refs":["CH_MHON_T_13:SD4","CH_MHON_T_14:A7","CH_MHON_T_14:SD4"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[]},{"id":"CL_MHON_06:MME-04","text":"Numbers cease to be bare quantities and become indices of operations, whose causal forms are section, position, doubling, squaring, and agreement.","refs":["CH_MHON_T_13:DNX4","CH_MHON_T_13:ALT2","CH_MHON_T_14:ALT2"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[]},{"id":"CL_MHON_06:MME-05","text":"Reading necessarily becomes diagrammatic and translative: to interpret is to follow relations among codes, not to extract the sense of a single word or immobile figure.","refs":["CH_MHON_T_12:SD3","CH_MHON_T_13:ALT3","CH_MHON_T_14:ALT3"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[]}],"original_name":"Macro Map-Effects"},{"number":"14","name":"Altitude Integration","items":[{"id":"CL_MHON_06:ALT-B01","text":"At the level of the surface, the Sun, Moon, Taurus, Aries, and cross are explicitly arranged through dignities, angles, parts, notations, and letters.","refs":["CH_MHON_T_12:ALT1","CH_MHON_T_13:ALT1","CH_MHON_T_14:ALT1"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[]},{"id":"CL_MHON_06:ALT-B02","text":"At the structural level, relation, proximity, division, conversion of position, and composition are operations that generate meaning and number.","refs":["CH_MHON_T_12:ALT2","CH_MHON_T_13:ALT2","CH_MHON_T_14:ALT2"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[]},{"id":"CL_MHON_06:ALT-B03","text":"At the metastrategic level, interpretation does not seek a single code, but imposes the passage among disciplines as a condition of full knowledge.","refs":["CH_MHON_T_12:ALT3","CH_MHON_T_13:SD6","CH_MHON_T_14:ALT3"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[]},{"id":"CL_MHON_06:ALT-B04","text":"At the ontological-temporal level, reality is a transmutative order in which relations produce dignities, numbers emerge from figures, and unity returns from coordinated plurality.","refs":["CH_MHON_T_12:ALT4","CH_MHON_T_13:ALT4","CH_MHON_T_14:ALT4"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[]},{"id":"CL_MHON_06:ALT-B05","text":"At the methodological-performative level, the very arrangement of arguments effects what it asserts, because the text successively divides, converts, numbers, and reorders figures into unity.","refs":["CH_MHON_T_12:FGS7","CH_MHON_T_13:FGS7","CH_MHON_T_14:FGS6"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[]}],"original_name":"Altitude Integration"},{"number":"15","name":"Internal Tensions, Mutation Points, and Fault Lines","items":[{"id":"CL_MHON_06:FL-01","text":"The equality and inequality of the parts of the cross are simultaneously admitted, but the principal demonstration privileges the symmetry of similar parts; a tension remains between plurality of distributions and the norm of justice.","refs":["CH_MHON_T_13:A2","CH_MHON_T_13:A4","CH_MHON_T_13:MRT5"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[]},{"id":"CL_MHON_06:FL-02","text":"The passage from geometry to letter rests on symbolic congruences and ancient custom, not everywhere on fully demonstrated formal necessity.","refs":["CH_MHON_T_13:DNX6","CH_MHON_T_13:MRT4","CH_MHON_T_14:MRT6"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[]},{"id":"CL_MHON_06:FL-03","text":"The reduction of multiplied magnitudes to the centenary requires a circular structure of recursion, but the formal account of this return remains less explained.","refs":["CH_MHON_T_13:D7","CH_MHON_T_13:ME5","CH_MHON_T_13:MRT7"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[]},{"id":"CL_MHON_06:FL-04","text":"Closure in Light and the Word is more postulated than numerically demonstrated, because the nexus between the divided cross, letters, and the necessity of the masterful conclusion remains incomplete.","refs":["CH_MHON_T_14:D6","CH_MHON_T_14:MRT4","CH_MHON_T_14:MRT7"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[]},{"id":"CL_MHON_06:MP-01","text":"Exaltation changes from an astrological term into a general form of transmutation, by which a local relation receives interpretative dignity.","refs":["CH_MHON_T_12:D1","CH_MHON_T_12:SD2","CH_MHON_T_12:ALT2"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[]},{"id":"CL_MHON_06:MP-02","text":"The cross changes from a geometrical sign into an arithmetic operator when position, doubling, and squaring convert figures into numerical powers.","refs":["CH_MHON_T_13:D3","CH_MHON_T_13:SD1","CH_MHON_T_13:ME1"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[]},{"id":"CL_MHON_06:MP-03","text":"Unity changes from a singular character into a consensual effect, when denary power and plurality of letters are coordinated toward the Word.","refs":["CH_MHON_T_13:A13","CH_MHON_T_13:D8","CH_MHON_T_14:SD4"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[]}],"original_name":"Internal Tensions, Mutation Points, and Fault Lines"},{"number":"16","name":"Hidden System Image","items":[{"id":"CL_MHON_06:HSI-01","text":"The world is implicitly conceived as a network of operative figures, in which positions, angles, parts, and proximities are not accidents, but channels of ontological and semantic production.","refs":["CH_MHON_T_12:ME1","CH_MHON_T_13:A5","CH_MHON_T_14:ALT2"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[]},{"id":"CL_MHON_06:HSI-02","text":"Within this machine, the subject becomes an interpreter-initiate, whose action is not to discover conversions from nothing, but to reduce relations already distributed into a readable order.","refs":["CH_MHON_T_12:A12","CH_MHON_T_12:SD5","CH_MHON_T_13:ALT3"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[]},{"id":"CL_MHON_06:HSI-03","text":"Time is the time of passage and return: the figure remains the same enough to be recognized, but changes enough to acquire a new power.","refs":["CH_MHON_T_12:ALT4","CH_MHON_T_13:A7","CH_MHON_T_14:SD3"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[]},{"id":"CL_MHON_06:HSI-04","text":"The interpretative contest is not simply between a true and false sign, but between a reading that sustains conversions and a reading that closes the sign within one inert code.","refs":["CH_MHON_T_13:DNX1","CH_MHON_T_13:SNX2","CH_MHON_T_14:SNX1"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[]}],"original_name":"Hidden System Image"},{"number":"17","name":"Minimal Whole-Book Reconstruction Test","items":[{"id":"CL_MHON_06:MRT-B01","text":"The axioms concerning relation, operational figure, conversion of codes, and composite unity suffice to restore the principal machine of this group.","refs":["CH_MHON_T_12:SD1","CH_MHON_T_13:SD3","CH_MHON_T_14:MRT5"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[]},{"id":"CL_MHON_06:MRT-B02","text":"The firmest structure is where proximity, position, division, and composition are demonstrated as productive operations, not as explanatory ornaments.","refs":["CH_MHON_T_12:ALT2","CH_MHON_T_13:SD1","CH_MHON_T_14:ALT2"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[]},{"id":"CL_MHON_06:MRT-B03","text":"The necessity of the passage from geometrical analogy to letter and the nexus of the final calculations with the conclusion concerning Light and the Word remain less determined.","refs":["CH_MHON_T_13:MRT4","CH_MHON_T_14:MRT4","CH_MHON_T_14:MRT6"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[]},{"id":"CL_MHON_06:MRT-B04","text":"The coherence of the group is not the identity of local propositions, but the persistence of the rule by which the same sign returns through diverse conversions to a broader structure.","refs":["CH_MHON_T_12:SD3","CH_MHON_T_13:SD2","CH_MHON_T_14:SD2"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[]},{"id":"CL_MHON_06:MRT-B05","text":"The reconstruction preserves the true movement if astrology, geometry, arithmetic, and verbal theology are read as stages of conversion, not as separate chapters or parallel arguments.","refs":["CH_MHON_T_13:AS6","CH_MHON_T_13:MRT8","CH_MHON_T_14:MRT7"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[]}],"original_name":"Minimal Whole-Book Reconstruction Test"}],"revision":"CL_MHON_06@R001","source_variant_id":"canonical:CL_MHON_06:v1","source_language_code":"la","original_title":"Chapters 16–18"}],"identity":{"layer":"CL","kicker":"Cluster · Monas Hieroglyphica","title":"Chapters 16–18","context":"Theorem Xv. · Theorem Xvi. · Theorem Xvii.","original_title":"Chapters 16–18","original_context":"Theor. Xv. · Theor. Xvi. · Theor. Xvii."},"original_title":"Chapters 16–18","title_language_code":"en"},{"id":"CL_MHON_07","layer":"CL","title":"Chapters 19–21","order":7,"parents":["CH_MHON_T_15","CH_MHON_THER","CH_MHON_T_16"],"revision":"CL_MHON_07@R001","sha256":"0de241bf3f20851a6dbe16b397ad907b800bd1d8b921df01c4e45e1e26c731d0","sections":[{"number":"1","name":"Book-Scale Argumental Spine","items":[{"id":"CL_MHON_07:BAS-B01","text":"Quaestio centralis est quomodo Monas ordinem caelestem, constitutionem corpoream et discrimen numericum in una machina proportionis coniungat, quin gradus eorum confundantur.","refs":["CH_MHON_T_15:AS1","CH_MHON_THER:AS1","CH_MHON_T_16:AS1"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[]},{"id":"CL_MHON_07:BAS-B02","text":"Quaestio a simplici descriptione corporum transfertur ad condicionem qua influxus, figura et punctum vim recipiunt vel amittunt secundum situm structuralem.","refs":["CH_MHON_T_15:AS2","CH_MHON_THER:AS2","CH_MHON_T_16:AS4"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[]},{"id":"CL_MHON_07:BAS-B03","text":"Thesis gregis est Monadem non rem isolatam, sed regulam translationis exhibere, qua differentiae caelestes, materiae corporales et formae numerales in ordinem internum rediguntur.","refs":["CH_MHON_T_15:SD6","CH_MHON_THER:SD1","CH_MHON_T_16:SD1"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[]},{"id":"CL_MHON_07:BAS-B04","text":"Fundamentum argumenti ponit signum hieroglyphicum et effectum materialem non esse ordines separatos, quia figurae et influxus simul conditiones operationum naturalium atque artificialium constituunt.","refs":["CH_MHON_T_15:SD1","CH_MHON_THER:A7"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[]},{"id":"CL_MHON_07:BAS-B05","text":"Probatio non unico more procedit: visus Cabalisticus, effectus pyronomici, compositio materialis et demonstratio geometrica mutuo se sustinent sine in unam probationis formam redactis.","refs":["CH_MHON_T_15:SD5","CH_MHON_THER:ALT3"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[]},{"id":"CL_MHON_07:BAS-B06","text":"Argumentatio fit hieroglyphice analogica, causaliter distributiva et demonstrative discriminans: analogia transit inter campos, causalitas corpus sustentat, geometria retinendum et reiciendum separat.","refs":["CH_MHON_T_15:AS6","CH_MHON_T_16:AS2"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[]},{"id":"CL_MHON_07:BAS-B07","text":"Motus gregis non est linearis, sed spiralis: caelum in materiam descendit, materia per motum transformatur, et transformata proportio ad Monadem tamquam attractorem redit.","refs":["CH_MHON_T_15:MRT2","CH_MHON_T_16:SD3"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[]},{"id":"CL_MHON_07:BAS-B08","text":"Clausura in virtute et autarkia Monae consistit, sed non transitum causalitatis caelestis in materiam neque transitum a figura geometrica ad valorem spiritualem plene determinat.","refs":["CH_MHON_T_15:A17","CH_MHON_THER:SNX3","CH_MHON_T_16:MRT2"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[]}],"original_name":"Book-Scale Argumental Spine"},{"number":"2","name":"Global Load-Bearing Axioms","items":[{"id":"CL_MHON_07:GA-01","text":"[Attractor] Monas est centrum translationum quo astronomia, materia, fabula, ars et numerus non identificantur, sed ad proportionem communem ordinantur.","refs":["CH_MHON_T_15:SD6"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[]},{"id":"CL_MHON_07:GA-02","text":"[Persistent] Corpora caelestia in inferiora non solum significative sed corporaliter agunt, effectusque eorum in formis materialibus determinatis apparent.","refs":["CH_MHON_THER:A2","CH_MHON_THER:A7"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[]},{"id":"CL_MHON_07:GA-03","text":"[Mutating] Motus circularis vel ovalis, initio figura caelestis, fit principium coordinationis naturalis, artificialis et restitutivae.","refs":["CH_MHON_T_15:D2","CH_MHON_T_16:SD3"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[]},{"id":"CL_MHON_07:GA-04","text":"[Persistent] Proportio est regula qua compositio, identitas, inclusio et restitutio aestimantur.","refs":["CH_MHON_T_15:A6","CH_MHON_T_16:A10"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[]},{"id":"CL_MHON_07:GA-05","text":"[Mutating] Nullum elementum vim invariabilem retinet: punctum, motus et materia secundum configurationem possunt copulare, separare, conservare aut confundere.","refs":["CH_MHON_T_16:D2","CH_MHON_T_16:ME1"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[]},{"id":"CL_MHON_07:GA-06","text":"[Emergent] Corporeitas terrestris non est materia per se sufficiens, sed effectus sustentatus distributionis solaris et lunaris in principia ignea atque aquea.","refs":["CH_MHON_THER:SD2","CH_MHON_THER:NX2"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[]},{"id":"CL_MHON_07:GA-07","text":"[Terminal] Transformare non significat materiam annihilare, sed partes eius selective dissolvere, absorbere et in novam proportionem disponere.","refs":["CH_MHON_T_15:SD4","CH_MHON_T_16:SD2"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[]},{"id":"CL_MHON_07:GA-08","text":"[Attractor] Restitutio valida ad proportionem et materiam propriam redit nec perfectionem ex numeris vel principiis exterioribus recipit.","refs":["CH_MHON_T_16:A9","CH_MHON_T_16:A10"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[]},{"id":"CL_MHON_07:GA-09","text":"[Persistent] Cognitio systematis requirit interpretationem motuum, signorum, temporum et effectuum; sola inspectio immediata non sufficit.","refs":["CH_MHON_T_15:SD5","CH_MHON_THER:NX4"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[]},{"id":"CL_MHON_07:GA-10","text":"[Emergent] Integritas non ex retentione omnium datorum, sed ex discernimento structurali eorum quae unitatem constituunt et eorum quae proportionem obscurant oritur.","refs":["CH_MHON_T_16:SD2","CH_MHON_T_16:ME2"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[]}],"original_name":"Global Load-Bearing Axioms"},{"number":"3","name":"Recurrence, Mutation, and Displacement Matrix","items":[{"id":"CL_MHON_07:RMD-01","text":"Astronomia redit functio mutata: primum quasi Parens et Magistra hieroglyphica, deinde ut distributio causalis Solis et Lunae in corpora inferiora.","refs":["CH_MHON_T_15:A1","CH_MHON_THER:D1"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[]},{"id":"CL_MHON_07:RMD-02","text":"Circulatio ovalis non identice repetitur, sed e motu caelesti in volutationen Scarabei, revolutionem artificiosam et reditum proportionalem Monae mutatur.","refs":["CH_MHON_T_15:D2","CH_MHON_T_15:D6","CH_MHON_T_16:D5"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[]},{"id":"CL_MHON_07:RMD-03","text":"Ovum ut microstructura materialis non persistit ut obiectum universale, sed logica eius compositionis in corporeitatem elementatam et criterium proportionalitatis absorbetur.","refs":["CH_MHON_T_15:D3","CH_MHON_THER:D4","CH_MHON_T_16:ME4"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[]},{"id":"CL_MHON_07:RMD-04","text":"Certamen Aquilae et Scarabei ex narratione animalium in structuram generalem disruptionis, artis subtilis et purificationis transfertur.","refs":["CH_MHON_T_15:D4","CH_MHON_T_16:D4"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[]},{"id":"CL_MHON_07:RMD-05","text":"Punctum e medio necessario ternarii in elementum superfluum quaternarii invertitur; identitas materialis manet, functio structuralis mutatur.","refs":["CH_MHON_T_16:D2"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[]},{"id":"CL_MHON_07:RMD-06","text":"Destructio redit non ut terminus, sed ut transitus: disruptio ovi in absorptionem transit, remotio puncti in distinctionem, resectio in restitutionem.","refs":["CH_MHON_T_15:D7","CH_MHON_T_16:SD2"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[]},{"id":"CL_MHON_07:RMD-07","text":"Monas mutatur a tabula totalizante disciplinarum in operator clausurae qui ordinem internum servat et exteriora numeralia excludit.","refs":["CH_MHON_T_15:SD6","CH_MHON_T_16:SD3"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[]},{"id":"CL_MHON_07:RMD-08","text":"Cognitio redit e visu Cabalistico ad Analysim Pyronomicam; in utroque casu effectus vel figura non sunt data nuda, sed indices interpretatione ordinandi.","refs":["CH_MHON_T_15:NX1","CH_MHON_THER:ALT3"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[]}],"original_name":"Recurrence, Mutation, and Displacement Matrix"},{"number":"4","name":"Lower-Layer vs Current-Level Distinction","items":[{"id":"CL_MHON_07:CGD-01","text":"Aquila, Scarabeus, Iuppiter et Heliocanthari manent figurae locales; ad gradum gregalem non transferuntur ut agentes universales, sed ut casus temporis, conflictus et limitationis transformationis.","refs":["CH_MHON_T_15:A8","CH_MHON_T_15:A10","CH_MHON_T_15:A11"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[]},{"id":"CL_MHON_07:CGD-02","text":"Correspondentiae albuminis, vitelli et calcis manent codices materiales locales, sed earum functio superior fit demonstratio quod metamorphosis per compositionem ordinatam, non per mixtionem indeterminatam, operatur.","refs":["CH_MHON_T_15:A7","CH_MHON_T_15:A14","CH_MHON_T_15:NX6"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[]},{"id":"CL_MHON_07:CGD-03","text":"Distinctio Humoris Aquei et Liquoris Ignei non manet sola doctrina elementaris, sed amplificatur in axioma quod corpus terrestris originem causaliter distributam habet.","refs":["CH_MHON_THER:A4","CH_MHON_THER:A5","CH_MHON_THER:D5"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[]},{"id":"CL_MHON_07:CGD-04","text":"Lineae et punctum, localiter figura geometrica, ad gradum praesentem fiunt grammatica functionalis qua necessitas, superfluitas, unitas et separatio discernuntur.","refs":["CH_MHON_T_16:A1","CH_MHON_T_16:D1","CH_MHON_T_16:ME1"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[]},{"id":"CL_MHON_07:CGD-05","text":"Auctoritas philosophorum antiquorum et Anaxagorae manet testimonium locale; non sustinet axioma gregale nisi recurrens proportio, motus et translatio inter ordines eam formaliter excipiant.","refs":["CH_MHON_T_15:A15","CH_MHON_T_15:MRT7"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[]},{"id":"CL_MHON_07:CGD-06","text":"Structura proprie gregalis nascitur ubi causalitas caelestis, ars metamorphica et purificatio numeralis sub eodem criterio proportionis et distinctionis coordinantur.","refs":["CH_MHON_T_15:SD2","CH_MHON_T_16:SD2","CH_MHON_T_16:ME4"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[]}],"original_name":"Lower-Layer vs Current-Level Distinction"},{"number":"5","name":"Global Apophatic Structure","items":[{"id":"CL_MHON_07:GNX-01","text":"Systema excludit corpus terrestre autonomum, quod sine influxu caelesti aut sine distributione elementari sufficienter intelligi posset.","refs":["CH_MHON_THER:NX2","CH_MHON_THER:DNX1"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"Negative","passages":[]},{"id":"CL_MHON_07:GNX-02","text":"Systema excludit sermonem neutralem de natura, in quo figura, signum, interpretatio et effectus ab invicem seiungi possint.","refs":["CH_MHON_T_15:NX1","CH_MHON_T_15:SNX2"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"Negative","passages":[]},{"id":"CL_MHON_07:GNX-03","text":"Systema excludit aequalitatem causarum caelestium et identitatem functionalem elementorum geometricorum.","refs":["CH_MHON_THER:NX1","CH_MHON_T_16:DNX1"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"Negative","passages":[]},{"id":"CL_MHON_07:GNX-04","text":"Systema excludit et destructionem terminalem et plenitudinem indiscriminatam, quia purificatio requirit resectionem sine perdita proportione propria.","refs":["CH_MHON_T_15:DNX5","CH_MHON_T_16:SNX2"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"Negative","passages":[]},{"id":"CL_MHON_07:GNX-05","text":"Systema excludit fundamentum extrinsecum Monae, sive sub specie numeri additi sive sub specie principii exterioris.","refs":["CH_MHON_T_16:NX5","CH_MHON_T_16:SNX3"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"Negative","passages":[]}],"original_name":"Global Apophatic Structure"},{"number":"6","name":"Deep Book Apophatics","items":[{"id":"CL_MHON_07:DBN-01","text":"Falsum esse debet signum hieroglyphicum esse ornamentum super materiam autonomam; aliter mediatio inter caelum, naturam et artem dissolveretur.","refs":["CH_MHON_T_15:SD1","CH_MHON_THER:DNX2"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"Negative","passages":[]},{"id":"CL_MHON_07:DBN-02","text":"Falsum esse debet eandem rem eandem vim in omni configuratione servare; mutatio situs est condicio ipsius intelligibilitatis structuralis.","refs":["CH_MHON_T_16:D2","CH_MHON_T_16:DNX1"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"Negative","passages":[]},{"id":"CL_MHON_07:DBN-03","text":"Falsum esse debet integritatem coincidere cum conservatione omnium partium; systema perdet criterium purificationis, si nullum superfluum esse possit.","refs":["CH_MHON_T_16:SD2","CH_MHON_T_16:SNX2"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"Negative","passages":[]},{"id":"CL_MHON_07:DBN-04","text":"Falsum esse debet ordines caelestes, materiales et numerales immediate se contingere sine medio formali, causali vel copulativo.","refs":["CH_MHON_THER:SNX1","CH_MHON_T_16:SNX1"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"Negative","passages":[]}],"original_name":"Deep Book Apophatics"},{"number":"7","name":"Super-Apophatics of the Whole","items":[{"id":"CL_MHON_07:BSA-01","text":"Possibilitas exterioris principii quod Monadem perficiat, dirigat aut compleat indisponibilis est; perfectio solum per numeros et proportionem proprios admittitur.","refs":["CH_MHON_T_16:A9","CH_MHON_T_16:SNX3"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"Negative","passages":[]},{"id":"CL_MHON_07:BSA-02","text":"Eventus naturalis omnino fortuitus, nulli figurae, proportionis aut influxui obnoxius, systemati ontologice inadmissibilis fit.","refs":["CH_MHON_T_15:SNX3"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"Negative","passages":[]},{"id":"CL_MHON_07:BSA-03","text":"Abolitio totalis ordinis vel materiae prohibetur: imminutio potest esse restitutiva, sed deletio sine residuo nullum iter ad proportionem relinqueret.","refs":["CH_MHON_T_15:SNX4","CH_MHON_T_16:DNX3"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"Negative","passages":[]},{"id":"CL_MHON_07:BSA-04","text":"Nexus omnino immediatus et plane perspicuus inter caelum et materiam non datur; systema medium requirit, sed mechanismum transmutationis non exhaurit.","refs":["CH_MHON_THER:SNX3","CH_MHON_T_16:SNX1"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"Tension","passages":[]}],"original_name":"Super-Apophatics of the Whole"},{"number":"8","name":"Cross-Unit Derivative Propositions","items":[{"id":"CL_MHON_07:XD-01","text":"Signum hieroglyphicum hoc gradu non potest esse symbolum separatum, quia fit interfacies qua differentia caelestis in effectum corporalem et formam operationis traducitur.","refs":["CL_MHON_07:GA-01","CL_MHON_07:GA-02","CL_MHON_07:GA-09","CH_MHON_T_15:SD1","CH_MHON_THER:SD3"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"Composite","passages":[]},{"id":"CL_MHON_07:XD-02","text":"Circulatio efficit ut forma superior in operationem materialem descendat: revolutio non imitatur caelum decorative, sed materiam ad metamorphosin et restitutionem disponit.","refs":["CL_MHON_07:GA-03","CL_MHON_07:GA-04","CL_MHON_07:GA-07","CH_MHON_T_15:D6","CH_MHON_T_15:A14","CH_MHON_T_16:D5"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"Composite","passages":[]},{"id":"CL_MHON_07:XD-03","text":"Subtractio structuralis eandem legem cum disruptionis metamorphicae processu participat: imminutio est generativa ubi distinctionem servat et proportionem restituit.","refs":["CL_MHON_07:GA-05","CL_MHON_07:GA-07","CL_MHON_07:GA-10","CH_MHON_T_15:D7","CH_MHON_T_16:D3"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"Composite","passages":[]},{"id":"CL_MHON_07:XD-04","text":"Duo termini per se non sufficiunt: corpus mortale sustentationem ex distributione Solis et Lunae requirit, binarium linearum punctum copulativum requirit.","refs":["CL_MHON_07:GA-05","CL_MHON_07:GA-06","CH_MHON_THER:SD2","CH_MHON_T_16:D1"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"Composite","passages":[]},{"id":"CL_MHON_07:XD-05","text":"Visus Cabalisticus et Analysis Pyronomica complentur sine plena reductione alterius ad alterum: figurae cognitionem dirigunt, effectus causalitatem attestantur.","refs":["CL_MHON_07:GA-09","CH_MHON_T_15:NX1","CH_MHON_THER:ALT3"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"Tension","passages":[]},{"id":"CL_MHON_07:XD-06","text":"Ars efficax non introducit principium extrinsecum, sed materiam iam datam per motum, temperationem et proportionem internam reconfigurat.","refs":["CL_MHON_07:GA-04","CL_MHON_07:GA-08","CH_MHON_T_15:SD2","CH_MHON_T_16:NX5"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"Composite","passages":[]}],"original_name":"Cross-Unit Derivative Propositions"},{"number":"9","name":"Book-Scale Super-Derivatives","items":[{"id":"CL_MHON_07:BSD-01","text":"Realitas materialis describitur ut status dependens intra circuitum caelestis influxus, compositionis elementaris, motus artificiosi et restitutionis proportionalis.","refs":["CH_MHON_THER:SD4","CH_MHON_T_16:SD3"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[]},{"id":"CL_MHON_07:BSD-02","text":"Causalitas non est simplex descensus a superiori ad inferius, sed distributio transscalaris in qua figurae ordinant, influxus sustentant et operationes transformant.","refs":["CH_MHON_T_15:SD1","CH_MHON_THER:A7","CH_MHON_T_16:SD1"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[]},{"id":"CL_MHON_07:BSD-03","text":"Tempus fit series curriculorum et redituum: tempora animalium, circulatio motuum et restitutio Monae unam temporalitatem metamorphicam constituunt.","refs":["CH_MHON_T_15:A11","CH_MHON_T_16:D5"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[]},{"id":"CL_MHON_07:BSD-04","text":"Potentia agendi non in dominatione materiae ex nihilo consistit, sed in cognitione temporis, motus, proportionis et eorum quae resecanda sunt.","refs":["CH_MHON_T_15:D5","CH_MHON_T_16:D4"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[]},{"id":"CL_MHON_07:BSD-05","text":"Purificatio reclassificatur ut forma generationis: quod superfluum removetur non simpliciter perit, sed spatium distinctioni et materiae propriae restituit.","refs":["CH_MHON_T_15:SD4","CH_MHON_T_16:SD2"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[]},{"id":"CL_MHON_07:BSD-06","text":"Veritas huius systematis ex concordia interpretativa et efficacia causali constituitur, non ex una forma demonstrationis abstracte separata.","refs":["CH_MHON_T_15:SD5","CH_MHON_THER:MRT4"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[]}],"original_name":"Book-Scale Super-Derivatives"},{"number":"10","name":"Recursive Loops, Escalation Paths, and Conceptual Attractors","items":[{"id":"CL_MHON_07:RLE-01","text":"Caelum → cursus ovalis → motus Scarabei → volutatio artificiosa → metamorphosis ovi constituit circuitum quo figura superior in materiam operantem redit.","refs":["CH_MHON_T_15:D2","CH_MHON_T_15:D6","CH_MHON_T_15:A13"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[]},{"id":"CL_MHON_07:RLE-02","text":"Influxus caelestis → effectus corporeus → Analysis Pyronomica → confirmatio ordinis causalitatis caelestis constituit circuitum amplificationis inter causam positam et effectum observatum.","refs":["CH_MHON_THER:A2","CH_MHON_THER:FGS4"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[]},{"id":"CL_MHON_07:RLE-03","text":"Binarium → punctum copulativum → ternarium → remotio puncti → quaternarium → purificatio exponit circuitum in quo unitas et distinctio alternatim producuntur.","refs":["CH_MHON_T_16:FGS1","CH_MHON_T_16:FGS2"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[]},{"id":"CL_MHON_07:RLE-04","text":"Disruptio → absorptio → purificatio → restitutio ostendit quod terminus non clausura est, sed operator novae compositionis.","refs":["CH_MHON_T_15:D7","CH_MHON_T_16:SD2"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[]},{"id":"CL_MHON_07:RLE-05","text":"Proportio operatur ut attractor distributus: motus, corpora, puncta et materia ad eam referuntur ut retentio et resectio diiudicentur.","refs":["CH_MHON_T_16:A10","CH_MHON_T_16:ME4"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[]}],"original_name":"Recursive Loops, Escalation Paths, and Conceptual Attractors"},{"number":"11","name":"Formal Generative Schema of the Whole Book","items":[{"id":"CL_MHON_07:FGS-B01","text":"Inaequalitas caelestis → praecipua vis Solis et Lunae → distributio aquei et ignei → corporeitas terrestris dependens.","refs":["CH_MHON_THER:FGS1","CH_MHON_THER:FGS3"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[]},{"id":"CL_MHON_07:FGS-B02","text":"Figura caelestis → cursus ovalis → proportio Naturae → translatio hieroglyphica inter caelum, corpus et artem.","refs":["CH_MHON_T_15:FGS2","CH_MHON_T_15:D2"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[]},{"id":"CL_MHON_07:FGS-B03","text":"Proportio materialis → ovum compositum → certamen animalium → schema operationis artificiosae.","refs":["CH_MHON_T_15:FGS3","CH_MHON_T_15:D4","CH_MHON_T_15:FGS6"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[]},{"id":"CL_MHON_07:FGS-B04","text":"Disruptio → temperatio → volutatio et revolutio → absorptio albuminis → metamorphosis ordinata.","refs":["CH_MHON_T_15:FGS6","CH_MHON_T_15:FGS7"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[]},{"id":"CL_MHON_07:FGS-B05","text":"Examen configurationis → distinctio puncti necessarii et superflui → conservatio unitatis constitutivae → purificatio distinctionis.","refs":["CH_MHON_T_16:FGS2","CH_MHON_T_16:D4"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[]},{"id":"CL_MHON_07:FGS-B06","text":"Proportio interna → resectio externorum et confusivorum → reditus ad materiam propriam → clausura Monae.","refs":["CH_MHON_T_16:FGS3","CH_MHON_T_16:FGS4"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[]}],"original_name":"Formal Generative Schema of the Whole Book"},{"number":"12","name":"Whole-Book Argument Flow Map","items":[{"id":"CL_MHON_07:AFM-B01","text":"Astronomia caelestis → visus Cabalisticus → anatomia Monae in Natura et Vita.","refs":["CH_MHON_T_15:FGS1","CH_MHON_T_15:A2"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[]},{"id":"CL_MHON_07:AFM-B02","text":"Motus caelestis → figura ovalis → proportio naturalis → figurae hieroglyphicae.","refs":["CH_MHON_T_15:FGS2","CH_MHON_T_15:A6"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[]},{"id":"CL_MHON_07:AFM-B03","text":"Proportio Naturae → compositio ovi → disruptio → metamorphosis artificialis.","refs":["CH_MHON_T_15:FGS3","CH_MHON_T_15:FGS4","CH_MHON_T_15:FGS6"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[]},{"id":"CL_MHON_07:AFM-B04","text":"Inaequalitas caelestis → distributio Solis et Lunae → Humor Aqueus et Liquor Igneus → sustentatio corporeitatis terrestris.","refs":["CH_MHON_THER:FGS1","CH_MHON_THER:FGS2","CH_MHON_THER:FGS3"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[]},{"id":"CL_MHON_07:AFM-B05","text":"Binarium → punctum necessario copulativum → quaternarium distinctum → purificatio → restitutio Monae ad materiam propriam.","refs":["CH_MHON_T_16:FGS1","CH_MHON_T_16:FGS3"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[]}],"original_name":"Whole-Book Argument Flow Map"},{"number":"13","name":"Macro Map-Effects","items":[{"id":"CL_MHON_07:MME-01","text":"Caelum, natura, ars et geometria reclassificantur ut ordines medii inter se transferibiles, non ut regiones causaliter segregatae.","refs":["CH_MHON_T_15:ME1","CH_MHON_T_15:SD6"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[]},{"id":"CL_MHON_07:MME-02","text":"Corpus inferius reclassificatur ex materia passiva in nodum influxuum, elementorum, proportionum et operationum metamorphicarum.","refs":["CH_MHON_THER:ME1","CH_MHON_THER:D5"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[]},{"id":"CL_MHON_07:MME-03","text":"Oppositio inter naturale et artificiale corruit, non quia ars naturae aequatur simpliciter, sed quia ars motum naturalem internaliter imitans transformationem producit.","refs":["CH_MHON_T_15:ME3","CH_MHON_T_15:SD2"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[]},{"id":"CL_MHON_07:MME-04","text":"Oppositio inter generationem et destructionem mutatur: disruptio, subtractio et purificatio fiunt modi differentiatae productionis.","refs":["CH_MHON_T_15:ME4","CH_MHON_T_16:ME2"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[]},{"id":"CL_MHON_07:MME-05","text":"Additio et subtractio desinunt esse operationes quantitative neutrae; earum valor pendet a munere quod in configuratione unitatis obtinent.","refs":["CH_MHON_T_16:SD1","CH_MHON_T_16:ME1"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[]}],"original_name":"Macro Map-Effects"},{"number":"14","name":"Altitude Integration","items":[{"id":"CL_MHON_07:ALT-B01","text":"[Surface] Sol, Luna, ovum, Aquila, Scarabeus, lineae et punctum exhibentur ut obiecta immediata per quae systema suas relationes explicat.","refs":["CH_MHON_T_15:ALT1","CH_MHON_THER:ALT1","CH_MHON_T_16:ALT1"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[]},{"id":"CL_MHON_07:ALT-B02","text":"[Structural] Influxus, proportio, circulatio, compositio, discontinuatio et purificatio constituunt machinam recurrentem quae obiecta diversa eodem ordine disponit.","refs":["CH_MHON_T_15:ALT2","CH_MHON_THER:ALT2","CH_MHON_T_16:ALT2"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[]},{"id":"CL_MHON_07:ALT-B03","text":"[Metastrategic] Interpretatio hieroglyphica et Analysis Pyronomica rematerializant lectionem: signum, effectus et figura fiunt instrumenta discriminis et responsionis.","refs":["CH_MHON_T_15:ALT3","CH_MHON_THER:ALT3","CH_MHON_T_16:ALT3"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[]},{"id":"CL_MHON_07:ALT-B04","text":"[Ontological/Temporal] Realitas ordinatur per circuitus caelestes, sustentationes elementares, mutationes materiales et reditus proportionales potius quam per substantias autonomas.","refs":["CH_MHON_T_15:ALT4","CH_MHON_THER:ALT4","CH_MHON_T_16:ALT4"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[]},{"id":"CL_MHON_07:ALT-B05","text":"[Methodological/Performative] Forma argumenti assertiones suas efficit, quia ipsa transit inter fabulam, operationem, effectum et figuram, eosque transitus pro conditionibus cognitionis imponit.","refs":["CH_MHON_T_15:AS6","CH_MHON_THER:ALT3","CH_MHON_T_16:ALT3"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[]}],"original_name":"Altitude Integration"},{"number":"15","name":"Internal Tensions, Mutation Points, and Fault Lines","items":[{"id":"CL_MHON_07:FL-01","text":"Causalitas caelestis affirmatur et per effectus defenditur, sed mechanismus quo influxus caelestes in materiam terrestrem transmutantur non plene declaratur.","refs":["CH_MHON_THER:SNX3","CH_MHON_THER:MRT3"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"Tension","passages":[]},{"id":"CL_MHON_07:FL-02","text":"Concordantia hieroglyphica et demonstratio pyronomica diversa genera probationis retinent; nulla regula communis explicatur qua analogia et effectus in eandem necessitatem redigantur.","refs":["CH_MHON_T_15:MRT3","CH_MHON_THER:MRT5"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"Tension","passages":[]},{"id":"CL_MHON_07:FL-03","text":"Mutatio puncti ex necessario in superfluum principalis est operator systematis, sed criterium quo configuratio hanc inversionem legitimat ultra figuram ipsam non plene determinatur.","refs":["CH_MHON_T_16:D2","CH_MHON_T_16:MRT3"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"Tension","passages":[]},{"id":"CL_MHON_07:FL-04","text":"Monas sibi sufficiens dicitur, sed expansio et restitutio simul artificis industriae committuntur; autarkia et operatio externa non omnino conciliantur.","refs":["CH_MHON_T_16:A9","CH_MHON_T_16:MRT5"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"Tension","passages":[]},{"id":"CL_MHON_07:MP-01","text":"Figura caelestis mutatur in motum artis ubi circulatio ovalis per volutationen Scarabei et revolutionem operationis materialiter efficax fit.","refs":["CH_MHON_T_15:D2","CH_MHON_T_15:D6"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"Tension","passages":[]},{"id":"CL_MHON_07:MP-02","text":"Destructio mutatur in restitutionem ubi ovum disruptum non extinguitur, sed albumen in liquorem vitellinum absorptum aliam compositionem parit.","refs":["CH_MHON_T_15:D7","CH_MHON_T_15:A14"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"Tension","passages":[]},{"id":"CL_MHON_07:MP-03","text":"Numerus mutatur in iudicium qualitativum ubi punctum non quantitate, sed munere copulativo aut confusivo aestimatur.","refs":["CH_MHON_T_16:SD1","CH_MHON_T_16:ME1"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"Tension","passages":[]}],"original_name":"Internal Tensions, Mutation Points, and Fault Lines"},{"number":"16","name":"Hidden System Image","items":[{"id":"CL_MHON_07:HSI-01","text":"Realitas implicite fingitur machina transductionum, in qua influxus caelestes distribuuntur, figurae eos ordinant, materia eos suscipit, et operatio eos reconfigurat.","refs":["CH_MHON_T_15:SD1","CH_MHON_THER:A2","CH_MHON_T_15:SD2"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[]},{"id":"CL_MHON_07:HSI-02","text":"Subiectum non est spectator autonomus, sed interpres-operator qui tempora, motus, effectus et superfluitates discernere debet.","refs":["CH_MHON_T_15:D5","CH_MHON_T_15:SD5","CH_MHON_T_16:D4"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[]},{"id":"CL_MHON_07:HSI-03","text":"Tempus non est simplex successio, sed circulus sustentationis, latitationis, disruptionis et reditus proportionalis.","refs":["CH_MHON_T_15:A11","CH_MHON_T_15:D6","CH_MHON_T_16:D5"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[]},{"id":"CL_MHON_07:HSI-04","text":"Certamen non per abolitionem adversarii definitur, sed per navigationem inter iniuriam, limitationem, transformationem et conservationem residui proportionati.","refs":["CH_MHON_T_15:D4","CH_MHON_T_15:MRT6","CH_MHON_T_16:SD2"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[]}],"original_name":"Hidden System Image"},{"number":"17","name":"Minimal Whole-Book Reconstruction Test","items":[{"id":"CL_MHON_07:MRT-B01","text":"Axiomata praesentia sufficiunt ad restituendam machinam gregalem: influxus caelestis, proportio, motus, metamorphosis, purificatio et reditus Monadicus.","refs":["CH_MHON_T_15:MRT1","CH_MHON_T_16:MRT1"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[]},{"id":"CL_MHON_07:MRT-B02","text":"Structura firmissima est ubi eadem relatio formaliter recurrit inter circulum caelestem, motum artificiosum, reditum proportionalem et discrimen puncti.","refs":["CH_MHON_T_15:MRT2","CH_MHON_T_16:D5","CH_MHON_T_16:ME1"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[]},{"id":"CL_MHON_07:MRT-B03","text":"Insufficienter determinata manent conversio influxus in corpus, ratio correspondentiarum hieroglyphicarum, et transitus a geometria ad iudicium spirituale.","refs":["CH_MHON_T_15:MRT4","CH_MHON_THER:SNX3","CH_MHON_T_16:MRT2"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[]},{"id":"CL_MHON_07:MRT-B04","text":"Synthesis cohaeret ut matrix generativa, non ut demonstratio univoca: vis eius pendet a recurrenti translatione proportionis inter ordines heterogeneos.","refs":["CH_MHON_T_15:MRT5","CH_MHON_THER:MRT6","CH_MHON_T_16:MRT3"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[]}],"original_name":"Minimal Whole-Book Reconstruction Test"}],"language_code":"la","language_name":"Latina","translations":[{"id":"CAREVAR_20261006T223410394680Z_2e44b3be","language_code":"en","language_name":"English","title":"Chapters 19–21","source_sha256":"0de241bf3f20851a6dbe16b397ad907b800bd1d8b921df01c4e45e1e26c731d0","sha256":"bc4ff4a1c9464219a06e37b32bc3b5a40f2ed9f3b29639392a339a8388ff3142","rendition_type":"machine","review_status":"machine","model":"gpt-5.6-terra","created":"2026-10-06T22:34:10.396668+00:00","sections":[{"number":"1","name":"Book-Scale Argumental Spine","items":[{"id":"CL_MHON_07:BAS-B01","text":"The central question is how the Monad joins celestial order, corporeal constitution, and numerical distinction in one machine of proportion, without confusing their levels.","refs":["CH_MHON_T_15:AS1","CH_MHON_THER:AS1","CH_MHON_T_16:AS1"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[]},{"id":"CL_MHON_07:BAS-B02","text":"The question shifts from a simple description of bodies to the condition under which influence, figure, and point receive or lose force according to structural position.","refs":["CH_MHON_T_15:AS2","CH_MHON_THER:AS2","CH_MHON_T_16:AS4"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[]},{"id":"CL_MHON_07:BAS-B03","text":"The thesis of the group is that the Monad presents not an isolated thing, but a rule of translation, by which celestial differences, corporeal materials, and numerical forms are reduced to an internal order.","refs":["CH_MHON_T_15:SD6","CH_MHON_THER:SD1","CH_MHON_T_16:SD1"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[]},{"id":"CL_MHON_07:BAS-B04","text":"The foundation of the argument holds that the hieroglyphic sign and material effect are not separate orders, because figures and influences together constitute the conditions of natural and artificial operations.","refs":["CH_MHON_T_15:SD1","CH_MHON_THER:A7"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[]},{"id":"CL_MHON_07:BAS-B05","text":"The proof does not proceed in a single manner: Cabalistic vision, pyronomic effects, material composition, and geometric demonstration mutually support one another without being reduced to one form of proof.","refs":["CH_MHON_T_15:SD5","CH_MHON_THER:ALT3"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[]},{"id":"CL_MHON_07:BAS-B06","text":"The argumentation is hieroglyphically analogical, causally distributive, and demonstratively discriminating: analogy passes between fields, causality sustains the body, geometry separates what is to be retained and rejected.","refs":["CH_MHON_T_15:AS6","CH_MHON_T_16:AS2"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[]},{"id":"CL_MHON_07:BAS-B07","text":"The movement of the group is not linear but spiral: heaven descends into matter, matter is transformed through motion, and transformed proportion returns to the Monad as to an attractor.","refs":["CH_MHON_T_15:MRT2","CH_MHON_T_16:SD3"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[]},{"id":"CL_MHON_07:BAS-B08","text":"The closure consists in the virtue and autarky of the Monad, but it does not fully determine either the passage of celestial causality into matter or the passage from geometric figure to spiritual value.","refs":["CH_MHON_T_15:A17","CH_MHON_THER:SNX3","CH_MHON_T_16:MRT2"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[]}],"original_name":"Book-Scale Argumental Spine"},{"number":"2","name":"Global Load-Bearing Axioms","items":[{"id":"CL_MHON_07:GA-01","text":"[Attractor] The Monad is the center of translations by which astronomy, matter, fable, art, and number are not identified, but ordered to a common proportion.","refs":["CH_MHON_T_15:SD6"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[]},{"id":"CL_MHON_07:GA-02","text":"[Persistent] Celestial bodies act upon inferior things not only significatively but corporeally, and their effects appear in determinate material forms.","refs":["CH_MHON_THER:A2","CH_MHON_THER:A7"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[]},{"id":"CL_MHON_07:GA-03","text":"[Mutating] Circular or oval motion, initially a celestial figure, becomes a principle of natural, artificial, and restitutive coordination.","refs":["CH_MHON_T_15:D2","CH_MHON_T_16:SD3"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[]},{"id":"CL_MHON_07:GA-04","text":"[Persistent] Proportion is the rule by which composition, identity, inclusion, and restitution are assessed.","refs":["CH_MHON_T_15:A6","CH_MHON_T_16:A10"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[]},{"id":"CL_MHON_07:GA-05","text":"[Mutating] No element retains invariable force: point, motion, and matter can join, separate, preserve, or confuse according to configuration.","refs":["CH_MHON_T_16:D2","CH_MHON_T_16:ME1"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[]},{"id":"CL_MHON_07:GA-06","text":"[Emergent] Terrestrial corporeality is not self-sufficient matter, but the sustained effect of solar and lunar distribution into fiery and aqueous principles.","refs":["CH_MHON_THER:SD2","CH_MHON_THER:NX2"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[]},{"id":"CL_MHON_07:GA-07","text":"[Terminal] To transform does not mean to annihilate matter, but selectively to dissolve, absorb, and dispose its parts into a new proportion.","refs":["CH_MHON_T_15:SD4","CH_MHON_T_16:SD2"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[]},{"id":"CL_MHON_07:GA-08","text":"[Attractor] Valid restitution returns to proportion and proper matter, and does not receive perfection from numbers or external principles.","refs":["CH_MHON_T_16:A9","CH_MHON_T_16:A10"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[]},{"id":"CL_MHON_07:GA-09","text":"[Persistent] Knowledge of the system requires interpretation of motions, signs, times, and effects; immediate inspection alone does not suffice.","refs":["CH_MHON_T_15:SD5","CH_MHON_THER:NX4"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[]},{"id":"CL_MHON_07:GA-10","text":"[Emergent] Integrity arises not from retaining all given elements, but from structurally discerning those that constitute unity from those that obscure proportion.","refs":["CH_MHON_T_16:SD2","CH_MHON_T_16:ME2"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[]}],"original_name":"Global Load-Bearing Axioms"},{"number":"3","name":"Recurrence, Mutation, and Displacement Matrix","items":[{"id":"CL_MHON_07:RMD-01","text":"Astronomy returns with a changed function: first as hieroglyphic Parent and Teacher, then as the causal distribution of the Sun and Moon into inferior bodies.","refs":["CH_MHON_T_15:A1","CH_MHON_THER:D1"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[]},{"id":"CL_MHON_07:RMD-02","text":"Oval circulation is not repeated identically, but is transformed from celestial motion into the rolling of the Scarab, artificial revolution, and the proportional return of the Monad.","refs":["CH_MHON_T_15:D2","CH_MHON_T_15:D6","CH_MHON_T_16:D5"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[]},{"id":"CL_MHON_07:RMD-03","text":"The egg as a material microstructure does not persist as a universal object, but the logic of its composition is absorbed into elemental corporeality and the criterion of proportionality.","refs":["CH_MHON_T_15:D3","CH_MHON_THER:D4","CH_MHON_T_16:ME4"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[]},{"id":"CL_MHON_07:RMD-04","text":"The contest of the Eagle and the Scarab is transferred from an animal narrative into a general structure of disruption, subtle art, and purification.","refs":["CH_MHON_T_15:D4","CH_MHON_T_16:D4"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[]},{"id":"CL_MHON_07:RMD-05","text":"The point is inverted from the necessary middle of the ternary into a superfluous element of the quaternary; material identity remains, structural function changes.","refs":["CH_MHON_T_16:D2"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[]},{"id":"CL_MHON_07:RMD-06","text":"Destruction returns not as an end, but as a transition: disruption of the egg passes into absorption, removal of the point into distinction, resection into restitution.","refs":["CH_MHON_T_15:D7","CH_MHON_T_16:SD2"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[]},{"id":"CL_MHON_07:RMD-07","text":"The Monad changes from a totalizing table of disciplines into an operator of closure that preserves internal order and excludes external numerical elements.","refs":["CH_MHON_T_15:SD6","CH_MHON_T_16:SD3"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[]},{"id":"CL_MHON_07:RMD-08","text":"Knowledge returns from Cabalistic vision to Analysis Pyronomica; in both cases effects or figures are not bare data, but indicators to be ordered by interpretation.","refs":["CH_MHON_T_15:NX1","CH_MHON_THER:ALT3"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[]}],"original_name":"Recurrence, Mutation, and Displacement Matrix"},{"number":"4","name":"Lower-Layer vs Current-Level Distinction","items":[{"id":"CL_MHON_07:CGD-01","text":"The Eagle, Scarab, Jupiter, and Heliocanthari remain local figures; they are not transferred to the group level as universal agents, but as cases of time, conflict, and the limitation of transformation.","refs":["CH_MHON_T_15:A8","CH_MHON_T_15:A10","CH_MHON_T_15:A11"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[]},{"id":"CL_MHON_07:CGD-02","text":"The correspondences of albumen, yolk, and shell remain local material codes, but their higher function becomes a demonstration that metamorphosis operates through ordered composition, not through indeterminate mixture.","refs":["CH_MHON_T_15:A7","CH_MHON_T_15:A14","CH_MHON_T_15:NX6"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[]},{"id":"CL_MHON_07:CGD-03","text":"The distinction between Humor Aqueus and Liquor Igneus does not remain merely elementary doctrine, but is amplified into the axiom that the terrestrial body has a causally distributed origin.","refs":["CH_MHON_THER:A4","CH_MHON_THER:A5","CH_MHON_THER:D5"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[]},{"id":"CL_MHON_07:CGD-04","text":"Lines and point, locally a geometric figure, become at the present level a functional grammar by which necessity, superfluity, unity, and separation are discerned.","refs":["CH_MHON_T_16:A1","CH_MHON_T_16:D1","CH_MHON_T_16:ME1"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[]},{"id":"CL_MHON_07:CGD-05","text":"The authority of ancient philosophers and Anaxagoras remains local testimony; it does not sustain a group axiom unless recurrent proportion, motion, and translation between orders formally receive it.","refs":["CH_MHON_T_15:A15","CH_MHON_T_15:MRT7"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[]},{"id":"CL_MHON_07:CGD-06","text":"The properly group-scale structure arises where celestial causality, metamorphic art, and numerical purification are coordinated under the same criterion of proportion and distinction.","refs":["CH_MHON_T_15:SD2","CH_MHON_T_16:SD2","CH_MHON_T_16:ME4"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[]}],"original_name":"Lower-Layer vs Current-Level Distinction"},{"number":"5","name":"Global Apophatic Structure","items":[{"id":"CL_MHON_07:GNX-01","text":"The system excludes an autonomous terrestrial body that could be sufficiently understood without celestial influence or elemental distribution.","refs":["CH_MHON_THER:NX2","CH_MHON_THER:DNX1"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"Negative","passages":[]},{"id":"CL_MHON_07:GNX-02","text":"The system excludes a neutral discourse on nature in which figure, sign, interpretation, and effect can be separated from one another.","refs":["CH_MHON_T_15:NX1","CH_MHON_T_15:SNX2"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"Negative","passages":[]},{"id":"CL_MHON_07:GNX-03","text":"The system excludes the equality of celestial causes and the functional identity of geometric elements.","refs":["CH_MHON_THER:NX1","CH_MHON_T_16:DNX1"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"Negative","passages":[]},{"id":"CL_MHON_07:GNX-04","text":"The system excludes both terminal destruction and indiscriminate fullness, because purification requires resection without loss of proper proportion.","refs":["CH_MHON_T_15:DNX5","CH_MHON_T_16:SNX2"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"Negative","passages":[]},{"id":"CL_MHON_07:GNX-05","text":"The system excludes an extrinsic foundation of the Monad, whether under the form of an added number or under the form of an external principle.","refs":["CH_MHON_T_16:NX5","CH_MHON_T_16:SNX3"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"Negative","passages":[]}],"original_name":"Global Apophatic Structure"},{"number":"6","name":"Deep Book Apophatics","items":[{"id":"CL_MHON_07:DBN-01","text":"It must be false that the hieroglyphic sign is an ornament upon autonomous matter; otherwise mediation between heaven, nature, and art would dissolve.","refs":["CH_MHON_T_15:SD1","CH_MHON_THER:DNX2"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"Negative","passages":[]},{"id":"CL_MHON_07:DBN-02","text":"It must be false that the same thing preserves the same force in every configuration; change of position is the condition of structural intelligibility itself.","refs":["CH_MHON_T_16:D2","CH_MHON_T_16:DNX1"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"Negative","passages":[]},{"id":"CL_MHON_07:DBN-03","text":"It must be false that integrity coincides with the preservation of all parts; the system would lose its criterion of purification if nothing could be superfluous.","refs":["CH_MHON_T_16:SD2","CH_MHON_T_16:SNX2"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"Negative","passages":[]},{"id":"CL_MHON_07:DBN-04","text":"It must be false that celestial, material, and numerical orders immediately touch one another without a formal, causal, or connective medium.","refs":["CH_MHON_THER:SNX1","CH_MHON_T_16:SNX1"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"Negative","passages":[]}],"original_name":"Deep Book Apophatics"},{"number":"7","name":"Super-Apophatics of the Whole","items":[{"id":"CL_MHON_07:BSA-01","text":"The possibility of an external principle that perfects, directs, or completes the Monad is unavailable; perfection is admitted only through its own numbers and proportion.","refs":["CH_MHON_T_16:A9","CH_MHON_T_16:SNX3"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"Negative","passages":[]},{"id":"CL_MHON_07:BSA-02","text":"A wholly fortuitous natural event, subject to no figure, proportion, or influence, becomes ontologically inadmissible to the system.","refs":["CH_MHON_T_15:SNX3"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"Negative","passages":[]},{"id":"CL_MHON_07:BSA-03","text":"The total abolition of order or matter is prohibited: diminution can be restitutive, but deletion without remainder would leave no path to proportion.","refs":["CH_MHON_T_15:SNX4","CH_MHON_T_16:DNX3"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"Negative","passages":[]},{"id":"CL_MHON_07:BSA-04","text":"A wholly immediate and plainly transparent connection between heaven and matter is not given; the system requires a medium, but does not exhaust the mechanism of transmutation.","refs":["CH_MHON_THER:SNX3","CH_MHON_T_16:SNX1"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"Tension","passages":[]}],"original_name":"Super-Apophatics of the Whole"},{"number":"8","name":"Cross-Unit Derivative Propositions","items":[{"id":"CL_MHON_07:XD-01","text":"At this level, the hieroglyphic sign cannot be a separate symbol, because it becomes the interface through which celestial difference is conveyed into corporeal effect and the form of operation.","refs":["CL_MHON_07:GA-01","CL_MHON_07:GA-02","CL_MHON_07:GA-09","CH_MHON_T_15:SD1","CH_MHON_THER:SD3"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"Composite","passages":[]},{"id":"CL_MHON_07:XD-02","text":"Circulation causes higher form to descend into material operation: revolution does not decoratively imitate heaven, but disposes matter toward metamorphosis and restitution.","refs":["CL_MHON_07:GA-03","CL_MHON_07:GA-04","CL_MHON_07:GA-07","CH_MHON_T_15:D6","CH_MHON_T_15:A14","CH_MHON_T_16:D5"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"Composite","passages":[]},{"id":"CL_MHON_07:XD-03","text":"Structural subtraction shares the same law as the process of metamorphic disruption: diminution is generative where it preserves distinction and restores proportion.","refs":["CL_MHON_07:GA-05","CL_MHON_07:GA-07","CL_MHON_07:GA-10","CH_MHON_T_15:D7","CH_MHON_T_16:D3"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"Composite","passages":[]},{"id":"CL_MHON_07:XD-04","text":"Two terms by themselves do not suffice: the mortal body requires support from the distribution of the Sun and Moon, and the binary of lines requires a connective point.","refs":["CL_MHON_07:GA-05","CL_MHON_07:GA-06","CH_MHON_THER:SD2","CH_MHON_T_16:D1"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"Composite","passages":[]},{"id":"CL_MHON_07:XD-05","text":"Cabalistic vision and Analysis Pyronomica complement one another without the full reduction of one to the other: figures direct knowledge, effects attest causality.","refs":["CL_MHON_07:GA-09","CH_MHON_T_15:NX1","CH_MHON_THER:ALT3"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"Tension","passages":[]},{"id":"CL_MHON_07:XD-06","text":"Effective art does not introduce an extrinsic principle, but reconfigures matter already given through motion, tempering, and internal proportion.","refs":["CL_MHON_07:GA-04","CL_MHON_07:GA-08","CH_MHON_T_15:SD2","CH_MHON_T_16:NX5"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"Composite","passages":[]}],"original_name":"Cross-Unit Derivative Propositions"},{"number":"9","name":"Book-Scale Super-Derivatives","items":[{"id":"CL_MHON_07:BSD-01","text":"Material reality is described as a dependent state within a circuit of celestial influence, elemental composition, artificial motion, and proportional restitution.","refs":["CH_MHON_THER:SD4","CH_MHON_T_16:SD3"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[]},{"id":"CL_MHON_07:BSD-02","text":"Causality is not a simple descent from higher to lower, but a trans-scalar distribution in which figures order, influences sustain, and operations transform.","refs":["CH_MHON_T_15:SD1","CH_MHON_THER:A7","CH_MHON_T_16:SD1"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[]},{"id":"CL_MHON_07:BSD-03","text":"Time becomes a series of courses and returns: the times of animals, the circulation of motions, and the restitution of the Monad constitute one metamorphic temporality.","refs":["CH_MHON_T_15:A11","CH_MHON_T_16:D5"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[]},{"id":"CL_MHON_07:BSD-04","text":"The power to act consists not in mastery of matter from nothing, but in knowledge of time, motion, proportion, and those things that must be resected.","refs":["CH_MHON_T_15:D5","CH_MHON_T_16:D4"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[]},{"id":"CL_MHON_07:BSD-05","text":"Purification is reclassified as a form of generation: what is removed as superfluous does not simply perish, but restores space to distinction and proper matter.","refs":["CH_MHON_T_15:SD4","CH_MHON_T_16:SD2"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[]},{"id":"CL_MHON_07:BSD-06","text":"The truth of this system is constituted by interpretive concord and causal efficacy, not by a single form of demonstration abstractly separated.","refs":["CH_MHON_T_15:SD5","CH_MHON_THER:MRT4"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[]}],"original_name":"Book-Scale Super-Derivatives"},{"number":"10","name":"Recursive Loops, Escalation Paths, and Conceptual Attractors","items":[{"id":"CL_MHON_07:RLE-01","text":"Heaven → oval course → motion of the Scarab → artificial rolling → metamorphosis of the egg constitutes a circuit by which higher figure returns into operative matter.","refs":["CH_MHON_T_15:D2","CH_MHON_T_15:D6","CH_MHON_T_15:A13"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[]},{"id":"CL_MHON_07:RLE-02","text":"Celestial influence → corporeal effect → Analysis Pyronomica → confirmation of the order of celestial causality constitutes a circuit of amplification between posited cause and observed effect.","refs":["CH_MHON_THER:A2","CH_MHON_THER:FGS4"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[]},{"id":"CL_MHON_07:RLE-03","text":"Binary → connective point → ternary → removal of the point → quaternary → purification sets forth a circuit in which unity and distinction are alternately produced.","refs":["CH_MHON_T_16:FGS1","CH_MHON_T_16:FGS2"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[]},{"id":"CL_MHON_07:RLE-04","text":"Disruption → absorption → purification → restitution shows that the end is not closure, but an operator of new composition.","refs":["CH_MHON_T_15:D7","CH_MHON_T_16:SD2"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[]},{"id":"CL_MHON_07:RLE-05","text":"Proportion operates as a distributed attractor: motions, bodies, points, and matter are referred to it so that retention and resection may be judged.","refs":["CH_MHON_T_16:A10","CH_MHON_T_16:ME4"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[]}],"original_name":"Recursive Loops, Escalation Paths, and Conceptual Attractors"},{"number":"11","name":"Formal Generative Schema of the Whole Book","items":[{"id":"CL_MHON_07:FGS-B01","text":"Celestial inequality → preeminent force of the Sun and Moon → distribution of the aqueous and fiery → dependent terrestrial corporeality.","refs":["CH_MHON_THER:FGS1","CH_MHON_THER:FGS3"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[]},{"id":"CL_MHON_07:FGS-B02","text":"Celestial figure → oval course → proportion of Nature → hieroglyphic translation between heaven, body, and art.","refs":["CH_MHON_T_15:FGS2","CH_MHON_T_15:D2"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[]},{"id":"CL_MHON_07:FGS-B03","text":"Material proportion → composite egg → contest of animals → schema of artificial operation.","refs":["CH_MHON_T_15:FGS3","CH_MHON_T_15:D4","CH_MHON_T_15:FGS6"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[]},{"id":"CL_MHON_07:FGS-B04","text":"Disruption → tempering → rolling and revolution → absorption of albumen → ordered metamorphosis.","refs":["CH_MHON_T_15:FGS6","CH_MHON_T_15:FGS7"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[]},{"id":"CL_MHON_07:FGS-B05","text":"Examination of configuration → distinction of necessary and superfluous point → preservation of constitutive unity → purification of distinction.","refs":["CH_MHON_T_16:FGS2","CH_MHON_T_16:D4"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[]},{"id":"CL_MHON_07:FGS-B06","text":"Internal proportion → resection of external and confusive elements → return to proper matter → closure of the Monad.","refs":["CH_MHON_T_16:FGS3","CH_MHON_T_16:FGS4"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[]}],"original_name":"Formal Generative Schema of the Whole Book"},{"number":"12","name":"Whole-Book Argument Flow Map","items":[{"id":"CL_MHON_07:AFM-B01","text":"Celestial astronomy → Cabalistic vision → anatomy of the Monad in Nature and Life.","refs":["CH_MHON_T_15:FGS1","CH_MHON_T_15:A2"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[]},{"id":"CL_MHON_07:AFM-B02","text":"Celestial motion → oval figure → natural proportion → hieroglyphic figures.","refs":["CH_MHON_T_15:FGS2","CH_MHON_T_15:A6"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[]},{"id":"CL_MHON_07:AFM-B03","text":"Proportion of Nature → composition of the egg → disruption → artificial metamorphosis.","refs":["CH_MHON_T_15:FGS3","CH_MHON_T_15:FGS4","CH_MHON_T_15:FGS6"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[]},{"id":"CL_MHON_07:AFM-B04","text":"Celestial inequality → distribution of the Sun and Moon → Humor Aqueus and Liquor Igneus → sustentation of terrestrial corporeality.","refs":["CH_MHON_THER:FGS1","CH_MHON_THER:FGS2","CH_MHON_THER:FGS3"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[]},{"id":"CL_MHON_07:AFM-B05","text":"Binary → necessarily connective point → distinct quaternary → purification → restitution of the Monad to proper matter.","refs":["CH_MHON_T_16:FGS1","CH_MHON_T_16:FGS3"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[]}],"original_name":"Whole-Book Argument Flow Map"},{"number":"13","name":"Macro Map-Effects","items":[{"id":"CL_MHON_07:MME-01","text":"Heaven, nature, art, and geometry are reclassified as mutually translatable intermediate orders, not as causally segregated regions.","refs":["CH_MHON_T_15:ME1","CH_MHON_T_15:SD6"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[]},{"id":"CL_MHON_07:MME-02","text":"The inferior body is reclassified from passive matter into a node of influences, elements, proportions, and metamorphic operations.","refs":["CH_MHON_THER:ME1","CH_MHON_THER:D5"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[]},{"id":"CL_MHON_07:MME-03","text":"The opposition between natural and artificial collapses, not because art is simply equated with nature, but because art internally imitating natural motion produces transformation.","refs":["CH_MHON_T_15:ME3","CH_MHON_T_15:SD2"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[]},{"id":"CL_MHON_07:MME-04","text":"The opposition between generation and destruction changes: disruption, subtraction, and purification become differentiated modes of production.","refs":["CH_MHON_T_15:ME4","CH_MHON_T_16:ME2"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[]},{"id":"CL_MHON_07:MME-05","text":"Addition and subtraction cease to be quantitatively neutral operations; their value depends on the role they hold in the configuration of unity.","refs":["CH_MHON_T_16:SD1","CH_MHON_T_16:ME1"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[]}],"original_name":"Macro Map-Effects"},{"number":"14","name":"Altitude Integration","items":[{"id":"CL_MHON_07:ALT-B01","text":"[Surface] The Sun, Moon, egg, Eagle, Scarab, lines, and point are presented as immediate objects through which the system explains its relations.","refs":["CH_MHON_T_15:ALT1","CH_MHON_THER:ALT1","CH_MHON_T_16:ALT1"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[]},{"id":"CL_MHON_07:ALT-B02","text":"[Structural] Influence, proportion, circulation, composition, discontinuation, and purification constitute a recurrent machine that disposes diverse objects in the same order.","refs":["CH_MHON_T_15:ALT2","CH_MHON_THER:ALT2","CH_MHON_T_16:ALT2"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[]},{"id":"CL_MHON_07:ALT-B03","text":"[Metastrategic] Hieroglyphic interpretation and Analysis Pyronomica rematerialize reading: sign, effect, and figure become instruments of discrimination and response.","refs":["CH_MHON_T_15:ALT3","CH_MHON_THER:ALT3","CH_MHON_T_16:ALT3"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[]},{"id":"CL_MHON_07:ALT-B04","text":"[Ontological/Temporal] Reality is ordered through celestial circuits, elemental sustentations, material changes, and proportional returns rather than through autonomous substances.","refs":["CH_MHON_T_15:ALT4","CH_MHON_THER:ALT4","CH_MHON_T_16:ALT4"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[]},{"id":"CL_MHON_07:ALT-B05","text":"[Methodological/Performative] The form of the argument renders its assertions effective, because it itself passes among fable, operation, effect, and figure, and imposes those passages as conditions of knowledge.","refs":["CH_MHON_T_15:AS6","CH_MHON_THER:ALT3","CH_MHON_T_16:ALT3"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[]}],"original_name":"Altitude Integration"},{"number":"15","name":"Internal Tensions, Mutation Points, and Fault Lines","items":[{"id":"CL_MHON_07:FL-01","text":"Celestial causality is affirmed and defended through effects, but the mechanism by which celestial influences are transmuted into terrestrial matter is not fully declared.","refs":["CH_MHON_THER:SNX3","CH_MHON_THER:MRT3"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"Tension","passages":[]},{"id":"CL_MHON_07:FL-02","text":"Hieroglyphic concordance and pyronomic demonstration retain different kinds of proof; no common rule is explained by which analogy and effect are reduced to the same necessity.","refs":["CH_MHON_T_15:MRT3","CH_MHON_THER:MRT5"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"Tension","passages":[]},{"id":"CL_MHON_07:FL-03","text":"The change of the point from necessary to superfluous is the principal operator of the system, but the criterion by which configuration legitimates this inversion beyond the figure itself is not fully determined.","refs":["CH_MHON_T_16:D2","CH_MHON_T_16:MRT3"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"Tension","passages":[]},{"id":"CL_MHON_07:FL-04","text":"The Monad is said to be self-sufficient, but expansion and restitution are at the same time entrusted to the industry of the artificer; autarky and external operation are not wholly reconciled.","refs":["CH_MHON_T_16:A9","CH_MHON_T_16:MRT5"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"Tension","passages":[]},{"id":"CL_MHON_07:MP-01","text":"Celestial figure is transformed into the motion of art where oval circulation becomes materially effective through the rolling of the Scarab and the revolution of operation.","refs":["CH_MHON_T_15:D2","CH_MHON_T_15:D6"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"Tension","passages":[]},{"id":"CL_MHON_07:MP-02","text":"Destruction is transformed into restitution where the disrupted egg is not extinguished, but albumen absorbed into the yolk-liquid produces another composition.","refs":["CH_MHON_T_15:D7","CH_MHON_T_15:A14"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"Tension","passages":[]},{"id":"CL_MHON_07:MP-03","text":"Number is transformed into qualitative judgment where the point is assessed not by quantity but by connective or confusive function.","refs":["CH_MHON_T_16:SD1","CH_MHON_T_16:ME1"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"Tension","passages":[]}],"original_name":"Internal Tensions, Mutation Points, and Fault Lines"},{"number":"16","name":"Hidden System Image","items":[{"id":"CL_MHON_07:HSI-01","text":"Reality is implicitly fashioned as a machine of transductions, in which celestial influences are distributed, figures order them, matter receives them, and operation reconfigures them.","refs":["CH_MHON_T_15:SD1","CH_MHON_THER:A2","CH_MHON_T_15:SD2"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[]},{"id":"CL_MHON_07:HSI-02","text":"The subject is not an autonomous spectator, but an interpreter-operator who must discern times, motions, effects, and superfluities.","refs":["CH_MHON_T_15:D5","CH_MHON_T_15:SD5","CH_MHON_T_16:D4"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[]},{"id":"CL_MHON_07:HSI-03","text":"Time is not simple succession, but a circle of sustentation, latency, disruption, and proportional return.","refs":["CH_MHON_T_15:A11","CH_MHON_T_15:D6","CH_MHON_T_16:D5"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[]},{"id":"CL_MHON_07:HSI-04","text":"Contest is defined not through the abolition of the adversary, but through navigation among injury, limitation, transformation, and preservation of the proportioned remainder.","refs":["CH_MHON_T_15:D4","CH_MHON_T_15:MRT6","CH_MHON_T_16:SD2"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[]}],"original_name":"Hidden System Image"},{"number":"17","name":"Minimal Whole-Book Reconstruction Test","items":[{"id":"CL_MHON_07:MRT-B01","text":"The present axioms suffice to reconstruct the group-scale machine: celestial influence, proportion, motion, metamorphosis, purification, and Monadic return.","refs":["CH_MHON_T_15:MRT1","CH_MHON_T_16:MRT1"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[]},{"id":"CL_MHON_07:MRT-B02","text":"The firmest structure is where the same relation formally recurs among the celestial circle, artificial motion, proportional return, and distinction of the point.","refs":["CH_MHON_T_15:MRT2","CH_MHON_T_16:D5","CH_MHON_T_16:ME1"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[]},{"id":"CL_MHON_07:MRT-B03","text":"The conversion of influence into body, the rationale of hieroglyphic correspondences, and the passage from geometry to spiritual judgment remain insufficiently determined.","refs":["CH_MHON_T_15:MRT4","CH_MHON_THER:SNX3","CH_MHON_T_16:MRT2"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[]},{"id":"CL_MHON_07:MRT-B04","text":"The synthesis coheres as a generative matrix, not as a univocal demonstration: its force depends on the recurrent translation of proportion among heterogeneous orders.","refs":["CH_MHON_T_15:MRT5","CH_MHON_THER:MRT6","CH_MHON_T_16:MRT3"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[]}],"original_name":"Minimal Whole-Book Reconstruction Test"}],"revision":"CL_MHON_07@R001","source_variant_id":"canonical:CL_MHON_07:v1","source_language_code":"la","original_title":"Chapters 19–21"}],"identity":{"layer":"CL","kicker":"Cluster · Monas Hieroglyphica","title":"Chapters 19–21","context":"Theorem Xviii. · T H E Ò R. X I X. · T H E O R. X X.","original_title":"Chapters 19–21","original_context":"Theor. Xviii. · T H E Ò R. X I X. · T H E O R. X X."},"original_title":"Chapters 19–21","title_language_code":"en"},{"id":"CL_MHON_08","layer":"CL","title":"Chapters 22–23","order":8,"parents":["CH_MHON_T_17","CH_MHON_T_18"],"revision":"CL_MHON_08@R001","sha256":"78c9a43e133bd028ba3915905d5ac03c8d4b3c2903b57522d4b889cb8f3a0fd9","sections":[{"number":"1","name":"Book-Scale Argumental Spine","items":[{"id":"CL_MHON_08:ABS-01","text":"Quaestio centralis est quomodo MONAS, divisionem et dissolutionem nexuum sustinens, simul corpus differentiabile et principium operativum manere possit.","refs":["CH_MHON_T_17:AS6","CH_MHON_T_18:AS2"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[]},{"id":"CL_MHON_08:ABS-02","text":"Thesis globalis ponit analysim partium non ut corruptionem unitatis, sed ut condicionem qua munera, gradus et operationes intra originem communem explicantur.","refs":["CH_MHON_T_17:A10","CH_MHON_T_18:A3"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[]},{"id":"CL_MHON_08:ABS-03","text":"Argumentatio a situ, proportione et figura incipit, sed per metathesin ostendit formam localem non esse aspectum inertem, sed vectorem mutationis functionalis.","refs":["CH_MHON_T_17:A1","CH_MHON_T_18:D2"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[]},{"id":"CL_MHON_08:ABS-04","text":"Ars figuram incompletam componere et in instrumentum transferre potest, sed compositio artificiosa possessionem naturalem perfectionis non efficit.","refs":["CH_MHON_T_17:A8","CH_MHON_T_18:D5"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[]},{"id":"CL_MHON_08:ABS-05","text":"Genus argumentationis fit hieroglyphico-operativum: signa valent non ex sola recognitione visiva, sed ex recta compositione, operatione et dispositione interpretis.","refs":["CH_MHON_T_17:AS2","CH_MHON_T_18:SD2"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[]},{"id":"CL_MHON_08:ABS-06","text":"Motus systematis est recursivus: dissectio producit differentiam, differentia metathesin admittit, metathesis operationem producit, operatio autem distinctionem naturae et artificii denuo exigere facit.","refs":["CH_MHON_T_17:AS5","CH_MHON_T_18:D1"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[]},{"id":"CL_MHON_08:ABS-07","text":"Clausura partialis per sympathiam monadicam obtinetur, sed inexhaustio mysteriorum impedit ne unio finalis in explicationem exhaustivam convertatur.","refs":["CH_MHON_T_17:A11","CH_MHON_T_18:A1"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[]},{"id":"CL_MHON_08:ABS-08","text":"Scientia huius systematis distributa est: revelatio selectiva partium cognitionem producit, dum accessus ad subtiliora per gradus initiationis limitatur.","refs":["CH_MHON_T_18:A2","CH_MHON_T_18:SD4"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[]}],"original_name":"Book-Scale Argumental Spine"},{"number":"2","name":"Global Load-Bearing Axioms","items":[{"id":"CL_MHON_08:GA-01","text":"[Persistent] Unitas MONADIS non abolitur cum partes distinguuntur, quia origo communis relationem, sympathiam et possibilitatem reunionis servat.","refs":["CH_MHON_T_17:A10","CH_MHON_T_17:A11"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[]},{"id":"CL_MHON_08:GA-02","text":"[Mutating] Figura localis initio statum essentialem indicat, sed per metathesin in medium translationis inter formam, vas et munus convertitur.","refs":["CH_MHON_T_17:A1","CH_MHON_T_18:D2"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[]},{"id":"CL_MHON_08:GA-03","text":"[Persistent] Similitudo geometrica neque identitatem essentialem neque perfectionem naturalem demonstrat; inter proprietatem, analogiam et effectum artificiosum discernendum est.","refs":["CH_MHON_T_17:DNX1","CH_MHON_T_17:A8"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[]},{"id":"CL_MHON_08:GA-04","text":"[Emergent] Dissolutio et notatio nexuum sunt operationes generativae, quia eadem dissectio inventarium formarum et recompositionum futurorum producit.","refs":["CH_MHON_T_17:DNX4","CH_MHON_T_18:D1"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[]},{"id":"CL_MHON_08:GA-05","text":"[Attractor] Ars mediat inter potentiam incompletam et formam compositam, sed effectus eius naturalem plenitudinem non automatice substituit.","refs":["CH_MHON_T_17:SD1","CH_MHON_T_17:A8"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[]},{"id":"CL_MHON_08:GA-06","text":"[Mutating] Signum geometricum fit condensatio materialis et operativa, cuius efficacia ex instrumento, materia et condicionibus ambitus pendet.","refs":["CH_MHON_T_18:D3","CH_MHON_T_18:D5"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[]},{"id":"CL_MHON_08:GA-07","text":"[Emergent] Apodixis non est probatio universaliter exterior, sed eventus signi recte intellecti et operati ab interprete disposito.","refs":["CH_MHON_T_18:A15","CH_MHON_T_18:D8"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[]},{"id":"CL_MHON_08:GA-08","text":"[Terminal] Inexhaustio MONADIS clausuram interpretativam impedit et omnem conclusionem in principium ulterioris extractionis reducit.","refs":["CH_MHON_T_17:NX7","CH_MHON_T_18:A1"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[]}],"original_name":"Global Load-Bearing Axioms"},{"number":"3","name":"Recurrence, Mutation, and Displacement Matrix","items":[{"id":"CL_MHON_08:RMD-01","text":"[Redditus functionalis] Recta, circulus et semicirculus redeunt, sed a signis status localis ad formas symmetriae, vasorum et operationum migrant.","refs":["CH_MHON_T_17:A4","CH_MHON_T_18:A6"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[]},{"id":"CL_MHON_08:RMD-02","text":"[Transformata] Dissectio transit a distinctione anatomica partium ad inventionem repertorii ex quo instrumenta et nexus novi componuntur.","refs":["CH_MHON_T_17:AS6","CH_MHON_T_18:D1"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[]},{"id":"CL_MHON_08:RMD-03","text":"[Inversio] Artificiositas, quae prius defectum naturalis solaritatis indicat, postea efficax modus translationis in vasa et operationes fit.","refs":["CH_MHON_T_17:AS5","CH_MHON_T_18:D3"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[]},{"id":"CL_MHON_08:RMD-04","text":"[Absorptio] Unitas mutatur a sympathia post dissectionem ad microcosmum generativum, in quo differentiae formales, materiales et cosmologicae ad idem principium referuntur.","refs":["CH_MHON_T_17:SD4","CH_MHON_T_18:SD1"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[]},{"id":"CL_MHON_08:RMD-05","text":"[Persistentia mutans] Mysterium persistit, sed iam non est solum residuum ignotum; fit regula quae seriem figurarum, operationum et interpretationum reaperit.","refs":["CH_MHON_T_17:SNX2","CH_MHON_T_18:DNX5"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[]}],"original_name":"Recurrence, Mutation, and Displacement Matrix"},{"number":"4","name":"Lower-Layer vs Current-Level Distinction","items":[{"id":"CL_MHON_08:CGD-01","text":"Attributiones particulares B, D et C ad inferioritatem, stabilitatem vel indeterminationem locales manent; in gradu praesentis gregis sola regula relatio inter situm, mensuram et statum retinetur.","refs":["CH_MHON_T_17:AS3","CH_MHON_T_17:A9"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[]},{"id":"CL_MHON_08:CGD-02","text":"Nomina singularium litterarum et vasorum locales sunt; structura superior metathesin retinet ut regulam generalem qua eadem elementa diversa munera suscipiunt.","refs":["CH_MHON_T_18:A5","CH_MHON_T_18:A7"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[]},{"id":"CL_MHON_08:CGD-03","text":"Distinctio naturae et artis ampliatur: non iam solum quaeritur utrum figura solaritatem habeat, sed utrum operatio formam tantum repraesentet an condiciones efficaciae materialis sustineat.","refs":["CH_MHON_T_17:SNX1","CH_MHON_T_18:D5"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[]},{"id":"CL_MHON_08:CGD-04","text":"Discretio partium sursum amplificatur, quia analysis non solum proprietates separat, sed materiam recompositionis et relationis monadicae producit.","refs":["CH_MHON_T_17:A10","CH_MHON_T_18:D1"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[]},{"id":"CL_MHON_08:CGD-05","text":"Recognitio visiva sola superatur; lectio valida nunc requirit operationem rectam, condicionem materialem et gradum initiationis.","refs":["CH_MHON_T_17:ALT3","CH_MHON_T_18:DNX6"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[]}],"original_name":"Lower-Layer vs Current-Level Distinction"},{"number":"5","name":"Global Apophatic Structure","items":[{"id":"CL_MHON_08:GNX-01","text":"Systema negat identitatem immediatam inter formam visibilem, essentiam naturalem et rem significatam.","refs":["CH_MHON_T_17:SNX3","CH_MHON_T_18:SNX2"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[]},{"id":"CL_MHON_08:GNX-02","text":"Systema negat possibilitatem geometricam connexionis sufficere ad unitatem substantialem vel perfectionem naturalem.","refs":["CH_MHON_T_17:DNX2","CH_MHON_T_17:SNX1"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[]},{"id":"CL_MHON_08:GNX-03","text":"Systema negat et causam omnino externam et observatorem omnino extrinsecum, quia intelligere requirit participationem in ordine monadico-operativo.","refs":["CH_MHON_T_18:DNX1","CH_MHON_T_18:SNX1"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[]},{"id":"CL_MHON_08:GNX-04","text":"Systema negat revelationem aequaliter publicam et interpretationem definitive exhaustivam; occultatio et inexhaustio sunt limites constitutivi.","refs":["CH_MHON_T_17:SNX2","CH_MHON_T_18:SNX5"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[]}],"original_name":"Global Apophatic Structure"},{"number":"6","name":"Deep Book Apophatics","items":[{"id":"CL_MHON_08:DBN-01","text":"Falsum esse debet quod dissectio partes in substantias autonomas dissolvit, quia systema tantum manet si differentia relationem originis communis retinet.","refs":["CH_MHON_T_17:DNX4","CH_MHON_T_18:DNX4"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[]},{"id":"CL_MHON_08:DBN-02","text":"Falsum esse debet quod symbolum sit neutrum aut quod praedicata vitalitatis ex sola specie figuralis proprie sequantur.","refs":["CH_MHON_T_17:DNX3","CH_MHON_T_18:DNX2"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[]},{"id":"CL_MHON_08:DBN-03","text":"Falsum esse debet quod effectus artificiosus idem sit ac transformatio ontologica; aliter distinctio inter potentiam, compositionem et perfectionem corrueret.","refs":["CH_MHON_T_17:SD1","CH_MHON_T_17:A8"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[]},{"id":"CL_MHON_08:DBN-04","text":"Falsum esse debet quod materia et ambitus sint adiuncta indifferentia, quia defectus vel efficacia operationis a condicionibus physicis pendet.","refs":["CH_MHON_T_18:SNX3","CH_MHON_T_18:D5"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[]}],"original_name":"Deep Book Apophatics"},{"number":"7","name":"Super-Apophatics of the Whole","items":[{"id":"CL_MHON_08:BSA-01","text":"Inadmissibilis est observator qui totum systema sine initiatione possideat, quia dispositio interpretis pars mechanismi probationis est.","refs":["CH_MHON_T_18:SNX1","CH_MHON_T_18:D8"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[]},{"id":"CL_MHON_08:BSA-02","text":"Inadmissibilis est coincidentia plena figurae, essentiae et operationis, quia forma potest munus artificiosum suscipere sine perfectione naturali possessa.","refs":["CH_MHON_T_17:NX2","CH_MHON_T_17:A8"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[]},{"id":"CL_MHON_08:BSA-03","text":"Inadmissibilis est unitas immobilis et indivisa, quia MONAS suam unitatem non ante differentiam, sed per differentiam et reunionem retinet.","refs":["CH_MHON_T_17:AS6","CH_MHON_T_17:A11"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[]}],"original_name":"Super-Apophatics of the Whole"},{"number":"8","name":"Cross-Unit Derivative Propositions","items":[{"id":"CL_MHON_08:XD-01","text":"Dissolutio nexuum fit productiva quia partes, originem communem retinentes, simul inventarium recompositionis et relationis monadicae efficiunt.","refs":["CL_MHON_08:GA-01","CL_MHON_08:GA-04","CH_MHON_T_17:A10","CH_MHON_T_18:D1"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"Composite","passages":[]},{"id":"CL_MHON_08:XD-02","text":"Metathesis munus figurae mutare potest, sed non potest ex sola compositione artificiali essentiam naturalem iam possessam efficere.","refs":["CL_MHON_08:GA-02","CL_MHON_08:GA-03","CL_MHON_08:GA-05","CH_MHON_T_17:A8","CH_MHON_T_18:D2"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"Composite","passages":[]},{"id":"CL_MHON_08:XD-03","text":"Ambitus externus fit limes internus operationis: aer et ventus efficaciam determinant, dum exclusio eorum cum clausura monadica tensionem constituit.","refs":["CL_MHON_08:GA-06","CH_MHON_T_18:A11","CH_MHON_T_18:NX4"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"Tension","passages":[]},{"id":"CL_MHON_08:XD-04","text":"Incompletio B et progressio initiatoria simul efficiunt ut cognitio non per saltum ad plenitudinem, sed per gradus, specimina et praeparationes moveatur.","refs":["CL_MHON_08:GA-05","CL_MHON_08:GA-07","CH_MHON_T_17:D2","CH_MHON_T_18:D4"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"Composite","passages":[]},{"id":"CL_MHON_08:XD-05","text":"Signum in apodixin convertitur non quia figura per se sufficit, sed quia interpretatio analogica in operationem rectam et subiectum dispositum transit.","refs":["CL_MHON_08:GA-03","CL_MHON_08:GA-06","CL_MHON_08:GA-07","CH_MHON_T_18:D8","CH_MHON_T_18:DNX6"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"Composite","passages":[]}],"original_name":"Cross-Unit Derivative Propositions"},{"number":"9","name":"Book-Scale Super-Derivatives","items":[{"id":"CL_MHON_08:BSD-01","text":"Realitas systematis non est aggregatum rerum discretarum, sed campus relationum in quo unitas per differentiationem, translationem et recompositionem generatur.","refs":["CL_MHON_08:GA-01","CL_MHON_08:GA-04","CH_MHON_T_17:SD4","CH_MHON_T_18:SD3"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"From","passages":[]},{"id":"CL_MHON_08:BSD-02","text":"Auctoritas cognitionis transfertur a verificatione communiter exteriori ad congruentiam inter signum, apparatus, operationem et dispositionem initiati.","refs":["CL_MHON_08:GA-06","CL_MHON_08:GA-07","CH_MHON_T_18:SD6","CH_MHON_T_18:D8"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"From","passages":[]},{"id":"CL_MHON_08:BSD-03","text":"Tempus non est progressus lineariter cumulativus, sed iteratum intervallum inter potentiam, compositionem, correctionem et inexhaustionem.","refs":["CL_MHON_08:GA-05","CL_MHON_08:GA-08","CH_MHON_T_17:D3","CH_MHON_T_18:A12"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"From","passages":[]},{"id":"CL_MHON_08:BSD-04","text":"Ars reclassificatur ut disciplina limitata: non creat plenitudinem ex nihilo, sed potentias incompositas ordinat sub condicionibus materialibus et symbolicis.","refs":["CL_MHON_08:GA-05","CL_MHON_08:GA-06","CH_MHON_T_17:SD1","CH_MHON_T_18:SD2"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"From","passages":[]}],"original_name":"Book-Scale Super-Derivatives"},{"number":"10","name":"Recursive Loops, Escalation Paths, and Conceptual Attractors","items":[{"id":"CL_MHON_08:RLE-01","text":"Circuitus primarius est dissectio → notatio → recompositio → recognitio originis communis → nova dissectio.","refs":["CH_MHON_T_17:DNX4","CH_MHON_T_18:D1"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[]},{"id":"CL_MHON_08:RLE-02","text":"Via amplificationis est potentia figurata → connexio artificiosa → distinctio effectus a perfectione naturali → lectura accuratior potentiae.","refs":["CH_MHON_T_17:NX3","CH_MHON_T_18:D2"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[]},{"id":"CL_MHON_08:RLE-03","text":"Circuitus initiatorius est secretum → specimen → praeparatio subtilior → accessus restrictus ad ulteriora secreta.","refs":["CH_MHON_T_18:A2","CH_MHON_T_18:A10"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[]},{"id":"CL_MHON_08:RLE-04","text":"Duo attractores systema ordinant: unio monadica differentias ad originem retrahit, inexhaustio autem originem ab omni saturatione interpretativa subtrahit.","refs":["CH_MHON_T_17:A11","CH_MHON_T_18:A1"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[]}],"original_name":"Recursive Loops, Escalation Paths, and Conceptual Attractors"},{"number":"11","name":"Formal Generative Schema of the Whole Book","items":[{"id":"CL_MHON_08:FGS-B01","text":"Commutatio vel inexhaustio MONADIS → dissolutio corporis aut nexus → distinctio partium notabilium.","refs":["CH_MHON_T_17:AS1","CH_MHON_T_18:A3"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[]},{"id":"CL_MHON_08:FGS-B02","text":"Partes distinctae → lectura situs, figurae et proportionis → separatio status naturalis, analogici et artificialis.","refs":["CH_MHON_T_17:AS2","CH_MHON_T_17:DNX1"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[]},{"id":"CL_MHON_08:FGS-B03","text":"Partes notatae → metathesis localis → translatio inter litteram, figuram, vas et instrumentum.","refs":["CH_MHON_T_18:D1","CH_MHON_T_18:D3"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[]},{"id":"CL_MHON_08:FGS-B04","text":"Compositio artificiosa → effectus operativus condicionatus → prohibitio ne effectus pro perfectione naturali habeatur.","refs":["CH_MHON_T_17:A8","CH_MHON_T_18:D5"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[]},{"id":"CL_MHON_08:FGS-B05","text":"Operatio recta ab initiato exercita → signum in apodixin transit → unio monadica restituitur sine exhauritione mysterii.","refs":["CH_MHON_T_18:D8","CH_MHON_T_17:NX7"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[]}],"original_name":"Formal Generative Schema of the Whole Book"},{"number":"12","name":"Whole-Book Argument Flow Map","items":[{"id":"CL_MHON_08:AFM-B01","text":"Commutatio localis et inexhaustio MONADIS → quaestio de differentiabilitate principii unius.","refs":["CH_MHON_T_17:AS1","CH_MHON_T_18:A1"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[]},{"id":"CL_MHON_08:AFM-B02","text":"Dissolutio nexus → notatio partium → classificatio per situm, figuram et relationem.","refs":["CH_MHON_T_18:A3","CH_MHON_T_17:A1"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[]},{"id":"CL_MHON_08:AFM-B03","text":"Classificatio figuralis → distinctio naturae, analogiae et artificii → recusatio perfectionis immediate datae.","refs":["CH_MHON_T_17:AS4","CH_MHON_T_17:A8"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[]},{"id":"CL_MHON_08:AFM-B04","text":"Metathesis partium → compositio vasorum et instrumentorum → translatio figurae in operationem materialem.","refs":["CH_MHON_T_18:A5","CH_MHON_T_18:D3"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[]},{"id":"CL_MHON_08:AFM-B05","text":"Operatio condicionata et initiatoria → apodixis selectiva → reditus ad mysterium inexhaustum.","refs":["CH_MHON_T_18:D8","CH_MHON_T_18:A2"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[]},{"id":"CL_MHON_08:AFM-B06","text":"Differentia explicata → sympathia et unio monadica → apertura ad ulteriora mysteria.","refs":["CH_MHON_T_17:A11","CH_MHON_T_17:NX7"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[]}],"original_name":"Whole-Book Argument Flow Map"},{"number":"13","name":"Macro Map-Effects","items":[{"id":"CL_MHON_08:MME-01","text":"MONAS reclassificatur ex signo simplici in machinam internam quae formas, partes, instrumenta et relationes generare potest.","refs":["CH_MHON_T_17:ME1","CH_MHON_T_18:ME1"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[]},{"id":"CL_MHON_08:MME-02","text":"Oppositio inter unitatem et distinctionem corruit, quia discretio non unitati adversatur sed modum eius operativum constituit.","refs":["CH_MHON_T_17:ME4","CH_MHON_T_18:SD3"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[]},{"id":"CL_MHON_08:MME-03","text":"Oppositio inter mysticum et practicum cadit, quia ars materialis fit via cognitionis mysticae sine reductione mysterii ad usum vulgarem.","refs":["CH_MHON_T_17:ME2","CH_MHON_T_18:ME3"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[]},{"id":"CL_MHON_08:MME-04","text":"Campus cognitionis describitur ut scala initiationis, in qua occultatio, specimen, praeparatio et apodixis differentes facultates interpretandi distribuunt.","refs":["CH_MHON_T_18:ME4","CH_MHON_T_18:SNX5"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[]},{"id":"CL_MHON_08:MME-05","text":"Tempus et ambitus physicus reclassificantur ut condiciones constitutivae operationis, non ut circumstantiae posteriores aut externae.","refs":["CH_MHON_T_18:ME5","CH_MHON_T_18:D5"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[]}],"original_name":"Macro Map-Effects"},{"number":"14","name":"Altitude Integration","items":[{"id":"CL_MHON_08:ALT-B01","text":"In gradu superficiei, membra B, D et C atque litterae α, λ, δ et ω apparent ut figurae distinctae, vasa et signa.","refs":["CH_MHON_T_17:ALT1","CH_MHON_T_18:ALT1"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[]},{"id":"CL_MHON_08:ALT-B02","text":"In gradu structurali, figura, directio, proportio, dissolutio nexus et metathesis regulas differentiationis et recompositionis constituunt.","refs":["CH_MHON_T_17:ALT2","CH_MHON_T_18:ALT2"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[]},{"id":"CL_MHON_08:ALT-B03","text":"In gradu metastrategico, interpretatio mutatur ab inspectione ad regimen accessus, operationis et facultatis apodicticae.","refs":["CH_MHON_T_17:ALT3","CH_MHON_T_18:ALT3"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[]},{"id":"CL_MHON_08:ALT-B04","text":"In gradu ontologico-temporali, esse et vivere sunt gradus initiales, dum MONAS fit campus internus ubi corpus, mundus, horae et transformatio connectuntur.","refs":["CH_MHON_T_17:ALT4","CH_MHON_T_18:ALT4"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[]},{"id":"CL_MHON_08:ALT-B05","text":"In gradu methodologico-performativo, forma systematis suam thesim perficit: signa non solum exponit, sed per operationem rectam ad statum apodicticum disponit.","refs":["CH_MHON_T_18:SD2","CH_MHON_T_18:D8"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[]}],"original_name":"Altitude Integration"},{"number":"15","name":"Internal Tensions, Mutation Points, and Fault Lines","items":[{"id":"CL_MHON_08:FL-01","text":"Tensionem constituit quod ars formas effective componere potest, sed transitus ab efficacia figurali ad perfectionem naturalem vel ad vim materialem plene demonstratus non est.","refs":["CH_MHON_T_17:A8","CH_MHON_T_18:MRT4"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[]},{"id":"CL_MHON_08:FL-02","text":"Clausura monadica et conditio aeris externi non perfecte componuntur: MONAS nihil extra se admittere dicitur, sed functio λ ab ambitu externo vulneratur.","refs":["CH_MHON_T_18:NX4","CH_MHON_T_18:A11"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[]},{"id":"CL_MHON_08:MP-01","text":"Punctum mutationis est ubi anatomia MONADIS desinit esse sola discretio interpretativa et fit apparatus ad compositionem vasorum ac munerum.","refs":["CH_MHON_T_17:D6","CH_MHON_T_18:D1"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[]},{"id":"CL_MHON_08:MP-02","text":"Punctum mutationis est ubi imperfectio B non solum status figuralis manet, sed schema progressionis initiatoriae et operativae efficit.","refs":["CH_MHON_T_17:D2","CH_MHON_T_18:D4"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[]}],"original_name":"Internal Tensions, Mutation Points, and Fault Lines"},{"number":"16","name":"Hidden System Image","items":[{"id":"CL_MHON_08:HSI-01","text":"Realitas implicite figuratur ut campus monadicus qui se in differentias explicat, eas per operationem reordinat, et numquam in collectionem rerum independentium dissolvitur.","refs":["CH_MHON_T_18:SD1","CH_MHON_T_17:SD4"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[]},{"id":"CL_MHON_08:HSI-02","text":"Subiectum implicite fit initiatus operator, qui figuram non tantum contemplatur, sed condiciones, apparatus et ordinem signorum recte administrat.","refs":["CH_MHON_T_18:SD2","CH_MHON_T_18:A16"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[]},{"id":"CL_MHON_08:HSI-03","text":"Tempus implicite est temporis-horarum campus iterabilis, in quo plenitudo differtur, operatio reditur et mysterium permanet inexhaustum.","refs":["CH_MHON_T_18:A12","CH_MHON_T_18:A1"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[]}],"original_name":"Hidden System Image"},{"number":"17","name":"Minimal Whole-Book Reconstruction Test","items":[{"id":"CL_MHON_08:MRT-B01","text":"Axiomata de unitate relationis, differentia figuralis, distinctione naturae et artificii, operatione initiatoria atque inexhaustione sufficiunt ad machinam huius gregis restituendam.","refs":["CH_MHON_T_17:MRT1","CH_MHON_T_18:MRT1"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[]},{"id":"CL_MHON_08:MRT-B02","text":"Structura firmissima est ubi dissectio, metathesis, symmetria, conditio materialis et relatio monadica in unam seriem inferentialem comprimuntur.","refs":["CH_MHON_T_17:MRT2","CH_MHON_T_18:MRT2"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[]},{"id":"CL_MHON_08:MRT-B03","text":"Restitutio non determinat omnem geometriam neque proceduras occultas, sed tensionem inter figuram, efficaciam, secretum et inexhaustionem servat ut conditionem operationis.","refs":["CH_MHON_T_18:MRT3","CH_MHON_T_18:MRT7"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[]}],"original_name":"Minimal Whole-Book Reconstruction Test"}],"language_code":"la","language_name":"Latina","translations":[{"id":"CAREVAR_20261006T223436054441Z_93f56e8e","language_code":"en","language_name":"English","title":"Chapters 22–23","source_sha256":"78c9a43e133bd028ba3915905d5ac03c8d4b3c2903b57522d4b889cb8f3a0fd9","sha256":"bb7c67d0982a3d0349304166f13d750467dff6a9764e0ba61ff57ad59353c583","rendition_type":"machine","review_status":"machine","model":"gpt-5.6-terra","created":"2026-10-06T22:34:36.056708+00:00","sections":[{"number":"1","name":"Book-Scale Argumental Spine","items":[{"id":"CL_MHON_08:ABS-01","text":"The central question is how the MONAD, sustaining division and the dissolution of connections, can at once remain a differentiable body and an operative principle.","refs":["CH_MHON_T_17:AS6","CH_MHON_T_18:AS2"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[]},{"id":"CL_MHON_08:ABS-02","text":"The global thesis posits the analysis of parts not as a corruption of unity, but as the condition under which functions, degrees, and operations are unfolded within a common origin.","refs":["CH_MHON_T_17:A10","CH_MHON_T_18:A3"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[]},{"id":"CL_MHON_08:ABS-03","text":"The argument begins from position, proportion, and figure, but through metathesis shows that local form is not an inert aspect, but a vector of functional change.","refs":["CH_MHON_T_17:A1","CH_MHON_T_18:D2"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[]},{"id":"CL_MHON_08:ABS-04","text":"Art can compose an incomplete figure and transfer it into an instrument, but artificial composition does not produce the natural possession of perfection.","refs":["CH_MHON_T_17:A8","CH_MHON_T_18:D5"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[]},{"id":"CL_MHON_08:ABS-05","text":"The mode of argument becomes hieroglyphic-operative: signs prevail not by visual recognition alone, but by correct composition, operation, and the disposition of the interpreter.","refs":["CH_MHON_T_17:AS2","CH_MHON_T_18:SD2"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[]},{"id":"CL_MHON_08:ABS-06","text":"The movement of the system is recursive: dissection produces difference, difference admits metathesis, metathesis produces operation, and operation in turn requires anew the distinction of nature and art.","refs":["CH_MHON_T_17:AS5","CH_MHON_T_18:D1"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[]},{"id":"CL_MHON_08:ABS-07","text":"Partial closure is attained through monadic sympathy, but the inexhaustibility of mysteries prevents final union from being converted into exhaustive explication.","refs":["CH_MHON_T_17:A11","CH_MHON_T_18:A1"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[]},{"id":"CL_MHON_08:ABS-08","text":"The knowledge of this system is distributed: selective revelation of parts produces knowledge, while access to subtler matters is limited by degrees of initiation.","refs":["CH_MHON_T_18:A2","CH_MHON_T_18:SD4"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[]}],"original_name":"Book-Scale Argumental Spine"},{"number":"2","name":"Global Load-Bearing Axioms","items":[{"id":"CL_MHON_08:GA-01","text":"[Persistent] The unity of the MONAD is not abolished when parts are distinguished, because common origin preserves relation, sympathy, and the possibility of reunion.","refs":["CH_MHON_T_17:A10","CH_MHON_T_17:A11"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[]},{"id":"CL_MHON_08:GA-02","text":"[Mutating] Local figure initially indicates an essential state, but through metathesis is converted into a medium of translation between form, vessel, and function.","refs":["CH_MHON_T_17:A1","CH_MHON_T_18:D2"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[]},{"id":"CL_MHON_08:GA-03","text":"[Persistent] Geometrical similarity demonstrates neither essential identity nor natural perfection; a distinction must be made between property, analogy, and artificial effect.","refs":["CH_MHON_T_17:DNX1","CH_MHON_T_17:A8"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[]},{"id":"CL_MHON_08:GA-04","text":"[Emergent] The dissolution and marking of connections are generative operations, because the same dissection produces an inventory of forms and future recompositions.","refs":["CH_MHON_T_17:DNX4","CH_MHON_T_18:D1"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[]},{"id":"CL_MHON_08:GA-05","text":"[Attractor] Art mediates between incomplete potentiality and composed form, but its effect does not automatically replace natural fullness.","refs":["CH_MHON_T_17:SD1","CH_MHON_T_17:A8"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[]},{"id":"CL_MHON_08:GA-06","text":"[Mutating] The geometrical sign becomes a material and operative condensation, whose efficacy depends on the instrument, matter, and environmental conditions.","refs":["CH_MHON_T_18:D3","CH_MHON_T_18:D5"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[]},{"id":"CL_MHON_08:GA-07","text":"[Emergent] Apodixis is not universally external proof, but the outcome of a sign correctly understood and operated by a suitably disposed interpreter.","refs":["CH_MHON_T_18:A15","CH_MHON_T_18:D8"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[]},{"id":"CL_MHON_08:GA-08","text":"[Terminal] The inexhaustibility of the MONAD prevents interpretive closure and reduces every conclusion to the principle of further extraction.","refs":["CH_MHON_T_17:NX7","CH_MHON_T_18:A1"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[]}],"original_name":"Global Load-Bearing Axioms"},{"number":"3","name":"Recurrence, Mutation, and Displacement Matrix","items":[{"id":"CL_MHON_08:RMD-01","text":"[Functional return] The straight line, circle, and semicircle recur, but migrate from signs of local state to forms of symmetry, vessels, and operations.","refs":["CH_MHON_T_17:A4","CH_MHON_T_18:A6"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[]},{"id":"CL_MHON_08:RMD-02","text":"[Transformed] Dissection passes from the anatomical distinction of parts to the discovery of a repertoire from which instruments and new connections are composed.","refs":["CH_MHON_T_17:AS6","CH_MHON_T_18:D1"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[]},{"id":"CL_MHON_08:RMD-03","text":"[Inversion] Artificiality, which earlier indicates the lack of natural solarity, later becomes an effective mode of translation into vessels and operations.","refs":["CH_MHON_T_17:AS5","CH_MHON_T_18:D3"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[]},{"id":"CL_MHON_08:RMD-04","text":"[Absorption] Unity changes from sympathy after dissection into a generative microcosm, in which formal, material, and cosmological differences are referred to the same principle.","refs":["CH_MHON_T_17:SD4","CH_MHON_T_18:SD1"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[]},{"id":"CL_MHON_08:RMD-05","text":"[Mutating persistence] Mystery persists, but is no longer merely an unknown remainder; it becomes the rule that reopens the series of figures, operations, and interpretations.","refs":["CH_MHON_T_17:SNX2","CH_MHON_T_18:DNX5"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[]}],"original_name":"Recurrence, Mutation, and Displacement Matrix"},{"number":"4","name":"Lower-Layer vs Current-Level Distinction","items":[{"id":"CL_MHON_08:CGD-01","text":"The particular attributions of B, D, and C to local inferiority, stability, or indeterminacy remain at the lower level; at the level of the present group, only the rule of the relation between position, measure, and state is retained.","refs":["CH_MHON_T_17:AS3","CH_MHON_T_17:A9"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[]},{"id":"CL_MHON_08:CGD-02","text":"The names of individual letters and vessels are local; the higher structure retains metathesis as the general rule by which the same elements assume different functions.","refs":["CH_MHON_T_18:A5","CH_MHON_T_18:A7"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[]},{"id":"CL_MHON_08:CGD-03","text":"The distinction between nature and art is expanded: it is no longer asked only whether a figure possesses solarity, but whether an operation merely represents form or sustains the conditions of material efficacy.","refs":["CH_MHON_T_17:SNX1","CH_MHON_T_18:D5"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[]},{"id":"CL_MHON_08:CGD-04","text":"The discrimination of parts is amplified upward, because analysis not only separates properties but produces the material of recomposition and monadic relation.","refs":["CH_MHON_T_17:A10","CH_MHON_T_18:D1"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[]},{"id":"CL_MHON_08:CGD-05","text":"Visual recognition alone is surpassed; valid reading now requires correct operation, material condition, and degree of initiation.","refs":["CH_MHON_T_17:ALT3","CH_MHON_T_18:DNX6"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[]}],"original_name":"Lower-Layer vs Current-Level Distinction"},{"number":"5","name":"Global Apophatic Structure","items":[{"id":"CL_MHON_08:GNX-01","text":"The system denies an immediate identity between visible form, natural essence, and the thing signified.","refs":["CH_MHON_T_17:SNX3","CH_MHON_T_18:SNX2"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[]},{"id":"CL_MHON_08:GNX-02","text":"The system denies that the geometrical possibility of connection suffices for substantial unity or natural perfection.","refs":["CH_MHON_T_17:DNX2","CH_MHON_T_17:SNX1"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[]},{"id":"CL_MHON_08:GNX-03","text":"The system denies both a wholly external cause and a wholly extrinsic observer, because understanding requires participation in the monadic-operative order.","refs":["CH_MHON_T_18:DNX1","CH_MHON_T_18:SNX1"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[]},{"id":"CL_MHON_08:GNX-04","text":"The system denies equally public revelation and definitively exhaustive interpretation; concealment and inexhaustibility are constitutive limits.","refs":["CH_MHON_T_17:SNX2","CH_MHON_T_18:SNX5"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[]}],"original_name":"Global Apophatic Structure"},{"number":"6","name":"Deep Book Apophatics","items":[{"id":"CL_MHON_08:DBN-01","text":"It must be false that dissection dissolves parts into autonomous substances, because the system remains only if difference retains the relation of common origin.","refs":["CH_MHON_T_17:DNX4","CH_MHON_T_18:DNX4"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[]},{"id":"CL_MHON_08:DBN-02","text":"It must be false that the symbol is neutral or that predicates of vitality properly follow from the figure's appearance alone.","refs":["CH_MHON_T_17:DNX3","CH_MHON_T_18:DNX2"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[]},{"id":"CL_MHON_08:DBN-03","text":"It must be false that an artificial effect is identical with ontological transformation; otherwise the distinction between potentiality, composition, and perfection would collapse.","refs":["CH_MHON_T_17:SD1","CH_MHON_T_17:A8"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[]},{"id":"CL_MHON_08:DBN-04","text":"It must be false that matter and environment are indifferent adjuncts, because the failure or efficacy of an operation depends on physical conditions.","refs":["CH_MHON_T_18:SNX3","CH_MHON_T_18:D5"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[]}],"original_name":"Deep Book Apophatics"},{"number":"7","name":"Super-Apophatics of the Whole","items":[{"id":"CL_MHON_08:BSA-01","text":"An observer who possesses the whole system without initiation is inadmissible, because the interpreter's disposition is part of the mechanism of proof.","refs":["CH_MHON_T_18:SNX1","CH_MHON_T_18:D8"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[]},{"id":"CL_MHON_08:BSA-02","text":"The full coincidence of figure, essence, and operation is inadmissible, because form can assume an artificial function without possessing natural perfection.","refs":["CH_MHON_T_17:NX2","CH_MHON_T_17:A8"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[]},{"id":"CL_MHON_08:BSA-03","text":"An immobile and undivided unity is inadmissible, because the MONAD retains its unity not before difference but through difference and reunion.","refs":["CH_MHON_T_17:AS6","CH_MHON_T_17:A11"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[]}],"original_name":"Super-Apophatics of the Whole"},{"number":"8","name":"Cross-Unit Derivative Propositions","items":[{"id":"CL_MHON_08:XD-01","text":"The dissolution of connections becomes productive because the parts, retaining their common origin, simultaneously constitute an inventory of recomposition and monadic relation.","refs":["CL_MHON_08:GA-01","CL_MHON_08:GA-04","CH_MHON_T_17:A10","CH_MHON_T_18:D1"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"Composite","passages":[]},{"id":"CL_MHON_08:XD-02","text":"Metathesis can alter the function of a figure, but cannot produce, from artificial composition alone, a natural essence already possessed.","refs":["CL_MHON_08:GA-02","CL_MHON_08:GA-03","CL_MHON_08:GA-05","CH_MHON_T_17:A8","CH_MHON_T_18:D2"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"Composite","passages":[]},{"id":"CL_MHON_08:XD-03","text":"The external environment becomes an internal limit of operation: air and wind determine efficacy, while their exclusion creates a tension with monadic closure.","refs":["CL_MHON_08:GA-06","CH_MHON_T_18:A11","CH_MHON_T_18:NX4"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"Tension","passages":[]},{"id":"CL_MHON_08:XD-04","text":"The incompletion of B and initiatory progression together bring it about that knowledge moves not by a leap to fullness, but through degrees, specimens, and preparations.","refs":["CL_MHON_08:GA-05","CL_MHON_08:GA-07","CH_MHON_T_17:D2","CH_MHON_T_18:D4"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"Composite","passages":[]},{"id":"CL_MHON_08:XD-05","text":"A sign is converted into apodixis not because the figure suffices in itself, but because analogical interpretation passes into correct operation and a suitably disposed subject.","refs":["CL_MHON_08:GA-03","CL_MHON_08:GA-06","CL_MHON_08:GA-07","CH_MHON_T_18:D8","CH_MHON_T_18:DNX6"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"Composite","passages":[]}],"original_name":"Cross-Unit Derivative Propositions"},{"number":"9","name":"Book-Scale Super-Derivatives","items":[{"id":"CL_MHON_08:BSD-01","text":"The reality of the system is not an aggregate of discrete things, but a field of relations in which unity is generated through differentiation, translation, and recomposition.","refs":["CL_MHON_08:GA-01","CL_MHON_08:GA-04","CH_MHON_T_17:SD4","CH_MHON_T_18:SD3"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"From","passages":[]},{"id":"CL_MHON_08:BSD-02","text":"The authority of knowledge is transferred from commonly external verification to congruence between sign, apparatus, operation, and the disposition of the initiate.","refs":["CL_MHON_08:GA-06","CL_MHON_08:GA-07","CH_MHON_T_18:SD6","CH_MHON_T_18:D8"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"From","passages":[]},{"id":"CL_MHON_08:BSD-03","text":"Time is not linearly cumulative progress, but a repeated interval between potentiality, composition, correction, and inexhaustibility.","refs":["CL_MHON_08:GA-05","CL_MHON_08:GA-08","CH_MHON_T_17:D3","CH_MHON_T_18:A12"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"From","passages":[]},{"id":"CL_MHON_08:BSD-04","text":"Art is reclassified as a limited discipline: it does not create fullness from nothing, but orders uncomposed potentialities under material and symbolic conditions.","refs":["CL_MHON_08:GA-05","CL_MHON_08:GA-06","CH_MHON_T_17:SD1","CH_MHON_T_18:SD2"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"From","passages":[]}],"original_name":"Book-Scale Super-Derivatives"},{"number":"10","name":"Recursive Loops, Escalation Paths, and Conceptual Attractors","items":[{"id":"CL_MHON_08:RLE-01","text":"The primary circuit is dissection → marking → recomposition → recognition of common origin → new dissection.","refs":["CH_MHON_T_17:DNX4","CH_MHON_T_18:D1"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[]},{"id":"CL_MHON_08:RLE-02","text":"The path of amplification is figured potentiality → artificial connection → distinction of effect from natural perfection → more accurate reading of potentiality.","refs":["CH_MHON_T_17:NX3","CH_MHON_T_18:D2"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[]},{"id":"CL_MHON_08:RLE-03","text":"The initiatory circuit is secret → specimen → subtler preparation → restricted access to further secrets.","refs":["CH_MHON_T_18:A2","CH_MHON_T_18:A10"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[]},{"id":"CL_MHON_08:RLE-04","text":"Two attractors order the system: monadic union draws differences back to origin, while inexhaustibility withdraws origin from every interpretive saturation.","refs":["CH_MHON_T_17:A11","CH_MHON_T_18:A1"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[]}],"original_name":"Recursive Loops, Escalation Paths, and Conceptual Attractors"},{"number":"11","name":"Formal Generative Schema of the Whole Book","items":[{"id":"CL_MHON_08:FGS-B01","text":"Change or inexhaustibility of the MONAD → dissolution of body or connection → distinction of markable parts.","refs":["CH_MHON_T_17:AS1","CH_MHON_T_18:A3"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[]},{"id":"CL_MHON_08:FGS-B02","text":"Distinct parts → reading of position, figure, and proportion → separation of natural, analogical, and artificial state.","refs":["CH_MHON_T_17:AS2","CH_MHON_T_17:DNX1"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[]},{"id":"CL_MHON_08:FGS-B03","text":"Marked parts → local metathesis → translation between letter, figure, vessel, and instrument.","refs":["CH_MHON_T_18:D1","CH_MHON_T_18:D3"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[]},{"id":"CL_MHON_08:FGS-B04","text":"Artificial composition → conditioned operative effect → prohibition against taking the effect for natural perfection.","refs":["CH_MHON_T_17:A8","CH_MHON_T_18:D5"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[]},{"id":"CL_MHON_08:FGS-B05","text":"Correct operation performed by the initiate → the sign passes into apodixis → monadic union is restored without exhausting the mystery.","refs":["CH_MHON_T_18:D8","CH_MHON_T_17:NX7"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[]}],"original_name":"Formal Generative Schema of the Whole Book"},{"number":"12","name":"Whole-Book Argument Flow Map","items":[{"id":"CL_MHON_08:AFM-B01","text":"Local change and the inexhaustibility of the MONAD → question concerning the differentiability of a single principle.","refs":["CH_MHON_T_17:AS1","CH_MHON_T_18:A1"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[]},{"id":"CL_MHON_08:AFM-B02","text":"Dissolution of connection → marking of parts → classification by position, figure, and relation.","refs":["CH_MHON_T_18:A3","CH_MHON_T_17:A1"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[]},{"id":"CL_MHON_08:AFM-B03","text":"Figural classification → distinction of nature, analogy, and art → refusal of immediately given perfection.","refs":["CH_MHON_T_17:AS4","CH_MHON_T_17:A8"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[]},{"id":"CL_MHON_08:AFM-B04","text":"Metathesis of parts → composition of vessels and instruments → translation of figure into material operation.","refs":["CH_MHON_T_18:A5","CH_MHON_T_18:D3"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[]},{"id":"CL_MHON_08:AFM-B05","text":"Conditioned and initiatory operation → selective apodixis → return to inexhausted mystery.","refs":["CH_MHON_T_18:D8","CH_MHON_T_18:A2"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[]},{"id":"CL_MHON_08:AFM-B06","text":"Explicated difference → sympathy and monadic union → opening to further mysteries.","refs":["CH_MHON_T_17:A11","CH_MHON_T_17:NX7"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[]}],"original_name":"Whole-Book Argument Flow Map"},{"number":"13","name":"Macro Map-Effects","items":[{"id":"CL_MHON_08:MME-01","text":"The MONAD is reclassified from a simple sign into an internal machine capable of generating forms, parts, instruments, and relations.","refs":["CH_MHON_T_17:ME1","CH_MHON_T_18:ME1"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[]},{"id":"CL_MHON_08:MME-02","text":"The opposition between unity and distinction collapses, because discrimination does not oppose unity but constitutes its operative mode.","refs":["CH_MHON_T_17:ME4","CH_MHON_T_18:SD3"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[]},{"id":"CL_MHON_08:MME-03","text":"The opposition between mystical and practical falls away, because material art becomes a path of mystical knowledge without reducing mystery to common use.","refs":["CH_MHON_T_17:ME2","CH_MHON_T_18:ME3"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[]},{"id":"CL_MHON_08:MME-04","text":"The field of knowledge is described as a scale of initiation, in which concealment, specimen, preparation, and apodixis distribute different capacities for interpretation.","refs":["CH_MHON_T_18:ME4","CH_MHON_T_18:SNX5"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[]},{"id":"CL_MHON_08:MME-05","text":"Time and the physical environment are reclassified as constitutive conditions of operation, not as subsequent or external circumstances.","refs":["CH_MHON_T_18:ME5","CH_MHON_T_18:D5"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[]}],"original_name":"Macro Map-Effects"},{"number":"14","name":"Altitude Integration","items":[{"id":"CL_MHON_08:ALT-B01","text":"At the surface level, the members B, D, and C and the letters α, λ, δ, and ω appear as distinct figures, vessels, and signs.","refs":["CH_MHON_T_17:ALT1","CH_MHON_T_18:ALT1"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[]},{"id":"CL_MHON_08:ALT-B02","text":"At the structural level, figure, direction, proportion, dissolution of connection, and metathesis constitute the rules of differentiation and recomposition.","refs":["CH_MHON_T_17:ALT2","CH_MHON_T_18:ALT2"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[]},{"id":"CL_MHON_08:ALT-B03","text":"At the metastrategic level, interpretation changes from inspection to the governance of access, operation, and apodictic capacity.","refs":["CH_MHON_T_17:ALT3","CH_MHON_T_18:ALT3"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[]},{"id":"CL_MHON_08:ALT-B04","text":"At the ontological-temporal level, being and living are initial degrees, while the MONAD becomes an internal field where body, world, hours, and transformation are connected.","refs":["CH_MHON_T_17:ALT4","CH_MHON_T_18:ALT4"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[]},{"id":"CL_MHON_08:ALT-B05","text":"At the methodological-performative level, the form of the system accomplishes its thesis: it not only expounds signs, but through correct operation disposes toward an apodictic state.","refs":["CH_MHON_T_18:SD2","CH_MHON_T_18:D8"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[]}],"original_name":"Altitude Integration"},{"number":"15","name":"Internal Tensions, Mutation Points, and Fault Lines","items":[{"id":"CL_MHON_08:FL-01","text":"A tension is constituted by the fact that art can effectively compose forms, but the transition from figural efficacy to natural perfection or to material force is not fully demonstrated.","refs":["CH_MHON_T_17:A8","CH_MHON_T_18:MRT4"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[]},{"id":"CL_MHON_08:FL-02","text":"Monadic closure and the condition of external air are not perfectly reconciled: the MONAD is said to admit nothing outside itself, but the function of λ is affected by the external environment.","refs":["CH_MHON_T_18:NX4","CH_MHON_T_18:A11"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[]},{"id":"CL_MHON_08:MP-01","text":"The point of mutation is where the anatomy of the MONAD ceases to be merely interpretive discrimination and becomes an apparatus for composing vessels and functions.","refs":["CH_MHON_T_17:D6","CH_MHON_T_18:D1"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[]},{"id":"CL_MHON_08:MP-02","text":"The point of mutation is where the imperfection of B remains not merely a figural state, but produces a schema of initiatory and operative progression.","refs":["CH_MHON_T_17:D2","CH_MHON_T_18:D4"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[]}],"original_name":"Internal Tensions, Mutation Points, and Fault Lines"},{"number":"16","name":"Hidden System Image","items":[{"id":"CL_MHON_08:HSI-01","text":"Reality is implicitly figured as a monadic field that unfolds itself into differences, reorders them through operation, and is never dissolved into a collection of independent things.","refs":["CH_MHON_T_18:SD1","CH_MHON_T_17:SD4"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[]},{"id":"CL_MHON_08:HSI-02","text":"The subject implicitly becomes an initiated operator, who not only contemplates the figure but correctly administers conditions, apparatuses, and the order of signs.","refs":["CH_MHON_T_18:SD2","CH_MHON_T_18:A16"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[]},{"id":"CL_MHON_08:HSI-03","text":"Time is implicitly an iterable field of time-hours, in which fullness is deferred, operation returns, and mystery remains inexhausted.","refs":["CH_MHON_T_18:A12","CH_MHON_T_18:A1"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[]}],"original_name":"Hidden System Image"},{"number":"17","name":"Minimal Whole-Book Reconstruction Test","items":[{"id":"CL_MHON_08:MRT-B01","text":"Axioms concerning unity of relation, figural difference, the distinction of nature and art, initiatory operation, and inexhaustibility suffice to reconstruct the machine of this group.","refs":["CH_MHON_T_17:MRT1","CH_MHON_T_18:MRT1"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[]},{"id":"CL_MHON_08:MRT-B02","text":"The firmest structure is where dissection, metathesis, symmetry, material condition, and monadic relation are compressed into a single inferential series.","refs":["CH_MHON_T_17:MRT2","CH_MHON_T_18:MRT2"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[]},{"id":"CL_MHON_08:MRT-B03","text":"The reconstruction does not determine all geometry or hidden procedures, but preserves the tension between figure, efficacy, secrecy, and inexhaustibility as a condition of operation.","refs":["CH_MHON_T_18:MRT3","CH_MHON_T_18:MRT7"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[]}],"original_name":"Minimal Whole-Book Reconstruction Test"}],"revision":"CL_MHON_08@R001","source_variant_id":"canonical:CL_MHON_08:v1","source_language_code":"la","original_title":"Chapters 22–23"}],"identity":{"layer":"CL","kicker":"Cluster · Monas Hieroglyphica","title":"Chapters 22–23","context":"Theorem Xxi. · Theorem Xxii.","original_title":"Chapters 22–23","original_context":"Theor. Xxi. · Theor. Xxii."},"original_title":"Chapters 22–23","title_language_code":"en"},{"id":"CL_MHON_09","layer":"CL","title":"Chapters 24–25","order":9,"parents":["CH_MHON_T_19","CH_MHON_T_20"],"revision":"CL_MHON_09@R001","sha256":"c386ef2dfb14fb0f46a2b1b94d1788267ef635ececa2cc405674c3953a47a1eb","sections":[{"number":"1","name":"Book-Scale Argumental Spine","items":[{"id":"CL_MHON_09:AS-B01","text":"Quaestio huius gregis est quomodo figura Monadis ex commensurationibus geometricis constituatur ita ut idem corpus signi ordinem elementorum, numerorum et rerum creatarum ferre possit.","refs":["CH_MHON_T_19:AS1","CH_MHON_T_20:AS1"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[]},{"id":"CL_MHON_09:AS-B02","text":"Thesis globalis ponit geometriam non ut imaginem inertem, sed ut apparatum transpositionis quo heterogeneitas elementaris in ordinem numeralem et reditum cosmicum convertitur.","refs":["CH_MHON_T_19:SD1","CH_MHON_T_20:SD1"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[]},{"id":"CL_MHON_09:AS-B03","text":"Argumentatio fit constructiva, numerologico-metaphysica et analogica: proportio geometrica auctoritatem acquirit dum in numeros, tempora, scripturam et ordinem cultualem transfertur.","refs":["CH_MHON_T_19:AS2","CH_MHON_T_20:SD3"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[]},{"id":"CL_MHON_09:AS-B04","text":"Motus a quaternario mensurali ad numerum XXIV non simpliciter incrementum est, sed mutatio qua limes causalitatis creatae in formam completionis circularis recodificatur.","refs":["CH_MHON_T_19:D6","CH_MHON_T_20:D3"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[]},{"id":"CL_MHON_09:AS-B05","text":"Motus systematis est spiraliter recursivus: inaequalitas inducit artem ordinantem, ars producit mensuram, mensura producit numerum, numerus autem figuram ad originem et reditum refert.","refs":["CH_MHON_T_19:AS6","CH_MHON_T_20:SD1"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[]},{"id":"CL_MHON_09:AS-B06","text":"Clausura est duplex: creaturae motus intra limites potestatis creatae sistitur, sed eadem limitatio in ordine circulari ad voluntatem creatoris reditum significat.","refs":["CH_MHON_T_19:DNX4","CH_MHON_T_20:SD4"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[]},{"id":"CL_MHON_09:AS-B07","text":"Systema disciplinam hieroglyphicam stabilire conatur in qua figura recte consignata non solum mysterium exhibet, sed ipsum ordinem transmissibilem efficit.","refs":["CH_MHON_T_19:SD4","CH_MHON_T_20:D5"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[]}],"original_name":"Book-Scale Argumental Spine"},{"number":"2","name":"Global Load-Bearing Axioms","items":[{"id":"CL_MHON_09:GA-01","text":"[Persistent] Ars ordinans naturam inaequalem non abolit, sed eam in relationes aequales et tractabiles redigit.","refs":["CH_MHON_T_19:A3","CH_MHON_T_19:SD2"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[]},{"id":"CL_MHON_09:GA-02","text":"[Persistent] Veritas figurae in commensurationibus invariantibus residet, non in ornamentis, litteris contingentibus aut mera figurae specie.","refs":["CH_MHON_T_19:A5","CH_MHON_T_19:SNX3"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[]},{"id":"CL_MHON_09:GA-03","text":"[Mutating] Quaternarius est operator structurae, qui a partitione linearum ad permutationem, mensuram et ordinationem rerum migrat.","refs":["CH_MHON_T_19:A9","CH_MHON_T_20:A5"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[]},{"id":"CL_MHON_09:GA-04","text":"[Persistent] Potestas creata plenissime exercita non potest Monadem vel ordinem creatum in motum indefinitum transducere.","refs":["CH_MHON_T_19:NX7","CH_MHON_T_19:SNX1"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[]},{"id":"CL_MHON_09:GA-05","text":"[Emergent] Clausura systematis non est mera cessatio, quia effusio a puncto orta per lineam et circulum in reditum ordinatum comprehenditur.","refs":["CH_MHON_T_20:AS2","CH_MHON_T_20:A3"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[]},{"id":"CL_MHON_09:GA-06","text":"[Mutating] Numerus XXIV quaternarium non substituit simpliciter, sed eius vim metamorphicam temporalem, cosmicam et apocalypticam extendit.","refs":["CH_MHON_T_20:AS4","CH_MHON_T_20:SD2"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[]},{"id":"CL_MHON_09:GA-07","text":"[Mutating] Figura, numerus et scriptura unum actum consignationis constituunt, quo mysterium formae transmissibile fit sine reductione ad visibilem superficiem.","refs":["CH_MHON_T_20:A10","CH_MHON_T_20:D5"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[]},{"id":"CL_MHON_09:GA-08","text":"[Terminal] Ordinatio rerum creatarum ultimam rationem non in seipsa, sed in voluntate creatoris et in relatione ad gloriam divinam recipit.","refs":["CH_MHON_T_20:A8","CH_MHON_T_20:SNX3"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[]},{"id":"CL_MHON_09:GA-09","text":"[Attractor] Relatio invisibilis, sive mensura sive centrum sive proportio, apparentiam figurae dirigit et eius necessitatem constituit.","refs":["CH_MHON_T_19:NX3","CH_MHON_T_19:SNX3"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[]},{"id":"CL_MHON_09:GA-10","text":"[Attractor] Cognitio valida postulat intellectum qui figuram simul geometricam, numericam et symbolicam legere potest, dum visus vulgaris in superficie deficit.","refs":["CH_MHON_T_20:NX3","CH_MHON_T_20:D6"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[]}],"original_name":"Global Load-Bearing Axioms"},{"number":"3","name":"Recurrence, Mutation, and Displacement Matrix","items":[{"id":"CL_MHON_09:RMD-01","text":"[Identitas stabilis] Mensura communis persistit ut principium constructionis, sed transit a modulo locali figurae ad condicionem universalem ordinis demonstrabilis.","refs":["CH_MHON_T_19:A5","CH_MHON_T_20:A1"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[]},{"id":"CL_MHON_09:RMD-02","text":"[Mutatio functionalis] Quaternarius redit non ut idem numerus linearum, sed ut structura cuius metamorphosis per XXIV completio temporalis et cosmica declaratur.","refs":["CH_MHON_T_19:A9","CH_MHON_T_20:A5"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[]},{"id":"CL_MHON_09:RMD-03","text":"[Absorptio] Aequalitas artificialis, quae prius naturae inaequalitatem corrigit, in clausura circulari absorbetur ut effectus completionis potius quam oppositio simplicis.","refs":["CH_MHON_T_19:SD2","CH_MHON_T_20:NX1"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[]},{"id":"CL_MHON_09:RMD-04","text":"[Amplificatio] Distinctio inter visibile et necessarium a centro occulto et ornamento contingente ad discrimen inter sensum vulgarem et intellectum symbolicum extenditur.","refs":["CH_MHON_T_19:SNX3","CH_MHON_T_20:D6"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[]},{"id":"CL_MHON_09:RMD-05","text":"[Tensio] Metathesis combinatoria et metamorphosis quaternaria iuxtaponuntur ut momenta transformationis, sed regula quae unam operationem in alteram convertat non explicatur.","refs":["CH_MHON_T_19:D5","CH_MHON_T_20:D3"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[]},{"id":"CL_MHON_09:RMD-06","text":"[Amplificatio] Veritas symmetriarum, prius in tempore ulterius manifestanda, ad explicitam relationem universi ad voluntatem Dei elevatur.","refs":["CH_MHON_T_19:SNX2","CH_MHON_T_20:A8"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[]}],"original_name":"Recurrence, Mutation, and Displacement Matrix"},{"number":"4","name":"Lower-Layer vs Current-Level Distinction","items":[{"id":"CL_MHON_09:CGD-01","text":"Relationes AB, AC, AD, AE et DE manent machinae locales calibrationis geometricae; in gradu gregis valent tantum ut exemplar relationis invariantium ad figuram manifestam.","refs":["CH_MHON_T_19:A6","CH_MHON_T_19:MRT2"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[]},{"id":"CL_MHON_09:CGD-02","text":"Circuitus aequinoctialis et figurae apocalypticae sunt referentiae locales; structura superior non in ipsis consistit, sed in functione qua circulus, tempus et cultus idem schema completionis portant.","refs":["CH_MHON_T_20:AS3","CH_MHON_T_20:A7"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[]},{"id":"CL_MHON_09:CGD-03","text":"Multiplicatio numerorum, si sola arithmetica manet, localis et insufficiens est; ad gradum praesentem ascendit solum cum numerus formam transformationis determinat.","refs":["CH_MHON_T_19:NX5","CH_MHON_T_20:AS4"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[]},{"id":"CL_MHON_09:CGD-04","text":"Distinctio inter symmetrias necessarias et ornamenta contingenta desinit esse regula artis localis et fit norma generalis qua figura, doctrina et transmissio discernuntur.","refs":["CH_MHON_T_19:A7","CH_MHON_T_20:NX4"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[]},{"id":"CL_MHON_09:CGD-05","text":"Terminatio motus non evanescit, sed recodificatur: quod localiter est imminutio impulsus creati, globaliter fit clausura circularis sub ordine divino.","refs":["CH_MHON_T_19:NX7","CH_MHON_T_20:ME5"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[]}],"original_name":"Lower-Layer vs Current-Level Distinction"},{"number":"5","name":"Global Apophatic Structure","items":[{"id":"CL_MHON_09:GNX-01","text":"Aequalitas naturalis rei creatae excluditur; aequalitas admissa est tantum ut effectus artis ordinantis.","refs":["CH_MHON_T_19:NX1","CH_MHON_T_19:A3"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"Negative","passages":[]},{"id":"CL_MHON_09:GNX-02","text":"Ornamentum, littera et centrum visibile excluduntur ut fundamenta ultimae necessitatis figurae.","refs":["CH_MHON_T_19:NX2","CH_MHON_T_19:NX3"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"Negative","passages":[]},{"id":"CL_MHON_09:GNX-03","text":"Numerus excluditur ut quantitas nuda, quia numerus legitimus debet structuram transformationis et ordinationis gerere.","refs":["CH_MHON_T_19:NX5","CH_MHON_T_20:NX2"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"Negative","passages":[]},{"id":"CL_MHON_09:GNX-04","text":"Progressus creatus autonomus excluditur, quia neque creatura neque causa influentialis sibi finem vel transitum ultra proprium ordinem praebet.","refs":["CH_MHON_T_19:NX7","CH_MHON_T_20:NX5"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"Negative","passages":[]},{"id":"CL_MHON_09:GNX-05","text":"Disiunctio absoluta principii, fluxus, finis et mysterii percipiendi excluditur; haec momenta tantum intra unam circulationem interpretari possunt.","refs":["CH_MHON_T_20:DNX1","CH_MHON_T_20:NX3"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"Negative","passages":[]}],"original_name":"Global Apophatic Structure"},{"number":"6","name":"Deep Book Apophatics","items":[{"id":"CL_MHON_09:DBN-01","text":"Systema corrueret si figura esset compositio libera signorum, quia proportiones antecedunt et determinant quid figura valida esse possit.","refs":["CH_MHON_T_19:DNX1","CH_MHON_T_19:A7"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"Negative","passages":[]},{"id":"CL_MHON_09:DBN-02","text":"Systema corrueret si pluralitas numerorum per se cognitionem augeret, quia numerus requirit internam vim illuminandi et transformandi.","refs":["CH_MHON_T_19:DNX3","CH_MHON_T_20:DNX2"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"Negative","passages":[]},{"id":"CL_MHON_09:DBN-03","text":"Systema corrueret si clausura vel progressus infinitus vel quies statica esset; clausura valida simul finem causalitatis et circuitum completionis continet.","refs":["CH_MHON_T_19:DNX4","CH_MHON_T_20:NX1"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"Negative","passages":[]},{"id":"CL_MHON_09:DBN-04","text":"Systema corrueret si mysterium ad perceptionem communem aut ad litteram sine interpretatione reduceretur.","refs":["CH_MHON_T_20:DNX3","CH_MHON_T_20:D6"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"Negative","passages":[]}],"original_name":"Deep Book Apophatics"},{"number":"7","name":"Super-Apophatics of the Whole","items":[{"id":"CL_MHON_09:BSA-01","text":"Possibilitas causalitatis creatae absolutae est indisponibilis: omne agens creatum intra mensuram, terminum et relationem superiorem manet.","refs":["CH_MHON_T_19:SNX1","CH_MHON_T_19:NX7"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"Negative","passages":[]},{"id":"CL_MHON_09:BSA-02","text":"Perspectiva quae veritatem figurae in sola visibilitate possideret est inadmissibilis, quia necessitas in relationibus occultis et intellectu non-vulgari collocatur.","refs":["CH_MHON_T_19:SNX3","CH_MHON_T_20:NX3"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"Negative","passages":[]},{"id":"CL_MHON_09:BSA-03","text":"Finis rerum extra creationem, adorationem et voluntatem divinam systemati non patet.","refs":["CH_MHON_T_20:SNX3","CH_MHON_T_20:A8"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"Negative","passages":[]},{"id":"CL_MHON_09:BSA-04","text":"Translatio a proportione geometrica ad auctoritatem cosmicam vel scripturalem non recipit regulam formalem plene explicitam; haec absentia manet limes internus systematis.","refs":["CH_MHON_T_19:MRT3","CH_MHON_T_20:MRT5"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"Tension","passages":[]}],"original_name":"Super-Apophatics of the Whole"},{"number":"8","name":"Cross-Unit Derivative Propositions","items":[{"id":"CL_MHON_09:XD-01","text":"Forma geometrica fit medium operationis: heterogeneitatem naturalem corrigit et eandem correctionem in cursum emanationis, completionis et reditus transfert.","refs":["CL_MHON_09:GA-01","CL_MHON_09:GA-02","CL_MHON_09:GA-05","CH_MHON_T_19:D1","CH_MHON_T_20:SD1"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"Composite","passages":[]},{"id":"CL_MHON_09:XD-02","text":"Numerus non solum ordinem describit, sed transitum licitum constituit: quaternarius permutativus fit XXIV metamorphicus, quo mutatio ipsa mensuratur.","refs":["CL_MHON_09:GA-03","CL_MHON_09:GA-06","CH_MHON_T_19:SD3","CH_MHON_T_20:D3"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"Composite","passages":[]},{"id":"CL_MHON_09:XD-03","text":"Discrimen inter invariantem figuram et superficiem variabilem producitur etiam ut discrimen inter intellectum ordinatum et visum vulgarem.","refs":["CL_MHON_09:GA-02","CL_MHON_09:GA-07","CL_MHON_09:GA-09","CL_MHON_09:GA-10","CH_MHON_T_19:ME5","CH_MHON_T_20:D6"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"Composite","passages":[]},{"id":"CL_MHON_09:XD-04","text":"Limes potentiae creatae mutatur in condicionem reditus: circulus non est motus sui iuris, sed clausura quae ordinem creatum ad voluntatem divinam refert.","refs":["CL_MHON_09:GA-04","CL_MHON_09:GA-05","CL_MHON_09:GA-08","CH_MHON_T_19:D6","CH_MHON_T_20:SD4"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"Composite","passages":[]},{"id":"CL_MHON_09:XD-05","text":"Nexus quaternarii cum XXIV efficit transitionem globaliter necessariam, sed formaliter incompletam, quia analogia geometrica et auctoritas apocalyptica sine regula conversionis demonstrata copulantur.","refs":["CL_MHON_09:GA-03","CL_MHON_09:GA-06","CH_MHON_T_19:MRT3","CH_MHON_T_20:MRT5"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"Tension","passages":[]}],"original_name":"Cross-Unit Derivative Propositions"},{"number":"9","name":"Book-Scale Super-Derivatives","items":[{"id":"CL_MHON_09:BSD-01","text":"Geometria reclassificatur ut grammatica inter campos: eadem operatio relationes elementares, corporis figuram, numeros, tempora et ordinem cultualem componit.","refs":["CH_MHON_T_19:SD1","CH_MHON_T_20:SD2"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[]},{"id":"CL_MHON_09:BSD-02","text":"Tempus non figuratur ut series aperta, sed ut circulatio finita in qua completio horaria et limes motus creati mutuo se determinant.","refs":["CH_MHON_T_19:DNX4","CH_MHON_T_20:D2"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[]},{"id":"CL_MHON_09:BSD-03","text":"Subiectum humanum neque dominus causalis neque spectator passivus fit, sed artifex-interpres cui facultas ordinandi et consignandi intra datam auctoritatem conceditur.","refs":["CH_MHON_T_19:A3","CH_MHON_T_20:A9"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[]},{"id":"CL_MHON_09:BSD-04","text":"Potestas artis est realis sed derivata: naturam inaequalem ordinare potest, ordinem tamen creatum nec sibi fundamentum nec infinitam continuationem praebere potest.","refs":["CH_MHON_T_19:DNX2","CH_MHON_T_19:SNX1"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[]}],"original_name":"Book-Scale Super-Derivatives"},{"number":"10","name":"Recursive Loops, Escalation Paths, and Conceptual Attractors","items":[{"id":"CL_MHON_09:RLE-01","text":"Punctum monadicum effunditur in lineam, linea per extrema circumducitur in circulum, et circulus principium ut completionem reformat.","refs":["CH_MHON_T_20:AS2","CH_MHON_T_20:A3"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[]},{"id":"CL_MHON_09:RLE-02","text":"Inaequalitas elementaris → reductio artificialis → symmetria figurae → numerus ordinans → applicatio ad tempora et virtutes constituit viam amplificationis a materia ad cosmologiam.","refs":["CH_MHON_T_19:FGS1","CH_MHON_T_19:A10"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[]},{"id":"CL_MHON_09:RLE-03","text":"Proportio invisibilis → figura consignata → intellectus ordinatus → transmissio disciplinae → conservatio proportionis constituit circuitum epistemicum systematis.","refs":["CH_MHON_T_19:SD4","CH_MHON_T_20:D5"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[]},{"id":"CL_MHON_09:RLE-04","text":"Attractor systematis est relatio non-apparens, quae centrum, mensuram et necessitatem continet, dum ornamenta et aspectus superficies circa eam variantur.","refs":["CH_MHON_T_19:NX3","CH_MHON_T_19:SNX3"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[]},{"id":"CL_MHON_09:RLE-05","text":"Escalatio a quaternario ad XXIV simul attractor et commissura fragilis est: completionem maiorem promittit, sed mechanismum conversionis aperte non exhibet.","refs":["CH_MHON_T_19:MRT6","CH_MHON_T_20:MRT2"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[]}],"original_name":"Recursive Loops, Escalation Paths, and Conceptual Attractors"},{"number":"11","name":"Formal Generative Schema of the Whole Book","items":[{"id":"CL_MHON_09:FGS-B01","text":"Inaequalitas elementorum → reductio artificialis → aequalitas ordinata → corpus figurae commensuratum.","refs":["CH_MHON_T_19:FGS1","CH_MHON_T_19:A3"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[]},{"id":"CL_MHON_09:FGS-B02","text":"Modulus AB → proportiones duplices et triplae → invariantes geometricae → distinctio necessarii a contingenti.","refs":["CH_MHON_T_19:FGS2","CH_MHON_T_19:FGS5"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[]},{"id":"CL_MHON_09:FGS-B03","text":"Quaternarius geometricus → divisio mystica → permutatio et metathesis → numerus ut operator mensurae et transformationis.","refs":["CH_MHON_T_19:FGS3","CH_MHON_T_19:A8"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[]},{"id":"CL_MHON_09:FGS-B04","text":"Monas restituta → imminutio causalitatis creatae → terminus motus → negatio progressus infiniti.","refs":["CH_MHON_T_19:FGS4","CH_MHON_T_19:NX7"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[]},{"id":"CL_MHON_09:FGS-B05","text":"Punctum monadicum → effusio linearis → circumductio circularis → XXIV ut completio temporalis, cosmica et apocalyptica.","refs":["CH_MHON_T_20:FGS1","CH_MHON_T_20:FGS4"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[]},{"id":"CL_MHON_09:FGS-B06","text":"Forma geometrica + numerus transformativus + auctoritas symbolica → mysterium consignabile; translatio tamen inter haec ordines analogica manet, non plene formalizata.","refs":["CH_MHON_T_20:SD3","CH_MHON_T_20:MRT5"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[]}],"original_name":"Formal Generative Schema of the Whole Book"},{"number":"12","name":"Whole-Book Argument Flow Map","items":[{"id":"CL_MHON_09:AFM-B01","text":"Elementa inaequalia → ars aequans → crux commensurata → modulus relationum invariantium.","refs":["CH_MHON_T_19:AF1","CH_MHON_T_19:FGS2"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[]},{"id":"CL_MHON_09:AFM-B02","text":"Proportiones crucis → quaternarius linearum → divisio et metathesis → numerus applicabilis naturae, temporibus et gradibus.","refs":["CH_MHON_T_19:AF2","CH_MHON_T_19:AF3"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[]},{"id":"CL_MHON_09:AFM-B03","text":"Monas restituta → imminutio impulsus creati → clausura finita → recusatio progressus indefiniti.","refs":["CH_MHON_T_19:AF4","CH_MHON_T_19:DNX4"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[]},{"id":"CL_MHON_09:AFM-B04","text":"Punctum principiale → effusio linearis → circulus clausivus → XXIV metamorphicum → ordinatio omnium ad voluntatem divinam.","refs":["CH_MHON_T_20:AF1","CH_MHON_T_20:AF2"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[]}],"original_name":"Whole-Book Argument Flow Map"},{"number":"13","name":"Macro Map-Effects","items":[{"id":"CL_MHON_09:MME-01","text":"Figura geometrica reclassificatur ex signo discreto in tabulam ontologicam quae originem, processum et finem in una architectura refert.","refs":["CH_MHON_T_19:ME1","CH_MHON_T_20:ME1"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[]},{"id":"CL_MHON_09:MME-02","text":"Numerus reclassificatur ex enumeratione in matricem relationum, permutationum, temporum et completionum.","refs":["CH_MHON_T_19:ME3","CH_MHON_T_20:ME2"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[]},{"id":"CL_MHON_09:MME-03","text":"Oppositio inter figuram geometricam et symbolum religiosum solvitur, quia utrumque eandem ordinationem relationum invisibilium manifestat.","refs":["CH_MHON_T_19:ME5","CH_MHON_T_20:ME3"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[]},{"id":"CL_MHON_09:MME-04","text":"Lectio necessaria mutatur: oculus vulgaris non iam iudex primarius est, sed interpretatio geometrica, numerica et symbolica simul postulatur.","refs":["CH_MHON_T_20:ME4","CH_MHON_T_20:D6"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[]}],"original_name":"Macro Map-Effects"},{"number":"14","name":"Altitude Integration","items":[{"id":"CL_MHON_09:ALT-B01","text":"Gradus superficiei exhibet puncta, rectas, circulos, crucem, mensuram AB, quaternarium et numerum XXIV.","refs":["CH_MHON_T_19:ALT1","CH_MHON_T_20:ALT1"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[]},{"id":"CL_MHON_09:ALT-B02","text":"Gradus structuralis describit conversionem inaequalitatis in proportionem, proportionis in numerum, et numeri in ordinem transformationis.","refs":["CH_MHON_T_19:ALT2","CH_MHON_T_20:ALT2"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[]},{"id":"CL_MHON_09:ALT-B03","text":"Gradus metastrategicus disciplinam defendit per separationem invariantium a contingentibus et per displacementum lectoris a visu vulgari ad intellectum ordinatum.","refs":["CH_MHON_T_19:ALT3","CH_MHON_T_20:ALT3"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[]},{"id":"CL_MHON_09:ALT-B04","text":"Gradus ontologicus et temporalis res creatas intelligit ut inaequales, mensurabiles, finite mobiles et circulariter ad principium divinum relatas.","refs":["CH_MHON_T_19:ALT4","CH_MHON_T_20:ALT4"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[]},{"id":"CL_MHON_09:ALT-B05","text":"Gradus methodologicus et performativus systema implet per ipsam consignationem figurae, qua doctrina non solum exponitur sed in signo transmissibili stabilitur.","refs":["CH_MHON_T_19:SD4","CH_MHON_T_20:D5"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[]}],"original_name":"Altitude Integration"},{"number":"15","name":"Internal Tensions, Mutation Points, and Fault Lines","items":[{"id":"CL_MHON_09:FL-01","text":"Crux partes aequales et inaequales simul continere dicitur, dum aequalitas elementorum alibi ut effectus artis contra naturam inaequalem describitur.","refs":["CH_MHON_T_19:MRT4","CH_MHON_T_19:NX1"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"Tension","passages":[]},{"id":"CL_MHON_09:FL-02","text":"Transitus a proportione geometrica ad mensuras rerum, tempora, auctoritatem cosmicam et sensum scripturalem asseritur, sed regula formalis huius translationis non exhibetur.","refs":["CH_MHON_T_19:MRT3","CH_MHON_T_20:MRT5"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"Tension","passages":[]},{"id":"CL_MHON_09:FL-03","text":"Finis quaternarius vel denarius et completio quaternarii per XXIV simul necessarii ponuntur, quamvis nexus operativus utriusque clausurae incompletus permaneat.","refs":["CH_MHON_T_19:MRT6","CH_MHON_T_20:MRT2"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"Tension","passages":[]},{"id":"CL_MHON_09:MP-01","text":"Punctum mutationis primum est cum crux desinit esse signum religiosum vel ornamentale et fit instrumentum commensurationis elementaris et numericae.","refs":["CH_MHON_T_19:ME1","CH_MHON_T_19:D2"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"Composite","passages":[]},{"id":"CL_MHON_09:MP-02","text":"Punctum mutationis secundum est cum quaternarius a structura linearum ad formam metamorphosis per XXIV numerum transfertur.","refs":["CH_MHON_T_20:A5","CH_MHON_T_20:AS4"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"Tension","passages":[]},{"id":"CL_MHON_09:MP-03","text":"Punctum mutationis tertium est cum limes causalitatis creatae non solum motum sistit, sed clausuram in reditum circularem sub ordine divino mutat.","refs":["CH_MHON_T_19:DNX4","CH_MHON_T_20:ME5"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"Tension","passages":[]}],"original_name":"Internal Tensions, Mutation Points, and Fault Lines"},{"number":"16","name":"Hidden System Image","items":[{"id":"CL_MHON_09:HSI-01","text":"Realitas implicite imaginatur ut machina relationum quae heterogeneitatem materiei per mensuram, numerum et circulationem in ordinem finitum convertit.","refs":["CH_MHON_T_19:SD1","CH_MHON_T_19:FGS1"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[]},{"id":"CL_MHON_09:HSI-02","text":"Subiectum est artifex-interpres: inaequalitatem non creat, sed eam ordinat, figuram consignet et mysterium sine dominio absoluto tradit.","refs":["CH_MHON_T_19:DNX2","CH_MHON_T_20:A9"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[]},{"id":"CL_MHON_09:HSI-03","text":"Tempus est effusio cum reditu, non linea infinita: completio motum colligit sine eo quod creatura sibi ipsi originem aut finem sufficiens fiat.","refs":["CH_MHON_T_20:SD1","CH_MHON_T_19:DNX4"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[]}],"original_name":"Hidden System Image"},{"number":"17","name":"Minimal Whole-Book Reconstruction Test","items":[{"id":"CL_MHON_09:MRT-B01","text":"Restitutio sufficit si nexus inter inaequalitatem elementorum, artem aequantem, invariantes mensurales, quaternarium transformativum, limitem causalitatis et clausuram circularem servatur.","refs":["CH_MHON_T_19:MRT1","CH_MHON_T_20:MRT1"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[]},{"id":"CL_MHON_09:MRT-B02","text":"Structura firmissima consistit in concordantia inter constructionem geometricam, circulum temporalem et notionem completionis; ibi relationes explicitae mutuo se confirmant.","refs":["CH_MHON_T_19:MRT2","CH_MHON_T_20:MRT3"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[]},{"id":"CL_MHON_09:MRT-B03","text":"Quod reductioni axiomaticae maxime resistit est translatio a geometria ad cosmologiam et apocalypsim, necnon identificatio exacta limitis quaternarii cum metamorphosi XXIV.","refs":["CH_MHON_T_19:MRT3","CH_MHON_T_20:MRT2"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[]}],"original_name":"Minimal Whole-Book Reconstruction Test"}],"language_code":"la","language_name":"Latina","translations":[{"id":"CAREVAR_20261006T223506704115Z_c8d52d57","language_code":"en","language_name":"English","title":"Chapters 24–25","source_sha256":"c386ef2dfb14fb0f46a2b1b94d1788267ef635ececa2cc405674c3953a47a1eb","sha256":"1a48e85d58435753e097d62ffa547d2455828d951d5f2f289424085d38898885","rendition_type":"machine","review_status":"machine","model":"gpt-5.6-terra","created":"2026-10-06T22:35:06.706247+00:00","sections":[{"number":"1","name":"Book-Scale Argumental Spine","items":[{"id":"CL_MHON_09:AS-B01","text":"The question of this cluster is how the figure of the Monad is constituted from geometrical commensurations so that the same body of signs may bear the order of the elements, numbers, and created things.","refs":["CH_MHON_T_19:AS1","CH_MHON_T_20:AS1"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[]},{"id":"CL_MHON_09:AS-B02","text":"The global thesis posits geometry not as an inert image, but as an apparatus of transposition by which elemental heterogeneity is converted into numerical order and cosmic return.","refs":["CH_MHON_T_19:SD1","CH_MHON_T_20:SD1"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[]},{"id":"CL_MHON_09:AS-B03","text":"The argument proceeds constructively, numerologico-metaphysically, and analogically: geometrical proportion acquires authority while it is transferred into numbers, times, scripture, and cultic order.","refs":["CH_MHON_T_19:AS2","CH_MHON_T_20:SD3"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[]},{"id":"CL_MHON_09:AS-B04","text":"The movement from the mensural quaternary to the number XXIV is not simply an increase, but a change by which the limit of created causality is recodified into the form of circular completion.","refs":["CH_MHON_T_19:D6","CH_MHON_T_20:D3"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[]},{"id":"CL_MHON_09:AS-B05","text":"The movement of the system is spirally recursive: inequality induces ordering art, art produces measure, measure produces number, while number refers the figure to origin and return.","refs":["CH_MHON_T_19:AS6","CH_MHON_T_20:SD1"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[]},{"id":"CL_MHON_09:AS-B06","text":"The closure is twofold: the motion of creatures is arrested within the limits of created power, but that same limitation signifies return to the will of the creator in circular order.","refs":["CH_MHON_T_19:DNX4","CH_MHON_T_20:SD4"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[]},{"id":"CL_MHON_09:AS-B07","text":"The system attempts to establish a hieroglyphic discipline in which a rightly inscribed figure not only displays mystery, but makes transmissible order itself.","refs":["CH_MHON_T_19:SD4","CH_MHON_T_20:D5"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[]}],"original_name":"Book-Scale Argumental Spine"},{"number":"2","name":"Global Load-Bearing Axioms","items":[{"id":"CL_MHON_09:GA-01","text":"[Persistent] Ordering art does not abolish unequal nature, but reduces it to equal and manageable relations.","refs":["CH_MHON_T_19:A3","CH_MHON_T_19:SD2"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[]},{"id":"CL_MHON_09:GA-02","text":"[Persistent] The truth of the figure resides in invariant commensurations, not in ornaments, contingent letters, or the mere appearance of the figure.","refs":["CH_MHON_T_19:A5","CH_MHON_T_19:SNX3"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[]},{"id":"CL_MHON_09:GA-03","text":"[Mutating] The quaternary is an operator of structure, migrating from the partition of lines to permutation, measure, and the ordering of things.","refs":["CH_MHON_T_19:A9","CH_MHON_T_20:A5"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[]},{"id":"CL_MHON_09:GA-04","text":"[Persistent] Created power, even when exercised most fully, cannot lead the Monad or created order into indefinite motion.","refs":["CH_MHON_T_19:NX7","CH_MHON_T_19:SNX1"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[]},{"id":"CL_MHON_09:GA-05","text":"[Emergent] The closure of the system is not mere cessation, because the effusion originating from the point is comprehended through line and circle in ordered return.","refs":["CH_MHON_T_20:AS2","CH_MHON_T_20:A3"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[]},{"id":"CL_MHON_09:GA-06","text":"[Mutating] The number XXIV does not simply replace the quaternary, but extends its metamorphic force temporally, cosmically, and apocalyptically.","refs":["CH_MHON_T_20:AS4","CH_MHON_T_20:SD2"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[]},{"id":"CL_MHON_09:GA-07","text":"[Mutating] Figure, number, and writing constitute one act of inscription, by which the mystery of form becomes transmissible without reduction to visible surface.","refs":["CH_MHON_T_20:A10","CH_MHON_T_20:D5"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[]},{"id":"CL_MHON_09:GA-08","text":"[Terminal] The ordering of created things receives its ultimate rationale not in itself, but in the will of the creator and in relation to divine glory.","refs":["CH_MHON_T_20:A8","CH_MHON_T_20:SNX3"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[]},{"id":"CL_MHON_09:GA-09","text":"[Attractor] An invisible relation, whether measure, center, or proportion, directs the appearance of the figure and constitutes its necessity.","refs":["CH_MHON_T_19:NX3","CH_MHON_T_19:SNX3"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[]},{"id":"CL_MHON_09:GA-10","text":"[Attractor] Valid knowledge requires an intellect capable of reading the figure simultaneously geometrically, numerically, and symbolically, while ordinary sight fails at the surface.","refs":["CH_MHON_T_20:NX3","CH_MHON_T_20:D6"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[]}],"original_name":"Global Load-Bearing Axioms"},{"number":"3","name":"Recurrence, Mutation, and Displacement Matrix","items":[{"id":"CL_MHON_09:RMD-01","text":"[Stable identity] Common measure persists as a principle of construction, but passes from a local module of the figure to a universal condition of demonstrable order.","refs":["CH_MHON_T_19:A5","CH_MHON_T_20:A1"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[]},{"id":"CL_MHON_09:RMD-02","text":"[Functional mutation] The quaternary returns not as the same number of lines, but as a structure whose metamorphosis is declared through XXIV as temporal and cosmic completion.","refs":["CH_MHON_T_19:A9","CH_MHON_T_20:A5"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[]},{"id":"CL_MHON_09:RMD-03","text":"[Absorption] Artificial equality, which previously corrects the inequality of nature, is absorbed into circular closure as an effect of completion rather than a simple opposition.","refs":["CH_MHON_T_19:SD2","CH_MHON_T_20:NX1"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[]},{"id":"CL_MHON_09:RMD-04","text":"[Amplification] The distinction between the visible and the necessary is extended from the hidden center and contingent ornament to the distinction between ordinary sense and symbolic intellect.","refs":["CH_MHON_T_19:SNX3","CH_MHON_T_20:D6"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[]},{"id":"CL_MHON_09:RMD-05","text":"[Tension] Combinatorial metathesis and quaternary metamorphosis are juxtaposed as moments of transformation, but the rule converting one operation into the other is not explained.","refs":["CH_MHON_T_19:D5","CH_MHON_T_20:D3"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[]},{"id":"CL_MHON_09:RMD-06","text":"[Amplification] The truth of symmetries, formerly to be manifested further in time, is elevated to an explicit relation of the universe to the will of God.","refs":["CH_MHON_T_19:SNX2","CH_MHON_T_20:A8"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[]}],"original_name":"Recurrence, Mutation, and Displacement Matrix"},{"number":"4","name":"Lower-Layer vs Current-Level Distinction","items":[{"id":"CL_MHON_09:CGD-01","text":"The relations AB, AC, AD, AE and DE remain local mechanisms of geometrical calibration; at the level of the cluster they are valid only as an exemplar of the relation of invariants to manifest figure.","refs":["CH_MHON_T_19:A6","CH_MHON_T_19:MRT2"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[]},{"id":"CL_MHON_09:CGD-02","text":"The equinoctial circuit and apocalyptic figures are local referents; the higher structure does not consist in them themselves, but in the function by which circle, time, and cult bear the same schema of completion.","refs":["CH_MHON_T_20:AS3","CH_MHON_T_20:A7"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[]},{"id":"CL_MHON_09:CGD-03","text":"The multiplication of numbers, if it remains mere arithmetic, is local and insufficient; it rises to the present level only when number determines the form of transformation.","refs":["CH_MHON_T_19:NX5","CH_MHON_T_20:AS4"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[]},{"id":"CL_MHON_09:CGD-04","text":"The distinction between necessary symmetries and contingent ornaments ceases to be a rule of local art and becomes a general norm by which figure, doctrine, and transmission are discerned.","refs":["CH_MHON_T_19:A7","CH_MHON_T_20:NX4"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[]},{"id":"CL_MHON_09:CGD-05","text":"The termination of motion does not disappear, but is recodified: what locally is the diminution of created impetus globally becomes circular closure under divine order.","refs":["CH_MHON_T_19:NX7","CH_MHON_T_20:ME5"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[]}],"original_name":"Lower-Layer vs Current-Level Distinction"},{"number":"5","name":"Global Apophatic Structure","items":[{"id":"CL_MHON_09:GNX-01","text":"Natural equality of the created thing is excluded; equality is admitted only as an effect of ordering art.","refs":["CH_MHON_T_19:NX1","CH_MHON_T_19:A3"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"Negative","passages":[]},{"id":"CL_MHON_09:GNX-02","text":"Ornament, letter, and visible center are excluded as foundations of the ultimate necessity of the figure.","refs":["CH_MHON_T_19:NX2","CH_MHON_T_19:NX3"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"Negative","passages":[]},{"id":"CL_MHON_09:GNX-03","text":"Number is excluded as bare quantity, because legitimate number must bear a structure of transformation and ordering.","refs":["CH_MHON_T_19:NX5","CH_MHON_T_20:NX2"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"Negative","passages":[]},{"id":"CL_MHON_09:GNX-04","text":"Autonomous created progress is excluded, because neither creature nor influential cause provides itself an end or a passage beyond its proper order.","refs":["CH_MHON_T_19:NX7","CH_MHON_T_20:NX5"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"Negative","passages":[]},{"id":"CL_MHON_09:GNX-05","text":"The absolute disjunction of principle, flux, end, and mystery of apprehension is excluded; these moments can be interpreted only within one circulation.","refs":["CH_MHON_T_20:DNX1","CH_MHON_T_20:NX3"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"Negative","passages":[]}],"original_name":"Global Apophatic Structure"},{"number":"6","name":"Deep Book Apophatics","items":[{"id":"CL_MHON_09:DBN-01","text":"The system would collapse if the figure were a free composition of signs, because proportions precede and determine what a valid figure can be.","refs":["CH_MHON_T_19:DNX1","CH_MHON_T_19:A7"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"Negative","passages":[]},{"id":"CL_MHON_09:DBN-02","text":"The system would collapse if a plurality of numbers increased knowledge by itself, because number requires an internal power of illumination and transformation.","refs":["CH_MHON_T_19:DNX3","CH_MHON_T_20:DNX2"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"Negative","passages":[]},{"id":"CL_MHON_09:DBN-03","text":"The system would collapse if closure were either infinite progress or static rest; valid closure contains at once the end of causality and the circuit of completion.","refs":["CH_MHON_T_19:DNX4","CH_MHON_T_20:NX1"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"Negative","passages":[]},{"id":"CL_MHON_09:DBN-04","text":"The system would collapse if mystery were reduced to common perception or to the letter without interpretation.","refs":["CH_MHON_T_20:DNX3","CH_MHON_T_20:D6"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"Negative","passages":[]}],"original_name":"Deep Book Apophatics"},{"number":"7","name":"Super-Apophatics of the Whole","items":[{"id":"CL_MHON_09:BSA-01","text":"The possibility of absolute created causality is unavailable: every created agent remains within measure, limit, and a higher relation.","refs":["CH_MHON_T_19:SNX1","CH_MHON_T_19:NX7"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"Negative","passages":[]},{"id":"CL_MHON_09:BSA-02","text":"A perspective that would possess the truth of the figure in visibility alone is inadmissible, because necessity is located in hidden relations and non-ordinary intellect.","refs":["CH_MHON_T_19:SNX3","CH_MHON_T_20:NX3"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"Negative","passages":[]},{"id":"CL_MHON_09:BSA-03","text":"An end of things outside creation, worship, and divine will is not open to the system.","refs":["CH_MHON_T_20:SNX3","CH_MHON_T_20:A8"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"Negative","passages":[]},{"id":"CL_MHON_09:BSA-04","text":"The translation from geometrical proportion to cosmic or scriptural authority does not receive a fully explicit formal rule; this absence remains an internal limit of the system.","refs":["CH_MHON_T_19:MRT3","CH_MHON_T_20:MRT5"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"Tension","passages":[]}],"original_name":"Super-Apophatics of the Whole"},{"number":"8","name":"Cross-Unit Derivative Propositions","items":[{"id":"CL_MHON_09:XD-01","text":"Geometrical form becomes a medium of operation: it corrects natural heterogeneity and transfers that same correction into the course of emanation, completion, and return.","refs":["CL_MHON_09:GA-01","CL_MHON_09:GA-02","CL_MHON_09:GA-05","CH_MHON_T_19:D1","CH_MHON_T_20:SD1"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"Composite","passages":[]},{"id":"CL_MHON_09:XD-02","text":"Number not only describes order, but constitutes a legitimate passage: the permutative quaternary becomes the metamorphic XXIV, by which change itself is measured.","refs":["CL_MHON_09:GA-03","CL_MHON_09:GA-06","CH_MHON_T_19:SD3","CH_MHON_T_20:D3"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"Composite","passages":[]},{"id":"CL_MHON_09:XD-03","text":"The distinction between invariant figure and variable surface is also produced as the distinction between ordered intellect and ordinary sight.","refs":["CL_MHON_09:GA-02","CL_MHON_09:GA-07","CL_MHON_09:GA-09","CL_MHON_09:GA-10","CH_MHON_T_19:ME5","CH_MHON_T_20:D6"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"Composite","passages":[]},{"id":"CL_MHON_09:XD-04","text":"The limit of created power is transformed into a condition of return: the circle is not self-governing motion, but a closure referring created order to divine will.","refs":["CL_MHON_09:GA-04","CL_MHON_09:GA-05","CL_MHON_09:GA-08","CH_MHON_T_19:D6","CH_MHON_T_20:SD4"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"Composite","passages":[]},{"id":"CL_MHON_09:XD-05","text":"The nexus of the quaternary with XXIV makes the transition globally necessary, but formally incomplete, because geometrical analogy and apocalyptic authority are joined without a demonstrated rule of conversion.","refs":["CL_MHON_09:GA-03","CL_MHON_09:GA-06","CH_MHON_T_19:MRT3","CH_MHON_T_20:MRT5"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"Tension","passages":[]}],"original_name":"Cross-Unit Derivative Propositions"},{"number":"9","name":"Book-Scale Super-Derivatives","items":[{"id":"CL_MHON_09:BSD-01","text":"Geometry is reclassified as a grammar between fields: the same operation composes elementary relations, the figure of the body, numbers, times, and cultic order.","refs":["CH_MHON_T_19:SD1","CH_MHON_T_20:SD2"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[]},{"id":"CL_MHON_09:BSD-02","text":"Time is not figured as an open series, but as a finite circulation in which hourly completion and the limit of created motion mutually determine one another.","refs":["CH_MHON_T_19:DNX4","CH_MHON_T_20:D2"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[]},{"id":"CL_MHON_09:BSD-03","text":"The human subject becomes neither causal master nor passive spectator, but artisan-interpreter, to whom the capacity for ordering and inscribing is granted within given authority.","refs":["CH_MHON_T_19:A3","CH_MHON_T_20:A9"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[]},{"id":"CL_MHON_09:BSD-04","text":"The power of art is real but derived: it can order unequal nature, yet it cannot provide created order either its own foundation or infinite continuation.","refs":["CH_MHON_T_19:DNX2","CH_MHON_T_19:SNX1"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[]}],"original_name":"Book-Scale Super-Derivatives"},{"number":"10","name":"Recursive Loops, Escalation Paths, and Conceptual Attractors","items":[{"id":"CL_MHON_09:RLE-01","text":"The monadic point effuses into a line, the line is led around through its extremities into a circle, and the circle reforms the principle as completion.","refs":["CH_MHON_T_20:AS2","CH_MHON_T_20:A3"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[]},{"id":"CL_MHON_09:RLE-02","text":"Elemental inequality → artificial reduction → symmetry of figure → ordering number → application to times and virtues constitutes a path of amplification from matter to cosmology.","refs":["CH_MHON_T_19:FGS1","CH_MHON_T_19:A10"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[]},{"id":"CL_MHON_09:RLE-03","text":"Invisible proportion → inscribed figure → ordered intellect → transmission of discipline → preservation of proportion constitutes the epistemic circuit of the system.","refs":["CH_MHON_T_19:SD4","CH_MHON_T_20:D5"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[]},{"id":"CL_MHON_09:RLE-04","text":"The attractor of the system is the non-apparent relation, which contains center, measure, and necessity, while ornaments and surface aspects vary around it.","refs":["CH_MHON_T_19:NX3","CH_MHON_T_19:SNX3"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[]},{"id":"CL_MHON_09:RLE-05","text":"The escalation from the quaternary to XXIV is at once an attractor and a fragile seam: it promises greater completion, but does not openly display the mechanism of conversion.","refs":["CH_MHON_T_19:MRT6","CH_MHON_T_20:MRT2"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[]}],"original_name":"Recursive Loops, Escalation Paths, and Conceptual Attractors"},{"number":"11","name":"Formal Generative Schema of the Whole Book","items":[{"id":"CL_MHON_09:FGS-B01","text":"Inequality of elements → artificial reduction → ordered equality → commensurated body of the figure.","refs":["CH_MHON_T_19:FGS1","CH_MHON_T_19:A3"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[]},{"id":"CL_MHON_09:FGS-B02","text":"Module AB → double and triple proportions → geometrical invariants → distinction of the necessary from the contingent.","refs":["CH_MHON_T_19:FGS2","CH_MHON_T_19:FGS5"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[]},{"id":"CL_MHON_09:FGS-B03","text":"Geometrical quaternary → mystical division → permutation and metathesis → number as operator of measure and transformation.","refs":["CH_MHON_T_19:FGS3","CH_MHON_T_19:A8"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[]},{"id":"CL_MHON_09:FGS-B04","text":"Restored Monad → diminution of created causality → limit of motion → negation of infinite progress.","refs":["CH_MHON_T_19:FGS4","CH_MHON_T_19:NX7"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[]},{"id":"CL_MHON_09:FGS-B05","text":"Monadic point → linear effusion → circular circumduction → XXIV as temporal, cosmic, and apocalyptic completion.","refs":["CH_MHON_T_20:FGS1","CH_MHON_T_20:FGS4"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[]},{"id":"CL_MHON_09:FGS-B06","text":"Geometrical form + transformative number + symbolic authority → inscribable mystery; the translation among these orders nevertheless remains analogical, not fully formalized.","refs":["CH_MHON_T_20:SD3","CH_MHON_T_20:MRT5"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[]}],"original_name":"Formal Generative Schema of the Whole Book"},{"number":"12","name":"Whole-Book Argument Flow Map","items":[{"id":"CL_MHON_09:AFM-B01","text":"Unequal elements → equalizing art → commensurated cross → module of invariant relations.","refs":["CH_MHON_T_19:AF1","CH_MHON_T_19:FGS2"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[]},{"id":"CL_MHON_09:AFM-B02","text":"Proportions of the cross → quaternary of lines → division and metathesis → number applicable to nature, times, and degrees.","refs":["CH_MHON_T_19:AF2","CH_MHON_T_19:AF3"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[]},{"id":"CL_MHON_09:AFM-B03","text":"Restored Monad → diminution of created impetus → finite closure → rejection of indefinite progress.","refs":["CH_MHON_T_19:AF4","CH_MHON_T_19:DNX4"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[]},{"id":"CL_MHON_09:AFM-B04","text":"Principial point → linear effusion → closing circle → metamorphic XXIV → ordering of all things to divine will.","refs":["CH_MHON_T_20:AF1","CH_MHON_T_20:AF2"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[]}],"original_name":"Whole-Book Argument Flow Map"},{"number":"13","name":"Macro Map-Effects","items":[{"id":"CL_MHON_09:MME-01","text":"The geometrical figure is reclassified from a discrete sign into an ontological map that refers origin, process, and end within one architecture.","refs":["CH_MHON_T_19:ME1","CH_MHON_T_20:ME1"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[]},{"id":"CL_MHON_09:MME-02","text":"Number is reclassified from enumeration into a matrix of relations, permutations, times, and completions.","refs":["CH_MHON_T_19:ME3","CH_MHON_T_20:ME2"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[]},{"id":"CL_MHON_09:MME-03","text":"The opposition between geometrical figure and religious symbol is dissolved, because each manifests the same ordering of invisible relations.","refs":["CH_MHON_T_19:ME5","CH_MHON_T_20:ME3"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[]},{"id":"CL_MHON_09:MME-04","text":"Necessary reading is transformed: ordinary sight is no longer the primary judge, but geometrical, numerical, and symbolic interpretation is simultaneously required.","refs":["CH_MHON_T_20:ME4","CH_MHON_T_20:D6"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[]}],"original_name":"Macro Map-Effects"},{"number":"14","name":"Altitude Integration","items":[{"id":"CL_MHON_09:ALT-B01","text":"The level of surface displays points, straight lines, circles, the cross, measure AB, the quaternary, and the number XXIV.","refs":["CH_MHON_T_19:ALT1","CH_MHON_T_20:ALT1"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[]},{"id":"CL_MHON_09:ALT-B02","text":"The structural level describes the conversion of inequality into proportion, of proportion into number, and of number into an order of transformation.","refs":["CH_MHON_T_19:ALT2","CH_MHON_T_20:ALT2"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[]},{"id":"CL_MHON_09:ALT-B03","text":"The metastrategic level defends the discipline through the separation of invariants from contingents and through the displacement of the reader from ordinary sight to ordered intellect.","refs":["CH_MHON_T_19:ALT3","CH_MHON_T_20:ALT3"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[]},{"id":"CL_MHON_09:ALT-B04","text":"The ontological and temporal level understands created things as unequal, measurable, finitely mobile, and circularly related to the divine principle.","refs":["CH_MHON_T_19:ALT4","CH_MHON_T_20:ALT4"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[]},{"id":"CL_MHON_09:ALT-B05","text":"The methodological and performative level fulfills the system through the very inscription of the figure, by which doctrine is not only expounded but established in a transmissible sign.","refs":["CH_MHON_T_19:SD4","CH_MHON_T_20:D5"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[]}],"original_name":"Altitude Integration"},{"number":"15","name":"Internal Tensions, Mutation Points, and Fault Lines","items":[{"id":"CL_MHON_09:FL-01","text":"The cross is said to contain equal and unequal parts simultaneously, while the equality of elements is elsewhere described as an effect of art against unequal nature.","refs":["CH_MHON_T_19:MRT4","CH_MHON_T_19:NX1"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"Tension","passages":[]},{"id":"CL_MHON_09:FL-02","text":"The transition from geometrical proportion to the measures of things, times, cosmic authority, and scriptural meaning is asserted, but the formal rule of this translation is not displayed.","refs":["CH_MHON_T_19:MRT3","CH_MHON_T_20:MRT5"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"Tension","passages":[]},{"id":"CL_MHON_09:FL-03","text":"A quaternary or denary end and the completion of the quaternary through XXIV are simultaneously posited as necessary, although the operative nexus of both closures remains incomplete.","refs":["CH_MHON_T_19:MRT6","CH_MHON_T_20:MRT2"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"Tension","passages":[]},{"id":"CL_MHON_09:MP-01","text":"The first point of mutation is when the cross ceases to be a religious or ornamental sign and becomes an instrument of elemental and numerical commensuration.","refs":["CH_MHON_T_19:ME1","CH_MHON_T_19:D2"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"Composite","passages":[]},{"id":"CL_MHON_09:MP-02","text":"The second point of mutation is when the quaternary is transferred from a structure of lines to a form of metamorphosis through the number XXIV.","refs":["CH_MHON_T_20:A5","CH_MHON_T_20:AS4"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"Tension","passages":[]},{"id":"CL_MHON_09:MP-03","text":"The third point of mutation is when the limit of created causality not only arrests motion, but transforms closure into circular return under divine order.","refs":["CH_MHON_T_19:DNX4","CH_MHON_T_20:ME5"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"Tension","passages":[]}],"original_name":"Internal Tensions, Mutation Points, and Fault Lines"},{"number":"16","name":"Hidden System Image","items":[{"id":"CL_MHON_09:HSI-01","text":"Reality is implicitly imagined as a machine of relations that converts the heterogeneity of matter through measure, number, and circulation into finite order.","refs":["CH_MHON_T_19:SD1","CH_MHON_T_19:FGS1"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[]},{"id":"CL_MHON_09:HSI-02","text":"The subject is an artisan-interpreter: it does not create inequality, but orders it, inscribes the figure, and transmits mystery without absolute mastery.","refs":["CH_MHON_T_19:DNX2","CH_MHON_T_20:A9"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[]},{"id":"CL_MHON_09:HSI-03","text":"Time is effusion with return, not an infinite line: completion gathers motion without the creature becoming sufficient to itself as origin or end.","refs":["CH_MHON_T_20:SD1","CH_MHON_T_19:DNX4"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[]}],"original_name":"Hidden System Image"},{"number":"17","name":"Minimal Whole-Book Reconstruction Test","items":[{"id":"CL_MHON_09:MRT-B01","text":"The reconstruction suffices if the nexus among the inequality of elements, equalizing art, mensural invariants, transformative quaternary, limit of causality, and circular closure is preserved.","refs":["CH_MHON_T_19:MRT1","CH_MHON_T_20:MRT1"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[]},{"id":"CL_MHON_09:MRT-B02","text":"The firmest structure consists in the concordance between geometrical construction, temporal circle, and the notion of completion; there explicit relations mutually confirm one another.","refs":["CH_MHON_T_19:MRT2","CH_MHON_T_20:MRT3"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[]},{"id":"CL_MHON_09:MRT-B03","text":"What most resists axiomatic reduction is the translation from geometry to cosmology and apocalypse, as well as the exact identification of the quaternary limit with the metamorphosis of XXIV.","refs":["CH_MHON_T_19:MRT3","CH_MHON_T_20:MRT2"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[]}],"original_name":"Minimal Whole-Book Reconstruction Test"}],"revision":"CL_MHON_09@R001","source_variant_id":"canonical:CL_MHON_09:v1","source_language_code":"la","original_title":"Chapters 24–25"}],"identity":{"layer":"CL","kicker":"Cluster · Monas Hieroglyphica","title":"Chapters 24–25","context":"T H E O R. Xxiii. · Theorem Xxiiii.","original_title":"Chapters 24–25","original_context":"T H E O R. Xxiii. · Theor. Xxiiii."},"original_title":"Chapters 24–25","title_language_code":"en"},{"id":"MC_MHON_L1_01","layer":"MC","title":"Mhon L1 01","order":1,"parents":["CL_MHON_01","CL_MHON_02","CL_MHON_03"],"revision":"MC_MHON_L1_01@R001","sha256":"501f36f3d327569975bc9529e186c0f85956bbc0db99511924869309b0d59582","sections":[{"number":"1","name":"Book-Scale Argumental Spine","items":[{"id":"MC_MHON_L1_01:BAS-01","text":"Systema praesentis gradus quaerit quomodo character minimus geometriam, cosmologiam, temporalitatem, motum et numerum in unam machinam generativam contrahere possit.","refs":["CL_MHON_01:BAS-01","CL_MHON_02:BS-01","CL_MHON_03:SP-01"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"Composite","passages":[]},{"id":"MC_MHON_L1_01:BAS-02","text":"Thesis globalis formam geometricam constituit tamquam operationem qua res manifestantur, relationes cosmicae tempus producunt, et motus materiales in numeros atque signa doctrinalia convertuntur.","refs":["CL_MHON_01:BAS-02","CL_MHON_02:BS-02","CL_MHON_03:SP-02"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"Composite","passages":[]},{"id":"MC_MHON_L1_01:BAS-03","text":"Argumentatio regressum ad principia simplicia cum reconstructione symbolica coniungit, ut punctum, centrum, linea, circulus et crux non vocabula communia sed gradus unius causalitatis fiant.","refs":["CL_MHON_01:BAS-03","CL_MHON_02:BS-03","CL_MHON_03:SP-03"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"Composite","passages":[]},{"id":"MC_MHON_L1_01:BAS-04","text":"Motus est recursivus, spiralis et cumulativus: forma comprimitur in principium, principium per campos diversos explicatur, atque reditus formam mutat potius quam identice repetit.","refs":["CL_MHON_01:BAS-04","CL_MHON_02:BS-04","CL_MHON_03:SP-04"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"Composite","passages":[]},{"id":"MC_MHON_L1_01:BAS-05","text":"Probatio operatur per necessitatem geometricam, translationem relationalem et fecunditatem analogicam, quamquam vis deductiva in omnibus translationibus aequalis non est.","refs":["CL_MHON_01:BAS-05","CL_MHON_02:BS-03","CL_MHON_03:BSD-06"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"Tension","passages":[]},{"id":"MC_MHON_L1_01:BAS-06","text":"Directio systematis procedit a signo manifesto ad principium simplex, inde ad relationes cosmicas et fluxionales, postremo ad ordines temporales, numerales et doctrinales.","refs":["CL_MHON_01:BSD-03","CL_MHON_02:XD-05","CL_MHON_03:XD-05"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"Composite","passages":[]},{"id":"MC_MHON_L1_01:BAS-07","text":"Forma expositionis conclusionem suam perficit, quia compressio, dispositio figurarum et concatenatio correspondentiarum ipsam unitatem operativam quam affirmant efficiunt.","refs":["CL_MHON_01:ALT-B05","CL_MHON_02:ALT-B05","CL_MHON_03:ALT-B05"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"Composite","passages":[]},{"id":"MC_MHON_L1_01:BAS-08","text":"Clausura est generativa potius quam immobilis: centrum manet, sed peripheria, Luna, fluxus et series numerica novos circuitus ex clausuris prioribus aperiunt.","refs":["CL_MHON_01:BAS-07","CL_MHON_02:BS-05","CL_MHON_03:SP-06"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"Composite","passages":[]},{"id":"MC_MHON_L1_01:BAS-09","text":"Systema cohaeret circa relationem inter unitatem et transformationem, sed nexus inter necessitatem geometricam, causalitatem ontologicam et correspondentiam symbolicam terminaliter non demonstratur.","refs":["CL_MHON_01:MRT-B02","CL_MHON_02:MRT-B02","CL_MHON_03:MRT-B03"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"Tension","passages":[]}],"original_name":"Book-Scale Argumental Spine"},{"number":"2","name":"Global Load-Bearing Axioms","items":[{"id":"MC_MHON_L1_01:GA-01","text":"[Persistent] Forma geometrica non est imago passiva, sed operatio quae manifestationem, ordinationem vel translationem rei efficit.","refs":["CL_MHON_01:GA-03","CL_MHON_02:BS-03","CL_MHON_03:GA-01"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"Composite","passages":[]},{"id":"MC_MHON_L1_01:GA-02","text":"[Mutating] Punctum ex minimo ingrediente in centrum ontologicum et cosmicum, deinde in originem fluxus atque productionis convertitur.","refs":["CL_MHON_01:RMD-02","CL_MHON_02:RMD-01","CL_MHON_03:RMD-03"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"Composite","passages":[]},{"id":"MC_MHON_L1_01:GA-03","text":"[Persistent] Identitas cuiuslibet elementi constituitur per relationem ad principium, centrum, mediatorem, complementum vel locum, non per substantiam isolatam.","refs":["CL_MHON_01:GA-05","CL_MHON_02:GA-03","CL_MHON_03:GA-07"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"Composite","passages":[]},{"id":"MC_MHON_L1_01:GA-04","text":"[Emergent] Geometria est grammatica ontologica communis qua dependentia rerum, distributio cosmica et productio symbolica eodem schemate leguntur.","refs":["CL_MHON_01:GA-06","CL_MHON_02:BSD-01","CL_MHON_03:BSD-01"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"Composite","passages":[]},{"id":"MC_MHON_L1_01:GA-05","text":"[Mutating] Identitas stabilis cedit identitati per transformationem servatae, quia subordinatio, disparitio, reditus et repetitio statum elementi mutant sine eius functione destruenda.","refs":["CL_MHON_01:RMD-01","CL_MHON_02:GA-09","CL_MHON_03:MP-04"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"Composite","passages":[]},{"id":"MC_MHON_L1_01:GA-06","text":"[Emergent] Partialitas, dislocatio et defectus sunt condiciones generativae ordinis, quia complementum vel restitutio ex ipsis unitatem ampliorem producit.","refs":["CL_MHON_01:XD-06","CL_MHON_02:GA-06","CL_MHON_03:XD-04"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"Composite","passages":[]},{"id":"MC_MHON_L1_01:GA-07","text":"[Emergent] Tempus non est receptaculum praedatum, sed effectus regressus ad originem, complementi oppositorum, reditus periodici et manifestationis latentis.","refs":["CL_MHON_01:BSD-03","CL_MHON_02:BSD-02","CL_MHON_03:BSD-05"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"Composite","passages":[]},{"id":"MC_MHON_L1_01:GA-08","text":"[Persistent] Structura simplex per disciplinas migrat, ita ut cosmologia, physica, numeratio, magia et doctrina translationes eiusdem ordinis fiant.","refs":["CL_MHON_01:GA-12","CL_MHON_02:RMD-08","CL_MHON_03:GA-07"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"Composite","passages":[]},{"id":"MC_MHON_L1_01:GA-09","text":"[Persistent] Differentia formalis minima vim semanticam et causalitatem relationis afficit; integritas obiecti igitur ab integritate configurationis pendet.","refs":["CL_MHON_01:GA-08","CL_MHON_02:ALT-B05","CL_MHON_03:BSD-04"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"Composite","passages":[]},{"id":"MC_MHON_L1_01:GA-10","text":"[Persistent] Cognitio legitima relationes generatrices reconstructive persequitur, non proprietates isolatas vel similitudines nominales tantum colligit.","refs":["CL_MHON_01:BSD-01","CL_MHON_02:ALT-B03","CL_MHON_03:MME-05"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"Composite","passages":[]},{"id":"MC_MHON_L1_01:GA-11","text":"[Terminal] Nulla figura, corpus, numerus vel peripheria suam rationem extra relationes generatrices autonome possidet.","refs":["CL_MHON_01:GNX-03","CL_MHON_02:GNX-02","CL_MHON_03:GA-09"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"Negative","passages":[]},{"id":"MC_MHON_L1_01:GA-12","text":"[Mutating] Reditus non restaurat simpliciter statum priorem, sed per singulam repetitionem amplitudinem ontologicam, temporalem vel symbolicam auget.","refs":["CL_MHON_01:RLE-01","CL_MHON_02:RLE-03","CL_MHON_03:RLE-04"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"Composite","passages":[]},{"id":"MC_MHON_L1_01:GA-13","text":"[Attractor] Unitas monadica-relationalis attrahit centrum, complementum, numerum, figuram, litteram et locum in configurationem distributam.","refs":["CL_MHON_01:RLE-06","CL_MHON_02:RLE-04","CL_MHON_03:RLE-05"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"Composite","passages":[]},{"id":"MC_MHON_L1_01:GA-14","text":"[Terminal] Clausura systematis est aperta et transformativa: invarians relationis servatur, sed circuitus sequens semper novum principium vel ordinem latentem instituit.","refs":["CL_MHON_01:BAS-08","CL_MHON_02:BS-05","CL_MHON_03:SP-06"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"Tension","passages":[]}],"original_name":"Global Load-Bearing Axioms"},{"number":"3","name":"Recurrence, Mutation, and Displacement Matrix","items":[{"id":"MC_MHON_L1_01:RMD-01","text":"Punctum identitatem minimam retinet, sed munus eius ab ingrediente figurae ad centrum universale, referente cosmico et fonte fluxus successive amplificatur.","refs":["CL_MHON_01:RMD-02","CL_MHON_02:RMD-01","CL_MHON_03:RMD-03"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"Composite","passages":[]},{"id":"MC_MHON_L1_01:RMD-02","text":"Linea ex forma simplicissima et dependente transit in viam restitutionis atque genealogiam fluxus materialis; stabilitas descriptiva fit operatio directionalis.","refs":["CL_MHON_01:RMD-01","CL_MHON_03:RMD-04","CL_MHON_03:RMD-05"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"Composite","passages":[]},{"id":"MC_MHON_L1_01:RMD-03","text":"Circulus et peripheria primum dependentiam a centro exprimunt, deinde circulationem cosmicam efficiunt, postremo munus transformationis suae repetitioni rectilineae cedunt.","refs":["CL_MHON_01:RLE-03","CL_MHON_02:HSI-03","CL_MHON_03:RLE-04"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"Composite","passages":[]},{"id":"MC_MHON_L1_01:RMD-04","text":"Monas ut nomen extra primam unitatem recedit, sed eius functio persistit in relatione completiva et unitate symbolica distributa.","refs":["CL_MHON_01:RMD-03","CL_MHON_02:RLE-04","CL_MHON_03:RLE-05"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"Composite","passages":[]},{"id":"MC_MHON_L1_01:RMD-05","text":"Repraesentatio ex productione manifestationis in constitutionem ordinis cosmici et deinde in concatenationem numeri, litterae atque loci transfertur.","refs":["CL_MHON_01:RMD-04","CL_MHON_02:ALT-B05","CL_MHON_03:BSD-04"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"Composite","passages":[]},{"id":"MC_MHON_L1_01:RMD-06","text":"Lumen ex modo manifestationis in mediationem Solarem et tandem in principium temporale mutatur, dum in campo numerali eius munus a fluxu et translatione symbolica absorbetur.","refs":["CL_MHON_01:GA-03","CL_MHON_02:RMD-05","CL_MHON_02:MP-02"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"Composite","passages":[]},{"id":"MC_MHON_L1_01:RMD-07","text":"Complementum mutatur ex subordinatione pluralitatis centro in completionem Lunarem et postea in aggregationem numericam quae quaternarium ultra se ipsum extendit.","refs":["CL_MHON_01:XD-06","CL_MHON_02:GA-06","CL_MHON_03:GA-06"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"Composite","passages":[]},{"id":"MC_MHON_L1_01:RMD-08","text":"Reditus functionem conservat sed ambitum mutat: regressus ontologicus, circuitus Lunaris, restitutio naturalis et repetitio numerica sunt mutationes status eiusdem machinae.","refs":["CL_MHON_01:BSD-03","CL_MHON_02:RMD-06","CL_MHON_03:RLE-01"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"Composite","passages":[]},{"id":"MC_MHON_L1_01:RMD-09","text":"Exactitudo materialis, localiter proprietas impressionis, in normam generalem integritatis relationum figurativarum et symbolicarum amplificatur.","refs":["CL_MHON_01:BAS-06","CL_MHON_01:GA-08","CL_MHON_03:BSD-04"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"Composite","passages":[]},{"id":"MC_MHON_L1_01:RMD-10","text":"Occultatio mutatur in absentiam Lunarem et latentiam ordinis numerici; velamen doctrinale evanescit ut institutum locale, sed non-manifestum permanet tamquam fons transitionis.","refs":["CL_MHON_01:RMD-05","CL_MHON_02:GA-09","CL_MHON_03:BSA-04"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"Composite","passages":[]}],"original_name":"Recurrence, Mutation, and Displacement Matrix"},{"number":"4","name":"Lower-Layer vs Current-Level Distinction","items":[{"id":"MC_MHON_L1_01:CGD-01","text":"Patrocinium regium, administratio typographica et delectus recipientium mechanismi locales manent; ad gradum praesentem ascendit tantum principium quod integritas relationis condiciones materiales requirit.","refs":["CL_MHON_01:CGD-01","CL_MHON_01:GA-11"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"Direct","passages":[]},{"id":"MC_MHON_L1_01:CGD-02","text":"Distinctio inter non-existentia et latentia non globalizatur secundum proprias definitiones; sursum persistit sola thesis quod absentia vel possibilitas in processum manifestationis includi potest.","refs":["CL_MHON_01:CGD-02","CL_MHON_01:XD-08"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"Direct","passages":[]},{"id":"MC_MHON_L1_01:CGD-03","text":"Terra, Sol et Luna manent determinata cosmologica localia; praesentis gradus structuram sustinet relatio inter centrum, mediationem, partialitatem et complementum.","refs":["CL_MHON_02:CGD-01","CL_MHON_02:CGD-03"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"Composite","passages":[]},{"id":"MC_MHON_L1_01:CGD-04","text":"Sedes naturales, stilla et Mechanica Magia sunt exempla localia; globaliter servatur transitus quo dislocatio per fluxum ordinatum in figuram producibilem restituitur.","refs":["CL_MHON_03:GA-03","CL_MHON_03:CGD-03","CL_MHON_03:XD-04"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"Composite","passages":[]},{"id":"MC_MHON_L1_01:CGD-05","text":"Littera Romana, octonarius et nexus particularis ternarii cum septenario non ascendunt in axiomata universalia, sed testantur identitatem symbolicam per ordines heterogeneos distribui.","refs":["CL_MHON_03:CGD-02","CL_MHON_03:RMD-07","CL_MHON_03:CGD-06"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"Tension","passages":[]},{"id":"MC_MHON_L1_01:CGD-06","text":"Dependentia figurae a puncto, Lunaris a Sole et numeri a relationibus suis sursum amplificatur in legem communem non-autonomiae.","refs":["CL_MHON_01:GNX-03","CL_MHON_02:GNX-02","CL_MHON_03:BSA-03"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"Composite","passages":[]},{"id":"MC_MHON_L1_01:CGD-07","text":"Productio per formam, tempus per complementum et signum per fluxum coniunguntur tantum hoc gradu in unam causalitatem translationis.","refs":["CL_MHON_01:XD-02","CL_MHON_02:BSD-02","CL_MHON_03:XD-01"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"Composite","passages":[]},{"id":"MC_MHON_L1_01:CGD-08","text":"Maior transitus inter unitates fit cum punctum immobile et centrum distributivum in fontem motus convertuntur, sine amissione muneris originarii.","refs":["CL_MHON_01:MP-02","CL_MHON_02:MP-01","CL_MHON_03:MP-01"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"Composite","passages":[]},{"id":"MC_MHON_L1_01:CGD-09","text":"Imago geometriae ut collectionis figurarum separatarum obsoleta fit; praesens gradus eam ut seriem relationum generatricium, temporalium et transdisciplinarium codificat.","refs":["CL_MHON_01:MME-01","CL_MHON_02:BSD-01","CL_MHON_03:CGD-07"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"Composite","passages":[]}],"original_name":"Lower-Layer vs Current-Level Distinction"},{"number":"5","name":"Global Apophatic Structure","items":[{"id":"MC_MHON_L1_01:GNX-01","text":"Signum non potest esse repraesentatio passiva vel conventio extrinseca, quia forma intra systema rem manifestat aut ordinem eius constituit.","refs":["CL_MHON_01:GNX-01","CL_MHON_01:GNX-02","CL_MHON_02:ALT-B05"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"Negative","passages":[]},{"id":"MC_MHON_L1_01:GNX-02","text":"Nulla figura, Luna, peripheria vel series numerica autonome intelligitur extra principium et relationes suas.","refs":["CL_MHON_01:GNX-03","CL_MHON_02:GNX-02","CL_MHON_03:DBN-03"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"Negative","passages":[]},{"id":"MC_MHON_L1_01:GNX-03","text":"Identitas fixa et tempus mere lineare excluduntur, quia disparitio, reditus et repetitio identitatem per mutationem constituunt.","refs":["CL_MHON_02:GNX-03","CL_MHON_02:GNX-04","CL_MHON_03:MP-04"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"Negative","passages":[]},{"id":"MC_MHON_L1_01:GNX-04","text":"Numerus non est mensura externa vel series abstracta quae a figura, materia, littera et loco separari possit.","refs":["CL_MHON_03:GNX-01","CL_MHON_03:GNX-05","CL_MHON_03:BSA-03"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"Negative","passages":[]},{"id":"MC_MHON_L1_01:GNX-05","text":"Geometria, cosmologia, physica, magia et doctrina non sunt regiones absolutis limitibus clausae.","refs":["CL_MHON_01:GNX-07","CL_MHON_02:GNX-01","CL_MHON_03:DBN-04"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"Negative","passages":[]},{"id":"MC_MHON_L1_01:GNX-06","text":"Ordo non potest esse immobilis, quia centrum distributionem, complementum diem et dislocatio restitutionem producunt.","refs":["CL_MHON_01:GA-09","CL_MHON_02:BS-05","CL_MHON_03:XD-04"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"Negative","passages":[]},{"id":"MC_MHON_L1_01:GNX-07","text":"Differentia materialis vel figurativa non potest ut semantice indifferens repudiari, cum identitas inter relationes exactas distribuatur.","refs":["CL_MHON_01:GA-08","CL_MHON_01:RLE-04","CL_MHON_03:BSD-04"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"Negative","passages":[]},{"id":"MC_MHON_L1_01:GNX-08","text":"Auctoritas, nomen commune aut analogia inexaminata probationem intrinsecam supplere nequeunt, quamquam systema ipso transitu analogico saepe nititur.","refs":["CL_MHON_01:GA-10","CL_MHON_01:CGD-06","CL_MHON_03:BSD-06"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"Tension","passages":[]}],"original_name":"Global Apophatic Structure"},{"number":"6","name":"Deep Book Apophatics","items":[{"id":"MC_MHON_L1_01:DBN-01","text":"Falsum esse oportet realitatem residuum absolute informe continere quod nulli manifestationi, relationi aut ordinationi formali pateat.","refs":["CL_MHON_01:DBN-01","CL_MHON_03:BSA-01"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"Negative","passages":[]},{"id":"MC_MHON_L1_01:DBN-02","text":"Falsum esse oportet ordines essendi, significandi et producendi fundamentaliter separatos esse.","refs":["CL_MHON_01:DBN-02","CL_MHON_01:MME-02","CL_MHON_03:BSA-02"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"Negative","passages":[]},{"id":"MC_MHON_L1_01:DBN-03","text":"Falsum esse oportet extensionem, pluralitatem vel partialitatem se ipsas sine centro, mediatore aut complemento fundare posse.","refs":["CL_MHON_01:DBN-03","CL_MHON_02:DBN-01","CL_MHON_03:GNX-04"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"Negative","passages":[]},{"id":"MC_MHON_L1_01:DBN-04","text":"Falsum esse oportet tempus ante relationes figurales, circuitus et transformationes iam perfecte constitutum esse.","refs":["CL_MHON_02:DBN-02","CL_MHON_02:BSD-02","CL_MHON_03:BSD-05"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"Negative","passages":[]},{"id":"MC_MHON_L1_01:DBN-05","text":"Falsum esse oportet causalitatem physicam, necessitatem geometricam et operationem symbolicam campos mutuo externos constituere.","refs":["CL_MHON_01:GA-06","CL_MHON_03:GA-04","CL_MHON_03:DBN-04"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"Negative","passages":[]},{"id":"MC_MHON_L1_01:DBN-06","text":"Falsum esse oportet absentiam, dislocationem et partialitatem esse meras privationes, quia unumquodque statum sequentem generat.","refs":["CL_MHON_02:DBN-03","CL_MHON_03:XD-04","CL_MHON_01:XD-08"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"Negative","passages":[]}],"original_name":"Deep Book Apophatics"},{"number":"7","name":"Super-Apophatics of the Whole","items":[{"id":"MC_MHON_L1_01:BSA-01","text":"Purum exterius, quod neque forma, neque relatio, neque possibilitas manifestationis attingat, ontologice indisponibile est.","refs":["CL_MHON_01:BSA-02","CL_MHON_02:BSA-03","CL_MHON_03:BSA-01"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"Negative","passages":[]},{"id":"MC_MHON_L1_01:BSA-02","text":"Autonomia absoluta impossibilis est: peripheria centro, Luna Soli, linea puncto et numerus configurationi suae respondent.","refs":["CL_MHON_01:BSA-01","CL_MHON_02:BSA-02","CL_MHON_03:BSA-03"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"Negative","passages":[]},{"id":"MC_MHON_L1_01:BSA-03","text":"Realitas uniformis sine centro, dignitate, asymmetria aut complemento excluditur, quia differentia relationalis ipsa ordinem producit.","refs":["CL_MHON_02:BSA-01","CL_MHON_01:XD-06","CL_MHON_03:XD-04"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"Negative","passages":[]},{"id":"MC_MHON_L1_01:BSA-04","text":"Demonstratio totaliter clausa manet possibilitas indisponibilis: systema necessitatem formalem petit, sed amplificationes eius ultimae analogiam et correspondentiam requirunt.","refs":["CL_MHON_01:MRT-B02","CL_MHON_02:FL-03","CL_MHON_03:FL-01"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"Tension","passages":[]},{"id":"MC_MHON_L1_01:BSA-05","text":"Immediatio pura excluditur, quia manifestatio semper per figuram, mediationem Solarem, fluxum aut ordinationem numericam transit.","refs":["CL_MHON_01:GNX-02","CL_MHON_02:GA-05","CL_MHON_03:RMD-05"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"Negative","passages":[]}],"original_name":"Super-Apophatics of the Whole"},{"number":"8","name":"Cross-Unit Derivative Propositions","items":[{"id":"MC_MHON_L1_01:XD-01","text":"Monas ad praesentem gradum desinit esse sola figura nominata et fit functio distributa quae centrum, complementum et unitatem symbolicam sub eadem lege dependentiae ordinat.","refs":["MC_MHON_L1_01:GA-03","MC_MHON_L1_01:GA-04","MC_MHON_L1_01:GA-13","CL_MHON_01:RMD-03","CL_MHON_02:RLE-04","CL_MHON_03:RLE-05"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"Composite","passages":[]},{"id":"MC_MHON_L1_01:XD-02","text":"Punctum simul invarians et productivum fit: quod extensionem centro continet, idem per fluxum lineam et ulteriores compositiones generat.","refs":["MC_MHON_L1_01:GA-02","MC_MHON_L1_01:GA-04","CL_MHON_01:RLE-03","CL_MHON_03:RMD-03","CL_MHON_03:XD-05"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"Composite","passages":[]},{"id":"MC_MHON_L1_01:XD-03","text":"Manifestatio rei, productio diei et institutio signi sunt formae eiusdem causalitatis qua relatio ordinata aliquid legibile ad actum perducit.","refs":["MC_MHON_L1_01:GA-01","MC_MHON_L1_01:GA-07","MC_MHON_L1_01:GA-08","CL_MHON_01:XD-02","CL_MHON_02:BSD-02","CL_MHON_03:XD-03"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"Composite","passages":[]},{"id":"MC_MHON_L1_01:XD-04","text":"Partialitas et dislocatio non sunt defectus corrigendi extrinsecus, sed differentiae internae ex quibus complementum, restitutio et amplificatio emergunt.","refs":["MC_MHON_L1_01:GA-05","MC_MHON_L1_01:GA-06","CL_MHON_02:GA-06","CL_MHON_03:XD-04","CL_MHON_01:XD-06"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"Composite","passages":[]},{"id":"MC_MHON_L1_01:XD-05","text":"Regressus ontologicus, circuitus Lunaris, reditus naturalis et repetitio configurativa communem legem ostendunt: reditus originem repetit tantum ut novum gradum producat.","refs":["MC_MHON_L1_01:GA-07","MC_MHON_L1_01:GA-12","MC_MHON_L1_01:GA-14","CL_MHON_01:BSD-03","CL_MHON_02:RMD-06","CL_MHON_03:RLE-04"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"Composite","passages":[]},{"id":"MC_MHON_L1_01:XD-06","text":"Cum identitas inter relationes distribuatur, exactitudo non requirit immobilitatem elementi, sed conservationem structurae per mutationes eius.","refs":["MC_MHON_L1_01:GA-05","MC_MHON_L1_01:GA-09","CL_MHON_01:GA-08","CL_MHON_02:GA-09","CL_MHON_03:BSD-04"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"Composite","passages":[]},{"id":"MC_MHON_L1_01:XD-07","text":"Geometria fit medium commune causalitatum ontologicarum, cosmicarum, physicarum et magicarum, non quia differentiae abolentur sed quia earum conversiones formaliter ordinantur.","refs":["MC_MHON_L1_01:GA-04","MC_MHON_L1_01:GA-08","MC_MHON_L1_01:GA-10","CL_MHON_01:GA-06","CL_MHON_02:RMD-08","CL_MHON_03:XD-06"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"Composite","passages":[]},{"id":"MC_MHON_L1_01:XD-08","text":"Exigentia demonstrationis et usus analogiae se mutuo sustentant et impediunt: prima translationes legitimat, secunda ambitum earum ultra quod demonstratum est extendit.","refs":["MC_MHON_L1_01:GA-08","MC_MHON_L1_01:GA-10","CL_MHON_01:GA-10","CL_MHON_01:FL-06","CL_MHON_03:FL-01"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"Tension","passages":[]},{"id":"MC_MHON_L1_01:XD-09","text":"Non-manifestum unum campum dynamicum format, quia latentia, disparitio et configuratio nondum apparens sunt tempora transitionis, non absoluta exteriora.","refs":["MC_MHON_L1_01:GA-05","MC_MHON_L1_01:GA-07","MC_MHON_L1_01:GA-12","CL_MHON_01:XD-08","CL_MHON_02:GA-09","CL_MHON_03:BSA-04"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"Composite","passages":[]}],"original_name":"Cross-Unit Derivative Propositions"},{"number":"9","name":"Book-Scale Super-Derivatives","items":[{"id":"MC_MHON_L1_01:BSD-01","text":"Ontologia huius gradus est relationalis et operationalis: ens est nexus dependentiarum qui per figuram, positionem, conversionem et compositionem producitur.","refs":["CL_MHON_01:BSD-02","CL_MHON_02:BSD-01","CL_MHON_03:BSD-03"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"Composite","passages":[]},{"id":"MC_MHON_L1_01:BSD-02","text":"Causalitas non procedit sola successione efficienti, sed per constitutionem formalem, mediationem relationalem et translationem inter ordines.","refs":["CL_MHON_01:XD-01","CL_MHON_02:BSD-04","CL_MHON_03:BSD-02"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"Composite","passages":[]},{"id":"MC_MHON_L1_01:BSD-03","text":"Tempus fit compositio plurium recursionum: regressus ad prius ontologicum, circuitus apparitionis, restitutio sedis et manifestatio ulterioris ordinis.","refs":["CL_MHON_01:HSI-03","CL_MHON_02:HSI-03","CL_MHON_03:HSI-03"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"Composite","passages":[]},{"id":"MC_MHON_L1_01:BSD-04","text":"Subiectum est lector-constructor et trajectoria mutabilis, qui dependentias restituit atque ipse per conversionem, dislocationem et recompositionem configuratur.","refs":["CL_MHON_01:HSI-02","CL_MHON_02:BSD-03","CL_MHON_03:HSI-02"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"Composite","passages":[]},{"id":"MC_MHON_L1_01:BSD-05","text":"Cognitio fit ars genealogica: obiectum intellegitur cum series a principio minimo per mediationes ad formam manifestam et locum symbolicum restituitur.","refs":["CL_MHON_01:BSD-01","CL_MHON_02:ALT-B03","CL_MHON_03:MME-05"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"Composite","passages":[]},{"id":"MC_MHON_L1_01:BSD-06","text":"Potentia non est autonomia, sed facultas convertendi dependentiam in manifestationem, partialitatem in complementum et dislocationem in ordinem.","refs":["CL_MHON_01:XD-06","CL_MHON_02:HSI-04","CL_MHON_03:XD-04"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"Composite","passages":[]},{"id":"MC_MHON_L1_01:BSD-07","text":"Possibilitas acquirit statum temporalem: quod nondum manifestum est potest per relationem formalem apparere, evanescere et alio gradu recurrere.","refs":["CL_MHON_01:XD-08","CL_MHON_02:MP-03","CL_MHON_03:RLE-04"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"Composite","passages":[]},{"id":"MC_MHON_L1_01:BSD-08","text":"Methodus systematis performativa est, quia ipsa dispositio figurarum, numerorum et regressuum unitatem transdisciplinarem quam proponit materialiter construit.","refs":["CL_MHON_01:ALT-B05","CL_MHON_02:ALT-B05","CL_MHON_03:ALT-B05"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"Composite","passages":[]}],"original_name":"Book-Scale Super-Derivatives"},{"number":"10","name":"Recursive Loops, Escalation Paths, and Conceptual Attractors","items":[{"id":"MC_MHON_L1_01:RLE-01","text":"Compressio formarum in unitatem generat re-expansionem per cosmologiam, temporalitatem et numerum; effectus expansae series ad unitatem originariam rursus referuntur.","refs":["CL_MHON_01:RLE-01","CL_MHON_02:RLE-03","CL_MHON_03:RLE-03"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"Composite","passages":[]},{"id":"MC_MHON_L1_01:RLE-02","text":"Centrum peripheriam constituit, peripheria necessitatem centri manifestat, atque translatio centri in fluxum relationem staticam in productionem convertit.","refs":["CL_MHON_01:RLE-03","CL_MHON_02:RMD-01","CL_MHON_03:RMD-03"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"Composite","passages":[]},{"id":"MC_MHON_L1_01:RLE-03","text":"Partialitas complementum postulat, complementum unitatem temporalem vel numericam producit, et unitas nova initium ulterioris seriei fit.","refs":["CL_MHON_02:GA-06","CL_MHON_02:RLE-05","CL_MHON_03:GA-06"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"Composite","passages":[]},{"id":"MC_MHON_L1_01:RLE-04","text":"Dislocatio reditum movet, reditus rectam constituit, recta fluxum ordinat, atque figura restituta novam compositionem admittit.","refs":["CL_MHON_03:GA-03","CL_MHON_03:RMD-04","CL_MHON_03:RLE-01"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"Composite","passages":[]},{"id":"MC_MHON_L1_01:RLE-05","text":"Punctum producit lineam, linea figuram, figura numerum, numerus litteram et locum; locus symbolicus extrema catenae ad principium configurativum reducit.","refs":["CL_MHON_03:XD-05","CL_MHON_03:XD-03","CL_MHON_03:RMD-06"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"Composite","passages":[]},{"id":"MC_MHON_L1_01:RLE-06","text":"Manifestatio latentiam dissolvit, sed simul novam absentiam vel structuram nondum apparentem parit; revelatio igitur suum velamen recursivum generat.","refs":["CL_MHON_01:RLE-02","CL_MHON_02:RMD-06","CL_MHON_03:BSA-04"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"Composite","passages":[]},{"id":"MC_MHON_L1_01:RLE-07","text":"Analogia ordines remotos coniungit, coniunctio fecunditatem systematis auget, sed amplificatio hiatus demonstrativos producit qui novas correspondentias postulant.","refs":["CL_MHON_01:CGD-07","CL_MHON_02:FL-03","CL_MHON_03:RLE-06"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"Tension","passages":[]},{"id":"MC_MHON_L1_01:RLE-08","text":"Attractor supremus est unitas relationalis transformativa, in qua centrum invarians et fluxus mutabilis non conciliantur per immobilitatem, sed per recursionem ordinatam.","refs":["CL_MHON_01:RLE-06","CL_MHON_02:RLE-04","CL_MHON_03:RLE-05"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"Tension","passages":[]}],"original_name":"Recursive Loops, Escalation Paths, and Conceptual Attractors"},{"number":"11","name":"Formal Generative Schema of the Whole Book","items":[{"id":"MC_MHON_L1_01:FGS-B01","text":"Creatio legibilis → campus formarum operativarum → compressio in repertorium geometricum minimum.","refs":["CL_MHON_01:GA-01","CL_MHON_01:GA-02"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"Composite","passages":[]},{"id":"MC_MHON_L1_01:FGS-B02","text":"Punctum-Monas → centrum → linea et circulus → pluralitas extensa principio simplici subordinata.","refs":["CL_MHON_01:RMD-02","CL_MHON_01:RLE-03"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"Composite","passages":[]},{"id":"MC_MHON_L1_01:FGS-B03","text":"Centrum geometricum → distributio cosmica → dignitas Solaris → relatio Lunaris mutabilis.","refs":["CL_MHON_02:GA-01","CL_MHON_02:GA-02","CL_MHON_02:GA-03"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"Composite","passages":[]},{"id":"MC_MHON_L1_01:FGS-B04","text":"Partialitas Lunaris → complementum Solare → coniunctio contrariorum → dies → principium Lucis.","refs":["CL_MHON_02:GA-06","CL_MHON_02:GA-07","CL_MHON_02:GA-08"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"Composite","passages":[]},{"id":"MC_MHON_L1_01:FGS-B05","text":"Punctum productivum → fluxus → linea recta → restitutio partium dislocatarum → ordo naturalis.","refs":["CL_MHON_03:RMD-03","CL_MHON_03:GA-03","CL_MHON_03:RMD-04"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"Composite","passages":[]},{"id":"MC_MHON_L1_01:FGS-B06","text":"Figura rectilinea → quaternarius → denarius → crux → littera, traditio et locus symbolicus.","refs":["CL_MHON_03:GA-05","CL_MHON_03:GA-06","CL_MHON_03:RMD-06"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"Composite","passages":[]},{"id":"MC_MHON_L1_01:FGS-B07","text":"Latentia vel absentia → relatio manifestans → apparitio → reditus variatus → amplificatio ordinis.","refs":["CL_MHON_01:RLE-02","CL_MHON_02:GA-09","CL_MHON_03:RLE-04"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"Composite","passages":[]},{"id":"MC_MHON_L1_01:FGS-B08","text":"Forma exacta + relatio conservata → identitas operativa; corruptio configurationis → interruptio translationis → error cognitionis.","refs":["CL_MHON_01:GA-08","CL_MHON_01:RLE-04","CL_MHON_03:BSD-04"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"Composite","passages":[]},{"id":"MC_MHON_L1_01:FGS-B09","text":"Necessitas geometrica → translatio analogica → convergentia transdisciplinaris → clausura symbolica incompleta → nova exigentia reconstructionis.","refs":["CL_MHON_01:GA-10","CL_MHON_03:BSD-06","CL_MHON_03:SP-06"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"Tension","passages":[]}],"original_name":"Formal Generative Schema of the Whole Book"},{"number":"12","name":"Whole-Book Argument Flow Map","items":[{"id":"MC_MHON_L1_01:AFM-B01","text":"Scriptura creationis → reductio ad Punctum-Monadem → centrum et peripheria → distributio cosmica → complementum temporale → fluxus naturalis → amplificatio numerico-symbolica.","refs":["CL_MHON_01:BAS-02","CL_MHON_02:XD-04","CL_MHON_03:XD-05"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"Composite","passages":[]},{"id":"MC_MHON_L1_01:AFM-B02","text":"Figura minima → relatio generatrix → mutatio identitatis → reditus variatus → translatio inter campos → unitas distributa.","refs":["CL_MHON_01:GA-03","CL_MHON_02:RMD-04","CL_MHON_03:BSD-04"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"Composite","passages":[]},{"id":"MC_MHON_L1_01:AFM-B03","text":"Negatio autonomiae → reconstructio dependentiarum → absorptio absentiae in transitionem → clausura transformativa → residuum analogicum nondum demonstratum.","refs":["CL_MHON_01:GNX-03","CL_MHON_02:DBN-03","CL_MHON_03:FL-01"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"Tension","passages":[]}],"original_name":"Whole-Book Argument Flow Map"},{"number":"13","name":"Macro Map-Effects","items":[{"id":"MC_MHON_L1_01:MME-01","text":"Geometria reclassificatur ex instrumento descriptionis in grammaticam qua esse, tempus, causalitas et significatio producuntur.","refs":["CL_MHON_01:MME-01","CL_MHON_02:BSD-05","CL_MHON_03:BSD-01"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"Composite","passages":[]},{"id":"MC_MHON_L1_01:MME-02","text":"Oppositio inter signum et rem corruit, quia configuratio eadem rem manifestat, ordinem eius distribuit et locum symbolicum instituit.","refs":["CL_MHON_01:MME-02","CL_MHON_02:ALT-B05","CL_MHON_03:XD-03"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"Composite","passages":[]},{"id":"MC_MHON_L1_01:MME-03","text":"Oppositio inter unitatem et pluralitatem substituitur relationibus centri, mediationis et complementi.","refs":["CL_MHON_01:MME-04","CL_MHON_02:GA-05","CL_MHON_02:GA-10"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"Composite","passages":[]},{"id":"MC_MHON_L1_01:MME-04","text":"Tempus reclassificatur ex successione externa in effectum relationis, reditus et manifestationis ulterioris.","refs":["CL_MHON_01:BSD-03","CL_MHON_02:MME-04","CL_MHON_03:BSD-05"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"Composite","passages":[]},{"id":"MC_MHON_L1_01:MME-05","text":"Numerus reclassificatur ex quantitate abstracta in amplitudinem transformationis quae figuram, litteram, doctrinam et locum conectit.","refs":["CL_MHON_03:GA-06","CL_MHON_03:MME-02","CL_MHON_03:BSD-04"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"Composite","passages":[]},{"id":"MC_MHON_L1_01:MME-06","text":"Absentia, partialitas et dislocatio reclassificantur ex defectibus in motores transitionis et productionis ordinis.","refs":["CL_MHON_02:GA-09","CL_MHON_02:GA-06","CL_MHON_03:MME-03"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"Composite","passages":[]},{"id":"MC_MHON_L1_01:MME-07","text":"Fines disciplinarum mutantur in vias translationis: differentiae servantur, sed nulla regio auctoritatem ontologicam separatam retinet.","refs":["CL_MHON_01:GNX-07","CL_MHON_02:RMD-08","CL_MHON_03:DBN-04"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"Composite","passages":[]},{"id":"MC_MHON_L1_01:MME-08","text":"Lectio fit reconstructio genealogica configurationis, non descriptio superficiei neque collectio correspondentiarum nominalium.","refs":["CL_MHON_01:BSD-01","CL_MHON_02:ALT-B03","CL_MHON_03:MME-05"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"Composite","passages":[]}],"original_name":"Macro Map-Effects"},{"number":"14","name":"Altitude Integration","items":[{"id":"MC_MHON_L1_01:ALT-B01","text":"In gradu superficiei apparent character Monadis, punctum, linea, circulus, Sol, Luna, stilla, crux et series numericae tamquam obiecta determinata.","refs":["CL_MHON_01:ALT-B01","CL_MHON_02:ALT-B01","CL_MHON_03:ALT-B01"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"Composite","passages":[]},{"id":"MC_MHON_L1_01:ALT-B02","text":"In gradu structurali operantur dependentia a centro, mediatio, complementum, fluxus, restitutio et aggregatio tamquam mechanismi communes.","refs":["CL_MHON_01:ALT-B02","CL_MHON_02:ALT-B02","CL_MHON_03:ALT-B02"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"Composite","passages":[]},{"id":"MC_MHON_L1_01:ALT-B03","text":"In gradu metastrategico certamen de legitimitate transfertur a nominibus et apparentiis ad reconstructionem relationum quae figuram producunt.","refs":["CL_MHON_01:ALT-B03","CL_MHON_02:ALT-B03","CL_MHON_03:ALT-B03"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"Composite","passages":[]},{"id":"MC_MHON_L1_01:ALT-B04","text":"In gradu ontologico et temporali realitas est scriptura relationum transformabilium, causalitas est translatio formalis, et tempus est reditus qui statum mutat.","refs":["CL_MHON_01:ALT-B04","CL_MHON_02:ALT-B04","CL_MHON_03:ALT-B04"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"Composite","passages":[]},{"id":"MC_MHON_L1_01:ALT-B05","text":"In gradu methodologico et performativo dispositio textus figuras comprimit, relationes producit et ordines heterogeneos in unam machinam reconstructivam cogit.","refs":["CL_MHON_01:ALT-B05","CL_MHON_02:ALT-B05","CL_MHON_03:ALT-B05"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"Composite","passages":[]}],"original_name":"Altitude Integration"},{"number":"15","name":"Internal Tensions, Mutation Points, and Fault Lines","items":[{"id":"MC_MHON_L1_01:FL-01","text":"Centrum invarians et punctum fluxionale non plene congruunt: alterum dependentiam stabilem, alterum productionem motu continuo explicat.","refs":["CL_MHON_01:RLE-03","CL_MHON_03:RMD-03","CL_MHON_03:MP-01"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"Tension","passages":[]},{"id":"MC_MHON_L1_01:FL-02","text":"Necessitas demonstrativa et amplificatio analogica diversam vim epistemicam habent, sed systema saepe transitum inter eas sine regula universali perficit.","refs":["CL_MHON_01:GA-10","CL_MHON_01:FL-06","CL_MHON_03:FL-01"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"Tension","passages":[]},{"id":"MC_MHON_L1_01:FL-03","text":"Exactitudo figurae fixae et identitas per transformationem servata tensionem efficiunt, quia nondum semper constat quae variationes structuram servent et quae corrumpant.","refs":["CL_MHON_01:GA-08","CL_MHON_02:GA-09","CL_MHON_03:BSD-04"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"Tension","passages":[]},{"id":"MC_MHON_L1_01:FL-04","text":"Principium hierarchicum ordinem producit, sed partialitas et dislocatio etiam virtutem generativam possident; causalitas inter dominationem et emergentiam oscillat.","refs":["CL_MHON_02:GA-02","CL_MHON_02:GA-06","CL_MHON_03:XD-04"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"Tension","passages":[]},{"id":"MC_MHON_L1_01:FL-05","text":"Universalitas legibilitatis cum restrictione competentiae manet fragilis, quia campi signorum communis praesentia facultatem recte interpretandi non distribuit.","refs":["CL_MHON_01:BAS-08","CL_MHON_01:DBN-05","CL_MHON_01:MRT-B05"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"Tension","passages":[]},{"id":"MC_MHON_L1_01:FL-06","text":"Relatio symbolica et causalitas physica saepe eadem figura exprimuntur, sed status ontologicus huius identitatis non ubique definitur.","refs":["CL_MHON_01:FL-02","CL_MHON_02:FL-03","CL_MHON_03:FL-04"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"Tension","passages":[]},{"id":"MC_MHON_L1_01:MP-01","text":"Punctum mutatur ab elemento minimo scripturae in centrum ontologicum, referens cosmicum et principium fluxus productivi.","refs":["CL_MHON_01:MP-02","CL_MHON_02:MP-01","CL_MHON_03:MP-01"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"Tension","passages":[]},{"id":"MC_MHON_L1_01:MP-02","text":"Linea mutatur a forma primaria in effectum Puncti, viam restitutionis et tractum fluxus materialis.","refs":["CL_MHON_01:RMD-01","CL_MHON_01:CGD-05","CL_MHON_03:RMD-04"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"Tension","passages":[]},{"id":"MC_MHON_L1_01:MP-03","text":"Repraesentatio mutatur ab imitatione posteriore in manifestationem productivam, deinde in ordinationem cosmicam et institutionem loci symbolici.","refs":["CL_MHON_01:MP-01","CL_MHON_02:ALT-B05","CL_MHON_03:XD-03"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"Tension","passages":[]},{"id":"MC_MHON_L1_01:MP-04","text":"Tempus mutatur ab ordine originis ad diem relatione productum et tandem ad reditum spiralem qui latentiam manifestat.","refs":["CL_MHON_01:MP-04","CL_MHON_02:BSD-02","CL_MHON_03:HSI-03"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"Tension","passages":[]},{"id":"MC_MHON_L1_01:MP-05","text":"Repetitio mutatur ab invariantia relationis in reditum periodicum et postremo in productionem configuris antea latentis.","refs":["CL_MHON_01:RLE-03","CL_MHON_02:RMD-06","CL_MHON_03:MP-04"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"Tension","passages":[]},{"id":"MC_MHON_L1_01:MP-06","text":"Numerus mutatur ab enumeratione in structuram extensivam quae figuram, crucem, litteram et locum doctrinalem constituit.","refs":["CL_MHON_03:GA-06","CL_MHON_03:RMD-02","CL_MHON_03:MP-02"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"Tension","passages":[]}],"original_name":"Internal Tensions, Mutation Points, and Fault Lines"},{"number":"16","name":"Hidden System Image","items":[{"id":"MC_MHON_L1_01:HSI-01","text":"Realitas implicite est machina scriptoria, concentrica et fluxionalis, in qua centrum invarians motus, circuitus et translationes symbolicas generat.","refs":["CL_MHON_01:HSI-01","CL_MHON_02:HSI-01","CL_MHON_03:HSI-01"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"Composite","passages":[]},{"id":"MC_MHON_L1_01:HSI-02","text":"Subiectum intra machinam est lector-constructor dislocabilis et relationalis, qui formam suam per conversionem, reditum et recompositionem acquirit.","refs":["CL_MHON_01:HSI-02","CL_MHON_02:HSI-02","CL_MHON_03:HSI-02"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"Composite","passages":[]},{"id":"MC_MHON_L1_01:HSI-03","text":"Tempus est spiralis regressus: ad principium redit, sed singulo circuitu novam formam, diem vel configurationem latentem producit.","refs":["CL_MHON_01:HSI-03","CL_MHON_02:HSI-03","CL_MHON_03:HSI-03"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"Composite","passages":[]},{"id":"MC_MHON_L1_01:HSI-04","text":"Certamen principale est conservare canalem exactum inter principium et transformationes eius contra autonomiam partium, corruptionem relationum et clausuram praematuram.","refs":["CL_MHON_01:HSI-04","CL_MHON_02:HSI-04","CL_MHON_03:HSI-04"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"Composite","passages":[]}],"original_name":"Hidden System Image"},{"number":"17","name":"Minimal Whole-Book Reconstruction Test","items":[{"id":"MC_MHON_L1_01:MRT-B01","text":"Axiomata praesentia sufficiunt ad restituendam machinam principalem: forma operativa ad principium simplex regreditur, per relationes explicatur et in tempus, fluxum atque symbolum redit.","refs":["CL_MHON_01:MRT-B01","CL_MHON_02:MRT-B01","CL_MHON_03:MRT-B01"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"Composite","passages":[]},{"id":"MC_MHON_L1_01:MRT-B02","text":"Structura firmissima est continuatio inter non-autonomiam elementorum, recursionem generativam et translationem geometriae in ordines superiores.","refs":["CL_MHON_01:MRT-B03","CL_MHON_02:MRT-B02","CL_MHON_03:MRT-B03"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"Composite","passages":[]},{"id":"MC_MHON_L1_01:MRT-B03","text":"Minus determinata manet ratio qua necessitas geometrica causalitatem ontologicam, astronomicam, physicam et magicam sine saltu analogico constituit.","refs":["CL_MHON_01:MRT-B02","CL_MHON_02:FL-03","CL_MHON_03:FL-03"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"Tension","passages":[]},{"id":"MC_MHON_L1_01:MRT-B04","text":"Systema hoc gradu cohaeret non per identitatem terminorum, sed per reditum functionalem eiusdem schemae dependentiae, transformationis et complementi.","refs":["CL_MHON_01:MRT-B04","CL_MHON_02:MRT-B02","CL_MHON_03:MRT-B02"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"Composite","passages":[]},{"id":"MC_MHON_L1_01:MRT-B05","text":"Cohaerentia maxime nititur ordine recursivo et vi performativa dispositionis, non catena deductiva uniformiter completa.","refs":["CL_MHON_01:ALT-B05","CL_MHON_02:MRT-B03","CL_MHON_03:MRT-B04"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"Tension","passages":[]},{"id":"MC_MHON_L1_01:MRT-B06","text":"Restitutio verum motum servat dum compressionem, regressum, circulationem et amplificationem distinguit neque mutationes earum in repetitionem identicam complanat.","refs":["CL_MHON_01:MRT-B07","CL_MHON_02:MRT-B03","CL_MHON_03:MRT-B04"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"Composite","passages":[]},{"id":"MC_MHON_L1_01:MRT-B07","text":"Reductioni axiomaticae resistunt differentiae inter demonstrationem et analogiam, causalitatem physicam et symbolicam, atque exactam conservationem et mutationem legitimam formae.","refs":["CL_MHON_01:MRT-B06","CL_MHON_02:FL-03","CL_MHON_03:FL-04"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"Tension","passages":[]}],"original_name":"Minimal Whole-Book Reconstruction Test"}],"language_code":"la","language_name":"Latina","translations":[{"id":"CAREVAR_20261006T223555814019Z_e5e8ef5c","language_code":"en","language_name":"English","title":"Mhon L1 01","source_sha256":"501f36f3d327569975bc9529e186c0f85956bbc0db99511924869309b0d59582","sha256":"d20d1cebaa1f0a3c2f13dd84ed0e1476c18fa3dab313a30d8bd1ea06880c56a9","rendition_type":"machine","review_status":"machine","model":"gpt-5.6-terra","created":"2026-10-06T22:35:55.816126+00:00","sections":[{"number":"1","name":"Book-Scale Argumental Spine","items":[{"id":"MC_MHON_L1_01:BAS-01","text":"The system of the present level asks how the minimal character can condense geometry, cosmology, temporality, motion, and number into a single generative machine.","refs":["CL_MHON_01:BAS-01","CL_MHON_02:BS-01","CL_MHON_03:SP-01"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"Composite","passages":[]},{"id":"MC_MHON_L1_01:BAS-02","text":"The global thesis establishes geometric form as the operation by which things are manifested, cosmic relations produce time, and material motions are converted into numbers and doctrinal signs.","refs":["CL_MHON_01:BAS-02","CL_MHON_02:BS-02","CL_MHON_03:SP-02"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"Composite","passages":[]},{"id":"MC_MHON_L1_01:BAS-03","text":"The argument joins regression to simple principles with symbolic reconstruction, so that point, center, line, circle, and cross become not common terms but stages of a single causality.","refs":["CL_MHON_01:BAS-03","CL_MHON_02:BS-03","CL_MHON_03:SP-03"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"Composite","passages":[]},{"id":"MC_MHON_L1_01:BAS-04","text":"The movement is recursive, spiral, and cumulative: form is compressed into a principle, the principle is unfolded through diverse fields, and the return transforms form rather than repeating it identically.","refs":["CL_MHON_01:BAS-04","CL_MHON_02:BS-04","CL_MHON_03:SP-04"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"Composite","passages":[]},{"id":"MC_MHON_L1_01:BAS-05","text":"Proof operates through geometric necessity, relational translation, and analogical fecundity, although deductive force is not equal in all translations.","refs":["CL_MHON_01:BAS-05","CL_MHON_02:BS-03","CL_MHON_03:BSD-06"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"Tension","passages":[]},{"id":"MC_MHON_L1_01:BAS-06","text":"The direction of the system proceeds from the manifest sign to the simple principle, thence to cosmic and fluxional relations, and finally to temporal, numerical, and doctrinal orders.","refs":["CL_MHON_01:BSD-03","CL_MHON_02:XD-05","CL_MHON_03:XD-05"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"Composite","passages":[]},{"id":"MC_MHON_L1_01:BAS-07","text":"The form of exposition accomplishes its conclusion, because compression, the arrangement of figures, and the concatenation of correspondences produce the very operative unity that they affirm.","refs":["CL_MHON_01:ALT-B05","CL_MHON_02:ALT-B05","CL_MHON_03:ALT-B05"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"Composite","passages":[]},{"id":"MC_MHON_L1_01:BAS-08","text":"Closure is generative rather than immobile: the center remains, but the periphery, Moon, flux, and numerical series open new circuits from prior closures.","refs":["CL_MHON_01:BAS-07","CL_MHON_02:BS-05","CL_MHON_03:SP-06"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"Composite","passages":[]},{"id":"MC_MHON_L1_01:BAS-09","text":"The system coheres around the relation between unity and transformation, but the connection between geometric necessity, ontological causality, and symbolic correspondence is not ultimately demonstrated.","refs":["CL_MHON_01:MRT-B02","CL_MHON_02:MRT-B02","CL_MHON_03:MRT-B03"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"Tension","passages":[]}],"original_name":"Book-Scale Argumental Spine"},{"number":"2","name":"Global Load-Bearing Axioms","items":[{"id":"MC_MHON_L1_01:GA-01","text":"[Persistent] Geometric form is not a passive image, but an operation that effects the manifestation, ordering, or translation of a thing.","refs":["CL_MHON_01:GA-03","CL_MHON_02:BS-03","CL_MHON_03:GA-01"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"Composite","passages":[]},{"id":"MC_MHON_L1_01:GA-02","text":"[Mutating] The point is transformed from a minimal constituent into an ontological and cosmic center, then into the origin of flux and production.","refs":["CL_MHON_01:RMD-02","CL_MHON_02:RMD-01","CL_MHON_03:RMD-03"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"Composite","passages":[]},{"id":"MC_MHON_L1_01:GA-03","text":"[Persistent] The identity of each element is constituted through relation to principle, center, mediator, complement, or place, not through isolated substance.","refs":["CL_MHON_01:GA-05","CL_MHON_02:GA-03","CL_MHON_03:GA-07"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"Composite","passages":[]},{"id":"MC_MHON_L1_01:GA-04","text":"[Emergent] Geometry is a common ontological grammar through which the dependence of things, cosmic distribution, and symbolic production are read according to the same schema.","refs":["CL_MHON_01:GA-06","CL_MHON_02:BSD-01","CL_MHON_03:BSD-01"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"Composite","passages":[]},{"id":"MC_MHON_L1_01:GA-05","text":"[Mutating] Stable identity yields to identity preserved through transformation, because subordination, disappearance, return, and repetition alter an element's state without destroying its function.","refs":["CL_MHON_01:RMD-01","CL_MHON_02:GA-09","CL_MHON_03:MP-04"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"Composite","passages":[]},{"id":"MC_MHON_L1_01:GA-06","text":"[Emergent] Partiality, displacement, and deficiency are generative conditions of order, because complement or restoration produces a broader unity from them.","refs":["CL_MHON_01:XD-06","CL_MHON_02:GA-06","CL_MHON_03:XD-04"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"Composite","passages":[]},{"id":"MC_MHON_L1_01:GA-07","text":"[Emergent] Time is not a pre-given receptacle, but an effect of return to origin, the complement of opposites, periodic return, and latent manifestation.","refs":["CL_MHON_01:BSD-03","CL_MHON_02:BSD-02","CL_MHON_03:BSD-05"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"Composite","passages":[]},{"id":"MC_MHON_L1_01:GA-08","text":"[Persistent] A simple structure migrates through disciplines, so that cosmology, physics, numeration, magic, and doctrine become translations of the same order.","refs":["CL_MHON_01:GA-12","CL_MHON_02:RMD-08","CL_MHON_03:GA-07"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"Composite","passages":[]},{"id":"MC_MHON_L1_01:GA-09","text":"[Persistent] Minimal formal difference affects semantic force and relational causality; therefore, the integrity of an object depends on the integrity of its configuration.","refs":["CL_MHON_01:GA-08","CL_MHON_02:ALT-B05","CL_MHON_03:BSD-04"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"Composite","passages":[]},{"id":"MC_MHON_L1_01:GA-10","text":"[Persistent] Legitimate knowledge reconstructively follows generative relations; it does not merely collect isolated properties or nominal similarities.","refs":["CL_MHON_01:BSD-01","CL_MHON_02:ALT-B03","CL_MHON_03:MME-05"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"Composite","passages":[]},{"id":"MC_MHON_L1_01:GA-11","text":"[Terminal] No figure, body, number, or periphery autonomously possesses its rationale outside generative relations.","refs":["CL_MHON_01:GNX-03","CL_MHON_02:GNX-02","CL_MHON_03:GA-09"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"Negative","passages":[]},{"id":"MC_MHON_L1_01:GA-12","text":"[Mutating] Return does not simply restore the prior state, but increases ontological, temporal, or symbolic magnitude through each repetition.","refs":["CL_MHON_01:RLE-01","CL_MHON_02:RLE-03","CL_MHON_03:RLE-04"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"Composite","passages":[]},{"id":"MC_MHON_L1_01:GA-13","text":"[Attractor] Monadically-relational unity draws center, complement, number, figure, letter, and place into a distributed configuration.","refs":["CL_MHON_01:RLE-06","CL_MHON_02:RLE-04","CL_MHON_03:RLE-05"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"Composite","passages":[]},{"id":"MC_MHON_L1_01:GA-14","text":"[Terminal] The closure of the system is open and transformative: the invariant of relation is preserved, but the following circuit always establishes a new principle or latent order.","refs":["CL_MHON_01:BAS-08","CL_MHON_02:BS-05","CL_MHON_03:SP-06"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"Tension","passages":[]}],"original_name":"Global Load-Bearing Axioms"},{"number":"3","name":"Recurrence, Mutation, and Displacement Matrix","items":[{"id":"MC_MHON_L1_01:RMD-01","text":"The point retains its minimal identity, but its role is successively amplified from a constituent of figure to universal center, cosmic referent, and source of flux.","refs":["CL_MHON_01:RMD-02","CL_MHON_02:RMD-01","CL_MHON_03:RMD-03"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"Composite","passages":[]},{"id":"MC_MHON_L1_01:RMD-02","text":"The line passes from the simplest and dependent form into a path of restoration and a genealogy of material flux; descriptive stability becomes directional operation.","refs":["CL_MHON_01:RMD-01","CL_MHON_03:RMD-04","CL_MHON_03:RMD-05"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"Composite","passages":[]},{"id":"MC_MHON_L1_01:RMD-03","text":"Circle and periphery first express dependence on the center, then effect cosmic circulation, and finally yield their transformative role to rectilinear repetition.","refs":["CL_MHON_01:RLE-03","CL_MHON_02:HSI-03","CL_MHON_03:RLE-04"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"Composite","passages":[]},{"id":"MC_MHON_L1_01:RMD-04","text":"The monad recedes as a name beyond the first unity, but its function persists in the complementary relation and distributed symbolic unity.","refs":["CL_MHON_01:RMD-03","CL_MHON_02:RLE-04","CL_MHON_03:RLE-05"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"Composite","passages":[]},{"id":"MC_MHON_L1_01:RMD-05","text":"Representation is transferred from the production of manifestation into the constitution of cosmic order and then into the concatenation of number, letter, and place.","refs":["CL_MHON_01:RMD-04","CL_MHON_02:ALT-B05","CL_MHON_03:BSD-04"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"Composite","passages":[]},{"id":"MC_MHON_L1_01:RMD-06","text":"Light changes from a mode of manifestation into Solar mediation and finally into a temporal principle, while in the numerical field its role is absorbed by flux and symbolic translation.","refs":["CL_MHON_01:GA-03","CL_MHON_02:RMD-05","CL_MHON_02:MP-02"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"Composite","passages":[]},{"id":"MC_MHON_L1_01:RMD-07","text":"Complement changes from the subordination of plurality to the center into Lunar completion and later into numerical aggregation that extends the quaternary beyond itself.","refs":["CL_MHON_01:XD-06","CL_MHON_02:GA-06","CL_MHON_03:GA-06"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"Composite","passages":[]},{"id":"MC_MHON_L1_01:RMD-08","text":"Return preserves function but changes scope: ontological regression, Lunar circuit, natural restoration, and numerical repetition are transformations of the state of the same machine.","refs":["CL_MHON_01:BSD-03","CL_MHON_02:RMD-06","CL_MHON_03:RLE-01"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"Composite","passages":[]},{"id":"MC_MHON_L1_01:RMD-09","text":"Material exactness, locally a property of impression, is amplified into a general norm of the integrity of figurative and symbolic relations.","refs":["CL_MHON_01:BAS-06","CL_MHON_01:GA-08","CL_MHON_03:BSD-04"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"Composite","passages":[]},{"id":"MC_MHON_L1_01:RMD-10","text":"Concealment changes into Lunar absence and the latency of numerical order; the doctrinal veil vanishes as a local institution, but the non-manifest remains as a source of transition.","refs":["CL_MHON_01:RMD-05","CL_MHON_02:GA-09","CL_MHON_03:BSA-04"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"Composite","passages":[]}],"original_name":"Recurrence, Mutation, and Displacement Matrix"},{"number":"4","name":"Lower-Layer vs Current-Level Distinction","items":[{"id":"MC_MHON_L1_01:CGD-01","text":"Royal patronage, typographic administration, and the selection of recipients remain local mechanisms; only the principle that relational integrity requires material conditions rises to the present level.","refs":["CL_MHON_01:CGD-01","CL_MHON_01:GA-11"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"Direct","passages":[]},{"id":"MC_MHON_L1_01:CGD-02","text":"The distinction between non-existence and latency is not globalized according to its proper definitions; only the thesis persists upward that absence or possibility can be included in the process of manifestation.","refs":["CL_MHON_01:CGD-02","CL_MHON_01:XD-08"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"Direct","passages":[]},{"id":"MC_MHON_L1_01:CGD-03","text":"Earth, Sun, and Moon remain local cosmological determinations; the structure of the present level is sustained by the relation among center, mediation, partiality, and complement.","refs":["CL_MHON_02:CGD-01","CL_MHON_02:CGD-03"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"Composite","passages":[]},{"id":"MC_MHON_L1_01:CGD-04","text":"Natural seats, the drop, and Mechanica Magia are local examples; globally, there is preserved the transition by which displacement is restored through ordered flux into producible figure.","refs":["CL_MHON_03:GA-03","CL_MHON_03:CGD-03","CL_MHON_03:XD-04"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"Composite","passages":[]},{"id":"MC_MHON_L1_01:CGD-05","text":"The Roman letter, the octonary, and the particular connection of the ternary with the septenary do not rise into universal axioms, but they attest that symbolic identity is distributed through heterogeneous orders.","refs":["CL_MHON_03:CGD-02","CL_MHON_03:RMD-07","CL_MHON_03:CGD-06"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"Tension","passages":[]},{"id":"MC_MHON_L1_01:CGD-06","text":"The dependence of figure on point, of the Lunar on the Solar, and of number on its relations is amplified upward into a common law of non-autonomy.","refs":["CL_MHON_01:GNX-03","CL_MHON_02:GNX-02","CL_MHON_03:BSA-03"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"Composite","passages":[]},{"id":"MC_MHON_L1_01:CGD-07","text":"Production through form, time through complement, and sign through flux are joined only at this level into a single causality of translation.","refs":["CL_MHON_01:XD-02","CL_MHON_02:BSD-02","CL_MHON_03:XD-01"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"Composite","passages":[]},{"id":"MC_MHON_L1_01:CGD-08","text":"The greater transition between units occurs when the immobile point and distributive center are converted into a source of motion, without loss of the original function.","refs":["CL_MHON_01:MP-02","CL_MHON_02:MP-01","CL_MHON_03:MP-01"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"Composite","passages":[]},{"id":"MC_MHON_L1_01:CGD-09","text":"The image of geometry as a collection of separate figures becomes obsolete; the present level codifies it as a series of generative, temporal, and transdisciplinary relations.","refs":["CL_MHON_01:MME-01","CL_MHON_02:BSD-01","CL_MHON_03:CGD-07"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"Composite","passages":[]}],"original_name":"Lower-Layer vs Current-Level Distinction"},{"number":"5","name":"Global Apophatic Structure","items":[{"id":"MC_MHON_L1_01:GNX-01","text":"A sign cannot be a passive representation or an extrinsic convention, because form within the system manifests a thing or constitutes its order.","refs":["CL_MHON_01:GNX-01","CL_MHON_01:GNX-02","CL_MHON_02:ALT-B05"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"Negative","passages":[]},{"id":"MC_MHON_L1_01:GNX-02","text":"No figure, Moon, periphery, or numerical series is understood autonomously outside its principle and relations.","refs":["CL_MHON_01:GNX-03","CL_MHON_02:GNX-02","CL_MHON_03:DBN-03"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"Negative","passages":[]},{"id":"MC_MHON_L1_01:GNX-03","text":"Fixed identity and merely linear time are excluded, because disappearance, return, and repetition constitute identity through change.","refs":["CL_MHON_02:GNX-03","CL_MHON_02:GNX-04","CL_MHON_03:MP-04"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"Negative","passages":[]},{"id":"MC_MHON_L1_01:GNX-04","text":"Number is not an external measure or an abstract series that can be separated from figure, matter, letter, and place.","refs":["CL_MHON_03:GNX-01","CL_MHON_03:GNX-05","CL_MHON_03:BSA-03"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"Negative","passages":[]},{"id":"MC_MHON_L1_01:GNX-05","text":"Geometry, cosmology, physics, magic, and doctrine are not regions closed by absolute boundaries.","refs":["CL_MHON_01:GNX-07","CL_MHON_02:GNX-01","CL_MHON_03:DBN-04"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"Negative","passages":[]},{"id":"MC_MHON_L1_01:GNX-06","text":"Order cannot be immobile, because the center produces distribution, complement produces day, and displacement produces restoration.","refs":["CL_MHON_01:GA-09","CL_MHON_02:BS-05","CL_MHON_03:XD-04"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"Negative","passages":[]},{"id":"MC_MHON_L1_01:GNX-07","text":"Material or figurative difference cannot be dismissed as semantically indifferent, since identity is distributed among exact relations.","refs":["CL_MHON_01:GA-08","CL_MHON_01:RLE-04","CL_MHON_03:BSD-04"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"Negative","passages":[]},{"id":"MC_MHON_L1_01:GNX-08","text":"Authority, a common name, or unexamined analogy cannot replace intrinsic proof, although the system often relies on analogical transition itself.","refs":["CL_MHON_01:GA-10","CL_MHON_01:CGD-06","CL_MHON_03:BSD-06"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"Tension","passages":[]}],"original_name":"Global Apophatic Structure"},{"number":"6","name":"Deep Book Apophatics","items":[{"id":"MC_MHON_L1_01:DBN-01","text":"It must be false that reality contains an absolutely formless residual that is open to no manifestation, relation, or formal ordering.","refs":["CL_MHON_01:DBN-01","CL_MHON_03:BSA-01"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"Negative","passages":[]},{"id":"MC_MHON_L1_01:DBN-02","text":"It must be false that the orders of being, signifying, and producing are fundamentally separate.","refs":["CL_MHON_01:DBN-02","CL_MHON_01:MME-02","CL_MHON_03:BSA-02"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"Negative","passages":[]},{"id":"MC_MHON_L1_01:DBN-03","text":"It must be false that extension, plurality, or partiality can ground themselves without center, mediator, or complement.","refs":["CL_MHON_01:DBN-03","CL_MHON_02:DBN-01","CL_MHON_03:GNX-04"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"Negative","passages":[]},{"id":"MC_MHON_L1_01:DBN-04","text":"It must be false that time is already perfectly constituted prior to figurative relations, circuits, and transformations.","refs":["CL_MHON_02:DBN-02","CL_MHON_02:BSD-02","CL_MHON_03:BSD-05"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"Negative","passages":[]},{"id":"MC_MHON_L1_01:DBN-05","text":"It must be false that physical causality, geometric necessity, and symbolic operation constitute mutually external fields.","refs":["CL_MHON_01:GA-06","CL_MHON_03:GA-04","CL_MHON_03:DBN-04"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"Negative","passages":[]},{"id":"MC_MHON_L1_01:DBN-06","text":"It must be false that absence, displacement, and partiality are mere privations, because each generates a subsequent state.","refs":["CL_MHON_02:DBN-03","CL_MHON_03:XD-04","CL_MHON_01:XD-08"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"Negative","passages":[]}],"original_name":"Deep Book Apophatics"},{"number":"7","name":"Super-Apophatics of the Whole","items":[{"id":"MC_MHON_L1_01:BSA-01","text":"A pure outside, touched neither by form, relation, nor the possibility of manifestation, is ontologically unavailable.","refs":["CL_MHON_01:BSA-02","CL_MHON_02:BSA-03","CL_MHON_03:BSA-01"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"Negative","passages":[]},{"id":"MC_MHON_L1_01:BSA-02","text":"Absolute autonomy is impossible: the periphery answers to the center, the Moon to the Sun, the line to the point, and number to its configuration.","refs":["CL_MHON_01:BSA-01","CL_MHON_02:BSA-02","CL_MHON_03:BSA-03"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"Negative","passages":[]},{"id":"MC_MHON_L1_01:BSA-03","text":"A uniform reality without center, dignity, asymmetry, or complement is excluded, because relational difference itself produces order.","refs":["CL_MHON_02:BSA-01","CL_MHON_01:XD-06","CL_MHON_03:XD-04"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"Negative","passages":[]},{"id":"MC_MHON_L1_01:BSA-04","text":"Wholly closed demonstration remains an unavailable possibility: the system seeks formal necessity, but its ultimate amplifications require analogy and correspondence.","refs":["CL_MHON_01:MRT-B02","CL_MHON_02:FL-03","CL_MHON_03:FL-01"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"Tension","passages":[]},{"id":"MC_MHON_L1_01:BSA-05","text":"Pure immediacy is excluded, because manifestation always passes through figure, Solar mediation, flux, or numerical ordering.","refs":["CL_MHON_01:GNX-02","CL_MHON_02:GA-05","CL_MHON_03:RMD-05"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"Negative","passages":[]}],"original_name":"Super-Apophatics of the Whole"},{"number":"8","name":"Cross-Unit Derivative Propositions","items":[{"id":"MC_MHON_L1_01:XD-01","text":"At the present level, the monad ceases to be only a named figure and becomes a distributed function that orders center, complement, and symbolic unity under the same law of dependence.","refs":["MC_MHON_L1_01:GA-03","MC_MHON_L1_01:GA-04","MC_MHON_L1_01:GA-13","CL_MHON_01:RMD-03","CL_MHON_02:RLE-04","CL_MHON_03:RLE-05"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"Composite","passages":[]},{"id":"MC_MHON_L1_01:XD-02","text":"The point becomes at once invariant and productive: that which contains extension in the center also generates through flux the line and further compositions.","refs":["MC_MHON_L1_01:GA-02","MC_MHON_L1_01:GA-04","CL_MHON_01:RLE-03","CL_MHON_03:RMD-03","CL_MHON_03:XD-05"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"Composite","passages":[]},{"id":"MC_MHON_L1_01:XD-03","text":"The manifestation of a thing, the production of day, and the institution of a sign are forms of the same causality by which an ordered relation brings something legible into actuality.","refs":["MC_MHON_L1_01:GA-01","MC_MHON_L1_01:GA-07","MC_MHON_L1_01:GA-08","CL_MHON_01:XD-02","CL_MHON_02:BSD-02","CL_MHON_03:XD-03"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"Composite","passages":[]},{"id":"MC_MHON_L1_01:XD-04","text":"Partiality and displacement are not defects to be corrected extrinsically, but internal differences from which complement, restoration, and amplification emerge.","refs":["MC_MHON_L1_01:GA-05","MC_MHON_L1_01:GA-06","CL_MHON_02:GA-06","CL_MHON_03:XD-04","CL_MHON_01:XD-06"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"Composite","passages":[]},{"id":"MC_MHON_L1_01:XD-05","text":"Ontological regression, Lunar circuit, natural return, and configurative repetition show a common law: return repeats the origin only in order to produce a new level.","refs":["MC_MHON_L1_01:GA-07","MC_MHON_L1_01:GA-12","MC_MHON_L1_01:GA-14","CL_MHON_01:BSD-03","CL_MHON_02:RMD-06","CL_MHON_03:RLE-04"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"Composite","passages":[]},{"id":"MC_MHON_L1_01:XD-06","text":"Since identity is distributed among relations, exactness does not require the immobility of an element, but the preservation of structure through its changes.","refs":["MC_MHON_L1_01:GA-05","MC_MHON_L1_01:GA-09","CL_MHON_01:GA-08","CL_MHON_02:GA-09","CL_MHON_03:BSD-04"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"Composite","passages":[]},{"id":"MC_MHON_L1_01:XD-07","text":"Geometry becomes the common medium of ontological, cosmic, physical, and magical causalities, not because differences are abolished but because their conversions are formally ordered.","refs":["MC_MHON_L1_01:GA-04","MC_MHON_L1_01:GA-08","MC_MHON_L1_01:GA-10","CL_MHON_01:GA-06","CL_MHON_02:RMD-08","CL_MHON_03:XD-06"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"Composite","passages":[]},{"id":"MC_MHON_L1_01:XD-08","text":"The demand for demonstration and the use of analogy mutually support and impede one another: the former legitimates translations, the latter extends their scope beyond what has been demonstrated.","refs":["MC_MHON_L1_01:GA-08","MC_MHON_L1_01:GA-10","CL_MHON_01:GA-10","CL_MHON_01:FL-06","CL_MHON_03:FL-01"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"Tension","passages":[]},{"id":"MC_MHON_L1_01:XD-09","text":"The non-manifest forms a single dynamic field, because latency, disappearance, and not-yet-apparent configuration are times of transition, not absolute outsides.","refs":["MC_MHON_L1_01:GA-05","MC_MHON_L1_01:GA-07","MC_MHON_L1_01:GA-12","CL_MHON_01:XD-08","CL_MHON_02:GA-09","CL_MHON_03:BSA-04"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"Composite","passages":[]}],"original_name":"Cross-Unit Derivative Propositions"},{"number":"9","name":"Book-Scale Super-Derivatives","items":[{"id":"MC_MHON_L1_01:BSD-01","text":"The ontology of this level is relational and operational: being is a nexus of dependencies produced through figure, position, conversion, and composition.","refs":["CL_MHON_01:BSD-02","CL_MHON_02:BSD-01","CL_MHON_03:BSD-03"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"Composite","passages":[]},{"id":"MC_MHON_L1_01:BSD-02","text":"Causality does not proceed through efficient succession alone, but through formal constitution, relational mediation, and translation between orders.","refs":["CL_MHON_01:XD-01","CL_MHON_02:BSD-04","CL_MHON_03:BSD-02"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"Composite","passages":[]},{"id":"MC_MHON_L1_01:BSD-03","text":"Time becomes a composition of several recursions: regression to ontological priority, the circuit of appearance, restoration of place, and manifestation of a further order.","refs":["CL_MHON_01:HSI-03","CL_MHON_02:HSI-03","CL_MHON_03:HSI-03"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"Composite","passages":[]},{"id":"MC_MHON_L1_01:BSD-04","text":"The subject is a reader-constructor and mutable trajectory, who restores dependencies and is himself configured through conversion, displacement, and recomposition.","refs":["CL_MHON_01:HSI-02","CL_MHON_02:BSD-03","CL_MHON_03:HSI-02"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"Composite","passages":[]},{"id":"MC_MHON_L1_01:BSD-05","text":"Knowledge becomes a genealogical art: an object is understood when the series from the minimal principle through mediations to manifest form and symbolic place is restored.","refs":["CL_MHON_01:BSD-01","CL_MHON_02:ALT-B03","CL_MHON_03:MME-05"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"Composite","passages":[]},{"id":"MC_MHON_L1_01:BSD-06","text":"Power is not autonomy, but the capacity to convert dependence into manifestation, partiality into complement, and displacement into order.","refs":["CL_MHON_01:XD-06","CL_MHON_02:HSI-04","CL_MHON_03:XD-04"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"Composite","passages":[]},{"id":"MC_MHON_L1_01:BSD-07","text":"Possibility acquires temporal status: what is not yet manifest can appear through formal relation, disappear, and recur at another level.","refs":["CL_MHON_01:XD-08","CL_MHON_02:MP-03","CL_MHON_03:RLE-04"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"Composite","passages":[]},{"id":"MC_MHON_L1_01:BSD-08","text":"The system's method is performative, because the arrangement itself of figures, numbers, and regressions materially constructs the transdisciplinary unity that it proposes.","refs":["CL_MHON_01:ALT-B05","CL_MHON_02:ALT-B05","CL_MHON_03:ALT-B05"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"Composite","passages":[]}],"original_name":"Book-Scale Super-Derivatives"},{"number":"10","name":"Recursive Loops, Escalation Paths, and Conceptual Attractors","items":[{"id":"MC_MHON_L1_01:RLE-01","text":"The compression of forms into unity generates re-expansion through cosmology, temporality, and number; the effects of the expanded series are again referred back to the original unity.","refs":["CL_MHON_01:RLE-01","CL_MHON_02:RLE-03","CL_MHON_03:RLE-03"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"Composite","passages":[]},{"id":"MC_MHON_L1_01:RLE-02","text":"The center constitutes the periphery, the periphery manifests the necessity of the center, and the translation of the center into flux converts a static relation into production.","refs":["CL_MHON_01:RLE-03","CL_MHON_02:RMD-01","CL_MHON_03:RMD-03"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"Composite","passages":[]},{"id":"MC_MHON_L1_01:RLE-03","text":"Partiality demands complement, complement produces temporal or numerical unity, and the new unity becomes the beginning of a further series.","refs":["CL_MHON_02:GA-06","CL_MHON_02:RLE-05","CL_MHON_03:GA-06"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"Composite","passages":[]},{"id":"MC_MHON_L1_01:RLE-04","text":"Displacement moves return, return constitutes the straight line, the straight line orders flux, and the restored figure admits a new composition.","refs":["CL_MHON_03:GA-03","CL_MHON_03:RMD-04","CL_MHON_03:RLE-01"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"Composite","passages":[]},{"id":"MC_MHON_L1_01:RLE-05","text":"The point produces the line, the line the figure, the figure number, number letter and place; symbolic place leads the extremity of the chain back to the configurative principle.","refs":["CL_MHON_03:XD-05","CL_MHON_03:XD-03","CL_MHON_03:RMD-06"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"Composite","passages":[]},{"id":"MC_MHON_L1_01:RLE-06","text":"Manifestation dissolves latency, but at the same time gives rise to a new absence or not-yet-apparent structure; revelation therefore generates its own recursive veil.","refs":["CL_MHON_01:RLE-02","CL_MHON_02:RMD-06","CL_MHON_03:BSA-04"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"Composite","passages":[]},{"id":"MC_MHON_L1_01:RLE-07","text":"Analogy joins remote orders, the conjunction increases the system's fecundity, but amplification produces demonstrative gaps that demand new correspondences.","refs":["CL_MHON_01:CGD-07","CL_MHON_02:FL-03","CL_MHON_03:RLE-06"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"Tension","passages":[]},{"id":"MC_MHON_L1_01:RLE-08","text":"The supreme attractor is transformative relational unity, in which invariant center and mutable flux are reconciled not through immobility but through ordered recursion.","refs":["CL_MHON_01:RLE-06","CL_MHON_02:RLE-04","CL_MHON_03:RLE-05"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"Tension","passages":[]}],"original_name":"Recursive Loops, Escalation Paths, and Conceptual Attractors"},{"number":"11","name":"Formal Generative Schema of the Whole Book","items":[{"id":"MC_MHON_L1_01:FGS-B01","text":"Legible creation → field of operative forms → compression into a minimal geometric repertory.","refs":["CL_MHON_01:GA-01","CL_MHON_01:GA-02"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"Composite","passages":[]},{"id":"MC_MHON_L1_01:FGS-B02","text":"Point-Monad → center → line and circle → extended plurality subordinated to a simple principle.","refs":["CL_MHON_01:RMD-02","CL_MHON_01:RLE-03"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"Composite","passages":[]},{"id":"MC_MHON_L1_01:FGS-B03","text":"Geometric center → cosmic distribution → Solar dignity → mutable Lunar relation.","refs":["CL_MHON_02:GA-01","CL_MHON_02:GA-02","CL_MHON_02:GA-03"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"Composite","passages":[]},{"id":"MC_MHON_L1_01:FGS-B04","text":"Lunar partiality → Solar complement → conjunction of opposites → day → principle of Light.","refs":["CL_MHON_02:GA-06","CL_MHON_02:GA-07","CL_MHON_02:GA-08"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"Composite","passages":[]},{"id":"MC_MHON_L1_01:FGS-B05","text":"Productive point → flux → straight line → restoration of displaced parts → natural order.","refs":["CL_MHON_03:RMD-03","CL_MHON_03:GA-03","CL_MHON_03:RMD-04"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"Composite","passages":[]},{"id":"MC_MHON_L1_01:FGS-B06","text":"Rectilinear figure → quaternary → denary → cross → letter, tradition, and symbolic place.","refs":["CL_MHON_03:GA-05","CL_MHON_03:GA-06","CL_MHON_03:RMD-06"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"Composite","passages":[]},{"id":"MC_MHON_L1_01:FGS-B07","text":"Latency or absence → manifesting relation → appearance → varied return → amplification of order.","refs":["CL_MHON_01:RLE-02","CL_MHON_02:GA-09","CL_MHON_03:RLE-04"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"Composite","passages":[]},{"id":"MC_MHON_L1_01:FGS-B08","text":"Exact form + preserved relation → operative identity; corruption of configuration → interruption of translation → error of knowledge.","refs":["CL_MHON_01:GA-08","CL_MHON_01:RLE-04","CL_MHON_03:BSD-04"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"Composite","passages":[]},{"id":"MC_MHON_L1_01:FGS-B09","text":"Geometric necessity → analogical translation → transdisciplinary convergence → incomplete symbolic closure → new demand for reconstruction.","refs":["CL_MHON_01:GA-10","CL_MHON_03:BSD-06","CL_MHON_03:SP-06"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"Tension","passages":[]}],"original_name":"Formal Generative Schema of the Whole Book"},{"number":"12","name":"Whole-Book Argument Flow Map","items":[{"id":"MC_MHON_L1_01:AFM-B01","text":"Writing of creation → reduction to the Point-Monad → center and periphery → cosmic distribution → temporal complement → natural flux → numerical-symbolic amplification.","refs":["CL_MHON_01:BAS-02","CL_MHON_02:XD-04","CL_MHON_03:XD-05"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"Composite","passages":[]},{"id":"MC_MHON_L1_01:AFM-B02","text":"Minimal figure → generative relation → transformation of identity → varied return → translation between fields → distributed unity.","refs":["CL_MHON_01:GA-03","CL_MHON_02:RMD-04","CL_MHON_03:BSD-04"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"Composite","passages":[]},{"id":"MC_MHON_L1_01:AFM-B03","text":"Negation of autonomy → reconstruction of dependencies → absorption of absence into transition → transformative closure → analogical residue not yet demonstrated.","refs":["CL_MHON_01:GNX-03","CL_MHON_02:DBN-03","CL_MHON_03:FL-01"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"Tension","passages":[]}],"original_name":"Whole-Book Argument Flow Map"},{"number":"13","name":"Macro Map-Effects","items":[{"id":"MC_MHON_L1_01:MME-01","text":"Geometry is reclassified from an instrument of description into a grammar through which being, time, causality, and meaning are produced.","refs":["CL_MHON_01:MME-01","CL_MHON_02:BSD-05","CL_MHON_03:BSD-01"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"Composite","passages":[]},{"id":"MC_MHON_L1_01:MME-02","text":"The opposition between sign and thing collapses, because the same configuration manifests the thing, distributes its order, and institutes symbolic place.","refs":["CL_MHON_01:MME-02","CL_MHON_02:ALT-B05","CL_MHON_03:XD-03"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"Composite","passages":[]},{"id":"MC_MHON_L1_01:MME-03","text":"The opposition between unity and plurality is replaced by relations of center, mediation, and complement.","refs":["CL_MHON_01:MME-04","CL_MHON_02:GA-05","CL_MHON_02:GA-10"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"Composite","passages":[]},{"id":"MC_MHON_L1_01:MME-04","text":"Time is reclassified from external succession into an effect of relation, return, and further manifestation.","refs":["CL_MHON_01:BSD-03","CL_MHON_02:MME-04","CL_MHON_03:BSD-05"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"Composite","passages":[]},{"id":"MC_MHON_L1_01:MME-05","text":"Number is reclassified from abstract quantity into a magnitude of transformation that connects figure, letter, doctrine, and place.","refs":["CL_MHON_03:GA-06","CL_MHON_03:MME-02","CL_MHON_03:BSD-04"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"Composite","passages":[]},{"id":"MC_MHON_L1_01:MME-06","text":"Absence, partiality, and displacement are reclassified from deficiencies into motors of transition and the production of order.","refs":["CL_MHON_02:GA-09","CL_MHON_02:GA-06","CL_MHON_03:MME-03"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"Composite","passages":[]},{"id":"MC_MHON_L1_01:MME-07","text":"The boundaries of disciplines become paths of translation: differences are preserved, but no region retains separate ontological authority.","refs":["CL_MHON_01:GNX-07","CL_MHON_02:RMD-08","CL_MHON_03:DBN-04"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"Composite","passages":[]},{"id":"MC_MHON_L1_01:MME-08","text":"Reading becomes a genealogical reconstruction of configuration, not a description of surface nor a collection of nominal correspondences.","refs":["CL_MHON_01:BSD-01","CL_MHON_02:ALT-B03","CL_MHON_03:MME-05"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"Composite","passages":[]}],"original_name":"Macro Map-Effects"},{"number":"14","name":"Altitude Integration","items":[{"id":"MC_MHON_L1_01:ALT-B01","text":"At the level of surface, the character of the Monad, point, line, circle, Sun, Moon, drop, cross, and numerical series appear as determinate objects.","refs":["CL_MHON_01:ALT-B01","CL_MHON_02:ALT-B01","CL_MHON_03:ALT-B01"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"Composite","passages":[]},{"id":"MC_MHON_L1_01:ALT-B02","text":"At the structural level, dependence on the center, mediation, complement, flux, restoration, and aggregation operate as common mechanisms.","refs":["CL_MHON_01:ALT-B02","CL_MHON_02:ALT-B02","CL_MHON_03:ALT-B02"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"Composite","passages":[]},{"id":"MC_MHON_L1_01:ALT-B03","text":"At the metastrategic level, the contest over legitimacy is transferred from names and appearances to the reconstruction of relations that produce figure.","refs":["CL_MHON_01:ALT-B03","CL_MHON_02:ALT-B03","CL_MHON_03:ALT-B03"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"Composite","passages":[]},{"id":"MC_MHON_L1_01:ALT-B04","text":"At the ontological and temporal level, reality is a writing of transformable relations, causality is formal translation, and time is return that changes state.","refs":["CL_MHON_01:ALT-B04","CL_MHON_02:ALT-B04","CL_MHON_03:ALT-B04"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"Composite","passages":[]},{"id":"MC_MHON_L1_01:ALT-B05","text":"At the methodological and performative level, the arrangement of the text compresses figures, produces relations, and compels heterogeneous orders into a single reconstructive machine.","refs":["CL_MHON_01:ALT-B05","CL_MHON_02:ALT-B05","CL_MHON_03:ALT-B05"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"Composite","passages":[]}],"original_name":"Altitude Integration"},{"number":"15","name":"Internal Tensions, Mutation Points, and Fault Lines","items":[{"id":"MC_MHON_L1_01:FL-01","text":"The invariant center and fluxional point do not fully agree: the one explains stable dependence, the other production through continuous motion.","refs":["CL_MHON_01:RLE-03","CL_MHON_03:RMD-03","CL_MHON_03:MP-01"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"Tension","passages":[]},{"id":"MC_MHON_L1_01:FL-02","text":"Demonstrative necessity and analogical amplification have different epistemic force, but the system often accomplishes the transition between them without a universal rule.","refs":["CL_MHON_01:GA-10","CL_MHON_01:FL-06","CL_MHON_03:FL-01"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"Tension","passages":[]},{"id":"MC_MHON_L1_01:FL-03","text":"The exactness of fixed figure and identity preserved through transformation produce tension, because it is not always yet clear which variations preserve structure and which corrupt it.","refs":["CL_MHON_01:GA-08","CL_MHON_02:GA-09","CL_MHON_03:BSD-04"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"Tension","passages":[]},{"id":"MC_MHON_L1_01:FL-04","text":"The hierarchical principle produces order, but partiality and displacement also possess generative power; causality oscillates between domination and emergence.","refs":["CL_MHON_02:GA-02","CL_MHON_02:GA-06","CL_MHON_03:XD-04"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"Tension","passages":[]},{"id":"MC_MHON_L1_01:FL-05","text":"The universality of legibility remains fragile alongside the restriction of competence, because the common presence of fields of signs does not distribute the capacity to interpret correctly.","refs":["CL_MHON_01:BAS-08","CL_MHON_01:DBN-05","CL_MHON_01:MRT-B05"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"Tension","passages":[]},{"id":"MC_MHON_L1_01:FL-06","text":"Symbolic relation and physical causality are often expressed by the same figure, but the ontological status of this identity is not everywhere defined.","refs":["CL_MHON_01:FL-02","CL_MHON_02:FL-03","CL_MHON_03:FL-04"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"Tension","passages":[]},{"id":"MC_MHON_L1_01:MP-01","text":"The point changes from a minimal element of writing into an ontological center, cosmic referent, and principle of productive flux.","refs":["CL_MHON_01:MP-02","CL_MHON_02:MP-01","CL_MHON_03:MP-01"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"Tension","passages":[]},{"id":"MC_MHON_L1_01:MP-02","text":"The line changes from a primary form into an effect of the Point, a path of restoration, and a course of material flux.","refs":["CL_MHON_01:RMD-01","CL_MHON_01:CGD-05","CL_MHON_03:RMD-04"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"Tension","passages":[]},{"id":"MC_MHON_L1_01:MP-03","text":"Representation changes from subsequent imitation into productive manifestation, then into cosmic ordering and the institution of symbolic place.","refs":["CL_MHON_01:MP-01","CL_MHON_02:ALT-B05","CL_MHON_03:XD-03"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"Tension","passages":[]},{"id":"MC_MHON_L1_01:MP-04","text":"Time changes from the order of origin into day produced by relation and finally into spiral return that manifests latency.","refs":["CL_MHON_01:MP-04","CL_MHON_02:BSD-02","CL_MHON_03:HSI-03"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"Tension","passages":[]},{"id":"MC_MHON_L1_01:MP-05","text":"Repetition changes from the invariance of relation into periodic return and finally into the production of a previously latent configuration.","refs":["CL_MHON_01:RLE-03","CL_MHON_02:RMD-06","CL_MHON_03:MP-04"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"Tension","passages":[]},{"id":"MC_MHON_L1_01:MP-06","text":"Number changes from enumeration into an extensive structure that constitutes figure, cross, letter, and doctrinal place.","refs":["CL_MHON_03:GA-06","CL_MHON_03:RMD-02","CL_MHON_03:MP-02"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"Tension","passages":[]}],"original_name":"Internal Tensions, Mutation Points, and Fault Lines"},{"number":"16","name":"Hidden System Image","items":[{"id":"MC_MHON_L1_01:HSI-01","text":"Reality is implicitly a scriptorial, concentric, and fluxional machine, in which the invariant center generates motion, circuits, and symbolic translations.","refs":["CL_MHON_01:HSI-01","CL_MHON_02:HSI-01","CL_MHON_03:HSI-01"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"Composite","passages":[]},{"id":"MC_MHON_L1_01:HSI-02","text":"The subject within the machine is a displaceable and relational reader-constructor, who acquires his form through conversion, return, and recomposition.","refs":["CL_MHON_01:HSI-02","CL_MHON_02:HSI-02","CL_MHON_03:HSI-02"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"Composite","passages":[]},{"id":"MC_MHON_L1_01:HSI-03","text":"Time is a spiral regression: it returns to the principle, but with each circuit produces a new form, day, or latent configuration.","refs":["CL_MHON_01:HSI-03","CL_MHON_02:HSI-03","CL_MHON_03:HSI-03"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"Composite","passages":[]},{"id":"MC_MHON_L1_01:HSI-04","text":"The principal contest is to preserve the exact channel between the principle and its transformations against the autonomy of parts, the corruption of relations, and premature closure.","refs":["CL_MHON_01:HSI-04","CL_MHON_02:HSI-04","CL_MHON_03:HSI-04"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"Composite","passages":[]}],"original_name":"Hidden System Image"},{"number":"17","name":"Minimal Whole-Book Reconstruction Test","items":[{"id":"MC_MHON_L1_01:MRT-B01","text":"The present axioms suffice to restore the principal machine: operative form regresses to a simple principle, is unfolded through relations, and returns in time, flux, and symbol.","refs":["CL_MHON_01:MRT-B01","CL_MHON_02:MRT-B01","CL_MHON_03:MRT-B01"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"Composite","passages":[]},{"id":"MC_MHON_L1_01:MRT-B02","text":"The firmest structure is the continuity between the non-autonomy of elements, generative recursion, and the translation of geometry into higher orders.","refs":["CL_MHON_01:MRT-B03","CL_MHON_02:MRT-B02","CL_MHON_03:MRT-B03"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"Composite","passages":[]},{"id":"MC_MHON_L1_01:MRT-B03","text":"Less determined remains the manner in which geometric necessity constitutes ontological, astronomical, physical, and magical causality without an analogical leap.","refs":["CL_MHON_01:MRT-B02","CL_MHON_02:FL-03","CL_MHON_03:FL-03"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"Tension","passages":[]},{"id":"MC_MHON_L1_01:MRT-B04","text":"At this level the system coheres not through the identity of terms, but through the functional return of the same schema of dependence, transformation, and complement.","refs":["CL_MHON_01:MRT-B04","CL_MHON_02:MRT-B02","CL_MHON_03:MRT-B02"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"Composite","passages":[]},{"id":"MC_MHON_L1_01:MRT-B05","text":"Coherence relies most strongly on recursive order and the performative force of arrangement, not on a uniformly complete deductive chain.","refs":["CL_MHON_01:ALT-B05","CL_MHON_02:MRT-B03","CL_MHON_03:MRT-B04"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"Tension","passages":[]},{"id":"MC_MHON_L1_01:MRT-B06","text":"Restoration preserves the true movement while distinguishing compression, regression, circulation, and amplification, and does not flatten their transformations into identical repetition.","refs":["CL_MHON_01:MRT-B07","CL_MHON_02:MRT-B03","CL_MHON_03:MRT-B04"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"Composite","passages":[]},{"id":"MC_MHON_L1_01:MRT-B07","text":"Axiomatic reduction is resisted by the differences between demonstration and analogy, physical and symbolic causality, and the exact preservation and legitimate transformation of form.","refs":["CL_MHON_01:MRT-B06","CL_MHON_02:FL-03","CL_MHON_03:FL-04"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"Tension","passages":[]}],"original_name":"Minimal Whole-Book Reconstruction Test"}],"revision":"MC_MHON_L1_01@R001","source_variant_id":"canonical:MC_MHON_L1_01:v1","source_language_code":"la","original_title":"Mhon L1 01"}],"identity":{"layer":"MC","kicker":"Meta-cluster · Monas Hieroglyphica","title":"Mhon L1 01","context":"Chapters 01–09 · Front matter · Theorem I. · Theorem Ii. · Theorem Iii. · T H E O R E M Iiii. · T H E O R E M V. · T H E O R E M V I. · T H E O R. V I I. · Theorem Viii.","original_title":"Mhon L1 01","original_context":"Chapters 01–09 · Front matter · Theorema I. · Theorema Ii. · Theorema Iii. · T H E O R E M A Iiii. · T H E O R E M A V. · T H E O R E M A V I. · T H E O R. V I I. · Theor. Viii."},"original_title":"Mhon L1 01","title_language_code":"en"},{"id":"MC_MHON_L1_02","layer":"MC","title":"Mhon L1 02","order":2,"parents":["CL_MHON_04","CL_MHON_05","CL_MHON_06"],"revision":"MC_MHON_L1_02@R001","sha256":"a03e0df45f29a15359b0100c7cbe933ee612eba90f6ad0291f09dd0bd44e7307","sections":[{"number":"1","name":"Book-Scale Argumental Spine","items":[{"id":"MC_MHON_L1_02:BAS-01","text":"Systema quaerit quomodo unitas hieroglyphica, figura cosmica, partitio geometrica, successio planetaria et conversio numerico-litteralis in unum ordinem transformationum componi possint sine relationis initialis amissione.","refs":["CL_MHON_04:BAS-01","CL_MHON_05:BS-01","CL_MHON_06:ABS-01"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[]},{"id":"MC_MHON_L1_02:BAS-02","text":"Thesis metagregis est signum hieroglyphicum non esse rem stabilem, sed operatorium distributum quod differentias producit, mediatur, transfert atque sub unitate relationis reordinat.","refs":["CL_MHON_04:CGD-05","CL_MHON_06:ABS-02"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[]},{"id":"MC_MHON_L1_02:BAS-03","text":"Argumentatio reconstructiva fit, quia necessitas ordinis non ex definitione univoca eruitur, sed ex serie conversionum in quibus figura, elementum, planeta, numerus et Verbum mutuas functiones accipiunt.","refs":["CL_MHON_05:BS-06","CL_MHON_06:ABS-03"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[]},{"id":"MC_MHON_L1_02:BAS-04","text":"Motus principalis est spiralis: unitas in distinctionem distribuitur, distinctio per proportionem vel mediationem ordinatur, et effectus ordinatus ad unitatem iterum legibilem revertitur.","refs":["CL_MHON_04:BAS-04","CL_MHON_06:ABS-05"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"Composite","passages":[]},{"id":"MC_MHON_L1_02:BAS-05","text":"MONAS iam non fungitur ut principium simplex praecedens, sed ut attractor relationum in quo differentiationes cosmicae, materiales et numerales sine reductione mutua conveniunt.","refs":["CL_MHON_04:RMD-01","CL_MHON_06:GA-10"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"Composite","passages":[]},{"id":"MC_MHON_L1_02:BAS-06","text":"Clausurae materiales et verbales, impressio in Terram et coordinatio in Lux atque Verbum, termini operativi sunt potius quam fines absoluti.","refs":["CL_MHON_05:BS-08","CL_MHON_06:GA-09"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"Composite","passages":[]},{"id":"MC_MHON_L1_02:BAS-07","text":"Systema clausuram completam recusat, quia proportio occultata et reditus Verbi ad machinam interpretativam residuum servant ex quo nova conversio incipit.","refs":["CL_MHON_04:BSA-03","CL_MHON_06:RLE-05"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"Tension","passages":[]},{"id":"MC_MHON_L1_02:BAS-08","text":"Directio metagregis est ab ordine disciplinarum separatarum ad machinam translatricem, in qua astronomia, geometria, alchimia, arithmetica et sermo sunt diversi status eiusdem operationis.","refs":["CL_MHON_04:BSD-02","CL_MHON_06:GA-04"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"Composite","passages":[]}],"original_name":"Book-Scale Argumental Spine"},{"number":"2","name":"Global Load-Bearing Axioms","items":[{"id":"MC_MHON_L1_02:GA-01","text":"[Persistent] Signum hieroglyphicum relationes non solum repraesentat, sed eas in forma tractabili producit atque distribuit.","refs":["CL_MHON_04:GLA-04","CL_MHON_06:GA-02"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[]},{"id":"MC_MHON_L1_02:GA-02","text":"[Persistent] Unitas per distinctionem conservatur: divisio, partitio et pluralitas non sunt externae MONADI, sed modi quibus illa operatur.","refs":["CL_MHON_04:GLA-01","CL_MHON_06:GA-03"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[]},{"id":"MC_MHON_L1_02:GA-03","text":"[Persistent] Geometria est grammatica generalis conversionis, qua situs, vicinitas, sectio et circulatio differentias in relationes producibiles vertunt.","refs":["CL_MHON_04:GLA-03","CL_MHON_06:GA-01"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[]},{"id":"MC_MHON_L1_02:GA-04","text":"[Mutating] MONAS mutatur a termino congruentiae vel unitatis initialis in centrum distributivum quod separationes, proportiones, codices et reditus coordinat.","refs":["CL_MHON_04:GLA-06","CL_MHON_06:GA-10"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[]},{"id":"MC_MHON_L1_02:GA-05","text":"[Mutating] Figurae caelestes mutari possunt a locis astronomicis in principia operationum, successionum, virtutum et transformationum.","refs":["CL_MHON_04:GLA-07","CL_MHON_05:RMD-01","CL_MHON_06:GA-06"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[]},{"id":"MC_MHON_L1_02:GA-06","text":"[Persistent] Causalitas est medialis et distributa: figurae, ignis, anima, Spiritus, radii, elementa et Terra functiones conversionis inter se participant.","refs":["CL_MHON_04:CGD-04","CL_MHON_05:GA-09"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"Composite","passages":[]},{"id":"MC_MHON_L1_02:GA-07","text":"[Emergent] Astronomia, geometria, arithmetica, alphabetum et theologia verbalis sunt codices convertibiles unius machinae hieroglyphicae.","refs":["CL_MHON_04:BSD-02","CL_MHON_06:GA-04"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"Composite","passages":[]},{"id":"MC_MHON_L1_02:GA-08","text":"[Attractor] Mercurius et MONAS functionem attractorum complementariorum exercent, quia unus transitum inter cosmum, animam et materiam, altera conversionum unitatem continet.","refs":["CL_MHON_05:RLE-06","CL_MHON_05:GA-04"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"Composite","passages":[]},{"id":"MC_MHON_L1_02:GA-09","text":"[Emergent] Terra non est receptaculum passivum, sed nodus productivus ubi figura, influxus, nutritio et impressio in novam continuationem convertuntur.","refs":["CL_MHON_05:RMD-06","CL_MHON_05:XD-06"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"Composite","passages":[]},{"id":"MC_MHON_L1_02:GA-10","text":"[Terminal] Lux, Verbum et impressio materialis clausuras locales efficiunt, in quibus plures conversiones ad formam coordinatam perveniunt sine machinae extinctione.","refs":["CL_MHON_05:GA-10","CL_MHON_06:GA-09"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"Composite","passages":[]},{"id":"MC_MHON_L1_02:GA-11","text":"[Attractor] Proportio occulta et potentia numeralis unitatem relationum ordinant, sed earum regula numquam ita explicatur ut ulterior interpretatio superflua fiat.","refs":["CL_MHON_04:GLA-10","CL_MHON_06:GA-07"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"Tension","passages":[]},{"id":"MC_MHON_L1_02:GA-12","text":"[Emergent] Cognitio valida est operativa: interpretans figuras dividere, convertere, comparare et iterum componere debet ut earum virtutes intellegat.","refs":["CL_MHON_04:ALT-B05","CL_MHON_06:BSD-04"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"Composite","passages":[]}],"original_name":"Global Load-Bearing Axioms"},{"number":"3","name":"Recurrence, Mutation, and Displacement Matrix","items":[{"id":"MC_MHON_L1_02:RMD-01","text":"MONAS identitatem nominalem retinet, sed a principio unitatis transit ad effectum coordinationis plurium conversionum.","refs":["CL_MHON_04:RMD-01","CL_MHON_06:RMD-05"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"Composite","passages":[]},{"id":"MC_MHON_L1_02:RMD-02","text":"Separatio e Lineis et circulis migrat ad elementa, successiones planetarias et partitiones crucis, ubi iam non dissolutio sed productio potentiae est.","refs":["CL_MHON_04:RMD-02","CL_MHON_05:RMD-04","CL_MHON_06:MP-02"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"Composite","passages":[]},{"id":"MC_MHON_L1_02:RMD-03","text":"Circularitas e figura geometrica mutatur in circuitum relationum, quo forma redit cum alia facultate numerali, cosmologica aut semantica.","refs":["CL_MHON_04:RMD-03","CL_MHON_06:RLE-01"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"Composite","passages":[]},{"id":"MC_MHON_L1_02:RMD-04","text":"Luna et Sol ab elementis compositionalibus ad media substituibilia, principia generativa et canales influxus dislocantur.","refs":["CL_MHON_05:RMD-02","CL_MHON_05:RMD-03"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"Composite","passages":[]},{"id":"MC_MHON_L1_02:RMD-05","text":"Mercurius a gradu planetario in Microcosmum, Adam et mediatorem transformationis expanditur, ita ut identitas eius in transitu consistat.","refs":["CL_MHON_05:GA-05","CL_MHON_05:RLE-06"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"Composite","passages":[]},{"id":"MC_MHON_L1_02:RMD-06","text":"Terra mutatur a termino cosmologico vel receptivo in medium activum nutritionis, impressionis et continuationis causalis.","refs":["CL_MHON_05:RMD-06","CL_MHON_05:XD-06"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"Composite","passages":[]},{"id":"MC_MHON_L1_02:RMD-07","text":"Proportio denaria et quinarietas a regulis localibus elementorum vel figurae ad attractores compositionis inter codices transeunt.","refs":["CL_MHON_04:RMD-04","CL_MHON_06:RMD-04"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"Composite","passages":[]},{"id":"MC_MHON_L1_02:RMD-08","text":"Occultum desinit esse mera absentia et fit residuum internum ordinis, quod clausuram impedit atque reditum interpretativum excitat.","refs":["CL_MHON_04:RMD-07","CL_MHON_06:RLE-05"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"Tension","passages":[]}],"original_name":"Recurrence, Mutation, and Displacement Matrix"},{"number":"4","name":"Lower-Layer vs Current-Level Distinction","items":[{"id":"MC_MHON_L1_02:CGD-01","text":"Locales manent constructiones singularum Linearum, numeri revolutionum, domus planetariae, formae Mercurii et calculi particulares.","refs":["CL_MHON_04:CGD-01","CL_MHON_05:CGD-01"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[]},{"id":"MC_MHON_L1_02:CGD-02","text":"In gradu praesenti amplificatur regula quod identitas per transformationem et transitum functionum definitur, non per statum fixum.","refs":["CL_MHON_05:CGD-02","CL_MHON_05:RMD-10"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"Composite","passages":[]},{"id":"MC_MHON_L1_02:CGD-03","text":"Geometria, anima, Spiritus, ignis et radii in hoc gradu non sunt causae separatae, sed diversa media unius distributionis relationum.","refs":["CL_MHON_04:CGD-04","CL_MHON_05:RMD-07","CL_MHON_06:GA-04"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"Composite","passages":[]},{"id":"MC_MHON_L1_02:CGD-04","text":"Impressio in Terram, receptio figurae et nutritio Magisterii ad unam logicam materialitatis activae rediguntur.","refs":["CL_MHON_05:CGD-05","CL_MHON_05:XD-06"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"Composite","passages":[]},{"id":"MC_MHON_L1_02:CGD-05","text":"Obsoleta fit imago signi immobili, planetae independentis et disciplinae clausae, quia omnes in circulationem conversionum absorbentur.","refs":["CL_MHON_05:CGD-07","CL_MHON_06:GNX-01"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"Negative","passages":[]},{"id":"MC_MHON_L1_02:CGD-06","text":"Correspondentiae exactae localiter postulatae non ascendunt ad legem universalem demonstratam, quia ipsae in metagregi puncta fragilitatis manent.","refs":["CL_MHON_05:XD-08","CL_MHON_05:FL-01"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"Tension","passages":[]},{"id":"MC_MHON_L1_02:CGD-07","text":"Novum obiectum huius gradus est machina translatrix quae figuram, genealogiam, numerum, influxum, materiam et sermonem sub eadem regula mutationis componit.","refs":["CL_MHON_05:CGD-08","CL_MHON_06:MME-01"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"Composite","passages":[]}],"original_name":"Lower-Layer vs Current-Level Distinction"},{"number":"5","name":"Global Apophatic Structure","items":[{"id":"MC_MHON_L1_02:GNX-01","text":"Systema excludit signum quod ad imaginem nudam, nomen mythicum, litteram solitariam aut quantitatem abstractam reducatur.","refs":["CL_MHON_04:GNX-03","CL_MHON_05:GNX-01","CL_MHON_06:GNX-01"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"Negative","passages":[]},{"id":"MC_MHON_L1_02:GNX-02","text":"Systema excludit unitatem immobilem et oppositum rigidum inter unum et multa.","refs":["CL_MHON_04:GNX-02","CL_MHON_06:BSA-04"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"Negative","passages":[]},{"id":"MC_MHON_L1_02:GNX-03","text":"Systema excludit originem causalitatis unicam, sive pure caelestem sive pure terrestre definitam.","refs":["CL_MHON_05:GNX-03","CL_MHON_05:BSA-04"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"Negative","passages":[]},{"id":"MC_MHON_L1_02:GNX-04","text":"Systema excludit materiam passivam, quia Terra, corpus et receptaculum effectus non solum recipiunt, sed prolongant.","refs":["CL_MHON_05:GNX-05","CL_MHON_06:GNX-04"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"Negative","passages":[]},{"id":"MC_MHON_L1_02:GNX-05","text":"Systema excludit cognitionem immediatam sine operatione, quia intelligere postulat transitum per figuras, divisiones, media et conversiones.","refs":["CL_MHON_04:GNX-05","CL_MHON_06:BSA-01"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"Negative","passages":[]},{"id":"MC_MHON_L1_02:GNX-06","text":"Systema excludit mensuram omnino explicitam et interpretationem semel conclusam, quia occultum residuale et ulterior derivatio structuraliter necessaria manent.","refs":["CL_MHON_04:BSA-03","CL_MHON_06:GNX-05"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"Negative","passages":[]}],"original_name":"Global Apophatic Structure"},{"number":"6","name":"Deep Book Apophatics","items":[{"id":"MC_MHON_L1_02:DBN-01","text":"Falsum esse debet mundum in quo figurae sint res passivae et disciplinae ordines autonomi, quia machina huius gradus figuras operari et disciplinas converti requirit.","refs":["CL_MHON_06:DBN-01","CL_MHON_06:DBN-02"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"Negative","passages":[]},{"id":"MC_MHON_L1_02:DBN-02","text":"Falsum esse debet identitatem consistere in substantia invariabili, quia identitas planetaria, numeralis et hieroglyphica per mutationem medii durat.","refs":["CL_MHON_05:CGD-02","CL_MHON_06:GA-03"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"Negative","passages":[]},{"id":"MC_MHON_L1_02:DBN-03","text":"Falsum esse debet Magisterium vel effectum hieroglyphicum ex principio caelesti solitario oriri, quia receptio, nutritio et Terra causae constitutivae sunt.","refs":["CL_MHON_05:DBN-03","CL_MHON_05:BSA-02"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"Negative","passages":[]},{"id":"MC_MHON_L1_02:DBN-04","text":"Falsum esse debet numerum a figura separari vel multiplicationem additivam esse, quia numerus ex situ, partitione, geminatione et comparatione emergit.","refs":["CL_MHON_06:DBN-04","CL_MHON_06:GA-08"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"Negative","passages":[]},{"id":"MC_MHON_L1_02:DBN-05","text":"Falsum esse debet Verbum immediatum vel clausuram semanticam sine derivatione esse, quia Verbum terminale coordinatio operationum priorum est.","refs":["CL_MHON_06:DBN-05","CL_MHON_06:BSA-03"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"Negative","passages":[]}],"original_name":"Deep Book Apophatics"},{"number":"7","name":"Super-Apophatics of the Whole","items":[{"id":"MC_MHON_L1_02:BSA-01","text":"Indisponibilis est sensus pure immediatus, quia tota machina intellectionem per transitum inter figuras, numeros, litteras, media et compositiones ordinat.","refs":["CL_MHON_06:BSA-01","CL_MHON_04:BSA-01"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"Negative","passages":[]},{"id":"MC_MHON_L1_02:BSA-02","text":"Ontologice inadmissibilis est realitas ex rebus mutuo indifferentibus composita, quia situs, vicinitas, divisio et consensus habent vim effectivam.","refs":["CL_MHON_06:BSA-02","CL_MHON_04:BSA-04"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"Negative","passages":[]},{"id":"MC_MHON_L1_02:BSA-03","text":"Vetatur clausura quae occultum, pluralitatem aut mutationem omnino exhauriat, quia talis clausura attractores conversionis destrueret.","refs":["CL_MHON_04:BSA-03","CL_MHON_06:BSA-03"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"Negative","passages":[]},{"id":"MC_MHON_L1_02:BSA-04","text":"Indisponibilis est positio subiecti ut artificis autonomi et originarii, quia interpretans et operans intra medias relationes, influxus et figuras productas constituitur.","refs":["CL_MHON_05:BSA-01","CL_MHON_06:BSD-04"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"Negative","passages":[]}],"original_name":"Super-Apophatics of the Whole"},{"number":"8","name":"Cross-Unit Derivative Propositions","items":[{"id":"MC_MHON_L1_02:XD-01","text":"Relatio astralis et partitio geometrica eandem legem causalitatis manifestant: proprietas, dignitas et numerus non ex termino isolato, sed ex dispositione relatorum emergunt.","refs":["MC_MHON_L1_02:GA-03","MC_MHON_L1_02:GA-07","CL_MHON_06:XD-01","CL_MHON_06:GA-05"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"Composite","passages":[]},{"id":"MC_MHON_L1_02:XD-02","text":"Substitutio mediorum planetariorum et differentiatio MONADIS indicant identitatem per translationem functionis servari, non per immobilitatem substantiarum.","refs":["MC_MHON_L1_02:GA-02","MC_MHON_L1_02:GA-05","MC_MHON_L1_02:GA-06","CL_MHON_05:XD-04","CL_MHON_04:RMD-01"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"Tension","passages":[]},{"id":"MC_MHON_L1_02:XD-03","text":"Receptio materialis non terminat influxum, sed illum in figuram impressam, nutritionem et circulationem renovatam reconstituit.","refs":["MC_MHON_L1_02:GA-06","MC_MHON_L1_02:GA-09","CL_MHON_05:XD-06","CL_MHON_05:FGS-B06"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"Composite","passages":[]},{"id":"MC_MHON_L1_02:XD-04","text":"Nulla lingua interpretativa praevalet absolute, quia figura eadem per geometriam, astronomiam, numerum, litteram et Verbum successive intelligibilis fit.","refs":["MC_MHON_L1_02:GA-07","MC_MHON_L1_02:GA-12","CL_MHON_06:XD-06","CL_MHON_04:BSD-02"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"Composite","passages":[]},{"id":"MC_MHON_L1_02:XD-05","text":"Proportio et numerus non sunt instrumenta clausurae solum, sed motores escalationis qui ordinem occultum in novas formas compositionis transferunt.","refs":["MC_MHON_L1_02:GA-03","MC_MHON_L1_02:GA-11","CL_MHON_04:XD-04","CL_MHON_06:RLE-02"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"Tension","passages":[]},{"id":"MC_MHON_L1_02:XD-06","text":"Mercurius-Microcosmus et MONAS-attractor simul efficiunt ut homo, planeta et figura non sint imagines reciprocae tantum, sed nodi eiusdem transitus cosmico-materialis.","refs":["MC_MHON_L1_02:GA-08","MC_MHON_L1_02:GA-09","CL_MHON_05:XD-03","CL_MHON_05:RLE-06"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"Composite","passages":[]},{"id":"MC_MHON_L1_02:XD-07","text":"Lux et Verbum clausuram semanticam praebent solum retroactive, quia ad figuram relegendam redeunt et machinam conversionis iterum aperiunt.","refs":["MC_MHON_L1_02:GA-10","MC_MHON_L1_02:GA-12","CL_MHON_06:FGS-B05","CL_MHON_06:RLE-05"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"Tension","passages":[]}],"original_name":"Cross-Unit Derivative Propositions"},{"number":"9","name":"Book-Scale Super-Derivatives","items":[{"id":"MC_MHON_L1_02:BSD-01","text":"Realitas reconfiguratur ut campus relationum convertibilium, in quo identitas non antecedit operationes sed per eas permanet.","refs":["CL_MHON_06:BSD-01","CL_MHON_04:BSD-01"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"Composite","passages":[]},{"id":"MC_MHON_L1_02:BSD-02","text":"Causalitas fit distributa et medialis, quia figurae, radii, elementa, animae, ignes et receptacula terrestra effectus solum per circulationem producunt.","refs":["CL_MHON_04:BSD-05","CL_MHON_05:BSD-03"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"Composite","passages":[]},{"id":"MC_MHON_L1_02:BSD-03","text":"Tempus fit forma recursionis, revolutionis et redistributionis, non continens neutrum processibus externis impositum.","refs":["CL_MHON_04:BSD-04","CL_MHON_06:BSD-03"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"Composite","passages":[]},{"id":"MC_MHON_L1_02:BSD-04","text":"Subiectum cognoscens non est spectator exterior, sed operator localis qui conversiones legendo, dividendo et componendo intra machinam constituitur.","refs":["CL_MHON_04:BSD-03","CL_MHON_05:BSD-04","CL_MHON_06:BSD-04"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"Composite","passages":[]},{"id":"MC_MHON_L1_02:BSD-05","text":"Verbum et impressio materialis non sunt termini oppositi, sed duo modi in quibus figurae conversiones suas efficacias semanticam et corporalem condensant.","refs":["CL_MHON_05:BSD-01","CL_MHON_06:BSD-05"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"Composite","passages":[]},{"id":"MC_MHON_L1_02:BSD-06","text":"Methodus valida mutatur ab extractione sensus stabilis ad probationem per seriem transformationum, ubi legere est mechanismum generativum restituere.","refs":["CL_MHON_04:BSD-06","CL_MHON_06:MME-05"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"Composite","passages":[]},{"id":"MC_MHON_L1_02:BSD-07","text":"Origo simplex dissolvitur in rete dependentiarum inter principia, media, receptacula et effectus, ita ut genesis semper distributa maneat.","refs":["CL_MHON_05:BSD-07","CL_MHON_05:BSD-08"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"Composite","passages":[]}],"original_name":"Book-Scale Super-Derivatives"},{"number":"10","name":"Recursive Loops, Escalation Paths, and Conceptual Attractors","items":[{"id":"MC_MHON_L1_02:RLE-01","text":"Circuitus fundamentalis est unitas → distinctio → proportio → coniunctio → unitas relecta ut capacitas novae differentiationis.","refs":["CL_MHON_04:RLE-01","CL_MHON_06:RLE-01"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"Composite","passages":[]},{"id":"MC_MHON_L1_02:RLE-02","text":"Compositio signorum successionem producit, successio transformationem, transformatio figuram materialem, et figura materialis ordinem occultum retroactive testatur.","refs":["CL_MHON_05:RLE-01"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"Composite","passages":[]},{"id":"MC_MHON_L1_02:RLE-03","text":"Circuitus trans-campalis connectit figuram astronomicam, operationem elementarem, dispositionem geometricam, codicem numeralem et productionem Verbi.","refs":["CL_MHON_04:RLE-02","CL_MHON_06:RLE-03"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"Composite","passages":[]},{"id":"MC_MHON_L1_02:RLE-04","text":"Substitutio medii est circuitus conservativus: Luna, Spiritus, anima, Sol, Mercurius aut Terra functionem instabilem alterius recipiunt sine fine operis amisso.","refs":["CL_MHON_05:RLE-02","CL_MHON_05:HSI-05"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"Composite","passages":[]},{"id":"MC_MHON_L1_02:RLE-05","text":"Escalatio fit per translationem eiusdem functionis a spatio ad elementum, ab elemento ad tempus, a tempore ad corpus et a corpore ad sermonem.","refs":["CL_MHON_04:RLE-03","CL_MHON_05:RLE-05"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"Composite","passages":[]},{"id":"MC_MHON_L1_02:RLE-06","text":"MONAS, Mercurius, proportio occulta et Verbum sunt attractores heterogenei, qui dispersionem figurarum in ordinem convertibilem cogunt sine univoca reductione.","refs":["CL_MHON_04:RLE-04","CL_MHON_05:RLE-06","CL_MHON_06:RLE-04"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"Composite","passages":[]},{"id":"MC_MHON_L1_02:RLE-07","text":"Clausura formalis est etiam punctum reingressus, quia terminus numeralis, materialis vel verbaliter magistralis processum sistit et simul novam quaestionem de regula efficit.","refs":["CL_MHON_04:RLE-05","CL_MHON_06:RLE-05"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"Tension","passages":[]}],"original_name":"Recursive Loops, Escalation Paths, and Conceptual Attractors"},{"number":"11","name":"Formal Generative Schema of the Whole Book","items":[{"id":"MC_MHON_L1_02:FGS-B01","text":"Relatio initialis → figura determinata → distinctio ordinata, ubi unitas non perditur sed in partes operativas distribuitur.","refs":["CL_MHON_04:GLA-01","CL_MHON_06:GA-01"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"Composite","passages":[]},{"id":"MC_MHON_L1_02:FGS-B02","text":"Partitio, vicinitas, situs et circulatio → proportio, dignitas vel numerus, ubi forma geometrica proprietates relationis producit.","refs":["CL_MHON_04:GLA-03","CL_MHON_06:XD-01"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"Composite","passages":[]},{"id":"MC_MHON_L1_02:FGS-B03","text":"Figura cosmica vel geometrica → translatio hieroglyphica → littera, elementum, planeta aut potentia numeralis.","refs":["CL_MHON_05:FGS-B01","CL_MHON_06:FGS-B02"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"Composite","passages":[]},{"id":"MC_MHON_L1_02:FGS-B04","text":"Media spiritualia et materiales circulatoria → transitus inter caelum, animam, corpus et Terram → influxus operativus.","refs":["CL_MHON_05:XD-05","CL_MHON_05:HSI-01"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"Composite","passages":[]},{"id":"MC_MHON_L1_02:FGS-B05","text":"Receptio terrestris → nutritio, impressio et continuatio → reditus figurae ut effectus causalis.","refs":["CL_MHON_05:FGS-B06","CL_MHON_05:MME-04"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"Composite","passages":[]},{"id":"MC_MHON_L1_02:FGS-B06","text":"Proportio et compositio numeralis → coordinatio pluralium virium → Lux, Verbum aut figura materialiter completa.","refs":["CL_MHON_06:FGS-B04","CL_MHON_06:GA-09"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"Composite","passages":[]},{"id":"MC_MHON_L1_02:FGS-B07","text":"Residuum occultum, ambiguitas medii et incompletio regulae → relectio retroactiva → regeneratio machinae.","refs":["CL_MHON_04:BSA-03","CL_MHON_05:FL-02","CL_MHON_06:RLE-05"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"Tension","passages":[]}],"original_name":"Formal Generative Schema of the Whole Book"},{"number":"12","name":"Whole-Book Argument Flow Map","items":[{"id":"MC_MHON_L1_02:AFM-B01","text":"Unitas relationis → separatio geometrica → proportio ordinans → unitas differentiata.","refs":["CL_MHON_04:AFM-B01"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"Composite","passages":[]},{"id":"MC_MHON_L1_02:AFM-B02","text":"Figura astralis → compositio hieroglyphica → Natura Lunaris et Solaris → Mercurius ut medium transformativum.","refs":["CL_MHON_05:AFM-B01"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"Composite","passages":[]},{"id":"MC_MHON_L1_02:AFM-B03","text":"Mercurius, anima, Spiritus et radii → receptio terrestris → nutritio, impressio et circulatio reconstituta.","refs":["CL_MHON_05:FGS-B04","CL_MHON_05:AFM-B03"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"Composite","passages":[]},{"id":"MC_MHON_L1_02:AFM-B04","text":"Relatio situs et partitionis → figura operationalis → littera et numerus → potentia multiplicata.","refs":["CL_MHON_06:AFM-B01","CL_MHON_06:AFM-B04"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"Composite","passages":[]},{"id":"MC_MHON_L1_02:AFM-B05","text":"Potentia numeralis → coordinatio semanticorum codicum → Lux et Verbum → figura retroactive relecta.","refs":["CL_MHON_06:AFM-B05","CL_MHON_06:FGS-B05"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"Composite","passages":[]},{"id":"MC_MHON_L1_02:AFM-B06","text":"Clausura materialis vel verbalis → residuum occultum → substitutio medii → novus circuitus conversionis.","refs":["CL_MHON_04:BSA-03","CL_MHON_05:HSI-05"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"Tension","passages":[]}],"original_name":"Whole-Book Argument Flow Map"},{"number":"13","name":"Macro Map-Effects","items":[{"id":"MC_MHON_L1_02:MME-01","text":"Obiectum primarium reclassificatur: non est signum isolatum, sed nexus qui ordines cosmicos, materiales, numerales et verbales in operationem coniungit.","refs":["CL_MHON_04:MME-01","CL_MHON_06:MME-01"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"Composite","passages":[]},{"id":"MC_MHON_L1_02:MME-02","text":"Oppositio inter unitatem et distinctionem, sicut oppositio inter caelum et Terram, solvitur in gradus unius circulationis transformativae.","refs":["CL_MHON_04:MME-02","CL_MHON_05:MME-02"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"Composite","passages":[]},{"id":"MC_MHON_L1_02:MME-03","text":"Tempus, Terra et corpus reclassificantur ut agri constructionis, receptionis et reproductionis, non ut termini passivi relationum caelestium.","refs":["CL_MHON_04:MME-03","CL_MHON_05:MME-04"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"Composite","passages":[]},{"id":"MC_MHON_L1_02:MME-04","text":"Numeri et litterae desinunt esse notationes nudae et fiunt indices causarum formalium, quarum vis in sectione, situ, geminatione et coordinatione consistit.","refs":["CL_MHON_06:MME-04","CL_MHON_06:BSD-05"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"Composite","passages":[]},{"id":"MC_MHON_L1_02:MME-05","text":"Lectio necessaria fit diagrammatica, comparativa et translatrice, quia sensus unius vocabuli aut figurae immobilis structuram producere non potest.","refs":["CL_MHON_04:MME-05","CL_MHON_06:MME-05"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"Composite","passages":[]},{"id":"MC_MHON_L1_02:MME-06","text":"Anatomia, hieroglyphum et figura composita fiunt tabulae compressae quibus cosmologia, alchimia, anthropologia et ars interpretandi simul operari possunt.","refs":["CL_MHON_05:MME-07","CL_MHON_06:GA-02"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"Composite","passages":[]}],"original_name":"Macro Map-Effects"},{"number":"14","name":"Altitude Integration","items":[{"id":"MC_MHON_L1_02:ALT-B01","text":"Gradus superficiei continet MONADEM, Solem, Lunam, Arietem, Taurum, Mercurium, crucem, ignem, radios, Terram, numeros et litteras ut signa expressa.","refs":["CL_MHON_04:ALT-B01","CL_MHON_05:ALT-B01","CL_MHON_06:ALT-B01"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[]},{"id":"MC_MHON_L1_02:ALT-B02","text":"Gradus structuralis ostendit eandem machinam in separatione, successione, conversione, substitutione, circulatione et compositione.","refs":["CL_MHON_04:ALT-B02","CL_MHON_05:ALT-B02","CL_MHON_06:ALT-B02"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"Composite","passages":[]},{"id":"MC_MHON_L1_02:ALT-B03","text":"Gradus metastrategicus transit a quaestione quid signum significet ad quaestionem quomodo signum relationes, effectus et campos cognitionis producat.","refs":["CL_MHON_04:ALT-B03","CL_MHON_05:ALT-B03","CL_MHON_06:ALT-B03"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"Composite","passages":[]},{"id":"MC_MHON_L1_02:ALT-B04","text":"Gradus ontologico-temporalis realitatem ponit ut ordinem transmutativum, in quo unitas, tempus, dignitas, corpus et locus ex operationibus emergunt.","refs":["CL_MHON_04:ALT-B04","CL_MHON_05:ALT-B04","CL_MHON_06:ALT-B04"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"Composite","passages":[]},{"id":"MC_MHON_L1_02:ALT-B05","text":"Gradus methodologicus-performativus efficit ut textus figuras dividat, convertat, numeret et reconiungat, ita ut forma expositionis propriam thesim exsequatur.","refs":["CL_MHON_04:ALT-B05","CL_MHON_05:ALT-B05","CL_MHON_06:ALT-B05"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"Composite","passages":[]}],"original_name":"Altitude Integration"},{"number":"15","name":"Internal Tensions, Mutation Points, and Fault Lines","items":[{"id":"MC_MHON_L1_02:FL-01","text":"Proportio et correspondentia exacta promittuntur ut fundamenta ordinis, sed regula numerica vel mechanismus completus earum saepe non exhibetur.","refs":["CL_MHON_04:FL-01","CL_MHON_05:XD-08","CL_MHON_05:FL-01"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"Tension","passages":[]},{"id":"MC_MHON_L1_02:FL-02","text":"Mercurius oscillat inter planetam, Microcosmum, Adam, mediatorem et locum substituibilem, ita ut eius fertilitas systemica simul eius identitatem instabilem efficiat.","refs":["CL_MHON_05:FL-02","CL_MHON_05:GA-05"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"Tension","passages":[]},{"id":"MC_MHON_L1_02:FL-03","text":"Transitus a geometria ad litteram vel Verbum saepe congruentia symbolica sustentatur, non necessitate formali ubique demonstrata.","refs":["CL_MHON_06:FL-02","CL_MHON_06:FL-04"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"Tension","passages":[]},{"id":"MC_MHON_L1_02:FL-04","text":"Causalitas inter caelum, animae media et Terram distribuitur, sed pondus relativum principii caelestis, receptaculi terrestris et influxus radialis non definitive determinatur.","refs":["CL_MHON_05:FL-04","CL_MHON_05:HSI-04"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"Tension","passages":[]},{"id":"MC_MHON_L1_02:MP-01","text":"Aries mutatur a loco zodiacali in principium transformationis, quo situs astronomicus in operationem internam convertitur.","refs":["CL_MHON_04:MP-01","CL_MHON_04:GLA-07"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"Composite","passages":[]},{"id":"MC_MHON_L1_02:MP-02","text":"Signum mutatur a figura indicativa in operatorium, cum hieroglyphum relationes et potentias generare incipit.","refs":["CL_MHON_04:CGD-05","CL_MHON_06:ABS-02"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"Composite","passages":[]},{"id":"MC_MHON_L1_02:MP-03","text":"Crux et figurae numerales mutari incipiunt cum situs, geminatio et quadratura geometriae vim arithmeticam tribuunt.","refs":["CL_MHON_06:MP-02","CL_MHON_06:GA-08"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"Composite","passages":[]},{"id":"MC_MHON_L1_02:MP-04","text":"Numerus mutatur in Verbum cum compositio quantitativa non iam finis sit, sed coordinatio semanticarum et theologico-verbalium virium.","refs":["CL_MHON_06:CGD-06","CL_MHON_06:BSD-05"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"Composite","passages":[]}],"original_name":"Internal Tensions, Mutation Points, and Fault Lines"},{"number":"16","name":"Hidden System Image","items":[{"id":"MC_MHON_L1_02:HSI-01","text":"Mundus implicite est machina circulationis et conversionis, in qua figurae, media, corpora et receptacula mutuo se producunt.","refs":["CL_MHON_04:HSI-01","CL_MHON_05:HSI-01","CL_MHON_06:HSI-01"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"Composite","passages":[]},{"id":"MC_MHON_L1_02:HSI-02","text":"Subiectum est operator-medialis potius quam dominus externus: Mercurius-Anima et interpres-initiatum relationes distributas legunt atque transducunt.","refs":["CL_MHON_05:HSI-02","CL_MHON_06:HSI-02"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"Composite","passages":[]},{"id":"MC_MHON_L1_02:HSI-03","text":"Tempus est spirale, quia forma recognoscibilis per reditus mutationem suscipit et in alio campo novam potentiam efficit.","refs":["CL_MHON_04:HSI-03","CL_MHON_06:HSI-03"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"Composite","passages":[]},{"id":"MC_MHON_L1_02:HSI-04","text":"Certamen centrale opponit clausuram formalem et residuum occultum, non simpliciter verum et falsum signum.","refs":["CL_MHON_04:HSI-04","CL_MHON_06:HSI-04"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"Tension","passages":[]},{"id":"MC_MHON_L1_02:HSI-05","text":"Fuga vel mobilitas intra machinam non est abolitio relationum, sed substitutio medii et translatio codicis cum unus terminus functio sua deficere incipit.","refs":["CL_MHON_05:HSI-05","CL_MHON_05:XD-04"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"Composite","passages":[]}],"original_name":"Hidden System Image"},{"number":"17","name":"Minimal Whole-Book Reconstruction Test","items":[{"id":"MC_MHON_L1_02:MRT-B01","text":"Axiomata de unitate differentiata, figura operationali, causalitate mediali, conversione codicum, receptione activa et clausura non-finali sufficiunt ad machinam minimam metagregis restituendam.","refs":["CL_MHON_04:MRT-B01","CL_MHON_05:MRT-B01","CL_MHON_06:MRT-B01"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[]},{"id":"MC_MHON_L1_02:MRT-B02","text":"Structura firmissima est ubi relatio, partitio, mediatio et conversio demonstrantur ut operationes productrices potius quam ornatus analogici.","refs":["CL_MHON_04:MRT-B02","CL_MHON_05:MRT-B03","CL_MHON_06:MRT-B02"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"Composite","passages":[]},{"id":"MC_MHON_L1_02:MRT-B03","text":"Minus determinata manent mensura exacta proportionis, leges revolutionum, identitas Mercurii, efficacia radiorum, transitus a geometria ad litteram et nexus calculorum ad Verbum.","refs":["CL_MHON_04:MRT-B04","CL_MHON_05:MRT-B04","CL_MHON_06:MRT-B03"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"Tension","passages":[]},{"id":"MC_MHON_L1_02:MRT-B04","text":"Restitutio verum motum servat si reditus figurarum legitur ut mutatio functionis et scalae, non ut repetitio doctrinalis aut series capitulorum independentium.","refs":["CL_MHON_04:MRT-B03","CL_MHON_05:MRT-B05","CL_MHON_06:MRT-B05"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"Composite","passages":[]}],"original_name":"Minimal Whole-Book Reconstruction Test"}],"language_code":"la","language_name":"Latina","translations":[{"id":"CAREVAR_20261006T223628891631Z_f418bea1","language_code":"en","language_name":"English","title":"Mhon L1 02","source_sha256":"a03e0df45f29a15359b0100c7cbe933ee612eba90f6ad0291f09dd0bd44e7307","sha256":"4a5b4862bdb540fc59e66e7e1c3188c105393cb230cd5dfec9f28189e40c6ab8","rendition_type":"machine","review_status":"machine","model":"gpt-5.6-terra","created":"2026-10-06T22:36:28.893589+00:00","sections":[{"number":"1","name":"Book-Scale Argumental Spine","items":[{"id":"MC_MHON_L1_02:BAS-01","text":"The system asks how hieroglyphic unity, cosmic figure, geometric partition, planetary succession, and numerical-literal conversion may be composed into a single order of transformations without loss of the initial relation.","refs":["CL_MHON_04:BAS-01","CL_MHON_05:BS-01","CL_MHON_06:ABS-01"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[]},{"id":"MC_MHON_L1_02:BAS-02","text":"The metaggregate's thesis is that the hieroglyphic sign is not a stable thing, but a distributed operator that produces, mediates, transfers, and reorders differences under the unity of relation.","refs":["CL_MHON_04:CGD-05","CL_MHON_06:ABS-02"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[]},{"id":"MC_MHON_L1_02:BAS-03","text":"The argument proceeds reconstructively, because the necessity of order is derived not from a univocal definition but from a series of conversions in which figure, element, planet, number, and Word assume reciprocal functions.","refs":["CL_MHON_05:BS-06","CL_MHON_06:ABS-03"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[]},{"id":"MC_MHON_L1_02:BAS-04","text":"The principal movement is spiral: unity is distributed into distinction, distinction is ordered through proportion or mediation, and the ordered effect returns to unity rendered readable anew.","refs":["CL_MHON_04:BAS-04","CL_MHON_06:ABS-05"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"Composite","passages":[]},{"id":"MC_MHON_L1_02:BAS-05","text":"MONAS no longer functions as a preceding simple principle, but as an attractor of relations in which cosmic, material, and numerical differentiations converge without mutual reduction.","refs":["CL_MHON_04:RMD-01","CL_MHON_06:GA-10"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"Composite","passages":[]},{"id":"MC_MHON_L1_02:BAS-06","text":"Material and verbal closures, impression upon Earth and coordination in Light and Word, are operative termini rather than absolute ends.","refs":["CL_MHON_05:BS-08","CL_MHON_06:GA-09"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"Composite","passages":[]},{"id":"MC_MHON_L1_02:BAS-07","text":"The system refuses complete closure, because hidden proportion and the return of the Word to the interpretative machine preserve a remainder from which a new conversion begins.","refs":["CL_MHON_04:BSA-03","CL_MHON_06:RLE-05"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"Tension","passages":[]},{"id":"MC_MHON_L1_02:BAS-08","text":"The metaggregate's direction is from an order of separate disciplines toward a translational machine, in which astronomy, geometry, alchemy, arithmetic, and discourse are diverse states of the same operation.","refs":["CL_MHON_04:BSD-02","CL_MHON_06:GA-04"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"Composite","passages":[]}],"original_name":"Book-Scale Argumental Spine"},{"number":"2","name":"Global Load-Bearing Axioms","items":[{"id":"MC_MHON_L1_02:GA-01","text":"[Persistent] The hieroglyphic sign not only represents relations, but produces and distributes them in tractable form.","refs":["CL_MHON_04:GLA-04","CL_MHON_06:GA-02"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[]},{"id":"MC_MHON_L1_02:GA-02","text":"[Persistent] Unity is preserved through distinction: division, partition, and plurality are not external to MONAS, but modes by which it operates.","refs":["CL_MHON_04:GLA-01","CL_MHON_06:GA-03"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[]},{"id":"MC_MHON_L1_02:GA-03","text":"[Persistent] Geometry is the general grammar of conversion, by which position, proximity, section, and circulation transform differences into producible relations.","refs":["CL_MHON_04:GLA-03","CL_MHON_06:GA-01"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[]},{"id":"MC_MHON_L1_02:GA-04","text":"[Mutating] MONAS changes from a term of congruence or initial unity into a distributive center coordinating separations, proportions, codes, and returns.","refs":["CL_MHON_04:GLA-06","CL_MHON_06:GA-10"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[]},{"id":"MC_MHON_L1_02:GA-05","text":"[Mutating] Celestial figures may change from astronomical locations into principles of operations, successions, powers, and transformations.","refs":["CL_MHON_04:GLA-07","CL_MHON_05:RMD-01","CL_MHON_06:GA-06"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[]},{"id":"MC_MHON_L1_02:GA-06","text":"[Persistent] Causality is medial and distributed: figures, fire, soul, Spirit, rays, elements, and Earth participate mutually in functions of conversion.","refs":["CL_MHON_04:CGD-04","CL_MHON_05:GA-09"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"Composite","passages":[]},{"id":"MC_MHON_L1_02:GA-07","text":"[Emergent] Astronomy, geometry, arithmetic, alphabet, and verbal theology are convertible codes of one hieroglyphic machine.","refs":["CL_MHON_04:BSD-02","CL_MHON_06:GA-04"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"Composite","passages":[]},{"id":"MC_MHON_L1_02:GA-08","text":"[Attractor] Mercury and MONAS perform the function of complementary attractors, because the former contains the passage between cosmos, soul, and matter, while the latter contains the unity of conversions.","refs":["CL_MHON_05:RLE-06","CL_MHON_05:GA-04"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"Composite","passages":[]},{"id":"MC_MHON_L1_02:GA-09","text":"[Emergent] Earth is not a passive receptacle, but a productive node where figure, influx, nourishment, and impression are converted into a new continuation.","refs":["CL_MHON_05:RMD-06","CL_MHON_05:XD-06"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"Composite","passages":[]},{"id":"MC_MHON_L1_02:GA-10","text":"[Terminal] Light, Word, and material impression effect local closures, in which multiple conversions arrive at coordinated form without the extinction of the machine.","refs":["CL_MHON_05:GA-10","CL_MHON_06:GA-09"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"Composite","passages":[]},{"id":"MC_MHON_L1_02:GA-11","text":"[Attractor] Hidden proportion and numerical power order the unity of relations, but their rule is never so explicated that further interpretation becomes superfluous.","refs":["CL_MHON_04:GLA-10","CL_MHON_06:GA-07"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"Tension","passages":[]},{"id":"MC_MHON_L1_02:GA-12","text":"[Emergent] Valid knowledge is operative: the interpreter must divide, convert, compare, and recombine figures in order to understand their powers.","refs":["CL_MHON_04:ALT-B05","CL_MHON_06:BSD-04"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"Composite","passages":[]}],"original_name":"Global Load-Bearing Axioms"},{"number":"3","name":"Recurrence, Mutation, and Displacement Matrix","items":[{"id":"MC_MHON_L1_02:RMD-01","text":"MONAS retains nominal identity, but passes from a principle of unity to the effect of coordinating multiple conversions.","refs":["CL_MHON_04:RMD-01","CL_MHON_06:RMD-05"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"Composite","passages":[]},{"id":"MC_MHON_L1_02:RMD-02","text":"Separation migrates from Lines and circles to elements, planetary successions, and partitions of the cross, where it is no longer dissolution but the production of power.","refs":["CL_MHON_04:RMD-02","CL_MHON_05:RMD-04","CL_MHON_06:MP-02"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"Composite","passages":[]},{"id":"MC_MHON_L1_02:RMD-03","text":"Circularity changes from a geometric figure into a circuit of relations, through which form returns with another numerical, cosmological, or semantic faculty.","refs":["CL_MHON_04:RMD-03","CL_MHON_06:RLE-01"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"Composite","passages":[]},{"id":"MC_MHON_L1_02:RMD-04","text":"Moon and Sun are displaced from compositional elements to substitutable media, generative principles, and channels of influx.","refs":["CL_MHON_05:RMD-02","CL_MHON_05:RMD-03"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"Composite","passages":[]},{"id":"MC_MHON_L1_02:RMD-05","text":"Mercury expands from a planetary degree into Microcosm, Adam, and mediator of transformation, so that its identity consists in passage.","refs":["CL_MHON_05:GA-05","CL_MHON_05:RLE-06"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"Composite","passages":[]},{"id":"MC_MHON_L1_02:RMD-06","text":"Earth changes from a cosmological or receptive term into an active medium of nourishment, impression, and causal continuation.","refs":["CL_MHON_05:RMD-06","CL_MHON_05:XD-06"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"Composite","passages":[]},{"id":"MC_MHON_L1_02:RMD-07","text":"The tenfold proportion and quinarity pass from local rules of elements or figure to attractors of composition across codes.","refs":["CL_MHON_04:RMD-04","CL_MHON_06:RMD-04"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"Composite","passages":[]},{"id":"MC_MHON_L1_02:RMD-08","text":"The hidden ceases to be mere absence and becomes an internal remainder of order, which impedes closure and awakens interpretative return.","refs":["CL_MHON_04:RMD-07","CL_MHON_06:RLE-05"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"Tension","passages":[]}],"original_name":"Recurrence, Mutation, and Displacement Matrix"},{"number":"4","name":"Lower-Layer vs Current-Level Distinction","items":[{"id":"MC_MHON_L1_02:CGD-01","text":"The constructions of individual Lines, numbers of revolutions, planetary houses, forms of Mercury, and particular calculations remain local.","refs":["CL_MHON_04:CGD-01","CL_MHON_05:CGD-01"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[]},{"id":"MC_MHON_L1_02:CGD-02","text":"At the present level, the rule is amplified that identity is defined through transformation and the passage of functions, not through a fixed state.","refs":["CL_MHON_05:CGD-02","CL_MHON_05:RMD-10"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"Composite","passages":[]},{"id":"MC_MHON_L1_02:CGD-03","text":"At this level, geometry, soul, Spirit, fire, and rays are not separate causes, but diverse media of one distribution of relations.","refs":["CL_MHON_04:CGD-04","CL_MHON_05:RMD-07","CL_MHON_06:GA-04"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"Composite","passages":[]},{"id":"MC_MHON_L1_02:CGD-04","text":"Impression upon Earth, reception of figure, and nourishment of the Magistery are reduced to one logic of active materiality.","refs":["CL_MHON_05:CGD-05","CL_MHON_05:XD-06"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"Composite","passages":[]},{"id":"MC_MHON_L1_02:CGD-05","text":"The image of the immobile sign, independent planet, and closed discipline becomes obsolete, because all are absorbed into the circulation of conversions.","refs":["CL_MHON_05:CGD-07","CL_MHON_06:GNX-01"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"Negative","passages":[]},{"id":"MC_MHON_L1_02:CGD-06","text":"Exact correspondences locally required do not ascend to a demonstrated universal law, because within the metaggregate they themselves remain points of fragility.","refs":["CL_MHON_05:XD-08","CL_MHON_05:FL-01"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"Tension","passages":[]},{"id":"MC_MHON_L1_02:CGD-07","text":"The new object of this level is a translational machine that composes figure, genealogy, number, influx, matter, and discourse under the same rule of mutation.","refs":["CL_MHON_05:CGD-08","CL_MHON_06:MME-01"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"Composite","passages":[]}],"original_name":"Lower-Layer vs Current-Level Distinction"},{"number":"5","name":"Global Apophatic Structure","items":[{"id":"MC_MHON_L1_02:GNX-01","text":"The system excludes a sign reduced to a bare image, mythical name, isolated letter, or abstract quantity.","refs":["CL_MHON_04:GNX-03","CL_MHON_05:GNX-01","CL_MHON_06:GNX-01"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"Negative","passages":[]},{"id":"MC_MHON_L1_02:GNX-02","text":"The system excludes immobile unity and a rigid opposition between one and many.","refs":["CL_MHON_04:GNX-02","CL_MHON_06:BSA-04"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"Negative","passages":[]},{"id":"MC_MHON_L1_02:GNX-03","text":"The system excludes a single origin of causality, whether defined as purely celestial or purely terrestrial.","refs":["CL_MHON_05:GNX-03","CL_MHON_05:BSA-04"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"Negative","passages":[]},{"id":"MC_MHON_L1_02:GNX-04","text":"The system excludes passive matter, because Earth, body, and receptacle not only receive effects but prolong them.","refs":["CL_MHON_05:GNX-05","CL_MHON_06:GNX-04"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"Negative","passages":[]},{"id":"MC_MHON_L1_02:GNX-05","text":"The system excludes immediate knowledge without operation, because understanding requires passage through figures, divisions, media, and conversions.","refs":["CL_MHON_04:GNX-05","CL_MHON_06:BSA-01"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"Negative","passages":[]},{"id":"MC_MHON_L1_02:GNX-06","text":"The system excludes a wholly explicit measure and an interpretation concluded once and for all, because residual hiddenness and further derivation remain structurally necessary.","refs":["CL_MHON_04:BSA-03","CL_MHON_06:GNX-05"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"Negative","passages":[]}],"original_name":"Global Apophatic Structure"},{"number":"6","name":"Deep Book Apophatics","items":[{"id":"MC_MHON_L1_02:DBN-01","text":"A world in which figures are passive things and disciplines autonomous orders must be false, because the machine of this level requires figures to operate and disciplines to be converted.","refs":["CL_MHON_06:DBN-01","CL_MHON_06:DBN-02"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"Negative","passages":[]},{"id":"MC_MHON_L1_02:DBN-02","text":"Identity consisting in invariant substance must be false, because planetary, numerical, and hieroglyphic identity endures through mutation of medium.","refs":["CL_MHON_05:CGD-02","CL_MHON_06:GA-03"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"Negative","passages":[]},{"id":"MC_MHON_L1_02:DBN-03","text":"The Magistery or hieroglyphic effect arising from a solitary celestial principle must be false, because reception, nourishment, and Earth are constitutive causes.","refs":["CL_MHON_05:DBN-03","CL_MHON_05:BSA-02"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"Negative","passages":[]},{"id":"MC_MHON_L1_02:DBN-04","text":"Number being separable from figure, or multiplication being additive, must be false, because number emerges from position, partition, doubling, and comparison.","refs":["CL_MHON_06:DBN-04","CL_MHON_06:GA-08"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"Negative","passages":[]},{"id":"MC_MHON_L1_02:DBN-05","text":"The Word being immediate or semantic closure without derivation must be false, because the terminal Word is coordination of prior operations.","refs":["CL_MHON_06:DBN-05","CL_MHON_06:BSA-03"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"Negative","passages":[]}],"original_name":"Deep Book Apophatics"},{"number":"7","name":"Super-Apophatics of the Whole","items":[{"id":"MC_MHON_L1_02:BSA-01","text":"A purely immediate sense is unavailable, because the entire machine orders understanding through passage among figures, numbers, letters, media, and compositions.","refs":["CL_MHON_06:BSA-01","CL_MHON_04:BSA-01"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"Negative","passages":[]},{"id":"MC_MHON_L1_02:BSA-02","text":"A reality composed of mutually indifferent things is ontologically inadmissible, because position, proximity, division, and concord possess effective force.","refs":["CL_MHON_06:BSA-02","CL_MHON_04:BSA-04"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"Negative","passages":[]},{"id":"MC_MHON_L1_02:BSA-03","text":"A closure that wholly exhausts hiddenness, plurality, or mutation is forbidden, because such a closure would destroy the attractors of conversion.","refs":["CL_MHON_04:BSA-03","CL_MHON_06:BSA-03"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"Negative","passages":[]},{"id":"MC_MHON_L1_02:BSA-04","text":"The position of the subject as autonomous and originating artificer is unavailable, because interpreter and operator are constituted within mediated relations, influxes, and produced figures.","refs":["CL_MHON_05:BSA-01","CL_MHON_06:BSD-04"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"Negative","passages":[]}],"original_name":"Super-Apophatics of the Whole"},{"number":"8","name":"Cross-Unit Derivative Propositions","items":[{"id":"MC_MHON_L1_02:XD-01","text":"Astral relation and geometric partition manifest the same law of causality: property, dignity, and number emerge not from an isolated term, but from the disposition of relata.","refs":["MC_MHON_L1_02:GA-03","MC_MHON_L1_02:GA-07","CL_MHON_06:XD-01","CL_MHON_06:GA-05"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"Composite","passages":[]},{"id":"MC_MHON_L1_02:XD-02","text":"The substitution of planetary media and differentiation of MONAS indicate that identity is preserved through translation of function, not through the immobility of substances.","refs":["MC_MHON_L1_02:GA-02","MC_MHON_L1_02:GA-05","MC_MHON_L1_02:GA-06","CL_MHON_05:XD-04","CL_MHON_04:RMD-01"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"Tension","passages":[]},{"id":"MC_MHON_L1_02:XD-03","text":"Material reception does not terminate influx, but reconstitutes it into impressed figure, nourishment, and renewed circulation.","refs":["MC_MHON_L1_02:GA-06","MC_MHON_L1_02:GA-09","CL_MHON_05:XD-06","CL_MHON_05:FGS-B06"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"Composite","passages":[]},{"id":"MC_MHON_L1_02:XD-04","text":"No interpretative language prevails absolutely, because the same figure becomes successively intelligible through geometry, astronomy, number, letter, and Word.","refs":["MC_MHON_L1_02:GA-07","MC_MHON_L1_02:GA-12","CL_MHON_06:XD-06","CL_MHON_04:BSD-02"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"Composite","passages":[]},{"id":"MC_MHON_L1_02:XD-05","text":"Proportion and number are not merely instruments of closure, but motors of escalation that transfer hidden order into new forms of composition.","refs":["MC_MHON_L1_02:GA-03","MC_MHON_L1_02:GA-11","CL_MHON_04:XD-04","CL_MHON_06:RLE-02"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"Tension","passages":[]},{"id":"MC_MHON_L1_02:XD-06","text":"Mercury-Microcosm and MONAS-attractor jointly bring it about that man, planet, and figure are not merely reciprocal images, but nodes of the same cosmic-material passage.","refs":["MC_MHON_L1_02:GA-08","MC_MHON_L1_02:GA-09","CL_MHON_05:XD-03","CL_MHON_05:RLE-06"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"Composite","passages":[]},{"id":"MC_MHON_L1_02:XD-07","text":"Light and Word provide semantic closure only retroactively, because they return to reread the figure and reopen the machine of conversion.","refs":["MC_MHON_L1_02:GA-10","MC_MHON_L1_02:GA-12","CL_MHON_06:FGS-B05","CL_MHON_06:RLE-05"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"Tension","passages":[]}],"original_name":"Cross-Unit Derivative Propositions"},{"number":"9","name":"Book-Scale Super-Derivatives","items":[{"id":"MC_MHON_L1_02:BSD-01","text":"Reality is reconfigured as a field of convertible relations, in which identity does not precede operations but persists through them.","refs":["CL_MHON_06:BSD-01","CL_MHON_04:BSD-01"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"Composite","passages":[]},{"id":"MC_MHON_L1_02:BSD-02","text":"Causality becomes distributed and medial, because figures, rays, elements, souls, fires, and terrestrial receptacles produce effects only through circulation.","refs":["CL_MHON_04:BSD-05","CL_MHON_05:BSD-03"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"Composite","passages":[]},{"id":"MC_MHON_L1_02:BSD-03","text":"Time becomes a form of recursion, revolution, and redistribution, not a neutral container imposed upon external processes.","refs":["CL_MHON_04:BSD-04","CL_MHON_06:BSD-03"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"Composite","passages":[]},{"id":"MC_MHON_L1_02:BSD-04","text":"The knowing subject is not an external spectator, but a local operator constituted within the machine by reading, dividing, and composing conversions.","refs":["CL_MHON_04:BSD-03","CL_MHON_05:BSD-04","CL_MHON_06:BSD-04"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"Composite","passages":[]},{"id":"MC_MHON_L1_02:BSD-05","text":"Word and material impression are not opposed terms, but two modes in which figures condense their conversions' semantic and bodily efficacy.","refs":["CL_MHON_05:BSD-01","CL_MHON_06:BSD-05"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"Composite","passages":[]},{"id":"MC_MHON_L1_02:BSD-06","text":"The valid method changes from extraction of stable meaning to testing through a series of transformations, where reading is the restoration of a generative mechanism.","refs":["CL_MHON_04:BSD-06","CL_MHON_06:MME-05"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"Composite","passages":[]},{"id":"MC_MHON_L1_02:BSD-07","text":"Simple origin dissolves into a network of dependencies among principles, media, receptacles, and effects, so that genesis remains always distributed.","refs":["CL_MHON_05:BSD-07","CL_MHON_05:BSD-08"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"Composite","passages":[]}],"original_name":"Book-Scale Super-Derivatives"},{"number":"10","name":"Recursive Loops, Escalation Paths, and Conceptual Attractors","items":[{"id":"MC_MHON_L1_02:RLE-01","text":"The fundamental circuit is unity → distinction → proportion → conjunction → unity reread as the capacity for new differentiation.","refs":["CL_MHON_04:RLE-01","CL_MHON_06:RLE-01"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"Composite","passages":[]},{"id":"MC_MHON_L1_02:RLE-02","text":"The composition of signs produces succession, succession transformation, transformation material figure, and material figure retroactively attests hidden order.","refs":["CL_MHON_05:RLE-01"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"Composite","passages":[]},{"id":"MC_MHON_L1_02:RLE-03","text":"The trans-field circuit connects astronomical figure, elemental operation, geometric disposition, numerical code, and production of the Word.","refs":["CL_MHON_04:RLE-02","CL_MHON_06:RLE-03"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"Composite","passages":[]},{"id":"MC_MHON_L1_02:RLE-04","text":"Substitution of medium is a conservative circuit: Moon, Spirit, soul, Sun, Mercury, or Earth receive another's unstable function without the end of the work being lost.","refs":["CL_MHON_05:RLE-02","CL_MHON_05:HSI-05"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"Composite","passages":[]},{"id":"MC_MHON_L1_02:RLE-05","text":"Escalation occurs through translation of the same function from space to element, from element to time, from time to body, and from body to discourse.","refs":["CL_MHON_04:RLE-03","CL_MHON_05:RLE-05"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"Composite","passages":[]},{"id":"MC_MHON_L1_02:RLE-06","text":"MONAS, Mercury, hidden proportion, and Word are heterogeneous attractors that compel the dispersion of figures into convertible order without univocal reduction.","refs":["CL_MHON_04:RLE-04","CL_MHON_05:RLE-06","CL_MHON_06:RLE-04"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"Composite","passages":[]},{"id":"MC_MHON_L1_02:RLE-07","text":"Formal closure is also a point of re-entry, because the numerical, material, or verbally magisterial term halts the process and at once generates a new question concerning the rule.","refs":["CL_MHON_04:RLE-05","CL_MHON_06:RLE-05"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"Tension","passages":[]}],"original_name":"Recursive Loops, Escalation Paths, and Conceptual Attractors"},{"number":"11","name":"Formal Generative Schema of the Whole Book","items":[{"id":"MC_MHON_L1_02:FGS-B01","text":"Initial relation → determinate figure → ordered distinction, where unity is not lost but distributed into operative parts.","refs":["CL_MHON_04:GLA-01","CL_MHON_06:GA-01"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"Composite","passages":[]},{"id":"MC_MHON_L1_02:FGS-B02","text":"Partition, proximity, position, and circulation → proportion, dignity, or number, where geometric form produces properties of relation.","refs":["CL_MHON_04:GLA-03","CL_MHON_06:XD-01"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"Composite","passages":[]},{"id":"MC_MHON_L1_02:FGS-B03","text":"Cosmic or geometric figure → hieroglyphic translation → letter, element, planet, or numerical power.","refs":["CL_MHON_05:FGS-B01","CL_MHON_06:FGS-B02"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"Composite","passages":[]},{"id":"MC_MHON_L1_02:FGS-B04","text":"Spiritual and material circulatory media → passage among heaven, soul, body, and Earth → operative influx.","refs":["CL_MHON_05:XD-05","CL_MHON_05:HSI-01"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"Composite","passages":[]},{"id":"MC_MHON_L1_02:FGS-B05","text":"Terrestrial reception → nourishment, impression, and continuation → return of figure as causal effect.","refs":["CL_MHON_05:FGS-B06","CL_MHON_05:MME-04"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"Composite","passages":[]},{"id":"MC_MHON_L1_02:FGS-B06","text":"Proportion and numerical composition → coordination of multiple powers → Light, Word, or materially completed figure.","refs":["CL_MHON_06:FGS-B04","CL_MHON_06:GA-09"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"Composite","passages":[]},{"id":"MC_MHON_L1_02:FGS-B07","text":"Hidden remainder, ambiguity of medium, and incompletion of rule → retroactive rereading → regeneration of the machine.","refs":["CL_MHON_04:BSA-03","CL_MHON_05:FL-02","CL_MHON_06:RLE-05"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"Tension","passages":[]}],"original_name":"Formal Generative Schema of the Whole Book"},{"number":"12","name":"Whole-Book Argument Flow Map","items":[{"id":"MC_MHON_L1_02:AFM-B01","text":"Unity of relation → geometric separation → ordering proportion → differentiated unity.","refs":["CL_MHON_04:AFM-B01"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"Composite","passages":[]},{"id":"MC_MHON_L1_02:AFM-B02","text":"Astral figure → hieroglyphic composition → Lunar and Solar Nature → Mercury as transformative medium.","refs":["CL_MHON_05:AFM-B01"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"Composite","passages":[]},{"id":"MC_MHON_L1_02:AFM-B03","text":"Mercury, soul, Spirit, and rays → terrestrial reception → nourishment, impression, and reconstituted circulation.","refs":["CL_MHON_05:FGS-B04","CL_MHON_05:AFM-B03"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"Composite","passages":[]},{"id":"MC_MHON_L1_02:AFM-B04","text":"Relation of position and partition → operational figure → letter and number → multiplied power.","refs":["CL_MHON_06:AFM-B01","CL_MHON_06:AFM-B04"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"Composite","passages":[]},{"id":"MC_MHON_L1_02:AFM-B05","text":"Numerical power → coordination of semantic codes → Light and Word → figure retroactively reread.","refs":["CL_MHON_06:AFM-B05","CL_MHON_06:FGS-B05"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"Composite","passages":[]},{"id":"MC_MHON_L1_02:AFM-B06","text":"Material or verbal closure → hidden remainder → substitution of medium → new circuit of conversion.","refs":["CL_MHON_04:BSA-03","CL_MHON_05:HSI-05"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"Tension","passages":[]}],"original_name":"Whole-Book Argument Flow Map"},{"number":"13","name":"Macro Map-Effects","items":[{"id":"MC_MHON_L1_02:MME-01","text":"The primary object is reclassified: it is not an isolated sign, but a nexus joining cosmic, material, numerical, and verbal orders into operation.","refs":["CL_MHON_04:MME-01","CL_MHON_06:MME-01"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"Composite","passages":[]},{"id":"MC_MHON_L1_02:MME-02","text":"The opposition between unity and distinction, like that between heaven and Earth, is dissolved into stages of one transformative circulation.","refs":["CL_MHON_04:MME-02","CL_MHON_05:MME-02"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"Composite","passages":[]},{"id":"MC_MHON_L1_02:MME-03","text":"Time, Earth, and body are reclassified as fields of construction, reception, and reproduction, not as passive termini of celestial relations.","refs":["CL_MHON_04:MME-03","CL_MHON_05:MME-04"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"Composite","passages":[]},{"id":"MC_MHON_L1_02:MME-04","text":"Numbers and letters cease to be bare notations and become indices of formal causes, whose force consists in section, position, doubling, and coordination.","refs":["CL_MHON_06:MME-04","CL_MHON_06:BSD-05"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"Composite","passages":[]},{"id":"MC_MHON_L1_02:MME-05","text":"Reading necessarily becomes diagrammatic, comparative, and translational, because the meaning of one word or immobile figure cannot produce structure.","refs":["CL_MHON_04:MME-05","CL_MHON_06:MME-05"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"Composite","passages":[]},{"id":"MC_MHON_L1_02:MME-06","text":"Anatomy, hieroglyph, and composite figure become compressed tables through which cosmology, alchemy, anthropology, and the art of interpretation may operate simultaneously.","refs":["CL_MHON_05:MME-07","CL_MHON_06:GA-02"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"Composite","passages":[]}],"original_name":"Macro Map-Effects"},{"number":"14","name":"Altitude Integration","items":[{"id":"MC_MHON_L1_02:ALT-B01","text":"The surface level contains MONAS, Sun, Moon, Aries, Taurus, Mercury, cross, fire, rays, Earth, numbers, and letters as explicit signs.","refs":["CL_MHON_04:ALT-B01","CL_MHON_05:ALT-B01","CL_MHON_06:ALT-B01"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[]},{"id":"MC_MHON_L1_02:ALT-B02","text":"The structural level shows the same machine in separation, succession, conversion, substitution, circulation, and composition.","refs":["CL_MHON_04:ALT-B02","CL_MHON_05:ALT-B02","CL_MHON_06:ALT-B02"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"Composite","passages":[]},{"id":"MC_MHON_L1_02:ALT-B03","text":"The metastrategic level passes from the question of what a sign means to the question of how a sign produces relations, effects, and fields of knowledge.","refs":["CL_MHON_04:ALT-B03","CL_MHON_05:ALT-B03","CL_MHON_06:ALT-B03"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"Composite","passages":[]},{"id":"MC_MHON_L1_02:ALT-B04","text":"The ontological-temporal level posits reality as a transmutative order, in which unity, time, dignity, body, and place emerge from operations.","refs":["CL_MHON_04:ALT-B04","CL_MHON_05:ALT-B04","CL_MHON_06:ALT-B04"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"Composite","passages":[]},{"id":"MC_MHON_L1_02:ALT-B05","text":"The methodological-performative level makes the text divide, convert, count, and reconnect figures, so that the form of exposition carries out its own thesis.","refs":["CL_MHON_04:ALT-B05","CL_MHON_05:ALT-B05","CL_MHON_06:ALT-B05"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"Composite","passages":[]}],"original_name":"Altitude Integration"},{"number":"15","name":"Internal Tensions, Mutation Points, and Fault Lines","items":[{"id":"MC_MHON_L1_02:FL-01","text":"Proportion and exact correspondence are promised as foundations of order, but their numerical rule or complete mechanism is often not exhibited.","refs":["CL_MHON_04:FL-01","CL_MHON_05:XD-08","CL_MHON_05:FL-01"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"Tension","passages":[]},{"id":"MC_MHON_L1_02:FL-02","text":"Mercury oscillates among planet, Microcosm, Adam, mediator, and substitutable place, so that its systemic fertility simultaneously renders its identity unstable.","refs":["CL_MHON_05:FL-02","CL_MHON_05:GA-05"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"Tension","passages":[]},{"id":"MC_MHON_L1_02:FL-03","text":"The passage from geometry to letter or Word is often sustained by symbolic congruence, not by formally demonstrated necessity everywhere.","refs":["CL_MHON_06:FL-02","CL_MHON_06:FL-04"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"Tension","passages":[]},{"id":"MC_MHON_L1_02:FL-04","text":"Causality is distributed among heaven, mediating souls, and Earth, but the relative weight of celestial principle, terrestrial receptacle, and radial influx is not definitively determined.","refs":["CL_MHON_05:FL-04","CL_MHON_05:HSI-04"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"Tension","passages":[]},{"id":"MC_MHON_L1_02:MP-01","text":"Aries changes from a zodiacal place into a principle of transformation, whereby astronomical position is converted into internal operation.","refs":["CL_MHON_04:MP-01","CL_MHON_04:GLA-07"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"Composite","passages":[]},{"id":"MC_MHON_L1_02:MP-02","text":"The sign changes from an indicative figure into an operator, when the hieroglyph begins to generate relations and powers.","refs":["CL_MHON_04:CGD-05","CL_MHON_06:ABS-02"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"Composite","passages":[]},{"id":"MC_MHON_L1_02:MP-03","text":"The cross and numerical figures begin to change when position, doubling, and squaring bestow arithmetic force upon geometry.","refs":["CL_MHON_06:MP-02","CL_MHON_06:GA-08"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"Composite","passages":[]},{"id":"MC_MHON_L1_02:MP-04","text":"Number changes into Word when quantitative composition is no longer an end, but coordination of semantic and theological-verbal powers.","refs":["CL_MHON_06:CGD-06","CL_MHON_06:BSD-05"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"Composite","passages":[]}],"original_name":"Internal Tensions, Mutation Points, and Fault Lines"},{"number":"16","name":"Hidden System Image","items":[{"id":"MC_MHON_L1_02:HSI-01","text":"The world is implicitly a machine of circulation and conversion, in which figures, media, bodies, and receptacles mutually produce one another.","refs":["CL_MHON_04:HSI-01","CL_MHON_05:HSI-01","CL_MHON_06:HSI-01"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"Composite","passages":[]},{"id":"MC_MHON_L1_02:HSI-02","text":"The subject is an operator-medium rather than an external master: Mercury-Soul and the initiated interpreter read and transduce distributed relations.","refs":["CL_MHON_05:HSI-02","CL_MHON_06:HSI-02"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"Composite","passages":[]},{"id":"MC_MHON_L1_02:HSI-03","text":"Time is spiral, because recognizable form undergoes change through returns and effects a new power in another field.","refs":["CL_MHON_04:HSI-03","CL_MHON_06:HSI-03"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"Composite","passages":[]},{"id":"MC_MHON_L1_02:HSI-04","text":"The central contest opposes formal closure and hidden remainder, not simply true and false sign.","refs":["CL_MHON_04:HSI-04","CL_MHON_06:HSI-04"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"Tension","passages":[]},{"id":"MC_MHON_L1_02:HSI-05","text":"Flight or mobility within the machine is not abolition of relations, but substitution of medium and translation of code when one term begins to fail in its function.","refs":["CL_MHON_05:HSI-05","CL_MHON_05:XD-04"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"Composite","passages":[]}],"original_name":"Hidden System Image"},{"number":"17","name":"Minimal Whole-Book Reconstruction Test","items":[{"id":"MC_MHON_L1_02:MRT-B01","text":"The axioms of differentiated unity, operational figure, medial causality, conversion of codes, active reception, and non-final closure suffice to restore the minimal machine of the metaggregate.","refs":["CL_MHON_04:MRT-B01","CL_MHON_05:MRT-B01","CL_MHON_06:MRT-B01"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[]},{"id":"MC_MHON_L1_02:MRT-B02","text":"The firmest structure is where relation, partition, mediation, and conversion are demonstrated as productive operations rather than analogical ornaments.","refs":["CL_MHON_04:MRT-B02","CL_MHON_05:MRT-B03","CL_MHON_06:MRT-B02"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"Composite","passages":[]},{"id":"MC_MHON_L1_02:MRT-B03","text":"The exact measure of proportion, laws of revolutions, identity of Mercury, efficacy of rays, passage from geometry to letter, and connection of calculations to Word remain less determined.","refs":["CL_MHON_04:MRT-B04","CL_MHON_05:MRT-B04","CL_MHON_06:MRT-B03"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"Tension","passages":[]},{"id":"MC_MHON_L1_02:MRT-B04","text":"The reconstruction preserves the true movement if the return of figures is read as change of function and scale, not as doctrinal repetition or a series of independent chapters.","refs":["CL_MHON_04:MRT-B03","CL_MHON_05:MRT-B05","CL_MHON_06:MRT-B05"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"Composite","passages":[]}],"original_name":"Minimal Whole-Book Reconstruction Test"}],"revision":"MC_MHON_L1_02@R001","source_variant_id":"canonical:MC_MHON_L1_02:v1","source_language_code":"la","original_title":"Mhon L1 02"}],"identity":{"layer":"MC","kicker":"Meta-cluster · Monas Hieroglyphica","title":"Mhon L1 02","context":"Chapters 10–18 · T H E O R. I X. · T H E O R. X. · Theorem Xi. · T H E O R. Xii. · Theorem Xiii. · Theorem Xiii. · Theorem Xv. · Theorem Xvi. · Theorem Xvii.","original_title":"Mhon L1 02","original_context":"Chapters 10–18 · T H E O R. I X. · T H E O R. X. · Theor. Xi. · T H E O R. Xii. · Theor. Xiii. · Theorema Xiii. · Theor. Xv. · Theor. Xvi. · Theor. Xvii."},"original_title":"Mhon L1 02","title_language_code":"en"},{"id":"MC_MHON_L1_03","layer":"MC","title":"Mhon L1 03","order":3,"parents":["CL_MHON_07","CL_MHON_08","CL_MHON_09"],"revision":"MC_MHON_L1_03@R001","sha256":"31533405b0665570c73af5b928772b02e99df9c658a18efc15cb18229dec1ab3","sections":[{"number":"1","name":"Book-Scale Argumental Spine","items":[{"id":"MC_MHON_L1_03:AS-01","text":"Quaestio centralis est quomodo Monas ordines caelestes, materiales, figurales et numericos in una machina comprehendat, dum divisionem internam et commensurationem sustinet neque differentias eorum abolet.","refs":["CL_MHON_07:BAS-B01","CL_MHON_08:ABS-01","CL_MHON_09:AS-B01"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[]},{"id":"MC_MHON_L1_03:AS-02","text":"Thesis metagregalis Monadem constituit non ut rem simpliciter unam, sed ut structuram relationis quae se distinguit, partes reordinat et originem, processum atque finem in eodem signo colligit.","refs":["CL_MHON_07:BAS-B03","CL_MHON_08:MME-01","CL_MHON_09:MME-01"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[]},{"id":"MC_MHON_L1_03:AS-03","text":"Argumentatio fit transductiva, hieroglyphico-operativa et numerologico-metaphysica: analogia campos coniungit, operatio efficaciam condicionat, geometria invariantes secernit, numerus mutationem ordinat.","refs":["CL_MHON_07:BAS-B06","CL_MHON_08:ABS-05","CL_MHON_09:AS-B03"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[]},{"id":"MC_MHON_L1_03:AS-04","text":"Directio systematis procedit ab influxu in corpus, a corpore in dissectionem et recompositionem, a recompositione in mensuram consignatam, atque inde in reditum ad ordinem divinum.","refs":["CL_MHON_07:BAS-B07","CL_MHON_08:AFM-B06","CL_MHON_09:AFM-B04"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[]},{"id":"MC_MHON_L1_03:AS-05","text":"Motus verus est spiralis et mutatione actus: differentia operationem generat, operatio novam distinctionem postulat, mensura in numerum amplificatur, et terminus in principium ulterioris ordinationis convertitur.","refs":["CL_MHON_08:ABS-06","CL_MHON_09:AS-B05","CL_MHON_07:RLE-04"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[]},{"id":"MC_MHON_L1_03:AS-06","text":"Clausura est simul interna, derivata et inexhausta: Monas proportionem propriam tuetur, sed eius completio neque ab operatione neque a voluntate creatoris neque ab ulteriori extractione mysterii separari potest.","refs":["CL_MHON_07:BAS-B08","CL_MHON_08:ABS-07","CL_MHON_09:AS-B06"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[]}],"original_name":"Book-Scale Argumental Spine"},{"number":"2","name":"Global Load-Bearing Axioms","items":[{"id":"MC_MHON_L1_03:GA-01","text":"[Attractor] Monas est centrum relationale quod translationes, differentias internas et ordinem cosmicum colligit sine eorum reductione ad identitatem simplicem.","refs":["CL_MHON_07:GA-01","CL_MHON_08:MME-01","CL_MHON_09:MME-01"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[]},{"id":"MC_MHON_L1_03:GA-02","text":"[Persistent] Proportio sive relatio invisibilis est criterium quo compositio, figura, transformatio et clausura validantur.","refs":["CL_MHON_07:GA-04","CL_MHON_09:GA-02","CL_MHON_09:GA-09"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[]},{"id":"MC_MHON_L1_03:GA-03","text":"[Mutating] Vis elementi non est proprietas invariabilis, sed functio situs, nexus et configurationis; metathesis igitur rem non necessario mutat substantialiter, sed munus eius transponit.","refs":["CL_MHON_07:GA-05","CL_MHON_08:GA-02","CL_MHON_08:CGD-02"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[]},{"id":"MC_MHON_L1_03:GA-04","text":"[Emergent] Differentiatio unitatem non corrumpit, sed repertorium partium et relationum producit ex quo reunio et compositio ulterior oriuntur.","refs":["CL_MHON_08:GA-01","CL_MHON_08:GA-04","CL_MHON_08:MME-02"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[]},{"id":"MC_MHON_L1_03:GA-05","text":"[Persistent] Translatio inter caelum, materiam, figuram, numerum et mysterium mediationem requirit; similitudo sola neque identitatem neque necessitatem causaliter constituit.","refs":["CL_MHON_07:DBN-04","CL_MHON_08:GA-03","CL_MHON_09:BSD-01"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[]},{"id":"MC_MHON_L1_03:GA-06","text":"[Mutating] Ars est potentia realis sed derivata: materiam datam et potentias incompletas ordinat, neque perfectionem naturalem aut fundamentum sui ex nihilo producit.","refs":["CL_MHON_07:XD-06","CL_MHON_08:GA-05","CL_MHON_09:BSD-04"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[]},{"id":"MC_MHON_L1_03:GA-07","text":"[Mutating] Signum transit ab indice hieroglyphico ad condensatorem materialem, instrumentum operationis et actum consignationis transmissibilem.","refs":["CL_MHON_07:BAS-B04","CL_MHON_08:GA-06","CL_MHON_09:GA-07"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[]},{"id":"MC_MHON_L1_03:GA-08","text":"[Persistent] Cognitio valida requirit congruentiam interpretationis, effectus, operationis et dispositionis interpretis; visus immediatus aut vulgaris non sufficit.","refs":["CL_MHON_07:GA-09","CL_MHON_08:GA-07","CL_MHON_09:GA-10"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[]},{"id":"MC_MHON_L1_03:GA-09","text":"[Emergent] Dissolutio, subtractio et terminatio sunt operationes generativae ubi relationem originis servant et novam distinctionem vel circulationem producunt.","refs":["CL_MHON_07:GA-07","CL_MHON_08:GA-04","CL_MHON_09:GA-05"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[]},{"id":"MC_MHON_L1_03:GA-10","text":"[Persistent] Corporeitas et causalitas creatae sunt reales sed dependentes: utraque distributione superiori, mensura et limite ordinatur.","refs":["CL_MHON_07:GA-06","CL_MHON_09:GA-04","CL_MHON_09:BSA-01"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[]},{"id":"MC_MHON_L1_03:GA-11","text":"[Mutating] Circulus mutatur a figura caelesti in motum artificiosum, circuitum differentiae et formam cosmicam reditus ad principium.","refs":["CL_MHON_07:RMD-02","CL_MHON_08:RMD-01","CL_MHON_09:RLE-01"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[]},{"id":"MC_MHON_L1_03:GA-12","text":"[Emergent] Numerus est operator qualitativus qui munera configurationis, permutationis et metamorphosis determinat, non quantitas nuda.","refs":["CL_MHON_07:MP-03","CL_MHON_09:GA-03","CL_MHON_09:XD-02"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[]},{"id":"MC_MHON_L1_03:GA-13","text":"[Terminal] Clausura valida est reditus finitus ad proportionem propriam et ad voluntatem creatoris, non progressus creatus indefinitus.","refs":["CL_MHON_07:GA-08","CL_MHON_09:GNX-04","CL_MHON_09:GA-08"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[]},{"id":"MC_MHON_L1_03:GA-14","text":"[Attractor] Inexhaustio et relatio occulta prohibent ut clausura structurali in saturationem interpretativam convertatur.","refs":["CL_MHON_08:GA-08","CL_MHON_09:GA-09","CL_MHON_09:RMD-04"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[]}],"original_name":"Global Load-Bearing Axioms"},{"number":"3","name":"Recurrence, Mutation, and Displacement Matrix","items":[{"id":"MC_MHON_L1_03:RMD-01","text":"[Absorptio] Monas a centro translationum in machinam internam differentiarum et tandem in tabulam ontologicam originis, processus et finis amplificatur.","refs":["CL_MHON_07:RMD-07","CL_MHON_08:MME-01","CL_MHON_09:MME-01"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[]},{"id":"MC_MHON_L1_03:RMD-02","text":"[Identitas stabilis] Proportio persistit tamquam regula iudicii, sed ambitus eius a compositione materiali ad invariantem universalem ordinis demonstrabilis extenditur.","refs":["CL_MHON_07:GA-04","CL_MHON_09:RMD-01"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[]},{"id":"MC_MHON_L1_03:RMD-03","text":"[Mutatio functionalis] Circulatio transit a cursu caelesti ad transformationem artificialem, inde ad recursionem distinctionis et denique ad completionem cosmicam.","refs":["CL_MHON_07:RMD-02","CL_MHON_08:RMD-01","CL_MHON_09:RLE-01"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[]},{"id":"MC_MHON_L1_03:RMD-04","text":"[Transformatio terminalis] Destructio et dissolutio amittunt vim terminalem: primum distinctionem materialem, deinde inventarium recompositionis, postremo circuitum completionis efficiunt.","refs":["CL_MHON_07:RMD-06","CL_MHON_08:GA-04","CL_MHON_09:DBN-03"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[]},{"id":"MC_MHON_L1_03:RMD-05","text":"[Inversio] Punctum a copula necessaria in superfluitatem confusivam invertitur, deinde tamquam centrum effusionis et reditus restituitur.","refs":["CL_MHON_07:RMD-05","CL_MHON_09:RLE-01"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[]},{"id":"MC_MHON_L1_03:RMD-06","text":"[Amplificatio] Quaternarius a discrimine localis configurationis ad structuram ordinationis migrat atque per XXIV in metamorphosin temporalem et cosmicam amplificatur.","refs":["CL_MHON_07:RMD-05","CL_MHON_09:GA-03","CL_MHON_09:RMD-02"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[]},{"id":"MC_MHON_L1_03:RMD-07","text":"[Mutatio functionalis] Ars a motu naturam imitante ad translationem instrumentorum et tandem ad potestatem creatam, realem sed limitatam, progreditur.","refs":["CL_MHON_07:MME-03","CL_MHON_08:RMD-03","CL_MHON_09:BSD-04"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[]},{"id":"MC_MHON_L1_03:RMD-08","text":"[Tensio] Exterioritas primum ut supplementum Monae excluditur, deinde ut ambitus efficaciae materialis redit, postremo ut superior relatio ad creatorem recipitur.","refs":["CL_MHON_07:GNX-05","CL_MHON_08:XD-03","CL_MHON_09:GA-08"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[]},{"id":"MC_MHON_L1_03:RMD-09","text":"[Redditus functionalis] Mysterium a residuo occulto in regulam extractionis recurrentis et deinde in formam consignabilem sed non exhaustam mutatur.","refs":["CL_MHON_08:RMD-05","CL_MHON_09:GA-07"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[]}],"original_name":"Recurrence, Mutation, and Displacement Matrix"},{"number":"4","name":"Lower-Layer vs Current-Level Distinction","items":[{"id":"MC_MHON_L1_03:CGD-01","text":"Aquila, Scarabeus, ovum et correspondentiae eius manent machinae locales metamorphosis; ad gradum metagregalem tantum structura conflictus, compositionis et absorptionis ascendit.","refs":["CL_MHON_07:CGD-01","CL_MHON_07:CGD-02"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[]},{"id":"MC_MHON_L1_03:CGD-02","text":"Litterae, membra et vasa manent notationes locales; sursum transfertur metathesis qua elementa eadem munera diversa accipiunt.","refs":["CL_MHON_08:CGD-01","CL_MHON_08:CGD-02"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[]},{"id":"MC_MHON_L1_03:CGD-03","text":"Mensurae AB, relationes linearum et imagines apocalypticae manent apparatus calibrationis locales; praesentis gradus structura retinet distinctionem invariantium a contingentibus et nexum completionis cum tempore.","refs":["CL_MHON_09:CGD-01","CL_MHON_09:CGD-02"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[]},{"id":"MC_MHON_L1_03:CGD-04","text":"Materiae particulares et condiciones ambitus non universalizantur, sed earum functio amplificatur in regulam qua efficacia semper compositione et situ physico condicionatur.","refs":["CL_MHON_07:CGD-02","CL_MHON_08:CGD-03"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[]},{"id":"MC_MHON_L1_03:CGD-05","text":"Structura proprie metagregalis oritur ubi distributio caelestis, dissectio monadica et commensuratio geometrica ut tres modi eiusdem ordinationis proportionalis coordinantur.","refs":["CL_MHON_07:CGD-06","CL_MHON_08:CGD-04","CL_MHON_09:ALT-B02"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[]},{"id":"MC_MHON_L1_03:CGD-06","text":"Recognitio visiva, arithmetica nuda et correspondentia isolata superantur; valent tantum si in operationem, transformationem et intellectum ordinatum inseruntur.","refs":["CL_MHON_08:CGD-05","CL_MHON_09:CGD-03","CL_MHON_09:MME-04"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[]},{"id":"MC_MHON_L1_03:CGD-07","text":"Transitus maior praesentis gradus procedit a causalitate distributa per machinam internam differentiarum ad grammaticam geometricam inter campos.","refs":["CL_MHON_07:BSD-02","CL_MHON_08:MME-01","CL_MHON_09:BSD-01"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[]}],"original_name":"Lower-Layer vs Current-Level Distinction"},{"number":"5","name":"Global Apophatic Structure","items":[{"id":"MC_MHON_L1_03:GNX-01","text":"Systema excludit materiam autonomam et causalitatem creatam absolutam; corpus et agens intra distributionem, mensuram et relationem superiorem manent.","refs":["CL_MHON_07:GNX-01","CL_MHON_09:BSA-01"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"Negative","passages":[]},{"id":"MC_MHON_L1_03:GNX-02","text":"Systema excludit signum ornamentale vel neutrum, quia figura debet relationem, condicionem operationis aut actum consignationis gerere.","refs":["CL_MHON_07:DBN-01","CL_MHON_08:DBN-02"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"Negative","passages":[]},{"id":"MC_MHON_L1_03:GNX-03","text":"Systema excludit identitatem immediatam figurae, essentiae et operationis; similitudo et efficacia non conferunt per se perfectionem naturalem.","refs":["CL_MHON_08:GNX-01","CL_MHON_08:BSA-02"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"Negative","passages":[]},{"id":"MC_MHON_L1_03:GNX-04","text":"Systema excludit vim elementorum ab omni configuratione independentem; situs et nexus constituunt quid elementum possit.","refs":["CL_MHON_07:DBN-02","CL_MHON_07:GA-05"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"Negative","passages":[]},{"id":"MC_MHON_L1_03:GNX-05","text":"Systema excludit artem quae essentiam naturalem ex nihilo producat vel sibi fundamentum causalitatis praebeat.","refs":["CL_MHON_08:DBN-03","CL_MHON_09:BSD-04"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"Negative","passages":[]},{"id":"MC_MHON_L1_03:GNX-06","text":"Systema excludit unitatem immobilem et indivisam, quia unitas monadica tantum per differentiam conservatam et reunionem operatur.","refs":["CL_MHON_08:BSA-03","CL_MHON_08:GA-01"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"Negative","passages":[]},{"id":"MC_MHON_L1_03:GNX-07","text":"Systema excludit tam progressum creatum infinitum quam clausuram inertem; terminus legitimus circulationem finitam et reditum producit.","refs":["CL_MHON_09:GNX-04","CL_MHON_09:DBN-03"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"Negative","passages":[]},{"id":"MC_MHON_L1_03:GNX-08","text":"Systema excludit cognitionem universaliter publicam, visualem aut exhaustivam; accessus, operatio et intellectus symbolicus sunt condiciones constitutivae.","refs":["CL_MHON_08:GNX-04","CL_MHON_09:DBN-04"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"Negative","passages":[]}],"original_name":"Global Apophatic Structure"},{"number":"6","name":"Deep Book Apophatics","items":[{"id":"MC_MHON_L1_03:DBN-01","text":"Falsum esse debet ordines heterogeneos sine mediatore converti posse; sine proportione vel regula translationis unitas systematis in analogias seiunctas dissolveretur.","refs":["CL_MHON_07:DBN-04","CL_MHON_09:BSA-04"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"Negative","passages":[]},{"id":"MC_MHON_L1_03:DBN-02","text":"Falsum esse debet distinctionem partes in substantias autonomas mutare; aliter dissectio relationem originis communis et facultatem reunionis perderet.","refs":["CL_MHON_08:DBN-01","CL_MHON_08:GA-01"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"Negative","passages":[]},{"id":"MC_MHON_L1_03:DBN-03","text":"Falsum esse debet effectum artificialem et perfectionem naturalem coincidere; aliter ars derivata in causalitatem absolutam converteretur.","refs":["CL_MHON_08:DBN-03","CL_MHON_09:BSD-04"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"Negative","passages":[]},{"id":"MC_MHON_L1_03:DBN-04","text":"Distinctio inter supplementum operationale, condicionem ambientalem et principium ontologicum necessaria est; sine ea autarkia Monae et dependentia creata in contradictionem simplicem caderent.","refs":["CL_MHON_08:FL-02","CL_MHON_09:GA-08"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"Tension","passages":[]},{"id":"MC_MHON_L1_03:DBN-05","text":"Falsum esse debet completionem significationem exhaurire; aliter consignatio mysterii seriem interpretationum clauderet potius quam transmitteret.","refs":["CL_MHON_08:GA-08","CL_MHON_09:GA-07"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"Negative","passages":[]},{"id":"MC_MHON_L1_03:DBN-06","text":"Falsum esse debet omnem imminutio­nem esse meram privationem; purificatio, dissolutio et limes tantum constant si absentia ordinata aliquid producit.","refs":["CL_MHON_07:DBN-03","CL_MHON_09:DBN-03"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"Negative","passages":[]}],"original_name":"Deep Book Apophatics"},{"number":"7","name":"Super-Apophatics of the Whole","items":[{"id":"MC_MHON_L1_03:BSA-01","text":"Systema circumagit legem universalem conversionis inter analogiam hieroglyphicam, causalitatem materialem, geometriam et auctoritatem cosmicam, sed talem legem nusquam plene praesto habet.","refs":["CL_MHON_07:FL-02","CL_MHON_09:BSA-04"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"Tension","passages":[]},{"id":"MC_MHON_L1_03:BSA-02","text":"Possibilitas creaturae quae sibi originem, mensuram et continuationem infinitam conferat ontologice inadmissibilis est.","refs":["CL_MHON_09:BSA-01","CL_MHON_09:GNX-04"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"Negative","passages":[]},{"id":"MC_MHON_L1_03:BSA-03","text":"Perspectiva totaliter extrinseca, quae totam Monadem sine dispositione vel initiatione possideat, indisponibilis manet.","refs":["CL_MHON_08:BSA-01","CL_MHON_09:GA-10"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"Negative","passages":[]},{"id":"MC_MHON_L1_03:BSA-04","text":"Principium exterior Monae ut supplementum perfectionis vetatur, sed creator tamquam ratio ontologica ultima retinetur; absentia distinctionis explicitae inter hos sensus exterioritatis systema ordinat.","refs":["CL_MHON_07:BSA-01","CL_MHON_09:GA-08"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"Tension","passages":[]},{"id":"MC_MHON_L1_03:BSA-05","text":"Interpretatio terminalis omnino perspicua impossibilis est: omnis reditus validus mysterium restituit potius quam consumit.","refs":["CL_MHON_08:GA-08","CL_MHON_08:AFM-B05"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"Negative","passages":[]}],"original_name":"Super-Apophatics of the Whole"},{"number":"8","name":"Cross-Unit Derivative Propositions","items":[{"id":"MC_MHON_L1_03:XD-01","text":"Efficacia transscalaris oritur cum invarians proportionalis per configurationes mutabiles transit: idem principium ordinem conservat, dum munera eius in materia, figura et numero mutantur.","refs":["MC_MHON_L1_03:GA-02","MC_MHON_L1_03:GA-03","MC_MHON_L1_03:GA-05","CL_MHON_07:GA-05","CL_MHON_08:GA-02","CL_MHON_09:GA-02"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"Composite","passages":[]},{"id":"MC_MHON_L1_03:XD-02","text":"Subtractio materialis, dissectio monadica et limes causalitatis creatae unam structuram negativam generativam constituunt: negatio recte ordinata differentiam, repertorium et reditum producit.","refs":["MC_MHON_L1_03:GA-04","MC_MHON_L1_03:GA-09","MC_MHON_L1_03:GA-13","CL_MHON_07:XD-03","CL_MHON_08:GA-04","CL_MHON_09:XD-04"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"Composite","passages":[]},{"id":"MC_MHON_L1_03:XD-03","text":"Ars efficax neque naturae opponitur neque cum ea coincidit: potentias receptas reconfigurat, sed mensuram et causalitatem quibus operatur non sibi ipsa confert.","refs":["MC_MHON_L1_03:GA-06","MC_MHON_L1_03:GA-10","CL_MHON_07:XD-06","CL_MHON_08:GA-05","CL_MHON_09:BSD-04"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"Composite","passages":[]},{"id":"MC_MHON_L1_03:XD-04","text":"Signum fit apparatus completus ubi relationem caelestem traducit, condicionem materialem condensat et ordinem numericum consignat; significatio et operatio ibi separari nequeunt.","refs":["MC_MHON_L1_03:GA-07","MC_MHON_L1_03:GA-08","CL_MHON_07:XD-01","CL_MHON_08:GA-06","CL_MHON_09:GA-07"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"Composite","passages":[]},{"id":"MC_MHON_L1_03:XD-05","text":"Circulus est operator scalae: descensum caelestem in metamorphosin materialem, recursionem differentiae et reditum finitum ad principium convertit.","refs":["MC_MHON_L1_03:GA-11","MC_MHON_L1_03:GA-13","CL_MHON_07:RMD-02","CL_MHON_08:RLE-01","CL_MHON_09:RLE-01"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"Composite","passages":[]},{"id":"MC_MHON_L1_03:XD-06","text":"Numerus qualitatem configurationis formalizat: discrimen localis puncti per quaternarium ordinatur et in XXIV tamquam mensura mutationis cosmicae amplificatur.","refs":["MC_MHON_L1_03:GA-03","MC_MHON_L1_03:GA-12","CL_MHON_07:MP-03","CL_MHON_09:GA-03","CL_MHON_09:XD-02"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"Composite","passages":[]},{"id":"MC_MHON_L1_03:XD-07","text":"Probatio huius systematis non est deductio exterior simplex, sed congruentia inter effectum, operationem initiatam et intellectum qui structuram invisibilem in superficie discernit.","refs":["MC_MHON_L1_03:GA-08","MC_MHON_L1_03:GA-14","CL_MHON_07:XD-05","CL_MHON_08:XD-05","CL_MHON_09:GA-10"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"Composite","passages":[]},{"id":"MC_MHON_L1_03:XD-08","text":"Autarkia monadica tantum partialiter stabilitur si exterioritas dividitur in supplementum illegitimum, condicionem ambientalem et causam ontologicam; fontes distinctionem exigunt sed non formaliter perficiunt.","refs":["MC_MHON_L1_03:GA-06","MC_MHON_L1_03:GA-10","MC_MHON_L1_03:GA-13","CL_MHON_07:GNX-05","CL_MHON_08:XD-03","CL_MHON_09:GA-08"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"Tension","passages":[]},{"id":"MC_MHON_L1_03:XD-09","text":"Clausura et inexhaustio non sunt opposita simplicia: restitutio structuram finit, sed mysterium iter interpretationis et operationis denuo aperit.","refs":["MC_MHON_L1_03:GA-13","MC_MHON_L1_03:GA-14","CL_MHON_07:GA-08","CL_MHON_08:GA-08","CL_MHON_09:CGD-05"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"Tension","passages":[]}],"original_name":"Cross-Unit Derivative Propositions"},{"number":"9","name":"Book-Scale Super-Derivatives","items":[{"id":"MC_MHON_L1_03:BSD-01","text":"Realitas fit campus relationum transductivarum in quo materia, figura et numerus sunt status diversos eiusdem machinae ordinationis, non substantiae independentes.","refs":["CL_MHON_07:BSD-01","CL_MHON_08:BSD-01","CL_MHON_09:HSI-01"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[]},{"id":"MC_MHON_L1_03:BSD-02","text":"Causalitas est distributa et asymmetrica: superiora sustentant, ars reconfigurat, mensura limitat, sed nullum agens creatum totam seriem sibi appropriat.","refs":["CL_MHON_07:BSD-02","CL_MHON_09:BSA-01","CL_MHON_09:BSD-04"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[]},{"id":"MC_MHON_L1_03:BSD-03","text":"Tempus est spiralis iteratio potentiae, operationis, correctionis et reditus, non successio indefinita neque circulus identice repetitus.","refs":["CL_MHON_07:BSD-03","CL_MHON_08:BSD-03","CL_MHON_09:BSD-02"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[]},{"id":"MC_MHON_L1_03:BSD-04","text":"Subiectum fit artifex-interpres initiatus: neque spectator autonomus neque dominus causalis, sed administrator temporum, instrumentorum, signorum et limitum.","refs":["CL_MHON_07:HSI-02","CL_MHON_08:HSI-02","CL_MHON_09:HSI-02"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[]},{"id":"MC_MHON_L1_03:BSD-05","text":"Veritas constituitur ex concordia inter invariantem relationem, apparatum materialem, operationem rectam et effectum observabilem; nulla harum condicionum sola sufficit.","refs":["CL_MHON_07:BSD-06","CL_MHON_08:BSD-02","CL_MHON_09:GA-02"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[]},{"id":"MC_MHON_L1_03:BSD-06","text":"Auctoritas epistemica distribuitur per gradus accessus, sed figura consignata eandem disciplinam transmissibilem facit; scientia simul restringitur et propagatur.","refs":["CL_MHON_08:ABS-08","CL_MHON_08:MME-04","CL_MHON_09:AS-B07"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[]},{"id":"MC_MHON_L1_03:BSD-07","text":"Historia systematis non tendit ad plenitudinem cumulative possessam, sed ad clausuras locales quarum terminus novam extractionem sub mensura aperit.","refs":["CL_MHON_07:RLE-04","CL_MHON_08:GA-08","CL_MHON_09:GNX-04"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[]},{"id":"MC_MHON_L1_03:BSD-08","text":"Potentia agendi consistit in electione, compositione et resectione sub mensuris receptis; libertas operatoris est configurativa, non absoluta.","refs":["CL_MHON_07:BSD-04","CL_MHON_08:BSD-04","CL_MHON_09:BSD-03"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[]}],"original_name":"Book-Scale Super-Derivatives"},{"number":"10","name":"Recursive Loops, Escalation Paths, and Conceptual Attractors","items":[{"id":"MC_MHON_L1_03:RLE-01","text":"Influxus caelestis → effectus corporeus → analysis effectus → confirmatio ordinis causalitatis constituit circuitum quo causa posita per materiam interpretatam ad se redit.","refs":["CL_MHON_07:RLE-02"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[]},{"id":"MC_MHON_L1_03:RLE-02","text":"Dissectio → notatio → metathesis → operatio → recognitio originis communis → nova dissectio constituit recursionem internam Monae.","refs":["CL_MHON_08:RLE-01"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[]},{"id":"MC_MHON_L1_03:RLE-03","text":"Inaequalitas → ars ordinans → mensura → numerus → tempus cosmicum → voluntas divina constituit viam amplificationis a materia ad cosmologiam.","refs":["CL_MHON_09:AS-B05","CL_MHON_09:RLE-02"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[]},{"id":"MC_MHON_L1_03:RLE-04","text":"Disruptio → absorptio → purificatio → reunio sine exhaustione efficit ut omnis restitutio novae differentiae initium fiat.","refs":["CL_MHON_07:RLE-04","CL_MHON_08:FGS-B05"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[]},{"id":"MC_MHON_L1_03:RLE-05","text":"Signum → apparatus → operatio recta → apodixis → consignatio transmissibilis → nova interpretatio constituit circuitum epistemicum.","refs":["CL_MHON_08:XD-05","CL_MHON_09:GA-07"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[]},{"id":"MC_MHON_L1_03:RLE-06","text":"Proportio invisibilis est attractor distributus qui mutationes situs, materiae et numeri ad commune discrimen necessarii et contingentis reducit.","refs":["CL_MHON_07:RLE-05","CL_MHON_09:RLE-04"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[]},{"id":"MC_MHON_L1_03:RLE-07","text":"Duo attractores systema simul regunt: unio monadica differentias retrahit, inexhaustio autem prohibet ne attractor in possessionem plenam convertatur.","refs":["CL_MHON_08:RLE-04","CL_MHON_08:GA-08"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[]},{"id":"MC_MHON_L1_03:RLE-08","text":"Amplificatio quaternarii in XXIV est simul attractor completionis et commissura fragilis, quia mutationem cosmicam promittit sine regula conversionis geometricae perfecta.","refs":["CL_MHON_09:RLE-05","CL_MHON_09:XD-05"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[]}],"original_name":"Recursive Loops, Escalation Paths, and Conceptual Attractors"},{"number":"11","name":"Formal Generative Schema of the Whole Book","items":[{"id":"MC_MHON_L1_03:FGS-B01","text":"Heterogeneitas caelestis et elementaris → distributio asymmetrica virium → corpus dependens et natura inaequalis.","refs":["CL_MHON_07:GA-06","CL_MHON_09:GA-01"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[]},{"id":"MC_MHON_L1_03:FGS-B02","text":"Relatio invisibilis → proportio → mensura invarians → distinctio necessarii a contingenti.","refs":["CL_MHON_07:GA-04","CL_MHON_09:GA-02"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[]},{"id":"MC_MHON_L1_03:FGS-B03","text":"Unitas composita → dissectio et notatio → partes relatae → repertorium recompositionis.","refs":["CL_MHON_08:GA-04","CL_MHON_08:CGD-04"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[]},{"id":"MC_MHON_L1_03:FGS-B04","text":"Pars configurata → mutatio situs → metathesis functionalis → translatio inter figuram, vas et instrumentum.","refs":["CL_MHON_07:GA-05","CL_MHON_08:FGS-B03"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[]},{"id":"MC_MHON_L1_03:FGS-B05","text":"Potentia data → ars ordinans → operatio materialiter condicionata → effectus realis sine perfectione naturali absoluta.","refs":["CL_MHON_07:XD-06","CL_MHON_08:BSD-04"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[]},{"id":"MC_MHON_L1_03:FGS-B06","text":"Configuratio quaternaria → permutatio → numerus transformativus → amplificatio temporalis et cosmica per XXIV.","refs":["CL_MHON_09:GA-03","CL_MHON_09:RMD-02"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[]},{"id":"MC_MHON_L1_03:FGS-B07","text":"Potestas creata → exercitium finitum → imminutio impulsus → clausura circularis → reditus ad ordinem superiorem.","refs":["CL_MHON_09:GA-04","CL_MHON_09:CGD-05"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[]},{"id":"MC_MHON_L1_03:FGS-B08","text":"Operatio recta → restitutio unionis → consignatio mysterii → clausura structurata sine exhaustione interpretativa.","refs":["CL_MHON_08:FGS-B05","CL_MHON_09:GA-08"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[]}],"original_name":"Formal Generative Schema of the Whole Book"},{"number":"12","name":"Whole-Book Argument Flow Map","items":[{"id":"MC_MHON_L1_03:AFM-B01","text":"Influxus caelestis → compositio materialis → dissectio monadica → metathesis operativa → commensuratio geometrica → numerus transformativus → clausura circularis.","refs":["CL_MHON_07:BAS-B07","CL_MHON_08:ABS-06","CL_MHON_09:AS-B04"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[]},{"id":"MC_MHON_L1_03:AFM-B02","text":"Materia dependens → motus artificiosus → dissolutio selectiva → recompositio proportionata → restitutio sine annihilatione.","refs":["CL_MHON_07:GA-07","CL_MHON_08:XD-01"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[]},{"id":"MC_MHON_L1_03:AFM-B03","text":"Figura hieroglyphica → interpretatio condicionata → apparatus operativus → apodixis initiata → consignatio transmissibilis.","refs":["CL_MHON_07:BAS-B04","CL_MHON_08:XD-05","CL_MHON_09:GA-07"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[]},{"id":"MC_MHON_L1_03:AFM-B04","text":"Negatio autonomiae → exclusio identitatis immediatae → limitatio artis → terminatio causalitatis creatae → relatio ad principium.","refs":["CL_MHON_07:GNX-01","CL_MHON_08:GNX-01","CL_MHON_09:BSA-01"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[]},{"id":"MC_MHON_L1_03:AFM-B05","text":"Proportio propria → unio monadica → completio finita → reditus divinus → mysterium inexhaustum.","refs":["CL_MHON_07:GA-08","CL_MHON_08:AFM-B06","CL_MHON_09:GA-08"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[]}],"original_name":"Whole-Book Argument Flow Map"},{"number":"13","name":"Macro Map-Effects","items":[{"id":"MC_MHON_L1_03:MME-01","text":"Monas reclassificatur ex unitate simplici in machinam transductivam quae differentias producit, ordinat et ad originem refert.","refs":["CL_MHON_07:GA-01","CL_MHON_08:MME-01"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[]},{"id":"MC_MHON_L1_03:MME-02","text":"Corpus reclassificatur ex materia passiva in nodum influxuum, compositionum et mensurarum, cuius identitas a configuratione pendet.","refs":["CL_MHON_07:MME-02","CL_MHON_07:GA-05"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[]},{"id":"MC_MHON_L1_03:MME-03","text":"Geometria et hieroglyphum reclassificantur ex repraesentationibus in grammaticam transductionis quae materiam, operationem et cosmologiam coordinat.","refs":["CL_MHON_07:FGS-B02","CL_MHON_09:BSD-01"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[]},{"id":"MC_MHON_L1_03:MME-04","text":"Oppositio unitatis et differentiae corruit, quia unitas metagregalis non ante distinctionem sed per conservationem relationis inter partes constat.","refs":["CL_MHON_08:GA-01","CL_MHON_08:MME-02"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[]},{"id":"MC_MHON_L1_03:MME-05","text":"Oppositio naturae et artis recodificatur: ars naturam interne ordinat et imitatur, sed eius plenitudinem causaliter non usurpat.","refs":["CL_MHON_07:MME-03","CL_MHON_08:DBN-03"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[]},{"id":"MC_MHON_L1_03:MME-06","text":"Destructio, subtractio et limes reclassificantur ut modi productionis differentialis, non ut purae privationes.","refs":["CL_MHON_07:BSD-05","CL_MHON_08:GA-04","CL_MHON_09:XD-04"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[]},{"id":"MC_MHON_L1_03:MME-07","text":"Numerus reclassificatur ex enumeratione in matricem qualitativam quae permutationem, metamorphosin, tempus et completionem ordinat.","refs":["CL_MHON_07:MP-03","CL_MHON_09:MME-02"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[]},{"id":"MC_MHON_L1_03:MME-08","text":"Lectio reclassificatur ex visu in participationem disciplinatam, ubi effectus, apparatus, mensura et initiatio simul auctoritatem constituunt.","refs":["CL_MHON_08:CGD-05","CL_MHON_09:MME-04"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[]}],"original_name":"Macro Map-Effects"},{"number":"14","name":"Altitude Integration","items":[{"id":"MC_MHON_L1_03:ALT-B01","text":"[Gradus superficiei] Corpora caelestia, ovum, animalia, litterae, vasa, puncta, lineae, crux, quaternarius et XXIV praebent obiecta immediata per quae relationes systematis manifestantur.","refs":["CL_MHON_07:ALT-B01","CL_MHON_08:ALT-B01","CL_MHON_09:ALT-B01"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[]},{"id":"MC_MHON_L1_03:ALT-B02","text":"[Gradus structuralis] Influxus, proportio, dissectio, metathesis, commensuratio et circulatio constituunt machinam qua heterogeneitas in differentiam ordinatam et reditum convertitur.","refs":["CL_MHON_07:ALT-B02","CL_MHON_08:ALT-B02","CL_MHON_09:ALT-B02"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[]},{"id":"MC_MHON_L1_03:ALT-B03","text":"[Gradus metastrategicus] Interpretatio fit regimen accessus et interventionis: lector debet distinguere invarians a contingenti, potentiam ab essentia et operationem legitimam ab analogia nuda.","refs":["CL_MHON_07:ALT-B03","CL_MHON_08:ALT-B03","CL_MHON_09:ALT-B03"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[]},{"id":"MC_MHON_L1_03:ALT-B04","text":"[Gradus ontologicus / temporalis] Realitas constat ex entibus dependentibus, finite mobilibus et recursive relatis; tempus est effusio, transformatio et reditus, non linea infinita.","refs":["CL_MHON_07:ALT-B04","CL_MHON_08:ALT-B04","CL_MHON_09:HSI-03"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[]},{"id":"MC_MHON_L1_03:ALT-B05","text":"[Gradus methodologicus / performativus] Forma operis thesim suam efficit, quia ipsa inter fabulam, materiam, geometriam, numerum et auctoritatem transit atque doctrinam in signo consignato stabilit.","refs":["CL_MHON_07:ALT-B05","CL_MHON_08:ALT-B05","CL_MHON_09:ALT-B05"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[]}],"original_name":"Altitude Integration"},{"number":"15","name":"Internal Tensions, Mutation Points, and Fault Lines","items":[{"id":"MC_MHON_L1_03:FL-01","text":"Translatio ab analogia hieroglyphica ad causalitatem materialem et auctoritatem cosmicam necessaria ponitur, sed regula formalis communis diversis probationis generibus deest.","refs":["CL_MHON_07:FL-01","CL_MHON_09:FL-02"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"Tension","passages":[]},{"id":"MC_MHON_L1_03:FL-02","text":"Autarkia Monae, dependentia operationis ab ambitu et subordinatio ordinis creati voluntati divinae tres sensus exterioritatis producunt qui non plene conciliantur.","refs":["CL_MHON_07:FL-04","CL_MHON_08:FL-02","CL_MHON_09:GA-08"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"Tension","passages":[]},{"id":"MC_MHON_L1_03:FL-03","text":"Ars efficaciam realem et aequalitatem ordinatam producit, sed terminus inter reconfigurationem validam et perfectionem naturalem acquisitam instabilis manet.","refs":["CL_MHON_08:FL-01","CL_MHON_09:GNX-01","CL_MHON_09:BSD-04"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"Tension","passages":[]},{"id":"MC_MHON_L1_03:FL-04","text":"Metathesis quaternaria et metamorphosis per XXIV iuunguntur ut scalae eiusdem transformationis, quamquam nexus operativus inter permutationem geometricam et completionem cosmicam incompletus est.","refs":["CL_MHON_09:RMD-05","CL_MHON_09:XD-05","CL_MHON_09:FL-03"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"Tension","passages":[]},{"id":"MC_MHON_L1_03:FL-05","text":"Occultatio initiatoria et consignatio transmissibilis simul necessariae sunt, sed criterium quo disciplina communicatur sine revelatione aequaliter publica non plene declaratur.","refs":["CL_MHON_08:GNX-04","CL_MHON_09:AS-B07"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"Tension","passages":[]},{"id":"MC_MHON_L1_03:MP-01","text":"Punctum mutatur ex copula necessaria in superfluitatem removendam, deinde in centrum principiale effusionis circularis.","refs":["CL_MHON_07:RMD-05","CL_MHON_09:RLE-01"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"Tension","passages":[]},{"id":"MC_MHON_L1_03:MP-02","text":"Circulus mutatur ex figura caelesti in motum artis et tandem in formam clausurae quae principium tamquam completionem restituit.","refs":["CL_MHON_07:MP-01","CL_MHON_09:RLE-01"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"Composite","passages":[]},{"id":"MC_MHON_L1_03:MP-03","text":"Dissectio mutatur ex anatomia interpretativa in apparatum qui vasa, munera et novas relationes componit.","refs":["CL_MHON_08:MP-01"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"Composite","passages":[]},{"id":"MC_MHON_L1_03:MP-04","text":"Signum mutatur ex figura recognoscenda in operationem materialiter condicionatam et deinde in consignationem disciplinarem.","refs":["CL_MHON_08:GA-06","CL_MHON_09:GA-07"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"Composite","passages":[]},{"id":"MC_MHON_L1_03:MP-05","text":"Numerus mutatur ex discrimine qualitativo configurationis in operator structuram, tempus et metamorphosin ordinantem.","refs":["CL_MHON_07:MP-03","CL_MHON_09:GA-03","CL_MHON_09:MP-02"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"Composite","passages":[]},{"id":"MC_MHON_L1_03:MP-06","text":"Limes mutatur ex cessatione potentiae creatae in clausuram circularis reditus sub ordine divino.","refs":["CL_MHON_09:XD-04","CL_MHON_09:MP-03"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"Tension","passages":[]}],"original_name":"Internal Tensions, Mutation Points, and Fault Lines"},{"number":"16","name":"Hidden System Image","items":[{"id":"MC_MHON_L1_03:HSI-01","text":"Realitas implicite est machina transductionum concentricarum: influxus distribuitur, materia configuratur, partes distinguuntur, figura mensuratur et numerus circulationem claudit.","refs":["CL_MHON_07:HSI-01","CL_MHON_09:HSI-01"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[]},{"id":"MC_MHON_L1_03:HSI-02","text":"Monas implicite est centrum quod se explicat in differentias sine sui dispersione et eas per proportionem ad relationem communem retrahit.","refs":["CL_MHON_08:HSI-01","CL_MHON_07:RLE-05"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[]},{"id":"MC_MHON_L1_03:HSI-03","text":"Subiectum implicite est initiatus artifex-interpres qui neque exterius contemplatur neque absolute dominatur, sed mensuras, tempora et apparatus administrat.","refs":["CL_MHON_08:HSI-02","CL_MHON_09:HSI-02"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[]},{"id":"MC_MHON_L1_03:HSI-04","text":"Tempus implicite est spiralis effusionis, operationis, latitationis et reditus, cuius completiones finitae iterationem non identicam admittunt.","refs":["CL_MHON_07:HSI-03","CL_MHON_08:HSI-03","CL_MHON_09:HSI-03"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[]},{"id":"MC_MHON_L1_03:HSI-05","text":"Navigatio possibilis non est fuga ab ordine, sed electio proportionata inter disruptionem, compositionem, resectionem et reditum intra limites causalitatis creatae.","refs":["CL_MHON_07:HSI-04","CL_MHON_07:BSD-04","CL_MHON_09:BSA-01"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[]}],"original_name":"Hidden System Image"},{"number":"17","name":"Minimal Whole-Book Reconstruction Test","items":[{"id":"MC_MHON_L1_03:MRT-B01","text":"Axiomata proportionis, configurationis, differentiae generativae, artis derivatae, signi operativi, numeri transformativi, limitis creati et inexhaustionis sufficiunt ad machinam metagregalem restituendam.","refs":["CL_MHON_07:MRT-B01","CL_MHON_08:MRT-B01","CL_MHON_09:MRT-B01"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[]},{"id":"MC_MHON_L1_03:MRT-B02","text":"Structura firmissima est ubi circulatio, dissectio et commensuratio eandem legem servant: mutatio relationem communem non delet, sed eam alio gradu manifestat.","refs":["CL_MHON_07:MRT-B02","CL_MHON_08:MRT-B02","CL_MHON_09:MRT-B02"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[]},{"id":"MC_MHON_L1_03:MRT-B03","text":"Insufficienter determinatae manent conversio influxus in corpus, discrimen exactum efficaciae artificialis a perfectione naturali et transitus a geometria ad cosmologiam atque apocalypsim.","refs":["CL_MHON_07:MRT-B03","CL_MHON_08:MRT-B03","CL_MHON_09:MRT-B03"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[]},{"id":"MC_MHON_L1_03:MRT-B04","text":"Restitutio cohaeret ut matrix recursiva translationis, non ut demonstratio univoca; motum verum servat si clausuram et inexhaustionem, proportionem et mutationem, autarkiam et dependentiam simul retinet.","refs":["CL_MHON_07:MRT-B04","CL_MHON_08:ABS-07","CL_MHON_09:AS-B06"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"Tension","passages":[]}],"original_name":"Minimal Whole-Book Reconstruction Test"}],"language_code":"la","language_name":"Latina","translations":[{"id":"CAREVAR_20261006T223707604463Z_f1a6d959","language_code":"en","language_name":"English","title":"Mhon L1 03","source_sha256":"31533405b0665570c73af5b928772b02e99df9c658a18efc15cb18229dec1ab3","sha256":"3bfeece9791a081180db5306223257cace720ac7db596d91f542a9c97f4b8722","rendition_type":"machine","review_status":"machine","model":"gpt-5.6-terra","created":"2026-10-06T22:37:07.606421+00:00","sections":[{"number":"1","name":"Book-Scale Argumental Spine","items":[{"id":"MC_MHON_L1_03:AS-01","text":"The central question is how the Monad comprehends celestial, material, figural, and numerical orders in one machine, while sustaining their internal division and commensuration and not abolishing their differences.","refs":["CL_MHON_07:BAS-B01","CL_MHON_08:ABS-01","CL_MHON_09:AS-B01"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[]},{"id":"MC_MHON_L1_03:AS-02","text":"The metagregal thesis establishes the Monad not as a simply one thing, but as a relational structure that distinguishes itself, reorders parts, and gathers origin, process, and end in the same sign.","refs":["CL_MHON_07:BAS-B03","CL_MHON_08:MME-01","CL_MHON_09:MME-01"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[]},{"id":"MC_MHON_L1_03:AS-03","text":"The argument proceeds transductively, hieroglyphic-operatively, and numerological-metaphysically: analogy joins fields, operation conditions efficacy, geometry separates invariants, number orders change.","refs":["CL_MHON_07:BAS-B06","CL_MHON_08:ABS-05","CL_MHON_09:AS-B03"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[]},{"id":"MC_MHON_L1_03:AS-04","text":"The direction of the system proceeds from influx into body, from body into dissection and recomposition, from recomposition into inscribed measure, and thence into return to divine order.","refs":["CL_MHON_07:BAS-B07","CL_MHON_08:AFM-B06","CL_MHON_09:AFM-B04"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[]},{"id":"MC_MHON_L1_03:AS-05","text":"The true movement is spiral and activated by change: difference generates operation, operation demands a new distinction, measure is amplified into number, and the end is converted into the principle of further ordering.","refs":["CL_MHON_08:ABS-06","CL_MHON_09:AS-B05","CL_MHON_07:RLE-04"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[]},{"id":"MC_MHON_L1_03:AS-06","text":"Closure is at once internal, derived, and inexhausted: the Monad preserves its own proportion, but its completion cannot be separated either from operation, from the creator's will, or from further extraction of mystery.","refs":["CL_MHON_07:BAS-B08","CL_MHON_08:ABS-07","CL_MHON_09:AS-B06"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[]}],"original_name":"Book-Scale Argumental Spine"},{"number":"2","name":"Global Load-Bearing Axioms","items":[{"id":"MC_MHON_L1_03:GA-01","text":"[Attractor] The Monad is a relational center that gathers translations, internal differences, and cosmic order without reducing them to simple identity.","refs":["CL_MHON_07:GA-01","CL_MHON_08:MME-01","CL_MHON_09:MME-01"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[]},{"id":"MC_MHON_L1_03:GA-02","text":"[Persistent] Proportion, or invisible relation, is the criterion by which composition, figure, transformation, and closure are validated.","refs":["CL_MHON_07:GA-04","CL_MHON_09:GA-02","CL_MHON_09:GA-09"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[]},{"id":"MC_MHON_L1_03:GA-03","text":"[Mutating] The force of an element is not an invariable property, but a function of position, connection, and configuration; metathesis therefore does not necessarily change a thing substantially, but transposes its function.","refs":["CL_MHON_07:GA-05","CL_MHON_08:GA-02","CL_MHON_08:CGD-02"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[]},{"id":"MC_MHON_L1_03:GA-04","text":"[Emergent] Differentiation does not corrupt unity, but produces a repertoire of parts and relations from which reunion and further composition arise.","refs":["CL_MHON_08:GA-01","CL_MHON_08:GA-04","CL_MHON_08:MME-02"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[]},{"id":"MC_MHON_L1_03:GA-05","text":"[Persistent] Translation among heaven, matter, figure, number, and mystery requires mediation; similarity alone establishes neither identity nor causal necessity.","refs":["CL_MHON_07:DBN-04","CL_MHON_08:GA-03","CL_MHON_09:BSD-01"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[]},{"id":"MC_MHON_L1_03:GA-06","text":"[Mutating] Art is a real but derived power: it orders given matter and incomplete powers, and does not produce natural perfection or its own foundation from nothing.","refs":["CL_MHON_07:XD-06","CL_MHON_08:GA-05","CL_MHON_09:BSD-04"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[]},{"id":"MC_MHON_L1_03:GA-07","text":"[Mutating] The sign passes from hieroglyphic index to material condenser, instrument of operation, and transmissible act of inscription.","refs":["CL_MHON_07:BAS-B04","CL_MHON_08:GA-06","CL_MHON_09:GA-07"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[]},{"id":"MC_MHON_L1_03:GA-08","text":"[Persistent] Valid knowledge requires congruence of interpretation, effect, operation, and the disposition of the interpreter; immediate or vulgar sight does not suffice.","refs":["CL_MHON_07:GA-09","CL_MHON_08:GA-07","CL_MHON_09:GA-10"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[]},{"id":"MC_MHON_L1_03:GA-09","text":"[Emergent] Dissolution, subtraction, and termination are generative operations when they preserve the relation of origin and produce a new distinction or circulation.","refs":["CL_MHON_07:GA-07","CL_MHON_08:GA-04","CL_MHON_09:GA-05"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[]},{"id":"MC_MHON_L1_03:GA-10","text":"[Persistent] Corporeality and created causality are real but dependent: each is ordered by a higher distribution, measure, and limit.","refs":["CL_MHON_07:GA-06","CL_MHON_09:GA-04","CL_MHON_09:BSA-01"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[]},{"id":"MC_MHON_L1_03:GA-11","text":"[Mutating] The circle changes from a celestial figure into artificial movement, a circuit of difference, and a cosmic form of return to principle.","refs":["CL_MHON_07:RMD-02","CL_MHON_08:RMD-01","CL_MHON_09:RLE-01"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[]},{"id":"MC_MHON_L1_03:GA-12","text":"[Emergent] Number is a qualitative operator that determines the functions of configuration, permutation, and metamorphosis, not bare quantity.","refs":["CL_MHON_07:MP-03","CL_MHON_09:GA-03","CL_MHON_09:XD-02"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[]},{"id":"MC_MHON_L1_03:GA-13","text":"[Terminal] Valid closure is a finite return to proper proportion and to the creator's will, not indefinite created progress.","refs":["CL_MHON_07:GA-08","CL_MHON_09:GNX-04","CL_MHON_09:GA-08"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[]},{"id":"MC_MHON_L1_03:GA-14","text":"[Attractor] Inexhaustion and hidden relation prevent structural closure from being converted into interpretive saturation.","refs":["CL_MHON_08:GA-08","CL_MHON_09:GA-09","CL_MHON_09:RMD-04"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[]}],"original_name":"Global Load-Bearing Axioms"},{"number":"3","name":"Recurrence, Mutation, and Displacement Matrix","items":[{"id":"MC_MHON_L1_03:RMD-01","text":"[Absorption] The Monad is amplified from a center of translations into an internal machine of differences and finally into an ontological table of origin, process, and end.","refs":["CL_MHON_07:RMD-07","CL_MHON_08:MME-01","CL_MHON_09:MME-01"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[]},{"id":"MC_MHON_L1_03:RMD-02","text":"[Stable identity] Proportion persists as a rule of judgment, but its scope extends from material composition to a universal invariant of demonstrable order.","refs":["CL_MHON_07:GA-04","CL_MHON_09:RMD-01"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[]},{"id":"MC_MHON_L1_03:RMD-03","text":"[Functional mutation] Circulation passes from celestial course to artificial transformation, thence to the recursion of distinction, and finally to cosmic completion.","refs":["CL_MHON_07:RMD-02","CL_MHON_08:RMD-01","CL_MHON_09:RLE-01"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[]},{"id":"MC_MHON_L1_03:RMD-04","text":"[Terminal transformation] Destruction and dissolution lose terminal force: first they effect material distinction, then an inventory for recomposition, and finally a circuit of completion.","refs":["CL_MHON_07:RMD-06","CL_MHON_08:GA-04","CL_MHON_09:DBN-03"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[]},{"id":"MC_MHON_L1_03:RMD-05","text":"[Inversion] The point is inverted from necessary junction into confusing superfluity, then restored as a center of effusion and return.","refs":["CL_MHON_07:RMD-05","CL_MHON_09:RLE-01"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[]},{"id":"MC_MHON_L1_03:RMD-06","text":"[Amplification] The quaternity moves from the discrimination of local configuration to a structure of ordering and is amplified through XXIV into temporal and cosmic metamorphosis.","refs":["CL_MHON_07:RMD-05","CL_MHON_09:GA-03","CL_MHON_09:RMD-02"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[]},{"id":"MC_MHON_L1_03:RMD-07","text":"[Functional mutation] Art progresses from motion imitating nature to the translation of instruments and finally to created power, real but limited.","refs":["CL_MHON_07:MME-03","CL_MHON_08:RMD-03","CL_MHON_09:BSD-04"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[]},{"id":"MC_MHON_L1_03:RMD-08","text":"[Tension] Exteriority is first excluded as a supplement to the Monad, then returns as the sphere of material efficacy, and finally is received as a higher relation to the creator.","refs":["CL_MHON_07:GNX-05","CL_MHON_08:XD-03","CL_MHON_09:GA-08"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[]},{"id":"MC_MHON_L1_03:RMD-09","text":"[Functional return] Mystery changes from a hidden residue into a rule of recurrent extraction and then into an inscribable but unexhausted form.","refs":["CL_MHON_08:RMD-05","CL_MHON_09:GA-07"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[]}],"original_name":"Recurrence, Mutation, and Displacement Matrix"},{"number":"4","name":"Lower-Layer vs Current-Level Distinction","items":[{"id":"MC_MHON_L1_03:CGD-01","text":"The eagle, scarab, egg, and their correspondences remain local machines of metamorphosis; only the structure of conflict, composition, and absorption rises to the metagregal level.","refs":["CL_MHON_07:CGD-01","CL_MHON_07:CGD-02"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[]},{"id":"MC_MHON_L1_03:CGD-02","text":"Letters, members, and vessels remain local notations; what is transferred upward is the metathesis by which the same elements assume different functions.","refs":["CL_MHON_08:CGD-01","CL_MHON_08:CGD-02"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[]},{"id":"MC_MHON_L1_03:CGD-03","text":"Measures AB, relations of lines, and apocalyptic images remain local calibration apparatuses; the structure of the present level retains the distinction of invariants from contingents and the connection of completion with time.","refs":["CL_MHON_09:CGD-01","CL_MHON_09:CGD-02"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[]},{"id":"MC_MHON_L1_03:CGD-04","text":"Particular materials and environmental conditions are not universalized, but their function is amplified into the rule that efficacy is always conditioned by composition and physical situation.","refs":["CL_MHON_07:CGD-02","CL_MHON_08:CGD-03"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[]},{"id":"MC_MHON_L1_03:CGD-05","text":"The properly metagregal structure arises where celestial distribution, monadic dissection, and geometric commensuration are coordinated as three modes of the same proportional ordering.","refs":["CL_MHON_07:CGD-06","CL_MHON_08:CGD-04","CL_MHON_09:ALT-B02"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[]},{"id":"MC_MHON_L1_03:CGD-06","text":"Visual recognition, bare arithmetic, and isolated correspondence are surpassed; they are valid only if inserted into operation, transformation, and ordered understanding.","refs":["CL_MHON_08:CGD-05","CL_MHON_09:CGD-03","CL_MHON_09:MME-04"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[]},{"id":"MC_MHON_L1_03:CGD-07","text":"The major transition of the present level proceeds from distributed causality through the internal machine of differences to a geometric grammar between fields.","refs":["CL_MHON_07:BSD-02","CL_MHON_08:MME-01","CL_MHON_09:BSD-01"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[]}],"original_name":"Lower-Layer vs Current-Level Distinction"},{"number":"5","name":"Global Apophatic Structure","items":[{"id":"MC_MHON_L1_03:GNX-01","text":"The system excludes autonomous matter and absolute created causality; body and agent remain within distribution, measure, and higher relation.","refs":["CL_MHON_07:GNX-01","CL_MHON_09:BSA-01"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"Negative","passages":[]},{"id":"MC_MHON_L1_03:GNX-02","text":"The system excludes ornamental or neutral sign, because figure must bear a relation, a condition of operation, or an act of inscription.","refs":["CL_MHON_07:DBN-01","CL_MHON_08:DBN-02"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"Negative","passages":[]},{"id":"MC_MHON_L1_03:GNX-03","text":"The system excludes immediate identity of figure, essence, and operation; similarity and efficacy do not of themselves confer natural perfection.","refs":["CL_MHON_08:GNX-01","CL_MHON_08:BSA-02"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"Negative","passages":[]},{"id":"MC_MHON_L1_03:GNX-04","text":"The system excludes a force of elements independent of every configuration; position and connection constitute what an element can do.","refs":["CL_MHON_07:DBN-02","CL_MHON_07:GA-05"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"Negative","passages":[]},{"id":"MC_MHON_L1_03:GNX-05","text":"The system excludes art that produces natural essence from nothing or provides itself with the foundation of causality.","refs":["CL_MHON_08:DBN-03","CL_MHON_09:BSD-04"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"Negative","passages":[]},{"id":"MC_MHON_L1_03:GNX-06","text":"The system excludes immobile and undivided unity, because monadic unity operates only through preserved difference and reunion.","refs":["CL_MHON_08:BSA-03","CL_MHON_08:GA-01"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"Negative","passages":[]},{"id":"MC_MHON_L1_03:GNX-07","text":"The system excludes both infinite created progress and inert closure; a legitimate end produces finite circulation and return.","refs":["CL_MHON_09:GNX-04","CL_MHON_09:DBN-03"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"Negative","passages":[]},{"id":"MC_MHON_L1_03:GNX-08","text":"The system excludes universally public, visual, or exhaustive knowledge; access, operation, and symbolic understanding are constitutive conditions.","refs":["CL_MHON_08:GNX-04","CL_MHON_09:DBN-04"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"Negative","passages":[]}],"original_name":"Global Apophatic Structure"},{"number":"6","name":"Deep Book Apophatics","items":[{"id":"MC_MHON_L1_03:DBN-01","text":"It must be false that heterogeneous orders can be converted without a mediator; without proportion or a rule of translation, the unity of the system would dissolve into disconnected analogies.","refs":["CL_MHON_07:DBN-04","CL_MHON_09:BSA-04"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"Negative","passages":[]},{"id":"MC_MHON_L1_03:DBN-02","text":"It must be false that distinction turns parts into autonomous substances; otherwise, dissection would lose the relation of common origin and the capacity for reunion.","refs":["CL_MHON_08:DBN-01","CL_MHON_08:GA-01"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"Negative","passages":[]},{"id":"MC_MHON_L1_03:DBN-03","text":"It must be false that artificial effect and natural perfection coincide; otherwise, derived art would be converted into absolute causality.","refs":["CL_MHON_08:DBN-03","CL_MHON_09:BSD-04"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"Negative","passages":[]},{"id":"MC_MHON_L1_03:DBN-04","text":"The distinction among operational supplement, environmental condition, and ontological principle is necessary; without it, the autarky of the Monad and created dependence would fall into simple contradiction.","refs":["CL_MHON_08:FL-02","CL_MHON_09:GA-08"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"Tension","passages":[]},{"id":"MC_MHON_L1_03:DBN-05","text":"It must be false that completion exhausts meaning; otherwise, the inscription of mystery would close rather than transmit a series of interpretations.","refs":["CL_MHON_08:GA-08","CL_MHON_09:GA-07"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"Negative","passages":[]},{"id":"MC_MHON_L1_03:DBN-06","text":"It must be false that every diminution is mere privation; purification, dissolution, and limit hold only if ordered absence produces something.","refs":["CL_MHON_07:DBN-03","CL_MHON_09:DBN-03"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"Negative","passages":[]}],"original_name":"Deep Book Apophatics"},{"number":"7","name":"Super-Apophatics of the Whole","items":[{"id":"MC_MHON_L1_03:BSA-01","text":"The system revolves around a universal law of conversion among hieroglyphic analogy, material causality, geometry, and cosmic authority, but nowhere has such a law fully available.","refs":["CL_MHON_07:FL-02","CL_MHON_09:BSA-04"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"Tension","passages":[]},{"id":"MC_MHON_L1_03:BSA-02","text":"The possibility of a creature conferring upon itself origin, measure, and infinite continuation is ontologically inadmissible.","refs":["CL_MHON_09:BSA-01","CL_MHON_09:GNX-04"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"Negative","passages":[]},{"id":"MC_MHON_L1_03:BSA-03","text":"A totally extrinsic perspective, possessing the whole Monad without disposition or initiation, remains unavailable.","refs":["CL_MHON_08:BSA-01","CL_MHON_09:GA-10"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"Negative","passages":[]},{"id":"MC_MHON_L1_03:BSA-04","text":"An exterior principle to the Monad as a supplement of perfection is prohibited, but the creator is retained as ultimate ontological ground; the absence of an explicit distinction between these senses of exteriority orders the system.","refs":["CL_MHON_07:BSA-01","CL_MHON_09:GA-08"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"Tension","passages":[]},{"id":"MC_MHON_L1_03:BSA-05","text":"A completely transparent terminal interpretation is impossible: every valid return restores mystery rather than consumes it.","refs":["CL_MHON_08:GA-08","CL_MHON_08:AFM-B05"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"Negative","passages":[]}],"original_name":"Super-Apophatics of the Whole"},{"number":"8","name":"Cross-Unit Derivative Propositions","items":[{"id":"MC_MHON_L1_03:XD-01","text":"Transscalar efficacy arises when a proportional invariant passes through mutable configurations: the same principle preserves order while its functions change in matter, figure, and number.","refs":["MC_MHON_L1_03:GA-02","MC_MHON_L1_03:GA-03","MC_MHON_L1_03:GA-05","CL_MHON_07:GA-05","CL_MHON_08:GA-02","CL_MHON_09:GA-02"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"Composite","passages":[]},{"id":"MC_MHON_L1_03:XD-02","text":"Material subtraction, monadic dissection, and the limit of created causality constitute one generative negative structure: rightly ordered negation produces difference, repertoire, and return.","refs":["MC_MHON_L1_03:GA-04","MC_MHON_L1_03:GA-09","MC_MHON_L1_03:GA-13","CL_MHON_07:XD-03","CL_MHON_08:GA-04","CL_MHON_09:XD-04"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"Composite","passages":[]},{"id":"MC_MHON_L1_03:XD-03","text":"Effective art neither opposes nature nor coincides with it: it reconfigures received powers, but does not confer upon itself the measure and causality by which it operates.","refs":["MC_MHON_L1_03:GA-06","MC_MHON_L1_03:GA-10","CL_MHON_07:XD-06","CL_MHON_08:GA-05","CL_MHON_09:BSD-04"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"Composite","passages":[]},{"id":"MC_MHON_L1_03:XD-04","text":"The sign becomes a complete apparatus where it translates celestial relation, condenses material condition, and inscribes numerical order; signification and operation cannot there be separated.","refs":["MC_MHON_L1_03:GA-07","MC_MHON_L1_03:GA-08","CL_MHON_07:XD-01","CL_MHON_08:GA-06","CL_MHON_09:GA-07"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"Composite","passages":[]},{"id":"MC_MHON_L1_03:XD-05","text":"The circle is an operator of scale: it converts celestial descent into material metamorphosis, recursion of difference, and finite return to principle.","refs":["MC_MHON_L1_03:GA-11","MC_MHON_L1_03:GA-13","CL_MHON_07:RMD-02","CL_MHON_08:RLE-01","CL_MHON_09:RLE-01"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"Composite","passages":[]},{"id":"MC_MHON_L1_03:XD-06","text":"Number formalizes the quality of configuration: the discrimination of the local point is ordered through the quaternity and amplified in XXIV as the measure of cosmic change.","refs":["MC_MHON_L1_03:GA-03","MC_MHON_L1_03:GA-12","CL_MHON_07:MP-03","CL_MHON_09:GA-03","CL_MHON_09:XD-02"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"Composite","passages":[]},{"id":"MC_MHON_L1_03:XD-07","text":"The proof of this system is not simple exterior deduction, but congruence among effect, initiated operation, and an understanding that discerns invisible structure on the surface.","refs":["MC_MHON_L1_03:GA-08","MC_MHON_L1_03:GA-14","CL_MHON_07:XD-05","CL_MHON_08:XD-05","CL_MHON_09:GA-10"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"Composite","passages":[]},{"id":"MC_MHON_L1_03:XD-08","text":"Monadic autarky is only partially established if exteriority is divided into illegitimate supplement, environmental condition, and ontological cause; the sources require the distinction but do not formally complete it.","refs":["MC_MHON_L1_03:GA-06","MC_MHON_L1_03:GA-10","MC_MHON_L1_03:GA-13","CL_MHON_07:GNX-05","CL_MHON_08:XD-03","CL_MHON_09:GA-08"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"Tension","passages":[]},{"id":"MC_MHON_L1_03:XD-09","text":"Closure and inexhaustion are not simple opposites: restoration completes structure, but mystery reopens the course of interpretation and operation.","refs":["MC_MHON_L1_03:GA-13","MC_MHON_L1_03:GA-14","CL_MHON_07:GA-08","CL_MHON_08:GA-08","CL_MHON_09:CGD-05"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"Tension","passages":[]}],"original_name":"Cross-Unit Derivative Propositions"},{"number":"9","name":"Book-Scale Super-Derivatives","items":[{"id":"MC_MHON_L1_03:BSD-01","text":"Reality becomes a field of transductive relations in which matter, figure, and number are different states of the same machine of ordering, not independent substances.","refs":["CL_MHON_07:BSD-01","CL_MHON_08:BSD-01","CL_MHON_09:HSI-01"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[]},{"id":"MC_MHON_L1_03:BSD-02","text":"Causality is distributed and asymmetrical: higher things sustain, art reconfigures, measure limits, but no created agent appropriates the whole series to itself.","refs":["CL_MHON_07:BSD-02","CL_MHON_09:BSA-01","CL_MHON_09:BSD-04"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[]},{"id":"MC_MHON_L1_03:BSD-03","text":"Time is a spiral repetition of power, operation, correction, and return, not indefinite succession nor an identically repeated circle.","refs":["CL_MHON_07:BSD-03","CL_MHON_08:BSD-03","CL_MHON_09:BSD-02"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[]},{"id":"MC_MHON_L1_03:BSD-04","text":"The subject becomes an initiated artisan-interpreter: neither autonomous spectator nor causal master, but administrator of times, instruments, signs, and limits.","refs":["CL_MHON_07:HSI-02","CL_MHON_08:HSI-02","CL_MHON_09:HSI-02"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[]},{"id":"MC_MHON_L1_03:BSD-05","text":"Truth is constituted by concordance among invariant relation, material apparatus, correct operation, and observable effect; none of these conditions alone suffices.","refs":["CL_MHON_07:BSD-06","CL_MHON_08:BSD-02","CL_MHON_09:GA-02"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[]},{"id":"MC_MHON_L1_03:BSD-06","text":"Epistemic authority is distributed through degrees of access, but the inscribed figure makes the same discipline transmissible; knowledge is simultaneously restricted and propagated.","refs":["CL_MHON_08:ABS-08","CL_MHON_08:MME-04","CL_MHON_09:AS-B07"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[]},{"id":"MC_MHON_L1_03:BSD-07","text":"The history of the system tends not toward cumulatively possessed fullness, but toward local closures whose end opens a new extraction under measure.","refs":["CL_MHON_07:RLE-04","CL_MHON_08:GA-08","CL_MHON_09:GNX-04"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[]},{"id":"MC_MHON_L1_03:BSD-08","text":"The power to act consists in choice, composition, and resection under received measures; the operator's freedom is configurative, not absolute.","refs":["CL_MHON_07:BSD-04","CL_MHON_08:BSD-04","CL_MHON_09:BSD-03"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[]}],"original_name":"Book-Scale Super-Derivatives"},{"number":"10","name":"Recursive Loops, Escalation Paths, and Conceptual Attractors","items":[{"id":"MC_MHON_L1_03:RLE-01","text":"Celestial influx → bodily effect → analysis of effect → confirmation of causal order constitutes the circuit by which the posited cause returns to itself through interpreted matter.","refs":["CL_MHON_07:RLE-02"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[]},{"id":"MC_MHON_L1_03:RLE-02","text":"Dissection → notation → metathesis → operation → recognition of common origin → new dissection constitutes the Monad's internal recursion.","refs":["CL_MHON_08:RLE-01"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[]},{"id":"MC_MHON_L1_03:RLE-03","text":"Inequality → ordering art → measure → number → cosmic time → divine will constitutes a path of amplification from matter to cosmology.","refs":["CL_MHON_09:AS-B05","CL_MHON_09:RLE-02"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[]},{"id":"MC_MHON_L1_03:RLE-04","text":"Disruption → absorption → purification → reunion without exhaustion causes every restoration to become the beginning of a new difference.","refs":["CL_MHON_07:RLE-04","CL_MHON_08:FGS-B05"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[]},{"id":"MC_MHON_L1_03:RLE-05","text":"Sign → apparatus → correct operation → apodixis → transmissible inscription → new interpretation constitutes an epistemic circuit.","refs":["CL_MHON_08:XD-05","CL_MHON_09:GA-07"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[]},{"id":"MC_MHON_L1_03:RLE-06","text":"Invisible proportion is a distributed attractor that reduces changes of position, matter, and number to a common discrimination of necessity and contingency.","refs":["CL_MHON_07:RLE-05","CL_MHON_09:RLE-04"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[]},{"id":"MC_MHON_L1_03:RLE-07","text":"Two attractors simultaneously govern the system: monadic union draws back differences, while inexhaustion prevents the attractor from becoming full possession.","refs":["CL_MHON_08:RLE-04","CL_MHON_08:GA-08"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[]},{"id":"MC_MHON_L1_03:RLE-08","text":"The amplification of the quaternity into XXIV is at once an attractor of completion and a fragile joint, because it promises cosmic change without a perfected rule of geometric conversion.","refs":["CL_MHON_09:RLE-05","CL_MHON_09:XD-05"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[]}],"original_name":"Recursive Loops, Escalation Paths, and Conceptual Attractors"},{"number":"11","name":"Formal Generative Schema of the Whole Book","items":[{"id":"MC_MHON_L1_03:FGS-B01","text":"Celestial and elemental heterogeneity → asymmetrical distribution of forces → dependent body and unequal nature.","refs":["CL_MHON_07:GA-06","CL_MHON_09:GA-01"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[]},{"id":"MC_MHON_L1_03:FGS-B02","text":"Invisible relation → proportion → invariant measure → distinction of necessity from contingency.","refs":["CL_MHON_07:GA-04","CL_MHON_09:GA-02"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[]},{"id":"MC_MHON_L1_03:FGS-B03","text":"Composite unity → dissection and notation → related parts → repertoire of recomposition.","refs":["CL_MHON_08:GA-04","CL_MHON_08:CGD-04"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[]},{"id":"MC_MHON_L1_03:FGS-B04","text":"Configured part → change of position → functional metathesis → translation among figure, vessel, and instrument.","refs":["CL_MHON_07:GA-05","CL_MHON_08:FGS-B03"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[]},{"id":"MC_MHON_L1_03:FGS-B05","text":"Given power → ordering art → materially conditioned operation → real effect without absolute natural perfection.","refs":["CL_MHON_07:XD-06","CL_MHON_08:BSD-04"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[]},{"id":"MC_MHON_L1_03:FGS-B06","text":"Quaternary configuration → permutation → transformative number → temporal and cosmic amplification through XXIV.","refs":["CL_MHON_09:GA-03","CL_MHON_09:RMD-02"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[]},{"id":"MC_MHON_L1_03:FGS-B07","text":"Created power → finite exercise → diminution of impulse → circular closure → return to higher order.","refs":["CL_MHON_09:GA-04","CL_MHON_09:CGD-05"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[]},{"id":"MC_MHON_L1_03:FGS-B08","text":"Correct operation → restoration of union → inscription of mystery → structured closure without interpretive exhaustion.","refs":["CL_MHON_08:FGS-B05","CL_MHON_09:GA-08"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[]}],"original_name":"Formal Generative Schema of the Whole Book"},{"number":"12","name":"Whole-Book Argument Flow Map","items":[{"id":"MC_MHON_L1_03:AFM-B01","text":"Celestial influx → material composition → monadic dissection → operative metathesis → geometric commensuration → transformative number → circular closure.","refs":["CL_MHON_07:BAS-B07","CL_MHON_08:ABS-06","CL_MHON_09:AS-B04"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[]},{"id":"MC_MHON_L1_03:AFM-B02","text":"Dependent matter → artificial movement → selective dissolution → proportionate recomposition → restoration without annihilation.","refs":["CL_MHON_07:GA-07","CL_MHON_08:XD-01"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[]},{"id":"MC_MHON_L1_03:AFM-B03","text":"Hieroglyphic figure → conditioned interpretation → operative apparatus → initiated apodixis → transmissible inscription.","refs":["CL_MHON_07:BAS-B04","CL_MHON_08:XD-05","CL_MHON_09:GA-07"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[]},{"id":"MC_MHON_L1_03:AFM-B04","text":"Negation of autonomy → exclusion of immediate identity → limitation of art → termination of created causality → relation to principle.","refs":["CL_MHON_07:GNX-01","CL_MHON_08:GNX-01","CL_MHON_09:BSA-01"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[]},{"id":"MC_MHON_L1_03:AFM-B05","text":"Proper proportion → monadic union → finite completion → divine return → inexhausted mystery.","refs":["CL_MHON_07:GA-08","CL_MHON_08:AFM-B06","CL_MHON_09:GA-08"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[]}],"original_name":"Whole-Book Argument Flow Map"},{"number":"13","name":"Macro Map-Effects","items":[{"id":"MC_MHON_L1_03:MME-01","text":"The Monad is reclassified from simple unity into a transductive machine that produces, orders, and refers differences back to origin.","refs":["CL_MHON_07:GA-01","CL_MHON_08:MME-01"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[]},{"id":"MC_MHON_L1_03:MME-02","text":"The body is reclassified from passive matter into a node of influxes, compositions, and measures, whose identity depends on configuration.","refs":["CL_MHON_07:MME-02","CL_MHON_07:GA-05"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[]},{"id":"MC_MHON_L1_03:MME-03","text":"Geometry and hieroglyph are reclassified from representations into a grammar of transduction coordinating matter, operation, and cosmology.","refs":["CL_MHON_07:FGS-B02","CL_MHON_09:BSD-01"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[]},{"id":"MC_MHON_L1_03:MME-04","text":"The opposition of unity and difference collapses, because metagregal unity consists not before distinction but through preservation of relation among parts.","refs":["CL_MHON_08:GA-01","CL_MHON_08:MME-02"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[]},{"id":"MC_MHON_L1_03:MME-05","text":"The opposition of nature and art is recoded: art internally orders and imitates nature, but does not causally usurp its fullness.","refs":["CL_MHON_07:MME-03","CL_MHON_08:DBN-03"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[]},{"id":"MC_MHON_L1_03:MME-06","text":"Destruction, subtraction, and limit are reclassified as modes of differential production, not as pure privations.","refs":["CL_MHON_07:BSD-05","CL_MHON_08:GA-04","CL_MHON_09:XD-04"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[]},{"id":"MC_MHON_L1_03:MME-07","text":"Number is reclassified from enumeration into a qualitative matrix that orders permutation, metamorphosis, time, and completion.","refs":["CL_MHON_07:MP-03","CL_MHON_09:MME-02"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[]},{"id":"MC_MHON_L1_03:MME-08","text":"Reading is reclassified from sight into disciplined participation, where effect, apparatus, measure, and initiation together constitute authority.","refs":["CL_MHON_08:CGD-05","CL_MHON_09:MME-04"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[]}],"original_name":"Macro Map-Effects"},{"number":"14","name":"Altitude Integration","items":[{"id":"MC_MHON_L1_03:ALT-B01","text":"[Surface level] Celestial bodies, egg, animals, letters, vessels, points, lines, cross, quaternity, and XXIV provide the immediate objects through which the system's relations are manifested.","refs":["CL_MHON_07:ALT-B01","CL_MHON_08:ALT-B01","CL_MHON_09:ALT-B01"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[]},{"id":"MC_MHON_L1_03:ALT-B02","text":"[Structural level] Influx, proportion, dissection, metathesis, commensuration, and circulation constitute the machine by which heterogeneity is converted into ordered difference and return.","refs":["CL_MHON_07:ALT-B02","CL_MHON_08:ALT-B02","CL_MHON_09:ALT-B02"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[]},{"id":"MC_MHON_L1_03:ALT-B03","text":"[Metastrategic level] Interpretation becomes governance of access and intervention: the reader must distinguish invariant from contingent, power from essence, and legitimate operation from bare analogy.","refs":["CL_MHON_07:ALT-B03","CL_MHON_08:ALT-B03","CL_MHON_09:ALT-B03"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[]},{"id":"MC_MHON_L1_03:ALT-B04","text":"[Ontological / temporal level] Reality consists of dependent, finitely mobile, and recursively related beings; time is effusion, transformation, and return, not an infinite line.","refs":["CL_MHON_07:ALT-B04","CL_MHON_08:ALT-B04","CL_MHON_09:HSI-03"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[]},{"id":"MC_MHON_L1_03:ALT-B05","text":"[Methodological / performative level] The form of the work effects its thesis, because it itself passes among fable, matter, geometry, number, and authority and stabilizes doctrine in the inscribed sign.","refs":["CL_MHON_07:ALT-B05","CL_MHON_08:ALT-B05","CL_MHON_09:ALT-B05"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[]}],"original_name":"Altitude Integration"},{"number":"15","name":"Internal Tensions, Mutation Points, and Fault Lines","items":[{"id":"MC_MHON_L1_03:FL-01","text":"The translation from hieroglyphic analogy to material causality and cosmic authority is posited as necessary, but a common formal rule for the diverse kinds of proof is lacking.","refs":["CL_MHON_07:FL-01","CL_MHON_09:FL-02"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"Tension","passages":[]},{"id":"MC_MHON_L1_03:FL-02","text":"The autarky of the Monad, the dependence of operation on environment, and the subordination of created order to divine will produce three senses of exteriority that are not fully reconciled.","refs":["CL_MHON_07:FL-04","CL_MHON_08:FL-02","CL_MHON_09:GA-08"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"Tension","passages":[]},{"id":"MC_MHON_L1_03:FL-03","text":"Art produces real efficacy and ordered equality, but the boundary between valid reconfiguration and acquired natural perfection remains unstable.","refs":["CL_MHON_08:FL-01","CL_MHON_09:GNX-01","CL_MHON_09:BSD-04"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"Tension","passages":[]},{"id":"MC_MHON_L1_03:FL-04","text":"Quaternary metathesis and metamorphosis through XXIV are joined as scales of the same transformation, although the operative connection between geometric permutation and cosmic completion is incomplete.","refs":["CL_MHON_09:RMD-05","CL_MHON_09:XD-05","CL_MHON_09:FL-03"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"Tension","passages":[]},{"id":"MC_MHON_L1_03:FL-05","text":"Initiatory concealment and transmissible inscription are simultaneously necessary, but the criterion by which discipline is communicated without equally public revelation is not fully declared.","refs":["CL_MHON_08:GNX-04","CL_MHON_09:AS-B07"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"Tension","passages":[]},{"id":"MC_MHON_L1_03:MP-01","text":"The point changes from necessary junction into removable superfluity, then into the principial center of circular effusion.","refs":["CL_MHON_07:RMD-05","CL_MHON_09:RLE-01"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"Tension","passages":[]},{"id":"MC_MHON_L1_03:MP-02","text":"The circle changes from celestial figure into the movement of art and finally into the form of closure that restores principle as completion.","refs":["CL_MHON_07:MP-01","CL_MHON_09:RLE-01"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"Composite","passages":[]},{"id":"MC_MHON_L1_03:MP-03","text":"Dissection changes from interpretive anatomy into an apparatus that composes vessels, functions, and new relations.","refs":["CL_MHON_08:MP-01"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"Composite","passages":[]},{"id":"MC_MHON_L1_03:MP-04","text":"The sign changes from a recognizable figure into materially conditioned operation and then into disciplinary inscription.","refs":["CL_MHON_08:GA-06","CL_MHON_09:GA-07"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"Composite","passages":[]},{"id":"MC_MHON_L1_03:MP-05","text":"Number changes from a qualitative discrimination of configuration into an operator ordering structure, time, and metamorphosis.","refs":["CL_MHON_07:MP-03","CL_MHON_09:GA-03","CL_MHON_09:MP-02"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"Composite","passages":[]},{"id":"MC_MHON_L1_03:MP-06","text":"Limit changes from the cessation of created power into the closure of circular return under divine order.","refs":["CL_MHON_09:XD-04","CL_MHON_09:MP-03"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"Tension","passages":[]}],"original_name":"Internal Tensions, Mutation Points, and Fault Lines"},{"number":"16","name":"Hidden System Image","items":[{"id":"MC_MHON_L1_03:HSI-01","text":"Reality is implicitly a machine of concentric transductions: influx is distributed, matter configured, parts distinguished, figure measured, and number closes circulation.","refs":["CL_MHON_07:HSI-01","CL_MHON_09:HSI-01"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[]},{"id":"MC_MHON_L1_03:HSI-02","text":"The Monad is implicitly a center that unfolds itself into differences without dispersing itself and draws them back through proportion to common relation.","refs":["CL_MHON_08:HSI-01","CL_MHON_07:RLE-05"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[]},{"id":"MC_MHON_L1_03:HSI-03","text":"The subject is implicitly an initiated artisan-interpreter who neither contemplates from outside nor absolutely dominates, but administers measures, times, and apparatuses.","refs":["CL_MHON_08:HSI-02","CL_MHON_09:HSI-02"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[]},{"id":"MC_MHON_L1_03:HSI-04","text":"Time is implicitly a spiral of effusion, operation, concealment, and return, whose finite completions admit non-identical iteration.","refs":["CL_MHON_07:HSI-03","CL_MHON_08:HSI-03","CL_MHON_09:HSI-03"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[]},{"id":"MC_MHON_L1_03:HSI-05","text":"Possible navigation is not flight from order, but proportionate choice among disruption, composition, resection, and return within the limits of created causality.","refs":["CL_MHON_07:HSI-04","CL_MHON_07:BSD-04","CL_MHON_09:BSA-01"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[]}],"original_name":"Hidden System Image"},{"number":"17","name":"Minimal Whole-Book Reconstruction Test","items":[{"id":"MC_MHON_L1_03:MRT-B01","text":"The axioms of proportion, configuration, generative difference, derived art, operative sign, transformative number, created limit, and inexhaustion suffice to reconstruct the metagregal machine.","refs":["CL_MHON_07:MRT-B01","CL_MHON_08:MRT-B01","CL_MHON_09:MRT-B01"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[]},{"id":"MC_MHON_L1_03:MRT-B02","text":"The firmest structure is where circulation, dissection, and commensuration preserve the same law: change does not erase common relation, but manifests it at another level.","refs":["CL_MHON_07:MRT-B02","CL_MHON_08:MRT-B02","CL_MHON_09:MRT-B02"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[]},{"id":"MC_MHON_L1_03:MRT-B03","text":"The conversion of influx into body, the exact distinction of artificial efficacy from natural perfection, and the transition from geometry to cosmology and apocalypse remain insufficiently determined.","refs":["CL_MHON_07:MRT-B03","CL_MHON_08:MRT-B03","CL_MHON_09:MRT-B03"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[]},{"id":"MC_MHON_L1_03:MRT-B04","text":"The reconstruction coheres as a recursive matrix of translation, not as a univocal demonstration; it preserves true movement if it retains together closure and inexhaustion, proportion and change, autarky and dependence.","refs":["CL_MHON_07:MRT-B04","CL_MHON_08:ABS-07","CL_MHON_09:AS-B06"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"Tension","passages":[]}],"original_name":"Minimal Whole-Book Reconstruction Test"}],"revision":"MC_MHON_L1_03@R001","source_variant_id":"canonical:MC_MHON_L1_03:v1","source_language_code":"la","original_title":"Mhon L1 03"}],"identity":{"layer":"MC","kicker":"Meta-cluster · Monas Hieroglyphica","title":"Mhon L1 03","context":"Chapters 19–25 · Theorem Xviii. · T H E Ò R. X I X. · T H E O R. X X. · Theorem Xxi. · Theorem Xxii. · T H E O R. Xxiii. · Theorem Xxiiii.","original_title":"Mhon L1 03","original_context":"Chapters 19–25 · Theor. Xviii. · T H E Ò R. X I X. · T H E O R. X X. · Theor. Xxi. · Theor. Xxii. · T H E O R. Xxiii. · Theor. Xxiiii."},"original_title":"Mhon L1 03","title_language_code":"en"},{"id":"WB_MHON","layer":"WB","title":"The Hieroglyphic Monad","order":1,"parents":["MC_MHON_L1_01","MC_MHON_L1_02","MC_MHON_L1_03"],"revision":"WB_MHON@R002","sha256":"f169ba6e36c64acdfddf502840ebd1da39862fa596e26359935155f1b90b4f1a","sections":[{"number":"1","name":"Book-Scale Argumental Spine","items":[{"id":"WB_MHON:AS-B01","text":"Systema quaerit quomodo character minimus ordines geometricos, cosmicos, materiales, temporales et numericos in unam machinam contrahere possit sine differentiarum abolitione.","refs":["MC_MHON_L1_01:BAS-01","MC_MHON_L1_03:AS-01"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"Composite","passages":[]},{"id":"WB_MHON:AS-B02","text":"Monas non est obiectum stabile, sed operator relationalis qui distinctiones producit, per media distribuit et per reditum ordinatum ad unitatem reformatam colligit.","refs":["MC_MHON_L1_02:BAS-02","MC_MHON_L1_03:AS-02"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"Composite","passages":[]},{"id":"WB_MHON:AS-B03","text":"Argumentatio est reconstructiva et transductiva: geometria invariantes secernit, analogia campos coniungit, operatio efficaciam probat, numerus autem mutationes commensurat.","refs":["MC_MHON_L1_02:BAS-03","MC_MHON_L1_03:AS-03"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"Composite","passages":[]},{"id":"WB_MHON:AS-B04","text":"Motus procedit a signo manifesto ad principium minimum, inde per differentiationes cosmicas et materiales, postremo ad commensurationem, consignationem et reditum finitum.","refs":["MC_MHON_L1_01:BAS-06","MC_MHON_L1_03:AS-04"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"Composite","passages":[]},{"id":"WB_MHON:AS-B05","text":"Progressio est spiralis, cumulativa et mutatione acta, quia singulus reditus relationem priorem servat dum eius campum, functionem aut statum auget.","refs":["MC_MHON_L1_01:BAS-04","MC_MHON_L1_03:AS-05"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"Composite","passages":[]},{"id":"WB_MHON:AS-B06","text":"Probatio non est deductio uniforme clausa, sed congruentia inter necessitatem formalem, mediationem materialem, operationem rectam, effectum et intellectum interpretis.","refs":["MC_MHON_L1_01:BAS-05","MC_MHON_L1_03:BSD-05"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"Tension","passages":[]},{"id":"WB_MHON:AS-B07","text":"Necessitas geometrica in grammaticam translationis amplificatur, sed omnis transitus inter caelum, materiam, figuram et mysterium mediatore indiget quem analogia sola non supplet.","refs":["MC_MHON_L1_01:XD-08","MC_MHON_L1_03:GA-05"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"Tension","passages":[]},{"id":"WB_MHON:AS-B08","text":"Forma expositionis propriam thesim perficit, quoniam figuras dividendo, convertendo, numerando et recomponendo unitatem transductivam quam affirmat actu constituit.","refs":["MC_MHON_L1_01:BAS-07","MC_MHON_L1_03:ALT-B05"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"Composite","passages":[]},{"id":"WB_MHON:AS-B09","text":"Clausura totius libri est duplex: potentiam creatam ad proportionem et principium finit, sed mysterium atque relationem occultam tamquam causas ulterioris interpretationis reservat.","refs":["MC_MHON_L1_01:GA-14","MC_MHON_L1_03:GA-13","MC_MHON_L1_03:XD-09"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"Tension","passages":[]}],"original_name":"Book-Scale Argumental Spine"},{"number":"2","name":"Global Load-Bearing Axioms","items":[{"id":"WB_MHON:GA-01","text":"[Persistent] Forma vel signum non rem iam constitutam passive imitatur, sed relationes manifestat, distribuit et in apparatum operationis convertit.","refs":["MC_MHON_L1_01:GA-01","MC_MHON_L1_02:GA-01","MC_MHON_L1_03:GA-07"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"Composite","passages":[]},{"id":"WB_MHON:GA-02","text":"[Persistent] Relatio sive proportio identitatem et efficaciam terminorum antecedit; elementum est id quod intra configurationem generatricem agere potest.","refs":["MC_MHON_L1_01:GA-03","MC_MHON_L1_03:GA-02"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"Composite","passages":[]},{"id":"WB_MHON:GA-03","text":"[Emergent] Unitas monadica per distinctionem conservatur, quia divisio partes relatas producit quarum recompositio ampliorem unitatem efficit.","refs":["MC_MHON_L1_02:GA-02","MC_MHON_L1_03:GA-04"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"Composite","passages":[]},{"id":"WB_MHON:GA-04","text":"[Mutating] Identitas persistit ut invarians relationis per mutationem situs, medii et functionis, non ut substantia immobilis.","refs":["MC_MHON_L1_01:GA-05","MC_MHON_L1_03:GA-03"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"Composite","passages":[]},{"id":"WB_MHON:GA-05","text":"[Persistent] Geometria est grammatica ontologica et transductiva qua situs, centrum, sectio, vicinitas, circulatio et proportio in causalitatem convertuntur.","refs":["MC_MHON_L1_01:GA-04","MC_MHON_L1_02:GA-03"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"Composite","passages":[]},{"id":"WB_MHON:GA-06","text":"[Emergent] Causalitas est medialis, distributa et asymmetrica: principia, figurae, animae, radii, corpora et receptacula effectum participant, sed non aequaliter fundant.","refs":["MC_MHON_L1_02:GA-06","MC_MHON_L1_03:BSD-02"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"Composite","passages":[]},{"id":"WB_MHON:GA-07","text":"[Mutating] Materia transit a termino receptivo ad nodum activum qui influxum nutrit, imprimit, reconfigurat et in circulationem remittit.","refs":["MC_MHON_L1_02:GA-09","MC_MHON_L1_03:MME-02"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"Composite","passages":[]},{"id":"WB_MHON:GA-08","text":"[Emergent] Tempus non est receptaculum praedatum, sed spiralis recursionis effectus quo apparitio, complementum, operatio et reditus statum relationis mutant.","refs":["MC_MHON_L1_01:GA-07","MC_MHON_L1_02:BSD-03","MC_MHON_L1_03:BSD-03"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"Composite","passages":[]},{"id":"WB_MHON:GA-09","text":"[Mutating] Numerus ex mensura externa in operator qualitativum transit qui configurationem, permutationem, amplificationem et completionem temporalem ordinat.","refs":["MC_MHON_L1_01:GNX-04","MC_MHON_L1_03:GA-12"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"Composite","passages":[]},{"id":"WB_MHON:GA-10","text":"[Emergent] Partialitas, subtractio, dissolutio et terminatio generativae fiunt cum relationem originis servant atque differentiam recomponibilem producunt.","refs":["MC_MHON_L1_01:GA-06","MC_MHON_L1_03:GA-09"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"Composite","passages":[]},{"id":"WB_MHON:GA-11","text":"[Persistent] Cognitio valida est reconstructiva et operativa: relationes generatrices persequitur atque interpretationem, operationem, effectum et dispositionem interpretis congruere postulat.","refs":["MC_MHON_L1_01:GA-10","MC_MHON_L1_02:GA-12","MC_MHON_L1_03:GA-08"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"Composite","passages":[]},{"id":"WB_MHON:GA-12","text":"[Terminal] Ars potentia realis sed derivata est: configurationes mutat et potentias perficit localiter, neque originem, mensuram aut causalitatem suam absolute producit.","refs":["MC_MHON_L1_03:GA-06","MC_MHON_L1_03:BSD-08"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"Composite","passages":[]},{"id":"WB_MHON:GA-13","text":"[Emergent] Ordines geometricus, cosmicus, materialis, numeralis, alphabeticus et doctrinalis sunt codices convertibiles, non regiones causaliter seiunctae.","refs":["MC_MHON_L1_01:GA-08","MC_MHON_L1_02:GA-07"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"Composite","passages":[]},{"id":"WB_MHON:GA-14","text":"[Terminal] Clausura legitima finitam restitutionem proportionis producit, sed saturationem semanticam impedit atque novum circuitum interpretativum relinquit.","refs":["MC_MHON_L1_01:GA-14","MC_MHON_L1_03:GA-13","MC_MHON_L1_03:GA-14"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"Tension","passages":[]},{"id":"WB_MHON:GA-15","text":"[Attractor] Attractor totius libri est unitas monadica proportionalis, quae dispersionem formarum retrahit sine earum mutationibus in identitatem simplicem redigendis.","refs":["MC_MHON_L1_01:GA-13","MC_MHON_L1_03:GA-01","MC_MHON_L1_03:RLE-06"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"Composite","passages":[]}],"original_name":"Global Load-Bearing Axioms"},{"number":"3","name":"Recurrence, Mutation, and Displacement Matrix","items":[{"id":"WB_MHON:RMD-01","text":"Punctum ab ingrediente minimo in centrum ontologicum et fontem fluxus amplificatur, deinde tamquam copula superflua excluditur et ut centrum effusionis restituitur.","refs":["MC_MHON_L1_01:RMD-01","MC_MHON_L1_03:RMD-05"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"Tension","passages":[]},{"id":"WB_MHON:RMD-02","text":"Monas a nomine unitatis in functionem distributam, inde in attractorem conversionum et tandem in machinam internam originis, processus et finis mutatur.","refs":["MC_MHON_L1_01:RMD-04","MC_MHON_L1_02:RMD-01","MC_MHON_L1_03:RMD-01"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"Composite","passages":[]},{"id":"WB_MHON:RMD-03","text":"Geometria a repertorio formarum transit ad medium commune causalitatum, grammaticam conversionis et instrumentum calibrationis transductivae.","refs":["MC_MHON_L1_01:XD-07","MC_MHON_L1_02:GA-03","MC_MHON_L1_03:MME-03"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"Composite","passages":[]},{"id":"WB_MHON:RMD-04","text":"Signum ex manifestatione productiva in operatorium relationum, deinde in apparatum materialiter condicionatum et consignationem disciplinae transmissibilem migrat.","refs":["MC_MHON_L1_01:RMD-05","MC_MHON_L1_02:MP-02","MC_MHON_L1_03:MP-04"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"Composite","passages":[]},{"id":"WB_MHON:RMD-05","text":"Terra et corpus a locis cosmologicis aut receptaculis transeunt in nodos activos impressionis, nutritionis, compositionis et mensurae.","refs":["MC_MHON_L1_01:CGD-03","MC_MHON_L1_02:RMD-06","MC_MHON_L1_03:MME-02"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"Composite","passages":[]},{"id":"WB_MHON:RMD-06","text":"Circularitas a dependentia peripheriae et circulatione cosmica mutatur in circuitum conversionum, recursionem differentiae et formam finiti reditus.","refs":["MC_MHON_L1_01:RMD-03","MC_MHON_L1_02:RMD-03","MC_MHON_L1_03:RMD-03"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"Composite","passages":[]},{"id":"WB_MHON:RMD-07","text":"Numerus ab extensione symbolica ad motorem compositionis, qualitatem configurationis et mensuram metamorphosis temporalis amplificatur.","refs":["MC_MHON_L1_01:MME-05","MC_MHON_L1_02:XD-05","MC_MHON_L1_03:MME-07"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"Composite","passages":[]},{"id":"WB_MHON:RMD-08","text":"Occultatio ex statu absentiae in residuum internum conversionis et tandem in mysterium consignabile sed inexhaustum transfertur.","refs":["MC_MHON_L1_01:RMD-10","MC_MHON_L1_02:RMD-08","MC_MHON_L1_03:RMD-09"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"Composite","passages":[]},{"id":"WB_MHON:RMD-09","text":"Potentia agendi ex conversione dependentiae in operationem interpretativam transit, deinde ut ars configurativa realis sed causaliter limitata determinatur.","refs":["MC_MHON_L1_01:BSD-06","MC_MHON_L1_02:GA-12","MC_MHON_L1_03:RMD-07"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"Composite","passages":[]},{"id":"WB_MHON:RMD-10","text":"Clausura a peripheria generativa ad impressiones materiales et verbales progreditur, deinde in reditum finitum convertitur qui relationem occultam non consumit.","refs":["MC_MHON_L1_01:BAS-08","MC_MHON_L1_02:BAS-06","MC_MHON_L1_03:GA-13","MC_MHON_L1_03:GA-14"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"Tension","passages":[]},{"id":"WB_MHON:RMD-11","text":"Exterioritas pura primum negatur, mediatio immediationi substituitur, postremo exterioritas in supplementum illicitum, condicionem ambientalem et causam ontologicam distingui debet.","refs":["MC_MHON_L1_01:BSA-01","MC_MHON_L1_02:BSA-01","MC_MHON_L1_03:RMD-08"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"Tension","passages":[]}],"original_name":"Recurrence, Mutation, and Displacement Matrix"},{"number":"4","name":"Lower-Layer vs Current-Level Distinction","items":[{"id":"WB_MHON:CGD-01","text":"Patrocinium, constructiones linearum, animalia symbolica et apparatus singulares localia manent; ascendit tantum regula quod relatio exacta condicionibus materialibus et figurativis sustentatur.","refs":["MC_MHON_L1_01:CGD-01","MC_MHON_L1_02:CGD-01","MC_MHON_L1_03:CGD-01"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"Direct","passages":[]},{"id":"WB_MHON:CGD-02","text":"Sol, Luna, Mercurius et alii termini cosmici non fiunt substantiae globales; functiones mediationis, complementi, substitutionis et transductionis gradum libri ordinant.","refs":["MC_MHON_L1_01:CGD-03","MC_MHON_L1_02:RMD-05","MC_MHON_L1_02:CGD-03"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"Composite","passages":[]},{"id":"WB_MHON:CGD-03","text":"Octonarius, quinarietas, quaternarius et XXIV localia discrimina retinent; globalis est tantum thesis numerum qualitatem configurationis et amplificationis exprimere.","refs":["MC_MHON_L1_01:CGD-05","MC_MHON_L1_02:RMD-07","MC_MHON_L1_03:RMD-06"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"Composite","passages":[]},{"id":"WB_MHON:CGD-04","text":"Stillae, impressiones, materias et ambitus singulares non oportet universalizare; ascendit materialitas activa cuius efficacia a situ et compositione dependet.","refs":["MC_MHON_L1_01:CGD-04","MC_MHON_L1_02:CGD-04","MC_MHON_L1_03:CGD-04"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"Composite","passages":[]},{"id":"WB_MHON:CGD-05","text":"Dependentia figurae a puncto et planetae a relatione in causalitatem distributam amplificatur, ubi nullum terminum effectum sibi soli appropriare licet.","refs":["MC_MHON_L1_01:CGD-06","MC_MHON_L1_02:BSD-02","MC_MHON_L1_03:BSD-02"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"Composite","passages":[]},{"id":"WB_MHON:CGD-06","text":"Connexio inter formam, tempus et signum in machinam translatricem ascendit, cuius figura completa relationem, materiam et consignationem simul continet.","refs":["MC_MHON_L1_01:CGD-07","MC_MHON_L1_02:CGD-07","MC_MHON_L1_03:XD-04"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"Composite","passages":[]},{"id":"WB_MHON:CGD-07","text":"Apertura generativa priorum circuituum a clausura terminali non aboletur, sed per finitudinem causalitatis creatae et reditum ad principium qualificatur.","refs":["MC_MHON_L1_01:BAS-08","MC_MHON_L1_03:GA-13"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"Tension","passages":[]},{"id":"WB_MHON:CGD-08","text":"Correspondentiae particulares valent ut machinae locales translationis; non ascendunt in legem universalem nisi mediatio, mensura et necessitas transitus demonstrari possunt.","refs":["MC_MHON_L1_01:GNX-08","MC_MHON_L1_02:CGD-06","MC_MHON_L1_03:BSA-01"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"Tension","passages":[]}],"original_name":"Lower-Layer vs Current-Level Distinction"},{"number":"5","name":"Global Apophatic Structure","items":[{"id":"WB_MHON:GNX-01","text":"Signum passivum, ornamentale aut mere conventionale excluditur, quia figura relationem producere, operationem condicionare vel disciplinam consignare debet.","refs":["MC_MHON_L1_01:GNX-01","MC_MHON_L1_02:GNX-01","MC_MHON_L1_03:GNX-02"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"Negative","passages":[]},{"id":"WB_MHON:GNX-02","text":"Autonomia termini excluditur: unitas, figura, materia et agens creatum solum intra relationes centri, mediationis, mensurae et principii operantur.","refs":["MC_MHON_L1_01:BSA-02","MC_MHON_L1_02:GNX-02","MC_MHON_L1_03:GNX-01"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"Negative","passages":[]},{"id":"WB_MHON:GNX-03","text":"Materia passiva excluditur, quoniam receptio, nutritio, impressio et configuratio continuationem causalem constituunt.","refs":["MC_MHON_L1_02:GNX-04","MC_MHON_L1_03:GNX-01"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"Negative","passages":[]},{"id":"WB_MHON:GNX-04","text":"Identitas fixa et unitas indivisa excluduntur, quia permanentia tantum per translationem functionis et relationem partium conservatur.","refs":["MC_MHON_L1_01:GNX-03","MC_MHON_L1_02:DBN-02","MC_MHON_L1_03:GNX-06"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"Negative","passages":[]},{"id":"WB_MHON:GNX-05","text":"Tempus lineare praedatum et progressus creatus indefinitus excluduntur; tempus ex circuitibus finitis transformationis et reditus emergit.","refs":["MC_MHON_L1_01:DBN-04","MC_MHON_L1_03:GNX-07"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"Negative","passages":[]},{"id":"WB_MHON:GNX-06","text":"Disciplinae clausae et sensus immediatus excluduntur, quia intellectus per conversionem codicum atque media figurativa procedit.","refs":["MC_MHON_L1_01:GNX-05","MC_MHON_L1_02:BSA-01"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"Negative","passages":[]},{"id":"WB_MHON:GNX-07","text":"Numerus nudus et arithmetica a configuratione separata excluduntur, cum vis numeralis e situ, partitione et permutatione nascatur.","refs":["MC_MHON_L1_01:GNX-04","MC_MHON_L1_02:DBN-04","MC_MHON_L1_03:CGD-06"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"Negative","passages":[]},{"id":"WB_MHON:GNX-08","text":"Demonstratio totaliter clausa et interpretatio exhaustiva excluduntur, quia mensura occulta et mysterium terminale ulteriorem reconstructionem exigunt.","refs":["MC_MHON_L1_01:BSA-04","MC_MHON_L1_02:GNX-06","MC_MHON_L1_03:BSA-05"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"Negative","passages":[]},{"id":"WB_MHON:GNX-09","text":"Ars absoluta excluditur: operatio creata effectum realem efficere potest, sed essentiam naturalem, mensuram et fundamentum causalitatis ex nihilo non producit.","refs":["MC_MHON_L1_03:GNX-05","MC_MHON_L1_03:XD-03"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"Negative","passages":[]}],"original_name":"Global Apophatic Structure"},{"number":"6","name":"Deep Book Apophatics","items":[{"id":"WB_MHON:DBN-01","text":"Falsum esse oportet realitatem continere residuum absolute informe aut res mutuo indifferentes, quia systema omne ens intra situm et relationem effectivam collocat.","refs":["MC_MHON_L1_01:DBN-01","MC_MHON_L1_02:BSA-02"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"Negative","passages":[]},{"id":"WB_MHON:DBN-02","text":"Falsum esse oportet ordines essendi, significandi, producendi et mensurandi fundamentaliter separatos aut sine mediatore convertibiles esse.","refs":["MC_MHON_L1_01:DBN-02","MC_MHON_L1_02:DBN-01","MC_MHON_L1_03:DBN-01"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"Negative","passages":[]},{"id":"WB_MHON:DBN-03","text":"Falsum esse oportet distinctionem unitatem destruere aut partes in substantias autonomas mutare, quia divisio facultatem reunionis conservare debet.","refs":["MC_MHON_L1_01:DBN-03","MC_MHON_L1_03:DBN-02"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"Negative","passages":[]},{"id":"WB_MHON:DBN-04","text":"Falsum esse oportet causalitatem ex unica origine efficiente procedere; effectus requirit principium, media, receptaculum et mensuram asymmetrice cooperantia.","refs":["MC_MHON_L1_01:DBN-05","MC_MHON_L1_02:GNX-03","MC_MHON_L1_03:BSD-02"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"Negative","passages":[]},{"id":"WB_MHON:DBN-05","text":"Falsum esse oportet absentiam, dislocationem, subtractionem et dissolutionem esse privationes steriles, quia negatio ordinata distinctionem et circuitum sequentem generat.","refs":["MC_MHON_L1_01:DBN-06","MC_MHON_L1_03:XD-02"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"Negative","passages":[]},{"id":"WB_MHON:DBN-06","text":"Falsum esse oportet creaturam sibi originem, mensuram et continuationem infinitam conferre posse; aliter ars derivata in causalitatem absolutam converteretur.","refs":["MC_MHON_L1_03:BSA-02","MC_MHON_L1_03:GA-06"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"Negative","passages":[]},{"id":"WB_MHON:DBN-07","text":"Falsum esse oportet aut analogiam nudam aut deductionem externam solam probationem sufficientem praebere; systema congruentiam inter relationem, operationem et effectum requirit.","refs":["MC_MHON_L1_01:XD-08","MC_MHON_L1_03:XD-07"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"Tension","passages":[]}],"original_name":"Deep Book Apophatics"},{"number":"7","name":"Super-Apophatics of the Whole","items":[{"id":"WB_MHON:BSA-01","text":"Purum exterius relationibus indisponibile est, sed creator ut causa ontologica ultima retinetur; haec exterioritatis asymmetria systema ordinat sine plena resolutione.","refs":["MC_MHON_L1_01:BSA-01","MC_MHON_L1_03:BSA-04"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"Tension","passages":[]},{"id":"WB_MHON:BSA-02","text":"Sensus immediate perspicuus impossibilis est, quia nulla perspectiva exterior totam Monadem sine conversione, disciplina et dispositione interpretis possidet.","refs":["MC_MHON_L1_02:BSA-01","MC_MHON_L1_03:BSA-03"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"Negative","passages":[]},{"id":"WB_MHON:BSA-03","text":"Autonomia absoluta sive termini sive operatoris ontologice inadmissibilis est; potentia semper intra dependentiam et mensuram exercetur.","refs":["MC_MHON_L1_01:BSA-02","MC_MHON_L1_02:BSA-04"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"Negative","passages":[]},{"id":"WB_MHON:BSA-04","text":"Lex universalis quae analogiam, geometriam, causalitatem materialem et auctoritatem cosmicam perfecte convertat desideratur, sed numquam plene praesto est.","refs":["MC_MHON_L1_01:BSA-04","MC_MHON_L1_03:BSA-01"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"Tension","passages":[]},{"id":"WB_MHON:BSA-05","text":"Saturatio interpretativa terminalis impossibilis est: completio structuram finit, sed mysterium et relatio occulta iter operationis denuo aperiunt.","refs":["MC_MHON_L1_03:GA-14","MC_MHON_L1_03:BSA-05"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"Negative","passages":[]},{"id":"WB_MHON:BSA-06","text":"Progressus creatus infinitus indisponibilis est, quia omnis potentia legitima terminum, restitutionem proportionis et relationem ad principium requirit.","refs":["MC_MHON_L1_03:GNX-07","MC_MHON_L1_03:GA-13"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"Negative","passages":[]}],"original_name":"Super-Apophatics of the Whole"},{"number":"8","name":"Cross-Unit Derivative Propositions","items":[{"id":"WB_MHON:XD-01","text":"Monas est invarians relationis per transformationes: unitas eius non praecedit distinctiones tamquam substantia simplex, sed ex coordinatione earum efficitur.","refs":["MC_MHON_L1_01:XD-01","MC_MHON_L1_02:GA-04","MC_MHON_L1_03:RMD-01","WB_MHON:GA-02","WB_MHON:GA-03","WB_MHON:GA-04","WB_MHON:GA-15"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"Composite","passages":[]},{"id":"WB_MHON:XD-02","text":"Signum completum simul manifestat, operatur et consignat; significatio igitur est effectus apparatus relationalis et materialis, non additamentum operationi externum.","refs":["MC_MHON_L1_01:XD-03","MC_MHON_L1_02:GA-01","MC_MHON_L1_03:XD-04","WB_MHON:GA-01","WB_MHON:GA-13"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"Composite","passages":[]},{"id":"WB_MHON:XD-03","text":"Efficacia materialis neque receptione passiva neque arte absoluta constat, sed circulatione inter potentiam datam, receptaculum activum et reconfigurationem mensuratam.","refs":["MC_MHON_L1_02:GA-09","MC_MHON_L1_03:XD-03","MC_MHON_L1_03:GA-10","WB_MHON:GA-06","WB_MHON:GA-07","WB_MHON:GA-12"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"Composite","passages":[]},{"id":"WB_MHON:XD-04","text":"Tempus totius libri est spiralis finita: regressum ontologicum, revolutionem convertibilem et terminationem potentiae creatae in eadem recursionis forma componit.","refs":["MC_MHON_L1_01:BSD-03","MC_MHON_L1_02:BSD-03","MC_MHON_L1_03:BSD-03","WB_MHON:GA-08","WB_MHON:GA-14"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"Composite","passages":[]},{"id":"WB_MHON:XD-05","text":"Differentia negativa fit principium productionis, quia partialitas, separatio, dissectio et subtractio repertorium relationum componendarum aperiunt.","refs":["MC_MHON_L1_01:XD-04","MC_MHON_L1_02:RMD-02","MC_MHON_L1_03:XD-02","WB_MHON:GA-03","WB_MHON:GA-10"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"Composite","passages":[]},{"id":"WB_MHON:XD-06","text":"Probatio valida est reconstructio congruentiae inter genealogiam formae, seriem conversionum, operationem rectam et effectum intellectum.","refs":["MC_MHON_L1_01:BSD-05","MC_MHON_L1_02:BSD-06","MC_MHON_L1_03:XD-07","WB_MHON:GA-05","WB_MHON:GA-11"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"Composite","passages":[]},{"id":"WB_MHON:XD-07","text":"Numerus qualitatem relationis formalizat atque conversionem inter figuram, materiam, litteram et tempus efficit, sed non eam ex quantitate sola deducit.","refs":["MC_MHON_L1_01:MME-05","MC_MHON_L1_02:XD-05","MC_MHON_L1_03:XD-06","WB_MHON:GA-09","WB_MHON:GA-13"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"Composite","passages":[]},{"id":"WB_MHON:XD-08","text":"Clausura duplicem statum habet: finem operationis constituit et retroactive condicionem novi circuitus interpretativi efficit.","refs":["MC_MHON_L1_01:BSA-04","MC_MHON_L1_02:XD-07","MC_MHON_L1_03:XD-09","WB_MHON:GA-14"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"Tension","passages":[]},{"id":"WB_MHON:XD-09","text":"Translatio analogica est simul instrumentum amplificationis et commissura epistemica fragilis, quia ambitum systematis auget sine lege conversionis ubique demonstrata.","refs":["MC_MHON_L1_01:XD-08","MC_MHON_L1_02:FL-03","MC_MHON_L1_03:FL-01","WB_MHON:GA-05","WB_MHON:GA-11","WB_MHON:GA-13"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"Tension","passages":[]},{"id":"WB_MHON:XD-10","text":"Potentia subiecti est administratio configurativa dependentiarum: lector, operator et artifex agunt eligendo, dividendo et componendo, non originem causalitatis usurpando.","refs":["MC_MHON_L1_01:BSD-06","MC_MHON_L1_02:BSD-04","MC_MHON_L1_03:BSD-08","WB_MHON:GA-11","WB_MHON:GA-12"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"Composite","passages":[]}],"original_name":"Cross-Unit Derivative Propositions"},{"number":"9","name":"Book-Scale Super-Derivatives","items":[{"id":"WB_MHON:BSD-01","text":"Ontologia libri realitatem constituit ut campum transductionum, in quo res sunt status relative stabiles circuituum formalis, materialis, numeralis et symbolici.","refs":["MC_MHON_L1_01:BSD-01","MC_MHON_L1_02:BSD-01","MC_MHON_L1_03:BSD-01","WB_MHON:GA-02","WB_MHON:GA-04","WB_MHON:GA-13"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"Composite","passages":[]},{"id":"WB_MHON:BSD-02","text":"Causalitas fit syntaxis mediationum asymmetricarum: principium sustentat, figura ordinat, medium transfert, materia prolongat et mensura effectum limitat.","refs":["MC_MHON_L1_01:BSD-02","MC_MHON_L1_02:BSD-02","MC_MHON_L1_03:BSD-02","WB_MHON:GA-05","WB_MHON:GA-06","WB_MHON:GA-07"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"Composite","passages":[]},{"id":"WB_MHON:BSD-03","text":"Semiosis est circuitus causaliter efficax, quia eadem configuratio rem manifestat, effectum materialem condensat et formam transmissibilem instituit.","refs":["MC_MHON_L1_01:MME-02","MC_MHON_L1_02:BSD-05","MC_MHON_L1_03:XD-04","WB_MHON:GA-01","WB_MHON:GA-13"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"Composite","passages":[]},{"id":"WB_MHON:BSD-04","text":"Tempus non metitur machinam extrinsecus, sed ex eius differentiationibus, conversionibus, exhaustionibus localibus et reditibus finitis generatur.","refs":["MC_MHON_L1_01:BSD-03","MC_MHON_L1_02:BSD-03","MC_MHON_L1_03:BSD-03","WB_MHON:GA-08","WB_MHON:GA-14"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"Composite","passages":[]},{"id":"WB_MHON:BSD-05","text":"Subiectum est operator-interpres intra machinam constitutus, qui relationes administrat sed neque spectator exterior neque dominus causalis fit.","refs":["MC_MHON_L1_01:BSD-04","MC_MHON_L1_02:BSD-04","MC_MHON_L1_03:BSD-04","WB_MHON:GA-11","WB_MHON:GA-12"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"Composite","passages":[]},{"id":"WB_MHON:BSD-06","text":"Libertas est facultas configurationis sub mensura: potentia agendi crescit per rectam mediationem et selectionem, non per exemptionem a dependentia.","refs":["MC_MHON_L1_01:BSD-06","MC_MHON_L1_03:BSD-08","WB_MHON:GA-06","WB_MHON:GA-12"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"Composite","passages":[]},{"id":"WB_MHON:BSD-07","text":"Historia huius systematis constat ex clausuris localibus quae effectum perficiunt, potentiam finiunt et sub relatione occulta novam extractionem aperiunt.","refs":["MC_MHON_L1_01:GA-14","MC_MHON_L1_03:BSD-07","WB_MHON:GA-08","WB_MHON:GA-14"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"Composite","passages":[]},{"id":"WB_MHON:BSD-08","text":"Veritas fit concordia transmodalis inter invariantem, apparatum, operationem et effectum, sed eius vis minuitur ubi transitus analogicus regulam communem non exhibet.","refs":["MC_MHON_L1_01:BAS-05","MC_MHON_L1_03:BSD-05","MC_MHON_L1_03:FL-01","WB_MHON:GA-05","WB_MHON:GA-11"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"Tension","passages":[]},{"id":"WB_MHON:BSD-09","text":"Auctoritas epistemica simul restringitur et distribuitur: initiatio qualitatem accessus ordinat, consignatio autem operationem ultra praesentiam magistri transmittit.","refs":["MC_MHON_L1_01:FL-05","MC_MHON_L1_03:BSD-06","WB_MHON:GA-01","WB_MHON:GA-11"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"Tension","passages":[]}],"original_name":"Book-Scale Super-Derivatives"},{"number":"10","name":"Recursive Loops, Escalation Paths, and Conceptual Attractors","items":[{"id":"WB_MHON:RLE-01","text":"Compressio formarum in unitatem differentiationem generat; differentiae proportionantur, recomponuntur et ad unitatem redeunt quae novam divisionem iam continet.","refs":["MC_MHON_L1_01:RLE-01","MC_MHON_L1_02:RLE-01","MC_MHON_L1_03:RLE-07"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"Composite","passages":[]},{"id":"WB_MHON:RLE-02","text":"Centrum peripheriam constituit, peripheria motum requirit, motus in materiam imprimitur, et impressio tamquam signum causae ad centrum refertur.","refs":["MC_MHON_L1_01:RLE-02","MC_MHON_L1_02:XD-03","MC_MHON_L1_03:XD-04"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"Composite","passages":[]},{"id":"WB_MHON:RLE-03","text":"Unitas distinctionem parit, distinctio proportionem postulat, proportio coniunctionem efficit, et coniunctio unitatem ut capacitatem ulterioris differentiae restituit.","refs":["MC_MHON_L1_02:RLE-01","MC_MHON_L1_03:XD-09"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"Composite","passages":[]},{"id":"WB_MHON:RLE-04","text":"Dislocatio aut dissolutio repertorium partium aperit; compositio mensurata ordinem restituit, sed restitutio novam differentiam producit.","refs":["MC_MHON_L1_01:RLE-04","MC_MHON_L1_03:RMD-04"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"Composite","passages":[]},{"id":"WB_MHON:RLE-05","text":"Latentia per manifestationem solvitur, sed effectus manifestus residuum occultum producit quod ulteriorem interpretationem et conversionem incitat.","refs":["MC_MHON_L1_01:RLE-06","MC_MHON_L1_02:RMD-08","MC_MHON_L1_03:RMD-09"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"Composite","passages":[]},{"id":"WB_MHON:RLE-06","text":"Amplificatio numeralis figuram in litteram, potentiam et tempus transfert; singulus gradus maiorem ordinem promittit sed novam regulam conversionis postulat.","refs":["MC_MHON_L1_01:RLE-05","MC_MHON_L1_02:XD-05","MC_MHON_L1_03:RLE-08"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"Tension","passages":[]},{"id":"WB_MHON:RLE-07","text":"Substitutio medii conservat circuitum cum terminus particularis deficit, quia functio inter Lunam, Mercurium, animam, Spiritum, Terram aut codicem transferri potest.","refs":["MC_MHON_L1_02:RLE-04","MC_MHON_L1_02:HSI-05","MC_MHON_L1_03:GA-05"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"Composite","passages":[]},{"id":"WB_MHON:RLE-08","text":"Monas, proportio, Mercurius et inexhaustio attractores diversos constituunt: primi dispersionem colligunt, ultimum prohibet ne collectio in possessionem plenam claudatur.","refs":["MC_MHON_L1_02:RLE-06","MC_MHON_L1_03:RLE-06","MC_MHON_L1_03:RLE-07"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"Composite","passages":[]},{"id":"WB_MHON:RLE-09","text":"Terminus operationem sistit, proportionem restituit et principium retroactive manifestat; eadem clausura, quatenus mysterium servat, punctum reingressus fit.","refs":["MC_MHON_L1_02:RLE-07","MC_MHON_L1_03:GA-13","MC_MHON_L1_03:XD-09"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"Tension","passages":[]}],"original_name":"Recursive Loops, Escalation Paths, and Conceptual Attractors"},{"number":"11","name":"Formal Generative Schema of the Whole Book","items":[{"id":"WB_MHON:FGS-B01","text":"Signum manifestum → reductio ad repertorium geometricum minimum → inventio relationis generatricis.","refs":["MC_MHON_L1_01:BAS-06","MC_MHON_L1_01:BAS-03"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"Composite","passages":[]},{"id":"WB_MHON:FGS-B02","text":"Punctum sive centrum → extensio et peripheria → unitas distincta in partes operativas.","refs":["MC_MHON_L1_01:BAS-03","MC_MHON_L1_02:GA-02"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"Composite","passages":[]},{"id":"WB_MHON:FGS-B03","text":"Distinctio configurata → situs, sectio et vicinitas → proportio invisibilis → identitas relationalis.","refs":["MC_MHON_L1_02:RMD-02","MC_MHON_L1_03:GA-02"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"Composite","passages":[]},{"id":"WB_MHON:FGS-B04","text":"Configuratio → mediatio cosmica et spiritualis → circulatio functionis → translatio inter codices.","refs":["MC_MHON_L1_02:GA-06","MC_MHON_L1_03:GA-05"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"Composite","passages":[]},{"id":"WB_MHON:FGS-B05","text":"Influxus configuratus → receptio materialis activa → nutritio, impressio et continuatio causalis.","refs":["MC_MHON_L1_02:GA-09","MC_MHON_L1_03:CGD-04"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"Composite","passages":[]},{"id":"WB_MHON:FGS-B06","text":"Corpus compositum → dissectio et subtractio → repertorium partium → metathesis et ars recompositiva.","refs":["MC_MHON_L1_03:RMD-04","MC_MHON_L1_03:RMD-07"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"Composite","passages":[]},{"id":"WB_MHON:FGS-B07","text":"Recompositio proportionata → commensuratio geometrica → numerus qualitativus → amplificatio temporalis.","refs":["MC_MHON_L1_03:GA-12","MC_MHON_L1_03:XD-06"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"Composite","passages":[]},{"id":"WB_MHON:FGS-B08","text":"Effectus coordinatus → Lux, Verbum aut figura completa → consignatio materialis et doctrinalis.","refs":["MC_MHON_L1_02:GA-10","MC_MHON_L1_03:GA-07"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"Composite","passages":[]},{"id":"WB_MHON:FGS-B09","text":"Exhaustio potentiae creatae → restitutio proportionis → reditus ad principium → mysterium servatum → novus circuitus interpretativus.","refs":["MC_MHON_L1_03:GA-13","MC_MHON_L1_03:GA-14","MC_MHON_L1_03:BSA-04"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"Tension","passages":[]}],"original_name":"Formal Generative Schema of the Whole Book"},{"number":"12","name":"Whole-Book Argument Flow Map","items":[{"id":"WB_MHON:AFM-B01","text":"Character manifestus → Punctum-Monas → centrum et peripheria → distributio cosmica → mediatio materialis → figura operativa → numerus transformativus → reditus finitus.","refs":["MC_MHON_L1_01:BAS-01","MC_MHON_L1_03:AS-04"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"Composite","passages":[]},{"id":"WB_MHON:AFM-B02","text":"Unitas relationalis → differentiatio → proportio → translatio inter codices → impressio et effectus → recompositio mensurata → unitas relecta.","refs":["MC_MHON_L1_02:BAS-04","MC_MHON_L1_03:GA-02"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"Composite","passages":[]},{"id":"WB_MHON:AFM-B03","text":"Negatio autonomiae → activatio partialitatis → distributio causalitatis → limitatio artis → terminatio potentiae creatae → relatio ad principium.","refs":["MC_MHON_L1_01:BSA-02","MC_MHON_L1_03:GNX-05"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"Negative","passages":[]},{"id":"WB_MHON:AFM-B04","text":"Necessitas geometrica → translatio analogica → probatio operativa → congruentia effectus → clausura localis → residuum occultum → nova reconstructio.","refs":["MC_MHON_L1_01:XD-08","MC_MHON_L1_03:XD-07"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"Tension","passages":[]}],"original_name":"Whole-Book Argument Flow Map"},{"number":"13","name":"Macro Map-Effects","items":[{"id":"WB_MHON:MME-01","text":"Geometria reclassificatur ex instrumento descriptionis in grammaticam ontologicam, causalitatem configurativam et rationem calibrationis inter campos.","refs":["MC_MHON_L1_01:MME-01","MC_MHON_L1_03:MME-03"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"Composite","passages":[]},{"id":"WB_MHON:MME-02","text":"Monas reclassificatur ex unitate simplici in centrum transductivum quod distinctiones producit, conversiones colligit et limites earum ordinat.","refs":["MC_MHON_L1_02:BAS-05","MC_MHON_L1_03:MME-01"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"Composite","passages":[]},{"id":"WB_MHON:MME-03","text":"Materia reclassificatur ex receptaculo passivo in condicionem co-causalem impressionis, nutritionis, operationis et verificationis.","refs":["MC_MHON_L1_02:GNX-04","MC_MHON_L1_03:MME-02"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"Composite","passages":[]},{"id":"WB_MHON:MME-04","text":"Numerus reclassificatur ex quantitate abstracta in matricem qualitativam quae functiones, permutationes, tempora et metamorphoses coordinat.","refs":["MC_MHON_L1_01:MME-05","MC_MHON_L1_03:MME-07"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"Composite","passages":[]},{"id":"WB_MHON:MME-05","text":"Tempus reclassificatur ex successione externa in effectum recursionis, conversionis et finitae restitutionis potentiae.","refs":["MC_MHON_L1_01:MME-04","MC_MHON_L1_03:BSD-03"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"Composite","passages":[]},{"id":"WB_MHON:MME-06","text":"Oppositio inter signum et rem corruit, quia figura eadem manifestationem, operationem materialem et transmissionem doctrinae efficit.","refs":["MC_MHON_L1_01:MME-02","MC_MHON_L1_03:XD-04"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"Composite","passages":[]},{"id":"WB_MHON:MME-07","text":"Oppositio naturae et artis recodificatur: ars potentias naturales configurat et ordinat, sed perfectionem naturalem causaliter non usurpat.","refs":["MC_MHON_L1_03:XD-03","MC_MHON_L1_03:MME-05"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"Composite","passages":[]},{"id":"WB_MHON:MME-08","text":"Lectio reclassificatur ex visu aut extractione sensus in participationem reconstructivam quae figuras dividit, operationes probat et invariantes a contingentibus secernit.","refs":["MC_MHON_L1_01:MME-08","MC_MHON_L1_03:MME-08"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"Composite","passages":[]},{"id":"WB_MHON:MME-09","text":"Fines disciplinarum reclassificantur ut canales translationis, ita ut differentiae codicum serventur sine autonomia ontologica.","refs":["MC_MHON_L1_01:MME-07","MC_MHON_L1_02:GA-07"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"Composite","passages":[]}],"original_name":"Macro Map-Effects"},{"number":"14","name":"Altitude Integration","items":[{"id":"WB_MHON:ALT-B01","text":"In gradu superficiei punctum, linea, circulus, Sol, Luna, Mercurius, Terra, crux, litterae, animalia et numeri apparent ut obiecta determinata.","refs":["MC_MHON_L1_01:ALT-B01","MC_MHON_L1_03:ALT-B01"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"Composite","passages":[]},{"id":"WB_MHON:ALT-B02","text":"In gradu structurali centrum, distinctio, proportio, mediatio, circulatio, dissectio, metathesis et reditus unam machinam transformationis constituunt.","refs":["MC_MHON_L1_01:ALT-B02","MC_MHON_L1_03:ALT-B02"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"Composite","passages":[]},{"id":"WB_MHON:ALT-B03","text":"In gradu metastrategico quaestio a significatione termini ad regimen conversionum transfertur, ut interpres invariantia, media et limites operationis discernat.","refs":["MC_MHON_L1_02:ALT-B03","MC_MHON_L1_03:ALT-B03"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"Composite","passages":[]},{"id":"WB_MHON:ALT-B04","text":"In gradu ontologico et temporali realitas est reticulum entium dependentium, causalitas transductio distributa, tempus autem spiralis finita mutationis et reditus.","refs":["MC_MHON_L1_01:ALT-B04","MC_MHON_L1_03:ALT-B04"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"Composite","passages":[]},{"id":"WB_MHON:ALT-B05","text":"In gradu methodologico et performativo textus figuras comprimit, dividit, convertit et recomponit, ita ut forma expositionis machinam assertam exsequatur.","refs":["MC_MHON_L1_01:ALT-B05","MC_MHON_L1_02:ALT-B05"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"Composite","passages":[]}],"original_name":"Altitude Integration"},{"number":"15","name":"Internal Tensions, Mutation Points, and Fault Lines","items":[{"id":"WB_MHON:FL-01","text":"Necessitas geometrica et amplificatio analogica diversas vires epistemicas retinent, quamquam systema eas tamquam gradus unius translationis tractat.","refs":["MC_MHON_L1_01:FL-02","MC_MHON_L1_03:FL-01"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"Tension","passages":[]},{"id":"WB_MHON:FL-02","text":"Exactitudo configurationis et identitas per transformationem tensionem efficiunt, quia regula quae mutationes legitimas a corruptione discernat non semper exhibetur.","refs":["MC_MHON_L1_01:FL-03","MC_MHON_L1_02:FL-01"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"Tension","passages":[]},{"id":"WB_MHON:FL-03","text":"Autarkia Monae, dependentia operationis ab ambitu et subordinatio creaturae principio superiori diversos exterioritatis sensus producunt qui non plene conciliantur.","refs":["MC_MHON_L1_03:XD-08","MC_MHON_L1_03:FL-02"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"Tension","passages":[]},{"id":"WB_MHON:FL-04","text":"Causalitas distribuitur inter media, receptacula et effectus, sed simul ordinem asymmetricum et hierarchicum retinet; pondus relativum nodorum incertum manet.","refs":["MC_MHON_L1_01:FL-04","MC_MHON_L1_02:FL-04"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"Tension","passages":[]},{"id":"WB_MHON:FL-05","text":"Ars effectum realem efficit, sed terminus inter reconfigurationem validam et perfectionem naturalem acquisitam instabilis manet.","refs":["MC_MHON_L1_03:DBN-03","MC_MHON_L1_03:FL-03"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"Tension","passages":[]},{"id":"WB_MHON:FL-06","text":"Disciplina initiatoria et consignatio transmissibilis simul postulantur, sed criterium communicationis quae neque secreta privat neque universaliter publicat incompletum est.","refs":["MC_MHON_L1_03:BSD-06","MC_MHON_L1_03:FL-05"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"Tension","passages":[]},{"id":"WB_MHON:FL-07","text":"Clausura finita et inexhaustio interpretationis mutuo indigent, sed incertum est utrum altera terminum ontologicum, altera solum epistemicum significet.","refs":["MC_MHON_L1_03:GA-13","MC_MHON_L1_03:XD-09"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"Tension","passages":[]},{"id":"WB_MHON:MP-01","text":"Monas mutatur ab elemento unitatis in functionem coordinationis et tandem in tabulam transductivam originis, differentiae et reditus.","refs":["MC_MHON_L1_02:RMD-01","MC_MHON_L1_03:RMD-01"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"Composite","passages":[]},{"id":"WB_MHON:MP-02","text":"Punctum mutatur ab ingrediente et centro necessario in copulam removendam, deinde in principium effusionis circularis restitutum.","refs":["MC_MHON_L1_01:MP-01","MC_MHON_L1_03:MP-01"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"Tension","passages":[]},{"id":"WB_MHON:MP-03","text":"Signum mutatur ex repraesentatione productiva in operatorium, apparatum materialem et consignationem disciplinae.","refs":["MC_MHON_L1_01:MP-03","MC_MHON_L1_03:MP-04"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"Composite","passages":[]},{"id":"WB_MHON:MP-04","text":"Numerus mutatur ex enumeratione et extensione figurae in operator qualitativum temporis, permutationis et metamorphosis.","refs":["MC_MHON_L1_01:MP-06","MC_MHON_L1_03:MP-05"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"Composite","passages":[]},{"id":"WB_MHON:MP-05","text":"Materia mutatur ex receptaculo cosmico in nodum productivum, sed eius activitas sub condicionibus situs, mensurae et influxus manet.","refs":["MC_MHON_L1_02:RMD-06","MC_MHON_L1_03:MME-02"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"Composite","passages":[]},{"id":"WB_MHON:MP-06","text":"Circulus mutatur ex figura et circulatione caelesti in motum artis, recursionem differentiae et clausuram finiti reditus.","refs":["MC_MHON_L1_03:GA-11","MC_MHON_L1_03:MP-02"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"Composite","passages":[]}],"original_name":"Internal Tensions, Mutation Points, and Fault Lines"},{"number":"16","name":"Hidden System Image","items":[{"id":"WB_MHON:HSI-01","text":"Realitas implicite est machina scriptoria et transductiva concentrica, in qua centrum formas distribuit, materia eas recipit, numerus commensurat et reditus circuitum claudit.","refs":["MC_MHON_L1_01:HSI-01","MC_MHON_L1_03:HSI-01"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"Composite","passages":[]},{"id":"WB_MHON:HSI-02","text":"Monas est centrum quod se in differentias explicat sine dispersione absoluta atque eas per proportionem ad relationem communem retrahit.","refs":["MC_MHON_L1_03:GA-01","MC_MHON_L1_03:HSI-02"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"Composite","passages":[]},{"id":"WB_MHON:HSI-03","text":"Subiectum est lector, operator et artifex initiatus qui intra machinam mensuras, tempora, instrumenta et translationes administrat.","refs":["MC_MHON_L1_01:HSI-02","MC_MHON_L1_03:HSI-03"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"Composite","passages":[]},{"id":"WB_MHON:HSI-04","text":"Tempus est spiralis finita effusionis, operationis, occultationis et reditus, cuius circuitus relationem servant sed statum eius mutant.","refs":["MC_MHON_L1_01:HSI-03","MC_MHON_L1_03:HSI-04"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"Composite","passages":[]},{"id":"WB_MHON:HSI-05","text":"Navigatio possibilis consistit in conservando canalem proportionalem inter principium et transformationes contra autonomiam partium, corruptionem configurationis et dominium causale absolutum.","refs":["MC_MHON_L1_01:HSI-04","MC_MHON_L1_03:HSI-05"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"Composite","passages":[]}],"original_name":"Hidden System Image"},{"number":"17","name":"Minimal Whole-Book Reconstruction Test","items":[{"id":"WB_MHON:MRT-B01","text":"Axiomata relationis prioris, unitatis differentiatae, formae operativae, causalitatis medialis, numeri qualitativi, artis derivatae et clausurae inexhaustae sufficiunt ad machinam libri restituendam.","refs":["MC_MHON_L1_01:MRT-B01","MC_MHON_L1_02:MRT-B01","MC_MHON_L1_03:MRT-B01"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"Composite","passages":[]},{"id":"WB_MHON:MRT-B02","text":"Structura firmissima est ubi dependentia, distinctio, mediatio et commensuratio eandem legem servant: mutatio relationem communem non delet sed manifestat.","refs":["MC_MHON_L1_01:MRT-B02","MC_MHON_L1_02:MRT-B02","MC_MHON_L1_03:MRT-B02"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"Composite","passages":[]},{"id":"WB_MHON:MRT-B03","text":"Minus determinata manet lex quae necessitatem geometricam in causalitatem cosmicam, efficaciam materialem, litteram, numerum et auctoritatem metaphysicam convertat.","refs":["MC_MHON_L1_01:MRT-B03","MC_MHON_L1_02:MRT-B03","MC_MHON_L1_03:MRT-B03"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"Tension","passages":[]},{"id":"WB_MHON:MRT-B04","text":"Systema cohaeret per reditum functionalem relationis, non per identitatem terminorum neque per deductionem univocam.","refs":["MC_MHON_L1_01:MRT-B04","MC_MHON_L1_03:MRT-B04"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"Composite","passages":[]},{"id":"WB_MHON:MRT-B05","text":"Cohaerentia nititur recursionis ordine et formae performativae vi, quae hiatus inter probationis genera saepe comprimit potius quam formaliter solvit.","refs":["MC_MHON_L1_01:MRT-B05","MC_MHON_L1_03:ALT-B05"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"Tension","passages":[]},{"id":"WB_MHON:MRT-B06","text":"Reductioni axiomaticae resistunt differentiae inter analogiam et demonstrationem, operationem artificialem et perfectionem naturalem, clausuram finitam et inexhaustionem interpretativam.","refs":["MC_MHON_L1_01:MRT-B07","MC_MHON_L1_02:MRT-B03","MC_MHON_L1_03:MRT-B03"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"Tension","passages":[]}],"original_name":"Minimal Whole-Book Reconstruction Test"}],"language_code":"la","language_name":"Latina","translations":[{"id":"CAREVAR_20261006T223753666360Z_4a5c4150","language_code":"en","language_name":"English","title":"The Hieroglyphic Monad","source_sha256":"f169ba6e36c64acdfddf502840ebd1da39862fa596e26359935155f1b90b4f1a","sha256":"6140153396f318697c50f913be4951b6e74b2e4a246c637b175d32d7ea4389b9","rendition_type":"machine","review_status":"machine","model":"gpt-5.6-terra","created":"2026-10-06T22:37:53.668261+00:00","sections":[{"number":"1","name":"Book-Scale Argumental Spine","items":[{"id":"WB_MHON:AS-B01","text":"The system asks how a minimal character can contract geometric, cosmic, material, temporal, and numerical orders into a single machine without abolishing their differences.","refs":["MC_MHON_L1_01:BAS-01","MC_MHON_L1_03:AS-01"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"Composite","passages":[]},{"id":"WB_MHON:AS-B02","text":"The Monad is not a stable object, but a relational operator that produces distinctions, distributes them through media, and gathers them through ordered return into reformed unity.","refs":["MC_MHON_L1_02:BAS-02","MC_MHON_L1_03:AS-02"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"Composite","passages":[]},{"id":"WB_MHON:AS-B03","text":"The argumentation is reconstructive and transductive: geometry separates invariants, analogy connects fields, operation tests efficacy, while number measures changes.","refs":["MC_MHON_L1_02:BAS-03","MC_MHON_L1_03:AS-03"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"Composite","passages":[]},{"id":"WB_MHON:AS-B04","text":"The movement proceeds from a manifest sign to a minimal principle, thence through cosmic and material differentiations, finally to commensuration, consignment, and finite return.","refs":["MC_MHON_L1_01:BAS-06","MC_MHON_L1_03:AS-04"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"Composite","passages":[]},{"id":"WB_MHON:AS-B05","text":"The progression is spiral, cumulative, and driven by change, because each return preserves the prior relation while increasing its field, function, or state.","refs":["MC_MHON_L1_01:BAS-04","MC_MHON_L1_03:AS-05"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"Composite","passages":[]},{"id":"WB_MHON:AS-B06","text":"Proof is not a uniformly closed deduction, but congruence among formal necessity, material mediation, correct operation, effect, and the interpreter's understanding.","refs":["MC_MHON_L1_01:BAS-05","MC_MHON_L1_03:BSD-05"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"Tension","passages":[]},{"id":"WB_MHON:AS-B07","text":"Geometrical necessity is extended into a grammar of translation, but every transition between heaven, matter, figure, and mystery requires a mediator that analogy alone does not supply.","refs":["MC_MHON_L1_01:XD-08","MC_MHON_L1_03:GA-05"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"Tension","passages":[]},{"id":"WB_MHON:AS-B08","text":"The form of exposition accomplishes its own thesis, since by dividing, converting, numbering, and recomposing figures it actually constitutes the transductive unity it affirms.","refs":["MC_MHON_L1_01:BAS-07","MC_MHON_L1_03:ALT-B05"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"Composite","passages":[]},{"id":"WB_MHON:AS-B09","text":"The closure of the whole book is double: it terminates created power in proportion, but reserves mystery and hidden relation as causes of further interpretation.","refs":["MC_MHON_L1_01:GA-14","MC_MHON_L1_03:GA-13","MC_MHON_L1_03:XD-09"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"Tension","passages":[]}],"original_name":"Book-Scale Argumental Spine"},{"number":"2","name":"Global Load-Bearing Axioms","items":[{"id":"WB_MHON:GA-01","text":"[Persistent] Form or sign does not passively imitate an already constituted thing, but manifests, distributes, and converts relations into an apparatus of operation.","refs":["MC_MHON_L1_01:GA-01","MC_MHON_L1_02:GA-01","MC_MHON_L1_03:GA-07"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"Composite","passages":[]},{"id":"WB_MHON:GA-02","text":"[Persistent] Relation or proportion precedes the identity and efficacy of terms; an element is that which can act within a generative configuration.","refs":["MC_MHON_L1_01:GA-03","MC_MHON_L1_03:GA-02"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"Composite","passages":[]},{"id":"WB_MHON:GA-03","text":"[Emergent] Monadic unity is preserved through distinction, because division produces related parts whose recomposition effects a broader unity.","refs":["MC_MHON_L1_02:GA-02","MC_MHON_L1_03:GA-04"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"Composite","passages":[]},{"id":"WB_MHON:GA-04","text":"[Mutating] Identity persists as an invariant of relation through change of position, medium, and function, not as an immobile substance.","refs":["MC_MHON_L1_01:GA-05","MC_MHON_L1_03:GA-03"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"Composite","passages":[]},{"id":"WB_MHON:GA-05","text":"[Persistent] Geometry is an ontological and transductive grammar through which position, center, section, proximity, circulation, and proportion are converted into causality.","refs":["MC_MHON_L1_01:GA-04","MC_MHON_L1_02:GA-03"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"Composite","passages":[]},{"id":"WB_MHON:GA-06","text":"[Emergent] Causality is medial, distributed, and asymmetrical: principles, figures, souls, rays, bodies, and receptacles participate in effect, but do not ground it equally.","refs":["MC_MHON_L1_02:GA-06","MC_MHON_L1_03:BSD-02"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"Composite","passages":[]},{"id":"WB_MHON:GA-07","text":"[Mutating] Matter passes from a receptive term into an active node that nourishes, imprints, reconfigures, and returns influx into circulation.","refs":["MC_MHON_L1_02:GA-09","MC_MHON_L1_03:MME-02"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"Composite","passages":[]},{"id":"WB_MHON:GA-08","text":"[Emergent] Time is not a pre-given receptacle, but the effect of a spiral of recursion through which manifestation, completion, operation, and return alter the state of relation.","refs":["MC_MHON_L1_01:GA-07","MC_MHON_L1_02:BSD-03","MC_MHON_L1_03:BSD-03"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"Composite","passages":[]},{"id":"WB_MHON:GA-09","text":"[Mutating] Number passes from external measure into a qualitative operator that orders configuration, permutation, amplification, and temporal completion.","refs":["MC_MHON_L1_01:GNX-04","MC_MHON_L1_03:GA-12"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"Composite","passages":[]},{"id":"WB_MHON:GA-10","text":"[Emergent] Partiality, subtraction, dissolution, and termination become generative when they preserve the relation of origin and produce recomposable difference.","refs":["MC_MHON_L1_01:GA-06","MC_MHON_L1_03:GA-09"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"Composite","passages":[]},{"id":"WB_MHON:GA-11","text":"[Persistent] Valid knowledge is reconstructive and operative: it traces generative relations and requires interpretation, operation, effect, and the interpreter's disposition to be congruent.","refs":["MC_MHON_L1_01:GA-10","MC_MHON_L1_02:GA-12","MC_MHON_L1_03:GA-08"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"Composite","passages":[]},{"id":"WB_MHON:GA-12","text":"[Terminal] Art is real but derivative power: it changes configurations and locally perfects powers, yet does not absolutely produce its origin, measure, or causality.","refs":["MC_MHON_L1_03:GA-06","MC_MHON_L1_03:BSD-08"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"Composite","passages":[]},{"id":"WB_MHON:GA-13","text":"[Emergent] Geometric, cosmic, material, numerical, alphabetic, and doctrinal orders are convertible codes, not causally separate regions.","refs":["MC_MHON_L1_01:GA-08","MC_MHON_L1_02:GA-07"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"Composite","passages":[]},{"id":"WB_MHON:GA-14","text":"[Terminal] Legitimate closure produces a finite restoration of proportion, but prevents semantic saturation and leaves a new interpretive circuit.","refs":["MC_MHON_L1_01:GA-14","MC_MHON_L1_03:GA-13","MC_MHON_L1_03:GA-14"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"Tension","passages":[]},{"id":"WB_MHON:GA-15","text":"[Attractor] The attractor of the whole book is proportional monadic unity, which draws back the dispersion of forms without reducing their changes to simple identity.","refs":["MC_MHON_L1_01:GA-13","MC_MHON_L1_03:GA-01","MC_MHON_L1_03:RLE-06"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"Composite","passages":[]}],"original_name":"Global Load-Bearing Axioms"},{"number":"3","name":"Recurrence, Mutation, and Displacement Matrix","items":[{"id":"WB_MHON:RMD-01","text":"The point is amplified from minimal entry into ontological center and source of flow, then excluded as a superfluous copula and restored as a center of effusion.","refs":["MC_MHON_L1_01:RMD-01","MC_MHON_L1_03:RMD-05"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"Tension","passages":[]},{"id":"WB_MHON:RMD-02","text":"The Monad changes from the name of unity into a distributed function, thence into an attractor of conversions, and finally into the internal machine of origin, process, and end.","refs":["MC_MHON_L1_01:RMD-04","MC_MHON_L1_02:RMD-01","MC_MHON_L1_03:RMD-01"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"Composite","passages":[]},{"id":"WB_MHON:RMD-03","text":"Geometry passes from a repertory of forms to a common medium of causalities, a grammar of conversion, and an instrument of transductive calibration.","refs":["MC_MHON_L1_01:XD-07","MC_MHON_L1_02:GA-03","MC_MHON_L1_03:MME-03"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"Composite","passages":[]},{"id":"WB_MHON:RMD-04","text":"The sign migrates from productive manifestation into an operator of relations, then into a materially conditioned apparatus and a transmissible consignment of discipline.","refs":["MC_MHON_L1_01:RMD-05","MC_MHON_L1_02:MP-02","MC_MHON_L1_03:MP-04"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"Composite","passages":[]},{"id":"WB_MHON:RMD-05","text":"Earth and body pass from cosmological places or receptacles into active nodes of impression, nourishment, composition, and measure.","refs":["MC_MHON_L1_01:CGD-03","MC_MHON_L1_02:RMD-06","MC_MHON_L1_03:MME-02"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"Composite","passages":[]},{"id":"WB_MHON:RMD-06","text":"Circularity changes from dependence of the periphery and cosmic circulation into a circuit of conversions, recursion of difference, and the form of finite return.","refs":["MC_MHON_L1_01:RMD-03","MC_MHON_L1_02:RMD-03","MC_MHON_L1_03:RMD-03"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"Composite","passages":[]},{"id":"WB_MHON:RMD-07","text":"Number is amplified from symbolic extension into an engine of composition, a quality of configuration, and a measure of temporal metamorphosis.","refs":["MC_MHON_L1_01:MME-05","MC_MHON_L1_02:XD-05","MC_MHON_L1_03:MME-07"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"Composite","passages":[]},{"id":"WB_MHON:RMD-08","text":"Concealment is transferred from a state of absence into an internal remainder of conversion and finally into a mystery that may be consigned but remains inexhausted.","refs":["MC_MHON_L1_01:RMD-10","MC_MHON_L1_02:RMD-08","MC_MHON_L1_03:RMD-09"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"Composite","passages":[]},{"id":"WB_MHON:RMD-09","text":"The power to act passes from conversion of dependence into interpretive operation, then is determined as real but causally limited configurative art.","refs":["MC_MHON_L1_01:BSD-06","MC_MHON_L1_02:GA-12","MC_MHON_L1_03:RMD-07"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"Composite","passages":[]},{"id":"WB_MHON:RMD-10","text":"Closure progresses from generative periphery to material and verbal impressions, then converts into a finite return that does not consume hidden relation.","refs":["MC_MHON_L1_01:BAS-08","MC_MHON_L1_02:BAS-06","MC_MHON_L1_03:GA-13","MC_MHON_L1_03:GA-14"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"Tension","passages":[]},{"id":"WB_MHON:RMD-11","text":"Pure exteriority is first denied, mediation replaces immediacy, and finally exteriority must be distinguished into illicit supplement, environmental condition, and ontological cause.","refs":["MC_MHON_L1_01:BSA-01","MC_MHON_L1_02:BSA-01","MC_MHON_L1_03:RMD-08"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"Tension","passages":[]}],"original_name":"Recurrence, Mutation, and Displacement Matrix"},{"number":"4","name":"Lower-Layer vs Current-Level Distinction","items":[{"id":"WB_MHON:CGD-01","text":"Patronage, constructions of lines, symbolic animals, and singular apparatuses remain local; only the rule ascends that exact relation is sustained by material and figurative conditions.","refs":["MC_MHON_L1_01:CGD-01","MC_MHON_L1_02:CGD-01","MC_MHON_L1_03:CGD-01"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"Direct","passages":[]},{"id":"WB_MHON:CGD-02","text":"The Sun, Moon, Mercury, and other cosmic terms do not become global substances; functions of mediation, completion, substitution, and transduction order the level of the book.","refs":["MC_MHON_L1_01:CGD-03","MC_MHON_L1_02:RMD-05","MC_MHON_L1_02:CGD-03"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"Composite","passages":[]},{"id":"WB_MHON:CGD-03","text":"The octonary, quinarity, quaternary, and XXIV retain local distinctions; only the thesis that number expresses the quality of configuration and amplification is global.","refs":["MC_MHON_L1_01:CGD-05","MC_MHON_L1_02:RMD-07","MC_MHON_L1_03:RMD-06"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"Composite","passages":[]},{"id":"WB_MHON:CGD-04","text":"Drops, impressions, particular materials, and environments must not be universalized; what ascends is active materiality whose efficacy depends on position and composition.","refs":["MC_MHON_L1_01:CGD-04","MC_MHON_L1_02:CGD-04","MC_MHON_L1_03:CGD-04"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"Composite","passages":[]},{"id":"WB_MHON:CGD-05","text":"The dependence of figure on point and of planet on relation is amplified into distributed causality, in which no term may appropriate effect to itself alone.","refs":["MC_MHON_L1_01:CGD-06","MC_MHON_L1_02:BSD-02","MC_MHON_L1_03:BSD-02"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"Composite","passages":[]},{"id":"WB_MHON:CGD-06","text":"The connection among form, time, and sign ascends into a translating machine, whose complete figure contains relation, matter, and consignment together.","refs":["MC_MHON_L1_01:CGD-07","MC_MHON_L1_02:CGD-07","MC_MHON_L1_03:XD-04"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"Composite","passages":[]},{"id":"WB_MHON:CGD-07","text":"The generative openness of prior circuits is not abolished by terminal closure, but qualified through the finitude of created causality and return to the principle.","refs":["MC_MHON_L1_01:BAS-08","MC_MHON_L1_03:GA-13"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"Tension","passages":[]},{"id":"WB_MHON:CGD-08","text":"Particular correspondences are valid as local machines of translation; they do not ascend into universal law unless mediation, measure, and necessity of transition can be demonstrated.","refs":["MC_MHON_L1_01:GNX-08","MC_MHON_L1_02:CGD-06","MC_MHON_L1_03:BSA-01"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"Tension","passages":[]}],"original_name":"Lower-Layer vs Current-Level Distinction"},{"number":"5","name":"Global Apophatic Structure","items":[{"id":"WB_MHON:GNX-01","text":"The passive, ornamental, or merely conventional sign is excluded, because figure must produce relation, condition operation, or consign discipline.","refs":["MC_MHON_L1_01:GNX-01","MC_MHON_L1_02:GNX-01","MC_MHON_L1_03:GNX-02"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"Negative","passages":[]},{"id":"WB_MHON:GNX-02","text":"Autonomy of the term is excluded: unity, figure, matter, and created agent operate only within relations of center, mediation, measure, and principle.","refs":["MC_MHON_L1_01:BSA-02","MC_MHON_L1_02:GNX-02","MC_MHON_L1_03:GNX-01"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"Negative","passages":[]},{"id":"WB_MHON:GNX-03","text":"Passive matter is excluded, since reception, nourishment, impression, and configuration constitute causal continuation.","refs":["MC_MHON_L1_02:GNX-04","MC_MHON_L1_03:GNX-01"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"Negative","passages":[]},{"id":"WB_MHON:GNX-04","text":"Fixed identity and undivided unity are excluded, because permanence is preserved only through translation of function and relation of parts.","refs":["MC_MHON_L1_01:GNX-03","MC_MHON_L1_02:DBN-02","MC_MHON_L1_03:GNX-06"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"Negative","passages":[]},{"id":"WB_MHON:GNX-05","text":"Pre-given linear time and indefinite created progress are excluded; time emerges from finite circuits of transformation and return.","refs":["MC_MHON_L1_01:DBN-04","MC_MHON_L1_03:GNX-07"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"Negative","passages":[]},{"id":"WB_MHON:GNX-06","text":"Closed disciplines and immediate sense are excluded, because understanding proceeds through conversion of codes and figurative media.","refs":["MC_MHON_L1_01:GNX-05","MC_MHON_L1_02:BSA-01"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"Negative","passages":[]},{"id":"WB_MHON:GNX-07","text":"Bare number and arithmetic separated from configuration are excluded, since numerical force arises from position, partition, and permutation.","refs":["MC_MHON_L1_01:GNX-04","MC_MHON_L1_02:DBN-04","MC_MHON_L1_03:CGD-06"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"Negative","passages":[]},{"id":"WB_MHON:GNX-08","text":"Totally closed demonstration and exhaustive interpretation are excluded, because hidden measure and terminal mystery demand further reconstruction.","refs":["MC_MHON_L1_01:BSA-04","MC_MHON_L1_02:GNX-06","MC_MHON_L1_03:BSA-05"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"Negative","passages":[]},{"id":"WB_MHON:GNX-09","text":"Absolute art is excluded: created operation can produce a real effect, but does not produce natural essence, measure, and the ground of causality from nothing.","refs":["MC_MHON_L1_03:GNX-05","MC_MHON_L1_03:XD-03"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"Negative","passages":[]}],"original_name":"Global Apophatic Structure"},{"number":"6","name":"Deep Book Apophatics","items":[{"id":"WB_MHON:DBN-01","text":"It must be false that reality contains a residue absolutely without form or things mutually indifferent, because the system places every being within effective position and relation.","refs":["MC_MHON_L1_01:DBN-01","MC_MHON_L1_02:BSA-02"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"Negative","passages":[]},{"id":"WB_MHON:DBN-02","text":"It must be false that the orders of being, signifying, producing, and measuring are fundamentally separate or convertible without a mediator.","refs":["MC_MHON_L1_01:DBN-02","MC_MHON_L1_02:DBN-01","MC_MHON_L1_03:DBN-01"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"Negative","passages":[]},{"id":"WB_MHON:DBN-03","text":"It must be false that distinction destroys unity or turns parts into autonomous substances, because division must preserve the capacity for reunion.","refs":["MC_MHON_L1_01:DBN-03","MC_MHON_L1_03:DBN-02"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"Negative","passages":[]},{"id":"WB_MHON:DBN-04","text":"It must be false that causality proceeds from a single efficient origin; effect requires principle, media, receptacle, and measure cooperating asymmetrically.","refs":["MC_MHON_L1_01:DBN-05","MC_MHON_L1_02:GNX-03","MC_MHON_L1_03:BSD-02"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"Negative","passages":[]},{"id":"WB_MHON:DBN-05","text":"It must be false that absence, dislocation, subtraction, and dissolution are sterile privations, because ordered negation generates distinction and the following circuit.","refs":["MC_MHON_L1_01:DBN-06","MC_MHON_L1_03:XD-02"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"Negative","passages":[]},{"id":"WB_MHON:DBN-06","text":"It must be false that a creature can confer upon itself origin, measure, and infinite continuation; otherwise derivative art would be converted into absolute causality.","refs":["MC_MHON_L1_03:BSA-02","MC_MHON_L1_03:GA-06"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"Negative","passages":[]},{"id":"WB_MHON:DBN-07","text":"It must be false that either bare analogy or external deduction alone provides sufficient proof; the system requires congruence among relation, operation, and effect.","refs":["MC_MHON_L1_01:XD-08","MC_MHON_L1_03:XD-07"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"Tension","passages":[]}],"original_name":"Deep Book Apophatics"},{"number":"7","name":"Super-Apophatics of the Whole","items":[{"id":"WB_MHON:BSA-01","text":"Pure exteriority is unavailable to relations, but the creator is retained as ultimate ontological cause; this asymmetry of exteriority orders the system without full resolution.","refs":["MC_MHON_L1_01:BSA-01","MC_MHON_L1_03:BSA-04"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"Tension","passages":[]},{"id":"WB_MHON:BSA-02","text":"Immediately evident sense is impossible, because no external perspective possesses the whole Monad without conversion, discipline, and the interpreter's disposition.","refs":["MC_MHON_L1_02:BSA-01","MC_MHON_L1_03:BSA-03"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"Negative","passages":[]},{"id":"WB_MHON:BSA-03","text":"Absolute autonomy of either term or operator is ontologically inadmissible; power is always exercised within dependence and measure.","refs":["MC_MHON_L1_01:BSA-02","MC_MHON_L1_02:BSA-04"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"Negative","passages":[]},{"id":"WB_MHON:BSA-04","text":"A universal law that perfectly converts analogy, geometry, material causality, and cosmic authority is desired, but never fully available.","refs":["MC_MHON_L1_01:BSA-04","MC_MHON_L1_03:BSA-01"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"Tension","passages":[]},{"id":"WB_MHON:BSA-05","text":"Terminal interpretive saturation is impossible: completion terminates structure, but mystery and hidden relation reopen the course of operation.","refs":["MC_MHON_L1_03:GA-14","MC_MHON_L1_03:BSA-05"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"Negative","passages":[]},{"id":"WB_MHON:BSA-06","text":"Infinite created progress is unavailable, because every legitimate power requires a limit, restoration of proportion, and relation to the principle.","refs":["MC_MHON_L1_03:GNX-07","MC_MHON_L1_03:GA-13"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"Negative","passages":[]}],"original_name":"Super-Apophatics of the Whole"},{"number":"8","name":"Cross-Unit Derivative Propositions","items":[{"id":"WB_MHON:XD-01","text":"The Monad is an invariant of relation through transformations: its unity does not precede distinctions as a simple substance, but is effected by their coordination.","refs":["MC_MHON_L1_01:XD-01","MC_MHON_L1_02:GA-04","MC_MHON_L1_03:RMD-01","WB_MHON:GA-02","WB_MHON:GA-03","WB_MHON:GA-04","WB_MHON:GA-15"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"Composite","passages":[]},{"id":"WB_MHON:XD-02","text":"The complete sign simultaneously manifests, operates, and consigns; signification is therefore the effect of a relational and material apparatus, not an addition external to operation.","refs":["MC_MHON_L1_01:XD-03","MC_MHON_L1_02:GA-01","MC_MHON_L1_03:XD-04","WB_MHON:GA-01","WB_MHON:GA-13"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"Composite","passages":[]},{"id":"WB_MHON:XD-03","text":"Material efficacy consists neither in passive reception nor in absolute art, but in circulation among given power, active receptacle, and measured reconfiguration.","refs":["MC_MHON_L1_02:GA-09","MC_MHON_L1_03:XD-03","MC_MHON_L1_03:GA-10","WB_MHON:GA-06","WB_MHON:GA-07","WB_MHON:GA-12"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"Composite","passages":[]},{"id":"WB_MHON:XD-04","text":"The time of the whole book is a finite spiral: it composes ontological regression, convertible revolution, and termination of created power in the same form of recursion.","refs":["MC_MHON_L1_01:BSD-03","MC_MHON_L1_02:BSD-03","MC_MHON_L1_03:BSD-03","WB_MHON:GA-08","WB_MHON:GA-14"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"Composite","passages":[]},{"id":"WB_MHON:XD-05","text":"Negative difference becomes a principle of production, because partiality, separation, dissection, and subtraction open a repertory of relations to be composed.","refs":["MC_MHON_L1_01:XD-04","MC_MHON_L1_02:RMD-02","MC_MHON_L1_03:XD-02","WB_MHON:GA-03","WB_MHON:GA-10"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"Composite","passages":[]},{"id":"WB_MHON:XD-06","text":"Valid proof is a reconstruction of congruence among the genealogy of form, series of conversions, correct operation, and understood effect.","refs":["MC_MHON_L1_01:BSD-05","MC_MHON_L1_02:BSD-06","MC_MHON_L1_03:XD-07","WB_MHON:GA-05","WB_MHON:GA-11"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"Composite","passages":[]},{"id":"WB_MHON:XD-07","text":"Number formalizes the quality of relation and effects conversion among figure, matter, letter, and time, but does not deduce it from quantity alone.","refs":["MC_MHON_L1_01:MME-05","MC_MHON_L1_02:XD-05","MC_MHON_L1_03:XD-06","WB_MHON:GA-09","WB_MHON:GA-13"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"Composite","passages":[]},{"id":"WB_MHON:XD-08","text":"Closure has a double status: it constitutes the end of operation and retroactively effects the condition of a new interpretive circuit.","refs":["MC_MHON_L1_01:BSA-04","MC_MHON_L1_02:XD-07","MC_MHON_L1_03:XD-09","WB_MHON:GA-14"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"Tension","passages":[]},{"id":"WB_MHON:XD-09","text":"Analogical translation is at once an instrument of amplification and a fragile epistemic seam, because it increases the system's scope without a law of conversion demonstrated everywhere.","refs":["MC_MHON_L1_01:XD-08","MC_MHON_L1_02:FL-03","MC_MHON_L1_03:FL-01","WB_MHON:GA-05","WB_MHON:GA-11","WB_MHON:GA-13"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"Tension","passages":[]},{"id":"WB_MHON:XD-10","text":"The power of the subject is configurative administration of dependencies: reader, operator, and artisan act by selecting, dividing, and composing, not by usurping the origin of causality.","refs":["MC_MHON_L1_01:BSD-06","MC_MHON_L1_02:BSD-04","MC_MHON_L1_03:BSD-08","WB_MHON:GA-11","WB_MHON:GA-12"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"Composite","passages":[]}],"original_name":"Cross-Unit Derivative Propositions"},{"number":"9","name":"Book-Scale Super-Derivatives","items":[{"id":"WB_MHON:BSD-01","text":"The ontology of the book constitutes reality as a field of transductions, in which things are relatively stable states of formal, material, numerical, and symbolic circuits.","refs":["MC_MHON_L1_01:BSD-01","MC_MHON_L1_02:BSD-01","MC_MHON_L1_03:BSD-01","WB_MHON:GA-02","WB_MHON:GA-04","WB_MHON:GA-13"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"Composite","passages":[]},{"id":"WB_MHON:BSD-02","text":"Causality becomes a syntax of asymmetrical mediations: principle sustains, figure orders, medium transfers, matter prolongs, and measure limits effect.","refs":["MC_MHON_L1_01:BSD-02","MC_MHON_L1_02:BSD-02","MC_MHON_L1_03:BSD-02","WB_MHON:GA-05","WB_MHON:GA-06","WB_MHON:GA-07"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"Composite","passages":[]},{"id":"WB_MHON:BSD-03","text":"Semiosis is a causally efficacious circuit, because the same configuration manifests a thing, condenses material effect, and institutes a transmissible form.","refs":["MC_MHON_L1_01:MME-02","MC_MHON_L1_02:BSD-05","MC_MHON_L1_03:XD-04","WB_MHON:GA-01","WB_MHON:GA-13"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"Composite","passages":[]},{"id":"WB_MHON:BSD-04","text":"Time does not measure the machine extrinsically, but is generated from its differentiations, conversions, local exhaustions, and finite returns.","refs":["MC_MHON_L1_01:BSD-03","MC_MHON_L1_02:BSD-03","MC_MHON_L1_03:BSD-03","WB_MHON:GA-08","WB_MHON:GA-14"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"Composite","passages":[]},{"id":"WB_MHON:BSD-05","text":"The subject is an operator-interpreter constituted within the machine, who administers relations but becomes neither external spectator nor causal master.","refs":["MC_MHON_L1_01:BSD-04","MC_MHON_L1_02:BSD-04","MC_MHON_L1_03:BSD-04","WB_MHON:GA-11","WB_MHON:GA-12"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"Composite","passages":[]},{"id":"WB_MHON:BSD-06","text":"Freedom is the capacity for configuration under measure: the power to act grows through correct mediation and selection, not through exemption from dependence.","refs":["MC_MHON_L1_01:BSD-06","MC_MHON_L1_03:BSD-08","WB_MHON:GA-06","WB_MHON:GA-12"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"Composite","passages":[]},{"id":"WB_MHON:BSD-07","text":"The history of this system consists of local closures that complete effect, terminate power, and open a new extraction under hidden relation.","refs":["MC_MHON_L1_01:GA-14","MC_MHON_L1_03:BSD-07","WB_MHON:GA-08","WB_MHON:GA-14"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"Composite","passages":[]},{"id":"WB_MHON:BSD-08","text":"Truth becomes transmodal concordance among invariant, apparatus, operation, and effect, but its force diminishes where analogical transition does not exhibit a common rule.","refs":["MC_MHON_L1_01:BAS-05","MC_MHON_L1_03:BSD-05","MC_MHON_L1_03:FL-01","WB_MHON:GA-05","WB_MHON:GA-11"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"Tension","passages":[]},{"id":"WB_MHON:BSD-09","text":"Epistemic authority is simultaneously restricted and distributed: initiation orders the quality of access, while consignment transmits operation beyond the master's presence.","refs":["MC_MHON_L1_01:FL-05","MC_MHON_L1_03:BSD-06","WB_MHON:GA-01","WB_MHON:GA-11"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"Tension","passages":[]}],"original_name":"Book-Scale Super-Derivatives"},{"number":"10","name":"Recursive Loops, Escalation Paths, and Conceptual Attractors","items":[{"id":"WB_MHON:RLE-01","text":"The compression of forms into unity generates differentiation; differences are proportioned, recomposed, and return to a unity that already contains new division.","refs":["MC_MHON_L1_01:RLE-01","MC_MHON_L1_02:RLE-01","MC_MHON_L1_03:RLE-07"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"Composite","passages":[]},{"id":"WB_MHON:RLE-02","text":"The center constitutes the periphery, the periphery requires motion, motion is impressed into matter, and the impression is referred back to the center as a sign of cause.","refs":["MC_MHON_L1_01:RLE-02","MC_MHON_L1_02:XD-03","MC_MHON_L1_03:XD-04"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"Composite","passages":[]},{"id":"WB_MHON:RLE-03","text":"Unity begets distinction, distinction demands proportion, proportion effects conjunction, and conjunction restores unity as a capacity for further difference.","refs":["MC_MHON_L1_02:RLE-01","MC_MHON_L1_03:XD-09"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"Composite","passages":[]},{"id":"WB_MHON:RLE-04","text":"Dislocation or dissolution opens a repertory of parts; measured composition restores order, but restoration produces new difference.","refs":["MC_MHON_L1_01:RLE-04","MC_MHON_L1_03:RMD-04"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"Composite","passages":[]},{"id":"WB_MHON:RLE-05","text":"Latency is resolved through manifestation, but manifest effect produces a hidden remainder that incites further interpretation and conversion.","refs":["MC_MHON_L1_01:RLE-06","MC_MHON_L1_02:RMD-08","MC_MHON_L1_03:RMD-09"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"Composite","passages":[]},{"id":"WB_MHON:RLE-06","text":"Numerical amplification transfers figure into letter, power, and time; each stage promises greater order but demands a new rule of conversion.","refs":["MC_MHON_L1_01:RLE-05","MC_MHON_L1_02:XD-05","MC_MHON_L1_03:RLE-08"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"Tension","passages":[]},{"id":"WB_MHON:RLE-07","text":"Substitution of a medium preserves the circuit when a particular term fails, because function can be transferred among Moon, Mercury, soul, Spirit, Earth, or code.","refs":["MC_MHON_L1_02:RLE-04","MC_MHON_L1_02:HSI-05","MC_MHON_L1_03:GA-05"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"Composite","passages":[]},{"id":"WB_MHON:RLE-08","text":"The Monad, proportion, Mercury, and inexhaustion constitute different attractors: the first three gather dispersion, while the last prevents gathering from closing into full possession.","refs":["MC_MHON_L1_02:RLE-06","MC_MHON_L1_03:RLE-06","MC_MHON_L1_03:RLE-07"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"Composite","passages":[]},{"id":"WB_MHON:RLE-09","text":"The limit stops operation, restores proportion, and retroactively manifests the principle; the same closure, insofar as it preserves mystery, becomes a point of re-entry.","refs":["MC_MHON_L1_02:RLE-07","MC_MHON_L1_03:GA-13","MC_MHON_L1_03:XD-09"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"Tension","passages":[]}],"original_name":"Recursive Loops, Escalation Paths, and Conceptual Attractors"},{"number":"11","name":"Formal Generative Schema of the Whole Book","items":[{"id":"WB_MHON:FGS-B01","text":"Manifest sign → reduction to a minimal geometric repertory → discovery of generative relation.","refs":["MC_MHON_L1_01:BAS-06","MC_MHON_L1_01:BAS-03"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"Composite","passages":[]},{"id":"WB_MHON:FGS-B02","text":"Point or center → extension and periphery → unity distinguished into operative parts.","refs":["MC_MHON_L1_01:BAS-03","MC_MHON_L1_02:GA-02"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"Composite","passages":[]},{"id":"WB_MHON:FGS-B03","text":"Configured distinction → position, section, and proximity → invisible proportion → relational identity.","refs":["MC_MHON_L1_02:RMD-02","MC_MHON_L1_03:GA-02"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"Composite","passages":[]},{"id":"WB_MHON:FGS-B04","text":"Configuration → cosmic and spiritual mediation → circulation of function → translation among codes.","refs":["MC_MHON_L1_02:GA-06","MC_MHON_L1_03:GA-05"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"Composite","passages":[]},{"id":"WB_MHON:FGS-B05","text":"Configured influx → active material reception → nourishment, impression, and causal continuation.","refs":["MC_MHON_L1_02:GA-09","MC_MHON_L1_03:CGD-04"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"Composite","passages":[]},{"id":"WB_MHON:FGS-B06","text":"Composite body → dissection and subtraction → repertory of parts → metathesis and recompositive art.","refs":["MC_MHON_L1_03:RMD-04","MC_MHON_L1_03:RMD-07"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"Composite","passages":[]},{"id":"WB_MHON:FGS-B07","text":"Proportioned recomposition → geometrical commensuration → qualitative number → temporal amplification.","refs":["MC_MHON_L1_03:GA-12","MC_MHON_L1_03:XD-06"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"Composite","passages":[]},{"id":"WB_MHON:FGS-B08","text":"Coordinated effect → Light, Word, or complete figure → material and doctrinal consignment.","refs":["MC_MHON_L1_02:GA-10","MC_MHON_L1_03:GA-07"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"Composite","passages":[]},{"id":"WB_MHON:FGS-B09","text":"Exhaustion of created power → restoration of proportion → return to principle → preserved mystery → new interpretive circuit.","refs":["MC_MHON_L1_03:GA-13","MC_MHON_L1_03:GA-14","MC_MHON_L1_03:BSA-04"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"Tension","passages":[]}],"original_name":"Formal Generative Schema of the Whole Book"},{"number":"12","name":"Whole-Book Argument Flow Map","items":[{"id":"WB_MHON:AFM-B01","text":"Manifest character → Point-Monad → center and periphery → cosmic distribution → material mediation → operative figure → transformative number → finite return.","refs":["MC_MHON_L1_01:BAS-01","MC_MHON_L1_03:AS-04"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"Composite","passages":[]},{"id":"WB_MHON:AFM-B02","text":"Relational unity → differentiation → proportion → translation among codes → impression and effect → measured recomposition → reread unity.","refs":["MC_MHON_L1_02:BAS-04","MC_MHON_L1_03:GA-02"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"Composite","passages":[]},{"id":"WB_MHON:AFM-B03","text":"Negation of autonomy → activation of partiality → distribution of causality → limitation of art → termination of created power → relation to principle.","refs":["MC_MHON_L1_01:BSA-02","MC_MHON_L1_03:GNX-05"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"Negative","passages":[]},{"id":"WB_MHON:AFM-B04","text":"Geometrical necessity → analogical translation → operative proof → congruence of effect → local closure → hidden remainder → new reconstruction.","refs":["MC_MHON_L1_01:XD-08","MC_MHON_L1_03:XD-07"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"Tension","passages":[]}],"original_name":"Whole-Book Argument Flow Map"},{"number":"13","name":"Macro Map-Effects","items":[{"id":"WB_MHON:MME-01","text":"Geometry is reclassified from an instrument of description into an ontological grammar, configurative causality, and a means of calibration among fields.","refs":["MC_MHON_L1_01:MME-01","MC_MHON_L1_03:MME-03"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"Composite","passages":[]},{"id":"WB_MHON:MME-02","text":"The Monad is reclassified from simple unity into a transductive center that produces distinctions, gathers conversions, and orders their limits.","refs":["MC_MHON_L1_02:BAS-05","MC_MHON_L1_03:MME-01"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"Composite","passages":[]},{"id":"WB_MHON:MME-03","text":"Matter is reclassified from passive receptacle into the co-causal condition of impression, nourishment, operation, and verification.","refs":["MC_MHON_L1_02:GNX-04","MC_MHON_L1_03:MME-02"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"Composite","passages":[]},{"id":"WB_MHON:MME-04","text":"Number is reclassified from abstract quantity into a qualitative matrix that coordinates functions, permutations, times, and metamorphoses.","refs":["MC_MHON_L1_01:MME-05","MC_MHON_L1_03:MME-07"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"Composite","passages":[]},{"id":"WB_MHON:MME-05","text":"Time is reclassified from external succession into the effect of recursion, conversion, and finite restoration of power.","refs":["MC_MHON_L1_01:MME-04","MC_MHON_L1_03:BSD-03"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"Composite","passages":[]},{"id":"WB_MHON:MME-06","text":"The opposition between sign and thing collapses, because the same figure effects manifestation, material operation, and transmission of doctrine.","refs":["MC_MHON_L1_01:MME-02","MC_MHON_L1_03:XD-04"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"Composite","passages":[]},{"id":"WB_MHON:MME-07","text":"The opposition of nature and art is recoded: art configures and orders natural powers, but does not causally usurp natural perfection.","refs":["MC_MHON_L1_03:XD-03","MC_MHON_L1_03:MME-05"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"Composite","passages":[]},{"id":"WB_MHON:MME-08","text":"Reading is reclassified from sight or extraction of sense into reconstructive participation that divides figures, tests operations, and separates invariants from contingencies.","refs":["MC_MHON_L1_01:MME-08","MC_MHON_L1_03:MME-08"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"Composite","passages":[]},{"id":"WB_MHON:MME-09","text":"The boundaries of disciplines are reclassified as channels of translation, so that differences of codes are preserved without ontological autonomy.","refs":["MC_MHON_L1_01:MME-07","MC_MHON_L1_02:GA-07"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"Composite","passages":[]}],"original_name":"Macro Map-Effects"},{"number":"14","name":"Altitude Integration","items":[{"id":"WB_MHON:ALT-B01","text":"At the surface level, point, line, circle, Sun, Moon, Mercury, Earth, cross, letters, animals, and numbers appear as determinate objects.","refs":["MC_MHON_L1_01:ALT-B01","MC_MHON_L1_03:ALT-B01"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"Composite","passages":[]},{"id":"WB_MHON:ALT-B02","text":"At the structural level, center, distinction, proportion, mediation, circulation, dissection, metathesis, and return constitute a single machine of transformation.","refs":["MC_MHON_L1_01:ALT-B02","MC_MHON_L1_03:ALT-B02"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"Composite","passages":[]},{"id":"WB_MHON:ALT-B03","text":"At the metastrategic level, the question shifts from the meaning of a term to the governance of conversions, so that the interpreter may discern invariants, media, and limits of operation.","refs":["MC_MHON_L1_02:ALT-B03","MC_MHON_L1_03:ALT-B03"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"Composite","passages":[]},{"id":"WB_MHON:ALT-B04","text":"At the ontological and temporal level, reality is a network of dependent beings, causality is distributed transduction, while time is a finite spiral of change and return.","refs":["MC_MHON_L1_01:ALT-B04","MC_MHON_L1_03:ALT-B04"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"Composite","passages":[]},{"id":"WB_MHON:ALT-B05","text":"At the methodological and performative level, the text compresses, divides, converts, and recomposes figures, such that the form of exposition executes the asserted machine.","refs":["MC_MHON_L1_01:ALT-B05","MC_MHON_L1_02:ALT-B05"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"Composite","passages":[]}],"original_name":"Altitude Integration"},{"number":"15","name":"Internal Tensions, Mutation Points, and Fault Lines","items":[{"id":"WB_MHON:FL-01","text":"Geometrical necessity and analogical amplification retain different epistemic forces, although the system treats them as stages of a single translation.","refs":["MC_MHON_L1_01:FL-02","MC_MHON_L1_03:FL-01"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"Tension","passages":[]},{"id":"WB_MHON:FL-02","text":"Exactitude of configuration and identity through transformation produce tension, because the rule that distinguishes legitimate changes from corruption is not always exhibited.","refs":["MC_MHON_L1_01:FL-03","MC_MHON_L1_02:FL-01"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"Tension","passages":[]},{"id":"WB_MHON:FL-03","text":"The autarky of the Monad, the dependence of operation on environment, and the subordination of the creature to a higher principle produce different senses of exteriority that are not fully reconciled.","refs":["MC_MHON_L1_03:XD-08","MC_MHON_L1_03:FL-02"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"Tension","passages":[]},{"id":"WB_MHON:FL-04","text":"Causality is distributed among media, receptacles, and effects, but at the same time retains an asymmetrical and hierarchical order; the relative weight of nodes remains uncertain.","refs":["MC_MHON_L1_01:FL-04","MC_MHON_L1_02:FL-04"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"Tension","passages":[]},{"id":"WB_MHON:FL-05","text":"Art produces real effect, but the boundary between valid reconfiguration and acquired natural perfection remains unstable.","refs":["MC_MHON_L1_03:DBN-03","MC_MHON_L1_03:FL-03"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"Tension","passages":[]},{"id":"WB_MHON:FL-06","text":"Initiatory discipline and transmissible consignment are simultaneously required, but the criterion of communication that neither privatizes as secret nor makes universally public remains incomplete.","refs":["MC_MHON_L1_03:BSD-06","MC_MHON_L1_03:FL-05"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"Tension","passages":[]},{"id":"WB_MHON:FL-07","text":"Finite closure and inexhaustion of interpretation mutually require one another, but it is uncertain whether one signifies an ontological limit, the other only an epistemic one.","refs":["MC_MHON_L1_03:GA-13","MC_MHON_L1_03:XD-09"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"Tension","passages":[]},{"id":"WB_MHON:MP-01","text":"The Monad changes from an element of unity into a function of coordination and finally into a transductive map of origin, difference, and return.","refs":["MC_MHON_L1_02:RMD-01","MC_MHON_L1_03:RMD-01"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"Composite","passages":[]},{"id":"WB_MHON:MP-02","text":"The point changes from entry and necessary center into a removable copula, then into a restored principle of circular effusion.","refs":["MC_MHON_L1_01:MP-01","MC_MHON_L1_03:MP-01"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"Tension","passages":[]},{"id":"WB_MHON:MP-03","text":"The sign changes from productive representation into an operator, a material apparatus, and a consignment of discipline.","refs":["MC_MHON_L1_01:MP-03","MC_MHON_L1_03:MP-04"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"Composite","passages":[]},{"id":"WB_MHON:MP-04","text":"Number changes from enumeration and extension of figure into a qualitative operator of time, permutation, and metamorphosis.","refs":["MC_MHON_L1_01:MP-06","MC_MHON_L1_03:MP-05"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"Composite","passages":[]},{"id":"WB_MHON:MP-05","text":"Matter changes from cosmic receptacle into a productive node, but its activity remains under the conditions of position, measure, and influx.","refs":["MC_MHON_L1_02:RMD-06","MC_MHON_L1_03:MME-02"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"Composite","passages":[]},{"id":"WB_MHON:MP-06","text":"The circle changes from figure and celestial circulation into the motion of art, recursion of difference, and closure of finite return.","refs":["MC_MHON_L1_03:GA-11","MC_MHON_L1_03:MP-02"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"Composite","passages":[]}],"original_name":"Internal Tensions, Mutation Points, and Fault Lines"},{"number":"16","name":"Hidden System Image","items":[{"id":"WB_MHON:HSI-01","text":"Reality is implicitly a concentric writing and transductive machine, in which the center distributes forms, matter receives them, number commensurates, and return closes the circuit.","refs":["MC_MHON_L1_01:HSI-01","MC_MHON_L1_03:HSI-01"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"Composite","passages":[]},{"id":"WB_MHON:HSI-02","text":"The Monad is a center that unfolds itself into differences without absolute dispersion and draws them back through proportion to common relation.","refs":["MC_MHON_L1_03:GA-01","MC_MHON_L1_03:HSI-02"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"Composite","passages":[]},{"id":"WB_MHON:HSI-03","text":"The subject is the initiated reader, operator, and artisan who administers measures, times, instruments, and translations within the machine.","refs":["MC_MHON_L1_01:HSI-02","MC_MHON_L1_03:HSI-03"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"Composite","passages":[]},{"id":"WB_MHON:HSI-04","text":"Time is a finite spiral of effusion, operation, concealment, and return, whose circuits preserve relation but alter its state.","refs":["MC_MHON_L1_01:HSI-03","MC_MHON_L1_03:HSI-04"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"Composite","passages":[]},{"id":"WB_MHON:HSI-05","text":"Possible navigation consists in preserving a proportional channel between principle and transformations against the autonomy of parts, corruption of configuration, and absolute causal dominion.","refs":["MC_MHON_L1_01:HSI-04","MC_MHON_L1_03:HSI-05"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"Composite","passages":[]}],"original_name":"Hidden System Image"},{"number":"17","name":"Minimal Whole-Book Reconstruction Test","items":[{"id":"WB_MHON:MRT-B01","text":"The axioms of prior relation, differentiated unity, operative form, medial causality, qualitative number, derivative art, and inexhausted closure suffice to restore the machine of the book.","refs":["MC_MHON_L1_01:MRT-B01","MC_MHON_L1_02:MRT-B01","MC_MHON_L1_03:MRT-B01"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"Composite","passages":[]},{"id":"WB_MHON:MRT-B02","text":"The firmest structure is where dependence, distinction, mediation, and commensuration preserve the same law: change does not erase common relation but manifests it.","refs":["MC_MHON_L1_01:MRT-B02","MC_MHON_L1_02:MRT-B02","MC_MHON_L1_03:MRT-B02"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"Composite","passages":[]},{"id":"WB_MHON:MRT-B03","text":"Less determined remains the law that converts geometrical necessity into cosmic causality, material efficacy, letter, number, and metaphysical authority.","refs":["MC_MHON_L1_01:MRT-B03","MC_MHON_L1_02:MRT-B03","MC_MHON_L1_03:MRT-B03"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"Tension","passages":[]},{"id":"WB_MHON:MRT-B04","text":"The system coheres through the functional return of relation, not through identity of terms nor through univocal deduction.","refs":["MC_MHON_L1_01:MRT-B04","MC_MHON_L1_03:MRT-B04"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"Composite","passages":[]},{"id":"WB_MHON:MRT-B05","text":"Coherence relies on the order of recursion and the force of performative form, which often compresses rather than formally resolves the gaps among kinds of proof.","refs":["MC_MHON_L1_01:MRT-B05","MC_MHON_L1_03:ALT-B05"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"Tension","passages":[]},{"id":"WB_MHON:MRT-B06","text":"Differences between analogy and demonstration, artificial operation and natural perfection, finite closure and interpretive inexhaustion resist axiomatic reduction.","refs":["MC_MHON_L1_01:MRT-B07","MC_MHON_L1_02:MRT-B03","MC_MHON_L1_03:MRT-B03"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"Tension","passages":[]}],"original_name":"Minimal Whole-Book Reconstruction Test"}],"revision":"WB_MHON@R002","source_variant_id":"canonical:WB_MHON:WB_MHON@R002","source_language_code":"la","original_title":"Monas Hieroglyphica"}],"identity":{"layer":"WB","kicker":"Whole book","title":"The Hieroglyphic Monad","context":"","original_title":"Monas Hieroglyphica"},"original_title":"Monas Hieroglyphica","title_language_code":"en"}],"seal":"SEAL_MHON_E70740882C0756CA2C1F","version_work_id":"WORK_MONAS_HIEROGLYPHICA_JOHN_DEE","version_label":"Added 6 Oct 2026 · Run 6 Oct 2026 · gpt-5.6-terra / gpt-5.6-sol","version_revision":"52890843bb7130a42de40b64394d2e7eab31e3577aafbe5984ab7b55769c1f01","original_title":"Monas Hieroglyphica","title_language_code":"en","title_translation":{"schema":"care-title-heading-translation/1","language_code":"en","rendition_type":"machine","originals_preserved":true}},{"id":"WORK_STORE_EUROPEAN_SPINOZA_ETHICA","slug":"beta-cb20d40c005d7d22","title":"Ethics","author":"Baruch Spinoza","edition":"Latin text, Charles Appuhn edition, Garnier 1913; Wikisource transcription","year":"1677","description":"","cover_url":"./assets/covers/beta-cb20d40c005d7d22-d25aa60e1847c527.png","source_url":"https://la.wikisource.org/wiki/Ethica","source_notices":{"provider":"Wikisource originals","source_url":"https://la.wikisource.org/wiki/Ethica","licence":"https://creativecommons.org/licenses/by-sa/4.0/","statement":"CC BY-SA 4.0 transcription; underlying edition recorded separately"},"language_code":"la","language_name":"Latina","sources":[{"id":"RAW_QGOH_PPDD","title":"Part One - Concerning God · passage 1","order":1,"chapter_id":"CH_QGOH_PPDD","text":"## ETHICES\n\n## PARS PRIMA\n\n## DE DEO\n\nDEFINITIONES\n\nI. Per causam sui intelligo id, cujus essentia involvit existentiam ; sive id, cujus natura non potest concipi nisi existens.\n\nII. Ea res dicitur in suo genere finita, quæ alià ejusdem naturæ terminari potest. Ex. gr. corpus dicitur finitum, quia aliud semper majus concipimus. Sic cogitatio aliâ cogitatione terminatur. At corpus non terminatur cogitatione ; nec cogitatio corpore.\n\nIII. Per substantiam intelligo id, quod in se est, et per se concipitur, hoc est id, cujus conceptus non indiget conceptus alterius rei, a quo formari debeat.\n\nIV. Per attributum intelligo id, quod intellectus de substantia percipit tanquam ejusdem essentiam constituens.\n\nV. Per modum intelligo substantiæ affectiones, sive id, quod in alio est, per quod etiam concipitur.\n\nVI. Per Deum intelligo ens absolute infinitum, hoc est, substantiam constantem infinitis attributis, quorum unumquodque æternam et infinitam essentiam exprimit.\n\nEXPLICATIO\n\nDico absolute infinitum, non autem in suo genere ; quicquid enim in suo genere tantum infinitum est, infinita de eo attributa negare possumus ; quod autem absolute infinitum est, ad ejus essentiam pertinet quicquid essentiam exprimit et negationem nullam involvit.\n\nVII. Ea res libera dicetur, quae ex sola suae naturæ necessitate existit, et a se sola ad agendum determinatur : Necessaria autem vel potius coacta, quæ ab alio determinatur ad existendum et operandum certa ac determinata ratione.\n\nVIII. Per æternitatem intelligo ipsam existentiam, quatenus ex sola rei æternæ definitione necessario sequi concipitur.\n\nEXPLICATIO\n\nTalis enim existentia ut æterna veritas, sicut rei essentia, concipitur, proptereaque per durationem aut tempus explicari non potest, tametsi duratio principio et fine carere concipiatur.\n\nAXIOMATA\n\nI. Omnia, quæ sunt, vel in se vel in alio sunt.\n\nII. Id, quod per aliud non potest concipi, per se concipi debet.\n\nIII. Ex data causa determinata necessario sequitur effectus, et contra, si nulla detur determinata causa, impossibile est, ut effectus sequatur.\n\nIV. Effectus cognitio a cognitione causæ dependet, et eandem involvit.\n\nV. Quæ nihil commune cum se invicem habent, etiam per se invicem intelligi non possunt, sive conceptus unius alterius conceptum non involvit.\n\nVI. Idea vera debet cum suo ideato convenire.\n\nVII. Quicquid ut non existens potest concipi, ejus essentia non involvit existentiam.\n\nPROPOSITIO I\n\nSubstantia prior est natura suis affectionibus.\n\nDEMONSTRATIO\n\npatet ex Definitione 3 et 5\n\nPROPOSITIO II\n\nDuæ substantiæ, diversa attributa habentes, nihil inter se commune habent.\n\nDEMONSTRATIO\n\npatet etiam ex Defin. 3. Unaquæque enim in se debet esse, et per se debet concipi, sive conceptus unius conceptum alterius non involvit.\n\nPROPOSITIO III\n\nQuæ res nihil commune inter se habent, earum una alterius causa esse non potest.\n\nDEMONSTRATIO\n\nSi nihil commune cum se invicem habent, ergo (per Axiom. 5), nec per se invicem possunt intelligi, adeoque (per Axiom. 4) una alterius causa esse non potest. Q. E. D.\n\nPROPOSITIO IV\n\nDuæ aut plures res distinctæ, vel inter se distinguuntur ex diversitate attributorum substantiarum, vel ex diversitate earundem affectionum.\n\nDEMONSTRATIO\n\nOmnia, quæ sunt, vel in se vel in alio sunt (per Axiom. 1), hoc est (per Defin. 3 et 5), extra intellectum nihil datur præter substantias earumque affectiones. Nihil ergo extra intellectum datur, per quod plures res distingui inter se possunt, præter substantias, sive quod idem est (per Defin. 4), earum attributa, earumque affectiones. Q. E. D.\n\nPROPOSITIO V\n\nIn rerum natura non possunt dari duæ aut plures substantiæ ejusdem naturæ sive attributi.\n\nDEMONSTRATIO\n\nSi darentur plures distinctæ, deberent inter se distingui vel ex diversitate attributorum, vel ex diversitate affectionum (per Prop. pæced.) Si tantum ex diversitate attributorum, concedetur ergo, non dari nisi unam ejusdem attributi. At si ex diversitate affectionum, cum substantia sit prior natura suis affectionibus (per Prop. I), depositis ergo affectionibus, et in se considerata, hoc est (per Defin. 3 et Axiom. 6) vere considerata, non poterit concipi ab alia distingui hoc est (per Prop. præced.), non poterunt dari plures, sed tantum una. Q. E. D.\n\nPROPOSITIO VI\n\nUna substantia non potest produci ab alia substantia.\n\nDEMONSTRATIO\n\nIn rerum natura non possunt dari duæ substantiæ ejusdem attributi (per Prop. præced.), hoc est (per Prop. 2), quæ aliquid inter se commune habent. Adeoque (per Prop. 3), una alterius causa esse nequit, sive una ab alia non potest produci, Q. E. D.\n\nCOROLLARIUM\n\nHinc sequitur, substantiam ab alio produci non posse. Nam in rerum natura nihil datur præter substantias earumque affectiones, ut patet ex Axiom. 1 et Defin. 3 et 5. Atqui a substantia produci non potest (per præced Prop.). Ergo substantia absolute ab alio produci non potest. Q. E. D.\n\nALITER\n\nDemonstratur hoc etiam facilius ex absurdo contradictorio. Nam si substantia ab alio posset produci, ejus cognitio a cognitione suæ causæ deberet pendere (per Axiom. 4) ; adeoque (per Defin. 3) non esset substantia.\n\nPROPOSITIO VII\n\nAd naturam substantiæ pertinet existere.\n\nDEMONSTRATIO\n\nSubstantia non potest produci ab alio (per Coroll. Prop. præced.) ; erit itaque causa sui, id est (per Defin. 1), ipsius essentia involvit necessario existentiam, sive ad ejus naturam pertinet existere. Q. E. D.\n\nPROPOSITIO VIII\n\nOmnis substantia est necessario infinita.\n\nDEMONSTRATIO\n\nSubstantia unius attributi, non nisi unica existit (per Prop. 5), et ad ipsius naturam pertinet existere (per Prop. 7). Erit ergo de ipsius natura vel finita vel infinita existere. At non finita. Nam (per Defin. 2) debere terminari ab alia ejusdem naturæ, quæ etiam necessario deberet existere (per Prop. 7) ; adeoque darentur duæ substantiæ ejusdem attributi, quod est absurdum (per Prop. 5). Existit ergo infinita. Q. E. D.\n\nSCHOLIUM I\n\nCum finitum esse revera sit ex parte negatio, et infinitum absoluta affirmatio existentiæ alicujus naturæ, sequitur ergo ex sola 7 Prop. omnem substantiam debere esse infinitam.\n\nSCHOLIUM II\n\nNon dubito quin omnibus, qui de rebus confuse judicant, nec res per primas suas causas noscere consueverunt, difficile sit, demonstrationem 7 Prop. concipere ; nimirum quia non distinguunt inter modificationes substantiarum et ipsas substantias, neque sciunt, quomodo res producuntur. Unde fit, ut principium, quod res naturales habere vident substantiis affingant ; qui enim veras rerum causas ignorant, omnia confundunt, et sine ulla mentis repugnantia tam arbores quam homines loquentes fingunt, et homines tam ex lapidibus quam ex semine formari, et quascunque formas in alias quascunque mutari imaginantur. Sic etiam, qui naturam divinam cura humana confundunt, facile Deo affectus humanos tribuunt, præsertim quamdiu etiam ignorant, quomodo affectus in mente producuntur. Si autem homines ad naturam substantiæ attenderent, minime de veritate 7 Prop. dubitarent ; imo hæc Prop. omnibus axioma esset, et inter notiones communes numeraretur. Nam per substantiam intelligerent id, quod in se est et per se concipitur, hoc est id, cujus cognitio non indiget cognitione alterius rei. Per modificationes autem id, quod in alio est, et quarum conceptus a conceptu rei, in qua sunt, formatur : quocirca modificationum non existentium veras ideas possumus habere ; quandoquidem, quamvis non existant actu extra intellectum, earum tamen essentia ita in alio comprehenditur, ut per idem concipi possint. Verum substantiarum veritas extra intellectum non est, nisi in se ipsis, quia per se concipiuntur. Si quis ergo diceret, se claram et distinctam, hoc est veram ideam substantiæ habere, et nihilominus dubitare, num talis substantia existat, idem hercle esset ac si diceret, se veram habere ideam, et nihilominus dubitare, num falsa sit (ut satis attendenti fit manifestum) ; vel, si quis statuat, substantiam, creari, simul statuit, ideam falsam factam esse veram, quo sane nihil absurdius concipi potest ; adeoque fatendum necessario est substantiæ existentiam, sicut ejus essentiam, æternam esse veritatem. Atque hinc alio modo concludere possumus, non dari nisi unicam ejusdem naturæ quod hic ostendere operæ pretium esse duxi. Ut autem hoc ordine faciam, notandum est : I veram uniuscu jusque rei definitionem nihil involvere neque exprimere præter rei definitæ naturam. Ex quo sequitur hoc II, nempe nullam definitionem certum aliquem numerum individuorum involvere neque exprimere, quandoquidem nihil aliud exprimit, quam naturam rei definitæ. Ex. gr. definitio trianguli nihil aliud exprimit, quam simplicem naturam trianguli ; at non certum aliquem triangulorum numerum. III Notandum, dari necessario uniuscujusque rei existentis certam aliquam causam, propter quam existit. IV. Denique notandum, hanc causam, propter quam aliqua res existit, vel debere contineri in ipsa natura et definitione rei existentis (nimirum quod ad ipsius naturam pertinet existere) vel debere extra ipsam dari. His positis, sequitur quod, si in natura certus aliquis numerus individuorum existat, debeat necessario dari causa, cur illa individua, et cur non plura nec pauciora existunt. Si ex. gr. in rerum natura 20 homines existant (quos, majoris perspicuitatis causa, suppono simul existere, nec alios antea in Natura exstitisse), non satis erit (ut scilicet rationem reddamus, cur 20 homines existant), causam naturæ humanæ in genere ostendere ; sed insuper necesse erit, causam ostendere, cur non plures nec pauciores, quam 20 existant ; quandoquidem (per III Notam) uniuscujusque debet necessario dari causa, cur existat. At haæc causa (per Notam II et III) non potest in ipsa natura humana contineri, quandoquidem vera hominis definitio numerum vicenarium non involvit ; adeoque (per Notam IV) causa, cur hi viginti homines existunt, et consequenter cur unusquisque existit, debet necessario extra unumquemque dari ; et propterea absolute concludendum, omne id, cujus naturæ plura individua existere possunt, debere necessario ut existant, causam externam habere. Jam quoniam ad naturam substantiæ (per jam ostensa in hoc Schol.) pertinet existere, debet ejus definitio necessariam existentiam involvere, et consequenter ex sola ejus definitione debet ipsius existentia concludi. At ex ipsius definitione (ut jam ex Nota II et III ostendimus) non potest sequi plurium substantiarum existentia ; sequitur ergo ex ea necessario, unicam tantum ejusdem naturæ existere, ut proponebatur.\n\nPROPOSITIO IX\n\nQuo plus realitatis aut esse unaquæque res habet, eo plura attributa ipsi competunt.\n\nDEMONSTRATIO\n\npatet ex Defin. 4.\n\nPROPOSITIO X\n\nUnumquodque unius substantiæ attributum per se concipi debet.\n\nDEMONSTRATIO\n\nAttributum enim est id, quod intellectus de substantia percipit tamquam ejus essentiam constituens (per Defin. 4) ; adeoque (per Defin. 3) per se concipi debet. Q. E. D.\n\nSCHOLIUM\n\nEx his apparet, quod, quamvis duo attributa realiter distincta concipiantur, hoc est unum sine ope alterius, non possumus tamen inde concludere, ipsa duo entia, sive duas diversas substantias constituere ; id enim est de natura substantiæ, ut unumquodque ejus attributorum per se concipiatur ; quandoquidem omnia, quæ habet, attributa simul in ipsa semper fuerunt, nec unum ab alio produci potuit ; sed unumquodque realitatem sive esse substantiæ exprimit. Longe ergo abest, ut absurdum sit, uni substantiæ plura attributa tribuere ; quin nihil in natura clarius, quam quod unumquodque ens sub aliquo attributo debeat concipi, et, quo plus realitatis aut esse habeat, eo plura attributa, quæ et necessitatem, sive æternitatem, et infinitatem exprimunt, habeat ; et consequenter nihil etiam clarius, quam quod ens absolute infinitum necessario sit definiendum (ut Defin. 6 tradidimus) ens, quod constat infinitis attributis, quorum unumquodque æternam et infinitam certam essentiam exprimit. Si quis autem jam quærit, ex quo ergo signo diversitatem substantiarum poterimus dignoscere, legat sequentes Propositiones, quæ ostendunt in rerum natura non nisi unicam substantiam existere, eamque absolute infinitam esse, quapropter id signum frustra quæreretur.\n\nPROPOSITIO XI\n\nDeus, sive substantia constans infinitis attributis, quorum unumquodque æternam et infinitam essentiam exprimit, necessario existit.\n\nDEMONSTRATIO\n\nSi negas, concipe, si fieri potest, Deum non existere. Ergo (per Axiom. 7) ejus essentia non involvit existentiam. Atqui hoc (per Proposit. 7) est absurdum : Ergo Deus necessario existit. Q. E. D.\n\nALITER\n\nCujuscunque rei assignari debet causa seu ratio, tam cur existit, quam cur non existit. Ex. gr. si triangulus existit, ratio seu causa dari debet, cur existit ; si autem non existit, ratio etiam seu causa dari debet, quæ impedit, quo minus existat, sive quæ ejus existentiam tollat. Hæc vero ratio seu causa vel in natura rei contineri debet, vel extra ipsam. Ex. gr. rationem, cur circulus quadratus non existat, ipsa ejus natura indicat ; nimirum quia contradictionem involvit. Cur autem contra substantia existat, ex sola etiam ejus natura sequitur, quæ scilicet existentiam involvit (vide Prop. 7). At ratio, cur circulus vel triangulus existit, vel cur non existit, ex eorum natura non sequitur, sed ex ordine universæ naturæ corporeæ ; ex eo enim sequi debet, vel jam triangulum necessario existere, vel impossibile esse ut jam existat. Atque hæc per se manifesta sunt. Ex quibus sequitur, id necessario existere, cujus nulla ratio nec causa datur, quæ impedit quominus existat. Si itaque nulla ratio nec causa dari possit, quæ impedit quo minus Deus existat, vel quæ ejus existentiam tollat, omnino concludendum est, eundem necessario existere. At si talis ratio seu causa daretur, ea vel in ipsa Dei natura vel extra ipsam dari deberet, hoc est in alia substantia alterius naturæ. Nam si ejusdem naturæ esset, eo ipso concederetur dari Deum. At substantia, quæ alterius esset naturæ, nihil cum Deo commune habere (per 2 Prop.), adeoque neque ejus existentiam ponere neque tollere posset. Cum igitur ratio seu causa, quæ divinam existentiam tollat, extra divinam naturam dari non possit, debebit necessario dari, siquidem non existit, in ipsa ejus natura, quæ propterea contradictionem involveret. Atqui hoc de Ente absolute infinito et summe perfecto affirmare, absurdum est ; ergo nec in Deo nec extra Deum ulla causa seu ratio datur, quæ ejus existentiam tollat, ac proinde Deus necessario existit. Q. E. D.\n\nALITER\n\nPosse non existere impotentia est, et contra posse existere potentia est (ut per se notum). Si itaque id, quod jam necessario existit, non nisi entia finita sunt ; sunt ergo entia finita potentiora Ente absolute infinito ; atqui hoc (ut per se notum) absurdum est ; ergo vel nihil existit, vel Ens absolute infinitum necessario etiam existit. Atqui nos vel in nobis, vel in alio, quod necessario existit, existimus (vid. Axiom. 1 et Prop. 7). Ergo Ens absolute infinitum, hoc est (per Defin. 6) Deus necessario existit. Q. E. D.\n\nSCHOLIUM\n\nIn hac ultima demonstratione Dei existentiam a posteriori ostendere volui, ut demonstratio facilius perciperetur ; non autem propterea, quod ex hoc eodem fundamento Dei existentia a priori non sequatur. Nam, cum posse existere potentia sit, sequitur, quo plus realitatis alicujus rei naturæ competit, eo plus virium a se habere, ut existat ; adeoque Ens absolute infinitum sive Deum, infinitam absolute potentiam existendi a se habere, qui propterea absolute existit. Multi tamen forsan non facile hujus demonstrationis evidentiam videre poterunt, quia assueti sunt eas solummodo res contemplari, quae a causis externis fiunt ; et ex his, quæ cito fiunt, hoc est quae facile existunt, eas etiam facile perire vident, et contra eas res factu difficiliores judicant, hoc est ad existendum non adeo faciles, ad quas plura pertinere concipiunt. Verum, ut ab his præjudiciis liberentur, non opus habeo hic ostendere, qua ratione hoc enunciatum, quod cito fit, cito perit, verum sit, nec etiam, an respectu totius naturæ omnia æque facilia sint an secus. Sed hoc tantum notare sufficit, me hic non loqui de rebus, quæ a causis externis fiunt, sed de solis substantiis, quæ (per Prop. 6) a nulla causa externa produci possunt. Quoad res enim, quæ a causis externis fiunt, sive eæ multis partibus constent sive paucis, quicquid perfectionis sive realitatis habent, id omne virtuti causæ externæ debetur, adeoque earum existentia ex sola perfectione causæ externæ, non autem suæ oritur. Contra, quicquid substantia perfectionis habet, nulli causæ externæ debetur ; quare ejus etiam existentia ex sola ejus natura sequi debet, quæ proinde nihil aliud est, quam ejus essentia. Perfectio igitur rei existentiam non tollit, sed contra ponit ; imperfectio autem contra eandem tollit ; adeoque de nullius rei existentia certiores esse possumus, quam de existentia Entis absolute infiniti seu perfecti, hoc est Dei. Nam quandoquidem ejus essentia omnem imperfectionem secludit, absolutamque perfectionem involvit, eo ipso omnem causam dubitandi de ipsius existentia tollit, summamque de eadem certitudinem dat, quod mediocriter attendenti perspicuum fore credo.\n\nPROPOSITIO XII\n\nNullum substantiæ attributum potest vere concipi, ex quo sequatur, substantiam posse dividi.\n\nDEMONSTRATIO\n\nPartes enim, in quas substantia sic concepta divideretur, vel naturam substantiæ retinebunt, vel non. Si primum, tum (per 8 Prop.) unaquæque pars debebit esse infinita, et (per Prop. 6) causa sui, et (per Prop. 5) constare debebit ex diverso attributo, adeoque ex una substantia plures constitui poterunt, quod (per Prop. 6) est absurdum. Adde, quod partes (per Prop. 2) nihil commune cum suo toto haberent, et totum (per Defin. 4 et Prop. 10) absque suis partibus et esse et concipi posset, quod absurdum esse nemo dubitare poterit. Si autem secundum ponatur, quod scilicet partes naturam substantiæ non retinebunt : ergo, cum tota substantia in æquales partes esset divisa, naturam substantiæ amitteret, et esse desineret, quod (per Prop. 7) est absurdum.\n\nPROPOSITIO XIII\n\nSubstantia absolute infmita est indivisibilis.\n\nDEMONSTRATIO\n\nSi enim divisibilis esset, partes, in quas divideretur, vel naturam subsiantiæ absolute infinitæ retinebunt, vel non. Si primum, dabuntur ergo plures substantiæ ejusdem naturæ, quod (per Prop. 5) est absurdum. Si secundum ponatur, ergo (ut supra) poterit substantia absolute infinita desinere esse, quod (per Prop. 11) est etiam absurdum.\n\nCOROLLARIUM\n\nEx his sequitur, nullam substantiam, et consequenter nullam substantiam corpoream, quatenus substantia est, esse divisibilem.\n\nSCHOLIUM\n\nQuod substantia sit indivisibilîs, simplicius ex hoc solo intelligitur, quod natura substantiæ non potest concipi nisi infinita, et quod per partem substantiæ nihil aliud intelligi polest quam substantia finita, quod (per Prop. 8) manifestam contradictionem implicat.\n\nPROPOSITIO XIV\n\nPræter Deum nulla dari neque concipi potest substantia.\n\nDEMONSTRATIO\n\nCum Deus sit ens absolute infmitum, de quo nullum attributum, quod essentiam substantiæ exprimit, negari potest (per Defin. 6), isque necessario existat (per Prop. 11) ; si aliqua substantia præter Deum daretur, ea explicari deberet per aliquod attributum Dei, sicque duæ substantiæ ejusdem attributi existerent, quod (per Prop. 5) est absurdum ; adeoque, nulla substantia extra Deum dari potest, et consequenter non etiam concipi. Nam si posset concipi, deberet necessario concipi ut existens ; atqui hoc (per primam partem hujus Demonst.) est absurdum. Ergo extra Deum nulla dari neque concipi potest substantia. Q. E. D.\n\nCOROLLARIUM I\n\nHinc clarissime sequitur : I° Deum esse unicum, hoc est (per Defin. 6) in rerum natura non nisi unam substantiam dari, eamque absolute infinitam esse, ut in Scholio Prop. 10 jam innuimus.\n\nCOROLLARIUM II\n\nSequitur : II° rem extensam et rem cogitantem vel Dei attributa esse, vel (per Axiom. 1) affectiones attributorum Dei.\n\nPROPOSITIO XV\n\nQuicquid est, in Deo est, et nihil sine Deo esse neque concipi potest.\n\nDEMONSTRATIO\n\nPræter Deum nulla datur neque concipi potest substantia (per Prop. 14), hoc est (per Defin. 3) res, quæ in se est et per se concipitur. Modi autem (per Defin. 5) sine substantia nec esse nec concipi possunt ; quare hi in sola divina natura esse, et per ipsam solam concipi possunt- Atqui præter substantias et modos nil datur (per Axioma 1). Ergo nihil sine Deo esse neque concipi potest. Q. E. D.\n\nSCHOLIUM","paragraphs":[{"id":"RAW_QGOH_PPDD:P0001","text":"## ETHICES","kind":"passage"},{"id":"RAW_QGOH_PPDD:P0002","text":"## PARS PRIMA","kind":"passage"},{"id":"RAW_QGOH_PPDD:P0003","text":"## DE DEO","kind":"passage"},{"id":"RAW_QGOH_PPDD:P0004","text":"DEFINITIONES","kind":"passage"},{"id":"RAW_QGOH_PPDD:P0005","text":"I. Per causam sui intelligo id, cujus essentia involvit existentiam ; sive id, cujus natura non potest concipi nisi existens.","kind":"passage"},{"id":"RAW_QGOH_PPDD:P0006","text":"II. Ea res dicitur in suo genere finita, quæ alià ejusdem naturæ terminari potest. Ex. gr. corpus dicitur finitum, quia aliud semper majus concipimus. Sic cogitatio aliâ cogitatione terminatur. At corpus non terminatur cogitatione ; nec cogitatio corpore.","kind":"passage"},{"id":"RAW_QGOH_PPDD:P0007","text":"III. Per substantiam intelligo id, quod in se est, et per se concipitur, hoc est id, cujus conceptus non indiget conceptus alterius rei, a quo formari debeat.","kind":"passage"},{"id":"RAW_QGOH_PPDD:P0008","text":"IV. Per attributum intelligo id, quod intellectus de substantia percipit tanquam ejusdem essentiam constituens.","kind":"passage"},{"id":"RAW_QGOH_PPDD:P0009","text":"V. Per modum intelligo substantiæ affectiones, sive id, quod in alio est, per quod etiam concipitur.","kind":"passage"},{"id":"RAW_QGOH_PPDD:P0010","text":"VI. Per Deum intelligo ens absolute infinitum, hoc est, substantiam constantem infinitis attributis, quorum unumquodque æternam et infinitam essentiam exprimit.","kind":"passage"},{"id":"RAW_QGOH_PPDD:P0011","text":"EXPLICATIO","kind":"passage"},{"id":"RAW_QGOH_PPDD:P0012","text":"Dico absolute infinitum, non autem in suo genere ; quicquid enim in suo genere tantum infinitum est, infinita de eo attributa negare possumus ; quod autem absolute infinitum est, ad ejus essentiam pertinet quicquid essentiam exprimit et negationem nullam involvit.","kind":"passage"},{"id":"RAW_QGOH_PPDD:P0013","text":"VII. Ea res libera dicetur, quae ex sola suae naturæ necessitate existit, et a se sola ad agendum determinatur : Necessaria autem vel potius coacta, quæ ab alio determinatur ad existendum et operandum certa ac determinata ratione.","kind":"passage"},{"id":"RAW_QGOH_PPDD:P0014","text":"VIII. Per æternitatem intelligo ipsam existentiam, quatenus ex sola rei æternæ definitione necessario sequi concipitur.","kind":"passage"},{"id":"RAW_QGOH_PPDD:P0015","text":"EXPLICATIO","kind":"passage"},{"id":"RAW_QGOH_PPDD:P0016","text":"Talis enim existentia ut æterna veritas, sicut rei essentia, concipitur, proptereaque per durationem aut tempus explicari non potest, tametsi duratio principio et fine carere concipiatur.","kind":"passage"},{"id":"RAW_QGOH_PPDD:P0017","text":"AXIOMATA","kind":"passage"},{"id":"RAW_QGOH_PPDD:P0018","text":"I. Omnia, quæ sunt, vel in se vel in alio sunt.","kind":"passage"},{"id":"RAW_QGOH_PPDD:P0019","text":"II. Id, quod per aliud non potest concipi, per se concipi debet.","kind":"passage"},{"id":"RAW_QGOH_PPDD:P0020","text":"III. Ex data causa determinata necessario sequitur effectus, et contra, si nulla detur determinata causa, impossibile est, ut effectus sequatur.","kind":"passage"},{"id":"RAW_QGOH_PPDD:P0021","text":"IV. Effectus cognitio a cognitione causæ dependet, et eandem involvit.","kind":"passage"},{"id":"RAW_QGOH_PPDD:P0022","text":"V. Quæ nihil commune cum se invicem habent, etiam per se invicem intelligi non possunt, sive conceptus unius alterius conceptum non involvit.","kind":"passage"},{"id":"RAW_QGOH_PPDD:P0023","text":"VI. Idea vera debet cum suo ideato convenire.","kind":"passage"},{"id":"RAW_QGOH_PPDD:P0024","text":"VII. Quicquid ut non existens potest concipi, ejus essentia non involvit existentiam.","kind":"passage"},{"id":"RAW_QGOH_PPDD:P0025","text":"PROPOSITIO I","kind":"passage"},{"id":"RAW_QGOH_PPDD:P0026","text":"Substantia prior est natura suis affectionibus.","kind":"passage"},{"id":"RAW_QGOH_PPDD:P0027","text":"DEMONSTRATIO","kind":"passage"},{"id":"RAW_QGOH_PPDD:P0028","text":"patet ex Definitione 3 et 5","kind":"passage"},{"id":"RAW_QGOH_PPDD:P0029","text":"PROPOSITIO II","kind":"passage"},{"id":"RAW_QGOH_PPDD:P0030","text":"Duæ substantiæ, diversa attributa habentes, nihil inter se commune habent.","kind":"passage"},{"id":"RAW_QGOH_PPDD:P0031","text":"DEMONSTRATIO","kind":"passage"},{"id":"RAW_QGOH_PPDD:P0032","text":"patet etiam ex Defin. 3. Unaquæque enim in se debet esse, et per se debet concipi, sive conceptus unius conceptum alterius non involvit.","kind":"passage"},{"id":"RAW_QGOH_PPDD:P0033","text":"PROPOSITIO III","kind":"passage"},{"id":"RAW_QGOH_PPDD:P0034","text":"Quæ res nihil commune inter se habent, earum una alterius causa esse non potest.","kind":"passage"},{"id":"RAW_QGOH_PPDD:P0035","text":"DEMONSTRATIO","kind":"passage"},{"id":"RAW_QGOH_PPDD:P0036","text":"Si nihil commune cum se invicem habent, ergo (per Axiom. 5), nec per se invicem possunt intelligi, adeoque (per Axiom. 4) una alterius causa esse non potest. Q. E. D.","kind":"passage"},{"id":"RAW_QGOH_PPDD:P0037","text":"PROPOSITIO IV","kind":"passage"},{"id":"RAW_QGOH_PPDD:P0038","text":"Duæ aut plures res distinctæ, vel inter se distinguuntur ex diversitate attributorum substantiarum, vel ex diversitate earundem affectionum.","kind":"passage"},{"id":"RAW_QGOH_PPDD:P0039","text":"DEMONSTRATIO","kind":"passage"},{"id":"RAW_QGOH_PPDD:P0040","text":"Omnia, quæ sunt, vel in se vel in alio sunt (per Axiom. 1), hoc est (per Defin. 3 et 5), extra intellectum nihil datur præter substantias earumque affectiones. Nihil ergo extra intellectum datur, per quod plures res distingui inter se possunt, præter substantias, sive quod idem est (per Defin. 4), earum attributa, earumque affectiones. Q. E. D.","kind":"passage"},{"id":"RAW_QGOH_PPDD:P0041","text":"PROPOSITIO V","kind":"passage"},{"id":"RAW_QGOH_PPDD:P0042","text":"In rerum natura non possunt dari duæ aut plures substantiæ ejusdem naturæ sive attributi.","kind":"passage"},{"id":"RAW_QGOH_PPDD:P0043","text":"DEMONSTRATIO","kind":"passage"},{"id":"RAW_QGOH_PPDD:P0044","text":"Si darentur plures distinctæ, deberent inter se distingui vel ex diversitate attributorum, vel ex diversitate affectionum (per Prop. pæced.) Si tantum ex diversitate attributorum, concedetur ergo, non dari nisi unam ejusdem attributi. At si ex diversitate affectionum, cum substantia sit prior natura suis affectionibus (per Prop. I), depositis ergo affectionibus, et in se considerata, hoc est (per Defin. 3 et Axiom. 6) vere considerata, non poterit concipi ab alia distingui hoc est (per Prop. præced.), non poterunt dari plures, sed tantum una. Q. E. D.","kind":"passage"},{"id":"RAW_QGOH_PPDD:P0045","text":"PROPOSITIO VI","kind":"passage"},{"id":"RAW_QGOH_PPDD:P0046","text":"Una substantia non potest produci ab alia substantia.","kind":"passage"},{"id":"RAW_QGOH_PPDD:P0047","text":"DEMONSTRATIO","kind":"passage"},{"id":"RAW_QGOH_PPDD:P0048","text":"In rerum natura non possunt dari duæ substantiæ ejusdem attributi (per Prop. præced.), hoc est (per Prop. 2), quæ aliquid inter se commune habent. Adeoque (per Prop. 3), una alterius causa esse nequit, sive una ab alia non potest produci, Q. E. D.","kind":"passage"},{"id":"RAW_QGOH_PPDD:P0049","text":"COROLLARIUM","kind":"passage"},{"id":"RAW_QGOH_PPDD:P0050","text":"Hinc sequitur, substantiam ab alio produci non posse. Nam in rerum natura nihil datur præter substantias earumque affectiones, ut patet ex Axiom. 1 et Defin. 3 et 5. Atqui a substantia produci non potest (per præced Prop.). Ergo substantia absolute ab alio produci non potest. Q. E. D.","kind":"passage"},{"id":"RAW_QGOH_PPDD:P0051","text":"ALITER","kind":"passage"},{"id":"RAW_QGOH_PPDD:P0052","text":"Demonstratur hoc etiam facilius ex absurdo contradictorio. Nam si substantia ab alio posset produci, ejus cognitio a cognitione suæ causæ deberet pendere (per Axiom. 4) ; adeoque (per Defin. 3) non esset substantia.","kind":"passage"},{"id":"RAW_QGOH_PPDD:P0053","text":"PROPOSITIO VII","kind":"passage"},{"id":"RAW_QGOH_PPDD:P0054","text":"Ad naturam substantiæ pertinet existere.","kind":"passage"},{"id":"RAW_QGOH_PPDD:P0055","text":"DEMONSTRATIO","kind":"passage"},{"id":"RAW_QGOH_PPDD:P0056","text":"Substantia non potest produci ab alio (per Coroll. Prop. præced.) ; erit itaque causa sui, id est (per Defin. 1), ipsius essentia involvit necessario existentiam, sive ad ejus naturam pertinet existere. Q. E. D.","kind":"passage"},{"id":"RAW_QGOH_PPDD:P0057","text":"PROPOSITIO VIII","kind":"passage"},{"id":"RAW_QGOH_PPDD:P0058","text":"Omnis substantia est necessario infinita.","kind":"passage"},{"id":"RAW_QGOH_PPDD:P0059","text":"DEMONSTRATIO","kind":"passage"},{"id":"RAW_QGOH_PPDD:P0060","text":"Substantia unius attributi, non nisi unica existit (per Prop. 5), et ad ipsius naturam pertinet existere (per Prop. 7). Erit ergo de ipsius natura vel finita vel infinita existere. At non finita. Nam (per Defin. 2) debere terminari ab alia ejusdem naturæ, quæ etiam necessario deberet existere (per Prop. 7) ; adeoque darentur duæ substantiæ ejusdem attributi, quod est absurdum (per Prop. 5). Existit ergo infinita. Q. E. D.","kind":"passage"},{"id":"RAW_QGOH_PPDD:P0061","text":"SCHOLIUM I","kind":"passage"},{"id":"RAW_QGOH_PPDD:P0062","text":"Cum finitum esse revera sit ex parte negatio, et infinitum absoluta affirmatio existentiæ alicujus naturæ, sequitur ergo ex sola 7 Prop. omnem substantiam debere esse infinitam.","kind":"passage"},{"id":"RAW_QGOH_PPDD:P0063","text":"SCHOLIUM II","kind":"passage"},{"id":"RAW_QGOH_PPDD:P0064","text":"Non dubito quin omnibus, qui de rebus confuse judicant, nec res per primas suas causas noscere consueverunt, difficile sit, demonstrationem 7 Prop. concipere ; nimirum quia non distinguunt inter modificationes substantiarum et ipsas substantias, neque sciunt, quomodo res producuntur. Unde fit, ut principium, quod res naturales habere vident substantiis affingant ; qui enim veras rerum causas ignorant, omnia confundunt, et sine ulla mentis repugnantia tam arbores quam homines loquentes fingunt, et homines tam ex lapidibus quam ex semine formari, et quascunque formas in alias quascunque mutari imaginantur. Sic etiam, qui naturam divinam cura humana confundunt, facile Deo affectus humanos tribuunt, præsertim quamdiu etiam ignorant, quomodo affectus in mente producuntur. Si autem homines ad naturam substantiæ attenderent, minime de veritate 7 Prop. dubitarent ; imo hæc Prop.","kind":"passage"},{"id":"RAW_QGOH_PPDD:P0065","text":"omnibus axioma esset, et inter notiones communes numeraretur. Nam per substantiam intelligerent id, quod in se est et per se concipitur, hoc est id, cujus cognitio non indiget cognitione alterius rei. Per modificationes autem id, quod in alio est, et quarum conceptus a conceptu rei, in qua sunt, formatur : quocirca modificationum non existentium veras ideas possumus habere ; quandoquidem, quamvis non existant actu extra intellectum, earum tamen essentia ita in alio comprehenditur, ut per idem concipi possint. Verum substantiarum veritas extra intellectum non est, nisi in se ipsis, quia per se concipiuntur.","kind":"passage"},{"id":"RAW_QGOH_PPDD:P0066","text":"Si quis ergo diceret, se claram et distinctam, hoc est veram ideam substantiæ habere, et nihilominus dubitare, num talis substantia existat, idem hercle esset ac si diceret, se veram habere ideam, et nihilominus dubitare, num falsa sit (ut satis attendenti fit manifestum) ; vel, si quis statuat, substantiam, creari, simul statuit, ideam falsam factam esse veram, quo sane nihil absurdius concipi potest ; adeoque fatendum necessario est substantiæ existentiam, sicut ejus essentiam, æternam esse veritatem. Atque hinc alio modo concludere possumus, non dari nisi unicam ejusdem naturæ quod hic ostendere operæ pretium esse duxi. Ut autem hoc ordine faciam, notandum est : I veram uniuscu jusque rei definitionem nihil involvere neque exprimere præter rei definitæ naturam.","kind":"passage"},{"id":"RAW_QGOH_PPDD:P0067","text":"Ex quo sequitur hoc II, nempe nullam definitionem certum aliquem numerum individuorum involvere neque exprimere, quandoquidem nihil aliud exprimit, quam naturam rei definitæ. Ex. gr. definitio trianguli nihil aliud exprimit, quam simplicem naturam trianguli ; at non certum aliquem triangulorum numerum. III Notandum, dari necessario uniuscujusque rei existentis certam aliquam causam, propter quam existit. IV. Denique notandum, hanc causam, propter quam aliqua res existit, vel debere contineri in ipsa natura et definitione rei existentis (nimirum quod ad ipsius naturam pertinet existere) vel debere extra ipsam dari. His positis, sequitur quod, si in natura certus aliquis numerus individuorum existat, debeat necessario dari causa, cur illa individua, et cur non plura nec pauciora existunt. Si ex. gr.","kind":"passage"},{"id":"RAW_QGOH_PPDD:P0068","text":"in rerum natura 20 homines existant (quos, majoris perspicuitatis causa, suppono simul existere, nec alios antea in Natura exstitisse), non satis erit (ut scilicet rationem reddamus, cur 20 homines existant), causam naturæ humanæ in genere ostendere ; sed insuper necesse erit, causam ostendere, cur non plures nec pauciores, quam 20 existant ; quandoquidem (per III Notam) uniuscujusque debet necessario dari causa, cur existat. At haæc causa (per Notam II et III) non potest in ipsa natura humana contineri, quandoquidem vera hominis definitio numerum vicenarium non involvit ; adeoque (per Notam IV) causa, cur hi viginti homines existunt, et consequenter cur unusquisque existit, debet necessario extra unumquemque dari ; et propterea absolute concludendum, omne id, cujus naturæ plura individua existere possunt, debere necessario ut existant, causam externam habere.","kind":"passage"},{"id":"RAW_QGOH_PPDD:P0069","text":"Jam quoniam ad naturam substantiæ (per jam ostensa in hoc Schol.) pertinet existere, debet ejus definitio necessariam existentiam involvere, et consequenter ex sola ejus definitione debet ipsius existentia concludi. At ex ipsius definitione (ut jam ex Nota II et III ostendimus) non potest sequi plurium substantiarum existentia ; sequitur ergo ex ea necessario, unicam tantum ejusdem naturæ existere, ut proponebatur.","kind":"passage"},{"id":"RAW_QGOH_PPDD:P0070","text":"PROPOSITIO IX","kind":"passage"},{"id":"RAW_QGOH_PPDD:P0071","text":"Quo plus realitatis aut esse unaquæque res habet, eo plura attributa ipsi competunt.","kind":"passage"},{"id":"RAW_QGOH_PPDD:P0072","text":"DEMONSTRATIO","kind":"passage"},{"id":"RAW_QGOH_PPDD:P0073","text":"patet ex Defin. 4.","kind":"passage"},{"id":"RAW_QGOH_PPDD:P0074","text":"PROPOSITIO X","kind":"passage"},{"id":"RAW_QGOH_PPDD:P0075","text":"Unumquodque unius substantiæ attributum per se concipi debet.","kind":"passage"},{"id":"RAW_QGOH_PPDD:P0076","text":"DEMONSTRATIO","kind":"passage"},{"id":"RAW_QGOH_PPDD:P0077","text":"Attributum enim est id, quod intellectus de substantia percipit tamquam ejus essentiam constituens (per Defin. 4) ; adeoque (per Defin. 3) per se concipi debet. Q. E. D.","kind":"passage"},{"id":"RAW_QGOH_PPDD:P0078","text":"SCHOLIUM","kind":"passage"},{"id":"RAW_QGOH_PPDD:P0079","text":"Ex his apparet, quod, quamvis duo attributa realiter distincta concipiantur, hoc est unum sine ope alterius, non possumus tamen inde concludere, ipsa duo entia, sive duas diversas substantias constituere ; id enim est de natura substantiæ, ut unumquodque ejus attributorum per se concipiatur ; quandoquidem omnia, quæ habet, attributa simul in ipsa semper fuerunt, nec unum ab alio produci potuit ; sed unumquodque realitatem sive esse substantiæ exprimit. Longe ergo abest, ut absurdum sit, uni substantiæ plura attributa tribuere ; quin nihil in natura clarius, quam quod unumquodque ens sub aliquo attributo debeat concipi, et, quo plus realitatis aut esse habeat, eo plura attributa, quæ et necessitatem, sive æternitatem, et infinitatem exprimunt, habeat ; et consequenter nihil etiam clarius, quam quod ens absolute infinitum necessario sit definiendum (ut Defin.","kind":"passage"},{"id":"RAW_QGOH_PPDD:P0080","text":"6 tradidimus) ens, quod constat infinitis attributis, quorum unumquodque æternam et infinitam certam essentiam exprimit. Si quis autem jam quærit, ex quo ergo signo diversitatem substantiarum poterimus dignoscere, legat sequentes Propositiones, quæ ostendunt in rerum natura non nisi unicam substantiam existere, eamque absolute infinitam esse, quapropter id signum frustra quæreretur.","kind":"passage"},{"id":"RAW_QGOH_PPDD:P0081","text":"PROPOSITIO XI","kind":"passage"},{"id":"RAW_QGOH_PPDD:P0082","text":"Deus, sive substantia constans infinitis attributis, quorum unumquodque æternam et infinitam essentiam exprimit, necessario existit.","kind":"passage"},{"id":"RAW_QGOH_PPDD:P0083","text":"DEMONSTRATIO","kind":"passage"},{"id":"RAW_QGOH_PPDD:P0084","text":"Si negas, concipe, si fieri potest, Deum non existere. Ergo (per Axiom. 7) ejus essentia non involvit existentiam. Atqui hoc (per Proposit. 7) est absurdum : Ergo Deus necessario existit. Q. E. D.","kind":"passage"},{"id":"RAW_QGOH_PPDD:P0085","text":"ALITER","kind":"passage"},{"id":"RAW_QGOH_PPDD:P0086","text":"Cujuscunque rei assignari debet causa seu ratio, tam cur existit, quam cur non existit. Ex. gr. si triangulus existit, ratio seu causa dari debet, cur existit ; si autem non existit, ratio etiam seu causa dari debet, quæ impedit, quo minus existat, sive quæ ejus existentiam tollat. Hæc vero ratio seu causa vel in natura rei contineri debet, vel extra ipsam. Ex. gr. rationem, cur circulus quadratus non existat, ipsa ejus natura indicat ; nimirum quia contradictionem involvit. Cur autem contra substantia existat, ex sola etiam ejus natura sequitur, quæ scilicet existentiam involvit (vide Prop. 7). At ratio, cur circulus vel triangulus existit, vel cur non existit, ex eorum natura non sequitur, sed ex ordine universæ naturæ corporeæ ; ex eo enim sequi debet, vel jam triangulum necessario existere, vel impossibile esse ut jam existat. Atque hæc per se manifesta sunt.","kind":"passage"},{"id":"RAW_QGOH_PPDD:P0087","text":"Ex quibus sequitur, id necessario existere, cujus nulla ratio nec causa datur, quæ impedit quominus existat. Si itaque nulla ratio nec causa dari possit, quæ impedit quo minus Deus existat, vel quæ ejus existentiam tollat, omnino concludendum est, eundem necessario existere. At si talis ratio seu causa daretur, ea vel in ipsa Dei natura vel extra ipsam dari deberet, hoc est in alia substantia alterius naturæ. Nam si ejusdem naturæ esset, eo ipso concederetur dari Deum. At substantia, quæ alterius esset naturæ, nihil cum Deo commune habere (per 2 Prop.), adeoque neque ejus existentiam ponere neque tollere posset. Cum igitur ratio seu causa, quæ divinam existentiam tollat, extra divinam naturam dari non possit, debebit necessario dari, siquidem non existit, in ipsa ejus natura, quæ propterea contradictionem involveret.","kind":"passage"},{"id":"RAW_QGOH_PPDD:P0088","text":"Atqui hoc de Ente absolute infinito et summe perfecto affirmare, absurdum est ; ergo nec in Deo nec extra Deum ulla causa seu ratio datur, quæ ejus existentiam tollat, ac proinde Deus necessario existit. Q. E. D.","kind":"passage"},{"id":"RAW_QGOH_PPDD:P0089","text":"ALITER","kind":"passage"},{"id":"RAW_QGOH_PPDD:P0090","text":"Posse non existere impotentia est, et contra posse existere potentia est (ut per se notum). Si itaque id, quod jam necessario existit, non nisi entia finita sunt ; sunt ergo entia finita potentiora Ente absolute infinito ; atqui hoc (ut per se notum) absurdum est ; ergo vel nihil existit, vel Ens absolute infinitum necessario etiam existit. Atqui nos vel in nobis, vel in alio, quod necessario existit, existimus (vid. Axiom. 1 et Prop. 7). Ergo Ens absolute infinitum, hoc est (per Defin. 6) Deus necessario existit. Q. E. D.","kind":"passage"},{"id":"RAW_QGOH_PPDD:P0091","text":"SCHOLIUM","kind":"passage"},{"id":"RAW_QGOH_PPDD:P0092","text":"In hac ultima demonstratione Dei existentiam a posteriori ostendere volui, ut demonstratio facilius perciperetur ; non autem propterea, quod ex hoc eodem fundamento Dei existentia a priori non sequatur. Nam, cum posse existere potentia sit, sequitur, quo plus realitatis alicujus rei naturæ competit, eo plus virium a se habere, ut existat ; adeoque Ens absolute infinitum sive Deum, infinitam absolute potentiam existendi a se habere, qui propterea absolute existit. Multi tamen forsan non facile hujus demonstrationis evidentiam videre poterunt, quia assueti sunt eas solummodo res contemplari, quae a causis externis fiunt ; et ex his, quæ cito fiunt, hoc est quae facile existunt, eas etiam facile perire vident, et contra eas res factu difficiliores judicant, hoc est ad existendum non adeo faciles, ad quas plura pertinere concipiunt.","kind":"passage"},{"id":"RAW_QGOH_PPDD:P0093","text":"Verum, ut ab his præjudiciis liberentur, non opus habeo hic ostendere, qua ratione hoc enunciatum, quod cito fit, cito perit, verum sit, nec etiam, an respectu totius naturæ omnia æque facilia sint an secus. Sed hoc tantum notare sufficit, me hic non loqui de rebus, quæ a causis externis fiunt, sed de solis substantiis, quæ (per Prop. 6) a nulla causa externa produci possunt. Quoad res enim, quæ a causis externis fiunt, sive eæ multis partibus constent sive paucis, quicquid perfectionis sive realitatis habent, id omne virtuti causæ externæ debetur, adeoque earum existentia ex sola perfectione causæ externæ, non autem suæ oritur. Contra, quicquid substantia perfectionis habet, nulli causæ externæ debetur ; quare ejus etiam existentia ex sola ejus natura sequi debet, quæ proinde nihil aliud est, quam ejus essentia.","kind":"passage"},{"id":"RAW_QGOH_PPDD:P0094","text":"Perfectio igitur rei existentiam non tollit, sed contra ponit ; imperfectio autem contra eandem tollit ; adeoque de nullius rei existentia certiores esse possumus, quam de existentia Entis absolute infiniti seu perfecti, hoc est Dei. Nam quandoquidem ejus essentia omnem imperfectionem secludit, absolutamque perfectionem involvit, eo ipso omnem causam dubitandi de ipsius existentia tollit, summamque de eadem certitudinem dat, quod mediocriter attendenti perspicuum fore credo.","kind":"passage"},{"id":"RAW_QGOH_PPDD:P0095","text":"PROPOSITIO XII","kind":"passage"},{"id":"RAW_QGOH_PPDD:P0096","text":"Nullum substantiæ attributum potest vere concipi, ex quo sequatur, substantiam posse dividi.","kind":"passage"},{"id":"RAW_QGOH_PPDD:P0097","text":"DEMONSTRATIO","kind":"passage"},{"id":"RAW_QGOH_PPDD:P0098","text":"Partes enim, in quas substantia sic concepta divideretur, vel naturam substantiæ retinebunt, vel non. Si primum, tum (per 8 Prop.) unaquæque pars debebit esse infinita, et (per Prop. 6) causa sui, et (per Prop. 5) constare debebit ex diverso attributo, adeoque ex una substantia plures constitui poterunt, quod (per Prop. 6) est absurdum. Adde, quod partes (per Prop. 2) nihil commune cum suo toto haberent, et totum (per Defin. 4 et Prop. 10) absque suis partibus et esse et concipi posset, quod absurdum esse nemo dubitare poterit. Si autem secundum ponatur, quod scilicet partes naturam substantiæ non retinebunt : ergo, cum tota substantia in æquales partes esset divisa, naturam substantiæ amitteret, et esse desineret, quod (per Prop. 7) est absurdum.","kind":"passage"},{"id":"RAW_QGOH_PPDD:P0099","text":"PROPOSITIO XIII","kind":"passage"},{"id":"RAW_QGOH_PPDD:P0100","text":"Substantia absolute infmita est indivisibilis.","kind":"passage"},{"id":"RAW_QGOH_PPDD:P0101","text":"DEMONSTRATIO","kind":"passage"},{"id":"RAW_QGOH_PPDD:P0102","text":"Si enim divisibilis esset, partes, in quas divideretur, vel naturam subsiantiæ absolute infinitæ retinebunt, vel non. Si primum, dabuntur ergo plures substantiæ ejusdem naturæ, quod (per Prop. 5) est absurdum. Si secundum ponatur, ergo (ut supra) poterit substantia absolute infinita desinere esse, quod (per Prop. 11) est etiam absurdum.","kind":"passage"},{"id":"RAW_QGOH_PPDD:P0103","text":"COROLLARIUM","kind":"passage"},{"id":"RAW_QGOH_PPDD:P0104","text":"Ex his sequitur, nullam substantiam, et consequenter nullam substantiam corpoream, quatenus substantia est, esse divisibilem.","kind":"passage"},{"id":"RAW_QGOH_PPDD:P0105","text":"SCHOLIUM","kind":"passage"},{"id":"RAW_QGOH_PPDD:P0106","text":"Quod substantia sit indivisibilîs, simplicius ex hoc solo intelligitur, quod natura substantiæ non potest concipi nisi infinita, et quod per partem substantiæ nihil aliud intelligi polest quam substantia finita, quod (per Prop. 8) manifestam contradictionem implicat.","kind":"passage"},{"id":"RAW_QGOH_PPDD:P0107","text":"PROPOSITIO XIV","kind":"passage"},{"id":"RAW_QGOH_PPDD:P0108","text":"Præter Deum nulla dari neque concipi potest substantia.","kind":"passage"},{"id":"RAW_QGOH_PPDD:P0109","text":"DEMONSTRATIO","kind":"passage"},{"id":"RAW_QGOH_PPDD:P0110","text":"Cum Deus sit ens absolute infmitum, de quo nullum attributum, quod essentiam substantiæ exprimit, negari potest (per Defin. 6), isque necessario existat (per Prop. 11) ; si aliqua substantia præter Deum daretur, ea explicari deberet per aliquod attributum Dei, sicque duæ substantiæ ejusdem attributi existerent, quod (per Prop. 5) est absurdum ; adeoque, nulla substantia extra Deum dari potest, et consequenter non etiam concipi. Nam si posset concipi, deberet necessario concipi ut existens ; atqui hoc (per primam partem hujus Demonst.) est absurdum. Ergo extra Deum nulla dari neque concipi potest substantia. Q. E. D.","kind":"passage"},{"id":"RAW_QGOH_PPDD:P0111","text":"COROLLARIUM I","kind":"passage"},{"id":"RAW_QGOH_PPDD:P0112","text":"Hinc clarissime sequitur : I° Deum esse unicum, hoc est (per Defin. 6) in rerum natura non nisi unam substantiam dari, eamque absolute infinitam esse, ut in Scholio Prop. 10 jam innuimus.","kind":"passage"},{"id":"RAW_QGOH_PPDD:P0113","text":"COROLLARIUM II","kind":"passage"},{"id":"RAW_QGOH_PPDD:P0114","text":"Sequitur : II° rem extensam et rem cogitantem vel Dei attributa esse, vel (per Axiom. 1) affectiones attributorum Dei.","kind":"passage"},{"id":"RAW_QGOH_PPDD:P0115","text":"PROPOSITIO XV","kind":"passage"},{"id":"RAW_QGOH_PPDD:P0116","text":"Quicquid est, in Deo est, et nihil sine Deo esse neque concipi potest.","kind":"passage"},{"id":"RAW_QGOH_PPDD:P0117","text":"DEMONSTRATIO","kind":"passage"},{"id":"RAW_QGOH_PPDD:P0118","text":"Præter Deum nulla datur neque concipi potest substantia (per Prop. 14), hoc est (per Defin. 3) res, quæ in se est et per se concipitur. Modi autem (per Defin. 5) sine substantia nec esse nec concipi possunt ; quare hi in sola divina natura esse, et per ipsam solam concipi possunt- Atqui præter substantias et modos nil datur (per Axioma 1). Ergo nihil sine Deo esse neque concipi potest. Q. E. D.","kind":"passage"},{"id":"RAW_QGOH_PPDD:P0119","text":"SCHOLIUM","kind":"passage"}],"sha256":"3d49fc92cbe840125cfb0a4a2fcbae9bc2759c6495a210949120e1564312f4c9","language_code":"la","language_name":"Latina","translations":[{"id":"SOURCE_TR_20261015T012524908647Z_0781167d","language_code":"en","language_name":"English","title":"Part One - Concerning God · passage 1","source_sha256":"3d49fc92cbe840125cfb0a4a2fcbae9bc2759c6495a210949120e1564312f4c9","sha256":"1e1ae6e1424566aaf698856b941b70b354f0cbcb4e440342d02e951147dc7d7d","rendition_type":"machine","review_status":"machine","model":"gpt-5.6-terra","created":"2026-10-15T01:25:24.908685+00:00","paragraphs":[{"id":"RAW_QGOH_PPDD:P0001","text":"## ETHICS","kind":"passage","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_QGOH_PPDD:P0001"]},{"id":"RAW_QGOH_PPDD:P0002","text":"## PART ONE","kind":"passage","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_QGOH_PPDD:P0002"]},{"id":"RAW_QGOH_PPDD:P0003","text":"## CONCERNING GOD","kind":"passage","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_QGOH_PPDD:P0003"]},{"id":"RAW_QGOH_PPDD:P0004","text":"DEFINITIONS","kind":"passage","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_QGOH_PPDD:P0004"]},{"id":"RAW_QGOH_PPDD:P0005","text":"I. By cause of itself I understand that whose essence involves existence; or that whose nature cannot be conceived except as existing.","kind":"passage","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_QGOH_PPDD:P0005"]},{"id":"RAW_QGOH_PPDD:P0006","text":"II. A thing is said to be finite in its kind when it can be bounded by another of the same nature. For example, a body is called finite because we always conceive another greater body. Thus thought is bounded by another thought. But a body is not bounded by thought; nor thought by a body.","kind":"passage","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_QGOH_PPDD:P0006"]},{"id":"RAW_QGOH_PPDD:P0007","text":"III. By substance I understand that which is in itself and is conceived through itself, that is, that whose concept does not require the concept of another thing, from which it must be formed.","kind":"passage","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_QGOH_PPDD:P0007"]},{"id":"RAW_QGOH_PPDD:P0008","text":"IV. By attribute I understand that which the intellect perceives of substance as constituting its essence.","kind":"passage","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_QGOH_PPDD:P0008"]},{"id":"RAW_QGOH_PPDD:P0009","text":"V. By mode I understand the affections of substance, or that which is in another, through which it is also conceived.","kind":"passage","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_QGOH_PPDD:P0009"]},{"id":"RAW_QGOH_PPDD:P0010","text":"VI. By God I understand an absolutely infinite being, that is, a substance consisting of infinite attributes, each of which expresses an eternal and infinite essence.","kind":"passage","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_QGOH_PPDD:P0010"]},{"id":"RAW_QGOH_PPDD:P0011","text":"EXPLANATION","kind":"passage","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_QGOH_PPDD:P0011"]},{"id":"RAW_QGOH_PPDD:P0012","text":"I say absolutely infinite, not, however, infinite in its kind; for of whatever is infinite only in its kind, we can deny infinite attributes; but whatever is absolutely infinite, to its essence pertains whatever expresses essence and involves no negation.","kind":"passage","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_QGOH_PPDD:P0012"]},{"id":"RAW_QGOH_PPDD:P0013","text":"VII. That thing shall be called free which exists from the necessity of its own nature alone and is determined to act by itself alone; but necessary, or rather compelled, that which is determined by another to exist and to act in a certain and determinate manner.","kind":"passage","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_QGOH_PPDD:P0013"]},{"id":"RAW_QGOH_PPDD:P0014","text":"VIII. By eternity I understand existence itself, insofar as it is conceived necessarily to follow from the definition alone of an eternal thing.","kind":"passage","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_QGOH_PPDD:P0014"]},{"id":"RAW_QGOH_PPDD:P0015","text":"EXPLANATION","kind":"passage","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_QGOH_PPDD:P0015"]},{"id":"RAW_QGOH_PPDD:P0016","text":"For such existence is conceived as an eternal truth, just as is the essence of a thing, and therefore cannot be explained through duration or time, even though duration be conceived as lacking beginning and end.","kind":"passage","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_QGOH_PPDD:P0016"]},{"id":"RAW_QGOH_PPDD:P0017","text":"AXIOMS","kind":"passage","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_QGOH_PPDD:P0017"]},{"id":"RAW_QGOH_PPDD:P0018","text":"I. All things that are, are either in themselves or in another.","kind":"passage","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_QGOH_PPDD:P0018"]},{"id":"RAW_QGOH_PPDD:P0019","text":"II. That which cannot be conceived through another must be conceived through itself.","kind":"passage","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_QGOH_PPDD:P0019"]},{"id":"RAW_QGOH_PPDD:P0020","text":"III. From a given determinate cause an effect necessarily follows, and conversely, if no determinate cause be given, it is impossible for an effect to follow.","kind":"passage","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_QGOH_PPDD:P0020"]},{"id":"RAW_QGOH_PPDD:P0021","text":"IV. The knowledge of an effect depends on the knowledge of the cause, and involves the same.","kind":"passage","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_QGOH_PPDD:P0021"]},{"id":"RAW_QGOH_PPDD:P0022","text":"V. Things which have nothing in common with one another also cannot be understood through one another; that is, the concept of one does not involve the concept of the other.","kind":"passage","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_QGOH_PPDD:P0022"]},{"id":"RAW_QGOH_PPDD:P0023","text":"VI. A true idea must agree with its ideate.","kind":"passage","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_QGOH_PPDD:P0023"]},{"id":"RAW_QGOH_PPDD:P0024","text":"VII. Whatever can be conceived as not existing, its essence does not involve existence.","kind":"passage","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_QGOH_PPDD:P0024"]},{"id":"RAW_QGOH_PPDD:P0025","text":"PROPOSITION I","kind":"passage","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_QGOH_PPDD:P0025"]},{"id":"RAW_QGOH_PPDD:P0026","text":"Substance is by nature prior to its affections.","kind":"passage","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_QGOH_PPDD:P0026"]},{"id":"RAW_QGOH_PPDD:P0027","text":"DEMONSTRATION","kind":"passage","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_QGOH_PPDD:P0027"]},{"id":"RAW_QGOH_PPDD:P0028","text":"This is evident from Definitions 3 and 5.","kind":"passage","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_QGOH_PPDD:P0028"]},{"id":"RAW_QGOH_PPDD:P0029","text":"PROPOSITION II","kind":"passage","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_QGOH_PPDD:P0029"]},{"id":"RAW_QGOH_PPDD:P0030","text":"Two substances having different attributes have nothing in common with one another.","kind":"passage","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_QGOH_PPDD:P0030"]},{"id":"RAW_QGOH_PPDD:P0031","text":"DEMONSTRATION","kind":"passage","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_QGOH_PPDD:P0031"]},{"id":"RAW_QGOH_PPDD:P0032","text":"This is also evident from Definition 3. For each must be in itself and must be conceived through itself; that is, the concept of one does not involve the concept of the other.","kind":"passage","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_QGOH_PPDD:P0032"]},{"id":"RAW_QGOH_PPDD:P0033","text":"PROPOSITION III","kind":"passage","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_QGOH_PPDD:P0033"]},{"id":"RAW_QGOH_PPDD:P0034","text":"Things which have nothing in common with one another cannot be the cause, one of the other.","kind":"passage","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_QGOH_PPDD:P0034"]},{"id":"RAW_QGOH_PPDD:P0035","text":"DEMONSTRATION","kind":"passage","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_QGOH_PPDD:P0035"]},{"id":"RAW_QGOH_PPDD:P0036","text":"If they have nothing in common with one another, then (by Axiom 5) they cannot be understood through one another, and consequently (by Axiom 4) one cannot be the cause of the other. Q. E. D.","kind":"passage","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_QGOH_PPDD:P0036"]},{"id":"RAW_QGOH_PPDD:P0037","text":"PROPOSITION IV","kind":"passage","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_QGOH_PPDD:P0037"]},{"id":"RAW_QGOH_PPDD:P0038","text":"Two or more distinct things are distinguished from one another either by the difference of the attributes of the substances, or by the difference of their affections.","kind":"passage","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_QGOH_PPDD:P0038"]},{"id":"RAW_QGOH_PPDD:P0039","text":"DEMONSTRATION","kind":"passage","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_QGOH_PPDD:P0039"]},{"id":"RAW_QGOH_PPDD:P0040","text":"All things that are, are either in themselves or in another (by Axiom 1); that is (by Definitions 3 and 5), outside the intellect nothing is given except substances and their affections. Therefore, outside the intellect nothing is given through which several things can be distinguished from one another, except substances, or, what is the same thing (by Definition 4), their attributes and their affections. Q. E. D.","kind":"passage","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_QGOH_PPDD:P0040"]},{"id":"RAW_QGOH_PPDD:P0041","text":"PROPOSITION V","kind":"passage","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_QGOH_PPDD:P0041"]},{"id":"RAW_QGOH_PPDD:P0042","text":"In the nature of things there cannot be given two or more substances of the same nature or attribute.","kind":"passage","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_QGOH_PPDD:P0042"]},{"id":"RAW_QGOH_PPDD:P0043","text":"DEMONSTRATION","kind":"passage","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_QGOH_PPDD:P0043"]},{"id":"RAW_QGOH_PPDD:P0044","text":"If several distinct substances were given, they would have to be distinguished from one another either by the difference of their attributes or by the difference of their affections (by the preceding Proposition). If only by the difference of attributes, it will therefore be granted that only one of the same attribute is given. But if by the difference of affections, since substance is by nature prior to its affections (by Proposition I), then, the affections having been set aside and it being considered in itself—that is (by Definition 3 and Axiom 6), being considered truly—it cannot be conceived as distinguished from another; that is (by the preceding Proposition), several cannot be given, but only one. Q. E. D.","kind":"passage","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_QGOH_PPDD:P0044"]},{"id":"RAW_QGOH_PPDD:P0045","text":"PROPOSITION VI","kind":"passage","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_QGOH_PPDD:P0045"]},{"id":"RAW_QGOH_PPDD:P0046","text":"One substance cannot be produced by another substance.","kind":"passage","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_QGOH_PPDD:P0046"]},{"id":"RAW_QGOH_PPDD:P0047","text":"DEMONSTRATION","kind":"passage","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_QGOH_PPDD:P0047"]},{"id":"RAW_QGOH_PPDD:P0048","text":"In the nature of things there cannot be given two substances of the same attribute (by the preceding Proposition), that is (by Proposition 2), which have something in common with one another. Consequently (by Proposition 3), one cannot be the cause of the other, or one cannot be produced by the other. Q. E. D.","kind":"passage","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_QGOH_PPDD:P0048"]},{"id":"RAW_QGOH_PPDD:P0049","text":"COROLLARY","kind":"passage","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_QGOH_PPDD:P0049"]},{"id":"RAW_QGOH_PPDD:P0050","text":"From this it follows that a substance cannot be produced by another. For in the nature of things nothing is given besides substances and their affections, as is evident from Axiom 1 and Definitions 3 and 5. But it cannot be produced by a substance (by the preceding Proposition). Therefore a substance absolutely cannot be produced by another. Q. E. D.","kind":"passage","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_QGOH_PPDD:P0050"]},{"id":"RAW_QGOH_PPDD:P0051","text":"OTHERWISE","kind":"passage","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_QGOH_PPDD:P0051"]},{"id":"RAW_QGOH_PPDD:P0052","text":"This is also demonstrated more easily by a contradiction in the absurd. For if a substance could be produced by another, the cognition of it would have to depend upon the cognition of its cause (by Axiom 4); and thus (by Definition 3) it would not be a substance.","kind":"passage","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_QGOH_PPDD:P0052"]},{"id":"RAW_QGOH_PPDD:P0053","text":"PROPOSITION VII","kind":"passage","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_QGOH_PPDD:P0053"]},{"id":"RAW_QGOH_PPDD:P0054","text":"To the nature of substance belongs existence.","kind":"passage","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_QGOH_PPDD:P0054"]},{"id":"RAW_QGOH_PPDD:P0055","text":"DEMONSTRATION","kind":"passage","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_QGOH_PPDD:P0055"]},{"id":"RAW_QGOH_PPDD:P0056","text":"A substance cannot be produced by another (by the Corollary of the preceding Proposition); it will therefore be the cause of itself, that is (by Definition 1), its essence necessarily involves existence, or, existence belongs to its nature. Q. E. D.","kind":"passage","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_QGOH_PPDD:P0056"]},{"id":"RAW_QGOH_PPDD:P0057","text":"PROPOSITION VIII","kind":"passage","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_QGOH_PPDD:P0057"]},{"id":"RAW_QGOH_PPDD:P0058","text":"Every substance is necessarily infinite.","kind":"passage","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_QGOH_PPDD:P0058"]},{"id":"RAW_QGOH_PPDD:P0059","text":"DEMONSTRATION","kind":"passage","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_QGOH_PPDD:P0059"]},{"id":"RAW_QGOH_PPDD:P0060","text":"A substance of one attribute exists only as unique (by Proposition 5), and existence belongs to its nature (by Proposition 7). Therefore it will belong to its nature to exist either as finite or as infinite. But not as finite. For (by Definition 2) it would have to be bounded by another of the same nature, which also would necessarily have to exist (by Proposition 7); and thus there would be two substances of the same attribute, which is absurd (by Proposition 5). Therefore it exists as infinite. Q. E. D.","kind":"passage","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_QGOH_PPDD:P0060"]},{"id":"RAW_QGOH_PPDD:P0061","text":"SCHOLIUM I","kind":"passage","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_QGOH_PPDD:P0061"]},{"id":"RAW_QGOH_PPDD:P0062","text":"Since to be finite is in reality partly a negation, and the infinite is the absolute affirmation of the existence of some nature, it follows therefore from Proposition 7 alone that every substance must be infinite.","kind":"passage","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_QGOH_PPDD:P0062"]},{"id":"RAW_QGOH_PPDD:P0063","text":"SCHOLIUM II","kind":"passage","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_QGOH_PPDD:P0063"]},{"id":"RAW_QGOH_PPDD:P0064","text":"I do not doubt that it is difficult for all who judge confusedly concerning things, and have not been accustomed to know things through their first causes, to conceive the demonstration of Proposition 7; namely, because they do not distinguish between modifications of substances and substances themselves, nor do they know how things are produced. Whence it comes about that they ascribe to substances the beginning which natural things seem to have; for those who are ignorant of the true causes of things confuse everything, and, with no repugnance of mind, feign both trees speaking like men and men to be formed both from stones and from seed, and imagine any forms whatever to be changed into any others whatever. So too, those who confuse the divine nature with human care easily attribute human affects to God, especially so long as they are also ignorant of how affects are produced in the mind. But if men attended to the nature of substance, they would by no means doubt the truth of Proposition 7; indeed, this Proposition","kind":"passage","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_QGOH_PPDD:P0064"]},{"id":"RAW_QGOH_PPDD:P0065","text":"would be an axiom to all, and would be reckoned among the common notions. For by substance they would understand that which is in itself and is conceived through itself, that is, that whose cognition does not require the cognition of another thing. But by modifications, that which is in another, and whose concept is formed from the concept of the thing in which they are; for which reason we can have true ideas of non-existing modifications, since, although they do not actually exist outside the intellect, their essence is nevertheless so comprehended in another that they can be conceived through the same. But the truth of substances is not outside the intellect except in themselves, because they are conceived through themselves.","kind":"passage","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_QGOH_PPDD:P0065"]},{"id":"RAW_QGOH_PPDD:P0066","text":"If therefore anyone were to say that he has a clear and distinct, that is, true idea of substance, and nevertheless doubts whether such a substance exists, this by Hercules would be the same as if he were to say that he has a true idea and nevertheless doubts whether it is false (as becomes manifest to one who attends sufficiently); or, if anyone maintains that substance is created, he thereby maintains that a false idea has been made true, than which surely nothing more absurd can be conceived; and therefore it must necessarily be admitted that the existence of substance, just as its essence, is an eternal truth. And hence we can conclude in another way that there is only one [substance] of the same nature, which I judged it worthwhile to show here. But in order that I may do this in order, it must be noted: I. that the true definition of any thing whatsoever involves or expresses nothing except the nature of the thing defined.","kind":"passage","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_QGOH_PPDD:P0066"]},{"id":"RAW_QGOH_PPDD:P0067","text":"From which this follows, II, namely, that no definition involves or expresses any certain number of individuals, since it expresses nothing other than the nature of the thing defined. For example, the definition of a triangle expresses nothing other than the simple nature of a triangle, but not any certain number of triangles. III. It must be noted that there must necessarily be given for each existing thing some certain cause on account of which it exists. IV. Finally, it must be noted that this cause on account of which some thing exists must either be contained in the very nature and definition of the existing thing (namely, that existence belongs to its nature), or must be given outside it. Once these points have been posited, it follows that, if some certain number of individuals exists in nature, a cause must necessarily be given why those individuals, and why not more or fewer, exist. If, for example,","kind":"passage","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_QGOH_PPDD:P0067"]},{"id":"RAW_QGOH_PPDD:P0068","text":"twenty men exist in the nature of things (whom, for the sake of greater clarity, I suppose to exist at the same time, and that no others had previously existed in Nature), it will not be enough (namely, in order that we may render a reason why twenty men exist) to show the cause of human nature in general; but it will in addition be necessary to show the cause why not more or fewer than twenty exist; since (by Note III) there must necessarily be given for each one a cause why he exists. But this cause (by Notes II and III) cannot be contained in human nature itself, since the true definition of man does not involve the number twenty; and consequently (by Note IV) the cause why these twenty men exist, and consequently why each one exists, must necessarily be given outside each one; and therefore it must be concluded absolutely that everything of whose nature several individuals can exist must necessarily, in order that they exist, have an external cause.","kind":"passage","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_QGOH_PPDD:P0068"]},{"id":"RAW_QGOH_PPDD:P0069","text":"Now since it belongs to the nature of substance (by what has already been shown in this Scholium) to exist, its definition must involve necessary existence, and consequently its existence must be concluded from its definition alone. But from its definition (as we have just shown from Notes II and III) the existence of several substances cannot follow; therefore it follows necessarily from it that only one of the same nature exists, as was proposed.","kind":"passage","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_QGOH_PPDD:P0069"]},{"id":"RAW_QGOH_PPDD:P0070","text":"PROPOSITION IX","kind":"passage","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_QGOH_PPDD:P0070"]},{"id":"RAW_QGOH_PPDD:P0071","text":"The more reality or being each thing has, the more attributes belong to it.","kind":"passage","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_QGOH_PPDD:P0071"]},{"id":"RAW_QGOH_PPDD:P0072","text":"DEMONSTRATION","kind":"passage","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_QGOH_PPDD:P0072"]},{"id":"RAW_QGOH_PPDD:P0073","text":"This is clear from Definition 4.","kind":"passage","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_QGOH_PPDD:P0073"]},{"id":"RAW_QGOH_PPDD:P0074","text":"PROPOSITION X","kind":"passage","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_QGOH_PPDD:P0074"]},{"id":"RAW_QGOH_PPDD:P0075","text":"Each attribute of one substance must be conceived through itself.","kind":"passage","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_QGOH_PPDD:P0075"]},{"id":"RAW_QGOH_PPDD:P0076","text":"DEMONSTRATION","kind":"passage","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_QGOH_PPDD:P0076"]},{"id":"RAW_QGOH_PPDD:P0077","text":"For an attribute is that which the intellect perceives of substance as constituting its essence (by Def. 4); and therefore (by Def. 3) it must be conceived through itself. Q. E. D.","kind":"passage","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_QGOH_PPDD:P0077"]},{"id":"RAW_QGOH_PPDD:P0078","text":"SCHOLIUM","kind":"passage","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_QGOH_PPDD:P0078"]},{"id":"RAW_QGOH_PPDD:P0079","text":"From these things it is apparent that, although two attributes are conceived as really distinct, that is, one without the aid of the other, we nevertheless cannot conclude from this that they constitute two beings, or two different substances; for it is of the nature of substance that each one of its attributes be conceived through itself; since all the attributes it has have always been in it together, nor could one be produced by another; but each expresses the reality, or being, of substance. Therefore it is far from absurd to attribute several attributes to one substance; indeed, nothing in nature is clearer than that each being must be conceived under some attribute, and that the more reality or being it has, the more attributes it has, which express both necessity, or eternity, and infinity; and consequently nothing is clearer than that a being absolutely infinite must necessarily be defined (as in Def.","kind":"passage","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_QGOH_PPDD:P0079"]},{"id":"RAW_QGOH_PPDD:P0080","text":"6 we have stated) as a being consisting of infinite attributes, each of which expresses a certain eternal and infinite essence. But if anyone now asks by what sign, then, we shall be able to discern the diversity of substances, let him read the following Propositions, which show that in the nature of things there exists only one substance, and that it is absolutely infinite; wherefore that sign would be sought in vain.","kind":"passage","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_QGOH_PPDD:P0080"]},{"id":"RAW_QGOH_PPDD:P0081","text":"PROPOSITION XI","kind":"passage","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_QGOH_PPDD:P0081"]},{"id":"RAW_QGOH_PPDD:P0082","text":"God, or a substance consisting of infinite attributes, each of which expresses an eternal and infinite essence, necessarily exists.","kind":"passage","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_QGOH_PPDD:P0082"]},{"id":"RAW_QGOH_PPDD:P0083","text":"DEMONSTRATION","kind":"passage","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_QGOH_PPDD:P0083"]},{"id":"RAW_QGOH_PPDD:P0084","text":"If you deny it, conceive, if possible, that God does not exist. Therefore (by Axiom 7) his essence does not involve existence. But this (by Proposition 7) is absurd. Therefore God necessarily exists. Q. E. D.","kind":"passage","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_QGOH_PPDD:P0084"]},{"id":"RAW_QGOH_PPDD:P0085","text":"OTHERWISE","kind":"passage","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_QGOH_PPDD:P0085"]},{"id":"RAW_QGOH_PPDD:P0086","text":"For any thing, a cause or reason must be assigned, both why it exists and why it does not exist. For example, if a triangle exists, a reason or cause must be given why it exists; but if it does not exist, a reason or cause must likewise be given which prevents it from existing, or which removes its existence. But this reason or cause must be contained either in the nature of the thing or outside it. For example, the nature of a square circle itself indicates the reason why it does not exist; namely, because it involves a contradiction. But, on the other hand, why substance exists follows also from its nature alone, which indeed involves existence (see Prop. 7). But the reason why a circle or triangle exists, or why it does not exist, does not follow from their nature, but from the order of the whole corporeal nature; for from this it must follow either that a triangle now necessarily exists, or that it is impossible for it now to exist. And these things are self-evident.","kind":"passage","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_QGOH_PPDD:P0086"]},{"id":"RAW_QGOH_PPDD:P0087","text":"From these things it follows that that necessarily exists for which no reason or cause is given that prevents it from existing. Therefore, if no reason or cause can be given which prevents God from existing, or which removes his existence, it must altogether be concluded that he necessarily exists. But if such a reason or cause were given, it would have to be given either in God's own nature or outside it, that is, in another substance of a different nature. For if it were of the same nature, by that very fact it would be conceded that God is given. But a substance which was of another nature could have nothing in common with God (by Prop. 2), and therefore could neither posit nor remove his existence. Since, therefore, a reason or cause which removes the divine existence cannot be given outside the divine nature, it must necessarily be given, if indeed he does not exist, in his very nature, which would therefore involve a contradiction.","kind":"passage","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_QGOH_PPDD:P0087"]},{"id":"RAW_QGOH_PPDD:P0088","text":"But to affirm this of a Being absolutely infinite and supremely perfect is absurd; therefore neither in God nor outside God is any cause or reason given which removes his existence, and consequently God necessarily exists. Q. E. D.","kind":"passage","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_QGOH_PPDD:P0088"]},{"id":"RAW_QGOH_PPDD:P0089","text":"OTHERWISE","kind":"passage","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_QGOH_PPDD:P0089"]},{"id":"RAW_QGOH_PPDD:P0090","text":"To be able not to exist is impotence, and conversely to be able to exist is power (as is known through itself). Therefore, if those things which now necessarily exist are only finite beings, finite beings are more powerful than a Being absolutely infinite; but this (as is known through itself) is absurd; therefore either nothing exists, or a Being absolutely infinite necessarily also exists. But we exist either in ourselves or in another which necessarily exists (see Axiom 1 and Prop. 7). Therefore a Being absolutely infinite, that is (by Def. 6), God, necessarily exists. Q. E. D.","kind":"passage","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_QGOH_PPDD:P0090"]},{"id":"RAW_QGOH_PPDD:P0091","text":"SCHOLIUM","kind":"passage","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_QGOH_PPDD:P0091"]},{"id":"RAW_QGOH_PPDD:P0092","text":"In this last demonstration I wished to show God's existence a posteriori, so that the demonstration might be more easily perceived; but not for that reason because God's existence does not follow a priori from this same foundation. For, since being able to exist is power, it follows that the more reality belongs to the nature of any thing, the more powers it has of itself for existing; and therefore that a Being absolutely infinite, or God, has of itself an absolutely infinite power of existing, and therefore absolutely exists. Yet perhaps many will not easily be able to see the evidence of this demonstration, because they are accustomed to contemplate only those things which are made by external causes; and from those things which are made quickly, that is, which easily exist, they see that they also easily perish; and conversely they judge those things more difficult to make, that is, not so easy to exist, for which they conceive that more things are required.","kind":"passage","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_QGOH_PPDD:P0092"]},{"id":"RAW_QGOH_PPDD:P0093","text":"But, in order that they may be freed from these prejudices, I do not need here to show in what way this statement, that what is quickly made quickly perishes, is true, nor even whether, with respect to the whole of nature, all things are equally easy or otherwise. But it is sufficient only to note that I am not speaking here of things which are made by external causes, but of substances alone, which (by Prop. 6) cannot be produced by any external cause. For as to things which are made by external causes, whether they consist of many parts or few, whatever perfection or reality they have, all of it is due to the power of the external cause, and therefore their existence arises from the perfection of the external cause alone, and not from their own. On the contrary, whatever perfection substance has is due to no external cause; wherefore its existence too must follow from its nature alone, which accordingly is nothing other than its essence.","kind":"passage","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_QGOH_PPDD:P0093"]},{"id":"RAW_QGOH_PPDD:P0094","text":"Therefore perfection does not remove a thing's existence, but on the contrary posits it; imperfection, however, on the contrary removes it; and therefore we can be more certain of the existence of no thing than of the existence of a Being absolutely infinite or perfect, that is, God. For since his essence excludes every imperfection and involves absolute perfection, by that very fact it removes every cause for doubting his existence and gives the highest certainty concerning it, which, I believe, will be clear to one who attends moderately.","kind":"passage","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_QGOH_PPDD:P0094"]},{"id":"RAW_QGOH_PPDD:P0095","text":"PROPOSITION XII","kind":"passage","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_QGOH_PPDD:P0095"]},{"id":"RAW_QGOH_PPDD:P0096","text":"No attribute of substance can truly be conceived from which it follows that substance can be divided.","kind":"passage","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_QGOH_PPDD:P0096"]},{"id":"RAW_QGOH_PPDD:P0097","text":"DEMONSTRATION","kind":"passage","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_QGOH_PPDD:P0097"]},{"id":"RAW_QGOH_PPDD:P0098","text":"For the parts into which substance thus conceived would be divided will either retain the nature of substance, or they will not. If the former, then (by Prop. 8) each part will have to be infinite, and (by Prop. 6) the cause of itself, and (by Prop. 5) will have to consist of a different attribute; and thus from one substance several may be constituted, which (by Prop. 6) is absurd. Add that the parts (by Prop. 2) would have nothing in common with their whole, and the whole (by Def. 4 and Prop. 10) could both be and be conceived without its parts, which no one can doubt is absurd. But if the latter be posited, namely, that the parts will not retain the nature of substance, then, since the whole substance would be divided into equal parts, it would lose the nature of substance and cease to be, which (by Prop. 7) is absurd.","kind":"passage","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_QGOH_PPDD:P0098"]},{"id":"RAW_QGOH_PPDD:P0099","text":"PROPOSITION XIII","kind":"passage","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_QGOH_PPDD:P0099"]},{"id":"RAW_QGOH_PPDD:P0100","text":"Substance absolutely infinite is indivisible.","kind":"passage","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_QGOH_PPDD:P0100"]},{"id":"RAW_QGOH_PPDD:P0101","text":"DEMONSTRATION","kind":"passage","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_QGOH_PPDD:P0101"]},{"id":"RAW_QGOH_PPDD:P0102","text":"For if it were divisible, the parts into which it would be divided would either retain the nature of substance absolutely infinite, or they would not. If the former, then several substances of the same nature would be given, which (by Prop. 5) is absurd. If the latter be posited, then (as above) substance absolutely infinite could cease to be, which (by Prop. 11) is likewise absurd.","kind":"passage","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_QGOH_PPDD:P0102"]},{"id":"RAW_QGOH_PPDD:P0103","text":"COROLLARY","kind":"passage","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_QGOH_PPDD:P0103"]},{"id":"RAW_QGOH_PPDD:P0104","text":"From these it follows that no substance, and consequently no corporeal substance, insofar as it is substance, is divisible.","kind":"passage","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_QGOH_PPDD:P0104"]},{"id":"RAW_QGOH_PPDD:P0105","text":"SCHOLIUM","kind":"passage","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_QGOH_PPDD:P0105"]},{"id":"RAW_QGOH_PPDD:P0106","text":"That substance is indivisible is understood more simply from this alone: that the nature of substance cannot be conceived except as infinite, and that by a part of substance nothing else can be understood than finite substance, which (by Prop. 8) involves a manifest contradiction.","kind":"passage","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_QGOH_PPDD:P0106"]},{"id":"RAW_QGOH_PPDD:P0107","text":"PROPOSITION XIV","kind":"passage","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_QGOH_PPDD:P0107"]},{"id":"RAW_QGOH_PPDD:P0108","text":"Besides God, no substance can be given or conceived.","kind":"passage","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_QGOH_PPDD:P0108"]},{"id":"RAW_QGOH_PPDD:P0109","text":"DEMONSTRATION","kind":"passage","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_QGOH_PPDD:P0109"]},{"id":"RAW_QGOH_PPDD:P0110","text":"Since God is an absolutely infinite being, of whom no attribute that expresses the essence of substance can be denied (by Def. 6), and since He necessarily exists (by Prop. 11); if any substance besides God were given, it would have to be explained through some attribute of God, and thus two substances of the same attribute would exist, which (by Prop. 5) is absurd. Therefore, no substance can be given outside God, and consequently cannot be conceived either. For if it could be conceived, it would necessarily have to be conceived as existing; but this (by the first part of this Demonstration) is absurd. Therefore, outside God no substance can be given or conceived. Q. E. D.","kind":"passage","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_QGOH_PPDD:P0110"]},{"id":"RAW_QGOH_PPDD:P0111","text":"COROLLARY I","kind":"passage","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_QGOH_PPDD:P0111"]},{"id":"RAW_QGOH_PPDD:P0112","text":"From this it follows most clearly: I. that God is unique; that is (by Def. 6), that in the nature of things there is given only one substance, and that it is absolutely infinite, as we have already intimated in the Scholium to Prop. 10.","kind":"passage","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_QGOH_PPDD:P0112"]},{"id":"RAW_QGOH_PPDD:P0113","text":"COROLLARY II","kind":"passage","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_QGOH_PPDD:P0113"]},{"id":"RAW_QGOH_PPDD:P0114","text":"It follows: II. that an extended thing and a thinking thing are either attributes of God, or (by Axiom 1) affections of the attributes of God.","kind":"passage","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_QGOH_PPDD:P0114"]},{"id":"RAW_QGOH_PPDD:P0115","text":"PROPOSITION XV","kind":"passage","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_QGOH_PPDD:P0115"]},{"id":"RAW_QGOH_PPDD:P0116","text":"Whatever is, is in God, and nothing can be or be conceived without God.","kind":"passage","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_QGOH_PPDD:P0116"]},{"id":"RAW_QGOH_PPDD:P0117","text":"DEMONSTRATION","kind":"passage","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_QGOH_PPDD:P0117"]},{"id":"RAW_QGOH_PPDD:P0118","text":"Besides God, no substance is given or can be conceived (by Prop. 14), that is (by Def. 3), no thing which is in itself and is conceived through itself. But modes (by Def. 5) can neither be nor be conceived without substance; wherefore they can be in the divine nature alone, and be conceived through it alone. But besides substances and modes nothing is given (by Axiom 1). Therefore nothing can be or be conceived without God. Q. E. D.","kind":"passage","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_QGOH_PPDD:P0118"]},{"id":"RAW_QGOH_PPDD:P0119","text":"SCHOLIUM","kind":"passage","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_QGOH_PPDD:P0119"]}],"source_digest":"4c63c295f6d309b3c1a1c11f8fb880f8133375993b20c6dcdea035c580de7e1a","source_language_code":"la","original_title":"Pars prima - De Deo · passage 1"}],"passage_basis":"frozen-grounding","original_title":"Pars prima - De Deo · passage 1","title_language_code":"en"},{"id":"RAW_QGOH_PP_2","title":"Part One - Concerning God · passage 2","order":2,"chapter_id":"CH_QGOH_PP_2","text":"Sunt, qui Deum instar hominis corpore et mente constantem, atque passionibus obnoxium fingunt : sed, quam longe hi a vera Dei cognitione aberrent, satis ex jam demonstratis constat. Sed hos mitto : nam omnes, qui naturam divinam aliquo modo contemplati sunt, Deum esse corporeum negant. Quod etiam optime probant ex eo, quod per corpus intelligimus quamcunque quantitatem, longam, latam et profundam, certa aliqua figura terminatam, quo nihil absurdius de Deo, ente scilicet absolute infinito, dici potest. Attamen interim aliis rationibus, quibus hoc idem demonstrare conantur, clare ostendunt, se substantiam ipsam corpoream sive extensam a natura divina omnino removere, atque ipsam a Deo creatam statuunt. Ex qua autem divina potentia creari potuerit, prorsus ignorant : quod clare ostendit, illos id, quod ipsimet dicunt non intelligere. Ego saltem satis clare, meo quidem judicio, demonstravi (vide Coroll. Prop. 6 et Schol. 2 Prop. 8) nullam substantiam ab alio posse produci vel creari. Porro Prop. 14 ostendimus, præter Deum nullam dari neque concipi posse substantiam ; atque hinc conclusimus substantiam extensam unum ex infinitis Dei attributis esse. Verum, ad pleniorem explicationem, adversariorum argumenta refutabo, quæ omnia huc redeunt. Primo, quod substantia corporea, quatenus substantia, constat, ut putant, partibus ; et ideo eandem infinitam posse esse, et consequenter ad Deum pertinere posse, negant. Atque hoc multis exemplis explicant ex quibus unum aut alterum afferam. Si substantia corporea, ajunt, est infinita, concipiatur in duas partes dividi ; erit unaquæque pars vel finita, vel infinita. Si illud, componitur ergo infinitum ex duabus partibus finitis, quod est absurdum. Si hoc, datur ergo infinitum duplo majus alio infinito, quod etiam est absurdum. Porro, si quantitas infinita mensuretur partibus pedes æquantibus, infinitis talibus partibus constare debebit ; ut et, si partibus mensuretur digitos æquantibus ; ac propterea unus numerus infinitus erit duodecies major alio infinito. Denique, si ex uno puncto infinitæ cujusdam quantitatis concipiatur, duas lineas, ut AB, AC, certa ac determinata in initio distantia in infinitum protendi ; certum est, distantiam inter B et C continuo augeri, et tandem ex determinata indeterminabilem fore. Cum igitur hæc absurda sequantur, ut putant, ex eo, quod quantitas infinita supponitur : inde concludunt, substantiam corpoream debere esse finitam, et consequenter ad Dei essentiam non pertinere. Secundum argumentum petitur etiam a summa Dei perfectione. Deus enim, inquiunt, cum sit ens summe perfectum, pati non potest : atqui substantia corporea, quandoquidem divisibilis est, pati potest, sequitur ergo, ipsam ad Dei essentiam non pertinere. Hæc sunt, quæ apud scriptores invenio argumenta, quibus ostendere conantur, substantiam corpoream divina natura indignam esse, nec ad eandem posse pertinere. Verum enim vero, si quis recte attendat, me ad hæc jam respondisse comperiet ; quandoquidem hæc argumenta in eo tantum fundantur, quod substantiam corpoream ex partibus componi supponunt, quod jam (Prop. 12, cum Coroll. Prop. 13) absurdum esse ostendi. Deinde si quis rem recte perpendere velit, videbit omnia illa absurda (siquidem omnia absurda sunt, de quo jam non disputo), ex quibus concludere volunt, substantiam extensam finitam esse, minime ex eo sequi, quod quantitas infinita supponatur : sed quod quantitatem infinitam mensurabilem, et ex partibus finitis conflari supponunt ; quare ex absurdis, quæ inde sequuntur, nihil aliud concludere possunt ; quam quod quantitas infinita non sit mensurabilis, et quod ex partibus finitis conflari non possit. Atque hoc idem est, quod nos supra (Proposit. 12, etc.) jam demonstravimus. Quare telum, quod in nos intendunt, in se ipsos revera conjiciunt. Si igitur ipsi ex suo hoc absurdo concludere tamen volunt, substantiam extensam debere esse finitam, nihil aliud hercle faciunt, quam si quis ex eo, quod finxit circulum quadrati proprietates habere, concludit, circulum non habere centrum, ex quo omnes ad circumferentiam ductæ linæ sunt æquales. Nam substantiam corpoream, quæ non nisi infinita, non nisi unica, et non nisi indivisibilis potest concipi (vid. Prop. 8, 5 et 12), eam ipsi ad concludendum, eandem esse finitam, ex partibus finitis conflari, et multiplicem esse et divisibilem concipiunt. Sic etiam alii, postquam fingunt lineam ex punctis componi, multa sciunt invenire argumenta, quibus ostendant lineam non posse in infinitum dividi. Et profecto non minus absurdum est ponere, quod substantia corporea ex corporibus sive partibus componatur, quam quod corpus ex superficiebus, superficies ex lineis, lineæ denique ex punctis componantur. Atque hoc omnes, qui claram rationem infallibilem esse sciunt, fateri debent, et imprimis ii, qui negant dari vacuum. Nam si substantia corporea ita posset dividi, ut ejus partes realiter distinctæ essent ; cur ergo una pars non posset annihilari, manentibus reliquis ut ante inter se connexis ? Et cur omnes ita aptari debent, ne detur vacuum ? Sane rerum, quæ realiter ab invicem distinctæ sunt, una sine alia esse et in suo statu manere potest. Cum igitur vacuum in Natura non detur (de quo alias), sed omnes partes ita concurrere debent, ne detur vacuum ; sequitur hinc etiam, easdem non posse realiter distingui, hoc est, substantiam corpoream, quatenus substantia est, non posse dividi. Si quis tamen jam quærat, cur nos ex natura ita propensi simus ad dividendam quantitatem ? Ei respondeo, quod quantitas duobus modis a nobis concipitur, abstracte scilicet, sive superficialiter, prout nempe ipsam imaginamur, vel ut substantia, quod a solo intellectu fit. Si itaque ad quantitatem attendimus, prout in imaginatione est, quod sæpe et facilius a nobis fit, reperietur finita, divisibilis, et ex partibus conflata ; si autem ad ipsam prout in intellectu est attendimus, et eam quatenus substantia est concipimus, quod difficillime fit, tum, ut jam satis demonstravimus, infinita, unica, et indivisibilis reperietur. Quod omnibus, qui inter imaginationem et intellectum distinguere sciverint, satis manifestum erit : præcipue si ad hoc etiam attendatur, quod materia ubique eadem est, nec partes in eadem distinguuntur, nisi quatenus materiam diversimode affectam esse concipimus, unde ejus partes modaliter tantum distinguuntur, non autem realiter. Ex. gr. aquam, quatenus aqua est, dividi concipimus, ejusque partes ab invicem separari ; at non quatenus substantia est corporea ; eatenus enim neque separatur neque dividitur. Porro, aqua, quatenus aqua, generatur et corrumpitur ; at, quatenus substantia, nec generatur nec corrumpitur. Atque his me ad secundum argumentum etiam respondisse puto : quandoquidem id in eo etiam fundatur, quod materia, quatenus substantia, divisibilis sit et ex partibus confletur. Et quamvis hoc non esset, nescio, cur divina natura indigna esset : quandoquidem (per Prop. 14) extra Deum nulla substantia dari potest, a qua ipsa pateretur. Omnia, inquam, in Deo sunt, et omnia, quæ fiunt, per solas leges infinitæ Dei naturæ fiunt, et ex necessitate ejus essentiæ (ut mox ostendam) sequuntur ; quare nulla ratione dici potest, Deum ab alio pati, aut substantiam extensam divina natura indignam esse, tametsi divisibilis supponatur, dummodo æterna et infinita concedatur. Sed de his impræsentiarum satis.\n\nPROPOSITIO XVI\n\nEx necessitate divinæ naturæ infinita infinitis modis (hoc est omnia, quæ sub inlellectum infinitum cadere possunt) sequi debent.\n\nDEMONSTRATIO\n\nHæc Propositio unicuique manifesta esse debet, si modo ad hoc attendat, quod ex data cujuscunque rei definitione plures proprietates intellectus concludit, quæ revera ex eadem (hoc est, ipsa rei essentia) necessario sequuntur, et eo plures, quo plus realitatis rei definitio exprimit, hoc est, quo plus realitatis rei definitæ essentia involvit. Cum autem natura divina infinita absolute attributa habeat (per Defin. 6), quorum etiam unumquodque infinitam essentiam in suo genere exprimit, ex. ejusdem ergo necessitate infinita infinitis modis (hoc est omnia, quæ sub intellectum infinitum cadere possunt) necessario sequi debent. Q. E. D.\n\nCOROLLARIUM I\n\nHinc sequitur I°, Deum omnium rerum, quæ sub intellectum infinitum cadere possunt, esse causam efficientem.\n\nCOROLLARIUM II\n\nSequitur II°, Deum causam esse per se, non vero per accidens.\n\nCOROLLARIUM III\n\nSequitur III°, Deum esse absolute causam primam.\n\nPROPOSITIO XVII\n\nDeus ex solis suæ naturæ legibus, et a nemine coactus agit.\n\nDEMONSTRATIO\n\nEx sola divinæ naturæ necessitate, vel (quod idem est) ex solis ejusdem naturæ legibus, infinita absolute sequi, modo Prop. 16 ostendimus ; et Prop. 15 demonstravimus, nihil sine Deo esse nec concipi posse, sed omnia in Deo esse ; quare nihil extra ipsum esse potest, a quo ad agendum determinetur vel cogatur, atque adeo Deus ex solis suæ naturæ legibus et a nemine coactus agit. Q. E. D.\n\nCOROLLARIUM I\n\nHinc sequitur : I° nullam dari causam, quae Deum extrinsece vel intrinsece, præter ipsius naturæ perfectionem, incitet ad agendum.\n\nCOROLLARIUM II\n\nSequitur : II° solum Deum esse causam liberam. Deus enim solus ex sola suæ naturæ necessitate existit (per Prop. 11 et Coroll. 1 Prop. 14), et ex sola suæ naturæ necessitate agit (per Prop. præced.). Adeoque (per Defin. 7) solus est causa libera. Q. E. D.\n\nSCHOLIUM\n\nAlii putant, Deum esse causam liberam propterea quod potest, ut putant, efficere, ut ea, quæ ex ejus natura sequi diximus, hoc est quæ in ejus potestate sunt, non fiant, sive ut ab ipso non producantur. Sed hoc idem est ac si dicerent, quod Deus potest efficere, ut ex natura trianguli non sequatur, ejus tres angulos æquales esse duobus rectis ; sive ut ex data causa non sequatur effectus, quod est absurdum. Porro infra absque ope hujus Propositionis ostendam, ad Dei naturam neque intellectum neque voluntatem pertinere. Scio equidem plures esse, qui putant se posse demonstrare, ad Dei naturam summum intellectum et liberam voluntatem pertinere ; nihil enim perfectius cognoscere sese ajunt, quod Deo tribuere possunt, quam id, quod in nobis summa est perfectio. Porro, tametsi Deum actu summe intelligentem concipiant, non tamen credunt, eum posse omnia quæ actu intelligit efficere ut existant, nam se eo modo Dei potentiam destruere putant. Si omnia, inquiunt, quæ in ejus intellectu sunt, creavisset, nihil tum amplius creare potuisset, quod credunt Dei omnipotentiæ repugnare ; ideoque maluerunt Deum ad omnia indifferentem statuere, nec aliud creantem præter id, quod absoluta quadam voluntate decrevit creare. Verum ego me satis clare ostendisse puto (vid. Prop. 16), a summa Dei potentia, sive infinita natura, infinita infinitis modis, hoc est omnia, necessario effluxisse, vel semper eadem necessitate sequi : eodem modo ac ex natura trianguli ab æterno et in æternum sequitur, ejus tres angulos æquales esse duobus rectis. Quare Dei omnipotentia actu ab æterno fuit, et in æternum in eadem actualitate manebit. Et hoc modo Dei omnipotentia longe, meo quidem judicio, perfectior statuitur. Imo adversarii Dei omnipotentiam (liceat aperte loqui) negare videntur. Coguntur enim fateri, Deum infinita creabilia intelligere, quæ tamen nunquam creare poterit. Non alias, si scilicet omnia ; quæ intelligit, crearet, suam, juxta ipsos, exhauriret omnipotentiam, et se imperfectum redderet. Ut igitur Deum perfectum statuant, eo rediguntur, ut simul statuere debeant, ipsum non posse omnia efficere, ad quæ ejus potentia se extendit, quo absurdius, aut Dei omnipotentiæ magis repugnans, non video quid fingi possit. Porro, ut de intellectu et voluntate, quos Deo communiter tribuimus, hic etiam aliquid dicam : si ad æternam Dei essentiam intellectus scilicet et voluntas pertinent, aliud sane per utrumque hoc attributum intelligendum est, quam quod vulgo solent homines. Nam intellectus et voluntas, qui Dei essentiam constituerent, a nostro intellectu et voluntate toto cœlo differre deberent, nec in ulla re, præterquam in nomine, convenire possent ; non aliter scilicet, quam inter se conveniunt canis, signum cœleste, et canis, animal latrans. Quod sic demonstrabo. Si intellectus ad divinam naturam pertinet, non poterit, uti noster intellectus, posterior (ut plerisque placet), vel simul natura esse cum rebus intellectis, quando quidem Deus omnibus rebus prior est causalitate (per Coroll. 1 Prop. 16) ; sed contra veritas et formalis rerum essentia ideo talis est, quia talis in Dei intellectu existit objective. Quare Dei intellectus, quatenus Dei essentiam constituere concipitur, est revera causa rerum, tam earum essentiæ quam earum existentiæ ; quod ab iis videtur etiam fuisse animadversum, qui Dei intellectum, voluntatem, et potentiam unum et idem esse asseruerunt. Cum itaque Dei intellectus sit unica rerum causa, videlicet (ut ostendimus) tam earum essentiæ quam earum existentiæ, debet ipse necessario ab iisdem differre, tam ratione essentiæ quam ratione existentiæ. Nam causatum differt a sua causa præcise in eo, quod a causa habet. Ex. gr. homo est causa existentiæ non vero essentiæ alterius hominis ; est enim hæc æterna veritas : et ideo secundum essentiam prorsus convenire possunt ; in existendo autem differre debent ; et propterea, si unius existentia pereat, non ideo alterius peribit ; sed si unius essentia destrui posset et fieri falsa, destrueretur etiam alterius essentia. Quapropter res, quæ et essentiæ et existentiæ alicujus effectus est causa, a tali effectu differre debet, tam ratione essentiæ quam ratione existentiæ. Atqui Dei intellectus est et essentiæ et existentiæ nostri intellectus causa : ergo Dei intellectus, quatenus divinam essentiam constituere concipitur, a nostro intellectu tam ratione essentiæ quam ratione existentiæ differt, nec in ulla re, præterquam in nomine, cum eo convenire potest, ut volebamus. Circa voluntatem eodem modo proceditur, ut facile unusquisque videre potest.\n\nPROPOSITIO XVIII\n\nDeus est omnium rerum causa immanens, non vero transiens.\n\nDEMONSTRATIO\n\nOmnia quæ sunt, in Deo sunt et per Deum concipi debent (per Prop. 15) ; adeoque (per Coroll. 1 Prop. 16 hujus) Deus rerum, quæ in ipso sunt, est causa ; quod est primum. Deinde extra Deum nulla potest dari substantia (per Prop. 14), hoc est (per Defin. 3) res, quæ extra Deum in se sit ; quod erat secundum. Deus ergo est omnium rerum causa immanens, non vero transiens. Q. E. D.\n\nPROPOSITIO XIX\n\nDeus, sive omnia Dei attributa sunt æterna.\n\nDEMONSTRATIO\n\nDeus enim (per Defin. 6) est substantia, quæ (per Prop. 11) necessario existit, hoc est (per Prop. 7), ad cujus naturam pertinet existere, sive (quod idem est) ex cujus definitione sequitur ipsum existere, adeoque (per Defin. 8) est æternus. Deinde per Dei attributa intelligendum est id, quod (per Defin. 4) divinæ substantiæ essentiam exprimit, hoc est id, quod ad substantiam pertinet : id ipsum, inquam, ipsa attributa involvere debent. Atqui ad naturam substantiæ (ut jam ex Prop. 7 demonstravi) pertinet æternitas ; ergo unumquodque attributorum æternitatem involvere debet, adeoque omnia sunt æterna. Q. E. D.\n\nSCHOLIUM\n\nHæc Propositio quam clarissime etiam patet ex modo, quo (Prop. 11) Dei existentiam demonstravi ; ex ea, inquam, demonstratione constat, Dei existentiam, sicut ejus essentiam, æternam esse veritatem. Deinde (Prop. 19 Principiorum Cartesii) alio etiam modo Dei æternitatem demonstravi, nec opus est eum hic repetere.\n\nPROPOSITIO XX\n\nDei existentia ejusque essentia unum et idem sunt.\n\nDEMONSTRATIO\n\nDeus (per anteced. Prop.) ejusque omnia attributa sunt æterna, hoc est (per Defin. 8), unumquodque ejus attributorum existentiam exprimit. Eadem ergo Dei attributa, quæ (per Defin. 4) Dei æternam essentiam explicant, ejus simul æternam existentiam explicant, hoc est, illud ipsum, quod essentiam Dei constituit, constituit simul ipsius existentiam, adeoque hæc et ipsius essentia unum et idem sunt. Q. E. D.\n\nCOROLLARIUM I\n\nHinc sequitur : I° Dei existentiam, sicut ejus essentiam, æternam esse veritatem.\n\nCOROLLARIUM II\n\nSequitur : II°, Deum, sive omnia Dei attributa esse immutabilia. Nam si ratione existentiæ mutarentur, deberent etiam (per Prop. præced.) ratione essentiæ mutari, hoc est (ut per se notum) ex veris falsa fieri, quod est absurdum.\n\nPROPOSITIO XXI\n\nOmnia quæ ex absoluta natura alicujus attributi Dei sequuntur, semper et infinita existere debuerunt, sive per idem attributum æterna et infinita sunt.\n\nDEMONSTRATIO\n\nConcipe, si fieri potest (siquidem neges), aliquid in alique Dei attributo ex ipsius absoluta natura sequi, quod finitum sit, et determinatam habeat existentiam sive durationem, ex. gr. ideam Dei in cogitatione. At cogitatio, quandoquidem Dei attributum supponitur, est necessario (per Prop. 11) sua natura infinita. Verum, quatenus ipsa ideam Dei habet, finita supponitur. At (per Defin. 2) finita concipi non potest nisi per ipsam cogitationem determinetur. Sed non per ipsam cogitationem, quatenus ideam Dei constituit ; eatenus enim finita supponitur esse. Ergo per cogitationem, quatenus ideam Dei non constituit ; quæ tamen (per Prop. 11) necessario existere debet. Datur igitur cogitatio non constituens ideam Dei, ac propterea ex ejus natura, quatenus est absoluta cogitatio, non sequitur necessario idea Dei. (Concipitur enim ideam Dei constituens et non constituens.) Quod est contra hypothesin. Quare si idea Dei in cogitatione, aut aliquid (perinde est, quicquid sumatur, quandoquidem demonstratio universalis est) in aliquo Dei attributo ex necessitate absolutæ naturæ ipsius attributi sequatur, id debet necessario esse infinitum. Quod erat primum.\n\nDeinde id, quod ex necessitate naturæ alicujus attributi ita sequitur, non potest determinatam habere durationem. Nam, si neges, supponatur res, quæ ex necessitate naturæ alicujus attributi sequitur, dari in aliquo Dei attributo, ex. gr. idea Dei in cogitatione, eaque supponatur aliquando non exstitisse, vel non exstitura. Cum autem cogitatio Dei attributum supponatur, debet et necessario, et immutabilis existere (per Prop. 11 et Coroll. 2 Prop. 20). Quare ultra limites durationis ideæ Dei (supponitur enim aliquando non exstitisse, aut non exstitura) cogitatio sine idea Dei existere debebit. Atqui hoc est contra hypothesin ; supponitur enim, ex data cogitatione necessario sequi ideam Dei. Ergo idea Dei in cogitatione, aut aliquid, quod necessario ex absoluta natura alicujus attributi Dei sequitur, non potest determinatam habere durationem ; sed per idem attributum æternum est. Quod erat secundum. Nota, hoc idem esse affirmandum de quacunque re, quæ in aliquo Dei attributo ex Dei absoluta natura necessario sequitur.\n\nPROPOSITIO XXII\n\nQuicquid ex aliquo Dei attributo, quatenus modificatum est tali modificatione, quæ et necessario et infinita per idem existit, sequitur, debet quoque et necessario et infinitum existere.\n\nDEMONSTRATIO\n\nHujus Propositionis demonstratio procedit eodem modo ac demonstratio præcedentis.\n\nPROPOSITIO XXIII\n\nOmnis modus, qui et necessario et infinitus existit, necessario sequi debuit, vel ex absoluta natura alicujus attributi Dei, vel ex aliquo attributo modificato modificatione, quæ et necessario et infinita existit.\n\nDEMONSTRATIO\n\nModus enim in alio est, per quod concipi debet (per Defin. 5), hoc est (per Prop. 15), in solo Deo est, et per solum Deum concipi potest. Si ergo modus concipitur necessario existere, et infinitus esse, utrumque hoc debet necessario concludi sive percipi per aliquod Dei attributum, quatenus idem concipitur infinitatem et necessitatem existentiæ, sive (quod per Defin. 8 idem est) æternitatem exprimere, hoc est (per Defin. 6 et Prop. 19), quatenus absolute consideratur. Modus ergo, qui et necessario et infinitus existit, ex absoluta natura alicujus Dei attributi sequi debuit ; hocque vel immediate (de quo Prop. 21), vel mediante aliqua modificatione, quæ ex ejus absoluta natura sequitur, hoc est (per Prop. præced.), quæ et necesssario et infinita existit. Q. E. D.\n\nPROPOSITIO XXIV\n\nRerum a Deo productarum essentia non involvit existentiam.\n\nDEMONSTRATIO\n\nPatet ex Definitione I. Id enim, cujus natura (in se scilicet considerata) involvit existentiam, causa est sui, et ex sola suæ naturæ necessitate existit.\n\nCOROLLARIUM\n\nHinc sequitur, Deum non tantum esse causam, ut res incipiant existere ; sed etiam ut in existendo perseverent, sive (ut termino Scholastico utar) Deum esse causam essendi rerum. Nam, sive res existant sive non existant, quotiescunque ad earum essentiam attendimus, eandem nec existentiam nec durationem involvere comperimus ; adeoque earum essentia neque suas existentiæ neque suæ durationis potest esse causa; sed tantum Deus, ad cujus solam naturam pertinet existere (per Coroll. 1 Prop. 14).\n\nPROPOSITIO XXV\n\nDeus non tantum est causa efficiens rerum existentiæ, sed etiam essentiæ.\n\nDEMONSTRATIO\n\nSi negas, ergo rerum essentiæ Deus non est causa ; adeoque (per Axioma 4) potest rerum essentia sine Deo concipi : atqui hoc (per Prop. 15) est absurdum. Ergo rerum etiam essentiæ Deus est causa. Q. E. D.\n\nSCHOLIIUM\n\nHæc Propositio clarius sequitur ex Propositione 16. Ex ea enim sequitur, quod ex data natura divina, tam rerum essentia quam existentia debeat necessario concludi ; et, ut verbo dicam, eo sensu, quo Deus dicitur causa sui, etiam omnium rerum causa dicendus est, quod adhuc clarius ex sequenti Corollario constabit.\n\nCOROLLARIUM\n\nRes particulares nihil sunt, nisi Dei attributorum affectiones, sive modi, quibus Dei attributa certo et determinato modo exprimuntur. Demonstratio patet ex Propositione 15 et Definitione 5.\n\nPROPOSITIO XXVI\n\nRes, quæ ad aliquid operandum determinata est, a Deo necessario fuit determinata ; et, quæ a Deo non est determinata, non potest se ipsam ad operandum determinare.\n\nDEMONSTRATIO\n\nId, per quod res determinatæ ad aliquid operandum dicuntur, necessario quid positivum est (ut per se notum) ; adeoque tam ejus essentiæ quam existentiæ Deus ex necessitate suæ naturæ est causa efficiens (per Prop. 25 et 16) ; quod erat primum. Ex quo etiam, quod secundo proponitur, clarissime sequitur. Nam si res, quæ a Deo determinata non est, se ipsam determinare posset, prima pars hujus falsa esset ; quod est absurdum, ut ostendimus.\n\nPROPOSITIO XXVII\n\nRes, quæ a Deo ad aliquid operandum determinata est, se ipsam indeterminatam reddere non potest.\n\nDEMONSTRATIO\n\nHæc Propositio patet ex Axiomate tertio.\n\nPROPOSITIO XXVIII\n\nQuodcunque singulare, sive quævis res, quæ finita est, et determinatam habet existentiam, non potest existere, nec ad operandum determinari, nisi ad existendum et operandum determinetur ab alia causa, quæ etiam finita est et determinatam habet existentiam : et rursus hæc causa non potest etiam existere, neque ad operandum determinari, nisi ab alia, quæ etiam finita est et determinatam habet existentiam, determinetur ad existendum et operandum, et sic in infinitum.\n\nDEMONSTRATIO","paragraphs":[{"id":"RAW_QGOH_PP_2:P0001","text":"Sunt, qui Deum instar hominis corpore et mente constantem, atque passionibus obnoxium fingunt : sed, quam longe hi a vera Dei cognitione aberrent, satis ex jam demonstratis constat. Sed hos mitto : nam omnes, qui naturam divinam aliquo modo contemplati sunt, Deum esse corporeum negant. Quod etiam optime probant ex eo, quod per corpus intelligimus quamcunque quantitatem, longam, latam et profundam, certa aliqua figura terminatam, quo nihil absurdius de Deo, ente scilicet absolute infinito, dici potest. Attamen interim aliis rationibus, quibus hoc idem demonstrare conantur, clare ostendunt, se substantiam ipsam corpoream sive extensam a natura divina omnino removere, atque ipsam a Deo creatam statuunt. Ex qua autem divina potentia creari potuerit, prorsus ignorant : quod clare ostendit, illos id, quod ipsimet dicunt non intelligere.","kind":"passage"},{"id":"RAW_QGOH_PP_2:P0002","text":"Ego saltem satis clare, meo quidem judicio, demonstravi (vide Coroll. Prop. 6 et Schol. 2 Prop. 8) nullam substantiam ab alio posse produci vel creari. Porro Prop. 14 ostendimus, præter Deum nullam dari neque concipi posse substantiam ; atque hinc conclusimus substantiam extensam unum ex infinitis Dei attributis esse. Verum, ad pleniorem explicationem, adversariorum argumenta refutabo, quæ omnia huc redeunt. Primo, quod substantia corporea, quatenus substantia, constat, ut putant, partibus ; et ideo eandem infinitam posse esse, et consequenter ad Deum pertinere posse, negant. Atque hoc multis exemplis explicant ex quibus unum aut alterum afferam. Si substantia corporea, ajunt, est infinita, concipiatur in duas partes dividi ; erit unaquæque pars vel finita, vel infinita. Si illud, componitur ergo infinitum ex duabus partibus finitis, quod est absurdum.","kind":"passage"},{"id":"RAW_QGOH_PP_2:P0003","text":"Si hoc, datur ergo infinitum duplo majus alio infinito, quod etiam est absurdum. Porro, si quantitas infinita mensuretur partibus pedes æquantibus, infinitis talibus partibus constare debebit ; ut et, si partibus mensuretur digitos æquantibus ; ac propterea unus numerus infinitus erit duodecies major alio infinito. Denique, si ex uno puncto infinitæ cujusdam quantitatis concipiatur, duas lineas, ut AB, AC, certa ac determinata in initio distantia in infinitum protendi ; certum est, distantiam inter B et C continuo augeri, et tandem ex determinata indeterminabilem fore. Cum igitur hæc absurda sequantur, ut putant, ex eo, quod quantitas infinita supponitur : inde concludunt, substantiam corpoream debere esse finitam, et consequenter ad Dei essentiam non pertinere. Secundum argumentum petitur etiam a summa Dei perfectione.","kind":"passage"},{"id":"RAW_QGOH_PP_2:P0004","text":"Deus enim, inquiunt, cum sit ens summe perfectum, pati non potest : atqui substantia corporea, quandoquidem divisibilis est, pati potest, sequitur ergo, ipsam ad Dei essentiam non pertinere. Hæc sunt, quæ apud scriptores invenio argumenta, quibus ostendere conantur, substantiam corpoream divina natura indignam esse, nec ad eandem posse pertinere. Verum enim vero, si quis recte attendat, me ad hæc jam respondisse comperiet ; quandoquidem hæc argumenta in eo tantum fundantur, quod substantiam corpoream ex partibus componi supponunt, quod jam (Prop. 12, cum Coroll. Prop. 13) absurdum esse ostendi.","kind":"passage"},{"id":"RAW_QGOH_PP_2:P0005","text":"Deinde si quis rem recte perpendere velit, videbit omnia illa absurda (siquidem omnia absurda sunt, de quo jam non disputo), ex quibus concludere volunt, substantiam extensam finitam esse, minime ex eo sequi, quod quantitas infinita supponatur : sed quod quantitatem infinitam mensurabilem, et ex partibus finitis conflari supponunt ; quare ex absurdis, quæ inde sequuntur, nihil aliud concludere possunt ; quam quod quantitas infinita non sit mensurabilis, et quod ex partibus finitis conflari non possit. Atque hoc idem est, quod nos supra (Proposit. 12, etc.) jam demonstravimus. Quare telum, quod in nos intendunt, in se ipsos revera conjiciunt.","kind":"passage"},{"id":"RAW_QGOH_PP_2:P0006","text":"Si igitur ipsi ex suo hoc absurdo concludere tamen volunt, substantiam extensam debere esse finitam, nihil aliud hercle faciunt, quam si quis ex eo, quod finxit circulum quadrati proprietates habere, concludit, circulum non habere centrum, ex quo omnes ad circumferentiam ductæ linæ sunt æquales. Nam substantiam corpoream, quæ non nisi infinita, non nisi unica, et non nisi indivisibilis potest concipi (vid. Prop. 8, 5 et 12), eam ipsi ad concludendum, eandem esse finitam, ex partibus finitis conflari, et multiplicem esse et divisibilem concipiunt. Sic etiam alii, postquam fingunt lineam ex punctis componi, multa sciunt invenire argumenta, quibus ostendant lineam non posse in infinitum dividi.","kind":"passage"},{"id":"RAW_QGOH_PP_2:P0007","text":"Et profecto non minus absurdum est ponere, quod substantia corporea ex corporibus sive partibus componatur, quam quod corpus ex superficiebus, superficies ex lineis, lineæ denique ex punctis componantur. Atque hoc omnes, qui claram rationem infallibilem esse sciunt, fateri debent, et imprimis ii, qui negant dari vacuum. Nam si substantia corporea ita posset dividi, ut ejus partes realiter distinctæ essent ; cur ergo una pars non posset annihilari, manentibus reliquis ut ante inter se connexis ? Et cur omnes ita aptari debent, ne detur vacuum ? Sane rerum, quæ realiter ab invicem distinctæ sunt, una sine alia esse et in suo statu manere potest. Cum igitur vacuum in Natura non detur (de quo alias), sed omnes partes ita concurrere debent, ne detur vacuum ; sequitur hinc etiam, easdem non posse realiter distingui, hoc est, substantiam corpoream, quatenus substantia est, non posse dividi.","kind":"passage"},{"id":"RAW_QGOH_PP_2:P0008","text":"Si quis tamen jam quærat, cur nos ex natura ita propensi simus ad dividendam quantitatem ? Ei respondeo, quod quantitas duobus modis a nobis concipitur, abstracte scilicet, sive superficialiter, prout nempe ipsam imaginamur, vel ut substantia, quod a solo intellectu fit. Si itaque ad quantitatem attendimus, prout in imaginatione est, quod sæpe et facilius a nobis fit, reperietur finita, divisibilis, et ex partibus conflata ; si autem ad ipsam prout in intellectu est attendimus, et eam quatenus substantia est concipimus, quod difficillime fit, tum, ut jam satis demonstravimus, infinita, unica, et indivisibilis reperietur.","kind":"passage"},{"id":"RAW_QGOH_PP_2:P0009","text":"Quod omnibus, qui inter imaginationem et intellectum distinguere sciverint, satis manifestum erit : præcipue si ad hoc etiam attendatur, quod materia ubique eadem est, nec partes in eadem distinguuntur, nisi quatenus materiam diversimode affectam esse concipimus, unde ejus partes modaliter tantum distinguuntur, non autem realiter. Ex. gr. aquam, quatenus aqua est, dividi concipimus, ejusque partes ab invicem separari ; at non quatenus substantia est corporea ; eatenus enim neque separatur neque dividitur. Porro, aqua, quatenus aqua, generatur et corrumpitur ; at, quatenus substantia, nec generatur nec corrumpitur. Atque his me ad secundum argumentum etiam respondisse puto : quandoquidem id in eo etiam fundatur, quod materia, quatenus substantia, divisibilis sit et ex partibus confletur. Et quamvis hoc non esset, nescio, cur divina natura indigna esset : quandoquidem (per Prop.","kind":"passage"},{"id":"RAW_QGOH_PP_2:P0010","text":"14) extra Deum nulla substantia dari potest, a qua ipsa pateretur. Omnia, inquam, in Deo sunt, et omnia, quæ fiunt, per solas leges infinitæ Dei naturæ fiunt, et ex necessitate ejus essentiæ (ut mox ostendam) sequuntur ; quare nulla ratione dici potest, Deum ab alio pati, aut substantiam extensam divina natura indignam esse, tametsi divisibilis supponatur, dummodo æterna et infinita concedatur. Sed de his impræsentiarum satis.","kind":"passage"},{"id":"RAW_QGOH_PP_2:P0011","text":"PROPOSITIO XVI","kind":"passage"},{"id":"RAW_QGOH_PP_2:P0012","text":"Ex necessitate divinæ naturæ infinita infinitis modis (hoc est omnia, quæ sub inlellectum infinitum cadere possunt) sequi debent.","kind":"passage"},{"id":"RAW_QGOH_PP_2:P0013","text":"DEMONSTRATIO","kind":"passage"},{"id":"RAW_QGOH_PP_2:P0014","text":"Hæc Propositio unicuique manifesta esse debet, si modo ad hoc attendat, quod ex data cujuscunque rei definitione plures proprietates intellectus concludit, quæ revera ex eadem (hoc est, ipsa rei essentia) necessario sequuntur, et eo plures, quo plus realitatis rei definitio exprimit, hoc est, quo plus realitatis rei definitæ essentia involvit. Cum autem natura divina infinita absolute attributa habeat (per Defin. 6), quorum etiam unumquodque infinitam essentiam in suo genere exprimit, ex. ejusdem ergo necessitate infinita infinitis modis (hoc est omnia, quæ sub intellectum infinitum cadere possunt) necessario sequi debent. Q. E. D.","kind":"passage"},{"id":"RAW_QGOH_PP_2:P0015","text":"COROLLARIUM I","kind":"passage"},{"id":"RAW_QGOH_PP_2:P0016","text":"Hinc sequitur I°, Deum omnium rerum, quæ sub intellectum infinitum cadere possunt, esse causam efficientem.","kind":"passage"},{"id":"RAW_QGOH_PP_2:P0017","text":"COROLLARIUM II","kind":"passage"},{"id":"RAW_QGOH_PP_2:P0018","text":"Sequitur II°, Deum causam esse per se, non vero per accidens.","kind":"passage"},{"id":"RAW_QGOH_PP_2:P0019","text":"COROLLARIUM III","kind":"passage"},{"id":"RAW_QGOH_PP_2:P0020","text":"Sequitur III°, Deum esse absolute causam primam.","kind":"passage"},{"id":"RAW_QGOH_PP_2:P0021","text":"PROPOSITIO XVII","kind":"passage"},{"id":"RAW_QGOH_PP_2:P0022","text":"Deus ex solis suæ naturæ legibus, et a nemine coactus agit.","kind":"passage"},{"id":"RAW_QGOH_PP_2:P0023","text":"DEMONSTRATIO","kind":"passage"},{"id":"RAW_QGOH_PP_2:P0024","text":"Ex sola divinæ naturæ necessitate, vel (quod idem est) ex solis ejusdem naturæ legibus, infinita absolute sequi, modo Prop. 16 ostendimus ; et Prop. 15 demonstravimus, nihil sine Deo esse nec concipi posse, sed omnia in Deo esse ; quare nihil extra ipsum esse potest, a quo ad agendum determinetur vel cogatur, atque adeo Deus ex solis suæ naturæ legibus et a nemine coactus agit. Q. E. D.","kind":"passage"},{"id":"RAW_QGOH_PP_2:P0025","text":"COROLLARIUM I","kind":"passage"},{"id":"RAW_QGOH_PP_2:P0026","text":"Hinc sequitur : I° nullam dari causam, quae Deum extrinsece vel intrinsece, præter ipsius naturæ perfectionem, incitet ad agendum.","kind":"passage"},{"id":"RAW_QGOH_PP_2:P0027","text":"COROLLARIUM II","kind":"passage"},{"id":"RAW_QGOH_PP_2:P0028","text":"Sequitur : II° solum Deum esse causam liberam. Deus enim solus ex sola suæ naturæ necessitate existit (per Prop. 11 et Coroll. 1 Prop. 14), et ex sola suæ naturæ necessitate agit (per Prop. præced.). Adeoque (per Defin. 7) solus est causa libera. Q. E. D.","kind":"passage"},{"id":"RAW_QGOH_PP_2:P0029","text":"SCHOLIUM","kind":"passage"},{"id":"RAW_QGOH_PP_2:P0030","text":"Alii putant, Deum esse causam liberam propterea quod potest, ut putant, efficere, ut ea, quæ ex ejus natura sequi diximus, hoc est quæ in ejus potestate sunt, non fiant, sive ut ab ipso non producantur. Sed hoc idem est ac si dicerent, quod Deus potest efficere, ut ex natura trianguli non sequatur, ejus tres angulos æquales esse duobus rectis ; sive ut ex data causa non sequatur effectus, quod est absurdum. Porro infra absque ope hujus Propositionis ostendam, ad Dei naturam neque intellectum neque voluntatem pertinere. Scio equidem plures esse, qui putant se posse demonstrare, ad Dei naturam summum intellectum et liberam voluntatem pertinere ; nihil enim perfectius cognoscere sese ajunt, quod Deo tribuere possunt, quam id, quod in nobis summa est perfectio.","kind":"passage"},{"id":"RAW_QGOH_PP_2:P0031","text":"Porro, tametsi Deum actu summe intelligentem concipiant, non tamen credunt, eum posse omnia quæ actu intelligit efficere ut existant, nam se eo modo Dei potentiam destruere putant. Si omnia, inquiunt, quæ in ejus intellectu sunt, creavisset, nihil tum amplius creare potuisset, quod credunt Dei omnipotentiæ repugnare ; ideoque maluerunt Deum ad omnia indifferentem statuere, nec aliud creantem præter id, quod absoluta quadam voluntate decrevit creare. Verum ego me satis clare ostendisse puto (vid. Prop. 16), a summa Dei potentia, sive infinita natura, infinita infinitis modis, hoc est omnia, necessario effluxisse, vel semper eadem necessitate sequi : eodem modo ac ex natura trianguli ab æterno et in æternum sequitur, ejus tres angulos æquales esse duobus rectis. Quare Dei omnipotentia actu ab æterno fuit, et in æternum in eadem actualitate manebit.","kind":"passage"},{"id":"RAW_QGOH_PP_2:P0032","text":"Et hoc modo Dei omnipotentia longe, meo quidem judicio, perfectior statuitur. Imo adversarii Dei omnipotentiam (liceat aperte loqui) negare videntur. Coguntur enim fateri, Deum infinita creabilia intelligere, quæ tamen nunquam creare poterit. Non alias, si scilicet omnia ; quæ intelligit, crearet, suam, juxta ipsos, exhauriret omnipotentiam, et se imperfectum redderet. Ut igitur Deum perfectum statuant, eo rediguntur, ut simul statuere debeant, ipsum non posse omnia efficere, ad quæ ejus potentia se extendit, quo absurdius, aut Dei omnipotentiæ magis repugnans, non video quid fingi possit. Porro, ut de intellectu et voluntate, quos Deo communiter tribuimus, hic etiam aliquid dicam : si ad æternam Dei essentiam intellectus scilicet et voluntas pertinent, aliud sane per utrumque hoc attributum intelligendum est, quam quod vulgo solent homines.","kind":"passage"},{"id":"RAW_QGOH_PP_2:P0033","text":"Nam intellectus et voluntas, qui Dei essentiam constituerent, a nostro intellectu et voluntate toto cœlo differre deberent, nec in ulla re, præterquam in nomine, convenire possent ; non aliter scilicet, quam inter se conveniunt canis, signum cœleste, et canis, animal latrans. Quod sic demonstrabo. Si intellectus ad divinam naturam pertinet, non poterit, uti noster intellectus, posterior (ut plerisque placet), vel simul natura esse cum rebus intellectis, quando quidem Deus omnibus rebus prior est causalitate (per Coroll. 1 Prop. 16) ; sed contra veritas et formalis rerum essentia ideo talis est, quia talis in Dei intellectu existit objective.","kind":"passage"},{"id":"RAW_QGOH_PP_2:P0034","text":"Quare Dei intellectus, quatenus Dei essentiam constituere concipitur, est revera causa rerum, tam earum essentiæ quam earum existentiæ ; quod ab iis videtur etiam fuisse animadversum, qui Dei intellectum, voluntatem, et potentiam unum et idem esse asseruerunt. Cum itaque Dei intellectus sit unica rerum causa, videlicet (ut ostendimus) tam earum essentiæ quam earum existentiæ, debet ipse necessario ab iisdem differre, tam ratione essentiæ quam ratione existentiæ. Nam causatum differt a sua causa præcise in eo, quod a causa habet. Ex. gr. homo est causa existentiæ non vero essentiæ alterius hominis ; est enim hæc æterna veritas : et ideo secundum essentiam prorsus convenire possunt ; in existendo autem differre debent ; et propterea, si unius existentia pereat, non ideo alterius peribit ; sed si unius essentia destrui posset et fieri falsa, destrueretur etiam alterius essentia.","kind":"passage"},{"id":"RAW_QGOH_PP_2:P0035","text":"Quapropter res, quæ et essentiæ et existentiæ alicujus effectus est causa, a tali effectu differre debet, tam ratione essentiæ quam ratione existentiæ. Atqui Dei intellectus est et essentiæ et existentiæ nostri intellectus causa : ergo Dei intellectus, quatenus divinam essentiam constituere concipitur, a nostro intellectu tam ratione essentiæ quam ratione existentiæ differt, nec in ulla re, præterquam in nomine, cum eo convenire potest, ut volebamus. Circa voluntatem eodem modo proceditur, ut facile unusquisque videre potest.","kind":"passage"},{"id":"RAW_QGOH_PP_2:P0036","text":"PROPOSITIO XVIII","kind":"passage"},{"id":"RAW_QGOH_PP_2:P0037","text":"Deus est omnium rerum causa immanens, non vero transiens.","kind":"passage"},{"id":"RAW_QGOH_PP_2:P0038","text":"DEMONSTRATIO","kind":"passage"},{"id":"RAW_QGOH_PP_2:P0039","text":"Omnia quæ sunt, in Deo sunt et per Deum concipi debent (per Prop. 15) ; adeoque (per Coroll. 1 Prop. 16 hujus) Deus rerum, quæ in ipso sunt, est causa ; quod est primum. Deinde extra Deum nulla potest dari substantia (per Prop. 14), hoc est (per Defin. 3) res, quæ extra Deum in se sit ; quod erat secundum. Deus ergo est omnium rerum causa immanens, non vero transiens. Q. E. D.","kind":"passage"},{"id":"RAW_QGOH_PP_2:P0040","text":"PROPOSITIO XIX","kind":"passage"},{"id":"RAW_QGOH_PP_2:P0041","text":"Deus, sive omnia Dei attributa sunt æterna.","kind":"passage"},{"id":"RAW_QGOH_PP_2:P0042","text":"DEMONSTRATIO","kind":"passage"},{"id":"RAW_QGOH_PP_2:P0043","text":"Deus enim (per Defin. 6) est substantia, quæ (per Prop. 11) necessario existit, hoc est (per Prop. 7), ad cujus naturam pertinet existere, sive (quod idem est) ex cujus definitione sequitur ipsum existere, adeoque (per Defin. 8) est æternus. Deinde per Dei attributa intelligendum est id, quod (per Defin. 4) divinæ substantiæ essentiam exprimit, hoc est id, quod ad substantiam pertinet : id ipsum, inquam, ipsa attributa involvere debent. Atqui ad naturam substantiæ (ut jam ex Prop. 7 demonstravi) pertinet æternitas ; ergo unumquodque attributorum æternitatem involvere debet, adeoque omnia sunt æterna. Q. E. D.","kind":"passage"},{"id":"RAW_QGOH_PP_2:P0044","text":"SCHOLIUM","kind":"passage"},{"id":"RAW_QGOH_PP_2:P0045","text":"Hæc Propositio quam clarissime etiam patet ex modo, quo (Prop. 11) Dei existentiam demonstravi ; ex ea, inquam, demonstratione constat, Dei existentiam, sicut ejus essentiam, æternam esse veritatem. Deinde (Prop. 19 Principiorum Cartesii) alio etiam modo Dei æternitatem demonstravi, nec opus est eum hic repetere.","kind":"passage"},{"id":"RAW_QGOH_PP_2:P0046","text":"PROPOSITIO XX","kind":"passage"},{"id":"RAW_QGOH_PP_2:P0047","text":"Dei existentia ejusque essentia unum et idem sunt.","kind":"passage"},{"id":"RAW_QGOH_PP_2:P0048","text":"DEMONSTRATIO","kind":"passage"},{"id":"RAW_QGOH_PP_2:P0049","text":"Deus (per anteced. Prop.) ejusque omnia attributa sunt æterna, hoc est (per Defin. 8), unumquodque ejus attributorum existentiam exprimit. Eadem ergo Dei attributa, quæ (per Defin. 4) Dei æternam essentiam explicant, ejus simul æternam existentiam explicant, hoc est, illud ipsum, quod essentiam Dei constituit, constituit simul ipsius existentiam, adeoque hæc et ipsius essentia unum et idem sunt. Q. E. D.","kind":"passage"},{"id":"RAW_QGOH_PP_2:P0050","text":"COROLLARIUM I","kind":"passage"},{"id":"RAW_QGOH_PP_2:P0051","text":"Hinc sequitur : I° Dei existentiam, sicut ejus essentiam, æternam esse veritatem.","kind":"passage"},{"id":"RAW_QGOH_PP_2:P0052","text":"COROLLARIUM II","kind":"passage"},{"id":"RAW_QGOH_PP_2:P0053","text":"Sequitur : II°, Deum, sive omnia Dei attributa esse immutabilia. Nam si ratione existentiæ mutarentur, deberent etiam (per Prop. præced.) ratione essentiæ mutari, hoc est (ut per se notum) ex veris falsa fieri, quod est absurdum.","kind":"passage"},{"id":"RAW_QGOH_PP_2:P0054","text":"PROPOSITIO XXI","kind":"passage"},{"id":"RAW_QGOH_PP_2:P0055","text":"Omnia quæ ex absoluta natura alicujus attributi Dei sequuntur, semper et infinita existere debuerunt, sive per idem attributum æterna et infinita sunt.","kind":"passage"},{"id":"RAW_QGOH_PP_2:P0056","text":"DEMONSTRATIO","kind":"passage"},{"id":"RAW_QGOH_PP_2:P0057","text":"Concipe, si fieri potest (siquidem neges), aliquid in alique Dei attributo ex ipsius absoluta natura sequi, quod finitum sit, et determinatam habeat existentiam sive durationem, ex. gr. ideam Dei in cogitatione. At cogitatio, quandoquidem Dei attributum supponitur, est necessario (per Prop. 11) sua natura infinita. Verum, quatenus ipsa ideam Dei habet, finita supponitur. At (per Defin. 2) finita concipi non potest nisi per ipsam cogitationem determinetur. Sed non per ipsam cogitationem, quatenus ideam Dei constituit ; eatenus enim finita supponitur esse. Ergo per cogitationem, quatenus ideam Dei non constituit ; quæ tamen (per Prop. 11) necessario existere debet. Datur igitur cogitatio non constituens ideam Dei, ac propterea ex ejus natura, quatenus est absoluta cogitatio, non sequitur necessario idea Dei. (Concipitur enim ideam Dei constituens et non constituens.)","kind":"passage"},{"id":"RAW_QGOH_PP_2:P0058","text":"Quod est contra hypothesin. Quare si idea Dei in cogitatione, aut aliquid (perinde est, quicquid sumatur, quandoquidem demonstratio universalis est) in aliquo Dei attributo ex necessitate absolutæ naturæ ipsius attributi sequatur, id debet necessario esse infinitum. Quod erat primum.","kind":"passage"},{"id":"RAW_QGOH_PP_2:P0059","text":"Deinde id, quod ex necessitate naturæ alicujus attributi ita sequitur, non potest determinatam habere durationem. Nam, si neges, supponatur res, quæ ex necessitate naturæ alicujus attributi sequitur, dari in aliquo Dei attributo, ex. gr. idea Dei in cogitatione, eaque supponatur aliquando non exstitisse, vel non exstitura. Cum autem cogitatio Dei attributum supponatur, debet et necessario, et immutabilis existere (per Prop. 11 et Coroll. 2 Prop. 20). Quare ultra limites durationis ideæ Dei (supponitur enim aliquando non exstitisse, aut non exstitura) cogitatio sine idea Dei existere debebit. Atqui hoc est contra hypothesin ; supponitur enim, ex data cogitatione necessario sequi ideam Dei. Ergo idea Dei in cogitatione, aut aliquid, quod necessario ex absoluta natura alicujus attributi Dei sequitur, non potest determinatam habere durationem ; sed per idem attributum æternum est.","kind":"passage"},{"id":"RAW_QGOH_PP_2:P0060","text":"Quod erat secundum. Nota, hoc idem esse affirmandum de quacunque re, quæ in aliquo Dei attributo ex Dei absoluta natura necessario sequitur.","kind":"passage"},{"id":"RAW_QGOH_PP_2:P0061","text":"PROPOSITIO XXII","kind":"passage"},{"id":"RAW_QGOH_PP_2:P0062","text":"Quicquid ex aliquo Dei attributo, quatenus modificatum est tali modificatione, quæ et necessario et infinita per idem existit, sequitur, debet quoque et necessario et infinitum existere.","kind":"passage"},{"id":"RAW_QGOH_PP_2:P0063","text":"DEMONSTRATIO","kind":"passage"},{"id":"RAW_QGOH_PP_2:P0064","text":"Hujus Propositionis demonstratio procedit eodem modo ac demonstratio præcedentis.","kind":"passage"},{"id":"RAW_QGOH_PP_2:P0065","text":"PROPOSITIO XXIII","kind":"passage"},{"id":"RAW_QGOH_PP_2:P0066","text":"Omnis modus, qui et necessario et infinitus existit, necessario sequi debuit, vel ex absoluta natura alicujus attributi Dei, vel ex aliquo attributo modificato modificatione, quæ et necessario et infinita existit.","kind":"passage"},{"id":"RAW_QGOH_PP_2:P0067","text":"DEMONSTRATIO","kind":"passage"},{"id":"RAW_QGOH_PP_2:P0068","text":"Modus enim in alio est, per quod concipi debet (per Defin. 5), hoc est (per Prop. 15), in solo Deo est, et per solum Deum concipi potest. Si ergo modus concipitur necessario existere, et infinitus esse, utrumque hoc debet necessario concludi sive percipi per aliquod Dei attributum, quatenus idem concipitur infinitatem et necessitatem existentiæ, sive (quod per Defin. 8 idem est) æternitatem exprimere, hoc est (per Defin. 6 et Prop. 19), quatenus absolute consideratur. Modus ergo, qui et necessario et infinitus existit, ex absoluta natura alicujus Dei attributi sequi debuit ; hocque vel immediate (de quo Prop. 21), vel mediante aliqua modificatione, quæ ex ejus absoluta natura sequitur, hoc est (per Prop. præced.), quæ et necesssario et infinita existit. Q. E. D.","kind":"passage"},{"id":"RAW_QGOH_PP_2:P0069","text":"PROPOSITIO XXIV","kind":"passage"},{"id":"RAW_QGOH_PP_2:P0070","text":"Rerum a Deo productarum essentia non involvit existentiam.","kind":"passage"},{"id":"RAW_QGOH_PP_2:P0071","text":"DEMONSTRATIO","kind":"passage"},{"id":"RAW_QGOH_PP_2:P0072","text":"Patet ex Definitione I. Id enim, cujus natura (in se scilicet considerata) involvit existentiam, causa est sui, et ex sola suæ naturæ necessitate existit.","kind":"passage"},{"id":"RAW_QGOH_PP_2:P0073","text":"COROLLARIUM","kind":"passage"},{"id":"RAW_QGOH_PP_2:P0074","text":"Hinc sequitur, Deum non tantum esse causam, ut res incipiant existere ; sed etiam ut in existendo perseverent, sive (ut termino Scholastico utar) Deum esse causam essendi rerum. Nam, sive res existant sive non existant, quotiescunque ad earum essentiam attendimus, eandem nec existentiam nec durationem involvere comperimus ; adeoque earum essentia neque suas existentiæ neque suæ durationis potest esse causa; sed tantum Deus, ad cujus solam naturam pertinet existere (per Coroll. 1 Prop. 14).","kind":"passage"},{"id":"RAW_QGOH_PP_2:P0075","text":"PROPOSITIO XXV","kind":"passage"},{"id":"RAW_QGOH_PP_2:P0076","text":"Deus non tantum est causa efficiens rerum existentiæ, sed etiam essentiæ.","kind":"passage"},{"id":"RAW_QGOH_PP_2:P0077","text":"DEMONSTRATIO","kind":"passage"},{"id":"RAW_QGOH_PP_2:P0078","text":"Si negas, ergo rerum essentiæ Deus non est causa ; adeoque (per Axioma 4) potest rerum essentia sine Deo concipi : atqui hoc (per Prop. 15) est absurdum. Ergo rerum etiam essentiæ Deus est causa. Q. E. D.","kind":"passage"},{"id":"RAW_QGOH_PP_2:P0079","text":"SCHOLIIUM","kind":"passage"},{"id":"RAW_QGOH_PP_2:P0080","text":"Hæc Propositio clarius sequitur ex Propositione 16. Ex ea enim sequitur, quod ex data natura divina, tam rerum essentia quam existentia debeat necessario concludi ; et, ut verbo dicam, eo sensu, quo Deus dicitur causa sui, etiam omnium rerum causa dicendus est, quod adhuc clarius ex sequenti Corollario constabit.","kind":"passage"},{"id":"RAW_QGOH_PP_2:P0081","text":"COROLLARIUM","kind":"passage"},{"id":"RAW_QGOH_PP_2:P0082","text":"Res particulares nihil sunt, nisi Dei attributorum affectiones, sive modi, quibus Dei attributa certo et determinato modo exprimuntur. Demonstratio patet ex Propositione 15 et Definitione 5.","kind":"passage"},{"id":"RAW_QGOH_PP_2:P0083","text":"PROPOSITIO XXVI","kind":"passage"},{"id":"RAW_QGOH_PP_2:P0084","text":"Res, quæ ad aliquid operandum determinata est, a Deo necessario fuit determinata ; et, quæ a Deo non est determinata, non potest se ipsam ad operandum determinare.","kind":"passage"},{"id":"RAW_QGOH_PP_2:P0085","text":"DEMONSTRATIO","kind":"passage"},{"id":"RAW_QGOH_PP_2:P0086","text":"Id, per quod res determinatæ ad aliquid operandum dicuntur, necessario quid positivum est (ut per se notum) ; adeoque tam ejus essentiæ quam existentiæ Deus ex necessitate suæ naturæ est causa efficiens (per Prop. 25 et 16) ; quod erat primum. Ex quo etiam, quod secundo proponitur, clarissime sequitur. Nam si res, quæ a Deo determinata non est, se ipsam determinare posset, prima pars hujus falsa esset ; quod est absurdum, ut ostendimus.","kind":"passage"},{"id":"RAW_QGOH_PP_2:P0087","text":"PROPOSITIO XXVII","kind":"passage"},{"id":"RAW_QGOH_PP_2:P0088","text":"Res, quæ a Deo ad aliquid operandum determinata est, se ipsam indeterminatam reddere non potest.","kind":"passage"},{"id":"RAW_QGOH_PP_2:P0089","text":"DEMONSTRATIO","kind":"passage"},{"id":"RAW_QGOH_PP_2:P0090","text":"Hæc Propositio patet ex Axiomate tertio.","kind":"passage"},{"id":"RAW_QGOH_PP_2:P0091","text":"PROPOSITIO XXVIII","kind":"passage"},{"id":"RAW_QGOH_PP_2:P0092","text":"Quodcunque singulare, sive quævis res, quæ finita est, et determinatam habet existentiam, non potest existere, nec ad operandum determinari, nisi ad existendum et operandum determinetur ab alia causa, quæ etiam finita est et determinatam habet existentiam : et rursus hæc causa non potest etiam existere, neque ad operandum determinari, nisi ab alia, quæ etiam finita est et determinatam habet existentiam, determinetur ad existendum et operandum, et sic in infinitum.","kind":"passage"},{"id":"RAW_QGOH_PP_2:P0093","text":"DEMONSTRATIO","kind":"passage"}],"sha256":"42ecc86b35ed1e98a1d49da5d9b0b3544744b912f7d35c57a9b01b7b492c3a0e","language_code":"la","language_name":"Latina","translations":[{"id":"SOURCE_TR_20261015T012621532705Z_cf20accc","language_code":"en","language_name":"English","title":"Part One - Concerning God · passage 2","source_sha256":"42ecc86b35ed1e98a1d49da5d9b0b3544744b912f7d35c57a9b01b7b492c3a0e","sha256":"a72bc06b0fce9da298d65993729d2cbea8f44fd7723346b2b7b5d562afa8b7ee","rendition_type":"machine","review_status":"machine","model":"gpt-5.6-terra","created":"2026-10-15T01:26:21.532957+00:00","paragraphs":[{"id":"RAW_QGOH_PP_2:P0001","text":"There are those who feign God to be constituted, after the manner of a human being, of body and mind, and subject to passions; but how far these wander from the true knowledge of God is sufficiently clear from what has already been demonstrated. But I pass these by; for all who have in any manner contemplated the divine nature deny that God is corporeal. They also prove this most excellently from the fact that by body we understand any quantity whatsoever, long, broad, and deep, bounded by some determinate figure—than which nothing more absurd can be said of God, that is, of an absolutely infinite being. Yet meanwhile, by other arguments by which they endeavor to demonstrate this same thing, they clearly show that they entirely remove corporeal or extended substance itself from the divine nature, and maintain that it was created by God. But by what divine power it could have been created, they are altogether ignorant; which clearly shows that they do not themselves understand what they say.","kind":"passage","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_QGOH_PP_2:P0001"]},{"id":"RAW_QGOH_PP_2:P0002","text":"I, at least, have demonstrated sufficiently clearly, in my judgment (see the Corollary to Prop. 6 and Scholium 2 to Prop. 8), that no substance can be produced or created by another. Further, in Prop. 14 we showed that no substance can be given or conceived except God; and from this we concluded that extended substance is one of the infinite attributes of God. But, for a fuller explanation, I shall refute the arguments of the opponents, all of which come down to this. First, that corporeal substance, insofar as it is substance, consists, as they think, of parts; and therefore they deny that it can be infinite, and consequently that it can pertain to God. And they explain this by many examples, one or two of which I shall adduce. If corporeal substance, they say, is infinite, let it be conceived as divided into two parts; each part will be either finite or infinite. If the former, then the infinite is composed of two finite parts, which is absurd.","kind":"passage","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_QGOH_PP_2:P0002"]},{"id":"RAW_QGOH_PP_2:P0003","text":"If the latter, then there is an infinite twice as great as another infinite, which is also absurd. Further, if an infinite quantity is measured by parts equal to feet, it will have to consist of infinitely many such parts; and likewise if it is measured by parts equal to inches; and therefore one infinite number will be twelve times greater than another infinite number. Finally, if from one point of a certain infinite quantity two lines, such as AB and AC, are conceived to be extended to infinity at a certain and determinate initial distance apart, it is certain that the distance between B and C will continually increase, and will at last become indeterminable from being determinate. Since, therefore, these absurdities follow, as they think, from the supposition that quantity is infinite, they conclude from this that corporeal substance must be finite, and consequently does not pertain to the essence of God. The second argument is likewise drawn from God's supreme perfection.","kind":"passage","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_QGOH_PP_2:P0003"]},{"id":"RAW_QGOH_PP_2:P0004","text":"For God, they say, since He is a being supremely perfect, cannot undergo anything; but corporeal substance, since it is divisible, can undergo something; therefore it follows that it does not pertain to the essence of God. These are the arguments that I find in writers by which they endeavor to show that corporeal substance is unworthy of the divine nature and cannot pertain to it. But in truth, if anyone attends rightly, he will find that I have already replied to these; since these arguments are founded solely on the supposition that corporeal substance is composed of parts, which I have already shown to be absurd (Prop. 12, with the Corollary to Prop. 13).","kind":"passage","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_QGOH_PP_2:P0004"]},{"id":"RAW_QGOH_PP_2:P0005","text":"Next, if anyone wishes to consider the matter rightly, he will see that all those absurdities (if indeed they are all absurd, a matter I shall not now dispute), from which they wish to conclude that extended substance is finite, by no means follow from the supposition that quantity is infinite; but rather from their supposing that infinite quantity is measurable and compounded of finite parts. Hence, from the absurdities that follow from this, they can conclude nothing other than that infinite quantity is not measurable and cannot be compounded of finite parts. And this is the very same thing that we have already demonstrated above (Prop. 12, etc.). Therefore, the weapon that they direct against us they in fact hurl against themselves.","kind":"passage","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_QGOH_PP_2:P0005"]},{"id":"RAW_QGOH_PP_2:P0006","text":"If, then, from this absurdity of theirs they nevertheless wish to conclude that extended substance must be finite, they truly do nothing other than someone who, from having feigned that a circle has the properties of a square, concludes that a circle has no center from which all lines drawn to the circumference are equal. For corporeal substance—which can be conceived only as infinite, only as unique, and only as indivisible (see Props. 8, 5, and 12)—in order to conclude that it is finite, compounded of finite parts, multiple, and divisible, they conceive it as such. Thus also others, after feigning that a line is composed of points, know how to discover many arguments by which they show that a line cannot be divided to infinity.","kind":"passage","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_QGOH_PP_2:P0006"]},{"id":"RAW_QGOH_PP_2:P0007","text":"And indeed it is no less absurd to posit that corporeal substance is composed of bodies or parts than that a body is composed of surfaces, surfaces of lines, and finally lines of points. And all who know that clear reason is infallible must admit this, and especially those who deny that a vacuum is given. For if corporeal substance could be divided in such a way that its parts were really distinct, why then could not one part be annihilated, while the remaining parts remained connected with one another as before? And why must all be so fitted together that there is no vacuum? Certainly, of things that are really distinct from one another, one can be without the other and remain in its own state. Since, therefore, a vacuum is not given in Nature (of which elsewhere), but all parts must so concur that there is no vacuum, it follows also from this that they cannot be really distinguished; that is, corporeal substance, insofar as it is substance, cannot be divided.","kind":"passage","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_QGOH_PP_2:P0007"]},{"id":"RAW_QGOH_PP_2:P0008","text":"But if anyone now asks why we are by nature so inclined to divide quantity, I answer him that quantity is conceived by us in two ways: abstractly, that is, superficially, insofar as we imagine it; or as substance, which is done by the intellect alone. If, therefore, we attend to quantity insofar as it is in the imagination—which we often do and do more easily—it will be found finite, divisible, and compounded of parts; but if we attend to it insofar as it is in the intellect, and conceive it insofar as it is substance—which is done with the greatest difficulty—then, as we have already sufficiently demonstrated, it will be found infinite, unique, and indivisible.","kind":"passage","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_QGOH_PP_2:P0008"]},{"id":"RAW_QGOH_PP_2:P0009","text":"This will be sufficiently manifest to all who know how to distinguish between imagination and intellect; especially if attention is also given to this, that matter is everywhere the same, and its parts are not distinguished within it except insofar as we conceive matter to be affected in different ways; whence its parts are distinguished only modally, not really. For example, we conceive water, insofar as it is water, to be divided, and its parts to be separated from one another; but not insofar as it is corporeal substance; for in that respect it is neither separated nor divided. Further, water, insofar as it is water, is generated and corrupted; but insofar as it is substance, it is neither generated nor corrupted. And by these things I think that I have also replied to the second argument; since it too is founded on the supposition that matter, insofar as it is substance, is divisible and compounded of parts. And even if this were not so, I do not know why it would be unworthy of the divine nature; since (by Prop.","kind":"passage","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_QGOH_PP_2:P0009"]},{"id":"RAW_QGOH_PP_2:P0010","text":"14) outside God no substance can be given from which He might undergo something. All things, I say, are in God, and all things that come to pass come to pass through the laws alone of God's infinite nature, and follow from the necessity of His essence (as I shall presently show); and therefore it can in no way be said that God undergoes something from another, or that extended substance is unworthy of the divine nature, even if it is supposed divisible, provided it is granted to be eternal and infinite. But enough of these matters for the present.","kind":"passage","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_QGOH_PP_2:P0010"]},{"id":"RAW_QGOH_PP_2:P0011","text":"PROPOSITION XVI","kind":"passage","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_QGOH_PP_2:P0011"]},{"id":"RAW_QGOH_PP_2:P0012","text":"From the necessity of the divine nature there must follow infinitely many things in infinitely many modes (that is, all things that can fall under an infinite intellect).","kind":"passage","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_QGOH_PP_2:P0012"]},{"id":"RAW_QGOH_PP_2:P0013","text":"DEMONSTRATION","kind":"passage","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_QGOH_PP_2:P0013"]},{"id":"RAW_QGOH_PP_2:P0014","text":"This Proposition ought to be manifest to everyone, if only one attends to this: that from the given definition of any thing whatever the intellect infers several properties which in fact necessarily follow from it (that is, from the very essence of the thing), and all the more properties as the definition of the thing expresses more reality, that is, as the essence of the defined thing involves more reality. But since the divine nature has absolutely infinite attributes (by Def. 6), each one of which also expresses an infinite essence in its kind, therefore from the necessity of that nature there must necessarily follow infinitely many things in infinitely many modes (that is, all things that can fall under an infinite intellect). Q. E. D.","kind":"passage","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_QGOH_PP_2:P0014"]},{"id":"RAW_QGOH_PP_2:P0015","text":"COROLLARY I","kind":"passage","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_QGOH_PP_2:P0015"]},{"id":"RAW_QGOH_PP_2:P0016","text":"From this it follows, first, that God is the efficient cause of all things that can fall under an infinite intellect.","kind":"passage","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_QGOH_PP_2:P0016"]},{"id":"RAW_QGOH_PP_2:P0017","text":"COROLLARY II","kind":"passage","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_QGOH_PP_2:P0017"]},{"id":"RAW_QGOH_PP_2:P0018","text":"It follows, secondly, that God is a cause through Himself, but not accidentally.","kind":"passage","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_QGOH_PP_2:P0018"]},{"id":"RAW_QGOH_PP_2:P0019","text":"COROLLARY III","kind":"passage","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_QGOH_PP_2:P0019"]},{"id":"RAW_QGOH_PP_2:P0020","text":"It follows, thirdly, that God is absolutely the first cause.","kind":"passage","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_QGOH_PP_2:P0020"]},{"id":"RAW_QGOH_PP_2:P0021","text":"PROPOSITION XVII","kind":"passage","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_QGOH_PP_2:P0021"]},{"id":"RAW_QGOH_PP_2:P0022","text":"God acts solely from the laws of His own nature, and is compelled by no one.","kind":"passage","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_QGOH_PP_2:P0022"]},{"id":"RAW_QGOH_PP_2:P0023","text":"DEMONSTRATION","kind":"passage","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_QGOH_PP_2:P0023"]},{"id":"RAW_QGOH_PP_2:P0024","text":"From the necessity of the divine nature alone, or (which is the same) from the laws of that nature alone, infinitely many things absolutely follow, as we have just shown in Prop. 16; and in Prop. 15 we demonstrated that nothing can be or be conceived without God, but that all things are in God. Therefore nothing can be outside Him by which He may be determined or compelled to act; and thus God acts solely from the laws of His own nature and is compelled by no one. Q. E. D.","kind":"passage","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_QGOH_PP_2:P0024"]},{"id":"RAW_QGOH_PP_2:P0025","text":"COROLLARY I","kind":"passage","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_QGOH_PP_2:P0025"]},{"id":"RAW_QGOH_PP_2:P0026","text":"From this it follows: 1° that no cause is given which, extrinsically or intrinsically, apart from the perfection of his own nature, incites God to act.","kind":"passage","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_QGOH_PP_2:P0026"]},{"id":"RAW_QGOH_PP_2:P0027","text":"COROLLARY II","kind":"passage","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_QGOH_PP_2:P0027"]},{"id":"RAW_QGOH_PP_2:P0028","text":"It follows: 2° that God alone is a free cause. For God alone exists from the necessity of his nature alone (by Prop. 11 and Coroll. 1 of Prop. 14), and acts from the necessity of his nature alone (by the preceding Prop.). And therefore (by Def. 7) he alone is a free cause. Q. E. D.","kind":"passage","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_QGOH_PP_2:P0028"]},{"id":"RAW_QGOH_PP_2:P0029","text":"SCHOLIUM","kind":"passage","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_QGOH_PP_2:P0029"]},{"id":"RAW_QGOH_PP_2:P0030","text":"Others think that God is a free cause because, as they think, he can bring it about that those things which we have said follow from his nature—that is, which are in his power—do not come to pass, or are not produced by him. But this is the same as if they were to say that God can bring it about that it does not follow from the nature of a triangle that its three angles are equal to two right angles; or that an effect does not follow from a given cause, which is absurd. Moreover, below, without the aid of this Proposition, I shall show that neither intellect nor will pertains to God's nature. I know indeed that there are many who think that they can demonstrate that supreme intellect and free will pertain to God's nature; for they say that they know of nothing more perfect which they can attribute to God than that which is the highest perfection in us.","kind":"passage","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_QGOH_PP_2:P0030"]},{"id":"RAW_QGOH_PP_2:P0031","text":"Moreover, although they conceive God as actually supremely intelligent, they nevertheless do not believe that he can bring it about that all the things which he actually understands exist, for they think that in this way they would destroy God's power. If, they say, he had created all the things that are in his intellect, he could then create nothing further, which they believe to be repugnant to God's omnipotence; and therefore they preferred to posit God as indifferent toward all things, creating nothing other than that which he decreed to create by some absolute will. But I think that I have shown sufficiently clearly (see Prop. 16) that from God's supreme power, or infinite nature, infinitely many things in infinitely many modes—that is, all things—have necessarily flowed, or always follow with the same necessity: in the same way as it follows from the nature of a triangle, from eternity and unto eternity, that its three angles are equal to two right angles. Wherefore God's omnipotence has actually been from eternity, and will remain unto eternity in the same actuality.","kind":"passage","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_QGOH_PP_2:P0031"]},{"id":"RAW_QGOH_PP_2:P0032","text":"And in this way God's omnipotence is established as far more perfect, in my judgment indeed. Nay, the opponents seem (if it is permitted to speak openly) to deny God's omnipotence. For they are compelled to admit that God understands infinitely many creatable things which nevertheless he will never be able to create. Otherwise, namely if he created all the things that he understands, he would, according to them, exhaust his omnipotence and render himself imperfect. Therefore, in order to establish God as perfect, they are reduced to maintaining at the same time that he cannot bring about all things to which his power extends; than which I do not see what more absurd, or more repugnant to God's omnipotence, could be imagined. Moreover, in order that I may say here also something concerning the intellect and will which we commonly attribute to God: if intellect and will, that is, pertain to God's eternal essence, surely something other must be understood by each of these attributes than men are commonly wont to understand.","kind":"passage","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_QGOH_PP_2:P0032"]},{"id":"RAW_QGOH_PP_2:P0033","text":"For the intellect and will which would constitute God's essence would have to differ from our intellect and will by the whole heaven, and could agree in no respect save in name; just as, namely, a dog, the celestial sign, and a dog, a barking animal, agree with one another. I shall demonstrate this as follows. If intellect pertains to the divine nature, it will not be able, as our intellect is, to be posterior to, or simultaneous in nature with, the things understood (as most please), since God is prior to all things in causality (by Coroll. 1 of Prop. 16); but on the contrary, the truth and formal essence of things is such only because it exists objectively as such in God's intellect.","kind":"passage","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_QGOH_PP_2:P0033"]},{"id":"RAW_QGOH_PP_2:P0034","text":"Wherefore God's intellect, insofar as it is conceived to constitute God's essence, is in truth the cause of things, both of their essence and of their existence; which seems also to have been observed by those who asserted that God's intellect, will, and power are one and the same. Since, therefore, God's intellect is the sole cause of things, namely (as we have shown), both of their essence and of their existence, it must necessarily differ from them both in respect of essence and in respect of existence. For the effect differs from its cause precisely in that which it has from the cause. For example, a man is the cause of the existence, but not of the essence, of another man; for this is an eternal truth, and therefore they can agree entirely in respect of essence; but in existing they must differ; and therefore, if the existence of one perishes, the other's will not thereby perish; but if the essence of one could be destroyed and made false, the essence of the other also would be destroyed.","kind":"passage","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_QGOH_PP_2:P0034"]},{"id":"RAW_QGOH_PP_2:P0035","text":"For this reason, a thing which is the cause of both the essence and the existence of some effect must differ from such an effect both in respect of essence and in respect of existence. But God's intellect is the cause of both the essence and existence of our intellect; therefore God's intellect, insofar as it is conceived to constitute the divine essence, differs from our intellect both in respect of essence and in respect of existence, and can agree with it in no respect save in name, as we wished. The same procedure applies concerning the will, as anyone can easily see.","kind":"passage","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_QGOH_PP_2:P0035"]},{"id":"RAW_QGOH_PP_2:P0036","text":"PROPOSITION XVIII","kind":"passage","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_QGOH_PP_2:P0036"]},{"id":"RAW_QGOH_PP_2:P0037","text":"God is the immanent, but not the transitive, cause of all things.","kind":"passage","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_QGOH_PP_2:P0037"]},{"id":"RAW_QGOH_PP_2:P0038","text":"DEMONSTRATION","kind":"passage","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_QGOH_PP_2:P0038"]},{"id":"RAW_QGOH_PP_2:P0039","text":"All things that are, are in God and must be conceived through God (by Prop. 15); and therefore (by Coroll. 1 of Prop. 16 of this Part) God is the cause of the things that are in him; which was the first point. Next, no substance can be given outside God (by Prop. 14), that is (by Def. 3), no thing which is in itself outside God; which was the second point. Therefore God is the immanent, but not the transitive, cause of all things. Q. E. D.","kind":"passage","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_QGOH_PP_2:P0039"]},{"id":"RAW_QGOH_PP_2:P0040","text":"PROPOSITION XIX","kind":"passage","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_QGOH_PP_2:P0040"]},{"id":"RAW_QGOH_PP_2:P0041","text":"God, or all God's attributes, are eternal.","kind":"passage","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_QGOH_PP_2:P0041"]},{"id":"RAW_QGOH_PP_2:P0042","text":"DEMONSTRATION","kind":"passage","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_QGOH_PP_2:P0042"]},{"id":"RAW_QGOH_PP_2:P0043","text":"For God (by Def. 6) is a substance which (by Prop. 11) necessarily exists, that is (by Prop. 7), to whose nature it pertains to exist, or (which is the same) from whose definition it follows that he himself exists, and therefore (by Def. 8) is eternal. Further, by God's attributes must be understood that which (by Def. 4) expresses the essence of the divine substance, that is, that which pertains to substance: the attributes themselves, I say, must involve this very thing. But eternity pertains to the nature of substance (as I have just demonstrated from Prop. 7); therefore each one of the attributes must involve eternity, and consequently all are eternal. Q. E. D.","kind":"passage","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_QGOH_PP_2:P0043"]},{"id":"RAW_QGOH_PP_2:P0044","text":"SCHOLIUM","kind":"passage","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_QGOH_PP_2:P0044"]},{"id":"RAW_QGOH_PP_2:P0045","text":"This Proposition is also most clearly evident from the manner in which (Prop. 11) I demonstrated God's existence; from that demonstration, I say, it is clear that God's existence, like his essence, is an eternal truth. Further, I have also demonstrated God's eternity in another manner (Prop. 19 of Descartes' Principles), and there is no need to repeat it here.","kind":"passage","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_QGOH_PP_2:P0045"]},{"id":"RAW_QGOH_PP_2:P0046","text":"PROPOSITION XX","kind":"passage","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_QGOH_PP_2:P0046"]},{"id":"RAW_QGOH_PP_2:P0047","text":"God's existence and his essence are one and the same.","kind":"passage","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_QGOH_PP_2:P0047"]},{"id":"RAW_QGOH_PP_2:P0048","text":"DEMONSTRATION","kind":"passage","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_QGOH_PP_2:P0048"]},{"id":"RAW_QGOH_PP_2:P0049","text":"God (by the preceding Prop.) and all His attributes are eternal, that is (by Def. 8), each one of His attributes expresses existence. Therefore the same attributes of God, which (by Def. 4) explain God's eternal essence, at the same time explain His eternal existence; that is, that very thing which constitutes the essence of God at the same time constitutes His existence, and consequently this and His essence are one and the same. Q. E. D.","kind":"passage","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_QGOH_PP_2:P0049"]},{"id":"RAW_QGOH_PP_2:P0050","text":"COROLLARY I","kind":"passage","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_QGOH_PP_2:P0050"]},{"id":"RAW_QGOH_PP_2:P0051","text":"Hence it follows: I°. that God's existence, like His essence, is an eternal truth.","kind":"passage","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_QGOH_PP_2:P0051"]},{"id":"RAW_QGOH_PP_2:P0052","text":"COROLLARY II","kind":"passage","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_QGOH_PP_2:P0052"]},{"id":"RAW_QGOH_PP_2:P0053","text":"It follows: II°. that God, or all God's attributes, are immutable. For if they were changed in respect of existence, they would also have to be changed (by the preceding Prop.) in respect of essence, that is (as is self-evident), to become false from true, which is absurd.","kind":"passage","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_QGOH_PP_2:P0053"]},{"id":"RAW_QGOH_PP_2:P0054","text":"PROPOSITION XXI","kind":"passage","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_QGOH_PP_2:P0054"]},{"id":"RAW_QGOH_PP_2:P0055","text":"All things which follow from the absolute nature of any attribute of God must have always existed, and be infinite; or, through the same attribute, they are eternal and infinite.","kind":"passage","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_QGOH_PP_2:P0055"]},{"id":"RAW_QGOH_PP_2:P0056","text":"DEMONSTRATION","kind":"passage","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_QGOH_PP_2:P0056"]},{"id":"RAW_QGOH_PP_2:P0057","text":"Conceive, if possible (if indeed you deny it), that something follows from the absolute nature of God Himself in some attribute of God which is finite, and has a determinate existence or duration—for example, an idea of God in thought. But thought, since it is supposed to be an attribute of God, is necessarily (by Prop. 11) infinite by its nature. Yet, insofar as it has the idea of God, it is supposed finite. But (by Def. 2) it cannot be conceived as finite unless it is determined through thought itself. But not through thought itself insofar as it constitutes the idea of God; for in that respect it is supposed to be finite. Therefore, through thought insofar as it does not constitute the idea of God; which nevertheless (by Prop. 11) must necessarily exist. Therefore there is thought not constituting the idea of God, and consequently from its nature, insofar as it is absolute thought, the idea of God does not necessarily follow. (For thought is conceived as constituting, and as not constituting, the idea of God.)","kind":"passage","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_QGOH_PP_2:P0057"]},{"id":"RAW_QGOH_PP_2:P0058","text":"This is contrary to the hypothesis. Therefore, if the idea of God in thought, or anything (it is the same, whatever may be taken, since the demonstration is universal) in any attribute of God follows from the necessity of the absolute nature of that attribute itself, it must necessarily be infinite. This was the first point.","kind":"passage","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_QGOH_PP_2:P0058"]},{"id":"RAW_QGOH_PP_2:P0059","text":"Next, that which thus follows from the necessity of the nature of any attribute cannot have a determinate duration. For, if you deny it, let a thing which follows from the necessity of the nature of some attribute be supposed to be given in some attribute of God—for example, the idea of God in thought—and let it be supposed at some time not to have existed, or not to be going to exist. But since thought is supposed to be an attribute of God, it must exist both necessarily and immutable (by Prop. 11 and Coroll. 2 of Prop. 20). Therefore, beyond the limits of the duration of the idea of God (for it is supposed at some time not to have existed, or not to be going to exist), thought will have to exist without the idea of God. But this is contrary to the hypothesis; for it is supposed that from given thought the idea of God necessarily follows. Therefore the idea of God in thought, or anything which necessarily follows from the absolute nature of any attribute of God, cannot have a determinate duration; but through the same attribute it is eternal.","kind":"passage","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_QGOH_PP_2:P0059"]},{"id":"RAW_QGOH_PP_2:P0060","text":"This was the second point. Note that the same must be affirmed of every thing which necessarily follows from the absolute nature of God in some attribute of God.","kind":"passage","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_QGOH_PP_2:P0060"]},{"id":"RAW_QGOH_PP_2:P0061","text":"PROPOSITION XXII","kind":"passage","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_QGOH_PP_2:P0061"]},{"id":"RAW_QGOH_PP_2:P0062","text":"Whatever follows from any attribute of God insofar as it is modified by such a modification as exists both necessarily and infinitely through that same attribute, must likewise exist both necessarily and infinitely.","kind":"passage","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_QGOH_PP_2:P0062"]},{"id":"RAW_QGOH_PP_2:P0063","text":"DEMONSTRATION","kind":"passage","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_QGOH_PP_2:P0063"]},{"id":"RAW_QGOH_PP_2:P0064","text":"The demonstration of this Proposition proceeds in the same manner as the demonstration of the preceding one.","kind":"passage","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_QGOH_PP_2:P0064"]},{"id":"RAW_QGOH_PP_2:P0065","text":"PROPOSITION XXIII","kind":"passage","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_QGOH_PP_2:P0065"]},{"id":"RAW_QGOH_PP_2:P0066","text":"Every mode which exists both necessarily and infinitely must necessarily have followed either from the absolute nature of some attribute of God, or from some attribute modified by a modification which exists both necessarily and infinitely.","kind":"passage","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_QGOH_PP_2:P0066"]},{"id":"RAW_QGOH_PP_2:P0067","text":"DEMONSTRATION","kind":"passage","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_QGOH_PP_2:P0067"]},{"id":"RAW_QGOH_PP_2:P0068","text":"For a mode is in another, through which it must be conceived (by Def. 5), that is (by Prop. 15), it is in God alone, and can be conceived through God alone. Therefore, if a mode is conceived to exist necessarily and to be infinite, both of these must necessarily be inferred or perceived through some attribute of God, insofar as the same is conceived to express infinity and necessity of existence, or (which by Def. 8 is the same) eternity, that is (by Def. 6 and Prop. 19), insofar as it is considered absolutely. Therefore a mode which exists both necessarily and infinitely must have followed from the absolute nature of some attribute of God; and this either immediately (concerning which, Prop. 21), or through some modification which follows from its absolute nature, that is (by the preceding Prop.), which exists both necessarily and infinitely. Q. E. D.","kind":"passage","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_QGOH_PP_2:P0068"]},{"id":"RAW_QGOH_PP_2:P0069","text":"PROPOSITION XXIV","kind":"passage","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_QGOH_PP_2:P0069"]},{"id":"RAW_QGOH_PP_2:P0070","text":"The essence of things produced by God does not involve existence.","kind":"passage","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_QGOH_PP_2:P0070"]},{"id":"RAW_QGOH_PP_2:P0071","text":"DEMONSTRATION","kind":"passage","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_QGOH_PP_2:P0071"]},{"id":"RAW_QGOH_PP_2:P0072","text":"This is clear from Definition I. For that whose nature (considered, namely, in itself) involves existence is the cause of itself, and exists from the necessity of its nature alone.","kind":"passage","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_QGOH_PP_2:P0072"]},{"id":"RAW_QGOH_PP_2:P0073","text":"COROLLARY","kind":"passage","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_QGOH_PP_2:P0073"]},{"id":"RAW_QGOH_PP_2:P0074","text":"From this it follows that God is not only the cause of things beginning to exist, but also of their persevering in existence; or, to use the Scholastic term, that God is the cause of the being of things. For, whether things exist or do not exist, whenever we attend to their essence, we find that it involves neither existence nor duration; and therefore their essence can be the cause neither of their existence nor of their duration, but only God, to whose nature alone it pertains to exist (by Corollary 1 of Proposition 14).","kind":"passage","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_QGOH_PP_2:P0074"]},{"id":"RAW_QGOH_PP_2:P0075","text":"PROPOSITION XXV","kind":"passage","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_QGOH_PP_2:P0075"]},{"id":"RAW_QGOH_PP_2:P0076","text":"God is not only the efficient cause of the existence of things, but also of their essence.","kind":"passage","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_QGOH_PP_2:P0076"]},{"id":"RAW_QGOH_PP_2:P0077","text":"DEMONSTRATION","kind":"passage","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_QGOH_PP_2:P0077"]},{"id":"RAW_QGOH_PP_2:P0078","text":"If you deny this, then God is not the cause of the essences of things; and therefore (by Axiom 4) the essence of things can be conceived without God. But this (by Proposition 15) is absurd. Therefore God is also the cause of the essences of things. Q. E. D.","kind":"passage","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_QGOH_PP_2:P0078"]},{"id":"RAW_QGOH_PP_2:P0079","text":"SCHOLIUM","kind":"passage","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_QGOH_PP_2:P0079"]},{"id":"RAW_QGOH_PP_2:P0080","text":"This Proposition follows more clearly from Proposition 16. For from it follows that, from the given divine nature, both the essence and the existence of things must necessarily be concluded; and, to put it in a word, in the sense in which God is said to be the cause of himself, he must also be said to be the cause of all things, as will be still more clearly apparent from the following Corollary.","kind":"passage","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_QGOH_PP_2:P0080"]},{"id":"RAW_QGOH_PP_2:P0081","text":"COROLLARY","kind":"passage","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_QGOH_PP_2:P0081"]},{"id":"RAW_QGOH_PP_2:P0082","text":"Particular things are nothing but affections of God's attributes, or modes by which God's attributes are expressed in a certain and determinate manner. The demonstration is evident from Proposition 15 and Definition 5.","kind":"passage","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_QGOH_PP_2:P0082"]},{"id":"RAW_QGOH_PP_2:P0083","text":"PROPOSITION XXVI","kind":"passage","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_QGOH_PP_2:P0083"]},{"id":"RAW_QGOH_PP_2:P0084","text":"A thing which has been determined to act in some way has necessarily been determined by God; and one which has not been determined by God cannot determine itself to act.","kind":"passage","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_QGOH_PP_2:P0084"]},{"id":"RAW_QGOH_PP_2:P0085","text":"DEMONSTRATION","kind":"passage","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_QGOH_PP_2:P0085"]},{"id":"RAW_QGOH_PP_2:P0086","text":"That by which things are said to be determined to act in some way is necessarily something positive (as is known through itself); and therefore God, from the necessity of his nature, is the efficient cause both of its essence and of its existence (by Propositions 25 and 16); which was the first point. From this also follows most clearly what is proposed secondly. For if a thing which has not been determined by God could determine itself, the first part of this would be false; which, as we have shown, is absurd.","kind":"passage","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_QGOH_PP_2:P0086"]},{"id":"RAW_QGOH_PP_2:P0087","text":"PROPOSITION XXVII","kind":"passage","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_QGOH_PP_2:P0087"]},{"id":"RAW_QGOH_PP_2:P0088","text":"A thing which has been determined by God to act in some way cannot render itself indeterminate.","kind":"passage","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_QGOH_PP_2:P0088"]},{"id":"RAW_QGOH_PP_2:P0089","text":"DEMONSTRATION","kind":"passage","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_QGOH_PP_2:P0089"]},{"id":"RAW_QGOH_PP_2:P0090","text":"This Proposition is evident from the third Axiom.","kind":"passage","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_QGOH_PP_2:P0090"]},{"id":"RAW_QGOH_PP_2:P0091","text":"PROPOSITION XXVIII","kind":"passage","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_QGOH_PP_2:P0091"]},{"id":"RAW_QGOH_PP_2:P0092","text":"Whatever is singular, or any thing which is finite and has a determinate existence, cannot exist or be determined to act unless it is determined to exist and act by another cause, which is also finite and has a determinate existence; and again, this cause also cannot exist or be determined to act unless it is determined to exist and act by another cause which is also finite and has a determinate existence; and so on to infinity.","kind":"passage","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_QGOH_PP_2:P0092"]},{"id":"RAW_QGOH_PP_2:P0093","text":"DEMONSTRATION","kind":"passage","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_QGOH_PP_2:P0093"]}],"source_digest":"2edeb06f9b1affa2ad6a812a081caf7dae2f6245064f44b2ec2354c93a12c03e","source_language_code":"la","original_title":"Pars prima - De Deo · passage 2"}],"passage_basis":"frozen-grounding","original_title":"Pars prima - De Deo · passage 2","title_language_code":"en"},{"id":"RAW_QGOH_PP_3","title":"Part One - Concerning God · passage 3","order":3,"chapter_id":"CH_QGOH_PP_3","text":"Quicquid determinatum est ad existendum et operandum, a Deo sic determinatum est (per Prop. 26 et Coroll. Prop. 24). At id, quod finitum est et determinatam habet existentiam, ab absoluta natura alicujus Dei attributi produci non potuit : quicquid enim ex absoluta natura alicujus Dei attributi sequitur, id infinitum et æternum est (per Prop. 21). Debuit ergo ex Deo, vel aliquo ejus attributo sequi, quatenus aliquo modo affectum consideratur ; præter enim substantiam et modos nil datur (per Axiom. 1 et Defin. 3 et 5) ; et modi (per Coroll. Prop. 25) nihil sunt nisi Dei attributorum affectiones. At ex Deo, vel aliquo ejus attributo, quatenus affectum est modificatione, quæ æterna et infinita est, sequi etiam non potuit (per Prop. 22). Debuit ergo sequi, vel ad existendum et operandum determînari, a Deo vel aliquo ejus attributo, quatenus modificatum est modificatione quæ finita est et determinatam habet existentiam. Quod erat primum. Deinde hæc rursus causa, sive hic modus (per eandem rationem qua primam partem hujus jamjam demonstravimus) debuit etiam determinari ab alia, quæ etiam finita est et determinatam habet existentiam, et rursus haec ultima (per eandem rationem) ab alia, et sic semper (per eandem rationem) in infinitum. Q. E. D.\n\nSCHOLIUM\n\nCum quædam a Deo immediate produci debuerunt, videlicet ea, quæ ex absoluta ejus natura necessario sequuntur, mediantibus his primis quæ tamen sine Deo nec esse nec concipi possunt ; hinc sequitur : 1° quod Deus sit rerum immediate ab ipso productarum causa absolute proxima ; non vero in suo genere, ut ajunt. Nam Dei effectus sine sua causa nec esse nec concipi possunt (per Prop. 15 et Coroll. Prop. 24). Sequitur : 2° quod Deus non potest proprie dici causa esse remota rerum singularium, nisi forte ea de causa, ut scilicet has ab iis, quas immediate produxit, vel potius quæ ex absoluta ejus natura sequuntur, distinguamus, Nam per causam remotam talem intelligimus, quæ cum effectu nullo modo conjuncta est. At omnia, quae sunt, in Deo sunt, et a Deo ita dependent, ut sine ipso nec esse nec concipi possint.\n\nPROPOSITIO XXIX\n\nIn rerum natura nullum datur contingens ; sed omnia ex necessitate divinæ naturæ determinata sunt ad certo modo existendum et operandum.\n\nDEMONSTRATIO\n\nQuicquid est, in Deo est (per Prop. 15) : Deus autem non potest dici res contingens : Nam (per Prop. 11) necessario, non vero contingenter existit. Modi deinde divinæ naturæ ex eadem etiam necessario, non vero contingenter secuti sunt (per Prop. 16), idque vel quatenus divina natura absolute (per Prop. 21), vel quatenus certo modo ad agendum determinata consideratur (per Prop. 27). Porro horum modorum Deus non tantum est causa, quatenus simpliciter existunt (per Coroll. Prop. 24), sed etiam (per Prop. 26) quatenus ad aliquid operandum determinati considerantur. Quod si a Deo (per eand. Prop.) determinati non sint, impossibile, non vero contingens est, ut se ipsos determinent ; et contra (per Prop. 27) si a Deo determinati sint, impossibile, non vero contingens est, ut se ipsos indeterminatos reddant. Quare omnia ex necessitate divinæ naturæ determinata sunt, non tantum ad existendum, sed etiam ad certo modo existendum et operandum, nullumque datur contingens. Q. E. D.\n\nSCHOLIUM\n\nAntequam ulterius pergam, hic, quid nobis per Naturam Naturantem, et quid per Naturam Naturatam intelligendum sit, explicare volo, vel potius monere. Nam ex antecedentibus jam constare existimo, nempe, quod per Naturam Naturantem nobis intelligendum est id, quod in se est et per se concipitur, sive talia substantiae attributa, quæ æternam et infinitam essentiam exprimunt, hoc est (per Coroll. 1 Prop. 14 et Coroll. 2 Prop. 17), Deus quatenus ut causa libera consideratur. Per Naturam Naturatam autem intelligo id omne, quod ex necessitate Dei naturæ, sive uniuscu jusque Dei attributorum sequitur, hoc est omnes Dei attributorum modos, quatenus considerantur ut res, quæ in Deo sunt, et quæ sine Deo nec esse nec concipi possunt.\n\nPROPOSITIO XXX\n\nIntellectus, actu finitus aut actu infinitus, Dei attributa Deique affectiones comprehendere debet, et nihil aliud.\n\nDEMONSTRATIO\n\nIdea vera debet convenire cum suo ideato (per Axiom. 6), hoc est (ut per se notum) id, quod in intellectu objective continetur, debet necessario in Natura dari : atqui in Natura (per Coroll. 1 Prop. 14) non nisi una substantia datur, nempe Deus ; nec ullæ aliæ affectiones (per Prop. 15), quam quæ in Deo sunt, et quæ (per eandem Prop.) sine Deo nec esse nec concipi possunt ; ergo intellectus, actu finitus aut actu infinitus, Dei attributa Deique affectiones comprehendere debet, et nihil aliud. Q. E. D.\n\nPROPOSITIO XXXI\n\nIntellectus actu, sive is finitus sit sive infinitus, ut et voluntas, cupiditas, amor, etc., ad Naturam Naturatam, non vero ad Naturantem referri debent.\n\nDEMONSTRATIO\n\nPer intellectum enim (ut per se notum) non intelligimus absolutam cogitationem, sed certum tantum modum cogitandi, qui modus ab aliis, scilicet cupiditate, amore, etc., differt, adeoque (per Defin. 5) per absolutam cogitationem concipi debet ; nempe (per. Prop. 15 et Defin. 6) per aliquod Dei attributum, quod æternam et infinitam cogitationis essentiam exprimit, ita concipi debet, ut sine ipso nec esse nec concipi possit ; ac propterea (per Schol. Prop. 29) ad Naturam Naturatam, non vero Naturantem referri debet, ut etiam reliqui modi cogitandi. Q. E. D.\n\nSCHOLIUM\n\nRatio, cur hic loquar de intellectu actu, non est, quia concedo, ullum dari intellectum potentia ; sed, quia omnem confusionem vitare cupio, nolui loqui, nisi de re nobis quam clarissime percepta, de ipsa scilicet intellectione, qua nihil nobis clarius percipitur. Nihil enim intelligere possumus, quod ad perfectiorem intellectionis cognitionem non conducat.\n\nPROPOSITIO XXXII\n\nVoluntas non potest vocari causa libera, sed tantum necessaria.\n\nDEMONSTRATIO\n\nVoluntas certus tantum cogitandi modus est, sicuti intellectus ; adeoque (per Prop. 28) unaquæque volitio non potest existere, neque ad operandum determinari, nisi ab alia causa determinetur, et hæc rursus ab alia, et sic porro in infinitum. Quod si voluntas infinita supponatur, debet etiam ad existendum et operandum determinari a Deo, non quatenus substantia absolute infinita est, sed quatenus attributum habet, quod infinitam et æternam cogitationis essentiam exprimit (per Prop. 23). Quocumque igitur modo, sive finita sive infinita, concipiatur, causam requirit, a qua ad existendum et operandum determinetur ; adeoque (per Defin. 7) non potest dici causa libera, sed tantum necessaria vel coacta. Q. E. D.\n\nCOROLLARIUM I\n\nHinc sequitur : I° Deum non operari ex libertate voluntatis.\n\nCOROLLARIUM II\n\nSequitur : II° voluntatem et intellectum ad Dei naturam ita sese habere, ut motus et quies, et absolute ut omnia naturalia, quæ (per Prop. 29) a Deo ad existendum et operandum certo modo determinari debent. Nam voluntas, ut reliqua omnia, causa indiget, a qua ad existendum et operandum certo modo determinetur. Et, quamvis ex data voluntate sive intellectu infinita sequantur, non tamen propterea Deus magis dici potest ex libertate voluntatis agere, quam propter ea, quæ ex motu et quiete sequuntur (infinita enim ex his etiam sequuntur), dici potest ex libertate motus et quietis agere. Quare voluntas ad Dei naturam non magis pertinet, quam reliqua naturalia, sed ad ipsam eodem modo sese habet, ut motus et quies, et omnia reliqua, quæ ostendimus ex necessitate divinæ naturæ sequi, et ab eadem ad existendum et operandum certo modo determinari.\n\nPROPOSITIO XXXIII\n\nRes nullo alio modo, neque alio ordine a Deo produci potuerunt, quam productæ sunt.\n\nDEMONSTRATIO\n\nRes enim omnes data Dei natura necessario secutæ sunt (per Prop. 16), et ex necessitate naturæ Dei determinatæ sunt ad certo modo existendum et operandum (per Prop. 29). Si itaque res alterius naturæ potuissent esse, vel alio modo ad operandum determinari, ut naturæ ordo alius esset ; ergo Dei etiam natura alia posset esse, quam jam est ; ac proinde (per Prop. 11) illa etiam deberet existere, et consequenter duo vel plures possent dari Dii, quod (per Coroll. 1 Prop. 14) est absurdum. Quapropte res nullo alio modo, neque alio ordine, etc. Q. E. D.\n\nSCHOLIUM I\n\nQuoniam his luce meridiana clarius ostendi, nihil absolute in rebus dari, propter quod contingentes dicantur, explicare jam paucis volo, quid nobis per Contingens erit intelligendum ; sed prius, quid per Necessarium, et Impossibile. Res aliqua necessaria dicitur, vel ratione suæ essentiæ, vel ratione causæ. Rei enim alicujus existentia vel ex ipsius essentia et definitione, vel ex data causa efficiente necessario sequitur. Deinde his etiam de causis res aliqua impossibilis dicitur ; nimirum quia vel ipsius essentia seu definitio contradictionem involvit vel quia nulla causa externa datur, ad talem rem producendam determinata. At res aliqua nulla alia de causa contingens dicitur, nisi respectu defectus nostræ cognitionis. Res enim, cujus essentiam contradictionem involvere ignoramus, vel de qua probe scimus, eandem nullam contradictionem involvere et tamen de ipsius existentia nihil certo affirmare possumus, propterea quod ordo causarum nos latet ; ea nunquam, nec ut necessaria, nec ut impossibilis videri nobis potest, ideoque eandem vel contingentem, vel possibilem vocamus.\n\nSCHOLIUM II\n\nEx præcedentibus clare sequitur, res summa perfectione a Deo fuisse productas : quandoquidem ex data perfectissima natura necessario secutæ sunt. Neque hoc Deum ullius arguit imperfectionis ; ipsius enim perfectio hoc nos affirmare coëgit. Imo ex hujus contrario clare sequeretur (ut modo ostendi), Deum non esse summe perfectum ; nimirum quia, si res alio modo fuissent productæ, Deo alia natura esset tribuenda, diversa ab ea, quam ex consideratione Entis perfectissimi coacti sumus ei tribuere. Verum non dubito, quin multi hanc sententiam ut absurdam explodant, nec animum ad eandem perpendendam instituere velint ; idque nulla alia de causa, quam quia Deo aliam libertatem assueti sunt attribuere, longe diversam ab illa, quæ a nobis (Defin. 7) tradita est, videlicet absolutam voluntatem. Verum neque etiam dubito, si rem meditari vellent, nostrarumque demonstrationum seriem recte secum perpendere, quin tandem talem libertatem, qualem jam Deo tribuunt, non tantum ut nugatoriam, sed ut magnum scientiæ obstaculum, plane rejiciant. Nec opus est, ut ea, quæ in Scholio Propositionis 17 dicta sunt, hic repetam. Attamen in eorum gratiam adhuc ostendam, quod, quamvis concedatur, voluntatem ad Dei essentiam pertinere, ex ejus perfectione nihilominus sequatur, res nullo alio potuisse modo neque ordine a Deo creari ; quod facile erit ostendere, si prius consideremus id, quod ipsimet concedunt, videlicet ex solo Dei decreto et voluntate pendere, ut unaquæque res id quod est, sit. Nam alias Deus omnium rerum causa non esset. Deinde quod omnia Dei decreta ab æterno ab ipso Deo sancita fuerunt. Nam alias imperfectionis et inconstante argueretur. At cum in æterno non detur quando, ante, nec post : hinc, ex sola scilicet Dei perfectione, sequitur, Deum aliud decernere nunquam posse, nec unquam potuisse ; sive Deum ante sua decreta non fuisse, nec sine ipsis esse posse. At dicent, quod, quamvis supponeretur, quod Deus aliam rerum naturam fecisset, vel quod ab æterno aliud de Natura ejusque ordine decrevisset, nulla inde in Deo sequeretur imperfectio. Verum si hoc dicant, concedent simul, Deum posse sua mutare decreta. Nam si Deus de Natura ejusque ordine aliud, quam decrevit, decrevisset ; hoc est, ut aliud de Natura voluisset et concepisset ; alium necessario, quam jam habet intellectum, et aliam, quam jam habet voluntatem, habuisset. Et si Deo alium intellectum aliamque voluntatem tribuere licet, absque ulla ejus essentiæ ejusque perfectionis mutatione ; quid causæ est, cur jam non possit sua de rebus creatis decreta mutare, et nihilominus æque perfectus manere ? Ejus enim intellectus et voluntas circa res creatas et earum ordinem, in respectu suæ essentiæ et perfectionis, perinde est quomodocunque concipiatur. Deinde omnes, quos vidi, Philosophi concedunt, nullum in Deo dari intellectum potentia, sed tantum actu ; cum autem et ejus intellectus et ejus voluntas ab ejusdem essentia non distinguantur, ut etiam omnes concedunt, sequitur ergo hinc etiam, quod, si Deus alium intellectum actu habuisset, et aliam voluntatem, ejus etiam essentia alia necessario esset ; ac proinde (ut a principio conclusi), si aliter res, quam jam sunt, a Deo productæ essent, Dei intellectus ejusque voluntas hoc est (ut conceditur) ejus essentia, alia esse deberet, quod est absurdum.\n\nCum itaque res nullo alio modo nec ordine a Deo produci potuerint, et, hoc verum esse, ex summa Dei perfectione sequatur ; nulla profecto sana ratio persuadere nobis potest, ut credamus, quod Deus noluerit omnia, quæ in suo intellectu sunt, eadem illa perfectione, qua ipsa intelligit, creare. At dicent, in rebus nullam esse perfectionem neque imperfectionem ; sed id, quod in ipsis est, propter quod perfectæ sunt aut imperfectæ, et bonæ aut malæ dicuntur, a Dei tantum voluntate pendere : atque adeo si Deus voluisset, potuisset efficere, ut id, quod jam perfectio est, summa esset imperfectio, et contra. Verum quid hoc aliud esset, quam aperte affirmare, quod Deus, qui id, quod vult, necessario intelligit, sua voluntate efficere potest, ut res alio modo, quam intelligit, intelligat ; quod (ut modo ostendi) magnum est absurdum. Quare argumentum in ipsos retorquere possum, hoc modo. Omnia a Dei potestate pendent. Ut res itaque aliter se habere possint, Dei necessario voluntas aliter se habere etiam deberet ; atqui Dei voluntas aliter se habere nequit (ut modo ex Dei perfectione evidentissime ostendimus). Ergo neque res aliter se habere possunt. Fateor, hanc opinionem, quæ omnia indifferenti cuidam Dei voluntati subjicit, et ab ipsius beneplacito omnia pendere statuit, minus a vero aberrare, quam illorum, qui statuunt, Deum omnia sub ratione boni agere. Nam hi aliquid extra Deum videntur ponere, quod a Deo non dependet, ad quod Deus, tanquam ad exemplar, in operando attendit, vel ad quod, tanquam ad certum scopum, collineat. Quod profecto nihil aliud est, quam Deum fato subjicere, quo nihil de Deo absurdius statui potest, quem ostendimus tam omnium rerum essentiæ, quam earum existentiæ, primam et unicam liberam causam esse. Quare non est, ut in hoc absurdo refutando tempus consumam.\n\nPROPOSITIO XXXIV\n\nDei potentia est ipsa ipsius essentia.\n\nDEMONSTRATIO\n\nEx sola enim necessitate Dei essentiæ sequitur, Deum esse causam sui (per Prop. 11) et (per Prop. 16 ejusque Coroll.) omnium rerum. Ergo potentia Dei, qua ipse, et omnia, sunt et agunt, est ipsa ipsius essentia. Q. E. D.\n\nPROPOSITIO XXXV\n\nQuicquid concipimus in Dei potestate esse, id necessario est.\n\nDEMONSTRATIO\n\nQuicquid enim in Dei potestate est, id (per Prop. præced.) in ejus essentia ita debet comprehendi, ut ex ea necessario sequatur, adeoque necessario est. Q. E. D.\n\nPROPOSITIO XXXVI\n\nNihil existit, ex cujus natura aliquis effectus non sequatur.\n\nDEMONSTRATIO\n\nQuicquid existit, Dei naturam sive essentiam certo et determinato modo exprimit (per Coroll. Prop. 25), hoc est (per Prop. 34), quicquid existit, Dei potentiam quæ omnium rerum causa est, certo et determinato modo exprimit, adeoque (per Prop. 16) ex eo aliquis effectus sequi debet. Q. E. D.\n\nAPPENDIX\n\nHis Dei naturam ejusque proprietates explicui, ut, quod necessario existit ; quod sit unicus ; quod ex sola suæ naturæ necessitate sit et agat ; quod sit omnium rerum causa libera, et quomodo ; quod omnia in Deo sint, et ab ipso ita pendeant, ut sine ipso nec esse nec concipi possint ; et denique quod omnia a Deo fuerint prædeterminata, non quidem ex libertate voluntatis, sive absoluto beneplacito, sed ex absoluta Dei natura, sive infinita potentia. Porro ubicunque data fuit occasio, præjudicia, quæ impedire poterant, quo minus meæ demonstrationes perciperentur, amovere curavi ; sed quia non pauca adhuc restant præjudicia, quæ etiam, imo maxime impedire poterant, et possunt, quo minus homines rerum concatenationem eo, quo ipsam explicui, modo amplecti possint, eadem hic ad examen rationis vocare operæ pretium duxi. Et quoniam omnia, quæ hic indicare suscipio, præjudicia pendent ab hoc uno, quod scilicet communiter supponant homines, omnes res naturales ut ipsos, propter finem agere, imo ipsum Deum omnia ad certum aliquem finem dirigere, pro certo statuant : dicunt enim, Deum omnia propter hominem fecisse, hominem autem, ut ipsum coleret ; Hoc igitur unum prius considerabo, quærendo scilicet primo causam, cur plerique hoc in præjudicio acquiescant, et omnes natura adeo propensi sin, ad idem amplectendum. Deinde ejusdem falsitatem ostendam et tandem, quomodo ex hoc orta sint præjudicia de bono et malo, merito et peccato, laude et vituperio, ordine et confusione, pulchritudine et deformitate, et de aliis hujus generis. Verum hæc ab humanæ mentis natura deducere, non est hujus loci. Satis hic erit, si pro fundamento id capiam, quod apud omnes debet esse in confesso ; nempe hoc, quod omnes homines rerum causarum ignari nascuntur, et quod omnes appetitum habent suum utile quærendi, cujus rei sunt conscii. Ex his enim sequitur, primo, quod homines se liberos esse opinentur, quandoquidem suarum volitionum suique appetitus sunt conscii, et de causis, a quibus disponuntur ad appetendum et volendum, quia earum sunt ignari nec per somnium cogitant. Sequitur secundo, homines omnia propter finem agere ; videlicet propter utile, quod appetunt ; unde fit, ut semper rerum peractarum causas finales tantum scire expetant, et ubi ipsas audiverint, quiescant ; nimirum quia nullam habent causam ulterius dubitandi. Sin autem easdem ex alio audire nequeant, nihil iis restat, nisi ut ad semet se convertant, et ad fines, a quibus ipsi ad similia determinari solent, reflectant, et si exsuo ingenio ingenium alterius necessario dijudicant. Porro cum in se et extra se non pauca reperiant media, quæ ad suum utile assequendum non parum conducant, ut ex. gr. oculos ad videndum, dentes ad masticandum, herbas et animantia ad alimentum, solem ad illuminandum, mare ad alendum pisces, etc. ; hinc factum, ut omnia naturalia tanquam ad suum utile media considerent. Et quia illa media ab ipsis inventa, non autem parata esse sciunt : hinc causam credendi habuerunt, aliquem alium esse, qui illa media in eorum usum paraverit. Nam postquam res ut media consideraverunt, credere non potuerunt, easdem se ipsas feciss ; sed ex mediis, quæ sibi ipsi parare solent, concludere debuerunt, dari aliquem, vel aliquos Naturæ rectores, humana præditos libertate, qui ipsis omnia curaverint, et in eorum usum omnia fecerint. Atque horum etiam ingenium, quandoquidem de eo nunquam quid audiverant, ex suo judicare debuerunt ; atque hinc statuerunt, Deos omnia in hominum usum dirigere, ut homines sibi devinciant, et in summo ab iisdem honore habeantur ; unde factum, ut unusquisque diversos Deum colendi modos ex suo ingenio excogitaverit, ut Deus eos supra reliquos diligeret, et totam Naturam in usum cœcæ illorum cupiditatis et insatiabilis avaritiæ dirigeret. Atque ita hoc præjudicium in superstitionem versum, et altas in mentibus egit radices ; quod in causa fuit, ut unusquisque maximo conatu omnium rerum causas finales intelligere easque explicare studeret. Sed dum quæsiverunt ostendere, Naturam nihil frustra (hoc est, quod in usum hominum non sit) agere, nihil aliud videntur ostendisse, quam Naturam Deosque æque ac homines delirare. Vide quæso, quo res tandem evasit ! Inter tot Naturæ commoda non pauca reperire debuerunt incommoda, tempestates scilicet, terræ motus, morbos, etc., atque hæc statuerunt propterea evenire, quod Dii irati essent ob injurias sibi ab hominibus factas, sive ob peccata in suo cultu commissa ; et quamvis experientia indies reclamaret, ac infinitis exemplis ostenderet commoda atque incommoda piis æque ac impiis promiscue evenire, non ideo ab inveterato præjudicio destiterunt : facilius enimi iis fuit, hoc inter alia incognita, quorum usum ignorabant, ponere, et sic præsentem suum et innatum statum ignorantiæ retinere, quam totam illam fabricam destruere et novam excogitare. Unde pro certo statuerunt, Deorum judicia humanum captum longissime superare : quæ sane unica fuisset causa, ut veritas humanum genus in æternum lateret ; nisi Mathesis, quæ non circa fines sed tantum circa figurarum essentias et proprietates versatur, aliam veritatis normam hominibus ostendisset ; et præter Mathesin aliæ etiam adsignari possunt causæ (quas hic enumerare supervacaneum est), a quibus fieri potuit, ut homines communia hæc præjudicia animadverterent, et in veram rerum cognitionem ducerentur.","paragraphs":[{"id":"RAW_QGOH_PP_3:DISPLAY-0001","text":"Quicquid determinatum est ad existendum et operandum, a Deo sic determinatum est (per Prop. 26 et Coroll. Prop. 24). At id, quod finitum est et determinatam habet existentiam, ab absoluta natura alicujus Dei attributi produci non potuit : quicquid enim ex absoluta natura alicujus Dei attributi sequitur, id infinitum et æternum est (per Prop. 21). Debuit ergo ex Deo, vel aliquo ejus attributo sequi, quatenus aliquo modo affectum consideratur ; præter enim substantiam et modos nil datur (per Axiom. 1 et Defin. 3 et 5) ; et modi (per Coroll. Prop. 25) nihil sunt nisi Dei attributorum affectiones. At ex Deo, vel aliquo ejus attributo, quatenus affectum est modificatione, quæ æterna et infinita est, sequi etiam non potuit (per Prop. 22). Debuit ergo sequi, vel ad existendum et operandum determînari, a Deo vel aliquo ejus attributo, quatenus modificatum est modificatione quæ finita est et determinatam habet existentiam. Quod erat primum. Deinde hæc rursus causa, sive hic modus (per eandem rationem qua primam partem hujus jamjam demonstravimus) debuit etiam determinari ab alia, quæ etiam finita est et determinatam habet existentiam, et rursus haec ultima (per eandem rationem) ab alia, et sic semper (per eandem rationem) in infinitum. Q. E. D.","kind":"display"},{"id":"RAW_QGOH_PP_3:DISPLAY-0002","text":"SCHOLIUM","kind":"display"},{"id":"RAW_QGOH_PP_3:DISPLAY-0003","text":"Cum quædam a Deo immediate produci debuerunt, videlicet ea, quæ ex absoluta ejus natura necessario sequuntur, mediantibus his primis quæ tamen sine Deo nec esse nec concipi possunt ; hinc sequitur : 1° quod Deus sit rerum immediate ab ipso productarum causa absolute proxima ; non vero in suo genere, ut ajunt. Nam Dei effectus sine sua causa nec esse nec concipi possunt (per Prop. 15 et Coroll. Prop. 24). Sequitur : 2° quod Deus non potest proprie dici causa esse remota rerum singularium, nisi forte ea de causa, ut scilicet has ab iis, quas immediate produxit, vel potius quæ ex absoluta ejus natura sequuntur, distinguamus, Nam per causam remotam talem intelligimus, quæ cum effectu nullo modo conjuncta est. At omnia, quae sunt, in Deo sunt, et a Deo ita dependent, ut sine ipso nec esse nec concipi possint.","kind":"display"},{"id":"RAW_QGOH_PP_3:DISPLAY-0004","text":"PROPOSITIO XXIX","kind":"display"},{"id":"RAW_QGOH_PP_3:DISPLAY-0005","text":"In rerum natura nullum datur contingens ; sed omnia ex necessitate divinæ naturæ determinata sunt ad certo modo existendum et operandum.","kind":"display"},{"id":"RAW_QGOH_PP_3:DISPLAY-0006","text":"DEMONSTRATIO","kind":"display"},{"id":"RAW_QGOH_PP_3:DISPLAY-0007","text":"Quicquid est, in Deo est (per Prop. 15) : Deus autem non potest dici res contingens : Nam (per Prop. 11) necessario, non vero contingenter existit. Modi deinde divinæ naturæ ex eadem etiam necessario, non vero contingenter secuti sunt (per Prop. 16), idque vel quatenus divina natura absolute (per Prop. 21), vel quatenus certo modo ad agendum determinata consideratur (per Prop. 27). Porro horum modorum Deus non tantum est causa, quatenus simpliciter existunt (per Coroll. Prop. 24), sed etiam (per Prop. 26) quatenus ad aliquid operandum determinati considerantur. Quod si a Deo (per eand. Prop.) determinati non sint, impossibile, non vero contingens est, ut se ipsos determinent ; et contra (per Prop. 27) si a Deo determinati sint, impossibile, non vero contingens est, ut se ipsos indeterminatos reddant. Quare omnia ex necessitate divinæ naturæ determinata sunt, non tantum ad existendum, sed etiam ad certo modo existendum et operandum, nullumque datur contingens. Q. E. D.","kind":"display"},{"id":"RAW_QGOH_PP_3:DISPLAY-0008","text":"SCHOLIUM","kind":"display"},{"id":"RAW_QGOH_PP_3:DISPLAY-0009","text":"Antequam ulterius pergam, hic, quid nobis per Naturam Naturantem, et quid per Naturam Naturatam intelligendum sit, explicare volo, vel potius monere. Nam ex antecedentibus jam constare existimo, nempe, quod per Naturam Naturantem nobis intelligendum est id, quod in se est et per se concipitur, sive talia substantiae attributa, quæ æternam et infinitam essentiam exprimunt, hoc est (per Coroll. 1 Prop. 14 et Coroll. 2 Prop. 17), Deus quatenus ut causa libera consideratur. Per Naturam Naturatam autem intelligo id omne, quod ex necessitate Dei naturæ, sive uniuscu jusque Dei attributorum sequitur, hoc est omnes Dei attributorum modos, quatenus considerantur ut res, quæ in Deo sunt, et quæ sine Deo nec esse nec concipi possunt.","kind":"display"},{"id":"RAW_QGOH_PP_3:DISPLAY-0010","text":"PROPOSITIO XXX","kind":"display"},{"id":"RAW_QGOH_PP_3:DISPLAY-0011","text":"Intellectus, actu finitus aut actu infinitus, Dei attributa Deique affectiones comprehendere debet, et nihil aliud.","kind":"display"},{"id":"RAW_QGOH_PP_3:DISPLAY-0012","text":"DEMONSTRATIO","kind":"display"},{"id":"RAW_QGOH_PP_3:DISPLAY-0013","text":"Idea vera debet convenire cum suo ideato (per Axiom. 6), hoc est (ut per se notum) id, quod in intellectu objective continetur, debet necessario in Natura dari : atqui in Natura (per Coroll. 1 Prop. 14) non nisi una substantia datur, nempe Deus ; nec ullæ aliæ affectiones (per Prop. 15), quam quæ in Deo sunt, et quæ (per eandem Prop.) sine Deo nec esse nec concipi possunt ; ergo intellectus, actu finitus aut actu infinitus, Dei attributa Deique affectiones comprehendere debet, et nihil aliud. Q. E. D.","kind":"display"},{"id":"RAW_QGOH_PP_3:DISPLAY-0014","text":"PROPOSITIO XXXI","kind":"display"},{"id":"RAW_QGOH_PP_3:DISPLAY-0015","text":"Intellectus actu, sive is finitus sit sive infinitus, ut et voluntas, cupiditas, amor, etc., ad Naturam Naturatam, non vero ad Naturantem referri debent.","kind":"display"},{"id":"RAW_QGOH_PP_3:DISPLAY-0016","text":"DEMONSTRATIO","kind":"display"},{"id":"RAW_QGOH_PP_3:DISPLAY-0017","text":"Per intellectum enim (ut per se notum) non intelligimus absolutam cogitationem, sed certum tantum modum cogitandi, qui modus ab aliis, scilicet cupiditate, amore, etc., differt, adeoque (per Defin. 5) per absolutam cogitationem concipi debet ; nempe (per. Prop. 15 et Defin. 6) per aliquod Dei attributum, quod æternam et infinitam cogitationis essentiam exprimit, ita concipi debet, ut sine ipso nec esse nec concipi possit ; ac propterea (per Schol. Prop. 29) ad Naturam Naturatam, non vero Naturantem referri debet, ut etiam reliqui modi cogitandi. Q. E. D.","kind":"display"},{"id":"RAW_QGOH_PP_3:DISPLAY-0018","text":"SCHOLIUM","kind":"display"},{"id":"RAW_QGOH_PP_3:DISPLAY-0019","text":"Ratio, cur hic loquar de intellectu actu, non est, quia concedo, ullum dari intellectum potentia ; sed, quia omnem confusionem vitare cupio, nolui loqui, nisi de re nobis quam clarissime percepta, de ipsa scilicet intellectione, qua nihil nobis clarius percipitur. Nihil enim intelligere possumus, quod ad perfectiorem intellectionis cognitionem non conducat.","kind":"display"},{"id":"RAW_QGOH_PP_3:DISPLAY-0020","text":"PROPOSITIO XXXII","kind":"display"},{"id":"RAW_QGOH_PP_3:DISPLAY-0021","text":"Voluntas non potest vocari causa libera, sed tantum necessaria.","kind":"display"},{"id":"RAW_QGOH_PP_3:DISPLAY-0022","text":"DEMONSTRATIO","kind":"display"},{"id":"RAW_QGOH_PP_3:DISPLAY-0023","text":"Voluntas certus tantum cogitandi modus est, sicuti intellectus ; adeoque (per Prop. 28) unaquæque volitio non potest existere, neque ad operandum determinari, nisi ab alia causa determinetur, et hæc rursus ab alia, et sic porro in infinitum. Quod si voluntas infinita supponatur, debet etiam ad existendum et operandum determinari a Deo, non quatenus substantia absolute infinita est, sed quatenus attributum habet, quod infinitam et æternam cogitationis essentiam exprimit (per Prop. 23). Quocumque igitur modo, sive finita sive infinita, concipiatur, causam requirit, a qua ad existendum et operandum determinetur ; adeoque (per Defin. 7) non potest dici causa libera, sed tantum necessaria vel coacta. Q. E. D.","kind":"display"},{"id":"RAW_QGOH_PP_3:DISPLAY-0024","text":"COROLLARIUM I","kind":"display"},{"id":"RAW_QGOH_PP_3:DISPLAY-0025","text":"Hinc sequitur : I° Deum non operari ex libertate voluntatis.","kind":"display"},{"id":"RAW_QGOH_PP_3:DISPLAY-0026","text":"COROLLARIUM II","kind":"display"},{"id":"RAW_QGOH_PP_3:DISPLAY-0027","text":"Sequitur : II° voluntatem et intellectum ad Dei naturam ita sese habere, ut motus et quies, et absolute ut omnia naturalia, quæ (per Prop. 29) a Deo ad existendum et operandum certo modo determinari debent. Nam voluntas, ut reliqua omnia, causa indiget, a qua ad existendum et operandum certo modo determinetur. Et, quamvis ex data voluntate sive intellectu infinita sequantur, non tamen propterea Deus magis dici potest ex libertate voluntatis agere, quam propter ea, quæ ex motu et quiete sequuntur (infinita enim ex his etiam sequuntur), dici potest ex libertate motus et quietis agere. Quare voluntas ad Dei naturam non magis pertinet, quam reliqua naturalia, sed ad ipsam eodem modo sese habet, ut motus et quies, et omnia reliqua, quæ ostendimus ex necessitate divinæ naturæ sequi, et ab eadem ad existendum et operandum certo modo determinari.","kind":"display"},{"id":"RAW_QGOH_PP_3:DISPLAY-0028","text":"PROPOSITIO XXXIII","kind":"display"},{"id":"RAW_QGOH_PP_3:DISPLAY-0029","text":"Res nullo alio modo, neque alio ordine a Deo produci potuerunt, quam productæ sunt.","kind":"display"},{"id":"RAW_QGOH_PP_3:DISPLAY-0030","text":"DEMONSTRATIO","kind":"display"},{"id":"RAW_QGOH_PP_3:DISPLAY-0031","text":"Res enim omnes data Dei natura necessario secutæ sunt (per Prop. 16), et ex necessitate naturæ Dei determinatæ sunt ad certo modo existendum et operandum (per Prop. 29). Si itaque res alterius naturæ potuissent esse, vel alio modo ad operandum determinari, ut naturæ ordo alius esset ; ergo Dei etiam natura alia posset esse, quam jam est ; ac proinde (per Prop. 11) illa etiam deberet existere, et consequenter duo vel plures possent dari Dii, quod (per Coroll. 1 Prop. 14) est absurdum. Quapropte res nullo alio modo, neque alio ordine, etc. Q. E. D.","kind":"display"},{"id":"RAW_QGOH_PP_3:DISPLAY-0032","text":"SCHOLIUM I","kind":"display"},{"id":"RAW_QGOH_PP_3:DISPLAY-0033","text":"Quoniam his luce meridiana clarius ostendi, nihil absolute in rebus dari, propter quod contingentes dicantur, explicare jam paucis volo, quid nobis per Contingens erit intelligendum ; sed prius, quid per Necessarium, et Impossibile. Res aliqua necessaria dicitur, vel ratione suæ essentiæ, vel ratione causæ. Rei enim alicujus existentia vel ex ipsius essentia et definitione, vel ex data causa efficiente necessario sequitur. Deinde his etiam de causis res aliqua impossibilis dicitur ; nimirum quia vel ipsius essentia seu definitio contradictionem involvit vel quia nulla causa externa datur, ad talem rem producendam determinata. At res aliqua nulla alia de causa contingens dicitur, nisi respectu defectus nostræ cognitionis. Res enim, cujus essentiam contradictionem involvere ignoramus, vel de qua probe scimus, eandem nullam contradictionem involvere et tamen de ipsius existentia nihil certo affirmare possumus, propterea quod ordo causarum nos latet ; ea nunquam, nec ut necessaria, nec ut impossibilis videri nobis potest, ideoque eandem vel contingentem, vel possibilem vocamus.","kind":"display"},{"id":"RAW_QGOH_PP_3:DISPLAY-0034","text":"SCHOLIUM II","kind":"display"},{"id":"RAW_QGOH_PP_3:DISPLAY-0035","text":"Ex præcedentibus clare sequitur, res summa perfectione a Deo fuisse productas : quandoquidem ex data perfectissima natura necessario secutæ sunt. Neque hoc Deum ullius arguit imperfectionis ; ipsius enim perfectio hoc nos affirmare coëgit. Imo ex hujus contrario clare sequeretur (ut modo ostendi), Deum non esse summe perfectum ; nimirum quia, si res alio modo fuissent productæ, Deo alia natura esset tribuenda, diversa ab ea, quam ex consideratione Entis perfectissimi coacti sumus ei tribuere. Verum non dubito, quin multi hanc sententiam ut absurdam explodant, nec animum ad eandem perpendendam instituere velint ; idque nulla alia de causa, quam quia Deo aliam libertatem assueti sunt attribuere, longe diversam ab illa, quæ a nobis (Defin. 7) tradita est, videlicet absolutam voluntatem. Verum neque etiam dubito, si rem meditari vellent, nostrarumque demonstrationum seriem recte secum perpendere, quin tandem talem libertatem, qualem jam Deo tribuunt, non tantum ut nugatoriam, sed ut magnum scientiæ obstaculum, plane rejiciant. Nec opus est, ut ea, quæ in Scholio Propositionis 17 dicta sunt, hic repetam. Attamen in eorum gratiam adhuc ostendam, quod, quamvis concedatur, voluntatem ad Dei essentiam pertinere, ex ejus perfectione nihilominus sequatur, res nullo alio potuisse modo neque ordine a Deo creari ; quod facile erit ostendere, si prius consideremus id, quod ipsimet concedunt, videlicet ex solo Dei decreto et voluntate pendere, ut unaquæque res id quod est, sit. Nam alias Deus omnium rerum causa non esset. Deinde quod omnia Dei decreta ab æterno ab ipso Deo sancita fuerunt. Nam alias imperfectionis et inconstante argueretur. At cum in æterno non detur quando, ante, nec post : hinc, ex sola scilicet Dei perfectione, sequitur, Deum aliud decernere nunquam posse, nec unquam potuisse ; sive Deum ante sua decreta non fuisse, nec sine ipsis esse posse. At dicent, quod, quamvis supponeretur, quod Deus aliam rerum naturam fecisset, vel quod ab æterno aliud de Natura ejusque ordine decrevisset, nulla inde in Deo sequeretur imperfectio. Verum si hoc dicant, concedent simul, Deum posse sua mutare decreta. Nam si Deus de Natura ejusque ordine aliud, quam decrevit, decrevisset ; hoc est, ut aliud de Natura voluisset et concepisset ; alium necessario, quam jam habet intellectum, et aliam, quam jam habet voluntatem, habuisset. Et si Deo alium intellectum aliamque voluntatem tribuere licet, absque ulla ejus essentiæ ejusque perfectionis mutatione ; quid causæ est, cur jam non possit sua de rebus creatis decreta mutare, et nihilominus æque perfectus manere ? Ejus enim intellectus et voluntas circa res creatas et earum ordinem, in respectu suæ essentiæ et perfectionis, perinde est quomodocunque concipiatur. Deinde omnes, quos vidi, Philosophi concedunt, nullum in Deo dari intellectum potentia, sed tantum actu ; cum autem et ejus intellectus et ejus voluntas ab ejusdem essentia non distinguantur, ut etiam omnes concedunt, sequitur ergo hinc etiam, quod, si Deus alium intellectum actu habuisset, et aliam voluntatem, ejus etiam essentia alia necessario esset ; ac proinde (ut a principio conclusi), si aliter res, quam jam sunt, a Deo productæ essent, Dei intellectus ejusque voluntas hoc est (ut conceditur) ejus essentia, alia esse deberet, quod est absurdum.","kind":"display"},{"id":"RAW_QGOH_PP_3:DISPLAY-0036","text":"Cum itaque res nullo alio modo nec ordine a Deo produci potuerint, et, hoc verum esse, ex summa Dei perfectione sequatur ; nulla profecto sana ratio persuadere nobis potest, ut credamus, quod Deus noluerit omnia, quæ in suo intellectu sunt, eadem illa perfectione, qua ipsa intelligit, creare. At dicent, in rebus nullam esse perfectionem neque imperfectionem ; sed id, quod in ipsis est, propter quod perfectæ sunt aut imperfectæ, et bonæ aut malæ dicuntur, a Dei tantum voluntate pendere : atque adeo si Deus voluisset, potuisset efficere, ut id, quod jam perfectio est, summa esset imperfectio, et contra. Verum quid hoc aliud esset, quam aperte affirmare, quod Deus, qui id, quod vult, necessario intelligit, sua voluntate efficere potest, ut res alio modo, quam intelligit, intelligat ; quod (ut modo ostendi) magnum est absurdum. Quare argumentum in ipsos retorquere possum, hoc modo. Omnia a Dei potestate pendent. Ut res itaque aliter se habere possint, Dei necessario voluntas aliter se habere etiam deberet ; atqui Dei voluntas aliter se habere nequit (ut modo ex Dei perfectione evidentissime ostendimus). Ergo neque res aliter se habere possunt. Fateor, hanc opinionem, quæ omnia indifferenti cuidam Dei voluntati subjicit, et ab ipsius beneplacito omnia pendere statuit, minus a vero aberrare, quam illorum, qui statuunt, Deum omnia sub ratione boni agere. Nam hi aliquid extra Deum videntur ponere, quod a Deo non dependet, ad quod Deus, tanquam ad exemplar, in operando attendit, vel ad quod, tanquam ad certum scopum, collineat. Quod profecto nihil aliud est, quam Deum fato subjicere, quo nihil de Deo absurdius statui potest, quem ostendimus tam omnium rerum essentiæ, quam earum existentiæ, primam et unicam liberam causam esse. Quare non est, ut in hoc absurdo refutando tempus consumam.","kind":"display"},{"id":"RAW_QGOH_PP_3:DISPLAY-0037","text":"PROPOSITIO XXXIV","kind":"display"},{"id":"RAW_QGOH_PP_3:DISPLAY-0038","text":"Dei potentia est ipsa ipsius essentia.","kind":"display"},{"id":"RAW_QGOH_PP_3:DISPLAY-0039","text":"DEMONSTRATIO","kind":"display"},{"id":"RAW_QGOH_PP_3:DISPLAY-0040","text":"Ex sola enim necessitate Dei essentiæ sequitur, Deum esse causam sui (per Prop. 11) et (per Prop. 16 ejusque Coroll.) omnium rerum. Ergo potentia Dei, qua ipse, et omnia, sunt et agunt, est ipsa ipsius essentia. Q. E. D.","kind":"display"},{"id":"RAW_QGOH_PP_3:DISPLAY-0041","text":"PROPOSITIO XXXV","kind":"display"},{"id":"RAW_QGOH_PP_3:DISPLAY-0042","text":"Quicquid concipimus in Dei potestate esse, id necessario est.","kind":"display"},{"id":"RAW_QGOH_PP_3:DISPLAY-0043","text":"DEMONSTRATIO","kind":"display"},{"id":"RAW_QGOH_PP_3:DISPLAY-0044","text":"Quicquid enim in Dei potestate est, id (per Prop. præced.) in ejus essentia ita debet comprehendi, ut ex ea necessario sequatur, adeoque necessario est. Q. E. D.","kind":"display"},{"id":"RAW_QGOH_PP_3:DISPLAY-0045","text":"PROPOSITIO XXXVI","kind":"display"},{"id":"RAW_QGOH_PP_3:DISPLAY-0046","text":"Nihil existit, ex cujus natura aliquis effectus non sequatur.","kind":"display"},{"id":"RAW_QGOH_PP_3:DISPLAY-0047","text":"DEMONSTRATIO","kind":"display"},{"id":"RAW_QGOH_PP_3:DISPLAY-0048","text":"Quicquid existit, Dei naturam sive essentiam certo et determinato modo exprimit (per Coroll. Prop. 25), hoc est (per Prop. 34), quicquid existit, Dei potentiam quæ omnium rerum causa est, certo et determinato modo exprimit, adeoque (per Prop. 16) ex eo aliquis effectus sequi debet. Q. E. D.","kind":"display"},{"id":"RAW_QGOH_PP_3:DISPLAY-0049","text":"APPENDIX","kind":"display"},{"id":"RAW_QGOH_PP_3:DISPLAY-0050","text":"His Dei naturam ejusque proprietates explicui, ut, quod necessario existit ; quod sit unicus ; quod ex sola suæ naturæ necessitate sit et agat ; quod sit omnium rerum causa libera, et quomodo ; quod omnia in Deo sint, et ab ipso ita pendeant, ut sine ipso nec esse nec concipi possint ; et denique quod omnia a Deo fuerint prædeterminata, non quidem ex libertate voluntatis, sive absoluto beneplacito, sed ex absoluta Dei natura, sive infinita potentia. Porro ubicunque data fuit occasio, præjudicia, quæ impedire poterant, quo minus meæ demonstrationes perciperentur, amovere curavi ; sed quia non pauca adhuc restant præjudicia, quæ etiam, imo maxime impedire poterant, et possunt, quo minus homines rerum concatenationem eo, quo ipsam explicui, modo amplecti possint, eadem hic ad examen rationis vocare operæ pretium duxi. Et quoniam omnia, quæ hic indicare suscipio, præjudicia pendent ab hoc uno, quod scilicet communiter supponant homines, omnes res naturales ut ipsos, propter finem agere, imo ipsum Deum omnia ad certum aliquem finem dirigere, pro certo statuant : dicunt enim, Deum omnia propter hominem fecisse, hominem autem, ut ipsum coleret ; Hoc igitur unum prius considerabo, quærendo scilicet primo causam, cur plerique hoc in præjudicio acquiescant, et omnes natura adeo propensi sin, ad idem amplectendum. Deinde ejusdem falsitatem ostendam et tandem, quomodo ex hoc orta sint præjudicia de bono et malo, merito et peccato, laude et vituperio, ordine et confusione, pulchritudine et deformitate, et de aliis hujus generis. Verum hæc ab humanæ mentis natura deducere, non est hujus loci. Satis hic erit, si pro fundamento id capiam, quod apud omnes debet esse in confesso ; nempe hoc, quod omnes homines rerum causarum ignari nascuntur, et quod omnes appetitum habent suum utile quærendi, cujus rei sunt conscii. Ex his enim sequitur, primo, quod homines se liberos esse opinentur, quandoquidem suarum volitionum suique appetitus sunt conscii, et de causis, a quibus disponuntur ad appetendum et volendum, quia earum sunt ignari nec per somnium cogitant. Sequitur secundo, homines omnia propter finem agere ; videlicet propter utile, quod appetunt ; unde fit, ut semper rerum peractarum causas finales tantum scire expetant, et ubi ipsas audiverint, quiescant ; nimirum quia nullam habent causam ulterius dubitandi. Sin autem easdem ex alio audire nequeant, nihil iis restat, nisi ut ad semet se convertant, et ad fines, a quibus ipsi ad similia determinari solent, reflectant, et si exsuo ingenio ingenium alterius necessario dijudicant. Porro cum in se et extra se non pauca reperiant media, quæ ad suum utile assequendum non parum conducant, ut ex. gr. oculos ad videndum, dentes ad masticandum, herbas et animantia ad alimentum, solem ad illuminandum, mare ad alendum pisces, etc. ; hinc factum, ut omnia naturalia tanquam ad suum utile media considerent. Et quia illa media ab ipsis inventa, non autem parata esse sciunt : hinc causam credendi habuerunt, aliquem alium esse, qui illa media in eorum usum paraverit. Nam postquam res ut media consideraverunt, credere non potuerunt, easdem se ipsas feciss ; sed ex mediis, quæ sibi ipsi parare solent, concludere debuerunt, dari aliquem, vel aliquos Naturæ rectores, humana præditos libertate, qui ipsis omnia curaverint, et in eorum usum omnia fecerint. Atque horum etiam ingenium, quandoquidem de eo nunquam quid audiverant, ex suo judicare debuerunt ; atque hinc statuerunt, Deos omnia in hominum usum dirigere, ut homines sibi devinciant, et in summo ab iisdem honore habeantur ; unde factum, ut unusquisque diversos Deum colendi modos ex suo ingenio excogitaverit, ut Deus eos supra reliquos diligeret, et totam Naturam in usum cœcæ illorum cupiditatis et insatiabilis avaritiæ dirigeret. Atque ita hoc præjudicium in superstitionem versum, et altas in mentibus egit radices ; quod in causa fuit, ut unusquisque maximo conatu omnium rerum causas finales intelligere easque explicare studeret. Sed dum quæsiverunt ostendere, Naturam nihil frustra (hoc est, quod in usum hominum non sit) agere, nihil aliud videntur ostendisse, quam Naturam Deosque æque ac homines delirare. Vide quæso, quo res tandem evasit ! Inter tot Naturæ commoda non pauca reperire debuerunt incommoda, tempestates scilicet, terræ motus, morbos, etc., atque hæc statuerunt propterea evenire, quod Dii irati essent ob injurias sibi ab hominibus factas, sive ob peccata in suo cultu commissa ; et quamvis experientia indies reclamaret, ac infinitis exemplis ostenderet commoda atque incommoda piis æque ac impiis promiscue evenire, non ideo ab inveterato præjudicio destiterunt : facilius enimi iis fuit, hoc inter alia incognita, quorum usum ignorabant, ponere, et sic præsentem suum et innatum statum ignorantiæ retinere, quam totam illam fabricam destruere et novam excogitare. Unde pro certo statuerunt, Deorum judicia humanum captum longissime superare : quæ sane unica fuisset causa, ut veritas humanum genus in æternum lateret ; nisi Mathesis, quæ non circa fines sed tantum circa figurarum essentias et proprietates versatur, aliam veritatis normam hominibus ostendisset ; et præter Mathesin aliæ etiam adsignari possunt causæ (quas hic enumerare supervacaneum est), a quibus fieri potuit, ut homines communia hæc præjudicia animadverterent, et in veram rerum cognitionem ducerentur.","kind":"display"}],"sha256":"44d2044100bcb560f9f1fa05e0a78032de0d053cefaf72bb9395aba386e081f2","language_code":"la","language_name":"Latina","translations":[{"id":"SOURCE_TR_20261015T012710768164Z_8ff36f61","language_code":"en","language_name":"English","title":"Part One - Concerning God · passage 3","source_sha256":"44d2044100bcb560f9f1fa05e0a78032de0d053cefaf72bb9395aba386e081f2","sha256":"38f6eb1a44fa53d4aa90d950120270e134c7d3a05249aea0a76395e8627fd244","rendition_type":"machine","review_status":"machine","model":"gpt-5.6-terra","created":"2026-10-15T01:27:10.768318+00:00","paragraphs":[{"id":"SOURCE_TR_20261015T012710768164Z_8ff36f61:DISPLAY-0001","text":"Whatever is determined to exist and to act is so determined by God (by Prop. 26 and Corollary of Prop. 24). But that which is finite and has a determinate existence could not have been produced from the absolute nature of any attribute of God; for whatever follows from the absolute nature of any attribute of God is infinite and eternal (by Prop. 21). Therefore it had to follow from God, or from some attribute of him, insofar as it is considered as affected in some way; for besides substance and modes nothing is given (by Axiom 1 and Definitions 3 and 5); and modes (by Corollary of Prop. 25) are nothing but affections of God's attributes. But neither could it follow from God, or from some attribute of him, insofar as it is affected by a modification which is eternal and infinite (by Prop. 22).","kind":"display","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_QGOH_PP_3:DISPLAY-0001"]},{"id":"SOURCE_TR_20261015T012710768164Z_8ff36f61:DISPLAY-0002","text":"Therefore it had to follow, or be determined to exist and to act, by God or by some attribute of him, insofar as it is modified by a modification which is finite and has a determinate existence. This was the first point. Next, this cause, or this mode (by the same reasoning by which we have just now demonstrated the first part of this), also had to be determined by another, which likewise is finite and has a determinate existence, and again this latter by another (by the same reasoning), and so always (by the same reasoning) to infinity. Q. E. D.","kind":"display","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_QGOH_PP_3:DISPLAY-0001"]},{"id":"RAW_QGOH_PP_3:DISPLAY-0002","text":"SCHOLIUM","kind":"display","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_QGOH_PP_3:DISPLAY-0002"]},{"id":"RAW_QGOH_PP_3:DISPLAY-0003","text":"Since certain things had to be produced immediately by God—namely, those which necessarily follow from his absolute nature—by means of these first things, which nevertheless can neither be nor be conceived without God, it follows: 1° that God is the absolutely proximate cause of things immediately produced by him; but not proximate in his kind, as they say. For God's effects can neither be nor be conceived without their cause (by Prop. 15 and Corollary of Prop. 24). It follows: 2° that God cannot properly be called the remote cause of singular things, unless perhaps for this reason, namely, in order that we may distinguish these from those which he immediately produced, or rather which follow from his absolute nature. For by a remote cause we understand one which is in no way conjoined with its effect. But all things that are are in God, and so depend on God that without him they can neither be nor be conceived.","kind":"display","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_QGOH_PP_3:DISPLAY-0003"]},{"id":"RAW_QGOH_PP_3:DISPLAY-0004","text":"PROPOSITION XXIX","kind":"display","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_QGOH_PP_3:DISPLAY-0004"]},{"id":"RAW_QGOH_PP_3:DISPLAY-0005","text":"In the nature of things nothing contingent is given; but all things are determined from the necessity of the divine nature to exist and to act in a certain manner.","kind":"display","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_QGOH_PP_3:DISPLAY-0005"]},{"id":"RAW_QGOH_PP_3:DISPLAY-0006","text":"DEMONSTRATION","kind":"display","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_QGOH_PP_3:DISPLAY-0006"]},{"id":"SOURCE_TR_20261015T012710768164Z_8ff36f61:DISPLAY-0008","text":"Whatever is, is in God (by Prop. 15); but God cannot be called a contingent thing. For (by Prop. 11) he exists necessarily, not contingently. Further, the modes of the divine nature also follow from it necessarily, not contingently (by Prop. 16), and this either insofar as the divine nature is considered absolutely (by Prop. 21), or insofar as it is considered as determined to act in a certain manner (by Prop. 27). Moreover, God is the cause of these modes not only insofar as they simply exist (by Corollary of Prop. 24), but also (by Prop. 26) insofar as they are considered as determined to produce something. If they are not determined by God (by the same Prop.), it is impossible, not contingent, that they should determine themselves; and conversely (by Prop. 27), if they are determined by God, it is impossible, not contingent, that they should render themselves undetermined.","kind":"display","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_QGOH_PP_3:DISPLAY-0007"]},{"id":"SOURCE_TR_20261015T012710768164Z_8ff36f61:DISPLAY-0009","text":"Wherefore all things are determined from the necessity of the divine nature, not only to exist, but also to exist and to act in a certain manner, and nothing contingent is given. Q. E. D.","kind":"display","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_QGOH_PP_3:DISPLAY-0007"]},{"id":"RAW_QGOH_PP_3:DISPLAY-0008","text":"SCHOLIUM","kind":"display","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_QGOH_PP_3:DISPLAY-0008"]},{"id":"RAW_QGOH_PP_3:DISPLAY-0009","text":"Before proceeding further, I wish here to explain, or rather to note, what is to be understood by Nature naturing and what by Nature natured. For from what precedes I think it is now established, namely, that by Nature naturing we are to understand that which is in itself and is conceived through itself, or such attributes of substance as express an eternal and infinite essence, that is (by Corollary 1 of Prop. 14 and Corollary 2 of Prop. 17), God insofar as he is considered as a free cause. But by Nature natured I understand everything which follows from the necessity of God's nature, or of each one of God's attributes; that is, all the modes of God's attributes, insofar as they are considered as things which are in God and which can neither be nor be conceived without God.","kind":"display","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_QGOH_PP_3:DISPLAY-0009"]},{"id":"RAW_QGOH_PP_3:DISPLAY-0010","text":"PROPOSITION XXX","kind":"display","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_QGOH_PP_3:DISPLAY-0010"]},{"id":"RAW_QGOH_PP_3:DISPLAY-0011","text":"An intellect actually finite or actually infinite must comprehend God's attributes and God's affections, and nothing else.","kind":"display","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_QGOH_PP_3:DISPLAY-0011"]},{"id":"RAW_QGOH_PP_3:DISPLAY-0012","text":"DEMONSTRATION","kind":"display","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_QGOH_PP_3:DISPLAY-0012"]},{"id":"RAW_QGOH_PP_3:DISPLAY-0013","text":"A true idea must agree with its ideate (by Axiom 6), that is (as is known through itself), that which is contained objectively in the intellect must necessarily be given in Nature. But in Nature (by Corollary 1 of Prop. 14) only one substance is given, namely God; nor are any affections given (by Prop. 15) other than those which are in God and which (by the same Prop.) can neither be nor be conceived without God; therefore an intellect actually finite or actually infinite must comprehend God's attributes and God's affections, and nothing else. Q. E. D.","kind":"display","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_QGOH_PP_3:DISPLAY-0013"]},{"id":"RAW_QGOH_PP_3:DISPLAY-0014","text":"PROPOSITION XXXI","kind":"display","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_QGOH_PP_3:DISPLAY-0014"]},{"id":"RAW_QGOH_PP_3:DISPLAY-0015","text":"The intellect in act, whether finite or infinite, as also will, desire, love, etc., must be referred to Nature natured, not to Nature naturing.","kind":"display","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_QGOH_PP_3:DISPLAY-0015"]},{"id":"RAW_QGOH_PP_3:DISPLAY-0016","text":"DEMONSTRATION","kind":"display","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_QGOH_PP_3:DISPLAY-0016"]},{"id":"RAW_QGOH_PP_3:DISPLAY-0017","text":"For by intellect (as is known through itself) we do not understand absolute thought, but only a certain mode of thinking, which mode differs from others, namely desire, love, etc., and therefore (by Definition 5) must be conceived through absolute thought; namely (by Prop. 15 and Definition 6), it must be conceived through some attribute of God which expresses the eternal and infinite essence of thought, in such a way that without it it can neither be nor be conceived; and therefore (by the Scholium of Prop. 29) it must be referred to Nature natured, not to Nature naturing, as must also the remaining modes of thinking. Q. E. D.","kind":"display","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_QGOH_PP_3:DISPLAY-0017"]},{"id":"RAW_QGOH_PP_3:DISPLAY-0018","text":"SCHOLIUM","kind":"display","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_QGOH_PP_3:DISPLAY-0018"]},{"id":"RAW_QGOH_PP_3:DISPLAY-0019","text":"The reason why I speak here of intellect in act is not that I concede that any intellect is given in potency; but because, wishing to avoid all confusion, I did not wish to speak except of a thing perceived by us as clearly as possible, namely of intellection itself, than which nothing is perceived by us more clearly. For we can understand nothing which does not conduce to a more perfect knowledge of intellection.","kind":"display","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_QGOH_PP_3:DISPLAY-0019"]},{"id":"RAW_QGOH_PP_3:DISPLAY-0020","text":"PROPOSITION XXXII","kind":"display","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_QGOH_PP_3:DISPLAY-0020"]},{"id":"RAW_QGOH_PP_3:DISPLAY-0021","text":"Will cannot be called a free cause, but only a necessary one.","kind":"display","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_QGOH_PP_3:DISPLAY-0021"]},{"id":"RAW_QGOH_PP_3:DISPLAY-0022","text":"DEMONSTRATION","kind":"display","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_QGOH_PP_3:DISPLAY-0022"]},{"id":"RAW_QGOH_PP_3:DISPLAY-0023","text":"Will is only a certain mode of thinking, just as intellect is; and therefore (by Prop. 28) each volition can neither exist nor be determined to act unless it is determined by another cause, and this again by another, and so on to infinity. But if an infinite will be supposed, it too must be determined by God to exist and act, not insofar as he is substance absolutely infinite, but insofar as he has an attribute which expresses an infinite and eternal essence of thought (by Prop. 23). Therefore, in whatever manner it be conceived, whether finite or infinite, it requires a cause by which it is determined to exist and act; and consequently (by Def. 7) it cannot be called a free cause, but only a necessary or compelled one. Q. E. D.","kind":"display","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_QGOH_PP_3:DISPLAY-0023"]},{"id":"RAW_QGOH_PP_3:DISPLAY-0024","text":"COROLLARY I","kind":"display","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_QGOH_PP_3:DISPLAY-0024"]},{"id":"RAW_QGOH_PP_3:DISPLAY-0025","text":"Hence it follows: I. that God does not act from freedom of the will.","kind":"display","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_QGOH_PP_3:DISPLAY-0025"]},{"id":"RAW_QGOH_PP_3:DISPLAY-0026","text":"COROLLARY II","kind":"display","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_QGOH_PP_3:DISPLAY-0026"]},{"id":"RAW_QGOH_PP_3:DISPLAY-0027","text":"It follows: II. that will and intellect are related to God's nature in the same way as motion and rest, and absolutely as are all natural things, which (by Prop. 29) must be determined by God to exist and act in a certain manner. For will, like all other things, needs a cause by which it is determined to exist and act in a certain manner. And although infinitely many things follow from a given will or intellect, God nevertheless cannot on that account be said to act from freedom of the will any more than, because of those things which follow from motion and rest (for infinitely many things also follow from these), he can be said to act from freedom of motion and rest. Therefore will pertains to God's nature no more than other natural things do, but is related to it in the same way as motion and rest, and all the other things which we have shown to follow from the necessity of the divine nature and to be determined by it to exist and act in a certain manner.","kind":"display","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_QGOH_PP_3:DISPLAY-0027"]},{"id":"RAW_QGOH_PP_3:DISPLAY-0028","text":"PROPOSITION XXXIII","kind":"display","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_QGOH_PP_3:DISPLAY-0028"]},{"id":"RAW_QGOH_PP_3:DISPLAY-0029","text":"Things could have been produced by God in no other manner and in no other order than they have been produced.","kind":"display","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_QGOH_PP_3:DISPLAY-0029"]},{"id":"RAW_QGOH_PP_3:DISPLAY-0030","text":"DEMONSTRATION","kind":"display","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_QGOH_PP_3:DISPLAY-0030"]},{"id":"RAW_QGOH_PP_3:DISPLAY-0031","text":"For all things have necessarily followed from the given nature of God (by Prop. 16), and from the necessity of God's nature have been determined to exist and act in a certain manner (by Prop. 29). If, therefore, things could have been of another nature, or determined to act in another manner, so that the order of nature were different, then God's nature also could be other than it now is; and consequently (by Prop. 11) that nature too would have to exist, and hence two or more Gods could be given—which (by Coroll. 1 of Prop. 14) is absurd. Wherefore things could be produced in no other manner, nor in another order, etc. Q. E. D.","kind":"display","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_QGOH_PP_3:DISPLAY-0031"]},{"id":"RAW_QGOH_PP_3:DISPLAY-0032","text":"SCHOLIUM I","kind":"display","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_QGOH_PP_3:DISPLAY-0032"]},{"id":"SOURCE_TR_20261015T012710768164Z_8ff36f61:DISPLAY-0035","text":"Since by these things I have shown more clearly than the light of noon that there is absolutely nothing in things on account of which they may be called contingent, I now wish briefly to explain what is to be understood by us through Contingent; but first, what through Necessary and Impossible. A thing is called necessary either by reason of its essence or by reason of its cause. For the existence of any thing follows necessarily either from its essence and definition itself, or from a given efficient cause. Further, for these same reasons a thing is called impossible; namely, because either its essence or definition involves a contradiction, or because no external cause is given that is determined to produce such a thing. But a thing is called contingent for no other reason than in respect of the defect of our knowledge.","kind":"display","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_QGOH_PP_3:DISPLAY-0033"]},{"id":"SOURCE_TR_20261015T012710768164Z_8ff36f61:DISPLAY-0036","text":"For a thing whose essence we are ignorant to involve a contradiction, or concerning which we know well that it involves no contradiction, and yet can affirm nothing certain about its existence because the order of causes is hidden from us—for us that thing can never appear either as necessary or as impossible, and therefore we call it either contingent or possible.","kind":"display","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_QGOH_PP_3:DISPLAY-0033"]},{"id":"RAW_QGOH_PP_3:DISPLAY-0034","text":"SCHOLIUM II","kind":"display","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_QGOH_PP_3:DISPLAY-0034"]},{"id":"SOURCE_TR_20261015T012710768164Z_8ff36f61:DISPLAY-0038","text":"From what precedes it clearly follows that things were produced by God with the highest perfection, since they necessarily followed from a given most perfect nature. Nor does this convict God of any imperfection; for his perfection itself compelled us to affirm this. Indeed, from the contrary of this it would clearly follow (as I have just shown) that God is not supremely perfect; namely, because if things had been produced in another manner, another nature would have to be attributed to God, different from that which we have been compelled to attribute to him from consideration of the most perfect Being. But I do not doubt that many will reject this opinion as absurd, and will not be willing to set their minds to consider it; and this for no other reason than because they are accustomed to attribute to God another freedom, far different from that which has been set forth by us (Def. 7), namely, absolute will.","kind":"display","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_QGOH_PP_3:DISPLAY-0035"]},{"id":"SOURCE_TR_20261015T012710768164Z_8ff36f61:DISPLAY-0039","text":"But neither do I doubt that, if they should wish to meditate upon the matter and rightly weigh with themselves the sequence of our demonstrations, they would at last utterly reject such freedom as they now attribute to God, not only as frivolous, but as a great obstacle to knowledge. Nor is there any need for me to repeat here what was said in the Scholium of Proposition 17. Nevertheless, for their benefit, I shall further show that, even if it be granted that will pertains to God's essence, it nevertheless follows from his perfection that things could have been created by God in no other manner nor order; which will be easy to show if we first consider what they themselves concede, namely, that it depends on God's decree and will alone that each thing is what it is. For otherwise God would not be the cause of all things. Next, that all God's decrees were established by God himself from eternity. For otherwise he would be charged with imperfection and inconstancy.","kind":"display","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_QGOH_PP_3:DISPLAY-0035"]},{"id":"SOURCE_TR_20261015T012710768164Z_8ff36f61:DISPLAY-0040","text":"But since in eternity there is no when, before, or after: hence, namely from God's perfection alone, it follows that God can never decree otherwise, nor could ever have done so; or that God was not before his decrees, and cannot be without them. But they will say that, even if it were supposed that God had made another nature of things, or had decreed otherwise from eternity concerning Nature and its order, no imperfection in God would follow from this. But if they say this, they will at the same time concede that God can change his decrees. For if God had decreed otherwise concerning Nature and its order than he has decreed—that is, had willed and conceived otherwise concerning Nature—he would necessarily have had an intellect other than that which he now has, and a will other than that which he now has.","kind":"display","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_QGOH_PP_3:DISPLAY-0035"]},{"id":"SOURCE_TR_20261015T012710768164Z_8ff36f61:DISPLAY-0041","text":"And if it is permissible to attribute to God another intellect and another will, without any change in his essence and his perfection, what reason is there why he should not now be able to change his decrees concerning created things and nevertheless remain equally perfect? For his intellect and will concerning created things and their order are alike, however they may be conceived, in relation to his essence and perfection.","kind":"display","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_QGOH_PP_3:DISPLAY-0035"]},{"id":"SOURCE_TR_20261015T012710768164Z_8ff36f61:DISPLAY-0042","text":"Further, all the philosophers whom I have seen concede that there is no intellect in God in potency, but only in act; but since both his intellect and his will are not distinguished from his essence, as all also concede, it follows from this too that, if God had had another intellect in act and another will, his essence too would necessarily have been other; and consequently (as I concluded at the outset), if things had been produced by God otherwise than they now are, God's intellect and his will—that is (as is conceded), his essence—would have to be other, which is absurd.","kind":"display","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_QGOH_PP_3:DISPLAY-0035"]},{"id":"SOURCE_TR_20261015T012710768164Z_8ff36f61:DISPLAY-0043","text":"Since, therefore, things could have been produced by God in no other manner or order, and since that this is true follows from God's highest perfection, surely no sound reason can persuade us to believe that God did not will to create all the things that are in his intellect with that same perfection with which he understands them. But they will say that there is no perfection or imperfection in things; rather, that which is in them on account of which they are called perfect or imperfect, and good or bad, depends upon God's will alone; and therefore, if God had willed, he could have brought it about that what is now perfection should be the highest imperfection, and conversely. But what else would this be than openly to affirm that God, who necessarily understands what he wills, can by his will bring it about that he understands things otherwise than he understands them; which (as I have just shown) is a great absurdity. Therefore I can turn the argument back against them in this manner.","kind":"display","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_QGOH_PP_3:DISPLAY-0036"]},{"id":"SOURCE_TR_20261015T012710768164Z_8ff36f61:DISPLAY-0044","text":"All things depend upon God's power. Therefore, in order that things might be otherwise, God's will too would necessarily have to be otherwise; but God's will cannot be otherwise (as we have just shown most evidently from God's perfection). Therefore things too cannot be otherwise. I confess that this opinion, which subjects all things to a certain indifferent will of God and maintains that all things depend upon his good pleasure, strays less far from the truth than the opinion of those who maintain that God does all things under the aspect of the good. For these seem to posit something outside God which does not depend upon God, to which God attends in acting as to an exemplar, or at which he aims as at a certain end. This indeed is nothing other than subjecting God to fate, than which nothing more absurd can be maintained concerning God, whom we have shown to be the first and only free cause both of the essences of all things and of their existences.","kind":"display","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_QGOH_PP_3:DISPLAY-0036"]},{"id":"SOURCE_TR_20261015T012710768164Z_8ff36f61:DISPLAY-0045","text":"Therefore there is no need for me to spend time in refuting this absurdity.","kind":"display","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_QGOH_PP_3:DISPLAY-0036"]},{"id":"RAW_QGOH_PP_3:DISPLAY-0037","text":"PROPOSITION XXXIV","kind":"display","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_QGOH_PP_3:DISPLAY-0037"]},{"id":"RAW_QGOH_PP_3:DISPLAY-0038","text":"God's power is his very essence.","kind":"display","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_QGOH_PP_3:DISPLAY-0038"]},{"id":"RAW_QGOH_PP_3:DISPLAY-0039","text":"DEMONSTRATION","kind":"display","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_QGOH_PP_3:DISPLAY-0039"]},{"id":"RAW_QGOH_PP_3:DISPLAY-0040","text":"For from the necessity of God's essence alone it follows that God is the cause of himself (by Prop. 11) and (by Prop. 16 and its Corollary) of all things. Therefore God's power, by which he himself and all things are and act, is his very essence. Q. E. D.","kind":"display","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_QGOH_PP_3:DISPLAY-0040"]},{"id":"RAW_QGOH_PP_3:DISPLAY-0041","text":"PROPOSITION XXXV","kind":"display","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_QGOH_PP_3:DISPLAY-0041"]},{"id":"RAW_QGOH_PP_3:DISPLAY-0042","text":"Whatever we conceive to be in God's power necessarily is.","kind":"display","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_QGOH_PP_3:DISPLAY-0042"]},{"id":"RAW_QGOH_PP_3:DISPLAY-0043","text":"DEMONSTRATION","kind":"display","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_QGOH_PP_3:DISPLAY-0043"]},{"id":"RAW_QGOH_PP_3:DISPLAY-0044","text":"For whatever is in God's power must (by the preceding Prop.) be comprehended in his essence in such a way that it necessarily follows from it, and therefore necessarily is. Q. E. D.","kind":"display","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_QGOH_PP_3:DISPLAY-0044"]},{"id":"RAW_QGOH_PP_3:DISPLAY-0045","text":"PROPOSITION XXXVI","kind":"display","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_QGOH_PP_3:DISPLAY-0045"]},{"id":"RAW_QGOH_PP_3:DISPLAY-0046","text":"Nothing exists from whose nature no effect follows.","kind":"display","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_QGOH_PP_3:DISPLAY-0046"]},{"id":"RAW_QGOH_PP_3:DISPLAY-0047","text":"DEMONSTRATION","kind":"display","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_QGOH_PP_3:DISPLAY-0047"]},{"id":"RAW_QGOH_PP_3:DISPLAY-0048","text":"Whatever exists expresses God's nature or essence in a certain and determinate manner (by the Corollary to Prop. 25); that is (by Prop. 34), whatever exists expresses, in a certain and determinate manner, God's power, which is the cause of all things; and therefore (by Prop. 16) some effect must follow from it. Q. E. D.","kind":"display","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_QGOH_PP_3:DISPLAY-0048"]},{"id":"RAW_QGOH_PP_3:DISPLAY-0049","text":"APPENDIX","kind":"display","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_QGOH_PP_3:DISPLAY-0049"]},{"id":"SOURCE_TR_20261015T012710768164Z_8ff36f61:DISPLAY-0059","text":"In these matters I have explained God's nature and his properties: namely, that he necessarily exists; that he is unique; that he is and acts from the necessity of his nature alone; that he is the free cause of all things, and how; that all things are in God and depend upon him in such a way that without him they can neither be nor be conceived; and finally, that all things have been predetermined by God, not indeed from freedom of will, or absolute good pleasure, but from God's absolute nature, or infinite power. Moreover, wherever occasion was given, I have taken care to remove prejudices that might hinder my demonstrations from being perceived; but since not a few prejudices still remain which could, indeed most especially could and can, prevent men from being able to embrace the concatenation of things in the manner in which I have explained it, I thought it worthwhile here to summon them to the examination of reason.","kind":"display","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_QGOH_PP_3:DISPLAY-0050"]},{"id":"SOURCE_TR_20261015T012710768164Z_8ff36f61:DISPLAY-0060","text":"And since all the prejudices which I undertake to point out here depend upon this one thing, namely, that men commonly suppose that all natural things, like themselves, act for an end, indeed, they hold it as certain that God himself directs all things toward some certain end: for they say that God made all things for the sake of man, and man in order that he might worship him. I shall therefore first consider this one point, inquiring first, namely, into the cause why most people acquiesce in this prejudice, and why all are by nature so inclined to embrace the same. I shall then show its falsity, and finally how from this have arisen prejudices concerning good and evil, merit and sin, praise and blame, order and confusion, beauty and deformity, and other things of this kind. But to deduce these from the nature of the human mind is not the business of this place.","kind":"display","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_QGOH_PP_3:DISPLAY-0050"]},{"id":"SOURCE_TR_20261015T012710768164Z_8ff36f61:DISPLAY-0061","text":"Here it will suffice if I take as a foundation what ought to be admitted by all: namely, that all men are born ignorant of the causes of things, and that all have an appetite for seeking their own advantage, of which they are conscious. For from these it follows, first, that men think themselves free, since they are conscious of their volitions and their appetite, and do not even dream, because they are ignorant of them, of the causes by which they are disposed to desiring and willing. Secondly, it follows that men act in all things for an end; namely, for the advantage that they desire. Hence it comes about that they always seek only to know the final causes of things done, and when they have heard them, they rest content, since they have no cause for further doubt.","kind":"display","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_QGOH_PP_3:DISPLAY-0050"]},{"id":"SOURCE_TR_20261015T012710768164Z_8ff36f61:DISPLAY-0062","text":"But if they cannot hear those same causes from another, nothing remains for them except to turn back upon themselves and reflect upon the ends by which they themselves are usually determined to similar things, and thus they necessarily judge another's disposition from their own. Moreover, since they find in themselves and outside themselves not a few means which contribute not a little to attaining their own advantage—for example, eyes for seeing, teeth for chewing, plants and animals for food, the sun for giving light, the sea for nourishing fish, etc.—hence it has come about that they regard all natural things as means to their own advantage. And because they know that those means were found by them, not prepared by them, they had cause to believe that there was some other being who had prepared those means for their use.","kind":"display","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_QGOH_PP_3:DISPLAY-0050"]},{"id":"SOURCE_TR_20261015T012710768164Z_8ff36f61:DISPLAY-0063","text":"For after they had regarded things as means, they could not believe that those things had made themselves; but from the means which they themselves are accustomed to prepare, they had to conclude that there are one or more rulers of Nature, endowed with human freedom, who have taken care of all things for them and made all things for their use. And since they had never heard anything concerning the disposition of these rulers, they also had to judge it from their own; and hence they maintained that the gods direct all things to the use of men, in order to bind men to themselves and be held by them in the highest honor. Thus it came about that each person devised, from his own disposition, different modes of worshipping God, so that God might love them above the rest and direct the whole of Nature to the service of their blind desire and insatiable avarice.","kind":"display","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_QGOH_PP_3:DISPLAY-0050"]},{"id":"SOURCE_TR_20261015T012710768164Z_8ff36f61:DISPLAY-0064","text":"And so this prejudice turned into superstition and struck deep roots in men's minds; which was the reason why each person strove with the greatest effort to understand and explain the final causes of all things. But while they sought to show that Nature does nothing in vain (that is, nothing that is not for men's use), they seem to have shown nothing other than that Nature and the gods are as delirious as men. See, I pray, to what the matter has finally come!","kind":"display","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_QGOH_PP_3:DISPLAY-0050"]},{"id":"SOURCE_TR_20261015T012710768164Z_8ff36f61:DISPLAY-0065","text":"Amid so many advantages of Nature they had to find not a few disadvantages—namely, storms, earthquakes, diseases, etc.—and they maintained that these occur because the gods were angry over wrongs done them by men, or sins committed in their worship. And although experience cried out against this daily and showed by infinitely many examples that advantages and disadvantages befall the pious and impious alike indiscriminately, they did not therefore abandon their inveterate prejudice: for it was easier for them to place this among the other unknown things whose use they did not know, and thus retain their present and inborn state of ignorance, than to destroy that entire structure and devise a new one.","kind":"display","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_QGOH_PP_3:DISPLAY-0050"]},{"id":"SOURCE_TR_20261015T012710768164Z_8ff36f61:DISPLAY-0066","text":"Hence they held as certain that the judgments of the gods far surpass human comprehension. This indeed would have been the sole cause why truth should lie hidden from the human race forever, had not Mathematics, which is concerned not with ends but only with the essences and properties of figures, shown men another standard of truth; and besides Mathematics, other causes also can be assigned (which it is superfluous to enumerate here), by which it could come about that men might notice these common prejudices and be led to a true knowledge of things.","kind":"display","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_QGOH_PP_3:DISPLAY-0050"]}],"source_digest":"7d8f7a5866439a145a2d6c9f4d774d3b498e5a7a19f0366c626b4778c3d17f2e","source_language_code":"la","original_title":"Pars prima - De Deo · passage 3"}],"original_title":"Pars prima - De Deo · passage 3","title_language_code":"en"},{"id":"RAW_QGOH_PP_4","title":"Part One - Concerning God · passage 4","order":4,"chapter_id":"CH_QGOH_PP_4","text":"His satis explicui id, quod primo loco promisi. Ut jam autem ostendam, Naturam finem nullum sibi præfixum habere, et omnes causas finales nihil nisi humana esse figmenta, non opus est multis. Credo enim id jam satis constare, tam ex fundamentis et causis, unde hoc præjudicium originem suam traxisse ostendi, quam ex Propositione 16 et Corollariis Propositionis 32, et præterea ex iis omnibus, quibus ostendi, omnia naturæ æterna quadam necessitate, summaque perfectione procedere. Hoc tamen adhuc addam, nempe, hanc de fine doctrinam Naturam omnino evertere. Nam id, quod revera causa est, ut effectum considerat, et contra. Deinde id, quod natura prius est, facit posterius. Et denique id, quod supremum et perfectissimum est, reddit imperfectissimum. Nam (duobus prioribus omissis, quia per se manifesta sunt), ut ex Propositionibus 21, 22 et 23 constat, ille effectus perfectissimus est, qui a Deo immediate producitur, et quo aliquid pluribus causis intermediis indiget, ut producatur, eo imperfectius est. At si res, quæ immediate a Deo productæ sunt, ea de causa factæ essent, ut Deus finem assequeretur suum, tum necessario ultimæ, quarum de causa priores factæ sunt, omnium præstantissimæ essent. Deinde hæc doctrina Dei perfectionem tollit : Nam, si Deus propter finem agit aliquid necessario appetit, quo caret. Et quamvis Theologi et Metaphysici distinguant inter finem indigentiæ et finem assimilationis, fatentur tamen, Deum omnia propter se, non vero propter res creandas egisse ; quia nihil ante creationem præter Deum assignare possunt, propter quod Deus ageret ; adeoque necessario fateri coguntur, Deum iis, propter quæ media parare voluit, caruisse, eaque cupivisse, ut per se clarum. Nec hic prætereundum est, quod hujus doctrinæ sectatores, qui in assignandis rerum finibus suum ingenium ostentare voluerunt, ad hanc suam doctrinam probandam novum attulerunt modum argumentandi, reducendo scilicet, non ad impossible, sed ad ignorantiam ; quod ostendit nullum aliud fuisse huic doctrinæ argumentandi medium. Nam si ex. gr. ex culmine aliquo lapis in alicujus caput ceciderit, eumque interfecerit, hoc modo demonstrabunt lapidem ad hominem interficiendum cecidisse. Ni enim eum in finem, Deo id volonte, ceciderit, quomodo tot circumstantiæ (sæpe enim multæ simul concurrunt) casu concurrere potuerunt ? Respondebis fortasse, id ex eo, quod ventus flavit, et quod homo illac iter habebat, evenisse. At instabunt, cur ventus illo tempore flavit ? cur homo illo eodemque tempore illac iter habebat ? Si iterum respondeas, ventum tum ortum, quia mare præcedenti die, tempore adhuc tranquillo, agitari inceperat ; et quod homo ab amico invitatus fuerat ; instabunt iterum, quia nullus rogandi finis, cur autem mare agitabatur ? cur homo in illud tempus invitatus fuit? et sic porro causarum causas rogare non cessabunt, donec ad Dei voluntatem, hoc est ignorantiæ asylum, confugeris. Sic etiam, ubi corporis humani fabricam vident, stupescunt, et ex eo, quod tantæ artis causas ignorant, concludunt, eandem non mechanica, sed divina vel supernaturali arte fabricari, talique modo constitui, ut una pars alteram non lædat. Atque hinc fit, ut qui miraculorum causas veras quærit, quique res naturales ut doctus intelligere, non autem ut stultus admirari studet, passim pro hæretico et impio habeatur et proclametur ab iis, quos vulgus tanquam Naturæ Deorumque interpretes adorat. Nam sciunt quod, sublata ignorantia, stupor, hoc est unicum argumentandi tuendæque suæ auctoritatis medium, quod habent, tollitur. Sed hæc relinquo, et ad id, quod tertio loco hic agere constitui, pergo. Postquam homines sibi persuaserunt, omnia, quæ fiunt, propter ipsos fieri ; id in unaquaque re præcipuum judicare debuerunt, quod ipsis utilissimum, et illa omnia præstantissima æstimare, a quibus optime afficiebantur. Unde has formare debuerunt notiones, quibus rerum naturas explicarent, scilicet Bonum, Malum, Ordinem, Confusionem, Calidum, Frigidum, Pulchritudinem et Deformitatem : et quia se liberos existimant, inde hæ notiones ortæ sunt, scilicet Laus et Vituperium, Peccatum et Meritum ; sed has infra, postquam de natura humana egero, illas autem hic breviter explicabo. Nempe id omne, quod ad valetudinem et Dei cultum conducit, Bonum, quod autem iis contrarium est, Malum vocaverunt. Et quia ii, qui rerum naturam non intelligunt, nihil de rebus affirmant, sed res tantummodo imaginantur, et imaginationem pro intellectu capiunt, ideo Ordinem in rébus esse firmiter credunt, rerum suæque naturæ ignari. Nam cum ita sint dispositæ, ut, cum nobis per sensus repræsentantur, eas facile imaginari, et consequenter earum facile recordari possimus, easdem bene ordinatas ; si vero contra, ipsas male ordinatas, sive confusas esse dicimus. Et quoniam ea nobis præ cæteris grata sunt, quæ facile imaginari possumus, ideo homines ordinem confusioni præferunt, quasi ordo aliquid in Natura præter respectum ad nostram imaginationem esset ; dicuntque Deum omnia ordine creasse, et hoc modo ipsi nescientes Deo imaginationem tribuunt ; nisi velint forte, Deum, humanæ imaginationi providentem, res omnes eo disposuisse modo, quo ipsas facillime imaginari possent ; nec moram forsan iis injiciet, quod infinita reperiantur, quæ nostram imaginationem longe superant, et plurima, quæ ipsam, propter ejus imbecillitatem, confundunt. Sed de hac re satis. Cæteræ deinde notiones etiam præter imaginandi modos, quibus imaginatio diversimode afficitur, nihil sunt, et tamen ab ignaris tanquam præcipua rerum attributa considerantur ; quia, ut jam diximus, res omnes propter ipsos factas esse credunt ; et rei alicujus naturam bonam vel malam, sanam vel putridam et corruptam dicunt, prout ab eadem afficiuntur. Ex gr. si motus, quem nervi ab objectis, per oculos repræsentatis, accipiunt, valetudini conducat, objecta, a quibus causatur, pulchra dicuntur, quæ autem contrarium motum cient, deformia. Quæ deinde per nares sensum movent, odorifera vel fœtida vocant, quæ per linguam, dulcia aut amara, sapida aut insipida, etc. Quæ autem per tactum, dura aut mollia, aspera aut lævia, etc. Et quæ denique aures movent, strepitum, sonum, vel harmoniam edere dicuntur, quorum postremum homines adeo dementavit, ut Deum etiam harmonia delectari crederent. Nec desunt Philosophi, qui sibi persuaserint, motus cœlestes harmoniam componere. Quæ omnia satis ostendunt, unumquemque pro dispositione cerebri de rebus judicasse vel potius imaginationis affectiones pro rebus accepisse. Quare non mirum est (ut hoc etiam obiter notemus), quod inter homines tot, quot experimur, controversiæ ortæ sint, ex quibus tandem Scepticismus. Nam, quamvis humana corpora in multis conveniant, in plurimis tamen discrepant, et ideo id, quod uni bonum, alteri malum videtur ; quod uni ordinatum, alteri confusum ; quod uni gratum, alteri ingratum est et sic de cæteris, quibus hic supersedeo cum quia hujus loci non est de his ex professo agere, tum quia hoc omnes satis experti sunt. Omnibus enim in ore est, Quot capita, tot sensus ; Suo quemque sensu abundare ; Non minora cerebrorum quam palatorum esse discrimina : quæ sententiæ satis ostendunt, homines pro dispositione cerebri de rebus judicare, resque potius imaginari quam intelligere. Res enim si intellexissent, illæ omnes teste Mathesi, si non allicerent, ad minimum convincerent.\n\nVidemus itaque omnes notiones, quibus vulgus solet Naturam explicare, modos esse tantummodo imaginandi, nec ullius rei naturam, sed tantum imaginationis constitutionem indicare ; et quia nomina habent, quasi essent entium imaginationem existentium, eadem entia, non rationis, sed imaginationis voco ; atque adeo omnia argumenta, quæ contra nos ex similibus notionibus petuntur, facile propulsari possunt. Solent enim multi sic argumentari. Si omnia ex necessitate perfectissimæ Dei naturæ sunt consecuta, unde ergo tot imperfectiones in Natura ? Videlicet rerum corruptio ad fœtorem, usque, rerum deformitas quæ nauseam moveat, confusio, malum, peccatum, etc. Sed, ut modo dixi, facile confutantur. Nam rerum perfectio ex sola earum natura et potentia est æstimanda, nec ideo res magis aut minus perfectæ sunt, propterea quod hominum sensum delectant vel offendunt, quod humanæ naturæ conducunt vel quod eidem repugnant. Iis autem, qui quærunt, cur Deus omnes homines non ita creavit, ut solo rationis ductu gubernarentur ? nihil aliud respondeo, quam quia ei non defuit materia ad omnia, ex summo nimirum ad infimum perfectionis gradum, creanda ; vel magis proprie loquendo, quia ipsius Naturæ leges adeo amplæ fuerunt, ut sufficerent ad omnia, quæ ab aliquo infinito intellectu concipi possunt, producenda, ut Propositione 16 demonstravi. Hæc sunt, quæ hic notare suscepi, præjudicia. Si quædam hujus farinæ adhuc restant, poterunt eadem ab unoquoque mediocri meditatione emendari.\n\nFINIS PARTIS PRIMÆ","paragraphs":[{"id":"RAW_QGOH_PP_4:P0001","text":"His satis explicui id, quod primo loco promisi. Ut jam autem ostendam, Naturam finem nullum sibi præfixum habere, et omnes causas finales nihil nisi humana esse figmenta, non opus est multis. Credo enim id jam satis constare, tam ex fundamentis et causis, unde hoc præjudicium originem suam traxisse ostendi, quam ex Propositione 16 et Corollariis Propositionis 32, et præterea ex iis omnibus, quibus ostendi, omnia naturæ æterna quadam necessitate, summaque perfectione procedere. Hoc tamen adhuc addam, nempe, hanc de fine doctrinam Naturam omnino evertere. Nam id, quod revera causa est, ut effectum considerat, et contra. Deinde id, quod natura prius est, facit posterius. Et denique id, quod supremum et perfectissimum est, reddit imperfectissimum.","kind":"passage"},{"id":"RAW_QGOH_PP_4:P0002","text":"Nam (duobus prioribus omissis, quia per se manifesta sunt), ut ex Propositionibus 21, 22 et 23 constat, ille effectus perfectissimus est, qui a Deo immediate producitur, et quo aliquid pluribus causis intermediis indiget, ut producatur, eo imperfectius est. At si res, quæ immediate a Deo productæ sunt, ea de causa factæ essent, ut Deus finem assequeretur suum, tum necessario ultimæ, quarum de causa priores factæ sunt, omnium præstantissimæ essent. Deinde hæc doctrina Dei perfectionem tollit : Nam, si Deus propter finem agit aliquid necessario appetit, quo caret.","kind":"passage"},{"id":"RAW_QGOH_PP_4:P0003","text":"Et quamvis Theologi et Metaphysici distinguant inter finem indigentiæ et finem assimilationis, fatentur tamen, Deum omnia propter se, non vero propter res creandas egisse ; quia nihil ante creationem præter Deum assignare possunt, propter quod Deus ageret ; adeoque necessario fateri coguntur, Deum iis, propter quæ media parare voluit, caruisse, eaque cupivisse, ut per se clarum. Nec hic prætereundum est, quod hujus doctrinæ sectatores, qui in assignandis rerum finibus suum ingenium ostentare voluerunt, ad hanc suam doctrinam probandam novum attulerunt modum argumentandi, reducendo scilicet, non ad impossible, sed ad ignorantiam ; quod ostendit nullum aliud fuisse huic doctrinæ argumentandi medium. Nam si ex. gr. ex culmine aliquo lapis in alicujus caput ceciderit, eumque interfecerit, hoc modo demonstrabunt lapidem ad hominem interficiendum cecidisse.","kind":"passage"},{"id":"RAW_QGOH_PP_4:P0004","text":"Ni enim eum in finem, Deo id volonte, ceciderit, quomodo tot circumstantiæ (sæpe enim multæ simul concurrunt) casu concurrere potuerunt ? Respondebis fortasse, id ex eo, quod ventus flavit, et quod homo illac iter habebat, evenisse. At instabunt, cur ventus illo tempore flavit ? cur homo illo eodemque tempore illac iter habebat ? Si iterum respondeas, ventum tum ortum, quia mare præcedenti die, tempore adhuc tranquillo, agitari inceperat ; et quod homo ab amico invitatus fuerat ; instabunt iterum, quia nullus rogandi finis, cur autem mare agitabatur ? cur homo in illud tempus invitatus fuit? et sic porro causarum causas rogare non cessabunt, donec ad Dei voluntatem, hoc est ignorantiæ asylum, confugeris.","kind":"passage"},{"id":"RAW_QGOH_PP_4:P0005","text":"Sic etiam, ubi corporis humani fabricam vident, stupescunt, et ex eo, quod tantæ artis causas ignorant, concludunt, eandem non mechanica, sed divina vel supernaturali arte fabricari, talique modo constitui, ut una pars alteram non lædat. Atque hinc fit, ut qui miraculorum causas veras quærit, quique res naturales ut doctus intelligere, non autem ut stultus admirari studet, passim pro hæretico et impio habeatur et proclametur ab iis, quos vulgus tanquam Naturæ Deorumque interpretes adorat. Nam sciunt quod, sublata ignorantia, stupor, hoc est unicum argumentandi tuendæque suæ auctoritatis medium, quod habent, tollitur. Sed hæc relinquo, et ad id, quod tertio loco hic agere constitui, pergo.","kind":"passage"},{"id":"RAW_QGOH_PP_4:P0006","text":"Postquam homines sibi persuaserunt, omnia, quæ fiunt, propter ipsos fieri ; id in unaquaque re præcipuum judicare debuerunt, quod ipsis utilissimum, et illa omnia præstantissima æstimare, a quibus optime afficiebantur. Unde has formare debuerunt notiones, quibus rerum naturas explicarent, scilicet Bonum, Malum, Ordinem, Confusionem, Calidum, Frigidum, Pulchritudinem et Deformitatem : et quia se liberos existimant, inde hæ notiones ortæ sunt, scilicet Laus et Vituperium, Peccatum et Meritum ; sed has infra, postquam de natura humana egero, illas autem hic breviter explicabo. Nempe id omne, quod ad valetudinem et Dei cultum conducit, Bonum, quod autem iis contrarium est, Malum vocaverunt.","kind":"passage"},{"id":"RAW_QGOH_PP_4:P0007","text":"Et quia ii, qui rerum naturam non intelligunt, nihil de rebus affirmant, sed res tantummodo imaginantur, et imaginationem pro intellectu capiunt, ideo Ordinem in rébus esse firmiter credunt, rerum suæque naturæ ignari. Nam cum ita sint dispositæ, ut, cum nobis per sensus repræsentantur, eas facile imaginari, et consequenter earum facile recordari possimus, easdem bene ordinatas ; si vero contra, ipsas male ordinatas, sive confusas esse dicimus.","kind":"passage"},{"id":"RAW_QGOH_PP_4:P0008","text":"Et quoniam ea nobis præ cæteris grata sunt, quæ facile imaginari possumus, ideo homines ordinem confusioni præferunt, quasi ordo aliquid in Natura præter respectum ad nostram imaginationem esset ; dicuntque Deum omnia ordine creasse, et hoc modo ipsi nescientes Deo imaginationem tribuunt ; nisi velint forte, Deum, humanæ imaginationi providentem, res omnes eo disposuisse modo, quo ipsas facillime imaginari possent ; nec moram forsan iis injiciet, quod infinita reperiantur, quæ nostram imaginationem longe superant, et plurima, quæ ipsam, propter ejus imbecillitatem, confundunt. Sed de hac re satis.","kind":"passage"},{"id":"RAW_QGOH_PP_4:P0009","text":"Cæteræ deinde notiones etiam præter imaginandi modos, quibus imaginatio diversimode afficitur, nihil sunt, et tamen ab ignaris tanquam præcipua rerum attributa considerantur ; quia, ut jam diximus, res omnes propter ipsos factas esse credunt ; et rei alicujus naturam bonam vel malam, sanam vel putridam et corruptam dicunt, prout ab eadem afficiuntur. Ex gr. si motus, quem nervi ab objectis, per oculos repræsentatis, accipiunt, valetudini conducat, objecta, a quibus causatur, pulchra dicuntur, quæ autem contrarium motum cient, deformia. Quæ deinde per nares sensum movent, odorifera vel fœtida vocant, quæ per linguam, dulcia aut amara, sapida aut insipida, etc. Quæ autem per tactum, dura aut mollia, aspera aut lævia, etc. Et quæ denique aures movent, strepitum, sonum, vel harmoniam edere dicuntur, quorum postremum homines adeo dementavit, ut Deum etiam harmonia delectari crederent.","kind":"passage"},{"id":"RAW_QGOH_PP_4:P0010","text":"Nec desunt Philosophi, qui sibi persuaserint, motus cœlestes harmoniam componere. Quæ omnia satis ostendunt, unumquemque pro dispositione cerebri de rebus judicasse vel potius imaginationis affectiones pro rebus accepisse. Quare non mirum est (ut hoc etiam obiter notemus), quod inter homines tot, quot experimur, controversiæ ortæ sint, ex quibus tandem Scepticismus. Nam, quamvis humana corpora in multis conveniant, in plurimis tamen discrepant, et ideo id, quod uni bonum, alteri malum videtur ; quod uni ordinatum, alteri confusum ; quod uni gratum, alteri ingratum est et sic de cæteris, quibus hic supersedeo cum quia hujus loci non est de his ex professo agere, tum quia hoc omnes satis experti sunt.","kind":"passage"},{"id":"RAW_QGOH_PP_4:P0011","text":"Omnibus enim in ore est, Quot capita, tot sensus ; Suo quemque sensu abundare ; Non minora cerebrorum quam palatorum esse discrimina : quæ sententiæ satis ostendunt, homines pro dispositione cerebri de rebus judicare, resque potius imaginari quam intelligere. Res enim si intellexissent, illæ omnes teste Mathesi, si non allicerent, ad minimum convincerent.","kind":"passage"},{"id":"RAW_QGOH_PP_4:P0012","text":"Videmus itaque omnes notiones, quibus vulgus solet Naturam explicare, modos esse tantummodo imaginandi, nec ullius rei naturam, sed tantum imaginationis constitutionem indicare ; et quia nomina habent, quasi essent entium imaginationem existentium, eadem entia, non rationis, sed imaginationis voco ; atque adeo omnia argumenta, quæ contra nos ex similibus notionibus petuntur, facile propulsari possunt. Solent enim multi sic argumentari. Si omnia ex necessitate perfectissimæ Dei naturæ sunt consecuta, unde ergo tot imperfectiones in Natura ? Videlicet rerum corruptio ad fœtorem, usque, rerum deformitas quæ nauseam moveat, confusio, malum, peccatum, etc. Sed, ut modo dixi, facile confutantur.","kind":"passage"},{"id":"RAW_QGOH_PP_4:P0013","text":"Nam rerum perfectio ex sola earum natura et potentia est æstimanda, nec ideo res magis aut minus perfectæ sunt, propterea quod hominum sensum delectant vel offendunt, quod humanæ naturæ conducunt vel quod eidem repugnant. Iis autem, qui quærunt, cur Deus omnes homines non ita creavit, ut solo rationis ductu gubernarentur ? nihil aliud respondeo, quam quia ei non defuit materia ad omnia, ex summo nimirum ad infimum perfectionis gradum, creanda ; vel magis proprie loquendo, quia ipsius Naturæ leges adeo amplæ fuerunt, ut sufficerent ad omnia, quæ ab aliquo infinito intellectu concipi possunt, producenda, ut Propositione 16 demonstravi. Hæc sunt, quæ hic notare suscepi, præjudicia. Si quædam hujus farinæ adhuc restant, poterunt eadem ab unoquoque mediocri meditatione emendari.","kind":"passage"},{"id":"RAW_QGOH_PP_4:P0014","text":"FINIS PARTIS PRIMÆ","kind":"passage"}],"sha256":"063a6117ac82a02f6f404ff181dd665dfa982a4701c49726df323a4d23ba0def","language_code":"la","language_name":"Latina","translations":[{"id":"SOURCE_TR_20261015T012731366939Z_4da5cbc3","language_code":"en","language_name":"English","title":"Part One - Concerning God · passage 4","source_sha256":"063a6117ac82a02f6f404ff181dd665dfa982a4701c49726df323a4d23ba0def","sha256":"baa57588c5aceaa9f47dc9d5c6ccfb6774565df965d58e0f1f609ac6c3ed8be1","rendition_type":"machine","review_status":"machine","model":"gpt-5.6-terra","created":"2026-10-15T01:27:31.367082+00:00","paragraphs":[{"id":"RAW_QGOH_PP_4:P0001","text":"With these things I have sufficiently explained what I promised in the first place. But in order now to show that Nature has set before itself no end, and that all final causes are nothing but human fictions, there is no need of much. For I believe that this is already sufficiently established, both from the foundations and causes from which I showed that this prejudice derived its origin, and from Proposition 16 and the Corollaries of Proposition 32, and moreover from all those things by which I showed that all things in Nature proceed by a certain eternal necessity and with the highest perfection. Yet I shall add this further, namely, that this doctrine concerning an end utterly overturns Nature. For it regards as an effect that which is in reality a cause, and conversely. Next, it makes that which is prior by nature posterior. And finally, it renders that which is highest and most perfect most imperfect.","kind":"passage","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_QGOH_PP_4:P0001"]},{"id":"RAW_QGOH_PP_4:P0002","text":"For (omitting the first two, because they are self-evident), as is clear from Propositions 21, 22, and 23, that effect is most perfect which is produced immediately by God; and the more intermediate causes something requires in order to be produced, the more imperfect it is. But if the things which were produced immediately by God had been made for the sake of attaining God's end, then necessarily the last things, for the sake of which the earlier things were made, would be the most excellent of all. Next, this doctrine takes away God's perfection: for, if God acts for the sake of an end, He necessarily desires something which He lacks.","kind":"passage","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_QGOH_PP_4:P0002"]},{"id":"RAW_QGOH_PP_4:P0003","text":"And although Theologians and Metaphysicians distinguish between an end of need and an end of assimilation, they nevertheless admit that God has done all things for His own sake, and not for the sake of things to be created; for before creation they can assign nothing besides God for the sake of which God might act; and therefore they are necessarily compelled to confess that God lacked those things for which He wished to prepare means, and desired them, as is clear of itself. Nor should it here be passed over that the adherents of this doctrine, who wished to display their ingenuity in assigning the ends of things, have introduced, to prove this doctrine of theirs, a new manner of arguing: namely, reducing not to the impossible, but to ignorance; which shows that there was no other means of arguing for this doctrine. For if, for example, a stone falls from some height upon someone's head and kills him, they will demonstrate in this way that the stone fell in order to kill the man.","kind":"passage","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_QGOH_PP_4:P0003"]},{"id":"RAW_QGOH_PP_4:P0004","text":"For unless it fell to that end, God so willing it, how could so many circumstances (for often many concur at once) have concurred by chance? Perhaps you will answer that this happened because the wind was blowing and because the man was making his way there. But they will press on: why was the wind blowing at that time? Why was the man making his way there at that same time? If you answer again that the wind then arose because the sea had begun to be agitated on the preceding day, when the weather was still calm, and that the man had been invited by a friend, they will press on again, since there is no end to asking: but why was the sea agitated? Why was the man invited for that time? And so they will not cease asking for causes of causes until you take refuge in the will of God, that is, the asylum of ignorance.","kind":"passage","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_QGOH_PP_4:P0004"]},{"id":"RAW_QGOH_PP_4:P0005","text":"Likewise, when they see the structure of the human body, they are astonished, and from the fact that they are ignorant of the causes of so great an art, they conclude that it is fashioned not by mechanical art but by divine or supernatural art, and so constituted that one part does not injure another. And hence it comes about that one who seeks the true causes of miracles, and who endeavors to understand natural things as a learned person, rather than to admire them as a fool, is everywhere regarded and proclaimed as a heretic and impious man by those whom the common people worship as interpreters of Nature and of the Gods. For they know that, once ignorance is removed, astonishment—that is, the sole means they possess of arguing and maintaining their authority—is removed. But I leave these matters and proceed to what I resolved to treat here in the third place.","kind":"passage","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_QGOH_PP_4:P0005"]},{"id":"RAW_QGOH_PP_4:P0006","text":"After men had persuaded themselves that all things which happen happen for their sake, they had to judge as chief in each thing that which was most useful to themselves, and to esteem most excellent all those things by which they were most beneficially affected. Hence they had to form these notions by which to explain the natures of things: namely, Good, Evil, Order, Confusion, Hot, Cold, Beauty, and Ugliness. And because they consider themselves free, there arose from this the notions Praise and Blame, Sin and Merit; but I shall treat of these below, after I have dealt with human nature, whereas I shall briefly explain the former here. Namely, they called Good everything that conduces to health and to the worship of God, and Evil that which is contrary to these.","kind":"passage","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_QGOH_PP_4:P0006"]},{"id":"RAW_QGOH_PP_4:P0007","text":"And because those who do not understand the nature of things affirm nothing about things, but merely imagine things and take imagination for intellect, they therefore firmly believe that there is Order in things, being ignorant of things and of their own nature. For when things are so disposed that, when they are represented to us through the senses, we can easily imagine them and consequently easily remember them, we call them well ordered; but if the contrary, badly ordered, or confused.","kind":"passage","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_QGOH_PP_4:P0007"]},{"id":"RAW_QGOH_PP_4:P0008","text":"And because those things are more pleasing to us than others which we can easily imagine, men therefore prefer order to confusion, as though order were something in Nature apart from a relation to our imagination; and they say that God created all things in order, and thus, unbeknownst to themselves, attribute imagination to God—unless perhaps they wish that God, providing for human imagination, had disposed all things in such a way that men could most easily imagine them. Nor will it perhaps give them pause that infinitely many things are found which far surpass our imagination, and very many which confuse it because of its weakness. But enough on this matter.","kind":"passage","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_QGOH_PP_4:P0008"]},{"id":"RAW_QGOH_PP_4:P0009","text":"The remaining notions, then, are likewise nothing but modes of imagining by which the imagination is variously affected, and yet they are regarded by the ignorant as chief attributes of things, because, as we have already said, they believe that all things have been made for their sake; and they call the nature of some thing good or bad, sound or putrid and corrupt, according as they are affected by it. For example, if the motion which the nerves receive from objects represented through the eyes conduces to health, the objects which cause it are called beautiful; but those which excite the contrary motion, ugly. Those which move sensation through the nostrils they call fragrant or fetid; those through the tongue, sweet or bitter, savory or insipid, etc. Those through touch, hard or soft, rough or smooth, etc. And finally, those which move the ears are said to produce noise, sound, or harmony; the last of which has so maddened men that they have believed even God to delight in harmony.","kind":"passage","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_QGOH_PP_4:P0009"]},{"id":"RAW_QGOH_PP_4:P0010","text":"Nor are there lacking Philosophers who have persuaded themselves that the celestial motions compose harmony. All these things sufficiently show that everyone has judged things according to the disposition of his brain, or rather has taken affections of the imagination for things. Hence it is not surprising (to note this also in passing) that among men there have arisen as many controversies as we experience, from which Skepticism has finally arisen. For although human bodies agree in many respects, they nevertheless differ in very many; and therefore what seems good to one seems evil to another; what seems ordered to one, confused to another; what is pleasing to one, displeasing to another; and so with the rest, which I pass over here, both because it is not the business of this place to treat of these matters expressly, and because everyone has sufficiently experienced this.","kind":"passage","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_QGOH_PP_4:P0010"]},{"id":"RAW_QGOH_PP_4:P0011","text":"For everyone has on his lips: As many heads, so many opinions; Each abounds in his own judgment; the differences of brains are no less than those of palates. These sayings sufficiently show that men judge things according to the disposition of their brain, and imagine things rather than understand them. For if they understood things, then, as Mathematics bears witness, all things would, if they did not attract them, at least convince them.","kind":"passage","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_QGOH_PP_4:P0011"]},{"id":"RAW_QGOH_PP_4:P0012","text":"We see, therefore, that all the notions by which the common people are accustomed to explain Nature are merely modes of imagining, and indicate the constitution not of any thing but only of the imagination; and because they have names as if they were beings existing outside the imagination, I call them beings not of reason but of imagination; and accordingly all arguments brought against us from similar notions can easily be repelled. For many are accustomed to argue thus: If all things have followed from the necessity of God's most perfect nature, whence then are there so many imperfections in Nature? Namely, the corruption of things even to foulness, the ugliness of things that provokes nausea, confusion, evil, sin, etc. But, as I have just said, they are easily refuted.","kind":"passage","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_QGOH_PP_4:P0012"]},{"id":"RAW_QGOH_PP_4:P0013","text":"For the perfection of things is to be estimated from their nature and power alone; nor are things therefore more or less perfect because they delight or offend human sense, because they conduce to human nature or are repugnant to it. To those who ask why God did not create all men so that they might be governed by the guidance of reason alone, I reply nothing other than that He did not lack material for creating all things, from the highest to the lowest degree of perfection; or, to speak more properly, that the laws of Nature itself were ample enough to suffice for producing everything that can be conceived by some infinite intellect, as I demonstrated in Proposition 16. These are the prejudices that I undertook to note here. If any of this sort still remain, they can be corrected by anyone with moderate reflection.","kind":"passage","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_QGOH_PP_4:P0013"]},{"id":"RAW_QGOH_PP_4:P0014","text":"END OF PART ONE","kind":"passage","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_QGOH_PP_4:P0014"]}],"source_digest":"5138d3f3ae0fed8cb84650c36fe69fdf54511fec9b7717c47b2f06a938df6b25","source_language_code":"la","original_title":"Pars prima - De Deo · passage 4"}],"passage_basis":"frozen-grounding","original_title":"Pars prima - De Deo · passage 4","title_language_code":"en"},{"id":"RAW_QGOH_PSDN","title":"Part Two - On the Nature and Origin of the Mind · passage 1","order":5,"chapter_id":"CH_QGOH_PSDN","text":"## DE NATURA ET ORIGINE MENTIS\n\nTranseo jam ad ea explicanda, quæ ex Dei, sive Entis æterni et infiniti, essentia necessario debuerunt sequi. Non quidem omnia ; infinita enim infinitis modis ex ipsa debere sequi Prop. 16 Part. 1 demonstravimus : sed ea solummodo, quæ nos ad Mentis humanæ, ejusque summæ beatitudinis, cognitionem quasi manu ducere possunt.\n\nDEFINITIONES\n\nI. Per corpus intelligo modum, qui Dei essentiam, quatenus ut res extensa consideratur, certo et determinato modo exprimit; vid. Coroll. Prop. 25 p. I.\n\nII. Ad essentiam alicujus rei id pertinere dico, quo dato res necessario ponitur, et quo sublato res necessario tollitur ; vel id, sine quo res, et vice versa quod sine re, nec esse nec concipi potest.\n\nIII. Per ideam intelligo Mentis conceptum, quem Mens format propterea quod res est cogitans.\n\nEXPLICATIO\n\nDico potius conceptum quam perceptionem, quia perceptionis nomen indicare videtur, Mentem ab objecto pati ; at conceptus actionem Mentis exprimere videtur.\n\nIV. Per ideam adæquatam intelligo ideam, quae, quatenus in se sine relatione ad objectum consideratur, omnes veræ ideæ proprietates sive denominationes intrinsecas habet.\n\nEXPLICATIO\n\nDico intrinsecas, ut illam secludam, quæ extrinseca est, nempe convenientiam ideæ cum suo ideato.\n\nV. Duratio est indefinita existendi continuatio.\n\nEXPLICATIO\n\nDico indefinitam, quia per ipsam rei existentis naturam determinari nequaquam potest, neque etiam a causa efficiente, quæ scilicet rei existentiam necessario ponit, non autem tollit.\n\nVI. Per realitatem et perfectionem idem intelligo.\n\nVII. Per res singulares intelligo res, quæ finitæ sunt, et determinatam habent existentiam. Quod si plura individua in una actione ita concurrant, ut omnia simul unius effectus sint causa, eadem omnia eatenus ut unam rem singularem considero.\n\nAXIOMATA\n\nI. Hominis essentia non involvit necessariam existentiam, hoc est, ex Naturæ ordine tam fieri potest, ut hic et ille homo existat, quam ut non existat.\n\nII. Homo cogitat.\n\nIII. Modi cogitandi, ut amor, cupiditas, vel quicunque nomine affectus animi insigniuntur, non dantur, nisi in eodem Individuo detur idea rei amatæ, desideratæ, etc. At idea dari potest, quamvis nullus alius detur cogitandi modus.\n\nIV. Nos corpus quoddam multis modis affici sentimus.\n\nV. Nullas res singulares, præter corpora et cogitandi modos, sentimus nec percipimus.\n\nPostulata vide post 13 Propositionem.\n\nPROPOSITIO I\n\nCogitatio attributum Dei est, sive Deus est res cogitans.\n\nDEMONSTRATIO\n\nSingulares cogitationes, sive hæc et illa cogitatio, modi sunt, qui Dei naturam certo et determinato modo exprimunt (per Coroll. Prop. 25, p. I). Competit ergo Deo (per Defin. 5 p. I) attributum, cujus conceptum singulares omnes cogitationes involvunt, per quod etiam concipiuntur. Est igitur Cogitatio unum ex infinitis Dei attributis, quod Dei æternam et infinitam essentiam exprimit (vid. Defin. 6 p. I), sive Deus est res cogitans. Q. E. D.\n\nSCHOLIUM\n\nPatet etiam hæc Propositio ex hoc, quod nos possumus ens cogitans infinitum concipere. Nam quo plura ens cogitans potest cogitare, eo plus realitatis sive perfectionis idem continere concipimus ; ergo ens, quod infinita infinitis modis cogitare potest, est necessario virtute cogitandi infinitum. Cum itaque, ad solam cogitationem attendendo, Ens infinitum concipiamus, est necessario (per Defin. 4 et 6 p. I). Cogitatio unum ex infinitis Dei attributis, ut volebamus.\n\nPROPOSITIO II\n\nExtensio attributum Dei est, sive Deus est res extensa.\n\nDEMONSTRATIO\n\nHujus eodem modo procedit, ac demonstratio præcedentis Propositionis.\n\nPROPOSITIO III\n\nIn Deo datur necessario idea, tam ejus essentiæ, quam omnium, quæ ex ipsius essentia necessario sequuntur.\n\nDEMONSTRATIO\n\nDeum enim (per Prop. 1 hujus) infinita infinitis modis cogitare, sive (quod idem est, per Prop. 16 p. I) ideam suæ essentiæ, et omnium, quæ necessario ex ea sequuntur, formare potest. Atqui omne id, quod in Dei potestate est, necessario est (per Prop. 35 p. I) ergo datur necessario talis idea, et (per Prop. 15, p. I) non nisi in Deo. Q. E. D.\n\nSCHOLIUM\n\nVulgus per Dei potentiam intelligit Dei liberam voluntatem, et jus in omnia, quæ sunt, quæque propterea communiter ut contingentia considerantur. Deum enim potestatem omnia destruendi habere dicunt, et in nihilum redigendi. Dei porro potentiam cum potentia Regum sæpissime comparant. Sed hoc in Corollario 1 et 2 Propositionis 32 partis I refutavimus, et Propositione 16 partis I ostendimus, Deum eadem necessitate agere, qua seipsum intelligit ; hoc est, sicuti ex necessitate divinæ naturæ sequitur (sicut omnes uno ore statuunt) ut Deus seipsum intelligat, eadem etiam necessitate sequitur, ut Deus infinita infinitis modis agat. Deinde Propositione 34 partis I ostendimus, Dei potentiam nihil esse, præterquam Dei actuosam essentiam ; adeoque tam nobis impossibile est concipere, Deum non agere, quam Deum non esse. Porro si hæc ulterius persequi liberet, possem hic ostendere, potentiam illam, quam vulgus Deo affingit, non tantum humanam esse (quod ostendit Deum hominem, vel instar hominis a vulgo concipi), sed etiam impotentiam involvere. Sed nolo de eadem re toties sermonem instituere. Lectorem solummodo iterum atque iterum rogo, ut, quæ in prima parte, ex Propositione 16, usque ad finem, de hac re dicta sunt, semel atque iterum perpendat. Nam nemo ea, quæ volo, percipere recte poterit, nisi magnopere caveat, ne Dei potentiam cum humana Regum potentia vel jure confundat.\n\nPROPOSITIO IV\n\nIdea Dei, ex qua infinita infinitis modis sequuntur, unica tantum esse potest.\n\nDEMONSTRATIO\n\nIntellectus infinitus nihil, præter Dei attributa ejusque affectiones, comprehendit (per Prop. 30 p. I). Atqui Deus est unicus (per Coroll. 1 Prop. 14 p. I). Ergo idea Dei, ex qua infinita infinitis modis sequuntur, unica tantum esse potest. Q. E. D.\n\nPROPOSITIO V\n\nEsse formale idearum Deum, quatenus tantum ut res cogitans consideratur, pro causa agnoscit, et non quatenus alio attributo explicatur. Hoc est, tam Dei attributorum, quam rerum singularium ideæ, non ipsa ideata sive res perceptas pro causa efficiente agnoscunt, sed ipsum Deum, quatenus est res cogitans.\n\nDEMONSTRATIO\n\npatet quidem ex Propositione 3 hujus. Ibi enim concludebamus, Deum ideam suæ essentiæ, et omnium, quæ ex ea necessario sequuntur, formare posse ex hoc solo, nempe quod Deus est res cogitans, et non ex eo, quod sit suæ ideæ objectum. Quare esse formale idearum Deum, quatenus est res cogitans, pro causa agnoscit. Sed aliter hoc modo demonstratur. Esse formale idearum modus est cogitandi (ut per se notum), hoc est (per Coroll. Prop. 25 p. I) modus, qui Dei naturam quatenus tantum est res cogitans certo modo exprimit, adeoque (per Prop. 10 p. I) nullius alterius attributi Dei conceptum involvit, et consequenter (per Axiom. 4 p. I) nullius alterius attributi nisi cogitationis est effectus : adeoque esse formale idearum Deum, quatenus tantum ut res cogitans consideratur, etc. Q. E. D.\n\nPROPOSITIO VI\n\nCujuscunque attributi modi Deum, quatenus tantum sub illo attributo, cujus modi sunt, et non quatenus sub ullo alio consideratur, pro causa habent.\n\nDEMONSTRATIO\n\nUnumquodque enim attributum per se absque alio concipitur (per Prop. 10 p. I). Quare uniuscujusque attributi modi conceptum sui attributi, non autem alterius involvunt ; adeoque (per Axiom. 4 p. I) Deum, quatenus sub illo attributo, cujus modi sunt, et non quatenus sub ullo alio consideratur, pro causa habent. Q. E. D.\n\nCOROLLARIUM\n\nHinc sequitur, quod esse formale rerum, quæ modi non sunt cogitandi, non sequitur ideo ex divina natura, quia res prius cognovit ; sed eodem modo eademque necessitate res ideatæ ex suis attributis consequuntur et concluduntur, ac ideas ex attributo Cogitationis consequi ostendimus.\n\nPROPOSITIO VII\n\nOrdo et connexio idearum idem est, ac ordo et connexio rerum.\n\nDEMONSTRATIO\n\npatet ex Ax. 4 p. I. Nam cujuscunque causati idea a cognitione causæ, cujus est effectus, dependet.\n\nCOROLLARIUM\n\nHinc sequitur, quod Dei cogitandi potentia æqualis est ipsius actuali agendi potentiæ. Hoc est, quicquid ex infinita Dei natura sequitur formaliter, id omne ex Dei idea eodem ordine eademque connexione sequitur in Deo objective.\n\nSCHOLIUM\n\nHic, antequam ulterius pergamus, revocandum nobis in memoriam est id, quod supra ostendimus ; nempe quod quicquid ab infinito intellectu percipi potest tanquam substantiæ essentiam constituens, id omne ad unicam tantum substantiam pertinet, et consequenter quod substantia cogitans et substantia extensa una eademque est substantia, quæ jam sub hoc, jam sub illo attributo comprehenditur. Sic etiam modus extensionis et idea illius modi una eademque est res, sed duobus modis expressa ; quod quidam Hebræorum quasi per nebulam vidisse videntur, qui scilicet statuunt, Deum, Dei intellectum, resque ab ipso intellectas, unum et idem esse. Ex. gr. circulus in Natura existens, et idea circuli existentis, quæ etiam in Deo est, una eademque est res, quæ per diversa attributa explicatur ; et ideo sive Naturam sub attributo Extensionis, sive sub attributo Cogitationis, sive sub alio quocunque concipiamus, unum eundemque ordinem, sive unam eandemque causarum connexionem, hoc est easdem res, invicem sequi reperiemus. Nec ulla alia de causa dixi, quod Deus sit causa ideæ ex. gr. circuli quatenus tantum est res cogitans, et circuli quatenus tantum est res extensa, nisi quia esse formale ideæ circuli non nisi per alium cogitandi modum, tanquam causam proximam et ille iterum per alium, et sic in infinitum, potest percipi ; ita ut, quamdiu res ut cogitandi modi considerantur, ordinem totius Naturæ, sive causarum connexionem, per solum Cogitationis attributum explicare debemus ; et quatenus ut modi Extensionis considerantur, ordo etiam totius Naturæ per solum Extensionis attributum explicari debet, et idem de aliis attributis intelligo. Quare rerum, ut in se sunt, Deus revera est causa quatenus infinitis constat attributis ; nec impræsentiarum hæc clarius possum explicare.\n\nPROPOSITIO VIII\n\nIdeæ rerum singularium, sive modorum, non existentium ita debent comprehendi in Dei infinita idea, ac rerum singularium, sive modorum, essentiæ formales in Dei attributis continentur.\n\nDEMONSTRATIO\n\nHæc Propositio patet ex præcedenti ; sed intelligitur clarius ex præcedenti Scholio.\n\nCOROLLARIUM\n\nHinc sequitur, quod, quamdiu res singulares non existunt, nisi quatenus in Dei attributis comprehenduntur, earum esse objectivum, sive ideæ, non existunt, nisi quatenus infinita Dei idea existit ; et ubi res singulares dicuntur existere, non tantum quatenus in Dei attributis comprehenduntur, sed quatenus etiam durare dicuntur, earum ideæ etiam existentiam, per quam durare dicuntur, involvent.\n\nSCHOLIUM\n\nSi quis ad uberiorem hujus rei explicationem exemplum desideret, nullum sane dare potero, quod rem, de qua hic loquor, utpote unicam, adæquate explicet ; conabor tamen rem, ut fieri potest, illustrare. Nempe circulus talis est naturæ, ut omnium linearum rectarum, in eodem sese invicem secantium, rectangula facta a segmentis sint inter se æqualia ; quare in circulo infinita inter se æqualia rectangula continentur ; attamen nullum eorum potest dici existere, nisi quatenus circulus existit, nec etiam alicujus horum rectangulorum idea potest dici existere, nisi quatenus in circuli idea comprehenditur. Concipiantur jam ex infinitis illis duo tantum, nempe E et D existere. Sane eorum etiam ideæ jam non tantum existunt, quatenus solummodo in circuli idea comprehenduntur ; sed etiam, quatenus illorum rectangulorum existentiam involvunt ; quo fit, ut a reliquis reliquorum rectangulorum ideis distinguantur.\n\nPROPOSITIO IX\n\nIdea rei singularis, actu existentis, Deum pro causa habet, non quatenus infinitus est, sed quatenus alia rei singularis actu existentis idea affectus consideratur, cujus etiam Deus est causa, quatenus alia tertia affectus est et sic in infinitum.\n\nDEMONSTRATIO\n\nIdea rei singularis, actu existentis, modus singularis cogitandi est, et a reliquis distinctus (per Coroll. et Schol. Prop. 8 hujus) ; adeoque (per Prop. 6 hujus) Deum, quatenus est tantum res cogitans, pro causa habet. At non (per Prop. 28 p. I), quatenus est res absolute cogitans, sed quatenus alio cogitandi modo affectus consideratur ; et hujus etiam Deus est causa, quatenus alio affectus est, et sic in infinitum. Atqui ordo et connexio idearum (per Prop. 7 hujus) idem est ac ordo et connexio causarum ; ergo unius singularis ideæ alia idea, sive Deus, quatenus alia idea affectus consideratur, est causa, et hujus etiam, quatenus alia, affectus est, et sic in infinitum. Q. E. D.\n\nCOROLLARIUM\n\nQuicquid in singulari cujuscunque ideæ objecto contingit ejus datur in Deo cognitio, quatenus tantum ejusdem objecti ideam habet.\n\nDEMONSTRATIO\n\nQuicquid in objecto cujuscunque ideæ contingit, ejus datur in Deo idea (per Prop. 3 hujus) non quatenus infinitus est, sed quatenus alia rei singularis idea affectus consideratur (per præced. Prop.) ; sed (per Prop. 7 hujus) ordo et connexio idearum idem est ac ordo et connexio rerum ; erit ergo cognitio ejus, quod in singulari aliquo objecto contingit, in Deo, quatenus tantum ejusdem objecti habet ideam. Q. E. D.\n\nPROPOSITIO X\n\nAd essentiam hominis non pertinet esse substantiæ, sive substantia formam hominis non constituit.\n\nDEMONSTRATIO\n\nEsse enim substantiæ involvit necessariam existentiam (per Prop. 7 p. I). Si igitur ad hominis essentiam pertinet esse substantiæ, data ergo substantia daretur necessario homo (per Defin. 2 hujus), et consequenter homo necessario existeret, quod (per Ax. 1 hujus) est absurdum. Ergo etc. Q. E. D.\n\nSCHOLIUM\n\nDemonstratur etiam hæc Propositio ex Propositione 5 p. I, nempe quod duæ ejusdem naturæ substantiæ non dentur. Cum autem plures homines existere possint, ergo id, quod hominis formam constituit, non est esse substantiæ. Patet præterea hæc Propositio ex reliquis substantiæ proprietatibus, videlicet, quod substantia sit sua natura. infinita, immutabilis, indivisibilis etc., ut facile unusquisque videre potest.\n\nCOROLLARIUM\n\nHinc sequitur, essentiam hominis constitui a certis Dei attributorum modificationibus. Nam esse substantiæ (per Prop. præced.) ad essentiam hominis non pertinet. Est ergo (per Prop. 15 p. I) aliquid, quod in Deo est, et quod sine Deo nec esse nec concipi potest, sive (per Coroll. Prop. 25 p. I) affectio, sive modus, qui Dei naturam certo et determinato modo exprimit.\n\nSCHOLIUM\n\nOmnes sane concedere debent, nihil sine Deo esse neque concipi posse. Nam apud omnes in confesso est, quod Deus omnium rerum, tam earum essentiae quam earum existentiæ, unica est causa ; hoc est, Deus non tantum est causa rerum secundum fieri, ut ajunt, sed etiam secundum esse. At interim plerique id ad essentiam alicujus rei pertinere dicunt, sine quo res nec esse nec concipi potest ; adeoque vel naturam Dei ad essentiam rerum creatarum pertinere, vel res creatas sine Deo vel esse vel concipi posse credunt, vel, quod certius est, sibi non satis constant. Cujus rei causam fuisse credo, quod ordinem Philosophandi non tenuerint. Nam naturam divinam, quam ante omnia contemplari debebant, quia tam cognitione quam natura prior est, ordine cognitionis ultimam, et res, quæ sensuum objecta vocantur, omnibus priores esse crediderunt ; unde factum est, ut, dum res naturales contemplati sunt, de nulla re minus cogitaverint, quam de divina natura, et cum postea animum ad divinam naturam contemplandum appulerint, de nulla re minus cogitare potuerint, quam de primis suis figmentis, quibus rerum naturalium cognitionem superstruxerant, utpote quæ ad cognitionem divinæ naturæ nihil juvare poterant ; adeoque nihil mirum, si sibi passim contradixerint. Sed hoc mitto. Nam meum intentum hic tantum fuit, causam reddere, cur non dixerim, id ad essentiam alicujus rei pertinere, sine quo res nec esse nec concipi potest ; nimirum quia res singulares non possunt sine Deo esse nec concipi, et tamen Deus ad earum essentiam non pertinet ; sed id necessario essentiam alicujus rei constituere dixi, quo dato res ponitur, et quo sublato res tollitur : vel id, sine quo res, et vice versa id, quod sine re, nec esse nec concipi potest.\n\nPROPOSITIO XI\n\nPrimum, quod actuale Mentis humanæ esse constituit, nihil aliud est, quam idea rei alicujus singularis actu existentis.\n\nDEMONSTRATIO\n\nEssentia hominis (per Coroll. præced. Prop.) a certis Dei attributorum modis constituitur ; nempe (per Axioma 2 hujus) a modis cogitandi, quorum omnium (per Axiom. 3 hujus) idea natura prior est, et ea data reliqui modi (quibus, scilicet idea natura prior est), in eodem debent esse individuo (per idem Axioma) ; atque adeo idea primum est, quod humanæ Mentis esse constituit. At non idea rei non existentis. Nam tum (per Coroll. Prop. 8 hujus) ipsa idea non posset dici existere ; erit ergo idea rei actu existentis. At non rei infinitæ. Res namque infinita (per Prop. 21 et 22 p. I) debet semper necessario existere ; atqui hoc (per Axiom. 1 hujus) est absurdum ; ergo primum, quod esse humanæ Mentis actuale constituit, est idea rei singularis actu existentis. Q. E. D.\n\nCOROLLARIUM\n\nHinc sequitur, Mentem humanam partem esse infiniti intellectus Dei ; ac proinde cum dicimus, Mentem humanam hoc vel illud percipere, nihil aliud dicimus, quam quod Deus non quatenus infinitus est, sed quatenus per naturam humanæ Mentis explicatur, sive quatenus humanæ Mentis essentiam constituit, hanc vel illam habet ideam ; et cum dicimus Deum hanc vel illam ideam habere, non tantum quatenus naturam humanæ Mentis constituit, sed quatenus simul cum Mente humana alterius rei etiam habet ideam, tum dicimus Mentem humanam rem ex parte, sive inadæquate percipere.\n\nSCHOLIUM\n\nHic sine dubio Lectores hærebunt, multaque comminiscentur, quæ moram injiciant, et hac de causa ipsos rogo, ut lento gradu mecum pergant, nec de his judicium ferant, donec omnia perlegerint.\n\nPROPOSITIO XII\n\nQuicquid in objecto ideæ humanam Mentem constituentis contingit, id ab humana Mente debet percipi, sive ejus rei dabitur in Mente necessario idea : Hoc est, si objectum ideæ humanam Mentem constituentis sit corpus, nihil in eo corpore poterit contingere, quod a Mente non percipiatur.\n\nDEMONSTRATIO\n\nQuicquid enim in objecto cujuscunque ideæ contingit, ejus rei datur necessario in Deo cognitio (per Coroll. Prop. 9 hujus), quatenus ejusdem objecti idea affectus consideratur, hoc est (per Prop. 11 hujus), quatenus mentem alicujus rei constituit. Quicquid igitur in objecto ideæ humanam Mentem constituentis contingit, ejus datur necessario in Deo cognitio, quatenus naturam humanæ Mentis constituit, hoc est (per Coroll. Prop. 11 hujus), ejus rei cognitio erit necessario in Mente, sive Mens id percipit. Q. E. D.\n\nSCHOLIUM\n\nHæc Propositio patet etiam et clarius intelligitur ex Schol. Prop. 7 hujus, quod vide.\n\nPROPOSITIO XIII\n\nObjectum ideæ humanam Mentem constituentis est Corpus, sive certus extensionis modus actu existens, et nihil aliud.\n\nDEMONSTRATIO\n\nSi enim Corpus non esset humanæ Mentis objectum, ideæ affectionum Corporis non essent in Deo (per Coroll. Prop. 9 hujus), quatenus Mentem nostram, sed quatenus alterius rei mentem constitueret ; hoc est (per Coroll. Prop. 11 hujus), ideæ affectionum Corporis non essent in nostra Mente. Atqui (per Axiom. 4 hujus) ideas affectionum Corporis habemus. Ergo objectum ideæ humanam Mentem constituentis est Corpus, idque (per Prop. 11 hujus) actu existens. Deinde, si præter Corpus etiam aliud esset Mentis objectum, cum nihil (per Prop. 36 p. I) existat, ex quo aliquis effectus non sequatur, deberet (per Prop. 11 hujus) necessario alicujus ejus effectus idea in Mente nostra dari ; atqui (per Axiom. 5 hujus) nulla ejus idea datur. Ergo objectum nostræ Mentis est Corpus existens et nihil aliud. Q. E. D.\n\nCOROLLARIUM\n\nHinc sequitur, hominem Mente et Corpore constare, et Corpus humanum, prout ipsum sentimus, existere.\n\nSCHOLIUM\n\nEx his non tanturn intelligimus, Mentem humanam unitam esse Corpori, sed etiam, quid per Mentis et Corporis unionem intelligendum sit. Verum ipsam adæquate, sive distincte, intelligere nemo poterit, nisi prius nostri Corporis naturam adæquate cognoscat. Nam ea, quæ hucusque ostendimus, admodum communia sunt, nec magis ad homines quam ad reliqua Individua pertinent, quæ omnia, quamvis diversis gradibus, animata tamen sunt. Nam cujuscunque rei datur necessario in Deo idea, cujus Deus est causa, eodem modo ac humani Corporis ideæ : atque adeo, quicquid de idea humani Corporis diximus, id de cujuscunque rei idea necessario dicendum est. Attamen nec etiam negare possumus ideas inter se, ut ipsa objecta, differre unamque alia præstantiorem esse, plusque realitatis continere, prout objectum unius objecto alterius præstantius est, plusque realitatis continet ; ac propterea ad determinandum, quid Mens humana reliquis intersit, quidque reliquis præstet, necesse nobis est ejus objecti, ut diximus, hoc est, Corporis humani naturam cognoscere. Eam autem hic explicare nec possum, nec id ad ea, quæ demonstrare volo necesse est. Hoc tamen in genere dico, quo Corpus aliquod reliquis aptius est ad plura simul agendum vel patiendum, eo ejus Mens reliquis aptior est ad plura simul percipiendum ; et quo unius corporis actiones magis ab ipso solo pendent, et quo minus alia corpora cum eodem in agendo concurrunt, eo ejus mens aptior est ad distincte intelligendum. Atque ex his præstantiam unius mentis præ aliis cognoscere possumus : deinde causam etiam videre, cur nostri Corporis non nisi admodum confusam habeamus cognitionem, et alia plura, quæ in sequentibus ex his deducam. Qua de causa operæ pretium esse duxi, hæc ipsa accuratius explicare et demonstrare, ad quod necesse est, pauca de natura corporum præmittere.\n\nAXIOMA I\n\nOmnia corpora vel moventur vel quiescunt.\n\nAXIOMA II\n\nUnumquodque corpus jam tardius jam celerius movetur.\n\nLEMMA I\n\nCorpora ratione motus et quietis, celeritatis et tarditatis, et non ratione substantiæ ab invicem distinguuntur.\n\nDEMONSTRATIO\n\nPrimam partem hujus per se notam suppono. At quod ratione substantiæ non distinguantur corpora, patet tam ex Prop. 5 quam 8 p. I. Sed clarius ex iis, quæ in Schol. Prop. 15, p. I dicta sunt.\n\nLEMMA II\n\nOmnia corpora in quibusdam conveniunt.\n\nDEMONSTRATIO\n\nIn his enim omnia corpora conveniunt, quod unius ejusdemque attributi conceptum involvunt (per Defin. 1 hujus). Deinde, quod jam tardius jam celerius, et absolute jam moveri jam quiescere possunt.\n\nLEMMA III\n\nCorpus motum vel quiescens ad motum vel quietem determinari debuit ab alio corpore, quod etiam ad motum vel quietem determinatum fuit ab alio, et illud iterum ab alio, et sic in infinitum.\n\nDEMONSTRATIO\n\nCorpora (per Defin. 1 hujus) res singulares sunt, quæ (per Lemma 1) ratione motus et quietis ab invicem distinguuntur ; adeoque (per Prop. 28 p. I) unumquodque ad motum vel quietem necessario determinari debuit ab alia re singulari, nempe (per Prop. 6 hujus) ab alio corpore, quod (per Axiom. 1) etiam vel movetur vel quiescit. At hoc etiam (per eandem rationem) moveri vel quiescere non potuit, nisi ab alio ad motum vel quietem determinatum fuisset, et hoc iterum (per eandem rationem) ab alio, et sic in infinitum. Q. E. D.\n\nCOROLLARIUM","paragraphs":[{"id":"RAW_QGOH_PSDN:P0001","text":"## DE NATURA ET ORIGINE MENTIS","kind":"passage"},{"id":"RAW_QGOH_PSDN:P0002","text":"Transeo jam ad ea explicanda, quæ ex Dei, sive Entis æterni et infiniti, essentia necessario debuerunt sequi. Non quidem omnia ; infinita enim infinitis modis ex ipsa debere sequi Prop. 16 Part. 1 demonstravimus : sed ea solummodo, quæ nos ad Mentis humanæ, ejusque summæ beatitudinis, cognitionem quasi manu ducere possunt.","kind":"passage"},{"id":"RAW_QGOH_PSDN:P0003","text":"DEFINITIONES","kind":"passage"},{"id":"RAW_QGOH_PSDN:P0004","text":"I. Per corpus intelligo modum, qui Dei essentiam, quatenus ut res extensa consideratur, certo et determinato modo exprimit; vid. Coroll. Prop. 25 p. I.","kind":"passage"},{"id":"RAW_QGOH_PSDN:P0005","text":"II. Ad essentiam alicujus rei id pertinere dico, quo dato res necessario ponitur, et quo sublato res necessario tollitur ; vel id, sine quo res, et vice versa quod sine re, nec esse nec concipi potest.","kind":"passage"},{"id":"RAW_QGOH_PSDN:P0006","text":"III. Per ideam intelligo Mentis conceptum, quem Mens format propterea quod res est cogitans.","kind":"passage"},{"id":"RAW_QGOH_PSDN:P0007","text":"EXPLICATIO","kind":"passage"},{"id":"RAW_QGOH_PSDN:P0008","text":"Dico potius conceptum quam perceptionem, quia perceptionis nomen indicare videtur, Mentem ab objecto pati ; at conceptus actionem Mentis exprimere videtur.","kind":"passage"},{"id":"RAW_QGOH_PSDN:P0009","text":"IV. Per ideam adæquatam intelligo ideam, quae, quatenus in se sine relatione ad objectum consideratur, omnes veræ ideæ proprietates sive denominationes intrinsecas habet.","kind":"passage"},{"id":"RAW_QGOH_PSDN:P0010","text":"EXPLICATIO","kind":"passage"},{"id":"RAW_QGOH_PSDN:P0011","text":"Dico intrinsecas, ut illam secludam, quæ extrinseca est, nempe convenientiam ideæ cum suo ideato.","kind":"passage"},{"id":"RAW_QGOH_PSDN:P0012","text":"V. Duratio est indefinita existendi continuatio.","kind":"passage"},{"id":"RAW_QGOH_PSDN:P0013","text":"EXPLICATIO","kind":"passage"},{"id":"RAW_QGOH_PSDN:P0014","text":"Dico indefinitam, quia per ipsam rei existentis naturam determinari nequaquam potest, neque etiam a causa efficiente, quæ scilicet rei existentiam necessario ponit, non autem tollit.","kind":"passage"},{"id":"RAW_QGOH_PSDN:P0015","text":"VI. Per realitatem et perfectionem idem intelligo.","kind":"passage"},{"id":"RAW_QGOH_PSDN:P0016","text":"VII. Per res singulares intelligo res, quæ finitæ sunt, et determinatam habent existentiam. Quod si plura individua in una actione ita concurrant, ut omnia simul unius effectus sint causa, eadem omnia eatenus ut unam rem singularem considero.","kind":"passage"},{"id":"RAW_QGOH_PSDN:P0017","text":"AXIOMATA","kind":"passage"},{"id":"RAW_QGOH_PSDN:P0018","text":"I. Hominis essentia non involvit necessariam existentiam, hoc est, ex Naturæ ordine tam fieri potest, ut hic et ille homo existat, quam ut non existat.","kind":"passage"},{"id":"RAW_QGOH_PSDN:P0019","text":"II. Homo cogitat.","kind":"passage"},{"id":"RAW_QGOH_PSDN:P0020","text":"III. Modi cogitandi, ut amor, cupiditas, vel quicunque nomine affectus animi insigniuntur, non dantur, nisi in eodem Individuo detur idea rei amatæ, desideratæ, etc. At idea dari potest, quamvis nullus alius detur cogitandi modus.","kind":"passage"},{"id":"RAW_QGOH_PSDN:P0021","text":"IV. Nos corpus quoddam multis modis affici sentimus.","kind":"passage"},{"id":"RAW_QGOH_PSDN:P0022","text":"V. Nullas res singulares, præter corpora et cogitandi modos, sentimus nec percipimus.","kind":"passage"},{"id":"RAW_QGOH_PSDN:P0023","text":"Postulata vide post 13 Propositionem.","kind":"passage"},{"id":"RAW_QGOH_PSDN:P0024","text":"PROPOSITIO I","kind":"passage"},{"id":"RAW_QGOH_PSDN:P0025","text":"Cogitatio attributum Dei est, sive Deus est res cogitans.","kind":"passage"},{"id":"RAW_QGOH_PSDN:P0026","text":"DEMONSTRATIO","kind":"passage"},{"id":"RAW_QGOH_PSDN:P0027","text":"Singulares cogitationes, sive hæc et illa cogitatio, modi sunt, qui Dei naturam certo et determinato modo exprimunt (per Coroll. Prop. 25, p. I). Competit ergo Deo (per Defin. 5 p. I) attributum, cujus conceptum singulares omnes cogitationes involvunt, per quod etiam concipiuntur. Est igitur Cogitatio unum ex infinitis Dei attributis, quod Dei æternam et infinitam essentiam exprimit (vid. Defin. 6 p. I), sive Deus est res cogitans. Q. E. D.","kind":"passage"},{"id":"RAW_QGOH_PSDN:P0028","text":"SCHOLIUM","kind":"passage"},{"id":"RAW_QGOH_PSDN:P0029","text":"Patet etiam hæc Propositio ex hoc, quod nos possumus ens cogitans infinitum concipere. Nam quo plura ens cogitans potest cogitare, eo plus realitatis sive perfectionis idem continere concipimus ; ergo ens, quod infinita infinitis modis cogitare potest, est necessario virtute cogitandi infinitum. Cum itaque, ad solam cogitationem attendendo, Ens infinitum concipiamus, est necessario (per Defin. 4 et 6 p. I). Cogitatio unum ex infinitis Dei attributis, ut volebamus.","kind":"passage"},{"id":"RAW_QGOH_PSDN:P0030","text":"PROPOSITIO II","kind":"passage"},{"id":"RAW_QGOH_PSDN:P0031","text":"Extensio attributum Dei est, sive Deus est res extensa.","kind":"passage"},{"id":"RAW_QGOH_PSDN:P0032","text":"DEMONSTRATIO","kind":"passage"},{"id":"RAW_QGOH_PSDN:P0033","text":"Hujus eodem modo procedit, ac demonstratio præcedentis Propositionis.","kind":"passage"},{"id":"RAW_QGOH_PSDN:P0034","text":"PROPOSITIO III","kind":"passage"},{"id":"RAW_QGOH_PSDN:P0035","text":"In Deo datur necessario idea, tam ejus essentiæ, quam omnium, quæ ex ipsius essentia necessario sequuntur.","kind":"passage"},{"id":"RAW_QGOH_PSDN:P0036","text":"DEMONSTRATIO","kind":"passage"},{"id":"RAW_QGOH_PSDN:P0037","text":"Deum enim (per Prop. 1 hujus) infinita infinitis modis cogitare, sive (quod idem est, per Prop. 16 p. I) ideam suæ essentiæ, et omnium, quæ necessario ex ea sequuntur, formare potest. Atqui omne id, quod in Dei potestate est, necessario est (per Prop. 35 p. I) ergo datur necessario talis idea, et (per Prop. 15, p. I) non nisi in Deo. Q. E. D.","kind":"passage"},{"id":"RAW_QGOH_PSDN:P0038","text":"SCHOLIUM","kind":"passage"},{"id":"RAW_QGOH_PSDN:P0039","text":"Vulgus per Dei potentiam intelligit Dei liberam voluntatem, et jus in omnia, quæ sunt, quæque propterea communiter ut contingentia considerantur. Deum enim potestatem omnia destruendi habere dicunt, et in nihilum redigendi. Dei porro potentiam cum potentia Regum sæpissime comparant. Sed hoc in Corollario 1 et 2 Propositionis 32 partis I refutavimus, et Propositione 16 partis I ostendimus, Deum eadem necessitate agere, qua seipsum intelligit ; hoc est, sicuti ex necessitate divinæ naturæ sequitur (sicut omnes uno ore statuunt) ut Deus seipsum intelligat, eadem etiam necessitate sequitur, ut Deus infinita infinitis modis agat. Deinde Propositione 34 partis I ostendimus, Dei potentiam nihil esse, præterquam Dei actuosam essentiam ; adeoque tam nobis impossibile est concipere, Deum non agere, quam Deum non esse.","kind":"passage"},{"id":"RAW_QGOH_PSDN:P0040","text":"Porro si hæc ulterius persequi liberet, possem hic ostendere, potentiam illam, quam vulgus Deo affingit, non tantum humanam esse (quod ostendit Deum hominem, vel instar hominis a vulgo concipi), sed etiam impotentiam involvere. Sed nolo de eadem re toties sermonem instituere. Lectorem solummodo iterum atque iterum rogo, ut, quæ in prima parte, ex Propositione 16, usque ad finem, de hac re dicta sunt, semel atque iterum perpendat. Nam nemo ea, quæ volo, percipere recte poterit, nisi magnopere caveat, ne Dei potentiam cum humana Regum potentia vel jure confundat.","kind":"passage"},{"id":"RAW_QGOH_PSDN:P0041","text":"PROPOSITIO IV","kind":"passage"},{"id":"RAW_QGOH_PSDN:P0042","text":"Idea Dei, ex qua infinita infinitis modis sequuntur, unica tantum esse potest.","kind":"passage"},{"id":"RAW_QGOH_PSDN:P0043","text":"DEMONSTRATIO","kind":"passage"},{"id":"RAW_QGOH_PSDN:P0044","text":"Intellectus infinitus nihil, præter Dei attributa ejusque affectiones, comprehendit (per Prop. 30 p. I). Atqui Deus est unicus (per Coroll. 1 Prop. 14 p. I). Ergo idea Dei, ex qua infinita infinitis modis sequuntur, unica tantum esse potest. Q. E. D.","kind":"passage"},{"id":"RAW_QGOH_PSDN:P0045","text":"PROPOSITIO V","kind":"passage"},{"id":"RAW_QGOH_PSDN:P0046","text":"Esse formale idearum Deum, quatenus tantum ut res cogitans consideratur, pro causa agnoscit, et non quatenus alio attributo explicatur. Hoc est, tam Dei attributorum, quam rerum singularium ideæ, non ipsa ideata sive res perceptas pro causa efficiente agnoscunt, sed ipsum Deum, quatenus est res cogitans.","kind":"passage"},{"id":"RAW_QGOH_PSDN:P0047","text":"DEMONSTRATIO","kind":"passage"},{"id":"RAW_QGOH_PSDN:P0048","text":"patet quidem ex Propositione 3 hujus. Ibi enim concludebamus, Deum ideam suæ essentiæ, et omnium, quæ ex ea necessario sequuntur, formare posse ex hoc solo, nempe quod Deus est res cogitans, et non ex eo, quod sit suæ ideæ objectum. Quare esse formale idearum Deum, quatenus est res cogitans, pro causa agnoscit. Sed aliter hoc modo demonstratur. Esse formale idearum modus est cogitandi (ut per se notum), hoc est (per Coroll. Prop. 25 p. I) modus, qui Dei naturam quatenus tantum est res cogitans certo modo exprimit, adeoque (per Prop. 10 p. I) nullius alterius attributi Dei conceptum involvit, et consequenter (per Axiom. 4 p. I) nullius alterius attributi nisi cogitationis est effectus : adeoque esse formale idearum Deum, quatenus tantum ut res cogitans consideratur, etc. Q. E. D.","kind":"passage"},{"id":"RAW_QGOH_PSDN:P0049","text":"PROPOSITIO VI","kind":"passage"},{"id":"RAW_QGOH_PSDN:P0050","text":"Cujuscunque attributi modi Deum, quatenus tantum sub illo attributo, cujus modi sunt, et non quatenus sub ullo alio consideratur, pro causa habent.","kind":"passage"},{"id":"RAW_QGOH_PSDN:P0051","text":"DEMONSTRATIO","kind":"passage"},{"id":"RAW_QGOH_PSDN:P0052","text":"Unumquodque enim attributum per se absque alio concipitur (per Prop. 10 p. I). Quare uniuscujusque attributi modi conceptum sui attributi, non autem alterius involvunt ; adeoque (per Axiom. 4 p. I) Deum, quatenus sub illo attributo, cujus modi sunt, et non quatenus sub ullo alio consideratur, pro causa habent. Q. E. D.","kind":"passage"},{"id":"RAW_QGOH_PSDN:P0053","text":"COROLLARIUM","kind":"passage"},{"id":"RAW_QGOH_PSDN:P0054","text":"Hinc sequitur, quod esse formale rerum, quæ modi non sunt cogitandi, non sequitur ideo ex divina natura, quia res prius cognovit ; sed eodem modo eademque necessitate res ideatæ ex suis attributis consequuntur et concluduntur, ac ideas ex attributo Cogitationis consequi ostendimus.","kind":"passage"},{"id":"RAW_QGOH_PSDN:P0055","text":"PROPOSITIO VII","kind":"passage"},{"id":"RAW_QGOH_PSDN:P0056","text":"Ordo et connexio idearum idem est, ac ordo et connexio rerum.","kind":"passage"},{"id":"RAW_QGOH_PSDN:P0057","text":"DEMONSTRATIO","kind":"passage"},{"id":"RAW_QGOH_PSDN:P0058","text":"patet ex Ax. 4 p. I. Nam cujuscunque causati idea a cognitione causæ, cujus est effectus, dependet.","kind":"passage"},{"id":"RAW_QGOH_PSDN:P0059","text":"COROLLARIUM","kind":"passage"},{"id":"RAW_QGOH_PSDN:P0060","text":"Hinc sequitur, quod Dei cogitandi potentia æqualis est ipsius actuali agendi potentiæ. Hoc est, quicquid ex infinita Dei natura sequitur formaliter, id omne ex Dei idea eodem ordine eademque connexione sequitur in Deo objective.","kind":"passage"},{"id":"RAW_QGOH_PSDN:P0061","text":"SCHOLIUM","kind":"passage"},{"id":"RAW_QGOH_PSDN:P0062","text":"Hic, antequam ulterius pergamus, revocandum nobis in memoriam est id, quod supra ostendimus ; nempe quod quicquid ab infinito intellectu percipi potest tanquam substantiæ essentiam constituens, id omne ad unicam tantum substantiam pertinet, et consequenter quod substantia cogitans et substantia extensa una eademque est substantia, quæ jam sub hoc, jam sub illo attributo comprehenditur. Sic etiam modus extensionis et idea illius modi una eademque est res, sed duobus modis expressa ; quod quidam Hebræorum quasi per nebulam vidisse videntur, qui scilicet statuunt, Deum, Dei intellectum, resque ab ipso intellectas, unum et idem esse. Ex. gr.","kind":"passage"},{"id":"RAW_QGOH_PSDN:P0063","text":"circulus in Natura existens, et idea circuli existentis, quæ etiam in Deo est, una eademque est res, quæ per diversa attributa explicatur ; et ideo sive Naturam sub attributo Extensionis, sive sub attributo Cogitationis, sive sub alio quocunque concipiamus, unum eundemque ordinem, sive unam eandemque causarum connexionem, hoc est easdem res, invicem sequi reperiemus. Nec ulla alia de causa dixi, quod Deus sit causa ideæ ex. gr.","kind":"passage"},{"id":"RAW_QGOH_PSDN:P0064","text":"circuli quatenus tantum est res cogitans, et circuli quatenus tantum est res extensa, nisi quia esse formale ideæ circuli non nisi per alium cogitandi modum, tanquam causam proximam et ille iterum per alium, et sic in infinitum, potest percipi ; ita ut, quamdiu res ut cogitandi modi considerantur, ordinem totius Naturæ, sive causarum connexionem, per solum Cogitationis attributum explicare debemus ; et quatenus ut modi Extensionis considerantur, ordo etiam totius Naturæ per solum Extensionis attributum explicari debet, et idem de aliis attributis intelligo. Quare rerum, ut in se sunt, Deus revera est causa quatenus infinitis constat attributis ; nec impræsentiarum hæc clarius possum explicare.","kind":"passage"},{"id":"RAW_QGOH_PSDN:P0065","text":"PROPOSITIO VIII","kind":"passage"},{"id":"RAW_QGOH_PSDN:P0066","text":"Ideæ rerum singularium, sive modorum, non existentium ita debent comprehendi in Dei infinita idea, ac rerum singularium, sive modorum, essentiæ formales in Dei attributis continentur.","kind":"passage"},{"id":"RAW_QGOH_PSDN:P0067","text":"DEMONSTRATIO","kind":"passage"},{"id":"RAW_QGOH_PSDN:P0068","text":"Hæc Propositio patet ex præcedenti ; sed intelligitur clarius ex præcedenti Scholio.","kind":"passage"},{"id":"RAW_QGOH_PSDN:P0069","text":"COROLLARIUM","kind":"passage"},{"id":"RAW_QGOH_PSDN:P0070","text":"Hinc sequitur, quod, quamdiu res singulares non existunt, nisi quatenus in Dei attributis comprehenduntur, earum esse objectivum, sive ideæ, non existunt, nisi quatenus infinita Dei idea existit ; et ubi res singulares dicuntur existere, non tantum quatenus in Dei attributis comprehenduntur, sed quatenus etiam durare dicuntur, earum ideæ etiam existentiam, per quam durare dicuntur, involvent.","kind":"passage"},{"id":"RAW_QGOH_PSDN:P0071","text":"SCHOLIUM","kind":"passage"},{"id":"RAW_QGOH_PSDN:P0072","text":"Si quis ad uberiorem hujus rei explicationem exemplum desideret, nullum sane dare potero, quod rem, de qua hic loquor, utpote unicam, adæquate explicet ; conabor tamen rem, ut fieri potest, illustrare. Nempe circulus talis est naturæ, ut omnium linearum rectarum, in eodem sese invicem secantium, rectangula facta a segmentis sint inter se æqualia ; quare in circulo infinita inter se æqualia rectangula continentur ; attamen nullum eorum potest dici existere, nisi quatenus circulus existit, nec etiam alicujus horum rectangulorum idea potest dici existere, nisi quatenus in circuli idea comprehenditur. Concipiantur jam ex infinitis illis duo tantum, nempe E et D existere.","kind":"passage"},{"id":"RAW_QGOH_PSDN:P0073","text":"Sane eorum etiam ideæ jam non tantum existunt, quatenus solummodo in circuli idea comprehenduntur ; sed etiam, quatenus illorum rectangulorum existentiam involvunt ; quo fit, ut a reliquis reliquorum rectangulorum ideis distinguantur.","kind":"passage"},{"id":"RAW_QGOH_PSDN:P0074","text":"PROPOSITIO IX","kind":"passage"},{"id":"RAW_QGOH_PSDN:P0075","text":"Idea rei singularis, actu existentis, Deum pro causa habet, non quatenus infinitus est, sed quatenus alia rei singularis actu existentis idea affectus consideratur, cujus etiam Deus est causa, quatenus alia tertia affectus est et sic in infinitum.","kind":"passage"},{"id":"RAW_QGOH_PSDN:P0076","text":"DEMONSTRATIO","kind":"passage"},{"id":"RAW_QGOH_PSDN:P0077","text":"Idea rei singularis, actu existentis, modus singularis cogitandi est, et a reliquis distinctus (per Coroll. et Schol. Prop. 8 hujus) ; adeoque (per Prop. 6 hujus) Deum, quatenus est tantum res cogitans, pro causa habet. At non (per Prop. 28 p. I), quatenus est res absolute cogitans, sed quatenus alio cogitandi modo affectus consideratur ; et hujus etiam Deus est causa, quatenus alio affectus est, et sic in infinitum. Atqui ordo et connexio idearum (per Prop. 7 hujus) idem est ac ordo et connexio causarum ; ergo unius singularis ideæ alia idea, sive Deus, quatenus alia idea affectus consideratur, est causa, et hujus etiam, quatenus alia, affectus est, et sic in infinitum. Q. E. D.","kind":"passage"},{"id":"RAW_QGOH_PSDN:P0078","text":"COROLLARIUM","kind":"passage"},{"id":"RAW_QGOH_PSDN:P0079","text":"Quicquid in singulari cujuscunque ideæ objecto contingit ejus datur in Deo cognitio, quatenus tantum ejusdem objecti ideam habet.","kind":"passage"},{"id":"RAW_QGOH_PSDN:P0080","text":"DEMONSTRATIO","kind":"passage"},{"id":"RAW_QGOH_PSDN:P0081","text":"Quicquid in objecto cujuscunque ideæ contingit, ejus datur in Deo idea (per Prop. 3 hujus) non quatenus infinitus est, sed quatenus alia rei singularis idea affectus consideratur (per præced. Prop.) ; sed (per Prop. 7 hujus) ordo et connexio idearum idem est ac ordo et connexio rerum ; erit ergo cognitio ejus, quod in singulari aliquo objecto contingit, in Deo, quatenus tantum ejusdem objecti habet ideam. Q. E. D.","kind":"passage"},{"id":"RAW_QGOH_PSDN:P0082","text":"PROPOSITIO X","kind":"passage"},{"id":"RAW_QGOH_PSDN:P0083","text":"Ad essentiam hominis non pertinet esse substantiæ, sive substantia formam hominis non constituit.","kind":"passage"},{"id":"RAW_QGOH_PSDN:P0084","text":"DEMONSTRATIO","kind":"passage"},{"id":"RAW_QGOH_PSDN:P0085","text":"Esse enim substantiæ involvit necessariam existentiam (per Prop. 7 p. I). Si igitur ad hominis essentiam pertinet esse substantiæ, data ergo substantia daretur necessario homo (per Defin. 2 hujus), et consequenter homo necessario existeret, quod (per Ax. 1 hujus) est absurdum. Ergo etc. Q. E. D.","kind":"passage"},{"id":"RAW_QGOH_PSDN:P0086","text":"SCHOLIUM","kind":"passage"},{"id":"RAW_QGOH_PSDN:P0087","text":"Demonstratur etiam hæc Propositio ex Propositione 5 p. I, nempe quod duæ ejusdem naturæ substantiæ non dentur. Cum autem plures homines existere possint, ergo id, quod hominis formam constituit, non est esse substantiæ. Patet præterea hæc Propositio ex reliquis substantiæ proprietatibus, videlicet, quod substantia sit sua natura. infinita, immutabilis, indivisibilis etc., ut facile unusquisque videre potest.","kind":"passage"},{"id":"RAW_QGOH_PSDN:P0088","text":"COROLLARIUM","kind":"passage"},{"id":"RAW_QGOH_PSDN:P0089","text":"Hinc sequitur, essentiam hominis constitui a certis Dei attributorum modificationibus. Nam esse substantiæ (per Prop. præced.) ad essentiam hominis non pertinet. Est ergo (per Prop. 15 p. I) aliquid, quod in Deo est, et quod sine Deo nec esse nec concipi potest, sive (per Coroll. Prop. 25 p. I) affectio, sive modus, qui Dei naturam certo et determinato modo exprimit.","kind":"passage"},{"id":"RAW_QGOH_PSDN:P0090","text":"SCHOLIUM","kind":"passage"},{"id":"RAW_QGOH_PSDN:P0091","text":"Omnes sane concedere debent, nihil sine Deo esse neque concipi posse. Nam apud omnes in confesso est, quod Deus omnium rerum, tam earum essentiae quam earum existentiæ, unica est causa ; hoc est, Deus non tantum est causa rerum secundum fieri, ut ajunt, sed etiam secundum esse. At interim plerique id ad essentiam alicujus rei pertinere dicunt, sine quo res nec esse nec concipi potest ; adeoque vel naturam Dei ad essentiam rerum creatarum pertinere, vel res creatas sine Deo vel esse vel concipi posse credunt, vel, quod certius est, sibi non satis constant. Cujus rei causam fuisse credo, quod ordinem Philosophandi non tenuerint.","kind":"passage"},{"id":"RAW_QGOH_PSDN:P0092","text":"Nam naturam divinam, quam ante omnia contemplari debebant, quia tam cognitione quam natura prior est, ordine cognitionis ultimam, et res, quæ sensuum objecta vocantur, omnibus priores esse crediderunt ; unde factum est, ut, dum res naturales contemplati sunt, de nulla re minus cogitaverint, quam de divina natura, et cum postea animum ad divinam naturam contemplandum appulerint, de nulla re minus cogitare potuerint, quam de primis suis figmentis, quibus rerum naturalium cognitionem superstruxerant, utpote quæ ad cognitionem divinæ naturæ nihil juvare poterant ; adeoque nihil mirum, si sibi passim contradixerint. Sed hoc mitto.","kind":"passage"},{"id":"RAW_QGOH_PSDN:P0093","text":"Nam meum intentum hic tantum fuit, causam reddere, cur non dixerim, id ad essentiam alicujus rei pertinere, sine quo res nec esse nec concipi potest ; nimirum quia res singulares non possunt sine Deo esse nec concipi, et tamen Deus ad earum essentiam non pertinet ; sed id necessario essentiam alicujus rei constituere dixi, quo dato res ponitur, et quo sublato res tollitur : vel id, sine quo res, et vice versa id, quod sine re, nec esse nec concipi potest.","kind":"passage"},{"id":"RAW_QGOH_PSDN:P0094","text":"PROPOSITIO XI","kind":"passage"},{"id":"RAW_QGOH_PSDN:P0095","text":"Primum, quod actuale Mentis humanæ esse constituit, nihil aliud est, quam idea rei alicujus singularis actu existentis.","kind":"passage"},{"id":"RAW_QGOH_PSDN:P0096","text":"DEMONSTRATIO","kind":"passage"},{"id":"RAW_QGOH_PSDN:P0097","text":"Essentia hominis (per Coroll. præced. Prop.) a certis Dei attributorum modis constituitur ; nempe (per Axioma 2 hujus) a modis cogitandi, quorum omnium (per Axiom. 3 hujus) idea natura prior est, et ea data reliqui modi (quibus, scilicet idea natura prior est), in eodem debent esse individuo (per idem Axioma) ; atque adeo idea primum est, quod humanæ Mentis esse constituit. At non idea rei non existentis. Nam tum (per Coroll. Prop. 8 hujus) ipsa idea non posset dici existere ; erit ergo idea rei actu existentis. At non rei infinitæ. Res namque infinita (per Prop. 21 et 22 p. I) debet semper necessario existere ; atqui hoc (per Axiom. 1 hujus) est absurdum ; ergo primum, quod esse humanæ Mentis actuale constituit, est idea rei singularis actu existentis. Q. E. D.","kind":"passage"},{"id":"RAW_QGOH_PSDN:P0098","text":"COROLLARIUM","kind":"passage"},{"id":"RAW_QGOH_PSDN:P0099","text":"Hinc sequitur, Mentem humanam partem esse infiniti intellectus Dei ; ac proinde cum dicimus, Mentem humanam hoc vel illud percipere, nihil aliud dicimus, quam quod Deus non quatenus infinitus est, sed quatenus per naturam humanæ Mentis explicatur, sive quatenus humanæ Mentis essentiam constituit, hanc vel illam habet ideam ; et cum dicimus Deum hanc vel illam ideam habere, non tantum quatenus naturam humanæ Mentis constituit, sed quatenus simul cum Mente humana alterius rei etiam habet ideam, tum dicimus Mentem humanam rem ex parte, sive inadæquate percipere.","kind":"passage"},{"id":"RAW_QGOH_PSDN:P0100","text":"SCHOLIUM","kind":"passage"},{"id":"RAW_QGOH_PSDN:P0101","text":"Hic sine dubio Lectores hærebunt, multaque comminiscentur, quæ moram injiciant, et hac de causa ipsos rogo, ut lento gradu mecum pergant, nec de his judicium ferant, donec omnia perlegerint.","kind":"passage"},{"id":"RAW_QGOH_PSDN:P0102","text":"PROPOSITIO XII","kind":"passage"},{"id":"RAW_QGOH_PSDN:P0103","text":"Quicquid in objecto ideæ humanam Mentem constituentis contingit, id ab humana Mente debet percipi, sive ejus rei dabitur in Mente necessario idea : Hoc est, si objectum ideæ humanam Mentem constituentis sit corpus, nihil in eo corpore poterit contingere, quod a Mente non percipiatur.","kind":"passage"},{"id":"RAW_QGOH_PSDN:P0104","text":"DEMONSTRATIO","kind":"passage"},{"id":"RAW_QGOH_PSDN:P0105","text":"Quicquid enim in objecto cujuscunque ideæ contingit, ejus rei datur necessario in Deo cognitio (per Coroll. Prop. 9 hujus), quatenus ejusdem objecti idea affectus consideratur, hoc est (per Prop. 11 hujus), quatenus mentem alicujus rei constituit. Quicquid igitur in objecto ideæ humanam Mentem constituentis contingit, ejus datur necessario in Deo cognitio, quatenus naturam humanæ Mentis constituit, hoc est (per Coroll. Prop. 11 hujus), ejus rei cognitio erit necessario in Mente, sive Mens id percipit. Q. E. D.","kind":"passage"},{"id":"RAW_QGOH_PSDN:P0106","text":"SCHOLIUM","kind":"passage"},{"id":"RAW_QGOH_PSDN:P0107","text":"Hæc Propositio patet etiam et clarius intelligitur ex Schol. Prop. 7 hujus, quod vide.","kind":"passage"},{"id":"RAW_QGOH_PSDN:P0108","text":"PROPOSITIO XIII","kind":"passage"},{"id":"RAW_QGOH_PSDN:P0109","text":"Objectum ideæ humanam Mentem constituentis est Corpus, sive certus extensionis modus actu existens, et nihil aliud.","kind":"passage"},{"id":"RAW_QGOH_PSDN:P0110","text":"DEMONSTRATIO","kind":"passage"},{"id":"RAW_QGOH_PSDN:P0111","text":"Si enim Corpus non esset humanæ Mentis objectum, ideæ affectionum Corporis non essent in Deo (per Coroll. Prop. 9 hujus), quatenus Mentem nostram, sed quatenus alterius rei mentem constitueret ; hoc est (per Coroll. Prop. 11 hujus), ideæ affectionum Corporis non essent in nostra Mente. Atqui (per Axiom. 4 hujus) ideas affectionum Corporis habemus. Ergo objectum ideæ humanam Mentem constituentis est Corpus, idque (per Prop. 11 hujus) actu existens. Deinde, si præter Corpus etiam aliud esset Mentis objectum, cum nihil (per Prop. 36 p. I) existat, ex quo aliquis effectus non sequatur, deberet (per Prop. 11 hujus) necessario alicujus ejus effectus idea in Mente nostra dari ; atqui (per Axiom. 5 hujus) nulla ejus idea datur. Ergo objectum nostræ Mentis est Corpus existens et nihil aliud. Q. E. D.","kind":"passage"},{"id":"RAW_QGOH_PSDN:P0112","text":"COROLLARIUM","kind":"passage"},{"id":"RAW_QGOH_PSDN:P0113","text":"Hinc sequitur, hominem Mente et Corpore constare, et Corpus humanum, prout ipsum sentimus, existere.","kind":"passage"},{"id":"RAW_QGOH_PSDN:P0114","text":"SCHOLIUM","kind":"passage"},{"id":"RAW_QGOH_PSDN:P0115","text":"Ex his non tanturn intelligimus, Mentem humanam unitam esse Corpori, sed etiam, quid per Mentis et Corporis unionem intelligendum sit. Verum ipsam adæquate, sive distincte, intelligere nemo poterit, nisi prius nostri Corporis naturam adæquate cognoscat. Nam ea, quæ hucusque ostendimus, admodum communia sunt, nec magis ad homines quam ad reliqua Individua pertinent, quæ omnia, quamvis diversis gradibus, animata tamen sunt. Nam cujuscunque rei datur necessario in Deo idea, cujus Deus est causa, eodem modo ac humani Corporis ideæ : atque adeo, quicquid de idea humani Corporis diximus, id de cujuscunque rei idea necessario dicendum est.","kind":"passage"},{"id":"RAW_QGOH_PSDN:P0116","text":"Attamen nec etiam negare possumus ideas inter se, ut ipsa objecta, differre unamque alia præstantiorem esse, plusque realitatis continere, prout objectum unius objecto alterius præstantius est, plusque realitatis continet ; ac propterea ad determinandum, quid Mens humana reliquis intersit, quidque reliquis præstet, necesse nobis est ejus objecti, ut diximus, hoc est, Corporis humani naturam cognoscere. Eam autem hic explicare nec possum, nec id ad ea, quæ demonstrare volo necesse est. Hoc tamen in genere dico, quo Corpus aliquod reliquis aptius est ad plura simul agendum vel patiendum, eo ejus Mens reliquis aptior est ad plura simul percipiendum ; et quo unius corporis actiones magis ab ipso solo pendent, et quo minus alia corpora cum eodem in agendo concurrunt, eo ejus mens aptior est ad distincte intelligendum.","kind":"passage"},{"id":"RAW_QGOH_PSDN:P0117","text":"Atque ex his præstantiam unius mentis præ aliis cognoscere possumus : deinde causam etiam videre, cur nostri Corporis non nisi admodum confusam habeamus cognitionem, et alia plura, quæ in sequentibus ex his deducam. Qua de causa operæ pretium esse duxi, hæc ipsa accuratius explicare et demonstrare, ad quod necesse est, pauca de natura corporum præmittere.","kind":"passage"},{"id":"RAW_QGOH_PSDN:P0118","text":"AXIOMA I","kind":"passage"},{"id":"RAW_QGOH_PSDN:P0119","text":"Omnia corpora vel moventur vel quiescunt.","kind":"passage"},{"id":"RAW_QGOH_PSDN:P0120","text":"AXIOMA II","kind":"passage"},{"id":"RAW_QGOH_PSDN:P0121","text":"Unumquodque corpus jam tardius jam celerius movetur.","kind":"passage"},{"id":"RAW_QGOH_PSDN:P0122","text":"LEMMA I","kind":"passage"},{"id":"RAW_QGOH_PSDN:P0123","text":"Corpora ratione motus et quietis, celeritatis et tarditatis, et non ratione substantiæ ab invicem distinguuntur.","kind":"passage"},{"id":"RAW_QGOH_PSDN:P0124","text":"DEMONSTRATIO","kind":"passage"},{"id":"RAW_QGOH_PSDN:P0125","text":"Primam partem hujus per se notam suppono. At quod ratione substantiæ non distinguantur corpora, patet tam ex Prop. 5 quam 8 p. I. Sed clarius ex iis, quæ in Schol. Prop. 15, p. I dicta sunt.","kind":"passage"},{"id":"RAW_QGOH_PSDN:P0126","text":"LEMMA II","kind":"passage"},{"id":"RAW_QGOH_PSDN:P0127","text":"Omnia corpora in quibusdam conveniunt.","kind":"passage"},{"id":"RAW_QGOH_PSDN:P0128","text":"DEMONSTRATIO","kind":"passage"},{"id":"RAW_QGOH_PSDN:P0129","text":"In his enim omnia corpora conveniunt, quod unius ejusdemque attributi conceptum involvunt (per Defin. 1 hujus). Deinde, quod jam tardius jam celerius, et absolute jam moveri jam quiescere possunt.","kind":"passage"},{"id":"RAW_QGOH_PSDN:P0130","text":"LEMMA III","kind":"passage"},{"id":"RAW_QGOH_PSDN:P0131","text":"Corpus motum vel quiescens ad motum vel quietem determinari debuit ab alio corpore, quod etiam ad motum vel quietem determinatum fuit ab alio, et illud iterum ab alio, et sic in infinitum.","kind":"passage"},{"id":"RAW_QGOH_PSDN:P0132","text":"DEMONSTRATIO","kind":"passage"},{"id":"RAW_QGOH_PSDN:P0133","text":"Corpora (per Defin. 1 hujus) res singulares sunt, quæ (per Lemma 1) ratione motus et quietis ab invicem distinguuntur ; adeoque (per Prop. 28 p. I) unumquodque ad motum vel quietem necessario determinari debuit ab alia re singulari, nempe (per Prop. 6 hujus) ab alio corpore, quod (per Axiom. 1) etiam vel movetur vel quiescit. At hoc etiam (per eandem rationem) moveri vel quiescere non potuit, nisi ab alio ad motum vel quietem determinatum fuisset, et hoc iterum (per eandem rationem) ab alio, et sic in infinitum. Q. E. D.","kind":"passage"},{"id":"RAW_QGOH_PSDN:P0134","text":"COROLLARIUM","kind":"passage"}],"sha256":"59597f0be2127c53f5adedf3c46e280887227533f0e2c4814e16906d709ac72c","language_code":"la","language_name":"Latina","translations":[{"id":"SOURCE_TR_20261015T012833153589Z_292b3a02","language_code":"en","language_name":"English","title":"Part Two - On the Nature and Origin of the Mind · passage 1","source_sha256":"59597f0be2127c53f5adedf3c46e280887227533f0e2c4814e16906d709ac72c","sha256":"0fce60992e0048ac7fb6ddd5adb9e82209c1b87409d093aa2417e9a81577e1e4","rendition_type":"machine","review_status":"machine","model":"gpt-5.6-terra","created":"2026-10-15T01:28:33.153626+00:00","paragraphs":[{"id":"RAW_QGOH_PSDN:P0001","text":"## ON THE NATURE AND ORIGIN OF THE MIND","kind":"passage","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_QGOH_PSDN:P0001"]},{"id":"RAW_QGOH_PSDN:P0002","text":"I now pass on to explain those things which had necessarily to follow from the essence of God, or the eternal and infinite Being. Not indeed all of them; for in Proposition 16, Part I, we demonstrated that infinitely many things must follow from it in infinitely many ways; but only those which can, as it were, lead us by the hand to the knowledge of the human Mind and of its highest blessedness.","kind":"passage","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_QGOH_PSDN:P0002"]},{"id":"RAW_QGOH_PSDN:P0003","text":"DEFINITIONS","kind":"passage","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_QGOH_PSDN:P0003"]},{"id":"RAW_QGOH_PSDN:P0004","text":"I. By body I understand a mode which expresses the essence of God, insofar as it is considered as an extended thing, in a certain and determinate manner; see Corollary to Proposition 25, Part I.","kind":"passage","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_QGOH_PSDN:P0004"]},{"id":"RAW_QGOH_PSDN:P0005","text":"II. I say that that pertains to the essence of a thing which, when given, necessarily posits the thing, and which, when removed, necessarily removes the thing; or that without which the thing, and conversely that which without the thing, can neither be nor be conceived.","kind":"passage","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_QGOH_PSDN:P0005"]},{"id":"RAW_QGOH_PSDN:P0006","text":"III. By idea I understand a conception of the Mind which the Mind forms because it is a thinking thing.","kind":"passage","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_QGOH_PSDN:P0006"]},{"id":"RAW_QGOH_PSDN:P0007","text":"EXPLANATION","kind":"passage","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_QGOH_PSDN:P0007"]},{"id":"RAW_QGOH_PSDN:P0008","text":"I say conception rather than perception, because the term perception seems to indicate that the Mind is acted upon by the object; whereas conception seems to express an action of the Mind.","kind":"passage","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_QGOH_PSDN:P0008"]},{"id":"RAW_QGOH_PSDN:P0009","text":"IV. By an adequate idea I understand an idea which, insofar as it is considered in itself without relation to the object, has all the properties, or intrinsic denominations, of a true idea.","kind":"passage","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_QGOH_PSDN:P0009"]},{"id":"RAW_QGOH_PSDN:P0010","text":"EXPLANATION","kind":"passage","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_QGOH_PSDN:P0010"]},{"id":"RAW_QGOH_PSDN:P0011","text":"I say intrinsic, in order to exclude that which is extrinsic, namely, the agreement of the idea with its ideate.","kind":"passage","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_QGOH_PSDN:P0011"]},{"id":"RAW_QGOH_PSDN:P0012","text":"V. Duration is an indefinite continuation of existing.","kind":"passage","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_QGOH_PSDN:P0012"]},{"id":"RAW_QGOH_PSDN:P0013","text":"EXPLANATION","kind":"passage","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_QGOH_PSDN:P0013"]},{"id":"RAW_QGOH_PSDN:P0014","text":"I say indefinite because it can in no way be determined through the nature itself of the existing thing, nor indeed by the efficient cause, which of course necessarily posits the existence of the thing, but does not remove it.","kind":"passage","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_QGOH_PSDN:P0014"]},{"id":"RAW_QGOH_PSDN:P0015","text":"VI. By reality and perfection I understand the same thing.","kind":"passage","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_QGOH_PSDN:P0015"]},{"id":"RAW_QGOH_PSDN:P0016","text":"VII. By singular things I understand things that are finite and have a determinate existence. But if several individuals concur in one action in such a way that they are all together the cause of one effect, I consider them all, to that extent, as one singular thing.","kind":"passage","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_QGOH_PSDN:P0016"]},{"id":"RAW_QGOH_PSDN:P0017","text":"AXIOMS","kind":"passage","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_QGOH_PSDN:P0017"]},{"id":"RAW_QGOH_PSDN:P0018","text":"I. The essence of man does not involve necessary existence; that is, from the order of Nature it can happen as much that this or that man exists as that he does not exist.","kind":"passage","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_QGOH_PSDN:P0018"]},{"id":"RAW_QGOH_PSDN:P0019","text":"II. Man thinks.","kind":"passage","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_QGOH_PSDN:P0019"]},{"id":"RAW_QGOH_PSDN:P0020","text":"III. Modes of thinking, such as love, desire, or whatever are designated by the name of affects of the mind, are not given unless in the same Individual there is given an idea of the thing loved, desired, etc. But an idea may be given, although no other mode of thinking is given.","kind":"passage","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_QGOH_PSDN:P0020"]},{"id":"RAW_QGOH_PSDN:P0021","text":"IV. We feel that a certain body is affected in many ways.","kind":"passage","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_QGOH_PSDN:P0021"]},{"id":"RAW_QGOH_PSDN:P0022","text":"V. We sense and perceive no singular things except bodies and modes of thinking.","kind":"passage","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_QGOH_PSDN:P0022"]},{"id":"RAW_QGOH_PSDN:P0023","text":"See the Postulates after Proposition 13.","kind":"passage","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_QGOH_PSDN:P0023"]},{"id":"RAW_QGOH_PSDN:P0024","text":"PROPOSITION I","kind":"passage","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_QGOH_PSDN:P0024"]},{"id":"RAW_QGOH_PSDN:P0025","text":"Thought is an attribute of God, or God is a thinking thing.","kind":"passage","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_QGOH_PSDN:P0025"]},{"id":"RAW_QGOH_PSDN:P0026","text":"DEMONSTRATION","kind":"passage","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_QGOH_PSDN:P0026"]},{"id":"RAW_QGOH_PSDN:P0027","text":"Singular thoughts, or this and that thought, are modes which express God's nature in a certain and determinate manner (by Corollary to Prop. 25, Part I). Therefore there belongs to God (by Def. 5, Part I) an attribute whose concept all singular thoughts involve, and through which they are also conceived. Therefore Thought is one of the infinite attributes of God, which expresses God's eternal and infinite essence (see Def. 6, Part I), or God is a thinking thing. Q. E. D.","kind":"passage","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_QGOH_PSDN:P0027"]},{"id":"RAW_QGOH_PSDN:P0028","text":"SCHOLIUM","kind":"passage","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_QGOH_PSDN:P0028"]},{"id":"RAW_QGOH_PSDN:P0029","text":"This Proposition is also evident from the fact that we can conceive an infinite thinking being. For the more things a thinking being can think, the more reality or perfection we conceive it to contain; therefore, a being which can think infinitely many things in infinitely many ways is necessarily infinite in virtue of thought. Since, therefore, by attending to thought alone, we conceive an infinite Being, Thought is necessarily (by Defs. 4 and 6, Part I) one of the infinite attributes of God, as we wished.","kind":"passage","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_QGOH_PSDN:P0029"]},{"id":"RAW_QGOH_PSDN:P0030","text":"PROPOSITION II","kind":"passage","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_QGOH_PSDN:P0030"]},{"id":"RAW_QGOH_PSDN:P0031","text":"Extension is an attribute of God, or God is an extended thing.","kind":"passage","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_QGOH_PSDN:P0031"]},{"id":"RAW_QGOH_PSDN:P0032","text":"DEMONSTRATION","kind":"passage","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_QGOH_PSDN:P0032"]},{"id":"RAW_QGOH_PSDN:P0033","text":"The demonstration of this proceeds in the same manner as the demonstration of the preceding Proposition.","kind":"passage","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_QGOH_PSDN:P0033"]},{"id":"RAW_QGOH_PSDN:P0034","text":"PROPOSITION III","kind":"passage","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_QGOH_PSDN:P0034"]},{"id":"RAW_QGOH_PSDN:P0035","text":"In God there is necessarily given an idea, both of his essence and of all things which necessarily follow from his essence.","kind":"passage","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_QGOH_PSDN:P0035"]},{"id":"RAW_QGOH_PSDN:P0036","text":"DEMONSTRATION","kind":"passage","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_QGOH_PSDN:P0036"]},{"id":"RAW_QGOH_PSDN:P0037","text":"For God (by Prop. 1 of this Part) can think infinitely many things in infinitely many ways, or (which is the same, by Prop. 16, Part I) can form an idea of his essence and of all things which necessarily follow from it. But whatever is in God's power necessarily is (by Prop. 35, Part I); therefore such an idea is necessarily given, and (by Prop. 15, Part I) not except in God. Q. E. D.","kind":"passage","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_QGOH_PSDN:P0037"]},{"id":"RAW_QGOH_PSDN:P0038","text":"SCHOLIUM","kind":"passage","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_QGOH_PSDN:P0038"]},{"id":"RAW_QGOH_PSDN:P0039","text":"By God's power the vulgar understand God's free will, and a right over all things that are, and which on that account are commonly regarded as contingent. For they say that God has the power to destroy all things and reduce them to nothing. Moreover, they very frequently compare God's power with the power of kings. But we refuted this in Corollaries 1 and 2 of Proposition 32 of Part I, and in Proposition 16 of Part I we showed that God acts by the same necessity by which he understands himself; that is, just as it follows from the necessity of the divine nature (as all unanimously maintain) that God understands himself, so by the same necessity it also follows that God acts in infinitely many ways upon infinitely many things. Further, in Proposition 34 of Part I we showed that God's power is nothing other than God's active essence; and therefore it is as impossible for us to conceive that God does not act as that God does not exist.","kind":"passage","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_QGOH_PSDN:P0039"]},{"id":"RAW_QGOH_PSDN:P0040","text":"Furthermore, if I were inclined to pursue these matters further, I could here show that that power which the vulgar ascribe to God is not only human (which shows that God is conceived by the vulgar as a man, or after the manner of a man), but also involves impotence. But I do not wish to discourse so often on the same matter. I only ask the reader again and again to ponder, once and again, what was said on this matter in the first part, from Proposition 16 to the end. For no one will be able rightly to perceive what I wish unless he takes great care not to confuse God's power with the human power or right of kings.","kind":"passage","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_QGOH_PSDN:P0040"]},{"id":"RAW_QGOH_PSDN:P0041","text":"PROPOSITION IV","kind":"passage","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_QGOH_PSDN:P0041"]},{"id":"RAW_QGOH_PSDN:P0042","text":"The idea of God, from which infinitely many things follow in infinitely many ways, can be only one.","kind":"passage","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_QGOH_PSDN:P0042"]},{"id":"RAW_QGOH_PSDN:P0043","text":"DEMONSTRATION","kind":"passage","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_QGOH_PSDN:P0043"]},{"id":"RAW_QGOH_PSDN:P0044","text":"The infinite intellect comprehends nothing except God's attributes and their affections (by Prop. 30, Part I). But God is unique (by Corollary 1 to Prop. 14, Part I). Therefore the idea of God, from which infinitely many things follow in infinitely many ways, can be only one. Q. E. D.","kind":"passage","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_QGOH_PSDN:P0044"]},{"id":"RAW_QGOH_PSDN:P0045","text":"PROPOSITION V","kind":"passage","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_QGOH_PSDN:P0045"]},{"id":"RAW_QGOH_PSDN:P0046","text":"The formal being of ideas acknowledges God, insofar as he is considered only as a thinking thing, as its cause, and not insofar as he is explained by another attribute. That is, the ideas both of God's attributes and of singular things acknowledge as their efficient cause not the ideata themselves, or the things perceived, but God himself, insofar as he is a thinking thing.","kind":"passage","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_QGOH_PSDN:P0046"]},{"id":"RAW_QGOH_PSDN:P0047","text":"DEMONSTRATION","kind":"passage","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_QGOH_PSDN:P0047"]},{"id":"RAW_QGOH_PSDN:P0048","text":"This is indeed evident from Proposition 3 of this Part. For there we concluded that God can form an idea of his essence and of all things which necessarily follow from it from this alone, namely, that God is a thinking thing, and not from the fact that he is the object of his own idea. Wherefore the formal being of ideas acknowledges God, insofar as he is a thinking thing, as its cause. But this is demonstrated in another way as follows. The formal being of ideas is a mode of thinking (as is known through itself), that is (by Corollary to Prop. 25, Part I), a mode which expresses God's nature, insofar as he is only a thinking thing, in a certain manner, and therefore (by Prop. 10, Part I) involves the concept of no other attribute of God, and consequently (by Axiom 4, Part I) is the effect of no attribute other than thought; therefore the formal being of ideas acknowledges God, insofar as he is considered only as a thinking thing, etc. Q. E. D.","kind":"passage","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_QGOH_PSDN:P0048"]},{"id":"RAW_QGOH_PSDN:P0049","text":"PROPOSITION VI","kind":"passage","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_QGOH_PSDN:P0049"]},{"id":"RAW_QGOH_PSDN:P0050","text":"The modes of any attribute have God, insofar as He is considered only under that attribute of which they are modes, and not insofar as He is considered under any other, as their cause.","kind":"passage","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_QGOH_PSDN:P0050"]},{"id":"RAW_QGOH_PSDN:P0051","text":"DEMONSTRATION","kind":"passage","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_QGOH_PSDN:P0051"]},{"id":"RAW_QGOH_PSDN:P0052","text":"For each attribute is conceived through itself without any other (by Prop. 10, Part I). Wherefore the modes of each attribute involve the concept of their own attribute, but not that of another; and consequently (by Axiom 4, Part I) they have God, insofar as He is considered under that attribute of which they are modes, and not insofar as He is considered under any other, as their cause. Q. E. D.","kind":"passage","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_QGOH_PSDN:P0052"]},{"id":"RAW_QGOH_PSDN:P0053","text":"COROLLARY","kind":"passage","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_QGOH_PSDN:P0053"]},{"id":"RAW_QGOH_PSDN:P0054","text":"Hence it follows that the formal being of things which are not modes of thinking does not follow from the divine nature because God first knew the things; but things ideated follow and are inferred from their attributes in the same manner and with the same necessity as we have shown that ideas follow from the attribute of Thought.","kind":"passage","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_QGOH_PSDN:P0054"]},{"id":"RAW_QGOH_PSDN:P0055","text":"PROPOSITION VII","kind":"passage","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_QGOH_PSDN:P0055"]},{"id":"RAW_QGOH_PSDN:P0056","text":"The order and connection of ideas is the same as the order and connection of things.","kind":"passage","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_QGOH_PSDN:P0056"]},{"id":"RAW_QGOH_PSDN:P0057","text":"DEMONSTRATION","kind":"passage","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_QGOH_PSDN:P0057"]},{"id":"RAW_QGOH_PSDN:P0058","text":"This is evident from Axiom 4, Part I. For the idea of any effect depends on the knowledge of the cause of which it is the effect.","kind":"passage","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_QGOH_PSDN:P0058"]},{"id":"RAW_QGOH_PSDN:P0059","text":"COROLLARY","kind":"passage","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_QGOH_PSDN:P0059"]},{"id":"RAW_QGOH_PSDN:P0060","text":"Hence it follows that God's power of thinking is equal to His actual power of acting. That is, whatever follows formally from the infinite nature of God, all this follows objectively in God from the idea of God in the same order and with the same connection.","kind":"passage","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_QGOH_PSDN:P0060"]},{"id":"RAW_QGOH_PSDN:P0061","text":"SCHOLIUM","kind":"passage","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_QGOH_PSDN:P0061"]},{"id":"RAW_QGOH_PSDN:P0062","text":"Here, before proceeding further, we must recall what we have shown above; namely, that whatever can be perceived by an infinite intellect as constituting the essence of substance pertains wholly to one substance alone, and consequently that thinking substance and extended substance are one and the same substance, which is now comprehended under this attribute, now under that. Thus also a mode of extension and the idea of that mode are one and the same thing, expressed in two ways; which certain of the Hebrews seem to have seen as if through a mist, namely those who maintain that God, God's intellect, and the things understood by Him are one and the same. For example,","kind":"passage","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_QGOH_PSDN:P0062"]},{"id":"RAW_QGOH_PSDN:P0063","text":"a circle existing in Nature, and the idea of the existing circle, which is also in God, are one and the same thing, which is explained through different attributes; and therefore, whether we conceive Nature under the attribute of Extension, or under the attribute of Thought, or under any other whatsoever, we shall find one and the same order, or one and the same connection of causes, that is, the same things, following one another. Nor did I say for any other reason that God is the cause of the idea, for example,","kind":"passage","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_QGOH_PSDN:P0063"]},{"id":"RAW_QGOH_PSDN:P0064","text":"of a circle insofar only as He is a thinking thing, and of the circle insofar only as He is an extended thing, except because the formal being of the idea of a circle can be perceived only through another mode of thinking as its proximate cause, and this again through another, and so on to infinity; so that, so long as things are considered as modes of thinking, we must explain the order of the whole of Nature, or the connection of causes, through the attribute of Thought alone; and insofar as they are considered as modes of Extension, the order also of the whole of Nature must be explained through the attribute of Extension alone; and I understand the same concerning the other attributes. Wherefore God is truly the cause of things as they are in themselves, insofar as He consists of infinite attributes; nor can I at present explain these matters more clearly.","kind":"passage","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_QGOH_PSDN:P0064"]},{"id":"RAW_QGOH_PSDN:P0065","text":"PROPOSITION VIII","kind":"passage","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_QGOH_PSDN:P0065"]},{"id":"RAW_QGOH_PSDN:P0066","text":"The ideas of singular things, or modes, that do not exist must be comprehended in God's infinite idea in the same way as the formal essences of singular things, or modes, are contained in God's attributes.","kind":"passage","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_QGOH_PSDN:P0066"]},{"id":"RAW_QGOH_PSDN:P0067","text":"DEMONSTRATION","kind":"passage","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_QGOH_PSDN:P0067"]},{"id":"RAW_QGOH_PSDN:P0068","text":"This Proposition is evident from the preceding one; but it is understood more clearly from the preceding Scholium.","kind":"passage","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_QGOH_PSDN:P0068"]},{"id":"RAW_QGOH_PSDN:P0069","text":"COROLLARY","kind":"passage","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_QGOH_PSDN:P0069"]},{"id":"RAW_QGOH_PSDN:P0070","text":"Hence it follows that, so long as singular things do not exist except insofar as they are comprehended in God's attributes, their objective being, or ideas, do not exist except insofar as God's infinite idea exists; and when singular things are said to exist, not only insofar as they are comprehended in God's attributes, but insofar also as they are said to endure, their ideas will likewise involve the existence through which they are said to endure.","kind":"passage","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_QGOH_PSDN:P0070"]},{"id":"RAW_QGOH_PSDN:P0071","text":"SCHOLIUM","kind":"passage","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_QGOH_PSDN:P0071"]},{"id":"RAW_QGOH_PSDN:P0072","text":"If anyone desires an example for a fuller explanation of this matter, I certainly shall not be able to give one that adequately explains the thing of which I here speak, since it is unique; nevertheless, I shall endeavour to illustrate the matter as far as possible. Namely, a circle is of such a nature that the rectangles made by the segments of all straight lines intersecting one another within it are equal to one another; wherefore infinitely many mutually equal rectangles are contained in a circle; yet none of them can be said to exist except insofar as the circle exists, nor can the idea of any one of these rectangles be said to exist except insofar as it is comprehended in the idea of the circle. Now let only two of those infinite [rectangles] be conceived to exist, namely E and D.","kind":"passage","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_QGOH_PSDN:P0072"]},{"id":"RAW_QGOH_PSDN:P0073","text":"Certainly, their ideas now exist not only insofar as they are comprehended merely in the idea of the circle, but also insofar as they involve the existence of those rectangles; whence it comes about that they are distinguished from the remaining ideas of the remaining rectangles.","kind":"passage","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_QGOH_PSDN:P0073"]},{"id":"RAW_QGOH_PSDN:P0074","text":"PROPOSITION IX","kind":"passage","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_QGOH_PSDN:P0074"]},{"id":"RAW_QGOH_PSDN:P0075","text":"The idea of a singular thing actually existing has God for its cause, not insofar as He is infinite, but insofar as He is considered as affected by another idea of a singular thing actually existing, of which also God is the cause insofar as He is affected by another third idea, and so on to infinity.","kind":"passage","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_QGOH_PSDN:P0075"]},{"id":"RAW_QGOH_PSDN:P0076","text":"DEMONSTRATION","kind":"passage","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_QGOH_PSDN:P0076"]},{"id":"RAW_QGOH_PSDN:P0077","text":"The idea of a singular thing actually existing is a singular mode of thinking, and distinct from the rest (by the Corollary and Scholium of Prop. 8 of this Part); and therefore (by Prop. 6 of this Part) it has God, insofar as He is only a thinking thing, for its cause. But not (by Prop. 28, Part I) insofar as He is a thing thinking absolutely, but insofar as He is considered as affected by another mode of thinking; and God is also the cause of this, insofar as He is affected by another mode, and so on to infinity. But the order and connection of ideas (by Prop. 7 of this Part) is the same as the order and connection of causes; therefore, another idea, or God insofar as He is considered as affected by another idea, is the cause of one singular idea, and of this also insofar as He is affected by another idea, and so on to infinity. Q. E. D.","kind":"passage","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_QGOH_PSDN:P0077"]},{"id":"RAW_QGOH_PSDN:P0078","text":"COROLLARY","kind":"passage","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_QGOH_PSDN:P0078"]},{"id":"RAW_QGOH_PSDN:P0079","text":"Whatever occurs in the object of any singular idea, there is given in God a cognition of it, insofar as He has only the idea of that same object.","kind":"passage","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_QGOH_PSDN:P0079"]},{"id":"RAW_QGOH_PSDN:P0080","text":"DEMONSTRATION","kind":"passage","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_QGOH_PSDN:P0080"]},{"id":"RAW_QGOH_PSDN:P0081","text":"Whatever occurs in the object of any idea, there is given in God an idea of it (by Prop. 3 of this Part), not insofar as He is infinite, but insofar as He is considered as affected by another idea of a singular thing (by the preceding Prop.); but (by Prop. 7 of this Part) the order and connection of ideas is the same as the order and connection of things; therefore the cognition of that which occurs in some singular object will be in God, insofar as He has only the idea of that same object. Q. E. D.","kind":"passage","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_QGOH_PSDN:P0081"]},{"id":"RAW_QGOH_PSDN:P0082","text":"PROPOSITION X","kind":"passage","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_QGOH_PSDN:P0082"]},{"id":"RAW_QGOH_PSDN:P0083","text":"Being substance does not pertain to the essence of man; or substance does not constitute the form of man.","kind":"passage","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_QGOH_PSDN:P0083"]},{"id":"RAW_QGOH_PSDN:P0084","text":"DEMONSTRATION","kind":"passage","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_QGOH_PSDN:P0084"]},{"id":"RAW_QGOH_PSDN:P0085","text":"For the being of substance involves necessary existence (by Prop. 7, Part I). If, therefore, being substance pertained to the essence of man, then, substance being given, man would necessarily be given (by Def. 2 of this Part), and consequently man would necessarily exist—which (by Ax. 1 of this Part) is absurd. Therefore, etc. Q. E. D.","kind":"passage","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_QGOH_PSDN:P0085"]},{"id":"RAW_QGOH_PSDN:P0086","text":"SCHOLIUM","kind":"passage","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_QGOH_PSDN:P0086"]},{"id":"RAW_QGOH_PSDN:P0087","text":"This Proposition is also demonstrated from Proposition 5, Part I, namely, that two substances of the same nature are not given. But since several men can exist, therefore that which constitutes the form of man is not being substance. Moreover, this Proposition is evident from the remaining properties of substance, namely, that substance is by its nature infinite, immutable, indivisible, etc., as anyone can easily see.","kind":"passage","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_QGOH_PSDN:P0087"]},{"id":"RAW_QGOH_PSDN:P0088","text":"COROLLARY","kind":"passage","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_QGOH_PSDN:P0088"]},{"id":"RAW_QGOH_PSDN:P0089","text":"Hence it follows that the essence of man is constituted by certain modifications of God's attributes. For being substance (by the preceding Prop.) does not pertain to the essence of man. Therefore (by Prop. 15, Part I) it is something which is in God, and which can neither be nor be conceived without God, or (by the Corollary of Prop. 25, Part I) an affection, or mode, which expresses God's nature in a certain and determinate manner.","kind":"passage","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_QGOH_PSDN:P0089"]},{"id":"RAW_QGOH_PSDN:P0090","text":"SCHOLIUM","kind":"passage","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_QGOH_PSDN:P0090"]},{"id":"RAW_QGOH_PSDN:P0091","text":"Certainly, all must grant that nothing can be or be conceived without God. For it is admitted by all that God is the sole cause of all things, both of their essences and of their existences; that is, God is not only the cause of things according to becoming, as they say, but also according to being. Yet meanwhile most say that that pertains to the essence of some thing without which the thing can neither be nor be conceived; and therefore they either believe that the nature of God pertains to the essence of created things, or that created things can either be or be conceived without God, or, what is more certain, that they are not sufficiently consistent with themselves. I believe the cause of this was that they did not maintain the order of philosophizing.","kind":"passage","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_QGOH_PSDN:P0091"]},{"id":"RAW_QGOH_PSDN:P0092","text":"For they believed that the divine nature, which they ought to have contemplated before all else, because it is prior both in cognition and in nature, was last in the order of cognition, and that the things which are called objects of the senses were prior to all things; whence it came about that, while they contemplated natural things, they thought of nothing less than of the divine nature; and when afterward they had applied their mind to contemplating the divine nature, they could think of nothing less than of their first fictions, upon which they had built the cognition of natural things, since these could be of no assistance toward the cognition of the divine nature; and thus it is not at all surprising if they contradicted themselves everywhere. But I pass this over.","kind":"passage","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_QGOH_PSDN:P0092"]},{"id":"RAW_QGOH_PSDN:P0093","text":"For my intention here was only to give the reason why I did not say that that pertains to the essence of some thing without which the thing can neither be nor be conceived; namely, because singular things can neither be nor be conceived without God, and yet God does not pertain to their essence. But I said that that necessarily constitutes the essence of some thing, which, when given, posits the thing, and which, when removed, removes the thing; or that without which the thing, and conversely that which without the thing, can neither be nor be conceived.","kind":"passage","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_QGOH_PSDN:P0093"]},{"id":"RAW_QGOH_PSDN:P0094","text":"PROPOSITION XI","kind":"passage","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_QGOH_PSDN:P0094"]},{"id":"RAW_QGOH_PSDN:P0095","text":"The first thing which constitutes the actual being of the human Mind is nothing other than the idea of some singular thing actually existing.","kind":"passage","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_QGOH_PSDN:P0095"]},{"id":"RAW_QGOH_PSDN:P0096","text":"DEMONSTRATION","kind":"passage","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_QGOH_PSDN:P0096"]},{"id":"RAW_QGOH_PSDN:P0097","text":"The essence of man (by the Corollary of the preceding Proposition) is constituted by certain modes of God’s attributes; namely (by Axiom 2 of this Part), by modes of thinking, of all of which (by Axiom 3 of this Part) the idea is by nature prior, and, that being given, the remaining modes (to which, that is, the idea is by nature prior) must be in the same individual (by the same Axiom); and therefore an idea is the first thing that constitutes the being of the human Mind. But not the idea of a thing that does not exist. For then (by the Corollary of Proposition 8 of this Part) the idea itself could not be said to exist; therefore it will be the idea of a thing actually existing. But not of an infinite thing. For an infinite thing (by Propositions 21 and 22, Part I) must necessarily always exist; but this (by Axiom 1 of this Part) is absurd; therefore the first thing that constitutes the actual being of the human Mind is the idea of a singular thing actually existing. Q. E. D.","kind":"passage","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_QGOH_PSDN:P0097"]},{"id":"RAW_QGOH_PSDN:P0098","text":"COROLLARY","kind":"passage","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_QGOH_PSDN:P0098"]},{"id":"RAW_QGOH_PSDN:P0099","text":"From this it follows that the human Mind is a part of God’s infinite intellect; and consequently, when we say that the human Mind perceives this or that, we say nothing other than that God, not insofar as he is infinite, but insofar as he is explicated through the nature of the human Mind, or insofar as he constitutes the essence of the human Mind, has this or that idea; and when we say that God has this or that idea, not only insofar as he constitutes the nature of the human Mind, but insofar as he also has, together with the human Mind, the idea of another thing, then we say that the human Mind perceives the thing in part, or inadequately.","kind":"passage","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_QGOH_PSDN:P0099"]},{"id":"RAW_QGOH_PSDN:P0100","text":"SCHOLIUM","kind":"passage","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_QGOH_PSDN:P0100"]},{"id":"RAW_QGOH_PSDN:P0101","text":"Here, without doubt, Readers will hesitate, and devise many things that may cause delay; and for this reason I ask them to proceed with me at a slow pace, and not to pass judgment on these matters until they have read through everything.","kind":"passage","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_QGOH_PSDN:P0101"]},{"id":"RAW_QGOH_PSDN:P0102","text":"PROPOSITION XII","kind":"passage","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_QGOH_PSDN:P0102"]},{"id":"RAW_QGOH_PSDN:P0103","text":"Whatever occurs in the object of the idea constituting the human Mind must be perceived by the human Mind, or there will necessarily be an idea of that thing in the Mind. That is, if the object of the idea constituting the human Mind is a body, nothing can occur in that body which is not perceived by the Mind.","kind":"passage","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_QGOH_PSDN:P0103"]},{"id":"RAW_QGOH_PSDN:P0104","text":"DEMONSTRATION","kind":"passage","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_QGOH_PSDN:P0104"]},{"id":"RAW_QGOH_PSDN:P0105","text":"For whatever occurs in the object of any idea, knowledge of that thing is necessarily given in God (by the Corollary of Proposition 9 of this Part), insofar as he is considered as affected by the idea of the same object, that is (by Proposition 11 of this Part), insofar as he constitutes the mind of some thing. Therefore whatever occurs in the object of the idea constituting the human Mind, knowledge thereof is necessarily given in God insofar as he constitutes the nature of the human Mind, that is (by the Corollary of Proposition 11 of this Part), knowledge of that thing will necessarily be in the Mind, or the Mind perceives it. Q. E. D.","kind":"passage","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_QGOH_PSDN:P0105"]},{"id":"RAW_QGOH_PSDN:P0106","text":"SCHOLIUM","kind":"passage","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_QGOH_PSDN:P0106"]},{"id":"RAW_QGOH_PSDN:P0107","text":"This Proposition is also evident and understood more clearly from the Scholium of Proposition 7 of this Part, which see.","kind":"passage","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_QGOH_PSDN:P0107"]},{"id":"RAW_QGOH_PSDN:P0108","text":"PROPOSITION XIII","kind":"passage","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_QGOH_PSDN:P0108"]},{"id":"RAW_QGOH_PSDN:P0109","text":"The object of the idea constituting the human Mind is a Body, or a certain mode of Extension actually existing, and nothing else.","kind":"passage","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_QGOH_PSDN:P0109"]},{"id":"RAW_QGOH_PSDN:P0110","text":"DEMONSTRATION","kind":"passage","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_QGOH_PSDN:P0110"]},{"id":"RAW_QGOH_PSDN:P0111","text":"For if the Body were not the object of the human Mind, the ideas of the affections of the Body would not be in God (by the Corollary of Proposition 9 of this Part) insofar as he constituted our Mind, but insofar as he constituted the mind of another thing; that is (by the Corollary of Proposition 11 of this Part), the ideas of the affections of the Body would not be in our Mind. But (by Axiom 4 of this Part) we have ideas of the affections of the Body. Therefore the object of the idea constituting the human Mind is a Body, and one actually existing (by Proposition 11 of this Part). Further, if besides Body there were also another object of the Mind, since nothing exists (by Proposition 36, Part I) from which no effect follows, there would necessarily have to be (by Proposition 11 of this Part) in our Mind an idea of some effect of it; but (by Axiom 5 of this Part) no idea of it is given. Therefore the object of our Mind is an existing Body and nothing else. Q. E. D.","kind":"passage","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_QGOH_PSDN:P0111"]},{"id":"RAW_QGOH_PSDN:P0112","text":"COROLLARY","kind":"passage","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_QGOH_PSDN:P0112"]},{"id":"RAW_QGOH_PSDN:P0113","text":"From this it follows that man consists of Mind and Body, and that the human Body, as we sense it, exists.","kind":"passage","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_QGOH_PSDN:P0113"]},{"id":"RAW_QGOH_PSDN:P0114","text":"SCHOLIUM","kind":"passage","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_QGOH_PSDN:P0114"]},{"id":"RAW_QGOH_PSDN:P0115","text":"From these things we understand not only that the human Mind is united to the Body, but also what is to be understood by the union of Mind and Body. But no one will be able to understand that union adequately, or distinctly, unless he first knows adequately the nature of our Body. For the things we have shown thus far are very common, and pertain no more to human beings than to the remaining Individuals, all of which, though in different degrees, are nevertheless animate. For an idea of every thing is necessarily given in God, of which God is the cause, in the same manner as the idea of the human Body; and therefore whatever we have said of the idea of the human Body must necessarily be said of the idea of every thing.","kind":"passage","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_QGOH_PSDN:P0115"]},{"id":"RAW_QGOH_PSDN:P0116","text":"Nevertheless, neither can we deny that ideas differ among themselves, as their objects themselves do, and that one is more excellent than another and contains more reality, insofar as the object of the one is more excellent than the object of the other and contains more reality; and therefore, in order to determine how the human Mind differs from the rest, and how it excels the rest, it is necessary for us to know the nature of its object, as we have said, that is, of the human Body. But I can neither explain that here, nor is it necessary for the things that I wish to demonstrate. Yet this I say in general: the more a body is apt, beyond others, to act or to be acted upon in many ways at once, the more its Mind is apt, beyond others, to perceive many things at once; and the more the actions of one body depend upon that body alone, and the less other bodies concur with it in acting, the more apt its mind is to understand distinctly.","kind":"passage","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_QGOH_PSDN:P0116"]},{"id":"RAW_QGOH_PSDN:P0117","text":"And from these things we can know the excellence of one mind above others; then also see the cause why we have only a very confused knowledge of our Body, and many other things which I shall deduce from these in what follows. For this reason I have judged it worthwhile to explain and demonstrate these very things more accurately; for which purpose it is necessary to preface a few things concerning the nature of bodies.","kind":"passage","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_QGOH_PSDN:P0117"]},{"id":"RAW_QGOH_PSDN:P0118","text":"AXIOM I","kind":"passage","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_QGOH_PSDN:P0118"]},{"id":"RAW_QGOH_PSDN:P0119","text":"All bodies either move or are at rest.","kind":"passage","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_QGOH_PSDN:P0119"]},{"id":"RAW_QGOH_PSDN:P0120","text":"AXIOM II","kind":"passage","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_QGOH_PSDN:P0120"]},{"id":"RAW_QGOH_PSDN:P0121","text":"Every body is now moved more slowly, now more swiftly.","kind":"passage","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_QGOH_PSDN:P0121"]},{"id":"RAW_QGOH_PSDN:P0122","text":"LEMMA I","kind":"passage","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_QGOH_PSDN:P0122"]},{"id":"RAW_QGOH_PSDN:P0123","text":"Bodies are distinguished from one another by reason of motion and rest, swiftness and slowness, and not by reason of substance.","kind":"passage","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_QGOH_PSDN:P0123"]},{"id":"RAW_QGOH_PSDN:P0124","text":"DEMONSTRATION","kind":"passage","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_QGOH_PSDN:P0124"]},{"id":"RAW_QGOH_PSDN:P0125","text":"I assume the first part of this as self-evident. But that bodies are not distinguished by reason of substance is evident both from Prop. 5 and 8, pt. I. But more clearly from those things which have been said in the Schol. to Prop. 15, pt. I.","kind":"passage","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_QGOH_PSDN:P0125"]},{"id":"RAW_QGOH_PSDN:P0126","text":"LEMMA II","kind":"passage","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_QGOH_PSDN:P0126"]},{"id":"RAW_QGOH_PSDN:P0127","text":"All bodies agree in certain things.","kind":"passage","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_QGOH_PSDN:P0127"]},{"id":"RAW_QGOH_PSDN:P0128","text":"DEMONSTRATION","kind":"passage","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_QGOH_PSDN:P0128"]},{"id":"RAW_QGOH_PSDN:P0129","text":"For all bodies agree in this, that they involve the concept of one and the same attribute (by Def. 1 of this Part). Next, that they can now be moved more slowly, now more swiftly, and absolutely, now move, now be at rest.","kind":"passage","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_QGOH_PSDN:P0129"]},{"id":"RAW_QGOH_PSDN:P0130","text":"LEMMA III","kind":"passage","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_QGOH_PSDN:P0130"]},{"id":"RAW_QGOH_PSDN:P0131","text":"A body in motion or at rest must have been determined to motion or rest by another body, which likewise was determined to motion or rest by another, and that again by another, and so on to infinity.","kind":"passage","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_QGOH_PSDN:P0131"]},{"id":"RAW_QGOH_PSDN:P0132","text":"DEMONSTRATION","kind":"passage","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_QGOH_PSDN:P0132"]},{"id":"RAW_QGOH_PSDN:P0133","text":"Bodies (by Def. 1 of this Part) are singular things, which (by Lemma 1) are distinguished from one another by reason of motion and rest; and therefore (by Prop. 28, pt. I) each one must necessarily have been determined to motion or rest by another singular thing, namely (by Prop. 6 of this Part), by another body, which (by Axiom 1) likewise either moves or is at rest. But this too (by the same reasoning) could not move or be at rest unless it had been determined to motion or rest by another, and this again (by the same reasoning) by another, and so on to infinity. Q. E. D.","kind":"passage","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_QGOH_PSDN:P0133"]},{"id":"RAW_QGOH_PSDN:P0134","text":"COROLLARY","kind":"passage","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_QGOH_PSDN:P0134"]}],"source_digest":"e051f2a47a5a408ba3a4d05e8fdd4365860ee949b4f341be9408764574f7d15e","source_language_code":"la","original_title":"Pars secunda - De natura et origine mentis · passage 1"}],"passage_basis":"frozen-grounding","original_title":"Pars secunda - De natura et origine mentis · passage 1","title_language_code":"en"},{"id":"RAW_QGOH_PS_2","title":"Part Two - On the Nature and Origin of the Mind · passage 2","order":6,"chapter_id":"CH_QGOH_PS_2","text":"Hinc sequitur, corpus motum tamdiu moveri, donec ab alio corpore ad quiescendum determinetur ; et corpus quiescens tamdiu etiam quiescere, donec ab alio ad motum determinetur. Quod etiam per se notum est. Nam cum suppono, corpus ex. gr. A quiescere, nec ad alia corpora mota attendo, nihil de corpore A dicere potero, nisi quod quiescat. Quod si postea contingat, ut corpus A moveatur, id sane evenire non potuit ex eo, quod quiescebat ; ex eo enim nil aliud sequi poterat, quam ut corpus A quiesceret. Si contra supponatur A moveri, quotiescunque ad A tantum attendimus, nihil de eodem affirmare poterimus, nisi quod moveatur. Quod si postea contingat, ut A quiescat, id sane evenire etiam non potuit ex motu, quem habebat ; ex motu enim nihil aliud sequi poterat, quam ut A moveretur ; contingit itaque a re, quæ non erat in A, nempe a causa externa, a qua ad quiescendum determinatum fuit.\n\nAXIOMA I\n\nOmnes modi, quibus corpus aliquod ab alio afficitur corpore, ex natura corporis affecti et simul ex natura corporis afficientis sequuntur ; ita ut unum idemque corpus diversimode moveatur pro diversitate naturæ corporum moventium, et contra ut diversa corpora ab uno eodemque corpore diversimode moveantur.\n\nAXIOMA II\n\nCum corpus motum alteri quiescenti, quod dimovere nequit, impingit, reflectitur, ut moveri pergat, et angulus lineæ motus reflectionis cum plano corporis quiescentis, cui impegit, æqualis erit angulo, quem linea motus incidentiæ cum eodem plano efficit.\n\nAtque hæc de corporibus simplicissimis, quæ scilicet solo motu et quiete, celeritate et tarditate ab invicem distinguuntur : jam ad composita ascendamus.\n\nDEFINITIO\n\nCum corpora aliquot ejusdem aut diversæ magnitudinis a reliquis ita coercentur, ut invicem incumbant, vel si eodem aut diversis celeritatis gradibus moventur, ut motus suos invicem certa quadam ratione communicent, illa corpora invicem unita dicemus, et omnia simul unum corpus sive Individuum componere, quod a reliquis per hanc corporum unionem distinguitur.\n\nAXIOMA III\n\nQuo partes Individui, vel corporis compositi, secundum majores vel minores superficies sibi invicem incumbunt, eo difficilius vel facilius cogi possunt, ut situm suum mutent, et consequenter eo facilius vel difficilius effici potest, ut ipsum Individuum aliam figuram induat. Atque hinc corpora, quorum partes secundum magnas superficies invicem incumbunt, dura ; quorum autem partes secundum parvas, mollia ; et quorum denique partes inter se moventur, fluida vocabo.\n\nLEMMA IV\n\nSi corporis, sive Individui, quod ex pluribus corporibus componitur, quædam corpora segregentur, et simul totidem alia ejusdem naturæ eorum loco succedant, retinebit Individuum suam naturam uti antea, absque ulla ejus formæ mutatione.\n\nDEMONSTRATIO\n\nCorpora enim (per Lem. 1) ratione substantiæ non distinguuntur ; id autem, quod formam Individui constituit, in corporum unione (per Defin. præc.) consistit ; atqui hæc (per hypothesin), tametsi corporum continua fiat mutatio, retinetur : retinebit ergo Individuum, tam ratione substantiæ quam modi, suam naturam uti ante. Q. E. D.\n\nLEMMA V\n\nSi partes, Individuum componentes, majores minoresve evadant, ea tamen proportione, ut omnes eandem ut antea ad invicem motus et quietis rationem servent, retinebit itidem Individuum suam naturam ut antea, absque ulla formæ mutatione.\n\nDEMONSTRATIO\n\nHujus eadem est ac præcedentis Lemmatis.\n\nLEMMA VI\n\nSi corpora quædam, Individuum componentia, motum, quem versus unam partem habent, aliam versus flectere cogantur ; at ita ut motus suos continuare possint, atque invicem eadem qua antea ratione communicare ; retinebit itidem Individuum suam naturam, absque ulla formæ mutatione.\n\nDEMONSTRATIO\n\nper se patet. Id enim omne retinere supponitur, quod in ejusdem definitione formam ipsius constituere diximus.\n\nLEMMA VII\n\nRetinet præterea Individuum sic compositum suam naturam, sive id secundum totum moveatur sive quiescat, sive versus hanc sive versus illam partem moveatur, dummodo unaquæque pars motum suum retineat, eumque uti antea reliquis communicet.\n\nDEMONSTRATIO\n\nPatet ex ipsius definitione, quam vide ante Lem. 4.\n\nSCHOLIUM\n\nHis itaque videmus, qua ratione Individuum compositum possit multis modis affici, ejus nihilominus natura servata. Atque hucusque Individuum concepimus, quod non nisi ex corporibus, quæ solo motu et quiete, celeritate et tarditate inter se distinguuntur, hoc est, quod ex corporibus simplicissimis componitur. Quod si jam aliud concipiamus, ex pluribus diversæ naturæ Individuis compositum, idem pluribus aliis modis posse affici reperiemus, ipsius nihilominus natura servata. Nam quandoquidem ejus unaquæque pars ex pluribus corporibus est composita ; poterit ergo (per Lem. præced.) unaquæque pars, absque ulla ipsius naturæ mutatione, jam tardius jam celerius moveri, et consequenter motus suos citius vel tardius reliquis communicare. Quod si præterea tertium Individuorum genus, ex his secundis compositum, concipiamus, idem multis aliis modis affici posse reperiemus, absque ulla ejus formæ mutatione. Et si sic porro in infinitum pergamus, facile concipiemus, totam Naturam unum esse Individuum, cujus partes, hoc est omnia corpora, infinitis modis variant, absque ulla totius Individui mutatione. Atque hæc, si animus fuisset de corpore ex professo agere, prolixius explicare et demonstrare debuissem. Sed jam dixi me aliud velle, nec alia de causa hæc adferre, quam quia ex ipsis ea, quæ demonstrare constitui, facile possum deducere.\n\nPOSTULATA\n\nI. Corpus humanum componitur ex plurimis (diversæ naturæ) individuis, quorum unumquodque valde compositum est.\n\nII. Individuorum, ex quibus Corpus humanum componitur, quædam fluida, quædam mollia, et quædam denique dura sunt.\n\nIII. Individua Corpus humanum componentia, et consequenter ipsum humanum Corpus a corporibus externis plurimis modis afficitur.\n\nIV. Corpus humanum indiget, ut conservetur, plurimis aliis corporibus, a quibus continuo quasi regeneratur.\n\nV. Cum Corporis humani pars fluida a corpore externo determinatur, ut in aliam mollem sæpe impingat, ejus planum mutat, et veluti quædam corporis externi impellentis vestigia eidem imprimit. VI. Corpus humanum potest corpora externa plurimis modis movere, plurimisque modis disponere.\n\nPROPOSITIO XIV\n\nMens humana apta est ad plurima percipiendum, et eo aptior, quo ejus Corpus pluribus modis disponi potest.\n\nDEMONSTRATIO\n\nCorpus enim humanum (per Post. 3 et 6) plurimis modis a corporibus externis afficitur, disponiturque ad corpora externa plurimis modis afficiendum. At omnia, quæ in Corpore humano contingunt (per Prop. 12 hujus), Mens humana percipere debet ; est ergo Mens humana apta ad plurima percipiendum, et eo aptior, etc. Q. E. D.\n\nPROPOSITIO XV\n\nIdea, quæ esse formale humanæ Mentis constituit, non est simplex, sed ex plurimis ideis composita.\n\nDEMONSTRATIO\n\nIdea, quæ esse formale humanæ Mentis constituit, est idea Corporis (per Prop. 13 hujus), quod (per Post. 1) ex plurimis valde compositis Individuis componitur. At cujuscunque Individui, corpus componentis, datur necessario (per Coroll. Prop. 8 hujus) in Deo idea ; ergo (per Prop. 7 hujus) idea Corporis humani ex plurimis hisce partium componentium ideis est composita. Q. E. D.\n\nPROPOSITIO XVI\n\nIdea cujuscunque modi, quo Corpus humanum a corporibus externis afficitur, involvere debet naturam Corporis humani, et simul naturam corporis externi.\n\nDEMONSTRATIO\n\nOmnes enim modi, quibus corpus aliquod afficitur, ex natura corporis affecti, et simul ex natura corporis afficientis sequuntur (per Axiom. 1 post. Coroll. Lem. 3) : quare eorum idea (per Axiom. 4 p. I), utriusque corporis naturam necessario involvet ; adeoque idea cujuscunque modi, quo Corpus humanum a corpore externo afficitur, Corporis humani et corporis externi naturam involvit. Q. E. D.\n\nCOROLLARIUM I\n\nHinc sequitur primo, Mentem humanam plurimorum corporum naturam una cum sui corporis natura percipere.\n\nCOROLLARIUM II\n\nSequitur secundo, quod ideæ, quas corporum externorum habemus magis nostri corporis constitutionem quam corporum externorum naturam indicant ; quod in Appendice partis primæ multis exemplis explicuit.\n\nPROPOSITIO XVII\n\nSi humanum Corpus affectum est modo, qui naturam Corporis alicujus externi involvit, Mens humana idem corpus externum ut actu existens, vel ut sibi præsens contemplabitur, donec Corpus afficiatur affectu, qui ejusdem corporis existentiam vel præsentiam secludat.\n\nDEMONSTRATIO\n\npatet. Nam quandiu Corpus humanum sic affectum est tamdiu Mens humana (per Prop. 12 hujus) hanc corporis affectionem contemplabitur ; hoc est (per Prop. præced.) ideam habebit modi actu existentis, quæ naturam corporis externi involvit ; hoc est ideam, quæ existentiam vel præsentiam naturæ corporis externi non secludit, sed ponit ; adeoque Mens (per Coroll. 1 præced.) corpus externum ut actu existens, vel ut præsens contemplabitur, donec afficiatur etc. Q. E. D.\n\nCOROLLARIUM\n\nMens corpora externa, a quibus Corpus humanum semel affectum fuit, quamvis non existant nec præsentia sint, contemplari tamen poterit, velut præsentia essent.\n\nDEMONSTRATIO\n\nDum corpora externa Corporis humani partes fluidas ita déterminant, ut in molliores sæpe impingant, earum plana (per Post. 5) mutant ; unde fit (vide Axiom. 2 post Coroll. Lem. 3) ut inde alio modo reflectantur quam antea solebant, et ut etiam postea, iisdem novis planis spontaneo suo motu occurrendo, eodem modo reflectantur, ac cum a corporibus externis versus illa plana impuilsæ sunt ; et consequenter, ut Corpus humanum, dum sic reflexæ moveri pergunt, eodem modo afficiant, de quo Mens (per Prop. 12 hujus) iterum cogitabit ; hoc est (per Prop. 17 hujus), Mens iterum corpus externum ut præsens contemplabitur ; et hoc toties, quoties Corporis humani partes fluidæ spontaneo suo motu iisdem planis occurrent. Quare, quamvis corpora externa, a quibus Corpus humanum affectum semel fuit, non existant, Mens tamen eadem toties ut præsentia contemplabitur, quoties hæc corporis actio repetetur. Q. E. D.\n\nSCHOLIUM\n\nVidemus itaque qui fieri potest ut ea, quæ non sunt, veluti præsentia contemplemur, ut sæpe fit. Et fieri potest, ut hoc aliis de causis contingat ; sed mihi hic sufficit ostendisse unam, per quam rem sic possim explicare, ac si ipsam per veram causam ostendissem ; nec tamen credo me a vera longe aberrare, quandoquidem omnia illa, quæ sumpsi, postulata vix quicquam continent, quod non constet experientia, de qua nobis non licet dubitare, postquam ostendimus Corpus humanum, prout ipsum sentimus, existere (vide Coroll. post Prop. 13 hujus). Præterea (ex Coroll. præced. et Coroll. 2 Prop. 16 hujus) clare intelligimus, quænam sit differenlia inter ideam ex. gr. Petri, quæ essentiam Mentis ipsius Petri constituit, et inter ideam ipsius Petri quæ in alio homine, puta in Paulo est. Illa enim essentiam Corporis ipsius Petri directe explicat, nec existentiam involvit, nisi quamdiu Petrus existit ; hæc autem magis constitutionem Corporis Pauli quam Petri naturam indicat, et ideo, durante illa Corporis Pauli constitutione, Mens Pauli, quamvis Petrus non existat, ipsum tamen ut sibi præsentem contemplabitur. Porro, ut verba usitata retineamus, Corporis humani affectiones, quarum ideæ corpora, externa velut nobis præsentia repræsentant, rerum imagines vocabimus, tametsi rerum figuras non referunt. Et cum Mens hac ratione contemplatur corpora, eandem imaginari dicemus. Atque hic, ut quid sit error indicare incipiam, notetis velim, Mentis imaginationes in se spectatas nihil erroris continere ; sive Mentem ex eo, quod imaginatur, non errare ; sed tantum quatenus consideratur carere idea, quæ existentiam illarum rerum, quas sibi præsentes imaginatur, secludat. Nam si Mens, dum res non existentes ut sibi præsentes imaginatur, simul sciret, res illas revera non existere, hanc sane imaginandi potentiam virtuti suæ naturæ, non vitio tribueret ; præsertim si hæc imaginandi facultas a sola sua natura penderet, hoc est (per Defin. 7 p. I), si hæc Mentis imaginandi facultas libera esset.\n\nPROPOSITIO XVIII\n\nSi Corpus humanum a duobus vel pluribus corporibus simul affectum fuerit semel, ubi Mens postea eorum aliquod imaginabitur, statim et aliorum recordabitur.\n\nDEMONSTRATIO\n\nMens (per Coroll. præced.) corpus aliquod ea de causa imaginatur, quia scilicet humanum Corpus a corporis externi vestigiis eodem modo afficitur disponiturque, ac affectum est, cum quædam ejus partes ab ipso corpore externo fuerunt impulsæ : sed (per hypothesin) Corpus tum ita fuit dispositum, ut Mens duo simul corpora imaginaretur, ergo jam etiam duo simul imaginabitur, atque Mens ubi alterutrum imaginabitur, statim et alterius recordabitur. Q. E. D.\n\nSCHOLIUM\n\nHinc clare intelligimus, quid sit Memoria. Est enim nihil aliud quam quædam concatenatio idearum, naturam rerum, quæ extra Corpus humanum sunt, involventium, quæ in Mente fit secundum ordinem et concatenationem affectionum Corporis humani. Dico primo, concatenationem esse illarum tantum idearum, quæ naturam, rerum quæ extra Corpus humanum sunt, involvunt ; non autem idearum, quæ earundem rerum naturam explicant ; sunt enim revera (per Prop. 16 hujus) ideæ affectionum Corporis humani, quæ tam hujus quam corporum externorum naturam involvunt. Dico secundo, hanc concatenationem fieri secundum ordinem et concatenationem affectionum Corporis humani, ut ipsam distinguerem a concatenatione idearum, quæ fit secundum ordinem intellectus, quo res per primas suas causas Mens percipit, et qui in omnibus hominibus idem est. Atque hinc porro clare intelligimus, cur Mens ex cogitatione unius rei statim in alterius rei cogitationem incidat, quæ nullam cum priore habet similitudinem ; ut ex. gr. ex cogitatione vocis pomi homo Romanus statim in cogitationem fructus incidet qui nullam cum articulato illo sono habet similitudinem, nec aliquid commune, nisi quod ejusdem hominis Corpus ab his duobus affectum sæpe fuit ; hoc est, quod ipse homo sæpe vocem pomum audivit, dum ipsum fructum videret ; et sic unusquisque ex una in aliam cogitationem incidet, prout rerum imagines uniuscujusque consuetudo in corpore ordinavit. Nam miles ex. gr. visis in arena equi vestigiis statim ex cogitatione equi in cogitationem equitis, et inde in cogitationem belli, etc. incidet. At rusticus ex cogitatione equi in cogitationem aratri, agri, etc. incidet ; et sic unusquisque, prout rerum imagines consuevit hoc vel alio modo jungere et concatenare, ex una in hanc vel in aliam incidet cogitationem.\n\nPROPOSITIO XIX\n\nMens humana ipsum humanum Corpus non cognoscit, nec ipsum existere scit, nisi per ideas affectionum, quibus Corpus afficitur.\n\nDEMONSTRATIO\n\nMens enim humana est ipsa idea sive cognitio Corporis humani (per Prop. 13 hujus), quæ (per Prop. 9 hujus) in Deo quidem est, quatenus alia rei singularis idea affectus consideratur ; vel, quia (per Post. 4) Corpus humanum plurimis corporibus indiget, a quibus continuo quasi regeneratur ; et ordo et connexio idearum idem est (per Prop. 7 hujus) ac ordo et connexio causarum ; erit hæc idea in Deo, quatenus plurimarum rerum singularium ideis affectus consideratur. Deus itaque ideam Corporis humani habet, sive Corpus humanum cognoscit quatenus plurimis aliis ideis affectus est, et non quatenus naturam humanæ Mentis constituit, hoc est (per Coroll. Prop. 11 hujus), Mens humana Corpus humanum non cognoscit. At ideæ affectionum Corporis in Deo sunt, quatenus humanæ Mentis naturam constituit, sive Mens humana easdem affectiones percipit (per Prop. 12 hujus) ; et consequenter (per Prop. 16 hujus) ipsum Corpus humanum, idque (per Prop. 17 hujus) ut actu existens ; percipit ergo eatenus tantum Mens humana ipsum humanum Corpus. Q. E. D.\n\nPROPOSITIO XX\n\nMentis humanæ datur etiam in Deo idea sive cognitio, quæ in Deo eodem modo sequitur, et ad Deum eodem modo refertur, ac idea sive cognitio Corporis humani.\n\nDEMONSTRATIO\n\nCogitatio attributum Dei est (per Prop. 1 hujus) ; adeoque (per Prop. 3 hujus) tam ejus quam omnium ejus affectionum, et consequenter (per Prop. 11 hujus) Mentis etiam humanæ debet necessario in Deo dari idea. Deinde hæc Mentis idea sive cognitio non sequitur in Deo dari, quatenus infinitus, sed quatenus alia rei singularis idea affectus est (per Prop. 9 hujus). Sed ordo et connexio idearum idem est ac ordo et connexio causarum (per Prop. 7 hujus) ; sequitur ergo hæc Mentis idea sive cognitio in Deo, et ad Deum eodem modo refertur, ac idea sive cognitio Corporis. Q. E. D.\n\nPROPOSITIO XXI\n\nHæc Mentis idea eodem modo unita est Menti, ac ipsa Mens unita est Corpori.\n\nDEMONSTRATIO\n\nMentem unitam esse Corpori ex eo ostendimus, quod scilicet Corpus Mentis sit objectum (vide Prop. 12 et 13 hujus) : adeoque per eandem illam rationem idea Mentis cum suo obiecto, hoc est cum ipsa Mente, eodem modo unita esse debet, ac ipsa Mens unita est Corpori. Q. E. D.\n\nSCHOLIUM\n\nHæc Propositio longe clarius intelligitur ex dictis in Schol. Prop. 7 hujus ; ibi enim ostendimus Corporis ideam et Corpus, hoc est (per Prop. 13 hujus) Mentem et Corpus, unum et idem esse Individuum, quod jam sub Cogitationis jam sub Extensionis attributo concipitur ; quare Mentis idea et ipsa Mens una eademque est res, quæ sub uno eodemque attributo, nempe Cogitationis, concipitur. Mentis, inquam, idea et ipsa Mens in Deo eadem necessitate ex eadem cogitandi potentia sequuntur dari. Nam revera idea Mentis, hoc est idea ideæ, nihil aliud est quam forma ideæ, quatenus hæc ut modus cogitandi, absque relatione ad objectum consideratur ; simul ac enim quis aliquid scit, eo ipso scit, se id scire, et simul scit, se scire, quod scit, et sic in infinitum. Sed de his postea\n\nPROPOSITIO XXII\n\nMens humana non tantum Corporis affectiones sed etiam harum affectionum ideas percipit.\n\nDEMONSTRATIO\n\nAffectionum idearum ideæ in Deo eodem modo sequuntur, et ad Deum eodem modo referuntur, ac ipsæ affectionum ideæ ; quod eodem modo demonstratur ac Propositio 20 hujus. At ideæ affectionum Corporis in Mente humana sunt (per Prop. 12 hujus), hoc est (per Coroll. Prop. 11 hujus) in Deo, quatenus humanæ Mentis essentiam constituit ; ergo harum idearum ideæ in Deo erunt, quatenus humanæ Mentis cognitionem sive ideam habet, hoc est (per Prop. 21 hujus) in ipsa Mente humana, quæ propterea non tantum Corporis affectiones, sed earum etiam ideas percipit. Q. E. D.\n\nPROPOSITIO XXIII\n\nMens se ipsam non cognoscit, nisi quatenus Corporis affectionum ideat percipit.\n\nDEMONSTRATIO\n\nMentis idea sive cognitio (per Prop. 20 hujus) in Deo eodem modo sequitur, et ad Deum eodem modo refertur, ac Corporis idea sive cognitio. At quoniam (per Prop. 19 hujus) Mens humana ipsum humanum Corpus non cognoscit ; hoc est (per Coroll. Prop. 11 hujus) quoniam cognitio Corporis humani ad Deum non refertur, quatenus humanæ Mentis naturam constituit ; ergo nec cognitio Mentis ad Deum refertur, quatenus essentiam Mentis humanæ constituit ; atque adeo (per idem Coroll. Prop. 11 hujus) Mens humana eatenus se ipsam non cognoscit. Deinde affectionum, quibus Corpus afficitur, ideæ naturam ipsius Corporis humani involvunt (per Prop. 16 hujus), hoc est (per Prop. 13 hujus) cum natura Mentis conveniunt ; quare harum idearum cognitio cognitionem Mentis necessario involvet : at (per Prop. præced.) harum idearum cognitio in ipsa humana Mente est ; ergo Mens humana eatenus tantum se ipsam novit. Q. E. D.\n\nPROPOSITIO XXIV\n\nMens humana partium Corpus humanum componentium adæquatam cognitionem non involvit.\n\nDEMONSTRATIO\n\nPartes Corpus humanum componentes ad essentiam ipsius Corporis non pertinent, nisi quatenus motus suos certa quadam ratione invicem communicant (vide Defin. post Coroll. Lemmat. 3), et non quatenus ut Individua, absque relatione ad humanum Corpus, considerari possunt. Sunt enim partes humani Corporis (per Post 1) valde composita Individua, quorum partes (per Lem. 4) a Corpore humano, servata omnino ejusdem natura et forma, segregari possunt, motusque suos (vide Axiom. 1 post Lem. 3) aliis corporibus alia ratione communicare ; adeoque (per Prop. 3 hujus) cujuscunque partis idea sive cognitio in Deo erit, et quidem (per Prop. 9 hujus) quatenus affectus consideratur alia idea rei singularis, quæ res singularis ipsa parte ordine Naturæ prior est (per Prop. 7 hujus). Quod idem præterea etiam de quacunque parte ipsius Individui Corpus humanum componentis est dicendum ; adeoque cujuscunque partis Corpus humanum componentis cognitio in Deo est, quatenus plurimis rerum ideis affectus est, et non quatenus Corporis humani tantum habet ideam, hoc est (per Prop. 13 hujus) ideam, quæ humanæ Mentis naturam constituit ; atque adeo (per Coroll. Prop. 11 hujus) humana Mens partium Corpus humanum componentium adæquatam cognitionem non involvit. Q. E. D.\n\nPROPOSITXO XXV\n\nIdea cujuscunque affectionis Corporis humani adæquatam corporis externi cognitionem non involvit.\n\nDEMONSTRATIO\n\nIdeam affectionis Corporis humani eatenus corporis externi naturam involvere ostendimus (vide Prop. 16 hujus), quatenus externum ipsum humanum Corpus certo quodam modo determinat. At quatenus externum corpus Individuum est, quod ad Corpus humanum non refertur, ejus idea sive cognitio in Deo est (per Prop. 9 hujus), quatenus Deus affectus consideratur alterius rei idea quæ (per Prop. 7 hujus) ipso corpore externo prior est natura. Quare corporis externi adæquata cognitio in Deo non est, quatenus ideam affectionis humani Corporis habet, sive idea affectionis Corporis humani adæquatam corporis externi cognitionem non involvit. Q. E. D.\n\nPROPOSITIO XXVI\n\nMens humana nullum corpus externum ut actu existens percipit, nisi per ideas affectionum sui Corporis.\n\nDEMONSTRATIO\n\nSi a corpore aliquo externo Corpus humanum nullo modo affectum est ; ergo (per Prop. 7 hujus) nec idea Corporis humani, hoc est (per Prop. 13 hujus) nec Mens humana, idea existentiæ illius corporis ullo etiam modo affecta est, sive existentiam illius corporis externi ullo modo percipit. At quatenus Corpus humanum a corpore aliquo externo aliquo modo afficitur, eatenus (per Prop. 16 hujus cum Coroll. ejusdem) corpus externum percipit. Q. E. D.\n\nCOROLLARIUM\n\nQuatenus Mens humana corpus externum imaginatur, eatenus adæquatam ejus cognitionem non habet.\n\nDEMONSTRATIO\n\nCum Mens humana per ideas affectionum sui Corporis corpora externa contemplatur, eandem tum imaginari dicimus (vide Schol. Prop. 17 hujus) ; nec Mens alia ratione (per Prop. præced.) corpora externa ut actu existentia imaginari potest. Atque adeo (per Prop. 25 hujus) quatenus Mens corpora externa imaginatur, eorum adæquatam cognitionem non habet. Q. E. D.\n\nPROPOSITIO XXVII\n\nIdea cujuscunque affectionis Corporis humani adæquatam ipsius humani Corporis cognitionem non involvit.\n\nDEMONSTRATIO\n\nQuælibet idea cujuscunque affectionis humani Corporis eatenus naturam Corporis humani involvit, quatenus ipsum humanum Corpus certo quodam modo affici consideratur (vide Prop. 16 hujus). At quatenus Corpus humanum Individuum est, quod multis aliis modis affici potest, ejus idea, etc. (Vide Demonst. Prop. 25 hujus).\n\nPROPOSITIO XXVIII\n\nIdeæ affectionum Corporis humani, quatenus ad humanam Mentem tantum referunt, non sunt clari et distinctæ, sed confusæ.\n\nDEMONSTRATIO\n\nIdeæ enim affectionum Corporis humani tam corporum externorum, quam ipsius humani Corporis naturam involvunt (per Prop. 16 hujus) ; nec tantum Corporis humani, sed ejus etiam partium naturam involvere debent ; affectiones namque modi sunt (per Post. 3), quibus partes Corporis humani, et consequenter totum Corpus afficitur. At (per Prop. 24 et 25 hujus) corporum externorum adæquata cognitio, ut et partium Corpus humanum componentium, in Deo non est, quatenus humana Mente, sed quatenus aliis ideis affectus consideratur. Sunt ergo hæ affectionum ideæ, quatenus ad solam humanam Mentem referuntur, veluti consequentiæ absque præmissis, hoc est (ut per se notum) ideæ confusæ. Q. E. D.\n\nSCHOLIUM","paragraphs":[{"id":"RAW_QGOH_PS_2:P0001","text":"Hinc sequitur, corpus motum tamdiu moveri, donec ab alio corpore ad quiescendum determinetur ; et corpus quiescens tamdiu etiam quiescere, donec ab alio ad motum determinetur. Quod etiam per se notum est. Nam cum suppono, corpus ex. gr. A quiescere, nec ad alia corpora mota attendo, nihil de corpore A dicere potero, nisi quod quiescat. Quod si postea contingat, ut corpus A moveatur, id sane evenire non potuit ex eo, quod quiescebat ; ex eo enim nil aliud sequi poterat, quam ut corpus A quiesceret. Si contra supponatur A moveri, quotiescunque ad A tantum attendimus, nihil de eodem affirmare poterimus, nisi quod moveatur. Quod si postea contingat, ut A quiescat, id sane evenire etiam non potuit ex motu, quem habebat ; ex motu enim nihil aliud sequi poterat, quam ut A moveretur ; contingit itaque a re, quæ non erat in A, nempe a causa externa, a qua ad quiescendum determinatum fuit.","kind":"passage"},{"id":"RAW_QGOH_PS_2:P0002","text":"AXIOMA I","kind":"passage"},{"id":"RAW_QGOH_PS_2:P0003","text":"Omnes modi, quibus corpus aliquod ab alio afficitur corpore, ex natura corporis affecti et simul ex natura corporis afficientis sequuntur ; ita ut unum idemque corpus diversimode moveatur pro diversitate naturæ corporum moventium, et contra ut diversa corpora ab uno eodemque corpore diversimode moveantur.","kind":"passage"},{"id":"RAW_QGOH_PS_2:P0004","text":"AXIOMA II","kind":"passage"},{"id":"RAW_QGOH_PS_2:P0005","text":"Cum corpus motum alteri quiescenti, quod dimovere nequit, impingit, reflectitur, ut moveri pergat, et angulus lineæ motus reflectionis cum plano corporis quiescentis, cui impegit, æqualis erit angulo, quem linea motus incidentiæ cum eodem plano efficit.","kind":"passage"},{"id":"RAW_QGOH_PS_2:P0006","text":"Atque hæc de corporibus simplicissimis, quæ scilicet solo motu et quiete, celeritate et tarditate ab invicem distinguuntur : jam ad composita ascendamus.","kind":"passage"},{"id":"RAW_QGOH_PS_2:P0007","text":"DEFINITIO","kind":"passage"},{"id":"RAW_QGOH_PS_2:P0008","text":"Cum corpora aliquot ejusdem aut diversæ magnitudinis a reliquis ita coercentur, ut invicem incumbant, vel si eodem aut diversis celeritatis gradibus moventur, ut motus suos invicem certa quadam ratione communicent, illa corpora invicem unita dicemus, et omnia simul unum corpus sive Individuum componere, quod a reliquis per hanc corporum unionem distinguitur.","kind":"passage"},{"id":"RAW_QGOH_PS_2:P0009","text":"AXIOMA III","kind":"passage"},{"id":"RAW_QGOH_PS_2:P0010","text":"Quo partes Individui, vel corporis compositi, secundum majores vel minores superficies sibi invicem incumbunt, eo difficilius vel facilius cogi possunt, ut situm suum mutent, et consequenter eo facilius vel difficilius effici potest, ut ipsum Individuum aliam figuram induat. Atque hinc corpora, quorum partes secundum magnas superficies invicem incumbunt, dura ; quorum autem partes secundum parvas, mollia ; et quorum denique partes inter se moventur, fluida vocabo.","kind":"passage"},{"id":"RAW_QGOH_PS_2:P0011","text":"LEMMA IV","kind":"passage"},{"id":"RAW_QGOH_PS_2:P0012","text":"Si corporis, sive Individui, quod ex pluribus corporibus componitur, quædam corpora segregentur, et simul totidem alia ejusdem naturæ eorum loco succedant, retinebit Individuum suam naturam uti antea, absque ulla ejus formæ mutatione.","kind":"passage"},{"id":"RAW_QGOH_PS_2:P0013","text":"DEMONSTRATIO","kind":"passage"},{"id":"RAW_QGOH_PS_2:P0014","text":"Corpora enim (per Lem. 1) ratione substantiæ non distinguuntur ; id autem, quod formam Individui constituit, in corporum unione (per Defin. præc.) consistit ; atqui hæc (per hypothesin), tametsi corporum continua fiat mutatio, retinetur : retinebit ergo Individuum, tam ratione substantiæ quam modi, suam naturam uti ante. Q. E. D.","kind":"passage"},{"id":"RAW_QGOH_PS_2:P0015","text":"LEMMA V","kind":"passage"},{"id":"RAW_QGOH_PS_2:P0016","text":"Si partes, Individuum componentes, majores minoresve evadant, ea tamen proportione, ut omnes eandem ut antea ad invicem motus et quietis rationem servent, retinebit itidem Individuum suam naturam ut antea, absque ulla formæ mutatione.","kind":"passage"},{"id":"RAW_QGOH_PS_2:P0017","text":"DEMONSTRATIO","kind":"passage"},{"id":"RAW_QGOH_PS_2:P0018","text":"Hujus eadem est ac præcedentis Lemmatis.","kind":"passage"},{"id":"RAW_QGOH_PS_2:P0019","text":"LEMMA VI","kind":"passage"},{"id":"RAW_QGOH_PS_2:P0020","text":"Si corpora quædam, Individuum componentia, motum, quem versus unam partem habent, aliam versus flectere cogantur ; at ita ut motus suos continuare possint, atque invicem eadem qua antea ratione communicare ; retinebit itidem Individuum suam naturam, absque ulla formæ mutatione.","kind":"passage"},{"id":"RAW_QGOH_PS_2:P0021","text":"DEMONSTRATIO","kind":"passage"},{"id":"RAW_QGOH_PS_2:P0022","text":"per se patet. Id enim omne retinere supponitur, quod in ejusdem definitione formam ipsius constituere diximus.","kind":"passage"},{"id":"RAW_QGOH_PS_2:P0023","text":"LEMMA VII","kind":"passage"},{"id":"RAW_QGOH_PS_2:P0024","text":"Retinet præterea Individuum sic compositum suam naturam, sive id secundum totum moveatur sive quiescat, sive versus hanc sive versus illam partem moveatur, dummodo unaquæque pars motum suum retineat, eumque uti antea reliquis communicet.","kind":"passage"},{"id":"RAW_QGOH_PS_2:P0025","text":"DEMONSTRATIO","kind":"passage"},{"id":"RAW_QGOH_PS_2:P0026","text":"Patet ex ipsius definitione, quam vide ante Lem. 4.","kind":"passage"},{"id":"RAW_QGOH_PS_2:P0027","text":"SCHOLIUM","kind":"passage"},{"id":"RAW_QGOH_PS_2:P0028","text":"His itaque videmus, qua ratione Individuum compositum possit multis modis affici, ejus nihilominus natura servata. Atque hucusque Individuum concepimus, quod non nisi ex corporibus, quæ solo motu et quiete, celeritate et tarditate inter se distinguuntur, hoc est, quod ex corporibus simplicissimis componitur. Quod si jam aliud concipiamus, ex pluribus diversæ naturæ Individuis compositum, idem pluribus aliis modis posse affici reperiemus, ipsius nihilominus natura servata. Nam quandoquidem ejus unaquæque pars ex pluribus corporibus est composita ; poterit ergo (per Lem. præced.) unaquæque pars, absque ulla ipsius naturæ mutatione, jam tardius jam celerius moveri, et consequenter motus suos citius vel tardius reliquis communicare.","kind":"passage"},{"id":"RAW_QGOH_PS_2:P0029","text":"Quod si præterea tertium Individuorum genus, ex his secundis compositum, concipiamus, idem multis aliis modis affici posse reperiemus, absque ulla ejus formæ mutatione. Et si sic porro in infinitum pergamus, facile concipiemus, totam Naturam unum esse Individuum, cujus partes, hoc est omnia corpora, infinitis modis variant, absque ulla totius Individui mutatione. Atque hæc, si animus fuisset de corpore ex professo agere, prolixius explicare et demonstrare debuissem. Sed jam dixi me aliud velle, nec alia de causa hæc adferre, quam quia ex ipsis ea, quæ demonstrare constitui, facile possum deducere.","kind":"passage"},{"id":"RAW_QGOH_PS_2:P0030","text":"POSTULATA","kind":"passage"},{"id":"RAW_QGOH_PS_2:P0031","text":"I. Corpus humanum componitur ex plurimis (diversæ naturæ) individuis, quorum unumquodque valde compositum est.","kind":"passage"},{"id":"RAW_QGOH_PS_2:P0032","text":"II. Individuorum, ex quibus Corpus humanum componitur, quædam fluida, quædam mollia, et quædam denique dura sunt.","kind":"passage"},{"id":"RAW_QGOH_PS_2:P0033","text":"III. Individua Corpus humanum componentia, et consequenter ipsum humanum Corpus a corporibus externis plurimis modis afficitur.","kind":"passage"},{"id":"RAW_QGOH_PS_2:P0034","text":"IV. Corpus humanum indiget, ut conservetur, plurimis aliis corporibus, a quibus continuo quasi regeneratur.","kind":"passage"},{"id":"RAW_QGOH_PS_2:P0035","text":"V. Cum Corporis humani pars fluida a corpore externo determinatur, ut in aliam mollem sæpe impingat, ejus planum mutat, et veluti quædam corporis externi impellentis vestigia eidem imprimit. VI. Corpus humanum potest corpora externa plurimis modis movere, plurimisque modis disponere.","kind":"passage"},{"id":"RAW_QGOH_PS_2:P0036","text":"PROPOSITIO XIV","kind":"passage"},{"id":"RAW_QGOH_PS_2:P0037","text":"Mens humana apta est ad plurima percipiendum, et eo aptior, quo ejus Corpus pluribus modis disponi potest.","kind":"passage"},{"id":"RAW_QGOH_PS_2:P0038","text":"DEMONSTRATIO","kind":"passage"},{"id":"RAW_QGOH_PS_2:P0039","text":"Corpus enim humanum (per Post. 3 et 6) plurimis modis a corporibus externis afficitur, disponiturque ad corpora externa plurimis modis afficiendum. At omnia, quæ in Corpore humano contingunt (per Prop. 12 hujus), Mens humana percipere debet ; est ergo Mens humana apta ad plurima percipiendum, et eo aptior, etc. Q. E. D.","kind":"passage"},{"id":"RAW_QGOH_PS_2:P0040","text":"PROPOSITIO XV","kind":"passage"},{"id":"RAW_QGOH_PS_2:P0041","text":"Idea, quæ esse formale humanæ Mentis constituit, non est simplex, sed ex plurimis ideis composita.","kind":"passage"},{"id":"RAW_QGOH_PS_2:P0042","text":"DEMONSTRATIO","kind":"passage"},{"id":"RAW_QGOH_PS_2:P0043","text":"Idea, quæ esse formale humanæ Mentis constituit, est idea Corporis (per Prop. 13 hujus), quod (per Post. 1) ex plurimis valde compositis Individuis componitur. At cujuscunque Individui, corpus componentis, datur necessario (per Coroll. Prop. 8 hujus) in Deo idea ; ergo (per Prop. 7 hujus) idea Corporis humani ex plurimis hisce partium componentium ideis est composita. Q. E. D.","kind":"passage"},{"id":"RAW_QGOH_PS_2:P0044","text":"PROPOSITIO XVI","kind":"passage"},{"id":"RAW_QGOH_PS_2:P0045","text":"Idea cujuscunque modi, quo Corpus humanum a corporibus externis afficitur, involvere debet naturam Corporis humani, et simul naturam corporis externi.","kind":"passage"},{"id":"RAW_QGOH_PS_2:P0046","text":"DEMONSTRATIO","kind":"passage"},{"id":"RAW_QGOH_PS_2:P0047","text":"Omnes enim modi, quibus corpus aliquod afficitur, ex natura corporis affecti, et simul ex natura corporis afficientis sequuntur (per Axiom. 1 post. Coroll. Lem. 3) : quare eorum idea (per Axiom. 4 p. I), utriusque corporis naturam necessario involvet ; adeoque idea cujuscunque modi, quo Corpus humanum a corpore externo afficitur, Corporis humani et corporis externi naturam involvit. Q. E. D.","kind":"passage"},{"id":"RAW_QGOH_PS_2:P0048","text":"COROLLARIUM I","kind":"passage"},{"id":"RAW_QGOH_PS_2:P0049","text":"Hinc sequitur primo, Mentem humanam plurimorum corporum naturam una cum sui corporis natura percipere.","kind":"passage"},{"id":"RAW_QGOH_PS_2:P0050","text":"COROLLARIUM II","kind":"passage"},{"id":"RAW_QGOH_PS_2:P0051","text":"Sequitur secundo, quod ideæ, quas corporum externorum habemus magis nostri corporis constitutionem quam corporum externorum naturam indicant ; quod in Appendice partis primæ multis exemplis explicuit.","kind":"passage"},{"id":"RAW_QGOH_PS_2:P0052","text":"PROPOSITIO XVII","kind":"passage"},{"id":"RAW_QGOH_PS_2:P0053","text":"Si humanum Corpus affectum est modo, qui naturam Corporis alicujus externi involvit, Mens humana idem corpus externum ut actu existens, vel ut sibi præsens contemplabitur, donec Corpus afficiatur affectu, qui ejusdem corporis existentiam vel præsentiam secludat.","kind":"passage"},{"id":"RAW_QGOH_PS_2:P0054","text":"DEMONSTRATIO","kind":"passage"},{"id":"RAW_QGOH_PS_2:P0055","text":"patet. Nam quandiu Corpus humanum sic affectum est tamdiu Mens humana (per Prop. 12 hujus) hanc corporis affectionem contemplabitur ; hoc est (per Prop. præced.) ideam habebit modi actu existentis, quæ naturam corporis externi involvit ; hoc est ideam, quæ existentiam vel præsentiam naturæ corporis externi non secludit, sed ponit ; adeoque Mens (per Coroll. 1 præced.) corpus externum ut actu existens, vel ut præsens contemplabitur, donec afficiatur etc. Q. E. D.","kind":"passage"},{"id":"RAW_QGOH_PS_2:P0056","text":"COROLLARIUM","kind":"passage"},{"id":"RAW_QGOH_PS_2:P0057","text":"Mens corpora externa, a quibus Corpus humanum semel affectum fuit, quamvis non existant nec præsentia sint, contemplari tamen poterit, velut præsentia essent.","kind":"passage"},{"id":"RAW_QGOH_PS_2:P0058","text":"DEMONSTRATIO","kind":"passage"},{"id":"RAW_QGOH_PS_2:P0059","text":"Dum corpora externa Corporis humani partes fluidas ita déterminant, ut in molliores sæpe impingant, earum plana (per Post. 5) mutant ; unde fit (vide Axiom. 2 post Coroll. Lem. 3) ut inde alio modo reflectantur quam antea solebant, et ut etiam postea, iisdem novis planis spontaneo suo motu occurrendo, eodem modo reflectantur, ac cum a corporibus externis versus illa plana impuilsæ sunt ; et consequenter, ut Corpus humanum, dum sic reflexæ moveri pergunt, eodem modo afficiant, de quo Mens (per Prop. 12 hujus) iterum cogitabit ; hoc est (per Prop. 17 hujus), Mens iterum corpus externum ut præsens contemplabitur ; et hoc toties, quoties Corporis humani partes fluidæ spontaneo suo motu iisdem planis occurrent. Quare, quamvis corpora externa, a quibus Corpus humanum affectum semel fuit, non existant, Mens tamen eadem toties ut præsentia contemplabitur, quoties hæc corporis actio repetetur.","kind":"passage"},{"id":"RAW_QGOH_PS_2:P0060","text":"Q. E. D.","kind":"passage"},{"id":"RAW_QGOH_PS_2:P0061","text":"SCHOLIUM","kind":"passage"},{"id":"RAW_QGOH_PS_2:P0062","text":"Videmus itaque qui fieri potest ut ea, quæ non sunt, veluti præsentia contemplemur, ut sæpe fit. Et fieri potest, ut hoc aliis de causis contingat ; sed mihi hic sufficit ostendisse unam, per quam rem sic possim explicare, ac si ipsam per veram causam ostendissem ; nec tamen credo me a vera longe aberrare, quandoquidem omnia illa, quæ sumpsi, postulata vix quicquam continent, quod non constet experientia, de qua nobis non licet dubitare, postquam ostendimus Corpus humanum, prout ipsum sentimus, existere (vide Coroll. post Prop. 13 hujus). Præterea (ex Coroll. præced. et Coroll. 2 Prop. 16 hujus) clare intelligimus, quænam sit differenlia inter ideam ex. gr. Petri, quæ essentiam Mentis ipsius Petri constituit, et inter ideam ipsius Petri quæ in alio homine, puta in Paulo est.","kind":"passage"},{"id":"RAW_QGOH_PS_2:P0063","text":"Illa enim essentiam Corporis ipsius Petri directe explicat, nec existentiam involvit, nisi quamdiu Petrus existit ; hæc autem magis constitutionem Corporis Pauli quam Petri naturam indicat, et ideo, durante illa Corporis Pauli constitutione, Mens Pauli, quamvis Petrus non existat, ipsum tamen ut sibi præsentem contemplabitur. Porro, ut verba usitata retineamus, Corporis humani affectiones, quarum ideæ corpora, externa velut nobis præsentia repræsentant, rerum imagines vocabimus, tametsi rerum figuras non referunt. Et cum Mens hac ratione contemplatur corpora, eandem imaginari dicemus. Atque hic, ut quid sit error indicare incipiam, notetis velim, Mentis imaginationes in se spectatas nihil erroris continere ; sive Mentem ex eo, quod imaginatur, non errare ; sed tantum quatenus consideratur carere idea, quæ existentiam illarum rerum, quas sibi præsentes imaginatur, secludat.","kind":"passage"},{"id":"RAW_QGOH_PS_2:P0064","text":"Nam si Mens, dum res non existentes ut sibi præsentes imaginatur, simul sciret, res illas revera non existere, hanc sane imaginandi potentiam virtuti suæ naturæ, non vitio tribueret ; præsertim si hæc imaginandi facultas a sola sua natura penderet, hoc est (per Defin. 7 p. I), si hæc Mentis imaginandi facultas libera esset.","kind":"passage"},{"id":"RAW_QGOH_PS_2:P0065","text":"PROPOSITIO XVIII","kind":"passage"},{"id":"RAW_QGOH_PS_2:P0066","text":"Si Corpus humanum a duobus vel pluribus corporibus simul affectum fuerit semel, ubi Mens postea eorum aliquod imaginabitur, statim et aliorum recordabitur.","kind":"passage"},{"id":"RAW_QGOH_PS_2:P0067","text":"DEMONSTRATIO","kind":"passage"},{"id":"RAW_QGOH_PS_2:P0068","text":"Mens (per Coroll. præced.) corpus aliquod ea de causa imaginatur, quia scilicet humanum Corpus a corporis externi vestigiis eodem modo afficitur disponiturque, ac affectum est, cum quædam ejus partes ab ipso corpore externo fuerunt impulsæ : sed (per hypothesin) Corpus tum ita fuit dispositum, ut Mens duo simul corpora imaginaretur, ergo jam etiam duo simul imaginabitur, atque Mens ubi alterutrum imaginabitur, statim et alterius recordabitur. Q. E. D.","kind":"passage"},{"id":"RAW_QGOH_PS_2:P0069","text":"SCHOLIUM","kind":"passage"},{"id":"RAW_QGOH_PS_2:P0070","text":"Hinc clare intelligimus, quid sit Memoria. Est enim nihil aliud quam quædam concatenatio idearum, naturam rerum, quæ extra Corpus humanum sunt, involventium, quæ in Mente fit secundum ordinem et concatenationem affectionum Corporis humani. Dico primo, concatenationem esse illarum tantum idearum, quæ naturam, rerum quæ extra Corpus humanum sunt, involvunt ; non autem idearum, quæ earundem rerum naturam explicant ; sunt enim revera (per Prop. 16 hujus) ideæ affectionum Corporis humani, quæ tam hujus quam corporum externorum naturam involvunt. Dico secundo, hanc concatenationem fieri secundum ordinem et concatenationem affectionum Corporis humani, ut ipsam distinguerem a concatenatione idearum, quæ fit secundum ordinem intellectus, quo res per primas suas causas Mens percipit, et qui in omnibus hominibus idem est.","kind":"passage"},{"id":"RAW_QGOH_PS_2:P0071","text":"Atque hinc porro clare intelligimus, cur Mens ex cogitatione unius rei statim in alterius rei cogitationem incidat, quæ nullam cum priore habet similitudinem ; ut ex. gr. ex cogitatione vocis pomi homo Romanus statim in cogitationem fructus incidet qui nullam cum articulato illo sono habet similitudinem, nec aliquid commune, nisi quod ejusdem hominis Corpus ab his duobus affectum sæpe fuit ; hoc est, quod ipse homo sæpe vocem pomum audivit, dum ipsum fructum videret ; et sic unusquisque ex una in aliam cogitationem incidet, prout rerum imagines uniuscujusque consuetudo in corpore ordinavit. Nam miles ex. gr. visis in arena equi vestigiis statim ex cogitatione equi in cogitationem equitis, et inde in cogitationem belli, etc. incidet. At rusticus ex cogitatione equi in cogitationem aratri, agri, etc.","kind":"passage"},{"id":"RAW_QGOH_PS_2:P0072","text":"incidet ; et sic unusquisque, prout rerum imagines consuevit hoc vel alio modo jungere et concatenare, ex una in hanc vel in aliam incidet cogitationem.","kind":"passage"},{"id":"RAW_QGOH_PS_2:P0073","text":"PROPOSITIO XIX","kind":"passage"},{"id":"RAW_QGOH_PS_2:P0074","text":"Mens humana ipsum humanum Corpus non cognoscit, nec ipsum existere scit, nisi per ideas affectionum, quibus Corpus afficitur.","kind":"passage"},{"id":"RAW_QGOH_PS_2:P0075","text":"DEMONSTRATIO","kind":"passage"},{"id":"RAW_QGOH_PS_2:P0076","text":"Mens enim humana est ipsa idea sive cognitio Corporis humani (per Prop. 13 hujus), quæ (per Prop. 9 hujus) in Deo quidem est, quatenus alia rei singularis idea affectus consideratur ; vel, quia (per Post. 4) Corpus humanum plurimis corporibus indiget, a quibus continuo quasi regeneratur ; et ordo et connexio idearum idem est (per Prop. 7 hujus) ac ordo et connexio causarum ; erit hæc idea in Deo, quatenus plurimarum rerum singularium ideis affectus consideratur. Deus itaque ideam Corporis humani habet, sive Corpus humanum cognoscit quatenus plurimis aliis ideis affectus est, et non quatenus naturam humanæ Mentis constituit, hoc est (per Coroll. Prop. 11 hujus), Mens humana Corpus humanum non cognoscit. At ideæ affectionum Corporis in Deo sunt, quatenus humanæ Mentis naturam constituit, sive Mens humana easdem affectiones percipit (per Prop. 12 hujus) ; et consequenter (per Prop.","kind":"passage"},{"id":"RAW_QGOH_PS_2:P0077","text":"16 hujus) ipsum Corpus humanum, idque (per Prop. 17 hujus) ut actu existens ; percipit ergo eatenus tantum Mens humana ipsum humanum Corpus. Q. E. D.","kind":"passage"},{"id":"RAW_QGOH_PS_2:P0078","text":"PROPOSITIO XX","kind":"passage"},{"id":"RAW_QGOH_PS_2:P0079","text":"Mentis humanæ datur etiam in Deo idea sive cognitio, quæ in Deo eodem modo sequitur, et ad Deum eodem modo refertur, ac idea sive cognitio Corporis humani.","kind":"passage"},{"id":"RAW_QGOH_PS_2:P0080","text":"DEMONSTRATIO","kind":"passage"},{"id":"RAW_QGOH_PS_2:P0081","text":"Cogitatio attributum Dei est (per Prop. 1 hujus) ; adeoque (per Prop. 3 hujus) tam ejus quam omnium ejus affectionum, et consequenter (per Prop. 11 hujus) Mentis etiam humanæ debet necessario in Deo dari idea. Deinde hæc Mentis idea sive cognitio non sequitur in Deo dari, quatenus infinitus, sed quatenus alia rei singularis idea affectus est (per Prop. 9 hujus). Sed ordo et connexio idearum idem est ac ordo et connexio causarum (per Prop. 7 hujus) ; sequitur ergo hæc Mentis idea sive cognitio in Deo, et ad Deum eodem modo refertur, ac idea sive cognitio Corporis. Q. E. D.","kind":"passage"},{"id":"RAW_QGOH_PS_2:P0082","text":"PROPOSITIO XXI","kind":"passage"},{"id":"RAW_QGOH_PS_2:P0083","text":"Hæc Mentis idea eodem modo unita est Menti, ac ipsa Mens unita est Corpori.","kind":"passage"},{"id":"RAW_QGOH_PS_2:P0084","text":"DEMONSTRATIO","kind":"passage"},{"id":"RAW_QGOH_PS_2:P0085","text":"Mentem unitam esse Corpori ex eo ostendimus, quod scilicet Corpus Mentis sit objectum (vide Prop. 12 et 13 hujus) : adeoque per eandem illam rationem idea Mentis cum suo obiecto, hoc est cum ipsa Mente, eodem modo unita esse debet, ac ipsa Mens unita est Corpori. Q. E. D.","kind":"passage"},{"id":"RAW_QGOH_PS_2:P0086","text":"SCHOLIUM","kind":"passage"},{"id":"RAW_QGOH_PS_2:P0087","text":"Hæc Propositio longe clarius intelligitur ex dictis in Schol. Prop. 7 hujus ; ibi enim ostendimus Corporis ideam et Corpus, hoc est (per Prop. 13 hujus) Mentem et Corpus, unum et idem esse Individuum, quod jam sub Cogitationis jam sub Extensionis attributo concipitur ; quare Mentis idea et ipsa Mens una eademque est res, quæ sub uno eodemque attributo, nempe Cogitationis, concipitur. Mentis, inquam, idea et ipsa Mens in Deo eadem necessitate ex eadem cogitandi potentia sequuntur dari. Nam revera idea Mentis, hoc est idea ideæ, nihil aliud est quam forma ideæ, quatenus hæc ut modus cogitandi, absque relatione ad objectum consideratur ; simul ac enim quis aliquid scit, eo ipso scit, se id scire, et simul scit, se scire, quod scit, et sic in infinitum. Sed de his postea","kind":"passage"},{"id":"RAW_QGOH_PS_2:P0088","text":"PROPOSITIO XXII","kind":"passage"},{"id":"RAW_QGOH_PS_2:P0089","text":"Mens humana non tantum Corporis affectiones sed etiam harum affectionum ideas percipit.","kind":"passage"},{"id":"RAW_QGOH_PS_2:P0090","text":"DEMONSTRATIO","kind":"passage"},{"id":"RAW_QGOH_PS_2:P0091","text":"Affectionum idearum ideæ in Deo eodem modo sequuntur, et ad Deum eodem modo referuntur, ac ipsæ affectionum ideæ ; quod eodem modo demonstratur ac Propositio 20 hujus. At ideæ affectionum Corporis in Mente humana sunt (per Prop. 12 hujus), hoc est (per Coroll. Prop. 11 hujus) in Deo, quatenus humanæ Mentis essentiam constituit ; ergo harum idearum ideæ in Deo erunt, quatenus humanæ Mentis cognitionem sive ideam habet, hoc est (per Prop. 21 hujus) in ipsa Mente humana, quæ propterea non tantum Corporis affectiones, sed earum etiam ideas percipit. Q. E. D.","kind":"passage"},{"id":"RAW_QGOH_PS_2:P0092","text":"PROPOSITIO XXIII","kind":"passage"},{"id":"RAW_QGOH_PS_2:P0093","text":"Mens se ipsam non cognoscit, nisi quatenus Corporis affectionum ideat percipit.","kind":"passage"},{"id":"RAW_QGOH_PS_2:P0094","text":"DEMONSTRATIO","kind":"passage"},{"id":"RAW_QGOH_PS_2:P0095","text":"Mentis idea sive cognitio (per Prop. 20 hujus) in Deo eodem modo sequitur, et ad Deum eodem modo refertur, ac Corporis idea sive cognitio. At quoniam (per Prop. 19 hujus) Mens humana ipsum humanum Corpus non cognoscit ; hoc est (per Coroll. Prop. 11 hujus) quoniam cognitio Corporis humani ad Deum non refertur, quatenus humanæ Mentis naturam constituit ; ergo nec cognitio Mentis ad Deum refertur, quatenus essentiam Mentis humanæ constituit ; atque adeo (per idem Coroll. Prop. 11 hujus) Mens humana eatenus se ipsam non cognoscit. Deinde affectionum, quibus Corpus afficitur, ideæ naturam ipsius Corporis humani involvunt (per Prop. 16 hujus), hoc est (per Prop. 13 hujus) cum natura Mentis conveniunt ; quare harum idearum cognitio cognitionem Mentis necessario involvet : at (per Prop. præced.) harum idearum cognitio in ipsa humana Mente est ; ergo Mens humana eatenus tantum se ipsam novit.","kind":"passage"},{"id":"RAW_QGOH_PS_2:P0096","text":"Q. E. D.","kind":"passage"},{"id":"RAW_QGOH_PS_2:P0097","text":"PROPOSITIO XXIV","kind":"passage"},{"id":"RAW_QGOH_PS_2:P0098","text":"Mens humana partium Corpus humanum componentium adæquatam cognitionem non involvit.","kind":"passage"},{"id":"RAW_QGOH_PS_2:P0099","text":"DEMONSTRATIO","kind":"passage"},{"id":"RAW_QGOH_PS_2:P0100","text":"Partes Corpus humanum componentes ad essentiam ipsius Corporis non pertinent, nisi quatenus motus suos certa quadam ratione invicem communicant (vide Defin. post Coroll. Lemmat. 3), et non quatenus ut Individua, absque relatione ad humanum Corpus, considerari possunt. Sunt enim partes humani Corporis (per Post 1) valde composita Individua, quorum partes (per Lem. 4) a Corpore humano, servata omnino ejusdem natura et forma, segregari possunt, motusque suos (vide Axiom. 1 post Lem. 3) aliis corporibus alia ratione communicare ; adeoque (per Prop. 3 hujus) cujuscunque partis idea sive cognitio in Deo erit, et quidem (per Prop. 9 hujus) quatenus affectus consideratur alia idea rei singularis, quæ res singularis ipsa parte ordine Naturæ prior est (per Prop. 7 hujus).","kind":"passage"},{"id":"RAW_QGOH_PS_2:P0101","text":"Quod idem præterea etiam de quacunque parte ipsius Individui Corpus humanum componentis est dicendum ; adeoque cujuscunque partis Corpus humanum componentis cognitio in Deo est, quatenus plurimis rerum ideis affectus est, et non quatenus Corporis humani tantum habet ideam, hoc est (per Prop. 13 hujus) ideam, quæ humanæ Mentis naturam constituit ; atque adeo (per Coroll. Prop. 11 hujus) humana Mens partium Corpus humanum componentium adæquatam cognitionem non involvit. Q. E. D.","kind":"passage"},{"id":"RAW_QGOH_PS_2:P0102","text":"PROPOSITXO XXV","kind":"passage"},{"id":"RAW_QGOH_PS_2:P0103","text":"Idea cujuscunque affectionis Corporis humani adæquatam corporis externi cognitionem non involvit.","kind":"passage"},{"id":"RAW_QGOH_PS_2:P0104","text":"DEMONSTRATIO","kind":"passage"},{"id":"RAW_QGOH_PS_2:P0105","text":"Ideam affectionis Corporis humani eatenus corporis externi naturam involvere ostendimus (vide Prop. 16 hujus), quatenus externum ipsum humanum Corpus certo quodam modo determinat. At quatenus externum corpus Individuum est, quod ad Corpus humanum non refertur, ejus idea sive cognitio in Deo est (per Prop. 9 hujus), quatenus Deus affectus consideratur alterius rei idea quæ (per Prop. 7 hujus) ipso corpore externo prior est natura. Quare corporis externi adæquata cognitio in Deo non est, quatenus ideam affectionis humani Corporis habet, sive idea affectionis Corporis humani adæquatam corporis externi cognitionem non involvit. Q. E. D.","kind":"passage"},{"id":"RAW_QGOH_PS_2:P0106","text":"PROPOSITIO XXVI","kind":"passage"},{"id":"RAW_QGOH_PS_2:P0107","text":"Mens humana nullum corpus externum ut actu existens percipit, nisi per ideas affectionum sui Corporis.","kind":"passage"},{"id":"RAW_QGOH_PS_2:P0108","text":"DEMONSTRATIO","kind":"passage"},{"id":"RAW_QGOH_PS_2:P0109","text":"Si a corpore aliquo externo Corpus humanum nullo modo affectum est ; ergo (per Prop. 7 hujus) nec idea Corporis humani, hoc est (per Prop. 13 hujus) nec Mens humana, idea existentiæ illius corporis ullo etiam modo affecta est, sive existentiam illius corporis externi ullo modo percipit. At quatenus Corpus humanum a corpore aliquo externo aliquo modo afficitur, eatenus (per Prop. 16 hujus cum Coroll. ejusdem) corpus externum percipit. Q. E. D.","kind":"passage"},{"id":"RAW_QGOH_PS_2:P0110","text":"COROLLARIUM","kind":"passage"},{"id":"RAW_QGOH_PS_2:P0111","text":"Quatenus Mens humana corpus externum imaginatur, eatenus adæquatam ejus cognitionem non habet.","kind":"passage"},{"id":"RAW_QGOH_PS_2:P0112","text":"DEMONSTRATIO","kind":"passage"},{"id":"RAW_QGOH_PS_2:P0113","text":"Cum Mens humana per ideas affectionum sui Corporis corpora externa contemplatur, eandem tum imaginari dicimus (vide Schol. Prop. 17 hujus) ; nec Mens alia ratione (per Prop. præced.) corpora externa ut actu existentia imaginari potest. Atque adeo (per Prop. 25 hujus) quatenus Mens corpora externa imaginatur, eorum adæquatam cognitionem non habet. Q. E. D.","kind":"passage"},{"id":"RAW_QGOH_PS_2:P0114","text":"PROPOSITIO XXVII","kind":"passage"},{"id":"RAW_QGOH_PS_2:P0115","text":"Idea cujuscunque affectionis Corporis humani adæquatam ipsius humani Corporis cognitionem non involvit.","kind":"passage"},{"id":"RAW_QGOH_PS_2:P0116","text":"DEMONSTRATIO","kind":"passage"},{"id":"RAW_QGOH_PS_2:P0117","text":"Quælibet idea cujuscunque affectionis humani Corporis eatenus naturam Corporis humani involvit, quatenus ipsum humanum Corpus certo quodam modo affici consideratur (vide Prop. 16 hujus). At quatenus Corpus humanum Individuum est, quod multis aliis modis affici potest, ejus idea, etc. (Vide Demonst. Prop. 25 hujus).","kind":"passage"},{"id":"RAW_QGOH_PS_2:P0118","text":"PROPOSITIO XXVIII","kind":"passage"},{"id":"RAW_QGOH_PS_2:P0119","text":"Ideæ affectionum Corporis humani, quatenus ad humanam Mentem tantum referunt, non sunt clari et distinctæ, sed confusæ.","kind":"passage"},{"id":"RAW_QGOH_PS_2:P0120","text":"DEMONSTRATIO","kind":"passage"},{"id":"RAW_QGOH_PS_2:P0121","text":"Ideæ enim affectionum Corporis humani tam corporum externorum, quam ipsius humani Corporis naturam involvunt (per Prop. 16 hujus) ; nec tantum Corporis humani, sed ejus etiam partium naturam involvere debent ; affectiones namque modi sunt (per Post. 3), quibus partes Corporis humani, et consequenter totum Corpus afficitur. At (per Prop. 24 et 25 hujus) corporum externorum adæquata cognitio, ut et partium Corpus humanum componentium, in Deo non est, quatenus humana Mente, sed quatenus aliis ideis affectus consideratur. Sunt ergo hæ affectionum ideæ, quatenus ad solam humanam Mentem referuntur, veluti consequentiæ absque præmissis, hoc est (ut per se notum) ideæ confusæ. Q. E. D.","kind":"passage"},{"id":"RAW_QGOH_PS_2:P0122","text":"SCHOLIUM","kind":"passage"}],"sha256":"ec27851b3d7ad4071cdb1fe3dae115b4fc54bfdf20c6310d97e9d9a8b07460c2","language_code":"la","language_name":"Latina","translations":[{"id":"SOURCE_TR_20261015T012934780751Z_f3bd6d64","language_code":"en","language_name":"English","title":"Part Two - On the Nature and Origin of the Mind · passage 2","source_sha256":"ec27851b3d7ad4071cdb1fe3dae115b4fc54bfdf20c6310d97e9d9a8b07460c2","sha256":"9b8de6e8f5bcbcfedb2848f863373864cbe184472b7c4fb186c86418af21bd1c","rendition_type":"machine","review_status":"machine","model":"gpt-5.6-terra","created":"2026-10-15T01:29:34.780780+00:00","paragraphs":[{"id":"RAW_QGOH_PS_2:P0001","text":"Hence it follows that a body in motion continues to move until it is determined to rest by another body; and that a body at rest likewise continues to rest until it is determined to motion by another. This too is known through itself. For when I suppose, for example, that body A is at rest, and do not attend to other bodies in motion, I shall be able to say nothing about body A except that it is at rest. But if it should afterwards happen that body A moves, this certainly could not have come about from its being at rest; for nothing else could have followed from that than that body A should remain at rest. If, on the contrary, A is supposed to move, whenever we attend only to A, we shall be able to affirm nothing of it except that it moves. But if it should afterwards happen that A is at rest, this likewise certainly could not have come about from the motion which it had; for nothing else could have followed from motion than that A should move; it happens, therefore, from something that was not in A, namely, from an external cause, by which it was determined to rest.","kind":"passage","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_QGOH_PS_2:P0001"]},{"id":"RAW_QGOH_PS_2:P0002","text":"AXIOM I","kind":"passage","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_QGOH_PS_2:P0002"]},{"id":"RAW_QGOH_PS_2:P0003","text":"All the modes by which any body is affected by another body follow from the nature of the body affected and, at the same time, from the nature of the affecting body; so that one and the same body is moved differently according to the diversity of the natures of the bodies moving it, and conversely, different bodies are moved differently by one and the same body.","kind":"passage","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_QGOH_PS_2:P0003"]},{"id":"RAW_QGOH_PS_2:P0004","text":"AXIOM II","kind":"passage","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_QGOH_PS_2:P0004"]},{"id":"RAW_QGOH_PS_2:P0005","text":"When a moving body strikes another body at rest, which it cannot dislodge, it is reflected so as to continue moving, and the angle of the line of reflected motion with the plane of the body at rest which it struck will be equal to the angle which the line of incident motion makes with the same plane.","kind":"passage","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_QGOH_PS_2:P0005"]},{"id":"RAW_QGOH_PS_2:P0006","text":"And these matters concerning the simplest bodies, namely, those which are distinguished from one another solely by motion and rest, speed and slowness: let us now ascend to composite bodies.","kind":"passage","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_QGOH_PS_2:P0006"]},{"id":"RAW_QGOH_PS_2:P0007","text":"DEFINITION","kind":"passage","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_QGOH_PS_2:P0007"]},{"id":"RAW_QGOH_PS_2:P0008","text":"When a number of bodies, of the same or of different magnitude, are so constrained by the others that they press upon one another, or, if they move with the same or with different degrees of speed, communicate their motions to one another in a certain fixed ratio, we shall say that those bodies are united with one another, and that all together they compose one body, or Individual, which is distinguished from the others by this union of bodies.","kind":"passage","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_QGOH_PS_2:P0008"]},{"id":"RAW_QGOH_PS_2:P0009","text":"AXIOM III","kind":"passage","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_QGOH_PS_2:P0009"]},{"id":"RAW_QGOH_PS_2:P0010","text":"The greater or lesser the surfaces upon which the parts of an Individual, or composite body, press upon one another, the more difficult or easy it is for them to be compelled to change their position, and consequently the more easy or difficult it can be for the Individual itself to assume another figure. Hence I shall call bodies whose parts press upon one another over large surfaces hard; those whose parts do so over small surfaces soft; and finally, those whose parts move among themselves fluid.","kind":"passage","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_QGOH_PS_2:P0010"]},{"id":"RAW_QGOH_PS_2:P0011","text":"LEMMA IV","kind":"passage","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_QGOH_PS_2:P0011"]},{"id":"RAW_QGOH_PS_2:P0012","text":"If, from a body, or Individual, which is composed of several bodies, certain bodies are separated, and at the same time an equal number of others of the same nature succeed in their place, the Individual will retain its nature as before, without any change of its form.","kind":"passage","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_QGOH_PS_2:P0012"]},{"id":"RAW_QGOH_PS_2:P0013","text":"DEMONSTRATION","kind":"passage","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_QGOH_PS_2:P0013"]},{"id":"RAW_QGOH_PS_2:P0014","text":"For bodies (by Lemma 1) are not distinguished in respect of substance; but that which constitutes the form of the Individual consists in the union of bodies (by the preceding Definition); but this union (by the hypothesis), although there is a continuous change of bodies, is retained: therefore the Individual will retain its nature as before, both in respect of substance and of mode. Q. E. D.","kind":"passage","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_QGOH_PS_2:P0014"]},{"id":"RAW_QGOH_PS_2:P0015","text":"LEMMA V","kind":"passage","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_QGOH_PS_2:P0015"]},{"id":"RAW_QGOH_PS_2:P0016","text":"If the parts composing an Individual become greater or lesser, provided nevertheless that they all preserve the same ratio of motion and rest to one another as before, the Individual will likewise retain its nature as before, without any change of form.","kind":"passage","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_QGOH_PS_2:P0016"]},{"id":"RAW_QGOH_PS_2:P0017","text":"DEMONSTRATION","kind":"passage","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_QGOH_PS_2:P0017"]},{"id":"RAW_QGOH_PS_2:P0018","text":"Its demonstration is the same as that of the preceding Lemma.","kind":"passage","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_QGOH_PS_2:P0018"]},{"id":"RAW_QGOH_PS_2:P0019","text":"LEMMA VI","kind":"passage","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_QGOH_PS_2:P0019"]},{"id":"RAW_QGOH_PS_2:P0020","text":"If certain bodies composing an Individual are compelled to bend in another direction the motion which they have toward one direction, but in such a way that they can continue their motions and communicate them to one another in the same ratio as before, the Individual will likewise retain its nature, without any change of form.","kind":"passage","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_QGOH_PS_2:P0020"]},{"id":"RAW_QGOH_PS_2:P0021","text":"DEMONSTRATION","kind":"passage","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_QGOH_PS_2:P0021"]},{"id":"RAW_QGOH_PS_2:P0022","text":"This is self-evident. For it is supposed to retain everything which, in its definition, we said constitutes its form.","kind":"passage","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_QGOH_PS_2:P0022"]},{"id":"RAW_QGOH_PS_2:P0023","text":"LEMMA VII","kind":"passage","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_QGOH_PS_2:P0023"]},{"id":"RAW_QGOH_PS_2:P0024","text":"Moreover, an Individual so composed retains its nature, whether it moves or is at rest as a whole, whether it moves in this direction or in that, provided that each part retains its motion and communicates it to the others as before.","kind":"passage","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_QGOH_PS_2:P0024"]},{"id":"RAW_QGOH_PS_2:P0025","text":"DEMONSTRATION","kind":"passage","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_QGOH_PS_2:P0025"]},{"id":"RAW_QGOH_PS_2:P0026","text":"This is evident from its very definition, which see before Lemma 4.","kind":"passage","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_QGOH_PS_2:P0026"]},{"id":"RAW_QGOH_PS_2:P0027","text":"SCHOLIUM","kind":"passage","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_QGOH_PS_2:P0027"]},{"id":"RAW_QGOH_PS_2:P0028","text":"By these things, therefore, we see in what manner a composite Individual can be affected in many ways, while its nature is nevertheless preserved. And hitherto we have conceived an Individual which is composed only of bodies that are distinguished from one another by motion and rest, by speed and slowness, that is, which is composed of the simplest bodies. But if we now conceive another, composed of several Individuals of diverse nature, we shall find that it can be affected in many other ways, while its nature is nevertheless preserved. For since each one of its parts is composed of several bodies, each part will therefore be able (by the preceding Lemma), without any change in its nature, now to move more slowly, now more quickly, and consequently to communicate its motions to the rest more quickly or more slowly.","kind":"passage","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_QGOH_PS_2:P0028"]},{"id":"RAW_QGOH_PS_2:P0029","text":"But if, further, we conceive a third kind of Individuals, composed of these secondary ones, we shall find that it can be affected in many other ways, without any change of its form. And if we thus proceed further to infinity, we shall easily conceive that the whole of Nature is one Individual, whose parts, that is, all bodies, vary in infinitely many ways, without any change of the whole Individual. And these matters, if it had been my purpose to treat expressly of body, I should have explained and demonstrated more fully. But I have already said that I wish something else, and that I adduce these things for no other reason than because from them I can easily deduce those things which I have resolved to demonstrate.","kind":"passage","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_QGOH_PS_2:P0029"]},{"id":"RAW_QGOH_PS_2:P0030","text":"POSTULATES","kind":"passage","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_QGOH_PS_2:P0030"]},{"id":"RAW_QGOH_PS_2:P0031","text":"I. The human Body is composed of very many Individuals (of diverse nature), each of which is highly composite.","kind":"passage","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_QGOH_PS_2:P0031"]},{"id":"RAW_QGOH_PS_2:P0032","text":"II. Of the Individuals of which the human Body is composed, some are fluid, some soft, and finally some hard.","kind":"passage","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_QGOH_PS_2:P0032"]},{"id":"RAW_QGOH_PS_2:P0033","text":"III. The Individuals composing the human Body, and consequently the human Body itself, are affected in very many ways by external bodies.","kind":"passage","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_QGOH_PS_2:P0033"]},{"id":"RAW_QGOH_PS_2:P0034","text":"IV. The human Body, in order to be preserved, requires very many other bodies, by which it is continually, as it were, regenerated.","kind":"passage","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_QGOH_PS_2:P0034"]},{"id":"RAW_QGOH_PS_2:P0035","text":"V. When a fluid part of the human Body is determined by an external body so as frequently to strike against another soft part, it changes its surface and, as it were, impresses upon it certain traces of the external body impelling it. VI. The human Body can move external bodies in very many ways, and dispose them in very many ways.","kind":"passage","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_QGOH_PS_2:P0035"]},{"id":"RAW_QGOH_PS_2:P0036","text":"PROPOSITION XIV","kind":"passage","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_QGOH_PS_2:P0036"]},{"id":"RAW_QGOH_PS_2:P0037","text":"The human Mind is capable of perceiving very many things, and is the more capable the more ways its Body can be disposed.","kind":"passage","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_QGOH_PS_2:P0037"]},{"id":"RAW_QGOH_PS_2:P0038","text":"DEMONSTRATION","kind":"passage","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_QGOH_PS_2:P0038"]},{"id":"RAW_QGOH_PS_2:P0039","text":"For the human Body (by Postulates 3 and 6) is affected in very many ways by external bodies, and is disposed to be affected by external bodies in very many ways. But the human Mind must perceive all things that happen in the human Body (by Proposition 12 of this Part); therefore the human Mind is capable of perceiving very many things, and is the more capable, etc. Q. E. D.","kind":"passage","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_QGOH_PS_2:P0039"]},{"id":"RAW_QGOH_PS_2:P0040","text":"PROPOSITION XV","kind":"passage","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_QGOH_PS_2:P0040"]},{"id":"RAW_QGOH_PS_2:P0041","text":"The idea which constitutes the formal being of the human Mind is not simple, but composed of very many ideas.","kind":"passage","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_QGOH_PS_2:P0041"]},{"id":"RAW_QGOH_PS_2:P0042","text":"DEMONSTRATION","kind":"passage","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_QGOH_PS_2:P0042"]},{"id":"RAW_QGOH_PS_2:P0043","text":"The idea which constitutes the formal being of the human Mind is the idea of the Body (by Proposition 13 of this Part), which (by Postulate 1) is composed of very many highly composite Individuals. But of every Individual composing the Body there is necessarily (by the Corollary to Proposition 8 of this Part) an idea in God; therefore (by Proposition 7 of this Part) the idea of the human Body is composed of these very many ideas of the component parts. Q. E. D.","kind":"passage","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_QGOH_PS_2:P0043"]},{"id":"RAW_QGOH_PS_2:P0044","text":"PROPOSITION XVI","kind":"passage","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_QGOH_PS_2:P0044"]},{"id":"RAW_QGOH_PS_2:P0045","text":"The idea of every mode by which the human Body is affected by external bodies must involve the nature of the human Body and at the same time the nature of the external body.","kind":"passage","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_QGOH_PS_2:P0045"]},{"id":"RAW_QGOH_PS_2:P0046","text":"DEMONSTRATION","kind":"passage","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_QGOH_PS_2:P0046"]},{"id":"RAW_QGOH_PS_2:P0047","text":"For all the modes by which any body is affected follow from the nature of the affected body and at the same time from the nature of the affecting body (by Axiom 1 after the Corollary to Lemma 3); wherefore their idea (by Axiom 4, Part I) will necessarily involve the nature of both bodies; and therefore the idea of every mode by which the human Body is affected by an external body involves the nature of the human Body and of the external body. Q. E. D.","kind":"passage","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_QGOH_PS_2:P0047"]},{"id":"RAW_QGOH_PS_2:P0048","text":"COROLLARY I","kind":"passage","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_QGOH_PS_2:P0048"]},{"id":"RAW_QGOH_PS_2:P0049","text":"From this it follows, first, that the human Mind perceives the nature of a great many bodies together with the nature of its own body.","kind":"passage","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_QGOH_PS_2:P0049"]},{"id":"RAW_QGOH_PS_2:P0050","text":"COROLLARY II","kind":"passage","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_QGOH_PS_2:P0050"]},{"id":"RAW_QGOH_PS_2:P0051","text":"It follows, secondly, that the ideas which we have of external bodies indicate the constitution of our own body more than the nature of the external bodies; this was explained by many examples in the Appendix to Part I.","kind":"passage","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_QGOH_PS_2:P0051"]},{"id":"RAW_QGOH_PS_2:P0052","text":"PROPOSITION XVII","kind":"passage","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_QGOH_PS_2:P0052"]},{"id":"RAW_QGOH_PS_2:P0053","text":"If the human Body is affected in a mode which involves the nature of some external Body, the human Mind will contemplate that same external body as actually existing, or as present to itself, until the Body is affected by an affection which excludes the existence or presence of that same body.","kind":"passage","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_QGOH_PS_2:P0053"]},{"id":"RAW_QGOH_PS_2:P0054","text":"DEMONSTRATION","kind":"passage","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_QGOH_PS_2:P0054"]},{"id":"RAW_QGOH_PS_2:P0055","text":"This is evident. For as long as the human Body is thus affected, the human Mind (by Prop. 12 of this Part) will contemplate this affection of the body; that is (by the preceding Prop.), it will have an idea of a mode actually existing, which involves the nature of the external body; that is, an idea which does not exclude but posits the existence or presence of the nature of the external body; and therefore the Mind (by Coroll. 1 of the preceding Prop.) will contemplate the external body as actually existing, or as present, until it is affected, etc. Q. E. D.","kind":"passage","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_QGOH_PS_2:P0055"]},{"id":"RAW_QGOH_PS_2:P0056","text":"COROLLARY","kind":"passage","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_QGOH_PS_2:P0056"]},{"id":"RAW_QGOH_PS_2:P0057","text":"The Mind will nevertheless be able to contemplate as if they were present external bodies by which the human Body has once been affected, although they neither exist nor are present.","kind":"passage","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_QGOH_PS_2:P0057"]},{"id":"RAW_QGOH_PS_2:P0058","text":"DEMONSTRATION","kind":"passage","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_QGOH_PS_2:P0058"]},{"id":"RAW_QGOH_PS_2:P0059","text":"While external bodies determine the fluid parts of the human Body in such a way that they frequently impinge upon the softer parts, they alter their surfaces (by Post. 5); whence it comes about (see Axiom 2 after the Coroll. to Lemma 3) that they are thereby reflected otherwise than they were wont before, and that afterwards also, when by their spontaneous motion they encounter those same new surfaces, they are reflected in the same manner as when they were impelled by the external bodies toward those surfaces; and consequently that, while they continue to move thus reflected, they affect the human Body in the same manner, concerning which the Mind (by Prop. 12 of this Part) will again think; that is (by Prop. 17 of this Part), the Mind will again contemplate the external body as present; and this as often as the fluid parts of the human Body, by their spontaneous motion, encounter the same surfaces. Wherefore, although the external bodies by which the human Body was once affected do not exist, the Mind will nevertheless contemplate them as present as often as this action of the body is repeated.","kind":"passage","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_QGOH_PS_2:P0059"]},{"id":"RAW_QGOH_PS_2:P0060","text":"Q. E. D.","kind":"passage","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_QGOH_PS_2:P0060"]},{"id":"RAW_QGOH_PS_2:P0061","text":"SCHOLIUM","kind":"passage","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_QGOH_PS_2:P0061"]},{"id":"RAW_QGOH_PS_2:P0062","text":"Thus we see how it can come about that we contemplate things which are not as if they were present, as often happens. And it may be that this occurs from other causes; but here it suffices for me to have shown one through which I can explain the matter as if I had shown it through its true cause; and yet I do not believe that I have strayed far from the truth, since all those postulates which I have assumed contain scarcely anything that is not established by experience, concerning which we are not permitted to doubt, after we have shown that the human Body, as we sense it, exists (see the Coroll. after Prop. 13 of this Part). Moreover (from the preceding Coroll. and Coroll. 2 of Prop. 16 of this Part), we clearly understand what the difference is between, for example, the idea of Peter, which constitutes the essence of Peter's Mind itself, and the idea of Peter himself which is in another man, say in Paul.","kind":"passage","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_QGOH_PS_2:P0062"]},{"id":"RAW_QGOH_PS_2:P0063","text":"For the former directly explains the essence of Peter's Body itself, and does not involve existence except so long as Peter exists; whereas the latter indicates the constitution of Paul's Body more than Peter's nature, and therefore, while that constitution of Paul's Body endures, Paul's Mind, although Peter does not exist, will nevertheless contemplate him as present to itself. Further, in order to retain customary words, we shall call the affections of the human Body, the ideas of which represent external bodies as if present to us, images of things, although they do not reproduce the figures of things. And when the Mind contemplates bodies in this way, we shall say that it imagines. And here, in order that I may begin to indicate what error is, I should like you to note that the imaginations of the Mind, considered in themselves, contain no error; or that the Mind does not err from the fact that it imagines, but only insofar as it is considered to lack an idea which excludes the existence of those things which it imagines as present to itself.","kind":"passage","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_QGOH_PS_2:P0063"]},{"id":"RAW_QGOH_PS_2:P0064","text":"For if, while the Mind imagines non-existing things as present to itself, it at the same time knew that those things did not really exist, it would certainly attribute this power of imagining to the virtue of its nature, not to a defect; especially if this faculty of imagining depended on its nature alone, that is (by Def. 7, Part I), if this faculty of the Mind for imagining were free.","kind":"passage","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_QGOH_PS_2:P0064"]},{"id":"RAW_QGOH_PS_2:P0065","text":"PROPOSITION XVIII","kind":"passage","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_QGOH_PS_2:P0065"]},{"id":"RAW_QGOH_PS_2:P0066","text":"If the human Body has once been affected simultaneously by two or more bodies, when the Mind afterwards imagines one of them, it will immediately remember the others also.","kind":"passage","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_QGOH_PS_2:P0066"]},{"id":"RAW_QGOH_PS_2:P0067","text":"DEMONSTRATION","kind":"passage","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_QGOH_PS_2:P0067"]},{"id":"RAW_QGOH_PS_2:P0068","text":"The Mind (by the preceding Coroll.) imagines a body for the reason that, namely, the human Body is affected and disposed by the traces of an external body in the same manner as it was affected when some of its parts were impelled by that external body; but (by the hypothesis) the Body was then so disposed that the Mind imagined two bodies simultaneously; therefore it will now also imagine two simultaneously, and when the Mind imagines either one, it will immediately remember the other also. Q. E. D.","kind":"passage","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_QGOH_PS_2:P0068"]},{"id":"RAW_QGOH_PS_2:P0069","text":"SCHOLIUM","kind":"passage","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_QGOH_PS_2:P0069"]},{"id":"RAW_QGOH_PS_2:P0070","text":"From this we clearly understand what Memory is. For it is nothing other than a certain concatenation of ideas involving the nature of things which are outside the human Body, a concatenation which takes place in the Mind according to the order and concatenation of the affections of the human Body. I say, first, that it is a concatenation only of those ideas which involve the nature of things which are outside the human Body, and not of ideas which explain the nature of those same things; for they are in reality (by Prop. 16 of this Part) ideas of affections of the human Body, which involve the nature both of this body and of external bodies. I say, secondly, that this concatenation takes place according to the order and concatenation of the affections of the human Body, in order to distinguish it from the concatenation of ideas which takes place according to the order of the intellect, by which the Mind perceives things through their first causes, and which is the same in all men.","kind":"passage","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_QGOH_PS_2:P0070"]},{"id":"RAW_QGOH_PS_2:P0071","text":"And from this, further, we clearly understand why the Mind falls at once from the thought of one thing into the thought of another thing which has no resemblance to the former; as, for example, from the thought of the word pomum a Roman will at once fall into the thought of a fruit which has no resemblance to that articulated sound, nor anything in common with it, except that the body of the same man has often been affected by these two things; that is, that the man himself has often heard the word pomum while seeing the fruit itself; and thus each one will fall from one thought into another, according as habit has ordered the images of things in the body of each. For a soldier, for example, on seeing the tracks of a horse in the sand, will at once fall from the thought of a horse into the thought of a rider, and thence into the thought of war, etc. But a peasant, from the thought of a horse, into the thought of a plough, a field, etc.","kind":"passage","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_QGOH_PS_2:P0071"]},{"id":"RAW_QGOH_PS_2:P0072","text":"will fall; and thus each one, according as he has been accustomed to join and concatenate the images of things in this or that manner, will fall from one thought into this or that other thought.","kind":"passage","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_QGOH_PS_2:P0072"]},{"id":"RAW_QGOH_PS_2:P0073","text":"PROPOSITION XIX","kind":"passage","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_QGOH_PS_2:P0073"]},{"id":"RAW_QGOH_PS_2:P0074","text":"The human Mind does not know the human Body itself, nor does it know that it exists, except through ideas of the affections by which the Body is affected.","kind":"passage","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_QGOH_PS_2:P0074"]},{"id":"RAW_QGOH_PS_2:P0075","text":"DEMONSTRATION","kind":"passage","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_QGOH_PS_2:P0075"]},{"id":"RAW_QGOH_PS_2:P0076","text":"For the human Mind is itself the idea, or knowledge, of the human Body (by Prop. 13 of this), which (by Prop. 9 of this) is indeed in God insofar as He is considered as affected by another idea of a singular thing; or, because (by Post. 4) the human Body needs very many bodies, by which it is continually, as it were, regenerated; and the order and connection of ideas is the same (by Prop. 7 of this) as the order and connection of causes; this idea will be in God insofar as He is considered as affected by the ideas of very many singular things. Therefore God has the idea of the human Body, or knows the human Body, insofar as He is affected by very many other ideas, and not insofar as He constitutes the nature of the human Mind; that is (by Coroll. Prop. 11 of this), the human Mind does not know the human Body. But the ideas of the affections of the Body are in God insofar as He constitutes the nature of the human Mind, or the human Mind perceives the same affections (by Prop. 12 of this); and consequently (by Prop.","kind":"passage","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_QGOH_PS_2:P0076"]},{"id":"RAW_QGOH_PS_2:P0077","text":"16 of this) the human Body itself, and this (by Prop. 17 of this) as actually existing; therefore the human Mind perceives the human Body itself only insofar as this is the case. Q. E. D.","kind":"passage","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_QGOH_PS_2:P0077"]},{"id":"RAW_QGOH_PS_2:P0078","text":"PROPOSITION XX","kind":"passage","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_QGOH_PS_2:P0078"]},{"id":"RAW_QGOH_PS_2:P0079","text":"There is also given in God an idea, or knowledge, of the human Mind, which follows in God in the same manner, and is related to God in the same manner, as the idea, or knowledge, of the human Body.","kind":"passage","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_QGOH_PS_2:P0079"]},{"id":"RAW_QGOH_PS_2:P0080","text":"DEMONSTRATION","kind":"passage","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_QGOH_PS_2:P0080"]},{"id":"RAW_QGOH_PS_2:P0081","text":"Thought is an attribute of God (by Prop. 1 of this); and therefore (by Prop. 3 of this) an idea of both it and all its affections, and consequently (by Prop. 11 of this) also of the human Mind, must necessarily be given in God. Next, this idea, or knowledge, of the Mind does not follow as given in God insofar as He is infinite, but insofar as He is affected by another idea of a singular thing (by Prop. 9 of this). But the order and connection of ideas is the same as the order and connection of causes (by Prop. 7 of this); therefore this idea, or knowledge, of the Mind follows in God, and is related to God in the same manner, as the idea, or knowledge, of the Body. Q. E. D.","kind":"passage","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_QGOH_PS_2:P0081"]},{"id":"RAW_QGOH_PS_2:P0082","text":"PROPOSITION XXI","kind":"passage","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_QGOH_PS_2:P0082"]},{"id":"RAW_QGOH_PS_2:P0083","text":"This idea of the Mind is united to the Mind in the same manner as the Mind itself is united to the Body.","kind":"passage","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_QGOH_PS_2:P0083"]},{"id":"RAW_QGOH_PS_2:P0084","text":"DEMONSTRATION","kind":"passage","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_QGOH_PS_2:P0084"]},{"id":"RAW_QGOH_PS_2:P0085","text":"We showed that the Mind is united to the Body from the fact, namely, that the Body is the object of the Mind (see Props. 12 and 13 of this); therefore, by that same reasoning, the idea of the Mind must be united to its object, that is, to the Mind itself, in the same manner as the Mind itself is united to the Body. Q. E. D.","kind":"passage","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_QGOH_PS_2:P0085"]},{"id":"RAW_QGOH_PS_2:P0086","text":"SCHOLIUM","kind":"passage","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_QGOH_PS_2:P0086"]},{"id":"RAW_QGOH_PS_2:P0087","text":"This Proposition is understood far more clearly from what was said in the Schol. to Prop. 7 of this; for there we showed that the idea of the Body and the Body, that is (by Prop. 13 of this), the Mind and the Body, are one and the same Individual, which is now conceived under the attribute of Thought, now under that of Extension; wherefore the idea of the Mind and the Mind itself are one and the same thing, which is conceived under one and the same attribute, namely, that of Thought. The idea of the Mind, I say, and the Mind itself follow as given in God by the same necessity from the same power of thinking. For in truth the idea of the Mind, that is, the idea of an idea, is nothing other than the form of an idea, insofar as this is considered as a mode of thinking, without relation to an object; for as soon as anyone knows something, by that very fact he knows that he knows it, and at the same time knows that he knows what he knows, and so on to infinity. But concerning these matters later.","kind":"passage","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_QGOH_PS_2:P0087"]},{"id":"RAW_QGOH_PS_2:P0088","text":"PROPOSITION XXII","kind":"passage","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_QGOH_PS_2:P0088"]},{"id":"RAW_QGOH_PS_2:P0089","text":"The human Mind perceives not only the affections of the Body, but also the ideas of these affections.","kind":"passage","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_QGOH_PS_2:P0089"]},{"id":"RAW_QGOH_PS_2:P0090","text":"DEMONSTRATION","kind":"passage","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_QGOH_PS_2:P0090"]},{"id":"RAW_QGOH_PS_2:P0091","text":"The ideas of the ideas of affections follow in God in the same manner, and are related to God in the same manner, as the ideas of affections themselves; which is demonstrated in the same manner as Prop. 20 of this. But the ideas of the affections of the Body are in the human Mind (by Prop. 12 of this), that is (by Coroll. Prop. 11 of this), in God insofar as He constitutes the essence of the human Mind; therefore the ideas of these ideas will be in God insofar as He has the knowledge, or idea, of the human Mind, that is (by Prop. 21 of this), in the human Mind itself, which therefore perceives not only the affections of the Body, but also their ideas. Q. E. D.","kind":"passage","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_QGOH_PS_2:P0091"]},{"id":"RAW_QGOH_PS_2:P0092","text":"PROPOSITION XXIII","kind":"passage","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_QGOH_PS_2:P0092"]},{"id":"RAW_QGOH_PS_2:P0093","text":"The Mind does not know itself except insofar as it perceives the ideas of the affections of the Body.","kind":"passage","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_QGOH_PS_2:P0093"]},{"id":"RAW_QGOH_PS_2:P0094","text":"DEMONSTRATION","kind":"passage","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_QGOH_PS_2:P0094"]},{"id":"RAW_QGOH_PS_2:P0095","text":"The idea, or knowledge, of the Mind (by Prop. 20 of this) follows in God in the same manner, and is related to God in the same manner, as the idea, or knowledge, of the Body. But since (by Prop. 19 of this) the human Mind does not know the human Body itself; that is (by Coroll. Prop. 11 of this), since the knowledge of the human Body is not related to God insofar as He constitutes the nature of the human Mind; therefore neither is the knowledge of the Mind related to God insofar as He constitutes the essence of the human Mind; and hence (by the same Coroll. Prop. 11 of this) the human Mind does not, insofar as this is the case, know itself. Further, the ideas of the affections by which the Body is affected involve the nature of the human Body itself (by Prop. 16 of this), that is (by Prop. 13 of this), they agree with the nature of the Mind; wherefore knowledge of these ideas will necessarily involve knowledge of the Mind. But (by the preceding Prop.) knowledge of these ideas is in the human Mind itself; therefore the human Mind knows itself only insofar as this is the case.","kind":"passage","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_QGOH_PS_2:P0095"]},{"id":"RAW_QGOH_PS_2:P0096","text":"Q. E. D.","kind":"passage","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_QGOH_PS_2:P0096"]},{"id":"RAW_QGOH_PS_2:P0097","text":"PROPOSITION XXIV","kind":"passage","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_QGOH_PS_2:P0097"]},{"id":"RAW_QGOH_PS_2:P0098","text":"The human mind does not involve adequate knowledge of the parts composing the human body.","kind":"passage","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_QGOH_PS_2:P0098"]},{"id":"RAW_QGOH_PS_2:P0099","text":"DEMONSTRATION","kind":"passage","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_QGOH_PS_2:P0099"]},{"id":"RAW_QGOH_PS_2:P0100","text":"The parts composing the human body do not pertain to the essence of the body itself, except insofar as they communicate their motions to one another according to a certain fixed ratio (see Def. after Coroll. of Lemma 3), and not insofar as they can be considered as Individuals, without relation to the human body. For the parts of the human body are (by Post. 1) highly composite Individuals, whose parts (by Lemma 4) can be separated from the human body, with its nature and form wholly preserved, and can communicate their motions (see Axiom 1 after Lemma 3) to other bodies in another ratio; and therefore (by Prop. 3 of this Part) the idea or knowledge of any part whatsoever will be in God, and indeed (by Prop. 9 of this Part) insofar as he is considered as affected by another idea of a singular thing, which singular thing is itself prior in the order of Nature to the part (by Prop. 7 of this Part).","kind":"passage","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_QGOH_PS_2:P0100"]},{"id":"RAW_QGOH_PS_2:P0101","text":"Moreover, the same must also be said of any part whatsoever of the Individual composing the human body; and therefore knowledge of any part whatsoever composing the human body is in God insofar as he is affected by a great many ideas of things, and not insofar as he has only the idea of the human body, that is (by Prop. 13 of this Part), the idea which constitutes the nature of the human Mind; and therefore (by the Coroll. of Prop. 11 of this Part) the human Mind does not involve adequate knowledge of the parts composing the human body. Q. E. D.","kind":"passage","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_QGOH_PS_2:P0101"]},{"id":"RAW_QGOH_PS_2:P0102","text":"PROPOSITION XXV","kind":"passage","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_QGOH_PS_2:P0102"]},{"id":"RAW_QGOH_PS_2:P0103","text":"The idea of any affection whatsoever of the human body does not involve adequate knowledge of an external body.","kind":"passage","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_QGOH_PS_2:P0103"]},{"id":"RAW_QGOH_PS_2:P0104","text":"DEMONSTRATION","kind":"passage","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_QGOH_PS_2:P0104"]},{"id":"RAW_QGOH_PS_2:P0105","text":"We have shown that the idea of an affection of the human body involves the nature of an external body insofar as the external body itself determines the human body in a certain manner (see Prop. 16 of this Part). But insofar as the external body is an Individual which is not related to the human body, its idea or knowledge is in God (by Prop. 9 of this Part), insofar as God is considered as affected by the idea of another thing which (by Prop. 7 of this Part) is by nature prior to the external body itself. Therefore adequate knowledge of the external body is not in God insofar as he has the idea of an affection of the human body; or, the idea of an affection of the human body does not involve adequate knowledge of the external body. Q. E. D.","kind":"passage","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_QGOH_PS_2:P0105"]},{"id":"RAW_QGOH_PS_2:P0106","text":"PROPOSITION XXVI","kind":"passage","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_QGOH_PS_2:P0106"]},{"id":"RAW_QGOH_PS_2:P0107","text":"The human Mind perceives no external body as actually existing except through ideas of affections of its own Body.","kind":"passage","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_QGOH_PS_2:P0107"]},{"id":"RAW_QGOH_PS_2:P0108","text":"DEMONSTRATION","kind":"passage","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_QGOH_PS_2:P0108"]},{"id":"RAW_QGOH_PS_2:P0109","text":"If the human Body is in no way affected by any external body, then (by Prop. 7 of this Part) neither is the idea of the human Body, that is (by Prop. 13 of this Part), nor is the human Mind, in any way affected by the idea of the existence of that body; that is, it in no way perceives the existence of that external body. But insofar as the human Body is in some way affected by an external body, it thereby (by Prop. 16 of this Part together with its Corollary) perceives the external body. Q. E. D.","kind":"passage","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_QGOH_PS_2:P0109"]},{"id":"RAW_QGOH_PS_2:P0110","text":"COROLLARY","kind":"passage","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_QGOH_PS_2:P0110"]},{"id":"RAW_QGOH_PS_2:P0111","text":"Insofar as the human Mind imagines an external body, it does not have adequate knowledge of it.","kind":"passage","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_QGOH_PS_2:P0111"]},{"id":"RAW_QGOH_PS_2:P0112","text":"DEMONSTRATION","kind":"passage","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_QGOH_PS_2:P0112"]},{"id":"RAW_QGOH_PS_2:P0113","text":"When the human Mind contemplates external bodies through ideas of affections of its own Body, we say that it then imagines them (see Schol. of Prop. 17 of this Part); nor can the Mind by any other means (by the preceding Prop.) imagine external bodies as actually existing. And therefore (by Prop. 25 of this Part), insofar as the Mind imagines external bodies, it does not have adequate knowledge of them. Q. E. D.","kind":"passage","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_QGOH_PS_2:P0113"]},{"id":"RAW_QGOH_PS_2:P0114","text":"PROPOSITION XXVII","kind":"passage","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_QGOH_PS_2:P0114"]},{"id":"RAW_QGOH_PS_2:P0115","text":"The idea of any affection whatsoever of the human Body does not involve adequate knowledge of the human Body itself.","kind":"passage","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_QGOH_PS_2:P0115"]},{"id":"RAW_QGOH_PS_2:P0116","text":"DEMONSTRATION","kind":"passage","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_QGOH_PS_2:P0116"]},{"id":"RAW_QGOH_PS_2:P0117","text":"Every idea of any affection whatsoever of the human Body involves the nature of the human Body insofar as the human Body itself is considered as affected in a certain manner (see Prop. 16 of this Part). But insofar as the human Body is an Individual which can be affected in many other ways, its idea, etc. (See the Demonstration of Prop. 25 of this Part).","kind":"passage","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_QGOH_PS_2:P0117"]},{"id":"RAW_QGOH_PS_2:P0118","text":"PROPOSITION XXVIII","kind":"passage","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_QGOH_PS_2:P0118"]},{"id":"RAW_QGOH_PS_2:P0119","text":"The ideas of affections of the human Body, insofar as they are referred only to the human Mind, are not clear and distinct, but confused.","kind":"passage","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_QGOH_PS_2:P0119"]},{"id":"RAW_QGOH_PS_2:P0120","text":"DEMONSTRATION","kind":"passage","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_QGOH_PS_2:P0120"]},{"id":"RAW_QGOH_PS_2:P0121","text":"For the ideas of the affections of the human Body involve the nature both of external bodies and of the human Body itself (by Prop. 16 of this Part); nor only the nature of the human Body, but also that of its parts must they involve; for affections are modes (by Post. 3) by which the parts of the human Body, and consequently the whole Body, are affected. But (by Props. 24 and 25 of this Part) adequate knowledge of external bodies, as also of the parts composing the human Body, is not in God insofar as He is considered as affected with the human Mind, but insofar as He is considered as affected with other ideas. Therefore these ideas of affections, insofar as they are referred to the human Mind alone, are like consequences without premises, that is (as is known through itself), confused ideas. Q. E. D.","kind":"passage","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_QGOH_PS_2:P0121"]},{"id":"RAW_QGOH_PS_2:P0122","text":"SCHOLIUM","kind":"passage","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_QGOH_PS_2:P0122"]}],"source_digest":"afe01e485c1657774fce35613aee833ced071bc79cdaa0fa769e42254835419c","source_language_code":"la","original_title":"Pars secunda - De natura et origine mentis · passage 2"}],"passage_basis":"frozen-grounding","original_title":"Pars secunda - De natura et origine mentis · passage 2","title_language_code":"en"},{"id":"RAW_QGOH_PS_3","title":"Part Two - On the Nature and Origin of the Mind · passage 3","order":7,"chapter_id":"CH_QGOH_PS_3","text":"Idea, quæ naturam Mentis humanæ constituit, demonstratur eodem modo non esse, in se sola considerata, clara et distincta ; ut etiam idea Mentis humanæ, et ideæ idearum affectionum Corporis humani, quatenus ad solam Mentem referuntur, quod unusquisque facile videre potest.\n\nPROPOSITIO XXIX\n\nIdea ideæ cujuscunque affectionis Corporis humani adæquatam humanæ Mentis cognitionem non involvit.\n\nDEMONSTRATIO\n\nIdea enim affectionis Corporis humani (per Prop. 27 hujus) adæquatam ipsius Corporis cognitionem non involvit, sive ejus naturam adæquate non exprimit ; hoc est (per Prop. 13 hujus), cum natura Mentis non convenit adæquate ; adeoque (per Axiom. 6 p. I) hujus ideæ idea adæquate humanæ Mentis naturam non exprimit, sive adæquatam ejus cognitionem non involvit. Q. E. D.\n\nCOROLLARIUM\n\nHinc sequitur, Mentem humanam, quoties ex communi Naturæ ordine res percipit, nec sui ipsius, nec sui Corporis, nec corporum externorum adæquatam, sed confusam tantum et mutilatam habere cognitionem. Nam Mens se ipsam non cognoscit, nisi quatenus ideas affectionum Corporis percipit (per Prop. 23 hujus). Corpus autem suum (per Prop. 19 hujus) non percipit, nisi per ipsas affectionum ideas, per quas etiam tantum (per Prop. 26 hujus) corpora externa percipit ; atque adeo, quatenus eas habet, nec sui ipsius (per Prop. 29 hujus), nec sui Corporis (per Prop. 27 hujus), nec corporum externorum (per Prop. 25 hujus) habet adæquatam cognitionem, sed tantum (per Prop. 28 hujus cum ejus Schol.) mutilatam et confusam. Q. E. D.\n\nSCHOLIUM\n\nDico expresse, quod Mens nec sui ipsius, nec sui Corporis, nec corporum externorum adæquatam, sed confusam tantum cognitionem habeat, quoties ex communi Naturæ ordine res percipit ; hoc est, quoties externe, ex rerum nempe fortuito occursu, determinatur ad hoc vel illud contemplandum, et non quoties interne, ex eo scilicet, quod res plures simul contemplatur, determinatur ad earundem convenientias, differentias et oppugnantias intelligendum ; quoties enim hoc vel alio modo interne disponitur, tum res clare et distincte contemplatur, ut infra ostendam.\n\nPROPOSITIO XXX\n\nNos de duratione nostri Corporis nullam nisi admodum inadæquatam cognitionem habere possumus.\n\nDEMONSTRATIO\n\nNostri corporis duratio ab ejus essentia non dependet (per Ax. I hujus), nec etiam ab absoluta Dei natura (per Prop. 21 p. I). Sed (per Prop. 28 p. I) ad existendum et operandum determinatur a talibus causis, quæ etiam ab aliis determinatæ sunt ad existendum et operandum certa ac determinata ratione, et hæ iterum ab aliis, et sic in infinitum. Nostri igitur Corporis duratio a communi Naturæ ordine et rerum constitutione pendet. Qua autem ratione res constitutæ sint, ejus rei adæquata cognitio datur in Deo, quatenus earum omnium ideas, et non quatenus tantum humani Corporis ideam habet (per Coroll. Prop. 9 hujus) ; quare cognitio durationis nostri Corporis est in Deo admodum inadæquata, quatenus tantum naturam Mentis humanæ constituere consideratur ; hoc est (per Coroll. Prop. 11 hujus), hæc cognitio est in nostra Mente admodum inadæquata. Q. E. D.\n\nPROPOSITIO XXXI\n\nNos de duratione rerum singularium, quæ extra nos sunt, nullam nisi admodum inadæquatam cognitionem habere possumus.\n\nDEMONSTRATIO\n\nUnaquæque enim res singularis, sicuti humanum Corpus, ab alia re singulari determinari debet ad existendum et operandum certa ac determinata ratione ; et hæc iterum ab alia, et sic in infinitum (per Prop. 28 p. I). Cum autem ex hac communi rerum singularium proprietate in præcedenti Prop. demonstraverimus, nos de duratione nostri Corporis non nisi admodum inadæquatam cognitionem habere ; ergo hoc idem de rerum singularium duratione erit concludendum, quod scilicet ejus non nisi admodum inadæquatam cognitionem habere possumus. Q. E. D.\n\nCOROLLARIUM\n\nHinc sequitur, omnes res particulares contingentes et corruptibiles esse. Nam de earum duratione nullam adæquatam cognitionem habere possumus (per Prop. præced.), et hoc est id, quod per rerum contingentiam et corruptionis possibilitatem nobis est intelligendum (vide Schol. 1 Prop. 33 p. I). Nam (per Prop 29 p. I) præter hoc nullum datur contingens.\n\nPROPOSITIO XXXII\n\nOmnes ideæ, quatenus ad Deum referuntur, veræ sunt.\n\nDEMONSTRATIO\n\nOmnes enim ideæ, quæ in Deo sunt, cum suis ideatis omnino conveniunt (per Coroll. Prop. 7 hujus), adeoque (per Ax. 6 p. I) omnes veræ sunt. Q. E. D.\n\nPROPOSITIO XXXIII\n\nNihil in ideis positivum est, propter quod falsæ dicuntur.\n\nDEMONSTRATIO\n\nSi negas, concipe, si fieri potest, modum positivum cogitandi, qui formam erroris sive falsitatis constituat. Hic cogitandi modus non potest esse in Deo (per Prop. præced.) ; extra Deum autem etiam nec esse nec concipi potest (per Prop. 15, p. I). Atque adeo nihil potest dari positivum in ideis, propter quod falsæ dicuntur. Q. E. D.\n\nPROPOSITIO XXXIV\n\nOmnis idea, quæ in nobis est absoluta, sive adæquata et perfecta vera est.\n\nDEMONSTRATIO\n\nCum dicimus, dari in nobis ideam adæquatam et perfectam, nihil aliud dicimus (per Coroll. Prop. 11 hujus), quam quod in Deo, quatenus nostræ Mentis essentiam constituit, detur idea adæquata et perfecta ; et consequenter (per Prop. 32 hujus) nihil aliud dicimus, quam quod talis idea sit vera. Q. E. D.\n\nPROPOSITIO XXXV\n\nFalsitas consistit in cognitionis privatione, quam ideæ inadæquatæ, sive mutilatæ et confusæ involvunt.\n\nDEMONSTRATIO\n\nNihil in ideis positivum datur, quod falsitatis formam constituat (per Prop. 33 hujus) ; at falsitas in absolula privatione consistere nequit (Mentes enim, non Corpora, errare nec falli dicuntur), neque etiam in absoluta ignorantia ; diversa enim sunt ignorare et errare ; quare in cognitionis privatione, quam rerum inadæquata cognitio, sive ideæ inadæquatæ et confusæ involvunt, consistit. Q. E. D.\n\nSCHOLIUM\n\nIn Scholio Prop. 17 hujus Partis explicui, qua ratione error in cognitionis privatione consistit ; sed ad uberiorem hujus rei explicationem exemplum dabo. Nempe falluntur homines, quod se liberos esse putant ; quæ opinio in hoc solo consistit, quod suarum actionum sint conscii, et ignari causarum, a quibus determinantur. Hæc ergo est eorum libertatis idea, quod suarum actionum nullam cognoscant causam. Nam quod ajunt, humanas actiones a voluntate pendere, verba sunt, quorum nullam habent ideam. Quid enim voluntas sit, et quomodo moveat Corpus, ignorant omnes ; qui aliud jactant et animæ sedes et habitacula fingunt, vel risum vel nauseam movere solent. Sic cum solem intuemur, eum ducentos circiter pedes a nobis distare imaginamur ; qui error in hac sola imaginatione non consistit, sed in eo, quod dum ipsum sic imaginamur, veram ejus distantiam, et hujus imaginationis causam ignoramus. Nam tametsi postea cognoscamus eundem ultra 600 terræ diametros a nobis distare, ipsum nihilominus prope adesse imaginabimur ; non enim solem adeo propinquum imaginamur propterea, quod veram ejus distantiam ignoramus, sed propterea, quod affectio nostri Corporis essentiam solis involvit, quatenus ipsum Corpus ab eodem afficitur.\n\nPROPOSITIO XXXVI\n\nIdeæ inadæquatae et confusæ eadem necessitate consequuntur, ac adæquatæ sive claræ ac distinctæ ideæ.\n\nDEMONSTRATIO\n\nIdeæ omnes in Deo sunt (per Prop. 15 p. I) ; et, quatenus ad Deum referuntur, sunt veræ (per Prop. 32 hujus) et (per Coroll. Prop. 7 hujus) adæquatæ ; adeoque nullæ inadæquatæ nec confusæ sunt, nisi quatenus ad singularem alicujus Mentem referuntur (qua de re vide Prop. 24 et 28 hujus) : adeoque omnes, tam adæquatæ quam inadæquatæ eadem necessitate (per Coroll. Prop. 6 hujus) consequuntur. Q. E. D.\n\nPROPOSITIO XXXVII\n\nId, quod omnibus commune (de his vide supra Lemma 2), quodque æque in parte ac in toto est, nullius rei singularis essentiam constituit.\n\nDEMONSTRATIO\n\nSi negas, concipe, si fieri potest, id essentiam alicujus rei singularis constituere ; nempe essentiam B. Ergo (per Defin. 2 hujus) id sine B non poterit esse neque concipi ; atqui hoc est contra hypothesin : Ergo id ad essentiam B non pertinet, nec alterius rei singularis essentiam constituit. Q. E. D.\n\nPROPOSITIO XXXVIII\n\nIlla, quæ omnibus communia, quæque æque in parte ac in toto sunt, non possunt concipi nisi adæquate.\n\nDEMONSTRATIO\n\nSit A aliquid, quod omnibus corporibus commune, quodque æque in parte cujuscunque corporis ac in toto est. Dico A non posse concipi nisi adæquate. Nam ejus idea (per Coroll. Prop. 7 hujus) erit necessario in Deo adæquata, tam quatenus ideam Corporis humani, quam quatenus ideas habet ejusdem affectionum, quæ (per Prop. 16, 25 et 27 hujus) tam Corporis humani quam corporum externorum naturam ex parte involvunt ; hoc est (per Prop. 12 et 13 hujus), hæc idea erit necessario in Deo adæquata, quatenus Mentem humanam constituit, sive quatenus ideas habet, quæ in Mente humana sunt ; Mens igitur (per Coroll. prop. 11 hujus) A necessario adæquate percipit, idque tam quatenus se, quam quatenus suum, vel quodcunque externum corpus percipit, nec A alio modo potest concipi. Q. E. D.\n\nCOROLLARIUM\n\nHinc sequitur, dari quasdam ideas, sive notiones, omnibus hominibus communes ; nam (per Lem. II) omnia corpora in quibusdam conveniunt, quæ (per Prop. præced.) ab omnibus debent adæquate, sive clare et distincte percipi.\n\nPROPOSITIO XXXIX\n\nId, quod Corpori humano et quibusdam corporibus externis, a quibus Corpus humanum affici solet, commune est et proprium, quodque in cujuscunque horum parte æque est ac in toto, ejus etiam idea erit in Mente adæquata.\n\nDEMONSTRATIO\n\nSit A id, quod Corpori humano et quibusdam corporibus externis commune est et proprium, quodque æque in humano Corpore ac in iisdem corporibus externis, et quod denique æque in cujuscunque corporis externi parte ac in toto est. Ipsius A dabitur in Deo idea adæquata (per Coroll. Prop. 7 hujus), tam quatenus ideam Corporis humani, quam quatenus positorum corporum externorum ideas habet. Ponatur jam humanum Corpus a corpore externo affici per id, quod cum eo habet commune, hoc est ab A : hujus affectionis idea proprietatem A involvet (per Prop. 16 hujus), atque adeo (per idem Coroll. Prop. 7 hujus) idea hujus affectionis, quatenus proprietatem A involvit, erit in Deo adæquata, quatenus idea Corporis humani affectus est ; hoc est (per Prop. 13 hujus) quatenus Mentis humanæ naturam constituit ; adeoque (per Coroll. Prop. 11 hujus) hæc idea est etiam in Mente humana adæquata. Q. E. D.\n\nCOROLLARIUM\n\nHinc sequitur, quod Mens eo aptior est ad plura adæquate percipiendum, quo ejus Corpus plura habet cum aliis corporibus communia.\n\nPROPOSITIO XL\n\nQuæcunque ideæ in Mente sequuntur ex ideis, quæ in ipsa sunt adæquatæ, sunt etiam adæquatæ.\n\nDEMONSTRATIO\n\npatet. Nam cum dicimus, in Mente humana ideam sequi ex ideis, quæ in ipsa sunt adæquatæ, nihil aliud dicimus (per Coroll. Prop. 11 hujus), quam quod in ipso Divino intellectu detur idea, cujus Deus est causa, non quatenus infinitus est, nec quatenus plurimarum rerum singularium ideis affectus est, sed quatenus tantum humanæ Mentis essentiam constituit.\n\nSCHOLIUM I\n\nHis causam Notionum, quæ Communes vocantur ; quæque ratiocini nostri fundamenta sunt, explicui. Sed aliæ quorundam axiomatum sive notionum communium causæ dantur, quas hac nostra methodo explicare e re foret ; ex iis namque constaret, quænam notiones præ reliquis utiliores, quænam vero vix ullius usus essent. Deinde quænam communes, et quænam iis tantum, qui præjudiciis non laborant, claræ et distinctæ, et quænam denique male fundatæ sint. Præterea constaret, unde notiones illas, quas Secundas vocant, et consequenter axiomata, quæ in iisdem fundantur, suam duxerunt originem, et alia, quæ circa hæc aliquando meditatus sum. Sed quoniam hæc alii dicavi Tractatui, et etiam, ne propter nimiam hujus rei prolixitatem fastidium crearem, hac re hic supersedere decrevi. Attamen ne quid horum omittam, quod scitu necessarium sit, causas breviter addam, ex quibus terinini Transcendentales dicti suam duxerunt originem, ut Ens, Res, Aliquid. Hi termini ex hoc oriuntur, quod scilicet humanum Corpus, quandoquidem limitatum est, tantum est capax certi imaginum numeri (quid imago sit explicui in Schol. Prop. 17 hujus) in se distincte simul formandi ; qui si excedatur, hæ imagines confundi incipient ; et si hic imaginum numerus, quarum Corpus est capax, ut eas in se simul distincte formet, longe excedatur, omnes inter se plane confundentur. Cum hoc ita se habeat, patet ex Coroll. Prop. 17 et Prop. 18 hujus, quod Mens humana tot corpora distincte simul imaginari poterit, quot in ipsius Corpore imagines possunt simul formari. At ubi imagines incorpore plane confunduntur, Mens etiam omnia corpora confuse sine ulla distinctione imaginabitur, et quasi sub uno attributo comprehendet, nempe sub attributo Entis, Rei, etc. Potest hoc etiam ex eo deduci, quod imagines non semper æque vigeant, et ex aliis causis his analogis, quas hic explicare non est opus ; nam ad nostrum, ad quem collineamus, scopum unam tantum sufficit considerare. Nam omnes huc redeunt, quod hi termini ideas significent summo gradu confusas. Ex similibus deinde causis ortæ sunt notiones illæ, quas Universales vocant, ut Homo, Equus, Canis, etc. videlicet, quia in Corpore humano tot imagines ex. gr. hominum formantur simul, ut vim imaginandi, non quidem penitus, sed eo usque tamen superent, ut singulorum parvas differentias (videlicet uniuscujusque colorem magnitudinem, etc.) eorumque determinatum numerum Mens imaginari nequeat, et id tantum, in quo omnes, quatenus Corpus ab iisdem afficitur, conveniunt, distincte imaginetur ; nam ab eo Corpus maxime, scilicet ab unoquoque singulari, affectum fuit ; atque hoc nomine hominis exprimit, hocque de infinitis singularibus prædicat. Nam singularium determinatum numerum, ut diximus, imaginari nequit. Sed notandum, has notiones non ab omnibus eodem modo formari, sed apud unumquemque variare pro ratione rei, a qua Corpus affectum sæpius fuit, quamque facilius Mens imaginatur vel recordatur. Ex. gr. qui sæpius cum admiratione hominum staturam contemplati sunt, sub nomine hominis intelligent animal erectæ staturæ ; qui vero aliud assueti sunt contemplari, aliam hominum communem imaginem formabunt, nempe, hominem esse animal risibile ; animal bipes sine plumis ; animal rationale ; et sic de reliquis unusquisque pro dispositione sui corporis rerum universales imagines formabit. Quare non mirum est quod inter Philosophos, qui res naturales per solas rerum imagines explicare voluerunt, tot sint ortæ controversiæ.\n\nSCHOLIUM II\n\nEx omnibus supra dictis clare apparet, nos multa percipere, et notiones universales formare I° ex singularibus, nobis per sensus mutilate, confuse, et sine ordine ad intellectum repræsentais (Vide Coroll. Prop. 29 hujus) : et ideo tales perceptiones cognitionem ab experientia vaga vocare consuevi ; II° ex signis, ex. gr. ex eo, quod auditis aut lectis quibusdam verbis rerum recordemur, et earum quasdam ideas formemus similes iis, per quas res imaginamur (Vide Schol. Prop. 18 hujus). Utrumque hune res contemplandi modum cognitionem primi generis, opinionem, vel Imaginationem in posterum vocalio ; III° denique ex eo, quod notiones communes, rerumque proprietatum ideas adæquatas habemus (Vide Coroll. Prop. 38 et 39 cum ejus Coroll. et Prop. 40 hujus) ; atque hunc Rationem et secundi generis cognitionem vocabo. Præter hæc duo cognitionis genera datur, ut in sequentihus ostendam, aliud tertium, quod Scientiam Intuitivam vocabimus. Atque hoc cognoscendi genus procedit ab adæquata idea essentiæ formalis quorundam Dei attributorum ad adæquatam cognitionem essentiæ rerum. Hæc omnia unius rei exemplo explicabo. Dantur ex. gr. tres numeri, ad quartum obtinendum, qui sit ad tertium ut secundus ad primum. Non dubitant mercatores secundum in tertium ducere, et productum per primum dividere ; quia scilicet ea, quæ a magistro, absque ulla demonstratione audiverunt, nondum tradiderunt oblivioni, vel quia id sæpe in numeris simplicissimis experti sunt, vel ex vi Demonstrationis Prop. 19 lib. VII Euclid., nempe ex communi proprietate proportionalium. At in numeris simplicissimis nihil horum opus est. Ex. gr. datis numeris 1, 2, 3, nemo non videt, quartum numerum proportionalem esse 6, atque hoc multo clarius, quia ex ipsa ratione, quam primum ad secundum habere uno intuitu videmus, ipsum quartum concludimus.\n\nPROPOSITIO XLI\n\nCognitio primi gencris unica est falsitatis causa, secundi autem et tertii est necessario vera.\n\nDEMONSTRATIO\n\nAd primi generis cognitionem illas omnes ideas diximus in præced. Schol. pertinere, quæ sunt inadæquatæ et confusæ ; atque adeo (per Prop. 35 hujus) hæc cognitio unica est falsitatis causa. Deinde ad cognitionem secundi et tertii illas pertinere diximus, quæ sunt adæquatæ ; adeoque (per Prop. 34 hujus) est necessario vera. Q. E. D..\n\nPROPOSITIO XLII\n\nSecundi et tertii, et non primi generis cognitio docet nos verum a falso distinguere.\n\nDEMONSTRATIO\n\nHæc Propositio per se patet. Qui enim inter verum et falsum scit distinguere, debet adæquatam veri et falsi habere ideam ; hoc est (per 2 Schol. Prop. 40 hujus), verum et falsum secundo aut tertio cognitionis genere cognoscere.\n\nPROPOSITIO XLIII\n\nQui veram habet ideam, simul scit se veram habere ideam, nec de rei veritate potest dubitare.\n\nDEMONSTRATIO\n\nIdea vera in nobis est illa, quæ in Deo, quatenus per naturam Mentis humanæ explicatur, est adæquata (per Coroll. Prop. 11 hujus). Ponamus itaque, dari in Deo, quatenus per naturam Mentis humanæ explicatur, ideam adæquatam A. Hujus ideæ debet necessario dari etiam in Deo idea, quæ ad Deum eodem modo refertur ac idea A (per Prop. 20 hujus, cujus Demonstratio universalis est). At idea A ad Deum referri supponitur, quatenus per naturam Mentis humanæ explicatur ; ergo etiam idea ideæ A ad Deum eodem modo debet referri ; hoc est (per idem Coroll. Prop. 11 hujus), hæc adæquata idea ideæ A erit in ipsa Mente, quæ ideam adæquatam A habet ; adeoque qui adæquatam habet ideam, sive (per Prop. 34 hujus) qui vere rem cognoscit, debet simul suæ cognitionis adæquatam habere ideam, sive veram cognitionem ; hoc est (ut per se manifestum), debet simul esse certus. Q. E. D.\n\nSCHOLIUM\n\nIn Scholio Propositions 21 hujus Partis explicui, quid sit idea ideæ ; sed notandum, præcedentem Propositionem per se satis esse manifestam. Nam nemo, qui veram habet ideam, ignorat veram ideam summam certitudinem involvere ; veram namque habere ideam nihil aliud significat quam perfecte sive optime rem cognoscere ; nec sane aliquis de hac re dubitare potest, nisi putet, ideam quid mutum instar picturæ in tabula, et non modum cogitandi esse, nempe ipsum intelligere ; et, quæso, quis scire potest, se rem aliquam intelligere, nisi prius rem intelligat ? hoc est, quis potest scire, se de aliqua re certum esse, nisi prius de ea re certus sit ? Deinde quid idea vera clarius et certius dari potest, quod norma sit veritatis ? Sane sicut lux seipsam et tenebras manifestat, sic veritas norma sui et falsi est. Atque his me ad has quæstiones respondisse puto, nempe : si idea vera, quatenus tantum dicitur cum suo ideato convenire, a falsa distinguitur ; nihil ergo realitatis aut perfectionis idea vera habet præ falsa (quandoquidem per solam denominationem extrinsecam distinguuntur) et consequenter neque etiam homo, qui veras, præ illo, qui falsas tantum ideas habet ? Deinde, undefit, ut homines falsas habeant ideas ? Et denique, unde aliquis certo scire potest, se ideas habere, quæ cum suis ideatis conveniant ? At has, inquam, quæstiones me jam respondisse puto. Nam quod ad differentiam inter ideam veram et falsam attinet, constat ex Propositione 35 hujus, illam ad hanc sese habere, ut ens ad non ens. Falsitatis autem causas a Propositione 19 usque ad 35 cum ejus Scholio clarissime ostendi. Ex quibus etiam apparet, quid homo, qui veras habet ideas, homini, qui non nisi falsas habet, intersit. Quod denique ultimum attinet, nempe, undenam homo scire potest se habere ideam, quæ cum suo ideato conveniat ; id modo satis superque ostendi, ex hoc solo oriri, quod ideam habet, quæ cum suo ideato convenit, sive quod veritas sui sit norma. His adde, quod Mens nostra quatenus res vere percipit, pars et infiniti Dei intellectus (per Coroll. Prop. 11 hujus) ; adeoque tam necesse est, ut Mentis claræ et distincte ideæ veræ sint, ac Dei ideæ.\n\nPROPOSITIO XLIV\n\nDe natura Rationis non est, res ut contingentes, sed ut necessarias contemplari.\n\nDEMONSTRATIO\n\nDe natura Rationis est, res vere percipere (per Prop. 41 hujus) nempe (par Ax. 6 p. I) ut in se sunt, hoc est (per Prop. 29 p. I) non ut contingentes, sed ut necessarias. Q. E. D.\n\nCOROLLARIUM I\n\nHinc sequitur, a sola imaginatione pendere, quod res, tam respectu præteriti quam futuri, ut contingentes contemplemur.\n\nSCHOLIUM\n\nQua autem ratione hoc fiat, paucis explicabo. Ostendimus supra (Prop. 17 hujus cum ejus Coroll.), Mentem, quamvis res non existant, eas tamen semper ut sibi præsentes imaginari, nisi causæ occurrant, quæ earum præsentem existentiam secludant. Deinde (Prop. 18 hujus) ostendimus, quod, si Corpus humanum semel a duobus corporibus externis simul affectum fuit, ubi Mens postea eorum alterutrum imaginabitur, statim et alterius recordabitur, hoc est, ambo ut sibi præsentia contemplabitur, nisi causæ occurrant, quæ eorum præsentem existentiam secludant. Præterea nemo dubitat, quin etiam tempus imaginemur, nempe ex eo, quod corpora alia aliis tardius, vel celerius, vel æque celeriter moveri imaginemur. Ponamus itaque puerum, qui heri prima vice hora matutina viderit Petrum, meridiana autem Paulum, et vespertina Simeonem, atque hodie iterum matutina hora Petrum. Ex Propositione 18 hujus patet, quod simulac matutinam lucem videt, illico solem eandem cæli, quam die præcedenti, viderit, partem percurrentem, sive diem integrum, et simul cum tempore matutino Petrum, cum meridiano autem Paulum, et cum vespertino Simeonem imaginabitur, hoc est, Pauli et Simeonis existentiam cum relatione ad futurum tempus imaginabitur ; et contra, si hora vespertina Simeonem videat, Paulum et Petrum ad tempus præteritum referet, eosdem scilicet simul cum tempore præterito imaginando ; atque hæc eo constantius, quo sæpius eos eodem hoc ordine viderit. Quod si aliquando contingat, ut alia quadam vespera loco Simeonis Jacobum videat, tum sequenti mane cum tempore vespertino jam Simeonem, jam Jacobum, non vero ambos simul imaginabitur. Nam alterutrum tantum, non autem ambos simul tempore vespertino vidisse supponitur. Fluctuabitur itaque ejus imaginatio, et cum futuro tempore vespertino jam hunc jam illum imaginabitur, hoc est, neutrum certo, sed utrumque contingenter futurum contemplabitur. Atque hæc imaginationis fluctuatio eadem erit, si imaginatio rerum sit, quas eodem modo cum relations ad tempus præteritum vel præsens contemplamur ; et consequenter res, tam ad tempus præsens quam ad præteritum vel futurum relatas ut contingentes imaginabimur.\n\nCOROLLARIUM II\n\nDe natura Rationis est, res sub quadam æternitatis specie percipere.\n\nDEMONSTRATIO\n\nDe natura enim Rationis est, res ut necessarias, et non ut contingentes contemplari (per Prop. præced.). Hanc autem rerum necessitatem (per Prop. 41 hujus) vere, hoc est (per Axiom. 6 p. I) ut in se est, percipit. Sed (per Prop. 16 p. I) hæc rerum necessitas est ipsa Dei æternæ naturæ necessitas. Ergo de natura Rationis est, res sub hac æternitatis specie contemplari. Adde, quod fundamenta Rationis notiones sint (per Prop. 38 hujus), quæ illa explicant, quæ omnibus communia sunt, quæque (per Prop. 37 hujus) nullius rei singularis essentiam explicant ; quæque propterea absque ulla temporis relatione, sed sub quadam æternitatis specie debent concipi. Q. E. D.\n\nPROPOSITIO XLV\n\nUnaquæque cujuscunque corporis, vel rei singularis actu existentis, idea Dei æternam et infinitam essentiam necessario involvit.\n\nDEMONSTRATIO","paragraphs":[{"id":"RAW_QGOH_PS_3:P0001","text":"Idea, quæ naturam Mentis humanæ constituit, demonstratur eodem modo non esse, in se sola considerata, clara et distincta ; ut etiam idea Mentis humanæ, et ideæ idearum affectionum Corporis humani, quatenus ad solam Mentem referuntur, quod unusquisque facile videre potest.","kind":"passage"},{"id":"RAW_QGOH_PS_3:P0002","text":"PROPOSITIO XXIX","kind":"passage"},{"id":"RAW_QGOH_PS_3:P0003","text":"Idea ideæ cujuscunque affectionis Corporis humani adæquatam humanæ Mentis cognitionem non involvit.","kind":"passage"},{"id":"RAW_QGOH_PS_3:P0004","text":"DEMONSTRATIO","kind":"passage"},{"id":"RAW_QGOH_PS_3:P0005","text":"Idea enim affectionis Corporis humani (per Prop. 27 hujus) adæquatam ipsius Corporis cognitionem non involvit, sive ejus naturam adæquate non exprimit ; hoc est (per Prop. 13 hujus), cum natura Mentis non convenit adæquate ; adeoque (per Axiom. 6 p. I) hujus ideæ idea adæquate humanæ Mentis naturam non exprimit, sive adæquatam ejus cognitionem non involvit. Q. E. D.","kind":"passage"},{"id":"RAW_QGOH_PS_3:P0006","text":"COROLLARIUM","kind":"passage"},{"id":"RAW_QGOH_PS_3:P0007","text":"Hinc sequitur, Mentem humanam, quoties ex communi Naturæ ordine res percipit, nec sui ipsius, nec sui Corporis, nec corporum externorum adæquatam, sed confusam tantum et mutilatam habere cognitionem. Nam Mens se ipsam non cognoscit, nisi quatenus ideas affectionum Corporis percipit (per Prop. 23 hujus). Corpus autem suum (per Prop. 19 hujus) non percipit, nisi per ipsas affectionum ideas, per quas etiam tantum (per Prop. 26 hujus) corpora externa percipit ; atque adeo, quatenus eas habet, nec sui ipsius (per Prop. 29 hujus), nec sui Corporis (per Prop. 27 hujus), nec corporum externorum (per Prop. 25 hujus) habet adæquatam cognitionem, sed tantum (per Prop. 28 hujus cum ejus Schol.) mutilatam et confusam. Q. E. D.","kind":"passage"},{"id":"RAW_QGOH_PS_3:P0008","text":"SCHOLIUM","kind":"passage"},{"id":"RAW_QGOH_PS_3:P0009","text":"Dico expresse, quod Mens nec sui ipsius, nec sui Corporis, nec corporum externorum adæquatam, sed confusam tantum cognitionem habeat, quoties ex communi Naturæ ordine res percipit ; hoc est, quoties externe, ex rerum nempe fortuito occursu, determinatur ad hoc vel illud contemplandum, et non quoties interne, ex eo scilicet, quod res plures simul contemplatur, determinatur ad earundem convenientias, differentias et oppugnantias intelligendum ; quoties enim hoc vel alio modo interne disponitur, tum res clare et distincte contemplatur, ut infra ostendam.","kind":"passage"},{"id":"RAW_QGOH_PS_3:P0010","text":"PROPOSITIO XXX","kind":"passage"},{"id":"RAW_QGOH_PS_3:P0011","text":"Nos de duratione nostri Corporis nullam nisi admodum inadæquatam cognitionem habere possumus.","kind":"passage"},{"id":"RAW_QGOH_PS_3:P0012","text":"DEMONSTRATIO","kind":"passage"},{"id":"RAW_QGOH_PS_3:P0013","text":"Nostri corporis duratio ab ejus essentia non dependet (per Ax. I hujus), nec etiam ab absoluta Dei natura (per Prop. 21 p. I). Sed (per Prop. 28 p. I) ad existendum et operandum determinatur a talibus causis, quæ etiam ab aliis determinatæ sunt ad existendum et operandum certa ac determinata ratione, et hæ iterum ab aliis, et sic in infinitum. Nostri igitur Corporis duratio a communi Naturæ ordine et rerum constitutione pendet. Qua autem ratione res constitutæ sint, ejus rei adæquata cognitio datur in Deo, quatenus earum omnium ideas, et non quatenus tantum humani Corporis ideam habet (per Coroll. Prop. 9 hujus) ; quare cognitio durationis nostri Corporis est in Deo admodum inadæquata, quatenus tantum naturam Mentis humanæ constituere consideratur ; hoc est (per Coroll. Prop. 11 hujus), hæc cognitio est in nostra Mente admodum inadæquata. Q. E. D.","kind":"passage"},{"id":"RAW_QGOH_PS_3:P0014","text":"PROPOSITIO XXXI","kind":"passage"},{"id":"RAW_QGOH_PS_3:P0015","text":"Nos de duratione rerum singularium, quæ extra nos sunt, nullam nisi admodum inadæquatam cognitionem habere possumus.","kind":"passage"},{"id":"RAW_QGOH_PS_3:P0016","text":"DEMONSTRATIO","kind":"passage"},{"id":"RAW_QGOH_PS_3:P0017","text":"Unaquæque enim res singularis, sicuti humanum Corpus, ab alia re singulari determinari debet ad existendum et operandum certa ac determinata ratione ; et hæc iterum ab alia, et sic in infinitum (per Prop. 28 p. I). Cum autem ex hac communi rerum singularium proprietate in præcedenti Prop. demonstraverimus, nos de duratione nostri Corporis non nisi admodum inadæquatam cognitionem habere ; ergo hoc idem de rerum singularium duratione erit concludendum, quod scilicet ejus non nisi admodum inadæquatam cognitionem habere possumus. Q. E. D.","kind":"passage"},{"id":"RAW_QGOH_PS_3:P0018","text":"COROLLARIUM","kind":"passage"},{"id":"RAW_QGOH_PS_3:P0019","text":"Hinc sequitur, omnes res particulares contingentes et corruptibiles esse. Nam de earum duratione nullam adæquatam cognitionem habere possumus (per Prop. præced.), et hoc est id, quod per rerum contingentiam et corruptionis possibilitatem nobis est intelligendum (vide Schol. 1 Prop. 33 p. I). Nam (per Prop 29 p. I) præter hoc nullum datur contingens.","kind":"passage"},{"id":"RAW_QGOH_PS_3:P0020","text":"PROPOSITIO XXXII","kind":"passage"},{"id":"RAW_QGOH_PS_3:P0021","text":"Omnes ideæ, quatenus ad Deum referuntur, veræ sunt.","kind":"passage"},{"id":"RAW_QGOH_PS_3:P0022","text":"DEMONSTRATIO","kind":"passage"},{"id":"RAW_QGOH_PS_3:P0023","text":"Omnes enim ideæ, quæ in Deo sunt, cum suis ideatis omnino conveniunt (per Coroll. Prop. 7 hujus), adeoque (per Ax. 6 p. I) omnes veræ sunt. Q. E. D.","kind":"passage"},{"id":"RAW_QGOH_PS_3:P0024","text":"PROPOSITIO XXXIII","kind":"passage"},{"id":"RAW_QGOH_PS_3:P0025","text":"Nihil in ideis positivum est, propter quod falsæ dicuntur.","kind":"passage"},{"id":"RAW_QGOH_PS_3:P0026","text":"DEMONSTRATIO","kind":"passage"},{"id":"RAW_QGOH_PS_3:P0027","text":"Si negas, concipe, si fieri potest, modum positivum cogitandi, qui formam erroris sive falsitatis constituat. Hic cogitandi modus non potest esse in Deo (per Prop. præced.) ; extra Deum autem etiam nec esse nec concipi potest (per Prop. 15, p. I). Atque adeo nihil potest dari positivum in ideis, propter quod falsæ dicuntur. Q. E. D.","kind":"passage"},{"id":"RAW_QGOH_PS_3:P0028","text":"PROPOSITIO XXXIV","kind":"passage"},{"id":"RAW_QGOH_PS_3:P0029","text":"Omnis idea, quæ in nobis est absoluta, sive adæquata et perfecta vera est.","kind":"passage"},{"id":"RAW_QGOH_PS_3:P0030","text":"DEMONSTRATIO","kind":"passage"},{"id":"RAW_QGOH_PS_3:P0031","text":"Cum dicimus, dari in nobis ideam adæquatam et perfectam, nihil aliud dicimus (per Coroll. Prop. 11 hujus), quam quod in Deo, quatenus nostræ Mentis essentiam constituit, detur idea adæquata et perfecta ; et consequenter (per Prop. 32 hujus) nihil aliud dicimus, quam quod talis idea sit vera. Q. E. D.","kind":"passage"},{"id":"RAW_QGOH_PS_3:P0032","text":"PROPOSITIO XXXV","kind":"passage"},{"id":"RAW_QGOH_PS_3:P0033","text":"Falsitas consistit in cognitionis privatione, quam ideæ inadæquatæ, sive mutilatæ et confusæ involvunt.","kind":"passage"},{"id":"RAW_QGOH_PS_3:P0034","text":"DEMONSTRATIO","kind":"passage"},{"id":"RAW_QGOH_PS_3:P0035","text":"Nihil in ideis positivum datur, quod falsitatis formam constituat (per Prop. 33 hujus) ; at falsitas in absolula privatione consistere nequit (Mentes enim, non Corpora, errare nec falli dicuntur), neque etiam in absoluta ignorantia ; diversa enim sunt ignorare et errare ; quare in cognitionis privatione, quam rerum inadæquata cognitio, sive ideæ inadæquatæ et confusæ involvunt, consistit. Q. E. D.","kind":"passage"},{"id":"RAW_QGOH_PS_3:P0036","text":"SCHOLIUM","kind":"passage"},{"id":"RAW_QGOH_PS_3:P0037","text":"In Scholio Prop. 17 hujus Partis explicui, qua ratione error in cognitionis privatione consistit ; sed ad uberiorem hujus rei explicationem exemplum dabo. Nempe falluntur homines, quod se liberos esse putant ; quæ opinio in hoc solo consistit, quod suarum actionum sint conscii, et ignari causarum, a quibus determinantur. Hæc ergo est eorum libertatis idea, quod suarum actionum nullam cognoscant causam. Nam quod ajunt, humanas actiones a voluntate pendere, verba sunt, quorum nullam habent ideam. Quid enim voluntas sit, et quomodo moveat Corpus, ignorant omnes ; qui aliud jactant et animæ sedes et habitacula fingunt, vel risum vel nauseam movere solent. Sic cum solem intuemur, eum ducentos circiter pedes a nobis distare imaginamur ; qui error in hac sola imaginatione non consistit, sed in eo, quod dum ipsum sic imaginamur, veram ejus distantiam, et hujus imaginationis causam ignoramus.","kind":"passage"},{"id":"RAW_QGOH_PS_3:P0038","text":"Nam tametsi postea cognoscamus eundem ultra 600 terræ diametros a nobis distare, ipsum nihilominus prope adesse imaginabimur ; non enim solem adeo propinquum imaginamur propterea, quod veram ejus distantiam ignoramus, sed propterea, quod affectio nostri Corporis essentiam solis involvit, quatenus ipsum Corpus ab eodem afficitur.","kind":"passage"},{"id":"RAW_QGOH_PS_3:P0039","text":"PROPOSITIO XXXVI","kind":"passage"},{"id":"RAW_QGOH_PS_3:P0040","text":"Ideæ inadæquatae et confusæ eadem necessitate consequuntur, ac adæquatæ sive claræ ac distinctæ ideæ.","kind":"passage"},{"id":"RAW_QGOH_PS_3:P0041","text":"DEMONSTRATIO","kind":"passage"},{"id":"RAW_QGOH_PS_3:P0042","text":"Ideæ omnes in Deo sunt (per Prop. 15 p. I) ; et, quatenus ad Deum referuntur, sunt veræ (per Prop. 32 hujus) et (per Coroll. Prop. 7 hujus) adæquatæ ; adeoque nullæ inadæquatæ nec confusæ sunt, nisi quatenus ad singularem alicujus Mentem referuntur (qua de re vide Prop. 24 et 28 hujus) : adeoque omnes, tam adæquatæ quam inadæquatæ eadem necessitate (per Coroll. Prop. 6 hujus) consequuntur. Q. E. D.","kind":"passage"},{"id":"RAW_QGOH_PS_3:P0043","text":"PROPOSITIO XXXVII","kind":"passage"},{"id":"RAW_QGOH_PS_3:P0044","text":"Id, quod omnibus commune (de his vide supra Lemma 2), quodque æque in parte ac in toto est, nullius rei singularis essentiam constituit.","kind":"passage"},{"id":"RAW_QGOH_PS_3:P0045","text":"DEMONSTRATIO","kind":"passage"},{"id":"RAW_QGOH_PS_3:P0046","text":"Si negas, concipe, si fieri potest, id essentiam alicujus rei singularis constituere ; nempe essentiam B. Ergo (per Defin. 2 hujus) id sine B non poterit esse neque concipi ; atqui hoc est contra hypothesin : Ergo id ad essentiam B non pertinet, nec alterius rei singularis essentiam constituit. Q. E. D.","kind":"passage"},{"id":"RAW_QGOH_PS_3:P0047","text":"PROPOSITIO XXXVIII","kind":"passage"},{"id":"RAW_QGOH_PS_3:P0048","text":"Illa, quæ omnibus communia, quæque æque in parte ac in toto sunt, non possunt concipi nisi adæquate.","kind":"passage"},{"id":"RAW_QGOH_PS_3:P0049","text":"DEMONSTRATIO","kind":"passage"},{"id":"RAW_QGOH_PS_3:P0050","text":"Sit A aliquid, quod omnibus corporibus commune, quodque æque in parte cujuscunque corporis ac in toto est. Dico A non posse concipi nisi adæquate. Nam ejus idea (per Coroll. Prop. 7 hujus) erit necessario in Deo adæquata, tam quatenus ideam Corporis humani, quam quatenus ideas habet ejusdem affectionum, quæ (per Prop. 16, 25 et 27 hujus) tam Corporis humani quam corporum externorum naturam ex parte involvunt ; hoc est (per Prop. 12 et 13 hujus), hæc idea erit necessario in Deo adæquata, quatenus Mentem humanam constituit, sive quatenus ideas habet, quæ in Mente humana sunt ; Mens igitur (per Coroll. prop. 11 hujus) A necessario adæquate percipit, idque tam quatenus se, quam quatenus suum, vel quodcunque externum corpus percipit, nec A alio modo potest concipi. Q. E. D.","kind":"passage"},{"id":"RAW_QGOH_PS_3:P0051","text":"COROLLARIUM","kind":"passage"},{"id":"RAW_QGOH_PS_3:P0052","text":"Hinc sequitur, dari quasdam ideas, sive notiones, omnibus hominibus communes ; nam (per Lem. II) omnia corpora in quibusdam conveniunt, quæ (per Prop. præced.) ab omnibus debent adæquate, sive clare et distincte percipi.","kind":"passage"},{"id":"RAW_QGOH_PS_3:P0053","text":"PROPOSITIO XXXIX","kind":"passage"},{"id":"RAW_QGOH_PS_3:P0054","text":"Id, quod Corpori humano et quibusdam corporibus externis, a quibus Corpus humanum affici solet, commune est et proprium, quodque in cujuscunque horum parte æque est ac in toto, ejus etiam idea erit in Mente adæquata.","kind":"passage"},{"id":"RAW_QGOH_PS_3:P0055","text":"DEMONSTRATIO","kind":"passage"},{"id":"RAW_QGOH_PS_3:P0056","text":"Sit A id, quod Corpori humano et quibusdam corporibus externis commune est et proprium, quodque æque in humano Corpore ac in iisdem corporibus externis, et quod denique æque in cujuscunque corporis externi parte ac in toto est. Ipsius A dabitur in Deo idea adæquata (per Coroll. Prop. 7 hujus), tam quatenus ideam Corporis humani, quam quatenus positorum corporum externorum ideas habet. Ponatur jam humanum Corpus a corpore externo affici per id, quod cum eo habet commune, hoc est ab A : hujus affectionis idea proprietatem A involvet (per Prop. 16 hujus), atque adeo (per idem Coroll. Prop. 7 hujus) idea hujus affectionis, quatenus proprietatem A involvit, erit in Deo adæquata, quatenus idea Corporis humani affectus est ; hoc est (per Prop. 13 hujus) quatenus Mentis humanæ naturam constituit ; adeoque (per Coroll. Prop. 11 hujus) hæc idea est etiam in Mente humana adæquata. Q. E. D.","kind":"passage"},{"id":"RAW_QGOH_PS_3:P0057","text":"COROLLARIUM","kind":"passage"},{"id":"RAW_QGOH_PS_3:P0058","text":"Hinc sequitur, quod Mens eo aptior est ad plura adæquate percipiendum, quo ejus Corpus plura habet cum aliis corporibus communia.","kind":"passage"},{"id":"RAW_QGOH_PS_3:P0059","text":"PROPOSITIO XL","kind":"passage"},{"id":"RAW_QGOH_PS_3:P0060","text":"Quæcunque ideæ in Mente sequuntur ex ideis, quæ in ipsa sunt adæquatæ, sunt etiam adæquatæ.","kind":"passage"},{"id":"RAW_QGOH_PS_3:P0061","text":"DEMONSTRATIO","kind":"passage"},{"id":"RAW_QGOH_PS_3:P0062","text":"patet. Nam cum dicimus, in Mente humana ideam sequi ex ideis, quæ in ipsa sunt adæquatæ, nihil aliud dicimus (per Coroll. Prop. 11 hujus), quam quod in ipso Divino intellectu detur idea, cujus Deus est causa, non quatenus infinitus est, nec quatenus plurimarum rerum singularium ideis affectus est, sed quatenus tantum humanæ Mentis essentiam constituit.","kind":"passage"},{"id":"RAW_QGOH_PS_3:P0063","text":"SCHOLIUM I","kind":"passage"},{"id":"RAW_QGOH_PS_3:P0064","text":"His causam Notionum, quæ Communes vocantur ; quæque ratiocini nostri fundamenta sunt, explicui. Sed aliæ quorundam axiomatum sive notionum communium causæ dantur, quas hac nostra methodo explicare e re foret ; ex iis namque constaret, quænam notiones præ reliquis utiliores, quænam vero vix ullius usus essent. Deinde quænam communes, et quænam iis tantum, qui præjudiciis non laborant, claræ et distinctæ, et quænam denique male fundatæ sint. Præterea constaret, unde notiones illas, quas Secundas vocant, et consequenter axiomata, quæ in iisdem fundantur, suam duxerunt originem, et alia, quæ circa hæc aliquando meditatus sum. Sed quoniam hæc alii dicavi Tractatui, et etiam, ne propter nimiam hujus rei prolixitatem fastidium crearem, hac re hic supersedere decrevi.","kind":"passage"},{"id":"RAW_QGOH_PS_3:P0065","text":"Attamen ne quid horum omittam, quod scitu necessarium sit, causas breviter addam, ex quibus terinini Transcendentales dicti suam duxerunt originem, ut Ens, Res, Aliquid. Hi termini ex hoc oriuntur, quod scilicet humanum Corpus, quandoquidem limitatum est, tantum est capax certi imaginum numeri (quid imago sit explicui in Schol. Prop. 17 hujus) in se distincte simul formandi ; qui si excedatur, hæ imagines confundi incipient ; et si hic imaginum numerus, quarum Corpus est capax, ut eas in se simul distincte formet, longe excedatur, omnes inter se plane confundentur. Cum hoc ita se habeat, patet ex Coroll. Prop. 17 et Prop. 18 hujus, quod Mens humana tot corpora distincte simul imaginari poterit, quot in ipsius Corpore imagines possunt simul formari.","kind":"passage"},{"id":"RAW_QGOH_PS_3:P0066","text":"At ubi imagines incorpore plane confunduntur, Mens etiam omnia corpora confuse sine ulla distinctione imaginabitur, et quasi sub uno attributo comprehendet, nempe sub attributo Entis, Rei, etc. Potest hoc etiam ex eo deduci, quod imagines non semper æque vigeant, et ex aliis causis his analogis, quas hic explicare non est opus ; nam ad nostrum, ad quem collineamus, scopum unam tantum sufficit considerare. Nam omnes huc redeunt, quod hi termini ideas significent summo gradu confusas. Ex similibus deinde causis ortæ sunt notiones illæ, quas Universales vocant, ut Homo, Equus, Canis, etc. videlicet, quia in Corpore humano tot imagines ex. gr. hominum formantur simul, ut vim imaginandi, non quidem penitus, sed eo usque tamen superent, ut singulorum parvas differentias (videlicet uniuscujusque colorem magnitudinem, etc.)","kind":"passage"},{"id":"RAW_QGOH_PS_3:P0067","text":"eorumque determinatum numerum Mens imaginari nequeat, et id tantum, in quo omnes, quatenus Corpus ab iisdem afficitur, conveniunt, distincte imaginetur ; nam ab eo Corpus maxime, scilicet ab unoquoque singulari, affectum fuit ; atque hoc nomine hominis exprimit, hocque de infinitis singularibus prædicat. Nam singularium determinatum numerum, ut diximus, imaginari nequit. Sed notandum, has notiones non ab omnibus eodem modo formari, sed apud unumquemque variare pro ratione rei, a qua Corpus affectum sæpius fuit, quamque facilius Mens imaginatur vel recordatur. Ex. gr.","kind":"passage"},{"id":"RAW_QGOH_PS_3:P0068","text":"qui sæpius cum admiratione hominum staturam contemplati sunt, sub nomine hominis intelligent animal erectæ staturæ ; qui vero aliud assueti sunt contemplari, aliam hominum communem imaginem formabunt, nempe, hominem esse animal risibile ; animal bipes sine plumis ; animal rationale ; et sic de reliquis unusquisque pro dispositione sui corporis rerum universales imagines formabit. Quare non mirum est quod inter Philosophos, qui res naturales per solas rerum imagines explicare voluerunt, tot sint ortæ controversiæ.","kind":"passage"},{"id":"RAW_QGOH_PS_3:P0069","text":"SCHOLIUM II","kind":"passage"},{"id":"RAW_QGOH_PS_3:P0070","text":"Ex omnibus supra dictis clare apparet, nos multa percipere, et notiones universales formare I° ex singularibus, nobis per sensus mutilate, confuse, et sine ordine ad intellectum repræsentais (Vide Coroll. Prop. 29 hujus) : et ideo tales perceptiones cognitionem ab experientia vaga vocare consuevi ; II° ex signis, ex. gr. ex eo, quod auditis aut lectis quibusdam verbis rerum recordemur, et earum quasdam ideas formemus similes iis, per quas res imaginamur (Vide Schol. Prop. 18 hujus). Utrumque hune res contemplandi modum cognitionem primi generis, opinionem, vel Imaginationem in posterum vocalio ; III° denique ex eo, quod notiones communes, rerumque proprietatum ideas adæquatas habemus (Vide Coroll. Prop. 38 et 39 cum ejus Coroll. et Prop. 40 hujus) ; atque hunc Rationem et secundi generis cognitionem vocabo.","kind":"passage"},{"id":"RAW_QGOH_PS_3:P0071","text":"Præter hæc duo cognitionis genera datur, ut in sequentihus ostendam, aliud tertium, quod Scientiam Intuitivam vocabimus. Atque hoc cognoscendi genus procedit ab adæquata idea essentiæ formalis quorundam Dei attributorum ad adæquatam cognitionem essentiæ rerum. Hæc omnia unius rei exemplo explicabo. Dantur ex. gr. tres numeri, ad quartum obtinendum, qui sit ad tertium ut secundus ad primum. Non dubitant mercatores secundum in tertium ducere, et productum per primum dividere ; quia scilicet ea, quæ a magistro, absque ulla demonstratione audiverunt, nondum tradiderunt oblivioni, vel quia id sæpe in numeris simplicissimis experti sunt, vel ex vi Demonstrationis Prop. 19 lib. VII Euclid., nempe ex communi proprietate proportionalium. At in numeris simplicissimis nihil horum opus est. Ex. gr.","kind":"passage"},{"id":"RAW_QGOH_PS_3:P0072","text":"datis numeris 1, 2, 3, nemo non videt, quartum numerum proportionalem esse 6, atque hoc multo clarius, quia ex ipsa ratione, quam primum ad secundum habere uno intuitu videmus, ipsum quartum concludimus.","kind":"passage"},{"id":"RAW_QGOH_PS_3:P0073","text":"PROPOSITIO XLI","kind":"passage"},{"id":"RAW_QGOH_PS_3:P0074","text":"Cognitio primi gencris unica est falsitatis causa, secundi autem et tertii est necessario vera.","kind":"passage"},{"id":"RAW_QGOH_PS_3:P0075","text":"DEMONSTRATIO","kind":"passage"},{"id":"RAW_QGOH_PS_3:P0076","text":"Ad primi generis cognitionem illas omnes ideas diximus in præced. Schol. pertinere, quæ sunt inadæquatæ et confusæ ; atque adeo (per Prop. 35 hujus) hæc cognitio unica est falsitatis causa. Deinde ad cognitionem secundi et tertii illas pertinere diximus, quæ sunt adæquatæ ; adeoque (per Prop. 34 hujus) est necessario vera. Q. E. D..","kind":"passage"},{"id":"RAW_QGOH_PS_3:P0077","text":"PROPOSITIO XLII","kind":"passage"},{"id":"RAW_QGOH_PS_3:P0078","text":"Secundi et tertii, et non primi generis cognitio docet nos verum a falso distinguere.","kind":"passage"},{"id":"RAW_QGOH_PS_3:P0079","text":"DEMONSTRATIO","kind":"passage"},{"id":"RAW_QGOH_PS_3:P0080","text":"Hæc Propositio per se patet. Qui enim inter verum et falsum scit distinguere, debet adæquatam veri et falsi habere ideam ; hoc est (per 2 Schol. Prop. 40 hujus), verum et falsum secundo aut tertio cognitionis genere cognoscere.","kind":"passage"},{"id":"RAW_QGOH_PS_3:P0081","text":"PROPOSITIO XLIII","kind":"passage"},{"id":"RAW_QGOH_PS_3:P0082","text":"Qui veram habet ideam, simul scit se veram habere ideam, nec de rei veritate potest dubitare.","kind":"passage"},{"id":"RAW_QGOH_PS_3:P0083","text":"DEMONSTRATIO","kind":"passage"},{"id":"RAW_QGOH_PS_3:P0084","text":"Idea vera in nobis est illa, quæ in Deo, quatenus per naturam Mentis humanæ explicatur, est adæquata (per Coroll. Prop. 11 hujus). Ponamus itaque, dari in Deo, quatenus per naturam Mentis humanæ explicatur, ideam adæquatam A. Hujus ideæ debet necessario dari etiam in Deo idea, quæ ad Deum eodem modo refertur ac idea A (per Prop. 20 hujus, cujus Demonstratio universalis est). At idea A ad Deum referri supponitur, quatenus per naturam Mentis humanæ explicatur ; ergo etiam idea ideæ A ad Deum eodem modo debet referri ; hoc est (per idem Coroll. Prop. 11 hujus), hæc adæquata idea ideæ A erit in ipsa Mente, quæ ideam adæquatam A habet ; adeoque qui adæquatam habet ideam, sive (per Prop. 34 hujus) qui vere rem cognoscit, debet simul suæ cognitionis adæquatam habere ideam, sive veram cognitionem ; hoc est (ut per se manifestum), debet simul esse certus. Q. E. D.","kind":"passage"},{"id":"RAW_QGOH_PS_3:P0085","text":"SCHOLIUM","kind":"passage"},{"id":"RAW_QGOH_PS_3:P0086","text":"In Scholio Propositions 21 hujus Partis explicui, quid sit idea ideæ ; sed notandum, præcedentem Propositionem per se satis esse manifestam. Nam nemo, qui veram habet ideam, ignorat veram ideam summam certitudinem involvere ; veram namque habere ideam nihil aliud significat quam perfecte sive optime rem cognoscere ; nec sane aliquis de hac re dubitare potest, nisi putet, ideam quid mutum instar picturæ in tabula, et non modum cogitandi esse, nempe ipsum intelligere ; et, quæso, quis scire potest, se rem aliquam intelligere, nisi prius rem intelligat ? hoc est, quis potest scire, se de aliqua re certum esse, nisi prius de ea re certus sit ? Deinde quid idea vera clarius et certius dari potest, quod norma sit veritatis ? Sane sicut lux seipsam et tenebras manifestat, sic veritas norma sui et falsi est.","kind":"passage"},{"id":"RAW_QGOH_PS_3:P0087","text":"Atque his me ad has quæstiones respondisse puto, nempe : si idea vera, quatenus tantum dicitur cum suo ideato convenire, a falsa distinguitur ; nihil ergo realitatis aut perfectionis idea vera habet præ falsa (quandoquidem per solam denominationem extrinsecam distinguuntur) et consequenter neque etiam homo, qui veras, præ illo, qui falsas tantum ideas habet ? Deinde, undefit, ut homines falsas habeant ideas ? Et denique, unde aliquis certo scire potest, se ideas habere, quæ cum suis ideatis conveniant ? At has, inquam, quæstiones me jam respondisse puto. Nam quod ad differentiam inter ideam veram et falsam attinet, constat ex Propositione 35 hujus, illam ad hanc sese habere, ut ens ad non ens. Falsitatis autem causas a Propositione 19 usque ad 35 cum ejus Scholio clarissime ostendi. Ex quibus etiam apparet, quid homo, qui veras habet ideas, homini, qui non nisi falsas habet, intersit.","kind":"passage"},{"id":"RAW_QGOH_PS_3:P0088","text":"Quod denique ultimum attinet, nempe, undenam homo scire potest se habere ideam, quæ cum suo ideato conveniat ; id modo satis superque ostendi, ex hoc solo oriri, quod ideam habet, quæ cum suo ideato convenit, sive quod veritas sui sit norma. His adde, quod Mens nostra quatenus res vere percipit, pars et infiniti Dei intellectus (per Coroll. Prop. 11 hujus) ; adeoque tam necesse est, ut Mentis claræ et distincte ideæ veræ sint, ac Dei ideæ.","kind":"passage"},{"id":"RAW_QGOH_PS_3:P0089","text":"PROPOSITIO XLIV","kind":"passage"},{"id":"RAW_QGOH_PS_3:P0090","text":"De natura Rationis non est, res ut contingentes, sed ut necessarias contemplari.","kind":"passage"},{"id":"RAW_QGOH_PS_3:P0091","text":"DEMONSTRATIO","kind":"passage"},{"id":"RAW_QGOH_PS_3:P0092","text":"De natura Rationis est, res vere percipere (per Prop. 41 hujus) nempe (par Ax. 6 p. I) ut in se sunt, hoc est (per Prop. 29 p. I) non ut contingentes, sed ut necessarias. Q. E. D.","kind":"passage"},{"id":"RAW_QGOH_PS_3:P0093","text":"COROLLARIUM I","kind":"passage"},{"id":"RAW_QGOH_PS_3:P0094","text":"Hinc sequitur, a sola imaginatione pendere, quod res, tam respectu præteriti quam futuri, ut contingentes contemplemur.","kind":"passage"},{"id":"RAW_QGOH_PS_3:P0095","text":"SCHOLIUM","kind":"passage"},{"id":"RAW_QGOH_PS_3:P0096","text":"Qua autem ratione hoc fiat, paucis explicabo. Ostendimus supra (Prop. 17 hujus cum ejus Coroll.), Mentem, quamvis res non existant, eas tamen semper ut sibi præsentes imaginari, nisi causæ occurrant, quæ earum præsentem existentiam secludant. Deinde (Prop. 18 hujus) ostendimus, quod, si Corpus humanum semel a duobus corporibus externis simul affectum fuit, ubi Mens postea eorum alterutrum imaginabitur, statim et alterius recordabitur, hoc est, ambo ut sibi præsentia contemplabitur, nisi causæ occurrant, quæ eorum præsentem existentiam secludant. Præterea nemo dubitat, quin etiam tempus imaginemur, nempe ex eo, quod corpora alia aliis tardius, vel celerius, vel æque celeriter moveri imaginemur. Ponamus itaque puerum, qui heri prima vice hora matutina viderit Petrum, meridiana autem Paulum, et vespertina Simeonem, atque hodie iterum matutina hora Petrum.","kind":"passage"},{"id":"RAW_QGOH_PS_3:P0097","text":"Ex Propositione 18 hujus patet, quod simulac matutinam lucem videt, illico solem eandem cæli, quam die præcedenti, viderit, partem percurrentem, sive diem integrum, et simul cum tempore matutino Petrum, cum meridiano autem Paulum, et cum vespertino Simeonem imaginabitur, hoc est, Pauli et Simeonis existentiam cum relatione ad futurum tempus imaginabitur ; et contra, si hora vespertina Simeonem videat, Paulum et Petrum ad tempus præteritum referet, eosdem scilicet simul cum tempore præterito imaginando ; atque hæc eo constantius, quo sæpius eos eodem hoc ordine viderit. Quod si aliquando contingat, ut alia quadam vespera loco Simeonis Jacobum videat, tum sequenti mane cum tempore vespertino jam Simeonem, jam Jacobum, non vero ambos simul imaginabitur. Nam alterutrum tantum, non autem ambos simul tempore vespertino vidisse supponitur.","kind":"passage"},{"id":"RAW_QGOH_PS_3:P0098","text":"Fluctuabitur itaque ejus imaginatio, et cum futuro tempore vespertino jam hunc jam illum imaginabitur, hoc est, neutrum certo, sed utrumque contingenter futurum contemplabitur. Atque hæc imaginationis fluctuatio eadem erit, si imaginatio rerum sit, quas eodem modo cum relations ad tempus præteritum vel præsens contemplamur ; et consequenter res, tam ad tempus præsens quam ad præteritum vel futurum relatas ut contingentes imaginabimur.","kind":"passage"},{"id":"RAW_QGOH_PS_3:P0099","text":"COROLLARIUM II","kind":"passage"},{"id":"RAW_QGOH_PS_3:P0100","text":"De natura Rationis est, res sub quadam æternitatis specie percipere.","kind":"passage"},{"id":"RAW_QGOH_PS_3:P0101","text":"DEMONSTRATIO","kind":"passage"},{"id":"RAW_QGOH_PS_3:P0102","text":"De natura enim Rationis est, res ut necessarias, et non ut contingentes contemplari (per Prop. præced.). Hanc autem rerum necessitatem (per Prop. 41 hujus) vere, hoc est (per Axiom. 6 p. I) ut in se est, percipit. Sed (per Prop. 16 p. I) hæc rerum necessitas est ipsa Dei æternæ naturæ necessitas. Ergo de natura Rationis est, res sub hac æternitatis specie contemplari. Adde, quod fundamenta Rationis notiones sint (per Prop. 38 hujus), quæ illa explicant, quæ omnibus communia sunt, quæque (per Prop. 37 hujus) nullius rei singularis essentiam explicant ; quæque propterea absque ulla temporis relatione, sed sub quadam æternitatis specie debent concipi. Q. E. D.","kind":"passage"},{"id":"RAW_QGOH_PS_3:P0103","text":"PROPOSITIO XLV","kind":"passage"},{"id":"RAW_QGOH_PS_3:P0104","text":"Unaquæque cujuscunque corporis, vel rei singularis actu existentis, idea Dei æternam et infinitam essentiam necessario involvit.","kind":"passage"},{"id":"RAW_QGOH_PS_3:P0105","text":"DEMONSTRATIO","kind":"passage"}],"sha256":"447bea4dafeceb99c3231e3eac0fb767267774a299cb7a7a63aa687ab4ca7cc7","language_code":"la","language_name":"Latina","translations":[{"id":"SOURCE_TR_20261015T013032805031Z_3eb2ea50","language_code":"en","language_name":"English","title":"Part Two - On the Nature and Origin of the Mind · passage 3","source_sha256":"447bea4dafeceb99c3231e3eac0fb767267774a299cb7a7a63aa687ab4ca7cc7","sha256":"09a9d688118ff11cdc0ad7064dc939f1d26f9913b86c74d288521555b109fc30","rendition_type":"machine","review_status":"machine","model":"gpt-5.6-terra","created":"2026-10-15T01:30:32.805060+00:00","paragraphs":[{"id":"RAW_QGOH_PS_3:P0001","text":"The idea which constitutes the nature of the human Mind is demonstrated, in the same manner, not to be, when considered in itself alone, clear and distinct; as also the idea of the human Mind, and the ideas of the ideas of the affections of the human Body, insofar as they are referred to the Mind alone, as anyone may easily see.","kind":"passage","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_QGOH_PS_3:P0001"]},{"id":"RAW_QGOH_PS_3:P0002","text":"PROPOSITION XXIX","kind":"passage","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_QGOH_PS_3:P0002"]},{"id":"RAW_QGOH_PS_3:P0003","text":"The idea of the idea of any affection of the human Body does not involve an adequate knowledge of the human Mind.","kind":"passage","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_QGOH_PS_3:P0003"]},{"id":"RAW_QGOH_PS_3:P0004","text":"DEMONSTRATION","kind":"passage","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_QGOH_PS_3:P0004"]},{"id":"RAW_QGOH_PS_3:P0005","text":"For the idea of an affection of the human Body (by Prop. 27 of this Part) does not involve an adequate knowledge of the Body itself, or, does not adequately express its nature; that is (by Prop. 13 of this Part), it does not agree adequately with the nature of the Mind; and therefore (by Axiom 6, Part I) the idea of this idea does not adequately express the nature of the human Mind, or, does not involve an adequate knowledge thereof. Q. E. D.","kind":"passage","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_QGOH_PS_3:P0005"]},{"id":"RAW_QGOH_PS_3:P0006","text":"COROLLARY","kind":"passage","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_QGOH_PS_3:P0006"]},{"id":"RAW_QGOH_PS_3:P0007","text":"Hence it follows that the human Mind, whenever it perceives things according to the common order of Nature, has neither an adequate knowledge of itself, nor of its Body, nor of external bodies, but only a confused and mutilated knowledge. For the Mind does not know itself except insofar as it perceives the ideas of the affections of the Body (by Prop. 23 of this Part). But it does not perceive its Body (by Prop. 19 of this Part) except through the ideas of the affections themselves, through which also alone (by Prop. 26 of this Part) it perceives external bodies; and therefore, insofar as it has these, it has neither an adequate knowledge of itself (by Prop. 29 of this Part), nor of its Body (by Prop. 27 of this Part), nor of external bodies (by Prop. 25 of this Part), but only (by Prop. 28 of this Part together with its Schol.) a mutilated and confused knowledge. Q. E. D.","kind":"passage","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_QGOH_PS_3:P0007"]},{"id":"RAW_QGOH_PS_3:P0008","text":"SCHOLIUM","kind":"passage","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_QGOH_PS_3:P0008"]},{"id":"RAW_QGOH_PS_3:P0009","text":"I say expressly that the Mind has neither an adequate knowledge of itself, nor of its Body, nor of external bodies, but only a confused knowledge, whenever it perceives things according to the common order of Nature; that is, whenever it is determined externally, namely by the fortuitous encounter of things, to contemplate this or that, and not whenever it is determined internally, namely by the fact that it contemplates several things at once, to understand their agreements, differences, and oppositions; for whenever it is internally disposed in this or another way, then it contemplates things clearly and distinctly, as I shall show below.","kind":"passage","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_QGOH_PS_3:P0009"]},{"id":"RAW_QGOH_PS_3:P0010","text":"PROPOSITION XXX","kind":"passage","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_QGOH_PS_3:P0010"]},{"id":"RAW_QGOH_PS_3:P0011","text":"We can have no knowledge of the duration of our Body except one that is very inadequate.","kind":"passage","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_QGOH_PS_3:P0011"]},{"id":"RAW_QGOH_PS_3:P0012","text":"DEMONSTRATION","kind":"passage","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_QGOH_PS_3:P0012"]},{"id":"RAW_QGOH_PS_3:P0013","text":"The duration of our Body does not depend on its essence (by Axiom I of this Part), nor even on the absolute nature of God (by Prop. 21, Part I). But (by Prop. 28, Part I) it is determined to exist and to act by such causes as are also determined by others to exist and to act in a certain and determinate manner, and these again by others, and so on to infinity. Therefore the duration of our Body depends on the common order of Nature and the constitution of things. But an adequate knowledge of the manner in which things are constituted is given in God insofar as He has the ideas of them all, and not insofar as He has only the idea of the human Body (by Coroll. Prop. 9 of this Part); wherefore the knowledge of the duration of our Body is very inadequate in God insofar as He is considered as constituting only the nature of the human Mind; that is (by Coroll. Prop. 11 of this Part), this knowledge is very inadequate in our Mind. Q. E. D.","kind":"passage","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_QGOH_PS_3:P0013"]},{"id":"RAW_QGOH_PS_3:P0014","text":"PROPOSITION XXXI","kind":"passage","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_QGOH_PS_3:P0014"]},{"id":"RAW_QGOH_PS_3:P0015","text":"We can have no knowledge of the duration of singular things which are outside us except one that is very inadequate.","kind":"passage","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_QGOH_PS_3:P0015"]},{"id":"RAW_QGOH_PS_3:P0016","text":"DEMONSTRATION","kind":"passage","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_QGOH_PS_3:P0016"]},{"id":"RAW_QGOH_PS_3:P0017","text":"For every singular thing, just as the human Body, must be determined by another singular thing to exist and to act in a certain and determinate manner; and this again by another, and so on to infinity (by Prop. 28, Part I). But since from this common property of singular things we demonstrated in the preceding Proposition that we have only a very inadequate knowledge of the duration of our Body; therefore the same conclusion must be drawn concerning the duration of singular things, namely, that we can have only a very inadequate knowledge thereof. Q. E. D.","kind":"passage","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_QGOH_PS_3:P0017"]},{"id":"RAW_QGOH_PS_3:P0018","text":"COROLLARY","kind":"passage","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_QGOH_PS_3:P0018"]},{"id":"RAW_QGOH_PS_3:P0019","text":"Hence it follows that all particular things are contingent and corruptible. For we can have no adequate knowledge of their duration (by the preceding Prop.), and this is what is to be understood by the contingency of things and the possibility of corruption (see Schol. 1, Prop. 33, Part I). For (by Prop. 29, Part I) apart from this there is nothing contingent.","kind":"passage","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_QGOH_PS_3:P0019"]},{"id":"RAW_QGOH_PS_3:P0020","text":"PROPOSITION XXXII","kind":"passage","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_QGOH_PS_3:P0020"]},{"id":"RAW_QGOH_PS_3:P0021","text":"All ideas, insofar as they are referred to God, are true.","kind":"passage","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_QGOH_PS_3:P0021"]},{"id":"RAW_QGOH_PS_3:P0022","text":"DEMONSTRATION","kind":"passage","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_QGOH_PS_3:P0022"]},{"id":"RAW_QGOH_PS_3:P0023","text":"For all ideas that are in God agree entirely with their ideata (by Coroll. Prop. 7 of this Part), and therefore (by Axiom 6, Part I) are all true. Q. E. D.","kind":"passage","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_QGOH_PS_3:P0023"]},{"id":"RAW_QGOH_PS_3:P0024","text":"PROPOSITION XXXIII","kind":"passage","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_QGOH_PS_3:P0024"]},{"id":"RAW_QGOH_PS_3:P0025","text":"There is nothing positive in ideas on account of which they are called false.","kind":"passage","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_QGOH_PS_3:P0025"]},{"id":"RAW_QGOH_PS_3:P0026","text":"DEMONSTRATION","kind":"passage","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_QGOH_PS_3:P0026"]},{"id":"RAW_QGOH_PS_3:P0027","text":"If you deny this, conceive, if possible, a positive mode of thinking that constitutes the form of error or falsity. This mode of thinking cannot be in God (by the preceding Proposition); and outside God it can neither exist nor be conceived (by Prop. 15, pt. I). Therefore there can be nothing positive in ideas on account of which they are called false. Q. E. D.","kind":"passage","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_QGOH_PS_3:P0027"]},{"id":"RAW_QGOH_PS_3:P0028","text":"PROPOSITION XXXIV","kind":"passage","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_QGOH_PS_3:P0028"]},{"id":"RAW_QGOH_PS_3:P0029","text":"Every idea that is in us is, if absolute, or adequate and perfect, true.","kind":"passage","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_QGOH_PS_3:P0029"]},{"id":"RAW_QGOH_PS_3:P0030","text":"DEMONSTRATION","kind":"passage","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_QGOH_PS_3:P0030"]},{"id":"RAW_QGOH_PS_3:P0031","text":"When we say that there is in us an adequate and perfect idea, we say nothing other (by Coroll. to Prop. 11 of this Part) than that in God, insofar as he constitutes the essence of our Mind, there is an adequate and perfect idea; and consequently (by Prop. 32 of this Part) we say nothing other than that such an idea is true. Q. E. D.","kind":"passage","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_QGOH_PS_3:P0031"]},{"id":"RAW_QGOH_PS_3:P0032","text":"PROPOSITION XXXV","kind":"passage","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_QGOH_PS_3:P0032"]},{"id":"RAW_QGOH_PS_3:P0033","text":"Falsity consists in the privation of knowledge which inadequate, or mutilated and confused, ideas involve.","kind":"passage","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_QGOH_PS_3:P0033"]},{"id":"RAW_QGOH_PS_3:P0034","text":"DEMONSTRATION","kind":"passage","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_QGOH_PS_3:P0034"]},{"id":"RAW_QGOH_PS_3:P0035","text":"There is nothing positive in ideas that constitutes the form of falsity (by Prop. 33 of this Part); but falsity cannot consist in absolute privation (for Minds, not Bodies, are said to err or to be deceived), nor indeed in absolute ignorance; for to be ignorant and to err are different things. Therefore it consists in the privation of knowledge which inadequate knowledge of things, or inadequate and confused ideas, involve. Q. E. D.","kind":"passage","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_QGOH_PS_3:P0035"]},{"id":"RAW_QGOH_PS_3:P0036","text":"SCHOLIUM","kind":"passage","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_QGOH_PS_3:P0036"]},{"id":"RAW_QGOH_PS_3:P0037","text":"In the Scholium to Prop. 17 of this Part I explained in what way error consists in the privation of knowledge; but, for a fuller explanation of this matter, I shall give an example. Men are deceived, namely, because they think themselves free; and this opinion consists solely in their being conscious of their actions and ignorant of the causes by which they are determined. This, then, is their idea of freedom: that they know no cause of their actions. For when they say that human actions depend on the will, these are words of which they have no idea. For what the will is, and how it moves the Body, all are ignorant; those who boast otherwise and devise seats and dwelling-places of the soul are wont to provoke either laughter or nausea. Thus, when we look at the sun, we imagine it to be about two hundred feet distant from us; which error does not consist in this imagination alone, but in the fact that, while we imagine it thus, we are ignorant of its true distance and of the cause of this imagination.","kind":"passage","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_QGOH_PS_3:P0037"]},{"id":"RAW_QGOH_PS_3:P0038","text":"For although we may afterward know that it is more than 600 diameters of the earth distant from us, we shall nevertheless imagine it to be near; for we do not imagine the sun to be so close because we are ignorant of its true distance, but because the affection of our Body involves the essence of the sun insofar as the Body itself is affected by it.","kind":"passage","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_QGOH_PS_3:P0038"]},{"id":"RAW_QGOH_PS_3:P0039","text":"PROPOSITION XXXVI","kind":"passage","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_QGOH_PS_3:P0039"]},{"id":"RAW_QGOH_PS_3:P0040","text":"Inadequate and confused ideas follow with the same necessity as adequate, or clear and distinct, ideas.","kind":"passage","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_QGOH_PS_3:P0040"]},{"id":"RAW_QGOH_PS_3:P0041","text":"DEMONSTRATION","kind":"passage","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_QGOH_PS_3:P0041"]},{"id":"RAW_QGOH_PS_3:P0042","text":"All ideas are in God (by Prop. 15, pt. I); and, insofar as they are referred to God, they are true (by Prop. 32 of this Part) and (by Coroll. to Prop. 7 of this Part) adequate; and therefore none are inadequate or confused except insofar as they are referred to the singular Mind of someone (on which matter see Props. 24 and 28 of this Part): therefore all, both adequate and inadequate, follow with the same necessity (by Coroll. to Prop. 6 of this Part). Q. E. D.","kind":"passage","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_QGOH_PS_3:P0042"]},{"id":"RAW_QGOH_PS_3:P0043","text":"PROPOSITION XXXVII","kind":"passage","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_QGOH_PS_3:P0043"]},{"id":"RAW_QGOH_PS_3:P0044","text":"That which is common to all (concerning these, see Lemma 2 above), and which is equally in the part and in the whole, does not constitute the essence of any singular thing.","kind":"passage","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_QGOH_PS_3:P0044"]},{"id":"RAW_QGOH_PS_3:P0045","text":"DEMONSTRATION","kind":"passage","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_QGOH_PS_3:P0045"]},{"id":"RAW_QGOH_PS_3:P0046","text":"If you deny this, conceive, if possible, that it constitutes the essence of some singular thing, namely, the essence of B. Therefore (by Def. 2 of this Part) it will not be able to be or be conceived without B; but this is contrary to the hypothesis. Therefore it does not pertain to the essence of B, nor does it constitute the essence of any other singular thing. Q. E. D.","kind":"passage","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_QGOH_PS_3:P0046"]},{"id":"RAW_QGOH_PS_3:P0047","text":"PROPOSITION XXXVIII","kind":"passage","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_QGOH_PS_3:P0047"]},{"id":"RAW_QGOH_PS_3:P0048","text":"Those things which are common to all, and which are equally in the part and in the whole, can be conceived only adequately.","kind":"passage","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_QGOH_PS_3:P0048"]},{"id":"RAW_QGOH_PS_3:P0049","text":"DEMONSTRATION","kind":"passage","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_QGOH_PS_3:P0049"]},{"id":"RAW_QGOH_PS_3:P0050","text":"Let A be something common to all bodies, and which is equally in a part of any body and in the whole. I say that A cannot be conceived except adequately. For its idea (by Corollary to Prop. 7 of this Part) will necessarily be adequate in God, both insofar as He has the idea of the human Body and insofar as He has the ideas of its affections, which (by Props. 16, 25, and 27 of this Part) involve in part the nature both of the human Body and of external bodies; that is (by Props. 12 and 13 of this Part), this idea will necessarily be adequate in God insofar as it constitutes the human Mind, or insofar as He has the ideas which are in the human Mind; therefore (by Corollary to Prop. 11 of this Part) the Mind necessarily perceives A adequately, both insofar as it perceives itself and insofar as it perceives its own body, or any external body, nor can A be conceived in any other way. Q. E. D.","kind":"passage","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_QGOH_PS_3:P0050"]},{"id":"RAW_QGOH_PS_3:P0051","text":"COROLLARY","kind":"passage","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_QGOH_PS_3:P0051"]},{"id":"RAW_QGOH_PS_3:P0052","text":"Hence it follows that there are certain ideas, or notions, common to all men; for (by Lemma II) all bodies agree in certain respects, which (by the preceding Prop.) must be perceived adequately, or clearly and distinctly, by all.","kind":"passage","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_QGOH_PS_3:P0052"]},{"id":"RAW_QGOH_PS_3:P0053","text":"PROPOSITION XXXIX","kind":"passage","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_QGOH_PS_3:P0053"]},{"id":"RAW_QGOH_PS_3:P0054","text":"That which is common and proper to the human Body and to certain external bodies by which the human Body is usually affected, and which is equally in a part of each of these and in the whole, the idea of this also will be adequate in the Mind.","kind":"passage","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_QGOH_PS_3:P0054"]},{"id":"RAW_QGOH_PS_3:P0055","text":"DEMONSTRATION","kind":"passage","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_QGOH_PS_3:P0055"]},{"id":"RAW_QGOH_PS_3:P0056","text":"Let A be that which is common and proper to the human Body and to certain external bodies, and which is equally in the human Body and in those same external bodies, and which, finally, is equally in a part of any external body and in the whole. There will be in God an adequate idea of A (by Corollary to Prop. 7 of this Part), both insofar as He has the idea of the human Body and insofar as He has the ideas of the posited external bodies. Now suppose that the human Body is affected by an external body through that which it has in common with it, that is, by A: the idea of this affection will involve the property of A (by Prop. 16 of this Part), and hence (by the same Corollary to Prop. 7 of this Part) the idea of this affection, insofar as it involves the property of A, will be adequate in God insofar as He is affected by the idea of the human Body; that is (by Prop. 13 of this Part), insofar as it constitutes the nature of the human Mind; and therefore (by Corollary to Prop. 11 of this Part) this idea is also adequate in the human Mind. Q. E. D.","kind":"passage","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_QGOH_PS_3:P0056"]},{"id":"RAW_QGOH_PS_3:P0057","text":"COROLLARY","kind":"passage","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_QGOH_PS_3:P0057"]},{"id":"RAW_QGOH_PS_3:P0058","text":"Hence it follows that the Mind is the more apt to perceive many things adequately, the more its Body has in common with other bodies.","kind":"passage","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_QGOH_PS_3:P0058"]},{"id":"RAW_QGOH_PS_3:P0059","text":"PROPOSITION XL","kind":"passage","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_QGOH_PS_3:P0059"]},{"id":"RAW_QGOH_PS_3:P0060","text":"Whatever ideas follow in the Mind from ideas which are adequate in it are also adequate.","kind":"passage","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_QGOH_PS_3:P0060"]},{"id":"RAW_QGOH_PS_3:P0061","text":"DEMONSTRATION","kind":"passage","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_QGOH_PS_3:P0061"]},{"id":"RAW_QGOH_PS_3:P0062","text":"This is evident. For when we say that an idea follows in the human Mind from ideas which are adequate in it, we say nothing other (by Corollary to Prop. 11 of this Part) than that there is given in the divine intellect an idea of which God is the cause, not insofar as He is infinite, nor insofar as He is affected by the ideas of very many singular things, but insofar only as He constitutes the essence of the human Mind.","kind":"passage","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_QGOH_PS_3:P0062"]},{"id":"RAW_QGOH_PS_3:P0063","text":"SCHOLIUM I","kind":"passage","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_QGOH_PS_3:P0063"]},{"id":"RAW_QGOH_PS_3:P0064","text":"By these things I have explained the cause of the notions which are called Common, and which are the foundations of our reasoning. But there are other causes of certain axioms, or common notions, which it would be fitting to explain by this our method; for from them it would be clear which notions are more useful than the rest, and which are of scarcely any use. Next, which are common, and which are clear and distinct only to those who are not troubled by prejudices, and which, finally, are ill-founded. Moreover, it would be clear whence those notions which they call Second, and consequently the axioms founded upon them, derive their origin, and other matters on which I have at times reflected. But because I have devoted these matters to another Treatise, and also lest I create distaste through the excessive length of this subject, I have decided to refrain from the matter here.","kind":"passage","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_QGOH_PS_3:P0064"]},{"id":"RAW_QGOH_PS_3:P0065","text":"Nevertheless, lest I omit anything among these matters which it is necessary to know, I shall briefly add the causes from which the terms called Transcendental derive their origin, such as Being, Thing, Something. These terms arise from this, namely, that the human Body, since it is limited, is capable of forming distinctly in itself at the same time only a certain number of images (what an image is I explained in the Scholium to Prop. 17 of this Part); if this number is exceeded, these images will begin to be confused; and if this number of images, of which the Body is capable so as to form them distinctly in itself at the same time, is greatly exceeded, they will all be utterly confused with one another. This being so, it is evident from the Corollary to Prop. 17 and Prop. 18 of this Part that the human Mind will be able distinctly to imagine at the same time as many bodies as images can be formed at the same time in its Body.","kind":"passage","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_QGOH_PS_3:P0065"]},{"id":"RAW_QGOH_PS_3:P0066","text":"But when the images are utterly confused in the body, the Mind too will imagine all bodies confusedly, without any distinction, and will comprehend them as it were under one attribute, namely under the attribute of Being, Thing, etc. This can also be inferred from the fact that images do not always have equal force, and from other causes analogous to these, which there is no need to explain here; for, for the end at which we aim, it suffices to consider one alone. For they all come down to this: that these terms signify ideas confused to the highest degree. From similar causes there then arose those notions which they call Universals, such as Man, Horse, Dog, etc.; namely, because so many images, for example, of men are formed at the same time in the human Body that they surpass the power of imagining, not indeed entirely, but nevertheless to such an extent that the Mind cannot imagine the small differences of the individuals (namely, the colour, size, etc. of each one)","kind":"passage","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_QGOH_PS_3:P0066"]},{"id":"RAW_QGOH_PS_3:P0067","text":"and their determinate number, and imagines distinctly only that in which they all agree insofar as the Body is affected by them; for by this the Body has been affected most, namely by each singular thing; and it expresses this by the name man, and predicates it of infinitely many singulars. For it cannot imagine a determinate number of singulars, as we have said. But it should be noted that these notions are not formed in the same way by everyone, but vary in each person according to the nature of the thing by which the Body has more often been affected, and which the Mind more easily imagines or recollects. For example,","kind":"passage","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_QGOH_PS_3:P0067"]},{"id":"RAW_QGOH_PS_3:P0068","text":"those who have more often contemplated with admiration the stature of men will understand under the name man an animal of erect stature; but those accustomed to contemplate something else will form another common image of men, namely that man is an animal capable of laughter; a featherless biped animal; a rational animal; and thus, concerning the rest, each person will form universal images of things according to the disposition of his body. Therefore it is not surprising that so many controversies have arisen among Philosophers who have wished to explain natural things through images of things alone.","kind":"passage","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_QGOH_PS_3:P0068"]},{"id":"RAW_QGOH_PS_3:P0069","text":"SCHOLIUM II","kind":"passage","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_QGOH_PS_3:P0069"]},{"id":"RAW_QGOH_PS_3:P0070","text":"From all the above it clearly appears that we perceive many things and form universal notions: I° from singular things represented to us through the senses mutilatedly, confusedly, and without order for the intellect (see Corollary to Prop. 29 of this Part); and therefore I have been accustomed to call such perceptions knowledge from vague experience; II° from signs, for example, from the fact that, on hearing or reading certain words, we recollect things and form certain ideas of them similar to those through which we imagine things (see Scholium to Prop. 18 of this Part). Hereafter I shall call both these ways of contemplating things knowledge of the first kind, opinion, or Imagination; III° finally, from the fact that we have common notions and adequate ideas of the properties of things (see Corollary to Props. 38 and 39, together with its Corollary, and Prop. 40 of this Part); and I shall call this Reason and knowledge of the second kind.","kind":"passage","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_QGOH_PS_3:P0070"]},{"id":"RAW_QGOH_PS_3:P0071","text":"Besides these two kinds of knowledge there is, as I shall show in what follows, another third kind, which we shall call Intuitive Knowledge. And this kind of knowing proceeds from an adequate idea of the formal essence of certain attributes of God to adequate knowledge of the essence of things. I shall explain all these things by the example of one matter. There are, for example, three numbers, in order to obtain a fourth which is to the third as the second is to the first. Merchants do not hesitate to multiply the second by the third and divide the product by the first; because, indeed, they have not yet consigned to oblivion those things which they heard from their teacher without any demonstration, or because they have often experienced this in the simplest numbers, or from the force of the Demonstration of Prop. 19, Book VII of Euclid, namely from the common property of proportionals. But in the simplest numbers none of these is needed. For example,","kind":"passage","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_QGOH_PS_3:P0071"]},{"id":"RAW_QGOH_PS_3:P0072","text":"given the numbers 1, 2, 3, everyone sees that the fourth proportional number is 6, and this much more clearly, because from the very relation which we see at a single glance that the first bears to the second, we infer the fourth itself.","kind":"passage","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_QGOH_PS_3:P0072"]},{"id":"RAW_QGOH_PS_3:P0073","text":"PROPOSITION XLI","kind":"passage","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_QGOH_PS_3:P0073"]},{"id":"RAW_QGOH_PS_3:P0074","text":"Knowledge of the first kind is the sole cause of falsity; that of the second and third is necessarily true.","kind":"passage","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_QGOH_PS_3:P0074"]},{"id":"RAW_QGOH_PS_3:P0075","text":"DEMONSTRATION","kind":"passage","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_QGOH_PS_3:P0075"]},{"id":"RAW_QGOH_PS_3:P0076","text":"We said in the preceding Scholium that all those ideas which are inadequate and confused pertain to knowledge of the first kind; and therefore (by Prop. 35 of this Part) this knowledge is the sole cause of falsity. Next, we said that those which are adequate pertain to knowledge of the second and third kinds; and therefore (by Prop. 34 of this Part) it is necessarily true. Q. E. D.","kind":"passage","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_QGOH_PS_3:P0076"]},{"id":"RAW_QGOH_PS_3:P0077","text":"PROPOSITION XLII","kind":"passage","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_QGOH_PS_3:P0077"]},{"id":"RAW_QGOH_PS_3:P0078","text":"Knowledge of the second and third kinds, and not of the first kind, teaches us to distinguish the true from the false.","kind":"passage","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_QGOH_PS_3:P0078"]},{"id":"RAW_QGOH_PS_3:P0079","text":"DEMONSTRATION","kind":"passage","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_QGOH_PS_3:P0079"]},{"id":"RAW_QGOH_PS_3:P0080","text":"This Proposition is self-evident. For one who knows how to distinguish between the true and the false must have an adequate idea of the true and the false; that is (by the 2nd Scholium of Prop. 40 of this Part), know the true and the false by the second or third kind of knowledge.","kind":"passage","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_QGOH_PS_3:P0080"]},{"id":"RAW_QGOH_PS_3:P0081","text":"PROPOSITION XLIII","kind":"passage","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_QGOH_PS_3:P0081"]},{"id":"RAW_QGOH_PS_3:P0082","text":"He who has a true idea at the same time knows that he has a true idea, and cannot doubt the truth of the thing.","kind":"passage","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_QGOH_PS_3:P0082"]},{"id":"RAW_QGOH_PS_3:P0083","text":"DEMONSTRATION","kind":"passage","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_QGOH_PS_3:P0083"]},{"id":"RAW_QGOH_PS_3:P0084","text":"A true idea in us is one which in God, insofar as he is explained through the nature of the human Mind, is adequate (by the Corollary to Prop. 11 of this Part). Let us suppose, then, that there is in God, insofar as he is explained through the nature of the human Mind, an adequate idea A. There must necessarily also be given in God an idea of this idea, which is related to God in the same manner as idea A (by Prop. 20 of this Part, the Demonstration of which is universal). But idea A is supposed to be related to God insofar as he is explained through the nature of the human Mind; therefore the idea of idea A must likewise be related to God in the same manner; that is (by the same Corollary to Prop. 11 of this Part), this adequate idea of idea A will be in the very Mind which has the adequate idea A; and therefore one who has an adequate idea, or (by Prop. 34 of this Part) one who truly knows a thing, must at the same time have an adequate idea of his knowledge, or true knowledge; that is (as is self-evident), he must at the same time be certain. Q. E. D.","kind":"passage","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_QGOH_PS_3:P0084"]},{"id":"RAW_QGOH_PS_3:P0085","text":"SCHOLIUM","kind":"passage","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_QGOH_PS_3:P0085"]},{"id":"RAW_QGOH_PS_3:P0086","text":"In the Scholium to Proposition 21 of this Part I explained what an idea of an idea is; but it should be noted that the preceding Proposition is sufficiently manifest in itself. For no one who has a true idea is unaware that a true idea involves the highest certainty; for to have a true idea means nothing other than to know a thing perfectly, or in the best way. Nor indeed can anyone doubt this, unless he thinks that an idea is some mute thing, like a painting on a panel, and not a mode of thinking, namely, understanding itself; and, I ask, who can know that he understands some thing unless he first understands the thing? That is, who can know that he is certain concerning some thing unless he is first certain concerning that thing? Furthermore, what can be given that is clearer and more certain than a true idea, to be a norm of truth? Certainly, just as light makes both itself and darkness manifest, so truth is the norm both of itself and of the false.","kind":"passage","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_QGOH_PS_3:P0086"]},{"id":"RAW_QGOH_PS_3:P0087","text":"And by these things I think that I have answered these questions, namely: if a true idea, insofar as it is said merely to agree with its ideate, is distinguished from a false one, then a true idea has no more reality or perfection than a false one (since they are distinguished only by an extrinsic denomination); and consequently neither does the man who has true ideas have [any advantage] over one who has only false ideas? Next, whence does it come that men have false ideas? And finally, whence can anyone certainly know that he has ideas which agree with their ideates? But, I say, I think that I have already answered these questions. For as regards the difference between a true and a false idea, it is clear from Proposition 35 of this Part that the former is related to the latter as being to non-being. But I have shown the causes of falsity most clearly from Proposition 19 through 35, together with its Scholium. From these things it is also apparent how a man who has true ideas differs from a man who has none but false ideas.","kind":"passage","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_QGOH_PS_3:P0087"]},{"id":"RAW_QGOH_PS_3:P0088","text":"Finally, as regards the last point, namely, whence a man can know that he has an idea which agrees with its ideate: I have shown this more than sufficiently merely from the fact that it arises from this alone, that he has an idea which agrees with its ideate, or that truth is the norm of itself. Add to this that our Mind, insofar as it perceives things truly, is a part of the infinite intellect of God (by the Corollary to Prop. 11 of this Part); and therefore it is as necessary that the clear and distinct ideas of the Mind be true as are the ideas of God.","kind":"passage","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_QGOH_PS_3:P0088"]},{"id":"RAW_QGOH_PS_3:P0089","text":"PROPOSITION XLIV","kind":"passage","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_QGOH_PS_3:P0089"]},{"id":"RAW_QGOH_PS_3:P0090","text":"It is not of the nature of Reason to contemplate things as contingent, but as necessary.","kind":"passage","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_QGOH_PS_3:P0090"]},{"id":"RAW_QGOH_PS_3:P0091","text":"DEMONSTRATION","kind":"passage","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_QGOH_PS_3:P0091"]},{"id":"RAW_QGOH_PS_3:P0092","text":"It is of the nature of Reason to perceive things truly (by Prop. 41 of this Part), namely (by Ax. 6, Part I), as they are in themselves; that is (by Prop. 29, Part I), not as contingent, but as necessary. Q. E. D.","kind":"passage","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_QGOH_PS_3:P0092"]},{"id":"RAW_QGOH_PS_3:P0093","text":"COROLLARY I","kind":"passage","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_QGOH_PS_3:P0093"]},{"id":"RAW_QGOH_PS_3:P0094","text":"Hence it follows that it depends solely on imagination that we contemplate things as contingent, both with respect to the past and to the future.","kind":"passage","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_QGOH_PS_3:P0094"]},{"id":"RAW_QGOH_PS_3:P0095","text":"SCHOLIUM","kind":"passage","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_QGOH_PS_3:P0095"]},{"id":"RAW_QGOH_PS_3:P0096","text":"But in what manner this comes about, I shall explain briefly. We showed above (Prop. 17 of this Part, with its Corollary) that, although things do not exist, the Mind nevertheless always imagines them as present to itself, unless causes occur which exclude their present existence. Next (Prop. 18 of this Part), we showed that if the human Body has once been affected simultaneously by two external bodies, whenever the Mind afterwards imagines either one of them, it will immediately remember the other also; that is, it will contemplate both as present to itself, unless causes occur which exclude their present existence. Moreover, no one doubts that we also imagine time, namely, from the fact that we imagine some bodies to move more slowly, or more quickly, or equally quickly than others. Let us suppose, then, a boy who yesterday for the first time saw Peter at the morning hour, Paul at noon, and Simeon in the evening, and who today again [sees] Peter at the morning hour.","kind":"passage","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_QGOH_PS_3:P0096"]},{"id":"RAW_QGOH_PS_3:P0097","text":"From Proposition 18 of this Part it is clear that, as soon as he sees the morning light, he will immediately imagine the sun traversing the same part of the sky as on the preceding day, or the entire day, and at the same time Peter together with the morning time, Paul with the noonday time, and Simeon with the evening time; that is, he will imagine the existence of Paul and Simeon in relation to future time. And conversely, if in the evening hour he sees Simeon, he will refer Paul and Peter to past time, imagining them, that is, together with past time; and this the more constantly, the more often he has seen them in this same order. But if it should sometimes happen that on some other evening he sees Jacob in place of Simeon, then on the following morning, together with the evening time, he will imagine now Simeon, now Jacob, but not both at once. For it is supposed that he saw only one or the other, but not both together, at the evening time.","kind":"passage","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_QGOH_PS_3:P0097"]},{"id":"RAW_QGOH_PS_3:P0098","text":"Therefore his imagination will fluctuate, and with the future evening time he will imagine now this one, now that one; that is, he will contemplate neither as certainly, but each as contingently, future. And this fluctuation of the imagination will be the same if the imagination is of things which we contemplate in the same manner with relation to past or present time; and consequently, we shall imagine things referred both to present time and to past or future time as contingent.","kind":"passage","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_QGOH_PS_3:P0098"]},{"id":"RAW_QGOH_PS_3:P0099","text":"COROLLARY II","kind":"passage","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_QGOH_PS_3:P0099"]},{"id":"RAW_QGOH_PS_3:P0100","text":"It is of the nature of Reason to perceive things under a certain aspect of eternity.","kind":"passage","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_QGOH_PS_3:P0100"]},{"id":"RAW_QGOH_PS_3:P0101","text":"DEMONSTRATION","kind":"passage","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_QGOH_PS_3:P0101"]},{"id":"RAW_QGOH_PS_3:P0102","text":"For it is of the nature of Reason to contemplate things as necessary, and not as contingent (by the preceding Proposition). But it perceives this necessity of things (by Proposition 41 of this Part) truly, that is (by Axiom 6, Part I), as it is in itself. But (by Proposition 16, Part I) this necessity of things is the very necessity of God's eternal nature. Therefore it is of the nature of Reason to contemplate things under this aspect of eternity. Add that the foundations of Reason are notions (by Proposition 38 of this Part) which explain those things that are common to all, and which (by Proposition 37 of this Part) explain the essence of no singular thing; and which therefore ought to be conceived without any relation to time, but under a certain aspect of eternity. Q. E. D.","kind":"passage","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_QGOH_PS_3:P0102"]},{"id":"RAW_QGOH_PS_3:P0103","text":"PROPOSITION XLV","kind":"passage","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_QGOH_PS_3:P0103"]},{"id":"RAW_QGOH_PS_3:P0104","text":"The idea of each body, or of each singular thing actually existing, necessarily involves the eternal and infinite essence of God.","kind":"passage","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_QGOH_PS_3:P0104"]},{"id":"RAW_QGOH_PS_3:P0105","text":"DEMONSTRATION","kind":"passage","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_QGOH_PS_3:P0105"]}],"source_digest":"cc08c026af5339a5c32b658efaf82a78cfaf9f2b65e8e9f19b509b9f6519658f","source_language_code":"la","original_title":"Pars secunda - De natura et origine mentis · passage 3"}],"passage_basis":"frozen-grounding","original_title":"Pars secunda - De natura et origine mentis · passage 3","title_language_code":"en"},{"id":"RAW_QGOH_PS_4","title":"Part Two - On the Nature and Origin of the Mind · passage 4","order":8,"chapter_id":"CH_QGOH_PS_4","text":"Idea rei singularis, actu existentis, ipsius rei tam essentiam quam existentiam necessario involvit (per Coroll. Prop. 8 hujus) : At res singulares (per Prop. 15 p. I) non possunt sine Deo concipi ; sed, quia (per Prop. 6 hujus) Deum pro causa habent, quatenus sub attributo consideratur, cujus res ipsæ modi sunt, debent necessario earum ideæ (per Axiom. 4 p. I) ipsarum attributi conceptum, hoc est (per Defin. 6 p. I) Dei æternam et infinitam essentiam involvere. Q. E. D.\n\nSCHOLIUM\n\nHic per existentiam non intelligo durationem, hoc est existentiam, quatenus abstracte concipitur, et tanquam quædam quantitatis species. Nam loquor de ipsa natura existentiæ, quæ rebus singularibus tribuitur propterea, quod ex æterna necessitate Dei naturæ infinita infinitis modis sequuntur (vide Prop. 16 p. I). Loquor, inquam, de ipsa existentia rerum singularium, quatenus in Deo sunt. Nam, etsi unaquaeque ab alia re singulari determinetur ad certo modo existendum, vis tamen, qua unaquæque in existendo perseverat, ex æterna necessitate naturæ Dei sequitur. Qua de re vide Coroll. Prop. 24 p. I\n\nPROPOSITIO XLVI\n\nCognitio æternæ et infinitæ essentiæ Dei, quam unaquæque idea involvit est adæquata et perfecta.\n\nDEMONSTRATIO\n\nDemonstratio præcedentis Propositionis universalis est, et, sive res ut pars sive ut totum consideretur, ejus idea, sive totius sit sive partis (per Prop. præced.), Dei æternam et infinitam essentiam involvet. Quare id, quod cognitionem æternæ et infinitiæ essentiæ Dei dat, omnibus commune, et æque in parte ac in toto est, adeoque (per Prop. 38 hujus) erit hæc cognitio adæquata. Q. E. D.\n\nPROPOSITIO XLVII\n\nMens humana adæquatam habet cognitionem æternæ et infinitæ essentiæ Dei.\n\nDEMONSTRATIO\n\nMens humana ideas habet (per Prop. 22 hujus), ex quibus (per Prop. 23 hujus) se suumque Corpus (per Prop. 19 hujus) et (per Coroll. 1 Prop. 16 et per Prop. 17 hujus) corpora externa, ut actu existentia, percipit ; adeoque (per Prop. 45 et 46 hujus) cognitionem æternae et infinitæ essentiæ Dei habet adæquatam. Q. E. D.\n\nSCHOLIUM\n\nHinc videmus, Dei infinitam essentiam ejusque æternitatem omnibus esse notam. Cum autem omnia in Deo sint et per Deum concipiantur, sequitur, nos ex cognitione hac plurima posse deducere, quæ adæquate cognoscamus, atque adeo tertium illud cognitionis genus formare de quo diximus in Scholio 2 Propositionis 40 hujus Partis, et de cujus præstantia et utilitate in quinta Parte erit nobis dicendi locus. Quod autem homines non æque claram Dei ac notionum communium habeant cognitionem, inde fit, quod Deum imaginari nequeant ut corpora, et quod nomen Deus junxerunt imaginibus rerum, quas videre solent, quod homines vix vitare possunt, quia continuo a corporibus externis afficiuntur. Et profecto plerique errores in hoc solo consistunt, quod scilicet nomina rebus non recte applicamus. Cum enim aliquis ait, lineas, quæ ex centro circuli ad ejusdem circumferentiam ducuntur, esse inæquales ille sane aliud, tum saltem, per circulum intelligit, quam Mathematici. Sic cum homines in calculo errant, alios numeros in mente, alios in charta habent. Quare si ipsorum Mentem spectes, non errant sane ; videntur tamen errare, quia ipsos in mente putamus habere numeros, qui in charta sunt. Si hoc non esset, nihil eosdem errare crederemus ; ut non credidi quendam errare, quem nuper audivi clamantem, suum atrium volasse in gallinam vicini, quia scilicet ipsius mens satis perspecta mihi videbatur. Atque hinc pleræque oriuntur controversiæ, nempe quia homines mentem suam non recte explicant, vel quia alterius memtem male interpretantur. Nam revera, dum sibi maxime contradicunt, vel eadem vel de diversis rebus cogitant, ita ut, quos in alio errores et absurda esse putant, non sint.\n\nPROPOSITIO XLVIII\n\nIn Mente nulla est absoluta sive libera voluntas ; sed Mens ad hoc vel illud volendum determinatur a causa, quæ etiam ab alia determinata est, et hæc iterum ab alia, et sic in infinitum.\n\nDEMONSTRATIO\n\nMens certus et determinatus modus cogitandi est (per Prop. 11 hujus) adeoque (per Coroll. 2 Prop. 17 p. I) suarum actionum non potest esse causa libera, sive absolutam facultatem volendi et nolendi habere non potest ; sed ad hoc vel illud volendum (per Prop. 28 p. I) determinari debet a causa, quæ etiam ab alia determinata est, et hæciterum ab alia, etc. Q. E. D.\n\nSCHOLIUM\n\nEodem hoc modo demonstratur, in Mente nullam dari facultatem absolutam intelligendi, cupiendi, amandi, etc. Unde sequitur, has et similes facultates vel prorsus fictitias, vel nihil esse præter entia Metaphysica, sive universalia, quæ ex particularibus formare solemus. Adeo ut intellectus et voluntas ad hanc et illam ideam, vel ad hanc et illam volitionem eodem modo sese habeant, ac lapideitas ad hunc et illum lapidem, vel ut homo ad Petrum et Paulum. Causam autem, cur homines se liberos esse putent, explicuimus in Appendice Partis primæ. Verum antequam ulterius pergam, venit hic notandum, me per voluntatem affirmandi et negandi facultatem, non autem cupiditatem intelligere ; facultatem, inquam, intelligo, qua Mens, quid verum quidve falsum sit, affirmat vel negat, et non cupiditatem, qua Mens res appetit vel aversatur. At postquam demonstravimus, has facultates notiones esse universales, quæ a singularibus, ex quibus easdem formamus, non distinguuntur, inquirendum jam est, an ipsæ volitiones aliquid sint præter ipsas rerum ideas. Inquirendum, inquam, est, an in Mente alia affirmatio et negatio detur præter illam quam idea, quatenus idea est, involvit, qua de re vide sequentem Propositionem, ut et Definitionem 3 hujus, ne cogitatio in picturas incidat. Non enim per ideas imagines, quales in fundo oculi, et, si placet, in medio cerebro formantur, sed Cogitationis conceptus intelligo.\n\nPROPOSITIO XLIX\n\nIn Mente nulla datur volitio, sive affirmatio et negatio, præter illam, quam idea, quatenus idea est, involvit.\n\nDEMONSTRATIO\n\nIn Mente (per Prop. præced.) nulla datur absoluta facultas volendi et nolendi, sed tantum singulares volitiones, nempe hæc et illa affirmatio, et hæc et illa negatio. Concipiamus itaque singularem aliquam volitionem, nempe modum cogitandi, quo Mens affirmat, tres angulos trianguli æquales esse duobus redis. Hæc affirmatio conceptum sive ideam trianguli involvit, hoc est, sine idea trianguli non potest concipi. Idem enim est, si dicam, quod A conceptum B debeat involvere ac quod A sine B non possit concipi. Deinde hæc affirmatio (per Axiom. 3 hujus) non potest etiam sine idea trianguli esse. Hæc ergo affirmatio sine idea trianguli nec esse nec concipit potest. Porro hæc trianguli idea hanc eandem affirmationem involvere debet, nempe quod tres ejus anguli æquentur duobus rectis. Quare et vice versa hæc trianguli idea sine hac affirmatione nec esse nec concipi potest adeoque (per Defin. 2 hujus) hæc affirmatio ad essentiam ideæ trianguli pertinet, nec aliud præter ipsam est. Et quod de hac volitione diximus (quandoquidem eam ad libitum sumpsimus), dicendum etiam est de quacunque volitione, nempe quod præter ideam nihil sit. Q. E. D.\n\nCOROLLARIUM\n\nVoluntas et intellectus unum et idem sunt.\n\nDEMONSTRATIO\n\nVoluntas et intellectus nihil præter ipsas singulares volitiones et ideas sunt (per Prop. 48 hujus et ejusdem Schol.). At singularis volitio et idea (per Prop. præced.) unum et idem sunt ; ergo voluntas et intellectus unum et idem sunt. Q. E. D.\n\nSCHOLIUM\n\nHis causam, quæ communiter erroris esse statuitur, sustulimus. Supra autem ostendimus, falsitatem in sola privatione, quam ideæ mutilatæ et confusæ involvunt, consistere. Quare idea falsa, quatenus falsa est, certitudinem non involvit. Cum itaque dicimus, hominem in falsis acquiescere, nec de iis dubitare, non ideo ipsum certum esse sed tantum non dubitare dicimus, vel quod in falsis acquiescit, quia nullæ causæ dantur, quæ efficiant, ut ipsius imaginatio fluctuetur. Qua de re vide Scholium Propositionis 44 hujus Partis. Quantumvis igitur homo falsis adhærere supponatur, nunquam tamen ipsum certum esse dicemus. Nam per certitudinem quid positivum intelligimus (vide Prop. 43 hujus cum ejusdem Schol.) non vero dubitationis privationem. At per certitudinis privationem falsitatem intelligimus. Sed ad uberiorem explicationem præcedentis Propositionis quædam monenda supersunt. Superest deinde, ut ad objectiones, quæ in nostram hanc doctrinam objici possunt, respondeam ; et denique, ut omnem amoveam scrupulum, operæ pretium esse duxi, hujus doctrinæ quasdam utilitates indicare. Quasdam, inquam ; nam præcipuæ ex iis, quæ in quinta Parte dicemus, melius intelligentur. Incipio igitur a primo, Lectoresque moneo, ut accurate distinguant inter Ideam, sive Mentis conceptum, et inter Imagines rerum, quas imaginamur. Deinde necesse est, ut distinguant inter ideas et Verba quibus res significamus. Nam quia hæc tria, imagines scilicet, verba, et ideæ, a multis vel plane confunduntur, vel non satis accurate, vel denique non satis caute distinguuntur, ideo hanc de voluntate doctrinam, sicut prorsus necessariam, tam ad speculationem quam ad vitam sapienter instituendam, plane ignorarunt. Quippe, qui putant ideas consistere in imaginibus, quæ in nobis ex corporum occursu formantur, sibi persuadent, ideas illas rerum, quarum similem nullam imaginem formare possumus, non esse ideas, sed tantum figmenta, quæ ex libero voluntatis arbitrio fingimus ; ideas igitur veluti picturas in tabula mutas aspiciunt, et, hoc præjudicio præoccupati, non vident ideam, quatenus idea est, affirmationem aut negationem involvere. Deinde, qui verba confundunt cum idea, vel cum ipsa affirmatione, quam idea involvit, putant se posse contra id, quod sentiunt, velle ; quando aliquid solis verbis contra id, quod sentiunt, affirmant aut negant. Hæc autem præjudicia exuere facile is poterit, qui ad naturam Cogitationis attendit, quæ Extensionis conceptum minime involvit, atque adeo clare intelliget, ideam (quandoquidem modus cogitandi est) neque in rei alicujus imagine, neque in verbis consistere. Verborum namque et imaginum essentia a solis motibus corporeis constituitur, qui cogitationis conceptum minime involvunt.\n\nAtque hæc pauca de bis monuisse sufficiat ; quare ad prædictas objectiones transeo. Harum prima est, quod constare putant, voluntatem latius se extendere quam intellectum, atque adeo ab eodem diversam esse. Ratio autem, cur putant, voluntatem latius se extendere quam intellectum, est, quia se experiri ajunt, se non majore assentiendi, sive affirmandi et negandi facultate indigere ad infinitis aliis rebus, quas non percipimus, assentiendum, quam jam habemus, at quidem majore facultate intelligendi. Distinguitur ergo voluntas ab intellectu, quod finitus hic sit, illa autem infinita. Secundo nobis objici potest, quod experientia nihil clarius videatur docere, quam quod nostrum judicium possumus suspendere, ne rebus, quas percipimus, assentiamur ; quod hinc etiam confirmatur, quod nemo dicitur decipi, quatenus aliquid percipit, sed tantum quatenus assentitur aut dissentitur. Ex. gr. qui equum alatum fingit, non ideo concedit dari equum alatum, hoc est, non ideo decipitur, nisi simul concedat, dari equum alatum ; nihil igitur clarius videtur docere experientia, quam quod voluntas, sive facultas assentiendi, libera sit, et a facultate intelligendi diversa. Tertio objici potest, quod una affirmatio non plus realitatis videtur continere quam alia ; hoc est, non majore potentia indigere videmur ad affirmandum, verum esse id, quod verum est, quam ad aliquid, quod falsum est, verum esse affirmandum ; at unam ideam plus realitatis, sive perfectionis, quam aliam habere percipimus ; quantum enim objecta alia aliis præstantiora, tantum etiam eorum ideæ aliæ aliis perfectiores sunt ; ex quibus etiam constare videtur differentia inter voluntatem et intellectum. Quarto objici potest, si homo non operatur ex libertate voluntatis, quid ergo fiet, si in æquilibrio sit, ut Buridani asina ? famene et siti peribit ? quod si concedam, viderer asinam vel hominis statuam, non hominem concipere ; si autem negem, ergo seipsum determinabit, et consequenter eundi facultatem, et faciendi quicquid velit, habet. Præter hæc alia forsan possunt objici ; sed quia inculcare non teneor, quid unusquisque somniare potest, ad has objectiones tantum respondere curabo, idque quam potero breviter. Et quidem ad primam dico, me concedere, voluntatem latius se extendere quam intellectum, si per intellectum claras tantummodo et distinctas ideas intelligant ; sed nego voluntatem latius se extendere quam perceptiones, sive concipiendi facultatem ; nec sane video, cur facultas volendi potius dicenda est infinita quam sentiendi facultas ; sicut enim infinita (unum tamen post aliud ; nam infinita simul affirmare non possumus) eadem volendi facultate possumus affirmare, sic etiam infinita corpora (unum nempe post aliud) eadem sentiendi facultate possumus sentire sive percipere. Quod si dicant, infinita dari, quæ percipere non possumus ? regero, nos ea ipsa nulla cogitatione, et consequenter nulla volendi facultate posse assequi. At dicunt, si Deus vellet efficere, ut ea etiam perciperemus, majorem quidem facultatem percipiendi deberet nobis dare, sed non majorem quam dedit volendi facultatem ; quod idem est ac si dicerent, quod si Deus velit efficere, ut infinita alia entia intelligeremus, necesse quidem esset, ut nobis daret majorem intellectum, sed non universaliorem entis ideam, quam dedit, ad eadem infinita entia amplectendum. Ostendimus enim voluntatem ens esse universale, sive ideam, qua omnes singulares volitiones, hoc est id, quod iis omnibus commune est, explicamus. Cum itaque hanc omnium volitionum communem sive universalem ideam facultatem esse credant, minime mirum, si hanc facultatem ultra limites intellectus in infinitum se extendere dicant. Universale enim æque de uno ac de pluribus, ac de infinitis individuis dicitur. Ad secundam objectionem respondeo negando, nos liberam habere potestatem judicium suspendendi. Nam cum dicimus, aliquem judicium suspendere, nihil aliud dicimus, quam quod videt, se rem non adæquate percipere. Est igitur judicii suspensio revera perceptio, et non libera voluntas. Quod ut clare intelligatur, concipiamus puerum, equum [alatum] imaginantem, nec aliud quicquam percipientem. Quandoquidem hæc imaginatio equi existentiam involvit (per Coroll. Prop. 17 hujus), nec puer quicquam percipit, quod equi existentiam tollat, ille necessario equum ut praæsentem contemplabitur ; nec de ejus existentia poterit dubitare, quamvis de eadem non sit certus. Atque hoc quotidie in somnis experimur, nec credo aliquem esse, qui putet se, dum somniat, liberam habere potestatem suspendendi de iis, quæ somniat, judicium, efficiendique, ut ea, quæ se videre somniat, non somniet ; et nihilominus contingit, ut etiam in somnis judicium suspendamus, nempe cum somniamus, nos somniare. Porro concedo neminem decipi quatenus percipit, hoc est, Mentis imaginationes in se consideratas nihil erroris involvere concedo (vide Schol. Prop. 17 hujus) ; sed nego, hominem nihil affirmare quatenus percipit. Nam quid aliud est equum alatum percipere, quam alas de equo affirmare ? Si enim Mens præter equum alatum nihil aliud perciperet, eundem sibi præsentem contemplaretur, nec causam haberet ullam dubitandi de ejusdem existentia, nec ullam dissentiendi facultatem, nisi imaginatio equi alati juncta sit ideæ, quæ existentiam ejusdem equi tollit, vel quod percipit, ideam equi alati, quam habet, esse inadæquatam atque tum vel ejusdem equi existentiam necessario negabit, vel de eadem necessario dubitabit. Atque his puto me ad tertiam etiam objectionem respondisse, nempe, quod voluntas universale quid sit, quod de omnibus ideis prædicatur, quodque id tantum significat, quod omnibus ideis commune est, nempe affirmationem, cujus propterea adæquata essentia, quatenus sic abstracte concipitur, debet esse in unaquaque idea, et hac ratione tantum in omnibus eadem; sed non quatenus consideratur essentiam ideæ constituere ; nam eatenus singulares affirmationes æque inter se differunt, ac ipsæ ideæ. Ex. gr. affirmatio, quam idea circuli, ab illa, quam idea trianguli involvit, æque differt, ac idea circuli ab idea trianguli. Deinde absolute nego, nos æquali cogitandi potentia indigere ad affirmandum, verum esse id, quod verum est, quam ad affirmandum, verum esse id, quod falsum est. Nam hæ duæ affirmationes, si mentem spectes, se habent ab invicem, ut ens ad non-ens ; nihil enim in ideis positivum est, quod falsitatis formam constituit (vide Prop. 35 hujus cum ejus Schol. et Schol. Prop. 47 hujus). Quare hic apprime venit notandum, quam facile decipimur, quando universalia cum singularibus, et entia rationis et abstracta cum realibus confundimus. Quod denique ad quartam objectionem attinet, dico, me omnino concedere, quod homo in tali æquilibrio positus (nempe qui nihil aliud percipit quam sitem et famem, talem cibum et talem potum, qui æque ab eo distant) fame et siti peribit. Si me rogant, an talis homo non potius asinus quam homo sit æstimandus ? dico me nescire, ut etiam nescio, quanti æstimandus sit ille, qui se pensilem facit, et quanti æstimandi sint pueri, stulti, vesani, etc.\n\nSuperest tandem indicare, quantum hujus doctrine cognitio ad usum vitæ conferat, quod facile ex his animadvertemus. Nempe : I° quatenus docet nos ex solo Dei nutu agere, divinæque naturæ esse participes, et eo magis, quo perfectiores actiones agimus, et quo magis magisque Deum intelligimus. Hæc ergo doctrina, præterquam quod animum omnimode quietum reddit, hoc etiam habet, quod nos docet, in quo nostra summa felicitas sive beatitudo consistit, nempe in sola Dei cognitione, ex qua ad ea tantum agenda inducimur, quæ amor et pietas suadent. Unde clare intelligimus, quantum illi a vera virtutis æstimatione aberrant, qui pro virtute et optimis actionibus, tanquam pro summa servitute, summis præmiis a Deo decorari exspectant, quasi ipsa virtus Deique servitus non esset ipsa felicitas et summa libertas. II° Quatenus docet, quomodo circa res fortunæ, sive quæ in nostra potestate non sunt, hoc est, circa res, quæ ex nostra natura non sequuntur, nos gerere debeamus ; nempe utramque fortunæ faciem æquo animo expectare et ferre ; nimirum quia omnia ab æterno Dei decreto eadem necessitate sequuntur, ac ex essentia trianguli sequitur, quod tres ejus anguli sunt æquales duobus rectis. III° Confert hæc doctrina ad vitam socialem, quatenus docet ; neminem odio habere, contemnere, irridere, nemini irasci, invidere. Præterea quatenus docet, ut unusquisque suis sit contentus, et proximo auxilio, non ex muliebri misericordia, partialitate, neque superstitione, sed ex solo Rationis ductu, prout scilicet tempus et res postulat, ut in tertia Parte ostendam. IV° Denique confert etiam hæc doctrina non parum ad communem societatem : quatenus docet, qua ratione cives gubernandi sint et ducendi, nempe non ut serviant, sed ut libere ea, quæ optima sunt, agant. Atque his quæ in hoc Schol. agere constitueram absolvi, et eo finem huic nostræ secundæ Parti impono ; in qua puto me naturam Mentis humanæ ejusque proprietates satis prolixe, et quantum rei difficultas fert, clare explicuisse, atque talia tradidisse, ex quibus multa præclara, maxime utilia, et cognitu necessaria concludi possunt, ut partim ex sequentibus constabit.\n\nFinis Secundæ Partis","paragraphs":[{"id":"RAW_QGOH_PS_4:P0001","text":"Idea rei singularis, actu existentis, ipsius rei tam essentiam quam existentiam necessario involvit (per Coroll. Prop. 8 hujus) : At res singulares (per Prop. 15 p. I) non possunt sine Deo concipi ; sed, quia (per Prop. 6 hujus) Deum pro causa habent, quatenus sub attributo consideratur, cujus res ipsæ modi sunt, debent necessario earum ideæ (per Axiom. 4 p. I) ipsarum attributi conceptum, hoc est (per Defin. 6 p. I) Dei æternam et infinitam essentiam involvere. Q. E. D.","kind":"passage"},{"id":"RAW_QGOH_PS_4:P0002","text":"SCHOLIUM","kind":"passage"},{"id":"RAW_QGOH_PS_4:P0003","text":"Hic per existentiam non intelligo durationem, hoc est existentiam, quatenus abstracte concipitur, et tanquam quædam quantitatis species. Nam loquor de ipsa natura existentiæ, quæ rebus singularibus tribuitur propterea, quod ex æterna necessitate Dei naturæ infinita infinitis modis sequuntur (vide Prop. 16 p. I). Loquor, inquam, de ipsa existentia rerum singularium, quatenus in Deo sunt. Nam, etsi unaquaeque ab alia re singulari determinetur ad certo modo existendum, vis tamen, qua unaquæque in existendo perseverat, ex æterna necessitate naturæ Dei sequitur. Qua de re vide Coroll. Prop. 24 p. I","kind":"passage"},{"id":"RAW_QGOH_PS_4:P0004","text":"PROPOSITIO XLVI","kind":"passage"},{"id":"RAW_QGOH_PS_4:P0005","text":"Cognitio æternæ et infinitæ essentiæ Dei, quam unaquæque idea involvit est adæquata et perfecta.","kind":"passage"},{"id":"RAW_QGOH_PS_4:P0006","text":"DEMONSTRATIO","kind":"passage"},{"id":"RAW_QGOH_PS_4:P0007","text":"Demonstratio præcedentis Propositionis universalis est, et, sive res ut pars sive ut totum consideretur, ejus idea, sive totius sit sive partis (per Prop. præced.), Dei æternam et infinitam essentiam involvet. Quare id, quod cognitionem æternæ et infinitiæ essentiæ Dei dat, omnibus commune, et æque in parte ac in toto est, adeoque (per Prop. 38 hujus) erit hæc cognitio adæquata. Q. E. D.","kind":"passage"},{"id":"RAW_QGOH_PS_4:P0008","text":"PROPOSITIO XLVII","kind":"passage"},{"id":"RAW_QGOH_PS_4:P0009","text":"Mens humana adæquatam habet cognitionem æternæ et infinitæ essentiæ Dei.","kind":"passage"},{"id":"RAW_QGOH_PS_4:P0010","text":"DEMONSTRATIO","kind":"passage"},{"id":"RAW_QGOH_PS_4:P0011","text":"Mens humana ideas habet (per Prop. 22 hujus), ex quibus (per Prop. 23 hujus) se suumque Corpus (per Prop. 19 hujus) et (per Coroll. 1 Prop. 16 et per Prop. 17 hujus) corpora externa, ut actu existentia, percipit ; adeoque (per Prop. 45 et 46 hujus) cognitionem æternae et infinitæ essentiæ Dei habet adæquatam. Q. E. D.","kind":"passage"},{"id":"RAW_QGOH_PS_4:P0012","text":"SCHOLIUM","kind":"passage"},{"id":"RAW_QGOH_PS_4:P0013","text":"Hinc videmus, Dei infinitam essentiam ejusque æternitatem omnibus esse notam. Cum autem omnia in Deo sint et per Deum concipiantur, sequitur, nos ex cognitione hac plurima posse deducere, quæ adæquate cognoscamus, atque adeo tertium illud cognitionis genus formare de quo diximus in Scholio 2 Propositionis 40 hujus Partis, et de cujus præstantia et utilitate in quinta Parte erit nobis dicendi locus. Quod autem homines non æque claram Dei ac notionum communium habeant cognitionem, inde fit, quod Deum imaginari nequeant ut corpora, et quod nomen Deus junxerunt imaginibus rerum, quas videre solent, quod homines vix vitare possunt, quia continuo a corporibus externis afficiuntur. Et profecto plerique errores in hoc solo consistunt, quod scilicet nomina rebus non recte applicamus.","kind":"passage"},{"id":"RAW_QGOH_PS_4:P0014","text":"Cum enim aliquis ait, lineas, quæ ex centro circuli ad ejusdem circumferentiam ducuntur, esse inæquales ille sane aliud, tum saltem, per circulum intelligit, quam Mathematici. Sic cum homines in calculo errant, alios numeros in mente, alios in charta habent. Quare si ipsorum Mentem spectes, non errant sane ; videntur tamen errare, quia ipsos in mente putamus habere numeros, qui in charta sunt. Si hoc non esset, nihil eosdem errare crederemus ; ut non credidi quendam errare, quem nuper audivi clamantem, suum atrium volasse in gallinam vicini, quia scilicet ipsius mens satis perspecta mihi videbatur. Atque hinc pleræque oriuntur controversiæ, nempe quia homines mentem suam non recte explicant, vel quia alterius memtem male interpretantur. Nam revera, dum sibi maxime contradicunt, vel eadem vel de diversis rebus cogitant, ita ut, quos in alio errores et absurda esse putant, non sint.","kind":"passage"},{"id":"RAW_QGOH_PS_4:P0015","text":"PROPOSITIO XLVIII","kind":"passage"},{"id":"RAW_QGOH_PS_4:P0016","text":"In Mente nulla est absoluta sive libera voluntas ; sed Mens ad hoc vel illud volendum determinatur a causa, quæ etiam ab alia determinata est, et hæc iterum ab alia, et sic in infinitum.","kind":"passage"},{"id":"RAW_QGOH_PS_4:P0017","text":"DEMONSTRATIO","kind":"passage"},{"id":"RAW_QGOH_PS_4:P0018","text":"Mens certus et determinatus modus cogitandi est (per Prop. 11 hujus) adeoque (per Coroll. 2 Prop. 17 p. I) suarum actionum non potest esse causa libera, sive absolutam facultatem volendi et nolendi habere non potest ; sed ad hoc vel illud volendum (per Prop. 28 p. I) determinari debet a causa, quæ etiam ab alia determinata est, et hæciterum ab alia, etc. Q. E. D.","kind":"passage"},{"id":"RAW_QGOH_PS_4:P0019","text":"SCHOLIUM","kind":"passage"},{"id":"RAW_QGOH_PS_4:P0020","text":"Eodem hoc modo demonstratur, in Mente nullam dari facultatem absolutam intelligendi, cupiendi, amandi, etc. Unde sequitur, has et similes facultates vel prorsus fictitias, vel nihil esse præter entia Metaphysica, sive universalia, quæ ex particularibus formare solemus. Adeo ut intellectus et voluntas ad hanc et illam ideam, vel ad hanc et illam volitionem eodem modo sese habeant, ac lapideitas ad hunc et illum lapidem, vel ut homo ad Petrum et Paulum. Causam autem, cur homines se liberos esse putent, explicuimus in Appendice Partis primæ. Verum antequam ulterius pergam, venit hic notandum, me per voluntatem affirmandi et negandi facultatem, non autem cupiditatem intelligere ; facultatem, inquam, intelligo, qua Mens, quid verum quidve falsum sit, affirmat vel negat, et non cupiditatem, qua Mens res appetit vel aversatur.","kind":"passage"},{"id":"RAW_QGOH_PS_4:P0021","text":"At postquam demonstravimus, has facultates notiones esse universales, quæ a singularibus, ex quibus easdem formamus, non distinguuntur, inquirendum jam est, an ipsæ volitiones aliquid sint præter ipsas rerum ideas. Inquirendum, inquam, est, an in Mente alia affirmatio et negatio detur præter illam quam idea, quatenus idea est, involvit, qua de re vide sequentem Propositionem, ut et Definitionem 3 hujus, ne cogitatio in picturas incidat. Non enim per ideas imagines, quales in fundo oculi, et, si placet, in medio cerebro formantur, sed Cogitationis conceptus intelligo.","kind":"passage"},{"id":"RAW_QGOH_PS_4:P0022","text":"PROPOSITIO XLIX","kind":"passage"},{"id":"RAW_QGOH_PS_4:P0023","text":"In Mente nulla datur volitio, sive affirmatio et negatio, præter illam, quam idea, quatenus idea est, involvit.","kind":"passage"},{"id":"RAW_QGOH_PS_4:P0024","text":"DEMONSTRATIO","kind":"passage"},{"id":"RAW_QGOH_PS_4:P0025","text":"In Mente (per Prop. præced.) nulla datur absoluta facultas volendi et nolendi, sed tantum singulares volitiones, nempe hæc et illa affirmatio, et hæc et illa negatio. Concipiamus itaque singularem aliquam volitionem, nempe modum cogitandi, quo Mens affirmat, tres angulos trianguli æquales esse duobus redis. Hæc affirmatio conceptum sive ideam trianguli involvit, hoc est, sine idea trianguli non potest concipi. Idem enim est, si dicam, quod A conceptum B debeat involvere ac quod A sine B non possit concipi. Deinde hæc affirmatio (per Axiom. 3 hujus) non potest etiam sine idea trianguli esse. Hæc ergo affirmatio sine idea trianguli nec esse nec concipit potest. Porro hæc trianguli idea hanc eandem affirmationem involvere debet, nempe quod tres ejus anguli æquentur duobus rectis. Quare et vice versa hæc trianguli idea sine hac affirmatione nec esse nec concipi potest adeoque (per Defin.","kind":"passage"},{"id":"RAW_QGOH_PS_4:P0026","text":"2 hujus) hæc affirmatio ad essentiam ideæ trianguli pertinet, nec aliud præter ipsam est. Et quod de hac volitione diximus (quandoquidem eam ad libitum sumpsimus), dicendum etiam est de quacunque volitione, nempe quod præter ideam nihil sit. Q. E. D.","kind":"passage"},{"id":"RAW_QGOH_PS_4:P0027","text":"COROLLARIUM","kind":"passage"},{"id":"RAW_QGOH_PS_4:P0028","text":"Voluntas et intellectus unum et idem sunt.","kind":"passage"},{"id":"RAW_QGOH_PS_4:P0029","text":"DEMONSTRATIO","kind":"passage"},{"id":"RAW_QGOH_PS_4:P0030","text":"Voluntas et intellectus nihil præter ipsas singulares volitiones et ideas sunt (per Prop. 48 hujus et ejusdem Schol.). At singularis volitio et idea (per Prop. præced.) unum et idem sunt ; ergo voluntas et intellectus unum et idem sunt. Q. E. D.","kind":"passage"},{"id":"RAW_QGOH_PS_4:P0031","text":"SCHOLIUM","kind":"passage"},{"id":"RAW_QGOH_PS_4:P0032","text":"His causam, quæ communiter erroris esse statuitur, sustulimus. Supra autem ostendimus, falsitatem in sola privatione, quam ideæ mutilatæ et confusæ involvunt, consistere. Quare idea falsa, quatenus falsa est, certitudinem non involvit. Cum itaque dicimus, hominem in falsis acquiescere, nec de iis dubitare, non ideo ipsum certum esse sed tantum non dubitare dicimus, vel quod in falsis acquiescit, quia nullæ causæ dantur, quæ efficiant, ut ipsius imaginatio fluctuetur. Qua de re vide Scholium Propositionis 44 hujus Partis. Quantumvis igitur homo falsis adhærere supponatur, nunquam tamen ipsum certum esse dicemus. Nam per certitudinem quid positivum intelligimus (vide Prop. 43 hujus cum ejusdem Schol.) non vero dubitationis privationem. At per certitudinis privationem falsitatem intelligimus. Sed ad uberiorem explicationem præcedentis Propositionis quædam monenda supersunt.","kind":"passage"},{"id":"RAW_QGOH_PS_4:P0033","text":"Superest deinde, ut ad objectiones, quæ in nostram hanc doctrinam objici possunt, respondeam ; et denique, ut omnem amoveam scrupulum, operæ pretium esse duxi, hujus doctrinæ quasdam utilitates indicare. Quasdam, inquam ; nam præcipuæ ex iis, quæ in quinta Parte dicemus, melius intelligentur. Incipio igitur a primo, Lectoresque moneo, ut accurate distinguant inter Ideam, sive Mentis conceptum, et inter Imagines rerum, quas imaginamur. Deinde necesse est, ut distinguant inter ideas et Verba quibus res significamus. Nam quia hæc tria, imagines scilicet, verba, et ideæ, a multis vel plane confunduntur, vel non satis accurate, vel denique non satis caute distinguuntur, ideo hanc de voluntate doctrinam, sicut prorsus necessariam, tam ad speculationem quam ad vitam sapienter instituendam, plane ignorarunt.","kind":"passage"},{"id":"RAW_QGOH_PS_4:P0034","text":"Quippe, qui putant ideas consistere in imaginibus, quæ in nobis ex corporum occursu formantur, sibi persuadent, ideas illas rerum, quarum similem nullam imaginem formare possumus, non esse ideas, sed tantum figmenta, quæ ex libero voluntatis arbitrio fingimus ; ideas igitur veluti picturas in tabula mutas aspiciunt, et, hoc præjudicio præoccupati, non vident ideam, quatenus idea est, affirmationem aut negationem involvere. Deinde, qui verba confundunt cum idea, vel cum ipsa affirmatione, quam idea involvit, putant se posse contra id, quod sentiunt, velle ; quando aliquid solis verbis contra id, quod sentiunt, affirmant aut negant. Hæc autem præjudicia exuere facile is poterit, qui ad naturam Cogitationis attendit, quæ Extensionis conceptum minime involvit, atque adeo clare intelliget, ideam (quandoquidem modus cogitandi est) neque in rei alicujus imagine, neque in verbis consistere.","kind":"passage"},{"id":"RAW_QGOH_PS_4:P0035","text":"Verborum namque et imaginum essentia a solis motibus corporeis constituitur, qui cogitationis conceptum minime involvunt.","kind":"passage"},{"id":"RAW_QGOH_PS_4:P0036","text":"Atque hæc pauca de bis monuisse sufficiat ; quare ad prædictas objectiones transeo. Harum prima est, quod constare putant, voluntatem latius se extendere quam intellectum, atque adeo ab eodem diversam esse. Ratio autem, cur putant, voluntatem latius se extendere quam intellectum, est, quia se experiri ajunt, se non majore assentiendi, sive affirmandi et negandi facultate indigere ad infinitis aliis rebus, quas non percipimus, assentiendum, quam jam habemus, at quidem majore facultate intelligendi. Distinguitur ergo voluntas ab intellectu, quod finitus hic sit, illa autem infinita. Secundo nobis objici potest, quod experientia nihil clarius videatur docere, quam quod nostrum judicium possumus suspendere, ne rebus, quas percipimus, assentiamur ; quod hinc etiam confirmatur, quod nemo dicitur decipi, quatenus aliquid percipit, sed tantum quatenus assentitur aut dissentitur. Ex. gr.","kind":"passage"},{"id":"RAW_QGOH_PS_4:P0037","text":"qui equum alatum fingit, non ideo concedit dari equum alatum, hoc est, non ideo decipitur, nisi simul concedat, dari equum alatum ; nihil igitur clarius videtur docere experientia, quam quod voluntas, sive facultas assentiendi, libera sit, et a facultate intelligendi diversa. Tertio objici potest, quod una affirmatio non plus realitatis videtur continere quam alia ; hoc est, non majore potentia indigere videmur ad affirmandum, verum esse id, quod verum est, quam ad aliquid, quod falsum est, verum esse affirmandum ; at unam ideam plus realitatis, sive perfectionis, quam aliam habere percipimus ; quantum enim objecta alia aliis præstantiora, tantum etiam eorum ideæ aliæ aliis perfectiores sunt ; ex quibus etiam constare videtur differentia inter voluntatem et intellectum.","kind":"passage"},{"id":"RAW_QGOH_PS_4:P0038","text":"Quarto objici potest, si homo non operatur ex libertate voluntatis, quid ergo fiet, si in æquilibrio sit, ut Buridani asina ? famene et siti peribit ? quod si concedam, viderer asinam vel hominis statuam, non hominem concipere ; si autem negem, ergo seipsum determinabit, et consequenter eundi facultatem, et faciendi quicquid velit, habet. Præter hæc alia forsan possunt objici ; sed quia inculcare non teneor, quid unusquisque somniare potest, ad has objectiones tantum respondere curabo, idque quam potero breviter.","kind":"passage"},{"id":"RAW_QGOH_PS_4:P0039","text":"Et quidem ad primam dico, me concedere, voluntatem latius se extendere quam intellectum, si per intellectum claras tantummodo et distinctas ideas intelligant ; sed nego voluntatem latius se extendere quam perceptiones, sive concipiendi facultatem ; nec sane video, cur facultas volendi potius dicenda est infinita quam sentiendi facultas ; sicut enim infinita (unum tamen post aliud ; nam infinita simul affirmare non possumus) eadem volendi facultate possumus affirmare, sic etiam infinita corpora (unum nempe post aliud) eadem sentiendi facultate possumus sentire sive percipere. Quod si dicant, infinita dari, quæ percipere non possumus ? regero, nos ea ipsa nulla cogitatione, et consequenter nulla volendi facultate posse assequi.","kind":"passage"},{"id":"RAW_QGOH_PS_4:P0040","text":"At dicunt, si Deus vellet efficere, ut ea etiam perciperemus, majorem quidem facultatem percipiendi deberet nobis dare, sed non majorem quam dedit volendi facultatem ; quod idem est ac si dicerent, quod si Deus velit efficere, ut infinita alia entia intelligeremus, necesse quidem esset, ut nobis daret majorem intellectum, sed non universaliorem entis ideam, quam dedit, ad eadem infinita entia amplectendum. Ostendimus enim voluntatem ens esse universale, sive ideam, qua omnes singulares volitiones, hoc est id, quod iis omnibus commune est, explicamus. Cum itaque hanc omnium volitionum communem sive universalem ideam facultatem esse credant, minime mirum, si hanc facultatem ultra limites intellectus in infinitum se extendere dicant. Universale enim æque de uno ac de pluribus, ac de infinitis individuis dicitur.","kind":"passage"},{"id":"RAW_QGOH_PS_4:P0041","text":"Ad secundam objectionem respondeo negando, nos liberam habere potestatem judicium suspendendi. Nam cum dicimus, aliquem judicium suspendere, nihil aliud dicimus, quam quod videt, se rem non adæquate percipere. Est igitur judicii suspensio revera perceptio, et non libera voluntas. Quod ut clare intelligatur, concipiamus puerum, equum [alatum] imaginantem, nec aliud quicquam percipientem. Quandoquidem hæc imaginatio equi existentiam involvit (per Coroll. Prop. 17 hujus), nec puer quicquam percipit, quod equi existentiam tollat, ille necessario equum ut praæsentem contemplabitur ; nec de ejus existentia poterit dubitare, quamvis de eadem non sit certus.","kind":"passage"},{"id":"RAW_QGOH_PS_4:P0042","text":"Atque hoc quotidie in somnis experimur, nec credo aliquem esse, qui putet se, dum somniat, liberam habere potestatem suspendendi de iis, quæ somniat, judicium, efficiendique, ut ea, quæ se videre somniat, non somniet ; et nihilominus contingit, ut etiam in somnis judicium suspendamus, nempe cum somniamus, nos somniare. Porro concedo neminem decipi quatenus percipit, hoc est, Mentis imaginationes in se consideratas nihil erroris involvere concedo (vide Schol. Prop. 17 hujus) ; sed nego, hominem nihil affirmare quatenus percipit. Nam quid aliud est equum alatum percipere, quam alas de equo affirmare ?","kind":"passage"},{"id":"RAW_QGOH_PS_4:P0043","text":"Si enim Mens præter equum alatum nihil aliud perciperet, eundem sibi præsentem contemplaretur, nec causam haberet ullam dubitandi de ejusdem existentia, nec ullam dissentiendi facultatem, nisi imaginatio equi alati juncta sit ideæ, quæ existentiam ejusdem equi tollit, vel quod percipit, ideam equi alati, quam habet, esse inadæquatam atque tum vel ejusdem equi existentiam necessario negabit, vel de eadem necessario dubitabit.","kind":"passage"},{"id":"RAW_QGOH_PS_4:P0044","text":"Atque his puto me ad tertiam etiam objectionem respondisse, nempe, quod voluntas universale quid sit, quod de omnibus ideis prædicatur, quodque id tantum significat, quod omnibus ideis commune est, nempe affirmationem, cujus propterea adæquata essentia, quatenus sic abstracte concipitur, debet esse in unaquaque idea, et hac ratione tantum in omnibus eadem; sed non quatenus consideratur essentiam ideæ constituere ; nam eatenus singulares affirmationes æque inter se differunt, ac ipsæ ideæ. Ex. gr. affirmatio, quam idea circuli, ab illa, quam idea trianguli involvit, æque differt, ac idea circuli ab idea trianguli. Deinde absolute nego, nos æquali cogitandi potentia indigere ad affirmandum, verum esse id, quod verum est, quam ad affirmandum, verum esse id, quod falsum est.","kind":"passage"},{"id":"RAW_QGOH_PS_4:P0045","text":"Nam hæ duæ affirmationes, si mentem spectes, se habent ab invicem, ut ens ad non-ens ; nihil enim in ideis positivum est, quod falsitatis formam constituit (vide Prop. 35 hujus cum ejus Schol. et Schol. Prop. 47 hujus). Quare hic apprime venit notandum, quam facile decipimur, quando universalia cum singularibus, et entia rationis et abstracta cum realibus confundimus. Quod denique ad quartam objectionem attinet, dico, me omnino concedere, quod homo in tali æquilibrio positus (nempe qui nihil aliud percipit quam sitem et famem, talem cibum et talem potum, qui æque ab eo distant) fame et siti peribit. Si me rogant, an talis homo non potius asinus quam homo sit æstimandus ? dico me nescire, ut etiam nescio, quanti æstimandus sit ille, qui se pensilem facit, et quanti æstimandi sint pueri, stulti, vesani, etc.","kind":"passage"},{"id":"RAW_QGOH_PS_4:P0046","text":"Superest tandem indicare, quantum hujus doctrine cognitio ad usum vitæ conferat, quod facile ex his animadvertemus. Nempe : I° quatenus docet nos ex solo Dei nutu agere, divinæque naturæ esse participes, et eo magis, quo perfectiores actiones agimus, et quo magis magisque Deum intelligimus. Hæc ergo doctrina, præterquam quod animum omnimode quietum reddit, hoc etiam habet, quod nos docet, in quo nostra summa felicitas sive beatitudo consistit, nempe in sola Dei cognitione, ex qua ad ea tantum agenda inducimur, quæ amor et pietas suadent. Unde clare intelligimus, quantum illi a vera virtutis æstimatione aberrant, qui pro virtute et optimis actionibus, tanquam pro summa servitute, summis præmiis a Deo decorari exspectant, quasi ipsa virtus Deique servitus non esset ipsa felicitas et summa libertas.","kind":"passage"},{"id":"RAW_QGOH_PS_4:P0047","text":"II° Quatenus docet, quomodo circa res fortunæ, sive quæ in nostra potestate non sunt, hoc est, circa res, quæ ex nostra natura non sequuntur, nos gerere debeamus ; nempe utramque fortunæ faciem æquo animo expectare et ferre ; nimirum quia omnia ab æterno Dei decreto eadem necessitate sequuntur, ac ex essentia trianguli sequitur, quod tres ejus anguli sunt æquales duobus rectis. III° Confert hæc doctrina ad vitam socialem, quatenus docet ; neminem odio habere, contemnere, irridere, nemini irasci, invidere. Præterea quatenus docet, ut unusquisque suis sit contentus, et proximo auxilio, non ex muliebri misericordia, partialitate, neque superstitione, sed ex solo Rationis ductu, prout scilicet tempus et res postulat, ut in tertia Parte ostendam.","kind":"passage"},{"id":"RAW_QGOH_PS_4:P0048","text":"IV° Denique confert etiam hæc doctrina non parum ad communem societatem : quatenus docet, qua ratione cives gubernandi sint et ducendi, nempe non ut serviant, sed ut libere ea, quæ optima sunt, agant. Atque his quæ in hoc Schol. agere constitueram absolvi, et eo finem huic nostræ secundæ Parti impono ; in qua puto me naturam Mentis humanæ ejusque proprietates satis prolixe, et quantum rei difficultas fert, clare explicuisse, atque talia tradidisse, ex quibus multa præclara, maxime utilia, et cognitu necessaria concludi possunt, ut partim ex sequentibus constabit.","kind":"passage"},{"id":"RAW_QGOH_PS_4:P0049","text":"Finis Secundæ Partis","kind":"passage"}],"sha256":"218c358371469460db8b621e9fbaddd48ead916ae877893f371bec53616ed477","language_code":"la","language_name":"Latina","translations":[{"id":"SOURCE_TR_20261015T013123620011Z_f79484bb","language_code":"en","language_name":"English","title":"Part Two - On the Nature and Origin of the Mind · passage 4","source_sha256":"218c358371469460db8b621e9fbaddd48ead916ae877893f371bec53616ed477","sha256":"64a58d6d5f2c104d1726eaff8623fb2599e6651e20f17a4d4358f6e8b20a35a4","rendition_type":"machine","review_status":"machine","model":"gpt-5.6-terra","created":"2026-10-15T01:31:23.620105+00:00","paragraphs":[{"id":"RAW_QGOH_PS_4:P0001","text":"The idea of a singular thing actually existing necessarily involves both the essence and the existence of that thing itself (by the Corollary to Prop. 8 of this Part): But singular things (by Prop. 15, Part I) cannot be conceived without God; but, because (by Prop. 6 of this Part) they have God as their cause, insofar as He is considered under the attribute of which the things themselves are modes, their ideas must necessarily (by Axiom 4, Part I) involve the concept of their attribute, that is (by Def. 6, Part I), the eternal and infinite essence of God. Q. E. D.","kind":"passage","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_QGOH_PS_4:P0001"]},{"id":"RAW_QGOH_PS_4:P0002","text":"SCHOLIUM","kind":"passage","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_QGOH_PS_4:P0002"]},{"id":"RAW_QGOH_PS_4:P0003","text":"Here, by existence, I do not understand duration, that is, existence insofar as it is conceived abstractly and as a certain species of quantity. For I am speaking of the very nature of existence which is attributed to singular things because infinitely many things follow in infinitely many modes from the eternal necessity of God's nature (see Prop. 16, Part I). I speak, I say, of the very existence of singular things, insofar as they are in God. For, although each is determined by another singular thing to exist in a certain manner, nevertheless the force by which each perseveres in existing follows from the eternal necessity of God's nature. On this matter see the Corollary to Prop. 24, Part I.","kind":"passage","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_QGOH_PS_4:P0003"]},{"id":"RAW_QGOH_PS_4:P0004","text":"PROPOSITION XLVI","kind":"passage","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_QGOH_PS_4:P0004"]},{"id":"RAW_QGOH_PS_4:P0005","text":"The knowledge of the eternal and infinite essence of God, which each idea involves, is adequate and perfect.","kind":"passage","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_QGOH_PS_4:P0005"]},{"id":"RAW_QGOH_PS_4:P0006","text":"DEMONSTRATION","kind":"passage","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_QGOH_PS_4:P0006"]},{"id":"RAW_QGOH_PS_4:P0007","text":"The demonstration of the preceding Proposition is universal, and, whether a thing is considered as a part or as a whole, its idea, whether it be of the whole or of the part (by the preceding Prop.), will involve the eternal and infinite essence of God. Therefore that which gives the knowledge of the eternal and infinite essence of God is common to all, and is equally in the part as in the whole; and hence (by Prop. 38 of this Part) this knowledge will be adequate. Q. E. D.","kind":"passage","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_QGOH_PS_4:P0007"]},{"id":"RAW_QGOH_PS_4:P0008","text":"PROPOSITION XLVII","kind":"passage","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_QGOH_PS_4:P0008"]},{"id":"RAW_QGOH_PS_4:P0009","text":"The human Mind has an adequate knowledge of the eternal and infinite essence of God.","kind":"passage","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_QGOH_PS_4:P0009"]},{"id":"RAW_QGOH_PS_4:P0010","text":"DEMONSTRATION","kind":"passage","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_QGOH_PS_4:P0010"]},{"id":"RAW_QGOH_PS_4:P0011","text":"The human Mind has ideas (by Prop. 22 of this Part), from which (by Prop. 23 of this Part) it perceives itself and its Body (by Prop. 19 of this Part), and (by Coroll. 1 to Prop. 16 and by Prop. 17 of this Part) external bodies, as actually existing; and hence (by Props. 45 and 46 of this Part) it has an adequate knowledge of the eternal and infinite essence of God. Q. E. D.","kind":"passage","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_QGOH_PS_4:P0011"]},{"id":"RAW_QGOH_PS_4:P0012","text":"SCHOLIUM","kind":"passage","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_QGOH_PS_4:P0012"]},{"id":"RAW_QGOH_PS_4:P0013","text":"From this we see that the infinite essence of God and His eternity are known to all. But since all things are in God and are conceived through God, it follows that from this knowledge we can deduce very many things which we know adequately, and thus form that third kind of knowledge of which we spoke in Scholium 2 of Proposition 40 of this Part, and concerning whose excellence and usefulness there will be occasion for us to speak in Part V. But the reason why men do not have an equally clear knowledge of God and of common notions is that they cannot imagine God as they can bodies, and that they have joined the name God to images of things which they are accustomed to see—a thing men can scarcely avoid, since they are continually affected by external bodies. And indeed most errors consist in this alone, namely, that we do not apply names correctly to things.","kind":"passage","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_QGOH_PS_4:P0013"]},{"id":"RAW_QGOH_PS_4:P0014","text":"For when someone says that the lines which are drawn from the centre of a circle to its circumference are unequal, he certainly understands something other, at least then, by a circle than Mathematicians do. Thus, when men make an error in calculation, they have one set of numbers in their mind and another on paper. Hence, if you consider their Mind, they certainly do not err; yet they seem to err because we think that they have in their mind the numbers that are on the paper. If this were not so, we would not believe that they erred at all; just as I did not believe that a certain man, whom I recently heard crying out that his courtyard had flown into his neighbour's hen, was in error, because his mind seemed sufficiently clear to me. And from this arise most controversies, namely, because men do not explain their own mind correctly, or interpret another's mind badly. For in truth, while they contradict one another most vehemently, they are thinking either of the same things or of different things, so that the errors and absurdities which they suppose to be in another are not there.","kind":"passage","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_QGOH_PS_4:P0014"]},{"id":"RAW_QGOH_PS_4:P0015","text":"PROPOSITION XLVIII","kind":"passage","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_QGOH_PS_4:P0015"]},{"id":"RAW_QGOH_PS_4:P0016","text":"There is in the Mind no absolute, or free, will; rather, the Mind is determined to will this or that by a cause which is also determined by another, and this again by another, and so on to infinity.","kind":"passage","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_QGOH_PS_4:P0016"]},{"id":"RAW_QGOH_PS_4:P0017","text":"DEMONSTRATION","kind":"passage","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_QGOH_PS_4:P0017"]},{"id":"RAW_QGOH_PS_4:P0018","text":"The Mind is a certain and determinate mode of thinking (by Prop. 11 of this Part), and hence (by Coroll. 2 to Prop. 17, Part I) cannot be the free cause of its actions, or have an absolute faculty of willing and not willing; rather, it must be determined to will this or that (by Prop. 28, Part I) by a cause which is also determined by another, and this again by another, etc. Q. E. D.","kind":"passage","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_QGOH_PS_4:P0018"]},{"id":"RAW_QGOH_PS_4:P0019","text":"SCHOLIUM","kind":"passage","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_QGOH_PS_4:P0019"]},{"id":"RAW_QGOH_PS_4:P0020","text":"In this same way it is demonstrated that there is in the Mind no absolute faculty of understanding, desiring, loving, etc. From which it follows that these and similar faculties are either entirely fictitious, or are nothing but metaphysical entities, or universals, which we are accustomed to form from particulars. Thus intellect and will are related to this and that idea, or to this and that volition, in the same way as stoniness is related to this and that stone, or as man is related to Peter and Paul. Moreover, we explained in the Appendix to Part I the cause why men think themselves free. But before proceeding further, it should here be noted that by will I understand the faculty of affirming and denying, and not desire; I understand, I say, the faculty by which the Mind affirms or denies what is true or false, and not the desire by which the Mind seeks or shuns things.","kind":"passage","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_QGOH_PS_4:P0020"]},{"id":"RAW_QGOH_PS_4:P0021","text":"But after we have demonstrated that these faculties are universal notions, which are not distinguished from the singulars from which we form them, we must now inquire whether volitions themselves are anything besides the ideas of things themselves. We must inquire, I say, whether there is in the Mind any other affirmation and negation besides that which an idea, insofar as it is an idea, involves; concerning which see the following Proposition, as also Definition 3 of this Part, lest thought fall into pictures. For by ideas I understand not images such as are formed in the fundus of the eye and, if you please, in the middle of the brain, but concepts of Thought.","kind":"passage","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_QGOH_PS_4:P0021"]},{"id":"RAW_QGOH_PS_4:P0022","text":"PROPOSITION XLIX","kind":"passage","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_QGOH_PS_4:P0022"]},{"id":"RAW_QGOH_PS_4:P0023","text":"In the Mind there is given no volition, or affirmation and negation, besides that which an idea, insofar as it is an idea, involves.","kind":"passage","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_QGOH_PS_4:P0023"]},{"id":"RAW_QGOH_PS_4:P0024","text":"DEMONSTRATION","kind":"passage","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_QGOH_PS_4:P0024"]},{"id":"RAW_QGOH_PS_4:P0025","text":"In the Mind (by the preceding Proposition) there is given no absolute faculty of willing and not willing, but only singular volitions, namely, this and that affirmation, and this and that negation. Let us therefore conceive some singular volition, namely, a mode of thinking by which the Mind affirms that the three angles of a triangle are equal to two right [angles]. This affirmation involves the concept, or idea, of a triangle; that is, without the idea of a triangle it cannot be conceived. For it is the same if I say that A must involve the concept of B, and that A cannot be conceived without B. Further, this affirmation (by Axiom 3 of this Part) cannot exist without the idea of a triangle. Therefore this affirmation can neither be nor be conceived without the idea of a triangle. Moreover, this idea of a triangle must involve this same affirmation, namely, that its three angles are equal to two right [angles]. Wherefore, conversely, this idea of a triangle can neither be nor be conceived without this affirmation, and therefore (by Definition.","kind":"passage","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_QGOH_PS_4:P0025"]},{"id":"RAW_QGOH_PS_4:P0026","text":"2 of this Part) this affirmation pertains to the essence of the idea of a triangle, and is nothing other than the idea itself. And what we have said of this volition (since we took it at pleasure) must also be said of any volition whatsoever, namely, that it is nothing apart from the idea. Q. E. D.","kind":"passage","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_QGOH_PS_4:P0026"]},{"id":"RAW_QGOH_PS_4:P0027","text":"COROLLARY","kind":"passage","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_QGOH_PS_4:P0027"]},{"id":"RAW_QGOH_PS_4:P0028","text":"Will and intellect are one and the same.","kind":"passage","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_QGOH_PS_4:P0028"]},{"id":"RAW_QGOH_PS_4:P0029","text":"DEMONSTRATION","kind":"passage","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_QGOH_PS_4:P0029"]},{"id":"RAW_QGOH_PS_4:P0030","text":"Will and intellect are nothing apart from the singular volitions and ideas themselves (by Proposition 48 of this Part and its Scholium). But a singular volition and an idea (by the preceding Proposition) are one and the same; therefore will and intellect are one and the same. Q. E. D.","kind":"passage","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_QGOH_PS_4:P0030"]},{"id":"RAW_QGOH_PS_4:P0031","text":"SCHOLIUM","kind":"passage","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_QGOH_PS_4:P0031"]},{"id":"RAW_QGOH_PS_4:P0032","text":"By these things we have removed the cause which is commonly held to be that of error. But above we have shown that falsity consists solely in the privation which mutilated and confused ideas involve. Wherefore a false idea, insofar as it is false, does not involve certainty. Therefore, when we say that a man acquiesces in falsehoods and does not doubt them, we do not thereby say that he is certain, but only that he does not doubt, or that he acquiesces in falsehoods because no causes are given which bring it about that his imagination fluctuates. On this matter see the Scholium of Proposition 44 of this Part. Therefore, however much a man may be supposed to adhere to falsehoods, we shall nevertheless never say that he is certain. For by certainty we understand something positive (see Proposition 43 of this Part with its Scholium), not the privation of doubt. But by the privation of certainty we understand falsity. Yet, for a fuller explanation of the preceding Proposition, certain things remain to be noted.","kind":"passage","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_QGOH_PS_4:P0032"]},{"id":"RAW_QGOH_PS_4:P0033","text":"It remains, then, for me to reply to the objections which can be raised against this doctrine of ours; and finally, in order to remove every scruple, I have thought it worthwhile to indicate certain advantages of this doctrine. Certain advantages, I say; for the chief of them will be better understood from what we shall say in Part Five. I begin, therefore, with the first, and warn readers to distinguish carefully between an Idea, or a concept of the Mind, and Images of things which we imagine. Next, it is necessary that they distinguish between ideas and the Words by which we signify things. For because these three things, namely images, words, and ideas, are by many either altogether confused, or not distinguished sufficiently accurately, or finally not distinguished sufficiently cautiously, they have been wholly ignorant of this doctrine concerning the will, although it is absolutely necessary both for speculation and for wisely ordering life.","kind":"passage","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_QGOH_PS_4:P0033"]},{"id":"RAW_QGOH_PS_4:P0034","text":"Indeed, those who think that ideas consist in images which are formed in us from the encounters of bodies persuade themselves that those ideas of things of which we can form no similar image are not ideas, but only fictions which we feign by the free choice of the will; thus they look upon ideas as though they were mute pictures on a tablet, and, preoccupied by this prejudice, fail to see that an idea, insofar as it is an idea, involves affirmation or negation. Next, those who confuse words with an idea, or with the very affirmation which the idea involves, think that they can will contrary to what they feel, when by words alone they affirm or deny something contrary to what they feel. But one will easily be able to lay aside these prejudices if he attends to the nature of Thought, which does not at all involve the concept of Extension, and will consequently understand clearly that an idea (since it is a mode of thinking) consists neither in the image of any thing nor in words.","kind":"passage","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_QGOH_PS_4:P0034"]},{"id":"RAW_QGOH_PS_4:P0035","text":"For the essence of words and images is constituted solely by bodily motions, which do not at all involve the concept of thought.","kind":"passage","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_QGOH_PS_4:P0035"]},{"id":"RAW_QGOH_PS_4:P0036","text":"And let it suffice to have given this brief warning concerning these matters; wherefore I proceed to the aforesaid objections. The first of these is that they think it established that the will extends more widely than the intellect, and is therefore distinct from it. Now the reason why they think the will extends more widely than the intellect is that they say they experience no need of a greater faculty of assenting, or of affirming and denying, in order to assent to infinitely many other things which we do not perceive, than that which we now possess; but they do need a greater faculty of understanding. Thus will is distinguished from intellect because the latter is finite, whereas the former is infinite. Secondly, it can be objected to us that experience seems to teach nothing more clearly than that we can suspend our judgment so as not to assent to things which we perceive; this is also confirmed by the fact that no one is said to be deceived insofar as he perceives something, but only insofar as he assents or dissents. For example,","kind":"passage","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_QGOH_PS_4:P0036"]},{"id":"RAW_QGOH_PS_4:P0037","text":"he who imagines a winged horse does not thereby grant that a winged horse exists; that is, he is not thereby deceived, unless he at the same time grants that a winged horse exists. Therefore experience seems to teach nothing more clearly than that the will, or faculty of assenting, is free and distinct from the faculty of understanding. Thirdly, it can be objected that one affirmation seems to contain no more reality than another; that is, we do not seem to need greater power to affirm as true that which is true than to affirm as true something which is false. But we perceive that one idea has more reality, or perfection, than another; for insofar as some objects surpass others, so too are their ideas more perfect than one another; from which the difference between will and intellect also seems to be established.","kind":"passage","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_QGOH_PS_4:P0037"]},{"id":"RAW_QGOH_PS_4:P0038","text":"Fourthly, it can be objected: if a man does not act from freedom of the will, what then will happen if he is in equilibrium, like Buridan's ass? Will he perish of hunger and thirst? If I grant this, I should seem to conceive an ass, or a statue of a man, not a man; but if I deny it, then he will determine himself, and consequently has the faculty of going and of doing whatever he wishes. Besides these, other objections may perhaps be raised; but because I am not obliged to press home whatever anyone can dream up, I shall endeavor to reply only to these objections, and as briefly as I can.","kind":"passage","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_QGOH_PS_4:P0038"]},{"id":"RAW_QGOH_PS_4:P0039","text":"And indeed, to the first I say that I grant that the will extends more widely than the intellect, if by intellect they understand only clear and distinct ideas; but I deny that the will extends more widely than perceptions, or the faculty of conceiving. Nor indeed do I see why the faculty of willing should rather be called infinite than the faculty of sensing; for just as by the same faculty of willing we can affirm infinitely many things (yet one after another, since we cannot affirm infinitely many things at once), so also by the same faculty of sensing we can sense or perceive infinitely many bodies (namely, one after another). If they say that there are infinitely many things which we cannot perceive, I shall reply that we are unable to attain those very things by any thought, and consequently by no faculty of willing.","kind":"passage","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_QGOH_PS_4:P0039"]},{"id":"RAW_QGOH_PS_4:P0040","text":"But they say that if God willed to bring it about that we also perceived those things, He would indeed have to give us a greater faculty of perceiving, but not a greater faculty of willing than He has given; which is the same as if they were to say that, if God willed to bring it about that we understood infinitely many other beings, it would indeed be necessary for Him to give us a greater intellect, but not a more universal idea of being than He has given, in order to comprehend those same infinitely many beings. For we have shown that the will is a universal being, or an idea by which we explain all singular volitions, that is, what is common to them all. Therefore, since they believe this common or universal idea of all volitions to be a faculty, it is not at all surprising if they say that this faculty extends infinitely beyond the limits of the intellect. For the universal is said equally of one, of many, and of infinitely many individuals.","kind":"passage","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_QGOH_PS_4:P0040"]},{"id":"RAW_QGOH_PS_4:P0041","text":"To the second objection I reply by denying that we have a free power of suspending judgment. For when we say that someone suspends judgment, we say nothing other than that he sees that he does not adequately perceive the matter. Therefore suspension of judgment is in truth a perception, and not a free will. In order to understand this clearly, let us conceive a child imagining a winged horse and perceiving nothing else whatever. Since this imagination involves the existence of the horse (by the Corollary of Proposition 17 of this Part), and the child perceives nothing that removes the existence of the horse, he will necessarily contemplate the horse as present to him; nor will he be able to doubt its existence, although he is not certain of it.","kind":"passage","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_QGOH_PS_4:P0041"]},{"id":"RAW_QGOH_PS_4:P0042","text":"And this we experience daily in dreams, nor do I believe there is anyone who thinks that, while dreaming, he has the free power to suspend judgment concerning the things he dreams, and to bring it about that he does not dream those things which he dreams that he sees; and nevertheless it happens that even in dreams we suspend judgment, namely, when we dream that we are dreaming. Furthermore, I grant that no one is deceived insofar as he perceives; that is, I grant that the imaginations of the Mind, considered in themselves, involve no error (see the Scholium of Proposition 17 of this Part); but I deny that a man affirms nothing insofar as he perceives. For what else is it to perceive a winged horse than to affirm wings of the horse?","kind":"passage","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_QGOH_PS_4:P0042"]},{"id":"RAW_QGOH_PS_4:P0043","text":"For if the Mind perceived nothing other than a winged horse, it would contemplate it as present to itself, and would have no cause whatever for doubting its existence, nor any faculty of dissenting, unless the imagination of the winged horse is joined to an idea which removes the existence of that same horse, or unless it perceives that the idea of the winged horse which it has is inadequate; and then it will necessarily either deny the existence of that same horse or necessarily doubt it.","kind":"passage","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_QGOH_PS_4:P0043"]},{"id":"RAW_QGOH_PS_4:P0044","text":"And by these things I think I have also replied to the third objection, namely, that the will is something universal, which is predicated of all ideas and signifies only what is common to all ideas, namely affirmation; therefore its adequate essence, insofar as it is conceived thus abstractly, must be the same in each and every idea, and in this respect only the same in all; but not insofar as it is considered to constitute the essence of an idea; for to that extent singular affirmations differ from one another just as the ideas themselves do. For example, the affirmation which the idea of a circle involves differs from that which the idea of a triangle involves just as much as the idea of a circle differs from the idea of a triangle. Further, I absolutely deny that we need equal power of thinking in order to affirm as true that which is true and in order to affirm as true that which is false.","kind":"passage","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_QGOH_PS_4:P0044"]},{"id":"RAW_QGOH_PS_4:P0045","text":"For these two affirmations, if you consider the mind, stand in relation to one another as being to non-being; for there is nothing positive in ideas which constitutes the form of falsity (see Proposition 35 of this Part with its Scholium, and the Scholium of Proposition 47 of this Part). Wherefore it is especially to be noted here how easily we are deceived when we confuse universals with singulars, and beings of reason and abstractions with real things. Finally, as regards the fourth objection, I say that I entirely grant that a man placed in such an equilibrium (namely, one who perceives nothing other than thirst and hunger, such food and such drink as are equally distant from him) will perish from hunger and thirst. If they ask me whether such a man should not rather be regarded as an ass than as a man, I say that I do not know; just as I also do not know at what value he is to be esteemed who hangs himself, or at what value children, fools, madmen, etc., are to be esteemed.","kind":"passage","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_QGOH_PS_4:P0045"]},{"id":"RAW_QGOH_PS_4:P0046","text":"It remains finally to indicate how much knowledge of this doctrine contributes to the use of life, which we shall readily observe from the following. Namely: I. insofar as it teaches us that we act solely by the command of God, and are participants in the divine nature, and this the more the more perfect actions we perform, and the more and more we understand God. Therefore, besides rendering the mind altogether tranquil, this doctrine also has this advantage: it teaches us wherein our highest happiness, or blessedness, consists, namely, in the knowledge of God alone, from which we are induced to do only those things which love and piety advise. From this we clearly understand how far those depart from the true estimation of virtue who expect to be adorned by God with the highest rewards for virtue and the best actions, as though for the highest servitude—as if virtue itself and the service of God were not happiness itself and the highest freedom.","kind":"passage","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_QGOH_PS_4:P0046"]},{"id":"RAW_QGOH_PS_4:P0047","text":"II. Insofar as it teaches how we ought to conduct ourselves concerning things of fortune, or things which are not in our power, that is, concerning things which do not follow from our nature: namely, to await and bear with an equal mind each face of fortune; since all things follow from the eternal decree of God with the same necessity as it follows from the essence of a triangle that its three angles are equal to two right [angles]. III. This doctrine contributes to social life insofar as it teaches us to hate, despise, mock, be angry with, or envy no one. Moreover, insofar as it teaches that each should be content with his own, and should assist his neighbor, not from womanish compassion, partiality, or superstition, but from the guidance of Reason alone, as indeed time and circumstance require, as I shall show in Part Three.","kind":"passage","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_QGOH_PS_4:P0047"]},{"id":"RAW_QGOH_PS_4:P0048","text":"IV. Finally, this doctrine also contributes not a little to the commonwealth, insofar as it teaches by what method citizens ought to be governed and led: namely, not so that they may be slaves, but so that they may freely do what is best. And with these things I have completed what I had resolved to treat in this Scholium, and here I set an end to this our Second Part; in it I think that I have explained the nature of the human Mind and its properties at sufficient length, and, so far as the difficulty of the matter allows, clearly, and have set forth such things from which many excellent, most useful, and necessary things to know can be inferred, as will be apparent in part from what follows.","kind":"passage","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_QGOH_PS_4:P0048"]},{"id":"RAW_QGOH_PS_4:P0049","text":"End of the Second Part","kind":"passage","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_QGOH_PS_4:P0049"]}],"source_digest":"a04155c2f0f82418d1a68708e14588cb6d19780b17e3a6e852f3a41add24469e","source_language_code":"la","original_title":"Pars secunda - De natura et origine mentis · passage 4"}],"passage_basis":"frozen-grounding","original_title":"Pars secunda - De natura et origine mentis · passage 4","title_language_code":"en"},{"id":"RAW_QGOH_PTDO","title":"Part Three - On the Origin and Nature of the Affects · passage 1","order":9,"chapter_id":"CH_QGOH_PTDO","text":"## DE ORIGINE ET NATURA AFFECTUUM\n\nPlerique, qui de Affectibus et hominum vivendi ratione scripserunt, videntur non de rebus naturalibus, quæ communis Naturæ leges sequuntur, sed de rebus, quæ extra Naturam sunt, agere. Imo hominem in Natura veluti imperium in imperio concipere videntur. Nam hominem Naturæ ordinem magis pertubare quam sequi, ipsumque in suas actiones absolutam habere potentiam, nec aliunde quam a se ipso determinari credunt. Humanæ deinde impotentiæ et inconstantiæ causam non communi Naturæ potentiæ, sed nescio cui naturæ humanæ vitio tribuunt, quam propterea flent, rident, contemnunt, vel, quod plerumque fit, detestantur ; et qui humanæ Mentis impotentiam eloquentius vel argutius carpere novit, veluti divinus habetur. Non defuerunt tamen viri præstantissimi (quorum labori et industriæ nos multum debere fatemur), qui de recta vivendi ratione præclara multa scripserint, et plena prudentiæ consilia mortalibus dederint ; verum Affectuum naturam et vires, et quid contra Mens in iisdem moderandis possit, nemo, quod sciam, determinavit. Scio equidem celeberrimum Cartesium, licet etiam crediderit, Mentem in suas actiones absolutam habere potentiam. Affectus tamen humanos per primas suas causas explicare, simulque viam ostendere studuisse, qua Mens in Affectus absolutum habere possit imperium ; sed, mea quidem sententia, nihil præter magni sui ingenii acumen ostendit, ut suo loco demonstrabo. Nam ad illos revertere volo, qui hominum Affectus et actiones detestari vel ridere malunt, quam intelligere. His sine dubio mirun videbitur, quod hominum vitia et ineptias more Geometrico tractare aggrediar, et certa ratione demonstrare velim ea, quæ Rationi repugnare, quæque vana, absurda, et horrenda esse clamitant. Sed mea hæc est ratio. Nihil in Natura fit, quod ipsius vitio possit tribui ; est namque Natura semper eadem, et ubique una eademque ejus virtus et agendi potentia ; hoc est, Naturæ leges et regulæ, secundum quas omnia fiunt, et ex unis formis in alias mutantur, sunt ubique et semper eædem atque adeo una eademque etiam debet esse ratio rerum qualiumcunque naturam intelligendi, nempe per leges et regulas Naturæ universales. Affectus itaque odii, iræ, invidiæ, etc., in se considerati ex eadem Naturæ necessitate et virtute consequuntur, ac reliqua singularia ; ac proinde certas causas agnoscunt, per quas intelliguntur, certasque proprietates habent, cognitione nostra æque dignas ac proprietates cujuscunque alterius rei, cujus sola contemplatione delectamur. De Affectuum itaque natura et viribus, ac Mentis in eosdem potentia, eadem Methodo agam, qua in præcedentitus de Deo et Mente egi, et humanas actiones atque appetibus considerabo perinde, ac si quæstio de lineis, planis, aut de corporibus esset.\n\nDEFINITIONES\n\nI. Causam adæquatam appello eam, cujus effectus potest clare et distincte per eandem percipi, Inadæquatam autem, seu partialem, illam voco, cujus effectus per ipsam solam intelligi nequit.\n\nII. Nos tum agere dico, cum aliquid in nobis aut extra nos fit, cujus adæquata sumus causa, hoc est (per Defin. præced.) cum ex nostra natura aliquid in nobis aut extra nos sequitur, quod per eandem solam potest clare et distincte intelligi. At contra nos pati dico, cum in nobis aliquid fit, vel ex nostra natura aliquid sequitur, cujus nos non nisi partialis sumus causa.\n\nIII. Per Affectus intelligo Corporis affectiones, quibus ipsius Corporis agendi potentia augetur vel minuitur, juvatur vel coërcetur, et simul harum affectionum ideas.\n\nSi itaque alicujus harum affectionum adæquata possimus esse causa, tum per Affectum actionem intelligo ; alias passionem.\n\nPOSTULATA\n\nI. Corpus humanum potest multis affici modis, quibus ipsius agendi potentia augetur vel minuitur, et etiam aliis, qui ejusdem agendi potentiam nec majorem nec minorem reddunt.\n\nHoc Postulatum seu Axioma nititur Postulato 1 et Lemmat. 5 et 7, quæ vide post Prop. 13, p. II.\n\nII. Corpus humanum multas pati potest mutationes et nihilominus retinere objectorum impressiones seu vestigia (de quibus vide Post. 5 p. II), et consequenter easdem rerum imagines (quarum Defin. vide Schol. Prop. 17 p. II).\n\nPROPOSITIO I\n\nMens nostra quædam agit, quædam vero patitur ; nempe quatenus adæquatas habet ideas, eatenus quædam necessario agit, et quatenus ideas habet inadæquatas, eatenus necessario quædam patitur.\n\nDEMONSTRATIO\n\nCujuscunque humanæ Mentis ideæ aliæ adæquatæ sunt, aliæ autem mutilatæ et confusæ (per Schol. 2 Prop. 40 p. II). Ideæ autem, quæ in alicujus Mente sunt adæquatæ, sunt in Deo adæquatæ, quatenus ejusdem Mentis essentiam constituit (per Coroll. Prop. 11 p. II), et quæ deinde inadæquatæ sunt in Mente, sunt etiam in Deo (per idem Coroll.) adæquatæ, non quatenus ejusdem solummodo Mentis essentiam, sed quatenus etiam aliarum rerum Mentes in se simul continet. Deinde ex data quacunque idea aliquis effectus sequi necessario debet (per Prop. 36 p. I), cujus effectus Deus causa est adæquata (vid. Defin. 1 hujus), non quatenus infinitus est, sed quatenus data illa idea, affectus consideratur (vid. Prop. 9 p. II). At ejus effectus, cujus Deus est causa, quatenus affectus est idea, quæ in alicujus Mente est adæquata, illa eadem Mens est causa adæquata (per Coroll. Prop. 11 p. II). Ergo Mens nostra (per Defin. 2 hujus) quatenus ideas habet adæquatas, quædam necessario agit ; Quod erat primum. Deinde quicquid necessario sequitur ex idea, quæ in Deo est adæquata, non quatenus Mentem unius hominis tantum, sed quatenus aliarum rerum Mentes simul cum ejusdem hominis Mente in se habet, ejus (per idem Coroll. Prop. 11 p. II) illius hominis Mens non est causa adæquata, sed partialis ; ac proinde (per Defin. 2 hujus) Mens quatenus ideas inadæquatas habet, quædam necessario patitur ; Quod erat secundum. Ergo Mens nostra, etc. Q. E. D.\n\nCOROLLARIUM\n\nHinc sequitur, Mentem eo pluribus passionibus esse obnoxiam, quo plures ideas inadæquatas habet, et contra eo plura agere, quo plures habet adæquatas.\n\nPROPOSITIO II\n\nNec Corpus Mentem ad cogitandum, nec Mens Corpus ad motum, neque ad quietem, nec ad aliquid (si quid est) aliud determinare potest.\n\nDEMONSTRATIO\n\nOmnes cogitandi modi Deum quatenus res est cogitans, et non quatenus alio attributo explicatur, pro causa habent (per Prop. 6 p. II) ; id ergo, quod Mentem ad cogitandum determinat, modus Cogitandi est, et non Extensionis ; hoc est (per Defin. 1 p. II), non est Corpus ; Quod erat primum. Corporis deinde motus et quies abalio oriri debet corpore, quod etiam ad motum vel quietem determinatum fuit ab alio, et absolute quicquid incorpore oritur, id a Deo oriri debuit, quatenus aliquo Extensionis modo, et non quatenus aliquo Cogitandi modo affectus consideratur (per eand. Prop. 6 p. II), hoc est, a Mente, quæ (per Prop. 11 p. II) modus cogitandi est, oriri non potest ; Quod erat secundum. Ergo nec Corpus Mentem, etc. Q. E. D.\n\nSCHOLIUM\n\nHæc clarius intelliguntur ex iis, quæ in Scholio Propositionis 7 Partis II dicta sunt, quod scilicet Mens et Corpus una eademque res sit, quæ jam sub Cogitationis, jam sub Extensionis attributo concipitur. Unde fit, ut ordo sive rerum concatenatio una sit, sive Natura sub hoc sive sub illo attributo concipiatur ; consequenter ut ordo actionum et passionum Corporis nostri simul sit natura cum ordine actionum et passionum Mentis ; Quod etiam patet ex modo, quo Propositionem 12 Partis II demonstravimus. At, quamvis hæc ita se habeant, ut nulla dubitandi ratio supersit, vix tamen credo, nisi rem experientia comprobavero, homines induci posse ad hæc æquo animo perpendendum ; adeo firmiter persuasi sunt, Corpus ex solo Mentis nutu jam moveri, jam quiescere plurimaque agere, quæ a sola Mentis voluntate et excogitandi arte pendent. Etenim, quid Corpus possit, nemo hucusque determinavit, hoc est, neminem hucusque experientia docuit, quid Corpus ex solis legibus Naturæ, quatenus corporea tantum consideratur, possit agere, et quid non possit, nisi a Mente determinetur. Nam nemo hucusque Corporis fabricam tam accurate novit, ut omnes ejus functiones potuerit explicare, ut jam taceam, quod in Brutis plura observentur, quæ humanam sagacitatem longe superant, et quod somnambuli in somnis plurima agant, quæ vigilando non auderent ; quod satis ostendit, ipsum Corpus ex solis suæ naturæ legibus multa posse, quæ ipsius Mens admiratur. Deinde nemo scit, qua ratione, quibusve mediis Mens moveat Corpus neque quot motus gradus possit Corpori tribuere, quantaque cum celeritate idem movere queat. Unde sequitur, cum homines dicunt, hanc vel illam actionem Corporis oriri a Mente, quæ imperium in Corpus habet, eos nescire, quid dicant, nec aliud agere, quam speciosis verbis fateri, se veram illius actionis causam absque admiratione ignorare. At dicent, sive sciant, sive nesciant, quibus mediis Mens moveat Corpus, se tamen, experiri, quod, nisi Mens humana apta esset ad excogitandum, Corpus iners esset. Deinde se experiri, in sola Mentis potestate esse, tam loqui quam tacere, et alia multa, quæ proinde a Mentis decreto pendere credunt. Sed, quod ad primum attinet, ipsos rogo, num experientia non etiam doceat, quod si contra Corpus iners sit, Mens simul ad cogitandum sit inepta ? Nam cum Corpus somno quiescit, Mens simul cum ipso sopita manet, nec potestatem habet, veluti cum vigilat, excogitandi. Deinde omnes expertos esse credo, Mentem non semper æque aptam esse ad cogitandum de eodem subjecto, sed, prout Corpus aptius est, ut in eo hujus vel illius objecti imago excitetur, ita Mentem aptiorem esse ad hoc vel illud objectum contemplandum. At dicent, ex solis legibus Naturæ, quatenus corporea tantum consideratur, fieri non posse, ut causæ ædificiorum, picturarum rerumque hujusmodi, quæ sola humana arte fiunt possint deduci, nec Corpus humanum, nisi a Mente determinaretur ducereturque, pote esset ad templum aliquod ædificandum. Verum ego jam ostendi, ipsos nescire, quid Corpus possit, quidve ex sola ipsius naturæ contemplatione possit deduci, ipsosque plurima experiri ex solis Naturæ legibus fieri, quæ nunquam credidissent posse fieri, nisi ex Mentis directione ; ut sunt ea, quæ somnambuli in somnis agunt, quæque ipsi dum vigilant, admirantur. Addo hic ipsam Corporis humani fabricam ; quæ artificio longissime superat omnes, quæ humana arte fabricatæ sunt, ut jam taceam, quod supra ostenderim, ex Natura, sub quovis attributo considerata, infinita sequi. Quod porro ad secundum attinet, sane longe felicius sese res humanæ haberent, si æque in hominis potestate esset tam tacere quam loqui. At experientia satis superque docet, homines nihil minus in potestate habere quam linguam, nec minus posse quam appetitus moderari suos ; unde factum, ut plerique credant, nos ea tantum libere agere, quæ leviter petimus, quia earum rerum appetitus facile contrahi potest memoria alterius rei, cujus frequenter recordamur, sed illa minime, quæ magno cum affectu petimus, et qui alterius rei memoria sedari nequit. Verum enim vero nisi experti essent, nos plura agere, quorum postea pœnitet, nosque sæpe, quando sc. contrariis affectibus conflictamur, meliora videre et deteriora sequi, nihil impediret, quo minus crederent, nos omnia libere agere. Sic infans se lac libere appetere credit, puer autem iratus vindictam velle, et timidus fugam. Ebrius deinde credit, se ex libero Mentis decreto ea loqui, quæ postea sobrius vellet tacuisse ; sic delirans, garrula, puer, et hujus farinæ plurimi, ex libero Mentis decreto credunt loqui, cum tamen loquendi impetum, quem habent, continere nequeant ; ita ut ipsa experientia non minus clare quam Ratio doceat, quod homines ea sola de causa liberos se esse credant, quia suorum actionum sunt conscii, et causarum, a quibus determinantur, ignari ; et præterea, quod Mentis decreta nihil sint præter ipsos appetitus, quæ propterea varia sunt pro varia Corporis dispositione. Nam unusquisque ex suo affectu omnia moderatur, et qui præterea contrariis affectibus conflictantur, quid velint, nesciunt ; qui autem nullo facili momento huc atque illuc pelluntur. Quæ omnia profecto clare ostendunt, Mentis tam decretum, quam appetitum et Corporis determinationem, simul esse natura, vel potius unam eandemque rem, quam, quando sub Cogitationis attributo consideratur et per ipsum explicatur, Decretum appellamus, et quando sub Extensionis attributo consideratur, et ex legibus motus et quietis deducitur, Determinationem vocamus ; quod adhuc clarius ex jam dicendis patebit. Nam aliud est, quod hic apprime notari vellem, nempe quod nos nihil ex Mentis decreto agere possumus, nisi ejus recordemur. Ex. gr. non possumus verbum loqui, nisi ejusdem recordemur. Deinde in libera Mentis potestate non est, rei alicujus recordari vel ejusdem oblivisci. Quare hoc tantum in Mentis potestate esse creditur, quod rem, cujus recordamur, vel tacere vel loqui ex solo Mentis decreto possumus. Verum cum nos loqui somniamus, credimus nos ex libero Mentis decreto loqui, nec tamen loquimur, vel, si loquimur, id ex Corporis spontaneo motu fit. Somniamus deinde, nos quædam homines celare, idque eodem Mentis decreto, quo, dum vigilamus, ea, quæ scimus, tacemus. Somniamus denique nos ex Mentis decreto quædam agere, quæ, dum vigilamus, non audemus. Atque adeo pervelim scire, an in Mente duo decretorum genera dentur, Phantasticorum unum, et Liberorum alterum ? Quod si eo usque insanire non libet, necessario concedendum est, hoc Mentis decretum, quod liberum, esse creditur, ab ipsa imaginatione sive memoria non distingui, nec aliud esse præter illam affirmationem, quam idea, quatenus idea est, necessario involvit (vide Pr. 49 p. II). Atque adeo hæc Mentis decreta eadem necessitate in Mente oriuntur, ac ideæ rerum actu existentium. Qui igitur credunt, se ex libero Mentis decreto loqui, vel tacere, vel quiequam agere, oculis apertis somniant.\n\nPROPOSITIO III\n\nMentis actiones ex solis ideis adæquatis oriuntur ; passiones autem a solis inadæquatis pendent.\n\nDEMONSTRATIO\n\nPrimum, quod Mentis essentiam constituit, nihil aliud est quam dea Corporis actu existentis (per Prop. 11 et 13 p. II), quæ (per Prop. 15 p. II) ex multis aliis componitur, quarum quædam (per Coroll. Prop. 38 p. II) sunt adæquatae, quædam autem inadæquatæ (per Coroll. Prop. 29 p. II). Quicquid ergo ex Mentis natura sequitur, et cujus Mens causa est proxima, per quam id debet intelligi, necessario ex idea adæquata vel inadæquata sequi debet. At quatenus Mens (per Prop. 1 hujus) ideas habet inadæquatas, eatenus necessario patitur ; ergo Mentis actiones ex solis ideis adæquatis sequuntur, et Mens propterea tantum patitur, quia ideas habet inadæquatas. Q. E. D.\n\nSCHOLIUM\n\nVidemus itaque passiones ad Mentem non referri, nisi quatenus aliquid habet, quod negationem involvit, sive quatenus consideratur Naturæ pars, quæ per se absque aliis non potest clare et distincte percipi ; et hac ratione ostendere possem, passiones eodem modo ad res singulares ac ad Mentem referri, nec alia ratione posse percipi ; sed meum institutum est, de sola Mente humana agere.\n\nPROPOSITIO IV\n\nNulla res, nisi a causa externa, potest destrui.\n\nDEMONSTRATIO\n\nHæc Propositio per se patet ; definitio enim cujuscunque rei ipsius rei essentiam affirmat, sed non negat ; sive rei essentiam ponit, sed non tollit. Dum itaque ad rem ipsam tantum, non autem ad causas externas attendimus, nihil in eadem poterimus invenire, quod ipsam possit destruere. Q. E. D..\n\nPROPOSITIO V\n\nRes eatenus contrariæ sunt naturæ, hoc est, eatenus in eodem subjecto esse nequeunt, quatenus una alteram potest destruere.\n\nDEMONSTRATIO\n\nSi enim inter se convenire, vel in eodem subjecto simul esse possent, posset ergo in eodem subjecto aliquid dari, quod ipsum posset destruere, quod (per Prop. præced.) est absurdum. Ergo res etc. Q. E. D.\n\nPROPOSITIO VI\n\nUnaquæque res, quantum in se est, in suo esse perseverare conatur.\n\nDEMONSTRATIO\n\nRes enim singulares modi sunt, quibus Dei attributa certo et determinato modo exprimuntur (per Coroll. Prop. 25 p. I) ; hoc est (per Prop. 34 p. I) res, quæ Dei potentiam, qua Deus est et agit, certo et determinato modo exprimunt ; neque ulla res aliquid in se habet, a quo possit destrui, sive quod ejus existentiam tollat (per Prop. 4 hujus) ; sed contra ei omni, quod ejusdem existentiam potest tollere, opponitur (per Prop. præced.) ; adeoque quantum potest, et in se est, in suo esse perseverare conatur. Q. E. D.\n\nPROPOSITIO VII\n\nConatus, quo unaquæque res in suo esse perseverare conatur, nihil est præter ipsius rei actualem essentiam.\n\nDEMONSTRATIO\n\nEx data cujuscunque rei essentia quædam necessario sequuntur (per Prop. 36 p. I), nec res aliud possunt, quam id, quod ex determinata earum natura necessario sequitur (per Prop. 29 p. I) ; quare cujuscunque rei potentia sive conatus, quo ipsa vel sola vel cum aliis quidquam agit vel agere conatur, hoc est (per Prop. 6 hujus) potentia sive conatus, quo in suo esse perseverare conatur, nihil est præter ipsius rei datam sive actualem essentiam. Q. E. D.\n\nPROPOSITIO VIII\n\nConatus, quo unaquæque res in suo esse perseverare conatur, nullum tempus finitum, sed indefinitum involvit.\n\nDEMONSTRATIO\n\nSi enim tempus limitatum involveret, quod rei durationem determinaret, tum ex sola ipsa potentia, qua res existit, sequeretur, quod res post limitatum illud tempus non posset existere, sed quod deberet destrui ; atqui hoc (per Prop. 4 hujus) est absurdum : ergo conatus, quo res existit, nullum tempus definitum involvit ; sed contra, quoniam (per eandem Prop. 4 hujus), si a nulla externa causa destruatur, eadem potentia, qua jam existit, existere perget semper, ergo hic conatus tempus indefinitum involvit. Q. E. D.\n\nPROPOSITIO IX\n\nMens tam quatenus claras et distinctas, quam quatenus confusas habet ideas, conatur in suo esse perseverare indefinita quadam duratione, et hujus sui conatus est conscia.\n\nDEMONSTRATIO\n\nMentis essentia ex ideis adæquatis et inadæquatis constituitur (ut in Prop. 3 hujus ostendimus) ; adeoque (per Prop. 7 hujus) tam quatenus has quam quatenus iilas habet, in suo esse perseverare conatur ; idque (per Prop. 8 hujus) indefinita quadam duratione. Cum autem Mens (per Prop. 23 p. II) per ideas affectionum Corporis necessario sui sit conscia est ergo (per Prop. 7 hujus) Mens sui conatus conscia. Q. E. D.\n\nSCHOLIUM\n\nHic conatus, cum ad Mentem solam refertur, Voluntas appellatur, sed cum ad Mentem et Corpus simul refertur, vocatur Appetitus ; qui proinde nihil aliud est quam ipsa hominis essentia, ex cujus natura ea, quæ ipsius conservationi inserviunt, necessario sequuntur ; atque adeo homo ad eadem agendum determinatus est. Deinde inter Appetitum et Cupiditatem nulla est differentia, nisi quod cupiditas ad homines plerumque referatur, quatenus sui appetitus sunt conscii, et propterea sic definiri potest, nempe Cupiditas est appetitus cum ejusdem conscientia. Constat itaque ex his omnibus, nihil nos conari, velle, appetere, neque cupere, quia id bonum esse judicamus ; sed contra, nos propterea aliquid bonum esse judicare, quia id conamur, volumus, appetimus, atque cupimus.\n\nPROPOSITIO X\n\nIdea, quæ Corporis nostri existentiam secludit, in nostra Mente dari nequit, sed eidem est contraria.\n\nDEMONSTRATIO\n\nQuicquid Corpus nostrum potest destruere, in eodem dari nequit (per Prop. 5 hujus), adeoque neque ejus rei idea potest in Deo dari, quatenus nostri Corporis ideam habet (per Coroll. Prop. 9 p. I) ; hoc est (per Prop. 11 et 13 p. II), ejus rei idea in nostra Mente dari nequit ; sed contra, quoniam (per Prop. 11 et 13 p. II) primum, quod Mentis essentiam constituit, est idea corporis actu existentis, primum et præcipuum nostræ Mentis conatus est (per Prop. 7 hujus), Corporis nostri existentiam affirmare ; atque adeo idea, quæ Corporis nostri existentiam negat, nostræ Menti est contraria. Q. E. D.\n\nPROPOSITIO XI\n\nQuicquid Corporis nostri agendi potentiam auget vel minuit, juvat vel coercet, ejusdem rei idea Mentis nostræ cogitandi potentiam auget vel minuit, juvat vel coercet.\n\nDEMONSTRATIO\n\nHæc Propositio patet ex Propositione 7 Partis II, vel etiam ex Propositione 14, Partis II.\n\nSCHOLIUM\n\nVidimus itaque Mentem magnas posse pati mutationes, et jam ad majorem, jam autem ad minorem perfectionem transire, quæ quidem passiones nobis explicant affectus Lætitiæ et Tristitiæ. Per Lætitiam itaque in sequentibus intelligam passionem, qua Mens ad majorem perfectionem transit. Per Tristitiam autem passionem, qua ipsa ad minorem transit perfectionem. Porro affectum Lætitiæ, ad Mentem et Corpus simul relatum, Titillationem vel Hilaritatem voco ; Tristitiæ autem Dolorem vel Melancholiam. Sed notandum, Titillationem et Dolorem ad hominem referri, quando una ejus pars præ reliquis est affecta, Hilaritatem autem et Melancholiam, quando omnes pariter sunt affectæ. Quid deinde Cupiditas, sit, in Scholio Propositionis 9, hujus Partis explicui ; et præter hos tres nullum alium agnosco affectum primarium ; reliquos ex his tribus oriri in seqq. ostendam. Sed antequam ulterius pergam, lubet hic fusius Propositionem 10 hujus Partis explicare, ut clarius intelligatur, qua ratione idea ideæ sit contraria.\n\nIn Scholio Propositionis 17 Partis II ostendimus, ideam, quæ Mentis essentiam constituit, Corporis existentiam tamdiu involvere, quamdiu ipsum Corpus existit. Deinde ex iis, quæ in Coroll. Prop. 8 Part. II et in ejusdem Schol. ostendimus, sequitur, præsentem nostræ Mentis existentiam ab hoc solo pendere, quod sc. Mens actualem Corporis existentiam involvit. Denique Mentis potentiam, qua ipsa res imaginatur earumque recordatur, ab hoc etiam pendere ostendimus (vid. Prop. 17 et 18, p. II cum ejus Scholio), quod ipsa actualem Corporis existentiam involvit. Ex quibus sequitur, Mentis præsentem existentiam ejusque imaginandi potentiam tolli, simulatque Mens præsentem Corporis existentiam affirmare desinit. At causa, cur Mens hanc Corporis existentiam affirmare desinit, non potest esse ipsa Mens (per Prop. 4 hujus), nec etiam, quod Corpus esse desinit. Nam (per Prop. 6 p. II) causa, cur Mens Corporis existentiam affirmat, non est, quia Corpus existere incepit : quare, per eandem rationem, nec ipsius Corporis existentiam affirmare desinit, quia Corpus esse desinit, sed (per Prop. 17 p. II) hoc ab alia idea oritur, quæ nostri Corporis, et consequenter nostræ Mentis præsentem existentiam secludit, quæque adeo ideæ, quæ nostræ Mentis essentiam constituit, est contraria.\n\nPROPOSITIO XII\n\nMens, quantum potest, ea imaginari conatur, quæ Corporis agendi potentiam augent vel juvant.\n\nDEMONSTRATIO\n\nQuamdiu humanum Corpus affectum est modo, qui naturam corporis alicujus externi involvit, tamdiu Mens humana idem corpus ut præsens contemplabitur (per Prop. 17 p. II), et consequenter (per Prop. 7 p. II), quamdiu Mens humana aliquod externum Corpus ut præsens contemplatur, hoc est (per ejusdem Prop. 17 Schol.) imaginatur, tamdiu humanum Corpus affectum est modo, qui naturam ejusdem corporis externi involvit ; atque adeo, quamdiu Mens ea imaginatur, quæ Corporis nostri agendi potentiam augent vel juvant, tamdiu Corpus affectum est modis, qui ejusdem agendi potentiam augent vel juvant (vid. Post. 1 hujus) ; et consequenter (per Prop. 11 hujus) tamdiu Mentis cogitandi potentia augetur vel juvatur ; ac proinde (per Prop. 6 vel 9 hujus) Mens, quantum potest, eadem imaginari conatur. Q. E. D.\n\nPROPOSITIO XIII\n\nCum Mens ea imaginatur, quæ Corporis agendi potentiam minuunt vel coercent, conatur, quantum potest, rerum recordari, quæ horum existentiam secludunt.\n\nDEMONSTRATIO\n\nQuamdiu Mens quicquam tale imaginatur, tamdiu Mentis et Corporis potentia minuitur vel coercetur (ut in præced. Prop. demonstravimus) ; et nihilominus id tamdiu imaginabitur, donec Mens aliud imaginetur, quod hujus præsentem existentiam secludat (per Prop. 17 p. II) ; hoc est (ut modo ostendimus), Mentis et Corporis potentia tamdiu minuitur vel coercetur, donec Mens aliud imaginetur, quod hujus existentiam secludit quodque adeo Mens (per Prop. 9 hujus), quantum potest, imaginari vel recordari conabitur. Q. E. D.\n\nCOROLLARIUM","paragraphs":[{"id":"RAW_QGOH_PTDO:P0001","text":"## DE ORIGINE ET NATURA AFFECTUUM","kind":"passage"},{"id":"RAW_QGOH_PTDO:P0002","text":"Plerique, qui de Affectibus et hominum vivendi ratione scripserunt, videntur non de rebus naturalibus, quæ communis Naturæ leges sequuntur, sed de rebus, quæ extra Naturam sunt, agere. Imo hominem in Natura veluti imperium in imperio concipere videntur. Nam hominem Naturæ ordinem magis pertubare quam sequi, ipsumque in suas actiones absolutam habere potentiam, nec aliunde quam a se ipso determinari credunt. Humanæ deinde impotentiæ et inconstantiæ causam non communi Naturæ potentiæ, sed nescio cui naturæ humanæ vitio tribuunt, quam propterea flent, rident, contemnunt, vel, quod plerumque fit, detestantur ; et qui humanæ Mentis impotentiam eloquentius vel argutius carpere novit, veluti divinus habetur.","kind":"passage"},{"id":"RAW_QGOH_PTDO:P0003","text":"Non defuerunt tamen viri præstantissimi (quorum labori et industriæ nos multum debere fatemur), qui de recta vivendi ratione præclara multa scripserint, et plena prudentiæ consilia mortalibus dederint ; verum Affectuum naturam et vires, et quid contra Mens in iisdem moderandis possit, nemo, quod sciam, determinavit. Scio equidem celeberrimum Cartesium, licet etiam crediderit, Mentem in suas actiones absolutam habere potentiam. Affectus tamen humanos per primas suas causas explicare, simulque viam ostendere studuisse, qua Mens in Affectus absolutum habere possit imperium ; sed, mea quidem sententia, nihil præter magni sui ingenii acumen ostendit, ut suo loco demonstrabo. Nam ad illos revertere volo, qui hominum Affectus et actiones detestari vel ridere malunt, quam intelligere.","kind":"passage"},{"id":"RAW_QGOH_PTDO:P0004","text":"His sine dubio mirun videbitur, quod hominum vitia et ineptias more Geometrico tractare aggrediar, et certa ratione demonstrare velim ea, quæ Rationi repugnare, quæque vana, absurda, et horrenda esse clamitant. Sed mea hæc est ratio. Nihil in Natura fit, quod ipsius vitio possit tribui ; est namque Natura semper eadem, et ubique una eademque ejus virtus et agendi potentia ; hoc est, Naturæ leges et regulæ, secundum quas omnia fiunt, et ex unis formis in alias mutantur, sunt ubique et semper eædem atque adeo una eademque etiam debet esse ratio rerum qualiumcunque naturam intelligendi, nempe per leges et regulas Naturæ universales.","kind":"passage"},{"id":"RAW_QGOH_PTDO:P0005","text":"Affectus itaque odii, iræ, invidiæ, etc., in se considerati ex eadem Naturæ necessitate et virtute consequuntur, ac reliqua singularia ; ac proinde certas causas agnoscunt, per quas intelliguntur, certasque proprietates habent, cognitione nostra æque dignas ac proprietates cujuscunque alterius rei, cujus sola contemplatione delectamur. De Affectuum itaque natura et viribus, ac Mentis in eosdem potentia, eadem Methodo agam, qua in præcedentitus de Deo et Mente egi, et humanas actiones atque appetibus considerabo perinde, ac si quæstio de lineis, planis, aut de corporibus esset.","kind":"passage"},{"id":"RAW_QGOH_PTDO:P0006","text":"DEFINITIONES","kind":"passage"},{"id":"RAW_QGOH_PTDO:P0007","text":"I. Causam adæquatam appello eam, cujus effectus potest clare et distincte per eandem percipi, Inadæquatam autem, seu partialem, illam voco, cujus effectus per ipsam solam intelligi nequit.","kind":"passage"},{"id":"RAW_QGOH_PTDO:P0008","text":"II. Nos tum agere dico, cum aliquid in nobis aut extra nos fit, cujus adæquata sumus causa, hoc est (per Defin. præced.) cum ex nostra natura aliquid in nobis aut extra nos sequitur, quod per eandem solam potest clare et distincte intelligi. At contra nos pati dico, cum in nobis aliquid fit, vel ex nostra natura aliquid sequitur, cujus nos non nisi partialis sumus causa.","kind":"passage"},{"id":"RAW_QGOH_PTDO:P0009","text":"III. Per Affectus intelligo Corporis affectiones, quibus ipsius Corporis agendi potentia augetur vel minuitur, juvatur vel coërcetur, et simul harum affectionum ideas.","kind":"passage"},{"id":"RAW_QGOH_PTDO:P0010","text":"Si itaque alicujus harum affectionum adæquata possimus esse causa, tum per Affectum actionem intelligo ; alias passionem.","kind":"passage"},{"id":"RAW_QGOH_PTDO:P0011","text":"POSTULATA","kind":"passage"},{"id":"RAW_QGOH_PTDO:P0012","text":"I. Corpus humanum potest multis affici modis, quibus ipsius agendi potentia augetur vel minuitur, et etiam aliis, qui ejusdem agendi potentiam nec majorem nec minorem reddunt.","kind":"passage"},{"id":"RAW_QGOH_PTDO:P0013","text":"Hoc Postulatum seu Axioma nititur Postulato 1 et Lemmat. 5 et 7, quæ vide post Prop. 13, p. II.","kind":"passage"},{"id":"RAW_QGOH_PTDO:P0014","text":"II. Corpus humanum multas pati potest mutationes et nihilominus retinere objectorum impressiones seu vestigia (de quibus vide Post. 5 p. II), et consequenter easdem rerum imagines (quarum Defin. vide Schol. Prop. 17 p. II).","kind":"passage"},{"id":"RAW_QGOH_PTDO:P0015","text":"PROPOSITIO I","kind":"passage"},{"id":"RAW_QGOH_PTDO:P0016","text":"Mens nostra quædam agit, quædam vero patitur ; nempe quatenus adæquatas habet ideas, eatenus quædam necessario agit, et quatenus ideas habet inadæquatas, eatenus necessario quædam patitur.","kind":"passage"},{"id":"RAW_QGOH_PTDO:P0017","text":"DEMONSTRATIO","kind":"passage"},{"id":"RAW_QGOH_PTDO:P0018","text":"Cujuscunque humanæ Mentis ideæ aliæ adæquatæ sunt, aliæ autem mutilatæ et confusæ (per Schol. 2 Prop. 40 p. II). Ideæ autem, quæ in alicujus Mente sunt adæquatæ, sunt in Deo adæquatæ, quatenus ejusdem Mentis essentiam constituit (per Coroll. Prop. 11 p. II), et quæ deinde inadæquatæ sunt in Mente, sunt etiam in Deo (per idem Coroll.) adæquatæ, non quatenus ejusdem solummodo Mentis essentiam, sed quatenus etiam aliarum rerum Mentes in se simul continet. Deinde ex data quacunque idea aliquis effectus sequi necessario debet (per Prop. 36 p. I), cujus effectus Deus causa est adæquata (vid. Defin. 1 hujus), non quatenus infinitus est, sed quatenus data illa idea, affectus consideratur (vid. Prop. 9 p. II). At ejus effectus, cujus Deus est causa, quatenus affectus est idea, quæ in alicujus Mente est adæquata, illa eadem Mens est causa adæquata (per Coroll. Prop. 11 p. II).","kind":"passage"},{"id":"RAW_QGOH_PTDO:P0019","text":"Ergo Mens nostra (per Defin. 2 hujus) quatenus ideas habet adæquatas, quædam necessario agit ; Quod erat primum. Deinde quicquid necessario sequitur ex idea, quæ in Deo est adæquata, non quatenus Mentem unius hominis tantum, sed quatenus aliarum rerum Mentes simul cum ejusdem hominis Mente in se habet, ejus (per idem Coroll. Prop. 11 p. II) illius hominis Mens non est causa adæquata, sed partialis ; ac proinde (per Defin. 2 hujus) Mens quatenus ideas inadæquatas habet, quædam necessario patitur ; Quod erat secundum. Ergo Mens nostra, etc. Q. E. D.","kind":"passage"},{"id":"RAW_QGOH_PTDO:P0020","text":"COROLLARIUM","kind":"passage"},{"id":"RAW_QGOH_PTDO:P0021","text":"Hinc sequitur, Mentem eo pluribus passionibus esse obnoxiam, quo plures ideas inadæquatas habet, et contra eo plura agere, quo plures habet adæquatas.","kind":"passage"},{"id":"RAW_QGOH_PTDO:P0022","text":"PROPOSITIO II","kind":"passage"},{"id":"RAW_QGOH_PTDO:P0023","text":"Nec Corpus Mentem ad cogitandum, nec Mens Corpus ad motum, neque ad quietem, nec ad aliquid (si quid est) aliud determinare potest.","kind":"passage"},{"id":"RAW_QGOH_PTDO:P0024","text":"DEMONSTRATIO","kind":"passage"},{"id":"RAW_QGOH_PTDO:P0025","text":"Omnes cogitandi modi Deum quatenus res est cogitans, et non quatenus alio attributo explicatur, pro causa habent (per Prop. 6 p. II) ; id ergo, quod Mentem ad cogitandum determinat, modus Cogitandi est, et non Extensionis ; hoc est (per Defin. 1 p. II), non est Corpus ; Quod erat primum. Corporis deinde motus et quies abalio oriri debet corpore, quod etiam ad motum vel quietem determinatum fuit ab alio, et absolute quicquid incorpore oritur, id a Deo oriri debuit, quatenus aliquo Extensionis modo, et non quatenus aliquo Cogitandi modo affectus consideratur (per eand. Prop. 6 p. II), hoc est, a Mente, quæ (per Prop. 11 p. II) modus cogitandi est, oriri non potest ; Quod erat secundum. Ergo nec Corpus Mentem, etc. Q. E. D.","kind":"passage"},{"id":"RAW_QGOH_PTDO:P0026","text":"SCHOLIUM","kind":"passage"},{"id":"RAW_QGOH_PTDO:P0027","text":"Hæc clarius intelliguntur ex iis, quæ in Scholio Propositionis 7 Partis II dicta sunt, quod scilicet Mens et Corpus una eademque res sit, quæ jam sub Cogitationis, jam sub Extensionis attributo concipitur. Unde fit, ut ordo sive rerum concatenatio una sit, sive Natura sub hoc sive sub illo attributo concipiatur ; consequenter ut ordo actionum et passionum Corporis nostri simul sit natura cum ordine actionum et passionum Mentis ; Quod etiam patet ex modo, quo Propositionem 12 Partis II demonstravimus. At, quamvis hæc ita se habeant, ut nulla dubitandi ratio supersit, vix tamen credo, nisi rem experientia comprobavero, homines induci posse ad hæc æquo animo perpendendum ; adeo firmiter persuasi sunt, Corpus ex solo Mentis nutu jam moveri, jam quiescere plurimaque agere, quæ a sola Mentis voluntate et excogitandi arte pendent.","kind":"passage"},{"id":"RAW_QGOH_PTDO:P0028","text":"Etenim, quid Corpus possit, nemo hucusque determinavit, hoc est, neminem hucusque experientia docuit, quid Corpus ex solis legibus Naturæ, quatenus corporea tantum consideratur, possit agere, et quid non possit, nisi a Mente determinetur. Nam nemo hucusque Corporis fabricam tam accurate novit, ut omnes ejus functiones potuerit explicare, ut jam taceam, quod in Brutis plura observentur, quæ humanam sagacitatem longe superant, et quod somnambuli in somnis plurima agant, quæ vigilando non auderent ; quod satis ostendit, ipsum Corpus ex solis suæ naturæ legibus multa posse, quæ ipsius Mens admiratur. Deinde nemo scit, qua ratione, quibusve mediis Mens moveat Corpus neque quot motus gradus possit Corpori tribuere, quantaque cum celeritate idem movere queat.","kind":"passage"},{"id":"RAW_QGOH_PTDO:P0029","text":"Unde sequitur, cum homines dicunt, hanc vel illam actionem Corporis oriri a Mente, quæ imperium in Corpus habet, eos nescire, quid dicant, nec aliud agere, quam speciosis verbis fateri, se veram illius actionis causam absque admiratione ignorare. At dicent, sive sciant, sive nesciant, quibus mediis Mens moveat Corpus, se tamen, experiri, quod, nisi Mens humana apta esset ad excogitandum, Corpus iners esset. Deinde se experiri, in sola Mentis potestate esse, tam loqui quam tacere, et alia multa, quæ proinde a Mentis decreto pendere credunt. Sed, quod ad primum attinet, ipsos rogo, num experientia non etiam doceat, quod si contra Corpus iners sit, Mens simul ad cogitandum sit inepta ? Nam cum Corpus somno quiescit, Mens simul cum ipso sopita manet, nec potestatem habet, veluti cum vigilat, excogitandi.","kind":"passage"},{"id":"RAW_QGOH_PTDO:P0030","text":"Deinde omnes expertos esse credo, Mentem non semper æque aptam esse ad cogitandum de eodem subjecto, sed, prout Corpus aptius est, ut in eo hujus vel illius objecti imago excitetur, ita Mentem aptiorem esse ad hoc vel illud objectum contemplandum. At dicent, ex solis legibus Naturæ, quatenus corporea tantum consideratur, fieri non posse, ut causæ ædificiorum, picturarum rerumque hujusmodi, quæ sola humana arte fiunt possint deduci, nec Corpus humanum, nisi a Mente determinaretur ducereturque, pote esset ad templum aliquod ædificandum. Verum ego jam ostendi, ipsos nescire, quid Corpus possit, quidve ex sola ipsius naturæ contemplatione possit deduci, ipsosque plurima experiri ex solis Naturæ legibus fieri, quæ nunquam credidissent posse fieri, nisi ex Mentis directione ; ut sunt ea, quæ somnambuli in somnis agunt, quæque ipsi dum vigilant, admirantur.","kind":"passage"},{"id":"RAW_QGOH_PTDO:P0031","text":"Addo hic ipsam Corporis humani fabricam ; quæ artificio longissime superat omnes, quæ humana arte fabricatæ sunt, ut jam taceam, quod supra ostenderim, ex Natura, sub quovis attributo considerata, infinita sequi. Quod porro ad secundum attinet, sane longe felicius sese res humanæ haberent, si æque in hominis potestate esset tam tacere quam loqui. At experientia satis superque docet, homines nihil minus in potestate habere quam linguam, nec minus posse quam appetitus moderari suos ; unde factum, ut plerique credant, nos ea tantum libere agere, quæ leviter petimus, quia earum rerum appetitus facile contrahi potest memoria alterius rei, cujus frequenter recordamur, sed illa minime, quæ magno cum affectu petimus, et qui alterius rei memoria sedari nequit. Verum enim vero nisi experti essent, nos plura agere, quorum postea pœnitet, nosque sæpe, quando sc.","kind":"passage"},{"id":"RAW_QGOH_PTDO:P0032","text":"contrariis affectibus conflictamur, meliora videre et deteriora sequi, nihil impediret, quo minus crederent, nos omnia libere agere. Sic infans se lac libere appetere credit, puer autem iratus vindictam velle, et timidus fugam. Ebrius deinde credit, se ex libero Mentis decreto ea loqui, quæ postea sobrius vellet tacuisse ; sic delirans, garrula, puer, et hujus farinæ plurimi, ex libero Mentis decreto credunt loqui, cum tamen loquendi impetum, quem habent, continere nequeant ; ita ut ipsa experientia non minus clare quam Ratio doceat, quod homines ea sola de causa liberos se esse credant, quia suorum actionum sunt conscii, et causarum, a quibus determinantur, ignari ; et præterea, quod Mentis decreta nihil sint præter ipsos appetitus, quæ propterea varia sunt pro varia Corporis dispositione.","kind":"passage"},{"id":"RAW_QGOH_PTDO:P0033","text":"Nam unusquisque ex suo affectu omnia moderatur, et qui præterea contrariis affectibus conflictantur, quid velint, nesciunt ; qui autem nullo facili momento huc atque illuc pelluntur. Quæ omnia profecto clare ostendunt, Mentis tam decretum, quam appetitum et Corporis determinationem, simul esse natura, vel potius unam eandemque rem, quam, quando sub Cogitationis attributo consideratur et per ipsum explicatur, Decretum appellamus, et quando sub Extensionis attributo consideratur, et ex legibus motus et quietis deducitur, Determinationem vocamus ; quod adhuc clarius ex jam dicendis patebit. Nam aliud est, quod hic apprime notari vellem, nempe quod nos nihil ex Mentis decreto agere possumus, nisi ejus recordemur. Ex. gr. non possumus verbum loqui, nisi ejusdem recordemur. Deinde in libera Mentis potestate non est, rei alicujus recordari vel ejusdem oblivisci.","kind":"passage"},{"id":"RAW_QGOH_PTDO:P0034","text":"Quare hoc tantum in Mentis potestate esse creditur, quod rem, cujus recordamur, vel tacere vel loqui ex solo Mentis decreto possumus. Verum cum nos loqui somniamus, credimus nos ex libero Mentis decreto loqui, nec tamen loquimur, vel, si loquimur, id ex Corporis spontaneo motu fit. Somniamus deinde, nos quædam homines celare, idque eodem Mentis decreto, quo, dum vigilamus, ea, quæ scimus, tacemus. Somniamus denique nos ex Mentis decreto quædam agere, quæ, dum vigilamus, non audemus. Atque adeo pervelim scire, an in Mente duo decretorum genera dentur, Phantasticorum unum, et Liberorum alterum ? Quod si eo usque insanire non libet, necessario concedendum est, hoc Mentis decretum, quod liberum, esse creditur, ab ipsa imaginatione sive memoria non distingui, nec aliud esse præter illam affirmationem, quam idea, quatenus idea est, necessario involvit (vide Pr. 49 p. II).","kind":"passage"},{"id":"RAW_QGOH_PTDO:P0035","text":"Atque adeo hæc Mentis decreta eadem necessitate in Mente oriuntur, ac ideæ rerum actu existentium. Qui igitur credunt, se ex libero Mentis decreto loqui, vel tacere, vel quiequam agere, oculis apertis somniant.","kind":"passage"},{"id":"RAW_QGOH_PTDO:P0036","text":"PROPOSITIO III","kind":"passage"},{"id":"RAW_QGOH_PTDO:P0037","text":"Mentis actiones ex solis ideis adæquatis oriuntur ; passiones autem a solis inadæquatis pendent.","kind":"passage"},{"id":"RAW_QGOH_PTDO:P0038","text":"DEMONSTRATIO","kind":"passage"},{"id":"RAW_QGOH_PTDO:P0039","text":"Primum, quod Mentis essentiam constituit, nihil aliud est quam dea Corporis actu existentis (per Prop. 11 et 13 p. II), quæ (per Prop. 15 p. II) ex multis aliis componitur, quarum quædam (per Coroll. Prop. 38 p. II) sunt adæquatae, quædam autem inadæquatæ (per Coroll. Prop. 29 p. II). Quicquid ergo ex Mentis natura sequitur, et cujus Mens causa est proxima, per quam id debet intelligi, necessario ex idea adæquata vel inadæquata sequi debet. At quatenus Mens (per Prop. 1 hujus) ideas habet inadæquatas, eatenus necessario patitur ; ergo Mentis actiones ex solis ideis adæquatis sequuntur, et Mens propterea tantum patitur, quia ideas habet inadæquatas. Q. E. D.","kind":"passage"},{"id":"RAW_QGOH_PTDO:P0040","text":"SCHOLIUM","kind":"passage"},{"id":"RAW_QGOH_PTDO:P0041","text":"Videmus itaque passiones ad Mentem non referri, nisi quatenus aliquid habet, quod negationem involvit, sive quatenus consideratur Naturæ pars, quæ per se absque aliis non potest clare et distincte percipi ; et hac ratione ostendere possem, passiones eodem modo ad res singulares ac ad Mentem referri, nec alia ratione posse percipi ; sed meum institutum est, de sola Mente humana agere.","kind":"passage"},{"id":"RAW_QGOH_PTDO:P0042","text":"PROPOSITIO IV","kind":"passage"},{"id":"RAW_QGOH_PTDO:P0043","text":"Nulla res, nisi a causa externa, potest destrui.","kind":"passage"},{"id":"RAW_QGOH_PTDO:P0044","text":"DEMONSTRATIO","kind":"passage"},{"id":"RAW_QGOH_PTDO:P0045","text":"Hæc Propositio per se patet ; definitio enim cujuscunque rei ipsius rei essentiam affirmat, sed non negat ; sive rei essentiam ponit, sed non tollit. Dum itaque ad rem ipsam tantum, non autem ad causas externas attendimus, nihil in eadem poterimus invenire, quod ipsam possit destruere. Q. E. D..","kind":"passage"},{"id":"RAW_QGOH_PTDO:P0046","text":"PROPOSITIO V","kind":"passage"},{"id":"RAW_QGOH_PTDO:P0047","text":"Res eatenus contrariæ sunt naturæ, hoc est, eatenus in eodem subjecto esse nequeunt, quatenus una alteram potest destruere.","kind":"passage"},{"id":"RAW_QGOH_PTDO:P0048","text":"DEMONSTRATIO","kind":"passage"},{"id":"RAW_QGOH_PTDO:P0049","text":"Si enim inter se convenire, vel in eodem subjecto simul esse possent, posset ergo in eodem subjecto aliquid dari, quod ipsum posset destruere, quod (per Prop. præced.) est absurdum. Ergo res etc. Q. E. D.","kind":"passage"},{"id":"RAW_QGOH_PTDO:P0050","text":"PROPOSITIO VI","kind":"passage"},{"id":"RAW_QGOH_PTDO:P0051","text":"Unaquæque res, quantum in se est, in suo esse perseverare conatur.","kind":"passage"},{"id":"RAW_QGOH_PTDO:P0052","text":"DEMONSTRATIO","kind":"passage"},{"id":"RAW_QGOH_PTDO:P0053","text":"Res enim singulares modi sunt, quibus Dei attributa certo et determinato modo exprimuntur (per Coroll. Prop. 25 p. I) ; hoc est (per Prop. 34 p. I) res, quæ Dei potentiam, qua Deus est et agit, certo et determinato modo exprimunt ; neque ulla res aliquid in se habet, a quo possit destrui, sive quod ejus existentiam tollat (per Prop. 4 hujus) ; sed contra ei omni, quod ejusdem existentiam potest tollere, opponitur (per Prop. præced.) ; adeoque quantum potest, et in se est, in suo esse perseverare conatur. Q. E. D.","kind":"passage"},{"id":"RAW_QGOH_PTDO:P0054","text":"PROPOSITIO VII","kind":"passage"},{"id":"RAW_QGOH_PTDO:P0055","text":"Conatus, quo unaquæque res in suo esse perseverare conatur, nihil est præter ipsius rei actualem essentiam.","kind":"passage"},{"id":"RAW_QGOH_PTDO:P0056","text":"DEMONSTRATIO","kind":"passage"},{"id":"RAW_QGOH_PTDO:P0057","text":"Ex data cujuscunque rei essentia quædam necessario sequuntur (per Prop. 36 p. I), nec res aliud possunt, quam id, quod ex determinata earum natura necessario sequitur (per Prop. 29 p. I) ; quare cujuscunque rei potentia sive conatus, quo ipsa vel sola vel cum aliis quidquam agit vel agere conatur, hoc est (per Prop. 6 hujus) potentia sive conatus, quo in suo esse perseverare conatur, nihil est præter ipsius rei datam sive actualem essentiam. Q. E. D.","kind":"passage"},{"id":"RAW_QGOH_PTDO:P0058","text":"PROPOSITIO VIII","kind":"passage"},{"id":"RAW_QGOH_PTDO:P0059","text":"Conatus, quo unaquæque res in suo esse perseverare conatur, nullum tempus finitum, sed indefinitum involvit.","kind":"passage"},{"id":"RAW_QGOH_PTDO:P0060","text":"DEMONSTRATIO","kind":"passage"},{"id":"RAW_QGOH_PTDO:P0061","text":"Si enim tempus limitatum involveret, quod rei durationem determinaret, tum ex sola ipsa potentia, qua res existit, sequeretur, quod res post limitatum illud tempus non posset existere, sed quod deberet destrui ; atqui hoc (per Prop. 4 hujus) est absurdum : ergo conatus, quo res existit, nullum tempus definitum involvit ; sed contra, quoniam (per eandem Prop. 4 hujus), si a nulla externa causa destruatur, eadem potentia, qua jam existit, existere perget semper, ergo hic conatus tempus indefinitum involvit. Q. E. D.","kind":"passage"},{"id":"RAW_QGOH_PTDO:P0062","text":"PROPOSITIO IX","kind":"passage"},{"id":"RAW_QGOH_PTDO:P0063","text":"Mens tam quatenus claras et distinctas, quam quatenus confusas habet ideas, conatur in suo esse perseverare indefinita quadam duratione, et hujus sui conatus est conscia.","kind":"passage"},{"id":"RAW_QGOH_PTDO:P0064","text":"DEMONSTRATIO","kind":"passage"},{"id":"RAW_QGOH_PTDO:P0065","text":"Mentis essentia ex ideis adæquatis et inadæquatis constituitur (ut in Prop. 3 hujus ostendimus) ; adeoque (per Prop. 7 hujus) tam quatenus has quam quatenus iilas habet, in suo esse perseverare conatur ; idque (per Prop. 8 hujus) indefinita quadam duratione. Cum autem Mens (per Prop. 23 p. II) per ideas affectionum Corporis necessario sui sit conscia est ergo (per Prop. 7 hujus) Mens sui conatus conscia. Q. E. D.","kind":"passage"},{"id":"RAW_QGOH_PTDO:P0066","text":"SCHOLIUM","kind":"passage"},{"id":"RAW_QGOH_PTDO:P0067","text":"Hic conatus, cum ad Mentem solam refertur, Voluntas appellatur, sed cum ad Mentem et Corpus simul refertur, vocatur Appetitus ; qui proinde nihil aliud est quam ipsa hominis essentia, ex cujus natura ea, quæ ipsius conservationi inserviunt, necessario sequuntur ; atque adeo homo ad eadem agendum determinatus est. Deinde inter Appetitum et Cupiditatem nulla est differentia, nisi quod cupiditas ad homines plerumque referatur, quatenus sui appetitus sunt conscii, et propterea sic definiri potest, nempe Cupiditas est appetitus cum ejusdem conscientia. Constat itaque ex his omnibus, nihil nos conari, velle, appetere, neque cupere, quia id bonum esse judicamus ; sed contra, nos propterea aliquid bonum esse judicare, quia id conamur, volumus, appetimus, atque cupimus.","kind":"passage"},{"id":"RAW_QGOH_PTDO:P0068","text":"PROPOSITIO X","kind":"passage"},{"id":"RAW_QGOH_PTDO:P0069","text":"Idea, quæ Corporis nostri existentiam secludit, in nostra Mente dari nequit, sed eidem est contraria.","kind":"passage"},{"id":"RAW_QGOH_PTDO:P0070","text":"DEMONSTRATIO","kind":"passage"},{"id":"RAW_QGOH_PTDO:P0071","text":"Quicquid Corpus nostrum potest destruere, in eodem dari nequit (per Prop. 5 hujus), adeoque neque ejus rei idea potest in Deo dari, quatenus nostri Corporis ideam habet (per Coroll. Prop. 9 p. I) ; hoc est (per Prop. 11 et 13 p. II), ejus rei idea in nostra Mente dari nequit ; sed contra, quoniam (per Prop. 11 et 13 p. II) primum, quod Mentis essentiam constituit, est idea corporis actu existentis, primum et præcipuum nostræ Mentis conatus est (per Prop. 7 hujus), Corporis nostri existentiam affirmare ; atque adeo idea, quæ Corporis nostri existentiam negat, nostræ Menti est contraria. Q. E. D.","kind":"passage"},{"id":"RAW_QGOH_PTDO:P0072","text":"PROPOSITIO XI","kind":"passage"},{"id":"RAW_QGOH_PTDO:P0073","text":"Quicquid Corporis nostri agendi potentiam auget vel minuit, juvat vel coercet, ejusdem rei idea Mentis nostræ cogitandi potentiam auget vel minuit, juvat vel coercet.","kind":"passage"},{"id":"RAW_QGOH_PTDO:P0074","text":"DEMONSTRATIO","kind":"passage"},{"id":"RAW_QGOH_PTDO:P0075","text":"Hæc Propositio patet ex Propositione 7 Partis II, vel etiam ex Propositione 14, Partis II.","kind":"passage"},{"id":"RAW_QGOH_PTDO:P0076","text":"SCHOLIUM","kind":"passage"},{"id":"RAW_QGOH_PTDO:P0077","text":"Vidimus itaque Mentem magnas posse pati mutationes, et jam ad majorem, jam autem ad minorem perfectionem transire, quæ quidem passiones nobis explicant affectus Lætitiæ et Tristitiæ. Per Lætitiam itaque in sequentibus intelligam passionem, qua Mens ad majorem perfectionem transit. Per Tristitiam autem passionem, qua ipsa ad minorem transit perfectionem. Porro affectum Lætitiæ, ad Mentem et Corpus simul relatum, Titillationem vel Hilaritatem voco ; Tristitiæ autem Dolorem vel Melancholiam. Sed notandum, Titillationem et Dolorem ad hominem referri, quando una ejus pars præ reliquis est affecta, Hilaritatem autem et Melancholiam, quando omnes pariter sunt affectæ. Quid deinde Cupiditas, sit, in Scholio Propositionis 9, hujus Partis explicui ; et præter hos tres nullum alium agnosco affectum primarium ; reliquos ex his tribus oriri in seqq. ostendam.","kind":"passage"},{"id":"RAW_QGOH_PTDO:P0078","text":"Sed antequam ulterius pergam, lubet hic fusius Propositionem 10 hujus Partis explicare, ut clarius intelligatur, qua ratione idea ideæ sit contraria.","kind":"passage"},{"id":"RAW_QGOH_PTDO:P0079","text":"In Scholio Propositionis 17 Partis II ostendimus, ideam, quæ Mentis essentiam constituit, Corporis existentiam tamdiu involvere, quamdiu ipsum Corpus existit. Deinde ex iis, quæ in Coroll. Prop. 8 Part. II et in ejusdem Schol. ostendimus, sequitur, præsentem nostræ Mentis existentiam ab hoc solo pendere, quod sc. Mens actualem Corporis existentiam involvit. Denique Mentis potentiam, qua ipsa res imaginatur earumque recordatur, ab hoc etiam pendere ostendimus (vid. Prop. 17 et 18, p. II cum ejus Scholio), quod ipsa actualem Corporis existentiam involvit. Ex quibus sequitur, Mentis præsentem existentiam ejusque imaginandi potentiam tolli, simulatque Mens præsentem Corporis existentiam affirmare desinit. At causa, cur Mens hanc Corporis existentiam affirmare desinit, non potest esse ipsa Mens (per Prop. 4 hujus), nec etiam, quod Corpus esse desinit. Nam (per Prop. 6 p.","kind":"passage"},{"id":"RAW_QGOH_PTDO:P0080","text":"II) causa, cur Mens Corporis existentiam affirmat, non est, quia Corpus existere incepit : quare, per eandem rationem, nec ipsius Corporis existentiam affirmare desinit, quia Corpus esse desinit, sed (per Prop. 17 p. II) hoc ab alia idea oritur, quæ nostri Corporis, et consequenter nostræ Mentis præsentem existentiam secludit, quæque adeo ideæ, quæ nostræ Mentis essentiam constituit, est contraria.","kind":"passage"},{"id":"RAW_QGOH_PTDO:P0081","text":"PROPOSITIO XII","kind":"passage"},{"id":"RAW_QGOH_PTDO:P0082","text":"Mens, quantum potest, ea imaginari conatur, quæ Corporis agendi potentiam augent vel juvant.","kind":"passage"},{"id":"RAW_QGOH_PTDO:P0083","text":"DEMONSTRATIO","kind":"passage"},{"id":"RAW_QGOH_PTDO:P0084","text":"Quamdiu humanum Corpus affectum est modo, qui naturam corporis alicujus externi involvit, tamdiu Mens humana idem corpus ut præsens contemplabitur (per Prop. 17 p. II), et consequenter (per Prop. 7 p. II), quamdiu Mens humana aliquod externum Corpus ut præsens contemplatur, hoc est (per ejusdem Prop. 17 Schol.) imaginatur, tamdiu humanum Corpus affectum est modo, qui naturam ejusdem corporis externi involvit ; atque adeo, quamdiu Mens ea imaginatur, quæ Corporis nostri agendi potentiam augent vel juvant, tamdiu Corpus affectum est modis, qui ejusdem agendi potentiam augent vel juvant (vid. Post. 1 hujus) ; et consequenter (per Prop. 11 hujus) tamdiu Mentis cogitandi potentia augetur vel juvatur ; ac proinde (per Prop. 6 vel 9 hujus) Mens, quantum potest, eadem imaginari conatur. Q. E. D.","kind":"passage"},{"id":"RAW_QGOH_PTDO:P0085","text":"PROPOSITIO XIII","kind":"passage"},{"id":"RAW_QGOH_PTDO:P0086","text":"Cum Mens ea imaginatur, quæ Corporis agendi potentiam minuunt vel coercent, conatur, quantum potest, rerum recordari, quæ horum existentiam secludunt.","kind":"passage"},{"id":"RAW_QGOH_PTDO:P0087","text":"DEMONSTRATIO","kind":"passage"},{"id":"RAW_QGOH_PTDO:P0088","text":"Quamdiu Mens quicquam tale imaginatur, tamdiu Mentis et Corporis potentia minuitur vel coercetur (ut in præced. Prop. demonstravimus) ; et nihilominus id tamdiu imaginabitur, donec Mens aliud imaginetur, quod hujus præsentem existentiam secludat (per Prop. 17 p. II) ; hoc est (ut modo ostendimus), Mentis et Corporis potentia tamdiu minuitur vel coercetur, donec Mens aliud imaginetur, quod hujus existentiam secludit quodque adeo Mens (per Prop. 9 hujus), quantum potest, imaginari vel recordari conabitur. Q. E. D.","kind":"passage"},{"id":"RAW_QGOH_PTDO:P0089","text":"COROLLARIUM","kind":"passage"}],"sha256":"5e005f2a8dcfb8185ff87df1ce35282420d187a949d34de9a9f9da867ac651ce","language_code":"la","language_name":"Latina","translations":[{"id":"SOURCE_TR_20261015T013220222751Z_c7973b66","language_code":"en","language_name":"English","title":"Part Three - On the Origin and Nature of the Affects · passage 1","source_sha256":"5e005f2a8dcfb8185ff87df1ce35282420d187a949d34de9a9f9da867ac651ce","sha256":"2350811a7cb6782987c60ed6fb1475135b9f58424194f5542fc4801296d19633","rendition_type":"machine","review_status":"machine","model":"gpt-5.6-terra","created":"2026-10-15T01:32:20.222876+00:00","paragraphs":[{"id":"RAW_QGOH_PTDO:P0001","text":"## ON THE ORIGIN AND NATURE OF THE AFFECTS","kind":"passage","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_QGOH_PTDO:P0001"]},{"id":"RAW_QGOH_PTDO:P0002","text":"Most of those who have written on the Affects and on the manner of living of men seem to deal, not with natural things, which follow the common laws of Nature, but with things which are outside Nature. Indeed, they seem to conceive man in Nature as a dominion within a dominion. For they believe that man disturbs rather than follows the order of Nature, that he has absolute power over his actions, and that he is determined by no source other than himself. They then attribute the cause of human impotence and inconstancy, not to the power of common Nature, but to I know not what defect of human nature, which they therefore bewail, ridicule, despise, or, as most often happens, execrate; and he who knows how to censure the impotence of the human Mind more eloquently or more shrewdly is regarded as it were divine.","kind":"passage","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_QGOH_PTDO:P0002"]},{"id":"RAW_QGOH_PTDO:P0003","text":"Yet there have not been lacking most distinguished men (to whose labour and diligence we confess that we owe much), who have written many excellent things concerning the right manner of living and given mortals counsels full of prudence; but no one, so far as I know, has determined the nature and powers of the Affects, and what the Mind can do in moderating them. I know indeed that the most celebrated Descartes, although he too believed that the Mind has absolute power over its actions, nevertheless endeavoured to explain human Affects through their first causes, and at the same time to show the way by which the Mind might have absolute dominion over the Affects; but, in my judgment, he showed nothing beyond the acuteness of his great genius, as I shall demonstrate in its place. For I wish to return to those who prefer to execrate or ridicule men's Affects and actions rather than to understand them.","kind":"passage","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_QGOH_PTDO:P0003"]},{"id":"RAW_QGOH_PTDO:P0004","text":"To these, without doubt, it will seem strange that I should undertake to treat men's vices and follies in the Geometrical manner, and should wish to demonstrate by certain reasoning those things which they cry out to be contrary to Reason, vain, absurd, and dreadful. But this is my reasoning. Nothing happens in Nature which can be attributed to a defect in it; for Nature is always the same, and its virtue and power of acting are everywhere one and the same; that is, the laws and rules of Nature according to which all things come to be and change from one form into another are everywhere and always the same, and therefore the method of understanding the nature of things of whatever kind must likewise be one and the same, namely through the universal laws and rules of Nature.","kind":"passage","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_QGOH_PTDO:P0004"]},{"id":"RAW_QGOH_PTDO:P0005","text":"Thus the Affects of hatred, anger, envy, etc., considered in themselves, follow from the same necessity and virtue of Nature as the other singular things; and accordingly they acknowledge certain causes through which they are understood, and possess certain properties no less worthy of our knowledge than the properties of any other thing, in the sole contemplation of which we take delight. Therefore I shall treat the nature and powers of the Affects, and the power of the Mind over them, by the same Method by which in the preceding parts I treated God and the Mind, and I shall consider human actions and appetites just as if the question concerned lines, planes, or bodies.","kind":"passage","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_QGOH_PTDO:P0005"]},{"id":"RAW_QGOH_PTDO:P0006","text":"DEFINITIONS","kind":"passage","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_QGOH_PTDO:P0006"]},{"id":"RAW_QGOH_PTDO:P0007","text":"I. By an adequate cause I understand one whose effect can be clearly and distinctly perceived through that cause itself. But by an inadequate, or partial, cause I understand one whose effect cannot be understood through it alone.","kind":"passage","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_QGOH_PTDO:P0007"]},{"id":"RAW_QGOH_PTDO:P0008","text":"II. I say that we act when something takes place in us or outside us of which we are the adequate cause; that is (by the preceding Definition), when from our nature there follows, in us or outside us, something that can be clearly and distinctly understood through our nature alone. Conversely, I say that we are acted upon when something takes place in us, or when something follows from our nature, of which we are only a partial cause.","kind":"passage","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_QGOH_PTDO:P0008"]},{"id":"RAW_QGOH_PTDO:P0009","text":"III. By Affects I understand the affections of the Body by which the Body's power of acting is increased or diminished, assisted or restrained, and at the same time the ideas of these affections.","kind":"passage","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_QGOH_PTDO:P0009"]},{"id":"RAW_QGOH_PTDO:P0010","text":"If, therefore, we can be the adequate cause of any one of these affections, then by Affect I understand an action; otherwise, a passion.","kind":"passage","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_QGOH_PTDO:P0010"]},{"id":"RAW_QGOH_PTDO:P0011","text":"POSTULATES","kind":"passage","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_QGOH_PTDO:P0011"]},{"id":"RAW_QGOH_PTDO:P0012","text":"I. The human Body can be affected in many ways by which its power of acting is increased or diminished, and also in other ways which render its power of acting neither greater nor less.","kind":"passage","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_QGOH_PTDO:P0012"]},{"id":"RAW_QGOH_PTDO:P0013","text":"This Postulate, or Axiom, rests upon Postulate 1 and Lemmas 5 and 7, which see after Proposition 13, Part II.","kind":"passage","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_QGOH_PTDO:P0013"]},{"id":"RAW_QGOH_PTDO:P0014","text":"II. The human Body can undergo many changes and nevertheless retain the impressions or traces of objects (concerning which see Post. 5, Part II), and consequently the same images of things (for the definition of which see Schol. Prop. 17, Part II).","kind":"passage","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_QGOH_PTDO:P0014"]},{"id":"RAW_QGOH_PTDO:P0015","text":"PROPOSITION I","kind":"passage","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_QGOH_PTDO:P0015"]},{"id":"RAW_QGOH_PTDO:P0016","text":"Our Mind acts in certain respects and suffers in certain respects; namely, insofar as it has adequate ideas, it necessarily acts in certain respects, and insofar as it has inadequate ideas, it necessarily suffers in certain respects.","kind":"passage","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_QGOH_PTDO:P0016"]},{"id":"RAW_QGOH_PTDO:P0017","text":"DEMONSTRATION","kind":"passage","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_QGOH_PTDO:P0017"]},{"id":"RAW_QGOH_PTDO:P0018","text":"Of the ideas of every human Mind, some are adequate, while others are mutilated and confused (by Schol. 2, Prop. 40, Part II). But the ideas which are adequate in someone's Mind are adequate in God insofar as He constitutes the essence of that Mind (by Coroll. Prop. 11, Part II); and those which are inadequate in the Mind are also adequate in God (by the same Corollary), not insofar as He contains only the essence of that Mind, but insofar as He also contains within Himself at the same time the Minds of other things. Further, from any given idea some effect must necessarily follow (by Prop. 36, Part I), of which effect God is the adequate cause (see Def. 1 of this Part), not insofar as He is infinite, but insofar as He is considered as affected by that given idea (see Prop. 9, Part II). But of that effect of which God is the cause insofar as He is affected by an idea which is adequate in someone's Mind, that same Mind is the adequate cause (by Coroll. Prop. 11, Part II).","kind":"passage","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_QGOH_PTDO:P0018"]},{"id":"RAW_QGOH_PTDO:P0019","text":"Therefore our Mind (by Def. 2 of this Part), insofar as it has adequate ideas, necessarily acts in certain respects; which was the first point. Further, whatever necessarily follows from an idea which is adequate in God, not insofar as He has in Himself only the Mind of one man, but insofar as He has at the same time the Minds of other things together with the Mind of that man, of that effect (by the same Coroll. Prop. 11, Part II) the Mind of that man is not the adequate cause, but a partial cause; and accordingly (by Def. 2 of this Part), insofar as the Mind has inadequate ideas, it necessarily suffers in certain respects; which was the second point. Therefore our Mind, etc. Q. E. D.","kind":"passage","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_QGOH_PTDO:P0019"]},{"id":"RAW_QGOH_PTDO:P0020","text":"COROLLARY","kind":"passage","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_QGOH_PTDO:P0020"]},{"id":"RAW_QGOH_PTDO:P0021","text":"From this it follows that the Mind is subject to more passions in proportion as it has more inadequate ideas, and conversely that it acts in more ways in proportion as it has more adequate ideas.","kind":"passage","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_QGOH_PTDO:P0021"]},{"id":"RAW_QGOH_PTDO:P0022","text":"PROPOSITION II","kind":"passage","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_QGOH_PTDO:P0022"]},{"id":"RAW_QGOH_PTDO:P0023","text":"Neither can the Body determine the Mind to think, nor can the Mind determine the Body to motion, nor to rest, nor to anything else (if there is anything else).","kind":"passage","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_QGOH_PTDO:P0023"]},{"id":"RAW_QGOH_PTDO:P0024","text":"DEMONSTRATION","kind":"passage","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_QGOH_PTDO:P0024"]},{"id":"RAW_QGOH_PTDO:P0025","text":"All modes of thinking have God, insofar as he is a thinking thing, and not insofar as he is explained by another attribute, as their cause (by Prop. 6, p. II); therefore that which determines the Mind to think is a mode of Thinking, and not of Extension; that is (by Def. 1, p. II), it is not a Body; which was the first point. Next, the motion and rest of a Body must arise from another body, which was also determined to motion or rest by another; and absolutely whatever arises in a body must have arisen from God insofar as he is considered affected by some mode of Extension, and not insofar as by some mode of Thinking (by the same Prop. 6, p. II); that is, it cannot arise from the Mind, which (by Prop. 11, p. II) is a mode of thinking; which was the second point. Therefore neither does the Body determine the Mind, etc. Q. E. D.","kind":"passage","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_QGOH_PTDO:P0025"]},{"id":"RAW_QGOH_PTDO:P0026","text":"SCHOLIUM","kind":"passage","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_QGOH_PTDO:P0026"]},{"id":"RAW_QGOH_PTDO:P0027","text":"These matters are understood more clearly from what was said in the Scholium to Proposition 7 of Part II, namely, that Mind and Body are one and the same thing, which is now conceived under the attribute of Thought, now under that of Extension. Hence it comes about that the order, or concatenation, of things is one, whether Nature be conceived under this or under that attribute; consequently, that the order of the actions and passions of our Body is simultaneous in nature with the order of the actions and passions of the Mind; which is also evident from the manner in which we demonstrated Proposition 12 of Part II. But although these matters stand thus, so that no ground for doubting remains, I nevertheless scarcely believe that, unless I confirm the matter by experience, men can be induced to weigh these things with an even mind; so firmly are they persuaded that the Body now moves, now is at rest, and does very many things, solely at the nod of the Mind, things which depend upon the will of the Mind alone and upon the art of devising.","kind":"passage","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_QGOH_PTDO:P0027"]},{"id":"RAW_QGOH_PTDO:P0028","text":"For what the Body can do, no one has hitherto determined; that is, experience has hitherto taught no one what the Body, from the laws of Nature alone, insofar as it is considered only as corporeal, can do, and what it cannot do unless it is determined by the Mind. For no one has hitherto known the fabric of the Body so accurately that he could explain all its functions, not to mention that in Brutes many things are observed which far surpass human sagacity, and that sleepwalkers do very many things in sleep which they would not dare while awake; which sufficiently shows that the Body itself, from the laws of its own nature alone, can do many things at which its Mind wonders. Further, no one knows by what method or by what means the Mind moves the Body, nor how many degrees of motion it can impart to the Body, nor with what speed it can move the same.","kind":"passage","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_QGOH_PTDO:P0028"]},{"id":"RAW_QGOH_PTDO:P0029","text":"Whence it follows that, when men say that this or that action of the Body arises from the Mind, which has command over the Body, they do not know what they are saying, and do nothing other than confess in specious words that they are ignorant, without wonder, of the true cause of that action. But they will say that, whether they know or do not know by what means the Mind moves the Body, they nevertheless experience that, unless the human Mind were fit for devising, the Body would be inert. Further, they experience that it is in the power of the Mind alone both to speak and to be silent, and many other things, which they therefore believe depend on the Mind's decree. But, as to the first point, I ask them whether experience does not also teach that, if on the contrary the Body is inert, the Mind is at the same time unfit for thinking? For when the Body rests in sleep, the Mind at the same time remains asleep with it, and has no power, as it has when awake, of devising.","kind":"passage","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_QGOH_PTDO:P0029"]},{"id":"RAW_QGOH_PTDO:P0030","text":"Further, I believe that all have experienced that the Mind is not always equally fit for thinking about the same subject, but that, as the Body is more fit for the image of this or that object to be aroused in it, so the Mind is more fit for contemplating this or that object. But they will say that from the laws of Nature alone, insofar as it is considered only as corporeal, it cannot come about that the causes of buildings, paintings, and things of this kind, which are made by human art alone, can be deduced, nor that the human Body, unless it were determined and directed by the Mind, could be capable of building some temple. But I have already shown that they do not know what the Body can do, or what can be deduced from the contemplation of its nature alone, and that they experience very many things to be done from the laws of Nature alone which they would never have believed could be done unless under the direction of the Mind; such as those things which sleepwalkers do in sleep and at which they themselves, while awake, wonder.","kind":"passage","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_QGOH_PTDO:P0030"]},{"id":"RAW_QGOH_PTDO:P0031","text":"Here I add the very fabric of the human Body, which in workmanship far surpasses all things fabricated by human art, not to mention that I have shown above that infinitely many things follow from Nature considered under any attribute whatever. Further, as to the second point, surely human affairs would stand much more happily if it were equally in man's power both to be silent and to speak. But experience teaches more than sufficiently that men have nothing less in their power than their tongue, and can do nothing less than moderate their appetites; whence it has come about that most believe that we act freely only in those matters which we seek lightly, because the appetite for those things can easily be restrained by the memory of another thing which we frequently recall, but by no means in those matters which we seek with great affect and which cannot be allayed by the memory of another thing. But in truth, unless they had experienced that we do many things of which we afterwards repent, and that we often, when we","kind":"passage","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_QGOH_PTDO:P0031"]},{"id":"RAW_QGOH_PTDO:P0032","text":"are conflicted by contrary affects, see the better things and follow the worse, nothing would prevent them from believing that we do all things freely. Thus the infant believes that it freely desires milk; the angry boy, that he wishes vengeance; and the timid man, flight. Next, the drunkard believes that by a free decree of the Mind he speaks those things which afterwards, when sober, he would have wished to have kept silent; thus the delirious man, the chatterbox, the boy, and very many of this sort believe that they speak by a free decree of the Mind, although they cannot restrain the impulse to speak which they have; so that experience itself teaches no less clearly than Reason that men believe themselves to be free for no other cause than because they are conscious of their actions and ignorant of the causes by which they are determined; and further, that the decrees of the Mind are nothing but the appetites themselves, which therefore vary according to the varying disposition of the Body.","kind":"passage","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_QGOH_PTDO:P0032"]},{"id":"RAW_QGOH_PTDO:P0033","text":"For each man regulates all things according to his own affect, and those who are moreover conflicted by contrary affects do not know what they want; while those who are subject to no affect are driven hither and thither by the slightest impulse. All these things certainly show clearly that both the decree of the Mind and the appetite and the determination of the Body are simultaneous in nature, or rather are one and the same thing, which, when it is considered under the attribute of Thought and explained through it, we call Decree, and when it is considered under the attribute of Extension and deduced from the laws of motion and rest, we call Determination; as will appear still more clearly from what is now to be said. For there is another point which I should especially wish to be noted here, namely, that we can do nothing by a decree of the Mind unless we remember it. For example, we cannot speak a word unless we remember it. Further, it is not in the free power of the Mind to remember or to forget any thing.","kind":"passage","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_QGOH_PTDO:P0033"]},{"id":"RAW_QGOH_PTDO:P0034","text":"Wherefore it is believed that this alone is in the power of the Mind: that, by the Mind's decree alone, we can either be silent or speak of a thing which we remember. But when we dream that we are speaking, we believe that we speak by a free decree of the Mind, and yet we do not speak; or, if we do speak, it happens by the spontaneous motion of the Body. Next, we dream that we conceal certain things from men, and that by the same decree of the Mind by which, while awake, we keep silent about those things which we know. Finally, we dream that by a decree of the Mind we do certain things which, while awake, we do not dare. And therefore I should very much like to know whether there are in the Mind two kinds of decrees, one Fantastical and the other Free? If it does not please one to rage so far, one must necessarily concede that this decree of the Mind, which is believed to be free, is not distinguished from imagination itself or memory, and is nothing other than that affirmation which the idea, insofar as it is an idea, necessarily involves (see Pr. 49, p. II).","kind":"passage","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_QGOH_PTDO:P0034"]},{"id":"RAW_QGOH_PTDO:P0035","text":"And therefore these decrees of the Mind arise in the Mind with the same necessity as the ideas of things actually existing. Those, therefore, who believe that by a free decree of the Mind they speak, or are silent, or do anything whatever, dream with their eyes open.","kind":"passage","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_QGOH_PTDO:P0035"]},{"id":"RAW_QGOH_PTDO:P0036","text":"PROPOSITION III","kind":"passage","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_QGOH_PTDO:P0036"]},{"id":"RAW_QGOH_PTDO:P0037","text":"The actions of the Mind arise from adequate ideas alone; but the passions depend upon inadequate ideas alone.","kind":"passage","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_QGOH_PTDO:P0037"]},{"id":"RAW_QGOH_PTDO:P0038","text":"DEMONSTRATION","kind":"passage","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_QGOH_PTDO:P0038"]},{"id":"RAW_QGOH_PTDO:P0039","text":"First, that which constitutes the essence of the Mind is nothing other than the idea of a Body actually existing (by Props. 11 and 13, p. II), which (by Prop. 15, p. II) is composed of many others, of which some (by the Coroll. to Prop. 38, p. II) are adequate, while others are inadequate (by the Coroll. to Prop. 29, p. II). Therefore whatever follows from the nature of the Mind, and of which the Mind is the proximate cause, through which it must be understood, must necessarily follow from an adequate or inadequate idea. But insofar as the Mind (by Prop. 1 of this Part) has inadequate ideas, it necessarily suffers; therefore the actions of the Mind follow from adequate ideas alone, and the Mind therefore suffers only because it has inadequate ideas. Q. E. D.","kind":"passage","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_QGOH_PTDO:P0039"]},{"id":"RAW_QGOH_PTDO:P0040","text":"SCHOLIUM","kind":"passage","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_QGOH_PTDO:P0040"]},{"id":"RAW_QGOH_PTDO:P0041","text":"We see, therefore, that passions are referred to the Mind only insofar as it has something that involves negation, or insofar as it is considered as a part of Nature which cannot be clearly and distinctly perceived through itself without other things; and in this manner I could show that passions are referred in the same way to singular things as to the Mind, and cannot be perceived in any other manner; but my purpose is to treat of the human Mind alone.","kind":"passage","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_QGOH_PTDO:P0041"]},{"id":"RAW_QGOH_PTDO:P0042","text":"PROPOSITION IV","kind":"passage","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_QGOH_PTDO:P0042"]},{"id":"RAW_QGOH_PTDO:P0043","text":"No thing can be destroyed except by an external cause.","kind":"passage","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_QGOH_PTDO:P0043"]},{"id":"RAW_QGOH_PTDO:P0044","text":"DEMONSTRATION","kind":"passage","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_QGOH_PTDO:P0044"]},{"id":"RAW_QGOH_PTDO:P0045","text":"This Proposition is self-evident; for the definition of any thing affirms the essence of the thing itself, but does not negate it; or, it posits the essence of the thing, but does not take it away. Therefore, while we attend only to the thing itself and not to external causes, we shall be able to find nothing in it which can destroy it. Q. E. D..","kind":"passage","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_QGOH_PTDO:P0045"]},{"id":"RAW_QGOH_PTDO:P0046","text":"PROPOSITION V","kind":"passage","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_QGOH_PTDO:P0046"]},{"id":"RAW_QGOH_PTDO:P0047","text":"Things are contrary in nature insofar as, that is, insofar as they cannot be in the same subject, one can destroy the other.","kind":"passage","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_QGOH_PTDO:P0047"]},{"id":"RAW_QGOH_PTDO:P0048","text":"DEMONSTRATION","kind":"passage","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_QGOH_PTDO:P0048"]},{"id":"RAW_QGOH_PTDO:P0049","text":"For if they could agree with one another, or be together at the same time in the same subject, then something could be given in the same subject which could destroy that very thing, which (by the preceding Prop.) is absurd. Therefore things, etc. Q. E. D.","kind":"passage","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_QGOH_PTDO:P0049"]},{"id":"RAW_QGOH_PTDO:P0050","text":"PROPOSITION VI","kind":"passage","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_QGOH_PTDO:P0050"]},{"id":"RAW_QGOH_PTDO:P0051","text":"Each thing, insofar as it is in itself, strives to persevere in its being.","kind":"passage","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_QGOH_PTDO:P0051"]},{"id":"RAW_QGOH_PTDO:P0052","text":"DEMONSTRATION","kind":"passage","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_QGOH_PTDO:P0052"]},{"id":"RAW_QGOH_PTDO:P0053","text":"For singular things are modes by which God's attributes are expressed in a certain and determinate manner (by Coroll. Prop. 25 p. I); that is (by Prop. 34 p. I), things which express, in a certain and determinate manner, God's power by which God is and acts; and no thing has anything in itself by which it can be destroyed, or which can remove its existence (by Prop. 4 of this Part); but, on the contrary, it is opposed to everything which can remove the existence of the same thing (by the preceding Prop.); and therefore, insofar as it can and insofar as it is in itself, it strives to persevere in its being. Q. E. D.","kind":"passage","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_QGOH_PTDO:P0053"]},{"id":"RAW_QGOH_PTDO:P0054","text":"PROPOSITION VII","kind":"passage","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_QGOH_PTDO:P0054"]},{"id":"RAW_QGOH_PTDO:P0055","text":"The striving by which each thing strives to persevere in its being is nothing but the actual essence of the thing itself.","kind":"passage","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_QGOH_PTDO:P0055"]},{"id":"RAW_QGOH_PTDO:P0056","text":"DEMONSTRATION","kind":"passage","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_QGOH_PTDO:P0056"]},{"id":"RAW_QGOH_PTDO:P0057","text":"From the given essence of any thing certain things necessarily follow (by Prop. 36 p. I), nor can things do anything other than that which necessarily follows from their determinate nature (by Prop. 29 p. I); wherefore the power or striving of any thing, by which it, either alone or together with others, does or strives to do anything—that is (by Prop. 6 of this Part), the power or striving by which it strives to persevere in its being—is nothing but the given, or actual, essence of the thing itself. Q. E. D.","kind":"passage","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_QGOH_PTDO:P0057"]},{"id":"RAW_QGOH_PTDO:P0058","text":"PROPOSITION VIII","kind":"passage","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_QGOH_PTDO:P0058"]},{"id":"RAW_QGOH_PTDO:P0059","text":"The striving by which each thing strives to persevere in its being involves no finite time, but an indefinite one.","kind":"passage","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_QGOH_PTDO:P0059"]},{"id":"RAW_QGOH_PTDO:P0060","text":"DEMONSTRATION","kind":"passage","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_QGOH_PTDO:P0060"]},{"id":"RAW_QGOH_PTDO:P0061","text":"For if it involved a limited time which determined the duration of the thing, then from the power alone by which the thing exists it would follow that, after that limited time, the thing could not exist, but would have to be destroyed; but this (by Prop. 4 of this Part) is absurd. Therefore the striving by which the thing exists involves no definite time; but, on the contrary, since (by the same Prop. 4 of this Part), if it is not destroyed by any external cause, it will continue always to exist by the same power by which it now exists, therefore this striving involves an indefinite time. Q. E. D.","kind":"passage","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_QGOH_PTDO:P0061"]},{"id":"RAW_QGOH_PTDO:P0062","text":"PROPOSITION IX","kind":"passage","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_QGOH_PTDO:P0062"]},{"id":"RAW_QGOH_PTDO:P0063","text":"The Mind, both insofar as it has clear and distinct ideas and insofar as it has confused ideas, strives to persevere in its being for an indefinite duration, and is conscious of this its striving.","kind":"passage","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_QGOH_PTDO:P0063"]},{"id":"RAW_QGOH_PTDO:P0064","text":"DEMONSTRATION","kind":"passage","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_QGOH_PTDO:P0064"]},{"id":"RAW_QGOH_PTDO:P0065","text":"The essence of the Mind is constituted from adequate and inadequate ideas (as we showed in Prop. 3 of this Part); and therefore (by Prop. 7 of this Part), both insofar as it has the former and insofar as it has the latter, it strives to persevere in its being; and this (by Prop. 8 of this Part) for an indefinite duration. But since the Mind (by Prop. 23 p. II) is necessarily conscious of itself through the ideas of the affections of the Body, therefore (by Prop. 7 of this Part) the Mind is conscious of its striving. Q. E. D.","kind":"passage","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_QGOH_PTDO:P0065"]},{"id":"RAW_QGOH_PTDO:P0066","text":"SCHOLIUM","kind":"passage","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_QGOH_PTDO:P0066"]},{"id":"RAW_QGOH_PTDO:P0067","text":"This striving, when it is referred to the Mind alone, is called Will; but when it is referred to the Mind and Body together, it is called Appetite; which is therefore nothing other than the very essence of man, from whose nature those things which serve his preservation necessarily follow; and consequently man is determined to act upon them. Further, there is no difference between Appetite and Desire, except that Desire is for the most part referred to men insofar as they are conscious of their appetite, and therefore can be defined thus, namely, Desire is appetite together with consciousness of the same. It is therefore evident from all this that we neither strive for, nor will, nor seek, nor desire anything because we judge it to be good; but, on the contrary, we judge something to be good because we strive for, will, seek, and desire it.","kind":"passage","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_QGOH_PTDO:P0067"]},{"id":"RAW_QGOH_PTDO:P0068","text":"PROPOSITION X","kind":"passage","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_QGOH_PTDO:P0068"]},{"id":"RAW_QGOH_PTDO:P0069","text":"An idea which excludes the existence of our Body cannot be given in our Mind, but is contrary to it.","kind":"passage","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_QGOH_PTDO:P0069"]},{"id":"RAW_QGOH_PTDO:P0070","text":"DEMONSTRATION","kind":"passage","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_QGOH_PTDO:P0070"]},{"id":"RAW_QGOH_PTDO:P0071","text":"Whatever can destroy our Body cannot be given in it (by Prop. 5 of this Part), and consequently neither can the idea of that thing be given in God insofar as he has the idea of our Body (by Coroll. Prop. 9 p. I); that is (by Prop. 11 and 13 p. II), the idea of that thing cannot be given in our Mind. But, on the contrary, since (by Prop. 11 and 13 p. II) the first thing which constitutes the essence of the Mind is the idea of a body actually existing, the first and principal striving of our Mind is (by Prop. 7 of this Part) to affirm the existence of our Body; and therefore the idea which denies the existence of our Body is contrary to our Mind. Q. E. D.","kind":"passage","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_QGOH_PTDO:P0071"]},{"id":"RAW_QGOH_PTDO:P0072","text":"PROPOSITION XI","kind":"passage","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_QGOH_PTDO:P0072"]},{"id":"RAW_QGOH_PTDO:P0073","text":"Whatever increases or diminishes, aids or restrains our Body’s power of acting, the idea of the same thing increases or diminishes, aids or restrains our Mind’s power of thinking.","kind":"passage","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_QGOH_PTDO:P0073"]},{"id":"RAW_QGOH_PTDO:P0074","text":"DEMONSTRATION","kind":"passage","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_QGOH_PTDO:P0074"]},{"id":"RAW_QGOH_PTDO:P0075","text":"This Proposition is evident from Proposition 7 of Part II, or also from Proposition 14 of Part II.","kind":"passage","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_QGOH_PTDO:P0075"]},{"id":"RAW_QGOH_PTDO:P0076","text":"SCHOLIUM","kind":"passage","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_QGOH_PTDO:P0076"]},{"id":"RAW_QGOH_PTDO:P0077","text":"We have seen, therefore, that the Mind can undergo great changes, and now pass to a greater, now to a lesser perfection; and these passions indeed explain to us the affects of Joy and Sadness. By Joy, therefore, in what follows I shall understand the passion by which the Mind passes to a greater perfection. By Sadness, however, the passion by which it passes to a lesser perfection. Moreover, the affect of Joy, referred at once to Mind and Body, I call Titillation or Cheerfulness; that of Sadness, however, Pain or Melancholy. But it should be noted that Titillation and Pain are referred to a man when one part of him is affected more than the rest; Cheerfulness and Melancholy, however, when all are affected equally. What Desire is, moreover, I explained in the Scholium of Proposition 9 of this Part; and besides these three I acknowledge no other primary affect; I shall show in what follows that the rest arise from these three.","kind":"passage","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_QGOH_PTDO:P0077"]},{"id":"RAW_QGOH_PTDO:P0078","text":"But before I proceed further, I wish here to explain Proposition 10 of this Part more fully, so that it may be understood more clearly in what manner an idea is contrary to an idea.","kind":"passage","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_QGOH_PTDO:P0078"]},{"id":"RAW_QGOH_PTDO:P0079","text":"In the Scholium of Proposition 17 of Part II, we showed that the idea which constitutes the essence of the Mind involves the existence of the Body so long as the Body itself exists. Next, from those things which we showed in the Corollary of Proposition 8 of Part II and in its Scholium, it follows that the present existence of our Mind depends on this alone, namely, that the Mind involves the actual existence of the Body. Finally, we showed that the power of the Mind, by which it imagines things and remembers them, also depends on this (see Propositions 17 and 18, Part II, with its Scholium), namely, that it involves the actual existence of the Body. From these things it follows that the Mind’s present existence and its power of imagining are taken away as soon as the Mind ceases to affirm the Body’s present existence. But the cause why the Mind ceases to affirm this existence of the Body cannot be the Mind itself (by Proposition 4 of this Part), nor can it be because the Body ceases to exist. For (by Proposition 6, Part","kind":"passage","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_QGOH_PTDO:P0079"]},{"id":"RAW_QGOH_PTDO:P0080","text":"II) the cause why the Mind affirms the Body’s existence is not that the Body began to exist; therefore, for the same reason, it does not cease to affirm the Body’s existence because the Body ceases to exist; but (by Proposition 17, Part II) this arises from another idea, which excludes the present existence of our Body, and consequently of our Mind, and which is thus contrary to the idea that constitutes the essence of our Mind.","kind":"passage","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_QGOH_PTDO:P0080"]},{"id":"RAW_QGOH_PTDO:P0081","text":"PROPOSITION XII","kind":"passage","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_QGOH_PTDO:P0081"]},{"id":"RAW_QGOH_PTDO:P0082","text":"The Mind, insofar as it can, strives to imagine those things which increase or aid the Body’s power of acting.","kind":"passage","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_QGOH_PTDO:P0082"]},{"id":"RAW_QGOH_PTDO:P0083","text":"DEMONSTRATION","kind":"passage","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_QGOH_PTDO:P0083"]},{"id":"RAW_QGOH_PTDO:P0084","text":"So long as the human Body is affected in a mode which involves the nature of some external body, the human Mind will contemplate that same body as present (by Proposition 17, Part II); and consequently (by Proposition 7, Part II), so long as the human Mind contemplates some external Body as present, that is (by the Scholium of the same Proposition 17), imagines it, so long the human Body is affected in a mode which involves the nature of that same external body; and thus, so long as the Mind imagines those things which increase or aid our Body’s power of acting, the Body is affected in modes which increase or aid its power of acting (see Postulate 1 of this Part); and consequently (by Proposition 11 of this Part) the Mind’s power of thinking is increased or aided; and therefore (by Proposition 6 or 9 of this Part) the Mind, insofar as it can, strives to imagine those same things. Q. E. D.","kind":"passage","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_QGOH_PTDO:P0084"]},{"id":"RAW_QGOH_PTDO:P0085","text":"PROPOSITION XIII","kind":"passage","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_QGOH_PTDO:P0085"]},{"id":"RAW_QGOH_PTDO:P0086","text":"When the Mind imagines those things which diminish or restrain the Body’s power of acting, it strives, insofar as it can, to remember things which exclude the existence of these.","kind":"passage","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_QGOH_PTDO:P0086"]},{"id":"RAW_QGOH_PTDO:P0087","text":"DEMONSTRATION","kind":"passage","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_QGOH_PTDO:P0087"]},{"id":"RAW_QGOH_PTDO:P0088","text":"So long as the Mind imagines anything of this kind, the power of the Mind and Body is diminished or restrained (as we demonstrated in the preceding Proposition); and nevertheless it will imagine it so long as the Mind does not imagine something else which excludes its present existence (by Proposition 17, Part II); that is (as we have just shown), the power of the Mind and Body is diminished or restrained so long as the Mind does not imagine something else which excludes its existence, and which consequently the Mind (by Proposition 9 of this Part), insofar as it can, will strive to imagine or remember. Q. E. D.","kind":"passage","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_QGOH_PTDO:P0088"]},{"id":"RAW_QGOH_PTDO:P0089","text":"COROLLARY","kind":"passage","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_QGOH_PTDO:P0089"]}],"source_digest":"e1a0df81abadade4749b9b364d9b71e7e2b2042bdfc1498dc1677a0d21dcc955","source_language_code":"la","original_title":"Pars tertia - De origine et natura affectuum · passage 1"}],"passage_basis":"frozen-grounding","original_title":"Pars tertia - De origine et natura affectuum · passage 1","title_language_code":"en"},{"id":"RAW_QGOH_PT_2","title":"Part Three - On the Origin and Nature of the Affects · passage 2","order":10,"chapter_id":"CH_QGOH_PT_2","text":"Hinc sequitur, quod Mens ea imaginari aversatur, quæ ipsius et Corporis potentiam minuunt vel coercent.\n\nSCHOLIUM\n\nEx his clare intelligimus, quid Amor, quidque Odium sit. Nempe Amor nihil aliud est, quam Lætitia concomitante idea causæ externæ ; et Odium nihil aliud, quam Tristitia concomitante idea causæ externæ. Videmus deinde, quod ille, qui amat, necessario conatur rem, quam amat, præsentem habere et conservare ; et contra, qui odit, rem, quam odio habet, amovere et destruere conatur. Sed de his omnibus in seqq. prolixius.\n\nPROPOSITIO XIV\n\nSi Mens duobus affectibus simul affecta semel fuit, ubi postea eorum alterutro afficietur, afficietur etiam altero.\n\nDEMONSTRATIO\n\nSi Corpus humanum a duobus corporibus simul affectum semel fuit, ubi Mens postea eorum alterutrum imaginatur, statim et alterius recordabitur (per Prop. 18 p. II). At Mentis imaginationes magis nostri Corporis affectus quam corporum externorum naturam indicant (per Coroll. 2 Prop. 16 p. II) : ergo si Corpus, et consequenter Mens (vid. Defin. 3 hujus), duobus affectibus semel affecta fuit, ubi postea eorum alterutro afficietur, afficietur etiam altero. Q. E. D.\n\nPROPOSITIO XV\n\nRes quæcunque potest esse per accidens causa Lætitiæ, Tristitiæ, vel Cupiditatis.\n\nDEMONSTRATIO\n\nPonatur Mens duobus affectibus simul affici, uno scilicet, qui ejus agendi potentiam neque auget neque minuit, et altero, qui eandem vel auget vel minuit (vide Post. 1 hujus). Ex præcedenti Propositione patet, quod ubi Mens postea illo a sua vera causa, quæ (per hypothesin) per se ejus cogitandi potentiam nec auget nec minuit, afficietur, statim et hoc altero, qui ipsius cogitandi potentiam auget vel minuit, hoc est (per Schol. Prop. 11 hujus) Lætitia vel Tristitia afficietur ; atque adeo illa res non per se, sed per accidens causa erit Lætitiae vel Tristitiæ. Atque hac eadem via facile ostendi potest, rem illam posse per accidens causam esse Cupiditatis. Q. E. D.\n\nCOROLLARIUM\n\nEx eo solo, quod rem aliquam affectu Lætitiæ vel Tristitiæ, cujus ipsa non est causa efficiens, contemplati sumus, eandem amare vel odio habere possumus.\n\nDEMONSTRATIO\n\nNam ex eo solo fit (per Prop. 14 hujus), ut Mens hanc rem postea imaginando, affectu Lætitiæ vel Tristitiæ afficiatur, hoc est(per Schol. Prop. 11 hujus), ut Mentis et Corporis potentia augeatur vel minuatur, etc. Et consequenter (per Prop. 12 hujus), ut Mens eandem imaginari cupiat vel (per Coroll. 13 hujus) aversetur ; hoc est (per Schol. Prop. 13 hujus), ut eandem amet vel odio habeat. Q. E. D.\n\nSCHOLIUM\n\nHinc intelligimus, qui fieri potest, ut quædam amemus vel odio habeamus absque ulla causa nobis cognita ; sed tantum ex Sympathia (ut ajunt) et Antipathia. Atque huc referenda etiam ea objecta, quæ nos Lætitia vel Tristitia afficiunt ex eo solo, quod aliquid simile habent objectis, quæ nos iisdem affectibus afficere solent, ut in seq. Prop. ostendam. Scio equidem Auctores, qui primi hæc nomina Sympathiæ et Antipathiæ introduxerunt, significare iisdem voluisse rerum occultas quasdam qualitates ; sed nihilominus credo nobis licere, per eadem notas vel manifestas etiam qualitates intelligere.\n\nPROPOSITIO XVI\n\nEx eo solo, quod rem aliquam aliquid habere imaginamur simile objecto, quod Mentem Lætitia vel Tristitia afficere solet ; quamvis id, in quo res objecto est similis, non sit horum affectuum efficiens causa ; eam tamen amabimus vel odio habebimus.\n\nDEMONSTRATIO\n\nId, quod simile est objecto in ipso objecta (per hypothesin) cum affectu Lætitiæ vel Tristitiæ contemplati sumus ; atque adeo (per Prop. 14 hujus), cum Mens ejus imagine afficietur, statim etiam hoc vel illo afficietur affectu, et consequenter res, quam hoc idem habere percipimus, erit (per Prop. 15 hujus) per accidens Lætitiæ vel Tristitiæ causa ; adeoque (per præced. Coroll.), quamvis id, in quo objecto est similis, non sit horum affectuum causa efficiens, eam tamen amabimus vel odio habebimus. Q. E. D.\n\nPROPOSITIO XVII\n\nSi rem, quæ nos Tristitiæ affectu afficere solet, aliquid habere imaginamur simile alteri, quæ nos æque magno Lætitiæ affectu solet afficere, eandem odio habebimus et simul amabimus.\n\nDEMONSTRATIO\n\nEst enim (per hypothesin) hæc res per se Tristitiæ causa, et (per Schol. Prop. 13 hujus), quatenus eandem hoc affectu imaginamur, eandem odio habemus : et quatenus præterea aliquid habere imaginamur simile alter, quæ nos æque magno Lætitiæ affectu afficere solet, æque magno Lætitiæ conamine amabimus (per Prop. præc.) ; atque adeo eandem odio habebimus et simul amabimus. Q. E. D.\n\nSCHOLIUM\n\nHæc Mentis constitutio, quæ scilicet ex duobus contrariis affectibus oritur, Animi vocatur Fluctuatio, quæ proinde affectum respicit, ut dubitatio imaginationem (vid. Schol. Prop. 44 p. II) ; nec Animi Fluctuatio et dubitatio inter se differunt, nisi secundum majus et minus. Sed notandum, me in Propositione præcedenti has animi fluctuationes ex causis deduxisse, quæ per se unius, et per accidens alterius affectus sunt causa ; quod ideo feci, quia sic facilius ex præcedentibus deduci poterant ; at non quod negem, animi fluctuationes plerumque oriri ab objecto, quod utriusque affectus sit efficiens causa. Nam Corpus humanum (per Post. 1 p. II) ex plurimis diversæ naturæ individuis componitur, atque adeo (per Ax. 1 post Lem. 3 quod vide post Prop. 13 p. II) ab uno eodemque corpore plurimis diversisque modis potest affici ; et contra, quia una eademque res multis modis potest affici, multis ergo etiam diversisque modis unam eandemque Corporis partem afficere poterit. Ex quibus facile concipere possumus, unum idemque objectum posse esse causam multorum contrariorumque affectuum.\n\nPROPOSITIO XVIII\n\nHomo ex imagine rei præteritæ aut futuræ eodem Lætitiæ et Tristitiæ affectu afficitur, ac ex imagine rei præsentis.\n\nDEMONSTRATIO\n\nQuamdiu homo rei alicujus imagine affectus est, rem ut præsentem, tametsi non existat, contemplabitur (per Prop. 17 p. II cum ejusdem Coroll.), nec ipsam ut præteritam aut futuram imaginatur nisi quatenus ejus imago juncta est imagini temporis præteriti aut futuri (vide Schol. Prop. 44 p. II). Quare rei imago, in se sola considerata, eadem est, sive ad tempus futurum vel præteritum, sive ad præsens referatur ; hoc est (per Coroll. 2 Prop. 16 p. II), Corporis constitutio seu affectus idem est, sive imago sit rei præteritæ vel futuræ, sive præsentis ; atque adeo affectus Lætitiæ et Tristitiæ idem est, sive imago sit rei præteritæ aut futuræ, sive præsentis. Q. E. D.\n\nSCHOLIUM I\n\nRem eatenus præteritam aut futuram hic voco, quatenus ab eadem affecti fuimus aut afficiemur. Ex. gr. quatenus ipsam vidimus aut videbimus, nos refecit, aut reficiet, nos læsit aut lædet, etc. Quatenus enim eandem sic imaginamur, eatenus ejus existentiam affirmamus ; hoc est, Corpus nullo affectu afficitur, qui rei existentiam secludat, atque adeo (per Prop. 17 p. II) Corpus ejusdem rei imagine eodem modo afficitur, ac si res ipsa præsens adesset. Verum enim vero, quia plerumque fit, ut ii, qui plura sunt experti, fluctuent, quamdiu rem ut futuram vel præteritam contemplantur, deque rei eventu ut plurimum dubitent (vid. Schol. Prop. 44 p. II) ; hinc fit, ut affectus, qui ex similibus rerum imaginibus oriuntur, non sint adeo constantes sed ut plerumque aliarum rerum imaginibus perturbentur, donec homines de rei eventu certiores fiant.\n\nSCHOLIUM II\n\nEx modo dictis intelligimus, quid sit Spes, Metus, Securitas, Desperatio, Gaudium, et Conscientiæ morsus. Spes namque nihil aliud est, quam inconstans Lætitia, orta ex imagine rei futuræ vel præteritæ, de cujus eventu dubitamus. Metus contra inconstans Tristitia, ex rei dubiæ imagine etiam orta. Porro si horum affectuum dubitatio tollatur ex Spe fit Securitas et ex Metu Desperatio ; nempe Lætitia, vel Tristitia, orta ex imagine rei, quam metuimus, vel speravimus. Gaudium deinde est Lætitia orta ex imagine rei præteritæ, de cujus eventu dubitavimus. Conscientiæ denique morsus est Tristitia, opposita Gaudio.\n\nPROPOSITIO XIX\n\nQui id, quod amat, destrui imaginatur, contristabitur ; si autem conservari, lætabitur.\n\nDEMONSTRATIO\n\nMens, quantum potest, ea imaginari conatur, quæ Corporis agendi potentiam augent vel juvant (per Prop. 12 hujus), hoc est (per Schol. Prop. 13 hujus) ea quæ amat. At imaginatio ab iis juvatur, quæ rei existentiam ponunt, et contra coercetur iis, quæ rei existentiam secludunt (per Prop. 17 p. II) ; ergo rerum imagines, quæ rei amatæ existentiam ponunt, Mentis conatum, quo rem amatam imaginari conatur, juvant, hoc est (per Schol. Prop. 11 hujus) Lætitia Mentem afficiunt ; et quæ contra rei amatæ existentiam secludunt, eundem Mentis conatum coercent, hoc est (per idem Schol.) Tristitia Mentem afficiunt. Qui itaque id, quod amat, destrui imaginatur, contristabitur, etc. Q. E. D.\n\nPROPOSITIO XX\n\nQui id, quod odio habet, destrui imaginatur, lætabitur.\n\nDEMONSTRATIO\n\nMens (per 13 Prop. hujus) ea imaginari conatur, quæ rerum existentiam, quibus Corporis agendi potentia minuitur vel coercetur, secludunt ; hoc est (per Schol. ejusdem Prop.) ea imaginari conatur, quæ rerum, quas odio habet, existentiam secludunt ; atque adeo rei imago, quæ existentiam ejus, quod Mens odio habet, secludit, hunc Mentis conatum juvat, hoc est (per Schol. Prop. 11 hujus) Mentem Lætitia afficit. Qui itaque id, quod odio habet, destrui imaginatur, lætabitur. Q. E. D.\n\nPROPOSITIO XXI\n\nQui id, quod amat, Lætitia vel Tristitia affectum imaginatur, Lætitia etiam vel Tristitia afficietur ; et uterque hic affectus major aut minor erit in amante, prout uterque major aut minor est in re amata.\n\nDEMONSTRATIO\n\nRerum imagines (ut in Prop. 19 hujus demonstravimus), quæ rei amatæ existentiam ponunt, Mentis conatum, quo ipsam rem amatam imaginari conatur, juvant. Sed Lætitia existentiam rei lætæ ponit, et eo magis, quo Lætitiæ affectus major est : est enim (per Schol. Prop. 11 hujus) transitio ad majorem perfectionem : ergo imago Lætitiæ rei amatæ in amante ipsius Mentis conatum juvat, hoc est (per Schol. Prop. 11 hujus) amantem Lætitia afficit, et eo majori, quo major hic affectus in re amata fuerit. Quod erat primum. Deinde quatenus res aliqua Tristitia (afficitur, eatenus destruitur, et eo magis, quo majori afficitur Tristitia per idem Schol. Prop. 11 hujus) ; adeoque (per Prop. 19 hujus) qui id quod amat, Tristitia affici imaginatur, Tristitia etiam afficietur, et eo majori, quo major hic affectus in re amata fuerit. Q. E. D.\n\nPROPOSITIO XXII\n\nSi aliquem imaginamur Laetitia afficere rem, quam amamus, Amore erga cum afficiemur. Si contra eundem imaginamur Tristitia eandem afficere contra Odio etiam contra ipsum afficiemur.\n\nDEMONSTRATIO\n\nQui rem, quam amamus, Lætitia vel Tristitia afficit, ille nos Lætitia vel Tristitia etiam afficit, si nimirum rem amatam Lætitia illa vel Tristitia affectam imaginamur (per præced. Prop.). At hæc Laetitia vel Tristitia in nobis supponitur dari concomitante idea causæ externæ ; ergo (per Schol. Prop. 13 hujus), si aliquem imaginamur Lætitia vel Tristitia afficere rem, quam amamus, erga eundem Amore vel Odio afficiemur. Q. E. D.\n\nSCHOLIUM\n\nPropositio 21 nobis explicat, quid sit Commiseratio, quam definire possumus, quod sit Tristitia orta ex alterius damno. Quo autem nomine appellanda sit Lætitia, quæ ex alterius bono oritur, nescio. Porro Amorem erga illum, qui alteri bene fecit, Favorem, et contra Odium erga illum, qui alteri male fecit, Indignationem appellabimus. Denique notandum, nos non tantum misereri rei, quam amavimus (ut in Prop. 21 ostendimus) : Sed etiam ejus, quam antea nullo affectu prosecuti sumus, modo eam nobis similem judicemus (et infra ostendam) ; Atque adeo ei etiam favere, qui simili bene fecit, et contra id eum indignari, qui simili damnum intulit.\n\nPROPOSITIO XXIII\n\nQui id, quod odio habet, Tristitia affectum imaginatur, lætabitur ; si contra idem Lætitia affectum esse imaginetur, contristabitur ; et uterque hic affectus major aut minor erit, prout ejus contrarius major aut minor est in eo, quod odio habet.\n\nDEMONSTRATIO\n\nQuatenus res odiosa Tristitia afficitur, eatenus destruitur, et eo magis, quo majori Tristitia afficitur (per Schol. Prop. 11 hujus). Qui igitur (per Prop. 20 hujus) rem, quam odio habet, Tristitia affici imaginatur, Lætitia contra afficietur ; et eo majori, quo majori Tristitia rem odiosam affectam esse imaginatur ; Quod erat primum. Deinde Lætitia existentiam rei lætæ ponit (per idem Schol. Prop. 11 hujus), et eo magis, quo major Lætitia concipitur. Si quis eum, quem odio habet, Lætitia affectum imaginatur, hæc imaginatio (per Prop. 13 hujus) ejusdem conatum coercebit ; hoc est (per Schol. Prop. 11 hujus), is, qui odio habet, Tristitia afficietur, etc. Q. E. D.\n\nSCHOLIUM\n\nHæc Lætitia vix solida et absque ullo animi conflictu esse potest. Nam (ut statim in Propositione 27 hujus ostendam) quatenus rem sibi similem Tristitiæ affectu affici imaginatur, eatenus contristari debet ; et contra, si eandem Lætitia affici imaginetur. Sed hic ad solum Odium attendimus.\n\nPROPOSITIO XXIV\n\nSi aliquem imaginamur Lætitia afficere rem, quam odio habemus, Odio etiam erga eum afficiemur. Si contra eundem imaginamur Tristitia eandem rem afficere, Amore ergo ipsum afficiemur.\n\nDEMONSTRATIO\n\nDemonstratur eodem modo hæc Propositio, ac Propositio 22 hujus, quam vide.\n\nSCHOLIUM\n\nHi et similes Odii affectus ad Invidiam referuntur, quæ propterea nihil aliud est, quam ipsum Odium, quatenus id consideratur hominem ita disponere, ut malo alterius gaudeat, et contra ut ejusdem bono contristetur.\n\nPROPOSITIO XXV\n\nId omne de nobis deque re amata affirmare conamur, quod nos, vel rem amatam, Lætitia afficere imaginamur ; et contra id omne negare, quod nos, vel rem amatam, Tristitia afficere imaginamur.\n\nDEMONSTRATIO\n\nQuod rem amatam Lætitia vel Tristitia afficere imaginamur, id nos Lætitia vel Tristitia afficit (per Prop. 21 hujus). At Mens (per Prop. 12 hujus) ea, quæ nos Lætitia afficiunt, quantum potest conatur imaginari, hoc est (per Prop. 17 p. II et ejus Coroll.) ut præsentia contemplari ; et contra (per Prop. 13 hujus), quæ nos Tristitia afficiunt, eorum existentiam secludere ; ergo id omne de nobis deque re amata affirmare conamur, quod nos, vel rem amatam, Lætitia afficere imaginamur, et contra. Q. E. D.\n\nPROPOSITIO XXVI\n\nId omne de re, quam odio habemus, affirmare conamur, quod ipsam Tristitia afficere imaginamur, et id contra negare quod ipsam Lætitia afficere imaginamur.\n\nDEMONSTRATIO\n\nSequitur hæc Propositio ex Propositione 23, ut præcedens ex Propositione 21 hujus.\n\nSCHOLIUM\n\nHis videmus, facile contingere, ut homo de se deque re amata plus justo, et contra de re, quam odit, minus justo sentiat ; quæ quidem imaginatio, quando ipsum hominem respicit, qui de se plus justo sentit, Superbia vocatur, et species Delirii est, quia homo oculis apertis somniat, se omnia illa posse, quæ sola imaginatione assequitur, quæque propterea veluti realia contemplatur, iisque exultat, quamdiu ea imaginari non potest, quæ horum existentiam secludunt, et ipsius agendi potentiam determinant. Est igitur Superbia Lætitia ex eo orta, quod homo de se plus justo sentit. Deinde Lætitia, quæ ex eo oritur, quod homo de alio plus justo sentit, Existimatio vocatur ; et illa denique Despectus, quæ ex eo oritur, quod de alio minus justo sentit.\n\nPROPOSITIO XXVII\n\nEx eo, quod rem nobis similem, et quam nullo affectu prosecuti sumus, aliquo affectu affici imaginamur, eo ipso simili affectu afficimur.\n\nDEMONSTRATIO\n\nRerum imagines sunt Corporis humani affectiones, quarum ideæ corpora externa veluti nobis præsentia repræsentant (per Schol. Prop. 17 p. II) ; hoc est (per Prop. 16 p. II) quarum ideæ naturam nostri Corporis, et simul præsentem externi corporis naturam involvunt. Si igitur corporis externi natura similis sit naturæ nostri Corporis, tum idea corporis externi, quod imaginamur, affectionem nostri Corporis involvet similem affectioni corporis externi ; et consequenter si aliquem nobis similem aliquo affectu affectum imaginamur, hæc imaginatio affectionem nostri Corporis huic affectui similem exprimet ; adeoque ex hoc, quod rem aliquam nobis similem aliquo affectu affici imaginamur simili cum ipsa affectu afficimur. Quod si rem nobis similem odio habeamus, eatenus (per Prop. 23 hujus) contrario affectu cum ipsa afficiemur, non autem simili. Q. E. D.\n\nSCHOLIUM\n\nHæc affectuum imitatio, quando ad Tristitiam refertur, vocatur Commiseratio (de qua vide Schol. Prop. 22 hujus) ; sed ad Cupiditatem relata Æmulatio, quæ proinde nihil aliud est, quam alicujus rei Cupiditas, quæ in nobis ingeneratur ex eo, quod alios nobis similes eandem Cupiditatem habere imaginamur.\n\nCOROLLARIUM I\n\nSi aliquem, quem nullo affectu prosecuti sumus, imaginamur Lætitia afficere rem nobis similem, Amore erga eundem afficiemur. Si contra eundem imaginamur eandem Tristitia afficere, contra Odio erga ipsum afficiemur.\n\nDEMONSTRATIO\n\nHoc eodem modo ex Propositione præcedente demonstratur, ac Propositio 22 hujus ex Propositione 21.\n\nCOROLLARIUM II\n\nRem cujus nos miseret, odio habere non possumus ex eo, quod ipsius miseria nos Tristitia afficit.\n\nDEMONSTRATIO\n\nSi enim ex eo nos eandem odio habere possemus, tum (per Prop. 23 hujus) ex ipsius Tristitia lætaremur, quod est contra hypothesin.\n\nCOROLLARIUM III\n\nRem, cujus nos miseret, a miseria quantum possumus liberare conabimur.\n\nDEMONSTRATIO\n\nId, quod rem, cujus nos miseret, Tristitia afficit, nos simili etiam Tristitia afficit (per Prop. præced.) ; adeoque omne id, quod ejus rei existentiam tollit, sive quod rem destruit, comminisci conabimur (per Prop. 13 hujus) hoc est (per Schol. Prop. 9 hujus), id destruere appetemus, sive ad id destruendum determinabimur ; atque adeo rem, cujus miseremur, a sua miseria liberare conabimur. Q. E. D.\n\nSCHOLIUM\n\nHæc voluntas sive appetitus benefaciendi, qui ex eo oritur, quod rei, in quam beneficium conferre volumus, nos miseret, Benevolentia vocatur quæ proinde nihil aliud est, quam Cupiditas ex commiseratione orta. Cæterum de Amore et Odio erga illum, qui rei, quam nobis similem esse imaginamur, bene aut male fecit, vide Schol. Prop. 22 hujus.\n\nPROPOSITIO XXVIII\n\nId omne, quod ad Lætitiam conducere imaginamur, conamur promovere ut fiat ; quod vero eidem repugnare, sive ad Tristitiam conducere imaginamur amovere vel destruere conamur.\n\nDEMONSTRATIO\n\nQuod ad Lætitiam conducere imaginamur, quantum possumus, imaginari conamur (per Prop. 12 hujus) ; hoc est (per Prop. 17 p. II), id quantum possumus conabimur ut præsens sive ut actu existens contemplari. Sed Mentis conatus seu potentia in cogitando æqualis, et simul, natura est cum Corporis conatu seu potentia in agendo (ut clare sequitur ex Coroll. Prop. 7 et Coroll. Prop. 11 p. II) : ergo, ut id existat, absolute conamur, sive (quod per Schol. Prop. 9 hujus idem est) appetimus et intendimus ; Quod erat primum. Deinde si id, quod Tristitiæ causam esse credimus, hoc est (per Schol. Prop. 13 hujus), si id, quod odio habemus, destrui imaginamur, lætabimur (per Prop. 20 hujus) ; adeoque idem (per primam hujus partem) conabimur destruere, sive (per Prop. 13 hujus) a nobis amovere, ne ipsum ut præsens contemplemur ; Quod erat secundum. Ergo id omne, quod ad Lætitiam, etc. Q. E. D.\n\nPROPOSITIO XXIX\n\nNos id omne etiam agere conabimur, quod homines cum Lætitia aspicere imaginamur, et contra id agere aversabimur, quod homines aversari imaginamur.\n\nDEMONSTRATIO\n\nEx eo, quod imaginamur homines aliquid amare vel odio habere, nos idem amabimus vel odio habebimus (per Prop. 27 hujus) ; hoc est (per Schol. Prop. 13 hujus), eo ipso ejus rei præsentia lætabimur vel contristabimur ; adeoque (per præc. Prop.) id omne, quod homines amare cum Lætitia aspicere imaginamur, conabimur agere, etc. Q. E. D.\n\nSCHOLIUM\n\nHic conatus aliquid agendi, et etiam omittendi, ea sola de causa, ut hominibus placeamus, vocatur Ambitio, præsertim quando adeo impense vulgo placere conamur, ut cum nostro aut alterius damno quædam agamus vel omittamus ; alias Humanitas appellari solet. Deinde Lætitiam, qua alterius actionem, qua nos conatus est delectari, imaginamur, Laudem voco ; Tristitiam vero, qua contra ejusdem actionem aversamur, Vituperium voco.\n\nPROPOSITIO XXX\n\nSi quis aliquid egit, quod reliquos Lætitia afficere imaginatur, is Lætitia, concomitante idea sui tanquam causa, afficietur ; sive se ipsum cum Lætitia contemplabitur. Si contra aliquid egit, quod reliquos Tristitia afficere imaginatur, se ipsum cum Tristitia contra contemplabitur.\n\nDEMONSTRATIO\n\nQui se reliquos Lætitia vel Tristitia afficere imaginatur, eo ipso (per Prop. 27 hujus) Lætitia vel Tristitia afficietur. Cum autem homo (per Prop. 19 et 23 p. II) sui sit conscius per affectiones, quibus ad agendum determinatur ; ergo qui aliquid egit, quod ipse imaginatur reliquos Lætitia afficere, Lætitia cum conscientia sui tanquam causa afficietur, sive, seipsum cum Lætitia contemplabitur, et contra. Q. E. D.\n\nSCHOLIUM\n\nCum Amor (per Schol. Prop. 13 hujus) sit Lætitia concomitante idea causæ externæ, et Odium Tristitia concomitante etiam idea causæ externæ ; erit ergo hæc Lætitia et Tristitia Amoris et Odii species. Sed quia Amor et Odium ad objecta externa referuntur, ideo hos Affectus aliis nominibus significabimus ; nempe Lætitiam concomitante idea causæ internæ Gloriam, et Tristitiam huic contrariam Pudorem appellabimus : Intellige, quando Lætitia vel Tristitia ex eo oritur, quod homo se laudari vel vituperari credit ; alias Lætitiam concomitante idea causæ internæ Acquiescentiam in se ipso, Tristitiam vero eidem contrariam Pœnitentiam vocabo. Deinde quia (per Coroll. 17 p. II) fieri potest, ut Lætitia, qua aliquis se reliquos afficere imaginatur, imaginaria tantum sit, et (per Prop. 25 hujus) unusquisque de se id omne conatur imaginari, quod se Lætitia afficere imaginatur ; facile ergo fieri potest, ut gloriosus superbus sit, et se omnibus gratum esse imaginetur, quando omnibus molestus est.\n\nPROPOSITIO XXXI\n\nSi aliquem imaginamur amare, vel cupere, vel odio habere aliquid, quod ipsi amamus, cupimus, vel odio habemus, eo ipso rem constantius amabimus, etc. Si autem id, quod amamus, cum aversari imaginamur, vel contratum animi fluctuationem patiemur.\n\nDEMONSTRATIO\n\nEx eo solo, quod aliquem aliquid amare imaginamur, eo ipso idem amabimus (per Prop. 27 hujus). At sine hoc nos idem amare supponimus ; accedit ergo Amori nova causa, a qua fovetur ; atque adeo id, quod amamus, hoc ipso constantius amabimus. Deinde ex eo, quod aliquem aliquid aversari imaginamur, idem aversabimur (per eandem Prop.). At si supponamus, nos eodem tempore id ipsum amare, eodem ergo tempore hoc idem amabimus et aversabimur, sive (vid. Schol. Prop. 17 hujus) animi fluctuationem patiemur. Q. E. D.\n\nCOROLLARIUM\n\nHinc et ex Prop. 28 hujus sequitur, unumquemque quantum potest conari, ut unusquisque id, quod ipse amat, amet, et quod ipse odit, odio etiam habeat ; unde illud Poetæ :\n\nSperemus pariter, pariter metuamus amantes ;\n\nFerreus est, si quis, quod sinit alter, amat.\n\nSCHOLIUM\n\nHic conatus efficiendi, ut unusquisque probet id, quod ipse amat vel odio habet, revera est Ambitio (vid. Schol. 29 hujus) ; atque adeo videmus unumquemque ex natura appetere, ut reliqui ex ipsius ingenio vivant ; quod dum omnes pariter appetunt, pariter sibi impedimento, et dum omnes ab omnibus laudari seu amari volunt, odio invicem sunt.\n\nPROPOSITIO XXXII\n\nSi aliquem re aliqua, qua unus solus potiri potest, gaudere imaginamur conabimur efficere, ne ille illa re potiatur.\n\nDEMONSTRATIO\n\nEx eo solo, quod aliquem re aliqua gaudere imaginamur (per Prop. 27 hujus cum ejusdem 1 Coroll.), rem illam amabimus eaque gaudere cupiemus. At (per hypothesin) huic Lætitiæ obstare imaginamur, quod ille eadem hac re gaudeat ergo (per Prop. 28 hujus), ne ille eadem potiatur, conabimur. Q. E. D.\n\nSCHOLIUM","paragraphs":[{"id":"RAW_QGOH_PT_2:P0001","text":"Hinc sequitur, quod Mens ea imaginari aversatur, quæ ipsius et Corporis potentiam minuunt vel coercent.","kind":"passage"},{"id":"RAW_QGOH_PT_2:P0002","text":"SCHOLIUM","kind":"passage"},{"id":"RAW_QGOH_PT_2:P0003","text":"Ex his clare intelligimus, quid Amor, quidque Odium sit. Nempe Amor nihil aliud est, quam Lætitia concomitante idea causæ externæ ; et Odium nihil aliud, quam Tristitia concomitante idea causæ externæ. Videmus deinde, quod ille, qui amat, necessario conatur rem, quam amat, præsentem habere et conservare ; et contra, qui odit, rem, quam odio habet, amovere et destruere conatur. Sed de his omnibus in seqq. prolixius.","kind":"passage"},{"id":"RAW_QGOH_PT_2:P0004","text":"PROPOSITIO XIV","kind":"passage"},{"id":"RAW_QGOH_PT_2:P0005","text":"Si Mens duobus affectibus simul affecta semel fuit, ubi postea eorum alterutro afficietur, afficietur etiam altero.","kind":"passage"},{"id":"RAW_QGOH_PT_2:P0006","text":"DEMONSTRATIO","kind":"passage"},{"id":"RAW_QGOH_PT_2:P0007","text":"Si Corpus humanum a duobus corporibus simul affectum semel fuit, ubi Mens postea eorum alterutrum imaginatur, statim et alterius recordabitur (per Prop. 18 p. II). At Mentis imaginationes magis nostri Corporis affectus quam corporum externorum naturam indicant (per Coroll. 2 Prop. 16 p. II) : ergo si Corpus, et consequenter Mens (vid. Defin. 3 hujus), duobus affectibus semel affecta fuit, ubi postea eorum alterutro afficietur, afficietur etiam altero. Q. E. D.","kind":"passage"},{"id":"RAW_QGOH_PT_2:P0008","text":"PROPOSITIO XV","kind":"passage"},{"id":"RAW_QGOH_PT_2:P0009","text":"Res quæcunque potest esse per accidens causa Lætitiæ, Tristitiæ, vel Cupiditatis.","kind":"passage"},{"id":"RAW_QGOH_PT_2:P0010","text":"DEMONSTRATIO","kind":"passage"},{"id":"RAW_QGOH_PT_2:P0011","text":"Ponatur Mens duobus affectibus simul affici, uno scilicet, qui ejus agendi potentiam neque auget neque minuit, et altero, qui eandem vel auget vel minuit (vide Post. 1 hujus). Ex præcedenti Propositione patet, quod ubi Mens postea illo a sua vera causa, quæ (per hypothesin) per se ejus cogitandi potentiam nec auget nec minuit, afficietur, statim et hoc altero, qui ipsius cogitandi potentiam auget vel minuit, hoc est (per Schol. Prop. 11 hujus) Lætitia vel Tristitia afficietur ; atque adeo illa res non per se, sed per accidens causa erit Lætitiae vel Tristitiæ. Atque hac eadem via facile ostendi potest, rem illam posse per accidens causam esse Cupiditatis. Q. E. D.","kind":"passage"},{"id":"RAW_QGOH_PT_2:P0012","text":"COROLLARIUM","kind":"passage"},{"id":"RAW_QGOH_PT_2:P0013","text":"Ex eo solo, quod rem aliquam affectu Lætitiæ vel Tristitiæ, cujus ipsa non est causa efficiens, contemplati sumus, eandem amare vel odio habere possumus.","kind":"passage"},{"id":"RAW_QGOH_PT_2:P0014","text":"DEMONSTRATIO","kind":"passage"},{"id":"RAW_QGOH_PT_2:P0015","text":"Nam ex eo solo fit (per Prop. 14 hujus), ut Mens hanc rem postea imaginando, affectu Lætitiæ vel Tristitiæ afficiatur, hoc est(per Schol. Prop. 11 hujus), ut Mentis et Corporis potentia augeatur vel minuatur, etc. Et consequenter (per Prop. 12 hujus), ut Mens eandem imaginari cupiat vel (per Coroll. 13 hujus) aversetur ; hoc est (per Schol. Prop. 13 hujus), ut eandem amet vel odio habeat. Q. E. D.","kind":"passage"},{"id":"RAW_QGOH_PT_2:P0016","text":"SCHOLIUM","kind":"passage"},{"id":"RAW_QGOH_PT_2:P0017","text":"Hinc intelligimus, qui fieri potest, ut quædam amemus vel odio habeamus absque ulla causa nobis cognita ; sed tantum ex Sympathia (ut ajunt) et Antipathia. Atque huc referenda etiam ea objecta, quæ nos Lætitia vel Tristitia afficiunt ex eo solo, quod aliquid simile habent objectis, quæ nos iisdem affectibus afficere solent, ut in seq. Prop. ostendam. Scio equidem Auctores, qui primi hæc nomina Sympathiæ et Antipathiæ introduxerunt, significare iisdem voluisse rerum occultas quasdam qualitates ; sed nihilominus credo nobis licere, per eadem notas vel manifestas etiam qualitates intelligere.","kind":"passage"},{"id":"RAW_QGOH_PT_2:P0018","text":"PROPOSITIO XVI","kind":"passage"},{"id":"RAW_QGOH_PT_2:P0019","text":"Ex eo solo, quod rem aliquam aliquid habere imaginamur simile objecto, quod Mentem Lætitia vel Tristitia afficere solet ; quamvis id, in quo res objecto est similis, non sit horum affectuum efficiens causa ; eam tamen amabimus vel odio habebimus.","kind":"passage"},{"id":"RAW_QGOH_PT_2:P0020","text":"DEMONSTRATIO","kind":"passage"},{"id":"RAW_QGOH_PT_2:P0021","text":"Id, quod simile est objecto in ipso objecta (per hypothesin) cum affectu Lætitiæ vel Tristitiæ contemplati sumus ; atque adeo (per Prop. 14 hujus), cum Mens ejus imagine afficietur, statim etiam hoc vel illo afficietur affectu, et consequenter res, quam hoc idem habere percipimus, erit (per Prop. 15 hujus) per accidens Lætitiæ vel Tristitiæ causa ; adeoque (per præced. Coroll.), quamvis id, in quo objecto est similis, non sit horum affectuum causa efficiens, eam tamen amabimus vel odio habebimus. Q. E. D.","kind":"passage"},{"id":"RAW_QGOH_PT_2:P0022","text":"PROPOSITIO XVII","kind":"passage"},{"id":"RAW_QGOH_PT_2:P0023","text":"Si rem, quæ nos Tristitiæ affectu afficere solet, aliquid habere imaginamur simile alteri, quæ nos æque magno Lætitiæ affectu solet afficere, eandem odio habebimus et simul amabimus.","kind":"passage"},{"id":"RAW_QGOH_PT_2:P0024","text":"DEMONSTRATIO","kind":"passage"},{"id":"RAW_QGOH_PT_2:P0025","text":"Est enim (per hypothesin) hæc res per se Tristitiæ causa, et (per Schol. Prop. 13 hujus), quatenus eandem hoc affectu imaginamur, eandem odio habemus : et quatenus præterea aliquid habere imaginamur simile alter, quæ nos æque magno Lætitiæ affectu afficere solet, æque magno Lætitiæ conamine amabimus (per Prop. præc.) ; atque adeo eandem odio habebimus et simul amabimus. Q. E. D.","kind":"passage"},{"id":"RAW_QGOH_PT_2:P0026","text":"SCHOLIUM","kind":"passage"},{"id":"RAW_QGOH_PT_2:P0027","text":"Hæc Mentis constitutio, quæ scilicet ex duobus contrariis affectibus oritur, Animi vocatur Fluctuatio, quæ proinde affectum respicit, ut dubitatio imaginationem (vid. Schol. Prop. 44 p. II) ; nec Animi Fluctuatio et dubitatio inter se differunt, nisi secundum majus et minus. Sed notandum, me in Propositione præcedenti has animi fluctuationes ex causis deduxisse, quæ per se unius, et per accidens alterius affectus sunt causa ; quod ideo feci, quia sic facilius ex præcedentibus deduci poterant ; at non quod negem, animi fluctuationes plerumque oriri ab objecto, quod utriusque affectus sit efficiens causa. Nam Corpus humanum (per Post. 1 p. II) ex plurimis diversæ naturæ individuis componitur, atque adeo (per Ax. 1 post Lem. 3 quod vide post Prop. 13 p.","kind":"passage"},{"id":"RAW_QGOH_PT_2:P0028","text":"II) ab uno eodemque corpore plurimis diversisque modis potest affici ; et contra, quia una eademque res multis modis potest affici, multis ergo etiam diversisque modis unam eandemque Corporis partem afficere poterit. Ex quibus facile concipere possumus, unum idemque objectum posse esse causam multorum contrariorumque affectuum.","kind":"passage"},{"id":"RAW_QGOH_PT_2:P0029","text":"PROPOSITIO XVIII","kind":"passage"},{"id":"RAW_QGOH_PT_2:P0030","text":"Homo ex imagine rei præteritæ aut futuræ eodem Lætitiæ et Tristitiæ affectu afficitur, ac ex imagine rei præsentis.","kind":"passage"},{"id":"RAW_QGOH_PT_2:P0031","text":"DEMONSTRATIO","kind":"passage"},{"id":"RAW_QGOH_PT_2:P0032","text":"Quamdiu homo rei alicujus imagine affectus est, rem ut præsentem, tametsi non existat, contemplabitur (per Prop. 17 p. II cum ejusdem Coroll.), nec ipsam ut præteritam aut futuram imaginatur nisi quatenus ejus imago juncta est imagini temporis præteriti aut futuri (vide Schol. Prop. 44 p. II). Quare rei imago, in se sola considerata, eadem est, sive ad tempus futurum vel præteritum, sive ad præsens referatur ; hoc est (per Coroll. 2 Prop. 16 p. II), Corporis constitutio seu affectus idem est, sive imago sit rei præteritæ vel futuræ, sive præsentis ; atque adeo affectus Lætitiæ et Tristitiæ idem est, sive imago sit rei præteritæ aut futuræ, sive præsentis. Q. E. D.","kind":"passage"},{"id":"RAW_QGOH_PT_2:P0033","text":"SCHOLIUM I","kind":"passage"},{"id":"RAW_QGOH_PT_2:P0034","text":"Rem eatenus præteritam aut futuram hic voco, quatenus ab eadem affecti fuimus aut afficiemur. Ex. gr. quatenus ipsam vidimus aut videbimus, nos refecit, aut reficiet, nos læsit aut lædet, etc. Quatenus enim eandem sic imaginamur, eatenus ejus existentiam affirmamus ; hoc est, Corpus nullo affectu afficitur, qui rei existentiam secludat, atque adeo (per Prop. 17 p. II) Corpus ejusdem rei imagine eodem modo afficitur, ac si res ipsa præsens adesset. Verum enim vero, quia plerumque fit, ut ii, qui plura sunt experti, fluctuent, quamdiu rem ut futuram vel præteritam contemplantur, deque rei eventu ut plurimum dubitent (vid. Schol. Prop. 44 p. II) ; hinc fit, ut affectus, qui ex similibus rerum imaginibus oriuntur, non sint adeo constantes sed ut plerumque aliarum rerum imaginibus perturbentur, donec homines de rei eventu certiores fiant.","kind":"passage"},{"id":"RAW_QGOH_PT_2:P0035","text":"SCHOLIUM II","kind":"passage"},{"id":"RAW_QGOH_PT_2:P0036","text":"Ex modo dictis intelligimus, quid sit Spes, Metus, Securitas, Desperatio, Gaudium, et Conscientiæ morsus. Spes namque nihil aliud est, quam inconstans Lætitia, orta ex imagine rei futuræ vel præteritæ, de cujus eventu dubitamus. Metus contra inconstans Tristitia, ex rei dubiæ imagine etiam orta. Porro si horum affectuum dubitatio tollatur ex Spe fit Securitas et ex Metu Desperatio ; nempe Lætitia, vel Tristitia, orta ex imagine rei, quam metuimus, vel speravimus. Gaudium deinde est Lætitia orta ex imagine rei præteritæ, de cujus eventu dubitavimus. Conscientiæ denique morsus est Tristitia, opposita Gaudio.","kind":"passage"},{"id":"RAW_QGOH_PT_2:P0037","text":"PROPOSITIO XIX","kind":"passage"},{"id":"RAW_QGOH_PT_2:P0038","text":"Qui id, quod amat, destrui imaginatur, contristabitur ; si autem conservari, lætabitur.","kind":"passage"},{"id":"RAW_QGOH_PT_2:P0039","text":"DEMONSTRATIO","kind":"passage"},{"id":"RAW_QGOH_PT_2:P0040","text":"Mens, quantum potest, ea imaginari conatur, quæ Corporis agendi potentiam augent vel juvant (per Prop. 12 hujus), hoc est (per Schol. Prop. 13 hujus) ea quæ amat. At imaginatio ab iis juvatur, quæ rei existentiam ponunt, et contra coercetur iis, quæ rei existentiam secludunt (per Prop. 17 p. II) ; ergo rerum imagines, quæ rei amatæ existentiam ponunt, Mentis conatum, quo rem amatam imaginari conatur, juvant, hoc est (per Schol. Prop. 11 hujus) Lætitia Mentem afficiunt ; et quæ contra rei amatæ existentiam secludunt, eundem Mentis conatum coercent, hoc est (per idem Schol.) Tristitia Mentem afficiunt. Qui itaque id, quod amat, destrui imaginatur, contristabitur, etc. Q. E. D.","kind":"passage"},{"id":"RAW_QGOH_PT_2:P0041","text":"PROPOSITIO XX","kind":"passage"},{"id":"RAW_QGOH_PT_2:P0042","text":"Qui id, quod odio habet, destrui imaginatur, lætabitur.","kind":"passage"},{"id":"RAW_QGOH_PT_2:P0043","text":"DEMONSTRATIO","kind":"passage"},{"id":"RAW_QGOH_PT_2:P0044","text":"Mens (per 13 Prop. hujus) ea imaginari conatur, quæ rerum existentiam, quibus Corporis agendi potentia minuitur vel coercetur, secludunt ; hoc est (per Schol. ejusdem Prop.) ea imaginari conatur, quæ rerum, quas odio habet, existentiam secludunt ; atque adeo rei imago, quæ existentiam ejus, quod Mens odio habet, secludit, hunc Mentis conatum juvat, hoc est (per Schol. Prop. 11 hujus) Mentem Lætitia afficit. Qui itaque id, quod odio habet, destrui imaginatur, lætabitur. Q. E. D.","kind":"passage"},{"id":"RAW_QGOH_PT_2:P0045","text":"PROPOSITIO XXI","kind":"passage"},{"id":"RAW_QGOH_PT_2:P0046","text":"Qui id, quod amat, Lætitia vel Tristitia affectum imaginatur, Lætitia etiam vel Tristitia afficietur ; et uterque hic affectus major aut minor erit in amante, prout uterque major aut minor est in re amata.","kind":"passage"},{"id":"RAW_QGOH_PT_2:P0047","text":"DEMONSTRATIO","kind":"passage"},{"id":"RAW_QGOH_PT_2:P0048","text":"Rerum imagines (ut in Prop. 19 hujus demonstravimus), quæ rei amatæ existentiam ponunt, Mentis conatum, quo ipsam rem amatam imaginari conatur, juvant. Sed Lætitia existentiam rei lætæ ponit, et eo magis, quo Lætitiæ affectus major est : est enim (per Schol. Prop. 11 hujus) transitio ad majorem perfectionem : ergo imago Lætitiæ rei amatæ in amante ipsius Mentis conatum juvat, hoc est (per Schol. Prop. 11 hujus) amantem Lætitia afficit, et eo majori, quo major hic affectus in re amata fuerit. Quod erat primum. Deinde quatenus res aliqua Tristitia (afficitur, eatenus destruitur, et eo magis, quo majori afficitur Tristitia per idem Schol. Prop. 11 hujus) ; adeoque (per Prop. 19 hujus) qui id quod amat, Tristitia affici imaginatur, Tristitia etiam afficietur, et eo majori, quo major hic affectus in re amata fuerit. Q. E. D.","kind":"passage"},{"id":"RAW_QGOH_PT_2:P0049","text":"PROPOSITIO XXII","kind":"passage"},{"id":"RAW_QGOH_PT_2:P0050","text":"Si aliquem imaginamur Laetitia afficere rem, quam amamus, Amore erga cum afficiemur. Si contra eundem imaginamur Tristitia eandem afficere contra Odio etiam contra ipsum afficiemur.","kind":"passage"},{"id":"RAW_QGOH_PT_2:P0051","text":"DEMONSTRATIO","kind":"passage"},{"id":"RAW_QGOH_PT_2:P0052","text":"Qui rem, quam amamus, Lætitia vel Tristitia afficit, ille nos Lætitia vel Tristitia etiam afficit, si nimirum rem amatam Lætitia illa vel Tristitia affectam imaginamur (per præced. Prop.). At hæc Laetitia vel Tristitia in nobis supponitur dari concomitante idea causæ externæ ; ergo (per Schol. Prop. 13 hujus), si aliquem imaginamur Lætitia vel Tristitia afficere rem, quam amamus, erga eundem Amore vel Odio afficiemur. Q. E. D.","kind":"passage"},{"id":"RAW_QGOH_PT_2:P0053","text":"SCHOLIUM","kind":"passage"},{"id":"RAW_QGOH_PT_2:P0054","text":"Propositio 21 nobis explicat, quid sit Commiseratio, quam definire possumus, quod sit Tristitia orta ex alterius damno. Quo autem nomine appellanda sit Lætitia, quæ ex alterius bono oritur, nescio. Porro Amorem erga illum, qui alteri bene fecit, Favorem, et contra Odium erga illum, qui alteri male fecit, Indignationem appellabimus. Denique notandum, nos non tantum misereri rei, quam amavimus (ut in Prop. 21 ostendimus) : Sed etiam ejus, quam antea nullo affectu prosecuti sumus, modo eam nobis similem judicemus (et infra ostendam) ; Atque adeo ei etiam favere, qui simili bene fecit, et contra id eum indignari, qui simili damnum intulit.","kind":"passage"},{"id":"RAW_QGOH_PT_2:P0055","text":"PROPOSITIO XXIII","kind":"passage"},{"id":"RAW_QGOH_PT_2:P0056","text":"Qui id, quod odio habet, Tristitia affectum imaginatur, lætabitur ; si contra idem Lætitia affectum esse imaginetur, contristabitur ; et uterque hic affectus major aut minor erit, prout ejus contrarius major aut minor est in eo, quod odio habet.","kind":"passage"},{"id":"RAW_QGOH_PT_2:P0057","text":"DEMONSTRATIO","kind":"passage"},{"id":"RAW_QGOH_PT_2:P0058","text":"Quatenus res odiosa Tristitia afficitur, eatenus destruitur, et eo magis, quo majori Tristitia afficitur (per Schol. Prop. 11 hujus). Qui igitur (per Prop. 20 hujus) rem, quam odio habet, Tristitia affici imaginatur, Lætitia contra afficietur ; et eo majori, quo majori Tristitia rem odiosam affectam esse imaginatur ; Quod erat primum. Deinde Lætitia existentiam rei lætæ ponit (per idem Schol. Prop. 11 hujus), et eo magis, quo major Lætitia concipitur. Si quis eum, quem odio habet, Lætitia affectum imaginatur, hæc imaginatio (per Prop. 13 hujus) ejusdem conatum coercebit ; hoc est (per Schol. Prop. 11 hujus), is, qui odio habet, Tristitia afficietur, etc. Q. E. D.","kind":"passage"},{"id":"RAW_QGOH_PT_2:P0059","text":"SCHOLIUM","kind":"passage"},{"id":"RAW_QGOH_PT_2:P0060","text":"Hæc Lætitia vix solida et absque ullo animi conflictu esse potest. Nam (ut statim in Propositione 27 hujus ostendam) quatenus rem sibi similem Tristitiæ affectu affici imaginatur, eatenus contristari debet ; et contra, si eandem Lætitia affici imaginetur. Sed hic ad solum Odium attendimus.","kind":"passage"},{"id":"RAW_QGOH_PT_2:P0061","text":"PROPOSITIO XXIV","kind":"passage"},{"id":"RAW_QGOH_PT_2:P0062","text":"Si aliquem imaginamur Lætitia afficere rem, quam odio habemus, Odio etiam erga eum afficiemur. Si contra eundem imaginamur Tristitia eandem rem afficere, Amore ergo ipsum afficiemur.","kind":"passage"},{"id":"RAW_QGOH_PT_2:P0063","text":"DEMONSTRATIO","kind":"passage"},{"id":"RAW_QGOH_PT_2:P0064","text":"Demonstratur eodem modo hæc Propositio, ac Propositio 22 hujus, quam vide.","kind":"passage"},{"id":"RAW_QGOH_PT_2:P0065","text":"SCHOLIUM","kind":"passage"},{"id":"RAW_QGOH_PT_2:P0066","text":"Hi et similes Odii affectus ad Invidiam referuntur, quæ propterea nihil aliud est, quam ipsum Odium, quatenus id consideratur hominem ita disponere, ut malo alterius gaudeat, et contra ut ejusdem bono contristetur.","kind":"passage"},{"id":"RAW_QGOH_PT_2:P0067","text":"PROPOSITIO XXV","kind":"passage"},{"id":"RAW_QGOH_PT_2:P0068","text":"Id omne de nobis deque re amata affirmare conamur, quod nos, vel rem amatam, Lætitia afficere imaginamur ; et contra id omne negare, quod nos, vel rem amatam, Tristitia afficere imaginamur.","kind":"passage"},{"id":"RAW_QGOH_PT_2:P0069","text":"DEMONSTRATIO","kind":"passage"},{"id":"RAW_QGOH_PT_2:P0070","text":"Quod rem amatam Lætitia vel Tristitia afficere imaginamur, id nos Lætitia vel Tristitia afficit (per Prop. 21 hujus). At Mens (per Prop. 12 hujus) ea, quæ nos Lætitia afficiunt, quantum potest conatur imaginari, hoc est (per Prop. 17 p. II et ejus Coroll.) ut præsentia contemplari ; et contra (per Prop. 13 hujus), quæ nos Tristitia afficiunt, eorum existentiam secludere ; ergo id omne de nobis deque re amata affirmare conamur, quod nos, vel rem amatam, Lætitia afficere imaginamur, et contra. Q. E. D.","kind":"passage"},{"id":"RAW_QGOH_PT_2:P0071","text":"PROPOSITIO XXVI","kind":"passage"},{"id":"RAW_QGOH_PT_2:P0072","text":"Id omne de re, quam odio habemus, affirmare conamur, quod ipsam Tristitia afficere imaginamur, et id contra negare quod ipsam Lætitia afficere imaginamur.","kind":"passage"},{"id":"RAW_QGOH_PT_2:P0073","text":"DEMONSTRATIO","kind":"passage"},{"id":"RAW_QGOH_PT_2:P0074","text":"Sequitur hæc Propositio ex Propositione 23, ut præcedens ex Propositione 21 hujus.","kind":"passage"},{"id":"RAW_QGOH_PT_2:P0075","text":"SCHOLIUM","kind":"passage"},{"id":"RAW_QGOH_PT_2:P0076","text":"His videmus, facile contingere, ut homo de se deque re amata plus justo, et contra de re, quam odit, minus justo sentiat ; quæ quidem imaginatio, quando ipsum hominem respicit, qui de se plus justo sentit, Superbia vocatur, et species Delirii est, quia homo oculis apertis somniat, se omnia illa posse, quæ sola imaginatione assequitur, quæque propterea veluti realia contemplatur, iisque exultat, quamdiu ea imaginari non potest, quæ horum existentiam secludunt, et ipsius agendi potentiam determinant. Est igitur Superbia Lætitia ex eo orta, quod homo de se plus justo sentit. Deinde Lætitia, quæ ex eo oritur, quod homo de alio plus justo sentit, Existimatio vocatur ; et illa denique Despectus, quæ ex eo oritur, quod de alio minus justo sentit.","kind":"passage"},{"id":"RAW_QGOH_PT_2:P0077","text":"PROPOSITIO XXVII","kind":"passage"},{"id":"RAW_QGOH_PT_2:P0078","text":"Ex eo, quod rem nobis similem, et quam nullo affectu prosecuti sumus, aliquo affectu affici imaginamur, eo ipso simili affectu afficimur.","kind":"passage"},{"id":"RAW_QGOH_PT_2:P0079","text":"DEMONSTRATIO","kind":"passage"},{"id":"RAW_QGOH_PT_2:P0080","text":"Rerum imagines sunt Corporis humani affectiones, quarum ideæ corpora externa veluti nobis præsentia repræsentant (per Schol. Prop. 17 p. II) ; hoc est (per Prop. 16 p. II) quarum ideæ naturam nostri Corporis, et simul præsentem externi corporis naturam involvunt. Si igitur corporis externi natura similis sit naturæ nostri Corporis, tum idea corporis externi, quod imaginamur, affectionem nostri Corporis involvet similem affectioni corporis externi ; et consequenter si aliquem nobis similem aliquo affectu affectum imaginamur, hæc imaginatio affectionem nostri Corporis huic affectui similem exprimet ; adeoque ex hoc, quod rem aliquam nobis similem aliquo affectu affici imaginamur simili cum ipsa affectu afficimur. Quod si rem nobis similem odio habeamus, eatenus (per Prop. 23 hujus) contrario affectu cum ipsa afficiemur, non autem simili. Q. E. D.","kind":"passage"},{"id":"RAW_QGOH_PT_2:P0081","text":"SCHOLIUM","kind":"passage"},{"id":"RAW_QGOH_PT_2:P0082","text":"Hæc affectuum imitatio, quando ad Tristitiam refertur, vocatur Commiseratio (de qua vide Schol. Prop. 22 hujus) ; sed ad Cupiditatem relata Æmulatio, quæ proinde nihil aliud est, quam alicujus rei Cupiditas, quæ in nobis ingeneratur ex eo, quod alios nobis similes eandem Cupiditatem habere imaginamur.","kind":"passage"},{"id":"RAW_QGOH_PT_2:P0083","text":"COROLLARIUM I","kind":"passage"},{"id":"RAW_QGOH_PT_2:P0084","text":"Si aliquem, quem nullo affectu prosecuti sumus, imaginamur Lætitia afficere rem nobis similem, Amore erga eundem afficiemur. Si contra eundem imaginamur eandem Tristitia afficere, contra Odio erga ipsum afficiemur.","kind":"passage"},{"id":"RAW_QGOH_PT_2:P0085","text":"DEMONSTRATIO","kind":"passage"},{"id":"RAW_QGOH_PT_2:P0086","text":"Hoc eodem modo ex Propositione præcedente demonstratur, ac Propositio 22 hujus ex Propositione 21.","kind":"passage"},{"id":"RAW_QGOH_PT_2:P0087","text":"COROLLARIUM II","kind":"passage"},{"id":"RAW_QGOH_PT_2:P0088","text":"Rem cujus nos miseret, odio habere non possumus ex eo, quod ipsius miseria nos Tristitia afficit.","kind":"passage"},{"id":"RAW_QGOH_PT_2:P0089","text":"DEMONSTRATIO","kind":"passage"},{"id":"RAW_QGOH_PT_2:P0090","text":"Si enim ex eo nos eandem odio habere possemus, tum (per Prop. 23 hujus) ex ipsius Tristitia lætaremur, quod est contra hypothesin.","kind":"passage"},{"id":"RAW_QGOH_PT_2:P0091","text":"COROLLARIUM III","kind":"passage"},{"id":"RAW_QGOH_PT_2:P0092","text":"Rem, cujus nos miseret, a miseria quantum possumus liberare conabimur.","kind":"passage"},{"id":"RAW_QGOH_PT_2:P0093","text":"DEMONSTRATIO","kind":"passage"},{"id":"RAW_QGOH_PT_2:P0094","text":"Id, quod rem, cujus nos miseret, Tristitia afficit, nos simili etiam Tristitia afficit (per Prop. præced.) ; adeoque omne id, quod ejus rei existentiam tollit, sive quod rem destruit, comminisci conabimur (per Prop. 13 hujus) hoc est (per Schol. Prop. 9 hujus), id destruere appetemus, sive ad id destruendum determinabimur ; atque adeo rem, cujus miseremur, a sua miseria liberare conabimur. Q. E. D.","kind":"passage"},{"id":"RAW_QGOH_PT_2:P0095","text":"SCHOLIUM","kind":"passage"},{"id":"RAW_QGOH_PT_2:P0096","text":"Hæc voluntas sive appetitus benefaciendi, qui ex eo oritur, quod rei, in quam beneficium conferre volumus, nos miseret, Benevolentia vocatur quæ proinde nihil aliud est, quam Cupiditas ex commiseratione orta. Cæterum de Amore et Odio erga illum, qui rei, quam nobis similem esse imaginamur, bene aut male fecit, vide Schol. Prop. 22 hujus.","kind":"passage"},{"id":"RAW_QGOH_PT_2:P0097","text":"PROPOSITIO XXVIII","kind":"passage"},{"id":"RAW_QGOH_PT_2:P0098","text":"Id omne, quod ad Lætitiam conducere imaginamur, conamur promovere ut fiat ; quod vero eidem repugnare, sive ad Tristitiam conducere imaginamur amovere vel destruere conamur.","kind":"passage"},{"id":"RAW_QGOH_PT_2:P0099","text":"DEMONSTRATIO","kind":"passage"},{"id":"RAW_QGOH_PT_2:P0100","text":"Quod ad Lætitiam conducere imaginamur, quantum possumus, imaginari conamur (per Prop. 12 hujus) ; hoc est (per Prop. 17 p. II), id quantum possumus conabimur ut præsens sive ut actu existens contemplari. Sed Mentis conatus seu potentia in cogitando æqualis, et simul, natura est cum Corporis conatu seu potentia in agendo (ut clare sequitur ex Coroll. Prop. 7 et Coroll. Prop. 11 p. II) : ergo, ut id existat, absolute conamur, sive (quod per Schol. Prop. 9 hujus idem est) appetimus et intendimus ; Quod erat primum. Deinde si id, quod Tristitiæ causam esse credimus, hoc est (per Schol. Prop. 13 hujus), si id, quod odio habemus, destrui imaginamur, lætabimur (per Prop. 20 hujus) ; adeoque idem (per primam hujus partem) conabimur destruere, sive (per Prop. 13 hujus) a nobis amovere, ne ipsum ut præsens contemplemur ; Quod erat secundum. Ergo id omne, quod ad Lætitiam, etc. Q. E. D.","kind":"passage"},{"id":"RAW_QGOH_PT_2:P0101","text":"PROPOSITIO XXIX","kind":"passage"},{"id":"RAW_QGOH_PT_2:P0102","text":"Nos id omne etiam agere conabimur, quod homines cum Lætitia aspicere imaginamur, et contra id agere aversabimur, quod homines aversari imaginamur.","kind":"passage"},{"id":"RAW_QGOH_PT_2:P0103","text":"DEMONSTRATIO","kind":"passage"},{"id":"RAW_QGOH_PT_2:P0104","text":"Ex eo, quod imaginamur homines aliquid amare vel odio habere, nos idem amabimus vel odio habebimus (per Prop. 27 hujus) ; hoc est (per Schol. Prop. 13 hujus), eo ipso ejus rei præsentia lætabimur vel contristabimur ; adeoque (per præc. Prop.) id omne, quod homines amare cum Lætitia aspicere imaginamur, conabimur agere, etc. Q. E. D.","kind":"passage"},{"id":"RAW_QGOH_PT_2:P0105","text":"SCHOLIUM","kind":"passage"},{"id":"RAW_QGOH_PT_2:P0106","text":"Hic conatus aliquid agendi, et etiam omittendi, ea sola de causa, ut hominibus placeamus, vocatur Ambitio, præsertim quando adeo impense vulgo placere conamur, ut cum nostro aut alterius damno quædam agamus vel omittamus ; alias Humanitas appellari solet. Deinde Lætitiam, qua alterius actionem, qua nos conatus est delectari, imaginamur, Laudem voco ; Tristitiam vero, qua contra ejusdem actionem aversamur, Vituperium voco.","kind":"passage"},{"id":"RAW_QGOH_PT_2:P0107","text":"PROPOSITIO XXX","kind":"passage"},{"id":"RAW_QGOH_PT_2:P0108","text":"Si quis aliquid egit, quod reliquos Lætitia afficere imaginatur, is Lætitia, concomitante idea sui tanquam causa, afficietur ; sive se ipsum cum Lætitia contemplabitur. Si contra aliquid egit, quod reliquos Tristitia afficere imaginatur, se ipsum cum Tristitia contra contemplabitur.","kind":"passage"},{"id":"RAW_QGOH_PT_2:P0109","text":"DEMONSTRATIO","kind":"passage"},{"id":"RAW_QGOH_PT_2:P0110","text":"Qui se reliquos Lætitia vel Tristitia afficere imaginatur, eo ipso (per Prop. 27 hujus) Lætitia vel Tristitia afficietur. Cum autem homo (per Prop. 19 et 23 p. II) sui sit conscius per affectiones, quibus ad agendum determinatur ; ergo qui aliquid egit, quod ipse imaginatur reliquos Lætitia afficere, Lætitia cum conscientia sui tanquam causa afficietur, sive, seipsum cum Lætitia contemplabitur, et contra. Q. E. D.","kind":"passage"},{"id":"RAW_QGOH_PT_2:P0111","text":"SCHOLIUM","kind":"passage"},{"id":"RAW_QGOH_PT_2:P0112","text":"Cum Amor (per Schol. Prop. 13 hujus) sit Lætitia concomitante idea causæ externæ, et Odium Tristitia concomitante etiam idea causæ externæ ; erit ergo hæc Lætitia et Tristitia Amoris et Odii species. Sed quia Amor et Odium ad objecta externa referuntur, ideo hos Affectus aliis nominibus significabimus ; nempe Lætitiam concomitante idea causæ internæ Gloriam, et Tristitiam huic contrariam Pudorem appellabimus : Intellige, quando Lætitia vel Tristitia ex eo oritur, quod homo se laudari vel vituperari credit ; alias Lætitiam concomitante idea causæ internæ Acquiescentiam in se ipso, Tristitiam vero eidem contrariam Pœnitentiam vocabo. Deinde quia (per Coroll. 17 p. II) fieri potest, ut Lætitia, qua aliquis se reliquos afficere imaginatur, imaginaria tantum sit, et (per Prop.","kind":"passage"},{"id":"RAW_QGOH_PT_2:P0113","text":"25 hujus) unusquisque de se id omne conatur imaginari, quod se Lætitia afficere imaginatur ; facile ergo fieri potest, ut gloriosus superbus sit, et se omnibus gratum esse imaginetur, quando omnibus molestus est.","kind":"passage"},{"id":"RAW_QGOH_PT_2:P0114","text":"PROPOSITIO XXXI","kind":"passage"},{"id":"RAW_QGOH_PT_2:P0115","text":"Si aliquem imaginamur amare, vel cupere, vel odio habere aliquid, quod ipsi amamus, cupimus, vel odio habemus, eo ipso rem constantius amabimus, etc. Si autem id, quod amamus, cum aversari imaginamur, vel contratum animi fluctuationem patiemur.","kind":"passage"},{"id":"RAW_QGOH_PT_2:P0116","text":"DEMONSTRATIO","kind":"passage"},{"id":"RAW_QGOH_PT_2:P0117","text":"Ex eo solo, quod aliquem aliquid amare imaginamur, eo ipso idem amabimus (per Prop. 27 hujus). At sine hoc nos idem amare supponimus ; accedit ergo Amori nova causa, a qua fovetur ; atque adeo id, quod amamus, hoc ipso constantius amabimus. Deinde ex eo, quod aliquem aliquid aversari imaginamur, idem aversabimur (per eandem Prop.). At si supponamus, nos eodem tempore id ipsum amare, eodem ergo tempore hoc idem amabimus et aversabimur, sive (vid. Schol. Prop. 17 hujus) animi fluctuationem patiemur. Q. E. D.","kind":"passage"},{"id":"RAW_QGOH_PT_2:P0118","text":"COROLLARIUM","kind":"passage"},{"id":"RAW_QGOH_PT_2:P0119","text":"Hinc et ex Prop. 28 hujus sequitur, unumquemque quantum potest conari, ut unusquisque id, quod ipse amat, amet, et quod ipse odit, odio etiam habeat ; unde illud Poetæ :","kind":"passage"},{"id":"RAW_QGOH_PT_2:P0120","text":"Speremus pariter, pariter metuamus amantes ;","kind":"passage"},{"id":"RAW_QGOH_PT_2:P0121","text":"Ferreus est, si quis, quod sinit alter, amat.","kind":"passage"},{"id":"RAW_QGOH_PT_2:P0122","text":"SCHOLIUM","kind":"passage"},{"id":"RAW_QGOH_PT_2:P0123","text":"Hic conatus efficiendi, ut unusquisque probet id, quod ipse amat vel odio habet, revera est Ambitio (vid. Schol. 29 hujus) ; atque adeo videmus unumquemque ex natura appetere, ut reliqui ex ipsius ingenio vivant ; quod dum omnes pariter appetunt, pariter sibi impedimento, et dum omnes ab omnibus laudari seu amari volunt, odio invicem sunt.","kind":"passage"},{"id":"RAW_QGOH_PT_2:P0124","text":"PROPOSITIO XXXII","kind":"passage"},{"id":"RAW_QGOH_PT_2:P0125","text":"Si aliquem re aliqua, qua unus solus potiri potest, gaudere imaginamur conabimur efficere, ne ille illa re potiatur.","kind":"passage"},{"id":"RAW_QGOH_PT_2:P0126","text":"DEMONSTRATIO","kind":"passage"},{"id":"RAW_QGOH_PT_2:P0127","text":"Ex eo solo, quod aliquem re aliqua gaudere imaginamur (per Prop. 27 hujus cum ejusdem 1 Coroll.), rem illam amabimus eaque gaudere cupiemus. At (per hypothesin) huic Lætitiæ obstare imaginamur, quod ille eadem hac re gaudeat ergo (per Prop. 28 hujus), ne ille eadem potiatur, conabimur. Q. E. D.","kind":"passage"},{"id":"RAW_QGOH_PT_2:P0128","text":"SCHOLIUM","kind":"passage"}],"sha256":"e4a771808105c177fff19cb842bbbc8575296e84492113cb6490cf9dc7dd39a0","language_code":"la","language_name":"Latina","translations":[{"id":"SOURCE_TR_20261015T013330340063Z_bb99dd22","language_code":"en","language_name":"English","title":"Part Three - On the Origin and Nature of the Affects · passage 2","source_sha256":"e4a771808105c177fff19cb842bbbc8575296e84492113cb6490cf9dc7dd39a0","sha256":"86d45c1dca98feb22c900c2b0a0929a708651c280066415205fc0da7739ae2a4","rendition_type":"machine","review_status":"machine","model":"gpt-5.6-terra","created":"2026-10-15T01:33:30.340213+00:00","paragraphs":[{"id":"RAW_QGOH_PT_2:P0001","text":"It follows from this that the Mind is averse to imagining those things which diminish or restrain its own power and that of the Body.","kind":"passage","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_QGOH_PT_2:P0001"]},{"id":"RAW_QGOH_PT_2:P0002","text":"SCHOLIUM","kind":"passage","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_QGOH_PT_2:P0002"]},{"id":"RAW_QGOH_PT_2:P0003","text":"From these things we clearly understand what Love is, and what Hatred is. Namely, Love is nothing but Joy accompanied by the idea of an external cause; and Hatred is nothing but Sadness accompanied by the idea of an external cause. We see, further, that one who loves necessarily strives to have present and preserve the thing which he loves; and, on the contrary, one who hates strives to remove and destroy the thing which he holds in hatred. But concerning all these matters more at length in what follows.","kind":"passage","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_QGOH_PT_2:P0003"]},{"id":"RAW_QGOH_PT_2:P0004","text":"PROPOSITION XIV","kind":"passage","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_QGOH_PT_2:P0004"]},{"id":"RAW_QGOH_PT_2:P0005","text":"If the Mind has once been affected simultaneously by two affects, then, when it is afterwards affected by either of them, it will also be affected by the other.","kind":"passage","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_QGOH_PT_2:P0005"]},{"id":"RAW_QGOH_PT_2:P0006","text":"DEMONSTRATION","kind":"passage","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_QGOH_PT_2:P0006"]},{"id":"RAW_QGOH_PT_2:P0007","text":"If the human Body has once been affected simultaneously by two bodies, then, when the Mind afterwards imagines either of them, it will immediately recollect the other also (by Prop. 18, pt. II). But the imaginations of the Mind indicate the affections of our Body more than the nature of external bodies (by Coroll. 2, Prop. 16, pt. II). Therefore, if the Body, and consequently the Mind (see Def. 3 of this part), has once been affected by two affects, then, when it is afterwards affected by either of them, it will also be affected by the other. Q. E. D.","kind":"passage","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_QGOH_PT_2:P0007"]},{"id":"RAW_QGOH_PT_2:P0008","text":"PROPOSITION XV","kind":"passage","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_QGOH_PT_2:P0008"]},{"id":"RAW_QGOH_PT_2:P0009","text":"Any thing whatever can accidentally be a cause of Joy, Sadness, or Desire.","kind":"passage","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_QGOH_PT_2:P0009"]},{"id":"RAW_QGOH_PT_2:P0010","text":"DEMONSTRATION","kind":"passage","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_QGOH_PT_2:P0010"]},{"id":"RAW_QGOH_PT_2:P0011","text":"Let the Mind be affected simultaneously by two affects: one, namely, which neither increases nor diminishes its power of acting, and another which either increases or diminishes the same (see Post. 1 of this part). From the preceding Proposition it is clear that, when the Mind is afterwards affected by the former from its true cause, which (by hypothesis) of itself neither increases nor diminishes its power of thinking, it will immediately also be affected by the latter, which increases or diminishes its power of thinking; that is (by the Schol. to Prop. 11 of this part), it will be affected by Joy or Sadness. Thus that thing will be a cause of Joy or Sadness not in itself, but accidentally. And by this same way it can easily be shown that that thing can accidentally be a cause of Desire. Q. E. D.","kind":"passage","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_QGOH_PT_2:P0011"]},{"id":"RAW_QGOH_PT_2:P0012","text":"COROLLARY","kind":"passage","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_QGOH_PT_2:P0012"]},{"id":"RAW_QGOH_PT_2:P0013","text":"From the mere fact that we have contemplated a thing with an affect of Joy or Sadness, of which it is not the efficient cause, we can love or hold that same thing in hatred.","kind":"passage","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_QGOH_PT_2:P0013"]},{"id":"RAW_QGOH_PT_2:P0014","text":"DEMONSTRATION","kind":"passage","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_QGOH_PT_2:P0014"]},{"id":"RAW_QGOH_PT_2:P0015","text":"For from this mere fact it comes about (by Prop. 14 of this part) that, afterwards imagining this thing, the Mind is affected with an affect of Joy or Sadness; that is (by the Schol. to Prop. 11 of this part), that the power of the Mind and Body is increased or diminished, etc. And consequently (by Prop. 12 of this part), that the Mind desires to imagine the same thing, or (by Coroll. 13 of this part) is averse to it; that is (by the Schol. to Prop. 13 of this part), that it loves or holds it in hatred. Q. E. D.","kind":"passage","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_QGOH_PT_2:P0015"]},{"id":"RAW_QGOH_PT_2:P0016","text":"SCHOLIUM","kind":"passage","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_QGOH_PT_2:P0016"]},{"id":"RAW_QGOH_PT_2:P0017","text":"From this we understand how it can happen that we love or hold certain things in hatred without any cause known to us, but solely from Sympathy (as they say) and Antipathy. And to this are also to be referred those objects which affect us with Joy or Sadness merely because they have something similar to objects which are accustomed to affect us with the same affects, as I shall show in the following Proposition. I know indeed that the Authors who first introduced these names of Sympathy and Antipathy wished to signify by them certain occult qualities of things; nevertheless, I believe that we are permitted to understand by the same names also qualities that are known or manifest.","kind":"passage","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_QGOH_PT_2:P0017"]},{"id":"RAW_QGOH_PT_2:P0018","text":"PROPOSITION XVI","kind":"passage","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_QGOH_PT_2:P0018"]},{"id":"RAW_QGOH_PT_2:P0019","text":"From the mere fact that we imagine a thing to have something similar to an object which is accustomed to affect the Mind with Joy or Sadness, although that in which the thing is similar to the object is not the efficient cause of these affects, we shall nevertheless love or hold it in hatred.","kind":"passage","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_QGOH_PT_2:P0019"]},{"id":"RAW_QGOH_PT_2:P0020","text":"DEMONSTRATION","kind":"passage","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_QGOH_PT_2:P0020"]},{"id":"RAW_QGOH_PT_2:P0021","text":"That which is similar to the object, in the object itself we have contemplated (by hypothesis) with an affect of Joy or Sadness; and therefore (by Prop. 14 of this part), when the Mind is affected by its image, it will immediately also be affected by this or that affect. Consequently, the thing which we perceive to have this same thing will be (by Prop. 15 of this part) accidentally a cause of Joy or Sadness; and therefore (by the preceding Corollary), although that in which it is similar to the object is not the efficient cause of these affects, we shall nevertheless love or hold it in hatred. Q. E. D.","kind":"passage","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_QGOH_PT_2:P0021"]},{"id":"RAW_QGOH_PT_2:P0022","text":"PROPOSITION XVII","kind":"passage","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_QGOH_PT_2:P0022"]},{"id":"RAW_QGOH_PT_2:P0023","text":"If we imagine that a thing which is accustomed to affect us with an affect of Sadness has something similar to another thing which is accustomed to affect us with an equally great affect of Joy, we shall hold it in hatred and at the same time love it.","kind":"passage","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_QGOH_PT_2:P0023"]},{"id":"RAW_QGOH_PT_2:P0024","text":"DEMONSTRATION","kind":"passage","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_QGOH_PT_2:P0024"]},{"id":"RAW_QGOH_PT_2:P0025","text":"For this thing is (by hypothesis) in itself a cause of Sadness, and (by the Scholium to Proposition 13 of this Part), insofar as we imagine it with this affect, we hate it; and insofar as we further imagine it to have something similar to another thing, which is accustomed to affect us with an equally great affect of Joy, we shall love it with an equally great striving of Joy (by the preceding Proposition); and thus we shall hate and at the same time love it. Q. E. D.","kind":"passage","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_QGOH_PT_2:P0025"]},{"id":"RAW_QGOH_PT_2:P0026","text":"SCHOLIUM","kind":"passage","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_QGOH_PT_2:P0026"]},{"id":"RAW_QGOH_PT_2:P0027","text":"This constitution of the Mind, which indeed arises from two contrary affects, is called Fluctuation of Mind, which therefore stands in relation to affect as doubt does to imagination (see the Scholium to Proposition 44, Part II); nor do Fluctuation of Mind and doubt differ from one another except according to greater and less. But it should be noted that, in the preceding Proposition, I deduced these fluctuations of mind from causes which are through themselves the cause of one affect, and accidentally of the other; this I did because they could thus be more easily deduced from what preceded; but not because I deny that fluctuations of mind for the most part arise from an object which is the efficient cause of both affects. For the human Body (by Postulate 1, Part II) is composed of very many individuals of diverse nature, and therefore (by Axiom 1 after Lemma 3, which see after Proposition 13, Part","kind":"passage","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_QGOH_PT_2:P0027"]},{"id":"RAW_QGOH_PT_2:P0028","text":"II) can be affected by one and the same body in very many and diverse ways; and conversely, since one and the same thing can be affected in many ways, it will therefore also be able to affect one and the same part of the Body in many and diverse ways. From these things we can easily conceive that one and the same object can be the cause of many and contrary affects.","kind":"passage","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_QGOH_PT_2:P0028"]},{"id":"RAW_QGOH_PT_2:P0029","text":"PROPOSITION XVIII","kind":"passage","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_QGOH_PT_2:P0029"]},{"id":"RAW_QGOH_PT_2:P0030","text":"A man is affected with the same affect of Joy and Sadness by the image of a past or future thing as by the image of a present thing.","kind":"passage","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_QGOH_PT_2:P0030"]},{"id":"RAW_QGOH_PT_2:P0031","text":"DEMONSTRATION","kind":"passage","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_QGOH_PT_2:P0031"]},{"id":"RAW_QGOH_PT_2:P0032","text":"So long as a man is affected by the image of some thing, he will contemplate the thing as present, even though it does not exist (by Proposition 17, Part II, with its Corollary), nor does he imagine it as past or future except insofar as its image is joined to the image of past or future time (see the Scholium to Proposition 44, Part II). Therefore the image of a thing, considered in itself alone, is the same whether it is referred to future or past time, or to the present; that is (by Corollary 2 to Proposition 16, Part II), the constitution or affect of the Body is the same whether the image is of a past or future thing, or of a present thing; and therefore the affect of Joy and Sadness is the same whether the image is of a past or future thing, or of a present thing. Q. E. D.","kind":"passage","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_QGOH_PT_2:P0032"]},{"id":"RAW_QGOH_PT_2:P0033","text":"SCHOLIUM I","kind":"passage","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_QGOH_PT_2:P0033"]},{"id":"RAW_QGOH_PT_2:P0034","text":"Here I call a thing past or future insofar as we have been or shall be affected by it. For example, insofar as we have seen or shall see it, it has refreshed or will refresh us, it has harmed or will harm us, etc. For insofar as we imagine it in this way, we affirm its existence; that is, the Body is affected by no affect that excludes the existence of the thing, and therefore (by Proposition 17, Part II) the Body is affected by the image of the same thing in the same manner as if the thing itself were present. But since it most often happens that those who have experienced more things fluctuate, so long as they contemplate a thing as future or past, and for the most part doubt the outcome of the thing (see the Scholium to Proposition 44, Part II), it follows that the affects arising from similar images of things are not so constant, but are for the most part disturbed by images of other things, until men become more certain about the outcome of the thing.","kind":"passage","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_QGOH_PT_2:P0034"]},{"id":"RAW_QGOH_PT_2:P0035","text":"SCHOLIUM II","kind":"passage","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_QGOH_PT_2:P0035"]},{"id":"RAW_QGOH_PT_2:P0036","text":"From what has just been said, we understand what Hope, Fear, Security, Despair, Gladness, and remorse of Conscience are. For Hope is nothing other than an inconstant Joy arising from the image of a future or past thing, concerning whose outcome we doubt. Fear, on the other hand, is an inconstant Sadness, likewise arising from the image of a doubtful thing. Further, if doubt is removed from these affects, Hope becomes Security and Fear Despair; namely, Joy or Sadness arising from the image of a thing which we feared or hoped for. Gladness, moreover, is Joy arising from the image of a past thing, concerning whose outcome we had doubted. Finally, remorse of Conscience is Sadness, opposed to Gladness.","kind":"passage","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_QGOH_PT_2:P0036"]},{"id":"RAW_QGOH_PT_2:P0037","text":"PROPOSITION XIX","kind":"passage","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_QGOH_PT_2:P0037"]},{"id":"RAW_QGOH_PT_2:P0038","text":"He who imagines that what he loves is destroyed will be saddened; but if he imagines it preserved, he will rejoice.","kind":"passage","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_QGOH_PT_2:P0038"]},{"id":"RAW_QGOH_PT_2:P0039","text":"DEMONSTRATION","kind":"passage","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_QGOH_PT_2:P0039"]},{"id":"RAW_QGOH_PT_2:P0040","text":"The Mind, as far as it can, strives to imagine those things which increase or assist the Body's power of acting (by Proposition 12 of this Part), that is (by the Scholium to Proposition 13 of this Part), those things which it loves. But imagination is assisted by things which posit the existence of a thing, and, on the contrary, is restrained by things which exclude the existence of a thing (by Proposition 17, Part II); therefore images of things which posit the existence of the thing loved assist the striving of the Mind by which it strives to imagine the thing loved, that is (by the Scholium to Proposition 11 of this Part), they affect the Mind with Joy; and those which, on the contrary, exclude the existence of the thing loved restrain the same striving of the Mind, that is (by the same Scholium), they affect the Mind with Sadness. Therefore he who imagines that what he loves is destroyed will be saddened, etc. Q. E. D.","kind":"passage","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_QGOH_PT_2:P0040"]},{"id":"RAW_QGOH_PT_2:P0041","text":"PROPOSITION XX","kind":"passage","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_QGOH_PT_2:P0041"]},{"id":"RAW_QGOH_PT_2:P0042","text":"He who imagines that what he hates is destroyed will rejoice.","kind":"passage","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_QGOH_PT_2:P0042"]},{"id":"RAW_QGOH_PT_2:P0043","text":"DEMONSTRATION","kind":"passage","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_QGOH_PT_2:P0043"]},{"id":"RAW_QGOH_PT_2:P0044","text":"The Mind (by Proposition 13 of this Part) strives to imagine those things which exclude the existence of things by which the Body's power of acting is diminished or restrained; that is (by the Scholium to the same Proposition), it strives to imagine those things which exclude the existence of things which it hates; and therefore the image of a thing which excludes the existence of what the Mind hates assists this striving of the Mind, that is (by the Scholium to Proposition 11 of this Part) affects the Mind with Joy. Therefore he who imagines that what he hates is destroyed will rejoice. Q. E. D.","kind":"passage","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_QGOH_PT_2:P0044"]},{"id":"RAW_QGOH_PT_2:P0045","text":"PROPOSITION XXI","kind":"passage","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_QGOH_PT_2:P0045"]},{"id":"RAW_QGOH_PT_2:P0046","text":"He who imagines that what he loves is affected with Joy or Sadness will also be affected with Joy or Sadness; and each of these affects will be greater or less in the lover, according as each is greater or less in the thing loved.","kind":"passage","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_QGOH_PT_2:P0046"]},{"id":"RAW_QGOH_PT_2:P0047","text":"DEMONSTRATION","kind":"passage","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_QGOH_PT_2:P0047"]},{"id":"RAW_QGOH_PT_2:P0048","text":"Images of things (as we demonstrated in Proposition 19 of this Part) which posit the existence of a thing loved assist the striving of the Mind by which it strives to imagine the thing loved itself. But Joy posits the existence of the joyful thing, and does so the more as the affect of Joy is greater; for it is (by the Scholium to Proposition 11 of this Part) a transition to greater perfection: therefore the image of the Joy of the thing loved assists, in the lover, the striving of his own Mind, that is (by the Scholium to Proposition 11 of this Part), it affects the lover with Joy, and with so much the greater Joy as this affect has been greater in the thing loved. This was the first point. Next, insofar as a thing is affected with Sadness, it is destroyed, and the more so as it is affected with greater Sadness (by the same Scholium to Proposition 11 of this Part); and thus (by Proposition 19 of this Part) he who imagines that what he loves is affected with Sadness will also be affected with Sadness, and with so much the greater Sadness as this affect has been greater in the thing loved. Q. E. D.","kind":"passage","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_QGOH_PT_2:P0048"]},{"id":"RAW_QGOH_PT_2:P0049","text":"PROPOSITION XXII","kind":"passage","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_QGOH_PT_2:P0049"]},{"id":"RAW_QGOH_PT_2:P0050","text":"If we imagine that someone affects with Joy a thing which we love, we shall also be affected with Love toward him. If, on the contrary, we imagine that the same person affects the same thing with Sadness, we shall also, on the contrary, be affected with Hatred toward him.","kind":"passage","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_QGOH_PT_2:P0050"]},{"id":"RAW_QGOH_PT_2:P0051","text":"DEMONSTRATION","kind":"passage","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_QGOH_PT_2:P0051"]},{"id":"RAW_QGOH_PT_2:P0052","text":"He who affects with Joy or Sadness a thing which we love also affects us with Joy or Sadness, provided, of course, that we imagine the beloved thing to be affected with that Joy or Sadness (by the preceding Prop.). But this Joy or Sadness is supposed to be given in us accompanied by the idea of an external cause; therefore (by the Schol. of Prop. 13 of this part), if we imagine that someone affects with Joy or Sadness a thing which we love, we shall be affected toward him with Love or Hatred. Q. E. D.","kind":"passage","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_QGOH_PT_2:P0052"]},{"id":"RAW_QGOH_PT_2:P0053","text":"SCHOLIUM","kind":"passage","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_QGOH_PT_2:P0053"]},{"id":"RAW_QGOH_PT_2:P0054","text":"Proposition 21 explains to us what Compassion is, which we may define as Sadness arising from another's harm. But by what name the Joy which arises from another's good is to be called, I do not know. Further, we shall call Love toward him who has done good to another Favor, and, conversely, Hatred toward him who has done evil to another Indignation. Finally, it must be noted that we not only pity a thing which we have loved (as we showed in Prop. 21), but also one which we have previously pursued with no affect, provided that we judge it to be similar to ourselves (and I shall show this below); and thus we also favor him who has done good to one like ourselves, and conversely are indignant with him who has inflicted harm upon one like ourselves.","kind":"passage","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_QGOH_PT_2:P0054"]},{"id":"RAW_QGOH_PT_2:P0055","text":"PROPOSITION XXIII","kind":"passage","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_QGOH_PT_2:P0055"]},{"id":"RAW_QGOH_PT_2:P0056","text":"He who imagines that that which he hates is affected with Sadness will rejoice; if, on the contrary, he imagines that it is affected with Joy, he will be saddened; and each of these affects will be greater or lesser according as its contrary is greater or lesser in that which he hates.","kind":"passage","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_QGOH_PT_2:P0056"]},{"id":"RAW_QGOH_PT_2:P0057","text":"DEMONSTRATION","kind":"passage","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_QGOH_PT_2:P0057"]},{"id":"RAW_QGOH_PT_2:P0058","text":"Insofar as a hateful thing is affected with Sadness, it is destroyed, and the more so as it is affected with greater Sadness (by the Schol. of Prop. 11 of this part). Therefore he who (by Prop. 20 of this part) imagines that a thing which he hates is affected with Sadness will, on the contrary, be affected with Joy; and with so much the greater Joy as he imagines the hateful thing to be affected with greater Sadness. This was the first point. Next, Joy posits the existence of the joyful thing (by the same Schol. of Prop. 11 of this part), and the more so as greater Joy is conceived. If someone imagines that he whom he hates is affected with Joy, this imagination (by Prop. 13 of this part) will restrain his striving; that is (by the Schol. of Prop. 11 of this part), he who hates will be affected with Sadness, etc. Q. E. D.","kind":"passage","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_QGOH_PT_2:P0058"]},{"id":"RAW_QGOH_PT_2:P0059","text":"SCHOLIUM","kind":"passage","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_QGOH_PT_2:P0059"]},{"id":"RAW_QGOH_PT_2:P0060","text":"This Joy can scarcely be solid and without any conflict of mind. For (as I shall show immediately in Proposition 27 of this part), insofar as he imagines a thing similar to himself to be affected with an affect of Sadness, he must be saddened; and conversely, if he imagines it to be affected with Joy. But here we attend only to Hatred.","kind":"passage","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_QGOH_PT_2:P0060"]},{"id":"RAW_QGOH_PT_2:P0061","text":"PROPOSITION XXIV","kind":"passage","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_QGOH_PT_2:P0061"]},{"id":"RAW_QGOH_PT_2:P0062","text":"If we imagine that someone affects with Joy a thing which we hate, we shall also be affected with Hatred toward him. If, on the contrary, we imagine that the same person affects the same thing with Sadness, we shall therefore be affected with Love toward him.","kind":"passage","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_QGOH_PT_2:P0062"]},{"id":"RAW_QGOH_PT_2:P0063","text":"DEMONSTRATION","kind":"passage","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_QGOH_PT_2:P0063"]},{"id":"RAW_QGOH_PT_2:P0064","text":"This Proposition is demonstrated in the same manner as Proposition 22 of this part; see it.","kind":"passage","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_QGOH_PT_2:P0064"]},{"id":"RAW_QGOH_PT_2:P0065","text":"SCHOLIUM","kind":"passage","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_QGOH_PT_2:P0065"]},{"id":"RAW_QGOH_PT_2:P0066","text":"These and similar affects of Hatred are referred to Envy, which therefore is nothing other than Hatred itself, insofar as it is considered to dispose a man in such a way that he rejoices at another's misfortune and, conversely, is saddened at the same person's good.","kind":"passage","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_QGOH_PT_2:P0066"]},{"id":"RAW_QGOH_PT_2:P0067","text":"PROPOSITION XXV","kind":"passage","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_QGOH_PT_2:P0067"]},{"id":"RAW_QGOH_PT_2:P0068","text":"We strive to affirm of ourselves and of a thing loved everything which we imagine affects us, or the loved thing, with Joy; and, conversely, to deny everything which we imagine affects us, or the loved thing, with Sadness.","kind":"passage","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_QGOH_PT_2:P0068"]},{"id":"RAW_QGOH_PT_2:P0069","text":"DEMONSTRATION","kind":"passage","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_QGOH_PT_2:P0069"]},{"id":"RAW_QGOH_PT_2:P0070","text":"Whatever we imagine to affect a loved thing with Joy or Sadness affects us with Joy or Sadness (by Prop. 21 of this part). But the Mind (by Prop. 12 of this part) strives, as far as it can, to imagine those things which affect us with Joy, that is (by Prop. 17, part II, and its Coroll.) to contemplate them as present; and conversely (by Prop. 13 of this part), to exclude the existence of those things which affect us with Sadness; therefore we strive to affirm of ourselves and of a loved thing everything which we imagine affects us, or the loved thing, with Joy, and conversely. Q. E. D.","kind":"passage","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_QGOH_PT_2:P0070"]},{"id":"RAW_QGOH_PT_2:P0071","text":"PROPOSITION XXVI","kind":"passage","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_QGOH_PT_2:P0071"]},{"id":"RAW_QGOH_PT_2:P0072","text":"We strive to affirm of a thing which we hate everything which we imagine affects it with Sadness, and, conversely, to deny that which we imagine affects it with Joy.","kind":"passage","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_QGOH_PT_2:P0072"]},{"id":"RAW_QGOH_PT_2:P0073","text":"DEMONSTRATION","kind":"passage","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_QGOH_PT_2:P0073"]},{"id":"RAW_QGOH_PT_2:P0074","text":"This Proposition follows from Proposition 23, as the preceding one from Proposition 21 of this Part.","kind":"passage","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_QGOH_PT_2:P0074"]},{"id":"RAW_QGOH_PT_2:P0075","text":"SCHOLIUM","kind":"passage","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_QGOH_PT_2:P0075"]},{"id":"RAW_QGOH_PT_2:P0076","text":"From these things we see that it easily happens that a man thinks more highly than is just of himself and of a thing loved, and, on the contrary, less highly than is just of a thing which he hates; this imagination, indeed, when it concerns the man himself, who thinks more highly than is just of himself, is called Pride, and is a species of Delirium, because a man dreams with his eyes open that he can do all those things which he attains by imagination alone, and which he therefore contemplates as though real, and exults in them, so long as he cannot imagine those things which exclude the existence of these and determine his own power of acting. Pride, therefore, is Joy arising from the fact that a man thinks more highly than is just of himself. Further, Joy which arises from the fact that a man thinks more highly than is just of another is called Esteem; and finally, that Joy which arises from the fact that he thinks less highly than is just of another is called Contempt.","kind":"passage","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_QGOH_PT_2:P0076"]},{"id":"RAW_QGOH_PT_2:P0077","text":"PROPOSITION XXVII","kind":"passage","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_QGOH_PT_2:P0077"]},{"id":"RAW_QGOH_PT_2:P0078","text":"From the fact that we imagine a thing similar to ourselves, and which we have pursued with no affect, to be affected with some affect, we are thereby affected with a similar affect.","kind":"passage","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_QGOH_PT_2:P0078"]},{"id":"RAW_QGOH_PT_2:P0079","text":"DEMONSTRATION","kind":"passage","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_QGOH_PT_2:P0079"]},{"id":"RAW_QGOH_PT_2:P0080","text":"Images of things are affections of the human Body, the ideas of which represent external bodies as though present to us (by Schol. Prop. 17 p. II); that is (by Prop. 16 p. II), the ideas of which involve the nature of our Body, and at the same time the present nature of the external body. If, therefore, the nature of the external body is similar to the nature of our Body, then the idea of the external body which we imagine will involve an affection of our Body similar to the affection of the external body; and consequently, if we imagine someone similar to ourselves to be affected with some affect, this imagination will express an affection of our Body similar to this affect; and hence, from the fact that we imagine a thing similar to ourselves to be affected with some affect, we are affected with an affect similar to it. But if we hate a thing similar to ourselves, then (by Prop. 23 of this Part) we shall be affected with an affect contrary to it, not with a similar one. Q. E. D.","kind":"passage","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_QGOH_PT_2:P0080"]},{"id":"RAW_QGOH_PT_2:P0081","text":"SCHOLIUM","kind":"passage","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_QGOH_PT_2:P0081"]},{"id":"RAW_QGOH_PT_2:P0082","text":"This imitation of the affects, when it is referred to Sadness, is called Pity (concerning which see Schol. Prop. 22 of this Part); but when referred to Desire, Emulation, which consequently is nothing other than the Desire for some thing that is engendered in us from the fact that we imagine others similar to ourselves to have the same Desire.","kind":"passage","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_QGOH_PT_2:P0082"]},{"id":"RAW_QGOH_PT_2:P0083","text":"COROLLARY I","kind":"passage","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_QGOH_PT_2:P0083"]},{"id":"RAW_QGOH_PT_2:P0084","text":"If we imagine someone whom we have pursued with no affect to affect with Joy a thing similar to ourselves, we shall be affected with Love toward him. If, on the contrary, we imagine him to affect the same thing with Sadness, we shall, on the contrary, be affected with Hatred toward him.","kind":"passage","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_QGOH_PT_2:P0084"]},{"id":"RAW_QGOH_PT_2:P0085","text":"DEMONSTRATION","kind":"passage","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_QGOH_PT_2:P0085"]},{"id":"RAW_QGOH_PT_2:P0086","text":"This is demonstrated in the same way from the preceding Proposition as Proposition 22 of this Part from Proposition 21.","kind":"passage","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_QGOH_PT_2:P0086"]},{"id":"RAW_QGOH_PT_2:P0087","text":"COROLLARY II","kind":"passage","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_QGOH_PT_2:P0087"]},{"id":"RAW_QGOH_PT_2:P0088","text":"We cannot hate a thing which we pity on the ground that its misery affects us with Sadness.","kind":"passage","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_QGOH_PT_2:P0088"]},{"id":"RAW_QGOH_PT_2:P0089","text":"DEMONSTRATION","kind":"passage","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_QGOH_PT_2:P0089"]},{"id":"RAW_QGOH_PT_2:P0090","text":"For if on that ground we could hate the same thing, then (by Prop. 23 of this Part) we should rejoice at its Sadness, which is contrary to the hypothesis.","kind":"passage","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_QGOH_PT_2:P0090"]},{"id":"RAW_QGOH_PT_2:P0091","text":"COROLLARY III","kind":"passage","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_QGOH_PT_2:P0091"]},{"id":"RAW_QGOH_PT_2:P0092","text":"We shall endeavor, as far as we can, to free from misery a thing which we pity.","kind":"passage","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_QGOH_PT_2:P0092"]},{"id":"RAW_QGOH_PT_2:P0093","text":"DEMONSTRATION","kind":"passage","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_QGOH_PT_2:P0093"]},{"id":"RAW_QGOH_PT_2:P0094","text":"That by which the thing which we pity is affected with Sadness also affects us with a similar Sadness (by the preceding Prop.); and hence we shall endeavor to think of everything which takes away the existence of that thing, or which destroys the thing (by Prop. 13 of this Part), that is (by Schol. Prop. 9 of this Part), we shall desire to destroy it, or shall be determined to destroy it; and hence we shall endeavor to free the thing which we pity from its misery. Q. E. D.","kind":"passage","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_QGOH_PT_2:P0094"]},{"id":"RAW_QGOH_PT_2:P0095","text":"SCHOLIUM","kind":"passage","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_QGOH_PT_2:P0095"]},{"id":"RAW_QGOH_PT_2:P0096","text":"This will, or Desire, to do good, which arises from the fact that we pity the thing upon which we wish to confer a benefit, is called Benevolence, which consequently is nothing other than Desire arising from pity. Moreover, concerning Love and Hatred toward one who has done good or evil to a thing which we imagine to be similar to ourselves, see Schol. Prop. 22 of this Part.","kind":"passage","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_QGOH_PT_2:P0096"]},{"id":"RAW_QGOH_PT_2:P0097","text":"PROPOSITION XXVIII","kind":"passage","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_QGOH_PT_2:P0097"]},{"id":"RAW_QGOH_PT_2:P0098","text":"We strive to further the occurrence of everything that we imagine to conduce to Joy; but, on the other hand, we strive to remove or destroy whatever we imagine to be opposed to it, or to conduce to Sadness.","kind":"passage","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_QGOH_PT_2:P0098"]},{"id":"RAW_QGOH_PT_2:P0099","text":"DEMONSTRATION","kind":"passage","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_QGOH_PT_2:P0099"]},{"id":"RAW_QGOH_PT_2:P0100","text":"We strive, as far as we can, to imagine that which we imagine to conduce to Joy (by Prop. 12 of this Part); that is (by Prop. 17, Part II), we shall strive, as far as we can, to contemplate it as present, or as actually existing. But the striving, or power, of the Mind in thinking is equal and simultaneous by nature with the striving, or power, of the Body in acting (as follows clearly from Coroll. Prop. 7 and Coroll. Prop. 11, Part II); therefore we strive absolutely that it should exist, or (which, by Schol. Prop. 9 of this Part, is the same) we desire and intend it; which was the first point. Next, if we imagine that that which we believe to be a cause of Sadness, that is (by Schol. Prop. 13 of this Part), that which we hate, is destroyed, we shall rejoice (by Prop. 20 of this Part); and therefore (by the first part of this demonstration) we shall strive to destroy the same, or (by Prop. 13 of this Part) to remove it from us, lest we contemplate it as present; which was the second point. Therefore, everything that conduces to Joy, etc. Q. E. D.","kind":"passage","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_QGOH_PT_2:P0100"]},{"id":"RAW_QGOH_PT_2:P0101","text":"PROPOSITION XXIX","kind":"passage","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_QGOH_PT_2:P0101"]},{"id":"RAW_QGOH_PT_2:P0102","text":"We shall also strive to do everything which we imagine men regard with Joy; and, on the other hand, we shall be averse to doing that which we imagine men are averse to.","kind":"passage","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_QGOH_PT_2:P0102"]},{"id":"RAW_QGOH_PT_2:P0103","text":"DEMONSTRATION","kind":"passage","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_QGOH_PT_2:P0103"]},{"id":"RAW_QGOH_PT_2:P0104","text":"From our imagining that men love or hate something, we shall love or hate the same thing (by Prop. 27 of this Part); that is (by Schol. Prop. 13 of this Part), by that very fact we shall rejoice or be saddened at the presence of that thing; and therefore (by the preceding Prop.) we shall strive to do everything which we imagine men regard with Joy, etc. Q. E. D.","kind":"passage","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_QGOH_PT_2:P0104"]},{"id":"RAW_QGOH_PT_2:P0105","text":"SCHOLIUM","kind":"passage","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_QGOH_PT_2:P0105"]},{"id":"RAW_QGOH_PT_2:P0106","text":"This striving to do something, and also to omit something, solely in order that we may please men, is called Ambition, especially when we strive so eagerly to please the multitude that, to our own or another's detriment, we do or omit certain things; otherwise it is usually called Humanity. Further, I call the Joy by which we imagine another's action, by which he has striven to delight us, Praise; but the Sadness by which, on the contrary, we are averse to that same action, I call Blame.","kind":"passage","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_QGOH_PT_2:P0106"]},{"id":"RAW_QGOH_PT_2:P0107","text":"PROPOSITION XXX","kind":"passage","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_QGOH_PT_2:P0107"]},{"id":"RAW_QGOH_PT_2:P0108","text":"If anyone has done something which he imagines affects the rest with Joy, he will be affected with Joy, accompanied by the idea of himself as cause; or he will contemplate himself with Joy. If, on the contrary, he has done something which he imagines affects the rest with Sadness, he will, on the contrary, contemplate himself with Sadness.","kind":"passage","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_QGOH_PT_2:P0108"]},{"id":"RAW_QGOH_PT_2:P0109","text":"DEMONSTRATION","kind":"passage","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_QGOH_PT_2:P0109"]},{"id":"RAW_QGOH_PT_2:P0110","text":"He who imagines that he affects the rest with Joy or Sadness is, by that very fact (by Prop. 27 of this Part), affected with Joy or Sadness. But since man (by Prop. 19 and 23, Part II) is conscious of himself through the affections by which he is determined to act; therefore, he who has done something which he himself imagines affects the rest with Joy will be affected with Joy, together with consciousness of himself as cause; or, he will contemplate himself with Joy, and conversely. Q. E. D.","kind":"passage","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_QGOH_PT_2:P0110"]},{"id":"RAW_QGOH_PT_2:P0111","text":"SCHOLIUM","kind":"passage","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_QGOH_PT_2:P0111"]},{"id":"RAW_QGOH_PT_2:P0112","text":"Since Love (by Schol. Prop. 13 of this Part) is Joy accompanied by the idea of an external cause, and Hatred is Sadness likewise accompanied by the idea of an external cause, this Joy and Sadness will therefore be species of Love and Hatred. But because Love and Hatred are referred to external objects, we shall signify these Affects by other names; namely, Joy accompanied by the idea of an internal cause we shall call Glory, and the Sadness contrary to this we shall call Shame. Understand this when Joy or Sadness arises from a man's believing that he is praised or blamed; otherwise, Joy accompanied by the idea of an internal cause I shall call Self-contentment, while the Sadness contrary to it I shall call Repentance. Further, since (by Coroll. 17, Part II) it can happen that the Joy by which someone imagines that he affects the rest is merely imaginary, and (by Prop.","kind":"passage","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_QGOH_PT_2:P0112"]},{"id":"RAW_QGOH_PT_2:P0113","text":"25 of this Part) everyone strives to imagine of himself everything which he imagines affects him with Joy, it can therefore easily happen that one who is glorious is proud, and imagines himself pleasing to all when he is troublesome to all.","kind":"passage","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_QGOH_PT_2:P0113"]},{"id":"RAW_QGOH_PT_2:P0114","text":"PROPOSITION XXXI","kind":"passage","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_QGOH_PT_2:P0114"]},{"id":"RAW_QGOH_PT_2:P0115","text":"If we imagine someone to love, or desire, or hate something which we ourselves love, desire, or hate, we shall, by that very fact, love the thing more steadfastly, etc. But if we imagine him to be averse to that which we love, we shall suffer a fluctuation of mind contrary to this.","kind":"passage","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_QGOH_PT_2:P0115"]},{"id":"RAW_QGOH_PT_2:P0116","text":"DEMONSTRATION","kind":"passage","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_QGOH_PT_2:P0116"]},{"id":"RAW_QGOH_PT_2:P0117","text":"From the mere fact that we imagine someone to love something, we shall, by that very fact, love the same thing (by Prop. 27 of this Part). But without this we are supposed to love the same thing; therefore, a new cause is added to Love, by which it is fostered; and hence we shall, by this very fact, love that which we love more steadfastly. Further, from our imagining someone to be averse to something, we shall be averse to the same thing (by the same Prop.). But if we suppose that at the same time we love that very thing, then at the same time we shall love and be averse to this same thing, or (see Schol. Prop. 17 of this Part) we shall suffer a fluctuation of mind. Q. E. D.","kind":"passage","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_QGOH_PT_2:P0117"]},{"id":"RAW_QGOH_PT_2:P0118","text":"COROLLARY","kind":"passage","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_QGOH_PT_2:P0118"]},{"id":"RAW_QGOH_PT_2:P0119","text":"Hence, and from Prop. 28 of this Part, it follows that everyone strives, as far as he can, that everyone should love what he himself loves, and should also hate what he himself hates; whence that saying of the Poet:","kind":"passage","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_QGOH_PT_2:P0119"]},{"id":"RAW_QGOH_PT_2:P0120","text":"Let us lovers hope alike, let us fear alike;","kind":"passage","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_QGOH_PT_2:P0120"]},{"id":"RAW_QGOH_PT_2:P0121","text":"He is made of iron, if anyone loves what another permits.","kind":"passage","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_QGOH_PT_2:P0121"]},{"id":"RAW_QGOH_PT_2:P0122","text":"SCHOLIUM","kind":"passage","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_QGOH_PT_2:P0122"]},{"id":"RAW_QGOH_PT_2:P0123","text":"This endeavor to bring it about that everyone should approve what he himself loves or hates is in reality Ambition (see the Scholium to Prop. 29 of this Part); and thus we see that everyone, from nature, strives that the rest should live according to his own disposition; and while all alike strive after this, they are alike an obstacle to one another, and while all wish to be praised or loved by all, they are mutually hateful.","kind":"passage","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_QGOH_PT_2:P0123"]},{"id":"RAW_QGOH_PT_2:P0124","text":"PROPOSITION XXXII","kind":"passage","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_QGOH_PT_2:P0124"]},{"id":"RAW_QGOH_PT_2:P0125","text":"If we imagine someone to take pleasure in some thing of which only one can be possessed, we shall strive to bring it about that he does not possess that thing.","kind":"passage","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_QGOH_PT_2:P0125"]},{"id":"RAW_QGOH_PT_2:P0126","text":"DEMONSTRATION","kind":"passage","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_QGOH_PT_2:P0126"]},{"id":"RAW_QGOH_PT_2:P0127","text":"From the sole fact that we imagine someone to take pleasure in some thing (by Prop. 27 of this Part together with its Corollary 1), we shall love that thing and desire to take pleasure in it. But (by the hypothesis) we imagine that his taking pleasure in this same thing stands in the way of this Joy; therefore (by Prop. 28 of this Part), we shall strive that he does not possess the same thing. Q. E. D.","kind":"passage","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_QGOH_PT_2:P0127"]},{"id":"RAW_QGOH_PT_2:P0128","text":"SCHOLIUM","kind":"passage","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_QGOH_PT_2:P0128"]}],"source_digest":"8da4467dcf7b05f17cf2bab4419f1bc35b6908a16b05d45dc0ef72899f8ed3fd","source_language_code":"la","original_title":"Pars tertia - De origine et natura affectuum · passage 2"}],"passage_basis":"frozen-grounding","original_title":"Pars tertia - De origine et natura affectuum · passage 2","title_language_code":"en"},{"id":"RAW_QGOH_PT_3","title":"Part Three - On the Origin and Nature of the Affects · passage 3","order":11,"chapter_id":"CH_QGOH_PT_3","text":"Videmus itaque, cum hominum natura plerumque ita comparatum esse, ut eorum, quibus male est, misereantur, et quibus bene est, invideant, et (per Prop. præc.) eo majore odio, quo rem, qua alium potiri imaginantur, magis amant. Videmus deinde, ex eadem naturæ humanæ proprietate, ex qua sequitur, homines esse misericordes, sequi etiam, eosdem esse invidos et ambitiosos. Denique, si ipsam experientiam consulere velimus, ipsam hæc omnia docere experiemur ; præsertim si ad priores nostræ ætatis annos attenderimus. Nam pueros, quia eorum corpus continuo veluti in æquilibrio est, ex hoc solo ridere vel flere experimur, quod alios ridere vel flere vident ; et quicquid præterea vident alios facere, id imitari statim cupiunt, et omnia denique sibi cupiunt quibus alios delectari imaginantur ; nimirum quia rerum imagines, ut diximus, sunt ipsæ humani Corporis affectiones, sive modi, quibus Corpus humanum a causis externis afficitur disponiturque ad hoc vel illud agendum.\n\nPROPOSITIO XXXIII\n\nCum rem nobis similem amamus, conamur, quantum possumus, efficere, ut nos contra amet.\n\nDEMONSTRATIO\n\nRem, quam amamus præ reliquis, quantum possumus, imaginari conamur (per Prop. 12 hujus). Si igitur res nobis sit similis, ipsam præ reliquis Lætitia afficere conabimur (per Prop. 29 hujus), sive conabimur, quantum possumus, efficere, ut res amata Lætitia afficiatur concomitante idea nostri, hoc est (per Schol. Prop. 13 hujus), ut nos contra amet. Q. E. D.\n\nPROPOSITIO XXXIV\n\nQuo majori affectu rem amatam erga nos affectam esse imaginamur, eo magis gloriabimur.\n\nDEMONSTRATIO\n\nNos (per Prop. præced.) conamur, quantum possumus, ut res amata. nos contra amet ; hoc est (per Schol. Prop. 13 hujus), ut res amata Lætitia afficiatur concomitante idea nostri. Quo itaque rem amatam majori Lætitia nostra de causa affectam esse imaginamur, eo magis hic conatus juvatur ; hoc est (per Prop. 11 hujus cum ejus Schol.), eo majore Lætitia afficimur. At cum ex eo lætemur, quod alium nobis similem Lætitia affecimus, tum nosmet cum Lætitia contemplamur (per Prop. 30 hujus) : ergo quo majori affectu rem amatam erga nos affectam esse imaginamur, eo majori Lætitia nosmet contemplabimur, sive (per Schol. Prop. 30 hujus) eo magis gloriabimur. Q. E. D.\n\nPROPOSITIO XXXV\n\nSi quis imaginatur rem amatam eodem vel arctiore vinculo Amicitiæ, quo ipse eadem solus potiebatur, alium sibi jungere, Odio erga ipsam rem amatam afficietur, et illi alteri invidebit.\n\nDEMONSTRATIO\n\nQuo quis majore amore rem amatam erga se affectam esse imaginatur, eo magis gloriabitur (per præced. Prop.), hoc est (per Schol. Prop. 30 hujus) lætabitur ; adeoque (per Prop. 28 hujus) conabitur, quantum potest, imaginarirem amatam ipsi quum arctissime devinctam ; qui quidem conatus sive appetitus fomentatur, si alium idem sibi cupere imaginatur (per Prop. 31 hujus). At hic conatus sive appetitus ab ipsius rei amatæ imagine concomitante imagine illius, quem res amata sibi jungit coerceri supponitur ; ergo (per Schol. Prop. 11 hujus) eo ipso Tristitia afficietur, concomitante idea rei amatæ tanquam causa, et simul imagine alterius, hoc est (per Schol. Prop. 13 hujus), odio erga rem amatam afficietur, et simul erga illum alterum (per Coroll. Prop. 15 hujus), cui propterea (per Prop. 23 hujus), quod re amata delectatur, invidebit. Q. E. D.\n\nSCHOLIUM\n\nHoc Odium erga rem amatam Invidiæ junctum Zelotypia vocatur, quæ proinde nihil aliud est, quam animi fluctuatio orta ex Amore et Odio simul, concomitante idea alterius, cui invidetur. Præterea hoc Odium erga rem amatam majus erit pro ratione Lætitiæ, qua Zelotypus ex reciproco rei amatæ Amore solebat affici, et etiam pro ratione affectus, quo erga illum, quem sibi rem amatam jungere imaginatur, affectus erat. Nam si eum oderat, eo ipso rem amatam (per Prop. 24 hujus) odio habebit, quia ipsam id, quod ipse odio habet, Lætitia afficere imaginatur ; et etiam (per Coroll. Prop. 15 hujus) ex eo, quod rei amatæ imaginem imagini ejus, quem odit, jungere cogitur. Quæ ratio plerumque locum habet in Amore erga fæminam ; qui enim imaginatur mulierem, quam amat, alteri sese prostituere, non solum ex eo, quod ipsius appetitus coercetur, contristabitur, sed etiam, quia rei amatæ imaginem pudendis et excrementis alterius jungere cogitur, eandem aversatur ; ad quod denique accedit, quod Zelotypus non eodem vultu, quem res amata ei præbere solebat, ab eadem excipiatur, qua etiam de causa amans contristatur, ut jam ostendam.\n\nPROPOSITIO XXXVI\n\nQui rei, qua semel delectatusest, recordatur, cupit eamdem cum iisdem potiri circumstantiis, ac cum primo ipsa delectatus est.\n\nDEMONSTRATIO\n\nQuicquid homo simul cum re, quæ ipsum delectavit, vidit, id omne (per Prop. 15 hujus) eri per accidens Lætitiae causa ; adeoque (per Prop. 28 hujus) omni eo simul cum re, quæ ipsum delectavit, potiri cupiet, sive re cum omnibus iisdem circumstantiis potiri cupiet, ac cum primo eadem delectatus est. Q. E. D.\n\nCOROLLARIUM\n\nSi itaque unam ex iis circumstantiis deficere compererit, amans contristabitur.\n\nDEMONSTRATIO\n\nNam quatenus aliquam circumstantiam deficere comperit, eatenus aliquid imaginatur, quod ejus rei existentiam secludit. Cum autem ejus rei sive circumstantiæ (per Prop. præc.) sit præ amore cupidus, ergo (per Prop. 19 hujus), quatenus eandem deficere imaginatur, contristabitur. Q. E. D.\n\nSCHOLIUM\n\nHæc Tristitia, quatenus absentiam ejus, quod amamus, respicit, Desiderium vocatur.\n\nPROPOSITIO XXXVII\n\nCupiditas, quæ præ Tristitia vel Lætitia præque Odio vel Amore oritur, eo est major, quo affectus major est.\n\nDEMONSTRATIO\n\nTristitia hominis agendi potentiam (per Schol. Prop. 11 hujus) minuit vel coercet, hoc est (per Prop. 7 hujus), conatum, quo homo in suo esse perseverare conatur, minuit vel coercet ; adeoque (per Prop. 5 hujus) huic conatui est contraria ; et quicquid homo Tristitia affectus conatur est Tristitiam amovere. At (per Tristitiæ Defin.) quo Tristitia major est, eo majori parti hominis agendi potentiæ necesse est opponi ; ergo quo major Tristitia est, eo majore agendi potentia conabitur homo contra Tristitiam amovere ; hoc est (per Schol. Prop. 9 hujus), eo majore cupiditate sive appetitu conabitur Tristitiam amovere. Deinde, quoniam Lætitia (per idem Schol. Prop. 11 hujus) hominis agendi potentiam auget vel juvat, facile eadem via demonstratur, quod homo, Lætitia affectus, nihil aliud cupit quam eandem conservare, idque eo majore Cupiditate, quo Lætitia major erit. Denique, quoniam Odium et Amor sunt ipsi Tristitiæ vel Lætitiæ affectus, sequitur eodem modo, quod conatus, appetitus, sive Cupiditas, quæ præ Odio vel Amore oritur, major erit pro ratione Odii et Amoris. Q. E. D.\n\nPROPOSITIO XXXVIII\n\nSi quis rem amatam odio habere inceperit, ita ut Amor plane aboleatur, eandem majore odio, ex pari causa, prosequetur, quam si ipsam nunquam amavisset, et eo majori, quo Amor antea major fuerat.\n\nDEMONSTRATIO\n\nNam si quis rem, quam amat, odio habere incipit, plures ejus appetitus coercentur, quam si eandem non amavisset. Amor namque Lætitia est (per Schol. Prop. 13 hujus) quam homo, quantum potest (per Prop. 28 hujus), conservare conatur ; idque (per idem Schol.) rem amatam ut præsentem contemplando, eandemque (per Prop. 21 hujus) Lætitia, quantum potest, afficiendo ; qui quidem conatus (per Prop. præc.) eo est major quo amor major est, ut et conatus efficiendi, ut res amata ipsum contra amet (vid. Prop. 33 hujus). At hi conatus odio erga rem amatam coercentur (per Coroll. Prop. 13 et per Prop. 23 hujus) ; ergo amans (per Schol. Prop. 11 hujus) hac etiam de causa Tristitia afficietur, et eo majori, quo Amor major fuerat ; hoc est, præter Tristitiam, quæ Odii fuit causa, alia ex eo oritur, quod rem amavit, et consequenter majore Tristitiæ affectu rem amatam contemplabitur, hoc est (per Schol. Prop. 13 hujus) majori odio prosequetur, quam si eandem non amavisset, et eo majori, quo amor major fuerat. Q. E. D.\n\nPROPOSITIO XXXIX\n\nQui aliquem Odio habet, ei malum inferre conabitur, nisi ex eo majus sibi malum oriri timeat : et contra, qui aliquem amat, ei eadem lege benefacere conabitur.\n\nDEMONSTRATIO\n\nAliquem odio habere est (per Schol. Prop. 13 hujus) aliquem ut Tristitiæ causam imaginari ; adeoque (per Prop. 28 hujus) is, qui aliquem odio habet, eundem amovere vel destruere conabitur. Sed si inde aliquid tristius, sive (quod idem est) majus malum sibi timeat, idque se vitare posse credit, non inferendo ei, quem odit, malum, quod meditabatur, a malo inferendo (per eandem Prop. 28 hujus) abstinere cupiet ; idque (per Prop. 37 hujus) majore conatu, quam quo tenebatur inferendi malum, qui propterea prævalebit, ut volebamus. Secundæ partis demonstratio eodem modo procedit. Ergo qui aliquem odio habet etc. Q. E. D.\n\nSCHOLIUM\n\nPer bonum hic intelligo omne genus Lætitiæ, et quicquid porro ad eandem conducit, et, præcipue id, quod desiderio, qualecunque illud sit, satisfacit. Per malum autem omne Tristitiæ genus, et præcipue id, quod desiderium frustratur. Supra enim (in Schol. Prop. 9 hujus) ostendimus, nos nihil cupere, quia id bonum esse judicamus, sed contra id bonum vocamus, quod cupimus ; et consequenter id, quod aversamur, malum appellamus ; quare unusquisque ex suo affectu judicat seu æstimat, quid bonum, quid malum, quid melius, quid pejus, et quid denique optimum, quidve pessimum sit. Sic Avarus argenti copiam optimum, ejus autem inopiam pessimum judicat Ambitiosus autem nihil æque ac Gloriam cupit, et contra nihil æque ac Pudorem reformidat. Invido deinde nihil jucundius quam alterius infelicitas, et nihil molestius quam aliena felicitas ; ac sic unusquisque ex suo affectu rem aliquam bonam aut malam, utilem aut inutilem esse judicat. Cæterum hic affectus, quo homo ita disponitur, ut id, quod vult, nolit, vel ut id, quod non vult, velit, Timor vocatur, qui proinde nihil aliud est, quam metus quatenus homo ab eodem disponitur ad malum, quod futurum judicat, minore vitandum (Vid. Prop. 28 hujus). Sed si malum, quod timet, Pudor sit, tum Timor appellatur Verecundia. Denique si Cupiditas malum futurum vitandi coercetur Timore alterius mali, ita ut quid potius velit, nesciat, tum Metus vocatur Consternatio, præcipue si utrumque malum, quod timetur, ex maximis sit.\n\nPROPOSITIO XL\n\nQui se odio haberi ab aliquo imaginatur, nec se ullam odii causam illi dedisse credit, eundem odio contra habebit.\n\nDEMONSTRATIO\n\nQui aliquem odio affectum imaginatur, eo ipso etiam odio afficietur (per Prop. 27 hujus), hoc est (per Schol. Prop. 13 hujus), Tristitia concomitante idea causæ externæ. At ipse (per hypothesin) nullam hujus Tristitiæ causam imaginatur præter illum, ipsum odio habet ; ergo ex hoc, quod se odio haberi ab aliquo imaginatur, Tristitia afficietur concomitante idea ejus, qui ipsum odio habet, sive (per idem Schol.) eundem odio habebit. Q. E. D.\n\nSCHOLIUM\n\nQuod si se justam Odii causam præbuisse imaginatur, tum (per Prop. 30 hujus et ejusdem Schol.) Pudore afficietur. Sed hoc (per Prop. 25 hujus) raro contingit. Præterea hæc Odii reciprocatio oriri etiam potest ex eo, quod Odium sequatur conatus malum inferendi ei, qui odio habetur (per Prop. 39 hujus). Qui igitur se odio haberi ab aliquo imaginatur, eundem alicujus mali sive Tristitiæ causam imaginabitur ; atque adeo Tristitia afficietur, seu Metu, concomitante idea ejus, qui ipsum odio habet, tanquam causa, hoc est, odio contra afficietur, ut supra.\n\nCOROLLARIUM I\n\nQui, quem amat, odio erga se affectum imaginatur, Odio et Amore simul conflictabitur. Nam quatenus imaginatur, ab eodem se odio haberi, determinatur (per Prop. præced.) ad eundem contra odio habendum. At (per hypothesin) ipsum nihilominus amat : ergo Odio et Amore simul conflictabitur.\n\nCOROLLARIUM II\n\nSi aliquis imaginatur, ab aliquo, quem antea nullo affectu prosecutus est, malum aliquod præ Odio sibi illatum esse, statim idem malum eidem referre conabitur.\n\nDEMONSTRATIO\n\nQui aliquem Odio erga se affectum esse imaginatur, eum contra (per præced. Prop.) odio habebit, et (per Prop. 26 hujus) id omne comminisci conabitur, quod eundem possit Tristitia afficere, atque id eidem (per Prop. 39 hujus) inferre studebit. At (per hypothesin) primum, quod hujusmodi imaginatur, est malum sibi illatum ; ergo idem statim eidem inferre conabitur. Q. E. D.\n\nSCHOLIUM\n\nConatus malum inferendi ei, quem odimus, Ira vocatur ; conatus autem malum nobis illatum referendi Vindicta appellatur.\n\nPROPOSITIO XLI\n\nSi quis ab aliquo se amari imaginatur, nec se ullam ad id causam dedisse credit (quod per Coroll. Prop. 15 et per Prop. 16 hujus fieri potest), eundem contra amabit.\n\nDEMONSTRATIO\n\nHæc Propositio eadem via demonstratur ac præcedens. Cujus etiam Scholium vide.\n\nSCHOLIUM\n\nQuod si se justam Amoris causam præbuisse crediderit, gloriabitur (per Prop. 30 hujus cum ejusdem Schol.) ; quod quidem (per Prop. 25 hujus) frequentius contingit, et cujus contrarium evenire diximus, quando aliquis ab aliquo se odio haberi imaginatur (vide Schol. Prop. præced.). Porro hic reciprocus Amor, et consequenter (per Prop. 39 hujus) conatus benefaciendi ei, qui nos amat, quique (per eandem Prop. 39 hujus) nobis benefacere conatur, Gratia seu Gratitudo vocatur ; atque adeo apparet, homines longe paratiores esse ad Vindictam, quam ad referendum beneficium.\n\nCOROLLARIUM\n\nQui ab eo, quem odio habet, se amari imaginatur, Odio et Amore simul conflictabitur. Quod eadem via, qua primum præcedentis Coroll., demonstratur.\n\nSCHOLIUM\n\nQuod si Odium prævaluerit, ei, a quo amatur, malum inferre conabitur ; qui quidem affectus Crudelitas appellatur, præcipue si illum, qui amat, nullam Odii communem causam præbuisse creditur.\n\nPROPOSITIO XLII\n\nQui in aliquem, Amore aut spe Gloriae motus, beneficium contulit, contristabitur, si viderit, beneficium ingrato animo accipi.\n\nDEMONSTRATIO\n\nQui rem aliquam sibi similem amat, conatur, quantum potest, efficere, ut ab ipsa contra ametur (per Prop. 33 hujus). Qui igitur præ amore in aliquem beneficium contulit, id fecit desiderio, quo tenetur, ut contra ametur, hoc est (per Prop. 34 hujus) spe Gloriæ sive (per Schol. Prop. 30 hujus) Lætitiæ ; adeoque (per Prop. 12 hujus) hanc Gloriæ causam, quantum potest, imaginari, sive ut actu existentem contemplari conabitur. At (per hypothesin) aliud imaginatur, quod ejusdem causæ existentiam secludit : ergo (per Prop. 19 hujus) eo ipso contristabitur. Q. E. D.\n\nPROPOSITIO XLIII\n\nOdium reciproco odio augetur, et Amore contra deleri potest.\n\nDEMONSTRATIO\n\nQui eum, quem odit, Odio contra erga se affectum esse imaginatur eo ipso (per Prop. 40 hujus) novum Odium oritur, durante (per hypothesin) adhuc primo. Sed si contra eundem Amore erga se affectum esse imaginatur, quatenus hoc imaginatur, eatenus (per Prop. 30 hujus) se ipsum cum Lætitia contemplatur, et eatenus (per Prop. 29 hujus) eidem placere conabitur ; hoc est (per Prop. 41 hujus), eatenus conatur ipsum odio non habere, nullaque Tristitia afficere ; qui quidem conatus (per Prop. 37 hujus) major vel minor erit pro ratione affectus, ex quo oritur ; atque adeo si major fuerit illo, qui ex Odio oritur et quo rem, quam odit (per Prop. 26 hujus), Tristitia afficere conatur, ei prævalebit, et odium ex animo delebit. Q. E. D.\n\nPROPOSITIO XLIV\n\nOdium, quod Amore plane vincitur, in Amorem transit ; et Amor propterea major est, quam si Odium non præcessisset.\n\nDEMONSTRATIO\n\nEodem modo procedit ac Propositions 38 hujus. Nam qui rem, quam odit, sive quam cum Tristitia contemplari solebat, amare incipit, eo ipso, quod amat, lætatur, et huic Lætitiæ, quam Amor involvit (vide ejus Defin. in Schol. Prop. 13 hujus), illa etiam accedit quæ ex. eo oritur, quod conatus amovendi Tristitiam, quem Odium involvit (ut in Prop. 37 hujus ostendimus), prorsus juvatur, concomitante idea ejus, quem odio habuit, tanquam causa.\n\nSCHOLIUM\n\nQuamvis res ita se habeat, nemo tamen conabitur rem aliquam odio habere, vel Tristitia affici, ut majori hac Lætitia fruatur ; hoc est, nemo spe damnum recuperandi, damnum sibi inferri cupiet, nec ægrotare desiderabit spe convalescendi. Nam unusquisque suum esse conservare, et Tristitiam, quantum potest, amovere semper conabitur. Quod si contra concipi posset, hominem posse cupere aliquem odio habere, ut eum postea majori amore prosequatur, tum eundem odio habere semper desiderabit. Nam quo Odium majus fuerit, eo Amor erit major, atque adeo desiderabit semper, ut Odium magis magisque augeatur, et eadem de causa homo magis ac magis ægrotare conabitur, ut majori Laetitia ex restauranda valetudine postea fruatur ; atque adeo semper ægrotare conabitur, quod (per Prop. 6 hujus) est absurdum.\n\nPROPOSITIO XLV\n\nSi quis aliquem sibi similem Odio in rem sibi similem, quam amat, affectum esse imaginatur, eum odio habebit.\n\nDEMONSTRATIO\n\nNam res amata eum, qui ipsam odit, odio contra habet (per Prop. 40 hujus) : adeoque amans, qui aliquem imaginatur rem amatam odio habere, eo ipso rem amatam Odio hoc est (per Schol. Prop. 13 hujus) Tristitia, affectam esse imaginatur et consequenter (per Prop. 21 hujus) contristatur, idque concomitante idea ejus, qui rem amatam odit, tanquam causa ; hoc est (per Schol. Prop. 13 hujus) ipsum odio habebit. Q. E. D.\n\nPROPOSITIO XLVI\n\nSi quis ab aliquo cujusdam classis sive nationis a sua diversæ, Lætitia vel Tristitia affectus fuerit, concomitante ejus idea, sub nomine universali classis vel nationis, tanquam causa : is non tantum illum, sed omnes ejusdem classis vel nationis amabit vel odio habebit.\n\nDEMONSTRATIO\n\nHujus rei demonstratio patet ex Propositione 16 hujus Partis.\n\nPROPOSITIO XLVII\n\nLætitia quæ ex eo oritur, quod scilicet rem, quam odimus, destrui aut alio malo affici imaginamur, non oritur absque ulla animi Tristitia.\n\nDEMONSTRATIO\n\nPatet ex Prop. 27 hujus. Nam quatenus rem nobis similem Tristitia affici imaginamur, eatenus constristamur.\n\nSCHOLIUM\n\nPotest hæc Propositio etiam demonstrari ex Corollario Propositionis 17 Partis II. Quoties enim rei recordamur, quamvis ipsa actu non existat, eandem tamen ut præsentem contemplamur, Corpusque eodem modo afficitur ; quare quatenus rei memoria viget, eatenus homo determinatur ad eandem cum Tristitia contemplandum, quæ determinatio, manente adhuc rei imagine, coercetur quidem memoria illarum rerum, quæ hujus existentiam secludunt, sed non tollitur : atque adeo homo eatenus tantum lætatur, quatenus hæc determinatio coercetur ; et hinc fit, ut hæc Laetitia, quæ ex rei, quam odimus, malo oritur, toties repetatur, quoties ejusdem rei recordamur. Nam, uti diximus, quando ejusdem rei imago excitatur, quia hæc ipsius rei existentiam involvit, hominem determinat ad rem cum eadem Tristitia contemplandum, qua eandem contemplari solebat, cum ipsa existeret. Sed quia ejusdem rei imagini alias junxit, quæ ejusdem existentiam secludunt, ideo hæc ad Tristitiam determinatio statim coercetur, et homo de novo lætatur, et hoc toties, quoties hæc repetitio fit. Atque hæc eadem est causa, cur homines lætantur, quoties alicujus jam præteriti mali recordantur, et cur pericula, a quibus liberati sunt, narrare gaudeant. Nam ubi aliquod periculum imaginantur, idem veluti adhuc futurum contemplantur, et ad id metuendum determinantur ; quæ determinatio de novo coercetur idea libertatis, quam hujus periculi ideæ junxerunt, cum ab eodem liberati sunt, quæque eos de novo securos reddit ; atque adeo de novo lætantur.\n\nPROPOSITIO XLVIII\n\nAmor et Odium ex. gr. erga Petrum destruitur, si Tristitia, quam hoc, et Lætitia, quam ille involvit, ideæ alterius causæ jungatur ; et eatenus uterque diminuitur, quatenus imaginamur Petrum non solum fuisse alterutrius causam.\n\nDEMONSTRATIO\n\nPatet ex sola Amoris et Odii definitione ; quam vide in Schol. Prop. 13 hujus. Nam propter hoc solum Lætitia vocatur Amor, et Tristitia Odium erga Petrum, quia scilicet Petrus hujus vel illius affectus causa esse consideratur. Hoc itaque prorsus vel ex parte sublato, affectus quoque erga Petrum prorsus vel parte diminuitur. Q. E. D.\n\nPROPOSITIO XLIX\n\nAmor et Odium erga rem, quam liberam esse imaginamur, major ex pari causa uterque debet esse, quam erga necessariam.\n\nDEMONSTRATIO\n\nRes, quam liberam esse imaginamur, debet (per Defin. 7 p. I) per se absque aliis percipi. Si igitur eandem Lætitiæ vel Tristitiæ causam esse imaginemur, eo ipso (per Schol. Prop. 13 hujus) eandem amabimus vel odio habebimus, idque (per Prop. præced.) summo Amore vel Odio, qui ex dato affectu oriri potest. Sed si rem, quæ ejusdem affectus est causa ut necessariam imaginemur, tum (per eandem Defin. 7 p. I) ipsam non solam, sed cum aliis ejusdem affectus causam esse imaginabimur, atque adeo (per Prop. præc.) Amor et Odium erga ipsam minor erit. Q. E. D.\n\nSCHOLIUM\n\nHinc sequitur, homines, quia se liberos esse existimant, majore Amore vel odio se invicem prosequi, quam alia ; ad quod accedit affectuum imitatio, de qua vide Prop. 27, 34, 40 et 43 hujus.\n\nPROPOSITIO L\n\nRes quæcunque potest esse per accidens Spei aut Metus causa.\n\nDEMONSTRATIO\n\nHæc Propositio eadem via demonstratur, qua Propositio 15 hujus quam vide una cum Schol. 2 Propositionis 18 hujus.\n\nSCHOLIUM\n\nRes, quæ per accidens Spei aut Metus sunt causæ, bona aut mala omina vocantur. Deinde quatenus hæc eadem omina sunt Spei aut Metus causa, eatenus (per Defin. Spei et Metus, quam vide in Schol. 2 Prop. 18 hujus) Lætitiæ aut Tristitiæ sunt causa, et consequenter (per Coroll. Prop. 15 hujus) eatenus eadem amamus vel odio habemus, et (per Prop. 28 hujus) tanquam media ad ea, quæ speramus adhibere, vel tanquam obstacula aut Metus causas amovere conamur. Præterea ex Propositione 25 hujus sequitur, nos natura ita esse constitutos, ut ea, quæ speramus, facile, quæ autem timemus, difficile credamus, et ut de iis plus minusve justo sentiamus. Atque ex his ortæ sunt Superstitiones, quibus homines ubique conflictantur. Cæterum non puto operæ esse pretium, animi hic ostendere fluctuationes, quæ ex Spe et Metu oriuntur ; quandoquidem ex sola horum affectuum definitione sequitur, non dari Spem sine Metu, neque Metum sine Spe (ut fusius suo loco explicabimus) ; et præterea quandoquidem, quatenus aliquid speramus aut metuimus, eatenus idem amamus vel odio habemus ; atque adeo quicquid de Amore et Odio diximus, facile unusquisque Spei et Metui applicare poterit.\n\nPROPOSITIO LI\n\nDiversi homines ab uno eodemque objecto diversimode affici possunt, et unus idemque homo ab uno eodemque objecto potest diversis temporibus diversimode affici.\n\nDEMONSTRATIO\n\nCorpus humanum (per Post. 3 p. II) a corporibus externis plurimis modis afficitur. Possunt igitur eodem tempore duo homines diversimode esse affecti ; atque adeo (per Axiom. 1, quod est post Lemma 3, quod vide post Prop. 13 p. II) ab uno eodemque objecto possunt diversimode affici. Deinde (per idem Postul.) Corpus humanum potest jam hoc jam alio modo esse affectum ; et consequenter (per idem Axiom.) ab uno eodemque objecto diversis temporibus diversimode affici. Q. E. D.\n\nSCHOLIUM","paragraphs":[{"id":"RAW_QGOH_PT_3:P0001","text":"Videmus itaque, cum hominum natura plerumque ita comparatum esse, ut eorum, quibus male est, misereantur, et quibus bene est, invideant, et (per Prop. præc.) eo majore odio, quo rem, qua alium potiri imaginantur, magis amant. Videmus deinde, ex eadem naturæ humanæ proprietate, ex qua sequitur, homines esse misericordes, sequi etiam, eosdem esse invidos et ambitiosos. Denique, si ipsam experientiam consulere velimus, ipsam hæc omnia docere experiemur ; præsertim si ad priores nostræ ætatis annos attenderimus.","kind":"passage"},{"id":"RAW_QGOH_PT_3:P0002","text":"Nam pueros, quia eorum corpus continuo veluti in æquilibrio est, ex hoc solo ridere vel flere experimur, quod alios ridere vel flere vident ; et quicquid præterea vident alios facere, id imitari statim cupiunt, et omnia denique sibi cupiunt quibus alios delectari imaginantur ; nimirum quia rerum imagines, ut diximus, sunt ipsæ humani Corporis affectiones, sive modi, quibus Corpus humanum a causis externis afficitur disponiturque ad hoc vel illud agendum.","kind":"passage"},{"id":"RAW_QGOH_PT_3:P0003","text":"PROPOSITIO XXXIII","kind":"passage"},{"id":"RAW_QGOH_PT_3:P0004","text":"Cum rem nobis similem amamus, conamur, quantum possumus, efficere, ut nos contra amet.","kind":"passage"},{"id":"RAW_QGOH_PT_3:P0005","text":"DEMONSTRATIO","kind":"passage"},{"id":"RAW_QGOH_PT_3:P0006","text":"Rem, quam amamus præ reliquis, quantum possumus, imaginari conamur (per Prop. 12 hujus). Si igitur res nobis sit similis, ipsam præ reliquis Lætitia afficere conabimur (per Prop. 29 hujus), sive conabimur, quantum possumus, efficere, ut res amata Lætitia afficiatur concomitante idea nostri, hoc est (per Schol. Prop. 13 hujus), ut nos contra amet. Q. E. D.","kind":"passage"},{"id":"RAW_QGOH_PT_3:P0007","text":"PROPOSITIO XXXIV","kind":"passage"},{"id":"RAW_QGOH_PT_3:P0008","text":"Quo majori affectu rem amatam erga nos affectam esse imaginamur, eo magis gloriabimur.","kind":"passage"},{"id":"RAW_QGOH_PT_3:P0009","text":"DEMONSTRATIO","kind":"passage"},{"id":"RAW_QGOH_PT_3:P0010","text":"Nos (per Prop. præced.) conamur, quantum possumus, ut res amata. nos contra amet ; hoc est (per Schol. Prop. 13 hujus), ut res amata Lætitia afficiatur concomitante idea nostri. Quo itaque rem amatam majori Lætitia nostra de causa affectam esse imaginamur, eo magis hic conatus juvatur ; hoc est (per Prop. 11 hujus cum ejus Schol.), eo majore Lætitia afficimur. At cum ex eo lætemur, quod alium nobis similem Lætitia affecimus, tum nosmet cum Lætitia contemplamur (per Prop. 30 hujus) : ergo quo majori affectu rem amatam erga nos affectam esse imaginamur, eo majori Lætitia nosmet contemplabimur, sive (per Schol. Prop. 30 hujus) eo magis gloriabimur. Q. E. D.","kind":"passage"},{"id":"RAW_QGOH_PT_3:P0011","text":"PROPOSITIO XXXV","kind":"passage"},{"id":"RAW_QGOH_PT_3:P0012","text":"Si quis imaginatur rem amatam eodem vel arctiore vinculo Amicitiæ, quo ipse eadem solus potiebatur, alium sibi jungere, Odio erga ipsam rem amatam afficietur, et illi alteri invidebit.","kind":"passage"},{"id":"RAW_QGOH_PT_3:P0013","text":"DEMONSTRATIO","kind":"passage"},{"id":"RAW_QGOH_PT_3:P0014","text":"Quo quis majore amore rem amatam erga se affectam esse imaginatur, eo magis gloriabitur (per præced. Prop.), hoc est (per Schol. Prop. 30 hujus) lætabitur ; adeoque (per Prop. 28 hujus) conabitur, quantum potest, imaginarirem amatam ipsi quum arctissime devinctam ; qui quidem conatus sive appetitus fomentatur, si alium idem sibi cupere imaginatur (per Prop. 31 hujus). At hic conatus sive appetitus ab ipsius rei amatæ imagine concomitante imagine illius, quem res amata sibi jungit coerceri supponitur ; ergo (per Schol. Prop. 11 hujus) eo ipso Tristitia afficietur, concomitante idea rei amatæ tanquam causa, et simul imagine alterius, hoc est (per Schol. Prop. 13 hujus), odio erga rem amatam afficietur, et simul erga illum alterum (per Coroll. Prop. 15 hujus), cui propterea (per Prop. 23 hujus), quod re amata delectatur, invidebit. Q. E. D.","kind":"passage"},{"id":"RAW_QGOH_PT_3:P0015","text":"SCHOLIUM","kind":"passage"},{"id":"RAW_QGOH_PT_3:P0016","text":"Hoc Odium erga rem amatam Invidiæ junctum Zelotypia vocatur, quæ proinde nihil aliud est, quam animi fluctuatio orta ex Amore et Odio simul, concomitante idea alterius, cui invidetur. Præterea hoc Odium erga rem amatam majus erit pro ratione Lætitiæ, qua Zelotypus ex reciproco rei amatæ Amore solebat affici, et etiam pro ratione affectus, quo erga illum, quem sibi rem amatam jungere imaginatur, affectus erat. Nam si eum oderat, eo ipso rem amatam (per Prop. 24 hujus) odio habebit, quia ipsam id, quod ipse odio habet, Lætitia afficere imaginatur ; et etiam (per Coroll. Prop. 15 hujus) ex eo, quod rei amatæ imaginem imagini ejus, quem odit, jungere cogitur.","kind":"passage"},{"id":"RAW_QGOH_PT_3:P0017","text":"Quæ ratio plerumque locum habet in Amore erga fæminam ; qui enim imaginatur mulierem, quam amat, alteri sese prostituere, non solum ex eo, quod ipsius appetitus coercetur, contristabitur, sed etiam, quia rei amatæ imaginem pudendis et excrementis alterius jungere cogitur, eandem aversatur ; ad quod denique accedit, quod Zelotypus non eodem vultu, quem res amata ei præbere solebat, ab eadem excipiatur, qua etiam de causa amans contristatur, ut jam ostendam.","kind":"passage"},{"id":"RAW_QGOH_PT_3:P0018","text":"PROPOSITIO XXXVI","kind":"passage"},{"id":"RAW_QGOH_PT_3:P0019","text":"Qui rei, qua semel delectatusest, recordatur, cupit eamdem cum iisdem potiri circumstantiis, ac cum primo ipsa delectatus est.","kind":"passage"},{"id":"RAW_QGOH_PT_3:P0020","text":"DEMONSTRATIO","kind":"passage"},{"id":"RAW_QGOH_PT_3:P0021","text":"Quicquid homo simul cum re, quæ ipsum delectavit, vidit, id omne (per Prop. 15 hujus) eri per accidens Lætitiae causa ; adeoque (per Prop. 28 hujus) omni eo simul cum re, quæ ipsum delectavit, potiri cupiet, sive re cum omnibus iisdem circumstantiis potiri cupiet, ac cum primo eadem delectatus est. Q. E. D.","kind":"passage"},{"id":"RAW_QGOH_PT_3:P0022","text":"COROLLARIUM","kind":"passage"},{"id":"RAW_QGOH_PT_3:P0023","text":"Si itaque unam ex iis circumstantiis deficere compererit, amans contristabitur.","kind":"passage"},{"id":"RAW_QGOH_PT_3:P0024","text":"DEMONSTRATIO","kind":"passage"},{"id":"RAW_QGOH_PT_3:P0025","text":"Nam quatenus aliquam circumstantiam deficere comperit, eatenus aliquid imaginatur, quod ejus rei existentiam secludit. Cum autem ejus rei sive circumstantiæ (per Prop. præc.) sit præ amore cupidus, ergo (per Prop. 19 hujus), quatenus eandem deficere imaginatur, contristabitur. Q. E. D.","kind":"passage"},{"id":"RAW_QGOH_PT_3:P0026","text":"SCHOLIUM","kind":"passage"},{"id":"RAW_QGOH_PT_3:P0027","text":"Hæc Tristitia, quatenus absentiam ejus, quod amamus, respicit, Desiderium vocatur.","kind":"passage"},{"id":"RAW_QGOH_PT_3:P0028","text":"PROPOSITIO XXXVII","kind":"passage"},{"id":"RAW_QGOH_PT_3:P0029","text":"Cupiditas, quæ præ Tristitia vel Lætitia præque Odio vel Amore oritur, eo est major, quo affectus major est.","kind":"passage"},{"id":"RAW_QGOH_PT_3:P0030","text":"DEMONSTRATIO","kind":"passage"},{"id":"RAW_QGOH_PT_3:P0031","text":"Tristitia hominis agendi potentiam (per Schol. Prop. 11 hujus) minuit vel coercet, hoc est (per Prop. 7 hujus), conatum, quo homo in suo esse perseverare conatur, minuit vel coercet ; adeoque (per Prop. 5 hujus) huic conatui est contraria ; et quicquid homo Tristitia affectus conatur est Tristitiam amovere. At (per Tristitiæ Defin.) quo Tristitia major est, eo majori parti hominis agendi potentiæ necesse est opponi ; ergo quo major Tristitia est, eo majore agendi potentia conabitur homo contra Tristitiam amovere ; hoc est (per Schol. Prop. 9 hujus), eo majore cupiditate sive appetitu conabitur Tristitiam amovere. Deinde, quoniam Lætitia (per idem Schol. Prop. 11 hujus) hominis agendi potentiam auget vel juvat, facile eadem via demonstratur, quod homo, Lætitia affectus, nihil aliud cupit quam eandem conservare, idque eo majore Cupiditate, quo Lætitia major erit.","kind":"passage"},{"id":"RAW_QGOH_PT_3:P0032","text":"Denique, quoniam Odium et Amor sunt ipsi Tristitiæ vel Lætitiæ affectus, sequitur eodem modo, quod conatus, appetitus, sive Cupiditas, quæ præ Odio vel Amore oritur, major erit pro ratione Odii et Amoris. Q. E. D.","kind":"passage"},{"id":"RAW_QGOH_PT_3:P0033","text":"PROPOSITIO XXXVIII","kind":"passage"},{"id":"RAW_QGOH_PT_3:P0034","text":"Si quis rem amatam odio habere inceperit, ita ut Amor plane aboleatur, eandem majore odio, ex pari causa, prosequetur, quam si ipsam nunquam amavisset, et eo majori, quo Amor antea major fuerat.","kind":"passage"},{"id":"RAW_QGOH_PT_3:P0035","text":"DEMONSTRATIO","kind":"passage"},{"id":"RAW_QGOH_PT_3:P0036","text":"Nam si quis rem, quam amat, odio habere incipit, plures ejus appetitus coercentur, quam si eandem non amavisset. Amor namque Lætitia est (per Schol. Prop. 13 hujus) quam homo, quantum potest (per Prop. 28 hujus), conservare conatur ; idque (per idem Schol.) rem amatam ut præsentem contemplando, eandemque (per Prop. 21 hujus) Lætitia, quantum potest, afficiendo ; qui quidem conatus (per Prop. præc.) eo est major quo amor major est, ut et conatus efficiendi, ut res amata ipsum contra amet (vid. Prop. 33 hujus). At hi conatus odio erga rem amatam coercentur (per Coroll. Prop. 13 et per Prop. 23 hujus) ; ergo amans (per Schol. Prop. 11 hujus) hac etiam de causa Tristitia afficietur, et eo majori, quo Amor major fuerat ; hoc est, præter Tristitiam, quæ Odii fuit causa, alia ex eo oritur, quod rem amavit, et consequenter majore Tristitiæ affectu rem amatam contemplabitur, hoc est (per Schol.","kind":"passage"},{"id":"RAW_QGOH_PT_3:P0037","text":"Prop. 13 hujus) majori odio prosequetur, quam si eandem non amavisset, et eo majori, quo amor major fuerat. Q. E. D.","kind":"passage"},{"id":"RAW_QGOH_PT_3:P0038","text":"PROPOSITIO XXXIX","kind":"passage"},{"id":"RAW_QGOH_PT_3:P0039","text":"Qui aliquem Odio habet, ei malum inferre conabitur, nisi ex eo majus sibi malum oriri timeat : et contra, qui aliquem amat, ei eadem lege benefacere conabitur.","kind":"passage"},{"id":"RAW_QGOH_PT_3:P0040","text":"DEMONSTRATIO","kind":"passage"},{"id":"RAW_QGOH_PT_3:P0041","text":"Aliquem odio habere est (per Schol. Prop. 13 hujus) aliquem ut Tristitiæ causam imaginari ; adeoque (per Prop. 28 hujus) is, qui aliquem odio habet, eundem amovere vel destruere conabitur. Sed si inde aliquid tristius, sive (quod idem est) majus malum sibi timeat, idque se vitare posse credit, non inferendo ei, quem odit, malum, quod meditabatur, a malo inferendo (per eandem Prop. 28 hujus) abstinere cupiet ; idque (per Prop. 37 hujus) majore conatu, quam quo tenebatur inferendi malum, qui propterea prævalebit, ut volebamus. Secundæ partis demonstratio eodem modo procedit. Ergo qui aliquem odio habet etc. Q. E. D.","kind":"passage"},{"id":"RAW_QGOH_PT_3:P0042","text":"SCHOLIUM","kind":"passage"},{"id":"RAW_QGOH_PT_3:P0043","text":"Per bonum hic intelligo omne genus Lætitiæ, et quicquid porro ad eandem conducit, et, præcipue id, quod desiderio, qualecunque illud sit, satisfacit. Per malum autem omne Tristitiæ genus, et præcipue id, quod desiderium frustratur. Supra enim (in Schol. Prop. 9 hujus) ostendimus, nos nihil cupere, quia id bonum esse judicamus, sed contra id bonum vocamus, quod cupimus ; et consequenter id, quod aversamur, malum appellamus ; quare unusquisque ex suo affectu judicat seu æstimat, quid bonum, quid malum, quid melius, quid pejus, et quid denique optimum, quidve pessimum sit. Sic Avarus argenti copiam optimum, ejus autem inopiam pessimum judicat Ambitiosus autem nihil æque ac Gloriam cupit, et contra nihil æque ac Pudorem reformidat.","kind":"passage"},{"id":"RAW_QGOH_PT_3:P0044","text":"Invido deinde nihil jucundius quam alterius infelicitas, et nihil molestius quam aliena felicitas ; ac sic unusquisque ex suo affectu rem aliquam bonam aut malam, utilem aut inutilem esse judicat. Cæterum hic affectus, quo homo ita disponitur, ut id, quod vult, nolit, vel ut id, quod non vult, velit, Timor vocatur, qui proinde nihil aliud est, quam metus quatenus homo ab eodem disponitur ad malum, quod futurum judicat, minore vitandum (Vid. Prop. 28 hujus). Sed si malum, quod timet, Pudor sit, tum Timor appellatur Verecundia. Denique si Cupiditas malum futurum vitandi coercetur Timore alterius mali, ita ut quid potius velit, nesciat, tum Metus vocatur Consternatio, præcipue si utrumque malum, quod timetur, ex maximis sit.","kind":"passage"},{"id":"RAW_QGOH_PT_3:P0045","text":"PROPOSITIO XL","kind":"passage"},{"id":"RAW_QGOH_PT_3:P0046","text":"Qui se odio haberi ab aliquo imaginatur, nec se ullam odii causam illi dedisse credit, eundem odio contra habebit.","kind":"passage"},{"id":"RAW_QGOH_PT_3:P0047","text":"DEMONSTRATIO","kind":"passage"},{"id":"RAW_QGOH_PT_3:P0048","text":"Qui aliquem odio affectum imaginatur, eo ipso etiam odio afficietur (per Prop. 27 hujus), hoc est (per Schol. Prop. 13 hujus), Tristitia concomitante idea causæ externæ. At ipse (per hypothesin) nullam hujus Tristitiæ causam imaginatur præter illum, ipsum odio habet ; ergo ex hoc, quod se odio haberi ab aliquo imaginatur, Tristitia afficietur concomitante idea ejus, qui ipsum odio habet, sive (per idem Schol.) eundem odio habebit. Q. E. D.","kind":"passage"},{"id":"RAW_QGOH_PT_3:P0049","text":"SCHOLIUM","kind":"passage"},{"id":"RAW_QGOH_PT_3:P0050","text":"Quod si se justam Odii causam præbuisse imaginatur, tum (per Prop. 30 hujus et ejusdem Schol.) Pudore afficietur. Sed hoc (per Prop. 25 hujus) raro contingit. Præterea hæc Odii reciprocatio oriri etiam potest ex eo, quod Odium sequatur conatus malum inferendi ei, qui odio habetur (per Prop. 39 hujus). Qui igitur se odio haberi ab aliquo imaginatur, eundem alicujus mali sive Tristitiæ causam imaginabitur ; atque adeo Tristitia afficietur, seu Metu, concomitante idea ejus, qui ipsum odio habet, tanquam causa, hoc est, odio contra afficietur, ut supra.","kind":"passage"},{"id":"RAW_QGOH_PT_3:P0051","text":"COROLLARIUM I","kind":"passage"},{"id":"RAW_QGOH_PT_3:P0052","text":"Qui, quem amat, odio erga se affectum imaginatur, Odio et Amore simul conflictabitur. Nam quatenus imaginatur, ab eodem se odio haberi, determinatur (per Prop. præced.) ad eundem contra odio habendum. At (per hypothesin) ipsum nihilominus amat : ergo Odio et Amore simul conflictabitur.","kind":"passage"},{"id":"RAW_QGOH_PT_3:P0053","text":"COROLLARIUM II","kind":"passage"},{"id":"RAW_QGOH_PT_3:P0054","text":"Si aliquis imaginatur, ab aliquo, quem antea nullo affectu prosecutus est, malum aliquod præ Odio sibi illatum esse, statim idem malum eidem referre conabitur.","kind":"passage"},{"id":"RAW_QGOH_PT_3:P0055","text":"DEMONSTRATIO","kind":"passage"},{"id":"RAW_QGOH_PT_3:P0056","text":"Qui aliquem Odio erga se affectum esse imaginatur, eum contra (per præced. Prop.) odio habebit, et (per Prop. 26 hujus) id omne comminisci conabitur, quod eundem possit Tristitia afficere, atque id eidem (per Prop. 39 hujus) inferre studebit. At (per hypothesin) primum, quod hujusmodi imaginatur, est malum sibi illatum ; ergo idem statim eidem inferre conabitur. Q. E. D.","kind":"passage"},{"id":"RAW_QGOH_PT_3:P0057","text":"SCHOLIUM","kind":"passage"},{"id":"RAW_QGOH_PT_3:P0058","text":"Conatus malum inferendi ei, quem odimus, Ira vocatur ; conatus autem malum nobis illatum referendi Vindicta appellatur.","kind":"passage"},{"id":"RAW_QGOH_PT_3:P0059","text":"PROPOSITIO XLI","kind":"passage"},{"id":"RAW_QGOH_PT_3:P0060","text":"Si quis ab aliquo se amari imaginatur, nec se ullam ad id causam dedisse credit (quod per Coroll. Prop. 15 et per Prop. 16 hujus fieri potest), eundem contra amabit.","kind":"passage"},{"id":"RAW_QGOH_PT_3:P0061","text":"DEMONSTRATIO","kind":"passage"},{"id":"RAW_QGOH_PT_3:P0062","text":"Hæc Propositio eadem via demonstratur ac præcedens. Cujus etiam Scholium vide.","kind":"passage"},{"id":"RAW_QGOH_PT_3:P0063","text":"SCHOLIUM","kind":"passage"},{"id":"RAW_QGOH_PT_3:P0064","text":"Quod si se justam Amoris causam præbuisse crediderit, gloriabitur (per Prop. 30 hujus cum ejusdem Schol.) ; quod quidem (per Prop. 25 hujus) frequentius contingit, et cujus contrarium evenire diximus, quando aliquis ab aliquo se odio haberi imaginatur (vide Schol. Prop. præced.). Porro hic reciprocus Amor, et consequenter (per Prop. 39 hujus) conatus benefaciendi ei, qui nos amat, quique (per eandem Prop. 39 hujus) nobis benefacere conatur, Gratia seu Gratitudo vocatur ; atque adeo apparet, homines longe paratiores esse ad Vindictam, quam ad referendum beneficium.","kind":"passage"},{"id":"RAW_QGOH_PT_3:P0065","text":"COROLLARIUM","kind":"passage"},{"id":"RAW_QGOH_PT_3:P0066","text":"Qui ab eo, quem odio habet, se amari imaginatur, Odio et Amore simul conflictabitur. Quod eadem via, qua primum præcedentis Coroll., demonstratur.","kind":"passage"},{"id":"RAW_QGOH_PT_3:P0067","text":"SCHOLIUM","kind":"passage"},{"id":"RAW_QGOH_PT_3:P0068","text":"Quod si Odium prævaluerit, ei, a quo amatur, malum inferre conabitur ; qui quidem affectus Crudelitas appellatur, præcipue si illum, qui amat, nullam Odii communem causam præbuisse creditur.","kind":"passage"},{"id":"RAW_QGOH_PT_3:P0069","text":"PROPOSITIO XLII","kind":"passage"},{"id":"RAW_QGOH_PT_3:P0070","text":"Qui in aliquem, Amore aut spe Gloriae motus, beneficium contulit, contristabitur, si viderit, beneficium ingrato animo accipi.","kind":"passage"},{"id":"RAW_QGOH_PT_3:P0071","text":"DEMONSTRATIO","kind":"passage"},{"id":"RAW_QGOH_PT_3:P0072","text":"Qui rem aliquam sibi similem amat, conatur, quantum potest, efficere, ut ab ipsa contra ametur (per Prop. 33 hujus). Qui igitur præ amore in aliquem beneficium contulit, id fecit desiderio, quo tenetur, ut contra ametur, hoc est (per Prop. 34 hujus) spe Gloriæ sive (per Schol. Prop. 30 hujus) Lætitiæ ; adeoque (per Prop. 12 hujus) hanc Gloriæ causam, quantum potest, imaginari, sive ut actu existentem contemplari conabitur. At (per hypothesin) aliud imaginatur, quod ejusdem causæ existentiam secludit : ergo (per Prop. 19 hujus) eo ipso contristabitur. Q. E. D.","kind":"passage"},{"id":"RAW_QGOH_PT_3:P0073","text":"PROPOSITIO XLIII","kind":"passage"},{"id":"RAW_QGOH_PT_3:P0074","text":"Odium reciproco odio augetur, et Amore contra deleri potest.","kind":"passage"},{"id":"RAW_QGOH_PT_3:P0075","text":"DEMONSTRATIO","kind":"passage"},{"id":"RAW_QGOH_PT_3:P0076","text":"Qui eum, quem odit, Odio contra erga se affectum esse imaginatur eo ipso (per Prop. 40 hujus) novum Odium oritur, durante (per hypothesin) adhuc primo. Sed si contra eundem Amore erga se affectum esse imaginatur, quatenus hoc imaginatur, eatenus (per Prop. 30 hujus) se ipsum cum Lætitia contemplatur, et eatenus (per Prop. 29 hujus) eidem placere conabitur ; hoc est (per Prop. 41 hujus), eatenus conatur ipsum odio non habere, nullaque Tristitia afficere ; qui quidem conatus (per Prop. 37 hujus) major vel minor erit pro ratione affectus, ex quo oritur ; atque adeo si major fuerit illo, qui ex Odio oritur et quo rem, quam odit (per Prop. 26 hujus), Tristitia afficere conatur, ei prævalebit, et odium ex animo delebit. Q. E. D.","kind":"passage"},{"id":"RAW_QGOH_PT_3:P0077","text":"PROPOSITIO XLIV","kind":"passage"},{"id":"RAW_QGOH_PT_3:P0078","text":"Odium, quod Amore plane vincitur, in Amorem transit ; et Amor propterea major est, quam si Odium non præcessisset.","kind":"passage"},{"id":"RAW_QGOH_PT_3:P0079","text":"DEMONSTRATIO","kind":"passage"},{"id":"RAW_QGOH_PT_3:P0080","text":"Eodem modo procedit ac Propositions 38 hujus. Nam qui rem, quam odit, sive quam cum Tristitia contemplari solebat, amare incipit, eo ipso, quod amat, lætatur, et huic Lætitiæ, quam Amor involvit (vide ejus Defin. in Schol. Prop. 13 hujus), illa etiam accedit quæ ex. eo oritur, quod conatus amovendi Tristitiam, quem Odium involvit (ut in Prop. 37 hujus ostendimus), prorsus juvatur, concomitante idea ejus, quem odio habuit, tanquam causa.","kind":"passage"},{"id":"RAW_QGOH_PT_3:P0081","text":"SCHOLIUM","kind":"passage"},{"id":"RAW_QGOH_PT_3:P0082","text":"Quamvis res ita se habeat, nemo tamen conabitur rem aliquam odio habere, vel Tristitia affici, ut majori hac Lætitia fruatur ; hoc est, nemo spe damnum recuperandi, damnum sibi inferri cupiet, nec ægrotare desiderabit spe convalescendi. Nam unusquisque suum esse conservare, et Tristitiam, quantum potest, amovere semper conabitur. Quod si contra concipi posset, hominem posse cupere aliquem odio habere, ut eum postea majori amore prosequatur, tum eundem odio habere semper desiderabit. Nam quo Odium majus fuerit, eo Amor erit major, atque adeo desiderabit semper, ut Odium magis magisque augeatur, et eadem de causa homo magis ac magis ægrotare conabitur, ut majori Laetitia ex restauranda valetudine postea fruatur ; atque adeo semper ægrotare conabitur, quod (per Prop. 6 hujus) est absurdum.","kind":"passage"},{"id":"RAW_QGOH_PT_3:P0083","text":"PROPOSITIO XLV","kind":"passage"},{"id":"RAW_QGOH_PT_3:P0084","text":"Si quis aliquem sibi similem Odio in rem sibi similem, quam amat, affectum esse imaginatur, eum odio habebit.","kind":"passage"},{"id":"RAW_QGOH_PT_3:P0085","text":"DEMONSTRATIO","kind":"passage"},{"id":"RAW_QGOH_PT_3:P0086","text":"Nam res amata eum, qui ipsam odit, odio contra habet (per Prop. 40 hujus) : adeoque amans, qui aliquem imaginatur rem amatam odio habere, eo ipso rem amatam Odio hoc est (per Schol. Prop. 13 hujus) Tristitia, affectam esse imaginatur et consequenter (per Prop. 21 hujus) contristatur, idque concomitante idea ejus, qui rem amatam odit, tanquam causa ; hoc est (per Schol. Prop. 13 hujus) ipsum odio habebit. Q. E. D.","kind":"passage"},{"id":"RAW_QGOH_PT_3:P0087","text":"PROPOSITIO XLVI","kind":"passage"},{"id":"RAW_QGOH_PT_3:P0088","text":"Si quis ab aliquo cujusdam classis sive nationis a sua diversæ, Lætitia vel Tristitia affectus fuerit, concomitante ejus idea, sub nomine universali classis vel nationis, tanquam causa : is non tantum illum, sed omnes ejusdem classis vel nationis amabit vel odio habebit.","kind":"passage"},{"id":"RAW_QGOH_PT_3:P0089","text":"DEMONSTRATIO","kind":"passage"},{"id":"RAW_QGOH_PT_3:P0090","text":"Hujus rei demonstratio patet ex Propositione 16 hujus Partis.","kind":"passage"},{"id":"RAW_QGOH_PT_3:P0091","text":"PROPOSITIO XLVII","kind":"passage"},{"id":"RAW_QGOH_PT_3:P0092","text":"Lætitia quæ ex eo oritur, quod scilicet rem, quam odimus, destrui aut alio malo affici imaginamur, non oritur absque ulla animi Tristitia.","kind":"passage"},{"id":"RAW_QGOH_PT_3:P0093","text":"DEMONSTRATIO","kind":"passage"},{"id":"RAW_QGOH_PT_3:P0094","text":"Patet ex Prop. 27 hujus. Nam quatenus rem nobis similem Tristitia affici imaginamur, eatenus constristamur.","kind":"passage"},{"id":"RAW_QGOH_PT_3:P0095","text":"SCHOLIUM","kind":"passage"},{"id":"RAW_QGOH_PT_3:P0096","text":"Potest hæc Propositio etiam demonstrari ex Corollario Propositionis 17 Partis II. Quoties enim rei recordamur, quamvis ipsa actu non existat, eandem tamen ut præsentem contemplamur, Corpusque eodem modo afficitur ; quare quatenus rei memoria viget, eatenus homo determinatur ad eandem cum Tristitia contemplandum, quæ determinatio, manente adhuc rei imagine, coercetur quidem memoria illarum rerum, quæ hujus existentiam secludunt, sed non tollitur : atque adeo homo eatenus tantum lætatur, quatenus hæc determinatio coercetur ; et hinc fit, ut hæc Laetitia, quæ ex rei, quam odimus, malo oritur, toties repetatur, quoties ejusdem rei recordamur. Nam, uti diximus, quando ejusdem rei imago excitatur, quia hæc ipsius rei existentiam involvit, hominem determinat ad rem cum eadem Tristitia contemplandum, qua eandem contemplari solebat, cum ipsa existeret.","kind":"passage"},{"id":"RAW_QGOH_PT_3:P0097","text":"Sed quia ejusdem rei imagini alias junxit, quæ ejusdem existentiam secludunt, ideo hæc ad Tristitiam determinatio statim coercetur, et homo de novo lætatur, et hoc toties, quoties hæc repetitio fit. Atque hæc eadem est causa, cur homines lætantur, quoties alicujus jam præteriti mali recordantur, et cur pericula, a quibus liberati sunt, narrare gaudeant. Nam ubi aliquod periculum imaginantur, idem veluti adhuc futurum contemplantur, et ad id metuendum determinantur ; quæ determinatio de novo coercetur idea libertatis, quam hujus periculi ideæ junxerunt, cum ab eodem liberati sunt, quæque eos de novo securos reddit ; atque adeo de novo lætantur.","kind":"passage"},{"id":"RAW_QGOH_PT_3:P0098","text":"PROPOSITIO XLVIII","kind":"passage"},{"id":"RAW_QGOH_PT_3:P0099","text":"Amor et Odium ex. gr. erga Petrum destruitur, si Tristitia, quam hoc, et Lætitia, quam ille involvit, ideæ alterius causæ jungatur ; et eatenus uterque diminuitur, quatenus imaginamur Petrum non solum fuisse alterutrius causam.","kind":"passage"},{"id":"RAW_QGOH_PT_3:P0100","text":"DEMONSTRATIO","kind":"passage"},{"id":"RAW_QGOH_PT_3:P0101","text":"Patet ex sola Amoris et Odii definitione ; quam vide in Schol. Prop. 13 hujus. Nam propter hoc solum Lætitia vocatur Amor, et Tristitia Odium erga Petrum, quia scilicet Petrus hujus vel illius affectus causa esse consideratur. Hoc itaque prorsus vel ex parte sublato, affectus quoque erga Petrum prorsus vel parte diminuitur. Q. E. D.","kind":"passage"},{"id":"RAW_QGOH_PT_3:P0102","text":"PROPOSITIO XLIX","kind":"passage"},{"id":"RAW_QGOH_PT_3:P0103","text":"Amor et Odium erga rem, quam liberam esse imaginamur, major ex pari causa uterque debet esse, quam erga necessariam.","kind":"passage"},{"id":"RAW_QGOH_PT_3:P0104","text":"DEMONSTRATIO","kind":"passage"},{"id":"RAW_QGOH_PT_3:P0105","text":"Res, quam liberam esse imaginamur, debet (per Defin. 7 p. I) per se absque aliis percipi. Si igitur eandem Lætitiæ vel Tristitiæ causam esse imaginemur, eo ipso (per Schol. Prop. 13 hujus) eandem amabimus vel odio habebimus, idque (per Prop. præced.) summo Amore vel Odio, qui ex dato affectu oriri potest. Sed si rem, quæ ejusdem affectus est causa ut necessariam imaginemur, tum (per eandem Defin. 7 p. I) ipsam non solam, sed cum aliis ejusdem affectus causam esse imaginabimur, atque adeo (per Prop. præc.) Amor et Odium erga ipsam minor erit. Q. E. D.","kind":"passage"},{"id":"RAW_QGOH_PT_3:P0106","text":"SCHOLIUM","kind":"passage"},{"id":"RAW_QGOH_PT_3:P0107","text":"Hinc sequitur, homines, quia se liberos esse existimant, majore Amore vel odio se invicem prosequi, quam alia ; ad quod accedit affectuum imitatio, de qua vide Prop. 27, 34, 40 et 43 hujus.","kind":"passage"},{"id":"RAW_QGOH_PT_3:P0108","text":"PROPOSITIO L","kind":"passage"},{"id":"RAW_QGOH_PT_3:P0109","text":"Res quæcunque potest esse per accidens Spei aut Metus causa.","kind":"passage"},{"id":"RAW_QGOH_PT_3:P0110","text":"DEMONSTRATIO","kind":"passage"},{"id":"RAW_QGOH_PT_3:P0111","text":"Hæc Propositio eadem via demonstratur, qua Propositio 15 hujus quam vide una cum Schol. 2 Propositionis 18 hujus.","kind":"passage"},{"id":"RAW_QGOH_PT_3:P0112","text":"SCHOLIUM","kind":"passage"},{"id":"RAW_QGOH_PT_3:P0113","text":"Res, quæ per accidens Spei aut Metus sunt causæ, bona aut mala omina vocantur. Deinde quatenus hæc eadem omina sunt Spei aut Metus causa, eatenus (per Defin. Spei et Metus, quam vide in Schol. 2 Prop. 18 hujus) Lætitiæ aut Tristitiæ sunt causa, et consequenter (per Coroll. Prop. 15 hujus) eatenus eadem amamus vel odio habemus, et (per Prop. 28 hujus) tanquam media ad ea, quæ speramus adhibere, vel tanquam obstacula aut Metus causas amovere conamur. Præterea ex Propositione 25 hujus sequitur, nos natura ita esse constitutos, ut ea, quæ speramus, facile, quæ autem timemus, difficile credamus, et ut de iis plus minusve justo sentiamus. Atque ex his ortæ sunt Superstitiones, quibus homines ubique conflictantur.","kind":"passage"},{"id":"RAW_QGOH_PT_3:P0114","text":"Cæterum non puto operæ esse pretium, animi hic ostendere fluctuationes, quæ ex Spe et Metu oriuntur ; quandoquidem ex sola horum affectuum definitione sequitur, non dari Spem sine Metu, neque Metum sine Spe (ut fusius suo loco explicabimus) ; et præterea quandoquidem, quatenus aliquid speramus aut metuimus, eatenus idem amamus vel odio habemus ; atque adeo quicquid de Amore et Odio diximus, facile unusquisque Spei et Metui applicare poterit.","kind":"passage"},{"id":"RAW_QGOH_PT_3:P0115","text":"PROPOSITIO LI","kind":"passage"},{"id":"RAW_QGOH_PT_3:P0116","text":"Diversi homines ab uno eodemque objecto diversimode affici possunt, et unus idemque homo ab uno eodemque objecto potest diversis temporibus diversimode affici.","kind":"passage"},{"id":"RAW_QGOH_PT_3:P0117","text":"DEMONSTRATIO","kind":"passage"},{"id":"RAW_QGOH_PT_3:P0118","text":"Corpus humanum (per Post. 3 p. II) a corporibus externis plurimis modis afficitur. Possunt igitur eodem tempore duo homines diversimode esse affecti ; atque adeo (per Axiom. 1, quod est post Lemma 3, quod vide post Prop. 13 p. II) ab uno eodemque objecto possunt diversimode affici. Deinde (per idem Postul.) Corpus humanum potest jam hoc jam alio modo esse affectum ; et consequenter (per idem Axiom.) ab uno eodemque objecto diversis temporibus diversimode affici. Q. E. D.","kind":"passage"},{"id":"RAW_QGOH_PT_3:P0119","text":"SCHOLIUM","kind":"passage"}],"sha256":"b7039bc328a494c7c68ec8053bd979f2a08090d39001fa4e993933ca1257b13c","language_code":"la","language_name":"Latina","translations":[{"id":"SOURCE_TR_20261015T013434101636Z_38ae95b4","language_code":"en","language_name":"English","title":"Part Three - On the Origin and Nature of the Affects · passage 3","source_sha256":"b7039bc328a494c7c68ec8053bd979f2a08090d39001fa4e993933ca1257b13c","sha256":"0a89c687097218f24545ebdc6d8f9fb758e37c7b2c3ccb29f212883c9ae5035c","rendition_type":"machine","review_status":"machine","model":"gpt-5.6-terra","created":"2026-10-15T01:34:34.101795+00:00","paragraphs":[{"id":"RAW_QGOH_PT_3:P0001","text":"We see, therefore, that, since human nature is for the most part so constituted that people pity those who fare badly, and envy those who fare well, and (by the preceding Proposition) do so with greater hatred the more they love the thing which they imagine another to possess. We see next that, from the same property of human nature from which it follows that men are compassionate, it also follows that they are envious and ambitious. Finally, if we should wish to consult experience itself, we shall find that it teaches all these things; especially if we attend to the earlier years of our life.","kind":"passage","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_QGOH_PT_3:P0001"]},{"id":"RAW_QGOH_PT_3:P0002","text":"For we find that children, because their body is continually as it were in a state of equilibrium, laugh or weep from this alone, that they see others laugh or weep; and whatever else they see others do, they immediately desire to imitate, and finally they desire for themselves all things by which they imagine others are delighted; namely because, as we have said, the images of things are themselves affections of the human Body, or modes by which the human Body is affected by external causes and disposed to do this or that.","kind":"passage","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_QGOH_PT_3:P0002"]},{"id":"RAW_QGOH_PT_3:P0003","text":"PROPOSITION XXXIII","kind":"passage","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_QGOH_PT_3:P0003"]},{"id":"RAW_QGOH_PT_3:P0004","text":"When we love a thing similar to ourselves, we strive, as far as we can, to bring it about that it loves us in return.","kind":"passage","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_QGOH_PT_3:P0004"]},{"id":"RAW_QGOH_PT_3:P0005","text":"DEMONSTRATION","kind":"passage","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_QGOH_PT_3:P0005"]},{"id":"RAW_QGOH_PT_3:P0006","text":"We strive, as far as we can, to imagine a thing which we love above all others (by Prop. 12 of this Part). If, therefore, the thing is similar to ourselves, we shall strive to affect it with Joy above all others (by Prop. 29 of this Part), that is, we shall strive, as far as we can, to bring it about that the beloved thing is affected with Joy accompanied by the idea of ourselves; that is (by the Scholium to Prop. 13 of this Part), that it loves us in return. Q. E. D.","kind":"passage","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_QGOH_PT_3:P0006"]},{"id":"RAW_QGOH_PT_3:P0007","text":"PROPOSITION XXXIV","kind":"passage","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_QGOH_PT_3:P0007"]},{"id":"RAW_QGOH_PT_3:P0008","text":"The greater the affect with which we imagine the beloved thing to be affected toward us, the more we shall glory.","kind":"passage","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_QGOH_PT_3:P0008"]},{"id":"RAW_QGOH_PT_3:P0009","text":"DEMONSTRATION","kind":"passage","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_QGOH_PT_3:P0009"]},{"id":"RAW_QGOH_PT_3:P0010","text":"We strive (by the preceding Proposition), as far as we can, that the beloved thing should love us in return; that is (by the Scholium to Prop. 13 of this Part), that the beloved thing should be affected with Joy accompanied by the idea of ourselves. Therefore, the greater the Joy with which we imagine the beloved thing to be affected on our account, the more this striving is assisted; that is (by Prop. 11 of this Part with its Scholium), the greater the Joy with which we are affected. But when we rejoice because we have affected another similar to ourselves with Joy, then we contemplate ourselves with Joy (by Prop. 30 of this Part); therefore, the greater the affect with which we imagine the beloved thing to be affected toward us, the greater the Joy with which we shall contemplate ourselves, or (by the Scholium to Prop. 30 of this Part) the more we shall glory. Q. E. D.","kind":"passage","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_QGOH_PT_3:P0010"]},{"id":"RAW_QGOH_PT_3:P0011","text":"PROPOSITION XXXV","kind":"passage","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_QGOH_PT_3:P0011"]},{"id":"RAW_QGOH_PT_3:P0012","text":"If anyone imagines that a beloved thing is joining another to itself by the same or a closer bond of Friendship than that by which he alone possessed the same thing, he will be affected with Hatred toward the beloved thing itself, and will envy that other person.","kind":"passage","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_QGOH_PT_3:P0012"]},{"id":"RAW_QGOH_PT_3:P0013","text":"DEMONSTRATION","kind":"passage","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_QGOH_PT_3:P0013"]},{"id":"RAW_QGOH_PT_3:P0014","text":"The greater the love with which anyone imagines the beloved thing to be affected toward him, the more he will glory (by the preceding Proposition), that is (by the Scholium to Prop. 30 of this Part), he will rejoice; and consequently (by Prop. 28 of this Part) he will strive, as far as he can, to imagine the beloved thing bound to him as closely as possible; which striving, or appetite, is indeed fostered if he imagines that another desires the same thing for himself (by Prop. 31 of this Part). But this striving, or appetite, is supposed to be checked by the image of the beloved thing itself accompanied by the image of the one whom the beloved thing joins to itself; therefore (by the Scholium to Prop. 11 of this Part) he will thereby be affected with Sadness, accompanied by the idea of the beloved thing as a cause, and at the same time by the image of the other person; that is (by the Scholium to Prop. 13 of this Part), he will be affected with hatred toward the beloved thing, and at the same time toward that other person (by the Corollary to Prop. 15 of this Part), whom consequently (by Prop. 23 of this Part), because he delights in the beloved thing, he will envy. Q. E. D.","kind":"passage","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_QGOH_PT_3:P0014"]},{"id":"RAW_QGOH_PT_3:P0015","text":"SCHOLIUM","kind":"passage","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_QGOH_PT_3:P0015"]},{"id":"RAW_QGOH_PT_3:P0016","text":"This Hatred toward the beloved thing, joined with Envy, is called Jealousy, which is therefore nothing but a fluctuation of the mind arising from Love and Hatred together, accompanied by the idea of another whom one envies. Moreover, this Hatred toward the beloved thing will be greater in proportion to the Joy by which the jealous person used to be affected from the reciprocal Love of the beloved thing, and also in proportion to the affect by which he was affected toward the person whom he imagines to be joining the beloved thing to himself. For if he hated him, by that very fact he will hate the beloved thing (by Prop. 24 of this Part), because he imagines that it affects with Joy that which he hates; and also (by the Corollary to Prop. 15 of this Part), because he is compelled to join the image of the beloved thing to the image of him whom he hates.","kind":"passage","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_QGOH_PT_3:P0016"]},{"id":"RAW_QGOH_PT_3:P0017","text":"This account has for the most part a place in Love toward a woman; for he who imagines that the woman whom he loves prostitutes herself to another will be saddened not only because his own appetite is checked, but also because, being compelled to join the image of the beloved thing to the genitals and excrements of another, he is averse to her; to which there finally is added that the jealous person is not received by the beloved thing with the same countenance which it used to present to him, for which reason also the lover is saddened, as I shall now show.","kind":"passage","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_QGOH_PT_3:P0017"]},{"id":"RAW_QGOH_PT_3:P0018","text":"PROPOSITION XXXVI","kind":"passage","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_QGOH_PT_3:P0018"]},{"id":"RAW_QGOH_PT_3:P0019","text":"He who recalls a thing by which he was once delighted desires to possess the same thing with the same circumstances as when he was first delighted by it.","kind":"passage","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_QGOH_PT_3:P0019"]},{"id":"RAW_QGOH_PT_3:P0020","text":"DEMONSTRATION","kind":"passage","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_QGOH_PT_3:P0020"]},{"id":"RAW_QGOH_PT_3:P0021","text":"Whatever a man saw at the same time as the thing which delighted him, all of it will be, by accident, a cause of Joy (by Prop. 15 of this Part); and consequently (by Prop. 28 of this Part) he will desire to possess all of it together with the thing which delighted him, or to possess the thing with all the same circumstances as when he was first delighted by it. Q. E. D.","kind":"passage","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_QGOH_PT_3:P0021"]},{"id":"RAW_QGOH_PT_3:P0022","text":"COROLLARY","kind":"passage","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_QGOH_PT_3:P0022"]},{"id":"RAW_QGOH_PT_3:P0023","text":"If, therefore, he discovers that one of those circumstances is lacking, the lover will be saddened.","kind":"passage","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_QGOH_PT_3:P0023"]},{"id":"RAW_QGOH_PT_3:P0024","text":"DEMONSTRATION","kind":"passage","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_QGOH_PT_3:P0024"]},{"id":"RAW_QGOH_PT_3:P0025","text":"For insofar as he finds that some circumstance is lacking, to that extent he imagines something which excludes the existence of that thing. But since he is desirous of that thing or circumstance out of love (by the preceding Prop.), therefore (by Prop. 19 of this Part), insofar as he imagines it to be lacking, he will be saddened. Q. E. D.","kind":"passage","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_QGOH_PT_3:P0025"]},{"id":"RAW_QGOH_PT_3:P0026","text":"SCHOLIUM","kind":"passage","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_QGOH_PT_3:P0026"]},{"id":"RAW_QGOH_PT_3:P0027","text":"This Sadness, insofar as it concerns the absence of what we love, is called Desire.","kind":"passage","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_QGOH_PT_3:P0027"]},{"id":"RAW_QGOH_PT_3:P0028","text":"PROPOSITION XXXVII","kind":"passage","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_QGOH_PT_3:P0028"]},{"id":"RAW_QGOH_PT_3:P0029","text":"Desire which arises from Sadness or Joy, and from Hatred or Love, is greater in proportion as the affect is greater.","kind":"passage","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_QGOH_PT_3:P0029"]},{"id":"RAW_QGOH_PT_3:P0030","text":"DEMONSTRATION","kind":"passage","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_QGOH_PT_3:P0030"]},{"id":"RAW_QGOH_PT_3:P0031","text":"Sadness diminishes or restrains a man's power of acting (by the Schol. of Prop. 11 of this Part), that is (by Prop. 7 of this Part), it diminishes or restrains the striving by which a man strives to persevere in his being; and therefore (by Prop. 5 of this Part) it is contrary to this striving; and whatever a man affected with Sadness strives for is to remove Sadness. But (by the Def. of Sadness), the greater the Sadness, the greater the part of the man's power of acting to which it must be opposed; therefore the greater the Sadness, the greater the power of acting with which the man will strive to remove Sadness; that is (by the Schol. of Prop. 9 of this Part), with the greater desire or appetite he will strive to remove Sadness. Further, since Joy (by the same Schol. of Prop. 11 of this Part) increases or assists a man's power of acting, it is easily demonstrated in the same way that a man affected with Joy desires nothing other than to preserve it, and does so with the greater Desire, the greater the Joy may be.","kind":"passage","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_QGOH_PT_3:P0031"]},{"id":"RAW_QGOH_PT_3:P0032","text":"Finally, since Hatred and Love are themselves affects of Sadness or Joy, it follows in the same manner that the striving, appetite, or Desire which arises from Hatred or Love will be greater in proportion to the Hatred and Love. Q. E. D.","kind":"passage","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_QGOH_PT_3:P0032"]},{"id":"RAW_QGOH_PT_3:P0033","text":"PROPOSITION XXXVIII","kind":"passage","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_QGOH_PT_3:P0033"]},{"id":"RAW_QGOH_PT_3:P0034","text":"If someone begins to hate a thing loved, so that Love is utterly abolished, he will pursue it, from an equal cause, with greater hatred than if he had never loved it, and with so much the greater hatred as the Love had previously been greater.","kind":"passage","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_QGOH_PT_3:P0034"]},{"id":"RAW_QGOH_PT_3:P0035","text":"DEMONSTRATION","kind":"passage","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_QGOH_PT_3:P0035"]},{"id":"RAW_QGOH_PT_3:P0036","text":"For if someone begins to hate a thing which he loves, more of his appetites are restrained than if he had not loved it. For Love is Joy (by the Schol. of Prop. 13 of this Part), which a man strives, as far as he can (by Prop. 28 of this Part), to preserve; and this he does (by the same Schol.) by contemplating the loved thing as present, and by affecting it with Joy, as far as he can (by Prop. 21 of this Part); which striving indeed (by the preceding Prop.) is greater the greater the love is, as is also the striving to bring it about that the loved thing should love him in return (see Prop. 33 of this Part). But these strivings are restrained by hatred toward the loved thing (by the Corollary of Prop. 13 and by Prop. 23 of this Part); therefore the lover (by the Schol. of Prop. 11 of this Part) will for this reason also be affected with Sadness, and with so much the greater Sadness as the Love had been greater; that is, besides the Sadness which was the cause of Hatred, another arises from the fact that he loved the thing, and consequently he will contemplate the loved thing with a greater affect of Sadness, that is (by the Schol.","kind":"passage","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_QGOH_PT_3:P0036"]},{"id":"RAW_QGOH_PT_3:P0037","text":"of Prop. 13 of this Part) he will pursue it with greater hatred than if he had not loved it, and with so much the greater hatred as the love had been greater. Q. E. D.","kind":"passage","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_QGOH_PT_3:P0037"]},{"id":"RAW_QGOH_PT_3:P0038","text":"PROPOSITION XXXIX","kind":"passage","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_QGOH_PT_3:P0038"]},{"id":"RAW_QGOH_PT_3:P0039","text":"He who hates someone will strive to inflict evil upon him, unless he fears that a greater evil will arise for himself from it; and conversely, he who loves someone will strive, by the same law, to do him good.","kind":"passage","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_QGOH_PT_3:P0039"]},{"id":"RAW_QGOH_PT_3:P0040","text":"DEMONSTRATION","kind":"passage","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_QGOH_PT_3:P0040"]},{"id":"RAW_QGOH_PT_3:P0041","text":"To hate someone is (by the Schol. of Prop. 13 of this Part) to imagine someone as a cause of Sadness; and therefore (by Prop. 28 of this Part), he who hates someone will strive to remove or destroy him. But if he fears from this something sadder, or (which is the same) a greater evil, for himself, and believes that he can avoid it by not inflicting upon him whom he hates the evil which he had intended, he will desire to abstain from inflicting the evil (by the same Prop. 28 of this Part); and this (by Prop. 37 of this Part) with a greater striving than that by which he was impelled to inflict evil, which therefore will prevail, as we wished. The demonstration of the second part proceeds in the same manner. Therefore, he who hates someone, etc. Q. E. D.","kind":"passage","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_QGOH_PT_3:P0041"]},{"id":"RAW_QGOH_PT_3:P0042","text":"SCHOLIUM","kind":"passage","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_QGOH_PT_3:P0042"]},{"id":"RAW_QGOH_PT_3:P0043","text":"By good here I understand every kind of Joy, and whatever further contributes to it, and especially that which satisfies desire, whatever that may be. By evil, however, I understand every kind of Sadness, and especially that which frustrates desire. For we have shown above (in the Schol. of Prop. 9 of this Part) that we desire nothing because we judge it to be good; but, on the contrary, we call good that which we desire; and consequently we call evil that which we are averse to; wherefore each judges or esteems, from his own affect, what is good, what evil, what better, what worse, and finally what is best and what worst. Thus the Avaricious man judges an abundance of money to be best, but a lack of it to be worst. The Ambitious man, however, desires nothing so much as Glory, and dreads nothing so much as Shame.","kind":"passage","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_QGOH_PT_3:P0043"]},{"id":"RAW_QGOH_PT_3:P0044","text":"Further, to the Envious nothing is more pleasing than another's misfortune, and nothing more troublesome than another's good fortune; and thus each judges, from his own affect, that some thing is good or bad, useful or useless. Moreover, this affect by which a man is so disposed that he is unwilling to will what he wills, or that he wills what he does not will, is called Fear, which therefore is nothing other than timidity insofar as a man is thereby disposed to avoid, by a lesser evil, an evil which he judges to be future (See Prop. 28 of this Part). But if the evil which he fears is Shame, then Fear is called Modesty. Finally, if the Desire of avoiding a future evil is restrained by Fear of another evil, so that he does not know what he would rather will, then Fear is called Consternation, especially if each of the two evils feared is among the greatest.","kind":"passage","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_QGOH_PT_3:P0044"]},{"id":"RAW_QGOH_PT_3:P0045","text":"PROPOSITION XL","kind":"passage","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_QGOH_PT_3:P0045"]},{"id":"RAW_QGOH_PT_3:P0046","text":"He who imagines that he is hated by someone, and does not believe that he has given him any cause for hatred, will in turn hate him.","kind":"passage","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_QGOH_PT_3:P0046"]},{"id":"RAW_QGOH_PT_3:P0047","text":"DEMONSTRATION","kind":"passage","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_QGOH_PT_3:P0047"]},{"id":"RAW_QGOH_PT_3:P0048","text":"He who imagines someone affected with hatred will thereby also be affected with hatred (by Prop. 27 of this Part), that is (by the Schol. of Prop. 13 of this Part), with Sadness accompanied by the idea of an external cause. But he (by the hypothesis) imagines no cause of this Sadness besides the one who hates him; therefore, from imagining himself to be hated by someone, he will be affected with Sadness accompanied by the idea of him who hates him, or (by the same Schol.) he will hate him in return. Q. E. D.","kind":"passage","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_QGOH_PT_3:P0048"]},{"id":"RAW_QGOH_PT_3:P0049","text":"SCHOLIUM","kind":"passage","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_QGOH_PT_3:P0049"]},{"id":"RAW_QGOH_PT_3:P0050","text":"But if he imagines that he has furnished a just cause for Hatred, then (by Prop. 30 of this Part and its Scholium) he will be affected with Shame. But this (by Prop. 25 of this Part) rarely happens. Moreover, this reciprocation of Hatred can also arise from the fact that Hatred is followed by an endeavor to inflict evil upon him who is hated (by Prop. 39 of this Part). Therefore, he who imagines that he is hated by someone will imagine that same person to be the cause of some evil or Sadness; and thus he will be affected with Sadness, or Fear, accompanied by the idea of him who hates him as cause, that is, he will in return be affected with Hatred, as above.","kind":"passage","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_QGOH_PT_3:P0050"]},{"id":"RAW_QGOH_PT_3:P0051","text":"COROLLARY I","kind":"passage","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_QGOH_PT_3:P0051"]},{"id":"RAW_QGOH_PT_3:P0052","text":"He who imagines that one whom he loves is affected with Hatred toward him will be torn by Hatred and Love at the same time. For insofar as he imagines that he is hated by that person, he is determined (by the preceding Prop.) to hate that person in return. But (by hypothesis) he nevertheless loves him; therefore he will be torn by Hatred and Love at the same time.","kind":"passage","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_QGOH_PT_3:P0052"]},{"id":"RAW_QGOH_PT_3:P0053","text":"COROLLARY II","kind":"passage","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_QGOH_PT_3:P0053"]},{"id":"RAW_QGOH_PT_3:P0054","text":"If anyone imagines that some evil has been inflicted upon him out of Hatred by someone whom he had previously pursued with no affect, he will immediately endeavor to repay that same evil to that person.","kind":"passage","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_QGOH_PT_3:P0054"]},{"id":"RAW_QGOH_PT_3:P0055","text":"DEMONSTRATION","kind":"passage","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_QGOH_PT_3:P0055"]},{"id":"RAW_QGOH_PT_3:P0056","text":"He who imagines that someone is affected with Hatred toward him will hate him in return (by the preceding Prop.), and (by Prop. 26 of this Part) will endeavor to devise everything that can affect him with Sadness, and (by Prop. 39 of this Part) will strive to inflict it upon him. But (by hypothesis) the first thing that he imagines of this kind is an evil inflicted upon himself; therefore he will immediately endeavor to inflict the same upon him. Q. E. D.","kind":"passage","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_QGOH_PT_3:P0056"]},{"id":"RAW_QGOH_PT_3:P0057","text":"SCHOLIUM","kind":"passage","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_QGOH_PT_3:P0057"]},{"id":"RAW_QGOH_PT_3:P0058","text":"The endeavor to inflict evil upon one whom we hate is called Anger; but the endeavor to repay an evil inflicted upon us is called Vengeance.","kind":"passage","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_QGOH_PT_3:P0058"]},{"id":"RAW_QGOH_PT_3:P0059","text":"PROPOSITION XLI","kind":"passage","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_QGOH_PT_3:P0059"]},{"id":"RAW_QGOH_PT_3:P0060","text":"If anyone imagines that he is loved by someone, and does not believe that he has given any cause for this (which can occur by Coroll. of Prop. 15 and by Prop. 16 of this Part), he will love that person in return.","kind":"passage","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_QGOH_PT_3:P0060"]},{"id":"RAW_QGOH_PT_3:P0061","text":"DEMONSTRATION","kind":"passage","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_QGOH_PT_3:P0061"]},{"id":"RAW_QGOH_PT_3:P0062","text":"This Proposition is demonstrated by the same method as the preceding one. See also its Scholium.","kind":"passage","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_QGOH_PT_3:P0062"]},{"id":"RAW_QGOH_PT_3:P0063","text":"SCHOLIUM","kind":"passage","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_QGOH_PT_3:P0063"]},{"id":"RAW_QGOH_PT_3:P0064","text":"But if he believes that he has furnished a just cause for Love, he will glory (by Prop. 30 of this Part together with its Scholium); and this indeed (by Prop. 25 of this Part) happens more frequently, and we said that its contrary occurs when someone imagines that he is hated by someone else (see Schol. of the preceding Prop.). Furthermore, this reciprocal Love, and consequently (by Prop. 39 of this Part) the endeavor to do good to one who loves us and who (by the same Prop. 39 of this Part) endeavors to do good to us, is called Favor or Gratitude; and thus it appears that men are far more ready for Vengeance than for returning a benefit.","kind":"passage","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_QGOH_PT_3:P0064"]},{"id":"RAW_QGOH_PT_3:P0065","text":"COROLLARY","kind":"passage","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_QGOH_PT_3:P0065"]},{"id":"RAW_QGOH_PT_3:P0066","text":"He who imagines that he is loved by one whom he hates will be torn by Hatred and Love at the same time. This is demonstrated by the same method as the first Corollary of the preceding Proposition.","kind":"passage","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_QGOH_PT_3:P0066"]},{"id":"RAW_QGOH_PT_3:P0067","text":"SCHOLIUM","kind":"passage","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_QGOH_PT_3:P0067"]},{"id":"RAW_QGOH_PT_3:P0068","text":"But if Hatred prevails, he will endeavor to inflict evil upon him by whom he is loved; this affect is called Cruelty, especially if he who loves is believed to have furnished no common cause of Hatred.","kind":"passage","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_QGOH_PT_3:P0068"]},{"id":"RAW_QGOH_PT_3:P0069","text":"PROPOSITION XLII","kind":"passage","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_QGOH_PT_3:P0069"]},{"id":"RAW_QGOH_PT_3:P0070","text":"He who, moved by Love or by hope of Glory, has conferred a benefit upon someone will be saddened if he sees the benefit received with an ungrateful mind.","kind":"passage","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_QGOH_PT_3:P0070"]},{"id":"RAW_QGOH_PT_3:P0071","text":"DEMONSTRATION","kind":"passage","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_QGOH_PT_3:P0071"]},{"id":"RAW_QGOH_PT_3:P0072","text":"He who loves some thing like himself endeavors, as far as he can, to bring it about that he is loved in return by it (by Prop. 33 of this Part). Therefore, he who out of love has conferred a benefit upon someone has done so from the desire by which he is bound to be loved in return, that is (by Prop. 34 of this Part), from hope of Glory, or (by the Schol. of Prop. 30 of this Part) of Joy; and consequently (by Prop. 12 of this Part) he will endeavor, as far as he can, to imagine this cause of Glory, or to contemplate it as actually existing. But (by hypothesis) he imagines something else which excludes the existence of that cause; therefore (by Prop. 19 of this Part) he will thereby be saddened. Q. E. D.","kind":"passage","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_QGOH_PT_3:P0072"]},{"id":"RAW_QGOH_PT_3:P0073","text":"PROPOSITION XLIII","kind":"passage","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_QGOH_PT_3:P0073"]},{"id":"RAW_QGOH_PT_3:P0074","text":"Hatred is increased by reciprocal hatred, and can, on the other hand, be destroyed by Love.","kind":"passage","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_QGOH_PT_3:P0074"]},{"id":"RAW_QGOH_PT_3:P0075","text":"DEMONSTRATION","kind":"passage","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_QGOH_PT_3:P0075"]},{"id":"RAW_QGOH_PT_3:P0076","text":"He who imagines that he whom he hates is affected toward him with reciprocal Hatred, by that very fact (by Prop. 40 of this Part) a new Hatred arises, while the first (by hypothesis) still endures. But if, on the contrary, he imagines that the same person is affected toward him with Love, insofar as he imagines this, he contemplates himself with Joy (by Prop. 30 of this Part), and insofar (by Prop. 29 of this Part) he will strive to please him; that is (by Prop. 41 of this Part), insofar he strives not to hate him and not to affect him with any Sadness; and this striving (by Prop. 37 of this Part) will be greater or less in proportion to the affect from which it arises. And therefore, if it is greater than that which arises from Hatred, and by which he strives to affect with Sadness the thing which he hates (by Prop. 26 of this Part), it will prevail over it and will erase hatred from the mind. Q. E. D.","kind":"passage","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_QGOH_PT_3:P0076"]},{"id":"RAW_QGOH_PT_3:P0077","text":"PROPOSITION XLIV","kind":"passage","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_QGOH_PT_3:P0077"]},{"id":"RAW_QGOH_PT_3:P0078","text":"Hatred which is wholly conquered by Love passes into Love; and Love is therefore greater than if Hatred had not preceded it.","kind":"passage","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_QGOH_PT_3:P0078"]},{"id":"RAW_QGOH_PT_3:P0079","text":"DEMONSTRATION","kind":"passage","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_QGOH_PT_3:P0079"]},{"id":"RAW_QGOH_PT_3:P0080","text":"It proceeds in the same way as in Proposition 38 of this Part. For he who begins to love a thing which he hates, or which he was accustomed to contemplate with Sadness, rejoices by that very fact that he loves; and to this Joy, which Love involves (see its Definition in the Scholium to Prop. 13 of this Part), there is added also that which arises from the fact that the striving to remove the Sadness which Hatred involves (as we showed in Prop. 37 of this Part) is wholly aided, accompanied by the idea, as cause, of him whom he hated.","kind":"passage","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_QGOH_PT_3:P0080"]},{"id":"RAW_QGOH_PT_3:P0081","text":"SCHOLIUM","kind":"passage","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_QGOH_PT_3:P0081"]},{"id":"RAW_QGOH_PT_3:P0082","text":"Although things stand thus, nevertheless no one will strive to hate any thing, or to be affected with Sadness, in order that he may enjoy this greater Joy; that is, no one will desire that harm be inflicted upon him in the hope of recovering a loss, nor will he desire to be ill in the hope of recovering. For everyone will always strive to preserve his own being and to remove Sadness, as far as he can. But if, on the contrary, it could be conceived that a man could desire to hate someone, so that he might afterward pursue him with greater love, then he would always desire to hate him. For the greater the Hatred, the greater will be the Love; and consequently he would always desire that the Hatred be increased more and more. And for the same reason a man would strive to be ill more and more, so that he might afterward enjoy greater Joy from restored health; and consequently he would always strive to be ill, which (by Prop. 6 of this Part) is absurd.","kind":"passage","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_QGOH_PT_3:P0082"]},{"id":"RAW_QGOH_PT_3:P0083","text":"PROPOSITION XLV","kind":"passage","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_QGOH_PT_3:P0083"]},{"id":"RAW_QGOH_PT_3:P0084","text":"If someone imagines that one like himself is affected with Hatred toward a thing like himself which he loves, he will hate him.","kind":"passage","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_QGOH_PT_3:P0084"]},{"id":"RAW_QGOH_PT_3:P0085","text":"DEMONSTRATION","kind":"passage","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_QGOH_PT_3:P0085"]},{"id":"RAW_QGOH_PT_3:P0086","text":"For the loved thing has reciprocal hatred toward him who hates it (by Prop. 40 of this Part); and thus the lover who imagines that someone hates the loved thing, by that very fact imagines the loved thing to be affected with Hatred, that is (by the Scholium to Prop. 13 of this Part), with Sadness; and consequently (by Prop. 21 of this Part) he is saddened, accompanied by the idea, as cause, of him who hates the loved thing; that is (by the Scholium to Prop. 13 of this Part), he will hate him. Q. E. D.","kind":"passage","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_QGOH_PT_3:P0086"]},{"id":"RAW_QGOH_PT_3:P0087","text":"PROPOSITION XLVI","kind":"passage","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_QGOH_PT_3:P0087"]},{"id":"RAW_QGOH_PT_3:P0088","text":"If anyone has been affected with Joy or Sadness by someone of a class or nation different from his own, accompanied by the idea of that person, under the universal name of the class or nation, as cause: he will love or hate not only that person, but all of the same class or nation.","kind":"passage","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_QGOH_PT_3:P0088"]},{"id":"RAW_QGOH_PT_3:P0089","text":"DEMONSTRATION","kind":"passage","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_QGOH_PT_3:P0089"]},{"id":"RAW_QGOH_PT_3:P0090","text":"The demonstration of this is evident from Proposition 16 of this Part.","kind":"passage","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_QGOH_PT_3:P0090"]},{"id":"RAW_QGOH_PT_3:P0091","text":"PROPOSITION XLVII","kind":"passage","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_QGOH_PT_3:P0091"]},{"id":"RAW_QGOH_PT_3:P0092","text":"The Joy which arises from our imagining that a thing which we hate is destroyed or affected by some other evil does not arise without some Sadness of mind.","kind":"passage","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_QGOH_PT_3:P0092"]},{"id":"RAW_QGOH_PT_3:P0093","text":"DEMONSTRATION","kind":"passage","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_QGOH_PT_3:P0093"]},{"id":"RAW_QGOH_PT_3:P0094","text":"This is evident from Prop. 27 of this Part. For insofar as we imagine a thing like ourselves to be affected with Sadness, we are saddened.","kind":"passage","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_QGOH_PT_3:P0094"]},{"id":"RAW_QGOH_PT_3:P0095","text":"SCHOLIUM","kind":"passage","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_QGOH_PT_3:P0095"]},{"id":"RAW_QGOH_PT_3:P0096","text":"This Proposition can also be demonstrated from the Corollary to Proposition 17 of Part II. For whenever we remember a thing, although it does not actually exist, we nevertheless contemplate it as present, and the Body is affected in the same way. Therefore, insofar as the memory of the thing remains vigorous, a man is determined to contemplate it with Sadness; which determination, while the image of the thing still remains, is indeed restrained by the memory of those things which exclude its existence, but is not removed. And therefore a man rejoices only insofar as this determination is restrained; and hence it comes about that this Joy, which arises from the evil of a thing which we hate, is repeated as often as we remember that same thing. For, as we have said, when the image of that same thing is aroused, because it involves the existence of the thing itself, it determines a man to contemplate the thing with the same Sadness with which he was accustomed to contemplate it when it existed.","kind":"passage","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_QGOH_PT_3:P0096"]},{"id":"RAW_QGOH_PT_3:P0097","text":"But because he has joined to the image of the same thing other images which exclude its existence, this determination toward Sadness is immediately restrained, and the man rejoices anew; and this happens as often as this repetition occurs. And this is likewise the cause why men rejoice whenever they recall some evil now past, and why they delight in recounting dangers from which they have been freed. For when they imagine some danger, they contemplate it as if it were still to come, and are determined to fear it; which determination is anew restrained by the idea of freedom, which they joined to the idea of this danger when they were freed from it, and which makes them secure anew; and consequently they rejoice anew.","kind":"passage","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_QGOH_PT_3:P0097"]},{"id":"RAW_QGOH_PT_3:P0098","text":"PROPOSITION XLVIII","kind":"passage","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_QGOH_PT_3:P0098"]},{"id":"RAW_QGOH_PT_3:P0099","text":"Love and Hatred, for example toward Peter, are destroyed if the Sadness which the latter, and the Joy which the former involves, are joined to the idea of another cause; and each is diminished insofar as we imagine that Peter was not the sole cause of either.","kind":"passage","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_QGOH_PT_3:P0099"]},{"id":"RAW_QGOH_PT_3:P0100","text":"DEMONSTRATION","kind":"passage","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_QGOH_PT_3:P0100"]},{"id":"RAW_QGOH_PT_3:P0101","text":"This is evident from the definition of Love and Hatred alone; see it in the Scholium to Proposition 13 of this Part. For it is solely because of this that Joy is called Love, and Sadness Hatred, toward Peter: namely, because Peter is considered to be the cause of this or that affect. Therefore, if this is wholly or in part removed, the affect toward Peter likewise is wholly or in part diminished. Q. E. D.","kind":"passage","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_QGOH_PT_3:P0101"]},{"id":"RAW_QGOH_PT_3:P0102","text":"PROPOSITION XLIX","kind":"passage","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_QGOH_PT_3:P0102"]},{"id":"RAW_QGOH_PT_3:P0103","text":"Love and Hatred toward a thing which we imagine to be free must, from an equal cause, each be greater than toward a necessary thing.","kind":"passage","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_QGOH_PT_3:P0103"]},{"id":"RAW_QGOH_PT_3:P0104","text":"DEMONSTRATION","kind":"passage","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_QGOH_PT_3:P0104"]},{"id":"RAW_QGOH_PT_3:P0105","text":"A thing which we imagine to be free must (by Def. 7, Part I) be perceived through itself, without other things. If, therefore, we imagine it to be the cause of Joy or Sadness, by that very fact (by the Scholium to Prop. 13 of this Part) we shall love or hate it; and this (by the preceding Prop.) with the greatest Love or Hatred which can arise from the given affect. But if we imagine as necessary the thing which is the cause of the same affect, then (by the same Def. 7, Part I) we shall imagine it to be not the sole cause, but a cause of the same affect together with others; and consequently (by the preceding Prop.) Love and Hatred toward it will be less. Q. E. D.","kind":"passage","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_QGOH_PT_3:P0105"]},{"id":"RAW_QGOH_PT_3:P0106","text":"SCHOLIUM","kind":"passage","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_QGOH_PT_3:P0106"]},{"id":"RAW_QGOH_PT_3:P0107","text":"From this it follows that men, because they believe themselves to be free, pursue one another with greater Love or Hatred than they do other things; to which is added the imitation of the affects, concerning which see Props. 27, 34, 40, and 43 of this Part.","kind":"passage","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_QGOH_PT_3:P0107"]},{"id":"RAW_QGOH_PT_3:P0108","text":"PROPOSITION L","kind":"passage","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_QGOH_PT_3:P0108"]},{"id":"RAW_QGOH_PT_3:P0109","text":"Anything whatsoever can accidentally be a cause of Hope or Fear.","kind":"passage","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_QGOH_PT_3:P0109"]},{"id":"RAW_QGOH_PT_3:P0110","text":"DEMONSTRATION","kind":"passage","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_QGOH_PT_3:P0110"]},{"id":"RAW_QGOH_PT_3:P0111","text":"This Proposition is demonstrated in the same way as Proposition 15 of this Part; see that together with Scholium 2 to Proposition 18 of this Part.","kind":"passage","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_QGOH_PT_3:P0111"]},{"id":"RAW_QGOH_PT_3:P0112","text":"SCHOLIUM","kind":"passage","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_QGOH_PT_3:P0112"]},{"id":"RAW_QGOH_PT_3:P0113","text":"Things which are accidentally causes of Hope or Fear are called good or bad omens. Further, insofar as these same omens are causes of Hope or Fear, they are (by the definition of Hope and Fear, which see in Scholium 2 to Prop. 18 of this Part) causes of Joy or Sadness, and consequently (by the Corollary to Prop. 15 of this Part) insofar as this is so, we love or hate them, and (by Prop. 28 of this Part) endeavor to use them as means for attaining the things we hope for, or to remove them as obstacles or causes of Fear. Moreover, from Proposition 25 of this Part it follows that we are so constituted by nature that we easily believe those things which we hope for, but with difficulty those which we fear, and that we judge of them more or less favorably than is just. And from these have arisen the Superstitions with which men everywhere are afflicted.","kind":"passage","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_QGOH_PT_3:P0113"]},{"id":"RAW_QGOH_PT_3:P0114","text":"Besides, I do not think it worth the trouble here to set forth the fluctuations of mind which arise from Hope and Fear; since from the definition of these affects alone it follows that there can be no Hope without Fear, nor Fear without Hope (as we shall explain more fully in its place); and further, since insofar as we hope or fear something, we love or hate it; and therefore whatever we have said concerning Love and Hatred, anyone will easily be able to apply to Hope and Fear.","kind":"passage","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_QGOH_PT_3:P0114"]},{"id":"RAW_QGOH_PT_3:P0115","text":"PROPOSITION LI","kind":"passage","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_QGOH_PT_3:P0115"]},{"id":"RAW_QGOH_PT_3:P0116","text":"Different men can be affected in different ways by one and the same object, and one and the same man can be affected in different ways by one and the same object at different times.","kind":"passage","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_QGOH_PT_3:P0116"]},{"id":"RAW_QGOH_PT_3:P0117","text":"DEMONSTRATION","kind":"passage","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_QGOH_PT_3:P0117"]},{"id":"RAW_QGOH_PT_3:P0118","text":"The human Body (by Post. 3, Part II) is affected in very many ways by external bodies. Therefore, at the same time two men can be affected in different ways; and consequently (by Axiom 1, which follows Lemma 3, see it after Prop. 13, Part II) they can be affected in different ways by one and the same object. Further (by the same Postulate), the human Body can now be affected in this way, now in another way; and consequently (by the same Axiom) it can be affected in different ways by one and the same object at different times. Q. E. D.","kind":"passage","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_QGOH_PT_3:P0118"]},{"id":"RAW_QGOH_PT_3:P0119","text":"SCHOLIUM","kind":"passage","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_QGOH_PT_3:P0119"]}],"source_digest":"a9eb1029c31428c9f1d53dac707c79d9d89ce8aa03f90b4f9e713199230f3f56","source_language_code":"la","original_title":"Pars tertia - De origine et natura affectuum · passage 3"}],"passage_basis":"frozen-grounding","original_title":"Pars tertia - De origine et natura affectuum · passage 3","title_language_code":"en"},{"id":"RAW_QGOH_PT_4","title":"Part Three - On the Origin and Nature of the Affects · passage 4","order":12,"chapter_id":"CH_QGOH_PT_4","text":"Videmus itaque fieri posse, ut quod hic amat, alter odio habeat ; et quod hic metuit, alter non metuat ; et ut unus idemque homo jam amet, quod antea oderit, et ut jam audeat, quod antea timuit, etc. Deinde quia unusquisque ex suo affectu judicat, quid bonum, quid malum, quid melius, et quid pejus sit (vide Schol. Prop. 39 hujus), sequitur, homines tam judicio quam affectu variare posse ; et hinc fit, ut cum alios aliis comparamus, ex sola affectuum differentia a nobis distinguantur, et ut alios intrepidos, alios timidos, alios denique alio nomine appellemus. Ex. gr. illum ego intrepidum vocabo, qui malum contemnit, quod ego timere soleo ; et si præterea ad hoc attendam, quod ejus Cupiditas malum inferendi ei, quem odit, et benefaciendi ei, quem amat, non coercetur timore mali, a quo ego contineri soleo, ipsum audacem appellabo. Deinde ille mihi timidus videbitur, qui malum timet, quod ego contemnere soleo ; et si insuper ad hoc attendam, quod ejus Cupiditas coercetur timore mali, quod me continere nequit, ipsum pusillanimem esse dicam ; et sic unusquisque judicabit. Denique ex hac hominis natura et judicii inconstantia, ut et quod homo sæpe ex solo affectu de rebus judicat, et quod res, quas ad Lætitiam vel Tristitiam facere credit, quasque propterea (per Prop. 28 hujus), ut fiant, promovere, vel amovere conatur, sæpe non nisi imaginariæ sint ; ut jam taceam alia, quæ in 2 Parte ostendimus, de rerum incertitudine ; facile concipimus, hominem posse sæpe in causa esse, tam ut contristetur, quam ut lætetur, sive ut tam Tristitia quam Lætitia afficiatur, concomitante idea sui tanquam causa ; atque adeo facile intelligimus, quid Pœnitentia, et quid Acquiescentia in se ipso sit. Nempe Pœnitentia est Tristitia concomitante idea sui, et Acquiescentia in se ipso est Lætitia concomitante idea sui, tanquam causa, et hi affectus vehementissimi sunt, quia homines se liberos esse credunt (vid. Prop. 49 hujus).\n\nPROPOSITIO LII\n\nObjectum, quod simul cum aliis antea vidimus, vel quod nihil habere imaginamur, nisi quod commune est pluribus, non tamdiu contemplabimur, ac illud, quod aliquid singulare habere imaginamur.\n\nDEMONSTRATIO\n\nSimulatque objectum, quod cum aliis vidimus, imaginamur, statim et aliorum recordamur (per Prop. 18 p. II, cujus etiam Schol. vide), et sic ex unius contemplatione statim in contemplationem alterius incidimus. Atque eadem est ratio objecti, quod nihil habere imaginamur, nisi quod commune est pluribus. Nam eo ipso supponimus, nos nihil in eo contemplari, quod antea cum aliis non viderimus. Verum cum supponimus, nos in objecto aliquo aliquid singulare, quod antea nunquam vidimus, imaginari, nihil aliud dicimus, quam quod Mens, dum illud objectum contemplatur, nullum aliud in se habeat, in cujus contemplationem ex contemplatione illius incidere potest ; atque adeo ad illud solum contemplandum determinata est. Ergo objectum, etc. Q. E. D.\n\nSCHOLIUM\n\nHæc Mentis affectio, sive rei singularis imaginatio, quatenus sola in Mente versatur, vocatur Admiratio ; quæ si ab objecto, quod timemus, moveatur, Consternatio dicitur, quia mali Admiratio hominem suspensum in sola sui contemplatione ita tenet, ut de aliis cogitare non valeat, quibus illud malum vitare posset. Sed si id, quod admiramur, sit hominis alicujus prudentia, industria, vel aliquid hujusmodi, quia eo ipso hominem nobis longe antecellere contemplamur, tum Admiratio vocatur Veneratio ; alias Horror, si hominis iram, invidiam, etc., admiramur. Deinde, si hominis, quem amamus, prudentiam, industriam, etc., admiramur, Amor eo ipso (per Prop. 12 hujus) major erit, et hunc Amorem Admirationi sive Venerationi junctum Devotionem vocamus. Et ad hunc modum concipere etiam possumus Odium, Spem, Securitatem, et alios Affectus Admirationi junctos ; atque adeo plures Affectus deducere poterimus, quam qui receptis vocabulis indicari solent. Unde apparet, Affectuum nomina inventa esse magis ex eorum vulgari usu, quam ex eorundem accurata cognitione.\n\nAdmirationi opponitur Contemptus, cujus tamen causa hæc plerumque est, quod sc. ex eo, quod aliquem rem aliquam admirari, amare, metuere, etc., videmus, vel ex eo, quod res aliqua primo aspectu apparet, similis rebus, quas admiramur, amamus, metuimus, etc. (per Prop. 15 cum ejus Coroll. et Prop. 27 hujus), determinamur ad eandem rem admirandum, amandum, metuendum, etc. Sed si ex ipsius rei præsentia, vel accuratiore contemplatione, id omne de eadem negare cogamur, quod causa Admirationis, Amoris, Metus, etc. esse potest, tum Mens ex ipsa rei præsentia magis ad ea cogitandum, quæ in objecto non sunt, quam quæ in ipso sunt, determinata manet ; cum tamen contra ex objecti præsentia id præcipue cogitare soleat, quod id objecto est. Porro sicut Devotio ex rei, quam amamus, Admiratione, sic Irrisio ex rei, quam odimus vel metuimus, Contemptu oritur, et Dedignatio ex stultitiæ Contemptu, sicuti Veneratio ex Admiratione prudentiæ. Possumus denique, Amorem, Spem, Gloriam, et alios Affectus junctos Contemptui concipere atque inde alios præterea Affectus deducere, quos etiam nullo singulari vocabulo ab aliis distinguera solemus.\n\nPROPOSITIO LIII\n\nCum Mens se ipsam, suamque agendi potentiam contemplatur, lætatur ; et eo magis, quo se suamque agendi potentiam distinctius imaginatur.\n\nDEMONSTRATIO\n\nHomo se ipsum non cognoscit, nisi per affectiones sui Corporis earumque ideas (per Prop. 19 et 23 p. II). Cum ergo fit, ut Mens se ipsam possit contemplari, eo ipso ad majorem perfectionem transire, hoc est (per Schol. Prop. 11 hujus), Lætitia affici supponitur et eo majori, quo se suamque agendi potentiam distinctius imaginari potest. Q. E. D.\n\nCOROLLARIUM\n\nHæc Lætitia magis magisque fovetur, quo magis homo se ab aliis laudari imaginatur. Nam quo magis se ab aliis laudari imaginatur, eo majori Lætitia alios ab ipso affici imaginatur, idque concomitante idea sui (per Schol. Prop. 29 hujus) ; atque adeo (per Prop. 27 hujus) ipse majore Lætitia concomitante idea sui afficitur. Q. E. D.\n\nPROPOSITIO LIV\n\nMens ea tantum imaginari conatur, quæ ipsius agendi potentiam ponunt.\n\nDEMONSTRATIO\n\nMentis conatus sive potentia est ipsa ipsius Mentis essentia (per Prop. 7 hujus) ; Mentis autem essentia (ut per se notum) id tantum, quod Mens est et potest, affirmat ; at non id, quod non est neque potest ; adeoque id tantum imaginari conatur, quod ipsius agendi potentiam affirmat sive ponit. Q. E. D.\n\nPROPOSITIO LV\n\nCum Mens suam impotentiam imaginatur, eo ipso contristatur.\n\nDEMONSTRATIO\n\nMentis essentia id tantum, quod Mens est et potest, affirmat, sive de natura Mentis est, ea tantummodo imaginari, quæ ipsius agendi potentiam ponunt (per Prop. præced.). Cum itaque dicimus, quod Mens, dum se ipsam contemplatur, suam imaginatur impotentiam, nihil aliud dicimus, quam quod, dum Mens aliquid imaginari conatur, quod ipsius agendi potentiam ponit, hic ejus conatus coercetur, sive (per Schol. Prop. 11 hujus) quod ipsa contristatur. Q. E. D.\n\nCOROLLARIUM\n\nHæc Tristitia magis ac magis fovetur, si se ab aliis vituperari imaginatur ; quod eodem demonstratur, ac Coroll. Prop. 53 hujus.\n\nSCHOLIUM\n\nHæc Tristitia concomitante idea nostræ imbecillitatis Humilitas appellatur ; Lætitia autem, quæ ex contemplatione nostri oritur, Philautia, vel Acquiescentia in se ipso vocatur. Et quoniam hæc toties repetitur, quoties homo suas virtutes sive suam agendi potentiam contemplatur, hinc ergo etiam fit, ut unusquisque facta sua narrare, suique tam corporis quam animi vires ostentare gestiat, et ut homines hac de causa sibi invicem molesti sint. Ex quibus iterum sequitur, homines natura esse invidos (vid. Schol. Prop. 24 et Schol. Prop. 32 hujus), sive ob suorum æqualium imbecillitatem gaudere, et contra propter eorundem virtutem contristari. Nam quoties unusquisque suas actiones imaginatur, toties Lætitia (per Prop. 53 hujus) afficitur, et eo majore, quo actiones plus perfectionis exprimere, et easdem distinctius imaginatur ; hoc est (per illa, quæ in Schol. 1 Prop. 40 p. II dicta sunt), quo magis easdem ab aliis distinguere et ut res singulares contemplari potest. Quare unusquisque ex contemplatione sui tunc maxime gaudebit, quando aliquid in se contemplatur, quod de reliquis negat. Sed si id, quod de se affirmat, ad universalem hominis vel animalis ideam refert, non tantopere gaudebit ; et contra contristabitur, si suas, ad aliorum actiones comparatas, imbecilliores esse imaginetur ; quam quidem Tristitiam (per Prop. 28 hujus) amovere conabitur, idque suorum æqualium actiones perperam interpretando, vel suas quantum potest adornando. Apparet igitur, homines natura proclives esse ad Odium et Invidiam, ad quam accedit ipsa educatio. Nam parentes solo honoris et Invidiæ stimulo liberos ad virtutem concitare solent. Sed scrupulus forsan remanet, quod non raro hominum virtutes admiremur, eosque veneremur. Hunc ergo ut amoveam, sequens addam Corollarium.\n\nCOROLLARIUM\n\nNemo virtutem alicui, nisi æquali, invidet.\n\nDEMONSTRATIO\n\nInvidia est ipsum Odium (vid. Schol. Prop. 24 hujus), sive (per Schol. Prop. 13 hujus) Tristitia, hoc est (per Schol. Prop. 11 hujus) affectio, qua hominis agendi potentia seu conatus coercetur. At homo (per Schol. Prop. 9 hujus) nihil agere conatur neque cupit, nisi quod ex data sua natura sequi potest ; ergo homo nullam de se agendi potentiam seu (quod idem est) virtutem prædicari cupiet, quæ naturæ alterius est propria, et suæ aliena ; adeoque ejus Cupiditas coerceri, hoc est (per Schol. Prop. 11 hujus) ipse contristari, nequit ex eo, quod aliquam virtutem in aliquo ipsi dissimili contemplatur, et consequenter neque ei invidere poterit. At quidem suo æquali, qui cum ipso ejusdem naturæ supponitur. Q. E. D.\n\nSCHOLIUM\n\nCum igitur supra in Scholio Propositionis 52 hujus Partis dixerimus, nos hominem venerari ex eo, quod ipsius prudentiam, fortitudinem, etc., admiramur, id fit (ut ex ipsa Prop. patet) quia has virtutes et singulariter inesse, et non ut nostræ naturæ communes imaginamur ; adeoque easdem ipsi non magis invidebimus, quam arboribus altitudinem, et leonibus fortitudinem, etc.\n\nPROPOSITIO LVI\n\nLætitiæ, Tristitiæ, et Cupiditatis, et consequenter uniuscujusque affectus, qui ex his componitur, ut animi Fluctuationis, vel qui ab his derivatur nempe Amoris, Odii, Spei, Metus, etc., tot species dantur, quot sunt species objectorum, a quibus afficimur.\n\nDEMONSTRATIO\n\nLætitia et Tristitia, et consequenter affectus, qui ex his componuntur vel ex his derivantur, passiones sunt (per Schol. Prop. 11 hujus) ; nos autem (per Prop. 1 hujus) necessario patimur, quatenus ideas habemus inadæquatas ; et quatenus easdem habemus (per Prop. 3 hujus), eatenus tantum patimur, hoc est (vid. Schol. 1 Prop. 40 p. II), eatenus tantum necessario patimur, quatenus imaginamur, sive (vid. Prop. 17 p. II cum ejus Schol.) quatenus afficimur affectu, qui naturam nostri Corporis et naturam corporis externi involvit. Natura igitur uniuscujusque passionis ita necessario debet explicari, ut objecti, a quo afficimur, natura exprimatur. Nempe Lætitia, quæ ex objecto ex. gr. A oritur, naturam ipsius objecti A, et Lætitia, quæ ex objecto B oritur ; ipsius objecti B naturam involvit ; atque adeo hi duo Lætitiæ affectus natura sunt diversi, quia ex causis diversæ naturæ oriuntur. Sic etiam Tristitiæ affectus, qui ex uno objecto oritur, diversus natura est a Tristitia, quæ ab alia causa oritur ; quod etiam de Amore, Odio, Spe, Metu, animi Fluctuatione, etc., intelligendum est : ac proinde Lætitiæ, Tristitiæ, Amoris, Odii, etc., tot species necessario dantur, quot sunt species objectorum, a quibus afficimur. At Cupiditas est ipsa uniuscujusque essentia, seu natura, quatenus ex data quacunque ejus constitutione determinata concipitur ad aliquid agendum (vid. Schol. Prop. 9 hujus) ; ergo, prout unusquisque a causis externis hac aut illa Lætitiæ, Tristitiæ, Amoris, Odii, etc., specie afficitur, hoc est, prout ejus natura hoc aut alio modo constituitur, ita ejus Cupiditas alia atque alia esse, et natura unius a natura alterius Cupiditatis tantum differre necesse est, quantum affectus, a quibus unaquæque oritur, inter se differunt. Dantur itaque tot species Cupiditatis, quot sunt species Lætitiæ, Tristitiæ, Amoris, etc., et consequenter (per jam ostensa) quot sunt objectorum species, a quibus afficimur. Q. E. D.\n\nSCHOLIUM\n\nInter affectuum species, quæ (per Prop. præc.) perplurimæ esse debent, insignes sunt Luxuria, Ebrietas, Libido, Avaritia et Ambitio, quæ non nisi Amoris vel Cupiditatis sunt notiones, quæ hujus utriusque affectus naturam explicant per objecta, ad quæ referuntur. Nam per Luxuriam, Ebrietatem, Libidinem, Avaritiam, et Ambitionem nihil aliud intelligimus, quam convivandi, potandi, coeundi, divitiarum et gloriæ immoderatum Amorem vel Cupiditatem. Præterea hi affectus, quatenus eos per solum objectum, ad quod referuntur, ab aliis distinguimus, contrarios non habent. Nam Temperantia, quam Luxuriæ, et Sobrietas, quam Ebrietati, et denique Castitas, quam Libidini opponere solemus, affectus seu passiones non sunt, sed animi indicant potentiam, quæ hos affectus moderatur. Cæterum reliquas Affectuum species hic explicare nec possum (quia tot sunt, quot objectorum species), nec, si possem, necesse est. Nam ad id, quod intendimus, nempe ad affectuum vires, et Mentis in eosdem potentiam, determinandum, nobis sufficit, uniuscujusque affectus generalem habere definitionem. Sufficit, inquam, nobis, Affectuum et Mentis communes proprietates intelligere, ut determinare possimus, qualis et quanta sit Mentis potentia in moderandis et coercendis Affectibus. Quamvis itaque magna sit differentia inter hunc et illum Amoris, Odii, vel Cupiditatis affectum, ex. gr. inter Amorem erga liberos et inter amorem erga uxorem, nobis tamen has differentias cognoscere, et affectuum naturam et originem ulterius indagare, non est opus.\n\nPROPOSITIO LVII\n\nQuilibet uniuscujusque individui affectus ab affectu alterius tantum discrepat, quantum essentia unius ab essentia alterius differt.\n\nDEMONSTRATIO\n\nHæc Propositio patet ex Axiom. 1, quod vide post Lem. 3 Schol. Prop. 13, p. 2. At nihilominus eandem ex trium primitivorum affectuum definitionibus demonstrabimus.\n\nOmnes affectus ad Cupiditatem, Lætitiam, vel Tristitiam referuntur, ut eorum quas dedimus definitiones ostendunt. At Cupiditas est ipsa uniuscujusque natura seu essentia (vide ejus Defin. in Schol. Prop. 9 ujus) ; ergo uniuscujusque individui Cupiditas a Cupiditate alterius tantum discrepat, quantum natura seu essentia unius ab essentia alterius differt. Lætitia deinde et Tristitia passiones sunt, quibus uniuscujusque potentia seu conatus in suo esse perseverandi, augetur vel minuitur, juvatur vel coercetur (per Prop. 11 hujus et ejus Schol.). At per conatum in suo esse perseverandi, quatenus ad Mentem et Corpus simul refertur. Appetitum et Cupiditatem intelligimus (vide Schol. Prop. 9 hujus) ; ergo Lætitia et Tristitia est ipsa Cupiditas sive Appetitus, quatenus a causis externis augetur vel minuitur, juvatur vel coercetur, hoc est (per idem Schol.) est ipsa cujusque natura ; atque adeo uniuscujusque Lætitia vel Tristitia a Lætitia vel Tristitia alterius tantum etiam discrepat, quantum natura seu essentia unius ab essentia alterius differt et consequenter quilibet uniuscujusque individui affectus ab affectu alterius tantum discrepat, etc. Q. E. D.\n\nSCHOLIUM\n\nHinc sequitur, affectus animalium, quæ irrationalia dicuntur (bruta enim sentire nequaquam dubitare possumus, postquam Mentis novimus originem), ab affectibus hominum tantum differre, quantum eorum natura a natura humana differt. Fertur quidem equus et homo Libidine procreandi ; at ille Libidine equina, hic autem humana. Sic etiam Libidines et Appetitus insectorum, piscium, et avium, alii atque alii esse debent. Quamvis itaque unumquodque individuum sua, qua constat natura contentum vivat eaque gaudeat, vita tamen illa, qua unumquodque est contentum, et gaudium nihil aliud est, quam idea seu anima ejusdem individui, atque adeo gaudium unius a gaudio alterius tantum natura discrepat, quantum essentia unius ab essentia alterius differt. Denique ex præcedenti Propositione sequitur, non parum etiam interesse inter gaudium, quo ebrius ex. gr. ducitur, et inter gaudium, quo potitur Philosophus, quod hic in transitu monere volui. Atque hæc de affectibus, qui ad hominem referuntur, quatenus patitur. Superest, ut pauca addam de iis, qui ad eundem referuntur, quatenus agit.\n\nPROPOSITIO LVIII\n\nPræter Lætitiam et Cupiditatem, quæ passiones sunt, alii Lætitiæ et Cupiditatis affectus dantur, qui ad nos, quatenus agimus, referuntur.\n\nDEMONSTRATIO\n\nCum Mens se ipsam suamque agendi potentiam concipit, lætatur (per Prop. 53 hujus) : Mens autem se ipsam necessario contemplatur, quando veram sive adæquatam ideam concipit (per Prop. 43 p. II). At Mens quasdam ideas adæquatas concipit (per Schol. 2 Prop. 40 p. II). Ergo eatenus etiam lætatur, quatenus ideas adæquatas concipit, hoc est (per Prop. 1 hujus) quatenus agit. Deinde Mens tam quatenus claras et distinctas, quam quatenus confusas habeL ideas, in suo esse persevare conatur (per Prop. 9 hujus). At per conatum Cupiditatem intelligimus (per ejusdem Schol.) ; ergo Cupiditas ad nos refertur, etiam quatenus întelligimus, sive (per Prop. 1 hujus) quatenus agimus. Q. E. D.\n\nPROPOSITIO LIX\n\nInter omnes affectus, qui ad Mentem, quatenus agit, referuntur, nulli sunt, quam qui ad Lætitiam vel Cupiditatem referuntur.\n\nDEMONSTRATIO\n\nOmnes affectus ad Cupiditatem, Lætitiam, vel Tristitiam referuntur, ut eorum quas dedimus, definitiones ostendunt. Per Tristitiam autem intelligimus, quod Mentis cogitandi potentia minuitur vel coercetur (per Prop. 11 hujus et ejus Schol.) ; adeoque Mens quatenus contristatur, eatenus ejus intelligendi, hoc est ejus agendi potentia (per Prop. 1 hujus) minuitur vel coercetur ; adeoque nulli Tristitiæ affectus ad Mentem referri possunt, quatenus agit ; sed tantum affectus Lætitiæ et Cupiditatis qui (per Prop. præc.) eatenus etiam ad Mentem referuntur. Q. E. D.\n\nSCHOLIUM\n\nOmnes actiones, quæ sequuntur ex affectibus, qui ad Mentem referuntur, quatenus intelligit ad Fortitudinem refero, quam in Animositatem et Generositatem distinguo. Nam per Animositatem intelligo Cupiditatem, qua unusquisque conatur suum esse ex solo Rationis dictamine conservare.\n\nPer Generositatem autem Cupiditatem intelligo, qua unusquisque ex solo Rationis dictamine conatur reliquos homines juvare, et sibi amicitia jungere. Eas itaque actiones, quæ solum agentis utile intendunt, ad Animositatem, et quæ alterius etiam utile intendunt, ad Generositatem refero. Temperantia igitur, Sobrietas, et Animi in periculis Præsentia, etc. Animositatis sunt species ; Modestia autem, Clementia, etc., species Generositatis sunt. Atque his puto me præcipuos affectus, animique fluctuationes, quæ ex compositione trium primitivorum affectuum, nempe Cupiditatis, Lætitiæ, et Tristitiæ oriuntur, explicuisse, perque primas suas causas ostendisse. Ex quibus apparet, nos a causis externis multis modis agitari, nosque perinde ut maris undæ, a contrariis ventis agitatæ, fluctuari, nostri eventus atque fati inscios. At dixi, me præcipuos tantum, non omnes, qui dari possunt, animi conflictus ostendisse. Nam eadem via, qua supra, procedendo, facile possumus ostendere, Amorem esse junctum Pœnitentiæ, Dedignationi, Pudori, etc. Imo unicuique ex jam dictis clare constare credo, affectus tot modis alii cum aliis componi, indeque tot variationes oriri, ut nullo numero definiri queant. Sed ad meum institutum præcipuos tantum enumeravisse sufficit : nam reliqui, quos omisi, plus curiositatis quam utilitatis haberent. Attamen de Amore hoc notandum restat, quod scilicet sæpissime contingit, dum re, quam appetebamus, fruimur, ut Corpus ex ea fruitione novam acquirat constitutionem, a qua aliter determinatur, et aliæ rerum imagines in eo excitantur, et simul Mens alia imaginari, aliaque cupere incipit. Ex. gr. cum aliquid, quod nos sapore delectare solet, imaginamur, eodem frui, nempe comedere cupimus. At quamdiu eodem sic fruimur, stomachus adimpletur, Corpusque aliter constituitur. Si igitur, Corpore jam aliter disposito, ejusdem cibi imago, quia ipse præsens adest, fomentetur, et consequenter conatus etiam, sive Cupiditas, eundem comedendi ; huic Cupiditati seu conatui nova illa constitutio repugnabit, et consequenter cibi, quem appetebamus, præsentia odiosa erit, et hoc est quod Fastidium et Tædium vocamus. Cæterum Corporis affectiones externas, quæ in affectibus observantur, ut sunt tremor, livor, singultus, risus, etc., neglexi, quia ad solum Corpus absque ulla ad Mentem relatione referuntur. Denique de affectuum definitionibus quædam notanda sunt, quas propterea hic ordine repetam, et quid in unaquaque observandum est, iisdem interponam.\n\nAFFECTUUM DEFINITIONES\n\nI\n\nCupiditas est ipsa hominis essentia, quatenus ex data quacunque ejus affectione determinata concipitur ad aliquid agendum.\n\nEXPLICATIO\n\nDiximus supra in Scholio Propositions 9 hujus Partis, Cupiditatem esse appetitum cum ejusdem conscientia ; appetitum autem esse ipsam hominis essentiam, quatenus determinata est ad ea agendum quæ ipsius conservationi inserviunt. Sed in eodem Scholio etiam monui, me revera inter humanum appetitum et Cupiditatem nullam agnoscere differentiam. Nam sive homo sui appetitus sit conscius, sive non sit, manet tamen appetitus unus idemque ; a que adeo, ne tautologiam committere viderer, Cupiditatem per appetitum explicare nolui, sed eandem ita definire studui, ut omnes humanæ naturæ conatus, quos nomine appetitus, voluntatis, cupiditatis, vel impetus significamus, una comprehenderem. Potueram enim dicere, Cupiditatem esse ipsam hominis essentiam, quatenus determinata concipitur ad aliquid agendum ; sed ex hac definitione (per Prop. 23 p. II) non sequeretur, quod Mens possit suæ Cupiditatis sive appetitus esse conscia. Igitur, ut hujus conscientiæ causam involverem, necesse fuit (per eandem Prop.) addere, quatenus ex data quacunque ejus affectione determinata, etc. Nam per affectionem humanæ essentiæ quamcunque ejusdem essentiæ constitutionem intelligimus, sive ea sit innata, sive adventicia, sive quod ipsa per solum Cogitationis, sive per solum Extensionis attributum concipiatur, sive denique quod ad utrumque simul referatur. Hic igitur Cupiditatis nomine intelligo hominis quoscunque conatus, impetus, appetitus, et volitiones, qui pro varia ejusdem hominis constitutione varii, et non raro adeo sibi invicem oppositi sunt, ut homo diversimode trahatur, et, quo se vertat, nesciat.\n\nII\n\nLætitia est hominis transitio a minore ad majorem perfectionem.\n\nIII\n\nTristitia est hominis transitio a majore ad minorem perfectionem.\n\nEXPLICATIO\n\nDico transitionem. Nam Laetitia non est ipsa perfectio. Si enim homo cum perfectione, ad quam transit, nasceretur, ejusdem absque Lætitiæ affectu compos esset ; quod clarius apparet ex Tristitiæ affectu, qui huic est contrarius. Nam quod Tristitia in transitione ad minorem perfectionem consistit, non autem in ipsa minore perfectione, nemo negare potest quandoquidem homo eatenus contristari nequit, quatenus alicujus perfectionis est particeps. Nec dicere possumus, quod Tristitia in privatione majoris perfectionis consistat ; nam privatio nihil est, Tristitiæ autem affectus actus est, qui propterea nullus alius esse potest, quam actus transeundi ad minorem perfectionem hoc est, actus quo hominis agendi potentia minuitur vel coercetur (vide Schol. Prop. 11 hujus). Cæterum definitiones Hilaritatis, Titillationis, Melancholiæ, et Doloris omitto, quia ad Corpus potissimum referuntur, et non nisi Lætitiæ aut Tristitiæ sunt species.\n\nIV\n\nAdmiratio est rei alicujus imaginatio, in qua Mens defixa propterea manet, quia hæc singularis imaginatio nullam cum reliquis habet connexionem (vide Prop. 52 hujus cum ejusdem Schol.)\n\nEXPLICATIO","paragraphs":[{"id":"RAW_QGOH_PT_4:P0001","text":"Videmus itaque fieri posse, ut quod hic amat, alter odio habeat ; et quod hic metuit, alter non metuat ; et ut unus idemque homo jam amet, quod antea oderit, et ut jam audeat, quod antea timuit, etc. Deinde quia unusquisque ex suo affectu judicat, quid bonum, quid malum, quid melius, et quid pejus sit (vide Schol. Prop. 39 hujus), sequitur, homines tam judicio quam affectu variare posse ; et hinc fit, ut cum alios aliis comparamus, ex sola affectuum differentia a nobis distinguantur, et ut alios intrepidos, alios timidos, alios denique alio nomine appellemus. Ex. gr. illum ego intrepidum vocabo, qui malum contemnit, quod ego timere soleo ; et si præterea ad hoc attendam, quod ejus Cupiditas malum inferendi ei, quem odit, et benefaciendi ei, quem amat, non coercetur timore mali, a quo ego contineri soleo, ipsum audacem appellabo.","kind":"passage"},{"id":"RAW_QGOH_PT_4:P0002","text":"Deinde ille mihi timidus videbitur, qui malum timet, quod ego contemnere soleo ; et si insuper ad hoc attendam, quod ejus Cupiditas coercetur timore mali, quod me continere nequit, ipsum pusillanimem esse dicam ; et sic unusquisque judicabit. Denique ex hac hominis natura et judicii inconstantia, ut et quod homo sæpe ex solo affectu de rebus judicat, et quod res, quas ad Lætitiam vel Tristitiam facere credit, quasque propterea (per Prop. 28 hujus), ut fiant, promovere, vel amovere conatur, sæpe non nisi imaginariæ sint ; ut jam taceam alia, quæ in 2 Parte ostendimus, de rerum incertitudine ; facile concipimus, hominem posse sæpe in causa esse, tam ut contristetur, quam ut lætetur, sive ut tam Tristitia quam Lætitia afficiatur, concomitante idea sui tanquam causa ; atque adeo facile intelligimus, quid Pœnitentia, et quid Acquiescentia in se ipso sit.","kind":"passage"},{"id":"RAW_QGOH_PT_4:P0003","text":"Nempe Pœnitentia est Tristitia concomitante idea sui, et Acquiescentia in se ipso est Lætitia concomitante idea sui, tanquam causa, et hi affectus vehementissimi sunt, quia homines se liberos esse credunt (vid. Prop. 49 hujus).","kind":"passage"},{"id":"RAW_QGOH_PT_4:P0004","text":"PROPOSITIO LII","kind":"passage"},{"id":"RAW_QGOH_PT_4:P0005","text":"Objectum, quod simul cum aliis antea vidimus, vel quod nihil habere imaginamur, nisi quod commune est pluribus, non tamdiu contemplabimur, ac illud, quod aliquid singulare habere imaginamur.","kind":"passage"},{"id":"RAW_QGOH_PT_4:P0006","text":"DEMONSTRATIO","kind":"passage"},{"id":"RAW_QGOH_PT_4:P0007","text":"Simulatque objectum, quod cum aliis vidimus, imaginamur, statim et aliorum recordamur (per Prop. 18 p. II, cujus etiam Schol. vide), et sic ex unius contemplatione statim in contemplationem alterius incidimus. Atque eadem est ratio objecti, quod nihil habere imaginamur, nisi quod commune est pluribus. Nam eo ipso supponimus, nos nihil in eo contemplari, quod antea cum aliis non viderimus. Verum cum supponimus, nos in objecto aliquo aliquid singulare, quod antea nunquam vidimus, imaginari, nihil aliud dicimus, quam quod Mens, dum illud objectum contemplatur, nullum aliud in se habeat, in cujus contemplationem ex contemplatione illius incidere potest ; atque adeo ad illud solum contemplandum determinata est. Ergo objectum, etc. Q. E. D.","kind":"passage"},{"id":"RAW_QGOH_PT_4:P0008","text":"SCHOLIUM","kind":"passage"},{"id":"RAW_QGOH_PT_4:P0009","text":"Hæc Mentis affectio, sive rei singularis imaginatio, quatenus sola in Mente versatur, vocatur Admiratio ; quæ si ab objecto, quod timemus, moveatur, Consternatio dicitur, quia mali Admiratio hominem suspensum in sola sui contemplatione ita tenet, ut de aliis cogitare non valeat, quibus illud malum vitare posset. Sed si id, quod admiramur, sit hominis alicujus prudentia, industria, vel aliquid hujusmodi, quia eo ipso hominem nobis longe antecellere contemplamur, tum Admiratio vocatur Veneratio ; alias Horror, si hominis iram, invidiam, etc., admiramur. Deinde, si hominis, quem amamus, prudentiam, industriam, etc., admiramur, Amor eo ipso (per Prop. 12 hujus) major erit, et hunc Amorem Admirationi sive Venerationi junctum Devotionem vocamus.","kind":"passage"},{"id":"RAW_QGOH_PT_4:P0010","text":"Et ad hunc modum concipere etiam possumus Odium, Spem, Securitatem, et alios Affectus Admirationi junctos ; atque adeo plures Affectus deducere poterimus, quam qui receptis vocabulis indicari solent. Unde apparet, Affectuum nomina inventa esse magis ex eorum vulgari usu, quam ex eorundem accurata cognitione.","kind":"passage"},{"id":"RAW_QGOH_PT_4:P0011","text":"Admirationi opponitur Contemptus, cujus tamen causa hæc plerumque est, quod sc. ex eo, quod aliquem rem aliquam admirari, amare, metuere, etc., videmus, vel ex eo, quod res aliqua primo aspectu apparet, similis rebus, quas admiramur, amamus, metuimus, etc. (per Prop. 15 cum ejus Coroll. et Prop. 27 hujus), determinamur ad eandem rem admirandum, amandum, metuendum, etc. Sed si ex ipsius rei præsentia, vel accuratiore contemplatione, id omne de eadem negare cogamur, quod causa Admirationis, Amoris, Metus, etc. esse potest, tum Mens ex ipsa rei præsentia magis ad ea cogitandum, quæ in objecto non sunt, quam quæ in ipso sunt, determinata manet ; cum tamen contra ex objecti præsentia id præcipue cogitare soleat, quod id objecto est.","kind":"passage"},{"id":"RAW_QGOH_PT_4:P0012","text":"Porro sicut Devotio ex rei, quam amamus, Admiratione, sic Irrisio ex rei, quam odimus vel metuimus, Contemptu oritur, et Dedignatio ex stultitiæ Contemptu, sicuti Veneratio ex Admiratione prudentiæ. Possumus denique, Amorem, Spem, Gloriam, et alios Affectus junctos Contemptui concipere atque inde alios præterea Affectus deducere, quos etiam nullo singulari vocabulo ab aliis distinguera solemus.","kind":"passage"},{"id":"RAW_QGOH_PT_4:P0013","text":"PROPOSITIO LIII","kind":"passage"},{"id":"RAW_QGOH_PT_4:P0014","text":"Cum Mens se ipsam, suamque agendi potentiam contemplatur, lætatur ; et eo magis, quo se suamque agendi potentiam distinctius imaginatur.","kind":"passage"},{"id":"RAW_QGOH_PT_4:P0015","text":"DEMONSTRATIO","kind":"passage"},{"id":"RAW_QGOH_PT_4:P0016","text":"Homo se ipsum non cognoscit, nisi per affectiones sui Corporis earumque ideas (per Prop. 19 et 23 p. II). Cum ergo fit, ut Mens se ipsam possit contemplari, eo ipso ad majorem perfectionem transire, hoc est (per Schol. Prop. 11 hujus), Lætitia affici supponitur et eo majori, quo se suamque agendi potentiam distinctius imaginari potest. Q. E. D.","kind":"passage"},{"id":"RAW_QGOH_PT_4:P0017","text":"COROLLARIUM","kind":"passage"},{"id":"RAW_QGOH_PT_4:P0018","text":"Hæc Lætitia magis magisque fovetur, quo magis homo se ab aliis laudari imaginatur. Nam quo magis se ab aliis laudari imaginatur, eo majori Lætitia alios ab ipso affici imaginatur, idque concomitante idea sui (per Schol. Prop. 29 hujus) ; atque adeo (per Prop. 27 hujus) ipse majore Lætitia concomitante idea sui afficitur. Q. E. D.","kind":"passage"},{"id":"RAW_QGOH_PT_4:P0019","text":"PROPOSITIO LIV","kind":"passage"},{"id":"RAW_QGOH_PT_4:P0020","text":"Mens ea tantum imaginari conatur, quæ ipsius agendi potentiam ponunt.","kind":"passage"},{"id":"RAW_QGOH_PT_4:P0021","text":"DEMONSTRATIO","kind":"passage"},{"id":"RAW_QGOH_PT_4:P0022","text":"Mentis conatus sive potentia est ipsa ipsius Mentis essentia (per Prop. 7 hujus) ; Mentis autem essentia (ut per se notum) id tantum, quod Mens est et potest, affirmat ; at non id, quod non est neque potest ; adeoque id tantum imaginari conatur, quod ipsius agendi potentiam affirmat sive ponit. Q. E. D.","kind":"passage"},{"id":"RAW_QGOH_PT_4:P0023","text":"PROPOSITIO LV","kind":"passage"},{"id":"RAW_QGOH_PT_4:P0024","text":"Cum Mens suam impotentiam imaginatur, eo ipso contristatur.","kind":"passage"},{"id":"RAW_QGOH_PT_4:P0025","text":"DEMONSTRATIO","kind":"passage"},{"id":"RAW_QGOH_PT_4:P0026","text":"Mentis essentia id tantum, quod Mens est et potest, affirmat, sive de natura Mentis est, ea tantummodo imaginari, quæ ipsius agendi potentiam ponunt (per Prop. præced.). Cum itaque dicimus, quod Mens, dum se ipsam contemplatur, suam imaginatur impotentiam, nihil aliud dicimus, quam quod, dum Mens aliquid imaginari conatur, quod ipsius agendi potentiam ponit, hic ejus conatus coercetur, sive (per Schol. Prop. 11 hujus) quod ipsa contristatur. Q. E. D.","kind":"passage"},{"id":"RAW_QGOH_PT_4:P0027","text":"COROLLARIUM","kind":"passage"},{"id":"RAW_QGOH_PT_4:P0028","text":"Hæc Tristitia magis ac magis fovetur, si se ab aliis vituperari imaginatur ; quod eodem demonstratur, ac Coroll. Prop. 53 hujus.","kind":"passage"},{"id":"RAW_QGOH_PT_4:P0029","text":"SCHOLIUM","kind":"passage"},{"id":"RAW_QGOH_PT_4:P0030","text":"Hæc Tristitia concomitante idea nostræ imbecillitatis Humilitas appellatur ; Lætitia autem, quæ ex contemplatione nostri oritur, Philautia, vel Acquiescentia in se ipso vocatur. Et quoniam hæc toties repetitur, quoties homo suas virtutes sive suam agendi potentiam contemplatur, hinc ergo etiam fit, ut unusquisque facta sua narrare, suique tam corporis quam animi vires ostentare gestiat, et ut homines hac de causa sibi invicem molesti sint. Ex quibus iterum sequitur, homines natura esse invidos (vid. Schol. Prop. 24 et Schol. Prop. 32 hujus), sive ob suorum æqualium imbecillitatem gaudere, et contra propter eorundem virtutem contristari. Nam quoties unusquisque suas actiones imaginatur, toties Lætitia (per Prop. 53 hujus) afficitur, et eo majore, quo actiones plus perfectionis exprimere, et easdem distinctius imaginatur ; hoc est (per illa, quæ in Schol. 1 Prop. 40 p.","kind":"passage"},{"id":"RAW_QGOH_PT_4:P0031","text":"II dicta sunt), quo magis easdem ab aliis distinguere et ut res singulares contemplari potest. Quare unusquisque ex contemplatione sui tunc maxime gaudebit, quando aliquid in se contemplatur, quod de reliquis negat. Sed si id, quod de se affirmat, ad universalem hominis vel animalis ideam refert, non tantopere gaudebit ; et contra contristabitur, si suas, ad aliorum actiones comparatas, imbecilliores esse imaginetur ; quam quidem Tristitiam (per Prop. 28 hujus) amovere conabitur, idque suorum æqualium actiones perperam interpretando, vel suas quantum potest adornando. Apparet igitur, homines natura proclives esse ad Odium et Invidiam, ad quam accedit ipsa educatio. Nam parentes solo honoris et Invidiæ stimulo liberos ad virtutem concitare solent. Sed scrupulus forsan remanet, quod non raro hominum virtutes admiremur, eosque veneremur. Hunc ergo ut amoveam, sequens addam Corollarium.","kind":"passage"},{"id":"RAW_QGOH_PT_4:P0032","text":"COROLLARIUM","kind":"passage"},{"id":"RAW_QGOH_PT_4:P0033","text":"Nemo virtutem alicui, nisi æquali, invidet.","kind":"passage"},{"id":"RAW_QGOH_PT_4:P0034","text":"DEMONSTRATIO","kind":"passage"},{"id":"RAW_QGOH_PT_4:P0035","text":"Invidia est ipsum Odium (vid. Schol. Prop. 24 hujus), sive (per Schol. Prop. 13 hujus) Tristitia, hoc est (per Schol. Prop. 11 hujus) affectio, qua hominis agendi potentia seu conatus coercetur. At homo (per Schol. Prop. 9 hujus) nihil agere conatur neque cupit, nisi quod ex data sua natura sequi potest ; ergo homo nullam de se agendi potentiam seu (quod idem est) virtutem prædicari cupiet, quæ naturæ alterius est propria, et suæ aliena ; adeoque ejus Cupiditas coerceri, hoc est (per Schol. Prop. 11 hujus) ipse contristari, nequit ex eo, quod aliquam virtutem in aliquo ipsi dissimili contemplatur, et consequenter neque ei invidere poterit. At quidem suo æquali, qui cum ipso ejusdem naturæ supponitur. Q. E. D.","kind":"passage"},{"id":"RAW_QGOH_PT_4:P0036","text":"SCHOLIUM","kind":"passage"},{"id":"RAW_QGOH_PT_4:P0037","text":"Cum igitur supra in Scholio Propositionis 52 hujus Partis dixerimus, nos hominem venerari ex eo, quod ipsius prudentiam, fortitudinem, etc., admiramur, id fit (ut ex ipsa Prop. patet) quia has virtutes et singulariter inesse, et non ut nostræ naturæ communes imaginamur ; adeoque easdem ipsi non magis invidebimus, quam arboribus altitudinem, et leonibus fortitudinem, etc.","kind":"passage"},{"id":"RAW_QGOH_PT_4:P0038","text":"PROPOSITIO LVI","kind":"passage"},{"id":"RAW_QGOH_PT_4:P0039","text":"Lætitiæ, Tristitiæ, et Cupiditatis, et consequenter uniuscujusque affectus, qui ex his componitur, ut animi Fluctuationis, vel qui ab his derivatur nempe Amoris, Odii, Spei, Metus, etc., tot species dantur, quot sunt species objectorum, a quibus afficimur.","kind":"passage"},{"id":"RAW_QGOH_PT_4:P0040","text":"DEMONSTRATIO","kind":"passage"},{"id":"RAW_QGOH_PT_4:P0041","text":"Lætitia et Tristitia, et consequenter affectus, qui ex his componuntur vel ex his derivantur, passiones sunt (per Schol. Prop. 11 hujus) ; nos autem (per Prop. 1 hujus) necessario patimur, quatenus ideas habemus inadæquatas ; et quatenus easdem habemus (per Prop. 3 hujus), eatenus tantum patimur, hoc est (vid. Schol. 1 Prop. 40 p. II), eatenus tantum necessario patimur, quatenus imaginamur, sive (vid. Prop. 17 p. II cum ejus Schol.) quatenus afficimur affectu, qui naturam nostri Corporis et naturam corporis externi involvit. Natura igitur uniuscujusque passionis ita necessario debet explicari, ut objecti, a quo afficimur, natura exprimatur. Nempe Lætitia, quæ ex objecto ex. gr. A oritur, naturam ipsius objecti A, et Lætitia, quæ ex objecto B oritur ; ipsius objecti B naturam involvit ; atque adeo hi duo Lætitiæ affectus natura sunt diversi, quia ex causis diversæ naturæ oriuntur.","kind":"passage"},{"id":"RAW_QGOH_PT_4:P0042","text":"Sic etiam Tristitiæ affectus, qui ex uno objecto oritur, diversus natura est a Tristitia, quæ ab alia causa oritur ; quod etiam de Amore, Odio, Spe, Metu, animi Fluctuatione, etc., intelligendum est : ac proinde Lætitiæ, Tristitiæ, Amoris, Odii, etc., tot species necessario dantur, quot sunt species objectorum, a quibus afficimur. At Cupiditas est ipsa uniuscujusque essentia, seu natura, quatenus ex data quacunque ejus constitutione determinata concipitur ad aliquid agendum (vid. Schol. Prop. 9 hujus) ; ergo, prout unusquisque a causis externis hac aut illa Lætitiæ, Tristitiæ, Amoris, Odii, etc., specie afficitur, hoc est, prout ejus natura hoc aut alio modo constituitur, ita ejus Cupiditas alia atque alia esse, et natura unius a natura alterius Cupiditatis tantum differre necesse est, quantum affectus, a quibus unaquæque oritur, inter se differunt.","kind":"passage"},{"id":"RAW_QGOH_PT_4:P0043","text":"Dantur itaque tot species Cupiditatis, quot sunt species Lætitiæ, Tristitiæ, Amoris, etc., et consequenter (per jam ostensa) quot sunt objectorum species, a quibus afficimur. Q. E. D.","kind":"passage"},{"id":"RAW_QGOH_PT_4:P0044","text":"SCHOLIUM","kind":"passage"},{"id":"RAW_QGOH_PT_4:P0045","text":"Inter affectuum species, quæ (per Prop. præc.) perplurimæ esse debent, insignes sunt Luxuria, Ebrietas, Libido, Avaritia et Ambitio, quæ non nisi Amoris vel Cupiditatis sunt notiones, quæ hujus utriusque affectus naturam explicant per objecta, ad quæ referuntur. Nam per Luxuriam, Ebrietatem, Libidinem, Avaritiam, et Ambitionem nihil aliud intelligimus, quam convivandi, potandi, coeundi, divitiarum et gloriæ immoderatum Amorem vel Cupiditatem. Præterea hi affectus, quatenus eos per solum objectum, ad quod referuntur, ab aliis distinguimus, contrarios non habent. Nam Temperantia, quam Luxuriæ, et Sobrietas, quam Ebrietati, et denique Castitas, quam Libidini opponere solemus, affectus seu passiones non sunt, sed animi indicant potentiam, quæ hos affectus moderatur.","kind":"passage"},{"id":"RAW_QGOH_PT_4:P0046","text":"Cæterum reliquas Affectuum species hic explicare nec possum (quia tot sunt, quot objectorum species), nec, si possem, necesse est. Nam ad id, quod intendimus, nempe ad affectuum vires, et Mentis in eosdem potentiam, determinandum, nobis sufficit, uniuscujusque affectus generalem habere definitionem. Sufficit, inquam, nobis, Affectuum et Mentis communes proprietates intelligere, ut determinare possimus, qualis et quanta sit Mentis potentia in moderandis et coercendis Affectibus. Quamvis itaque magna sit differentia inter hunc et illum Amoris, Odii, vel Cupiditatis affectum, ex. gr. inter Amorem erga liberos et inter amorem erga uxorem, nobis tamen has differentias cognoscere, et affectuum naturam et originem ulterius indagare, non est opus.","kind":"passage"},{"id":"RAW_QGOH_PT_4:P0047","text":"PROPOSITIO LVII","kind":"passage"},{"id":"RAW_QGOH_PT_4:P0048","text":"Quilibet uniuscujusque individui affectus ab affectu alterius tantum discrepat, quantum essentia unius ab essentia alterius differt.","kind":"passage"},{"id":"RAW_QGOH_PT_4:P0049","text":"DEMONSTRATIO","kind":"passage"},{"id":"RAW_QGOH_PT_4:P0050","text":"Hæc Propositio patet ex Axiom. 1, quod vide post Lem. 3 Schol. Prop. 13, p. 2. At nihilominus eandem ex trium primitivorum affectuum definitionibus demonstrabimus.","kind":"passage"},{"id":"RAW_QGOH_PT_4:P0051","text":"Omnes affectus ad Cupiditatem, Lætitiam, vel Tristitiam referuntur, ut eorum quas dedimus definitiones ostendunt. At Cupiditas est ipsa uniuscujusque natura seu essentia (vide ejus Defin. in Schol. Prop. 9 ujus) ; ergo uniuscujusque individui Cupiditas a Cupiditate alterius tantum discrepat, quantum natura seu essentia unius ab essentia alterius differt. Lætitia deinde et Tristitia passiones sunt, quibus uniuscujusque potentia seu conatus in suo esse perseverandi, augetur vel minuitur, juvatur vel coercetur (per Prop. 11 hujus et ejus Schol.). At per conatum in suo esse perseverandi, quatenus ad Mentem et Corpus simul refertur. Appetitum et Cupiditatem intelligimus (vide Schol. Prop. 9 hujus) ; ergo Lætitia et Tristitia est ipsa Cupiditas sive Appetitus, quatenus a causis externis augetur vel minuitur, juvatur vel coercetur, hoc est (per idem Schol.)","kind":"passage"},{"id":"RAW_QGOH_PT_4:P0052","text":"est ipsa cujusque natura ; atque adeo uniuscujusque Lætitia vel Tristitia a Lætitia vel Tristitia alterius tantum etiam discrepat, quantum natura seu essentia unius ab essentia alterius differt et consequenter quilibet uniuscujusque individui affectus ab affectu alterius tantum discrepat, etc. Q. E. D.","kind":"passage"},{"id":"RAW_QGOH_PT_4:P0053","text":"SCHOLIUM","kind":"passage"},{"id":"RAW_QGOH_PT_4:P0054","text":"Hinc sequitur, affectus animalium, quæ irrationalia dicuntur (bruta enim sentire nequaquam dubitare possumus, postquam Mentis novimus originem), ab affectibus hominum tantum differre, quantum eorum natura a natura humana differt. Fertur quidem equus et homo Libidine procreandi ; at ille Libidine equina, hic autem humana. Sic etiam Libidines et Appetitus insectorum, piscium, et avium, alii atque alii esse debent. Quamvis itaque unumquodque individuum sua, qua constat natura contentum vivat eaque gaudeat, vita tamen illa, qua unumquodque est contentum, et gaudium nihil aliud est, quam idea seu anima ejusdem individui, atque adeo gaudium unius a gaudio alterius tantum natura discrepat, quantum essentia unius ab essentia alterius differt. Denique ex præcedenti Propositione sequitur, non parum etiam interesse inter gaudium, quo ebrius ex. gr.","kind":"passage"},{"id":"RAW_QGOH_PT_4:P0055","text":"ducitur, et inter gaudium, quo potitur Philosophus, quod hic in transitu monere volui. Atque hæc de affectibus, qui ad hominem referuntur, quatenus patitur. Superest, ut pauca addam de iis, qui ad eundem referuntur, quatenus agit.","kind":"passage"},{"id":"RAW_QGOH_PT_4:P0056","text":"PROPOSITIO LVIII","kind":"passage"},{"id":"RAW_QGOH_PT_4:P0057","text":"Præter Lætitiam et Cupiditatem, quæ passiones sunt, alii Lætitiæ et Cupiditatis affectus dantur, qui ad nos, quatenus agimus, referuntur.","kind":"passage"},{"id":"RAW_QGOH_PT_4:P0058","text":"DEMONSTRATIO","kind":"passage"},{"id":"RAW_QGOH_PT_4:P0059","text":"Cum Mens se ipsam suamque agendi potentiam concipit, lætatur (per Prop. 53 hujus) : Mens autem se ipsam necessario contemplatur, quando veram sive adæquatam ideam concipit (per Prop. 43 p. II). At Mens quasdam ideas adæquatas concipit (per Schol. 2 Prop. 40 p. II). Ergo eatenus etiam lætatur, quatenus ideas adæquatas concipit, hoc est (per Prop. 1 hujus) quatenus agit. Deinde Mens tam quatenus claras et distinctas, quam quatenus confusas habeL ideas, in suo esse persevare conatur (per Prop. 9 hujus). At per conatum Cupiditatem intelligimus (per ejusdem Schol.) ; ergo Cupiditas ad nos refertur, etiam quatenus întelligimus, sive (per Prop. 1 hujus) quatenus agimus. Q. E. D.","kind":"passage"},{"id":"RAW_QGOH_PT_4:P0060","text":"PROPOSITIO LIX","kind":"passage"},{"id":"RAW_QGOH_PT_4:P0061","text":"Inter omnes affectus, qui ad Mentem, quatenus agit, referuntur, nulli sunt, quam qui ad Lætitiam vel Cupiditatem referuntur.","kind":"passage"},{"id":"RAW_QGOH_PT_4:P0062","text":"DEMONSTRATIO","kind":"passage"},{"id":"RAW_QGOH_PT_4:P0063","text":"Omnes affectus ad Cupiditatem, Lætitiam, vel Tristitiam referuntur, ut eorum quas dedimus, definitiones ostendunt. Per Tristitiam autem intelligimus, quod Mentis cogitandi potentia minuitur vel coercetur (per Prop. 11 hujus et ejus Schol.) ; adeoque Mens quatenus contristatur, eatenus ejus intelligendi, hoc est ejus agendi potentia (per Prop. 1 hujus) minuitur vel coercetur ; adeoque nulli Tristitiæ affectus ad Mentem referri possunt, quatenus agit ; sed tantum affectus Lætitiæ et Cupiditatis qui (per Prop. præc.) eatenus etiam ad Mentem referuntur. Q. E. D.","kind":"passage"},{"id":"RAW_QGOH_PT_4:P0064","text":"SCHOLIUM","kind":"passage"},{"id":"RAW_QGOH_PT_4:P0065","text":"Omnes actiones, quæ sequuntur ex affectibus, qui ad Mentem referuntur, quatenus intelligit ad Fortitudinem refero, quam in Animositatem et Generositatem distinguo. Nam per Animositatem intelligo Cupiditatem, qua unusquisque conatur suum esse ex solo Rationis dictamine conservare.","kind":"passage"},{"id":"RAW_QGOH_PT_4:P0066","text":"Per Generositatem autem Cupiditatem intelligo, qua unusquisque ex solo Rationis dictamine conatur reliquos homines juvare, et sibi amicitia jungere. Eas itaque actiones, quæ solum agentis utile intendunt, ad Animositatem, et quæ alterius etiam utile intendunt, ad Generositatem refero. Temperantia igitur, Sobrietas, et Animi in periculis Præsentia, etc. Animositatis sunt species ; Modestia autem, Clementia, etc., species Generositatis sunt. Atque his puto me præcipuos affectus, animique fluctuationes, quæ ex compositione trium primitivorum affectuum, nempe Cupiditatis, Lætitiæ, et Tristitiæ oriuntur, explicuisse, perque primas suas causas ostendisse. Ex quibus apparet, nos a causis externis multis modis agitari, nosque perinde ut maris undæ, a contrariis ventis agitatæ, fluctuari, nostri eventus atque fati inscios.","kind":"passage"},{"id":"RAW_QGOH_PT_4:P0067","text":"At dixi, me præcipuos tantum, non omnes, qui dari possunt, animi conflictus ostendisse. Nam eadem via, qua supra, procedendo, facile possumus ostendere, Amorem esse junctum Pœnitentiæ, Dedignationi, Pudori, etc. Imo unicuique ex jam dictis clare constare credo, affectus tot modis alii cum aliis componi, indeque tot variationes oriri, ut nullo numero definiri queant. Sed ad meum institutum præcipuos tantum enumeravisse sufficit : nam reliqui, quos omisi, plus curiositatis quam utilitatis haberent. Attamen de Amore hoc notandum restat, quod scilicet sæpissime contingit, dum re, quam appetebamus, fruimur, ut Corpus ex ea fruitione novam acquirat constitutionem, a qua aliter determinatur, et aliæ rerum imagines in eo excitantur, et simul Mens alia imaginari, aliaque cupere incipit. Ex. gr. cum aliquid, quod nos sapore delectare solet, imaginamur, eodem frui, nempe comedere cupimus.","kind":"passage"},{"id":"RAW_QGOH_PT_4:P0068","text":"At quamdiu eodem sic fruimur, stomachus adimpletur, Corpusque aliter constituitur. Si igitur, Corpore jam aliter disposito, ejusdem cibi imago, quia ipse præsens adest, fomentetur, et consequenter conatus etiam, sive Cupiditas, eundem comedendi ; huic Cupiditati seu conatui nova illa constitutio repugnabit, et consequenter cibi, quem appetebamus, præsentia odiosa erit, et hoc est quod Fastidium et Tædium vocamus. Cæterum Corporis affectiones externas, quæ in affectibus observantur, ut sunt tremor, livor, singultus, risus, etc., neglexi, quia ad solum Corpus absque ulla ad Mentem relatione referuntur. Denique de affectuum definitionibus quædam notanda sunt, quas propterea hic ordine repetam, et quid in unaquaque observandum est, iisdem interponam.","kind":"passage"},{"id":"RAW_QGOH_PT_4:P0069","text":"AFFECTUUM DEFINITIONES","kind":"passage"},{"id":"RAW_QGOH_PT_4:P0070","text":"I\n\nCupiditas est ipsa hominis essentia, quatenus ex data quacunque ejus affectione determinata concipitur ad aliquid agendum.","kind":"passage"},{"id":"RAW_QGOH_PT_4:P0071","text":"EXPLICATIO","kind":"passage"},{"id":"RAW_QGOH_PT_4:P0072","text":"Diximus supra in Scholio Propositions 9 hujus Partis, Cupiditatem esse appetitum cum ejusdem conscientia ; appetitum autem esse ipsam hominis essentiam, quatenus determinata est ad ea agendum quæ ipsius conservationi inserviunt. Sed in eodem Scholio etiam monui, me revera inter humanum appetitum et Cupiditatem nullam agnoscere differentiam. Nam sive homo sui appetitus sit conscius, sive non sit, manet tamen appetitus unus idemque ; a que adeo, ne tautologiam committere viderer, Cupiditatem per appetitum explicare nolui, sed eandem ita definire studui, ut omnes humanæ naturæ conatus, quos nomine appetitus, voluntatis, cupiditatis, vel impetus significamus, una comprehenderem. Potueram enim dicere, Cupiditatem esse ipsam hominis essentiam, quatenus determinata concipitur ad aliquid agendum ; sed ex hac definitione (per Prop. 23 p.","kind":"passage"},{"id":"RAW_QGOH_PT_4:P0073","text":"II) non sequeretur, quod Mens possit suæ Cupiditatis sive appetitus esse conscia. Igitur, ut hujus conscientiæ causam involverem, necesse fuit (per eandem Prop.) addere, quatenus ex data quacunque ejus affectione determinata, etc. Nam per affectionem humanæ essentiæ quamcunque ejusdem essentiæ constitutionem intelligimus, sive ea sit innata, sive adventicia, sive quod ipsa per solum Cogitationis, sive per solum Extensionis attributum concipiatur, sive denique quod ad utrumque simul referatur. Hic igitur Cupiditatis nomine intelligo hominis quoscunque conatus, impetus, appetitus, et volitiones, qui pro varia ejusdem hominis constitutione varii, et non raro adeo sibi invicem oppositi sunt, ut homo diversimode trahatur, et, quo se vertat, nesciat.","kind":"passage"},{"id":"RAW_QGOH_PT_4:P0074","text":"II","kind":"passage"},{"id":"RAW_QGOH_PT_4:P0075","text":"Lætitia est hominis transitio a minore ad majorem perfectionem.","kind":"passage"},{"id":"RAW_QGOH_PT_4:P0076","text":"III","kind":"passage"},{"id":"RAW_QGOH_PT_4:P0077","text":"Tristitia est hominis transitio a majore ad minorem perfectionem.","kind":"passage"},{"id":"RAW_QGOH_PT_4:P0078","text":"EXPLICATIO","kind":"passage"},{"id":"RAW_QGOH_PT_4:P0079","text":"Dico transitionem. Nam Laetitia non est ipsa perfectio. Si enim homo cum perfectione, ad quam transit, nasceretur, ejusdem absque Lætitiæ affectu compos esset ; quod clarius apparet ex Tristitiæ affectu, qui huic est contrarius. Nam quod Tristitia in transitione ad minorem perfectionem consistit, non autem in ipsa minore perfectione, nemo negare potest quandoquidem homo eatenus contristari nequit, quatenus alicujus perfectionis est particeps. Nec dicere possumus, quod Tristitia in privatione majoris perfectionis consistat ; nam privatio nihil est, Tristitiæ autem affectus actus est, qui propterea nullus alius esse potest, quam actus transeundi ad minorem perfectionem hoc est, actus quo hominis agendi potentia minuitur vel coercetur (vide Schol. Prop. 11 hujus).","kind":"passage"},{"id":"RAW_QGOH_PT_4:P0080","text":"Cæterum definitiones Hilaritatis, Titillationis, Melancholiæ, et Doloris omitto, quia ad Corpus potissimum referuntur, et non nisi Lætitiæ aut Tristitiæ sunt species.","kind":"passage"},{"id":"RAW_QGOH_PT_4:P0081","text":"IV","kind":"passage"},{"id":"RAW_QGOH_PT_4:P0082","text":"Admiratio est rei alicujus imaginatio, in qua Mens defixa propterea manet, quia hæc singularis imaginatio nullam cum reliquis habet connexionem (vide Prop. 52 hujus cum ejusdem Schol.)","kind":"passage"},{"id":"RAW_QGOH_PT_4:P0083","text":"EXPLICATIO","kind":"passage"}],"sha256":"d4ec954b8860eba628ff66573b16a98b7a7bfd3df2b3d725a48341f9cf33ef09","language_code":"la","language_name":"Latina","translations":[{"id":"SOURCE_TR_20261015T013531448831Z_d6ede9fc","language_code":"en","language_name":"English","title":"Part Three - On the Origin and Nature of the Affects · passage 4","source_sha256":"d4ec954b8860eba628ff66573b16a98b7a7bfd3df2b3d725a48341f9cf33ef09","sha256":"2b756a3fab67871cce199b581a9e55bbf4590e40aabea92d48e7bd59610d4f30","rendition_type":"machine","review_status":"machine","model":"gpt-5.6-terra","created":"2026-10-15T01:35:31.448866+00:00","paragraphs":[{"id":"RAW_QGOH_PT_4:P0001","text":"We see, therefore, that it can happen that what one person loves, another may hate; and that what one fears, another may not fear; and that one and the same man may now love what he formerly hated, and may now dare what he formerly feared, etc. Further, because everyone judges from his own affect what is good, what evil, what better, and what worse (see the Scholium to Prop. 39 of this Part), it follows that men can vary both in judgment and in affect; and hence it comes about that, when we compare some with others, we distinguish them from one another solely by the difference of their affects, and call some intrepid, others timid, and finally others by some other name. For example, I shall call that man intrepid who despises an evil which I am accustomed to fear; and if, moreover, I attend to this, that his Desire to inflict evil on him whom he hates, and to do good to him whom he loves, is not restrained by the fear of an evil by which I am accustomed to be restrained, I shall call him daring.","kind":"passage","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_QGOH_PT_4:P0001"]},{"id":"RAW_QGOH_PT_4:P0002","text":"Further, he will seem timid to me who fears an evil which I am accustomed to despise; and if I further attend to this, that his Desire is restrained by the fear of an evil which cannot restrain me, I shall say that he is faint-hearted; and thus everyone will judge. Finally, from this nature of man and inconstancy of judgment—namely, both that a man often judges concerning things from affect alone, and that the things which he believes conduce to Joy or Sadness, and which therefore (by Prop. 28 of this Part) he strives to promote or remove in order that they may come about, are often merely imaginary—to say nothing now of the other things which we have shown in Part II concerning the uncertainty of things, we easily conceive that a man can often be the cause both that he be saddened and that he be gladdened, or that he be affected both with Sadness and with Joy, accompanied by the idea of himself as cause; and thus we easily understand what Repentance and what Acquiescence in oneself are.","kind":"passage","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_QGOH_PT_4:P0002"]},{"id":"RAW_QGOH_PT_4:P0003","text":"Namely, Repentance is Sadness accompanied by the idea of oneself, and Acquiescence in oneself is Joy accompanied by the idea of oneself as cause; and these affects are most vehement because men believe themselves to be free (see Prop. 49 of this Part).","kind":"passage","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_QGOH_PT_4:P0003"]},{"id":"RAW_QGOH_PT_4:P0004","text":"PROPOSITION LII","kind":"passage","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_QGOH_PT_4:P0004"]},{"id":"RAW_QGOH_PT_4:P0005","text":"We shall not contemplate an object which we have previously seen together with others, or which we imagine to have nothing except what is common to many, as long as one which we imagine to have something singular.","kind":"passage","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_QGOH_PT_4:P0005"]},{"id":"RAW_QGOH_PT_4:P0006","text":"DEMONSTRATION","kind":"passage","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_QGOH_PT_4:P0006"]},{"id":"RAW_QGOH_PT_4:P0007","text":"As soon as we imagine an object which we have seen with others, we immediately also recollect the others (by Prop. 18, Part II, see also its Scholium), and thus from the contemplation of one we immediately fall into the contemplation of another. The same reasoning applies to an object which we imagine to have nothing except what is common to many. For by that very fact we suppose that we contemplate nothing in it which we have not previously seen with others. But when we suppose that we imagine in some object something singular which we have never before seen, we say nothing other than that the Mind, while it contemplates that object, has in itself no other object into whose contemplation it can fall from the contemplation of that one; and thus it is determined to contemplate that one alone. Therefore the object, etc. Q. E. D.","kind":"passage","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_QGOH_PT_4:P0007"]},{"id":"RAW_QGOH_PT_4:P0008","text":"SCHOLIUM","kind":"passage","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_QGOH_PT_4:P0008"]},{"id":"RAW_QGOH_PT_4:P0009","text":"This affection of the Mind, or imagination of a singular thing, insofar as it occupies the Mind alone, is called Wonder; and if it is aroused by an object which we fear, it is called Consternation, because Wonder at an evil so holds a man suspended in the contemplation of that evil alone that he cannot think of other things by which he could avoid that evil. But if what we wonder at is a certain man's prudence, diligence, or something of this sort, because thereby we contemplate the man as greatly surpassing us, then Wonder is called Veneration; otherwise, Horror, if we wonder at a man's anger, envy, etc. Further, if we wonder at the prudence, diligence, etc. of a man whom we love, Love will thereby (by Prop. 12 of this Part) be greater, and this Love joined with Wonder or Veneration we call Devotion.","kind":"passage","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_QGOH_PT_4:P0009"]},{"id":"RAW_QGOH_PT_4:P0010","text":"And in this way we can also conceive Hatred, Hope, Security, and other Affects joined with Wonder; and thus we shall be able to deduce more Affects than are customarily indicated by received terms. From this it is apparent that the names of the Affects were invented more from their common use than from an accurate knowledge of them.","kind":"passage","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_QGOH_PT_4:P0010"]},{"id":"RAW_QGOH_PT_4:P0011","text":"Contempt is opposed to Wonder, the cause of which, however, is generally this: namely, because, from seeing that someone wonders at, loves, fears, etc. some thing, or because some thing at first sight appears similar to things which we wonder at, love, fear, etc. (by Prop. 15 with its Corollary and Prop. 27 of this Part), we are determined to wonder at, love, fear, etc. the same thing. But if, from the presence of the thing itself, or from a more careful contemplation of it, we are compelled to deny of it everything which can be the cause of Wonder, Love, Fear, etc., then, from the very presence of the thing, the Mind remains more determined to think of those things which are not in the object than of those which are in it; whereas, on the contrary, from the presence of an object it is ordinarily accustomed especially to think of that which is in the object.","kind":"passage","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_QGOH_PT_4:P0011"]},{"id":"RAW_QGOH_PT_4:P0012","text":"Further, just as Devotion arises from Wonder at a thing which we love, so Mockery arises from Contempt for a thing which we hate or fear, and Indignation from Contempt for stupidity, just as Veneration arises from Wonder at prudence. Finally, we can conceive Love, Hope, Glory, and other Affects joined with Contempt, and thence deduce yet other Affects, which we are also not accustomed to distinguish from others by any singular term.","kind":"passage","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_QGOH_PT_4:P0012"]},{"id":"RAW_QGOH_PT_4:P0013","text":"PROPOSITION LIII","kind":"passage","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_QGOH_PT_4:P0013"]},{"id":"RAW_QGOH_PT_4:P0014","text":"When the Mind contemplates itself and its power of acting, it rejoices; and it does so the more as it imagines itself and its power of acting more distinctly.","kind":"passage","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_QGOH_PT_4:P0014"]},{"id":"RAW_QGOH_PT_4:P0015","text":"DEMONSTRATION","kind":"passage","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_QGOH_PT_4:P0015"]},{"id":"RAW_QGOH_PT_4:P0016","text":"A man does not know himself except through the affections of his Body and their ideas (by Props. 19 and 23, Part II). Therefore, when it happens that the Mind can contemplate itself, by that very fact it is supposed to pass to a greater perfection, that is (by the Scholium to Prop. 11 of this Part), to be affected with Joy; and with the greater Joy, the more distinctly it can imagine itself and its power of acting. Q. E. D.","kind":"passage","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_QGOH_PT_4:P0016"]},{"id":"RAW_QGOH_PT_4:P0017","text":"COROLLARY","kind":"passage","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_QGOH_PT_4:P0017"]},{"id":"RAW_QGOH_PT_4:P0018","text":"This Joy is fostered more and more as a man imagines himself to be praised by others. For the more he imagines himself to be praised by others, the greater the Joy with which he imagines others to be affected by him, and this accompanied by the idea of himself (by the Scholium to Prop. 29 of this Part); and hence (by Prop. 27 of this Part) he himself is affected with greater Joy accompanied by the idea of himself. Q. E. D.","kind":"passage","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_QGOH_PT_4:P0018"]},{"id":"RAW_QGOH_PT_4:P0019","text":"PROPOSITION LIV","kind":"passage","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_QGOH_PT_4:P0019"]},{"id":"RAW_QGOH_PT_4:P0020","text":"The Mind strives to imagine only those things which posit its power of acting.","kind":"passage","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_QGOH_PT_4:P0020"]},{"id":"RAW_QGOH_PT_4:P0021","text":"DEMONSTRATION","kind":"passage","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_QGOH_PT_4:P0021"]},{"id":"RAW_QGOH_PT_4:P0022","text":"The striving, or power, of the Mind is the very essence of the Mind itself (by Prop. 7 of this Part); but the essence of the Mind (as is self-evident) affirms only that which the Mind is and can do, but not that which it is not and cannot do; and therefore it strives to imagine only that which affirms or posits its power of acting. Q. E. D.","kind":"passage","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_QGOH_PT_4:P0022"]},{"id":"RAW_QGOH_PT_4:P0023","text":"PROPOSITION LV","kind":"passage","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_QGOH_PT_4:P0023"]},{"id":"RAW_QGOH_PT_4:P0024","text":"When the Mind imagines its own powerlessness, it is thereby saddened.","kind":"passage","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_QGOH_PT_4:P0024"]},{"id":"RAW_QGOH_PT_4:P0025","text":"DEMONSTRATION","kind":"passage","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_QGOH_PT_4:P0025"]},{"id":"RAW_QGOH_PT_4:P0026","text":"The essence of the Mind affirms only that which the Mind is and can do; or, it belongs to the nature of the Mind to imagine only those things which posit its power of acting (by the preceding Proposition). Therefore, when we say that the Mind, while it contemplates itself, imagines its own impotence, we say nothing other than that, while the Mind endeavors to imagine something which posits its power of acting, this endeavor of it is restrained, or (by the Scholium to Proposition 11 of this Part) that it is saddened. Q. E. D.","kind":"passage","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_QGOH_PT_4:P0026"]},{"id":"RAW_QGOH_PT_4:P0027","text":"COROLLARY","kind":"passage","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_QGOH_PT_4:P0027"]},{"id":"RAW_QGOH_PT_4:P0028","text":"This Sadness is more and more fostered if it imagines that it is blamed by others; this is demonstrated in the same way as the Corollary to Proposition 53 of this Part.","kind":"passage","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_QGOH_PT_4:P0028"]},{"id":"RAW_QGOH_PT_4:P0029","text":"SCHOLIUM","kind":"passage","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_QGOH_PT_4:P0029"]},{"id":"RAW_QGOH_PT_4:P0030","text":"This Sadness, accompanied by the idea of our weakness, is called Humility; but Joy, which arises from the contemplation of ourselves, is called Self-love, or Acquiescence in oneself. And since this is repeated as often as a man contemplates his virtues, or his power of acting, it therefore also comes about that everyone is eager to recount his deeds and to display the powers both of his body and of his mind, and that men are for this reason troublesome to one another. From this it again follows that men are by nature envious (see the Scholium to Proposition 24 and the Scholium to Proposition 32 of this Part), or that they rejoice at the weakness of their equals and, conversely, are saddened by their virtue. For as often as everyone imagines his actions, he is affected with Joy (by Proposition 53 of this Part), and with the greater Joy the more perfection the actions express and the more distinctly he imagines them; that is (by those things which were said in Scholium 1 to Proposition 40, p.","kind":"passage","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_QGOH_PT_4:P0030"]},{"id":"RAW_QGOH_PT_4:P0031","text":"II), the more he can distinguish them from others and contemplate them as singular things. Therefore, everyone will rejoice most from the contemplation of himself when he contemplates in himself something which he denies of the rest. But if he refers what he affirms of himself to the universal idea of man or animal, he will not rejoice so greatly; and conversely he will be saddened if he imagines his own actions, compared with the actions of others, to be weaker; which Sadness indeed he will endeavor to remove (by Proposition 28 of this Part), and this by wrongly interpreting the actions of his equals, or by embellishing his own as much as he can. It is clear, therefore, that men are by nature prone to Hatred and Envy, to which education itself is added. For parents are accustomed to incite their children to virtue by the stimulus of honor and Envy alone. But perhaps there remains a scruple, namely, that we not infrequently admire and venerate men's virtues. Therefore, in order to remove this, I shall add the following Corollary.","kind":"passage","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_QGOH_PT_4:P0031"]},{"id":"RAW_QGOH_PT_4:P0032","text":"COROLLARY","kind":"passage","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_QGOH_PT_4:P0032"]},{"id":"RAW_QGOH_PT_4:P0033","text":"No one envies virtue in another unless in an equal.","kind":"passage","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_QGOH_PT_4:P0033"]},{"id":"RAW_QGOH_PT_4:P0034","text":"DEMONSTRATION","kind":"passage","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_QGOH_PT_4:P0034"]},{"id":"RAW_QGOH_PT_4:P0035","text":"Envy is Hatred itself (see the Scholium to Proposition 24 of this Part), or (by the Scholium to Proposition 13 of this Part) Sadness, that is (by the Scholium to Proposition 11 of this Part), an affection by which a man's power of acting, or endeavor, is restrained. But a man (by the Scholium to Proposition 9 of this Part) endeavors to do and desires nothing except what can follow from his given nature; therefore, a man will desire no power of acting, or (which is the same thing) virtue, to be ascribed to himself that is proper to the nature of another and alien to his own; and consequently his Desire cannot be restrained, that is (by the Scholium to Proposition 11 of this Part), he cannot be saddened, from the fact that he contemplates some virtue in someone unlike himself, and consequently he will not be able to envy him. But he can indeed envy his equal, who is supposed to be of the same nature as himself. Q. E. D.","kind":"passage","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_QGOH_PT_4:P0035"]},{"id":"RAW_QGOH_PT_4:P0036","text":"SCHOLIUM","kind":"passage","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_QGOH_PT_4:P0036"]},{"id":"RAW_QGOH_PT_4:P0037","text":"Therefore, since above, in the Scholium to Proposition 52 of this Part, we said that we venerate a man because we admire his prudence, fortitude, etc., this happens (as is clear from the Proposition itself) because we imagine these virtues to be singularly present in him, and not as common to our nature; and consequently we shall no more envy him for them than we envy trees their height, or lions their fortitude, etc.","kind":"passage","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_QGOH_PT_4:P0037"]},{"id":"RAW_QGOH_PT_4:P0038","text":"PROPOSITION LVI","kind":"passage","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_QGOH_PT_4:P0038"]},{"id":"RAW_QGOH_PT_4:P0039","text":"There are as many species of Joy, Sadness, and Desire, and consequently of every affect composed of these, such as Fluctuation of Mind, or derived from them, namely Love, Hatred, Hope, Fear, etc., as there are species of objects by which we are affected.","kind":"passage","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_QGOH_PT_4:P0039"]},{"id":"RAW_QGOH_PT_4:P0040","text":"DEMONSTRATION","kind":"passage","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_QGOH_PT_4:P0040"]},{"id":"RAW_QGOH_PT_4:P0041","text":"Joy and Sadness, and consequently the affects which are composed of or derived from them, are passions (by the Scholium to Proposition 11 of this Part); but we (by Proposition 1 of this Part) necessarily suffer insofar as we have inadequate ideas; and insofar as we have them (by Proposition 3 of this Part), we suffer only insofar, that is (see Scholium 1 to Proposition 40, p. II), we necessarily suffer only insofar as we imagine, or (see Proposition 17, p. II, with its Scholium) insofar as we are affected by an affect which involves the nature of our Body and the nature of an external body. Therefore the nature of each passion must necessarily be so explained that the nature of the object by which we are affected is expressed. Namely, Joy which arises from object A, for example, involves the nature of object A itself; and Joy which arises from object B involves the nature of object B itself; and therefore these two affects of Joy are different in nature, because they arise from causes of different nature.","kind":"passage","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_QGOH_PT_4:P0041"]},{"id":"RAW_QGOH_PT_4:P0042","text":"Thus also the affect of Sadness which arises from one object differs in nature from the Sadness which arises from another cause; and this is likewise to be understood of Love, Hatred, Hope, Fear, Fluctuation of Mind, etc. And therefore there are necessarily as many species of Joy, Sadness, Love, Hatred, etc., as there are species of objects by which we are affected. But Desire is the very essence, or nature, of each individual, insofar as it is conceived as determined from any given constitution of it to doing something (see the Scholium to Proposition 9 of this Part); therefore, according as each individual is affected by external causes with this or that species of Joy, Sadness, Love, Hatred, etc., that is, according as his nature is constituted in this or that manner, so his Desire must be now one, now another, and the nature of one Desire must differ from the nature of another Desire as much as the affects from which each arises differ from one another.","kind":"passage","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_QGOH_PT_4:P0042"]},{"id":"RAW_QGOH_PT_4:P0043","text":"Therefore there are as many species of Desire as there are species of Joy, Sadness, Love, etc., and consequently (by what has now been shown) as there are species of objects by which we are affected. Q. E. D.","kind":"passage","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_QGOH_PT_4:P0043"]},{"id":"RAW_QGOH_PT_4:P0044","text":"SCHOLIUM","kind":"passage","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_QGOH_PT_4:P0044"]},{"id":"RAW_QGOH_PT_4:P0045","text":"Among the species of affects, which (by the preceding Proposition) must be very numerous, notable are Gluttony, Drunkenness, Lust, Avarice, and Ambition; these are nothing but notions of Love or Desire, which explain the nature of each of these two affects through the objects to which they are referred. For by Gluttony, Drunkenness, Lust, Avarice, and Ambition we understand nothing but an immoderate Love or Desire of feasting, drinking, copulating, riches, and glory. Furthermore, these affects, insofar as we distinguish them from others by the object alone to which they are referred, have no contraries. For Temperance, which we are accustomed to oppose to Gluttony, and Sobriety, which to Drunkenness, and finally Chastity, which to Lust, are not affects or passions, but indicate a power of mind which moderates these affects.","kind":"passage","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_QGOH_PT_4:P0045"]},{"id":"RAW_QGOH_PT_4:P0046","text":"Moreover, I can neither explain here the remaining species of Affects (because there are as many as there are species of objects), nor, if I could, is it necessary. For in order to determine what we intend, namely, the powers of the affects and the power of the Mind over them, it suffices for us to have a general definition of each affect. It suffices, I say, for us to understand the common properties of the Affects and of the Mind, so that we may determine what and how great is the power of the Mind in moderating and restraining the Affects. Although, therefore, there is a great difference between this and that affect of Love, Hatred, or Desire—for example, between love toward children and love toward a wife—it is nevertheless not necessary for us to know these differences or to investigate further the nature and origin of the affects.","kind":"passage","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_QGOH_PT_4:P0046"]},{"id":"RAW_QGOH_PT_4:P0047","text":"PROPOSITION LVII","kind":"passage","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_QGOH_PT_4:P0047"]},{"id":"RAW_QGOH_PT_4:P0048","text":"The affect of each individual differs from the affect of another as much as the essence of the one differs from the essence of the other.","kind":"passage","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_QGOH_PT_4:P0048"]},{"id":"RAW_QGOH_PT_4:P0049","text":"DEMONSTRATION","kind":"passage","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_QGOH_PT_4:P0049"]},{"id":"RAW_QGOH_PT_4:P0050","text":"This Proposition is evident from Axiom 1, which see after Lemma 3, Scholium of Proposition 13, Part 2. Nevertheless, we shall demonstrate it from the definitions of the three primitive affects.","kind":"passage","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_QGOH_PT_4:P0050"]},{"id":"RAW_QGOH_PT_4:P0051","text":"All affects are referred to Desire, Joy, or Sadness, as the definitions of them which we have given show. But Desire is the very nature or essence of each individual (see its Definition in the Scholium of Proposition 9 of this Part); therefore the Desire of each individual differs from the Desire of another only insofar as the nature or essence of the one differs from the essence of the other. Joy and Sadness, moreover, are passions by which the power of each individual, or its striving to persevere in its being, is increased or diminished, aided or restrained (by Proposition 11 of this Part and its Scholium). But by striving to persevere in its being, insofar as it is referred to Mind and Body together, we understand Appetite and Desire (see the Scholium of Proposition 9 of this Part); therefore Joy and Sadness are Desire itself, or Appetite, insofar as it is increased or diminished, aided or restrained by external causes, that is (by the same Scholium)","kind":"passage","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_QGOH_PT_4:P0051"]},{"id":"RAW_QGOH_PT_4:P0052","text":"the very nature of each; and thus the Joy or Sadness of each individual likewise differs from the Joy or Sadness of another only insofar as the nature or essence of the one differs from the essence of the other, and consequently each affect of each individual differs from an affect of another only insofar, etc. Q. E. D.","kind":"passage","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_QGOH_PT_4:P0052"]},{"id":"RAW_QGOH_PT_4:P0053","text":"SCHOLIUM","kind":"passage","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_QGOH_PT_4:P0053"]},{"id":"RAW_QGOH_PT_4:P0054","text":"Hence it follows that the affects of animals, which are called irrational (for after we have known the origin of the Mind, we can in no way doubt that brutes feel), differ from the affects of human beings only insofar as their nature differs from human nature. A horse indeed is carried by the lust of procreation, and so is a human being; but the former by equine lust, the latter by human lust. Thus too the lusts and appetites of insects, fishes, and birds must be different in each case. Therefore, although each individual lives content with its own nature, of which it consists, and rejoices in it, nevertheless that life with which each is content, and that joy, is nothing other than the idea or soul of the same individual; and thus the joy of one differs by nature from the joy of another only insofar as the essence of the one differs from the essence of the other. Finally, it follows from the preceding Proposition that there is also no small difference between the joy by which a drunkard, for example,","kind":"passage","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_QGOH_PT_4:P0054"]},{"id":"RAW_QGOH_PT_4:P0055","text":"is carried away, and the joy possessed by a Philosopher, which I wished to note here in passing. And these matters concern affects that are referred to a human being insofar as he is acted upon. It remains for me to add a few words concerning those which are referred to him insofar as he acts.","kind":"passage","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_QGOH_PT_4:P0055"]},{"id":"RAW_QGOH_PT_4:P0056","text":"PROPOSITION LVIII","kind":"passage","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_QGOH_PT_4:P0056"]},{"id":"RAW_QGOH_PT_4:P0057","text":"Besides Joy and Desire, which are passions, there are other affects of Joy and Desire which are referred to us insofar as we act.","kind":"passage","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_QGOH_PT_4:P0057"]},{"id":"RAW_QGOH_PT_4:P0058","text":"DEMONSTRATION","kind":"passage","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_QGOH_PT_4:P0058"]},{"id":"RAW_QGOH_PT_4:P0059","text":"When the Mind conceives itself and its power of acting, it rejoices (by Proposition 53 of this Part); but the Mind necessarily contemplates itself when it conceives a true, or adequate, idea (by Proposition 43, Part II). But the Mind conceives some adequate ideas (by Scholium 2 of Proposition 40, Part II). Therefore, it also rejoices insofar as it conceives adequate ideas, that is (by Proposition 1 of this Part), insofar as it acts. Further, the Mind strives to persevere in its being both insofar as it has clear and distinct ideas and insofar as it has confused ideas (by Proposition 9 of this Part). But by striving we understand Desire (by the Scholium to that Proposition); therefore Desire is referred to us even insofar as we understand, or (by Proposition 1 of this Part) insofar as we act. Q. E. D.","kind":"passage","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_QGOH_PT_4:P0059"]},{"id":"RAW_QGOH_PT_4:P0060","text":"PROPOSITION LIX","kind":"passage","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_QGOH_PT_4:P0060"]},{"id":"RAW_QGOH_PT_4:P0061","text":"Among all the affects which are referred to the Mind insofar as it acts, there are none except those which are referred to Joy or Desire.","kind":"passage","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_QGOH_PT_4:P0061"]},{"id":"RAW_QGOH_PT_4:P0062","text":"DEMONSTRATION","kind":"passage","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_QGOH_PT_4:P0062"]},{"id":"RAW_QGOH_PT_4:P0063","text":"All affects are referred to Desire, Joy, or Sadness, as the definitions of them which we have given show. But by Sadness we understand that the Mind's power of thinking is diminished or restrained (by Proposition 11 of this Part and its Scholium); and therefore, insofar as the Mind is saddened, its power of understanding, that is, its power of acting (by Proposition 1 of this Part), is diminished or restrained; and therefore no affects of Sadness can be referred to the Mind insofar as it acts, but only affects of Joy and Desire, which (by the preceding Proposition) are also referred to the Mind insofar as it acts. Q. E. D.","kind":"passage","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_QGOH_PT_4:P0063"]},{"id":"RAW_QGOH_PT_4:P0064","text":"SCHOLIUM","kind":"passage","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_QGOH_PT_4:P0064"]},{"id":"RAW_QGOH_PT_4:P0065","text":"I refer all actions which follow from affects that are referred to the Mind insofar as it understands to Fortitude, which I distinguish into Strength of Mind and Generosity. For by Strength of Mind I understand the Desire by which each one strives, from the dictate of Reason alone, to preserve his own being.","kind":"passage","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_QGOH_PT_4:P0065"]},{"id":"RAW_QGOH_PT_4:P0066","text":"But by Generosity I understand the Desire by which each one strives, from the dictate of Reason alone, to aid other human beings and to join them to himself in friendship. Therefore I refer those actions which intend the advantage of the agent alone to Strength of Mind, and those which also intend the advantage of another to Generosity. Thus Temperance, Sobriety, and Presence of Mind amid dangers, etc., are species of Strength of Mind; whereas Modesty, Clemency, etc., are species of Generosity. And by these, I think, I have explained the principal affects and fluctuations of the mind which arise from the composition of the three primitive affects, namely Desire, Joy, and Sadness, and have shown them through their first causes. From these it appears that we are agitated in many ways by external causes, and that, like waves of the sea stirred by contrary winds, we fluctuate, unaware of our outcome and fate.","kind":"passage","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_QGOH_PT_4:P0066"]},{"id":"RAW_QGOH_PT_4:P0067","text":"But I said that I had shown only the principal conflicts of the mind, not all that can be given. For, proceeding by the same method as above, we can easily show that Love is joined with Repentance, Indignation, Shame, etc. Indeed, I believe it is clear to everyone from what has already been said that affects are combined with one another in so many ways, and that hence so many variations arise that they cannot be determined by any number. But for my purpose it suffices to have enumerated only the principal ones; for the rest, which I have omitted, would have more curiosity than utility. Yet concerning Love this point remains to be noted: namely, that it very often happens, while we enjoy a thing which we had desired, that from this enjoyment the Body acquires a new constitution, by which it is otherwise determined, and other images of things are aroused in it, and at the same time the Mind begins to imagine other things and to desire other things. For example, when we imagine something that is wont to delight us by its taste, we desire to enjoy it, namely, to eat it.","kind":"passage","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_QGOH_PT_4:P0067"]},{"id":"RAW_QGOH_PT_4:P0068","text":"But while we thus enjoy it, the stomach is filled and the Body is otherwise constituted. If, therefore, once the Body has been otherwise disposed, the image of the same food is fostered because it is itself present, and consequently the striving also, or Desire, to eat the same food, this new constitution will resist that Desire or striving; and consequently the presence of the food which we had desired will be hateful, and this is what we call Disgust and Weariness. Besides, I have passed over the external affections of the Body which are observed in affects, such as trembling, pallor, sobbing, laughter, etc., because they are referred to the Body alone, without any relation to the Mind. Finally, there are certain points to be noted concerning the definitions of the affects; for that reason I shall repeat them here in order, and shall insert along with them what is to be observed in each.","kind":"passage","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_QGOH_PT_4:P0068"]},{"id":"RAW_QGOH_PT_4:P0069","text":"DEFINITIONS OF THE AFFECTS","kind":"passage","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_QGOH_PT_4:P0069"]},{"id":"RAW_QGOH_PT_4:P0070","text":"I\n\nDesire is the very essence of a human being, insofar as it is conceived as determined, from any given affection of it, to do something.","kind":"passage","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_QGOH_PT_4:P0070"]},{"id":"RAW_QGOH_PT_4:P0071","text":"EXPLANATION","kind":"passage","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_QGOH_PT_4:P0071"]},{"id":"RAW_QGOH_PT_4:P0072","text":"We said above, in the Scholium of Proposition 9 of this Part, that Desire is appetite together with consciousness thereof; but appetite is the very essence of a human being, insofar as it is determined to do those things which serve his preservation. But in the same Scholium I also noted that I recognize in reality no difference between human appetite and Desire. For whether a human being is conscious of his appetite or is not conscious of it, the appetite nevertheless remains one and the same; and therefore, lest I should seem to commit a tautology, I did not wish to explain Desire through appetite, but endeavored to define it in such a way as to comprehend in one all the strivings of human nature which we signify by the name of appetite, will, desire, or impulse. For I could have said that Desire is the very essence of a human being, insofar as it is conceived as determined to do something; but from this definition (by Proposition 23, Part","kind":"passage","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_QGOH_PT_4:P0072"]},{"id":"RAW_QGOH_PT_4:P0073","text":"II) it would not follow that the Mind can be conscious of its Desire, or appetite. Therefore, in order to involve the cause of this consciousness, it was necessary (by the same Proposition) to add, insofar as from any given affection of it determined, etc. For by any affection of the human essence we understand any constitution of that same essence, whether it be innate or adventitious, whether it be conceived through the attribute of Thought alone, or through that of Extension alone, or finally whether it be referred to both together. Here, therefore, by the name Desire I understand any strivings, impulses, appetites, and volitions of a man, which vary according to the varying constitution of the same man, and are not infrequently so opposed to one another that the man is drawn in different directions and does not know where to turn.","kind":"passage","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_QGOH_PT_4:P0073"]},{"id":"RAW_QGOH_PT_4:P0074","text":"II","kind":"passage","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_QGOH_PT_4:P0074"]},{"id":"RAW_QGOH_PT_4:P0075","text":"Joy is man's transition from a lesser to a greater perfection.","kind":"passage","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_QGOH_PT_4:P0075"]},{"id":"RAW_QGOH_PT_4:P0076","text":"III","kind":"passage","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_QGOH_PT_4:P0076"]},{"id":"RAW_QGOH_PT_4:P0077","text":"Sadness is man's transition from a greater to a lesser perfection.","kind":"passage","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_QGOH_PT_4:P0077"]},{"id":"RAW_QGOH_PT_4:P0078","text":"EXPLANATION","kind":"passage","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_QGOH_PT_4:P0078"]},{"id":"RAW_QGOH_PT_4:P0079","text":"I say transition. For Joy is not perfection itself. For if a man were born with the perfection to which he passes, he would possess it without an affect of Joy; this appears more clearly from the affect of Sadness, which is contrary to it. For that Sadness consists in the transition to a lesser perfection, and not in lesser perfection itself, no one can deny, since a man cannot be saddened insofar as he partakes of some perfection. Nor can we say that Sadness consists in the privation of a greater perfection; for privation is nothing, whereas the affect of Sadness is an act, which consequently can be no other than the act of passing to a lesser perfection, that is, an act by which man's power of acting is diminished or restrained (see the Scholium to Proposition 11 of this Part).","kind":"passage","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_QGOH_PT_4:P0079"]},{"id":"RAW_QGOH_PT_4:P0080","text":"Moreover, I omit the definitions of Cheerfulness, Titillation, Melancholy, and Pain, because they are referred chiefly to the Body, and are only species of Joy or Sadness.","kind":"passage","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_QGOH_PT_4:P0080"]},{"id":"RAW_QGOH_PT_4:P0081","text":"IV","kind":"passage","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_QGOH_PT_4:P0081"]},{"id":"RAW_QGOH_PT_4:P0082","text":"Wonder is the imagination of some thing, in which the Mind remains fixed because this singular imagination has no connection with the rest (see Proposition 52 of this Part together with its Scholium).","kind":"passage","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_QGOH_PT_4:P0082"]},{"id":"RAW_QGOH_PT_4:P0083","text":"EXPLANATION","kind":"passage","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_QGOH_PT_4:P0083"]}],"source_digest":"d233cbdc8ced36fc77ae4e763a6227fc1840e7c499484f687aa0b739898aaaab","source_language_code":"la","original_title":"Pars tertia - De origine et natura affectuum · passage 4"}],"passage_basis":"frozen-grounding","original_title":"Pars tertia - De origine et natura affectuum · passage 4","title_language_code":"en"},{"id":"RAW_QGOH_PT_5","title":"Part Three - On the Origin and Nature of the Affects · passage 5","order":13,"chapter_id":"CH_QGOH_PT_5","text":"In Scholio Propositionis 18 Partis II ostendimus, quænam sit causa cur Mens ex contemplatione unius rei statim in alterius rei cogitationem incidat, videlicet quia earum rerum imagines invicem concatenatæ et ita ordinatæ sunt, ut alia aliam sequatur ; quod quidem concipi nequit quando rei imago nova est, sed Mens in ejusdem rei contemplatione detinebitur, donec ab aliis causis ad alia cogitandum determinetur. Rei itaque novæ imaginatio in se considerata ejusdem naturæ est ac reliquæ ; et hac de causa ego Admirationem inter affectus non numero, nec causam video, cur id facerem, quandoquidem hæc Mentis distractio ex nulla causa positiva, quæ Mentem ab aliis distrahat, oritur, sed tantum ex eo, quod causa, cur Mens ex unius rei contemplatione ad alia cogitandum determinatur, deficit. Tres igitur (ut in Schol. Prop. 11 hujus monui) tantum affectus primitivos seu primarios agnosco, nempe Lætitiæ, Tristitiæ, et Cupiditatis ; nec alia de causa verba de Admiratione feci, quam quia usu factum est, ut quidam affectus, qui ex tribus primitivis derivantur, aliis nominibus indicari soleant, quando ad objecta, quæ admiramur, referuntur ; quæ quidem ratio me ex æquo movet, ut etiam Contemptus definitionem his adjungam.\n\nV\n\nContemptus est rei alicujus imaginatio, quæ Mentem adeo parum tangit, ut ipsa Mens ex rei præsentia magis moveatur ad ea imaginandum, quæ in ipsa re non sunt, quam quæ in ipsa sunt (vide Schol. Prop. 52 hujus).\n\nDefinitiones Venerationis et Dedignationis missas hic facio, quia nulli, quod sciam, affectus ex his nomen trahunt.\n\nVI\n\nAmor est Lætitia concomitante idea causæ externæ.\n\nEXPLICATIO\n\nHæc Definitio satis clare Amoris essentiam explicat ; illa vero Auctorum, qui definiunt Amorem esse voluntatem amantis se jungendi rei amatæ, non Amoris essentiam, sed ejus proprietatem exprimit, et, quia Amoris essentia non satis ab Auctoribus perspecta fuit, ideo neque ejus proprietatis ullum clarum conceptum habere potuerunt ; et hinc factum, ut eorum definitionem admodum obscuram esse omnes judicaverint. Verum notandum, cum dico, proprietatem esse in amante, se voluntate jungere rei amatæ, me per voluntatem non intelligere consensum, vel animi deliberationem seu liberum decretum (nam hoc fictitium esse demonstraviinus Propositione 48 partis II), nec etiam Cupiditatem sese jungendi rei amatæ, quando abest, vel perseverandi in ipsius præsentia, quando adest ; potest namque amor absque hac aut illa Cupiditate concipi ; sed per voluntatem me Acquiescentiam intelligere, quæ est in Amante ob rei amatæ præsentiam, a qua Lætitia amantis corroboratur, aut saltem fovetur.\n\nVII\n\nOdium est Tristitia concomitante idea causæ externæ.\n\nEXPLICATIO\n\nQuæ hic notanda sunt, ex dictis in præcedentis Definitionis Explicatione facile percipiuntur (vide præterea Schol. Prop. 13 hujus).\n\nVIII\n\nPropensio est Lætitia concomitante idea alicujus rei, quæ per accidens causa est Lætitiæ.\n\nIX\n\nAversio est Tristitia concomitante idea alicujus rei, quæ per accidens causa est Tristitiæ (de his vide Prop. 15 Schol. hujus).\n\nX\n\nDevotio est Amor erga eum, quem admiramur.\n\nEXPLICATIO\n\nAdmirationem oriri ex rei novitate, ostendimus Propositione 52 hujus. Si igitur contingat, ut id, quod admiramur, sæpe imaginemur, idem admirari desinemus ; atque adeo videmus, Devotionis affectum facile in simplicem Amorem degenerare.\n\nXI\n\nIrrisio est Lætitia orta ex eo, quod aliquid, quod contemnimus, in re quam odimus, inessse imaginamur.\n\nEXPLICATIO\n\nQuatenus rem, quam odimus, contemnimus, eatenus de eadem existentiam negamus (vide Schol. Prop. 52 hujus), et eatenus (per Prop. 20 hujus) lætamur. Sed quoniam supponimus, hominem id, quod irridet, odio tamen habere, sequitur, hanc Lætitiam solidam non esse (vid. Schol. Prop. 47 hujus).\n\nXII\n\nSpes est inconstans Lætitia, orta ex idea rei futuræ vel præteritæ, de cujus eventu aliquatenus dubitamus.\n\nXIII\n\nMetus est inconstans Tristitia, orta ex irea rei futuræ vel præteritæ, de cujus eventu aliquatenus dubitamus (vide de his Schol. 2 Prop. 18 hujus).\n\nEXPLICATIO\n\nEx his definitionibus sequitur, non dari Spem sine Metu, neque Metum sine Spe. Qui enim Spe pendet et de rei eventu dubitat, is aliquid imaginari supponitur, quod rei futuræ existentiam secludit; atque adeo eatenus contristari (per Prop. 19 hujus), et consequenter, dum Spe pendet, metuere, ut res eveniat. Qui autem contra in Metu est, hoc est, de rei, quam odit, eventu dubitat, aliquid etiam imaginatur, quod ejusdem rei existentiam secludit ; atque adeo (per Prop. 20 hujus) lætatur, et consequenter eatenus Spem habet, ne eveniat.\n\nXIV\n\nSecuritas est Lætitia orta ex idea rei futuræ vel præteritæ, de qua dubitandi causa sublata est.\n\nXV\n\nDesperatio est Tristitia orta ex idea rei futuræ vel præteritæ de qua dubitandi causa sublata est.\n\nEXPLICATIO\n\nOritur itaque ex Spe Securitas, et ex Metu Desperatio, quando de rei eventu dubitandi causa tollitur ; quod fit, quia homo rem præteritam vel futuram adesse imaginatur et ut præsentem contemplatur, vel quia alia imaginatur, quæ existentiam earum rerum secludunt, quæ ipsi dubium injiciebant. Nam tametsi de rerum singularium eventu (per Coroll. Prop. 31 p. II) nunquam possumus esse certi, fieri tamen potest, ut de earum eventu non dubitemus. Aliud enim esse ostendimus (vide Schol. Prop. 49 p. II) de re non dubitare, aliud rei certitudinem habere ; atque adeo fieri potest, ut ex imagine rei præteritæ aut futuræ eodem Lætitiæ vel Tristitiæ affectu afficiamur, ac ex rei præsentis imagine, ut in Propositione 18 hujus demonstravimus, quam cum ejusdem Scholio vide.\n\nXVI\n\nGaudium est Lætitia concomitante idea rei præteritæ, quæ præter Spem evenit.\n\nXVII\n\nConscientiæ morsus est Tristitia concomitante idea rei præteritæ, quæ præter Spem evenit.\n\nXVIII\n\nCommiseratio est Tristitia concomitante idea mali, quod alteri, quem nobis similem esse imaginamur, evenit (vid. Schol. Prop. 22 et Schol. 27 hujus).\n\nEXPLICATIO\n\nInter Commiserationem et Misericordiam nulla videtur esse differentia, nisi forte, quod Commiseratio singularem affectum respiciat, Misericordia autem ejus habitum.\n\nXIX\n\nFavor est Amor erga aliquem, qui alteri benefecit.\n\nXX\n\nIndignatio est Odium erga aliquem, qui alteri malefecit.\n\nEXPLICATIO\n\nHæc nomina ex communi usu aliud significare scio. Sed meum institutum non est, verborum significationem, sed rerum naturam explicare, easque iis vocabulis indicare, quorum significatio, quam ex usu habent, a significatione, qua eadem usurpare volo, non omnino abhorret, quod semel monuisse sufficiat. Caeterum horum affectuum causam vide in Corollario 1 Propositionis 27 et Scholio Propositionis 22 hujus Partis.\n\nXXI\n\nExistimatio est de aliquo præ Amore plus justo sentire.\n\nXXII\n\nDespectus est de aliquo præ Odio minus justo sentire.\n\nEXPLICATIO\n\nEst itaque Existimatio Amoris, et Despectus Odii effectus sive proprietas ; atque adeo potest Existimatio etiam definiri, quod sit Amor, quatenus hominem ita afficit, ut de re amata plus justo sentiat, et contra Despectus, quod sit. Odium, quatenus hominem ita afficit, ut de eo, quem odio habet, minus justo sentiat (vide de his Schol. Prop. 26 hujus).\n\nXXIII\n\nInvidia est Odium, quatenus hominem ita afficit, ut ex alterius felicitate contristetur, et contra, ut ex alterius malo gaudeat.\n\nEXPLICATIO\n\nInvidiæ opponitur communiter Misericordia, quæ proinde, invita vocabuli significatione, sic definiri potest :\n\nXXIV\n\nMisericordia est Amor, quatenus hominem ita afficit, ut ex bono alterius gaudeat, et contra ut ex alterius malo contristetur.\n\nEXPLICATIO\n\nCaeterum de Invidia vide Schol. Prop. 24 et Schol. Prop. 32 hujus. Atque hi affectus Lætitiæ et Tristitiæ sunt, quos idea rei externæ comitatur tanquam causa per se vel per accidens. Hinc ad alios transeo, quos idea rei internæ comitatur tanquam causa.\n\nXXV\n\nAcquiescentia in se ipso est Lætitia orta ex eo, quod homo se ipsum suamque agendi potentiam contemplatur.\n\nXXVI\n\nHumilitas est Tristitia orta ex eo, quod homo suam impotentiam sive imbecillitatem contemplatur.\n\nEXPLICATIO\n\nAcquiescentia in se ipso Humilitati opponitur, quatenus per eandem intelligimus Lætitiam, quæ ex eo oritur, quod nostram agendi potentiam contemplamur ; sed quatenus per ipsam etiam intelligimus Lætitiam concomitante idea alicujus facti, quod nos ex Mentis libero decreto fecisse credimus, tum Pœnitentiae opponitur, quæ a nobis sic definitur :\n\nXXVII\n\nPœnitentia est Tristitia concomitante idea alicujus facti, quod nos ex libero Mentis decreto fecisse credimus.\n\nEXPLICATIO\n\nHorum affectuum causas ostendimus in Schol. Prop. 51 hujus, et Prop. 53, 54 et 55 hujus, ejusque Schol. De libero autem Mentis decreto vide Schol. Prop. 35 p. II. Sed hic præterea notandum venit, mirum non esse, quod omnes omnino actus, qui ex consuetudine pravi vocantur, sequatur Tristitia, et illos, qui recti dicuntur, Lætitia. Nam hoc ab educatione potissimum pendere, facile ex supra dictis intelligimus. Parentes nimirum, illos exprobrando, liberosque propter eosdem sæpe objurgando, hos contra suadendo et laudando, effecerunt, ut Tristitiæ commotiones illis, Lætitiæ vero his jungerentur. Quod ipsa etiam experientia comprobatur. Nam consuetudo et Religio non est omnibus eadem, sed contra, quæ apud alios sacra, apud alios profana, et quæ apud alios honesta, apud alios turpia sunt. Prout igitur unusquisque educatus est, ita facti alicujus pœnitet, vel eodem gloriatur.\n\nXXVIII\n\nSuperbia est de se præ Amore sui plus justo sentire.\n\nEXPLICATIO\n\nDiffert igitur Superbia ab Existimatione, quod hæc ad objectum externum, Superbia autem ad ipsum hominem, de se plus justo sentientem, referatur. Cæterum, ut Existimatio Amoris, sic Superbia Philautiæ effectus vel proprietas est, quæ propterea etiam definiri potest, quod sit Amor sui, sive Acquiescentia in se ipso, quatenus hominem ita afficit, ut de se plus justo sentiat (vid. Schol. Prop. 26 hujus). Huic affectui non datur contrarius. Nam nemo de se, præ Odio sui, minus justo sentit ; imo nemo de se minus justo sentit, quatenus imaginatur, se hoc vel illud non posse. Nam quicquid homo imaginatur se non posse, id necessario imaginatur, et hac imaginatione ita disponitur, ut id agere revera non possit, quod se non posse imaginatur. Quamdiu enim imaginatur se hoc vel illud non posse, tamdiu ad agendum non est determinatus, et consequenter tamdiu impossibile ei est, ut id agat. Verumenimvero si ad illa attendamus, quæ a sola opinione pendent, concipere poterimus fieri posse, ut homo de se minus justo sentiat ; fieri enim potest, ut aliquis, dum tristis imbecillitatem contemplatur suam, imaginetur, se ab omnibus contemni, idque dum reliqui nihil minus cogitant, quam ipsum contemnere. Potest præterea homo de se minus justo sentire, si aliquid de se in præsenti neget cum relatione ad futurum tempus, cujus est incertus ; ut quod neget, se nihil certi posse concipere, nihilque nisi prava vel turpia posse cupere vel agere, etc. Possumus deinde dicere, aliquem de se minus justo sentire, cum videmus, ipsum ex nimio pudoris metu ea non audere, quæ alii ipsi æquales audent. Hunc igitur affectum possumus Superbiæ opponere, quem Abjectionem vocabo ; nam ut ex Acquiescentia in se ipso Superbia, sic ex Humilitate Abjectio oritur, quæ proinde a nobis sic definitur.\n\nXXIX\n\nAbjectio est de se præ Tristitia minus justo sentire.\n\nEXPLICATIO\n\nSolemus tamen sæpe Superbiæ Humililatem opponere, sed tum magis ad utriusque effectus quam naturam attendimus. Solemus namque illum superbum vocare, qui nimis gloriatur (vid. Schol. Prop. 30 hujus), qui non nisi virtutes suas, et aliorum non nisi vitia narrat, qui omnibus præferri vult, et qui denique ea gravitate et ornatu incedit, quo solent alii, qui longe supra ipsum sunt positi. Contra illum humilem vocamus, qui sæpius erubescit, qui sua vitia fatetur, et aliorum virtutes narrat, qui omnibus cedit, et qui denique submisso capite ambulat, et se ornare negligit. Cæterum hi affectus, nempe Humilitas et Abjectio, rarissimi sunt. Nam natura humana, in se considerata, contra eosdem quantum potest, nititur (vide Prop. 13 et 54 hujus) ; et ideo, qui maxime creduntur abjecti et humiles esse, maxime plerumque ambitiosi et invidi sunt.\n\nXXX\n\nGloria est Lætitia concomitante idea alicujus nostræ actionis, quam alios laudare imaginamur.\n\nXXXI\n\nPudor est Tristitia concomitante idea alicujus actionis, quam alios vituperare imaginamur.\n\nEXPLICATIO\n\nDe his vide Scholium Propositionis 30 hujus Partis. Sed hic notanda est differentia, quæ est inter Pudorem et Verecundiam. Est enim Pudor Tristitia, quæ sequitur factum, cujus pudet. Verecundia autem est Metus seu Timor Pudoris, quo homo continetur, ne aliquid turpe committat. Verecundiæ opponi solet Impudentia, quæ revera affectus non est, ut suo loco ostendam ; sed affectuum nomina (ut jam monui) magis eorum usum quam naturam respiciunt. Atque his Lætitiæ et Tristitiæ affectus, quos explicare proposueram, absolvi. Pergo itaque ad illos, quos ad Cupiditatem refero.\n\nXXXII\n\nDesiderium est Cupiditas sive Appetitus re aliqua potiundi, quæ ejusdem rei memoria fovetur, et simul aliarum rerum memoria, quæ ejusdem rei appetendæ existentiam secludunt, coercetur.\n\nEXPLICATIO\n\nCum alicujus rei recordamur, ut jam sæpe diximus, eo ipso disponimur ad eandem eodem affectu contemplandum, ac si res præsens adesset ; sed hæc dispositio, seu conatus, dum vigilamus, plerumque cohibetur ab imaginibus rerum, quæ existentiam ejus, cujus recordamur, secludunt. Quando itaque rei meminimus, quæ nos aliquo Lætitiæ genere afficit, eo ipso conamur eandem, cum eodem Lætitiæ affectu ut præsentem contemplari, qui quidem conatus statim cohibetur memoria rerum, quæ illius existentiam secludunt. Quare desiderium revera Tristitia est, quæ Lætitiæ opponitur illi, quæ ex absentia rei, quam odimus, oritur, de qua vide Scholium Propositionis 47 hujus partis. Sed quia nomen Desiderium Cupiditatem respicere videtur, ideo hunc affectum ad Cupiditatis affectus refero.\n\nXXXIII\n\nÆmulatio est alicujus rei Cupiditas, quæ nobis ingeneratur ex eo, quod alios eandem Cupiditatem habere imaginamur.\n\nEXPLICATIO\n\nQui fugit, quia alios fugere, vel qui timet, quia alios timere videt ; vel etiam ille, qui ex eo, quod aliquem manum suam combussisse videt, manum ad se contrahit, corpusque movet, quasi ipsius manus combureretur ; eum imitari quidem alterius affectum, sed non eundem æmulari dicemus ; non quia aliam æmulationis, aliam imitationis novimus causam, sed quia usu factum est, ut illum tantum vocemus æmulum, qui id, quod honestum utile, vel jucundum esse judicamus, imitatur. Cæterum de Æmulalionis causa vide Propositionem 27 hujus Partis cum ejus Scholio. Cur autem huic affectui plerumque juncta sit Invidia, de eovide Propositionem 32 hujus cum ejusdem Scholio.\n\nXXXIV\n\nGratia seu Gratitudo est Cupiditas, seu Amoris studium, quo ei benefacere conamur, qui in nos pari amoris affectu beneficium contulit. Vide Prop. 39 cum Schol. Prop. 41 hujus.\n\nXXXV\n\nBenevolentia est Cupiditas benefaciendi ei, cujus nos miseret. Vide Schol. Prop. 27 hujus.\n\nXXXVI\n\nIra est Cupiditas, qua ex Odio incitamur ad illi, quem odimus, malum inferendum. Vide Prop. 39 hujus.\n\nXXXVII\n\nVindicta est Cupiditas, qua ex reciproco Odio concitamur ad malum inferendum ei, qui nobis pari affectu damnum intulit Vide 2 Coroll. Prop. 40 hujus cum ejusdem Schol.\n\nXXXVIII\n\nCrudelitas seu Sævitia est Cupiditas, qua aliquis concitatur ad malum inferendum ei, quem amamus, vel cujus non miseret.\n\nEXPLICATIO\n\nCrudelitati opponitur Clementia, quæ passio non est, sed animi potentia, quo homo iram et vindictam moderatur.\n\nXXXIX\n\nTimor est Cupiditas majus, quod metuimus, malum minore vitandi. Vide Schol. Prop. 39 hujus.\n\nXL\n\nAudacia est Cupiditas, qua aliquis incitatur ad aliquid agendum cum periculo, quod ejus æquales subire metuunt.\n\nXLI\n\nPusillanimitas dicitur de eo, cujus Cupiditas coercetur timore periculi, quod ejus æquales subire audent.\n\nEXPLICATIO\n\nEst igitur Pusillanimitas nihil aliud, quam Metus alicujus mali, quod plerique non solent metuere ; quare ipsam ad Cupiditatis affectus non refero. Eandem tamen hic explicare volui, quia, quatenus ad Cupiditatem attendimus, affectui Audaciæ revera opponitur.\n\nXLII\n\nConsternatio dicitur de eo, cujus Cupiditas malum vitandi coercetur admiratione mali, quod timet.\n\nEXPLICATIO\n\nEst itaque Consternatio Pusillanimitatis species. Sed quia Consternatio ex duplici Timore oritur, ideo commodius definiri potest, quod sit Metus, qui hominem stupefactum aut fluctuantem ita continet, ut is malum amovere non possit. Dico stupefactum, quatenus ejus Cupiditatem malum amovendi admiratione coerceri intelligimus. Fluctuantem autem dico, quatenus concipimus eandem Cupiditatem coerceri Timore alterius mali, quod ipsum æque cruciat : unde fit, ut, quodnam ex duobus avertat, nesciat. De his vide Schol. Prop. 39 et Schol. Prop. 52 hujus. Cæterum de Pusillanimitate et Audacia vide Schol. Prop. 51 hujus.\n\nXLIII\n\nHumanitas seu Modestia est Cupiditas ea faciendi, quæ hominibus placent, et omittendi, quæ displicent.\n\nXLIV\n\nAmbitio est immodica gloriæ Cupiditas.\n\nEXPLICATIO\n\nAmbitio est Cupiditas, qua omnes affectus (per Prop. 27 et 31 hujus) foventur et corroborantur ; et ideo hic affectus vix superari potest. Nam quamdiu homo aliqua Cupiditate tenetur, hac simul necessario tenetur. Optimus quisque, inquit Cicero, maxime gloria ducitur. Philosophi etiam libris, quos de contemnenda gloria scribunt, nomen suum inscribunt, etc.\n\nXLV\n\nLuxuria est immoderata convivandi Cupiditas, vel etiam Amor.\n\nXLVI\n\nEbrietas est immoderata potandi Cupiditas et Amor.\n\nXLVII\n\nAvaritia est immoderata divitiarum Cupiditas et Armor.\n\nXLVIII\n\nLibido est etiam Cupiditas et Amor in commiscendis corporibus.\n\nEXPLICATIO\n\nSive hæc coeundi Cupiditas moderata sit, sive non sit, Libido appellari solet. Porro hi quinque affectus (ut in Schol. Prop. 56 hujus monui) contrarios non habent. Nam Modestia species est Ambitionis, de qua vide Schol. Prop. 29 hujus. Temperantiam deinde, Sobrietatem, et Castitatem Mentis potentiam, non autem passionem indicare, jam etiam monui. Et tametsi fieri potest, ut homo avarus, ambitiosus, vel timidus a nimio cibo, potu, et coitu, abstineat ; Avaritia tamen, Ambitio et Timor luxuriæ, ebrietati, vel libidini non sunt contrarii. Nam avarus in cibum et potum alienum se ingurgitare plerumque desiderat. Ambitiosus autem, modo speret fore clam, in nulla re sibi temperabit, et si inter ebrios vivat et libidinosos, ideo quia ambitiosus est, proclivior erit ad eadem vitia. Timidus denique id, quod non vult, facit. Nam quamvis mortis vitandæ causa divitias in mare projiciat, manet tamen avarus et si libidinosus tristis est, quod sibi morem gerere nequeat, non desinit propterea libidinosus esse. Et absolute hi affectus non tam ipsos actus convivandi, potandi, etc., respiciunt, quam ipsum Appetitum et Amorem. Nihil igitur his affectibus opponi potest, præter Generositatem et Animositatem, de quibus in seqq.\n\nDefinitiones Zelotypiæ et reliquarum animi fluctuationum silentio prætermitto, tam quia ex compositione affectuum, quos jam definivimus oriuntur, quam quia pleræque nomina non habent ; quod ostendit ad usum vitæ sufficere, easdem in genere tantummodo noscere. Cæterum ex Definitionibus affectuum, quos explicuimus, liquet eos omnes a Cupiditate, Lætitia, vel Tristitia oriri, seu potius nihil præter hos tres esse, quorum unusquisque variis nominibus appellari solet propter varias eorum relationes et denominationes extrinsecas. Si jam ad hos primitivos, et ad ea, quæ de natura Mentis supra diximus, attendere velimus, affectus, quatenus ad solam Mentem referuntur, sic definere poterimus.\n\nAFFECTUUM GENERALIS DEFINITIO\n\nAffectus, qui animi Pathema dicitur, est confusa idea qua Mens majorem vel minorem sui Corporis, vel alicujus ejus partis, existendi vim, quam antea, affirmat, et qua data ipsa Mens ad hoc potius quam ad illud cogitandum determinatur.\n\nEXPLICATIO\n\nDico primo. Affectum seu passionem animi esse confusam ideam. Nam Mentem eatenus tantum pati ostendimus (vide Prop. 3 hujus), quatenus ideas inadæquatas sive confusas habet. Dico deinde, qua Mens majorem vel minorem sui Corporis, vel alicujus ejus partis, existendi vim, quam antea, affirmat. Omnes enim corporum ideæ, quas habemus, magis nostri Corporis actualem constitutionem (per Coroll. 2 Prop. 16 p. II), quam corporis externi naturam indicant ; at hæc, quæ affectus formam constituit, Corporis, vel alicujus ejus partis, constitutionem indicare vel exprimere debet, quam ipsum Corpus vel aliqua ejus pars habet, ex eo, quod ipsius agendi potentia sive existendi vis augetur vel minuitur, juvatur vel coercetur. Sed notandum, cum dico, majorem vel minorem existendi vim, quam antea, me non intelligere, quod Mens præsentem, Corporis constitutionem cum præterita comparat, sed quod idea, quæ affectus formam constituit, aliquid de Corpore affirmat, quod plus minusve realitatis revera involvit, quam antea. Et quia essentia Mentis in hoc consistit (per Prop. 11 et 13 p. II), quod sui Corporis actualem existentiam affirmat, et nos per perfectionem ipsam rei essentiam intelligimus ; sequitur ergo, quod Mens ad majorem minoremve perfectionem transit, quando ei aliquid de suo Corpore, vel aliqua ejus parte, affirmare contingit, quod plus minusve realitatis involvit, quam antea. Cum igitur supra dixerim, Mentis cogitandi potentiam augeri vel minui, nihil aliud intelligere volui, quam quod Mens ideam sui Corporis, vel alicujus ejus partis, formaverit, quæ plus minusve realitatis exprimit, quam de suo Corpore affirmaverat. Nam idearum præstantia, et actualis cogitandi potentia, ex objecti præstantia, æstimatur. Addidi denique, et qua data idea Mens ad hoc potius quam ad aliud cogitandum determinatur, ut præter Laetitiæ et Tristitiæ naturam, quam prima definitionis pars explicat, Cupiditatis etiam naturam exprimerem.\n\nFinis Tertiæ Partis","paragraphs":[{"id":"RAW_QGOH_PT_5:P0001","text":"In Scholio Propositionis 18 Partis II ostendimus, quænam sit causa cur Mens ex contemplatione unius rei statim in alterius rei cogitationem incidat, videlicet quia earum rerum imagines invicem concatenatæ et ita ordinatæ sunt, ut alia aliam sequatur ; quod quidem concipi nequit quando rei imago nova est, sed Mens in ejusdem rei contemplatione detinebitur, donec ab aliis causis ad alia cogitandum determinetur. Rei itaque novæ imaginatio in se considerata ejusdem naturæ est ac reliquæ ; et hac de causa ego Admirationem inter affectus non numero, nec causam video, cur id facerem, quandoquidem hæc Mentis distractio ex nulla causa positiva, quæ Mentem ab aliis distrahat, oritur, sed tantum ex eo, quod causa, cur Mens ex unius rei contemplatione ad alia cogitandum determinatur, deficit. Tres igitur (ut in Schol. Prop.","kind":"passage"},{"id":"RAW_QGOH_PT_5:P0002","text":"11 hujus monui) tantum affectus primitivos seu primarios agnosco, nempe Lætitiæ, Tristitiæ, et Cupiditatis ; nec alia de causa verba de Admiratione feci, quam quia usu factum est, ut quidam affectus, qui ex tribus primitivis derivantur, aliis nominibus indicari soleant, quando ad objecta, quæ admiramur, referuntur ; quæ quidem ratio me ex æquo movet, ut etiam Contemptus definitionem his adjungam.","kind":"passage"},{"id":"RAW_QGOH_PT_5:P0003","text":"V\n\nContemptus est rei alicujus imaginatio, quæ Mentem adeo parum tangit, ut ipsa Mens ex rei præsentia magis moveatur ad ea imaginandum, quæ in ipsa re non sunt, quam quæ in ipsa sunt (vide Schol. Prop. 52 hujus).","kind":"passage"},{"id":"RAW_QGOH_PT_5:P0004","text":"Definitiones Venerationis et Dedignationis missas hic facio, quia nulli, quod sciam, affectus ex his nomen trahunt.","kind":"passage"},{"id":"RAW_QGOH_PT_5:P0005","text":"VI","kind":"passage"},{"id":"RAW_QGOH_PT_5:P0006","text":"Amor est Lætitia concomitante idea causæ externæ.","kind":"passage"},{"id":"RAW_QGOH_PT_5:P0007","text":"EXPLICATIO","kind":"passage"},{"id":"RAW_QGOH_PT_5:P0008","text":"Hæc Definitio satis clare Amoris essentiam explicat ; illa vero Auctorum, qui definiunt Amorem esse voluntatem amantis se jungendi rei amatæ, non Amoris essentiam, sed ejus proprietatem exprimit, et, quia Amoris essentia non satis ab Auctoribus perspecta fuit, ideo neque ejus proprietatis ullum clarum conceptum habere potuerunt ; et hinc factum, ut eorum definitionem admodum obscuram esse omnes judicaverint.","kind":"passage"},{"id":"RAW_QGOH_PT_5:P0009","text":"Verum notandum, cum dico, proprietatem esse in amante, se voluntate jungere rei amatæ, me per voluntatem non intelligere consensum, vel animi deliberationem seu liberum decretum (nam hoc fictitium esse demonstraviinus Propositione 48 partis II), nec etiam Cupiditatem sese jungendi rei amatæ, quando abest, vel perseverandi in ipsius præsentia, quando adest ; potest namque amor absque hac aut illa Cupiditate concipi ; sed per voluntatem me Acquiescentiam intelligere, quæ est in Amante ob rei amatæ præsentiam, a qua Lætitia amantis corroboratur, aut saltem fovetur.","kind":"passage"},{"id":"RAW_QGOH_PT_5:P0010","text":"VII","kind":"passage"},{"id":"RAW_QGOH_PT_5:P0011","text":"Odium est Tristitia concomitante idea causæ externæ.","kind":"passage"},{"id":"RAW_QGOH_PT_5:P0012","text":"EXPLICATIO","kind":"passage"},{"id":"RAW_QGOH_PT_5:P0013","text":"Quæ hic notanda sunt, ex dictis in præcedentis Definitionis Explicatione facile percipiuntur (vide præterea Schol. Prop. 13 hujus).","kind":"passage"},{"id":"RAW_QGOH_PT_5:P0014","text":"VIII","kind":"passage"},{"id":"RAW_QGOH_PT_5:P0015","text":"Propensio est Lætitia concomitante idea alicujus rei, quæ per accidens causa est Lætitiæ.","kind":"passage"},{"id":"RAW_QGOH_PT_5:P0016","text":"IX","kind":"passage"},{"id":"RAW_QGOH_PT_5:P0017","text":"Aversio est Tristitia concomitante idea alicujus rei, quæ per accidens causa est Tristitiæ (de his vide Prop. 15 Schol. hujus).","kind":"passage"},{"id":"RAW_QGOH_PT_5:P0018","text":"X\n\nDevotio est Amor erga eum, quem admiramur.","kind":"passage"},{"id":"RAW_QGOH_PT_5:P0019","text":"EXPLICATIO","kind":"passage"},{"id":"RAW_QGOH_PT_5:P0020","text":"Admirationem oriri ex rei novitate, ostendimus Propositione 52 hujus. Si igitur contingat, ut id, quod admiramur, sæpe imaginemur, idem admirari desinemus ; atque adeo videmus, Devotionis affectum facile in simplicem Amorem degenerare.","kind":"passage"},{"id":"RAW_QGOH_PT_5:P0021","text":"XI","kind":"passage"},{"id":"RAW_QGOH_PT_5:P0022","text":"Irrisio est Lætitia orta ex eo, quod aliquid, quod contemnimus, in re quam odimus, inessse imaginamur.","kind":"passage"},{"id":"RAW_QGOH_PT_5:P0023","text":"EXPLICATIO","kind":"passage"},{"id":"RAW_QGOH_PT_5:P0024","text":"Quatenus rem, quam odimus, contemnimus, eatenus de eadem existentiam negamus (vide Schol. Prop. 52 hujus), et eatenus (per Prop. 20 hujus) lætamur. Sed quoniam supponimus, hominem id, quod irridet, odio tamen habere, sequitur, hanc Lætitiam solidam non esse (vid. Schol. Prop. 47 hujus).","kind":"passage"},{"id":"RAW_QGOH_PT_5:P0025","text":"XII","kind":"passage"},{"id":"RAW_QGOH_PT_5:P0026","text":"Spes est inconstans Lætitia, orta ex idea rei futuræ vel præteritæ, de cujus eventu aliquatenus dubitamus.","kind":"passage"},{"id":"RAW_QGOH_PT_5:P0027","text":"XIII","kind":"passage"},{"id":"RAW_QGOH_PT_5:P0028","text":"Metus est inconstans Tristitia, orta ex irea rei futuræ vel præteritæ, de cujus eventu aliquatenus dubitamus (vide de his Schol. 2 Prop. 18 hujus).","kind":"passage"},{"id":"RAW_QGOH_PT_5:P0029","text":"EXPLICATIO","kind":"passage"},{"id":"RAW_QGOH_PT_5:P0030","text":"Ex his definitionibus sequitur, non dari Spem sine Metu, neque Metum sine Spe. Qui enim Spe pendet et de rei eventu dubitat, is aliquid imaginari supponitur, quod rei futuræ existentiam secludit; atque adeo eatenus contristari (per Prop. 19 hujus), et consequenter, dum Spe pendet, metuere, ut res eveniat. Qui autem contra in Metu est, hoc est, de rei, quam odit, eventu dubitat, aliquid etiam imaginatur, quod ejusdem rei existentiam secludit ; atque adeo (per Prop. 20 hujus) lætatur, et consequenter eatenus Spem habet, ne eveniat.","kind":"passage"},{"id":"RAW_QGOH_PT_5:P0031","text":"XIV","kind":"passage"},{"id":"RAW_QGOH_PT_5:P0032","text":"Securitas est Lætitia orta ex idea rei futuræ vel præteritæ, de qua dubitandi causa sublata est.","kind":"passage"},{"id":"RAW_QGOH_PT_5:P0033","text":"XV","kind":"passage"},{"id":"RAW_QGOH_PT_5:P0034","text":"Desperatio est Tristitia orta ex idea rei futuræ vel præteritæ de qua dubitandi causa sublata est.","kind":"passage"},{"id":"RAW_QGOH_PT_5:P0035","text":"EXPLICATIO","kind":"passage"},{"id":"RAW_QGOH_PT_5:P0036","text":"Oritur itaque ex Spe Securitas, et ex Metu Desperatio, quando de rei eventu dubitandi causa tollitur ; quod fit, quia homo rem præteritam vel futuram adesse imaginatur et ut præsentem contemplatur, vel quia alia imaginatur, quæ existentiam earum rerum secludunt, quæ ipsi dubium injiciebant. Nam tametsi de rerum singularium eventu (per Coroll. Prop. 31 p. II) nunquam possumus esse certi, fieri tamen potest, ut de earum eventu non dubitemus. Aliud enim esse ostendimus (vide Schol. Prop. 49 p. II) de re non dubitare, aliud rei certitudinem habere ; atque adeo fieri potest, ut ex imagine rei præteritæ aut futuræ eodem Lætitiæ vel Tristitiæ affectu afficiamur, ac ex rei præsentis imagine, ut in Propositione 18 hujus demonstravimus, quam cum ejusdem Scholio vide.","kind":"passage"},{"id":"RAW_QGOH_PT_5:P0037","text":"XVI","kind":"passage"},{"id":"RAW_QGOH_PT_5:P0038","text":"Gaudium est Lætitia concomitante idea rei præteritæ, quæ præter Spem evenit.","kind":"passage"},{"id":"RAW_QGOH_PT_5:P0039","text":"XVII","kind":"passage"},{"id":"RAW_QGOH_PT_5:P0040","text":"Conscientiæ morsus est Tristitia concomitante idea rei præteritæ, quæ præter Spem evenit.","kind":"passage"},{"id":"RAW_QGOH_PT_5:P0041","text":"XVIII","kind":"passage"},{"id":"RAW_QGOH_PT_5:P0042","text":"Commiseratio est Tristitia concomitante idea mali, quod alteri, quem nobis similem esse imaginamur, evenit (vid. Schol. Prop. 22 et Schol. 27 hujus).","kind":"passage"},{"id":"RAW_QGOH_PT_5:P0043","text":"EXPLICATIO","kind":"passage"},{"id":"RAW_QGOH_PT_5:P0044","text":"Inter Commiserationem et Misericordiam nulla videtur esse differentia, nisi forte, quod Commiseratio singularem affectum respiciat, Misericordia autem ejus habitum.","kind":"passage"},{"id":"RAW_QGOH_PT_5:P0045","text":"XIX","kind":"passage"},{"id":"RAW_QGOH_PT_5:P0046","text":"Favor est Amor erga aliquem, qui alteri benefecit.","kind":"passage"},{"id":"RAW_QGOH_PT_5:P0047","text":"XX","kind":"passage"},{"id":"RAW_QGOH_PT_5:P0048","text":"Indignatio est Odium erga aliquem, qui alteri malefecit.","kind":"passage"},{"id":"RAW_QGOH_PT_5:P0049","text":"EXPLICATIO","kind":"passage"},{"id":"RAW_QGOH_PT_5:P0050","text":"Hæc nomina ex communi usu aliud significare scio. Sed meum institutum non est, verborum significationem, sed rerum naturam explicare, easque iis vocabulis indicare, quorum significatio, quam ex usu habent, a significatione, qua eadem usurpare volo, non omnino abhorret, quod semel monuisse sufficiat. Caeterum horum affectuum causam vide in Corollario 1 Propositionis 27 et Scholio Propositionis 22 hujus Partis.","kind":"passage"},{"id":"RAW_QGOH_PT_5:P0051","text":"XXI","kind":"passage"},{"id":"RAW_QGOH_PT_5:P0052","text":"Existimatio est de aliquo præ Amore plus justo sentire.","kind":"passage"},{"id":"RAW_QGOH_PT_5:P0053","text":"XXII","kind":"passage"},{"id":"RAW_QGOH_PT_5:P0054","text":"Despectus est de aliquo præ Odio minus justo sentire.","kind":"passage"},{"id":"RAW_QGOH_PT_5:P0055","text":"EXPLICATIO","kind":"passage"},{"id":"RAW_QGOH_PT_5:P0056","text":"Est itaque Existimatio Amoris, et Despectus Odii effectus sive proprietas ; atque adeo potest Existimatio etiam definiri, quod sit Amor, quatenus hominem ita afficit, ut de re amata plus justo sentiat, et contra Despectus, quod sit. Odium, quatenus hominem ita afficit, ut de eo, quem odio habet, minus justo sentiat (vide de his Schol. Prop. 26 hujus).","kind":"passage"},{"id":"RAW_QGOH_PT_5:P0057","text":"XXIII","kind":"passage"},{"id":"RAW_QGOH_PT_5:P0058","text":"Invidia est Odium, quatenus hominem ita afficit, ut ex alterius felicitate contristetur, et contra, ut ex alterius malo gaudeat.","kind":"passage"},{"id":"RAW_QGOH_PT_5:P0059","text":"EXPLICATIO","kind":"passage"},{"id":"RAW_QGOH_PT_5:P0060","text":"Invidiæ opponitur communiter Misericordia, quæ proinde, invita vocabuli significatione, sic definiri potest :","kind":"passage"},{"id":"RAW_QGOH_PT_5:P0061","text":"XXIV","kind":"passage"},{"id":"RAW_QGOH_PT_5:P0062","text":"Misericordia est Amor, quatenus hominem ita afficit, ut ex bono alterius gaudeat, et contra ut ex alterius malo contristetur.","kind":"passage"},{"id":"RAW_QGOH_PT_5:P0063","text":"EXPLICATIO","kind":"passage"},{"id":"RAW_QGOH_PT_5:P0064","text":"Caeterum de Invidia vide Schol. Prop. 24 et Schol. Prop. 32 hujus. Atque hi affectus Lætitiæ et Tristitiæ sunt, quos idea rei externæ comitatur tanquam causa per se vel per accidens. Hinc ad alios transeo, quos idea rei internæ comitatur tanquam causa.","kind":"passage"},{"id":"RAW_QGOH_PT_5:P0065","text":"XXV","kind":"passage"},{"id":"RAW_QGOH_PT_5:P0066","text":"Acquiescentia in se ipso est Lætitia orta ex eo, quod homo se ipsum suamque agendi potentiam contemplatur.","kind":"passage"},{"id":"RAW_QGOH_PT_5:P0067","text":"XXVI","kind":"passage"},{"id":"RAW_QGOH_PT_5:P0068","text":"Humilitas est Tristitia orta ex eo, quod homo suam impotentiam sive imbecillitatem contemplatur.","kind":"passage"},{"id":"RAW_QGOH_PT_5:P0069","text":"EXPLICATIO","kind":"passage"},{"id":"RAW_QGOH_PT_5:P0070","text":"Acquiescentia in se ipso Humilitati opponitur, quatenus per eandem intelligimus Lætitiam, quæ ex eo oritur, quod nostram agendi potentiam contemplamur ; sed quatenus per ipsam etiam intelligimus Lætitiam concomitante idea alicujus facti, quod nos ex Mentis libero decreto fecisse credimus, tum Pœnitentiae opponitur, quæ a nobis sic definitur :","kind":"passage"},{"id":"RAW_QGOH_PT_5:P0071","text":"XXVII","kind":"passage"},{"id":"RAW_QGOH_PT_5:P0072","text":"Pœnitentia est Tristitia concomitante idea alicujus facti, quod nos ex libero Mentis decreto fecisse credimus.","kind":"passage"},{"id":"RAW_QGOH_PT_5:P0073","text":"EXPLICATIO","kind":"passage"},{"id":"RAW_QGOH_PT_5:P0074","text":"Horum affectuum causas ostendimus in Schol. Prop. 51 hujus, et Prop. 53, 54 et 55 hujus, ejusque Schol. De libero autem Mentis decreto vide Schol. Prop. 35 p. II. Sed hic præterea notandum venit, mirum non esse, quod omnes omnino actus, qui ex consuetudine pravi vocantur, sequatur Tristitia, et illos, qui recti dicuntur, Lætitia. Nam hoc ab educatione potissimum pendere, facile ex supra dictis intelligimus. Parentes nimirum, illos exprobrando, liberosque propter eosdem sæpe objurgando, hos contra suadendo et laudando, effecerunt, ut Tristitiæ commotiones illis, Lætitiæ vero his jungerentur. Quod ipsa etiam experientia comprobatur. Nam consuetudo et Religio non est omnibus eadem, sed contra, quæ apud alios sacra, apud alios profana, et quæ apud alios honesta, apud alios turpia sunt. Prout igitur unusquisque educatus est, ita facti alicujus pœnitet, vel eodem gloriatur.","kind":"passage"},{"id":"RAW_QGOH_PT_5:P0075","text":"XXVIII","kind":"passage"},{"id":"RAW_QGOH_PT_5:P0076","text":"Superbia est de se præ Amore sui plus justo sentire.","kind":"passage"},{"id":"RAW_QGOH_PT_5:P0077","text":"EXPLICATIO","kind":"passage"},{"id":"RAW_QGOH_PT_5:P0078","text":"Differt igitur Superbia ab Existimatione, quod hæc ad objectum externum, Superbia autem ad ipsum hominem, de se plus justo sentientem, referatur. Cæterum, ut Existimatio Amoris, sic Superbia Philautiæ effectus vel proprietas est, quæ propterea etiam definiri potest, quod sit Amor sui, sive Acquiescentia in se ipso, quatenus hominem ita afficit, ut de se plus justo sentiat (vid. Schol. Prop. 26 hujus). Huic affectui non datur contrarius. Nam nemo de se, præ Odio sui, minus justo sentit ; imo nemo de se minus justo sentit, quatenus imaginatur, se hoc vel illud non posse. Nam quicquid homo imaginatur se non posse, id necessario imaginatur, et hac imaginatione ita disponitur, ut id agere revera non possit, quod se non posse imaginatur. Quamdiu enim imaginatur se hoc vel illud non posse, tamdiu ad agendum non est determinatus, et consequenter tamdiu impossibile ei est, ut id agat.","kind":"passage"},{"id":"RAW_QGOH_PT_5:P0079","text":"Verumenimvero si ad illa attendamus, quæ a sola opinione pendent, concipere poterimus fieri posse, ut homo de se minus justo sentiat ; fieri enim potest, ut aliquis, dum tristis imbecillitatem contemplatur suam, imaginetur, se ab omnibus contemni, idque dum reliqui nihil minus cogitant, quam ipsum contemnere. Potest præterea homo de se minus justo sentire, si aliquid de se in præsenti neget cum relatione ad futurum tempus, cujus est incertus ; ut quod neget, se nihil certi posse concipere, nihilque nisi prava vel turpia posse cupere vel agere, etc. Possumus deinde dicere, aliquem de se minus justo sentire, cum videmus, ipsum ex nimio pudoris metu ea non audere, quæ alii ipsi æquales audent. Hunc igitur affectum possumus Superbiæ opponere, quem Abjectionem vocabo ; nam ut ex Acquiescentia in se ipso Superbia, sic ex Humilitate Abjectio oritur, quæ proinde a nobis sic definitur.","kind":"passage"},{"id":"RAW_QGOH_PT_5:P0080","text":"XXIX","kind":"passage"},{"id":"RAW_QGOH_PT_5:P0081","text":"Abjectio est de se præ Tristitia minus justo sentire.","kind":"passage"},{"id":"RAW_QGOH_PT_5:P0082","text":"EXPLICATIO","kind":"passage"},{"id":"RAW_QGOH_PT_5:P0083","text":"Solemus tamen sæpe Superbiæ Humililatem opponere, sed tum magis ad utriusque effectus quam naturam attendimus. Solemus namque illum superbum vocare, qui nimis gloriatur (vid. Schol. Prop. 30 hujus), qui non nisi virtutes suas, et aliorum non nisi vitia narrat, qui omnibus præferri vult, et qui denique ea gravitate et ornatu incedit, quo solent alii, qui longe supra ipsum sunt positi. Contra illum humilem vocamus, qui sæpius erubescit, qui sua vitia fatetur, et aliorum virtutes narrat, qui omnibus cedit, et qui denique submisso capite ambulat, et se ornare negligit. Cæterum hi affectus, nempe Humilitas et Abjectio, rarissimi sunt. Nam natura humana, in se considerata, contra eosdem quantum potest, nititur (vide Prop. 13 et 54 hujus) ; et ideo, qui maxime creduntur abjecti et humiles esse, maxime plerumque ambitiosi et invidi sunt.","kind":"passage"},{"id":"RAW_QGOH_PT_5:P0084","text":"XXX","kind":"passage"},{"id":"RAW_QGOH_PT_5:P0085","text":"Gloria est Lætitia concomitante idea alicujus nostræ actionis, quam alios laudare imaginamur.","kind":"passage"},{"id":"RAW_QGOH_PT_5:P0086","text":"XXXI","kind":"passage"},{"id":"RAW_QGOH_PT_5:P0087","text":"Pudor est Tristitia concomitante idea alicujus actionis, quam alios vituperare imaginamur.","kind":"passage"},{"id":"RAW_QGOH_PT_5:P0088","text":"EXPLICATIO","kind":"passage"},{"id":"RAW_QGOH_PT_5:P0089","text":"De his vide Scholium Propositionis 30 hujus Partis. Sed hic notanda est differentia, quæ est inter Pudorem et Verecundiam. Est enim Pudor Tristitia, quæ sequitur factum, cujus pudet. Verecundia autem est Metus seu Timor Pudoris, quo homo continetur, ne aliquid turpe committat. Verecundiæ opponi solet Impudentia, quæ revera affectus non est, ut suo loco ostendam ; sed affectuum nomina (ut jam monui) magis eorum usum quam naturam respiciunt. Atque his Lætitiæ et Tristitiæ affectus, quos explicare proposueram, absolvi. Pergo itaque ad illos, quos ad Cupiditatem refero.","kind":"passage"},{"id":"RAW_QGOH_PT_5:P0090","text":"XXXII","kind":"passage"},{"id":"RAW_QGOH_PT_5:P0091","text":"Desiderium est Cupiditas sive Appetitus re aliqua potiundi, quæ ejusdem rei memoria fovetur, et simul aliarum rerum memoria, quæ ejusdem rei appetendæ existentiam secludunt, coercetur.","kind":"passage"},{"id":"RAW_QGOH_PT_5:P0092","text":"EXPLICATIO","kind":"passage"},{"id":"RAW_QGOH_PT_5:P0093","text":"Cum alicujus rei recordamur, ut jam sæpe diximus, eo ipso disponimur ad eandem eodem affectu contemplandum, ac si res præsens adesset ; sed hæc dispositio, seu conatus, dum vigilamus, plerumque cohibetur ab imaginibus rerum, quæ existentiam ejus, cujus recordamur, secludunt. Quando itaque rei meminimus, quæ nos aliquo Lætitiæ genere afficit, eo ipso conamur eandem, cum eodem Lætitiæ affectu ut præsentem contemplari, qui quidem conatus statim cohibetur memoria rerum, quæ illius existentiam secludunt. Quare desiderium revera Tristitia est, quæ Lætitiæ opponitur illi, quæ ex absentia rei, quam odimus, oritur, de qua vide Scholium Propositionis 47 hujus partis. Sed quia nomen Desiderium Cupiditatem respicere videtur, ideo hunc affectum ad Cupiditatis affectus refero.","kind":"passage"},{"id":"RAW_QGOH_PT_5:P0094","text":"XXXIII","kind":"passage"},{"id":"RAW_QGOH_PT_5:P0095","text":"Æmulatio est alicujus rei Cupiditas, quæ nobis ingeneratur ex eo, quod alios eandem Cupiditatem habere imaginamur.","kind":"passage"},{"id":"RAW_QGOH_PT_5:P0096","text":"EXPLICATIO","kind":"passage"},{"id":"RAW_QGOH_PT_5:P0097","text":"Qui fugit, quia alios fugere, vel qui timet, quia alios timere videt ; vel etiam ille, qui ex eo, quod aliquem manum suam combussisse videt, manum ad se contrahit, corpusque movet, quasi ipsius manus combureretur ; eum imitari quidem alterius affectum, sed non eundem æmulari dicemus ; non quia aliam æmulationis, aliam imitationis novimus causam, sed quia usu factum est, ut illum tantum vocemus æmulum, qui id, quod honestum utile, vel jucundum esse judicamus, imitatur. Cæterum de Æmulalionis causa vide Propositionem 27 hujus Partis cum ejus Scholio. Cur autem huic affectui plerumque juncta sit Invidia, de eovide Propositionem 32 hujus cum ejusdem Scholio.","kind":"passage"},{"id":"RAW_QGOH_PT_5:P0098","text":"XXXIV","kind":"passage"},{"id":"RAW_QGOH_PT_5:P0099","text":"Gratia seu Gratitudo est Cupiditas, seu Amoris studium, quo ei benefacere conamur, qui in nos pari amoris affectu beneficium contulit. Vide Prop. 39 cum Schol. Prop. 41 hujus.","kind":"passage"},{"id":"RAW_QGOH_PT_5:P0100","text":"XXXV","kind":"passage"},{"id":"RAW_QGOH_PT_5:P0101","text":"Benevolentia est Cupiditas benefaciendi ei, cujus nos miseret. Vide Schol. Prop. 27 hujus.","kind":"passage"},{"id":"RAW_QGOH_PT_5:P0102","text":"XXXVI","kind":"passage"},{"id":"RAW_QGOH_PT_5:P0103","text":"Ira est Cupiditas, qua ex Odio incitamur ad illi, quem odimus, malum inferendum. Vide Prop. 39 hujus.","kind":"passage"},{"id":"RAW_QGOH_PT_5:P0104","text":"XXXVII","kind":"passage"},{"id":"RAW_QGOH_PT_5:P0105","text":"Vindicta est Cupiditas, qua ex reciproco Odio concitamur ad malum inferendum ei, qui nobis pari affectu damnum intulit Vide 2 Coroll. Prop. 40 hujus cum ejusdem Schol.","kind":"passage"},{"id":"RAW_QGOH_PT_5:P0106","text":"XXXVIII","kind":"passage"},{"id":"RAW_QGOH_PT_5:P0107","text":"Crudelitas seu Sævitia est Cupiditas, qua aliquis concitatur ad malum inferendum ei, quem amamus, vel cujus non miseret.","kind":"passage"},{"id":"RAW_QGOH_PT_5:P0108","text":"EXPLICATIO","kind":"passage"},{"id":"RAW_QGOH_PT_5:P0109","text":"Crudelitati opponitur Clementia, quæ passio non est, sed animi potentia, quo homo iram et vindictam moderatur.","kind":"passage"},{"id":"RAW_QGOH_PT_5:P0110","text":"XXXIX","kind":"passage"},{"id":"RAW_QGOH_PT_5:P0111","text":"Timor est Cupiditas majus, quod metuimus, malum minore vitandi. Vide Schol. Prop. 39 hujus.","kind":"passage"},{"id":"RAW_QGOH_PT_5:P0112","text":"XL","kind":"passage"},{"id":"RAW_QGOH_PT_5:P0113","text":"Audacia est Cupiditas, qua aliquis incitatur ad aliquid agendum cum periculo, quod ejus æquales subire metuunt.","kind":"passage"},{"id":"RAW_QGOH_PT_5:P0114","text":"XLI","kind":"passage"},{"id":"RAW_QGOH_PT_5:P0115","text":"Pusillanimitas dicitur de eo, cujus Cupiditas coercetur timore periculi, quod ejus æquales subire audent.","kind":"passage"},{"id":"RAW_QGOH_PT_5:P0116","text":"EXPLICATIO","kind":"passage"},{"id":"RAW_QGOH_PT_5:P0117","text":"Est igitur Pusillanimitas nihil aliud, quam Metus alicujus mali, quod plerique non solent metuere ; quare ipsam ad Cupiditatis affectus non refero. Eandem tamen hic explicare volui, quia, quatenus ad Cupiditatem attendimus, affectui Audaciæ revera opponitur.","kind":"passage"},{"id":"RAW_QGOH_PT_5:P0118","text":"XLII","kind":"passage"},{"id":"RAW_QGOH_PT_5:P0119","text":"Consternatio dicitur de eo, cujus Cupiditas malum vitandi coercetur admiratione mali, quod timet.","kind":"passage"},{"id":"RAW_QGOH_PT_5:P0120","text":"EXPLICATIO","kind":"passage"},{"id":"RAW_QGOH_PT_5:P0121","text":"Est itaque Consternatio Pusillanimitatis species. Sed quia Consternatio ex duplici Timore oritur, ideo commodius definiri potest, quod sit Metus, qui hominem stupefactum aut fluctuantem ita continet, ut is malum amovere non possit. Dico stupefactum, quatenus ejus Cupiditatem malum amovendi admiratione coerceri intelligimus. Fluctuantem autem dico, quatenus concipimus eandem Cupiditatem coerceri Timore alterius mali, quod ipsum æque cruciat : unde fit, ut, quodnam ex duobus avertat, nesciat. De his vide Schol. Prop. 39 et Schol. Prop. 52 hujus. Cæterum de Pusillanimitate et Audacia vide Schol. Prop. 51 hujus.","kind":"passage"},{"id":"RAW_QGOH_PT_5:P0122","text":"XLIII","kind":"passage"},{"id":"RAW_QGOH_PT_5:P0123","text":"Humanitas seu Modestia est Cupiditas ea faciendi, quæ hominibus placent, et omittendi, quæ displicent.","kind":"passage"},{"id":"RAW_QGOH_PT_5:P0124","text":"XLIV","kind":"passage"},{"id":"RAW_QGOH_PT_5:P0125","text":"Ambitio est immodica gloriæ Cupiditas.","kind":"passage"},{"id":"RAW_QGOH_PT_5:P0126","text":"EXPLICATIO","kind":"passage"},{"id":"RAW_QGOH_PT_5:P0127","text":"Ambitio est Cupiditas, qua omnes affectus (per Prop. 27 et 31 hujus) foventur et corroborantur ; et ideo hic affectus vix superari potest. Nam quamdiu homo aliqua Cupiditate tenetur, hac simul necessario tenetur. Optimus quisque, inquit Cicero, maxime gloria ducitur. Philosophi etiam libris, quos de contemnenda gloria scribunt, nomen suum inscribunt, etc.","kind":"passage"},{"id":"RAW_QGOH_PT_5:P0128","text":"XLV","kind":"passage"},{"id":"RAW_QGOH_PT_5:P0129","text":"Luxuria est immoderata convivandi Cupiditas, vel etiam Amor.","kind":"passage"},{"id":"RAW_QGOH_PT_5:P0130","text":"XLVI","kind":"passage"},{"id":"RAW_QGOH_PT_5:P0131","text":"Ebrietas est immoderata potandi Cupiditas et Amor.","kind":"passage"},{"id":"RAW_QGOH_PT_5:P0132","text":"XLVII","kind":"passage"},{"id":"RAW_QGOH_PT_5:P0133","text":"Avaritia est immoderata divitiarum Cupiditas et Armor.","kind":"passage"},{"id":"RAW_QGOH_PT_5:P0134","text":"XLVIII","kind":"passage"},{"id":"RAW_QGOH_PT_5:P0135","text":"Libido est etiam Cupiditas et Amor in commiscendis corporibus.","kind":"passage"},{"id":"RAW_QGOH_PT_5:P0136","text":"EXPLICATIO","kind":"passage"},{"id":"RAW_QGOH_PT_5:P0137","text":"Sive hæc coeundi Cupiditas moderata sit, sive non sit, Libido appellari solet. Porro hi quinque affectus (ut in Schol. Prop. 56 hujus monui) contrarios non habent. Nam Modestia species est Ambitionis, de qua vide Schol. Prop. 29 hujus. Temperantiam deinde, Sobrietatem, et Castitatem Mentis potentiam, non autem passionem indicare, jam etiam monui. Et tametsi fieri potest, ut homo avarus, ambitiosus, vel timidus a nimio cibo, potu, et coitu, abstineat ; Avaritia tamen, Ambitio et Timor luxuriæ, ebrietati, vel libidini non sunt contrarii. Nam avarus in cibum et potum alienum se ingurgitare plerumque desiderat. Ambitiosus autem, modo speret fore clam, in nulla re sibi temperabit, et si inter ebrios vivat et libidinosos, ideo quia ambitiosus est, proclivior erit ad eadem vitia. Timidus denique id, quod non vult, facit.","kind":"passage"},{"id":"RAW_QGOH_PT_5:P0138","text":"Nam quamvis mortis vitandæ causa divitias in mare projiciat, manet tamen avarus et si libidinosus tristis est, quod sibi morem gerere nequeat, non desinit propterea libidinosus esse. Et absolute hi affectus non tam ipsos actus convivandi, potandi, etc., respiciunt, quam ipsum Appetitum et Amorem. Nihil igitur his affectibus opponi potest, præter Generositatem et Animositatem, de quibus in seqq.","kind":"passage"},{"id":"RAW_QGOH_PT_5:P0139","text":"Definitiones Zelotypiæ et reliquarum animi fluctuationum silentio prætermitto, tam quia ex compositione affectuum, quos jam definivimus oriuntur, quam quia pleræque nomina non habent ; quod ostendit ad usum vitæ sufficere, easdem in genere tantummodo noscere. Cæterum ex Definitionibus affectuum, quos explicuimus, liquet eos omnes a Cupiditate, Lætitia, vel Tristitia oriri, seu potius nihil præter hos tres esse, quorum unusquisque variis nominibus appellari solet propter varias eorum relationes et denominationes extrinsecas. Si jam ad hos primitivos, et ad ea, quæ de natura Mentis supra diximus, attendere velimus, affectus, quatenus ad solam Mentem referuntur, sic definere poterimus.","kind":"passage"},{"id":"RAW_QGOH_PT_5:P0140","text":"AFFECTUUM GENERALIS DEFINITIO","kind":"passage"},{"id":"RAW_QGOH_PT_5:P0141","text":"Affectus, qui animi Pathema dicitur, est confusa idea qua Mens majorem vel minorem sui Corporis, vel alicujus ejus partis, existendi vim, quam antea, affirmat, et qua data ipsa Mens ad hoc potius quam ad illud cogitandum determinatur.","kind":"passage"},{"id":"RAW_QGOH_PT_5:P0142","text":"EXPLICATIO","kind":"passage"},{"id":"RAW_QGOH_PT_5:P0143","text":"Dico primo. Affectum seu passionem animi esse confusam ideam. Nam Mentem eatenus tantum pati ostendimus (vide Prop. 3 hujus), quatenus ideas inadæquatas sive confusas habet. Dico deinde, qua Mens majorem vel minorem sui Corporis, vel alicujus ejus partis, existendi vim, quam antea, affirmat. Omnes enim corporum ideæ, quas habemus, magis nostri Corporis actualem constitutionem (per Coroll. 2 Prop. 16 p. II), quam corporis externi naturam indicant ; at hæc, quæ affectus formam constituit, Corporis, vel alicujus ejus partis, constitutionem indicare vel exprimere debet, quam ipsum Corpus vel aliqua ejus pars habet, ex eo, quod ipsius agendi potentia sive existendi vis augetur vel minuitur, juvatur vel coercetur.","kind":"passage"},{"id":"RAW_QGOH_PT_5:P0144","text":"Sed notandum, cum dico, majorem vel minorem existendi vim, quam antea, me non intelligere, quod Mens præsentem, Corporis constitutionem cum præterita comparat, sed quod idea, quæ affectus formam constituit, aliquid de Corpore affirmat, quod plus minusve realitatis revera involvit, quam antea. Et quia essentia Mentis in hoc consistit (per Prop. 11 et 13 p. II), quod sui Corporis actualem existentiam affirmat, et nos per perfectionem ipsam rei essentiam intelligimus ; sequitur ergo, quod Mens ad majorem minoremve perfectionem transit, quando ei aliquid de suo Corpore, vel aliqua ejus parte, affirmare contingit, quod plus minusve realitatis involvit, quam antea.","kind":"passage"},{"id":"RAW_QGOH_PT_5:P0145","text":"Cum igitur supra dixerim, Mentis cogitandi potentiam augeri vel minui, nihil aliud intelligere volui, quam quod Mens ideam sui Corporis, vel alicujus ejus partis, formaverit, quæ plus minusve realitatis exprimit, quam de suo Corpore affirmaverat. Nam idearum præstantia, et actualis cogitandi potentia, ex objecti præstantia, æstimatur. Addidi denique, et qua data idea Mens ad hoc potius quam ad aliud cogitandum determinatur, ut præter Laetitiæ et Tristitiæ naturam, quam prima definitionis pars explicat, Cupiditatis etiam naturam exprimerem.","kind":"passage"},{"id":"RAW_QGOH_PT_5:P0146","text":"Finis Tertiæ Partis","kind":"passage"}],"sha256":"fcc322fc4d7609d11c9550df7413ba346919829f45a5cddf49d6a64767d965d3","language_code":"la","language_name":"Latina","translations":[{"id":"SOURCE_TR_20261015T013636602751Z_052cdd7a","language_code":"en","language_name":"English","title":"Part Three - On the Origin and Nature of the Affects · passage 5","source_sha256":"fcc322fc4d7609d11c9550df7413ba346919829f45a5cddf49d6a64767d965d3","sha256":"c22e028eb81ad66dc2512cd255ff5fbb09fcd117974df57d04151b871d24f303","rendition_type":"machine","review_status":"machine","model":"gpt-5.6-terra","created":"2026-10-15T01:36:36.602930+00:00","paragraphs":[{"id":"RAW_QGOH_PT_5:P0001","text":"In the Scholium to Proposition 18 of Part II we showed what is the cause why the Mind, from the contemplation of one thing, immediately falls upon the thought of another thing: namely, because the images of those things are mutually concatenated and so ordered that one follows another; which indeed cannot be conceived when the image of a thing is new, but the Mind will be held in the contemplation of the same thing until it is determined by other causes to think of other things. Therefore, the imagination of a new thing, considered in itself, is of the same nature as the rest; and for this reason I do not number Admiration among the affects, nor do I see any reason why I should do so, since this distraction of the Mind arises from no positive cause that distracts the Mind from other things, but only from the fact that the cause is lacking why the Mind is determined, from the contemplation of one thing, to think of other things. Therefore I acknowledge only three (as I noted in the Schol. to Prop.","kind":"passage","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_QGOH_PT_5:P0001"]},{"id":"RAW_QGOH_PT_5:P0002","text":"11 of this Part) primitive or primary affects, namely Joy, Sadness, and Desire; nor did I speak of Admiration for any other reason than that it has become customary for certain affects that are derived from the three primitive ones to be designated by other names when they are referred to objects which we admire; and this same reason equally moves me to add here also the definition of Contempt.","kind":"passage","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_QGOH_PT_5:P0002"]},{"id":"RAW_QGOH_PT_5:P0003","text":"V\n\nContempt is the imagination of some thing which affects the Mind so little that the Mind itself, from the presence of the thing, is moved more to imagine those things which are not in the thing than those which are in it (see the Schol. to Prop. 52 of this Part).","kind":"passage","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_QGOH_PT_5:P0003"]},{"id":"RAW_QGOH_PT_5:P0004","text":"I pass over here the definitions of Veneration and Indignation, because, so far as I know, no affects take their name from these.","kind":"passage","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_QGOH_PT_5:P0004"]},{"id":"RAW_QGOH_PT_5:P0005","text":"VI","kind":"passage","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_QGOH_PT_5:P0005"]},{"id":"RAW_QGOH_PT_5:P0006","text":"Love is Joy accompanied by the idea of an external cause.","kind":"passage","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_QGOH_PT_5:P0006"]},{"id":"RAW_QGOH_PT_5:P0007","text":"EXPLANATION","kind":"passage","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_QGOH_PT_5:P0007"]},{"id":"RAW_QGOH_PT_5:P0008","text":"This Definition explains the essence of Love sufficiently clearly; but that of the Authors who define Love as the will of the lover to unite himself with the thing loved expresses not the essence of Love but a property of it; and because the essence of Love was not sufficiently perceived by the Authors, they could therefore have no clear conception of its property either; and hence it came about that everyone judged their definition to be exceedingly obscure.","kind":"passage","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_QGOH_PT_5:P0008"]},{"id":"RAW_QGOH_PT_5:P0009","text":"But it should be noted that, when I say that it is a property in the lover to unite himself by will with the thing loved, by will I do not understand consent, or deliberation of the mind, or a free decree (for I have demonstrated that this is fictitious in Proposition 48 of Part II), nor even the Desire to unite oneself with the thing loved when it is absent, or to persevere in its presence when it is present; for love can be conceived without this or that Desire; but by will I understand the Acquiescence that is in the Lover on account of the presence of the thing loved, by which the Joy of the lover is strengthened, or at least cherished.","kind":"passage","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_QGOH_PT_5:P0009"]},{"id":"RAW_QGOH_PT_5:P0010","text":"VII","kind":"passage","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_QGOH_PT_5:P0010"]},{"id":"RAW_QGOH_PT_5:P0011","text":"Hate is Sadness accompanied by the idea of an external cause.","kind":"passage","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_QGOH_PT_5:P0011"]},{"id":"RAW_QGOH_PT_5:P0012","text":"EXPLANATION","kind":"passage","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_QGOH_PT_5:P0012"]},{"id":"RAW_QGOH_PT_5:P0013","text":"What should here be noted is easily understood from what was said in the Explanation of the preceding Definition (see moreover the Schol. to Prop. 13 of this Part).","kind":"passage","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_QGOH_PT_5:P0013"]},{"id":"RAW_QGOH_PT_5:P0014","text":"VIII","kind":"passage","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_QGOH_PT_5:P0014"]},{"id":"RAW_QGOH_PT_5:P0015","text":"Inclination is Joy accompanied by the idea of some thing which is accidentally a cause of Joy.","kind":"passage","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_QGOH_PT_5:P0015"]},{"id":"RAW_QGOH_PT_5:P0016","text":"IX","kind":"passage","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_QGOH_PT_5:P0016"]},{"id":"RAW_QGOH_PT_5:P0017","text":"Aversion is Sadness accompanied by the idea of some thing which is accidentally a cause of Sadness (concerning these, see the Schol. to Prop. 15 of this Part).","kind":"passage","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_QGOH_PT_5:P0017"]},{"id":"RAW_QGOH_PT_5:P0018","text":"X\n\nDevotion is Love toward one whom we admire.","kind":"passage","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_QGOH_PT_5:P0018"]},{"id":"RAW_QGOH_PT_5:P0019","text":"EXPLANATION","kind":"passage","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_QGOH_PT_5:P0019"]},{"id":"RAW_QGOH_PT_5:P0020","text":"We showed in Proposition 52 of this Part that Admiration arises from the novelty of a thing. If therefore it happens that we frequently imagine what we admire, we shall cease to admire the same thing; and thus we see that the affect of Devotion easily degenerates into simple Love.","kind":"passage","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_QGOH_PT_5:P0020"]},{"id":"RAW_QGOH_PT_5:P0021","text":"XI","kind":"passage","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_QGOH_PT_5:P0021"]},{"id":"RAW_QGOH_PT_5:P0022","text":"Derision is Joy arising from the fact that we imagine that something which we contemn is present in a thing which we hate.","kind":"passage","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_QGOH_PT_5:P0022"]},{"id":"RAW_QGOH_PT_5:P0023","text":"EXPLANATION","kind":"passage","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_QGOH_PT_5:P0023"]},{"id":"RAW_QGOH_PT_5:P0024","text":"Insofar as we contemn a thing which we hate, we deny existence to it (see the Schol. to Prop. 52 of this Part), and insofar (by Prop. 20 of this Part) we rejoice. But since we suppose that a man nevertheless hates that which he derides, it follows that this Joy is not solid (see the Schol. to Prop. 47 of this Part).","kind":"passage","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_QGOH_PT_5:P0024"]},{"id":"RAW_QGOH_PT_5:P0025","text":"XII","kind":"passage","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_QGOH_PT_5:P0025"]},{"id":"RAW_QGOH_PT_5:P0026","text":"Hope is an inconstant Joy, arising from the idea of a future or past thing, about whose outcome we to some extent doubt.","kind":"passage","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_QGOH_PT_5:P0026"]},{"id":"RAW_QGOH_PT_5:P0027","text":"XIII","kind":"passage","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_QGOH_PT_5:P0027"]},{"id":"RAW_QGOH_PT_5:P0028","text":"Fear is an inconstant Sadness, arising from the idea of a future or past thing, about whose outcome we to some extent doubt (see Scholium 2 of Proposition 18 of this Part).","kind":"passage","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_QGOH_PT_5:P0028"]},{"id":"RAW_QGOH_PT_5:P0029","text":"EXPLANATION","kind":"passage","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_QGOH_PT_5:P0029"]},{"id":"RAW_QGOH_PT_5:P0030","text":"From these definitions it follows that there is no Hope without Fear, nor Fear without Hope. For one who depends on Hope and doubts the outcome of a thing is supposed to imagine something that excludes the existence of the future thing; and accordingly, to that extent, to be saddened (by Proposition 19 of this Part), and consequently, while he depends on Hope, to fear that the thing may come to pass. But one who, on the contrary, is in Fear—that is, who doubts the outcome of a thing which he hates—also imagines something that excludes the existence of that same thing; and accordingly (by Proposition 20 of this Part) he rejoices, and consequently to that extent has Hope that it may not come to pass.","kind":"passage","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_QGOH_PT_5:P0030"]},{"id":"RAW_QGOH_PT_5:P0031","text":"XIV","kind":"passage","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_QGOH_PT_5:P0031"]},{"id":"RAW_QGOH_PT_5:P0032","text":"Security is Joy arising from the idea of a future or past thing, concerning which the cause for doubting has been removed.","kind":"passage","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_QGOH_PT_5:P0032"]},{"id":"RAW_QGOH_PT_5:P0033","text":"XV","kind":"passage","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_QGOH_PT_5:P0033"]},{"id":"RAW_QGOH_PT_5:P0034","text":"Despair is Sadness arising from the idea of a future or past thing, concerning which the cause for doubting has been removed.","kind":"passage","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_QGOH_PT_5:P0034"]},{"id":"RAW_QGOH_PT_5:P0035","text":"EXPLANATION","kind":"passage","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_QGOH_PT_5:P0035"]},{"id":"RAW_QGOH_PT_5:P0036","text":"Thus Security arises from Hope, and Despair from Fear, when the cause for doubting about the outcome of a thing is removed; which happens because a man imagines the past or future thing to be at hand and contemplates it as present, or because he imagines other things that exclude the existence of those things which cast doubt upon him. For although we can never be certain of the outcome of singular things (by the Corollary of Proposition 31, Part II), it can nevertheless happen that we do not doubt their outcome. For we have shown (see the Scholium of Proposition 49, Part II) that not to doubt a thing is one thing, and to have certainty of the thing another; and accordingly, it can happen that we are affected by the image of a past or future thing with the same affect of Joy or Sadness as by the image of a present thing, as we demonstrated in Proposition 18 of this Part; see that Proposition together with its Scholium.","kind":"passage","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_QGOH_PT_5:P0036"]},{"id":"RAW_QGOH_PT_5:P0037","text":"XVI","kind":"passage","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_QGOH_PT_5:P0037"]},{"id":"RAW_QGOH_PT_5:P0038","text":"Gladness is Joy accompanied by the idea of a past thing which has come to pass beyond Hope.","kind":"passage","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_QGOH_PT_5:P0038"]},{"id":"RAW_QGOH_PT_5:P0039","text":"XVII","kind":"passage","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_QGOH_PT_5:P0039"]},{"id":"RAW_QGOH_PT_5:P0040","text":"Remorse of conscience is Sadness accompanied by the idea of a past thing which has come to pass beyond Hope.","kind":"passage","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_QGOH_PT_5:P0040"]},{"id":"RAW_QGOH_PT_5:P0041","text":"XVIII","kind":"passage","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_QGOH_PT_5:P0041"]},{"id":"RAW_QGOH_PT_5:P0042","text":"Compassion is Sadness accompanied by the idea of an evil which has befallen another whom we imagine to be like ourselves (see the Scholium of Proposition 22 and the Scholium of Proposition 27 of this Part).","kind":"passage","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_QGOH_PT_5:P0042"]},{"id":"RAW_QGOH_PT_5:P0043","text":"EXPLANATION","kind":"passage","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_QGOH_PT_5:P0043"]},{"id":"RAW_QGOH_PT_5:P0044","text":"There seems to be no difference between Compassion and Mercy, unless perhaps that Compassion concerns a singular affect, while Mercy concerns its habitual disposition.","kind":"passage","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_QGOH_PT_5:P0044"]},{"id":"RAW_QGOH_PT_5:P0045","text":"XIX","kind":"passage","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_QGOH_PT_5:P0045"]},{"id":"RAW_QGOH_PT_5:P0046","text":"Favor is Love toward someone who has done good to another.","kind":"passage","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_QGOH_PT_5:P0046"]},{"id":"RAW_QGOH_PT_5:P0047","text":"XX","kind":"passage","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_QGOH_PT_5:P0047"]},{"id":"RAW_QGOH_PT_5:P0048","text":"Indignation is Hatred toward someone who has done harm to another.","kind":"passage","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_QGOH_PT_5:P0048"]},{"id":"RAW_QGOH_PT_5:P0049","text":"EXPLANATION","kind":"passage","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_QGOH_PT_5:P0049"]},{"id":"RAW_QGOH_PT_5:P0050","text":"I know that these names signify something else in common usage. But my purpose is not to explain the meaning of words, but the nature of things, and to indicate them by those terms whose meaning, as they have it from usage, does not altogether differ from the meaning in which I wish to employ them; it will suffice to have given this warning once. For the rest, see the cause of these affects in Corollary 1 of Proposition 27 and the Scholium of Proposition 22 of this Part.","kind":"passage","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_QGOH_PT_5:P0050"]},{"id":"RAW_QGOH_PT_5:P0051","text":"XXI","kind":"passage","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_QGOH_PT_5:P0051"]},{"id":"RAW_QGOH_PT_5:P0052","text":"Esteem is thinking of someone, on account of Love, more highly than is just.","kind":"passage","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_QGOH_PT_5:P0052"]},{"id":"RAW_QGOH_PT_5:P0053","text":"XXII","kind":"passage","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_QGOH_PT_5:P0053"]},{"id":"RAW_QGOH_PT_5:P0054","text":"Contempt is thinking of someone, on account of Hatred, less highly than is just.","kind":"passage","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_QGOH_PT_5:P0054"]},{"id":"RAW_QGOH_PT_5:P0055","text":"EXPLANATION","kind":"passage","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_QGOH_PT_5:P0055"]},{"id":"RAW_QGOH_PT_5:P0056","text":"Esteem, therefore, is an effect or property of Love, and Contempt of Hatred; and accordingly Esteem may also be defined as Love insofar as it affects a man so that he thinks of the thing loved more highly than is just; and, conversely, Contempt as Hatred insofar as it affects a man so that he thinks less highly than is just of him whom he hates (see concerning these the Scholium of Proposition 26 of this Part).","kind":"passage","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_QGOH_PT_5:P0056"]},{"id":"RAW_QGOH_PT_5:P0057","text":"XXIII","kind":"passage","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_QGOH_PT_5:P0057"]},{"id":"RAW_QGOH_PT_5:P0058","text":"Envy is Hatred insofar as it affects a man so that he is saddened by another's good fortune and, conversely, rejoices in another's misfortune.","kind":"passage","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_QGOH_PT_5:P0058"]},{"id":"RAW_QGOH_PT_5:P0059","text":"EXPLANATION","kind":"passage","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_QGOH_PT_5:P0059"]},{"id":"RAW_QGOH_PT_5:P0060","text":"Mercy is commonly opposed to Envy, and therefore, contrary to the meaning of the word, may be defined as follows:","kind":"passage","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_QGOH_PT_5:P0060"]},{"id":"RAW_QGOH_PT_5:P0061","text":"XXIV","kind":"passage","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_QGOH_PT_5:P0061"]},{"id":"RAW_QGOH_PT_5:P0062","text":"Mercy is Love insofar as it affects a man so that he rejoices in another's good and, conversely, is saddened by another's evil.","kind":"passage","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_QGOH_PT_5:P0062"]},{"id":"RAW_QGOH_PT_5:P0063","text":"EXPLANATION","kind":"passage","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_QGOH_PT_5:P0063"]},{"id":"RAW_QGOH_PT_5:P0064","text":"For the rest, concerning Envy see the Scholium of Proposition 24 and the Scholium of Proposition 32 of this Part. And these are affects of Joy and Sadness which are accompanied by the idea of an external thing as cause, either through itself or accidentally. Hence I pass on to others, which are accompanied by the idea of an internal thing as cause.","kind":"passage","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_QGOH_PT_5:P0064"]},{"id":"RAW_QGOH_PT_5:P0065","text":"XXV","kind":"passage","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_QGOH_PT_5:P0065"]},{"id":"RAW_QGOH_PT_5:P0066","text":"Self-contentment is Joy arising from the fact that a man contemplates himself and his power of acting.","kind":"passage","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_QGOH_PT_5:P0066"]},{"id":"RAW_QGOH_PT_5:P0067","text":"XXVI","kind":"passage","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_QGOH_PT_5:P0067"]},{"id":"RAW_QGOH_PT_5:P0068","text":"Humility is Sadness arising from the fact that a man contemplates his own powerlessness or weakness.","kind":"passage","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_QGOH_PT_5:P0068"]},{"id":"RAW_QGOH_PT_5:P0069","text":"EXPLANATION","kind":"passage","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_QGOH_PT_5:P0069"]},{"id":"RAW_QGOH_PT_5:P0070","text":"Self-contentment is opposed to Humility, insofar as by the latter we understand the Joy which arises from the fact that we contemplate our power of acting; but insofar as by it we also understand Joy accompanied by the idea of some deed which we believe that we have done from the free decree of the Mind, it is then opposed to Repentance, which we define as follows:","kind":"passage","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_QGOH_PT_5:P0070"]},{"id":"RAW_QGOH_PT_5:P0071","text":"XXVII","kind":"passage","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_QGOH_PT_5:P0071"]},{"id":"RAW_QGOH_PT_5:P0072","text":"Repentance is Sadness accompanied by the idea of some deed which we believe that we have done from a free decree of the Mind.","kind":"passage","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_QGOH_PT_5:P0072"]},{"id":"RAW_QGOH_PT_5:P0073","text":"EXPLANATION","kind":"passage","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_QGOH_PT_5:P0073"]},{"id":"RAW_QGOH_PT_5:P0074","text":"We have shown the causes of these affects in the Scholium to Proposition 51 of this [Part], and in Propositions 53, 54, and 55 of this [Part], and its Scholium. But concerning the free decree of the Mind, see the Scholium to Proposition 35, Part II. Yet here it should further be noted that it is not surprising that Sadness should follow absolutely all acts that are customarily called wrong, and Joy those that are called right. For that this depends chiefly on education is easily understood from what has been said above. For parents, by reproaching the former, and often scolding their children on account of them, but by approving and praising the latter, have brought it about that the agitations of Sadness are joined to the former, and those of Joy to the latter. Experience itself also confirms this. For custom and Religion are not the same for everyone; but, on the contrary, what is sacred among some is profane among others, and what is honorable among some is shameful among others. Therefore, according as each person has been educated, so he repents of some deed, or glories in it.","kind":"passage","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_QGOH_PT_5:P0074"]},{"id":"RAW_QGOH_PT_5:P0075","text":"XXVIII","kind":"passage","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_QGOH_PT_5:P0075"]},{"id":"RAW_QGOH_PT_5:P0076","text":"Pride is thinking of oneself more highly than is just, from Self-love.","kind":"passage","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_QGOH_PT_5:P0076"]},{"id":"RAW_QGOH_PT_5:P0077","text":"EXPLANATION","kind":"passage","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_QGOH_PT_5:P0077"]},{"id":"RAW_QGOH_PT_5:P0078","text":"Pride therefore differs from Esteem in that the latter is referred to an external object, whereas Pride is referred to the very man himself, who thinks of himself more highly than is just. Moreover, just as Esteem is an effect or property of Love, so Pride is an effect or property of Self-love; and therefore it can also be defined as Love of oneself, or Acquiescence in oneself, insofar as it affects a man so that he thinks of himself more highly than is just (see Schol. Prop. 26 of this [Part]). No contrary is given to this affect. For no one thinks of himself less highly than is just from Hatred of himself; indeed, no one thinks of himself less highly than is just insofar as he imagines that he cannot do this or that. For whatever a man imagines that he cannot do, he necessarily imagines, and by this imagination he is so disposed that he is in fact unable to do that which he imagines he cannot do. For as long as he imagines that he cannot do this or that, so long is he not determined to act, and consequently, so long is it impossible for him to do it.","kind":"passage","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_QGOH_PT_5:P0078"]},{"id":"RAW_QGOH_PT_5:P0079","text":"But indeed, if we attend to those things which depend on opinion alone, we shall be able to conceive that it can come about that a man thinks of himself less highly than is just. For it can happen that someone, while sadly contemplating his own weakness, imagines himself to be despised by everyone, while the rest think of nothing less than despising him. Moreover, a man can think of himself less highly than is just if, with relation to a future time of which he is uncertain, he denies something of himself in the present; for example, if he denies that he can conceive anything certain, and that he can desire or do nothing except what is wrong or shameful, etc. We can further say that someone thinks of himself less highly than is just when we see that, from an excessive fear of shame, he does not dare to do those things which others equal to himself dare to do. Therefore we can oppose this affect to Pride, and I shall call it Abjectness; for as Pride arises from Acquiescence in oneself, so Abjectness arises from Humility, and hence is defined by us as follows.","kind":"passage","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_QGOH_PT_5:P0079"]},{"id":"RAW_QGOH_PT_5:P0080","text":"XXIX","kind":"passage","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_QGOH_PT_5:P0080"]},{"id":"RAW_QGOH_PT_5:P0081","text":"Abjectness is thinking of oneself less highly than is just, from Sadness.","kind":"passage","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_QGOH_PT_5:P0081"]},{"id":"RAW_QGOH_PT_5:P0082","text":"EXPLANATION","kind":"passage","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_QGOH_PT_5:P0082"]},{"id":"RAW_QGOH_PT_5:P0083","text":"Yet we often oppose Humility to Pride, but then we attend more to the effects of each than to their nature. For we are accustomed to call proud one who boasts too much (see Schol. Prop. 30 of this [Part]), who recounts only his own virtues and only others' vices, who wishes to be preferred to everyone, and who finally proceeds with that gravity and adornment with which others who are placed far above him are accustomed [to proceed]. On the other hand, we call humble one who blushes more often, who confesses his own vices and recounts the virtues of others, who yields to everyone, and who finally walks with bowed head and neglects to adorn himself. Moreover, these affects—namely, Humility and Abjectness—are exceedingly rare. For human nature, considered in itself, strives against them as much as it can (see Props. 13 and 54 of this [Part]); and therefore those who are believed to be most abject and humble are for the most part most ambitious and envious.","kind":"passage","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_QGOH_PT_5:P0083"]},{"id":"RAW_QGOH_PT_5:P0084","text":"XXX","kind":"passage","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_QGOH_PT_5:P0084"]},{"id":"RAW_QGOH_PT_5:P0085","text":"Glory is Joy accompanied by the idea of some action of ours which we imagine others to praise.","kind":"passage","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_QGOH_PT_5:P0085"]},{"id":"RAW_QGOH_PT_5:P0086","text":"XXXI","kind":"passage","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_QGOH_PT_5:P0086"]},{"id":"RAW_QGOH_PT_5:P0087","text":"Shame is Sadness accompanied by the idea of some action which we imagine others to blame.","kind":"passage","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_QGOH_PT_5:P0087"]},{"id":"RAW_QGOH_PT_5:P0088","text":"EXPLANATION","kind":"passage","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_QGOH_PT_5:P0088"]},{"id":"RAW_QGOH_PT_5:P0089","text":"On these, see the Scholium to Proposition 30 of this Part. But here the difference between Shame and Modesty is to be noted. For Shame is the Sadness which follows a deed of which one is ashamed. Modesty, however, is the Fear or Dread of Shame, by which a man is restrained from committing something shameful. Impudence is customarily opposed to Modesty; but in truth it is not an affect, as I shall show in its place. But the names of affects (as I have already warned) regard their use more than their nature. And with these I have completed the affects of Joy and Sadness which I had proposed to explain. I proceed therefore to those which I refer to Desire.","kind":"passage","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_QGOH_PT_5:P0089"]},{"id":"RAW_QGOH_PT_5:P0090","text":"XXXII","kind":"passage","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_QGOH_PT_5:P0090"]},{"id":"RAW_QGOH_PT_5:P0091","text":"Longing is Desire or Appetite for obtaining some thing, which is fostered by the memory of that same thing and is at the same time restrained by the memory of other things that exclude the existence of the thing to be desired.","kind":"passage","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_QGOH_PT_5:P0091"]},{"id":"RAW_QGOH_PT_5:P0092","text":"EXPLANATION","kind":"passage","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_QGOH_PT_5:P0092"]},{"id":"RAW_QGOH_PT_5:P0093","text":"When we remember some thing, as we have often already said, by that very fact we are disposed to contemplate it with the same affect as if the thing were present; but this disposition, or striving, while we are awake, is for the most part checked by images of things that exclude the existence of that which we remember. Therefore, when we remember a thing that affects us with some kind of Joy, by that very fact we strive to contemplate it as present with the same affect of Joy; and this striving is immediately checked by the memory of things that exclude its existence. Wherefore Longing is in truth a Sadness, which is opposed to that Joy which arises from the absence of a thing that we hate, concerning which see the Scholium to Proposition 47 of this Part. But because the name Longing seems to have regard to Desire, I therefore refer this affect to the affects of Desire.","kind":"passage","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_QGOH_PT_5:P0093"]},{"id":"RAW_QGOH_PT_5:P0094","text":"XXXIII","kind":"passage","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_QGOH_PT_5:P0094"]},{"id":"RAW_QGOH_PT_5:P0095","text":"Emulation is Desire for some thing which is generated in us from the fact that we imagine others to have the same Desire.","kind":"passage","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_QGOH_PT_5:P0095"]},{"id":"RAW_QGOH_PT_5:P0096","text":"EXPLANATION","kind":"passage","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_QGOH_PT_5:P0096"]},{"id":"RAW_QGOH_PT_5:P0097","text":"He who flees because he sees others flee, or who fears because he sees others fear; or likewise he who, because he sees that someone has burned his hand, draws his own hand back toward himself and moves his body as though his own hand were being burned—we shall indeed say that he imitates another's affect, but not that he emulates the same one; not because we know one cause of emulation and another of imitation, but because usage has made it the case that we call only that man an emulator who imitates what we judge to be honorable, useful, or pleasant. For the rest, concerning the cause of Emulation, see Proposition 27 of this Part with its Scholium. But why Envy is for the most part joined to this affect, see Proposition 32 of this Part with its Scholium.","kind":"passage","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_QGOH_PT_5:P0097"]},{"id":"RAW_QGOH_PT_5:P0098","text":"XXXIV","kind":"passage","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_QGOH_PT_5:P0098"]},{"id":"RAW_QGOH_PT_5:P0099","text":"Favor, or Gratitude, is Desire, or the striving of Love, by which we endeavor to do good to one who has conferred a benefit upon us with an equal affect of love. See Prop. 39 with the Scholium of Prop. 41 of this Part.","kind":"passage","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_QGOH_PT_5:P0099"]},{"id":"RAW_QGOH_PT_5:P0100","text":"XXXV","kind":"passage","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_QGOH_PT_5:P0100"]},{"id":"RAW_QGOH_PT_5:P0101","text":"Benevolence is the Desire to do good to one whom we pity. See the Scholium of Prop. 27 of this Part.","kind":"passage","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_QGOH_PT_5:P0101"]},{"id":"RAW_QGOH_PT_5:P0102","text":"XXXVI","kind":"passage","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_QGOH_PT_5:P0102"]},{"id":"RAW_QGOH_PT_5:P0103","text":"Anger is the Desire by which, from Hate, we are incited to inflict evil upon one whom we hate. See Prop. 39 of this Part.","kind":"passage","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_QGOH_PT_5:P0103"]},{"id":"RAW_QGOH_PT_5:P0104","text":"XXXVII","kind":"passage","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_QGOH_PT_5:P0104"]},{"id":"RAW_QGOH_PT_5:P0105","text":"Vengeance is the Desire by which, from reciprocal Hate, we are stirred to inflict evil upon one who has inflicted harm upon us with an equal affect. See Corollary 2 of Prop. 40 of this Part with its Scholium.","kind":"passage","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_QGOH_PT_5:P0105"]},{"id":"RAW_QGOH_PT_5:P0106","text":"XXXVIII","kind":"passage","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_QGOH_PT_5:P0106"]},{"id":"RAW_QGOH_PT_5:P0107","text":"Cruelty, or Savagery, is the Desire by which someone is stirred to inflict evil upon one whom we love, or upon one whom we do not pity.","kind":"passage","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_QGOH_PT_5:P0107"]},{"id":"RAW_QGOH_PT_5:P0108","text":"EXPLANATION","kind":"passage","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_QGOH_PT_5:P0108"]},{"id":"RAW_QGOH_PT_5:P0109","text":"Clemency is opposed to Cruelty; it is not a passion, but a power of mind by which a man moderates anger and vengeance.","kind":"passage","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_QGOH_PT_5:P0109"]},{"id":"RAW_QGOH_PT_5:P0110","text":"XXXIX","kind":"passage","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_QGOH_PT_5:P0110"]},{"id":"RAW_QGOH_PT_5:P0111","text":"Fear is the Desire to avoid a lesser evil which we fear by means of a greater one. See the Scholium of Prop. 39 of this Part.","kind":"passage","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_QGOH_PT_5:P0111"]},{"id":"RAW_QGOH_PT_5:P0112","text":"XL","kind":"passage","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_QGOH_PT_5:P0112"]},{"id":"RAW_QGOH_PT_5:P0113","text":"Boldness is the Desire by which someone is incited to undertake something with a danger which his equals fear to undergo.","kind":"passage","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_QGOH_PT_5:P0113"]},{"id":"RAW_QGOH_PT_5:P0114","text":"XLI","kind":"passage","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_QGOH_PT_5:P0114"]},{"id":"RAW_QGOH_PT_5:P0115","text":"Faintheartedness is said of one whose Desire is checked by the fear of a danger which his equals dare to undergo.","kind":"passage","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_QGOH_PT_5:P0115"]},{"id":"RAW_QGOH_PT_5:P0116","text":"EXPLANATION","kind":"passage","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_QGOH_PT_5:P0116"]},{"id":"RAW_QGOH_PT_5:P0117","text":"Faintheartedness, therefore, is nothing but the Fear of some evil which most people are not accustomed to fear; and for this reason I do not refer it to the affects of Desire. Yet I have wished to explain it here, because, insofar as we attend to Desire, it is truly opposed to the affect of Boldness.","kind":"passage","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_QGOH_PT_5:P0117"]},{"id":"RAW_QGOH_PT_5:P0118","text":"XLII","kind":"passage","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_QGOH_PT_5:P0118"]},{"id":"RAW_QGOH_PT_5:P0119","text":"Consternation is said of one whose Desire to avoid an evil is checked by astonishment at the evil which he fears.","kind":"passage","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_QGOH_PT_5:P0119"]},{"id":"RAW_QGOH_PT_5:P0120","text":"EXPLANATION","kind":"passage","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_QGOH_PT_5:P0120"]},{"id":"RAW_QGOH_PT_5:P0121","text":"Consternation, therefore, is a species of Faint-heartedness. But because Consternation arises from a twofold Fear, it can more conveniently be defined as a Dread that so holds a man stupefied or wavering that he cannot remove the evil. I say stupefied, insofar as we understand that his Desire to remove the evil is restrained by astonishment. But I say wavering insofar as we conceive that the same Desire is restrained by the Fear of another evil, which torments him equally; whence it comes about that he does not know which of the two to avert. On these matters see the Schol. to Prop. 39 and the Schol. to Prop. 52 of this Part. Moreover, concerning Faint-heartedness and Boldness, see the Schol. to Prop. 51 of this Part.","kind":"passage","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_QGOH_PT_5:P0121"]},{"id":"RAW_QGOH_PT_5:P0122","text":"XLIII","kind":"passage","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_QGOH_PT_5:P0122"]},{"id":"RAW_QGOH_PT_5:P0123","text":"Humanity, or Modesty, is the Desire to do those things that please men, and to omit those that displease them.","kind":"passage","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_QGOH_PT_5:P0123"]},{"id":"RAW_QGOH_PT_5:P0124","text":"XLIV","kind":"passage","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_QGOH_PT_5:P0124"]},{"id":"RAW_QGOH_PT_5:P0125","text":"Ambition is an immoderate Desire for glory.","kind":"passage","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_QGOH_PT_5:P0125"]},{"id":"RAW_QGOH_PT_5:P0126","text":"EXPLANATION","kind":"passage","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_QGOH_PT_5:P0126"]},{"id":"RAW_QGOH_PT_5:P0127","text":"Ambition is the Desire by which all the affects are fostered and strengthened (by Props. 27 and 31 of this Part); and therefore this affect can scarcely be overcome. For so long as a man is held by any Desire, he is at the same time necessarily held by this one. “The best of men,” says Cicero, “are most especially led by glory.” Even philosophers inscribe their names on the books that they write concerning the contempt of glory, etc.","kind":"passage","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_QGOH_PT_5:P0127"]},{"id":"RAW_QGOH_PT_5:P0128","text":"XLV","kind":"passage","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_QGOH_PT_5:P0128"]},{"id":"RAW_QGOH_PT_5:P0129","text":"Luxury is an immoderate Desire, or also Love, of feasting.","kind":"passage","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_QGOH_PT_5:P0129"]},{"id":"RAW_QGOH_PT_5:P0130","text":"XLVI","kind":"passage","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_QGOH_PT_5:P0130"]},{"id":"RAW_QGOH_PT_5:P0131","text":"Drunkenness is an immoderate Desire and Love of drinking.","kind":"passage","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_QGOH_PT_5:P0131"]},{"id":"RAW_QGOH_PT_5:P0132","text":"XLVII","kind":"passage","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_QGOH_PT_5:P0132"]},{"id":"RAW_QGOH_PT_5:P0133","text":"Avarice is an immoderate Desire and Love of riches.","kind":"passage","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_QGOH_PT_5:P0133"]},{"id":"RAW_QGOH_PT_5:P0134","text":"XLVIII","kind":"passage","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_QGOH_PT_5:P0134"]},{"id":"RAW_QGOH_PT_5:P0135","text":"Lust is likewise Desire and Love in the mingling of bodies.","kind":"passage","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_QGOH_PT_5:P0135"]},{"id":"RAW_QGOH_PT_5:P0136","text":"EXPLANATION","kind":"passage","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_QGOH_PT_5:P0136"]},{"id":"RAW_QGOH_PT_5:P0137","text":"Whether this Desire for coition be moderate or not, it is usually called Lust. Moreover, these five affects (as I have noted in the Schol. to Prop. 56 of this Part) have no contraries. For Modesty is a species of Ambition; concerning which see the Schol. to Prop. 29 of this Part. Further, I have already noted that Temperance, Sobriety, and Chastity indicate a power of the Mind, not a passion. And although it can happen that an avaricious, ambitious, or timid man abstains from excessive food, drink, and coition, Avarice, nevertheless, Ambition, and Fear are not contrary to luxury, drunkenness, or lust. For the avaricious man generally desires to gorge himself on another’s food and drink. But the ambitious man, provided he hopes that it will be done secretly, will restrain himself in nothing; and if he lives among drunkards and lustful men, precisely because he is ambitious, he will be more prone to the same vices. Finally, the timid man does what he does not will.","kind":"passage","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_QGOH_PT_5:P0137"]},{"id":"RAW_QGOH_PT_5:P0138","text":"For although, in order to avoid death, he casts his riches into the sea, he nevertheless remains avaricious; and if the lustful man is sad because he cannot indulge himself, he does not for that reason cease to be lustful. And absolutely, these affects have regard not so much to the very acts of feasting, drinking, etc., as to Appetite and Love themselves. Therefore nothing can be opposed to these affects except Generosity and Strength of Mind, concerning which in what follows.","kind":"passage","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_QGOH_PT_5:P0138"]},{"id":"RAW_QGOH_PT_5:P0139","text":"I pass over in silence the definitions of Jealousy and the remaining fluctuations of the mind, both because they arise from a composition of the affects that we have already defined, and because most of them have no names; which shows that, for the use of life, it suffices to know them only in general. Moreover, from the definitions of the affects that we have explained, it is clear that they all arise from Desire, Joy, or Sadness; or rather, that they are nothing other than these three, each of which is usually called by various names on account of their various relations and extrinsic denominations. If we now wish to attend to these primitive affects, and to what we have said above concerning the nature of the Mind, we shall be able to define the affects, insofar as they are referred to the Mind alone, as follows.","kind":"passage","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_QGOH_PT_5:P0139"]},{"id":"RAW_QGOH_PT_5:P0140","text":"GENERAL DEFINITION OF THE AFFECTS","kind":"passage","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_QGOH_PT_5:P0140"]},{"id":"RAW_QGOH_PT_5:P0141","text":"An affect, which is called a passion of the mind, is a confused idea by which the Mind affirms of its Body, or of some part of it, a greater or lesser force of existing than before, and by which, when it is given, the Mind itself is determined to think of this rather than of that.","kind":"passage","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_QGOH_PT_5:P0141"]},{"id":"RAW_QGOH_PT_5:P0142","text":"EXPLANATION","kind":"passage","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_QGOH_PT_5:P0142"]},{"id":"RAW_QGOH_PT_5:P0143","text":"First, I say that an affect, or passion of the mind, is a confused idea. For we have shown (see Prop. 3 of this Part) that the Mind suffers only insofar as it has inadequate, or confused, ideas. Next, I say, by which the Mind affirms of its Body, or of some part of it, a greater or lesser force of existing than before. For all the ideas of bodies that we have indicate the actual constitution of our Body (by Coroll. 2 to Prop. 16, Part II) more than the nature of the external body; but this idea, which constitutes the form of an affect, must indicate or express the constitution of the Body, or of some part of it, which the Body itself, or some part of it, has from the fact that its power of acting, or force of existing, is increased or diminished, aided or restrained.","kind":"passage","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_QGOH_PT_5:P0143"]},{"id":"RAW_QGOH_PT_5:P0144","text":"But it should be noted that when I say a greater or lesser force of existing than before, I do not mean that the Mind compares the present constitution of the Body with a past one; but that the idea which constitutes the form of an affect affirms something of the Body that in reality involves more or less reality than before. And because the essence of the Mind consists in this (by Props. 11 and 13, Part II), that it affirms the actual existence of its Body, and we understand by perfection the very essence of a thing; it follows, therefore, that the Mind passes to a greater or lesser perfection when it happens to affirm of its Body, or of some part of it, something that involves more or less reality than before.","kind":"passage","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_QGOH_PT_5:P0144"]},{"id":"RAW_QGOH_PT_5:P0145","text":"Therefore, when I said above that the power of the Mind for thinking is increased or diminished, I intended to understand nothing other than that the Mind has formed an idea of its Body, or of some part of it, which expresses more or less reality than it had affirmed of its Body. For the excellence of ideas, and the actual power of thinking, is estimated from the excellence of the object. Finally, I added, “and by which, given an idea, the Mind is determined to think of this rather than of that,” so that, besides the nature of Joy and Sadness, which the first part of the definition explains, I might also express the nature of Desire.","kind":"passage","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_QGOH_PT_5:P0145"]},{"id":"RAW_QGOH_PT_5:P0146","text":"End of the Third Part","kind":"passage","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_QGOH_PT_5:P0146"]}],"source_digest":"ecf47034822fb034924993d678ec4b812908c4b38b564a173bbea9d57a555237","source_language_code":"la","original_title":"Pars tertia - De origine et natura affectuum · passage 5"}],"passage_basis":"frozen-grounding","original_title":"Pars tertia - De origine et natura affectuum · passage 5","title_language_code":"en"},{"id":"RAW_QGOH_PQDS","title":"Part Four - On Human Bondage, or the Powers of the Affects · passage 1","order":14,"chapter_id":"CH_QGOH_PQDS","text":"## ETHICES\n\n## PARS QUARTA\n\n## DE SERVITUTE HUMANA\n\n## SEU DE AFFECTUUM VIRIBUS\n\nPRÆFATiO\n\nHumanam impotentiam in moderandis et coercendis affectibus Servitutem voco ; homo enim affectibus obnoxius sui juris non est, sed fortunæ ; in cujus potestate ila est, ut sæpe coactus sit, quanquam meliora sibi videat, deteriora tamen sequi. Hujus rei causam, et quid præterea affectus boni vel mali habent, in hac Parte demonstrare proposui. Sed antequam incipiam, pauca de perfectione et imperfectione, deque bono et malo præfari lubet.\n\nQui rem aliquam facere constituit, eamque perfecit, rem suam perfectam esse, non tantum ipse, sed etiam unusquisque, qui mentem Auctoris illius operis et scopum recte noverit, aut se novisse crediderit, dicet. Ex. gr. si quis aliquod opus (quod suppono nondum esse peractum) viderit, noveritque scopum Auctoris illius operis esse domum ædificare, is domum imperfectam esse dicet, et contra perfectam, simulatque opus ad finem, quem hujus Auctor eidem dare constituerat, perductum viderit. Verum si quis opus aliquod videt, cujus simile nunquam viderat, nec mentem opificis novit, is sane scire non poterit, opusne illud perfectum an imperfectum sit. Atque hæc videtur prima fuisse horum vocabulorum significatio. Sed postquam homines ideas universales formare, et domuum, ædificiorum, turrium, etc. exemplaria excogitare, et alia rerum exemplaria aliis præferre inceperunt, factum est, ut unusquisque id perfectum vocaret, quod cum universali idea, quam ejusmodi rei formaverat, videret convenire, et id contra imperfectum, quod cum concepto suo exemplari minus convenire videret, quanquam ex opificis sententia consummatum plane esset. Nec alia videtur esse ratio, cur res naturales etiam, quæ scilicet humana manu non sunt factæ, perfectas aut imperfectas vulgo appellent ; solent namque homines tam rerum naturalium quam artificialium ideas formare universales, quas rerum veluti exemplaria habent, et quas Naturam (quam nihil nisi alicujus finis causa agere existimant) intueri credunt sibique exemplaria proponere. Cum itaque aliquid in Natura fieri vident, quod cum concepto exemplari, quod rei ejusmodi habent, minus convenit, ipsam Naturam tum defecisse vel peccavisse remque illam imperfectam reliquisse credunt. Videmus itaque homines consuevisse, res naturales perfectas aut imperfectas vocare, magis ex præjudicio quam ex earum vera cognitione. Ostendimus enim in Primæ Partis Appendice, Naturam propter finem non agere ; æternum namque illud et infinitum Ens, quod Deum seu. Naturam appellamus eadem, qua existit, necessitate agit. Ex qua enim naturæ necessitate existit, ex eadem ipsum ageire ostendimus (Prop. 16 p. I). Ratio igitur, seu causa, cur Deus seu Natura agit, et cur existit, una eademque est. Ut ergo nullius finis causa existit, nullius etiam finis causa agit ; sed ut existendi, sic et agendi principium, vel finem, habet nullum. Causa autem, quæ finalis dicitur, nihil est præter ipsum humanum appetitum, quatenus is alicujus rei veluti principium seu causa primaria consideratur. Ex. gr. cum dicimus habitationem causam fuisse finalem hujus aut illius domus, nihil tum sane intelligimus aliud, quam quod homo ex eo, quod vitæ domesticæ commoda imaginatus est, appetitum habuit ædificandi domum. Quare habitatio, quatenus ut finalis causa consideratur, nihil est præter hunc singularem appetitum, qui revera causa est efficiens ; quæ ut prima consideratur, quia homines suorum appetituum causas communiter ignorant. Sunt namque, ut jam sæpe dixi, suarum quidem actionum ut appetituum conscii, sed ignari causarum, a quibus ad aliquid appetendum determinantur. Quod præterea vulgo ajunt, Naturam aliquando deficere vel peccare, resque interea imperfectas producere, inter commenta numero, de quibus in Appendice Partis Primæ egi. Perfectio igitur et imperfectio revera modi solummodo cogitandi sunt, nempe notiones, quas fingere solemus ex eo, quod ejusdem speciei aut generis individua ad invicem comparamus : et hac de causa supra (Defin. 6 p. II) dixi, me per realitatem et perfectionem idem intelligere. Solemus enim omnia Naturæ individua ad unum genus, quod generalissimum appellatur, revocare ; nempe ad notionem Entis, quæ ad omnia absolute Naturæ individua pertinet. Quatenus itaque Naturæ individua ad hoc genus revocamus, et ad invicem comparamus, et alia plus entitatis seu realitatis quam alia habere comperimus, eatenus alia aliis perfectiora esse dicimus ; et quatenus iisdem aliquid tribuimus, quod negationem involvit, ut terminus, finis, impotentia, etc., eatenus ipsa imperfecta appellamus, quia nostram Mentem non æque afficiunt ac illa, quæ perfecta vocamus, et non quod ipsis aliquid, quod suum sit, deficiat, vel quod Natura peccaverit. Nihil enim naturæ alicujus rei competit, nisi id, quod ex necessitate naturæ causæ efficientis sequitur, et quicquid ex necessitate naturæ causæ efficientis sequitur, id necessario fit.\n\nBonum et malum quod attinet, nihil etiam positivum in rebus, in se scilicet consideratis, indicant, nec aliud sunt præter cogitandi modos, seu notiones, quas formamus ex eo, quod res ad invicem comparamus. Nam una eademque res potest eodem tempore bona et mala, et etiam indifferens esse. Ex. gr. musica bona est Melancholico, mala Lugenti ; Surdo autem neque bona neque mala. Verum, quamvis se res ita habeat, nobis tamen hæc vocabula retinenda sunt. Nam quia ideam hominis, tanquam naturæ humanæ exemplar, quod intueamur formare cupimus, nobis ex usu erit, hæc eadem vocabula eo, quo dixi, sensu retinere. Per bonum itaque in seqq. intelligam id, quod certo scimus medium esse, ut ad exemplar humanæ naturæ, quod nobis proponimus, magis magisque accedamus. Per malum autem id, quod certo scimus impedire, quo minus idem exemplar referamus. Deinde homines perfectiores aut imperfectiores dicemus, quatenus ad hoc idem exemplar magis aut minus accedunt. Nam apprime notandum est, cum dico, aliquem a minore ad majorem perfectionem transire, et contra, me non intelligere, quod ex una essentia seu forma in aliam mutatur ; Equus namque ex. gr. tam destruitur, si in hominem, quam si in insectum mutetur ; sed quod ejus agendi potentiam, quatenus hæc per ipsius naturam intelligitur, augeri vel minui concipimus. Denique per perfectionem in genere realitatem, uti dixi, intelligam ; hoc est, rei cujuscunque essentiam, quatenus certo modo existit et operatur, nulla ipsius durationis habita ratione. Nam nulla res singularis potest ideo dici perfectior, quia plus temporis in existendo perseveravit ; quippe rerum duratio ex earum essentia determinari nequit ; quandoquidem rerum essentia nullum certum et determinatum existendi tempus involvit ; sed res quæcunque, sive ea perfectior sit, sive minus, eadem vi, qua existere incipit, semper in existendo perseverare poterit, ita ut omnes hac in re æquales sint.\n\nDEFINITIONES\n\nI. Per bonum id intelligam, quod certo scimus nobis esse utile.\n\nII. Per malum autem id, quod certo scimus impedire, quo minus boni alicujus simus compotes.\n\n(De his præcedentem vide præfationem sub finem.)\n\nIII. Res singulares voco contingentes, quatenus dum ad earum solam essentiam attendimus, nihil invenimus, quod earum existentiam necessario ponat, vel quod ipsam necessario secludat.\n\nIV. Easdem res singulares voco possibiles, quatenus, dum ad causas, ex quibus produci debent, attendimus, nescimus, an ipsæ determinatæ sint ad easdem producendum.\n\n(In Schol. 1 Prop. 33 p. I inter possibile et contingens nullam feci differentiam, quia ibi non opus erat hæc accurate distinguere.)\n\nV. Per contrarios affectus in seqq. intelligam eos, qui hominem diversum trahunt, quamvis ejusdem sint generis, ut luxuries et avaritia, quæ amoris sunt species ; nec natura, sed per accidens sunt contrarii.\n\nVI. Quid per affectum erga rem futuram, præsentem, et præteritam, intelligam, explicui in Schol. 1 et 2 Prop. 18 p. III, quod vide.\n\n(Sed venit hic præterea notandum, quod ut loci, sic etiam temporis distantiam non nisi usque ad certum quendam limitem possumus distincte imaginari ; hoc est, sicut omnia illa objecta, quæ ultra ducentos pedes a nobis distant, seu quorum distantia a loco, in quo sumus, illam superat, quam distincte imaginamur, æque longe a nobis distare, et perinde, ac si in eodem plano essent, imaginari solemus ; sic etiam objecta, quorum existendi tempus longiore a præsenti intervallo abesse imaginamur, quam quod distincte imaginari solemus, omnia æque longe a præsenti distare imaginamur, et ad unum quasi temporis momentum referimus.)\n\nVII. Per finem, cujus causa aliquid facimus, appetitum intelligo.\n\nVIII. Per virtutem et potentiam idem intelligo ; hoc est (per Prop. 7 p. III), virtus, quatenus ad hominem refertur, est ipsa hominis essentia seu natura, quatenus potestatem habet quædam efficiendi, quæ per solas ipsius naturæ leges possunt intelligi.\n\nAXIOMA\n\nNulla res singularis in rerum Natura datur, qua potentior et fortior non detur alia. Sed quacunque data datur alia potentior, a qua illa data potest destrui.\n\nPROPOSITIO I\n\nNihil, quod idea falsa positivum habet, tollitur præsentia veri, quatenus verum.\n\nDEMONSTRATIO\n\nFalsitas in sola privatione cognitionis, quam ideæ inadæquatæ involvunt, consistit (per Prop. 35 p. II) nec ipsæ aliquid habent positivum, propter quod falsæ dicuntur (per Prop. 33 p. II) ; sed contra, quatenus ad Deum referuntur, veræ sunt (per Prop. 32 p. II). Si igitur id, quod idea falsa positivum habet, præsentia veri, quatenus verum est, tolleretur, tolleretur ergo idea vera a se ipsa, quod (per Prop. 4 p. III) est absurdum. Ergo nihil quod idea etc. Q. E. D.\n\nSCHOLIUM\n\nIntelligitur hæc Propositio clarius ex 2 Coroll. Prop. 16 p. II. Nam imaginatio idea est, quæ magis Corporis humani præsentem constitutionem, quam corporis externi naturam indicat ; non quidem distincte, sed confuse ; unde fit, ut Mens errare dicatur. Ex. gr. cum solem intuemur, eundem ducentos circiter pedes a nobis distare imaginamur ; in quo tamdiu fallimur, quamdiu veram ejus distantiam ignoramus ; sed cognita ejusdem distantia tollitur quidem error, sed non imaginatio, hoc est idea solis, quæ ejusdem naturam eatenus tantum explicat, quatenus Corpus ab eodem afficitur ; adeoque, quamvis veram ejusdem distantiam noscamus, ipsum nihilominus prope nobis adesse imaginabimur. Nam ut in Schol. Prop. 35 p. II diximus, non ea de causa solem adeo propinquum imaginamur, quia ejus veram distantiam ignoramus ; sed quia Mens eatenus magnitudinem solis concipit, quatenus Corpus ab eodem afficitur. Sic cum solis radii, aquæ superficiei incidentes, ad nostros oculos reflectuntur, eundem perinde, ac si in aqua esset imaginamur, tametsi verum ejus locum noverimus ; et sic reliquæ imaginationes, quibus Mens fallitur, sive eæ naturalem Corporis constitutionem, sive quod ejusdem agendi potentiam augeri vel minui indicant, vero non sunt contrariæ, nec ejusdem præsentia evanescunt. Fit quidem, cum falso aliquod malum timemus, ut timor evanescat audito vero nuntio ; sed contra etiam fit, cum malum, quod certe venturum est, timemus, ut timor etiam evanescat audito falso nuntio ; atque adeo imaginationes non præsentia veri, quatenus verum, evanescunt, sed quia aliæ occurrunt iis fortiores, quæ rerum, quas imaginamur, præsentem existentiam secludunt ut Prop. 17 p. II ostendimus.\n\nPROPOSITIO II\n\nNos eatenus patimur, quatenus Naturæ sumus pars, quæ per se absque aliis non potest concipi.\n\nDEMONSTRATIO\n\nNos tum pati dicimur, cum aliquid in nobis oritur, cujus non nisi partialis sumus causa (per Defin. 2 p. III) hoc est (per Defin. 1 p. III) aliquid, quod ex solis legibus nostræ naturæ deduci nequit. Patimur igitur, quatenus Naturæ sumus pars, quæ per se absque aliis nequit concipi. Q. E. D.\n\nPROPOSITIO III\n\nVis, qua homo in existendo perseverat, limitata est, et a potentia causarum externarum infinite superatur.\n\nDEMONSTRATIO\n\nPatet ex Axiomate hujus. Nam dato homine datur aliquid aliud, puta A, potentius, et dato A datur deinde aliud, puta B, ipso A potentius, et hoc in infinitum ; ac proinde potentia hominis potentia alterius rei definitur, et a potentia causarum externarum infinite superatur. Q. E. D.\n\nPROPOSITIO IV\n\nFieri non potest, ut homo non sit Naturæ pars, et ut nullas possit pati mutationes, nisi quæ per solam suam naturam possint intelligi, quarumque adæquata sit causa.\n\nDEMONSTRATIO\n\nPotentia, qua res singulares, et consequenter homo suum esse conservat, est ipsa Dei sive Naturæ potentia (per Coroll. Prop. 24 p. I), non quatenus infinita est, sed quatenus per humanam actualem essentiam explicari potest (per Prop. 7 p. III). Potentia itaque hominis, quatenus per ipsius actualem essentiam explicatur, pars est infinitæ Dei seu Naturæ potentiæ, hoc est (per Prop. 34 p. I) essentiæ ; Quod erat primum. Deinde si fieri posset, ut homo nullas posset pati mutationes, nisi quæ per solam ipsius hominis naturam possint intelligi, sequeretur (per Prop. 4 et 6 p. III), ut non posset perire, sed ut semper necessario existeret ; atque hoc sequi deberet ex causa, cujus potentia finita aut infinita sit ; nempe vel ex sola hominis potentia, qui scilicet potis esset, ut a se removeret reliquas mutationes, quæ a causis externis oriri possent ; vel infinita Naturæ potentia, a qua omnia singularia ita dirigerentur, ut homo nullas alias posset pati mutationes, nisi quæ ipsius conservationi inserviunt. At primum (per Prop. præc., cujus demonstratio universalis est, et ad omnes res singulares applicari potest) est absurdum. Ergo, si fieri posset, ut homo nullas pateretur mutationes, nisi quæ per solam ipsius hominis naturam possent intelligi, et consequenter (sicut jam ostendimus) ut semper necessario existeret ; id sequi deberet ex Dei infinita potentia, et consequenter (per Prop. 16 p. I) ex necessitate divinæ naturæ, quatenus alicujus hominis idea affectus consideratur, totius Naturæ ordo, quatenus ipsa sub Extensionis et Cogitationis attributis concipitur, deduci deberet ; atque adeo (per Prop. 21 p. I) sequeretur, ut homo esset infinitus, quod (per I part. hujus demonstrationis) est absurdum. Fieri itaque nequit, ut homo nullas alias patiatur mutationes, nisi quarum ipse adæquata sit causa. Q. E. D.\n\nCOROLLARIUM\n\nHinc sequitur, hominem necessario passionibus esse semper obnoxium, communemque Naturæ ordinem sequi et eidem parere, seseque eidem quantum rerum natura exigit accommodare.\n\nPROPOSITIO V\n\nVis et incrementum cujuscunque passionis, ejusque in existendo perseverantia non definitur potentia, qua nos in existendo perseverare conamur, sed causæ externæ potentia cum nostra comparata.\n\nDEMONSTRATIO\n\nPassionis essentia non potest per solam nostram essentiam explicari (per Defin. 1 et 2 p. III), hoc est (per Prop. 7 p. III), passionis potentia definiri nequit potentia, qua in nostro esse perseverare conamur, sed (ut Prop. 16 p. II ostensum est) definiri necessario debet potentia causæ externæ cum nostra comparata. Q. E. D.\n\nPROPOSITIO VI\n\nVis alicujus passionis, seu affectus, reliquas hominis actiones, seu potentiam, superare potest, ita ut affectus pertinaciter homini adhæreat.\n\nDEMONSTRATIO\n\nVis et incrementum cujuscunque passionis, ejusque in existendo perseverantia, definitur potentia causæ externæ cum nostra comparata (per Prop. præced.) ; adeoque (per Prop. 3 hujus) hominis potentiam superare potest, etc. Q. E. D.\n\nPROPOSITIO VII\n\nAffectus coerceri nec tolli potest, nisi per affectum contrarium et fortiorem affectu coercendo.\n\nDEMONSTRATIO\n\nAffectus, quatenus ad Mentem refertur, est idea, qua Mens majorem vel minorem sui corporis existendi vim, quam antea, affirmat (per generalem Affectuum Definitionem, quæ reperitur sub finem Tertiæ Partis). Cum igitur Mens aliquo affectu conflictatur, Corpus afficitur simul affectione, qua ejus agendi potentia augetur vel minuitur. Porro hæc Corporis affectio (per Prop. 5 hujus) vim a sua causa accipit perseverandi in suo esse ; quæ proinde nec coerceri nec tolli potest, nisi a causa corporea (per Prop. 6 p. II) quæ corpus afficiat affectione illi contraria (per Prop. 5 p. III) et fortiore (per axioma hujus) ; atque adeo (per Prop. 12 p. II). Mens afficietur idea affectionis fortioris et contrariæ priori, hoc est (per general. Affectuum Defin.), Mens afficietur affectu fortiori et contrario priori, qui scilicet prioris existentiam secludet vel tollet ; ac proinde affectus nec tolli nec coerceri potest, nisi per affectum contrarium et fortiorem. Q. E. D.\n\nCOROLLARIUM\n\nAffectus, quatenus ad Mentem refertur nec coerceri nec tolli potest, nisi per ideam Corporis affectionis contrariæ et fortioris affectione, qua patimur. Nam affectus, quo patimur, nec coerceri nec tolli potest nisi per affectum eodem fortiorem eique contrarium (per Prop. præced.) hoc est (per gener. Affect. Defin.), nisi per ideam Corporis affectionis fortioris et contrariæ affectioni, qua patimur.\n\nPROPOSITIO VIII\n\nCognitio boni et mali nihil aliud est, quam Lætitiæ vel Tristitiæ affectus, quatenus ejus sumus conscii.\n\nDEMONSTRATIO\n\nId bonum aut malum vocamus, quod nostro esse conservando prodest vel obest (per Defin. 1 et 2 hujus), hoc est (per Prop. 7 p. III), quod nostram agendi potentiam auget vel minuit, juvat vel coercet. Quatenus itaque (per Defin. Lætitiæ et Tristitiæ, quas vide in Schol. Prop. 11 p. III) rem aliquam nos Lætitia vel Tristitia afficere percipimus, eandem bonam aut malam vocamus ; atque adeo boni et mali cognitio nihil aliud est, quam Lætitiæ vel Tristitiæ idea, quæ ex ipso Lætitiæ vel Tristitiæ affectu necessario sequitur (per Prop. 22 p. II). At hæc idea eodem modo unita est affectui, ac Mens unita est Corpori (per Prop. 21 p. II) ; hoc est (ut in Schol. ejusdem Prop. ostensum), hæc idea ab ipso affectu, sive (per Gen. Affect. Defin.) ab idea Corporis affectionis, revera non distinguitur, nisi solo conceptu ; ergo hæc cognitio boni et mali nihil est aliud, quam ipse affectus, quatenus ejusdem sumus conscii. Q. E. D.\n\nPROPOSITIO IX\n\nAffectus, cujus causam in præsenti nobis adesse imaginamur, fortior est, quam si eandem non adesse imaginaremur.\n\nDEMONSTRATIO\n\nImaginatio est idea, qua Mens rem ut præsentem contemplatur (vide ejus Defin. in Schol. Pr. 17 p. II), quæ tamen magis Corporis humani constitutionem, quam rei externæ naturam indicat (per Coroll. 2 Prop. 16 p. II). Est igitur affectus (per gen. Affect. Defin.) imaginatio, quatenus corporis constitutionem indicat. At imaginatio (per Prop. 17 p. II) intensior est, quamdiu nihil imaginamur, quod rei externæ præsentem existentiam secludit ; ergo etiam affectus, cujus causam in præsenti nobis adesse imaginamur, intensior seu fortior est, quam si eandem non adesse imaginaremur. Q. E. D.\n\nSCHOLIUM\n\nCum supra in Propositione 18 partis III dixerim, nos ex rei futuræ vel præteritæ imagine eodem affectu affici, ac si res, quam imaginamur præsens esset, expresse monui, id verum esse, quatenus ad solam ipsius rei imaginem attendimus ; est enim ejusdem naturæ, sive res præsentes imaginati simus sive non simus : sed non negavi, eandem debiliorem reddi, quando alias res nobis præsentes contemplamur quæ rei futuræ præsentem existentiam secludunt ; quod tum monere neglexi, quia in hac Parte de affectuum viribus agere constitueram.\n\nCOROLLARIUM\n\nImago rei futuræ vel præteritæ, hoc est rei, quam cum relatione ad tempus futurum vel præteritum secluso præsenti contemplamur, cæteris paribus, debilior est imagine rei præsentis ; et consequenter affectus erga rem futuram vel præteritam, cæteris paribus, remissior est affectu erga rem præsentem.\n\nPROPOSITIO X\n\nErga rem futuram, quam cito affuturam imaginamur, intensius afficimur, quam si ejus existendi tempus longius a præsenti distare imaginaremur ; et memoria rei, quam non diu præteriisse imaginamur, intensius etiam afficimur, quam si eandem diu præteriisse imaginaremur.\n\nDEMONSTRATIO\n\nQuatenus enim rem cito affuturam, vel non diu præteriisse imaginamur, eo ipso aliquid imaginamur, quod rei præsentiam minus secludit, quam si ejusdem futurum existendi tempus longius a præsenti distare, vel quod dudum præterierit, imaginaremur (ut per se notum) ; adeoque (per præced. Prop.) eatenus intensius erga eandem afficiemur. Q. E. D.\n\nSCHOLIUM\n\nEx iis, quæ ad Definitionem 6 hujus Partis notavimus, sequitur, nos erga objecta, quæ a præsenti longiore temporis intervallo distant, quam quod imaginando determinare possumus, quamvis ab invicem longo temporis intervallo distare intelligamus, æque tamen remisse affici.Q. E. D.\n\nPROPOSITIO XI\n\nAffectus erga rem, quam ut necessariam imaginamur, cæteris paribus, intensior est, quam erga possibilem vel contingentem, sive non necessariam.\n\nDEMONSTRATIO\n\nQuatenus rem aliquam necessariam esse imaginamur, eatenus ejus existentiam affirmamus, et contra rei existentiam negamus, quatenus eandem non necessariam esse imaginamur (per Schol. 1 Prop. 33 p. I) ; ac proinde (per Prop. 9 hujus) affectus erga rem necessariam, cæteris paribus, intensior est, quam erga non necessariam. Q. E. D.\n\nPROPOSITIO XII\n\nAffectus erga rem, quam scimus in præsenti non existere, et quam ut possibilem imaginamur, cæteris paribus, intensior est, quam erga contingentem.\n\nDEMONSTRATIO\n\nQuatenus rem ut contingentem imaginamur, nulla alterius rei imagine afficimur, quæ rei existentiam ponat (per Defin. 3 hujus) : sed contra (secundum hypothesin) quædam imaginamur, quæ ejusdem præsentem existentiam secludunt. At quatenus rem in futurum possibilem esse imaginamur, eatenus quædam imaginamur, quæ ejusdem existentiam ponunt (per Defin. 4 hujus), hoc est (per Prop. 18 p. III), quæ Spem vel Metum fovent ; atque adeo affectus erga rem possibilem vehementior est. Q. E. D.\n\nCOROLLARIUM\n\nAffectus erga rem, quam scimus in præsenti non existere, et quam ut contingentem imaginamur, multo remissior est, quam si rem in præsenti nobis adesse imaginaremur.\n\nDEMONSTRATIO\n\nAffectus erga rem, quam in præsenti existere imaginamur, intensior est, quam si eandem ut futuram imaginaremur (per Coroll. Prop. 9 hujus), et multo vehementior est, si tempus futurum a præsenti multum distare imaginaremur (per Prop. 10 hujus). Est itaque affectus erga rem, cujus existendi tempus longe a præsenti distare imaginamur, multo remissior, quam si eandem ut præsentem imaginaremur ; et nihilominus (per Prop. præced.) intensior est, quam si eandem rem ut contingentem imaginaremur ; atque adeo affectus erga rem contingentem multo remissior erit, quam si rem in præsenti nobis adesse imaginaremur. Q. E. D.\n\nPROPOSITIO XIII\n\nAffectus erga rem contingentem, quam scimus in præsenti non existere, cæteris paribus, remissior est, quam affectus erga rem præteritam.\n\nDEMONSTRATIO\n\nQuatenus rem ut contingentem imaginamur, nulla alterius rei imagine afficimur, quæ rei existentiam ponat (per Defin. 3 hujus), sed contra (secundum hypothesin) quædam imaginamur, quæ ejusdem præsentem existentiam secludunt. Verum quatenus eandem cum relatione ad tempus præteritum imaginamur, eatenus aliquid imaginari supponimur, quod ipsam ad memoriam redigit, sive quod rei imaginem excitat (vide Prop. 18 p. II cum ejusdem Schol.) ac proinde eatenus efficit, ut ipsam, ac si præsens esset, contemplemur (per Coroll. Prop. 17 p. II). Atque adeo (per Prop. 9 hujus) affectus erga rem contingentem, quam scimus in præsenti non existere, cæteris paribus, remissior erit, quam affectus erga rem præteritam. Q. E. D.\n\nPROPOSITIO XIV\n\nVera boni et mali cognitio, quatenus vera, nullum affectum coercere potest, sed tantum ut affectus consideratur.\n\nDEMONSTRATIO\n\nAffectus est idea, qua Mens majorem vel minorem sui Corporis existendi vim, quam antea, affirmat (per gen. Aff. Defin.) ; atque adeo (per Prop. 1 hujus) nihil positivum habet, quod præsentia veri tolli possit ; et consequenter vera boni et mali cognitio, quatenus vera, nullum affectum coercere potest. At quatenus affectus est (vide Prop. 8 hujus), si fortior affectu coercendo sit, eatenus tantum (per Prop. 7 hujus) affectum coercere poterit. Q. E. D.\n\nPROPOSITIO XV","paragraphs":[{"id":"RAW_QGOH_PQDS:DISPLAY-0001","text":"## ETHICES","kind":"display"},{"id":"RAW_QGOH_PQDS:DISPLAY-0002","text":"## PARS QUARTA","kind":"display"},{"id":"RAW_QGOH_PQDS:DISPLAY-0003","text":"## DE SERVITUTE HUMANA","kind":"display"},{"id":"RAW_QGOH_PQDS:DISPLAY-0004","text":"## SEU DE AFFECTUUM VIRIBUS","kind":"display"},{"id":"RAW_QGOH_PQDS:DISPLAY-0005","text":"PRÆFATiO","kind":"display"},{"id":"RAW_QGOH_PQDS:DISPLAY-0006","text":"Humanam impotentiam in moderandis et coercendis affectibus Servitutem voco ; homo enim affectibus obnoxius sui juris non est, sed fortunæ ; in cujus potestate ila est, ut sæpe coactus sit, quanquam meliora sibi videat, deteriora tamen sequi. Hujus rei causam, et quid præterea affectus boni vel mali habent, in hac Parte demonstrare proposui. Sed antequam incipiam, pauca de perfectione et imperfectione, deque bono et malo præfari lubet.","kind":"display"},{"id":"RAW_QGOH_PQDS:DISPLAY-0007","text":"Qui rem aliquam facere constituit, eamque perfecit, rem suam perfectam esse, non tantum ipse, sed etiam unusquisque, qui mentem Auctoris illius operis et scopum recte noverit, aut se novisse crediderit, dicet. Ex. gr. si quis aliquod opus (quod suppono nondum esse peractum) viderit, noveritque scopum Auctoris illius operis esse domum ædificare, is domum imperfectam esse dicet, et contra perfectam, simulatque opus ad finem, quem hujus Auctor eidem dare constituerat, perductum viderit. Verum si quis opus aliquod videt, cujus simile nunquam viderat, nec mentem opificis novit, is sane scire non poterit, opusne illud perfectum an imperfectum sit. Atque hæc videtur prima fuisse horum vocabulorum significatio. Sed postquam homines ideas universales formare, et domuum, ædificiorum, turrium, etc. exemplaria excogitare, et alia rerum exemplaria aliis præferre inceperunt, factum est, ut unusquisque id perfectum vocaret, quod cum universali idea, quam ejusmodi rei formaverat, videret convenire, et id contra imperfectum, quod cum concepto suo exemplari minus convenire videret, quanquam ex opificis sententia consummatum plane esset. Nec alia videtur esse ratio, cur res naturales etiam, quæ scilicet humana manu non sunt factæ, perfectas aut imperfectas vulgo appellent ; solent namque homines tam rerum naturalium quam artificialium ideas formare universales, quas rerum veluti exemplaria habent, et quas Naturam (quam nihil nisi alicujus finis causa agere existimant) intueri credunt sibique exemplaria proponere. Cum itaque aliquid in Natura fieri vident, quod cum concepto exemplari, quod rei ejusmodi habent, minus convenit, ipsam Naturam tum defecisse vel peccavisse remque illam imperfectam reliquisse credunt. Videmus itaque homines consuevisse, res naturales perfectas aut imperfectas vocare, magis ex præjudicio quam ex earum vera cognitione. Ostendimus enim in Primæ Partis Appendice, Naturam propter finem non agere ; æternum namque illud et infinitum Ens, quod Deum seu. Naturam appellamus eadem, qua existit, necessitate agit. Ex qua enim naturæ necessitate existit, ex eadem ipsum ageire ostendimus (Prop. 16 p. I). Ratio igitur, seu causa, cur Deus seu Natura agit, et cur existit, una eademque est. Ut ergo nullius finis causa existit, nullius etiam finis causa agit ; sed ut existendi, sic et agendi principium, vel finem, habet nullum. Causa autem, quæ finalis dicitur, nihil est præter ipsum humanum appetitum, quatenus is alicujus rei veluti principium seu causa primaria consideratur. Ex. gr. cum dicimus habitationem causam fuisse finalem hujus aut illius domus, nihil tum sane intelligimus aliud, quam quod homo ex eo, quod vitæ domesticæ commoda imaginatus est, appetitum habuit ædificandi domum. Quare habitatio, quatenus ut finalis causa consideratur, nihil est præter hunc singularem appetitum, qui revera causa est efficiens ; quæ ut prima consideratur, quia homines suorum appetituum causas communiter ignorant. Sunt namque, ut jam sæpe dixi, suarum quidem actionum ut appetituum conscii, sed ignari causarum, a quibus ad aliquid appetendum determinantur. Quod præterea vulgo ajunt, Naturam aliquando deficere vel peccare, resque interea imperfectas producere, inter commenta numero, de quibus in Appendice Partis Primæ egi. Perfectio igitur et imperfectio revera modi solummodo cogitandi sunt, nempe notiones, quas fingere solemus ex eo, quod ejusdem speciei aut generis individua ad invicem comparamus : et hac de causa supra (Defin. 6 p. II) dixi, me per realitatem et perfectionem idem intelligere. Solemus enim omnia Naturæ individua ad unum genus, quod generalissimum appellatur, revocare ; nempe ad notionem Entis, quæ ad omnia absolute Naturæ individua pertinet. Quatenus itaque Naturæ individua ad hoc genus revocamus, et ad invicem comparamus, et alia plus entitatis seu realitatis quam alia habere comperimus, eatenus alia aliis perfectiora esse dicimus ; et quatenus iisdem aliquid tribuimus, quod negationem involvit, ut terminus, finis, impotentia, etc., eatenus ipsa imperfecta appellamus, quia nostram Mentem non æque afficiunt ac illa, quæ perfecta vocamus, et non quod ipsis aliquid, quod suum sit, deficiat, vel quod Natura peccaverit. Nihil enim naturæ alicujus rei competit, nisi id, quod ex necessitate naturæ causæ efficientis sequitur, et quicquid ex necessitate naturæ causæ efficientis sequitur, id necessario fit.","kind":"display"},{"id":"RAW_QGOH_PQDS:DISPLAY-0008","text":"Bonum et malum quod attinet, nihil etiam positivum in rebus, in se scilicet consideratis, indicant, nec aliud sunt præter cogitandi modos, seu notiones, quas formamus ex eo, quod res ad invicem comparamus. Nam una eademque res potest eodem tempore bona et mala, et etiam indifferens esse. Ex. gr. musica bona est Melancholico, mala Lugenti ; Surdo autem neque bona neque mala. Verum, quamvis se res ita habeat, nobis tamen hæc vocabula retinenda sunt. Nam quia ideam hominis, tanquam naturæ humanæ exemplar, quod intueamur formare cupimus, nobis ex usu erit, hæc eadem vocabula eo, quo dixi, sensu retinere. Per bonum itaque in seqq. intelligam id, quod certo scimus medium esse, ut ad exemplar humanæ naturæ, quod nobis proponimus, magis magisque accedamus. Per malum autem id, quod certo scimus impedire, quo minus idem exemplar referamus. Deinde homines perfectiores aut imperfectiores dicemus, quatenus ad hoc idem exemplar magis aut minus accedunt. Nam apprime notandum est, cum dico, aliquem a minore ad majorem perfectionem transire, et contra, me non intelligere, quod ex una essentia seu forma in aliam mutatur ; Equus namque ex. gr. tam destruitur, si in hominem, quam si in insectum mutetur ; sed quod ejus agendi potentiam, quatenus hæc per ipsius naturam intelligitur, augeri vel minui concipimus. Denique per perfectionem in genere realitatem, uti dixi, intelligam ; hoc est, rei cujuscunque essentiam, quatenus certo modo existit et operatur, nulla ipsius durationis habita ratione. Nam nulla res singularis potest ideo dici perfectior, quia plus temporis in existendo perseveravit ; quippe rerum duratio ex earum essentia determinari nequit ; quandoquidem rerum essentia nullum certum et determinatum existendi tempus involvit ; sed res quæcunque, sive ea perfectior sit, sive minus, eadem vi, qua existere incipit, semper in existendo perseverare poterit, ita ut omnes hac in re æquales sint.","kind":"display"},{"id":"RAW_QGOH_PQDS:DISPLAY-0009","text":"DEFINITIONES","kind":"display"},{"id":"RAW_QGOH_PQDS:DISPLAY-0010","text":"I. Per bonum id intelligam, quod certo scimus nobis esse utile.","kind":"display"},{"id":"RAW_QGOH_PQDS:DISPLAY-0011","text":"II. Per malum autem id, quod certo scimus impedire, quo minus boni alicujus simus compotes.","kind":"display"},{"id":"RAW_QGOH_PQDS:DISPLAY-0012","text":"(De his præcedentem vide præfationem sub finem.)","kind":"display"},{"id":"RAW_QGOH_PQDS:DISPLAY-0013","text":"III. Res singulares voco contingentes, quatenus dum ad earum solam essentiam attendimus, nihil invenimus, quod earum existentiam necessario ponat, vel quod ipsam necessario secludat.","kind":"display"},{"id":"RAW_QGOH_PQDS:DISPLAY-0014","text":"IV. Easdem res singulares voco possibiles, quatenus, dum ad causas, ex quibus produci debent, attendimus, nescimus, an ipsæ determinatæ sint ad easdem producendum.","kind":"display"},{"id":"RAW_QGOH_PQDS:DISPLAY-0015","text":"(In Schol. 1 Prop. 33 p. I inter possibile et contingens nullam feci differentiam, quia ibi non opus erat hæc accurate distinguere.)","kind":"display"},{"id":"RAW_QGOH_PQDS:DISPLAY-0016","text":"V. Per contrarios affectus in seqq. intelligam eos, qui hominem diversum trahunt, quamvis ejusdem sint generis, ut luxuries et avaritia, quæ amoris sunt species ; nec natura, sed per accidens sunt contrarii.","kind":"display"},{"id":"RAW_QGOH_PQDS:DISPLAY-0017","text":"VI. Quid per affectum erga rem futuram, præsentem, et præteritam, intelligam, explicui in Schol. 1 et 2 Prop. 18 p. III, quod vide.","kind":"display"},{"id":"RAW_QGOH_PQDS:DISPLAY-0018","text":"(Sed venit hic præterea notandum, quod ut loci, sic etiam temporis distantiam non nisi usque ad certum quendam limitem possumus distincte imaginari ; hoc est, sicut omnia illa objecta, quæ ultra ducentos pedes a nobis distant, seu quorum distantia a loco, in quo sumus, illam superat, quam distincte imaginamur, æque longe a nobis distare, et perinde, ac si in eodem plano essent, imaginari solemus ; sic etiam objecta, quorum existendi tempus longiore a præsenti intervallo abesse imaginamur, quam quod distincte imaginari solemus, omnia æque longe a præsenti distare imaginamur, et ad unum quasi temporis momentum referimus.)","kind":"display"},{"id":"RAW_QGOH_PQDS:DISPLAY-0019","text":"VII. Per finem, cujus causa aliquid facimus, appetitum intelligo.","kind":"display"},{"id":"RAW_QGOH_PQDS:DISPLAY-0020","text":"VIII. Per virtutem et potentiam idem intelligo ; hoc est (per Prop. 7 p. III), virtus, quatenus ad hominem refertur, est ipsa hominis essentia seu natura, quatenus potestatem habet quædam efficiendi, quæ per solas ipsius naturæ leges possunt intelligi.","kind":"display"},{"id":"RAW_QGOH_PQDS:DISPLAY-0021","text":"AXIOMA","kind":"display"},{"id":"RAW_QGOH_PQDS:DISPLAY-0022","text":"Nulla res singularis in rerum Natura datur, qua potentior et fortior non detur alia. Sed quacunque data datur alia potentior, a qua illa data potest destrui.","kind":"display"},{"id":"RAW_QGOH_PQDS:DISPLAY-0023","text":"PROPOSITIO I","kind":"display"},{"id":"RAW_QGOH_PQDS:DISPLAY-0024","text":"Nihil, quod idea falsa positivum habet, tollitur præsentia veri, quatenus verum.","kind":"display"},{"id":"RAW_QGOH_PQDS:DISPLAY-0025","text":"DEMONSTRATIO","kind":"display"},{"id":"RAW_QGOH_PQDS:DISPLAY-0026","text":"Falsitas in sola privatione cognitionis, quam ideæ inadæquatæ involvunt, consistit (per Prop. 35 p. II) nec ipsæ aliquid habent positivum, propter quod falsæ dicuntur (per Prop. 33 p. II) ; sed contra, quatenus ad Deum referuntur, veræ sunt (per Prop. 32 p. II). Si igitur id, quod idea falsa positivum habet, præsentia veri, quatenus verum est, tolleretur, tolleretur ergo idea vera a se ipsa, quod (per Prop. 4 p. III) est absurdum. Ergo nihil quod idea etc. Q. E. D.","kind":"display"},{"id":"RAW_QGOH_PQDS:DISPLAY-0027","text":"SCHOLIUM","kind":"display"},{"id":"RAW_QGOH_PQDS:DISPLAY-0028","text":"Intelligitur hæc Propositio clarius ex 2 Coroll. Prop. 16 p. II. Nam imaginatio idea est, quæ magis Corporis humani præsentem constitutionem, quam corporis externi naturam indicat ; non quidem distincte, sed confuse ; unde fit, ut Mens errare dicatur. Ex. gr. cum solem intuemur, eundem ducentos circiter pedes a nobis distare imaginamur ; in quo tamdiu fallimur, quamdiu veram ejus distantiam ignoramus ; sed cognita ejusdem distantia tollitur quidem error, sed non imaginatio, hoc est idea solis, quæ ejusdem naturam eatenus tantum explicat, quatenus Corpus ab eodem afficitur ; adeoque, quamvis veram ejusdem distantiam noscamus, ipsum nihilominus prope nobis adesse imaginabimur. Nam ut in Schol. Prop. 35 p. II diximus, non ea de causa solem adeo propinquum imaginamur, quia ejus veram distantiam ignoramus ; sed quia Mens eatenus magnitudinem solis concipit, quatenus Corpus ab eodem afficitur. Sic cum solis radii, aquæ superficiei incidentes, ad nostros oculos reflectuntur, eundem perinde, ac si in aqua esset imaginamur, tametsi verum ejus locum noverimus ; et sic reliquæ imaginationes, quibus Mens fallitur, sive eæ naturalem Corporis constitutionem, sive quod ejusdem agendi potentiam augeri vel minui indicant, vero non sunt contrariæ, nec ejusdem præsentia evanescunt. Fit quidem, cum falso aliquod malum timemus, ut timor evanescat audito vero nuntio ; sed contra etiam fit, cum malum, quod certe venturum est, timemus, ut timor etiam evanescat audito falso nuntio ; atque adeo imaginationes non præsentia veri, quatenus verum, evanescunt, sed quia aliæ occurrunt iis fortiores, quæ rerum, quas imaginamur, præsentem existentiam secludunt ut Prop. 17 p. II ostendimus.","kind":"display"},{"id":"RAW_QGOH_PQDS:DISPLAY-0029","text":"PROPOSITIO II","kind":"display"},{"id":"RAW_QGOH_PQDS:DISPLAY-0030","text":"Nos eatenus patimur, quatenus Naturæ sumus pars, quæ per se absque aliis non potest concipi.","kind":"display"},{"id":"RAW_QGOH_PQDS:DISPLAY-0031","text":"DEMONSTRATIO","kind":"display"},{"id":"RAW_QGOH_PQDS:DISPLAY-0032","text":"Nos tum pati dicimur, cum aliquid in nobis oritur, cujus non nisi partialis sumus causa (per Defin. 2 p. III) hoc est (per Defin. 1 p. III) aliquid, quod ex solis legibus nostræ naturæ deduci nequit. Patimur igitur, quatenus Naturæ sumus pars, quæ per se absque aliis nequit concipi. Q. E. D.","kind":"display"},{"id":"RAW_QGOH_PQDS:DISPLAY-0033","text":"PROPOSITIO III","kind":"display"},{"id":"RAW_QGOH_PQDS:DISPLAY-0034","text":"Vis, qua homo in existendo perseverat, limitata est, et a potentia causarum externarum infinite superatur.","kind":"display"},{"id":"RAW_QGOH_PQDS:DISPLAY-0035","text":"DEMONSTRATIO","kind":"display"},{"id":"RAW_QGOH_PQDS:DISPLAY-0036","text":"Patet ex Axiomate hujus. Nam dato homine datur aliquid aliud, puta A, potentius, et dato A datur deinde aliud, puta B, ipso A potentius, et hoc in infinitum ; ac proinde potentia hominis potentia alterius rei definitur, et a potentia causarum externarum infinite superatur. Q. E. D.","kind":"display"},{"id":"RAW_QGOH_PQDS:DISPLAY-0037","text":"PROPOSITIO IV","kind":"display"},{"id":"RAW_QGOH_PQDS:DISPLAY-0038","text":"Fieri non potest, ut homo non sit Naturæ pars, et ut nullas possit pati mutationes, nisi quæ per solam suam naturam possint intelligi, quarumque adæquata sit causa.","kind":"display"},{"id":"RAW_QGOH_PQDS:DISPLAY-0039","text":"DEMONSTRATIO","kind":"display"},{"id":"RAW_QGOH_PQDS:DISPLAY-0040","text":"Potentia, qua res singulares, et consequenter homo suum esse conservat, est ipsa Dei sive Naturæ potentia (per Coroll. Prop. 24 p. I), non quatenus infinita est, sed quatenus per humanam actualem essentiam explicari potest (per Prop. 7 p. III). Potentia itaque hominis, quatenus per ipsius actualem essentiam explicatur, pars est infinitæ Dei seu Naturæ potentiæ, hoc est (per Prop. 34 p. I) essentiæ ; Quod erat primum. Deinde si fieri posset, ut homo nullas posset pati mutationes, nisi quæ per solam ipsius hominis naturam possint intelligi, sequeretur (per Prop. 4 et 6 p. III), ut non posset perire, sed ut semper necessario existeret ; atque hoc sequi deberet ex causa, cujus potentia finita aut infinita sit ; nempe vel ex sola hominis potentia, qui scilicet potis esset, ut a se removeret reliquas mutationes, quæ a causis externis oriri possent ; vel infinita Naturæ potentia, a qua omnia singularia ita dirigerentur, ut homo nullas alias posset pati mutationes, nisi quæ ipsius conservationi inserviunt. At primum (per Prop. præc., cujus demonstratio universalis est, et ad omnes res singulares applicari potest) est absurdum. Ergo, si fieri posset, ut homo nullas pateretur mutationes, nisi quæ per solam ipsius hominis naturam possent intelligi, et consequenter (sicut jam ostendimus) ut semper necessario existeret ; id sequi deberet ex Dei infinita potentia, et consequenter (per Prop. 16 p. I) ex necessitate divinæ naturæ, quatenus alicujus hominis idea affectus consideratur, totius Naturæ ordo, quatenus ipsa sub Extensionis et Cogitationis attributis concipitur, deduci deberet ; atque adeo (per Prop. 21 p. I) sequeretur, ut homo esset infinitus, quod (per I part. hujus demonstrationis) est absurdum. Fieri itaque nequit, ut homo nullas alias patiatur mutationes, nisi quarum ipse adæquata sit causa. Q. E. D.","kind":"display"},{"id":"RAW_QGOH_PQDS:DISPLAY-0041","text":"COROLLARIUM","kind":"display"},{"id":"RAW_QGOH_PQDS:DISPLAY-0042","text":"Hinc sequitur, hominem necessario passionibus esse semper obnoxium, communemque Naturæ ordinem sequi et eidem parere, seseque eidem quantum rerum natura exigit accommodare.","kind":"display"},{"id":"RAW_QGOH_PQDS:DISPLAY-0043","text":"PROPOSITIO V","kind":"display"},{"id":"RAW_QGOH_PQDS:DISPLAY-0044","text":"Vis et incrementum cujuscunque passionis, ejusque in existendo perseverantia non definitur potentia, qua nos in existendo perseverare conamur, sed causæ externæ potentia cum nostra comparata.","kind":"display"},{"id":"RAW_QGOH_PQDS:DISPLAY-0045","text":"DEMONSTRATIO","kind":"display"},{"id":"RAW_QGOH_PQDS:DISPLAY-0046","text":"Passionis essentia non potest per solam nostram essentiam explicari (per Defin. 1 et 2 p. III), hoc est (per Prop. 7 p. III), passionis potentia definiri nequit potentia, qua in nostro esse perseverare conamur, sed (ut Prop. 16 p. II ostensum est) definiri necessario debet potentia causæ externæ cum nostra comparata. Q. E. D.","kind":"display"},{"id":"RAW_QGOH_PQDS:DISPLAY-0047","text":"PROPOSITIO VI","kind":"display"},{"id":"RAW_QGOH_PQDS:DISPLAY-0048","text":"Vis alicujus passionis, seu affectus, reliquas hominis actiones, seu potentiam, superare potest, ita ut affectus pertinaciter homini adhæreat.","kind":"display"},{"id":"RAW_QGOH_PQDS:DISPLAY-0049","text":"DEMONSTRATIO","kind":"display"},{"id":"RAW_QGOH_PQDS:DISPLAY-0050","text":"Vis et incrementum cujuscunque passionis, ejusque in existendo perseverantia, definitur potentia causæ externæ cum nostra comparata (per Prop. præced.) ; adeoque (per Prop. 3 hujus) hominis potentiam superare potest, etc. Q. E. D.","kind":"display"},{"id":"RAW_QGOH_PQDS:DISPLAY-0051","text":"PROPOSITIO VII","kind":"display"},{"id":"RAW_QGOH_PQDS:DISPLAY-0052","text":"Affectus coerceri nec tolli potest, nisi per affectum contrarium et fortiorem affectu coercendo.","kind":"display"},{"id":"RAW_QGOH_PQDS:DISPLAY-0053","text":"DEMONSTRATIO","kind":"display"},{"id":"RAW_QGOH_PQDS:DISPLAY-0054","text":"Affectus, quatenus ad Mentem refertur, est idea, qua Mens majorem vel minorem sui corporis existendi vim, quam antea, affirmat (per generalem Affectuum Definitionem, quæ reperitur sub finem Tertiæ Partis). Cum igitur Mens aliquo affectu conflictatur, Corpus afficitur simul affectione, qua ejus agendi potentia augetur vel minuitur. Porro hæc Corporis affectio (per Prop. 5 hujus) vim a sua causa accipit perseverandi in suo esse ; quæ proinde nec coerceri nec tolli potest, nisi a causa corporea (per Prop. 6 p. II) quæ corpus afficiat affectione illi contraria (per Prop. 5 p. III) et fortiore (per axioma hujus) ; atque adeo (per Prop. 12 p. II). Mens afficietur idea affectionis fortioris et contrariæ priori, hoc est (per general. Affectuum Defin.), Mens afficietur affectu fortiori et contrario priori, qui scilicet prioris existentiam secludet vel tollet ; ac proinde affectus nec tolli nec coerceri potest, nisi per affectum contrarium et fortiorem. Q. E. D.","kind":"display"},{"id":"RAW_QGOH_PQDS:DISPLAY-0055","text":"COROLLARIUM","kind":"display"},{"id":"RAW_QGOH_PQDS:DISPLAY-0056","text":"Affectus, quatenus ad Mentem refertur nec coerceri nec tolli potest, nisi per ideam Corporis affectionis contrariæ et fortioris affectione, qua patimur. Nam affectus, quo patimur, nec coerceri nec tolli potest nisi per affectum eodem fortiorem eique contrarium (per Prop. præced.) hoc est (per gener. Affect. Defin.), nisi per ideam Corporis affectionis fortioris et contrariæ affectioni, qua patimur.","kind":"display"},{"id":"RAW_QGOH_PQDS:DISPLAY-0057","text":"PROPOSITIO VIII","kind":"display"},{"id":"RAW_QGOH_PQDS:DISPLAY-0058","text":"Cognitio boni et mali nihil aliud est, quam Lætitiæ vel Tristitiæ affectus, quatenus ejus sumus conscii.","kind":"display"},{"id":"RAW_QGOH_PQDS:DISPLAY-0059","text":"DEMONSTRATIO","kind":"display"},{"id":"RAW_QGOH_PQDS:DISPLAY-0060","text":"Id bonum aut malum vocamus, quod nostro esse conservando prodest vel obest (per Defin. 1 et 2 hujus), hoc est (per Prop. 7 p. III), quod nostram agendi potentiam auget vel minuit, juvat vel coercet. Quatenus itaque (per Defin. Lætitiæ et Tristitiæ, quas vide in Schol. Prop. 11 p. III) rem aliquam nos Lætitia vel Tristitia afficere percipimus, eandem bonam aut malam vocamus ; atque adeo boni et mali cognitio nihil aliud est, quam Lætitiæ vel Tristitiæ idea, quæ ex ipso Lætitiæ vel Tristitiæ affectu necessario sequitur (per Prop. 22 p. II). At hæc idea eodem modo unita est affectui, ac Mens unita est Corpori (per Prop. 21 p. II) ; hoc est (ut in Schol. ejusdem Prop. ostensum), hæc idea ab ipso affectu, sive (per Gen. Affect. Defin.) ab idea Corporis affectionis, revera non distinguitur, nisi solo conceptu ; ergo hæc cognitio boni et mali nihil est aliud, quam ipse affectus, quatenus ejusdem sumus conscii. Q. E. D.","kind":"display"},{"id":"RAW_QGOH_PQDS:DISPLAY-0061","text":"PROPOSITIO IX","kind":"display"},{"id":"RAW_QGOH_PQDS:DISPLAY-0062","text":"Affectus, cujus causam in præsenti nobis adesse imaginamur, fortior est, quam si eandem non adesse imaginaremur.","kind":"display"},{"id":"RAW_QGOH_PQDS:DISPLAY-0063","text":"DEMONSTRATIO","kind":"display"},{"id":"RAW_QGOH_PQDS:DISPLAY-0064","text":"Imaginatio est idea, qua Mens rem ut præsentem contemplatur (vide ejus Defin. in Schol. Pr. 17 p. II), quæ tamen magis Corporis humani constitutionem, quam rei externæ naturam indicat (per Coroll. 2 Prop. 16 p. II). Est igitur affectus (per gen. Affect. Defin.) imaginatio, quatenus corporis constitutionem indicat. At imaginatio (per Prop. 17 p. II) intensior est, quamdiu nihil imaginamur, quod rei externæ præsentem existentiam secludit ; ergo etiam affectus, cujus causam in præsenti nobis adesse imaginamur, intensior seu fortior est, quam si eandem non adesse imaginaremur. Q. E. D.","kind":"display"},{"id":"RAW_QGOH_PQDS:DISPLAY-0065","text":"SCHOLIUM","kind":"display"},{"id":"RAW_QGOH_PQDS:DISPLAY-0066","text":"Cum supra in Propositione 18 partis III dixerim, nos ex rei futuræ vel præteritæ imagine eodem affectu affici, ac si res, quam imaginamur præsens esset, expresse monui, id verum esse, quatenus ad solam ipsius rei imaginem attendimus ; est enim ejusdem naturæ, sive res præsentes imaginati simus sive non simus : sed non negavi, eandem debiliorem reddi, quando alias res nobis præsentes contemplamur quæ rei futuræ præsentem existentiam secludunt ; quod tum monere neglexi, quia in hac Parte de affectuum viribus agere constitueram.","kind":"display"},{"id":"RAW_QGOH_PQDS:DISPLAY-0067","text":"COROLLARIUM","kind":"display"},{"id":"RAW_QGOH_PQDS:DISPLAY-0068","text":"Imago rei futuræ vel præteritæ, hoc est rei, quam cum relatione ad tempus futurum vel præteritum secluso præsenti contemplamur, cæteris paribus, debilior est imagine rei præsentis ; et consequenter affectus erga rem futuram vel præteritam, cæteris paribus, remissior est affectu erga rem præsentem.","kind":"display"},{"id":"RAW_QGOH_PQDS:DISPLAY-0069","text":"PROPOSITIO X","kind":"display"},{"id":"RAW_QGOH_PQDS:DISPLAY-0070","text":"Erga rem futuram, quam cito affuturam imaginamur, intensius afficimur, quam si ejus existendi tempus longius a præsenti distare imaginaremur ; et memoria rei, quam non diu præteriisse imaginamur, intensius etiam afficimur, quam si eandem diu præteriisse imaginaremur.","kind":"display"},{"id":"RAW_QGOH_PQDS:DISPLAY-0071","text":"DEMONSTRATIO","kind":"display"},{"id":"RAW_QGOH_PQDS:DISPLAY-0072","text":"Quatenus enim rem cito affuturam, vel non diu præteriisse imaginamur, eo ipso aliquid imaginamur, quod rei præsentiam minus secludit, quam si ejusdem futurum existendi tempus longius a præsenti distare, vel quod dudum præterierit, imaginaremur (ut per se notum) ; adeoque (per præced. Prop.) eatenus intensius erga eandem afficiemur. Q. E. D.","kind":"display"},{"id":"RAW_QGOH_PQDS:DISPLAY-0073","text":"SCHOLIUM","kind":"display"},{"id":"RAW_QGOH_PQDS:DISPLAY-0074","text":"Ex iis, quæ ad Definitionem 6 hujus Partis notavimus, sequitur, nos erga objecta, quæ a præsenti longiore temporis intervallo distant, quam quod imaginando determinare possumus, quamvis ab invicem longo temporis intervallo distare intelligamus, æque tamen remisse affici.Q. E. D.","kind":"display"},{"id":"RAW_QGOH_PQDS:DISPLAY-0075","text":"PROPOSITIO XI","kind":"display"},{"id":"RAW_QGOH_PQDS:DISPLAY-0076","text":"Affectus erga rem, quam ut necessariam imaginamur, cæteris paribus, intensior est, quam erga possibilem vel contingentem, sive non necessariam.","kind":"display"},{"id":"RAW_QGOH_PQDS:DISPLAY-0077","text":"DEMONSTRATIO","kind":"display"},{"id":"RAW_QGOH_PQDS:DISPLAY-0078","text":"Quatenus rem aliquam necessariam esse imaginamur, eatenus ejus existentiam affirmamus, et contra rei existentiam negamus, quatenus eandem non necessariam esse imaginamur (per Schol. 1 Prop. 33 p. I) ; ac proinde (per Prop. 9 hujus) affectus erga rem necessariam, cæteris paribus, intensior est, quam erga non necessariam. Q. E. D.","kind":"display"},{"id":"RAW_QGOH_PQDS:DISPLAY-0079","text":"PROPOSITIO XII","kind":"display"},{"id":"RAW_QGOH_PQDS:DISPLAY-0080","text":"Affectus erga rem, quam scimus in præsenti non existere, et quam ut possibilem imaginamur, cæteris paribus, intensior est, quam erga contingentem.","kind":"display"},{"id":"RAW_QGOH_PQDS:DISPLAY-0081","text":"DEMONSTRATIO","kind":"display"},{"id":"RAW_QGOH_PQDS:DISPLAY-0082","text":"Quatenus rem ut contingentem imaginamur, nulla alterius rei imagine afficimur, quæ rei existentiam ponat (per Defin. 3 hujus) : sed contra (secundum hypothesin) quædam imaginamur, quæ ejusdem præsentem existentiam secludunt. At quatenus rem in futurum possibilem esse imaginamur, eatenus quædam imaginamur, quæ ejusdem existentiam ponunt (per Defin. 4 hujus), hoc est (per Prop. 18 p. III), quæ Spem vel Metum fovent ; atque adeo affectus erga rem possibilem vehementior est. Q. E. D.","kind":"display"},{"id":"RAW_QGOH_PQDS:DISPLAY-0083","text":"COROLLARIUM","kind":"display"},{"id":"RAW_QGOH_PQDS:DISPLAY-0084","text":"Affectus erga rem, quam scimus in præsenti non existere, et quam ut contingentem imaginamur, multo remissior est, quam si rem in præsenti nobis adesse imaginaremur.","kind":"display"},{"id":"RAW_QGOH_PQDS:DISPLAY-0085","text":"DEMONSTRATIO","kind":"display"},{"id":"RAW_QGOH_PQDS:DISPLAY-0086","text":"Affectus erga rem, quam in præsenti existere imaginamur, intensior est, quam si eandem ut futuram imaginaremur (per Coroll. Prop. 9 hujus), et multo vehementior est, si tempus futurum a præsenti multum distare imaginaremur (per Prop. 10 hujus). Est itaque affectus erga rem, cujus existendi tempus longe a præsenti distare imaginamur, multo remissior, quam si eandem ut præsentem imaginaremur ; et nihilominus (per Prop. præced.) intensior est, quam si eandem rem ut contingentem imaginaremur ; atque adeo affectus erga rem contingentem multo remissior erit, quam si rem in præsenti nobis adesse imaginaremur. Q. E. D.","kind":"display"},{"id":"RAW_QGOH_PQDS:DISPLAY-0087","text":"PROPOSITIO XIII","kind":"display"},{"id":"RAW_QGOH_PQDS:DISPLAY-0088","text":"Affectus erga rem contingentem, quam scimus in præsenti non existere, cæteris paribus, remissior est, quam affectus erga rem præteritam.","kind":"display"},{"id":"RAW_QGOH_PQDS:DISPLAY-0089","text":"DEMONSTRATIO","kind":"display"},{"id":"RAW_QGOH_PQDS:DISPLAY-0090","text":"Quatenus rem ut contingentem imaginamur, nulla alterius rei imagine afficimur, quæ rei existentiam ponat (per Defin. 3 hujus), sed contra (secundum hypothesin) quædam imaginamur, quæ ejusdem præsentem existentiam secludunt. Verum quatenus eandem cum relatione ad tempus præteritum imaginamur, eatenus aliquid imaginari supponimur, quod ipsam ad memoriam redigit, sive quod rei imaginem excitat (vide Prop. 18 p. II cum ejusdem Schol.) ac proinde eatenus efficit, ut ipsam, ac si præsens esset, contemplemur (per Coroll. Prop. 17 p. II). Atque adeo (per Prop. 9 hujus) affectus erga rem contingentem, quam scimus in præsenti non existere, cæteris paribus, remissior erit, quam affectus erga rem præteritam. Q. E. D.","kind":"display"},{"id":"RAW_QGOH_PQDS:DISPLAY-0091","text":"PROPOSITIO XIV","kind":"display"},{"id":"RAW_QGOH_PQDS:DISPLAY-0092","text":"Vera boni et mali cognitio, quatenus vera, nullum affectum coercere potest, sed tantum ut affectus consideratur.","kind":"display"},{"id":"RAW_QGOH_PQDS:DISPLAY-0093","text":"DEMONSTRATIO","kind":"display"},{"id":"RAW_QGOH_PQDS:DISPLAY-0094","text":"Affectus est idea, qua Mens majorem vel minorem sui Corporis existendi vim, quam antea, affirmat (per gen. Aff. Defin.) ; atque adeo (per Prop. 1 hujus) nihil positivum habet, quod præsentia veri tolli possit ; et consequenter vera boni et mali cognitio, quatenus vera, nullum affectum coercere potest. At quatenus affectus est (vide Prop. 8 hujus), si fortior affectu coercendo sit, eatenus tantum (per Prop. 7 hujus) affectum coercere poterit. Q. E. D.","kind":"display"},{"id":"RAW_QGOH_PQDS:DISPLAY-0095","text":"PROPOSITIO XV","kind":"display"}],"sha256":"2a460775553502bf371daf3c62715e3793e8c49150bd3b6df9de5cb3db6e9b23","language_code":"la","language_name":"Latina","translations":[{"id":"SOURCE_TR_20261015T013737282588Z_f957b028","language_code":"en","language_name":"English","title":"Part Four - On Human Bondage, or the Powers of the Affects · passage 1","source_sha256":"2a460775553502bf371daf3c62715e3793e8c49150bd3b6df9de5cb3db6e9b23","sha256":"b7ff01244ee443fa2fe6fd160984333e9536b5338239c18a4dcb1e5ae3c0f2eb","rendition_type":"machine","review_status":"machine","model":"gpt-5.6-terra","created":"2026-10-15T01:37:37.282721+00:00","paragraphs":[{"id":"RAW_QGOH_PQDS:DISPLAY-0001","text":"## ETHICS","kind":"display","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_QGOH_PQDS:DISPLAY-0001"]},{"id":"RAW_QGOH_PQDS:DISPLAY-0002","text":"## PART FOUR","kind":"display","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_QGOH_PQDS:DISPLAY-0002"]},{"id":"RAW_QGOH_PQDS:DISPLAY-0003","text":"## ON HUMAN BONDAGE","kind":"display","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_QGOH_PQDS:DISPLAY-0003"]},{"id":"RAW_QGOH_PQDS:DISPLAY-0004","text":"## OR ON THE POWERS OF THE AFFECTS","kind":"display","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_QGOH_PQDS:DISPLAY-0004"]},{"id":"RAW_QGOH_PQDS:DISPLAY-0005","text":"PREFACE","kind":"display","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_QGOH_PQDS:DISPLAY-0005"]},{"id":"RAW_QGOH_PQDS:DISPLAY-0006","text":"I call the human impotence in moderating and restraining the affects Bondage; for a man subject to affects is not his own master, but subject to fortune, in whose power he is to such an extent that he is often compelled, although he sees what is better for himself, nevertheless to follow what is worse. I have undertaken in this Part to demonstrate the cause of this, and also what good or evil the affects possess. But before I begin, I should like to say a few words beforehand concerning perfection and imperfection, and concerning good and evil.","kind":"display","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_QGOH_PQDS:DISPLAY-0006"]},{"id":"SOURCE_TR_20261015T013737282588Z_f957b028:DISPLAY-0007","text":"He who has resolved to make some thing, and has completed it, will call his thing perfect, not only himself, but also everyone who rightly knows, or believes himself to know, the mind and aim of the author of that work. For example, if someone sees some work (which I suppose has not yet been completed), and knows that the aim of the author of that work is to build a house, he will call the house imperfect; and conversely, perfect, as soon as he sees that the work has been brought to the end which its author had determined to give it. But if someone sees some work the like of which he has never seen, and does not know the mind of the craftsman, he surely will not be able to know whether that work is perfect or imperfect. And this seems to have been the first signification of these words. But after men began to form universal ideas, and to devise models of houses, buildings, towers, etc.,","kind":"display","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_QGOH_PQDS:DISPLAY-0007"]},{"id":"SOURCE_TR_20261015T013737282588Z_f957b028:DISPLAY-0008","text":"and to prefer certain models of things to others, it came about that each called perfect that which he saw to agree with the universal idea he had formed of a thing of that kind, and, conversely, imperfect that which he saw to agree less with his conceived model, although according to the craftsman's judgment it was entirely finished. Nor does there seem to be any other reason why natural things also, namely those not made by human hands, are commonly called perfect or imperfect; for men are accustomed to form universal ideas both of natural and artificial things, which they take as, so to speak, models of things, and which they believe Nature (which they suppose to act for the sake of no end other than some end) to behold and set before itself as models.","kind":"display","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_QGOH_PQDS:DISPLAY-0007"]},{"id":"SOURCE_TR_20261015T013737282588Z_f957b028:DISPLAY-0009","text":"Therefore, when they see something occur in Nature which agrees less with the conceived model they have of a thing of that kind, they believe that Nature itself has then failed or sinned, and has left that thing imperfect. We see, therefore, that men have become accustomed to call natural things perfect or imperfect more from prejudice than from true knowledge of them. For we showed in the Appendix to Part One that Nature does not act for the sake of an end; for that eternal and infinite Being which we call God, or Nature, acts by the same necessity by which it exists. For from the same necessity of nature by which it exists, we showed that it acts (Prop. 16 p. I). Therefore the reason, or cause, why God, or Nature, acts, and why it exists, is one and the same. Hence, just as it exists for the sake of no end, so neither does it act for the sake of any end; but, as it has no principle, or end, of existing, so it has none of acting.","kind":"display","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_QGOH_PQDS:DISPLAY-0007"]},{"id":"SOURCE_TR_20261015T013737282588Z_f957b028:DISPLAY-0010","text":"But the cause which is called final is nothing but human appetite itself, insofar as it is considered as, so to speak, the principle or primary cause of some thing. For example, when we say that habitation was the final cause of this or that house, we surely understand nothing else than that a man, because he imagined the conveniences of domestic life, had the appetite to build a house. Therefore habitation, insofar as it is considered as a final cause, is nothing but this singular appetite, which is in truth an efficient cause; it is considered as primary because men commonly are ignorant of the causes of their appetites. For, as I have often already said, they are conscious indeed of their actions and appetites, but ignorant of the causes by which they are determined to desire something. What they further commonly say, that Nature sometimes fails or sins and meanwhile produces imperfect things, I count among the fictions of which I treated in the Appendix to Part One.","kind":"display","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_QGOH_PQDS:DISPLAY-0007"]},{"id":"SOURCE_TR_20261015T013737282588Z_f957b028:DISPLAY-0011","text":"Therefore perfection and imperfection are in reality only modes of thinking, namely notions which we are accustomed to feign from the fact that we compare individuals of the same species or genus with one another; and for this reason I said above (Def. 6 p. II) that by reality and perfection I understand the same thing. For we are accustomed to reduce all individuals of Nature to one genus, which is called most general; namely, to the notion of Being, which pertains absolutely to all individual things of Nature.","kind":"display","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_QGOH_PQDS:DISPLAY-0007"]},{"id":"SOURCE_TR_20261015T013737282588Z_f957b028:DISPLAY-0012","text":"Therefore, insofar as we reduce the individuals of Nature to this genus and compare them with one another, and find that some have more being, or reality, than others, to that extent we say that some are more perfect than others; and insofar as we attribute to them something involving negation, such as limit, end, impotence, etc., to that extent we call them imperfect, because they do not affect our Mind equally with those which we call perfect, and not because something belonging to them is lacking to them, or because Nature has sinned. For nothing pertains to the nature of any thing except what follows from the necessity of the nature of its efficient cause, and whatever follows from the necessity of the nature of the efficient cause necessarily happens.","kind":"display","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_QGOH_PQDS:DISPLAY-0007"]},{"id":"SOURCE_TR_20261015T013737282588Z_f957b028:DISPLAY-0013","text":"As for good and evil, they likewise indicate nothing positive in things, considered in themselves, nor are they anything other than modes of thinking, or notions, which we form from comparing things with one another. For one and the same thing can at the same time be good and evil, and also indifferent. For example, music is good for the Melancholic, bad for one who mourns; but for the deaf it is neither good nor bad. Yet, although things stand thus, we must nevertheless retain these terms. For because we wish to form an idea of man, as a model of human nature, which we may behold, it will be useful to us to retain these same terms in the sense I have stated. Therefore, by good in what follows I shall understand that which we certainly know to be a means by which we may approach more and more closely the model of human nature which we set before ourselves. By evil, however, that which we certainly know to hinder our representing that same model.","kind":"display","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_QGOH_PQDS:DISPLAY-0008"]},{"id":"SOURCE_TR_20261015T013737282588Z_f957b028:DISPLAY-0014","text":"Next, we shall call men more perfect or less perfect insofar as they approach this same model more or less closely. For it must especially be noted that, when I say that someone passes from a lesser to a greater perfection, and conversely, I do not understand that he is changed from one essence, or form, into another; for a horse, for example, is as much destroyed if it is changed into a man as if it is changed into an insect; but that we conceive its power of acting, insofar as this is understood through its own nature, to be increased or diminished. Finally, by perfection in general I shall understand reality, as I have said; that is, the essence of any thing whatsoever, insofar as it exists and acts in a certain manner, no account being taken of its duration.","kind":"display","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_QGOH_PQDS:DISPLAY-0008"]},{"id":"SOURCE_TR_20261015T013737282588Z_f957b028:DISPLAY-0015","text":"For no singular thing can for that reason be called more perfect because it has persevered for a longer time in existing; since the duration of things cannot be determined from their essence, inasmuch as the essence of things involves no certain and determinate time of existing; but any thing whatsoever, whether it be more perfect or less so, will always be able to persevere in existing by the same force by which it begins to exist, so that all are equal in this respect.","kind":"display","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_QGOH_PQDS:DISPLAY-0008"]},{"id":"RAW_QGOH_PQDS:DISPLAY-0009","text":"DEFINITIONS","kind":"display","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_QGOH_PQDS:DISPLAY-0009"]},{"id":"RAW_QGOH_PQDS:DISPLAY-0010","text":"I. By good I shall understand that which we certainly know to be useful to us.","kind":"display","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_QGOH_PQDS:DISPLAY-0010"]},{"id":"RAW_QGOH_PQDS:DISPLAY-0011","text":"II. By evil, however, that which we certainly know hinders us from being able to attain some good.","kind":"display","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_QGOH_PQDS:DISPLAY-0011"]},{"id":"RAW_QGOH_PQDS:DISPLAY-0012","text":"(Concerning these, see the preceding Preface toward the end.)","kind":"display","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_QGOH_PQDS:DISPLAY-0012"]},{"id":"RAW_QGOH_PQDS:DISPLAY-0013","text":"III. I call singular things contingent insofar as, while we attend only to their essence, we find nothing that necessarily posits their existence or that necessarily excludes it.","kind":"display","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_QGOH_PQDS:DISPLAY-0013"]},{"id":"RAW_QGOH_PQDS:DISPLAY-0014","text":"IV. I call the same singular things possible insofar as, while we attend to the causes from which they ought to be produced, we do not know whether these causes are determined to produce them.","kind":"display","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_QGOH_PQDS:DISPLAY-0014"]},{"id":"RAW_QGOH_PQDS:DISPLAY-0015","text":"(In Schol. 1 Prop. 33 p. I I made no distinction between possible and contingent, because there it was not necessary to distinguish them accurately.)","kind":"display","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_QGOH_PQDS:DISPLAY-0015"]},{"id":"RAW_QGOH_PQDS:DISPLAY-0016","text":"V. By contrary affects in what follows I shall understand those which draw a man in different directions, although they are of the same genus, such as luxury and avarice, which are species of love; nor are they contrary by nature, but accidentally.","kind":"display","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_QGOH_PQDS:DISPLAY-0016"]},{"id":"RAW_QGOH_PQDS:DISPLAY-0017","text":"VI. What I understand by an affect toward a future, present, and past thing, I explained in Schol. 1 and 2 Prop. 18 p. III; see these.","kind":"display","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_QGOH_PQDS:DISPLAY-0017"]},{"id":"RAW_QGOH_PQDS:DISPLAY-0018","text":"(But here it must further be noted that, just as we can distinctly imagine distance of place only up to a certain limit, so also [we can distinctly imagine] distance of time only up to a certain limit; that is, just as we are accustomed to imagine all those objects which are more than two hundred feet distant from us, or whose distance from the place in which we are exceeds that which we distinctly imagine, as equally far distant from us, and as if they were in the same plane; so also we imagine objects whose time of existing we imagine to be separated from the present by a longer interval than that which we are accustomed distinctly to imagine, as all equally far distant from the present, and refer them, as it were, to one moment of time.)","kind":"display","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_QGOH_PQDS:DISPLAY-0018"]},{"id":"RAW_QGOH_PQDS:DISPLAY-0019","text":"VII. By the end for the sake of which we do something, I understand appetite.","kind":"display","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_QGOH_PQDS:DISPLAY-0019"]},{"id":"RAW_QGOH_PQDS:DISPLAY-0020","text":"VIII. By virtue and power I understand the same thing; that is (by Prop. 7 p. III), virtue, insofar as it is related to man, is man's very essence or nature, insofar as it has the power of bringing about certain things which can be understood through the laws of his nature alone.","kind":"display","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_QGOH_PQDS:DISPLAY-0020"]},{"id":"RAW_QGOH_PQDS:DISPLAY-0021","text":"AXIOM","kind":"display","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_QGOH_PQDS:DISPLAY-0021"]},{"id":"RAW_QGOH_PQDS:DISPLAY-0022","text":"No singular thing is given in the Nature of things than which another, more powerful and stronger, is not given. But given any thing, another more powerful is given, by which the given thing can be destroyed.","kind":"display","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_QGOH_PQDS:DISPLAY-0022"]},{"id":"RAW_QGOH_PQDS:DISPLAY-0023","text":"PROPOSITION I","kind":"display","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_QGOH_PQDS:DISPLAY-0023"]},{"id":"RAW_QGOH_PQDS:DISPLAY-0024","text":"Nothing that a false idea has positively is removed by the presence of the true, insofar as it is true.","kind":"display","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_QGOH_PQDS:DISPLAY-0024"]},{"id":"RAW_QGOH_PQDS:DISPLAY-0025","text":"DEMONSTRATION","kind":"display","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_QGOH_PQDS:DISPLAY-0025"]},{"id":"RAW_QGOH_PQDS:DISPLAY-0026","text":"Falsity consists in the privation of knowledge alone which inadequate ideas involve (by Prop. 35 p. II), nor do they themselves have anything positive on account of which they are called false (by Prop. 33 p. II); but, on the contrary, insofar as they are related to God, they are true (by Prop. 32 p. II). If, therefore, that which a false idea has positively were removed by the presence of the true, insofar as it is true, then a true idea would be removed by itself, which (by Prop. 4 p. III) is absurd. Therefore, nothing that an idea etc. Q. E. D.","kind":"display","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_QGOH_PQDS:DISPLAY-0026"]},{"id":"RAW_QGOH_PQDS:DISPLAY-0027","text":"SCHOLIUM","kind":"display","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_QGOH_PQDS:DISPLAY-0027"]},{"id":"SOURCE_TR_20261015T013737282588Z_f957b028:DISPLAY-0035","text":"This Proposition is understood more clearly from Coroll. 2 of Prop. 16 p. II. For imagination is an idea which indicates the present constitution of the human Body more than the nature of the external body; not indeed distinctly, but confusedly; whence it comes about that the Mind is said to err. For example, when we look at the sun, we imagine it to be about two hundred feet distant from us; in which we are deceived so long as we are ignorant of its true distance. But once its distance is known, the error is indeed removed, but not the imagination, that is, the idea of the sun, which explains its nature only insofar as the Body is affected by it; and therefore, although we know its true distance, we shall nevertheless imagine it to be near us. For, as we said in the Schol. of Prop. 35 p.","kind":"display","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_QGOH_PQDS:DISPLAY-0028"]},{"id":"SOURCE_TR_20261015T013737282588Z_f957b028:DISPLAY-0036","text":"II, we do not imagine the sun to be so close for the reason that we are ignorant of its true distance; rather, because the Mind conceives the magnitude of the sun insofar as the Body is affected by it. Thus, when the rays of the sun, falling upon the surface of water, are reflected to our eyes, we imagine it as if it were in the water, although we know its true place; and thus the other imaginations by which the Mind is deceived, whether they indicate the natural constitution of the Body or that its power of acting is increased or diminished, are not contrary to truth, nor do they vanish at its presence.","kind":"display","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_QGOH_PQDS:DISPLAY-0028"]},{"id":"SOURCE_TR_20261015T013737282588Z_f957b028:DISPLAY-0037","text":"Indeed, when we falsely fear some evil, it comes about that the fear vanishes upon hearing a true report; but, conversely, it also comes about, when we fear an evil that will certainly come, that the fear likewise vanishes upon hearing a false report; and therefore imaginations do not vanish through the presence of the true, insofar as it is true, but because other imaginations occur which are stronger than those and exclude the present existence of the things which we imagine, as we showed in Prop. 17 p. II.","kind":"display","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_QGOH_PQDS:DISPLAY-0028"]},{"id":"RAW_QGOH_PQDS:DISPLAY-0029","text":"PROPOSITION II","kind":"display","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_QGOH_PQDS:DISPLAY-0029"]},{"id":"RAW_QGOH_PQDS:DISPLAY-0030","text":"We suffer insofar as we are a part of Nature which cannot be conceived through itself without others.","kind":"display","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_QGOH_PQDS:DISPLAY-0030"]},{"id":"RAW_QGOH_PQDS:DISPLAY-0031","text":"DEMONSTRATION","kind":"display","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_QGOH_PQDS:DISPLAY-0031"]},{"id":"RAW_QGOH_PQDS:DISPLAY-0032","text":"We are said to suffer when something arises in us of which we are only a partial cause (by Defin. 2 p. III), that is (by Defin. 1 p. III), something which cannot be deduced from the laws of our nature alone. Therefore, we suffer insofar as we are a part of Nature which cannot be conceived through itself without others. Q. E. D.","kind":"display","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_QGOH_PQDS:DISPLAY-0032"]},{"id":"RAW_QGOH_PQDS:DISPLAY-0033","text":"PROPOSITION III","kind":"display","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_QGOH_PQDS:DISPLAY-0033"]},{"id":"RAW_QGOH_PQDS:DISPLAY-0034","text":"The force by which man perseveres in existing is limited, and is infinitely surpassed by the power of external causes.","kind":"display","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_QGOH_PQDS:DISPLAY-0034"]},{"id":"RAW_QGOH_PQDS:DISPLAY-0035","text":"DEMONSTRATION","kind":"display","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_QGOH_PQDS:DISPLAY-0035"]},{"id":"RAW_QGOH_PQDS:DISPLAY-0036","text":"This is evident from the Axiom of this. For, given a man, something else is given, say A, more powerful; and, given A, then something else is given, say B, more powerful than A itself, and so on to infinity; and consequently the power of man is determined by the power of another thing, and is infinitely surpassed by the power of external causes. Q. E. D.","kind":"display","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_QGOH_PQDS:DISPLAY-0036"]},{"id":"RAW_QGOH_PQDS:DISPLAY-0037","text":"PROPOSITION IV","kind":"display","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_QGOH_PQDS:DISPLAY-0037"]},{"id":"RAW_QGOH_PQDS:DISPLAY-0038","text":"It cannot come about that man is not a part of Nature, and that he can undergo no changes except those which can be understood through his nature alone, and of which he is the adequate cause.","kind":"display","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_QGOH_PQDS:DISPLAY-0038"]},{"id":"RAW_QGOH_PQDS:DISPLAY-0039","text":"DEMONSTRATION","kind":"display","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_QGOH_PQDS:DISPLAY-0039"]},{"id":"SOURCE_TR_20261015T013737282588Z_f957b028:DISPLAY-0049","text":"The power by which singular things, and consequently a human being, preserve their being, is the very power of God, or of Nature (by Coroll. Prop. 24, Part I), not insofar as it is infinite, but insofar as it can be explicated through the actual essence of the human being (by Prop. 7, Part III). Therefore the power of a human being, insofar as it is explicated through that person's actual essence, is a part of the infinite power of God, or of Nature, that is (by Prop. 34, Part I), of [his] essence; which was the first point. Next, if it could come about that a human being could undergo no changes except those which could be understood through the nature of the human being alone, it would follow (by Props. 4 and 6, Part","kind":"display","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_QGOH_PQDS:DISPLAY-0040"]},{"id":"SOURCE_TR_20261015T013737282588Z_f957b028:DISPLAY-0050","text":"III) that he could not perish, but that he would necessarily always exist; and this would have to follow from a cause whose power is finite or infinite—namely, either from the power of the human being alone, who would of course be able to remove from himself the remaining changes which could arise from external causes; or from the infinite power of Nature, by which all singular things would be so directed that a human being could undergo no changes other than those that serve his preservation. But the first (by the preceding Prop., whose demonstration is universal and can be applied to all singular things) is absurd. Therefore, if it could come about that a human being suffered no changes except those which could be understood through the nature of the human being alone, and consequently (as we have now shown) that he necessarily always existed, this would have to follow from the infinite power of God, and consequently (by Prop. 16, Part","kind":"display","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_QGOH_PQDS:DISPLAY-0040"]},{"id":"SOURCE_TR_20261015T013737282588Z_f957b028:DISPLAY-0051","text":"I) from the necessity of the divine nature, insofar as the idea of some human being is considered as affected; the order of the whole of Nature, insofar as it is conceived under the attributes of Extension and Thought, would have to be deduced; and thus (by Prop. 21, Part I) it would follow that a human being was infinite, which (by Part I of this demonstration) is absurd. Therefore it cannot come about that a human being undergoes no changes other than those of which he himself is the adequate cause. Q. E. D.","kind":"display","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_QGOH_PQDS:DISPLAY-0040"]},{"id":"RAW_QGOH_PQDS:DISPLAY-0041","text":"COROLLARY","kind":"display","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_QGOH_PQDS:DISPLAY-0041"]},{"id":"RAW_QGOH_PQDS:DISPLAY-0042","text":"From this it follows that a human being is necessarily always subject to passions, and follows and obeys the common order of Nature, and adapts himself to it insofar as the nature of things requires.","kind":"display","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_QGOH_PQDS:DISPLAY-0042"]},{"id":"RAW_QGOH_PQDS:DISPLAY-0043","text":"PROPOSITION V","kind":"display","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_QGOH_PQDS:DISPLAY-0043"]},{"id":"RAW_QGOH_PQDS:DISPLAY-0044","text":"The force and increase of any passion, and its perseverance in existing, are not defined by the power by which we strive to persevere in existing, but by the power of the external cause as compared with ours.","kind":"display","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_QGOH_PQDS:DISPLAY-0044"]},{"id":"RAW_QGOH_PQDS:DISPLAY-0045","text":"DEMONSTRATION","kind":"display","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_QGOH_PQDS:DISPLAY-0045"]},{"id":"RAW_QGOH_PQDS:DISPLAY-0046","text":"The essence of a passion cannot be explicated through our essence alone (by Defs. 1 and 2, Part III), that is (by Prop. 7, Part III), the power of a passion cannot be defined by the power by which we strive to persevere in our being; but (as was shown in Prop. 16, Part II) it must necessarily be defined by the power of the external cause as compared with ours. Q. E. D.","kind":"display","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_QGOH_PQDS:DISPLAY-0046"]},{"id":"RAW_QGOH_PQDS:DISPLAY-0047","text":"PROPOSITION VI","kind":"display","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_QGOH_PQDS:DISPLAY-0047"]},{"id":"RAW_QGOH_PQDS:DISPLAY-0048","text":"The force of any passion, or affect, can surpass a human being's other actions, or power, so that the affect stubbornly adheres to the human being.","kind":"display","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_QGOH_PQDS:DISPLAY-0048"]},{"id":"RAW_QGOH_PQDS:DISPLAY-0049","text":"DEMONSTRATION","kind":"display","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_QGOH_PQDS:DISPLAY-0049"]},{"id":"RAW_QGOH_PQDS:DISPLAY-0050","text":"The force and increase of any passion, and its perseverance in existing, are defined by the power of the external cause as compared with ours (by the preceding Prop.); and therefore (by Prop. 3 of this Part) it can surpass the human being's power, etc. Q. E. D.","kind":"display","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_QGOH_PQDS:DISPLAY-0050"]},{"id":"RAW_QGOH_PQDS:DISPLAY-0051","text":"PROPOSITION VII","kind":"display","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_QGOH_PQDS:DISPLAY-0051"]},{"id":"RAW_QGOH_PQDS:DISPLAY-0052","text":"An affect can neither be restrained nor taken away except by an affect contrary to it and stronger than the affect to be restrained.","kind":"display","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_QGOH_PQDS:DISPLAY-0052"]},{"id":"RAW_QGOH_PQDS:DISPLAY-0053","text":"DEMONSTRATION","kind":"display","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_QGOH_PQDS:DISPLAY-0053"]},{"id":"SOURCE_TR_20261015T013737282588Z_f957b028:DISPLAY-0065","text":"An affect, insofar as it is referred to the Mind, is an idea by which the Mind affirms a greater or lesser force of existing of its body than before (by the general Definition of the Affects, which is found at the end of Part Three). Therefore, when the Mind is conflicted by some affect, the Body is at the same time affected by an affection through which its power of acting is increased or diminished. Further, this affection of the Body (by Prop. 5 of this Part) receives from its cause the force of persevering in its being; and consequently it can neither be restrained nor taken away except by a corporeal cause (by Prop. 6, Part II) which affects the body with an affection contrary to it (by Prop. 5, Part III) and stronger than it (by the axiom of this Part); and consequently (by Prop. 12, Part II) the Mind will be affected by the idea of an affection stronger and contrary to the former, that is (by the general","kind":"display","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_QGOH_PQDS:DISPLAY-0054"]},{"id":"SOURCE_TR_20261015T013737282588Z_f957b028:DISPLAY-0066","text":"Definition of the Affects), the Mind will be affected by an affect stronger and contrary to the former, which will of course exclude or take away the existence of the former; and consequently an affect can neither be taken away nor restrained except by an affect contrary to it and stronger than it. Q. E. D.","kind":"display","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_QGOH_PQDS:DISPLAY-0054"]},{"id":"RAW_QGOH_PQDS:DISPLAY-0055","text":"COROLLARY","kind":"display","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_QGOH_PQDS:DISPLAY-0055"]},{"id":"RAW_QGOH_PQDS:DISPLAY-0056","text":"An affect, insofar as it is referred to the Mind, can neither be restrained nor taken away except by an idea of an affection of the Body contrary to and stronger than the affection by which we suffer. For the affect by which we suffer can neither be restrained nor taken away except by an affect stronger than it and contrary to it (by the preceding Prop.), that is (by the general Definition of the Affects), except by an idea of an affection of the Body stronger than and contrary to the affection by which we suffer.","kind":"display","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_QGOH_PQDS:DISPLAY-0056"]},{"id":"RAW_QGOH_PQDS:DISPLAY-0057","text":"PROPOSITION VIII","kind":"display","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_QGOH_PQDS:DISPLAY-0057"]},{"id":"RAW_QGOH_PQDS:DISPLAY-0058","text":"The knowledge of good and evil is nothing but an affect of Joy or Sadness, insofar as we are conscious of it.","kind":"display","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_QGOH_PQDS:DISPLAY-0058"]},{"id":"RAW_QGOH_PQDS:DISPLAY-0059","text":"DEMONSTRATION","kind":"display","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_QGOH_PQDS:DISPLAY-0059"]},{"id":"SOURCE_TR_20261015T013737282588Z_f957b028:DISPLAY-0072","text":"We call good or evil that which benefits or harms the preservation of our being (by Defs. 1 and 2 of this Part), that is (by Prop. 7, Part III), that which increases or diminishes, assists or restrains, our power of acting. Therefore, insofar as (by the Definitions of Joy and Sadness, see them in the Scholium of Prop. 11, Part III) we perceive some thing to affect us with Joy or Sadness, we call it good or evil; and thus the knowledge of good and evil is nothing but the idea of Joy or Sadness, which necessarily follows from the affect itself of Joy or Sadness (by Prop. 22, Part II). But this idea is united to the affect in the same manner as the Mind is united to the Body (by Prop. 21, Part II); that is (as was shown in the Scholium of the same Prop.), this idea differs from the affect itself, or (by the General Definition of the Affects)","kind":"display","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_QGOH_PQDS:DISPLAY-0060"]},{"id":"SOURCE_TR_20261015T013737282588Z_f957b028:DISPLAY-0073","text":"from the idea of an affection of the Body, is in reality not distinguished, except by conception alone; therefore this knowledge of good and evil is nothing but the affect itself, insofar as we are conscious of the same. Q. E. D.","kind":"display","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_QGOH_PQDS:DISPLAY-0060"]},{"id":"RAW_QGOH_PQDS:DISPLAY-0061","text":"PROPOSITION IX","kind":"display","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_QGOH_PQDS:DISPLAY-0061"]},{"id":"RAW_QGOH_PQDS:DISPLAY-0062","text":"An affect whose cause we imagine to be present with us is stronger than if we imagined it not to be present.","kind":"display","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_QGOH_PQDS:DISPLAY-0062"]},{"id":"RAW_QGOH_PQDS:DISPLAY-0063","text":"DEMONSTRATION","kind":"display","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_QGOH_PQDS:DISPLAY-0063"]},{"id":"RAW_QGOH_PQDS:DISPLAY-0064","text":"Imagination is an idea by which the Mind contemplates a thing as present (see its Definition in the Scholium to Prop. 17, Part II), which nevertheless indicates more the constitution of the human Body than the nature of the external thing (by Corollary 2, Prop. 16, Part II). Therefore an affect is (by the general Definition of the Affects) imagination, insofar as it indicates the constitution of the body. But imagination (by Prop. 17, Part II) is more intense so long as we imagine nothing that excludes the present existence of the external thing; therefore an affect also, whose cause we imagine to be present with us, is more intense, or stronger, than if we imagined it not to be present. Q. E. D.","kind":"display","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_QGOH_PQDS:DISPLAY-0064"]},{"id":"RAW_QGOH_PQDS:DISPLAY-0065","text":"SCHOLIUM","kind":"display","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_QGOH_PQDS:DISPLAY-0065"]},{"id":"RAW_QGOH_PQDS:DISPLAY-0066","text":"When above, in Proposition 18 of Part III, I said that we are affected by the image of a future or past thing with the same affect as if the thing which we imagine were present, I expressly cautioned that this is true insofar as we attend only to the image of the thing itself; for it is of the same nature whether we have imagined the things as present or not; but I did not deny that it is rendered weaker when we contemplate other things as present to us which exclude the present existence of the future thing; which I then neglected to mention, because I had resolved in this Part to treat of the powers of the affects.","kind":"display","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_QGOH_PQDS:DISPLAY-0066"]},{"id":"RAW_QGOH_PQDS:DISPLAY-0067","text":"COROLLARY","kind":"display","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_QGOH_PQDS:DISPLAY-0067"]},{"id":"RAW_QGOH_PQDS:DISPLAY-0068","text":"The image of a future or past thing, that is, of a thing which we contemplate in relation to future or past time, present time being excluded, is, other things being equal, weaker than the image of a present thing; and consequently an affect toward a future or past thing is, other things being equal, less intense than an affect toward a present thing.","kind":"display","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_QGOH_PQDS:DISPLAY-0068"]},{"id":"RAW_QGOH_PQDS:DISPLAY-0069","text":"PROPOSITION X","kind":"display","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_QGOH_PQDS:DISPLAY-0069"]},{"id":"RAW_QGOH_PQDS:DISPLAY-0070","text":"We are affected more intensely toward a future thing which we imagine will soon be at hand than if we imagined that the time of its existing were more distant from the present; and we are also affected more intensely by the memory of a thing which we imagine has not long since passed than if we imagined that it had passed long ago.","kind":"display","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_QGOH_PQDS:DISPLAY-0070"]},{"id":"RAW_QGOH_PQDS:DISPLAY-0071","text":"DEMONSTRATION","kind":"display","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_QGOH_PQDS:DISPLAY-0071"]},{"id":"RAW_QGOH_PQDS:DISPLAY-0072","text":"For insofar as we imagine a thing soon to be at hand, or not to have passed long ago, by that very fact we imagine something which excludes the presence of the thing less than if we imagined that the future time of its existing were more distant from the present, or that it had passed long ago (as is self-evident); and therefore (by the preceding Proposition) to that extent we shall be affected more intensely toward it. Q. E. D.","kind":"display","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_QGOH_PQDS:DISPLAY-0072"]},{"id":"RAW_QGOH_PQDS:DISPLAY-0073","text":"SCHOLIUM","kind":"display","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_QGOH_PQDS:DISPLAY-0073"]},{"id":"RAW_QGOH_PQDS:DISPLAY-0074","text":"From what we noted concerning Definition 6 of this Part, it follows that we are nevertheless affected equally faintly toward objects which are separated from the present by a longer interval of time than we can determine by imagining, even though we understand them to be separated from one another by a long interval of time. Q. E. D.","kind":"display","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_QGOH_PQDS:DISPLAY-0074"]},{"id":"RAW_QGOH_PQDS:DISPLAY-0075","text":"PROPOSITION XI","kind":"display","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_QGOH_PQDS:DISPLAY-0075"]},{"id":"RAW_QGOH_PQDS:DISPLAY-0076","text":"An affect toward a thing which we imagine as necessary is, other things being equal, more intense than toward a thing that is possible or contingent, or non-necessary.","kind":"display","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_QGOH_PQDS:DISPLAY-0076"]},{"id":"RAW_QGOH_PQDS:DISPLAY-0077","text":"DEMONSTRATION","kind":"display","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_QGOH_PQDS:DISPLAY-0077"]},{"id":"RAW_QGOH_PQDS:DISPLAY-0078","text":"Insofar as we imagine some thing to be necessary, to that extent we affirm its existence; and conversely, we deny the existence of a thing insofar as we imagine it to be non-necessary (by Scholium 1, Prop. 33, Part I); and consequently (by Prop. 9 of this Part) an affect toward a necessary thing is, other things being equal, more intense than toward a non-necessary thing. Q. E. D.","kind":"display","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_QGOH_PQDS:DISPLAY-0078"]},{"id":"RAW_QGOH_PQDS:DISPLAY-0079","text":"PROPOSITION XII","kind":"display","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_QGOH_PQDS:DISPLAY-0079"]},{"id":"RAW_QGOH_PQDS:DISPLAY-0080","text":"An affect toward a thing which we know does not presently exist, and which we imagine as possible, is, other things being equal, more intense than toward a contingent thing.","kind":"display","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_QGOH_PQDS:DISPLAY-0080"]},{"id":"RAW_QGOH_PQDS:DISPLAY-0081","text":"DEMONSTRATION","kind":"display","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_QGOH_PQDS:DISPLAY-0081"]},{"id":"RAW_QGOH_PQDS:DISPLAY-0082","text":"Insofar as we imagine a thing as contingent, we are affected by no image of another thing that posits the existence of the thing (by Definition 3 of this Part); but, on the contrary, according to the hypothesis, we imagine certain things which exclude its present existence. But insofar as we imagine a thing to be possible in the future, to that extent we imagine certain things which posit its existence (by Definition 4 of this Part), that is (by Prop. 18, Part III), which foster Hope or Fear; and thus an affect toward a possible thing is more vehement. Q. E. D.","kind":"display","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_QGOH_PQDS:DISPLAY-0082"]},{"id":"RAW_QGOH_PQDS:DISPLAY-0083","text":"COROLLARY","kind":"display","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_QGOH_PQDS:DISPLAY-0083"]},{"id":"RAW_QGOH_PQDS:DISPLAY-0084","text":"An affect toward a thing which we know not to exist at present, and which we imagine as contingent, is much more feeble than if we imagined the thing to be present to us.","kind":"display","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_QGOH_PQDS:DISPLAY-0084"]},{"id":"RAW_QGOH_PQDS:DISPLAY-0085","text":"DEMONSTRATION","kind":"display","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_QGOH_PQDS:DISPLAY-0085"]},{"id":"RAW_QGOH_PQDS:DISPLAY-0086","text":"An affect toward a thing which we imagine to exist at present is more intense than if we imagined the same thing as future (by Corollary to Proposition 9 of this Part), and is much more vehement if we imagine the future time to be far distant from the present (by Proposition 10 of this Part). Therefore an affect toward a thing whose time of existence we imagine to be far distant from the present is much more feeble than if we imagined the same thing as present; and nevertheless (by the preceding Proposition) it is more intense than if we imagined the same thing as contingent; and hence an affect toward a contingent thing will be much more feeble than if we imagined the thing to be present to us. Q. E. D.","kind":"display","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_QGOH_PQDS:DISPLAY-0086"]},{"id":"RAW_QGOH_PQDS:DISPLAY-0087","text":"PROPOSITION XIII","kind":"display","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_QGOH_PQDS:DISPLAY-0087"]},{"id":"RAW_QGOH_PQDS:DISPLAY-0088","text":"An affect toward a contingent thing, which we know not to exist at present, other things being equal, is more feeble than an affect toward a past thing.","kind":"display","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_QGOH_PQDS:DISPLAY-0088"]},{"id":"RAW_QGOH_PQDS:DISPLAY-0089","text":"DEMONSTRATION","kind":"display","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_QGOH_PQDS:DISPLAY-0089"]},{"id":"RAW_QGOH_PQDS:DISPLAY-0090","text":"Insofar as we imagine a thing as contingent, we are affected by no image of another thing which posits the existence of the thing (by Definition 3 of this Part); but, on the contrary (according to the hypothesis), we imagine certain things which exclude its present existence. But insofar as we imagine the same thing in relation to past time, we are thereby supposed to imagine something which recalls it to memory, or which arouses the image of the thing (see Proposition 18, Part II, with its Scholium), and consequently thereby causes us to contemplate it as if it were present (by Corollary to Proposition 17, Part II). And therefore (by Proposition 9 of this Part) an affect toward a contingent thing, which we know not to exist at present, other things being equal, will be more feeble than an affect toward a past thing. Q. E. D.","kind":"display","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_QGOH_PQDS:DISPLAY-0090"]},{"id":"RAW_QGOH_PQDS:DISPLAY-0091","text":"PROPOSITION XIV","kind":"display","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_QGOH_PQDS:DISPLAY-0091"]},{"id":"RAW_QGOH_PQDS:DISPLAY-0092","text":"True knowledge of good and evil, insofar as it is true, can restrain no affect, but only insofar as it is considered as an affect.","kind":"display","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_QGOH_PQDS:DISPLAY-0092"]},{"id":"RAW_QGOH_PQDS:DISPLAY-0093","text":"DEMONSTRATION","kind":"display","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_QGOH_PQDS:DISPLAY-0093"]},{"id":"RAW_QGOH_PQDS:DISPLAY-0094","text":"An affect is an idea by which the Mind affirms of its Body a greater or lesser power of existing than before (by the general Definition of the Affects); and therefore (by Proposition 1 of this Part) it has nothing positive which can be removed by the presence of the true; and consequently true knowledge of good and evil, insofar as it is true, can restrain no affect. But insofar as it is an affect (see Proposition 8 of this Part), if it is stronger than the affect to be restrained, only insofar (by Proposition 7 of this Part) will it be able to restrain the affect. Q. E. D.","kind":"display","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_QGOH_PQDS:DISPLAY-0094"]},{"id":"RAW_QGOH_PQDS:DISPLAY-0095","text":"PROPOSITION XV","kind":"display","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_QGOH_PQDS:DISPLAY-0095"]}],"source_digest":"e6f512e0a0e0fd56b0dad652588daa40b2cf8eb42564d33e3cc091a40e53cd3d","source_language_code":"la","original_title":"Pars quarta - De servitute humana seu de affectuum viribus · passage 1"}],"original_title":"Pars quarta - De servitute humana seu de affectuum viribus · passage 1","title_language_code":"en"},{"id":"RAW_QGOH_PQ_2","title":"Part Four - On Human Bondage, or the Powers of the Affects · passage 2","order":15,"chapter_id":"CH_QGOH_PQ_2","text":"Cupiditas, quæ ex vera boni et mali cognitione oritur, multis aliis Cupiditatibus, quæ ex affectibus, quibus conflictamur, oriuntur, restingui vel coerceri potest.\n\nDEMONSTRATIO\n\nEx vera boni et mali cognitione, quatenus hæc (per Prop. 8 hujus) affectus est, oritur necessario Cupiditas (per 1 Affect. Def.), quæ eo est major, quo affectus, ex quo oritur, major est (per Prop. 37 p. III). Sed quia hæc Cupiditas (per hypothesin) ex eo, quod aliquid vere intelligimus, oritur, sequitur ergo ipsa in nobis, quatenus agimus (per Prop. 1 p. III), atque adeo per solam nostram essentiam debet intelligi (per Defin. 2 p. III) ; et consequenter (per Prop. 7 p. III), ejus vis et incrementum sola humana potentia definiri debet. Porro Cupiditates, quæ ex affectibus, quibus conflictamur oriuntur, eo majores sunt, quo hi affectus vehementiores erunt ; atque adeo earum vis et incrementum etiam (per Prop. 5 hujus) potentia causarum externarum definiri debet, quæ, si cum nostra comparetur, nostram potentiam indefinite superat (per Prop. 3 hujus) : atque adeo Cupiditates, quæ ex similibus affectibus oriuntur, vehementiores esse possunt illa, quæ ex vera boni et mali cognitione oritur, ac proinde (per Prop. 7 hujus) eandem coercere vel restinguere poterunt. Q. E. D.\n\nPROPOSITIO XVI\n\nCupiditas, quæ ex cognitione boni et mali, quatenus hæc cognitio futurum respicit, oritur, facilius rerum Cupiditate, quæ in præsentia suaves sunt, coerceri vel restingui potest.\n\nDEMONSTRATIO\n\nAffectus erga rem, quam futuram imaginamur, remissior est, quam erga præsentem (per Coroll. Prop. 9 hujus). At Cupiditas, quæ ex vera boni et mali cognitione oritur, tametsi hæc cognitio circa res, quæ in præsentia bonæ sunt, versetur, restingui vel coerceri potest aliqua temeraria Cupiditate (per Prop. præced., cujus dem. universalis est) ; ergo Cupiditas, quæ ex eadem cognitione, quatenus hæc futurum respicit, oritur, facilius coerceri vel restingui poterit, etc. Q. E. D.\n\nPROPOSITIO XVII\n\nCupiditas, quæ oritur ex vera boni et mali cognitione, quatenus hæc circa res contingentes versatur, multo adhuc facilius coerceri potest Cupiditate rerum, quæ præsentes sunt.\n\nDEMONSTRATIO\n\nPropositio hæc eodem modo ac Prop. præced. demonstratur ex Coroll. Prop. 12 hujus.\n\nSCHOLIUM\n\nHis me causam ostendisse credo, cur homines opinione magis quam vera Ratione commoveantur, et cur vera boni et mali cognitio animi commotiones excitet, et sæpe omni libidinis generi cedat ; unde illud Poëtæ natum, Video meliora proboque, deteriora sequor. Quod idem etiam Ecclesiastes in mente habuisse videtur, cum dixit, Qui auget scientiam, auget dolorem. Atque hæc non eum in finem dico, ut inde concludam, præstabilius esse ignorare quam scire, vel quod stulto intelligens in moderandis affectibus nihil intersit, sed ideo quia necesse est, nostræ naturæ tam impotentiam quam potentiam noscere, ut determinare possimus, quid Ratio in moderandis affectibus possit, et quid non possit ; et in hac Parte de sola humana impotentia me acturum dixi. Nam de rationis in affectus potentia separatim agere constitui.\n\nPROPOSITIO XVIII\n\nCupiditas, quæ ex Lætitia oritur, cæteris paribus, fortior est Cupiditate, quæ ex Tristitia oritur.\n\nDEMONSTRATIO\n\nCupiditas est ipsa hominis essentia (per 1 Affect. Defin.), hoc est (per Prop. 7 p. III) conatus, quo homo in suo esse perseverare conatur. Quare Cupiditas, quæ ex Lætitia oritur, ipso Lætitiæ affectu (per Defin. Lætitiæ, quam vide in Schol. Prop. 11 p. III) juvatur vel augetur ; quæ autem contra ex Tristitia oritur, ipso Tristitiæ affectu (per idem Schol.) minuitur vel coercetur ; atque adeo vis Cupiditatis, quæ ex Lætitia oritur, potentia humana simul et potentia causæ externæ, quæ autem ex Tristitia, sola humana potentia definiri debet ; ac proinde hac illa fortior est. Q. E. D.\n\nSCHOLIUM\n\nHis paucis humanæ impotentiæ et inconstantiæ causas, et cur homines Rationis præcepta non servent, explicui. Superest jam, ut ostendam, quid id sit, quod Ratio nobis præscribit, et quinam affectus cum Rationis humanæ regulis conveniant, quinam contra iisdem contrarii sint. Sed antequam hæc prolixo nostro Geometrico ordine demonstrare incipiam, lubet ipsa Rationis dictamina hic prius breviter ostendere, ut ea, quæ sentio, facilius ab unoquoque percipiantur. Cum Ratio nihil contra Naturam postulet, postulat ergo ipsa, ut unusquisque seipsum amet, suum utile, quod revera utile est, quærat, et id omne, quod hominem ad majorem perfectionem revera ducit, appetat, et absolute, ut unusquisque suum esse, quantum in se est, conservare conetur. Quod quidem tam necessario verum est, quam quod totum sit sua parte majus (Vide Prop. 4 p. III). Deinde, quandoquidem virtus (per Defin. 8 hujus) nihil aliud est, quam ex legibus propriæ naturæ agere, et nemo suum esse (per Prop. 7 p. III) conservare conetur, nisi ex propriæ suæ naturæ legibus ; hinc sequitur primo, virtutis fundamentum esse ipsum conatum proprium esse conservandi, et felicitatem in eo consistere, quod homo suum esse conservare potest. Secundo sequitur, virtutem propter se esse appetendam, nec quicquam, quod ipsa præstabilius aut quod utilius nobis sit, dari, cujus causa deberet appeti. Tertio denique sequitur, eos, qui se interficiunt, animo esse impotentes, eosque a causis externis, suæ naturæ repugnantibus, prorsus vinci. Porro ex Postulato 4 partis II, sequitur nos efficere nunquam posse, ut nihil extra nos indigeamus ad nostrum esse conservandum, et ut ita vivamus, ut nullum commercium cum rebus, quæ extra nos sunt, habeamus ; et, si præterea nostram Mentem spectemus, sane noster intellectus imperfectior esset, si Mens sola esset, nec quicquam præter se ipsam intelligeret. Multa igitur extra nos dantur, quæ nobis utilia, quæque propterea appetenda sunt. Ex his nulla præstantiora excogitari possunt, quam ea, quæ cum nostra natura prorsus conveniunt. Si enim duo ex. gr. ejusdem prorsus naturæ individua invicem junguntur, individuum componunt singulo duplo potentius. Homini igitur nihil homine utilius ; nihil, inquam, homines præstantius ad suum esse conservandum optare possunt, quam quod omnes in omnibus ita conveniant, ut omnium Mentes et Corpora unam quasi Mentem unumque Corpus componant, et omnes simul, quantum possunt, suum esse conservare conentur, omnesque simul omnium commune utile sibi quærant ; ex quibus sequitur, homines, qui Ratione gubernantur, hoc est homines, qui ex ductu Rationis suum utile quærunt, nihil sibi appetere, quod reliquis hominibus non cupiant, atque adeo eosdem justos, fidos, atque honestos esse.\n\nHæc illa Rationis dictamina sunt, quæ hic paucis ostendere proposueram, antequam eadem prolixiore ordine demonstrare inciperem ; quod ea de causa feci, ut, si fieri posset, eorum attentionem mihi conciliarem, qui credunt, hoc principium, quod scilicet unusquisque suum utile quærere tenetur, impietatis, non autem virtutis et pietatis esse fundamentum. Postquam igitur rem sese contra habere breviter ostenderim, pergo ad eandem eadem via, qua huc usque progressi sumus, demonstrandum.\n\nPROPOSITIO XIX\n\nId unusquisque ex legibus suæ naturæ necessario appetit vel aversatur, quod bonum vel malum esse judicat.\n\nDEMONSTRATIO\n\nBoni et mali cognitio est (per Prop. 8 hujus) ipse Lætitiæ vel Tristitiæ affectus, quatenus ejusdem sumus conscii ; ac proinde (per Prop. 28 p. III) id unusquisque necessario appetit, quod bonum, et contra id aversatur, quod malum esse judicat. Sed hic appetitus nihil aliud est, quam ipsa hominis essentia seu natura (per Defin. App., quam vide in Schol. Prop. 9 p. III et 1 Aff. Defin.). Ergo unusquisque ex solis suæ naturæ legibus id necessario appetit vel aversatur, etc. Q. E. D.\n\nPROPOSITIO XX\n\nQuo magis unusquisque suum utile quærere, hoc est suum esse conservare, conatur et potest, eo magis virtute præditus est ; et contra, quatenus unusquisque suum utile, hoc est suum esse, conservare negligit eatenus est impotens.\n\nDEMONSTRATIO\n\nVirtus est ipsa humana potentia, quæ sola hominis essentia definitur (per Defin. 8 hujus), hoc est (per Prop. 7 p. III) quæ solo conatu, quo homo in suo esse perseverare conatur, definitur. Quo ergo unusquisque magis suum esse conservare conatur et potest eo magis virtute præditus est, et consequenter (per Prop. 4 et 6 p. III), quatenus aliquis suum esse conservare negligit, eatenus est impotens. Q. E. D.\n\nSCHOLIUM\n\nNemo igitur, nisi a causis externis et suæ naturæ contrariis victus suum utile appetere, sive suum esse conservare, negligit. Nemo, inquam, ex necessitate suæ naturæ, sed a causis externis coactus, alimenta aversatur, vel se ipsum interficit, quod multis modis fieri potest ; nempe interficit aliquis se ipsum coactus ab alio, qui ejus dexteram, qua ensem casu prehenderat, contorquet, et cogit versus cor ipsum gladium dirigere ; vel quod ex mandato Tyranni, ut Seneca, cogatur venas aperire suas, hoc est, majus malum minore vitare cupiat ; vel denique ex eo, quod causæ latentes externæ ejus imaginationem ita disponunt, et Corpus ita afficiunt, ut id aliam naturam priori contrariam induat, et cujus idea in Mente dari nequit (per Prop. 10 p. III). At quod homo ex necessitate suæ naturæ conetur non existere, vel in aliam formam mutari, tam est impossibile quam quod ex nihilo aliquid fiat, ut unusquisque mediocri meditatione videre potest.\n\nPROPOSITIO XXI\n\nNemo potest cupere beatum esse, bene agere, et bene vivere, qui simul non cupiat esse, agere, et vivere, hoc est, actu existere.\n\nDEMONSTRATIO\n\nHujus Propositionis demonstratio, seu potius res ipsa, per se patet, et etiam ex Cupiditatis definitione. Est enim cupiditas (per 1 Aff. Defin.) beate seu bene vivendi, agendi, etc., ipsa hominis essentia, hoc est (per Prop. 7 p. III) conatus, quo unusquisque suum esse conservare conatur. Ergo nemo potest cupere, etc. Q. E. D.\n\nPROPOSITIO XXII\n\nNulla virtus potest prior hac (nempe conatu sese conservandi) concipi.\n\nDEMONSTRATIO\n\nConatus sese conservandi est ipsa rei essentia (per Prop. 7 p. III). Si igitur aliqua virtus posset hac, nempe hoc conatu, prior concipi, conciperetur ergo (per Defin. 8 hujus) ipsa rei essentia se ipsa prior, quod (ut per se notum) est absurdum. Ergo nulla virtus, etc. Q. E. D.\n\nCOROLLARIUM\n\nConatus sese conservandi primum et unicum virtutis est fundamentum. Nam hoc principio nullum aliud potest prius concipi (per Prop. præced.), et absque ipso (per Prop. 21 hujus) nulla virtus potest concipi.\n\nPROPOSITIO XXIII\n\nHomo, quatenus ad aliquid agendum determinatur ex eo, quod ideas habet inadæquatas, non potest absolute dici, ex virtute agere ; sed tantum quatenus determinatur ex eo, quod intelligit.\n\nDEMONSTRATIO\n\nQuatenus homo ad agendum determinatur ex eo, quod inadæquatas habet ideas eatenus (per Prop. 1 p. III) patitur, hoc est (per Defin. 1 et 2 p. III) aliquid agit, quod per solam ejus essentiam non potest percipi, hoc est (per Defin. 8 hujus) quod ex ipsius virtute non sequitur. At quatenus ad aliquid agendum determinatur ex eo, quod intelligit, eatenus (per eandem Prop. 1 p. III) agit, hoc est (per Defin. 2 p. III) aliquid agit, quod per solam ipsius essentiam percipitur, sive (per Defin. 8 hujus) quod ex ipsius virtute adæquate sequitur. Q. E. D.\n\nPROPOSITIO XXIV\n\nEx virtute absolute agere nihil aliud in nobis est, quam ex ductu Rationis agere, vivere, suum esse conservare (hæc tria idem significant) ex fundamento proprium utile quærendi.\n\nDEMONSTRATIO\n\nEx virtute absolute agere, nihil aliud est (per Defin. 8 hujus), quam ex legibus propriæ naturæ agere. At nos eatenus tantummodo agimus, quatenus intelligimus (per Prop. 3 p. III) ; ergo ex virtute agere, nihil aliud in nobis est, quam ex ductu Rationis agere, vivere, suum esse conservare idque (per Coroll. Prop. 22 hujus) ex fundamento suum utile quærendi. Q. E. D.\n\nPROPOSITIO XXV\n\nNemo suum esse alterius rei causa conservare conatur.\n\nDEMONSTRATIO\n\nConatus, quo unaquæque res in suo esse perseverare conatur, sola ipsius rei essentia definitur (per Prop. 7 p. III) ; eaque sola data, non autem ex alterius rei essentia, necessario sequitur (per Prop. 6 p. III), ut unusquisque suum esse conservare conetur. Patet præterea hæc Propositio ex Coroll. Prop. 22 hujus Partis. Nam si homo alterius rei causa suum esse conservare conaretur, tum res illa primum esset virtutis fundamentum (ut per se notum) quod (per prædictum Coroll.) est absurdum. Ergo nemo suum esse, etc. Q. E. D.\n\nPROPOSITIO XXVI\n\nQuicquid ex Ratione conamur, nihil aliud est quam intelligere ; nec Mens, quatenus Ratione utitur, aliud sibi utile esse judicat, nisi id, quod ad intelligendum conducit.\n\nDEMONSTRATIO\n\nConatus sese conservandi nihil est præter ipsius rei essentiam (per Prop. 7 p. III), quæ, quatenus talis existit, vim habere concipitur ad perseverandum in existendo (per Prop. 6 p. III), et ea agendum, quæ ex data sua natura necessario sequuntur (vide Defin. Appetitus in Schol. Prop. 9 p. III). At Rationis essentia nihil aliud est quam Mens nostra, quatenus clare et distincte intelligit (vide ejus Defin. in 2 Schol. Prop. 40 p. II). Ergo (per Prop. 40 p. II) quicquid ex Ratione conamur, nihil aliud est quam intelligere. Deinde quoniam hic Mentis conatus, quo Mens, quatenus ratiocinatur, suum esse conatur conservare, nihil aliud est quam intelligere (per primam partem hujus) ; est ergo hic intelligendi conatus (per Coroll. Prop. 22 hujus) primum et unicum virtutis fundamentum ; nec alicujus finis causa (per Prop. 25 hujus) res intelligere conabimur : sed contra Mens, quatenus ratiocinatur, nihil sibi bonum esse concipere poterit nisi id, quod ad intelligendum conducit (per Defin. 1 hujus). Q. E. D.\n\nPROPOSITIO XXVII\n\nNihil certo scimus bonum aut malum esse, nisi id, quod ad intelligendum revera conducit, vel quod impedire potest, quo minus intelligamus.\n\nDEMONSTRATIO\n\nMens, quatenus ratiocinatur, nihil aliud appetit quam intelligere, nec aliud sibi utile esse judicat nisi id, quod ad intelligendum conducit (per Prop. præced.). At mens (per Prop. 41 et 43 p. II, cujus etiam Schol. vide) rerum certitudinem non habet, nisi quatenus ideas habet adæquatas, sive (quod per Schol. 2 Prop. 40 p. II idem est) quatenus ratiocinatur. Ergo nihil certo scimus bonum esse nisi id, quod ad intelligendum revera conducit ; et contra id malum, quod impedire potest, quo minus intelligamus. Q. E. D.\n\nPROPOSITIO XXVIII\n\nSummum Mentis bonum est Dei cognitio, et Summa Mentis virtus Deum cognoscere.\n\nDEMONSTRATIO\n\nSummum, quod Mens intelligere potest, Deus est, hoc est (per Defin. 6 p. I) Ens absolute infinitum, et sine quo (per Prop. 15 p. I) nihil esse neque concipi potest ; adeoque (per Prop. 26 et 27 hujus) summum Mentis utile, sive (per Defin. 1 hujus) bonum, est Dei cognitio. Deinde Mens, quatenus intelligit, eatenus tantum agit (per Prop. 1 et 3 p. III), et eatenus tantum (per Prop. 23 hujus) potest absolute dici, quod ex virtute agit. Est igitur Mentis absoluta virtus intelligere. At summum, quod Mens intelligere potest, Deus est (ut jamjam demonstravimus) ; Ergo Mentis summa virtus est Deum intelligere seu cognoscere. Q. E. D.\n\nPROPOSITIO XXIX\n\nRes quæcunque singularis, cujus natura a nostra prorsus est diversa nostram agendi potentiam nec juvare nec coercere potest, et absolute res nulla potest nobis bona aut mala esse, nisi commune aliquid nobiscum habeat.\n\nDEMONSTRATIO\n\nCujuscunque rei singularis, et consequenter (per Coroll. Prop. 10 p. II) hominis, potentia, qua existit et operatur, non determinatur nisi ab alia re singulari (per Prop. 28 p. I), cujus natura (per Prop. 6 p. II) per idem attributum debet intelligi, per quod natura humana concipitur. Nostra igitur agendi potentia, quomodocunque ea concipiatur, determinari, et consequenter juvari vel coerceri, potest potentia alterius rei singularis, quæ aliquid commune nobiscum habet, et non potentia rei cujus natura a nostra prorsus est diversa ; et quia id bonum aut malum vocamus, quod causa est Lætitiæ aut Tristitiæ (per Prop. 8 hujus), hoc est (per Schol. Prop. 11 p. III) quod nostram agendi potentiam auget vel minuit, juvat vel coercet ; ergo res, cujus natura a nostra prorsus est diversa, nobis neque bona neque mala esse potest. Q. E. D.\n\nPROPOSITIO XXX\n\nRes nulla per id, quod cum nostra natura commune habet, potest esse mala, sed quatenus nobis mala est, eatenus est nobis contraria.\n\nDEMONSTRATIO\n\nId malum vocamus, quod causa est Tristitiæ (per Prop. 8 hujus) hoc est (per ejus Definit., quam vide in Schol. Prop. 11 p. III), quod nostram agendi potentiam minuit vel coercet. Si igitur res aliqua per id, quod nobiscum habet commune, nobis esset mala ; posset ergo res id ipsum, quod nobiscum commune habet, minuere vel coercere, quod (per Prop. 4 p. III) est absurdum. Nulla igitur res per id, quod nobiscum commune habet, potest nobis esse mala, sed contra quatenus mala est, hoc est (ut jamjam ostendimus), quatenus nostram agendi potentiam minuere vel coercere potest, eatenus (per Prop. 5 p. III) nobis est contraria. Q. E. D.\n\nPROPOSITIO XXXI\n\nQuatenus res aliqua cum nostra natura convenit, eatenus necessario bona est.\n\nDEMONSTRATIO\n\nQuatenus enim res aliqua cum nostra natura convenit, non potest (per Prop. præced.) esse mala. Erit ergo necessario vel bona vel indifferens. Si hoc ponatur, nempe quod neque bona sit neque mala ; nihil ergo (per Defin. 1 hujus) ex ipsius natura sequetur, quod nostræ naturæ conservationi inservit, hoc est (per hypothesin) quod ipsius rei naturæ conservationi inservit ; sed hoc est absurdum (per Prop. 6 p. III), erit ergo, quatenus cum nostra natura convenit, necessario bona. Q. E. D.\n\nCOROLLARIUM\n\nHinc sequitur, quod, quo res aliqua magis cum nostra natura convenit, eo nobis est utilior seu magis bona, et contra, quo res aliqua nobis est utilior, eatenus cum nostra natura magis convenit. Nam quatenus cum nostra natura non convenit, erit necessario a nostra natura diversa, vel eidem contraria. Si diversa, tum (per Prop. 29 hujus) neque bona neque mala esse poterit ; si autem contraria, erit ergo etiam ei contraria, quæ cum nostra natura convenit, hoc est (per Prop. præced.) contraria bono seu mala. Nihil igitur, nisi quatenus cum nostra natura convenit, potest esse bonum, atque adeo, quo res aliqua magis cum nostra natura convenit, eo est utilior et contra. Q. E. D.\n\nPROPOSITIO XXXII\n\nQuatenus homines passionibus sunt obnoxii, non possunt eatenus dici quod natura conveniant.\n\nDEMONSTRATIO\n\nQuæ natura convenire dicuntur, potentia convenire intelliguntur (per Prop. 7 p. III) ; non autem impotentia seu negatione, et consequenter (vide Schol. Prop. 3 p. III) neque etiam passione ; quare homines, quatenus passionibus sunt obnoxii, non possunt dici, quod natura conveniant. Q. E. D.\n\nSCHOLIUM\n\nRes etiam per se patet ; qui enim ait, album et nigrum in eo solummodo convenire, quod neutrum sit rubrum, is absolute affirmat, album et nigrum nulla in re convenire. Sic etiam si quis ait, lapidem et hominem in hoc tantum convenire, quod uterque sit finitus, impotens, vel quod ex necessitate suæ naturæ non existit, vel denique quod a potentia causarum externarum indefinite superatur : is omnino affirmat lapidem et hominem nulla in re convenire ; quæ enim in sola negatione, sive in eo, quod non habent, conveniunt, ea revera nulla in re conveniunt.\n\nPROPOSITIO XXXIII\n\nHomines natura discrepare possunt, quatenus affectibus, qui passiones sunt, conflictantur ; et eatenus etiam unus idemque homo varius est et inconstans.\n\nDEMONSTRATIO\n\nAffectuum natura seu essentia non potest per solam nostram essentiam seu naturam explicari (per Defin. 1 et 2 p. III) ; sed potentia, hoc est (per Prop. 7 p. III) natura, causarum externarum, cum nostra comparata, definiri debet ; unde fit, ut uniuscujusque affectus tot species dentur, quot sunt species objectorum, a quibus afficimur (vide Prop. 56 p. III) et ut homines ab uno eodemque objecto diversimode afficiantur (vide Prop. 51 p. III), atque eatenus natura discrepent ; et denique ut unus idemque homo (per eandem Prop. 51 p. III) erga idem objectum diversimode afficiatur, atque eatenus varius sit, etc. Q. E. D.\n\nPROPOSITIO XXXIV\n\nQuatenus homines affectibus, qui passiones sunt, conflictantur, possunt invicem esse contrarii.\n\nDEMONSTRATIO\n\nHomo ex. gr. Petrus potest esse causa, ut Paulus contristetur, propterea quod aliquid habet simile rei, quam Paulus odit (per Prop. 16 p. III) ; vel propterea quod Petrus solus re aliqua potitur, quam ipse Paulus etiam amat (vide Prop. 32 p. III cum ejusdem Schol.), vel ob alias causas (harum præcipuas vide in Schol. Prop. 55 p. III) ; atque adeo inde fiet (per Defin. 7 Affect.), ut Paulus Petrum odio habeat : et consequenter facile fiet (per Prop. 40 p. III cum ejus Schol.), ut Petrus Paulum contra odio habeat, atque adeo (per Prop. 39 p. III) ut invicem malum inferre conentur, hoc est (per Prop. 30 hujus), ut invicem sint contrarii. At affectus Tristitiæ semper passio est (per Prop. 59 p. III) ; ergo homines, quatenus conflictantur affectibus, qui passiones sunt, possunt invicem esse contrarii. Q. E. D.\n\nSCHOLIUM\n\nDixi, quod Paulus odio Petrum habeat, quia imaginatur, id eundem possidere, quod ipse Paulus etiam amat ; unde prima fronte videtur sequi, quod hi duo ex eo, quod idem amant, et consequenter ex eo, quod natura conveniunt, sibi invicem damno sint ; atque adeo, si hoc verum est, falsæ essent Propositio 30 et 31 hujus Partis. Sed si rem æqua lance examinare velimus, hæc omnia convenire omnino videbimus. Nam hi duo non sunt invicem molesti, quatenus natura conveniunt, hoc est quatenus uterque idem amat, sed quatenus ab invicem discrepant. Nam quatenus uterque idem amat, eo ipso utriusque amor fovetur (per Prop. 31 p. III), hoc est (per Defin. 6 Affect.), eo ipso utriusque Lætitia fovetur. Quare longe abest, ut quatenus idem amant, et natura conveniunt, invicem molesti sint. Sed hujus rei causa, ut dixi, nulla alia est, quam quia natura discrepare supponuntur. Supponimus namque, Petrum ideam habere rei amatæ jam possessæ, et Paulum contra ideam rei amatæ amissæ. Unde fit ut hic Tristitia, et ille contra Lætitia afficiatur, atque eatenus invicem contrarii sint. Et ad hunc modum ostendere facile possumus, reliquas odii causas ab hoc solo pendere, quod homines natura discrepant, et non ab eo, in quo conveniunt.\n\nPROPOSITIO XXXV\n\nQuatenus homines ex ductu Rationis vivunt, eatenus tantum natura semper necessario conveniunt.\n\nDEMONSTRATIO\n\nQuatenus homines affectibus, qui passiones sunt, conflictantur, possunt esse natura diversi (per Prop. 33 hujus) et invicem contrarii (per Prop. præced.). Sed eatenus homines tantum agere dicuntur, quatenus ex ductu Rationis vivunt (per Prop. 3 p. III) ; atque adeo quicquid ex humana natura, quatenus Ratione definitur, sequitur, id (per Defin. 2 p. III) per solam humanam naturam, tanquam per proximam suam causam, debet intelligi. Sed quia unusquisque ex suæ naturæ legibus id appetit, quod bonum, et id amovere conatur, quod malum esse judicat (per Prop. 19 hujus) ; et cum præterea id, quod ex dictamine Rationis bonum aut malum esse judicamus, necessario bonum aut malum sit (per Prop. 41 p. II) ; Ergo homines, quatenus ex ductu Rationis vivunt, eatenus tantum ea necessario agunt, quæ humanæ naturæ et consequenter unicuique homini necessario bona sunt, hoc est (per Coroll. Prop. 31 hujus) quæ cum natura uniuscujusque hominis conveniunt ; atque adeo homines etiam inter se quatenus ex ductu Rationis vivunt, necessario semper conveniunt. Q. E. D.\n\nCOROLLARIUM I\n\nNihil singulare in rerum Natura datur, quod homini sit utilius quam homo, qui ex ductu Rationis vivit. Nam id homini utilissimum est, quod cum sua natura maxime convenit (per Coroll. Prop. 31 hujus), hoc est (ut per se notum) homo. At homo ex legibus suæ naturæ absolute agit, quando ex ductu Rationis vivit (per Defin. 2 p. III), et eatenus tantum cum natura alterius hominis necessario semper convenit (per Prop. præced.) ; ergo homini nihil inter res singulares utilius datur quam homo, etc. Q. E. D.\n\nCOROLLARIUM II","paragraphs":[{"id":"RAW_QGOH_PQ_2:P0001","text":"Cupiditas, quæ ex vera boni et mali cognitione oritur, multis aliis Cupiditatibus, quæ ex affectibus, quibus conflictamur, oriuntur, restingui vel coerceri potest.","kind":"passage"},{"id":"RAW_QGOH_PQ_2:P0002","text":"DEMONSTRATIO","kind":"passage"},{"id":"RAW_QGOH_PQ_2:P0003","text":"Ex vera boni et mali cognitione, quatenus hæc (per Prop. 8 hujus) affectus est, oritur necessario Cupiditas (per 1 Affect. Def.), quæ eo est major, quo affectus, ex quo oritur, major est (per Prop. 37 p. III). Sed quia hæc Cupiditas (per hypothesin) ex eo, quod aliquid vere intelligimus, oritur, sequitur ergo ipsa in nobis, quatenus agimus (per Prop. 1 p. III), atque adeo per solam nostram essentiam debet intelligi (per Defin. 2 p. III) ; et consequenter (per Prop. 7 p. III), ejus vis et incrementum sola humana potentia definiri debet. Porro Cupiditates, quæ ex affectibus, quibus conflictamur oriuntur, eo majores sunt, quo hi affectus vehementiores erunt ; atque adeo earum vis et incrementum etiam (per Prop. 5 hujus) potentia causarum externarum definiri debet, quæ, si cum nostra comparetur, nostram potentiam indefinite superat (per Prop.","kind":"passage"},{"id":"RAW_QGOH_PQ_2:P0004","text":"3 hujus) : atque adeo Cupiditates, quæ ex similibus affectibus oriuntur, vehementiores esse possunt illa, quæ ex vera boni et mali cognitione oritur, ac proinde (per Prop. 7 hujus) eandem coercere vel restinguere poterunt. Q. E. D.","kind":"passage"},{"id":"RAW_QGOH_PQ_2:P0005","text":"PROPOSITIO XVI","kind":"passage"},{"id":"RAW_QGOH_PQ_2:P0006","text":"Cupiditas, quæ ex cognitione boni et mali, quatenus hæc cognitio futurum respicit, oritur, facilius rerum Cupiditate, quæ in præsentia suaves sunt, coerceri vel restingui potest.","kind":"passage"},{"id":"RAW_QGOH_PQ_2:P0007","text":"DEMONSTRATIO","kind":"passage"},{"id":"RAW_QGOH_PQ_2:P0008","text":"Affectus erga rem, quam futuram imaginamur, remissior est, quam erga præsentem (per Coroll. Prop. 9 hujus). At Cupiditas, quæ ex vera boni et mali cognitione oritur, tametsi hæc cognitio circa res, quæ in præsentia bonæ sunt, versetur, restingui vel coerceri potest aliqua temeraria Cupiditate (per Prop. præced., cujus dem. universalis est) ; ergo Cupiditas, quæ ex eadem cognitione, quatenus hæc futurum respicit, oritur, facilius coerceri vel restingui poterit, etc. Q. E. D.","kind":"passage"},{"id":"RAW_QGOH_PQ_2:P0009","text":"PROPOSITIO XVII","kind":"passage"},{"id":"RAW_QGOH_PQ_2:P0010","text":"Cupiditas, quæ oritur ex vera boni et mali cognitione, quatenus hæc circa res contingentes versatur, multo adhuc facilius coerceri potest Cupiditate rerum, quæ præsentes sunt.","kind":"passage"},{"id":"RAW_QGOH_PQ_2:P0011","text":"DEMONSTRATIO","kind":"passage"},{"id":"RAW_QGOH_PQ_2:P0012","text":"Propositio hæc eodem modo ac Prop. præced. demonstratur ex Coroll. Prop. 12 hujus.","kind":"passage"},{"id":"RAW_QGOH_PQ_2:P0013","text":"SCHOLIUM","kind":"passage"},{"id":"RAW_QGOH_PQ_2:P0014","text":"His me causam ostendisse credo, cur homines opinione magis quam vera Ratione commoveantur, et cur vera boni et mali cognitio animi commotiones excitet, et sæpe omni libidinis generi cedat ; unde illud Poëtæ natum, Video meliora proboque, deteriora sequor. Quod idem etiam Ecclesiastes in mente habuisse videtur, cum dixit, Qui auget scientiam, auget dolorem. Atque hæc non eum in finem dico, ut inde concludam, præstabilius esse ignorare quam scire, vel quod stulto intelligens in moderandis affectibus nihil intersit, sed ideo quia necesse est, nostræ naturæ tam impotentiam quam potentiam noscere, ut determinare possimus, quid Ratio in moderandis affectibus possit, et quid non possit ; et in hac Parte de sola humana impotentia me acturum dixi. Nam de rationis in affectus potentia separatim agere constitui.","kind":"passage"},{"id":"RAW_QGOH_PQ_2:P0015","text":"PROPOSITIO XVIII","kind":"passage"},{"id":"RAW_QGOH_PQ_2:P0016","text":"Cupiditas, quæ ex Lætitia oritur, cæteris paribus, fortior est Cupiditate, quæ ex Tristitia oritur.","kind":"passage"},{"id":"RAW_QGOH_PQ_2:P0017","text":"DEMONSTRATIO","kind":"passage"},{"id":"RAW_QGOH_PQ_2:P0018","text":"Cupiditas est ipsa hominis essentia (per 1 Affect. Defin.), hoc est (per Prop. 7 p. III) conatus, quo homo in suo esse perseverare conatur. Quare Cupiditas, quæ ex Lætitia oritur, ipso Lætitiæ affectu (per Defin. Lætitiæ, quam vide in Schol. Prop. 11 p. III) juvatur vel augetur ; quæ autem contra ex Tristitia oritur, ipso Tristitiæ affectu (per idem Schol.) minuitur vel coercetur ; atque adeo vis Cupiditatis, quæ ex Lætitia oritur, potentia humana simul et potentia causæ externæ, quæ autem ex Tristitia, sola humana potentia definiri debet ; ac proinde hac illa fortior est. Q. E. D.","kind":"passage"},{"id":"RAW_QGOH_PQ_2:P0019","text":"SCHOLIUM","kind":"passage"},{"id":"RAW_QGOH_PQ_2:P0020","text":"His paucis humanæ impotentiæ et inconstantiæ causas, et cur homines Rationis præcepta non servent, explicui. Superest jam, ut ostendam, quid id sit, quod Ratio nobis præscribit, et quinam affectus cum Rationis humanæ regulis conveniant, quinam contra iisdem contrarii sint. Sed antequam hæc prolixo nostro Geometrico ordine demonstrare incipiam, lubet ipsa Rationis dictamina hic prius breviter ostendere, ut ea, quæ sentio, facilius ab unoquoque percipiantur. Cum Ratio nihil contra Naturam postulet, postulat ergo ipsa, ut unusquisque seipsum amet, suum utile, quod revera utile est, quærat, et id omne, quod hominem ad majorem perfectionem revera ducit, appetat, et absolute, ut unusquisque suum esse, quantum in se est, conservare conetur. Quod quidem tam necessario verum est, quam quod totum sit sua parte majus (Vide Prop. 4 p. III). Deinde, quandoquidem virtus (per Defin.","kind":"passage"},{"id":"RAW_QGOH_PQ_2:P0021","text":"8 hujus) nihil aliud est, quam ex legibus propriæ naturæ agere, et nemo suum esse (per Prop. 7 p. III) conservare conetur, nisi ex propriæ suæ naturæ legibus ; hinc sequitur primo, virtutis fundamentum esse ipsum conatum proprium esse conservandi, et felicitatem in eo consistere, quod homo suum esse conservare potest. Secundo sequitur, virtutem propter se esse appetendam, nec quicquam, quod ipsa præstabilius aut quod utilius nobis sit, dari, cujus causa deberet appeti. Tertio denique sequitur, eos, qui se interficiunt, animo esse impotentes, eosque a causis externis, suæ naturæ repugnantibus, prorsus vinci.","kind":"passage"},{"id":"RAW_QGOH_PQ_2:P0022","text":"Porro ex Postulato 4 partis II, sequitur nos efficere nunquam posse, ut nihil extra nos indigeamus ad nostrum esse conservandum, et ut ita vivamus, ut nullum commercium cum rebus, quæ extra nos sunt, habeamus ; et, si præterea nostram Mentem spectemus, sane noster intellectus imperfectior esset, si Mens sola esset, nec quicquam præter se ipsam intelligeret. Multa igitur extra nos dantur, quæ nobis utilia, quæque propterea appetenda sunt. Ex his nulla præstantiora excogitari possunt, quam ea, quæ cum nostra natura prorsus conveniunt. Si enim duo ex. gr. ejusdem prorsus naturæ individua invicem junguntur, individuum componunt singulo duplo potentius.","kind":"passage"},{"id":"RAW_QGOH_PQ_2:P0023","text":"Homini igitur nihil homine utilius ; nihil, inquam, homines præstantius ad suum esse conservandum optare possunt, quam quod omnes in omnibus ita conveniant, ut omnium Mentes et Corpora unam quasi Mentem unumque Corpus componant, et omnes simul, quantum possunt, suum esse conservare conentur, omnesque simul omnium commune utile sibi quærant ; ex quibus sequitur, homines, qui Ratione gubernantur, hoc est homines, qui ex ductu Rationis suum utile quærunt, nihil sibi appetere, quod reliquis hominibus non cupiant, atque adeo eosdem justos, fidos, atque honestos esse.","kind":"passage"},{"id":"RAW_QGOH_PQ_2:P0024","text":"Hæc illa Rationis dictamina sunt, quæ hic paucis ostendere proposueram, antequam eadem prolixiore ordine demonstrare inciperem ; quod ea de causa feci, ut, si fieri posset, eorum attentionem mihi conciliarem, qui credunt, hoc principium, quod scilicet unusquisque suum utile quærere tenetur, impietatis, non autem virtutis et pietatis esse fundamentum. Postquam igitur rem sese contra habere breviter ostenderim, pergo ad eandem eadem via, qua huc usque progressi sumus, demonstrandum.","kind":"passage"},{"id":"RAW_QGOH_PQ_2:P0025","text":"PROPOSITIO XIX","kind":"passage"},{"id":"RAW_QGOH_PQ_2:P0026","text":"Id unusquisque ex legibus suæ naturæ necessario appetit vel aversatur, quod bonum vel malum esse judicat.","kind":"passage"},{"id":"RAW_QGOH_PQ_2:P0027","text":"DEMONSTRATIO","kind":"passage"},{"id":"RAW_QGOH_PQ_2:P0028","text":"Boni et mali cognitio est (per Prop. 8 hujus) ipse Lætitiæ vel Tristitiæ affectus, quatenus ejusdem sumus conscii ; ac proinde (per Prop. 28 p. III) id unusquisque necessario appetit, quod bonum, et contra id aversatur, quod malum esse judicat. Sed hic appetitus nihil aliud est, quam ipsa hominis essentia seu natura (per Defin. App., quam vide in Schol. Prop. 9 p. III et 1 Aff. Defin.). Ergo unusquisque ex solis suæ naturæ legibus id necessario appetit vel aversatur, etc. Q. E. D.","kind":"passage"},{"id":"RAW_QGOH_PQ_2:P0029","text":"PROPOSITIO XX","kind":"passage"},{"id":"RAW_QGOH_PQ_2:P0030","text":"Quo magis unusquisque suum utile quærere, hoc est suum esse conservare, conatur et potest, eo magis virtute præditus est ; et contra, quatenus unusquisque suum utile, hoc est suum esse, conservare negligit eatenus est impotens.","kind":"passage"},{"id":"RAW_QGOH_PQ_2:P0031","text":"DEMONSTRATIO","kind":"passage"},{"id":"RAW_QGOH_PQ_2:P0032","text":"Virtus est ipsa humana potentia, quæ sola hominis essentia definitur (per Defin. 8 hujus), hoc est (per Prop. 7 p. III) quæ solo conatu, quo homo in suo esse perseverare conatur, definitur. Quo ergo unusquisque magis suum esse conservare conatur et potest eo magis virtute præditus est, et consequenter (per Prop. 4 et 6 p. III), quatenus aliquis suum esse conservare negligit, eatenus est impotens. Q. E. D.","kind":"passage"},{"id":"RAW_QGOH_PQ_2:P0033","text":"SCHOLIUM","kind":"passage"},{"id":"RAW_QGOH_PQ_2:P0034","text":"Nemo igitur, nisi a causis externis et suæ naturæ contrariis victus suum utile appetere, sive suum esse conservare, negligit. Nemo, inquam, ex necessitate suæ naturæ, sed a causis externis coactus, alimenta aversatur, vel se ipsum interficit, quod multis modis fieri potest ; nempe interficit aliquis se ipsum coactus ab alio, qui ejus dexteram, qua ensem casu prehenderat, contorquet, et cogit versus cor ipsum gladium dirigere ; vel quod ex mandato Tyranni, ut Seneca, cogatur venas aperire suas, hoc est, majus malum minore vitare cupiat ; vel denique ex eo, quod causæ latentes externæ ejus imaginationem ita disponunt, et Corpus ita afficiunt, ut id aliam naturam priori contrariam induat, et cujus idea in Mente dari nequit (per Prop. 10 p. III).","kind":"passage"},{"id":"RAW_QGOH_PQ_2:P0035","text":"At quod homo ex necessitate suæ naturæ conetur non existere, vel in aliam formam mutari, tam est impossibile quam quod ex nihilo aliquid fiat, ut unusquisque mediocri meditatione videre potest.","kind":"passage"},{"id":"RAW_QGOH_PQ_2:P0036","text":"PROPOSITIO XXI","kind":"passage"},{"id":"RAW_QGOH_PQ_2:P0037","text":"Nemo potest cupere beatum esse, bene agere, et bene vivere, qui simul non cupiat esse, agere, et vivere, hoc est, actu existere.","kind":"passage"},{"id":"RAW_QGOH_PQ_2:P0038","text":"DEMONSTRATIO","kind":"passage"},{"id":"RAW_QGOH_PQ_2:P0039","text":"Hujus Propositionis demonstratio, seu potius res ipsa, per se patet, et etiam ex Cupiditatis definitione. Est enim cupiditas (per 1 Aff. Defin.) beate seu bene vivendi, agendi, etc., ipsa hominis essentia, hoc est (per Prop. 7 p. III) conatus, quo unusquisque suum esse conservare conatur. Ergo nemo potest cupere, etc. Q. E. D.","kind":"passage"},{"id":"RAW_QGOH_PQ_2:P0040","text":"PROPOSITIO XXII","kind":"passage"},{"id":"RAW_QGOH_PQ_2:P0041","text":"Nulla virtus potest prior hac (nempe conatu sese conservandi) concipi.","kind":"passage"},{"id":"RAW_QGOH_PQ_2:P0042","text":"DEMONSTRATIO","kind":"passage"},{"id":"RAW_QGOH_PQ_2:P0043","text":"Conatus sese conservandi est ipsa rei essentia (per Prop. 7 p. III). Si igitur aliqua virtus posset hac, nempe hoc conatu, prior concipi, conciperetur ergo (per Defin. 8 hujus) ipsa rei essentia se ipsa prior, quod (ut per se notum) est absurdum. Ergo nulla virtus, etc. Q. E. D.","kind":"passage"},{"id":"RAW_QGOH_PQ_2:P0044","text":"COROLLARIUM","kind":"passage"},{"id":"RAW_QGOH_PQ_2:P0045","text":"Conatus sese conservandi primum et unicum virtutis est fundamentum. Nam hoc principio nullum aliud potest prius concipi (per Prop. præced.), et absque ipso (per Prop. 21 hujus) nulla virtus potest concipi.","kind":"passage"},{"id":"RAW_QGOH_PQ_2:P0046","text":"PROPOSITIO XXIII","kind":"passage"},{"id":"RAW_QGOH_PQ_2:P0047","text":"Homo, quatenus ad aliquid agendum determinatur ex eo, quod ideas habet inadæquatas, non potest absolute dici, ex virtute agere ; sed tantum quatenus determinatur ex eo, quod intelligit.","kind":"passage"},{"id":"RAW_QGOH_PQ_2:P0048","text":"DEMONSTRATIO","kind":"passage"},{"id":"RAW_QGOH_PQ_2:P0049","text":"Quatenus homo ad agendum determinatur ex eo, quod inadæquatas habet ideas eatenus (per Prop. 1 p. III) patitur, hoc est (per Defin. 1 et 2 p. III) aliquid agit, quod per solam ejus essentiam non potest percipi, hoc est (per Defin. 8 hujus) quod ex ipsius virtute non sequitur. At quatenus ad aliquid agendum determinatur ex eo, quod intelligit, eatenus (per eandem Prop. 1 p. III) agit, hoc est (per Defin. 2 p. III) aliquid agit, quod per solam ipsius essentiam percipitur, sive (per Defin. 8 hujus) quod ex ipsius virtute adæquate sequitur. Q. E. D.","kind":"passage"},{"id":"RAW_QGOH_PQ_2:P0050","text":"PROPOSITIO XXIV","kind":"passage"},{"id":"RAW_QGOH_PQ_2:P0051","text":"Ex virtute absolute agere nihil aliud in nobis est, quam ex ductu Rationis agere, vivere, suum esse conservare (hæc tria idem significant) ex fundamento proprium utile quærendi.","kind":"passage"},{"id":"RAW_QGOH_PQ_2:P0052","text":"DEMONSTRATIO","kind":"passage"},{"id":"RAW_QGOH_PQ_2:P0053","text":"Ex virtute absolute agere, nihil aliud est (per Defin. 8 hujus), quam ex legibus propriæ naturæ agere. At nos eatenus tantummodo agimus, quatenus intelligimus (per Prop. 3 p. III) ; ergo ex virtute agere, nihil aliud in nobis est, quam ex ductu Rationis agere, vivere, suum esse conservare idque (per Coroll. Prop. 22 hujus) ex fundamento suum utile quærendi. Q. E. D.","kind":"passage"},{"id":"RAW_QGOH_PQ_2:P0054","text":"PROPOSITIO XXV","kind":"passage"},{"id":"RAW_QGOH_PQ_2:P0055","text":"Nemo suum esse alterius rei causa conservare conatur.","kind":"passage"},{"id":"RAW_QGOH_PQ_2:P0056","text":"DEMONSTRATIO","kind":"passage"},{"id":"RAW_QGOH_PQ_2:P0057","text":"Conatus, quo unaquæque res in suo esse perseverare conatur, sola ipsius rei essentia definitur (per Prop. 7 p. III) ; eaque sola data, non autem ex alterius rei essentia, necessario sequitur (per Prop. 6 p. III), ut unusquisque suum esse conservare conetur. Patet præterea hæc Propositio ex Coroll. Prop. 22 hujus Partis. Nam si homo alterius rei causa suum esse conservare conaretur, tum res illa primum esset virtutis fundamentum (ut per se notum) quod (per prædictum Coroll.) est absurdum. Ergo nemo suum esse, etc. Q. E. D.","kind":"passage"},{"id":"RAW_QGOH_PQ_2:P0058","text":"PROPOSITIO XXVI","kind":"passage"},{"id":"RAW_QGOH_PQ_2:P0059","text":"Quicquid ex Ratione conamur, nihil aliud est quam intelligere ; nec Mens, quatenus Ratione utitur, aliud sibi utile esse judicat, nisi id, quod ad intelligendum conducit.","kind":"passage"},{"id":"RAW_QGOH_PQ_2:P0060","text":"DEMONSTRATIO","kind":"passage"},{"id":"RAW_QGOH_PQ_2:P0061","text":"Conatus sese conservandi nihil est præter ipsius rei essentiam (per Prop. 7 p. III), quæ, quatenus talis existit, vim habere concipitur ad perseverandum in existendo (per Prop. 6 p. III), et ea agendum, quæ ex data sua natura necessario sequuntur (vide Defin. Appetitus in Schol. Prop. 9 p. III). At Rationis essentia nihil aliud est quam Mens nostra, quatenus clare et distincte intelligit (vide ejus Defin. in 2 Schol. Prop. 40 p. II). Ergo (per Prop. 40 p. II) quicquid ex Ratione conamur, nihil aliud est quam intelligere. Deinde quoniam hic Mentis conatus, quo Mens, quatenus ratiocinatur, suum esse conatur conservare, nihil aliud est quam intelligere (per primam partem hujus) ; est ergo hic intelligendi conatus (per Coroll. Prop. 22 hujus) primum et unicum virtutis fundamentum ; nec alicujus finis causa (per Prop.","kind":"passage"},{"id":"RAW_QGOH_PQ_2:P0062","text":"25 hujus) res intelligere conabimur : sed contra Mens, quatenus ratiocinatur, nihil sibi bonum esse concipere poterit nisi id, quod ad intelligendum conducit (per Defin. 1 hujus). Q. E. D.","kind":"passage"},{"id":"RAW_QGOH_PQ_2:P0063","text":"PROPOSITIO XXVII","kind":"passage"},{"id":"RAW_QGOH_PQ_2:P0064","text":"Nihil certo scimus bonum aut malum esse, nisi id, quod ad intelligendum revera conducit, vel quod impedire potest, quo minus intelligamus.","kind":"passage"},{"id":"RAW_QGOH_PQ_2:P0065","text":"DEMONSTRATIO","kind":"passage"},{"id":"RAW_QGOH_PQ_2:P0066","text":"Mens, quatenus ratiocinatur, nihil aliud appetit quam intelligere, nec aliud sibi utile esse judicat nisi id, quod ad intelligendum conducit (per Prop. præced.). At mens (per Prop. 41 et 43 p. II, cujus etiam Schol. vide) rerum certitudinem non habet, nisi quatenus ideas habet adæquatas, sive (quod per Schol. 2 Prop. 40 p. II idem est) quatenus ratiocinatur. Ergo nihil certo scimus bonum esse nisi id, quod ad intelligendum revera conducit ; et contra id malum, quod impedire potest, quo minus intelligamus. Q. E. D.","kind":"passage"},{"id":"RAW_QGOH_PQ_2:P0067","text":"PROPOSITIO XXVIII","kind":"passage"},{"id":"RAW_QGOH_PQ_2:P0068","text":"Summum Mentis bonum est Dei cognitio, et Summa Mentis virtus Deum cognoscere.","kind":"passage"},{"id":"RAW_QGOH_PQ_2:P0069","text":"DEMONSTRATIO","kind":"passage"},{"id":"RAW_QGOH_PQ_2:P0070","text":"Summum, quod Mens intelligere potest, Deus est, hoc est (per Defin. 6 p. I) Ens absolute infinitum, et sine quo (per Prop. 15 p. I) nihil esse neque concipi potest ; adeoque (per Prop. 26 et 27 hujus) summum Mentis utile, sive (per Defin. 1 hujus) bonum, est Dei cognitio. Deinde Mens, quatenus intelligit, eatenus tantum agit (per Prop. 1 et 3 p. III), et eatenus tantum (per Prop. 23 hujus) potest absolute dici, quod ex virtute agit. Est igitur Mentis absoluta virtus intelligere. At summum, quod Mens intelligere potest, Deus est (ut jamjam demonstravimus) ; Ergo Mentis summa virtus est Deum intelligere seu cognoscere. Q. E. D.","kind":"passage"},{"id":"RAW_QGOH_PQ_2:P0071","text":"PROPOSITIO XXIX","kind":"passage"},{"id":"RAW_QGOH_PQ_2:P0072","text":"Res quæcunque singularis, cujus natura a nostra prorsus est diversa nostram agendi potentiam nec juvare nec coercere potest, et absolute res nulla potest nobis bona aut mala esse, nisi commune aliquid nobiscum habeat.","kind":"passage"},{"id":"RAW_QGOH_PQ_2:P0073","text":"DEMONSTRATIO","kind":"passage"},{"id":"RAW_QGOH_PQ_2:P0074","text":"Cujuscunque rei singularis, et consequenter (per Coroll. Prop. 10 p. II) hominis, potentia, qua existit et operatur, non determinatur nisi ab alia re singulari (per Prop. 28 p. I), cujus natura (per Prop. 6 p. II) per idem attributum debet intelligi, per quod natura humana concipitur. Nostra igitur agendi potentia, quomodocunque ea concipiatur, determinari, et consequenter juvari vel coerceri, potest potentia alterius rei singularis, quæ aliquid commune nobiscum habet, et non potentia rei cujus natura a nostra prorsus est diversa ; et quia id bonum aut malum vocamus, quod causa est Lætitiæ aut Tristitiæ (per Prop. 8 hujus), hoc est (per Schol. Prop. 11 p. III) quod nostram agendi potentiam auget vel minuit, juvat vel coercet ; ergo res, cujus natura a nostra prorsus est diversa, nobis neque bona neque mala esse potest. Q. E. D.","kind":"passage"},{"id":"RAW_QGOH_PQ_2:P0075","text":"PROPOSITIO XXX","kind":"passage"},{"id":"RAW_QGOH_PQ_2:P0076","text":"Res nulla per id, quod cum nostra natura commune habet, potest esse mala, sed quatenus nobis mala est, eatenus est nobis contraria.","kind":"passage"},{"id":"RAW_QGOH_PQ_2:P0077","text":"DEMONSTRATIO","kind":"passage"},{"id":"RAW_QGOH_PQ_2:P0078","text":"Id malum vocamus, quod causa est Tristitiæ (per Prop. 8 hujus) hoc est (per ejus Definit., quam vide in Schol. Prop. 11 p. III), quod nostram agendi potentiam minuit vel coercet. Si igitur res aliqua per id, quod nobiscum habet commune, nobis esset mala ; posset ergo res id ipsum, quod nobiscum commune habet, minuere vel coercere, quod (per Prop. 4 p. III) est absurdum. Nulla igitur res per id, quod nobiscum commune habet, potest nobis esse mala, sed contra quatenus mala est, hoc est (ut jamjam ostendimus), quatenus nostram agendi potentiam minuere vel coercere potest, eatenus (per Prop. 5 p. III) nobis est contraria. Q. E. D.","kind":"passage"},{"id":"RAW_QGOH_PQ_2:P0079","text":"PROPOSITIO XXXI","kind":"passage"},{"id":"RAW_QGOH_PQ_2:P0080","text":"Quatenus res aliqua cum nostra natura convenit, eatenus necessario bona est.","kind":"passage"},{"id":"RAW_QGOH_PQ_2:P0081","text":"DEMONSTRATIO","kind":"passage"},{"id":"RAW_QGOH_PQ_2:P0082","text":"Quatenus enim res aliqua cum nostra natura convenit, non potest (per Prop. præced.) esse mala. Erit ergo necessario vel bona vel indifferens. Si hoc ponatur, nempe quod neque bona sit neque mala ; nihil ergo (per Defin. 1 hujus) ex ipsius natura sequetur, quod nostræ naturæ conservationi inservit, hoc est (per hypothesin) quod ipsius rei naturæ conservationi inservit ; sed hoc est absurdum (per Prop. 6 p. III), erit ergo, quatenus cum nostra natura convenit, necessario bona. Q. E. D.","kind":"passage"},{"id":"RAW_QGOH_PQ_2:P0083","text":"COROLLARIUM","kind":"passage"},{"id":"RAW_QGOH_PQ_2:P0084","text":"Hinc sequitur, quod, quo res aliqua magis cum nostra natura convenit, eo nobis est utilior seu magis bona, et contra, quo res aliqua nobis est utilior, eatenus cum nostra natura magis convenit. Nam quatenus cum nostra natura non convenit, erit necessario a nostra natura diversa, vel eidem contraria. Si diversa, tum (per Prop. 29 hujus) neque bona neque mala esse poterit ; si autem contraria, erit ergo etiam ei contraria, quæ cum nostra natura convenit, hoc est (per Prop. præced.) contraria bono seu mala. Nihil igitur, nisi quatenus cum nostra natura convenit, potest esse bonum, atque adeo, quo res aliqua magis cum nostra natura convenit, eo est utilior et contra. Q. E. D.","kind":"passage"},{"id":"RAW_QGOH_PQ_2:P0085","text":"PROPOSITIO XXXII","kind":"passage"},{"id":"RAW_QGOH_PQ_2:P0086","text":"Quatenus homines passionibus sunt obnoxii, non possunt eatenus dici quod natura conveniant.","kind":"passage"},{"id":"RAW_QGOH_PQ_2:P0087","text":"DEMONSTRATIO","kind":"passage"},{"id":"RAW_QGOH_PQ_2:P0088","text":"Quæ natura convenire dicuntur, potentia convenire intelliguntur (per Prop. 7 p. III) ; non autem impotentia seu negatione, et consequenter (vide Schol. Prop. 3 p. III) neque etiam passione ; quare homines, quatenus passionibus sunt obnoxii, non possunt dici, quod natura conveniant. Q. E. D.","kind":"passage"},{"id":"RAW_QGOH_PQ_2:P0089","text":"SCHOLIUM","kind":"passage"},{"id":"RAW_QGOH_PQ_2:P0090","text":"Res etiam per se patet ; qui enim ait, album et nigrum in eo solummodo convenire, quod neutrum sit rubrum, is absolute affirmat, album et nigrum nulla in re convenire. Sic etiam si quis ait, lapidem et hominem in hoc tantum convenire, quod uterque sit finitus, impotens, vel quod ex necessitate suæ naturæ non existit, vel denique quod a potentia causarum externarum indefinite superatur : is omnino affirmat lapidem et hominem nulla in re convenire ; quæ enim in sola negatione, sive in eo, quod non habent, conveniunt, ea revera nulla in re conveniunt.","kind":"passage"},{"id":"RAW_QGOH_PQ_2:P0091","text":"PROPOSITIO XXXIII","kind":"passage"},{"id":"RAW_QGOH_PQ_2:P0092","text":"Homines natura discrepare possunt, quatenus affectibus, qui passiones sunt, conflictantur ; et eatenus etiam unus idemque homo varius est et inconstans.","kind":"passage"},{"id":"RAW_QGOH_PQ_2:P0093","text":"DEMONSTRATIO","kind":"passage"},{"id":"RAW_QGOH_PQ_2:P0094","text":"Affectuum natura seu essentia non potest per solam nostram essentiam seu naturam explicari (per Defin. 1 et 2 p. III) ; sed potentia, hoc est (per Prop. 7 p. III) natura, causarum externarum, cum nostra comparata, definiri debet ; unde fit, ut uniuscujusque affectus tot species dentur, quot sunt species objectorum, a quibus afficimur (vide Prop. 56 p. III) et ut homines ab uno eodemque objecto diversimode afficiantur (vide Prop. 51 p. III), atque eatenus natura discrepent ; et denique ut unus idemque homo (per eandem Prop. 51 p. III) erga idem objectum diversimode afficiatur, atque eatenus varius sit, etc. Q. E. D.","kind":"passage"},{"id":"RAW_QGOH_PQ_2:P0095","text":"PROPOSITIO XXXIV","kind":"passage"},{"id":"RAW_QGOH_PQ_2:P0096","text":"Quatenus homines affectibus, qui passiones sunt, conflictantur, possunt invicem esse contrarii.","kind":"passage"},{"id":"RAW_QGOH_PQ_2:P0097","text":"DEMONSTRATIO","kind":"passage"},{"id":"RAW_QGOH_PQ_2:P0098","text":"Homo ex. gr. Petrus potest esse causa, ut Paulus contristetur, propterea quod aliquid habet simile rei, quam Paulus odit (per Prop. 16 p. III) ; vel propterea quod Petrus solus re aliqua potitur, quam ipse Paulus etiam amat (vide Prop. 32 p. III cum ejusdem Schol.), vel ob alias causas (harum præcipuas vide in Schol. Prop. 55 p. III) ; atque adeo inde fiet (per Defin. 7 Affect.), ut Paulus Petrum odio habeat : et consequenter facile fiet (per Prop. 40 p. III cum ejus Schol.), ut Petrus Paulum contra odio habeat, atque adeo (per Prop. 39 p. III) ut invicem malum inferre conentur, hoc est (per Prop. 30 hujus), ut invicem sint contrarii. At affectus Tristitiæ semper passio est (per Prop. 59 p. III) ; ergo homines, quatenus conflictantur affectibus, qui passiones sunt, possunt invicem esse contrarii. Q. E. D.","kind":"passage"},{"id":"RAW_QGOH_PQ_2:P0099","text":"SCHOLIUM","kind":"passage"},{"id":"RAW_QGOH_PQ_2:P0100","text":"Dixi, quod Paulus odio Petrum habeat, quia imaginatur, id eundem possidere, quod ipse Paulus etiam amat ; unde prima fronte videtur sequi, quod hi duo ex eo, quod idem amant, et consequenter ex eo, quod natura conveniunt, sibi invicem damno sint ; atque adeo, si hoc verum est, falsæ essent Propositio 30 et 31 hujus Partis. Sed si rem æqua lance examinare velimus, hæc omnia convenire omnino videbimus. Nam hi duo non sunt invicem molesti, quatenus natura conveniunt, hoc est quatenus uterque idem amat, sed quatenus ab invicem discrepant. Nam quatenus uterque idem amat, eo ipso utriusque amor fovetur (per Prop. 31 p. III), hoc est (per Defin. 6 Affect.), eo ipso utriusque Lætitia fovetur. Quare longe abest, ut quatenus idem amant, et natura conveniunt, invicem molesti sint. Sed hujus rei causa, ut dixi, nulla alia est, quam quia natura discrepare supponuntur.","kind":"passage"},{"id":"RAW_QGOH_PQ_2:P0101","text":"Supponimus namque, Petrum ideam habere rei amatæ jam possessæ, et Paulum contra ideam rei amatæ amissæ. Unde fit ut hic Tristitia, et ille contra Lætitia afficiatur, atque eatenus invicem contrarii sint. Et ad hunc modum ostendere facile possumus, reliquas odii causas ab hoc solo pendere, quod homines natura discrepant, et non ab eo, in quo conveniunt.","kind":"passage"},{"id":"RAW_QGOH_PQ_2:P0102","text":"PROPOSITIO XXXV","kind":"passage"},{"id":"RAW_QGOH_PQ_2:P0103","text":"Quatenus homines ex ductu Rationis vivunt, eatenus tantum natura semper necessario conveniunt.","kind":"passage"},{"id":"RAW_QGOH_PQ_2:P0104","text":"DEMONSTRATIO","kind":"passage"},{"id":"RAW_QGOH_PQ_2:P0105","text":"Quatenus homines affectibus, qui passiones sunt, conflictantur, possunt esse natura diversi (per Prop. 33 hujus) et invicem contrarii (per Prop. præced.). Sed eatenus homines tantum agere dicuntur, quatenus ex ductu Rationis vivunt (per Prop. 3 p. III) ; atque adeo quicquid ex humana natura, quatenus Ratione definitur, sequitur, id (per Defin. 2 p. III) per solam humanam naturam, tanquam per proximam suam causam, debet intelligi. Sed quia unusquisque ex suæ naturæ legibus id appetit, quod bonum, et id amovere conatur, quod malum esse judicat (per Prop. 19 hujus) ; et cum præterea id, quod ex dictamine Rationis bonum aut malum esse judicamus, necessario bonum aut malum sit (per Prop. 41 p. II) ; Ergo homines, quatenus ex ductu Rationis vivunt, eatenus tantum ea necessario agunt, quæ humanæ naturæ et consequenter unicuique homini necessario bona sunt, hoc est (per Coroll. Prop.","kind":"passage"},{"id":"RAW_QGOH_PQ_2:P0106","text":"31 hujus) quæ cum natura uniuscujusque hominis conveniunt ; atque adeo homines etiam inter se quatenus ex ductu Rationis vivunt, necessario semper conveniunt. Q. E. D.","kind":"passage"},{"id":"RAW_QGOH_PQ_2:P0107","text":"COROLLARIUM I","kind":"passage"},{"id":"RAW_QGOH_PQ_2:P0108","text":"Nihil singulare in rerum Natura datur, quod homini sit utilius quam homo, qui ex ductu Rationis vivit. Nam id homini utilissimum est, quod cum sua natura maxime convenit (per Coroll. Prop. 31 hujus), hoc est (ut per se notum) homo. At homo ex legibus suæ naturæ absolute agit, quando ex ductu Rationis vivit (per Defin. 2 p. III), et eatenus tantum cum natura alterius hominis necessario semper convenit (per Prop. præced.) ; ergo homini nihil inter res singulares utilius datur quam homo, etc. Q. E. D.","kind":"passage"},{"id":"RAW_QGOH_PQ_2:P0109","text":"COROLLARIUM II","kind":"passage"}],"sha256":"cd94957ef02b48dae76ac1f722c9bccd230be2f4459c72191da38a4d30e70094","language_code":"la","language_name":"Latina","translations":[{"id":"SOURCE_TR_20261015T013838615628Z_4481454a","language_code":"en","language_name":"English","title":"Part Four - On Human Bondage, or the Powers of the Affects · passage 2","source_sha256":"cd94957ef02b48dae76ac1f722c9bccd230be2f4459c72191da38a4d30e70094","sha256":"adf45a64d42e673f356888293c29b04a50a7f935d73e99119e00ca76e2915924","rendition_type":"machine","review_status":"machine","model":"gpt-5.6-terra","created":"2026-10-15T01:38:38.615677+00:00","paragraphs":[{"id":"RAW_QGOH_PQ_2:P0001","text":"Desire, which arises from a true knowledge of good and evil, can be extinguished or restrained by many other Desires, which arise from the affects by which we are assailed.","kind":"passage","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_QGOH_PQ_2:P0001"]},{"id":"RAW_QGOH_PQ_2:P0002","text":"DEMONSTRATION","kind":"passage","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_QGOH_PQ_2:P0002"]},{"id":"RAW_QGOH_PQ_2:P0003","text":"From a true knowledge of good and evil, insofar as this knowledge (by Prop. 8 of this Part) is an affect, Desire necessarily arises (by Def. 1 of the Affects), which is greater insofar as the affect from which it arises is greater (by Prop. 37, Part III). But because this Desire (by hypothesis) arises from our truly understanding something, it follows that it is in us insofar as we act (by Prop. 1, Part III), and therefore must be understood through our essence alone (by Def. 2, Part III); and consequently (by Prop. 7, Part III), its force and increase must be defined by human power alone. Further, the Desires which arise from the affects by which we are assailed are greater insofar as these affects are more vehement; and therefore their force and increase must also (by Prop. 5 of this Part) be defined by the power of external causes, which, if compared with ours, indefinitely surpasses our power (by Prop.","kind":"passage","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_QGOH_PQ_2:P0003"]},{"id":"RAW_QGOH_PQ_2:P0004","text":"3 of this Part); and therefore Desires which arise from similar affects can be more vehement than that which arises from a true knowledge of good and evil, and consequently (by Prop. 7 of this Part) will be able to restrain or extinguish it. Q. E. D.","kind":"passage","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_QGOH_PQ_2:P0004"]},{"id":"RAW_QGOH_PQ_2:P0005","text":"PROPOSITION XVI","kind":"passage","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_QGOH_PQ_2:P0005"]},{"id":"RAW_QGOH_PQ_2:P0006","text":"Desire, which arises from the knowledge of good and evil, insofar as this knowledge concerns the future, can more easily be restrained or extinguished by Desire for things which are pleasant in the present.","kind":"passage","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_QGOH_PQ_2:P0006"]},{"id":"RAW_QGOH_PQ_2:P0007","text":"DEMONSTRATION","kind":"passage","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_QGOH_PQ_2:P0007"]},{"id":"RAW_QGOH_PQ_2:P0008","text":"An affect toward a thing which we imagine to be future is more remiss than toward a present one (by the Corollary to Prop. 9 of this Part). But Desire which arises from a true knowledge of good and evil, even though this knowledge is concerned with things which are good in the present, can be extinguished or restrained by some rash Desire (by the preceding Prop., whose demonstration is universal); therefore Desire which arises from the same knowledge, insofar as it concerns the future, can more easily be restrained or extinguished, etc. Q. E. D.","kind":"passage","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_QGOH_PQ_2:P0008"]},{"id":"RAW_QGOH_PQ_2:P0009","text":"PROPOSITION XVII","kind":"passage","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_QGOH_PQ_2:P0009"]},{"id":"RAW_QGOH_PQ_2:P0010","text":"Desire which arises from a true knowledge of good and evil, insofar as this knowledge is concerned with contingent things, can still much more easily be restrained by Desire for things which are present.","kind":"passage","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_QGOH_PQ_2:P0010"]},{"id":"RAW_QGOH_PQ_2:P0011","text":"DEMONSTRATION","kind":"passage","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_QGOH_PQ_2:P0011"]},{"id":"RAW_QGOH_PQ_2:P0012","text":"This Proposition is demonstrated in the same way as the preceding Proposition, from the Corollary to Prop. 12 of this Part.","kind":"passage","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_QGOH_PQ_2:P0012"]},{"id":"RAW_QGOH_PQ_2:P0013","text":"SCHOLIUM","kind":"passage","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_QGOH_PQ_2:P0013"]},{"id":"RAW_QGOH_PQ_2:P0014","text":"By these things I believe that I have shown the reason why men are moved more by opinion than by true Reason, and why a true knowledge of good and evil arouses commotions of the mind, and often yields to every kind of lust; whence arose that saying of the Poet: “I see and approve the better things, I follow the worse.” Ecclesiastes too seems to have had the same thing in mind when he said: “He who increases knowledge increases sorrow.” And I say these things not in order to infer from them that it is preferable to be ignorant than to know, or that in moderating the affects the intelligent man differs in nothing from the fool, but because it is necessary to know both the impotence and the power of our nature, so that we may determine what Reason can do in moderating the affects, and what it cannot do; and I said that in this Part I would treat only of human impotence. For I have resolved to treat separately of the power of reason over the affects.","kind":"passage","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_QGOH_PQ_2:P0014"]},{"id":"RAW_QGOH_PQ_2:P0015","text":"PROPOSITION XVIII","kind":"passage","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_QGOH_PQ_2:P0015"]},{"id":"RAW_QGOH_PQ_2:P0016","text":"Desire which arises from Joy is, other things being equal, stronger than Desire which arises from Sadness.","kind":"passage","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_QGOH_PQ_2:P0016"]},{"id":"RAW_QGOH_PQ_2:P0017","text":"DEMONSTRATION","kind":"passage","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_QGOH_PQ_2:P0017"]},{"id":"RAW_QGOH_PQ_2:P0018","text":"Desire is the very essence of man (by Def. 1 of the Affects), that is (by Prop. 7, Part III), the striving by which a man strives to persevere in his being. Therefore Desire which arises from Joy is aided or increased by the very affect of Joy (by the Definition of Joy, which see in the Scholium to Prop. 11, Part III); but that which, on the contrary, arises from Sadness is diminished or restrained by the very affect of Sadness (by the same Scholium); and therefore the force of Desire which arises from Joy must be defined by human power together with the power of the external cause, whereas that which arises from Sadness must be defined by human power alone; and consequently the former is stronger than the latter. Q. E. D.","kind":"passage","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_QGOH_PQ_2:P0018"]},{"id":"RAW_QGOH_PQ_2:P0019","text":"SCHOLIUM","kind":"passage","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_QGOH_PQ_2:P0019"]},{"id":"RAW_QGOH_PQ_2:P0020","text":"In these few words I have explained the causes of human impotence and inconstancy, and why men do not observe the precepts of Reason. It now remains for me to show what it is that Reason prescribes to us, and which affects agree with the rules of human Reason, and which, on the contrary, are contrary to them. But before I begin to demonstrate these things in our extended Geometrical order, I should like first to set forth here briefly the dictates of Reason themselves, so that what I think may be more easily perceived by everyone. Since Reason demands nothing contrary to Nature, it therefore demands that everyone love himself, seek his own advantage, that which is truly advantageous, and desire everything that truly leads man to greater perfection; and absolutely, that everyone endeavor, as far as he can, to preserve his own being. This is indeed as necessarily true as that the whole is greater than its part (see Prop. 4, Part III). Next, since virtue (by Def.","kind":"passage","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_QGOH_PQ_2:P0020"]},{"id":"RAW_QGOH_PQ_2:P0021","text":"8 of this Part) is nothing other than acting according to the laws of one's own nature, and no one endeavors (by Prop. 7, Part III) to preserve his being except according to the laws of his own nature; from this it follows, first, that the foundation of virtue is the very striving to preserve one's own being, and that happiness consists in this, that a man is able to preserve his being. Second, it follows that virtue is to be desired for its own sake, and that nothing can be given which is more excellent than it, or more useful to us, for the sake of which it ought to be desired. Finally, third, it follows that those who kill themselves are weak in mind, and are utterly conquered by external causes repugnant to their nature.","kind":"passage","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_QGOH_PQ_2:P0021"]},{"id":"RAW_QGOH_PQ_2:P0022","text":"Further, from Postulate 4 of Part II, it follows that we can never bring it about that we need nothing outside ourselves in order to preserve our being, and that we live in such a way as to have no commerce with things that are outside us; and, moreover, if we consider our Mind, certainly our intellect would be more imperfect if the Mind were alone and understood nothing besides itself. Therefore many things outside us are given which are useful to us, and which for that reason are to be desired. Among these, none can be conceived more excellent than those which agree wholly with our nature. For if, for example, two individuals of exactly the same nature are joined to one another, they compose an individual twice as powerful as either one.","kind":"passage","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_QGOH_PQ_2:P0022"]},{"id":"RAW_QGOH_PQ_2:P0023","text":"Therefore nothing is more useful to man than man; nothing, I say, can men desire more excellent for preserving their being than that all should agree in all things in such a way that the Minds and Bodies of all compose, as it were, one Mind and one Body, and that all together endeavor, as far as they can, to preserve their being, and all together seek for themselves the common advantage of all; from which it follows that men who are governed by Reason, that is, men who seek their own advantage under the guidance of Reason, desire nothing for themselves that they do not wish for the rest of mankind, and are consequently just, faithful, and honorable.","kind":"passage","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_QGOH_PQ_2:P0023"]},{"id":"RAW_QGOH_PQ_2:P0024","text":"These are those dictates of Reason which I had proposed to set forth here in a few words, before beginning to demonstrate them in a more extended order; and I did this so that, if possible, I might win the attention of those who believe that this principle—namely, that everyone is bound to seek his own advantage—is the foundation of impiety, and not of virtue and piety. Having thus briefly shown that the matter stands otherwise, I proceed to demonstrate it by the same method by which we have progressed hitherto.","kind":"passage","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_QGOH_PQ_2:P0024"]},{"id":"RAW_QGOH_PQ_2:P0025","text":"PROPOSITION XIX","kind":"passage","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_QGOH_PQ_2:P0025"]},{"id":"RAW_QGOH_PQ_2:P0026","text":"Each person necessarily desires or shuns, from the laws of his own nature, that which he judges to be good or evil.","kind":"passage","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_QGOH_PQ_2:P0026"]},{"id":"RAW_QGOH_PQ_2:P0027","text":"DEMONSTRATION","kind":"passage","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_QGOH_PQ_2:P0027"]},{"id":"RAW_QGOH_PQ_2:P0028","text":"The knowledge of good and evil is (by Prop. 8 of this Part) the very affect of Joy or Sadness, insofar as we are conscious of it; and consequently (by Prop. 28, Part III) each person necessarily desires that which he judges to be good, and, on the contrary, shuns that which he judges to be evil. But this desire is nothing but the very essence or nature of man (by the Def. of Appetite, which see in the Schol. of Prop. 9, Part III, and Def. 1 of the Affects). Therefore each person, from the laws of his nature alone, necessarily desires or shuns this or that, etc. Q. E. D.","kind":"passage","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_QGOH_PQ_2:P0028"]},{"id":"RAW_QGOH_PQ_2:P0029","text":"PROPOSITION XX","kind":"passage","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_QGOH_PQ_2:P0029"]},{"id":"RAW_QGOH_PQ_2:P0030","text":"The more each person strives, and is able, to seek his own advantage, that is, to preserve his own being, the more is he endowed with virtue; and conversely, insofar as each person neglects to preserve his own advantage, that is, his own being, he is to that extent powerless.","kind":"passage","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_QGOH_PQ_2:P0030"]},{"id":"RAW_QGOH_PQ_2:P0031","text":"DEMONSTRATION","kind":"passage","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_QGOH_PQ_2:P0031"]},{"id":"RAW_QGOH_PQ_2:P0032","text":"Virtue is human power itself, which is defined by the essence of man alone (by Def. 8 of this Part), that is (by Prop. 7, Part III), which is defined by the striving alone by which a man strives to persevere in his being. Therefore, the more each person strives, and is able, to preserve his own being, the more is he endowed with virtue; and consequently (by Props. 4 and 6, Part III), insofar as anyone neglects to preserve his own being, he is to that extent powerless. Q. E. D.","kind":"passage","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_QGOH_PQ_2:P0032"]},{"id":"RAW_QGOH_PQ_2:P0033","text":"SCHOLIUM","kind":"passage","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_QGOH_PQ_2:P0033"]},{"id":"RAW_QGOH_PQ_2:P0034","text":"Therefore no one, unless overcome by external causes contrary to his nature, neglects to seek his own advantage, or to preserve his own being. No one, I say, from the necessity of his own nature, but compelled by external causes, shuns food or kills himself; this can happen in many ways. For example, someone kills himself because he is compelled by another, who twists his right hand, with which he had by chance grasped a sword, and forces him to direct the blade toward his own heart; or because, at the command of a Tyrant, as Seneca was, he is compelled to open his veins, that is, he wishes to avoid a greater evil by a lesser; or finally because hidden external causes so dispose his imagination and so affect his Body that it assumes another nature contrary to the former, an idea of which cannot be given in the Mind (by Prop. 10, Part III).","kind":"passage","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_QGOH_PQ_2:P0034"]},{"id":"RAW_QGOH_PQ_2:P0035","text":"But that a man should strive, from the necessity of his own nature, not to exist, or to be changed into another form, is as impossible as that something should come from nothing, as anyone can see with moderate reflection.","kind":"passage","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_QGOH_PQ_2:P0035"]},{"id":"RAW_QGOH_PQ_2:P0036","text":"PROPOSITION XXI","kind":"passage","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_QGOH_PQ_2:P0036"]},{"id":"RAW_QGOH_PQ_2:P0037","text":"No one can desire to be blessed, to act well, and to live well, unless he at the same time desires to be, to act, and to live, that is, to exist actually.","kind":"passage","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_QGOH_PQ_2:P0037"]},{"id":"RAW_QGOH_PQ_2:P0038","text":"DEMONSTRATION","kind":"passage","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_QGOH_PQ_2:P0038"]},{"id":"RAW_QGOH_PQ_2:P0039","text":"The demonstration of this Proposition, or rather the matter itself, is evident through itself, and also from the definition of Desire. For the desire (by Def. 1 of the Affects) of living blessedly or well, of acting, etc., is the very essence of man, that is (by Prop. 7, Part III), the striving by which each person strives to preserve his own being. Therefore no one can desire, etc. Q. E. D.","kind":"passage","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_QGOH_PQ_2:P0039"]},{"id":"RAW_QGOH_PQ_2:P0040","text":"PROPOSITION XXII","kind":"passage","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_QGOH_PQ_2:P0040"]},{"id":"RAW_QGOH_PQ_2:P0041","text":"No virtue can be conceived prior to this one (namely, the striving to preserve oneself).","kind":"passage","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_QGOH_PQ_2:P0041"]},{"id":"RAW_QGOH_PQ_2:P0042","text":"DEMONSTRATION","kind":"passage","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_QGOH_PQ_2:P0042"]},{"id":"RAW_QGOH_PQ_2:P0043","text":"The striving to preserve oneself is the very essence of a thing (by Prop. 7, Part III). If, therefore, some virtue could be conceived prior to this, namely, to this striving, then (by Def. 8 of this Part) the very essence of the thing would be conceived as prior to itself, which (as is known through itself) is absurd. Therefore no virtue, etc. Q. E. D.","kind":"passage","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_QGOH_PQ_2:P0043"]},{"id":"RAW_QGOH_PQ_2:P0044","text":"COROLLARY","kind":"passage","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_QGOH_PQ_2:P0044"]},{"id":"RAW_QGOH_PQ_2:P0045","text":"The striving to preserve oneself is the first and only foundation of virtue. For no other principle can be conceived prior to this one (by the preceding Prop.), and without it (by Prop. 21 of this Part) no virtue can be conceived.","kind":"passage","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_QGOH_PQ_2:P0045"]},{"id":"RAW_QGOH_PQ_2:P0046","text":"PROPOSITION XXIII","kind":"passage","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_QGOH_PQ_2:P0046"]},{"id":"RAW_QGOH_PQ_2:P0047","text":"Insofar as a man is determined to do something by the fact that he has inadequate ideas, he cannot absolutely be said to act from virtue; but only insofar as he is determined by the fact that he understands.","kind":"passage","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_QGOH_PQ_2:P0047"]},{"id":"RAW_QGOH_PQ_2:P0048","text":"DEMONSTRATION","kind":"passage","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_QGOH_PQ_2:P0048"]},{"id":"RAW_QGOH_PQ_2:P0049","text":"Insofar as a man is determined to act by the fact that he has inadequate ideas, he is to that extent passive (by Prop. 1, Part III), that is (by Defs. 1 and 2, Part III), he does something which cannot be perceived through his essence alone, that is (by Def. 8 of this Part), which does not follow from his power. But insofar as he is determined to do something by the fact that he understands, he is to that extent active (by the same Prop. 1, Part III), that is (by Def. 2, Part III), he does something which is perceived through his essence alone, or (by Def. 8 of this Part) which follows adequately from his power. Q. E. D.","kind":"passage","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_QGOH_PQ_2:P0049"]},{"id":"RAW_QGOH_PQ_2:P0050","text":"PROPOSITION XXIV","kind":"passage","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_QGOH_PQ_2:P0050"]},{"id":"RAW_QGOH_PQ_2:P0051","text":"To act absolutely from virtue is nothing else in us than to act, to live, to preserve one's being (these three signify the same thing) under the guidance of Reason, on the basis of seeking one's own advantage.","kind":"passage","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_QGOH_PQ_2:P0051"]},{"id":"RAW_QGOH_PQ_2:P0052","text":"DEMONSTRATION","kind":"passage","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_QGOH_PQ_2:P0052"]},{"id":"RAW_QGOH_PQ_2:P0053","text":"To act absolutely from virtue is nothing else (by Def. 8 of this Part) than to act according to the laws of one's own nature. But we act only insofar as we understand (by Prop. 3, Part III); therefore, to act from virtue is nothing else in us than to act, to live, and to preserve one's being under the guidance of Reason, and this (by the Corollary to Prop. 22 of this Part) on the basis of seeking one's own advantage. Q. E. D.","kind":"passage","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_QGOH_PQ_2:P0053"]},{"id":"RAW_QGOH_PQ_2:P0054","text":"PROPOSITION XXV","kind":"passage","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_QGOH_PQ_2:P0054"]},{"id":"RAW_QGOH_PQ_2:P0055","text":"No one endeavors to preserve his being for the sake of another thing.","kind":"passage","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_QGOH_PQ_2:P0055"]},{"id":"RAW_QGOH_PQ_2:P0056","text":"DEMONSTRATION","kind":"passage","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_QGOH_PQ_2:P0056"]},{"id":"RAW_QGOH_PQ_2:P0057","text":"The endeavor by which each thing endeavors to persevere in its being is defined by the essence of the thing alone (by Prop. 7, Part III); and, given this essence alone, and not from the essence of another thing, it necessarily follows (by Prop. 6, Part III) that each one endeavors to preserve its being. Moreover, this Proposition is evident from the Corollary to Prop. 22 of this Part. For if a man endeavored to preserve his being for the sake of another thing, then that thing would be the primary foundation of virtue (as is self-evident), which (by the aforesaid Corollary) is absurd. Therefore no one preserves his being, etc. Q. E. D.","kind":"passage","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_QGOH_PQ_2:P0057"]},{"id":"RAW_QGOH_PQ_2:P0058","text":"PROPOSITION XXVI","kind":"passage","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_QGOH_PQ_2:P0058"]},{"id":"RAW_QGOH_PQ_2:P0059","text":"Whatever we endeavor from Reason is nothing other than to understand; nor does the Mind, insofar as it uses Reason, judge anything to be useful to itself except what conduces to understanding.","kind":"passage","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_QGOH_PQ_2:P0059"]},{"id":"RAW_QGOH_PQ_2:P0060","text":"DEMONSTRATION","kind":"passage","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_QGOH_PQ_2:P0060"]},{"id":"RAW_QGOH_PQ_2:P0061","text":"The endeavor to preserve itself is nothing beyond the essence of the thing itself (by Prop. 7, Part III), which, insofar as it exists as such, is conceived to have a power of persevering in existing (by Prop. 6, Part III), and of doing those things which necessarily follow from its given nature (see the Definition of Appetite in the Scholium to Prop. 9, Part III). But the essence of Reason is nothing other than our Mind insofar as it understands clearly and distinctly (see its Definition in the second Scholium to Prop. 40, Part II). Therefore (by Prop. 40, Part II), whatever we endeavor from Reason is nothing other than to understand. Further, since this endeavor of the Mind, by which the Mind, insofar as it reasons, endeavors to preserve its being, is nothing other than to understand (by the first part of this Proposition), this endeavor to understand is therefore (by the Corollary to Prop. 22 of this Part) the primary and only foundation of virtue; nor shall we endeavor to understand things for the sake of any end (by Prop.","kind":"passage","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_QGOH_PQ_2:P0061"]},{"id":"RAW_QGOH_PQ_2:P0062","text":"25 of this Part); but, on the contrary, the Mind, insofar as it reasons, will be able to conceive nothing to be good for itself except what conduces to understanding (by Def. 1 of this Part). Q. E. D.","kind":"passage","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_QGOH_PQ_2:P0062"]},{"id":"RAW_QGOH_PQ_2:P0063","text":"PROPOSITION XXVII","kind":"passage","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_QGOH_PQ_2:P0063"]},{"id":"RAW_QGOH_PQ_2:P0064","text":"We know with certainty that nothing is good or evil except what truly conduces to understanding, or what can prevent us from understanding.","kind":"passage","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_QGOH_PQ_2:P0064"]},{"id":"RAW_QGOH_PQ_2:P0065","text":"DEMONSTRATION","kind":"passage","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_QGOH_PQ_2:P0065"]},{"id":"RAW_QGOH_PQ_2:P0066","text":"The Mind, insofar as it reasons, desires nothing other than to understand, nor does it judge anything useful to itself except what conduces to understanding (by the preceding Prop.). But the Mind (by Props. 41 and 43, Part II, see also the Scholium to the latter) has no certainty of things except insofar as it has adequate ideas, or (which, by the second Scholium to Prop. 40, Part II, is the same) insofar as it reasons. Therefore, we know with certainty that nothing is good except what truly conduces to understanding; and, conversely, that is evil which can prevent us from understanding. Q. E. D.","kind":"passage","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_QGOH_PQ_2:P0066"]},{"id":"RAW_QGOH_PQ_2:P0067","text":"PROPOSITION XXVIII","kind":"passage","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_QGOH_PQ_2:P0067"]},{"id":"RAW_QGOH_PQ_2:P0068","text":"The supreme good of the Mind is the knowledge of God, and the supreme virtue of the Mind is to know God.","kind":"passage","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_QGOH_PQ_2:P0068"]},{"id":"RAW_QGOH_PQ_2:P0069","text":"DEMONSTRATION","kind":"passage","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_QGOH_PQ_2:P0069"]},{"id":"RAW_QGOH_PQ_2:P0070","text":"The highest thing that the Mind can understand is God, that is (by Def. 6, Part I), a being absolutely infinite, and without whom (by Prop. 15, Part I) nothing can be or be conceived; and therefore (by Props. 26 and 27 of this Part) the Mind's highest advantage, or (by Def. 1 of this Part) good, is the knowledge of God. Further, the Mind, insofar as it understands, is active only to that extent (by Props. 1 and 3, Part III), and only to that extent (by Prop. 23 of this Part) can absolutely be said to act from virtue. Therefore, the absolute virtue of the Mind is to understand. But the highest thing the Mind can understand is God (as we have just now demonstrated); therefore the supreme virtue of the Mind is to understand, or to know, God. Q. E. D.","kind":"passage","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_QGOH_PQ_2:P0070"]},{"id":"RAW_QGOH_PQ_2:P0071","text":"PROPOSITION XXIX","kind":"passage","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_QGOH_PQ_2:P0071"]},{"id":"RAW_QGOH_PQ_2:P0072","text":"Any singular thing whose nature is entirely different from ours can neither assist nor restrain our power of acting; and absolutely no thing can be good or evil for us unless it has something in common with us.","kind":"passage","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_QGOH_PQ_2:P0072"]},{"id":"RAW_QGOH_PQ_2:P0073","text":"DEMONSTRATION","kind":"passage","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_QGOH_PQ_2:P0073"]},{"id":"RAW_QGOH_PQ_2:P0074","text":"The power by which any singular thing, and consequently (by Corollary to Prop. 10, Part II) a human being, exists and acts is determined only by another singular thing (by Prop. 28, Part I), whose nature (by Prop. 6, Part II) must be understood through the same attribute through which human nature is conceived. Therefore our power of acting, however it may be conceived, can be determined, and consequently assisted or restrained, by the power of another singular thing which has something in common with us, and not by the power of a thing whose nature is wholly different from ours; and since we call good or evil that which is the cause of Joy or Sadness (by Prop. 8 of this Part), that is (by the Scholium to Prop. 11, Part III), that which increases or diminishes, assists or restrains, our power of acting; therefore a thing whose nature is wholly different from ours can be neither good nor evil for us. Q. E. D.","kind":"passage","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_QGOH_PQ_2:P0074"]},{"id":"RAW_QGOH_PQ_2:P0075","text":"PROPOSITION XXX","kind":"passage","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_QGOH_PQ_2:P0075"]},{"id":"RAW_QGOH_PQ_2:P0076","text":"No thing can be evil through that which it has in common with our nature; but insofar as it is evil for us, it is contrary to us.","kind":"passage","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_QGOH_PQ_2:P0076"]},{"id":"RAW_QGOH_PQ_2:P0077","text":"DEMONSTRATION","kind":"passage","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_QGOH_PQ_2:P0077"]},{"id":"RAW_QGOH_PQ_2:P0078","text":"We call evil that which is the cause of Sadness (by Prop. 8 of this Part), that is (by its Definition, which see in the Scholium to Prop. 11, Part III), that which diminishes or restrains our power of acting. If, therefore, any thing were evil for us through that which it has in common with us, that thing itself which it has in common with us could diminish or restrain [it], which (by Prop. 4, Part III) is absurd. Therefore no thing can be evil for us through that which it has in common with us; but, on the contrary, insofar as it is evil, that is (as we have just shown), insofar as it can diminish or restrain our power of acting, it is (by Prop. 5, Part III) contrary to us. Q. E. D.","kind":"passage","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_QGOH_PQ_2:P0078"]},{"id":"RAW_QGOH_PQ_2:P0079","text":"PROPOSITION XXXI","kind":"passage","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_QGOH_PQ_2:P0079"]},{"id":"RAW_QGOH_PQ_2:P0080","text":"Insofar as a thing agrees with our nature, it is necessarily good.","kind":"passage","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_QGOH_PQ_2:P0080"]},{"id":"RAW_QGOH_PQ_2:P0081","text":"DEMONSTRATION","kind":"passage","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_QGOH_PQ_2:P0081"]},{"id":"RAW_QGOH_PQ_2:P0082","text":"For insofar as any thing agrees with our nature, it cannot (by the preceding Prop.) be evil. Therefore it will necessarily be either good or indifferent. If the latter is posited, namely, that it is neither good nor evil, then nothing (by Def. 1 of this Part) will follow from its nature which serves the preservation of our nature, that is (by the hypothesis), which serves the preservation of the nature of the thing itself; but this is absurd (by Prop. 6, Part III). Therefore, insofar as it agrees with our nature, it is necessarily good. Q. E. D.","kind":"passage","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_QGOH_PQ_2:P0082"]},{"id":"RAW_QGOH_PQ_2:P0083","text":"COROLLARY","kind":"passage","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_QGOH_PQ_2:P0083"]},{"id":"RAW_QGOH_PQ_2:P0084","text":"From this it follows that, the more a thing agrees with our nature, the more useful, or the better, it is for us; and conversely, the more useful a thing is to us, the more it agrees with our nature. For insofar as it does not agree with our nature, it will necessarily either differ from our nature or be contrary to it. If it differs, then (by Prop. 29 of this Part) it can be neither good nor evil; but if it is contrary, it will therefore also be contrary to that which agrees with our nature, that is (by the preceding Prop.), contrary to the good, or evil. Therefore nothing can be good except insofar as it agrees with our nature; and consequently, the more a thing agrees with our nature, the more useful it is, and conversely. Q. E. D.","kind":"passage","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_QGOH_PQ_2:P0084"]},{"id":"RAW_QGOH_PQ_2:P0085","text":"PROPOSITION XXXII","kind":"passage","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_QGOH_PQ_2:P0085"]},{"id":"RAW_QGOH_PQ_2:P0086","text":"Insofar as human beings are subject to passions, they cannot, to that extent, be said to agree in nature.","kind":"passage","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_QGOH_PQ_2:P0086"]},{"id":"RAW_QGOH_PQ_2:P0087","text":"DEMONSTRATION","kind":"passage","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_QGOH_PQ_2:P0087"]},{"id":"RAW_QGOH_PQ_2:P0088","text":"Things which are said to agree in nature are understood to agree in power (by Prop. 7, Part III), but not in impotence or negation, and consequently (see the Scholium to Prop. 3, Part III) not in passion either; therefore, insofar as human beings are subject to passions, they cannot be said to agree in nature. Q. E. D.","kind":"passage","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_QGOH_PQ_2:P0088"]},{"id":"RAW_QGOH_PQ_2:P0089","text":"SCHOLIUM","kind":"passage","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_QGOH_PQ_2:P0089"]},{"id":"RAW_QGOH_PQ_2:P0090","text":"The matter is also evident in itself. For one who says that white and black agree only in this, that neither is red, absolutely affirms that white and black agree in nothing. So too, if someone says that a stone and a human being agree only in this, that each is finite, impotent, or does not exist from the necessity of its own nature, or, finally, is indefinitely surpassed by the power of external causes, he altogether affirms that a stone and a human being agree in nothing; for things which agree only in a negation, or in that which they do not have, in reality agree in nothing.","kind":"passage","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_QGOH_PQ_2:P0090"]},{"id":"RAW_QGOH_PQ_2:P0091","text":"PROPOSITION XXXIII","kind":"passage","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_QGOH_PQ_2:P0091"]},{"id":"RAW_QGOH_PQ_2:P0092","text":"Human beings can differ in nature insofar as they are affected by affects which are passions; and to that extent one and the same human being is variable and inconstant.","kind":"passage","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_QGOH_PQ_2:P0092"]},{"id":"RAW_QGOH_PQ_2:P0093","text":"DEMONSTRATION","kind":"passage","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_QGOH_PQ_2:P0093"]},{"id":"RAW_QGOH_PQ_2:P0094","text":"The nature, or essence, of the affects cannot be explained through our essence or nature alone (by Defs. 1 and 2, Part III); rather, the power, that is (by Prop. 7, Part III), the nature, of external causes, compared with ours, must be defined. Hence it comes about that there are as many species of each affect as there are species of objects by which we are affected (see Prop. 56, Part III), and that human beings are affected in different ways by one and the same object (see Prop. 51, Part III), and to that extent differ in nature; and finally that one and the same human being (by the same Prop. 51, Part III) is affected in different ways toward the same object, and to that extent is variable, etc. Q. E. D.","kind":"passage","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_QGOH_PQ_2:P0094"]},{"id":"RAW_QGOH_PQ_2:P0095","text":"PROPOSITION XXXIV","kind":"passage","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_QGOH_PQ_2:P0095"]},{"id":"RAW_QGOH_PQ_2:P0096","text":"Insofar as human beings are affected by affects which are passions, they can be contrary to one another.","kind":"passage","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_QGOH_PQ_2:P0096"]},{"id":"RAW_QGOH_PQ_2:P0097","text":"DEMONSTRATION","kind":"passage","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_QGOH_PQ_2:P0097"]},{"id":"RAW_QGOH_PQ_2:P0098","text":"For example, a man, Peter, can be the cause of Paul's being saddened, because he has something similar to a thing which Paul hates (by Prop. 16, Part III); or because Peter alone possesses some thing which Paul himself also loves (see Prop. 32, Part III, with its Scholium); or for other causes (for the chief of these see the Scholium to Prop. 55, Part III); and thus it will follow (by Def. 7 of the Affects) that Paul will hate Peter; and consequently it will easily follow (by Prop. 40, Part III, with its Scholium) that Peter, in return, will hate Paul, and hence (by Prop. 39, Part III) that they will endeavour to inflict evil upon one another, that is (by Prop. 30 of this Part), that they will be contrary to one another. But the affect of Sadness is always a passion (by Prop. 59, Part III); therefore men, insofar as they are troubled by affects which are passions, can be contrary to one another. Q. E. D.","kind":"passage","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_QGOH_PQ_2:P0098"]},{"id":"RAW_QGOH_PQ_2:P0099","text":"SCHOLIUM","kind":"passage","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_QGOH_PQ_2:P0099"]},{"id":"RAW_QGOH_PQ_2:P0100","text":"I said that Paul hates Peter because he imagines that the latter possesses that which Paul himself also loves; whence at first sight it seems to follow that these two, from the fact that they love the same thing, and consequently from the fact that they agree in nature, are harmful to one another; and therefore, if this were true, Propositions 30 and 31 of this Part would be false. But if we should wish to examine the matter with an even balance, we shall see that all these things agree entirely. For these two are not troublesome to one another insofar as they agree in nature, that is, insofar as each loves the same thing, but insofar as they differ from one another. For insofar as each loves the same thing, by that very fact the love of each is fostered (by Prop. 31, Part III), that is (by Def. 6 of the Affects), by that very fact the Joy of each is fostered. Wherefore it is far from being the case that, insofar as they love the same thing and agree in nature, they are troublesome to one another. But the cause of this matter, as I said, is no other than that they are supposed to differ in nature.","kind":"passage","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_QGOH_PQ_2:P0100"]},{"id":"RAW_QGOH_PQ_2:P0101","text":"For we suppose that Peter has an idea of the beloved thing as already possessed, and Paul, on the contrary, an idea of the beloved thing as lost. Whence it follows that the latter is affected with Sadness, and the former, on the contrary, with Joy, and that to that extent they are contrary to one another. And in this way we can easily show that the remaining causes of hatred depend upon this alone, namely, that men differ in nature, and not upon that in which they agree.","kind":"passage","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_QGOH_PQ_2:P0101"]},{"id":"RAW_QGOH_PQ_2:P0102","text":"PROPOSITION XXXV","kind":"passage","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_QGOH_PQ_2:P0102"]},{"id":"RAW_QGOH_PQ_2:P0103","text":"Insofar as men live under the guidance of Reason, they necessarily always agree in nature.","kind":"passage","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_QGOH_PQ_2:P0103"]},{"id":"RAW_QGOH_PQ_2:P0104","text":"DEMONSTRATION","kind":"passage","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_QGOH_PQ_2:P0104"]},{"id":"RAW_QGOH_PQ_2:P0105","text":"Insofar as men are troubled by affects which are passions, they can be different in nature (by Prop. 33 of this Part) and contrary to one another (by the preceding Prop.). But men are said to act only insofar as they live under the guidance of Reason (by Prop. 3, Part III); and therefore whatever follows from human nature insofar as it is defined by Reason must (by Def. 2, Part III) be understood through human nature alone, as through its proximate cause. But since each, from the laws of his nature, seeks that which he judges to be good, and endeavours to remove that which he judges to be evil (by Prop. 19 of this Part); and since moreover that which, by the dictate of Reason, we judge to be good or evil is necessarily good or evil (by Prop. 41, Part II); therefore men, insofar as they live under the guidance of Reason, thereby alone necessarily do those things which are necessarily good for human nature and consequently for each man, that is (by the Corollary of Prop.","kind":"passage","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_QGOH_PQ_2:P0105"]},{"id":"RAW_QGOH_PQ_2:P0106","text":"31 of this Part), those things which agree with the nature of each man; and therefore men also, insofar as they live under the guidance of Reason, necessarily always agree with one another. Q. E. D.","kind":"passage","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_QGOH_PQ_2:P0106"]},{"id":"RAW_QGOH_PQ_2:P0107","text":"COROLLARY I","kind":"passage","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_QGOH_PQ_2:P0107"]},{"id":"RAW_QGOH_PQ_2:P0108","text":"Nothing singular is given in the Nature of things that is more useful to man than a man who lives under the guidance of Reason. For that is most useful to man which agrees most with his nature (by the Corollary of Prop. 31 of this Part), that is (as is known through itself), man. But a man acts absolutely from the laws of his nature when he lives under the guidance of Reason (by Def. 2, Part III), and only to that extent does he necessarily always agree with the nature of another man (by the preceding Prop.); therefore, among singular things, nothing more useful than a man is given to man, etc. Q. E. D.","kind":"passage","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_QGOH_PQ_2:P0108"]},{"id":"RAW_QGOH_PQ_2:P0109","text":"COROLLARY II","kind":"passage","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_QGOH_PQ_2:P0109"]}],"source_digest":"bd82ca2128b96c13eb438c36f2776422951614e64ee2f928dcb5aa92516a3e06","source_language_code":"la","original_title":"Pars quarta - De servitute humana seu de affectuum viribus · passage 2"}],"passage_basis":"frozen-grounding","original_title":"Pars quarta - De servitute humana seu de affectuum viribus · passage 2","title_language_code":"en"},{"id":"RAW_QGOH_PQ_3","title":"Part Four - On Human Bondage, or the Powers of the Affects · passage 3","order":16,"chapter_id":"CH_QGOH_PQ_3","text":"Cum maxime unusquisque homo suum sibi utile quærit, tum maxime homines sunt sibi invicem utiles. Nam quo magis unusquisque suum utile quærit, et se conservare conatur, eo magis virtute præditus est (per Prop. 20 hujus), sive, quod idem est (per Defin. 8 hujus), eo majore potentia præditus est ad agendum ex suæ naturæ legibus, hoc est (per Prop. 3 p. III) ad vivendum ex ductu Rationis. At homines tum maxime natura conveniunt, cum ex ductu Rationis vivunt (per Prop. præced.) ; ergo (per præc. Coroll.) tum maxime homines erunt sibi invicem utiles, cum maxime unusquisque suum utile sibi quærit. Q. E. D.\n\nSCHOLIUM\n\nQuæ modo ostendimus, ipsa etiam experientia quotidie tot tamque luculentis testimoniis testatur, ut omnibus fere in ore sit : hominem homini Deum esse. Fit tamen raro, ut homines ex ductu Rationis vivant ; sed cum iis ita comparatum est, ut plerumque invidi, atque invicem molesti sint. At nihilominus vitam solitariam vix transigere queunt, ita ut plerisque illa definitio, quod homo sit animal sociale, valde arriserit ; et revera res ita se habet, ut ex hominum communi societate multo plura commoda oriantur, quam damna. Rideant igitur, quantum velint, res humanas Satyrici, easque detestentur Theologi, et laudent, quantum possunt, Melancholici vitam incultam et agrestem, homines contemnant, et admirentur bruta ; experientur tamen, homines mutuo auxilio ea, quibus indigent, multo facilius sibi parare, et non nisi junctis viribus pericula, quæ ubique imminent, vitare posse ; ut jam taceam, quod multo præstabilius sit, et cognitione nostra magis dignum, hominum, quam brutorum facta contemplari. Sed de his alias prolixius.\n\nPROPOSITIO XXXVI\n\nSummum bonum eorum, qui virtutem sectantur, omnibus commune est, eoque omnes æque gaudere possunt.\n\nDEMONSTRATIO\n\nEx virtute agere est ex ductu Rationis agere (per Prop. 24 hujus) et quicquid ex Ratione conamur agere, est intelligere (per Prop. 26 hujus) ; atque adeo (per Prop. 28 hujus) summum bonum eorum, qui virtutem sectantur, est Deum cognoscere, hoc est (per Prop. 47 p. II et ejusdem Schol.) bonum, quod omnibus hominibus commune est, et ab omnibus hominibus, quatenus ejusdem sunt naturæ, possideri æque potest. Q. E. D.\n\nSCHOLIUM\n\nSi quis autem roget, quid si summum bonum eorum, qui virtutem sectantur, non esset omnibus commune ? an non inde, ut supra (vide Prop. 34 hujus), sequeretur, quod homines, qui ex ductu Rationis vivunt, hoc est (per Prop. 35 hujus) homines, quatenus natura conveniunt, essent invicem contrarii ? Is hoc sibi responsum habeat, non ex accidenti, sed ex ipsa natura Rationis oriri, ut hominis summum bonum omnibus sit commune, nimirum quia ex ipsa humana essentia, quatenus Ratione definitur, deducitur ; et quia homo nec esse nec concipi posset, si potestatem non haberet gaudendi hoc summo bono. Pertinet namque (per Prop. 47 p. II) ad Mentis humanæ essentiam, adæquatam habere cognitionem æternæ et infinitæ essentiæ Dei.\n\nPROPOSITIO XXXVII\n\nBonum, quod unusquisque, qui sectatur virtutem, sibi appetit, reliquis hominibus etiam cupiet, et eo magis, quo majorem Dei habuerit cognitionem.\n\nDEMONSTRATIO\n\nHomines quatenus ex ductu Rationis vivunt, sunt homini utilissimi (per Coroll. 1 Prop. 35 hujus) ; atque adeo (per Prop. 19 hujus) ex ductu Rationis conabimur necessario efficere, ut homines ex ductu Rationis vivant. At bonum, quod unusquisque, qui ex Rationis dictamine vivit, hoc est (per Prop. 24 hujus) qui virtutem sectatur, sibi appetit, est intelligere (per Prop. 26 hujus) ; ergo bonum, quod unusquisque, qui virtutem sectatur, sibi appetit, reliquis hominibus etiam cupiet. Deinde Cupiditas, quatenus ad Mentem refertur, est ipsa Mentis essentia (per 1 Affect. Defin.) ; Mentis autem essentia in cognitione consistit (per Prop. 11 p. II), quæ Dei cognitionem involvit (per Prop. 47 p. II), et sine qua (per Prop. 15 p. I), nec esse nec concipi potest ; adeoque quo Mentis essentia majorem Dei cognitionem involvit, eo Cupiditas, qua is, qui virtutem sectatur, bonum, quod sibi appetit, alteri cupit, etiam major erit. Q. E. D.\n\nALITER\n\nBonum, quod homo sibi appetit et amat, constantius amabit, si viderit, alios idem amare (per Prop. 31 p. III) ; atque adeo (per Coroll. ejusdem Prop.) conabitur, ut reliqui idem ament ; et quia hoc bonum (per Prop. præced.) omnibus commune est, eoque omnes gaudere possunt, conabitur ergo (per eandem rationem), ut omnes eodem gaudeant, et (per Prop. 37 p. III) eo magis, quo hoc bono magis fruetur. Q. E. D.\n\nSCHOLIUM I\n\nQui ex solo affectu conatur, ut reliqui ament, quod ipse amat, et ut reliqui ex suo ingenio vivant, solo impetu agit, et ideo odiosus est, præcipue iis, quibus alia placent, quique propterea etiam student, et eodem impetu conantur, ut reliqui contra ex ipsorum ingenio vivant. Deinde quoniam summum, quod homines ex affectu appetunt, bonum sæpe tale est, ut unus tantum ejus possit esse compos, hinc fit, ut qui amant, mente sibi non constent, et dum laudes rei, quam amant, narrare gaudent, timeant credi. At qui reliquos conatur Ratione ducere, non impetu, sed humaniter et benigne agit, et sibi mente maxime constat. Porro quicquid cupimus et agimus, cujus causa sumus, quatenus Dei habemus ideam, sive quatenus Deum cognoscimus, ad Religionem refero. Cupiditatem autem bene faciendi, quæ ex eo ingeneratur, quod ex Rationis ductu vivimus, Pietatem voco. Cupiditatem deinde, qua homo, qui ex ductu Rationis vivit, tenetur, ut reliquos sibi amicitia jungat, Honestatem voco et id honestum, quod homines, qui ex ductu Rationis vivunt, laudant et id contra turpe, quod conciliandæ amicitiæ repugnat. Differentia deinde inter veram virtutem et impotentiam facile ex supra dictis percipitur ; nempe quod vera virtus nihil aliud sit, quam ex solo Rationis ductu vivere ; atque adeo impotentia in hoc solo consistit, quod homo a rebus, quæ extra ipsum sunt, duci se patiatur, et ab iis ad ea agendum determinetur, quæ rerum externarum communis constitutio, non autem ea, quæ ipsa ipsius natura, in se sola considerata, postulat. Atque hæc illa sunt, quæ in Scholio Propositionis 18 hujus Partis demonstrare promisi, ex quibus apparet, legem illam de non mactandis brutis magis vana superstitione et muliebri misericordia, quam sana Ratione fundatam esse. Docet quidem ratio nostrum utile quærendi necessitudinem cum hominibus jungere ; sed non cum brutis, aut rebus, quarum natura a natura humana est diversa ; sed idem jus, quod illa in nos habent, nos in ea habere. Imo quia uniuscujusque jus virtute seu potentia uniuscujusque definitur, longe majus homines in bruta, quam hæc in homines jus habent. Nec tamen nego bruta sentire ; sed nego, quod propterea non liceat nostræ utilitati consulere, et iisdem ad libitum uti, eademque tractare prout nobis magis convenit ; quandoquidem nobiscum natura non conveniunt, et eorum affectus ab affectibus humanis sunt natura diversi (vide Schol. Prop. 57 p. III). Superest ut explicem quid justum, quid injustum, quid peccatum, et quid denique meritum sit. Præter hæc civitatis etiam quænam sint fundamenta ostendam. Sed de his vide seq. Scholium.\n\nSCHOLIUM II\n\nIn appendice Partis primæ explicare promisi, quid laus et vituperium, quid meritum et peccatum, quid justum et injustum sit. Laudem et vituperium quod attinet, in Scholio Propositionis 29 partis III explicui ; de reliquis autem hic jam erit dicendi locus. Sed prius pauca de statu hominis naturali et civili dicenda sunt.\n\nExistit unusquisque summo Naturæ jure, et consequenter summo Naturæ jure unusquisque ea agit, quæ ex suæ naturæ necessitate sequuntur ; atque adeo summo Naturæ jure unusquisque judicat, quid bonum, quid malum sit, suæque utilitati ex suo ingenio consulit (vide Prop. 19 et 20 hujus), seseque vindicat (vide Coroll. 2 Prop. 40 p. III), et id, quod amat, conservare, et id, quod odio habet, destruere conatur (vide Prop. 28 p. III). Quod si homines ex ductu Rationis viverent, potiretur unusquisque (per Coroll. 1 Prop. 35 hujus) hoc suo jure absque ullo alterius damno. Sed quia affectibus sunt obnoxii (per Coroll. Prop. 4 hujus), qui potentiam seu virtutem humanam longe superant (per Prop. 6 hujus) ideo sæpe diversi trahuntur (per Prop. 33 hujus), atque sibi invicem sunt contrarii (per Prop. 34 hujus), mutuo dum auxilio indigent (per Schol. Prop. 35 hujus). Ut igitur homines concorditer vivere et sibi auxilio esse possint, necesse est, ut jure suo naturali cedant, et se invicem securos reddant, se nihil acturos, quod possit in alterius damnum cedere. Qua autem ratione hoc fieri possit, ut scilicet homines, qui affectibus necessario sunt obnoxii (per Coroll. Prop. 4 hujus), atque inconstantes et varii (per Prop. 33 hujus), possint se invicem securos reddere, et fidem invicem habere, patet ex Propositione 7 hujus partis et Propositione 39 partis III. Nempe quod nullus affectus coerceri potest, nisi affectu fortiore et contrario affectui coercendo, et quod unusquisque ab inferendo damno abstinet timore majoris damni. Hac igitur lege Societas firmari poterit, si modo ipsa sibi vindicet jus, quod unusquisque habet, sese vindicandi, et de bono et malo judicandi ; quæque adeo potestatem habeat communem vivendi rationem præscribendi, legesque ferendi, easque non Ratione, quæ affectus coercere nequit (per Schol. Prop. 17 hujus), sed minis firmandi. Hæc autem Societas, legibus et potestate sese conservandi firmata, Civitas appellatur, et qui ipsius jure defenduntur, Cives ; ex quibus facile intelligimus, nihil in statu naturali dari, quod ex omnium consensu bonum aut malum sit ; quandoquidem unusquisque, qui, in statu est naturali, suæ tantummodo utilitati consulit, et ex suo ingenio, et quatenus suæ utilitatis tantum habet rationem, quid bonum quidve malum sit, decernit, et nemini, nisi sibi soli, obtemperare lege ulla tenetur. Atque adeo in statu naturali peccatum concipi nequit ; at quidem in statu Civili, ubi et communi consensu decernitur, quid bonum quidve malum sit, et unusquisque Civitati obtemperare tenetur. Est itaque peccatum nihil aliud quam inobedientia, quæ propterea solo Civitatis jure punitur, et contra obedientia Civi meritum ducitur, quia eo ipso dignus judicatur, qui Civitatis commodis gaudeat. Deinde in statu naturali nemo ex communi consensu alicujus rei est Dominus, nec in Natura aliquid datur, quod possit dici hujus hominis esse, et non illius ; sed omnia omnium sunt ; ac proinde in statu naturali nulla potest concipi voluntas unicuique suum tribuendi, aut alicui id, quod ejus sit, eripiendi ; hoc est, in statu naturali nihil fit, quod justum aut injustum possit dici ; at quidem in statu civili, ubi ex communi consensu decernitur, quid hujus, quidve illius sit. Ex quibus apparet, justum et injustum, peccatum et meritum, notiones esse extrinsecas ; non autem attributa, quæ Mentis naturam explicent. Sed de his satis.\n\nPROPOSITIO XXXVIII\n\nId, quod Corpus humanum ita disponit, ut pluribus modis possit affici, vel quod idem aptum reddit ad corpora externa pluribus modis afficiendum, homini est utile ; et eo utilius, quo Corpus ab eo aptius redditur, ut pluribus modis afficiatur, aliaque corpora afficiat ; et contra id noxium est, quod Corpus ad hæc minus aptum reddit.\n\nDEMONSTRATIO\n\nQuo corpus ad hæc aptius redditur, eo Mens aptior ad percipiendum redditur (per Prop. 14 p. II) ; adeoque id, quod Corpus hac ratione disponit, aptumque ad hæc reddit, est necessario bonum seu utile (per Prop. 26 et 27 hujus), et eo utilius, quo Corpus ad hæc aptius potest reddere ; et contra (per eandem Prop. 14 p. II inversam, et Prop. 26 et 27 hujus) noxium, si corpus ad hæc minus aptum reddat. Q. E. D.\n\nPROPOSITIO XXXIX\n\nQuæ efficiunt, ut motus et quietis ratio, quam Corporis humani partes ad invicem habent, conservetur, bona sunt ; et ea contra mala, quæ efficiunt, ut Corporis humani partes aliam ad invicem motus et quietis habeant rationem.\n\nDEMONSTRATIO\n\nCorpus humanum indiget, ut conservetur, plurimis aliis corporibus (per Postul. 4 p. II). At id, quod formam humani Corporis constituit, in hoc consistit, quod ejus partes motus suos certa quadam Ratione sibi invicem communicent (per Defin. ante Lem. 4, quam vide post Prop. 13 p. II). Ergo quæ efficiunt, ut motus et quietis ratio, quam Corporis humani partes ad invicem habent, conservetur, eadem humani Corporis formam conservant, et consequenter efficiunt (per Post. 3 et 6 p. II), ut Corpus humanum multis modis affici, et ut idem corpora externa multis modis afficere possit, adeoque (per Prop. præced.) bona sunt. Deinde, quæ efficiunt, ut Corporis humani partes aliam motus et quietis rationem obtineant, eadem (per eandem Defin. p. II), efficiunt, ut corpus humanum aliam formam induat, hoc est (ut per se notum, et in fine præfationis hujus partis monuimus), ut Corpus humanum destruatur, et consequenter ut omnino ineptum reddatur, ne possit pluribus modis affici, ac proinde (per Prop. præc.) mala sunt. Q. E. D.\n\nSCHOLIUM\n\nQuantum hæc Menti obesse vel prodesse possunt, in Quinta Parte explicabitur. Sed hic notandum, quod Corpus tum mortem obire intelligam, quando ejus partes ita disponuntur, ut aliam motus et quietis rationem ad invicem obtineant. Nam negare non audeo, Corpus humanum, retenta sanguinis circulatione, et aliis, propter quæ Corpus vivere existimatur, posse nihilominus in aliam naturam a sua prorsus diversam mutari. Nam nulla ratio me cogit, ut statuam Corpus non mori, nisi mutetur in cadaver ; quin ipsa experientia aliud suadere videtur. Fit namque aliquando, ut homo tales patiatur mutationes, ut non facile eundem illum esse dixerim ; ut de quodam Hispano Poeta narrare audivi, qui morbo correptus fuerat, et quamvis ex eo convaluerit, mansit tamen præteritæ suæ vitæ tam oblitus, ut Fabulas et Tragœdias, quas fecerat, suas non crediderit esse ; et sane pro infante adulto haberi potuisset, si vernaculæ etiam linguæ fuisset oblitus. Et si hoc incredibile videtur quid de infantibus dicemus ? Quorum naturam homo provectæ ætatis a sua tam diversam esse credit, ut persuaderi non posset, se unquam infantem fuisse, nisi ex aliis de se conjecturam faceret. Sed ne superstitiosis materiam suppeditem movendi novas quæstiones, malo hæc in medio relinquere.\n\nPROPOSITIO XL\n\nQuæ ad hominum communem Societatem conducunt, sive quæ efficiunt, ut homines concorditer vivant, utilia sunt, et illa contra mala, quæ discordiam in Civitatem inducunt.\n\nDEMONSTRATIO\n\nNam quæ efficiunt, ut homines concorditer vivant, simul efficiunt, ut ex ductu Rationis vivant (per Prop. 35 hujus), atque adeo (per Prop. 26 et 27 hujus) bona sunt, et (per eandem rationem) illa contra mala sunt, quæ discordias concitant. Q. E. D.\n\nPROPOSITIO XLI\n\nLætitia directe mala non est, sed bona ; Tristitia autem contra directe est mala.\n\nDEMONSTRATIO\n\nLætitia (per Prop. 11 p. III cum ejusdem Schol.) est affectus, quo corporis agendi potentia augetur vel juvatur ; Tristitia autem contra est affectus, quo corporis agendi potentia minuitur vel coercetur ; adeoque (per Prop. 38 hujus) Lætitia directe bona est, etc. Q. E. D.\n\nPROPOSITIO XLII\n\nHilaritas excessum habere nequit, sed semper bona est ; et contra Melancholia semper mala.\n\nDEMONSTRATIO\n\nHilaritas (vide ejus Defin. in Schol. Prop. 11 p. III) est Lætitia, quæ quatenus ad Corpus refertur, in hoc consistit, quod Corporis omnes partes pariter sint affectæ ; hoc est (per Prop. 11 p. III) quod Corporis agendi potentia augetur vel juvatur, ita ut omnes ejus partes eandem ad invicem motus et quietis rationem obtineant ; atque adeo (per Prop. 39 hujus) Hilaritas semper est bona, nec excessum habere potest. At Melancholia (cujus etiam Defin. vide in eodem Schol. Prop. 11 p. III) est Tristitia, quæ quatenus ad Corpus refertur, in hoc consistit, quod Corporis agendi potentia absolute minuitur vel coercetur ; adeoque (per Prop. 38 hujus) semper est mala. Q. E. D.\n\nPROPOSITIO XLIII\n\nTitillatio excessum habere potest, et mala esse ; Dolor autem eatenus potest esse bonus, quatenus Titillatio seu Lætitia est mala.\n\nDEMONSTRATIO\n\nTitillatio est Lætitia, quæ, quatenus ad Corpus refertur, in hoc consistit quod una vel aliquot ejus partes præ reliquis afficiuntur (vide ejus Defin. in Schol. Prop. 11 p. III) ; cujus affectus potentia tanta esse potest, ut reliquas Corporis actiones superet (per Prop. 6 hujus), eique pertinaciter adhæreat, atque adeo impediat quo minus Corpus aptum sit, ut plurimis aliis modis afficiatur ; adeoque (per Prop. 38 hujus) mala esse potest. Deinde Dolor, qui contra Tristitia est, in se solo consideratus, non potest esse bonus (per Prop. 41 hujus). Verum quia ejus vis et incrementum definitur potentia causæ externæ cum nostra comparata (per Prop. 5 hujus) possumus ergo hujus affectus infinitos virium concipere gradus et modos (per Prop. 3 hujus) ; atque adeo eundem talem concipere qui Titillationem possit coercere, ut excessum non habeat, et eatenus (per primam partem Prop. hujus) efficere, ne Corpus minus aptum reddatur ; ac proinde eatenus erit bonus. Q. E. D.\n\nPROPOSITIO XLIV\n\nAmor et cupiditas excessum habere possunt.\n\nDEMONSTRATIO\n\nAmor est Lætitia (per Defin. 6 Affect.) concomitante idea causæ externæ ; Titillatio igitur (per Schol. Prop. 11 p. III) concomitante idea causæ externæ Amor est ; atque adeo Amor (per Prop. præced.) excessum habere potest. Deinde Cupiditas eo est major, quo affectus, ex quo oritur major est (per Prop. 37 p. III). Quare ut affectus (per Prop. 6 hujus) reliquas hominis actiones superare potest ; sic etiam Cupiditas, quæ ex eodem affectu oritur, reliquas Cupiditates superare, ac proinde eundem excessum habere poterit, quem in præcedenti Propositione Titillationem habere ostendimus. Q. E. D.\n\nSCHOLIUM\n\nHilaritas, quam bonam esse dixi, concipitur facilius quam observatur. Nam affectus, quibus quotidie conflictamur, referuntur plerumque ad aliquam Corporis partem, quæ præ reliquis afficitur ; ac proinde affectus ut plurimum excessum habent, et Mentem in sola unius objecti contemplatione ita detinent, ut de aliis cogitare nequeat ; et quamvis homines pluribus affectibus obnoxii sint, atque adeo rari reperiantur, qui semper uno eodemque affectu conflictentur, non desunt tamen, quibus unus idemque affectus pertinaciter adhæreat. Videmus enim homines aliquando ab uno objecto ita affici, ut quamvis præsens non sit, ipsum tamen coram habere credant ; quod quando homini non dormienti accidit, eundem delirare dicimus vel insanire ; nec minus insanire creduntur, qui Amore ardent, quique noctes atque dies solam amasiam vel meretricem somniant, quia risum movere solent. At cum avarus de nulla alia re, quam de lucro vel de nummis cogitet, et ambitiosus de gloria, etc., hi non creduntur delirare, quia molesti solent esse, et Odio digni æstimantur. Sed revera Avaritia, Ambitio, Libido, etc. delirii species sunt, quamvis inter morbos non numerentur.\n\nPROPOSITIO XLV\n\nOdium nunquam potest esse bonum.\n\nDEMONSTRATIO\n\nHominem, quem odimus, destruere conamur (per Prop. 39 p. III), hoc est (per Prop. 37 hujus) aliquid conamur, quod malum est ; Ergo, etc. Q. E. D.\n\nSCHOLIUM\n\nNota, me hic et in seqq. per Odium illud tantum intelligere, quod est erga homines.\n\nCOROLLARIUM I\n\nInvidia, Irrisio, Contemptus, Ira, Vindicta, et reliqui affectus, qui ad Odium referuntur vel ex eodem oriuntur, mali sunt ; quod etiam ex Prop. 39 p. III et Prop. 37 hujus patet.\n\nCOROLLARIUM II\n\nQuicquid ex eo, quod odio affecti sumus, appetimus, turpe et in Civitate injustum est. Quod etiam patet ex Prop. 39 p. III et ex Defin. turpis et injusti, quas vide in Scholiis Prop. 37 hujus.\n\nSCHOLIUM\n\nInter Irrisionem (quam in I Coroll. malam esse dixi) et risum magnam agnosco differentiam. Nam risus, ut et jocus, mera est Lætitia ; adeoque, modo excessum non habeat, per se bonus est (per Prop. 41 hujus). Nihil profecto nisi torva et tristis superstitio delectari prohibet. Nam qui magis decet famem et sitim extinguere, quam melancholiam expellere ? Mea hæc est ratio, et sic animum induxi meum. Nullum numen, nec alius, nisi invidus, mea impotentia et incommodo delectatur, nec nobis lacrimas, singultus, metum, et alia hujusmodi, quæ animi impotentis sunt signa, virtuti ducit ; sed contra, quo majore Lætitia afficimur, eo ad majorem perfectionem transimus, hoc est, eo nos magis de natura divina participare necesse est. Rebus itaque uti, et iis, quantum fieri potest, delectari (non quidem ad nauseam usque, nam hoc delectari non est), viri est sapientis. Viri, inquam, sapientis est, moderato et suavi cibo et potu se reficere et recreare, ut et odoribus, plantarum virentium amœnitate, ornatu, musica, ludis exercitatoriis, theatris, et aliis hujusmodi, quibus unusquisque absque ullo alterius damno uti potest. Corpus namque humanum ex plurimis diversæ naturæ partibus componitur, quæ continuo novo alimento indigent et vario, ut totum Corpus ad omnia, quæ ex ipsius natura sequi possunt, æque aptum sit, et consequenter ut Mens etiam æque apta sit ad plura simul intelligendum. Hoc itaque vivendi institutum et cum nostris principiis et cum communi praxi optime convenit ; quare si quæ alia, hæc vivendi ratio optima est, et omnibus modis commendanda ; nec opus est de his clarius neque prolixius agere.\n\nPROPOSITIO XLVI\n\nQui ex ductu Rationis vivit, quantum potest conatur, alterius in ipsum Odium, Iram, Contemptum, etc., Amore contra, sive Generositate compensare.\n\nDEMONSTRATIO\n\nOmnes Odii affectus mali sunt (per Coroll. 1 præc. Prop.) ; adeoque, qui ex ductu Rationis vivit, quantum potest conabitur efficere, ne Odii affectibus conflictetur (per Prop. 19 hujus), et consequenter (per Prop. 37 hujus) conabitur, ne etiam alius eosdem patiatur affectus. At Odium Odio reciproco augetur, et Amore contra extingui potest (per Prop. 43 p. III), ita ut Odium in Amorem transeat (per Prop. 44 p. III) ; Ergo qui ex ductu Rationis vivit ; alterius Odium, etc. Amore contra compensare conabitur, hoc est Generositate (cujus Defin. vide in Schol. Prop. 59 p. III). Q. E. D.\n\nSCHOLIUM\n\nQui injurias reciproco Odio vindicare vult, misere profecto vivit. At qui contra studet Odium Amore expugnare, ille sane lætus et secure pugnat, æque facile pluribus ac uni homini resistit, et fortunæ auxilio quam minime indiget. Quos vero vincit, ii læti cedunt, non quidem ex defectu, sed ex incremento virium ; quæ omnia adeo clare ex solis Amoris et Intellectus definitionibus sequuntur, ut opus non sit eadem sigillatim demonstrare.\n\nPROPOSITIO XLVII\n\nSpei et Metus affectus non possunt esse per se boni.\n\nDEMONSTRATIO\n\nSpei et Metus affectus sine Tristitia non dantur. Nam Metus est (per 13 Affect. Defin.) Tristitia, et Spes (vide Explicationem 12 et 13 Affect. Defin.) non datur sine Metu ; ac proinde (per Prop. 41 hujus) hi affectus non possunt esse per se boni, sed tantum quatenus Lætitiæ excessum coercere possunt (per Prop. 43 hujus). Q. E. D.\n\nSCHOLIUM\n\nHuc accedit, quod hi affectus cognitionis defectum et Mentis impotentiam indicant ; et hac de causa etiam Securitas, Desperatio, Gaudium et Conscientiæ morsus animi impotentis sunt signa. Nam, quamvis Securitas et Gaudium affectus sint Lætitiæ, Tristitiam tamen eosdem præcessisse supponunt, nempe Spem et Metum. Quo itaque magis ex ductu Rationis vivere conamur, eo magis Spe minus pendere, et Metu nosmet liberare, et fortunæ quantum possumus, imperare conamur, nostrasque actiones certo Rationis consilio dirigere.\n\nPROPOSITIO XLVIII\n\nAffectus Existimationis et Despectus semper mali sunt.\n\nDEMONSTRATIO\n\nHi enim affectus (per 21 et 22 Affect. Defin.) rationi repugnant ; adeoque (per Prop. 26 et 27 hujus) mali sunt. Q. E. D.\n\nPROPOSITIO XLIX\n\nExistimatio facile hominem, qui existimatur, superbum reddit.\n\nDEMONSTRATIO\n\nSi videmus aliquem de nobis plus justo præ amore sentire, facile gloriabimur (per Schol. Prop. 41 p. III), sive Lætitia afficiemur (per 30 Affect. Defin.) ; et id boni, quod de nobis prædicari audimus, facile credemus (per Prop. 25 p. III) ; atque adeo de nobis præ amore nostri plus justo sentiemus, hoc est (per Defin. 28 Affect.) facile superbiemus. Q. E. D.\n\nPROPOSITIO L","paragraphs":[{"id":"RAW_QGOH_PQ_3:P0001","text":"Cum maxime unusquisque homo suum sibi utile quærit, tum maxime homines sunt sibi invicem utiles. Nam quo magis unusquisque suum utile quærit, et se conservare conatur, eo magis virtute præditus est (per Prop. 20 hujus), sive, quod idem est (per Defin. 8 hujus), eo majore potentia præditus est ad agendum ex suæ naturæ legibus, hoc est (per Prop. 3 p. III) ad vivendum ex ductu Rationis. At homines tum maxime natura conveniunt, cum ex ductu Rationis vivunt (per Prop. præced.) ; ergo (per præc. Coroll.) tum maxime homines erunt sibi invicem utiles, cum maxime unusquisque suum utile sibi quærit. Q. E. D.","kind":"passage"},{"id":"RAW_QGOH_PQ_3:P0002","text":"SCHOLIUM","kind":"passage"},{"id":"RAW_QGOH_PQ_3:P0003","text":"Quæ modo ostendimus, ipsa etiam experientia quotidie tot tamque luculentis testimoniis testatur, ut omnibus fere in ore sit : hominem homini Deum esse. Fit tamen raro, ut homines ex ductu Rationis vivant ; sed cum iis ita comparatum est, ut plerumque invidi, atque invicem molesti sint. At nihilominus vitam solitariam vix transigere queunt, ita ut plerisque illa definitio, quod homo sit animal sociale, valde arriserit ; et revera res ita se habet, ut ex hominum communi societate multo plura commoda oriantur, quam damna.","kind":"passage"},{"id":"RAW_QGOH_PQ_3:P0004","text":"Rideant igitur, quantum velint, res humanas Satyrici, easque detestentur Theologi, et laudent, quantum possunt, Melancholici vitam incultam et agrestem, homines contemnant, et admirentur bruta ; experientur tamen, homines mutuo auxilio ea, quibus indigent, multo facilius sibi parare, et non nisi junctis viribus pericula, quæ ubique imminent, vitare posse ; ut jam taceam, quod multo præstabilius sit, et cognitione nostra magis dignum, hominum, quam brutorum facta contemplari. Sed de his alias prolixius.","kind":"passage"},{"id":"RAW_QGOH_PQ_3:P0005","text":"PROPOSITIO XXXVI","kind":"passage"},{"id":"RAW_QGOH_PQ_3:P0006","text":"Summum bonum eorum, qui virtutem sectantur, omnibus commune est, eoque omnes æque gaudere possunt.","kind":"passage"},{"id":"RAW_QGOH_PQ_3:P0007","text":"DEMONSTRATIO","kind":"passage"},{"id":"RAW_QGOH_PQ_3:P0008","text":"Ex virtute agere est ex ductu Rationis agere (per Prop. 24 hujus) et quicquid ex Ratione conamur agere, est intelligere (per Prop. 26 hujus) ; atque adeo (per Prop. 28 hujus) summum bonum eorum, qui virtutem sectantur, est Deum cognoscere, hoc est (per Prop. 47 p. II et ejusdem Schol.) bonum, quod omnibus hominibus commune est, et ab omnibus hominibus, quatenus ejusdem sunt naturæ, possideri æque potest. Q. E. D.","kind":"passage"},{"id":"RAW_QGOH_PQ_3:P0009","text":"SCHOLIUM","kind":"passage"},{"id":"RAW_QGOH_PQ_3:P0010","text":"Si quis autem roget, quid si summum bonum eorum, qui virtutem sectantur, non esset omnibus commune ? an non inde, ut supra (vide Prop. 34 hujus), sequeretur, quod homines, qui ex ductu Rationis vivunt, hoc est (per Prop. 35 hujus) homines, quatenus natura conveniunt, essent invicem contrarii ? Is hoc sibi responsum habeat, non ex accidenti, sed ex ipsa natura Rationis oriri, ut hominis summum bonum omnibus sit commune, nimirum quia ex ipsa humana essentia, quatenus Ratione definitur, deducitur ; et quia homo nec esse nec concipi posset, si potestatem non haberet gaudendi hoc summo bono. Pertinet namque (per Prop. 47 p. II) ad Mentis humanæ essentiam, adæquatam habere cognitionem æternæ et infinitæ essentiæ Dei.","kind":"passage"},{"id":"RAW_QGOH_PQ_3:P0011","text":"PROPOSITIO XXXVII","kind":"passage"},{"id":"RAW_QGOH_PQ_3:P0012","text":"Bonum, quod unusquisque, qui sectatur virtutem, sibi appetit, reliquis hominibus etiam cupiet, et eo magis, quo majorem Dei habuerit cognitionem.","kind":"passage"},{"id":"RAW_QGOH_PQ_3:P0013","text":"DEMONSTRATIO","kind":"passage"},{"id":"RAW_QGOH_PQ_3:P0014","text":"Homines quatenus ex ductu Rationis vivunt, sunt homini utilissimi (per Coroll. 1 Prop. 35 hujus) ; atque adeo (per Prop. 19 hujus) ex ductu Rationis conabimur necessario efficere, ut homines ex ductu Rationis vivant. At bonum, quod unusquisque, qui ex Rationis dictamine vivit, hoc est (per Prop. 24 hujus) qui virtutem sectatur, sibi appetit, est intelligere (per Prop. 26 hujus) ; ergo bonum, quod unusquisque, qui virtutem sectatur, sibi appetit, reliquis hominibus etiam cupiet. Deinde Cupiditas, quatenus ad Mentem refertur, est ipsa Mentis essentia (per 1 Affect. Defin.) ; Mentis autem essentia in cognitione consistit (per Prop. 11 p. II), quæ Dei cognitionem involvit (per Prop. 47 p. II), et sine qua (per Prop. 15 p.","kind":"passage"},{"id":"RAW_QGOH_PQ_3:P0015","text":"I), nec esse nec concipi potest ; adeoque quo Mentis essentia majorem Dei cognitionem involvit, eo Cupiditas, qua is, qui virtutem sectatur, bonum, quod sibi appetit, alteri cupit, etiam major erit. Q. E. D.","kind":"passage"},{"id":"RAW_QGOH_PQ_3:P0016","text":"ALITER","kind":"passage"},{"id":"RAW_QGOH_PQ_3:P0017","text":"Bonum, quod homo sibi appetit et amat, constantius amabit, si viderit, alios idem amare (per Prop. 31 p. III) ; atque adeo (per Coroll. ejusdem Prop.) conabitur, ut reliqui idem ament ; et quia hoc bonum (per Prop. præced.) omnibus commune est, eoque omnes gaudere possunt, conabitur ergo (per eandem rationem), ut omnes eodem gaudeant, et (per Prop. 37 p. III) eo magis, quo hoc bono magis fruetur. Q. E. D.","kind":"passage"},{"id":"RAW_QGOH_PQ_3:P0018","text":"SCHOLIUM I","kind":"passage"},{"id":"RAW_QGOH_PQ_3:P0019","text":"Qui ex solo affectu conatur, ut reliqui ament, quod ipse amat, et ut reliqui ex suo ingenio vivant, solo impetu agit, et ideo odiosus est, præcipue iis, quibus alia placent, quique propterea etiam student, et eodem impetu conantur, ut reliqui contra ex ipsorum ingenio vivant. Deinde quoniam summum, quod homines ex affectu appetunt, bonum sæpe tale est, ut unus tantum ejus possit esse compos, hinc fit, ut qui amant, mente sibi non constent, et dum laudes rei, quam amant, narrare gaudent, timeant credi. At qui reliquos conatur Ratione ducere, non impetu, sed humaniter et benigne agit, et sibi mente maxime constat. Porro quicquid cupimus et agimus, cujus causa sumus, quatenus Dei habemus ideam, sive quatenus Deum cognoscimus, ad Religionem refero. Cupiditatem autem bene faciendi, quæ ex eo ingeneratur, quod ex Rationis ductu vivimus, Pietatem voco.","kind":"passage"},{"id":"RAW_QGOH_PQ_3:P0020","text":"Cupiditatem deinde, qua homo, qui ex ductu Rationis vivit, tenetur, ut reliquos sibi amicitia jungat, Honestatem voco et id honestum, quod homines, qui ex ductu Rationis vivunt, laudant et id contra turpe, quod conciliandæ amicitiæ repugnat. Differentia deinde inter veram virtutem et impotentiam facile ex supra dictis percipitur ; nempe quod vera virtus nihil aliud sit, quam ex solo Rationis ductu vivere ; atque adeo impotentia in hoc solo consistit, quod homo a rebus, quæ extra ipsum sunt, duci se patiatur, et ab iis ad ea agendum determinetur, quæ rerum externarum communis constitutio, non autem ea, quæ ipsa ipsius natura, in se sola considerata, postulat. Atque hæc illa sunt, quæ in Scholio Propositionis 18 hujus Partis demonstrare promisi, ex quibus apparet, legem illam de non mactandis brutis magis vana superstitione et muliebri misericordia, quam sana Ratione fundatam esse.","kind":"passage"},{"id":"RAW_QGOH_PQ_3:P0021","text":"Docet quidem ratio nostrum utile quærendi necessitudinem cum hominibus jungere ; sed non cum brutis, aut rebus, quarum natura a natura humana est diversa ; sed idem jus, quod illa in nos habent, nos in ea habere. Imo quia uniuscujusque jus virtute seu potentia uniuscujusque definitur, longe majus homines in bruta, quam hæc in homines jus habent. Nec tamen nego bruta sentire ; sed nego, quod propterea non liceat nostræ utilitati consulere, et iisdem ad libitum uti, eademque tractare prout nobis magis convenit ; quandoquidem nobiscum natura non conveniunt, et eorum affectus ab affectibus humanis sunt natura diversi (vide Schol. Prop. 57 p. III). Superest ut explicem quid justum, quid injustum, quid peccatum, et quid denique meritum sit. Præter hæc civitatis etiam quænam sint fundamenta ostendam. Sed de his vide seq. Scholium.","kind":"passage"},{"id":"RAW_QGOH_PQ_3:P0022","text":"SCHOLIUM II","kind":"passage"},{"id":"RAW_QGOH_PQ_3:P0023","text":"In appendice Partis primæ explicare promisi, quid laus et vituperium, quid meritum et peccatum, quid justum et injustum sit. Laudem et vituperium quod attinet, in Scholio Propositionis 29 partis III explicui ; de reliquis autem hic jam erit dicendi locus. Sed prius pauca de statu hominis naturali et civili dicenda sunt.","kind":"passage"},{"id":"RAW_QGOH_PQ_3:P0024","text":"Existit unusquisque summo Naturæ jure, et consequenter summo Naturæ jure unusquisque ea agit, quæ ex suæ naturæ necessitate sequuntur ; atque adeo summo Naturæ jure unusquisque judicat, quid bonum, quid malum sit, suæque utilitati ex suo ingenio consulit (vide Prop. 19 et 20 hujus), seseque vindicat (vide Coroll. 2 Prop. 40 p. III), et id, quod amat, conservare, et id, quod odio habet, destruere conatur (vide Prop. 28 p. III). Quod si homines ex ductu Rationis viverent, potiretur unusquisque (per Coroll. 1 Prop. 35 hujus) hoc suo jure absque ullo alterius damno. Sed quia affectibus sunt obnoxii (per Coroll. Prop. 4 hujus), qui potentiam seu virtutem humanam longe superant (per Prop. 6 hujus) ideo sæpe diversi trahuntur (per Prop. 33 hujus), atque sibi invicem sunt contrarii (per Prop. 34 hujus), mutuo dum auxilio indigent (per Schol. Prop. 35 hujus).","kind":"passage"},{"id":"RAW_QGOH_PQ_3:P0025","text":"Ut igitur homines concorditer vivere et sibi auxilio esse possint, necesse est, ut jure suo naturali cedant, et se invicem securos reddant, se nihil acturos, quod possit in alterius damnum cedere. Qua autem ratione hoc fieri possit, ut scilicet homines, qui affectibus necessario sunt obnoxii (per Coroll. Prop. 4 hujus), atque inconstantes et varii (per Prop. 33 hujus), possint se invicem securos reddere, et fidem invicem habere, patet ex Propositione 7 hujus partis et Propositione 39 partis III. Nempe quod nullus affectus coerceri potest, nisi affectu fortiore et contrario affectui coercendo, et quod unusquisque ab inferendo damno abstinet timore majoris damni.","kind":"passage"},{"id":"RAW_QGOH_PQ_3:P0026","text":"Hac igitur lege Societas firmari poterit, si modo ipsa sibi vindicet jus, quod unusquisque habet, sese vindicandi, et de bono et malo judicandi ; quæque adeo potestatem habeat communem vivendi rationem præscribendi, legesque ferendi, easque non Ratione, quæ affectus coercere nequit (per Schol. Prop. 17 hujus), sed minis firmandi. Hæc autem Societas, legibus et potestate sese conservandi firmata, Civitas appellatur, et qui ipsius jure defenduntur, Cives ; ex quibus facile intelligimus, nihil in statu naturali dari, quod ex omnium consensu bonum aut malum sit ; quandoquidem unusquisque, qui, in statu est naturali, suæ tantummodo utilitati consulit, et ex suo ingenio, et quatenus suæ utilitatis tantum habet rationem, quid bonum quidve malum sit, decernit, et nemini, nisi sibi soli, obtemperare lege ulla tenetur.","kind":"passage"},{"id":"RAW_QGOH_PQ_3:P0027","text":"Atque adeo in statu naturali peccatum concipi nequit ; at quidem in statu Civili, ubi et communi consensu decernitur, quid bonum quidve malum sit, et unusquisque Civitati obtemperare tenetur. Est itaque peccatum nihil aliud quam inobedientia, quæ propterea solo Civitatis jure punitur, et contra obedientia Civi meritum ducitur, quia eo ipso dignus judicatur, qui Civitatis commodis gaudeat. Deinde in statu naturali nemo ex communi consensu alicujus rei est Dominus, nec in Natura aliquid datur, quod possit dici hujus hominis esse, et non illius ; sed omnia omnium sunt ; ac proinde in statu naturali nulla potest concipi voluntas unicuique suum tribuendi, aut alicui id, quod ejus sit, eripiendi ; hoc est, in statu naturali nihil fit, quod justum aut injustum possit dici ; at quidem in statu civili, ubi ex communi consensu decernitur, quid hujus, quidve illius sit.","kind":"passage"},{"id":"RAW_QGOH_PQ_3:P0028","text":"Ex quibus apparet, justum et injustum, peccatum et meritum, notiones esse extrinsecas ; non autem attributa, quæ Mentis naturam explicent. Sed de his satis.","kind":"passage"},{"id":"RAW_QGOH_PQ_3:P0029","text":"PROPOSITIO XXXVIII","kind":"passage"},{"id":"RAW_QGOH_PQ_3:P0030","text":"Id, quod Corpus humanum ita disponit, ut pluribus modis possit affici, vel quod idem aptum reddit ad corpora externa pluribus modis afficiendum, homini est utile ; et eo utilius, quo Corpus ab eo aptius redditur, ut pluribus modis afficiatur, aliaque corpora afficiat ; et contra id noxium est, quod Corpus ad hæc minus aptum reddit.","kind":"passage"},{"id":"RAW_QGOH_PQ_3:P0031","text":"DEMONSTRATIO","kind":"passage"},{"id":"RAW_QGOH_PQ_3:P0032","text":"Quo corpus ad hæc aptius redditur, eo Mens aptior ad percipiendum redditur (per Prop. 14 p. II) ; adeoque id, quod Corpus hac ratione disponit, aptumque ad hæc reddit, est necessario bonum seu utile (per Prop. 26 et 27 hujus), et eo utilius, quo Corpus ad hæc aptius potest reddere ; et contra (per eandem Prop. 14 p. II inversam, et Prop. 26 et 27 hujus) noxium, si corpus ad hæc minus aptum reddat. Q. E. D.","kind":"passage"},{"id":"RAW_QGOH_PQ_3:P0033","text":"PROPOSITIO XXXIX","kind":"passage"},{"id":"RAW_QGOH_PQ_3:P0034","text":"Quæ efficiunt, ut motus et quietis ratio, quam Corporis humani partes ad invicem habent, conservetur, bona sunt ; et ea contra mala, quæ efficiunt, ut Corporis humani partes aliam ad invicem motus et quietis habeant rationem.","kind":"passage"},{"id":"RAW_QGOH_PQ_3:P0035","text":"DEMONSTRATIO","kind":"passage"},{"id":"RAW_QGOH_PQ_3:P0036","text":"Corpus humanum indiget, ut conservetur, plurimis aliis corporibus (per Postul. 4 p. II). At id, quod formam humani Corporis constituit, in hoc consistit, quod ejus partes motus suos certa quadam Ratione sibi invicem communicent (per Defin. ante Lem. 4, quam vide post Prop. 13 p. II). Ergo quæ efficiunt, ut motus et quietis ratio, quam Corporis humani partes ad invicem habent, conservetur, eadem humani Corporis formam conservant, et consequenter efficiunt (per Post. 3 et 6 p. II), ut Corpus humanum multis modis affici, et ut idem corpora externa multis modis afficere possit, adeoque (per Prop. præced.) bona sunt. Deinde, quæ efficiunt, ut Corporis humani partes aliam motus et quietis rationem obtineant, eadem (per eandem Defin. p.","kind":"passage"},{"id":"RAW_QGOH_PQ_3:P0037","text":"II), efficiunt, ut corpus humanum aliam formam induat, hoc est (ut per se notum, et in fine præfationis hujus partis monuimus), ut Corpus humanum destruatur, et consequenter ut omnino ineptum reddatur, ne possit pluribus modis affici, ac proinde (per Prop. præc.) mala sunt. Q. E. D.","kind":"passage"},{"id":"RAW_QGOH_PQ_3:P0038","text":"SCHOLIUM","kind":"passage"},{"id":"RAW_QGOH_PQ_3:P0039","text":"Quantum hæc Menti obesse vel prodesse possunt, in Quinta Parte explicabitur. Sed hic notandum, quod Corpus tum mortem obire intelligam, quando ejus partes ita disponuntur, ut aliam motus et quietis rationem ad invicem obtineant. Nam negare non audeo, Corpus humanum, retenta sanguinis circulatione, et aliis, propter quæ Corpus vivere existimatur, posse nihilominus in aliam naturam a sua prorsus diversam mutari. Nam nulla ratio me cogit, ut statuam Corpus non mori, nisi mutetur in cadaver ; quin ipsa experientia aliud suadere videtur.","kind":"passage"},{"id":"RAW_QGOH_PQ_3:P0040","text":"Fit namque aliquando, ut homo tales patiatur mutationes, ut non facile eundem illum esse dixerim ; ut de quodam Hispano Poeta narrare audivi, qui morbo correptus fuerat, et quamvis ex eo convaluerit, mansit tamen præteritæ suæ vitæ tam oblitus, ut Fabulas et Tragœdias, quas fecerat, suas non crediderit esse ; et sane pro infante adulto haberi potuisset, si vernaculæ etiam linguæ fuisset oblitus. Et si hoc incredibile videtur quid de infantibus dicemus ? Quorum naturam homo provectæ ætatis a sua tam diversam esse credit, ut persuaderi non posset, se unquam infantem fuisse, nisi ex aliis de se conjecturam faceret. Sed ne superstitiosis materiam suppeditem movendi novas quæstiones, malo hæc in medio relinquere.","kind":"passage"},{"id":"RAW_QGOH_PQ_3:P0041","text":"PROPOSITIO XL","kind":"passage"},{"id":"RAW_QGOH_PQ_3:P0042","text":"Quæ ad hominum communem Societatem conducunt, sive quæ efficiunt, ut homines concorditer vivant, utilia sunt, et illa contra mala, quæ discordiam in Civitatem inducunt.","kind":"passage"},{"id":"RAW_QGOH_PQ_3:P0043","text":"DEMONSTRATIO","kind":"passage"},{"id":"RAW_QGOH_PQ_3:P0044","text":"Nam quæ efficiunt, ut homines concorditer vivant, simul efficiunt, ut ex ductu Rationis vivant (per Prop. 35 hujus), atque adeo (per Prop. 26 et 27 hujus) bona sunt, et (per eandem rationem) illa contra mala sunt, quæ discordias concitant. Q. E. D.","kind":"passage"},{"id":"RAW_QGOH_PQ_3:P0045","text":"PROPOSITIO XLI","kind":"passage"},{"id":"RAW_QGOH_PQ_3:P0046","text":"Lætitia directe mala non est, sed bona ; Tristitia autem contra directe est mala.","kind":"passage"},{"id":"RAW_QGOH_PQ_3:P0047","text":"DEMONSTRATIO","kind":"passage"},{"id":"RAW_QGOH_PQ_3:P0048","text":"Lætitia (per Prop. 11 p. III cum ejusdem Schol.) est affectus, quo corporis agendi potentia augetur vel juvatur ; Tristitia autem contra est affectus, quo corporis agendi potentia minuitur vel coercetur ; adeoque (per Prop. 38 hujus) Lætitia directe bona est, etc. Q. E. D.","kind":"passage"},{"id":"RAW_QGOH_PQ_3:P0049","text":"PROPOSITIO XLII","kind":"passage"},{"id":"RAW_QGOH_PQ_3:P0050","text":"Hilaritas excessum habere nequit, sed semper bona est ; et contra Melancholia semper mala.","kind":"passage"},{"id":"RAW_QGOH_PQ_3:P0051","text":"DEMONSTRATIO","kind":"passage"},{"id":"RAW_QGOH_PQ_3:P0052","text":"Hilaritas (vide ejus Defin. in Schol. Prop. 11 p. III) est Lætitia, quæ quatenus ad Corpus refertur, in hoc consistit, quod Corporis omnes partes pariter sint affectæ ; hoc est (per Prop. 11 p. III) quod Corporis agendi potentia augetur vel juvatur, ita ut omnes ejus partes eandem ad invicem motus et quietis rationem obtineant ; atque adeo (per Prop. 39 hujus) Hilaritas semper est bona, nec excessum habere potest. At Melancholia (cujus etiam Defin. vide in eodem Schol. Prop. 11 p. III) est Tristitia, quæ quatenus ad Corpus refertur, in hoc consistit, quod Corporis agendi potentia absolute minuitur vel coercetur ; adeoque (per Prop. 38 hujus) semper est mala. Q. E. D.","kind":"passage"},{"id":"RAW_QGOH_PQ_3:P0053","text":"PROPOSITIO XLIII","kind":"passage"},{"id":"RAW_QGOH_PQ_3:P0054","text":"Titillatio excessum habere potest, et mala esse ; Dolor autem eatenus potest esse bonus, quatenus Titillatio seu Lætitia est mala.","kind":"passage"},{"id":"RAW_QGOH_PQ_3:P0055","text":"DEMONSTRATIO","kind":"passage"},{"id":"RAW_QGOH_PQ_3:P0056","text":"Titillatio est Lætitia, quæ, quatenus ad Corpus refertur, in hoc consistit quod una vel aliquot ejus partes præ reliquis afficiuntur (vide ejus Defin. in Schol. Prop. 11 p. III) ; cujus affectus potentia tanta esse potest, ut reliquas Corporis actiones superet (per Prop. 6 hujus), eique pertinaciter adhæreat, atque adeo impediat quo minus Corpus aptum sit, ut plurimis aliis modis afficiatur ; adeoque (per Prop. 38 hujus) mala esse potest. Deinde Dolor, qui contra Tristitia est, in se solo consideratus, non potest esse bonus (per Prop. 41 hujus). Verum quia ejus vis et incrementum definitur potentia causæ externæ cum nostra comparata (per Prop. 5 hujus) possumus ergo hujus affectus infinitos virium concipere gradus et modos (per Prop. 3 hujus) ; atque adeo eundem talem concipere qui Titillationem possit coercere, ut excessum non habeat, et eatenus (per primam partem Prop.","kind":"passage"},{"id":"RAW_QGOH_PQ_3:P0057","text":"hujus) efficere, ne Corpus minus aptum reddatur ; ac proinde eatenus erit bonus. Q. E. D.","kind":"passage"},{"id":"RAW_QGOH_PQ_3:P0058","text":"PROPOSITIO XLIV","kind":"passage"},{"id":"RAW_QGOH_PQ_3:P0059","text":"Amor et cupiditas excessum habere possunt.","kind":"passage"},{"id":"RAW_QGOH_PQ_3:P0060","text":"DEMONSTRATIO","kind":"passage"},{"id":"RAW_QGOH_PQ_3:P0061","text":"Amor est Lætitia (per Defin. 6 Affect.) concomitante idea causæ externæ ; Titillatio igitur (per Schol. Prop. 11 p. III) concomitante idea causæ externæ Amor est ; atque adeo Amor (per Prop. præced.) excessum habere potest. Deinde Cupiditas eo est major, quo affectus, ex quo oritur major est (per Prop. 37 p. III). Quare ut affectus (per Prop. 6 hujus) reliquas hominis actiones superare potest ; sic etiam Cupiditas, quæ ex eodem affectu oritur, reliquas Cupiditates superare, ac proinde eundem excessum habere poterit, quem in præcedenti Propositione Titillationem habere ostendimus. Q. E. D.","kind":"passage"},{"id":"RAW_QGOH_PQ_3:P0062","text":"SCHOLIUM","kind":"passage"},{"id":"RAW_QGOH_PQ_3:P0063","text":"Hilaritas, quam bonam esse dixi, concipitur facilius quam observatur. Nam affectus, quibus quotidie conflictamur, referuntur plerumque ad aliquam Corporis partem, quæ præ reliquis afficitur ; ac proinde affectus ut plurimum excessum habent, et Mentem in sola unius objecti contemplatione ita detinent, ut de aliis cogitare nequeat ; et quamvis homines pluribus affectibus obnoxii sint, atque adeo rari reperiantur, qui semper uno eodemque affectu conflictentur, non desunt tamen, quibus unus idemque affectus pertinaciter adhæreat. Videmus enim homines aliquando ab uno objecto ita affici, ut quamvis præsens non sit, ipsum tamen coram habere credant ; quod quando homini non dormienti accidit, eundem delirare dicimus vel insanire ; nec minus insanire creduntur, qui Amore ardent, quique noctes atque dies solam amasiam vel meretricem somniant, quia risum movere solent.","kind":"passage"},{"id":"RAW_QGOH_PQ_3:P0064","text":"At cum avarus de nulla alia re, quam de lucro vel de nummis cogitet, et ambitiosus de gloria, etc., hi non creduntur delirare, quia molesti solent esse, et Odio digni æstimantur. Sed revera Avaritia, Ambitio, Libido, etc. delirii species sunt, quamvis inter morbos non numerentur.","kind":"passage"},{"id":"RAW_QGOH_PQ_3:P0065","text":"PROPOSITIO XLV","kind":"passage"},{"id":"RAW_QGOH_PQ_3:P0066","text":"Odium nunquam potest esse bonum.","kind":"passage"},{"id":"RAW_QGOH_PQ_3:P0067","text":"DEMONSTRATIO","kind":"passage"},{"id":"RAW_QGOH_PQ_3:P0068","text":"Hominem, quem odimus, destruere conamur (per Prop. 39 p. III), hoc est (per Prop. 37 hujus) aliquid conamur, quod malum est ; Ergo, etc. Q. E. D.","kind":"passage"},{"id":"RAW_QGOH_PQ_3:P0069","text":"SCHOLIUM","kind":"passage"},{"id":"RAW_QGOH_PQ_3:P0070","text":"Nota, me hic et in seqq. per Odium illud tantum intelligere, quod est erga homines.","kind":"passage"},{"id":"RAW_QGOH_PQ_3:P0071","text":"COROLLARIUM I","kind":"passage"},{"id":"RAW_QGOH_PQ_3:P0072","text":"Invidia, Irrisio, Contemptus, Ira, Vindicta, et reliqui affectus, qui ad Odium referuntur vel ex eodem oriuntur, mali sunt ; quod etiam ex Prop. 39 p. III et Prop. 37 hujus patet.","kind":"passage"},{"id":"RAW_QGOH_PQ_3:P0073","text":"COROLLARIUM II","kind":"passage"},{"id":"RAW_QGOH_PQ_3:P0074","text":"Quicquid ex eo, quod odio affecti sumus, appetimus, turpe et in Civitate injustum est. Quod etiam patet ex Prop. 39 p. III et ex Defin. turpis et injusti, quas vide in Scholiis Prop. 37 hujus.","kind":"passage"},{"id":"RAW_QGOH_PQ_3:P0075","text":"SCHOLIUM","kind":"passage"},{"id":"RAW_QGOH_PQ_3:P0076","text":"Inter Irrisionem (quam in I Coroll. malam esse dixi) et risum magnam agnosco differentiam. Nam risus, ut et jocus, mera est Lætitia ; adeoque, modo excessum non habeat, per se bonus est (per Prop. 41 hujus). Nihil profecto nisi torva et tristis superstitio delectari prohibet. Nam qui magis decet famem et sitim extinguere, quam melancholiam expellere ? Mea hæc est ratio, et sic animum induxi meum. Nullum numen, nec alius, nisi invidus, mea impotentia et incommodo delectatur, nec nobis lacrimas, singultus, metum, et alia hujusmodi, quæ animi impotentis sunt signa, virtuti ducit ; sed contra, quo majore Lætitia afficimur, eo ad majorem perfectionem transimus, hoc est, eo nos magis de natura divina participare necesse est. Rebus itaque uti, et iis, quantum fieri potest, delectari (non quidem ad nauseam usque, nam hoc delectari non est), viri est sapientis.","kind":"passage"},{"id":"RAW_QGOH_PQ_3:P0077","text":"Viri, inquam, sapientis est, moderato et suavi cibo et potu se reficere et recreare, ut et odoribus, plantarum virentium amœnitate, ornatu, musica, ludis exercitatoriis, theatris, et aliis hujusmodi, quibus unusquisque absque ullo alterius damno uti potest. Corpus namque humanum ex plurimis diversæ naturæ partibus componitur, quæ continuo novo alimento indigent et vario, ut totum Corpus ad omnia, quæ ex ipsius natura sequi possunt, æque aptum sit, et consequenter ut Mens etiam æque apta sit ad plura simul intelligendum. Hoc itaque vivendi institutum et cum nostris principiis et cum communi praxi optime convenit ; quare si quæ alia, hæc vivendi ratio optima est, et omnibus modis commendanda ; nec opus est de his clarius neque prolixius agere.","kind":"passage"},{"id":"RAW_QGOH_PQ_3:P0078","text":"PROPOSITIO XLVI","kind":"passage"},{"id":"RAW_QGOH_PQ_3:P0079","text":"Qui ex ductu Rationis vivit, quantum potest conatur, alterius in ipsum Odium, Iram, Contemptum, etc., Amore contra, sive Generositate compensare.","kind":"passage"},{"id":"RAW_QGOH_PQ_3:P0080","text":"DEMONSTRATIO","kind":"passage"},{"id":"RAW_QGOH_PQ_3:P0081","text":"Omnes Odii affectus mali sunt (per Coroll. 1 præc. Prop.) ; adeoque, qui ex ductu Rationis vivit, quantum potest conabitur efficere, ne Odii affectibus conflictetur (per Prop. 19 hujus), et consequenter (per Prop. 37 hujus) conabitur, ne etiam alius eosdem patiatur affectus. At Odium Odio reciproco augetur, et Amore contra extingui potest (per Prop. 43 p. III), ita ut Odium in Amorem transeat (per Prop. 44 p. III) ; Ergo qui ex ductu Rationis vivit ; alterius Odium, etc. Amore contra compensare conabitur, hoc est Generositate (cujus Defin. vide in Schol. Prop. 59 p. III). Q. E. D.","kind":"passage"},{"id":"RAW_QGOH_PQ_3:P0082","text":"SCHOLIUM","kind":"passage"},{"id":"RAW_QGOH_PQ_3:P0083","text":"Qui injurias reciproco Odio vindicare vult, misere profecto vivit. At qui contra studet Odium Amore expugnare, ille sane lætus et secure pugnat, æque facile pluribus ac uni homini resistit, et fortunæ auxilio quam minime indiget. Quos vero vincit, ii læti cedunt, non quidem ex defectu, sed ex incremento virium ; quæ omnia adeo clare ex solis Amoris et Intellectus definitionibus sequuntur, ut opus non sit eadem sigillatim demonstrare.","kind":"passage"},{"id":"RAW_QGOH_PQ_3:P0084","text":"PROPOSITIO XLVII","kind":"passage"},{"id":"RAW_QGOH_PQ_3:P0085","text":"Spei et Metus affectus non possunt esse per se boni.","kind":"passage"},{"id":"RAW_QGOH_PQ_3:P0086","text":"DEMONSTRATIO","kind":"passage"},{"id":"RAW_QGOH_PQ_3:P0087","text":"Spei et Metus affectus sine Tristitia non dantur. Nam Metus est (per 13 Affect. Defin.) Tristitia, et Spes (vide Explicationem 12 et 13 Affect. Defin.) non datur sine Metu ; ac proinde (per Prop. 41 hujus) hi affectus non possunt esse per se boni, sed tantum quatenus Lætitiæ excessum coercere possunt (per Prop. 43 hujus). Q. E. D.","kind":"passage"},{"id":"RAW_QGOH_PQ_3:P0088","text":"SCHOLIUM","kind":"passage"},{"id":"RAW_QGOH_PQ_3:P0089","text":"Huc accedit, quod hi affectus cognitionis defectum et Mentis impotentiam indicant ; et hac de causa etiam Securitas, Desperatio, Gaudium et Conscientiæ morsus animi impotentis sunt signa. Nam, quamvis Securitas et Gaudium affectus sint Lætitiæ, Tristitiam tamen eosdem præcessisse supponunt, nempe Spem et Metum. Quo itaque magis ex ductu Rationis vivere conamur, eo magis Spe minus pendere, et Metu nosmet liberare, et fortunæ quantum possumus, imperare conamur, nostrasque actiones certo Rationis consilio dirigere.","kind":"passage"},{"id":"RAW_QGOH_PQ_3:P0090","text":"PROPOSITIO XLVIII","kind":"passage"},{"id":"RAW_QGOH_PQ_3:P0091","text":"Affectus Existimationis et Despectus semper mali sunt.","kind":"passage"},{"id":"RAW_QGOH_PQ_3:P0092","text":"DEMONSTRATIO","kind":"passage"},{"id":"RAW_QGOH_PQ_3:P0093","text":"Hi enim affectus (per 21 et 22 Affect. Defin.) rationi repugnant ; adeoque (per Prop. 26 et 27 hujus) mali sunt. Q. E. D.","kind":"passage"},{"id":"RAW_QGOH_PQ_3:P0094","text":"PROPOSITIO XLIX","kind":"passage"},{"id":"RAW_QGOH_PQ_3:P0095","text":"Existimatio facile hominem, qui existimatur, superbum reddit.","kind":"passage"},{"id":"RAW_QGOH_PQ_3:P0096","text":"DEMONSTRATIO","kind":"passage"},{"id":"RAW_QGOH_PQ_3:P0097","text":"Si videmus aliquem de nobis plus justo præ amore sentire, facile gloriabimur (per Schol. Prop. 41 p. III), sive Lætitia afficiemur (per 30 Affect. Defin.) ; et id boni, quod de nobis prædicari audimus, facile credemus (per Prop. 25 p. III) ; atque adeo de nobis præ amore nostri plus justo sentiemus, hoc est (per Defin. 28 Affect.) facile superbiemus. Q. E. D.","kind":"passage"},{"id":"RAW_QGOH_PQ_3:P0098","text":"PROPOSITIO L","kind":"passage"}],"sha256":"0d30c8815a1d472752c3896d173aa7786062ab6789346a3713274d5eab970a5f","language_code":"la","language_name":"Latina","translations":[{"id":"SOURCE_TR_20261015T013944159467Z_33884ef5","language_code":"en","language_name":"English","title":"Part Four - On Human Bondage, or the Powers of the Affects · passage 3","source_sha256":"0d30c8815a1d472752c3896d173aa7786062ab6789346a3713274d5eab970a5f","sha256":"8237bbe6231ee00b0c521406e0de4244752938c53298f6bdb801a961503c75cf","rendition_type":"machine","review_status":"machine","model":"gpt-5.6-terra","created":"2026-10-15T01:39:44.159499+00:00","paragraphs":[{"id":"RAW_QGOH_PQ_3:P0001","text":"To the greatest extent that each man seeks what is useful to himself, to that same extent men are most useful to one another. For the more each one seeks what is useful to himself, and strives to preserve himself, the more he is endowed with virtue (by Prop. 20 of this Part), or—which is the same thing (by Def. 8 of this Part)—the greater is the power with which he is endowed for acting according to the laws of his own nature, that is (by Prop. 3, Part III), for living according to the guidance of Reason. But men agree in nature most of all when they live according to the guidance of Reason (by the preceding Prop.); therefore (by the preceding Coroll.) men will be most useful to one another when each one most of all seeks what is useful to himself. Q.E.D.","kind":"passage","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_QGOH_PQ_3:P0001"]},{"id":"RAW_QGOH_PQ_3:P0002","text":"SCHOLIUM","kind":"passage","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_QGOH_PQ_3:P0002"]},{"id":"RAW_QGOH_PQ_3:P0003","text":"What we have just shown is also attested by daily experience itself through so many and such striking testimonies that it is almost on everyone's lips: man is a god to man. Yet it rarely happens that men live according to the guidance of Reason; rather, they are so constituted that for the most part they are envious and mutually troublesome. Nevertheless, they can scarcely pass their lives in solitude, so that the definition that man is a social animal has greatly pleased most people; and indeed the matter stands so that many more advantages arise from men's common society than disadvantages.","kind":"passage","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_QGOH_PQ_3:P0003"]},{"id":"RAW_QGOH_PQ_3:P0004","text":"Therefore let Satirists laugh at human affairs as much as they please, and let Theologians detest them, and let Melancholics praise, as much as they can, the uncultivated and rustic life; let them despise men and admire brutes: they will nevertheless find by experience that men can much more easily procure for themselves by mutual assistance the things they need, and that only by joining forces can they avoid the dangers that everywhere threaten them; not to mention that it is far more excellent, and more worthy of our knowledge, to contemplate the deeds of men than those of brutes. But on these matters elsewhere, more at length.","kind":"passage","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_QGOH_PQ_3:P0004"]},{"id":"RAW_QGOH_PQ_3:P0005","text":"PROPOSITION XXXVI","kind":"passage","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_QGOH_PQ_3:P0005"]},{"id":"RAW_QGOH_PQ_3:P0006","text":"The highest good of those who pursue virtue is common to all, and all can equally enjoy it.","kind":"passage","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_QGOH_PQ_3:P0006"]},{"id":"RAW_QGOH_PQ_3:P0007","text":"DEMONSTRATION","kind":"passage","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_QGOH_PQ_3:P0007"]},{"id":"RAW_QGOH_PQ_3:P0008","text":"To act from virtue is to act according to the guidance of Reason (by Prop. 24 of this Part), and whatever we strive to do from Reason is to understand (by Prop. 26 of this Part); and therefore (by Prop. 28 of this Part) the highest good of those who pursue virtue is to know God, that is (by Prop. 47, Part II, and its Schol.) a good that is common to all men and can be possessed equally by all men, insofar as they are of the same nature. Q.E.D.","kind":"passage","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_QGOH_PQ_3:P0008"]},{"id":"RAW_QGOH_PQ_3:P0009","text":"SCHOLIUM","kind":"passage","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_QGOH_PQ_3:P0009"]},{"id":"RAW_QGOH_PQ_3:P0010","text":"But if anyone should ask: what if the highest good of those who pursue virtue were not common to all? would it not then follow, as above (see Prop. 34 of this Part), that men who live according to the guidance of Reason, that is (by Prop. 35 of this Part), men insofar as they agree in nature, would be contrary to one another? Let him have this answer: that it arises not accidentally, but from the very nature of Reason, that man's highest good is common to all, namely because it is deduced from human essence itself, insofar as it is defined by Reason; and because man could neither be nor be conceived if he did not have the power of enjoying this highest good. For it pertains (by Prop. 47, Part II) to the essence of the human Mind to have an adequate knowledge of the eternal and infinite essence of God.","kind":"passage","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_QGOH_PQ_3:P0010"]},{"id":"RAW_QGOH_PQ_3:P0011","text":"PROPOSITION XXXVII","kind":"passage","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_QGOH_PQ_3:P0011"]},{"id":"RAW_QGOH_PQ_3:P0012","text":"The good which everyone who pursues virtue seeks for himself, he will also desire for other men, and the more so as he has a greater knowledge of God.","kind":"passage","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_QGOH_PQ_3:P0012"]},{"id":"RAW_QGOH_PQ_3:P0013","text":"DEMONSTRATION","kind":"passage","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_QGOH_PQ_3:P0013"]},{"id":"RAW_QGOH_PQ_3:P0014","text":"Men, insofar as they live according to the guidance of Reason, are most useful to man (by Coroll. 1, Prop. 35 of this Part); and therefore (by Prop. 19 of this Part) from the guidance of Reason we shall necessarily strive to bring it about that men live according to the guidance of Reason. But the good which everyone who lives according to the dictate of Reason, that is (by Prop. 24 of this Part), who pursues virtue, seeks for himself, is to understand (by Prop. 26 of this Part); therefore the good which everyone who pursues virtue seeks for himself, he will also desire for other men. Next, Desire, insofar as it is referred to the Mind, is the very essence of the Mind (by Def. 1 of the Affects); but the essence of the Mind consists in knowledge (by Prop. 11, Part II), which involves the knowledge of God (by Prop. 47, Part II), and without which (by Prop. 15, Part","kind":"passage","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_QGOH_PQ_3:P0014"]},{"id":"RAW_QGOH_PQ_3:P0015","text":"I), it can neither be nor be conceived; and therefore, the more the essence of the Mind involves a greater knowledge of God, the greater also will be the Desire by which he who pursues virtue desires for another the good which he seeks for himself. Q.E.D.","kind":"passage","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_QGOH_PQ_3:P0015"]},{"id":"RAW_QGOH_PQ_3:P0016","text":"OTHERWISE","kind":"passage","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_QGOH_PQ_3:P0016"]},{"id":"RAW_QGOH_PQ_3:P0017","text":"The good which a man seeks and loves for himself, he will love more constantly if he sees that others love the same thing (by Prop. 31, Part III); and therefore (by the Coroll. of the same Prop.) he will strive that others love the same thing; and because this good (by the preceding Prop.) is common to all, and all can enjoy it, therefore (by the same reasoning) he will strive that all enjoy it, and (by Prop. 37, Part III) the more so as he enjoys this good more. Q.E.D.","kind":"passage","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_QGOH_PQ_3:P0017"]},{"id":"RAW_QGOH_PQ_3:P0018","text":"SCHOLIUM I","kind":"passage","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_QGOH_PQ_3:P0018"]},{"id":"RAW_QGOH_PQ_3:P0019","text":"He who from affect alone strives that others love what he himself loves, and that others live according to his own disposition, acts by impulse alone, and is therefore hateful, especially to those who are pleased by other things, and who for that reason also endeavor, and strive with the same impulse, that others, on the contrary, live according to their disposition. Further, since the highest good which men seek from affect is often such that only one can possess it, it follows that those who love are not constant in mind, and while they delight in recounting the praises of the thing they love, they fear being believed. But he who strives to lead others by Reason acts not by impulse, but humanely and kindly, and is most constant in mind. Moreover, whatever we desire and do, of which we are the cause insofar as we have the idea of God, or insofar as we know God, I refer to Religion. But the Desire of doing good which is engendered from our living according to the guidance of Reason, I call Piety.","kind":"passage","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_QGOH_PQ_3:P0019"]},{"id":"RAW_QGOH_PQ_3:P0020","text":"Further, the Desire by which a man who lives according to the guidance of Reason is bound to join others to himself in friendship, I call Honesty; and I call honorable that which men who live according to the guidance of Reason praise, and, on the contrary, base that which is opposed to forming friendship. Further, the difference between true virtue and powerlessness is easily perceived from what has been said above; namely, that true virtue is nothing other than to live according to the guidance of Reason alone; and therefore powerlessness consists in this alone: that a man allows himself to be led by things that are outside him, and to be determined by them to do those things which the common constitution of external things, but not that which his own nature, considered in itself alone, demands. And these are the things which I promised to demonstrate in the Scholium of Proposition 18 of this Part, from which it is apparent that the law concerning not slaughtering brutes is founded more on empty superstition and womanish compassion than on sound Reason.","kind":"passage","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_QGOH_PQ_3:P0020"]},{"id":"RAW_QGOH_PQ_3:P0021","text":"Reason indeed teaches that the necessity of seeking our own advantage joins us with men, but not with brutes or with things whose nature is different from human nature; rather, we have the same right over them that they have over us. Indeed, since the right of each is defined by the virtue, or power, of each, men have far greater right over brutes than these have over men. Yet I do not deny that brutes feel; but I deny that for that reason it is not permitted us to consult our own advantage, to use them at our pleasure, and to treat them as is most suitable for us; since they do not agree with us in nature, and their affects differ in nature from human affects (see Schol. Prop. 57, Part III). It remains for me to explain what is just, what unjust, what sin, and finally what merit is. Besides these, I shall also show what the foundations of the state are. But on these matters see the following Scholium.","kind":"passage","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_QGOH_PQ_3:P0021"]},{"id":"RAW_QGOH_PQ_3:P0022","text":"SCHOLIUM II","kind":"passage","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_QGOH_PQ_3:P0022"]},{"id":"RAW_QGOH_PQ_3:P0023","text":"In the Appendix to Part I I promised to explain what praise and blame are, what merit and sin, what just and unjust, are. As regards praise and blame, I explained them in the Scholium of Proposition 29, Part III; but there will now be occasion to speak here concerning the rest. But first, a few things must be said concerning man's natural and civil state.","kind":"passage","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_QGOH_PQ_3:P0023"]},{"id":"RAW_QGOH_PQ_3:P0024","text":"Each thing exists by the supreme right of Nature, and consequently by the supreme right of Nature each does those things which follow from the necessity of its nature; and therefore by the supreme right of Nature each judges what is good and what is bad, and, according to its own disposition, provides for its own advantage (see Props. 19 and 20 of this Part), vindicates itself (see Coroll. 2, Prop. 40, Part III), and strives to preserve what it loves and to destroy what it hates (see Prop. 28, Part III). If men lived according to the guidance of Reason, each would possess (by Coroll. 1, Prop. 35 of this Part) this right of his without any harm to another. But because they are subject to affects (by the Coroll. of Prop. 4 of this Part), which far surpass human power, or virtue (by Prop. 6 of this Part), they are therefore often drawn in different directions (by Prop. 33 of this Part), and are contrary to one another (by Prop. 34 of this Part), while they need one another's mutual assistance (by the Schol. of Prop. 35 of this Part).","kind":"passage","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_QGOH_PQ_3:P0024"]},{"id":"RAW_QGOH_PQ_3:P0025","text":"Therefore, in order that human beings may be able to live harmoniously and be of assistance to one another, it is necessary that they yield their natural right and render one another secure that they will do nothing which may turn to another's harm. Now by what means this can be brought about—namely, that human beings, who are necessarily subject to affects (by the Corollary of Proposition 4 of this Part), and inconstant and variable (by Proposition 33 of this Part), may render one another secure and have mutual trust—is evident from Proposition 7 of this Part and Proposition 39 of Part III. Namely, that no affect can be restrained except by an affect stronger than and contrary to the affect to be restrained, and that everyone abstains from inflicting harm through fear of a greater harm.","kind":"passage","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_QGOH_PQ_3:P0025"]},{"id":"RAW_QGOH_PQ_3:P0026","text":"Therefore, by this law a Society can be established, provided that it claims for itself the right which each person has of avenging himself and of judging concerning good and evil; and consequently, provided that it has the power to prescribe a common rule of life and to enact laws, and to uphold them not by Reason, which cannot restrain the affects (by the Scholium of Proposition 17 of this Part), but by threats. Such a Society, established by laws and by the power of preserving itself, is called a State, and those who are defended by its right, Citizens; from which we readily understand that in the state of nature nothing is given which, by the consent of all, is good or evil; since each person who is in the state of nature consults only his own advantage, and, according to his own disposition and insofar as he takes account only of his own advantage, determines what is good and what evil, and is bound by no law to obey anyone except himself alone.","kind":"passage","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_QGOH_PQ_3:P0026"]},{"id":"RAW_QGOH_PQ_3:P0027","text":"And thus in the state of nature sin cannot be conceived; but it can indeed be conceived in the civil state, where by common consent it is determined what is good and what evil, and everyone is bound to obey the State. Sin, therefore, is nothing but disobedience, which is consequently punished by the right of the State alone; and, conversely, obedience is counted as merit in a Citizen, because by that very fact he is judged worthy to enjoy the advantages of the State. Further, in the state of nature no one is, by common consent, the owner of anything, nor is anything given in Nature that can be said to belong to this person and not to that; but all things belong to all. Consequently, in the state of nature no will can be conceived of rendering to each his own, or of taking from anyone what is his; that is, in the state of nature nothing takes place that can be called just or unjust; but it can indeed be so called in the civil state, where by common consent it is determined what belongs to this person and what to that.","kind":"passage","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_QGOH_PQ_3:P0027"]},{"id":"RAW_QGOH_PQ_3:P0028","text":"From these things it is apparent that just and unjust, sin and merit, are extrinsic notions, and not attributes which explain the nature of the Mind. But enough on these matters.","kind":"passage","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_QGOH_PQ_3:P0028"]},{"id":"RAW_QGOH_PQ_3:P0029","text":"PROPOSITION XXXVIII","kind":"passage","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_QGOH_PQ_3:P0029"]},{"id":"RAW_QGOH_PQ_3:P0030","text":"That which so disposes the human Body that it can be affected in many ways, or which renders the same fit to affect external bodies in many ways, is useful to a human being; and it is the more useful insofar as the Body is rendered by it more fit to be affected in many ways and to affect other bodies; and, conversely, that is harmful which renders the Body less fit for these things.","kind":"passage","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_QGOH_PQ_3:P0030"]},{"id":"RAW_QGOH_PQ_3:P0031","text":"DEMONSTRATION","kind":"passage","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_QGOH_PQ_3:P0031"]},{"id":"RAW_QGOH_PQ_3:P0032","text":"Insofar as the body is rendered more fit for these things, the Mind is rendered more fit for perceiving (by Proposition 14, Part II); and therefore that which disposes the Body in this manner and renders it fit for these things is necessarily good or useful (by Propositions 26 and 27 of this Part), and is the more useful insofar as it can render the Body more fit for these things; and, conversely (by the same Proposition 14 of Part II taken inversely, and by Propositions 26 and 27 of this Part), it is harmful if it renders the body less fit for these things. Q. E. D.","kind":"passage","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_QGOH_PQ_3:P0032"]},{"id":"RAW_QGOH_PQ_3:P0033","text":"PROPOSITION XXXIX","kind":"passage","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_QGOH_PQ_3:P0033"]},{"id":"RAW_QGOH_PQ_3:P0034","text":"Those things which bring about the preservation of the ratio of motion and rest which the parts of the human Body have to one another are good; and, conversely, those are evil which bring it about that the parts of the human Body have another ratio of motion and rest to one another.","kind":"passage","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_QGOH_PQ_3:P0034"]},{"id":"RAW_QGOH_PQ_3:P0035","text":"DEMONSTRATION","kind":"passage","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_QGOH_PQ_3:P0035"]},{"id":"RAW_QGOH_PQ_3:P0036","text":"The human Body requires, in order to be preserved, very many other bodies (by Postulate 4, Part II). But that which constitutes the form of the human Body consists in this: that its parts communicate their motions to one another according to a certain fixed ratio (by the Definition before Lemma 4, which see after Proposition 13, Part II). Therefore, those things which bring about the preservation of the ratio of motion and rest which the parts of the human Body have to one another likewise preserve the form of the human Body, and consequently bring it about (by Postulates 3 and 6, Part II) that the human Body can be affected in many ways, and that it can affect external bodies in many ways; and thus (by the preceding Proposition) they are good. Next, those things which bring it about that the parts of the human Body acquire another ratio of motion and rest likewise (by the same Definition, Part","kind":"passage","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_QGOH_PQ_3:P0036"]},{"id":"RAW_QGOH_PQ_3:P0037","text":"II) bring it about that the human Body assumes another form; that is (as is self-evident, and as we noted at the end of the Preface of this Part), that the human Body is destroyed and consequently rendered wholly unfit to be affected in many ways; and therefore (by the preceding Proposition) they are evil. Q. E. D.","kind":"passage","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_QGOH_PQ_3:P0037"]},{"id":"RAW_QGOH_PQ_3:P0038","text":"SCHOLIUM","kind":"passage","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_QGOH_PQ_3:P0038"]},{"id":"RAW_QGOH_PQ_3:P0039","text":"How much these things can harm or benefit the Mind will be explained in Part V. But here it should be noted that I understand the Body to undergo death when its parts are so disposed that they acquire another ratio of motion and rest to one another. For I do not dare deny that the human Body, while retaining the circulation of the blood and other things on account of which the Body is thought to live, may nevertheless be changed into another nature altogether different from its own. For no reason compels me to maintain that the Body does not die unless it is changed into a corpse; indeed, experience itself seems to suggest otherwise.","kind":"passage","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_QGOH_PQ_3:P0039"]},{"id":"RAW_QGOH_PQ_3:P0040","text":"For it sometimes happens that a man undergoes such changes that I would not readily say that he is the same man; as I have heard related of a certain Spanish poet, who had been seized by an illness and, although he recovered from it, nevertheless remained so forgetful of his past life that he did not believe the Fables and Tragedies which he had composed to be his own; and he could surely have been taken for an adult infant, had he also forgotten his native language. And if this seems incredible, what shall we say of infants? A man of advanced age believes their nature to be so different from his own that he could not be persuaded that he had ever been an infant, unless he formed a conjecture about himself from others. But lest I furnish the superstitious with material for stirring up new questions, I prefer to leave these matters undecided.","kind":"passage","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_QGOH_PQ_3:P0040"]},{"id":"RAW_QGOH_PQ_3:P0041","text":"PROPOSITION XL","kind":"passage","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_QGOH_PQ_3:P0041"]},{"id":"RAW_QGOH_PQ_3:P0042","text":"Those things which conduce to the common Society of human beings, or which bring it about that human beings live harmoniously, are useful; and, conversely, those things are evil which introduce discord into the State.","kind":"passage","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_QGOH_PQ_3:P0042"]},{"id":"RAW_QGOH_PQ_3:P0043","text":"DEMONSTRATION","kind":"passage","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_QGOH_PQ_3:P0043"]},{"id":"RAW_QGOH_PQ_3:P0044","text":"For those things which bring it about that human beings live harmoniously also bring it about that they live under the guidance of Reason (by Proposition 35 of this Part), and therefore (by Propositions 26 and 27 of this Part) are good; and (by the same reasoning), conversely, those things are evil which provoke discords. Q. E. D.","kind":"passage","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_QGOH_PQ_3:P0044"]},{"id":"RAW_QGOH_PQ_3:P0045","text":"PROPOSITION XLI","kind":"passage","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_QGOH_PQ_3:P0045"]},{"id":"RAW_QGOH_PQ_3:P0046","text":"Joy is not directly evil, but good; Sadness, on the other hand, is directly evil.","kind":"passage","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_QGOH_PQ_3:P0046"]},{"id":"RAW_QGOH_PQ_3:P0047","text":"DEMONSTRATION","kind":"passage","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_QGOH_PQ_3:P0047"]},{"id":"RAW_QGOH_PQ_3:P0048","text":"Joy (by Proposition 11, Part III, together with its Scholium) is an affect by which the body's power of acting is increased or assisted; Sadness, on the other hand, is an affect by which the body's power of acting is diminished or restrained; and therefore (by Proposition 38 of this Part) Joy is directly good, etc. Q. E. D.","kind":"passage","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_QGOH_PQ_3:P0048"]},{"id":"RAW_QGOH_PQ_3:P0049","text":"PROPOSITION XLII","kind":"passage","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_QGOH_PQ_3:P0049"]},{"id":"RAW_QGOH_PQ_3:P0050","text":"Cheerfulness cannot have excess, but is always good; and, on the contrary, Melancholy is always evil.","kind":"passage","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_QGOH_PQ_3:P0050"]},{"id":"RAW_QGOH_PQ_3:P0051","text":"DEMONSTRATION","kind":"passage","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_QGOH_PQ_3:P0051"]},{"id":"RAW_QGOH_PQ_3:P0052","text":"Cheerfulness (see its Definition in the Scholium to Prop. 11, Part III) is Joy which, insofar as it is referred to the Body, consists in this, that all the parts of the Body are equally affected; that is (by Prop. 11, Part III), that the Body's power of acting is increased or aided, so that all its parts maintain the same ratio of motion and rest to one another; and therefore (by Prop. 39 of this Part) Cheerfulness is always good, nor can it have excess. But Melancholy (the Definition of which also see in the same Scholium to Prop. 11, Part III) is Sadness which, insofar as it is referred to the Body, consists in this, that the Body's power of acting is absolutely diminished or restrained; and therefore (by Prop. 38 of this Part) is always evil. Q. E. D.","kind":"passage","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_QGOH_PQ_3:P0052"]},{"id":"RAW_QGOH_PQ_3:P0053","text":"PROPOSITION XLIII","kind":"passage","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_QGOH_PQ_3:P0053"]},{"id":"RAW_QGOH_PQ_3:P0054","text":"Titillation can have excess, and be evil; but Pain can be good insofar as Titillation, or Joy, is evil.","kind":"passage","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_QGOH_PQ_3:P0054"]},{"id":"RAW_QGOH_PQ_3:P0055","text":"DEMONSTRATION","kind":"passage","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_QGOH_PQ_3:P0055"]},{"id":"RAW_QGOH_PQ_3:P0056","text":"Titillation is Joy which, insofar as it is referred to the Body, consists in this, that one or several of its parts are affected more than the rest (see its Definition in the Scholium to Prop. 11, Part III); and the power of this affect can be so great that it surpasses the other actions of the Body (by Prop. 6 of this Part), adheres to it obstinately, and thus prevents the Body from being apt to be affected in many other ways; and therefore (by Prop. 38 of this Part) it can be evil. Next, Pain, which is, on the contrary, Sadness, considered in itself alone, cannot be good (by Prop. 41 of this Part). But since its force and increase are defined by the power of an external cause compared with our own (by Prop. 5 of this Part), we can therefore conceive infinite degrees and modes of forces of this affect (by Prop. 3 of this Part); and consequently conceive one such as can restrain Titillation, so that it does not have excess, and insofar (by the first part of the Proposition","kind":"passage","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_QGOH_PQ_3:P0056"]},{"id":"RAW_QGOH_PQ_3:P0057","text":"of this Part) bring it about that the Body is not rendered less apt; and consequently, insofar, it will be good. Q. E. D.","kind":"passage","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_QGOH_PQ_3:P0057"]},{"id":"RAW_QGOH_PQ_3:P0058","text":"PROPOSITION XLIV","kind":"passage","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_QGOH_PQ_3:P0058"]},{"id":"RAW_QGOH_PQ_3:P0059","text":"Love and desire can have excess.","kind":"passage","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_QGOH_PQ_3:P0059"]},{"id":"RAW_QGOH_PQ_3:P0060","text":"DEMONSTRATION","kind":"passage","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_QGOH_PQ_3:P0060"]},{"id":"RAW_QGOH_PQ_3:P0061","text":"Love is Joy (by Def. 6 of the Affects) accompanied by the idea of an external cause; therefore Titillation (by the Scholium to Prop. 11, Part III) accompanied by the idea of an external cause is Love; and consequently Love (by the preceding Prop.) can have excess. Next, Desire is greater to the extent that the affect from which it arises is greater (by Prop. 37, Part III). Wherefore, as an affect (by Prop. 6 of this Part) can surpass a man's other actions, so also the Desire which arises from the same affect can surpass the other Desires, and consequently can have the same excess which we showed Titillation to have in the preceding Proposition. Q. E. D.","kind":"passage","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_QGOH_PQ_3:P0061"]},{"id":"RAW_QGOH_PQ_3:P0062","text":"SCHOLIUM","kind":"passage","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_QGOH_PQ_3:P0062"]},{"id":"RAW_QGOH_PQ_3:P0063","text":"Cheerfulness, which I have said to be good, is conceived more easily than it is observed. For the affects with which we struggle daily are for the most part referred to some part of the Body which is affected more than the rest; and consequently the affects for the most part have excess, and so hold the Mind in the contemplation of one object alone that it cannot think of others; and although men are subject to several affects, and consequently few are found who are always struggling with one and the same affect, nevertheless there are not lacking those to whom one and the same affect obstinately clings. For we sometimes see men so affected by one object that, although it is not present, they nevertheless believe that they have it before them; and when this happens to a man who is not asleep, we say that he is delirious or insane; nor are those believed to be less insane who burn with Love and dream night and day only of a beloved woman or a harlot, because they are wont to provoke laughter.","kind":"passage","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_QGOH_PQ_3:P0063"]},{"id":"RAW_QGOH_PQ_3:P0064","text":"But when the avaricious man thinks of nothing other than gain or money, and the ambitious man of glory, etc., these are not believed to be delirious, because they are wont to be troublesome and are judged worthy of Hatred. But in truth Avarice, Ambition, Lust, etc., are species of delirium, although they are not numbered among diseases.","kind":"passage","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_QGOH_PQ_3:P0064"]},{"id":"RAW_QGOH_PQ_3:P0065","text":"PROPOSITION XLV","kind":"passage","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_QGOH_PQ_3:P0065"]},{"id":"RAW_QGOH_PQ_3:P0066","text":"Hatred can never be good.","kind":"passage","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_QGOH_PQ_3:P0066"]},{"id":"RAW_QGOH_PQ_3:P0067","text":"DEMONSTRATION","kind":"passage","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_QGOH_PQ_3:P0067"]},{"id":"RAW_QGOH_PQ_3:P0068","text":"We strive to destroy the man whom we hate (by Prop. 39, Part III), that is (by Prop. 37 of this Part), we strive for something which is evil; Therefore, etc. Q. E. D.","kind":"passage","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_QGOH_PQ_3:P0068"]},{"id":"RAW_QGOH_PQ_3:P0069","text":"SCHOLIUM","kind":"passage","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_QGOH_PQ_3:P0069"]},{"id":"RAW_QGOH_PQ_3:P0070","text":"Note that here and in what follows, by Hatred I understand only that which is toward men.","kind":"passage","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_QGOH_PQ_3:P0070"]},{"id":"RAW_QGOH_PQ_3:P0071","text":"COROLLARY I","kind":"passage","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_QGOH_PQ_3:P0071"]},{"id":"RAW_QGOH_PQ_3:P0072","text":"Envy, Derision, Contempt, Anger, Vengeance, and the remaining affects which are referred to Hatred or arise from it, are evil; as is also evident from Prop. 39, Part III and Prop. 37 of this Part.","kind":"passage","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_QGOH_PQ_3:P0072"]},{"id":"RAW_QGOH_PQ_3:P0073","text":"COROLLARY II","kind":"passage","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_QGOH_PQ_3:P0073"]},{"id":"RAW_QGOH_PQ_3:P0074","text":"Whatever we desire from the fact that we are affected by hatred is base and unjust in the State. This is also clear from Prop. 39 p. III and from the Definitions of the base and the unjust, which see in the Scholiums of Prop. 37 of this Part.","kind":"passage","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_QGOH_PQ_3:P0074"]},{"id":"RAW_QGOH_PQ_3:P0075","text":"SCHOLIUM","kind":"passage","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_QGOH_PQ_3:P0075"]},{"id":"RAW_QGOH_PQ_3:P0076","text":"Between Mockery (which in Coroll. I I said to be bad) and laughter I recognize a great difference. For laughter, as also jesting, is mere Joy; and therefore, provided that it has no excess, it is good in itself (by Prop. 41 of this Part). Indeed, nothing except a grim and sad superstition forbids one to take delight. For why is it more fitting to assuage hunger and thirst than to dispel melancholy? This is my rule, and thus have I persuaded myself. No deity, nor anyone else unless envious, takes delight in my weakness and discomfort, nor reckons as virtue in us tears, sobs, fear, and other things of this kind, which are signs of a powerless mind; but, on the contrary, the greater the Joy by which we are affected, the greater the perfection to which we pass, that is, the more we must participate in the divine nature. Therefore, to use things and to take delight in them, so far as possible (not indeed to the point of nausea, for that is not taking delight), is the part of a wise man.","kind":"passage","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_QGOH_PQ_3:P0076"]},{"id":"RAW_QGOH_PQ_3:P0077","text":"It is the part, I say, of a wise man to restore and refresh himself with moderate and pleasant food and drink, as also with scents, the charm of green plants, adornment, music, exercise-games, theatres, and other things of this kind, which each person can use without any injury to another. For the human Body is composed of very many parts of diverse natures, which continually require new and varied nourishment, so that the whole Body may be equally fitted for all the things that can follow from its nature, and consequently so that the Mind also may be equally fitted to understand many things at once. This manner of life therefore agrees best both with our principles and with common practice; wherefore, if any manner of life is best, this is so, and is to be recommended in every way; nor is there need to treat these matters more clearly or at greater length.","kind":"passage","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_QGOH_PQ_3:P0077"]},{"id":"RAW_QGOH_PQ_3:P0078","text":"PROPOSITION XLVI","kind":"passage","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_QGOH_PQ_3:P0078"]},{"id":"RAW_QGOH_PQ_3:P0079","text":"He who lives according to the guidance of Reason strives, as far as he can, to requite another's Hatred, Anger, Contempt, etc., toward him with Love in return, or Generosity.","kind":"passage","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_QGOH_PQ_3:P0079"]},{"id":"RAW_QGOH_PQ_3:P0080","text":"DEMONSTRATION","kind":"passage","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_QGOH_PQ_3:P0080"]},{"id":"RAW_QGOH_PQ_3:P0081","text":"All affects of Hatred are bad (by Coroll. 1 of the preceding Prop.); and therefore he who lives according to the guidance of Reason will, as far as he can, strive to bring it about that he is not assailed by affects of Hatred (by Prop. 19 of this Part), and consequently (by Prop. 37 of this Part) will strive that another also not suffer the same affects. But Hatred is increased by reciprocal Hatred, and can, on the contrary, be extinguished by Love (by Prop. 43 p. III), so that Hatred passes into Love (by Prop. 44 p. III); Therefore, he who lives according to the guidance of Reason will strive to requite another's Hatred, etc., with Love in return, that is, with Generosity (for the Defin. of which see the Schol. of Prop. 59 p. III). Q. E. D.","kind":"passage","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_QGOH_PQ_3:P0081"]},{"id":"RAW_QGOH_PQ_3:P0082","text":"SCHOLIUM","kind":"passage","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_QGOH_PQ_3:P0082"]},{"id":"RAW_QGOH_PQ_3:P0083","text":"He who wishes to avenge injuries by reciprocal Hatred surely lives miserably. But he who, on the contrary, strives to overcome Hatred with Love, certainly fights joyfully and securely, resists many men as easily as one, and has the least possible need of the aid of fortune. Moreover, those whom he conquers yield joyfully, indeed not from a defect but from an increase of strength; all of which follows so clearly from the definitions of Love and Intellect alone that there is no need to demonstrate them severally.","kind":"passage","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_QGOH_PQ_3:P0083"]},{"id":"RAW_QGOH_PQ_3:P0084","text":"PROPOSITION XLVII","kind":"passage","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_QGOH_PQ_3:P0084"]},{"id":"RAW_QGOH_PQ_3:P0085","text":"The affects of Hope and Fear cannot be good in themselves.","kind":"passage","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_QGOH_PQ_3:P0085"]},{"id":"RAW_QGOH_PQ_3:P0086","text":"DEMONSTRATION","kind":"passage","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_QGOH_PQ_3:P0086"]},{"id":"RAW_QGOH_PQ_3:P0087","text":"The affects of Hope and Fear are not given without Sadness. For Fear is (by Defin. 13 of the Affects) Sadness, and Hope (see the Explanation of Defins. 12 and 13 of the Affects) is not given without Fear; and therefore (by Prop. 41 of this Part) these affects cannot be good in themselves, but only insofar as they can restrain an excess of Joy (by Prop. 43 of this Part). Q. E. D.","kind":"passage","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_QGOH_PQ_3:P0087"]},{"id":"RAW_QGOH_PQ_3:P0088","text":"SCHOLIUM","kind":"passage","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_QGOH_PQ_3:P0088"]},{"id":"RAW_QGOH_PQ_3:P0089","text":"To this must be added that these affects indicate a defect of knowledge and a powerlessness of Mind; and for this reason also Security, Despair, Gladness, and Remorse of Conscience are signs of a powerless mind. For although Security and Gladness are affects of Joy, they nevertheless suppose that Sadness has preceded them, namely, Hope and Fear. Therefore, the more we strive to live according to the guidance of Reason, the more we strive to depend less on Hope, to free ourselves from Fear, to command fortune as far as we can, and to direct our actions by the sure counsel of Reason.","kind":"passage","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_QGOH_PQ_3:P0089"]},{"id":"RAW_QGOH_PQ_3:P0090","text":"PROPOSITION XLVIII","kind":"passage","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_QGOH_PQ_3:P0090"]},{"id":"RAW_QGOH_PQ_3:P0091","text":"The affects of Esteem and Contempt are always bad.","kind":"passage","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_QGOH_PQ_3:P0091"]},{"id":"RAW_QGOH_PQ_3:P0092","text":"DEMONSTRATION","kind":"passage","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_QGOH_PQ_3:P0092"]},{"id":"RAW_QGOH_PQ_3:P0093","text":"For these affects (by Defins. 21 and 22 of the Affects) are contrary to reason; and therefore (by Props. 26 and 27 of this Part) they are bad. Q. E. D.","kind":"passage","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_QGOH_PQ_3:P0093"]},{"id":"RAW_QGOH_PQ_3:P0094","text":"PROPOSITION XLIX","kind":"passage","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_QGOH_PQ_3:P0094"]},{"id":"RAW_QGOH_PQ_3:P0095","text":"Esteem easily renders proud the man who is esteemed.","kind":"passage","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_QGOH_PQ_3:P0095"]},{"id":"RAW_QGOH_PQ_3:P0096","text":"DEMONSTRATION","kind":"passage","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_QGOH_PQ_3:P0096"]},{"id":"RAW_QGOH_PQ_3:P0097","text":"If we see that someone has, out of love, an opinion of us beyond what is just, we shall easily glory (by the Scholium to Prop. 41, Part III), or be affected with Joy (by Def. 30 of the Affects); and we shall easily believe the good which we hear proclaimed of us (by Prop. 25, Part III); and thus, out of self-love, we shall have an opinion of ourselves beyond what is just, that is (by Def. 28 of the Affects), we shall easily become proud. Q.E.D.","kind":"passage","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_QGOH_PQ_3:P0097"]},{"id":"RAW_QGOH_PQ_3:P0098","text":"PROPOSITION L","kind":"passage","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_QGOH_PQ_3:P0098"]}],"source_digest":"67f31b5fec8379cca09eac184c514fbfd2b6a7a13c8adf1f90a448702882704e","source_language_code":"la","original_title":"Pars quarta - De servitute humana seu de affectuum viribus · passage 3"}],"passage_basis":"frozen-grounding","original_title":"Pars quarta - De servitute humana seu de affectuum viribus · passage 3","title_language_code":"en"},{"id":"RAW_QGOH_PQ_4","title":"Part Four - On Human Bondage, or the Powers of the Affects · passage 4","order":17,"chapter_id":"CH_QGOH_PQ_4","text":"Commiseratio in homine, qui ex ductu Rationis vivit, per se mala et inutilis est.\n\nDEMONSTRATIO\n\nCommiseratio enim (per 18 Affect. Defin.) Tristitia est ; ac proinde (per Prop. 41 hujus) per se mala ; bonum autem, quod ex ea sequitur, quod scilicet hominem, cujus nos miseret, a miseria liberare conamur (per Coroll. 3 Prop. 27 p. III), ex solo Rationis dictamine facere cupimus (per Prop. 37 hujus) ; nec nisi ex solo Rationis dictamine aliquid, quod certo scimus bonum esse, agere possumus (per Prop. 27 hujus) ; atque adeo commiseratio in homine, qui ex ductu Rationis vivit, per se mala est et inutilis. Q. E. D.\n\nCOROLLARIUM\n\nHinc sequitur, quod homo, qui ex dictamine Rationis vivit, conatur quantum potest, efficere, ne commiseratione tangatur.\n\nSCHOLIUM\n\nQui recte novit, omnia ex naturæ divinæ necessitate sequi, et secundum æternas Naturæ leges et regulas fieri, is sane nihil reperiet quod Odio, Risu aut Contemptu dignum sit, nec cujusquam miserebitur sed quantum humana fert virtus, conabitur bene agere, ut ajunt, et lætari. Huc accedit, quod is, qui Commiserationis affectu facile tangitur, et alterius miseria vel lacrimis movetur, sæpe aliquid agit, cujus postea ipsum pœnitet ; tam quia ex affectu nihil agimus, quod certo scimus bonum esse, quam quia facile falsis lacrimis decipimur. Atque hic expresse loquor de homine, qui ex ductu Rationis vivit. Nam qui nec Ratione nec Commiseratione movetur, ut aliis auxilio sit, is recte inhumanus appellatur, nam (per Prop. 27 p. III) homini dissimilis esse videtur.\n\nPROPOSITIO LI\n\nFavor Rationi non repugnat, sed cum eadem convenire et ab eadem oriri potest.\n\nDEMONSTRATIO\n\nEst enim Favor Amor erga illum, qui alteri benefecit (per 19 Affect. Defin.) ; atque adeo ad Mentem referri potest, quatenus hæc agere dicitur (per Prop. 59 p. III) ; hoc est (per Prop. 3 p. III), quatenus intelligit ; ac proinde cum Ratione convenit, etc. Q. E. D.\n\nALITER\n\nQui ex ductu Rationis vivit, bonum, quod sibi appetit, alteri etiam cupit (per Prop. 37 hujus) ; quare ex eo, quod ipse aliquem videt alteri benefacere, ipsius benefaciendi conatus juvatur, hoc est (per Schol. Prop. 11 p. III) lætabitur, idque (ex hypothesi) concomitante idea illius, qui alteri benefecit ; ac proinde (per 19 Affect. Defin.) ei favet. Q. E. D.\n\nSCHOLIUM\n\nIndignatio, prout ipsa a nobis definitur (vide 20 Affect. Defin.), est necessario mala (per Prop. 45 hujus) ; sed notandum, quod, quando summa potestas desiderio, quo tenetur, tutandæ pacis civem punit, qui alteri injuriam fecit, eandem civi indignari non dico, quia non Odio percita ad perdendum civem, sed pietate mota eundem punit.\n\nPROPOSITIO LII\n\nAcquiescentia in se ipso ex Ratione oriri potest, et ea sola acquiescentia, quæ ex Ratione oritur, summa est, quæ potest dari.\n\nDEMONSTRATIO\n\nAcquiescentia in se ipso est Lætitia orta ex eo, quod homo se ipsum suamque agendi potentiam contemplatur (per 25 Affect. Defin.). At vera hominis agendi potentia seu virtus est ipsa Ratio (per Prop. 3 p. III), quam homo clare et distincte contemplatur (per Prop. 40 et 43 p. II) ; Ergo Acquiescentia in se ipso ex Ratione oritur. Deinde nihil homo dum se ipsum contemplatur, clare et distincte, sive adæquate percipit, nisi ea, quæ ex ipsius agendi potentia sequuntur (per Defin. 2 p. III), hoc est (per Prop. 3 p. III) quæ ex ipsius intelligendi potentia sequuntur ; adeoque ex sola hac contemplatione summa, quæ dari potest Acquiescentia oritur. Q. E. D.\n\nSCHOLIUM\n\nEst revera Acquiescentia in se ipso summum, quod sperare possumus. Nam (ut Prop. 25 hujus ostendimus) nemo suum esse alicujus finis causa conservare conatur ; et quia hæc Acquiescentia magis magisque fovetur et corroboratur laudibus (per Coroll. Prop. 53 p. III), et contra (per Coroll. Prop. 55 p. III) vituperio magis magisque turbatur, ideo gloria maxime ducimur, et vitam cum probro vix ferre possumus.\n\nPROPOSITIO LIII\n\nHumilitas virtus non est, sive ex Ratione non oritur.\n\nDEMONSTRATIO\n\nHumilitas est Tristitia, quæ ex eo oritur, quod homo suam impotentiam contemplatur (per 26 Affect. Defin.). Quatenus autem homo se ipsum vera Ratione cognoscit, eatenus suam essentiam intelligere supponitur, hoc est (per Prop. 7 p. III) suam potentiam. Quare si homo, dum se ipsum contemplatur, aliquam suam impotentiam percipit, id non ex eo est, quod se intelligit sed (ut Prop. 55 p. III ostendimus) ex eo, quod ipsius agendi potentia coercetur. Quod si supponamus, hominem suam impotentiam concipere ex eo, quod aliquid se potentius intelligit, cujus cognitione suam agendi potentiam determinat, tum nihil aliud concipimus, quam quod homo se ipsum distincte intelligit, sive (per Prop. 26 hujus) quod ipsius agendi potentia juvatur. Quare Humilitas, seu Tristitia quæ ex eo oritur, quod homo suam impotentiam contemplatur, non ex vera contemplatione seu Ratione oritur, nec virtus, sed passio est. Q. E. D.\n\nPROPOSITIO LIV\n\nPœnitentia virtus non est, sive ex Ratione non oritur ; sed is, quem facti pœnitet, bis miser seu impotens est.\n\nDEMONSTRATIO\n\nHujus prima pars demonstratur ut præced. Propositio. Secunda autem ex sola hujus affectus Definitione (vide 27 Affect. Defin.) patet. Nam primo prava Cupiditate, dein Tristitia vinci se patitur.\n\nSCHOLIUM\n\nQuia homines raro ex dictamine Rationis vivunt, ideo hi duo affectus, nempe Humilitas et Pœnitentia, et præter hos Spes et Metus, plus utilitatis quam damni afferunt ; atque adeo, quandoquidem peccandum est, in istam partem potius peccandum. Nam, si homines animo impotentes æque omnes superbirent, nullius rei ipsos puderet, nec ipsi quicquam metuerent, qui vinculis conjungi constringique possent ? Terret vulgus, nisi metuat ; quare non mirum, quod Prophetæ, qui non paucorum, sed communi utilitati consuluerunt, tantopere Humilitatem, Pœnitentiam et Reverentiam commendaverint. Et revera, qui hisce affectibus sunt obnoxii, multo facilius quam alii duci possunt ut tandem ex ductu Rationis vivant, hoc est, ut liberi sint, et beatorum vita fruantur.\n\nPROPOSITIO LV\n\nMaxima Superbia vel Abjectio est maxima sui ignorantia.\n\nDEMONSTRATIO\n\nPatet ex Defin. 28 et 29 Affect.\n\nPROPOSITIO LVI\n\nMaxima Superbia vel Abjectio maximam animi impotentiam indicat.\n\nDEMONSTRATIO\n\nPrimum virtutis fundamentum est suum esse conservare (per Coroll. Prop. 22 hujus), idque ex ductu Rationis (per Prop. 24 hujus). Qui igitur se ipsum ignorat, omnium virtutum fundamentum, et consequenter omnes virtutes ignorat. Deinde ex virtute agere nihil aliud est, quam ex ductu Rationis agere (per Prop. 24 hujus) ; et qui ex ductu Rationis agit, scire necessario debet, se ex ductu Rationis agere (per Prop. 43 p. II). Qui itaque se ipsum, et consequenter (ut jam jam ostendimus) omnes virtutes, maxime ignorat, is minime ex virtute agit, hoc est (ut ex Defin. 8 hujus patet), maxime animo est impotens atque adeo (per Prop. præced.) maxima superbia vel Abjectio maximam animi impotentiam indicat. Q. E. D.\n\nCOROLLARIUM\n\nHinc clare sequitur, superbos et abjectos maxime affectibus esse obnoxios.\n\nSCHOLIUM\n\nAbjectio tamen facilius corrigi potest quam superbia ; quandoquidem hæc Lætitiæ, illa autem Tristitiæ est affectus, atque adeo (per Prop. 18 hujus) hæc illa fortior est.\n\nPROPOSITIO LVII\n\nSuperbus parasitorum seu adulatorum præsentiam amat, generosorum autem odit.\n\nDEMONSTRATIO\n\nSuperbia est Lætitia orta ex eo, quod homo de se plus justo sentit (per Defin. 28 et 6 Affect.) ; quam opinionem homo superbus, quantum potest, fovere conabitur (vide Schol. Prop. 13 p. III) ; adeoque parasitorum vel adulatorum (horum Definitiones omisi, quia nimis noti sunt) præsentiam amabit, et generosorum, qui de ipso ut par est, sentiunt, fugiet. Q. E. D.\n\nSCHOLIUM\n\nNimis longum foret hic omnia Superbiæ mala enumerare, quandoquidem omnibus affectibus obnoxii sunt superbi ; sed nullis minus, quam affectibus Amoris et Misericordiæ. Sed hic minime tacendum est, quod ille etiam superbus vocetur, qui de reliquis minus justo sentit, atque adeo hoc sensu Superbia definienda est, quod sit Lætitia orta ex falsa opinione, quod homo se supra reliquos esse putat. Et Abjectio huic Superbiæ contraria definienda esset Tristitia orta ex falsa opinione, quod homo se infra reliquos esse credit. At hoc posito facile concipimus, superbum necessario esse invidum (vide Schol. Prop. 55 p. III), et eos maxime odio habere, qui maxime ob virtutes laudantur ; nec facile eorum Odium Amore aut beneficio vinci (vide Schol. Prop. 41 p. III) ; et eorum tantummodo præsentia delectari, qui animo ejus impotenti morem gerunt, et ex stulto insanum faciunt.\n\nAbjectio quamvis Superbiæ sit contraria, est tamen abjectus superbo proximus. Nam, quandoquidem ejus Tristitia ex eo oritur, quod suam impotentiam ex aliorum potentia seu virtute judicat, levabitur ergo ejus Tristitia, hoc est lætabitur, si ejus imaginatio in alienis vitiis contemplandis occupetur, unde illud proverbium natum : solamen miseris socios habuisse malorum ; et contra eo magis contristabitur, quo se magis infra reliquos esse crediderit ; unde fit, ut nulli magis ad Invidiam sint proni, quam Abjecti ; et ut isti maxime hominum facta observare conentur ad carpendum magis, quam ad eadem corrigendum, et ut tandem solam Abjectionem laudent, eaque glorientur ; sed ita, ut tamen abjecti videantur. Atque hæc ex hoc affectu tam necessario sequuntur, quam ex natura trianguli, quod ejus tres anguli æquales sint duobus rectis ; et jam dixi, me hos et similes affectus malos vocare, quatenus ad solam humanam utilitatem attendo. Sed Naturæ leges communem Naturæ ordinem, cujus homo pars est, respiciunt ; quod hic in transitu monere volui, ne quis putaret me hic hominum vitia et absurda facta narrare, non autem rerum naturam et proprietates demonstrare voluisse. Nam, ut in Præfatione Partis tertiæ dixi, humanos affectus eorumque proprietates perinde considero, ac reliqua naturalia. Et sane humani affectus, si non humanam, Naturæ saltem potentiam et artificium non minus indicant, quam multa alia, quæ admiramur, quorumque contemplatione delectamur. Sed pergo de affectibus ea notare, quæ hominibus utilitatem adferunt, vel quæ iisdem damnum inferunt.\n\nPROPOSITIO LVIII\n\nGloria Rationi non repugnat, sed ab ea oriri potest.\n\nDEMONSTRATIO\n\nPatet ex 30 Affect. Defin. et ex definitione Honesti, quam vide in Schol. 1 Prop. 37 hujus.\n\nSCHOLIUM\n\nVana, quæ dicitur, Gloria est acquiescentia in se ipso, quæ sola vulgi opinione fovetur ; eaque cessante, cessat ipsa acquiescentia, hoc est (per Schol. Prop. 52 hujus) summum bonum, quod unusquisque amat ; unde fit, ut qui vulgi opinione gloriatur, quotidiana cura anxius nitatur, faciat, experiatur, ut famam conservet. Est namque vulgus varius et inconstans, atque adeo, nisi conservetur fama, cito abolescit ; imo quia omnes vulgi captare applausus cupiunt, facile unusquisque alterius famam reprimit ; ex quo, quandoquidem de summo, quod æstimatur, bono certatur, ingens libido oritur se invicem quocunque modo opprimendi, et qui tandem victor evadit, gloriatur magis, quod alteri obfuit, quam quod sibi profuit. Est igitur hæc gloria seu acquiescentia revera vana, quia nulla est. Quæ de Pudore notanda sunt, colliguntur facile ex iis, quæ de Misericordia et Pœnitentia diximus. Hoc tantum addo, quod ut Commiseratio, sic etiam Pudor, quamvis non sit virtus, bonus tamen est, quatenus indicat, homini, qui Pudore suffunditur, cupiditatem inesse honeste vivendi ; sicut dolor, qui eatenus bonus dicitur, quatenus indicat, partem læsam nondum esse putrefactam ; quare, quamvis homo, quem facti alicujus pudet, revera sit tristis, est tamen perfectior impudenti, qui nullam habet honeste vivendi cupiditatem.\n\nAtque hæc sunt, quæ de affectibus Lætitiæ et Tristitiæ notare susceperam. Ad Cupiditates quod attinet, hæ sane bonæ aut malæ sunt, quatenus ex bonis aut malis affectibus oriuntur. Sed omnes revera, quatenus ex affectibus, qui passiones sunt, in nobis ingenerantur, cœcæ sunt (ut facile colligitur ex iis, quæ in Schol. Prop. 44 hujus diximus), nec ullius usus essent, si homines facile duci possent, ut ex solo Rationis dictamine viverent, ut jam paucis ostendam.\n\nPROPOSITIO LIX\n\nAd omnes actiones, ad quas ex affectu, qui passio est, determinamur, possumus absque eo a Ratione determinari.\n\nDEMONSTRATIO\n\nEx Ratione agere nihil aliud est (per Prop. 3 et Defin. 2 p. III), quam ea agere, quæ ex necessitate nostræ naturæ, in se sola consideratæ, sequuntur. At Tristitia eatenus mala, est quatenus hanc agendi potentiam minuit vel coercet (per Prop. 41 hujus) ; ergo ex hoc affectu ad nullam actionem possumus determinari, quam non possemus agere, si Ratione duceremur. Præterea Lætitia eatenus mala est, quatenus impedit, quo minus homo ad agendum sit aptus (per Prop. 43 et 44 hujus) ; atque adeo eatenus etiam ad nullam actionem determinari possumus, quam non possemus agere, si Ratione duceremur. Denique quatenus Lætitia bona est, eatenus cum Ratione convenit (consistit enim in eo, quod hominis agendi potentia augetur vel juvatur) ; nec passio est, nisi quatenus hominis agendi potentia non eo usque augetur, ut se suasque actiones adæquate concipiat (per Prop. 3 p. III cum ejus Schol.) ; Quare si homo Lætitia affectus ad tantam perfectionem duceretur, ut se suasque actiones adæquate conciperet, ad easdem actiones, ad quas jam ex affectibus, qui passiones sunt, determinatur, aptus, imo aptior esset. At omnes affectus ad Lætitiam, Tristitiam, vel Cupiditatem referuntur (vide explicationem quartæ Aff. Defin.), et Cupiditas (per 1 Affect. Defin.) nihil aliud est, quam ipse agendi conatus ; ergo ad omnes actiones, ad quas ex affectu, qui passio est, determinamur, possumus absque eo sola Ratione duci. Q. E. D.\n\nALITER\n\nActio quæcunque eatenus dicitur mala, quatenus ex eo oritur, quod Odio aut aliquo malo affectu affecti sumus (vide Coroll. 1 Prop. 45 hujus). At nulla actio, in se sola considerata, bona aut mala est (ut in Præfatione hujus ostendimus), sed una eademque actio jam bona jam mala est ; ergo ad eandem actionem, quæ jam mala est, sive quæ ex aliquo malo affectu oritur, Ratione duci possumus (per Prop. 19 hujus). Q. E. D.\n\nSCHOLIUM\n\nExplicantur hæc clarius exemplo. Nempe verberandi actio, quatenus physice consideratur, et ad hoc tantum attendimus, quod homo brachium tollit, manum claudit, totumque brachium vi deorsum movet, virtus est, quæ ex Corporis humani fabrica concipitur. Si itaque homo, Ira vel Odio commotus, determinatur ad claudendam manum vel brachium movendum, id, ut in Parte secunda ostendimus, fit, quia una eademque actio potest jungi quibuscunque rerum imaginibus ; atque adeo tam ex iis imaginibus rerum, quas confuse, quam quas clare et distincte concipimus, ad unam eandemque actionem determinari possumus. Apparet itaque, quod omnis Cupiditas, quæ ex affectu, qui passio est, oritur, nullius esset usus, si homines Ratione duci possent. Videamus jam, cur Cupiditas, quæ ex affectu, qui passio est, oritur, cœca a nobis appellatur.\n\nPROPOSITIO LX\n\nCupiditas, quæ oritur ex Lætitia vel Tristitia, quæ ad unam vel ad aliquot, non autem ad omnes Corporis partes refertur, rationem utilitatis totius hominis non habet.\n\nDEMONSTRATIO\n\nPonatur ex. gr. Corporis pars A vi alicujus causæ externæ ita corroborari, ut reliquis prævaleat (per Prop. 6 hujus). Hæc pars vires suas amittere non conabitur, ut reliquæ Corporis partes suo fungantur officio ; deberet enim vim seu potentiam habere vires suas amittendi, quod (per Prop. 6 p. III) est absurdum. Conabitur itaque illa pars, et consequenter (per Prop. 7 et 12 p. III) Mens etiam, illum statum conservare ; adeoque Cupiditas, quæ ex tali affectu Lætitiæ oritur, rationem totius non habet. Quod si contra supponatur pars A coerceri, ut reliquæ prævaleant, eodem modo demonstratur, quod nec Cupiditas, quæ ex Tristitia oritur, rationem totius habeat. Q. E. D.\n\nSCHOLIUM\n\nCum itaque Lætitia plerumque (per Schol. Prop. 44 hujus) ad unam Corporis partem referatur, cupimus ergo plerumque nostrum esse conservare, nulla habita Ratione integræ nostræ valetudinis ; ad quod accedit, quod Cupiditates, quibus maxime tenemur (per Coroll. Prop. 9 hujus) temporis tantum præsentis, non autem futuri habent rationem.\n\nPROPOSITIO LXI\n\nCupiditas, quæ ex Ratione oritur, excessum habere nequit.\n\nDEMONSTRATIO\n\nCupiditas (per 1 Affect. Defin.), absolute considerata, est ipsa hominis essentia, quatenus quocunque modo determinata concipitur ad aliquid agendum ; adeoque Cupiditas, quæ ex Ratione oritur, hoc est (per Prop. 3 p. III) quæ in nobis ingeneratur quatenus agimus, est ipsa hominis essentia seu natura, quatenus determinata concipitur ad agendum ea, quæ per solam hominis essentiam adæquate concipiuntur (per Defin. 2 p. III). Si itaque hæc Cupiditas excessum habere posset, posset ergo humana natura in se sola considerata, se ipsam excedere, sive plus posset quam potest, quod manifesta est contradictio ; ac proinde hæc Cupiditas excessum habere nequit. Q. E. D.\n\nPROPOSITIO LXII\n\nQuatenus Mens ex Rationis dictamine res concipit, æque afficitur, sive idea sit rei futuræ vel præteritæ, sive præsentis.\n\nDEMONSTRATIO\n\nQuicquid Mens ducente Ratione concipit, id omne sub eadem æternitatis seu necessitatis specie concipit (per Coroll. 2 Prop. 44 p. II), eademque certitudine afficitur (per Prop. 43 p. II et ejus Schol.). Quare, sive idea sit rei futuræ vel præteritæ, sive præsentis, Mens eadem necessitate rem concipit, eademque certitudine afficitur ; et, sive idea sit rei futuræ vel præteritæ, sive præsentis, erit nihilominus æque vera (per Prop. 41 p. II) ; hoc est (per Defin. 4 p. II), habebit nihilominus semper easdem ideæ adæquatæ proprietates ; atque adeo, quatenus Mens ex Rationis dictamine res concipit, eodem modo afficitur, sive idea sit rei futuræ vel præteritæ, sive præsentis. Q. E. D.\n\nSCHOLIUM\n\nSi nos de rerum duratione adæquatam cognitionem habere, earumque existendi tempora Ratione determinare possemus, eodem affectu res futuras ac præsentes contemplaremur, et bonum, quod Mens ut futurum conciperet, perinde ac præsens appeteret, et consequenter bonum præsens minus pro majore bono futuro necessario negligeret, et quod in præsenti bonum esset, sed causa futuri alicujus mali, minime appeteret, ut mox demonstrabimus. Sed nos de duratione rerum (per Prop. 31 p. II) non nisi admodum inadæquatam cognitionem habere possumus, et rerum existendi tempora (per Schol. Prop. 44 p. II) sola imaginatione determinamus, quæ non æque afficitur imagine rei præsentis ac futuræ ; unde fit, ut vera boni et mali cognitio, quam habemus, non nisi abstracta sive universalis sit, et judicium, quod de rerum ordine et causarum nexu facimus, ut determinare possimus, quid nobis in præsenti bonum aut malum sit, sit potius imaginarium quam reale. Atque adeo mirum non est, si Cupiditas, quæ ex boni et mali cognitione, quatenus hæc futurum prospicit, oritur, facilius rerum Cupiditate, quæ in præsentia suaves sunt, coerceri potest, de quo vide Propositionem 16 hujus Partis.\n\nPROPOSITIO LXIII\n\nQui Metu ducitur, et bonum, ut malum vitet, agit, is Ratione non ducitur.\n\nDEMONSTRATIO\n\nOmnes affectus, qui ad Mentem, quatenus agit, hoc est (per Prop. 3 p. III) qui ad Rationem referuntur, nulli alii sunt, quam affectus Lætitiæ et Cupiditatis (per Prop. 59 p. III) ; atque adeo (per 13 Affect. Defin.) qui Metu ducitur, et bonum timore mali agit, is Ratione non ducitur. Q. E. D.\n\nSCHOLIUM\n\nSuperstitiosi, qui vitia exprobrare magis quam virtutes docere norunt, et qui homines non Ratione ducere, sed Metu ita continere student, ut malum potius fugiant quam virtutes ament, nil aliud intendunt, quam ut reliqui æque ac ipsi fiant miseri ; et ideo non mirum, si plerumque molesti et odiosi sint hominibus.\n\nCOROLLARIUM\n\nCupiditate, quæ ex Ratione oritur, bonum directe sequimur, et malum indirecte fugimus.\n\nDEMONSTRATIO\n\nNam Cupiditas, quæ ex Ratione oritur, ex solo Lætitiæ affectu, quæ passio non est, oriri potest (per Prop. 59 p. III), hoc est ex Lætitia, quæ excessum habere nequit (per Prop. 61 hujus), non autem ex Tristitia ; ac proinde hæc Cupiditas (per Prop. 8 hujus) ex cognitione boni, non autem mali oritur ; atque adeo ex ductu Rationis bonum directe appetimus, et eatenus tantum malum fugimus. Q. E. D.\n\nSCHOLIUM\n\nExplicatur hoc Corollarium exemplo ægri et sani. Comedit æger id, quod aversatur, timore mortis ; sanus autem cibo gaudet, et vita sic melius fruitur, quam si mortem timeret, eamque directe vitare cuperet. Sic judex, qui non Odio aut Ira, etc., sed solo Amore salutis publicæ reum mortis damnat, sola Ratione ducitur.\n\nPROPOSITIO LXIV\n\nCognitio mali cognitio est inadæquata.\n\nDEMONSTRATIO\n\nCognitio mali (per Prop. 8 hujus) est ipsa Tristitia, quatenus ejusdem sumus conscii. Tristitia autem est transitio ad minorem perfectionem (per 3 Affect. Defin.), quæ propterea per ipsam hominis essentiam intelligi nequit (per Prop. 6 et 7 p. III) ; ac proinde (per Defin. 2 p. III) passio est, quæ (per Prop. 3 p. III) ab ideis inadæquatis pendet ; et consequenter (per Prop. 29 p. II) ejus cognitio, nempe mali cognitio, est inadæquata. Q. E. D.\n\nCOROLLARIUM\n\nHinc sequitur, quod si Mens humana non nisi adæquatas haberet ideas, nullam mali formaret notionem.\n\nPROPOSITIO LXV\n\nDe duobus bonis majus, et de duobus malis minus ex Rationis ductu sequemur.\n\nDEMONSTRATIO\n\nBonum, quod impedit, quo minus majore bono fruamur, est revera malum ; malum enim et bonum (ut in Præfat. hujus ostendimus) de rebus dicitur, quatenus easdem ad invicem comparamus ; et (per eandem rationem) malum minus revera bonum est ; quare (per Coroll. Prop. 63 hujus) ex Rationis ductu bonum tantum majus, et malum minus, appetemus seu sequemur. Q. E. D.\n\nCOROLLARIUM\n\nMalum minus pro majore bono ex Rationis ductu sequemur, et bonum minus, quod causa est majoris mali, negligemus. Nam malum, quod hic dicitur minus, revera bonum est, et bonum contra malum ; quare (per Coroll. Prop. 63 hujus) illud appetemus, et hoc negligemus. Q. E. D.\n\nPROPOSITIO LXVI\n\nBonum majus futurum præ minore præsenti, et malum præsens minus præ majore futuro, ex Rationis ductu appetemus.\n\nDEMONSTRATIO\n\nSi Mens rei futuræ adæquatam posset habere cognitionem, eodem affectu erga rem futuram ac erga præsentem afficeretur (per Prop. 62 hujus) ; quare quatenus ad ipsam Rationem attendimus, ut in hac Propositione nos facere supponimus, res eadem est, sive majus bonum vel malum futurum, sive præsens supponatur ; ac proinde (per Prop. 65 hujus) bonum futurum majus præ minore præsenti, etc. appetemus. Q. E. D.\n\nCOROLLARIUM\n\nMalum præsens minus, quod est causa majoris futuri boni, ex Rationis ductu appetemus ; et bonum præsens minus, quod causa est majoris futuri mali, negligemus. Hoc Coroll. se habet ad præced. Prop. ut Coroll. Prop. 65 ad ipsam Prop. 65.\n\nSCHOLIUM\n\nSi igitur hæc cum iis conferantur, quæ in hac Parte usque ad Propositionem 18 de affectuum viribus ostendimus, facile videbimus, quid homo, qui solo affectu seu opinione, homini qui Ratione ducitur, intersit. Ille enim, velit nolit, ea, quæ maxime ignorat, agit ; hic autem nemini, nisi sibi, morem gerit, et ea tantum agit, quæ in vita prima esse novit, quæque propterea maxime cupit ; et ideo illum servum, hunc autem liberum voco, de cujus ingenio et vivendi Ratione pauca adhuc notare libet.\n\nPROPOSITIO LXVII\n\nHomo liber de nulla re minus quam de morte cogitat ; et ejus sapientia non mortis, sed vitæ meditatio est.\n\nDEMONSTRATIO\n\nHomo liber, hoc est qui ex solo Rationis dictamine vivit, mortis Metu non ducitur (per Prop. 63 hujus), sed bonum directe cupit (per Coroll. ejusdem Prop.), hoc est (per Prop. 24 hujus) agere, vivere, suum esse conservare ex fundamento proprium utile quærendi ; atque adeo nihil minus quam de morte cogitat, sed ejus sapientia vitæ est meditatio. Q. E. D.\n\nPROPOSITIO LXVIII\n\nSi homines liberi nascerentur, nullum boni et mali formarent conceptum, quamdiu liberi essent.\n\nDEMONSTRATIO","paragraphs":[{"id":"RAW_QGOH_PQ_4:P0001","text":"Commiseratio in homine, qui ex ductu Rationis vivit, per se mala et inutilis est.","kind":"passage"},{"id":"RAW_QGOH_PQ_4:P0002","text":"DEMONSTRATIO","kind":"passage"},{"id":"RAW_QGOH_PQ_4:P0003","text":"Commiseratio enim (per 18 Affect. Defin.) Tristitia est ; ac proinde (per Prop. 41 hujus) per se mala ; bonum autem, quod ex ea sequitur, quod scilicet hominem, cujus nos miseret, a miseria liberare conamur (per Coroll. 3 Prop. 27 p. III), ex solo Rationis dictamine facere cupimus (per Prop. 37 hujus) ; nec nisi ex solo Rationis dictamine aliquid, quod certo scimus bonum esse, agere possumus (per Prop. 27 hujus) ; atque adeo commiseratio in homine, qui ex ductu Rationis vivit, per se mala est et inutilis. Q. E. D.","kind":"passage"},{"id":"RAW_QGOH_PQ_4:P0004","text":"COROLLARIUM","kind":"passage"},{"id":"RAW_QGOH_PQ_4:P0005","text":"Hinc sequitur, quod homo, qui ex dictamine Rationis vivit, conatur quantum potest, efficere, ne commiseratione tangatur.","kind":"passage"},{"id":"RAW_QGOH_PQ_4:P0006","text":"SCHOLIUM","kind":"passage"},{"id":"RAW_QGOH_PQ_4:P0007","text":"Qui recte novit, omnia ex naturæ divinæ necessitate sequi, et secundum æternas Naturæ leges et regulas fieri, is sane nihil reperiet quod Odio, Risu aut Contemptu dignum sit, nec cujusquam miserebitur sed quantum humana fert virtus, conabitur bene agere, ut ajunt, et lætari. Huc accedit, quod is, qui Commiserationis affectu facile tangitur, et alterius miseria vel lacrimis movetur, sæpe aliquid agit, cujus postea ipsum pœnitet ; tam quia ex affectu nihil agimus, quod certo scimus bonum esse, quam quia facile falsis lacrimis decipimur. Atque hic expresse loquor de homine, qui ex ductu Rationis vivit. Nam qui nec Ratione nec Commiseratione movetur, ut aliis auxilio sit, is recte inhumanus appellatur, nam (per Prop. 27 p. III) homini dissimilis esse videtur.","kind":"passage"},{"id":"RAW_QGOH_PQ_4:P0008","text":"PROPOSITIO LI","kind":"passage"},{"id":"RAW_QGOH_PQ_4:P0009","text":"Favor Rationi non repugnat, sed cum eadem convenire et ab eadem oriri potest.","kind":"passage"},{"id":"RAW_QGOH_PQ_4:P0010","text":"DEMONSTRATIO","kind":"passage"},{"id":"RAW_QGOH_PQ_4:P0011","text":"Est enim Favor Amor erga illum, qui alteri benefecit (per 19 Affect. Defin.) ; atque adeo ad Mentem referri potest, quatenus hæc agere dicitur (per Prop. 59 p. III) ; hoc est (per Prop. 3 p. III), quatenus intelligit ; ac proinde cum Ratione convenit, etc. Q. E. D.","kind":"passage"},{"id":"RAW_QGOH_PQ_4:P0012","text":"ALITER","kind":"passage"},{"id":"RAW_QGOH_PQ_4:P0013","text":"Qui ex ductu Rationis vivit, bonum, quod sibi appetit, alteri etiam cupit (per Prop. 37 hujus) ; quare ex eo, quod ipse aliquem videt alteri benefacere, ipsius benefaciendi conatus juvatur, hoc est (per Schol. Prop. 11 p. III) lætabitur, idque (ex hypothesi) concomitante idea illius, qui alteri benefecit ; ac proinde (per 19 Affect. Defin.) ei favet. Q. E. D.","kind":"passage"},{"id":"RAW_QGOH_PQ_4:P0014","text":"SCHOLIUM","kind":"passage"},{"id":"RAW_QGOH_PQ_4:P0015","text":"Indignatio, prout ipsa a nobis definitur (vide 20 Affect. Defin.), est necessario mala (per Prop. 45 hujus) ; sed notandum, quod, quando summa potestas desiderio, quo tenetur, tutandæ pacis civem punit, qui alteri injuriam fecit, eandem civi indignari non dico, quia non Odio percita ad perdendum civem, sed pietate mota eundem punit.","kind":"passage"},{"id":"RAW_QGOH_PQ_4:P0016","text":"PROPOSITIO LII","kind":"passage"},{"id":"RAW_QGOH_PQ_4:P0017","text":"Acquiescentia in se ipso ex Ratione oriri potest, et ea sola acquiescentia, quæ ex Ratione oritur, summa est, quæ potest dari.","kind":"passage"},{"id":"RAW_QGOH_PQ_4:P0018","text":"DEMONSTRATIO","kind":"passage"},{"id":"RAW_QGOH_PQ_4:P0019","text":"Acquiescentia in se ipso est Lætitia orta ex eo, quod homo se ipsum suamque agendi potentiam contemplatur (per 25 Affect. Defin.). At vera hominis agendi potentia seu virtus est ipsa Ratio (per Prop. 3 p. III), quam homo clare et distincte contemplatur (per Prop. 40 et 43 p. II) ; Ergo Acquiescentia in se ipso ex Ratione oritur. Deinde nihil homo dum se ipsum contemplatur, clare et distincte, sive adæquate percipit, nisi ea, quæ ex ipsius agendi potentia sequuntur (per Defin. 2 p. III), hoc est (per Prop. 3 p. III) quæ ex ipsius intelligendi potentia sequuntur ; adeoque ex sola hac contemplatione summa, quæ dari potest Acquiescentia oritur. Q. E. D.","kind":"passage"},{"id":"RAW_QGOH_PQ_4:P0020","text":"SCHOLIUM","kind":"passage"},{"id":"RAW_QGOH_PQ_4:P0021","text":"Est revera Acquiescentia in se ipso summum, quod sperare possumus. Nam (ut Prop. 25 hujus ostendimus) nemo suum esse alicujus finis causa conservare conatur ; et quia hæc Acquiescentia magis magisque fovetur et corroboratur laudibus (per Coroll. Prop. 53 p. III), et contra (per Coroll. Prop. 55 p. III) vituperio magis magisque turbatur, ideo gloria maxime ducimur, et vitam cum probro vix ferre possumus.","kind":"passage"},{"id":"RAW_QGOH_PQ_4:P0022","text":"PROPOSITIO LIII","kind":"passage"},{"id":"RAW_QGOH_PQ_4:P0023","text":"Humilitas virtus non est, sive ex Ratione non oritur.","kind":"passage"},{"id":"RAW_QGOH_PQ_4:P0024","text":"DEMONSTRATIO","kind":"passage"},{"id":"RAW_QGOH_PQ_4:P0025","text":"Humilitas est Tristitia, quæ ex eo oritur, quod homo suam impotentiam contemplatur (per 26 Affect. Defin.). Quatenus autem homo se ipsum vera Ratione cognoscit, eatenus suam essentiam intelligere supponitur, hoc est (per Prop. 7 p. III) suam potentiam. Quare si homo, dum se ipsum contemplatur, aliquam suam impotentiam percipit, id non ex eo est, quod se intelligit sed (ut Prop. 55 p. III ostendimus) ex eo, quod ipsius agendi potentia coercetur. Quod si supponamus, hominem suam impotentiam concipere ex eo, quod aliquid se potentius intelligit, cujus cognitione suam agendi potentiam determinat, tum nihil aliud concipimus, quam quod homo se ipsum distincte intelligit, sive (per Prop. 26 hujus) quod ipsius agendi potentia juvatur.","kind":"passage"},{"id":"RAW_QGOH_PQ_4:P0026","text":"Quare Humilitas, seu Tristitia quæ ex eo oritur, quod homo suam impotentiam contemplatur, non ex vera contemplatione seu Ratione oritur, nec virtus, sed passio est. Q. E. D.","kind":"passage"},{"id":"RAW_QGOH_PQ_4:P0027","text":"PROPOSITIO LIV","kind":"passage"},{"id":"RAW_QGOH_PQ_4:P0028","text":"Pœnitentia virtus non est, sive ex Ratione non oritur ; sed is, quem facti pœnitet, bis miser seu impotens est.","kind":"passage"},{"id":"RAW_QGOH_PQ_4:P0029","text":"DEMONSTRATIO","kind":"passage"},{"id":"RAW_QGOH_PQ_4:P0030","text":"Hujus prima pars demonstratur ut præced. Propositio. Secunda autem ex sola hujus affectus Definitione (vide 27 Affect. Defin.) patet. Nam primo prava Cupiditate, dein Tristitia vinci se patitur.","kind":"passage"},{"id":"RAW_QGOH_PQ_4:P0031","text":"SCHOLIUM","kind":"passage"},{"id":"RAW_QGOH_PQ_4:P0032","text":"Quia homines raro ex dictamine Rationis vivunt, ideo hi duo affectus, nempe Humilitas et Pœnitentia, et præter hos Spes et Metus, plus utilitatis quam damni afferunt ; atque adeo, quandoquidem peccandum est, in istam partem potius peccandum. Nam, si homines animo impotentes æque omnes superbirent, nullius rei ipsos puderet, nec ipsi quicquam metuerent, qui vinculis conjungi constringique possent ? Terret vulgus, nisi metuat ; quare non mirum, quod Prophetæ, qui non paucorum, sed communi utilitati consuluerunt, tantopere Humilitatem, Pœnitentiam et Reverentiam commendaverint. Et revera, qui hisce affectibus sunt obnoxii, multo facilius quam alii duci possunt ut tandem ex ductu Rationis vivant, hoc est, ut liberi sint, et beatorum vita fruantur.","kind":"passage"},{"id":"RAW_QGOH_PQ_4:P0033","text":"PROPOSITIO LV","kind":"passage"},{"id":"RAW_QGOH_PQ_4:P0034","text":"Maxima Superbia vel Abjectio est maxima sui ignorantia.","kind":"passage"},{"id":"RAW_QGOH_PQ_4:P0035","text":"DEMONSTRATIO","kind":"passage"},{"id":"RAW_QGOH_PQ_4:P0036","text":"Patet ex Defin. 28 et 29 Affect.","kind":"passage"},{"id":"RAW_QGOH_PQ_4:P0037","text":"PROPOSITIO LVI","kind":"passage"},{"id":"RAW_QGOH_PQ_4:P0038","text":"Maxima Superbia vel Abjectio maximam animi impotentiam indicat.","kind":"passage"},{"id":"RAW_QGOH_PQ_4:P0039","text":"DEMONSTRATIO","kind":"passage"},{"id":"RAW_QGOH_PQ_4:P0040","text":"Primum virtutis fundamentum est suum esse conservare (per Coroll. Prop. 22 hujus), idque ex ductu Rationis (per Prop. 24 hujus). Qui igitur se ipsum ignorat, omnium virtutum fundamentum, et consequenter omnes virtutes ignorat. Deinde ex virtute agere nihil aliud est, quam ex ductu Rationis agere (per Prop. 24 hujus) ; et qui ex ductu Rationis agit, scire necessario debet, se ex ductu Rationis agere (per Prop. 43 p. II). Qui itaque se ipsum, et consequenter (ut jam jam ostendimus) omnes virtutes, maxime ignorat, is minime ex virtute agit, hoc est (ut ex Defin. 8 hujus patet), maxime animo est impotens atque adeo (per Prop. præced.) maxima superbia vel Abjectio maximam animi impotentiam indicat. Q. E. D.","kind":"passage"},{"id":"RAW_QGOH_PQ_4:P0041","text":"COROLLARIUM","kind":"passage"},{"id":"RAW_QGOH_PQ_4:P0042","text":"Hinc clare sequitur, superbos et abjectos maxime affectibus esse obnoxios.","kind":"passage"},{"id":"RAW_QGOH_PQ_4:P0043","text":"SCHOLIUM","kind":"passage"},{"id":"RAW_QGOH_PQ_4:P0044","text":"Abjectio tamen facilius corrigi potest quam superbia ; quandoquidem hæc Lætitiæ, illa autem Tristitiæ est affectus, atque adeo (per Prop. 18 hujus) hæc illa fortior est.","kind":"passage"},{"id":"RAW_QGOH_PQ_4:P0045","text":"PROPOSITIO LVII","kind":"passage"},{"id":"RAW_QGOH_PQ_4:P0046","text":"Superbus parasitorum seu adulatorum præsentiam amat, generosorum autem odit.","kind":"passage"},{"id":"RAW_QGOH_PQ_4:P0047","text":"DEMONSTRATIO","kind":"passage"},{"id":"RAW_QGOH_PQ_4:P0048","text":"Superbia est Lætitia orta ex eo, quod homo de se plus justo sentit (per Defin. 28 et 6 Affect.) ; quam opinionem homo superbus, quantum potest, fovere conabitur (vide Schol. Prop. 13 p. III) ; adeoque parasitorum vel adulatorum (horum Definitiones omisi, quia nimis noti sunt) præsentiam amabit, et generosorum, qui de ipso ut par est, sentiunt, fugiet. Q. E. D.","kind":"passage"},{"id":"RAW_QGOH_PQ_4:P0049","text":"SCHOLIUM","kind":"passage"},{"id":"RAW_QGOH_PQ_4:P0050","text":"Nimis longum foret hic omnia Superbiæ mala enumerare, quandoquidem omnibus affectibus obnoxii sunt superbi ; sed nullis minus, quam affectibus Amoris et Misericordiæ. Sed hic minime tacendum est, quod ille etiam superbus vocetur, qui de reliquis minus justo sentit, atque adeo hoc sensu Superbia definienda est, quod sit Lætitia orta ex falsa opinione, quod homo se supra reliquos esse putat. Et Abjectio huic Superbiæ contraria definienda esset Tristitia orta ex falsa opinione, quod homo se infra reliquos esse credit. At hoc posito facile concipimus, superbum necessario esse invidum (vide Schol. Prop. 55 p. III), et eos maxime odio habere, qui maxime ob virtutes laudantur ; nec facile eorum Odium Amore aut beneficio vinci (vide Schol. Prop. 41 p. III) ; et eorum tantummodo præsentia delectari, qui animo ejus impotenti morem gerunt, et ex stulto insanum faciunt.","kind":"passage"},{"id":"RAW_QGOH_PQ_4:P0051","text":"Abjectio quamvis Superbiæ sit contraria, est tamen abjectus superbo proximus. Nam, quandoquidem ejus Tristitia ex eo oritur, quod suam impotentiam ex aliorum potentia seu virtute judicat, levabitur ergo ejus Tristitia, hoc est lætabitur, si ejus imaginatio in alienis vitiis contemplandis occupetur, unde illud proverbium natum : solamen miseris socios habuisse malorum ; et contra eo magis contristabitur, quo se magis infra reliquos esse crediderit ; unde fit, ut nulli magis ad Invidiam sint proni, quam Abjecti ; et ut isti maxime hominum facta observare conentur ad carpendum magis, quam ad eadem corrigendum, et ut tandem solam Abjectionem laudent, eaque glorientur ; sed ita, ut tamen abjecti videantur.","kind":"passage"},{"id":"RAW_QGOH_PQ_4:P0052","text":"Atque hæc ex hoc affectu tam necessario sequuntur, quam ex natura trianguli, quod ejus tres anguli æquales sint duobus rectis ; et jam dixi, me hos et similes affectus malos vocare, quatenus ad solam humanam utilitatem attendo. Sed Naturæ leges communem Naturæ ordinem, cujus homo pars est, respiciunt ; quod hic in transitu monere volui, ne quis putaret me hic hominum vitia et absurda facta narrare, non autem rerum naturam et proprietates demonstrare voluisse. Nam, ut in Præfatione Partis tertiæ dixi, humanos affectus eorumque proprietates perinde considero, ac reliqua naturalia. Et sane humani affectus, si non humanam, Naturæ saltem potentiam et artificium non minus indicant, quam multa alia, quæ admiramur, quorumque contemplatione delectamur. Sed pergo de affectibus ea notare, quæ hominibus utilitatem adferunt, vel quæ iisdem damnum inferunt.","kind":"passage"},{"id":"RAW_QGOH_PQ_4:P0053","text":"PROPOSITIO LVIII","kind":"passage"},{"id":"RAW_QGOH_PQ_4:P0054","text":"Gloria Rationi non repugnat, sed ab ea oriri potest.","kind":"passage"},{"id":"RAW_QGOH_PQ_4:P0055","text":"DEMONSTRATIO","kind":"passage"},{"id":"RAW_QGOH_PQ_4:P0056","text":"Patet ex 30 Affect. Defin. et ex definitione Honesti, quam vide in Schol. 1 Prop. 37 hujus.","kind":"passage"},{"id":"RAW_QGOH_PQ_4:P0057","text":"SCHOLIUM","kind":"passage"},{"id":"RAW_QGOH_PQ_4:P0058","text":"Vana, quæ dicitur, Gloria est acquiescentia in se ipso, quæ sola vulgi opinione fovetur ; eaque cessante, cessat ipsa acquiescentia, hoc est (per Schol. Prop. 52 hujus) summum bonum, quod unusquisque amat ; unde fit, ut qui vulgi opinione gloriatur, quotidiana cura anxius nitatur, faciat, experiatur, ut famam conservet. Est namque vulgus varius et inconstans, atque adeo, nisi conservetur fama, cito abolescit ; imo quia omnes vulgi captare applausus cupiunt, facile unusquisque alterius famam reprimit ; ex quo, quandoquidem de summo, quod æstimatur, bono certatur, ingens libido oritur se invicem quocunque modo opprimendi, et qui tandem victor evadit, gloriatur magis, quod alteri obfuit, quam quod sibi profuit. Est igitur hæc gloria seu acquiescentia revera vana, quia nulla est. Quæ de Pudore notanda sunt, colliguntur facile ex iis, quæ de Misericordia et Pœnitentia diximus.","kind":"passage"},{"id":"RAW_QGOH_PQ_4:P0059","text":"Hoc tantum addo, quod ut Commiseratio, sic etiam Pudor, quamvis non sit virtus, bonus tamen est, quatenus indicat, homini, qui Pudore suffunditur, cupiditatem inesse honeste vivendi ; sicut dolor, qui eatenus bonus dicitur, quatenus indicat, partem læsam nondum esse putrefactam ; quare, quamvis homo, quem facti alicujus pudet, revera sit tristis, est tamen perfectior impudenti, qui nullam habet honeste vivendi cupiditatem.","kind":"passage"},{"id":"RAW_QGOH_PQ_4:P0060","text":"Atque hæc sunt, quæ de affectibus Lætitiæ et Tristitiæ notare susceperam. Ad Cupiditates quod attinet, hæ sane bonæ aut malæ sunt, quatenus ex bonis aut malis affectibus oriuntur. Sed omnes revera, quatenus ex affectibus, qui passiones sunt, in nobis ingenerantur, cœcæ sunt (ut facile colligitur ex iis, quæ in Schol. Prop. 44 hujus diximus), nec ullius usus essent, si homines facile duci possent, ut ex solo Rationis dictamine viverent, ut jam paucis ostendam.","kind":"passage"},{"id":"RAW_QGOH_PQ_4:P0061","text":"PROPOSITIO LIX","kind":"passage"},{"id":"RAW_QGOH_PQ_4:P0062","text":"Ad omnes actiones, ad quas ex affectu, qui passio est, determinamur, possumus absque eo a Ratione determinari.","kind":"passage"},{"id":"RAW_QGOH_PQ_4:P0063","text":"DEMONSTRATIO","kind":"passage"},{"id":"RAW_QGOH_PQ_4:P0064","text":"Ex Ratione agere nihil aliud est (per Prop. 3 et Defin. 2 p. III), quam ea agere, quæ ex necessitate nostræ naturæ, in se sola consideratæ, sequuntur. At Tristitia eatenus mala, est quatenus hanc agendi potentiam minuit vel coercet (per Prop. 41 hujus) ; ergo ex hoc affectu ad nullam actionem possumus determinari, quam non possemus agere, si Ratione duceremur. Præterea Lætitia eatenus mala est, quatenus impedit, quo minus homo ad agendum sit aptus (per Prop. 43 et 44 hujus) ; atque adeo eatenus etiam ad nullam actionem determinari possumus, quam non possemus agere, si Ratione duceremur. Denique quatenus Lætitia bona est, eatenus cum Ratione convenit (consistit enim in eo, quod hominis agendi potentia augetur vel juvatur) ; nec passio est, nisi quatenus hominis agendi potentia non eo usque augetur, ut se suasque actiones adæquate concipiat (per Prop. 3 p. III cum ejus Schol.)","kind":"passage"},{"id":"RAW_QGOH_PQ_4:P0065","text":"; Quare si homo Lætitia affectus ad tantam perfectionem duceretur, ut se suasque actiones adæquate conciperet, ad easdem actiones, ad quas jam ex affectibus, qui passiones sunt, determinatur, aptus, imo aptior esset. At omnes affectus ad Lætitiam, Tristitiam, vel Cupiditatem referuntur (vide explicationem quartæ Aff. Defin.), et Cupiditas (per 1 Affect. Defin.) nihil aliud est, quam ipse agendi conatus ; ergo ad omnes actiones, ad quas ex affectu, qui passio est, determinamur, possumus absque eo sola Ratione duci. Q. E. D.","kind":"passage"},{"id":"RAW_QGOH_PQ_4:P0066","text":"ALITER","kind":"passage"},{"id":"RAW_QGOH_PQ_4:P0067","text":"Actio quæcunque eatenus dicitur mala, quatenus ex eo oritur, quod Odio aut aliquo malo affectu affecti sumus (vide Coroll. 1 Prop. 45 hujus). At nulla actio, in se sola considerata, bona aut mala est (ut in Præfatione hujus ostendimus), sed una eademque actio jam bona jam mala est ; ergo ad eandem actionem, quæ jam mala est, sive quæ ex aliquo malo affectu oritur, Ratione duci possumus (per Prop. 19 hujus). Q. E. D.","kind":"passage"},{"id":"RAW_QGOH_PQ_4:P0068","text":"SCHOLIUM","kind":"passage"},{"id":"RAW_QGOH_PQ_4:P0069","text":"Explicantur hæc clarius exemplo. Nempe verberandi actio, quatenus physice consideratur, et ad hoc tantum attendimus, quod homo brachium tollit, manum claudit, totumque brachium vi deorsum movet, virtus est, quæ ex Corporis humani fabrica concipitur. Si itaque homo, Ira vel Odio commotus, determinatur ad claudendam manum vel brachium movendum, id, ut in Parte secunda ostendimus, fit, quia una eademque actio potest jungi quibuscunque rerum imaginibus ; atque adeo tam ex iis imaginibus rerum, quas confuse, quam quas clare et distincte concipimus, ad unam eandemque actionem determinari possumus. Apparet itaque, quod omnis Cupiditas, quæ ex affectu, qui passio est, oritur, nullius esset usus, si homines Ratione duci possent. Videamus jam, cur Cupiditas, quæ ex affectu, qui passio est, oritur, cœca a nobis appellatur.","kind":"passage"},{"id":"RAW_QGOH_PQ_4:P0070","text":"PROPOSITIO LX","kind":"passage"},{"id":"RAW_QGOH_PQ_4:P0071","text":"Cupiditas, quæ oritur ex Lætitia vel Tristitia, quæ ad unam vel ad aliquot, non autem ad omnes Corporis partes refertur, rationem utilitatis totius hominis non habet.","kind":"passage"},{"id":"RAW_QGOH_PQ_4:P0072","text":"DEMONSTRATIO","kind":"passage"},{"id":"RAW_QGOH_PQ_4:P0073","text":"Ponatur ex. gr. Corporis pars A vi alicujus causæ externæ ita corroborari, ut reliquis prævaleat (per Prop. 6 hujus). Hæc pars vires suas amittere non conabitur, ut reliquæ Corporis partes suo fungantur officio ; deberet enim vim seu potentiam habere vires suas amittendi, quod (per Prop. 6 p. III) est absurdum. Conabitur itaque illa pars, et consequenter (per Prop. 7 et 12 p. III) Mens etiam, illum statum conservare ; adeoque Cupiditas, quæ ex tali affectu Lætitiæ oritur, rationem totius non habet. Quod si contra supponatur pars A coerceri, ut reliquæ prævaleant, eodem modo demonstratur, quod nec Cupiditas, quæ ex Tristitia oritur, rationem totius habeat. Q. E. D.","kind":"passage"},{"id":"RAW_QGOH_PQ_4:P0074","text":"SCHOLIUM","kind":"passage"},{"id":"RAW_QGOH_PQ_4:P0075","text":"Cum itaque Lætitia plerumque (per Schol. Prop. 44 hujus) ad unam Corporis partem referatur, cupimus ergo plerumque nostrum esse conservare, nulla habita Ratione integræ nostræ valetudinis ; ad quod accedit, quod Cupiditates, quibus maxime tenemur (per Coroll. Prop. 9 hujus) temporis tantum præsentis, non autem futuri habent rationem.","kind":"passage"},{"id":"RAW_QGOH_PQ_4:P0076","text":"PROPOSITIO LXI","kind":"passage"},{"id":"RAW_QGOH_PQ_4:P0077","text":"Cupiditas, quæ ex Ratione oritur, excessum habere nequit.","kind":"passage"},{"id":"RAW_QGOH_PQ_4:P0078","text":"DEMONSTRATIO","kind":"passage"},{"id":"RAW_QGOH_PQ_4:P0079","text":"Cupiditas (per 1 Affect. Defin.), absolute considerata, est ipsa hominis essentia, quatenus quocunque modo determinata concipitur ad aliquid agendum ; adeoque Cupiditas, quæ ex Ratione oritur, hoc est (per Prop. 3 p. III) quæ in nobis ingeneratur quatenus agimus, est ipsa hominis essentia seu natura, quatenus determinata concipitur ad agendum ea, quæ per solam hominis essentiam adæquate concipiuntur (per Defin. 2 p. III). Si itaque hæc Cupiditas excessum habere posset, posset ergo humana natura in se sola considerata, se ipsam excedere, sive plus posset quam potest, quod manifesta est contradictio ; ac proinde hæc Cupiditas excessum habere nequit. Q. E. D.","kind":"passage"},{"id":"RAW_QGOH_PQ_4:P0080","text":"PROPOSITIO LXII","kind":"passage"},{"id":"RAW_QGOH_PQ_4:P0081","text":"Quatenus Mens ex Rationis dictamine res concipit, æque afficitur, sive idea sit rei futuræ vel præteritæ, sive præsentis.","kind":"passage"},{"id":"RAW_QGOH_PQ_4:P0082","text":"DEMONSTRATIO","kind":"passage"},{"id":"RAW_QGOH_PQ_4:P0083","text":"Quicquid Mens ducente Ratione concipit, id omne sub eadem æternitatis seu necessitatis specie concipit (per Coroll. 2 Prop. 44 p. II), eademque certitudine afficitur (per Prop. 43 p. II et ejus Schol.). Quare, sive idea sit rei futuræ vel præteritæ, sive præsentis, Mens eadem necessitate rem concipit, eademque certitudine afficitur ; et, sive idea sit rei futuræ vel præteritæ, sive præsentis, erit nihilominus æque vera (per Prop. 41 p. II) ; hoc est (per Defin. 4 p. II), habebit nihilominus semper easdem ideæ adæquatæ proprietates ; atque adeo, quatenus Mens ex Rationis dictamine res concipit, eodem modo afficitur, sive idea sit rei futuræ vel præteritæ, sive præsentis. Q. E. D.","kind":"passage"},{"id":"RAW_QGOH_PQ_4:P0084","text":"SCHOLIUM","kind":"passage"},{"id":"RAW_QGOH_PQ_4:P0085","text":"Si nos de rerum duratione adæquatam cognitionem habere, earumque existendi tempora Ratione determinare possemus, eodem affectu res futuras ac præsentes contemplaremur, et bonum, quod Mens ut futurum conciperet, perinde ac præsens appeteret, et consequenter bonum præsens minus pro majore bono futuro necessario negligeret, et quod in præsenti bonum esset, sed causa futuri alicujus mali, minime appeteret, ut mox demonstrabimus. Sed nos de duratione rerum (per Prop. 31 p. II) non nisi admodum inadæquatam cognitionem habere possumus, et rerum existendi tempora (per Schol. Prop. 44 p.","kind":"passage"},{"id":"RAW_QGOH_PQ_4:P0086","text":"II) sola imaginatione determinamus, quæ non æque afficitur imagine rei præsentis ac futuræ ; unde fit, ut vera boni et mali cognitio, quam habemus, non nisi abstracta sive universalis sit, et judicium, quod de rerum ordine et causarum nexu facimus, ut determinare possimus, quid nobis in præsenti bonum aut malum sit, sit potius imaginarium quam reale. Atque adeo mirum non est, si Cupiditas, quæ ex boni et mali cognitione, quatenus hæc futurum prospicit, oritur, facilius rerum Cupiditate, quæ in præsentia suaves sunt, coerceri potest, de quo vide Propositionem 16 hujus Partis.","kind":"passage"},{"id":"RAW_QGOH_PQ_4:P0087","text":"PROPOSITIO LXIII","kind":"passage"},{"id":"RAW_QGOH_PQ_4:P0088","text":"Qui Metu ducitur, et bonum, ut malum vitet, agit, is Ratione non ducitur.","kind":"passage"},{"id":"RAW_QGOH_PQ_4:P0089","text":"DEMONSTRATIO","kind":"passage"},{"id":"RAW_QGOH_PQ_4:P0090","text":"Omnes affectus, qui ad Mentem, quatenus agit, hoc est (per Prop. 3 p. III) qui ad Rationem referuntur, nulli alii sunt, quam affectus Lætitiæ et Cupiditatis (per Prop. 59 p. III) ; atque adeo (per 13 Affect. Defin.) qui Metu ducitur, et bonum timore mali agit, is Ratione non ducitur. Q. E. D.","kind":"passage"},{"id":"RAW_QGOH_PQ_4:P0091","text":"SCHOLIUM","kind":"passage"},{"id":"RAW_QGOH_PQ_4:P0092","text":"Superstitiosi, qui vitia exprobrare magis quam virtutes docere norunt, et qui homines non Ratione ducere, sed Metu ita continere student, ut malum potius fugiant quam virtutes ament, nil aliud intendunt, quam ut reliqui æque ac ipsi fiant miseri ; et ideo non mirum, si plerumque molesti et odiosi sint hominibus.","kind":"passage"},{"id":"RAW_QGOH_PQ_4:P0093","text":"COROLLARIUM","kind":"passage"},{"id":"RAW_QGOH_PQ_4:P0094","text":"Cupiditate, quæ ex Ratione oritur, bonum directe sequimur, et malum indirecte fugimus.","kind":"passage"},{"id":"RAW_QGOH_PQ_4:P0095","text":"DEMONSTRATIO","kind":"passage"},{"id":"RAW_QGOH_PQ_4:P0096","text":"Nam Cupiditas, quæ ex Ratione oritur, ex solo Lætitiæ affectu, quæ passio non est, oriri potest (per Prop. 59 p. III), hoc est ex Lætitia, quæ excessum habere nequit (per Prop. 61 hujus), non autem ex Tristitia ; ac proinde hæc Cupiditas (per Prop. 8 hujus) ex cognitione boni, non autem mali oritur ; atque adeo ex ductu Rationis bonum directe appetimus, et eatenus tantum malum fugimus. Q. E. D.","kind":"passage"},{"id":"RAW_QGOH_PQ_4:P0097","text":"SCHOLIUM","kind":"passage"},{"id":"RAW_QGOH_PQ_4:P0098","text":"Explicatur hoc Corollarium exemplo ægri et sani. Comedit æger id, quod aversatur, timore mortis ; sanus autem cibo gaudet, et vita sic melius fruitur, quam si mortem timeret, eamque directe vitare cuperet. Sic judex, qui non Odio aut Ira, etc., sed solo Amore salutis publicæ reum mortis damnat, sola Ratione ducitur.","kind":"passage"},{"id":"RAW_QGOH_PQ_4:P0099","text":"PROPOSITIO LXIV","kind":"passage"},{"id":"RAW_QGOH_PQ_4:P0100","text":"Cognitio mali cognitio est inadæquata.","kind":"passage"},{"id":"RAW_QGOH_PQ_4:P0101","text":"DEMONSTRATIO","kind":"passage"},{"id":"RAW_QGOH_PQ_4:P0102","text":"Cognitio mali (per Prop. 8 hujus) est ipsa Tristitia, quatenus ejusdem sumus conscii. Tristitia autem est transitio ad minorem perfectionem (per 3 Affect. Defin.), quæ propterea per ipsam hominis essentiam intelligi nequit (per Prop. 6 et 7 p. III) ; ac proinde (per Defin. 2 p. III) passio est, quæ (per Prop. 3 p. III) ab ideis inadæquatis pendet ; et consequenter (per Prop. 29 p. II) ejus cognitio, nempe mali cognitio, est inadæquata. Q. E. D.","kind":"passage"},{"id":"RAW_QGOH_PQ_4:P0103","text":"COROLLARIUM","kind":"passage"},{"id":"RAW_QGOH_PQ_4:P0104","text":"Hinc sequitur, quod si Mens humana non nisi adæquatas haberet ideas, nullam mali formaret notionem.","kind":"passage"},{"id":"RAW_QGOH_PQ_4:P0105","text":"PROPOSITIO LXV","kind":"passage"},{"id":"RAW_QGOH_PQ_4:P0106","text":"De duobus bonis majus, et de duobus malis minus ex Rationis ductu sequemur.","kind":"passage"},{"id":"RAW_QGOH_PQ_4:P0107","text":"DEMONSTRATIO","kind":"passage"},{"id":"RAW_QGOH_PQ_4:P0108","text":"Bonum, quod impedit, quo minus majore bono fruamur, est revera malum ; malum enim et bonum (ut in Præfat. hujus ostendimus) de rebus dicitur, quatenus easdem ad invicem comparamus ; et (per eandem rationem) malum minus revera bonum est ; quare (per Coroll. Prop. 63 hujus) ex Rationis ductu bonum tantum majus, et malum minus, appetemus seu sequemur. Q. E. D.","kind":"passage"},{"id":"RAW_QGOH_PQ_4:P0109","text":"COROLLARIUM","kind":"passage"},{"id":"RAW_QGOH_PQ_4:P0110","text":"Malum minus pro majore bono ex Rationis ductu sequemur, et bonum minus, quod causa est majoris mali, negligemus. Nam malum, quod hic dicitur minus, revera bonum est, et bonum contra malum ; quare (per Coroll. Prop. 63 hujus) illud appetemus, et hoc negligemus. Q. E. D.","kind":"passage"},{"id":"RAW_QGOH_PQ_4:P0111","text":"PROPOSITIO LXVI","kind":"passage"},{"id":"RAW_QGOH_PQ_4:P0112","text":"Bonum majus futurum præ minore præsenti, et malum præsens minus præ majore futuro, ex Rationis ductu appetemus.","kind":"passage"},{"id":"RAW_QGOH_PQ_4:P0113","text":"DEMONSTRATIO","kind":"passage"},{"id":"RAW_QGOH_PQ_4:P0114","text":"Si Mens rei futuræ adæquatam posset habere cognitionem, eodem affectu erga rem futuram ac erga præsentem afficeretur (per Prop. 62 hujus) ; quare quatenus ad ipsam Rationem attendimus, ut in hac Propositione nos facere supponimus, res eadem est, sive majus bonum vel malum futurum, sive præsens supponatur ; ac proinde (per Prop. 65 hujus) bonum futurum majus præ minore præsenti, etc. appetemus. Q. E. D.","kind":"passage"},{"id":"RAW_QGOH_PQ_4:P0115","text":"COROLLARIUM","kind":"passage"},{"id":"RAW_QGOH_PQ_4:P0116","text":"Malum præsens minus, quod est causa majoris futuri boni, ex Rationis ductu appetemus ; et bonum præsens minus, quod causa est majoris futuri mali, negligemus. Hoc Coroll. se habet ad præced. Prop. ut Coroll. Prop. 65 ad ipsam Prop. 65.","kind":"passage"},{"id":"RAW_QGOH_PQ_4:P0117","text":"SCHOLIUM","kind":"passage"},{"id":"RAW_QGOH_PQ_4:P0118","text":"Si igitur hæc cum iis conferantur, quæ in hac Parte usque ad Propositionem 18 de affectuum viribus ostendimus, facile videbimus, quid homo, qui solo affectu seu opinione, homini qui Ratione ducitur, intersit. Ille enim, velit nolit, ea, quæ maxime ignorat, agit ; hic autem nemini, nisi sibi, morem gerit, et ea tantum agit, quæ in vita prima esse novit, quæque propterea maxime cupit ; et ideo illum servum, hunc autem liberum voco, de cujus ingenio et vivendi Ratione pauca adhuc notare libet.","kind":"passage"},{"id":"RAW_QGOH_PQ_4:P0119","text":"PROPOSITIO LXVII","kind":"passage"},{"id":"RAW_QGOH_PQ_4:P0120","text":"Homo liber de nulla re minus quam de morte cogitat ; et ejus sapientia non mortis, sed vitæ meditatio est.","kind":"passage"},{"id":"RAW_QGOH_PQ_4:P0121","text":"DEMONSTRATIO","kind":"passage"},{"id":"RAW_QGOH_PQ_4:P0122","text":"Homo liber, hoc est qui ex solo Rationis dictamine vivit, mortis Metu non ducitur (per Prop. 63 hujus), sed bonum directe cupit (per Coroll. ejusdem Prop.), hoc est (per Prop. 24 hujus) agere, vivere, suum esse conservare ex fundamento proprium utile quærendi ; atque adeo nihil minus quam de morte cogitat, sed ejus sapientia vitæ est meditatio. Q. E. D.","kind":"passage"},{"id":"RAW_QGOH_PQ_4:P0123","text":"PROPOSITIO LXVIII","kind":"passage"},{"id":"RAW_QGOH_PQ_4:P0124","text":"Si homines liberi nascerentur, nullum boni et mali formarent conceptum, quamdiu liberi essent.","kind":"passage"},{"id":"RAW_QGOH_PQ_4:P0125","text":"DEMONSTRATIO","kind":"passage"}],"sha256":"808f888363c73f0011c73ed3dafed925eec13f5ed7f01de2e3b7e21d7283c137","language_code":"la","language_name":"Latina","translations":[{"id":"SOURCE_TR_20261015T014049170864Z_20f76463","language_code":"en","language_name":"English","title":"Part Four - On Human Bondage, or the Powers of the Affects · passage 4","source_sha256":"808f888363c73f0011c73ed3dafed925eec13f5ed7f01de2e3b7e21d7283c137","sha256":"41a99f3f07fcfee78eca75baf58b71ec773e1b210bed6d3a9f23317bd497db1b","rendition_type":"machine","review_status":"machine","model":"gpt-5.6-terra","created":"2026-10-15T01:40:49.171031+00:00","paragraphs":[{"id":"RAW_QGOH_PQ_4:P0001","text":"Pity in a man who lives according to the guidance of Reason is in itself bad and useless.","kind":"passage","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_QGOH_PQ_4:P0001"]},{"id":"RAW_QGOH_PQ_4:P0002","text":"DEMONSTRATION","kind":"passage","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_QGOH_PQ_4:P0002"]},{"id":"RAW_QGOH_PQ_4:P0003","text":"For Pity (by Definition 18 of the Affects) is Sadness; and consequently (by Proposition 41 of this Part) is in itself bad. But the good which follows from it—namely, that we endeavour to free from misery the man whom we pity (by Corollary 3 to Proposition 27, Part III)—we desire to do from the dictate of Reason alone (by Proposition 37 of this Part); nor can we act, except from the dictate of Reason alone, in any matter which we certainly know to be good (by Proposition 27 of this Part); and therefore Pity in a man who lives according to the guidance of Reason is in itself bad and useless. Q. E. D.","kind":"passage","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_QGOH_PQ_4:P0003"]},{"id":"RAW_QGOH_PQ_4:P0004","text":"COROLLARY","kind":"passage","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_QGOH_PQ_4:P0004"]},{"id":"RAW_QGOH_PQ_4:P0005","text":"From this it follows that a man who lives according to the dictate of Reason endeavours, as far as he can, to avoid being affected by Pity.","kind":"passage","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_QGOH_PQ_4:P0005"]},{"id":"RAW_QGOH_PQ_4:P0006","text":"SCHOLIUM","kind":"passage","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_QGOH_PQ_4:P0006"]},{"id":"RAW_QGOH_PQ_4:P0007","text":"He who rightly knows that all things follow from the necessity of the divine nature, and come to pass according to the eternal laws and rules of Nature, will surely find nothing worthy of Hatred, Laughter, or Contempt, nor will he pity anyone; but, so far as human power permits, he will endeavour, as they say, to act well and to rejoice. To this must be added that he who is easily affected by the affect of Pity, and moved by another's misery or tears, often does something of which he later repents; both because from an affect we do nothing that we certainly know to be good, and because we are easily deceived by false tears. And here I speak expressly of a man who lives according to the guidance of Reason. For he who is moved neither by Reason nor by Pity to be of assistance to others is rightly called inhuman, since (by Proposition 27, Part III) he appears unlike a man.","kind":"passage","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_QGOH_PQ_4:P0007"]},{"id":"RAW_QGOH_PQ_4:P0008","text":"PROPOSITION LI","kind":"passage","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_QGOH_PQ_4:P0008"]},{"id":"RAW_QGOH_PQ_4:P0009","text":"Favor does not conflict with Reason, but can agree with it and arise from it.","kind":"passage","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_QGOH_PQ_4:P0009"]},{"id":"RAW_QGOH_PQ_4:P0010","text":"DEMONSTRATION","kind":"passage","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_QGOH_PQ_4:P0010"]},{"id":"RAW_QGOH_PQ_4:P0011","text":"For Favor is Love toward him who has done good to another (by Definition 19 of the Affects); and therefore it can be referred to the Mind insofar as the latter is said to act (by Proposition 59, Part III); that is (by Proposition 3, Part III), insofar as it understands; and consequently it agrees with Reason, etc. Q. E. D.","kind":"passage","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_QGOH_PQ_4:P0011"]},{"id":"RAW_QGOH_PQ_4:P0012","text":"OTHERWISE","kind":"passage","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_QGOH_PQ_4:P0012"]},{"id":"RAW_QGOH_PQ_4:P0013","text":"He who lives according to the guidance of Reason desires for another also the good which he seeks for himself (by Proposition 37 of this Part); wherefore, from the fact that he sees someone doing good to another, his own endeavour to do good is aided, that is (by the Scholium to Proposition 11, Part III), he will rejoice, and this (by the hypothesis) with the accompanying idea of him who has done good to another; and consequently (by Definition 19 of the Affects) he favors him. Q. E. D.","kind":"passage","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_QGOH_PQ_4:P0013"]},{"id":"RAW_QGOH_PQ_4:P0014","text":"SCHOLIUM","kind":"passage","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_QGOH_PQ_4:P0014"]},{"id":"RAW_QGOH_PQ_4:P0015","text":"Indignation, insofar as it is defined by us (see Definition 20 of the Affects), is necessarily bad (by Proposition 45 of this Part); but it should be noted that, when the supreme power, from the desire by which it is bound to safeguard peace, punishes a citizen who has wronged another, I do not say that it is indignant with the citizen, since it punishes him not stirred by Hatred to destroy the citizen, but moved by piety.","kind":"passage","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_QGOH_PQ_4:P0015"]},{"id":"RAW_QGOH_PQ_4:P0016","text":"PROPOSITION LII","kind":"passage","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_QGOH_PQ_4:P0016"]},{"id":"RAW_QGOH_PQ_4:P0017","text":"Self-contentment can arise from Reason, and only that self-contentment which arises from Reason is the greatest that can be given.","kind":"passage","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_QGOH_PQ_4:P0017"]},{"id":"RAW_QGOH_PQ_4:P0018","text":"DEMONSTRATION","kind":"passage","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_QGOH_PQ_4:P0018"]},{"id":"RAW_QGOH_PQ_4:P0019","text":"Self-contentment is Joy arising from the fact that a man contemplates himself and his own power of acting (by Definition 25 of the Affects). But man's true power of acting, or virtue, is Reason itself (by Proposition 3, Part III), which a man contemplates clearly and distinctly (by Propositions 40 and 43, Part II). Therefore self-contentment arises from Reason. Next, while contemplating himself, a man perceives clearly and distinctly, or adequately, nothing except those things which follow from his own power of acting (by Definition 2, Part III), that is (by Proposition 3, Part III), which follow from his own power of understanding; and therefore from this contemplation alone arises the greatest self-contentment that can be given. Q. E. D.","kind":"passage","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_QGOH_PQ_4:P0019"]},{"id":"RAW_QGOH_PQ_4:P0020","text":"SCHOLIUM","kind":"passage","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_QGOH_PQ_4:P0020"]},{"id":"RAW_QGOH_PQ_4:P0021","text":"Self-contentment is in truth the highest thing that we can hope for. For (as we showed in Proposition 25 of this Part) no one endeavours to preserve his own being for the sake of any end; and because this self-contentment is more and more fostered and strengthened by praise (by the Corollary to Proposition 53, Part III), and, on the other hand (by the Corollary to Proposition 55, Part III), is more and more disturbed by blame, we are therefore led most of all by glory, and can scarcely endure life with disgrace.","kind":"passage","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_QGOH_PQ_4:P0021"]},{"id":"RAW_QGOH_PQ_4:P0022","text":"PROPOSITION LIII","kind":"passage","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_QGOH_PQ_4:P0022"]},{"id":"RAW_QGOH_PQ_4:P0023","text":"Humility is not a virtue, or does not arise from Reason.","kind":"passage","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_QGOH_PQ_4:P0023"]},{"id":"RAW_QGOH_PQ_4:P0024","text":"DEMONSTRATION","kind":"passage","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_QGOH_PQ_4:P0024"]},{"id":"RAW_QGOH_PQ_4:P0025","text":"Humility is Sadness, which arises from the fact that a man contemplates his own impotence (by Def. 26 of the Affects). But insofar as a man knows himself by true Reason, he is supposed to understand his essence, that is (by Prop. 7, Part III), his power. Therefore, if a man, while contemplating himself, perceives some impotence of his own, this does not arise from the fact that he understands himself, but (as we showed in Prop. 55, Part III) from the fact that his power of acting is restrained. But if we suppose that a man conceives his own impotence from the fact that he understands something more powerful than himself, by the knowledge of which he determines his own power of acting, then we conceive nothing other than that the man understands himself distinctly, or (by Prop. 26 of this Part) that his power of acting is assisted.","kind":"passage","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_QGOH_PQ_4:P0025"]},{"id":"RAW_QGOH_PQ_4:P0026","text":"Therefore Humility, or Sadness which arises from the fact that a man contemplates his own impotence, does not arise from true contemplation or Reason, and is not a virtue, but a passion. Q.E.D.","kind":"passage","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_QGOH_PQ_4:P0026"]},{"id":"RAW_QGOH_PQ_4:P0027","text":"PROPOSITION LIV","kind":"passage","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_QGOH_PQ_4:P0027"]},{"id":"RAW_QGOH_PQ_4:P0028","text":"Repentance is not a virtue, or does not arise from Reason; but he who repents of a deed is twice miserable or impotent.","kind":"passage","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_QGOH_PQ_4:P0028"]},{"id":"RAW_QGOH_PQ_4:P0029","text":"DEMONSTRATION","kind":"passage","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_QGOH_PQ_4:P0029"]},{"id":"RAW_QGOH_PQ_4:P0030","text":"The first part of this is demonstrated as was the preceding Proposition. But the second is evident from the Definition of this affect alone (see Def. 27 of the Affects). For he suffers himself first to be overcome by an evil Desire, then by Sadness.","kind":"passage","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_QGOH_PQ_4:P0030"]},{"id":"RAW_QGOH_PQ_4:P0031","text":"SCHOLIUM","kind":"passage","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_QGOH_PQ_4:P0031"]},{"id":"RAW_QGOH_PQ_4:P0032","text":"Because men rarely live according to the dictate of Reason, these two affects, namely Humility and Repentance, and besides these Hope and Fear, bring more advantage than harm; and thus, since one must err, one ought rather to err on this side. For if all men weak in mind were equally proud, ashamed of nothing and fearing nothing, by what bonds could they be joined and constrained? The multitude is terrified, unless it fears; wherefore it is not surprising that the Prophets, who took thought not for the advantage of a few but for the common advantage, should have commended Humility, Repentance, and Reverence so greatly. And indeed, those who are subject to these affects can much more easily than others be led so that at last they may live under the guidance of Reason, that is, may be free and enjoy the life of the blessed.","kind":"passage","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_QGOH_PQ_4:P0032"]},{"id":"RAW_QGOH_PQ_4:P0033","text":"PROPOSITION LV","kind":"passage","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_QGOH_PQ_4:P0033"]},{"id":"RAW_QGOH_PQ_4:P0034","text":"The greatest Pride or Abjection is the greatest ignorance of oneself.","kind":"passage","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_QGOH_PQ_4:P0034"]},{"id":"RAW_QGOH_PQ_4:P0035","text":"DEMONSTRATION","kind":"passage","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_QGOH_PQ_4:P0035"]},{"id":"RAW_QGOH_PQ_4:P0036","text":"This is evident from Defs. 28 and 29 of the Affects.","kind":"passage","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_QGOH_PQ_4:P0036"]},{"id":"RAW_QGOH_PQ_4:P0037","text":"PROPOSITION LVI","kind":"passage","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_QGOH_PQ_4:P0037"]},{"id":"RAW_QGOH_PQ_4:P0038","text":"The greatest Pride or Abjection indicates the greatest impotence of mind.","kind":"passage","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_QGOH_PQ_4:P0038"]},{"id":"RAW_QGOH_PQ_4:P0039","text":"DEMONSTRATION","kind":"passage","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_QGOH_PQ_4:P0039"]},{"id":"RAW_QGOH_PQ_4:P0040","text":"The first foundation of virtue is to preserve one's own being (by Cor. to Prop. 22 of this Part), and this by the guidance of Reason (by Prop. 24 of this Part). Therefore, he who is ignorant of himself is ignorant of the foundation of all the virtues and consequently of all the virtues. Further, to act from virtue is nothing other than to act from the guidance of Reason (by Prop. 24 of this Part); and he who acts from the guidance of Reason must necessarily know that he acts from the guidance of Reason (by Prop. 43, Part II). He, therefore, who is most ignorant of himself, and consequently (as we have just shown) of all the virtues, acts least from virtue, that is (as is clear from Def. 8 of this Part), is most impotent in mind; and therefore (by the preceding Prop.) the greatest pride or Abjection indicates the greatest impotence of mind. Q.E.D.","kind":"passage","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_QGOH_PQ_4:P0040"]},{"id":"RAW_QGOH_PQ_4:P0041","text":"COROLLARY","kind":"passage","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_QGOH_PQ_4:P0041"]},{"id":"RAW_QGOH_PQ_4:P0042","text":"From this it clearly follows that the proud and the abject are most subject to affects.","kind":"passage","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_QGOH_PQ_4:P0042"]},{"id":"RAW_QGOH_PQ_4:P0043","text":"SCHOLIUM","kind":"passage","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_QGOH_PQ_4:P0043"]},{"id":"RAW_QGOH_PQ_4:P0044","text":"Abjection, however, can be corrected more easily than pride, since the latter is an affect of Joy, the former of Sadness, and therefore (by Prop. 18 of this Part) the former is stronger than the latter.","kind":"passage","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_QGOH_PQ_4:P0044"]},{"id":"RAW_QGOH_PQ_4:P0045","text":"PROPOSITION LVII","kind":"passage","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_QGOH_PQ_4:P0045"]},{"id":"RAW_QGOH_PQ_4:P0046","text":"A proud man loves the presence of parasites or flatterers, but hates that of the generous.","kind":"passage","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_QGOH_PQ_4:P0046"]},{"id":"RAW_QGOH_PQ_4:P0047","text":"DEMONSTRATION","kind":"passage","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_QGOH_PQ_4:P0047"]},{"id":"RAW_QGOH_PQ_4:P0048","text":"Pride is Joy arising from the fact that a man thinks of himself more highly than is just (by Defs. 28 and 6 of the Affects); and the proud man will strive, as far as he can, to foster this opinion (see Schol. to Prop. 13, Part III); and thus he will love the presence of parasites or flatterers (I have omitted their definitions, because they are too well known), and will avoid the generous, who think of him as is proper. Q.E.D.","kind":"passage","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_QGOH_PQ_4:P0048"]},{"id":"RAW_QGOH_PQ_4:P0049","text":"SCHOLIUM","kind":"passage","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_QGOH_PQ_4:P0049"]},{"id":"RAW_QGOH_PQ_4:P0050","text":"It would be too long to enumerate here all the evils of Pride, since the proud are subject to all the affects; but to none less than to the affects of Love and Compassion. But here it must by no means be passed over in silence that he too is called proud who thinks less justly than he ought of the rest, and thus in this sense Pride is to be defined as Joy arising from the false opinion that a man thinks himself to be above the rest. And the Sadness contrary to this Pride would have to be defined as Abjection arising from the false opinion that a man believes himself to be below the rest. But, this being posited, we easily conceive that the proud person is necessarily envious (see Schol. Prop. 55 pt. III), and that they especially hate those who are especially praised on account of their virtues; nor is their Hatred easily overcome by Love or a benefit (see Schol. Prop. 41 pt. III); and that they take delight only in the presence of those who indulge their impotent mind, and make a madman out of a fool.","kind":"passage","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_QGOH_PQ_4:P0050"]},{"id":"RAW_QGOH_PQ_4:P0051","text":"Although Abjection is contrary to Pride, nevertheless the abject person is very close to the proud person. For, since his Sadness arises from the fact that he judges his own impotence from the power or virtue of others, his Sadness will therefore be relieved, that is, he will rejoice, if his imagination is occupied in contemplating the vices of others, whence that proverb arose: it is a consolation to the wretched to have companions in misfortune; and, conversely, he will be saddened all the more as he believes himself to be more below the rest; whence it comes about that none are more prone to Envy than the Abject, and that these especially endeavor to observe men's deeds in order to carp at them rather than to correct them, and that at last they praise Abjection alone and glory in it; but in such a way, nevertheless, that they may appear abject.","kind":"passage","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_QGOH_PQ_4:P0051"]},{"id":"RAW_QGOH_PQ_4:P0052","text":"And these things follow from this affect as necessarily as it follows from the nature of a triangle that its three angles are equal to two right angles; and I have already said that I call these and similar affects evil insofar as I attend solely to human advantage. But the laws of Nature have regard to the common order of Nature, of which man is a part; which I wished to note here in passing, lest anyone should think that I wished here to recount men's vices and absurd deeds, and not to demonstrate the nature and properties of things. For, as I said in the Preface to Part Three, I consider human affects and their properties in the same manner as the remaining natural things. And indeed human affects, if they do not indicate human power and artistry, at least indicate those of Nature no less than many other things which we admire and whose contemplation we enjoy. But I proceed to note concerning the affects those things which bring men advantage, or which inflict harm upon them.","kind":"passage","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_QGOH_PQ_4:P0052"]},{"id":"RAW_QGOH_PQ_4:P0053","text":"PROPOSITION LVIII","kind":"passage","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_QGOH_PQ_4:P0053"]},{"id":"RAW_QGOH_PQ_4:P0054","text":"Glory does not conflict with Reason, but can arise from it.","kind":"passage","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_QGOH_PQ_4:P0054"]},{"id":"RAW_QGOH_PQ_4:P0055","text":"DEMONSTRATION","kind":"passage","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_QGOH_PQ_4:P0055"]},{"id":"RAW_QGOH_PQ_4:P0056","text":"This is evident from Def. 30 of the Affects, and from the definition of the Honorable, which see in Schol. 1, Prop. 37 of this Part.","kind":"passage","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_QGOH_PQ_4:P0056"]},{"id":"RAW_QGOH_PQ_4:P0057","text":"SCHOLIUM","kind":"passage","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_QGOH_PQ_4:P0057"]},{"id":"RAW_QGOH_PQ_4:P0058","text":"So-called vain Glory is self-contentment that is sustained solely by the opinion of the multitude; and when that ceases, the self-contentment itself ceases, that is (by Schol. Prop. 52 of this Part), the highest good that each person loves; whence it comes about that one who glories in the opinion of the multitude anxiously strives, with daily concern, acts, and attempts to preserve his reputation. For the multitude is variable and inconstant, and thus, unless reputation is preserved, it quickly fades away; indeed, because all desire to win the applause of the multitude, each person easily represses another's reputation; from which, since there is competition for what is esteemed the highest good, there arises an immense desire to oppress one another in whatever way, and he who finally emerges victorious glories more in having harmed another than in having benefited himself. Therefore this glory, or self-contentment, is truly vain, because it is nothing. What is to be noted concerning Shame is easily gathered from what we have said concerning Compassion and Repentance.","kind":"passage","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_QGOH_PQ_4:P0058"]},{"id":"RAW_QGOH_PQ_4:P0059","text":"I add only this: that, just as Commiseration, so also Shame, although it is not a virtue, is nevertheless good insofar as it indicates that in the man who is suffused with Shame there is a desire to live honorably; just as pain is said to be good insofar as it indicates that the injured part is not yet putrefied; wherefore, although a man who is ashamed of some deed is in fact sad, he is nevertheless more perfect than the shameless man, who has no desire to live honorably.","kind":"passage","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_QGOH_PQ_4:P0059"]},{"id":"RAW_QGOH_PQ_4:P0060","text":"And these are the things that I had undertaken to note concerning the affects of Joy and Sadness. As regards Desires, these are indeed good or evil insofar as they arise from good or evil affects. But all of them, in truth, insofar as they are generated in us from affects that are passions, are blind (as is easily gathered from what we said in Schol. Prop. 44 of this Part), and would be of no use if men could easily be led to live from the dictate of Reason alone, as I shall now show briefly.","kind":"passage","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_QGOH_PQ_4:P0060"]},{"id":"RAW_QGOH_PQ_4:P0061","text":"PROPOSITION LIX","kind":"passage","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_QGOH_PQ_4:P0061"]},{"id":"RAW_QGOH_PQ_4:P0062","text":"To all actions to which we are determined by an affect that is a passion, we can, without it, be determined by Reason.","kind":"passage","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_QGOH_PQ_4:P0062"]},{"id":"RAW_QGOH_PQ_4:P0063","text":"DEMONSTRATION","kind":"passage","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_QGOH_PQ_4:P0063"]},{"id":"RAW_QGOH_PQ_4:P0064","text":"To act from Reason is nothing other (by Prop. 3 and Def. 2, pt. III) than to do those things which follow from the necessity of our nature, considered in itself alone. But Sadness is evil insofar as it diminishes or restrains this power of acting (by Prop. 41 of this Part); therefore, from this affect we can be determined to no action that we could not perform if we were led by Reason. Further, Joy is evil insofar as it prevents a man from being fit to act (by Props. 43 and 44 of this Part); and thus, insofar as it does so, we likewise can be determined to no action that we could not perform if we were led by Reason. Finally, insofar as Joy is good, it agrees with Reason (for it consists in this, that man's power of acting is increased or aided); nor is it a passion except insofar as man's power of acting is not increased to the point at which he adequately conceives himself and his actions (by Prop. 3, pt. III, together with its Scholium)","kind":"passage","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_QGOH_PQ_4:P0064"]},{"id":"RAW_QGOH_PQ_4:P0065","text":"; Wherefore, if a man affected with Joy were led to so great a perfection that he adequately conceived himself and his actions, he would be fit, indeed fitter, for the same actions to which he is now determined by affects that are passions. But all affects are referred to Joy, Sadness, or Desire (see the explanation of the fourth Def. of the Affects), and Desire (by Def. 1 of the Affects) is nothing but the very striving to act; therefore, to all actions to which we are determined by an affect that is a passion, we can be led, without it, by Reason alone. Q. E. D.","kind":"passage","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_QGOH_PQ_4:P0065"]},{"id":"RAW_QGOH_PQ_4:P0066","text":"OTHERWISE","kind":"passage","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_QGOH_PQ_4:P0066"]},{"id":"RAW_QGOH_PQ_4:P0067","text":"Any action whatsoever is called evil insofar as it arises from our being affected by Hatred or by some evil affect (see Cor. 1, Prop. 45 of this Part). But no action, considered in itself alone, is good or evil (as we showed in the Preface to this Part); rather, one and the same action is now good, now evil; therefore we can be led by Reason to the same action which is now evil, or which arises from some evil affect (by Prop. 19 of this Part). Q. E. D.","kind":"passage","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_QGOH_PQ_4:P0067"]},{"id":"RAW_QGOH_PQ_4:P0068","text":"SCHOLIUM","kind":"passage","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_QGOH_PQ_4:P0068"]},{"id":"RAW_QGOH_PQ_4:P0069","text":"These things are explained more clearly by an example. Namely, the action of striking, insofar as it is considered physically, and we attend only to this, that a man raises his arm, closes his hand, and forcefully moves his whole arm downward, is a virtue that is conceived from the structure of the human Body. If, therefore, a man, moved by Anger or Hatred, is determined to close his hand or move his arm, this happens, as we showed in Part Two, because one and the same action can be joined to any images of things; and thus we can be determined to one and the same action both by those images of things which we conceive confusedly and by those which we conceive clearly and distinctly. It is thus apparent that every Desire arising from an affect that is a passion would be of no use if men could be led by Reason. Let us now see why Desire arising from an affect that is a passion is called blind by us.","kind":"passage","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_QGOH_PQ_4:P0069"]},{"id":"RAW_QGOH_PQ_4:P0070","text":"PROPOSITION LX","kind":"passage","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_QGOH_PQ_4:P0070"]},{"id":"RAW_QGOH_PQ_4:P0071","text":"A Desire arising from Joy or Sadness that is referred to one or to several, but not to all, parts of the Body, does not take account of the advantage of the whole man.","kind":"passage","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_QGOH_PQ_4:P0071"]},{"id":"RAW_QGOH_PQ_4:P0072","text":"DEMONSTRATION","kind":"passage","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_QGOH_PQ_4:P0072"]},{"id":"RAW_QGOH_PQ_4:P0073","text":"Let, for example, a part A of the Body be so strengthened by the force of some external cause that it prevails over the rest (by Prop. 6 of this Part). This part will not strive to lose its forces, so that the other parts of the Body may perform their office; for it would have to have the force, or power, of losing its forces, which (by Prop. 6, Part III) is absurd. Therefore that part, and consequently (by Props. 7 and 12, Part III) the Mind also, will strive to preserve that state; and hence the Desire which arises from such an affect of Joy does not take the whole into account. But if, on the contrary, it be supposed that part A is restrained so that the rest prevail, it is demonstrated in the same way that neither does the Desire which arises from Sadness take the whole into account. Q. E. D.","kind":"passage","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_QGOH_PQ_4:P0073"]},{"id":"RAW_QGOH_PQ_4:P0074","text":"SCHOLIUM","kind":"passage","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_QGOH_PQ_4:P0074"]},{"id":"RAW_QGOH_PQ_4:P0075","text":"Since, therefore, Joy is for the most part (by the Schol. to Prop. 44 of this Part) referred to one part of the Body, we therefore for the most part desire to preserve our being, with no regard had to our whole health; to which is added that the Desires by which we are most strongly held (by the Coroll. to Prop. 9 of this Part) take account only of the present time, not of the future.","kind":"passage","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_QGOH_PQ_4:P0075"]},{"id":"RAW_QGOH_PQ_4:P0076","text":"PROPOSITION LXI","kind":"passage","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_QGOH_PQ_4:P0076"]},{"id":"RAW_QGOH_PQ_4:P0077","text":"Desire which arises from Reason cannot have excess.","kind":"passage","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_QGOH_PQ_4:P0077"]},{"id":"RAW_QGOH_PQ_4:P0078","text":"DEMONSTRATION","kind":"passage","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_QGOH_PQ_4:P0078"]},{"id":"RAW_QGOH_PQ_4:P0079","text":"Desire (by Def. 1 of the Affects), considered absolutely, is man's very essence, insofar as it is conceived as determined in any manner to do something; and hence Desire which arises from Reason, that is (by Prop. 3, Part III), which is engendered in us insofar as we act, is man's very essence or nature, insofar as it is conceived as determined to do those things which are adequately conceived through man's essence alone (by Def. 2, Part III). If, therefore, this Desire could have excess, then human nature, considered in itself alone, could exceed itself, or could do more than it can, which is a manifest contradiction; and therefore this Desire cannot have excess. Q. E. D.","kind":"passage","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_QGOH_PQ_4:P0079"]},{"id":"RAW_QGOH_PQ_4:P0080","text":"PROPOSITION LXII","kind":"passage","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_QGOH_PQ_4:P0080"]},{"id":"RAW_QGOH_PQ_4:P0081","text":"Insofar as the Mind conceives things at the dictate of Reason, it is affected equally, whether the idea is of a future or past thing, or of a present one.","kind":"passage","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_QGOH_PQ_4:P0081"]},{"id":"RAW_QGOH_PQ_4:P0082","text":"DEMONSTRATION","kind":"passage","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_QGOH_PQ_4:P0082"]},{"id":"RAW_QGOH_PQ_4:P0083","text":"Whatever the Mind conceives under the guidance of Reason, it conceives all under the same aspect of eternity, or necessity (by Coroll. 2 to Prop. 44, Part II), and is affected with the same certainty (by Prop. 43, Part II, and its Scholium). Wherefore, whether the idea is of a future or past thing, or of a present one, the Mind conceives the thing with the same necessity and is affected with the same certainty; and, whether the idea is of a future or past thing, or of a present one, it will nevertheless be equally true (by Prop. 41, Part II); that is (by Def. 4, Part II), it will nevertheless always have the same properties of an adequate idea; and hence, insofar as the Mind conceives things at the dictate of Reason, it is affected in the same way, whether the idea is of a future or past thing, or of a present one. Q. E. D.","kind":"passage","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_QGOH_PQ_4:P0083"]},{"id":"RAW_QGOH_PQ_4:P0084","text":"SCHOLIUM","kind":"passage","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_QGOH_PQ_4:P0084"]},{"id":"RAW_QGOH_PQ_4:P0085","text":"If we could have adequate knowledge of the duration of things, and determine by Reason their times of existing, we would contemplate future things with the same affect as present ones, and would desire the good which the Mind conceived as future just as if it were present; and consequently, we would necessarily neglect a lesser present good for the sake of a greater future good, and would by no means desire what was good in the present but the cause of some future evil, as we shall presently demonstrate. But we can have only a very inadequate knowledge of the duration of things (by Prop. 31, Part II), and determine the times of things' existing (by the Schol. to Prop. 44, Part","kind":"passage","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_QGOH_PQ_4:P0085"]},{"id":"RAW_QGOH_PQ_4:P0086","text":"II) by imagination alone, which is not affected equally by the image of a present thing and of a future one; whence it comes about that the true knowledge of good and evil which we have is only abstract, or universal, and that the judgment which we make concerning the order of things and the connection of causes, in order that we may determine what is good or evil for us in the present, is rather imaginary than real. And therefore it is not surprising that Desire which arises from the knowledge of good and evil, insofar as this foresees the future, can more easily be restrained by the Desire for things that are pleasant in the present; concerning which see Proposition 16 of this Part.","kind":"passage","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_QGOH_PQ_4:P0086"]},{"id":"RAW_QGOH_PQ_4:P0087","text":"PROPOSITION LXIII","kind":"passage","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_QGOH_PQ_4:P0087"]},{"id":"RAW_QGOH_PQ_4:P0088","text":"He who is guided by Fear, and does good in order to avoid evil, is not guided by Reason.","kind":"passage","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_QGOH_PQ_4:P0088"]},{"id":"RAW_QGOH_PQ_4:P0089","text":"DEMONSTRATION","kind":"passage","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_QGOH_PQ_4:P0089"]},{"id":"RAW_QGOH_PQ_4:P0090","text":"All affects which are related to the Mind insofar as it acts, that is (by Prop. 3, Part III), which are related to Reason, are none other than affects of Joy and Desire (by Prop. 59, Part III); and hence (by Def. 13 of the Affects), he who is guided by Fear, and does good from fear of evil, is not guided by Reason. Q. E. D.","kind":"passage","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_QGOH_PQ_4:P0090"]},{"id":"RAW_QGOH_PQ_4:P0091","text":"SCHOLIUM","kind":"passage","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_QGOH_PQ_4:P0091"]},{"id":"RAW_QGOH_PQ_4:P0092","text":"The superstitious, who know how to reproach vices rather than teach virtues, and who strive to guide men not by Reason but to restrain them by Fear in such a way that they flee evil rather than love virtues, intend nothing other than that the rest should become as miserable as they themselves are; and therefore it is not surprising if they are for the most part troublesome and hateful to men.","kind":"passage","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_QGOH_PQ_4:P0092"]},{"id":"RAW_QGOH_PQ_4:P0093","text":"COROLLARY","kind":"passage","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_QGOH_PQ_4:P0093"]},{"id":"RAW_QGOH_PQ_4:P0094","text":"By Desire which arises from Reason, we directly pursue good and indirectly flee evil.","kind":"passage","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_QGOH_PQ_4:P0094"]},{"id":"RAW_QGOH_PQ_4:P0095","text":"DEMONSTRATION","kind":"passage","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_QGOH_PQ_4:P0095"]},{"id":"RAW_QGOH_PQ_4:P0096","text":"For Desire which arises from Reason can arise only from the affect of Joy, which is not a passion (by Prop. 59, Part III), that is, from Joy which cannot have excess (by Prop. 61 of this Part), and not from Sadness; and hence this Desire (by Prop. 8 of this Part) arises from the knowledge of good, and not of evil; and thus, under the guidance of Reason, we directly seek good, and flee evil only insofar as that. Q. E. D.","kind":"passage","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_QGOH_PQ_4:P0096"]},{"id":"RAW_QGOH_PQ_4:P0097","text":"SCHOLIUM","kind":"passage","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_QGOH_PQ_4:P0097"]},{"id":"RAW_QGOH_PQ_4:P0098","text":"This Corollary is explained by the example of the sick man and the healthy man. The sick man eats that which he has an aversion to, from fear of death; whereas the healthy man takes pleasure in food, and thus enjoys life better than if he feared death and directly desired to avoid it. So the judge who condemns a guilty man to death, not from Hatred or Anger, etc., but solely from Love of the public welfare, is guided by Reason alone.","kind":"passage","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_QGOH_PQ_4:P0098"]},{"id":"RAW_QGOH_PQ_4:P0099","text":"PROPOSITION LXIV","kind":"passage","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_QGOH_PQ_4:P0099"]},{"id":"RAW_QGOH_PQ_4:P0100","text":"The knowledge of evil is inadequate knowledge.","kind":"passage","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_QGOH_PQ_4:P0100"]},{"id":"RAW_QGOH_PQ_4:P0101","text":"DEMONSTRATION","kind":"passage","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_QGOH_PQ_4:P0101"]},{"id":"RAW_QGOH_PQ_4:P0102","text":"The knowledge of evil (by Prop. 8 of this Part) is Sadness itself, insofar as we are conscious of it. But Sadness is a transition to a lesser perfection (by Def. 3 of the Affects), which therefore cannot be understood through the essence of man itself (by Props. 6 and 7, Part III); and consequently (by Def. 2, Part III) it is a passion, which (by Prop. 3, Part III) depends on inadequate ideas; and consequently (by Prop. 29, Part II) the knowledge of it, namely the knowledge of evil, is inadequate. Q. E. D.","kind":"passage","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_QGOH_PQ_4:P0102"]},{"id":"RAW_QGOH_PQ_4:P0103","text":"COROLLARY","kind":"passage","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_QGOH_PQ_4:P0103"]},{"id":"RAW_QGOH_PQ_4:P0104","text":"From this it follows that, if the human Mind had only adequate ideas, it would form no notion of evil.","kind":"passage","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_QGOH_PQ_4:P0104"]},{"id":"RAW_QGOH_PQ_4:P0105","text":"PROPOSITION LXV","kind":"passage","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_QGOH_PQ_4:P0105"]},{"id":"RAW_QGOH_PQ_4:P0106","text":"Of two goods we shall, under the guidance of Reason, pursue the greater, and of two evils the lesser.","kind":"passage","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_QGOH_PQ_4:P0106"]},{"id":"RAW_QGOH_PQ_4:P0107","text":"DEMONSTRATION","kind":"passage","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_QGOH_PQ_4:P0107"]},{"id":"RAW_QGOH_PQ_4:P0108","text":"A good which prevents us from enjoying a greater good is in reality an evil; for evil and good (as we have shown in the Preface to this Part) are predicated of things insofar as we compare them with one another; and (by the same reasoning) a lesser evil is in reality a good; wherefore (by Coroll. of Prop. 63 of this Part), under the guidance of Reason, we shall desire or pursue only the greater good and the lesser evil. Q. E. D.","kind":"passage","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_QGOH_PQ_4:P0108"]},{"id":"RAW_QGOH_PQ_4:P0109","text":"COROLLARY","kind":"passage","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_QGOH_PQ_4:P0109"]},{"id":"RAW_QGOH_PQ_4:P0110","text":"Under the guidance of Reason, we shall pursue a lesser evil for the sake of a greater good, and we shall neglect a lesser good which is the cause of a greater evil. For the evil which is here called lesser is in reality a good, and the good, conversely, an evil; wherefore (by Coroll. of Prop. 63 of this Part) we shall desire the former and neglect the latter. Q. E. D.","kind":"passage","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_QGOH_PQ_4:P0110"]},{"id":"RAW_QGOH_PQ_4:P0111","text":"PROPOSITION LXVI","kind":"passage","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_QGOH_PQ_4:P0111"]},{"id":"RAW_QGOH_PQ_4:P0112","text":"Under the guidance of Reason, we shall desire a greater future good in preference to a lesser present one, and a lesser present evil in preference to a greater future one.","kind":"passage","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_QGOH_PQ_4:P0112"]},{"id":"RAW_QGOH_PQ_4:P0113","text":"DEMONSTRATION","kind":"passage","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_QGOH_PQ_4:P0113"]},{"id":"RAW_QGOH_PQ_4:P0114","text":"If the Mind could have adequate knowledge of a future thing, it would be affected by the same affect toward the future thing as toward the present one (by Prop. 62 of this Part); wherefore, insofar as we attend to Reason itself, as we suppose ourselves to do in this Proposition, it is the same whether a greater future or present good or evil is supposed; and consequently (by Prop. 65 of this Part) we shall desire a greater future good in preference to a lesser present one, etc. Q. E. D.","kind":"passage","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_QGOH_PQ_4:P0114"]},{"id":"RAW_QGOH_PQ_4:P0115","text":"COROLLARY","kind":"passage","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_QGOH_PQ_4:P0115"]},{"id":"RAW_QGOH_PQ_4:P0116","text":"Under the guidance of Reason, we shall desire a lesser present evil which is the cause of a greater future good; and we shall neglect a lesser present good which is the cause of a greater future evil. This Corollary is related to the preceding Proposition as the Corollary of Prop. 65 is related to Prop. 65 itself.","kind":"passage","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_QGOH_PQ_4:P0116"]},{"id":"RAW_QGOH_PQ_4:P0117","text":"SCHOLIUM","kind":"passage","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_QGOH_PQ_4:P0117"]},{"id":"RAW_QGOH_PQ_4:P0118","text":"If, therefore, these matters are compared with those which we have shown in this Part, up to Proposition 18, concerning the powers of the affects, we shall readily see how much a man who is guided solely by affect or opinion differs from one who is guided by Reason. For the former, whether he wishes it or not, does those things of which he is most ignorant; whereas the latter complies with the wishes of no one but himself, and does only those things which he knows to be foremost in life, and which he therefore desires most. And so I call the former a slave, but the latter free; concerning whose character and manner of living I should still like to note a few things.","kind":"passage","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_QGOH_PQ_4:P0118"]},{"id":"RAW_QGOH_PQ_4:P0119","text":"PROPOSITION LXVII","kind":"passage","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_QGOH_PQ_4:P0119"]},{"id":"RAW_QGOH_PQ_4:P0120","text":"A free man thinks of nothing less than of death; and his wisdom is a meditation not on death but on life.","kind":"passage","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_QGOH_PQ_4:P0120"]},{"id":"RAW_QGOH_PQ_4:P0121","text":"DEMONSTRATION","kind":"passage","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_QGOH_PQ_4:P0121"]},{"id":"RAW_QGOH_PQ_4:P0122","text":"A free man, that is, one who lives according to the dictate of Reason alone, is not led by Fear of death (by Prop. 63 of this Part), but directly desires the good (by the Corollary of the same Prop.), that is (by Prop. 24 of this Part), to act, to live, to preserve his own being from the foundation of seeking his own advantage; and consequently he thinks of nothing less than of death, but his wisdom is a meditation on life. Q. E. D.","kind":"passage","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_QGOH_PQ_4:P0122"]},{"id":"RAW_QGOH_PQ_4:P0123","text":"PROPOSITION LXVIII","kind":"passage","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_QGOH_PQ_4:P0123"]},{"id":"RAW_QGOH_PQ_4:P0124","text":"If men were born free, they would form no concept of good and evil, so long as they were free.","kind":"passage","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_QGOH_PQ_4:P0124"]},{"id":"RAW_QGOH_PQ_4:P0125","text":"DEMONSTRATION","kind":"passage","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_QGOH_PQ_4:P0125"]}],"source_digest":"ba63b982dc09399ab376180c5ad06e2f4c81a446a5d57cd388c697c3fbeae070","source_language_code":"la","original_title":"Pars quarta - De servitute humana seu de affectuum viribus · passage 4"}],"passage_basis":"frozen-grounding","original_title":"Pars quarta - De servitute humana seu de affectuum viribus · passage 4","title_language_code":"en"},{"id":"RAW_QGOH_PQ_5","title":"Part Four - On Human Bondage, or the Powers of the Affects · passage 5","order":18,"chapter_id":"CH_QGOH_PQ_5","text":"Illum liberum esse dixi, qui sola ducitur Ratione ; qui itaque liber nascitur, et liber manet, non nisi adæquatas ideas habet ; ac proinde mali conceptum habet nullum (per Coroll. Prop. 64 hujus), et consequenter (nam bonum et malum correlata sunt) neque boni. Q. E. D.\n\nSCHOLIUM\n\nHujus Propositionis hypothesin falsam esse, nec posse concipi, nisi quatenus ad solam naturam humanam, seu potius ad Deum attendimus, non quatenus infinitus, sed quatenus tantummodo causa est, cur homo existat, patet ex 4 Propositione hujus Partis. Atque hoc, et alia quæ jam demonstravimus, videntur a Mose significari in illa primi hominis historia. In ea enim nulla alia Dei potentia concipitur, quam illa, qua hominem creavit, hoc est potentia, qua hominis solummodo utilitati consuluit ; atque eatenus narratur, quod Deus homini libero prohibuerit, ne de arbore cognitionis boni et mali comederet, et quod, simulac de ea comederet, statim mortem metueret potius quam vivere cuperet. Deinde, quod inventa ab homine uxore, quæ cum sua natura prorsus conveniebat, cognovit nihil posse in Natura dari, quod ipsi posset illa esse utilius ; sed quod, postquam bruta sibi similia esse credidit, statim eorum affectus imitari inceperit (vide Prop. 27 p. III), et libertatem suam amittere ; quam Patriarchæ postea recuperaverunt, ducti Spiritu Christi, hoc est Dei idea, a qua sola pendet, ut homo liber sit, et ut bonum, quod sibi cupit, reliquis hominibus cupiat, ut supra (per Prop. 37 hujus) demonstravimus.\n\nPROPOSITIO LXIX\n\nHominis liberi virtus æque magna cernitur in declinandis quam in superandis periculis.\n\nDEMONSTRATIO\n\nAffectus coerceri nec tolli potest, nisi affectu contrario, et fortiore affectu coercendo (per Prop. 7 hujus). At cœca Audacia et Metus affectus sunt, qui æque magni possunt concipi (per Prop. 5 et 3 hujus) : Ergo æque magna animi virtus, seu fortitudo (hujus Definitionem vide in Schol. Prop. 59 p. III) requiritur ad Audaciam quam ad Metum coercendum ; hoc est (per Defin. 40 et 41 Affect.), homo liber eadem animi virtute pericula declinat, qua eadem superare tentat. Q. E. D.\n\nCOROLLARIUM\n\nHomini igitur libero æque magnæ Animositati fuga in tempore, ac pugna ducitur : sive homo liber eadem Animositate, seu animi præsentia, qua certamen, fugam eligit.\n\nSCHOLIUM\n\nQuid Animositas sit, vel quid per ipsam intelligam, in Scholio Prop. 59 p. III explicui. Per periculum autem id omne intelligo, quod potest esse causa alicujus mali, nempe Tristitiæ, Odii, Discordiæ etc.\n\nPROPOSITIO LXX\n\nHomo liber, qui inter ignaros vivit, eorum, quantum potest, beneficia declinare studet.\n\nDEMONSTRATIO\n\nUnusquisque ex suo ingenio judicat, quid bonum sit (vide Schol. Prop. 39 p. III) ; ignarus igitur, qui in aliquem beneficium contulit, id ex suo ingenio æstimabit, et si minoris ab eo, cui datum est, æstimari videt, contristabitur (per Prop. 42 p. III). At homo liber reliquos homines amicitia sibi jungere (per Prop. 37 hujus), nec paria hominibus beneficia ex eorum affectu referre, sed se et reliquos libero Rationis judicio ducere, et ea tantum agere studet, quæ ipse prima esse novit : Ergo homo liber, ne ignaris odio sit, et ne eorum appetitui, sed soli Rationi obsequatur, eorum beneficia, quantum potest, declinare conabitur. Q. E. D.\n\nSCHOLIUM\n\nDico quantum potest. Nam quamvis homines ignari sint, sunt tamen homines, qui in necessitatibus humanum auxilium, quo nullum præstabilius est, adferre queunt ; atque adeo sæpe fit, ut necesse sit ab iisdem beneficium accipere, et consequenter iisdem contra ex eorum ingenio congratulari ; ad quod accedit, quod etiam in declinandis beneficiis cautio esse debet, ne videamur eosdem contemnere, vel præ Avaritia remunerationem timere, atque ita dum eorum Odium fugimus, eo ipso in eorum offensionem incurramus. Quare in declinandis beneficiis ratio utilis et honesti habenda est.\n\nPROPOSITIO LXXI\n\nSoli homines liberi erga invicem gratissimi sunt.\n\nDEMONSTRATIO\n\nSoli homines liberi sibi invicem utilissimi sunt, et maxima amicitiæ necessitudine invicem junguntur (per Prop. 35 hujus, et 1 ejus Coroll.) parique amoris studio sibi invicem benefacere conantur (per Prop. 37 hujus) ; adeoque (per 34 Affect. Defin.) soli homines liberi erga se invicem gratissimi sunt. Q. E. D.\n\nSCHOLIUM\n\nGratia, quam homines qui cœca Cupiditate ducuntur, invicem habent, mercatura seu aucupium potius, quam gratia plerumque est. Porro ingratitudo affectus non est. Est tamen ingratitudo turpis, quia plerumque hominem nimio Odio, Ira, vel Superbia, vel Avaritia, etc., affectum esse indicat. Nam qui præ stultitia dona compensare nescit, ingratus non est, et multo minus ille, qui donis non movetur meretricis, ut ipsius libidini inserviat, nec furis, ut ipsius furta celet, vel alterius similis. Nam hic contra animum habere constantem ostendit, qui scilicet se nullis donis ad suam vel communem perniciem patitur corrumpi.\n\nPROPOSITIO LXXII\n\nHomo liber nunquam dolo malo, sed semper cum fide agit.\n\nDEMONSTRATIO\n\nSi liber homo quicquam dolo malo, quatenus liber est, ageret, id ex dictamine Rationis ageret (nam eatenus tantum liber a nobis appellatur) : atque adeo dolo malo agere virtus esset (per Prop. 24 hujus), et consequenter (per eandem Prop.) unicuique ad suum esse conservandum consultius esset, dolo malo agere ; hoc est (ut per se notum), hominibus consultius esset, verbis solummodo convenire, re autem invicem esse contrarios, quod (per Coroll. Prop. 31 hujus) est absurdum. Ergo homo liber etc. Q. E. D.\n\nSCHOLIUM\n\nSi jam quæratur, quid si homo se perfidia a præsenti mortis periculo posset liberare, an non ratio suum esse conservandi omnino suadet, ut perfidus sit ? Respondebitur eodem modo, quod si Ratio id suadeat, suadet ergo id omnibus hominibus, atque adeo Ratio omnino suadet hominibus, ne nisi dolo malo paciscantur, vires conjungere et jura habere communia, hoc est, ne revera jura habeant communia, quod est absurdum.\n\nPROPOSITIO LXXIII\n\nHomo, qui Ratione ducitur, magis in Civitate, ubi ex communi decreto vivit, quam in solitudine, ubi sibi soli obtemperat, liber est.\n\nDEMONSTRATIO\n\nHomo, qui Ratione ducitur, non ducitur Metu ad obtemperandum (per Prop. 63 hujus) sed, quatenus suum esse ex Rationis dictamine conservare conatur, hoc est (per Schol. Prop. 66 hujus) quatenus libere vivere conatur, communis vitæ et utilitatis rationem tenere (per Prop. 37 hujus), et consequenter (ut in Schol. 2 Prop. 37 hujus ostendimus) ex communi Civitatis decreto vivere cupit. Cupit ergo homo, qui Ratione ducitur, ut liberius vivat, communia Civitatis jura tenere. Q. E. D.\n\nSCHOLIUM\n\nHæc et similia, quæ de vera hominis libertate ostendimus, ad Fortitudinem, hoc est (per Schol. Prop. 59 p. III) ad Animositatem et Generositatem referuntur. Nec operæ pretium duco, omnes Fortitudinis proprietates hic separatim demonstrare, et multo minus, quod vir fortis neminem odio habeat, nemini irascatur, invideat, indignetur, neminem despiciat, minimeque superbiat. Nam hæc, et omnia, quæ ad veram vitam et Religionem spectant, facile ex Propositione 37 et 46 hujus Partis convincuntur ; nempe quod Odium Amore contra vincendum sit, et quod unusquisque, qui Ratione ducitur, bonum, quod sibi appetit, reliquis etiam ut sit, cupiat. Ad quod accedit id, quod in Scholio Propositionis 50 hujus Partis, et aliis in locis notavimus : quod scilicet vir fortis hoc apprime consideret, nempe quod omnia ex necessitate divinæ naturæ sequantur, ac proinde quicquid molestum et malum esse cogitat, et quicquid præterea impium, horrendum, injustum, et turpe videtur, ex eo oritur, quod res ipsas perturbate, mutilate, et confuse concipit ; et hac de causa apprime conatur res, ut in se sunt, concipere, et veræ cognitionis impedimenta amovere, ut sunt Odium, Ira, Invidia, Irrisio, Superbia, et reliqua hujusmodi, quæ in præcedentibus notavimus ; atque adeo, quantum potest, conatur ut diximus, bene agere et lætari. Quousque autem humana virtus ad hæc consequenda se extendat, et quid possit, in sequenti Parte demonstrabo.\n\nAPPENDIX\n\nQuæ in hac Parte de recta vivendi Ratione tradidi, non sunt ita disposita ut uno aspectu videri possint, sed disperse a me demonstrata sunt, prout scilicet unum ex alio facilius deducere potuerim. Eadem igitur hic recolligere et ad summa capita redigere proposui.\n\nCAPUT I\n\nOmnes nostri conatus, seu Cupiditates, ex necessitate nostræ naturæ ita sequuntur, ut vel per ipsam solam, tanquam per proximam suam causam, possint intelligi, vel quatenus Naturæ sumus pars, quæ per se absque aliis individuis non potest adæquate concipi.\n\nCAPUT II\n\nCupiditates, quæ ex nostra natura ita sequuntur, ut per ipsam solam possit intelligi, sunt illæ, quæ ad Mentem referuntur, quatenus hæc ideis adæquatis constare concipitur ; reliquæ vero Cupiditates ad Mentem non referuntur, nisi quatenus res inadæquate concipit, et earum vis et incrementum non humana, sed rerum, quæ extra nos sunt, potentia definiri debet ; et ideo illæ recte actiones, hæ autem passiones vocantur ; illæ namque nostram potentiam semper indicant, et hæ contra nostram impotentiam et mutilatam cognitionem.\n\nCAPUT III\n\nNostræ actiones, hoc est Cupiditates illæ, quæ hominis potentia seu Ratione definiuntur, semper bonæ sunt, reliquæ autem tam bonæ quam malæ possunt esse.\n\nCAPUT IV\n\nIn vita itaque apprime utile est, intellectum seu Rationem, quantum possumus, perficere, et in hoc uno summa hominis felicitas seu beatitudo consistit ; quippe beatitudo nihil aliud est, quam ipsa animi acquiescentia, quæ ex Dei intuitiva cognitione oritur : at intellectum perficere nihil etiam aliud est, quam Deum, Deique attributa et actiones, quæ ex ipsius naturæ necessitate consequuntur, intelligere. Quare hominis, qui Ratione ducitur, finis ultimus, hoc est summa Cupiditas, qua reliquas omnes moderari studet, est illa, qua fertur ad se, resque omnes, quæ sub ipsius intelligentiam cadere possunt, adæquate concipiendum.\n\nCAPUT V\n\nNulla igitur vita rationalis est sine intelligentia ; et res eatenus tantum bonæ sunt, quatenus hominem juvant, ut Mentis vita fruatur, quæ intelligentia definitur. Quæ autem contra impediunt, quo minus homo Rationem perficere et rationali vita frui possit, eas solummodo malas esse dicimus.\n\nCAPUT VI\n\nSed quia omnia illa, quorum homo efficiens est causa, necessario bona sunt, nihil ergo mali homini evenire potest, nisi a causis externis ; nempe quatenus pars est totius Naturæ, cujus legibus humana natura obtemperare, et cui infinitis modis pœne sese accommodare cogitur.\n\nCAPUT VII\n\nNec fieri potest, ut homo non sit Naturæ pars, et communem ejus ordinem non sequatur ; sed si inter talia individua versetur, quæ cum ipsius hominis natura conveniunt, eo ipso hominis agendi potentia juvabitur et fovebitur. At si contra inter talia sit, quæ cum ipsius natura minime conveniunt, vix absque magna ipsius mutatione iisdem sese accommodare poterit.\n\nCAPUT VIII\n\nQuicquid in rerum Natura datur, quod judicamus malum esse, sive posse impedire, quo minus existere et vita rationali frui queamus, id a nobis removere ea via, quæ securior videtur, licet ; et quicquid contra datur, quod judicamus bonum sive utile esse ad nostrum esse conservandum, et vita rationali fruendum, id ad nostrum usum capere, et eo quocunque modo uti nobis licet ; et absolute id unicuique summo Naturæ jure facere licet, quod ad ipsius utilitatem conferre judicat.\n\nCAPUT IX\n\nNihil magis cum natura alicujus rei convenire potest, quam reliqua ejusdem speciei individua ; adeoque (per Caput VII) nihil homini ad suum esse conservandum, et vita rationali fruendum utilius datur quam homo, qui Ratione ducitur. Deinde, quia inter res singulares nihil novimus, quod homine, qui Ratione ducitur, sit præstantius, nulla ergo re magis potest unusquisque ostendere, quantum arte et ingenio valeat, quam in hominibus ita educandis, ut tandem ex proprio Rationis imperio vivant.\n\nCAPUT X\n\nQuatenus homines Invidia aut aliquo Odii affectu in se invicem feruntur, eatenus invicem contrarii sunt, et consequenter eo magis timendi, quo plus possunt quam reliqua Naturæ individua.\n\nCAPUT XI\n\nAnimi tamen non armis sed Amore et Generositate vincuntur.\n\nCAPUT XII\n\nHominibus apprime utile est consuetudines jungere, seseque iis vinculis astringere, quibus aptius de se omnibus unum efficiant, et absolute ea agere, quæ firmandis amicitiis inserviunt.\n\nCAPUT XIII\n\nSed ad hæc ars et vigilantia requiritur. Sunt enim homines varii (nam rari sunt, qui ex Rationis præscripto vivunt), et tamen plerumque invidi, et magis ad vindictam quam ad Misericordiam proclives. Unumquemque igitur ex ipsius ingenio ferre et sese continere, ne eorum affectus imitetur, singularis animi potentiæ opus est. At qui contra homines carpere, et vitia potius exprobrare quam virtutes docere, et hominum animos non firmare sed frangere norunt, ii et sibi et reliquis molesti sunt ; unde multi, præ nimia scilicet animi impatientia, falsoque religionis studio, inter bruta potius quam inter homines vivere maluerunt ; ut pueri vel adolescentes, qui parentum jurgia æquo animo ferre nequeunt, militatum confugiunt, et incommoda belli et imperium tyrannidis præ domesticis commodis et paternis admonitionibus eligunt, et quidvis oneris sibi imponi patiuntur, dummodo parentes ulciscantur.\n\nCAPUT XIV\n\nQuamvis igitur homines omnia plerumque ex sua libidine moderentur, ex eorum tamen communi societate multo plura commoda quam damna sequuntur. Quare satius est, eorum injurias æquo animo ferre, et studium iis adhibere, quæ concordiæ et amicitiæ conciliandæ inserviunt.\n\nCAPUT XV\n\nQuæ concordiam gignunt sunt illa, quæ ad justitiam, æquitatem, et honestatem referuntur. Nam homines præter id, quod injustum et iniquum est, etiam ægre ferunt, quod turpe habetur, sive quod aliquis receptos civitatis mores aspernatur. Amori autem conciliando illa apprime necessaria sunt, quæ ad Religionem et Pietatem spectant. De quibus vide Schol. 1 et 2 Prop. 37 et Schol. Prop. 46 et Schol. Prop. 73 p. IV.\n\nCAPUT XVI\n\nSolet præterea concordia ex Metu plerumque gigni, sed sine fide. Adde, quod Metus ex animi impotentia oritur, et propterea ad Rationis usum non pertinet ; ut nec Commiseratio, quamvis Pietatis speciem præ se ferre videatur.\n\nCAPUT XVII\n\nVincuntur præterea homines etiam largitate, præcipue ii qui non habent, unde comparare possint illa, quæ ad vitam sustentandam necessaria sunt. Attamen unicuique indigenti auxilium ferre, vires et utilitatem viri privati longe superat. Divitiæ namque viri privati longe impares sunt ad id suppeditandum. Unius præterea viri facultas limitatior est, quam ut omnes sibi possit amicitia jungere ; quare pauperum cura integræ societati incumbit, et ad communem tantum utilitatem spectat.\n\nCAPUT XVIII\n\nIn beneficiis accipiendis et gratia referenda alia prorsus debet esse cura, de qua vide Schol. Prop. 70 et Schol. Prop. 71 p. IV.\n\nCAPUT XIX\n\nAmor præterea meretricius, hoc est generandi libido, quæ ex forma oritur, et absolute omnis Amor, qui aliam causam præter animi libertatem agnoscit, facile in Odium transit ; nisi, quod pejus est, species delirii sit, atque tum magis discordia quam concordia fovetur. Vid. Schol. Prop. 31 p. III.\n\nCAPUT XX\n\nAd matrimonium quod attinet, certum est, ipsum cum Ratione convenire, si Cupiditas miscendi corpora non ex sola forma, sed etiam ex Amore liberos procreandi et sapienter educandi ingeneretur ; et præterea si utriusque, viri scilicet et fœminæ, Amor non solam formam, sed animi præcipue libertatem pro causa habeat.\n\nCAPUT XXI\n\nGignit præterea adulatio concordiam ; sed fœdo servitutis crimine, vel perfidia ; nulli quippe magis adulatione capiuntur quam superbi, qui primi esse volunt, nec sunt.\n\nCAPUT XXII\n\nAbjectioni falsa pietatis et religionis species inest. Et quamvis Abjectio Superbiæ sit contraria, est tamen abjectus superbo proximus. Vide Schol. Prop. 57 p. IV.\n\nCAPUT XXIII\n\nConfert præterea concordiæ Pudor in iis tantum, quæ celari non possunt. Deinde, quia ipse Pudor species est Tristitiæ, ad Rationis usum non spectat.\n\nCAPUT XXIV\n\nCæteri Tristitiæ erga homines affectus directe justitiæ, æquitati, honestati, pietati, et religioni opponuntur ; et, quamvis Indignatio æquitatis speciem præ se ferre videatur, ibi tamen sine lege vivitur, ubi unicuique de factis alterius judicium ferre, et suum vel alterius jus vindicare licet.\n\nCAPUT XXV\n\nModestia, hoc est Cupiditas hominibus placendi, quæ ex Ratione determinatur, ad Pietatem (ut in Schol. 1 Prop. 37 p. IV diximus) refertur. Sed si ex affectu oriatur, Ambitio est sive Cupiditas, qua homines falsa Pietatis imagine plerumque discordias et seditiones concitant. Nam qui reliquos consilio aut re juvare cupit, ut simul summo fruantur bono, is apprime studebit eorum sibi Amorem conciliare ; non autem eos in admirationem traducere, ut disciplina ex ipso habeat vocabulum, nec ullas absolute Invidiæ causas dare. In communibus deinde colloquiis cavebit hominum vitia referre, et de humana impotentia non nisi parce loqui curabit : at largiter de humana virtute seu potentia, et qua via possit perfici ; ut sic homines, non ex Metu aut aversione, sed solo Lætitiæ affectu moti ex Rationis præscripto, quantum in se est, conentur vivere.\n\nCAPUT XXVI\n\nPræter homines nihil singulare in Natura novimus, cujus Mente gaudere, et quod nobis amicitia aut aliquo consuetudinis genere jungere possumus ; adeoque quicquid in rerum Natura extra homines datur, id nostræ utilitatis ratio conservare, destruere, vel quocunque modo ad nostrum usum adaptare nos docet.\n\nCAPUT XXVII\n\nUtilitas, quam ex rebus, quæ extra nos sunt, capimus, est, præter experientiam et cognitionem, quam acquirimus ex eo, quod easdem observamus, et ex his formis in alias mutamus, præcipua corporis conservatio ; et hac Ratione res illæ imprimis utiles sunt, quæ Corpus ita alere et nutrire possunt, ut ejus omnes partes officio suo recte fungi queant. Nam quo Corpus aptius est, ut pluribus modis possit affici, et corpora externa pluribus modis afficere, eo Mens ad cogitandum est aptior (vide Prop. 38 et 39 p. IV). At hujus notæ perpauca in Natura esse videntur ; quare ad Corpus, ut requiritur, nutriendum, necesse est, multis naturæ diversæ alimentis uti. Quippe humanum Corpus ex plurimis diversæ naturæ partibus componitur, quæ continuo alimento indigent, et vario, ut totum Corpus ad omnia, quæ ex ipsius natura sequi possunt, æque aptum sit, et consequenter ut Mens etiam æque apta sit ad plura concipiendum.\n\nCAPUT XXVIII\n\nAd hæc autem comparandum vix uniuscujusque vires sufficerent, nisi homines operas mutuas traderent. Verum omnium rerum compendium pecunia attulit, unde factum, ut ejus imago Mentem vulgi maxime occupare soleat ; quia vix ullam Lætitiæ speciem imaginari possunt, nisi concomitante nummorum idea tanquam causa.\n\nCAPUT XXIX\n\nSed hoc vitium eorum tantum est, qui non ex indigentia, nec propter necessitates nummos quærunt, sed quia lucri artes didicerunt, quibus se magnifice efferunt. Cæterum Corpus ex consuetudine pascunt, sed parce, quia tantum de suis bonis se perdere credunt, quantum sui Corporis conservationi impendunt. At qui verum nummorum usum norunt, et divitiarum modum ex sola indigentia moderantur, paucis contenti vivunt.\n\nCAPUT XXX\n\nCum igitur res illæ sint bonæ, quæ Corporis partes juvant, ut suo officio fungantur, et Lætitia in eo consistat, quod hominis potentia, quatenus Mente et Corpore constat, juvatur vel augetur ; sunt ergo illa omnia, quæ Lætitiam afferunt, bona. Attamen, quoniam contra non eum in finem res agunt, ut nos Lætitia afficiant, nec earum agendi potentia ex nostra utilitate temperatur, et denique quoniam Lætitia plerumque ad unam Corporis partem potissimum refertur ; habent ergo plerumque Lætitiæ affectus (nisi Ratio et vigilantia adsit), et consequenter Cupiditates etiam, quæ ex iisdem generantur, excessum ; ad quod accedit, quod ex affectu id primum habeamus, quod in præsentia suave est, nec futura æquali animi affectu æstimare possumus. Vide Schol. Prop. 44 et Schol. Prop. 60 p. IV.\n\nCAPUT XXXI\n\nAt superstitio id contra videtur statuere bonum esse, quod Tristitiam, et id contra malum quod Lætitiam affert. Sed, ut jam diximus (vide Schol. Prop. 45 p. IV), nemo, nisi invidus, mea impotentia et incommodo delectatur. Nam quo majore Lætitia afficimur, eo ad majorem perfectionem transimus, et consequenter eo magis de natura divina participamus ; nec Lætitia unquam mala esse potest, quam nostræ utilitatis vera ratio moderatur. At qui contra Metu ducitur, et bonum, ut malum vitet, agit, is Ratione non ducitur.\n\nCAPUT XXXII\n\nSed humana potentia admodum limitata est, et a potentia causarum externarum infinite superatur ; atque adeo potestatem absolutam non habemus, res, quæ extra nos sunt, ad nostrum usum aptandi. Attamen ea, quæ nobis eveniunt contra id, quod nostræ utilitatis ratio postulat, æquo animo feremus, si conscii simus, nos functos nostro officio fuisse, et potentiam, quam habemus, non potuisse se eo usque extendere, ut eadem vitare possemus ; nosque partem totius Naturæ esse, cujus ordinem sequimur. Quod si clare et distincte intelligamus, pars illa nostri, quæ intelligentia definitur, hoc est pars melior nostri, in eo plane acquiescet, et in ea acquiescentia perseverare conabitur. Nam, quatenus intelligimus, nihil appetere nisi id, quod necessarium est, nec absolute, nisi in veris, acquiescere possumus ; adeoque quatenus hæc recte intelligimus, eatenus conatus melioris partis nostri cum ordine totius Naturæ convenit.\n\nFINIS QUARTÆ PARTIS","paragraphs":[{"id":"RAW_QGOH_PQ_5:P0001","text":"Illum liberum esse dixi, qui sola ducitur Ratione ; qui itaque liber nascitur, et liber manet, non nisi adæquatas ideas habet ; ac proinde mali conceptum habet nullum (per Coroll. Prop. 64 hujus), et consequenter (nam bonum et malum correlata sunt) neque boni. Q. E. D.","kind":"passage"},{"id":"RAW_QGOH_PQ_5:P0002","text":"SCHOLIUM","kind":"passage"},{"id":"RAW_QGOH_PQ_5:P0003","text":"Hujus Propositionis hypothesin falsam esse, nec posse concipi, nisi quatenus ad solam naturam humanam, seu potius ad Deum attendimus, non quatenus infinitus, sed quatenus tantummodo causa est, cur homo existat, patet ex 4 Propositione hujus Partis. Atque hoc, et alia quæ jam demonstravimus, videntur a Mose significari in illa primi hominis historia. In ea enim nulla alia Dei potentia concipitur, quam illa, qua hominem creavit, hoc est potentia, qua hominis solummodo utilitati consuluit ; atque eatenus narratur, quod Deus homini libero prohibuerit, ne de arbore cognitionis boni et mali comederet, et quod, simulac de ea comederet, statim mortem metueret potius quam vivere cuperet.","kind":"passage"},{"id":"RAW_QGOH_PQ_5:P0004","text":"Deinde, quod inventa ab homine uxore, quæ cum sua natura prorsus conveniebat, cognovit nihil posse in Natura dari, quod ipsi posset illa esse utilius ; sed quod, postquam bruta sibi similia esse credidit, statim eorum affectus imitari inceperit (vide Prop. 27 p. III), et libertatem suam amittere ; quam Patriarchæ postea recuperaverunt, ducti Spiritu Christi, hoc est Dei idea, a qua sola pendet, ut homo liber sit, et ut bonum, quod sibi cupit, reliquis hominibus cupiat, ut supra (per Prop. 37 hujus) demonstravimus.","kind":"passage"},{"id":"RAW_QGOH_PQ_5:P0005","text":"PROPOSITIO LXIX","kind":"passage"},{"id":"RAW_QGOH_PQ_5:P0006","text":"Hominis liberi virtus æque magna cernitur in declinandis quam in superandis periculis.","kind":"passage"},{"id":"RAW_QGOH_PQ_5:P0007","text":"DEMONSTRATIO","kind":"passage"},{"id":"RAW_QGOH_PQ_5:P0008","text":"Affectus coerceri nec tolli potest, nisi affectu contrario, et fortiore affectu coercendo (per Prop. 7 hujus). At cœca Audacia et Metus affectus sunt, qui æque magni possunt concipi (per Prop. 5 et 3 hujus) : Ergo æque magna animi virtus, seu fortitudo (hujus Definitionem vide in Schol. Prop. 59 p. III) requiritur ad Audaciam quam ad Metum coercendum ; hoc est (per Defin. 40 et 41 Affect.), homo liber eadem animi virtute pericula declinat, qua eadem superare tentat. Q. E. D.","kind":"passage"},{"id":"RAW_QGOH_PQ_5:P0009","text":"COROLLARIUM","kind":"passage"},{"id":"RAW_QGOH_PQ_5:P0010","text":"Homini igitur libero æque magnæ Animositati fuga in tempore, ac pugna ducitur : sive homo liber eadem Animositate, seu animi præsentia, qua certamen, fugam eligit.","kind":"passage"},{"id":"RAW_QGOH_PQ_5:P0011","text":"SCHOLIUM","kind":"passage"},{"id":"RAW_QGOH_PQ_5:P0012","text":"Quid Animositas sit, vel quid per ipsam intelligam, in Scholio Prop. 59 p. III explicui. Per periculum autem id omne intelligo, quod potest esse causa alicujus mali, nempe Tristitiæ, Odii, Discordiæ etc.","kind":"passage"},{"id":"RAW_QGOH_PQ_5:P0013","text":"PROPOSITIO LXX","kind":"passage"},{"id":"RAW_QGOH_PQ_5:P0014","text":"Homo liber, qui inter ignaros vivit, eorum, quantum potest, beneficia declinare studet.","kind":"passage"},{"id":"RAW_QGOH_PQ_5:P0015","text":"DEMONSTRATIO","kind":"passage"},{"id":"RAW_QGOH_PQ_5:P0016","text":"Unusquisque ex suo ingenio judicat, quid bonum sit (vide Schol. Prop. 39 p. III) ; ignarus igitur, qui in aliquem beneficium contulit, id ex suo ingenio æstimabit, et si minoris ab eo, cui datum est, æstimari videt, contristabitur (per Prop. 42 p. III). At homo liber reliquos homines amicitia sibi jungere (per Prop. 37 hujus), nec paria hominibus beneficia ex eorum affectu referre, sed se et reliquos libero Rationis judicio ducere, et ea tantum agere studet, quæ ipse prima esse novit : Ergo homo liber, ne ignaris odio sit, et ne eorum appetitui, sed soli Rationi obsequatur, eorum beneficia, quantum potest, declinare conabitur. Q. E. D.","kind":"passage"},{"id":"RAW_QGOH_PQ_5:P0017","text":"SCHOLIUM","kind":"passage"},{"id":"RAW_QGOH_PQ_5:P0018","text":"Dico quantum potest. Nam quamvis homines ignari sint, sunt tamen homines, qui in necessitatibus humanum auxilium, quo nullum præstabilius est, adferre queunt ; atque adeo sæpe fit, ut necesse sit ab iisdem beneficium accipere, et consequenter iisdem contra ex eorum ingenio congratulari ; ad quod accedit, quod etiam in declinandis beneficiis cautio esse debet, ne videamur eosdem contemnere, vel præ Avaritia remunerationem timere, atque ita dum eorum Odium fugimus, eo ipso in eorum offensionem incurramus. Quare in declinandis beneficiis ratio utilis et honesti habenda est.","kind":"passage"},{"id":"RAW_QGOH_PQ_5:P0019","text":"PROPOSITIO LXXI","kind":"passage"},{"id":"RAW_QGOH_PQ_5:P0020","text":"Soli homines liberi erga invicem gratissimi sunt.","kind":"passage"},{"id":"RAW_QGOH_PQ_5:P0021","text":"DEMONSTRATIO","kind":"passage"},{"id":"RAW_QGOH_PQ_5:P0022","text":"Soli homines liberi sibi invicem utilissimi sunt, et maxima amicitiæ necessitudine invicem junguntur (per Prop. 35 hujus, et 1 ejus Coroll.) parique amoris studio sibi invicem benefacere conantur (per Prop. 37 hujus) ; adeoque (per 34 Affect. Defin.) soli homines liberi erga se invicem gratissimi sunt. Q. E. D.","kind":"passage"},{"id":"RAW_QGOH_PQ_5:P0023","text":"SCHOLIUM","kind":"passage"},{"id":"RAW_QGOH_PQ_5:P0024","text":"Gratia, quam homines qui cœca Cupiditate ducuntur, invicem habent, mercatura seu aucupium potius, quam gratia plerumque est. Porro ingratitudo affectus non est. Est tamen ingratitudo turpis, quia plerumque hominem nimio Odio, Ira, vel Superbia, vel Avaritia, etc., affectum esse indicat. Nam qui præ stultitia dona compensare nescit, ingratus non est, et multo minus ille, qui donis non movetur meretricis, ut ipsius libidini inserviat, nec furis, ut ipsius furta celet, vel alterius similis. Nam hic contra animum habere constantem ostendit, qui scilicet se nullis donis ad suam vel communem perniciem patitur corrumpi.","kind":"passage"},{"id":"RAW_QGOH_PQ_5:P0025","text":"PROPOSITIO LXXII","kind":"passage"},{"id":"RAW_QGOH_PQ_5:P0026","text":"Homo liber nunquam dolo malo, sed semper cum fide agit.","kind":"passage"},{"id":"RAW_QGOH_PQ_5:P0027","text":"DEMONSTRATIO","kind":"passage"},{"id":"RAW_QGOH_PQ_5:P0028","text":"Si liber homo quicquam dolo malo, quatenus liber est, ageret, id ex dictamine Rationis ageret (nam eatenus tantum liber a nobis appellatur) : atque adeo dolo malo agere virtus esset (per Prop. 24 hujus), et consequenter (per eandem Prop.) unicuique ad suum esse conservandum consultius esset, dolo malo agere ; hoc est (ut per se notum), hominibus consultius esset, verbis solummodo convenire, re autem invicem esse contrarios, quod (per Coroll. Prop. 31 hujus) est absurdum. Ergo homo liber etc. Q. E. D.","kind":"passage"},{"id":"RAW_QGOH_PQ_5:P0029","text":"SCHOLIUM","kind":"passage"},{"id":"RAW_QGOH_PQ_5:P0030","text":"Si jam quæratur, quid si homo se perfidia a præsenti mortis periculo posset liberare, an non ratio suum esse conservandi omnino suadet, ut perfidus sit ? Respondebitur eodem modo, quod si Ratio id suadeat, suadet ergo id omnibus hominibus, atque adeo Ratio omnino suadet hominibus, ne nisi dolo malo paciscantur, vires conjungere et jura habere communia, hoc est, ne revera jura habeant communia, quod est absurdum.","kind":"passage"},{"id":"RAW_QGOH_PQ_5:P0031","text":"PROPOSITIO LXXIII","kind":"passage"},{"id":"RAW_QGOH_PQ_5:P0032","text":"Homo, qui Ratione ducitur, magis in Civitate, ubi ex communi decreto vivit, quam in solitudine, ubi sibi soli obtemperat, liber est.","kind":"passage"},{"id":"RAW_QGOH_PQ_5:P0033","text":"DEMONSTRATIO","kind":"passage"},{"id":"RAW_QGOH_PQ_5:P0034","text":"Homo, qui Ratione ducitur, non ducitur Metu ad obtemperandum (per Prop. 63 hujus) sed, quatenus suum esse ex Rationis dictamine conservare conatur, hoc est (per Schol. Prop. 66 hujus) quatenus libere vivere conatur, communis vitæ et utilitatis rationem tenere (per Prop. 37 hujus), et consequenter (ut in Schol. 2 Prop. 37 hujus ostendimus) ex communi Civitatis decreto vivere cupit. Cupit ergo homo, qui Ratione ducitur, ut liberius vivat, communia Civitatis jura tenere. Q. E. D.","kind":"passage"},{"id":"RAW_QGOH_PQ_5:P0035","text":"SCHOLIUM","kind":"passage"},{"id":"RAW_QGOH_PQ_5:P0036","text":"Hæc et similia, quæ de vera hominis libertate ostendimus, ad Fortitudinem, hoc est (per Schol. Prop. 59 p. III) ad Animositatem et Generositatem referuntur. Nec operæ pretium duco, omnes Fortitudinis proprietates hic separatim demonstrare, et multo minus, quod vir fortis neminem odio habeat, nemini irascatur, invideat, indignetur, neminem despiciat, minimeque superbiat. Nam hæc, et omnia, quæ ad veram vitam et Religionem spectant, facile ex Propositione 37 et 46 hujus Partis convincuntur ; nempe quod Odium Amore contra vincendum sit, et quod unusquisque, qui Ratione ducitur, bonum, quod sibi appetit, reliquis etiam ut sit, cupiat.","kind":"passage"},{"id":"RAW_QGOH_PQ_5:P0037","text":"Ad quod accedit id, quod in Scholio Propositionis 50 hujus Partis, et aliis in locis notavimus : quod scilicet vir fortis hoc apprime consideret, nempe quod omnia ex necessitate divinæ naturæ sequantur, ac proinde quicquid molestum et malum esse cogitat, et quicquid præterea impium, horrendum, injustum, et turpe videtur, ex eo oritur, quod res ipsas perturbate, mutilate, et confuse concipit ; et hac de causa apprime conatur res, ut in se sunt, concipere, et veræ cognitionis impedimenta amovere, ut sunt Odium, Ira, Invidia, Irrisio, Superbia, et reliqua hujusmodi, quæ in præcedentibus notavimus ; atque adeo, quantum potest, conatur ut diximus, bene agere et lætari. Quousque autem humana virtus ad hæc consequenda se extendat, et quid possit, in sequenti Parte demonstrabo.","kind":"passage"},{"id":"RAW_QGOH_PQ_5:P0038","text":"APPENDIX","kind":"passage"},{"id":"RAW_QGOH_PQ_5:P0039","text":"Quæ in hac Parte de recta vivendi Ratione tradidi, non sunt ita disposita ut uno aspectu videri possint, sed disperse a me demonstrata sunt, prout scilicet unum ex alio facilius deducere potuerim. Eadem igitur hic recolligere et ad summa capita redigere proposui.","kind":"passage"},{"id":"RAW_QGOH_PQ_5:P0040","text":"CAPUT I","kind":"passage"},{"id":"RAW_QGOH_PQ_5:P0041","text":"Omnes nostri conatus, seu Cupiditates, ex necessitate nostræ naturæ ita sequuntur, ut vel per ipsam solam, tanquam per proximam suam causam, possint intelligi, vel quatenus Naturæ sumus pars, quæ per se absque aliis individuis non potest adæquate concipi.","kind":"passage"},{"id":"RAW_QGOH_PQ_5:P0042","text":"CAPUT II","kind":"passage"},{"id":"RAW_QGOH_PQ_5:P0043","text":"Cupiditates, quæ ex nostra natura ita sequuntur, ut per ipsam solam possit intelligi, sunt illæ, quæ ad Mentem referuntur, quatenus hæc ideis adæquatis constare concipitur ; reliquæ vero Cupiditates ad Mentem non referuntur, nisi quatenus res inadæquate concipit, et earum vis et incrementum non humana, sed rerum, quæ extra nos sunt, potentia definiri debet ; et ideo illæ recte actiones, hæ autem passiones vocantur ; illæ namque nostram potentiam semper indicant, et hæ contra nostram impotentiam et mutilatam cognitionem.","kind":"passage"},{"id":"RAW_QGOH_PQ_5:P0044","text":"CAPUT III","kind":"passage"},{"id":"RAW_QGOH_PQ_5:P0045","text":"Nostræ actiones, hoc est Cupiditates illæ, quæ hominis potentia seu Ratione definiuntur, semper bonæ sunt, reliquæ autem tam bonæ quam malæ possunt esse.","kind":"passage"},{"id":"RAW_QGOH_PQ_5:P0046","text":"CAPUT IV","kind":"passage"},{"id":"RAW_QGOH_PQ_5:P0047","text":"In vita itaque apprime utile est, intellectum seu Rationem, quantum possumus, perficere, et in hoc uno summa hominis felicitas seu beatitudo consistit ; quippe beatitudo nihil aliud est, quam ipsa animi acquiescentia, quæ ex Dei intuitiva cognitione oritur : at intellectum perficere nihil etiam aliud est, quam Deum, Deique attributa et actiones, quæ ex ipsius naturæ necessitate consequuntur, intelligere. Quare hominis, qui Ratione ducitur, finis ultimus, hoc est summa Cupiditas, qua reliquas omnes moderari studet, est illa, qua fertur ad se, resque omnes, quæ sub ipsius intelligentiam cadere possunt, adæquate concipiendum.","kind":"passage"},{"id":"RAW_QGOH_PQ_5:P0048","text":"CAPUT V","kind":"passage"},{"id":"RAW_QGOH_PQ_5:P0049","text":"Nulla igitur vita rationalis est sine intelligentia ; et res eatenus tantum bonæ sunt, quatenus hominem juvant, ut Mentis vita fruatur, quæ intelligentia definitur. Quæ autem contra impediunt, quo minus homo Rationem perficere et rationali vita frui possit, eas solummodo malas esse dicimus.","kind":"passage"},{"id":"RAW_QGOH_PQ_5:P0050","text":"CAPUT VI","kind":"passage"},{"id":"RAW_QGOH_PQ_5:P0051","text":"Sed quia omnia illa, quorum homo efficiens est causa, necessario bona sunt, nihil ergo mali homini evenire potest, nisi a causis externis ; nempe quatenus pars est totius Naturæ, cujus legibus humana natura obtemperare, et cui infinitis modis pœne sese accommodare cogitur.","kind":"passage"},{"id":"RAW_QGOH_PQ_5:P0052","text":"CAPUT VII","kind":"passage"},{"id":"RAW_QGOH_PQ_5:P0053","text":"Nec fieri potest, ut homo non sit Naturæ pars, et communem ejus ordinem non sequatur ; sed si inter talia individua versetur, quæ cum ipsius hominis natura conveniunt, eo ipso hominis agendi potentia juvabitur et fovebitur. At si contra inter talia sit, quæ cum ipsius natura minime conveniunt, vix absque magna ipsius mutatione iisdem sese accommodare poterit.","kind":"passage"},{"id":"RAW_QGOH_PQ_5:P0054","text":"CAPUT VIII","kind":"passage"},{"id":"RAW_QGOH_PQ_5:P0055","text":"Quicquid in rerum Natura datur, quod judicamus malum esse, sive posse impedire, quo minus existere et vita rationali frui queamus, id a nobis removere ea via, quæ securior videtur, licet ; et quicquid contra datur, quod judicamus bonum sive utile esse ad nostrum esse conservandum, et vita rationali fruendum, id ad nostrum usum capere, et eo quocunque modo uti nobis licet ; et absolute id unicuique summo Naturæ jure facere licet, quod ad ipsius utilitatem conferre judicat.","kind":"passage"},{"id":"RAW_QGOH_PQ_5:P0056","text":"CAPUT IX","kind":"passage"},{"id":"RAW_QGOH_PQ_5:P0057","text":"Nihil magis cum natura alicujus rei convenire potest, quam reliqua ejusdem speciei individua ; adeoque (per Caput VII) nihil homini ad suum esse conservandum, et vita rationali fruendum utilius datur quam homo, qui Ratione ducitur. Deinde, quia inter res singulares nihil novimus, quod homine, qui Ratione ducitur, sit præstantius, nulla ergo re magis potest unusquisque ostendere, quantum arte et ingenio valeat, quam in hominibus ita educandis, ut tandem ex proprio Rationis imperio vivant.","kind":"passage"},{"id":"RAW_QGOH_PQ_5:P0058","text":"CAPUT X","kind":"passage"},{"id":"RAW_QGOH_PQ_5:P0059","text":"Quatenus homines Invidia aut aliquo Odii affectu in se invicem feruntur, eatenus invicem contrarii sunt, et consequenter eo magis timendi, quo plus possunt quam reliqua Naturæ individua.","kind":"passage"},{"id":"RAW_QGOH_PQ_5:P0060","text":"CAPUT XI","kind":"passage"},{"id":"RAW_QGOH_PQ_5:P0061","text":"Animi tamen non armis sed Amore et Generositate vincuntur.","kind":"passage"},{"id":"RAW_QGOH_PQ_5:P0062","text":"CAPUT XII","kind":"passage"},{"id":"RAW_QGOH_PQ_5:P0063","text":"Hominibus apprime utile est consuetudines jungere, seseque iis vinculis astringere, quibus aptius de se omnibus unum efficiant, et absolute ea agere, quæ firmandis amicitiis inserviunt.","kind":"passage"},{"id":"RAW_QGOH_PQ_5:P0064","text":"CAPUT XIII","kind":"passage"},{"id":"RAW_QGOH_PQ_5:P0065","text":"Sed ad hæc ars et vigilantia requiritur. Sunt enim homines varii (nam rari sunt, qui ex Rationis præscripto vivunt), et tamen plerumque invidi, et magis ad vindictam quam ad Misericordiam proclives. Unumquemque igitur ex ipsius ingenio ferre et sese continere, ne eorum affectus imitetur, singularis animi potentiæ opus est. At qui contra homines carpere, et vitia potius exprobrare quam virtutes docere, et hominum animos non firmare sed frangere norunt, ii et sibi et reliquis molesti sunt ; unde multi, præ nimia scilicet animi impatientia, falsoque religionis studio, inter bruta potius quam inter homines vivere maluerunt ; ut pueri vel adolescentes, qui parentum jurgia æquo animo ferre nequeunt, militatum confugiunt, et incommoda belli et imperium tyrannidis præ domesticis commodis et paternis admonitionibus eligunt, et quidvis oneris sibi imponi patiuntur, dummodo parentes ulciscantur.","kind":"passage"},{"id":"RAW_QGOH_PQ_5:P0066","text":"CAPUT XIV","kind":"passage"},{"id":"RAW_QGOH_PQ_5:P0067","text":"Quamvis igitur homines omnia plerumque ex sua libidine moderentur, ex eorum tamen communi societate multo plura commoda quam damna sequuntur. Quare satius est, eorum injurias æquo animo ferre, et studium iis adhibere, quæ concordiæ et amicitiæ conciliandæ inserviunt.","kind":"passage"},{"id":"RAW_QGOH_PQ_5:P0068","text":"CAPUT XV","kind":"passage"},{"id":"RAW_QGOH_PQ_5:P0069","text":"Quæ concordiam gignunt sunt illa, quæ ad justitiam, æquitatem, et honestatem referuntur. Nam homines præter id, quod injustum et iniquum est, etiam ægre ferunt, quod turpe habetur, sive quod aliquis receptos civitatis mores aspernatur. Amori autem conciliando illa apprime necessaria sunt, quæ ad Religionem et Pietatem spectant. De quibus vide Schol. 1 et 2 Prop. 37 et Schol. Prop. 46 et Schol. Prop. 73 p. IV.","kind":"passage"},{"id":"RAW_QGOH_PQ_5:P0070","text":"CAPUT XVI","kind":"passage"},{"id":"RAW_QGOH_PQ_5:P0071","text":"Solet præterea concordia ex Metu plerumque gigni, sed sine fide. Adde, quod Metus ex animi impotentia oritur, et propterea ad Rationis usum non pertinet ; ut nec Commiseratio, quamvis Pietatis speciem præ se ferre videatur.","kind":"passage"},{"id":"RAW_QGOH_PQ_5:P0072","text":"CAPUT XVII","kind":"passage"},{"id":"RAW_QGOH_PQ_5:P0073","text":"Vincuntur præterea homines etiam largitate, præcipue ii qui non habent, unde comparare possint illa, quæ ad vitam sustentandam necessaria sunt. Attamen unicuique indigenti auxilium ferre, vires et utilitatem viri privati longe superat. Divitiæ namque viri privati longe impares sunt ad id suppeditandum. Unius præterea viri facultas limitatior est, quam ut omnes sibi possit amicitia jungere ; quare pauperum cura integræ societati incumbit, et ad communem tantum utilitatem spectat.","kind":"passage"},{"id":"RAW_QGOH_PQ_5:P0074","text":"CAPUT XVIII","kind":"passage"},{"id":"RAW_QGOH_PQ_5:P0075","text":"In beneficiis accipiendis et gratia referenda alia prorsus debet esse cura, de qua vide Schol. Prop. 70 et Schol. Prop. 71 p. IV.","kind":"passage"},{"id":"RAW_QGOH_PQ_5:P0076","text":"CAPUT XIX","kind":"passage"},{"id":"RAW_QGOH_PQ_5:P0077","text":"Amor præterea meretricius, hoc est generandi libido, quæ ex forma oritur, et absolute omnis Amor, qui aliam causam præter animi libertatem agnoscit, facile in Odium transit ; nisi, quod pejus est, species delirii sit, atque tum magis discordia quam concordia fovetur. Vid. Schol. Prop. 31 p. III.","kind":"passage"},{"id":"RAW_QGOH_PQ_5:P0078","text":"CAPUT XX","kind":"passage"},{"id":"RAW_QGOH_PQ_5:P0079","text":"Ad matrimonium quod attinet, certum est, ipsum cum Ratione convenire, si Cupiditas miscendi corpora non ex sola forma, sed etiam ex Amore liberos procreandi et sapienter educandi ingeneretur ; et præterea si utriusque, viri scilicet et fœminæ, Amor non solam formam, sed animi præcipue libertatem pro causa habeat.","kind":"passage"},{"id":"RAW_QGOH_PQ_5:P0080","text":"CAPUT XXI","kind":"passage"},{"id":"RAW_QGOH_PQ_5:P0081","text":"Gignit præterea adulatio concordiam ; sed fœdo servitutis crimine, vel perfidia ; nulli quippe magis adulatione capiuntur quam superbi, qui primi esse volunt, nec sunt.","kind":"passage"},{"id":"RAW_QGOH_PQ_5:P0082","text":"CAPUT XXII","kind":"passage"},{"id":"RAW_QGOH_PQ_5:P0083","text":"Abjectioni falsa pietatis et religionis species inest. Et quamvis Abjectio Superbiæ sit contraria, est tamen abjectus superbo proximus. Vide Schol. Prop. 57 p. IV.","kind":"passage"},{"id":"RAW_QGOH_PQ_5:P0084","text":"CAPUT XXIII","kind":"passage"},{"id":"RAW_QGOH_PQ_5:P0085","text":"Confert præterea concordiæ Pudor in iis tantum, quæ celari non possunt. Deinde, quia ipse Pudor species est Tristitiæ, ad Rationis usum non spectat.","kind":"passage"},{"id":"RAW_QGOH_PQ_5:P0086","text":"CAPUT XXIV","kind":"passage"},{"id":"RAW_QGOH_PQ_5:P0087","text":"Cæteri Tristitiæ erga homines affectus directe justitiæ, æquitati, honestati, pietati, et religioni opponuntur ; et, quamvis Indignatio æquitatis speciem præ se ferre videatur, ibi tamen sine lege vivitur, ubi unicuique de factis alterius judicium ferre, et suum vel alterius jus vindicare licet.","kind":"passage"},{"id":"RAW_QGOH_PQ_5:P0088","text":"CAPUT XXV","kind":"passage"},{"id":"RAW_QGOH_PQ_5:P0089","text":"Modestia, hoc est Cupiditas hominibus placendi, quæ ex Ratione determinatur, ad Pietatem (ut in Schol. 1 Prop. 37 p. IV diximus) refertur. Sed si ex affectu oriatur, Ambitio est sive Cupiditas, qua homines falsa Pietatis imagine plerumque discordias et seditiones concitant. Nam qui reliquos consilio aut re juvare cupit, ut simul summo fruantur bono, is apprime studebit eorum sibi Amorem conciliare ; non autem eos in admirationem traducere, ut disciplina ex ipso habeat vocabulum, nec ullas absolute Invidiæ causas dare. In communibus deinde colloquiis cavebit hominum vitia referre, et de humana impotentia non nisi parce loqui curabit : at largiter de humana virtute seu potentia, et qua via possit perfici ; ut sic homines, non ex Metu aut aversione, sed solo Lætitiæ affectu moti ex Rationis præscripto, quantum in se est, conentur vivere.","kind":"passage"},{"id":"RAW_QGOH_PQ_5:P0090","text":"CAPUT XXVI","kind":"passage"},{"id":"RAW_QGOH_PQ_5:P0091","text":"Præter homines nihil singulare in Natura novimus, cujus Mente gaudere, et quod nobis amicitia aut aliquo consuetudinis genere jungere possumus ; adeoque quicquid in rerum Natura extra homines datur, id nostræ utilitatis ratio conservare, destruere, vel quocunque modo ad nostrum usum adaptare nos docet.","kind":"passage"},{"id":"RAW_QGOH_PQ_5:P0092","text":"CAPUT XXVII","kind":"passage"},{"id":"RAW_QGOH_PQ_5:P0093","text":"Utilitas, quam ex rebus, quæ extra nos sunt, capimus, est, præter experientiam et cognitionem, quam acquirimus ex eo, quod easdem observamus, et ex his formis in alias mutamus, præcipua corporis conservatio ; et hac Ratione res illæ imprimis utiles sunt, quæ Corpus ita alere et nutrire possunt, ut ejus omnes partes officio suo recte fungi queant. Nam quo Corpus aptius est, ut pluribus modis possit affici, et corpora externa pluribus modis afficere, eo Mens ad cogitandum est aptior (vide Prop. 38 et 39 p. IV). At hujus notæ perpauca in Natura esse videntur ; quare ad Corpus, ut requiritur, nutriendum, necesse est, multis naturæ diversæ alimentis uti.","kind":"passage"},{"id":"RAW_QGOH_PQ_5:P0094","text":"Quippe humanum Corpus ex plurimis diversæ naturæ partibus componitur, quæ continuo alimento indigent, et vario, ut totum Corpus ad omnia, quæ ex ipsius natura sequi possunt, æque aptum sit, et consequenter ut Mens etiam æque apta sit ad plura concipiendum.","kind":"passage"},{"id":"RAW_QGOH_PQ_5:P0095","text":"CAPUT XXVIII","kind":"passage"},{"id":"RAW_QGOH_PQ_5:P0096","text":"Ad hæc autem comparandum vix uniuscujusque vires sufficerent, nisi homines operas mutuas traderent. Verum omnium rerum compendium pecunia attulit, unde factum, ut ejus imago Mentem vulgi maxime occupare soleat ; quia vix ullam Lætitiæ speciem imaginari possunt, nisi concomitante nummorum idea tanquam causa.","kind":"passage"},{"id":"RAW_QGOH_PQ_5:P0097","text":"CAPUT XXIX","kind":"passage"},{"id":"RAW_QGOH_PQ_5:P0098","text":"Sed hoc vitium eorum tantum est, qui non ex indigentia, nec propter necessitates nummos quærunt, sed quia lucri artes didicerunt, quibus se magnifice efferunt. Cæterum Corpus ex consuetudine pascunt, sed parce, quia tantum de suis bonis se perdere credunt, quantum sui Corporis conservationi impendunt. At qui verum nummorum usum norunt, et divitiarum modum ex sola indigentia moderantur, paucis contenti vivunt.","kind":"passage"},{"id":"RAW_QGOH_PQ_5:P0099","text":"CAPUT XXX","kind":"passage"},{"id":"RAW_QGOH_PQ_5:P0100","text":"Cum igitur res illæ sint bonæ, quæ Corporis partes juvant, ut suo officio fungantur, et Lætitia in eo consistat, quod hominis potentia, quatenus Mente et Corpore constat, juvatur vel augetur ; sunt ergo illa omnia, quæ Lætitiam afferunt, bona. Attamen, quoniam contra non eum in finem res agunt, ut nos Lætitia afficiant, nec earum agendi potentia ex nostra utilitate temperatur, et denique quoniam Lætitia plerumque ad unam Corporis partem potissimum refertur ; habent ergo plerumque Lætitiæ affectus (nisi Ratio et vigilantia adsit), et consequenter Cupiditates etiam, quæ ex iisdem generantur, excessum ; ad quod accedit, quod ex affectu id primum habeamus, quod in præsentia suave est, nec futura æquali animi affectu æstimare possumus. Vide Schol. Prop. 44 et Schol. Prop. 60 p. IV.","kind":"passage"},{"id":"RAW_QGOH_PQ_5:P0101","text":"CAPUT XXXI","kind":"passage"},{"id":"RAW_QGOH_PQ_5:P0102","text":"At superstitio id contra videtur statuere bonum esse, quod Tristitiam, et id contra malum quod Lætitiam affert. Sed, ut jam diximus (vide Schol. Prop. 45 p. IV), nemo, nisi invidus, mea impotentia et incommodo delectatur. Nam quo majore Lætitia afficimur, eo ad majorem perfectionem transimus, et consequenter eo magis de natura divina participamus ; nec Lætitia unquam mala esse potest, quam nostræ utilitatis vera ratio moderatur. At qui contra Metu ducitur, et bonum, ut malum vitet, agit, is Ratione non ducitur.","kind":"passage"},{"id":"RAW_QGOH_PQ_5:P0103","text":"CAPUT XXXII","kind":"passage"},{"id":"RAW_QGOH_PQ_5:P0104","text":"Sed humana potentia admodum limitata est, et a potentia causarum externarum infinite superatur ; atque adeo potestatem absolutam non habemus, res, quæ extra nos sunt, ad nostrum usum aptandi. Attamen ea, quæ nobis eveniunt contra id, quod nostræ utilitatis ratio postulat, æquo animo feremus, si conscii simus, nos functos nostro officio fuisse, et potentiam, quam habemus, non potuisse se eo usque extendere, ut eadem vitare possemus ; nosque partem totius Naturæ esse, cujus ordinem sequimur. Quod si clare et distincte intelligamus, pars illa nostri, quæ intelligentia definitur, hoc est pars melior nostri, in eo plane acquiescet, et in ea acquiescentia perseverare conabitur.","kind":"passage"},{"id":"RAW_QGOH_PQ_5:P0105","text":"Nam, quatenus intelligimus, nihil appetere nisi id, quod necessarium est, nec absolute, nisi in veris, acquiescere possumus ; adeoque quatenus hæc recte intelligimus, eatenus conatus melioris partis nostri cum ordine totius Naturæ convenit.","kind":"passage"},{"id":"RAW_QGOH_PQ_5:P0106","text":"FINIS QUARTÆ PARTIS","kind":"passage"}],"sha256":"c75aa93dbfb7e0f84b939497c983597b75dfb6659a421848ff785c855874a1d8","language_code":"la","language_name":"Latina","translations":[{"id":"SOURCE_TR_20261015T014157508792Z_25f7f6bf","language_code":"en","language_name":"English","title":"Part Four - On Human Bondage, or the Powers of the Affects · passage 5","source_sha256":"c75aa93dbfb7e0f84b939497c983597b75dfb6659a421848ff785c855874a1d8","sha256":"3e4aded1f334b8bcdc451be9842f5d10ce525da536efd21be26c6aa472f8e00c","rendition_type":"machine","review_status":"machine","model":"gpt-5.6-terra","created":"2026-10-15T01:41:57.508824+00:00","paragraphs":[{"id":"RAW_QGOH_PQ_5:P0001","text":"I said that he is free who is led by Reason alone; who therefore is born free and remains free, has none but adequate ideas; and consequently has no concept of evil (by the Corollary of Proposition 64 of this Part), and consequently (for good and evil are correlates) neither of good. Q. E. D.","kind":"passage","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_QGOH_PQ_5:P0001"]},{"id":"RAW_QGOH_PQ_5:P0002","text":"SCHOLIUM","kind":"passage","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_QGOH_PQ_5:P0002"]},{"id":"RAW_QGOH_PQ_5:P0003","text":"That the hypothesis of this Proposition is false, and cannot be conceived except insofar as we attend to human nature alone, or rather to God, not insofar as he is infinite, but insofar only as he is the cause why man exists, is evident from Proposition 4 of this Part. And this, together with the other things which we have now demonstrated, seems to be signified by Moses in that history of the first man. For in it no other power of God is conceived than that by which he created man, that is, the power by which he provided only for man's advantage; and accordingly it is narrated that God forbade free man to eat of the tree of the knowledge of good and evil, and that, as soon as he ate of it, he would immediately fear death rather than desire to live.","kind":"passage","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_QGOH_PQ_5:P0003"]},{"id":"RAW_QGOH_PQ_5:P0004","text":"Then, that when he had found a wife who agreed altogether with his nature, he knew that nothing could be given in Nature which could be more useful to him than she; but that, after he believed the brutes to be like himself, he immediately began to imitate their affects (see Proposition 27, Part III), and to lose his freedom; which the Patriarchs afterward recovered, led by the Spirit of Christ, that is, by the idea of God, upon which alone it depends that man be free, and that he desire for the other men the good which he desires for himself, as we demonstrated above (by Proposition 37 of this Part).","kind":"passage","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_QGOH_PQ_5:P0004"]},{"id":"RAW_QGOH_PQ_5:P0005","text":"PROPOSITION LXIX","kind":"passage","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_QGOH_PQ_5:P0005"]},{"id":"RAW_QGOH_PQ_5:P0006","text":"The virtue of a free man is seen to be equally great in avoiding as in overcoming dangers.","kind":"passage","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_QGOH_PQ_5:P0006"]},{"id":"RAW_QGOH_PQ_5:P0007","text":"DEMONSTRATION","kind":"passage","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_QGOH_PQ_5:P0007"]},{"id":"RAW_QGOH_PQ_5:P0008","text":"An affect can neither be restrained nor removed except by a contrary affect, and by one stronger than the affect to be restrained (by Proposition 7 of this Part). But blind Daring and Fear are affects which can be conceived as equally great (by Propositions 5 and 3 of this Part): Therefore equally great strength of mind, or fortitude (for its definition see the Scholium to Proposition 59, Part III), is required to restrain Daring as to restrain Fear; that is (by Definitions 40 and 41 of the Affects), a free man avoids dangers by the same virtue of mind by which he attempts to overcome them. Q. E. D.","kind":"passage","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_QGOH_PQ_5:P0008"]},{"id":"RAW_QGOH_PQ_5:P0009","text":"COROLLARY","kind":"passage","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_QGOH_PQ_5:P0009"]},{"id":"RAW_QGOH_PQ_5:P0010","text":"Therefore, to a free man, timely flight is ascribed to as great a strength of mind as fighting; or, a free man chooses flight with the same strength of mind, or presence of mind, with which he chooses combat.","kind":"passage","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_QGOH_PQ_5:P0010"]},{"id":"RAW_QGOH_PQ_5:P0011","text":"SCHOLIUM","kind":"passage","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_QGOH_PQ_5:P0011"]},{"id":"RAW_QGOH_PQ_5:P0012","text":"What strength of mind is, or what I understand by it, I explained in the Scholium to Proposition 59, Part III. But by danger I understand everything which can be the cause of some evil, namely Sadness, Hatred, Discord, etc.","kind":"passage","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_QGOH_PQ_5:P0012"]},{"id":"RAW_QGOH_PQ_5:P0013","text":"PROPOSITION LXX","kind":"passage","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_QGOH_PQ_5:P0013"]},{"id":"RAW_QGOH_PQ_5:P0014","text":"A free man, who lives among the ignorant, endeavors, as far as he can, to avoid their benefits.","kind":"passage","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_QGOH_PQ_5:P0014"]},{"id":"RAW_QGOH_PQ_5:P0015","text":"DEMONSTRATION","kind":"passage","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_QGOH_PQ_5:P0015"]},{"id":"RAW_QGOH_PQ_5:P0016","text":"Everyone judges from his own disposition what is good (see the Scholium to Proposition 39, Part III); therefore an ignorant man who has conferred a benefit on someone will appraise it according to his own disposition, and if he sees it valued less highly by him to whom it was given, he will be saddened (by Proposition 42, Part III). But a free man endeavors to join other men to himself in friendship (by Proposition 37 of this Part), and not to return equal benefits to men from their affect, but to guide himself and the others by the free judgment of Reason, and to do only those things which he himself knows to be primary: Therefore, lest he be hateful to the ignorant, and lest he comply with their appetite rather than with Reason alone, a free man will endeavor, as far as he can, to avoid their benefits. Q. E. D.","kind":"passage","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_QGOH_PQ_5:P0016"]},{"id":"RAW_QGOH_PQ_5:P0017","text":"SCHOLIUM","kind":"passage","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_QGOH_PQ_5:P0017"]},{"id":"RAW_QGOH_PQ_5:P0018","text":"I say, as far as he can. For although men are ignorant, they are nevertheless men who, in necessities, can bring human aid, than which none is more excellent; and so it often happens that it is necessary to accept a benefit from them, and consequently in return to congratulate them according to their disposition. To this must be added that caution is necessary even in avoiding benefits, lest we seem to despise them, or to fear recompense through Avarice, and thus, while we flee their Hatred, incur their offense precisely thereby. Therefore, in avoiding benefits, account must be taken of what is useful and honorable.","kind":"passage","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_QGOH_PQ_5:P0018"]},{"id":"RAW_QGOH_PQ_5:P0019","text":"PROPOSITION LXXI","kind":"passage","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_QGOH_PQ_5:P0019"]},{"id":"RAW_QGOH_PQ_5:P0020","text":"Only free men are most grateful toward one another.","kind":"passage","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_QGOH_PQ_5:P0020"]},{"id":"RAW_QGOH_PQ_5:P0021","text":"DEMONSTRATION","kind":"passage","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_QGOH_PQ_5:P0021"]},{"id":"RAW_QGOH_PQ_5:P0022","text":"Only free men are most useful to one another, and are joined to one another by the greatest bond of friendship (by Proposition 35 of this Part and its first Corollary), and endeavor with equal zeal of love to do good to one another (by Proposition 37 of this Part); and therefore (by Definition 34 of the Affects), only free men are most grateful toward one another. Q. E. D.","kind":"passage","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_QGOH_PQ_5:P0022"]},{"id":"RAW_QGOH_PQ_5:P0023","text":"SCHOLIUM","kind":"passage","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_QGOH_PQ_5:P0023"]},{"id":"RAW_QGOH_PQ_5:P0024","text":"The gratitude which men led by blind Desire have for one another is for the most part rather commerce or bird-catching than gratitude. Further, ingratitude is not an affect. Yet ingratitude is shameful, because it for the most part indicates that a man is affected by excessive Hatred, Anger, or Pride, or Avarice, etc. For one who, through stupidity, does not know how to repay gifts is not ungrateful; still less is one who is not moved by the gifts of a prostitute to serve her lust, nor by those of a thief to conceal his thefts, or of another like person. For such a one shows, on the contrary, that he has a steadfast mind, namely, one who suffers himself to be corrupted by no gifts to his own or the common ruin.","kind":"passage","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_QGOH_PQ_5:P0024"]},{"id":"RAW_QGOH_PQ_5:P0025","text":"PROPOSITION LXXII","kind":"passage","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_QGOH_PQ_5:P0025"]},{"id":"RAW_QGOH_PQ_5:P0026","text":"A free man never acts with malicious deceit, but always in good faith.","kind":"passage","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_QGOH_PQ_5:P0026"]},{"id":"RAW_QGOH_PQ_5:P0027","text":"DEMONSTRATION","kind":"passage","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_QGOH_PQ_5:P0027"]},{"id":"RAW_QGOH_PQ_5:P0028","text":"If a free man were to act in any way with malicious deceit, insofar as he is free, he would act so from the dictate of Reason (for only to that extent do we call him free): and therefore to act with malicious deceit would be a virtue (by Prop. 24 of this Part), and consequently (by the same Prop.) it would be more advantageous for everyone, for the preservation of his own being, to act with malicious deceit; that is (as is self-evident), it would be more advantageous for men to agree only in words, while in fact being mutually opposed, which (by Coroll. to Prop. 31 of this Part) is absurd. Therefore a free man, etc. Q. E. D.","kind":"passage","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_QGOH_PQ_5:P0028"]},{"id":"RAW_QGOH_PQ_5:P0029","text":"SCHOLIUM","kind":"passage","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_QGOH_PQ_5:P0029"]},{"id":"RAW_QGOH_PQ_5:P0030","text":"If it is now asked what if a man could free himself from a present danger of death by perfidy: does not reason, in preserving his being, wholly advise him to be perfidious? The answer will be the same: if Reason advises this, then it advises it to all men, and therefore Reason altogether advises men not to make agreements, unite their forces, and possess common rights except with malicious deceit; that is, that they should not in reality possess common rights, which is absurd.","kind":"passage","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_QGOH_PQ_5:P0030"]},{"id":"RAW_QGOH_PQ_5:P0031","text":"PROPOSITION LXXIII","kind":"passage","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_QGOH_PQ_5:P0031"]},{"id":"RAW_QGOH_PQ_5:P0032","text":"A man who is guided by Reason is freer in a State, where he lives according to a common decree, than in solitude, where he obeys himself alone.","kind":"passage","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_QGOH_PQ_5:P0032"]},{"id":"RAW_QGOH_PQ_5:P0033","text":"DEMONSTRATION","kind":"passage","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_QGOH_PQ_5:P0033"]},{"id":"RAW_QGOH_PQ_5:P0034","text":"A man who is guided by Reason is not led by Fear to obey (by Prop. 63 of this Part); but insofar as he strives to preserve his being according to the dictate of Reason, that is (by the Schol. to Prop. 66 of this Part), insofar as he strives to live freely, he strives to observe the principle of common life and utility (by Prop. 37 of this Part), and consequently (as we have shown in Schol. 2 to Prop. 37 of this Part) he desires to live according to the common decree of the State. Therefore, in order to live more freely, a man who is guided by Reason desires to observe the common rights of the State. Q. E. D.","kind":"passage","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_QGOH_PQ_5:P0034"]},{"id":"RAW_QGOH_PQ_5:P0035","text":"SCHOLIUM","kind":"passage","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_QGOH_PQ_5:P0035"]},{"id":"RAW_QGOH_PQ_5:P0036","text":"These things, and others like them, which we have demonstrated concerning the true freedom of man, are referred to Fortitude, that is (by the Schol. to Prop. 59, Part III), to Courage and Generosity. Nor do I think it worthwhile to demonstrate here separately all the properties of Fortitude, and much less that a courageous man hates no one, is angry with no one, envies no one, is indignant with no one, despises no one, and is least of all proud. For these things, and all things which pertain to true life and Religion, are readily established from Propositions 37 and 46 of this Part; namely, that Hatred is to be overcome by Love, and that everyone who is guided by Reason desires that the good which he seeks for himself should also be for others.","kind":"passage","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_QGOH_PQ_5:P0036"]},{"id":"RAW_QGOH_PQ_5:P0037","text":"To this must be added what we have noted in the Scholium to Proposition 50 of this Part, and elsewhere: namely, that a courageous man considers especially this, that all things follow from the necessity of the divine nature, and consequently that whatever he judges to be troublesome and evil, and whatever besides appears impious, horrible, unjust, and base, arises from the fact that he conceives things themselves in a disturbed, mutilated, and confused manner; and for this reason he especially strives to conceive things as they are in themselves, and to remove the impediments to true knowledge, such as Hatred, Anger, Envy, Mockery, Pride, and the other things of this kind which we have noted above; and consequently, as far as he can, he strives, as we have said, to act well and to rejoice. But how far human virtue extends toward attaining these things, and what it can accomplish, I shall demonstrate in the following Part.","kind":"passage","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_QGOH_PQ_5:P0037"]},{"id":"RAW_QGOH_PQ_5:P0038","text":"APPENDIX","kind":"passage","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_QGOH_PQ_5:P0038"]},{"id":"RAW_QGOH_PQ_5:P0039","text":"The things which I have set forth in this Part concerning the right way of living are not so arranged that they can be seen at a single glance, but have been demonstrated by me in a scattered manner, according as I could more easily deduce one thing from another. I have therefore resolved here to collect the same things and reduce them to their principal heads.","kind":"passage","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_QGOH_PQ_5:P0039"]},{"id":"RAW_QGOH_PQ_5:P0040","text":"CHAPTER I","kind":"passage","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_QGOH_PQ_5:P0040"]},{"id":"RAW_QGOH_PQ_5:P0041","text":"All our strivings, or Desires, follow from the necessity of our nature in such a way that they can be understood either through that nature alone, as through their proximate cause, or insofar as we are a part of Nature which cannot be adequately conceived through itself without other individuals.","kind":"passage","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_QGOH_PQ_5:P0041"]},{"id":"RAW_QGOH_PQ_5:P0042","text":"CHAPTER II","kind":"passage","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_QGOH_PQ_5:P0042"]},{"id":"RAW_QGOH_PQ_5:P0043","text":"The Desires which follow from our nature in such a way that it can be understood through itself alone are those which are referred to the Mind insofar as it is conceived to consist of adequate ideas; but the remaining Desires are not referred to the Mind except insofar as it conceives things inadequately, and their force and increase must be defined not by human power but by the power of things which are outside us; and therefore the former are rightly called actions, but the latter passions; for the former always indicate our power, whereas the latter, on the contrary, indicate our impotence and mutilated knowledge.","kind":"passage","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_QGOH_PQ_5:P0043"]},{"id":"RAW_QGOH_PQ_5:P0044","text":"CHAPTER III","kind":"passage","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_QGOH_PQ_5:P0044"]},{"id":"RAW_QGOH_PQ_5:P0045","text":"Our actions, that is, those Desires which are defined by the power or Reason of man, are always good; but the rest can be as much good as evil.","kind":"passage","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_QGOH_PQ_5:P0045"]},{"id":"RAW_QGOH_PQ_5:P0046","text":"CHAPTER IV","kind":"passage","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_QGOH_PQ_5:P0046"]},{"id":"RAW_QGOH_PQ_5:P0047","text":"Therefore, in life it is above all useful to perfect the intellect or Reason as much as we can, and in this alone consists the highest happiness or blessedness of man; indeed, blessedness is nothing other than the very contentment of mind which arises from the intuitive knowledge of God: while to perfect the intellect is likewise nothing other than to understand God, and the attributes and actions of God which follow from the necessity of his nature. Wherefore the ultimate end of the man who is guided by Reason, that is, the highest Desire by which he strives to moderate all the others, is that by which he is borne toward conceiving adequately himself and all things that can fall under his intelligence.","kind":"passage","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_QGOH_PQ_5:P0047"]},{"id":"RAW_QGOH_PQ_5:P0048","text":"CHAPTER V","kind":"passage","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_QGOH_PQ_5:P0048"]},{"id":"RAW_QGOH_PQ_5:P0049","text":"Therefore no rational life exists without understanding; and things are good only insofar as they help a human being to enjoy the life of the Mind, which is defined by understanding. But those things which, on the contrary, prevent a human being from perfecting Reason and enjoying a rational life, we say are evil only.","kind":"passage","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_QGOH_PQ_5:P0049"]},{"id":"RAW_QGOH_PQ_5:P0050","text":"CHAPTER VI","kind":"passage","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_QGOH_PQ_5:P0050"]},{"id":"RAW_QGOH_PQ_5:P0051","text":"But since all those things of which a human being is the efficient cause are necessarily good, therefore no evil can happen to a human being except from external causes; namely, insofar as he is a part of the whole of Nature, whose laws human nature is bound to obey, and to which it is compelled to accommodate itself in almost infinitely many ways.","kind":"passage","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_QGOH_PQ_5:P0051"]},{"id":"RAW_QGOH_PQ_5:P0052","text":"CHAPTER VII","kind":"passage","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_QGOH_PQ_5:P0052"]},{"id":"RAW_QGOH_PQ_5:P0053","text":"Nor can it happen that a human being is not a part of Nature and does not follow its common order; but if he lives among such individuals as agree with the nature of the human being himself, by that very fact the human being's power of acting will be aided and fostered. But if, on the contrary, he is among such things as agree least with his own nature, he will scarcely be able to accommodate himself to them without a great change in himself.","kind":"passage","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_QGOH_PQ_5:P0053"]},{"id":"RAW_QGOH_PQ_5:P0054","text":"CHAPTER VIII","kind":"passage","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_QGOH_PQ_5:P0054"]},{"id":"RAW_QGOH_PQ_5:P0055","text":"Whatever is given in the Nature of things that we judge to be evil, or able to prevent us from existing and enjoying a rational life, we are permitted to remove from ourselves by the way that seems safer; and whatever, on the contrary, is given that we judge to be good or useful for preserving our being and enjoying a rational life, we are permitted to take for our use and to use in whatever way; and absolutely everyone is permitted, by the highest right of Nature, to do that which he judges to contribute to his own advantage.","kind":"passage","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_QGOH_PQ_5:P0055"]},{"id":"RAW_QGOH_PQ_5:P0056","text":"CHAPTER IX","kind":"passage","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_QGOH_PQ_5:P0056"]},{"id":"RAW_QGOH_PQ_5:P0057","text":"Nothing can agree more with the nature of any thing than the other individuals of the same species; and therefore (by Chapter VII) nothing more useful is given to a human being, for preserving his being and enjoying a rational life, than a human being who is guided by Reason. Further, since among singular things we know nothing more excellent than a human being guided by Reason, no one can therefore show more fully how much he can accomplish by skill and ingenuity than by so educating human beings that they may at last live according to Reason's own command.","kind":"passage","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_QGOH_PQ_5:P0057"]},{"id":"RAW_QGOH_PQ_5:P0058","text":"CHAPTER X","kind":"passage","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_QGOH_PQ_5:P0058"]},{"id":"RAW_QGOH_PQ_5:P0059","text":"Insofar as human beings are borne against one another by Envy or by some affect of Hate, they are contrary to one another, and consequently are to be feared all the more because they can do more than the other individuals of Nature.","kind":"passage","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_QGOH_PQ_5:P0059"]},{"id":"RAW_QGOH_PQ_5:P0060","text":"CHAPTER XI","kind":"passage","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_QGOH_PQ_5:P0060"]},{"id":"RAW_QGOH_PQ_5:P0061","text":"Minds, however, are conquered not by arms but by Love and Generosity.","kind":"passage","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_QGOH_PQ_5:P0061"]},{"id":"RAW_QGOH_PQ_5:P0062","text":"CHAPTER XII","kind":"passage","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_QGOH_PQ_5:P0062"]},{"id":"RAW_QGOH_PQ_5:P0063","text":"It is especially useful for human beings to join their practices together and to bind themselves by such bonds as may more fittingly make them all into one, and absolutely to do those things which serve to strengthen friendships.","kind":"passage","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_QGOH_PQ_5:P0063"]},{"id":"RAW_QGOH_PQ_5:P0064","text":"CHAPTER XIII","kind":"passage","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_QGOH_PQ_5:P0064"]},{"id":"RAW_QGOH_PQ_5:P0065","text":"But for these things skill and vigilance are required. For human beings are various (since those who live according to the prescription of Reason are rare), and yet are for the most part envious and more inclined to vengeance than to Compassion. Therefore, to bear with each one according to his own disposition, and to restrain oneself from imitating their affects, is the work of singular strength of mind. But those, on the contrary, who know how to censure human beings and to reproach vices rather than teach virtues, and not to strengthen but to break human beings' minds, are troublesome both to themselves and to others. Hence many, namely through excessive impatience of mind and false zeal for religion, have preferred to live among beasts rather than among human beings; just as boys or youths who cannot bear their parents' scoldings with equanimity flee to military service, and choose the hardships of war and the rule of tyranny in preference to domestic advantages and paternal admonitions, and suffer any burden to be imposed upon them, provided only that they may avenge themselves upon their parents.","kind":"passage","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_QGOH_PQ_5:P0065"]},{"id":"RAW_QGOH_PQ_5:P0066","text":"CHAPTER XIV","kind":"passage","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_QGOH_PQ_5:P0066"]},{"id":"RAW_QGOH_PQ_5:P0067","text":"Although, therefore, human beings for the most part govern all things according to their own desire, nevertheless far more advantages than disadvantages follow from their common society. Therefore it is preferable to bear their injuries with equanimity and to apply one's effort to those things which serve to establish concord and friendship.","kind":"passage","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_QGOH_PQ_5:P0067"]},{"id":"RAW_QGOH_PQ_5:P0068","text":"CHAPTER XV","kind":"passage","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_QGOH_PQ_5:P0068"]},{"id":"RAW_QGOH_PQ_5:P0069","text":"Those things which produce concord are those which pertain to justice, equity, and honesty. For human beings, besides what is unjust and unfair, also take ill what is held to be shameful, or when someone rejects the accepted customs of the state. But for conciliating Love, those things are especially necessary which concern Religion and Piety. On these, see Schol. 1 and 2 of Prop. 37, and Schol. of Prop. 46, and Schol. of Prop. 73, pt. IV.","kind":"passage","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_QGOH_PQ_5:P0069"]},{"id":"RAW_QGOH_PQ_5:P0070","text":"CHAPTER XVI","kind":"passage","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_QGOH_PQ_5:P0070"]},{"id":"RAW_QGOH_PQ_5:P0071","text":"Moreover, concord is usually for the most part produced from Fear, but without trust. Add that Fear arises from impotence of mind, and therefore does not pertain to the use of Reason; nor does Compassion, although it seems to present the appearance of Piety.","kind":"passage","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_QGOH_PQ_5:P0071"]},{"id":"RAW_QGOH_PQ_5:P0072","text":"CHAPTER XVII","kind":"passage","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_QGOH_PQ_5:P0072"]},{"id":"RAW_QGOH_PQ_5:P0073","text":"Moreover, men are also won over by generosity, especially those who do not have the means to procure the things necessary for sustaining life. Nevertheless, to bring aid to every needy person far surpasses the powers and advantage of a private individual. For the wealth of a private individual is far too unequal to supplying this. Moreover, the means of one man are too limited for him to be able to unite all to himself by friendship; therefore the care of the poor rests upon society as a whole, and concerns only the common advantage.","kind":"passage","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_QGOH_PQ_5:P0073"]},{"id":"RAW_QGOH_PQ_5:P0074","text":"CHAPTER XVIII","kind":"passage","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_QGOH_PQ_5:P0074"]},{"id":"RAW_QGOH_PQ_5:P0075","text":"In receiving benefits and returning gratitude there ought to be an altogether different concern, concerning which see Schol. Prop. 70 and Schol. Prop. 71, p. IV.","kind":"passage","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_QGOH_PQ_5:P0075"]},{"id":"RAW_QGOH_PQ_5:P0076","text":"CHAPTER XIX","kind":"passage","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_QGOH_PQ_5:P0076"]},{"id":"RAW_QGOH_PQ_5:P0077","text":"Moreover, meretricious love, that is, the lust of begetting which arises from beauty, and absolutely every Love which acknowledges a cause other than freedom of mind, easily passes into Hatred; unless, what is worse, it be a kind of madness, and then discord rather than concord is fostered. See Schol. Prop. 31, p. III.","kind":"passage","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_QGOH_PQ_5:P0077"]},{"id":"RAW_QGOH_PQ_5:P0078","text":"CHAPTER XX","kind":"passage","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_QGOH_PQ_5:P0078"]},{"id":"RAW_QGOH_PQ_5:P0079","text":"As regards marriage, it is certain that it agrees with Reason if the desire to unite bodies is generated not from beauty alone, but also from Love of begetting and wisely educating children; and moreover, if the Love of each, namely of the man and of the woman, has for its cause not beauty alone, but especially freedom of mind.","kind":"passage","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_QGOH_PQ_5:P0079"]},{"id":"RAW_QGOH_PQ_5:P0080","text":"CHAPTER XXI","kind":"passage","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_QGOH_PQ_5:P0080"]},{"id":"RAW_QGOH_PQ_5:P0081","text":"Moreover, flattery produces concord, but by the foul crime of servitude or treachery; for no one is more readily caught by flattery than the proud, who wish to be first, and are not.","kind":"passage","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_QGOH_PQ_5:P0081"]},{"id":"RAW_QGOH_PQ_5:P0082","text":"CHAPTER XXII","kind":"passage","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_QGOH_PQ_5:P0082"]},{"id":"RAW_QGOH_PQ_5:P0083","text":"A false appearance of piety and religion is inherent in Abjectness. And although Abjectness is contrary to Pride, the abject person is nevertheless close to the proud person. See Schol. Prop. 57, p. IV.","kind":"passage","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_QGOH_PQ_5:P0083"]},{"id":"RAW_QGOH_PQ_5:P0084","text":"CHAPTER XXIII","kind":"passage","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_QGOH_PQ_5:P0084"]},{"id":"RAW_QGOH_PQ_5:P0085","text":"Moreover, Shame contributes to concord only in matters that cannot be concealed. Then, because Shame itself is a species of Sadness, it does not pertain to the use of Reason.","kind":"passage","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_QGOH_PQ_5:P0085"]},{"id":"RAW_QGOH_PQ_5:P0086","text":"CHAPTER XXIV","kind":"passage","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_QGOH_PQ_5:P0086"]},{"id":"RAW_QGOH_PQ_5:P0087","text":"The other affects of Sadness toward men are directly opposed to justice, equity, uprightness, piety, and religion; and although Indignation seems to bear before itself an appearance of equity, nevertheless there one lives without law where each is permitted to pass judgment upon the deeds of another, and to vindicate his own right or another's.","kind":"passage","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_QGOH_PQ_5:P0087"]},{"id":"RAW_QGOH_PQ_5:P0088","text":"CHAPTER XXV","kind":"passage","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_QGOH_PQ_5:P0088"]},{"id":"RAW_QGOH_PQ_5:P0089","text":"Modesty, that is, the Desire to please men which is determined by Reason, is referred to Piety (as we said in Schol. 1, Prop. 37, p. IV). But if it arises from an affect, it is Ambition, or the Desire by which men for the most part stir up discords and seditions under a false image of Piety. For he who desires to assist the rest by counsel or deed, so that they may together enjoy the highest good, will strive especially to win their Love for himself; not, however, to lead them into admiration, so that a doctrine may take its name from him, nor absolutely to furnish any causes of Envy. Then, in ordinary conversation, he will beware of recounting men's vices, and will take care to speak only sparingly of human impotence; but he will speak abundantly of human virtue or power, and by what way it can be perfected; so that men, moved not by Fear or aversion, but by the affect of Joy alone, may, insofar as lies in them, endeavor to live according to the precept of Reason.","kind":"passage","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_QGOH_PQ_5:P0089"]},{"id":"RAW_QGOH_PQ_5:P0090","text":"CHAPTER XXVI","kind":"passage","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_QGOH_PQ_5:P0090"]},{"id":"RAW_QGOH_PQ_5:P0091","text":"Besides men, we know of no singular thing in Nature whose Mind we may enjoy, and which we can join to ourselves by friendship or by some kind of association; and therefore the principle of our advantage teaches us to preserve, destroy, or adapt in whatever way to our use whatever is given in the nature of things outside men.","kind":"passage","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_QGOH_PQ_5:P0091"]},{"id":"RAW_QGOH_PQ_5:P0092","text":"CHAPTER XXVII","kind":"passage","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_QGOH_PQ_5:P0092"]},{"id":"RAW_QGOH_PQ_5:P0093","text":"The advantage which we derive from things outside us is, besides the experience and knowledge that we acquire by observing them and changing them from one form into another, principally the preservation of the body; and by this reasoning those things are especially useful which can so feed and nourish the Body that all its parts may rightly perform their function. For the more apt the Body is to be affected in many ways and to affect external bodies in many ways, the more apt the Mind is for thinking (see Props. 38 and 39, p. IV). But very few things in Nature seem to be of this kind; therefore, to nourish the Body as is required, it is necessary to use many foods of diverse nature.","kind":"passage","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_QGOH_PQ_5:P0093"]},{"id":"RAW_QGOH_PQ_5:P0094","text":"For the human Body is composed of very many parts of diverse nature, which continually require varied nourishment, so that the whole Body may be equally apt for all things that can follow from its nature, and consequently so that the Mind too may be equally apt for conceiving many things.","kind":"passage","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_QGOH_PQ_5:P0094"]},{"id":"RAW_QGOH_PQ_5:P0095","text":"CHAPTER XXVIII","kind":"passage","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_QGOH_PQ_5:P0095"]},{"id":"RAW_QGOH_PQ_5:P0096","text":"But for procuring these things, the powers of each individual would scarcely suffice, unless men exchanged services with one another. Yet money has provided a compendium of all things, whence it has come about that its image is wont especially to occupy the mind of the common people; because they can scarcely imagine any kind of Joy unless accompanied by the idea of coins as its cause.","kind":"passage","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_QGOH_PQ_5:P0096"]},{"id":"RAW_QGOH_PQ_5:P0097","text":"CHAPTER XXIX","kind":"passage","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_QGOH_PQ_5:P0097"]},{"id":"RAW_QGOH_PQ_5:P0098","text":"But this fault belongs only to those who seek money not from need, nor for necessities, but because they have learned the arts of gain, by which they proudly exalt themselves. The rest nourish the Body according to custom, but sparingly, because they believe that they lose from their own goods as much as they expend on the preservation of their Body. But those who know the true use of money, and regulate the measure of wealth from need alone, live content with little.","kind":"passage","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_QGOH_PQ_5:P0098"]},{"id":"RAW_QGOH_PQ_5:P0099","text":"CHAPTER XXX","kind":"passage","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_QGOH_PQ_5:P0099"]},{"id":"RAW_QGOH_PQ_5:P0100","text":"Since, therefore, those things are good which assist the parts of the Body to perform their office, and Joy consists in this, that the power of man, insofar as he consists of Mind and Body, is assisted or increased; therefore all those things which bring Joy are good. Nevertheless, since things do not, on the other hand, act to the end of affecting us with Joy, nor is their power of acting regulated by our advantage, and finally since Joy is for the most part chiefly referred to one part of the Body; therefore the affects of Joy (unless Reason and vigilance are present), and consequently also the Desires which are generated from them, for the most part have excess; to which is added that from affect we hold first that which is pleasant in the present, nor can we estimate future things with an equal affect of mind. See Schol. Prop. 44 and Schol. Prop. 60, Part IV.","kind":"passage","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_QGOH_PQ_5:P0100"]},{"id":"RAW_QGOH_PQ_5:P0101","text":"CHAPTER XXXI","kind":"passage","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_QGOH_PQ_5:P0101"]},{"id":"RAW_QGOH_PQ_5:P0102","text":"But superstition appears, on the contrary, to establish that as good which brings Sadness, and, on the contrary, that as evil which brings Joy. But, as we have already said (see Schol. Prop. 45, Part IV), no one, unless envious, delights in my impotence and inconvenience. For the greater the Joy by which we are affected, the greater the perfection to which we pass, and consequently the more we participate in the divine nature; nor can Joy ever be evil when it is regulated by the true account of our advantage. But he who, on the contrary, is led by Fear, and acts rightly in order to avoid evil, is not led by Reason.","kind":"passage","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_QGOH_PQ_5:P0102"]},{"id":"RAW_QGOH_PQ_5:P0103","text":"CHAPTER XXXII","kind":"passage","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_QGOH_PQ_5:P0103"]},{"id":"RAW_QGOH_PQ_5:P0104","text":"But human power is exceedingly limited, and is infinitely surpassed by the power of external causes; and therefore we do not have absolute power to adapt things which are outside us to our use. Nevertheless, we shall bear with equanimity those things which happen to us contrary to what the account of our advantage requires, if we are conscious that we have performed our duty, and that the power which we have could not extend itself so far that we might have avoided them; and that we are a part of the whole of Nature, whose order we follow. If we understand this clearly and distinctly, that part of ourselves which is defined by understanding, that is, the better part of ourselves, will be wholly content in this, and will endeavor to persevere in that contentment.","kind":"passage","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_QGOH_PQ_5:P0104"]},{"id":"RAW_QGOH_PQ_5:P0105","text":"For insofar as we understand, we desire nothing except what is necessary, nor can we be absolutely content except in true things; and therefore, insofar as we rightly understand these things, to that extent the striving of the better part of ourselves agrees with the order of the whole of Nature.","kind":"passage","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_QGOH_PQ_5:P0105"]},{"id":"RAW_QGOH_PQ_5:P0106","text":"END OF THE FOURTH PART","kind":"passage","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_QGOH_PQ_5:P0106"]}],"source_digest":"ede49d898c261608b3c3be8870f7f6db960072ce7d0f9ce85e2c684512fd7959","source_language_code":"la","original_title":"Pars quarta - De servitute humana seu de affectuum viribus · passage 5"}],"passage_basis":"frozen-grounding","original_title":"Pars quarta - De servitute humana seu de affectuum viribus · passage 5","title_language_code":"en"},{"id":"RAW_QGOH_PQDP","title":"Part Five - On the Power of the Intellect, or Human Freedom · passage 1","order":19,"chapter_id":"CH_QGOH_PQDP","text":"## ETHICES\n\n## PARS QUINTA,\n\n## DE POTENTIA INTELLECTUS,\n\n## SEU DE LIBERTATE HUMANA.\n\nPRÆFATiO\n\nTranseo tandem ad alteram Ethices partem, quæ est de modo sive via, quæ ad Libertatem ducit. In hac ergo de potentia Rationis agam, ostendens quid ipsa Ratio in affectus possit, et deinde, quid Mentis Libertas seu Beatitudo sit ; ex quibus videbimus, quantum sapiens potior sit ignaro. Quomodo autem et qua via debeat Intellectus perfici, et qua deinde arte Corpus sit curandum, ut possit suo officio recte fungi, huc non pertinet ; hoc enim ad Medicinam, illud autem ad Logicam spectat. Hic igitur, ut dixi, de sola Mentis seu Rationis potentia agam, et ante omnia quantum et quale imperium in affectus habeat ad eosdem coercendum et moderandum, ostendam. Nam nos in ipsos imperium absolutum non habere, jam supra demonstravimus. Stoici tamen putarunt, eosdem a nostra voluntate absolute pendere, nosque iis absolute imperare posse. Attamen ab experientia reclamante, non vero ex suis principiis, coacti sunt fateri, usum et studium non parvum requiri ad eosdem coercendum et moderandum ; quod quidam exemplo duorum canum (si recte memini), unius scilicet domestici, alterius venatici conatus est ostendere nempe quia usu efficere tandem potuit, ut domesticus venari venaticus contra a leporibus sectandis abstinere assuesceret. Huic opinioni non parum favet Cartesius. Nam statuit, Animam seu Mentem unitam præcipue esse cuidam parti cerebri, glandulæ scilicet pineali dictæ, cujus ope Mens motus omnes, qui in Corpore excitantur, et objecta externa sentit, quamque Mens eo solo, quod vult, varie movere potest. Hanc glandulam in medio cerebri ita suspensam esse statuit, ut minimo spirituum animalium motu, possit moveri. Deinde statuit, quod hæc glans tot variis modis in medio cerebro suspendatur, quot variis modis spiritus animales in eandem impingunt, et quod præterea tot varia vestigia in eadem imprimantur, quot varia objecta externa ipsos spiritus animales versus eandem propellunt ; unde fit ut si glans postea ab Animæ voluntate, illam diversimode movente, hoc aut illo modo suspendatur, quo semel fuit suspensa a spiritibus, hoc aut illo modo agitatis, tum ipsa glans ipsos spiritus animales eodem modo propellet et determinabit, ac antea a simili glandulæ suspensione repulsi fuerant. Præter ea statuit, unamquamque Mentis voluntatem Natura esse unitam certo cuidam glandis motui. Ex. gr. si quis voluntatem habet objectum remotum intuendi, hæc voluntas efficiet, ut pupilla dilatetur ; sed si de sola dilatanda pupilla cogitet, nihil proderit ejus rei habere voluntatem, quia Natura non junxit motum glandis, qui inservit impellendis spiritibus versus nervum Opticum modo conveniente dilatandæ vel contrahendæ pupillæ, cum voluntate eandem dilatandi vel contrahendi, sed demum cum voluntate intuendi objecta remota vel proxima. Denique statuit, quod, etsi unusquisque motus hujus glandulæ videatur connexus esse per Naturam singulis ex nostris cogitationibus ab initio nostræ vitæ, aliis tamen per habitum possunt jungi ; quod probare conatur art. 50 p. 1 de Pass. Animæ. Ex his concludit, nullam esse tam imbecillem Animam, quæ non possit, cum bene dirigitur, acquirere potestatem absolutam in suas Passiones. Nam hæ, ut ab eo definiuntur, sunt perceptiones, aut sensus, aut commotiones animæ, quæ ad eam speciatim referuntur, quæque NB. producuntur, conservantur, et corroborantur, per aliquem motum spirituum (vide articulum 27 p. 1 Pass. Anim.). At quandoquidem cuilibet voluntati possumus jungere motum quemcunque glandis, et consequenter spirituum ; et determinatio voluntatis a sola nostra potestate pendet ; si igitur nostram voluntatem certis et firmis judiciis, secundum quæ nostræ vitæ actiones dirigere volumus, determinemus, et motus passionum, quas habere volumus, hisce judiciis jungamus, imperium acquiremus absolutum in nostras Passiones. Hæc est clarissimi hujus Viri sententia (quantum ex ipsius verbis conjicio) quam ego vix credidissem a tanto Viro prolatam esse, si minus acuta fuisset. Profecto mirari satis non possum, quod vir Philosophus, qui firmiter statuerat, nihil deducere, nisi ex principiis per se notis, et nihil affirmare, nisi quod clare et distincte perciperet, et qui toties Scholasticos reprehenderat, quod per occultas qualitates res obscuras voluerint explicare, Hypothesin sumat omni occulta qualitate occultiorem. Quid, quæso, per Mentis et Corporis unionem intelligit ? quem, inquam, clarum et distinctum conceptum habet cogitationis arctissime unitæ cuidam quantitatis portiunculæ ? Vellem sane, ut hanc unionem per proximam suam causam explicuisset. Sed ille Mentem a Corpore adeo distinctam conceperat, ut nec hujus unionis, nec ipsius Mentis ullam singularem causam assignare potuerit, sed necesse ipsi fuerit, ad causam totius Universi, hoc est ad Deum, recurrere. Deinde pervelim scire, quot motus gradus potest glandulæ isti pineali Mens tribuere, et quanta cum vi eandem suspensam tenere potest. Nam nescio an hæc glans tardius vel celerius a Mente circumagatur, quam a spiritibus animalibus, et an motus Passionum, quos firmis judiciis arcte junximus, non possint ab iisdem iterum a causis corporeis disjungi ; ex quo sequeretur, ut, quamvis Mens firmiter proposuerit contra pericula ire atque huic decreto motus audaciæ junxerit, viso tamen periculo glans ita suspendatur, ut Mens non nisi de fuga possit cogitare ; et sane, cum nulla detur ratio voluntatis ad motum, nulla etiam datur comparatio inter Mentis et Corporis potentiam seu vires ; et consequenter hujus vires nequaquam viribus illius determinari possunt. His adde, quod nec hæc glans ita in medio cerebro sita reperiatur, ut tam facile totque modis circumagi possit, et quod non omnes nervi ad cavitates usque cerebri protendantur. Denique omnia, quæ de voluntate ejusque libertate asserit, omitto, quandoquidem hæc falsa esse satis superque ostenderim. Igitur quia Mentis potentia, ut supra ostendi, sola intelligentia definitur, affectuum remedia, quæ omnes experiri quidem, sed non accurate observare nec distincte videre credo, sola Mentis cognitione determinabimus, et ex eadem illa omnia, quæ ad ipsius beatitudinem spectant, deducemus.\n\nAXIOMATA\n\nI. Si in eodem subjecto duæ contrariæ actiones excitentur, debebit necessario vel in utraque, vel in una sola mutatio fieri, donec desinant contrariæ esse.\n\nII. Effectus potentia definitur potentia ipsius causæ quatenus ejus essentia per ipsius causæ essentiam explicatur vel definitur.\n\nPatet hoc Axioma ex Prop. 7 p. 3.\n\nPROPOSITIO I\n\nProut cogitationes, rerumque ideæ, ordinantur et concatenantur in Mente, ita Corporis affectiones, seu rerum imagines, ad amussim ordinantur et concatenantur in Corpore.\n\nDEMONSTRATIO\n\nOrdo et connexio idearum idem est (per Prop. 7 p. II), ac ordo et connexio rerum, et vice versa ordo et connexio rerum idem est (per Coroll. Prop. 6 et 7 p. II), ac ordo et connexio idearum. Quare, sicuti ordo et connexio idearum in Mente fit secundum ordinem et concatenationem affectionum Corporis (per Prop. 18 p. II), sic vice versa (per Prop. 2 p. III) ordo et connexio affectionum Corporis fit, prout cogitationes, rerumque ideæ ordinantur et concatenantur in Mente. Q. E. D.\n\nPROPOSITIO II\n\nSi animi commotionem seu affectum a causæ externæ cogitatione amoveamus, et aliis jungamus cogitationibus, tum Amor seu Odium erga causam externam, ut et animi fluctuationes, quæ ex his affectibus oriuntur, destruentur.\n\nDEMONSTRATIO\n\nId enim, quod formam Amoris vel Odii constituit, est Lætitia vel Tristitia, concomitante idea causæ externæ (per Defin. 6 et 7 Affect.) : hac igitur sublata, Amoris vel Odii forma simul tollitur ; adeoque hi affectus et qui ex his oriuntur, destruuntur. Q. E. D.\n\nPROPOSITIO III\n\nAffectus, qui passio est, desinit esse passio, simulatque ejus claram et distinctam formamus ideam.\n\nDEMONSTRATIO\n\nAffectus, qui passio est, idea est confusa (per gener. Affect. Defin.). Si itaque ipsius affectus claram et distinctam formemus ideam, hæc idea ab ipso affectu, quatenus ad solam Mentem refertur, non nisi ratione distinguetur (per Prop. 21 p. II cum ejusdem Schol.) ; adeoque (per Prop. 3 p. III) affectus desinet esse passio. Q. E. D.\n\nCOROLLARIUM\n\nAffectus igitur eo magis in nostra potestate est, et Mens ab eo minus patitur, quo nobis est notior.\n\nPROPOSITIO IV\n\nNulla est Corporis affectio, cujus aliquem clarum et distinctum non possumus formare conceptum.\n\nDEMONSTRATIO\n\nQuæ omnibus communia sunt, non possunt concipi nisi adæquate (per Prop. 38 p. II) ; adeoque (per Prop. 12 et Lemma 2, quod habetur post Schol. Prop. 13 p. II) nulla est Corporis affectio, cujus aliquem clarum et distinctum non possumus formare conceptum. Q. E. D.\n\nCOROLLARIUM\n\nHinc sequitur, nullum esse affectum, cujus non possumus aliquem clarum et distinctum formare conceptum. Est namque affectus Corporis affectionis idea (per gen. Affect. Defin.), quæ propterea (per Prop. præced.) aliquem clarum et distinctum involvere debet conceptum.\n\nSCHOLIUM\n\nQuandoquidem nihil datur, ex quo aliquis effectus non sequatur (per Prop. 36 p. I), et quicquid ex idea, quæ in nobis est adæquata, sequitur, id omne clare et distincte intelligimus (per Prop. 40 p. II) ; hinc sequitur, unumquemque potestatem habere se suosque affectus, si non absolute, ex parte saltem clare et distincte intelligendi, et consequenter efficiendi, ut ab iisdem minus patiatur. Huic igitur rei præcipue danda est opera, ut unumquemque affectum, quantum fieri potest, clare et distincte cognoscamus ; ut sic Mens ex affectu ad illa cogitandum determinetur, quæ clare et distincte percipit, et in quibus plane acquiescit ; atque adeo, ut ipse affectus a cogitatione causæ externæ separetur, et veris jungatur cogitationibus ; ex quo fiet, ut non tantum Amor, Odium, etc. destruantur (per Prop. 2 hujus), sed ut etiam appetitus seu Cupiditates, quæ ex tali affectu oriri solent, excessum habere nequeant (per Prop. 61 p. IV). Nam apprime notandum est, unum eundemque esse appetitum, per quem homo tam agere quam pati dicitur. Ex. gr. cum natura humana ita comparatum esse ostendimus, ut unusquisque appetat, ut reliqui ex ipsius ingenio vivant (vide Schol. Prop. 31 p. III) ; qui quidem appetitus in homine, qui Ratione non ducitur, passio est, quæ Ambitio vocatur, nec multum a Superbia discrepat ; et contra in homine, qui ex Rationis dictamine vivit, actio seu virtus est, quæ Pietas appellatur (vide Schol. 1 Prop. 37 p. IV et II Demonstrat. ejusdem Prop.). Et hoc modo omnes appetitus seu Cupiditates eatenus tantum passiones sunt, quatenus ex ideis inadæquatis oriuntur ; atque eædem virtuti accensentur quando ab ideis adæquatis excitantur vel generantur. Nam omnes Cupiditates, quibus ad aliquid agendum determinamur, tam oriri possunt ab adæquatis quam ab inadæquatis ideis (vide Prop. 59 p. IV). Atque hoc (ut eo, unde digressus sum, revertar) affectuum remedio, quod scilicet in eorum vera cognitione consistit, nullum præstantius aliud, quod a nostra potestate pendeat, excogitari potest, quandoquidem nulla alia Mentis potentia datur, quam cogitandi et adæquatas ideas formandi, ut supra (per Prop. 3 p. III) ostendimus.\n\nPROPOSITIO V\n\nAffectus erga rem, quam simpliciter, et non ut necessariam neque ut possibilem, neque ut contingentem imaginamur, cæteris paribus, omnium est maximus.\n\nDEMONSTRATIO\n\nAffectus erga rem, quam liberam esse imaginamur, major est quam erga necessariam (per Prop. 49 p. III), et consequenter adhuc major quam erga illam, quam ut possibilem vel contingentem imaginamur (per Prop. 11 p. IV). At rem aliquam ut liberam imaginari nihil aliud esse potest, quam quod rem simpliciter imaginamur, dum causas, a quibus ipsa ad agendum determinata fuit, ignoramus (per illa, quæ in Schol. Prop. 35 p. II ostendimus) ; ergo affectus erga rem, quam simpliciter imaginamur, cæteris paribus major est, quam erga necessariam, possibilem, vel contingentem, et consequenter maximus. Q. E. D.\n\nPROPOSITIO VI\n\nQuatenus Mens res omnes ut necessarias intelligit, eatenus majorem in affectus potentiam habet, seu minus ab iisdem patitur.\n\nDEMONSTRATIO\n\nMens res omnes necessarias esse intelligit (per Prop. 29 p. I), et infinito causarum nexu determinari ad existendum et operandum (per Prop. 28 p. I) ; adeoque (per Prop. præced.) eatenus efficit, ut ab affectibus, qui ex iis oriuntur, minus patiatur, et (per Prop. 48 p. III) minus erga ipsas afficiatur. Q. E. D.\n\nSCHOLIUM\n\nQuo hæc cognitio, quod scilicet res necessariæ sint, magis circa res singulares, quas distinctius et magis vivide imaginamur, versatur, eo hæc Mentis in affectus potentia major est ; quod ipsa etiam experientia testatur. Videmus enim Tristitiam boni alicujus, quod periit, mitigari, simulac homo, qui id perdidit, considerat, bonum illud servari nulla ratione potuisse. Sic etiam videmus, quod nemo miseretur infantis, propterea quod nescit loqui, ambulare, ratiocinari, et quod denique tot annos quasi sui inscius vivat. At si plerique adulti, et unus aut alter infans nascerentur, tum unumquemque misereret infantum ; quia tum ipsam infantiam non ut rem naturalem et necessariam, sed ut Naturæ vitium seu peccatum consideraret ; et ad hunc modum plura alia notare possemus.\n\nPROPOSITIO VII\n\nAffectus, qui ex Ratione oriuntur vel excitantur, si ratio temporis habeatur, potentiores sunt iis, qui ad res singulares referuntur, quas ut absentes contemplamur.\n\nDEMONSTRATIO\n\nRem aliquam ut absentem non contemplamur ex affectu, quo eandem imaginamur, sed ex eo, quod Corpus alio afficitur affectu, qui ejusdem rei existentiam secludit (per Prop. 17 p. II). Quare affectus, qui ad rem quam ut absentem contemplamur, refertur, ejus naturæ non est, ut reliquas hominis actiones et potentiam superet (de quibus vide Prop. 6 p. IV), sed contra ejus naturæ est, ut ab iis affectionibus, quæ existentiam externæ ejus causæ secludunt, coerceri aliquo modo possit (per Prop. 9 p. IV). At affectus, qui ex Ratione oritur, refertur necessario ad communes rerum proprietates (vide Rationis defin. in 2 Schol. Prop. 40 p. II), quas semper ut præsentes contemplamur (nam nihil dari potest, quod earum præsentem existentiam secludat), et quas semper eodem modo imaginamur (per Prop. 38 p. II). Quare talis affectus idem semper manet ; et consequenter (per Ax. 1 hujus) affectus, qui eidem sunt contrarii, quique a suis causis externis non foventur, eidem magis magisque sese accommodare debebunt, donec non amplius sint contrarii ; et eatenus affectus, qui ex Ratione oritur, est potentior. Q. E. D.\n\nPROPOSITIO VIII\n\nQuo affectus aliquis a pluribus causis simul concurrentibus excitatur, eo major est.\n\nDEMONSTRATIO\n\nPlures causæ simul plus possunt quam si pauciores essent (per Prop. 7 p. III) : adeoque (per Prop. 5 p. IV), quo affectus aliquis a pluribus causis simul excitatur, eo fortior est. Q. E. D.\n\nSCHOLIUM\n\nHæc Propositio patet etiam ex Axiomate 2 hujus Partis.\n\nPROPOSITIO IX\n\nAffectus, qui ad plures et diversas causas refertur, quas Mens cum ipso affectu simul contemplatur, minus noxius est, et minus per ipsum patimur, et erga unamquamque causam minus afficimur, quam alius æque magnus affectus, qui ad unam solam vel pauciores causas refertur.\n\nDEMONSTRATIO\n\nAffectus eatenus tantum malus seu noxius est, quatenus Mens ab eo impeditur, quo minus possit cogitare (per Prop. 26 et 27 p. IV) : adeoque ille affectus, a quo Mens ad plura simul objecta contemplandum determinatur, minus noxius est, quam alius æque magnus affectus, qui Mentem in sola unius aut pauciorum objectorum contemplatione ita detinet, ut de aliis cogitare nequeat ; Quod erat primum. Deinde, quia Mentis essentia, hoc est (per Prop. 7 p. III) potentia, in sola cogitatione consistit (per Prop. 11 p. II), ergo Mens per affectum, a quo ad plura simul contemplandum determinatur, minus patitur, quam per æque magnum affectum, qui Mentem in sola unius aut pauciorum objectorum contemplatione occupatam tenet ; Quod erat secundum. Denique hic affectus (per Prop. 48 p. III), quatenus ad plures causas externas refertur, est etiam erga unamquamque minor. Q. E. D.\n\nPROPOSITIO X\n\nQuamdiu affectibus, qui nostræ naturæ sunt contrarii, non conflictamur, tamdiu potestatem habemus ordinandi et concatenandi Corporis affectiones secundum ordinem ad intellectum.\n\nDEMONSTRATIO\n\nAffectus, qui nostræ naturæ sunt contrarii, hoc est (per Prop. 30 p. IV) qui mali sunt, eatenus mali sunt, quatenus impediunt, quo minus Mens intelligat (per Prop. 27 p. IV). Quamdiu igitur affectibus, qui nostræ naturæ contrarii sunt, non conflictamur, tamdiu Mentis potentia, qua res intelligere conatur (per Prop. 26 p. IV), non impeditur, atque adeo tamdiu potestatem habet claras et distinctas ideas formandi, et alias ex aliis deducendi (vide 2 Schol. Prop. 40 et Schol. Prop. 47 p. II) ; et consequenter (per Prop. 1 hujus) tamdiu potestatem habemus ordinandi et concatenandi affectiones Corporis secundum ordinem ad intellectum. Q. E. D.\n\nSCHOLIUM\n\nHac potestate recte ordinandi et concatenandi Corporis affectiones efficere possumus, ut non facile malis affectibus afficiamur. Nam (per Prop. 7 hujus) major vis requiritur ad affectus, secundum ordinem ad intellectum ordinatos et concatenatos, coercendum, quam incertos et vagos. Optimum igitur, quod efficere possumus, quamdiu nostrorum affectuum perfectam cognitionem non habemus, est, rectam vivendi rationem seu certa vitæ dogmata concipere, eaque memoriæ mandare, et rebus particularibus, in vita frequenter obviis, continuo applicare, ut sic nostra imaginatio late iisdem afficiatur, et nobis in promptu sint semper. Ex. gr. inter vitæ dogmata posuimus (vide Prop. 46 p. IV, cum ejusdem Schol.), Odium Amore seu Generositate vincendum, non autem reciproco Odio compensandum. Ut autem hoc Rationis præscriptum semper in promptu habeamus, ubi usus erit, cogitandæ et sæpe meditandæ sunt communes hominum injuriæ, et quomodo et qua via Generositate optime propulsentur ; sic enim imaginem injuriæ imaginationi hujus dogmatis jungemus et nobis (per Prop. 18 p. II), in promptu semper erit, ubi nobis injuria afferetur. Quod si etiam in promptu habuerimus rationem nostri veri utilis, ac etiam boni, quod ex mutua amicitia et communi societate sequitur, et præterea quod ex recta vivendi ratione summa animi acquiescentia oriatur (per Prop. 52 p. IV), et quod homines, ut reliqua, ex naturæ necessitate agant : tum injuria, sive Odium, quod ex eadem oriri solet, minimam imaginationis partem occupabit, et facile superabitur ; vel si Ira, quæ ex maximis injuriis oriri solet, non adeo facile superetur, superabitur tamen, quamvis non sine animi fluctuatione, longe minore temporis spatio, quam si hæc non ita præmeditata habuissemus, ut patet ex Propositione 6, 7 et 8 hujus Partis. De Animositate ad Metum deponendum eodem modo cogitandum est ; enumeranda scilicet sunt et sæpe imaginanda communia vitæ pericula, et quomodo animi præsentia et fortitudine optime vitari et superari possunt. Sed notandum, quod nobis in ordinandis nostris cogitationibus et imaginibus, semper attendendum est (per Coroll. Prop. 63 p. IV et Prop. 59 p. III) ad illa, quæ in unaquaque re bona sunt, ut sic semper ex Lætitiæ affectu ad agendum determinemur. Ex. gr. si quis videt, se nimis Gloriam sectari, de ejus recto usu cogitet, et in quem finem sectanda sit, et quibus mediis acquiri possit ; sed non de ipsius abusu, et vanitate, et hominum inconstantia, vel aliis hujusmodi, de quibus nemo, nisi ex animi ægritudine, cogitat ; talibus enim cogitationibus maxime ambitiosi se maxime afflictant, quando de assequendo honore, quem ambiunt, desperant ; et, dum Iram evomunt, sapientes videri volunt. Quare certum est, eos gloriæ maxime esse cupidos, qui de ipsius abusu et mundi vanitate maxime clamant. Nec hoc ambitiosis proprium, sed omnibus commune est, quibus fortuna est adversa, et qui animo impotentes sunt. Nam pauper etiam, avarus de abusu pecuniæ et divitum vitiis non cessat loqui ; quo nihil aliud efficit, quam se afflictare et aliis ostendere, se non tantum paupertatem suam, sed etiam aliorum divitias iniquo animo ferre. Sic etiam, qui male ab amasia excepti sunt, nihil cogitant, quam de mulierum inconstantia et fallaci animo, et reliquis earundem decantatis vitiis ; quæ omnia statim oblivioni tradunt, simulac ab amasia iterum recipiuntur. Qui itaque suos affectus et appetitus ex solo Libertatis amore moderari studet, is quantum potest nitetur, virtutes earumque causas noscere, et animum gaudio, quod ex earum vera cognitione oritur, implere : at minime hominum vitia contemplari, hominesque obtrectare, et falsa libertatis specie gaudere. Atque hæc qui diligenter observabit (neque enim difficilia sunt) et exercebit, næ ille brevi temporis spatio actiones suas ex Rationis imperio plerumque dirigere poterit.\n\nPROPOSITIO XI\n\nQuo imago aliqua ad plures res refertur, eo frequentior est seu sæpius viget, et Mentem magis occupat.\n\nDEMONSTRATIO\n\nQuo enim imago seu affectus ad plures res refertur, eo plures dantur causæ, a quibus excitari et foveri potest, quas omnes Mens (per hypothesin) ex ipso affectu simul contemplatur ; atque adeo affectus eo frequentior est, seu sæpius viget, et (per Prop. 8 hujus) Mentem magis occupat. Q. E. D.\n\nPROPOSITIO XII\n\nRerum imagines facilius imaginibus, quæ ad res referuntur, quas clare et distincte intelligimus, junguntur quam aliis.\n\nDEMONSTRATIO\n\nRes, quas clare et distincte intelligimus, vel rerum communes proprietates sunt, vel quæ ex iis deducuntur (vide Rationis defin. in 2 Schol. Prop. 40 p. II), et consequenter sæpius (per Prop. præced.) in nobis excitantur ; adeoque facilius fieri potest, ut res alias simul cum his, quam cum aliis contemplemur, et consequenter (per Prop. 18 p. II) ut facilius cum his quam cum aliis jungantur. Q. E. D.\n\nPROPOSITIO XIII\n\nQuo imago aliqua pluribus aliis juncta est, eo sæpius viget.\n\nDEMONSTRATIO\n\nNam, quo imago aliqua pluribus aliis juncta est, eo (per Prop. 18 p. II) plures causæ dantur, a quibus excitari potest. Q. E. D.\n\nPROPOSITIO XIV\n\nMens efficere potest, ut omnes Corporis affectiones, seu rerum imagines ad Dei ideam referantur.\n\nDEMONSTRATIO\n\nNulla est Corporis affectio, cujus aliquem clarum et distinctum non possit Mens formare conceptum (per Prop. 4 hujus) ; adeoque efficere potest (per Prop. 15 p. I), ut omnes ad Dei ideam referuntur. Q. E. D.\n\nPROPOSITIO XV\n\nQui se suosque affectus clare et distincte intelligit, Deum amat, et eo magis, quo se suosque affectus magis intelligit.\n\nDEMONSTRATIO\n\nQui se suosque affectus clare et distincte intelligit, lætatur (per Prop. 53 p. III), idque concomitante idea Dei (per Prop. præced.) ; atque adeo (per 6 Affect. Defin.) Deum amat, et (per eandem rationem) eo magis, quo se suosque affectus magis intelligit. Q. E. D.\n\nPROPOSITIO XVI\n\nHic erga Deum Amor Mentem maxime occupare debet.\n\nDEMONSTRATIO\n\nEst enim hic Amor junctus omnibus Corporis affectionibus (per Prop. 14 hujus), quibus omnibus fovetur (per Prop. 15 hujus) ; atque adeo (per Prop. 11 hujus) Mentem maxime occupare debet. Q. E. D.\n\nPROPOSITIO XVII\n\nDeus expers est passionum, nec ullo Lætitiæ aut Tristitiæ affectu afficitur.\n\nDEMONSTRATIO\n\nIdeæ omnes, quatenus ad Deum referuntur, veræ sunt (per Prop. 32 p. II), hoc est (per Defin. 4 p. II) adæquatæ ; atque adeo (per Affect. gen. Defin.) Deus expers est passionum. Deinde Deus neque ad majorem neque ad minorem perfectionem transire potest (per 2 Coroll. Prop. 20 p. I) ; adeoque (per 2 et 3 Affect. Defin.) nullo Lætitiæ neque Tristitiæ affectu afficitur. Q. E. D.\n\nCOROLLARIUM\n\nDeus proprie loquendo neminem amat neque odio habet. Nam Deus (per Prop. præced.) nullo Lætitiæ neque Tristitiæ affectu afficitur, et consequenter (per 6 et 7 Affect. Defin.) neminem etiam amat neque odio habet.\n\nPROPOSITIO XVIII\n\nNemo potest Deum odio habere.\n\nDEMONSTRATIO","paragraphs":[{"id":"RAW_QGOH_PQDP:P0001","text":"## ETHICES","kind":"passage"},{"id":"RAW_QGOH_PQDP:P0002","text":"## PARS QUINTA,","kind":"passage"},{"id":"RAW_QGOH_PQDP:P0003","text":"## DE POTENTIA INTELLECTUS,","kind":"passage"},{"id":"RAW_QGOH_PQDP:P0004","text":"## SEU DE LIBERTATE HUMANA.","kind":"passage"},{"id":"RAW_QGOH_PQDP:P0005","text":"PRÆFATiO","kind":"passage"},{"id":"RAW_QGOH_PQDP:P0006","text":"Transeo tandem ad alteram Ethices partem, quæ est de modo sive via, quæ ad Libertatem ducit. In hac ergo de potentia Rationis agam, ostendens quid ipsa Ratio in affectus possit, et deinde, quid Mentis Libertas seu Beatitudo sit ; ex quibus videbimus, quantum sapiens potior sit ignaro. Quomodo autem et qua via debeat Intellectus perfici, et qua deinde arte Corpus sit curandum, ut possit suo officio recte fungi, huc non pertinet ; hoc enim ad Medicinam, illud autem ad Logicam spectat. Hic igitur, ut dixi, de sola Mentis seu Rationis potentia agam, et ante omnia quantum et quale imperium in affectus habeat ad eosdem coercendum et moderandum, ostendam. Nam nos in ipsos imperium absolutum non habere, jam supra demonstravimus. Stoici tamen putarunt, eosdem a nostra voluntate absolute pendere, nosque iis absolute imperare posse.","kind":"passage"},{"id":"RAW_QGOH_PQDP:P0007","text":"Attamen ab experientia reclamante, non vero ex suis principiis, coacti sunt fateri, usum et studium non parvum requiri ad eosdem coercendum et moderandum ; quod quidam exemplo duorum canum (si recte memini), unius scilicet domestici, alterius venatici conatus est ostendere nempe quia usu efficere tandem potuit, ut domesticus venari venaticus contra a leporibus sectandis abstinere assuesceret. Huic opinioni non parum favet Cartesius. Nam statuit, Animam seu Mentem unitam præcipue esse cuidam parti cerebri, glandulæ scilicet pineali dictæ, cujus ope Mens motus omnes, qui in Corpore excitantur, et objecta externa sentit, quamque Mens eo solo, quod vult, varie movere potest. Hanc glandulam in medio cerebri ita suspensam esse statuit, ut minimo spirituum animalium motu, possit moveri.","kind":"passage"},{"id":"RAW_QGOH_PQDP:P0008","text":"Deinde statuit, quod hæc glans tot variis modis in medio cerebro suspendatur, quot variis modis spiritus animales in eandem impingunt, et quod præterea tot varia vestigia in eadem imprimantur, quot varia objecta externa ipsos spiritus animales versus eandem propellunt ; unde fit ut si glans postea ab Animæ voluntate, illam diversimode movente, hoc aut illo modo suspendatur, quo semel fuit suspensa a spiritibus, hoc aut illo modo agitatis, tum ipsa glans ipsos spiritus animales eodem modo propellet et determinabit, ac antea a simili glandulæ suspensione repulsi fuerant. Præter ea statuit, unamquamque Mentis voluntatem Natura esse unitam certo cuidam glandis motui. Ex. gr.","kind":"passage"},{"id":"RAW_QGOH_PQDP:P0009","text":"si quis voluntatem habet objectum remotum intuendi, hæc voluntas efficiet, ut pupilla dilatetur ; sed si de sola dilatanda pupilla cogitet, nihil proderit ejus rei habere voluntatem, quia Natura non junxit motum glandis, qui inservit impellendis spiritibus versus nervum Opticum modo conveniente dilatandæ vel contrahendæ pupillæ, cum voluntate eandem dilatandi vel contrahendi, sed demum cum voluntate intuendi objecta remota vel proxima. Denique statuit, quod, etsi unusquisque motus hujus glandulæ videatur connexus esse per Naturam singulis ex nostris cogitationibus ab initio nostræ vitæ, aliis tamen per habitum possunt jungi ; quod probare conatur art. 50 p. 1 de Pass. Animæ. Ex his concludit, nullam esse tam imbecillem Animam, quæ non possit, cum bene dirigitur, acquirere potestatem absolutam in suas Passiones.","kind":"passage"},{"id":"RAW_QGOH_PQDP:P0010","text":"Nam hæ, ut ab eo definiuntur, sunt perceptiones, aut sensus, aut commotiones animæ, quæ ad eam speciatim referuntur, quæque NB. producuntur, conservantur, et corroborantur, per aliquem motum spirituum (vide articulum 27 p. 1 Pass. Anim.). At quandoquidem cuilibet voluntati possumus jungere motum quemcunque glandis, et consequenter spirituum ; et determinatio voluntatis a sola nostra potestate pendet ; si igitur nostram voluntatem certis et firmis judiciis, secundum quæ nostræ vitæ actiones dirigere volumus, determinemus, et motus passionum, quas habere volumus, hisce judiciis jungamus, imperium acquiremus absolutum in nostras Passiones. Hæc est clarissimi hujus Viri sententia (quantum ex ipsius verbis conjicio) quam ego vix credidissem a tanto Viro prolatam esse, si minus acuta fuisset.","kind":"passage"},{"id":"RAW_QGOH_PQDP:P0011","text":"Profecto mirari satis non possum, quod vir Philosophus, qui firmiter statuerat, nihil deducere, nisi ex principiis per se notis, et nihil affirmare, nisi quod clare et distincte perciperet, et qui toties Scholasticos reprehenderat, quod per occultas qualitates res obscuras voluerint explicare, Hypothesin sumat omni occulta qualitate occultiorem. Quid, quæso, per Mentis et Corporis unionem intelligit ? quem, inquam, clarum et distinctum conceptum habet cogitationis arctissime unitæ cuidam quantitatis portiunculæ ? Vellem sane, ut hanc unionem per proximam suam causam explicuisset. Sed ille Mentem a Corpore adeo distinctam conceperat, ut nec hujus unionis, nec ipsius Mentis ullam singularem causam assignare potuerit, sed necesse ipsi fuerit, ad causam totius Universi, hoc est ad Deum, recurrere.","kind":"passage"},{"id":"RAW_QGOH_PQDP:P0012","text":"Deinde pervelim scire, quot motus gradus potest glandulæ isti pineali Mens tribuere, et quanta cum vi eandem suspensam tenere potest. Nam nescio an hæc glans tardius vel celerius a Mente circumagatur, quam a spiritibus animalibus, et an motus Passionum, quos firmis judiciis arcte junximus, non possint ab iisdem iterum a causis corporeis disjungi ; ex quo sequeretur, ut, quamvis Mens firmiter proposuerit contra pericula ire atque huic decreto motus audaciæ junxerit, viso tamen periculo glans ita suspendatur, ut Mens non nisi de fuga possit cogitare ; et sane, cum nulla detur ratio voluntatis ad motum, nulla etiam datur comparatio inter Mentis et Corporis potentiam seu vires ; et consequenter hujus vires nequaquam viribus illius determinari possunt.","kind":"passage"},{"id":"RAW_QGOH_PQDP:P0013","text":"His adde, quod nec hæc glans ita in medio cerebro sita reperiatur, ut tam facile totque modis circumagi possit, et quod non omnes nervi ad cavitates usque cerebri protendantur. Denique omnia, quæ de voluntate ejusque libertate asserit, omitto, quandoquidem hæc falsa esse satis superque ostenderim. Igitur quia Mentis potentia, ut supra ostendi, sola intelligentia definitur, affectuum remedia, quæ omnes experiri quidem, sed non accurate observare nec distincte videre credo, sola Mentis cognitione determinabimus, et ex eadem illa omnia, quæ ad ipsius beatitudinem spectant, deducemus.","kind":"passage"},{"id":"RAW_QGOH_PQDP:P0014","text":"AXIOMATA","kind":"passage"},{"id":"RAW_QGOH_PQDP:P0015","text":"I. Si in eodem subjecto duæ contrariæ actiones excitentur, debebit necessario vel in utraque, vel in una sola mutatio fieri, donec desinant contrariæ esse.","kind":"passage"},{"id":"RAW_QGOH_PQDP:P0016","text":"II. Effectus potentia definitur potentia ipsius causæ quatenus ejus essentia per ipsius causæ essentiam explicatur vel definitur.","kind":"passage"},{"id":"RAW_QGOH_PQDP:P0017","text":"Patet hoc Axioma ex Prop. 7 p. 3.","kind":"passage"},{"id":"RAW_QGOH_PQDP:P0018","text":"PROPOSITIO I","kind":"passage"},{"id":"RAW_QGOH_PQDP:P0019","text":"Prout cogitationes, rerumque ideæ, ordinantur et concatenantur in Mente, ita Corporis affectiones, seu rerum imagines, ad amussim ordinantur et concatenantur in Corpore.","kind":"passage"},{"id":"RAW_QGOH_PQDP:P0020","text":"DEMONSTRATIO","kind":"passage"},{"id":"RAW_QGOH_PQDP:P0021","text":"Ordo et connexio idearum idem est (per Prop. 7 p. II), ac ordo et connexio rerum, et vice versa ordo et connexio rerum idem est (per Coroll. Prop. 6 et 7 p. II), ac ordo et connexio idearum. Quare, sicuti ordo et connexio idearum in Mente fit secundum ordinem et concatenationem affectionum Corporis (per Prop. 18 p. II), sic vice versa (per Prop. 2 p. III) ordo et connexio affectionum Corporis fit, prout cogitationes, rerumque ideæ ordinantur et concatenantur in Mente. Q. E. D.","kind":"passage"},{"id":"RAW_QGOH_PQDP:P0022","text":"PROPOSITIO II","kind":"passage"},{"id":"RAW_QGOH_PQDP:P0023","text":"Si animi commotionem seu affectum a causæ externæ cogitatione amoveamus, et aliis jungamus cogitationibus, tum Amor seu Odium erga causam externam, ut et animi fluctuationes, quæ ex his affectibus oriuntur, destruentur.","kind":"passage"},{"id":"RAW_QGOH_PQDP:P0024","text":"DEMONSTRATIO","kind":"passage"},{"id":"RAW_QGOH_PQDP:P0025","text":"Id enim, quod formam Amoris vel Odii constituit, est Lætitia vel Tristitia, concomitante idea causæ externæ (per Defin. 6 et 7 Affect.) : hac igitur sublata, Amoris vel Odii forma simul tollitur ; adeoque hi affectus et qui ex his oriuntur, destruuntur. Q. E. D.","kind":"passage"},{"id":"RAW_QGOH_PQDP:P0026","text":"PROPOSITIO III","kind":"passage"},{"id":"RAW_QGOH_PQDP:P0027","text":"Affectus, qui passio est, desinit esse passio, simulatque ejus claram et distinctam formamus ideam.","kind":"passage"},{"id":"RAW_QGOH_PQDP:P0028","text":"DEMONSTRATIO","kind":"passage"},{"id":"RAW_QGOH_PQDP:P0029","text":"Affectus, qui passio est, idea est confusa (per gener. Affect. Defin.). Si itaque ipsius affectus claram et distinctam formemus ideam, hæc idea ab ipso affectu, quatenus ad solam Mentem refertur, non nisi ratione distinguetur (per Prop. 21 p. II cum ejusdem Schol.) ; adeoque (per Prop. 3 p. III) affectus desinet esse passio. Q. E. D.","kind":"passage"},{"id":"RAW_QGOH_PQDP:P0030","text":"COROLLARIUM","kind":"passage"},{"id":"RAW_QGOH_PQDP:P0031","text":"Affectus igitur eo magis in nostra potestate est, et Mens ab eo minus patitur, quo nobis est notior.","kind":"passage"},{"id":"RAW_QGOH_PQDP:P0032","text":"PROPOSITIO IV","kind":"passage"},{"id":"RAW_QGOH_PQDP:P0033","text":"Nulla est Corporis affectio, cujus aliquem clarum et distinctum non possumus formare conceptum.","kind":"passage"},{"id":"RAW_QGOH_PQDP:P0034","text":"DEMONSTRATIO","kind":"passage"},{"id":"RAW_QGOH_PQDP:P0035","text":"Quæ omnibus communia sunt, non possunt concipi nisi adæquate (per Prop. 38 p. II) ; adeoque (per Prop. 12 et Lemma 2, quod habetur post Schol. Prop. 13 p. II) nulla est Corporis affectio, cujus aliquem clarum et distinctum non possumus formare conceptum. Q. E. D.","kind":"passage"},{"id":"RAW_QGOH_PQDP:P0036","text":"COROLLARIUM","kind":"passage"},{"id":"RAW_QGOH_PQDP:P0037","text":"Hinc sequitur, nullum esse affectum, cujus non possumus aliquem clarum et distinctum formare conceptum. Est namque affectus Corporis affectionis idea (per gen. Affect. Defin.), quæ propterea (per Prop. præced.) aliquem clarum et distinctum involvere debet conceptum.","kind":"passage"},{"id":"RAW_QGOH_PQDP:P0038","text":"SCHOLIUM","kind":"passage"},{"id":"RAW_QGOH_PQDP:P0039","text":"Quandoquidem nihil datur, ex quo aliquis effectus non sequatur (per Prop. 36 p. I), et quicquid ex idea, quæ in nobis est adæquata, sequitur, id omne clare et distincte intelligimus (per Prop. 40 p. II) ; hinc sequitur, unumquemque potestatem habere se suosque affectus, si non absolute, ex parte saltem clare et distincte intelligendi, et consequenter efficiendi, ut ab iisdem minus patiatur. Huic igitur rei præcipue danda est opera, ut unumquemque affectum, quantum fieri potest, clare et distincte cognoscamus ; ut sic Mens ex affectu ad illa cogitandum determinetur, quæ clare et distincte percipit, et in quibus plane acquiescit ; atque adeo, ut ipse affectus a cogitatione causæ externæ separetur, et veris jungatur cogitationibus ; ex quo fiet, ut non tantum Amor, Odium, etc. destruantur (per Prop.","kind":"passage"},{"id":"RAW_QGOH_PQDP:P0040","text":"2 hujus), sed ut etiam appetitus seu Cupiditates, quæ ex tali affectu oriri solent, excessum habere nequeant (per Prop. 61 p. IV). Nam apprime notandum est, unum eundemque esse appetitum, per quem homo tam agere quam pati dicitur. Ex. gr. cum natura humana ita comparatum esse ostendimus, ut unusquisque appetat, ut reliqui ex ipsius ingenio vivant (vide Schol. Prop. 31 p. III) ; qui quidem appetitus in homine, qui Ratione non ducitur, passio est, quæ Ambitio vocatur, nec multum a Superbia discrepat ; et contra in homine, qui ex Rationis dictamine vivit, actio seu virtus est, quæ Pietas appellatur (vide Schol. 1 Prop. 37 p. IV et II Demonstrat. ejusdem Prop.). Et hoc modo omnes appetitus seu Cupiditates eatenus tantum passiones sunt, quatenus ex ideis inadæquatis oriuntur ; atque eædem virtuti accensentur quando ab ideis adæquatis excitantur vel generantur.","kind":"passage"},{"id":"RAW_QGOH_PQDP:P0041","text":"Nam omnes Cupiditates, quibus ad aliquid agendum determinamur, tam oriri possunt ab adæquatis quam ab inadæquatis ideis (vide Prop. 59 p. IV). Atque hoc (ut eo, unde digressus sum, revertar) affectuum remedio, quod scilicet in eorum vera cognitione consistit, nullum præstantius aliud, quod a nostra potestate pendeat, excogitari potest, quandoquidem nulla alia Mentis potentia datur, quam cogitandi et adæquatas ideas formandi, ut supra (per Prop. 3 p. III) ostendimus.","kind":"passage"},{"id":"RAW_QGOH_PQDP:P0042","text":"PROPOSITIO V","kind":"passage"},{"id":"RAW_QGOH_PQDP:P0043","text":"Affectus erga rem, quam simpliciter, et non ut necessariam neque ut possibilem, neque ut contingentem imaginamur, cæteris paribus, omnium est maximus.","kind":"passage"},{"id":"RAW_QGOH_PQDP:P0044","text":"DEMONSTRATIO","kind":"passage"},{"id":"RAW_QGOH_PQDP:P0045","text":"Affectus erga rem, quam liberam esse imaginamur, major est quam erga necessariam (per Prop. 49 p. III), et consequenter adhuc major quam erga illam, quam ut possibilem vel contingentem imaginamur (per Prop. 11 p. IV). At rem aliquam ut liberam imaginari nihil aliud esse potest, quam quod rem simpliciter imaginamur, dum causas, a quibus ipsa ad agendum determinata fuit, ignoramus (per illa, quæ in Schol. Prop. 35 p. II ostendimus) ; ergo affectus erga rem, quam simpliciter imaginamur, cæteris paribus major est, quam erga necessariam, possibilem, vel contingentem, et consequenter maximus. Q. E. D.","kind":"passage"},{"id":"RAW_QGOH_PQDP:P0046","text":"PROPOSITIO VI","kind":"passage"},{"id":"RAW_QGOH_PQDP:P0047","text":"Quatenus Mens res omnes ut necessarias intelligit, eatenus majorem in affectus potentiam habet, seu minus ab iisdem patitur.","kind":"passage"},{"id":"RAW_QGOH_PQDP:P0048","text":"DEMONSTRATIO","kind":"passage"},{"id":"RAW_QGOH_PQDP:P0049","text":"Mens res omnes necessarias esse intelligit (per Prop. 29 p. I), et infinito causarum nexu determinari ad existendum et operandum (per Prop. 28 p. I) ; adeoque (per Prop. præced.) eatenus efficit, ut ab affectibus, qui ex iis oriuntur, minus patiatur, et (per Prop. 48 p. III) minus erga ipsas afficiatur. Q. E. D.","kind":"passage"},{"id":"RAW_QGOH_PQDP:P0050","text":"SCHOLIUM","kind":"passage"},{"id":"RAW_QGOH_PQDP:P0051","text":"Quo hæc cognitio, quod scilicet res necessariæ sint, magis circa res singulares, quas distinctius et magis vivide imaginamur, versatur, eo hæc Mentis in affectus potentia major est ; quod ipsa etiam experientia testatur. Videmus enim Tristitiam boni alicujus, quod periit, mitigari, simulac homo, qui id perdidit, considerat, bonum illud servari nulla ratione potuisse. Sic etiam videmus, quod nemo miseretur infantis, propterea quod nescit loqui, ambulare, ratiocinari, et quod denique tot annos quasi sui inscius vivat. At si plerique adulti, et unus aut alter infans nascerentur, tum unumquemque misereret infantum ; quia tum ipsam infantiam non ut rem naturalem et necessariam, sed ut Naturæ vitium seu peccatum consideraret ; et ad hunc modum plura alia notare possemus.","kind":"passage"},{"id":"RAW_QGOH_PQDP:P0052","text":"PROPOSITIO VII","kind":"passage"},{"id":"RAW_QGOH_PQDP:P0053","text":"Affectus, qui ex Ratione oriuntur vel excitantur, si ratio temporis habeatur, potentiores sunt iis, qui ad res singulares referuntur, quas ut absentes contemplamur.","kind":"passage"},{"id":"RAW_QGOH_PQDP:P0054","text":"DEMONSTRATIO","kind":"passage"},{"id":"RAW_QGOH_PQDP:P0055","text":"Rem aliquam ut absentem non contemplamur ex affectu, quo eandem imaginamur, sed ex eo, quod Corpus alio afficitur affectu, qui ejusdem rei existentiam secludit (per Prop. 17 p. II). Quare affectus, qui ad rem quam ut absentem contemplamur, refertur, ejus naturæ non est, ut reliquas hominis actiones et potentiam superet (de quibus vide Prop. 6 p. IV), sed contra ejus naturæ est, ut ab iis affectionibus, quæ existentiam externæ ejus causæ secludunt, coerceri aliquo modo possit (per Prop. 9 p. IV). At affectus, qui ex Ratione oritur, refertur necessario ad communes rerum proprietates (vide Rationis defin. in 2 Schol. Prop. 40 p. II), quas semper ut præsentes contemplamur (nam nihil dari potest, quod earum præsentem existentiam secludat), et quas semper eodem modo imaginamur (per Prop. 38 p. II). Quare talis affectus idem semper manet ; et consequenter (per Ax.","kind":"passage"},{"id":"RAW_QGOH_PQDP:P0056","text":"1 hujus) affectus, qui eidem sunt contrarii, quique a suis causis externis non foventur, eidem magis magisque sese accommodare debebunt, donec non amplius sint contrarii ; et eatenus affectus, qui ex Ratione oritur, est potentior. Q. E. D.","kind":"passage"},{"id":"RAW_QGOH_PQDP:P0057","text":"PROPOSITIO VIII","kind":"passage"},{"id":"RAW_QGOH_PQDP:P0058","text":"Quo affectus aliquis a pluribus causis simul concurrentibus excitatur, eo major est.","kind":"passage"},{"id":"RAW_QGOH_PQDP:P0059","text":"DEMONSTRATIO","kind":"passage"},{"id":"RAW_QGOH_PQDP:P0060","text":"Plures causæ simul plus possunt quam si pauciores essent (per Prop. 7 p. III) : adeoque (per Prop. 5 p. IV), quo affectus aliquis a pluribus causis simul excitatur, eo fortior est. Q. E. D.","kind":"passage"},{"id":"RAW_QGOH_PQDP:P0061","text":"SCHOLIUM","kind":"passage"},{"id":"RAW_QGOH_PQDP:P0062","text":"Hæc Propositio patet etiam ex Axiomate 2 hujus Partis.","kind":"passage"},{"id":"RAW_QGOH_PQDP:P0063","text":"PROPOSITIO IX","kind":"passage"},{"id":"RAW_QGOH_PQDP:P0064","text":"Affectus, qui ad plures et diversas causas refertur, quas Mens cum ipso affectu simul contemplatur, minus noxius est, et minus per ipsum patimur, et erga unamquamque causam minus afficimur, quam alius æque magnus affectus, qui ad unam solam vel pauciores causas refertur.","kind":"passage"},{"id":"RAW_QGOH_PQDP:P0065","text":"DEMONSTRATIO","kind":"passage"},{"id":"RAW_QGOH_PQDP:P0066","text":"Affectus eatenus tantum malus seu noxius est, quatenus Mens ab eo impeditur, quo minus possit cogitare (per Prop. 26 et 27 p. IV) : adeoque ille affectus, a quo Mens ad plura simul objecta contemplandum determinatur, minus noxius est, quam alius æque magnus affectus, qui Mentem in sola unius aut pauciorum objectorum contemplatione ita detinet, ut de aliis cogitare nequeat ; Quod erat primum. Deinde, quia Mentis essentia, hoc est (per Prop. 7 p. III) potentia, in sola cogitatione consistit (per Prop. 11 p. II), ergo Mens per affectum, a quo ad plura simul contemplandum determinatur, minus patitur, quam per æque magnum affectum, qui Mentem in sola unius aut pauciorum objectorum contemplatione occupatam tenet ; Quod erat secundum. Denique hic affectus (per Prop. 48 p. III), quatenus ad plures causas externas refertur, est etiam erga unamquamque minor. Q. E. D.","kind":"passage"},{"id":"RAW_QGOH_PQDP:P0067","text":"PROPOSITIO X","kind":"passage"},{"id":"RAW_QGOH_PQDP:P0068","text":"Quamdiu affectibus, qui nostræ naturæ sunt contrarii, non conflictamur, tamdiu potestatem habemus ordinandi et concatenandi Corporis affectiones secundum ordinem ad intellectum.","kind":"passage"},{"id":"RAW_QGOH_PQDP:P0069","text":"DEMONSTRATIO","kind":"passage"},{"id":"RAW_QGOH_PQDP:P0070","text":"Affectus, qui nostræ naturæ sunt contrarii, hoc est (per Prop. 30 p. IV) qui mali sunt, eatenus mali sunt, quatenus impediunt, quo minus Mens intelligat (per Prop. 27 p. IV). Quamdiu igitur affectibus, qui nostræ naturæ contrarii sunt, non conflictamur, tamdiu Mentis potentia, qua res intelligere conatur (per Prop. 26 p. IV), non impeditur, atque adeo tamdiu potestatem habet claras et distinctas ideas formandi, et alias ex aliis deducendi (vide 2 Schol. Prop. 40 et Schol. Prop. 47 p. II) ; et consequenter (per Prop. 1 hujus) tamdiu potestatem habemus ordinandi et concatenandi affectiones Corporis secundum ordinem ad intellectum. Q. E. D.","kind":"passage"},{"id":"RAW_QGOH_PQDP:P0071","text":"SCHOLIUM","kind":"passage"},{"id":"RAW_QGOH_PQDP:P0072","text":"Hac potestate recte ordinandi et concatenandi Corporis affectiones efficere possumus, ut non facile malis affectibus afficiamur. Nam (per Prop. 7 hujus) major vis requiritur ad affectus, secundum ordinem ad intellectum ordinatos et concatenatos, coercendum, quam incertos et vagos. Optimum igitur, quod efficere possumus, quamdiu nostrorum affectuum perfectam cognitionem non habemus, est, rectam vivendi rationem seu certa vitæ dogmata concipere, eaque memoriæ mandare, et rebus particularibus, in vita frequenter obviis, continuo applicare, ut sic nostra imaginatio late iisdem afficiatur, et nobis in promptu sint semper. Ex. gr. inter vitæ dogmata posuimus (vide Prop. 46 p. IV, cum ejusdem Schol.), Odium Amore seu Generositate vincendum, non autem reciproco Odio compensandum.","kind":"passage"},{"id":"RAW_QGOH_PQDP:P0073","text":"Ut autem hoc Rationis præscriptum semper in promptu habeamus, ubi usus erit, cogitandæ et sæpe meditandæ sunt communes hominum injuriæ, et quomodo et qua via Generositate optime propulsentur ; sic enim imaginem injuriæ imaginationi hujus dogmatis jungemus et nobis (per Prop. 18 p. II), in promptu semper erit, ubi nobis injuria afferetur. Quod si etiam in promptu habuerimus rationem nostri veri utilis, ac etiam boni, quod ex mutua amicitia et communi societate sequitur, et præterea quod ex recta vivendi ratione summa animi acquiescentia oriatur (per Prop. 52 p.","kind":"passage"},{"id":"RAW_QGOH_PQDP:P0074","text":"IV), et quod homines, ut reliqua, ex naturæ necessitate agant : tum injuria, sive Odium, quod ex eadem oriri solet, minimam imaginationis partem occupabit, et facile superabitur ; vel si Ira, quæ ex maximis injuriis oriri solet, non adeo facile superetur, superabitur tamen, quamvis non sine animi fluctuatione, longe minore temporis spatio, quam si hæc non ita præmeditata habuissemus, ut patet ex Propositione 6, 7 et 8 hujus Partis. De Animositate ad Metum deponendum eodem modo cogitandum est ; enumeranda scilicet sunt et sæpe imaginanda communia vitæ pericula, et quomodo animi præsentia et fortitudine optime vitari et superari possunt. Sed notandum, quod nobis in ordinandis nostris cogitationibus et imaginibus, semper attendendum est (per Coroll. Prop. 63 p. IV et Prop. 59 p. III) ad illa, quæ in unaquaque re bona sunt, ut sic semper ex Lætitiæ affectu ad agendum determinemur. Ex. gr.","kind":"passage"},{"id":"RAW_QGOH_PQDP:P0075","text":"si quis videt, se nimis Gloriam sectari, de ejus recto usu cogitet, et in quem finem sectanda sit, et quibus mediis acquiri possit ; sed non de ipsius abusu, et vanitate, et hominum inconstantia, vel aliis hujusmodi, de quibus nemo, nisi ex animi ægritudine, cogitat ; talibus enim cogitationibus maxime ambitiosi se maxime afflictant, quando de assequendo honore, quem ambiunt, desperant ; et, dum Iram evomunt, sapientes videri volunt. Quare certum est, eos gloriæ maxime esse cupidos, qui de ipsius abusu et mundi vanitate maxime clamant. Nec hoc ambitiosis proprium, sed omnibus commune est, quibus fortuna est adversa, et qui animo impotentes sunt. Nam pauper etiam, avarus de abusu pecuniæ et divitum vitiis non cessat loqui ; quo nihil aliud efficit, quam se afflictare et aliis ostendere, se non tantum paupertatem suam, sed etiam aliorum divitias iniquo animo ferre.","kind":"passage"},{"id":"RAW_QGOH_PQDP:P0076","text":"Sic etiam, qui male ab amasia excepti sunt, nihil cogitant, quam de mulierum inconstantia et fallaci animo, et reliquis earundem decantatis vitiis ; quæ omnia statim oblivioni tradunt, simulac ab amasia iterum recipiuntur. Qui itaque suos affectus et appetitus ex solo Libertatis amore moderari studet, is quantum potest nitetur, virtutes earumque causas noscere, et animum gaudio, quod ex earum vera cognitione oritur, implere : at minime hominum vitia contemplari, hominesque obtrectare, et falsa libertatis specie gaudere. Atque hæc qui diligenter observabit (neque enim difficilia sunt) et exercebit, næ ille brevi temporis spatio actiones suas ex Rationis imperio plerumque dirigere poterit.","kind":"passage"},{"id":"RAW_QGOH_PQDP:P0077","text":"PROPOSITIO XI","kind":"passage"},{"id":"RAW_QGOH_PQDP:P0078","text":"Quo imago aliqua ad plures res refertur, eo frequentior est seu sæpius viget, et Mentem magis occupat.","kind":"passage"},{"id":"RAW_QGOH_PQDP:P0079","text":"DEMONSTRATIO","kind":"passage"},{"id":"RAW_QGOH_PQDP:P0080","text":"Quo enim imago seu affectus ad plures res refertur, eo plures dantur causæ, a quibus excitari et foveri potest, quas omnes Mens (per hypothesin) ex ipso affectu simul contemplatur ; atque adeo affectus eo frequentior est, seu sæpius viget, et (per Prop. 8 hujus) Mentem magis occupat. Q. E. D.","kind":"passage"},{"id":"RAW_QGOH_PQDP:P0081","text":"PROPOSITIO XII","kind":"passage"},{"id":"RAW_QGOH_PQDP:P0082","text":"Rerum imagines facilius imaginibus, quæ ad res referuntur, quas clare et distincte intelligimus, junguntur quam aliis.","kind":"passage"},{"id":"RAW_QGOH_PQDP:P0083","text":"DEMONSTRATIO","kind":"passage"},{"id":"RAW_QGOH_PQDP:P0084","text":"Res, quas clare et distincte intelligimus, vel rerum communes proprietates sunt, vel quæ ex iis deducuntur (vide Rationis defin. in 2 Schol. Prop. 40 p. II), et consequenter sæpius (per Prop. præced.) in nobis excitantur ; adeoque facilius fieri potest, ut res alias simul cum his, quam cum aliis contemplemur, et consequenter (per Prop. 18 p. II) ut facilius cum his quam cum aliis jungantur. Q. E. D.","kind":"passage"},{"id":"RAW_QGOH_PQDP:P0085","text":"PROPOSITIO XIII","kind":"passage"},{"id":"RAW_QGOH_PQDP:P0086","text":"Quo imago aliqua pluribus aliis juncta est, eo sæpius viget.","kind":"passage"},{"id":"RAW_QGOH_PQDP:P0087","text":"DEMONSTRATIO","kind":"passage"},{"id":"RAW_QGOH_PQDP:P0088","text":"Nam, quo imago aliqua pluribus aliis juncta est, eo (per Prop. 18 p. II) plures causæ dantur, a quibus excitari potest. Q. E. D.","kind":"passage"},{"id":"RAW_QGOH_PQDP:P0089","text":"PROPOSITIO XIV","kind":"passage"},{"id":"RAW_QGOH_PQDP:P0090","text":"Mens efficere potest, ut omnes Corporis affectiones, seu rerum imagines ad Dei ideam referantur.","kind":"passage"},{"id":"RAW_QGOH_PQDP:P0091","text":"DEMONSTRATIO","kind":"passage"},{"id":"RAW_QGOH_PQDP:P0092","text":"Nulla est Corporis affectio, cujus aliquem clarum et distinctum non possit Mens formare conceptum (per Prop. 4 hujus) ; adeoque efficere potest (per Prop. 15 p. I), ut omnes ad Dei ideam referuntur. Q. E. D.","kind":"passage"},{"id":"RAW_QGOH_PQDP:P0093","text":"PROPOSITIO XV","kind":"passage"},{"id":"RAW_QGOH_PQDP:P0094","text":"Qui se suosque affectus clare et distincte intelligit, Deum amat, et eo magis, quo se suosque affectus magis intelligit.","kind":"passage"},{"id":"RAW_QGOH_PQDP:P0095","text":"DEMONSTRATIO","kind":"passage"},{"id":"RAW_QGOH_PQDP:P0096","text":"Qui se suosque affectus clare et distincte intelligit, lætatur (per Prop. 53 p. III), idque concomitante idea Dei (per Prop. præced.) ; atque adeo (per 6 Affect. Defin.) Deum amat, et (per eandem rationem) eo magis, quo se suosque affectus magis intelligit. Q. E. D.","kind":"passage"},{"id":"RAW_QGOH_PQDP:P0097","text":"PROPOSITIO XVI","kind":"passage"},{"id":"RAW_QGOH_PQDP:P0098","text":"Hic erga Deum Amor Mentem maxime occupare debet.","kind":"passage"},{"id":"RAW_QGOH_PQDP:P0099","text":"DEMONSTRATIO","kind":"passage"},{"id":"RAW_QGOH_PQDP:P0100","text":"Est enim hic Amor junctus omnibus Corporis affectionibus (per Prop. 14 hujus), quibus omnibus fovetur (per Prop. 15 hujus) ; atque adeo (per Prop. 11 hujus) Mentem maxime occupare debet. Q. E. D.","kind":"passage"},{"id":"RAW_QGOH_PQDP:P0101","text":"PROPOSITIO XVII","kind":"passage"},{"id":"RAW_QGOH_PQDP:P0102","text":"Deus expers est passionum, nec ullo Lætitiæ aut Tristitiæ affectu afficitur.","kind":"passage"},{"id":"RAW_QGOH_PQDP:P0103","text":"DEMONSTRATIO","kind":"passage"},{"id":"RAW_QGOH_PQDP:P0104","text":"Ideæ omnes, quatenus ad Deum referuntur, veræ sunt (per Prop. 32 p. II), hoc est (per Defin. 4 p. II) adæquatæ ; atque adeo (per Affect. gen. Defin.) Deus expers est passionum. Deinde Deus neque ad majorem neque ad minorem perfectionem transire potest (per 2 Coroll. Prop. 20 p. I) ; adeoque (per 2 et 3 Affect. Defin.) nullo Lætitiæ neque Tristitiæ affectu afficitur. Q. E. D.","kind":"passage"},{"id":"RAW_QGOH_PQDP:P0105","text":"COROLLARIUM","kind":"passage"},{"id":"RAW_QGOH_PQDP:P0106","text":"Deus proprie loquendo neminem amat neque odio habet. Nam Deus (per Prop. præced.) nullo Lætitiæ neque Tristitiæ affectu afficitur, et consequenter (per 6 et 7 Affect. Defin.) neminem etiam amat neque odio habet.","kind":"passage"},{"id":"RAW_QGOH_PQDP:P0107","text":"PROPOSITIO XVIII","kind":"passage"},{"id":"RAW_QGOH_PQDP:P0108","text":"Nemo potest Deum odio habere.","kind":"passage"},{"id":"RAW_QGOH_PQDP:P0109","text":"DEMONSTRATIO","kind":"passage"}],"sha256":"aaf497e79ee7381d63fd7225b3d53dbe754072470d03680567cf2f6fda75c9fe","language_code":"la","language_name":"Latina","translations":[{"id":"SOURCE_TR_20261015T014302549209Z_3425bb5b","language_code":"en","language_name":"English","title":"Part Five - On the Power of the Intellect, or Human Freedom · passage 1","source_sha256":"aaf497e79ee7381d63fd7225b3d53dbe754072470d03680567cf2f6fda75c9fe","sha256":"7eeba532f70045edf26e4a3040c925f1ec712a507ef1fcd10c684b69e299641d","rendition_type":"machine","review_status":"machine","model":"gpt-5.6-terra","created":"2026-10-15T01:43:02.549336+00:00","paragraphs":[{"id":"RAW_QGOH_PQDP:P0001","text":"## ETHICS","kind":"passage","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_QGOH_PQDP:P0001"]},{"id":"RAW_QGOH_PQDP:P0002","text":"## PART FIVE,","kind":"passage","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_QGOH_PQDP:P0002"]},{"id":"RAW_QGOH_PQDP:P0003","text":"## CONCERNING THE POWER OF THE INTELLECT,","kind":"passage","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_QGOH_PQDP:P0003"]},{"id":"RAW_QGOH_PQDP:P0004","text":"## OR CONCERNING HUMAN FREEDOM.","kind":"passage","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_QGOH_PQDP:P0004"]},{"id":"RAW_QGOH_PQDP:P0005","text":"PREFACE","kind":"passage","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_QGOH_PQDP:P0005"]},{"id":"RAW_QGOH_PQDP:P0006","text":"I pass at last to the other part of the Ethics, which concerns the manner, or way, that leads to Freedom. Here, therefore, I shall treat of the power of Reason, showing what Reason itself can do against the affects, and then what Freedom of Mind, or Blessedness, is; from which we shall see how much more powerful the wise man is than the ignorant. But how and by what way the Intellect ought to be perfected, and by what art the Body must then be cared for, so that it may be able rightly to perform its office, does not belong here; for this pertains to Medicine, that to Logic. Here, therefore, as I have said, I shall treat only of the power of the Mind, or Reason, and before all else shall show how great and what kind of dominion it has over the affects, for restraining and moderating them. For that we do not have absolute dominion over them, we have already demonstrated above. Yet the Stoics thought that they depend absolutely on our will, and that we can rule them absolutely.","kind":"passage","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_QGOH_PQDP:P0006"]},{"id":"RAW_QGOH_PQDP:P0007","text":"Nevertheless, compelled by protesting experience, though not by their own principles, they were forced to admit that no small practice and application are required to restrain and moderate them; which someone, if I remember rightly, attempted to show by the example of two dogs, namely, one domestic, the other hunting: because by practice he was finally able to bring it about that the domestic dog grew accustomed to hunt, and the hunting dog, conversely, to refrain from pursuing hares. This opinion is not a little favored by Descartes. For he maintains that the Soul, or Mind, is united chiefly to a certain part of the brain, called the pineal gland, by whose means the Mind senses all the motions excited in the Body and external objects, and which the Mind can variously move by this alone, that it wills. He maintains that this gland is so suspended in the middle of the brain that it can be moved by the slightest motion of the animal spirits.","kind":"passage","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_QGOH_PQDP:P0007"]},{"id":"RAW_QGOH_PQDP:P0008","text":"Next he maintains that this gland is suspended in the middle of the brain in as many various ways as the animal spirits strike against it in various ways, and that, moreover, as many various traces are impressed upon it as various external objects propel the animal spirits themselves toward it; whence it comes about that, if afterward the gland is suspended in this or that way by the Soul's will, moving it diversely, as it was once suspended in this or that way by spirits agitated in this or that way, then the gland itself will propel and determine the animal spirits in the same way as they had previously been driven back by a similar suspension of the gland. Besides this, he maintains that each volition of the Mind is by Nature united with a certain motion of the gland. For example,","kind":"passage","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_QGOH_PQDP:P0008"]},{"id":"RAW_QGOH_PQDP:P0009","text":"if someone has the will to behold a distant object, this will shall bring it about that the pupil is dilated; but if he thinks only of dilating the pupil, it will avail nothing to have the will to do so, because Nature has not joined the motion of the gland which serves to impel the spirits toward the optic nerve in the manner suitable for dilating or contracting the pupil, with the will to dilate or contract it, but only with the will to behold distant or near objects. Finally, he maintains that, although every motion of this gland seems from the beginning of our life to be connected by Nature with each of our thoughts, yet by habit they may be connected with others; which he endeavors to prove in art. 50, pt. 1, of the Passions of the Soul. From these things he concludes that there is no Soul so weak that, when it is well directed, it cannot acquire absolute power over its Passions.","kind":"passage","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_QGOH_PQDP:P0009"]},{"id":"RAW_QGOH_PQDP:P0010","text":"For these, as he defines them, are perceptions, or sensations, or commotions of the soul, which are referred specifically to it, and which, NB., are produced, preserved, and strengthened by some motion of the spirits (see article 27, pt. 1, Passions of the Soul). But since we can join to every volition whatever motion of the gland, and consequently of the spirits; and the determination of the will depends on our power alone; if, therefore, we determine our will by certain and firm judgments, according to which we wish to direct the actions of our life, and join to these judgments the motions of the passions which we wish to have, we shall acquire absolute dominion over our Passions. This is the opinion of this most illustrious Man (so far as I gather from his words), which I would scarcely have believed had been advanced by so great a Man, had it been less acute.","kind":"passage","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_QGOH_PQDP:P0010"]},{"id":"RAW_QGOH_PQDP:P0011","text":"Indeed, I cannot sufficiently wonder that a philosopher who had firmly resolved to deduce nothing except from principles known through themselves, and to affirm nothing except what he perceived clearly and distinctly, and who had so often reproached the Scholastics because they wished to explain obscure things through occult qualities, should adopt a Hypothesis more occult than any occult quality. What, I ask, does he understand by the union of Mind and Body? What, I say, clear and distinct conception does he have of a thought most closely united to a little portion of quantity? I should certainly wish that he had explained this union through its proximate cause. But he had conceived the Mind as so distinct from the Body that he could assign no singular cause either of this union or of the Mind itself, but was compelled to have recourse to the cause of the whole Universe, that is, to God.","kind":"passage","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_QGOH_PQDP:P0011"]},{"id":"RAW_QGOH_PQDP:P0012","text":"Further, I should very much like to know how many degrees of motion the Mind can impart to that pineal gland, and with how great a force it can hold it suspended. For I do not know whether this gland is turned more slowly or more swiftly by the Mind than by the animal spirits, and whether the motions of the Passions, which we have closely joined with firm judgments, cannot again be detached from them by bodily causes; from which it would follow that, although the Mind had firmly resolved to go against dangers and had joined motions of boldness to this decree, nevertheless, upon seeing a danger, the gland would be so suspended that the Mind could think of nothing but flight. And indeed, since there is no ratio of will to motion, neither is there any comparison between the power or forces of Mind and Body; and consequently the forces of the latter can in no way be determined by the forces of the former.","kind":"passage","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_QGOH_PQDP:P0012"]},{"id":"RAW_QGOH_PQDP:P0013","text":"Add to these things that this gland is not found to be so situated in the middle of the brain that it can be turned so easily and in so many ways, and that not all the nerves extend as far as the cavities of the brain. Finally, I omit all that he asserts concerning the will and its freedom, since I have shown more than sufficiently that these things are false. Therefore, because the power of the Mind, as I have shown above, is defined by understanding alone, we shall determine the remedies for the affects—which, I believe, all indeed experience, but do not accurately observe or distinctly see—by the knowledge of the Mind alone, and from this same knowledge shall deduce all those things which concern its blessedness.","kind":"passage","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_QGOH_PQDP:P0013"]},{"id":"RAW_QGOH_PQDP:P0014","text":"AXIOMS","kind":"passage","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_QGOH_PQDP:P0014"]},{"id":"RAW_QGOH_PQDP:P0015","text":"I. If two contrary actions are excited in the same subject, a change must necessarily take place either in both or in one alone, until they cease to be contrary.","kind":"passage","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_QGOH_PQDP:P0015"]},{"id":"RAW_QGOH_PQDP:P0016","text":"II. The power of an effect is defined by the power of its cause, insofar as its essence is explained or defined through the essence of its cause.","kind":"passage","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_QGOH_PQDP:P0016"]},{"id":"RAW_QGOH_PQDP:P0017","text":"This Axiom is evident from Prop. 7, pt. 3.","kind":"passage","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_QGOH_PQDP:P0017"]},{"id":"RAW_QGOH_PQDP:P0018","text":"PROPOSITION I","kind":"passage","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_QGOH_PQDP:P0018"]},{"id":"RAW_QGOH_PQDP:P0019","text":"According as thoughts and the ideas of things are ordered and connected in the Mind, so the affections of the Body, or images of things, are exactly ordered and connected in the Body.","kind":"passage","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_QGOH_PQDP:P0019"]},{"id":"RAW_QGOH_PQDP:P0020","text":"DEMONSTRATION","kind":"passage","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_QGOH_PQDP:P0020"]},{"id":"RAW_QGOH_PQDP:P0021","text":"The order and connection of ideas is the same (by Prop. 7, pt. II) as the order and connection of things, and conversely the order and connection of things is the same (by Coroll. of Props. 6 and 7, pt. II) as the order and connection of ideas. Wherefore, just as the order and connection of ideas in the Mind takes place according to the order and concatenation of the affections of the Body (by Prop. 18, pt. II), so conversely (by Prop. 2, pt. III) the order and connection of the affections of the Body takes place according as thoughts and the ideas of things are ordered and connected in the Mind. Q. E. D.","kind":"passage","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_QGOH_PQDP:P0021"]},{"id":"RAW_QGOH_PQDP:P0022","text":"PROPOSITION II","kind":"passage","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_QGOH_PQDP:P0022"]},{"id":"RAW_QGOH_PQDP:P0023","text":"If we remove a commotion of the mind, or affect, from the thought of an external cause and join it to other thoughts, then Love or Hate toward the external cause, as also the fluctuations of mind which arise from these affects, will be destroyed.","kind":"passage","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_QGOH_PQDP:P0023"]},{"id":"RAW_QGOH_PQDP:P0024","text":"DEMONSTRATION","kind":"passage","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_QGOH_PQDP:P0024"]},{"id":"RAW_QGOH_PQDP:P0025","text":"For that which constitutes the form of Love or Hate is Joy or Sadness, accompanied by the idea of an external cause (by Defs. 6 and 7 of the Affects); therefore, when this is removed, the form of Love or Hate is simultaneously removed; and consequently these affects, and those which arise from them, are destroyed. Q. E. D.","kind":"passage","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_QGOH_PQDP:P0025"]},{"id":"RAW_QGOH_PQDP:P0026","text":"PROPOSITION III","kind":"passage","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_QGOH_PQDP:P0026"]},{"id":"RAW_QGOH_PQDP:P0027","text":"An affect which is a passion ceases to be a passion as soon as we form a clear and distinct idea of it.","kind":"passage","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_QGOH_PQDP:P0027"]},{"id":"RAW_QGOH_PQDP:P0028","text":"DEMONSTRATION","kind":"passage","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_QGOH_PQDP:P0028"]},{"id":"RAW_QGOH_PQDP:P0029","text":"An affect which is a passion is a confused idea (by the general Definition of the Affects). If, therefore, we form a clear and distinct idea of the affect itself, this idea will be distinguished from the affect itself, insofar as it is referred to the Mind alone, only by reason (by Prop. 21, Part II, with its Scholium); and consequently (by Prop. 3, Part III) the affect will cease to be a passion. Q. E. D.","kind":"passage","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_QGOH_PQDP:P0029"]},{"id":"RAW_QGOH_PQDP:P0030","text":"COROLLARY","kind":"passage","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_QGOH_PQDP:P0030"]},{"id":"RAW_QGOH_PQDP:P0031","text":"Therefore an affect is the more within our power, and the Mind suffers the less from it, the more it is known to us.","kind":"passage","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_QGOH_PQDP:P0031"]},{"id":"RAW_QGOH_PQDP:P0032","text":"PROPOSITION IV","kind":"passage","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_QGOH_PQDP:P0032"]},{"id":"RAW_QGOH_PQDP:P0033","text":"There is no affection of the Body of which we cannot form some clear and distinct concept.","kind":"passage","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_QGOH_PQDP:P0033"]},{"id":"RAW_QGOH_PQDP:P0034","text":"DEMONSTRATION","kind":"passage","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_QGOH_PQDP:P0034"]},{"id":"RAW_QGOH_PQDP:P0035","text":"Those things which are common to all cannot be conceived except adequately (by Prop. 38, Part II); and consequently (by Prop. 12 and Lemma 2, which is found after the Scholium to Prop. 13, Part II) there is no affection of the Body of which we cannot form some clear and distinct concept. Q. E. D.","kind":"passage","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_QGOH_PQDP:P0035"]},{"id":"RAW_QGOH_PQDP:P0036","text":"COROLLARY","kind":"passage","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_QGOH_PQDP:P0036"]},{"id":"RAW_QGOH_PQDP:P0037","text":"From this it follows that there is no affect of which we cannot form some clear and distinct concept. For an affect is the idea of an affection of the Body (by the general Definition of the Affects), which consequently (by the preceding Prop.) must involve some clear and distinct concept.","kind":"passage","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_QGOH_PQDP:P0037"]},{"id":"RAW_QGOH_PQDP:P0038","text":"SCHOLIUM","kind":"passage","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_QGOH_PQDP:P0038"]},{"id":"RAW_QGOH_PQDP:P0039","text":"Since nothing is given from which some effect does not follow (by Prop. 36, Part I), and whatever follows from an idea which is adequate in us, we understand all that clearly and distinctly (by Prop. 40, Part II); from this it follows that everyone has the power of understanding himself and his affects, if not absolutely, at least in part, clearly and distinctly, and consequently of bringing it about that he suffers less from them. Therefore, we must especially strive to know each affect, as far as possible, clearly and distinctly; so that thus the Mind may be determined by the affect to think upon those things which it clearly and distinctly perceives, and in which it is wholly at rest; and thus, that the affect itself may be separated from the thought of an external cause and joined to true thoughts; from which it will come about that not only Love, Hate, etc. are destroyed (by Prop.","kind":"passage","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_QGOH_PQDP:P0039"]},{"id":"RAW_QGOH_PQDP:P0040","text":"2 of this Part), but also that the appetites or Desires which are wont to arise from such an affect cannot have excess (by Prop. 61, Part IV). For it must especially be noted that the appetite by which a man is said both to act and to suffer is one and the same. For example, when we showed that human nature is so constituted that everyone desires that the rest should live according to his own disposition (see the Scholium to Prop. 31, Part III), this appetite, indeed, in a man who is not led by Reason, is a passion, which is called Ambition, and differs little from Pride; whereas, on the contrary, in a man who lives according to the dictate of Reason, it is an action or virtue, which is called Piety (see Schol. 1 to Prop. 37, Part IV, and the second Demonstration of the same Prop.). And in this way all appetites or Desires are passions only insofar as they arise from inadequate ideas; and the same are counted as virtues when they are stirred up or generated by adequate ideas.","kind":"passage","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_QGOH_PQDP:P0040"]},{"id":"RAW_QGOH_PQDP:P0041","text":"For all Desires by which we are determined to do something can arise both from adequate and from inadequate ideas (see Prop. 59, Part IV). And of this remedy for the affects (that is, the remedy which consists in their true knowledge), no other more excellent remedy which depends upon our power can be devised, since no other power of the Mind is given than that of thinking and forming adequate ideas, as we have shown above (by Prop. 3, Part III).","kind":"passage","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_QGOH_PQDP:P0041"]},{"id":"RAW_QGOH_PQDP:P0042","text":"PROPOSITION V","kind":"passage","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_QGOH_PQDP:P0042"]},{"id":"RAW_QGOH_PQDP:P0043","text":"An affect toward a thing which we imagine simply, and not as necessary, nor as possible, nor as contingent, is, other things being equal, the greatest of all.","kind":"passage","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_QGOH_PQDP:P0043"]},{"id":"RAW_QGOH_PQDP:P0044","text":"DEMONSTRATION","kind":"passage","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_QGOH_PQDP:P0044"]},{"id":"RAW_QGOH_PQDP:P0045","text":"An affect toward a thing which we imagine to be free is greater than toward a necessary thing (by Prop. 49, Part III), and consequently still greater than toward one which we imagine as possible or contingent (by Prop. 11, Part IV). But to imagine some thing as free can be nothing other than to imagine the thing simply, while we are ignorant of the causes by which it was determined to act (by what we have shown in the Scholium to Prop. 35, Part II); therefore an affect toward a thing which we imagine simply is, other things being equal, greater than toward a necessary, possible, or contingent thing, and consequently is the greatest. Q. E. D.","kind":"passage","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_QGOH_PQDP:P0045"]},{"id":"RAW_QGOH_PQDP:P0046","text":"PROPOSITION VI","kind":"passage","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_QGOH_PQDP:P0046"]},{"id":"RAW_QGOH_PQDP:P0047","text":"Insofar as the Mind understands all things as necessary, it has the greater power over affects, or suffers less from them.","kind":"passage","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_QGOH_PQDP:P0047"]},{"id":"RAW_QGOH_PQDP:P0048","text":"DEMONSTRATION","kind":"passage","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_QGOH_PQDP:P0048"]},{"id":"RAW_QGOH_PQDP:P0049","text":"The Mind understands that all things are necessary (by Prop. 29, Part I), and are determined to exist and act by an infinite nexus of causes (by Prop. 28, Part I); and therefore (by the preceding Prop.) it brings it about, insofar as this is concerned, that it suffers less from the affects which arise from them, and (by Prop. 48, Part III) is less affected toward them. Q. E. D.","kind":"passage","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_QGOH_PQDP:P0049"]},{"id":"RAW_QGOH_PQDP:P0050","text":"SCHOLIUM","kind":"passage","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_QGOH_PQDP:P0050"]},{"id":"RAW_QGOH_PQDP:P0051","text":"The more this knowledge—that is, that things are necessary—is concerned with singular things, which we imagine more distinctly and more vividly, the greater is this power of the Mind over the affects; as experience itself also testifies. For we see that Sadness at the loss of some good is mitigated as soon as the man who has lost it considers that that good could in no way have been preserved. Thus we also see that no one pities an infant because it does not know how to speak, walk, or reason, and because, finally, it lives for so many years as if unaware of itself. But if most people were born adults, and one or two as infants, then everyone would pity infants; because then he would regard infancy itself not as a natural and necessary thing, but as a defect or sin of Nature; and in this manner we could note several other things.","kind":"passage","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_QGOH_PQDP:P0051"]},{"id":"RAW_QGOH_PQDP:P0052","text":"PROPOSITION VII","kind":"passage","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_QGOH_PQDP:P0052"]},{"id":"RAW_QGOH_PQDP:P0053","text":"An affect which arises from, or is aroused by, Reason is, provided time is taken into account, more powerful than those which are related to singular things that we contemplate as absent.","kind":"passage","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_QGOH_PQDP:P0053"]},{"id":"RAW_QGOH_PQDP:P0054","text":"DEMONSTRATION","kind":"passage","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_QGOH_PQDP:P0054"]},{"id":"RAW_QGOH_PQDP:P0055","text":"We do not contemplate a thing as absent from the affect by which we imagine it, but from the fact that the Body is affected by another affection which excludes the existence of that same thing (by Prop. 17, Part II). Therefore an affect which is related to a thing that we contemplate as absent is not of such a nature as to surpass the other actions and power of a human being (concerning which see Prop. 6, Part IV); but, on the contrary, it is of such a nature as to be capable in some manner of being restrained by those affections which exclude the existence of its external cause (by Prop. 9, Part IV). But an affect which arises from Reason is necessarily related to the common properties of things (see the definition of Reason in the 2nd Schol. of Prop. 40, Part II), which we always contemplate as present (for nothing can be given that excludes their present existence), and which we always imagine in the same manner (by Prop. 38, Part II). Therefore such an affect always remains the same; and consequently (by Ax.","kind":"passage","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_QGOH_PQDP:P0055"]},{"id":"RAW_QGOH_PQDP:P0056","text":"1 of this Part) the affects which are contrary to it, and which are not fostered by their external causes, must accommodate themselves to it more and more, until they are no longer contrary; and insofar as this is the case, the affect which arises from Reason is more powerful. Q. E. D.","kind":"passage","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_QGOH_PQDP:P0056"]},{"id":"RAW_QGOH_PQDP:P0057","text":"PROPOSITION VIII","kind":"passage","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_QGOH_PQDP:P0057"]},{"id":"RAW_QGOH_PQDP:P0058","text":"The more causes simultaneously concur in arousing an affect, the greater it is.","kind":"passage","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_QGOH_PQDP:P0058"]},{"id":"RAW_QGOH_PQDP:P0059","text":"DEMONSTRATION","kind":"passage","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_QGOH_PQDP:P0059"]},{"id":"RAW_QGOH_PQDP:P0060","text":"Several causes acting together can do more than if they were fewer (by Prop. 7, Part III); and therefore (by Prop. 5, Part IV), the more causes simultaneously arouse an affect, the stronger it is. Q. E. D.","kind":"passage","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_QGOH_PQDP:P0060"]},{"id":"RAW_QGOH_PQDP:P0061","text":"SCHOLIUM","kind":"passage","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_QGOH_PQDP:P0061"]},{"id":"RAW_QGOH_PQDP:P0062","text":"This Proposition is also evident from Axiom 2 of this Part.","kind":"passage","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_QGOH_PQDP:P0062"]},{"id":"RAW_QGOH_PQDP:P0063","text":"PROPOSITION IX","kind":"passage","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_QGOH_PQDP:P0063"]},{"id":"RAW_QGOH_PQDP:P0064","text":"An affect which is related to several and diverse causes, which the Mind simultaneously contemplates together with the affect itself, is less harmful, we suffer less through it, and we are less affected toward each individual cause, than by another affect equally great which is related to one cause alone or to fewer causes.","kind":"passage","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_QGOH_PQDP:P0064"]},{"id":"RAW_QGOH_PQDP:P0065","text":"DEMONSTRATION","kind":"passage","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_QGOH_PQDP:P0065"]},{"id":"RAW_QGOH_PQDP:P0066","text":"An affect is bad or harmful only insofar as the Mind is hindered by it from being able to think (by Props. 26 and 27, Part IV); and therefore that affect by which the Mind is determined to contemplate several objects simultaneously is less harmful than another affect equally great which so holds the Mind fixed in the contemplation of one or fewer objects alone that it cannot think of others; which was the first point. Next, since the essence of the Mind, that is (by Prop. 7, Part III), its power, consists in thought alone (by Prop. 11, Part II), the Mind therefore suffers less through an affect by which it is determined to contemplate several things simultaneously than through an equally great affect which keeps the Mind occupied in the contemplation of one or fewer objects alone; which was the second point. Finally, this affect (by Prop. 48, Part III), insofar as it is related to several external causes, is also less toward each one of them. Q. E. D.","kind":"passage","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_QGOH_PQDP:P0066"]},{"id":"RAW_QGOH_PQDP:P0067","text":"PROPOSITION X","kind":"passage","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_QGOH_PQDP:P0067"]},{"id":"RAW_QGOH_PQDP:P0068","text":"So long as we are not in conflict with affects that are contrary to our nature, we have the power to order and connect the affections of the Body according to the order of the intellect.","kind":"passage","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_QGOH_PQDP:P0068"]},{"id":"RAW_QGOH_PQDP:P0069","text":"DEMONSTRATION","kind":"passage","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_QGOH_PQDP:P0069"]},{"id":"RAW_QGOH_PQDP:P0070","text":"Affects which are contrary to our nature, that is (by Prop. 30, Part IV), which are bad, are bad insofar as they hinder the Mind from understanding (by Prop. 27, Part IV). Therefore, so long as we are not in conflict with affects that are contrary to our nature, the power of the Mind by which it strives to understand things (by Prop. 26, Part IV) is not hindered; and thus, so long, it has the power to form clear and distinct ideas and to deduce some from others (see the 2nd Schol. of Prop. 40 and the Schol. of Prop. 47, Part II); and consequently (by Prop. 1 of this Part), so long we have the power to order and connect the affections of the Body according to the order of the intellect. Q. E. D.","kind":"passage","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_QGOH_PQDP:P0070"]},{"id":"RAW_QGOH_PQDP:P0071","text":"SCHOLIUM","kind":"passage","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_QGOH_PQDP:P0071"]},{"id":"RAW_QGOH_PQDP:P0072","text":"By this power of rightly ordering and connecting the affections of the Body, we can bring it about that we are not easily affected by bad affects. For (by Prop. 7 of this Part) greater force is required to restrain affects ordered and connected according to the order of the intellect than uncertain and fluctuating ones. Therefore the best thing that we can do, so long as we do not possess a perfect knowledge of our affects, is to conceive a right rule of life, or certain maxims of life, and commit them to memory, and continually apply them to particular things frequently encountered in life, so that our imagination may thereby be extensively affected by them and they may always be readily at hand. For example, among the maxims of life we have set down (see Prop. 46, Part IV, with its Scholium) that Hatred is to be conquered by Love, or Generosity, and not repaid with reciprocal Hatred.","kind":"passage","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_QGOH_PQDP:P0072"]},{"id":"RAW_QGOH_PQDP:P0073","text":"But in order that we may always have this prescription of Reason at hand when there is need, the common injuries of men must be thought over and frequently meditated upon, and how and by what way they are best repelled by Generosity; for thus we shall join the image of injury to the imagination of this doctrine, and it will always be at hand for us (by Prop. 18, Part II) whenever an injury is done to us. And if we shall also have at hand the account of our true advantage, and likewise of the good that follows from mutual friendship and common society, and further that from the right way of living there arises the greatest contentment of mind (by Prop. 52, Part","kind":"passage","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_QGOH_PQDP:P0073"]},{"id":"RAW_QGOH_PQDP:P0074","text":"IV), and that men, like all other things, act from the necessity of nature: then injury, or the Hatred that is wont to arise from it, will occupy the smallest part of the imagination, and will easily be overcome; or, if Anger, which is wont to arise from the greatest injuries, is not overcome so easily, it will nevertheless be overcome, although not without fluctuation of mind, in a far shorter space of time than if we had not thus premeditated these things, as is clear from Propositions 6, 7, and 8 of this Part. Concerning Courage for laying aside Fear, we must think in the same way; namely, the common dangers of life must be enumerated and frequently imagined, and how they can best be avoided and overcome by presence of mind and fortitude. But it must be noted that, in ordering our thoughts and images, we must always attend (by Coroll. Prop. 63, Part IV, and Prop. 59, Part III) to those things that are good in each thing, so that we may thus always be determined to act from an affect of Joy. For example,","kind":"passage","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_QGOH_PQDP:P0074"]},{"id":"RAW_QGOH_PQDP:P0075","text":"if someone sees that he pursues Glory too much, let him think of its right use, and for what end it ought to be pursued, and by what means it can be acquired; but not of its abuse, vanity, the inconstancy of men, or other such things, of which no one thinks unless from sickness of mind. For by such thoughts the most ambitious afflict themselves most of all when they despair of obtaining the honor at which they aim; and, while they vomit forth Anger, they wish to seem wise. Therefore it is certain that those are most desirous of glory who cry out most loudly about its abuse and the vanity of the world. Nor is this peculiar to the ambitious, but is common to all to whom fortune is adverse and who are powerless in mind. For even a poor, avaricious man does not cease to speak of the abuse of money and the vices of the rich; by which he accomplishes nothing other than to afflict himself and to show others that he bears with an ill-disposed mind not only his own poverty but also the riches of others.","kind":"passage","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_QGOH_PQDP:P0075"]},{"id":"RAW_QGOH_PQDP:P0076","text":"So too, those who have been badly received by their beloved think of nothing but the inconstancy and deceitful mind of women, and their other oft-rehearsed vices; all of which they immediately consign to oblivion as soon as they are received again by their beloved. He therefore who strives to moderate his affects and appetites from the sole love of Freedom will, as far as he can, endeavor to know the virtues and their causes, and to fill his mind with the joy that arises from their true knowledge; but by no means to contemplate the vices of men, to disparage men, and to rejoice in a false appearance of freedom. And whoever will carefully observe these things (for they are not difficult) and practice them, by Hercules, in a short time will generally be able to direct his actions by the command of Reason.","kind":"passage","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_QGOH_PQDP:P0076"]},{"id":"RAW_QGOH_PQDP:P0077","text":"PROPOSITION XI","kind":"passage","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_QGOH_PQDP:P0077"]},{"id":"RAW_QGOH_PQDP:P0078","text":"The more things an image is referred to, the more frequent it is, or the more often it is active, and the more it occupies the Mind.","kind":"passage","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_QGOH_PQDP:P0078"]},{"id":"RAW_QGOH_PQDP:P0079","text":"DEMONSTRATION","kind":"passage","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_QGOH_PQDP:P0079"]},{"id":"RAW_QGOH_PQDP:P0080","text":"For the more things an image or affect is referred to, the more causes are given by which it can be excited and fostered, all of which the Mind (by hypothesis) simultaneously contemplates from the affect itself; and therefore the more frequent the affect is, or the more often it is active, and (by Prop. 8 of this Part) the more it occupies the Mind. Q. E. D.","kind":"passage","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_QGOH_PQDP:P0080"]},{"id":"RAW_QGOH_PQDP:P0081","text":"PROPOSITION XII","kind":"passage","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_QGOH_PQDP:P0081"]},{"id":"RAW_QGOH_PQDP:P0082","text":"Images of things are more easily joined with images that are referred to things which we understand clearly and distinctly than with others.","kind":"passage","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_QGOH_PQDP:P0082"]},{"id":"RAW_QGOH_PQDP:P0083","text":"DEMONSTRATION","kind":"passage","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_QGOH_PQDP:P0083"]},{"id":"RAW_QGOH_PQDP:P0084","text":"The things which we understand clearly and distinctly are either common properties of things, or things deduced from them (see the definition of Reason in the 2nd Scholium of Prop. 40, Part II), and consequently they are more frequently excited in us (by the preceding Prop.); and therefore it can more easily happen that we contemplate other things simultaneously with these than with others, and consequently (by Prop. 18, Part II) that they are more easily joined with these than with others. Q. E. D.","kind":"passage","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_QGOH_PQDP:P0084"]},{"id":"RAW_QGOH_PQDP:P0085","text":"PROPOSITION XIII","kind":"passage","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_QGOH_PQDP:P0085"]},{"id":"RAW_QGOH_PQDP:P0086","text":"The more other images an image is joined with, the more often it is active.","kind":"passage","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_QGOH_PQDP:P0086"]},{"id":"RAW_QGOH_PQDP:P0087","text":"DEMONSTRATION","kind":"passage","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_QGOH_PQDP:P0087"]},{"id":"RAW_QGOH_PQDP:P0088","text":"For the more other images an image is joined with, the more causes are given (by Prop. 18, Part II) by which it can be excited. Q. E. D.","kind":"passage","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_QGOH_PQDP:P0088"]},{"id":"RAW_QGOH_PQDP:P0089","text":"PROPOSITION XIV","kind":"passage","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_QGOH_PQDP:P0089"]},{"id":"RAW_QGOH_PQDP:P0090","text":"The Mind can bring it about that all the affections of the Body, or images of things, are referred to the idea of God.","kind":"passage","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_QGOH_PQDP:P0090"]},{"id":"RAW_QGOH_PQDP:P0091","text":"DEMONSTRATION","kind":"passage","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_QGOH_PQDP:P0091"]},{"id":"RAW_QGOH_PQDP:P0092","text":"There is no affection of the Body of which the Mind cannot form some clear and distinct conception (by Prop. 4 of this Part); and therefore it can bring it about (by Prop. 15, Part I) that all are referred to the idea of God. Q. E. D.","kind":"passage","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_QGOH_PQDP:P0092"]},{"id":"RAW_QGOH_PQDP:P0093","text":"PROPOSITION XV","kind":"passage","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_QGOH_PQDP:P0093"]},{"id":"RAW_QGOH_PQDP:P0094","text":"He who clearly and distinctly understands himself and his affects loves God, and does so the more as he understands himself and his affects more.","kind":"passage","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_QGOH_PQDP:P0094"]},{"id":"RAW_QGOH_PQDP:P0095","text":"DEMONSTRATION","kind":"passage","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_QGOH_PQDP:P0095"]},{"id":"RAW_QGOH_PQDP:P0096","text":"He who clearly and distinctly understands himself and his affects rejoices (by Prop. 53, Part III), and this with the accompanying idea of God (by the preceding Prop.); and therefore (by Def. 6 of the Affects) he loves God, and, by the same reasoning, does so the more as he understands himself and his affects more. Q. E. D.","kind":"passage","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_QGOH_PQDP:P0096"]},{"id":"RAW_QGOH_PQDP:P0097","text":"PROPOSITION XVI","kind":"passage","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_QGOH_PQDP:P0097"]},{"id":"RAW_QGOH_PQDP:P0098","text":"This Love toward God ought to occupy the Mind most of all.","kind":"passage","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_QGOH_PQDP:P0098"]},{"id":"RAW_QGOH_PQDP:P0099","text":"DEMONSTRATION","kind":"passage","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_QGOH_PQDP:P0099"]},{"id":"RAW_QGOH_PQDP:P0100","text":"For this Love is joined to all the affections of the Body (by Prop. 14 of this Part), by all of which it is fostered (by Prop. 15 of this Part); and therefore (by Prop. 11 of this Part) it ought to occupy the Mind most of all. Q. E. D.","kind":"passage","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_QGOH_PQDP:P0100"]},{"id":"RAW_QGOH_PQDP:P0101","text":"PROPOSITION XVII","kind":"passage","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_QGOH_PQDP:P0101"]},{"id":"RAW_QGOH_PQDP:P0102","text":"God is free from passions, nor is He affected by any affect of Joy or Sadness.","kind":"passage","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_QGOH_PQDP:P0102"]},{"id":"RAW_QGOH_PQDP:P0103","text":"DEMONSTRATION","kind":"passage","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_QGOH_PQDP:P0103"]},{"id":"RAW_QGOH_PQDP:P0104","text":"For all ideas, insofar as they are referred to God, are true (by Prop. 32, Part II), that is (by Def. 4, Part II), adequate; and therefore (by the General Definition of the Affects) God is free from passions. Further, God can pass neither to greater nor to lesser perfection (by Corollary 2 of Prop. 20, Part I); and therefore (by Definitions 2 and 3 of the Affects) He is affected by no affect of Joy or Sadness. Q. E. D.","kind":"passage","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_QGOH_PQDP:P0104"]},{"id":"RAW_QGOH_PQDP:P0105","text":"COROLLARY","kind":"passage","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_QGOH_PQDP:P0105"]},{"id":"RAW_QGOH_PQDP:P0106","text":"Properly speaking, God loves no one and hates no one. For God (by the preceding Prop.) is affected by no affect of Joy or Sadness, and consequently (by Definitions 6 and 7 of the Affects) He also loves no one and hates no one.","kind":"passage","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_QGOH_PQDP:P0106"]},{"id":"RAW_QGOH_PQDP:P0107","text":"PROPOSITION XVIII","kind":"passage","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_QGOH_PQDP:P0107"]},{"id":"RAW_QGOH_PQDP:P0108","text":"No one can hate God.","kind":"passage","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_QGOH_PQDP:P0108"]},{"id":"RAW_QGOH_PQDP:P0109","text":"DEMONSTRATION","kind":"passage","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_QGOH_PQDP:P0109"]}],"source_digest":"72e60fa018e40af4663ca5f743182e22870e2bfc8098786a7506fbf093468a64","source_language_code":"la","original_title":"Pars quinta - De potentia intellectus seu de libertate humana · passage 1"}],"passage_basis":"frozen-grounding","original_title":"Pars quinta - De potentia intellectus seu de libertate humana · passage 1","title_language_code":"en"},{"id":"RAW_QGOH_PQ_6","title":"Part Five - On the Power of the Intellect, or Human Freedom · passage 2","order":20,"chapter_id":"CH_QGOH_PQ_6","text":"Idea Dei, quæ in nobis est, est adæquata et perfecta (per Prop. 46 et 47 p. II) ; adeoque quatenus Deum contemplamur eatenus agimus (per Prop. 3 p. III) ; et consequenter (per Prop. 59 p. III) nulla potest dari Tristitia concomitante idea Dei, hoc est (per 7 Affect. Defin.), nemo Deum odio habere potest. Q. E. D.\n\nCOROLLARIUM\n\nAmor erga Deum in odium verti nequit.\n\nSCHOLIUM\n\nAt objici potest, quod, dum Deum omnium rerum causam intelligimus, eo ipso Deum Tristitiæ causam consideramus. Sed ad hoc respondeo, quod quatenus Tristitiæ causas intelligimus, eatenus (per Prop. 3 hujus) ipsa desinit esse passio, hoc est (per Prop. 59 p. III), eatenus desinit esse Tristitia ; atque adeo, quatenus Deum Tristitiæ causam esse intelligimus, eatenus lætamur.\n\nPROPOSITIO XIX\n\nQui Deum amat, conari non potest, ut Deus ipsum contra amet.\n\nDEMONSTRATIO\n\nSi homo id conaretur, cuperet ergo (per Coroll. Prop. 17 hujus), ut Deus, quem amat, non esset Deus, et consequenter (per Prop. 19 p. III) contristari cuperet, quod (per Prop. 28 p. III) est absurdum. Ergo, qui Deum amat etc. Q. E. D.\n\nPROPOSITIO XX\n\nHic erga Deum Amor, neque Invidiæ neque Zelotypiæ affectu inquinari potest ; sed eo magis fovetur, quo plures homines eodem Amoris vinculo cum Deo junctos imaginamur.\n\nDEMONSTRATIO\n\nHic erga Deum Amor summum bonum est, quod ex dictamine Rationis appetere possumus (per Prop. 28 p. IV), et omnibus hominibus commune est (per Prop. 36 p. IV), et omnes ut eodem gaudeant cupimus (per Prop. 37 p. IV) ; atque adeo (per 23 Affect. Defin.) Invidiæ affectu maculari nequit, neque etiam (per Prop. 18 hujus, et defin. Zelotypiæ, quam vide in Schol. Prop. 35 p. III) Zelotypiæ affectu ; sed contra (per Prop. 31 p. III) eo magis foveri debet, quo plures homines eodem gaudere imaginamur. Q. E. D.\n\nSCHOLIUM\n\nPossumus hoc eodem modo ostendere, nullum dari affectum, qui huic Amori directe sit contrarius, a quo hic ipse Amor possit destrui, atque adeo concludere possumus hunc, erga Deum Amorem omnium affectuum est constantissimum, nec, quatenus ad Corpus refertur, posse destrui, nisi cum ipso Corpore. Cujus autem naturæ sit, quatenus ad solam Mentem refertur, postea videbimus.\n\nAtque his omnia affectuum remedia, sive id omne, quod Mens, in se sola considerata, adversus affectus potest, comprehendi ; ex quibus apparet, Mentis in affectus potentiam consistere, \nI° in ipsa affectuum cognitione (vide Schol. Prop. 4 hujus) ; \nII° in eo, quod affectus a cogitatione causæ externæ, quam confuse imaginamur, separat (vide Prop. 2 cum eodem Schol. Prop. 4 hujus) ; \nIII° in tempore, quo affectiones, quæ ad res, quas intelligimus referuntur, illas superant, quæ ad res referuntur, quas confuse seu mutilate concipimus (vide Prop. 7 hujus) ; \nIV° in multitudine causarum, a quibus affectiones, quæ ad rerum communes proprietates vel ad Deum referuntur, foventur (vide Prop. 9 et 11 hujus) ; \nV° denique in ordine, quo Mens suos affectus ordinare et invicem concatenare potest (vide Schol. Prop. 10 et insuper Prop. 12, 13 et 14 hujus).\nSed ut hæc Mentis in affectus potentia melius intelligatur, venit apprime notandum, quod affectus a nobis magni appellantur, quando unius hominis affectum cum affectu alterius comparamus, et unum magis quam alium eodem affectu conflictari videmus ; vel quando unius ejusdemque hominis affectus ad invicem comparamus, eundemque uno affectu magis quam alio affici sive moveri comperimus. Nam (per Prop. 5 p. IV) vis cujuscunque affectus definitur potentia causæ externæ cum nostra comparata. At Mentis potentia sola cognitione definitur, impotentia autem seu passio a sola cognitionis privatione, hoc est ab eo, per quod ideæ dicuntur inadæquatæ, æstimatur ; ex quo sequitur, Mentem illam maxime pati, cujus maximam partem ideæ inadæquatæ constituunt ita ut, magis per id, quod patitur, quam per id, quod agit, dignoscatur ; et illam contra maxime agere, cujus maximam partem ideæ adæquatæ constituunt, ita ut, quamvis huic tot inadæquatæ ideæ quam illi insint, magis tamen per illas, quæ humanæ virtuti tribuuntur, quam per has, quæ humanam impotentiam arguunt, dignoscatur. Deinde notandum, animi ægritudines et infortunia potissimum originem trahere ex nimio Amore erga rem, quæ multis variationibus est obnoxia, et cujus nunquam compotes esse possumus. Nam nemo de re ulla, nisi quam amat, sollicitus anxiusve est ; neque injuriæ, suspiciones, inimicitiæ, etc. oriuntur, nisi ex Amore erga res, quarum nemo potest revera esse compos. Ex his itaque facile concipimus, quid clara et distincta cognitio, et præcipue tertium illud cognitionis genus (de quo vide Schol. Prop. 47 p. II) cujus fundamentum est ipsa Dei cognitio, in affectus potest ; quos nempe, quatenus passiones sunt, si non absolute tollit (vide Prop. 3 cum Schol. Prop. 4 hujus), saltem efficit, ut minimam Mentis partem constituant (vide Prop. 14 hujus). Deinde Amorem gignit erga rem immutabilem et æternam (vide Prop. 15 hujus), et cujus revera sumus compotes (vide Prop. 45 p. II) ; qui propterea nullis vitiis, quæ in communi Amore insunt, inquinari, sed semper major ac major esse potest (per Prop. 15 hujus), et Mentis maximam partem occupare (per Prop. 16 hujus), lateque afficere. Atque his omnia, quæ præsentem hanc vitam spectant, absolvi. Nam quod in hujus Scholii principio dixi, me his paucis omnia affectuum remedia amplexum esse, facile poterit unusquisque videre, qui ad hæc, quæ in hoc Scholio diximus, et simul ad Mentis ejusque affectuum definitiones, et denique ad Propositiones 1 et 3 Partis III attenderit. Tempus igitur jam est, ut ad illa transeam, quæ ad Mentis durationem sine relatione ad Corporis existentiam pertinent.\n\nPROPOSITIO XXI\n\nMens nihil imaginari potest, neque rerum præteritarum recordari, nisi durante Corpore.\n\nDEMONSTRATIO\n\nMens actualem sui Corporis existentiam non exprimit, neque etiam Corporis affectiones ut actuales concipit, nisi durante Corpore (per Coroll. Prop. 8 p. II) ; et consequenter (per Prop. 26 p. II) nullum corpus ut actu existens concipit, nisi durante suo Corpore ; ac proinde nihil imaginari (vide Imaginat. defin. in Schol. Prop. 17 p. II) ne que rerum præteritarum recordari potest, nisi durante Corpore (vide defin. Memoriæ in Schol. Prop. 18 p. II). Q. E. D.\n\nPROPOSITIO XXII\n\nIn Deo tamen datur necessario idea, quæ hujus et illius Corporis humani essentiam sub æternitatis specie exprimit.\n\nDEMONSTRATIO\n\nDeus non tantum est causa hujus et illius Corporis humani existentiæ, sed etiam essentiæ (per Prop. 25 p. I) ; quæ propterea per ipsam Dei essentiam necessario debet concipi (per Axiom. 4 p. I), idque æterna quadam necessitate (per Prop. 16 p. I) ; qui quidem conceptus necessario in Deo dari debet (per Prop. 3 p. II). Q. E. D.\n\nPROPOSITIO XXIII\n\nMens humana non potest cum Corpore absolute destrui, sed ejus aliquid remanet, quod æternum est.\n\nDEMONSTRATIO\n\nIn Deo datur necessario conceptus seu idea, quæ Corporis humani essentiam exprimit (per Prop. præced.), quæ propterea aliquid necessario est, quod ad essentiam Mentis humanæ pertinet (per Prop. 13 p. II). Sed Menti humanæ nullam durationem, quæ tempore definiri potest, tribuimus, nisi quatenus Corporis actualem existentiam, quæ per durationem explicatur et tempore definiri potest, exprimit ; hoc est (per Coroll. Prop. 8 p. II), ipsi durationem non tribuimus, nisi durante Corpore. Cum tamen aliquid nihilominus sit id, quod æterna quadam necessitate per ipsam Dei essentiam concipitur (per Prop. præced.), erit necessario hoc aliquid, quod ad Mentis essentiam pertinet, æternum. Q. E. D.\n\nSCHOLIUM\n\nEst, uti diximus, hæc idea quæ Corporis essentiam sub specie æternitatis exprimit, certus cogitandi modus, qui ad Mentis essentiam pertinet, quique necessario æternus est. Nec tamen fieri potest, ut recordemur nos ante Corpus exstitisse, quandoquidem nec in Corpore ulla ejus vestigia dari possunt, nec æternitas tempore definiri, nec ullam ad tempus relationem habere potest. At nihilominus sentimus experimurque, nos æternos esse. Nam Mens non minus res illas sentit, quas intelligendo concipit, quam quas in memoria habet. Mentis enim oculi, quibus res videt observatque, sunt ipsæ demonstrationes. Quamvis itaque non recordemur, nos ante Corpus exstitisse, sentimus tamen, Mentem nostram, quatenus Corporis essentiam sub æternitatis specie involvit, æternam esse, et hanc ejus existentiam tempore definiri, sive per durationem explicari, non posse. Mens igitur nostra eatenus tantum potest dici durare, ejusque existentia certo tempore definiri potest, quatenus actualem Corporis existentiam involvit ; et eatenus tantum potentiam habet rerum existentiam tempore determinandi, easque sub duratione concipiendi.\n\nPROPOSITIO XXIV\n\nQuo magis res singulares intelligimus, eo magis Deum intelligimus.\n\nDEMONSTRATIO\n\nPatet ex Coroll. Prop. 25 p. I.\n\nPROPOSITIO XXV\n\nSummus Mentis conatus summaque virtus est, res intelligere tertio cognitionis genere.\n\nDEMONSTRATIO\n\nTertium cognitionis genus procedit ab adæquata idea quorundam Dei attributorum ad adæquatam cognitionem essentiæ rerum (vide hujus defin. in 2 Schol. Prop. 40 p. II) ; et quo magis hoc modo res intelligimus, eo magis (per Prop. præced.) Deum intelligimus ; ac proinde (per Prop. 28 p. IV) summa Mentis virtus, hoc est (per Defin. 8 p. IV) Mentis potentia seu natura, sive (per Prop. 7 p. III) summus conatus, est, res intelligere tertio cognitionis genere. Q. E. D.\n\nPROPOSITIO XXVI\n\nQuo Mens aptior est ad res tertio cognitionis genere intelligendum, eo magis cupit, res eodem hoc cognitionis genere intelligere.\n\nDEMONSTRATIO\n\nPatet. Nam quatenus concipimus, Mentem aptam esse ad res hoc cognitionis genere intelligendum, eatenus eandem determinatam concipimus ad res eodem cognitionis genere intelligendum ; et consequenter (per 1 Affect. Defin.), quo Mens ad hoc aptior est, eo magis hoc cupit. Q. E. D.\n\nPROPOSITIO XXVII\n\nEx hoc tertio cognitionis genere summa, quæ dari potest, Mentis acquiescentia, oritur.\n\nDEMONSTRATIO\n\nSumma Mentis virtus est Deum cognoscere (per Prop. 28 p. IV), sive res tertio cognitionis genere intelligere (per Prop. 25 hujus) ; quæ quidem virtus eo major est, quo Mens hoc cognitionis genere magis res cognoscit (per Prop. 24 hujus) ; adeoque qui res hoc cognitionis genere cognoscit, is ad summam humanam perfectionem transit, et consequenter (per 2 Affect. Defin.) summa Lætitia afficitur, idque (per Prop. 43 p. II) concomitante idea sui suæque virtutis ; ac proinde (per 25 Affect. Defin.) ex hoc cognitionis genere summa quæ dari potest, oritur acquiescentia. Q. E. D.\n\nPROPOSITIO XXVIII\n\nConatus seu Cupiditas cognoscendi res tertio cognitionis genere oriri non potest ex primo, at quidem ex secundo cognitionis genere.\n\nDEMONSTRATIO\n\nHæc propositio per se patet. Nam quicquid clare et distincte intelligimus, id vel per se vel per illud, quod per se concipitur, intelligimus hoc est, ideæ, quæ in nobis claræ et distinctæ sunt, sive quæ ad tertium cognitionis genus referuntur (vide 2 Schol. Prop. 40 p. II), non possunt sequi ex ideis mutilatis et confusis, quæ (per idem Schol.) ad primum cognitionis genus referuntur, sed ex ideis adæquatis, sive (per idem Schol.) ex secundo et tertio cognitionis genere ; ac proinde (per 1 Affect. Defin.) Cupiditas cognoscendi res tertio cognitionis genere non potest oriri ex primo, at quidem ex secundo. Q. E. D.\n\nPROPOSITIO XXIX\n\nQuicquid Mens sub specie æternitatis intelligit, id ex eo non intelligit, quod Corporis præsentem actualem existentiam concipit, sed ex eo, quod Corporis essentiam concipit sub specie æternitatis.\n\nDEMONSTRATIO\n\nQuatenus Mens præsentem sui Corporis existentiam concipit, eatenus durationem concipit, quæ tempore determinari potest, et eatenus tantum potentiam habet concipiendi res cum relatione ad tempus (per Prop. 21 hujus et Prop. 26 p. II). At æternitas per durationem explicari nequit (per Defin. 8 p. I et ipsius explicationem). Ergo Mens eatenus potestatem non habet concipiendi res sub specie æternitatis ; sed quia de natura Rationis est, res sub specie æternitatis concipere (per 2 Coroll. Prop. 44 p. II), et ad Mentis naturam etiam pertinet, Corporis essentiam sub specie æternitatis concipere (per Prop. 23 hujus), et præter hæc duo nihil aliud ad Mentis essentiam pertinet (per Prop. 13 p. II) ; Ergo hæc potentia concipiendi res sub specie æternitatis ad Mentem non pertinet, nisi quatenus Corporis essentiam sub specie æternitatis concipit. Q. E. D.\n\nSCHOLIUM\n\nRes duobus modis a nobis ut actuales concipiuntur : vel quatenus easdem cum relatione ad certum tempus et locum existere, vel quatenus ipsas in Deo contineri, et ex naturæ divinæ necessitate consequi concipimus. Quæ autem hoc secundo modo ut veræ seu reales concipiuntur, eas sub æternitatis specie concipimus, et earum ideæ æternam et infinitam Dei essentiam involvunt, ut Propositione 45 Partis II ostendimus, cujus etiam Scholium vide.\n\nPROPOSITIO XXX\n\nMens nostra, quatenus se et Corpus sub æternitatis specie cognoscit, eatenus Dei cognitionem necessario habet, scitque se in Deo esse et per Deum concipi.\n\nDEMONSTRATIO\n\nÆternitas est ipsa Dei essentia, quatenus hæc necessariam involvit existentiam (per Defin. 8 p. I). Res igitur sub specie æternitatis concipere est res concipere, quatenus per Dei essentiam ut entia realia concipiuntur, sive quatenus per Dei essentiam involvunt existentiam ; adeoque Mens nostra, quatenus se et Corpus sub specie æternitatis concipit, eatenus Dei cognitionem necessario habet, scitque, etc. Q. E. D.\n\nPROPOSITIO XXXI\n\nTertium cognitionis genus pendet a Mente, tanquam a formali causa, quatenus Mens ipsa æterna est.\n\nDEMONSTRATIO\n\nMens nihil sub æternitatis specie concipit, nisi quatenus sui Corporis essentiam sub æternitatis specie concipit (per Prop. 29 hujus), hoc est (per Prop. 21 et 23 hujus), nisi quatenus æterna est ; adeoque (per Prop. præced.), quatenus æterna est, Dei habet cognitionem ; quæ quidem cognitio est necessario adæquata (per Prop. 46 p. II) ; ac proinde Mens, quatenus æterna est, ad illa omnia cognoscendum est apta, quæ ex data hac Dei cognitione consequi possunt (per Prop. 40 p. II), hoc est ad res tertio cognitionis genere cognoscendum (vide hujus defin. in 2 Schol. Prop. 40 p. II), cujus propterea Mens (per Defin. 1 p. III), quatenus æterna est, causa est adæquata seu formalis. Q. E. D.\n\nSCHOLIUM\n\nQuo igitur unusquisque hoc cognitionis genere plus pollet, eo melius sui et Dei conscius est, hoc est, eo est perfectior et beatior, quod adhuc clarius ex seqq. patebit. Sed hic notandum, quod, tametsi jam certi sumus, Mentem æternam esse, quatenus res sub æternitatis specie concipit, nos tamen, ut ea, quæ ostendere volumus, facilius explicentur et melius intelligantur, ipsam, tanquam jam inciperet esse, et res sub æternitatis specie intelligere jam inciperet, considerabimus, ut huc usque fecimus ; quod nobis absque ullo erroris periculo facere licet, modo nobis cautio sit nihil concludere, nisi ex perspicuis præmissis.\n\nPROPOSITIO XXXII\n\nQuicquid intelligimus tertio cognitionis genere, eo delectamur, et quidem concomitante idea Dei tanquam causa.\n\nDEMONSTRATIO\n\nEx hoc cognitionis genere summa, quæ dari potest, Mentis acquiescentia, hoc est (per 25 Affect. Defin.) Lætitia, oritur, eaque concomitante idea sui (per Prop. 27 hujus), et consequenter (per Prop. 30 hujus) concomitante etiam idea Dei tanquam causa. Q. E. D.\n\nCOROLLARIUM\n\nEx tertio cognitionis genere oritur necessario Amor Dei intellectualis. Nam ex hoc cognitionis genere oritur (per Prop. præced.) Lætitia concomitante idea Dei tanquam causa, hoc est (per 6 Affect. Defin.) Amor Dei, non quatenus ipsum ut præsentem imaginamur (per Prop. 29 hujus), sed quatenus Deum æternum esse intelligimus ; et hoc est, quod Amorem Dei intellectualem voco.\n\nPROPOSITIO XXXIII\n\nAmor Dei intellectualis, qui ex tertio cognitionis genere oritur, est æternus.\n\nDEMONSTRATIO\n\nTertium enim cognitionis genus (per Prop. 31 hujus, et Axiom. 3 p. I) est æternum ; adeoque (per idem Axiom. p. I) Amor, qui ex eodem oritur, est etiam necessario æternus. Q. E. D.\n\nSCHOLIUM\n\nQuamvis hic erga Deum Amor principium non habuerit (per Prop. præced.), habet tamen omnes Amoris perfectiones, perinde ac si ortus fuisset, sicut in Coroll. Prop. præc. finximus. Nec ulla hic est differentia, nisi quod Mens easdem has perfectiones, quas eidem jam accedere finximus, æternas habuerit, idque concomitante idea Dei tanquam causa æterna. Quod si Lætitia in transitione ad majorem perfectionem consistit, Beatitudo sane in eo consistere debet, quod Mens ipsa perfectione sit prædita.\n\nPROPOSITIO XXXIV\n\nMens non nisi durante Corpore obnoxia est affectibus, qui ad passiones referuntur.\n\nDEMONSTRATIO\n\nImaginatio est idea, qua Mens rem aliquam ut præsentem contemplatur (vide ejus defin. in Schol. Prop. 17 p. II) ; quæ tamen magis Corporis humani præsentem constitutionem, quam rei externæ naturam indicat (per 2 Coroll. Prop. 16 p. II). Est igitur affectus (per gen. Affect. Defin.) imaginatio, quatenus Corporis præsentem constitutionem indicat ; atque adeo (per Prop. 21 hujus) Mens non nisi durante corpore obnoxia est affectibus, qui ad passiones referuntur. Q. E. D.\n\nCOROLLARIUM\n\nHinc sequitur, nullum Amorem præter Amorem intellectualem esse æternum.\n\nSCHOLIUM\n\nSi ad hominum communem opinionem attendamus, videbimus, eos suæ Mentis æternitatis esse quidem conscios, sed ipsos eandem cum duratione confundere, eamque imaginationi seu memoriæ tribuere, quam post mortem remanere credunt.\n\nPROPOSITIO XXXV\n\nDeus se ipsum Amore intellectuali infinito amat.\n\nDEMONSTRATIO\n\nDeus est absolute infinitus (per Defin. 6 p. I), hoc est (per Defin. 6 p. II), Dei natura gaudet infinita perfectione, idque (per Prop. 3 p. II) concomitante idea sui, hoc est (per Prop. 11 et Defin. 1 p. I) idea suæ causæ ; et hoc est, quod in Coroll. Prop. 32, hujus Amorem intellectualem esse diximus.\n\nPROPOSITIO XXXVI\n\nMentis Amor intellectualis erga Deum est ipse Dei Amor, quo Deus se ipsum amat, non quatenus infinitus est, sed quatenus per essentiam humanæ Mentis, sub specie æternitatis consideratam, explicari potest ; hoc est, Mentis erga Deum Amor intellectualis pars est infiniti amoris, quo Deus se ipsum amat.\n\nDEMONSTRATIO\n\nHic Mentis Amor ad Mentis actiones referri debet (per Coroll. Prop. 32 hujus, et per Prop. 3 p. III) ; qui proinde actio est, qua Mens se ipsam contemplatur, concomitante idea Dei tanquam causa (per Prop. 32 hujus et ejus Coroll.), hoc est (per Coroll. Prop. 25 p. I et Coroll. Prop. 11 p. II) actio, qua Deus, quatenus per Mentem humanam explicari potest, seipsum contemplatur, concomitante idea sui ; atque adeo (per Prop. præced.) hic Mentis Amor pars est infiniti amoris, quo Deus seipsum amat. Q. E. D.\n\nCOROLLARIUM\n\nHinc sequitur, quod Deus, quatenus seipsum amat, homines amat, et consequenter, quod Amor Dei erga homines, et Mentis erga Deum Amor intellectualis, unum et idem sit.\n\nSCHOLIUM\n\nEx his clare intelligimus, qua in re nostra salus, seu Beatitudo, seu Libertas consistit ; nempe in constanti et æterno erga Deum Amore, sive in Amore Dei erga homines. Atque hic Amor seu Beatitudo in Sacris codicibus Gloria appellatur, nec immerito. Nam sive hic Amor ad Deum referatur, sive ad Mentem, recte animi Acquiescentia quæ revera a Gloria (per 25 et 30 Affect. Defin.) non distinguitur, appellari potest. Nam quatenus ad Deum refertur, est (per Prop. 35 hujus) Lætitia, liceat hoc adhuc vocabulo uti, concomitante idea sui, ut et quatenus ad Mentem refertur (per Prop. 27 hujus). Deinde quia Nostræ Mentis essentia in sola cognitione consistit, cujus principium et fundamentum Deus est (per Prop. 15 p. I et Schol. Prop. 47 p. II), hinc perspicuum nobis fit, quomodo et qua ratione Mens nostra secundum essentiam et existentiam ex natura divina sequatur, et continuo a Deo pendeat ; quod hic notare operæ pretium duxi, ut hoc exemplo ostenderem, quam rerum singularium cognitio, quam intuitivam sive tertii generis appellavi (vide 2 Schol. Prop. 40 p. II), polleat, potiorque sit cognitione universali, quam secundi generis esse dixi. Nam quamvis in Prima Parte generaliter ostenderim, omnia (et consequenter Mentem etiam humanam) a Deo secundum essentiam, et existentiam pendere ; illa tamen demonstratio, tametsi legitima sit et extra dubitationis aleam posita, non ita tamen Mentem nostram afficit, quam quando id ipsum ex ipsa essentia rei cujuscunque singularis quam a Deo pendere dicimus, concluditur.\n\nPROPOSITIO XXXVII\n\nNihil in Natura datur, quod huic Amori intellectuali sit contrarium, sive quod ipsum possit tollere.\n\nDEMONSTRATIO\n\nHic intellectualis Amor ex Mentis natura necessario sequitur, quatenus ipsa, ut æterna veritas, per Dei naturam consideratur (per Prop. 33 et 29 hujus). Si quid ergo daretur, quod huic Amori esset contrarium, id contrarium esset vero ; et consequenter id, quod hunc Amorem posset tollere, efficeret, ut id, quod verum est, falsum esset ; quod (ut per se notum) est absurdum. Ergo nihil in natura datur, etc. Q. E. D.\n\nSCHOLIUM\n\nPartis Quartæ Axioma res singulares respicit, quatenus cum relatione ad certum tempus et locum considerantur ; de quo neminem dubitare credo.\n\nPROPOSITIO XXXVIII\n\nQuo plures res secundo et tertio cognitionis genere Mens intelligit, eo minus ipsa ab affectibus, qui mali sunt, patitur, et mortem minus timet.\n\nDEMONSTRATIO\n\nMentis essentia in cognitione consistit (per Prop. 11 p. II) ; quo igitur Mens plures res cognoscit secundo et tertio cognitionis genere, eo major ejus pars remanet (per Prop. 29 et 23 hujus), et consequenter (per Prop. præced.) eo major ejus pars non tangitur ab affectibus, qui nostræ naturæ sunt contrarii, hoc est (per Prop. 30 p. IV) qui mali sunt. Quo itaque Mens plures res secundo et tertio cognitionis genere intelligit, eo major ejus pars illæsa manet, et consequenter minus ab affectibus patitur, etc. Q. E. D.\n\nSCHOLIUM\n\nHinc intelligimus id, quod in Schol. Prop. 39 p. IV attigi, et quod in hac Parte explicare promisi ; nempe quod mors eo minus est noxia, quo Mentis clara et distincta cognitio major est, et consequenter, quo Mens magis Deum amat. Deinde quia (per Prop. 27 hujus) ex tertio cognitionis genere summa, quæ dari potest, oritur acquiescentia, hinc sequitur, Mentem humanam posse ejus naturæ esse, ut id, quod ejus cum Corpore perire ostendimus (vide Prop. 21 hujus), in respectu ad id, quod ipsius remanet, nullius sit momenti. Sed de his mox prolixius.\n\nPROPOSITIO XXXIX\n\nQui Corpus ad plurima aptum habet, is Mentem habet, cujus maxima pars est æterna.\n\nDEMONSTRATIO\n\nQui Corpus ad plurima agendum aptum habet, is minime affectibus, qui mali sunt, conflictatur (per Prop. 38 p. IV), hoc est (per Prop. 30 p. IV) affectibus, qui naturæ nostræ sunt contrarii ; atque adeo (per Prop. 10 hujus) potestatem habet ordinandi et concatenandi Corporis affectiones secundum ordinem ad intellectum, et consequenter efficiendi (per Prop. 14 hujus), ut omnes Corporis affectiones ad Dei ideam referantur ; ex quo fiet (per Prop. 15 hujus), ut erga Deum afficiatur Amore, qui (per Prop. 16 hujus) Mentis maximam partem occupare sive constituere debet, ac proinde (per Prop. 33 hujus) Mentem habet cujus maxima pars est æterna. Q. E. D.\n\nSCHOLIUM","paragraphs":[{"id":"RAW_QGOH_PQ_6:P0001","text":"Idea Dei, quæ in nobis est, est adæquata et perfecta (per Prop. 46 et 47 p. II) ; adeoque quatenus Deum contemplamur eatenus agimus (per Prop. 3 p. III) ; et consequenter (per Prop. 59 p. III) nulla potest dari Tristitia concomitante idea Dei, hoc est (per 7 Affect. Defin.), nemo Deum odio habere potest. Q. E. D.","kind":"passage"},{"id":"RAW_QGOH_PQ_6:P0002","text":"COROLLARIUM","kind":"passage"},{"id":"RAW_QGOH_PQ_6:P0003","text":"Amor erga Deum in odium verti nequit.","kind":"passage"},{"id":"RAW_QGOH_PQ_6:P0004","text":"SCHOLIUM","kind":"passage"},{"id":"RAW_QGOH_PQ_6:P0005","text":"At objici potest, quod, dum Deum omnium rerum causam intelligimus, eo ipso Deum Tristitiæ causam consideramus. Sed ad hoc respondeo, quod quatenus Tristitiæ causas intelligimus, eatenus (per Prop. 3 hujus) ipsa desinit esse passio, hoc est (per Prop. 59 p. III), eatenus desinit esse Tristitia ; atque adeo, quatenus Deum Tristitiæ causam esse intelligimus, eatenus lætamur.","kind":"passage"},{"id":"RAW_QGOH_PQ_6:P0006","text":"PROPOSITIO XIX","kind":"passage"},{"id":"RAW_QGOH_PQ_6:P0007","text":"Qui Deum amat, conari non potest, ut Deus ipsum contra amet.","kind":"passage"},{"id":"RAW_QGOH_PQ_6:P0008","text":"DEMONSTRATIO","kind":"passage"},{"id":"RAW_QGOH_PQ_6:P0009","text":"Si homo id conaretur, cuperet ergo (per Coroll. Prop. 17 hujus), ut Deus, quem amat, non esset Deus, et consequenter (per Prop. 19 p. III) contristari cuperet, quod (per Prop. 28 p. III) est absurdum. Ergo, qui Deum amat etc. Q. E. D.","kind":"passage"},{"id":"RAW_QGOH_PQ_6:P0010","text":"PROPOSITIO XX","kind":"passage"},{"id":"RAW_QGOH_PQ_6:P0011","text":"Hic erga Deum Amor, neque Invidiæ neque Zelotypiæ affectu inquinari potest ; sed eo magis fovetur, quo plures homines eodem Amoris vinculo cum Deo junctos imaginamur.","kind":"passage"},{"id":"RAW_QGOH_PQ_6:P0012","text":"DEMONSTRATIO","kind":"passage"},{"id":"RAW_QGOH_PQ_6:P0013","text":"Hic erga Deum Amor summum bonum est, quod ex dictamine Rationis appetere possumus (per Prop. 28 p. IV), et omnibus hominibus commune est (per Prop. 36 p. IV), et omnes ut eodem gaudeant cupimus (per Prop. 37 p. IV) ; atque adeo (per 23 Affect. Defin.) Invidiæ affectu maculari nequit, neque etiam (per Prop. 18 hujus, et defin. Zelotypiæ, quam vide in Schol. Prop. 35 p. III) Zelotypiæ affectu ; sed contra (per Prop. 31 p. III) eo magis foveri debet, quo plures homines eodem gaudere imaginamur. Q. E. D.","kind":"passage"},{"id":"RAW_QGOH_PQ_6:P0014","text":"SCHOLIUM","kind":"passage"},{"id":"RAW_QGOH_PQ_6:P0015","text":"Possumus hoc eodem modo ostendere, nullum dari affectum, qui huic Amori directe sit contrarius, a quo hic ipse Amor possit destrui, atque adeo concludere possumus hunc, erga Deum Amorem omnium affectuum est constantissimum, nec, quatenus ad Corpus refertur, posse destrui, nisi cum ipso Corpore. Cujus autem naturæ sit, quatenus ad solam Mentem refertur, postea videbimus.","kind":"passage"},{"id":"RAW_QGOH_PQ_6:P0016","text":"Atque his omnia affectuum remedia, sive id omne, quod Mens, in se sola considerata, adversus affectus potest, comprehendi ; ex quibus apparet, Mentis in affectus potentiam consistere, \nI° in ipsa affectuum cognitione (vide Schol. Prop. 4 hujus) ; \nII° in eo, quod affectus a cogitatione causæ externæ, quam confuse imaginamur, separat (vide Prop. 2 cum eodem Schol. Prop. 4 hujus) ; \nIII° in tempore, quo affectiones, quæ ad res, quas intelligimus referuntur, illas superant, quæ ad res referuntur, quas confuse seu mutilate concipimus (vide Prop. 7 hujus) ; \nIV° in multitudine causarum, a quibus affectiones, quæ ad rerum communes proprietates vel ad Deum referuntur, foventur (vide Prop. 9 et 11 hujus) ; \nV° denique in ordine, quo Mens suos affectus ordinare et invicem concatenare potest (vide Schol. Prop. 10 et insuper Prop. 12, 13 et 14 hujus).","kind":"passage"},{"id":"RAW_QGOH_PQ_6:P0017","text":"Sed ut hæc Mentis in affectus potentia melius intelligatur, venit apprime notandum, quod affectus a nobis magni appellantur, quando unius hominis affectum cum affectu alterius comparamus, et unum magis quam alium eodem affectu conflictari videmus ; vel quando unius ejusdemque hominis affectus ad invicem comparamus, eundemque uno affectu magis quam alio affici sive moveri comperimus. Nam (per Prop. 5 p. IV) vis cujuscunque affectus definitur potentia causæ externæ cum nostra comparata.","kind":"passage"},{"id":"RAW_QGOH_PQ_6:P0018","text":"At Mentis potentia sola cognitione definitur, impotentia autem seu passio a sola cognitionis privatione, hoc est ab eo, per quod ideæ dicuntur inadæquatæ, æstimatur ; ex quo sequitur, Mentem illam maxime pati, cujus maximam partem ideæ inadæquatæ constituunt ita ut, magis per id, quod patitur, quam per id, quod agit, dignoscatur ; et illam contra maxime agere, cujus maximam partem ideæ adæquatæ constituunt, ita ut, quamvis huic tot inadæquatæ ideæ quam illi insint, magis tamen per illas, quæ humanæ virtuti tribuuntur, quam per has, quæ humanam impotentiam arguunt, dignoscatur. Deinde notandum, animi ægritudines et infortunia potissimum originem trahere ex nimio Amore erga rem, quæ multis variationibus est obnoxia, et cujus nunquam compotes esse possumus. Nam nemo de re ulla, nisi quam amat, sollicitus anxiusve est ; neque injuriæ, suspiciones, inimicitiæ, etc.","kind":"passage"},{"id":"RAW_QGOH_PQ_6:P0019","text":"oriuntur, nisi ex Amore erga res, quarum nemo potest revera esse compos. Ex his itaque facile concipimus, quid clara et distincta cognitio, et præcipue tertium illud cognitionis genus (de quo vide Schol. Prop. 47 p. II) cujus fundamentum est ipsa Dei cognitio, in affectus potest ; quos nempe, quatenus passiones sunt, si non absolute tollit (vide Prop. 3 cum Schol. Prop. 4 hujus), saltem efficit, ut minimam Mentis partem constituant (vide Prop. 14 hujus). Deinde Amorem gignit erga rem immutabilem et æternam (vide Prop. 15 hujus), et cujus revera sumus compotes (vide Prop. 45 p. II) ; qui propterea nullis vitiis, quæ in communi Amore insunt, inquinari, sed semper major ac major esse potest (per Prop. 15 hujus), et Mentis maximam partem occupare (per Prop. 16 hujus), lateque afficere. Atque his omnia, quæ præsentem hanc vitam spectant, absolvi.","kind":"passage"},{"id":"RAW_QGOH_PQ_6:P0020","text":"Nam quod in hujus Scholii principio dixi, me his paucis omnia affectuum remedia amplexum esse, facile poterit unusquisque videre, qui ad hæc, quæ in hoc Scholio diximus, et simul ad Mentis ejusque affectuum definitiones, et denique ad Propositiones 1 et 3 Partis III attenderit. Tempus igitur jam est, ut ad illa transeam, quæ ad Mentis durationem sine relatione ad Corporis existentiam pertinent.","kind":"passage"},{"id":"RAW_QGOH_PQ_6:P0021","text":"PROPOSITIO XXI","kind":"passage"},{"id":"RAW_QGOH_PQ_6:P0022","text":"Mens nihil imaginari potest, neque rerum præteritarum recordari, nisi durante Corpore.","kind":"passage"},{"id":"RAW_QGOH_PQ_6:P0023","text":"DEMONSTRATIO","kind":"passage"},{"id":"RAW_QGOH_PQ_6:P0024","text":"Mens actualem sui Corporis existentiam non exprimit, neque etiam Corporis affectiones ut actuales concipit, nisi durante Corpore (per Coroll. Prop. 8 p. II) ; et consequenter (per Prop. 26 p. II) nullum corpus ut actu existens concipit, nisi durante suo Corpore ; ac proinde nihil imaginari (vide Imaginat. defin. in Schol. Prop. 17 p. II) ne que rerum præteritarum recordari potest, nisi durante Corpore (vide defin. Memoriæ in Schol. Prop. 18 p. II). Q. E. D.","kind":"passage"},{"id":"RAW_QGOH_PQ_6:P0025","text":"PROPOSITIO XXII","kind":"passage"},{"id":"RAW_QGOH_PQ_6:P0026","text":"In Deo tamen datur necessario idea, quæ hujus et illius Corporis humani essentiam sub æternitatis specie exprimit.","kind":"passage"},{"id":"RAW_QGOH_PQ_6:P0027","text":"DEMONSTRATIO","kind":"passage"},{"id":"RAW_QGOH_PQ_6:P0028","text":"Deus non tantum est causa hujus et illius Corporis humani existentiæ, sed etiam essentiæ (per Prop. 25 p. I) ; quæ propterea per ipsam Dei essentiam necessario debet concipi (per Axiom. 4 p. I), idque æterna quadam necessitate (per Prop. 16 p. I) ; qui quidem conceptus necessario in Deo dari debet (per Prop. 3 p. II). Q. E. D.","kind":"passage"},{"id":"RAW_QGOH_PQ_6:P0029","text":"PROPOSITIO XXIII","kind":"passage"},{"id":"RAW_QGOH_PQ_6:P0030","text":"Mens humana non potest cum Corpore absolute destrui, sed ejus aliquid remanet, quod æternum est.","kind":"passage"},{"id":"RAW_QGOH_PQ_6:P0031","text":"DEMONSTRATIO","kind":"passage"},{"id":"RAW_QGOH_PQ_6:P0032","text":"In Deo datur necessario conceptus seu idea, quæ Corporis humani essentiam exprimit (per Prop. præced.), quæ propterea aliquid necessario est, quod ad essentiam Mentis humanæ pertinet (per Prop. 13 p. II). Sed Menti humanæ nullam durationem, quæ tempore definiri potest, tribuimus, nisi quatenus Corporis actualem existentiam, quæ per durationem explicatur et tempore definiri potest, exprimit ; hoc est (per Coroll. Prop. 8 p. II), ipsi durationem non tribuimus, nisi durante Corpore. Cum tamen aliquid nihilominus sit id, quod æterna quadam necessitate per ipsam Dei essentiam concipitur (per Prop. præced.), erit necessario hoc aliquid, quod ad Mentis essentiam pertinet, æternum. Q. E. D.","kind":"passage"},{"id":"RAW_QGOH_PQ_6:P0033","text":"SCHOLIUM","kind":"passage"},{"id":"RAW_QGOH_PQ_6:P0034","text":"Est, uti diximus, hæc idea quæ Corporis essentiam sub specie æternitatis exprimit, certus cogitandi modus, qui ad Mentis essentiam pertinet, quique necessario æternus est. Nec tamen fieri potest, ut recordemur nos ante Corpus exstitisse, quandoquidem nec in Corpore ulla ejus vestigia dari possunt, nec æternitas tempore definiri, nec ullam ad tempus relationem habere potest. At nihilominus sentimus experimurque, nos æternos esse. Nam Mens non minus res illas sentit, quas intelligendo concipit, quam quas in memoria habet. Mentis enim oculi, quibus res videt observatque, sunt ipsæ demonstrationes. Quamvis itaque non recordemur, nos ante Corpus exstitisse, sentimus tamen, Mentem nostram, quatenus Corporis essentiam sub æternitatis specie involvit, æternam esse, et hanc ejus existentiam tempore definiri, sive per durationem explicari, non posse.","kind":"passage"},{"id":"RAW_QGOH_PQ_6:P0035","text":"Mens igitur nostra eatenus tantum potest dici durare, ejusque existentia certo tempore definiri potest, quatenus actualem Corporis existentiam involvit ; et eatenus tantum potentiam habet rerum existentiam tempore determinandi, easque sub duratione concipiendi.","kind":"passage"},{"id":"RAW_QGOH_PQ_6:P0036","text":"PROPOSITIO XXIV","kind":"passage"},{"id":"RAW_QGOH_PQ_6:P0037","text":"Quo magis res singulares intelligimus, eo magis Deum intelligimus.","kind":"passage"},{"id":"RAW_QGOH_PQ_6:P0038","text":"DEMONSTRATIO","kind":"passage"},{"id":"RAW_QGOH_PQ_6:P0039","text":"Patet ex Coroll. Prop. 25 p. I.","kind":"passage"},{"id":"RAW_QGOH_PQ_6:P0040","text":"PROPOSITIO XXV","kind":"passage"},{"id":"RAW_QGOH_PQ_6:P0041","text":"Summus Mentis conatus summaque virtus est, res intelligere tertio cognitionis genere.","kind":"passage"},{"id":"RAW_QGOH_PQ_6:P0042","text":"DEMONSTRATIO","kind":"passage"},{"id":"RAW_QGOH_PQ_6:P0043","text":"Tertium cognitionis genus procedit ab adæquata idea quorundam Dei attributorum ad adæquatam cognitionem essentiæ rerum (vide hujus defin. in 2 Schol. Prop. 40 p. II) ; et quo magis hoc modo res intelligimus, eo magis (per Prop. præced.) Deum intelligimus ; ac proinde (per Prop. 28 p. IV) summa Mentis virtus, hoc est (per Defin. 8 p. IV) Mentis potentia seu natura, sive (per Prop. 7 p. III) summus conatus, est, res intelligere tertio cognitionis genere. Q. E. D.","kind":"passage"},{"id":"RAW_QGOH_PQ_6:P0044","text":"PROPOSITIO XXVI","kind":"passage"},{"id":"RAW_QGOH_PQ_6:P0045","text":"Quo Mens aptior est ad res tertio cognitionis genere intelligendum, eo magis cupit, res eodem hoc cognitionis genere intelligere.","kind":"passage"},{"id":"RAW_QGOH_PQ_6:P0046","text":"DEMONSTRATIO","kind":"passage"},{"id":"RAW_QGOH_PQ_6:P0047","text":"Patet. Nam quatenus concipimus, Mentem aptam esse ad res hoc cognitionis genere intelligendum, eatenus eandem determinatam concipimus ad res eodem cognitionis genere intelligendum ; et consequenter (per 1 Affect. Defin.), quo Mens ad hoc aptior est, eo magis hoc cupit. Q. E. D.","kind":"passage"},{"id":"RAW_QGOH_PQ_6:P0048","text":"PROPOSITIO XXVII","kind":"passage"},{"id":"RAW_QGOH_PQ_6:P0049","text":"Ex hoc tertio cognitionis genere summa, quæ dari potest, Mentis acquiescentia, oritur.","kind":"passage"},{"id":"RAW_QGOH_PQ_6:P0050","text":"DEMONSTRATIO","kind":"passage"},{"id":"RAW_QGOH_PQ_6:P0051","text":"Summa Mentis virtus est Deum cognoscere (per Prop. 28 p. IV), sive res tertio cognitionis genere intelligere (per Prop. 25 hujus) ; quæ quidem virtus eo major est, quo Mens hoc cognitionis genere magis res cognoscit (per Prop. 24 hujus) ; adeoque qui res hoc cognitionis genere cognoscit, is ad summam humanam perfectionem transit, et consequenter (per 2 Affect. Defin.) summa Lætitia afficitur, idque (per Prop. 43 p. II) concomitante idea sui suæque virtutis ; ac proinde (per 25 Affect. Defin.) ex hoc cognitionis genere summa quæ dari potest, oritur acquiescentia. Q. E. D.","kind":"passage"},{"id":"RAW_QGOH_PQ_6:P0052","text":"PROPOSITIO XXVIII","kind":"passage"},{"id":"RAW_QGOH_PQ_6:P0053","text":"Conatus seu Cupiditas cognoscendi res tertio cognitionis genere oriri non potest ex primo, at quidem ex secundo cognitionis genere.","kind":"passage"},{"id":"RAW_QGOH_PQ_6:P0054","text":"DEMONSTRATIO","kind":"passage"},{"id":"RAW_QGOH_PQ_6:P0055","text":"Hæc propositio per se patet. Nam quicquid clare et distincte intelligimus, id vel per se vel per illud, quod per se concipitur, intelligimus hoc est, ideæ, quæ in nobis claræ et distinctæ sunt, sive quæ ad tertium cognitionis genus referuntur (vide 2 Schol. Prop. 40 p. II), non possunt sequi ex ideis mutilatis et confusis, quæ (per idem Schol.) ad primum cognitionis genus referuntur, sed ex ideis adæquatis, sive (per idem Schol.) ex secundo et tertio cognitionis genere ; ac proinde (per 1 Affect. Defin.) Cupiditas cognoscendi res tertio cognitionis genere non potest oriri ex primo, at quidem ex secundo. Q. E. D.","kind":"passage"},{"id":"RAW_QGOH_PQ_6:P0056","text":"PROPOSITIO XXIX","kind":"passage"},{"id":"RAW_QGOH_PQ_6:P0057","text":"Quicquid Mens sub specie æternitatis intelligit, id ex eo non intelligit, quod Corporis præsentem actualem existentiam concipit, sed ex eo, quod Corporis essentiam concipit sub specie æternitatis.","kind":"passage"},{"id":"RAW_QGOH_PQ_6:P0058","text":"DEMONSTRATIO","kind":"passage"},{"id":"RAW_QGOH_PQ_6:P0059","text":"Quatenus Mens præsentem sui Corporis existentiam concipit, eatenus durationem concipit, quæ tempore determinari potest, et eatenus tantum potentiam habet concipiendi res cum relatione ad tempus (per Prop. 21 hujus et Prop. 26 p. II). At æternitas per durationem explicari nequit (per Defin. 8 p. I et ipsius explicationem). Ergo Mens eatenus potestatem non habet concipiendi res sub specie æternitatis ; sed quia de natura Rationis est, res sub specie æternitatis concipere (per 2 Coroll. Prop. 44 p. II), et ad Mentis naturam etiam pertinet, Corporis essentiam sub specie æternitatis concipere (per Prop. 23 hujus), et præter hæc duo nihil aliud ad Mentis essentiam pertinet (per Prop. 13 p. II) ; Ergo hæc potentia concipiendi res sub specie æternitatis ad Mentem non pertinet, nisi quatenus Corporis essentiam sub specie æternitatis concipit. Q. E. D.","kind":"passage"},{"id":"RAW_QGOH_PQ_6:P0060","text":"SCHOLIUM","kind":"passage"},{"id":"RAW_QGOH_PQ_6:P0061","text":"Res duobus modis a nobis ut actuales concipiuntur : vel quatenus easdem cum relatione ad certum tempus et locum existere, vel quatenus ipsas in Deo contineri, et ex naturæ divinæ necessitate consequi concipimus. Quæ autem hoc secundo modo ut veræ seu reales concipiuntur, eas sub æternitatis specie concipimus, et earum ideæ æternam et infinitam Dei essentiam involvunt, ut Propositione 45 Partis II ostendimus, cujus etiam Scholium vide.","kind":"passage"},{"id":"RAW_QGOH_PQ_6:P0062","text":"PROPOSITIO XXX","kind":"passage"},{"id":"RAW_QGOH_PQ_6:P0063","text":"Mens nostra, quatenus se et Corpus sub æternitatis specie cognoscit, eatenus Dei cognitionem necessario habet, scitque se in Deo esse et per Deum concipi.","kind":"passage"},{"id":"RAW_QGOH_PQ_6:P0064","text":"DEMONSTRATIO","kind":"passage"},{"id":"RAW_QGOH_PQ_6:P0065","text":"Æternitas est ipsa Dei essentia, quatenus hæc necessariam involvit existentiam (per Defin. 8 p. I). Res igitur sub specie æternitatis concipere est res concipere, quatenus per Dei essentiam ut entia realia concipiuntur, sive quatenus per Dei essentiam involvunt existentiam ; adeoque Mens nostra, quatenus se et Corpus sub specie æternitatis concipit, eatenus Dei cognitionem necessario habet, scitque, etc. Q. E. D.","kind":"passage"},{"id":"RAW_QGOH_PQ_6:P0066","text":"PROPOSITIO XXXI","kind":"passage"},{"id":"RAW_QGOH_PQ_6:P0067","text":"Tertium cognitionis genus pendet a Mente, tanquam a formali causa, quatenus Mens ipsa æterna est.","kind":"passage"},{"id":"RAW_QGOH_PQ_6:P0068","text":"DEMONSTRATIO","kind":"passage"},{"id":"RAW_QGOH_PQ_6:P0069","text":"Mens nihil sub æternitatis specie concipit, nisi quatenus sui Corporis essentiam sub æternitatis specie concipit (per Prop. 29 hujus), hoc est (per Prop. 21 et 23 hujus), nisi quatenus æterna est ; adeoque (per Prop. præced.), quatenus æterna est, Dei habet cognitionem ; quæ quidem cognitio est necessario adæquata (per Prop. 46 p. II) ; ac proinde Mens, quatenus æterna est, ad illa omnia cognoscendum est apta, quæ ex data hac Dei cognitione consequi possunt (per Prop. 40 p. II), hoc est ad res tertio cognitionis genere cognoscendum (vide hujus defin. in 2 Schol. Prop. 40 p. II), cujus propterea Mens (per Defin. 1 p. III), quatenus æterna est, causa est adæquata seu formalis. Q. E. D.","kind":"passage"},{"id":"RAW_QGOH_PQ_6:P0070","text":"SCHOLIUM","kind":"passage"},{"id":"RAW_QGOH_PQ_6:P0071","text":"Quo igitur unusquisque hoc cognitionis genere plus pollet, eo melius sui et Dei conscius est, hoc est, eo est perfectior et beatior, quod adhuc clarius ex seqq. patebit. Sed hic notandum, quod, tametsi jam certi sumus, Mentem æternam esse, quatenus res sub æternitatis specie concipit, nos tamen, ut ea, quæ ostendere volumus, facilius explicentur et melius intelligantur, ipsam, tanquam jam inciperet esse, et res sub æternitatis specie intelligere jam inciperet, considerabimus, ut huc usque fecimus ; quod nobis absque ullo erroris periculo facere licet, modo nobis cautio sit nihil concludere, nisi ex perspicuis præmissis.","kind":"passage"},{"id":"RAW_QGOH_PQ_6:P0072","text":"PROPOSITIO XXXII","kind":"passage"},{"id":"RAW_QGOH_PQ_6:P0073","text":"Quicquid intelligimus tertio cognitionis genere, eo delectamur, et quidem concomitante idea Dei tanquam causa.","kind":"passage"},{"id":"RAW_QGOH_PQ_6:P0074","text":"DEMONSTRATIO","kind":"passage"},{"id":"RAW_QGOH_PQ_6:P0075","text":"Ex hoc cognitionis genere summa, quæ dari potest, Mentis acquiescentia, hoc est (per 25 Affect. Defin.) Lætitia, oritur, eaque concomitante idea sui (per Prop. 27 hujus), et consequenter (per Prop. 30 hujus) concomitante etiam idea Dei tanquam causa. Q. E. D.","kind":"passage"},{"id":"RAW_QGOH_PQ_6:P0076","text":"COROLLARIUM","kind":"passage"},{"id":"RAW_QGOH_PQ_6:P0077","text":"Ex tertio cognitionis genere oritur necessario Amor Dei intellectualis. Nam ex hoc cognitionis genere oritur (per Prop. præced.) Lætitia concomitante idea Dei tanquam causa, hoc est (per 6 Affect. Defin.) Amor Dei, non quatenus ipsum ut præsentem imaginamur (per Prop. 29 hujus), sed quatenus Deum æternum esse intelligimus ; et hoc est, quod Amorem Dei intellectualem voco.","kind":"passage"},{"id":"RAW_QGOH_PQ_6:P0078","text":"PROPOSITIO XXXIII","kind":"passage"},{"id":"RAW_QGOH_PQ_6:P0079","text":"Amor Dei intellectualis, qui ex tertio cognitionis genere oritur, est æternus.","kind":"passage"},{"id":"RAW_QGOH_PQ_6:P0080","text":"DEMONSTRATIO","kind":"passage"},{"id":"RAW_QGOH_PQ_6:P0081","text":"Tertium enim cognitionis genus (per Prop. 31 hujus, et Axiom. 3 p. I) est æternum ; adeoque (per idem Axiom. p. I) Amor, qui ex eodem oritur, est etiam necessario æternus. Q. E. D.","kind":"passage"},{"id":"RAW_QGOH_PQ_6:P0082","text":"SCHOLIUM","kind":"passage"},{"id":"RAW_QGOH_PQ_6:P0083","text":"Quamvis hic erga Deum Amor principium non habuerit (per Prop. præced.), habet tamen omnes Amoris perfectiones, perinde ac si ortus fuisset, sicut in Coroll. Prop. præc. finximus. Nec ulla hic est differentia, nisi quod Mens easdem has perfectiones, quas eidem jam accedere finximus, æternas habuerit, idque concomitante idea Dei tanquam causa æterna. Quod si Lætitia in transitione ad majorem perfectionem consistit, Beatitudo sane in eo consistere debet, quod Mens ipsa perfectione sit prædita.","kind":"passage"},{"id":"RAW_QGOH_PQ_6:P0084","text":"PROPOSITIO XXXIV","kind":"passage"},{"id":"RAW_QGOH_PQ_6:P0085","text":"Mens non nisi durante Corpore obnoxia est affectibus, qui ad passiones referuntur.","kind":"passage"},{"id":"RAW_QGOH_PQ_6:P0086","text":"DEMONSTRATIO","kind":"passage"},{"id":"RAW_QGOH_PQ_6:P0087","text":"Imaginatio est idea, qua Mens rem aliquam ut præsentem contemplatur (vide ejus defin. in Schol. Prop. 17 p. II) ; quæ tamen magis Corporis humani præsentem constitutionem, quam rei externæ naturam indicat (per 2 Coroll. Prop. 16 p. II). Est igitur affectus (per gen. Affect. Defin.) imaginatio, quatenus Corporis præsentem constitutionem indicat ; atque adeo (per Prop. 21 hujus) Mens non nisi durante corpore obnoxia est affectibus, qui ad passiones referuntur. Q. E. D.","kind":"passage"},{"id":"RAW_QGOH_PQ_6:P0088","text":"COROLLARIUM","kind":"passage"},{"id":"RAW_QGOH_PQ_6:P0089","text":"Hinc sequitur, nullum Amorem præter Amorem intellectualem esse æternum.","kind":"passage"},{"id":"RAW_QGOH_PQ_6:P0090","text":"SCHOLIUM","kind":"passage"},{"id":"RAW_QGOH_PQ_6:P0091","text":"Si ad hominum communem opinionem attendamus, videbimus, eos suæ Mentis æternitatis esse quidem conscios, sed ipsos eandem cum duratione confundere, eamque imaginationi seu memoriæ tribuere, quam post mortem remanere credunt.","kind":"passage"},{"id":"RAW_QGOH_PQ_6:P0092","text":"PROPOSITIO XXXV","kind":"passage"},{"id":"RAW_QGOH_PQ_6:P0093","text":"Deus se ipsum Amore intellectuali infinito amat.","kind":"passage"},{"id":"RAW_QGOH_PQ_6:P0094","text":"DEMONSTRATIO","kind":"passage"},{"id":"RAW_QGOH_PQ_6:P0095","text":"Deus est absolute infinitus (per Defin. 6 p. I), hoc est (per Defin. 6 p. II), Dei natura gaudet infinita perfectione, idque (per Prop. 3 p. II) concomitante idea sui, hoc est (per Prop. 11 et Defin. 1 p. I) idea suæ causæ ; et hoc est, quod in Coroll. Prop. 32, hujus Amorem intellectualem esse diximus.","kind":"passage"},{"id":"RAW_QGOH_PQ_6:P0096","text":"PROPOSITIO XXXVI","kind":"passage"},{"id":"RAW_QGOH_PQ_6:P0097","text":"Mentis Amor intellectualis erga Deum est ipse Dei Amor, quo Deus se ipsum amat, non quatenus infinitus est, sed quatenus per essentiam humanæ Mentis, sub specie æternitatis consideratam, explicari potest ; hoc est, Mentis erga Deum Amor intellectualis pars est infiniti amoris, quo Deus se ipsum amat.","kind":"passage"},{"id":"RAW_QGOH_PQ_6:P0098","text":"DEMONSTRATIO","kind":"passage"},{"id":"RAW_QGOH_PQ_6:P0099","text":"Hic Mentis Amor ad Mentis actiones referri debet (per Coroll. Prop. 32 hujus, et per Prop. 3 p. III) ; qui proinde actio est, qua Mens se ipsam contemplatur, concomitante idea Dei tanquam causa (per Prop. 32 hujus et ejus Coroll.), hoc est (per Coroll. Prop. 25 p. I et Coroll. Prop. 11 p. II) actio, qua Deus, quatenus per Mentem humanam explicari potest, seipsum contemplatur, concomitante idea sui ; atque adeo (per Prop. præced.) hic Mentis Amor pars est infiniti amoris, quo Deus seipsum amat. Q. E. D.","kind":"passage"},{"id":"RAW_QGOH_PQ_6:P0100","text":"COROLLARIUM","kind":"passage"},{"id":"RAW_QGOH_PQ_6:P0101","text":"Hinc sequitur, quod Deus, quatenus seipsum amat, homines amat, et consequenter, quod Amor Dei erga homines, et Mentis erga Deum Amor intellectualis, unum et idem sit.","kind":"passage"},{"id":"RAW_QGOH_PQ_6:P0102","text":"SCHOLIUM","kind":"passage"},{"id":"RAW_QGOH_PQ_6:P0103","text":"Ex his clare intelligimus, qua in re nostra salus, seu Beatitudo, seu Libertas consistit ; nempe in constanti et æterno erga Deum Amore, sive in Amore Dei erga homines. Atque hic Amor seu Beatitudo in Sacris codicibus Gloria appellatur, nec immerito. Nam sive hic Amor ad Deum referatur, sive ad Mentem, recte animi Acquiescentia quæ revera a Gloria (per 25 et 30 Affect. Defin.) non distinguitur, appellari potest. Nam quatenus ad Deum refertur, est (per Prop. 35 hujus) Lætitia, liceat hoc adhuc vocabulo uti, concomitante idea sui, ut et quatenus ad Mentem refertur (per Prop. 27 hujus). Deinde quia Nostræ Mentis essentia in sola cognitione consistit, cujus principium et fundamentum Deus est (per Prop. 15 p. I et Schol. Prop. 47 p.","kind":"passage"},{"id":"RAW_QGOH_PQ_6:P0104","text":"II), hinc perspicuum nobis fit, quomodo et qua ratione Mens nostra secundum essentiam et existentiam ex natura divina sequatur, et continuo a Deo pendeat ; quod hic notare operæ pretium duxi, ut hoc exemplo ostenderem, quam rerum singularium cognitio, quam intuitivam sive tertii generis appellavi (vide 2 Schol. Prop. 40 p. II), polleat, potiorque sit cognitione universali, quam secundi generis esse dixi. Nam quamvis in Prima Parte generaliter ostenderim, omnia (et consequenter Mentem etiam humanam) a Deo secundum essentiam, et existentiam pendere ; illa tamen demonstratio, tametsi legitima sit et extra dubitationis aleam posita, non ita tamen Mentem nostram afficit, quam quando id ipsum ex ipsa essentia rei cujuscunque singularis quam a Deo pendere dicimus, concluditur.","kind":"passage"},{"id":"RAW_QGOH_PQ_6:P0105","text":"PROPOSITIO XXXVII","kind":"passage"},{"id":"RAW_QGOH_PQ_6:P0106","text":"Nihil in Natura datur, quod huic Amori intellectuali sit contrarium, sive quod ipsum possit tollere.","kind":"passage"},{"id":"RAW_QGOH_PQ_6:P0107","text":"DEMONSTRATIO","kind":"passage"},{"id":"RAW_QGOH_PQ_6:P0108","text":"Hic intellectualis Amor ex Mentis natura necessario sequitur, quatenus ipsa, ut æterna veritas, per Dei naturam consideratur (per Prop. 33 et 29 hujus). Si quid ergo daretur, quod huic Amori esset contrarium, id contrarium esset vero ; et consequenter id, quod hunc Amorem posset tollere, efficeret, ut id, quod verum est, falsum esset ; quod (ut per se notum) est absurdum. Ergo nihil in natura datur, etc. Q. E. D.","kind":"passage"},{"id":"RAW_QGOH_PQ_6:P0109","text":"SCHOLIUM","kind":"passage"},{"id":"RAW_QGOH_PQ_6:P0110","text":"Partis Quartæ Axioma res singulares respicit, quatenus cum relatione ad certum tempus et locum considerantur ; de quo neminem dubitare credo.","kind":"passage"},{"id":"RAW_QGOH_PQ_6:P0111","text":"PROPOSITIO XXXVIII","kind":"passage"},{"id":"RAW_QGOH_PQ_6:P0112","text":"Quo plures res secundo et tertio cognitionis genere Mens intelligit, eo minus ipsa ab affectibus, qui mali sunt, patitur, et mortem minus timet.","kind":"passage"},{"id":"RAW_QGOH_PQ_6:P0113","text":"DEMONSTRATIO","kind":"passage"},{"id":"RAW_QGOH_PQ_6:P0114","text":"Mentis essentia in cognitione consistit (per Prop. 11 p. II) ; quo igitur Mens plures res cognoscit secundo et tertio cognitionis genere, eo major ejus pars remanet (per Prop. 29 et 23 hujus), et consequenter (per Prop. præced.) eo major ejus pars non tangitur ab affectibus, qui nostræ naturæ sunt contrarii, hoc est (per Prop. 30 p. IV) qui mali sunt. Quo itaque Mens plures res secundo et tertio cognitionis genere intelligit, eo major ejus pars illæsa manet, et consequenter minus ab affectibus patitur, etc. Q. E. D.","kind":"passage"},{"id":"RAW_QGOH_PQ_6:P0115","text":"SCHOLIUM","kind":"passage"},{"id":"RAW_QGOH_PQ_6:P0116","text":"Hinc intelligimus id, quod in Schol. Prop. 39 p. IV attigi, et quod in hac Parte explicare promisi ; nempe quod mors eo minus est noxia, quo Mentis clara et distincta cognitio major est, et consequenter, quo Mens magis Deum amat. Deinde quia (per Prop. 27 hujus) ex tertio cognitionis genere summa, quæ dari potest, oritur acquiescentia, hinc sequitur, Mentem humanam posse ejus naturæ esse, ut id, quod ejus cum Corpore perire ostendimus (vide Prop. 21 hujus), in respectu ad id, quod ipsius remanet, nullius sit momenti. Sed de his mox prolixius.","kind":"passage"},{"id":"RAW_QGOH_PQ_6:P0117","text":"PROPOSITIO XXXIX","kind":"passage"},{"id":"RAW_QGOH_PQ_6:P0118","text":"Qui Corpus ad plurima aptum habet, is Mentem habet, cujus maxima pars est æterna.","kind":"passage"},{"id":"RAW_QGOH_PQ_6:P0119","text":"DEMONSTRATIO","kind":"passage"},{"id":"RAW_QGOH_PQ_6:P0120","text":"Qui Corpus ad plurima agendum aptum habet, is minime affectibus, qui mali sunt, conflictatur (per Prop. 38 p. IV), hoc est (per Prop. 30 p. IV) affectibus, qui naturæ nostræ sunt contrarii ; atque adeo (per Prop. 10 hujus) potestatem habet ordinandi et concatenandi Corporis affectiones secundum ordinem ad intellectum, et consequenter efficiendi (per Prop. 14 hujus), ut omnes Corporis affectiones ad Dei ideam referantur ; ex quo fiet (per Prop. 15 hujus), ut erga Deum afficiatur Amore, qui (per Prop. 16 hujus) Mentis maximam partem occupare sive constituere debet, ac proinde (per Prop. 33 hujus) Mentem habet cujus maxima pars est æterna. Q. E. D.","kind":"passage"},{"id":"RAW_QGOH_PQ_6:P0121","text":"SCHOLIUM","kind":"passage"}],"sha256":"55d7c6f634643446e668b79aa355c3917b8b3b5a27a52c7cc30e6e3c04a6fb2b","language_code":"la","language_name":"Latina","translations":[{"id":"SOURCE_TR_20261015T014404036947Z_9505491e","language_code":"en","language_name":"English","title":"Part Five - On the Power of the Intellect, or Human Freedom · passage 2","source_sha256":"55d7c6f634643446e668b79aa355c3917b8b3b5a27a52c7cc30e6e3c04a6fb2b","sha256":"f483f008abea0ef6f073a2c8684ef4972121e3f711b565c2067c4a6e077756b8","rendition_type":"machine","review_status":"machine","model":"gpt-5.6-terra","created":"2026-10-15T01:44:04.037096+00:00","paragraphs":[{"id":"RAW_QGOH_PQ_6:P0001","text":"The idea of God which is in us is adequate and perfect (by Props. 46 and 47, Pt. II); and therefore, insofar as we contemplate God, we act (by Prop. 3, Pt. III); and consequently (by Prop. 59, Pt. III), no Sadness can be given that is accompanied by the idea of God, that is (by Def. 7 of the Affects), no one can hate God. Q. E. D.","kind":"passage","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_QGOH_PQ_6:P0001"]},{"id":"RAW_QGOH_PQ_6:P0002","text":"COROLLARY","kind":"passage","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_QGOH_PQ_6:P0002"]},{"id":"RAW_QGOH_PQ_6:P0003","text":"Love toward God cannot be turned into hatred.","kind":"passage","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_QGOH_PQ_6:P0003"]},{"id":"RAW_QGOH_PQ_6:P0004","text":"SCHOLIUM","kind":"passage","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_QGOH_PQ_6:P0004"]},{"id":"RAW_QGOH_PQ_6:P0005","text":"But it may be objected that, while we understand God to be the cause of all things, we thereby consider God to be the cause of Sadness. But to this I reply that, insofar as we understand the causes of Sadness, it thereby (by Prop. 3 of this Part) ceases to be a passion, that is (by Prop. 59, Pt. III), it thereby ceases to be Sadness; and thus, insofar as we understand God to be the cause of Sadness, we rejoice.","kind":"passage","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_QGOH_PQ_6:P0005"]},{"id":"RAW_QGOH_PQ_6:P0006","text":"PROPOSITION XIX","kind":"passage","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_QGOH_PQ_6:P0006"]},{"id":"RAW_QGOH_PQ_6:P0007","text":"He who loves God cannot strive that God should love him in return.","kind":"passage","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_QGOH_PQ_6:P0007"]},{"id":"RAW_QGOH_PQ_6:P0008","text":"DEMONSTRATION","kind":"passage","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_QGOH_PQ_6:P0008"]},{"id":"RAW_QGOH_PQ_6:P0009","text":"If a man were to strive for this, he would therefore desire (by the Coroll. to Prop. 17 of this Part) that God, whom he loves, should not be God, and consequently (by Prop. 19, Pt. III) he would desire to be saddened, which (by Prop. 28, Pt. III) is absurd. Therefore, he who loves God, etc. Q. E. D.","kind":"passage","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_QGOH_PQ_6:P0009"]},{"id":"RAW_QGOH_PQ_6:P0010","text":"PROPOSITION XX","kind":"passage","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_QGOH_PQ_6:P0010"]},{"id":"RAW_QGOH_PQ_6:P0011","text":"This Love toward God can be stained neither by an affect of Envy nor of Jealousy; rather, it is nourished all the more as we imagine more men to be joined with God by the same bond of Love.","kind":"passage","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_QGOH_PQ_6:P0011"]},{"id":"RAW_QGOH_PQ_6:P0012","text":"DEMONSTRATION","kind":"passage","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_QGOH_PQ_6:P0012"]},{"id":"RAW_QGOH_PQ_6:P0013","text":"This Love toward God is the highest good which we can seek from the dictate of Reason (by Prop. 28, Pt. IV), and is common to all men (by Prop. 36, Pt. IV), and we desire that all should enjoy it equally (by Prop. 37, Pt. IV); and therefore (by Def. 23 of the Affects), it cannot be stained by an affect of Envy, nor also (by Prop. 18 of this Part, and the definition of Jealousy, which see in the Schol. to Prop. 35, Pt. III) by an affect of Jealousy; but, on the contrary (by Prop. 31, Pt. III), it must be nourished all the more as we imagine more men enjoying the same. Q. E. D.","kind":"passage","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_QGOH_PQ_6:P0013"]},{"id":"RAW_QGOH_PQ_6:P0014","text":"SCHOLIUM","kind":"passage","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_QGOH_PQ_6:P0014"]},{"id":"RAW_QGOH_PQ_6:P0015","text":"In the same manner, we can show that no affect is given which is directly contrary to this Love, by which this Love itself can be destroyed; and therefore we may conclude that this Love toward God is the most constant of all the affects, and, insofar as it is referred to the Body, cannot be destroyed except with the Body itself. But what its nature is insofar as it is referred to the Mind alone, we shall see later.","kind":"passage","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_QGOH_PQ_6:P0015"]},{"id":"RAW_QGOH_PQ_6:P0016","text":"And in these are comprehended all the remedies for the affects, or all that the Mind, considered in itself alone, can do against the affects; from which it is apparent that the power of the Mind over the affects consists,\nI° in the knowledge of the affects themselves (see the Schol. to Prop. 4 of this Part);\nII° in its separating an affect from the thought of an external cause which we imagine confusedly (see Prop. 2 together with the same Schol. to Prop. 4 of this Part);\nIII° in the time by which affections that are referred to things which we understand surpass those that are referred to things which we conceive confusedly or mutilatedly (see Prop. 7 of this Part);\nIV° in the multitude of causes by which affections that are referred to the common properties of things or to God are nourished (see Props. 9 and 11 of this Part);\nV° finally, in the order by which the Mind can order and concatenate its affects with one another (see the Schol. to Prop. 10 and moreover Props. 12, 13, and 14 of this Part).","kind":"passage","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_QGOH_PQ_6:P0016"]},{"id":"RAW_QGOH_PQ_6:P0017","text":"But in order that this power of the Mind over the affects may be better understood, it should especially be noted that affects are called great by us when we compare the affect of one man with the affect of another, and see one to be assailed by the same affect more than another; or when we compare the affects of one and the same man with one another, and find that he is affected or moved more by one affect than by another. For (by Prop. 5, Pt. IV) the force of any affect whatsoever is defined by the power of an external cause compared with our own.","kind":"passage","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_QGOH_PQ_6:P0017"]},{"id":"RAW_QGOH_PQ_6:P0018","text":"But the power of the Mind is defined by knowledge alone, whereas impotence, or passion, is estimated from the privation of knowledge alone, that is, from that by which ideas are called inadequate; from which it follows that that Mind suffers most whose greatest part is constituted by inadequate ideas, so that it is discerned more by what it suffers than by what it does; and, conversely, that Mind acts most whose greatest part is constituted by adequate ideas, so that, although as many inadequate ideas may be present in this Mind as in that, it is nevertheless discerned more by those ideas which are ascribed to human virtue than by these, which indicate human impotence. Next, it should be noted that sicknesses of the mind and misfortunes derive their origin chiefly from excessive Love toward a thing which is subject to many variations and of which we can never be masters. For no one is solicitous or anxious about any thing except that which he loves; nor do injuries, suspicions, enmities, etc.","kind":"passage","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_QGOH_PQ_6:P0018"]},{"id":"RAW_QGOH_PQ_6:P0019","text":"arise except from Love toward things of which no one can truly be master. From these points, therefore, we can easily conceive what clear and distinct knowledge, and especially that third kind of knowledge (concerning which see the Schol. to Prop. 47, Pt. II), whose foundation is the knowledge of God itself, can do against the affects: namely, insofar as they are passions, if it does not absolutely remove them (see Prop. 3 together with the Schol. to Prop. 4 of this Part), it at least brings it about that they constitute the smallest part of the Mind (see Prop. 14 of this Part). Further, it begets Love toward a thing immutable and eternal (see Prop. 15 of this Part), and of which we are truly masters (see Prop. 45, Pt. II); which Love, therefore, cannot be stained by any of the vices that are present in common Love, but can always become greater and greater (by Prop. 15 of this Part), occupy the greatest part of the Mind (by Prop. 16 of this Part), and affect it extensively. And with these matters, all that concerns the present life has been completed.","kind":"passage","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_QGOH_PQ_6:P0019"]},{"id":"RAW_QGOH_PQ_6:P0020","text":"For as to what I said at the beginning of this Scholium, that in these few things I had embraced all the remedies for the affects, anyone will readily be able to see this who has attended to those things which we have said in this Scholium, and at the same time to the definitions of the Mind and its affects, and finally to Propositions 1 and 3 of Part III. It is now time, therefore, for me to pass on to those matters which pertain to the duration of the Mind without relation to the existence of the Body.","kind":"passage","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_QGOH_PQ_6:P0020"]},{"id":"RAW_QGOH_PQ_6:P0021","text":"PROPOSITION XXI","kind":"passage","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_QGOH_PQ_6:P0021"]},{"id":"RAW_QGOH_PQ_6:P0022","text":"The Mind can imagine nothing, nor can it recollect past things, except while the Body endures.","kind":"passage","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_QGOH_PQ_6:P0022"]},{"id":"RAW_QGOH_PQ_6:P0023","text":"DEMONSTRATION","kind":"passage","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_QGOH_PQ_6:P0023"]},{"id":"RAW_QGOH_PQ_6:P0024","text":"The Mind does not express the actual existence of its Body, nor does it conceive the affections of the Body as actual, except while the Body endures (by the Coroll. to Prop. 8, Pt. II); and consequently (by Prop. 26, Pt. II), it conceives no body as actually existing except while its Body endures; and therefore it can imagine nothing (see the definition of Imagination in the Schol. to Prop. 17, Pt. II), nor recollect past things, except while the Body endures (see the definition of Memory in the Schol. to Prop. 18, Pt. II). Q. E. D.","kind":"passage","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_QGOH_PQ_6:P0024"]},{"id":"RAW_QGOH_PQ_6:P0025","text":"PROPOSITION XXII","kind":"passage","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_QGOH_PQ_6:P0025"]},{"id":"RAW_QGOH_PQ_6:P0026","text":"In God, nevertheless, there is necessarily given an idea which expresses, under a species of eternity, the essence of this and that human Body.","kind":"passage","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_QGOH_PQ_6:P0026"]},{"id":"RAW_QGOH_PQ_6:P0027","text":"DEMONSTRATION","kind":"passage","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_QGOH_PQ_6:P0027"]},{"id":"RAW_QGOH_PQ_6:P0028","text":"God is not only the cause of the existence of this and that human Body, but also of its essence (by Prop. 25, Part I); which must therefore necessarily be conceived through the very essence of God (by Axiom 4, Part I), and indeed by a certain eternal necessity (by Prop. 16, Part I); and this concept must necessarily be given in God (by Prop. 3, Part II). Q. E. D.","kind":"passage","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_QGOH_PQ_6:P0028"]},{"id":"RAW_QGOH_PQ_6:P0029","text":"PROPOSITION XXIII","kind":"passage","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_QGOH_PQ_6:P0029"]},{"id":"RAW_QGOH_PQ_6:P0030","text":"The human Mind cannot be absolutely destroyed with the Body, but there remains of it something which is eternal.","kind":"passage","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_QGOH_PQ_6:P0030"]},{"id":"RAW_QGOH_PQ_6:P0031","text":"DEMONSTRATION","kind":"passage","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_QGOH_PQ_6:P0031"]},{"id":"RAW_QGOH_PQ_6:P0032","text":"There is necessarily given in God a concept, or idea, which expresses the essence of the human Body (by the preceding Prop.); and this is therefore necessarily something which pertains to the essence of the human Mind (by Prop. 13, Part II). But we ascribe to the human Mind no duration that can be defined by time, except insofar as it expresses the actual existence of the Body, which is explicated through duration and can be defined by time; that is (by Coroll. to Prop. 8, Part II), we ascribe duration to it only while the Body endures. Nevertheless, since there is something which is conceived through the very essence of God by a certain eternal necessity (by the preceding Prop.), this something which pertains to the essence of the Mind will necessarily be eternal. Q. E. D.","kind":"passage","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_QGOH_PQ_6:P0032"]},{"id":"RAW_QGOH_PQ_6:P0033","text":"SCHOLIUM","kind":"passage","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_QGOH_PQ_6:P0033"]},{"id":"RAW_QGOH_PQ_6:P0034","text":"As we have said, this idea which expresses the essence of the Body under a species of eternity is a certain mode of thinking which pertains to the essence of the Mind, and which is necessarily eternal. Yet it cannot come about that we remember having existed before the Body, since no traces of this can be given in the Body, and eternity can neither be defined by time nor have any relation to time. Nevertheless, we feel and experience that we are eternal. For the Mind senses things which it conceives by understanding no less than those which it has in memory. For the eyes of the Mind, by which it sees and observes things, are demonstrations themselves. Therefore, although we do not remember having existed before the Body, we nevertheless feel that our Mind, insofar as it involves the essence of the Body under a species of eternity, is eternal, and that this existence of it cannot be defined by time, or explicated through duration.","kind":"passage","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_QGOH_PQ_6:P0034"]},{"id":"RAW_QGOH_PQ_6:P0035","text":"Therefore our Mind can be said to endure only insofar as it involves the actual existence of the Body, and only insofar can its existence be defined by a determinate time; and only insofar does it have the power to determine the existence of things by time, and to conceive them under duration.","kind":"passage","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_QGOH_PQ_6:P0035"]},{"id":"RAW_QGOH_PQ_6:P0036","text":"PROPOSITION XXIV","kind":"passage","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_QGOH_PQ_6:P0036"]},{"id":"RAW_QGOH_PQ_6:P0037","text":"The more we understand singular things, the more we understand God.","kind":"passage","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_QGOH_PQ_6:P0037"]},{"id":"RAW_QGOH_PQ_6:P0038","text":"DEMONSTRATION","kind":"passage","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_QGOH_PQ_6:P0038"]},{"id":"RAW_QGOH_PQ_6:P0039","text":"This is evident from the Corollary to Prop. 25, Part I.","kind":"passage","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_QGOH_PQ_6:P0039"]},{"id":"RAW_QGOH_PQ_6:P0040","text":"PROPOSITION XXV","kind":"passage","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_QGOH_PQ_6:P0040"]},{"id":"RAW_QGOH_PQ_6:P0041","text":"The highest striving and highest virtue of the Mind is to understand things by the third kind of knowledge.","kind":"passage","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_QGOH_PQ_6:P0041"]},{"id":"RAW_QGOH_PQ_6:P0042","text":"DEMONSTRATION","kind":"passage","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_QGOH_PQ_6:P0042"]},{"id":"RAW_QGOH_PQ_6:P0043","text":"The third kind of knowledge proceeds from an adequate idea of certain attributes of God to an adequate knowledge of the essence of things (see its definition in the second Scholium to Prop. 40, Part II); and the more we understand things in this way, the more (by the preceding Prop.) we understand God; and consequently (by Prop. 28, Part IV) the highest virtue of the Mind, that is (by Def. 8, Part IV), the power or nature of the Mind, or (by Prop. 7, Part III) its highest striving, is to understand things by the third kind of knowledge. Q. E. D.","kind":"passage","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_QGOH_PQ_6:P0043"]},{"id":"RAW_QGOH_PQ_6:P0044","text":"PROPOSITION XXVI","kind":"passage","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_QGOH_PQ_6:P0044"]},{"id":"RAW_QGOH_PQ_6:P0045","text":"The more apt the Mind is to understand things by the third kind of knowledge, the more it desires to understand things by this same kind of knowledge.","kind":"passage","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_QGOH_PQ_6:P0045"]},{"id":"RAW_QGOH_PQ_6:P0046","text":"DEMONSTRATION","kind":"passage","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_QGOH_PQ_6:P0046"]},{"id":"RAW_QGOH_PQ_6:P0047","text":"This is evident. For insofar as we conceive the Mind to be apt to understand things by this kind of knowledge, we thereby conceive it to be determined to understand things by the same kind of knowledge; and consequently (by Def. 1 of the Affects), the more apt the Mind is for this, the more it desires it. Q. E. D.","kind":"passage","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_QGOH_PQ_6:P0047"]},{"id":"RAW_QGOH_PQ_6:P0048","text":"PROPOSITION XXVII","kind":"passage","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_QGOH_PQ_6:P0048"]},{"id":"RAW_QGOH_PQ_6:P0049","text":"From this third kind of knowledge there arises the highest satisfaction of Mind that can be given.","kind":"passage","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_QGOH_PQ_6:P0049"]},{"id":"RAW_QGOH_PQ_6:P0050","text":"DEMONSTRATION","kind":"passage","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_QGOH_PQ_6:P0050"]},{"id":"RAW_QGOH_PQ_6:P0051","text":"The highest virtue of the Mind is to know God (by Prop. 28, Part IV), or to understand things by the third kind of knowledge (by Prop. 25 of this Part); and this virtue is the greater, the more the Mind knows things by this kind of knowledge (by Prop. 24 of this Part); and therefore he who knows things by this kind of knowledge passes to the highest human perfection, and consequently (by Def. 2 of the Affects) is affected with the highest Joy, and this (by Prop. 43, Part II) accompanied by the idea of himself and of his virtue; and hence (by Def. 25 of the Affects), from this kind of knowledge there arises the highest satisfaction that can be given. Q. E. D.","kind":"passage","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_QGOH_PQ_6:P0051"]},{"id":"RAW_QGOH_PQ_6:P0052","text":"PROPOSITION XXVIII","kind":"passage","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_QGOH_PQ_6:P0052"]},{"id":"RAW_QGOH_PQ_6:P0053","text":"The striving, or Desire, to know things by the third kind of knowledge cannot arise from the first, but indeed from the second kind of knowledge.","kind":"passage","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_QGOH_PQ_6:P0053"]},{"id":"RAW_QGOH_PQ_6:P0054","text":"DEMONSTRATION","kind":"passage","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_QGOH_PQ_6:P0054"]},{"id":"RAW_QGOH_PQ_6:P0055","text":"This proposition is self-evident. For whatever we understand clearly and distinctly, we understand either through itself or through that which is conceived through itself; that is, ideas which are clear and distinct in us, or which are referred to the third kind of knowledge (see the 2nd Schol. to Prop. 40, Part II), cannot follow from mutilated and confused ideas, which (by the same Schol.) are referred to the first kind of knowledge, but from adequate ideas, or (by the same Schol.) from the second and third kinds of knowledge; and therefore (by Def. 1 of the Affects) the Desire to know things by the third kind of knowledge cannot arise from the first, but indeed from the second. Q. E. D.","kind":"passage","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_QGOH_PQ_6:P0055"]},{"id":"RAW_QGOH_PQ_6:P0056","text":"PROPOSITION XXIX","kind":"passage","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_QGOH_PQ_6:P0056"]},{"id":"RAW_QGOH_PQ_6:P0057","text":"Whatever the Mind understands under a species of eternity, it does not understand from the fact that it conceives the present actual existence of the Body, but from the fact that it conceives the essence of the Body under a species of eternity.","kind":"passage","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_QGOH_PQ_6:P0057"]},{"id":"RAW_QGOH_PQ_6:P0058","text":"DEMONSTRATION","kind":"passage","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_QGOH_PQ_6:P0058"]},{"id":"RAW_QGOH_PQ_6:P0059","text":"Insofar as the Mind conceives the present existence of its Body, it conceives duration, which can be determined by time, and only insofar does it have the power of conceiving things with relation to time (by Prop. 21 of this Part and Prop. 26, Part II). But eternity cannot be explained by duration (by Def. 8, Part I and its explanation). Therefore, insofar, the Mind has no power to conceive things under a species of eternity; but since it is of the nature of Reason to conceive things under a species of eternity (by Coroll. 2 to Prop. 44, Part II), and it also pertains to the nature of the Mind to conceive the essence of the Body under a species of eternity (by Prop. 23 of this Part), and besides these two nothing else pertains to the essence of the Mind (by Prop. 13, Part II); therefore this power of conceiving things under a species of eternity pertains to the Mind only insofar as it conceives the essence of the Body under a species of eternity. Q. E. D.","kind":"passage","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_QGOH_PQ_6:P0059"]},{"id":"RAW_QGOH_PQ_6:P0060","text":"SCHOLIUM","kind":"passage","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_QGOH_PQ_6:P0060"]},{"id":"RAW_QGOH_PQ_6:P0061","text":"Things are conceived by us as actual in two ways: either insofar as we conceive them to exist in relation to a definite time and place, or insofar as we conceive them to be contained in God and to follow from the necessity of the divine nature. But those which are conceived as true or real in this second way, we conceive under a species of eternity, and their ideas involve the eternal and infinite essence of God, as we showed in Proposition 45 of Part II, whose Scholium also see.","kind":"passage","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_QGOH_PQ_6:P0061"]},{"id":"RAW_QGOH_PQ_6:P0062","text":"PROPOSITION XXX","kind":"passage","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_QGOH_PQ_6:P0062"]},{"id":"RAW_QGOH_PQ_6:P0063","text":"Our Mind, insofar as it knows itself and the Body under a species of eternity, necessarily has knowledge of God, and knows that it is in God and is conceived through God.","kind":"passage","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_QGOH_PQ_6:P0063"]},{"id":"RAW_QGOH_PQ_6:P0064","text":"DEMONSTRATION","kind":"passage","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_QGOH_PQ_6:P0064"]},{"id":"RAW_QGOH_PQ_6:P0065","text":"Eternity is the very essence of God, insofar as this involves necessary existence (by Def. 8, Part I). Therefore, to conceive things under a species of eternity is to conceive things insofar as they are conceived as real beings through the essence of God, or insofar as through the essence of God they involve existence; and therefore our Mind, insofar as it conceives itself and the Body under a species of eternity, necessarily has knowledge of God, and knows, etc. Q. E. D.","kind":"passage","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_QGOH_PQ_6:P0065"]},{"id":"RAW_QGOH_PQ_6:P0066","text":"PROPOSITION XXXI","kind":"passage","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_QGOH_PQ_6:P0066"]},{"id":"RAW_QGOH_PQ_6:P0067","text":"The third kind of knowledge depends on the Mind, as on its formal cause, insofar as the Mind itself is eternal.","kind":"passage","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_QGOH_PQ_6:P0067"]},{"id":"RAW_QGOH_PQ_6:P0068","text":"DEMONSTRATION","kind":"passage","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_QGOH_PQ_6:P0068"]},{"id":"RAW_QGOH_PQ_6:P0069","text":"The Mind conceives nothing under a species of eternity except insofar as it conceives the essence of its Body under a species of eternity (by Prop. 29 of this Part), that is (by Props. 21 and 23 of this Part), except insofar as it is eternal; and therefore (by the preceding Prop.), insofar as it is eternal, it has knowledge of God; and this knowledge is necessarily adequate (by Prop. 46, Part II); and consequently the Mind, insofar as it is eternal, is fitted to know all those things which can follow from this given knowledge of God (by Prop. 40, Part II), that is, to know things by the third kind of knowledge (see its definition in the 2nd Schol. to Prop. 40, Part II), of which therefore the Mind (by Def. 1, Part III), insofar as it is eternal, is the adequate, or formal, cause. Q. E. D.","kind":"passage","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_QGOH_PQ_6:P0069"]},{"id":"RAW_QGOH_PQ_6:P0070","text":"SCHOLIUM","kind":"passage","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_QGOH_PQ_6:P0070"]},{"id":"RAW_QGOH_PQ_6:P0071","text":"Therefore, the more each person is endowed with this kind of knowledge, the more conscious he is of himself and of God, that is, the more perfect and blessed he is; this will become still clearer from what follows. But here it must be noted that, although we are now certain that the Mind is eternal insofar as it conceives things under a species of eternity, nevertheless, so that those things which we wish to show may be more easily explained and better understood, we shall consider it as though it were now beginning to be and were now beginning to understand things under a species of eternity, as we have done hitherto; which we may do without any danger of error, provided that we take care to conclude nothing except from clear premises.","kind":"passage","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_QGOH_PQ_6:P0071"]},{"id":"RAW_QGOH_PQ_6:P0072","text":"PROPOSITION XXXII","kind":"passage","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_QGOH_PQ_6:P0072"]},{"id":"RAW_QGOH_PQ_6:P0073","text":"Whatever we understand by the third kind of knowledge, we take pleasure in, and indeed with the accompanying idea of God as cause.","kind":"passage","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_QGOH_PQ_6:P0073"]},{"id":"RAW_QGOH_PQ_6:P0074","text":"DEMONSTRATION","kind":"passage","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_QGOH_PQ_6:P0074"]},{"id":"RAW_QGOH_PQ_6:P0075","text":"From this kind of knowledge there arises the greatest satisfaction of Mind that can be given, that is (by Def. 25 of the Affects), Joy, and this with the accompanying idea of itself (by Prop. 27 of this Part), and consequently (by Prop. 30 of this Part) also with the accompanying idea of God as cause. Q. E. D.","kind":"passage","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_QGOH_PQ_6:P0075"]},{"id":"RAW_QGOH_PQ_6:P0076","text":"COROLLARY","kind":"passage","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_QGOH_PQ_6:P0076"]},{"id":"RAW_QGOH_PQ_6:P0077","text":"From the third kind of knowledge there necessarily arises the intellectual Love of God. For from this kind of knowledge there arises (by the preceding Prop.) Joy with the accompanying idea of God as cause, that is (by Def. 6 of the Affects), Love of God, not insofar as we imagine him as present (by Prop. 29 of this Part), but insofar as we understand God to be eternal; and this is what I call the intellectual Love of God.","kind":"passage","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_QGOH_PQ_6:P0077"]},{"id":"RAW_QGOH_PQ_6:P0078","text":"PROPOSITION XXXIII","kind":"passage","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_QGOH_PQ_6:P0078"]},{"id":"RAW_QGOH_PQ_6:P0079","text":"The intellectual Love of God which arises from the third kind of knowledge is eternal.","kind":"passage","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_QGOH_PQ_6:P0079"]},{"id":"RAW_QGOH_PQ_6:P0080","text":"DEMONSTRATION","kind":"passage","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_QGOH_PQ_6:P0080"]},{"id":"RAW_QGOH_PQ_6:P0081","text":"For the third kind of knowledge (by Prop. 31 of this Part, and Axiom 3, Part I) is eternal; and therefore (by the same Axiom, Part I) the Love which arises from it is also necessarily eternal. Q. E. D.","kind":"passage","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_QGOH_PQ_6:P0081"]},{"id":"RAW_QGOH_PQ_6:P0082","text":"SCHOLIUM","kind":"passage","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_QGOH_PQ_6:P0082"]},{"id":"RAW_QGOH_PQ_6:P0083","text":"Although this Love toward God has not had a beginning (by the preceding Prop.), it nevertheless has all the perfections of Love, just as if it had arisen, as we supposed in the Corollary to the preceding Prop. Nor is there any difference here, except that the Mind has had these same perfections, which we supposed to be now accruing to it, eternally, and this with the accompanying idea of God as eternal cause. And if Joy consists in the transition to a greater perfection, Blessedness surely must consist in the Mind's being endowed with perfection itself.","kind":"passage","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_QGOH_PQ_6:P0083"]},{"id":"RAW_QGOH_PQ_6:P0084","text":"PROPOSITION XXXIV","kind":"passage","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_QGOH_PQ_6:P0084"]},{"id":"RAW_QGOH_PQ_6:P0085","text":"The Mind is subject to affects which are referred to passions only while the Body endures.","kind":"passage","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_QGOH_PQ_6:P0085"]},{"id":"RAW_QGOH_PQ_6:P0086","text":"DEMONSTRATION","kind":"passage","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_QGOH_PQ_6:P0086"]},{"id":"RAW_QGOH_PQ_6:P0087","text":"Imagination is an idea by which the Mind contemplates some thing as present (see its definition in the Scholium to Prop. 17, Part II); yet it indicates the present constitution of the human Body more than the nature of the external thing (by Corollary 2 to Prop. 16, Part II). Therefore an affect is (by the general Definition of the Affects) imagination, insofar as it indicates the present constitution of the Body; and consequently (by Prop. 21 of this Part) the Mind is subject to affects which are referred to passions only while the Body endures. Q. E. D.","kind":"passage","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_QGOH_PQ_6:P0087"]},{"id":"RAW_QGOH_PQ_6:P0088","text":"COROLLARY","kind":"passage","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_QGOH_PQ_6:P0088"]},{"id":"RAW_QGOH_PQ_6:P0089","text":"Hence it follows that no Love except intellectual Love is eternal.","kind":"passage","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_QGOH_PQ_6:P0089"]},{"id":"RAW_QGOH_PQ_6:P0090","text":"SCHOLIUM","kind":"passage","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_QGOH_PQ_6:P0090"]},{"id":"RAW_QGOH_PQ_6:P0091","text":"If we attend to the common opinion of men, we shall see that they are indeed conscious of the eternity of their Mind, but that they confuse it with duration, and attribute it to the imagination, or memory, which they believe remains after death.","kind":"passage","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_QGOH_PQ_6:P0091"]},{"id":"RAW_QGOH_PQ_6:P0092","text":"PROPOSITION XXXV","kind":"passage","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_QGOH_PQ_6:P0092"]},{"id":"RAW_QGOH_PQ_6:P0093","text":"God loves himself with an infinite intellectual Love.","kind":"passage","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_QGOH_PQ_6:P0093"]},{"id":"RAW_QGOH_PQ_6:P0094","text":"DEMONSTRATION","kind":"passage","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_QGOH_PQ_6:P0094"]},{"id":"RAW_QGOH_PQ_6:P0095","text":"God is absolutely infinite (by Def. 6, Part I), that is (by Def. 6, Part II), God's nature enjoys infinite perfection, and this (by Prop. 3, Part II) with the accompanying idea of itself, that is (by Prop. 11 and Def. 1, Part I) the idea of its cause; and this is what, in the Corollary to Prop. 32 of this Part, we said was intellectual Love.","kind":"passage","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_QGOH_PQ_6:P0095"]},{"id":"RAW_QGOH_PQ_6:P0096","text":"PROPOSITION XXXVI","kind":"passage","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_QGOH_PQ_6:P0096"]},{"id":"RAW_QGOH_PQ_6:P0097","text":"The Mind's intellectual Love toward God is God's very Love, by which God loves himself, not insofar as he is infinite, but insofar as he can be explained through the essence of the human Mind, considered under the species of eternity; that is, the Mind's intellectual Love toward God is a part of the infinite love by which God loves himself.","kind":"passage","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_QGOH_PQ_6:P0097"]},{"id":"RAW_QGOH_PQ_6:P0098","text":"DEMONSTRATION","kind":"passage","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_QGOH_PQ_6:P0098"]},{"id":"RAW_QGOH_PQ_6:P0099","text":"This Love of the Mind must be referred to the Mind's actions (by Coroll. Prop. 32 of this Part, and by Prop. 3 p. III); and therefore it is an action by which the Mind contemplates itself, accompanied by the idea of God as cause (by Prop. 32 of this Part and its Coroll.); that is (by Coroll. Prop. 25 p. I and Coroll. Prop. 11 p. II), an action by which God, insofar as he can be explained through the human Mind, contemplates himself, accompanied by the idea of himself; and consequently (by the preceding Prop.) this Love of the Mind is a part of the infinite love by which God loves himself. Q. E. D.","kind":"passage","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_QGOH_PQ_6:P0099"]},{"id":"RAW_QGOH_PQ_6:P0100","text":"COROLLARY","kind":"passage","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_QGOH_PQ_6:P0100"]},{"id":"RAW_QGOH_PQ_6:P0101","text":"Hence it follows that God, insofar as he loves himself, loves men, and consequently that God's Love toward men and the Mind's intellectual Love toward God are one and the same.","kind":"passage","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_QGOH_PQ_6:P0101"]},{"id":"RAW_QGOH_PQ_6:P0102","text":"SCHOLIUM","kind":"passage","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_QGOH_PQ_6:P0102"]},{"id":"RAW_QGOH_PQ_6:P0103","text":"From these things we clearly understand wherein our salvation, or Blessedness, or Freedom consists; namely, in a constant and eternal Love toward God, or in God's Love toward men. And this Love or Blessedness is called Glory in the Sacred writings, and not without reason. For whether this Love is referred to God or to the Mind, it can rightly be called acquiescence of mind, which is in reality not distinguished from Glory (by 25 and 30 of the Definitions of the Affects). For insofar as it is referred to God, it is (by Prop. 35 of this Part) Joy, if it be permitted still to use this term, accompanied by the idea of himself, just as it is also insofar as it is referred to the Mind (by Prop. 27 of this Part). Further, because the essence of our Mind consists in knowledge alone, of which God is the principle and foundation (by Prop. 15 p. I and Schol. Prop. 47 p.","kind":"passage","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_QGOH_PQ_6:P0103"]},{"id":"RAW_QGOH_PQ_6:P0104","text":"II), it hence becomes clear to us how and for what reason our Mind follows from the divine nature according to essence and existence, and continually depends on God; which I thought worth noting here, in order to show by this example how powerful that knowledge of singular things is, which I called intuitive, or of the third kind (see 2 Schol. Prop. 40 p. II), and how much more excellent it is than universal knowledge, which I said is of the second kind. For although in Part I I showed generally that all things (and consequently also the human Mind) depend on God according to essence and existence, nevertheless that demonstration, although legitimate and placed beyond the hazard of doubt, does not affect our Mind as much as when the same conclusion is inferred from the very essence of any singular thing which we say depends on God.","kind":"passage","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_QGOH_PQ_6:P0104"]},{"id":"RAW_QGOH_PQ_6:P0105","text":"PROPOSITION XXXVII","kind":"passage","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_QGOH_PQ_6:P0105"]},{"id":"RAW_QGOH_PQ_6:P0106","text":"Nothing is given in Nature which is contrary to this intellectual Love, or which can take it away.","kind":"passage","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_QGOH_PQ_6:P0106"]},{"id":"RAW_QGOH_PQ_6:P0107","text":"DEMONSTRATION","kind":"passage","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_QGOH_PQ_6:P0107"]},{"id":"RAW_QGOH_PQ_6:P0108","text":"This intellectual Love follows necessarily from the nature of the Mind, insofar as the Mind itself, as an eternal truth, is considered through the nature of God (by Props. 33 and 29 of this Part). If, therefore, anything were given which was contrary to this Love, it would be contrary to truth; and consequently that which could take away this Love would bring it about that what is true was false; which (as is known through itself) is absurd. Therefore nothing is given in nature, etc. Q. E. D.","kind":"passage","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_QGOH_PQ_6:P0108"]},{"id":"RAW_QGOH_PQ_6:P0109","text":"SCHOLIUM","kind":"passage","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_QGOH_PQ_6:P0109"]},{"id":"RAW_QGOH_PQ_6:P0110","text":"The Axiom of Part IV concerns singular things insofar as they are considered in relation to a definite time and place; of which I believe no one doubts.","kind":"passage","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_QGOH_PQ_6:P0110"]},{"id":"RAW_QGOH_PQ_6:P0111","text":"PROPOSITION XXXVIII","kind":"passage","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_QGOH_PQ_6:P0111"]},{"id":"RAW_QGOH_PQ_6:P0112","text":"The more things the Mind understands by the second and third kinds of knowledge, the less it suffers from affects which are evil, and the less it fears death.","kind":"passage","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_QGOH_PQ_6:P0112"]},{"id":"RAW_QGOH_PQ_6:P0113","text":"DEMONSTRATION","kind":"passage","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_QGOH_PQ_6:P0113"]},{"id":"RAW_QGOH_PQ_6:P0114","text":"The essence of the Mind consists in knowledge (by Prop. 11 p. II); therefore, the more things the Mind knows by the second and third kinds of knowledge, the greater part of it remains (by Props. 29 and 23 of this Part), and consequently (by the preceding Prop.) the greater part of it is not touched by affects which are contrary to our nature, that is (by Prop. 30 p. IV), which are evil. Therefore, the more things the Mind understands by the second and third kinds of knowledge, the greater part of it remains unharmed, and consequently it suffers less from affects, etc. Q. E. D.","kind":"passage","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_QGOH_PQ_6:P0114"]},{"id":"RAW_QGOH_PQ_6:P0115","text":"SCHOLIUM","kind":"passage","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_QGOH_PQ_6:P0115"]},{"id":"RAW_QGOH_PQ_6:P0116","text":"From this we understand what I touched upon in Schol. Prop. 39 p. IV, and what I promised to explain in this Part; namely, that death is so much the less harmful as the Mind's clear and distinct knowledge is greater, and consequently, as the Mind loves God more. Further, because (by Prop. 27 of this Part) from the third kind of knowledge arises the greatest acquiescence that can be given, it follows that the human Mind can be of such a nature that what we have shown to perish with its Body (see Prop. 21 of this Part) is of no moment in comparison with what remains of it. But concerning these matters, more at length shortly.","kind":"passage","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_QGOH_PQ_6:P0116"]},{"id":"RAW_QGOH_PQ_6:P0117","text":"PROPOSITION XXXIX","kind":"passage","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_QGOH_PQ_6:P0117"]},{"id":"RAW_QGOH_PQ_6:P0118","text":"He who has a Body capable of very many things has a Mind the greatest part of which is eternal.","kind":"passage","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_QGOH_PQ_6:P0118"]},{"id":"RAW_QGOH_PQ_6:P0119","text":"DEMONSTRATION","kind":"passage","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_QGOH_PQ_6:P0119"]},{"id":"RAW_QGOH_PQ_6:P0120","text":"He who has a Body capable of doing very many things is least troubled by affects which are evil (by Prop. 38 p. IV), that is (by Prop. 30 p. IV), by affects which are contrary to our nature; and therefore (by Prop. 10 of this Part) has the power of ordering and connecting the affections of the Body according to the order of the intellect, and consequently of bringing it about (by Prop. 14 of this Part) that all the affections of the Body are referred to the idea of God; from which it will come about (by Prop. 15 of this Part) that he is affected with Love toward God, which (by Prop. 16 of this Part) must occupy or constitute the greatest part of the Mind, and therefore (by Prop. 33 of this Part) he has a Mind the greatest part of which is eternal. Q. E. D.","kind":"passage","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_QGOH_PQ_6:P0120"]},{"id":"RAW_QGOH_PQ_6:P0121","text":"SCHOLIUM","kind":"passage","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_QGOH_PQ_6:P0121"]}],"source_digest":"2d6b9d3caa580d62c6db16256430098cfa605b6e08ec8403fd3f4a7d2c7ab30a","source_language_code":"la","original_title":"Pars quinta - De potentia intellectus seu de libertate humana · passage 2"}],"passage_basis":"frozen-grounding","original_title":"Pars quinta - De potentia intellectus seu de libertate humana · passage 2","title_language_code":"en"},{"id":"RAW_QGOH_PQ_7","title":"Part Five - On the Power of the Intellect, or Human Freedom · passage 3","order":21,"chapter_id":"CH_QGOH_PQ_7","text":"Quia corpora humana ad plurima apta sunt, non dubium est, quin ejus naturæ possint esse, ut ad Mentes referantur, quæ magnam sui et Dei habeant cognitionem, et quarum maxima seu præcipua pars est æterna, atque adeo ut mortem vix timeant. Sed ut hæc clarius intelligantur, animadvertendum hic est, quod nos in continua vivimus variatione, et prout in melius sive in pejus mutamur, eo felices aut infelices dicimur. Qui enim ex infante vel puero in cadaver transiit, infelix dicitur, et contra id felicitati tribuitur, quod totum vitæ spatium Mente sana in Corpore sano percurrere potuerimus. Et revera qui Corpus habet, ut infans vel puer, ad paucissima aptum, et maxime pendens a causis externis, Mentem habet, quæ in se sola considerata nihil fere sui nec Dei nec rerum sit conscia ; et contra, qui corpus habet ad plurima aptum, Mentem habet, quæ in se sola considerata multum sui et Dei et rerum sit conscia ; In hac vita igitur apprime conamur, ut Corpus infantiæ in aliud, quantum ejus natura patitur, eique conducit, mutetur quod ad plurima aptum sit, quodque ad Mentem referatur, quæ sui et Dei et rerum plurimum sit conscia ; atque ita ut id omne, quod ad ipsius memoriam vel imaginationem refertur, in respectu ad intellectum vix alicujus sit momenti, ut in Schol. Prop. præced. jam dixi.\n\nPROPOSITIO XL\n\nQuo unaquæque res plus perfectionis habet, eo magis agit et minus patitur et contra, quo magis agit, eo perfectior est.\n\nDEMONSTRATIO\n\nQuo unaquæque res perfectior est, eo plus habet realitatis (per Defin. 6 p. II), et consequenter (per Prop. 3 p. III cum ejus Schol.) eo magis agit et minus patitur ; quæ quidem demonstratio inverso ordine eodem modo procedit, ex quo sequitur, ut res contra eo sit perfectior, quo magis agit. Q. E. D.\n\nCOROLLARIUM\n\nHinc sequitur, partem Mentis, quæ remanet, quantacunque ea sit, perfectiorem esse reliqua. Nam pars Mentis æterna (per Prop. 23 et 29 hujus) est intellectus, per quem solum nos agere dicimur (per Prop. 3 p. III) ; illa autem, quam perire ostendimus, est ipsa imaginatio (per Prop. 21 hujus), per quam solam dicimur pati (per Prop. 3 p. III et gen. Affect. Defin.) ; atque adeo (per Prop. præced.) illa, quantacunque ea sit, hac est perfectior. Q. E. D.\n\nSCHOLIUM\n\nHæc sunt, quæ de Mente, quatenus sine relatione ad Corporis existentiam consideratur, ostendere constitueram : ex quibus, et simul ex Prop. 21 p. I et aliis, apparet, quod Mens nostra, quatenus intelligit æternus cogitandi modus sit, qui alio æterno cogitandi modo determinatur, et hic iterum ab alio, et sic in infinitum ; ita ut omnes simul Dei æternum et infinitum intellectum constituant.\n\nPROPOSITIO XLI\n\nQuamvis nesciremus, Mentem nostram æternam esse, Pietatem tamen, et Religionem, et absolute omnia, quæ ad Animositatem et Generositatem referri ostendimus in quarta Parte, prima haberemus.\n\nDEMONSTRATIO\n\nPrimum et unicum virtutis seu recte vivendi Rationis fundamentum (per Coroll. Prop. 22 et per Prop. 24 p. IV) est suum utile quærere. Ad illa autem determinandum, quæ Ratio utilia esse dictat, nullam rationem habuimus Mentis æternitatis, quam demum in hac quinta Parte novimus. Quamvis igitur tum temporis ignoraverimus, Mentem esse æternam, illa tamen, quæ ad Animositatem et Generositatem referri ostendimus, prima habuimus ; atque adeo, quamvis etiam nunc hoc ipsum ignoraremus, eadem tamen Rationis præscripta prima haberemus. Q. E. D.\n\nSCHOLIUM\n\nCommunis vulgi persuasio alia videtur esse. Nam plerique videntur credere, se eatenus liberos esse, quatenus libidini parere licet, et eatenus de suo jure cedere, quatenus ex legis divinæ præscripto vivere tenentur. Pietatem igitur et Religionem, et absolute omnia, quæ ad animi Fortitudinem referuntur, onera esse credunt, quæ post mortem deponere, et pretium servitutis, nempe Pietatis et Religionis, accipere sperant ; nec hac Spe sola, sed etiam et præcipue Metu ne diris scilicet suppliciis post mortem puniantur, inducuntur, ut ex legis divinæ præscripto, quantum eorum fert tenuitas et impotens animus, vivant. Et nisi hæc Spes et Metus hominibus inessent, at contra si crederent, Mentes cum Corpore interire, nec restare miseris, Pietatis onere confectis, vivere longius ; ad ingenium redirent, et ex libidine omnia moderari, et fortunæ potius quam sibi parere vellent. Quæ mihi non minus absurda videntur, quam si quis, propterea quod non credit, se posse bonis alimentis Corpus in æternum nutrire, venenis potius et lethiferis se exsaturare vellet ; vel, quia videt Mentem non esse æternam seu immortalem, ideo amens mavult esse et sine Ratione vivere : quæ adeo absurda sunt, ut vix recenseri mereantur.\n\nPROPOSITIO XLII\n\nBeatitudo non est virtutis præmium, sed ipsa virtus ; nec eadem gaudemus, quia libidines coercemus, sed contra, quia eadem gaudemus, ideo libidines coercere possumus.\n\nDEMONSTRATIO\n\nBeatitudo in Amore erga Deum consistit (per Prop. 36 hujus, et ejus Schol.), qui quidem Amor ex tertio cognitionis genere oritur (per Coroll. Prop. 32 hujus) ; atque adeo hic Amor (per Prop. 59 et 3 p. III) ad Mentem, quatenus agit, referri debet ; ac proinde (per Defin. 8 p. IV) ipsa virtus est ; Quod erat primum. Deinde quo Mens hoc Amore divino seu beatitudine magis gaudet, eo plus intelligit (per Prop. 32 hujus), hoc est (per Coroll. Prop. 3 hujus), eo majorem in affectus habet potentiam, et (per Prop. 38 hujus) eo minus ab affectibus qui mali sunt, patitur ; atque adeo ex eo, quod Mens hoc Amore divino seu Beatitudine gaudet, potestatem habet libidines coercendi. Et quia humana potentia ad coercendos affectus in solo intellectu consistit, ergo nemo beatitudine gaudet, quia affectus coercuit, sed contra potestas libidines coercendi ex ipsa beatitudine oritur. Q. E. D.\n\nSCHOLIUM\n\nHis omnia, quæ de Mentis in affectus potentia, quæque de Mentis Libertate ostendere volueram, absolvi. Ex quibus apparet, quantum Sapiens polleat, potiorque sit ignaro, qui sola libidine agitur. Ignarus enim, præterquam quod a causis externis multis modis agitatur, nec unquam vera animi acquiescentia potitur, vivit præterea sui et Dei et rerum quasi inscius, et simulac pati desinit, simul etiam esse desinit. Cum contra sapiens, quatenus ut talis consideratur, vix animo movetur, sed sui et Dei et rerum æterna quadam necessitate conscius, nunquam esse desinit, sed semper vera animi acquiescentia potitur. Si jam via, quam ad hæc ducere ostendi, perardua videatur, inveniri tamen potest. Et sane arduum debet esse, quod adeo raro reperitur. Qui enim posset fieri, si salus in promptu esset, et sine magno labore reperiri posset, ut ab omnibus fere negligeretur ? Sed omnia præclara tam difficilia quam rara sunt.\n\nFINIS","paragraphs":[{"id":"RAW_QGOH_PQ_7:P0001","text":"Quia corpora humana ad plurima apta sunt, non dubium est, quin ejus naturæ possint esse, ut ad Mentes referantur, quæ magnam sui et Dei habeant cognitionem, et quarum maxima seu præcipua pars est æterna, atque adeo ut mortem vix timeant. Sed ut hæc clarius intelligantur, animadvertendum hic est, quod nos in continua vivimus variatione, et prout in melius sive in pejus mutamur, eo felices aut infelices dicimur. Qui enim ex infante vel puero in cadaver transiit, infelix dicitur, et contra id felicitati tribuitur, quod totum vitæ spatium Mente sana in Corpore sano percurrere potuerimus.","kind":"passage"},{"id":"RAW_QGOH_PQ_7:P0002","text":"Et revera qui Corpus habet, ut infans vel puer, ad paucissima aptum, et maxime pendens a causis externis, Mentem habet, quæ in se sola considerata nihil fere sui nec Dei nec rerum sit conscia ; et contra, qui corpus habet ad plurima aptum, Mentem habet, quæ in se sola considerata multum sui et Dei et rerum sit conscia ; In hac vita igitur apprime conamur, ut Corpus infantiæ in aliud, quantum ejus natura patitur, eique conducit, mutetur quod ad plurima aptum sit, quodque ad Mentem referatur, quæ sui et Dei et rerum plurimum sit conscia ; atque ita ut id omne, quod ad ipsius memoriam vel imaginationem refertur, in respectu ad intellectum vix alicujus sit momenti, ut in Schol. Prop. præced. jam dixi.","kind":"passage"},{"id":"RAW_QGOH_PQ_7:P0003","text":"PROPOSITIO XL","kind":"passage"},{"id":"RAW_QGOH_PQ_7:P0004","text":"Quo unaquæque res plus perfectionis habet, eo magis agit et minus patitur et contra, quo magis agit, eo perfectior est.","kind":"passage"},{"id":"RAW_QGOH_PQ_7:P0005","text":"DEMONSTRATIO","kind":"passage"},{"id":"RAW_QGOH_PQ_7:P0006","text":"Quo unaquæque res perfectior est, eo plus habet realitatis (per Defin. 6 p. II), et consequenter (per Prop. 3 p. III cum ejus Schol.) eo magis agit et minus patitur ; quæ quidem demonstratio inverso ordine eodem modo procedit, ex quo sequitur, ut res contra eo sit perfectior, quo magis agit. Q. E. D.","kind":"passage"},{"id":"RAW_QGOH_PQ_7:P0007","text":"COROLLARIUM","kind":"passage"},{"id":"RAW_QGOH_PQ_7:P0008","text":"Hinc sequitur, partem Mentis, quæ remanet, quantacunque ea sit, perfectiorem esse reliqua. Nam pars Mentis æterna (per Prop. 23 et 29 hujus) est intellectus, per quem solum nos agere dicimur (per Prop. 3 p. III) ; illa autem, quam perire ostendimus, est ipsa imaginatio (per Prop. 21 hujus), per quam solam dicimur pati (per Prop. 3 p. III et gen. Affect. Defin.) ; atque adeo (per Prop. præced.) illa, quantacunque ea sit, hac est perfectior. Q. E. D.","kind":"passage"},{"id":"RAW_QGOH_PQ_7:P0009","text":"SCHOLIUM","kind":"passage"},{"id":"RAW_QGOH_PQ_7:P0010","text":"Hæc sunt, quæ de Mente, quatenus sine relatione ad Corporis existentiam consideratur, ostendere constitueram : ex quibus, et simul ex Prop. 21 p. I et aliis, apparet, quod Mens nostra, quatenus intelligit æternus cogitandi modus sit, qui alio æterno cogitandi modo determinatur, et hic iterum ab alio, et sic in infinitum ; ita ut omnes simul Dei æternum et infinitum intellectum constituant.","kind":"passage"},{"id":"RAW_QGOH_PQ_7:P0011","text":"PROPOSITIO XLI","kind":"passage"},{"id":"RAW_QGOH_PQ_7:P0012","text":"Quamvis nesciremus, Mentem nostram æternam esse, Pietatem tamen, et Religionem, et absolute omnia, quæ ad Animositatem et Generositatem referri ostendimus in quarta Parte, prima haberemus.","kind":"passage"},{"id":"RAW_QGOH_PQ_7:P0013","text":"DEMONSTRATIO","kind":"passage"},{"id":"RAW_QGOH_PQ_7:P0014","text":"Primum et unicum virtutis seu recte vivendi Rationis fundamentum (per Coroll. Prop. 22 et per Prop. 24 p. IV) est suum utile quærere. Ad illa autem determinandum, quæ Ratio utilia esse dictat, nullam rationem habuimus Mentis æternitatis, quam demum in hac quinta Parte novimus. Quamvis igitur tum temporis ignoraverimus, Mentem esse æternam, illa tamen, quæ ad Animositatem et Generositatem referri ostendimus, prima habuimus ; atque adeo, quamvis etiam nunc hoc ipsum ignoraremus, eadem tamen Rationis præscripta prima haberemus. Q. E. D.","kind":"passage"},{"id":"RAW_QGOH_PQ_7:P0015","text":"SCHOLIUM","kind":"passage"},{"id":"RAW_QGOH_PQ_7:P0016","text":"Communis vulgi persuasio alia videtur esse. Nam plerique videntur credere, se eatenus liberos esse, quatenus libidini parere licet, et eatenus de suo jure cedere, quatenus ex legis divinæ præscripto vivere tenentur. Pietatem igitur et Religionem, et absolute omnia, quæ ad animi Fortitudinem referuntur, onera esse credunt, quæ post mortem deponere, et pretium servitutis, nempe Pietatis et Religionis, accipere sperant ; nec hac Spe sola, sed etiam et præcipue Metu ne diris scilicet suppliciis post mortem puniantur, inducuntur, ut ex legis divinæ præscripto, quantum eorum fert tenuitas et impotens animus, vivant. Et nisi hæc Spes et Metus hominibus inessent, at contra si crederent, Mentes cum Corpore interire, nec restare miseris, Pietatis onere confectis, vivere longius ; ad ingenium redirent, et ex libidine omnia moderari, et fortunæ potius quam sibi parere vellent.","kind":"passage"},{"id":"RAW_QGOH_PQ_7:P0017","text":"Quæ mihi non minus absurda videntur, quam si quis, propterea quod non credit, se posse bonis alimentis Corpus in æternum nutrire, venenis potius et lethiferis se exsaturare vellet ; vel, quia videt Mentem non esse æternam seu immortalem, ideo amens mavult esse et sine Ratione vivere : quæ adeo absurda sunt, ut vix recenseri mereantur.","kind":"passage"},{"id":"RAW_QGOH_PQ_7:P0018","text":"PROPOSITIO XLII","kind":"passage"},{"id":"RAW_QGOH_PQ_7:P0019","text":"Beatitudo non est virtutis præmium, sed ipsa virtus ; nec eadem gaudemus, quia libidines coercemus, sed contra, quia eadem gaudemus, ideo libidines coercere possumus.","kind":"passage"},{"id":"RAW_QGOH_PQ_7:P0020","text":"DEMONSTRATIO","kind":"passage"},{"id":"RAW_QGOH_PQ_7:P0021","text":"Beatitudo in Amore erga Deum consistit (per Prop. 36 hujus, et ejus Schol.), qui quidem Amor ex tertio cognitionis genere oritur (per Coroll. Prop. 32 hujus) ; atque adeo hic Amor (per Prop. 59 et 3 p. III) ad Mentem, quatenus agit, referri debet ; ac proinde (per Defin. 8 p. IV) ipsa virtus est ; Quod erat primum. Deinde quo Mens hoc Amore divino seu beatitudine magis gaudet, eo plus intelligit (per Prop. 32 hujus), hoc est (per Coroll. Prop. 3 hujus), eo majorem in affectus habet potentiam, et (per Prop. 38 hujus) eo minus ab affectibus qui mali sunt, patitur ; atque adeo ex eo, quod Mens hoc Amore divino seu Beatitudine gaudet, potestatem habet libidines coercendi. Et quia humana potentia ad coercendos affectus in solo intellectu consistit, ergo nemo beatitudine gaudet, quia affectus coercuit, sed contra potestas libidines coercendi ex ipsa beatitudine oritur. Q. E. D.","kind":"passage"},{"id":"RAW_QGOH_PQ_7:P0022","text":"SCHOLIUM","kind":"passage"},{"id":"RAW_QGOH_PQ_7:P0023","text":"His omnia, quæ de Mentis in affectus potentia, quæque de Mentis Libertate ostendere volueram, absolvi. Ex quibus apparet, quantum Sapiens polleat, potiorque sit ignaro, qui sola libidine agitur. Ignarus enim, præterquam quod a causis externis multis modis agitatur, nec unquam vera animi acquiescentia potitur, vivit præterea sui et Dei et rerum quasi inscius, et simulac pati desinit, simul etiam esse desinit. Cum contra sapiens, quatenus ut talis consideratur, vix animo movetur, sed sui et Dei et rerum æterna quadam necessitate conscius, nunquam esse desinit, sed semper vera animi acquiescentia potitur. Si jam via, quam ad hæc ducere ostendi, perardua videatur, inveniri tamen potest. Et sane arduum debet esse, quod adeo raro reperitur. Qui enim posset fieri, si salus in promptu esset, et sine magno labore reperiri posset, ut ab omnibus fere negligeretur ?","kind":"passage"},{"id":"RAW_QGOH_PQ_7:P0024","text":"Sed omnia præclara tam difficilia quam rara sunt.","kind":"passage"},{"id":"RAW_QGOH_PQ_7:P0025","text":"FINIS","kind":"passage"}],"sha256":"958acd91fb704bab64f44e7f940bad0f5dcf420cf95f7e308073980c2f821ec4","language_code":"la","language_name":"Latina","translations":[{"id":"SOURCE_TR_20261015T014425576796Z_2799bc33","language_code":"en","language_name":"English","title":"Part Five - On the Power of the Intellect, or Human Freedom · passage 3","source_sha256":"958acd91fb704bab64f44e7f940bad0f5dcf420cf95f7e308073980c2f821ec4","sha256":"c8ace4b854369ab51e28a5f1c33653f60b492fb01580bc8f6076bf020d424919","rendition_type":"machine","review_status":"machine","model":"gpt-5.6-terra","created":"2026-10-15T01:44:25.576830+00:00","paragraphs":[{"id":"RAW_QGOH_PQ_7:P0001","text":"Because human bodies are capable of very many things, there is no doubt that they can be of such a nature as to be related to Minds which have a great knowledge of themselves and of God, and whose greatest or principal part is eternal, and therefore that they scarcely fear death. But in order that these things may be understood more clearly, it must here be observed that we live in continual variation, and, according as we are changed for the better or for the worse, are called happy or unhappy. For one who has passed from being an infant or a boy into a corpse is called unhappy; and, on the contrary, it is attributed to happiness that we have been able to traverse the whole span of life with a sound Mind in a sound Body.","kind":"passage","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_QGOH_PQ_7:P0001"]},{"id":"RAW_QGOH_PQ_7:P0002","text":"And indeed, one who has a Body, as an infant or a boy does, capable of very few things and dependent in the highest degree upon external causes, has a Mind which, considered in itself alone, is scarcely conscious of itself, or of God, or of things; and conversely, one who has a Body capable of very many things has a Mind which, considered in itself alone, is greatly conscious of itself, of God, and of things. In this life, therefore, we especially strive that the Body of infancy may be changed into another which, as far as its nature permits and as is conducive to it, is capable of very many things and is related to a Mind that is very greatly conscious of itself, of God, and of things; and thus that all that pertains to its memory or imagination may be of scarcely any moment in comparison with the intellect, as I have already said in the Schol. of the preceding Prop.","kind":"passage","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_QGOH_PQ_7:P0002"]},{"id":"RAW_QGOH_PQ_7:P0003","text":"PROPOSITION XL","kind":"passage","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_QGOH_PQ_7:P0003"]},{"id":"RAW_QGOH_PQ_7:P0004","text":"The more perfection each thing has, the more it acts and the less it suffers; and conversely, the more it acts, the more perfect it is.","kind":"passage","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_QGOH_PQ_7:P0004"]},{"id":"RAW_QGOH_PQ_7:P0005","text":"DEMONSTRATION","kind":"passage","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_QGOH_PQ_7:P0005"]},{"id":"RAW_QGOH_PQ_7:P0006","text":"The more perfect each thing is, the more reality it has (by Defin. 6 p. II), and consequently (by Prop. 3 p. III with its Schol.) the more it acts and the less it suffers; and this demonstration proceeds in the same way in reverse order, from which it follows that, conversely, a thing is more perfect the more it acts. Q. E. D.","kind":"passage","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_QGOH_PQ_7:P0006"]},{"id":"RAW_QGOH_PQ_7:P0007","text":"COROLLARY","kind":"passage","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_QGOH_PQ_7:P0007"]},{"id":"RAW_QGOH_PQ_7:P0008","text":"From this it follows that the part of the Mind which remains, however great it may be, is more perfect than the rest. For the eternal part of the Mind (by Prop. 23 and 29 of this Part) is the intellect, through which alone we are said to act (by Prop. 3 p. III); but that which we have shown to perish is imagination itself (by Prop. 21 of this Part), through which alone we are said to suffer (by Prop. 3 p. III and the general Defin. of the Affects); and therefore (by the preceding Prop.), however great it may be, that part is more perfect than this. Q. E. D.","kind":"passage","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_QGOH_PQ_7:P0008"]},{"id":"RAW_QGOH_PQ_7:P0009","text":"SCHOLIUM","kind":"passage","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_QGOH_PQ_7:P0009"]},{"id":"RAW_QGOH_PQ_7:P0010","text":"These are the things which I had resolved to demonstrate concerning the Mind, insofar as it is considered without relation to the existence of the Body; from which, and at the same time from Prop. 21 p. I and other things, it appears that our Mind, insofar as it understands, is an eternal mode of thinking, which is determined by another eternal mode of thinking, and this again by another, and so on to infinity; so that all together constitute the eternal and infinite intellect of God.","kind":"passage","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_QGOH_PQ_7:P0010"]},{"id":"RAW_QGOH_PQ_7:P0011","text":"PROPOSITION XLI","kind":"passage","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_QGOH_PQ_7:P0011"]},{"id":"RAW_QGOH_PQ_7:P0012","text":"Although we did not know that our Mind is eternal, we should nevertheless hold Piety and Religion, and absolutely all things which we have shown to be related to Strength of Mind and Generosity in Part Four, as primary.","kind":"passage","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_QGOH_PQ_7:P0012"]},{"id":"RAW_QGOH_PQ_7:P0013","text":"DEMONSTRATION","kind":"passage","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_QGOH_PQ_7:P0013"]},{"id":"RAW_QGOH_PQ_7:P0014","text":"The first and sole foundation of virtue, or of the rule of right living (by Coroll. Prop. 22 and by Prop. 24 p. IV), is to seek one's own advantage. But for determining those things which Reason dictates to be useful, we had no account of the eternity of the Mind, which we have only come to know in this fifth Part. Therefore, although at that time we were ignorant that the Mind is eternal, we nevertheless held as primary those things which we have shown to be related to Strength of Mind and Generosity; and therefore, even if we were now ignorant of this very thing, we should nevertheless hold the same precepts of Reason as primary. Q. E. D.","kind":"passage","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_QGOH_PQ_7:P0014"]},{"id":"RAW_QGOH_PQ_7:P0015","text":"SCHOLIUM","kind":"passage","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_QGOH_PQ_7:P0015"]},{"id":"RAW_QGOH_PQ_7:P0016","text":"The common persuasion of the multitude seems to be otherwise. For most seem to believe that they are free insofar as they are permitted to obey lust, and that they surrender their right insofar as they are bound to live according to the prescription of the divine law. They therefore believe that Piety and Religion, and absolutely all things which are referred to Strength of Mind, are burdens which they hope to lay aside after death and to receive the price of their servitude, namely, of Piety and Religion. And they are induced to live according to the prescription of the divine law, as far as their poverty and feeble mind allow, not by this Hope alone, but also, and especially, by Fear—namely, lest they be punished after death by dreadful penalties. And if this Hope and Fear were not present in human beings, but, on the contrary, they believed that Minds perish with the Body, and that no longer life remains for the wretched, exhausted by the burden of Piety, they would return to their natural disposition and would wish to govern everything according to lust and to obey fortune rather than themselves.","kind":"passage","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_QGOH_PQ_7:P0016"]},{"id":"RAW_QGOH_PQ_7:P0017","text":"These things seem to me no less absurd than if someone, because he does not believe that he can nourish his Body eternally with good food, should prefer instead to gorge himself with poisons and deadly things; or because he sees that the Mind is not eternal or immortal, should therefore prefer to be mad and to live without Reason. These are so absurd that they scarcely deserve to be mentioned.","kind":"passage","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_QGOH_PQ_7:P0017"]},{"id":"RAW_QGOH_PQ_7:P0018","text":"PROPOSITION XLII","kind":"passage","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_QGOH_PQ_7:P0018"]},{"id":"RAW_QGOH_PQ_7:P0019","text":"Blessedness is not the reward of virtue, but virtue itself; nor do we enjoy it because we restrain lusts, but, on the contrary, because we enjoy it, we are able to restrain lusts.","kind":"passage","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_QGOH_PQ_7:P0019"]},{"id":"RAW_QGOH_PQ_7:P0020","text":"DEMONSTRATION","kind":"passage","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_QGOH_PQ_7:P0020"]},{"id":"RAW_QGOH_PQ_7:P0021","text":"Blessedness consists in Love toward God (by Prop. 36 of this Part, and its Schol.), which Love indeed arises from the third kind of knowledge (by Coroll. Prop. 32 of this Part); and therefore this Love (by Prop. 59 and 3 p. III) must be referred to the Mind insofar as it acts, and consequently (by Defin. 8 p. IV) is virtue itself. This was the first point. Next, the more the Mind enjoys this divine Love, or blessedness, the more it understands (by Prop. 32 of this Part), that is (by Coroll. Prop. 3 of this Part), the greater power it has over the affects, and (by Prop. 38 of this Part) the less it suffers from affects which are evil; and therefore, from the fact that the Mind enjoys this divine Love, or Blessedness, it has the power to restrain lusts. And because human power to restrain the affects consists in the intellect alone, therefore no one enjoys blessedness because he has restrained the affects; but, on the contrary, the power to restrain lusts arises from blessedness itself. Q. E. D.","kind":"passage","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_QGOH_PQ_7:P0021"]},{"id":"RAW_QGOH_PQ_7:P0022","text":"SCHOLIUM","kind":"passage","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_QGOH_PQ_7:P0022"]},{"id":"RAW_QGOH_PQ_7:P0023","text":"With these things I have completed all that I wished to show concerning the power of the Mind over the affects, and concerning the Freedom of the Mind. From them it appears how much the wise person can do, and how much more powerful he is than the ignorant person, who is driven by lust alone. For the ignorant person, besides being agitated in many ways by external causes and never possessing true tranquillity of mind, furthermore lives as though unconscious of himself, of God, and of things, and, as soon as he ceases to suffer, at the same time ceases to be. By contrast, the wise person, insofar as he is considered as such, is scarcely disturbed in mind, but, conscious of himself, of God, and of things by a certain eternal necessity, never ceases to be, and always possesses true tranquillity of mind. If now the way which I have shown to lead to these things seems very arduous, it can nevertheless be found. And indeed, that which is found so rarely must be arduous. For how could it come about, if salvation were at hand and could be found without great labor, that it should be neglected by almost everyone?","kind":"passage","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_QGOH_PQ_7:P0023"]},{"id":"RAW_QGOH_PQ_7:P0024","text":"But all excellent things are as difficult as they are rare.","kind":"passage","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_QGOH_PQ_7:P0024"]},{"id":"RAW_QGOH_PQ_7:P0025","text":"THE END","kind":"passage","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_QGOH_PQ_7:P0025"]}],"source_digest":"27b21df45120ba77d0f0cfeac7ad44f99425718c2d26058d686fb7db319525a0","source_language_code":"la","original_title":"Pars quinta - De potentia intellectus seu de libertate humana · passage 3"}],"passage_basis":"frozen-grounding","original_title":"Pars quinta - De potentia intellectus seu de libertate humana · passage 3","title_language_code":"en"}],"units":[{"id":"CH_QGOH_PPDD","layer":"CH","title":"Part One - Concerning God · passage 1","order":1,"parents":["RAW_QGOH_PPDD"],"revision":"CH_QGOH_PPDD@R001","sha256":"7205273c16eb141ed62783432e3d1f6b60d027cb5f86f30912e794c83547eaf7","sections":[{"number":"1","name":"Argumental Spine","items":[{"id":"CH_QGOH_PPDD:AS1","text":"Textus quaerit utrum substantia, cuius essentia existentiam involvit, necessario existat et utrum omne ens ad talem substantiam referatur.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_QGOH_PPDD:P0054","trace_type":"Structural","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"},{"id":"RAW_QGOH_PPDD:P0056","trace_type":"Structural","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"},{"id":"RAW_QGOH_PPDD:P0082","trace_type":"Structural","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"},{"id":"RAW_QGOH_PPDD:P0116","trace_type":"Structural","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_QGOH_PPDD:AS2","text":"Quaestio de Deo transfertur in quaestionem de differentiis inter id quod in se et per se concipitur et id quod in alio atque per aliud concipitur.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_QGOH_PPDD:P0007","trace_type":"Composite","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"},{"id":"RAW_QGOH_PPDD:P0009","trace_type":"Composite","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"},{"id":"RAW_QGOH_PPDD:P0018","trace_type":"Composite","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_QGOH_PPDD:AS3","text":"Argumentatio axiomatica-deductiva distinctionem substantiæ et modi convertit in exclusionem pluralitatis substantiarum eiusdem attributi et causalitatis externæ substantiæ.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_QGOH_PPDD:P0026","trace_type":"Structural","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"},{"id":"RAW_QGOH_PPDD:P0030","trace_type":"Structural","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"},{"id":"RAW_QGOH_PPDD:P0034","trace_type":"Structural","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"},{"id":"RAW_QGOH_PPDD:P0038","trace_type":"Structural","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"},{"id":"RAW_QGOH_PPDD:P0042","trace_type":"Structural","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"},{"id":"RAW_QGOH_PPDD:P0046","trace_type":"Structural","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_QGOH_PPDD:AS4","text":"Ex improductibilitate substantiæ deducitur eius causalitas sui, necessaria existentia et infinitas.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_QGOH_PPDD:P0046","trace_type":"Structural","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"},{"id":"RAW_QGOH_PPDD:P0050","trace_type":"Structural","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"},{"id":"RAW_QGOH_PPDD:P0054","trace_type":"Structural","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"},{"id":"RAW_QGOH_PPDD:P0056","trace_type":"Structural","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"},{"id":"RAW_QGOH_PPDD:P0058","trace_type":"Structural","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_QGOH_PPDD:AS5","text":"Deus definitur ut substantia absolute infinita; ex natura substantiæ eius existentia necessaria, unitas et indivisibilitas inferuntur.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_QGOH_PPDD:P0010","trace_type":"Composite","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"},{"id":"RAW_QGOH_PPDD:P0058","trace_type":"Composite","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"},{"id":"RAW_QGOH_PPDD:P0082","trace_type":"Composite","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"},{"id":"RAW_QGOH_PPDD:P0100","trace_type":"Composite","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_QGOH_PPDD:AS6","text":"Clausura argumenti negat substantiam extra Deum et omne ens, quatenus substantia vel modus est, in Deo collocat.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_QGOH_PPDD:P0108","trace_type":"Structural","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"},{"id":"RAW_QGOH_PPDD:P0114","trace_type":"Structural","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"},{"id":"RAW_QGOH_PPDD:P0116","trace_type":"Structural","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"},{"id":"RAW_QGOH_PPDD:P0118","trace_type":"Structural","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]}],"original_name":"Argumental Spine"},{"number":"2","name":"Load-Bearing Axioms","items":[{"id":"CH_QGOH_PPDD:A1","text":"Omnia quæ sunt vel in se vel in alio sunt.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_QGOH_PPDD:P0018","trace_type":"Direct","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_QGOH_PPDD:A2","text":"Substantia est id quod in se est et per se concipitur.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_QGOH_PPDD:P0007","trace_type":"Direct","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_QGOH_PPDD:A3","text":"Modus est substantiae affectio quae in alio est et per aliud concipitur.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_QGOH_PPDD:P0009","trace_type":"Direct","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_QGOH_PPDD:A4","text":"Res finita in suo genere terminatur ab alia re eiusdem naturæ.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_QGOH_PPDD:P0006","trace_type":"Direct","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_QGOH_PPDD:A5","text":"Attributum est id quod intellectus de substantia percipit tamquam eius essentiam constituens.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_QGOH_PPDD:P0008","trace_type":"Direct","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_QGOH_PPDD:A6","text":"Ex causa determinata effectus necessario sequitur, et sine causa determinata effectus sequi non potest.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_QGOH_PPDD:P0020","trace_type":"Direct","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_QGOH_PPDD:A7","text":"Cognitio effectus a cognitione causæ dependet eandemque involvit.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_QGOH_PPDD:P0021","trace_type":"Direct","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_QGOH_PPDD:A8","text":"Res quae nihil commune habent per se invicem intelligi non possunt.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_QGOH_PPDD:P0022","trace_type":"Direct","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_QGOH_PPDD:A9","text":"Idea vera cum suo ideato convenire debet.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_QGOH_PPDD:P0023","trace_type":"Direct","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_QGOH_PPDD:A10","text":"Cuius res non existens concipi potest, eius essentia existentiam non involvit.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_QGOH_PPDD:P0024","trace_type":"Direct","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_QGOH_PPDD:A11","text":"Causa sui est id cuius essentia existentiam involvit et cuius natura non potest concipi nisi existens.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_QGOH_PPDD:P0005","trace_type":"Direct","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_QGOH_PPDD:A12","text":"Deus est substantia infinitis attributis constans, quorum unumquodque aeternam et infinitam essentiam exprimit.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_QGOH_PPDD:P0010","trace_type":"Direct","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_QGOH_PPDD:A13","text":"Aeternitas est existentia quae ex sola rei aeternae definitione necessario sequitur nec per durationem vel tempus explicatur.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_QGOH_PPDD:P0014","trace_type":"Composite","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"},{"id":"RAW_QGOH_PPDD:P0016","trace_type":"Composite","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]}],"original_name":"Load-Bearing Axioms"},{"number":"3","name":"Apophatic Structure","items":[{"id":"CH_QGOH_PPDD:NX1","text":"Substantia neque in alio est neque per conceptum alterius rei concipitur; modus contra neutram independentiam habet.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_QGOH_PPDD:P0007","trace_type":"Negative","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"},{"id":"RAW_QGOH_PPDD:P0009","trace_type":"Negative","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_QGOH_PPDD:NX2","text":"Finitudo non per rem alterius naturæ, sed solum per terminum eiusdem naturæ definitur.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_QGOH_PPDD:P0006","trace_type":"Direct","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_QGOH_PPDD:NX3","text":"Infinitum absolute non est infinitum tantum in suo genere, quia nullum attributum essentiam exprimens ab eo negari potest.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_QGOH_PPDD:P0010","trace_type":"Negative","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"},{"id":"RAW_QGOH_PPDD:P0012","trace_type":"Negative","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_QGOH_PPDD:NX4","text":"Distinctio realis attributorum non sufficit ad pluralitatem substantiarum inferendam.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_QGOH_PPDD:P0079","trace_type":"Negative","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"},{"id":"RAW_QGOH_PPDD:P0080","trace_type":"Negative","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_QGOH_PPDD:NX5","text":"Substantia non est effectus causæ externæ neque res quae ab alia substantia produci possit.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_QGOH_PPDD:P0046","trace_type":"Negative","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"},{"id":"RAW_QGOH_PPDD:P0050","trace_type":"Negative","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_QGOH_PPDD:NX6","text":"Substantia, quatenus substantia est, non est divisibilis nec ex partibus substantiam retinentibus componitur.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_QGOH_PPDD:P0096","trace_type":"Negative","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"},{"id":"RAW_QGOH_PPDD:P0100","trace_type":"Negative","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"},{"id":"RAW_QGOH_PPDD:P0104","trace_type":"Negative","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_QGOH_PPDD:NX7","text":"Natura divina non est cum affectibus humanis aut cura humana confundenda.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_QGOH_PPDD:P0064","trace_type":"Negative","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]}],"original_name":"Apophatic Structure"},{"number":"4","name":"Derivative Propositions","items":[{"id":"CH_QGOH_PPDD:D1","text":"Substantia natura suis affectionibus prior est.","refs":["CH_QGOH_PPDD:A2","CH_QGOH_PPDD:A3"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"From","passages":[{"id":"RAW_QGOH_PPDD:P0026","trace_type":"Direct","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"},{"id":"RAW_QGOH_PPDD:P0028","trace_type":"Direct","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_QGOH_PPDD:D2","text":"Substantiae diversa attributa habentes nihil inter se commune habent.","refs":["CH_QGOH_PPDD:A2","CH_QGOH_PPDD:A5"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"From","passages":[{"id":"RAW_QGOH_PPDD:P0030","trace_type":"Direct","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"},{"id":"RAW_QGOH_PPDD:P0032","trace_type":"Direct","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_QGOH_PPDD:D3","text":"Res quae nihil commune habent una alterius causa esse non potest.","refs":["CH_QGOH_PPDD:A7","CH_QGOH_PPDD:A8","CH_QGOH_PPDD:D2"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"From","passages":[{"id":"RAW_QGOH_PPDD:P0034","trace_type":"Direct","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"},{"id":"RAW_QGOH_PPDD:P0036","trace_type":"Direct","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_QGOH_PPDD:D4","text":"Distinctio rerum extra intellectum solum ex diversitate attributorum substantiarum vel ex diversitate affectionum procedit.","refs":["CH_QGOH_PPDD:A1","CH_QGOH_PPDD:A2","CH_QGOH_PPDD:A3","CH_QGOH_PPDD:A5"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"From","passages":[{"id":"RAW_QGOH_PPDD:P0038","trace_type":"Direct","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"},{"id":"RAW_QGOH_PPDD:P0040","trace_type":"Direct","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_QGOH_PPDD:D5","text":"Duae aut plures substantiae eiusdem naturae sive attributi dari non possunt.","refs":["CH_QGOH_PPDD:A9","CH_QGOH_PPDD:D1","CH_QGOH_PPDD:D4"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"From","passages":[{"id":"RAW_QGOH_PPDD:P0042","trace_type":"Direct","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"},{"id":"RAW_QGOH_PPDD:P0044","trace_type":"Direct","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_QGOH_PPDD:D6","text":"Una substantia ab alia substantia produci non potest.","refs":["CH_QGOH_PPDD:D2","CH_QGOH_PPDD:D3","CH_QGOH_PPDD:D5"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"From","passages":[{"id":"RAW_QGOH_PPDD:P0046","trace_type":"Direct","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"},{"id":"RAW_QGOH_PPDD:P0048","trace_type":"Direct","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_QGOH_PPDD:D7","text":"Substantia ab alio produci non potest.","refs":["CH_QGOH_PPDD:A1","CH_QGOH_PPDD:A2","CH_QGOH_PPDD:A3","CH_QGOH_PPDD:D6"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"From","passages":[{"id":"RAW_QGOH_PPDD:P0050","trace_type":"Direct","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_QGOH_PPDD:D8","text":"Substantia, quia ab alio produci non potest, causa sui est; ideo eius essentia existentiam involvit.","refs":["CH_QGOH_PPDD:A11","CH_QGOH_PPDD:D7"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"From","passages":[{"id":"RAW_QGOH_PPDD:P0054","trace_type":"Direct","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"},{"id":"RAW_QGOH_PPDD:P0056","trace_type":"Direct","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_QGOH_PPDD:D9","text":"Omnis substantia necessario infinita est, quia finiri deberet ab alia substantia eiusdem naturae.","refs":["CH_QGOH_PPDD:A4","CH_QGOH_PPDD:D5","CH_QGOH_PPDD:D8"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"From","passages":[{"id":"RAW_QGOH_PPDD:P0058","trace_type":"Direct","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"},{"id":"RAW_QGOH_PPDD:P0060","trace_type":"Direct","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_QGOH_PPDD:D10","text":"Quo plus realitatis sive esse res habet, eo plura attributa ipsi competunt.","refs":["CH_QGOH_PPDD:A5"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"From","passages":[{"id":"RAW_QGOH_PPDD:P0071","trace_type":"Direct","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_QGOH_PPDD:D11","text":"Unumquodque attributum unius substantiae per se concipi debet.","refs":["CH_QGOH_PPDD:A2","CH_QGOH_PPDD:A5"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"From","passages":[{"id":"RAW_QGOH_PPDD:P0075","trace_type":"Direct","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"},{"id":"RAW_QGOH_PPDD:P0077","trace_type":"Direct","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_QGOH_PPDD:D12","text":"Plura attributa per se concepta unam eandemque substantiam exprimere possunt nec pluralitatem substantiarum necessario efficiunt.","refs":["CH_QGOH_PPDD:D10","CH_QGOH_PPDD:D11"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"From","passages":[{"id":"RAW_QGOH_PPDD:P0079","trace_type":"Direct","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_QGOH_PPDD:D13","text":"Deus, ut substantia infinitis attributis constans, necessario existit.","refs":["CH_QGOH_PPDD:A10","CH_QGOH_PPDD:A12","CH_QGOH_PPDD:D8"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"From","passages":[{"id":"RAW_QGOH_PPDD:P0082","trace_type":"Direct","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"},{"id":"RAW_QGOH_PPDD:P0084","trace_type":"Direct","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_QGOH_PPDD:D14","text":"Substantia absolute infinita indivisibilis est.","refs":["CH_QGOH_PPDD:D5","CH_QGOH_PPDD:D8","CH_QGOH_PPDD:D13"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"From","passages":[{"id":"RAW_QGOH_PPDD:P0096","trace_type":"Composite","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"},{"id":"RAW_QGOH_PPDD:P0100","trace_type":"Composite","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"},{"id":"RAW_QGOH_PPDD:P0102","trace_type":"Composite","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_QGOH_PPDD:D15","text":"Praeter Deum nulla substantia dari neque concipi potest.","refs":["CH_QGOH_PPDD:A12","CH_QGOH_PPDD:D5","CH_QGOH_PPDD:D13"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"From","passages":[{"id":"RAW_QGOH_PPDD:P0108","trace_type":"Direct","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"},{"id":"RAW_QGOH_PPDD:P0110","trace_type":"Direct","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_QGOH_PPDD:D16","text":"Quicquid est in Deo est, et nihil sine Deo esse neque concipi potest.","refs":["CH_QGOH_PPDD:A1","CH_QGOH_PPDD:A2","CH_QGOH_PPDD:A3","CH_QGOH_PPDD:D15"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"From","passages":[{"id":"RAW_QGOH_PPDD:P0116","trace_type":"Direct","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"},{"id":"RAW_QGOH_PPDD:P0118","trace_type":"Direct","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]}],"original_name":"Derivative Propositions"},{"number":"5","name":"Super-Derivatives","items":[{"id":"CH_QGOH_PPDD:SD1","text":"Explicatio substantiæ non procedit per seriem productionum externarum, sed per identitatem essentiae et existentiae.","refs":["CH_QGOH_PPDD:D7","CH_QGOH_PPDD:D8"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"From","passages":[{"id":"RAW_QGOH_PPDD:P0050","trace_type":"Composite","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"},{"id":"RAW_QGOH_PPDD:P0056","trace_type":"Composite","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_QGOH_PPDD:SD2","text":"Infinitas substantiæ non est quantitas indefinita, sed exclusio omnis termini homogeneae naturae et omnis negationis essentialis.","refs":["CH_QGOH_PPDD:A4","CH_QGOH_PPDD:A12","CH_QGOH_PPDD:D9"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"From","passages":[{"id":"RAW_QGOH_PPDD:P0006","trace_type":"Structural","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"},{"id":"RAW_QGOH_PPDD:P0010","trace_type":"Structural","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"},{"id":"RAW_QGOH_PPDD:P0012","trace_type":"Structural","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"},{"id":"RAW_QGOH_PPDD:P0058","trace_type":"Structural","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"},{"id":"RAW_QGOH_PPDD:P0062","trace_type":"Structural","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_QGOH_PPDD:SD3","text":"Multiplicitas attributorum non dividit substantiam, sed maiorem realitatem eiusdem substantiae exprimit.","refs":["CH_QGOH_PPDD:D10","CH_QGOH_PPDD:D11","CH_QGOH_PPDD:D12"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"From","passages":[{"id":"RAW_QGOH_PPDD:P0071","trace_type":"Composite","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"},{"id":"RAW_QGOH_PPDD:P0075","trace_type":"Composite","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"},{"id":"RAW_QGOH_PPDD:P0079","trace_type":"Composite","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_QGOH_PPDD:SD4","text":"Unitas Dei non significat exclusionem rerum, sed exclusionem omnis substantiae exterioris; res singulares ut modi intra naturam divinam ordinantur.","refs":["CH_QGOH_PPDD:D15","CH_QGOH_PPDD:D16"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"From","passages":[{"id":"RAW_QGOH_PPDD:P0108","trace_type":"Composite","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"},{"id":"RAW_QGOH_PPDD:P0112","trace_type":"Composite","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"},{"id":"RAW_QGOH_PPDD:P0116","trace_type":"Composite","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_QGOH_PPDD:SD5","text":"Extensio et cogitatio non constituunt ordines substantiales autonomos, sed attributa Dei vel affectiones attributorum Dei sunt.","refs":["CH_QGOH_PPDD:D15","CH_QGOH_PPDD:D16"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"From","passages":[{"id":"RAW_QGOH_PPDD:P0114","trace_type":"Direct","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"},{"id":"RAW_QGOH_PPDD:P0118","trace_type":"Direct","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_QGOH_PPDD:SD6","text":"Existentia aeterna substantiae non est duratio interminabilis, sed necessitas ex sola definitione rei orta.","refs":["CH_QGOH_PPDD:A13","CH_QGOH_PPDD:D8","CH_QGOH_PPDD:D13"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"From","passages":[{"id":"RAW_QGOH_PPDD:P0014","trace_type":"Composite","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"},{"id":"RAW_QGOH_PPDD:P0016","trace_type":"Composite","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"},{"id":"RAW_QGOH_PPDD:P0056","trace_type":"Composite","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"},{"id":"RAW_QGOH_PPDD:P0082","trace_type":"Composite","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]}],"original_name":"Super-Derivatives"},{"number":"6","name":"Deep Apophatics","items":[{"id":"CH_QGOH_PPDD:DNX1","text":"Falsum esse debet exemplar secundum quod omne ens, etiam substantia, ab externa causa producitur.","refs":["CH_QGOH_PPDD:D7","CH_QGOH_PPDD:D8"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"From","passages":[{"id":"RAW_QGOH_PPDD:P0050","trace_type":"Negative","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"},{"id":"RAW_QGOH_PPDD:P0056","trace_type":"Negative","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_QGOH_PPDD:DNX2","text":"Falsum esse debet exemplar secundum quod existentia substantiæ contingens est et ab essentia eius separabilis.","refs":["CH_QGOH_PPDD:A11","CH_QGOH_PPDD:D8"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"From","passages":[{"id":"RAW_QGOH_PPDD:P0005","trace_type":"Negative","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"},{"id":"RAW_QGOH_PPDD:P0056","trace_type":"Negative","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"},{"id":"RAW_QGOH_PPDD:P0064","trace_type":"Negative","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_QGOH_PPDD:DNX3","text":"Falsum esse debet principium quod attributa per se concepta tot substantias distinctas necessario constituunt.","refs":["CH_QGOH_PPDD:D11","CH_QGOH_PPDD:D12"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"From","passages":[{"id":"RAW_QGOH_PPDD:P0075","trace_type":"Negative","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"},{"id":"RAW_QGOH_PPDD:P0079","trace_type":"Negative","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_QGOH_PPDD:DNX4","text":"Falsum esse debet principium quod definitio naturae certum numerum individuorum ex se exprimat vel explicet.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_QGOH_PPDD:P0066","trace_type":"Negative","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"},{"id":"RAW_QGOH_PPDD:P0067","trace_type":"Negative","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_QGOH_PPDD:DNX5","text":"Idea clara et vera substantiae cum dubio de eius existentia componi non potest.","refs":["CH_QGOH_PPDD:A2","CH_QGOH_PPDD:A9","CH_QGOH_PPDD:D8"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"From","passages":[{"id":"RAW_QGOH_PPDD:P0065","trace_type":"Negative","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"},{"id":"RAW_QGOH_PPDD:P0066","trace_type":"Negative","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]}],"original_name":"Deep Apophatics"},{"number":"7","name":"Super-Apophatics","items":[{"id":"CH_QGOH_PPDD:SNX1","text":"Possibilitas ontologica vel conceptualis alicuius substantiae extra Deum praecluditur.","refs":["CH_QGOH_PPDD:D15"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"From","passages":[{"id":"RAW_QGOH_PPDD:P0108","trace_type":"Negative","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"},{"id":"RAW_QGOH_PPDD:P0110","trace_type":"Negative","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_QGOH_PPDD:SNX2","text":"Possibilitas causae internae vel externae quae existentiam Dei tollat praecluditur.","refs":["CH_QGOH_PPDD:D2","CH_QGOH_PPDD:D13"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"From","passages":[{"id":"RAW_QGOH_PPDD:P0087","trace_type":"Negative","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"},{"id":"RAW_QGOH_PPDD:P0088","trace_type":"Negative","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_QGOH_PPDD:SNX3","text":"Negatio essentialis non potest ad constitutionem entis absolute infiniti pertinere.","refs":["CH_QGOH_PPDD:A12"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"From","passages":[{"id":"RAW_QGOH_PPDD:P0012","trace_type":"Negative","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_QGOH_PPDD:SNX4","text":"Possibilitas quod existentia aeterna per tempus, etiam sine principio et fine, explicetur praecluditur.","refs":["CH_QGOH_PPDD:A13"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"From","passages":[{"id":"RAW_QGOH_PPDD:P0014","trace_type":"Negative","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"},{"id":"RAW_QGOH_PPDD:P0016","trace_type":"Negative","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]}],"original_name":"Super-Apophatics"},{"number":"8","name":"Formal Generative Schema","items":[{"id":"CH_QGOH_PPDD:FGS1","text":"In-se / in-alio → substantia / modus → dependentia conceptus modalis a substantia.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_QGOH_PPDD:P0007","trace_type":"Structural","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"},{"id":"RAW_QGOH_PPDD:P0009","trace_type":"Structural","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"},{"id":"RAW_QGOH_PPDD:P0018","trace_type":"Structural","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_QGOH_PPDD:FGS2","text":"Attributum / affectio → unica forma distinctionis rerum → exclusio pluralitatis substantiarum eiusdem attributi.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_QGOH_PPDD:P0008","trace_type":"Structural","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"},{"id":"RAW_QGOH_PPDD:P0038","trace_type":"Structural","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"},{"id":"RAW_QGOH_PPDD:P0042","trace_type":"Structural","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_QGOH_PPDD:FGS3","text":"Nulla communitas inter substantias diversarum naturarum → nulla causalitas mutua → nulla productio substantiae a substantia.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_QGOH_PPDD:P0030","trace_type":"Structural","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"},{"id":"RAW_QGOH_PPDD:P0034","trace_type":"Structural","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"},{"id":"RAW_QGOH_PPDD:P0046","trace_type":"Structural","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_QGOH_PPDD:FGS4","text":"Nulla productio externa → causa sui → essentia involvens existentiam → substantia necessario infinita.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_QGOH_PPDD:P0050","trace_type":"Structural","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"},{"id":"RAW_QGOH_PPDD:P0056","trace_type":"Structural","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"},{"id":"RAW_QGOH_PPDD:P0058","trace_type":"Structural","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_QGOH_PPDD:FGS5","text":"Substantia absolute infinita → Deus necessario existens et indivisibilis → nulla substantia extra Deum → modi omnes in Deo.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_QGOH_PPDD:P0082","trace_type":"Structural","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"},{"id":"RAW_QGOH_PPDD:P0100","trace_type":"Structural","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"},{"id":"RAW_QGOH_PPDD:P0108","trace_type":"Structural","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"},{"id":"RAW_QGOH_PPDD:P0116","trace_type":"Structural","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]}],"original_name":"Formal Generative Schema"},{"number":"9","name":"Argument Flow Map","items":[{"id":"CH_QGOH_PPDD:AF1","text":"Causa sui + substantia + attributum + modus → partitio entis in id quod in se est et id quod in alio est.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_QGOH_PPDD:P0005","trace_type":"Structural","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"},{"id":"RAW_QGOH_PPDD:P0007","trace_type":"Structural","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"},{"id":"RAW_QGOH_PPDD:P0008","trace_type":"Structural","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"},{"id":"RAW_QGOH_PPDD:P0009","trace_type":"Structural","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"},{"id":"RAW_QGOH_PPDD:P0018","trace_type":"Structural","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_QGOH_PPDD:AF2","text":"Partitio entis → distinctio per attributa vel affectiones → impossibilitas pluralitatis substantiarum eiusdem attributi.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_QGOH_PPDD:P0038","trace_type":"Structural","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"},{"id":"RAW_QGOH_PPDD:P0042","trace_type":"Structural","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"},{"id":"RAW_QGOH_PPDD:P0044","trace_type":"Structural","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_QGOH_PPDD:AF3","text":"Unitas eiusdem naturae + absentia communitatis → improductibilitas substantiæ.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_QGOH_PPDD:P0030","trace_type":"Structural","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"},{"id":"RAW_QGOH_PPDD:P0034","trace_type":"Structural","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"},{"id":"RAW_QGOH_PPDD:P0046","trace_type":"Structural","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_QGOH_PPDD:AF4","text":"Improductibilitas → causa sui → existentia necessaria → infinitas substantiæ.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_QGOH_PPDD:P0054","trace_type":"Structural","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"},{"id":"RAW_QGOH_PPDD:P0056","trace_type":"Structural","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"},{"id":"RAW_QGOH_PPDD:P0058","trace_type":"Structural","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_QGOH_PPDD:AF5","text":"Infinitas absoluta + attributa infinita → Deus necessario existens.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_QGOH_PPDD:P0010","trace_type":"Composite","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"},{"id":"RAW_QGOH_PPDD:P0082","trace_type":"Composite","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_QGOH_PPDD:AF6","text":"Necessitas et infinitas Dei → indivisibilitas → exclusio omnis substantiae exterioris.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_QGOH_PPDD:P0096","trace_type":"Structural","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"},{"id":"RAW_QGOH_PPDD:P0100","trace_type":"Structural","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"},{"id":"RAW_QGOH_PPDD:P0108","trace_type":"Structural","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_QGOH_PPDD:AF7","text":"Exclusio substantiae exterioris + dependentia modorum → quicquid est in Deo est et sine Deo nec est nec concipitur.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_QGOH_PPDD:P0110","trace_type":"Structural","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"},{"id":"RAW_QGOH_PPDD:P0116","trace_type":"Structural","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"},{"id":"RAW_QGOH_PPDD:P0118","trace_type":"Structural","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]}],"original_name":"Argument Flow Map"},{"number":"10","name":"Map-Effects","items":[{"id":"CH_QGOH_PPDD:ME1","text":"Res non iam ut individua aequaliter primaria disponuntur, sed ut substantia per se existens et modi ab ea conceptualiter dependentes.","refs":["CH_QGOH_PPDD:A1","CH_QGOH_PPDD:A2","CH_QGOH_PPDD:A3"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"From","passages":[{"id":"RAW_QGOH_PPDD:P0018","trace_type":"Composite","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"},{"id":"RAW_QGOH_PPDD:P0007","trace_type":"Composite","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"},{"id":"RAW_QGOH_PPDD:P0009","trace_type":"Composite","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_QGOH_PPDD:ME2","text":"Pluralitas numerica substantiarum non est species generalis entium, sed quaestio limitata per attributum et per causam externam individuorum.","refs":["CH_QGOH_PPDD:D5","CH_QGOH_PPDD:DNX4"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"From","passages":[{"id":"RAW_QGOH_PPDD:P0042","trace_type":"Structural","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"},{"id":"RAW_QGOH_PPDD:P0067","trace_type":"Structural","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"},{"id":"RAW_QGOH_PPDD:P0068","trace_type":"Structural","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_QGOH_PPDD:ME3","text":"Deus non collocatur iuxta res ut substantia una inter plures, sed ut unica substantia extra quam nulla substantia dari aut concipi potest.","refs":["CH_QGOH_PPDD:D13","CH_QGOH_PPDD:D15"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"From","passages":[{"id":"RAW_QGOH_PPDD:P0082","trace_type":"Composite","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"},{"id":"RAW_QGOH_PPDD:P0108","trace_type":"Composite","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"},{"id":"RAW_QGOH_PPDD:P0112","trace_type":"Composite","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_QGOH_PPDD:ME4","text":"Cogitatio et extensio reclassificantur ut attributa Dei vel affectiones attributorum Dei, non ut substantiae sibi invicem externae.","refs":["CH_QGOH_PPDD:D16","CH_QGOH_PPDD:SD5"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"From","passages":[{"id":"RAW_QGOH_PPDD:P0114","trace_type":"Direct","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"},{"id":"RAW_QGOH_PPDD:P0118","trace_type":"Direct","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_QGOH_PPDD:ME5","text":"Aeternitas reclassificatur ab ordine durationis ad ordinem necessitatis essentialis.","refs":["CH_QGOH_PPDD:A13","CH_QGOH_PPDD:SD6"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"From","passages":[{"id":"RAW_QGOH_PPDD:P0014","trace_type":"Direct","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"},{"id":"RAW_QGOH_PPDD:P0016","trace_type":"Direct","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]}],"original_name":"Map-Effects"},{"number":"11","name":"Altitude Levels","items":[{"id":"CH_QGOH_PPDD:ALT1","text":"Surface — Deus necessario existit, nulla substantia praeter Deum datur, et quicquid est in Deo est.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_QGOH_PPDD:P0082","trace_type":"Structural","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"},{"id":"RAW_QGOH_PPDD:P0108","trace_type":"Structural","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"},{"id":"RAW_QGOH_PPDD:P0116","trace_type":"Structural","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_QGOH_PPDD:ALT2","text":"Structural — Relatio substantiae ad modum est relatio independentiae ontologico-conceptualis ad dependentiam, cui attribuuntur causae, distinctiones et ordines entium.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_QGOH_PPDD:P0007","trace_type":"Structural","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"},{"id":"RAW_QGOH_PPDD:P0009","trace_type":"Structural","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"},{"id":"RAW_QGOH_PPDD:P0026","trace_type":"Structural","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"},{"id":"RAW_QGOH_PPDD:P0038","trace_type":"Structural","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_QGOH_PPDD:ALT3","text":"Metastrategic — Dubium de Deo vel de substantiae existentia reclassificatur ut confusio inter substantiam et modificationem, inter causam externam et causam sui, aut inter pluralitatem attributorum et pluralitatem substantiarum.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_QGOH_PPDD:P0064","trace_type":"Structural","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"},{"id":"RAW_QGOH_PPDD:P0079","trace_type":"Structural","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"},{"id":"RAW_QGOH_PPDD:P0084","trace_type":"Structural","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_QGOH_PPDD:ALT4","text":"Ontological/Temporal — Esse substantiae est necessarium, infinitum et aeternum; eius existentia non ex tempore, duratione aut eventu productivo intelligitur.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_QGOH_PPDD:P0005","trace_type":"Structural","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"},{"id":"RAW_QGOH_PPDD:P0010","trace_type":"Structural","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"},{"id":"RAW_QGOH_PPDD:P0014","trace_type":"Structural","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"},{"id":"RAW_QGOH_PPDD:P0056","trace_type":"Structural","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"},{"id":"RAW_QGOH_PPDD:P0058","trace_type":"Structural","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"},{"id":"RAW_QGOH_PPDD:P0082","trace_type":"Structural","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]}],"original_name":"Altitude Levels"},{"number":"12","name":"Minimal Reconstruction Test","items":[{"id":"CH_QGOH_PPDD:MRT1","text":"Axiomata de in-se, in-alio, conceptione per se, causalitate et finitudine sufficiunt ad seriem ab unitate substantiæ ad causalitatem sui, existentiam necessariam et infinitatem reconstruendam.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_QGOH_PPDD:P0005","trace_type":"Structural","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"},{"id":"RAW_QGOH_PPDD:P0007","trace_type":"Structural","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"},{"id":"RAW_QGOH_PPDD:P0009","trace_type":"Structural","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"},{"id":"RAW_QGOH_PPDD:P0018","trace_type":"Structural","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"},{"id":"RAW_QGOH_PPDD:P0020","trace_type":"Structural","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"},{"id":"RAW_QGOH_PPDD:P0022","trace_type":"Structural","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"},{"id":"RAW_QGOH_PPDD:P0026","trace_type":"Structural","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"},{"id":"RAW_QGOH_PPDD:P0046","trace_type":"Structural","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"},{"id":"RAW_QGOH_PPDD:P0054","trace_type":"Structural","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"},{"id":"RAW_QGOH_PPDD:P0058","trace_type":"Structural","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_QGOH_PPDD:MRT2","text":"Validitas clausurae finalis maxime pendet ab exhaustione ontologica: praeter substantias et earum modos nihil dari ponitur.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_QGOH_PPDD:P0018","trace_type":"Structural","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"},{"id":"RAW_QGOH_PPDD:P0007","trace_type":"Structural","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"},{"id":"RAW_QGOH_PPDD:P0009","trace_type":"Structural","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"},{"id":"RAW_QGOH_PPDD:P0116","trace_type":"Structural","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"},{"id":"RAW_QGOH_PPDD:P0118","trace_type":"Structural","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_QGOH_PPDD:MRT3","text":"Transitus a non-productione substantiæ per substantiam ad non-productionem substantiæ ab omni alio modorum causalitatem non plene explicat.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_QGOH_PPDD:P0046","trace_type":"Direct","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"},{"id":"RAW_QGOH_PPDD:P0050","trace_type":"Direct","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_QGOH_PPDD:MRT4","text":"Cohaerentia inter distinctionem realem attributorum et unitatem numericam unius substantiae in scholiis asseritur per simultaneam attributorum existentiam, sed nexus positivus ulterius non demonstratur.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_QGOH_PPDD:P0075","trace_type":"Direct","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"},{"id":"RAW_QGOH_PPDD:P0079","trace_type":"Direct","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_QGOH_PPDD:MRT5","text":"Demonstratio necessariae existentiae Dei requirit ut definitio entis absolute infiniti iam substantiam existentiae-capacem et omni attributo essentiali comprehensivam determinet.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_QGOH_PPDD:P0010","trace_type":"Composite","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"},{"id":"RAW_QGOH_PPDD:P0054","trace_type":"Composite","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"},{"id":"RAW_QGOH_PPDD:P0082","trace_type":"Composite","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_QGOH_PPDD:MRT6","text":"Restitutio motum textus servat, si catena improductibilitas → causa sui → existentia → infinitas → unicitas → immanentia retinetur, dum demonstrationes alternativa et defensio indivisibilitatis tamquam clausurae confirmatoriae agnoscuntur.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_QGOH_PPDD:P0046","trace_type":"Structural","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"},{"id":"RAW_QGOH_PPDD:P0050","trace_type":"Structural","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"},{"id":"RAW_QGOH_PPDD:P0054","trace_type":"Structural","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"},{"id":"RAW_QGOH_PPDD:P0058","trace_type":"Structural","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"},{"id":"RAW_QGOH_PPDD:P0082","trace_type":"Structural","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"},{"id":"RAW_QGOH_PPDD:P0100","trace_type":"Structural","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"},{"id":"RAW_QGOH_PPDD:P0108","trace_type":"Structural","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"},{"id":"RAW_QGOH_PPDD:P0116","trace_type":"Structural","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]}],"original_name":"Minimal Reconstruction Test"}],"language_code":"la","language_name":"Latina","translations":[{"id":"CAREVAR_20261015T014447202403Z_493886e8","language_code":"en","language_name":"English","title":"Part One - On God · passage 1","source_sha256":"7205273c16eb141ed62783432e3d1f6b60d027cb5f86f30912e794c83547eaf7","sha256":"650bfda4343b9d2236c0ffcf9437d2b5e7622e144ac06edca05a36dca5d8fc0b","rendition_type":"machine","review_status":"machine","model":"gpt-5.6-terra","created":"2026-10-15T01:44:47.203291+00:00","sections":[{"number":"1","name":"Argumentative Spine","items":[{"id":"CH_QGOH_PPDD:AS1","text":"The text asks whether substance, whose essence involves existence, necessarily exists and whether every being is referred to such a substance.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_QGOH_PPDD:P0054","trace_type":"Structural","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"},{"id":"RAW_QGOH_PPDD:P0056","trace_type":"Structural","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"},{"id":"RAW_QGOH_PPDD:P0082","trace_type":"Structural","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"},{"id":"RAW_QGOH_PPDD:P0116","trace_type":"Structural","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_QGOH_PPDD:AS2","text":"The question of God is transferred into the question of the differences between that which is conceived in itself and through itself and that which is conceived in another and through another.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_QGOH_PPDD:P0007","trace_type":"Composite","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"},{"id":"RAW_QGOH_PPDD:P0009","trace_type":"Composite","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"},{"id":"RAW_QGOH_PPDD:P0018","trace_type":"Composite","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_QGOH_PPDD:AS3","text":"The axiomatic-deductive argument converts the distinction between substance and mode into the exclusion of a plurality of substances of the same attribute and of the external causality of substance.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_QGOH_PPDD:P0026","trace_type":"Structural","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"},{"id":"RAW_QGOH_PPDD:P0030","trace_type":"Structural","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"},{"id":"RAW_QGOH_PPDD:P0034","trace_type":"Structural","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"},{"id":"RAW_QGOH_PPDD:P0038","trace_type":"Structural","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"},{"id":"RAW_QGOH_PPDD:P0042","trace_type":"Structural","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"},{"id":"RAW_QGOH_PPDD:P0046","trace_type":"Structural","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_QGOH_PPDD:AS4","text":"From the unproducibility of substance are deduced its self-causality, necessary existence, and infinity.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_QGOH_PPDD:P0046","trace_type":"Structural","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"},{"id":"RAW_QGOH_PPDD:P0050","trace_type":"Structural","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"},{"id":"RAW_QGOH_PPDD:P0054","trace_type":"Structural","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"},{"id":"RAW_QGOH_PPDD:P0056","trace_type":"Structural","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"},{"id":"RAW_QGOH_PPDD:P0058","trace_type":"Structural","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_QGOH_PPDD:AS5","text":"God is defined as absolutely infinite substance; from the nature of substance are inferred its necessary existence, unity, and indivisibility.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_QGOH_PPDD:P0010","trace_type":"Composite","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"},{"id":"RAW_QGOH_PPDD:P0058","trace_type":"Composite","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"},{"id":"RAW_QGOH_PPDD:P0082","trace_type":"Composite","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"},{"id":"RAW_QGOH_PPDD:P0100","trace_type":"Composite","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_QGOH_PPDD:AS6","text":"The closure of the argument denies substance outside God and places every being, insofar as it is substance or mode, in God.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_QGOH_PPDD:P0108","trace_type":"Structural","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"},{"id":"RAW_QGOH_PPDD:P0114","trace_type":"Structural","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"},{"id":"RAW_QGOH_PPDD:P0116","trace_type":"Structural","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"},{"id":"RAW_QGOH_PPDD:P0118","trace_type":"Structural","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]}]},{"number":"2","name":"Load-Bearing Axioms","items":[{"id":"CH_QGOH_PPDD:A1","text":"Everything that is, is either in itself or in another.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_QGOH_PPDD:P0018","trace_type":"Direct","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_QGOH_PPDD:A2","text":"Substance is that which is in itself and is conceived through itself.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_QGOH_PPDD:P0007","trace_type":"Direct","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_QGOH_PPDD:A3","text":"A mode is an affection of substance which is in another and is conceived through another.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_QGOH_PPDD:P0009","trace_type":"Direct","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_QGOH_PPDD:A4","text":"A thing finite in its kind is limited by another thing of the same nature.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_QGOH_PPDD:P0006","trace_type":"Direct","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_QGOH_PPDD:A5","text":"An attribute is that which the intellect perceives of substance as constituting its essence.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_QGOH_PPDD:P0008","trace_type":"Direct","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_QGOH_PPDD:A6","text":"From a determinate cause an effect necessarily follows, and without a determinate cause an effect cannot follow.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_QGOH_PPDD:P0020","trace_type":"Direct","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_QGOH_PPDD:A7","text":"Knowledge of the effect depends on and involves knowledge of the cause.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_QGOH_PPDD:P0021","trace_type":"Direct","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_QGOH_PPDD:A8","text":"Things which have nothing in common cannot be understood through one another.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_QGOH_PPDD:P0022","trace_type":"Direct","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_QGOH_PPDD:A9","text":"A true idea must agree with its ideate.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_QGOH_PPDD:P0023","trace_type":"Direct","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_QGOH_PPDD:A10","text":"If a non-existing thing can be conceived, its essence does not involve existence.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_QGOH_PPDD:P0024","trace_type":"Direct","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_QGOH_PPDD:A11","text":"The cause of itself is that whose essence involves existence and whose nature cannot be conceived except as existing.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_QGOH_PPDD:P0005","trace_type":"Direct","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_QGOH_PPDD:A12","text":"God is a substance consisting of infinite attributes, each of which expresses eternal and infinite essence.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_QGOH_PPDD:P0010","trace_type":"Direct","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_QGOH_PPDD:A13","text":"Eternity is existence which necessarily follows from the definition alone of an eternal thing and is not explained through duration or time.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_QGOH_PPDD:P0014","trace_type":"Composite","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"},{"id":"RAW_QGOH_PPDD:P0016","trace_type":"Composite","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]}],"original_name":"Load-Bearing Axioms"},{"number":"3","name":"Apophatic Structure","items":[{"id":"CH_QGOH_PPDD:NX1","text":"Substance is neither in another nor conceived through the concept of another thing; a mode, by contrast, possesses neither independence.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_QGOH_PPDD:P0007","trace_type":"Negative","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"},{"id":"RAW_QGOH_PPDD:P0009","trace_type":"Negative","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_QGOH_PPDD:NX2","text":"Finitude is defined not through a thing of another nature, but only through a limit of the same nature.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_QGOH_PPDD:P0006","trace_type":"Direct","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_QGOH_PPDD:NX3","text":"The absolutely infinite is not infinite merely in its kind, because no attribute expressing essence can be denied of it.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_QGOH_PPDD:P0010","trace_type":"Negative","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"},{"id":"RAW_QGOH_PPDD:P0012","trace_type":"Negative","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_QGOH_PPDD:NX4","text":"The real distinction of attributes is not sufficient for inferring a plurality of substances.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_QGOH_PPDD:P0079","trace_type":"Negative","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"},{"id":"RAW_QGOH_PPDD:P0080","trace_type":"Negative","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_QGOH_PPDD:NX5","text":"Substance is not the effect of an external cause, nor a thing that can be produced by another substance.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_QGOH_PPDD:P0046","trace_type":"Negative","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"},{"id":"RAW_QGOH_PPDD:P0050","trace_type":"Negative","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_QGOH_PPDD:NX6","text":"Substance, insofar as it is substance, is not divisible, nor is it composed of parts retaining the nature of substance.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_QGOH_PPDD:P0096","trace_type":"Negative","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"},{"id":"RAW_QGOH_PPDD:P0100","trace_type":"Negative","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"},{"id":"RAW_QGOH_PPDD:P0104","trace_type":"Negative","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_QGOH_PPDD:NX7","text":"Divine nature is not to be confused with human affections or human concern.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_QGOH_PPDD:P0064","trace_type":"Negative","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]}],"original_name":"Apophatic Structure"},{"number":"4","name":"Derivative Propositions","items":[{"id":"CH_QGOH_PPDD:D1","text":"By nature, substance is prior to its affections.","refs":["CH_QGOH_PPDD:A2","CH_QGOH_PPDD:A3"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"From","passages":[{"id":"RAW_QGOH_PPDD:P0026","trace_type":"Direct","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"},{"id":"RAW_QGOH_PPDD:P0028","trace_type":"Direct","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_QGOH_PPDD:D2","text":"Substances having different attributes have nothing in common with one another.","refs":["CH_QGOH_PPDD:A2","CH_QGOH_PPDD:A5"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"From","passages":[{"id":"RAW_QGOH_PPDD:P0030","trace_type":"Direct","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"},{"id":"RAW_QGOH_PPDD:P0032","trace_type":"Direct","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_QGOH_PPDD:D3","text":"Things which have nothing in common cannot be causes of one another.","refs":["CH_QGOH_PPDD:A7","CH_QGOH_PPDD:A8","CH_QGOH_PPDD:D2"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"From","passages":[{"id":"RAW_QGOH_PPDD:P0034","trace_type":"Direct","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"},{"id":"RAW_QGOH_PPDD:P0036","trace_type":"Direct","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_QGOH_PPDD:D4","text":"The distinction of things outside the intellect proceeds solely from the diversity of the attributes of substances or from the diversity of affections.","refs":["CH_QGOH_PPDD:A1","CH_QGOH_PPDD:A2","CH_QGOH_PPDD:A3","CH_QGOH_PPDD:A5"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"From","passages":[{"id":"RAW_QGOH_PPDD:P0038","trace_type":"Direct","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"},{"id":"RAW_QGOH_PPDD:P0040","trace_type":"Direct","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_QGOH_PPDD:D5","text":"Two or more substances of the same nature or attribute cannot be given.","refs":["CH_QGOH_PPDD:A9","CH_QGOH_PPDD:D1","CH_QGOH_PPDD:D4"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"From","passages":[{"id":"RAW_QGOH_PPDD:P0042","trace_type":"Direct","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"},{"id":"RAW_QGOH_PPDD:P0044","trace_type":"Direct","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_QGOH_PPDD:D6","text":"One substance cannot be produced by another substance.","refs":["CH_QGOH_PPDD:D2","CH_QGOH_PPDD:D3","CH_QGOH_PPDD:D5"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"From","passages":[{"id":"RAW_QGOH_PPDD:P0046","trace_type":"Direct","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"},{"id":"RAW_QGOH_PPDD:P0048","trace_type":"Direct","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_QGOH_PPDD:D7","text":"Substance cannot be produced by another.","refs":["CH_QGOH_PPDD:A1","CH_QGOH_PPDD:A2","CH_QGOH_PPDD:A3","CH_QGOH_PPDD:D6"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"From","passages":[{"id":"RAW_QGOH_PPDD:P0050","trace_type":"Direct","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_QGOH_PPDD:D8","text":"Substance, because it cannot be produced by another, is the cause of itself; therefore its essence involves existence.","refs":["CH_QGOH_PPDD:A11","CH_QGOH_PPDD:D7"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"From","passages":[{"id":"RAW_QGOH_PPDD:P0054","trace_type":"Direct","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"},{"id":"RAW_QGOH_PPDD:P0056","trace_type":"Direct","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_QGOH_PPDD:D9","text":"Every substance is necessarily infinite, because it would have to be limited by another substance of the same nature.","refs":["CH_QGOH_PPDD:A4","CH_QGOH_PPDD:D5","CH_QGOH_PPDD:D8"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"From","passages":[{"id":"RAW_QGOH_PPDD:P0058","trace_type":"Direct","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"},{"id":"RAW_QGOH_PPDD:P0060","trace_type":"Direct","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_QGOH_PPDD:D10","text":"The more reality or being a thing has, the more attributes belong to it.","refs":["CH_QGOH_PPDD:A5"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"From","passages":[{"id":"RAW_QGOH_PPDD:P0071","trace_type":"Direct","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_QGOH_PPDD:D11","text":"Each attribute of one substance must be conceived through itself.","refs":["CH_QGOH_PPDD:A2","CH_QGOH_PPDD:A5"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"From","passages":[{"id":"RAW_QGOH_PPDD:P0075","trace_type":"Direct","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"},{"id":"RAW_QGOH_PPDD:P0077","trace_type":"Direct","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_QGOH_PPDD:D12","text":"Several attributes conceived through themselves can express one and the same substance and do not necessarily produce a plurality of substances.","refs":["CH_QGOH_PPDD:D10","CH_QGOH_PPDD:D11"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"From","passages":[{"id":"RAW_QGOH_PPDD:P0079","trace_type":"Direct","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_QGOH_PPDD:D13","text":"God, as a substance consisting of infinite attributes, necessarily exists.","refs":["CH_QGOH_PPDD:A10","CH_QGOH_PPDD:A12","CH_QGOH_PPDD:D8"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"From","passages":[{"id":"RAW_QGOH_PPDD:P0082","trace_type":"Direct","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"},{"id":"RAW_QGOH_PPDD:P0084","trace_type":"Direct","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_QGOH_PPDD:D14","text":"Absolutely infinite substance is indivisible.","refs":["CH_QGOH_PPDD:D5","CH_QGOH_PPDD:D8","CH_QGOH_PPDD:D13"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"From","passages":[{"id":"RAW_QGOH_PPDD:P0096","trace_type":"Composite","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"},{"id":"RAW_QGOH_PPDD:P0100","trace_type":"Composite","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"},{"id":"RAW_QGOH_PPDD:P0102","trace_type":"Composite","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_QGOH_PPDD:D15","text":"Apart from God, no substance can be given or conceived.","refs":["CH_QGOH_PPDD:A12","CH_QGOH_PPDD:D5","CH_QGOH_PPDD:D13"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"From","passages":[{"id":"RAW_QGOH_PPDD:P0108","trace_type":"Direct","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"},{"id":"RAW_QGOH_PPDD:P0110","trace_type":"Direct","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_QGOH_PPDD:D16","text":"Whatever is, is in God, and nothing can be or be conceived without God.","refs":["CH_QGOH_PPDD:A1","CH_QGOH_PPDD:A2","CH_QGOH_PPDD:A3","CH_QGOH_PPDD:D15"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"From","passages":[{"id":"RAW_QGOH_PPDD:P0116","trace_type":"Direct","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"},{"id":"RAW_QGOH_PPDD:P0118","trace_type":"Direct","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]}],"original_name":"Derivative Propositions"},{"number":"5","name":"Super-Derivatives","items":[{"id":"CH_QGOH_PPDD:SD1","text":"The explanation of substance does not proceed through a series of external productions, but through the identity of essence and existence.","refs":["CH_QGOH_PPDD:D7","CH_QGOH_PPDD:D8"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"From","passages":[{"id":"RAW_QGOH_PPDD:P0050","trace_type":"Composite","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"},{"id":"RAW_QGOH_PPDD:P0056","trace_type":"Composite","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_QGOH_PPDD:SD2","text":"The infinity of substance is not indefinite quantity, but the exclusion of every limit of homogeneous nature and of every essential negation.","refs":["CH_QGOH_PPDD:A4","CH_QGOH_PPDD:A12","CH_QGOH_PPDD:D9"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"From","passages":[{"id":"RAW_QGOH_PPDD:P0006","trace_type":"Structural","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"},{"id":"RAW_QGOH_PPDD:P0010","trace_type":"Structural","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"},{"id":"RAW_QGOH_PPDD:P0012","trace_type":"Structural","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"},{"id":"RAW_QGOH_PPDD:P0058","trace_type":"Structural","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"},{"id":"RAW_QGOH_PPDD:P0062","trace_type":"Structural","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_QGOH_PPDD:SD3","text":"The multiplicity of attributes does not divide substance, but expresses the greater reality of the same substance.","refs":["CH_QGOH_PPDD:D10","CH_QGOH_PPDD:D11","CH_QGOH_PPDD:D12"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"From","passages":[{"id":"RAW_QGOH_PPDD:P0071","trace_type":"Composite","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"},{"id":"RAW_QGOH_PPDD:P0075","trace_type":"Composite","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"},{"id":"RAW_QGOH_PPDD:P0079","trace_type":"Composite","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_QGOH_PPDD:SD4","text":"The unity of God does not signify the exclusion of things, but the exclusion of every external substance; singular things are ordered as modes within divine nature.","refs":["CH_QGOH_PPDD:D15","CH_QGOH_PPDD:D16"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"From","passages":[{"id":"RAW_QGOH_PPDD:P0108","trace_type":"Composite","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"},{"id":"RAW_QGOH_PPDD:P0112","trace_type":"Composite","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"},{"id":"RAW_QGOH_PPDD:P0116","trace_type":"Composite","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_QGOH_PPDD:SD5","text":"Extension and thought do not constitute autonomous substantial orders, but are attributes of God or affections of the attributes of God.","refs":["CH_QGOH_PPDD:D15","CH_QGOH_PPDD:D16"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"From","passages":[{"id":"RAW_QGOH_PPDD:P0114","trace_type":"Direct","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"},{"id":"RAW_QGOH_PPDD:P0118","trace_type":"Direct","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_QGOH_PPDD:SD6","text":"The eternal existence of substance is not interminable duration, but necessity arising from the definition of the thing alone.","refs":["CH_QGOH_PPDD:A13","CH_QGOH_PPDD:D8","CH_QGOH_PPDD:D13"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"From","passages":[{"id":"RAW_QGOH_PPDD:P0014","trace_type":"Composite","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"},{"id":"RAW_QGOH_PPDD:P0016","trace_type":"Composite","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"},{"id":"RAW_QGOH_PPDD:P0056","trace_type":"Composite","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"},{"id":"RAW_QGOH_PPDD:P0082","trace_type":"Composite","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]}],"original_name":"Super-Derivatives"},{"number":"6","name":"Deep Apophatics","items":[{"id":"CH_QGOH_PPDD:DNX1","text":"The model according to which every being, including substance, is produced by an external cause must be false.","refs":["CH_QGOH_PPDD:D7","CH_QGOH_PPDD:D8"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"From","passages":[{"id":"RAW_QGOH_PPDD:P0050","trace_type":"Negative","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"},{"id":"RAW_QGOH_PPDD:P0056","trace_type":"Negative","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_QGOH_PPDD:DNX2","text":"The model according to which the existence of substance is contingent and separable from its essence must be false.","refs":["CH_QGOH_PPDD:A11","CH_QGOH_PPDD:D8"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"From","passages":[{"id":"RAW_QGOH_PPDD:P0005","trace_type":"Negative","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"},{"id":"RAW_QGOH_PPDD:P0056","trace_type":"Negative","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"},{"id":"RAW_QGOH_PPDD:P0064","trace_type":"Negative","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_QGOH_PPDD:DNX3","text":"The principle that attributes conceived through themselves necessarily constitute so many distinct substances must be false.","refs":["CH_QGOH_PPDD:D11","CH_QGOH_PPDD:D12"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"From","passages":[{"id":"RAW_QGOH_PPDD:P0075","trace_type":"Negative","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"},{"id":"RAW_QGOH_PPDD:P0079","trace_type":"Negative","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_QGOH_PPDD:DNX4","text":"The principle that the definition of a nature expresses or explains from itself a determinate number of individuals must be false.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_QGOH_PPDD:P0066","trace_type":"Negative","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"},{"id":"RAW_QGOH_PPDD:P0067","trace_type":"Negative","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_QGOH_PPDD:DNX5","text":"A clear and true idea of substance cannot be reconciled with doubt concerning its existence.","refs":["CH_QGOH_PPDD:A2","CH_QGOH_PPDD:A9","CH_QGOH_PPDD:D8"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"From","passages":[{"id":"RAW_QGOH_PPDD:P0065","trace_type":"Negative","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"},{"id":"RAW_QGOH_PPDD:P0066","trace_type":"Negative","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]}],"original_name":"Deep Apophatics"},{"number":"7","name":"Super-Apophatics","items":[{"id":"CH_QGOH_PPDD:SNX1","text":"The ontological or conceptual possibility of any substance outside God is precluded.","refs":["CH_QGOH_PPDD:D15"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"From","passages":[{"id":"RAW_QGOH_PPDD:P0108","trace_type":"Negative","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"},{"id":"RAW_QGOH_PPDD:P0110","trace_type":"Negative","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_QGOH_PPDD:SNX2","text":"The possibility of an internal or external cause that removes the existence of God is precluded.","refs":["CH_QGOH_PPDD:D2","CH_QGOH_PPDD:D13"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"From","passages":[{"id":"RAW_QGOH_PPDD:P0087","trace_type":"Negative","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"},{"id":"RAW_QGOH_PPDD:P0088","trace_type":"Negative","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_QGOH_PPDD:SNX3","text":"Essential negation cannot belong to the constitution of an absolutely infinite being.","refs":["CH_QGOH_PPDD:A12"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"From","passages":[{"id":"RAW_QGOH_PPDD:P0012","trace_type":"Negative","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_QGOH_PPDD:SNX4","text":"The possibility that eternal existence be explained through time, even without beginning and end, is precluded.","refs":["CH_QGOH_PPDD:A13"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"From","passages":[{"id":"RAW_QGOH_PPDD:P0014","trace_type":"Negative","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"},{"id":"RAW_QGOH_PPDD:P0016","trace_type":"Negative","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]}],"original_name":"Super-Apophatics"},{"number":"8","name":"Formal Generative Schema","items":[{"id":"CH_QGOH_PPDD:FGS1","text":"In-itself / in-another → substance / mode → dependence of the modal concept on substance.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_QGOH_PPDD:P0007","trace_type":"Structural","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"},{"id":"RAW_QGOH_PPDD:P0009","trace_type":"Structural","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"},{"id":"RAW_QGOH_PPDD:P0018","trace_type":"Structural","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_QGOH_PPDD:FGS2","text":"Attribute / affection → sole form of the distinction of things → exclusion of a plurality of substances of the same attribute.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_QGOH_PPDD:P0008","trace_type":"Structural","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"},{"id":"RAW_QGOH_PPDD:P0038","trace_type":"Structural","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"},{"id":"RAW_QGOH_PPDD:P0042","trace_type":"Structural","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_QGOH_PPDD:FGS3","text":"No community between substances of different natures → no mutual causality → no production of substance by substance.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_QGOH_PPDD:P0030","trace_type":"Structural","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"},{"id":"RAW_QGOH_PPDD:P0034","trace_type":"Structural","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"},{"id":"RAW_QGOH_PPDD:P0046","trace_type":"Structural","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_QGOH_PPDD:FGS4","text":"No external production → cause of itself → essence involving existence → necessarily infinite substance.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_QGOH_PPDD:P0050","trace_type":"Structural","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"},{"id":"RAW_QGOH_PPDD:P0056","trace_type":"Structural","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"},{"id":"RAW_QGOH_PPDD:P0058","trace_type":"Structural","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_QGOH_PPDD:FGS5","text":"Absolutely infinite substance → necessarily existing and indivisible God → no substance outside God → all modes in God.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_QGOH_PPDD:P0082","trace_type":"Structural","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"},{"id":"RAW_QGOH_PPDD:P0100","trace_type":"Structural","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"},{"id":"RAW_QGOH_PPDD:P0108","trace_type":"Structural","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"},{"id":"RAW_QGOH_PPDD:P0116","trace_type":"Structural","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]}],"original_name":"Formal Generative Schema"},{"number":"9","name":"Argument Flow Map","items":[{"id":"CH_QGOH_PPDD:AF1","text":"Cause of itself + substance + attribute + mode → partition of being into that which is in itself and that which is in another.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_QGOH_PPDD:P0005","trace_type":"Structural","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"},{"id":"RAW_QGOH_PPDD:P0007","trace_type":"Structural","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"},{"id":"RAW_QGOH_PPDD:P0008","trace_type":"Structural","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"},{"id":"RAW_QGOH_PPDD:P0009","trace_type":"Structural","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"},{"id":"RAW_QGOH_PPDD:P0018","trace_type":"Structural","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_QGOH_PPDD:AF2","text":"Partition of being → distinction through attributes or affections → impossibility of a plurality of substances of the same attribute.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_QGOH_PPDD:P0038","trace_type":"Structural","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"},{"id":"RAW_QGOH_PPDD:P0042","trace_type":"Structural","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"},{"id":"RAW_QGOH_PPDD:P0044","trace_type":"Structural","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_QGOH_PPDD:AF3","text":"Unity of the same nature + absence of community → unproducibility of substance.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_QGOH_PPDD:P0030","trace_type":"Structural","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"},{"id":"RAW_QGOH_PPDD:P0034","trace_type":"Structural","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"},{"id":"RAW_QGOH_PPDD:P0046","trace_type":"Structural","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_QGOH_PPDD:AF4","text":"Unproducibility → cause of itself → necessary existence → infinity of substance.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_QGOH_PPDD:P0054","trace_type":"Structural","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"},{"id":"RAW_QGOH_PPDD:P0056","trace_type":"Structural","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"},{"id":"RAW_QGOH_PPDD:P0058","trace_type":"Structural","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_QGOH_PPDD:AF5","text":"Absolute infinity + infinite attributes → necessarily existing God.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_QGOH_PPDD:P0010","trace_type":"Composite","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"},{"id":"RAW_QGOH_PPDD:P0082","trace_type":"Composite","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_QGOH_PPDD:AF6","text":"Necessity and infinity of God → indivisibility → exclusion of every external substance.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_QGOH_PPDD:P0096","trace_type":"Structural","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"},{"id":"RAW_QGOH_PPDD:P0100","trace_type":"Structural","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"},{"id":"RAW_QGOH_PPDD:P0108","trace_type":"Structural","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_QGOH_PPDD:AF7","text":"Exclusion of external substance + dependence of modes → whatever is, is in God, and without God neither is nor is conceived.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_QGOH_PPDD:P0110","trace_type":"Structural","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"},{"id":"RAW_QGOH_PPDD:P0116","trace_type":"Structural","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"},{"id":"RAW_QGOH_PPDD:P0118","trace_type":"Structural","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]}],"original_name":"Argument Flow Map"},{"number":"10","name":"Map-Effects","items":[{"id":"CH_QGOH_PPDD:ME1","text":"Things are no longer arranged as equally primary individuals, but as self-existing substance and modes conceptually dependent upon it.","refs":["CH_QGOH_PPDD:A1","CH_QGOH_PPDD:A2","CH_QGOH_PPDD:A3"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"From","passages":[{"id":"RAW_QGOH_PPDD:P0018","trace_type":"Composite","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"},{"id":"RAW_QGOH_PPDD:P0007","trace_type":"Composite","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"},{"id":"RAW_QGOH_PPDD:P0009","trace_type":"Composite","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_QGOH_PPDD:ME2","text":"The numerical plurality of substances is not a general species of beings, but a question limited by attribute and by the external cause of individuals.","refs":["CH_QGOH_PPDD:D5","CH_QGOH_PPDD:DNX4"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"From","passages":[{"id":"RAW_QGOH_PPDD:P0042","trace_type":"Structural","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"},{"id":"RAW_QGOH_PPDD:P0067","trace_type":"Structural","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"},{"id":"RAW_QGOH_PPDD:P0068","trace_type":"Structural","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_QGOH_PPDD:ME3","text":"God is not placed alongside things as one substance among many, but as the sole substance outside which no substance can be given or conceived.","refs":["CH_QGOH_PPDD:D13","CH_QGOH_PPDD:D15"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"From","passages":[{"id":"RAW_QGOH_PPDD:P0082","trace_type":"Composite","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"},{"id":"RAW_QGOH_PPDD:P0108","trace_type":"Composite","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"},{"id":"RAW_QGOH_PPDD:P0112","trace_type":"Composite","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_QGOH_PPDD:ME4","text":"Thought and extension are reclassified as attributes of God or affections of the attributes of God, not as substances external to one another.","refs":["CH_QGOH_PPDD:D16","CH_QGOH_PPDD:SD5"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"From","passages":[{"id":"RAW_QGOH_PPDD:P0114","trace_type":"Direct","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"},{"id":"RAW_QGOH_PPDD:P0118","trace_type":"Direct","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_QGOH_PPDD:ME5","text":"Eternity is reclassified from the order of duration to the order of essential necessity.","refs":["CH_QGOH_PPDD:A13","CH_QGOH_PPDD:SD6"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"From","passages":[{"id":"RAW_QGOH_PPDD:P0014","trace_type":"Direct","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"},{"id":"RAW_QGOH_PPDD:P0016","trace_type":"Direct","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]}],"original_name":"Map-Effects"},{"number":"11","name":"Altitude Levels","items":[{"id":"CH_QGOH_PPDD:ALT1","text":"Surface — God necessarily exists, no substance apart from God is given, and whatever is, is in God.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_QGOH_PPDD:P0082","trace_type":"Structural","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"},{"id":"RAW_QGOH_PPDD:P0108","trace_type":"Structural","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"},{"id":"RAW_QGOH_PPDD:P0116","trace_type":"Structural","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_QGOH_PPDD:ALT2","text":"Structural — The relation of substance to mode is a relation of ontological-conceptual independence to dependence, to which causes, distinctions, and orders of beings are attributed.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_QGOH_PPDD:P0007","trace_type":"Structural","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"},{"id":"RAW_QGOH_PPDD:P0009","trace_type":"Structural","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"},{"id":"RAW_QGOH_PPDD:P0026","trace_type":"Structural","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"},{"id":"RAW_QGOH_PPDD:P0038","trace_type":"Structural","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_QGOH_PPDD:ALT3","text":"Metastrategic — Doubt concerning God or the existence of substance is reclassified as confusion between substance and modification, between external cause and cause of itself, or between plurality of attributes and plurality of substances.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_QGOH_PPDD:P0064","trace_type":"Structural","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"},{"id":"RAW_QGOH_PPDD:P0079","trace_type":"Structural","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"},{"id":"RAW_QGOH_PPDD:P0084","trace_type":"Structural","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_QGOH_PPDD:ALT4","text":"Ontological/Temporal — The being of substance is necessary, infinite, and eternal; its existence is not understood from time, duration, or a productive event.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_QGOH_PPDD:P0005","trace_type":"Structural","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"},{"id":"RAW_QGOH_PPDD:P0010","trace_type":"Structural","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"},{"id":"RAW_QGOH_PPDD:P0014","trace_type":"Structural","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"},{"id":"RAW_QGOH_PPDD:P0056","trace_type":"Structural","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"},{"id":"RAW_QGOH_PPDD:P0058","trace_type":"Structural","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"},{"id":"RAW_QGOH_PPDD:P0082","trace_type":"Structural","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]}],"original_name":"Altitude Levels"},{"number":"12","name":"Minimal Reconstruction Test","items":[{"id":"CH_QGOH_PPDD:MRT1","text":"The axioms concerning in-itself, in-another, conception through itself, causality, and finitude suffice to reconstruct the sequence from the unity of substance to self-causality, necessary existence, and infinity.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_QGOH_PPDD:P0005","trace_type":"Structural","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"},{"id":"RAW_QGOH_PPDD:P0007","trace_type":"Structural","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"},{"id":"RAW_QGOH_PPDD:P0009","trace_type":"Structural","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"},{"id":"RAW_QGOH_PPDD:P0018","trace_type":"Structural","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"},{"id":"RAW_QGOH_PPDD:P0020","trace_type":"Structural","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"},{"id":"RAW_QGOH_PPDD:P0022","trace_type":"Structural","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"},{"id":"RAW_QGOH_PPDD:P0026","trace_type":"Structural","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"},{"id":"RAW_QGOH_PPDD:P0046","trace_type":"Structural","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"},{"id":"RAW_QGOH_PPDD:P0054","trace_type":"Structural","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"},{"id":"RAW_QGOH_PPDD:P0058","trace_type":"Structural","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_QGOH_PPDD:MRT2","text":"The validity of the final closure depends chiefly on ontological exhaustiveness: apart from substances and their modes, nothing is posited as given.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_QGOH_PPDD:P0018","trace_type":"Structural","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"},{"id":"RAW_QGOH_PPDD:P0007","trace_type":"Structural","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"},{"id":"RAW_QGOH_PPDD:P0009","trace_type":"Structural","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"},{"id":"RAW_QGOH_PPDD:P0116","trace_type":"Structural","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"},{"id":"RAW_QGOH_PPDD:P0118","trace_type":"Structural","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_QGOH_PPDD:MRT3","text":"The transition from the non-production of substance by substance to the non-production of substance by anything else does not fully explain the causality of modes.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_QGOH_PPDD:P0046","trace_type":"Direct","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"},{"id":"RAW_QGOH_PPDD:P0050","trace_type":"Direct","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_QGOH_PPDD:MRT4","text":"The coherence between the real distinction of attributes and the numerical unity of one substance is asserted in the scholia through the simultaneous existence of attributes, but the positive nexus is not further demonstrated.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_QGOH_PPDD:P0075","trace_type":"Direct","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"},{"id":"RAW_QGOH_PPDD:P0079","trace_type":"Direct","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_QGOH_PPDD:MRT5","text":"The demonstration of the necessary existence of God requires that the definition of an absolutely infinite being already determine a substance capable of existence and comprehensive of every essential attribute.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_QGOH_PPDD:P0010","trace_type":"Composite","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"},{"id":"RAW_QGOH_PPDD:P0054","trace_type":"Composite","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"},{"id":"RAW_QGOH_PPDD:P0082","trace_type":"Composite","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_QGOH_PPDD:MRT6","text":"The reconstruction preserves the movement of the text if the chain unproducibility → cause of itself → existence → infinity → uniqueness → immanence is retained, while alternative demonstrations and the defense of indivisibility are recognized as confirmatory closures.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_QGOH_PPDD:P0046","trace_type":"Structural","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"},{"id":"RAW_QGOH_PPDD:P0050","trace_type":"Structural","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"},{"id":"RAW_QGOH_PPDD:P0054","trace_type":"Structural","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"},{"id":"RAW_QGOH_PPDD:P0058","trace_type":"Structural","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"},{"id":"RAW_QGOH_PPDD:P0082","trace_type":"Structural","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"},{"id":"RAW_QGOH_PPDD:P0100","trace_type":"Structural","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"},{"id":"RAW_QGOH_PPDD:P0108","trace_type":"Structural","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"},{"id":"RAW_QGOH_PPDD:P0116","trace_type":"Structural","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]}],"original_name":"Minimal Reconstruction Test"}],"revision":"CH_QGOH_PPDD@R001","source_variant_id":"canonical:CH_QGOH_PPDD:v1","source_language_code":"la"}],"identity":{"layer":"CH","kicker":"Chapter 01","title":"Part One - Concerning God · passage 1","context":"Chapter 01","original_title":"Pars prima - De Deo · passage 1","original_context":"Chapter 01"},"original_title":"Pars prima - De Deo · passage 1","title_language_code":"en"},{"id":"CH_QGOH_PP_2","layer":"CH","title":"Part One - Concerning God · passage 2","order":2,"parents":["RAW_QGOH_PP_2"],"revision":"CH_QGOH_PP_2@R001","sha256":"3376096908e6ce5d875b0e7226c7b94784d0bdda8b5bf4517707f047f15c3524","sections":[{"number":"1","name":"Argumental Spine","items":[{"id":"CH_QGOH_PP_2:AS1","text":"Textus polemico-diagnostice examinat utrum extensio divinae naturae repugnet et utrum Dei causalitas libero arbitrio contingenti indigeat.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_QGOH_PP_2:P0001","trace_type":"Structural","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"},{"id":"RAW_QGOH_PP_2:P0010","trace_type":"Structural","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"},{"id":"RAW_QGOH_PP_2:P0022","trace_type":"Structural","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"},{"id":"RAW_QGOH_PP_2:P0030","trace_type":"Structural","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_QGOH_PP_2:AS2","text":"Argumenta contra extensionem ex falsa praesuppositione nascuntur, quod substantia corporea ex partibus finitis, mensurabilibus et realiter distinctis componatur.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_QGOH_PP_2:P0002","trace_type":"Direct","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"},{"id":"RAW_QGOH_PP_2:P0003","trace_type":"Direct","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"},{"id":"RAW_QGOH_PP_2:P0004","trace_type":"Direct","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"},{"id":"RAW_QGOH_PP_2:P0005","trace_type":"Direct","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_QGOH_PP_2:AS3","text":"Distinctio imaginationis et intellectus quantitatem ab objecto abstracto ad substantiam extensam transfert, unde extensio infinita, unica et indivisibilis intelligitur.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_QGOH_PP_2:P0008","trace_type":"Direct","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"},{"id":"RAW_QGOH_PP_2:P0009","trace_type":"Direct","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_QGOH_PP_2:AS4","text":"Definitio divinae naturae ut absolute infinitae necessitatem productionis convertit in principium: infinita infinitis modis necessario sequuntur.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_QGOH_PP_2:P0012","trace_type":"Direct","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"},{"id":"RAW_QGOH_PP_2:P0014","trace_type":"Direct","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_QGOH_PP_2:AS5","text":"Causalitas divina constituitur ut immanens, necessaria et libera ex propria natura; res particulares ut modi dependentiae causalis infinitae ordinantur.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_QGOH_PP_2:P0022","trace_type":"Structural","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"},{"id":"RAW_QGOH_PP_2:P0024","trace_type":"Structural","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"},{"id":"RAW_QGOH_PP_2:P0028","trace_type":"Structural","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"},{"id":"RAW_QGOH_PP_2:P0037","trace_type":"Structural","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"},{"id":"RAW_QGOH_PP_2:P0074","trace_type":"Structural","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"},{"id":"RAW_QGOH_PP_2:P0092","trace_type":"Structural","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]}],"original_name":"Argumental Spine"},{"number":"2","name":"Load-Bearing Axioms","items":[{"id":"CH_QGOH_PP_2:A1","text":"Deus est substantia absolute infinita, necessario existens, praeter quam nulla substantia datur neque concipi potest.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_QGOH_PP_2:P0001","trace_type":"Composite","confidence":"medium","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"},{"id":"RAW_QGOH_PP_2:P0002","trace_type":"Composite","confidence":"medium","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"},{"id":"RAW_QGOH_PP_2:P0043","trace_type":"Composite","confidence":"medium","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_QGOH_PP_2:A2","text":"Ex data rei definitione proprietates ex ipsius essentia necessario sequuntur.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_QGOH_PP_2:P0014","trace_type":"Direct","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_QGOH_PP_2:A3","text":"Attributum est id quod essentiam substantiae exprimit; attributa Dei essentiam infinitam in suo genere exprimunt.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_QGOH_PP_2:P0014","trace_type":"Composite","confidence":"medium","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"},{"id":"RAW_QGOH_PP_2:P0043","trace_type":"Composite","confidence":"medium","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_QGOH_PP_2:A4","text":"Extensio, quatenus substantiae attributum est, infinita, unica et indivisibilis concipitur; corpus est quantitas figura determinata terminata.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_QGOH_PP_2:P0001","trace_type":"Composite","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"},{"id":"RAW_QGOH_PP_2:P0006","trace_type":"Composite","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"},{"id":"RAW_QGOH_PP_2:P0008","trace_type":"Composite","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_QGOH_PP_2:A5","text":"Quantitas imaginatione concepta finita, divisibilis et ex partibus composita apparet; eadem intellectu ut substantia concepta infinita, unica et indivisibilis est.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_QGOH_PP_2:P0008","trace_type":"Direct","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"},{"id":"RAW_QGOH_PP_2:P0009","trace_type":"Direct","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_QGOH_PP_2:A6","text":"Omnia in Deo sunt et per Deum concipiuntur; res particulares sunt affectiones attributorum Dei.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_QGOH_PP_2:P0010","trace_type":"Direct","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"},{"id":"RAW_QGOH_PP_2:P0039","trace_type":"Direct","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"},{"id":"RAW_QGOH_PP_2:P0082","trace_type":"Direct","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_QGOH_PP_2:A7","text":"Extra Deum nulla res datur quae Deum ad agendum determinare, cogere vel pati facere possit.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_QGOH_PP_2:P0024","trace_type":"Direct","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"},{"id":"RAW_QGOH_PP_2:P0039","trace_type":"Direct","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_QGOH_PP_2:A8","text":"Ex data causa effectus necessario sequitur; non-sequentia effectus ex causa data contradictoria est.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_QGOH_PP_2:P0030","trace_type":"Direct","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_QGOH_PP_2:A9","text":"Causa libera ex sola suae naturae necessitate existit et agit.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_QGOH_PP_2:P0028","trace_type":"Direct","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_QGOH_PP_2:A10","text":"Essentia rei a Deo productae neque existentiam neque durationem involvit.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_QGOH_PP_2:P0070","trace_type":"Direct","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"},{"id":"RAW_QGOH_PP_2:P0074","trace_type":"Direct","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_QGOH_PP_2:A11","text":"Deus est causa efficiens tam essentiae quam existentiae rerum, atque causa earum perseverantiae in existendo.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_QGOH_PP_2:P0074","trace_type":"Direct","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"},{"id":"RAW_QGOH_PP_2:P0076","trace_type":"Direct","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_QGOH_PP_2:A12","text":"Omne singulare finitum et determinate existens ad existendum et operandum ab alia causa finita et determinata exigitur, et sic in infinitum.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_QGOH_PP_2:P0092","trace_type":"Direct","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_QGOH_PP_2:A13","text":"Res a Deo ad operandum determinata se ipsam indeterminatam reddere non potest.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_QGOH_PP_2:P0084","trace_type":"Direct","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"},{"id":"RAW_QGOH_PP_2:P0088","trace_type":"Direct","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]}],"original_name":"Load-Bearing Axioms"},{"number":"3","name":"Apophatic Structure","items":[{"id":"CH_QGOH_PP_2:NX1","text":"Deus non est corpus figura determinata terminatum neque persona humana passionibus obnoxia; negatio corporis finiti non est negatio extensionis attributivae.","refs":["CH_QGOH_PP_2:A4","CH_QGOH_PP_2:A5"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"From","passages":[{"id":"RAW_QGOH_PP_2:P0001","trace_type":"Negative","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"},{"id":"RAW_QGOH_PP_2:P0006","trace_type":"Negative","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"},{"id":"RAW_QGOH_PP_2:P0008","trace_type":"Negative","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_QGOH_PP_2:NX2","text":"Substantia extensa non est res a Deo creata vel extra Deum posita, sed attributum divinae substantiae.","refs":["CH_QGOH_PP_2:A1","CH_QGOH_PP_2:A4"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"From","passages":[{"id":"RAW_QGOH_PP_2:P0001","trace_type":"Negative","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"},{"id":"RAW_QGOH_PP_2:P0002","trace_type":"Negative","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"},{"id":"RAW_QGOH_PP_2:P0010","trace_type":"Negative","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_QGOH_PP_2:NX3","text":"Infinitas extensionis non est quantitas mensurabilis, ex partibus finitis conflata, multiplicabilis aut realiter divisibilis.","refs":["CH_QGOH_PP_2:A4","CH_QGOH_PP_2:A5"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"From","passages":[{"id":"RAW_QGOH_PP_2:P0005","trace_type":"Negative","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"},{"id":"RAW_QGOH_PP_2:P0006","trace_type":"Negative","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"},{"id":"RAW_QGOH_PP_2:P0008","trace_type":"Negative","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_QGOH_PP_2:NX4","text":"Libertas divina non est indifferentia inter producere et non producere, nec potestas effectum a causa data separandi.","refs":["CH_QGOH_PP_2:A8","CH_QGOH_PP_2:A9"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"From","passages":[{"id":"RAW_QGOH_PP_2:P0028","trace_type":"Negative","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"},{"id":"RAW_QGOH_PP_2:P0030","trace_type":"Negative","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_QGOH_PP_2:NX5","text":"Deus non agit ex impulsu externo, ex defectu interno, aut ex voluntate decreto contingenti constituta.","refs":["CH_QGOH_PP_2:A7","CH_QGOH_PP_2:A9"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"From","passages":[{"id":"RAW_QGOH_PP_2:P0024","trace_type":"Negative","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"},{"id":"RAW_QGOH_PP_2:P0026","trace_type":"Negative","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"},{"id":"RAW_QGOH_PP_2:P0030","trace_type":"Negative","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_QGOH_PP_2:NX6","text":"Deus non est causa transiens quae effectus extra se relinquit.","refs":["CH_QGOH_PP_2:A1","CH_QGOH_PP_2:A6","CH_QGOH_PP_2:A7"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"From","passages":[{"id":"RAW_QGOH_PP_2:P0037","trace_type":"Direct","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"},{"id":"RAW_QGOH_PP_2:P0039","trace_type":"Direct","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_QGOH_PP_2:NX7","text":"Res finita non est causa sui, nec principium sui actus, nec principium suae perseverantiae.","refs":["CH_QGOH_PP_2:A10","CH_QGOH_PP_2:A11","CH_QGOH_PP_2:A12","CH_QGOH_PP_2:A13"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"From","passages":[{"id":"RAW_QGOH_PP_2:P0070","trace_type":"Composite","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"},{"id":"RAW_QGOH_PP_2:P0074","trace_type":"Composite","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"},{"id":"RAW_QGOH_PP_2:P0084","trace_type":"Composite","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"},{"id":"RAW_QGOH_PP_2:P0092","trace_type":"Composite","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]}],"original_name":"Apophatic Structure"},{"number":"4","name":"Derivative Propositions","items":[{"id":"CH_QGOH_PP_2:D1","text":"Argumenta quae extensionem a Deo removent solum ostendunt quantitatem infinitam non esse mensurabilem nec ex partibus finitis conflari posse.","refs":["CH_QGOH_PP_2:A4","CH_QGOH_PP_2:A5"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"From","passages":[{"id":"RAW_QGOH_PP_2:P0005","trace_type":"Direct","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"},{"id":"RAW_QGOH_PP_2:P0006","trace_type":"Direct","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_QGOH_PP_2:D2","text":"Deus eiusque attributa sunt aeterna; divina existentia et divina essentia unum idemque sunt, atque attributa immutabilia sunt.","refs":["CH_QGOH_PP_2:A1","CH_QGOH_PP_2:A3"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"From","passages":[{"id":"RAW_QGOH_PP_2:P0041","trace_type":"Composite","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"},{"id":"RAW_QGOH_PP_2:P0043","trace_type":"Composite","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"},{"id":"RAW_QGOH_PP_2:P0047","trace_type":"Composite","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"},{"id":"RAW_QGOH_PP_2:P0053","trace_type":"Composite","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_QGOH_PP_2:D3","text":"Ex necessitate naturae divinae infinita infinitis modis sequuntur, id est omnia quae sub intellectum infinitum cadere possunt.","refs":["CH_QGOH_PP_2:A1","CH_QGOH_PP_2:A2","CH_QGOH_PP_2:A3"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"From","passages":[{"id":"RAW_QGOH_PP_2:P0012","trace_type":"Direct","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"},{"id":"RAW_QGOH_PP_2:P0014","trace_type":"Direct","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_QGOH_PP_2:D4","text":"Deus est causa efficiens rerum, causa per se et causa absolute prima, quae ex solis suae naturae legibus agit.","refs":["CH_QGOH_PP_2:A6","CH_QGOH_PP_2:A7","CH_QGOH_PP_2:A8","CH_QGOH_PP_2:A9"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"From","passages":[{"id":"RAW_QGOH_PP_2:P0016","trace_type":"Composite","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"},{"id":"RAW_QGOH_PP_2:P0018","trace_type":"Composite","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"},{"id":"RAW_QGOH_PP_2:P0020","trace_type":"Composite","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"},{"id":"RAW_QGOH_PP_2:P0022","trace_type":"Composite","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"},{"id":"RAW_QGOH_PP_2:P0024","trace_type":"Composite","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_QGOH_PP_2:D5","text":"Deus est causa omnium rerum immanens, quia effectus in Deo sunt et nulla substantia extra Deum subsistit.","refs":["CH_QGOH_PP_2:A1","CH_QGOH_PP_2:A6","CH_QGOH_PP_2:A7"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"From","passages":[{"id":"RAW_QGOH_PP_2:P0037","trace_type":"Direct","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"},{"id":"RAW_QGOH_PP_2:P0039","trace_type":"Direct","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_QGOH_PP_2:D6","text":"Modi particulares dependent a Deo non tantum ut incipiant existere, sed etiam quoad essentiam et continuationem existentiae.","refs":["CH_QGOH_PP_2:A6","CH_QGOH_PP_2:A10","CH_QGOH_PP_2:A11"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"From","passages":[{"id":"RAW_QGOH_PP_2:P0070","trace_type":"Composite","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"},{"id":"RAW_QGOH_PP_2:P0074","trace_type":"Composite","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"},{"id":"RAW_QGOH_PP_2:P0076","trace_type":"Composite","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_QGOH_PP_2:D7","text":"Omnis modus necessario infinitus vel immediate ex absoluta natura attributi divini sequitur vel mediante modificatione quae ipsa necessaria et infinita est.","refs":["CH_QGOH_PP_2:A2","CH_QGOH_PP_2:A3","CH_QGOH_PP_2:A6","CH_QGOH_PP_2:D3"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"From","passages":[{"id":"RAW_QGOH_PP_2:P0055","trace_type":"Direct","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"},{"id":"RAW_QGOH_PP_2:P0062","trace_type":"Direct","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"},{"id":"RAW_QGOH_PP_2:P0066","trace_type":"Direct","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"},{"id":"RAW_QGOH_PP_2:P0068","trace_type":"Direct","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_QGOH_PP_2:D8","text":"Ordo rerum finitarum est ordo determinationum finitarum in infinitum, in quo nulla res finita se ipsam ad agendum instituit aut a determinatione recepta liberat.","refs":["CH_QGOH_PP_2:A11","CH_QGOH_PP_2:A12","CH_QGOH_PP_2:A13"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"From","passages":[{"id":"RAW_QGOH_PP_2:P0084","trace_type":"Structural","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"},{"id":"RAW_QGOH_PP_2:P0088","trace_type":"Structural","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"},{"id":"RAW_QGOH_PP_2:P0092","trace_type":"Structural","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_QGOH_PP_2:D9","text":"Si intellectus aut voluntas ad divinam essentiam pertinerent, a facultatibus humanis non specie graduve, sed essentia et existentia differrent.","refs":["CH_QGOH_PP_2:A2","CH_QGOH_PP_2:A8","CH_QGOH_PP_2:D4"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"From","passages":[{"id":"RAW_QGOH_PP_2:P0032","trace_type":"Direct","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"},{"id":"RAW_QGOH_PP_2:P0033","trace_type":"Direct","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"},{"id":"RAW_QGOH_PP_2:P0034","trace_type":"Direct","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"},{"id":"RAW_QGOH_PP_2:P0035","trace_type":"Direct","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]}],"original_name":"Derivative Propositions"},{"number":"5","name":"Super-Derivatives","items":[{"id":"CH_QGOH_PP_2:SD1","text":"Creatio transiens substituitur dependentia immanenti: res non extra Deum producuntur, sed Dei attributa certo et determinato modo exprimunt.","refs":["CH_QGOH_PP_2:D5","CH_QGOH_PP_2:D6"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"From","passages":[{"id":"RAW_QGOH_PP_2:P0037","trace_type":"Composite","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"},{"id":"RAW_QGOH_PP_2:P0074","trace_type":"Composite","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"},{"id":"RAW_QGOH_PP_2:P0082","trace_type":"Composite","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_QGOH_PP_2:SD2","text":"Systema duplicem ordinem causalitatis constituit: modi infiniti ex natura absoluta attributorum oriuntur, res finitae per seriem causarum finitarum determinantur.","refs":["CH_QGOH_PP_2:D3","CH_QGOH_PP_2:D7","CH_QGOH_PP_2:D8"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"From","passages":[{"id":"RAW_QGOH_PP_2:P0055","trace_type":"Structural","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"},{"id":"RAW_QGOH_PP_2:P0066","trace_type":"Structural","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"},{"id":"RAW_QGOH_PP_2:P0092","trace_type":"Structural","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_QGOH_PP_2:SD3","text":"Omnipotentia non est potentia possibilitates non actu productas reservans, sed actualitas aeterna inexhaustibilis naturae necessario productivae.","refs":["CH_QGOH_PP_2:D3","CH_QGOH_PP_2:D4","CH_QGOH_PP_2:D9"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"From","passages":[{"id":"RAW_QGOH_PP_2:P0031","trace_type":"Direct","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"},{"id":"RAW_QGOH_PP_2:P0032","trace_type":"Direct","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_QGOH_PP_2:SD4","text":"Paradoxa infiniti non afficiunt esse infinitum, sed oriuntur cum schema imaginationis, mensurae et compositionis ad substantiam transfertur.","refs":["CH_QGOH_PP_2:D1","CH_QGOH_PP_2:D2"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"From","passages":[{"id":"RAW_QGOH_PP_2:P0005","trace_type":"Structural","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"},{"id":"RAW_QGOH_PP_2:P0006","trace_type":"Structural","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"},{"id":"RAW_QGOH_PP_2:P0008","trace_type":"Structural","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]}],"original_name":"Super-Derivatives"},{"number":"6","name":"Deep Apophatics","items":[{"id":"CH_QGOH_PP_2:DNX1","text":"Falsum esse debet exemplar ontologicum quo plures substantiae independentes, partes reales vel vacuum inter res subsistant.","refs":["CH_QGOH_PP_2:A1","CH_QGOH_PP_2:A4","CH_QGOH_PP_2:A6"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"From","passages":[{"id":"RAW_QGOH_PP_2:P0002","trace_type":"Negative","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"},{"id":"RAW_QGOH_PP_2:P0007","trace_type":"Negative","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"},{"id":"RAW_QGOH_PP_2:P0010","trace_type":"Negative","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_QGOH_PP_2:DNX2","text":"Falsa esse debet possibilitas qua effectus divinus ab essentia divina separari possit, vel qua Deus id quod intelligit producere non possit.","refs":["CH_QGOH_PP_2:A2","CH_QGOH_PP_2:A8","CH_QGOH_PP_2:D3"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"From","passages":[{"id":"RAW_QGOH_PP_2:P0014","trace_type":"Negative","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"},{"id":"RAW_QGOH_PP_2:P0030","trace_type":"Negative","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"},{"id":"RAW_QGOH_PP_2:P0031","trace_type":"Negative","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_QGOH_PP_2:DNX3","text":"Falsa esse debet assumptio quod finita individua propriam existentiam, durationem vel autonomam originem in se contineant.","refs":["CH_QGOH_PP_2:A10","CH_QGOH_PP_2:A11","CH_QGOH_PP_2:A12"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"From","passages":[{"id":"RAW_QGOH_PP_2:P0070","trace_type":"Negative","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"},{"id":"RAW_QGOH_PP_2:P0074","trace_type":"Negative","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"},{"id":"RAW_QGOH_PP_2:P0092","trace_type":"Negative","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]}],"original_name":"Deep Apophatics"},{"number":"7","name":"Super-Apophatics","items":[{"id":"CH_QGOH_PP_2:SNX1","text":"Omne “extra Deum” excluditur simul ut locus substantiae, causa, conditio intelligibilitatis et terminus causalitatis.","refs":["CH_QGOH_PP_2:A1","CH_QGOH_PP_2:A6","CH_QGOH_PP_2:A7","CH_QGOH_PP_2:D5"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"From","passages":[{"id":"RAW_QGOH_PP_2:P0002","trace_type":"Negative","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"},{"id":"RAW_QGOH_PP_2:P0010","trace_type":"Negative","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"},{"id":"RAW_QGOH_PP_2:P0039","trace_type":"Negative","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_QGOH_PP_2:SNX2","text":"Omne intervallum inter potentiam divinam et actualitatem divinam excluditur; divina potentia non potest esse capacitas otiosa aut futuribilis.","refs":["CH_QGOH_PP_2:A8","CH_QGOH_PP_2:D3","CH_QGOH_PP_2:SD3"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"From","passages":[{"id":"RAW_QGOH_PP_2:P0030","trace_type":"Negative","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"},{"id":"RAW_QGOH_PP_2:P0031","trace_type":"Negative","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"},{"id":"RAW_QGOH_PP_2:P0032","trace_type":"Negative","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]}],"original_name":"Super-Apophatics"},{"number":"8","name":"Formal Generative Schema","items":[{"id":"CH_QGOH_PP_2:FGS1","text":"Quantitas imaginata → partes finitae et mensura → paradoxa infiniti → falsa exclusio extensionis a natura divina.","refs":["CH_QGOH_PP_2:A4","CH_QGOH_PP_2:A5","CH_QGOH_PP_2:D1"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"From","passages":[{"id":"RAW_QGOH_PP_2:P0003","trace_type":"Structural","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"},{"id":"RAW_QGOH_PP_2:P0005","trace_type":"Structural","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"},{"id":"RAW_QGOH_PP_2:P0006","trace_type":"Structural","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_QGOH_PP_2:FGS2","text":"Substantia extensa intellecta → unitas et indivisibilitas → extensio ut attributum divinum, non ut corpus finitum.","refs":["CH_QGOH_PP_2:A1","CH_QGOH_PP_2:A4","CH_QGOH_PP_2:A5"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"From","passages":[{"id":"RAW_QGOH_PP_2:P0002","trace_type":"Structural","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"},{"id":"RAW_QGOH_PP_2:P0006","trace_type":"Structural","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"},{"id":"RAW_QGOH_PP_2:P0008","trace_type":"Structural","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_QGOH_PP_2:FGS3","text":"Definitio essentiae → proprietates necessariae → attributa absolute infinita → infinita infinitis modis sequuntur.","refs":["CH_QGOH_PP_2:A2","CH_QGOH_PP_2:A3","CH_QGOH_PP_2:D3"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"From","passages":[{"id":"RAW_QGOH_PP_2:P0014","trace_type":"Direct","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_QGOH_PP_2:FGS4","text":"Omnia in Deo + nulla causa extra Deum → actio ex propriis legibus → causa libera, per se et immanens.","refs":["CH_QGOH_PP_2:A6","CH_QGOH_PP_2:A7","CH_QGOH_PP_2:A9","CH_QGOH_PP_2:D4","CH_QGOH_PP_2:D5"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"From","passages":[{"id":"RAW_QGOH_PP_2:P0010","trace_type":"Structural","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"},{"id":"RAW_QGOH_PP_2:P0024","trace_type":"Structural","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"},{"id":"RAW_QGOH_PP_2:P0028","trace_type":"Structural","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"},{"id":"RAW_QGOH_PP_2:P0037","trace_type":"Structural","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_QGOH_PP_2:FGS5","text":"Essentia divina existentiam involvens / essentia modi existentiam non involvens → Deus causa essentiae et existentiae modorum → dependentia perseverans.","refs":["CH_QGOH_PP_2:D2","CH_QGOH_PP_2:A10","CH_QGOH_PP_2:A11","CH_QGOH_PP_2:D6"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"From","passages":[{"id":"RAW_QGOH_PP_2:P0047","trace_type":"Composite","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"},{"id":"RAW_QGOH_PP_2:P0070","trace_type":"Composite","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"},{"id":"RAW_QGOH_PP_2:P0074","trace_type":"Composite","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"},{"id":"RAW_QGOH_PP_2:P0076","trace_type":"Composite","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_QGOH_PP_2:FGS6","text":"Modus necessarius infinitus → attributum absolute consideratum vel modificatio infinita; res finita → causa finita determinans → regressus infinitus.","refs":["CH_QGOH_PP_2:D7","CH_QGOH_PP_2:D8"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"From","passages":[{"id":"RAW_QGOH_PP_2:P0066","trace_type":"Structural","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"},{"id":"RAW_QGOH_PP_2:P0068","trace_type":"Structural","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"},{"id":"RAW_QGOH_PP_2:P0092","trace_type":"Structural","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]}],"original_name":"Formal Generative Schema"},{"number":"9","name":"Argument Flow Map","items":[{"id":"CH_QGOH_PP_2:AF1","text":"Corpus figuratum et passibile → falsa identificatio extensionis cum corpore finito → exclusio apparens extensionis divinae.","refs":["CH_QGOH_PP_2:NX1","CH_QGOH_PP_2:NX2"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"From","passages":[{"id":"RAW_QGOH_PP_2:P0001","trace_type":"Structural","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"},{"id":"RAW_QGOH_PP_2:P0002","trace_type":"Structural","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"},{"id":"RAW_QGOH_PP_2:P0006","trace_type":"Structural","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_QGOH_PP_2:AF2","text":"Divisio, mensura et multiplicatio infiniti → diagnosis imaginationis → restitutio substantiae extensae indivisibilis.","refs":["CH_QGOH_PP_2:D1"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"From","passages":[{"id":"RAW_QGOH_PP_2:P0005","trace_type":"Structural","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"},{"id":"RAW_QGOH_PP_2:P0006","trace_type":"Structural","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"},{"id":"RAW_QGOH_PP_2:P0008","trace_type":"Structural","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_QGOH_PP_2:AF3","text":"Natura divina absolute infinita → consequentia necessaria proprietatum → productio infinita infinitis modis.","refs":["CH_QGOH_PP_2:D3"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"From","passages":[{"id":"RAW_QGOH_PP_2:P0012","trace_type":"Direct","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"},{"id":"RAW_QGOH_PP_2:P0014","trace_type":"Direct","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_QGOH_PP_2:AF4","text":"Nulla exterior determinatio → actio ex natura → libertas ut necessitas sui → recusatio voluntatis indifferentis.","refs":["CH_QGOH_PP_2:D4","CH_QGOH_PP_2:NX4"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"From","passages":[{"id":"RAW_QGOH_PP_2:P0024","trace_type":"Structural","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"},{"id":"RAW_QGOH_PP_2:P0028","trace_type":"Structural","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"},{"id":"RAW_QGOH_PP_2:P0030","trace_type":"Structural","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_QGOH_PP_2:AF5","text":"Omnia in Deo → causalitas immanens → modi ut affectiones attributorum → dependentia essentiae et existentiae.","refs":["CH_QGOH_PP_2:D5","CH_QGOH_PP_2:D6"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"From","passages":[{"id":"RAW_QGOH_PP_2:P0039","trace_type":"Structural","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"},{"id":"RAW_QGOH_PP_2:P0074","trace_type":"Structural","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"},{"id":"RAW_QGOH_PP_2:P0082","trace_type":"Structural","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_QGOH_PP_2:AF6","text":"Modus infinitus necessarius / res finita determinata → duplex architectura causalitatis → series finita causarum in infinitum.","refs":["CH_QGOH_PP_2:D7","CH_QGOH_PP_2:D8"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"From","passages":[{"id":"RAW_QGOH_PP_2:P0068","trace_type":"Structural","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"},{"id":"RAW_QGOH_PP_2:P0092","trace_type":"Structural","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]}],"original_name":"Argument Flow Map"},{"number":"10","name":"Map-Effects","items":[{"id":"CH_QGOH_PP_2:ME1","text":"Oppositio “Deus aut corpus aut incorporeus” dissolvitur per distinctionem inter corpus determinatum et extensionem ut attributum substantiae.","refs":["CH_QGOH_PP_2:D1","CH_QGOH_PP_2:NX1"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"From","passages":[{"id":"RAW_QGOH_PP_2:P0001","trace_type":"Tension","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"},{"id":"RAW_QGOH_PP_2:P0006","trace_type":"Tension","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"},{"id":"RAW_QGOH_PP_2:P0008","trace_type":"Tension","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_QGOH_PP_2:ME2","text":"Infinitum non amplius classificatur per numerum, mensuram vel aggregationem partium, sed per naturam substantiae et attributi.","refs":["CH_QGOH_PP_2:D1","CH_QGOH_PP_2:SD4"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"From","passages":[{"id":"RAW_QGOH_PP_2:P0005","trace_type":"Structural","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"},{"id":"RAW_QGOH_PP_2:P0008","trace_type":"Structural","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_QGOH_PP_2:ME3","text":"Libertas et necessitas desinunt esse oppositae, quia libertas divina definitur per actionem ex sola necessitate naturae.","refs":["CH_QGOH_PP_2:A9","CH_QGOH_PP_2:D4"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"From","passages":[{"id":"RAW_QGOH_PP_2:P0028","trace_type":"Direct","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"},{"id":"RAW_QGOH_PP_2:P0030","trace_type":"Direct","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_QGOH_PP_2:ME4","text":"Relatio Dei ad res redescribitur ab exteriori fabricatione ad immanentem causalitatem essentiae, existentiae et perseverantiae.","refs":["CH_QGOH_PP_2:D5","CH_QGOH_PP_2:D6","CH_QGOH_PP_2:SD1"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"From","passages":[{"id":"RAW_QGOH_PP_2:P0037","trace_type":"Structural","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"},{"id":"RAW_QGOH_PP_2:P0074","trace_type":"Structural","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"},{"id":"RAW_QGOH_PP_2:P0076","trace_type":"Structural","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"},{"id":"RAW_QGOH_PP_2:P0082","trace_type":"Structural","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_QGOH_PP_2:ME5","text":"Tempus et causalitas differunt secundum gradum entis: attributa et modi infiniti aeternitatem exprimunt, res finitae ordinem determinationum finitarum.","refs":["CH_QGOH_PP_2:D2","CH_QGOH_PP_2:D7","CH_QGOH_PP_2:D8"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"From","passages":[{"id":"RAW_QGOH_PP_2:P0041","trace_type":"Structural","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"},{"id":"RAW_QGOH_PP_2:P0055","trace_type":"Structural","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"},{"id":"RAW_QGOH_PP_2:P0066","trace_type":"Structural","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"},{"id":"RAW_QGOH_PP_2:P0092","trace_type":"Structural","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]}],"original_name":"Map-Effects"},{"number":"11","name":"Altitude Levels","items":[{"id":"CH_QGOH_PP_2:ALT1","text":"Surface — Textus refutat argumenta quae extensionem divinae naturae indignam faciunt et definit Deum causam omnium rerum.","refs":["CH_QGOH_PP_2:D1","CH_QGOH_PP_2:D4","CH_QGOH_PP_2:D5"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"From","passages":[{"id":"RAW_QGOH_PP_2:P0005","trace_type":"Structural","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"},{"id":"RAW_QGOH_PP_2:P0016","trace_type":"Structural","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"},{"id":"RAW_QGOH_PP_2:P0037","trace_type":"Structural","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_QGOH_PP_2:ALT2","text":"Structural — Argumentatio opponit imaginationem intellectui, substantiam modis, causalitatem immanentem causalitati transeunti, et determinationem necessitatemque indifferentiae.","refs":["CH_QGOH_PP_2:A5","CH_QGOH_PP_2:A6","CH_QGOH_PP_2:D4","CH_QGOH_PP_2:D5"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"From","passages":[{"id":"RAW_QGOH_PP_2:P0008","trace_type":"Structural","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"},{"id":"RAW_QGOH_PP_2:P0037","trace_type":"Structural","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"},{"id":"RAW_QGOH_PP_2:P0028","trace_type":"Structural","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"},{"id":"RAW_QGOH_PP_2:P0084","trace_type":"Structural","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_QGOH_PP_2:ALT3","text":"Metastrategic — Adversariorum reductio ad absurdum retorquetur: absurdum non sequitur ex infinito, sed ex praemissis adversariorum de partibus, mensura et compositione.","refs":["CH_QGOH_PP_2:D1","CH_QGOH_PP_2:SD4"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"From","passages":[{"id":"RAW_QGOH_PP_2:P0005","trace_type":"Structural","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"},{"id":"RAW_QGOH_PP_2:P0006","trace_type":"Structural","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_QGOH_PP_2:ALT4","text":"Ontological/Temporal — Deus est existentia et essentia aeterna idem; res finitae essentiam ab existentia distinguunt et per seriem causarum ad utramque dependent.","refs":["CH_QGOH_PP_2:D2","CH_QGOH_PP_2:D6","CH_QGOH_PP_2:D8"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"From","passages":[{"id":"RAW_QGOH_PP_2:P0047","trace_type":"Structural","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"},{"id":"RAW_QGOH_PP_2:P0070","trace_type":"Structural","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"},{"id":"RAW_QGOH_PP_2:P0074","trace_type":"Structural","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"},{"id":"RAW_QGOH_PP_2:P0092","trace_type":"Structural","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]}],"original_name":"Altitude Levels"},{"number":"12","name":"Minimal Reconstruction Test","items":[{"id":"CH_QGOH_PP_2:MRT1","text":"Axiomata de substantia unica, attributis, distinctione imaginationis et intellectus, causalitate necessaria et dependentia modorum sufficiunt ad motum principalem restituendum.","refs":["CH_QGOH_PP_2:A1","CH_QGOH_PP_2:A5","CH_QGOH_PP_2:A6","CH_QGOH_PP_2:A8","CH_QGOH_PP_2:A10"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"From","passages":[{"id":"RAW_QGOH_PP_2:P0002","trace_type":"Structural","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"},{"id":"RAW_QGOH_PP_2:P0008","trace_type":"Structural","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"},{"id":"RAW_QGOH_PP_2:P0010","trace_type":"Structural","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"},{"id":"RAW_QGOH_PP_2:P0030","trace_type":"Structural","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"},{"id":"RAW_QGOH_PP_2:P0074","trace_type":"Structural","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_QGOH_PP_2:MRT2","text":"Commissura firmissima est transitus ab errore mensurae ad indivisibilitatem substantiae, quia argumenta adversaria eodem supposito compositionali dissolvuntur.","refs":["CH_QGOH_PP_2:A4","CH_QGOH_PP_2:A5","CH_QGOH_PP_2:D1"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"From","passages":[{"id":"RAW_QGOH_PP_2:P0005","trace_type":"Structural","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"},{"id":"RAW_QGOH_PP_2:P0006","trace_type":"Structural","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"},{"id":"RAW_QGOH_PP_2:P0008","trace_type":"Structural","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_QGOH_PP_2:MRT3","text":"Tensio manet inter Deum ut determinantem omne agens et rem finitam ut immediate ab alia causa finita determinatam; immanentia hanc compatibilitatem postulat, sed relatio mediationis hic non plene formalizatur.","refs":["CH_QGOH_PP_2:D4","CH_QGOH_PP_2:D5","CH_QGOH_PP_2:D8"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"From","passages":[{"id":"RAW_QGOH_PP_2:P0024","trace_type":"Tension","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"},{"id":"RAW_QGOH_PP_2:P0037","trace_type":"Tension","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"},{"id":"RAW_QGOH_PP_2:P0084","trace_type":"Tension","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"},{"id":"RAW_QGOH_PP_2:P0092","trace_type":"Tension","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_QGOH_PP_2:MRT4","text":"Distinctio modorum infinitarum et rerum finitarum ordinem systematis servat, sed nexus exactus inter productionem aeternam et seriem finitam causalem compendiose tantum datur.","refs":["CH_QGOH_PP_2:D3","CH_QGOH_PP_2:D7","CH_QGOH_PP_2:D8"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"From","passages":[{"id":"RAW_QGOH_PP_2:P0055","trace_type":"Tension","confidence":"medium","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"},{"id":"RAW_QGOH_PP_2:P0068","trace_type":"Tension","confidence":"medium","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"},{"id":"RAW_QGOH_PP_2:P0092","trace_type":"Tension","confidence":"medium","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_QGOH_PP_2:MRT5","text":"Refutatio extensionis pendet ex negatione vacui et ex propositionibus prioribus de substantia, quae in hoc fonte invocantur potius quam denuo demonstrantur.","refs":["CH_QGOH_PP_2:A4","CH_QGOH_PP_2:A5"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"From","passages":[{"id":"RAW_QGOH_PP_2:P0007","trace_type":"Direct","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"},{"id":"RAW_QGOH_PP_2:P0008","trace_type":"Direct","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_QGOH_PP_2:MRT6","text":"De intellectu et voluntate divina conditionalis demonstratio aequivocationem cum facultatibus humanis stabilit, sed plena exclusio earum a natura divina ad demonstrationem posteriorem differtur.","refs":["CH_QGOH_PP_2:D9"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"From","passages":[{"id":"RAW_QGOH_PP_2:P0030","trace_type":"Direct","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"},{"id":"RAW_QGOH_PP_2:P0032","trace_type":"Direct","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"},{"id":"RAW_QGOH_PP_2:P0035","trace_type":"Direct","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_QGOH_PP_2:MRT7","text":"Propositio de regressu causarum finitarum structuraliter necessaria est, sed demonstratio eius in fonte dato post titulum “DEMONSTRATIO” non continuatur.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_QGOH_PP_2:P0091","trace_type":"Direct","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"},{"id":"RAW_QGOH_PP_2:P0092","trace_type":"Direct","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"},{"id":"RAW_QGOH_PP_2:P0093","trace_type":"Direct","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]}],"original_name":"Minimal Reconstruction Test"}],"language_code":"la","language_name":"Latina","translations":[{"id":"CAREVAR_20261015T014509045037Z_09ca45f3","language_code":"en","language_name":"English","title":"Part One - On God · passage 2","source_sha256":"3376096908e6ce5d875b0e7226c7b94784d0bdda8b5bf4517707f047f15c3524","sha256":"27c38ec641a55e183110caff2e93ee523ce67a2dc9146ebcff22b083a3346edc","rendition_type":"machine","review_status":"machine","model":"gpt-5.6-terra","created":"2026-10-15T01:45:09.047615+00:00","sections":[{"number":"1","name":"Argumental Spine","items":[{"id":"CH_QGOH_PP_2:AS1","text":"The text examines, polemically and diagnostically, whether extension is incompatible with the divine nature and whether God's causality requires a contingent free will.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_QGOH_PP_2:P0001","trace_type":"Structural","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"},{"id":"RAW_QGOH_PP_2:P0010","trace_type":"Structural","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"},{"id":"RAW_QGOH_PP_2:P0022","trace_type":"Structural","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"},{"id":"RAW_QGOH_PP_2:P0030","trace_type":"Structural","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_QGOH_PP_2:AS2","text":"Arguments against extension arise from the false presupposition that corporeal substance is composed of finite, measurable, and really distinct parts.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_QGOH_PP_2:P0002","trace_type":"Direct","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"},{"id":"RAW_QGOH_PP_2:P0003","trace_type":"Direct","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"},{"id":"RAW_QGOH_PP_2:P0004","trace_type":"Direct","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"},{"id":"RAW_QGOH_PP_2:P0005","trace_type":"Direct","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_QGOH_PP_2:AS3","text":"The distinction between imagination and intellect transfers quantity from the abstract object to extended substance, whence extension is understood as infinite, one, and indivisible.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_QGOH_PP_2:P0008","trace_type":"Direct","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"},{"id":"RAW_QGOH_PP_2:P0009","trace_type":"Direct","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_QGOH_PP_2:AS4","text":"The definition of the divine nature as absolutely infinite converts the necessity of production into a principle: infinitely many things follow necessarily in infinitely many modes.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_QGOH_PP_2:P0012","trace_type":"Direct","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"},{"id":"RAW_QGOH_PP_2:P0014","trace_type":"Direct","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_QGOH_PP_2:AS5","text":"Divine causality is constituted as immanent, necessary, and free from its own nature; particular things are ordered as modes of infinite causal dependence.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_QGOH_PP_2:P0022","trace_type":"Structural","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"},{"id":"RAW_QGOH_PP_2:P0024","trace_type":"Structural","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"},{"id":"RAW_QGOH_PP_2:P0028","trace_type":"Structural","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"},{"id":"RAW_QGOH_PP_2:P0037","trace_type":"Structural","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"},{"id":"RAW_QGOH_PP_2:P0074","trace_type":"Structural","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"},{"id":"RAW_QGOH_PP_2:P0092","trace_type":"Structural","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]}],"original_name":"Argumental Spine"},{"number":"2","name":"Load-Bearing Axioms","items":[{"id":"CH_QGOH_PP_2:A1","text":"God is an absolutely infinite substance, necessarily existing, besides which no substance is given or can be conceived.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_QGOH_PP_2:P0001","trace_type":"Composite","confidence":"medium","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"},{"id":"RAW_QGOH_PP_2:P0002","trace_type":"Composite","confidence":"medium","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"},{"id":"RAW_QGOH_PP_2:P0043","trace_type":"Composite","confidence":"medium","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_QGOH_PP_2:A2","text":"From a given definition of a thing, properties necessarily follow from its very essence.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_QGOH_PP_2:P0014","trace_type":"Direct","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_QGOH_PP_2:A3","text":"An attribute is that which expresses the essence of substance; God's attributes express infinite essence in their own kind.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_QGOH_PP_2:P0014","trace_type":"Composite","confidence":"medium","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"},{"id":"RAW_QGOH_PP_2:P0043","trace_type":"Composite","confidence":"medium","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_QGOH_PP_2:A4","text":"Extension, insofar as it is an attribute of substance, is conceived as infinite, one, and indivisible; body is quantity determined and bounded by figure.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_QGOH_PP_2:P0001","trace_type":"Composite","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"},{"id":"RAW_QGOH_PP_2:P0006","trace_type":"Composite","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"},{"id":"RAW_QGOH_PP_2:P0008","trace_type":"Composite","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_QGOH_PP_2:A5","text":"Quantity conceived by imagination appears finite, divisible, and composed of parts; the same quantity, conceived by intellect as substance, is infinite, one, and indivisible.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_QGOH_PP_2:P0008","trace_type":"Direct","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"},{"id":"RAW_QGOH_PP_2:P0009","trace_type":"Direct","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_QGOH_PP_2:A6","text":"All things are in God and are conceived through God; particular things are affections of God's attributes.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_QGOH_PP_2:P0010","trace_type":"Direct","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"},{"id":"RAW_QGOH_PP_2:P0039","trace_type":"Direct","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"},{"id":"RAW_QGOH_PP_2:P0082","trace_type":"Direct","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_QGOH_PP_2:A7","text":"Outside God no thing is given that can determine God to act, compel Him, or make Him suffer.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_QGOH_PP_2:P0024","trace_type":"Direct","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"},{"id":"RAW_QGOH_PP_2:P0039","trace_type":"Direct","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_QGOH_PP_2:A8","text":"From a given cause an effect necessarily follows; the non-following of an effect from a given cause is contradictory.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_QGOH_PP_2:P0030","trace_type":"Direct","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_QGOH_PP_2:A9","text":"A free cause exists and acts from the necessity of its nature alone.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_QGOH_PP_2:P0028","trace_type":"Direct","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_QGOH_PP_2:A10","text":"The essence of a thing produced by God involves neither existence nor duration.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_QGOH_PP_2:P0070","trace_type":"Direct","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"},{"id":"RAW_QGOH_PP_2:P0074","trace_type":"Direct","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_QGOH_PP_2:A11","text":"God is the efficient cause both of the essence and of the existence of things, and the cause of their persevering in existence.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_QGOH_PP_2:P0074","trace_type":"Direct","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"},{"id":"RAW_QGOH_PP_2:P0076","trace_type":"Direct","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_QGOH_PP_2:A12","text":"Every singular thing that is finite and exists in a determinate manner is required to exist and act by another finite and determinate cause, and so on to infinity.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_QGOH_PP_2:P0092","trace_type":"Direct","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_QGOH_PP_2:A13","text":"A thing determined by God to act cannot render itself undetermined.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_QGOH_PP_2:P0084","trace_type":"Direct","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"},{"id":"RAW_QGOH_PP_2:P0088","trace_type":"Direct","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]}],"original_name":"Load-Bearing Axioms"},{"number":"3","name":"Apophatic Structure","items":[{"id":"CH_QGOH_PP_2:NX1","text":"God is not a body bounded by determinate figure, nor a human person subject to passions; the negation of finite body is not the negation of attributive extension.","refs":["CH_QGOH_PP_2:A4","CH_QGOH_PP_2:A5"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"From","passages":[{"id":"RAW_QGOH_PP_2:P0001","trace_type":"Negative","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"},{"id":"RAW_QGOH_PP_2:P0006","trace_type":"Negative","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"},{"id":"RAW_QGOH_PP_2:P0008","trace_type":"Negative","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_QGOH_PP_2:NX2","text":"Extended substance is not a thing created by God or posited outside God, but an attribute of divine substance.","refs":["CH_QGOH_PP_2:A1","CH_QGOH_PP_2:A4"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"From","passages":[{"id":"RAW_QGOH_PP_2:P0001","trace_type":"Negative","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"},{"id":"RAW_QGOH_PP_2:P0002","trace_type":"Negative","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"},{"id":"RAW_QGOH_PP_2:P0010","trace_type":"Negative","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_QGOH_PP_2:NX3","text":"The infinity of extension is not measurable quantity, compounded of finite parts, multipliable, or really divisible.","refs":["CH_QGOH_PP_2:A4","CH_QGOH_PP_2:A5"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"From","passages":[{"id":"RAW_QGOH_PP_2:P0005","trace_type":"Negative","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"},{"id":"RAW_QGOH_PP_2:P0006","trace_type":"Negative","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"},{"id":"RAW_QGOH_PP_2:P0008","trace_type":"Negative","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_QGOH_PP_2:NX4","text":"Divine freedom is not indifference between producing and not producing, nor the power to separate an effect from a given cause.","refs":["CH_QGOH_PP_2:A8","CH_QGOH_PP_2:A9"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"From","passages":[{"id":"RAW_QGOH_PP_2:P0028","trace_type":"Negative","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"},{"id":"RAW_QGOH_PP_2:P0030","trace_type":"Negative","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_QGOH_PP_2:NX5","text":"God does not act from an external impulse, an internal deficiency, or a will constituted by a contingent decree.","refs":["CH_QGOH_PP_2:A7","CH_QGOH_PP_2:A9"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"From","passages":[{"id":"RAW_QGOH_PP_2:P0024","trace_type":"Negative","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"},{"id":"RAW_QGOH_PP_2:P0026","trace_type":"Negative","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"},{"id":"RAW_QGOH_PP_2:P0030","trace_type":"Negative","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_QGOH_PP_2:NX6","text":"God is not a transitive cause that leaves effects outside Himself.","refs":["CH_QGOH_PP_2:A1","CH_QGOH_PP_2:A6","CH_QGOH_PP_2:A7"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"From","passages":[{"id":"RAW_QGOH_PP_2:P0037","trace_type":"Direct","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"},{"id":"RAW_QGOH_PP_2:P0039","trace_type":"Direct","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_QGOH_PP_2:NX7","text":"A finite thing is not the cause of itself, nor the principle of its own action, nor the principle of its own perseverance.","refs":["CH_QGOH_PP_2:A10","CH_QGOH_PP_2:A11","CH_QGOH_PP_2:A12","CH_QGOH_PP_2:A13"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"From","passages":[{"id":"RAW_QGOH_PP_2:P0070","trace_type":"Composite","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"},{"id":"RAW_QGOH_PP_2:P0074","trace_type":"Composite","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"},{"id":"RAW_QGOH_PP_2:P0084","trace_type":"Composite","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"},{"id":"RAW_QGOH_PP_2:P0092","trace_type":"Composite","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]}],"original_name":"Apophatic Structure"},{"number":"4","name":"Derivative Propositions","items":[{"id":"CH_QGOH_PP_2:D1","text":"Arguments that remove extension from God show only that infinite quantity is not measurable and cannot be compounded of finite parts.","refs":["CH_QGOH_PP_2:A4","CH_QGOH_PP_2:A5"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"From","passages":[{"id":"RAW_QGOH_PP_2:P0005","trace_type":"Direct","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"},{"id":"RAW_QGOH_PP_2:P0006","trace_type":"Direct","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_QGOH_PP_2:D2","text":"God and His attributes are eternal; divine existence and divine essence are one and the same, and the attributes are immutable.","refs":["CH_QGOH_PP_2:A1","CH_QGOH_PP_2:A3"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"From","passages":[{"id":"RAW_QGOH_PP_2:P0041","trace_type":"Composite","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"},{"id":"RAW_QGOH_PP_2:P0043","trace_type":"Composite","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"},{"id":"RAW_QGOH_PP_2:P0047","trace_type":"Composite","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"},{"id":"RAW_QGOH_PP_2:P0053","trace_type":"Composite","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_QGOH_PP_2:D3","text":"From the necessity of the divine nature infinitely many things follow in infinitely many modes, that is, all things that can fall under infinite intellect.","refs":["CH_QGOH_PP_2:A1","CH_QGOH_PP_2:A2","CH_QGOH_PP_2:A3"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"From","passages":[{"id":"RAW_QGOH_PP_2:P0012","trace_type":"Direct","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"},{"id":"RAW_QGOH_PP_2:P0014","trace_type":"Direct","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_QGOH_PP_2:D4","text":"God is the efficient cause of things, the cause through itself, and the absolutely first cause, which acts from the laws of its nature alone.","refs":["CH_QGOH_PP_2:A6","CH_QGOH_PP_2:A7","CH_QGOH_PP_2:A8","CH_QGOH_PP_2:A9"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"From","passages":[{"id":"RAW_QGOH_PP_2:P0016","trace_type":"Composite","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"},{"id":"RAW_QGOH_PP_2:P0018","trace_type":"Composite","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"},{"id":"RAW_QGOH_PP_2:P0020","trace_type":"Composite","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"},{"id":"RAW_QGOH_PP_2:P0022","trace_type":"Composite","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"},{"id":"RAW_QGOH_PP_2:P0024","trace_type":"Composite","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_QGOH_PP_2:D5","text":"God is the immanent cause of all things, because effects are in God and no substance subsists outside God.","refs":["CH_QGOH_PP_2:A1","CH_QGOH_PP_2:A6","CH_QGOH_PP_2:A7"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"From","passages":[{"id":"RAW_QGOH_PP_2:P0037","trace_type":"Direct","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"},{"id":"RAW_QGOH_PP_2:P0039","trace_type":"Direct","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_QGOH_PP_2:D6","text":"Particular modes depend on God not only in order to begin to exist, but also with respect to essence and the continuation of existence.","refs":["CH_QGOH_PP_2:A6","CH_QGOH_PP_2:A10","CH_QGOH_PP_2:A11"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"From","passages":[{"id":"RAW_QGOH_PP_2:P0070","trace_type":"Composite","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"},{"id":"RAW_QGOH_PP_2:P0074","trace_type":"Composite","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"},{"id":"RAW_QGOH_PP_2:P0076","trace_type":"Composite","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_QGOH_PP_2:D7","text":"Every necessarily infinite mode follows either immediately from the absolute nature of a divine attribute or through a modification that is itself necessary and infinite.","refs":["CH_QGOH_PP_2:A2","CH_QGOH_PP_2:A3","CH_QGOH_PP_2:A6","CH_QGOH_PP_2:D3"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"From","passages":[{"id":"RAW_QGOH_PP_2:P0055","trace_type":"Direct","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"},{"id":"RAW_QGOH_PP_2:P0062","trace_type":"Direct","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"},{"id":"RAW_QGOH_PP_2:P0066","trace_type":"Direct","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"},{"id":"RAW_QGOH_PP_2:P0068","trace_type":"Direct","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_QGOH_PP_2:D8","text":"The order of finite things is an order of finite determinations to infinity, in which no finite thing establishes itself to act or frees itself from a received determination.","refs":["CH_QGOH_PP_2:A11","CH_QGOH_PP_2:A12","CH_QGOH_PP_2:A13"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"From","passages":[{"id":"RAW_QGOH_PP_2:P0084","trace_type":"Structural","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"},{"id":"RAW_QGOH_PP_2:P0088","trace_type":"Structural","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"},{"id":"RAW_QGOH_PP_2:P0092","trace_type":"Structural","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_QGOH_PP_2:D9","text":"If intellect or will belonged to the divine essence, they would differ from human faculties not in species or degree, but in essence and existence.","refs":["CH_QGOH_PP_2:A2","CH_QGOH_PP_2:A8","CH_QGOH_PP_2:D4"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"From","passages":[{"id":"RAW_QGOH_PP_2:P0032","trace_type":"Direct","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"},{"id":"RAW_QGOH_PP_2:P0033","trace_type":"Direct","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"},{"id":"RAW_QGOH_PP_2:P0034","trace_type":"Direct","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"},{"id":"RAW_QGOH_PP_2:P0035","trace_type":"Direct","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]}],"original_name":"Derivative Propositions"},{"number":"5","name":"Super-Derivatives","items":[{"id":"CH_QGOH_PP_2:SD1","text":"Transitive creation is replaced by immanent dependence: things are not produced outside God, but express God's attributes in a certain and determinate manner.","refs":["CH_QGOH_PP_2:D5","CH_QGOH_PP_2:D6"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"From","passages":[{"id":"RAW_QGOH_PP_2:P0037","trace_type":"Composite","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"},{"id":"RAW_QGOH_PP_2:P0074","trace_type":"Composite","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"},{"id":"RAW_QGOH_PP_2:P0082","trace_type":"Composite","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_QGOH_PP_2:SD2","text":"The system constitutes a twofold order of causality: infinite modes arise from the absolute nature of attributes, while finite things are determined through a series of finite causes.","refs":["CH_QGOH_PP_2:D3","CH_QGOH_PP_2:D7","CH_QGOH_PP_2:D8"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"From","passages":[{"id":"RAW_QGOH_PP_2:P0055","trace_type":"Structural","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"},{"id":"RAW_QGOH_PP_2:P0066","trace_type":"Structural","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"},{"id":"RAW_QGOH_PP_2:P0092","trace_type":"Structural","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_QGOH_PP_2:SD3","text":"Omnipotence is not a power reserving possibilities not actually produced, but the inexhaustible eternal actuality of a necessarily productive nature.","refs":["CH_QGOH_PP_2:D3","CH_QGOH_PP_2:D4","CH_QGOH_PP_2:D9"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"From","passages":[{"id":"RAW_QGOH_PP_2:P0031","trace_type":"Direct","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"},{"id":"RAW_QGOH_PP_2:P0032","trace_type":"Direct","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_QGOH_PP_2:SD4","text":"The paradoxes of the infinite do not affect infinite being, but arise when the schema of imagination, measure, and composition is transferred to substance.","refs":["CH_QGOH_PP_2:D1","CH_QGOH_PP_2:D2"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"From","passages":[{"id":"RAW_QGOH_PP_2:P0005","trace_type":"Structural","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"},{"id":"RAW_QGOH_PP_2:P0006","trace_type":"Structural","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"},{"id":"RAW_QGOH_PP_2:P0008","trace_type":"Structural","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]}],"original_name":"Super-Derivatives"},{"number":"6","name":"Deep Apophatics","items":[{"id":"CH_QGOH_PP_2:DNX1","text":"The ontological model according to which several independent substances, real parts, or a void between things subsist must be false.","refs":["CH_QGOH_PP_2:A1","CH_QGOH_PP_2:A4","CH_QGOH_PP_2:A6"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"From","passages":[{"id":"RAW_QGOH_PP_2:P0002","trace_type":"Negative","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"},{"id":"RAW_QGOH_PP_2:P0007","trace_type":"Negative","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"},{"id":"RAW_QGOH_PP_2:P0010","trace_type":"Negative","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_QGOH_PP_2:DNX2","text":"The possibility whereby a divine effect could be separated from the divine essence, or whereby God could not produce what He understands, must be false.","refs":["CH_QGOH_PP_2:A2","CH_QGOH_PP_2:A8","CH_QGOH_PP_2:D3"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"From","passages":[{"id":"RAW_QGOH_PP_2:P0014","trace_type":"Negative","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"},{"id":"RAW_QGOH_PP_2:P0030","trace_type":"Negative","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"},{"id":"RAW_QGOH_PP_2:P0031","trace_type":"Negative","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_QGOH_PP_2:DNX3","text":"The assumption that finite individuals contain within themselves their own existence, duration, or autonomous origin must be false.","refs":["CH_QGOH_PP_2:A10","CH_QGOH_PP_2:A11","CH_QGOH_PP_2:A12"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"From","passages":[{"id":"RAW_QGOH_PP_2:P0070","trace_type":"Negative","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"},{"id":"RAW_QGOH_PP_2:P0074","trace_type":"Negative","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"},{"id":"RAW_QGOH_PP_2:P0092","trace_type":"Negative","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]}],"original_name":"Deep Apophatics"},{"number":"7","name":"Super-Apophatics","items":[{"id":"CH_QGOH_PP_2:SNX1","text":"Every “outside God” is excluded at once as a place of substance, cause, condition of intelligibility, and terminus of causality.","refs":["CH_QGOH_PP_2:A1","CH_QGOH_PP_2:A6","CH_QGOH_PP_2:A7","CH_QGOH_PP_2:D5"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"From","passages":[{"id":"RAW_QGOH_PP_2:P0002","trace_type":"Negative","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"},{"id":"RAW_QGOH_PP_2:P0010","trace_type":"Negative","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"},{"id":"RAW_QGOH_PP_2:P0039","trace_type":"Negative","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_QGOH_PP_2:SNX2","text":"Every interval between divine power and divine actuality is excluded; divine power cannot be an idle or futurible capacity.","refs":["CH_QGOH_PP_2:A8","CH_QGOH_PP_2:D3","CH_QGOH_PP_2:SD3"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"From","passages":[{"id":"RAW_QGOH_PP_2:P0030","trace_type":"Negative","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"},{"id":"RAW_QGOH_PP_2:P0031","trace_type":"Negative","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"},{"id":"RAW_QGOH_PP_2:P0032","trace_type":"Negative","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]}],"original_name":"Super-Apophatics"},{"number":"8","name":"Formal Generative Schema","items":[{"id":"CH_QGOH_PP_2:FGS1","text":"Imagined quantity → finite parts and measure → paradoxes of the infinite → false exclusion of extension from the divine nature.","refs":["CH_QGOH_PP_2:A4","CH_QGOH_PP_2:A5","CH_QGOH_PP_2:D1"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"From","passages":[{"id":"RAW_QGOH_PP_2:P0003","trace_type":"Structural","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"},{"id":"RAW_QGOH_PP_2:P0005","trace_type":"Structural","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"},{"id":"RAW_QGOH_PP_2:P0006","trace_type":"Structural","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_QGOH_PP_2:FGS2","text":"Understood extended substance → unity and indivisibility → extension as a divine attribute, not as a finite body.","refs":["CH_QGOH_PP_2:A1","CH_QGOH_PP_2:A4","CH_QGOH_PP_2:A5"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"From","passages":[{"id":"RAW_QGOH_PP_2:P0002","trace_type":"Structural","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"},{"id":"RAW_QGOH_PP_2:P0006","trace_type":"Structural","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"},{"id":"RAW_QGOH_PP_2:P0008","trace_type":"Structural","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_QGOH_PP_2:FGS3","text":"Definition of essence → necessary properties → absolutely infinite attributes → infinitely many things follow in infinitely many modes.","refs":["CH_QGOH_PP_2:A2","CH_QGOH_PP_2:A3","CH_QGOH_PP_2:D3"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"From","passages":[{"id":"RAW_QGOH_PP_2:P0014","trace_type":"Direct","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_QGOH_PP_2:FGS4","text":"All things in God + no cause outside God → action from its own laws → free, through-itself, and immanent cause.","refs":["CH_QGOH_PP_2:A6","CH_QGOH_PP_2:A7","CH_QGOH_PP_2:A9","CH_QGOH_PP_2:D4","CH_QGOH_PP_2:D5"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"From","passages":[{"id":"RAW_QGOH_PP_2:P0010","trace_type":"Structural","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"},{"id":"RAW_QGOH_PP_2:P0024","trace_type":"Structural","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"},{"id":"RAW_QGOH_PP_2:P0028","trace_type":"Structural","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"},{"id":"RAW_QGOH_PP_2:P0037","trace_type":"Structural","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_QGOH_PP_2:FGS5","text":"Divine essence involving existence / essence of a mode not involving existence → God as cause of the essence and existence of modes → persevering dependence.","refs":["CH_QGOH_PP_2:D2","CH_QGOH_PP_2:A10","CH_QGOH_PP_2:A11","CH_QGOH_PP_2:D6"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"From","passages":[{"id":"RAW_QGOH_PP_2:P0047","trace_type":"Composite","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"},{"id":"RAW_QGOH_PP_2:P0070","trace_type":"Composite","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"},{"id":"RAW_QGOH_PP_2:P0074","trace_type":"Composite","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"},{"id":"RAW_QGOH_PP_2:P0076","trace_type":"Composite","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_QGOH_PP_2:FGS6","text":"Necessary infinite mode → attribute considered absolutely or infinite modification; finite thing → determining finite cause → infinite regress.","refs":["CH_QGOH_PP_2:D7","CH_QGOH_PP_2:D8"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"From","passages":[{"id":"RAW_QGOH_PP_2:P0066","trace_type":"Structural","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"},{"id":"RAW_QGOH_PP_2:P0068","trace_type":"Structural","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"},{"id":"RAW_QGOH_PP_2:P0092","trace_type":"Structural","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]}],"original_name":"Formal Generative Schema"},{"number":"9","name":"Argument Flow Map","items":[{"id":"CH_QGOH_PP_2:AF1","text":"Figured and passible body → false identification of extension with finite body → apparent exclusion of divine extension.","refs":["CH_QGOH_PP_2:NX1","CH_QGOH_PP_2:NX2"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"From","passages":[{"id":"RAW_QGOH_PP_2:P0001","trace_type":"Structural","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"},{"id":"RAW_QGOH_PP_2:P0002","trace_type":"Structural","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"},{"id":"RAW_QGOH_PP_2:P0006","trace_type":"Structural","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_QGOH_PP_2:AF2","text":"Division, measure, and multiplication of the infinite → diagnosis of imagination → restoration of indivisible extended substance.","refs":["CH_QGOH_PP_2:D1"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"From","passages":[{"id":"RAW_QGOH_PP_2:P0005","trace_type":"Structural","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"},{"id":"RAW_QGOH_PP_2:P0006","trace_type":"Structural","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"},{"id":"RAW_QGOH_PP_2:P0008","trace_type":"Structural","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_QGOH_PP_2:AF3","text":"Absolutely infinite divine nature → necessary consequence of properties → infinite production in infinitely many modes.","refs":["CH_QGOH_PP_2:D3"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"From","passages":[{"id":"RAW_QGOH_PP_2:P0012","trace_type":"Direct","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"},{"id":"RAW_QGOH_PP_2:P0014","trace_type":"Direct","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_QGOH_PP_2:AF4","text":"No external determination → action from nature → freedom as necessity of itself → rejection of indifferent will.","refs":["CH_QGOH_PP_2:D4","CH_QGOH_PP_2:NX4"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"From","passages":[{"id":"RAW_QGOH_PP_2:P0024","trace_type":"Structural","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"},{"id":"RAW_QGOH_PP_2:P0028","trace_type":"Structural","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"},{"id":"RAW_QGOH_PP_2:P0030","trace_type":"Structural","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_QGOH_PP_2:AF5","text":"All things in God → immanent causality → modes as affections of attributes → dependence of essence and existence.","refs":["CH_QGOH_PP_2:D5","CH_QGOH_PP_2:D6"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"From","passages":[{"id":"RAW_QGOH_PP_2:P0039","trace_type":"Structural","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"},{"id":"RAW_QGOH_PP_2:P0074","trace_type":"Structural","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"},{"id":"RAW_QGOH_PP_2:P0082","trace_type":"Structural","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_QGOH_PP_2:AF6","text":"Necessary infinite mode / determinate finite thing → twofold architecture of causality → finite series of causes to infinity.","refs":["CH_QGOH_PP_2:D7","CH_QGOH_PP_2:D8"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"From","passages":[{"id":"RAW_QGOH_PP_2:P0068","trace_type":"Structural","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"},{"id":"RAW_QGOH_PP_2:P0092","trace_type":"Structural","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]}],"original_name":"Argument Flow Map"},{"number":"10","name":"Map-Effects","items":[{"id":"CH_QGOH_PP_2:ME1","text":"The opposition “God either body or incorporeal” is dissolved through the distinction between determinate body and extension as an attribute of substance.","refs":["CH_QGOH_PP_2:D1","CH_QGOH_PP_2:NX1"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"From","passages":[{"id":"RAW_QGOH_PP_2:P0001","trace_type":"Tension","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"},{"id":"RAW_QGOH_PP_2:P0006","trace_type":"Tension","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"},{"id":"RAW_QGOH_PP_2:P0008","trace_type":"Tension","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_QGOH_PP_2:ME2","text":"The infinite is no longer classified through number, measure, or aggregation of parts, but through the nature of substance and attribute.","refs":["CH_QGOH_PP_2:D1","CH_QGOH_PP_2:SD4"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"From","passages":[{"id":"RAW_QGOH_PP_2:P0005","trace_type":"Structural","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"},{"id":"RAW_QGOH_PP_2:P0008","trace_type":"Structural","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_QGOH_PP_2:ME3","text":"Freedom and necessity cease to be opposites, because divine freedom is defined through action from the necessity of nature alone.","refs":["CH_QGOH_PP_2:A9","CH_QGOH_PP_2:D4"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"From","passages":[{"id":"RAW_QGOH_PP_2:P0028","trace_type":"Direct","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"},{"id":"RAW_QGOH_PP_2:P0030","trace_type":"Direct","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_QGOH_PP_2:ME4","text":"God's relation to things is redescribed from external fabrication to immanent causality of essence, existence, and perseverance.","refs":["CH_QGOH_PP_2:D5","CH_QGOH_PP_2:D6","CH_QGOH_PP_2:SD1"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"From","passages":[{"id":"RAW_QGOH_PP_2:P0037","trace_type":"Structural","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"},{"id":"RAW_QGOH_PP_2:P0074","trace_type":"Structural","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"},{"id":"RAW_QGOH_PP_2:P0076","trace_type":"Structural","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"},{"id":"RAW_QGOH_PP_2:P0082","trace_type":"Structural","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_QGOH_PP_2:ME5","text":"Time and causality differ according to the grade of being: attributes and infinite modes express eternity, finite things the order of finite determinations.","refs":["CH_QGOH_PP_2:D2","CH_QGOH_PP_2:D7","CH_QGOH_PP_2:D8"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"From","passages":[{"id":"RAW_QGOH_PP_2:P0041","trace_type":"Structural","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"},{"id":"RAW_QGOH_PP_2:P0055","trace_type":"Structural","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"},{"id":"RAW_QGOH_PP_2:P0066","trace_type":"Structural","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"},{"id":"RAW_QGOH_PP_2:P0092","trace_type":"Structural","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]}],"original_name":"Map-Effects"},{"number":"11","name":"Altitude Levels","items":[{"id":"CH_QGOH_PP_2:ALT1","text":"Surface — The text refutes arguments that render extension unworthy of the divine nature and defines God as the cause of all things.","refs":["CH_QGOH_PP_2:D1","CH_QGOH_PP_2:D4","CH_QGOH_PP_2:D5"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"From","passages":[{"id":"RAW_QGOH_PP_2:P0005","trace_type":"Structural","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"},{"id":"RAW_QGOH_PP_2:P0016","trace_type":"Structural","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"},{"id":"RAW_QGOH_PP_2:P0037","trace_type":"Structural","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_QGOH_PP_2:ALT2","text":"Structural — The argumentation opposes imagination to intellect, substance to modes, immanent causality to transitive causality, and determination and necessity to indifference.","refs":["CH_QGOH_PP_2:A5","CH_QGOH_PP_2:A6","CH_QGOH_PP_2:D4","CH_QGOH_PP_2:D5"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"From","passages":[{"id":"RAW_QGOH_PP_2:P0008","trace_type":"Structural","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"},{"id":"RAW_QGOH_PP_2:P0037","trace_type":"Structural","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"},{"id":"RAW_QGOH_PP_2:P0028","trace_type":"Structural","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"},{"id":"RAW_QGOH_PP_2:P0084","trace_type":"Structural","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_QGOH_PP_2:ALT3","text":"Metastrategic — The adversaries' reductio ad absurdum is turned back: the absurdity does not follow from the infinite, but from the adversaries' premises concerning parts, measure, and composition.","refs":["CH_QGOH_PP_2:D1","CH_QGOH_PP_2:SD4"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"From","passages":[{"id":"RAW_QGOH_PP_2:P0005","trace_type":"Structural","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"},{"id":"RAW_QGOH_PP_2:P0006","trace_type":"Structural","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_QGOH_PP_2:ALT4","text":"Ontological/Temporal — God is eternal existence and essence, one and the same; finite things distinguish essence from existence and depend on a series of causes for both.","refs":["CH_QGOH_PP_2:D2","CH_QGOH_PP_2:D6","CH_QGOH_PP_2:D8"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"From","passages":[{"id":"RAW_QGOH_PP_2:P0047","trace_type":"Structural","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"},{"id":"RAW_QGOH_PP_2:P0070","trace_type":"Structural","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"},{"id":"RAW_QGOH_PP_2:P0074","trace_type":"Structural","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"},{"id":"RAW_QGOH_PP_2:P0092","trace_type":"Structural","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]}],"original_name":"Altitude Levels"},{"number":"12","name":"Minimal Reconstruction Test","items":[{"id":"CH_QGOH_PP_2:MRT1","text":"The axioms concerning one substance, attributes, the distinction between imagination and intellect, necessary causality, and the dependence of modes suffice to reconstruct the principal movement.","refs":["CH_QGOH_PP_2:A1","CH_QGOH_PP_2:A5","CH_QGOH_PP_2:A6","CH_QGOH_PP_2:A8","CH_QGOH_PP_2:A10"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"From","passages":[{"id":"RAW_QGOH_PP_2:P0002","trace_type":"Structural","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"},{"id":"RAW_QGOH_PP_2:P0008","trace_type":"Structural","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"},{"id":"RAW_QGOH_PP_2:P0010","trace_type":"Structural","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"},{"id":"RAW_QGOH_PP_2:P0030","trace_type":"Structural","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"},{"id":"RAW_QGOH_PP_2:P0074","trace_type":"Structural","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_QGOH_PP_2:MRT2","text":"The strongest joint is the transition from the error of measure to the indivisibility of substance, because the adversaries' arguments are dissolved by the same compositional presupposition.","refs":["CH_QGOH_PP_2:A4","CH_QGOH_PP_2:A5","CH_QGOH_PP_2:D1"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"From","passages":[{"id":"RAW_QGOH_PP_2:P0005","trace_type":"Structural","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"},{"id":"RAW_QGOH_PP_2:P0006","trace_type":"Structural","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"},{"id":"RAW_QGOH_PP_2:P0008","trace_type":"Structural","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_QGOH_PP_2:MRT3","text":"A tension remains between God as determining every agent and the finite thing as immediately determined by another finite cause; immanence requires this compatibility, but the relation of mediation is not fully formalized here.","refs":["CH_QGOH_PP_2:D4","CH_QGOH_PP_2:D5","CH_QGOH_PP_2:D8"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"From","passages":[{"id":"RAW_QGOH_PP_2:P0024","trace_type":"Tension","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"},{"id":"RAW_QGOH_PP_2:P0037","trace_type":"Tension","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"},{"id":"RAW_QGOH_PP_2:P0084","trace_type":"Tension","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"},{"id":"RAW_QGOH_PP_2:P0092","trace_type":"Tension","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_QGOH_PP_2:MRT4","text":"The distinction between infinite modes and finite things preserves the order of the system, but the exact nexus between eternal production and the finite causal series is given only summarily.","refs":["CH_QGOH_PP_2:D3","CH_QGOH_PP_2:D7","CH_QGOH_PP_2:D8"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"From","passages":[{"id":"RAW_QGOH_PP_2:P0055","trace_type":"Tension","confidence":"medium","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"},{"id":"RAW_QGOH_PP_2:P0068","trace_type":"Tension","confidence":"medium","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"},{"id":"RAW_QGOH_PP_2:P0092","trace_type":"Tension","confidence":"medium","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_QGOH_PP_2:MRT5","text":"The refutation of extension depends on the negation of the void and on prior propositions concerning substance, which are invoked in this source rather than demonstrated anew.","refs":["CH_QGOH_PP_2:A4","CH_QGOH_PP_2:A5"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"From","passages":[{"id":"RAW_QGOH_PP_2:P0007","trace_type":"Direct","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"},{"id":"RAW_QGOH_PP_2:P0008","trace_type":"Direct","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_QGOH_PP_2:MRT6","text":"The conditional demonstration concerning divine intellect and will establishes their equivocation with human faculties, but their full exclusion from the divine nature is deferred to a later demonstration.","refs":["CH_QGOH_PP_2:D9"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"From","passages":[{"id":"RAW_QGOH_PP_2:P0030","trace_type":"Direct","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"},{"id":"RAW_QGOH_PP_2:P0032","trace_type":"Direct","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"},{"id":"RAW_QGOH_PP_2:P0035","trace_type":"Direct","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_QGOH_PP_2:MRT7","text":"The proposition concerning the regress of finite causes is structurally necessary, but its demonstration in the given source does not continue after the title “DEMONSTRATIO”.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_QGOH_PP_2:P0091","trace_type":"Direct","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"},{"id":"RAW_QGOH_PP_2:P0092","trace_type":"Direct","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"},{"id":"RAW_QGOH_PP_2:P0093","trace_type":"Direct","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]}],"original_name":"Minimal Reconstruction Test"}],"revision":"CH_QGOH_PP_2@R001","source_variant_id":"canonical:CH_QGOH_PP_2:v1","source_language_code":"la"}],"identity":{"layer":"CH","kicker":"Chapter 02","title":"Part One - Concerning God · passage 2","context":"Chapter 02","original_title":"Pars prima - De Deo · passage 2","original_context":"Chapter 02"},"original_title":"Pars prima - De Deo · passage 2","title_language_code":"en"},{"id":"CH_QGOH_PP_3","layer":"CH","title":"Part One - Concerning God · passage 3","order":3,"parents":["RAW_QGOH_PP_3"],"revision":"CH_QGOH_PP_3@R001","sha256":"b632a01a4019878e6caf0283d6c7a831804ce28b046b0245f2c4509900d03607","sections":[{"number":"1","name":"Argumental Spine","items":[{"id":"CH_QGOH_PP_3:AS1","text":"Textus quaerit qua ratione res finitae et determinatae intra Deum causentur, et quomodo ex hac causalitate contingentia, liberum arbitrium et ordo rerum alternativus excludantur.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[]},{"id":"CH_QGOH_PP_3:AS2","text":"Demonstratio a discrimine incipit inter effectum infinitum atque aeternum, qui ex natura absoluta attributi sequitur, et modum finitum, qui media modificatione finita causari debet.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[]},{"id":"CH_QGOH_PP_3:AS3","text":"Series causarum finitarum in infinitum producit thesim universalem: omnia ad existendum et operandum ex necessitate divinae naturae determinantur.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[]},{"id":"CH_QGOH_PP_3:AS4","text":"Necessitas ontologica per distinctionem Naturae Naturantis et Naturae Naturatae ordinatur, ut Deus causalis et modi causati non confundantur.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[]},{"id":"CH_QGOH_PP_3:AS5","text":"Intellectus, voluntas et ceterae affectiones mentales ad Naturam Naturatam rediguntur; ideo voluntas non est principium liberum nec divinum arbitrium.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[]},{"id":"CH_QGOH_PP_3:AS6","text":"Unicitas ordinis rerum ex identitate essentiae, potentiae et causalitatis divinae deducitur; possibilitas alterius ordinis in contradictionem cum natura divina incidit.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[]},{"id":"CH_QGOH_PP_3:AS7","text":"Appendix transit ab ontologia necessitatis ad genealogiam finalismi, explicans quomodo ignorantia causarum et conscientia appetitus naturam ad fines humanos ordinatam fingant.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[]}],"original_name":"Argumental Spine"},{"number":"2","name":"Load-Bearing Axioms","items":[{"id":"CH_QGOH_PP_3:A1","text":"Omne quod determinatum est ad existendum et operandum, a Deo determinatur.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[]},{"id":"CH_QGOH_PP_3:A2","text":"Quod ex absoluta natura alicuius attributi divini sequitur, infinitum et aeternum est.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[]},{"id":"CH_QGOH_PP_3:A3","text":"Modus finitus et determinatam existentiam habens non sequitur ex Deo vel attributo quatenus modificatione aeterna et infinita affectum consideratur.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[]},{"id":"CH_QGOH_PP_3:A4","text":"Modus finitus ex Deo vel attributo sequi debet quatenus modificatione finita et determinate existente modificatum est.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[]},{"id":"CH_QGOH_PP_3:A5","text":"Quaevis causa finita et determinata ab alia causa finita et determinata causatur, et haec concatenatio in infinitum procedit.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[]},{"id":"CH_QGOH_PP_3:A6","text":"Praeter substantiam et modos nihil datur, et modi sunt affectiones attributorum Dei.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[]},{"id":"CH_QGOH_PP_3:A7","text":"Omnia in Deo sunt et ab eo ita dependent ut sine eo nec esse nec concipi possint.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[]},{"id":"CH_QGOH_PP_3:A8","text":"Deus necessario existit, et modi divinae naturae necessario sequuntur atque a Deo causantur tam quatenus existunt quam quatenus agunt.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[]},{"id":"CH_QGOH_PP_3:A9","text":"Natura Naturans est Deus quatenus attributa essentiam aeternam et infinitam exprimentia causa libera consideratur; Natura Naturata est complexus modorum necessario sequentium.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[]},{"id":"CH_QGOH_PP_3:A10","text":"Idea vera cum suo ideato convenire debet, et in Natura nulla substantia praeter Deum nec affectiones praeter affectiones in Deo datas reperiuntur.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[]},{"id":"CH_QGOH_PP_3:A11","text":"Intellectus actu, sive finitus sive infinitus, et ceterae affectiones cogitandi sunt modi, non absoluta cogitatio.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[]},{"id":"CH_QGOH_PP_3:A12","text":"Voluntas, sive finita sive infinita concipiatur, causam requirit a qua ad existendum et operandum determinetur.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[]},{"id":"CH_QGOH_PP_3:A13","text":"Res alio modo aut alio ordine produci non possunt, quia alter ordo aliam naturam divinam et consequenter plures deos posceret.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[]},{"id":"CH_QGOH_PP_3:A14","text":"Necessitas ex essentia rei vel causa efficiente sequitur; impossibilitas ex contradictione essentiali vel absentia causae externae; contingentia et possibilitas ex cognitionis defectu oriuntur.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[]},{"id":"CH_QGOH_PP_3:A15","text":"Potentia Dei est ipsa essentia Dei, et quicquid in divina potestate concipitur necessario est.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[]},{"id":"CH_QGOH_PP_3:A16","text":"Omne existens divinam potentiam certo modo exprimit, atque ex natura cuiusque existentis aliquis effectus sequi debet.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[]},{"id":"CH_QGOH_PP_3:A17","text":"Ignorantia causarum, cum conscientia appetitus coniuncta, homines disponit ut se liberos et res naturales media ad fines humanos ordinata existiment.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[]},{"id":"CH_QGOH_PP_3:A18","text":"Mathesis essentias et proprietates figurarum, non fines, considerat atque normam veritatis a finalismo distinctam exhibet.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[]}],"original_name":"Load-Bearing Axioms"},{"number":"3","name":"Apophatic Structure","items":[{"id":"CH_QGOH_PP_3:NX1","text":"Modus finitus non est effectus immediatus naturae absolutae attributi nec modificationis aeternae et infinitae.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[]},{"id":"CH_QGOH_PP_3:NX2","text":"Nulla res finita est substantia independens aut ens extra affectiones divinas constitutum.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[]},{"id":"CH_QGOH_PP_3:NX3","text":"Modus a Deo determinatus se ipsum nec determinare nec indeterminatum reddere potest.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[]},{"id":"CH_QGOH_PP_3:NX4","text":"Deus non est causa remota rerum, quia nullus effectus a Deo causaliter et conceptualiter seiungitur.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[]},{"id":"CH_QGOH_PP_3:NX5","text":"Intellectus potentia non assumitur ut facultas distincta ab intellectione actuali.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[]},{"id":"CH_QGOH_PP_3:NX6","text":"Intellectus, voluntas, cupiditas et amor ad Naturam Naturantem non referuntur.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[]},{"id":"CH_QGOH_PP_3:NX7","text":"Voluntas non est causa libera, sed causa necessaria vel coacta.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[]},{"id":"CH_QGOH_PP_3:NX8","text":"Ordo rerum alternus nec natura rerum altera possibilis est.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[]},{"id":"CH_QGOH_PP_3:NX9","text":"Contingentia et possibilitas non sunt determinationes ontologicae rerum.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[]},{"id":"CH_QGOH_PP_3:NX10","text":"Deus non agit ex arbitrio voluntatis, nec Natura ex fine humano vel bono externo gubernatur.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[]}],"original_name":"Apophatic Structure"},{"number":"4","name":"Derivative Propositions","items":[{"id":"CH_QGOH_PP_3:D1","text":"Omnis res finita ad existendum et operandum per seriem causarum finitarum in infinitum determinatur.","refs":["CH_QGOH_PP_3:A1","CH_QGOH_PP_3:A2","CH_QGOH_PP_3:A3","CH_QGOH_PP_3:A4","CH_QGOH_PP_3:A5"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"From","passages":[]},{"id":"CH_QGOH_PP_3:D2","text":"Deus est causa absolute proxima rerum ab ipso immediate productarum, sed non causa remota, quia effectus divini sine Deo nec esse nec concipi possunt.","refs":["CH_QGOH_PP_3:A6","CH_QGOH_PP_3:A7","CH_QGOH_PP_3:A8"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"From","passages":[]},{"id":"CH_QGOH_PP_3:D3","text":"Determinatio divina complectitur non solum existentiam modorum, sed etiam certum eorum agendi modum.","refs":["CH_QGOH_PP_3:A1","CH_QGOH_PP_3:A8"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"From","passages":[]},{"id":"CH_QGOH_PP_3:D4","text":"Natura Naturans designat principium causale infinitum, Natura Naturata ordinem modalem ab eo necessario dependentem.","refs":["CH_QGOH_PP_3:A7","CH_QGOH_PP_3:A8","CH_QGOH_PP_3:A9"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"From","passages":[]},{"id":"CH_QGOH_PP_3:D5","text":"Intellectus actu finitus aut infinitus nihil comprehendit praeter attributa Dei et affectiones eorum.","refs":["CH_QGOH_PP_3:A7","CH_QGOH_PP_3:A10","CH_QGOH_PP_3:A11"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"From","passages":[]},{"id":"CH_QGOH_PP_3:D6","text":"Voluntas, etiam si infinita ponatur, non potest libertatem causaliter originariam possidere.","refs":["CH_QGOH_PP_3:A5","CH_QGOH_PP_3:A11","CH_QGOH_PP_3:A12"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"From","passages":[]},{"id":"CH_QGOH_PP_3:D7","text":"Voluntas et intellectus eodem ontologico ordine collocantur quo motus, quies et cetera naturalia.","refs":["CH_QGOH_PP_3:A11","CH_QGOH_PP_3:A12"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"From","passages":[]},{"id":"CH_QGOH_PP_3:D8","text":"Contingens et possibile sunt nomina epistemica rei cuius ordo causarum ignoratur, non modi reales ipsius rei.","refs":["CH_QGOH_PP_3:A8","CH_QGOH_PP_3:A14"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"From","passages":[]},{"id":"CH_QGOH_PP_3:D9","text":"Quicquid in potestate Dei concipitur necessario est, quia divina potentia ab essentia divina non distinguitur.","refs":["CH_QGOH_PP_3:A15"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"From","passages":[]},{"id":"CH_QGOH_PP_3:D10","text":"Nullum ens est causaliter otiosum, quia omne existens determinatam expressionem potentiae divinae et fontem effectus constituit.","refs":["CH_QGOH_PP_3:A15","CH_QGOH_PP_3:A16"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"From","passages":[]},{"id":"CH_QGOH_PP_3:D11","text":"Ordo rerum actualis non est electio inter ordines aeque possibiles, sed expressio unica naturae divinae.","refs":["CH_QGOH_PP_3:A8","CH_QGOH_PP_3:A13","CH_QGOH_PP_3:A15"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"From","passages":[]},{"id":"CH_QGOH_PP_3:D12","text":"Interpretatio finalis Naturae ex projectione appetituum humanorum in res ignoratis causis oritur, et in superstitionem de rectoribus Naturae convertitur.","refs":["CH_QGOH_PP_3:A17"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"From","passages":[]},{"id":"CH_QGOH_PP_3:D13","text":"Mathesis facultatem cognoscendi ostendit quae veritatem per essentias et proprietates, non per usus et fines, quaerit.","refs":["CH_QGOH_PP_3:A18"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"From","passages":[]}],"original_name":"Derivative Propositions"},{"number":"5","name":"Super-Derivatives","items":[{"id":"CH_QGOH_PP_3:SD1","text":"Systema causalitatem immanentem et clausam constituit: omnis causa, omnis effectus et omnis intelligibilitas intra Deum atque modos Dei manent.","refs":["CH_QGOH_PP_3:A4","CH_QGOH_PP_3:A5","CH_QGOH_PP_3:A6","CH_QGOH_PP_3:A7","CH_QGOH_PP_3:A8"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"From","passages":[]},{"id":"CH_QGOH_PP_3:SD2","text":"Libertas divina a facultate eligendi inter possibilia ad necessitatem qua Deus ex propria essentia agit transfertur.","refs":["CH_QGOH_PP_3:A9","CH_QGOH_PP_3:A12","CH_QGOH_PP_3:A13","CH_QGOH_PP_3:A15"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"From","passages":[]},{"id":"CH_QGOH_PP_3:SD3","text":"Necessitas rerum est ontologica, dum contingentia nomen est limitationis humanae cognitionis; ignorantia igitur non indicat indeterminationem rei.","refs":["CH_QGOH_PP_3:A8","CH_QGOH_PP_3:A14"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"From","passages":[]},{"id":"CH_QGOH_PP_3:SD4","text":"Actus mentales privilegio causali privantur, quia intellectus et voluntas non sunt principia supra naturam, sed affectiones naturae productae.","refs":["CH_QGOH_PP_3:A9","CH_QGOH_PP_3:A11","CH_QGOH_PP_3:A12","CH_QGOH_PP_3:A16"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"From","passages":[]},{"id":"CH_QGOH_PP_3:SD5","text":"Perfectionis affirmatio non designat ordinem a Deo praeoptatum, sed necessitatem qua natura divina se sine defectu et sine alternativa exprimit.","refs":["CH_QGOH_PP_3:A8","CH_QGOH_PP_3:A13","CH_QGOH_PP_3:A15"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"From","passages":[]},{"id":"CH_QGOH_PP_3:SD6","text":"Finalismus non est explicatio causalis rivalis, sed effectus secundarius condicionis humanae quae fines proprios pro structura Naturae sumit.","refs":["CH_QGOH_PP_3:A17","CH_QGOH_PP_3:A18"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"From","passages":[]}],"original_name":"Super-Derivatives"},{"number":"6","name":"Deep Apophatics","items":[{"id":"CH_QGOH_PP_3:DNX1","text":"Nulla causa finita prima aut seipsam originans admittitur, quia omne finitum in regressu causarum finitarum continetur.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[]},{"id":"CH_QGOH_PP_3:DNX2","text":"Separatio realis inter Creatorem transcendentem et ordinem creatum negatur, quia res omnes in Deo sunt et sine eo nec esse nec concipi possunt.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[]},{"id":"CH_QGOH_PP_3:DNX3","text":"Subiectum voluntarium autonomum tacite excluditur, quia omnis volitio ab alia causa determinatur.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[]},{"id":"CH_QGOH_PP_3:DNX4","text":"Mundus possibilis alternativus excluditur, quia alter rerum ordo aliam naturam divinam postularet.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[]},{"id":"CH_QGOH_PP_3:DNX5","text":"Ens pure passivum vel causaliter sterile excluditur, quia ex quocumque existente aliquis effectus sequi debet.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[]},{"id":"CH_QGOH_PP_3:DNX6","text":"Finis, bonum et utilitas humana ut principia explicativa Naturae excluduntur, quia ex ignorantia causarum et projectione appetitus oriuntur.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[]}],"original_name":"Deep Apophatics"},{"number":"7","name":"Super-Apophatics","items":[{"id":"CH_QGOH_PP_3:SNX1","text":"Possibilitas Dei deliberantis ante ordinem rerum, inter plures ordines eligentis, systemati praecluditur.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[]},{"id":"CH_QGOH_PP_3:SNX2","text":"Nulla norma veritatis, causalitatis vel bonitatis extra Deum statui potest, quia extra substantiam divinam et eius modos nihil datur.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[]},{"id":"CH_QGOH_PP_3:SNX3","text":"Natura ut apparatus ad usum hominis praeparatus admitti non potest, quia talis imago finem humanum in structuram rerum transfert.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[]},{"id":"CH_QGOH_PP_3:SNX4","text":"Contingentia ontica ut residuum inexplicabile vel ruptura ordinis causalis praecluditur.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[]}],"original_name":"Super-Apophatics"},{"number":"8","name":"Formal Generative Schema","items":[{"id":"CH_QGOH_PP_3:FGS1","text":"Natura absoluta attributi → effectus infinitus et aeternus; modus finitus → exclusio productionis immediatae ex absoluto.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[]},{"id":"CH_QGOH_PP_3:FGS2","text":"Exclusio originis absolutae modorum finitorum → mediatio per modificationem finitam → regressus causarum finitarum in infinitum.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[]},{"id":"CH_QGOH_PP_3:FGS3","text":"Regressus modalium causarum + inexistentia rerum extra Deum → dependentia universalis → necessitas existendi et agendi.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[]},{"id":"CH_QGOH_PP_3:FGS4","text":"Necessitas universalis → distinctio Naturae Naturantis et Naturae Naturatae → relocatio intellectus, voluntatis et affectuum inter modos.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[]},{"id":"CH_QGOH_PP_3:FGS5","text":"Voluntas ut modus causatus + exclusio ordinis alterius → recusatio liberi arbitrii et decreti divini indifferentis.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[]},{"id":"CH_QGOH_PP_3:FGS6","text":"Essentia divina = potentia divina → possibile in divina potestate = necessarium → omne existens = expressio activa potentiae.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[]},{"id":"CH_QGOH_PP_3:FGS7","text":"Ignorantia causarum + conscientia appetitus + interpretatio utilitatis → opinio libertatis → finalismus → rectores Naturae, superstitio et iudicia anthropomorphica.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[]}],"original_name":"Formal Generative Schema"},{"number":"9","name":"Argument Flow Map","items":[{"id":"CH_QGOH_PP_3:AF1","text":"Motus demonstrativus procedit a determinatione finita ad mediationem modalem finitam, deinde ad regressum causarum et necessitatem universalem.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[]},{"id":"CH_QGOH_PP_3:AF2","text":"Necessitas universalis → Natura Naturans / Natura Naturata → intellectus et voluntas ut modi → recusatio libertatis voluntariae.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[]},{"id":"CH_QGOH_PP_3:AF3","text":"Unicitas naturae divinae → exclusio ordinis alterius → identitas potentiae et essentiae → necessitas omnium possibilium.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[]},{"id":"CH_QGOH_PP_3:AF4","text":"Causalitas necessaria Naturae → genealogia ignorantiae humanae → constitutio finalismi → indicatio cognitionis mathematicae non-finalis.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[]}],"original_name":"Argument Flow Map"},{"number":"10","name":"Map-Effects","items":[{"id":"CH_QGOH_PP_3:ME1","text":"Res singularis finita reclassificatur ut nodus in serie infinita determinationum modalium, non ut ens causaliter sui iuris.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[]},{"id":"CH_QGOH_PP_3:ME2","text":"Contingens et possibile transferuntur ex ontologia rerum ad epistemologiam cognitionis defectivae.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[]},{"id":"CH_QGOH_PP_3:ME3","text":"Intellectus, voluntas et affectus ex ordine principiorum regentium ad ordinem modorum naturalium causatorum transferuntur.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[]},{"id":"CH_QGOH_PP_3:ME4","text":"Dei causalitas describitur ut immanens et proxima, non ut actio remota artificis extra effectus suos posita.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[]},{"id":"CH_QGOH_PP_3:ME5","text":"Oppositio inter necessitatem et perfectionem dissolvitur, quia perfectio iam non designat optionem optimam sed expressionem necessariam naturae divinae.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[]},{"id":"CH_QGOH_PP_3:ME6","text":"Explicatio finalis rerum naturalium reclassificatur ut projectio anthropologica orta ex ignorantia causarum, non ut descriptio ordinis Naturae.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[]}],"original_name":"Map-Effects"},{"number":"11","name":"Altitude Levels","items":[{"id":"CH_QGOH_PP_3:ALT1","text":"Surface — Textus affirmat nullum contingens dari, voluntatem non liberam esse, res aliter produci non potuisse, potentiam Dei eius essentiam esse, et omne existens effectum producere.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[]},{"id":"CH_QGOH_PP_3:ALT2","text":"Structural — Ordinem rerum constituit catena infinita modorum finitorum, intra substantiam divinam causaliter et conceptualiter comprehensa.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[]},{"id":"CH_QGOH_PP_3:ALT3","text":"Metastrategic — Argumentatio vocabula libertatis, possibilitatis, perfectionis et finalitatis ex eorum usu theologico-voluntaristico in analysin necessitatis et ignorantiae transfert.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[]},{"id":"CH_QGOH_PP_3:ALT4","text":"Ontological/Temporal — Aeternitas divina nullum ante vel post decretorum admittit, dum temporalis apparentia mutationis ad seriem modorum determinatorum refertur.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[]}],"original_name":"Altitude Levels"},{"number":"12","name":"Minimal Reconstruction Test","items":[{"id":"CH_QGOH_PP_3:MRT1","text":"Axiomata de effectu absoluto infinito, mediatione finita et regressu causarum sufficiunt ad restitutionem thesis de necessitate universali.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[]},{"id":"CH_QGOH_PP_3:MRT2","text":"Fortissimum systematis cardo est nexus inter immanentiam divinam, determinationem modalem et negationem contingentiae onticae.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[]},{"id":"CH_QGOH_PP_3:MRT3","text":"Distinctio Naturae Naturantis et Naturae Naturatae necessaria est ne Deus ut voluntas inter modos numerata aut modi ut substantiae independentes concipiantur.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[]},{"id":"CH_QGOH_PP_3:MRT4","text":"Identificatio potentiae cum essentia Dei stabilit transitum a causalitate divina ad necessitatem omnium quae in illa potestate concipiuntur.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[]},{"id":"CH_QGOH_PP_3:MRT5","text":"Commissura fragilis manet in vocabulo libertatis: Deus causa libera dicitur, voluntas autem necessaria vel coacta; distinctio pendet a definitione libertatis quae hic tantum praesupponitur.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[]},{"id":"CH_QGOH_PP_3:MRT6","text":"Transitus a necessitate ordinis ad affirmationem summae perfectionis requirit notionem perfectionis quae non per electionem, bonum externum aut comparationem graduum definiatur.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[]},{"id":"CH_QGOH_PP_3:MRT7","text":"Appendix genealogiam finalismi satis constituit per ignorantiam causarum et appetitum consciam, sed derivationem singulorum iudiciorum de bono, malo, merito et ordine in hoc loco adhuc promittit.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[]},{"id":"CH_QGOH_PP_3:MRT8","text":"Restitutio motum textus servat, quia a causalitate finiti ad necessitatem totalem, ab hac ad demotionem voluntatis, et denique ad criticam finalismi procedit.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[]}],"original_name":"Minimal Reconstruction Test"}],"language_code":"la","language_name":"Latina","translations":[{"id":"CAREVAR_20261015T014535001247Z_e67d312e","language_code":"en","language_name":"English","title":"Part One - On God · passage 3","source_sha256":"b632a01a4019878e6caf0283d6c7a831804ce28b046b0245f2c4509900d03607","sha256":"c6735b36acfdfa7b8a1d621710d86b6b24051679c78d49b40f08d4ba54d59489","rendition_type":"machine","review_status":"machine","model":"gpt-5.6-terra","created":"2026-10-15T01:45:35.002224+00:00","sections":[{"number":"1","name":"Argumental Spine","items":[{"id":"CH_QGOH_PP_3:AS1","text":"The text asks by what means finite and determinate things are caused within God, and how contingency, free will, and an alternative order of things are excluded by this causality.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[]},{"id":"CH_QGOH_PP_3:AS2","text":"The demonstration begins from the distinction between an infinite and eternal effect, which follows from the absolute nature of an attribute, and a finite mode, which must be caused through a finite modification.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[]},{"id":"CH_QGOH_PP_3:AS3","text":"The series of finite causes extending to infinity yields the universal thesis: all things are determined to exist and to act from the necessity of the divine nature.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[]},{"id":"CH_QGOH_PP_3:AS4","text":"Ontological necessity is ordered through the distinction between Natura Naturans and Natura Naturata, so that causal God and caused modes are not confused.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[]},{"id":"CH_QGOH_PP_3:AS5","text":"Intellect, will, and the other mental affections are reduced to Natura Naturata; therefore, the will is not a free principle or divine arbitrium.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[]},{"id":"CH_QGOH_PP_3:AS6","text":"The uniqueness of the order of things is deduced from the identity of divine essence, power, and causality; the possibility of another order falls into contradiction with the divine nature.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[]},{"id":"CH_QGOH_PP_3:AS7","text":"The appendix moves from the ontology of necessity to a genealogy of finalism, explaining how ignorance of causes and consciousness of appetite imagine nature as ordered toward human ends.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[]}],"original_name":"Argumental Spine"},{"number":"2","name":"Load-Bearing Axioms","items":[{"id":"CH_QGOH_PP_3:A1","text":"Everything that is determined to exist and to act is determined by God.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[]},{"id":"CH_QGOH_PP_3:A2","text":"Whatever follows from the absolute nature of some divine attribute is infinite and eternal.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[]},{"id":"CH_QGOH_PP_3:A3","text":"A finite mode having determinate existence does not follow from God or an attribute insofar as it is considered as affected by an eternal and infinite modification.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[]},{"id":"CH_QGOH_PP_3:A4","text":"A finite mode must follow from God or an attribute insofar as it is modified by a finite and determinately existing modification.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[]},{"id":"CH_QGOH_PP_3:A5","text":"Every finite and determinate cause is caused by another finite and determinate cause, and this concatenation proceeds to infinity.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[]},{"id":"CH_QGOH_PP_3:A6","text":"Apart from substance and modes, nothing is given, and modes are affections of the attributes of God.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[]},{"id":"CH_QGOH_PP_3:A7","text":"All things are in God and so depend upon him that without him they can neither be nor be conceived.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[]},{"id":"CH_QGOH_PP_3:A8","text":"God necessarily exists, and the modes of the divine nature necessarily follow and are caused by God, both insofar as they exist and insofar as they act.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[]},{"id":"CH_QGOH_PP_3:A9","text":"Natura Naturans is God considered as a free cause insofar as the attributes expressing eternal and infinite essence are considered; Natura Naturata is the complex of necessarily following modes.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[]},{"id":"CH_QGOH_PP_3:A10","text":"A true idea must agree with its ideate, and in Nature no substance besides God nor affections besides the affections given in God are found.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[]},{"id":"CH_QGOH_PP_3:A11","text":"Actual intellect, whether finite or infinite, and the other affections of thinking are modes, not absolute thought.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[]},{"id":"CH_QGOH_PP_3:A12","text":"Will, whether conceived as finite or infinite, requires a cause by which it is determined to exist and to act.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[]},{"id":"CH_QGOH_PP_3:A13","text":"Things cannot be produced in another way or in another order, because another order would require another divine nature and consequently several gods.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[]},{"id":"CH_QGOH_PP_3:A14","text":"Necessity follows from the essence of a thing or from an efficient cause; impossibility, from an essential contradiction or the absence of an external cause; contingency and possibility, from a defect of knowledge.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[]},{"id":"CH_QGOH_PP_3:A15","text":"The power of God is the very essence of God, and whatever is conceived to be in divine power necessarily is.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[]},{"id":"CH_QGOH_PP_3:A16","text":"Everything existing expresses divine power in a determinate manner, and some effect must follow from the nature of each existing thing.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[]},{"id":"CH_QGOH_PP_3:A17","text":"Ignorance of causes, joined with consciousness of appetite, disposes people to consider themselves free and natural things as means ordered to human ends.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[]},{"id":"CH_QGOH_PP_3:A18","text":"Mathematics considers the essences and properties of figures, not ends, and presents a norm of truth distinct from finalism.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[]}],"original_name":"Load-Bearing Axioms"},{"number":"3","name":"Apophatic Structure","items":[{"id":"CH_QGOH_PP_3:NX1","text":"A finite mode is not an immediate effect of the absolute nature of an attribute or of an eternal and infinite modification.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[]},{"id":"CH_QGOH_PP_3:NX2","text":"No finite thing is an independent substance or a being constituted outside divine affections.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[]},{"id":"CH_QGOH_PP_3:NX3","text":"A mode determined by God can neither determine itself nor render itself undetermined.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[]},{"id":"CH_QGOH_PP_3:NX4","text":"God is not a remote cause of things, because no effect is causally and conceptually separated from God.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[]},{"id":"CH_QGOH_PP_3:NX5","text":"Potential intellect is not assumed as a faculty distinct from actual intellection.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[]},{"id":"CH_QGOH_PP_3:NX6","text":"Intellect, will, desire, and love are not referred to Natura Naturans.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[]},{"id":"CH_QGOH_PP_3:NX7","text":"Will is not a free cause, but a necessary or compelled cause.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[]},{"id":"CH_QGOH_PP_3:NX8","text":"Neither an alternative order of things nor another nature of things is possible.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[]},{"id":"CH_QGOH_PP_3:NX9","text":"Contingency and possibility are not ontological determinations of things.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[]},{"id":"CH_QGOH_PP_3:NX10","text":"God does not act from the arbitrium of will, nor is Nature governed by a human end or external good.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[]}],"original_name":"Apophatic Structure"},{"number":"4","name":"Derivative Propositions","items":[{"id":"CH_QGOH_PP_3:D1","text":"Every finite thing is determined to exist and to act through an infinite series of finite causes.","refs":["CH_QGOH_PP_3:A1","CH_QGOH_PP_3:A2","CH_QGOH_PP_3:A3","CH_QGOH_PP_3:A4","CH_QGOH_PP_3:A5"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"From","passages":[]},{"id":"CH_QGOH_PP_3:D2","text":"God is the absolutely proximate cause of things immediately produced by him, but not a remote cause, because divine effects can neither be nor be conceived without God.","refs":["CH_QGOH_PP_3:A6","CH_QGOH_PP_3:A7","CH_QGOH_PP_3:A8"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"From","passages":[]},{"id":"CH_QGOH_PP_3:D3","text":"Divine determination encompasses not only the existence of modes, but also their determinate manner of acting.","refs":["CH_QGOH_PP_3:A1","CH_QGOH_PP_3:A8"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"From","passages":[]},{"id":"CH_QGOH_PP_3:D4","text":"Natura Naturans designates the infinite causal principle; Natura Naturata, the modal order necessarily dependent upon it.","refs":["CH_QGOH_PP_3:A7","CH_QGOH_PP_3:A8","CH_QGOH_PP_3:A9"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"From","passages":[]},{"id":"CH_QGOH_PP_3:D5","text":"Actual intellect, finite or infinite, comprehends nothing except the attributes of God and their affections.","refs":["CH_QGOH_PP_3:A7","CH_QGOH_PP_3:A10","CH_QGOH_PP_3:A11"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"From","passages":[]},{"id":"CH_QGOH_PP_3:D6","text":"Will, even if posited as infinite, cannot possess causally original freedom.","refs":["CH_QGOH_PP_3:A5","CH_QGOH_PP_3:A11","CH_QGOH_PP_3:A12"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"From","passages":[]},{"id":"CH_QGOH_PP_3:D7","text":"Will and intellect are situated in the same ontological order as motion, rest, and other natural things.","refs":["CH_QGOH_PP_3:A11","CH_QGOH_PP_3:A12"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"From","passages":[]},{"id":"CH_QGOH_PP_3:D8","text":"Contingent and possible are epistemic names for a thing whose order of causes is unknown, not real modes of the thing itself.","refs":["CH_QGOH_PP_3:A8","CH_QGOH_PP_3:A14"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"From","passages":[]},{"id":"CH_QGOH_PP_3:D9","text":"Whatever is conceived to be in the power of God necessarily is, because divine power is not distinguished from divine essence.","refs":["CH_QGOH_PP_3:A15"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"From","passages":[]},{"id":"CH_QGOH_PP_3:D10","text":"No being is causally idle, because every existing thing constitutes a determinate expression of divine power and a source of an effect.","refs":["CH_QGOH_PP_3:A15","CH_QGOH_PP_3:A16"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"From","passages":[]},{"id":"CH_QGOH_PP_3:D11","text":"The actual order of things is not a choice among equally possible orders, but the unique expression of the divine nature.","refs":["CH_QGOH_PP_3:A8","CH_QGOH_PP_3:A13","CH_QGOH_PP_3:A15"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"From","passages":[]},{"id":"CH_QGOH_PP_3:D12","text":"The final interpretation of Nature arises from the projection of human appetites onto things whose causes are unknown, and is transformed into superstition concerning rulers of Nature.","refs":["CH_QGOH_PP_3:A17"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"From","passages":[]},{"id":"CH_QGOH_PP_3:D13","text":"Mathematics displays a faculty of knowing that seeks truth through essences and properties, not through uses and ends.","refs":["CH_QGOH_PP_3:A18"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"From","passages":[]}],"original_name":"Derivative Propositions"},{"number":"5","name":"Super-Derivatives","items":[{"id":"CH_QGOH_PP_3:SD1","text":"The system establishes immanent and closed causality: every cause, every effect, and every intelligibility remain within God and the modes of God.","refs":["CH_QGOH_PP_3:A4","CH_QGOH_PP_3:A5","CH_QGOH_PP_3:A6","CH_QGOH_PP_3:A7","CH_QGOH_PP_3:A8"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"From","passages":[]},{"id":"CH_QGOH_PP_3:SD2","text":"Divine freedom is transferred from the faculty of choosing among possibilities to the necessity by which God acts from his own essence.","refs":["CH_QGOH_PP_3:A9","CH_QGOH_PP_3:A12","CH_QGOH_PP_3:A13","CH_QGOH_PP_3:A15"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"From","passages":[]},{"id":"CH_QGOH_PP_3:SD3","text":"The necessity of things is ontological, whereas contingency is a name for the limitation of human knowledge; ignorance therefore does not indicate the indeterminacy of a thing.","refs":["CH_QGOH_PP_3:A8","CH_QGOH_PP_3:A14"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"From","passages":[]},{"id":"CH_QGOH_PP_3:SD4","text":"Mental acts are deprived of causal privilege, because intellect and will are not principles above nature, but produced affections of nature.","refs":["CH_QGOH_PP_3:A9","CH_QGOH_PP_3:A11","CH_QGOH_PP_3:A12","CH_QGOH_PP_3:A16"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"From","passages":[]},{"id":"CH_QGOH_PP_3:SD5","text":"The affirmation of perfection does not designate an order preferred beforehand by God, but the necessity by which the divine nature expresses itself without defect and without alternative.","refs":["CH_QGOH_PP_3:A8","CH_QGOH_PP_3:A13","CH_QGOH_PP_3:A15"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"From","passages":[]},{"id":"CH_QGOH_PP_3:SD6","text":"Finalism is not a rival causal explanation, but a secondary effect of the human condition that takes its own ends for the structure of Nature.","refs":["CH_QGOH_PP_3:A17","CH_QGOH_PP_3:A18"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"From","passages":[]}],"original_name":"Super-Derivatives"},{"number":"6","name":"Deep Apophatics","items":[{"id":"CH_QGOH_PP_3:DNX1","text":"No first or self-originating finite cause is admitted, because every finite thing is contained within the regress of finite causes.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[]},{"id":"CH_QGOH_PP_3:DNX2","text":"A real separation between a transcendent Creator and the created order is denied, because all things are in God and without him can neither be nor be conceived.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[]},{"id":"CH_QGOH_PP_3:DNX3","text":"An autonomous voluntary subject is tacitly excluded, because every volition is determined by another cause.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[]},{"id":"CH_QGOH_PP_3:DNX4","text":"An alternative possible world is excluded, because another order of things would require another divine nature.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[]},{"id":"CH_QGOH_PP_3:DNX5","text":"A purely passive or causally sterile being is excluded, because some effect must follow from whatever exists.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[]},{"id":"CH_QGOH_PP_3:DNX6","text":"End, good, and human utility are excluded as explanatory principles of Nature, because they arise from ignorance of causes and the projection of appetite.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[]}],"original_name":"Deep Apophatics"},{"number":"7","name":"Super-Apophatics","items":[{"id":"CH_QGOH_PP_3:SNX1","text":"The possibility of a God deliberating before the order of things and choosing among several orders is foreclosed to the system.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[]},{"id":"CH_QGOH_PP_3:SNX2","text":"No norm of truth, causality, or goodness can be established outside God, because apart from divine substance and its modes nothing is given.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[]},{"id":"CH_QGOH_PP_3:SNX3","text":"Nature cannot be admitted as an apparatus prepared for human use, because such an image transfers a human end into the structure of things.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[]},{"id":"CH_QGOH_PP_3:SNX4","text":"Ontic contingency as an inexplicable remainder or rupture of the causal order is foreclosed.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[]}],"original_name":"Super-Apophatics"},{"number":"8","name":"Formal Generative Schema","items":[{"id":"CH_QGOH_PP_3:FGS1","text":"Absolute nature of an attribute → infinite and eternal effect; finite mode → exclusion of immediate production from the absolute.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[]},{"id":"CH_QGOH_PP_3:FGS2","text":"Exclusion of the absolute origin of finite modes → mediation through a finite modification → regress of finite causes to infinity.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[]},{"id":"CH_QGOH_PP_3:FGS3","text":"Regress of modal causes + nonexistence of things outside God → universal dependence → necessity of existing and acting.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[]},{"id":"CH_QGOH_PP_3:FGS4","text":"Universal necessity → distinction between Natura Naturans and Natura Naturata → relocation of intellect, will, and affects among the modes.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[]},{"id":"CH_QGOH_PP_3:FGS5","text":"Will as a caused mode + exclusion of another order → rejection of free will and of an indifferent divine decree.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[]},{"id":"CH_QGOH_PP_3:FGS6","text":"Divine essence = divine power → possible in divine power = necessary → every existing thing = active expression of power.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[]},{"id":"CH_QGOH_PP_3:FGS7","text":"Ignorance of causes + consciousness of appetite + interpretation of utility → opinion of freedom → finalism → rulers of Nature, superstition, and anthropomorphic judgments.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[]}],"original_name":"Formal Generative Schema"},{"number":"9","name":"Argument Flow Map","items":[{"id":"CH_QGOH_PP_3:AF1","text":"The demonstrative movement proceeds from finite determination to finite modal mediation, then to the regress of causes and universal necessity.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[]},{"id":"CH_QGOH_PP_3:AF2","text":"Universal necessity → Natura Naturans / Natura Naturata → intellect and will as modes → rejection of voluntary freedom.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[]},{"id":"CH_QGOH_PP_3:AF3","text":"Uniqueness of the divine nature → exclusion of another order → identity of power and essence → necessity of all possibilities.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[]},{"id":"CH_QGOH_PP_3:AF4","text":"Necessary causality of Nature → genealogy of human ignorance → constitution of finalism → indication of non-final mathematical knowledge.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[]}],"original_name":"Argument Flow Map"},{"number":"10","name":"Map-Effects","items":[{"id":"CH_QGOH_PP_3:ME1","text":"The finite singular thing is reclassified as a node in an infinite series of modal determinations, not as a causally self-governing being.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[]},{"id":"CH_QGOH_PP_3:ME2","text":"Contingent and possible are transferred from the ontology of things to the epistemology of defective knowledge.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[]},{"id":"CH_QGOH_PP_3:ME3","text":"Intellect, will, and affects are transferred from the order of governing principles to the order of caused natural modes.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[]},{"id":"CH_QGOH_PP_3:ME4","text":"God's causality is described as immanent and proximate, not as the remote action of an artificer positioned outside his effects.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[]},{"id":"CH_QGOH_PP_3:ME5","text":"The opposition between necessity and perfection is dissolved, because perfection no longer designates the best option but the necessary expression of the divine nature.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[]},{"id":"CH_QGOH_PP_3:ME6","text":"The final explanation of natural things is reclassified as an anthropological projection arising from ignorance of causes, not as a description of the order of Nature.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[]}],"original_name":"Map-Effects"},{"number":"11","name":"Altitude Levels","items":[{"id":"CH_QGOH_PP_3:ALT1","text":"Surface — The text affirms that nothing contingent is given, that will is not free, that things could not have been produced otherwise, that the power of God is his essence, and that every existing thing produces an effect.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[]},{"id":"CH_QGOH_PP_3:ALT2","text":"Structural — The order of things is constituted by an infinite chain of finite modes, causally and conceptually comprehended within divine substance.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[]},{"id":"CH_QGOH_PP_3:ALT3","text":"Metastrategic — The argument transfers the terms freedom, possibility, perfection, and finality from their theological-voluntarist use into an analysis of necessity and ignorance.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[]},{"id":"CH_QGOH_PP_3:ALT4","text":"Ontological/Temporal — Divine eternity admits no before or after of decrees, whereas the temporal appearance of change is referred to the series of determinate modes.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[]}],"original_name":"Altitude Levels"},{"number":"12","name":"Minimal Reconstruction Test","items":[{"id":"CH_QGOH_PP_3:MRT1","text":"The axioms concerning the infinite absolute effect, finite mediation, and regress of causes suffice for reconstructing the thesis of universal necessity.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[]},{"id":"CH_QGOH_PP_3:MRT2","text":"The strongest hinge of the system is the nexus between divine immanence, modal determination, and the denial of ontic contingency.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[]},{"id":"CH_QGOH_PP_3:MRT3","text":"The distinction between Natura Naturans and Natura Naturata is necessary lest God be conceived as a will numbered among modes, or modes as independent substances.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[]},{"id":"CH_QGOH_PP_3:MRT4","text":"The identification of power with the essence of God establishes the transition from divine causality to the necessity of all that is conceived in that power.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[]},{"id":"CH_QGOH_PP_3:MRT5","text":"A fragile juncture remains in the term freedom: God is called a free cause, while will is necessary or compelled; the distinction depends on a definition of freedom that is here only presupposed.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[]},{"id":"CH_QGOH_PP_3:MRT6","text":"The transition from the necessity of order to the affirmation of highest perfection requires a notion of perfection not defined through choice, external good, or comparison of degrees.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[]},{"id":"CH_QGOH_PP_3:MRT7","text":"The appendix sufficiently establishes the genealogy of finalism through ignorance of causes and conscious appetite, but still promises here the derivation of individual judgments concerning good, evil, merit, and order.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[]},{"id":"CH_QGOH_PP_3:MRT8","text":"The reconstruction preserves the movement of the text, because it proceeds from the causality of the finite to total necessity, from this to the demotion of will, and finally to the critique of finalism.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[]}],"original_name":"Minimal Reconstruction Test"}],"revision":"CH_QGOH_PP_3@R001","source_variant_id":"canonical:CH_QGOH_PP_3:v1","source_language_code":"la"}],"identity":{"layer":"CH","kicker":"Chapter 03","title":"Part One - Concerning God · passage 3","context":"Chapter 03","original_title":"Pars prima - De Deo · passage 3","original_context":"Chapter 03"},"original_title":"Pars prima - De Deo · passage 3","title_language_code":"en"},{"id":"CH_QGOH_PP_4","layer":"CH","title":"Part One - Concerning God · passage 4","order":4,"parents":["RAW_QGOH_PP_4"],"revision":"CH_QGOH_PP_4@R001","sha256":"167f528fde881a3f2b6183cee7f4817d505a993d1d4d8d7b731d2c564a01242a","sections":[{"number":"1","name":"Argumental Spine","items":[{"id":"CH_QGOH_PP_4:AS1","text":"Textus quaerit quo fundamento doctrina finium naturalium et vocabula vulgaria, quibus Natura imperfecta appellatur, sustineri possint.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_QGOH_PP_4:P0012","trace_type":"Composite","confidence":"medium","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"},{"id":"RAW_QGOH_PP_4:P0013","trace_type":"Composite","confidence":"medium","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_QGOH_PP_4:AS2","text":"Textus statuit Naturam nullo fine agere, sed omnia ex necessitate aeterna et perfectione naturae divinae consequi.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_QGOH_PP_4:P0001","trace_type":"Composite","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"},{"id":"RAW_QGOH_PP_4:P0012","trace_type":"Composite","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"},{"id":"RAW_QGOH_PP_4:P0013","trace_type":"Composite","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_QGOH_PP_4:AS3","text":"Argumentatio polemico-diagnostica finem transformat ex causa explicante in effectum imaginationis humanae, utilitatem propriam quaerentis.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_QGOH_PP_4:P0001","trace_type":"Structural","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"},{"id":"RAW_QGOH_PP_4:P0003","trace_type":"Structural","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"},{"id":"RAW_QGOH_PP_4:P0006","trace_type":"Structural","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_QGOH_PP_4:AS4","text":"Causalitas finalis dissolvitur, quia ordinem causalem invertit, Deum indigentem facit, et regressum interrogationum in ignorantiam concludit.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_QGOH_PP_4:P0001","trace_type":"Composite","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"},{"id":"RAW_QGOH_PP_4:P0002","trace_type":"Composite","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"},{"id":"RAW_QGOH_PP_4:P0004","trace_type":"Composite","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_QGOH_PP_4:AS5","text":"Notiones boni, mali, ordinis, confusionis, pulchritudinis et deformitatis reducuntur ad affectiones imaginativas corporum humanorum.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_QGOH_PP_4:P0006","trace_type":"Structural","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"},{"id":"RAW_QGOH_PP_4:P0007","trace_type":"Structural","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"},{"id":"RAW_QGOH_PP_4:P0008","trace_type":"Structural","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"},{"id":"RAW_QGOH_PP_4:P0009","trace_type":"Structural","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_QGOH_PP_4:AS6","text":"Perfectionis aestimatio ab effectu in sensus humanos ad naturam et potentiam singularium rerum restituitur.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_QGOH_PP_4:P0012","trace_type":"Composite","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"},{"id":"RAW_QGOH_PP_4:P0013","trace_type":"Composite","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]}],"original_name":"Argumental Spine"},{"number":"2","name":"Load-Bearing Axioms","items":[{"id":"CH_QGOH_PP_4:A1","text":"Omnia naturae ex necessitate aeterna naturae divinae consequuntur.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_QGOH_PP_4:P0001","trace_type":"Direct","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"},{"id":"RAW_QGOH_PP_4:P0012","trace_type":"Direct","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_QGOH_PP_4:A2","text":"Natura nullum finem sibi praefigit.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_QGOH_PP_4:P0001","trace_type":"Direct","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_QGOH_PP_4:A3","text":"Causa efficiens natura prior est effectu quem producit.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_QGOH_PP_4:P0001","trace_type":"Composite","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"},{"id":"RAW_QGOH_PP_4:P0002","trace_type":"Composite","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_QGOH_PP_4:A4","text":"Effectus a Deo immediate productus perfectior est effectu qui pluribus causis intermediis indiget.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_QGOH_PP_4:P0002","trace_type":"Direct","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_QGOH_PP_4:A5","text":"Agens qui propter finem agit id appetit quo caret.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_QGOH_PP_4:P0002","trace_type":"Composite","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"},{"id":"RAW_QGOH_PP_4:P0003","trace_type":"Composite","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_QGOH_PP_4:A6","text":"Deus nullo bono, obiecto vel perfectione extra se indiget.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_QGOH_PP_4:P0002","trace_type":"Composite","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"},{"id":"RAW_QGOH_PP_4:P0003","trace_type":"Composite","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_QGOH_PP_4:A7","text":"Ignorantia causarum efficientium non constituit probationem causae finalis.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_QGOH_PP_4:P0003","trace_type":"Composite","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"},{"id":"RAW_QGOH_PP_4:P0004","trace_type":"Composite","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"},{"id":"RAW_QGOH_PP_4:P0005","trace_type":"Composite","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_QGOH_PP_4:A8","text":"Homines res bonas vel malas iudicant prout res eorum valetudini, usui vel affectionibus conducunt aut repugnant.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_QGOH_PP_4:P0006","trace_type":"Composite","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"},{"id":"RAW_QGOH_PP_4:P0009","trace_type":"Composite","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"},{"id":"RAW_QGOH_PP_4:P0010","trace_type":"Composite","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_QGOH_PP_4:A9","text":"Imaginatio rerum dispositiones secundum facilitatem repraesentandi et recordandi ordinat.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_QGOH_PP_4:P0007","trace_type":"Direct","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"},{"id":"RAW_QGOH_PP_4:P0008","trace_type":"Direct","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_QGOH_PP_4:A10","text":"Dispositiones imaginativae diversorum corporum et cerebrorum inter homines discrepant.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_QGOH_PP_4:P0010","trace_type":"Direct","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"},{"id":"RAW_QGOH_PP_4:P0011","trace_type":"Direct","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_QGOH_PP_4:A11","text":"Perfectio rei ex sola eius natura et potentia aestimanda est.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_QGOH_PP_4:P0013","trace_type":"Direct","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_QGOH_PP_4:A12","text":"Leges Naturae ad omnia producenda quae ab intellectu infinito concipi possunt sufficiunt.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_QGOH_PP_4:P0013","trace_type":"Direct","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]}],"original_name":"Load-Bearing Axioms"},{"number":"3","name":"Apophatic Structure","items":[{"id":"CH_QGOH_PP_4:NX1","text":"Natura non est agens deliberans quod media ad finem externum disponit.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_QGOH_PP_4:P0001","trace_type":"Negative","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"},{"id":"RAW_QGOH_PP_4:P0006","trace_type":"Negative","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_QGOH_PP_4:NX2","text":"Deus non agit ad obtinendum quod nondum possidet.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_QGOH_PP_4:P0002","trace_type":"Negative","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"},{"id":"RAW_QGOH_PP_4:P0003","trace_type":"Negative","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_QGOH_PP_4:NX3","text":"Causa finalis non est principium explicativum aequale causae efficienti.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_QGOH_PP_4:P0001","trace_type":"Negative","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"},{"id":"RAW_QGOH_PP_4:P0003","trace_type":"Negative","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_QGOH_PP_4:NX4","text":"Concursus circumstantiarum ignotus non est casus qui voluntatem divinam postulat.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_QGOH_PP_4:P0003","trace_type":"Negative","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"},{"id":"RAW_QGOH_PP_4:P0004","trace_type":"Negative","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_QGOH_PP_4:NX5","text":"Admiratio corporis humani non demonstrat structuram eius esse supernaturalem vel non-mechanicam.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_QGOH_PP_4:P0005","trace_type":"Negative","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_QGOH_PP_4:NX6","text":"Ordo non est attributum Naturae extra relationem ad facilitatem humanae imaginationis.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_QGOH_PP_4:P0007","trace_type":"Negative","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"},{"id":"RAW_QGOH_PP_4:P0008","trace_type":"Negative","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_QGOH_PP_4:NX7","text":"Bonum, malum, pulchritudo et deformitas non indicant naturas intrinsecas rerum.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_QGOH_PP_4:P0009","trace_type":"Negative","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"},{"id":"RAW_QGOH_PP_4:P0012","trace_type":"Negative","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_QGOH_PP_4:NX8","text":"Imperfectio rerum non sequitur ex eo quod res sensum humanum offendunt aut utilitati humanae repugnant.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_QGOH_PP_4:P0013","trace_type":"Negative","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_QGOH_PP_4:NX9","text":"Vocabula imaginationis non sunt entia realia quae argumentis contra necessitatem Naturae adhiberi possunt.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_QGOH_PP_4:P0012","trace_type":"Negative","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]}],"original_name":"Apophatic Structure"},{"number":"4","name":"Derivative Propositions","items":[{"id":"CH_QGOH_PP_4:D1","text":"Doctrina finium invertit relationem causae et effectus, cum effectum postulatum tamquam rationem causae antecedentis tractet.","refs":["CH_QGOH_PP_4:A2","CH_QGOH_PP_4:A3"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"From","passages":[{"id":"RAW_QGOH_PP_4:P0001","trace_type":"Direct","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_QGOH_PP_4:D2","text":"Si res immediate productae media ad finem divinum essent, effectus ultimi praeponerentur causis prioribus contra gradum perfectionis causaliter constitutum.","refs":["CH_QGOH_PP_4:A3","CH_QGOH_PP_4:A4","CH_QGOH_PP_4:A5"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"From","passages":[{"id":"RAW_QGOH_PP_4:P0001","trace_type":"Composite","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"},{"id":"RAW_QGOH_PP_4:P0002","trace_type":"Composite","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_QGOH_PP_4:D3","text":"Teleologia perfectionem divinam tollit, quia Deum desiderio carentis subicit.","refs":["CH_QGOH_PP_4:A5","CH_QGOH_PP_4:A6"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"From","passages":[{"id":"RAW_QGOH_PP_4:P0002","trace_type":"Composite","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"},{"id":"RAW_QGOH_PP_4:P0003","trace_type":"Composite","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_QGOH_PP_4:D4","text":"Regressus a causa ignota ad voluntatem Dei explicandi munus non perficit, sed ignorantiam nomine causae occultat.","refs":["CH_QGOH_PP_4:A3","CH_QGOH_PP_4:A7"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"From","passages":[{"id":"RAW_QGOH_PP_4:P0003","trace_type":"Direct","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"},{"id":"RAW_QGOH_PP_4:P0004","trace_type":"Direct","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_QGOH_PP_4:D5","text":"Notiones aestimativae et sensibiles indicant constitutionem imaginationis iudicantis, non naturam obiecti iudicati.","refs":["CH_QGOH_PP_4:A8","CH_QGOH_PP_4:A9"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"From","passages":[{"id":"RAW_QGOH_PP_4:P0006","trace_type":"Composite","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"},{"id":"RAW_QGOH_PP_4:P0009","trace_type":"Composite","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_QGOH_PP_4:D6","text":"Dissensio iudiciorum humanorum sequitur ex varietate dispositionum imaginativarum, non necessario ex varietate naturarum rerum.","refs":["CH_QGOH_PP_4:A9","CH_QGOH_PP_4:A10"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"From","passages":[{"id":"RAW_QGOH_PP_4:P0010","trace_type":"Direct","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"},{"id":"RAW_QGOH_PP_4:P0011","trace_type":"Direct","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_QGOH_PP_4:D7","text":"Quaestio de malis et imperfectionibus Naturae solvenda est per naturam et potentiam rerum, non per comparationem cum commodo humano.","refs":["CH_QGOH_PP_4:A8","CH_QGOH_PP_4:A11"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"From","passages":[{"id":"RAW_QGOH_PP_4:P0012","trace_type":"Composite","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"},{"id":"RAW_QGOH_PP_4:P0013","trace_type":"Composite","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]}],"original_name":"Derivative Propositions"},{"number":"5","name":"Super-Derivatives","items":[{"id":"CH_QGOH_PP_4:SD1","text":"Systema teleologicum simul producit theologicam imminutam perfectionem Dei et epistemologiam imminutam cognitionem causarum efficientium.","refs":["CH_QGOH_PP_4:A3","CH_QGOH_PP_4:A5","CH_QGOH_PP_4:A6","CH_QGOH_PP_4:A7"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"From","passages":[{"id":"RAW_QGOH_PP_4:P0002","trace_type":"Structural","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"},{"id":"RAW_QGOH_PP_4:P0003","trace_type":"Structural","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"},{"id":"RAW_QGOH_PP_4:P0004","trace_type":"Structural","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"},{"id":"RAW_QGOH_PP_4:P0005","trace_type":"Structural","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_QGOH_PP_4:SD2","text":"Humanus anthropocentrismus transformat affectiones locales corporis in iudicia universalia de constitutione Naturae.","refs":["CH_QGOH_PP_4:A8","CH_QGOH_PP_4:A9","CH_QGOH_PP_4:A10"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"From","passages":[{"id":"RAW_QGOH_PP_4:P0006","trace_type":"Structural","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"},{"id":"RAW_QGOH_PP_4:P0009","trace_type":"Structural","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"},{"id":"RAW_QGOH_PP_4:P0010","trace_type":"Structural","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_QGOH_PP_4:SD3","text":"Sublata causalitate finali, Natura apparet ut campus productionis necessariae cuius varietas perfectionum non requirit normam externam.","refs":["CH_QGOH_PP_4:A1","CH_QGOH_PP_4:A2","CH_QGOH_PP_4:A11","CH_QGOH_PP_4:A12"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"From","passages":[{"id":"RAW_QGOH_PP_4:P0001","trace_type":"Structural","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"},{"id":"RAW_QGOH_PP_4:P0012","trace_type":"Structural","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"},{"id":"RAW_QGOH_PP_4:P0013","trace_type":"Structural","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_QGOH_PP_4:SD4","text":"Stupor conservat auctoritatem interpretum tantum dum causae verae occultae manent; cognitio causarum medium illius auctoritatis evacuat.","refs":["CH_QGOH_PP_4:A7","CH_QGOH_PP_4:A9"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"From","passages":[{"id":"RAW_QGOH_PP_4:P0004","trace_type":"Composite","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"},{"id":"RAW_QGOH_PP_4:P0005","trace_type":"Composite","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]}],"original_name":"Super-Derivatives"},{"number":"6","name":"Deep Apophatics","items":[{"id":"CH_QGOH_PP_4:DNX1","text":"Falsum esse debet quod utilitas humana sit mensura ontologica perfectionis rerum.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_QGOH_PP_4:P0006","trace_type":"Negative","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"},{"id":"RAW_QGOH_PP_4:P0013","trace_type":"Negative","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_QGOH_PP_4:DNX2","text":"Falsum esse debet quod ignorantia causalitatis licentiam praebeat ad causam transcendentem affirmandam.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_QGOH_PP_4:P0003","trace_type":"Negative","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"},{"id":"RAW_QGOH_PP_4:P0004","trace_type":"Negative","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_QGOH_PP_4:DNX3","text":"Falsum esse debet quod cognitio Naturae ex admiratione vel horrore sensibiliter constituto oriatur.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_QGOH_PP_4:P0005","trace_type":"Negative","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"},{"id":"RAW_QGOH_PP_4:P0012","trace_type":"Negative","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_QGOH_PP_4:DNX4","text":"Falsum esse debet quod Deus sit artifex qui finem praeconceptum per instrumenta ordinat.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_QGOH_PP_4:P0001","trace_type":"Negative","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"},{"id":"RAW_QGOH_PP_4:P0002","trace_type":"Negative","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"},{"id":"RAW_QGOH_PP_4:P0008","trace_type":"Negative","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_QGOH_PP_4:DNX5","text":"Falsum esse debet quod vocabula valoris et ordinis, quia nomina habent, res sive proprietates rerum significent.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_QGOH_PP_4:P0007","trace_type":"Negative","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"},{"id":"RAW_QGOH_PP_4:P0012","trace_type":"Negative","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]}],"original_name":"Deep Apophatics"},{"number":"7","name":"Super-Apophatics","items":[{"id":"CH_QGOH_PP_4:SNX1","text":"Systema excludit omnem normam extrinsecam ex qua Natura deficiens, prava vel incompleta iudicari possit.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_QGOH_PP_4:P0012","trace_type":"Composite","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"},{"id":"RAW_QGOH_PP_4:P0013","trace_type":"Composite","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_QGOH_PP_4:SNX2","text":"Systema excludit mundum factum propter hominem, quia haec possibilitas necessitatem productionis naturalis in privilegium imaginarium convertit.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_QGOH_PP_4:P0006","trace_type":"Composite","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"},{"id":"RAW_QGOH_PP_4:P0008","trace_type":"Composite","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_QGOH_PP_4:SNX3","text":"Systema excludit transitum legitimam ab experientia subiectiva ad structuram metaphysicam rerum sine cognitione causarum.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_QGOH_PP_4:P0003","trace_type":"Structural","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"},{"id":"RAW_QGOH_PP_4:P0004","trace_type":"Structural","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"},{"id":"RAW_QGOH_PP_4:P0012","trace_type":"Structural","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]}],"original_name":"Super-Apophatics"},{"number":"8","name":"Formal Generative Schema","items":[{"id":"CH_QGOH_PP_4:FGS1","text":"Necessitas naturae divinae → productio rerum sine fine praestituto.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_QGOH_PP_4:P0001","trace_type":"Direct","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_QGOH_PP_4:FGS2","text":"Productio sine fine → exclusio Dei indigentis et exclusio inversionis causae-effectus.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_QGOH_PP_4:P0001","trace_type":"Composite","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"},{"id":"RAW_QGOH_PP_4:P0002","trace_type":"Composite","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_QGOH_PP_4:FGS3","text":"Ignorantia causarum efficientium + inquisitio finis → voluntas divina ut asylum ignorantiae.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_QGOH_PP_4:P0003","trace_type":"Direct","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"},{"id":"RAW_QGOH_PP_4:P0004","trace_type":"Direct","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_QGOH_PP_4:FGS4","text":"Utilitas humana + imaginatio affecta → vocabula aestimativa, ordinativa et moralia tamquam modi imaginandi.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_QGOH_PP_4:P0006","trace_type":"Structural","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"},{"id":"RAW_QGOH_PP_4:P0009","trace_type":"Structural","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"},{"id":"RAW_QGOH_PP_4:P0010","trace_type":"Structural","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_QGOH_PP_4:FGS5","text":"Reductio vocabulorum ad imaginationem + mensura perfectionis ex natura et potentia → dissolutio objectionis ex malo, deformitate et corruptione.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_QGOH_PP_4:P0012","trace_type":"Composite","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"},{"id":"RAW_QGOH_PP_4:P0013","trace_type":"Composite","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_QGOH_PP_4:FGS6","text":"Leges Naturae amplissimae → varietas graduum perfectionis sine defectu materiae, finis vel ordinis transcendentis.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_QGOH_PP_4:P0013","trace_type":"Direct","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]}],"original_name":"Formal Generative Schema"},{"number":"9","name":"Argument Flow Map","items":[{"id":"CH_QGOH_PP_4:AF1","text":"Praeiudicium finis naturalis → detectio originis humanae et utilitariae.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_QGOH_PP_4:P0001","trace_type":"Composite","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"},{"id":"RAW_QGOH_PP_4:P0006","trace_type":"Composite","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_QGOH_PP_4:AF2","text":"Finis suppositus → inversio causalitatis + imminutio perfectionis divinae.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_QGOH_PP_4:P0001","trace_type":"Composite","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"},{"id":"RAW_QGOH_PP_4:P0002","trace_type":"Composite","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_QGOH_PP_4:AF3","text":"Circumstantiae ignotae → regressus interrogationum → voluntas Dei ut nomen ignorantiae.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_QGOH_PP_4:P0003","trace_type":"Direct","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"},{"id":"RAW_QGOH_PP_4:P0004","trace_type":"Direct","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_QGOH_PP_4:AF4","text":"Utilitas et affectio corporis → bonum/malum, ordo/confusio, pulchritudo/deformitas.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_QGOH_PP_4:P0006","trace_type":"Composite","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"},{"id":"RAW_QGOH_PP_4:P0009","trace_type":"Composite","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_QGOH_PP_4:AF5","text":"Varietas imaginandi → controversia et scepticismus → distinctio imaginationis ab intellectu.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_QGOH_PP_4:P0010","trace_type":"Structural","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"},{"id":"RAW_QGOH_PP_4:P0011","trace_type":"Structural","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"},{"id":"RAW_QGOH_PP_4:P0012","trace_type":"Structural","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_QGOH_PP_4:AF6","text":"Distinctio imaginationis ab intellectu → perfectio ex natura et potentia → defensio necessitatis Naturae.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_QGOH_PP_4:P0012","trace_type":"Composite","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"},{"id":"RAW_QGOH_PP_4:P0013","trace_type":"Composite","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]}],"original_name":"Argument Flow Map"},{"number":"10","name":"Map-Effects","items":[{"id":"CH_QGOH_PP_4:ME1","text":"Causa finalis reclassificatur ab elemento Naturae ad figmentum humanum ex ignorantia et utilitate ortum.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_QGOH_PP_4:P0001","trace_type":"Composite","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"},{"id":"RAW_QGOH_PP_4:P0003","trace_type":"Composite","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_QGOH_PP_4:ME2","text":"Deus reclassificatur ab artifice finem quaerente ad naturam infinitam cuius productio ex necessitate sequitur.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_QGOH_PP_4:P0001","trace_type":"Composite","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"},{"id":"RAW_QGOH_PP_4:P0013","trace_type":"Composite","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_QGOH_PP_4:ME3","text":"Malum, deformitas, confusio et corruptio reclassificantur ab imperfectionibus ontologicis ad relationes imaginativas vel utilitarias.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_QGOH_PP_4:P0009","trace_type":"Structural","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"},{"id":"RAW_QGOH_PP_4:P0012","trace_type":"Structural","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"},{"id":"RAW_QGOH_PP_4:P0013","trace_type":"Structural","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_QGOH_PP_4:ME4","text":"Ordo reclassificatur ab architectura obiectiva rerum ad modum quo res imaginationi facile repraesentantur et memoriae traduntur.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_QGOH_PP_4:P0007","trace_type":"Direct","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"},{"id":"RAW_QGOH_PP_4:P0008","trace_type":"Direct","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_QGOH_PP_4:ME5","text":"Scepticismus reclassificatur ab effectu necessario cognitionis ad effectum iudiciorum fundatorum in dispositionibus imaginativis discrepantibus.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_QGOH_PP_4:P0010","trace_type":"Direct","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"},{"id":"RAW_QGOH_PP_4:P0011","trace_type":"Direct","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]}],"original_name":"Map-Effects"},{"number":"11","name":"Altitude Levels","items":[{"id":"CH_QGOH_PP_4:ALT1","text":"Surface — Textus negat Naturam propter finem agere et negat imperfectiones vulgo nominatas contra perfectionem Naturae valere.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_QGOH_PP_4:P0001","trace_type":"Structural","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"},{"id":"RAW_QGOH_PP_4:P0012","trace_type":"Structural","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"},{"id":"RAW_QGOH_PP_4:P0013","trace_type":"Structural","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_QGOH_PP_4:ALT2","text":"Structural — Praeiudicium teleologicum generatur per concatenationem ignorantiae causarum, quaestus utilitatis et imaginationis corpore affectae.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_QGOH_PP_4:P0003","trace_type":"Structural","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"},{"id":"RAW_QGOH_PP_4:P0006","trace_type":"Structural","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"},{"id":"RAW_QGOH_PP_4:P0009","trace_type":"Structural","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"},{"id":"RAW_QGOH_PP_4:P0010","trace_type":"Structural","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_QGOH_PP_4:ALT3","text":"Metastrategic — Argumentatio adversaria a quaestione “cur hoc factum est?” ad quaestionem de causis efficientibus et de conditionibus iudicii transfertur.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_QGOH_PP_4:P0003","trace_type":"Structural","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"},{"id":"RAW_QGOH_PP_4:P0004","trace_type":"Structural","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"},{"id":"RAW_QGOH_PP_4:P0012","trace_type":"Structural","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_QGOH_PP_4:ALT4","text":"Ontological/Temporal — Natura constituitur ut ordo necessarius in quo prioritas causalis et gradus perfectionis non a finibus futuris, sed a potentia producente dependent.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_QGOH_PP_4:P0001","trace_type":"Structural","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"},{"id":"RAW_QGOH_PP_4:P0002","trace_type":"Structural","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"},{"id":"RAW_QGOH_PP_4:P0013","trace_type":"Structural","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]}],"original_name":"Altitude Levels"},{"number":"12","name":"Minimal Reconstruction Test","items":[{"id":"CH_QGOH_PP_4:MRT1","text":"Axiomata de necessitate Naturae, exclusionis finis et prioritate causae efficientis sufficiunt ad nucleum critique teleologiae restituendum.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_QGOH_PP_4:P0001","trace_type":"Composite","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"},{"id":"RAW_QGOH_PP_4:P0002","trace_type":"Composite","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_QGOH_PP_4:MRT2","text":"Axiomata de utilitate, imaginatione et varietate corporum sufficiunt ad generationem notionum aestimativarum et controversiarum explicandam.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_QGOH_PP_4:P0006","trace_type":"Composite","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"},{"id":"RAW_QGOH_PP_4:P0009","trace_type":"Composite","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"},{"id":"RAW_QGOH_PP_4:P0010","trace_type":"Composite","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_QGOH_PP_4:MRT3","text":"Nexus inter doctrinam metaphysicam necessitatis et doctrinam psychologicam imaginationis manet validus, sed transitum a dispositione corporali ad formas historicas auctoritatis tantum breviter determinat.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_QGOH_PP_4:P0009","trace_type":"Structural","confidence":"medium","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"},{"id":"RAW_QGOH_PP_4:P0010","trace_type":"Structural","confidence":"medium","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"},{"id":"RAW_QGOH_PP_4:P0012","trace_type":"Structural","confidence":"medium","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_QGOH_PP_4:MRT4","text":"Firmissimum systematis punctum est reductio causae finalis ad contradictionem causalitatis inversae, indigentiae divinae et ignorantiae explicativae.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_QGOH_PP_4:P0001","trace_type":"Structural","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"},{"id":"RAW_QGOH_PP_4:P0002","trace_type":"Structural","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"},{"id":"RAW_QGOH_PP_4:P0004","trace_type":"Structural","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_QGOH_PP_4:MRT5","text":"Commissura fragilis manet in reductione ordinis, pulchritudinis et valoris ad imaginationem, quia textus distinctionem inter relationem imaginativam et structuram obiectivam singulorum casuum non plene formalizat.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_QGOH_PP_4:P0007","trace_type":"Tension","confidence":"medium","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"},{"id":"RAW_QGOH_PP_4:P0008","trace_type":"Tension","confidence":"medium","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"},{"id":"RAW_QGOH_PP_4:P0009","trace_type":"Tension","confidence":"medium","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"},{"id":"RAW_QGOH_PP_4:P0013","trace_type":"Tension","confidence":"medium","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_QGOH_PP_4:MRT6","text":"Restitutio motum textus servat, si necessitas Naturae, critica teleologiae, genealogia notionum vulgarium et mensura perfectionis ex natura rei in eadem catena tenentur.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_QGOH_PP_4:P0001","trace_type":"Structural","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"},{"id":"RAW_QGOH_PP_4:P0006","trace_type":"Structural","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"},{"id":"RAW_QGOH_PP_4:P0010","trace_type":"Structural","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"},{"id":"RAW_QGOH_PP_4:P0013","trace_type":"Structural","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]}],"original_name":"Minimal Reconstruction Test"}],"language_code":"la","language_name":"Latina","translations":[{"id":"CAREVAR_20261015T014556072108Z_f61a2b5c","language_code":"en","language_name":"English","title":"Part One - On God · passage 4","source_sha256":"167f528fde881a3f2b6183cee7f4817d505a993d1d4d8d7b731d2c564a01242a","sha256":"293d469d1e94691e2317c56a1c49ca9c2b6460a459bf5cfd7bc0caba36069465","rendition_type":"machine","review_status":"machine","model":"gpt-5.6-terra","created":"2026-10-15T01:45:56.073053+00:00","sections":[{"number":"1","name":"Argumentative Spine","items":[{"id":"CH_QGOH_PP_4:AS1","text":"The text asks on what foundation the doctrine of natural ends and the common terms by which Nature is called imperfect can be sustained.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_QGOH_PP_4:P0012","trace_type":"Composite","confidence":"medium","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"},{"id":"RAW_QGOH_PP_4:P0013","trace_type":"Composite","confidence":"medium","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_QGOH_PP_4:AS2","text":"The text establishes that Nature acts for no end, but that all things follow from the eternal necessity and perfection of the divine nature.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_QGOH_PP_4:P0001","trace_type":"Composite","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"},{"id":"RAW_QGOH_PP_4:P0012","trace_type":"Composite","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"},{"id":"RAW_QGOH_PP_4:P0013","trace_type":"Composite","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_QGOH_PP_4:AS3","text":"The polemical-diagnostic argument transforms end from an explaining cause into an effect of the human imagination seeking its own advantage.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_QGOH_PP_4:P0001","trace_type":"Structural","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"},{"id":"RAW_QGOH_PP_4:P0003","trace_type":"Structural","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"},{"id":"RAW_QGOH_PP_4:P0006","trace_type":"Structural","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_QGOH_PP_4:AS4","text":"Final causality is dissolved because it inverts the causal order, makes God needy, and concludes a regress of questions in ignorance.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_QGOH_PP_4:P0001","trace_type":"Composite","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"},{"id":"RAW_QGOH_PP_4:P0002","trace_type":"Composite","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"},{"id":"RAW_QGOH_PP_4:P0004","trace_type":"Composite","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_QGOH_PP_4:AS5","text":"The notions of good, evil, order, confusion, beauty, and deformity are reduced to imaginative affections of human bodies.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_QGOH_PP_4:P0006","trace_type":"Structural","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"},{"id":"RAW_QGOH_PP_4:P0007","trace_type":"Structural","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"},{"id":"RAW_QGOH_PP_4:P0008","trace_type":"Structural","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"},{"id":"RAW_QGOH_PP_4:P0009","trace_type":"Structural","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_QGOH_PP_4:AS6","text":"The assessment of perfection is restored from its effect upon human senses to the nature and power of singular things.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_QGOH_PP_4:P0012","trace_type":"Composite","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"},{"id":"RAW_QGOH_PP_4:P0013","trace_type":"Composite","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]}]},{"number":"2","name":"Load-Bearing Axioms","items":[{"id":"CH_QGOH_PP_4:A1","text":"All things in nature follow from the eternal necessity of the divine nature.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_QGOH_PP_4:P0001","trace_type":"Direct","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"},{"id":"RAW_QGOH_PP_4:P0012","trace_type":"Direct","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_QGOH_PP_4:A2","text":"Nature sets before itself no end.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_QGOH_PP_4:P0001","trace_type":"Direct","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_QGOH_PP_4:A3","text":"An efficient cause is by nature prior to the effect that it produces.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_QGOH_PP_4:P0001","trace_type":"Composite","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"},{"id":"RAW_QGOH_PP_4:P0002","trace_type":"Composite","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_QGOH_PP_4:A4","text":"An effect immediately produced by God is more perfect than an effect that requires several intermediate causes.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_QGOH_PP_4:P0002","trace_type":"Direct","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_QGOH_PP_4:A5","text":"An agent that acts for an end desires that which it lacks.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_QGOH_PP_4:P0002","trace_type":"Composite","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"},{"id":"RAW_QGOH_PP_4:P0003","trace_type":"Composite","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_QGOH_PP_4:A6","text":"God needs no good, object, or perfection outside himself.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_QGOH_PP_4:P0002","trace_type":"Composite","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"},{"id":"RAW_QGOH_PP_4:P0003","trace_type":"Composite","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_QGOH_PP_4:A7","text":"Ignorance of efficient causes does not constitute proof of a final cause.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_QGOH_PP_4:P0003","trace_type":"Composite","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"},{"id":"RAW_QGOH_PP_4:P0004","trace_type":"Composite","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"},{"id":"RAW_QGOH_PP_4:P0005","trace_type":"Composite","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_QGOH_PP_4:A8","text":"Human beings judge things good or evil insofar as things conduce or are opposed to their health, use, or affections.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_QGOH_PP_4:P0006","trace_type":"Composite","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"},{"id":"RAW_QGOH_PP_4:P0009","trace_type":"Composite","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"},{"id":"RAW_QGOH_PP_4:P0010","trace_type":"Composite","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_QGOH_PP_4:A9","text":"Imagination orders the dispositions of things according to ease of representing and remembering them.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_QGOH_PP_4:P0007","trace_type":"Direct","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"},{"id":"RAW_QGOH_PP_4:P0008","trace_type":"Direct","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_QGOH_PP_4:A10","text":"The imaginative dispositions of different bodies and brains differ among human beings.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_QGOH_PP_4:P0010","trace_type":"Direct","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"},{"id":"RAW_QGOH_PP_4:P0011","trace_type":"Direct","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_QGOH_PP_4:A11","text":"The perfection of a thing is to be assessed from its nature and power alone.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_QGOH_PP_4:P0013","trace_type":"Direct","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_QGOH_PP_4:A12","text":"The laws of Nature suffice to produce all things that can be conceived by an infinite intellect.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_QGOH_PP_4:P0013","trace_type":"Direct","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]}],"original_name":"Load-Bearing Axioms"},{"number":"3","name":"Apophatic Structure","items":[{"id":"CH_QGOH_PP_4:NX1","text":"Nature is not a deliberating agent that disposes means toward an external end.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_QGOH_PP_4:P0001","trace_type":"Negative","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"},{"id":"RAW_QGOH_PP_4:P0006","trace_type":"Negative","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_QGOH_PP_4:NX2","text":"God does not act in order to obtain what he does not yet possess.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_QGOH_PP_4:P0002","trace_type":"Negative","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"},{"id":"RAW_QGOH_PP_4:P0003","trace_type":"Negative","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_QGOH_PP_4:NX3","text":"A final cause is not an explanatory principle equal to an efficient cause.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_QGOH_PP_4:P0001","trace_type":"Negative","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"},{"id":"RAW_QGOH_PP_4:P0003","trace_type":"Negative","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_QGOH_PP_4:NX4","text":"An unknown concurrence of circumstances is not chance that requires a divine will.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_QGOH_PP_4:P0003","trace_type":"Negative","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"},{"id":"RAW_QGOH_PP_4:P0004","trace_type":"Negative","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_QGOH_PP_4:NX5","text":"Wonder at the human body does not demonstrate that its structure is supernatural or non-mechanical.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_QGOH_PP_4:P0005","trace_type":"Negative","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_QGOH_PP_4:NX6","text":"Order is not an attribute of Nature apart from its relation to the ease of human imagination.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_QGOH_PP_4:P0007","trace_type":"Negative","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"},{"id":"RAW_QGOH_PP_4:P0008","trace_type":"Negative","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_QGOH_PP_4:NX7","text":"Good, evil, beauty, and deformity do not indicate intrinsic natures of things.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_QGOH_PP_4:P0009","trace_type":"Negative","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"},{"id":"RAW_QGOH_PP_4:P0012","trace_type":"Negative","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_QGOH_PP_4:NX8","text":"The imperfection of things does not follow from the fact that things offend human sense or conflict with human utility.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_QGOH_PP_4:P0013","trace_type":"Negative","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_QGOH_PP_4:NX9","text":"The terms of imagination are not real entities that can be employed as arguments against the necessity of Nature.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_QGOH_PP_4:P0012","trace_type":"Negative","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]}],"original_name":"Apophatic Structure"},{"number":"4","name":"Derivative Propositions","items":[{"id":"CH_QGOH_PP_4:D1","text":"The doctrine of ends inverts the relation of cause and effect, insofar as it treats the postulated effect as the reason for the antecedent cause.","refs":["CH_QGOH_PP_4:A2","CH_QGOH_PP_4:A3"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"From","passages":[{"id":"RAW_QGOH_PP_4:P0001","trace_type":"Direct","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_QGOH_PP_4:D2","text":"If immediately produced things were means to a divine end, the final effects would be preferred to prior causes, contrary to the causally established degree of perfection.","refs":["CH_QGOH_PP_4:A3","CH_QGOH_PP_4:A4","CH_QGOH_PP_4:A5"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"From","passages":[{"id":"RAW_QGOH_PP_4:P0001","trace_type":"Composite","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"},{"id":"RAW_QGOH_PP_4:P0002","trace_type":"Composite","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_QGOH_PP_4:D3","text":"Teleology removes divine perfection because it subjects God to the desire of one who lacks.","refs":["CH_QGOH_PP_4:A5","CH_QGOH_PP_4:A6"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"From","passages":[{"id":"RAW_QGOH_PP_4:P0002","trace_type":"Composite","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"},{"id":"RAW_QGOH_PP_4:P0003","trace_type":"Composite","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_QGOH_PP_4:D4","text":"The regress from an unknown cause to the will of God does not fulfill the explanatory function, but conceals ignorance under the name of cause.","refs":["CH_QGOH_PP_4:A3","CH_QGOH_PP_4:A7"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"From","passages":[{"id":"RAW_QGOH_PP_4:P0003","trace_type":"Direct","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"},{"id":"RAW_QGOH_PP_4:P0004","trace_type":"Direct","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_QGOH_PP_4:D5","text":"Evaluative and sensible notions indicate the constitution of the judging imagination, not the nature of the judged object.","refs":["CH_QGOH_PP_4:A8","CH_QGOH_PP_4:A9"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"From","passages":[{"id":"RAW_QGOH_PP_4:P0006","trace_type":"Composite","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"},{"id":"RAW_QGOH_PP_4:P0009","trace_type":"Composite","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_QGOH_PP_4:D6","text":"The disagreement of human judgments follows from the variety of imaginative dispositions, not necessarily from the variety of the natures of things.","refs":["CH_QGOH_PP_4:A9","CH_QGOH_PP_4:A10"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"From","passages":[{"id":"RAW_QGOH_PP_4:P0010","trace_type":"Direct","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"},{"id":"RAW_QGOH_PP_4:P0011","trace_type":"Direct","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_QGOH_PP_4:D7","text":"The question of the evils and imperfections of Nature must be resolved through the nature and power of things, not through comparison with human convenience.","refs":["CH_QGOH_PP_4:A8","CH_QGOH_PP_4:A11"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"From","passages":[{"id":"RAW_QGOH_PP_4:P0012","trace_type":"Composite","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"},{"id":"RAW_QGOH_PP_4:P0013","trace_type":"Composite","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]}],"original_name":"Derivative Propositions"},{"number":"5","name":"Super-Derivatives","items":[{"id":"CH_QGOH_PP_4:SD1","text":"The teleological system simultaneously produces a theological diminution of God's perfection and an epistemological diminution of knowledge of efficient causes.","refs":["CH_QGOH_PP_4:A3","CH_QGOH_PP_4:A5","CH_QGOH_PP_4:A6","CH_QGOH_PP_4:A7"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"From","passages":[{"id":"RAW_QGOH_PP_4:P0002","trace_type":"Structural","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"},{"id":"RAW_QGOH_PP_4:P0003","trace_type":"Structural","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"},{"id":"RAW_QGOH_PP_4:P0004","trace_type":"Structural","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"},{"id":"RAW_QGOH_PP_4:P0005","trace_type":"Structural","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_QGOH_PP_4:SD2","text":"Human anthropocentrism transforms local affections of the body into universal judgments about the constitution of Nature.","refs":["CH_QGOH_PP_4:A8","CH_QGOH_PP_4:A9","CH_QGOH_PP_4:A10"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"From","passages":[{"id":"RAW_QGOH_PP_4:P0006","trace_type":"Structural","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"},{"id":"RAW_QGOH_PP_4:P0009","trace_type":"Structural","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"},{"id":"RAW_QGOH_PP_4:P0010","trace_type":"Structural","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_QGOH_PP_4:SD3","text":"With final causality removed, Nature appears as a field of necessary production whose variety of perfections requires no external norm.","refs":["CH_QGOH_PP_4:A1","CH_QGOH_PP_4:A2","CH_QGOH_PP_4:A11","CH_QGOH_PP_4:A12"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"From","passages":[{"id":"RAW_QGOH_PP_4:P0001","trace_type":"Structural","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"},{"id":"RAW_QGOH_PP_4:P0012","trace_type":"Structural","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"},{"id":"RAW_QGOH_PP_4:P0013","trace_type":"Structural","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_QGOH_PP_4:SD4","text":"Wonder preserves the authority of interpreters only so long as true causes remain hidden; knowledge of causes voids the means of that authority.","refs":["CH_QGOH_PP_4:A7","CH_QGOH_PP_4:A9"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"From","passages":[{"id":"RAW_QGOH_PP_4:P0004","trace_type":"Composite","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"},{"id":"RAW_QGOH_PP_4:P0005","trace_type":"Composite","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]}],"original_name":"Super-Derivatives"},{"number":"6","name":"Deep Apophatics","items":[{"id":"CH_QGOH_PP_4:DNX1","text":"It must be false that human utility is the ontological measure of the perfection of things.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_QGOH_PP_4:P0006","trace_type":"Negative","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"},{"id":"RAW_QGOH_PP_4:P0013","trace_type":"Negative","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_QGOH_PP_4:DNX2","text":"It must be false that ignorance of causality grants license to affirm a transcendent cause.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_QGOH_PP_4:P0003","trace_type":"Negative","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"},{"id":"RAW_QGOH_PP_4:P0004","trace_type":"Negative","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_QGOH_PP_4:DNX3","text":"It must be false that knowledge of Nature arises from sensibly constituted admiration or horror.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_QGOH_PP_4:P0005","trace_type":"Negative","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"},{"id":"RAW_QGOH_PP_4:P0012","trace_type":"Negative","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_QGOH_PP_4:DNX4","text":"It must be false that God is an artisan who orders a preconceived end through instruments.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_QGOH_PP_4:P0001","trace_type":"Negative","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"},{"id":"RAW_QGOH_PP_4:P0002","trace_type":"Negative","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"},{"id":"RAW_QGOH_PP_4:P0008","trace_type":"Negative","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_QGOH_PP_4:DNX5","text":"It must be false that terms of value and order, because they have names, signify things or properties of things.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_QGOH_PP_4:P0007","trace_type":"Negative","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"},{"id":"RAW_QGOH_PP_4:P0012","trace_type":"Negative","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]}],"original_name":"Deep Apophatics"},{"number":"7","name":"Super-Apophatics","items":[{"id":"CH_QGOH_PP_4:SNX1","text":"The system excludes every extrinsic norm by which Nature could be judged deficient, depraved, or incomplete.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_QGOH_PP_4:P0012","trace_type":"Composite","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"},{"id":"RAW_QGOH_PP_4:P0013","trace_type":"Composite","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_QGOH_PP_4:SNX2","text":"The system excludes a world made for humankind, because this possibility converts the necessity of natural production into an imaginary privilege.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_QGOH_PP_4:P0006","trace_type":"Composite","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"},{"id":"RAW_QGOH_PP_4:P0008","trace_type":"Composite","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_QGOH_PP_4:SNX3","text":"The system excludes a legitimate transition from subjective experience to the metaphysical structure of things without knowledge of causes.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_QGOH_PP_4:P0003","trace_type":"Structural","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"},{"id":"RAW_QGOH_PP_4:P0004","trace_type":"Structural","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"},{"id":"RAW_QGOH_PP_4:P0012","trace_type":"Structural","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]}],"original_name":"Super-Apophatics"},{"number":"8","name":"Formal Generative Schema","items":[{"id":"CH_QGOH_PP_4:FGS1","text":"Necessity of the divine nature → production of things without a pre-established end.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_QGOH_PP_4:P0001","trace_type":"Direct","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_QGOH_PP_4:FGS2","text":"Production without an end → exclusion of a needy God and exclusion of the inversion of cause and effect.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_QGOH_PP_4:P0001","trace_type":"Composite","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"},{"id":"RAW_QGOH_PP_4:P0002","trace_type":"Composite","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_QGOH_PP_4:FGS3","text":"Ignorance of efficient causes + inquiry into end → divine will as an asylum of ignorance.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_QGOH_PP_4:P0003","trace_type":"Direct","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"},{"id":"RAW_QGOH_PP_4:P0004","trace_type":"Direct","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_QGOH_PP_4:FGS4","text":"Human utility + affected imagination → evaluative, ordering, and moral terms as modes of imagining.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_QGOH_PP_4:P0006","trace_type":"Structural","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"},{"id":"RAW_QGOH_PP_4:P0009","trace_type":"Structural","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"},{"id":"RAW_QGOH_PP_4:P0010","trace_type":"Structural","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_QGOH_PP_4:FGS5","text":"Reduction of terms to imagination + measure of perfection from nature and power → dissolution of the objection from evil, deformity, and corruption.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_QGOH_PP_4:P0012","trace_type":"Composite","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"},{"id":"RAW_QGOH_PP_4:P0013","trace_type":"Composite","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_QGOH_PP_4:FGS6","text":"Most extensive laws of Nature → variety of degrees of perfection without defect of matter, end, or transcendent order.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_QGOH_PP_4:P0013","trace_type":"Direct","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]}],"original_name":"Formal Generative Schema"},{"number":"9","name":"Argument Flow Map","items":[{"id":"CH_QGOH_PP_4:AF1","text":"Prejudice of natural end → detection of its human and utilitarian origin.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_QGOH_PP_4:P0001","trace_type":"Composite","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"},{"id":"RAW_QGOH_PP_4:P0006","trace_type":"Composite","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_QGOH_PP_4:AF2","text":"Supposed end → inversion of causality + diminution of divine perfection.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_QGOH_PP_4:P0001","trace_type":"Composite","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"},{"id":"RAW_QGOH_PP_4:P0002","trace_type":"Composite","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_QGOH_PP_4:AF3","text":"Unknown circumstances → regress of questions → will of God as the name of ignorance.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_QGOH_PP_4:P0003","trace_type":"Direct","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"},{"id":"RAW_QGOH_PP_4:P0004","trace_type":"Direct","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_QGOH_PP_4:AF4","text":"Utility and affection of the body → good/evil, order/confusion, beauty/deformity.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_QGOH_PP_4:P0006","trace_type":"Composite","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"},{"id":"RAW_QGOH_PP_4:P0009","trace_type":"Composite","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_QGOH_PP_4:AF5","text":"Variety of imagining → controversy and skepticism → distinction of imagination from intellect.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_QGOH_PP_4:P0010","trace_type":"Structural","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"},{"id":"RAW_QGOH_PP_4:P0011","trace_type":"Structural","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"},{"id":"RAW_QGOH_PP_4:P0012","trace_type":"Structural","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_QGOH_PP_4:AF6","text":"Distinction of imagination from intellect → perfection from nature and power → defense of the necessity of Nature.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_QGOH_PP_4:P0012","trace_type":"Composite","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"},{"id":"RAW_QGOH_PP_4:P0013","trace_type":"Composite","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]}],"original_name":"Argument Flow Map"},{"number":"10","name":"Map-Effects","items":[{"id":"CH_QGOH_PP_4:ME1","text":"Final cause is reclassified from an element of Nature to a human fiction arising from ignorance and utility.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_QGOH_PP_4:P0001","trace_type":"Composite","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"},{"id":"RAW_QGOH_PP_4:P0003","trace_type":"Composite","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_QGOH_PP_4:ME2","text":"God is reclassified from an artisan seeking an end to infinite nature whose production follows from necessity.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_QGOH_PP_4:P0001","trace_type":"Composite","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"},{"id":"RAW_QGOH_PP_4:P0013","trace_type":"Composite","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_QGOH_PP_4:ME3","text":"Evil, deformity, confusion, and corruption are reclassified from ontological imperfections to imaginative or utilitarian relations.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_QGOH_PP_4:P0009","trace_type":"Structural","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"},{"id":"RAW_QGOH_PP_4:P0012","trace_type":"Structural","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"},{"id":"RAW_QGOH_PP_4:P0013","trace_type":"Structural","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_QGOH_PP_4:ME4","text":"Order is reclassified from an objective architecture of things to the mode in which things are easily represented to imagination and committed to memory.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_QGOH_PP_4:P0007","trace_type":"Direct","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"},{"id":"RAW_QGOH_PP_4:P0008","trace_type":"Direct","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_QGOH_PP_4:ME5","text":"Skepticism is reclassified from a necessary effect of knowledge to an effect of judgments founded on discrepant imaginative dispositions.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_QGOH_PP_4:P0010","trace_type":"Direct","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"},{"id":"RAW_QGOH_PP_4:P0011","trace_type":"Direct","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]}],"original_name":"Map-Effects"},{"number":"11","name":"Altitude Levels","items":[{"id":"CH_QGOH_PP_4:ALT1","text":"Surface — The text denies that Nature acts for an end and denies that commonly named imperfections count against Nature's perfection.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_QGOH_PP_4:P0001","trace_type":"Structural","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"},{"id":"RAW_QGOH_PP_4:P0012","trace_type":"Structural","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"},{"id":"RAW_QGOH_PP_4:P0013","trace_type":"Structural","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_QGOH_PP_4:ALT2","text":"Structural — Teleological prejudice is generated through the concatenation of ignorance of causes, pursuit of utility, and bodily affected imagination.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_QGOH_PP_4:P0003","trace_type":"Structural","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"},{"id":"RAW_QGOH_PP_4:P0006","trace_type":"Structural","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"},{"id":"RAW_QGOH_PP_4:P0009","trace_type":"Structural","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"},{"id":"RAW_QGOH_PP_4:P0010","trace_type":"Structural","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_QGOH_PP_4:ALT3","text":"Metastrategic — The adversarial argument is shifted from the question “why was this made?” to the question of efficient causes and of the conditions of judgment.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_QGOH_PP_4:P0003","trace_type":"Structural","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"},{"id":"RAW_QGOH_PP_4:P0004","trace_type":"Structural","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"},{"id":"RAW_QGOH_PP_4:P0012","trace_type":"Structural","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_QGOH_PP_4:ALT4","text":"Ontological/Temporal — Nature is constituted as a necessary order in which causal priority and degrees of perfection depend not on future ends, but on productive power.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_QGOH_PP_4:P0001","trace_type":"Structural","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"},{"id":"RAW_QGOH_PP_4:P0002","trace_type":"Structural","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"},{"id":"RAW_QGOH_PP_4:P0013","trace_type":"Structural","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]}],"original_name":"Altitude Levels"},{"number":"12","name":"Minimal Reconstruction Test","items":[{"id":"CH_QGOH_PP_4:MRT1","text":"The axioms concerning the necessity of Nature, the exclusion of end, and the priority of the efficient cause suffice to reconstruct the core of the critique of teleology.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_QGOH_PP_4:P0001","trace_type":"Composite","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"},{"id":"RAW_QGOH_PP_4:P0002","trace_type":"Composite","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_QGOH_PP_4:MRT2","text":"The axioms concerning utility, imagination, and the variety of bodies suffice to explain the generation of evaluative notions and controversies.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_QGOH_PP_4:P0006","trace_type":"Composite","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"},{"id":"RAW_QGOH_PP_4:P0009","trace_type":"Composite","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"},{"id":"RAW_QGOH_PP_4:P0010","trace_type":"Composite","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_QGOH_PP_4:MRT3","text":"The connection between the metaphysical doctrine of necessity and the psychological doctrine of imagination remains valid, but determines only briefly the transition from bodily disposition to historical forms of authority.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_QGOH_PP_4:P0009","trace_type":"Structural","confidence":"medium","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"},{"id":"RAW_QGOH_PP_4:P0010","trace_type":"Structural","confidence":"medium","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"},{"id":"RAW_QGOH_PP_4:P0012","trace_type":"Structural","confidence":"medium","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_QGOH_PP_4:MRT4","text":"The strongest point of the system is the reduction of final cause to the contradiction of inverted causality, divine need, and explanatory ignorance.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_QGOH_PP_4:P0001","trace_type":"Structural","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"},{"id":"RAW_QGOH_PP_4:P0002","trace_type":"Structural","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"},{"id":"RAW_QGOH_PP_4:P0004","trace_type":"Structural","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_QGOH_PP_4:MRT5","text":"A fragile joint remains in the reduction of order, beauty, and value to imagination, because the text does not fully formalize the distinction between imaginative relation and the objective structure of individual cases.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_QGOH_PP_4:P0007","trace_type":"Tension","confidence":"medium","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"},{"id":"RAW_QGOH_PP_4:P0008","trace_type":"Tension","confidence":"medium","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"},{"id":"RAW_QGOH_PP_4:P0009","trace_type":"Tension","confidence":"medium","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"},{"id":"RAW_QGOH_PP_4:P0013","trace_type":"Tension","confidence":"medium","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_QGOH_PP_4:MRT6","text":"The reconstruction preserves the movement of the text if the necessity of Nature, the critique of teleology, the genealogy of common notions, and the measure of perfection from the nature of the thing are held in the same chain.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_QGOH_PP_4:P0001","trace_type":"Structural","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"},{"id":"RAW_QGOH_PP_4:P0006","trace_type":"Structural","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"},{"id":"RAW_QGOH_PP_4:P0010","trace_type":"Structural","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"},{"id":"RAW_QGOH_PP_4:P0013","trace_type":"Structural","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]}],"original_name":"Minimal Reconstruction Test"}],"revision":"CH_QGOH_PP_4@R001","source_variant_id":"canonical:CH_QGOH_PP_4:v1","source_language_code":"la"}],"identity":{"layer":"CH","kicker":"Chapter 04","title":"Part One - Concerning God · passage 4","context":"Chapter 04","original_title":"Pars prima - De Deo · passage 4","original_context":"Chapter 04"},"original_title":"Pars prima - De Deo · passage 4","title_language_code":"en"},{"id":"CH_QGOH_PSDN","layer":"CH","title":"Part Two - On the Nature and Origin of the Mind · passage 1","order":5,"parents":["RAW_QGOH_PSDN"],"revision":"CH_QGOH_PSDN@R001","sha256":"e44839f7c1df11bc8d1d89a907acdf97e31539b3a941d6e4f06a592fe53fd605","sections":[{"number":"1","name":"Argumental Spine","items":[{"id":"CH_QGOH_PSDN:AS1","text":"Textus quaerit quo ordine Mens humana ex necessitate divinae naturae intelligenda sit, ut via ad cognitionem mentis et beatitudinis aperiatur.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_QGOH_PSDN:P0002","trace_type":"Direct","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_QGOH_PSDN:AS2","text":"Thesis constitutiva est Mentem humanam non substantiam esse, sed ideam rei singularis actu existentis, cuius obiectum Corpus humanum est.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_QGOH_PSDN:P0095","trace_type":"Composite","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"},{"id":"RAW_QGOH_PSDN:P0109","trace_type":"Composite","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_QGOH_PSDN:AS3","text":"Argumentatio est metaphysico-deductiva et reconstructiva: a necessitate Dei et attributorum ad seriem modorum, inde ad structuram Mentis humanae procedit.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_QGOH_PSDN:P0002","trace_type":"Structural","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"},{"id":"RAW_QGOH_PSDN:P0035","trace_type":"Structural","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"},{"id":"RAW_QGOH_PSDN:P0095","trace_type":"Structural","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"},{"id":"RAW_QGOH_PSDN:P0109","trace_type":"Structural","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_QGOH_PSDN:AS4","text":"Causalitas intra attributum et identitas ordinis idearum cum ordine rerum impediunt ne Mens et Corpus ut res separatae aut causaliter transversae concipiantur.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_QGOH_PSDN:P0050","trace_type":"Composite","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"},{"id":"RAW_QGOH_PSDN:P0056","trace_type":"Composite","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"},{"id":"RAW_QGOH_PSDN:P0062","trace_type":"Composite","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"},{"id":"RAW_QGOH_PSDN:P0063","trace_type":"Composite","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"},{"id":"RAW_QGOH_PSDN:P0064","trace_type":"Composite","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_QGOH_PSDN:AS5","text":"Demonstratio Mentis in cognitionem Corporis transfertur, quia unionis Mentis et Corporis ratio atque graduum mentis differentia ex natura Corporis dependere ponuntur.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_QGOH_PSDN:P0115","trace_type":"Structural","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"},{"id":"RAW_QGOH_PSDN:P0116","trace_type":"Structural","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"},{"id":"RAW_QGOH_PSDN:P0117","trace_type":"Structural","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]}],"original_name":"Argumental Spine"},{"number":"2","name":"Load-Bearing Axioms","items":[{"id":"CH_QGOH_PSDN:A1","text":"Essentiam rei constituit id quo dato res necessario ponitur, quo sublato necessario tollitur, atque quod sine re nec esse nec concipi potest et vice versa.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_QGOH_PSDN:P0005","trace_type":"Direct","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_QGOH_PSDN:A2","text":"Res singularis est res finita existentiam determinatam habens.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_QGOH_PSDN:P0016","trace_type":"Direct","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_QGOH_PSDN:A3","text":"Nihil sine Deo esse neque concipi potest.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_QGOH_PSDN:P0091","trace_type":"Direct","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_QGOH_PSDN:A4","text":"Deus est unica causa tam essentiae quam existentiae rerum.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_QGOH_PSDN:P0091","trace_type":"Direct","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_QGOH_PSDN:A5","text":"Potentia Dei est eius essentia actuosa, ex qua Deus eadem necessitate agit qua se intelligit.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_QGOH_PSDN:P0039","trace_type":"Direct","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_QGOH_PSDN:A6","text":"Cogitatio est attributum Dei quod eius essentiam aeternam et infinitam exprimit.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_QGOH_PSDN:P0027","trace_type":"Direct","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_QGOH_PSDN:A7","text":"Extensio est attributum Dei quod eius essentiam aeternam et infinitam exprimit.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_QGOH_PSDN:P0031","trace_type":"Composite","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"},{"id":"RAW_QGOH_PSDN:P0033","trace_type":"Composite","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_QGOH_PSDN:A8","text":"Substantia cogitans et substantia extensa una eademque substantia sunt, sub diversis attributis concepta.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_QGOH_PSDN:P0062","trace_type":"Direct","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_QGOH_PSDN:A9","text":"Modus cuiusque attributi Deum causam habet quatenus sub illo solo attributo consideratur.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_QGOH_PSDN:P0050","trace_type":"Direct","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_QGOH_PSDN:A10","text":"Esse formale idearum Deum causam habet quatenus res cogitans est.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_QGOH_PSDN:P0046","trace_type":"Direct","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_QGOH_PSDN:A11","text":"Ordo et connexio idearum idem est ac ordo et connexio rerum.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_QGOH_PSDN:P0056","trace_type":"Direct","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_QGOH_PSDN:A12","text":"Idea rei singularis non existentis non est nisi quatenus in infinita Dei idea comprehenditur.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_QGOH_PSDN:P0066","trace_type":"Composite","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"},{"id":"RAW_QGOH_PSDN:P0068","trace_type":"Composite","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_QGOH_PSDN:A13","text":"Essentia hominis necessariam existentiam non involvit, nec substantia formam hominis constituit.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_QGOH_PSDN:P0018","trace_type":"Composite","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"},{"id":"RAW_QGOH_PSDN:P0083","trace_type":"Composite","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_QGOH_PSDN:A14","text":"Homo cogitat.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_QGOH_PSDN:P0019","trace_type":"Direct","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_QGOH_PSDN:A15","text":"Idea in eodem individuo natura prior est reliquis modis cogitandi.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_QGOH_PSDN:P0020","trace_type":"Composite","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"},{"id":"RAW_QGOH_PSDN:P0097","trace_type":"Composite","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_QGOH_PSDN:A16","text":"Homo Corpus multis modis affici sentit.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_QGOH_PSDN:P0021","trace_type":"Direct","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_QGOH_PSDN:A17","text":"Homo nullas res singulares praeter corpora et cogitandi modos sentit aut percipit.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_QGOH_PSDN:P0022","trace_type":"Direct","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_QGOH_PSDN:A18","text":"Corpora ratione motus et quietis distinguuntur, et determinatio cuiusque corporis ad motum vel quietem ab aliis corporibus in infinitum pendet.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_QGOH_PSDN:P0123","trace_type":"Composite","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"},{"id":"RAW_QGOH_PSDN:P0131","trace_type":"Composite","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"},{"id":"RAW_QGOH_PSDN:P0133","trace_type":"Composite","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]}],"original_name":"Load-Bearing Axioms"},{"number":"3","name":"Apophatic Structure","items":[{"id":"CH_QGOH_PSDN:NX1","text":"Potentia Dei non est libera voluntas regia neque ius contingenter destruendi aut conservandi res.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_QGOH_PSDN:P0039","trace_type":"Negative","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"},{"id":"RAW_QGOH_PSDN:P0040","trace_type":"Negative","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_QGOH_PSDN:NX2","text":"Idea non ab ideato, neque a modo alterius attributi, causam efficientem recipit.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_QGOH_PSDN:P0046","trace_type":"Negative","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"},{"id":"RAW_QGOH_PSDN:P0050","trace_type":"Negative","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"},{"id":"RAW_QGOH_PSDN:P0054","trace_type":"Negative","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_QGOH_PSDN:NX3","text":"Cogitatio et Extensio non sunt duae substantiae realiter separatae.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_QGOH_PSDN:P0062","trace_type":"Negative","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"},{"id":"RAW_QGOH_PSDN:P0063","trace_type":"Negative","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_QGOH_PSDN:NX4","text":"Homo non est substantia nec ex sua essentia necessario existit.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_QGOH_PSDN:P0018","trace_type":"Negative","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"},{"id":"RAW_QGOH_PSDN:P0083","trace_type":"Negative","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"},{"id":"RAW_QGOH_PSDN:P0085","trace_type":"Negative","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_QGOH_PSDN:NX5","text":"Deus, quamvis sine ipso res singulares nec sint nec concipiantur, ad essentiam earum secundum definitionem reciprocitatis non pertinet.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_QGOH_PSDN:P0091","trace_type":"Tension","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"},{"id":"RAW_QGOH_PSDN:P0093","trace_type":"Tension","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_QGOH_PSDN:NX6","text":"Primum esse actuale Mentis humanae non est idea rei non existentis neque rei infinitae.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_QGOH_PSDN:P0097","trace_type":"Negative","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_QGOH_PSDN:NX7","text":"Obiectum Mentis humanae non est aliquid praeter Corpus actu existens.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_QGOH_PSDN:P0109","trace_type":"Negative","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"},{"id":"RAW_QGOH_PSDN:P0111","trace_type":"Negative","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_QGOH_PSDN:NX8","text":"Adaequatio ideae non per convenientiam externam cum ideato definitur, sed per proprietates intrinsecas ideae verae.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_QGOH_PSDN:P0009","trace_type":"Negative","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"},{"id":"RAW_QGOH_PSDN:P0011","trace_type":"Negative","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]}],"original_name":"Apophatic Structure"},{"number":"4","name":"Derivative Propositions","items":[{"id":"CH_QGOH_PSDN:D1","text":"Deus necessario ideam suae essentiae format et omnium quae ex ea necessario sequuntur.","refs":["CH_QGOH_PSDN:A5","CH_QGOH_PSDN:A6"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"From","passages":[{"id":"RAW_QGOH_PSDN:P0035","trace_type":"Direct","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"},{"id":"RAW_QGOH_PSDN:P0037","trace_type":"Direct","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_QGOH_PSDN:D2","text":"Idea Dei ex qua infinita infinitis modis sequuntur unica est.","refs":["CH_QGOH_PSDN:A8","CH_QGOH_PSDN:D1"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"From","passages":[{"id":"RAW_QGOH_PSDN:P0042","trace_type":"Direct","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"},{"id":"RAW_QGOH_PSDN:P0044","trace_type":"Direct","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_QGOH_PSDN:D3","text":"Ideae formaliter intra attributum Cogitationis producuntur, sicut modi cuiusque alterius attributi intra proprium attributum producuntur.","refs":["CH_QGOH_PSDN:A9","CH_QGOH_PSDN:A10"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"From","passages":[{"id":"RAW_QGOH_PSDN:P0046","trace_type":"Composite","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"},{"id":"RAW_QGOH_PSDN:P0050","trace_type":"Composite","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_QGOH_PSDN:D4","text":"Modus Extensionis et idea eius non sunt res duae, sed una res per Extensionalem et Cogitativam expressionem intellecta.","refs":["CH_QGOH_PSDN:A8","CH_QGOH_PSDN:A11"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"From","passages":[{"id":"RAW_QGOH_PSDN:P0062","trace_type":"Direct","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"},{"id":"RAW_QGOH_PSDN:P0063","trace_type":"Direct","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_QGOH_PSDN:D5","text":"Essentia hominis est certa modificatio attributorum Dei, non ens per se subsistens.","refs":["CH_QGOH_PSDN:A3","CH_QGOH_PSDN:A4","CH_QGOH_PSDN:A13"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"From","passages":[{"id":"RAW_QGOH_PSDN:P0089","trace_type":"Composite","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"},{"id":"RAW_QGOH_PSDN:P0091","trace_type":"Composite","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_QGOH_PSDN:D6","text":"Primum quod actuale Mentis humanae esse constituit est idea rei singularis actu existentis.","refs":["CH_QGOH_PSDN:A2","CH_QGOH_PSDN:A12","CH_QGOH_PSDN:A13","CH_QGOH_PSDN:A14","CH_QGOH_PSDN:A15"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"From","passages":[{"id":"RAW_QGOH_PSDN:P0095","trace_type":"Direct","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"},{"id":"RAW_QGOH_PSDN:P0097","trace_type":"Direct","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_QGOH_PSDN:D7","text":"Mens humana pars est infiniti intellectus Dei et rem inadæquate percipit, cum idea eius simul ad seriem idearum aliarum rerum pertinet.","refs":["CH_QGOH_PSDN:D6","CH_QGOH_PSDN:A3","CH_QGOH_PSDN:A11"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"From","passages":[{"id":"RAW_QGOH_PSDN:P0099","trace_type":"Direct","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_QGOH_PSDN:D8","text":"Obiectum ideae Mentem humanam constituentis est Corpus actu existens et nihil aliud.","refs":["CH_QGOH_PSDN:D6","CH_QGOH_PSDN:A16","CH_QGOH_PSDN:A17"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"From","passages":[{"id":"RAW_QGOH_PSDN:P0109","trace_type":"Direct","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"},{"id":"RAW_QGOH_PSDN:P0111","trace_type":"Direct","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_QGOH_PSDN:D9","text":"Quicquid in Corpore, quod Mentis humanae obiectum est, contingit, a Mente humana necessario percipitur.","refs":["CH_QGOH_PSDN:D8","CH_QGOH_PSDN:A10","CH_QGOH_PSDN:A11"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"From","passages":[{"id":"RAW_QGOH_PSDN:P0103","trace_type":"Direct","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"},{"id":"RAW_QGOH_PSDN:P0105","trace_type":"Direct","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_QGOH_PSDN:D10","text":"Homo constat Mente et Corpore, atque Corpus humanum existit prout sentitur.","refs":["CH_QGOH_PSDN:D8","CH_QGOH_PSDN:D9"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"From","passages":[{"id":"RAW_QGOH_PSDN:P0113","trace_type":"Direct","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_QGOH_PSDN:D11","text":"Aptitudo Mentis ad plura simul percipiendum proportionatur aptitudini Corporis ad plura simul agendum vel patiendum; distinctior intellectio crescit quo actiones Corporis magis ex ipso solo dependent.","refs":["CH_QGOH_PSDN:D8","CH_QGOH_PSDN:A18"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"From","passages":[{"id":"RAW_QGOH_PSDN:P0116","trace_type":"Direct","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]}],"original_name":"Derivative Propositions"},{"number":"5","name":"Super-Derivatives","items":[{"id":"CH_QGOH_PSDN:SD1","text":"Problema commercii causalis inter Mentem et Corpus dissolvitur: causalitas transversalis inter attributa excluditur, dum identitas rei sub duplici expressione retinetur.","refs":["CH_QGOH_PSDN:A8","CH_QGOH_PSDN:A9","CH_QGOH_PSDN:A11","CH_QGOH_PSDN:D4"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"From","passages":[{"id":"RAW_QGOH_PSDN:P0050","trace_type":"Structural","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"},{"id":"RAW_QGOH_PSDN:P0062","trace_type":"Structural","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"},{"id":"RAW_QGOH_PSDN:P0063","trace_type":"Structural","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"},{"id":"RAW_QGOH_PSDN:P0064","trace_type":"Structural","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_QGOH_PSDN:SD2","text":"Cognitio humana non est accessus ad res in se separatas, sed ordo idearum localiter constitutus per Corpus cuius Mens idea est.","refs":["CH_QGOH_PSDN:D6","CH_QGOH_PSDN:D8","CH_QGOH_PSDN:D9"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"From","passages":[{"id":"RAW_QGOH_PSDN:P0095","trace_type":"Structural","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"},{"id":"RAW_QGOH_PSDN:P0099","trace_type":"Structural","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"},{"id":"RAW_QGOH_PSDN:P0109","trace_type":"Structural","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"},{"id":"RAW_QGOH_PSDN:P0103","trace_type":"Structural","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_QGOH_PSDN:SD3","text":"Differentia mentium non ex diversitate substantiarum animarum, sed ex gradu compositionis, passionis et actionis Corporum derivatur.","refs":["CH_QGOH_PSDN:D8","CH_QGOH_PSDN:D11"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"From","passages":[{"id":"RAW_QGOH_PSDN:P0115","trace_type":"Composite","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"},{"id":"RAW_QGOH_PSDN:P0116","trace_type":"Composite","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_QGOH_PSDN:SD4","text":"Necessitas divina, series modorum intra attributa et determinatio corporum constituunt naturam sine contingentia principiali et sine causa finali externa.","refs":["CH_QGOH_PSDN:A5","CH_QGOH_PSDN:A9","CH_QGOH_PSDN:A11","CH_QGOH_PSDN:A18"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"From","passages":[{"id":"RAW_QGOH_PSDN:P0039","trace_type":"Structural","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"},{"id":"RAW_QGOH_PSDN:P0050","trace_type":"Structural","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"},{"id":"RAW_QGOH_PSDN:P0064","trace_type":"Structural","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"},{"id":"RAW_QGOH_PSDN:P0131","trace_type":"Structural","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]}],"original_name":"Super-Derivatives"},{"number":"6","name":"Deep Apophatics","items":[{"id":"CH_QGOH_PSDN:DNX1","text":"Exemplar Dei transcendentis, qui extra naturam decretis voluntariis mundum gubernat, falsum esse debet ut causalitas immanens necessaria valeat.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_QGOH_PSDN:P0039","trace_type":"Negative","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"},{"id":"RAW_QGOH_PSDN:P0040","trace_type":"Negative","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_QGOH_PSDN:DNX2","text":"Exemplar animae incorporeae quae Corpus extrinsece movet vel a Corpore causaliter afficitur falsum esse debet ut attributorum causalitas propria servetur.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_QGOH_PSDN:P0050","trace_type":"Negative","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"},{"id":"RAW_QGOH_PSDN:P0062","trace_type":"Negative","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"},{"id":"RAW_QGOH_PSDN:P0064","trace_type":"Negative","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_QGOH_PSDN:DNX3","text":"Exemplar individui humani ut fundamenti sui ipsius falsum esse debet, quia homo est modificatio finita et necessario causata.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_QGOH_PSDN:P0083","trace_type":"Negative","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"},{"id":"RAW_QGOH_PSDN:P0089","trace_type":"Negative","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"},{"id":"RAW_QGOH_PSDN:P0097","trace_type":"Negative","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_QGOH_PSDN:DNX4","text":"Exemplar cognitionis humanae ut comprehensio totalitatis falsum esse debet, quia Mens humana pars intellectus infiniti et idea corporis singularis est.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_QGOH_PSDN:P0099","trace_type":"Negative","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"},{"id":"RAW_QGOH_PSDN:P0109","trace_type":"Negative","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_QGOH_PSDN:DNX5","text":"Exemplar essentiae ut mera dependentia unilateralis a Deo falsum esse debet, quia essentia reciprocitatem inter rem et id quod eam constituit postulat.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_QGOH_PSDN:P0005","trace_type":"Tension","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"},{"id":"RAW_QGOH_PSDN:P0091","trace_type":"Tension","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"},{"id":"RAW_QGOH_PSDN:P0093","trace_type":"Tension","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]}],"original_name":"Deep Apophatics"},{"number":"7","name":"Super-Apophatics","items":[{"id":"CH_QGOH_PSDN:SNX1","text":"Systema nullum locum ontologicum extra Deum relinquit, ex quo Deus, res finitae, ideae et corpora ut entia independenter comparanda conspici possint.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_QGOH_PSDN:P0062","trace_type":"Structural","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"},{"id":"RAW_QGOH_PSDN:P0064","trace_type":"Structural","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"},{"id":"RAW_QGOH_PSDN:P0091","trace_type":"Structural","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_QGOH_PSDN:SNX2","text":"Systema nullam mediationem primitivam inter Cogitationem et Extensionem admittit: quod mediatio videretur est eadem res sub diversis attributis expressa.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_QGOH_PSDN:P0062","trace_type":"Direct","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"},{"id":"RAW_QGOH_PSDN:P0063","trace_type":"Direct","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_QGOH_PSDN:SNX3","text":"Systema nullum criterium veritatis mere externum admittit, quia ratio ideae adaequatae in ipsa idea, non in sola relatione comparativa ad obiectum, collocatur.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_QGOH_PSDN:P0009","trace_type":"Direct","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"},{"id":"RAW_QGOH_PSDN:P0011","trace_type":"Direct","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]}],"original_name":"Super-Apophatics"},{"number":"8","name":"Formal Generative Schema","items":[{"id":"CH_QGOH_PSDN:FGS1","text":"Essentia divina actuosa necessaria → attributa infinita → modi necessario consequentes.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_QGOH_PSDN:P0035","trace_type":"Direct","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"},{"id":"RAW_QGOH_PSDN:P0037","trace_type":"Direct","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_QGOH_PSDN:FGS2","text":"Attributum determinatum → causalitas intra idem attributum → exclusio causalitatis inter attributa.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_QGOH_PSDN:P0050","trace_type":"Direct","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"},{"id":"RAW_QGOH_PSDN:P0052","trace_type":"Direct","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_QGOH_PSDN:FGS3","text":"Unica substantia + pluralitas attributorum → idem ens dupliciter expressum → parallelismus ordinis idearum et rerum.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_QGOH_PSDN:P0062","trace_type":"Direct","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"},{"id":"RAW_QGOH_PSDN:P0063","trace_type":"Direct","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"},{"id":"RAW_QGOH_PSDN:P0064","trace_type":"Direct","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_QGOH_PSDN:FGS4","text":"Homo non-substantialis + homo cogitans + prioritas ideae → essentia humana ut modificatio cogitativa ordinata circa ideam.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_QGOH_PSDN:P0019","trace_type":"Composite","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"},{"id":"RAW_QGOH_PSDN:P0020","trace_type":"Composite","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"},{"id":"RAW_QGOH_PSDN:P0089","trace_type":"Composite","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"},{"id":"RAW_QGOH_PSDN:P0097","trace_type":"Composite","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_QGOH_PSDN:FGS5","text":"Idea actualis rei singularis + sensus affectionum Corporis + exclusio aliorum obiectorum perceptorum → Mens humana ut idea Corporis actu existentis.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_QGOH_PSDN:P0021","trace_type":"Composite","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"},{"id":"RAW_QGOH_PSDN:P0022","trace_type":"Composite","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"},{"id":"RAW_QGOH_PSDN:P0095","trace_type":"Composite","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"},{"id":"RAW_QGOH_PSDN:P0109","trace_type":"Composite","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_QGOH_PSDN:FGS6","text":"Corpus ut obiectum Mentis → omnis eius affectio perceptibilis → gradus cognitionis ex gradu compositionis et autonomiae corporis → transitus ad physicam corporum.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_QGOH_PSDN:P0103","trace_type":"Structural","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"},{"id":"RAW_QGOH_PSDN:P0116","trace_type":"Structural","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"},{"id":"RAW_QGOH_PSDN:P0123","trace_type":"Structural","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]}],"original_name":"Formal Generative Schema"},{"number":"9","name":"Argument Flow Map","items":[{"id":"CH_QGOH_PSDN:AF1","text":"Necessitas essentiae divinae → Cogitatio et Extensio ut attributa → causalitas attributiva → parallelismus idearum et rerum → homo ut modus finitus → Mens ut idea actualis → Corpus ut obiectum unicum → perceptio affectionum Corporis → analysis motus et quietis.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_QGOH_PSDN:P0035","trace_type":"Structural","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"},{"id":"RAW_QGOH_PSDN:P0050","trace_type":"Structural","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"},{"id":"RAW_QGOH_PSDN:P0056","trace_type":"Structural","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"},{"id":"RAW_QGOH_PSDN:P0089","trace_type":"Structural","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"},{"id":"RAW_QGOH_PSDN:P0095","trace_type":"Structural","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"},{"id":"RAW_QGOH_PSDN:P0109","trace_type":"Structural","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"},{"id":"RAW_QGOH_PSDN:P0103","trace_type":"Structural","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"},{"id":"RAW_QGOH_PSDN:P0123","trace_type":"Structural","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_QGOH_PSDN:AF2","text":"Promissio cognitionis summae beatitudinis → constitutio metaphysica Mentis → definitio unionis Mentis et Corporis → praeparatio physica nondum absolutae beatitudinis demonstratio.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_QGOH_PSDN:P0002","trace_type":"Structural","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"},{"id":"RAW_QGOH_PSDN:P0095","trace_type":"Structural","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"},{"id":"RAW_QGOH_PSDN:P0113","trace_type":"Structural","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"},{"id":"RAW_QGOH_PSDN:P0115","trace_type":"Structural","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"},{"id":"RAW_QGOH_PSDN:P0117","trace_type":"Structural","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]}],"original_name":"Argument Flow Map"},{"number":"10","name":"Map-Effects","items":[{"id":"CH_QGOH_PSDN:ME1","text":"Deus reclassificatur ex voluntate externa et regia in causam immanentem, necessariam et actuosam omnium essentiarum atque existentiarum.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_QGOH_PSDN:P0039","trace_type":"Composite","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"},{"id":"RAW_QGOH_PSDN:P0091","trace_type":"Composite","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_QGOH_PSDN:ME2","text":"Unio Mentis et Corporis reclassificatur ex relatione inter duas res in relationem ideae ad idem Corpus sub alio attributo expressum.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_QGOH_PSDN:P0062","trace_type":"Composite","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"},{"id":"RAW_QGOH_PSDN:P0109","trace_type":"Composite","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"},{"id":"RAW_QGOH_PSDN:P0113","trace_type":"Composite","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_QGOH_PSDN:ME3","text":"Idea reclassificatur ex imagine a re recepta in modum Cogitationis formaliter intra Cogitationem causatum.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_QGOH_PSDN:P0008","trace_type":"Composite","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"},{"id":"RAW_QGOH_PSDN:P0046","trace_type":"Composite","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"},{"id":"RAW_QGOH_PSDN:P0050","trace_type":"Composite","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_QGOH_PSDN:ME4","text":"Individualitas corporis reclassificatur ex differentia substantiae in rationem motus, quietis, celeritatis et tarditatis.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_QGOH_PSDN:P0123","trace_type":"Direct","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"},{"id":"RAW_QGOH_PSDN:P0125","trace_type":"Direct","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_QGOH_PSDN:ME5","text":"Praestantia mentis reclassificatur ex dignitate animae separatae in potentiam corporis plura simul agendi, patiendi et ex se ordinandi.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_QGOH_PSDN:P0116","trace_type":"Direct","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"},{"id":"RAW_QGOH_PSDN:P0117","trace_type":"Direct","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]}],"original_name":"Map-Effects"},{"number":"11","name":"Altitude Levels","items":[{"id":"CH_QGOH_PSDN:ALT1","text":"Surface — Mens humana idea est Corporis actu existentis, et ea quae in hoc Corpore contingunt necessario percipit.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_QGOH_PSDN:P0109","trace_type":"Composite","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"},{"id":"RAW_QGOH_PSDN:P0103","trace_type":"Composite","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_QGOH_PSDN:ALT2","text":"Structural — Modi cogitandi et modi extensi secundum causalitates attributivas distinctas ordinantur, quarum ordines tamen unum eundemque nexum rerum exprimunt.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_QGOH_PSDN:P0050","trace_type":"Structural","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"},{"id":"RAW_QGOH_PSDN:P0056","trace_type":"Structural","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"},{"id":"RAW_QGOH_PSDN:P0062","trace_type":"Structural","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_QGOH_PSDN:ALT3","text":"Metastrategic — Ordo philosophandi a rebus sensuum ad Deum ascendens reicitur; natura divina, cognitione et natura prior, fundamentum explicativum ante res naturales collocatur.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_QGOH_PSDN:P0091","trace_type":"Direct","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"},{"id":"RAW_QGOH_PSDN:P0092","trace_type":"Direct","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_QGOH_PSDN:ALT4","text":"Ontological/Temporal — Deus aeternus necessario agit, res singulares finite et determinate existunt, earum duratio indefinita est, et corporum determinatio in seriem infinitam causarum extenditur.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_QGOH_PSDN:P0012","trace_type":"Composite","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"},{"id":"RAW_QGOH_PSDN:P0016","trace_type":"Composite","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"},{"id":"RAW_QGOH_PSDN:P0131","trace_type":"Composite","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]}],"original_name":"Altitude Levels"},{"number":"12","name":"Minimal Reconstruction Test","items":[{"id":"CH_QGOH_PSDN:MRT1","text":"Systema extractum sufficit ad conclusionem immediatam restituendam quod Mens humana sit idea Corporis actu existentis.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_QGOH_PSDN:P0095","trace_type":"Direct","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"},{"id":"RAW_QGOH_PSDN:P0109","trace_type":"Direct","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_QGOH_PSDN:MRT2","text":"Demonstratio pendet a principiis Partis primae de unica substantia, causalitate Dei et distinctione attributorum, quae hic assumuntur potius quam iterum fundantur.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_QGOH_PSDN:P0062","trace_type":"Structural","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"},{"id":"RAW_QGOH_PSDN:P0089","trace_type":"Structural","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"},{"id":"RAW_QGOH_PSDN:P0091","trace_type":"Structural","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_QGOH_PSDN:MRT3","text":"Commissura principalis manet inter identitatem Mentis et Corporis ut unius rei et necessitatem eos per causalitates attributivas omnino separatas explicandi.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_QGOH_PSDN:P0062","trace_type":"Tension","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"},{"id":"RAW_QGOH_PSDN:P0064","trace_type":"Tension","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_QGOH_PSDN:MRT4","text":"Distinctio inter Deum ut condicionem sine qua res finita nec est nec concipitur et Deum ut non pertinentem ad essentiam rei finitae solum per strictam reciprocitatis definitionem essentiae continetur.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_QGOH_PSDN:P0005","trace_type":"Tension","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"},{"id":"RAW_QGOH_PSDN:P0091","trace_type":"Tension","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"},{"id":"RAW_QGOH_PSDN:P0093","trace_type":"Tension","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_QGOH_PSDN:MRT5","text":"Correlatio inter potentiam Corporis et perfectionem Mentis affirmatur, sed mechanismus compositionis corporalis qui gradus illos explicet ad doctrinam physicam subsequentem differtur.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_QGOH_PSDN:P0116","trace_type":"Direct","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"},{"id":"RAW_QGOH_PSDN:P0117","trace_type":"Direct","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_QGOH_PSDN:MRT6","text":"Motus argumenti ad cognitionem beatitudinis dirigi dicitur, sed haec pars tantum condicionem ontologicam et epistemicam itineris ponit, non beatitudinem ipsam deducit.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_QGOH_PSDN:P0002","trace_type":"Structural","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"},{"id":"RAW_QGOH_PSDN:P0115","trace_type":"Structural","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"},{"id":"RAW_QGOH_PSDN:P0117","trace_type":"Structural","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]}],"original_name":"Minimal Reconstruction Test"}],"language_code":"la","language_name":"Latina","translations":[{"id":"CAREVAR_20261015T014951024579Z_328ebc9f","language_code":"en","language_name":"English","title":"Part Two - On the Nature and Origin of the Mind · passage 1","source_sha256":"e44839f7c1df11bc8d1d89a907acdf97e31539b3a941d6e4f06a592fe53fd605","sha256":"dd4978d79df943cda758c1639af535b69b67e72bbc6429a9a69f34a45dafdd12","rendition_type":"machine","review_status":"machine","model":"gpt-5.6-terra","created":"2026-10-15T01:49:51.026763+00:00","sections":[{"number":"1","name":"Argumental Spine","items":[{"id":"CH_QGOH_PSDN:AS1","text":"The text asks in what order the human Mind is to be understood from the necessity of the divine nature, so that the way to knowledge of the mind and blessedness may be opened.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_QGOH_PSDN:P0002","trace_type":"Direct","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_QGOH_PSDN:AS2","text":"The constitutive thesis is that the human Mind is not a substance, but the idea of an actually existing singular thing, whose object is the human Body.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_QGOH_PSDN:P0095","trace_type":"Composite","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"},{"id":"RAW_QGOH_PSDN:P0109","trace_type":"Composite","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_QGOH_PSDN:AS3","text":"The argumentation is metaphysical-deductive and reconstructive: it proceeds from the necessity of God and the attributes to the series of modes, thence to the structure of the human Mind.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_QGOH_PSDN:P0002","trace_type":"Structural","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"},{"id":"RAW_QGOH_PSDN:P0035","trace_type":"Structural","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"},{"id":"RAW_QGOH_PSDN:P0095","trace_type":"Structural","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"},{"id":"RAW_QGOH_PSDN:P0109","trace_type":"Structural","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_QGOH_PSDN:AS4","text":"Causality within an attribute and the identity of the order of ideas with the order of things prevent Mind and Body from being conceived as separate things or as causally transverse.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_QGOH_PSDN:P0050","trace_type":"Composite","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"},{"id":"RAW_QGOH_PSDN:P0056","trace_type":"Composite","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"},{"id":"RAW_QGOH_PSDN:P0062","trace_type":"Composite","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"},{"id":"RAW_QGOH_PSDN:P0063","trace_type":"Composite","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"},{"id":"RAW_QGOH_PSDN:P0064","trace_type":"Composite","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_QGOH_PSDN:AS5","text":"The demonstration of the Mind is transferred into knowledge of the Body, because the account of the union of Mind and Body and the difference in degrees of minds are held to depend on the nature of the Body.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_QGOH_PSDN:P0115","trace_type":"Structural","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"},{"id":"RAW_QGOH_PSDN:P0116","trace_type":"Structural","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"},{"id":"RAW_QGOH_PSDN:P0117","trace_type":"Structural","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]}],"original_name":"Argumental Spine"},{"number":"2","name":"Load-Bearing Axioms","items":[{"id":"CH_QGOH_PSDN:A1","text":"The essence of a thing is constituted by that which, if given, necessarily posits the thing, and which, if removed, necessarily removes it, and which can neither be nor be conceived without the thing, and vice versa.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_QGOH_PSDN:P0005","trace_type":"Direct","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_QGOH_PSDN:A2","text":"A singular thing is a finite thing having determinate existence.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_QGOH_PSDN:P0016","trace_type":"Direct","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_QGOH_PSDN:A3","text":"Nothing can be or be conceived without God.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_QGOH_PSDN:P0091","trace_type":"Direct","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_QGOH_PSDN:A4","text":"God is the sole cause both of the essence and of the existence of things.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_QGOH_PSDN:P0091","trace_type":"Direct","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_QGOH_PSDN:A5","text":"The power of God is his active essence, from which God acts with the same necessity by which he understands himself.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_QGOH_PSDN:P0039","trace_type":"Direct","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_QGOH_PSDN:A6","text":"Thought is an attribute of God that expresses his eternal and infinite essence.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_QGOH_PSDN:P0027","trace_type":"Direct","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_QGOH_PSDN:A7","text":"Extension is an attribute of God that expresses his eternal and infinite essence.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_QGOH_PSDN:P0031","trace_type":"Composite","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"},{"id":"RAW_QGOH_PSDN:P0033","trace_type":"Composite","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_QGOH_PSDN:A8","text":"Thinking substance and extended substance are one and the same substance, conceived under different attributes.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_QGOH_PSDN:P0062","trace_type":"Direct","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_QGOH_PSDN:A9","text":"A mode of each attribute has God as its cause insofar as he is considered under that attribute alone.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_QGOH_PSDN:P0050","trace_type":"Direct","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_QGOH_PSDN:A10","text":"The formal being of ideas has God as its cause insofar as he is a thinking thing.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_QGOH_PSDN:P0046","trace_type":"Direct","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_QGOH_PSDN:A11","text":"The order and connection of ideas is the same as the order and connection of things.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_QGOH_PSDN:P0056","trace_type":"Direct","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_QGOH_PSDN:A12","text":"The idea of a singular thing that does not exist is only insofar as it is comprehended in the infinite idea of God.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_QGOH_PSDN:P0066","trace_type":"Composite","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"},{"id":"RAW_QGOH_PSDN:P0068","trace_type":"Composite","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_QGOH_PSDN:A13","text":"The essence of man does not involve necessary existence, nor does substance constitute the form of man.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_QGOH_PSDN:P0018","trace_type":"Composite","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"},{"id":"RAW_QGOH_PSDN:P0083","trace_type":"Composite","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_QGOH_PSDN:A14","text":"Man thinks.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_QGOH_PSDN:P0019","trace_type":"Direct","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_QGOH_PSDN:A15","text":"In the same individual, an idea is by nature prior to the remaining modes of thinking.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_QGOH_PSDN:P0020","trace_type":"Composite","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"},{"id":"RAW_QGOH_PSDN:P0097","trace_type":"Composite","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_QGOH_PSDN:A16","text":"Man senses that the Body is affected in many ways.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_QGOH_PSDN:P0021","trace_type":"Direct","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_QGOH_PSDN:A17","text":"Man senses or perceives no singular things except bodies and modes of thinking.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_QGOH_PSDN:P0022","trace_type":"Direct","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_QGOH_PSDN:A18","text":"Bodies are distinguished by the relation of motion and rest, and the determination of each body to motion or rest depends on other bodies to infinity.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_QGOH_PSDN:P0123","trace_type":"Composite","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"},{"id":"RAW_QGOH_PSDN:P0131","trace_type":"Composite","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"},{"id":"RAW_QGOH_PSDN:P0133","trace_type":"Composite","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]}],"original_name":"Load-Bearing Axioms"},{"number":"3","name":"Apophatic Structure","items":[{"id":"CH_QGOH_PSDN:NX1","text":"The power of God is not free royal will, nor a right contingently to destroy or preserve things.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_QGOH_PSDN:P0039","trace_type":"Negative","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"},{"id":"RAW_QGOH_PSDN:P0040","trace_type":"Negative","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_QGOH_PSDN:NX2","text":"An idea does not receive its efficient cause from the ideate, nor from a mode of another attribute.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_QGOH_PSDN:P0046","trace_type":"Negative","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"},{"id":"RAW_QGOH_PSDN:P0050","trace_type":"Negative","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"},{"id":"RAW_QGOH_PSDN:P0054","trace_type":"Negative","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_QGOH_PSDN:NX3","text":"Thought and Extension are not two really separate substances.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_QGOH_PSDN:P0062","trace_type":"Negative","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"},{"id":"RAW_QGOH_PSDN:P0063","trace_type":"Negative","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_QGOH_PSDN:NX4","text":"Man is not a substance, nor does he necessarily exist from his essence.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_QGOH_PSDN:P0018","trace_type":"Negative","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"},{"id":"RAW_QGOH_PSDN:P0083","trace_type":"Negative","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"},{"id":"RAW_QGOH_PSDN:P0085","trace_type":"Negative","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_QGOH_PSDN:NX5","text":"God, although singular things can neither be nor be conceived without him, does not pertain to their essence according to the definition of reciprocity.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_QGOH_PSDN:P0091","trace_type":"Tension","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"},{"id":"RAW_QGOH_PSDN:P0093","trace_type":"Tension","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_QGOH_PSDN:NX6","text":"The first actual being of the human Mind is not the idea of a non-existing thing nor of an infinite thing.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_QGOH_PSDN:P0097","trace_type":"Negative","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_QGOH_PSDN:NX7","text":"The object of the human Mind is not anything other than an actually existing Body.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_QGOH_PSDN:P0109","trace_type":"Negative","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"},{"id":"RAW_QGOH_PSDN:P0111","trace_type":"Negative","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_QGOH_PSDN:NX8","text":"The adequacy of an idea is not defined by external correspondence with the ideate, but by the intrinsic properties of a true idea.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_QGOH_PSDN:P0009","trace_type":"Negative","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"},{"id":"RAW_QGOH_PSDN:P0011","trace_type":"Negative","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]}],"original_name":"Apophatic Structure"},{"number":"4","name":"Derivative Propositions","items":[{"id":"CH_QGOH_PSDN:D1","text":"God necessarily forms an idea of his essence and of all things that necessarily follow from it.","refs":["CH_QGOH_PSDN:A5","CH_QGOH_PSDN:A6"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"From","passages":[{"id":"RAW_QGOH_PSDN:P0035","trace_type":"Direct","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"},{"id":"RAW_QGOH_PSDN:P0037","trace_type":"Direct","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_QGOH_PSDN:D2","text":"The idea of God from which infinitely many things follow in infinitely many ways is unique.","refs":["CH_QGOH_PSDN:A8","CH_QGOH_PSDN:D1"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"From","passages":[{"id":"RAW_QGOH_PSDN:P0042","trace_type":"Direct","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"},{"id":"RAW_QGOH_PSDN:P0044","trace_type":"Direct","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_QGOH_PSDN:D3","text":"Ideas are formally produced within the attribute of Thought, just as the modes of each other attribute are produced within their own attribute.","refs":["CH_QGOH_PSDN:A9","CH_QGOH_PSDN:A10"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"From","passages":[{"id":"RAW_QGOH_PSDN:P0046","trace_type":"Composite","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"},{"id":"RAW_QGOH_PSDN:P0050","trace_type":"Composite","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_QGOH_PSDN:D4","text":"A mode of Extension and its idea are not two things, but one thing understood through extensive and cogitative expression.","refs":["CH_QGOH_PSDN:A8","CH_QGOH_PSDN:A11"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"From","passages":[{"id":"RAW_QGOH_PSDN:P0062","trace_type":"Direct","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"},{"id":"RAW_QGOH_PSDN:P0063","trace_type":"Direct","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_QGOH_PSDN:D5","text":"The essence of man is a certain modification of the attributes of God, not a being subsisting through itself.","refs":["CH_QGOH_PSDN:A3","CH_QGOH_PSDN:A4","CH_QGOH_PSDN:A13"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"From","passages":[{"id":"RAW_QGOH_PSDN:P0089","trace_type":"Composite","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"},{"id":"RAW_QGOH_PSDN:P0091","trace_type":"Composite","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_QGOH_PSDN:D6","text":"The first thing that constitutes the actual being of the human Mind is the idea of an actually existing singular thing.","refs":["CH_QGOH_PSDN:A2","CH_QGOH_PSDN:A12","CH_QGOH_PSDN:A13","CH_QGOH_PSDN:A14","CH_QGOH_PSDN:A15"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"From","passages":[{"id":"RAW_QGOH_PSDN:P0095","trace_type":"Direct","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"},{"id":"RAW_QGOH_PSDN:P0097","trace_type":"Direct","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_QGOH_PSDN:D7","text":"The human Mind is a part of the infinite intellect of God and perceives a thing inadequately when its idea simultaneously pertains to the series of ideas of other things.","refs":["CH_QGOH_PSDN:D6","CH_QGOH_PSDN:A3","CH_QGOH_PSDN:A11"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"From","passages":[{"id":"RAW_QGOH_PSDN:P0099","trace_type":"Direct","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_QGOH_PSDN:D8","text":"The object of the idea constituting the human Mind is an actually existing Body and nothing else.","refs":["CH_QGOH_PSDN:D6","CH_QGOH_PSDN:A16","CH_QGOH_PSDN:A17"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"From","passages":[{"id":"RAW_QGOH_PSDN:P0109","trace_type":"Direct","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"},{"id":"RAW_QGOH_PSDN:P0111","trace_type":"Direct","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_QGOH_PSDN:D9","text":"Whatever happens in the Body that is the object of the human Mind is necessarily perceived by the human Mind.","refs":["CH_QGOH_PSDN:D8","CH_QGOH_PSDN:A10","CH_QGOH_PSDN:A11"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"From","passages":[{"id":"RAW_QGOH_PSDN:P0103","trace_type":"Direct","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"},{"id":"RAW_QGOH_PSDN:P0105","trace_type":"Direct","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_QGOH_PSDN:D10","text":"Man consists of Mind and Body, and the human Body exists insofar as it is sensed.","refs":["CH_QGOH_PSDN:D8","CH_QGOH_PSDN:D9"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"From","passages":[{"id":"RAW_QGOH_PSDN:P0113","trace_type":"Direct","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_QGOH_PSDN:D11","text":"The aptitude of the Mind to perceive many things at once is proportionate to the aptitude of the Body to act or be acted upon in many ways at once; more distinct understanding increases insofar as the actions of the Body depend more upon it alone.","refs":["CH_QGOH_PSDN:D8","CH_QGOH_PSDN:A18"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"From","passages":[{"id":"RAW_QGOH_PSDN:P0116","trace_type":"Direct","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]}],"original_name":"Derivative Propositions"},{"number":"5","name":"Super-Derivatives","items":[{"id":"CH_QGOH_PSDN:SD1","text":"The problem of causal commerce between Mind and Body is dissolved: transverse causality between attributes is excluded, while the identity of the thing under a double expression is retained.","refs":["CH_QGOH_PSDN:A8","CH_QGOH_PSDN:A9","CH_QGOH_PSDN:A11","CH_QGOH_PSDN:D4"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"From","passages":[{"id":"RAW_QGOH_PSDN:P0050","trace_type":"Structural","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"},{"id":"RAW_QGOH_PSDN:P0062","trace_type":"Structural","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"},{"id":"RAW_QGOH_PSDN:P0063","trace_type":"Structural","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"},{"id":"RAW_QGOH_PSDN:P0064","trace_type":"Structural","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_QGOH_PSDN:SD2","text":"Human knowledge is not access to things separately in themselves, but an order of ideas locally constituted through the Body of which the Mind is the idea.","refs":["CH_QGOH_PSDN:D6","CH_QGOH_PSDN:D8","CH_QGOH_PSDN:D9"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"From","passages":[{"id":"RAW_QGOH_PSDN:P0095","trace_type":"Structural","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"},{"id":"RAW_QGOH_PSDN:P0099","trace_type":"Structural","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"},{"id":"RAW_QGOH_PSDN:P0109","trace_type":"Structural","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"},{"id":"RAW_QGOH_PSDN:P0103","trace_type":"Structural","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_QGOH_PSDN:SD3","text":"The difference of minds derives not from the diversity of substances of souls, but from the degree of composition, passion, and action of Bodies.","refs":["CH_QGOH_PSDN:D8","CH_QGOH_PSDN:D11"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"From","passages":[{"id":"RAW_QGOH_PSDN:P0115","trace_type":"Composite","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"},{"id":"RAW_QGOH_PSDN:P0116","trace_type":"Composite","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_QGOH_PSDN:SD4","text":"Divine necessity, the series of modes within attributes, and the determination of bodies constitute a nature without principled contingency and without external final cause.","refs":["CH_QGOH_PSDN:A5","CH_QGOH_PSDN:A9","CH_QGOH_PSDN:A11","CH_QGOH_PSDN:A18"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"From","passages":[{"id":"RAW_QGOH_PSDN:P0039","trace_type":"Structural","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"},{"id":"RAW_QGOH_PSDN:P0050","trace_type":"Structural","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"},{"id":"RAW_QGOH_PSDN:P0064","trace_type":"Structural","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"},{"id":"RAW_QGOH_PSDN:P0131","trace_type":"Structural","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]}],"original_name":"Super-Derivatives"},{"number":"6","name":"Deep Apophatics","items":[{"id":"CH_QGOH_PSDN:DNX1","text":"The model of a transcendent God who governs the world outside nature by voluntary decrees must be false if necessary immanent causality is to hold.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_QGOH_PSDN:P0039","trace_type":"Negative","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"},{"id":"RAW_QGOH_PSDN:P0040","trace_type":"Negative","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_QGOH_PSDN:DNX2","text":"The model of an incorporeal soul that moves the Body extrinsically or is causally affected by the Body must be false if the proper causality of the attributes is to be preserved.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_QGOH_PSDN:P0050","trace_type":"Negative","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"},{"id":"RAW_QGOH_PSDN:P0062","trace_type":"Negative","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"},{"id":"RAW_QGOH_PSDN:P0064","trace_type":"Negative","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_QGOH_PSDN:DNX3","text":"The model of the human individual as the foundation of itself must be false, because man is a finite and necessarily caused modification.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_QGOH_PSDN:P0083","trace_type":"Negative","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"},{"id":"RAW_QGOH_PSDN:P0089","trace_type":"Negative","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"},{"id":"RAW_QGOH_PSDN:P0097","trace_type":"Negative","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_QGOH_PSDN:DNX4","text":"The model of human knowledge as comprehension of the totality must be false, because the human Mind is a part of the infinite intellect and the idea of a singular body.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_QGOH_PSDN:P0099","trace_type":"Negative","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"},{"id":"RAW_QGOH_PSDN:P0109","trace_type":"Negative","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_QGOH_PSDN:DNX5","text":"The model of essence as mere unilateral dependence on God must be false, because essence requires reciprocity between a thing and that which constitutes it.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_QGOH_PSDN:P0005","trace_type":"Tension","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"},{"id":"RAW_QGOH_PSDN:P0091","trace_type":"Tension","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"},{"id":"RAW_QGOH_PSDN:P0093","trace_type":"Tension","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]}],"original_name":"Deep Apophatics"},{"number":"7","name":"Super-Apophatics","items":[{"id":"CH_QGOH_PSDN:SNX1","text":"The system leaves no ontological place outside God from which God, finite things, ideas, and bodies could be viewed as independently comparable beings.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_QGOH_PSDN:P0062","trace_type":"Structural","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"},{"id":"RAW_QGOH_PSDN:P0064","trace_type":"Structural","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"},{"id":"RAW_QGOH_PSDN:P0091","trace_type":"Structural","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_QGOH_PSDN:SNX2","text":"The system admits no primitive mediation between Thought and Extension: what would appear to be mediation is the same thing expressed under different attributes.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_QGOH_PSDN:P0062","trace_type":"Direct","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"},{"id":"RAW_QGOH_PSDN:P0063","trace_type":"Direct","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_QGOH_PSDN:SNX3","text":"The system admits no merely external criterion of truth, because the ground of the adequate idea is located in the idea itself, not in a merely comparative relation to the object.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_QGOH_PSDN:P0009","trace_type":"Direct","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"},{"id":"RAW_QGOH_PSDN:P0011","trace_type":"Direct","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]}],"original_name":"Super-Apophatics"},{"number":"8","name":"Formal Generative Schema","items":[{"id":"CH_QGOH_PSDN:FGS1","text":"Necessary active divine essence → infinite attributes → necessarily consequent modes.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_QGOH_PSDN:P0035","trace_type":"Direct","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"},{"id":"RAW_QGOH_PSDN:P0037","trace_type":"Direct","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_QGOH_PSDN:FGS2","text":"Determinate attribute → causality within the same attribute → exclusion of causality between attributes.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_QGOH_PSDN:P0050","trace_type":"Direct","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"},{"id":"RAW_QGOH_PSDN:P0052","trace_type":"Direct","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_QGOH_PSDN:FGS3","text":"One substance + plurality of attributes → the same being doubly expressed → parallelism of the order of ideas and things.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_QGOH_PSDN:P0062","trace_type":"Direct","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"},{"id":"RAW_QGOH_PSDN:P0063","trace_type":"Direct","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"},{"id":"RAW_QGOH_PSDN:P0064","trace_type":"Direct","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_QGOH_PSDN:FGS4","text":"Non-substantial man + thinking man + priority of the idea → human essence as a cogitative modification ordered around an idea.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_QGOH_PSDN:P0019","trace_type":"Composite","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"},{"id":"RAW_QGOH_PSDN:P0020","trace_type":"Composite","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"},{"id":"RAW_QGOH_PSDN:P0089","trace_type":"Composite","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"},{"id":"RAW_QGOH_PSDN:P0097","trace_type":"Composite","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_QGOH_PSDN:FGS5","text":"Actual idea of a singular thing + sensing of affections of the Body + exclusion of other perceived objects → human Mind as the idea of an actually existing Body.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_QGOH_PSDN:P0021","trace_type":"Composite","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"},{"id":"RAW_QGOH_PSDN:P0022","trace_type":"Composite","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"},{"id":"RAW_QGOH_PSDN:P0095","trace_type":"Composite","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"},{"id":"RAW_QGOH_PSDN:P0109","trace_type":"Composite","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_QGOH_PSDN:FGS6","text":"Body as object of the Mind → every affection of it perceptible → degrees of knowledge from the degree of composition and autonomy of the body → transition to the physics of bodies.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_QGOH_PSDN:P0103","trace_type":"Structural","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"},{"id":"RAW_QGOH_PSDN:P0116","trace_type":"Structural","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"},{"id":"RAW_QGOH_PSDN:P0123","trace_type":"Structural","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]}],"original_name":"Formal Generative Schema"},{"number":"9","name":"Argument Flow Map","items":[{"id":"CH_QGOH_PSDN:AF1","text":"Necessity of the divine essence → Thought and Extension as attributes → attributive causality → parallelism of ideas and things → man as finite mode → Mind as actual idea → Body as sole object → perception of affections of the Body → analysis of motion and rest.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_QGOH_PSDN:P0035","trace_type":"Structural","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"},{"id":"RAW_QGOH_PSDN:P0050","trace_type":"Structural","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"},{"id":"RAW_QGOH_PSDN:P0056","trace_type":"Structural","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"},{"id":"RAW_QGOH_PSDN:P0089","trace_type":"Structural","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"},{"id":"RAW_QGOH_PSDN:P0095","trace_type":"Structural","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"},{"id":"RAW_QGOH_PSDN:P0109","trace_type":"Structural","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"},{"id":"RAW_QGOH_PSDN:P0103","trace_type":"Structural","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"},{"id":"RAW_QGOH_PSDN:P0123","trace_type":"Structural","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_QGOH_PSDN:AF2","text":"Promise of knowledge of supreme blessedness → metaphysical constitution of the Mind → definition of the union of Mind and Body → physical preparation, not yet a demonstration of absolute blessedness.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_QGOH_PSDN:P0002","trace_type":"Structural","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"},{"id":"RAW_QGOH_PSDN:P0095","trace_type":"Structural","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"},{"id":"RAW_QGOH_PSDN:P0113","trace_type":"Structural","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"},{"id":"RAW_QGOH_PSDN:P0115","trace_type":"Structural","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"},{"id":"RAW_QGOH_PSDN:P0117","trace_type":"Structural","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]}],"original_name":"Argument Flow Map"},{"number":"10","name":"Map-Effects","items":[{"id":"CH_QGOH_PSDN:ME1","text":"God is reclassified from external and royal will into the immanent, necessary, and active cause of all essences and existences.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_QGOH_PSDN:P0039","trace_type":"Composite","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"},{"id":"RAW_QGOH_PSDN:P0091","trace_type":"Composite","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_QGOH_PSDN:ME2","text":"The union of Mind and Body is reclassified from a relation between two things into the relation of an idea to the same Body expressed under another attribute.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_QGOH_PSDN:P0062","trace_type":"Composite","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"},{"id":"RAW_QGOH_PSDN:P0109","trace_type":"Composite","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"},{"id":"RAW_QGOH_PSDN:P0113","trace_type":"Composite","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_QGOH_PSDN:ME3","text":"The idea is reclassified from an image received from a thing into a mode of Thought formally caused within Thought.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_QGOH_PSDN:P0008","trace_type":"Composite","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"},{"id":"RAW_QGOH_PSDN:P0046","trace_type":"Composite","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"},{"id":"RAW_QGOH_PSDN:P0050","trace_type":"Composite","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_QGOH_PSDN:ME4","text":"The individuality of a body is reclassified from a difference of substance into a relation of motion, rest, speed, and slowness.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_QGOH_PSDN:P0123","trace_type":"Direct","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"},{"id":"RAW_QGOH_PSDN:P0125","trace_type":"Direct","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_QGOH_PSDN:ME5","text":"The excellence of the mind is reclassified from the dignity of a separate soul into the power of the body to act, be acted upon, and order itself in many ways at once.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_QGOH_PSDN:P0116","trace_type":"Direct","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"},{"id":"RAW_QGOH_PSDN:P0117","trace_type":"Direct","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]}],"original_name":"Map-Effects"},{"number":"11","name":"Altitude Levels","items":[{"id":"CH_QGOH_PSDN:ALT1","text":"Surface — The human Mind is the idea of an actually existing Body, and it necessarily perceives those things that happen in this Body.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_QGOH_PSDN:P0109","trace_type":"Composite","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"},{"id":"RAW_QGOH_PSDN:P0103","trace_type":"Composite","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_QGOH_PSDN:ALT2","text":"Structural — Modes of thinking and extended modes are ordered according to distinct attributive causalities, whose orders nevertheless express one and the same connection of things.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_QGOH_PSDN:P0050","trace_type":"Structural","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"},{"id":"RAW_QGOH_PSDN:P0056","trace_type":"Structural","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"},{"id":"RAW_QGOH_PSDN:P0062","trace_type":"Structural","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_QGOH_PSDN:ALT3","text":"Metastrategic — The order of philosophizing that ascends from sensible things to God is rejected; divine nature, prior in knowledge and nature, is placed as the explanatory foundation before natural things.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_QGOH_PSDN:P0091","trace_type":"Direct","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"},{"id":"RAW_QGOH_PSDN:P0092","trace_type":"Direct","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_QGOH_PSDN:ALT4","text":"Ontological/Temporal — Eternal God necessarily acts, singular things exist finitely and determinately, their duration is indefinite, and the determination of bodies extends into an infinite series of causes.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_QGOH_PSDN:P0012","trace_type":"Composite","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"},{"id":"RAW_QGOH_PSDN:P0016","trace_type":"Composite","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"},{"id":"RAW_QGOH_PSDN:P0131","trace_type":"Composite","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]}],"original_name":"Altitude Levels"},{"number":"12","name":"Minimal Reconstruction Test","items":[{"id":"CH_QGOH_PSDN:MRT1","text":"The extracted system suffices to restore the immediate conclusion that the human Mind is the idea of an actually existing Body.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_QGOH_PSDN:P0095","trace_type":"Direct","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"},{"id":"RAW_QGOH_PSDN:P0109","trace_type":"Direct","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_QGOH_PSDN:MRT2","text":"The demonstration depends on the principles of Part One concerning the one substance, the causality of God, and the distinction of attributes, which are assumed here rather than grounded again.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_QGOH_PSDN:P0062","trace_type":"Structural","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"},{"id":"RAW_QGOH_PSDN:P0089","trace_type":"Structural","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"},{"id":"RAW_QGOH_PSDN:P0091","trace_type":"Structural","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_QGOH_PSDN:MRT3","text":"The principal juncture remains between the identity of Mind and Body as one thing and the necessity of explaining them through wholly separate attributive causalities.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_QGOH_PSDN:P0062","trace_type":"Tension","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"},{"id":"RAW_QGOH_PSDN:P0064","trace_type":"Tension","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_QGOH_PSDN:MRT4","text":"The distinction between God as the condition without which a finite thing neither is nor is conceived and God as not pertaining to the essence of a finite thing is maintained only through the strict definition of reciprocity of essence.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_QGOH_PSDN:P0005","trace_type":"Tension","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"},{"id":"RAW_QGOH_PSDN:P0091","trace_type":"Tension","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"},{"id":"RAW_QGOH_PSDN:P0093","trace_type":"Tension","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_QGOH_PSDN:MRT5","text":"The correlation between the power of the Body and the perfection of the Mind is affirmed, but the mechanism of bodily composition that would explain those degrees is deferred to the subsequent physical doctrine.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_QGOH_PSDN:P0116","trace_type":"Direct","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"},{"id":"RAW_QGOH_PSDN:P0117","trace_type":"Direct","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_QGOH_PSDN:MRT6","text":"The movement of the argument is said to be directed toward knowledge of blessedness, but this part only establishes the ontological and epistemic condition of the path; it does not deduce blessedness itself.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_QGOH_PSDN:P0002","trace_type":"Structural","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"},{"id":"RAW_QGOH_PSDN:P0115","trace_type":"Structural","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"},{"id":"RAW_QGOH_PSDN:P0117","trace_type":"Structural","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]}],"original_name":"Minimal Reconstruction Test"}],"revision":"CH_QGOH_PSDN@R001","source_variant_id":"canonical:CH_QGOH_PSDN:v1","source_language_code":"la"}],"identity":{"layer":"CH","kicker":"Chapter 05","title":"Part Two - On the Nature and Origin of the Mind · passage 1","context":"Chapter 05","original_title":"Pars secunda - De natura et origine mentis · passage 1","original_context":"Chapter 05"},"original_title":"Pars secunda - De natura et origine mentis · passage 1","title_language_code":"en"},{"id":"CH_QGOH_PS_2","layer":"CH","title":"Part Two - On the Nature and Origin of the Mind · passage 2","order":6,"parents":["RAW_QGOH_PS_2"],"revision":"CH_QGOH_PS_2@R001","sha256":"41e6270bc187ab1f843c2b89865abc6c5aa40e7651542c62637fa0f1fa2aa766","sections":[{"number":"1","name":"Argumental Spine","items":[{"id":"CH_QGOH_PS_2:AS1","text":"Textus quaerit quomodo ex constitutione motuum corporis compositi percipere, imaginari, meminisse atque se cognoscere Mens humana possit.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_QGOH_PS_2:P0037","trace_type":"Structural","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"},{"id":"RAW_QGOH_PS_2:P0057","trace_type":"Structural","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"},{"id":"RAW_QGOH_PS_2:P0070","trace_type":"Structural","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"},{"id":"RAW_QGOH_PS_2:P0074","trace_type":"Structural","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"},{"id":"RAW_QGOH_PS_2:P0093","trace_type":"Structural","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_QGOH_PS_2:AS2","text":"Cognitio Mentis non ex accessu immediato ad res, Corpus aut se ipsam oritur, sed ex ideis affectionum quibus Corpus humanum cum corporibus externis determinatur.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_QGOH_PS_2:P0045","trace_type":"Composite","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"},{"id":"RAW_QGOH_PS_2:P0074","trace_type":"Composite","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"},{"id":"RAW_QGOH_PS_2:P0107","trace_type":"Composite","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_QGOH_PS_2:AS3","text":"Argumentatio physico-genetica est: a perseverantia motus et quietis, per individuationem relationalem corporis, ad condiciones cognitionis et confusionis ascendit.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_QGOH_PS_2:P0001","trace_type":"Structural","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"},{"id":"RAW_QGOH_PS_2:P0008","trace_type":"Structural","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"},{"id":"RAW_QGOH_PS_2:P0031","trace_type":"Structural","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"},{"id":"RAW_QGOH_PS_2:P0045","trace_type":"Structural","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"},{"id":"RAW_QGOH_PS_2:P0057","trace_type":"Structural","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"},{"id":"RAW_QGOH_PS_2:P0074","trace_type":"Structural","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"},{"id":"RAW_QGOH_PS_2:P0119","trace_type":"Structural","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_QGOH_PS_2:AS4","text":"Identitas corporis componitur ex certa ratione motus et quietis inter partes, non ex identitate materiali partium, figurae, magnitudinis aut motus localis totius.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_QGOH_PS_2:P0008","trace_type":"Composite","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"},{"id":"RAW_QGOH_PS_2:P0012","trace_type":"Composite","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"},{"id":"RAW_QGOH_PS_2:P0016","trace_type":"Composite","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"},{"id":"RAW_QGOH_PS_2:P0020","trace_type":"Composite","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"},{"id":"RAW_QGOH_PS_2:P0024","trace_type":"Composite","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_QGOH_PS_2:AS5","text":"Affectio corporis simul naturam corporis affecti et corporis afficientis involvit; ideo perceptio externi est praesentatio externa per dispositionem proprii corporis.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_QGOH_PS_2:P0003","trace_type":"Composite","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"},{"id":"RAW_QGOH_PS_2:P0045","trace_type":"Composite","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"},{"id":"RAW_QGOH_PS_2:P0051","trace_type":"Composite","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_QGOH_PS_2:AS6","text":"Progressio concludit imaginationem et memoriam esse effectus vestigiorum ac concatenationum corporearum, cognitionem autem corporis, externorum et sui esse partialem atque, quatenus sola talibus affectionibus nititur, confusam.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_QGOH_PS_2:P0057","trace_type":"Structural","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"},{"id":"RAW_QGOH_PS_2:P0063","trace_type":"Structural","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"},{"id":"RAW_QGOH_PS_2:P0070","trace_type":"Structural","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"},{"id":"RAW_QGOH_PS_2:P0119","trace_type":"Structural","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"},{"id":"RAW_QGOH_PS_2:P0121","trace_type":"Structural","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]}],"original_name":"Argumental Spine"},{"number":"2","name":"Load-Bearing Axioms","items":[{"id":"CH_QGOH_PS_2:A1","text":"Corpus motum moveri et corpus quiescens quiescere perseverat, nisi causa externa ad contrarium statum determinet.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_QGOH_PS_2:P0001","trace_type":"Direct","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_QGOH_PS_2:A2","text":"Omnis modus quo corpus ab alio corpore afficitur ex natura corporis affecti simul et corporis afficientis sequitur.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_QGOH_PS_2:P0003","trace_type":"Direct","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_QGOH_PS_2:A3","text":"Individuum corporeum est unio corporum quae motus suos certa ratione invicem communicant.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_QGOH_PS_2:P0008","trace_type":"Direct","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_QGOH_PS_2:A4","text":"Natura Individui manet, dum ratio communicationis motus et quietis inter partes manet, etiamsi partes substituantur, magnitudine mutentur, directionem flectant aut Individuum totum moveatur.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_QGOH_PS_2:P0012","trace_type":"Composite","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"},{"id":"RAW_QGOH_PS_2:P0016","trace_type":"Composite","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"},{"id":"RAW_QGOH_PS_2:P0020","trace_type":"Composite","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"},{"id":"RAW_QGOH_PS_2:P0024","trace_type":"Composite","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_QGOH_PS_2:A5","text":"Vestigia corporum externorum in partibus fluidis Corporis humani dispositiones relinquere possunt quae posteriores motus reflexos eiusdem generis efficiunt.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_QGOH_PS_2:P0035","trace_type":"Composite","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"},{"id":"RAW_QGOH_PS_2:P0059","trace_type":"Composite","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"},{"id":"RAW_QGOH_PS_2:P0068","trace_type":"Composite","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_QGOH_PS_2:A6","text":"Corpus humanum est Individuum valde compositum, a plurimis corporibus externis afficitur, plurimis corporibus ad conservationem indiget, atque ipsa externa corpora disponere potest.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_QGOH_PS_2:P0031","trace_type":"Composite","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"},{"id":"RAW_QGOH_PS_2:P0033","trace_type":"Composite","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"},{"id":"RAW_QGOH_PS_2:P0034","trace_type":"Composite","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"},{"id":"RAW_QGOH_PS_2:P0035","trace_type":"Composite","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_QGOH_PS_2:A7","text":"Mens humana est idea Corporis humani, atque Corpus et Mens unum idemque Individuum sub attributis Extensionis et Cogitationis concipiuntur.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_QGOH_PS_2:P0043","trace_type":"Composite","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"},{"id":"RAW_QGOH_PS_2:P0083","trace_type":"Composite","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"},{"id":"RAW_QGOH_PS_2:P0087","trace_type":"Composite","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_QGOH_PS_2:A8","text":"Quidquid in Corpore humano contingit, Mens humana percipere debet.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_QGOH_PS_2:P0039","trace_type":"Direct","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_QGOH_PS_2:A9","text":"Idea affectionis Corporis humani naturam Corporis humani simul et naturam corporis externi involvit, sed constitutionem Corporis humani magis indicat quam naturam externi corporis per se.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_QGOH_PS_2:P0045","trace_type":"Composite","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"},{"id":"RAW_QGOH_PS_2:P0051","trace_type":"Composite","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_QGOH_PS_2:A10","text":"Ordo et connexio idearum idem est ac ordo et connexio causarum; idea cuiusque rei secundum contextum causarum a quo pendet determinatur.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_QGOH_PS_2:P0070","trace_type":"Composite","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"},{"id":"RAW_QGOH_PS_2:P0076","trace_type":"Composite","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"},{"id":"RAW_QGOH_PS_2:P0081","trace_type":"Composite","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]}],"original_name":"Load-Bearing Axioms"},{"number":"3","name":"Apophatic Structure","items":[{"id":"CH_QGOH_PS_2:NX1","text":"Mutatio motus in quietem aut quietis in motum non ex solo statu praesenti corporis sequitur, sed causam externam requirit.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_QGOH_PS_2:P0001","trace_type":"Negative","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_QGOH_PS_2:NX2","text":"Identitas Individui non est identitas numerica partium componentium nec immobilitas figurae, magnitudinis aut situs.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_QGOH_PS_2:P0012","trace_type":"Negative","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"},{"id":"RAW_QGOH_PS_2:P0016","trace_type":"Negative","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"},{"id":"RAW_QGOH_PS_2:P0020","trace_type":"Negative","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"},{"id":"RAW_QGOH_PS_2:P0024","trace_type":"Negative","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_QGOH_PS_2:NX3","text":"Corpus externum non ut res immediate data percipitur, sed tantum quatenus Corpus humanum ab eo afficitur.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_QGOH_PS_2:P0045","trace_type":"Negative","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"},{"id":"RAW_QGOH_PS_2:P0107","trace_type":"Negative","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"},{"id":"RAW_QGOH_PS_2:P0109","trace_type":"Negative","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_QGOH_PS_2:NX4","text":"Imaginatio in se spectata error non est; error est privatio ideae quae existentiam rei imaginatae secludat.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_QGOH_PS_2:P0063","trace_type":"Direct","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"},{"id":"RAW_QGOH_PS_2:P0064","trace_type":"Direct","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_QGOH_PS_2:NX5","text":"Memoria non est concatenatio idearum secundum ordinem intellectus et primas causas, sed secundum ordinem affectionum Corporis humani.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_QGOH_PS_2:P0070","trace_type":"Direct","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_QGOH_PS_2:NX6","text":"Mens nec Corpus suum, nec partes eius, nec corpora externa, nec se ipsam cognitione immediata et adaequata cognoscit.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_QGOH_PS_2:P0074","trace_type":"Composite","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"},{"id":"RAW_QGOH_PS_2:P0093","trace_type":"Composite","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"},{"id":"RAW_QGOH_PS_2:P0098","trace_type":"Composite","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"},{"id":"RAW_QGOH_PS_2:P0103","trace_type":"Composite","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"},{"id":"RAW_QGOH_PS_2:P0115","trace_type":"Composite","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]}],"original_name":"Apophatic Structure"},{"number":"4","name":"Derivative Propositions","items":[{"id":"CH_QGOH_PS_2:D1","text":"Mens eo plura percipere potest quo Corpus humanum pluribus modis disponi et affici potest.","refs":["CH_QGOH_PS_2:A6","CH_QGOH_PS_2:A8"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"From","passages":[{"id":"RAW_QGOH_PS_2:P0037","trace_type":"Direct","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"},{"id":"RAW_QGOH_PS_2:P0039","trace_type":"Direct","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_QGOH_PS_2:D2","text":"Idea quae esse formale Mentis constituit composita est, quia Corpus cuius idea est ex plurimis Individuis compositis constat.","refs":["CH_QGOH_PS_2:A3","CH_QGOH_PS_2:A6","CH_QGOH_PS_2:A7","CH_QGOH_PS_2:A10"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"From","passages":[{"id":"RAW_QGOH_PS_2:P0041","trace_type":"Direct","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"},{"id":"RAW_QGOH_PS_2:P0043","trace_type":"Direct","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_QGOH_PS_2:D3","text":"Perceptio cuiuslibet affectionis Corporis humani simul externum corpus et constitutionem proprii corporis exprimit, sed externum non secundum naturam suam independentem.","refs":["CH_QGOH_PS_2:A2","CH_QGOH_PS_2:A8","CH_QGOH_PS_2:A9"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"From","passages":[{"id":"RAW_QGOH_PS_2:P0045","trace_type":"Direct","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"},{"id":"RAW_QGOH_PS_2:P0051","trace_type":"Direct","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_QGOH_PS_2:D4","text":"Mens corpus externum ut praesens contemplatur quamdiu affectio Corporis humani existentiam vel praesentiam illius corporis non secludit.","refs":["CH_QGOH_PS_2:A8","CH_QGOH_PS_2:A9"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"From","passages":[{"id":"RAW_QGOH_PS_2:P0053","trace_type":"Direct","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"},{"id":"RAW_QGOH_PS_2:P0055","trace_type":"Direct","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_QGOH_PS_2:D5","text":"Vestigium corporale repetitum efficit ut Mens corpus externum iam absens ut praesens imaginari possit.","refs":["CH_QGOH_PS_2:A5","CH_QGOH_PS_2:A8","CH_QGOH_PS_2:A9"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"From","passages":[{"id":"RAW_QGOH_PS_2:P0057","trace_type":"Direct","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"},{"id":"RAW_QGOH_PS_2:P0059","trace_type":"Direct","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_QGOH_PS_2:D6","text":"Affectus olim simul dati concatenationem imaginativam efficiunt, ita ut imaginatio unius rei memoriam alterius excitet secundum consuetudinem corporis.","refs":["CH_QGOH_PS_2:A5","CH_QGOH_PS_2:A6","CH_QGOH_PS_2:A8"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"From","passages":[{"id":"RAW_QGOH_PS_2:P0066","trace_type":"Composite","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"},{"id":"RAW_QGOH_PS_2:P0068","trace_type":"Composite","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"},{"id":"RAW_QGOH_PS_2:P0071","trace_type":"Composite","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_QGOH_PS_2:D7","text":"Mens Corpus suum et corpora externa existentia cognoscit tantum per ideas affectionum sui Corporis.","refs":["CH_QGOH_PS_2:A7","CH_QGOH_PS_2:A8","CH_QGOH_PS_2:A9"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"From","passages":[{"id":"RAW_QGOH_PS_2:P0074","trace_type":"Composite","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"},{"id":"RAW_QGOH_PS_2:P0107","trace_type":"Composite","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_QGOH_PS_2:D8","text":"Mens affectiones Corporis et ideas earum percipit; ideo se ipsam cognoscit tantum quatenus ideas affectionum percipit.","refs":["CH_QGOH_PS_2:A7","CH_QGOH_PS_2:A8","CH_QGOH_PS_2:A10"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"From","passages":[{"id":"RAW_QGOH_PS_2:P0089","trace_type":"Direct","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"},{"id":"RAW_QGOH_PS_2:P0093","trace_type":"Direct","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_QGOH_PS_2:D9","text":"Idea affectionis, quatenus ad Mentem humanam tantum refertur, cognitionem adaequatam nec Corporis humani, nec partium eius, nec corporis externi involvit; ideo est confusa.","refs":["CH_QGOH_PS_2:A7","CH_QGOH_PS_2:A9","CH_QGOH_PS_2:A10"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"From","passages":[{"id":"RAW_QGOH_PS_2:P0098","trace_type":"Composite","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"},{"id":"RAW_QGOH_PS_2:P0103","trace_type":"Composite","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"},{"id":"RAW_QGOH_PS_2:P0115","trace_type":"Composite","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"},{"id":"RAW_QGOH_PS_2:P0119","trace_type":"Composite","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"},{"id":"RAW_QGOH_PS_2:P0121","trace_type":"Composite","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]}],"original_name":"Derivative Propositions"},{"number":"5","name":"Super-Derivatives","items":[{"id":"CH_QGOH_PS_2:SD1","text":"Identitas rerum corporalium est stabilitas relationis communicativae per mutationem, non persistentia materiae; individuum est forma dynamica quae substitutionem et variationem tolerat.","refs":["CH_QGOH_PS_2:A3","CH_QGOH_PS_2:A4"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"From","passages":[{"id":"RAW_QGOH_PS_2:P0008","trace_type":"Structural","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"},{"id":"RAW_QGOH_PS_2:P0012","trace_type":"Structural","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"},{"id":"RAW_QGOH_PS_2:P0016","trace_type":"Structural","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"},{"id":"RAW_QGOH_PS_2:P0024","trace_type":"Structural","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_QGOH_PS_2:SD2","text":"Eadem apertio corporis ad plures affectiones simul potentiam perceptivam auget et cognitionem ad constitutionem corporis recipientis alligat; extensio experientiae non transit ipso facto in adaequationem.","refs":["CH_QGOH_PS_2:A6","CH_QGOH_PS_2:A8","CH_QGOH_PS_2:A9","CH_QGOH_PS_2:A10"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"From","passages":[{"id":"RAW_QGOH_PS_2:P0037","trace_type":"Composite","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"},{"id":"RAW_QGOH_PS_2:P0045","trace_type":"Composite","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"},{"id":"RAW_QGOH_PS_2:P0051","trace_type":"Composite","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"},{"id":"RAW_QGOH_PS_2:P0098","trace_type":"Composite","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"},{"id":"RAW_QGOH_PS_2:P0103","trace_type":"Composite","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_QGOH_PS_2:SD3","text":"Imaginatio et memoria sunt temporalis perseverantia historiae affectionum corporis, qua praeteritum in praesentia mentis recurrit sine praesentia rei externae.","refs":["CH_QGOH_PS_2:A5","CH_QGOH_PS_2:A8","CH_QGOH_PS_2:A9"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"From","passages":[{"id":"RAW_QGOH_PS_2:P0057","trace_type":"Structural","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"},{"id":"RAW_QGOH_PS_2:P0059","trace_type":"Structural","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"},{"id":"RAW_QGOH_PS_2:P0066","trace_type":"Structural","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"},{"id":"RAW_QGOH_PS_2:P0070","trace_type":"Structural","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_QGOH_PS_2:SD4","text":"Reflexio mentis in se ipsam non instituit subiectum a corpore separatum, sed cognitionem secundi ordinis quae ex ideis affectionum corporalium procedit.","refs":["CH_QGOH_PS_2:A7","CH_QGOH_PS_2:A8","CH_QGOH_PS_2:A10"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"From","passages":[{"id":"RAW_QGOH_PS_2:P0083","trace_type":"Composite","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"},{"id":"RAW_QGOH_PS_2:P0087","trace_type":"Composite","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"},{"id":"RAW_QGOH_PS_2:P0089","trace_type":"Composite","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"},{"id":"RAW_QGOH_PS_2:P0093","trace_type":"Composite","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]}],"original_name":"Super-Derivatives"},{"number":"6","name":"Deep Apophatics","items":[{"id":"CH_QGOH_PS_2:DNX1","text":"Systema negat Mentem finitam posse esse centrum cognitionis corpore et affectionibus independens.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_QGOH_PS_2:P0074","trace_type":"Negative","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"},{"id":"RAW_QGOH_PS_2:P0093","trace_type":"Negative","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"},{"id":"RAW_QGOH_PS_2:P0107","trace_type":"Negative","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_QGOH_PS_2:DNX2","text":"Systema negat corpus esse rem cuius essentia in particulis permanentibus aut in figura invariabili consistat.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_QGOH_PS_2:P0012","trace_type":"Negative","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"},{"id":"RAW_QGOH_PS_2:P0016","trace_type":"Negative","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"},{"id":"RAW_QGOH_PS_2:P0024","trace_type":"Negative","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_QGOH_PS_2:DNX3","text":"Systema negat imaginem esse similitudinem figurativam rei externae; imago est idea affectionis quae externum velut praesens repraesentat.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_QGOH_PS_2:P0063","trace_type":"Negative","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_QGOH_PS_2:DNX4","text":"Systema negat concatenationes memoriae ex similitudine rerum aut ex ordine universali intellectus necessario nasci.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_QGOH_PS_2:P0070","trace_type":"Negative","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"},{"id":"RAW_QGOH_PS_2:P0071","trace_type":"Negative","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]}],"original_name":"Deep Apophatics"},{"number":"7","name":"Super-Apophatics","items":[{"id":"CH_QGOH_PS_2:SNX1","text":"Mentis finitae perspectivam extra historiam affectionum proprii corporis positam systema non admittit.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_QGOH_PS_2:P0045","trace_type":"Negative","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"},{"id":"RAW_QGOH_PS_2:P0070","trace_type":"Negative","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"},{"id":"RAW_QGOH_PS_2:P0074","trace_type":"Negative","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"},{"id":"RAW_QGOH_PS_2:P0093","trace_type":"Negative","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"},{"id":"RAW_QGOH_PS_2:P0107","trace_type":"Negative","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_QGOH_PS_2:SNX2","text":"Imaginatio per se absentiam causarum quae rem externam excludant supplere non potest; praesentia imaginata non constituit existentiam rei.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_QGOH_PS_2:P0053","trace_type":"Negative","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"},{"id":"RAW_QGOH_PS_2:P0057","trace_type":"Negative","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"},{"id":"RAW_QGOH_PS_2:P0063","trace_type":"Negative","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"},{"id":"RAW_QGOH_PS_2:P0111","trace_type":"Negative","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_QGOH_PS_2:SNX3","text":"Cognitio corporis externi ex sola affectione non potest naturam externi corporis ut per se et in suo ordine causali constitutam exhaurire.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_QGOH_PS_2:P0051","trace_type":"Negative","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"},{"id":"RAW_QGOH_PS_2:P0103","trace_type":"Negative","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"},{"id":"RAW_QGOH_PS_2:P0105","trace_type":"Negative","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"},{"id":"RAW_QGOH_PS_2:P0111","trace_type":"Negative","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]}],"original_name":"Super-Apophatics"},{"number":"8","name":"Formal Generative Schema","items":[{"id":"CH_QGOH_PS_2:FGS1","text":"Perseverantia motus et quietis → necessitas determinationis externae ad omnem mutationem status.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_QGOH_PS_2:P0001","trace_type":"Direct","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_QGOH_PS_2:FGS2","text":"Communicatio motuum certa ratione → constitutio Individui → conservatio identitatis per substitutionem et variationem partium.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_QGOH_PS_2:P0008","trace_type":"Composite","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"},{"id":"RAW_QGOH_PS_2:P0012","trace_type":"Composite","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"},{"id":"RAW_QGOH_PS_2:P0016","trace_type":"Composite","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"},{"id":"RAW_QGOH_PS_2:P0024","trace_type":"Composite","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_QGOH_PS_2:FGS3","text":"Compositio multiplicis Corporis humani et dependentia eius ab externis → pluralitas affectionum, dispositionum atque vestigiorum.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_QGOH_PS_2:P0031","trace_type":"Composite","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"},{"id":"RAW_QGOH_PS_2:P0032","trace_type":"Composite","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"},{"id":"RAW_QGOH_PS_2:P0033","trace_type":"Composite","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"},{"id":"RAW_QGOH_PS_2:P0035","trace_type":"Composite","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_QGOH_PS_2:FGS4","text":"Mens ut idea Corporis → perceptio necessariа affectionum Corporis → capacitas perceptiva proportionalis dispositionibus corporis.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_QGOH_PS_2:P0037","trace_type":"Composite","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"},{"id":"RAW_QGOH_PS_2:P0043","trace_type":"Composite","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"},{"id":"RAW_QGOH_PS_2:P0074","trace_type":"Composite","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_QGOH_PS_2:FGS5","text":"Affectio corporis ab externo → idea quae naturam utriusque corporis involvit → praesentatio externi per constitutionem proprii corporis.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_QGOH_PS_2:P0003","trace_type":"Direct","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"},{"id":"RAW_QGOH_PS_2:P0045","trace_type":"Direct","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"},{"id":"RAW_QGOH_PS_2:P0051","trace_type":"Direct","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_QGOH_PS_2:FGS6","text":"Vestigium corporale perseverans → repetitio affectionis → imaginatio rei absentis ut praesentis.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_QGOH_PS_2:P0035","trace_type":"Composite","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"},{"id":"RAW_QGOH_PS_2:P0057","trace_type":"Composite","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"},{"id":"RAW_QGOH_PS_2:P0059","trace_type":"Composite","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_QGOH_PS_2:FGS7","text":"Simultaneitas affectionum et consuetudo corporis → concatenatio imaginativa → memoria singulariter ordinata.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_QGOH_PS_2:P0066","trace_type":"Composite","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"},{"id":"RAW_QGOH_PS_2:P0070","trace_type":"Composite","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"},{"id":"RAW_QGOH_PS_2:P0071","trace_type":"Composite","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_QGOH_PS_2:FGS8","text":"Mediatio cognitionis per affectiones → absentia cognitionis causalis partium et externorum → confusio idearum; ideae affectionum perceptae → cognitio sui mediata.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_QGOH_PS_2:P0074","trace_type":"Structural","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"},{"id":"RAW_QGOH_PS_2:P0093","trace_type":"Structural","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"},{"id":"RAW_QGOH_PS_2:P0098","trace_type":"Structural","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"},{"id":"RAW_QGOH_PS_2:P0103","trace_type":"Structural","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"},{"id":"RAW_QGOH_PS_2:P0119","trace_type":"Structural","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"},{"id":"RAW_QGOH_PS_2:P0121","trace_type":"Structural","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]}],"original_name":"Formal Generative Schema"},{"number":"9","name":"Argument Flow Map","items":[{"id":"CH_QGOH_PS_2:AF1","text":"Perseverantia motus et quietis → causa externa mutationis → relatio motuum → Individuum compositum → identitas per rationem communicationis.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_QGOH_PS_2:P0001","trace_type":"Structural","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"},{"id":"RAW_QGOH_PS_2:P0008","trace_type":"Structural","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"},{"id":"RAW_QGOH_PS_2:P0012","trace_type":"Structural","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"},{"id":"RAW_QGOH_PS_2:P0024","trace_type":"Structural","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_QGOH_PS_2:AF2","text":"Corpus humanum multiplex et externis expositum → affectiones plurimae → Mens ut idea Corporis → perceptio plurium proportionalis dispositioni corporis.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_QGOH_PS_2:P0031","trace_type":"Structural","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"},{"id":"RAW_QGOH_PS_2:P0033","trace_type":"Structural","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"},{"id":"RAW_QGOH_PS_2:P0037","trace_type":"Structural","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"},{"id":"RAW_QGOH_PS_2:P0041","trace_type":"Structural","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_QGOH_PS_2:AF3","text":"Affectio a corpore externo → idea utriusque naturae → praesentia externa condicionata → vestigium repetitum → imaginatio et memoria.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_QGOH_PS_2:P0045","trace_type":"Structural","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"},{"id":"RAW_QGOH_PS_2:P0057","trace_type":"Structural","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"},{"id":"RAW_QGOH_PS_2:P0059","trace_type":"Structural","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"},{"id":"RAW_QGOH_PS_2:P0066","trace_type":"Structural","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"},{"id":"RAW_QGOH_PS_2:P0070","trace_type":"Structural","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_QGOH_PS_2:AF4","text":"Cognitio per affectiones → cognitio mediata Corporis, externorum et Mentis → defectus contextus causarum → inadaequatio et confusio.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_QGOH_PS_2:P0074","trace_type":"Structural","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"},{"id":"RAW_QGOH_PS_2:P0093","trace_type":"Structural","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"},{"id":"RAW_QGOH_PS_2:P0098","trace_type":"Structural","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"},{"id":"RAW_QGOH_PS_2:P0103","trace_type":"Structural","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"},{"id":"RAW_QGOH_PS_2:P0119","trace_type":"Structural","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"},{"id":"RAW_QGOH_PS_2:P0121","trace_type":"Structural","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]}],"original_name":"Argument Flow Map"},{"number":"10","name":"Map-Effects","items":[{"id":"CH_QGOH_PS_2:ME1","text":"Corpus reclassificatur ex aggregato materiae in individuam relationem motus et quietis, quae mutationes partium sine identitatis dissolutione sustinet.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_QGOH_PS_2:P0008","trace_type":"Composite","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"},{"id":"RAW_QGOH_PS_2:P0012","trace_type":"Composite","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"},{"id":"RAW_QGOH_PS_2:P0016","trace_type":"Composite","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"},{"id":"RAW_QGOH_PS_2:P0024","trace_type":"Composite","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_QGOH_PS_2:ME2","text":"Externum corpus fit obiectum quod in idea affectionis simul apparet et tamen per constitutionem Corporis humani indicatur.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_QGOH_PS_2:P0045","trace_type":"Direct","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"},{"id":"RAW_QGOH_PS_2:P0051","trace_type":"Direct","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_QGOH_PS_2:ME3","text":"Imaginatio reclassificatur ex cognitione falsa in praesentiam idealem ex vestigiis corporalibus ortam; falsitas non est forma imaginationis, sed defectus ideae secludentis.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_QGOH_PS_2:P0063","trace_type":"Direct","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"},{"id":"RAW_QGOH_PS_2:P0064","trace_type":"Direct","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_QGOH_PS_2:ME4","text":"Memoria reclassificatur ex thesauro imaginum in concatenationem idearum corporeis consuetudinibus ordinatam.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_QGOH_PS_2:P0070","trace_type":"Direct","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"},{"id":"RAW_QGOH_PS_2:P0071","trace_type":"Direct","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_QGOH_PS_2:ME5","text":"Cognitio sui reclassificatur ex intuitu interiori in perceptionem reflexivam idearum affectionum Corporis.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_QGOH_PS_2:P0089","trace_type":"Composite","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"},{"id":"RAW_QGOH_PS_2:P0093","trace_type":"Composite","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"},{"id":"RAW_QGOH_PS_2:P0095","trace_type":"Composite","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_QGOH_PS_2:ME6","text":"Oppositio inter experientiam multiplicem et cognitionem adaequatam dissolvitur: perceptio plurium potest crescere, dum cognitio causarum partium et externorum manet inadaequata.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_QGOH_PS_2:P0037","trace_type":"Tension","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"},{"id":"RAW_QGOH_PS_2:P0098","trace_type":"Tension","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"},{"id":"RAW_QGOH_PS_2:P0103","trace_type":"Tension","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"},{"id":"RAW_QGOH_PS_2:P0119","trace_type":"Tension","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]}],"original_name":"Map-Effects"},{"number":"11","name":"Altitude Levels","items":[{"id":"CH_QGOH_PS_2:ALT1","text":"Gradus superficiei: Mens plurima percipit, corpora externa imaginatur, absentia memorat, Corpus suum sentit, et se ipsam percipit.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_QGOH_PS_2:P0037","trace_type":"Composite","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"},{"id":"RAW_QGOH_PS_2:P0057","trace_type":"Composite","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"},{"id":"RAW_QGOH_PS_2:P0074","trace_type":"Composite","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"},{"id":"RAW_QGOH_PS_2:P0093","trace_type":"Composite","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_QGOH_PS_2:ALT2","text":"Gradus structuralis: effectus mentales generantur ex compositione corporis, ex reciproca affectione corporum, ex vestigiis fluidorum et ex ordine affectionum.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_QGOH_PS_2:P0003","trace_type":"Structural","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"},{"id":"RAW_QGOH_PS_2:P0008","trace_type":"Structural","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"},{"id":"RAW_QGOH_PS_2:P0035","trace_type":"Structural","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"},{"id":"RAW_QGOH_PS_2:P0045","trace_type":"Structural","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"},{"id":"RAW_QGOH_PS_2:P0070","trace_type":"Structural","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_QGOH_PS_2:ALT3","text":"Gradus metastrategicus: quaestio de veritate perceptionis transfertur ab apparenti similitudine rei ad conditiones corporales et causales sub quibus idea producitur.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_QGOH_PS_2:P0051","trace_type":"Structural","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"},{"id":"RAW_QGOH_PS_2:P0063","trace_type":"Structural","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"},{"id":"RAW_QGOH_PS_2:P0105","trace_type":"Structural","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"},{"id":"RAW_QGOH_PS_2:P0113","trace_type":"Structural","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_QGOH_PS_2:ALT4","text":"Gradus ontologicus et temporalis: Individua sunt rationes motus per variationem perseverantes, et Mens finita historiam corporis sui in imaginando et memorando repetit.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_QGOH_PS_2:P0012","trace_type":"Structural","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"},{"id":"RAW_QGOH_PS_2:P0024","trace_type":"Structural","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"},{"id":"RAW_QGOH_PS_2:P0057","trace_type":"Structural","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"},{"id":"RAW_QGOH_PS_2:P0070","trace_type":"Structural","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]}],"original_name":"Altitude Levels"},{"number":"12","name":"Minimal Reconstruction Test","items":[{"id":"CH_QGOH_PS_2:MRT1","text":"Axiomata extracta sufficiunt ad reconstruendum transitum a physica mutationis et individuationis ad doctrinam affectionis, imaginationis, memoriae et cognitionis mediatae.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_QGOH_PS_2:P0001","trace_type":"Structural","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"},{"id":"RAW_QGOH_PS_2:P0008","trace_type":"Structural","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"},{"id":"RAW_QGOH_PS_2:P0031","trace_type":"Structural","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"},{"id":"RAW_QGOH_PS_2:P0045","trace_type":"Structural","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"},{"id":"RAW_QGOH_PS_2:P0057","trace_type":"Structural","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"},{"id":"RAW_QGOH_PS_2:P0070","trace_type":"Structural","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"},{"id":"RAW_QGOH_PS_2:P0119","trace_type":"Structural","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_QGOH_PS_2:MRT2","text":"Commissura firmissima est nexus inter naturam relationalem Individui, compositam constitutionem Corporis humani, et ideam affectionis quae utrumque corpus involvit.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_QGOH_PS_2:P0008","trace_type":"Structural","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"},{"id":"RAW_QGOH_PS_2:P0012","trace_type":"Structural","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"},{"id":"RAW_QGOH_PS_2:P0031","trace_type":"Structural","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"},{"id":"RAW_QGOH_PS_2:P0043","trace_type":"Structural","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"},{"id":"RAW_QGOH_PS_2:P0045","trace_type":"Structural","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_QGOH_PS_2:MRT3","text":"Systema localiter pendet a principiis antea positis, praesertim a unitate Mentis et Corporis, a perceptione omnium affectionum, et ab identitate ordinis idearum cum ordine causarum.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_QGOH_PS_2:P0043","trace_type":"Structural","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"},{"id":"RAW_QGOH_PS_2:P0074","trace_type":"Structural","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"},{"id":"RAW_QGOH_PS_2:P0081","trace_type":"Structural","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"},{"id":"RAW_QGOH_PS_2:P0087","trace_type":"Structural","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"},{"id":"RAW_QGOH_PS_2:P0093","trace_type":"Structural","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_QGOH_PS_2:MRT4","text":"Transitus a natura singulorum compositorum ad Naturam totam ut unum Individuum proponitur ut facile concipiendus, sed ipse textus demonstrationem ampliorem consulto suspendit.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_QGOH_PS_2:P0028","trace_type":"Direct","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"},{"id":"RAW_QGOH_PS_2:P0029","trace_type":"Direct","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_QGOH_PS_2:MRT5","text":"Explicatio imaginationis per vestigia corporalia unam causam sufficientem exhibet, sed Scholium concedit rem etiam ex aliis causis contingere posse; exclusivitas causalis non demonstratur.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_QGOH_PS_2:P0059","trace_type":"Direct","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"},{"id":"RAW_QGOH_PS_2:P0062","trace_type":"Direct","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_QGOH_PS_2:MRT6","text":"Tensionem apparentem inter maiorem facultatem percipiendi et persistentem inadaequationem systema resolvit distinguendo pluralitatem affectionum a cognitione causarum earum.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_QGOH_PS_2:P0037","trace_type":"Tension","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"},{"id":"RAW_QGOH_PS_2:P0098","trace_type":"Tension","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"},{"id":"RAW_QGOH_PS_2:P0103","trace_type":"Tension","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"},{"id":"RAW_QGOH_PS_2:P0119","trace_type":"Tension","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_QGOH_PS_2:MRT7","text":"Doctrina cognitionis sui immediate sustinet tantum perceptionem reflexivam idearum affectionum; ulterior progressio ideae ideae in infinitum a textu ad tractationem posteriorem differtur.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_QGOH_PS_2:P0087","trace_type":"Composite","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"},{"id":"RAW_QGOH_PS_2:P0089","trace_type":"Composite","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"},{"id":"RAW_QGOH_PS_2:P0093","trace_type":"Composite","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"},{"id":"RAW_QGOH_PS_2:P0095","trace_type":"Composite","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]}],"original_name":"Minimal Reconstruction Test"}],"language_code":"la","language_name":"Latina","translations":[{"id":"CAREVAR_20261015T015014219104Z_2cdb6d05","language_code":"en","language_name":"English","title":"Part Two - On the Nature and Origin of the Mind · passage 2","source_sha256":"41e6270bc187ab1f843c2b89865abc6c5aa40e7651542c62637fa0f1fa2aa766","sha256":"7648b95df2c068426640b4186e90b00aa0e9413391bbee2a8ee2c840b57bf340","rendition_type":"machine","review_status":"machine","model":"gpt-5.6-terra","created":"2026-10-15T01:50:14.219982+00:00","sections":[{"number":"1","name":"Argumental Spine","items":[{"id":"CH_QGOH_PS_2:AS1","text":"The text asks how, from the constitution of the motions of the composite body, the human Mind can perceive, imagine, remember, and know itself.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_QGOH_PS_2:P0037","trace_type":"Structural","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"},{"id":"RAW_QGOH_PS_2:P0057","trace_type":"Structural","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"},{"id":"RAW_QGOH_PS_2:P0070","trace_type":"Structural","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"},{"id":"RAW_QGOH_PS_2:P0074","trace_type":"Structural","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"},{"id":"RAW_QGOH_PS_2:P0093","trace_type":"Structural","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_QGOH_PS_2:AS2","text":"Knowledge of the Mind arises not from immediate access to things, the Body, or itself, but from ideas of affections by which the human Body is determined together with external bodies.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_QGOH_PS_2:P0045","trace_type":"Composite","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"},{"id":"RAW_QGOH_PS_2:P0074","trace_type":"Composite","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"},{"id":"RAW_QGOH_PS_2:P0107","trace_type":"Composite","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_QGOH_PS_2:AS3","text":"The argument is physico-genetic: it ascends from the persistence of motion and rest, through the relational individuation of the body, to the conditions of knowledge and confusion.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_QGOH_PS_2:P0001","trace_type":"Structural","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"},{"id":"RAW_QGOH_PS_2:P0008","trace_type":"Structural","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"},{"id":"RAW_QGOH_PS_2:P0031","trace_type":"Structural","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"},{"id":"RAW_QGOH_PS_2:P0045","trace_type":"Structural","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"},{"id":"RAW_QGOH_PS_2:P0057","trace_type":"Structural","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"},{"id":"RAW_QGOH_PS_2:P0074","trace_type":"Structural","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"},{"id":"RAW_QGOH_PS_2:P0119","trace_type":"Structural","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_QGOH_PS_2:AS4","text":"The identity of the body consists in a determinate ratio of motion and rest among its parts, not in the material identity of the parts, figure, magnitude, or local motion of the whole.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_QGOH_PS_2:P0008","trace_type":"Composite","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"},{"id":"RAW_QGOH_PS_2:P0012","trace_type":"Composite","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"},{"id":"RAW_QGOH_PS_2:P0016","trace_type":"Composite","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"},{"id":"RAW_QGOH_PS_2:P0020","trace_type":"Composite","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"},{"id":"RAW_QGOH_PS_2:P0024","trace_type":"Composite","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_QGOH_PS_2:AS5","text":"An affection of the body involves at once the nature of the affected body and of the affecting body; therefore perception of the external is a presentation of the external through the disposition of one's own body.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_QGOH_PS_2:P0003","trace_type":"Composite","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"},{"id":"RAW_QGOH_PS_2:P0045","trace_type":"Composite","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"},{"id":"RAW_QGOH_PS_2:P0051","trace_type":"Composite","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_QGOH_PS_2:AS6","text":"The progression concludes that imagination and memory are effects of bodily traces and concatenations, while knowledge of the body, of external things, and of oneself is partial and, insofar as it rests solely on such affections, confused.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_QGOH_PS_2:P0057","trace_type":"Structural","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"},{"id":"RAW_QGOH_PS_2:P0063","trace_type":"Structural","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"},{"id":"RAW_QGOH_PS_2:P0070","trace_type":"Structural","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"},{"id":"RAW_QGOH_PS_2:P0119","trace_type":"Structural","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"},{"id":"RAW_QGOH_PS_2:P0121","trace_type":"Structural","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]}],"original_name":"Argumental Spine"},{"number":"2","name":"Load-Bearing Axioms","items":[{"id":"CH_QGOH_PS_2:A1","text":"A moving body persists in moving, and a resting body in resting, unless an external cause determines it to the contrary state.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_QGOH_PS_2:P0001","trace_type":"Direct","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_QGOH_PS_2:A2","text":"Every mode by which a body is affected by another body follows at once from the nature of the affected body and of the affecting body.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_QGOH_PS_2:P0003","trace_type":"Direct","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_QGOH_PS_2:A3","text":"A corporeal individual is a union of bodies which communicate their motions to one another according to a determinate ratio.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_QGOH_PS_2:P0008","trace_type":"Direct","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_QGOH_PS_2:A4","text":"The nature of an Individual remains so long as the ratio of communication of motion and rest among its parts remains, even if the parts are replaced, change in magnitude, alter direction, or the whole Individual is moved.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_QGOH_PS_2:P0012","trace_type":"Composite","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"},{"id":"RAW_QGOH_PS_2:P0016","trace_type":"Composite","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"},{"id":"RAW_QGOH_PS_2:P0020","trace_type":"Composite","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"},{"id":"RAW_QGOH_PS_2:P0024","trace_type":"Composite","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_QGOH_PS_2:A5","text":"Traces of external bodies can leave in the fluid parts of the human Body dispositions that produce subsequent reflex motions of the same kind.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_QGOH_PS_2:P0035","trace_type":"Composite","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"},{"id":"RAW_QGOH_PS_2:P0059","trace_type":"Composite","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"},{"id":"RAW_QGOH_PS_2:P0068","trace_type":"Composite","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_QGOH_PS_2:A6","text":"The human Body is a highly composite Individual, is affected by very many external bodies, requires very many bodies for its preservation, and can itself dispose external bodies.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_QGOH_PS_2:P0031","trace_type":"Composite","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"},{"id":"RAW_QGOH_PS_2:P0033","trace_type":"Composite","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"},{"id":"RAW_QGOH_PS_2:P0034","trace_type":"Composite","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"},{"id":"RAW_QGOH_PS_2:P0035","trace_type":"Composite","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_QGOH_PS_2:A7","text":"The human Mind is the idea of the human Body, and Body and Mind are conceived as one and the same Individual under the attributes of Extension and Thought.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_QGOH_PS_2:P0043","trace_type":"Composite","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"},{"id":"RAW_QGOH_PS_2:P0083","trace_type":"Composite","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"},{"id":"RAW_QGOH_PS_2:P0087","trace_type":"Composite","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_QGOH_PS_2:A8","text":"Whatever occurs in the human Body, the human Mind must perceive.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_QGOH_PS_2:P0039","trace_type":"Direct","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_QGOH_PS_2:A9","text":"The idea of an affection of the human Body involves at once the nature of the human Body and the nature of the external body, but indicates the constitution of the human Body more than the nature of the external body in itself.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_QGOH_PS_2:P0045","trace_type":"Composite","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"},{"id":"RAW_QGOH_PS_2:P0051","trace_type":"Composite","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_QGOH_PS_2:A10","text":"The order and connection of ideas is the same as the order and connection of causes; the idea of each thing is determined according to the context of causes on which it depends.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_QGOH_PS_2:P0070","trace_type":"Composite","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"},{"id":"RAW_QGOH_PS_2:P0076","trace_type":"Composite","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"},{"id":"RAW_QGOH_PS_2:P0081","trace_type":"Composite","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]}],"original_name":"Load-Bearing Axioms"},{"number":"3","name":"Apophatic Structure","items":[{"id":"CH_QGOH_PS_2:NX1","text":"A change from motion to rest or from rest to motion does not follow from the body's present state alone, but requires an external cause.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_QGOH_PS_2:P0001","trace_type":"Negative","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_QGOH_PS_2:NX2","text":"The identity of an Individual is not the numerical identity of its component parts, nor the immobility of its figure, magnitude, or position.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_QGOH_PS_2:P0012","trace_type":"Negative","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"},{"id":"RAW_QGOH_PS_2:P0016","trace_type":"Negative","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"},{"id":"RAW_QGOH_PS_2:P0020","trace_type":"Negative","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"},{"id":"RAW_QGOH_PS_2:P0024","trace_type":"Negative","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_QGOH_PS_2:NX3","text":"An external body is not perceived as an immediately given thing, but only insofar as the human Body is affected by it.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_QGOH_PS_2:P0045","trace_type":"Negative","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"},{"id":"RAW_QGOH_PS_2:P0107","trace_type":"Negative","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"},{"id":"RAW_QGOH_PS_2:P0109","trace_type":"Negative","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_QGOH_PS_2:NX4","text":"Imagination considered in itself is not error; error is the privation of an idea that would exclude the existence of the imagined thing.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_QGOH_PS_2:P0063","trace_type":"Direct","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"},{"id":"RAW_QGOH_PS_2:P0064","trace_type":"Direct","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_QGOH_PS_2:NX5","text":"Memory is not a concatenation of ideas according to the order of the intellect and first causes, but according to the order of the affections of the human Body.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_QGOH_PS_2:P0070","trace_type":"Direct","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_QGOH_PS_2:NX6","text":"The Mind knows neither its own Body, nor its parts, nor external bodies, nor itself by immediate and adequate knowledge.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_QGOH_PS_2:P0074","trace_type":"Composite","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"},{"id":"RAW_QGOH_PS_2:P0093","trace_type":"Composite","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"},{"id":"RAW_QGOH_PS_2:P0098","trace_type":"Composite","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"},{"id":"RAW_QGOH_PS_2:P0103","trace_type":"Composite","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"},{"id":"RAW_QGOH_PS_2:P0115","trace_type":"Composite","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]}],"original_name":"Apophatic Structure"},{"number":"4","name":"Derivative Propositions","items":[{"id":"CH_QGOH_PS_2:D1","text":"The Mind can perceive more things insofar as the human Body can be disposed and affected in more ways.","refs":["CH_QGOH_PS_2:A6","CH_QGOH_PS_2:A8"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"From","passages":[{"id":"RAW_QGOH_PS_2:P0037","trace_type":"Direct","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"},{"id":"RAW_QGOH_PS_2:P0039","trace_type":"Direct","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_QGOH_PS_2:D2","text":"The idea that constitutes the formal being of the Mind is composite, because the Body of which it is the idea consists of very many composite Individuals.","refs":["CH_QGOH_PS_2:A3","CH_QGOH_PS_2:A6","CH_QGOH_PS_2:A7","CH_QGOH_PS_2:A10"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"From","passages":[{"id":"RAW_QGOH_PS_2:P0041","trace_type":"Direct","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"},{"id":"RAW_QGOH_PS_2:P0043","trace_type":"Direct","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_QGOH_PS_2:D3","text":"The perception of any affection of the human Body expresses at once the external body and the constitution of one's own body, but not the external body according to its independent nature.","refs":["CH_QGOH_PS_2:A2","CH_QGOH_PS_2:A8","CH_QGOH_PS_2:A9"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"From","passages":[{"id":"RAW_QGOH_PS_2:P0045","trace_type":"Direct","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"},{"id":"RAW_QGOH_PS_2:P0051","trace_type":"Direct","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_QGOH_PS_2:D4","text":"The Mind contemplates an external body as present so long as an affection of the human Body does not exclude the existence or presence of that body.","refs":["CH_QGOH_PS_2:A8","CH_QGOH_PS_2:A9"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"From","passages":[{"id":"RAW_QGOH_PS_2:P0053","trace_type":"Direct","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"},{"id":"RAW_QGOH_PS_2:P0055","trace_type":"Direct","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_QGOH_PS_2:D5","text":"A repeated bodily trace causes the Mind to be able to imagine an external body, now absent, as present.","refs":["CH_QGOH_PS_2:A5","CH_QGOH_PS_2:A8","CH_QGOH_PS_2:A9"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"From","passages":[{"id":"RAW_QGOH_PS_2:P0057","trace_type":"Direct","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"},{"id":"RAW_QGOH_PS_2:P0059","trace_type":"Direct","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_QGOH_PS_2:D6","text":"Affections once given simultaneously produce an imaginative concatenation, such that the imagination of one thing evokes the memory of another according to the custom of the body.","refs":["CH_QGOH_PS_2:A5","CH_QGOH_PS_2:A6","CH_QGOH_PS_2:A8"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"From","passages":[{"id":"RAW_QGOH_PS_2:P0066","trace_type":"Composite","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"},{"id":"RAW_QGOH_PS_2:P0068","trace_type":"Composite","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"},{"id":"RAW_QGOH_PS_2:P0071","trace_type":"Composite","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_QGOH_PS_2:D7","text":"The Mind knows its own Body and external bodies as existing only through ideas of the affections of its Body.","refs":["CH_QGOH_PS_2:A7","CH_QGOH_PS_2:A8","CH_QGOH_PS_2:A9"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"From","passages":[{"id":"RAW_QGOH_PS_2:P0074","trace_type":"Composite","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"},{"id":"RAW_QGOH_PS_2:P0107","trace_type":"Composite","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_QGOH_PS_2:D8","text":"The Mind perceives the affections of the Body and their ideas; therefore it knows itself only insofar as it perceives ideas of affections.","refs":["CH_QGOH_PS_2:A7","CH_QGOH_PS_2:A8","CH_QGOH_PS_2:A10"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"From","passages":[{"id":"RAW_QGOH_PS_2:P0089","trace_type":"Direct","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"},{"id":"RAW_QGOH_PS_2:P0093","trace_type":"Direct","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_QGOH_PS_2:D9","text":"The idea of an affection, insofar as it is referred only to the human Mind, involves adequate knowledge neither of the human Body, nor of its parts, nor of the external body; therefore it is confused.","refs":["CH_QGOH_PS_2:A7","CH_QGOH_PS_2:A9","CH_QGOH_PS_2:A10"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"From","passages":[{"id":"RAW_QGOH_PS_2:P0098","trace_type":"Composite","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"},{"id":"RAW_QGOH_PS_2:P0103","trace_type":"Composite","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"},{"id":"RAW_QGOH_PS_2:P0115","trace_type":"Composite","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"},{"id":"RAW_QGOH_PS_2:P0119","trace_type":"Composite","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"},{"id":"RAW_QGOH_PS_2:P0121","trace_type":"Composite","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]}],"original_name":"Derivative Propositions"},{"number":"5","name":"Super-Derivatives","items":[{"id":"CH_QGOH_PS_2:SD1","text":"The identity of corporeal things is the stability of a communicative relation through change, not the persistence of matter; an individual is a dynamic form that tolerates substitution and variation.","refs":["CH_QGOH_PS_2:A3","CH_QGOH_PS_2:A4"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"From","passages":[{"id":"RAW_QGOH_PS_2:P0008","trace_type":"Structural","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"},{"id":"RAW_QGOH_PS_2:P0012","trace_type":"Structural","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"},{"id":"RAW_QGOH_PS_2:P0016","trace_type":"Structural","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"},{"id":"RAW_QGOH_PS_2:P0024","trace_type":"Structural","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_QGOH_PS_2:SD2","text":"The same openness of the body to more affections both increases perceptive power and binds knowledge to the constitution of the receiving body; extension of experience does not ipso facto pass into adequacy.","refs":["CH_QGOH_PS_2:A6","CH_QGOH_PS_2:A8","CH_QGOH_PS_2:A9","CH_QGOH_PS_2:A10"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"From","passages":[{"id":"RAW_QGOH_PS_2:P0037","trace_type":"Composite","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"},{"id":"RAW_QGOH_PS_2:P0045","trace_type":"Composite","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"},{"id":"RAW_QGOH_PS_2:P0051","trace_type":"Composite","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"},{"id":"RAW_QGOH_PS_2:P0098","trace_type":"Composite","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"},{"id":"RAW_QGOH_PS_2:P0103","trace_type":"Composite","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_QGOH_PS_2:SD3","text":"Imagination and memory are the temporal persistence of the history of the body's affections, by which the past recurs in the presence of the mind without the presence of the external thing.","refs":["CH_QGOH_PS_2:A5","CH_QGOH_PS_2:A8","CH_QGOH_PS_2:A9"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"From","passages":[{"id":"RAW_QGOH_PS_2:P0057","trace_type":"Structural","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"},{"id":"RAW_QGOH_PS_2:P0059","trace_type":"Structural","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"},{"id":"RAW_QGOH_PS_2:P0066","trace_type":"Structural","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"},{"id":"RAW_QGOH_PS_2:P0070","trace_type":"Structural","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_QGOH_PS_2:SD4","text":"The Mind's reflection upon itself does not establish a subject separate from the body, but a second-order knowledge proceeding from ideas of bodily affections.","refs":["CH_QGOH_PS_2:A7","CH_QGOH_PS_2:A8","CH_QGOH_PS_2:A10"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"From","passages":[{"id":"RAW_QGOH_PS_2:P0083","trace_type":"Composite","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"},{"id":"RAW_QGOH_PS_2:P0087","trace_type":"Composite","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"},{"id":"RAW_QGOH_PS_2:P0089","trace_type":"Composite","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"},{"id":"RAW_QGOH_PS_2:P0093","trace_type":"Composite","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]}],"original_name":"Super-Derivatives"},{"number":"6","name":"Deep Apophatics","items":[{"id":"CH_QGOH_PS_2:DNX1","text":"The system denies that the finite Mind can be a center of knowledge independent of body and affections.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_QGOH_PS_2:P0074","trace_type":"Negative","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"},{"id":"RAW_QGOH_PS_2:P0093","trace_type":"Negative","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"},{"id":"RAW_QGOH_PS_2:P0107","trace_type":"Negative","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_QGOH_PS_2:DNX2","text":"The system denies that the body is a thing whose essence consists in permanent particles or an invariant figure.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_QGOH_PS_2:P0012","trace_type":"Negative","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"},{"id":"RAW_QGOH_PS_2:P0016","trace_type":"Negative","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"},{"id":"RAW_QGOH_PS_2:P0024","trace_type":"Negative","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_QGOH_PS_2:DNX3","text":"The system denies that an image is a figurative likeness of an external thing; an image is an idea of an affection that represents the external as if present.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_QGOH_PS_2:P0063","trace_type":"Negative","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_QGOH_PS_2:DNX4","text":"The system denies that concatenations of memory necessarily arise from the similarity of things or from the universal order of the intellect.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_QGOH_PS_2:P0070","trace_type":"Negative","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"},{"id":"RAW_QGOH_PS_2:P0071","trace_type":"Negative","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]}],"original_name":"Deep Apophatics"},{"number":"7","name":"Super-Apophatics","items":[{"id":"CH_QGOH_PS_2:SNX1","text":"The system does not admit a perspective of the finite Mind situated outside the history of the affections of its own body.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_QGOH_PS_2:P0045","trace_type":"Negative","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"},{"id":"RAW_QGOH_PS_2:P0070","trace_type":"Negative","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"},{"id":"RAW_QGOH_PS_2:P0074","trace_type":"Negative","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"},{"id":"RAW_QGOH_PS_2:P0093","trace_type":"Negative","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"},{"id":"RAW_QGOH_PS_2:P0107","trace_type":"Negative","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_QGOH_PS_2:SNX2","text":"Imagination by itself cannot supply the absence of causes that exclude the external thing; imagined presence does not constitute the existence of the thing.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_QGOH_PS_2:P0053","trace_type":"Negative","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"},{"id":"RAW_QGOH_PS_2:P0057","trace_type":"Negative","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"},{"id":"RAW_QGOH_PS_2:P0063","trace_type":"Negative","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"},{"id":"RAW_QGOH_PS_2:P0111","trace_type":"Negative","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_QGOH_PS_2:SNX3","text":"Knowledge of an external body from affection alone cannot exhaust the nature of the external body as constituted in itself and in its own causal order.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_QGOH_PS_2:P0051","trace_type":"Negative","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"},{"id":"RAW_QGOH_PS_2:P0103","trace_type":"Negative","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"},{"id":"RAW_QGOH_PS_2:P0105","trace_type":"Negative","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"},{"id":"RAW_QGOH_PS_2:P0111","trace_type":"Negative","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]}],"original_name":"Super-Apophatics"},{"number":"8","name":"Formal Generative Schema","items":[{"id":"CH_QGOH_PS_2:FGS1","text":"Persistence of motion and rest → necessity of external determination for every change of state.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_QGOH_PS_2:P0001","trace_type":"Direct","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_QGOH_PS_2:FGS2","text":"Communication of motions according to a determinate ratio → constitution of an Individual → preservation of identity through substitution and variation of parts.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_QGOH_PS_2:P0008","trace_type":"Composite","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"},{"id":"RAW_QGOH_PS_2:P0012","trace_type":"Composite","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"},{"id":"RAW_QGOH_PS_2:P0016","trace_type":"Composite","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"},{"id":"RAW_QGOH_PS_2:P0024","trace_type":"Composite","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_QGOH_PS_2:FGS3","text":"Composition of the multiple human Body and its dependence on external things → plurality of affections, dispositions, and traces.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_QGOH_PS_2:P0031","trace_type":"Composite","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"},{"id":"RAW_QGOH_PS_2:P0032","trace_type":"Composite","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"},{"id":"RAW_QGOH_PS_2:P0033","trace_type":"Composite","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"},{"id":"RAW_QGOH_PS_2:P0035","trace_type":"Composite","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_QGOH_PS_2:FGS4","text":"Mind as idea of Body → necessary perception of affections of the Body → perceptive capacity proportional to bodily dispositions.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_QGOH_PS_2:P0037","trace_type":"Composite","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"},{"id":"RAW_QGOH_PS_2:P0043","trace_type":"Composite","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"},{"id":"RAW_QGOH_PS_2:P0074","trace_type":"Composite","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_QGOH_PS_2:FGS5","text":"Affection of the body by an external thing → idea involving the nature of both bodies → presentation of the external through the constitution of one's own body.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_QGOH_PS_2:P0003","trace_type":"Direct","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"},{"id":"RAW_QGOH_PS_2:P0045","trace_type":"Direct","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"},{"id":"RAW_QGOH_PS_2:P0051","trace_type":"Direct","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_QGOH_PS_2:FGS6","text":"Persistent bodily trace → repetition of affection → imagination of an absent thing as present.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_QGOH_PS_2:P0035","trace_type":"Composite","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"},{"id":"RAW_QGOH_PS_2:P0057","trace_type":"Composite","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"},{"id":"RAW_QGOH_PS_2:P0059","trace_type":"Composite","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_QGOH_PS_2:FGS7","text":"Simultaneity of affections and custom of the body → imaginative concatenation → singularly ordered memory.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_QGOH_PS_2:P0066","trace_type":"Composite","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"},{"id":"RAW_QGOH_PS_2:P0070","trace_type":"Composite","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"},{"id":"RAW_QGOH_PS_2:P0071","trace_type":"Composite","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_QGOH_PS_2:FGS8","text":"Mediation of knowledge through affections → absence of causal knowledge of parts and external things → confusion of ideas; perceived ideas of affections → mediated self-knowledge.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_QGOH_PS_2:P0074","trace_type":"Structural","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"},{"id":"RAW_QGOH_PS_2:P0093","trace_type":"Structural","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"},{"id":"RAW_QGOH_PS_2:P0098","trace_type":"Structural","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"},{"id":"RAW_QGOH_PS_2:P0103","trace_type":"Structural","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"},{"id":"RAW_QGOH_PS_2:P0119","trace_type":"Structural","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"},{"id":"RAW_QGOH_PS_2:P0121","trace_type":"Structural","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]}],"original_name":"Formal Generative Schema"},{"number":"9","name":"Argument Flow Map","items":[{"id":"CH_QGOH_PS_2:AF1","text":"Persistence of motion and rest → external cause of change → relation of motions → composite Individual → identity through the ratio of communication.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_QGOH_PS_2:P0001","trace_type":"Structural","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"},{"id":"RAW_QGOH_PS_2:P0008","trace_type":"Structural","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"},{"id":"RAW_QGOH_PS_2:P0012","trace_type":"Structural","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"},{"id":"RAW_QGOH_PS_2:P0024","trace_type":"Structural","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_QGOH_PS_2:AF2","text":"Multiple human Body exposed to external things → very many affections → Mind as idea of Body → perception of more things proportional to bodily disposition.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_QGOH_PS_2:P0031","trace_type":"Structural","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"},{"id":"RAW_QGOH_PS_2:P0033","trace_type":"Structural","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"},{"id":"RAW_QGOH_PS_2:P0037","trace_type":"Structural","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"},{"id":"RAW_QGOH_PS_2:P0041","trace_type":"Structural","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_QGOH_PS_2:AF3","text":"Affection by an external body → idea of both natures → conditioned external presence → repeated trace → imagination and memory.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_QGOH_PS_2:P0045","trace_type":"Structural","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"},{"id":"RAW_QGOH_PS_2:P0057","trace_type":"Structural","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"},{"id":"RAW_QGOH_PS_2:P0059","trace_type":"Structural","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"},{"id":"RAW_QGOH_PS_2:P0066","trace_type":"Structural","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"},{"id":"RAW_QGOH_PS_2:P0070","trace_type":"Structural","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_QGOH_PS_2:AF4","text":"Knowledge through affections → mediated knowledge of Body, external things, and Mind → lack of context of causes → inadequacy and confusion.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_QGOH_PS_2:P0074","trace_type":"Structural","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"},{"id":"RAW_QGOH_PS_2:P0093","trace_type":"Structural","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"},{"id":"RAW_QGOH_PS_2:P0098","trace_type":"Structural","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"},{"id":"RAW_QGOH_PS_2:P0103","trace_type":"Structural","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"},{"id":"RAW_QGOH_PS_2:P0119","trace_type":"Structural","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"},{"id":"RAW_QGOH_PS_2:P0121","trace_type":"Structural","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]}],"original_name":"Argument Flow Map"},{"number":"10","name":"Map-Effects","items":[{"id":"CH_QGOH_PS_2:ME1","text":"The body is reclassified from an aggregate of matter into an individual relation of motion and rest that sustains changes of parts without dissolution of identity.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_QGOH_PS_2:P0008","trace_type":"Composite","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"},{"id":"RAW_QGOH_PS_2:P0012","trace_type":"Composite","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"},{"id":"RAW_QGOH_PS_2:P0016","trace_type":"Composite","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"},{"id":"RAW_QGOH_PS_2:P0024","trace_type":"Composite","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_QGOH_PS_2:ME2","text":"The external body becomes an object that appears at once in the idea of affection and yet is indicated through the constitution of the human Body.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_QGOH_PS_2:P0045","trace_type":"Direct","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"},{"id":"RAW_QGOH_PS_2:P0051","trace_type":"Direct","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_QGOH_PS_2:ME3","text":"Imagination is reclassified from false knowledge into an ideal presence arising from bodily traces; falsity is not a form of imagination, but the lack of an excluding idea.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_QGOH_PS_2:P0063","trace_type":"Direct","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"},{"id":"RAW_QGOH_PS_2:P0064","trace_type":"Direct","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_QGOH_PS_2:ME4","text":"Memory is reclassified from a storehouse of images into a concatenation of ideas ordered by bodily customs.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_QGOH_PS_2:P0070","trace_type":"Direct","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"},{"id":"RAW_QGOH_PS_2:P0071","trace_type":"Direct","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_QGOH_PS_2:ME5","text":"Self-knowledge is reclassified from inner intuition into reflexive perception of ideas of affections of the Body.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_QGOH_PS_2:P0089","trace_type":"Composite","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"},{"id":"RAW_QGOH_PS_2:P0093","trace_type":"Composite","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"},{"id":"RAW_QGOH_PS_2:P0095","trace_type":"Composite","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_QGOH_PS_2:ME6","text":"The opposition between multiple experience and adequate knowledge is dissolved: perception of more things can increase while knowledge of the causes of parts and external things remains inadequate.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_QGOH_PS_2:P0037","trace_type":"Tension","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"},{"id":"RAW_QGOH_PS_2:P0098","trace_type":"Tension","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"},{"id":"RAW_QGOH_PS_2:P0103","trace_type":"Tension","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"},{"id":"RAW_QGOH_PS_2:P0119","trace_type":"Tension","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]}],"original_name":"Map-Effects"},{"number":"11","name":"Altitude Levels","items":[{"id":"CH_QGOH_PS_2:ALT1","text":"Surface level: the Mind perceives very many things, imagines external bodies, remembers absent things, feels its own Body, and perceives itself.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_QGOH_PS_2:P0037","trace_type":"Composite","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"},{"id":"RAW_QGOH_PS_2:P0057","trace_type":"Composite","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"},{"id":"RAW_QGOH_PS_2:P0074","trace_type":"Composite","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"},{"id":"RAW_QGOH_PS_2:P0093","trace_type":"Composite","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_QGOH_PS_2:ALT2","text":"Structural level: mental effects are generated from the composition of the body, from the reciprocal affection of bodies, from traces in fluids, and from the order of affections.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_QGOH_PS_2:P0003","trace_type":"Structural","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"},{"id":"RAW_QGOH_PS_2:P0008","trace_type":"Structural","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"},{"id":"RAW_QGOH_PS_2:P0035","trace_type":"Structural","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"},{"id":"RAW_QGOH_PS_2:P0045","trace_type":"Structural","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"},{"id":"RAW_QGOH_PS_2:P0070","trace_type":"Structural","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_QGOH_PS_2:ALT3","text":"Metastrategic level: the question of the truth of perception is transferred from the apparent likeness of the thing to the bodily and causal conditions under which the idea is produced.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_QGOH_PS_2:P0051","trace_type":"Structural","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"},{"id":"RAW_QGOH_PS_2:P0063","trace_type":"Structural","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"},{"id":"RAW_QGOH_PS_2:P0105","trace_type":"Structural","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"},{"id":"RAW_QGOH_PS_2:P0113","trace_type":"Structural","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_QGOH_PS_2:ALT4","text":"Ontological and temporal level: Individuals are ratios of motion persisting through variation, and the finite Mind repeats the history of its body in imagining and remembering.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_QGOH_PS_2:P0012","trace_type":"Structural","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"},{"id":"RAW_QGOH_PS_2:P0024","trace_type":"Structural","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"},{"id":"RAW_QGOH_PS_2:P0057","trace_type":"Structural","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"},{"id":"RAW_QGOH_PS_2:P0070","trace_type":"Structural","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]}],"original_name":"Altitude Levels"},{"number":"12","name":"Minimal Reconstruction Test","items":[{"id":"CH_QGOH_PS_2:MRT1","text":"The extracted axioms suffice to reconstruct the transition from the physics of change and individuation to the doctrine of affection, imagination, memory, and mediated knowledge.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_QGOH_PS_2:P0001","trace_type":"Structural","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"},{"id":"RAW_QGOH_PS_2:P0008","trace_type":"Structural","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"},{"id":"RAW_QGOH_PS_2:P0031","trace_type":"Structural","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"},{"id":"RAW_QGOH_PS_2:P0045","trace_type":"Structural","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"},{"id":"RAW_QGOH_PS_2:P0057","trace_type":"Structural","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"},{"id":"RAW_QGOH_PS_2:P0070","trace_type":"Structural","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"},{"id":"RAW_QGOH_PS_2:P0119","trace_type":"Structural","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_QGOH_PS_2:MRT2","text":"The firmest joint is the nexus between the relational nature of the Individual, the composite constitution of the human Body, and the idea of affection that involves both bodies.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_QGOH_PS_2:P0008","trace_type":"Structural","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"},{"id":"RAW_QGOH_PS_2:P0012","trace_type":"Structural","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"},{"id":"RAW_QGOH_PS_2:P0031","trace_type":"Structural","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"},{"id":"RAW_QGOH_PS_2:P0043","trace_type":"Structural","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"},{"id":"RAW_QGOH_PS_2:P0045","trace_type":"Structural","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_QGOH_PS_2:MRT3","text":"The system locally depends on previously established principles, especially on the unity of Mind and Body, the perception of all affections, and the identity of the order of ideas with the order of causes.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_QGOH_PS_2:P0043","trace_type":"Structural","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"},{"id":"RAW_QGOH_PS_2:P0074","trace_type":"Structural","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"},{"id":"RAW_QGOH_PS_2:P0081","trace_type":"Structural","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"},{"id":"RAW_QGOH_PS_2:P0087","trace_type":"Structural","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"},{"id":"RAW_QGOH_PS_2:P0093","trace_type":"Structural","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_QGOH_PS_2:MRT4","text":"The transition from the nature of individual composites to Nature as a whole as one Individual is proposed as easy to conceive, but the text itself deliberately suspends a fuller demonstration.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_QGOH_PS_2:P0028","trace_type":"Direct","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"},{"id":"RAW_QGOH_PS_2:P0029","trace_type":"Direct","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_QGOH_PS_2:MRT5","text":"The explanation of imagination through bodily traces exhibits one sufficient cause, but the Scholium grants that the thing can also occur from other causes; causal exclusivity is not demonstrated.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_QGOH_PS_2:P0059","trace_type":"Direct","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"},{"id":"RAW_QGOH_PS_2:P0062","trace_type":"Direct","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_QGOH_PS_2:MRT6","text":"The system resolves the apparent tension between a greater faculty of perceiving and persistent inadequacy by distinguishing the plurality of affections from knowledge of their causes.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_QGOH_PS_2:P0037","trace_type":"Tension","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"},{"id":"RAW_QGOH_PS_2:P0098","trace_type":"Tension","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"},{"id":"RAW_QGOH_PS_2:P0103","trace_type":"Tension","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"},{"id":"RAW_QGOH_PS_2:P0119","trace_type":"Tension","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_QGOH_PS_2:MRT7","text":"The doctrine of self-knowledge immediately supports only reflexive perception of ideas of affections; the further development of the idea of an idea to infinity is deferred by the text to later treatment.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_QGOH_PS_2:P0087","trace_type":"Composite","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"},{"id":"RAW_QGOH_PS_2:P0089","trace_type":"Composite","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"},{"id":"RAW_QGOH_PS_2:P0093","trace_type":"Composite","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"},{"id":"RAW_QGOH_PS_2:P0095","trace_type":"Composite","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]}],"original_name":"Minimal Reconstruction Test"}],"revision":"CH_QGOH_PS_2@R001","source_variant_id":"canonical:CH_QGOH_PS_2:v1","source_language_code":"la"}],"identity":{"layer":"CH","kicker":"Chapter 06","title":"Part Two - On the Nature and Origin of the Mind · passage 2","context":"Chapter 06","original_title":"Pars secunda - De natura et origine mentis · passage 2","original_context":"Chapter 06"},"original_title":"Pars secunda - De natura et origine mentis · passage 2","title_language_code":"en"},{"id":"CH_QGOH_PS_3","layer":"CH","title":"Part Two - On the Nature and Origin of the Mind · passage 3","order":7,"parents":["RAW_QGOH_PS_3"],"revision":"CH_QGOH_PS_3@R001","sha256":"e50c17d14073105fd43f670ad96bc3139ed0cb7cecff994420c69e260a48a9fa","sections":[{"number":"1","name":"Argumental Spine","items":[{"id":"CH_QGOH_PS_3:AS1","text":"Textus quaerit quibus condicionibus Mens humana se, Corpus suum et corpora externa adæquate cognoscere possit, et unde imminutio, error atque certitudo oriantur.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_QGOH_PS_3:P0007","trace_type":"Structural","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"},{"id":"RAW_QGOH_PS_3:P0011","trace_type":"Structural","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"},{"id":"RAW_QGOH_PS_3:P0015","trace_type":"Structural","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"},{"id":"RAW_QGOH_PS_3:P0104","trace_type":"Structural","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_QGOH_PS_3:AS2","text":"Thesis principalis statuit cognitionem ex affectionibus Corporis necessario mediatam, externam et fortuitam esse mutilatam, cognitionem vero ex communibus proprietatibus et ideis adæquatis ortam esse veram.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_QGOH_PS_3:P0007","trace_type":"Structural","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"},{"id":"RAW_QGOH_PS_3:P0052","trace_type":"Structural","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"},{"id":"RAW_QGOH_PS_3:P0074","trace_type":"Structural","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"},{"id":"RAW_QGOH_PS_3:P0076","trace_type":"Structural","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_QGOH_PS_3:AS3","text":"Argumentatio est simul diagnostica et reconstructiva: primo originem cognitionis confusæ exponit, deinde condiciones cognitionis rationalis et certæ constituit.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_QGOH_PS_3:P0007","trace_type":"Structural","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"},{"id":"RAW_QGOH_PS_3:P0009","trace_type":"Structural","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"},{"id":"RAW_QGOH_PS_3:P0070","trace_type":"Structural","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"},{"id":"RAW_QGOH_PS_3:P0071","trace_type":"Structural","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_QGOH_PS_3:AS4","text":"Motus primus procedit ab inadequatione idearum affectionum ad inadequatam cognitionem Mentis, Corporis, corporum externorum atque durationis rerum singularium.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_QGOH_PS_3:P0007","trace_type":"Structural","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"},{"id":"RAW_QGOH_PS_3:P0011","trace_type":"Structural","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"},{"id":"RAW_QGOH_PS_3:P0015","trace_type":"Structural","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_QGOH_PS_3:AS5","text":"Motus secundus transfert veritatem idearum ad earum relationem ad Deum, falsitatem autem ad privationem cognitionis in mente finita.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_QGOH_PS_3:P0021","trace_type":"Composite","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"},{"id":"RAW_QGOH_PS_3:P0033","trace_type":"Composite","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"},{"id":"RAW_QGOH_PS_3:P0042","trace_type":"Composite","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_QGOH_PS_3:AS6","text":"Motus tertius ex communibus corporum proprietatibus rationem, ex ideis adæquatis certitudinem, et ex ratione necessitatem rerum sub specie æternitatis deducit.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_QGOH_PS_3:P0048","trace_type":"Structural","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"},{"id":"RAW_QGOH_PS_3:P0054","trace_type":"Structural","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"},{"id":"RAW_QGOH_PS_3:P0070","trace_type":"Structural","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"},{"id":"RAW_QGOH_PS_3:P0090","trace_type":"Structural","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"},{"id":"RAW_QGOH_PS_3:P0100","trace_type":"Structural","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_QGOH_PS_3:AS7","text":"Terminus textus affirmat ideam cuiuscunque rei singularis actu existentis essentiam Dei æternam et infinitam necessario involvere.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_QGOH_PS_3:P0104","trace_type":"Direct","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]}],"original_name":"Argumental Spine"},{"number":"2","name":"Load-Bearing Axioms","items":[{"id":"CH_QGOH_PS_3:A1","text":"Mens se ipsam, Corpus suum et corpora externa non percipit nisi per ideas affectionum Corporis sui.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_QGOH_PS_3:P0007","trace_type":"Direct","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"},{"id":"RAW_QGOH_PS_3:P0009","trace_type":"Direct","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_QGOH_PS_3:A2","text":"Idea affectionis Corporis naturam Corporis non adæquate exprimit, nec idea illius ideæ naturam Mentis humanæ adæquate involvit.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_QGOH_PS_3:P0003","trace_type":"Direct","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"},{"id":"RAW_QGOH_PS_3:P0005","trace_type":"Direct","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_QGOH_PS_3:A3","text":"Perceptio ex fortuito rerum occursu externe determinata cognitionem confusam producit; dispositio interna, quae convenientias, differentias et oppugnantias rerum intelligit, cognitionem claram et distinctam producit.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_QGOH_PS_3:P0007","trace_type":"Direct","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"},{"id":"RAW_QGOH_PS_3:P0009","trace_type":"Direct","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_QGOH_PS_3:A4","text":"Duratio Corporis et rerum singularium ex infinita serie causarum singularium pendet, non ex essentia rei singularis neque ex absoluta Dei natura.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_QGOH_PS_3:P0013","trace_type":"Composite","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"},{"id":"RAW_QGOH_PS_3:P0017","trace_type":"Composite","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_QGOH_PS_3:A5","text":"Omnes ideæ, quatenus ad Deum referuntur, cum suis ideatis conveniunt et veræ sunt.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_QGOH_PS_3:P0021","trace_type":"Direct","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"},{"id":"RAW_QGOH_PS_3:P0023","trace_type":"Direct","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_QGOH_PS_3:A6","text":"Ideæ inadæquatæ et confusæ non sunt defectus in Deo, sed proveniunt ex eo quod ideæ ad Mentem singularem referuntur; tamen eadem necessitate consequuntur ac ideæ adæquatæ.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_QGOH_PS_3:P0040","trace_type":"Direct","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"},{"id":"RAW_QGOH_PS_3:P0042","trace_type":"Direct","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_QGOH_PS_3:A7","text":"Falsitas nullam formam positivam habet, sed consistit in privatione cognitionis quam ideæ mutilatæ, confusæ et inadæquatæ involvunt.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_QGOH_PS_3:P0033","trace_type":"Direct","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"},{"id":"RAW_QGOH_PS_3:P0035","trace_type":"Direct","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_QGOH_PS_3:A8","text":"Id quod omnibus commune est et æque in parte ac in toto adest nullius rei singularis essentiam constituit.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_QGOH_PS_3:P0044","trace_type":"Direct","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_QGOH_PS_3:A9","text":"Id quod omnibus commune est atque æque in parte ac in toto adest non potest nisi adæquate concipi.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_QGOH_PS_3:P0048","trace_type":"Direct","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_QGOH_PS_3:A10","text":"Proprietas Corpori humano et corporibus externis, a quibus affici solet, communis et propria ideam adæquatam in Mente humana constituit.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_QGOH_PS_3:P0054","trace_type":"Direct","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"},{"id":"RAW_QGOH_PS_3:P0056","trace_type":"Direct","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_QGOH_PS_3:A11","text":"Idea quae in Mente ex ideis adæquatis sequitur est ipsa adæquata.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_QGOH_PS_3:P0060","trace_type":"Direct","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"},{"id":"RAW_QGOH_PS_3:P0062","trace_type":"Direct","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_QGOH_PS_3:A12","text":"Capacitas imaginativa Corporis finita, cum imaginum multitudo superatur, imagines confundit et terminos transcendentales vel universales ex confusione producit.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_QGOH_PS_3:P0065","trace_type":"Composite","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"},{"id":"RAW_QGOH_PS_3:P0066","trace_type":"Composite","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"},{"id":"RAW_QGOH_PS_3:P0067","trace_type":"Composite","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_QGOH_PS_3:A13","text":"Cognitio primi generis ex singularibus confuse per sensus exhibitibus aut ex signis oritur; cognitio secundi generis ex notionibus communibus; scientia intuitiva ex idea adæquata attributorum Dei ad cognitionem essentiarum rerum procedit.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_QGOH_PS_3:P0070","trace_type":"Direct","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"},{"id":"RAW_QGOH_PS_3:P0071","trace_type":"Direct","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_QGOH_PS_3:A14","text":"Idea adæquata simul ideam sui continet; qui vere cognoscit simul certus est se vere cognoscere.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_QGOH_PS_3:P0082","trace_type":"Direct","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"},{"id":"RAW_QGOH_PS_3:P0084","trace_type":"Direct","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_QGOH_PS_3:A15","text":"Ratio res ut necessarias, non ut contingentes, sub quadam æternitatis specie percipit.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_QGOH_PS_3:P0090","trace_type":"Composite","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"},{"id":"RAW_QGOH_PS_3:P0092","trace_type":"Composite","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"},{"id":"RAW_QGOH_PS_3:P0100","trace_type":"Composite","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_QGOH_PS_3:A16","text":"Idea cuiuscunque corporis vel rei singularis actu existentis essentiam Dei æternam et infinitam necessario involvit.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_QGOH_PS_3:P0104","trace_type":"Direct","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_QGOH_PS_3:A17","text":"Conscientia actionum cum ignorantia causarum, a quibus actiones determinantor, opinionem libertatis producit; voluntas sine idea causalis explicata nihil explanat.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_QGOH_PS_3:P0037","trace_type":"Direct","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]}],"original_name":"Load-Bearing Axioms"},{"number":"3","name":"Apophatic Structure","items":[{"id":"CH_QGOH_PS_3:NX1","text":"Mens non habet cognitionem sui, Corporis sui aut corporum externorum immediate datam et a mediatione affectionum corporis independentem.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_QGOH_PS_3:P0007","trace_type":"Negative","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"},{"id":"RAW_QGOH_PS_3:P0009","trace_type":"Negative","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_QGOH_PS_3:NX2","text":"Ordo communis Naturæ, quatenus fortuitis occursibus Mentem externe determinat, non est via cognitionis claræ et distinctæ.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_QGOH_PS_3:P0007","trace_type":"Negative","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"},{"id":"RAW_QGOH_PS_3:P0009","trace_type":"Negative","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_QGOH_PS_3:NX3","text":"Duratio Corporis proprii et rerum singularium externarum non potest a Mente humana adæquate cognosci.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_QGOH_PS_3:P0011","trace_type":"Negative","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"},{"id":"RAW_QGOH_PS_3:P0015","trace_type":"Negative","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"},{"id":"RAW_QGOH_PS_3:P0017","trace_type":"Negative","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_QGOH_PS_3:NX4","text":"Falsitas neque positivum modum cogitandi neque absolutam ignorantiam constituit.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_QGOH_PS_3:P0025","trace_type":"Negative","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"},{"id":"RAW_QGOH_PS_3:P0035","trace_type":"Negative","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_QGOH_PS_3:NX5","text":"Proprietas communis non est essentia rei singularis.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_QGOH_PS_3:P0044","trace_type":"Negative","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"},{"id":"RAW_QGOH_PS_3:P0046","trace_type":"Negative","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_QGOH_PS_3:NX6","text":"Termini transcendentales et universalia imaginaria non sunt eo ipso notiones communes vel cognitiones rationales.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_QGOH_PS_3:P0065","trace_type":"Negative","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"},{"id":"RAW_QGOH_PS_3:P0066","trace_type":"Negative","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"},{"id":"RAW_QGOH_PS_3:P0070","trace_type":"Negative","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_QGOH_PS_3:NX7","text":"Cognitio primi generis non docet verum a falso distinguere.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_QGOH_PS_3:P0074","trace_type":"Negative","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"},{"id":"RAW_QGOH_PS_3:P0076","trace_type":"Negative","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"},{"id":"RAW_QGOH_PS_3:P0078","trace_type":"Negative","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_QGOH_PS_3:NX8","text":"Ratio res non contemplatur ut contingentes neque ex relationibus temporalibus imaginariis suum iudicium constituit.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_QGOH_PS_3:P0090","trace_type":"Negative","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"},{"id":"RAW_QGOH_PS_3:P0092","trace_type":"Negative","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_QGOH_PS_3:NX9","text":"Libertas voluntatis non probatur ex conscientia actionum, cum conscientia causarum determinantium abesse possit.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_QGOH_PS_3:P0037","trace_type":"Negative","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]}],"original_name":"Apophatic Structure"},{"number":"4","name":"Derivative Propositions","items":[{"id":"CH_QGOH_PS_3:D1","text":"Mens, quoties ex communi Naturæ ordine res percipit, cognitionem sui, Corporis sui et corporum externorum mutilatam atque confusam habet.","refs":["CH_QGOH_PS_3:A1","CH_QGOH_PS_3:A2","CH_QGOH_PS_3:A3"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"From","passages":[{"id":"RAW_QGOH_PS_3:P0007","trace_type":"Composite","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"},{"id":"RAW_QGOH_PS_3:P0009","trace_type":"Composite","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_QGOH_PS_3:D2","text":"Cognitio humana durationis Corporis proprii et rerum singularium externarum est admodum inadæquata, quia causae durationis in infinitum procedunt.","refs":["CH_QGOH_PS_3:A4","CH_QGOH_PS_3:A5","CH_QGOH_PS_3:A6"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"From","passages":[{"id":"RAW_QGOH_PS_3:P0013","trace_type":"Composite","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"},{"id":"RAW_QGOH_PS_3:P0017","trace_type":"Composite","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_QGOH_PS_3:D3","text":"Contingentia et corruptibilitas rerum particularium non designant indeterminationem causalitatis, sed conditionem sub qua earum duratio adæquate cognosci non potest.","refs":["CH_QGOH_PS_3:A4","CH_QGOH_PS_3:A5","CH_QGOH_PS_3:A6"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"From","passages":[{"id":"RAW_QGOH_PS_3:P0019","trace_type":"Direct","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_QGOH_PS_3:D4","text":"Error non requirit ideam positivam falsam, sed oritur ubi idea positiva habetur sine cognitione sufficiente rei et causarum eius.","refs":["CH_QGOH_PS_3:A5","CH_QGOH_PS_3:A6","CH_QGOH_PS_3:A7"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"From","passages":[{"id":"RAW_QGOH_PS_3:P0033","trace_type":"Composite","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"},{"id":"RAW_QGOH_PS_3:P0035","trace_type":"Composite","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"},{"id":"RAW_QGOH_PS_3:P0037","trace_type":"Composite","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_QGOH_PS_3:D5","text":"Ideæ inadæquatæ non sunt effectus contingentes aut extra ordinem Naturæ positi, sed necessario sequuntur cum ad Mentem finitam referuntur.","refs":["CH_QGOH_PS_3:A5","CH_QGOH_PS_3:A6"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"From","passages":[{"id":"RAW_QGOH_PS_3:P0040","trace_type":"Direct","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"},{"id":"RAW_QGOH_PS_3:P0042","trace_type":"Direct","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_QGOH_PS_3:D6","text":"Notiones communes sunt fundamenta ratiocinii, quia proprietates communes neque essentias singulares constituunt neque in parte a toto variant.","refs":["CH_QGOH_PS_3:A8","CH_QGOH_PS_3:A9","CH_QGOH_PS_3:A10"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"From","passages":[{"id":"RAW_QGOH_PS_3:P0044","trace_type":"Composite","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"},{"id":"RAW_QGOH_PS_3:P0048","trace_type":"Composite","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"},{"id":"RAW_QGOH_PS_3:P0064","trace_type":"Composite","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_QGOH_PS_3:D7","text":"Mens eo aptior est ad plura adæquate percipienda quo Corpus eius plura cum aliis corporibus communia habet.","refs":["CH_QGOH_PS_3:A10"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"From","passages":[{"id":"RAW_QGOH_PS_3:P0058","trace_type":"Direct","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_QGOH_PS_3:D8","text":"Universalia imaginaria variant secundum dispositionem Corporis, frequentiam affectionum et id quod Mens facilius imaginatur vel recordatur.","refs":["CH_QGOH_PS_3:A12"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"From","passages":[{"id":"RAW_QGOH_PS_3:P0067","trace_type":"Direct","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"},{"id":"RAW_QGOH_PS_3:P0068","trace_type":"Direct","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_QGOH_PS_3:D9","text":"Cognitio primi generis est unica falsitatis causa, dum cognitio secundi et tertii generis necessario vera est.","refs":["CH_QGOH_PS_3:A7","CH_QGOH_PS_3:A13"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"From","passages":[{"id":"RAW_QGOH_PS_3:P0074","trace_type":"Direct","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"},{"id":"RAW_QGOH_PS_3:P0076","trace_type":"Direct","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_QGOH_PS_3:D10","text":"Verum a falso distingui potest tantum cognitione secundi aut tertii generis, quia talis distinctio ideam adæquatam veri et falsi postulat.","refs":["CH_QGOH_PS_3:A13","CH_QGOH_PS_3:A14"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"From","passages":[{"id":"RAW_QGOH_PS_3:P0078","trace_type":"Composite","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"},{"id":"RAW_QGOH_PS_3:P0080","trace_type":"Composite","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_QGOH_PS_3:D11","text":"Certitudo non additur ideæ veræ ex probatione externa, sed in reflexiva adæquatione ipsius ideæ consistit.","refs":["CH_QGOH_PS_3:A5","CH_QGOH_PS_3:A14"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"From","passages":[{"id":"RAW_QGOH_PS_3:P0082","trace_type":"Direct","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"},{"id":"RAW_QGOH_PS_3:P0084","trace_type":"Direct","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_QGOH_PS_3:D12","text":"Imaginatio, ex fluctuante coniunctione imaginum cum ordinibus temporalibus, res futuras vel præteritas ut contingentes exhibet; ratio eas ut necessarias percipit.","refs":["CH_QGOH_PS_3:A13","CH_QGOH_PS_3:A15"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"From","passages":[{"id":"RAW_QGOH_PS_3:P0094","trace_type":"Composite","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"},{"id":"RAW_QGOH_PS_3:P0096","trace_type":"Composite","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"},{"id":"RAW_QGOH_PS_3:P0098","trace_type":"Composite","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"},{"id":"RAW_QGOH_PS_3:P0090","trace_type":"Composite","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_QGOH_PS_3:D13","text":"Intelligibilitas rei singularis actu existentis finitam eius existentiam cum essentia Dei æterna et infinita coniungit.","refs":["CH_QGOH_PS_3:A16"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"From","passages":[{"id":"RAW_QGOH_PS_3:P0104","trace_type":"Direct","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_QGOH_PS_3:D14","text":"Opinatio libertatis est forma erroris qua homo actiones suas sentit, causas autem quibus ad agendum determinatur ignorat.","refs":["CH_QGOH_PS_3:A7","CH_QGOH_PS_3:A17"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"From","passages":[{"id":"RAW_QGOH_PS_3:P0037","trace_type":"Direct","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]}],"original_name":"Derivative Propositions"},{"number":"5","name":"Super-Derivatives","items":[{"id":"CH_QGOH_PS_3:SD1","text":"Veritas et inadequatio non dividunt duas classes idearum per contentum positivum, sed eandem ideationalem realitatem secundum relationem ad intellectum Dei vel ad Mentem finitam ordinant.","refs":["CH_QGOH_PS_3:A2","CH_QGOH_PS_3:A5","CH_QGOH_PS_3:A6","CH_QGOH_PS_3:A7"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"From","passages":[{"id":"RAW_QGOH_PS_3:P0005","trace_type":"Structural","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"},{"id":"RAW_QGOH_PS_3:P0021","trace_type":"Structural","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"},{"id":"RAW_QGOH_PS_3:P0033","trace_type":"Structural","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"},{"id":"RAW_QGOH_PS_3:P0042","trace_type":"Structural","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_QGOH_PS_3:SD2","text":"Finitudo cognitiva non est imminutio causalitatis Naturæ, sed imminutio expressionis totius nexus causalis in idea ad Mentem singularem relata.","refs":["CH_QGOH_PS_3:A4","CH_QGOH_PS_3:A5","CH_QGOH_PS_3:A6"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"From","passages":[{"id":"RAW_QGOH_PS_3:P0013","trace_type":"Composite","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"},{"id":"RAW_QGOH_PS_3:P0042","trace_type":"Composite","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_QGOH_PS_3:SD3","text":"Rationalitas non ex abstractione generica a singularibus oritur, sed ex perceptione proprietatum corporalium realiter communium, quae in parte et in toto invariant.","refs":["CH_QGOH_PS_3:A8","CH_QGOH_PS_3:A9","CH_QGOH_PS_3:A10","CH_QGOH_PS_3:A12"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"From","passages":[{"id":"RAW_QGOH_PS_3:P0044","trace_type":"Structural","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"},{"id":"RAW_QGOH_PS_3:P0048","trace_type":"Structural","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"},{"id":"RAW_QGOH_PS_3:P0054","trace_type":"Structural","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"},{"id":"RAW_QGOH_PS_3:P0066","trace_type":"Structural","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_QGOH_PS_3:SD4","text":"Hierarchia cognitionis describit transitum ab affectione et signo, per communitatem structuralem, ad intuitivam deductionem essentiarum; incrementum cognitionis est imminutio dependentiae a casu imaginativo.","refs":["CH_QGOH_PS_3:A3","CH_QGOH_PS_3:A11","CH_QGOH_PS_3:A13"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"From","passages":[{"id":"RAW_QGOH_PS_3:P0009","trace_type":"Structural","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"},{"id":"RAW_QGOH_PS_3:P0060","trace_type":"Structural","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"},{"id":"RAW_QGOH_PS_3:P0070","trace_type":"Structural","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"},{"id":"RAW_QGOH_PS_3:P0071","trace_type":"Structural","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_QGOH_PS_3:SD5","text":"Contingentia temporalis est effectus imaginationis finitae, dum necessitas æterna est forma sub qua ratio ordinem rerum vere intelligit.","refs":["CH_QGOH_PS_3:A4","CH_QGOH_PS_3:A13","CH_QGOH_PS_3:A15"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"From","passages":[{"id":"RAW_QGOH_PS_3:P0094","trace_type":"Composite","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"},{"id":"RAW_QGOH_PS_3:P0098","trace_type":"Composite","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"},{"id":"RAW_QGOH_PS_3:P0100","trace_type":"Composite","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_QGOH_PS_3:SD6","text":"Certitudo vera est proprietas immanens cognitionis adæquatæ et non relatio posterior inter Mentem et tribunal externum verificationis.","refs":["CH_QGOH_PS_3:A5","CH_QGOH_PS_3:A14"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"From","passages":[{"id":"RAW_QGOH_PS_3:P0082","trace_type":"Direct","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"},{"id":"RAW_QGOH_PS_3:P0086","trace_type":"Direct","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]}],"original_name":"Super-Derivatives"},{"number":"6","name":"Deep Apophatics","items":[{"id":"CH_QGOH_PS_3:DNX1","text":"Falsum esse debet exemplar Mentis tamquam subiecti sibi, Corpori et rebus externis immediate praesentis.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_QGOH_PS_3:P0007","trace_type":"Negative","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"},{"id":"RAW_QGOH_PS_3:P0009","trace_type":"Negative","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_QGOH_PS_3:DNX2","text":"Falsum esse debet exemplar erroris tamquam entitatis positivæ, facultatis autonomæ aut ordinis causalis a veritate separati.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_QGOH_PS_3:P0025","trace_type":"Negative","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"},{"id":"RAW_QGOH_PS_3:P0027","trace_type":"Negative","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"},{"id":"RAW_QGOH_PS_3:P0035","trace_type":"Negative","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_QGOH_PS_3:DNX3","text":"Falsum esse debet exemplar universalis cognitionis quod universalia ex sola confusione vel depletione differentiarum singularium deducit.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_QGOH_PS_3:P0065","trace_type":"Negative","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"},{"id":"RAW_QGOH_PS_3:P0066","trace_type":"Negative","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"},{"id":"RAW_QGOH_PS_3:P0067","trace_type":"Negative","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_QGOH_PS_3:DNX4","text":"Falsum esse debet exemplar rationis a corpore seiunctæ, cum aptitudo Mentis ad cognitionem adæquatam a communitatibus Corporis cum aliis corporibus pendeat.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_QGOH_PS_3:P0054","trace_type":"Negative","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"},{"id":"RAW_QGOH_PS_3:P0058","trace_type":"Negative","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_QGOH_PS_3:DNX5","text":"Falsum esse debet exemplar contingentiae ut proprietatis rerum ipsarum, cum ratio res ut necessarias percipiat et contingentia ex imaginatione temporali oriatur.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_QGOH_PS_3:P0090","trace_type":"Negative","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"},{"id":"RAW_QGOH_PS_3:P0094","trace_type":"Negative","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"},{"id":"RAW_QGOH_PS_3:P0100","trace_type":"Negative","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_QGOH_PS_3:DNX6","text":"Falsum esse debet exemplar veritatis quod ideam veram solum per comparationem extrinsecam cum ideato cognosci posse supponit.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_QGOH_PS_3:P0082","trace_type":"Negative","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"},{"id":"RAW_QGOH_PS_3:P0086","trace_type":"Negative","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_QGOH_PS_3:DNX7","text":"Falsum esse debet exemplar libertatis quod conscientiam actionis pro origine causali actionis accipit.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_QGOH_PS_3:P0037","trace_type":"Negative","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]}],"original_name":"Deep Apophatics"},{"number":"7","name":"Super-Apophatics","items":[{"id":"CH_QGOH_PS_3:SNX1","text":"Systema nullum locum relinquit Mentis finitae extra ordinem causalem infinitum, unde durationem rerum singularium vel causas actionum suarum totaliter complecti possit.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_QGOH_PS_3:P0013","trace_type":"Structural","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"},{"id":"RAW_QGOH_PS_3:P0017","trace_type":"Structural","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"},{"id":"RAW_QGOH_PS_3:P0037","trace_type":"Structural","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_QGOH_PS_3:SNX2","text":"Systema nullum mundum falsitatis positivæ opponit mundo veritatis, quia ideæ omnes, quatenus in Deo sunt, veræ manent.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_QGOH_PS_3:P0021","trace_type":"Structural","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"},{"id":"RAW_QGOH_PS_3:P0033","trace_type":"Structural","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"},{"id":"RAW_QGOH_PS_3:P0042","trace_type":"Structural","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_QGOH_PS_3:SNX3","text":"Systema tempus imaginatum non admittit ut ultimum horizontem rationalis intelligibilitatis, quia ratio res sub specie æternitatis percipit.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_QGOH_PS_3:P0094","trace_type":"Structural","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"},{"id":"RAW_QGOH_PS_3:P0100","trace_type":"Structural","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"},{"id":"RAW_QGOH_PS_3:P0102","trace_type":"Structural","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_QGOH_PS_3:SNX4","text":"Systema nullam normam veritatis extra ipsam ideam adæquatam requirit, quia veritas se ipsam et falsitatem manifestat.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_QGOH_PS_3:P0082","trace_type":"Composite","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"},{"id":"RAW_QGOH_PS_3:P0086","trace_type":"Composite","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]}],"original_name":"Super-Apophatics"},{"number":"8","name":"Formal Generative Schema","items":[{"id":"CH_QGOH_PS_3:FGS1","text":"Affectio Corporis → idea affectionis → cognitio Mentis, Corporis et corporis externi necessario mediata.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_QGOH_PS_3:P0007","trace_type":"Direct","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"},{"id":"RAW_QGOH_PS_3:P0009","trace_type":"Direct","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_QGOH_PS_3:FGS2","text":"Mediatio per fortuitum occursum externum → idea mutilata et confusa → cognitio primi generis → possibilitas falsitatis.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_QGOH_PS_3:P0009","trace_type":"Composite","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"},{"id":"RAW_QGOH_PS_3:P0070","trace_type":"Composite","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"},{"id":"RAW_QGOH_PS_3:P0074","trace_type":"Composite","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_QGOH_PS_3:FGS3","text":"Duratio rei singularis → regressus causarum in infinitum → impossibilitas cognitionis humanæ adæquatæ durationis → contingentia et corruptibilitas quoad cognitionem.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_QGOH_PS_3:P0013","trace_type":"Direct","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"},{"id":"RAW_QGOH_PS_3:P0017","trace_type":"Direct","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"},{"id":"RAW_QGOH_PS_3:P0019","trace_type":"Direct","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_QGOH_PS_3:FGS4","text":"Relatio ideæ ad Deum → convenientia cum ideato → veritas; relatio eiusdem ideæ ad Mentem finitam → inadequatio → privatio cognitionis.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_QGOH_PS_3:P0021","trace_type":"Composite","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"},{"id":"RAW_QGOH_PS_3:P0033","trace_type":"Composite","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"},{"id":"RAW_QGOH_PS_3:P0042","trace_type":"Composite","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_QGOH_PS_3:FGS5","text":"Proprietas communis, æqualiter in parte et toto → non-essentialitas singularis → conceptio necessario adæquata → notio communis.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_QGOH_PS_3:P0044","trace_type":"Composite","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"},{"id":"RAW_QGOH_PS_3:P0048","trace_type":"Composite","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"},{"id":"RAW_QGOH_PS_3:P0054","trace_type":"Composite","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_QGOH_PS_3:FGS6","text":"Notio communis et idea adæquata → consecutio idearum adæquatarum → ratio → cognitio secundi generis.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_QGOH_PS_3:P0052","trace_type":"Composite","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"},{"id":"RAW_QGOH_PS_3:P0060","trace_type":"Composite","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"},{"id":"RAW_QGOH_PS_3:P0070","trace_type":"Composite","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_QGOH_PS_3:FGS7","text":"Idea adæquata → idea ideæ adæquata → certitudo immanens → distinctio veri et falsi.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_QGOH_PS_3:P0082","trace_type":"Direct","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"},{"id":"RAW_QGOH_PS_3:P0084","trace_type":"Direct","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_QGOH_PS_3:FGS8","text":"Ratio → necessitas rerum → species æternitatis → connexio rei singularis actu existentis cum essentia Dei æterna et infinita.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_QGOH_PS_3:P0090","trace_type":"Structural","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"},{"id":"RAW_QGOH_PS_3:P0100","trace_type":"Structural","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"},{"id":"RAW_QGOH_PS_3:P0104","trace_type":"Structural","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]}],"original_name":"Formal Generative Schema"},{"number":"9","name":"Argument Flow Map","items":[{"id":"CH_QGOH_PS_3:AF1","text":"Idea affectionis inadæquata → perceptio externa ex ordine communi Naturæ → cognitio mutilata Mentis, Corporis et corporum externorum → inadequata cognitio durationis.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_QGOH_PS_3:P0007","trace_type":"Structural","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"},{"id":"RAW_QGOH_PS_3:P0009","trace_type":"Structural","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"},{"id":"RAW_QGOH_PS_3:P0011","trace_type":"Structural","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_QGOH_PS_3:AF2","text":"Infinita concatenatio causarum → contingentia et corruptibilitas quoad cognitionem → relatio omnium idearum ad Deum → veritas idearum et falsitas ut privatio.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_QGOH_PS_3:P0017","trace_type":"Structural","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"},{"id":"RAW_QGOH_PS_3:P0019","trace_type":"Structural","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"},{"id":"RAW_QGOH_PS_3:P0021","trace_type":"Structural","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"},{"id":"RAW_QGOH_PS_3:P0033","trace_type":"Structural","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_QGOH_PS_3:AF3","text":"Proprietates corporum communes → notiones communes adæquatæ → ratio et consecutio idearum adæquatarum → distinctio inter imaginationem, rationem et scientiam intuitivam.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_QGOH_PS_3:P0054","trace_type":"Structural","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"},{"id":"RAW_QGOH_PS_3:P0058","trace_type":"Structural","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"},{"id":"RAW_QGOH_PS_3:P0070","trace_type":"Structural","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"},{"id":"RAW_QGOH_PS_3:P0071","trace_type":"Structural","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_QGOH_PS_3:AF4","text":"Idea vera reflexiva → certitudo → ratio rerum necessitas → species æternitatis → idea rei singularis actu existentis Dei essentiam involvens.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_QGOH_PS_3:P0082","trace_type":"Structural","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"},{"id":"RAW_QGOH_PS_3:P0090","trace_type":"Structural","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"},{"id":"RAW_QGOH_PS_3:P0100","trace_type":"Structural","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"},{"id":"RAW_QGOH_PS_3:P0104","trace_type":"Structural","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]}],"original_name":"Argument Flow Map"},{"number":"10","name":"Map-Effects","items":[{"id":"CH_QGOH_PS_3:ME1","text":"Cognitio sui, Corporis et mundi externi reclassificatur ut processus per affectiones Corporis mediatus, non ut praesentia immediata obiecti ad Mentem.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_QGOH_PS_3:P0007","trace_type":"Direct","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"},{"id":"RAW_QGOH_PS_3:P0009","trace_type":"Direct","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_QGOH_PS_3:ME2","text":"Falsitas reclassificatur ex proprietate positivæ ideæ in privationem cognitionis intra ideam necessario productam.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_QGOH_PS_3:P0033","trace_type":"Direct","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"},{"id":"RAW_QGOH_PS_3:P0035","trace_type":"Direct","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_QGOH_PS_3:ME3","text":"Oppositio inter universale et singulare dividitur in universalia imaginaria confusa et notiones communes structuraliter adæquatas.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_QGOH_PS_3:P0066","trace_type":"Composite","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"},{"id":"RAW_QGOH_PS_3:P0070","trace_type":"Composite","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_QGOH_PS_3:ME4","text":"Corpus fit conditio positiva cognitionis rationalis, quia communitates eius cum corporibus externis augent aptitudinem Mentis ad ideas adæquatas.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_QGOH_PS_3:P0054","trace_type":"Direct","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"},{"id":"RAW_QGOH_PS_3:P0058","trace_type":"Direct","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_QGOH_PS_3:ME5","text":"Contingentia transfertur ab ordine rerum ad modum imaginativum quo res in relationibus temporalibus concipiuntur.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_QGOH_PS_3:P0094","trace_type":"Direct","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"},{"id":"RAW_QGOH_PS_3:P0098","trace_type":"Direct","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_QGOH_PS_3:ME6","text":"Certitudo transfertur ab externis signis confirmationis ad reflexivam structuram ideæ veræ.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_QGOH_PS_3:P0082","trace_type":"Direct","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"},{"id":"RAW_QGOH_PS_3:P0086","trace_type":"Direct","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]}],"original_name":"Map-Effects"},{"number":"11","name":"Altitude Levels","items":[{"id":"CH_QGOH_PS_3:ALT1","text":"Gradus superficiei — Textus distinguit cognitionem confusam, cognitionem adæquatam, falsitatem, veritatem, certitudinem, imaginationem, rationem et scientiam intuitivam.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_QGOH_PS_3:P0070","trace_type":"Structural","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"},{"id":"RAW_QGOH_PS_3:P0071","trace_type":"Structural","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"},{"id":"RAW_QGOH_PS_3:P0074","trace_type":"Structural","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"},{"id":"RAW_QGOH_PS_3:P0082","trace_type":"Structural","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_QGOH_PS_3:ALT2","text":"Gradus structuralis — Cognitio humana ex relatione inter affectiones Corporis, ordinem causarum infinitum, capacitatem imaginativam finitam et proprietates corporibus communes constituitur.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_QGOH_PS_3:P0009","trace_type":"Structural","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"},{"id":"RAW_QGOH_PS_3:P0013","trace_type":"Structural","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"},{"id":"RAW_QGOH_PS_3:P0058","trace_type":"Structural","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"},{"id":"RAW_QGOH_PS_3:P0065","trace_type":"Structural","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_QGOH_PS_3:ALT3","text":"Gradus metastrategicus — Textus normam cognitionis transfert ab accumulatione imaginum, signorum et experientiarum vagarum ad productionem idearum ex notionibus communibus et ideis adæquatis.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_QGOH_PS_3:P0065","trace_type":"Structural","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"},{"id":"RAW_QGOH_PS_3:P0070","trace_type":"Structural","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"},{"id":"RAW_QGOH_PS_3:P0071","trace_type":"Structural","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_QGOH_PS_3:ALT4","text":"Gradus ontologicus vel temporalis — Veritas idearum fundatur in ordine divino necessario; contingentia pertinet ad imaginationem temporalem; ratio res sub specie æternitatis percipit.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_QGOH_PS_3:P0021","trace_type":"Structural","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"},{"id":"RAW_QGOH_PS_3:P0094","trace_type":"Structural","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"},{"id":"RAW_QGOH_PS_3:P0100","trace_type":"Structural","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"},{"id":"RAW_QGOH_PS_3:P0102","trace_type":"Structural","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]}],"original_name":"Altitude Levels"},{"number":"12","name":"Minimal Reconstruction Test","items":[{"id":"CH_QGOH_PS_3:MRT1","text":"Axiomata data sufficiunt ad restituendum motum principalem ab inadequatione affectionum corporalium ad cognitionem rationalem, certitudinem et necessitatem æternam.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_QGOH_PS_3:P0007","trace_type":"Structural","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"},{"id":"RAW_QGOH_PS_3:P0052","trace_type":"Structural","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"},{"id":"RAW_QGOH_PS_3:P0070","trace_type":"Structural","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"},{"id":"RAW_QGOH_PS_3:P0082","trace_type":"Structural","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"},{"id":"RAW_QGOH_PS_3:P0100","trace_type":"Structural","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_QGOH_PS_3:MRT2","text":"Enuntiatio de idea rei singularis actu existentis et essentia Dei terminalis manet in fonte dato sine demonstratione explicita; eius nexus inferentialis plenus hic restitui non potest.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_QGOH_PS_3:P0104","trace_type":"Direct","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_QGOH_PS_3:MRT3","text":"Systema maxime cohaeret ubi veritatem divinam et inadequationem finitam distinguit per diversam relationem eiusdem ideæ, non per duas ordines idearum positive separatos.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_QGOH_PS_3:P0021","trace_type":"Composite","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"},{"id":"RAW_QGOH_PS_3:P0042","trace_type":"Composite","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_QGOH_PS_3:MRT4","text":"Commissura fragilis manet transitus ab interna rerum contemplatione ad cognitionem clare et distincte formatam, quia conditio interna indicatur sed mechanismus completus eius productionis non plene explicatur.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_QGOH_PS_3:P0009","trace_type":"Tension","confidence":"medium","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"},{"id":"RAW_QGOH_PS_3:P0058","trace_type":"Tension","confidence":"medium","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_QGOH_PS_3:MRT5","text":"Distinctio inter notionem communem et universale imaginarium formaliter nititur proprietate æqualiter in parte et toto existente, sed applicatio eius ad conceptus singulares in textu limitata manet.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_QGOH_PS_3:P0044","trace_type":"Tension","confidence":"medium","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"},{"id":"RAW_QGOH_PS_3:P0048","trace_type":"Tension","confidence":"medium","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"},{"id":"RAW_QGOH_PS_3:P0066","trace_type":"Tension","confidence":"medium","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"},{"id":"RAW_QGOH_PS_3:P0067","trace_type":"Tension","confidence":"medium","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_QGOH_PS_3:MRT6","text":"Falsitas ut privatio et necessitas idearum inadæquatarum inter se cohaerent tantum si servatur distinctio inter positivam ideæ productionem et defectum cognitionis quam idea in Mente finita involvit.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_QGOH_PS_3:P0033","trace_type":"Composite","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"},{"id":"RAW_QGOH_PS_3:P0040","trace_type":"Composite","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"},{"id":"RAW_QGOH_PS_3:P0042","trace_type":"Composite","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_QGOH_PS_3:MRT7","text":"Certitudo ex idea ideæ deducta structuram scepticam excludit, sed vim suam ex principio reflexivitatis ideæ adæquatæ accipit, non ex probatione extrinseca independenter constituta.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_QGOH_PS_3:P0082","trace_type":"Structural","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"},{"id":"RAW_QGOH_PS_3:P0084","trace_type":"Structural","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"},{"id":"RAW_QGOH_PS_3:P0086","trace_type":"Structural","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]}],"original_name":"Minimal Reconstruction Test"}],"language_code":"la","language_name":"Latina","translations":[{"id":"CAREVAR_20261015T015042300825Z_0d7b01c7","language_code":"en","language_name":"English","title":"Part Two - On the Nature and Origin of the Mind · passage 3","source_sha256":"e50c17d14073105fd43f670ad96bc3139ed0cb7cecff994420c69e260a48a9fa","sha256":"3ee8a80d37cd7b188eb2ef27c648d5cae3c18a27c72cc52d9d401d0194af35b7","rendition_type":"machine","review_status":"machine","model":"gpt-5.6-terra","created":"2026-10-15T01:50:42.304183+00:00","sections":[{"number":"1","name":"Argumental Spine","items":[{"id":"CH_QGOH_PS_3:AS1","text":"The text asks under what conditions the human Mind can adequately know itself, its Body, and external bodies, and whence inadequacy, error, and certainty arise.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_QGOH_PS_3:P0007","trace_type":"Structural","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"},{"id":"RAW_QGOH_PS_3:P0011","trace_type":"Structural","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"},{"id":"RAW_QGOH_PS_3:P0015","trace_type":"Structural","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"},{"id":"RAW_QGOH_PS_3:P0104","trace_type":"Structural","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_QGOH_PS_3:AS2","text":"The principal thesis holds that knowledge necessarily mediated through affections of the Body, external and fortuitous, is mutilated, whereas knowledge arising from common properties and adequate ideas is true.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_QGOH_PS_3:P0007","trace_type":"Structural","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"},{"id":"RAW_QGOH_PS_3:P0052","trace_type":"Structural","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"},{"id":"RAW_QGOH_PS_3:P0074","trace_type":"Structural","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"},{"id":"RAW_QGOH_PS_3:P0076","trace_type":"Structural","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_QGOH_PS_3:AS3","text":"The argumentation is at once diagnostic and reconstructive: first it sets forth the origin of confused knowledge, then it establishes the conditions of rational and certain knowledge.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_QGOH_PS_3:P0007","trace_type":"Structural","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"},{"id":"RAW_QGOH_PS_3:P0009","trace_type":"Structural","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"},{"id":"RAW_QGOH_PS_3:P0070","trace_type":"Structural","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"},{"id":"RAW_QGOH_PS_3:P0071","trace_type":"Structural","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_QGOH_PS_3:AS4","text":"The first movement proceeds from the inadequacy of ideas of affections to inadequate knowledge of the Mind, the Body, external bodies, and the duration of singular things.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_QGOH_PS_3:P0007","trace_type":"Structural","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"},{"id":"RAW_QGOH_PS_3:P0011","trace_type":"Structural","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"},{"id":"RAW_QGOH_PS_3:P0015","trace_type":"Structural","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_QGOH_PS_3:AS5","text":"The second movement transfers the truth of ideas to their relation to God, but falsity to a privation of knowledge in the finite mind.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_QGOH_PS_3:P0021","trace_type":"Composite","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"},{"id":"RAW_QGOH_PS_3:P0033","trace_type":"Composite","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"},{"id":"RAW_QGOH_PS_3:P0042","trace_type":"Composite","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_QGOH_PS_3:AS6","text":"The third movement derives reason from common properties of bodies, certainty from adequate ideas, and the necessity of things under a certain species of eternity from reason.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_QGOH_PS_3:P0048","trace_type":"Structural","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"},{"id":"RAW_QGOH_PS_3:P0054","trace_type":"Structural","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"},{"id":"RAW_QGOH_PS_3:P0070","trace_type":"Structural","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"},{"id":"RAW_QGOH_PS_3:P0090","trace_type":"Structural","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"},{"id":"RAW_QGOH_PS_3:P0100","trace_type":"Structural","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_QGOH_PS_3:AS7","text":"The terminus of the text affirms that the idea of any singular thing actually existing necessarily involves the eternal and infinite essence of God.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_QGOH_PS_3:P0104","trace_type":"Direct","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]}],"original_name":"Argumental Spine"},{"number":"2","name":"Load-Bearing Axioms","items":[{"id":"CH_QGOH_PS_3:A1","text":"The Mind perceives neither itself, nor its Body, nor external bodies except through ideas of affections of its Body.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_QGOH_PS_3:P0007","trace_type":"Direct","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"},{"id":"RAW_QGOH_PS_3:P0009","trace_type":"Direct","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_QGOH_PS_3:A2","text":"The idea of an affection of the Body does not adequately express the nature of the Body, nor does the idea of that idea adequately involve the nature of the human Mind.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_QGOH_PS_3:P0003","trace_type":"Direct","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"},{"id":"RAW_QGOH_PS_3:P0005","trace_type":"Direct","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_QGOH_PS_3:A3","text":"Perception externally determined by the fortuitous encounter of things produces confused knowledge; an internal disposition that understands the agreements, differences, and oppositions of things produces clear and distinct knowledge.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_QGOH_PS_3:P0007","trace_type":"Direct","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"},{"id":"RAW_QGOH_PS_3:P0009","trace_type":"Direct","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_QGOH_PS_3:A4","text":"The duration of the Body and of singular things depends upon an infinite series of singular causes, not upon the essence of a singular thing nor upon the absolute nature of God.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_QGOH_PS_3:P0013","trace_type":"Composite","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"},{"id":"RAW_QGOH_PS_3:P0017","trace_type":"Composite","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_QGOH_PS_3:A5","text":"All ideas, insofar as they are referred to God, agree with their ideata and are true.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_QGOH_PS_3:P0021","trace_type":"Direct","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"},{"id":"RAW_QGOH_PS_3:P0023","trace_type":"Direct","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_QGOH_PS_3:A6","text":"Inadequate and confused ideas are not defects in God, but arise from the fact that ideas are referred to a singular Mind; nevertheless, they follow by the same necessity as adequate ideas.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_QGOH_PS_3:P0040","trace_type":"Direct","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"},{"id":"RAW_QGOH_PS_3:P0042","trace_type":"Direct","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_QGOH_PS_3:A7","text":"Falsity has no positive form, but consists in the privation of knowledge that mutilated, confused, and inadequate ideas involve.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_QGOH_PS_3:P0033","trace_type":"Direct","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"},{"id":"RAW_QGOH_PS_3:P0035","trace_type":"Direct","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_QGOH_PS_3:A8","text":"That which is common to all and is equally present in the part as in the whole constitutes the essence of no singular thing.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_QGOH_PS_3:P0044","trace_type":"Direct","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_QGOH_PS_3:A9","text":"That which is common to all and is equally present in the part as in the whole can be conceived only adequately.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_QGOH_PS_3:P0048","trace_type":"Direct","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_QGOH_PS_3:A10","text":"A property common and proper to the human Body and to the external bodies by which it is usually affected constitutes an adequate idea in the human Mind.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_QGOH_PS_3:P0054","trace_type":"Direct","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"},{"id":"RAW_QGOH_PS_3:P0056","trace_type":"Direct","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_QGOH_PS_3:A11","text":"An idea which follows in the Mind from adequate ideas is itself adequate.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_QGOH_PS_3:P0060","trace_type":"Direct","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"},{"id":"RAW_QGOH_PS_3:P0062","trace_type":"Direct","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_QGOH_PS_3:A12","text":"The finite imaginative capacity of the Body, when the multitude of images is exceeded, confuses images and produces transcendental or universal terms from confusion.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_QGOH_PS_3:P0065","trace_type":"Composite","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"},{"id":"RAW_QGOH_PS_3:P0066","trace_type":"Composite","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"},{"id":"RAW_QGOH_PS_3:P0067","trace_type":"Composite","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_QGOH_PS_3:A13","text":"Knowledge of the first kind arises from singular things confusedly presented through the senses or from signs; knowledge of the second kind arises from common notions; intuitive knowledge proceeds from an adequate idea of the attributes of God to knowledge of the essences of things.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_QGOH_PS_3:P0070","trace_type":"Direct","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"},{"id":"RAW_QGOH_PS_3:P0071","trace_type":"Direct","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_QGOH_PS_3:A14","text":"An adequate idea simultaneously contains the idea of itself; whoever truly knows is at the same time certain that he truly knows.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_QGOH_PS_3:P0082","trace_type":"Direct","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"},{"id":"RAW_QGOH_PS_3:P0084","trace_type":"Direct","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_QGOH_PS_3:A15","text":"Reason perceives things as necessary, not as contingent, under a certain species of eternity.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_QGOH_PS_3:P0090","trace_type":"Composite","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"},{"id":"RAW_QGOH_PS_3:P0092","trace_type":"Composite","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"},{"id":"RAW_QGOH_PS_3:P0100","trace_type":"Composite","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_QGOH_PS_3:A16","text":"The idea of any body or singular thing actually existing necessarily involves the eternal and infinite essence of God.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_QGOH_PS_3:P0104","trace_type":"Direct","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_QGOH_PS_3:A17","text":"Consciousness of actions together with ignorance of the causes by which actions are determined produces the opinion of freedom; will without an explained causal idea explains nothing.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_QGOH_PS_3:P0037","trace_type":"Direct","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]}],"original_name":"Load-Bearing Axioms"},{"number":"3","name":"Apophatic Structure","items":[{"id":"CH_QGOH_PS_3:NX1","text":"The Mind does not have knowledge of itself, its Body, or external bodies immediately given and independent of the mediation of affections of the body.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_QGOH_PS_3:P0007","trace_type":"Negative","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"},{"id":"RAW_QGOH_PS_3:P0009","trace_type":"Negative","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_QGOH_PS_3:NX2","text":"The common order of Nature, insofar as it externally determines the Mind through fortuitous encounters, is not a path to clear and distinct knowledge.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_QGOH_PS_3:P0007","trace_type":"Negative","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"},{"id":"RAW_QGOH_PS_3:P0009","trace_type":"Negative","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_QGOH_PS_3:NX3","text":"The duration of one's own Body and of external singular things cannot be adequately known by the human Mind.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_QGOH_PS_3:P0011","trace_type":"Negative","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"},{"id":"RAW_QGOH_PS_3:P0015","trace_type":"Negative","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"},{"id":"RAW_QGOH_PS_3:P0017","trace_type":"Negative","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_QGOH_PS_3:NX4","text":"Falsity constitutes neither a positive mode of thinking nor absolute ignorance.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_QGOH_PS_3:P0025","trace_type":"Negative","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"},{"id":"RAW_QGOH_PS_3:P0035","trace_type":"Negative","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_QGOH_PS_3:NX5","text":"A common property is not the essence of a singular thing.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_QGOH_PS_3:P0044","trace_type":"Negative","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"},{"id":"RAW_QGOH_PS_3:P0046","trace_type":"Negative","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_QGOH_PS_3:NX6","text":"Transcendental terms and imaginary universals are not thereby common notions or rational cognitions.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_QGOH_PS_3:P0065","trace_type":"Negative","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"},{"id":"RAW_QGOH_PS_3:P0066","trace_type":"Negative","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"},{"id":"RAW_QGOH_PS_3:P0070","trace_type":"Negative","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_QGOH_PS_3:NX7","text":"Knowledge of the first kind does not teach one to distinguish the true from the false.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_QGOH_PS_3:P0074","trace_type":"Negative","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"},{"id":"RAW_QGOH_PS_3:P0076","trace_type":"Negative","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"},{"id":"RAW_QGOH_PS_3:P0078","trace_type":"Negative","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_QGOH_PS_3:NX8","text":"Reason does not contemplate things as contingent, nor does it form its judgment from imaginary temporal relations.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_QGOH_PS_3:P0090","trace_type":"Negative","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"},{"id":"RAW_QGOH_PS_3:P0092","trace_type":"Negative","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_QGOH_PS_3:NX9","text":"Freedom of the will is not proved from consciousness of actions, since consciousness of determining causes may be absent.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_QGOH_PS_3:P0037","trace_type":"Negative","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]}],"original_name":"Apophatic Structure"},{"number":"4","name":"Derivative Propositions","items":[{"id":"CH_QGOH_PS_3:D1","text":"Whenever the Mind perceives things from the common order of Nature, it has mutilated and confused knowledge of itself, its Body, and external bodies.","refs":["CH_QGOH_PS_3:A1","CH_QGOH_PS_3:A2","CH_QGOH_PS_3:A3"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"From","passages":[{"id":"RAW_QGOH_PS_3:P0007","trace_type":"Composite","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"},{"id":"RAW_QGOH_PS_3:P0009","trace_type":"Composite","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_QGOH_PS_3:D2","text":"Human knowledge of the duration of its own Body and of external singular things is exceedingly inadequate, because the causes of duration proceed to infinity.","refs":["CH_QGOH_PS_3:A4","CH_QGOH_PS_3:A5","CH_QGOH_PS_3:A6"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"From","passages":[{"id":"RAW_QGOH_PS_3:P0013","trace_type":"Composite","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"},{"id":"RAW_QGOH_PS_3:P0017","trace_type":"Composite","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_QGOH_PS_3:D3","text":"The contingency and corruptibility of particular things do not designate an indeterminacy of causality, but the condition under which their duration cannot be adequately known.","refs":["CH_QGOH_PS_3:A4","CH_QGOH_PS_3:A5","CH_QGOH_PS_3:A6"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"From","passages":[{"id":"RAW_QGOH_PS_3:P0019","trace_type":"Direct","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_QGOH_PS_3:D4","text":"Error does not require a positive false idea, but arises where a positive idea is held without sufficient knowledge of the thing and its causes.","refs":["CH_QGOH_PS_3:A5","CH_QGOH_PS_3:A6","CH_QGOH_PS_3:A7"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"From","passages":[{"id":"RAW_QGOH_PS_3:P0033","trace_type":"Composite","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"},{"id":"RAW_QGOH_PS_3:P0035","trace_type":"Composite","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"},{"id":"RAW_QGOH_PS_3:P0037","trace_type":"Composite","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_QGOH_PS_3:D5","text":"Inadequate ideas are not contingent effects or things posited outside the order of Nature, but necessarily follow when they are referred to a finite Mind.","refs":["CH_QGOH_PS_3:A5","CH_QGOH_PS_3:A6"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"From","passages":[{"id":"RAW_QGOH_PS_3:P0040","trace_type":"Direct","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"},{"id":"RAW_QGOH_PS_3:P0042","trace_type":"Direct","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_QGOH_PS_3:D6","text":"Common notions are the foundations of reasoning, because common properties neither constitute singular essences nor vary in the part from the whole.","refs":["CH_QGOH_PS_3:A8","CH_QGOH_PS_3:A9","CH_QGOH_PS_3:A10"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"From","passages":[{"id":"RAW_QGOH_PS_3:P0044","trace_type":"Composite","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"},{"id":"RAW_QGOH_PS_3:P0048","trace_type":"Composite","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"},{"id":"RAW_QGOH_PS_3:P0064","trace_type":"Composite","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_QGOH_PS_3:D7","text":"The Mind is the more apt to perceive more things adequately insofar as its Body has more things in common with other bodies.","refs":["CH_QGOH_PS_3:A10"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"From","passages":[{"id":"RAW_QGOH_PS_3:P0058","trace_type":"Direct","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_QGOH_PS_3:D8","text":"Imaginary universals vary according to the disposition of the Body, the frequency of affections, and that which the Mind more easily imagines or recollects.","refs":["CH_QGOH_PS_3:A12"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"From","passages":[{"id":"RAW_QGOH_PS_3:P0067","trace_type":"Direct","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"},{"id":"RAW_QGOH_PS_3:P0068","trace_type":"Direct","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_QGOH_PS_3:D9","text":"Knowledge of the first kind is the sole cause of falsity, whereas knowledge of the second and third kinds is necessarily true.","refs":["CH_QGOH_PS_3:A7","CH_QGOH_PS_3:A13"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"From","passages":[{"id":"RAW_QGOH_PS_3:P0074","trace_type":"Direct","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"},{"id":"RAW_QGOH_PS_3:P0076","trace_type":"Direct","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_QGOH_PS_3:D10","text":"The true can be distinguished from the false only by knowledge of the second or third kind, because such a distinction requires an adequate idea of the true and the false.","refs":["CH_QGOH_PS_3:A13","CH_QGOH_PS_3:A14"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"From","passages":[{"id":"RAW_QGOH_PS_3:P0078","trace_type":"Composite","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"},{"id":"RAW_QGOH_PS_3:P0080","trace_type":"Composite","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_QGOH_PS_3:D11","text":"Certainty is not added to a true idea from external proof, but consists in the reflexive adequacy of the idea itself.","refs":["CH_QGOH_PS_3:A5","CH_QGOH_PS_3:A14"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"From","passages":[{"id":"RAW_QGOH_PS_3:P0082","trace_type":"Direct","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"},{"id":"RAW_QGOH_PS_3:P0084","trace_type":"Direct","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_QGOH_PS_3:D12","text":"Imagination, from the fluctuating conjunction of images with temporal orders, presents future or past things as contingent; reason perceives them as necessary.","refs":["CH_QGOH_PS_3:A13","CH_QGOH_PS_3:A15"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"From","passages":[{"id":"RAW_QGOH_PS_3:P0094","trace_type":"Composite","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"},{"id":"RAW_QGOH_PS_3:P0096","trace_type":"Composite","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"},{"id":"RAW_QGOH_PS_3:P0098","trace_type":"Composite","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"},{"id":"RAW_QGOH_PS_3:P0090","trace_type":"Composite","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_QGOH_PS_3:D13","text":"The intelligibility of a singular thing actually existing joins its finite existence with the eternal and infinite essence of God.","refs":["CH_QGOH_PS_3:A16"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"From","passages":[{"id":"RAW_QGOH_PS_3:P0104","trace_type":"Direct","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_QGOH_PS_3:D14","text":"The opinion of freedom is a form of error by which a human being feels his actions, but is ignorant of the causes by which he is determined to act.","refs":["CH_QGOH_PS_3:A7","CH_QGOH_PS_3:A17"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"From","passages":[{"id":"RAW_QGOH_PS_3:P0037","trace_type":"Direct","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]}],"original_name":"Derivative Propositions"},{"number":"5","name":"Super-Derivatives","items":[{"id":"CH_QGOH_PS_3:SD1","text":"Truth and inadequacy do not divide two classes of ideas by positive content, but order the same ideational reality according to its relation to the intellect of God or to the finite Mind.","refs":["CH_QGOH_PS_3:A2","CH_QGOH_PS_3:A5","CH_QGOH_PS_3:A6","CH_QGOH_PS_3:A7"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"From","passages":[{"id":"RAW_QGOH_PS_3:P0005","trace_type":"Structural","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"},{"id":"RAW_QGOH_PS_3:P0021","trace_type":"Structural","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"},{"id":"RAW_QGOH_PS_3:P0033","trace_type":"Structural","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"},{"id":"RAW_QGOH_PS_3:P0042","trace_type":"Structural","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_QGOH_PS_3:SD2","text":"Cognitive finitude is not a diminution of the causality of Nature, but a diminution of the expression of the whole causal nexus in an idea referred to a singular Mind.","refs":["CH_QGOH_PS_3:A4","CH_QGOH_PS_3:A5","CH_QGOH_PS_3:A6"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"From","passages":[{"id":"RAW_QGOH_PS_3:P0013","trace_type":"Composite","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"},{"id":"RAW_QGOH_PS_3:P0042","trace_type":"Composite","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_QGOH_PS_3:SD3","text":"Rationality arises not from generic abstraction from singulars, but from the perception of really common bodily properties, which are invariant in the part and in the whole.","refs":["CH_QGOH_PS_3:A8","CH_QGOH_PS_3:A9","CH_QGOH_PS_3:A10","CH_QGOH_PS_3:A12"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"From","passages":[{"id":"RAW_QGOH_PS_3:P0044","trace_type":"Structural","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"},{"id":"RAW_QGOH_PS_3:P0048","trace_type":"Structural","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"},{"id":"RAW_QGOH_PS_3:P0054","trace_type":"Structural","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"},{"id":"RAW_QGOH_PS_3:P0066","trace_type":"Structural","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_QGOH_PS_3:SD4","text":"The hierarchy of knowledge describes a transition from affection and sign, through structural commonality, to the intuitive deduction of essences; an increase in knowledge is a diminution of dependence on imaginative chance.","refs":["CH_QGOH_PS_3:A3","CH_QGOH_PS_3:A11","CH_QGOH_PS_3:A13"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"From","passages":[{"id":"RAW_QGOH_PS_3:P0009","trace_type":"Structural","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"},{"id":"RAW_QGOH_PS_3:P0060","trace_type":"Structural","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"},{"id":"RAW_QGOH_PS_3:P0070","trace_type":"Structural","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"},{"id":"RAW_QGOH_PS_3:P0071","trace_type":"Structural","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_QGOH_PS_3:SD5","text":"Temporal contingency is an effect of finite imagination, whereas eternal necessity is the form under which reason truly understands the order of things.","refs":["CH_QGOH_PS_3:A4","CH_QGOH_PS_3:A13","CH_QGOH_PS_3:A15"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"From","passages":[{"id":"RAW_QGOH_PS_3:P0094","trace_type":"Composite","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"},{"id":"RAW_QGOH_PS_3:P0098","trace_type":"Composite","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"},{"id":"RAW_QGOH_PS_3:P0100","trace_type":"Composite","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_QGOH_PS_3:SD6","text":"True certainty is an immanent property of adequate knowledge and not a subsequent relation between the Mind and an external tribunal of verification.","refs":["CH_QGOH_PS_3:A5","CH_QGOH_PS_3:A14"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"From","passages":[{"id":"RAW_QGOH_PS_3:P0082","trace_type":"Direct","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"},{"id":"RAW_QGOH_PS_3:P0086","trace_type":"Direct","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]}],"original_name":"Super-Derivatives"},{"number":"6","name":"Deep Apophatics","items":[{"id":"CH_QGOH_PS_3:DNX1","text":"The model of the Mind as a subject immediately present to itself, its Body, and external things must be false.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_QGOH_PS_3:P0007","trace_type":"Negative","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"},{"id":"RAW_QGOH_PS_3:P0009","trace_type":"Negative","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_QGOH_PS_3:DNX2","text":"The model of error as a positive entity, an autonomous faculty, or a causal order separated from truth must be false.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_QGOH_PS_3:P0025","trace_type":"Negative","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"},{"id":"RAW_QGOH_PS_3:P0027","trace_type":"Negative","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"},{"id":"RAW_QGOH_PS_3:P0035","trace_type":"Negative","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_QGOH_PS_3:DNX3","text":"The model of universal knowledge that derives universals from confusion alone or from the depletion of singular differences must be false.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_QGOH_PS_3:P0065","trace_type":"Negative","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"},{"id":"RAW_QGOH_PS_3:P0066","trace_type":"Negative","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"},{"id":"RAW_QGOH_PS_3:P0067","trace_type":"Negative","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_QGOH_PS_3:DNX4","text":"The model of reason separated from the body must be false, since the Mind's aptitude for adequate knowledge depends on the commonalities of the Body with other bodies.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_QGOH_PS_3:P0054","trace_type":"Negative","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"},{"id":"RAW_QGOH_PS_3:P0058","trace_type":"Negative","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_QGOH_PS_3:DNX5","text":"The model of contingency as a property of things themselves must be false, since reason perceives things as necessary and contingency arises from temporal imagination.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_QGOH_PS_3:P0090","trace_type":"Negative","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"},{"id":"RAW_QGOH_PS_3:P0094","trace_type":"Negative","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"},{"id":"RAW_QGOH_PS_3:P0100","trace_type":"Negative","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_QGOH_PS_3:DNX6","text":"The model of truth which supposes that a true idea can be known only through an extrinsic comparison with its ideatum must be false.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_QGOH_PS_3:P0082","trace_type":"Negative","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"},{"id":"RAW_QGOH_PS_3:P0086","trace_type":"Negative","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_QGOH_PS_3:DNX7","text":"The model of freedom that takes consciousness of action for the causal origin of action must be false.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_QGOH_PS_3:P0037","trace_type":"Negative","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]}],"original_name":"Deep Apophatics"},{"number":"7","name":"Super-Apophatics","items":[{"id":"CH_QGOH_PS_3:SNX1","text":"The system leaves no place for a finite Mind outside the infinite causal order, from which it could totally comprehend the duration of singular things or the causes of its own actions.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_QGOH_PS_3:P0013","trace_type":"Structural","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"},{"id":"RAW_QGOH_PS_3:P0017","trace_type":"Structural","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"},{"id":"RAW_QGOH_PS_3:P0037","trace_type":"Structural","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_QGOH_PS_3:SNX2","text":"The system opposes no world of positive falsity to the world of truth, because all ideas, insofar as they are in God, remain true.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_QGOH_PS_3:P0021","trace_type":"Structural","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"},{"id":"RAW_QGOH_PS_3:P0033","trace_type":"Structural","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"},{"id":"RAW_QGOH_PS_3:P0042","trace_type":"Structural","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_QGOH_PS_3:SNX3","text":"The system does not admit imagined time as the ultimate horizon of rational intelligibility, because reason perceives things under a species of eternity.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_QGOH_PS_3:P0094","trace_type":"Structural","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"},{"id":"RAW_QGOH_PS_3:P0100","trace_type":"Structural","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"},{"id":"RAW_QGOH_PS_3:P0102","trace_type":"Structural","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_QGOH_PS_3:SNX4","text":"The system requires no norm of truth outside the adequate idea itself, because truth manifests both itself and falsity.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_QGOH_PS_3:P0082","trace_type":"Composite","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"},{"id":"RAW_QGOH_PS_3:P0086","trace_type":"Composite","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]}],"original_name":"Super-Apophatics"},{"number":"8","name":"Formal Generative Schema","items":[{"id":"CH_QGOH_PS_3:FGS1","text":"Affection of the Body → idea of affection → necessarily mediated knowledge of the Mind, the Body, and the external body.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_QGOH_PS_3:P0007","trace_type":"Direct","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"},{"id":"RAW_QGOH_PS_3:P0009","trace_type":"Direct","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_QGOH_PS_3:FGS2","text":"Mediation through fortuitous external encounter → mutilated and confused idea → knowledge of the first kind → possibility of falsity.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_QGOH_PS_3:P0009","trace_type":"Composite","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"},{"id":"RAW_QGOH_PS_3:P0070","trace_type":"Composite","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"},{"id":"RAW_QGOH_PS_3:P0074","trace_type":"Composite","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_QGOH_PS_3:FGS3","text":"Duration of a singular thing → regress of causes to infinity → impossibility of adequate human knowledge of duration → contingency and corruptibility with respect to knowledge.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_QGOH_PS_3:P0013","trace_type":"Direct","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"},{"id":"RAW_QGOH_PS_3:P0017","trace_type":"Direct","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"},{"id":"RAW_QGOH_PS_3:P0019","trace_type":"Direct","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_QGOH_PS_3:FGS4","text":"Relation of idea to God → agreement with ideatum → truth; relation of the same idea to finite Mind → inadequacy → privation of knowledge.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_QGOH_PS_3:P0021","trace_type":"Composite","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"},{"id":"RAW_QGOH_PS_3:P0033","trace_type":"Composite","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"},{"id":"RAW_QGOH_PS_3:P0042","trace_type":"Composite","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_QGOH_PS_3:FGS5","text":"Common property, equally in part and whole → non-essentiality of the singular → necessarily adequate conception → common notion.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_QGOH_PS_3:P0044","trace_type":"Composite","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"},{"id":"RAW_QGOH_PS_3:P0048","trace_type":"Composite","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"},{"id":"RAW_QGOH_PS_3:P0054","trace_type":"Composite","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_QGOH_PS_3:FGS6","text":"Common notion and adequate idea → succession of adequate ideas → reason → knowledge of the second kind.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_QGOH_PS_3:P0052","trace_type":"Composite","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"},{"id":"RAW_QGOH_PS_3:P0060","trace_type":"Composite","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"},{"id":"RAW_QGOH_PS_3:P0070","trace_type":"Composite","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_QGOH_PS_3:FGS7","text":"Adequate idea → adequate idea of the idea → immanent certainty → distinction of true and false.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_QGOH_PS_3:P0082","trace_type":"Direct","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"},{"id":"RAW_QGOH_PS_3:P0084","trace_type":"Direct","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_QGOH_PS_3:FGS8","text":"Reason → necessity of things → species of eternity → connection of a singular thing actually existing with the eternal and infinite essence of God.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_QGOH_PS_3:P0090","trace_type":"Structural","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"},{"id":"RAW_QGOH_PS_3:P0100","trace_type":"Structural","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"},{"id":"RAW_QGOH_PS_3:P0104","trace_type":"Structural","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]}],"original_name":"Formal Generative Schema"},{"number":"9","name":"Argument Flow Map","items":[{"id":"CH_QGOH_PS_3:AF1","text":"Inadequate idea of affection → external perception from the common order of Nature → mutilated knowledge of Mind, Body, and external bodies → inadequate knowledge of duration.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_QGOH_PS_3:P0007","trace_type":"Structural","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"},{"id":"RAW_QGOH_PS_3:P0009","trace_type":"Structural","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"},{"id":"RAW_QGOH_PS_3:P0011","trace_type":"Structural","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_QGOH_PS_3:AF2","text":"Infinite concatenation of causes → contingency and corruptibility with respect to knowledge → relation of all ideas to God → truth of ideas and falsity as privation.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_QGOH_PS_3:P0017","trace_type":"Structural","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"},{"id":"RAW_QGOH_PS_3:P0019","trace_type":"Structural","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"},{"id":"RAW_QGOH_PS_3:P0021","trace_type":"Structural","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"},{"id":"RAW_QGOH_PS_3:P0033","trace_type":"Structural","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_QGOH_PS_3:AF3","text":"Common properties of bodies → adequate common notions → reason and succession of adequate ideas → distinction among imagination, reason, and intuitive knowledge.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_QGOH_PS_3:P0054","trace_type":"Structural","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"},{"id":"RAW_QGOH_PS_3:P0058","trace_type":"Structural","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"},{"id":"RAW_QGOH_PS_3:P0070","trace_type":"Structural","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"},{"id":"RAW_QGOH_PS_3:P0071","trace_type":"Structural","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_QGOH_PS_3:AF4","text":"Reflexive true idea → certainty → necessity of things through reason → species of eternity → idea of a singular thing actually existing involving the essence of God.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_QGOH_PS_3:P0082","trace_type":"Structural","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"},{"id":"RAW_QGOH_PS_3:P0090","trace_type":"Structural","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"},{"id":"RAW_QGOH_PS_3:P0100","trace_type":"Structural","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"},{"id":"RAW_QGOH_PS_3:P0104","trace_type":"Structural","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]}],"original_name":"Argument Flow Map"},{"number":"10","name":"Map-Effects","items":[{"id":"CH_QGOH_PS_3:ME1","text":"Knowledge of self, Body, and external world is reclassified as a process mediated through affections of the Body, not as the immediate presence of an object to the Mind.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_QGOH_PS_3:P0007","trace_type":"Direct","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"},{"id":"RAW_QGOH_PS_3:P0009","trace_type":"Direct","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_QGOH_PS_3:ME2","text":"Falsity is reclassified from a property of a positive idea into a privation of knowledge within a necessarily produced idea.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_QGOH_PS_3:P0033","trace_type":"Direct","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"},{"id":"RAW_QGOH_PS_3:P0035","trace_type":"Direct","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_QGOH_PS_3:ME3","text":"The opposition between universal and singular is divided into confused imaginary universals and structurally adequate common notions.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_QGOH_PS_3:P0066","trace_type":"Composite","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"},{"id":"RAW_QGOH_PS_3:P0070","trace_type":"Composite","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_QGOH_PS_3:ME4","text":"The Body becomes a positive condition of rational knowledge, because its commonalities with external bodies increase the Mind's aptitude for adequate ideas.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_QGOH_PS_3:P0054","trace_type":"Direct","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"},{"id":"RAW_QGOH_PS_3:P0058","trace_type":"Direct","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_QGOH_PS_3:ME5","text":"Contingency is transferred from the order of things to the imaginative mode in which things are conceived in temporal relations.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_QGOH_PS_3:P0094","trace_type":"Direct","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"},{"id":"RAW_QGOH_PS_3:P0098","trace_type":"Direct","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_QGOH_PS_3:ME6","text":"Certainty is transferred from external signs of confirmation to the reflexive structure of the true idea.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_QGOH_PS_3:P0082","trace_type":"Direct","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"},{"id":"RAW_QGOH_PS_3:P0086","trace_type":"Direct","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]}],"original_name":"Map-Effects"},{"number":"11","name":"Altitude Levels","items":[{"id":"CH_QGOH_PS_3:ALT1","text":"Surface level — The text distinguishes confused knowledge, adequate knowledge, falsity, truth, certainty, imagination, reason, and intuitive knowledge.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_QGOH_PS_3:P0070","trace_type":"Structural","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"},{"id":"RAW_QGOH_PS_3:P0071","trace_type":"Structural","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"},{"id":"RAW_QGOH_PS_3:P0074","trace_type":"Structural","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"},{"id":"RAW_QGOH_PS_3:P0082","trace_type":"Structural","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_QGOH_PS_3:ALT2","text":"Structural level — Human knowledge is constituted from the relation among affections of the Body, the infinite order of causes, finite imaginative capacity, and properties common to bodies.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_QGOH_PS_3:P0009","trace_type":"Structural","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"},{"id":"RAW_QGOH_PS_3:P0013","trace_type":"Structural","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"},{"id":"RAW_QGOH_PS_3:P0058","trace_type":"Structural","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"},{"id":"RAW_QGOH_PS_3:P0065","trace_type":"Structural","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_QGOH_PS_3:ALT3","text":"Metastrategic level — The text transfers the norm of knowledge from the accumulation of images, signs, and vague experiences to the production of ideas from common notions and adequate ideas.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_QGOH_PS_3:P0065","trace_type":"Structural","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"},{"id":"RAW_QGOH_PS_3:P0070","trace_type":"Structural","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"},{"id":"RAW_QGOH_PS_3:P0071","trace_type":"Structural","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_QGOH_PS_3:ALT4","text":"Ontological or temporal level — The truth of ideas is founded in the necessary divine order; contingency pertains to temporal imagination; reason perceives things under a species of eternity.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_QGOH_PS_3:P0021","trace_type":"Structural","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"},{"id":"RAW_QGOH_PS_3:P0094","trace_type":"Structural","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"},{"id":"RAW_QGOH_PS_3:P0100","trace_type":"Structural","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"},{"id":"RAW_QGOH_PS_3:P0102","trace_type":"Structural","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]}],"original_name":"Altitude Levels"},{"number":"12","name":"Minimal Reconstruction Test","items":[{"id":"CH_QGOH_PS_3:MRT1","text":"The given axioms suffice to restore the principal movement from the inadequacy of bodily affections to rational knowledge, certainty, and eternal necessity.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_QGOH_PS_3:P0007","trace_type":"Structural","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"},{"id":"RAW_QGOH_PS_3:P0052","trace_type":"Structural","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"},{"id":"RAW_QGOH_PS_3:P0070","trace_type":"Structural","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"},{"id":"RAW_QGOH_PS_3:P0082","trace_type":"Structural","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"},{"id":"RAW_QGOH_PS_3:P0100","trace_type":"Structural","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_QGOH_PS_3:MRT2","text":"The statement concerning the idea of a singular thing actually existing and the essence of God remains terminal in the given source without explicit demonstration; its full inferential nexus cannot be restored here.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_QGOH_PS_3:P0104","trace_type":"Direct","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_QGOH_PS_3:MRT3","text":"The system is most coherent where it distinguishes divine truth and finite inadequacy through the differing relation of the same idea, not through two positively separate orders of ideas.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_QGOH_PS_3:P0021","trace_type":"Composite","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"},{"id":"RAW_QGOH_PS_3:P0042","trace_type":"Composite","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_QGOH_PS_3:MRT4","text":"A fragile joint remains in the transition from the internal contemplation of things to clearly and distinctly formed knowledge, because the internal condition is indicated but the complete mechanism of its production is not fully explained.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_QGOH_PS_3:P0009","trace_type":"Tension","confidence":"medium","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"},{"id":"RAW_QGOH_PS_3:P0058","trace_type":"Tension","confidence":"medium","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_QGOH_PS_3:MRT5","text":"The distinction between a common notion and an imaginary universal is formally grounded in a property equally existing in part and whole, but its application to singular concepts remains limited in the text.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_QGOH_PS_3:P0044","trace_type":"Tension","confidence":"medium","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"},{"id":"RAW_QGOH_PS_3:P0048","trace_type":"Tension","confidence":"medium","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"},{"id":"RAW_QGOH_PS_3:P0066","trace_type":"Tension","confidence":"medium","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"},{"id":"RAW_QGOH_PS_3:P0067","trace_type":"Tension","confidence":"medium","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_QGOH_PS_3:MRT6","text":"Falsity as privation and the necessity of inadequate ideas cohere with one another only if the distinction is preserved between the positive production of an idea and the defect of knowledge which the idea involves in the finite Mind.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_QGOH_PS_3:P0033","trace_type":"Composite","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"},{"id":"RAW_QGOH_PS_3:P0040","trace_type":"Composite","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"},{"id":"RAW_QGOH_PS_3:P0042","trace_type":"Composite","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_QGOH_PS_3:MRT7","text":"Certainty deduced from the idea of the idea excludes a skeptical structure, but derives its force from the principle of the reflexivity of the adequate idea, not from independently established extrinsic proof.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_QGOH_PS_3:P0082","trace_type":"Structural","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"},{"id":"RAW_QGOH_PS_3:P0084","trace_type":"Structural","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"},{"id":"RAW_QGOH_PS_3:P0086","trace_type":"Structural","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]}],"original_name":"Minimal Reconstruction Test"}],"revision":"CH_QGOH_PS_3@R001","source_variant_id":"canonical:CH_QGOH_PS_3:v1","source_language_code":"la"}],"identity":{"layer":"CH","kicker":"Chapter 07","title":"Part Two - On the Nature and Origin of the Mind · passage 3","context":"Chapter 07","original_title":"Pars secunda - De natura et origine mentis · passage 3","original_context":"Chapter 07"},"original_title":"Pars secunda - De natura et origine mentis · passage 3","title_language_code":"en"},{"id":"CH_QGOH_PS_4","layer":"CH","title":"Part Two - On the Nature and Origin of the Mind · passage 4","order":8,"parents":["RAW_QGOH_PS_4"],"revision":"CH_QGOH_PS_4@R001","sha256":"27bd5e5e4bb21edfa8021b8307af1b8eaf7836ae4833ab353dde9a2036597dc6","sections":[{"number":"1","name":"Argumental Spine","items":[{"id":"CH_QGOH_PS_4:AS1","text":"Textus quaerit utrum ideae rerum singularium cognitionem Dei necessariam praebeant, et utrum voluntas sit facultas libera ab intellectu distincta.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_QGOH_PS_4:P0001","trace_type":"Composite","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"},{"id":"RAW_QGOH_PS_4:P0016","trace_type":"Composite","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"},{"id":"RAW_QGOH_PS_4:P0028","trace_type":"Composite","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_QGOH_PS_4:AS2","text":"Idea rei singularis actualis ad essentiam aeternam et infinitam Dei reducitur, quia res singularis nec concipi nec causari sine Deo potest.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_QGOH_PS_4:P0001","trace_type":"Direct","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_QGOH_PS_4:AS3","text":"Quod in omni idea rei singularis, tam partis quam totius, commune est, cognitionem Dei adaequatam constituit; Mens humana hanc cognitionem necessario possidet.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_QGOH_PS_4:P0005","trace_type":"Composite","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"},{"id":"RAW_QGOH_PS_4:P0007","trace_type":"Composite","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"},{"id":"RAW_QGOH_PS_4:P0011","trace_type":"Composite","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_QGOH_PS_4:AS4","text":"Mens, ut modus cogitandi determinatus, voluntatem liberam non habet; volitiones singulares sunt ipsae affirmationes quas ideae involvunt.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_QGOH_PS_4:P0016","trace_type":"Composite","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"},{"id":"RAW_QGOH_PS_4:P0023","trace_type":"Composite","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"},{"id":"RAW_QGOH_PS_4:P0030","trace_type":"Composite","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_QGOH_PS_4:AS5","text":"Error, dissentio et falsa libertatis experientia ex confusione idearum cum imaginibus, verbis, universalibus et privatione cognitionis oriuntur.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_QGOH_PS_4:P0013","trace_type":"Composite","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"},{"id":"RAW_QGOH_PS_4:P0014","trace_type":"Composite","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"},{"id":"RAW_QGOH_PS_4:P0032","trace_type":"Composite","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"},{"id":"RAW_QGOH_PS_4:P0034","trace_type":"Composite","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_QGOH_PS_4:AS6","text":"Necessitas divinae naturae cognitionem Dei, disciplinam affectuum, vitam socialem rationalem et libertatem practicam eodem systemate connectit.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_QGOH_PS_4:P0013","trace_type":"Structural","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"},{"id":"RAW_QGOH_PS_4:P0016","trace_type":"Structural","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"},{"id":"RAW_QGOH_PS_4:P0046","trace_type":"Structural","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"},{"id":"RAW_QGOH_PS_4:P0047","trace_type":"Structural","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"},{"id":"RAW_QGOH_PS_4:P0048","trace_type":"Structural","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]}],"original_name":"Argumental Spine"},{"number":"2","name":"Load-Bearing Axioms","items":[{"id":"CH_QGOH_PS_4:A1","text":"Idea rei singularis actu existentis essentiam et existentiam rei necessario involvit.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_QGOH_PS_4:P0001","trace_type":"Direct","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_QGOH_PS_4:A2","text":"Res singulares sine Deo concipi non possunt, quia sunt modi attributorum Dei et ab ipso causantur.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_QGOH_PS_4:P0001","trace_type":"Direct","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_QGOH_PS_4:A3","text":"Idea cuiusque rei singularis conceptum attributi divini atque essentiam Dei aeternam et infinitam involvit.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_QGOH_PS_4:P0001","trace_type":"Direct","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_QGOH_PS_4:A4","text":"Id quod omnibus commune est et aeque in parte ac in toto continetur adaequate cognosci potest.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_QGOH_PS_4:P0007","trace_type":"Direct","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_QGOH_PS_4:A5","text":"Mens humana ideas sui Corporis, sui ipsius et corporum externorum actu existentium habet.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_QGOH_PS_4:P0011","trace_type":"Direct","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_QGOH_PS_4:A6","text":"Mens est modus cogitandi certus et determinatus, cuius omnis volitio ab alia causa determinatur in infinitum.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_QGOH_PS_4:P0016","trace_type":"Composite","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"},{"id":"RAW_QGOH_PS_4:P0018","trace_type":"Composite","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_QGOH_PS_4:A7","text":"Facultates universales, ut intellectus et voluntas, nihil sunt praeter entia rationis ex actibus singularibus abstracta.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_QGOH_PS_4:P0020","trace_type":"Direct","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_QGOH_PS_4:A8","text":"Omnis idea, quatenus idea est, affirmationem vel negationem sibi propriam involvit.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_QGOH_PS_4:P0023","trace_type":"Direct","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_QGOH_PS_4:A9","text":"Volitio singularis extra ideam cuius est affirmatio nec esse nec concipi potest.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_QGOH_PS_4:P0025","trace_type":"Direct","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"},{"id":"RAW_QGOH_PS_4:P0026","trace_type":"Direct","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_QGOH_PS_4:A10","text":"Falsitas non est ens positivum, sed privatio cognitionis quam idea mutilata et confusa involvit.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_QGOH_PS_4:P0032","trace_type":"Direct","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_QGOH_PS_4:A11","text":"Imagines et verba ex motibus corporis constituuntur, cum idea sit conceptus Cogitationis.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_QGOH_PS_4:P0034","trace_type":"Direct","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"},{"id":"RAW_QGOH_PS_4:P0035","trace_type":"Direct","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_QGOH_PS_4:A12","text":"Iudicii suspensio perceptionem cognitionis inadequatae exprimit, non liberum assensum aut dissensum.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_QGOH_PS_4:P0041","trace_type":"Direct","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]}],"original_name":"Load-Bearing Axioms"},{"number":"3","name":"Apophatic Structure","items":[{"id":"CH_QGOH_PS_4:NX1","text":"Mens non est causa libera suarum volitionum neque facultatem absolutam volendi et nolendi possidet.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_QGOH_PS_4:P0016","trace_type":"Negative","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"},{"id":"RAW_QGOH_PS_4:P0018","trace_type":"Negative","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_QGOH_PS_4:NX2","text":"Voluntas non est potentia infinita intellectu latior, sed universale nomen affirmationibus singularibus commune.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_QGOH_PS_4:P0020","trace_type":"Negative","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"},{"id":"RAW_QGOH_PS_4:P0040","trace_type":"Negative","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_QGOH_PS_4:NX3","text":"Intellectus non est facultas separata a voluntate, quia idea singularis et affirmatio eius unum idemque sunt.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_QGOH_PS_4:P0023","trace_type":"Negative","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"},{"id":"RAW_QGOH_PS_4:P0030","trace_type":"Negative","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_QGOH_PS_4:NX4","text":"Idea non est imago corporis, pictura muta, neque vox qua res significatur.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_QGOH_PS_4:P0021","trace_type":"Negative","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"},{"id":"RAW_QGOH_PS_4:P0034","trace_type":"Negative","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"},{"id":"RAW_QGOH_PS_4:P0035","trace_type":"Negative","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_QGOH_PS_4:NX5","text":"Existentia rei singularis non est duratio abstracte concepta vel species quantitatis.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_QGOH_PS_4:P0003","trace_type":"Negative","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_QGOH_PS_4:NX6","text":"Falsitas non est certitudo positiva nec error est mera perceptio imaginationis secundum se spectata.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_QGOH_PS_4:P0032","trace_type":"Negative","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"},{"id":"RAW_QGOH_PS_4:P0045","trace_type":"Negative","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_QGOH_PS_4:NX7","text":"Iudicium suspendere non est voluntarie assensum continere, sed ideam insufficientem percipere.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_QGOH_PS_4:P0041","trace_type":"Negative","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_QGOH_PS_4:NX8","text":"Deus non clare cognoscitur per imagines corporum, quibus nomen Dei vulgo adiungitur.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_QGOH_PS_4:P0013","trace_type":"Negative","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]}],"original_name":"Apophatic Structure"},{"number":"4","name":"Derivative Propositions","items":[{"id":"CH_QGOH_PS_4:D1","text":"Quaelibet idea rei singularis actualis cognitionem essentiae Dei aeternae et infinitae continet.","refs":["CH_QGOH_PS_4:A1","CH_QGOH_PS_4:A2","CH_QGOH_PS_4:A3"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"From","passages":[{"id":"RAW_QGOH_PS_4:P0001","trace_type":"Direct","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_QGOH_PS_4:D2","text":"Cognitio Dei quam ideae rerum singularium continent est adaequata, quia id quod eam dat omnibus rebus commune est.","refs":["CH_QGOH_PS_4:A3","CH_QGOH_PS_4:A4"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"From","passages":[{"id":"RAW_QGOH_PS_4:P0005","trace_type":"Composite","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"},{"id":"RAW_QGOH_PS_4:P0007","trace_type":"Composite","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_QGOH_PS_4:D3","text":"Mens humana cognitionem essentiae Dei aeternae et infinitae adaequatam habet.","refs":["CH_QGOH_PS_4:A5","CH_QGOH_PS_4:D1","CH_QGOH_PS_4:D2"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"From","passages":[{"id":"RAW_QGOH_PS_4:P0009","trace_type":"Direct","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"},{"id":"RAW_QGOH_PS_4:P0011","trace_type":"Direct","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_QGOH_PS_4:D4","text":"Voluntas et intellectus unum idemque sunt, quia utrumque in ideis et volitionibus singularibus exhauritur.","refs":["CH_QGOH_PS_4:A7","CH_QGOH_PS_4:A8","CH_QGOH_PS_4:A9"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"From","passages":[{"id":"RAW_QGOH_PS_4:P0020","trace_type":"Composite","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"},{"id":"RAW_QGOH_PS_4:P0023","trace_type":"Composite","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"},{"id":"RAW_QGOH_PS_4:P0030","trace_type":"Composite","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_QGOH_PS_4:D5","text":"Qui equum alatum imaginatur eiusque existentiam nulla idea contraria tollit, existentiam illius necessario affirmat aut de ea dubitare nequit.","refs":["CH_QGOH_PS_4:A8","CH_QGOH_PS_4:A12"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"From","passages":[{"id":"RAW_QGOH_PS_4:P0041","trace_type":"Composite","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"},{"id":"RAW_QGOH_PS_4:P0043","trace_type":"Composite","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_QGOH_PS_4:D6","text":"Multi errores et controversiae non ex positiva falsitate sed ex discrepantia inter res, nomina, imagines et intellectus interpretantium procedunt.","refs":["CH_QGOH_PS_4:A10","CH_QGOH_PS_4:A11"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"From","passages":[{"id":"RAW_QGOH_PS_4:P0013","trace_type":"Composite","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"},{"id":"RAW_QGOH_PS_4:P0014","trace_type":"Composite","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"},{"id":"RAW_QGOH_PS_4:P0032","trace_type":"Composite","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_QGOH_PS_4:D7","text":"Cognitio Dei fundamentum praebet deductionibus ulterioribus et constitutioni tertii generis cognitionis.","refs":["CH_QGOH_PS_4:D2","CH_QGOH_PS_4:D3"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"From","passages":[{"id":"RAW_QGOH_PS_4:P0013","trace_type":"Direct","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_QGOH_PS_4:D8","text":"Necessitas causarum tollit meriti formam quae virtutem tamquam servitutem praemiis externis compensandam concipit.","refs":["CH_QGOH_PS_4:A2","CH_QGOH_PS_4:A6","CH_QGOH_PS_4:D4"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"From","passages":[{"id":"RAW_QGOH_PS_4:P0016","trace_type":"Composite","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"},{"id":"RAW_QGOH_PS_4:P0046","trace_type":"Composite","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]}],"original_name":"Derivative Propositions"},{"number":"5","name":"Super-Derivatives","items":[{"id":"CH_QGOH_PS_4:SD1","text":"Immanentia Dei non cognitionem singularium impedit, sed ipsum principium constituit quo cognitio communis, adaequata et demonstrativa fieri potest.","refs":["CH_QGOH_PS_4:A2","CH_QGOH_PS_4:A3","CH_QGOH_PS_4:A4","CH_QGOH_PS_4:D3"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"From","passages":[{"id":"RAW_QGOH_PS_4:P0001","trace_type":"Structural","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"},{"id":"RAW_QGOH_PS_4:P0007","trace_type":"Structural","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"},{"id":"RAW_QGOH_PS_4:P0013","trace_type":"Structural","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_QGOH_PS_4:SD2","text":"Libertatis locus a potestate contrarie eligendi ad intellectum necessitatis et actionem ex cognitione Dei procedentem transfertur.","refs":["CH_QGOH_PS_4:A6","CH_QGOH_PS_4:A8","CH_QGOH_PS_4:D4","CH_QGOH_PS_4:D8"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"From","passages":[{"id":"RAW_QGOH_PS_4:P0016","trace_type":"Structural","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"},{"id":"RAW_QGOH_PS_4:P0023","trace_type":"Structural","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"},{"id":"RAW_QGOH_PS_4:P0046","trace_type":"Structural","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_QGOH_PS_4:SD3","text":"Ethica affectuum et politica civium gubernatio eandem regulam sequuntur: homines non ut servi determinandi sunt, sed ita dirigendi ut ex ratione ea agant quae optima sunt.","refs":["CH_QGOH_PS_4:A6","CH_QGOH_PS_4:D7","CH_QGOH_PS_4:D8"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"From","passages":[{"id":"RAW_QGOH_PS_4:P0046","trace_type":"Structural","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"},{"id":"RAW_QGOH_PS_4:P0047","trace_type":"Structural","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"},{"id":"RAW_QGOH_PS_4:P0048","trace_type":"Structural","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_QGOH_PS_4:SD4","text":"Certitudo non est imminutio dubitationis, sed positivitatis cognitio; ideo stabilitas opinionis falsae certitudinem non constituit.","refs":["CH_QGOH_PS_4:A10","CH_QGOH_PS_4:A12"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"From","passages":[{"id":"RAW_QGOH_PS_4:P0032","trace_type":"Direct","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]}],"original_name":"Super-Derivatives"},{"number":"6","name":"Deep Apophatics","items":[{"id":"CH_QGOH_PS_4:DNX1","text":"Systema excludit subiectum mentale quod ante ideas suas stat et postea libere assentitur vel dissentit.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_QGOH_PS_4:P0016","trace_type":"Negative","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"},{"id":"RAW_QGOH_PS_4:P0023","trace_type":"Negative","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_QGOH_PS_4:DNX2","text":"Systema excludit cognitionem quae Deum velut obiectum externum et imaginabile apprehendit.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_QGOH_PS_4:P0013","trace_type":"Negative","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_QGOH_PS_4:DNX3","text":"Systema excludit falsitatem ut rem positivam quae ideis veris aequaliter opponatur.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_QGOH_PS_4:P0032","trace_type":"Negative","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"},{"id":"RAW_QGOH_PS_4:P0045","trace_type":"Negative","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_QGOH_PS_4:DNX4","text":"Systema excludit facultates psychologicas universales tamquam causas reales actuum singularium.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_QGOH_PS_4:P0020","trace_type":"Negative","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_QGOH_PS_4:DNX5","text":"Systema excludit oppositionem simplicem inter necessitatem causalitatemque, ex una parte, et vitam rationalem ac socialem, ex altera.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_QGOH_PS_4:P0046","trace_type":"Negative","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"},{"id":"RAW_QGOH_PS_4:P0047","trace_type":"Negative","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"},{"id":"RAW_QGOH_PS_4:P0048","trace_type":"Negative","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]}],"original_name":"Deep Apophatics"},{"number":"7","name":"Super-Apophatics","items":[{"id":"CH_QGOH_PS_4:SNX1","text":"Nulla origo absoluta cogitationis, iudicii vel actionis admissibilis est intra Mentem humanam.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_QGOH_PS_4:P0016","trace_type":"Negative","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"},{"id":"RAW_QGOH_PS_4:P0018","trace_type":"Negative","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_QGOH_PS_4:SNX2","text":"Nulla positio cognoscitiva extra ordinem divinum dari potest, quia res, ideae et earum causae in Deo sunt et per Deum concipiuntur.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_QGOH_PS_4:P0001","trace_type":"Negative","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"},{"id":"RAW_QGOH_PS_4:P0013","trace_type":"Negative","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_QGOH_PS_4:SNX3","text":"Nulla beatitudo legitime fundari potest in praemio extrinseco, cum virtus et Dei cognitio ipsam felicitatem constituant.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_QGOH_PS_4:P0046","trace_type":"Direct","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_QGOH_PS_4:SNX4","text":"Nulla libertas consistit in indifferentia inter contraria, quia indifferentia nihil aliud esset defectus determinationis cognitae.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_QGOH_PS_4:P0045","trace_type":"Negative","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"},{"id":"RAW_QGOH_PS_4:P0047","trace_type":"Negative","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]}],"original_name":"Super-Apophatics"},{"number":"8","name":"Formal Generative Schema","items":[{"id":"CH_QGOH_PS_4:FGS1","text":"Res singularis actu existens → idea essentiam et existentiam involvens → attributum divinum → essentia Dei aeterna et infinita → commune omnibus → cognitio adaequata Dei.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_QGOH_PS_4:P0001","trace_type":"Structural","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"},{"id":"RAW_QGOH_PS_4:P0005","trace_type":"Structural","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"},{"id":"RAW_QGOH_PS_4:P0007","trace_type":"Structural","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_QGOH_PS_4:FGS2","text":"Idea Mentis de Corpore, se ipsa et corporibus externis → participatio cognitionis communis Dei → facultas deductionis adaequatae → tertium genus cognitionis.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_QGOH_PS_4:P0011","trace_type":"Structural","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"},{"id":"RAW_QGOH_PS_4:P0013","trace_type":"Structural","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_QGOH_PS_4:FGS3","text":"Mens ut modus determinatus → recusatio voluntatis liberae → resolutio facultatum in actus singulares → identitas ideae et affirmationis → identitas intellectus et voluntatis.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_QGOH_PS_4:P0016","trace_type":"Structural","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"},{"id":"RAW_QGOH_PS_4:P0020","trace_type":"Structural","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"},{"id":"RAW_QGOH_PS_4:P0023","trace_type":"Structural","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"},{"id":"RAW_QGOH_PS_4:P0030","trace_type":"Structural","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_QGOH_PS_4:FGS4","text":"Confusio idearum cum imaginibus et verbis → universalia pro rebus singularibus habita → falsa libertatis et erroris descriptio → distinctio conceptus, imaginis, verbi et privationis.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_QGOH_PS_4:P0033","trace_type":"Structural","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"},{"id":"RAW_QGOH_PS_4:P0034","trace_type":"Structural","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"},{"id":"RAW_QGOH_PS_4:P0035","trace_type":"Structural","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_QGOH_PS_4:FGS5","text":"Necessitas divina intellecta → imminutio spei praemii et timoris fortunae → aequanimitas, ratio socialis et regimen civium ad libertatem ordinatum.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_QGOH_PS_4:P0046","trace_type":"Structural","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"},{"id":"RAW_QGOH_PS_4:P0047","trace_type":"Structural","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"},{"id":"RAW_QGOH_PS_4:P0048","trace_type":"Structural","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]}],"original_name":"Formal Generative Schema"},{"number":"9","name":"Argument Flow Map","items":[{"id":"CH_QGOH_PS_4:AF1","text":"Idea rei singularis actualis → Deus ut causa immanens → essentia divina in idea implicata → communitas cognitionis → cognitio Dei adaequata in Mente humana.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_QGOH_PS_4:P0001","trace_type":"Structural","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"},{"id":"RAW_QGOH_PS_4:P0007","trace_type":"Structural","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"},{"id":"RAW_QGOH_PS_4:P0011","trace_type":"Structural","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_QGOH_PS_4:AF2","text":"Cognitio adaequata Dei → examen voluntatis → determinatio infinita volitionum → affirmatio ideae intrinseca → voluntas et intellectus idem.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_QGOH_PS_4:P0016","trace_type":"Structural","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"},{"id":"RAW_QGOH_PS_4:P0020","trace_type":"Structural","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"},{"id":"RAW_QGOH_PS_4:P0023","trace_type":"Structural","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"},{"id":"RAW_QGOH_PS_4:P0030","trace_type":"Structural","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_QGOH_PS_4:AF3","text":"Identitas ideae et affirmationis → recusatio facultatis liberae → reinterpretatio dubitationis, falsitatis et erroris → dissolutio objectionum → usus ethicus et politicus necessitatis.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_QGOH_PS_4:P0023","trace_type":"Structural","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"},{"id":"RAW_QGOH_PS_4:P0032","trace_type":"Structural","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"},{"id":"RAW_QGOH_PS_4:P0041","trace_type":"Structural","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"},{"id":"RAW_QGOH_PS_4:P0046","trace_type":"Structural","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"},{"id":"RAW_QGOH_PS_4:P0048","trace_type":"Structural","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]}],"original_name":"Argument Flow Map"},{"number":"10","name":"Map-Effects","items":[{"id":"CH_QGOH_PS_4:ME1","text":"Deus reclassificatur ex obiecto imaginabili vel nomine vulgari in essentiam aeternam et infinitam ab omni idea rei singularis involutam.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_QGOH_PS_4:P0001","trace_type":"Composite","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"},{"id":"RAW_QGOH_PS_4:P0013","trace_type":"Composite","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_QGOH_PS_4:ME2","text":"Voluntas reclassificatur ex causa interiori libera in nomen universale affirmationum singularium causaliter determinatarum.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_QGOH_PS_4:P0020","trace_type":"Composite","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"},{"id":"RAW_QGOH_PS_4:P0030","trace_type":"Composite","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_QGOH_PS_4:ME3","text":"Error reclassificatur ex actu positivo falsum eligente in privationem cognitionis et in incongruentiam inter ideas, imagines et verba.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_QGOH_PS_4:P0032","trace_type":"Composite","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"},{"id":"RAW_QGOH_PS_4:P0034","trace_type":"Composite","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_QGOH_PS_4:ME4","text":"Iudicii suspensio reclassificatur ex imperio voluntatis in cognitionem propriae perceptionis inadequatae.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_QGOH_PS_4:P0041","trace_type":"Direct","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_QGOH_PS_4:ME5","text":"Libert as reclassificatur ex indifferentia electionis in actionem rationalem ex cognitione necessitatis et Dei procedentem.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_QGOH_PS_4:P0016","trace_type":"Structural","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"},{"id":"RAW_QGOH_PS_4:P0046","trace_type":"Structural","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"},{"id":"RAW_QGOH_PS_4:P0047","trace_type":"Structural","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_QGOH_PS_4:ME6","text":"Politica reclassificatur ex disciplina servili in artem qua cives diriguntur ut libere optima agant.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_QGOH_PS_4:P0048","trace_type":"Direct","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]}],"original_name":"Map-Effects"},{"number":"11","name":"Altitude Levels","items":[{"id":"CH_QGOH_PS_4:ALT1","text":"Surface — Textus demonstrat Mentem humanam cognitionem Dei adaequatam habere atque voluntatem liberam nullam habere.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_QGOH_PS_4:P0009","trace_type":"Composite","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"},{"id":"RAW_QGOH_PS_4:P0016","trace_type":"Composite","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_QGOH_PS_4:ALT2","text":"Structural — Ordo idearum, causarum et rerum singularium sub necessitate divina explicat tam cognitionem quam actus volitivos.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_QGOH_PS_4:P0001","trace_type":"Structural","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"},{"id":"RAW_QGOH_PS_4:P0013","trace_type":"Structural","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"},{"id":"RAW_QGOH_PS_4:P0016","trace_type":"Structural","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"},{"id":"RAW_QGOH_PS_4:P0030","trace_type":"Structural","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_QGOH_PS_4:ALT3","text":"Metastrategic — Obiectiones experientiae communis solvuntur non negando experientiam, sed vocabula eius — voluntatem, assensum, dubitationem, errorem — causaliter redefiniendo.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_QGOH_PS_4:P0033","trace_type":"Structural","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"},{"id":"RAW_QGOH_PS_4:P0034","trace_type":"Structural","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"},{"id":"RAW_QGOH_PS_4:P0036","trace_type":"Structural","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"},{"id":"RAW_QGOH_PS_4:P0041","trace_type":"Structural","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_QGOH_PS_4:ALT4","text":"Ontological/Temporal — Existentia singularium est effectus necessitatis aeternae Dei, non duratio abstracta nec eventus contingens extra ordinem divinum.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_QGOH_PS_4:P0003","trace_type":"Direct","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]}],"original_name":"Altitude Levels"},{"number":"12","name":"Minimal Reconstruction Test","items":[{"id":"CH_QGOH_PS_4:MRT1","text":"Axiomata de immanentia Dei, idea rei singularis, communitate cognitionis, determinatione Mentis et affirmatione intrinseca ideae sufficiunt ad nucleum demonstrativum restituendum.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_QGOH_PS_4:P0001","trace_type":"Structural","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"},{"id":"RAW_QGOH_PS_4:P0007","trace_type":"Structural","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"},{"id":"RAW_QGOH_PS_4:P0016","trace_type":"Structural","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"},{"id":"RAW_QGOH_PS_4:P0023","trace_type":"Structural","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_QGOH_PS_4:MRT2","text":"Firmissimus nexus est transitus ab idea rei singularis ad essentiam Dei et inde ad cognitionem Dei adaequatam.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_QGOH_PS_4:P0001","trace_type":"Structural","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"},{"id":"RAW_QGOH_PS_4:P0005","trace_type":"Structural","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"},{"id":"RAW_QGOH_PS_4:P0007","trace_type":"Structural","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_QGOH_PS_4:MRT3","text":"Identitas voluntatis et intellectus cohaeret tantum si voluntas stricte ad affirmationes singulares reducitur; sensus voluntatis ut cupiditatis expresse excludendus manet.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_QGOH_PS_4:P0020","trace_type":"Composite","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"},{"id":"RAW_QGOH_PS_4:P0023","trace_type":"Composite","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"},{"id":"RAW_QGOH_PS_4:P0030","trace_type":"Composite","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_QGOH_PS_4:MRT4","text":"Tensio manet inter cognitionem Dei omnibus datam et inaequalem claritatem qua homines eam actu explicant; textus hanc differentiam imaginibus corporum attribuit, non privationi ipsius cognitionis communis.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_QGOH_PS_4:P0009","trace_type":"Tension","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"},{"id":"RAW_QGOH_PS_4:P0013","trace_type":"Tension","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_QGOH_PS_4:MRT5","text":"Tensio altera manet inter necessitatem universalem et usum normativum doctrinae: ratio socialis et actio libera postulantur, sed transitus a cognitione necessitatis ad efficaciam practicam breviter tantum determinatur.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_QGOH_PS_4:P0016","trace_type":"Tension","confidence":"medium","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"},{"id":"RAW_QGOH_PS_4:P0046","trace_type":"Tension","confidence":"medium","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"},{"id":"RAW_QGOH_PS_4:P0048","trace_type":"Tension","confidence":"medium","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_QGOH_PS_4:MRT6","text":"Restitutio motum textus servat, si demonstratio epistemica, dissolutio voluntatis liberae, correctio erroris et exitus ethico-politicus tamquam una machina continua retinentur.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_QGOH_PS_4:P0001","trace_type":"Structural","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"},{"id":"RAW_QGOH_PS_4:P0013","trace_type":"Structural","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"},{"id":"RAW_QGOH_PS_4:P0016","trace_type":"Structural","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"},{"id":"RAW_QGOH_PS_4:P0032","trace_type":"Structural","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"},{"id":"RAW_QGOH_PS_4:P0046","trace_type":"Structural","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"},{"id":"RAW_QGOH_PS_4:P0048","trace_type":"Structural","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]}],"original_name":"Minimal Reconstruction Test"}],"language_code":"la","language_name":"Latina","translations":[{"id":"CAREVAR_20261015T015104968062Z_917e6c38","language_code":"en","language_name":"English","title":"Part Two - On the Nature and Origin of the Mind · passage 4","source_sha256":"27bd5e5e4bb21edfa8021b8307af1b8eaf7836ae4833ab353dde9a2036597dc6","sha256":"53526276955eed480e387cd6f6b6e79f73048fd9fe796755c5774da14563fb5a","rendition_type":"machine","review_status":"machine","model":"gpt-5.6-terra","created":"2026-10-15T01:51:04.969109+00:00","sections":[{"number":"1","name":"Argumental Spine","items":[{"id":"CH_QGOH_PS_4:AS1","text":"The text asks whether ideas of singular things furnish necessary knowledge of God, and whether the will is a free faculty distinct from the intellect.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_QGOH_PS_4:P0001","trace_type":"Composite","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"},{"id":"RAW_QGOH_PS_4:P0016","trace_type":"Composite","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"},{"id":"RAW_QGOH_PS_4:P0028","trace_type":"Composite","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_QGOH_PS_4:AS2","text":"The idea of an actual singular thing is reduced to the eternal and infinite essence of God, because a singular thing can neither be conceived nor caused without God.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_QGOH_PS_4:P0001","trace_type":"Direct","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_QGOH_PS_4:AS3","text":"What is common in every idea of a singular thing, both of the part and of the whole, constitutes adequate knowledge of God; the human Mind necessarily possesses this knowledge.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_QGOH_PS_4:P0005","trace_type":"Composite","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"},{"id":"RAW_QGOH_PS_4:P0007","trace_type":"Composite","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"},{"id":"RAW_QGOH_PS_4:P0011","trace_type":"Composite","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_QGOH_PS_4:AS4","text":"The Mind, as a determinate mode of thinking, has no free will; singular volitions are the very affirmations that ideas involve.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_QGOH_PS_4:P0016","trace_type":"Composite","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"},{"id":"RAW_QGOH_PS_4:P0023","trace_type":"Composite","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"},{"id":"RAW_QGOH_PS_4:P0030","trace_type":"Composite","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_QGOH_PS_4:AS5","text":"Error, disagreement, and the false experience of freedom arise from the confusion of ideas with images, words, universals, and privation of knowledge.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_QGOH_PS_4:P0013","trace_type":"Composite","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"},{"id":"RAW_QGOH_PS_4:P0014","trace_type":"Composite","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"},{"id":"RAW_QGOH_PS_4:P0032","trace_type":"Composite","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"},{"id":"RAW_QGOH_PS_4:P0034","trace_type":"Composite","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_QGOH_PS_4:AS6","text":"The necessity of the divine nature connects, within one and the same system, knowledge of God, the discipline of the affects, rational social life, and practical freedom.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_QGOH_PS_4:P0013","trace_type":"Structural","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"},{"id":"RAW_QGOH_PS_4:P0016","trace_type":"Structural","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"},{"id":"RAW_QGOH_PS_4:P0046","trace_type":"Structural","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"},{"id":"RAW_QGOH_PS_4:P0047","trace_type":"Structural","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"},{"id":"RAW_QGOH_PS_4:P0048","trace_type":"Structural","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]}],"original_name":"Argumental Spine"},{"number":"2","name":"Load-Bearing Axioms","items":[{"id":"CH_QGOH_PS_4:A1","text":"The idea of an actually existing singular thing necessarily involves the essence and existence of the thing.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_QGOH_PS_4:P0001","trace_type":"Direct","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_QGOH_PS_4:A2","text":"Singular things cannot be conceived without God, because they are modes of God's attributes and are caused by him.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_QGOH_PS_4:P0001","trace_type":"Direct","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_QGOH_PS_4:A3","text":"The idea of each singular thing involves the concept of a divine attribute and the eternal and infinite essence of God.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_QGOH_PS_4:P0001","trace_type":"Direct","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_QGOH_PS_4:A4","text":"That which is common to all and is equally contained in the part as in the whole can be adequately known.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_QGOH_PS_4:P0007","trace_type":"Direct","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_QGOH_PS_4:A5","text":"The human Mind has ideas of its Body, of itself, and of actually existing external bodies.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_QGOH_PS_4:P0011","trace_type":"Direct","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_QGOH_PS_4:A6","text":"The Mind is a certain and determinate mode of thinking, each of whose volitions is determined by another cause to infinity.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_QGOH_PS_4:P0016","trace_type":"Composite","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"},{"id":"RAW_QGOH_PS_4:P0018","trace_type":"Composite","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_QGOH_PS_4:A7","text":"Universal faculties, such as intellect and will, are nothing but beings of reason abstracted from singular acts.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_QGOH_PS_4:P0020","trace_type":"Direct","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_QGOH_PS_4:A8","text":"Every idea, insofar as it is an idea, involves an affirmation or negation proper to itself.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_QGOH_PS_4:P0023","trace_type":"Direct","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_QGOH_PS_4:A9","text":"A singular volition can neither be nor be conceived apart from the idea of which it is the affirmation.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_QGOH_PS_4:P0025","trace_type":"Direct","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"},{"id":"RAW_QGOH_PS_4:P0026","trace_type":"Direct","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_QGOH_PS_4:A10","text":"Falsity is not a positive being, but a privation of knowledge that a mutilated and confused idea involves.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_QGOH_PS_4:P0032","trace_type":"Direct","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_QGOH_PS_4:A11","text":"Images and words are constituted from motions of the body, whereas an idea is a concept of Thought.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_QGOH_PS_4:P0034","trace_type":"Direct","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"},{"id":"RAW_QGOH_PS_4:P0035","trace_type":"Direct","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_QGOH_PS_4:A12","text":"Suspension of judgment expresses the perception of inadequate knowledge, not free assent or dissent.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_QGOH_PS_4:P0041","trace_type":"Direct","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]}],"original_name":"Load-Bearing Axioms"},{"number":"3","name":"Apophatic Structure","items":[{"id":"CH_QGOH_PS_4:NX1","text":"The Mind is not the free cause of its volitions, nor does it possess an absolute faculty of willing and unwilling.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_QGOH_PS_4:P0016","trace_type":"Negative","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"},{"id":"RAW_QGOH_PS_4:P0018","trace_type":"Negative","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_QGOH_PS_4:NX2","text":"The will is not an infinite power broader than the intellect, but a universal name common to singular affirmations.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_QGOH_PS_4:P0020","trace_type":"Negative","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"},{"id":"RAW_QGOH_PS_4:P0040","trace_type":"Negative","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_QGOH_PS_4:NX3","text":"The intellect is not a faculty separate from the will, because a singular idea and its affirmation are one and the same.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_QGOH_PS_4:P0023","trace_type":"Negative","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"},{"id":"RAW_QGOH_PS_4:P0030","trace_type":"Negative","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_QGOH_PS_4:NX4","text":"An idea is not an image of a body, a mute painting, nor the voice by which a thing is signified.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_QGOH_PS_4:P0021","trace_type":"Negative","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"},{"id":"RAW_QGOH_PS_4:P0034","trace_type":"Negative","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"},{"id":"RAW_QGOH_PS_4:P0035","trace_type":"Negative","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_QGOH_PS_4:NX5","text":"The existence of a singular thing is not duration abstractly conceived or a species of quantity.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_QGOH_PS_4:P0003","trace_type":"Negative","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_QGOH_PS_4:NX6","text":"Falsity is not positive certainty, nor is error the mere perception of imagination considered in itself.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_QGOH_PS_4:P0032","trace_type":"Negative","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"},{"id":"RAW_QGOH_PS_4:P0045","trace_type":"Negative","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_QGOH_PS_4:NX7","text":"To suspend judgment is not voluntarily to withhold assent, but to perceive an insufficient idea.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_QGOH_PS_4:P0041","trace_type":"Negative","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_QGOH_PS_4:NX8","text":"God is not clearly known through images of bodies, to which the name of God is commonly attached.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_QGOH_PS_4:P0013","trace_type":"Negative","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]}],"original_name":"Apophatic Structure"},{"number":"4","name":"Derivative Propositions","items":[{"id":"CH_QGOH_PS_4:D1","text":"Every idea of an actual singular thing contains knowledge of the eternal and infinite essence of God.","refs":["CH_QGOH_PS_4:A1","CH_QGOH_PS_4:A2","CH_QGOH_PS_4:A3"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"From","passages":[{"id":"RAW_QGOH_PS_4:P0001","trace_type":"Direct","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_QGOH_PS_4:D2","text":"The knowledge of God that ideas of singular things contain is adequate, because that which gives it is common to all things.","refs":["CH_QGOH_PS_4:A3","CH_QGOH_PS_4:A4"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"From","passages":[{"id":"RAW_QGOH_PS_4:P0005","trace_type":"Composite","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"},{"id":"RAW_QGOH_PS_4:P0007","trace_type":"Composite","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_QGOH_PS_4:D3","text":"The human Mind has adequate knowledge of the eternal and infinite essence of God.","refs":["CH_QGOH_PS_4:A5","CH_QGOH_PS_4:D1","CH_QGOH_PS_4:D2"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"From","passages":[{"id":"RAW_QGOH_PS_4:P0009","trace_type":"Direct","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"},{"id":"RAW_QGOH_PS_4:P0011","trace_type":"Direct","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_QGOH_PS_4:D4","text":"Will and intellect are one and the same, because each is exhausted in singular ideas and volitions.","refs":["CH_QGOH_PS_4:A7","CH_QGOH_PS_4:A8","CH_QGOH_PS_4:A9"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"From","passages":[{"id":"RAW_QGOH_PS_4:P0020","trace_type":"Composite","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"},{"id":"RAW_QGOH_PS_4:P0023","trace_type":"Composite","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"},{"id":"RAW_QGOH_PS_4:P0030","trace_type":"Composite","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_QGOH_PS_4:D5","text":"Whoever imagines a winged horse, and whose idea of its existence is not removed by any contrary idea, necessarily affirms its existence or cannot doubt it.","refs":["CH_QGOH_PS_4:A8","CH_QGOH_PS_4:A12"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"From","passages":[{"id":"RAW_QGOH_PS_4:P0041","trace_type":"Composite","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"},{"id":"RAW_QGOH_PS_4:P0043","trace_type":"Composite","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_QGOH_PS_4:D6","text":"Many errors and controversies proceed not from positive falsity but from discrepancy between things, names, images, and the intellects of interpreters.","refs":["CH_QGOH_PS_4:A10","CH_QGOH_PS_4:A11"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"From","passages":[{"id":"RAW_QGOH_PS_4:P0013","trace_type":"Composite","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"},{"id":"RAW_QGOH_PS_4:P0014","trace_type":"Composite","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"},{"id":"RAW_QGOH_PS_4:P0032","trace_type":"Composite","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_QGOH_PS_4:D7","text":"Knowledge of God provides the foundation for further deductions and for the constitution of the third kind of knowledge.","refs":["CH_QGOH_PS_4:D2","CH_QGOH_PS_4:D3"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"From","passages":[{"id":"RAW_QGOH_PS_4:P0013","trace_type":"Direct","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_QGOH_PS_4:D8","text":"The necessity of causes removes the form of merit that conceives virtue as servitude to be compensated by external rewards.","refs":["CH_QGOH_PS_4:A2","CH_QGOH_PS_4:A6","CH_QGOH_PS_4:D4"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"From","passages":[{"id":"RAW_QGOH_PS_4:P0016","trace_type":"Composite","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"},{"id":"RAW_QGOH_PS_4:P0046","trace_type":"Composite","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]}],"original_name":"Derivative Propositions"},{"number":"5","name":"Super-Derivatives","items":[{"id":"CH_QGOH_PS_4:SD1","text":"The immanence of God does not impede knowledge of singular things, but constitutes the very principle by which common, adequate, and demonstrative knowledge can arise.","refs":["CH_QGOH_PS_4:A2","CH_QGOH_PS_4:A3","CH_QGOH_PS_4:A4","CH_QGOH_PS_4:D3"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"From","passages":[{"id":"RAW_QGOH_PS_4:P0001","trace_type":"Structural","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"},{"id":"RAW_QGOH_PS_4:P0007","trace_type":"Structural","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"},{"id":"RAW_QGOH_PS_4:P0013","trace_type":"Structural","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_QGOH_PS_4:SD2","text":"The locus of freedom is transferred from the power of choosing contraries to the understanding of necessity and action proceeding from knowledge of God.","refs":["CH_QGOH_PS_4:A6","CH_QGOH_PS_4:A8","CH_QGOH_PS_4:D4","CH_QGOH_PS_4:D8"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"From","passages":[{"id":"RAW_QGOH_PS_4:P0016","trace_type":"Structural","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"},{"id":"RAW_QGOH_PS_4:P0023","trace_type":"Structural","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"},{"id":"RAW_QGOH_PS_4:P0046","trace_type":"Structural","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_QGOH_PS_4:SD3","text":"The ethics of the affects and the political governance of citizens follow the same rule: human beings are not to be determined as slaves, but directed so that they act from reason in those things which are best.","refs":["CH_QGOH_PS_4:A6","CH_QGOH_PS_4:D7","CH_QGOH_PS_4:D8"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"From","passages":[{"id":"RAW_QGOH_PS_4:P0046","trace_type":"Structural","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"},{"id":"RAW_QGOH_PS_4:P0047","trace_type":"Structural","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"},{"id":"RAW_QGOH_PS_4:P0048","trace_type":"Structural","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_QGOH_PS_4:SD4","text":"Certainty is not the diminution of doubt, but knowledge of positivity; therefore, the stability of a false opinion does not constitute certainty.","refs":["CH_QGOH_PS_4:A10","CH_QGOH_PS_4:A12"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"From","passages":[{"id":"RAW_QGOH_PS_4:P0032","trace_type":"Direct","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]}],"original_name":"Super-Derivatives"},{"number":"6","name":"Deep Apophatics","items":[{"id":"CH_QGOH_PS_4:DNX1","text":"The system excludes a mental subject that stands before its ideas and subsequently freely assents or dissents.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_QGOH_PS_4:P0016","trace_type":"Negative","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"},{"id":"RAW_QGOH_PS_4:P0023","trace_type":"Negative","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_QGOH_PS_4:DNX2","text":"The system excludes knowledge that apprehends God as an external and imaginable object.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_QGOH_PS_4:P0013","trace_type":"Negative","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_QGOH_PS_4:DNX3","text":"The system excludes falsity as a positive thing equally opposed to true ideas.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_QGOH_PS_4:P0032","trace_type":"Negative","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"},{"id":"RAW_QGOH_PS_4:P0045","trace_type":"Negative","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_QGOH_PS_4:DNX4","text":"The system excludes universal psychological faculties as real causes of singular acts.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_QGOH_PS_4:P0020","trace_type":"Negative","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_QGOH_PS_4:DNX5","text":"The system excludes a simple opposition between necessity and causality, on the one hand, and rational and social life, on the other.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_QGOH_PS_4:P0046","trace_type":"Negative","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"},{"id":"RAW_QGOH_PS_4:P0047","trace_type":"Negative","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"},{"id":"RAW_QGOH_PS_4:P0048","trace_type":"Negative","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]}],"original_name":"Deep Apophatics"},{"number":"7","name":"Super-Apophatics","items":[{"id":"CH_QGOH_PS_4:SNX1","text":"No absolute origin of thought, judgment, or action is admissible within the human Mind.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_QGOH_PS_4:P0016","trace_type":"Negative","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"},{"id":"RAW_QGOH_PS_4:P0018","trace_type":"Negative","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_QGOH_PS_4:SNX2","text":"No cognitive position outside the divine order can be given, because things, ideas, and their causes are in God and are conceived through God.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_QGOH_PS_4:P0001","trace_type":"Negative","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"},{"id":"RAW_QGOH_PS_4:P0013","trace_type":"Negative","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_QGOH_PS_4:SNX3","text":"No blessedness can legitimately be founded on an extrinsic reward, since virtue and knowledge of God constitute happiness itself.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_QGOH_PS_4:P0046","trace_type":"Direct","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_QGOH_PS_4:SNX4","text":"No freedom consists in indifference between contraries, because indifference would be nothing other than a defect of known determination.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_QGOH_PS_4:P0045","trace_type":"Negative","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"},{"id":"RAW_QGOH_PS_4:P0047","trace_type":"Negative","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]}],"original_name":"Super-Apophatics"},{"number":"8","name":"Formal Generative Schema","items":[{"id":"CH_QGOH_PS_4:FGS1","text":"Actually existing singular thing → idea involving essence and existence → divine attribute → eternal and infinite essence of God → common to all → adequate knowledge of God.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_QGOH_PS_4:P0001","trace_type":"Structural","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"},{"id":"RAW_QGOH_PS_4:P0005","trace_type":"Structural","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"},{"id":"RAW_QGOH_PS_4:P0007","trace_type":"Structural","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_QGOH_PS_4:FGS2","text":"Idea of the Mind concerning the Body, itself, and external bodies → participation in the common knowledge of God → capacity for adequate deduction → third kind of knowledge.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_QGOH_PS_4:P0011","trace_type":"Structural","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"},{"id":"RAW_QGOH_PS_4:P0013","trace_type":"Structural","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_QGOH_PS_4:FGS3","text":"Mind as determinate mode → rejection of free will → resolution of faculties into singular acts → identity of idea and affirmation → identity of intellect and will.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_QGOH_PS_4:P0016","trace_type":"Structural","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"},{"id":"RAW_QGOH_PS_4:P0020","trace_type":"Structural","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"},{"id":"RAW_QGOH_PS_4:P0023","trace_type":"Structural","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"},{"id":"RAW_QGOH_PS_4:P0030","trace_type":"Structural","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_QGOH_PS_4:FGS4","text":"Confusion of ideas with images and words → universals taken for singular things → false account of freedom and error → distinction among concept, image, word, and privation.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_QGOH_PS_4:P0033","trace_type":"Structural","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"},{"id":"RAW_QGOH_PS_4:P0034","trace_type":"Structural","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"},{"id":"RAW_QGOH_PS_4:P0035","trace_type":"Structural","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_QGOH_PS_4:FGS5","text":"Divine necessity understood → diminution of hope for reward and fear of fortune → equanimity, social reason, and governance of citizens ordered toward freedom.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_QGOH_PS_4:P0046","trace_type":"Structural","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"},{"id":"RAW_QGOH_PS_4:P0047","trace_type":"Structural","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"},{"id":"RAW_QGOH_PS_4:P0048","trace_type":"Structural","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]}],"original_name":"Formal Generative Schema"},{"number":"9","name":"Argument Flow Map","items":[{"id":"CH_QGOH_PS_4:AF1","text":"Idea of an actual singular thing → God as immanent cause → divine essence implicated in the idea → communality of knowledge → adequate knowledge of God in the human Mind.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_QGOH_PS_4:P0001","trace_type":"Structural","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"},{"id":"RAW_QGOH_PS_4:P0007","trace_type":"Structural","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"},{"id":"RAW_QGOH_PS_4:P0011","trace_type":"Structural","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_QGOH_PS_4:AF2","text":"Adequate knowledge of God → examination of the will → infinite determination of volitions → intrinsic affirmation of the idea → will and intellect the same.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_QGOH_PS_4:P0016","trace_type":"Structural","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"},{"id":"RAW_QGOH_PS_4:P0020","trace_type":"Structural","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"},{"id":"RAW_QGOH_PS_4:P0023","trace_type":"Structural","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"},{"id":"RAW_QGOH_PS_4:P0030","trace_type":"Structural","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_QGOH_PS_4:AF3","text":"Identity of idea and affirmation → rejection of free faculty → reinterpretation of doubt, falsity, and error → dissolution of objections → ethical and political use of necessity.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_QGOH_PS_4:P0023","trace_type":"Structural","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"},{"id":"RAW_QGOH_PS_4:P0032","trace_type":"Structural","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"},{"id":"RAW_QGOH_PS_4:P0041","trace_type":"Structural","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"},{"id":"RAW_QGOH_PS_4:P0046","trace_type":"Structural","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"},{"id":"RAW_QGOH_PS_4:P0048","trace_type":"Structural","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]}],"original_name":"Argument Flow Map"},{"number":"10","name":"Map-Effects","items":[{"id":"CH_QGOH_PS_4:ME1","text":"God is reclassified from an imaginable object or common name into the eternal and infinite essence involved by every idea of a singular thing.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_QGOH_PS_4:P0001","trace_type":"Composite","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"},{"id":"RAW_QGOH_PS_4:P0013","trace_type":"Composite","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_QGOH_PS_4:ME2","text":"The will is reclassified from a free inner cause into a universal name for causally determined singular affirmations.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_QGOH_PS_4:P0020","trace_type":"Composite","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"},{"id":"RAW_QGOH_PS_4:P0030","trace_type":"Composite","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_QGOH_PS_4:ME3","text":"Error is reclassified from a positive act choosing what is false into a privation of knowledge and an incongruity among ideas, images, and words.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_QGOH_PS_4:P0032","trace_type":"Composite","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"},{"id":"RAW_QGOH_PS_4:P0034","trace_type":"Composite","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_QGOH_PS_4:ME4","text":"Suspension of judgment is reclassified from a command of the will into knowledge of one's own inadequate perception.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_QGOH_PS_4:P0041","trace_type":"Direct","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_QGOH_PS_4:ME5","text":"Freedom is reclassified from indifference of choice into rational action proceeding from knowledge of necessity and of God.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_QGOH_PS_4:P0016","trace_type":"Structural","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"},{"id":"RAW_QGOH_PS_4:P0046","trace_type":"Structural","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"},{"id":"RAW_QGOH_PS_4:P0047","trace_type":"Structural","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_QGOH_PS_4:ME6","text":"Politics is reclassified from servile discipline into the art by which citizens are directed to do what is best freely.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_QGOH_PS_4:P0048","trace_type":"Direct","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]}],"original_name":"Map-Effects"},{"number":"11","name":"Altitude Levels","items":[{"id":"CH_QGOH_PS_4:ALT1","text":"Surface — The text demonstrates that the human Mind has adequate knowledge of God and has no free will.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_QGOH_PS_4:P0009","trace_type":"Composite","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"},{"id":"RAW_QGOH_PS_4:P0016","trace_type":"Composite","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_QGOH_PS_4:ALT2","text":"Structural — The order of ideas, causes, and singular things under divine necessity explains both knowledge and volitional acts.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_QGOH_PS_4:P0001","trace_type":"Structural","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"},{"id":"RAW_QGOH_PS_4:P0013","trace_type":"Structural","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"},{"id":"RAW_QGOH_PS_4:P0016","trace_type":"Structural","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"},{"id":"RAW_QGOH_PS_4:P0030","trace_type":"Structural","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_QGOH_PS_4:ALT3","text":"Metastrategic — Objections from common experience are resolved not by denying experience, but by causally redefining its terms — will, assent, doubt, error.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_QGOH_PS_4:P0033","trace_type":"Structural","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"},{"id":"RAW_QGOH_PS_4:P0034","trace_type":"Structural","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"},{"id":"RAW_QGOH_PS_4:P0036","trace_type":"Structural","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"},{"id":"RAW_QGOH_PS_4:P0041","trace_type":"Structural","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_QGOH_PS_4:ALT4","text":"Ontological/Temporal — The existence of singular things is an effect of God's eternal necessity, not abstract duration nor a contingent event outside the divine order.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_QGOH_PS_4:P0003","trace_type":"Direct","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]}],"original_name":"Altitude Levels"},{"number":"12","name":"Minimal Reconstruction Test","items":[{"id":"CH_QGOH_PS_4:MRT1","text":"The axioms concerning the immanence of God, the idea of a singular thing, the communality of knowledge, the determination of the Mind, and the intrinsic affirmation of the idea suffice to restore the demonstrative core.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_QGOH_PS_4:P0001","trace_type":"Structural","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"},{"id":"RAW_QGOH_PS_4:P0007","trace_type":"Structural","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"},{"id":"RAW_QGOH_PS_4:P0016","trace_type":"Structural","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"},{"id":"RAW_QGOH_PS_4:P0023","trace_type":"Structural","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_QGOH_PS_4:MRT2","text":"The strongest connection is the transition from the idea of a singular thing to the essence of God and thence to adequate knowledge of God.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_QGOH_PS_4:P0001","trace_type":"Structural","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"},{"id":"RAW_QGOH_PS_4:P0005","trace_type":"Structural","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"},{"id":"RAW_QGOH_PS_4:P0007","trace_type":"Structural","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_QGOH_PS_4:MRT3","text":"The identity of will and intellect coheres only if the will is strictly reduced to singular affirmations; the sense of will as desire remains expressly to be excluded.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_QGOH_PS_4:P0020","trace_type":"Composite","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"},{"id":"RAW_QGOH_PS_4:P0023","trace_type":"Composite","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"},{"id":"RAW_QGOH_PS_4:P0030","trace_type":"Composite","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_QGOH_PS_4:MRT4","text":"A tension remains between knowledge of God given to all and the unequal clarity with which human beings actually explicate it; the text attributes this difference to images of bodies, not to a privation of common knowledge itself.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_QGOH_PS_4:P0009","trace_type":"Tension","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"},{"id":"RAW_QGOH_PS_4:P0013","trace_type":"Tension","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_QGOH_PS_4:MRT5","text":"A second tension remains between universal necessity and the normative use of the doctrine: social reason and free action are required, but the transition from knowledge of necessity to practical efficacy is determined only briefly.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_QGOH_PS_4:P0016","trace_type":"Tension","confidence":"medium","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"},{"id":"RAW_QGOH_PS_4:P0046","trace_type":"Tension","confidence":"medium","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"},{"id":"RAW_QGOH_PS_4:P0048","trace_type":"Tension","confidence":"medium","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_QGOH_PS_4:MRT6","text":"The reconstruction preserves the movement of the text if epistemic demonstration, the dissolution of free will, the correction of error, and the ethical-political outcome are retained as one continuous machine.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_QGOH_PS_4:P0001","trace_type":"Structural","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"},{"id":"RAW_QGOH_PS_4:P0013","trace_type":"Structural","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"},{"id":"RAW_QGOH_PS_4:P0016","trace_type":"Structural","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"},{"id":"RAW_QGOH_PS_4:P0032","trace_type":"Structural","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"},{"id":"RAW_QGOH_PS_4:P0046","trace_type":"Structural","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"},{"id":"RAW_QGOH_PS_4:P0048","trace_type":"Structural","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]}],"original_name":"Minimal Reconstruction Test"}],"revision":"CH_QGOH_PS_4@R001","source_variant_id":"canonical:CH_QGOH_PS_4:v1","source_language_code":"la"}],"identity":{"layer":"CH","kicker":"Chapter 08","title":"Part Two - On the Nature and Origin of the Mind · passage 4","context":"Chapter 08","original_title":"Pars secunda - De natura et origine mentis · passage 4","original_context":"Chapter 08"},"original_title":"Pars secunda - De natura et origine mentis · passage 4","title_language_code":"en"},{"id":"CH_QGOH_PTDO","layer":"CH","title":"Part Three - On the Origin and Nature of the Affects · passage 1","order":9,"parents":["RAW_QGOH_PTDO"],"revision":"CH_QGOH_PTDO@R001","sha256":"9bb94a014eec6018ade4744da238723605c2ad8b1e81ea2c9b87ac57ab86f8eb","sections":[{"number":"1","name":"Argumental Spine","items":[{"id":"CH_QGOH_PTDO:AS1","text":"Textus quaerit quomodo natura, vires et moderandi ratio affectuum determinandae sint, si homo Naturae exceptio non est.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_QGOH_PTDO:P0003","trace_type":"Direct","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_QGOH_PTDO:AS2","text":"Affectus ex vitiis moralibus vel perturbationibus ordinis Naturae in modos naturales necessario causatos reconstituit.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_QGOH_PTDO:P0004","trace_type":"Composite","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"},{"id":"RAW_QGOH_PTDO:P0005","trace_type":"Composite","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_QGOH_PTDO:AS3","text":"Argumentatio polemica et metaphysico-geometrica iudicium, derisionem et detestationem affectuum causali intellectione substituit.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_QGOH_PTDO:P0002","trace_type":"Structural","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"},{"id":"RAW_QGOH_PTDO:P0003","trace_type":"Structural","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"},{"id":"RAW_QGOH_PTDO:P0004","trace_type":"Structural","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"},{"id":"RAW_QGOH_PTDO:P0005","trace_type":"Structural","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_QGOH_PTDO:AS4","text":"Distinctio causae adaequatae et partialis fundamentum ponit quo agere a pati separatur.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_QGOH_PTDO:P0007","trace_type":"Composite","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"},{"id":"RAW_QGOH_PTDO:P0008","trace_type":"Composite","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"},{"id":"RAW_QGOH_PTDO:P0016","trace_type":"Composite","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"},{"id":"RAW_QGOH_PTDO:P0037","trace_type":"Composite","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_QGOH_PTDO:AS5","text":"Negata corporis in Mentem et Mentis in Corpus causalitate directa, actio et passio mentales ac corporales ut ordines simultanei unius rei describuntur.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_QGOH_PTDO:P0023","trace_type":"Composite","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"},{"id":"RAW_QGOH_PTDO:P0027","trace_type":"Composite","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_QGOH_PTDO:AS6","text":"Ex impossibilitate destructionis internae deducitur conatus perseverandi ut essentia actualis cuiusque rei, tempore indefinito tendens.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_QGOH_PTDO:P0043","trace_type":"Structural","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"},{"id":"RAW_QGOH_PTDO:P0051","trace_type":"Structural","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"},{"id":"RAW_QGOH_PTDO:P0055","trace_type":"Structural","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"},{"id":"RAW_QGOH_PTDO:P0059","trace_type":"Structural","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_QGOH_PTDO:AS7","text":"Conatus Mentis, cum corporis potentiae variationibus et imaginibus coniungitur, Cupiditatem, Laetitiam, Tristitiam et directionem imaginativam generat.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_QGOH_PTDO:P0063","trace_type":"Structural","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"},{"id":"RAW_QGOH_PTDO:P0067","trace_type":"Structural","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"},{"id":"RAW_QGOH_PTDO:P0073","trace_type":"Structural","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"},{"id":"RAW_QGOH_PTDO:P0077","trace_type":"Structural","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"},{"id":"RAW_QGOH_PTDO:P0082","trace_type":"Structural","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"},{"id":"RAW_QGOH_PTDO:P0086","trace_type":"Structural","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]}],"original_name":"Argumental Spine"},{"number":"2","name":"Load-Bearing Axioms","items":[{"id":"CH_QGOH_PTDO:A1","text":"Natura ubique eadem legibus et potentia agit, atque affectus eadem necessitate ac cetera singularia consequuntur.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_QGOH_PTDO:P0004","trace_type":"Direct","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"},{"id":"RAW_QGOH_PTDO:P0005","trace_type":"Direct","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_QGOH_PTDO:A2","text":"Causa adaequata est illa ex qua effectus per ipsam solam clare et distincte intelligitur; causa partialis ad talem intellectionem non sufficit.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_QGOH_PTDO:P0007","trace_type":"Direct","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_QGOH_PTDO:A3","text":"Agere est effectum habere cuius homo causa adaequata est; pati est effectum habere cuius homo causa non nisi partialis est.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_QGOH_PTDO:P0008","trace_type":"Direct","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_QGOH_PTDO:A4","text":"Affectus est affectio Corporis quae ipsius agendi potentiam auget, minuit, iuvat vel coercet, simulque idea eius affectionis.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_QGOH_PTDO:P0009","trace_type":"Direct","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_QGOH_PTDO:A5","text":"Corpus humanum multis modis affici potest, quibus eius agendi potentia variatur vel immutata manet.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_QGOH_PTDO:P0012","trace_type":"Direct","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_QGOH_PTDO:A6","text":"Corpus humanum per mutationes vestigia objectorum et rerum imagines retinere potest.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_QGOH_PTDO:P0014","trace_type":"Direct","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_QGOH_PTDO:A7","text":"Mentis actiones ex ideis adaequatis oriuntur, passiones vero ex ideis inad aequatis pendent.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_QGOH_PTDO:P0016","trace_type":"Composite","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"},{"id":"RAW_QGOH_PTDO:P0037","trace_type":"Composite","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_QGOH_PTDO:A8","text":"Corpus Mentem ad cogitandum non determinat, neque Mens Corpus ad motum, quietem aut aliam corporalem determinationem determinat.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_QGOH_PTDO:P0023","trace_type":"Direct","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_QGOH_PTDO:A9","text":"Mens et Corpus una eademque res sub attributis Cogitationis et Extensionis concipiuntur, ideoque ordines earum actionum et passionum simul natura sunt.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_QGOH_PTDO:P0027","trace_type":"Direct","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_QGOH_PTDO:A10","text":"Nulla res ex sola sua natura destrui potest; destructio causam externam requirit.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_QGOH_PTDO:P0043","trace_type":"Direct","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_QGOH_PTDO:A11","text":"Contrarietas rerum consistit in incompatibilitate qua una alteram destruere potest.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_QGOH_PTDO:P0047","trace_type":"Direct","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_QGOH_PTDO:A12","text":"Unaquaeque res, quantum in se est, in suo esse perseverare conatur.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_QGOH_PTDO:P0051","trace_type":"Direct","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_QGOH_PTDO:A13","text":"Conatus perseverandi nihil est praeter essentiam actualem rei.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_QGOH_PTDO:P0055","trace_type":"Direct","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_QGOH_PTDO:A14","text":"Conatus rei nullum tempus finitum involvit, sed durationem indefinitam.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_QGOH_PTDO:P0059","trace_type":"Direct","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_QGOH_PTDO:A15","text":"Mens sui conatus necessario conscia est.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_QGOH_PTDO:P0063","trace_type":"Direct","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_QGOH_PTDO:A16","text":"Voluntas est conatus Mentis, Appetitus conatus Mentis et Corporis, Cupiditas autem appetitus conscius.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_QGOH_PTDO:P0067","trace_type":"Direct","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_QGOH_PTDO:A17","text":"Variatio potentiae agendi Corporis simul variationem potentiae cogitandi Mentis inducit.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_QGOH_PTDO:P0073","trace_type":"Direct","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_QGOH_PTDO:A18","text":"Mens existentiam actualem Corporis affirmat, et idea quae illam existentiam secludit Mentis essentiae contraria est.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_QGOH_PTDO:P0069","trace_type":"Composite","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"},{"id":"RAW_QGOH_PTDO:P0071","trace_type":"Composite","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]}],"original_name":"Load-Bearing Axioms"},{"number":"3","name":"Apophatic Structure","items":[{"id":"CH_QGOH_PTDO:NX1","text":"Homo non est imperium in imperio neque res extra Naturae ordinem posita.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_QGOH_PTDO:P0002","trace_type":"Negative","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_QGOH_PTDO:NX2","text":"Affectus non sunt vitia Naturae, absurda moraliter exsecranda, neque materia derisionis loco causalitatis.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_QGOH_PTDO:P0002","trace_type":"Negative","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"},{"id":"RAW_QGOH_PTDO:P0003","trace_type":"Negative","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"},{"id":"RAW_QGOH_PTDO:P0005","trace_type":"Negative","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_QGOH_PTDO:NX3","text":"Mens non est princeps incorporeus qui Corpus iubendo movet, nec Corpus est causa efficiens cogitationis.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_QGOH_PTDO:P0023","trace_type":"Negative","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"},{"id":"RAW_QGOH_PTDO:P0027","trace_type":"Negative","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_QGOH_PTDO:NX4","text":"Decretum Mentis liberum non est principium actionis a memoria, imaginatione, appetitu et causis determinantibus separabile.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_QGOH_PTDO:P0032","trace_type":"Negative","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"},{"id":"RAW_QGOH_PTDO:P0033","trace_type":"Negative","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"},{"id":"RAW_QGOH_PTDO:P0034","trace_type":"Negative","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_QGOH_PTDO:NX5","text":"Passio totalem causalitatem sui subiecti excludit, quia causam partialem et ideam inad aequatam implicat.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_QGOH_PTDO:P0007","trace_type":"Composite","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"},{"id":"RAW_QGOH_PTDO:P0008","trace_type":"Composite","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"},{"id":"RAW_QGOH_PTDO:P0037","trace_type":"Composite","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_QGOH_PTDO:NX6","text":"Essentia rei negationem vel destructionem sui non continet, neque contraria in eodem subiecto consistere possunt quatenus se destruunt.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_QGOH_PTDO:P0043","trace_type":"Negative","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"},{"id":"RAW_QGOH_PTDO:P0045","trace_type":"Negative","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"},{"id":"RAW_QGOH_PTDO:P0047","trace_type":"Negative","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_QGOH_PTDO:NX7","text":"Bonum non est causa primigenia appetitus; iudicium boni appetitum sequitur.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_QGOH_PTDO:P0067","trace_type":"Negative","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]}],"original_name":"Apophatic Structure"},{"number":"4","name":"Derivative Propositions","items":[{"id":"CH_QGOH_PTDO:D1","text":"Mens eo pluribus passionibus obnoxia est quo plures ideas inad aequatas habet, atque eo plura agit quo plures adaequatas habet.","refs":["CH_QGOH_PTDO:A7"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"From","passages":[{"id":"RAW_QGOH_PTDO:P0021","trace_type":"Direct","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_QGOH_PTDO:D2","text":"Decreta Mentis, appetitus et determinationes Corporis non sunt causae transversae, sed expressiones simultaneae unius ordinis sub attributis diversis.","refs":["CH_QGOH_PTDO:A8","CH_QGOH_PTDO:A9"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"From","passages":[{"id":"RAW_QGOH_PTDO:P0033","trace_type":"Direct","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_QGOH_PTDO:D3","text":"Homines se liberos credunt quia actionum suarum conscii sunt, causarum autem quibus determinant ur ignorant.","refs":["CH_QGOH_PTDO:A1","CH_QGOH_PTDO:A8","CH_QGOH_PTDO:A16"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"From","passages":[{"id":"RAW_QGOH_PTDO:P0032","trace_type":"Direct","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_QGOH_PTDO:D4","text":"Passio Mentis non est proprietas positiva Mentis ut integrae, sed eius partialitatis et negationis indicium.","refs":["CH_QGOH_PTDO:A2","CH_QGOH_PTDO:A3","CH_QGOH_PTDO:A7"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"From","passages":[{"id":"RAW_QGOH_PTDO:P0041","trace_type":"Direct","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_QGOH_PTDO:D5","text":"Iudicium quo res bona appellatur effectus est Cupiditatis, non causa eius.","refs":["CH_QGOH_PTDO:A12","CH_QGOH_PTDO:A15","CH_QGOH_PTDO:A16"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"From","passages":[{"id":"RAW_QGOH_PTDO:P0067","trace_type":"Direct","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_QGOH_PTDO:D6","text":"Cupiditas, Laetitia et Tristitia affectus primarii constituuntur; Laetitia transitum ad maiorem perfectionem, Tristitia transitum ad minorem perfectionem designat.","refs":["CH_QGOH_PTDO:A4","CH_QGOH_PTDO:A12","CH_QGOH_PTDO:A16","CH_QGOH_PTDO:A17"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"From","passages":[{"id":"RAW_QGOH_PTDO:P0067","trace_type":"Composite","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"},{"id":"RAW_QGOH_PTDO:P0073","trace_type":"Composite","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"},{"id":"RAW_QGOH_PTDO:P0077","trace_type":"Composite","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_QGOH_PTDO:D7","text":"Mens, quatenus potest, ea imaginatur quae Corporis potentiam augent, et contra res memorat quae existentiam eorum secludunt quae potentiam minuunt.","refs":["CH_QGOH_PTDO:A5","CH_QGOH_PTDO:A6","CH_QGOH_PTDO:A12","CH_QGOH_PTDO:A17","CH_QGOH_PTDO:A18"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"From","passages":[{"id":"RAW_QGOH_PTDO:P0082","trace_type":"Direct","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"},{"id":"RAW_QGOH_PTDO:P0086","trace_type":"Direct","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_QGOH_PTDO:D8","text":"Mens praesentem Corporis existentiam affirmare desinit non ex se aut ex mera Corporis cessatione, sed ex alia idea quae illam praesentiam secludit.","refs":["CH_QGOH_PTDO:A10","CH_QGOH_PTDO:A18"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"From","passages":[{"id":"RAW_QGOH_PTDO:P0079","trace_type":"Direct","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"},{"id":"RAW_QGOH_PTDO:P0080","trace_type":"Direct","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]}],"original_name":"Derivative Propositions"},{"number":"5","name":"Super-Derivatives","items":[{"id":"CH_QGOH_PTDO:SD1","text":"Agere et pati gradus causalitatis sunt, non status morales binarii; libertas igitur ut maior adaequatio, non ut exemptio a necessitate, reconfiguratur.","refs":["CH_QGOH_PTDO:A1","CH_QGOH_PTDO:A2","CH_QGOH_PTDO:A3","CH_QGOH_PTDO:A7"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"From","passages":[{"id":"RAW_QGOH_PTDO:P0007","trace_type":"Composite","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"},{"id":"RAW_QGOH_PTDO:P0008","trace_type":"Composite","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"},{"id":"RAW_QGOH_PTDO:P0016","trace_type":"Composite","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"},{"id":"RAW_QGOH_PTDO:P0037","trace_type":"Composite","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_QGOH_PTDO:SD2","text":"Affectus non in sola Mente aut in solo Corpore collocari potest, quia affectio corporalis et idea eius unum processum sub duplici attributorum expressione constituunt.","refs":["CH_QGOH_PTDO:A4","CH_QGOH_PTDO:A8","CH_QGOH_PTDO:A9","CH_QGOH_PTDO:A17"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"From","passages":[{"id":"RAW_QGOH_PTDO:P0009","trace_type":"Structural","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"},{"id":"RAW_QGOH_PTDO:P0023","trace_type":"Structural","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"},{"id":"RAW_QGOH_PTDO:P0027","trace_type":"Structural","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"},{"id":"RAW_QGOH_PTDO:P0073","trace_type":"Structural","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_QGOH_PTDO:SD3","text":"Ordo valorum ex conatu emergit: bonum nomen est rei quam conatus iam appetit, non lex externa quae conatum antecedit.","refs":["CH_QGOH_PTDO:A12","CH_QGOH_PTDO:A13","CH_QGOH_PTDO:A16"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"From","passages":[{"id":"RAW_QGOH_PTDO:P0051","trace_type":"Composite","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"},{"id":"RAW_QGOH_PTDO:P0055","trace_type":"Composite","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"},{"id":"RAW_QGOH_PTDO:P0067","trace_type":"Composite","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_QGOH_PTDO:SD4","text":"Individuum nullum finem internum destructionis habet; eius duratio indefinita est, dum causae externae compositionem non destruunt.","refs":["CH_QGOH_PTDO:A10","CH_QGOH_PTDO:A12","CH_QGOH_PTDO:A13","CH_QGOH_PTDO:A14"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"From","passages":[{"id":"RAW_QGOH_PTDO:P0043","trace_type":"Structural","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"},{"id":"RAW_QGOH_PTDO:P0051","trace_type":"Structural","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"},{"id":"RAW_QGOH_PTDO:P0055","trace_type":"Structural","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"},{"id":"RAW_QGOH_PTDO:P0059","trace_type":"Structural","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_QGOH_PTDO:SD5","text":"Potestas Mentis in affectus, si datur, non potest esse imperium voluntatis supra Naturam, sed sola actio quae ex ideis adaequatis intra ordinem Naturae sequitur.","refs":["CH_QGOH_PTDO:A1","CH_QGOH_PTDO:A7","CH_QGOH_PTDO:A8","CH_QGOH_PTDO:A9"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"From","passages":[{"id":"RAW_QGOH_PTDO:P0004","trace_type":"Structural","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"},{"id":"RAW_QGOH_PTDO:P0023","trace_type":"Structural","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"},{"id":"RAW_QGOH_PTDO:P0027","trace_type":"Structural","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"},{"id":"RAW_QGOH_PTDO:P0037","trace_type":"Structural","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]}],"original_name":"Super-Derivatives"},{"number":"6","name":"Deep Apophatics","items":[{"id":"CH_QGOH_PTDO:DNX1","text":"Subiectum humanum causaliter clausum falsum esse debet, quia Mens, quatenus patitur, pars Naturae est quae per se sine aliis clare intelligi non potest.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_QGOH_PTDO:P0041","trace_type":"Direct","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_QGOH_PTDO:DNX2","text":"Passio essentiam positivam Mentis integrae non exprimit, sed defectum causalitatis plenae et negationem in eius intellectione implicat.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_QGOH_PTDO:P0041","trace_type":"Direct","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_QGOH_PTDO:DNX3","text":"Conscientia actionis scientia causarum non est; conscientia proinde libertatem originariam non probat.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_QGOH_PTDO:P0032","trace_type":"Composite","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"},{"id":"RAW_QGOH_PTDO:P0035","trace_type":"Composite","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_QGOH_PTDO:DNX4","text":"Ordo normativus appetitum antecedens falsus esse debet, si iudicia boni a Cupiditate oriuntur.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_QGOH_PTDO:P0067","trace_type":"Direct","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_QGOH_PTDO:DNX5","text":"Voluntas separata ab appetitu, memoria, imaginatione et dispositione Corporis in systemate locum non habet.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_QGOH_PTDO:P0033","trace_type":"Composite","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"},{"id":"RAW_QGOH_PTDO:P0034","trace_type":"Composite","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"},{"id":"RAW_QGOH_PTDO:P0067","trace_type":"Composite","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]}],"original_name":"Deep Apophatics"},{"number":"7","name":"Super-Apophatics","items":[{"id":"CH_QGOH_PTDO:SNX1","text":"Nullum tribunal extra Naturam manet ex quo affectus tamquam culpa, monstruositas aut defectus absolutus iudicari possit.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_QGOH_PTDO:P0002","trace_type":"Negative","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"},{"id":"RAW_QGOH_PTDO:P0003","trace_type":"Negative","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_QGOH_PTDO:SNX2","text":"Initium actionis omnino inconditum et a necessitate naturali liberum admitti non potest.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_QGOH_PTDO:P0004","trace_type":"Negative","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"},{"id":"RAW_QGOH_PTDO:P0032","trace_type":"Negative","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"},{"id":"RAW_QGOH_PTDO:P0035","trace_type":"Negative","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_QGOH_PTDO:SNX3","text":"Principium intrinsecum quo res se ipsam ad non-esse dirigeret excluditur.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_QGOH_PTDO:P0043","trace_type":"Negative","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"},{"id":"RAW_QGOH_PTDO:P0051","trace_type":"Negative","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"},{"id":"RAW_QGOH_PTDO:P0059","trace_type":"Negative","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_QGOH_PTDO:SNX4","text":"Affectus pure mentalis, corporeis potentiae variationibus carens, indisponibilis est.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_QGOH_PTDO:P0009","trace_type":"Negative","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"},{"id":"RAW_QGOH_PTDO:P0073","trace_type":"Negative","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]}],"original_name":"Super-Apophatics"},{"number":"8","name":"Formal Generative Schema","items":[{"id":"CH_QGOH_PTDO:FGS1","text":"Natura una et necessaria → affectus ut singularia naturalia → cognitio per leges universales.","refs":["CH_QGOH_PTDO:A1"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"From","passages":[{"id":"RAW_QGOH_PTDO:P0004","trace_type":"Direct","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"},{"id":"RAW_QGOH_PTDO:P0005","trace_type":"Direct","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_QGOH_PTDO:FGS2","text":"Causa adaequata vel partialis → agere vel pati → affectus ut actio vel passio.","refs":["CH_QGOH_PTDO:A2","CH_QGOH_PTDO:A3","CH_QGOH_PTDO:A4","CH_QGOH_PTDO:A7"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"From","passages":[{"id":"RAW_QGOH_PTDO:P0007","trace_type":"Structural","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"},{"id":"RAW_QGOH_PTDO:P0008","trace_type":"Structural","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"},{"id":"RAW_QGOH_PTDO:P0009","trace_type":"Structural","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"},{"id":"RAW_QGOH_PTDO:P0016","trace_type":"Structural","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"},{"id":"RAW_QGOH_PTDO:P0037","trace_type":"Structural","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_QGOH_PTDO:FGS3","text":"Distinctio causalitatis attributorum + identitas Mentis et Corporis → imminutio imperii voluntatis → simultaneitas ordinum mentalium et corporalium.","refs":["CH_QGOH_PTDO:A8","CH_QGOH_PTDO:A9"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"From","passages":[{"id":"RAW_QGOH_PTDO:P0023","trace_type":"Direct","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"},{"id":"RAW_QGOH_PTDO:P0027","trace_type":"Direct","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_QGOH_PTDO:FGS4","text":"Absentia destructionis internae + contrarietas destructiva → conatus perseverandi → essentia actualis → duratio indefinita.","refs":["CH_QGOH_PTDO:A10","CH_QGOH_PTDO:A11","CH_QGOH_PTDO:A12","CH_QGOH_PTDO:A13","CH_QGOH_PTDO:A14"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"From","passages":[{"id":"RAW_QGOH_PTDO:P0043","trace_type":"Structural","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"},{"id":"RAW_QGOH_PTDO:P0047","trace_type":"Structural","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"},{"id":"RAW_QGOH_PTDO:P0051","trace_type":"Structural","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"},{"id":"RAW_QGOH_PTDO:P0055","trace_type":"Structural","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"},{"id":"RAW_QGOH_PTDO:P0059","trace_type":"Structural","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_QGOH_PTDO:FGS5","text":"Conatus Mentis conscius + variatio potentiae Corporis et Mentis → Appetitus et Cupiditas → Laetitia et Tristitia → iudicium boni posterius.","refs":["CH_QGOH_PTDO:A15","CH_QGOH_PTDO:A16","CH_QGOH_PTDO:A17"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"From","passages":[{"id":"RAW_QGOH_PTDO:P0063","trace_type":"Structural","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"},{"id":"RAW_QGOH_PTDO:P0067","trace_type":"Structural","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"},{"id":"RAW_QGOH_PTDO:P0073","trace_type":"Structural","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"},{"id":"RAW_QGOH_PTDO:P0077","trace_type":"Structural","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_QGOH_PTDO:FGS6","text":"Vestigia Corporis + affirmatio corporis praesentis + conatus → imaginatio augmenti potentiae et memoria exclusionis imminutivae.","refs":["CH_QGOH_PTDO:A5","CH_QGOH_PTDO:A6","CH_QGOH_PTDO:A12","CH_QGOH_PTDO:A17","CH_QGOH_PTDO:A18"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"From","passages":[{"id":"RAW_QGOH_PTDO:P0014","trace_type":"Structural","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"},{"id":"RAW_QGOH_PTDO:P0069","trace_type":"Structural","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"},{"id":"RAW_QGOH_PTDO:P0071","trace_type":"Structural","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"},{"id":"RAW_QGOH_PTDO:P0082","trace_type":"Structural","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"},{"id":"RAW_QGOH_PTDO:P0086","trace_type":"Structural","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]}],"original_name":"Formal Generative Schema"},{"number":"9","name":"Argument Flow Map","items":[{"id":"CH_QGOH_PTDO:AF1","text":"Homo ut exceptio Naturae → affectus ut vitia iudicata → naturalizatio geometrica affectuum.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_QGOH_PTDO:P0002","trace_type":"Structural","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"},{"id":"RAW_QGOH_PTDO:P0004","trace_type":"Structural","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"},{"id":"RAW_QGOH_PTDO:P0005","trace_type":"Structural","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_QGOH_PTDO:AF2","text":"Naturalizatio → causa adaequata et partialis → actio et passio → ideae adaequatae et inad aequatae.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_QGOH_PTDO:P0007","trace_type":"Structural","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"},{"id":"RAW_QGOH_PTDO:P0008","trace_type":"Structural","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"},{"id":"RAW_QGOH_PTDO:P0016","trace_type":"Structural","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"},{"id":"RAW_QGOH_PTDO:P0037","trace_type":"Structural","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_QGOH_PTDO:AF3","text":"Dualismus imperativus Mentis et Corporis → causalitas intra attributa → una res sub duplici attributo → critica liberi decreti.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_QGOH_PTDO:P0023","trace_type":"Structural","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"},{"id":"RAW_QGOH_PTDO:P0027","trace_type":"Structural","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"},{"id":"RAW_QGOH_PTDO:P0032","trace_type":"Structural","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_QGOH_PTDO:AF4","text":"Destructio externa → conatus-essentialitas → Appetitus conscius → potentiae transitus → affectus primarii → imaginatio conservativa.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_QGOH_PTDO:P0043","trace_type":"Structural","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"},{"id":"RAW_QGOH_PTDO:P0051","trace_type":"Structural","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"},{"id":"RAW_QGOH_PTDO:P0067","trace_type":"Structural","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"},{"id":"RAW_QGOH_PTDO:P0073","trace_type":"Structural","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"},{"id":"RAW_QGOH_PTDO:P0077","trace_type":"Structural","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"},{"id":"RAW_QGOH_PTDO:P0082","trace_type":"Structural","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"},{"id":"RAW_QGOH_PTDO:P0086","trace_type":"Structural","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]}],"original_name":"Argument Flow Map"},{"number":"10","name":"Map-Effects","items":[{"id":"CH_QGOH_PTDO:ME1","text":"Affectus ex obiectis moraliter damnandis in effectus naturales certis causis et proprietatibus intelligibiles transferuntur.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_QGOH_PTDO:P0004","trace_type":"Direct","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"},{"id":"RAW_QGOH_PTDO:P0005","trace_type":"Direct","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_QGOH_PTDO:ME2","text":"Ager agentiae ex oppositione liberae voluntatis et coactionis ad distinctionem causae adaequatae et partialis transfertur.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_QGOH_PTDO:P0007","trace_type":"Composite","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"},{"id":"RAW_QGOH_PTDO:P0008","trace_type":"Composite","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"},{"id":"RAW_QGOH_PTDO:P0016","trace_type":"Composite","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"},{"id":"RAW_QGOH_PTDO:P0037","trace_type":"Composite","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_QGOH_PTDO:ME3","text":"Oppositio Mentis imperantis et Corporis oboedientis dissolvitur in duplicem expressionem unius ordinis.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_QGOH_PTDO:P0023","trace_type":"Direct","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"},{"id":"RAW_QGOH_PTDO:P0027","trace_type":"Direct","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_QGOH_PTDO:ME4","text":"Bonum ex causa praevia eligendi in effectum Cupiditatis reclassificatur.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_QGOH_PTDO:P0067","trace_type":"Direct","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_QGOH_PTDO:ME5","text":"Imaginatio et memoria ex facultatibus voluntarie administratis in operationes conatus, vestigiorum corporalium et variationum potentiae reordinantur.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_QGOH_PTDO:P0014","trace_type":"Composite","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"},{"id":"RAW_QGOH_PTDO:P0033","trace_type":"Composite","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"},{"id":"RAW_QGOH_PTDO:P0082","trace_type":"Composite","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"},{"id":"RAW_QGOH_PTDO:P0086","trace_type":"Composite","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]}],"original_name":"Map-Effects"},{"number":"11","name":"Altitude Levels","items":[{"id":"CH_QGOH_PTDO:ALT1","text":"Surface — Affectus definiuntur ut affectiones Corporis cum ideis earum, et Laetitia, Tristitia, Cupiditas ut formae primariae ponuntur.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_QGOH_PTDO:P0009","trace_type":"Composite","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"},{"id":"RAW_QGOH_PTDO:P0067","trace_type":"Composite","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"},{"id":"RAW_QGOH_PTDO:P0077","trace_type":"Composite","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_QGOH_PTDO:ALT2","text":"Structural — Adequatio causalis, partialitas, parallelismus attributorum, vestigia corporalia et conatus mechanismum actionis, passionis et imaginationis constituunt.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_QGOH_PTDO:P0007","trace_type":"Structural","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"},{"id":"RAW_QGOH_PTDO:P0009","trace_type":"Structural","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"},{"id":"RAW_QGOH_PTDO:P0014","trace_type":"Structural","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"},{"id":"RAW_QGOH_PTDO:P0016","trace_type":"Structural","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"},{"id":"RAW_QGOH_PTDO:P0023","trace_type":"Structural","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"},{"id":"RAW_QGOH_PTDO:P0051","trace_type":"Structural","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"},{"id":"RAW_QGOH_PTDO:P0073","trace_type":"Structural","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_QGOH_PTDO:ALT3","text":"Metastrategic — Textus intellectionem geometricam contra moralizationem affectuum et contra regimen voluntatis in Corpus instituit.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_QGOH_PTDO:P0002","trace_type":"Structural","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"},{"id":"RAW_QGOH_PTDO:P0004","trace_type":"Structural","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"},{"id":"RAW_QGOH_PTDO:P0005","trace_type":"Structural","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"},{"id":"RAW_QGOH_PTDO:P0027","trace_type":"Structural","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_QGOH_PTDO:ALT4","text":"Ontological/Temporal — Essentia rei est conatus affirmativus sine termino temporali interno; destructio et imminutio ex relationibus externis intelliguntur.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_QGOH_PTDO:P0043","trace_type":"Structural","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"},{"id":"RAW_QGOH_PTDO:P0051","trace_type":"Structural","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"},{"id":"RAW_QGOH_PTDO:P0055","trace_type":"Structural","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"},{"id":"RAW_QGOH_PTDO:P0059","trace_type":"Structural","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]}],"original_name":"Altitude Levels"},{"number":"12","name":"Minimal Reconstruction Test","items":[{"id":"CH_QGOH_PTDO:MRT1","text":"Systema extractum sufficit ad restituendam seriem fundamentalem: naturalizatio affectuum, distinctio causalis actionis et passionis, parallelismus Mentis et Corporis, conatus, atque affectuum primariorum formatio.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_QGOH_PTDO:P0004","trace_type":"Structural","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"},{"id":"RAW_QGOH_PTDO:P0005","trace_type":"Structural","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"},{"id":"RAW_QGOH_PTDO:P0007","trace_type":"Structural","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"},{"id":"RAW_QGOH_PTDO:P0023","trace_type":"Structural","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"},{"id":"RAW_QGOH_PTDO:P0027","trace_type":"Structural","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"},{"id":"RAW_QGOH_PTDO:P0051","trace_type":"Structural","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"},{"id":"RAW_QGOH_PTDO:P0067","trace_type":"Structural","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"},{"id":"RAW_QGOH_PTDO:P0077","trace_type":"Structural","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_QGOH_PTDO:MRT2","text":"Argumentatio non est autonoma intra hunc locum, quia demonstratio pendet a doctrinis prioribus de Deo, attributis, idea Corporis et ordine idearum.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_QGOH_PTDO:P0018","trace_type":"Structural","confidence":"medium","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"},{"id":"RAW_QGOH_PTDO:P0025","trace_type":"Structural","confidence":"medium","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"},{"id":"RAW_QGOH_PTDO:P0039","trace_type":"Structural","confidence":"medium","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_QGOH_PTDO:MRT3","text":"Distinctio ideae adaequatae et inad aequatae structuram actionis explicat, sed criterium practicum quo Mens adaequationem augeat hic nondum determinatur.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_QGOH_PTDO:P0016","trace_type":"Direct","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"},{"id":"RAW_QGOH_PTDO:P0021","trace_type":"Direct","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"},{"id":"RAW_QGOH_PTDO:P0037","trace_type":"Direct","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_QGOH_PTDO:MRT4","text":"Firmissimus nexus est concatenatio destructionis externae, conatus, essentiae actualis et durationis indefinitae.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_QGOH_PTDO:P0043","trace_type":"Structural","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"},{"id":"RAW_QGOH_PTDO:P0051","trace_type":"Structural","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"},{"id":"RAW_QGOH_PTDO:P0055","trace_type":"Structural","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"},{"id":"RAW_QGOH_PTDO:P0059","trace_type":"Structural","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_QGOH_PTDO:MRT5","text":"Vocabulum Mentis quae imaginari conatur non est decretum liberum esse debet; cohaerentia servatur tantum si conatus ut necessitas naturae, non ut imperium voluntatis, legitur.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_QGOH_PTDO:P0033","trace_type":"Structural","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"},{"id":"RAW_QGOH_PTDO:P0034","trace_type":"Structural","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"},{"id":"RAW_QGOH_PTDO:P0035","trace_type":"Structural","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"},{"id":"RAW_QGOH_PTDO:P0063","trace_type":"Structural","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"},{"id":"RAW_QGOH_PTDO:P0082","trace_type":"Structural","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"},{"id":"RAW_QGOH_PTDO:P0086","trace_type":"Structural","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_QGOH_PTDO:MRT6","text":"Commissura fragilis manet inter ideam quae Corporis existentiam simpliciter secludit et ideam quae praesentem Corporis existentiam secludit; textus distinctionem adhibet, sed formaliter non explicat.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_QGOH_PTDO:P0069","trace_type":"Tension","confidence":"medium","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"},{"id":"RAW_QGOH_PTDO:P0079","trace_type":"Tension","confidence":"medium","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"},{"id":"RAW_QGOH_PTDO:P0080","trace_type":"Tension","confidence":"medium","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_QGOH_PTDO:MRT7","text":"Taxonomia reliquorum affectuum tantum promittitur; haec unitas machinam trium affectuum primariorum et imaginativae conservationis constituit, non totum systema affectuum complet.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_QGOH_PTDO:P0077","trace_type":"Direct","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]}],"original_name":"Minimal Reconstruction Test"}],"language_code":"la","language_name":"Latina","translations":[{"id":"CAREVAR_20261015T015128892005Z_1e0059ae","language_code":"en","language_name":"English","title":"Part Three - On the Origin and Nature of the Affects · passage 1","source_sha256":"9bb94a014eec6018ade4744da238723605c2ad8b1e81ea2c9b87ac57ab86f8eb","sha256":"fba649d08c93fd26d73cc04d1496f19d5e2cdac7d12377d201ae05dd6989e309","rendition_type":"machine","review_status":"machine","model":"gpt-5.6-terra","created":"2026-10-15T01:51:28.895425+00:00","sections":[{"number":"1","name":"Argumental Spine","items":[{"id":"CH_QGOH_PTDO:AS1","text":"The text asks how the nature, powers, and manner of regulating the affects are to be determined, if the human being is not an exception to Nature.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_QGOH_PTDO:P0003","trace_type":"Direct","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_QGOH_PTDO:AS2","text":"It reconstitutes the affects from moral vices or disturbances of Nature's order into naturally and necessarily caused modes.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_QGOH_PTDO:P0004","trace_type":"Composite","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"},{"id":"RAW_QGOH_PTDO:P0005","trace_type":"Composite","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_QGOH_PTDO:AS3","text":"Polemical and metaphysico-geometrical argumentation replaces judgment, derision, and detestation of the affects with causal understanding.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_QGOH_PTDO:P0002","trace_type":"Structural","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"},{"id":"RAW_QGOH_PTDO:P0003","trace_type":"Structural","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"},{"id":"RAW_QGOH_PTDO:P0004","trace_type":"Structural","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"},{"id":"RAW_QGOH_PTDO:P0005","trace_type":"Structural","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_QGOH_PTDO:AS4","text":"The distinction between adequate and partial cause lays the foundation by which acting is separated from being acted upon.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_QGOH_PTDO:P0007","trace_type":"Composite","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"},{"id":"RAW_QGOH_PTDO:P0008","trace_type":"Composite","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"},{"id":"RAW_QGOH_PTDO:P0016","trace_type":"Composite","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"},{"id":"RAW_QGOH_PTDO:P0037","trace_type":"Composite","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_QGOH_PTDO:AS5","text":"With direct causality of Body upon Mind and of Mind upon Body denied, mental and bodily action and passion are described as simultaneous orders of one thing.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_QGOH_PTDO:P0023","trace_type":"Composite","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"},{"id":"RAW_QGOH_PTDO:P0027","trace_type":"Composite","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_QGOH_PTDO:AS6","text":"From the impossibility of internal destruction is deduced the striving to persevere as the actual essence of each thing, tending toward indefinite time.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_QGOH_PTDO:P0043","trace_type":"Structural","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"},{"id":"RAW_QGOH_PTDO:P0051","trace_type":"Structural","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"},{"id":"RAW_QGOH_PTDO:P0055","trace_type":"Structural","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"},{"id":"RAW_QGOH_PTDO:P0059","trace_type":"Structural","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_QGOH_PTDO:AS7","text":"The striving of the Mind, when conjoined with variations in the body's power and with images, generates Desire, Joy, Sadness, and imaginative direction.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_QGOH_PTDO:P0063","trace_type":"Structural","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"},{"id":"RAW_QGOH_PTDO:P0067","trace_type":"Structural","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"},{"id":"RAW_QGOH_PTDO:P0073","trace_type":"Structural","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"},{"id":"RAW_QGOH_PTDO:P0077","trace_type":"Structural","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"},{"id":"RAW_QGOH_PTDO:P0082","trace_type":"Structural","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"},{"id":"RAW_QGOH_PTDO:P0086","trace_type":"Structural","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]}],"original_name":"Argumental Spine"},{"number":"2","name":"Load-Bearing Axioms","items":[{"id":"CH_QGOH_PTDO:A1","text":"Nature acts everywhere by the same laws and power, and the affects follow with the same necessity as other singular things.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_QGOH_PTDO:P0004","trace_type":"Direct","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"},{"id":"RAW_QGOH_PTDO:P0005","trace_type":"Direct","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_QGOH_PTDO:A2","text":"An adequate cause is that from which the effect is clearly and distinctly understood through it alone; a partial cause does not suffice for such understanding.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_QGOH_PTDO:P0007","trace_type":"Direct","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_QGOH_PTDO:A3","text":"To act is to have an effect of which the human being is an adequate cause; to be acted upon is to have an effect of which the human being is only a partial cause.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_QGOH_PTDO:P0008","trace_type":"Direct","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_QGOH_PTDO:A4","text":"An affect is an affection of the Body that increases, diminishes, assists, or restrains its power of acting, together with the idea of that affection.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_QGOH_PTDO:P0009","trace_type":"Direct","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_QGOH_PTDO:A5","text":"The human Body can be affected in many ways, by which its power of acting is varied or remains unchanged.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_QGOH_PTDO:P0012","trace_type":"Direct","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_QGOH_PTDO:A6","text":"Through modifications, the human Body can retain traces of objects and images of things.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_QGOH_PTDO:P0014","trace_type":"Direct","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_QGOH_PTDO:A7","text":"The Mind's actions arise from adequate ideas, whereas its passions depend upon inadequate ideas.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_QGOH_PTDO:P0016","trace_type":"Composite","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"},{"id":"RAW_QGOH_PTDO:P0037","trace_type":"Composite","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_QGOH_PTDO:A8","text":"The Body does not determine the Mind to think, nor does the Mind determine the Body to motion, rest, or any other bodily determination.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_QGOH_PTDO:P0023","trace_type":"Direct","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_QGOH_PTDO:A9","text":"Mind and Body are conceived as one and the same thing under the attributes of Thought and Extension, and therefore the orders of their actions and passions are simultaneous by nature.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_QGOH_PTDO:P0027","trace_type":"Direct","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_QGOH_PTDO:A10","text":"No thing can be destroyed by its nature alone; destruction requires an external cause.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_QGOH_PTDO:P0043","trace_type":"Direct","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_QGOH_PTDO:A11","text":"The contrariety of things consists in the incompatibility by which one can destroy another.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_QGOH_PTDO:P0047","trace_type":"Direct","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_QGOH_PTDO:A12","text":"Each thing, insofar as it is in itself, strives to persevere in its being.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_QGOH_PTDO:P0051","trace_type":"Direct","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_QGOH_PTDO:A13","text":"The striving to persevere is nothing but the actual essence of a thing.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_QGOH_PTDO:P0055","trace_type":"Direct","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_QGOH_PTDO:A14","text":"The striving of a thing involves no finite time, but an indefinite duration.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_QGOH_PTDO:P0059","trace_type":"Direct","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_QGOH_PTDO:A15","text":"The Mind is necessarily conscious of its striving.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_QGOH_PTDO:P0063","trace_type":"Direct","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_QGOH_PTDO:A16","text":"Will is the striving of the Mind, Appetite the striving of Mind and Body, while Desire is conscious appetite.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_QGOH_PTDO:P0067","trace_type":"Direct","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_QGOH_PTDO:A17","text":"A variation in the Body's power of acting simultaneously brings about a variation in the Mind's power of thinking.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_QGOH_PTDO:P0073","trace_type":"Direct","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_QGOH_PTDO:A18","text":"The Mind affirms the actual existence of the Body, and an idea that excludes that existence is contrary to the essence of the Mind.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_QGOH_PTDO:P0069","trace_type":"Composite","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"},{"id":"RAW_QGOH_PTDO:P0071","trace_type":"Composite","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]}],"original_name":"Load-Bearing Axioms"},{"number":"3","name":"Apophatic Structure","items":[{"id":"CH_QGOH_PTDO:NX1","text":"The human being is not a dominion within a dominion, nor a thing placed outside Nature's order.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_QGOH_PTDO:P0002","trace_type":"Negative","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_QGOH_PTDO:NX2","text":"The affects are not vices of Nature, moral absurdities to be execrated, nor matter for derision in place of causality.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_QGOH_PTDO:P0002","trace_type":"Negative","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"},{"id":"RAW_QGOH_PTDO:P0003","trace_type":"Negative","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"},{"id":"RAW_QGOH_PTDO:P0005","trace_type":"Negative","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_QGOH_PTDO:NX3","text":"The Mind is not an incorporeal ruler that moves the Body by commanding, nor is the Body the efficient cause of thought.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_QGOH_PTDO:P0023","trace_type":"Negative","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"},{"id":"RAW_QGOH_PTDO:P0027","trace_type":"Negative","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_QGOH_PTDO:NX4","text":"A free decree of the Mind is not a principle of action separable from memory, imagination, appetite, and determining causes.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_QGOH_PTDO:P0032","trace_type":"Negative","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"},{"id":"RAW_QGOH_PTDO:P0033","trace_type":"Negative","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"},{"id":"RAW_QGOH_PTDO:P0034","trace_type":"Negative","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_QGOH_PTDO:NX5","text":"Passion excludes the total causality of its subject, because it entails a partial cause and an inadequate idea.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_QGOH_PTDO:P0007","trace_type":"Composite","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"},{"id":"RAW_QGOH_PTDO:P0008","trace_type":"Composite","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"},{"id":"RAW_QGOH_PTDO:P0037","trace_type":"Composite","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_QGOH_PTDO:NX6","text":"The essence of a thing does not contain the negation or destruction of itself, nor can contraries subsist in the same subject insofar as they destroy one another.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_QGOH_PTDO:P0043","trace_type":"Negative","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"},{"id":"RAW_QGOH_PTDO:P0045","trace_type":"Negative","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"},{"id":"RAW_QGOH_PTDO:P0047","trace_type":"Negative","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_QGOH_PTDO:NX7","text":"The good is not the original cause of appetite; judgment of the good follows appetite.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_QGOH_PTDO:P0067","trace_type":"Negative","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]}],"original_name":"Apophatic Structure"},{"number":"4","name":"Derivative Propositions","items":[{"id":"CH_QGOH_PTDO:D1","text":"The Mind is subject to more passions insofar as it has more inadequate ideas, and acts more insofar as it has more adequate ideas.","refs":["CH_QGOH_PTDO:A7"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"From","passages":[{"id":"RAW_QGOH_PTDO:P0021","trace_type":"Direct","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_QGOH_PTDO:D2","text":"The decrees of the Mind, appetites, and determinations of the Body are not transverse causes, but simultaneous expressions of one order under diverse attributes.","refs":["CH_QGOH_PTDO:A8","CH_QGOH_PTDO:A9"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"From","passages":[{"id":"RAW_QGOH_PTDO:P0033","trace_type":"Direct","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_QGOH_PTDO:D3","text":"Human beings believe themselves free because they are conscious of their actions but ignorant of the causes by which they are determined.","refs":["CH_QGOH_PTDO:A1","CH_QGOH_PTDO:A8","CH_QGOH_PTDO:A16"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"From","passages":[{"id":"RAW_QGOH_PTDO:P0032","trace_type":"Direct","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_QGOH_PTDO:D4","text":"A passion of the Mind is not a positive property of the Mind as whole, but an indication of its partiality and negation.","refs":["CH_QGOH_PTDO:A2","CH_QGOH_PTDO:A3","CH_QGOH_PTDO:A7"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"From","passages":[{"id":"RAW_QGOH_PTDO:P0041","trace_type":"Direct","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_QGOH_PTDO:D5","text":"The judgment by which a thing is called good is an effect of Desire, not its cause.","refs":["CH_QGOH_PTDO:A12","CH_QGOH_PTDO:A15","CH_QGOH_PTDO:A16"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"From","passages":[{"id":"RAW_QGOH_PTDO:P0067","trace_type":"Direct","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_QGOH_PTDO:D6","text":"Desire, Joy, and Sadness are constituted as primary affects; Joy signifies a transition to greater perfection, Sadness a transition to lesser perfection.","refs":["CH_QGOH_PTDO:A4","CH_QGOH_PTDO:A12","CH_QGOH_PTDO:A16","CH_QGOH_PTDO:A17"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"From","passages":[{"id":"RAW_QGOH_PTDO:P0067","trace_type":"Composite","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"},{"id":"RAW_QGOH_PTDO:P0073","trace_type":"Composite","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"},{"id":"RAW_QGOH_PTDO:P0077","trace_type":"Composite","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_QGOH_PTDO:D7","text":"The Mind, insofar as it can, imagines those things that increase the Body's power, and conversely recollects things that exclude the existence of those which diminish power.","refs":["CH_QGOH_PTDO:A5","CH_QGOH_PTDO:A6","CH_QGOH_PTDO:A12","CH_QGOH_PTDO:A17","CH_QGOH_PTDO:A18"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"From","passages":[{"id":"RAW_QGOH_PTDO:P0082","trace_type":"Direct","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"},{"id":"RAW_QGOH_PTDO:P0086","trace_type":"Direct","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_QGOH_PTDO:D8","text":"The Mind ceases to affirm the present existence of the Body not through itself or through the mere cessation of the Body, but through another idea that excludes that presence.","refs":["CH_QGOH_PTDO:A10","CH_QGOH_PTDO:A18"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"From","passages":[{"id":"RAW_QGOH_PTDO:P0079","trace_type":"Direct","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"},{"id":"RAW_QGOH_PTDO:P0080","trace_type":"Direct","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]}],"original_name":"Derivative Propositions"},{"number":"5","name":"Super-Derivatives","items":[{"id":"CH_QGOH_PTDO:SD1","text":"Acting and being acted upon are degrees of causality, not binary moral states; freedom is therefore reconfigured as greater adequacy, not as exemption from necessity.","refs":["CH_QGOH_PTDO:A1","CH_QGOH_PTDO:A2","CH_QGOH_PTDO:A3","CH_QGOH_PTDO:A7"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"From","passages":[{"id":"RAW_QGOH_PTDO:P0007","trace_type":"Composite","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"},{"id":"RAW_QGOH_PTDO:P0008","trace_type":"Composite","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"},{"id":"RAW_QGOH_PTDO:P0016","trace_type":"Composite","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"},{"id":"RAW_QGOH_PTDO:P0037","trace_type":"Composite","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_QGOH_PTDO:SD2","text":"An affect cannot be located in Mind alone or in Body alone, because bodily affection and its idea constitute one process under a twofold expression of attributes.","refs":["CH_QGOH_PTDO:A4","CH_QGOH_PTDO:A8","CH_QGOH_PTDO:A9","CH_QGOH_PTDO:A17"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"From","passages":[{"id":"RAW_QGOH_PTDO:P0009","trace_type":"Structural","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"},{"id":"RAW_QGOH_PTDO:P0023","trace_type":"Structural","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"},{"id":"RAW_QGOH_PTDO:P0027","trace_type":"Structural","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"},{"id":"RAW_QGOH_PTDO:P0073","trace_type":"Structural","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_QGOH_PTDO:SD3","text":"The order of values emerges from striving: good is the name of a thing that striving already desires, not an external law that precedes striving.","refs":["CH_QGOH_PTDO:A12","CH_QGOH_PTDO:A13","CH_QGOH_PTDO:A16"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"From","passages":[{"id":"RAW_QGOH_PTDO:P0051","trace_type":"Composite","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"},{"id":"RAW_QGOH_PTDO:P0055","trace_type":"Composite","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"},{"id":"RAW_QGOH_PTDO:P0067","trace_type":"Composite","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_QGOH_PTDO:SD4","text":"The individual has no internal end of destruction; its duration is indefinite, so long as external causes do not destroy its composition.","refs":["CH_QGOH_PTDO:A10","CH_QGOH_PTDO:A12","CH_QGOH_PTDO:A13","CH_QGOH_PTDO:A14"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"From","passages":[{"id":"RAW_QGOH_PTDO:P0043","trace_type":"Structural","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"},{"id":"RAW_QGOH_PTDO:P0051","trace_type":"Structural","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"},{"id":"RAW_QGOH_PTDO:P0055","trace_type":"Structural","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"},{"id":"RAW_QGOH_PTDO:P0059","trace_type":"Structural","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_QGOH_PTDO:SD5","text":"The power of the Mind over the affects, if it is given, cannot be a dominion of will over Nature, but only an action that follows from adequate ideas within Nature's order.","refs":["CH_QGOH_PTDO:A1","CH_QGOH_PTDO:A7","CH_QGOH_PTDO:A8","CH_QGOH_PTDO:A9"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"From","passages":[{"id":"RAW_QGOH_PTDO:P0004","trace_type":"Structural","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"},{"id":"RAW_QGOH_PTDO:P0023","trace_type":"Structural","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"},{"id":"RAW_QGOH_PTDO:P0027","trace_type":"Structural","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"},{"id":"RAW_QGOH_PTDO:P0037","trace_type":"Structural","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]}],"original_name":"Super-Derivatives"},{"number":"6","name":"Deep Apophatics","items":[{"id":"CH_QGOH_PTDO:DNX1","text":"The causally closed human subject must be false, because the Mind, insofar as it suffers, is a part of Nature that cannot be clearly understood through itself without other things.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_QGOH_PTDO:P0041","trace_type":"Direct","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_QGOH_PTDO:DNX2","text":"Passion does not express the positive essence of the whole Mind, but entails a deficiency of complete causality and a negation in its understanding.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_QGOH_PTDO:P0041","trace_type":"Direct","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_QGOH_PTDO:DNX3","text":"Consciousness of action is not knowledge of causes; consciousness therefore does not prove original freedom.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_QGOH_PTDO:P0032","trace_type":"Composite","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"},{"id":"RAW_QGOH_PTDO:P0035","trace_type":"Composite","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_QGOH_PTDO:DNX4","text":"A normative order preceding appetite must be false if judgments of good arise from Desire.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_QGOH_PTDO:P0067","trace_type":"Direct","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_QGOH_PTDO:DNX5","text":"Will separated from appetite, memory, imagination, and the disposition of the Body has no place in the system.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_QGOH_PTDO:P0033","trace_type":"Composite","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"},{"id":"RAW_QGOH_PTDO:P0034","trace_type":"Composite","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"},{"id":"RAW_QGOH_PTDO:P0067","trace_type":"Composite","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]}],"original_name":"Deep Apophatics"},{"number":"7","name":"Super-Apophatics","items":[{"id":"CH_QGOH_PTDO:SNX1","text":"No tribunal outside Nature remains from which the affects can be judged as guilt, monstrosity, or absolute defect.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_QGOH_PTDO:P0002","trace_type":"Negative","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"},{"id":"RAW_QGOH_PTDO:P0003","trace_type":"Negative","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_QGOH_PTDO:SNX2","text":"An absolutely unconditioned beginning of action, free from natural necessity, cannot be admitted.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_QGOH_PTDO:P0004","trace_type":"Negative","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"},{"id":"RAW_QGOH_PTDO:P0032","trace_type":"Negative","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"},{"id":"RAW_QGOH_PTDO:P0035","trace_type":"Negative","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_QGOH_PTDO:SNX3","text":"An intrinsic principle by which a thing would direct itself toward non-being is excluded.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_QGOH_PTDO:P0043","trace_type":"Negative","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"},{"id":"RAW_QGOH_PTDO:P0051","trace_type":"Negative","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"},{"id":"RAW_QGOH_PTDO:P0059","trace_type":"Negative","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_QGOH_PTDO:SNX4","text":"A purely mental affect, lacking bodily variations of power, is unavailable.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_QGOH_PTDO:P0009","trace_type":"Negative","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"},{"id":"RAW_QGOH_PTDO:P0073","trace_type":"Negative","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]}],"original_name":"Super-Apophatics"},{"number":"8","name":"Formal Generative Schema","items":[{"id":"CH_QGOH_PTDO:FGS1","text":"One necessary Nature → affects as natural singular things → knowledge through universal laws.","refs":["CH_QGOH_PTDO:A1"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"From","passages":[{"id":"RAW_QGOH_PTDO:P0004","trace_type":"Direct","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"},{"id":"RAW_QGOH_PTDO:P0005","trace_type":"Direct","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_QGOH_PTDO:FGS2","text":"Adequate or partial cause → acting or being acted upon → affect as action or passion.","refs":["CH_QGOH_PTDO:A2","CH_QGOH_PTDO:A3","CH_QGOH_PTDO:A4","CH_QGOH_PTDO:A7"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"From","passages":[{"id":"RAW_QGOH_PTDO:P0007","trace_type":"Structural","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"},{"id":"RAW_QGOH_PTDO:P0008","trace_type":"Structural","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"},{"id":"RAW_QGOH_PTDO:P0009","trace_type":"Structural","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"},{"id":"RAW_QGOH_PTDO:P0016","trace_type":"Structural","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"},{"id":"RAW_QGOH_PTDO:P0037","trace_type":"Structural","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_QGOH_PTDO:FGS3","text":"Distinction of causality of attributes + identity of Mind and Body → diminution of the dominion of will → simultaneity of mental and bodily orders.","refs":["CH_QGOH_PTDO:A8","CH_QGOH_PTDO:A9"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"From","passages":[{"id":"RAW_QGOH_PTDO:P0023","trace_type":"Direct","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"},{"id":"RAW_QGOH_PTDO:P0027","trace_type":"Direct","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_QGOH_PTDO:FGS4","text":"Absence of internal destruction + destructive contrariety → striving to persevere → actual essence → indefinite duration.","refs":["CH_QGOH_PTDO:A10","CH_QGOH_PTDO:A11","CH_QGOH_PTDO:A12","CH_QGOH_PTDO:A13","CH_QGOH_PTDO:A14"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"From","passages":[{"id":"RAW_QGOH_PTDO:P0043","trace_type":"Structural","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"},{"id":"RAW_QGOH_PTDO:P0047","trace_type":"Structural","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"},{"id":"RAW_QGOH_PTDO:P0051","trace_type":"Structural","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"},{"id":"RAW_QGOH_PTDO:P0055","trace_type":"Structural","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"},{"id":"RAW_QGOH_PTDO:P0059","trace_type":"Structural","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_QGOH_PTDO:FGS5","text":"Conscious striving of the Mind + variation of the power of Body and Mind → Appetite and Desire → Joy and Sadness → subsequent judgment of good.","refs":["CH_QGOH_PTDO:A15","CH_QGOH_PTDO:A16","CH_QGOH_PTDO:A17"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"From","passages":[{"id":"RAW_QGOH_PTDO:P0063","trace_type":"Structural","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"},{"id":"RAW_QGOH_PTDO:P0067","trace_type":"Structural","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"},{"id":"RAW_QGOH_PTDO:P0073","trace_type":"Structural","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"},{"id":"RAW_QGOH_PTDO:P0077","trace_type":"Structural","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_QGOH_PTDO:FGS6","text":"Traces of the Body + affirmation of the present body + striving → imagination of increase of power and memory of diminishing exclusion.","refs":["CH_QGOH_PTDO:A5","CH_QGOH_PTDO:A6","CH_QGOH_PTDO:A12","CH_QGOH_PTDO:A17","CH_QGOH_PTDO:A18"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"From","passages":[{"id":"RAW_QGOH_PTDO:P0014","trace_type":"Structural","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"},{"id":"RAW_QGOH_PTDO:P0069","trace_type":"Structural","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"},{"id":"RAW_QGOH_PTDO:P0071","trace_type":"Structural","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"},{"id":"RAW_QGOH_PTDO:P0082","trace_type":"Structural","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"},{"id":"RAW_QGOH_PTDO:P0086","trace_type":"Structural","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]}],"original_name":"Formal Generative Schema"},{"number":"9","name":"Argument Flow Map","items":[{"id":"CH_QGOH_PTDO:AF1","text":"Human being as exception to Nature → affects judged as vices → geometrical naturalization of affects.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_QGOH_PTDO:P0002","trace_type":"Structural","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"},{"id":"RAW_QGOH_PTDO:P0004","trace_type":"Structural","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"},{"id":"RAW_QGOH_PTDO:P0005","trace_type":"Structural","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_QGOH_PTDO:AF2","text":"Naturalization → adequate and partial cause → action and passion → adequate and inadequate ideas.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_QGOH_PTDO:P0007","trace_type":"Structural","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"},{"id":"RAW_QGOH_PTDO:P0008","trace_type":"Structural","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"},{"id":"RAW_QGOH_PTDO:P0016","trace_type":"Structural","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"},{"id":"RAW_QGOH_PTDO:P0037","trace_type":"Structural","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_QGOH_PTDO:AF3","text":"Imperative dualism of Mind and Body → causality within attributes → one thing under a twofold attribute → critique of free decree.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_QGOH_PTDO:P0023","trace_type":"Structural","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"},{"id":"RAW_QGOH_PTDO:P0027","trace_type":"Structural","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"},{"id":"RAW_QGOH_PTDO:P0032","trace_type":"Structural","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_QGOH_PTDO:AF4","text":"External destruction → striving-essence → conscious Appetite → transitions of power → primary affects → preservative imagination.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_QGOH_PTDO:P0043","trace_type":"Structural","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"},{"id":"RAW_QGOH_PTDO:P0051","trace_type":"Structural","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"},{"id":"RAW_QGOH_PTDO:P0067","trace_type":"Structural","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"},{"id":"RAW_QGOH_PTDO:P0073","trace_type":"Structural","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"},{"id":"RAW_QGOH_PTDO:P0077","trace_type":"Structural","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"},{"id":"RAW_QGOH_PTDO:P0082","trace_type":"Structural","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"},{"id":"RAW_QGOH_PTDO:P0086","trace_type":"Structural","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]}],"original_name":"Argument Flow Map"},{"number":"10","name":"Map-Effects","items":[{"id":"CH_QGOH_PTDO:ME1","text":"The affects are transferred from morally condemnable objects into natural effects intelligible through determinate causes and properties.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_QGOH_PTDO:P0004","trace_type":"Direct","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"},{"id":"RAW_QGOH_PTDO:P0005","trace_type":"Direct","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_QGOH_PTDO:ME2","text":"The field of agency is transferred from the opposition of free will and compulsion to the distinction of adequate and partial cause.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_QGOH_PTDO:P0007","trace_type":"Composite","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"},{"id":"RAW_QGOH_PTDO:P0008","trace_type":"Composite","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"},{"id":"RAW_QGOH_PTDO:P0016","trace_type":"Composite","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"},{"id":"RAW_QGOH_PTDO:P0037","trace_type":"Composite","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_QGOH_PTDO:ME3","text":"The opposition of commanding Mind and obedient Body is dissolved into the twofold expression of one order.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_QGOH_PTDO:P0023","trace_type":"Direct","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"},{"id":"RAW_QGOH_PTDO:P0027","trace_type":"Direct","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_QGOH_PTDO:ME4","text":"The good is reclassified from a prior cause of choosing into an effect of Desire.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_QGOH_PTDO:P0067","trace_type":"Direct","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_QGOH_PTDO:ME5","text":"Imagination and memory are reordered from voluntarily administered faculties into operations of striving, bodily traces, and variations of power.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_QGOH_PTDO:P0014","trace_type":"Composite","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"},{"id":"RAW_QGOH_PTDO:P0033","trace_type":"Composite","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"},{"id":"RAW_QGOH_PTDO:P0082","trace_type":"Composite","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"},{"id":"RAW_QGOH_PTDO:P0086","trace_type":"Composite","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]}],"original_name":"Map-Effects"},{"number":"11","name":"Altitude Levels","items":[{"id":"CH_QGOH_PTDO:ALT1","text":"Surface — Affects are defined as affections of the Body together with their ideas, and Joy, Sadness, and Desire are posited as primary forms.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_QGOH_PTDO:P0009","trace_type":"Composite","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"},{"id":"RAW_QGOH_PTDO:P0067","trace_type":"Composite","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"},{"id":"RAW_QGOH_PTDO:P0077","trace_type":"Composite","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_QGOH_PTDO:ALT2","text":"Structural — Causal adequacy, partiality, parallelism of attributes, bodily traces, and striving constitute the mechanism of action, passion, and imagination.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_QGOH_PTDO:P0007","trace_type":"Structural","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"},{"id":"RAW_QGOH_PTDO:P0009","trace_type":"Structural","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"},{"id":"RAW_QGOH_PTDO:P0014","trace_type":"Structural","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"},{"id":"RAW_QGOH_PTDO:P0016","trace_type":"Structural","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"},{"id":"RAW_QGOH_PTDO:P0023","trace_type":"Structural","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"},{"id":"RAW_QGOH_PTDO:P0051","trace_type":"Structural","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"},{"id":"RAW_QGOH_PTDO:P0073","trace_type":"Structural","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_QGOH_PTDO:ALT3","text":"Metastrategic — The text establishes geometrical understanding against the moralization of the affects and against the rule of will over the Body.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_QGOH_PTDO:P0002","trace_type":"Structural","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"},{"id":"RAW_QGOH_PTDO:P0004","trace_type":"Structural","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"},{"id":"RAW_QGOH_PTDO:P0005","trace_type":"Structural","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"},{"id":"RAW_QGOH_PTDO:P0027","trace_type":"Structural","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_QGOH_PTDO:ALT4","text":"Ontological/Temporal — The essence of a thing is affirmative striving without an internal temporal limit; destruction and diminution are understood from external relations.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_QGOH_PTDO:P0043","trace_type":"Structural","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"},{"id":"RAW_QGOH_PTDO:P0051","trace_type":"Structural","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"},{"id":"RAW_QGOH_PTDO:P0055","trace_type":"Structural","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"},{"id":"RAW_QGOH_PTDO:P0059","trace_type":"Structural","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]}],"original_name":"Altitude Levels"},{"number":"12","name":"Minimal Reconstruction Test","items":[{"id":"CH_QGOH_PTDO:MRT1","text":"The extracted system suffices to reconstruct the fundamental sequence: naturalization of the affects, the causal distinction of action and passion, the parallelism of Mind and Body, striving, and the formation of the primary affects.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_QGOH_PTDO:P0004","trace_type":"Structural","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"},{"id":"RAW_QGOH_PTDO:P0005","trace_type":"Structural","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"},{"id":"RAW_QGOH_PTDO:P0007","trace_type":"Structural","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"},{"id":"RAW_QGOH_PTDO:P0023","trace_type":"Structural","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"},{"id":"RAW_QGOH_PTDO:P0027","trace_type":"Structural","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"},{"id":"RAW_QGOH_PTDO:P0051","trace_type":"Structural","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"},{"id":"RAW_QGOH_PTDO:P0067","trace_type":"Structural","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"},{"id":"RAW_QGOH_PTDO:P0077","trace_type":"Structural","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_QGOH_PTDO:MRT2","text":"The argumentation is not autonomous within this passage, because the demonstration depends upon prior doctrines concerning God, attributes, the idea of the Body, and the order of ideas.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_QGOH_PTDO:P0018","trace_type":"Structural","confidence":"medium","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"},{"id":"RAW_QGOH_PTDO:P0025","trace_type":"Structural","confidence":"medium","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"},{"id":"RAW_QGOH_PTDO:P0039","trace_type":"Structural","confidence":"medium","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_QGOH_PTDO:MRT3","text":"The distinction between adequate and inadequate idea explains the structure of action, but the practical criterion by which the Mind may increase adequacy is not yet determined here.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_QGOH_PTDO:P0016","trace_type":"Direct","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"},{"id":"RAW_QGOH_PTDO:P0021","trace_type":"Direct","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"},{"id":"RAW_QGOH_PTDO:P0037","trace_type":"Direct","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_QGOH_PTDO:MRT4","text":"The strongest link is the concatenation of external destruction, striving, actual essence, and indefinite duration.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_QGOH_PTDO:P0043","trace_type":"Structural","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"},{"id":"RAW_QGOH_PTDO:P0051","trace_type":"Structural","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"},{"id":"RAW_QGOH_PTDO:P0055","trace_type":"Structural","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"},{"id":"RAW_QGOH_PTDO:P0059","trace_type":"Structural","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_QGOH_PTDO:MRT5","text":"The expression that the Mind strives to imagine must not be a free decree; coherence is preserved only if striving is read as a necessity of Nature, not as a dominion of will.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_QGOH_PTDO:P0033","trace_type":"Structural","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"},{"id":"RAW_QGOH_PTDO:P0034","trace_type":"Structural","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"},{"id":"RAW_QGOH_PTDO:P0035","trace_type":"Structural","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"},{"id":"RAW_QGOH_PTDO:P0063","trace_type":"Structural","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"},{"id":"RAW_QGOH_PTDO:P0082","trace_type":"Structural","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"},{"id":"RAW_QGOH_PTDO:P0086","trace_type":"Structural","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_QGOH_PTDO:MRT6","text":"A fragile joint remains between the idea that simply excludes the existence of the Body and the idea that excludes the present existence of the Body; the text employs the distinction but does not formally explain it.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_QGOH_PTDO:P0069","trace_type":"Tension","confidence":"medium","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"},{"id":"RAW_QGOH_PTDO:P0079","trace_type":"Tension","confidence":"medium","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"},{"id":"RAW_QGOH_PTDO:P0080","trace_type":"Tension","confidence":"medium","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_QGOH_PTDO:MRT7","text":"The taxonomy of the remaining affects is only promised; this unit constitutes the mechanism of the three primary affects and of imaginative preservation, and does not complete the entire system of affects.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_QGOH_PTDO:P0077","trace_type":"Direct","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]}],"original_name":"Minimal Reconstruction Test"}],"revision":"CH_QGOH_PTDO@R001","source_variant_id":"canonical:CH_QGOH_PTDO:v1","source_language_code":"la"}],"identity":{"layer":"CH","kicker":"Chapter 09","title":"Part Three - On the Origin and Nature of the Affects · passage 1","context":"Chapter 09","original_title":"Pars tertia - De origine et natura affectuum · passage 1","original_context":"Chapter 09"},"original_title":"Pars tertia - De origine et natura affectuum · passage 1","title_language_code":"en"},{"id":"CH_QGOH_PT_2","layer":"CH","title":"Part Three - On the Origin and Nature of the Affects · passage 2","order":10,"parents":["RAW_QGOH_PT_2"],"revision":"CH_QGOH_PT_2@R001","sha256":"bfb9103adeb8c25656064a0e7570aad142699c7edeb8280a6b8c075f6702d2c2","sections":[{"number":"1","name":"Argumental Spine","items":[{"id":"CH_QGOH_PT_2:AS1","text":"Textus quaerit qua lege imago rei, etiam causaliter neutrae, absenti aut incertae, in Lætitiam, Tristitiam, Amorem, Odium, Cupiditatem et actionem transire possit.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_QGOH_PT_2:P0013","trace_type":"Composite","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"},{"id":"RAW_QGOH_PT_2:P0032","trace_type":"Composite","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"},{"id":"RAW_QGOH_PT_2:P0034","trace_type":"Composite","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_QGOH_PT_2:AS2","text":"Thesis est affectus ex conatibus potentiae, conjunctionibus imaginativis et similitudine corporum generari, ita ut causae affectivae non cum causis efficientibus rei coincidere debeant.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_QGOH_PT_2:P0011","trace_type":"Composite","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"},{"id":"RAW_QGOH_PT_2:P0013","trace_type":"Composite","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"},{"id":"RAW_QGOH_PT_2:P0019","trace_type":"Composite","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_QGOH_PT_2:AS3","text":"Argumentatio est demonstrativo-genetica: affectiones compositae ex legibus imaginationis, memoriae, conatus et imitationis deducuntur.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_QGOH_PT_2:P0005","trace_type":"Structural","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"},{"id":"RAW_QGOH_PT_2:P0007","trace_type":"Structural","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"},{"id":"RAW_QGOH_PT_2:P0027","trace_type":"Structural","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"},{"id":"RAW_QGOH_PT_2:P0034","trace_type":"Structural","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"},{"id":"RAW_QGOH_PT_2:P0080","trace_type":"Structural","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"},{"id":"RAW_QGOH_PT_2:P0100","trace_type":"Structural","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_QGOH_PT_2:AS4","text":"Motus principalis progreditur a coaffectione simultanea ad causalitatem per accidens, inde ad Amorem et Odium, fluctuationem animi, temporalitatem imaginatam et affectuum communicationem.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_QGOH_PT_2:P0005","trace_type":"Structural","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"},{"id":"RAW_QGOH_PT_2:P0013","trace_type":"Structural","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"},{"id":"RAW_QGOH_PT_2:P0027","trace_type":"Structural","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"},{"id":"RAW_QGOH_PT_2:P0036","trace_type":"Structural","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"},{"id":"RAW_QGOH_PT_2:P0050","trace_type":"Structural","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"},{"id":"RAW_QGOH_PT_2:P0115","trace_type":"Structural","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"},{"id":"RAW_QGOH_PT_2:P0125","trace_type":"Structural","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_QGOH_PT_2:AS5","text":"Affectuum imitatio rem private amatam vel odiosam in ordinem socialem transfert, ubi approbatio, gloria, ambitio, imminutio alterius et certamen nascuntur.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_QGOH_PT_2:P0078","trace_type":"Structural","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"},{"id":"RAW_QGOH_PT_2:P0082","trace_type":"Structural","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"},{"id":"RAW_QGOH_PT_2:P0102","trace_type":"Structural","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"},{"id":"RAW_QGOH_PT_2:P0106","trace_type":"Structural","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"},{"id":"RAW_QGOH_PT_2:P0119","trace_type":"Structural","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"},{"id":"RAW_QGOH_PT_2:P0123","trace_type":"Structural","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_QGOH_PT_2:AS6","text":"Terminus praebitus constituit condicionem conflictus: si res ab uno tantum possideri potest, imaginata alterius fruitio conatum impeditivae privationis producit.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_QGOH_PT_2:P0125","trace_type":"Direct","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"},{"id":"RAW_QGOH_PT_2:P0127","trace_type":"Direct","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]}],"original_name":"Argumental Spine"},{"number":"2","name":"Load-Bearing Axioms","items":[{"id":"CH_QGOH_PT_2:A1","text":"Mens ea praesentia statuere conatur quae ipsius et Corporis agendi potentiam augent vel juvant, atque ea excludere quae eandem minuunt vel coercent.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_QGOH_PT_2:P0001","trace_type":"Composite","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"},{"id":"RAW_QGOH_PT_2:P0040","trace_type":"Composite","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"},{"id":"RAW_QGOH_PT_2:P0044","trace_type":"Composite","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"},{"id":"RAW_QGOH_PT_2:P0098","trace_type":"Composite","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_QGOH_PT_2:A2","text":"Lætitia est transitus ad majorem perfectionem seu potentiam; Tristitia est transitus ad minorem perfectionem seu potentiam.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_QGOH_PT_2:P0048","trace_type":"Direct","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_QGOH_PT_2:A3","text":"Affectus simul experti ita in memoria conjunguntur ut imminutio vel reimaginatio unius etiam alterum excitet.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_QGOH_PT_2:P0005","trace_type":"Direct","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"},{"id":"RAW_QGOH_PT_2:P0007","trace_type":"Direct","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_QGOH_PT_2:A4","text":"Res cuius propria natura Mentis potentiam nec auget nec minuit potest, per conjunctionem affectivam, causa per accidens Lætitiæ, Tristitiæ vel Cupiditatis fieri.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_QGOH_PT_2:P0009","trace_type":"Direct","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"},{"id":"RAW_QGOH_PT_2:P0011","trace_type":"Direct","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_QGOH_PT_2:A5","text":"Amor est Lætitia concomitante idea causæ externæ, et Odium est Tristitia concomitante idea causæ externæ.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_QGOH_PT_2:P0003","trace_type":"Direct","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_QGOH_PT_2:A6","text":"Qui amat rem praesentem habere et conservare conatur; qui odit rem amovere vel destruere conatur.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_QGOH_PT_2:P0003","trace_type":"Direct","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_QGOH_PT_2:A7","text":"Imago rei præteritæ, futuræ vel præsentis eundem affectum producit, quatenus rei existentiam affirmat; dubitatio eventus constantiam affectus minuit.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_QGOH_PT_2:P0030","trace_type":"Composite","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"},{"id":"RAW_QGOH_PT_2:P0032","trace_type":"Composite","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"},{"id":"RAW_QGOH_PT_2:P0034","trace_type":"Composite","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_QGOH_PT_2:A8","text":"Unum idemque objectum, propter pluralitatem et diversam affectionum Corporis compositionem, plurium contrariorumque affectuum causa esse potest.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_QGOH_PT_2:P0027","trace_type":"Direct","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"},{"id":"RAW_QGOH_PT_2:P0028","trace_type":"Direct","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_QGOH_PT_2:A9","text":"Mentis conatus in cogitando et Corporis conatus in agendo ejusdem naturæ sunt; ideo imaginatio Lætitiæ vel Tristitiæ in appetitum et actionem transire potest.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_QGOH_PT_2:P0100","trace_type":"Direct","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_QGOH_PT_2:A10","text":"Imago rei nobis similis affectu affectæ affectum similem producit, nisi res illa odio habeatur, quo casu affectus contrarius oritur.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_QGOH_PT_2:P0078","trace_type":"Direct","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"},{"id":"RAW_QGOH_PT_2:P0080","trace_type":"Direct","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_QGOH_PT_2:A11","text":"Homo per affectiones quibus ad agendum determinatur sui conscius est, atque Lætitia vel Tristitia idea causæ internæ coniuncta relationem ad se ipsum constituit.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_QGOH_PT_2:P0108","trace_type":"Direct","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"},{"id":"RAW_QGOH_PT_2:P0110","trace_type":"Direct","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_QGOH_PT_2:A12","text":"Si res ab uno tantum possideri potest, imaginata alterius ejusdem rei fruitio impedimentum proprii cupiditatis efficit.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_QGOH_PT_2:P0125","trace_type":"Direct","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"},{"id":"RAW_QGOH_PT_2:P0127","trace_type":"Direct","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]}],"original_name":"Load-Bearing Axioms"},{"number":"3","name":"Apophatic Structure","items":[{"id":"CH_QGOH_PT_2:NX1","text":"Causa affectiva non restringitur ad causam efficientem rei; res causaliter neutra per accidens amari vel odio haberi potest.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_QGOH_PT_2:P0011","trace_type":"Negative","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"},{"id":"RAW_QGOH_PT_2:P0013","trace_type":"Negative","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_QGOH_PT_2:NX2","text":"Sympathia et Antipathia non indigent qualitatibus occultis; similitudines manifestæ et conjunctiones imaginativæ ad earum explicationem sufficiunt.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_QGOH_PT_2:P0017","trace_type":"Negative","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"},{"id":"RAW_QGOH_PT_2:P0019","trace_type":"Negative","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"},{"id":"RAW_QGOH_PT_2:P0021","trace_type":"Negative","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_QGOH_PT_2:NX3","text":"Efficacia affectiva non exigit rem actu præsentem; imago præteriti vel futuri, si existentiam rei affirmat, ut præsens operatur.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_QGOH_PT_2:P0030","trace_type":"Negative","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"},{"id":"RAW_QGOH_PT_2:P0032","trace_type":"Negative","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"},{"id":"RAW_QGOH_PT_2:P0034","trace_type":"Negative","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_QGOH_PT_2:NX4","text":"Objectum non unicum nec stabilem valorem affectivum possidet; idem objectum simultanee amari et odio haberi potest.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_QGOH_PT_2:P0023","trace_type":"Negative","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"},{"id":"RAW_QGOH_PT_2:P0025","trace_type":"Negative","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"},{"id":"RAW_QGOH_PT_2:P0028","trace_type":"Negative","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_QGOH_PT_2:NX5","text":"Imitatio affectuum non est transmissio invariabilis; Odium relationem similitudinis in contrarium affectum vertere potest.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_QGOH_PT_2:P0080","trace_type":"Negative","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_QGOH_PT_2:NX6","text":"Gloria non probat veram aliorum approbationem; homo sibi omnibus gratum esse imaginari potest, cum omnibus sit molestus.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_QGOH_PT_2:P0112","trace_type":"Negative","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"},{"id":"RAW_QGOH_PT_2:P0113","trace_type":"Negative","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_QGOH_PT_2:NX7","text":"Benevolentia non ponitur voluntas prima aut pura; est Cupiditas ex Commiseratione orta.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_QGOH_PT_2:P0092","trace_type":"Negative","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"},{"id":"RAW_QGOH_PT_2:P0096","trace_type":"Negative","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]}],"original_name":"Apophatic Structure"},{"number":"4","name":"Derivative Propositions","items":[{"id":"CH_QGOH_PT_2:D1","text":"Objectum affectui concomitans potest, quamvis non sit causa efficiens, objectum Amoris vel Odii fieri.","refs":["CH_QGOH_PT_2:A3","CH_QGOH_PT_2:A4","CH_QGOH_PT_2:A5"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"From","passages":[{"id":"RAW_QGOH_PT_2:P0013","trace_type":"Composite","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"},{"id":"RAW_QGOH_PT_2:P0015","trace_type":"Composite","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_QGOH_PT_2:D2","text":"Similitudo rei cum objecto prius læto vel tristi sufficit ut res similis per accidens ametur vel odio habeatur.","refs":["CH_QGOH_PT_2:A3","CH_QGOH_PT_2:A4","CH_QGOH_PT_2:A5"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"From","passages":[{"id":"RAW_QGOH_PT_2:P0019","trace_type":"Direct","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"},{"id":"RAW_QGOH_PT_2:P0021","trace_type":"Direct","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_QGOH_PT_2:D3","text":"Fluctuatio animi oritur cum idem objectum simul sub relatione Lætitiæ et sub relatione Tristitiæ imaginatur.","refs":["CH_QGOH_PT_2:A5","CH_QGOH_PT_2:A8"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"From","passages":[{"id":"RAW_QGOH_PT_2:P0023","trace_type":"Direct","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"},{"id":"RAW_QGOH_PT_2:P0027","trace_type":"Direct","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_QGOH_PT_2:D4","text":"Spes et Metus sunt Lætitia et Tristitia inconstantes ex imagine rei dubiæ; sublata dubitatione in Securitatem et Desperationem transeunt.","refs":["CH_QGOH_PT_2:A2","CH_QGOH_PT_2:A7"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"From","passages":[{"id":"RAW_QGOH_PT_2:P0034","trace_type":"Direct","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"},{"id":"RAW_QGOH_PT_2:P0036","trace_type":"Direct","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_QGOH_PT_2:D5","text":"Imago conservationis rei amatæ Lætitiam, imago destructionis ejus Tristitiam producit; in re odiosa eadem ratio affectuum invertitur.","refs":["CH_QGOH_PT_2:A1","CH_QGOH_PT_2:A5","CH_QGOH_PT_2:A6"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"From","passages":[{"id":"RAW_QGOH_PT_2:P0038","trace_type":"Composite","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"},{"id":"RAW_QGOH_PT_2:P0040","trace_type":"Composite","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"},{"id":"RAW_QGOH_PT_2:P0042","trace_type":"Composite","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"},{"id":"RAW_QGOH_PT_2:P0044","trace_type":"Composite","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_QGOH_PT_2:D6","text":"Affectus imaginatus in re amata ad amantem eodem signo transit, atque affectus imaginatus in re odiosa ad odientem signo contrario transit.","refs":["CH_QGOH_PT_2:A1","CH_QGOH_PT_2:A2","CH_QGOH_PT_2:A5","CH_QGOH_PT_2:A6"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"From","passages":[{"id":"RAW_QGOH_PT_2:P0046","trace_type":"Composite","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"},{"id":"RAW_QGOH_PT_2:P0048","trace_type":"Composite","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"},{"id":"RAW_QGOH_PT_2:P0056","trace_type":"Composite","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"},{"id":"RAW_QGOH_PT_2:P0058","trace_type":"Composite","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_QGOH_PT_2:D7","text":"Qui rem amatam Lætitia vel Tristitia afficere imaginatur Favorem vel Indignationem excitat; qui rem odiosam Lætitia vel Tristitia afficere imaginatur Odium vel Amorem excitat.","refs":["CH_QGOH_PT_2:A5","CH_QGOH_PT_2:A6","CH_QGOH_PT_2:D6"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"From","passages":[{"id":"RAW_QGOH_PT_2:P0050","trace_type":"Composite","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"},{"id":"RAW_QGOH_PT_2:P0052","trace_type":"Composite","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"},{"id":"RAW_QGOH_PT_2:P0062","trace_type":"Composite","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"},{"id":"RAW_QGOH_PT_2:P0064","trace_type":"Composite","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_QGOH_PT_2:D8","text":"Imitatio affectus in re simili Commiserationem, Æmulationem et, ex Commiseratione, Benevolentiam producit; miseratio conatum liberandi rem miseram parit.","refs":["CH_QGOH_PT_2:A1","CH_QGOH_PT_2:A9","CH_QGOH_PT_2:A10"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"From","passages":[{"id":"RAW_QGOH_PT_2:P0078","trace_type":"Composite","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"},{"id":"RAW_QGOH_PT_2:P0080","trace_type":"Composite","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"},{"id":"RAW_QGOH_PT_2:P0082","trace_type":"Composite","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"},{"id":"RAW_QGOH_PT_2:P0092","trace_type":"Composite","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"},{"id":"RAW_QGOH_PT_2:P0096","trace_type":"Composite","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_QGOH_PT_2:D9","text":"Amor et Odium judicium inclinant: homo de se et re amata Lætitiam affirmans plus justo sentit, de re odiosa Lætitiam negans minus justo sentit.","refs":["CH_QGOH_PT_2:A1","CH_QGOH_PT_2:A2","CH_QGOH_PT_2:A5","CH_QGOH_PT_2:A6"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"From","passages":[{"id":"RAW_QGOH_PT_2:P0068","trace_type":"Composite","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"},{"id":"RAW_QGOH_PT_2:P0070","trace_type":"Composite","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"},{"id":"RAW_QGOH_PT_2:P0072","trace_type":"Composite","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"},{"id":"RAW_QGOH_PT_2:P0076","trace_type":"Composite","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_QGOH_PT_2:D10","text":"Imaginata aliorum approbatio actionem conformem et Lætitiam sui tanquam causæ producit; imaginata improbatio actionem aversandam et Tristitiam sui producit.","refs":["CH_QGOH_PT_2:A9","CH_QGOH_PT_2:A10","CH_QGOH_PT_2:A11"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"From","passages":[{"id":"RAW_QGOH_PT_2:P0102","trace_type":"Composite","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"},{"id":"RAW_QGOH_PT_2:P0104","trace_type":"Composite","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"},{"id":"RAW_QGOH_PT_2:P0108","trace_type":"Composite","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"},{"id":"RAW_QGOH_PT_2:P0110","trace_type":"Composite","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_QGOH_PT_2:D11","text":"Conatus faciendi ut alii eadem ament et oderint Amorem roborat, sed oppositione affectuum fluctuationem animi et, sub condicione exclusivæ possessionis, conatum impediendi alterum producit.","refs":["CH_QGOH_PT_2:A1","CH_QGOH_PT_2:A9","CH_QGOH_PT_2:A10","CH_QGOH_PT_2:A12"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"From","passages":[{"id":"RAW_QGOH_PT_2:P0115","trace_type":"Structural","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"},{"id":"RAW_QGOH_PT_2:P0117","trace_type":"Structural","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"},{"id":"RAW_QGOH_PT_2:P0119","trace_type":"Structural","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"},{"id":"RAW_QGOH_PT_2:P0123","trace_type":"Structural","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"},{"id":"RAW_QGOH_PT_2:P0125","trace_type":"Structural","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"},{"id":"RAW_QGOH_PT_2:P0127","trace_type":"Structural","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]}],"original_name":"Derivative Propositions"},{"number":"5","name":"Super-Derivatives","items":[{"id":"CH_QGOH_PT_2:SD1","text":"Ordo affectivus constituitur potius ex reticulo associationum, similitudinum et idearum causalium quam ex proprietatibus intrinsecis objectorum.","refs":["CH_QGOH_PT_2:A3","CH_QGOH_PT_2:A4","CH_QGOH_PT_2:A5","CH_QGOH_PT_2:D1","CH_QGOH_PT_2:D2"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"From","passages":[{"id":"RAW_QGOH_PT_2:P0005","trace_type":"Structural","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"},{"id":"RAW_QGOH_PT_2:P0011","trace_type":"Structural","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"},{"id":"RAW_QGOH_PT_2:P0019","trace_type":"Structural","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"},{"id":"RAW_QGOH_PT_2:P0021","trace_type":"Structural","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_QGOH_PT_2:SD2","text":"Affectus fit mechanismus selectivus cognitionis: quod Lætitiam sustentat affirmari, quod Tristitiam sustinet negari conatur, unde imminutio vel exaggeratio rei sequitur.","refs":["CH_QGOH_PT_2:A1","CH_QGOH_PT_2:D9"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"From","passages":[{"id":"RAW_QGOH_PT_2:P0068","trace_type":"Structural","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"},{"id":"RAW_QGOH_PT_2:P0070","trace_type":"Structural","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"},{"id":"RAW_QGOH_PT_2:P0072","trace_type":"Structural","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"},{"id":"RAW_QGOH_PT_2:P0076","trace_type":"Structural","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_QGOH_PT_2:SD3","text":"Differentia inter præteritum, futurum et præsens non est primaria differentia affectiva; differentiam efficacem constituit dubitatio de eventu.","refs":["CH_QGOH_PT_2:A2","CH_QGOH_PT_2:A7","CH_QGOH_PT_2:D4"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"From","passages":[{"id":"RAW_QGOH_PT_2:P0030","trace_type":"Structural","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"},{"id":"RAW_QGOH_PT_2:P0032","trace_type":"Structural","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"},{"id":"RAW_QGOH_PT_2:P0034","trace_type":"Structural","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"},{"id":"RAW_QGOH_PT_2:P0036","trace_type":"Structural","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_QGOH_PT_2:SD4","text":"Socialitas affectiva duplicem structuram habet: similitudo et communio affectuum connexionem pariunt, sed eadem communicatio cupiditatum contentionem et impedimentum reciproca producere potest.","refs":["CH_QGOH_PT_2:A10","CH_QGOH_PT_2:A12","CH_QGOH_PT_2:D8","CH_QGOH_PT_2:D11"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"From","passages":[{"id":"RAW_QGOH_PT_2:P0080","trace_type":"Structural","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"},{"id":"RAW_QGOH_PT_2:P0082","trace_type":"Structural","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"},{"id":"RAW_QGOH_PT_2:P0119","trace_type":"Structural","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"},{"id":"RAW_QGOH_PT_2:P0123","trace_type":"Structural","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_QGOH_PT_2:SD5","text":"Subjectum sibi ipsi non immediate apparet, sed per imaginatos affectus aliorum et per ideam sui tanquam causæ actionum aestimatur.","refs":["CH_QGOH_PT_2:A10","CH_QGOH_PT_2:A11","CH_QGOH_PT_2:D10"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"From","passages":[{"id":"RAW_QGOH_PT_2:P0108","trace_type":"Structural","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"},{"id":"RAW_QGOH_PT_2:P0110","trace_type":"Structural","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"},{"id":"RAW_QGOH_PT_2:P0112","trace_type":"Structural","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"},{"id":"RAW_QGOH_PT_2:P0113","trace_type":"Structural","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]}],"original_name":"Super-Derivatives"},{"number":"6","name":"Deep Apophatics","items":[{"id":"CH_QGOH_PT_2:DNX1","text":"Falsum esse debet affectum ex sola natura objecti et ex causis efficientibus cognitis determinari.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_QGOH_PT_2:P0011","trace_type":"Negative","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"},{"id":"RAW_QGOH_PT_2:P0013","trace_type":"Negative","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"},{"id":"RAW_QGOH_PT_2:P0017","trace_type":"Negative","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_QGOH_PT_2:DNX2","text":"Falsum esse debet cupiditates singulorum a relationibus imaginatis ad alios independenter constitui.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_QGOH_PT_2:P0102","trace_type":"Negative","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"},{"id":"RAW_QGOH_PT_2:P0119","trace_type":"Negative","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"},{"id":"RAW_QGOH_PT_2:P0123","trace_type":"Negative","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_QGOH_PT_2:DNX3","text":"Falsum esse debet præsentiam actualem rei condicionem necessariam omnis affectionis esse.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_QGOH_PT_2:P0030","trace_type":"Negative","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"},{"id":"RAW_QGOH_PT_2:P0032","trace_type":"Negative","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"},{"id":"RAW_QGOH_PT_2:P0034","trace_type":"Negative","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_QGOH_PT_2:DNX4","text":"Falsum esse debet unum objectum unam tantum dispositionem affectivam producere.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_QGOH_PT_2:P0023","trace_type":"Negative","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"},{"id":"RAW_QGOH_PT_2:P0027","trace_type":"Negative","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"},{"id":"RAW_QGOH_PT_2:P0028","trace_type":"Negative","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_QGOH_PT_2:DNX5","text":"Falsum esse debet judicium de se, de re amata et de re odiosa affective indifferens aut spontanee proportionatum esse.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_QGOH_PT_2:P0068","trace_type":"Negative","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"},{"id":"RAW_QGOH_PT_2:P0072","trace_type":"Negative","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"},{"id":"RAW_QGOH_PT_2:P0076","trace_type":"Negative","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_QGOH_PT_2:DNX6","text":"Falsum esse debet consensum affectivum inter homines per se stabilem pacem socialem efficere.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_QGOH_PT_2:P0119","trace_type":"Negative","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"},{"id":"RAW_QGOH_PT_2:P0123","trace_type":"Negative","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]}],"original_name":"Deep Apophatics"},{"number":"7","name":"Super-Apophatics","items":[{"id":"CH_QGOH_PT_2:SNX1","text":"Systema excludit cognitionem affectibus vacuam tamquam fundamentum primarium affirmationis, negationis et actionis.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_QGOH_PT_2:P0068","trace_type":"Negative","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"},{"id":"RAW_QGOH_PT_2:P0070","trace_type":"Negative","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"},{"id":"RAW_QGOH_PT_2:P0108","trace_type":"Negative","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"},{"id":"RAW_QGOH_PT_2:P0110","trace_type":"Negative","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_QGOH_PT_2:SNX2","text":"Systema excludit concordiam socialem spontaneam ex eo solo quod homines eadem amare et odio habere conantur.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_QGOH_PT_2:P0119","trace_type":"Negative","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"},{"id":"RAW_QGOH_PT_2:P0123","trace_type":"Negative","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_QGOH_PT_2:SNX3","text":"Systema excludit identitatem inter imaginem sui laudatam et veram aliorum de agente aestimationem.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_QGOH_PT_2:P0112","trace_type":"Negative","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"},{"id":"RAW_QGOH_PT_2:P0113","trace_type":"Negative","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_QGOH_PT_2:SNX4","text":"Systema excludit bonum desideratum tamquam objectum universaliter compatibile, cum exclusivitas possessionis cupiditatem in obstructionem convertat.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_QGOH_PT_2:P0125","trace_type":"Negative","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"},{"id":"RAW_QGOH_PT_2:P0127","trace_type":"Negative","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]}],"original_name":"Super-Apophatics"},{"number":"8","name":"Formal Generative Schema","items":[{"id":"CH_QGOH_PT_2:FGS1","text":"Potentia augenda vel minuenda → Lætitia vel Tristitia → imaginatio præsentificans vel excludens → Amor vel Odium → conatus conservandi vel removendi.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_QGOH_PT_2:P0001","trace_type":"Composite","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"},{"id":"RAW_QGOH_PT_2:P0003","trace_type":"Composite","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"},{"id":"RAW_QGOH_PT_2:P0038","trace_type":"Composite","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"},{"id":"RAW_QGOH_PT_2:P0042","trace_type":"Composite","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_QGOH_PT_2:FGS2","text":"Coaffectio simultanea → memoria conjunctiva → revocatio affectus → causalitas per accidens → Amor, Odium aut Cupiditas sine causa efficiente cognita.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_QGOH_PT_2:P0005","trace_type":"Composite","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"},{"id":"RAW_QGOH_PT_2:P0007","trace_type":"Composite","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"},{"id":"RAW_QGOH_PT_2:P0009","trace_type":"Composite","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"},{"id":"RAW_QGOH_PT_2:P0013","trace_type":"Composite","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_QGOH_PT_2:FGS3","text":"Imago temporalis existentiam affirmans → affectus instar præsentis → dubitatio eventus → Spes aut Metus → sublata dubitatione Securitas aut Desperatio.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_QGOH_PT_2:P0030","trace_type":"Direct","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"},{"id":"RAW_QGOH_PT_2:P0034","trace_type":"Direct","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"},{"id":"RAW_QGOH_PT_2:P0036","trace_type":"Direct","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_QGOH_PT_2:FGS4","text":"Similitudo corporis → imitatio affectus → Commiseratio aut Æmulatio → conatus benefaciendi, imitandi, approbandi aut improbando reagendi.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_QGOH_PT_2:P0078","trace_type":"Direct","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"},{"id":"RAW_QGOH_PT_2:P0080","trace_type":"Direct","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"},{"id":"RAW_QGOH_PT_2:P0082","trace_type":"Direct","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_QGOH_PT_2:FGS5","text":"Affectus alterius circa rem amatam vel odiosam → relatio ad agentem → Favor, Indignatio, Invidia, Amor aut Odium.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_QGOH_PT_2:P0050","trace_type":"Direct","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"},{"id":"RAW_QGOH_PT_2:P0052","trace_type":"Direct","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"},{"id":"RAW_QGOH_PT_2:P0062","trace_type":"Direct","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_QGOH_PT_2:FGS6","text":"Imaginata approbatio socialis → actio ad placendum → Gloria vel Acquiescentia in se ipso; imaginata improbatio → aversatio actionis → Pudor vel Pœnitentia.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_QGOH_PT_2:P0102","trace_type":"Composite","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"},{"id":"RAW_QGOH_PT_2:P0106","trace_type":"Composite","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"},{"id":"RAW_QGOH_PT_2:P0112","trace_type":"Composite","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_QGOH_PT_2:FGS7","text":"Cupiditas consensus affectivi → conatus conformandi alios → imminutio reciproca inter plures → conflictus; res unica → conatus ne alter ea potiatur.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_QGOH_PT_2:P0119","trace_type":"Structural","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"},{"id":"RAW_QGOH_PT_2:P0123","trace_type":"Structural","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"},{"id":"RAW_QGOH_PT_2:P0125","trace_type":"Structural","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"},{"id":"RAW_QGOH_PT_2:P0127","trace_type":"Structural","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]}],"original_name":"Formal Generative Schema"},{"number":"9","name":"Argument Flow Map","items":[{"id":"CH_QGOH_PT_2:AF1","text":"Conatus potentiae → Lætitia et Tristitia → Amor et Odium → conservatio rei amatæ vel remotio rei odiosæ.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_QGOH_PT_2:P0001","trace_type":"Composite","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"},{"id":"RAW_QGOH_PT_2:P0003","trace_type":"Composite","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"},{"id":"RAW_QGOH_PT_2:P0038","trace_type":"Composite","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"},{"id":"RAW_QGOH_PT_2:P0042","trace_type":"Composite","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_QGOH_PT_2:AF2","text":"Affectus simul conjuncti → memoria affectiva → causa per accidens → Amor vel Odium sine causa efficiente cognita.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_QGOH_PT_2:P0005","trace_type":"Composite","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"},{"id":"RAW_QGOH_PT_2:P0009","trace_type":"Composite","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"},{"id":"RAW_QGOH_PT_2:P0013","trace_type":"Composite","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_QGOH_PT_2:AF3","text":"Similitudo objectorum → translatio affectus → fluctuationes animi ex contrariis relationibus.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_QGOH_PT_2:P0019","trace_type":"Composite","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"},{"id":"RAW_QGOH_PT_2:P0023","trace_type":"Composite","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"},{"id":"RAW_QGOH_PT_2:P0027","trace_type":"Composite","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_QGOH_PT_2:AF4","text":"Imago præteriti vel futuri → affectus instar præsentis → dubitatio → Spes, Metus, Securitas, Desperatio, Gaudium et Conscientiæ morsus.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_QGOH_PT_2:P0030","trace_type":"Direct","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"},{"id":"RAW_QGOH_PT_2:P0034","trace_type":"Direct","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"},{"id":"RAW_QGOH_PT_2:P0036","trace_type":"Direct","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_QGOH_PT_2:AF5","text":"Affectus rei amatæ vel odiosæ → affectus observantis → relatio affectiva ad causam externam.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_QGOH_PT_2:P0050","trace_type":"Direct","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"},{"id":"RAW_QGOH_PT_2:P0052","trace_type":"Direct","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"},{"id":"RAW_QGOH_PT_2:P0062","trace_type":"Direct","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_QGOH_PT_2:AF6","text":"Similitudo inter homines → imitatio affectuum → Commiseratio, Æmulatio et Benevolentia → actio socialiter directa.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_QGOH_PT_2:P0078","trace_type":"Composite","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"},{"id":"RAW_QGOH_PT_2:P0082","trace_type":"Composite","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"},{"id":"RAW_QGOH_PT_2:P0092","trace_type":"Composite","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"},{"id":"RAW_QGOH_PT_2:P0096","trace_type":"Composite","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_QGOH_PT_2:AF7","text":"Approbatio imaginata → Ambitio et Gloria → conatus universalis conformandi alios → impedimentum mutuum → certamen de re exclusiva.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_QGOH_PT_2:P0102","trace_type":"Structural","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"},{"id":"RAW_QGOH_PT_2:P0106","trace_type":"Structural","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"},{"id":"RAW_QGOH_PT_2:P0119","trace_type":"Structural","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"},{"id":"RAW_QGOH_PT_2:P0123","trace_type":"Structural","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"},{"id":"RAW_QGOH_PT_2:P0125","trace_type":"Structural","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]}],"original_name":"Argument Flow Map"},{"number":"10","name":"Map-Effects","items":[{"id":"CH_QGOH_PT_2:ME1","text":"Affectus reclassificantur a responsionibus ad proprietates objectorum in effectus relationum inter conatum, imaginem, memoriam et ideam causæ.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_QGOH_PT_2:P0005","trace_type":"Structural","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"},{"id":"RAW_QGOH_PT_2:P0011","trace_type":"Structural","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"},{"id":"RAW_QGOH_PT_2:P0019","trace_type":"Structural","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"},{"id":"RAW_QGOH_PT_2:P0034","trace_type":"Structural","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_QGOH_PT_2:ME2","text":"Sympathia et Antipathia reclassificantur a nominibus qualitatum occultarum in effectus associationis et similitudinis manifestæ.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_QGOH_PT_2:P0017","trace_type":"Direct","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"},{"id":"RAW_QGOH_PT_2:P0019","trace_type":"Direct","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"},{"id":"RAW_QGOH_PT_2:P0021","trace_type":"Direct","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_QGOH_PT_2:ME3","text":"Præteritum, futurum et præsens reordinantur secundum modum affirmationis imaginativæ et gradum dubitationis, non secundum distantiam temporalem.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_QGOH_PT_2:P0030","trace_type":"Structural","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"},{"id":"RAW_QGOH_PT_2:P0032","trace_type":"Structural","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"},{"id":"RAW_QGOH_PT_2:P0034","trace_type":"Structural","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_QGOH_PT_2:ME4","text":"Approbatio, vituperium, gloria et pudor reclassificantur ut affectiones reflexivæ ex imaginata aliorum receptione oriundæ.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_QGOH_PT_2:P0106","trace_type":"Composite","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"},{"id":"RAW_QGOH_PT_2:P0112","trace_type":"Composite","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"},{"id":"RAW_QGOH_PT_2:P0113","trace_type":"Composite","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_QGOH_PT_2:ME5","text":"Beneficentia, misericordia et ambitio reclassificantur ut cupiditates causaliter derivatæ, non ut principia moralia primaria.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_QGOH_PT_2:P0054","trace_type":"Composite","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"},{"id":"RAW_QGOH_PT_2:P0092","trace_type":"Composite","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"},{"id":"RAW_QGOH_PT_2:P0096","trace_type":"Composite","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"},{"id":"RAW_QGOH_PT_2:P0106","trace_type":"Composite","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_QGOH_PT_2:ME6","text":"Consensus affectivus reclassificatur ut mechanismus simul corroborationis et conflictus, praesertim ubi res exclusive possidenda est.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_QGOH_PT_2:P0119","trace_type":"Structural","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"},{"id":"RAW_QGOH_PT_2:P0123","trace_type":"Structural","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"},{"id":"RAW_QGOH_PT_2:P0125","trace_type":"Structural","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"},{"id":"RAW_QGOH_PT_2:P0127","trace_type":"Structural","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]}],"original_name":"Map-Effects"},{"number":"11","name":"Altitude Levels","items":[{"id":"CH_QGOH_PT_2:ALT1","text":"Surface — Textus definit Amorem, Odium, Spem, Metum, Securitatem, Desperationem, Commiserationem, Æmulationem, Benevolentiam, Ambitionem, Gloriam et Pudorem.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_QGOH_PT_2:P0003","trace_type":"Structural","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"},{"id":"RAW_QGOH_PT_2:P0036","trace_type":"Structural","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"},{"id":"RAW_QGOH_PT_2:P0054","trace_type":"Structural","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"},{"id":"RAW_QGOH_PT_2:P0082","trace_type":"Structural","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"},{"id":"RAW_QGOH_PT_2:P0096","trace_type":"Structural","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"},{"id":"RAW_QGOH_PT_2:P0106","trace_type":"Structural","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"},{"id":"RAW_QGOH_PT_2:P0112","trace_type":"Structural","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_QGOH_PT_2:ALT2","text":"Structural — Affectus ex affectionibus Corporis, conjunctionibus memorialibus, similitudine imaginata et conatu augendæ potentiae producuntur.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_QGOH_PT_2:P0005","trace_type":"Structural","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"},{"id":"RAW_QGOH_PT_2:P0019","trace_type":"Structural","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"},{"id":"RAW_QGOH_PT_2:P0078","trace_type":"Structural","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"},{"id":"RAW_QGOH_PT_2:P0100","trace_type":"Structural","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_QGOH_PT_2:ALT3","text":"Metastrategic — Actio socialis describitur ut conatus affectiones aliorum conformandi, approbationem adipiscendi et impedimenta cupiditatis removendi.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_QGOH_PT_2:P0102","trace_type":"Structural","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"},{"id":"RAW_QGOH_PT_2:P0106","trace_type":"Structural","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"},{"id":"RAW_QGOH_PT_2:P0119","trace_type":"Structural","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"},{"id":"RAW_QGOH_PT_2:P0123","trace_type":"Structural","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"},{"id":"RAW_QGOH_PT_2:P0125","trace_type":"Structural","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_QGOH_PT_2:ALT4","text":"Ontological/Temporal — Realitas affective operans non requirit rei præsentiam actualem, sed imaginationem existentiam rei affirmantem; tempus affectum per dubitationem, non per solam temporalem positionem, modificat.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_QGOH_PT_2:P0030","trace_type":"Structural","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"},{"id":"RAW_QGOH_PT_2:P0032","trace_type":"Structural","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"},{"id":"RAW_QGOH_PT_2:P0034","trace_type":"Structural","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"},{"id":"RAW_QGOH_PT_2:P0036","trace_type":"Structural","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]}],"original_name":"Altitude Levels"},{"number":"12","name":"Minimal Reconstruction Test","items":[{"id":"CH_QGOH_PT_2:MRT1","text":"Series axiomatum sufficit ad restituendum transitum a conatu et affectu primario ad causalitatem per accidens, Amorem, Odium et actionem appetitivam.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_QGOH_PT_2:P0001","trace_type":"Structural","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"},{"id":"RAW_QGOH_PT_2:P0003","trace_type":"Structural","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"},{"id":"RAW_QGOH_PT_2:P0009","trace_type":"Structural","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"},{"id":"RAW_QGOH_PT_2:P0013","trace_type":"Structural","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"},{"id":"RAW_QGOH_PT_2:P0038","trace_type":"Structural","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"},{"id":"RAW_QGOH_PT_2:P0042","trace_type":"Structural","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_QGOH_PT_2:MRT2","text":"Structura interne cohaeret, quia memoria conjunctiva, valorem potentiae et ideam causæ eundem ordinem generativum complent.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_QGOH_PT_2:P0005","trace_type":"Structural","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"},{"id":"RAW_QGOH_PT_2:P0007","trace_type":"Structural","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"},{"id":"RAW_QGOH_PT_2:P0011","trace_type":"Structural","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"},{"id":"RAW_QGOH_PT_2:P0100","trace_type":"Structural","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_QGOH_PT_2:MRT3","text":"Pars firmissima est deductio qua similitudine, imitatione et imaginata aliorum approbatione affectus privatus in relationem socialem transit.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_QGOH_PT_2:P0078","trace_type":"Structural","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"},{"id":"RAW_QGOH_PT_2:P0080","trace_type":"Structural","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"},{"id":"RAW_QGOH_PT_2:P0102","trace_type":"Structural","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"},{"id":"RAW_QGOH_PT_2:P0110","trace_type":"Structural","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_QGOH_PT_2:MRT4","text":"Systema praesupponit propositiones antecedentes de conatu, parallelismo Mentis et Corporis, natura imaginationis et conscientia sui; hae in loco dato non iterum demonstrantur.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[]},{"id":"CH_QGOH_PT_2:MRT5","text":"Commissura fragilis manet inter causalitatem per accidens et ideam causæ externæ: objectum affective causale fieri potest quamvis causa efficiens affectus non sit.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_QGOH_PT_2:P0011","trace_type":"Tension","confidence":"medium","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"},{"id":"RAW_QGOH_PT_2:P0013","trace_type":"Tension","confidence":"medium","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"},{"id":"RAW_QGOH_PT_2:P0003","trace_type":"Tension","confidence":"medium","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_QGOH_PT_2:MRT6","text":"Fluctuatio animi admittitur, sed regula non datur qua affectus contrarii in actione finali componantur aut alter alterum superet.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[]},{"id":"CH_QGOH_PT_2:MRT7","text":"Antagonismus socialis non universaliter ex imitatione sola sequitur; indiget vel diversitate affectuum, vel cupiditate approbationis, vel conditione rei ab uno tantum possidendæ.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_QGOH_PT_2:P0119","trace_type":"Direct","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"},{"id":"RAW_QGOH_PT_2:P0123","trace_type":"Direct","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"},{"id":"RAW_QGOH_PT_2:P0125","trace_type":"Direct","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_QGOH_PT_2:MRT8","text":"Restitutio motum textus servat usque ad conatum impediendi alterius possessionem; scholium Propositionis XXXII in materia praebita non continetur.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_QGOH_PT_2:P0124","trace_type":"Direct","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"},{"id":"RAW_QGOH_PT_2:P0125","trace_type":"Direct","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"},{"id":"RAW_QGOH_PT_2:P0127","trace_type":"Direct","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"},{"id":"RAW_QGOH_PT_2:P0128","trace_type":"Direct","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]}],"original_name":"Minimal Reconstruction Test"}],"language_code":"la","language_name":"Latina","translations":[{"id":"CAREVAR_20261015T015154510823Z_3936876a","language_code":"en","language_name":"English","title":"Part Three — On the Origin and Nature of the Affects · passage 2","source_sha256":"bfb9103adeb8c25656064a0e7570aad142699c7edeb8280a6b8c075f6702d2c2","sha256":"9f552a8e86ff2e151b10c4a7bf07e44b948f8483573df3e9afc6413c646ec9bc","rendition_type":"machine","review_status":"machine","model":"gpt-5.6-terra","created":"2026-10-15T01:51:54.511832+00:00","sections":[{"number":"1","name":"Argumental Spine","items":[{"id":"CH_QGOH_PT_2:AS1","text":"The text asks by what law the image of a thing, even one causally neutral, absent, or uncertain, can pass into Joy, Sadness, Love, Hate, Desire, and action.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_QGOH_PT_2:P0013","trace_type":"Composite","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"},{"id":"RAW_QGOH_PT_2:P0032","trace_type":"Composite","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"},{"id":"RAW_QGOH_PT_2:P0034","trace_type":"Composite","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_QGOH_PT_2:AS2","text":"The thesis is that affects are generated from conatuses of power, imaginative conjunctions, and the similarity of bodies, such that affective causes need not coincide with the efficient causes of a thing.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_QGOH_PT_2:P0011","trace_type":"Composite","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"},{"id":"RAW_QGOH_PT_2:P0013","trace_type":"Composite","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"},{"id":"RAW_QGOH_PT_2:P0019","trace_type":"Composite","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_QGOH_PT_2:AS3","text":"The argument is demonstrative-genetic: composite affections are deduced from the laws of imagination, memory, conatus, and imitation.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_QGOH_PT_2:P0005","trace_type":"Structural","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"},{"id":"RAW_QGOH_PT_2:P0007","trace_type":"Structural","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"},{"id":"RAW_QGOH_PT_2:P0027","trace_type":"Structural","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"},{"id":"RAW_QGOH_PT_2:P0034","trace_type":"Structural","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"},{"id":"RAW_QGOH_PT_2:P0080","trace_type":"Structural","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"},{"id":"RAW_QGOH_PT_2:P0100","trace_type":"Structural","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_QGOH_PT_2:AS4","text":"The principal movement proceeds from simultaneous co-affection to accidental causality, thence to Love and Hate, fluctuation of mind, imagined temporality, and the communication of affects.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_QGOH_PT_2:P0005","trace_type":"Structural","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"},{"id":"RAW_QGOH_PT_2:P0013","trace_type":"Structural","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"},{"id":"RAW_QGOH_PT_2:P0027","trace_type":"Structural","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"},{"id":"RAW_QGOH_PT_2:P0036","trace_type":"Structural","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"},{"id":"RAW_QGOH_PT_2:P0050","trace_type":"Structural","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"},{"id":"RAW_QGOH_PT_2:P0115","trace_type":"Structural","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"},{"id":"RAW_QGOH_PT_2:P0125","trace_type":"Structural","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_QGOH_PT_2:AS5","text":"The imitation of affects transfers a privately loved or hated thing into the social order, where approval, glory, ambition, the diminution of another, and rivalry arise.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_QGOH_PT_2:P0078","trace_type":"Structural","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"},{"id":"RAW_QGOH_PT_2:P0082","trace_type":"Structural","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"},{"id":"RAW_QGOH_PT_2:P0102","trace_type":"Structural","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"},{"id":"RAW_QGOH_PT_2:P0106","trace_type":"Structural","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"},{"id":"RAW_QGOH_PT_2:P0119","trace_type":"Structural","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"},{"id":"RAW_QGOH_PT_2:P0123","trace_type":"Structural","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_QGOH_PT_2:AS6","text":"The provided limit establishes the condition of conflict: if a thing can be possessed by only one, the imagined enjoyment of another produces a conatus of obstructive deprivation.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_QGOH_PT_2:P0125","trace_type":"Direct","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"},{"id":"RAW_QGOH_PT_2:P0127","trace_type":"Direct","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]}],"original_name":"Argumental Spine"},{"number":"2","name":"Load-Bearing Axioms","items":[{"id":"CH_QGOH_PT_2:A1","text":"The Mind strives to posit as present those things that increase or aid its own and the Body's power of acting, and to exclude those that diminish or restrain the same.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_QGOH_PT_2:P0001","trace_type":"Composite","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"},{"id":"RAW_QGOH_PT_2:P0040","trace_type":"Composite","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"},{"id":"RAW_QGOH_PT_2:P0044","trace_type":"Composite","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"},{"id":"RAW_QGOH_PT_2:P0098","trace_type":"Composite","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_QGOH_PT_2:A2","text":"Joy is a transition to greater perfection or power; Sadness is a transition to lesser perfection or power.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_QGOH_PT_2:P0048","trace_type":"Direct","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_QGOH_PT_2:A3","text":"Affects experienced simultaneously are so conjoined in memory that the diminution or reimagination of one also arouses the other.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_QGOH_PT_2:P0005","trace_type":"Direct","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"},{"id":"RAW_QGOH_PT_2:P0007","trace_type":"Direct","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_QGOH_PT_2:A4","text":"A thing whose own nature neither increases nor diminishes the Mind's power can, through affective conjunction, become an accidental cause of Joy, Sadness, or Desire.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_QGOH_PT_2:P0009","trace_type":"Direct","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"},{"id":"RAW_QGOH_PT_2:P0011","trace_type":"Direct","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_QGOH_PT_2:A5","text":"Love is Joy accompanied by the idea of an external cause, and Hate is Sadness accompanied by the idea of an external cause.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_QGOH_PT_2:P0003","trace_type":"Direct","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_QGOH_PT_2:A6","text":"One who loves strives to have and preserve the present thing; one who hates strives to remove or destroy the thing.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_QGOH_PT_2:P0003","trace_type":"Direct","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_QGOH_PT_2:A7","text":"The image of a past, future, or present thing produces the same affect insofar as it affirms the existence of the thing; uncertainty of the outcome diminishes the constancy of the affect.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_QGOH_PT_2:P0030","trace_type":"Composite","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"},{"id":"RAW_QGOH_PT_2:P0032","trace_type":"Composite","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"},{"id":"RAW_QGOH_PT_2:P0034","trace_type":"Composite","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_QGOH_PT_2:A8","text":"One and the same object, owing to the plurality and diverse composition of the affections of the Body, can be the cause of multiple and contrary affects.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_QGOH_PT_2:P0027","trace_type":"Direct","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"},{"id":"RAW_QGOH_PT_2:P0028","trace_type":"Direct","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_QGOH_PT_2:A9","text":"The Mind's conatus in thinking and the Body's conatus in acting are of the same nature; therefore the imagination of Joy or Sadness can pass into appetite and action.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_QGOH_PT_2:P0100","trace_type":"Direct","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_QGOH_PT_2:A10","text":"The image of a thing similar to us and affected by an affect produces a similar affect, unless that thing is hated, in which case the contrary affect arises.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_QGOH_PT_2:P0078","trace_type":"Direct","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"},{"id":"RAW_QGOH_PT_2:P0080","trace_type":"Direct","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_QGOH_PT_2:A11","text":"A human being is conscious of himself through the affections by which he is determined to act, and Joy or Sadness conjoined with the idea of an internal cause establishes a relation to himself.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_QGOH_PT_2:P0108","trace_type":"Direct","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"},{"id":"RAW_QGOH_PT_2:P0110","trace_type":"Direct","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_QGOH_PT_2:A12","text":"If a thing can be possessed by only one, the imagined enjoyment by another of that same thing constitutes an impediment to one's own desire.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_QGOH_PT_2:P0125","trace_type":"Direct","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"},{"id":"RAW_QGOH_PT_2:P0127","trace_type":"Direct","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]}],"original_name":"Load-Bearing Axioms"},{"number":"3","name":"Apophatic Structure","items":[{"id":"CH_QGOH_PT_2:NX1","text":"An affective cause is not restricted to the efficient cause of a thing; a causally neutral thing can be loved or hated accidentally.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_QGOH_PT_2:P0011","trace_type":"Negative","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"},{"id":"RAW_QGOH_PT_2:P0013","trace_type":"Negative","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_QGOH_PT_2:NX2","text":"Sympathy and Antipathy do not require occult qualities; manifest similarities and imaginative conjunctions suffice for their explanation.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_QGOH_PT_2:P0017","trace_type":"Negative","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"},{"id":"RAW_QGOH_PT_2:P0019","trace_type":"Negative","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"},{"id":"RAW_QGOH_PT_2:P0021","trace_type":"Negative","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_QGOH_PT_2:NX3","text":"Affective efficacy does not require a thing actually to be present; the image of the past or future, if it affirms the thing's existence, operates as a present thing.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_QGOH_PT_2:P0030","trace_type":"Negative","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"},{"id":"RAW_QGOH_PT_2:P0032","trace_type":"Negative","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"},{"id":"RAW_QGOH_PT_2:P0034","trace_type":"Negative","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_QGOH_PT_2:NX4","text":"An object does not possess a single or stable affective value; the same object can simultaneously be loved and hated.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_QGOH_PT_2:P0023","trace_type":"Negative","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"},{"id":"RAW_QGOH_PT_2:P0025","trace_type":"Negative","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"},{"id":"RAW_QGOH_PT_2:P0028","trace_type":"Negative","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_QGOH_PT_2:NX5","text":"The imitation of affects is not invariant transmission; Hate can turn the relation of similarity into a contrary affect.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_QGOH_PT_2:P0080","trace_type":"Negative","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_QGOH_PT_2:NX6","text":"Glory does not prove the true approval of others; a person can imagine himself pleasing to all while being troublesome to all.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_QGOH_PT_2:P0112","trace_type":"Negative","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"},{"id":"RAW_QGOH_PT_2:P0113","trace_type":"Negative","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_QGOH_PT_2:NX7","text":"Benevolence is not posited as a primary or pure will; it is Desire arising from Compassion.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_QGOH_PT_2:P0092","trace_type":"Negative","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"},{"id":"RAW_QGOH_PT_2:P0096","trace_type":"Negative","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]}],"original_name":"Apophatic Structure"},{"number":"4","name":"Derivative Propositions","items":[{"id":"CH_QGOH_PT_2:D1","text":"An object accompanying an affect can become the object of Love or Hate, although it is not an efficient cause.","refs":["CH_QGOH_PT_2:A3","CH_QGOH_PT_2:A4","CH_QGOH_PT_2:A5"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"From","passages":[{"id":"RAW_QGOH_PT_2:P0013","trace_type":"Composite","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"},{"id":"RAW_QGOH_PT_2:P0015","trace_type":"Composite","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_QGOH_PT_2:D2","text":"The similarity of a thing to an object previously joyful or sad suffices for the similar thing to be loved or hated accidentally.","refs":["CH_QGOH_PT_2:A3","CH_QGOH_PT_2:A4","CH_QGOH_PT_2:A5"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"From","passages":[{"id":"RAW_QGOH_PT_2:P0019","trace_type":"Direct","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"},{"id":"RAW_QGOH_PT_2:P0021","trace_type":"Direct","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_QGOH_PT_2:D3","text":"Fluctuation of mind arises when the same object is imagined simultaneously under the relation of Joy and under the relation of Sadness.","refs":["CH_QGOH_PT_2:A5","CH_QGOH_PT_2:A8"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"From","passages":[{"id":"RAW_QGOH_PT_2:P0023","trace_type":"Direct","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"},{"id":"RAW_QGOH_PT_2:P0027","trace_type":"Direct","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_QGOH_PT_2:D4","text":"Hope and Fear are inconstant Joy and Sadness arising from the image of a doubtful thing; when doubt is removed, they pass into Security and Despair.","refs":["CH_QGOH_PT_2:A2","CH_QGOH_PT_2:A7"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"From","passages":[{"id":"RAW_QGOH_PT_2:P0034","trace_type":"Direct","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"},{"id":"RAW_QGOH_PT_2:P0036","trace_type":"Direct","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_QGOH_PT_2:D5","text":"The image of the preservation of a loved thing produces Joy, and the image of its destruction produces Sadness; in the case of a hated thing, the same relation of affects is inverted.","refs":["CH_QGOH_PT_2:A1","CH_QGOH_PT_2:A5","CH_QGOH_PT_2:A6"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"From","passages":[{"id":"RAW_QGOH_PT_2:P0038","trace_type":"Composite","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"},{"id":"RAW_QGOH_PT_2:P0040","trace_type":"Composite","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"},{"id":"RAW_QGOH_PT_2:P0042","trace_type":"Composite","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"},{"id":"RAW_QGOH_PT_2:P0044","trace_type":"Composite","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_QGOH_PT_2:D6","text":"An affect imagined in a loved thing passes to the lover with the same sign, and an affect imagined in a hated thing passes to the hater with the contrary sign.","refs":["CH_QGOH_PT_2:A1","CH_QGOH_PT_2:A2","CH_QGOH_PT_2:A5","CH_QGOH_PT_2:A6"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"From","passages":[{"id":"RAW_QGOH_PT_2:P0046","trace_type":"Composite","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"},{"id":"RAW_QGOH_PT_2:P0048","trace_type":"Composite","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"},{"id":"RAW_QGOH_PT_2:P0056","trace_type":"Composite","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"},{"id":"RAW_QGOH_PT_2:P0058","trace_type":"Composite","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_QGOH_PT_2:D7","text":"One who imagines a loved thing to be affected with Joy or Sadness arouses Favor or Indignation; one who imagines a hated thing to be affected with Joy or Sadness arouses Hate or Love.","refs":["CH_QGOH_PT_2:A5","CH_QGOH_PT_2:A6","CH_QGOH_PT_2:D6"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"From","passages":[{"id":"RAW_QGOH_PT_2:P0050","trace_type":"Composite","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"},{"id":"RAW_QGOH_PT_2:P0052","trace_type":"Composite","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"},{"id":"RAW_QGOH_PT_2:P0062","trace_type":"Composite","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"},{"id":"RAW_QGOH_PT_2:P0064","trace_type":"Composite","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_QGOH_PT_2:D8","text":"The imitation of affect in a similar thing produces Compassion, Emulation, and, from Compassion, Benevolence; pity gives rise to a conatus to free the pitiable thing.","refs":["CH_QGOH_PT_2:A1","CH_QGOH_PT_2:A9","CH_QGOH_PT_2:A10"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"From","passages":[{"id":"RAW_QGOH_PT_2:P0078","trace_type":"Composite","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"},{"id":"RAW_QGOH_PT_2:P0080","trace_type":"Composite","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"},{"id":"RAW_QGOH_PT_2:P0082","trace_type":"Composite","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"},{"id":"RAW_QGOH_PT_2:P0092","trace_type":"Composite","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"},{"id":"RAW_QGOH_PT_2:P0096","trace_type":"Composite","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_QGOH_PT_2:D9","text":"Love and Hate incline judgment: a person who affirms Joy of himself and of a loved thing feels more than is just, and one who denies Joy of a hated thing feels less than is just.","refs":["CH_QGOH_PT_2:A1","CH_QGOH_PT_2:A2","CH_QGOH_PT_2:A5","CH_QGOH_PT_2:A6"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"From","passages":[{"id":"RAW_QGOH_PT_2:P0068","trace_type":"Composite","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"},{"id":"RAW_QGOH_PT_2:P0070","trace_type":"Composite","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"},{"id":"RAW_QGOH_PT_2:P0072","trace_type":"Composite","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"},{"id":"RAW_QGOH_PT_2:P0076","trace_type":"Composite","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_QGOH_PT_2:D10","text":"The imagined approval of others produces conforming action and Joy in oneself as cause; imagined disapproval produces the avoidance of action and Sadness in oneself.","refs":["CH_QGOH_PT_2:A9","CH_QGOH_PT_2:A10","CH_QGOH_PT_2:A11"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"From","passages":[{"id":"RAW_QGOH_PT_2:P0102","trace_type":"Composite","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"},{"id":"RAW_QGOH_PT_2:P0104","trace_type":"Composite","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"},{"id":"RAW_QGOH_PT_2:P0108","trace_type":"Composite","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"},{"id":"RAW_QGOH_PT_2:P0110","trace_type":"Composite","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_QGOH_PT_2:D11","text":"The conatus to make others love and hate the same things strengthens Love, but through the opposition of affects produces fluctuation of mind and, under the condition of exclusive possession, a conatus to impede another.","refs":["CH_QGOH_PT_2:A1","CH_QGOH_PT_2:A9","CH_QGOH_PT_2:A10","CH_QGOH_PT_2:A12"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"From","passages":[{"id":"RAW_QGOH_PT_2:P0115","trace_type":"Structural","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"},{"id":"RAW_QGOH_PT_2:P0117","trace_type":"Structural","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"},{"id":"RAW_QGOH_PT_2:P0119","trace_type":"Structural","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"},{"id":"RAW_QGOH_PT_2:P0123","trace_type":"Structural","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"},{"id":"RAW_QGOH_PT_2:P0125","trace_type":"Structural","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"},{"id":"RAW_QGOH_PT_2:P0127","trace_type":"Structural","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]}],"original_name":"Derivative Propositions"},{"number":"5","name":"Super-Derivatives","items":[{"id":"CH_QGOH_PT_2:SD1","text":"The affective order is constituted rather by a network of associations, similarities, and causal ideas than by the intrinsic properties of objects.","refs":["CH_QGOH_PT_2:A3","CH_QGOH_PT_2:A4","CH_QGOH_PT_2:A5","CH_QGOH_PT_2:D1","CH_QGOH_PT_2:D2"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"From","passages":[{"id":"RAW_QGOH_PT_2:P0005","trace_type":"Structural","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"},{"id":"RAW_QGOH_PT_2:P0011","trace_type":"Structural","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"},{"id":"RAW_QGOH_PT_2:P0019","trace_type":"Structural","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"},{"id":"RAW_QGOH_PT_2:P0021","trace_type":"Structural","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_QGOH_PT_2:SD2","text":"Affect becomes a selective mechanism of cognition: what sustains Joy strives to be affirmed, what sustains Sadness strives to be denied, whence the diminution or exaggeration of a thing follows.","refs":["CH_QGOH_PT_2:A1","CH_QGOH_PT_2:D9"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"From","passages":[{"id":"RAW_QGOH_PT_2:P0068","trace_type":"Structural","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"},{"id":"RAW_QGOH_PT_2:P0070","trace_type":"Structural","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"},{"id":"RAW_QGOH_PT_2:P0072","trace_type":"Structural","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"},{"id":"RAW_QGOH_PT_2:P0076","trace_type":"Structural","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_QGOH_PT_2:SD3","text":"The difference between past, future, and present is not a primary affective difference; uncertainty concerning the outcome constitutes the efficacious difference.","refs":["CH_QGOH_PT_2:A2","CH_QGOH_PT_2:A7","CH_QGOH_PT_2:D4"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"From","passages":[{"id":"RAW_QGOH_PT_2:P0030","trace_type":"Structural","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"},{"id":"RAW_QGOH_PT_2:P0032","trace_type":"Structural","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"},{"id":"RAW_QGOH_PT_2:P0034","trace_type":"Structural","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"},{"id":"RAW_QGOH_PT_2:P0036","trace_type":"Structural","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_QGOH_PT_2:SD4","text":"Affective sociality has a double structure: similarity and the sharing of affects generate connection, but the same communication of desires can produce contention and reciprocal impediment.","refs":["CH_QGOH_PT_2:A10","CH_QGOH_PT_2:A12","CH_QGOH_PT_2:D8","CH_QGOH_PT_2:D11"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"From","passages":[{"id":"RAW_QGOH_PT_2:P0080","trace_type":"Structural","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"},{"id":"RAW_QGOH_PT_2:P0082","trace_type":"Structural","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"},{"id":"RAW_QGOH_PT_2:P0119","trace_type":"Structural","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"},{"id":"RAW_QGOH_PT_2:P0123","trace_type":"Structural","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_QGOH_PT_2:SD5","text":"The subject does not appear immediately to itself, but is assessed through the imagined affects of others and through the idea of itself as the cause of actions.","refs":["CH_QGOH_PT_2:A10","CH_QGOH_PT_2:A11","CH_QGOH_PT_2:D10"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"From","passages":[{"id":"RAW_QGOH_PT_2:P0108","trace_type":"Structural","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"},{"id":"RAW_QGOH_PT_2:P0110","trace_type":"Structural","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"},{"id":"RAW_QGOH_PT_2:P0112","trace_type":"Structural","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"},{"id":"RAW_QGOH_PT_2:P0113","trace_type":"Structural","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]}],"original_name":"Super-Derivatives"},{"number":"6","name":"Deep Apophatics","items":[{"id":"CH_QGOH_PT_2:DNX1","text":"It must be false that an affect is determined by the nature of the object alone and by known efficient causes.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_QGOH_PT_2:P0011","trace_type":"Negative","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"},{"id":"RAW_QGOH_PT_2:P0013","trace_type":"Negative","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"},{"id":"RAW_QGOH_PT_2:P0017","trace_type":"Negative","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_QGOH_PT_2:DNX2","text":"It must be false that the desires of individuals are constituted independently of imagined relations to others.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_QGOH_PT_2:P0102","trace_type":"Negative","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"},{"id":"RAW_QGOH_PT_2:P0119","trace_type":"Negative","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"},{"id":"RAW_QGOH_PT_2:P0123","trace_type":"Negative","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_QGOH_PT_2:DNX3","text":"It must be false that the actual presence of a thing is a necessary condition of every affection.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_QGOH_PT_2:P0030","trace_type":"Negative","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"},{"id":"RAW_QGOH_PT_2:P0032","trace_type":"Negative","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"},{"id":"RAW_QGOH_PT_2:P0034","trace_type":"Negative","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_QGOH_PT_2:DNX4","text":"It must be false that one object produces only one affective disposition.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_QGOH_PT_2:P0023","trace_type":"Negative","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"},{"id":"RAW_QGOH_PT_2:P0027","trace_type":"Negative","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"},{"id":"RAW_QGOH_PT_2:P0028","trace_type":"Negative","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_QGOH_PT_2:DNX5","text":"It must be false that judgment concerning oneself, a loved thing, and a hated thing is affectively indifferent or spontaneously proportionate.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_QGOH_PT_2:P0068","trace_type":"Negative","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"},{"id":"RAW_QGOH_PT_2:P0072","trace_type":"Negative","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"},{"id":"RAW_QGOH_PT_2:P0076","trace_type":"Negative","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_QGOH_PT_2:DNX6","text":"It must be false that affective agreement among human beings by itself produces stable social peace.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_QGOH_PT_2:P0119","trace_type":"Negative","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"},{"id":"RAW_QGOH_PT_2:P0123","trace_type":"Negative","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]}],"original_name":"Deep Apophatics"},{"number":"7","name":"Super-Apophatics","items":[{"id":"CH_QGOH_PT_2:SNX1","text":"The system excludes cognition devoid of affects as the primary foundation of affirmation, negation, and action.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_QGOH_PT_2:P0068","trace_type":"Negative","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"},{"id":"RAW_QGOH_PT_2:P0070","trace_type":"Negative","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"},{"id":"RAW_QGOH_PT_2:P0108","trace_type":"Negative","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"},{"id":"RAW_QGOH_PT_2:P0110","trace_type":"Negative","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_QGOH_PT_2:SNX2","text":"The system excludes spontaneous social concord arising merely from the fact that human beings strive to love and hate the same things.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_QGOH_PT_2:P0119","trace_type":"Negative","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"},{"id":"RAW_QGOH_PT_2:P0123","trace_type":"Negative","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_QGOH_PT_2:SNX3","text":"The system excludes identity between the praised image of oneself and the true assessment of the agent by others.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_QGOH_PT_2:P0112","trace_type":"Negative","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"},{"id":"RAW_QGOH_PT_2:P0113","trace_type":"Negative","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_QGOH_PT_2:SNX4","text":"The system excludes the desired good as a universally compatible object, since exclusivity of possession converts desire into obstruction.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_QGOH_PT_2:P0125","trace_type":"Negative","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"},{"id":"RAW_QGOH_PT_2:P0127","trace_type":"Negative","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]}],"original_name":"Super-Apophatics"},{"number":"8","name":"Formal Generative Schema","items":[{"id":"CH_QGOH_PT_2:FGS1","text":"Power to be increased or diminished → Joy or Sadness → imagination making present or excluding → Love or Hate → conatus of preserving or removing.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_QGOH_PT_2:P0001","trace_type":"Composite","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"},{"id":"RAW_QGOH_PT_2:P0003","trace_type":"Composite","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"},{"id":"RAW_QGOH_PT_2:P0038","trace_type":"Composite","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"},{"id":"RAW_QGOH_PT_2:P0042","trace_type":"Composite","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_QGOH_PT_2:FGS2","text":"Simultaneous co-affection → conjunctive memory → recall of affect → accidental causality → Love, Hate, or Desire without a known efficient cause.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_QGOH_PT_2:P0005","trace_type":"Composite","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"},{"id":"RAW_QGOH_PT_2:P0007","trace_type":"Composite","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"},{"id":"RAW_QGOH_PT_2:P0009","trace_type":"Composite","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"},{"id":"RAW_QGOH_PT_2:P0013","trace_type":"Composite","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_QGOH_PT_2:FGS3","text":"Temporal image affirming existence → affect as if present → uncertainty of outcome → Hope or Fear → when doubt is removed, Security or Despair.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_QGOH_PT_2:P0030","trace_type":"Direct","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"},{"id":"RAW_QGOH_PT_2:P0034","trace_type":"Direct","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"},{"id":"RAW_QGOH_PT_2:P0036","trace_type":"Direct","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_QGOH_PT_2:FGS4","text":"Similarity of body → imitation of affect → Compassion or Emulation → conatus of benefiting, imitating, approving, or reacting with disapproval.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_QGOH_PT_2:P0078","trace_type":"Direct","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"},{"id":"RAW_QGOH_PT_2:P0080","trace_type":"Direct","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"},{"id":"RAW_QGOH_PT_2:P0082","trace_type":"Direct","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_QGOH_PT_2:FGS5","text":"Another's affect concerning a loved or hated thing → relation to the agent → Favor, Indignation, Envy, Love, or Hate.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_QGOH_PT_2:P0050","trace_type":"Direct","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"},{"id":"RAW_QGOH_PT_2:P0052","trace_type":"Direct","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"},{"id":"RAW_QGOH_PT_2:P0062","trace_type":"Direct","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_QGOH_PT_2:FGS6","text":"Imagined social approval → action to please → Glory or Acquiescence in oneself; imagined disapproval → avoidance of action → Shame or Repentance.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_QGOH_PT_2:P0102","trace_type":"Composite","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"},{"id":"RAW_QGOH_PT_2:P0106","trace_type":"Composite","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"},{"id":"RAW_QGOH_PT_2:P0112","trace_type":"Composite","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_QGOH_PT_2:FGS7","text":"Desire for affective agreement → conatus to make others conform → reciprocal diminution among many → conflict; a single thing → conatus that another not obtain it.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_QGOH_PT_2:P0119","trace_type":"Structural","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"},{"id":"RAW_QGOH_PT_2:P0123","trace_type":"Structural","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"},{"id":"RAW_QGOH_PT_2:P0125","trace_type":"Structural","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"},{"id":"RAW_QGOH_PT_2:P0127","trace_type":"Structural","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]}],"original_name":"Formal Generative Schema"},{"number":"9","name":"Argument Flow Map","items":[{"id":"CH_QGOH_PT_2:AF1","text":"Conatus of power → Joy and Sadness → Love and Hate → preservation of the loved thing or removal of the hated thing.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_QGOH_PT_2:P0001","trace_type":"Composite","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"},{"id":"RAW_QGOH_PT_2:P0003","trace_type":"Composite","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"},{"id":"RAW_QGOH_PT_2:P0038","trace_type":"Composite","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"},{"id":"RAW_QGOH_PT_2:P0042","trace_type":"Composite","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_QGOH_PT_2:AF2","text":"Simultaneously conjoined affects → affective memory → accidental cause → Love or Hate without a known efficient cause.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_QGOH_PT_2:P0005","trace_type":"Composite","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"},{"id":"RAW_QGOH_PT_2:P0009","trace_type":"Composite","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"},{"id":"RAW_QGOH_PT_2:P0013","trace_type":"Composite","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_QGOH_PT_2:AF3","text":"Similarity of objects → transfer of affect → fluctuations of mind from contrary relations.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_QGOH_PT_2:P0019","trace_type":"Composite","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"},{"id":"RAW_QGOH_PT_2:P0023","trace_type":"Composite","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"},{"id":"RAW_QGOH_PT_2:P0027","trace_type":"Composite","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_QGOH_PT_2:AF4","text":"Image of the past or future → affect as if present → uncertainty → Hope, Fear, Security, Despair, Joy, and pangs of Conscience.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_QGOH_PT_2:P0030","trace_type":"Direct","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"},{"id":"RAW_QGOH_PT_2:P0034","trace_type":"Direct","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"},{"id":"RAW_QGOH_PT_2:P0036","trace_type":"Direct","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_QGOH_PT_2:AF5","text":"Affect of the loved or hated thing → affect of the observer → affective relation to the external cause.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_QGOH_PT_2:P0050","trace_type":"Direct","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"},{"id":"RAW_QGOH_PT_2:P0052","trace_type":"Direct","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"},{"id":"RAW_QGOH_PT_2:P0062","trace_type":"Direct","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_QGOH_PT_2:AF6","text":"Similarity among human beings → imitation of affects → Compassion, Emulation, and Benevolence → socially directed action.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_QGOH_PT_2:P0078","trace_type":"Composite","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"},{"id":"RAW_QGOH_PT_2:P0082","trace_type":"Composite","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"},{"id":"RAW_QGOH_PT_2:P0092","trace_type":"Composite","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"},{"id":"RAW_QGOH_PT_2:P0096","trace_type":"Composite","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_QGOH_PT_2:AF7","text":"Imagined approval → Ambition and Glory → universal conatus to make others conform → mutual impediment → rivalry over an exclusive thing.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_QGOH_PT_2:P0102","trace_type":"Structural","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"},{"id":"RAW_QGOH_PT_2:P0106","trace_type":"Structural","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"},{"id":"RAW_QGOH_PT_2:P0119","trace_type":"Structural","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"},{"id":"RAW_QGOH_PT_2:P0123","trace_type":"Structural","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"},{"id":"RAW_QGOH_PT_2:P0125","trace_type":"Structural","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]}],"original_name":"Argument Flow Map"},{"number":"10","name":"Map-Effects","items":[{"id":"CH_QGOH_PT_2:ME1","text":"Affects are reclassified from responses to the properties of objects into effects of relations among conatus, image, memory, and the idea of cause.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_QGOH_PT_2:P0005","trace_type":"Structural","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"},{"id":"RAW_QGOH_PT_2:P0011","trace_type":"Structural","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"},{"id":"RAW_QGOH_PT_2:P0019","trace_type":"Structural","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"},{"id":"RAW_QGOH_PT_2:P0034","trace_type":"Structural","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_QGOH_PT_2:ME2","text":"Sympathy and Antipathy are reclassified from names of occult qualities into effects of association and manifest similarity.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_QGOH_PT_2:P0017","trace_type":"Direct","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"},{"id":"RAW_QGOH_PT_2:P0019","trace_type":"Direct","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"},{"id":"RAW_QGOH_PT_2:P0021","trace_type":"Direct","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_QGOH_PT_2:ME3","text":"Past, future, and present are reordered according to the mode of imaginative affirmation and the degree of uncertainty, not according to temporal distance.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_QGOH_PT_2:P0030","trace_type":"Structural","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"},{"id":"RAW_QGOH_PT_2:P0032","trace_type":"Structural","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"},{"id":"RAW_QGOH_PT_2:P0034","trace_type":"Structural","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_QGOH_PT_2:ME4","text":"Approval, blame, glory, and shame are reclassified as reflexive affections arising from the imagined reception of others.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_QGOH_PT_2:P0106","trace_type":"Composite","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"},{"id":"RAW_QGOH_PT_2:P0112","trace_type":"Composite","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"},{"id":"RAW_QGOH_PT_2:P0113","trace_type":"Composite","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_QGOH_PT_2:ME5","text":"Beneficence, mercy, and ambition are reclassified as causally derived desires, not as primary moral principles.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_QGOH_PT_2:P0054","trace_type":"Composite","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"},{"id":"RAW_QGOH_PT_2:P0092","trace_type":"Composite","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"},{"id":"RAW_QGOH_PT_2:P0096","trace_type":"Composite","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"},{"id":"RAW_QGOH_PT_2:P0106","trace_type":"Composite","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_QGOH_PT_2:ME6","text":"Affective agreement is reclassified as a mechanism both of reinforcement and of conflict, especially where a thing is to be possessed exclusively.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_QGOH_PT_2:P0119","trace_type":"Structural","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"},{"id":"RAW_QGOH_PT_2:P0123","trace_type":"Structural","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"},{"id":"RAW_QGOH_PT_2:P0125","trace_type":"Structural","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"},{"id":"RAW_QGOH_PT_2:P0127","trace_type":"Structural","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]}],"original_name":"Map-Effects"},{"number":"11","name":"Altitude Levels","items":[{"id":"CH_QGOH_PT_2:ALT1","text":"Surface — The text defines Love, Hate, Hope, Fear, Security, Despair, Compassion, Emulation, Benevolence, Ambition, Glory, and Shame.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_QGOH_PT_2:P0003","trace_type":"Structural","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"},{"id":"RAW_QGOH_PT_2:P0036","trace_type":"Structural","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"},{"id":"RAW_QGOH_PT_2:P0054","trace_type":"Structural","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"},{"id":"RAW_QGOH_PT_2:P0082","trace_type":"Structural","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"},{"id":"RAW_QGOH_PT_2:P0096","trace_type":"Structural","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"},{"id":"RAW_QGOH_PT_2:P0106","trace_type":"Structural","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"},{"id":"RAW_QGOH_PT_2:P0112","trace_type":"Structural","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_QGOH_PT_2:ALT2","text":"Structural — Affects are produced from the affections of the Body, memorial conjunctions, imagined similarity, and the conatus to increase power.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_QGOH_PT_2:P0005","trace_type":"Structural","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"},{"id":"RAW_QGOH_PT_2:P0019","trace_type":"Structural","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"},{"id":"RAW_QGOH_PT_2:P0078","trace_type":"Structural","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"},{"id":"RAW_QGOH_PT_2:P0100","trace_type":"Structural","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_QGOH_PT_2:ALT3","text":"Metastrategic — Social action is described as a conatus to make the affections of others conform, to obtain approval, and to remove impediments to desire.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_QGOH_PT_2:P0102","trace_type":"Structural","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"},{"id":"RAW_QGOH_PT_2:P0106","trace_type":"Structural","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"},{"id":"RAW_QGOH_PT_2:P0119","trace_type":"Structural","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"},{"id":"RAW_QGOH_PT_2:P0123","trace_type":"Structural","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"},{"id":"RAW_QGOH_PT_2:P0125","trace_type":"Structural","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_QGOH_PT_2:ALT4","text":"Ontological/Temporal — Affectively operative reality does not require the actual presence of a thing, but an imagination affirming the existence of the thing; time modifies affect through uncertainty, not through temporal position alone.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_QGOH_PT_2:P0030","trace_type":"Structural","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"},{"id":"RAW_QGOH_PT_2:P0032","trace_type":"Structural","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"},{"id":"RAW_QGOH_PT_2:P0034","trace_type":"Structural","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"},{"id":"RAW_QGOH_PT_2:P0036","trace_type":"Structural","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]}],"original_name":"Altitude Levels"},{"number":"12","name":"Minimal Reconstruction Test","items":[{"id":"CH_QGOH_PT_2:MRT1","text":"The series of axioms suffices to reconstruct the passage from conatus and primary affect to accidental causality, Love, Hate, and appetitive action.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_QGOH_PT_2:P0001","trace_type":"Structural","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"},{"id":"RAW_QGOH_PT_2:P0003","trace_type":"Structural","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"},{"id":"RAW_QGOH_PT_2:P0009","trace_type":"Structural","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"},{"id":"RAW_QGOH_PT_2:P0013","trace_type":"Structural","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"},{"id":"RAW_QGOH_PT_2:P0038","trace_type":"Structural","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"},{"id":"RAW_QGOH_PT_2:P0042","trace_type":"Structural","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_QGOH_PT_2:MRT2","text":"The structure coheres internally, because conjunctive memory, the value of power, and the idea of cause complete the same generative order.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_QGOH_PT_2:P0005","trace_type":"Structural","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"},{"id":"RAW_QGOH_PT_2:P0007","trace_type":"Structural","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"},{"id":"RAW_QGOH_PT_2:P0011","trace_type":"Structural","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"},{"id":"RAW_QGOH_PT_2:P0100","trace_type":"Structural","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_QGOH_PT_2:MRT3","text":"The strongest part is the deduction by which, through similarity, imitation, and the imagined approval of others, private affect passes into social relation.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_QGOH_PT_2:P0078","trace_type":"Structural","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"},{"id":"RAW_QGOH_PT_2:P0080","trace_type":"Structural","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"},{"id":"RAW_QGOH_PT_2:P0102","trace_type":"Structural","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"},{"id":"RAW_QGOH_PT_2:P0110","trace_type":"Structural","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_QGOH_PT_2:MRT4","text":"The system presupposes antecedent propositions concerning conatus, the parallelism of Mind and Body, the nature of imagination, and self-consciousness; these are not demonstrated again in the given place.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[]},{"id":"CH_QGOH_PT_2:MRT5","text":"A fragile juncture remains between accidental causality and the idea of an external cause: an object can become affectively causal although it is not the efficient cause of the affect.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_QGOH_PT_2:P0011","trace_type":"Tension","confidence":"medium","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"},{"id":"RAW_QGOH_PT_2:P0013","trace_type":"Tension","confidence":"medium","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"},{"id":"RAW_QGOH_PT_2:P0003","trace_type":"Tension","confidence":"medium","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_QGOH_PT_2:MRT6","text":"Fluctuation of mind is admitted, but no rule is given by which contrary affects are composed in final action or one overcomes the other.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[]},{"id":"CH_QGOH_PT_2:MRT7","text":"Social antagonism does not universally follow from imitation alone; it requires either diversity of affects, or desire for approval, or the condition of a thing to be possessed by only one.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_QGOH_PT_2:P0119","trace_type":"Direct","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"},{"id":"RAW_QGOH_PT_2:P0123","trace_type":"Direct","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"},{"id":"RAW_QGOH_PT_2:P0125","trace_type":"Direct","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_QGOH_PT_2:MRT8","text":"The reconstruction preserves the movement of the text up to the conatus to impede another's possession; the scholium of Proposition XXXII is not contained in the provided material.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_QGOH_PT_2:P0124","trace_type":"Direct","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"},{"id":"RAW_QGOH_PT_2:P0125","trace_type":"Direct","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"},{"id":"RAW_QGOH_PT_2:P0127","trace_type":"Direct","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"},{"id":"RAW_QGOH_PT_2:P0128","trace_type":"Direct","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]}],"original_name":"Minimal Reconstruction Test"}],"revision":"CH_QGOH_PT_2@R001","source_variant_id":"canonical:CH_QGOH_PT_2:v1","source_language_code":"la"}],"identity":{"layer":"CH","kicker":"Chapter 10","title":"Part Three - On the Origin and Nature of the Affects · passage 2","context":"Chapter 10","original_title":"Pars tertia - De origine et natura affectuum · passage 2","original_context":"Chapter 10"},"original_title":"Pars tertia - De origine et natura affectuum · passage 2","title_language_code":"en"},{"id":"CH_QGOH_PT_3","layer":"CH","title":"Part Three - On the Origin and Nature of the Affects · passage 3","order":11,"parents":["RAW_QGOH_PT_3"],"revision":"CH_QGOH_PT_3@R001","sha256":"c19baed94ad5f798a8a312d3c1482e45183b4a4bf6f41f2f8c8ea3d85ba8ca78","sections":[{"number":"1","name":"Argumental Spine","items":[{"id":"CH_QGOH_PT_3:AS1","text":"Textus investigat quomodo imagines affectuum aliorum, causae affectivae attributae et conatus conservationis ex paucis mechanismis seriem passionum socialium producant.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_QGOH_PT_3:P0001","trace_type":"Structural","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"},{"id":"RAW_QGOH_PT_3:P0002","trace_type":"Structural","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"},{"id":"RAW_QGOH_PT_3:P0016","trace_type":"Structural","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"},{"id":"RAW_QGOH_PT_3:P0031","trace_type":"Structural","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"},{"id":"RAW_QGOH_PT_3:P0088","trace_type":"Structural","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"},{"id":"RAW_QGOH_PT_3:P0113","trace_type":"Structural","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"},{"id":"RAW_QGOH_PT_3:P0116","trace_type":"Structural","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_QGOH_PT_3:AS2","text":"Argumentatio est reconstructiva et quasi mechanica: affectiones Corporis, imagines rerum et imminutio vel augmentum potentiae agendi ponuntur ut causae sufficientes amoris, odii, cupiditatis et fluctuationum animi.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_QGOH_PT_3:P0002","trace_type":"Structural","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"},{"id":"RAW_QGOH_PT_3:P0006","trace_type":"Structural","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"},{"id":"RAW_QGOH_PT_3:P0031","trace_type":"Structural","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"},{"id":"RAW_QGOH_PT_3:P0041","trace_type":"Structural","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"},{"id":"RAW_QGOH_PT_3:P0101","trace_type":"Structural","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_QGOH_PT_3:AS3","text":"Motus primus procedit ab imitatione affectuum et amore rei similis ad conatum amoris reciproci obtinendi atque ad gloriam ex amore imaginato ortam.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_QGOH_PT_3:P0004","trace_type":"Structural","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"},{"id":"RAW_QGOH_PT_3:P0006","trace_type":"Structural","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"},{"id":"RAW_QGOH_PT_3:P0008","trace_type":"Structural","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"},{"id":"RAW_QGOH_PT_3:P0010","trace_type":"Structural","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_QGOH_PT_3:AS4","text":"Motus secundus ostendit quomodo exclusio, rivalitas, absentia condicionum desideratarum et coercitio appetitus amorem in zelotypiam, tristitiam, invidiam vel odium convertant.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_QGOH_PT_3:P0012","trace_type":"Structural","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"},{"id":"RAW_QGOH_PT_3:P0014","trace_type":"Structural","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"},{"id":"RAW_QGOH_PT_3:P0016","trace_type":"Structural","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"},{"id":"RAW_QGOH_PT_3:P0023","trace_type":"Structural","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"},{"id":"RAW_QGOH_PT_3:P0039","trace_type":"Structural","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"},{"id":"RAW_QGOH_PT_3:P0044","trace_type":"Structural","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_QGOH_PT_3:AS5","text":"Motus tertius affectus reciprocos constituit: odium imaginatum odium vicissim, malum acceptum vindictam, amor imaginatus amorem et gratiam, atque amor reciprocus dissolutionem odii generat.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_QGOH_PT_3:P0046","trace_type":"Structural","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"},{"id":"RAW_QGOH_PT_3:P0054","trace_type":"Structural","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"},{"id":"RAW_QGOH_PT_3:P0058","trace_type":"Structural","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"},{"id":"RAW_QGOH_PT_3:P0060","trace_type":"Structural","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"},{"id":"RAW_QGOH_PT_3:P0064","trace_type":"Structural","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"},{"id":"RAW_QGOH_PT_3:P0074","trace_type":"Structural","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"},{"id":"RAW_QGOH_PT_3:P0078","trace_type":"Structural","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_QGOH_PT_3:AS6","text":"Motus ultimus directionem affectuum ex causalitate imaginata explicat: causa redistributa affectum minuit, causa libera imaginata eum intendit, causa sub nomine classis vel nationis universalis affectum extendit, et varietas corporum uniformitatem affectivam impedit.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_QGOH_PT_3:P0099","trace_type":"Structural","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"},{"id":"RAW_QGOH_PT_3:P0103","trace_type":"Structural","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"},{"id":"RAW_QGOH_PT_3:P0105","trace_type":"Structural","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"},{"id":"RAW_QGOH_PT_3:P0088","trace_type":"Structural","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"},{"id":"RAW_QGOH_PT_3:P0116","trace_type":"Structural","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]}],"original_name":"Argumental Spine"},{"number":"2","name":"Load-Bearing Axioms","items":[{"id":"CH_QGOH_PT_3:A1","text":"Imago rei est affectio Corporis humani qua Corpus ab externis causis disponitur ad agendum.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_QGOH_PT_3:P0002","trace_type":"Direct","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_QGOH_PT_3:A2","text":"Qui affectum in re sibi simili imaginatur, ad affectum correspondentem imitandum determinatur.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_QGOH_PT_3:P0001","trace_type":"Composite","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"},{"id":"RAW_QGOH_PT_3:P0002","trace_type":"Composite","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_QGOH_PT_3:A3","text":"Amor est Laetitia concomitante idea causae externae, et Odium est Tristitia concomitante idea causae externae.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_QGOH_PT_3:P0041","trace_type":"Composite","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"},{"id":"RAW_QGOH_PT_3:P0101","trace_type":"Composite","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_QGOH_PT_3:A4","text":"Laetitia potentiam agendi atque conatum iuvat, Tristitia autem eandem minuit vel coercet.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_QGOH_PT_3:P0031","trace_type":"Direct","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_QGOH_PT_3:A5","text":"Homo, quatenus Laetitia vel Tristitia afficitur, conatur Laetitiam conservare et Tristitiam amovere.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_QGOH_PT_3:P0031","trace_type":"Direct","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_QGOH_PT_3:A6","text":"Amor rei similis conatum producit quo res amata Laetitia afficiatur concomitante idea amantis.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_QGOH_PT_3:P0004","trace_type":"Direct","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"},{"id":"RAW_QGOH_PT_3:P0006","trace_type":"Direct","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_QGOH_PT_3:A7","text":"Res vel circumstantia simul imaginata cum re laetificante vel tristicante per accidens causa eiusdem affectus fieri potest.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_QGOH_PT_3:P0021","trace_type":"Direct","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_QGOH_PT_3:A8","text":"Qui se causam Laetitiae rei sibi similis imaginatur, se ipsum cum Laetitia contemplatur et gloriam experitur.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_QGOH_PT_3:P0008","trace_type":"Direct","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"},{"id":"RAW_QGOH_PT_3:P0010","trace_type":"Direct","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_QGOH_PT_3:A9","text":"Imago rei quae appetitum impedit, vel condicionis desideratae absentiam involvit, Tristitiam cum idea impedientis causae producit.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_QGOH_PT_3:P0023","trace_type":"Composite","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"},{"id":"RAW_QGOH_PT_3:P0025","trace_type":"Composite","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"},{"id":"RAW_QGOH_PT_3:P0027","trace_type":"Composite","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_QGOH_PT_3:A10","text":"Amor et Odium in rem durant tantum quatenus res causa Laetitiae vel Tristitiae imaginatur.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_QGOH_PT_3:P0099","trace_type":"Composite","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"},{"id":"RAW_QGOH_PT_3:P0101","trace_type":"Composite","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_QGOH_PT_3:A11","text":"Causalitas affectiva inter plures causas distributa amorem vel odium erga unam minuit; causa libera imaginata tamquam causa solitaria affectum auget.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_QGOH_PT_3:P0099","trace_type":"Composite","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"},{"id":"RAW_QGOH_PT_3:P0103","trace_type":"Composite","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"},{"id":"RAW_QGOH_PT_3:P0105","trace_type":"Composite","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_QGOH_PT_3:A12","text":"Conatus malum inferendi ei quem quis odit ab intentione maioris mali sibi vitandi superari potest.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_QGOH_PT_3:P0039","trace_type":"Direct","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"},{"id":"RAW_QGOH_PT_3:P0041","trace_type":"Direct","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_QGOH_PT_3:A13","text":"Bonum et malum ex cupiditate et aversione affectiva aestimantur, non cupiditas ex iudicio boni vel mali antecedente nascitur.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_QGOH_PT_3:P0043","trace_type":"Direct","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_QGOH_PT_3:A14","text":"Spes et Metus affectus connexi sunt, quibus homo rem speratam amat et rem timentem odit, eamque vel obtinere vel amovere conatur.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_QGOH_PT_3:P0113","trace_type":"Composite","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"},{"id":"RAW_QGOH_PT_3:P0114","trace_type":"Composite","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_QGOH_PT_3:A15","text":"Homo ea quae sperat facile credit, ea quae timet difficilius credit, atque de utrisque plus minusve iusto iudicat.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_QGOH_PT_3:P0113","trace_type":"Direct","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_QGOH_PT_3:A16","text":"Diversa Corporum constitutio efficit ut idem obiectum diversos homines et eundem hominem diversis temporibus diversimode afficiat.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_QGOH_PT_3:P0116","trace_type":"Direct","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"},{"id":"RAW_QGOH_PT_3:P0118","trace_type":"Direct","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]}],"original_name":"Load-Bearing Axioms"},{"number":"3","name":"Apophatic Structure","items":[{"id":"CH_QGOH_PT_3:NX1","text":"Zelotypia non est affectus simplex, sed fluctuatio animi ex Amore et Odio simul orta, idea rivalis concomitante.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_QGOH_PT_3:P0016","trace_type":"Direct","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_QGOH_PT_3:NX2","text":"Desiderium non est absentia nuda rei amatae, sed Tristitia quatenus absentiam rei amatae respicit.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_QGOH_PT_3:P0027","trace_type":"Direct","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_QGOH_PT_3:NX3","text":"Timor non est mera cognitio mali futuri, sed dispositio qua homo malum maius per malum minus vitare conatur.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_QGOH_PT_3:P0044","trace_type":"Direct","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_QGOH_PT_3:NX4","text":"Verecundia non est genus Timoris separatum, sed Timor cuius malum futurum est Pudor.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_QGOH_PT_3:P0044","trace_type":"Direct","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_QGOH_PT_3:NX5","text":"Consternatio non est dubitatio theoretica, sed coercitio Cupiditatis per metum duorum malorum, qua homo quid potius velit ignorat.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_QGOH_PT_3:P0044","trace_type":"Direct","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_QGOH_PT_3:NX6","text":"Bonum et malum non sunt proprietates obiectivae quae Cupiditatem praecedunt, sed nomina affective imposita rebus desideratis vel aversatis.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_QGOH_PT_3:P0043","trace_type":"Direct","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_QGOH_PT_3:NX7","text":"Laetitia ex malo rei odiosae orta non est Laetitia pura, quia imaginatio Tristitiae rei similis necessario manet.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_QGOH_PT_3:P0092","trace_type":"Direct","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"},{"id":"RAW_QGOH_PT_3:P0094","trace_type":"Direct","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_QGOH_PT_3:NX8","text":"Amor post Odium auctus non efficit ut homo Odium vel Tristitiam tamquam medium ad maiorem Laetitiam appetat.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_QGOH_PT_3:P0082","trace_type":"Direct","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_QGOH_PT_3:NX9","text":"Omen non est signum intrinsece bonum aut malum, sed res per accidens Spem vel Metum excitans.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_QGOH_PT_3:P0113","trace_type":"Direct","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]}],"original_name":"Apophatic Structure"},{"number":"4","name":"Derivative Propositions","items":[{"id":"CH_QGOH_PT_3:D1","text":"Misericordia, Invidia et Ambitio ex eadem aptitudine ad affectus aliorum imaginandos et imitandos procedunt.","refs":["CH_QGOH_PT_3:A1","CH_QGOH_PT_3:A2"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"From","passages":[{"id":"RAW_QGOH_PT_3:P0001","trace_type":"Composite","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"},{"id":"RAW_QGOH_PT_3:P0002","trace_type":"Composite","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_QGOH_PT_3:D2","text":"Amor rei similis conatum amoris reciproci parit, et amor reciprocus imaginatus gloriam auget.","refs":["CH_QGOH_PT_3:A3","CH_QGOH_PT_3:A6","CH_QGOH_PT_3:A8"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"From","passages":[{"id":"RAW_QGOH_PT_3:P0004","trace_type":"Composite","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"},{"id":"RAW_QGOH_PT_3:P0006","trace_type":"Composite","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"},{"id":"RAW_QGOH_PT_3:P0008","trace_type":"Composite","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_QGOH_PT_3:D3","text":"Imaginatio rei amatae alteri arctius coniunctae appetitum amoris exclusivi coercet et Zelotypiam, hoc est Amorem et Odium cum Invidia, producit.","refs":["CH_QGOH_PT_3:A3","CH_QGOH_PT_3:A5","CH_QGOH_PT_3:A6","CH_QGOH_PT_3:A9"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"From","passages":[{"id":"RAW_QGOH_PT_3:P0012","trace_type":"Composite","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"},{"id":"RAW_QGOH_PT_3:P0014","trace_type":"Composite","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"},{"id":"RAW_QGOH_PT_3:P0016","trace_type":"Composite","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_QGOH_PT_3:D4","text":"Recordatio rei olim laetificantis cupiditatem eandem rem cum iisdem circumstantiis possidendi producit, et defectus unius circumstantiae Tristitiam generat.","refs":["CH_QGOH_PT_3:A5","CH_QGOH_PT_3:A7","CH_QGOH_PT_3:A9"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"From","passages":[{"id":"RAW_QGOH_PT_3:P0019","trace_type":"Composite","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"},{"id":"RAW_QGOH_PT_3:P0021","trace_type":"Composite","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"},{"id":"RAW_QGOH_PT_3:P0023","trace_type":"Composite","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_QGOH_PT_3:D5","text":"Cupiditas Laetitiam conservandi vel Tristitiam removendi eo maior fit quo affectus ex quo oritur maior est.","refs":["CH_QGOH_PT_3:A4","CH_QGOH_PT_3:A5"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"From","passages":[{"id":"RAW_QGOH_PT_3:P0029","trace_type":"Composite","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"},{"id":"RAW_QGOH_PT_3:P0031","trace_type":"Composite","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"},{"id":"RAW_QGOH_PT_3:P0032","trace_type":"Composite","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_QGOH_PT_3:D6","text":"Amor qui omnino in Odium transit maius Odium producit quam si res amata numquam amata fuisset, quia Odium etiam conatus ab amore relictos coercet.","refs":["CH_QGOH_PT_3:A3","CH_QGOH_PT_3:A4","CH_QGOH_PT_3:A5","CH_QGOH_PT_3:A6","CH_QGOH_PT_3:A9"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"From","passages":[{"id":"RAW_QGOH_PT_3:P0034","trace_type":"Direct","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"},{"id":"RAW_QGOH_PT_3:P0036","trace_type":"Direct","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"},{"id":"RAW_QGOH_PT_3:P0037","trace_type":"Direct","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_QGOH_PT_3:D7","text":"Qui odit malum inferre, qui amat benefacere conatur, nisi conatus prior metu maioris mali sibi imminentis superetur.","refs":["CH_QGOH_PT_3:A3","CH_QGOH_PT_3:A5","CH_QGOH_PT_3:A12"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"From","passages":[{"id":"RAW_QGOH_PT_3:P0039","trace_type":"Direct","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"},{"id":"RAW_QGOH_PT_3:P0041","trace_type":"Direct","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_QGOH_PT_3:D8","text":"Odium ab alio imaginatum, cum nulla causa proprii odii agnoscitur, Odium reciprocum producit; causa iusta sui odii imaginata Pudorem producit.","refs":["CH_QGOH_PT_3:A2","CH_QGOH_PT_3:A3","CH_QGOH_PT_3:A9"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"From","passages":[{"id":"RAW_QGOH_PT_3:P0046","trace_type":"Composite","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"},{"id":"RAW_QGOH_PT_3:P0048","trace_type":"Composite","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"},{"id":"RAW_QGOH_PT_3:P0050","trace_type":"Composite","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_QGOH_PT_3:D9","text":"Malum ex Odio acceptum Vindictam excitat, dum Amor ab alio imaginatus Amorem reciprocum et Gratitudinem producit; textus tamen homines ad Vindictam promptiores ponit quam ad beneficium referendum.","refs":["CH_QGOH_PT_3:A2","CH_QGOH_PT_3:A3","CH_QGOH_PT_3:A5","CH_QGOH_PT_3:A8"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"From","passages":[{"id":"RAW_QGOH_PT_3:P0054","trace_type":"Composite","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"},{"id":"RAW_QGOH_PT_3:P0058","trace_type":"Composite","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"},{"id":"RAW_QGOH_PT_3:P0064","trace_type":"Composite","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_QGOH_PT_3:D10","text":"Beneficium ex Amore vel spe Gloriae datum ingrata receptione Tristitiam producit, quia causa gloriae speratae imaginatur excludi.","refs":["CH_QGOH_PT_3:A6","CH_QGOH_PT_3:A7","CH_QGOH_PT_3:A8","CH_QGOH_PT_3:A9"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"From","passages":[{"id":"RAW_QGOH_PT_3:P0070","trace_type":"Direct","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"},{"id":"RAW_QGOH_PT_3:P0072","trace_type":"Direct","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_QGOH_PT_3:D11","text":"Odium odio reciproco augetur, Amor autem imaginatus conatum odii tollendi producit; si hic conatus praevalet, Odium deletur et in Amorem ampliorem transire potest.","refs":["CH_QGOH_PT_3:A3","CH_QGOH_PT_3:A4","CH_QGOH_PT_3:A5","CH_QGOH_PT_3:A8"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"From","passages":[{"id":"RAW_QGOH_PT_3:P0074","trace_type":"Direct","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"},{"id":"RAW_QGOH_PT_3:P0076","trace_type":"Direct","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"},{"id":"RAW_QGOH_PT_3:P0078","trace_type":"Direct","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_QGOH_PT_3:D12","text":"Qui aliquem rem amatam odio habere imaginatur, eum odit, quia eum causam Tristitiae rei amatae et consequenter propriae Tristitiae imaginatur.","refs":["CH_QGOH_PT_3:A2","CH_QGOH_PT_3:A3","CH_QGOH_PT_3:A9"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"From","passages":[{"id":"RAW_QGOH_PT_3:P0084","trace_type":"Direct","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"},{"id":"RAW_QGOH_PT_3:P0086","trace_type":"Direct","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_QGOH_PT_3:D13","text":"Laetitia vel Tristitia ab uno membro classis vel nationis diversae accepta ad omnes eiusdem classis vel nationis sub imagine universali extenditur.","refs":["CH_QGOH_PT_3:A3","CH_QGOH_PT_3:A7","CH_QGOH_PT_3:A10"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"From","passages":[{"id":"RAW_QGOH_PT_3:P0088","trace_type":"Direct","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_QGOH_PT_3:D14","text":"Laetitia ex malo rei odiosae repetitur, quia memoria rei Tristitiam pristinam renovat, quam idea mali vel liberationis simul coercet.","refs":["CH_QGOH_PT_3:A2","CH_QGOH_PT_3:A3","CH_QGOH_PT_3:A7"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"From","passages":[{"id":"RAW_QGOH_PT_3:P0092","trace_type":"Composite","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"},{"id":"RAW_QGOH_PT_3:P0096","trace_type":"Composite","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"},{"id":"RAW_QGOH_PT_3:P0097","trace_type":"Composite","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_QGOH_PT_3:D15","text":"Causa affectus ad aliam rem translata Amorem vel Odium erga causam priorem minuit aut destruit; causa libera imaginata eundem affectum intendit.","refs":["CH_QGOH_PT_3:A10","CH_QGOH_PT_3:A11"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"From","passages":[{"id":"RAW_QGOH_PT_3:P0099","trace_type":"Composite","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"},{"id":"RAW_QGOH_PT_3:P0101","trace_type":"Composite","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"},{"id":"RAW_QGOH_PT_3:P0103","trace_type":"Composite","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_QGOH_PT_3:D16","text":"Res per accidens Spem vel Metum excitantes amantur vel odio habentur, ut media rerum speratarum aut impedimenta rerum metuendarum; ex hac structura et ex credulitatis asymmetria Superstitiones nascuntur.","refs":["CH_QGOH_PT_3:A7","CH_QGOH_PT_3:A13","CH_QGOH_PT_3:A14","CH_QGOH_PT_3:A15"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"From","passages":[{"id":"RAW_QGOH_PT_3:P0109","trace_type":"Direct","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"},{"id":"RAW_QGOH_PT_3:P0113","trace_type":"Direct","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"},{"id":"RAW_QGOH_PT_3:P0114","trace_type":"Direct","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_QGOH_PT_3:D17","text":"Nulla affectio obiecti universaliter fixa est, quia idem obiectum pro diversa Corporum dispositione et pro diverso tempore diversos affectus generat.","refs":["CH_QGOH_PT_3:A16"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"From","passages":[{"id":"RAW_QGOH_PT_3:P0116","trace_type":"Direct","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"},{"id":"RAW_QGOH_PT_3:P0118","trace_type":"Direct","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]}],"original_name":"Derivative Propositions"},{"number":"5","name":"Super-Derivatives","items":[{"id":"CH_QGOH_PT_3:SD1","text":"Affectus interpersonales formam circuitus feedback habent: Amor imaginatus Amorem et Gloriam, Odium imaginatum Odium, et malum imaginatum Vindictam reproducit.","refs":["CH_QGOH_PT_3:A2","CH_QGOH_PT_3:A3","CH_QGOH_PT_3:A5","CH_QGOH_PT_3:A8"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"From","passages":[{"id":"RAW_QGOH_PT_3:P0046","trace_type":"Composite","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"},{"id":"RAW_QGOH_PT_3:P0054","trace_type":"Composite","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"},{"id":"RAW_QGOH_PT_3:P0074","trace_type":"Composite","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_QGOH_PT_3:SD2","text":"Zelotypia structuram triadicam instituit, in qua amans, res amata et rivalis non tres affectiones independentes, sed una machina coercitionis appetitus et redistributionis odii sunt.","refs":["CH_QGOH_PT_3:A3","CH_QGOH_PT_3:A6","CH_QGOH_PT_3:A9"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"From","passages":[{"id":"RAW_QGOH_PT_3:P0012","trace_type":"Structural","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"},{"id":"RAW_QGOH_PT_3:P0014","trace_type":"Structural","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"},{"id":"RAW_QGOH_PT_3:P0016","trace_type":"Structural","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_QGOH_PT_3:SD3","text":"Causalitas imaginata est operator directionis affectivae: individuatio causae affectum intendit, multiplicatio causarum eum diluit, et universalizatio causae sub classe vel natione eum collectivizat.","refs":["CH_QGOH_PT_3:A7","CH_QGOH_PT_3:A10","CH_QGOH_PT_3:A11"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"From","passages":[{"id":"RAW_QGOH_PT_3:P0099","trace_type":"Structural","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"},{"id":"RAW_QGOH_PT_3:P0103","trace_type":"Structural","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"},{"id":"RAW_QGOH_PT_3:P0105","trace_type":"Structural","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"},{"id":"RAW_QGOH_PT_3:P0088","trace_type":"Structural","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_QGOH_PT_3:SD4","text":"Memoria et absentia non sunt affective inertes: imago rei praeteritae praesentiam eius simulat, dum defectus circumstantiae vel memoria mali mechanismum Tristitiae et Laetitiae repetitae efficit.","refs":["CH_QGOH_PT_3:A7","CH_QGOH_PT_3:A9"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"From","passages":[{"id":"RAW_QGOH_PT_3:P0019","trace_type":"Composite","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"},{"id":"RAW_QGOH_PT_3:P0023","trace_type":"Composite","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"},{"id":"RAW_QGOH_PT_3:P0096","trace_type":"Composite","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"},{"id":"RAW_QGOH_PT_3:P0097","trace_type":"Composite","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_QGOH_PT_3:SD5","text":"Iudicia boni, mali, utilis, inutilis, ominosi et noxii describunt cartographiam affectivam desiderii, non ordinem valorum a desiderio independentem.","refs":["CH_QGOH_PT_3:A13","CH_QGOH_PT_3:A14","CH_QGOH_PT_3:A15"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"From","passages":[{"id":"RAW_QGOH_PT_3:P0043","trace_type":"Structural","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"},{"id":"RAW_QGOH_PT_3:P0044","trace_type":"Structural","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"},{"id":"RAW_QGOH_PT_3:P0113","trace_type":"Structural","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_QGOH_PT_3:SD6","text":"Systema simul admittit propagationem socialem affectuum per imagines universales et negat uniformitatem affectivam per diversitatem constitutivam Corporum.","refs":["CH_QGOH_PT_3:A7","CH_QGOH_PT_3:A16"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"From","passages":[{"id":"RAW_QGOH_PT_3:P0088","trace_type":"Tension","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"},{"id":"RAW_QGOH_PT_3:P0116","trace_type":"Tension","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"},{"id":"RAW_QGOH_PT_3:P0118","trace_type":"Tension","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]}],"original_name":"Super-Derivatives"},{"number":"6","name":"Deep Apophatics","items":[{"id":"CH_QGOH_PT_3:DNX1","text":"Systema tacite excludit affectum ut motum pure internum et sine causa externa imaginata, quia Amor et Odium per ideam causae externae constituuntur.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_QGOH_PT_3:P0041","trace_type":"Negative","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"},{"id":"RAW_QGOH_PT_3:P0101","trace_type":"Negative","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_QGOH_PT_3:DNX2","text":"Systema tacite excludit valorem obiectivum boni et mali, quia omnis valor ad structuram cupiditatis, aversionis et affectus reducitur.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_QGOH_PT_3:P0043","trace_type":"Negative","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_QGOH_PT_3:DNX3","text":"Systema tacite excludit relationem affectivam simpliciter dyadicam, quia amor, odium et gloria a tertiis imaginatis, rivalibus, classibus, nationibus et aliis causis mediantur.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_QGOH_PT_3:P0016","trace_type":"Negative","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"},{"id":"RAW_QGOH_PT_3:P0046","trace_type":"Negative","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"},{"id":"RAW_QGOH_PT_3:P0088","trace_type":"Negative","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_QGOH_PT_3:DNX4","text":"Systema tacite excludit identitatem stabilem affectus erga obiectum, quia memoria, circumstantia, causa adiuncta et status Corporis eius directionem mutare possunt.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_QGOH_PT_3:P0023","trace_type":"Negative","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"},{"id":"RAW_QGOH_PT_3:P0099","trace_type":"Negative","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"},{"id":"RAW_QGOH_PT_3:P0116","trace_type":"Negative","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_QGOH_PT_3:DNX5","text":"Systema tacite excludit usum rationaliter voluntarium Tristitiae ut medii ad Laetitiam maiorem, quia conatus necessario Tristitiam amovere et esse suum conservare tendit.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_QGOH_PT_3:P0082","trace_type":"Negative","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_QGOH_PT_3:DNX6","text":"Systema tacite excludit omen tamquam nuntium obiectivum futuri, quia vis ominis ex associatione accidentali cum Spe vel Metu procedit.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_QGOH_PT_3:P0109","trace_type":"Negative","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"},{"id":"RAW_QGOH_PT_3:P0113","trace_type":"Negative","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]}],"original_name":"Deep Apophatics"},{"number":"7","name":"Super-Apophatics","items":[{"id":"CH_QGOH_PT_3:SNX1","text":"Textus ordinatur circa negationem subiecti affective sui iuris: homo affectus suos non ex libera origine interna producit, sed ex imaginibus causarum, imitatione, conatu et constitutione corporis.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_QGOH_PT_3:P0002","trace_type":"Structural","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"},{"id":"RAW_QGOH_PT_3:P0031","trace_type":"Structural","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"},{"id":"RAW_QGOH_PT_3:P0101","trace_type":"Structural","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"},{"id":"RAW_QGOH_PT_3:P0118","trace_type":"Structural","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_QGOH_PT_3:SNX2","text":"Textus ordinatur circa absentiam tribunalium moralium transcendentium: bonum, malum, gratia, vindicta, crudelitas et superstitio ut effectus machinae affectivae ordinantur.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_QGOH_PT_3:P0043","trace_type":"Structural","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"},{"id":"RAW_QGOH_PT_3:P0058","trace_type":"Structural","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"},{"id":"RAW_QGOH_PT_3:P0064","trace_type":"Structural","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"},{"id":"RAW_QGOH_PT_3:P0113","trace_type":"Structural","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_QGOH_PT_3:SNX3","text":"Textus ordinatur circa impossibilitatem Laetitiae prorsus sine residuo Tristitiae, ubi Laetitia ex malo rei odiosae vel ex abolitione odii nascitur.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_QGOH_PT_3:P0092","trace_type":"Structural","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"},{"id":"RAW_QGOH_PT_3:P0096","trace_type":"Structural","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"},{"id":"RAW_QGOH_PT_3:P0078","trace_type":"Structural","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_QGOH_PT_3:SNX4","text":"Textus ordinatur circa impossibilitatem unius obiecti affective identici omnibus, quia obiectum nullam valorem affectivum invariantem praebet extra relationem ad Corpus afficiendum.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_QGOH_PT_3:P0116","trace_type":"Direct","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"},{"id":"RAW_QGOH_PT_3:P0118","trace_type":"Direct","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]}],"original_name":"Super-Apophatics"},{"number":"8","name":"Formal Generative Schema","items":[{"id":"CH_QGOH_PT_3:FGS1","text":"Affectio externa → imago Corporis → Laetitia vel Tristitia → idea causae externae → Amor vel Odium.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_QGOH_PT_3:P0002","trace_type":"Structural","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"},{"id":"RAW_QGOH_PT_3:P0041","trace_type":"Structural","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"},{"id":"RAW_QGOH_PT_3:P0101","trace_type":"Structural","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_QGOH_PT_3:FGS2","text":"Imago affectus in re simili → imitatio affectiva → Misericordia, Invidia vel Ambitio → expansio socialis affectus.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_QGOH_PT_3:P0001","trace_type":"Direct","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"},{"id":"RAW_QGOH_PT_3:P0002","trace_type":"Direct","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_QGOH_PT_3:FGS3","text":"Amor rei similis → conatus eam Laetitia afficiendi cum idea sui → Amor reciprocus imaginatus → Gloria.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_QGOH_PT_3:P0004","trace_type":"Structural","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"},{"id":"RAW_QGOH_PT_3:P0006","trace_type":"Structural","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"},{"id":"RAW_QGOH_PT_3:P0008","trace_type":"Structural","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_QGOH_PT_3:FGS4","text":"Rivalis, absentia vel impedimentum → coercitio appetitus → Tristitia → Odium, Invidia, Zelotypia, Vindicta vel Desiderium.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_QGOH_PT_3:P0012","trace_type":"Structural","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"},{"id":"RAW_QGOH_PT_3:P0027","trace_type":"Structural","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"},{"id":"RAW_QGOH_PT_3:P0058","trace_type":"Structural","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_QGOH_PT_3:FGS5","text":"Affectus reciprocus imaginatus → conatus respondendi → circulus augmentationis Odii vel dissolutio eius per Amorem.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_QGOH_PT_3:P0074","trace_type":"Direct","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"},{"id":"RAW_QGOH_PT_3:P0076","trace_type":"Direct","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"},{"id":"RAW_QGOH_PT_3:P0078","trace_type":"Direct","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_QGOH_PT_3:FGS6","text":"Redistributio causae / causa libera imaginata / causa universalis imaginata → imminutio / intensio / collectivizatio affectus.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_QGOH_PT_3:P0099","trace_type":"Composite","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"},{"id":"RAW_QGOH_PT_3:P0103","trace_type":"Composite","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"},{"id":"RAW_QGOH_PT_3:P0088","trace_type":"Composite","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_QGOH_PT_3:FGS7","text":"Memoria, Spes, Metus et associatio accidentalis → repetitio Laetitiae vel Tristitiae → Omina et Superstitio; varietas Corporum → exitus affectivi non uniformes.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_QGOH_PT_3:P0096","trace_type":"Structural","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"},{"id":"RAW_QGOH_PT_3:P0097","trace_type":"Structural","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"},{"id":"RAW_QGOH_PT_3:P0113","trace_type":"Structural","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"},{"id":"RAW_QGOH_PT_3:P0118","trace_type":"Structural","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]}],"original_name":"Formal Generative Schema"},{"number":"9","name":"Argument Flow Map","items":[{"id":"CH_QGOH_PT_3:AF1","text":"Affectio Corporis → imago rei et affectus alterius → imitatio affectiva → Misericordia, Invidia et Ambitio.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_QGOH_PT_3:P0001","trace_type":"Direct","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"},{"id":"RAW_QGOH_PT_3:P0002","trace_type":"Direct","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_QGOH_PT_3:AF2","text":"Amor rei similis → conatus amoris reciproci → Gloria ex amore imaginato → exclusivitas obstructa → Zelotypia.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_QGOH_PT_3:P0004","trace_type":"Structural","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"},{"id":"RAW_QGOH_PT_3:P0008","trace_type":"Structural","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"},{"id":"RAW_QGOH_PT_3:P0012","trace_type":"Structural","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"},{"id":"RAW_QGOH_PT_3:P0016","trace_type":"Structural","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_QGOH_PT_3:AF3","text":"Laetitia vel Tristitia intensior → Cupiditas intensior conservandi vel removendi → beneficium, maleficium, Vindicta aut Gratitudo.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_QGOH_PT_3:P0029","trace_type":"Structural","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"},{"id":"RAW_QGOH_PT_3:P0031","trace_type":"Structural","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"},{"id":"RAW_QGOH_PT_3:P0039","trace_type":"Structural","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"},{"id":"RAW_QGOH_PT_3:P0058","trace_type":"Structural","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"},{"id":"RAW_QGOH_PT_3:P0064","trace_type":"Structural","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_QGOH_PT_3:AF4","text":"Odium vel Amor reciprocus imaginatus → feedback affectivus → augmentum Odii aut eius deletio per Amorem.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_QGOH_PT_3:P0074","trace_type":"Direct","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"},{"id":"RAW_QGOH_PT_3:P0076","trace_type":"Direct","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_QGOH_PT_3:AF5","text":"Causa affectus reassignata → imminutio amoris vel odii; causa libera imaginata → intensio; causa classis vel nationis → extensio ad collectum.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_QGOH_PT_3:P0099","trace_type":"Composite","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"},{"id":"RAW_QGOH_PT_3:P0103","trace_type":"Composite","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"},{"id":"RAW_QGOH_PT_3:P0088","trace_type":"Composite","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_QGOH_PT_3:AF6","text":"Memoria et associatio accidentalis → Spes aut Metus → iudicium affectivum boni, mali et ominis → Superstitio; constitutio corporis variabilis → pluralitas exitum.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_QGOH_PT_3:P0096","trace_type":"Structural","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"},{"id":"RAW_QGOH_PT_3:P0113","trace_type":"Structural","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"},{"id":"RAW_QGOH_PT_3:P0118","trace_type":"Structural","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]}],"original_name":"Argument Flow Map"},{"number":"10","name":"Map-Effects","items":[{"id":"CH_QGOH_PT_3:ME1","text":"Amor et Odium reclassificantur ut relationes causales imaginatae, non ut proprietates intrinsecae personarum vel rerum.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_QGOH_PT_3:P0041","trace_type":"Direct","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"},{"id":"RAW_QGOH_PT_3:P0101","trace_type":"Direct","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_QGOH_PT_3:ME2","text":"Zelotypia, Desiderium, Timor, Verecundia, Consternatio, Ira, Vindicta et Gratitudo describuntur ut figurae compositae ex paucis affectionibus primariis generatae.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_QGOH_PT_3:P0016","trace_type":"Structural","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"},{"id":"RAW_QGOH_PT_3:P0027","trace_type":"Structural","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"},{"id":"RAW_QGOH_PT_3:P0044","trace_type":"Structural","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"},{"id":"RAW_QGOH_PT_3:P0058","trace_type":"Structural","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"},{"id":"RAW_QGOH_PT_3:P0064","trace_type":"Structural","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_QGOH_PT_3:ME3","text":"Relatio affectiva inter duos agentes fit relatio circulatoria, in qua affectus alterius imaginatus causam novi affectus et actionis propriae constituit.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_QGOH_PT_3:P0046","trace_type":"Structural","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"},{"id":"RAW_QGOH_PT_3:P0060","trace_type":"Structural","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"},{"id":"RAW_QGOH_PT_3:P0074","trace_type":"Structural","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_QGOH_PT_3:ME4","text":"Individuum, rivalis, classis, natio et omen in eodem campo causali ponuntur, quia omnes imagines capaces sunt Laetitiam, Tristitiam, Spem vel Metum dirigendi.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_QGOH_PT_3:P0016","trace_type":"Structural","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"},{"id":"RAW_QGOH_PT_3:P0088","trace_type":"Structural","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"},{"id":"RAW_QGOH_PT_3:P0113","trace_type":"Structural","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_QGOH_PT_3:ME5","text":"Distinctio inter causam solitariam, causam multiplicem et causam universalizatam fit principium mensurandi directionem atque intensionem affectus.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_QGOH_PT_3:P0099","trace_type":"Direct","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"},{"id":"RAW_QGOH_PT_3:P0103","trace_type":"Direct","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"},{"id":"RAW_QGOH_PT_3:P0105","trace_type":"Direct","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_QGOH_PT_3:ME6","text":"Bonum et malum transferuntur ex ordine rerum in ordinem aestimationum affectivarum, ita ut diversitas corporum diversitatem valorum efficiat.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_QGOH_PT_3:P0043","trace_type":"Composite","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"},{"id":"RAW_QGOH_PT_3:P0116","trace_type":"Composite","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]}],"original_name":"Map-Effects"},{"number":"11","name":"Altitude Levels","items":[{"id":"CH_QGOH_PT_3:ALT1","text":"Surface — Textus nomina et nexus passionum determinatarum explicat: Amorem, Odium, Zelotypiam, Desiderium, Timorem, Vindictam, Gratitudinem, Spem et Metum.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_QGOH_PT_3:P0016","trace_type":"Structural","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"},{"id":"RAW_QGOH_PT_3:P0027","trace_type":"Structural","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"},{"id":"RAW_QGOH_PT_3:P0044","trace_type":"Structural","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"},{"id":"RAW_QGOH_PT_3:P0058","trace_type":"Structural","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"},{"id":"RAW_QGOH_PT_3:P0064","trace_type":"Structural","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_QGOH_PT_3:ALT2","text":"Structural — Affectiones Corporis, imagines, associatio causarum, imminutio vel augmentum potentiae agendi et conatus sunt mechanismi qui passiones generant.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_QGOH_PT_3:P0002","trace_type":"Structural","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"},{"id":"RAW_QGOH_PT_3:P0031","trace_type":"Structural","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"},{"id":"RAW_QGOH_PT_3:P0099","trace_type":"Structural","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"},{"id":"RAW_QGOH_PT_3:P0118","trace_type":"Structural","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_QGOH_PT_3:ALT3","text":"Metastrategic — Certamen inter amantes, inimicos, rivales et collecta describitur ut certamen pro directione imaginum causalium, pro conservatione Laetitiae et pro remotione Tristitiae.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_QGOH_PT_3:P0012","trace_type":"Structural","confidence":"medium","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"},{"id":"RAW_QGOH_PT_3:P0064","trace_type":"Structural","confidence":"medium","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"},{"id":"RAW_QGOH_PT_3:P0088","trace_type":"Structural","confidence":"medium","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"},{"id":"RAW_QGOH_PT_3:P0113","trace_type":"Structural","confidence":"medium","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_QGOH_PT_3:ALT4","text":"Ontological/Temporal — Affectus dependet a relatione corporis ad causas externas et ab imaginibus temporis, quibus memoria praeteritum praesentificat, absentia causam Tristitiae agit, et idem obiectum pro diversa dispositione diversiter operatur.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_QGOH_PT_3:P0019","trace_type":"Structural","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"},{"id":"RAW_QGOH_PT_3:P0023","trace_type":"Structural","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"},{"id":"RAW_QGOH_PT_3:P0096","trace_type":"Structural","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"},{"id":"RAW_QGOH_PT_3:P0116","trace_type":"Structural","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"},{"id":"RAW_QGOH_PT_3:P0118","trace_type":"Structural","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]}],"original_name":"Altitude Levels"},{"number":"12","name":"Minimal Reconstruction Test","items":[{"id":"CH_QGOH_PT_3:MRT1","text":"Axiomata de imagine corporali, imitatione, conatu, Laetitia, Tristitia et causalitate imaginata sufficiunt ad seriem principalem ab Amore reciproco ad Zelotypiam, Vindictam, Gratitudinem et Superstitionem restituendam.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_QGOH_PT_3:P0002","trace_type":"Structural","confidence":"medium","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"},{"id":"RAW_QGOH_PT_3:P0006","trace_type":"Structural","confidence":"medium","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"},{"id":"RAW_QGOH_PT_3:P0016","trace_type":"Structural","confidence":"medium","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"},{"id":"RAW_QGOH_PT_3:P0054","trace_type":"Structural","confidence":"medium","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"},{"id":"RAW_QGOH_PT_3:P0064","trace_type":"Structural","confidence":"medium","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"},{"id":"RAW_QGOH_PT_3:P0113","trace_type":"Structural","confidence":"medium","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_QGOH_PT_3:MRT2","text":"Restitutio adhuc pendet a definitionibus et propositionibus anterioribus de Corporis affectionibus, similitudo, imaginatione et conatu, quas locus saepe per citationes internas supponit potius quam iterum demonstrat.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_QGOH_PT_3:P0006","trace_type":"Direct","confidence":"medium","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"},{"id":"RAW_QGOH_PT_3:P0031","trace_type":"Direct","confidence":"medium","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"},{"id":"RAW_QGOH_PT_3:P0041","trace_type":"Direct","confidence":"medium","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_QGOH_PT_3:MRT3","text":"Systema firmissimum est ubi una eademque regula, scilicet Laetitia vel Tristitia cum idea causae, ad affectiones diversas reducitur.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_QGOH_PT_3:P0041","trace_type":"Composite","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"},{"id":"RAW_QGOH_PT_3:P0101","trace_type":"Composite","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_QGOH_PT_3:MRT4","text":"Commissura fragilis manet inter causalitatem imaginatam et causalitatem veram, quia locus explicat quomodo imago affectum producat, sed non criterium dat quo imago causae a cognitione causae distinguatur.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[]},{"id":"CH_QGOH_PT_3:MRT5","text":"Tensio interna oritur inter propagationem affectus ad classes vel nationes et varietatem Corporum, quia prima generalizationem socialem permittit, altera uniformem responsionem affectivam negat.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_QGOH_PT_3:P0088","trace_type":"Tension","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"},{"id":"RAW_QGOH_PT_3:P0116","trace_type":"Tension","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"},{"id":"RAW_QGOH_PT_3:P0118","trace_type":"Tension","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_QGOH_PT_3:MRT6","text":"Tensio altera manet inter causam liberam imaginatam, quae affectum auget, et ordinem affectuum ex necessariis affectionibus Corporis deductum; locus vim imaginationis libertatis explicat, non statum metaphysicum libertatis resolvit.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_QGOH_PT_3:P0103","trace_type":"Tension","confidence":"medium","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"},{"id":"RAW_QGOH_PT_3:P0105","trace_type":"Tension","confidence":"medium","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"},{"id":"RAW_QGOH_PT_3:P0118","trace_type":"Tension","confidence":"medium","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_QGOH_PT_3:MRT7","text":"Transitio a spe et metu individualibus ad Superstitionem est compendiosa, quia mechanismus credulitatis affectivae datur, sed formae sociales stabilitatis superstitionis hic non explicantur.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_QGOH_PT_3:P0113","trace_type":"Composite","confidence":"medium","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"},{"id":"RAW_QGOH_PT_3:P0114","trace_type":"Composite","confidence":"medium","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_QGOH_PT_3:MRT8","text":"Structura extracta motum textus servat, quia ab affectione corporali et imagine causae ad circuitus interpersonales, collectivizationem affectuum et variationem corporalem progreditur.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_QGOH_PT_3:P0002","trace_type":"Structural","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"},{"id":"RAW_QGOH_PT_3:P0016","trace_type":"Structural","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"},{"id":"RAW_QGOH_PT_3:P0088","trace_type":"Structural","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"},{"id":"RAW_QGOH_PT_3:P0116","trace_type":"Structural","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"},{"id":"RAW_QGOH_PT_3:P0118","trace_type":"Structural","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]}],"original_name":"Minimal Reconstruction Test"}],"language_code":"la","language_name":"Latina","translations":[{"id":"CAREVAR_20261015T015227921997Z_77bfd984","language_code":"en","language_name":"English","title":"Part Three - On the Origin and Nature of the Affects · passage 3","source_sha256":"c19baed94ad5f798a8a312d3c1482e45183b4a4bf6f41f2f8c8ea3d85ba8ca78","sha256":"74448d57cfb8bf3af488cd6f577f1e114ced12b48bdf613e68497fdfc774b28e","rendition_type":"machine","review_status":"machine","model":"gpt-5.6-terra","created":"2026-10-15T01:52:27.925635+00:00","sections":[{"number":"1","name":"Argumental Spine","items":[{"id":"CH_QGOH_PT_3:AS1","text":"The text investigates how images of others' affects, attributed affective causes, and the striving for self-preservation produce a series of social passions from a few mechanisms.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_QGOH_PT_3:P0001","trace_type":"Structural","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"},{"id":"RAW_QGOH_PT_3:P0002","trace_type":"Structural","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"},{"id":"RAW_QGOH_PT_3:P0016","trace_type":"Structural","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"},{"id":"RAW_QGOH_PT_3:P0031","trace_type":"Structural","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"},{"id":"RAW_QGOH_PT_3:P0088","trace_type":"Structural","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"},{"id":"RAW_QGOH_PT_3:P0113","trace_type":"Structural","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"},{"id":"RAW_QGOH_PT_3:P0116","trace_type":"Structural","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_QGOH_PT_3:AS2","text":"The argument is reconstructive and quasi-mechanical: affections of the Body, images of things, and the diminution or increase of the power of acting are posited as sufficient causes of love, hate, desire, and fluctuations of mind.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_QGOH_PT_3:P0002","trace_type":"Structural","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"},{"id":"RAW_QGOH_PT_3:P0006","trace_type":"Structural","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"},{"id":"RAW_QGOH_PT_3:P0031","trace_type":"Structural","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"},{"id":"RAW_QGOH_PT_3:P0041","trace_type":"Structural","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"},{"id":"RAW_QGOH_PT_3:P0101","trace_type":"Structural","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_QGOH_PT_3:AS3","text":"The first movement proceeds from the imitation of affects and love for a similar thing to the striving to obtain reciprocal love, and to glory arising from imagined love.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_QGOH_PT_3:P0004","trace_type":"Structural","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"},{"id":"RAW_QGOH_PT_3:P0006","trace_type":"Structural","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"},{"id":"RAW_QGOH_PT_3:P0008","trace_type":"Structural","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"},{"id":"RAW_QGOH_PT_3:P0010","trace_type":"Structural","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_QGOH_PT_3:AS4","text":"The second movement shows how exclusion, rivalry, the absence of desired conditions, and the restraint of appetite transform love into jealousy, sadness, envy, or hate.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_QGOH_PT_3:P0012","trace_type":"Structural","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"},{"id":"RAW_QGOH_PT_3:P0014","trace_type":"Structural","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"},{"id":"RAW_QGOH_PT_3:P0016","trace_type":"Structural","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"},{"id":"RAW_QGOH_PT_3:P0023","trace_type":"Structural","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"},{"id":"RAW_QGOH_PT_3:P0039","trace_type":"Structural","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"},{"id":"RAW_QGOH_PT_3:P0044","trace_type":"Structural","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_QGOH_PT_3:AS5","text":"The third movement establishes reciprocal affects: imagined hate produces hate in return, evil received produces vengeance, imagined love produces love and gratitude, and reciprocal love produces the dissolution of hate.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_QGOH_PT_3:P0046","trace_type":"Structural","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"},{"id":"RAW_QGOH_PT_3:P0054","trace_type":"Structural","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"},{"id":"RAW_QGOH_PT_3:P0058","trace_type":"Structural","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"},{"id":"RAW_QGOH_PT_3:P0060","trace_type":"Structural","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"},{"id":"RAW_QGOH_PT_3:P0064","trace_type":"Structural","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"},{"id":"RAW_QGOH_PT_3:P0074","trace_type":"Structural","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"},{"id":"RAW_QGOH_PT_3:P0078","trace_type":"Structural","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_QGOH_PT_3:AS6","text":"The final movement explains the direction of affects through imagined causality: a redistributed cause diminishes an affect, an imagined free cause intensifies it, a cause under the name of a class or universal nation extends the affect, and the variety of bodies prevents affective uniformity.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_QGOH_PT_3:P0099","trace_type":"Structural","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"},{"id":"RAW_QGOH_PT_3:P0103","trace_type":"Structural","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"},{"id":"RAW_QGOH_PT_3:P0105","trace_type":"Structural","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"},{"id":"RAW_QGOH_PT_3:P0088","trace_type":"Structural","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"},{"id":"RAW_QGOH_PT_3:P0116","trace_type":"Structural","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]}],"original_name":"Argumental Spine"},{"number":"2","name":"Load-Bearing Axioms","items":[{"id":"CH_QGOH_PT_3:A1","text":"An image of a thing is an affection of the human Body by which the Body is disposed to act by external causes.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_QGOH_PT_3:P0002","trace_type":"Direct","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_QGOH_PT_3:A2","text":"One who imagines an affect in a thing similar to oneself is determined to imitate the corresponding affect.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_QGOH_PT_3:P0001","trace_type":"Composite","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"},{"id":"RAW_QGOH_PT_3:P0002","trace_type":"Composite","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_QGOH_PT_3:A3","text":"Love is Joy accompanied by the idea of an external cause, and Hate is Sadness accompanied by the idea of an external cause.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_QGOH_PT_3:P0041","trace_type":"Composite","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"},{"id":"RAW_QGOH_PT_3:P0101","trace_type":"Composite","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_QGOH_PT_3:A4","text":"Joy assists the power of acting and striving, whereas Sadness diminishes or restrains it.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_QGOH_PT_3:P0031","trace_type":"Direct","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_QGOH_PT_3:A5","text":"A human being, insofar as affected by Joy or Sadness, strives to preserve Joy and remove Sadness.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_QGOH_PT_3:P0031","trace_type":"Direct","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_QGOH_PT_3:A6","text":"Love for a similar thing produces a striving by which the loved thing may be affected with Joy accompanied by the idea of the lover.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_QGOH_PT_3:P0004","trace_type":"Direct","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"},{"id":"RAW_QGOH_PT_3:P0006","trace_type":"Direct","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_QGOH_PT_3:A7","text":"A thing or circumstance imagined together with a thing that gives joy or sadness can accidentally become a cause of the same affect.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_QGOH_PT_3:P0021","trace_type":"Direct","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_QGOH_PT_3:A8","text":"One who imagines oneself to be the cause of the Joy of a thing similar to oneself contemplates oneself with Joy and experiences glory.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_QGOH_PT_3:P0008","trace_type":"Direct","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"},{"id":"RAW_QGOH_PT_3:P0010","trace_type":"Direct","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_QGOH_PT_3:A9","text":"The image of a thing that impedes appetite, or involves the absence of a desired condition, produces Sadness with the idea of an impeding cause.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_QGOH_PT_3:P0023","trace_type":"Composite","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"},{"id":"RAW_QGOH_PT_3:P0025","trace_type":"Composite","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"},{"id":"RAW_QGOH_PT_3:P0027","trace_type":"Composite","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_QGOH_PT_3:A10","text":"Love and Hate endure toward a thing only insofar as the thing is imagined as a cause of Joy or Sadness.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_QGOH_PT_3:P0099","trace_type":"Composite","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"},{"id":"RAW_QGOH_PT_3:P0101","trace_type":"Composite","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_QGOH_PT_3:A11","text":"Affective causality distributed among several causes diminishes love or hate toward one cause; an imagined free cause, as though a solitary cause, increases the affect.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_QGOH_PT_3:P0099","trace_type":"Composite","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"},{"id":"RAW_QGOH_PT_3:P0103","trace_type":"Composite","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"},{"id":"RAW_QGOH_PT_3:P0105","trace_type":"Composite","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_QGOH_PT_3:A12","text":"The striving to inflict evil upon one whom one hates can be overcome by the intention of avoiding a greater evil to oneself.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_QGOH_PT_3:P0039","trace_type":"Direct","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"},{"id":"RAW_QGOH_PT_3:P0041","trace_type":"Direct","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_QGOH_PT_3:A13","text":"Good and evil are assessed from desire and affective aversion; desire does not arise from a prior judgment of good or evil.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_QGOH_PT_3:P0043","trace_type":"Direct","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_QGOH_PT_3:A14","text":"Hope and Fear are connected affects, through which a human being loves the hoped-for thing and hates the feared thing, and strives either to obtain it or to remove it.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_QGOH_PT_3:P0113","trace_type":"Composite","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"},{"id":"RAW_QGOH_PT_3:P0114","trace_type":"Composite","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_QGOH_PT_3:A15","text":"A human being readily believes what is hoped for, believes what is feared with greater difficulty, and judges both more or less than is just.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_QGOH_PT_3:P0113","trace_type":"Direct","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_QGOH_PT_3:A16","text":"The different constitution of Bodies causes the same object to affect different human beings, and the same human being at different times, in different ways.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_QGOH_PT_3:P0116","trace_type":"Direct","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"},{"id":"RAW_QGOH_PT_3:P0118","trace_type":"Direct","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]}],"original_name":"Load-Bearing Axioms"},{"number":"3","name":"Apophatic Structure","items":[{"id":"CH_QGOH_PT_3:NX1","text":"Jealousy is not a simple affect, but a fluctuation of mind arising simultaneously from Love and Hate, accompanied by the idea of a rival.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_QGOH_PT_3:P0016","trace_type":"Direct","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_QGOH_PT_3:NX2","text":"Desire is not the bare absence of the loved thing, but Sadness insofar as it regards the absence of the loved thing.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_QGOH_PT_3:P0027","trace_type":"Direct","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_QGOH_PT_3:NX3","text":"Fear is not mere cognition of a future evil, but a disposition by which a human being strives to avoid a greater evil through a lesser evil.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_QGOH_PT_3:P0044","trace_type":"Direct","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_QGOH_PT_3:NX4","text":"Shame is not a separate species of Fear, but Fear whose future evil is Shame.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_QGOH_PT_3:P0044","trace_type":"Direct","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_QGOH_PT_3:NX5","text":"Consternation is not theoretical doubt, but the restraint of Desire by fear of two evils, whereby a human being does not know what one prefers.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_QGOH_PT_3:P0044","trace_type":"Direct","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_QGOH_PT_3:NX6","text":"Good and evil are not objective properties that precede Desire, but affectively imposed names for things desired or averted.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_QGOH_PT_3:P0043","trace_type":"Direct","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_QGOH_PT_3:NX7","text":"Joy arising from the evil of a hated thing is not pure Joy, because the imagination of the Sadness of a similar thing necessarily remains.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_QGOH_PT_3:P0092","trace_type":"Direct","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"},{"id":"RAW_QGOH_PT_3:P0094","trace_type":"Direct","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_QGOH_PT_3:NX8","text":"Love increased after Hate does not make a human being desire Hate or Sadness as a means to greater Joy.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_QGOH_PT_3:P0082","trace_type":"Direct","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_QGOH_PT_3:NX9","text":"An omen is not an intrinsically good or evil sign, but a thing that accidentally arouses Hope or Fear.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_QGOH_PT_3:P0113","trace_type":"Direct","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]}],"original_name":"Apophatic Structure"},{"number":"4","name":"Derivative Propositions","items":[{"id":"CH_QGOH_PT_3:D1","text":"Pity, Envy, and Ambition proceed from the same aptitude for imagining and imitating the affects of others.","refs":["CH_QGOH_PT_3:A1","CH_QGOH_PT_3:A2"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"From","passages":[{"id":"RAW_QGOH_PT_3:P0001","trace_type":"Composite","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"},{"id":"RAW_QGOH_PT_3:P0002","trace_type":"Composite","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_QGOH_PT_3:D2","text":"Love for a similar thing gives rise to a striving for reciprocal love, and imagined reciprocal love increases glory.","refs":["CH_QGOH_PT_3:A3","CH_QGOH_PT_3:A6","CH_QGOH_PT_3:A8"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"From","passages":[{"id":"RAW_QGOH_PT_3:P0004","trace_type":"Composite","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"},{"id":"RAW_QGOH_PT_3:P0006","trace_type":"Composite","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"},{"id":"RAW_QGOH_PT_3:P0008","trace_type":"Composite","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_QGOH_PT_3:D3","text":"The imagination of a loved thing more closely joined to another restrains the appetite for exclusive love and produces Jealousy, that is, Love and Hate with Envy.","refs":["CH_QGOH_PT_3:A3","CH_QGOH_PT_3:A5","CH_QGOH_PT_3:A6","CH_QGOH_PT_3:A9"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"From","passages":[{"id":"RAW_QGOH_PT_3:P0012","trace_type":"Composite","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"},{"id":"RAW_QGOH_PT_3:P0014","trace_type":"Composite","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"},{"id":"RAW_QGOH_PT_3:P0016","trace_type":"Composite","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_QGOH_PT_3:D4","text":"The recollection of a thing that once gave joy produces a desire to possess the same thing with the same circumstances, and the lack of one circumstance generates Sadness.","refs":["CH_QGOH_PT_3:A5","CH_QGOH_PT_3:A7","CH_QGOH_PT_3:A9"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"From","passages":[{"id":"RAW_QGOH_PT_3:P0019","trace_type":"Composite","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"},{"id":"RAW_QGOH_PT_3:P0021","trace_type":"Composite","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"},{"id":"RAW_QGOH_PT_3:P0023","trace_type":"Composite","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_QGOH_PT_3:D5","text":"The Desire to preserve Joy or remove Sadness becomes greater in proportion as the affect from which it arises is greater.","refs":["CH_QGOH_PT_3:A4","CH_QGOH_PT_3:A5"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"From","passages":[{"id":"RAW_QGOH_PT_3:P0029","trace_type":"Composite","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"},{"id":"RAW_QGOH_PT_3:P0031","trace_type":"Composite","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"},{"id":"RAW_QGOH_PT_3:P0032","trace_type":"Composite","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_QGOH_PT_3:D6","text":"Love that wholly passes into Hate produces greater Hate than if the loved thing had never been loved, because Hate also restrains the strivings left behind by love.","refs":["CH_QGOH_PT_3:A3","CH_QGOH_PT_3:A4","CH_QGOH_PT_3:A5","CH_QGOH_PT_3:A6","CH_QGOH_PT_3:A9"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"From","passages":[{"id":"RAW_QGOH_PT_3:P0034","trace_type":"Direct","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"},{"id":"RAW_QGOH_PT_3:P0036","trace_type":"Direct","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"},{"id":"RAW_QGOH_PT_3:P0037","trace_type":"Direct","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_QGOH_PT_3:D7","text":"One who hates strives to inflict evil; one who loves strives to do good, unless the former striving is overcome by fear of a greater evil imminent to oneself.","refs":["CH_QGOH_PT_3:A3","CH_QGOH_PT_3:A5","CH_QGOH_PT_3:A12"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"From","passages":[{"id":"RAW_QGOH_PT_3:P0039","trace_type":"Direct","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"},{"id":"RAW_QGOH_PT_3:P0041","trace_type":"Direct","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_QGOH_PT_3:D8","text":"Hate imagined from another, when no cause of one's own hate is recognized, produces reciprocal Hate; an imagined just cause of one's own hate produces Shame.","refs":["CH_QGOH_PT_3:A2","CH_QGOH_PT_3:A3","CH_QGOH_PT_3:A9"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"From","passages":[{"id":"RAW_QGOH_PT_3:P0046","trace_type":"Composite","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"},{"id":"RAW_QGOH_PT_3:P0048","trace_type":"Composite","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"},{"id":"RAW_QGOH_PT_3:P0050","trace_type":"Composite","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_QGOH_PT_3:D9","text":"Evil received from Hate arouses Vengeance, whereas Love imagined from another produces reciprocal Love and Gratitude; the text nevertheless holds human beings to be more ready for Vengeance than for returning a benefit.","refs":["CH_QGOH_PT_3:A2","CH_QGOH_PT_3:A3","CH_QGOH_PT_3:A5","CH_QGOH_PT_3:A8"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"From","passages":[{"id":"RAW_QGOH_PT_3:P0054","trace_type":"Composite","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"},{"id":"RAW_QGOH_PT_3:P0058","trace_type":"Composite","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"},{"id":"RAW_QGOH_PT_3:P0064","trace_type":"Composite","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_QGOH_PT_3:D10","text":"A benefit given from Love or the hope of Glory produces Sadness when received ungratefully, because the cause of hoped-for glory is imagined to be excluded.","refs":["CH_QGOH_PT_3:A6","CH_QGOH_PT_3:A7","CH_QGOH_PT_3:A8","CH_QGOH_PT_3:A9"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"From","passages":[{"id":"RAW_QGOH_PT_3:P0070","trace_type":"Direct","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"},{"id":"RAW_QGOH_PT_3:P0072","trace_type":"Direct","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_QGOH_PT_3:D11","text":"Hate is increased by reciprocal hate, whereas imagined Love produces a striving to remove hate; if this striving prevails, Hate is destroyed and can pass into a greater Love.","refs":["CH_QGOH_PT_3:A3","CH_QGOH_PT_3:A4","CH_QGOH_PT_3:A5","CH_QGOH_PT_3:A8"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"From","passages":[{"id":"RAW_QGOH_PT_3:P0074","trace_type":"Direct","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"},{"id":"RAW_QGOH_PT_3:P0076","trace_type":"Direct","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"},{"id":"RAW_QGOH_PT_3:P0078","trace_type":"Direct","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_QGOH_PT_3:D12","text":"One who imagines someone to hate a loved thing hates that person, because one imagines that person to be the cause of the Sadness of the loved thing and consequently of one's own Sadness.","refs":["CH_QGOH_PT_3:A2","CH_QGOH_PT_3:A3","CH_QGOH_PT_3:A9"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"From","passages":[{"id":"RAW_QGOH_PT_3:P0084","trace_type":"Direct","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"},{"id":"RAW_QGOH_PT_3:P0086","trace_type":"Direct","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_QGOH_PT_3:D13","text":"Joy or Sadness received from one member of a different class or nation is extended to all of the same class or nation under a universal image.","refs":["CH_QGOH_PT_3:A3","CH_QGOH_PT_3:A7","CH_QGOH_PT_3:A10"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"From","passages":[{"id":"RAW_QGOH_PT_3:P0088","trace_type":"Direct","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_QGOH_PT_3:D14","text":"Joy arising from the evil of a hated thing is repeated, because the memory of the thing renews former Sadness, which the idea of evil or liberation simultaneously restrains.","refs":["CH_QGOH_PT_3:A2","CH_QGOH_PT_3:A3","CH_QGOH_PT_3:A7"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"From","passages":[{"id":"RAW_QGOH_PT_3:P0092","trace_type":"Composite","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"},{"id":"RAW_QGOH_PT_3:P0096","trace_type":"Composite","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"},{"id":"RAW_QGOH_PT_3:P0097","trace_type":"Composite","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_QGOH_PT_3:D15","text":"A cause of affect transferred to another thing diminishes or destroys Love or Hate toward the prior cause; an imagined free cause intensifies the same affect.","refs":["CH_QGOH_PT_3:A10","CH_QGOH_PT_3:A11"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"From","passages":[{"id":"RAW_QGOH_PT_3:P0099","trace_type":"Composite","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"},{"id":"RAW_QGOH_PT_3:P0101","trace_type":"Composite","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"},{"id":"RAW_QGOH_PT_3:P0103","trace_type":"Composite","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_QGOH_PT_3:D16","text":"Things that accidentally arouse Hope or Fear are loved or hated as means to hoped-for things or as impediments to feared things; Superstitions arise from this structure and from the asymmetry of credulity.","refs":["CH_QGOH_PT_3:A7","CH_QGOH_PT_3:A13","CH_QGOH_PT_3:A14","CH_QGOH_PT_3:A15"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"From","passages":[{"id":"RAW_QGOH_PT_3:P0109","trace_type":"Direct","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"},{"id":"RAW_QGOH_PT_3:P0113","trace_type":"Direct","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"},{"id":"RAW_QGOH_PT_3:P0114","trace_type":"Direct","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_QGOH_PT_3:D17","text":"No affection of an object is universally fixed, because the same object generates diverse affects according to the different disposition of Bodies and according to different times.","refs":["CH_QGOH_PT_3:A16"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"From","passages":[{"id":"RAW_QGOH_PT_3:P0116","trace_type":"Direct","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"},{"id":"RAW_QGOH_PT_3:P0118","trace_type":"Direct","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]}],"original_name":"Derivative Propositions"},{"number":"5","name":"Super-Derivatives","items":[{"id":"CH_QGOH_PT_3:SD1","text":"Interpersonal affects have the form of a feedback circuit: imagined Love reproduces Love and Glory, imagined Hate reproduces Hate, and imagined evil reproduces Vengeance.","refs":["CH_QGOH_PT_3:A2","CH_QGOH_PT_3:A3","CH_QGOH_PT_3:A5","CH_QGOH_PT_3:A8"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"From","passages":[{"id":"RAW_QGOH_PT_3:P0046","trace_type":"Composite","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"},{"id":"RAW_QGOH_PT_3:P0054","trace_type":"Composite","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"},{"id":"RAW_QGOH_PT_3:P0074","trace_type":"Composite","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_QGOH_PT_3:SD2","text":"Jealousy establishes a triadic structure in which the lover, the loved thing, and the rival are not three independent affections, but a single machine of restraint of appetite and redistribution of hate.","refs":["CH_QGOH_PT_3:A3","CH_QGOH_PT_3:A6","CH_QGOH_PT_3:A9"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"From","passages":[{"id":"RAW_QGOH_PT_3:P0012","trace_type":"Structural","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"},{"id":"RAW_QGOH_PT_3:P0014","trace_type":"Structural","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"},{"id":"RAW_QGOH_PT_3:P0016","trace_type":"Structural","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_QGOH_PT_3:SD3","text":"Imagined causality is an operator of affective direction: individuation of the cause intensifies the affect, multiplication of causes dilutes it, and universalization of the cause under a class or nation collectivizes it.","refs":["CH_QGOH_PT_3:A7","CH_QGOH_PT_3:A10","CH_QGOH_PT_3:A11"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"From","passages":[{"id":"RAW_QGOH_PT_3:P0099","trace_type":"Structural","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"},{"id":"RAW_QGOH_PT_3:P0103","trace_type":"Structural","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"},{"id":"RAW_QGOH_PT_3:P0105","trace_type":"Structural","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"},{"id":"RAW_QGOH_PT_3:P0088","trace_type":"Structural","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_QGOH_PT_3:SD4","text":"Memory and absence are not affectively inert: the image of a past thing simulates its presence, while the lack of a circumstance or the memory of evil effects a mechanism of Sadness and repeated Joy.","refs":["CH_QGOH_PT_3:A7","CH_QGOH_PT_3:A9"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"From","passages":[{"id":"RAW_QGOH_PT_3:P0019","trace_type":"Composite","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"},{"id":"RAW_QGOH_PT_3:P0023","trace_type":"Composite","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"},{"id":"RAW_QGOH_PT_3:P0096","trace_type":"Composite","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"},{"id":"RAW_QGOH_PT_3:P0097","trace_type":"Composite","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_QGOH_PT_3:SD5","text":"Judgments of good, evil, useful, useless, ominous, and harmful describe an affective cartography of desire, not an order of values independent of desire.","refs":["CH_QGOH_PT_3:A13","CH_QGOH_PT_3:A14","CH_QGOH_PT_3:A15"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"From","passages":[{"id":"RAW_QGOH_PT_3:P0043","trace_type":"Structural","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"},{"id":"RAW_QGOH_PT_3:P0044","trace_type":"Structural","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"},{"id":"RAW_QGOH_PT_3:P0113","trace_type":"Structural","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_QGOH_PT_3:SD6","text":"The system simultaneously admits the social propagation of affects through universal images and denies affective uniformity through the constitutive diversity of Bodies.","refs":["CH_QGOH_PT_3:A7","CH_QGOH_PT_3:A16"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"From","passages":[{"id":"RAW_QGOH_PT_3:P0088","trace_type":"Tension","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"},{"id":"RAW_QGOH_PT_3:P0116","trace_type":"Tension","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"},{"id":"RAW_QGOH_PT_3:P0118","trace_type":"Tension","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]}],"original_name":"Super-Derivatives"},{"number":"6","name":"Deep Apophatics","items":[{"id":"CH_QGOH_PT_3:DNX1","text":"The system tacitly excludes affect as a purely internal motion without an imagined external cause, because Love and Hate are constituted through the idea of an external cause.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_QGOH_PT_3:P0041","trace_type":"Negative","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"},{"id":"RAW_QGOH_PT_3:P0101","trace_type":"Negative","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_QGOH_PT_3:DNX2","text":"The system tacitly excludes the objective value of good and evil, because every value is reduced to the structure of desire, aversion, and affect.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_QGOH_PT_3:P0043","trace_type":"Negative","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_QGOH_PT_3:DNX3","text":"The system tacitly excludes a simply dyadic affective relation, because love, hate, and glory are mediated by imagined third parties, rivals, classes, nations, and other causes.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_QGOH_PT_3:P0016","trace_type":"Negative","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"},{"id":"RAW_QGOH_PT_3:P0046","trace_type":"Negative","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"},{"id":"RAW_QGOH_PT_3:P0088","trace_type":"Negative","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_QGOH_PT_3:DNX4","text":"The system tacitly excludes a stable identity of affect toward an object, because memory, circumstance, an associated cause, and the state of the Body can alter its direction.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_QGOH_PT_3:P0023","trace_type":"Negative","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"},{"id":"RAW_QGOH_PT_3:P0099","trace_type":"Negative","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"},{"id":"RAW_QGOH_PT_3:P0116","trace_type":"Negative","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_QGOH_PT_3:DNX5","text":"The system tacitly excludes the rationally voluntary use of Sadness as a means to greater Joy, because striving necessarily tends to remove Sadness and preserve its own being.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_QGOH_PT_3:P0082","trace_type":"Negative","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_QGOH_PT_3:DNX6","text":"The system tacitly excludes the omen as an objective message of the future, because the force of the omen proceeds from accidental association with Hope or Fear.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_QGOH_PT_3:P0109","trace_type":"Negative","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"},{"id":"RAW_QGOH_PT_3:P0113","trace_type":"Negative","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]}],"original_name":"Deep Apophatics"},{"number":"7","name":"Super-Apophatics","items":[{"id":"CH_QGOH_PT_3:SNX1","text":"The text is ordered around the negation of an affectively autonomous subject: a human being does not produce one's affects from a free internal origin, but from images of causes, imitation, striving, and bodily constitution.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_QGOH_PT_3:P0002","trace_type":"Structural","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"},{"id":"RAW_QGOH_PT_3:P0031","trace_type":"Structural","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"},{"id":"RAW_QGOH_PT_3:P0101","trace_type":"Structural","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"},{"id":"RAW_QGOH_PT_3:P0118","trace_type":"Structural","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_QGOH_PT_3:SNX2","text":"The text is ordered around the absence of transcendent moral tribunals: good, evil, gratitude, vengeance, cruelty, and superstition are ordered as effects of the affective machine.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_QGOH_PT_3:P0043","trace_type":"Structural","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"},{"id":"RAW_QGOH_PT_3:P0058","trace_type":"Structural","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"},{"id":"RAW_QGOH_PT_3:P0064","trace_type":"Structural","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"},{"id":"RAW_QGOH_PT_3:P0113","trace_type":"Structural","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_QGOH_PT_3:SNX3","text":"The text is ordered around the impossibility of Joy wholly without a residue of Sadness, where Joy arises from the evil of a hated thing or from the abolition of hate.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_QGOH_PT_3:P0092","trace_type":"Structural","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"},{"id":"RAW_QGOH_PT_3:P0096","trace_type":"Structural","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"},{"id":"RAW_QGOH_PT_3:P0078","trace_type":"Structural","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_QGOH_PT_3:SNX4","text":"The text is ordered around the impossibility of one object affectively identical for all, because an object provides no invariant affective value outside its relation to a Body to be affected.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_QGOH_PT_3:P0116","trace_type":"Direct","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"},{"id":"RAW_QGOH_PT_3:P0118","trace_type":"Direct","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]}],"original_name":"Super-Apophatics"},{"number":"8","name":"Formal Generative Schema","items":[{"id":"CH_QGOH_PT_3:FGS1","text":"External affection → image of the Body → Joy or Sadness → idea of an external cause → Love or Hate.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_QGOH_PT_3:P0002","trace_type":"Structural","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"},{"id":"RAW_QGOH_PT_3:P0041","trace_type":"Structural","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"},{"id":"RAW_QGOH_PT_3:P0101","trace_type":"Structural","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_QGOH_PT_3:FGS2","text":"Image of an affect in a similar thing → affective imitation → Pity, Envy, or Ambition → social expansion of affect.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_QGOH_PT_3:P0001","trace_type":"Direct","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"},{"id":"RAW_QGOH_PT_3:P0002","trace_type":"Direct","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_QGOH_PT_3:FGS3","text":"Love for a similar thing → striving to affect it with Joy accompanied by the idea of oneself → imagined reciprocal Love → Glory.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_QGOH_PT_3:P0004","trace_type":"Structural","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"},{"id":"RAW_QGOH_PT_3:P0006","trace_type":"Structural","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"},{"id":"RAW_QGOH_PT_3:P0008","trace_type":"Structural","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_QGOH_PT_3:FGS4","text":"Rival, absence, or impediment → restraint of appetite → Sadness → Hate, Envy, Jealousy, Vengeance, or Desire.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_QGOH_PT_3:P0012","trace_type":"Structural","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"},{"id":"RAW_QGOH_PT_3:P0027","trace_type":"Structural","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"},{"id":"RAW_QGOH_PT_3:P0058","trace_type":"Structural","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_QGOH_PT_3:FGS5","text":"Imagined reciprocal affect → striving to respond → cycle of the increase of Hate or its dissolution through Love.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_QGOH_PT_3:P0074","trace_type":"Direct","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"},{"id":"RAW_QGOH_PT_3:P0076","trace_type":"Direct","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"},{"id":"RAW_QGOH_PT_3:P0078","trace_type":"Direct","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_QGOH_PT_3:FGS6","text":"Redistribution of cause / imagined free cause / imagined universal cause → diminution / intensification / collectivization of affect.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_QGOH_PT_3:P0099","trace_type":"Composite","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"},{"id":"RAW_QGOH_PT_3:P0103","trace_type":"Composite","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"},{"id":"RAW_QGOH_PT_3:P0088","trace_type":"Composite","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_QGOH_PT_3:FGS7","text":"Memory, Hope, Fear, and accidental association → repetition of Joy or Sadness → Omens and Superstition; variety of Bodies → non-uniform affective outcomes.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_QGOH_PT_3:P0096","trace_type":"Structural","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"},{"id":"RAW_QGOH_PT_3:P0097","trace_type":"Structural","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"},{"id":"RAW_QGOH_PT_3:P0113","trace_type":"Structural","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"},{"id":"RAW_QGOH_PT_3:P0118","trace_type":"Structural","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]}],"original_name":"Formal Generative Schema"},{"number":"9","name":"Argument Flow Map","items":[{"id":"CH_QGOH_PT_3:AF1","text":"Affection of the Body → image of a thing and of another's affect → affective imitation → Pity, Envy, and Ambition.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_QGOH_PT_3:P0001","trace_type":"Direct","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"},{"id":"RAW_QGOH_PT_3:P0002","trace_type":"Direct","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_QGOH_PT_3:AF2","text":"Love for a similar thing → striving for reciprocal love → Glory from imagined love → obstructed exclusivity → Jealousy.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_QGOH_PT_3:P0004","trace_type":"Structural","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"},{"id":"RAW_QGOH_PT_3:P0008","trace_type":"Structural","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"},{"id":"RAW_QGOH_PT_3:P0012","trace_type":"Structural","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"},{"id":"RAW_QGOH_PT_3:P0016","trace_type":"Structural","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_QGOH_PT_3:AF3","text":"More intense Joy or Sadness → more intense Desire to preserve or remove → benefit, maleficence, Vengeance, or Gratitude.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_QGOH_PT_3:P0029","trace_type":"Structural","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"},{"id":"RAW_QGOH_PT_3:P0031","trace_type":"Structural","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"},{"id":"RAW_QGOH_PT_3:P0039","trace_type":"Structural","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"},{"id":"RAW_QGOH_PT_3:P0058","trace_type":"Structural","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"},{"id":"RAW_QGOH_PT_3:P0064","trace_type":"Structural","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_QGOH_PT_3:AF4","text":"Imagined reciprocal Hate or Love → affective feedback → increase of Hate or its destruction through Love.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_QGOH_PT_3:P0074","trace_type":"Direct","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"},{"id":"RAW_QGOH_PT_3:P0076","trace_type":"Direct","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_QGOH_PT_3:AF5","text":"Reassigned cause of affect → diminution of love or hate; imagined free cause → intensification; cause of class or nation → extension to the collective.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_QGOH_PT_3:P0099","trace_type":"Composite","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"},{"id":"RAW_QGOH_PT_3:P0103","trace_type":"Composite","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"},{"id":"RAW_QGOH_PT_3:P0088","trace_type":"Composite","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_QGOH_PT_3:AF6","text":"Memory and accidental association → Hope or Fear → affective judgment of good, evil, and omen → Superstition; variable bodily constitution → plurality of outcomes.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_QGOH_PT_3:P0096","trace_type":"Structural","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"},{"id":"RAW_QGOH_PT_3:P0113","trace_type":"Structural","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"},{"id":"RAW_QGOH_PT_3:P0118","trace_type":"Structural","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]}],"original_name":"Argument Flow Map"},{"number":"10","name":"Map-Effects","items":[{"id":"CH_QGOH_PT_3:ME1","text":"Love and Hate are reclassified as imagined causal relations, not as intrinsic properties of persons or things.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_QGOH_PT_3:P0041","trace_type":"Direct","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"},{"id":"RAW_QGOH_PT_3:P0101","trace_type":"Direct","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_QGOH_PT_3:ME2","text":"Jealousy, Desire, Fear, Shame, Consternation, Anger, Vengeance, and Gratitude are described as composite figures generated from a few primary affections.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_QGOH_PT_3:P0016","trace_type":"Structural","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"},{"id":"RAW_QGOH_PT_3:P0027","trace_type":"Structural","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"},{"id":"RAW_QGOH_PT_3:P0044","trace_type":"Structural","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"},{"id":"RAW_QGOH_PT_3:P0058","trace_type":"Structural","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"},{"id":"RAW_QGOH_PT_3:P0064","trace_type":"Structural","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_QGOH_PT_3:ME3","text":"The affective relation between two agents becomes a circulatory relation, in which the imagined affect of the other constitutes the cause of a new affect and action of one's own.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_QGOH_PT_3:P0046","trace_type":"Structural","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"},{"id":"RAW_QGOH_PT_3:P0060","trace_type":"Structural","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"},{"id":"RAW_QGOH_PT_3:P0074","trace_type":"Structural","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_QGOH_PT_3:ME4","text":"The individual, the rival, the class, the nation, and the omen are placed in the same causal field, because all are images capable of directing Joy, Sadness, Hope, or Fear.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_QGOH_PT_3:P0016","trace_type":"Structural","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"},{"id":"RAW_QGOH_PT_3:P0088","trace_type":"Structural","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"},{"id":"RAW_QGOH_PT_3:P0113","trace_type":"Structural","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_QGOH_PT_3:ME5","text":"The distinction between a solitary cause, a multiple cause, and a universalized cause becomes a principle for measuring the direction and intensity of affect.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_QGOH_PT_3:P0099","trace_type":"Direct","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"},{"id":"RAW_QGOH_PT_3:P0103","trace_type":"Direct","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"},{"id":"RAW_QGOH_PT_3:P0105","trace_type":"Direct","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_QGOH_PT_3:ME6","text":"Good and evil are transferred from the order of things to the order of affective evaluations, so that the diversity of bodies produces a diversity of values.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_QGOH_PT_3:P0043","trace_type":"Composite","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"},{"id":"RAW_QGOH_PT_3:P0116","trace_type":"Composite","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]}],"original_name":"Map-Effects"},{"number":"11","name":"Altitude Levels","items":[{"id":"CH_QGOH_PT_3:ALT1","text":"Surface — The text explains the names and connections of determinate passions: Love, Hate, Jealousy, Desire, Fear, Vengeance, Gratitude, Hope, and Fear.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_QGOH_PT_3:P0016","trace_type":"Structural","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"},{"id":"RAW_QGOH_PT_3:P0027","trace_type":"Structural","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"},{"id":"RAW_QGOH_PT_3:P0044","trace_type":"Structural","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"},{"id":"RAW_QGOH_PT_3:P0058","trace_type":"Structural","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"},{"id":"RAW_QGOH_PT_3:P0064","trace_type":"Structural","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_QGOH_PT_3:ALT2","text":"Structural — Affections of the Body, images, association of causes, diminution or increase of the power of acting, and striving are the mechanisms that generate passions.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_QGOH_PT_3:P0002","trace_type":"Structural","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"},{"id":"RAW_QGOH_PT_3:P0031","trace_type":"Structural","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"},{"id":"RAW_QGOH_PT_3:P0099","trace_type":"Structural","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"},{"id":"RAW_QGOH_PT_3:P0118","trace_type":"Structural","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_QGOH_PT_3:ALT3","text":"Metastrategic — The contest among lovers, enemies, rivals, and collectives is described as a contest for the direction of causal images, for the preservation of Joy, and for the removal of Sadness.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_QGOH_PT_3:P0012","trace_type":"Structural","confidence":"medium","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"},{"id":"RAW_QGOH_PT_3:P0064","trace_type":"Structural","confidence":"medium","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"},{"id":"RAW_QGOH_PT_3:P0088","trace_type":"Structural","confidence":"medium","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"},{"id":"RAW_QGOH_PT_3:P0113","trace_type":"Structural","confidence":"medium","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_QGOH_PT_3:ALT4","text":"Ontological/Temporal — Affect depends on the relation of the body to external causes and on images of time, by which memory makes the past present, absence acts as a cause of Sadness, and the same object operates differently according to different dispositions.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_QGOH_PT_3:P0019","trace_type":"Structural","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"},{"id":"RAW_QGOH_PT_3:P0023","trace_type":"Structural","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"},{"id":"RAW_QGOH_PT_3:P0096","trace_type":"Structural","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"},{"id":"RAW_QGOH_PT_3:P0116","trace_type":"Structural","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"},{"id":"RAW_QGOH_PT_3:P0118","trace_type":"Structural","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]}],"original_name":"Altitude Levels"},{"number":"12","name":"Minimal Reconstruction Test","items":[{"id":"CH_QGOH_PT_3:MRT1","text":"Axioms concerning the bodily image, imitation, striving, Joy, Sadness, and imagined causality suffice to reconstruct the principal series from reciprocal Love to Jealousy, Vengeance, Gratitude, and Superstition.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_QGOH_PT_3:P0002","trace_type":"Structural","confidence":"medium","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"},{"id":"RAW_QGOH_PT_3:P0006","trace_type":"Structural","confidence":"medium","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"},{"id":"RAW_QGOH_PT_3:P0016","trace_type":"Structural","confidence":"medium","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"},{"id":"RAW_QGOH_PT_3:P0054","trace_type":"Structural","confidence":"medium","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"},{"id":"RAW_QGOH_PT_3:P0064","trace_type":"Structural","confidence":"medium","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"},{"id":"RAW_QGOH_PT_3:P0113","trace_type":"Structural","confidence":"medium","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_QGOH_PT_3:MRT2","text":"The reconstruction still depends on earlier definitions and propositions concerning affections of the Body, similarity, imagination, and striving, which the passage often presupposes through internal citations rather than demonstrates again.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_QGOH_PT_3:P0006","trace_type":"Direct","confidence":"medium","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"},{"id":"RAW_QGOH_PT_3:P0031","trace_type":"Direct","confidence":"medium","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"},{"id":"RAW_QGOH_PT_3:P0041","trace_type":"Direct","confidence":"medium","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_QGOH_PT_3:MRT3","text":"The system is strongest where one and the same rule, namely Joy or Sadness with the idea of a cause, is reduced to diverse affections.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_QGOH_PT_3:P0041","trace_type":"Composite","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"},{"id":"RAW_QGOH_PT_3:P0101","trace_type":"Composite","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_QGOH_PT_3:MRT4","text":"A fragile joint remains between imagined causality and true causality, because the passage explains how an image produces an affect, but does not provide a criterion by which the image of a cause is distinguished from cognition of a cause.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[]},{"id":"CH_QGOH_PT_3:MRT5","text":"An internal tension arises between the propagation of affect to classes or nations and the variety of Bodies, because the former permits social generalization while the latter denies a uniform affective response.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_QGOH_PT_3:P0088","trace_type":"Tension","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"},{"id":"RAW_QGOH_PT_3:P0116","trace_type":"Tension","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"},{"id":"RAW_QGOH_PT_3:P0118","trace_type":"Tension","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_QGOH_PT_3:MRT6","text":"A further tension remains between an imagined free cause, which increases affect, and an order of affects deduced from the necessary affections of the Body; the passage explains the force of the imagination of freedom, but does not resolve the metaphysical status of freedom.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_QGOH_PT_3:P0103","trace_type":"Tension","confidence":"medium","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"},{"id":"RAW_QGOH_PT_3:P0105","trace_type":"Tension","confidence":"medium","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"},{"id":"RAW_QGOH_PT_3:P0118","trace_type":"Tension","confidence":"medium","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_QGOH_PT_3:MRT7","text":"The transition from individual hope and fear to Superstition is abbreviated, because the mechanism of affective credulity is given, but the social forms of the stability of superstition are not explained here.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_QGOH_PT_3:P0113","trace_type":"Composite","confidence":"medium","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"},{"id":"RAW_QGOH_PT_3:P0114","trace_type":"Composite","confidence":"medium","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_QGOH_PT_3:MRT8","text":"The extracted structure preserves the movement of the text, because it proceeds from bodily affection and the image of a cause to interpersonal circuits, the collectivization of affects, and bodily variation.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_QGOH_PT_3:P0002","trace_type":"Structural","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"},{"id":"RAW_QGOH_PT_3:P0016","trace_type":"Structural","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"},{"id":"RAW_QGOH_PT_3:P0088","trace_type":"Structural","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"},{"id":"RAW_QGOH_PT_3:P0116","trace_type":"Structural","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"},{"id":"RAW_QGOH_PT_3:P0118","trace_type":"Structural","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]}],"original_name":"Minimal Reconstruction Test"}],"revision":"CH_QGOH_PT_3@R001","source_variant_id":"canonical:CH_QGOH_PT_3:v1","source_language_code":"la"}],"identity":{"layer":"CH","kicker":"Chapter 11","title":"Part Three - On the Origin and Nature of the Affects · passage 3","context":"Chapter 11","original_title":"Pars tertia - De origine et natura affectuum · passage 3","original_context":"Chapter 11"},"original_title":"Pars tertia - De origine et natura affectuum · passage 3","title_language_code":"en"},{"id":"CH_QGOH_PT_4","layer":"CH","title":"Part Three - On the Origin and Nature of the Affects · passage 4","order":12,"parents":["RAW_QGOH_PT_4"],"revision":"CH_QGOH_PT_4@R001","sha256":"0a9e600e0ff47bd4b74274d4c5479e2fb0d7d09e5a865d4795e8e951348c7838","sections":[{"number":"1","name":"Argumental Spine","items":[{"id":"CH_QGOH_PT_4:AS1","text":"Textus quaerit qua lege affectus, iudicia de bono et malo, aestimationes sui atque aliorum, et actiones rationales ex essentia individui, obiectis externis, imaginatione et cognitione oriantur.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_QGOH_PT_4:P0001","trace_type":"Structural","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"},{"id":"RAW_QGOH_PT_4:P0041","trace_type":"Structural","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"},{"id":"RAW_QGOH_PT_4:P0066","trace_type":"Structural","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"},{"id":"RAW_QGOH_PT_4:P0072","trace_type":"Structural","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_QGOH_PT_4:AS2","text":"Thesis est affectus non esse proprietates fixas rerum neque nomina moralia per se clara, sed mutationes potentiae agendi causaliter determinatas.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_QGOH_PT_4:P0075","trace_type":"Composite","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"},{"id":"RAW_QGOH_PT_4:P0077","trace_type":"Composite","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"},{"id":"RAW_QGOH_PT_4:P0079","trace_type":"Composite","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_QGOH_PT_4:AS3","text":"Argumentatio est diagnostica et reconstructiva: vocabula vulgaria dissolvit, mechanismos imaginativos et causales substituit, deinde ex tribus affectibus primitivis varietates deducit.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_QGOH_PT_4:P0009","trace_type":"Structural","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"},{"id":"RAW_QGOH_PT_4:P0010","trace_type":"Structural","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"},{"id":"RAW_QGOH_PT_4:P0050","trace_type":"Structural","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"},{"id":"RAW_QGOH_PT_4:P0051","trace_type":"Structural","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"},{"id":"RAW_QGOH_PT_4:P0066","trace_type":"Structural","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_QGOH_PT_4:AS4","text":"Variatio affectuum inter homines et intra eundem hominem dissolvit iudicia affectiva ut mensuras universales et comparationes sociales exponit.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_QGOH_PT_4:P0001","trace_type":"Composite","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"},{"id":"RAW_QGOH_PT_4:P0030","trace_type":"Composite","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"},{"id":"RAW_QGOH_PT_4:P0031","trace_type":"Composite","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"},{"id":"RAW_QGOH_PT_4:P0048","trace_type":"Composite","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_QGOH_PT_4:AS5","text":"Mechanismus associationis et singularitatis imaginatae explicat Admirationem, Contemptum et affectus compositos.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_QGOH_PT_4:P0005","trace_type":"Composite","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"},{"id":"RAW_QGOH_PT_4:P0007","trace_type":"Composite","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"},{"id":"RAW_QGOH_PT_4:P0009","trace_type":"Composite","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"},{"id":"RAW_QGOH_PT_4:P0011","trace_type":"Composite","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_QGOH_PT_4:AS6","text":"Distinctio inter imaginationem inadequatam et ideam adaequatam transitum constituit a passionibus ad affectus activos, Fortitudinem, Animositatem et Generositatem.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_QGOH_PT_4:P0041","trace_type":"Structural","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"},{"id":"RAW_QGOH_PT_4:P0059","trace_type":"Structural","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"},{"id":"RAW_QGOH_PT_4:P0063","trace_type":"Structural","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"},{"id":"RAW_QGOH_PT_4:P0065","trace_type":"Structural","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"},{"id":"RAW_QGOH_PT_4:P0066","trace_type":"Structural","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]}],"original_name":"Argumental Spine"},{"number":"2","name":"Load-Bearing Axioms","items":[{"id":"CH_QGOH_PT_4:A1","text":"Cupiditas est ipsa hominis essentia, quatenus ex quacumque affectione determinatur ad agendum, sive homo illius appetitus conscius sit sive non sit.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_QGOH_PT_4:P0070","trace_type":"Direct","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"},{"id":"RAW_QGOH_PT_4:P0072","trace_type":"Direct","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"},{"id":"RAW_QGOH_PT_4:P0073","trace_type":"Direct","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_QGOH_PT_4:A2","text":"Conatus Mentis est ipsa eius essentia, quae affirmat id tantum quod Mens est et potest, atque imaginatur quae suam agendi potentiam ponunt.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_QGOH_PT_4:P0020","trace_type":"Direct","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"},{"id":"RAW_QGOH_PT_4:P0022","trace_type":"Direct","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_QGOH_PT_4:A3","text":"Laetitia est transitus ad maiorem perfectionem sive augmentum potentiae agendi; Tristitia est transitus ad minorem perfectionem sive imminutio aut coercitio eiusdem potentiae.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_QGOH_PT_4:P0075","trace_type":"Direct","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"},{"id":"RAW_QGOH_PT_4:P0077","trace_type":"Direct","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"},{"id":"RAW_QGOH_PT_4:P0079","trace_type":"Direct","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_QGOH_PT_4:A4","text":"Mens se ipsam non cognoscit nisi per affectiones Corporis earumque ideas; contemplatio suae agendi potentiae Laetitiam efficit, tanto maiorem quanto distinctior est.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_QGOH_PT_4:P0014","trace_type":"Direct","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"},{"id":"RAW_QGOH_PT_4:P0016","trace_type":"Direct","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_QGOH_PT_4:A5","text":"Mens patitur quatenus ideas inadequatas habet atque imaginatur affectionem quae naturam sui Corporis et corporis externi involvit.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_QGOH_PT_4:P0041","trace_type":"Direct","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_QGOH_PT_4:A6","text":"Natura cuiusque passionis naturam obiecti a quo excitatur necessario exprimit.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_QGOH_PT_4:P0041","trace_type":"Direct","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"},{"id":"RAW_QGOH_PT_4:P0042","trace_type":"Direct","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_QGOH_PT_4:A7","text":"Affectus cuiusque individui ab affectu alterius tantum differt quantum essentia unius ab essentia alterius differt.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_QGOH_PT_4:P0048","trace_type":"Direct","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"},{"id":"RAW_QGOH_PT_4:P0051","trace_type":"Direct","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"},{"id":"RAW_QGOH_PT_4:P0052","trace_type":"Direct","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_QGOH_PT_4:A8","text":"Imaginatio obiecti cum aliis antea visi in contemplationem aliorum transit; imaginatio rei singularis nullo nexu cum reliquis coniunctae Mentem ad unum obiectum figit.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_QGOH_PT_4:P0005","trace_type":"Direct","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"},{"id":"RAW_QGOH_PT_4:P0007","trace_type":"Direct","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_QGOH_PT_4:A9","text":"Affectus aliorum, quatenus cum idea sui imaginantur, reflexe proprium affectum de se augent vel minuunt.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_QGOH_PT_4:P0018","trace_type":"Composite","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"},{"id":"RAW_QGOH_PT_4:P0028","trace_type":"Composite","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"},{"id":"RAW_QGOH_PT_4:P0030","trace_type":"Composite","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_QGOH_PT_4:A10","text":"Imaginatio proprietatis singularis in se et negatae de reliquis auget Laetitiam sui; comparatio cum aequali potentiori Tristitiam parit.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_QGOH_PT_4:P0030","trace_type":"Direct","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"},{"id":"RAW_QGOH_PT_4:P0031","trace_type":"Direct","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_QGOH_PT_4:A11","text":"Mens, cum ideam veram sive adaequatam concipit, se ipsam suamque agendi potentiam contemplatur et eatenus agit.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_QGOH_PT_4:P0059","trace_type":"Direct","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_QGOH_PT_4:A12","text":"Nullus affectus Tristitiae ad Mentem quatenus agit referri potest, quia Tristitia ipsam intelligendi et agendi potentiam minuit aut coercet.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_QGOH_PT_4:P0063","trace_type":"Direct","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_QGOH_PT_4:A13","text":"Fruitione obiecti Corpus novam constitutionem acquirere potest, qua eadem imago et eadem Cupiditas impeditae in Fastidium aut Taedium convertuntur.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_QGOH_PT_4:P0067","trace_type":"Direct","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"},{"id":"RAW_QGOH_PT_4:P0068","trace_type":"Direct","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_QGOH_PT_4:A14","text":"Actiones ex affectibus Mentis intelligentis ortae ad Fortitudinem pertinent, quae in Animositatem et Generositatem dividitur.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_QGOH_PT_4:P0065","trace_type":"Direct","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"},{"id":"RAW_QGOH_PT_4:P0066","trace_type":"Direct","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]}],"original_name":"Load-Bearing Axioms"},{"number":"3","name":"Apophatic Structure","items":[{"id":"CH_QGOH_PT_4:NX1","text":"Bonum, malum, melius et peius non iudicantur ex proprietate invariabili rerum, sed ex affectu iudicantis.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_QGOH_PT_4:P0001","trace_type":"Direct","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"},{"id":"RAW_QGOH_PT_4:P0002","trace_type":"Direct","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_QGOH_PT_4:NX2","text":"Intrepidus, audax, timidus et pusillanimis non sunt praedicata absoluta, sed nomina comparativa ex diverso metu et diversa Cupiditate nata.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_QGOH_PT_4:P0001","trace_type":"Direct","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"},{"id":"RAW_QGOH_PT_4:P0002","trace_type":"Direct","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_QGOH_PT_4:NX3","text":"Admiratio non est cognitio obiecti per se excellentis, sed imaginatio singularis quae Mentem ab aliis contemplationibus excludit.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_QGOH_PT_4:P0007","trace_type":"Direct","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"},{"id":"RAW_QGOH_PT_4:P0009","trace_type":"Direct","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_QGOH_PT_4:NX4","text":"Contemptus non est mera absentia Admirationis, sed fixatio Mentis in iis quae obiecto desunt postquam causae prioris Admirationis, Amoris aut Metus negantur.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_QGOH_PT_4:P0011","trace_type":"Direct","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_QGOH_PT_4:NX5","text":"Nomina affectuum non constituunt taxonomiam accuratam naturae affectivae, sed ex usu vulgari plerumque proveniunt.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_QGOH_PT_4:P0010","trace_type":"Direct","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"},{"id":"RAW_QGOH_PT_4:P0012","trace_type":"Direct","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_QGOH_PT_4:NX6","text":"Cupiditas non distinguitur re ab appetitu inconsciō, voluntate aut impetu, sed eos omnes sub una essentia determinata comprehendit.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_QGOH_PT_4:P0072","trace_type":"Direct","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"},{"id":"RAW_QGOH_PT_4:P0073","trace_type":"Direct","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_QGOH_PT_4:NX7","text":"Laetitia et Tristitia non sunt status perfectionis aut privationis, sed actus transitus inter gradus potentiae.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_QGOH_PT_4:P0075","trace_type":"Negative","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"},{"id":"RAW_QGOH_PT_4:P0077","trace_type":"Negative","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"},{"id":"RAW_QGOH_PT_4:P0079","trace_type":"Negative","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_QGOH_PT_4:NX8","text":"Temperantia, Sobrietas et Castitas non sunt passiones neque contraria objectiva Luxuriae, Ebrietatis et Libidinis, sed indicia potentiae quae affectus moderatur.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_QGOH_PT_4:P0045","trace_type":"Direct","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_QGOH_PT_4:NX9","text":"Tristitia activa non datur; ubi Tristitia adest, imminutio potentiae Mentis actionem excludit.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_QGOH_PT_4:P0063","trace_type":"Negative","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"},{"id":"RAW_QGOH_PT_4:P0079","trace_type":"Negative","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_QGOH_PT_4:NX10","text":"Affectus humanos non separat ab affectibus animalium aliqua forma affectiva absoluta, sed sola differentia naturarum individualium.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_QGOH_PT_4:P0054","trace_type":"Direct","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]}],"original_name":"Apophatic Structure"},{"number":"4","name":"Derivative Propositions","items":[{"id":"CH_QGOH_PT_4:D1","text":"Iudicia de bono et malo atque vocabula characteris variant secundum constitutionem affectivam iudicantis, non secundum normam intrinsecam obiecti.","refs":["CH_QGOH_PT_4:A1","CH_QGOH_PT_4:A3","CH_QGOH_PT_4:A7"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"From","passages":[{"id":"RAW_QGOH_PT_4:P0001","trace_type":"Composite","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"},{"id":"RAW_QGOH_PT_4:P0048","trace_type":"Composite","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_QGOH_PT_4:D2","text":"Paenitentia est Tristitia cum idea sui ut causae, et Acquiescentia in se ipso est Laetitia cum eadem idea sui ut causae.","refs":["CH_QGOH_PT_4:A3","CH_QGOH_PT_4:A4"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"From","passages":[{"id":"RAW_QGOH_PT_4:P0002","trace_type":"Direct","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"},{"id":"RAW_QGOH_PT_4:P0003","trace_type":"Direct","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_QGOH_PT_4:D3","text":"Singularitas imaginata parit Admirationem; Admiratio mali metuti Consternationem, Admiratio prudentiae Venerationem, et Amor cum Admiratione coniunctus Devotionem producit.","refs":["CH_QGOH_PT_4:A5","CH_QGOH_PT_4:A8"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"From","passages":[{"id":"RAW_QGOH_PT_4:P0007","trace_type":"Direct","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"},{"id":"RAW_QGOH_PT_4:P0009","trace_type":"Direct","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_QGOH_PT_4:D4","text":"Contemptus expectationem affectivam frustratam in contemplationem defectuum rei convertit, unde Irrisio et Dedignatio oriri possunt.","refs":["CH_QGOH_PT_4:A5","CH_QGOH_PT_4:A8"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"From","passages":[{"id":"RAW_QGOH_PT_4:P0011","trace_type":"Direct","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"},{"id":"RAW_QGOH_PT_4:P0012","trace_type":"Direct","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_QGOH_PT_4:D5","text":"Imaginatio laudis alienae Acquiescentiam in se ipso fovet, et imaginatio vituperii Humilitatem sive Tristitiam ex idea propriae imbecillitatis auget.","refs":["CH_QGOH_PT_4:A3","CH_QGOH_PT_4:A4","CH_QGOH_PT_4:A9"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"From","passages":[{"id":"RAW_QGOH_PT_4:P0018","trace_type":"Composite","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"},{"id":"RAW_QGOH_PT_4:P0028","trace_type":"Composite","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"},{"id":"RAW_QGOH_PT_4:P0030","trace_type":"Composite","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_QGOH_PT_4:D6","text":"Contemplatio actionum propriarum ut singularium Philautiam, iactationem et studium se ornandi parit; comparatio cum aequalibus fortioribus Invidiam promovet.","refs":["CH_QGOH_PT_4:A3","CH_QGOH_PT_4:A4","CH_QGOH_PT_4:A10"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"From","passages":[{"id":"RAW_QGOH_PT_4:P0030","trace_type":"Direct","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"},{"id":"RAW_QGOH_PT_4:P0031","trace_type":"Direct","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_QGOH_PT_4:D7","text":"Nemo virtutem alicui nisi aequali invidet, quia sola virtus quae cum propria natura comparabilis est propriam Cupiditatem coercere potest.","refs":["CH_QGOH_PT_4:A1","CH_QGOH_PT_4:A3","CH_QGOH_PT_4:A7","CH_QGOH_PT_4:A10"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"From","passages":[{"id":"RAW_QGOH_PT_4:P0033","trace_type":"Direct","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"},{"id":"RAW_QGOH_PT_4:P0035","trace_type":"Direct","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_QGOH_PT_4:D8","text":"Species Laetitiae, Tristitiae, Amoris, Odii et Cupiditatis multiplicantur secundum species obiectorum et secundum diversas constitutiones individuorum.","refs":["CH_QGOH_PT_4:A1","CH_QGOH_PT_4:A5","CH_QGOH_PT_4:A6","CH_QGOH_PT_4:A7"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"From","passages":[{"id":"RAW_QGOH_PT_4:P0039","trace_type":"Composite","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"},{"id":"RAW_QGOH_PT_4:P0041","trace_type":"Composite","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"},{"id":"RAW_QGOH_PT_4:P0042","trace_type":"Composite","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"},{"id":"RAW_QGOH_PT_4:P0048","trace_type":"Composite","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_QGOH_PT_4:D9","text":"Affectus equi, hominis, insectorum, piscium et avium eandem legem essentialem sequuntur, sed natura variant quia essentiae eorum diversae sunt.","refs":["CH_QGOH_PT_4:A7"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"From","passages":[{"id":"RAW_QGOH_PT_4:P0054","trace_type":"Direct","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_QGOH_PT_4:D10","text":"Affectus activos solum Laetitia et Cupiditas constituunt; Animositas utilitatem agentis, Generositas utilitatem aliorum et amicitiam ex ratione intendit.","refs":["CH_QGOH_PT_4:A11","CH_QGOH_PT_4:A12","CH_QGOH_PT_4:A14"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"From","passages":[{"id":"RAW_QGOH_PT_4:P0057","trace_type":"Composite","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"},{"id":"RAW_QGOH_PT_4:P0061","trace_type":"Composite","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"},{"id":"RAW_QGOH_PT_4:P0063","trace_type":"Composite","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"},{"id":"RAW_QGOH_PT_4:P0065","trace_type":"Composite","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"},{"id":"RAW_QGOH_PT_4:P0066","trace_type":"Composite","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_QGOH_PT_4:D11","text":"Eadem res, prius appetita, ex mutatione constitutionis Corporis odiosa fieri potest, quia nova dispositio Cupiditati iam excitatae repugnat.","refs":["CH_QGOH_PT_4:A1","CH_QGOH_PT_4:A13"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"From","passages":[{"id":"RAW_QGOH_PT_4:P0067","trace_type":"Direct","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"},{"id":"RAW_QGOH_PT_4:P0068","trace_type":"Direct","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_QGOH_PT_4:D12","text":"Definitio generalis affectuum sufficit ad potentiam Mentis moderandi et coercendi affectus intelligendam, quamvis singularum specierum numerus indefinitus sit.","refs":["CH_QGOH_PT_4:A6","CH_QGOH_PT_4:A7","CH_QGOH_PT_4:A12"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"From","passages":[{"id":"RAW_QGOH_PT_4:P0045","trace_type":"Composite","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"},{"id":"RAW_QGOH_PT_4:P0046","trace_type":"Composite","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"},{"id":"RAW_QGOH_PT_4:P0063","trace_type":"Composite","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]}],"original_name":"Derivative Propositions"},{"number":"5","name":"Super-Derivatives","items":[{"id":"CH_QGOH_PT_4:SD1","text":"Trias Cupiditatis, Laetitiae et Tristitiae, cum differentiis obiecti, essentiae et imaginationis, machinam generatricem innumerabilium affectuum praebet sine inventario completo specierum.","refs":["CH_QGOH_PT_4:A1","CH_QGOH_PT_4:A3","CH_QGOH_PT_4:A5","CH_QGOH_PT_4:A6","CH_QGOH_PT_4:A7"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"From","passages":[{"id":"RAW_QGOH_PT_4:P0050","trace_type":"Structural","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"},{"id":"RAW_QGOH_PT_4:P0051","trace_type":"Structural","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"},{"id":"RAW_QGOH_PT_4:P0066","trace_type":"Structural","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"},{"id":"RAW_QGOH_PT_4:P0070","trace_type":"Structural","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"},{"id":"RAW_QGOH_PT_4:P0075","trace_type":"Structural","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"},{"id":"RAW_QGOH_PT_4:P0077","trace_type":"Structural","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_QGOH_PT_4:SD2","text":"Valor rerum non antecedit affectum, sed ex motibus potentiae, comparationibus et imaginibus constituitur; iudicium morale fit effectus dynamicae affectivae.","refs":["CH_QGOH_PT_4:A2","CH_QGOH_PT_4:A3","CH_QGOH_PT_4:A7","CH_QGOH_PT_4:A10"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"From","passages":[{"id":"RAW_QGOH_PT_4:P0001","trace_type":"Structural","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"},{"id":"RAW_QGOH_PT_4:P0014","trace_type":"Structural","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"},{"id":"RAW_QGOH_PT_4:P0031","trace_type":"Structural","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"},{"id":"RAW_QGOH_PT_4:P0079","trace_type":"Structural","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_QGOH_PT_4:SD3","text":"Honor, vituperium, educatio, iactatio et Invidia formant circuitum socialem quo homines potentiam suam per affectus imaginatos aliorum metiuntur et vicissim alterant.","refs":["CH_QGOH_PT_4:A4","CH_QGOH_PT_4:A9","CH_QGOH_PT_4:A10"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"From","passages":[{"id":"RAW_QGOH_PT_4:P0018","trace_type":"Structural","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"},{"id":"RAW_QGOH_PT_4:P0028","trace_type":"Structural","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"},{"id":"RAW_QGOH_PT_4:P0030","trace_type":"Structural","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"},{"id":"RAW_QGOH_PT_4:P0031","trace_type":"Structural","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_QGOH_PT_4:SD4","text":"Distinctio actionis et passionis non in exteriori specie facti consistit, sed in modo causali et cognitivo quo Mens ideas adaequatas vel inadequatas habet.","refs":["CH_QGOH_PT_4:A5","CH_QGOH_PT_4:A11","CH_QGOH_PT_4:A12"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"From","passages":[{"id":"RAW_QGOH_PT_4:P0041","trace_type":"Structural","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"},{"id":"RAW_QGOH_PT_4:P0059","trace_type":"Structural","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"},{"id":"RAW_QGOH_PT_4:P0063","trace_type":"Structural","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_QGOH_PT_4:SD5","text":"Affectus temporaliter instabiles sunt, quia non res staticae sed transitus potentiae atque mutationes constitutionis corporis sunt.","refs":["CH_QGOH_PT_4:A3","CH_QGOH_PT_4:A13"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"From","passages":[{"id":"RAW_QGOH_PT_4:P0075","trace_type":"Composite","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"},{"id":"RAW_QGOH_PT_4:P0077","trace_type":"Composite","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"},{"id":"RAW_QGOH_PT_4:P0067","trace_type":"Composite","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"},{"id":"RAW_QGOH_PT_4:P0068","trace_type":"Composite","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]}],"original_name":"Super-Derivatives"},{"number":"6","name":"Deep Apophatics","items":[{"id":"CH_QGOH_PT_4:DNX1","text":"Falsum esse debet subjectum humanum se ipsum ut causam liberam, immediatam et transparentem sufficienter cognoscere; cognitio sui per affectiones Corporis mediatur.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_QGOH_PT_4:P0016","trace_type":"Negative","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"},{"id":"RAW_QGOH_PT_4:P0072","trace_type":"Negative","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"},{"id":"RAW_QGOH_PT_4:P0073","trace_type":"Negative","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_QGOH_PT_4:DNX2","text":"Falsa esse debet mensura universalis qua res per se bonae, malae, terribiles aut venerandae pronuntiantur independenter ab affectu et constitutione individui.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_QGOH_PT_4:P0001","trace_type":"Negative","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"},{"id":"RAW_QGOH_PT_4:P0041","trace_type":"Negative","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_QGOH_PT_4:DNX3","text":"Falsa esse debet opinio quod nomina recepta affectuum species naturales perfecte dividant; compositio affectuum vocabularium vulgare excedit.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_QGOH_PT_4:P0010","trace_type":"Negative","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"},{"id":"RAW_QGOH_PT_4:P0012","trace_type":"Negative","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"},{"id":"RAW_QGOH_PT_4:P0067","trace_type":"Negative","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_QGOH_PT_4:DNX4","text":"Falsa esse debet opinio quod virtus sit contraria passio vel simplex imminutio Cupiditatis; virtus activa est ordinatio potentiae per rationem.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_QGOH_PT_4:P0045","trace_type":"Negative","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"},{"id":"RAW_QGOH_PT_4:P0065","trace_type":"Negative","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"},{"id":"RAW_QGOH_PT_4:P0066","trace_type":"Negative","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_QGOH_PT_4:DNX5","text":"Falsa esse debet separatio absoluta hominis ab animalibus in ordine affectivo; differentia affectuum proportionem differentiae essentiarum sequitur.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_QGOH_PT_4:P0054","trace_type":"Negative","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]}],"original_name":"Deep Apophatics"},{"number":"7","name":"Super-Apophatics","items":[{"id":"CH_QGOH_PT_4:SNX1","text":"Systema nullum affectum purum admittit, qui a constitutione individui, relatione ad obiectum, idea Corporis et ordine imaginationis omnino solutus sit.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_QGOH_PT_4:P0041","trace_type":"Negative","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"},{"id":"RAW_QGOH_PT_4:P0042","trace_type":"Negative","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"},{"id":"RAW_QGOH_PT_4:P0048","trace_type":"Negative","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"},{"id":"RAW_QGOH_PT_4:P0073","trace_type":"Negative","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_QGOH_PT_4:SNX2","text":"Systema nullam stabilitatem affectivam finalem supponit: potentia transit, corpora reconfigurantur, obiecta variant, et Cupiditates in contraria flecti possunt.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_QGOH_PT_4:P0001","trace_type":"Negative","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"},{"id":"RAW_QGOH_PT_4:P0067","trace_type":"Negative","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"},{"id":"RAW_QGOH_PT_4:P0068","trace_type":"Negative","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"},{"id":"RAW_QGOH_PT_4:P0073","trace_type":"Negative","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_QGOH_PT_4:SNX3","text":"Systema nullam clausuram lexicalem aut enumerationem ultimam affectuum concedit; sola reductio ad causas primitivas infinitam varietatem tractabilem reddit.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_QGOH_PT_4:P0010","trace_type":"Negative","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"},{"id":"RAW_QGOH_PT_4:P0043","trace_type":"Negative","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"},{"id":"RAW_QGOH_PT_4:P0046","trace_type":"Negative","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"},{"id":"RAW_QGOH_PT_4:P0067","trace_type":"Negative","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]}],"original_name":"Super-Apophatics"},{"number":"8","name":"Formal Generative Schema","items":[{"id":"CH_QGOH_PT_4:FGS1","text":"Essentia individualis sive conatus + affectio data → Cupiditas determinata ad agendum.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_QGOH_PT_4:P0070","trace_type":"Direct","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"},{"id":"RAW_QGOH_PT_4:P0072","trace_type":"Direct","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"},{"id":"RAW_QGOH_PT_4:P0073","trace_type":"Direct","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_QGOH_PT_4:FGS2","text":"Corpus affectum + obiectum externum + idea inadequata → passio cuius species naturam obiecti exprimit.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_QGOH_PT_4:P0041","trace_type":"Direct","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_QGOH_PT_4:FGS3","text":"Augmentum potentiae → Laetitia; imminutio aut coercitio potentiae → Tristitia; utrumque → novas Cupiditates et affectus compositos.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_QGOH_PT_4:P0066","trace_type":"Composite","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"},{"id":"RAW_QGOH_PT_4:P0075","trace_type":"Composite","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"},{"id":"RAW_QGOH_PT_4:P0077","trace_type":"Composite","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"},{"id":"RAW_QGOH_PT_4:P0079","trace_type":"Composite","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_QGOH_PT_4:FGS4","text":"Associatio imaginativa → transitus ad alia obiecta; singularitas sine nexu → fixatio Mentis → Admiratio vel affectus ex Admiratione compositi.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_QGOH_PT_4:P0005","trace_type":"Direct","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"},{"id":"RAW_QGOH_PT_4:P0007","trace_type":"Direct","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"},{"id":"RAW_QGOH_PT_4:P0009","trace_type":"Direct","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_QGOH_PT_4:FGS5","text":"Contemplatio sui + imago laudis, vituperii aut comparationis → Acquiescentia, Humilitas, Philautia, Invidia et certamen comparativum.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_QGOH_PT_4:P0018","trace_type":"Composite","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"},{"id":"RAW_QGOH_PT_4:P0028","trace_type":"Composite","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"},{"id":"RAW_QGOH_PT_4:P0030","trace_type":"Composite","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"},{"id":"RAW_QGOH_PT_4:P0031","trace_type":"Composite","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_QGOH_PT_4:FGS6","text":"Idea adaequata → actio Mentis → Laetitia et Cupiditas activa → Animositas vel Generositas.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_QGOH_PT_4:P0059","trace_type":"Composite","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"},{"id":"RAW_QGOH_PT_4:P0065","trace_type":"Composite","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"},{"id":"RAW_QGOH_PT_4:P0066","trace_type":"Composite","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_QGOH_PT_4:FGS7","text":"Nova constitutio Corporis ex fruitione → repugnantia ad Cupiditatem priorem → Fastidium aut Taedium → nova series affectuum.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_QGOH_PT_4:P0067","trace_type":"Direct","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"},{"id":"RAW_QGOH_PT_4:P0068","trace_type":"Direct","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]}],"original_name":"Formal Generative Schema"},{"number":"9","name":"Argument Flow Map","items":[{"id":"CH_QGOH_PT_4:AF1","text":"Variatio affectuum inter homines et intra eundem hominem → relativizatio boni, mali et nominum characteris.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_QGOH_PT_4:P0001","trace_type":"Composite","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"},{"id":"RAW_QGOH_PT_4:P0002","trace_type":"Composite","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"},{"id":"RAW_QGOH_PT_4:P0048","trace_type":"Composite","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_QGOH_PT_4:AF2","text":"Associatio imaginum et singularitas imaginata → Admiratio, Consternatio, Veneratio, Devotio et Contemptus.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_QGOH_PT_4:P0007","trace_type":"Composite","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"},{"id":"RAW_QGOH_PT_4:P0009","trace_type":"Composite","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"},{"id":"RAW_QGOH_PT_4:P0011","trace_type":"Composite","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_QGOH_PT_4:AF3","text":"Contemplatio potentiae propriae → Laetitia sui → laus, vituperium et comparatio socialis → Humilitas, Philautia et Invidia.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_QGOH_PT_4:P0014","trace_type":"Structural","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"},{"id":"RAW_QGOH_PT_4:P0018","trace_type":"Structural","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"},{"id":"RAW_QGOH_PT_4:P0030","trace_type":"Structural","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"},{"id":"RAW_QGOH_PT_4:P0031","trace_type":"Structural","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_QGOH_PT_4:AF4","text":"Differentia obiectorum et essentiarum → multiplicatio specierum affectuum → imminutio valoris taxonomiae vulgaris.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_QGOH_PT_4:P0039","trace_type":"Composite","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"},{"id":"RAW_QGOH_PT_4:P0041","trace_type":"Composite","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"},{"id":"RAW_QGOH_PT_4:P0042","trace_type":"Composite","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"},{"id":"RAW_QGOH_PT_4:P0046","trace_type":"Composite","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_QGOH_PT_4:AF5","text":"Inadequatio et causae externae → passiones et fluctuationes; adaequatio → actio, Laetitia activa et Cupiditas activa.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_QGOH_PT_4:P0041","trace_type":"Composite","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"},{"id":"RAW_QGOH_PT_4:P0059","trace_type":"Composite","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"},{"id":"RAW_QGOH_PT_4:P0063","trace_type":"Composite","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_QGOH_PT_4:AF6","text":"Distinctio affectuum activorum → Fortitudo → Animositas et Generositas → definitio retroactiva Cupiditatis, Laetitiae, Tristitiae et Admirationis.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_QGOH_PT_4:P0057","trace_type":"Structural","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"},{"id":"RAW_QGOH_PT_4:P0061","trace_type":"Structural","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"},{"id":"RAW_QGOH_PT_4:P0065","trace_type":"Structural","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"},{"id":"RAW_QGOH_PT_4:P0066","trace_type":"Structural","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]}],"original_name":"Argument Flow Map"},{"number":"10","name":"Map-Effects","items":[{"id":"CH_QGOH_PT_4:ME1","text":"Bonum et malum reclassificantur ut iudicia affectiva, non ut qualitates immediatae rerum.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_QGOH_PT_4:P0001","trace_type":"Direct","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"},{"id":"RAW_QGOH_PT_4:P0041","trace_type":"Direct","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_QGOH_PT_4:ME2","text":"Admiratio et Contemptus reclassificantur ut dispositiones attentionis imaginativae, non ut responsa simplicia ad dignitatem obiectivam obiecti.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_QGOH_PT_4:P0007","trace_type":"Composite","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"},{"id":"RAW_QGOH_PT_4:P0009","trace_type":"Composite","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"},{"id":"RAW_QGOH_PT_4:P0011","trace_type":"Composite","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_QGOH_PT_4:ME3","text":"Amor sui, Humilitas, laus, vituperium et Invidia collocantur in eodem campo comparationis socialis et reflexionis potentiae.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_QGOH_PT_4:P0018","trace_type":"Structural","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"},{"id":"RAW_QGOH_PT_4:P0028","trace_type":"Structural","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"},{"id":"RAW_QGOH_PT_4:P0030","trace_type":"Structural","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"},{"id":"RAW_QGOH_PT_4:P0031","trace_type":"Structural","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_QGOH_PT_4:ME4","text":"Affectus innumerabiles intelliguntur per grammaticam causalium primitivorum potius quam per catalogum nominum receptarum.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_QGOH_PT_4:P0010","trace_type":"Structural","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"},{"id":"RAW_QGOH_PT_4:P0050","trace_type":"Structural","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"},{"id":"RAW_QGOH_PT_4:P0051","trace_type":"Structural","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"},{"id":"RAW_QGOH_PT_4:P0067","trace_type":"Structural","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_QGOH_PT_4:ME5","text":"Virtus reclassificatur ut potentia activa Mentis ratione ordinata, non ut oppositio passionis determinatae.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_QGOH_PT_4:P0065","trace_type":"Direct","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"},{"id":"RAW_QGOH_PT_4:P0066","trace_type":"Direct","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_QGOH_PT_4:ME6","text":"Differentia hominis et animalis transfertur ab oppositione essentiali ad differentiam graduum et modorum naturae individualis.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_QGOH_PT_4:P0054","trace_type":"Direct","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]}],"original_name":"Map-Effects"},{"number":"11","name":"Altitude Levels","items":[{"id":"CH_QGOH_PT_4:ALT1","text":"Gradus superficiei — Textus definit affectus singulares, inter quos Admirationem, Contemptum, Paenitentiam, Humilitatem, Invidiam, Fastidium, Animositatem et Generositatem.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_QGOH_PT_4:P0009","trace_type":"Structural","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"},{"id":"RAW_QGOH_PT_4:P0011","trace_type":"Structural","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"},{"id":"RAW_QGOH_PT_4:P0003","trace_type":"Structural","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"},{"id":"RAW_QGOH_PT_4:P0030","trace_type":"Structural","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"},{"id":"RAW_QGOH_PT_4:P0068","trace_type":"Structural","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"},{"id":"RAW_QGOH_PT_4:P0065","trace_type":"Structural","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"},{"id":"RAW_QGOH_PT_4:P0066","trace_type":"Structural","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_QGOH_PT_4:ALT2","text":"Gradus structuralis — Affectus producuntur ex conatu, transitu potentiae, affectionibus Corporis, obiectis externis, imaginatione associativa et comparatione sociali.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_QGOH_PT_4:P0070","trace_type":"Structural","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"},{"id":"RAW_QGOH_PT_4:P0075","trace_type":"Structural","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"},{"id":"RAW_QGOH_PT_4:P0077","trace_type":"Structural","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"},{"id":"RAW_QGOH_PT_4:P0041","trace_type":"Structural","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"},{"id":"RAW_QGOH_PT_4:P0007","trace_type":"Structural","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"},{"id":"RAW_QGOH_PT_4:P0031","trace_type":"Structural","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_QGOH_PT_4:ALT3","text":"Gradus metastrategicus — Vocabula moralia et psychologica vulgaria subordinantur methodo quae ex paucis definitionibus communes proprietates affectuum et potentiam Mentis deducit.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_QGOH_PT_4:P0010","trace_type":"Structural","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"},{"id":"RAW_QGOH_PT_4:P0046","trace_type":"Structural","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"},{"id":"RAW_QGOH_PT_4:P0050","trace_type":"Structural","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"},{"id":"RAW_QGOH_PT_4:P0066","trace_type":"Structural","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_QGOH_PT_4:ALT4","text":"Gradus ontologicus vel temporalis — Individuum est essentia perseverans quae sub affectionibus mutabilibus determinatur; affectus sunt transitus et reconfigurationes, non formae immobiles.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_QGOH_PT_4:P0048","trace_type":"Structural","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"},{"id":"RAW_QGOH_PT_4:P0067","trace_type":"Structural","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"},{"id":"RAW_QGOH_PT_4:P0068","trace_type":"Structural","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"},{"id":"RAW_QGOH_PT_4:P0079","trace_type":"Structural","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]}],"original_name":"Altitude Levels"},{"number":"12","name":"Minimal Reconstruction Test","items":[{"id":"CH_QGOH_PT_4:MRT1","text":"Axiomata de Cupiditate, potentia, transitu Laetitiae et Tristitiae, imaginatione, obiectis externis et cognitione adaequata sufficiunt ad praecipuos motus argumentationis restituendos.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_QGOH_PT_4:P0070","trace_type":"Structural","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"},{"id":"RAW_QGOH_PT_4:P0075","trace_type":"Structural","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"},{"id":"RAW_QGOH_PT_4:P0077","trace_type":"Structural","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"},{"id":"RAW_QGOH_PT_4:P0007","trace_type":"Structural","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"},{"id":"RAW_QGOH_PT_4:P0041","trace_type":"Structural","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"},{"id":"RAW_QGOH_PT_4:P0059","trace_type":"Structural","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_QGOH_PT_4:MRT2","text":"Systema maxime cohaeret ubi affectus singulares ex una machina conatus, mutationis potentiae et causae externae deducuntur.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_QGOH_PT_4:P0050","trace_type":"Structural","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"},{"id":"RAW_QGOH_PT_4:P0051","trace_type":"Structural","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"},{"id":"RAW_QGOH_PT_4:P0066","trace_type":"Structural","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"},{"id":"RAW_QGOH_PT_4:P0070","trace_type":"Structural","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_QGOH_PT_4:MRT3","text":"Firmissimum nodum constituit distinctio inter passionem ex inadequatione ortam et actionem ex idea adaequata ortam.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_QGOH_PT_4:P0041","trace_type":"Composite","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"},{"id":"RAW_QGOH_PT_4:P0059","trace_type":"Composite","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"},{"id":"RAW_QGOH_PT_4:P0063","trace_type":"Composite","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_QGOH_PT_4:MRT4","text":"Tensio manet inter infinitam varietatem affectuum ex differentiis obiectorum ortam et sufficientiam practicam definitionum generalium quae has differentias non explicant.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_QGOH_PT_4:P0039","trace_type":"Tension","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"},{"id":"RAW_QGOH_PT_4:P0043","trace_type":"Tension","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"},{"id":"RAW_QGOH_PT_4:P0046","trace_type":"Tension","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_QGOH_PT_4:MRT5","text":"Transitus ab idea adaequata ad actionem Mentis innititur principiis Partis II, quae hic assumuntur potius quam intra hunc locum iterum fundantur.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_QGOH_PT_4:P0059","trace_type":"Direct","confidence":"medium","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_QGOH_PT_4:MRT6","text":"Distinctio inter aequalem, cuius virtus Invidiam parit, et non-aequalem, cuius virtus Venerationem parit, comparabilitatem naturarum praesupponit quam textus localiter non determinat.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_QGOH_PT_4:P0033","trace_type":"Tension","confidence":"medium","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"},{"id":"RAW_QGOH_PT_4:P0035","trace_type":"Tension","confidence":"medium","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"},{"id":"RAW_QGOH_PT_4:P0037","trace_type":"Tension","confidence":"medium","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_QGOH_PT_4:MRT7","text":"Vehementia Paenitentiae et Acquiescentiae ex credulitate libertatis explicatur, sed ipsa conditio illius credulitatis hic indicatur potius quam plene resolvitur.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_QGOH_PT_4:P0002","trace_type":"Tension","confidence":"medium","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"},{"id":"RAW_QGOH_PT_4:P0003","trace_type":"Tension","confidence":"medium","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_QGOH_PT_4:MRT8","text":"Restitutio motum verum servat: a variatione iudiciorum ad mechanismos imaginationis, ab illis ad structuram socialem, a passionibus ad actionem, et denique ad definitiones primitivas revertitur.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_QGOH_PT_4:P0001","trace_type":"Structural","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"},{"id":"RAW_QGOH_PT_4:P0007","trace_type":"Structural","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"},{"id":"RAW_QGOH_PT_4:P0031","trace_type":"Structural","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"},{"id":"RAW_QGOH_PT_4:P0041","trace_type":"Structural","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"},{"id":"RAW_QGOH_PT_4:P0065","trace_type":"Structural","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"},{"id":"RAW_QGOH_PT_4:P0070","trace_type":"Structural","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]}],"original_name":"Minimal Reconstruction Test"}],"language_code":"la","language_name":"Latina","translations":[{"id":"CAREVAR_20261015T015255999419Z_aa3789ce","language_code":"en","language_name":"English","title":"Part Three - On the Origin and Nature of the Affects · passage 4","source_sha256":"0a9e600e0ff47bd4b74274d4c5479e2fb0d7d09e5a865d4795e8e951348c7838","sha256":"3f25b79800af178fc5963ce3aa7b25bd32b5dbc12e4a397328164b6697e27451","rendition_type":"machine","review_status":"machine","model":"gpt-5.6-terra","created":"2026-10-15T01:52:56.000390+00:00","sections":[{"number":"1","name":"Argumental Spine","items":[{"id":"CH_QGOH_PT_4:AS1","text":"The text asks by what law affects, judgments concerning good and evil, estimations of oneself and others, and rational actions arise from the essence of the individual, external objects, imagination, and cognition.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_QGOH_PT_4:P0001","trace_type":"Structural","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"},{"id":"RAW_QGOH_PT_4:P0041","trace_type":"Structural","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"},{"id":"RAW_QGOH_PT_4:P0066","trace_type":"Structural","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"},{"id":"RAW_QGOH_PT_4:P0072","trace_type":"Structural","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_QGOH_PT_4:AS2","text":"The thesis is that affects are not fixed properties of things nor moral names clear in themselves, but causally determined changes in the power of acting.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_QGOH_PT_4:P0075","trace_type":"Composite","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"},{"id":"RAW_QGOH_PT_4:P0077","trace_type":"Composite","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"},{"id":"RAW_QGOH_PT_4:P0079","trace_type":"Composite","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_QGOH_PT_4:AS3","text":"The argumentation is diagnostic and reconstructive: it dissolves common terms, substitutes imaginative and causal mechanisms, then derives varieties from three primitive affects.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_QGOH_PT_4:P0009","trace_type":"Structural","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"},{"id":"RAW_QGOH_PT_4:P0010","trace_type":"Structural","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"},{"id":"RAW_QGOH_PT_4:P0050","trace_type":"Structural","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"},{"id":"RAW_QGOH_PT_4:P0051","trace_type":"Structural","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"},{"id":"RAW_QGOH_PT_4:P0066","trace_type":"Structural","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_QGOH_PT_4:AS4","text":"The variation of affects among human beings and within the same human being dissolves affective judgments as universal measures and exposes social comparisons.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_QGOH_PT_4:P0001","trace_type":"Composite","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"},{"id":"RAW_QGOH_PT_4:P0030","trace_type":"Composite","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"},{"id":"RAW_QGOH_PT_4:P0031","trace_type":"Composite","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"},{"id":"RAW_QGOH_PT_4:P0048","trace_type":"Composite","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_QGOH_PT_4:AS5","text":"The mechanism of association and imagined singularity explains Wonder, Contempt, and composite affects.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_QGOH_PT_4:P0005","trace_type":"Composite","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"},{"id":"RAW_QGOH_PT_4:P0007","trace_type":"Composite","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"},{"id":"RAW_QGOH_PT_4:P0009","trace_type":"Composite","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"},{"id":"RAW_QGOH_PT_4:P0011","trace_type":"Composite","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_QGOH_PT_4:AS6","text":"The distinction between inadequate imagination and adequate idea constitutes the transition from passions to active affects, Fortitude, Strength of Mind, and Generosity.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_QGOH_PT_4:P0041","trace_type":"Structural","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"},{"id":"RAW_QGOH_PT_4:P0059","trace_type":"Structural","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"},{"id":"RAW_QGOH_PT_4:P0063","trace_type":"Structural","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"},{"id":"RAW_QGOH_PT_4:P0065","trace_type":"Structural","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"},{"id":"RAW_QGOH_PT_4:P0066","trace_type":"Structural","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]}],"original_name":"Argumental Spine"},{"number":"2","name":"Load-Bearing Axioms","items":[{"id":"CH_QGOH_PT_4:A1","text":"Desire is the very essence of the human being, insofar as it is determined by any affection to act, whether the human being is conscious of that appetite or not.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_QGOH_PT_4:P0070","trace_type":"Direct","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"},{"id":"RAW_QGOH_PT_4:P0072","trace_type":"Direct","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"},{"id":"RAW_QGOH_PT_4:P0073","trace_type":"Direct","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_QGOH_PT_4:A2","text":"The striving of the Mind is its very essence, which affirms only what the Mind is and can do, and imagines those things that posit its power of acting.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_QGOH_PT_4:P0020","trace_type":"Direct","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"},{"id":"RAW_QGOH_PT_4:P0022","trace_type":"Direct","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_QGOH_PT_4:A3","text":"Joy is a transition to greater perfection, or an increase in the power of acting; Sadness is a transition to lesser perfection, or a diminution or restraint of that same power.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_QGOH_PT_4:P0075","trace_type":"Direct","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"},{"id":"RAW_QGOH_PT_4:P0077","trace_type":"Direct","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"},{"id":"RAW_QGOH_PT_4:P0079","trace_type":"Direct","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_QGOH_PT_4:A4","text":"The Mind does not know itself except through affections of the Body and their ideas; contemplation of its own power of acting produces Joy, greater insofar as it is more distinct.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_QGOH_PT_4:P0014","trace_type":"Direct","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"},{"id":"RAW_QGOH_PT_4:P0016","trace_type":"Direct","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_QGOH_PT_4:A5","text":"The Mind is acted upon insofar as it has inadequate ideas and imagines an affection that involves the nature of its own Body and of an external body.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_QGOH_PT_4:P0041","trace_type":"Direct","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_QGOH_PT_4:A6","text":"The nature of every passion necessarily expresses the nature of the object by which it is aroused.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_QGOH_PT_4:P0041","trace_type":"Direct","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"},{"id":"RAW_QGOH_PT_4:P0042","trace_type":"Direct","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_QGOH_PT_4:A7","text":"The affect of each individual differs from the affect of another only insofar as the essence of one differs from the essence of the other.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_QGOH_PT_4:P0048","trace_type":"Direct","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"},{"id":"RAW_QGOH_PT_4:P0051","trace_type":"Direct","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"},{"id":"RAW_QGOH_PT_4:P0052","trace_type":"Direct","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_QGOH_PT_4:A8","text":"Imagination of an object previously seen with others passes into contemplation of the others; imagination of a singular thing joined to the rest by no connection fixes the Mind upon one object.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_QGOH_PT_4:P0005","trace_type":"Direct","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"},{"id":"RAW_QGOH_PT_4:P0007","trace_type":"Direct","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_QGOH_PT_4:A9","text":"The affects of others, insofar as they are imagined together with an idea of oneself, reflexively increase or diminish one's own affect concerning oneself.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_QGOH_PT_4:P0018","trace_type":"Composite","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"},{"id":"RAW_QGOH_PT_4:P0028","trace_type":"Composite","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"},{"id":"RAW_QGOH_PT_4:P0030","trace_type":"Composite","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_QGOH_PT_4:A10","text":"Imagination of a singular property in oneself and denied of others increases Joy in oneself; comparison with a more powerful equal produces Sadness.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_QGOH_PT_4:P0030","trace_type":"Direct","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"},{"id":"RAW_QGOH_PT_4:P0031","trace_type":"Direct","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_QGOH_PT_4:A11","text":"When the Mind conceives a true or adequate idea, it contemplates itself and its own power of acting and, to that extent, acts.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_QGOH_PT_4:P0059","trace_type":"Direct","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_QGOH_PT_4:A12","text":"No affect of Sadness can be referred to the Mind insofar as it acts, because Sadness diminishes or restrains the very power of understanding and acting.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_QGOH_PT_4:P0063","trace_type":"Direct","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_QGOH_PT_4:A13","text":"Through enjoyment of an object, the Body can acquire a new constitution, by which the same image and the same Desire are impeded and converted into Disgust or Weariness.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_QGOH_PT_4:P0067","trace_type":"Direct","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"},{"id":"RAW_QGOH_PT_4:P0068","trace_type":"Direct","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_QGOH_PT_4:A14","text":"Actions arising from the affects of an understanding Mind belong to Fortitude, which is divided into Strength of Mind and Generosity.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_QGOH_PT_4:P0065","trace_type":"Direct","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"},{"id":"RAW_QGOH_PT_4:P0066","trace_type":"Direct","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]}],"original_name":"Load-Bearing Axioms"},{"number":"3","name":"Apophatic Structure","items":[{"id":"CH_QGOH_PT_4:NX1","text":"Good, evil, better, and worse are not judged from an invariable property of things, but from the affect of the one judging.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_QGOH_PT_4:P0001","trace_type":"Direct","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"},{"id":"RAW_QGOH_PT_4:P0002","trace_type":"Direct","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_QGOH_PT_4:NX2","text":"The fearless, bold, timid, and faint-hearted are not absolute predicates, but comparative names born from differing fear and differing Desire.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_QGOH_PT_4:P0001","trace_type":"Direct","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"},{"id":"RAW_QGOH_PT_4:P0002","trace_type":"Direct","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_QGOH_PT_4:NX3","text":"Wonder is not cognition of an object excellent in itself, but a singular imagination that excludes the Mind from other contemplations.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_QGOH_PT_4:P0007","trace_type":"Direct","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"},{"id":"RAW_QGOH_PT_4:P0009","trace_type":"Direct","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_QGOH_PT_4:NX4","text":"Contempt is not the mere absence of Wonder, but fixation of the Mind on what the object lacks after the causes of prior Wonder, Love, or Fear are denied.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_QGOH_PT_4:P0011","trace_type":"Direct","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_QGOH_PT_4:NX5","text":"Names of affects do not constitute an accurate taxonomy of affective nature, but for the most part arise from common usage.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_QGOH_PT_4:P0010","trace_type":"Direct","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"},{"id":"RAW_QGOH_PT_4:P0012","trace_type":"Direct","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_QGOH_PT_4:NX6","text":"Desire is not distinguished in reality from unconscious appetite, will, or impulse, but comprehends them all under one determined essence.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_QGOH_PT_4:P0072","trace_type":"Direct","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"},{"id":"RAW_QGOH_PT_4:P0073","trace_type":"Direct","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_QGOH_PT_4:NX7","text":"Joy and Sadness are not states of perfection or privation, but acts of transition between degrees of power.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_QGOH_PT_4:P0075","trace_type":"Negative","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"},{"id":"RAW_QGOH_PT_4:P0077","trace_type":"Negative","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"},{"id":"RAW_QGOH_PT_4:P0079","trace_type":"Negative","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_QGOH_PT_4:NX8","text":"Temperance, Sobriety, and Chastity are neither passions nor objective contraries of Luxury, Drunkenness, and Lust, but signs of a power that moderates affects.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_QGOH_PT_4:P0045","trace_type":"Direct","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_QGOH_PT_4:NX9","text":"There is no active Sadness; where Sadness is present, the diminution of the Mind's power excludes action.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_QGOH_PT_4:P0063","trace_type":"Negative","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"},{"id":"RAW_QGOH_PT_4:P0079","trace_type":"Negative","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_QGOH_PT_4:NX10","text":"Human affects are not separated from animal affects by any absolute affective form, but only by the difference of individual natures.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_QGOH_PT_4:P0054","trace_type":"Direct","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]}],"original_name":"Apophatic Structure"},{"number":"4","name":"Derivative Propositions","items":[{"id":"CH_QGOH_PT_4:D1","text":"Judgments concerning good and evil and terms of character vary according to the affective constitution of the one judging, not according to an intrinsic norm of the object.","refs":["CH_QGOH_PT_4:A1","CH_QGOH_PT_4:A3","CH_QGOH_PT_4:A7"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"From","passages":[{"id":"RAW_QGOH_PT_4:P0001","trace_type":"Composite","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"},{"id":"RAW_QGOH_PT_4:P0048","trace_type":"Composite","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_QGOH_PT_4:D2","text":"Repentance is Sadness with the idea of oneself as cause, and Self-satisfaction is Joy with the same idea of oneself as cause.","refs":["CH_QGOH_PT_4:A3","CH_QGOH_PT_4:A4"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"From","passages":[{"id":"RAW_QGOH_PT_4:P0002","trace_type":"Direct","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"},{"id":"RAW_QGOH_PT_4:P0003","trace_type":"Direct","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_QGOH_PT_4:D3","text":"Imagined singularity produces Wonder; Wonder at a feared evil produces Consternation, Wonder at prudence produces Veneration, and Love joined with Wonder produces Devotion.","refs":["CH_QGOH_PT_4:A5","CH_QGOH_PT_4:A8"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"From","passages":[{"id":"RAW_QGOH_PT_4:P0007","trace_type":"Direct","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"},{"id":"RAW_QGOH_PT_4:P0009","trace_type":"Direct","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_QGOH_PT_4:D4","text":"Contempt converts frustrated affective expectation into contemplation of the defects of a thing, from which Derision and Scorn can arise.","refs":["CH_QGOH_PT_4:A5","CH_QGOH_PT_4:A8"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"From","passages":[{"id":"RAW_QGOH_PT_4:P0011","trace_type":"Direct","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"},{"id":"RAW_QGOH_PT_4:P0012","trace_type":"Direct","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_QGOH_PT_4:D5","text":"Imagination of another's praise fosters Self-satisfaction, and imagination of blame increases Humility, or Sadness from the idea of one's own weakness.","refs":["CH_QGOH_PT_4:A3","CH_QGOH_PT_4:A4","CH_QGOH_PT_4:A9"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"From","passages":[{"id":"RAW_QGOH_PT_4:P0018","trace_type":"Composite","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"},{"id":"RAW_QGOH_PT_4:P0028","trace_type":"Composite","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"},{"id":"RAW_QGOH_PT_4:P0030","trace_type":"Composite","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_QGOH_PT_4:D6","text":"Contemplation of one's own actions as singular produces Self-love, boasting, and the striving to adorn oneself; comparison with stronger equals promotes Envy.","refs":["CH_QGOH_PT_4:A3","CH_QGOH_PT_4:A4","CH_QGOH_PT_4:A10"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"From","passages":[{"id":"RAW_QGOH_PT_4:P0030","trace_type":"Direct","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"},{"id":"RAW_QGOH_PT_4:P0031","trace_type":"Direct","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_QGOH_PT_4:D7","text":"No one envies virtue in anyone except an equal, because only virtue comparable with one's own nature can restrain one's own Desire.","refs":["CH_QGOH_PT_4:A1","CH_QGOH_PT_4:A3","CH_QGOH_PT_4:A7","CH_QGOH_PT_4:A10"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"From","passages":[{"id":"RAW_QGOH_PT_4:P0033","trace_type":"Direct","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"},{"id":"RAW_QGOH_PT_4:P0035","trace_type":"Direct","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_QGOH_PT_4:D8","text":"The species of Joy, Sadness, Love, Hate, and Desire are multiplied according to the species of objects and according to the diverse constitutions of individuals.","refs":["CH_QGOH_PT_4:A1","CH_QGOH_PT_4:A5","CH_QGOH_PT_4:A6","CH_QGOH_PT_4:A7"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"From","passages":[{"id":"RAW_QGOH_PT_4:P0039","trace_type":"Composite","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"},{"id":"RAW_QGOH_PT_4:P0041","trace_type":"Composite","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"},{"id":"RAW_QGOH_PT_4:P0042","trace_type":"Composite","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"},{"id":"RAW_QGOH_PT_4:P0048","trace_type":"Composite","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_QGOH_PT_4:D9","text":"The affects of the horse, human being, insects, fish, and birds follow the same essential law, but vary in nature because their essences are different.","refs":["CH_QGOH_PT_4:A7"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"From","passages":[{"id":"RAW_QGOH_PT_4:P0054","trace_type":"Direct","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_QGOH_PT_4:D10","text":"Only Joy and Desire constitute active affects; Strength of Mind aims at the agent's advantage, Generosity at the advantage of others and friendship from reason.","refs":["CH_QGOH_PT_4:A11","CH_QGOH_PT_4:A12","CH_QGOH_PT_4:A14"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"From","passages":[{"id":"RAW_QGOH_PT_4:P0057","trace_type":"Composite","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"},{"id":"RAW_QGOH_PT_4:P0061","trace_type":"Composite","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"},{"id":"RAW_QGOH_PT_4:P0063","trace_type":"Composite","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"},{"id":"RAW_QGOH_PT_4:P0065","trace_type":"Composite","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"},{"id":"RAW_QGOH_PT_4:P0066","trace_type":"Composite","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_QGOH_PT_4:D11","text":"The same thing, previously desired, can become hateful through a change in the constitution of the Body, because the new disposition conflicts with Desire already aroused.","refs":["CH_QGOH_PT_4:A1","CH_QGOH_PT_4:A13"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"From","passages":[{"id":"RAW_QGOH_PT_4:P0067","trace_type":"Direct","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"},{"id":"RAW_QGOH_PT_4:P0068","trace_type":"Direct","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_QGOH_PT_4:D12","text":"The general definition of affects suffices for understanding the Mind's power to moderate and restrain affects, although the number of individual species is indefinite.","refs":["CH_QGOH_PT_4:A6","CH_QGOH_PT_4:A7","CH_QGOH_PT_4:A12"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"From","passages":[{"id":"RAW_QGOH_PT_4:P0045","trace_type":"Composite","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"},{"id":"RAW_QGOH_PT_4:P0046","trace_type":"Composite","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"},{"id":"RAW_QGOH_PT_4:P0063","trace_type":"Composite","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]}],"original_name":"Derivative Propositions"},{"number":"5","name":"Super-Derivatives","items":[{"id":"CH_QGOH_PT_4:SD1","text":"The triad of Desire, Joy, and Sadness, together with differences of object, essence, and imagination, provides the generative machine of innumerable affects without a complete inventory of species.","refs":["CH_QGOH_PT_4:A1","CH_QGOH_PT_4:A3","CH_QGOH_PT_4:A5","CH_QGOH_PT_4:A6","CH_QGOH_PT_4:A7"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"From","passages":[{"id":"RAW_QGOH_PT_4:P0050","trace_type":"Structural","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"},{"id":"RAW_QGOH_PT_4:P0051","trace_type":"Structural","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"},{"id":"RAW_QGOH_PT_4:P0066","trace_type":"Structural","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"},{"id":"RAW_QGOH_PT_4:P0070","trace_type":"Structural","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"},{"id":"RAW_QGOH_PT_4:P0075","trace_type":"Structural","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"},{"id":"RAW_QGOH_PT_4:P0077","trace_type":"Structural","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_QGOH_PT_4:SD2","text":"The value of things does not precede affect, but is constituted from movements of power, comparisons, and images; moral judgment becomes an effect of affective dynamics.","refs":["CH_QGOH_PT_4:A2","CH_QGOH_PT_4:A3","CH_QGOH_PT_4:A7","CH_QGOH_PT_4:A10"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"From","passages":[{"id":"RAW_QGOH_PT_4:P0001","trace_type":"Structural","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"},{"id":"RAW_QGOH_PT_4:P0014","trace_type":"Structural","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"},{"id":"RAW_QGOH_PT_4:P0031","trace_type":"Structural","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"},{"id":"RAW_QGOH_PT_4:P0079","trace_type":"Structural","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_QGOH_PT_4:SD3","text":"Honor, blame, education, boasting, and Envy form a social circuit by which human beings measure and reciprocally alter their power through the imagined affects of others.","refs":["CH_QGOH_PT_4:A4","CH_QGOH_PT_4:A9","CH_QGOH_PT_4:A10"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"From","passages":[{"id":"RAW_QGOH_PT_4:P0018","trace_type":"Structural","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"},{"id":"RAW_QGOH_PT_4:P0028","trace_type":"Structural","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"},{"id":"RAW_QGOH_PT_4:P0030","trace_type":"Structural","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"},{"id":"RAW_QGOH_PT_4:P0031","trace_type":"Structural","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_QGOH_PT_4:SD4","text":"The distinction of action and passion does not consist in the external appearance of the deed, but in the causal and cognitive mode by which the Mind has adequate or inadequate ideas.","refs":["CH_QGOH_PT_4:A5","CH_QGOH_PT_4:A11","CH_QGOH_PT_4:A12"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"From","passages":[{"id":"RAW_QGOH_PT_4:P0041","trace_type":"Structural","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"},{"id":"RAW_QGOH_PT_4:P0059","trace_type":"Structural","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"},{"id":"RAW_QGOH_PT_4:P0063","trace_type":"Structural","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_QGOH_PT_4:SD5","text":"Affects are temporally unstable because they are not static things but transitions of power and changes in bodily constitution.","refs":["CH_QGOH_PT_4:A3","CH_QGOH_PT_4:A13"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"From","passages":[{"id":"RAW_QGOH_PT_4:P0075","trace_type":"Composite","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"},{"id":"RAW_QGOH_PT_4:P0077","trace_type":"Composite","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"},{"id":"RAW_QGOH_PT_4:P0067","trace_type":"Composite","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"},{"id":"RAW_QGOH_PT_4:P0068","trace_type":"Composite","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]}],"original_name":"Super-Derivatives"},{"number":"6","name":"Deep Apophatics","items":[{"id":"CH_QGOH_PT_4:DNX1","text":"It must be false that the human subject sufficiently knows itself as a free, immediate, and transparent cause; self-knowledge is mediated through affections of the Body.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_QGOH_PT_4:P0016","trace_type":"Negative","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"},{"id":"RAW_QGOH_PT_4:P0072","trace_type":"Negative","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"},{"id":"RAW_QGOH_PT_4:P0073","trace_type":"Negative","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_QGOH_PT_4:DNX2","text":"There must be a false universal measure by which things are declared good, evil, terrible, or venerable in themselves independently of the affect and constitution of the individual.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_QGOH_PT_4:P0001","trace_type":"Negative","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"},{"id":"RAW_QGOH_PT_4:P0041","trace_type":"Negative","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_QGOH_PT_4:DNX3","text":"It must be false that received names of affects perfectly divide natural species; the composition of affects exceeds common vocabulary.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_QGOH_PT_4:P0010","trace_type":"Negative","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"},{"id":"RAW_QGOH_PT_4:P0012","trace_type":"Negative","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"},{"id":"RAW_QGOH_PT_4:P0067","trace_type":"Negative","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_QGOH_PT_4:DNX4","text":"It must be false that virtue is a contrary passion or a simple diminution of Desire; active virtue is the ordering of power through reason.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_QGOH_PT_4:P0045","trace_type":"Negative","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"},{"id":"RAW_QGOH_PT_4:P0065","trace_type":"Negative","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"},{"id":"RAW_QGOH_PT_4:P0066","trace_type":"Negative","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_QGOH_PT_4:DNX5","text":"The absolute separation of the human being from animals in the affective order must be false; the difference of affects follows the proportion of the difference of essences.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_QGOH_PT_4:P0054","trace_type":"Negative","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]}],"original_name":"Deep Apophatics"},{"number":"7","name":"Super-Apophatics","items":[{"id":"CH_QGOH_PT_4:SNX1","text":"The system admits no pure affect wholly detached from the constitution of the individual, relation to the object, idea of the Body, and order of imagination.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_QGOH_PT_4:P0041","trace_type":"Negative","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"},{"id":"RAW_QGOH_PT_4:P0042","trace_type":"Negative","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"},{"id":"RAW_QGOH_PT_4:P0048","trace_type":"Negative","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"},{"id":"RAW_QGOH_PT_4:P0073","trace_type":"Negative","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_QGOH_PT_4:SNX2","text":"The system supposes no final affective stability: power passes, bodies are reconfigured, objects vary, and Desires can be bent into their contraries.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_QGOH_PT_4:P0001","trace_type":"Negative","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"},{"id":"RAW_QGOH_PT_4:P0067","trace_type":"Negative","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"},{"id":"RAW_QGOH_PT_4:P0068","trace_type":"Negative","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"},{"id":"RAW_QGOH_PT_4:P0073","trace_type":"Negative","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_QGOH_PT_4:SNX3","text":"The system grants no lexical closure or final enumeration of affects; only reduction to primitive causes renders infinite variety manageable.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_QGOH_PT_4:P0010","trace_type":"Negative","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"},{"id":"RAW_QGOH_PT_4:P0043","trace_type":"Negative","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"},{"id":"RAW_QGOH_PT_4:P0046","trace_type":"Negative","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"},{"id":"RAW_QGOH_PT_4:P0067","trace_type":"Negative","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]}],"original_name":"Super-Apophatics"},{"number":"8","name":"Formal Generative Schema","items":[{"id":"CH_QGOH_PT_4:FGS1","text":"Individual essence or striving + given affection → Desire determined to act.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_QGOH_PT_4:P0070","trace_type":"Direct","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"},{"id":"RAW_QGOH_PT_4:P0072","trace_type":"Direct","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"},{"id":"RAW_QGOH_PT_4:P0073","trace_type":"Direct","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_QGOH_PT_4:FGS2","text":"Affected Body + external object + inadequate idea → passion whose species expresses the nature of the object.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_QGOH_PT_4:P0041","trace_type":"Direct","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_QGOH_PT_4:FGS3","text":"Increase of power → Joy; diminution or restraint of power → Sadness; either → new Desires and composite affects.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_QGOH_PT_4:P0066","trace_type":"Composite","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"},{"id":"RAW_QGOH_PT_4:P0075","trace_type":"Composite","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"},{"id":"RAW_QGOH_PT_4:P0077","trace_type":"Composite","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"},{"id":"RAW_QGOH_PT_4:P0079","trace_type":"Composite","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_QGOH_PT_4:FGS4","text":"Imaginative association → transition to other objects; singularity without connection → fixation of the Mind → Wonder or affects composed from Wonder.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_QGOH_PT_4:P0005","trace_type":"Direct","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"},{"id":"RAW_QGOH_PT_4:P0007","trace_type":"Direct","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"},{"id":"RAW_QGOH_PT_4:P0009","trace_type":"Direct","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_QGOH_PT_4:FGS5","text":"Contemplation of oneself + image of praise, blame, or comparison → Self-satisfaction, Humility, Self-love, Envy, and comparative contest.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_QGOH_PT_4:P0018","trace_type":"Composite","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"},{"id":"RAW_QGOH_PT_4:P0028","trace_type":"Composite","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"},{"id":"RAW_QGOH_PT_4:P0030","trace_type":"Composite","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"},{"id":"RAW_QGOH_PT_4:P0031","trace_type":"Composite","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_QGOH_PT_4:FGS6","text":"Adequate idea → action of the Mind → active Joy and Desire → Strength of Mind or Generosity.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_QGOH_PT_4:P0059","trace_type":"Composite","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"},{"id":"RAW_QGOH_PT_4:P0065","trace_type":"Composite","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"},{"id":"RAW_QGOH_PT_4:P0066","trace_type":"Composite","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_QGOH_PT_4:FGS7","text":"New constitution of the Body through enjoyment → opposition to prior Desire → Disgust or Weariness → new series of affects.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_QGOH_PT_4:P0067","trace_type":"Direct","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"},{"id":"RAW_QGOH_PT_4:P0068","trace_type":"Direct","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]}],"original_name":"Formal Generative Schema"},{"number":"9","name":"Argument Flow Map","items":[{"id":"CH_QGOH_PT_4:AF1","text":"Variation of affects among human beings and within the same human being → relativization of good, evil, and names of character.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_QGOH_PT_4:P0001","trace_type":"Composite","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"},{"id":"RAW_QGOH_PT_4:P0002","trace_type":"Composite","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"},{"id":"RAW_QGOH_PT_4:P0048","trace_type":"Composite","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_QGOH_PT_4:AF2","text":"Association of images and imagined singularity → Wonder, Consternation, Veneration, Devotion, and Contempt.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_QGOH_PT_4:P0007","trace_type":"Composite","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"},{"id":"RAW_QGOH_PT_4:P0009","trace_type":"Composite","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"},{"id":"RAW_QGOH_PT_4:P0011","trace_type":"Composite","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_QGOH_PT_4:AF3","text":"Contemplation of one's own power → Joy in oneself → praise, blame, and social comparison → Humility, Self-love, and Envy.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_QGOH_PT_4:P0014","trace_type":"Structural","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"},{"id":"RAW_QGOH_PT_4:P0018","trace_type":"Structural","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"},{"id":"RAW_QGOH_PT_4:P0030","trace_type":"Structural","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"},{"id":"RAW_QGOH_PT_4:P0031","trace_type":"Structural","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_QGOH_PT_4:AF4","text":"Difference of objects and essences → multiplication of species of affects → diminution of the value of common taxonomy.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_QGOH_PT_4:P0039","trace_type":"Composite","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"},{"id":"RAW_QGOH_PT_4:P0041","trace_type":"Composite","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"},{"id":"RAW_QGOH_PT_4:P0042","trace_type":"Composite","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"},{"id":"RAW_QGOH_PT_4:P0046","trace_type":"Composite","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_QGOH_PT_4:AF5","text":"Inadequacy and external causes → passions and fluctuations; adequacy → action, active Joy, and active Desire.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_QGOH_PT_4:P0041","trace_type":"Composite","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"},{"id":"RAW_QGOH_PT_4:P0059","trace_type":"Composite","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"},{"id":"RAW_QGOH_PT_4:P0063","trace_type":"Composite","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_QGOH_PT_4:AF6","text":"Distinction of active affects → Fortitude → Strength of Mind and Generosity → retroactive definition of Desire, Joy, Sadness, and Wonder.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_QGOH_PT_4:P0057","trace_type":"Structural","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"},{"id":"RAW_QGOH_PT_4:P0061","trace_type":"Structural","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"},{"id":"RAW_QGOH_PT_4:P0065","trace_type":"Structural","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"},{"id":"RAW_QGOH_PT_4:P0066","trace_type":"Structural","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]}],"original_name":"Argument Flow Map"},{"number":"10","name":"Map-Effects","items":[{"id":"CH_QGOH_PT_4:ME1","text":"Good and evil are reclassified as affective judgments, not as immediate qualities of things.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_QGOH_PT_4:P0001","trace_type":"Direct","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"},{"id":"RAW_QGOH_PT_4:P0041","trace_type":"Direct","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_QGOH_PT_4:ME2","text":"Wonder and Contempt are reclassified as dispositions of imaginative attention, not as simple responses to the objective worth of the object.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_QGOH_PT_4:P0007","trace_type":"Composite","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"},{"id":"RAW_QGOH_PT_4:P0009","trace_type":"Composite","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"},{"id":"RAW_QGOH_PT_4:P0011","trace_type":"Composite","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_QGOH_PT_4:ME3","text":"Self-love, Humility, praise, blame, and Envy are placed in the same field of social comparison and reflection on power.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_QGOH_PT_4:P0018","trace_type":"Structural","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"},{"id":"RAW_QGOH_PT_4:P0028","trace_type":"Structural","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"},{"id":"RAW_QGOH_PT_4:P0030","trace_type":"Structural","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"},{"id":"RAW_QGOH_PT_4:P0031","trace_type":"Structural","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_QGOH_PT_4:ME4","text":"Innumerable affects are understood through a grammar of primitive causes rather than through a catalogue of received names.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_QGOH_PT_4:P0010","trace_type":"Structural","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"},{"id":"RAW_QGOH_PT_4:P0050","trace_type":"Structural","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"},{"id":"RAW_QGOH_PT_4:P0051","trace_type":"Structural","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"},{"id":"RAW_QGOH_PT_4:P0067","trace_type":"Structural","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_QGOH_PT_4:ME5","text":"Virtue is reclassified as the active power of the Mind ordered by reason, not as the opposition of a determinate passion.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_QGOH_PT_4:P0065","trace_type":"Direct","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"},{"id":"RAW_QGOH_PT_4:P0066","trace_type":"Direct","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_QGOH_PT_4:ME6","text":"The difference between human being and animal is transferred from essential opposition to difference in degrees and modes of individual nature.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_QGOH_PT_4:P0054","trace_type":"Direct","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]}],"original_name":"Map-Effects"},{"number":"11","name":"Altitude Levels","items":[{"id":"CH_QGOH_PT_4:ALT1","text":"Surface level — The text defines singular affects, among them Wonder, Contempt, Repentance, Humility, Envy, Disgust, Strength of Mind, and Generosity.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_QGOH_PT_4:P0009","trace_type":"Structural","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"},{"id":"RAW_QGOH_PT_4:P0011","trace_type":"Structural","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"},{"id":"RAW_QGOH_PT_4:P0003","trace_type":"Structural","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"},{"id":"RAW_QGOH_PT_4:P0030","trace_type":"Structural","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"},{"id":"RAW_QGOH_PT_4:P0068","trace_type":"Structural","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"},{"id":"RAW_QGOH_PT_4:P0065","trace_type":"Structural","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"},{"id":"RAW_QGOH_PT_4:P0066","trace_type":"Structural","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_QGOH_PT_4:ALT2","text":"Structural level — Affects are produced from striving, transition of power, affections of the Body, external objects, associative imagination, and social comparison.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_QGOH_PT_4:P0070","trace_type":"Structural","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"},{"id":"RAW_QGOH_PT_4:P0075","trace_type":"Structural","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"},{"id":"RAW_QGOH_PT_4:P0077","trace_type":"Structural","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"},{"id":"RAW_QGOH_PT_4:P0041","trace_type":"Structural","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"},{"id":"RAW_QGOH_PT_4:P0007","trace_type":"Structural","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"},{"id":"RAW_QGOH_PT_4:P0031","trace_type":"Structural","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_QGOH_PT_4:ALT3","text":"Metastrategic level — Common moral and psychological terms are subordinated to a method that derives from a few definitions the common properties of affects and the power of the Mind.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_QGOH_PT_4:P0010","trace_type":"Structural","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"},{"id":"RAW_QGOH_PT_4:P0046","trace_type":"Structural","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"},{"id":"RAW_QGOH_PT_4:P0050","trace_type":"Structural","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"},{"id":"RAW_QGOH_PT_4:P0066","trace_type":"Structural","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_QGOH_PT_4:ALT4","text":"Ontological or temporal level — The individual is a persevering essence determined under mutable affections; affects are transitions and reconfigurations, not immobile forms.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_QGOH_PT_4:P0048","trace_type":"Structural","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"},{"id":"RAW_QGOH_PT_4:P0067","trace_type":"Structural","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"},{"id":"RAW_QGOH_PT_4:P0068","trace_type":"Structural","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"},{"id":"RAW_QGOH_PT_4:P0079","trace_type":"Structural","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]}],"original_name":"Altitude Levels"},{"number":"12","name":"Minimal Reconstruction Test","items":[{"id":"CH_QGOH_PT_4:MRT1","text":"Axioms concerning Desire, power, the transition of Joy and Sadness, imagination, external objects, and adequate cognition suffice to reconstruct the principal movements of the argumentation.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_QGOH_PT_4:P0070","trace_type":"Structural","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"},{"id":"RAW_QGOH_PT_4:P0075","trace_type":"Structural","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"},{"id":"RAW_QGOH_PT_4:P0077","trace_type":"Structural","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"},{"id":"RAW_QGOH_PT_4:P0007","trace_type":"Structural","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"},{"id":"RAW_QGOH_PT_4:P0041","trace_type":"Structural","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"},{"id":"RAW_QGOH_PT_4:P0059","trace_type":"Structural","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_QGOH_PT_4:MRT2","text":"The system is most coherent where singular affects are derived from one mechanism of striving, change of power, and external cause.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_QGOH_PT_4:P0050","trace_type":"Structural","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"},{"id":"RAW_QGOH_PT_4:P0051","trace_type":"Structural","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"},{"id":"RAW_QGOH_PT_4:P0066","trace_type":"Structural","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"},{"id":"RAW_QGOH_PT_4:P0070","trace_type":"Structural","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_QGOH_PT_4:MRT3","text":"The strongest node is constituted by the distinction between passion arising from inadequacy and action arising from an adequate idea.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_QGOH_PT_4:P0041","trace_type":"Composite","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"},{"id":"RAW_QGOH_PT_4:P0059","trace_type":"Composite","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"},{"id":"RAW_QGOH_PT_4:P0063","trace_type":"Composite","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_QGOH_PT_4:MRT4","text":"A tension remains between the infinite variety of affects arising from differences of objects and the practical sufficiency of general definitions that do not explain these differences.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_QGOH_PT_4:P0039","trace_type":"Tension","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"},{"id":"RAW_QGOH_PT_4:P0043","trace_type":"Tension","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"},{"id":"RAW_QGOH_PT_4:P0046","trace_type":"Tension","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_QGOH_PT_4:MRT5","text":"The transition from adequate idea to action of the Mind rests upon principles of Part II, which are assumed here rather than grounded again within this passage.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_QGOH_PT_4:P0059","trace_type":"Direct","confidence":"medium","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_QGOH_PT_4:MRT6","text":"The distinction between the equal, whose virtue produces Envy, and the non-equal, whose virtue produces Veneration, presupposes a comparability of natures that the text does not determine locally.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_QGOH_PT_4:P0033","trace_type":"Tension","confidence":"medium","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"},{"id":"RAW_QGOH_PT_4:P0035","trace_type":"Tension","confidence":"medium","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"},{"id":"RAW_QGOH_PT_4:P0037","trace_type":"Tension","confidence":"medium","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_QGOH_PT_4:MRT7","text":"The vehemence of Repentance and Self-satisfaction is explained from belief in freedom, but the very condition of that belief is indicated here rather than fully resolved.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_QGOH_PT_4:P0002","trace_type":"Tension","confidence":"medium","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"},{"id":"RAW_QGOH_PT_4:P0003","trace_type":"Tension","confidence":"medium","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_QGOH_PT_4:MRT8","text":"The reconstruction preserves the true movement: from variation of judgments to mechanisms of imagination, from these to social structure, from passions to action, and finally returns to primitive definitions.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_QGOH_PT_4:P0001","trace_type":"Structural","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"},{"id":"RAW_QGOH_PT_4:P0007","trace_type":"Structural","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"},{"id":"RAW_QGOH_PT_4:P0031","trace_type":"Structural","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"},{"id":"RAW_QGOH_PT_4:P0041","trace_type":"Structural","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"},{"id":"RAW_QGOH_PT_4:P0065","trace_type":"Structural","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"},{"id":"RAW_QGOH_PT_4:P0070","trace_type":"Structural","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]}],"original_name":"Minimal Reconstruction Test"}],"revision":"CH_QGOH_PT_4@R001","source_variant_id":"canonical:CH_QGOH_PT_4:v1","source_language_code":"la"}],"identity":{"layer":"CH","kicker":"Chapter 12","title":"Part Three - On the Origin and Nature of the Affects · passage 4","context":"Chapter 12","original_title":"Pars tertia - De origine et natura affectuum · passage 4","original_context":"Chapter 12"},"original_title":"Pars tertia - De origine et natura affectuum · passage 4","title_language_code":"en"},{"id":"CH_QGOH_PT_5","layer":"CH","title":"Part Three - On the Origin and Nature of the Affects · passage 5","order":13,"parents":["RAW_QGOH_PT_5"],"revision":"CH_QGOH_PT_5@R001","sha256":"f66502148a277cedde882ef7d375c2b55b5b046909d1f8ed8b331a5f6c36e6ed","sections":[{"number":"1","name":"Argumental Spine","items":[{"id":"CH_QGOH_PT_5:AS1","text":"Textus quaerit utrum multitudo nominum affectuum multitudinem naturarum primariarum significet, an varias denominationes paucorum motuum fundamentalium.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_QGOH_PT_5:P0002","trace_type":"Composite","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"},{"id":"RAW_QGOH_PT_5:P0139","trace_type":"Composite","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_QGOH_PT_5:AS2","text":"Textus statuit tres tantum affectus primitivos dari, Lætitiam, Tristitiam et Cupiditatem, ex quibus reliqui per relationes, causas imaginatas et compositiones oriuntur.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_QGOH_PT_5:P0001","trace_type":"Direct","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"},{"id":"RAW_QGOH_PT_5:P0002","trace_type":"Direct","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"},{"id":"RAW_QGOH_PT_5:P0139","trace_type":"Direct","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_QGOH_PT_5:AS3","text":"Argumentatio est reconstructiva et diagnostica: falsas species affectuum, voluntatem liberam et opposita nominalia removet, ut ordinem causaliter generativum constituat.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_QGOH_PT_5:P0001","trace_type":"Structural","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"},{"id":"RAW_QGOH_PT_5:P0008","trace_type":"Structural","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"},{"id":"RAW_QGOH_PT_5:P0009","trace_type":"Structural","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"},{"id":"RAW_QGOH_PT_5:P0050","trace_type":"Structural","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"},{"id":"RAW_QGOH_PT_5:P0074","trace_type":"Structural","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"},{"id":"RAW_QGOH_PT_5:P0089","trace_type":"Structural","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"},{"id":"RAW_QGOH_PT_5:P0137","trace_type":"Structural","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"},{"id":"RAW_QGOH_PT_5:P0139","trace_type":"Structural","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_QGOH_PT_5:AS4","text":"Definitio singularis affectus per genus primarium, ideam causae, modum temporalem, relationem socialem vel aestimationem imaginatam componitur.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_QGOH_PT_5:P0006","trace_type":"Composite","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"},{"id":"RAW_QGOH_PT_5:P0026","trace_type":"Composite","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"},{"id":"RAW_QGOH_PT_5:P0042","trace_type":"Composite","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"},{"id":"RAW_QGOH_PT_5:P0085","trace_type":"Composite","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"},{"id":"RAW_QGOH_PT_5:P0095","trace_type":"Composite","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"},{"id":"RAW_QGOH_PT_5:P0141","trace_type":"Composite","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_QGOH_PT_5:AS5","text":"Clausura generalis affectum passivum ad ideam confusam reducit, qua Mens mutationem potentiae corporis affirmat et ad certum cogitandum determinatur.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_QGOH_PT_5:P0141","trace_type":"Direct","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"},{"id":"RAW_QGOH_PT_5:P0143","trace_type":"Direct","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"},{"id":"RAW_QGOH_PT_5:P0145","trace_type":"Direct","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]}],"original_name":"Argumental Spine"},{"number":"2","name":"Load-Bearing Axioms","items":[{"id":"CH_QGOH_PT_5:A1","text":"Mens eatenus patitur, quatenus ideas inadæquatas sive confusas habet.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_QGOH_PT_5:P0143","trace_type":"Direct","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_QGOH_PT_5:A2","text":"Affectus animi, quatenus passio est, idea confusa est qua Mens majorem vel minorem existendi vim sui Corporis affirmat.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_QGOH_PT_5:P0141","trace_type":"Direct","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"},{"id":"RAW_QGOH_PT_5:P0143","trace_type":"Direct","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_QGOH_PT_5:A3","text":"Essentia Mentis consistit in affirmatione actualis existentiae sui Corporis, cuius maior vel minor realitas transitum ad majorem vel minorem perfectionem constituit.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_QGOH_PT_5:P0144","trace_type":"Direct","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_QGOH_PT_5:A4","text":"Lætitia et Tristitia sunt transitus Mentis ad majorem vel minorem perfectionem seu potentiam agendi.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_QGOH_PT_5:P0144","trace_type":"Composite","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"},{"id":"RAW_QGOH_PT_5:P0145","trace_type":"Composite","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_QGOH_PT_5:A5","text":"Cupiditas est determinatio Mentis qua, data idea, ad hoc potius quam ad aliud cogitandum vel agendum fertur.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_QGOH_PT_5:P0141","trace_type":"Direct","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"},{"id":"RAW_QGOH_PT_5:P0145","trace_type":"Direct","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_QGOH_PT_5:A6","text":"Lætitia, Tristitia et Cupiditas sunt solae formae affectuum primitivae.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_QGOH_PT_5:P0002","trace_type":"Direct","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"},{"id":"RAW_QGOH_PT_5:P0139","trace_type":"Direct","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_QGOH_PT_5:A7","text":"Idea causae externae, internae vel per accidens adjunctae affectum primarium in affectum denominatum determinat.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_QGOH_PT_5:P0006","trace_type":"Composite","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"},{"id":"RAW_QGOH_PT_5:P0011","trace_type":"Composite","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"},{"id":"RAW_QGOH_PT_5:P0015","trace_type":"Composite","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"},{"id":"RAW_QGOH_PT_5:P0017","trace_type":"Composite","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"},{"id":"RAW_QGOH_PT_5:P0064","trace_type":"Composite","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_QGOH_PT_5:A8","text":"Imago rei præteritae vel futuræ Mentem eodem affectu afficere potest quo imago rei præsentis, atque dubitatio eventus modum affectus mutat.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_QGOH_PT_5:P0026","trace_type":"Composite","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"},{"id":"RAW_QGOH_PT_5:P0028","trace_type":"Composite","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"},{"id":"RAW_QGOH_PT_5:P0030","trace_type":"Composite","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"},{"id":"RAW_QGOH_PT_5:P0032","trace_type":"Composite","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"},{"id":"RAW_QGOH_PT_5:P0034","trace_type":"Composite","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"},{"id":"RAW_QGOH_PT_5:P0036","trace_type":"Composite","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"},{"id":"RAW_QGOH_PT_5:P0093","trace_type":"Composite","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_QGOH_PT_5:A9","text":"Imaginatio similitudinis, cupiditatis, judicii aut actionis aliorum affectus sociales generat.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_QGOH_PT_5:P0042","trace_type":"Composite","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"},{"id":"RAW_QGOH_PT_5:P0046","trace_type":"Composite","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"},{"id":"RAW_QGOH_PT_5:P0048","trace_type":"Composite","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"},{"id":"RAW_QGOH_PT_5:P0058","trace_type":"Composite","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"},{"id":"RAW_QGOH_PT_5:P0085","trace_type":"Composite","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"},{"id":"RAW_QGOH_PT_5:P0087","trace_type":"Composite","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"},{"id":"RAW_QGOH_PT_5:P0095","trace_type":"Composite","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"},{"id":"RAW_QGOH_PT_5:P0097","trace_type":"Composite","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_QGOH_PT_5:A10","text":"Educatio, consuetudo et religio Lætitiam aut Tristitiam cum actibus conjungunt et inde judiciorum moralium affectivam vim constituunt.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_QGOH_PT_5:P0074","trace_type":"Direct","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_QGOH_PT_5:A11","text":"Liberum Mentis decretum sive deliberatio voluntaria non est causa realis primaria affectuum.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_QGOH_PT_5:P0009","trace_type":"Composite","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"},{"id":"RAW_QGOH_PT_5:P0070","trace_type":"Composite","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"},{"id":"RAW_QGOH_PT_5:P0072","trace_type":"Composite","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_QGOH_PT_5:A12","text":"Naturae rerum explicatio praevalet usui vulgari vocabulorum, quamvis vocabula recepta retineri possint ad res indicandas.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_QGOH_PT_5:P0050","trace_type":"Direct","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"},{"id":"RAW_QGOH_PT_5:P0089","trace_type":"Direct","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"},{"id":"RAW_QGOH_PT_5:P0139","trace_type":"Direct","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]}],"original_name":"Load-Bearing Axioms"},{"number":"3","name":"Apophatic Structure","items":[{"id":"CH_QGOH_PT_5:NX1","text":"Admiratio non numeratur inter affectus, quia distractio Mentis ex novitate non ex causa positiva, sed ex defectu causae ad aliud cogitandum determinantis oritur.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_QGOH_PT_5:P0001","trace_type":"Negative","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"},{"id":"RAW_QGOH_PT_5:P0020","trace_type":"Negative","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_QGOH_PT_5:NX2","text":"Amor non est voluntas libera amantis sese rei amatæ jungendi, nec Cupiditas absentem rem assequendi aut praesentem retinendi.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_QGOH_PT_5:P0008","trace_type":"Negative","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"},{"id":"RAW_QGOH_PT_5:P0009","trace_type":"Negative","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_QGOH_PT_5:NX3","text":"Spes sine Metu, nec Metus sine Spe, dari potest, quia dubitatio utriusque rei imaginem et exclusionem implicat.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_QGOH_PT_5:P0026","trace_type":"Negative","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"},{"id":"RAW_QGOH_PT_5:P0028","trace_type":"Negative","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"},{"id":"RAW_QGOH_PT_5:P0030","trace_type":"Negative","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_QGOH_PT_5:NX4","text":"Sublata dubitandi causa non sequitur certitudo rei singularis, sed tantum status de re non dubitandi.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_QGOH_PT_5:P0036","trace_type":"Direct","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_QGOH_PT_5:NX5","text":"Pœnitentia non probat decretum Mentis liberum esse, sed ideam facti, quod homo se ex libero decreto fecisse credit, affective efficacem esse.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_QGOH_PT_5:P0070","trace_type":"Composite","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"},{"id":"RAW_QGOH_PT_5:P0072","trace_type":"Composite","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"},{"id":"RAW_QGOH_PT_5:P0074","trace_type":"Composite","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_QGOH_PT_5:NX6","text":"Impudentia affectus non est, quamvis usu vulgari Verecundiæ opponatur.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_QGOH_PT_5:P0089","trace_type":"Direct","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_QGOH_PT_5:NX7","text":"Clementia, Temperantia, Sobrietas et Castitas passiones non sunt, sed potentiae animi quibus passiones moderari possunt.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_QGOH_PT_5:P0109","trace_type":"Composite","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"},{"id":"RAW_QGOH_PT_5:P0137","trace_type":"Composite","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"},{"id":"RAW_QGOH_PT_5:P0138","trace_type":"Composite","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_QGOH_PT_5:NX8","text":"Contrarietas affectuum non ex actibus externis vel abstinentia determinatur, sed ex natura Appetitus et Amoris qui actus movent.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_QGOH_PT_5:P0137","trace_type":"Direct","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"},{"id":"RAW_QGOH_PT_5:P0138","trace_type":"Direct","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_QGOH_PT_5:NX9","text":"Fluctuationes compositae neque nomen proprium neque definitionem singularem necessario postulant.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_QGOH_PT_5:P0139","trace_type":"Direct","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]}],"original_name":"Apophatic Structure"},{"number":"4","name":"Derivative Propositions","items":[{"id":"CH_QGOH_PT_5:D1","text":"Amor et Odium non sunt affectiones simplices sui generis, sed Lætitia et Tristitia idea causae externae concomitatae.","refs":["CH_QGOH_PT_5:A4","CH_QGOH_PT_5:A6","CH_QGOH_PT_5:A7"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"From","passages":[{"id":"RAW_QGOH_PT_5:P0006","trace_type":"Composite","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"},{"id":"RAW_QGOH_PT_5:P0011","trace_type":"Composite","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"},{"id":"RAW_QGOH_PT_5:P0064","trace_type":"Composite","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_QGOH_PT_5:D2","text":"Spes et Metus sunt affectus inconstantes quorum una pars rei eventum affirmat, altera eiusdem rei existentiam imaginando secludit.","refs":["CH_QGOH_PT_5:A4","CH_QGOH_PT_5:A8"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"From","passages":[{"id":"RAW_QGOH_PT_5:P0026","trace_type":"Direct","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"},{"id":"RAW_QGOH_PT_5:P0028","trace_type":"Direct","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"},{"id":"RAW_QGOH_PT_5:P0030","trace_type":"Direct","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_QGOH_PT_5:D3","text":"Securitas et Desperatio ex Spe et Metu proveniunt, cum dubitationis causa tollitur, non cum cognitio certa eventus acquiritur.","refs":["CH_QGOH_PT_5:A4","CH_QGOH_PT_5:A8"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"From","passages":[{"id":"RAW_QGOH_PT_5:P0032","trace_type":"Composite","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"},{"id":"RAW_QGOH_PT_5:P0034","trace_type":"Composite","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"},{"id":"RAW_QGOH_PT_5:P0036","trace_type":"Composite","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_QGOH_PT_5:D4","text":"Desiderium, memoria rei jucundæ fultum et imaginibus rei absentiam affirmantibus coercitum, revera Tristitia est, sed ex usu ad Cupiditatem refertur.","refs":["CH_QGOH_PT_5:A4","CH_QGOH_PT_5:A5","CH_QGOH_PT_5:A8","CH_QGOH_PT_5:A12"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"From","passages":[{"id":"RAW_QGOH_PT_5:P0091","trace_type":"Direct","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"},{"id":"RAW_QGOH_PT_5:P0093","trace_type":"Direct","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_QGOH_PT_5:D5","text":"Favor, Indignatio, Invidia et Misericordia sunt variationes Amoris vel Odii, quas bonum aut malum alterius et imaginata similitudo ordinant.","refs":["CH_QGOH_PT_5:A6","CH_QGOH_PT_5:A7","CH_QGOH_PT_5:A9"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"From","passages":[{"id":"RAW_QGOH_PT_5:P0042","trace_type":"Composite","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"},{"id":"RAW_QGOH_PT_5:P0046","trace_type":"Composite","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"},{"id":"RAW_QGOH_PT_5:P0048","trace_type":"Composite","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"},{"id":"RAW_QGOH_PT_5:P0058","trace_type":"Composite","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"},{"id":"RAW_QGOH_PT_5:P0062","trace_type":"Composite","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_QGOH_PT_5:D6","text":"Gloria, Pudor et Pœnitentia non ex qualitate morali intrinseca actus oriuntur, sed ex imaginato judicio aliorum, autojudicio et affectiva institutione.","refs":["CH_QGOH_PT_5:A9","CH_QGOH_PT_5:A10","CH_QGOH_PT_5:A11"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"From","passages":[{"id":"RAW_QGOH_PT_5:P0072","trace_type":"Composite","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"},{"id":"RAW_QGOH_PT_5:P0074","trace_type":"Composite","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"},{"id":"RAW_QGOH_PT_5:P0085","trace_type":"Composite","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"},{"id":"RAW_QGOH_PT_5:P0087","trace_type":"Composite","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_QGOH_PT_5:D7","text":"Distinctio inter passionem et animi potentiam impedit ne moderatio Irae aut Vindictae pro affectu contrario habeatur.","refs":["CH_QGOH_PT_5:A1","CH_QGOH_PT_5:A12"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"From","passages":[{"id":"RAW_QGOH_PT_5:P0109","trace_type":"Composite","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"},{"id":"RAW_QGOH_PT_5:P0137","trace_type":"Composite","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"},{"id":"RAW_QGOH_PT_5:P0138","trace_type":"Composite","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_QGOH_PT_5:D8","text":"Multiplicatio nominum affectuum non multiplicat principia realia, quia denominationes ex relationibus causalibus et extrinsecis oriuntur.","refs":["CH_QGOH_PT_5:A6","CH_QGOH_PT_5:A7","CH_QGOH_PT_5:A12"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"From","passages":[{"id":"RAW_QGOH_PT_5:P0050","trace_type":"Direct","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"},{"id":"RAW_QGOH_PT_5:P0139","trace_type":"Direct","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]}],"original_name":"Derivative Propositions"},{"number":"5","name":"Super-Derivatives","items":[{"id":"CH_QGOH_PT_5:SD1","text":"Systema omnem pluralitatem affectivam ad triadem dynamicam reducit, cui adduntur variatio potentiae, idea causae, imaginatio temporalis et relatio socialis.","refs":["CH_QGOH_PT_5:A2","CH_QGOH_PT_5:A4","CH_QGOH_PT_5:A5","CH_QGOH_PT_5:A6","CH_QGOH_PT_5:A7","CH_QGOH_PT_5:A8","CH_QGOH_PT_5:A9"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"From","passages":[{"id":"RAW_QGOH_PT_5:P0064","trace_type":"Structural","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"},{"id":"RAW_QGOH_PT_5:P0139","trace_type":"Structural","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"},{"id":"RAW_QGOH_PT_5:P0141","trace_type":"Structural","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_QGOH_PT_5:SD2","text":"Affectus non primario naturam rei externae indicant, sed constitutionem actualem corporis et modum quo Mens illam per ideas confusas ordinat.","refs":["CH_QGOH_PT_5:A1","CH_QGOH_PT_5:A2","CH_QGOH_PT_5:A3"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"From","passages":[{"id":"RAW_QGOH_PT_5:P0143","trace_type":"Direct","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"},{"id":"RAW_QGOH_PT_5:P0144","trace_type":"Direct","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_QGOH_PT_5:SD3","text":"Tempus affectivum non dividitur stricte in praesens, praeteritum et futurum, quia memoria et exspectatio praesentem vim imaginis assumere possunt.","refs":["CH_QGOH_PT_5:A8"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"From","passages":[{"id":"RAW_QGOH_PT_5:P0036","trace_type":"Composite","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"},{"id":"RAW_QGOH_PT_5:P0093","trace_type":"Composite","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_QGOH_PT_5:SD4","text":"Iudicia recti, pravi, sacri, profani, honesti et turpis in campo affectuum apparent ut effectus institutionis et consuetudinis, non ut proprietates naturales actuum.","refs":["CH_QGOH_PT_5:A10","CH_QGOH_PT_5:A12"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"From","passages":[{"id":"RAW_QGOH_PT_5:P0050","trace_type":"Direct","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"},{"id":"RAW_QGOH_PT_5:P0074","trace_type":"Direct","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_QGOH_PT_5:SD5","text":"Subiectum affectivum non est auctor liber passionum suarum, sed locus in quo corporalis potentia, imaginatio et causae sociales in formas denominatas componuntur.","refs":["CH_QGOH_PT_5:A2","CH_QGOH_PT_5:A5","CH_QGOH_PT_5:A9","CH_QGOH_PT_5:A11"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"From","passages":[{"id":"RAW_QGOH_PT_5:P0070","trace_type":"Structural","confidence":"medium","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"},{"id":"RAW_QGOH_PT_5:P0095","trace_type":"Structural","confidence":"medium","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"},{"id":"RAW_QGOH_PT_5:P0141","trace_type":"Structural","confidence":"medium","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"},{"id":"RAW_QGOH_PT_5:P0143","trace_type":"Structural","confidence":"medium","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]}],"original_name":"Super-Derivatives"},{"number":"6","name":"Deep Apophatics","items":[{"id":"CH_QGOH_PT_5:DNX1","text":"Falsum esse oportet affectus nominatos esse naturas simplices et irreductibiles, alioquin reductio ad tres primitivos corrueret.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_QGOH_PT_5:P0002","trace_type":"Negative","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"},{"id":"RAW_QGOH_PT_5:P0139","trace_type":"Negative","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_QGOH_PT_5:DNX2","text":"Falsum esse oportet affectum cognitionem perspicuam causae externae praebere, cum eius idea primario constitutionem corporis indicet.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_QGOH_PT_5:P0143","trace_type":"Negative","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_QGOH_PT_5:DNX3","text":"Falsum esse oportet voluntatem liberam affectus ex nihilo instituere, cum ipsa deliberatio libera ad fictionem referatur.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_QGOH_PT_5:P0009","trace_type":"Negative","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"},{"id":"RAW_QGOH_PT_5:P0070","trace_type":"Negative","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"},{"id":"RAW_QGOH_PT_5:P0074","trace_type":"Negative","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_QGOH_PT_5:DNX4","text":"Falsum esse oportet actus per se laetos, tristes, honestos aut turpes esse, cum earum conjunctionum affectivarum causa in educatione et consuetudine ponatur.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_QGOH_PT_5:P0074","trace_type":"Negative","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_QGOH_PT_5:DNX5","text":"Falsum esse oportet taxonomiam affectuum exhauriri posse per vocabula recepta, cum plurima composita nomine careant et tantum in genere cognosci sufficiat.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_QGOH_PT_5:P0139","trace_type":"Negative","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]}],"original_name":"Deep Apophatics"},{"number":"7","name":"Super-Apophatics","items":[{"id":"CH_QGOH_PT_5:SNX1","text":"Carentia dubitationis affectivae non admittitur ut forma certitudinis de eventu rei singularis.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_QGOH_PT_5:P0036","trace_type":"Negative","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_QGOH_PT_5:SNX2","text":"Vocabularium morale et vulgare non admittitur ut tribunal ultimum naturae affectuum, quia rerum explicatio prior est usu sermonis.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_QGOH_PT_5:P0050","trace_type":"Negative","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"},{"id":"RAW_QGOH_PT_5:P0089","trace_type":"Negative","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_QGOH_PT_5:SNX3","text":"Affectus pure mentalis et a corporeae existentiae affirmatione separatus non admittitur, quia Mens in affectu de suo Corpore aliquid affirmat.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_QGOH_PT_5:P0141","trace_type":"Negative","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"},{"id":"RAW_QGOH_PT_5:P0144","trace_type":"Negative","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]}],"original_name":"Super-Apophatics"},{"number":"8","name":"Formal Generative Schema","items":[{"id":"CH_QGOH_PT_5:FGS1","text":"Constitutio Corporis mutata → idea confusa eius constitutionis → passio Mentis.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_QGOH_PT_5:P0141","trace_type":"Direct","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"},{"id":"RAW_QGOH_PT_5:P0143","trace_type":"Direct","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_QGOH_PT_5:FGS2","text":"Augmentum potentiae affirmatum → Lætitia; imminutio potentiae affirmata → Tristitia.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_QGOH_PT_5:P0144","trace_type":"Composite","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"},{"id":"RAW_QGOH_PT_5:P0145","trace_type":"Composite","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_QGOH_PT_5:FGS3","text":"Idea data quae Mentem ad hoc potius quam ad aliud fert → Cupiditas.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_QGOH_PT_5:P0141","trace_type":"Direct","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"},{"id":"RAW_QGOH_PT_5:P0145","trace_type":"Direct","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_QGOH_PT_5:FGS4","text":"Affectus primarius + idea causae externae, internae vel accidentariae → Amor, Odium, Acquiescentia, Humilitas, Propensio vel Aversio.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_QGOH_PT_5:P0006","trace_type":"Composite","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"},{"id":"RAW_QGOH_PT_5:P0011","trace_type":"Composite","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"},{"id":"RAW_QGOH_PT_5:P0015","trace_type":"Composite","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"},{"id":"RAW_QGOH_PT_5:P0017","trace_type":"Composite","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"},{"id":"RAW_QGOH_PT_5:P0066","trace_type":"Composite","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"},{"id":"RAW_QGOH_PT_5:P0068","trace_type":"Composite","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_QGOH_PT_5:FGS5","text":"Affectus primarius + imago rei praeteritae vel futurae + dubitatio aut exclusio imaginata → Spes, Metus, Securitas, Desperatio, Gaudium, Conscientiæ morsus vel Desiderium.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_QGOH_PT_5:P0026","trace_type":"Composite","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"},{"id":"RAW_QGOH_PT_5:P0028","trace_type":"Composite","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"},{"id":"RAW_QGOH_PT_5:P0032","trace_type":"Composite","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"},{"id":"RAW_QGOH_PT_5:P0034","trace_type":"Composite","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"},{"id":"RAW_QGOH_PT_5:P0038","trace_type":"Composite","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"},{"id":"RAW_QGOH_PT_5:P0040","trace_type":"Composite","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"},{"id":"RAW_QGOH_PT_5:P0091","trace_type":"Composite","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_QGOH_PT_5:FGS6","text":"Affectus primarius + imago alterius, similitudinis, laudis, vituperii, beneficii aut damni → affectus sociales, imitativi et comparativi.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_QGOH_PT_5:P0042","trace_type":"Composite","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"},{"id":"RAW_QGOH_PT_5:P0046","trace_type":"Composite","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"},{"id":"RAW_QGOH_PT_5:P0048","trace_type":"Composite","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"},{"id":"RAW_QGOH_PT_5:P0058","trace_type":"Composite","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"},{"id":"RAW_QGOH_PT_5:P0085","trace_type":"Composite","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"},{"id":"RAW_QGOH_PT_5:P0087","trace_type":"Composite","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"},{"id":"RAW_QGOH_PT_5:P0095","trace_type":"Composite","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_QGOH_PT_5:FGS7","text":"Affectus compositus + relatio extrinseca + nomen receptum → pluralitas affectuum denominata sine multiplicatione principiorum.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_QGOH_PT_5:P0002","trace_type":"Structural","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"},{"id":"RAW_QGOH_PT_5:P0050","trace_type":"Structural","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"},{"id":"RAW_QGOH_PT_5:P0139","trace_type":"Structural","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_QGOH_PT_5:FGS8","text":"Exclusio causae positivae, libertatis fictae, oppositionis mere nominalis et virtutis activae e classe passionum → fines systematis.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_QGOH_PT_5:P0001","trace_type":"Structural","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"},{"id":"RAW_QGOH_PT_5:P0009","trace_type":"Structural","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"},{"id":"RAW_QGOH_PT_5:P0109","trace_type":"Structural","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"},{"id":"RAW_QGOH_PT_5:P0137","trace_type":"Structural","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"},{"id":"RAW_QGOH_PT_5:P0138","trace_type":"Structural","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]}],"original_name":"Formal Generative Schema"},{"number":"9","name":"Argument Flow Map","items":[{"id":"CH_QGOH_PT_5:AF1","text":"Novitas rei → defectus transitus associativi → exclusio Admirationis e numero affectuum.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_QGOH_PT_5:P0001","trace_type":"Negative","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"},{"id":"RAW_QGOH_PT_5:P0020","trace_type":"Negative","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_QGOH_PT_5:AF2","text":"Exclusio falsi primitivi → positio Lætitiæ, Tristitiæ et Cupiditatis ut triadis fundamentalis.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_QGOH_PT_5:P0001","trace_type":"Composite","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"},{"id":"RAW_QGOH_PT_5:P0002","trace_type":"Composite","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"},{"id":"RAW_QGOH_PT_5:P0139","trace_type":"Composite","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_QGOH_PT_5:AF3","text":"Trias primitiva → idea causae externae, internae aut accidentariae → affectus causales denominati.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_QGOH_PT_5:P0006","trace_type":"Composite","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"},{"id":"RAW_QGOH_PT_5:P0011","trace_type":"Composite","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"},{"id":"RAW_QGOH_PT_5:P0015","trace_type":"Composite","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"},{"id":"RAW_QGOH_PT_5:P0017","trace_type":"Composite","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_QGOH_PT_5:AF4","text":"Affectus causales → imaginatio temporalis et dubitatio → affectus inconstantes, securi aut desperati.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_QGOH_PT_5:P0026","trace_type":"Composite","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"},{"id":"RAW_QGOH_PT_5:P0028","trace_type":"Composite","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"},{"id":"RAW_QGOH_PT_5:P0030","trace_type":"Composite","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"},{"id":"RAW_QGOH_PT_5:P0036","trace_type":"Composite","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_QGOH_PT_5:AF5","text":"Imaginatio aliorum et institutio moralis → affectus sociales, comparativi et reflexivi.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_QGOH_PT_5:P0042","trace_type":"Composite","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"},{"id":"RAW_QGOH_PT_5:P0058","trace_type":"Composite","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"},{"id":"RAW_QGOH_PT_5:P0074","trace_type":"Composite","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"},{"id":"RAW_QGOH_PT_5:P0085","trace_type":"Composite","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"},{"id":"RAW_QGOH_PT_5:P0087","trace_type":"Composite","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_QGOH_PT_5:AF6","text":"Correctio vocabulorum et exclusionum → imminutio specierum apparentium.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_QGOH_PT_5:P0050","trace_type":"Structural","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"},{"id":"RAW_QGOH_PT_5:P0089","trace_type":"Structural","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"},{"id":"RAW_QGOH_PT_5:P0139","trace_type":"Structural","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_QGOH_PT_5:AF7","text":"Taxonomia derivata → definitio generalis passionis ut idea confusa mutationis potentiae corporis.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_QGOH_PT_5:P0139","trace_type":"Direct","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"},{"id":"RAW_QGOH_PT_5:P0140","trace_type":"Direct","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"},{"id":"RAW_QGOH_PT_5:P0141","trace_type":"Direct","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]}],"original_name":"Argument Flow Map"},{"number":"10","name":"Map-Effects","items":[{"id":"CH_QGOH_PT_5:ME1","text":"Affectus nominati reclassificantur ut modi relationales trium motuum, non ut inventarium naturarum psychologicarum independentium.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_QGOH_PT_5:P0002","trace_type":"Direct","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"},{"id":"RAW_QGOH_PT_5:P0139","trace_type":"Direct","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_QGOH_PT_5:ME2","text":"Distinctio fit inter causam affectus, ideam causae concomitantem et constitutionem corporis quam idea affectiva immediate exprimit.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_QGOH_PT_5:P0064","trace_type":"Composite","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"},{"id":"RAW_QGOH_PT_5:P0143","trace_type":"Composite","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_QGOH_PT_5:ME3","text":"Oppositio inter praesens et non-praesens dissolvitur in campo affectivo, quia praeteritum et futurum per imaginationem vim praesentiae obtinere possunt.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_QGOH_PT_5:P0036","trace_type":"Direct","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"},{"id":"RAW_QGOH_PT_5:P0093","trace_type":"Direct","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_QGOH_PT_5:ME4","text":"Iudicia moralia reponuntur ex ordine proprietatum actuum in ordine affectuum per educationem, consuetudinem et religionem compositorum.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_QGOH_PT_5:P0074","trace_type":"Direct","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_QGOH_PT_5:ME5","text":"Virtutes moderatrices distinguuntur ab affectibus passivis, ita ut contrarietas moralis non confundatur cum contrarietate affectiva.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_QGOH_PT_5:P0109","trace_type":"Direct","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"},{"id":"RAW_QGOH_PT_5:P0137","trace_type":"Direct","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"},{"id":"RAW_QGOH_PT_5:P0138","trace_type":"Direct","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]}],"original_name":"Map-Effects"},{"number":"11","name":"Altitude Levels","items":[{"id":"CH_QGOH_PT_5:ALT1","text":"Surface — Textus definit seriem nominum affectivorum et eorum relationes immediatas ad Lætitiam, Tristitiam, Cupiditatem, causas et objecta.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_QGOH_PT_5:P0006","trace_type":"Structural","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"},{"id":"RAW_QGOH_PT_5:P0011","trace_type":"Structural","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"},{"id":"RAW_QGOH_PT_5:P0026","trace_type":"Structural","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"},{"id":"RAW_QGOH_PT_5:P0042","trace_type":"Structural","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"},{"id":"RAW_QGOH_PT_5:P0091","trace_type":"Structural","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_QGOH_PT_5:ALT2","text":"Structural — Affectus passivus explicatur per ideam confusam constitutionis corporis in qua potentia existendi et agendi augetur, minuitur, juvatur aut coercetur.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_QGOH_PT_5:P0141","trace_type":"Direct","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"},{"id":"RAW_QGOH_PT_5:P0143","trace_type":"Direct","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_QGOH_PT_5:ALT3","text":"Metastrategic — Argumentatio vocabula vulgariter recepta servat, sed eorum auctoritatem explicativam submittit reductioni rerum ad causas et relationes generativas.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_QGOH_PT_5:P0050","trace_type":"Direct","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"},{"id":"RAW_QGOH_PT_5:P0089","trace_type":"Direct","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_QGOH_PT_5:ALT4","text":"Ontological/Temporal — Mens actualem corporis existentiam affirmat, dum tempora affectiva per imaginationem ordinantur nec dubitationis absentia in certitudinem rei singularis transit.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_QGOH_PT_5:P0036","trace_type":"Composite","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"},{"id":"RAW_QGOH_PT_5:P0144","trace_type":"Composite","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]}],"original_name":"Altitude Levels"},{"number":"12","name":"Minimal Reconstruction Test","items":[{"id":"CH_QGOH_PT_5:MRT1","text":"Nucleus trium affectuum, ideae confusae et mutationis potentiae corporis sufficit ad legem generalem reductionis restituendam.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_QGOH_PT_5:P0139","trace_type":"Structural","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"},{"id":"RAW_QGOH_PT_5:P0141","trace_type":"Structural","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"},{"id":"RAW_QGOH_PT_5:P0143","trace_type":"Structural","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"},{"id":"RAW_QGOH_PT_5:P0145","trace_type":"Structural","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_QGOH_PT_5:MRT2","text":"Distinctiones causae, temporis, imaginationis socialis et institutionis sufficiunt ad familias principales definitionum derivatarum regenerandas.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_QGOH_PT_5:P0006","trace_type":"Structural","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"},{"id":"RAW_QGOH_PT_5:P0026","trace_type":"Structural","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"},{"id":"RAW_QGOH_PT_5:P0042","trace_type":"Structural","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"},{"id":"RAW_QGOH_PT_5:P0074","trace_type":"Structural","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"},{"id":"RAW_QGOH_PT_5:P0095","trace_type":"Structural","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_QGOH_PT_5:MRT3","text":"Derivatio singularum definitionum localium non est omnino autonoma, quia plures gradus ad propositiones et scholia anteriora remittuntur nec hic iterum demonstrantur.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_QGOH_PT_5:P0013","trace_type":"Direct","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"},{"id":"RAW_QGOH_PT_5:P0020","trace_type":"Direct","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"},{"id":"RAW_QGOH_PT_5:P0024","trace_type":"Direct","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"},{"id":"RAW_QGOH_PT_5:P0074","trace_type":"Direct","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"},{"id":"RAW_QGOH_PT_5:P0097","trace_type":"Direct","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_QGOH_PT_5:MRT4","text":"Commissura fragilis manet inter definitionem generalem corpoream et species sociales vel morales, quarum reductio ad variationem potentiae in hoc loco potius praesupponitur quam explicite perficitur.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_QGOH_PT_5:P0074","trace_type":"Tension","confidence":"medium","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"},{"id":"RAW_QGOH_PT_5:P0143","trace_type":"Tension","confidence":"medium","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_QGOH_PT_5:MRT5","text":"Tensio semantica manet inter principium naturae rerum et retentionem vocabulorum usu vulgari formatorum, quam textus pragmatico usu, non regula semantica exhaustiva, componit.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_QGOH_PT_5:P0050","trace_type":"Tension","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"},{"id":"RAW_QGOH_PT_5:P0089","trace_type":"Tension","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_QGOH_PT_5:MRT6","text":"Fictio decreti liberi et affectus Pœnitentiæ cohaerent tantum si credulitas libertatis ut causa imaginata efficax accipitur; nexus iste hic affirmatur, sed non plene re-deducitur.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_QGOH_PT_5:P0009","trace_type":"Tension","confidence":"medium","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"},{"id":"RAW_QGOH_PT_5:P0070","trace_type":"Tension","confidence":"medium","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"},{"id":"RAW_QGOH_PT_5:P0072","trace_type":"Tension","confidence":"medium","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"},{"id":"RAW_QGOH_PT_5:P0074","trace_type":"Tension","confidence":"medium","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_QGOH_PT_5:MRT7","text":"Restitutio motum verum servat, quia ab exclusione apparentium primitivorum procedit ad triadem, ab ea ad denominationes, et denique ad formam generalem passionis.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_QGOH_PT_5:P0001","trace_type":"Structural","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"},{"id":"RAW_QGOH_PT_5:P0002","trace_type":"Structural","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"},{"id":"RAW_QGOH_PT_5:P0139","trace_type":"Structural","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"},{"id":"RAW_QGOH_PT_5:P0141","trace_type":"Structural","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]}],"original_name":"Minimal Reconstruction Test"}],"language_code":"la","language_name":"Latina","translations":[{"id":"CAREVAR_20261015T015323973529Z_b57e65b7","language_code":"en","language_name":"English","title":"Part Three - On the Origin and Nature of the Affects · passage 5","source_sha256":"f66502148a277cedde882ef7d375c2b55b5b046909d1f8ed8b331a5f6c36e6ed","sha256":"d22a0f8469a711a29977d0120c6c12d48d51689964a162737d48953f6e26dcf9","rendition_type":"machine","review_status":"machine","model":"gpt-5.6-terra","created":"2026-10-15T01:53:23.983963+00:00","sections":[{"number":"1","name":"Argumental Spine","items":[{"id":"CH_QGOH_PT_5:AS1","text":"The text asks whether the multiplicity of names of affects signifies a multiplicity of primary natures, or various denominations of a few fundamental motions.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_QGOH_PT_5:P0002","trace_type":"Composite","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"},{"id":"RAW_QGOH_PT_5:P0139","trace_type":"Composite","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_QGOH_PT_5:AS2","text":"The text establishes that there are only three primitive affects, Joy, Sadness, and Desire, from which the rest arise through relations, imagined causes, and compositions.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_QGOH_PT_5:P0001","trace_type":"Direct","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"},{"id":"RAW_QGOH_PT_5:P0002","trace_type":"Direct","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"},{"id":"RAW_QGOH_PT_5:P0139","trace_type":"Direct","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_QGOH_PT_5:AS3","text":"The argumentation is reconstructive and diagnostic: it removes false species of affects, free will, and nominal opposites, in order to establish a causally generative order.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_QGOH_PT_5:P0001","trace_type":"Structural","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"},{"id":"RAW_QGOH_PT_5:P0008","trace_type":"Structural","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"},{"id":"RAW_QGOH_PT_5:P0009","trace_type":"Structural","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"},{"id":"RAW_QGOH_PT_5:P0050","trace_type":"Structural","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"},{"id":"RAW_QGOH_PT_5:P0074","trace_type":"Structural","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"},{"id":"RAW_QGOH_PT_5:P0089","trace_type":"Structural","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"},{"id":"RAW_QGOH_PT_5:P0137","trace_type":"Structural","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"},{"id":"RAW_QGOH_PT_5:P0139","trace_type":"Structural","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_QGOH_PT_5:AS4","text":"The definition of a particular affect is composed through a primary genus, the idea of a cause, a temporal mode, a social relation, or an imagined estimation.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_QGOH_PT_5:P0006","trace_type":"Composite","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"},{"id":"RAW_QGOH_PT_5:P0026","trace_type":"Composite","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"},{"id":"RAW_QGOH_PT_5:P0042","trace_type":"Composite","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"},{"id":"RAW_QGOH_PT_5:P0085","trace_type":"Composite","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"},{"id":"RAW_QGOH_PT_5:P0095","trace_type":"Composite","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"},{"id":"RAW_QGOH_PT_5:P0141","trace_type":"Composite","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_QGOH_PT_5:AS5","text":"The general conclusion reduces a passive affect to a confused idea by which the Mind affirms a change in the body's power and is determined to think in a certain way.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_QGOH_PT_5:P0141","trace_type":"Direct","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"},{"id":"RAW_QGOH_PT_5:P0143","trace_type":"Direct","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"},{"id":"RAW_QGOH_PT_5:P0145","trace_type":"Direct","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]}],"original_name":"Argumental Spine"},{"number":"2","name":"Load-Bearing Axioms","items":[{"id":"CH_QGOH_PT_5:A1","text":"The Mind suffers insofar as it has inadequate or confused ideas.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_QGOH_PT_5:P0143","trace_type":"Direct","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_QGOH_PT_5:A2","text":"An affect of the mind, insofar as it is a passion, is a confused idea by which the Mind affirms a greater or lesser force of existence of its Body.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_QGOH_PT_5:P0141","trace_type":"Direct","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"},{"id":"RAW_QGOH_PT_5:P0143","trace_type":"Direct","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_QGOH_PT_5:A3","text":"The essence of the Mind consists in the affirmation of the actual existence of its Body, whose greater or lesser reality constitutes a transition to greater or lesser perfection.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_QGOH_PT_5:P0144","trace_type":"Direct","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_QGOH_PT_5:A4","text":"Joy and Sadness are transitions of the Mind to greater or lesser perfection, or power of acting.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_QGOH_PT_5:P0144","trace_type":"Composite","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"},{"id":"RAW_QGOH_PT_5:P0145","trace_type":"Composite","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_QGOH_PT_5:A5","text":"Desire is a determination of the Mind by which, given an idea, it is borne to think or act upon this rather than that.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_QGOH_PT_5:P0141","trace_type":"Direct","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"},{"id":"RAW_QGOH_PT_5:P0145","trace_type":"Direct","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_QGOH_PT_5:A6","text":"Joy, Sadness, and Desire are the sole primitive forms of affects.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_QGOH_PT_5:P0002","trace_type":"Direct","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"},{"id":"RAW_QGOH_PT_5:P0139","trace_type":"Direct","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_QGOH_PT_5:A7","text":"The idea of an external, internal, or accidentally conjoined cause determines a primary affect into a denominated affect.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_QGOH_PT_5:P0006","trace_type":"Composite","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"},{"id":"RAW_QGOH_PT_5:P0011","trace_type":"Composite","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"},{"id":"RAW_QGOH_PT_5:P0015","trace_type":"Composite","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"},{"id":"RAW_QGOH_PT_5:P0017","trace_type":"Composite","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"},{"id":"RAW_QGOH_PT_5:P0064","trace_type":"Composite","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_QGOH_PT_5:A8","text":"The image of a past or future thing can affect the Mind with the same affect as the image of a present thing, and doubt about the outcome alters the mode of the affect.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_QGOH_PT_5:P0026","trace_type":"Composite","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"},{"id":"RAW_QGOH_PT_5:P0028","trace_type":"Composite","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"},{"id":"RAW_QGOH_PT_5:P0030","trace_type":"Composite","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"},{"id":"RAW_QGOH_PT_5:P0032","trace_type":"Composite","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"},{"id":"RAW_QGOH_PT_5:P0034","trace_type":"Composite","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"},{"id":"RAW_QGOH_PT_5:P0036","trace_type":"Composite","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"},{"id":"RAW_QGOH_PT_5:P0093","trace_type":"Composite","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_QGOH_PT_5:A9","text":"The imagination of another's likeness, desire, judgment, or action generates social affects.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_QGOH_PT_5:P0042","trace_type":"Composite","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"},{"id":"RAW_QGOH_PT_5:P0046","trace_type":"Composite","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"},{"id":"RAW_QGOH_PT_5:P0048","trace_type":"Composite","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"},{"id":"RAW_QGOH_PT_5:P0058","trace_type":"Composite","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"},{"id":"RAW_QGOH_PT_5:P0085","trace_type":"Composite","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"},{"id":"RAW_QGOH_PT_5:P0087","trace_type":"Composite","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"},{"id":"RAW_QGOH_PT_5:P0095","trace_type":"Composite","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"},{"id":"RAW_QGOH_PT_5:P0097","trace_type":"Composite","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_QGOH_PT_5:A10","text":"Education, custom, and religion join Joy or Sadness with acts and thereby constitute the affective force of moral judgments.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_QGOH_PT_5:P0074","trace_type":"Direct","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_QGOH_PT_5:A11","text":"A free decree of the Mind, or voluntary deliberation, is not a primary real cause of affects.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_QGOH_PT_5:P0009","trace_type":"Composite","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"},{"id":"RAW_QGOH_PT_5:P0070","trace_type":"Composite","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"},{"id":"RAW_QGOH_PT_5:P0072","trace_type":"Composite","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_QGOH_PT_5:A12","text":"The explanation of the nature of things prevails over the common use of words, although received terms may be retained to indicate things.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_QGOH_PT_5:P0050","trace_type":"Direct","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"},{"id":"RAW_QGOH_PT_5:P0089","trace_type":"Direct","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"},{"id":"RAW_QGOH_PT_5:P0139","trace_type":"Direct","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]}],"original_name":"Load-Bearing Axioms"},{"number":"3","name":"Apophatic Structure","items":[{"id":"CH_QGOH_PT_5:NX1","text":"Wonder is not counted among the affects, because the distraction of the Mind arising from novelty originates not from a positive cause but from the absence of a cause determining it to think of something else.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_QGOH_PT_5:P0001","trace_type":"Negative","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"},{"id":"RAW_QGOH_PT_5:P0020","trace_type":"Negative","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_QGOH_PT_5:NX2","text":"Love is not the free will of the lover to join himself to the beloved thing, nor is Desire the striving to attain an absent thing or retain a present one.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_QGOH_PT_5:P0008","trace_type":"Negative","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"},{"id":"RAW_QGOH_PT_5:P0009","trace_type":"Negative","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_QGOH_PT_5:NX3","text":"Hope cannot be given without Fear, nor Fear without Hope, because doubt implies both the image of each thing and its exclusion.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_QGOH_PT_5:P0026","trace_type":"Negative","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"},{"id":"RAW_QGOH_PT_5:P0028","trace_type":"Negative","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"},{"id":"RAW_QGOH_PT_5:P0030","trace_type":"Negative","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_QGOH_PT_5:NX4","text":"When the cause of doubting is removed, certainty of a particular thing does not follow, but only a state of not doubting concerning the thing.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_QGOH_PT_5:P0036","trace_type":"Direct","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_QGOH_PT_5:NX5","text":"Repentance does not prove that the decree of the Mind is free, but that the idea of a deed which a person believes himself to have done by free decree is affectively efficacious.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_QGOH_PT_5:P0070","trace_type":"Composite","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"},{"id":"RAW_QGOH_PT_5:P0072","trace_type":"Composite","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"},{"id":"RAW_QGOH_PT_5:P0074","trace_type":"Composite","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_QGOH_PT_5:NX6","text":"Shamelessness is not an affect, although in common usage it is opposed to Modesty.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_QGOH_PT_5:P0089","trace_type":"Direct","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_QGOH_PT_5:NX7","text":"Clemency, Temperance, Sobriety, and Chastity are not passions, but powers of the mind by which passions can be moderated.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_QGOH_PT_5:P0109","trace_type":"Composite","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"},{"id":"RAW_QGOH_PT_5:P0137","trace_type":"Composite","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"},{"id":"RAW_QGOH_PT_5:P0138","trace_type":"Composite","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_QGOH_PT_5:NX8","text":"The contrariety of affects is determined not by external acts or abstinence, but by the nature of the Appetite and Love that move the acts.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_QGOH_PT_5:P0137","trace_type":"Direct","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"},{"id":"RAW_QGOH_PT_5:P0138","trace_type":"Direct","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_QGOH_PT_5:NX9","text":"Composite fluctuations do not necessarily require either a proper name or a particular definition.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_QGOH_PT_5:P0139","trace_type":"Direct","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]}],"original_name":"Apophatic Structure"},{"number":"4","name":"Derivative Propositions","items":[{"id":"CH_QGOH_PT_5:D1","text":"Love and Hate are not simple affections of their own kind, but Joy and Sadness accompanied by the idea of an external cause.","refs":["CH_QGOH_PT_5:A4","CH_QGOH_PT_5:A6","CH_QGOH_PT_5:A7"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"From","passages":[{"id":"RAW_QGOH_PT_5:P0006","trace_type":"Composite","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"},{"id":"RAW_QGOH_PT_5:P0011","trace_type":"Composite","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"},{"id":"RAW_QGOH_PT_5:P0064","trace_type":"Composite","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_QGOH_PT_5:D2","text":"Hope and Fear are inconstant affects, one part of which affirms the outcome of a thing, while another, by imagining the existence of that same thing, excludes it.","refs":["CH_QGOH_PT_5:A4","CH_QGOH_PT_5:A8"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"From","passages":[{"id":"RAW_QGOH_PT_5:P0026","trace_type":"Direct","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"},{"id":"RAW_QGOH_PT_5:P0028","trace_type":"Direct","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"},{"id":"RAW_QGOH_PT_5:P0030","trace_type":"Direct","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_QGOH_PT_5:D3","text":"Security and Despair arise from Hope and Fear when the cause of doubt is removed, not when certain knowledge of the outcome is acquired.","refs":["CH_QGOH_PT_5:A4","CH_QGOH_PT_5:A8"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"From","passages":[{"id":"RAW_QGOH_PT_5:P0032","trace_type":"Composite","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"},{"id":"RAW_QGOH_PT_5:P0034","trace_type":"Composite","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"},{"id":"RAW_QGOH_PT_5:P0036","trace_type":"Composite","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_QGOH_PT_5:D4","text":"Longing, sustained by the memory of a pleasant thing and restrained by images affirming the thing's absence, is in truth Sadness, but by usage is referred to Desire.","refs":["CH_QGOH_PT_5:A4","CH_QGOH_PT_5:A5","CH_QGOH_PT_5:A8","CH_QGOH_PT_5:A12"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"From","passages":[{"id":"RAW_QGOH_PT_5:P0091","trace_type":"Direct","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"},{"id":"RAW_QGOH_PT_5:P0093","trace_type":"Direct","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_QGOH_PT_5:D5","text":"Favor, Indignation, Envy, and Pity are variations of Love or Hate, ordered by another's good or evil and imagined likeness.","refs":["CH_QGOH_PT_5:A6","CH_QGOH_PT_5:A7","CH_QGOH_PT_5:A9"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"From","passages":[{"id":"RAW_QGOH_PT_5:P0042","trace_type":"Composite","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"},{"id":"RAW_QGOH_PT_5:P0046","trace_type":"Composite","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"},{"id":"RAW_QGOH_PT_5:P0048","trace_type":"Composite","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"},{"id":"RAW_QGOH_PT_5:P0058","trace_type":"Composite","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"},{"id":"RAW_QGOH_PT_5:P0062","trace_type":"Composite","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_QGOH_PT_5:D6","text":"Glory, Shame, and Repentance arise not from the intrinsic moral quality of an act, but from the imagined judgment of others, self-judgment, and affective institution.","refs":["CH_QGOH_PT_5:A9","CH_QGOH_PT_5:A10","CH_QGOH_PT_5:A11"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"From","passages":[{"id":"RAW_QGOH_PT_5:P0072","trace_type":"Composite","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"},{"id":"RAW_QGOH_PT_5:P0074","trace_type":"Composite","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"},{"id":"RAW_QGOH_PT_5:P0085","trace_type":"Composite","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"},{"id":"RAW_QGOH_PT_5:P0087","trace_type":"Composite","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_QGOH_PT_5:D7","text":"The distinction between passion and power of mind prevents the moderation of Anger or Vengeance from being regarded as a contrary affect.","refs":["CH_QGOH_PT_5:A1","CH_QGOH_PT_5:A12"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"From","passages":[{"id":"RAW_QGOH_PT_5:P0109","trace_type":"Composite","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"},{"id":"RAW_QGOH_PT_5:P0137","trace_type":"Composite","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"},{"id":"RAW_QGOH_PT_5:P0138","trace_type":"Composite","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_QGOH_PT_5:D8","text":"The multiplication of names of affects does not multiply real principles, because denominations arise from causal and extrinsic relations.","refs":["CH_QGOH_PT_5:A6","CH_QGOH_PT_5:A7","CH_QGOH_PT_5:A12"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"From","passages":[{"id":"RAW_QGOH_PT_5:P0050","trace_type":"Direct","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"},{"id":"RAW_QGOH_PT_5:P0139","trace_type":"Direct","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]}],"original_name":"Derivative Propositions"},{"number":"5","name":"Super-Derivatives","items":[{"id":"CH_QGOH_PT_5:SD1","text":"The system reduces all affective plurality to a dynamic triad, to which are added variation of power, the idea of cause, temporal imagination, and social relation.","refs":["CH_QGOH_PT_5:A2","CH_QGOH_PT_5:A4","CH_QGOH_PT_5:A5","CH_QGOH_PT_5:A6","CH_QGOH_PT_5:A7","CH_QGOH_PT_5:A8","CH_QGOH_PT_5:A9"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"From","passages":[{"id":"RAW_QGOH_PT_5:P0064","trace_type":"Structural","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"},{"id":"RAW_QGOH_PT_5:P0139","trace_type":"Structural","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"},{"id":"RAW_QGOH_PT_5:P0141","trace_type":"Structural","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_QGOH_PT_5:SD2","text":"Affects do not primarily indicate the nature of the external thing, but the actual constitution of the body and the manner in which the Mind orders it through confused ideas.","refs":["CH_QGOH_PT_5:A1","CH_QGOH_PT_5:A2","CH_QGOH_PT_5:A3"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"From","passages":[{"id":"RAW_QGOH_PT_5:P0143","trace_type":"Direct","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"},{"id":"RAW_QGOH_PT_5:P0144","trace_type":"Direct","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_QGOH_PT_5:SD3","text":"Affective time is not strictly divided into present, past, and future, because memory and expectation can assume the present force of an image.","refs":["CH_QGOH_PT_5:A8"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"From","passages":[{"id":"RAW_QGOH_PT_5:P0036","trace_type":"Composite","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"},{"id":"RAW_QGOH_PT_5:P0093","trace_type":"Composite","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_QGOH_PT_5:SD4","text":"Judgments of right, wrong, sacred, profane, honorable, and shameful appear in the field of affects as effects of institution and custom, not as natural properties of acts.","refs":["CH_QGOH_PT_5:A10","CH_QGOH_PT_5:A12"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"From","passages":[{"id":"RAW_QGOH_PT_5:P0050","trace_type":"Direct","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"},{"id":"RAW_QGOH_PT_5:P0074","trace_type":"Direct","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_QGOH_PT_5:SD5","text":"The affective subject is not the free author of its passions, but the locus in which bodily power, imagination, and social causes are composed into denominated forms.","refs":["CH_QGOH_PT_5:A2","CH_QGOH_PT_5:A5","CH_QGOH_PT_5:A9","CH_QGOH_PT_5:A11"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"From","passages":[{"id":"RAW_QGOH_PT_5:P0070","trace_type":"Structural","confidence":"medium","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"},{"id":"RAW_QGOH_PT_5:P0095","trace_type":"Structural","confidence":"medium","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"},{"id":"RAW_QGOH_PT_5:P0141","trace_type":"Structural","confidence":"medium","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"},{"id":"RAW_QGOH_PT_5:P0143","trace_type":"Structural","confidence":"medium","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]}],"original_name":"Super-Derivatives"},{"number":"6","name":"Deep Apophatics","items":[{"id":"CH_QGOH_PT_5:DNX1","text":"It must be false that named affects are simple and irreducible natures; otherwise the reduction to three primitives would collapse.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_QGOH_PT_5:P0002","trace_type":"Negative","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"},{"id":"RAW_QGOH_PT_5:P0139","trace_type":"Negative","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_QGOH_PT_5:DNX2","text":"It must be false that an affect provides clear knowledge of an external cause, since its idea primarily indicates the constitution of the body.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_QGOH_PT_5:P0143","trace_type":"Negative","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_QGOH_PT_5:DNX3","text":"It must be false that free will institutes affects from nothing, since free deliberation itself is referred to fiction.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_QGOH_PT_5:P0009","trace_type":"Negative","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"},{"id":"RAW_QGOH_PT_5:P0070","trace_type":"Negative","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"},{"id":"RAW_QGOH_PT_5:P0074","trace_type":"Negative","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_QGOH_PT_5:DNX4","text":"It must be false that acts are in themselves joyful, sad, honorable, or shameful, since the cause of those affective conjunctions is placed in education and custom.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_QGOH_PT_5:P0074","trace_type":"Negative","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_QGOH_PT_5:DNX5","text":"It must be false that a taxonomy of affects can be exhausted through received terms, since very many composites lack a name and it suffices for them to be known only in general.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_QGOH_PT_5:P0139","trace_type":"Negative","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]}],"original_name":"Deep Apophatics"},{"number":"7","name":"Super-Apophatics","items":[{"id":"CH_QGOH_PT_5:SNX1","text":"The absence of affective doubt is not admitted as a form of certainty concerning the outcome of a particular thing.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_QGOH_PT_5:P0036","trace_type":"Negative","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_QGOH_PT_5:SNX2","text":"Moral and common vocabulary is not admitted as the ultimate tribunal of the nature of affects, because the explanation of things is prior to linguistic usage.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_QGOH_PT_5:P0050","trace_type":"Negative","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"},{"id":"RAW_QGOH_PT_5:P0089","trace_type":"Negative","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_QGOH_PT_5:SNX3","text":"A purely mental affect separated from the affirmation of bodily existence is not admitted, because in an affect the Mind affirms something concerning its Body.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_QGOH_PT_5:P0141","trace_type":"Negative","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"},{"id":"RAW_QGOH_PT_5:P0144","trace_type":"Negative","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]}],"original_name":"Super-Apophatics"},{"number":"8","name":"Formal Generative Schema","items":[{"id":"CH_QGOH_PT_5:FGS1","text":"Changed constitution of the Body → confused idea of that constitution → passion of the Mind.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_QGOH_PT_5:P0141","trace_type":"Direct","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"},{"id":"RAW_QGOH_PT_5:P0143","trace_type":"Direct","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_QGOH_PT_5:FGS2","text":"Affirmed increase of power → Joy; affirmed diminution of power → Sadness.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_QGOH_PT_5:P0144","trace_type":"Composite","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"},{"id":"RAW_QGOH_PT_5:P0145","trace_type":"Composite","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_QGOH_PT_5:FGS3","text":"A given idea that bears the Mind to this rather than that → Desire.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_QGOH_PT_5:P0141","trace_type":"Direct","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"},{"id":"RAW_QGOH_PT_5:P0145","trace_type":"Direct","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_QGOH_PT_5:FGS4","text":"Primary affect + idea of an external, internal, or accidental cause → Love, Hate, Self-Contentment, Humility, Inclination, or Aversion.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_QGOH_PT_5:P0006","trace_type":"Composite","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"},{"id":"RAW_QGOH_PT_5:P0011","trace_type":"Composite","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"},{"id":"RAW_QGOH_PT_5:P0015","trace_type":"Composite","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"},{"id":"RAW_QGOH_PT_5:P0017","trace_type":"Composite","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"},{"id":"RAW_QGOH_PT_5:P0066","trace_type":"Composite","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"},{"id":"RAW_QGOH_PT_5:P0068","trace_type":"Composite","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_QGOH_PT_5:FGS5","text":"Primary affect + image of a past or future thing + doubt or imagined exclusion → Hope, Fear, Security, Despair, Joy, Remorse of Conscience, or Longing.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_QGOH_PT_5:P0026","trace_type":"Composite","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"},{"id":"RAW_QGOH_PT_5:P0028","trace_type":"Composite","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"},{"id":"RAW_QGOH_PT_5:P0032","trace_type":"Composite","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"},{"id":"RAW_QGOH_PT_5:P0034","trace_type":"Composite","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"},{"id":"RAW_QGOH_PT_5:P0038","trace_type":"Composite","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"},{"id":"RAW_QGOH_PT_5:P0040","trace_type":"Composite","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"},{"id":"RAW_QGOH_PT_5:P0091","trace_type":"Composite","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_QGOH_PT_5:FGS6","text":"Primary affect + image of another, of likeness, praise, blame, benefit, or harm → social, imitative, and comparative affects.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_QGOH_PT_5:P0042","trace_type":"Composite","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"},{"id":"RAW_QGOH_PT_5:P0046","trace_type":"Composite","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"},{"id":"RAW_QGOH_PT_5:P0048","trace_type":"Composite","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"},{"id":"RAW_QGOH_PT_5:P0058","trace_type":"Composite","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"},{"id":"RAW_QGOH_PT_5:P0085","trace_type":"Composite","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"},{"id":"RAW_QGOH_PT_5:P0087","trace_type":"Composite","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"},{"id":"RAW_QGOH_PT_5:P0095","trace_type":"Composite","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_QGOH_PT_5:FGS7","text":"Composite affect + extrinsic relation + received name → denominated plurality of affects without multiplication of principles.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_QGOH_PT_5:P0002","trace_type":"Structural","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"},{"id":"RAW_QGOH_PT_5:P0050","trace_type":"Structural","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"},{"id":"RAW_QGOH_PT_5:P0139","trace_type":"Structural","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_QGOH_PT_5:FGS8","text":"Exclusion from the class of passions of a positive cause, fictitious freedom, merely nominal opposition, and active virtue → boundaries of the system.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_QGOH_PT_5:P0001","trace_type":"Structural","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"},{"id":"RAW_QGOH_PT_5:P0009","trace_type":"Structural","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"},{"id":"RAW_QGOH_PT_5:P0109","trace_type":"Structural","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"},{"id":"RAW_QGOH_PT_5:P0137","trace_type":"Structural","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"},{"id":"RAW_QGOH_PT_5:P0138","trace_type":"Structural","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]}],"original_name":"Formal Generative Schema"},{"number":"9","name":"Argument Flow Map","items":[{"id":"CH_QGOH_PT_5:AF1","text":"Novelty of a thing → lack of associative transition → exclusion of Wonder from the number of affects.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_QGOH_PT_5:P0001","trace_type":"Negative","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"},{"id":"RAW_QGOH_PT_5:P0020","trace_type":"Negative","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_QGOH_PT_5:AF2","text":"Exclusion of the false primitive → positing of Joy, Sadness, and Desire as the fundamental triad.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_QGOH_PT_5:P0001","trace_type":"Composite","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"},{"id":"RAW_QGOH_PT_5:P0002","trace_type":"Composite","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"},{"id":"RAW_QGOH_PT_5:P0139","trace_type":"Composite","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_QGOH_PT_5:AF3","text":"Primitive triad → idea of an external, internal, or accidental cause → denominated causal affects.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_QGOH_PT_5:P0006","trace_type":"Composite","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"},{"id":"RAW_QGOH_PT_5:P0011","trace_type":"Composite","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"},{"id":"RAW_QGOH_PT_5:P0015","trace_type":"Composite","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"},{"id":"RAW_QGOH_PT_5:P0017","trace_type":"Composite","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_QGOH_PT_5:AF4","text":"Causal affects → temporal imagination and doubt → inconstant, secure, or despairing affects.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_QGOH_PT_5:P0026","trace_type":"Composite","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"},{"id":"RAW_QGOH_PT_5:P0028","trace_type":"Composite","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"},{"id":"RAW_QGOH_PT_5:P0030","trace_type":"Composite","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"},{"id":"RAW_QGOH_PT_5:P0036","trace_type":"Composite","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_QGOH_PT_5:AF5","text":"Imagination of others and moral institution → social, comparative, and reflexive affects.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_QGOH_PT_5:P0042","trace_type":"Composite","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"},{"id":"RAW_QGOH_PT_5:P0058","trace_type":"Composite","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"},{"id":"RAW_QGOH_PT_5:P0074","trace_type":"Composite","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"},{"id":"RAW_QGOH_PT_5:P0085","trace_type":"Composite","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"},{"id":"RAW_QGOH_PT_5:P0087","trace_type":"Composite","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_QGOH_PT_5:AF6","text":"Correction of terms and exclusions → reduction of apparent species.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_QGOH_PT_5:P0050","trace_type":"Structural","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"},{"id":"RAW_QGOH_PT_5:P0089","trace_type":"Structural","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"},{"id":"RAW_QGOH_PT_5:P0139","trace_type":"Structural","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_QGOH_PT_5:AF7","text":"Derived taxonomy → general definition of passion as a confused idea of a change in bodily power.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_QGOH_PT_5:P0139","trace_type":"Direct","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"},{"id":"RAW_QGOH_PT_5:P0140","trace_type":"Direct","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"},{"id":"RAW_QGOH_PT_5:P0141","trace_type":"Direct","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]}],"original_name":"Argument Flow Map"},{"number":"10","name":"Map-Effects","items":[{"id":"CH_QGOH_PT_5:ME1","text":"Named affects are reclassified as relational modes of three motions, not as an inventory of independent psychological natures.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_QGOH_PT_5:P0002","trace_type":"Direct","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"},{"id":"RAW_QGOH_PT_5:P0139","trace_type":"Direct","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_QGOH_PT_5:ME2","text":"A distinction is made among the cause of an affect, the accompanying idea of the cause, and the constitution of the body that the affective idea immediately expresses.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_QGOH_PT_5:P0064","trace_type":"Composite","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"},{"id":"RAW_QGOH_PT_5:P0143","trace_type":"Composite","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_QGOH_PT_5:ME3","text":"The opposition between present and non-present is dissolved in the affective field, because past and future can acquire the force of presence through imagination.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_QGOH_PT_5:P0036","trace_type":"Direct","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"},{"id":"RAW_QGOH_PT_5:P0093","trace_type":"Direct","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_QGOH_PT_5:ME4","text":"Moral judgments are relocated from the order of properties of acts to the order of affects composed through education, custom, and religion.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_QGOH_PT_5:P0074","trace_type":"Direct","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_QGOH_PT_5:ME5","text":"Moderating virtues are distinguished from passive affects, so that moral contrariety is not confused with affective contrariety.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_QGOH_PT_5:P0109","trace_type":"Direct","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"},{"id":"RAW_QGOH_PT_5:P0137","trace_type":"Direct","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"},{"id":"RAW_QGOH_PT_5:P0138","trace_type":"Direct","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]}],"original_name":"Map-Effects"},{"number":"11","name":"Altitude Levels","items":[{"id":"CH_QGOH_PT_5:ALT1","text":"Surface — The text defines a series of affective names and their immediate relations to Joy, Sadness, Desire, causes, and objects.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_QGOH_PT_5:P0006","trace_type":"Structural","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"},{"id":"RAW_QGOH_PT_5:P0011","trace_type":"Structural","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"},{"id":"RAW_QGOH_PT_5:P0026","trace_type":"Structural","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"},{"id":"RAW_QGOH_PT_5:P0042","trace_type":"Structural","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"},{"id":"RAW_QGOH_PT_5:P0091","trace_type":"Structural","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_QGOH_PT_5:ALT2","text":"Structural — A passive affect is explained through a confused idea of the constitution of the body, in which the power of existing and acting is increased, diminished, aided, or restrained.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_QGOH_PT_5:P0141","trace_type":"Direct","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"},{"id":"RAW_QGOH_PT_5:P0143","trace_type":"Direct","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_QGOH_PT_5:ALT3","text":"Metastrategic — The argumentation retains commonly received terms, but submits their explanatory authority to the reduction of things to causes and generative relations.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_QGOH_PT_5:P0050","trace_type":"Direct","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"},{"id":"RAW_QGOH_PT_5:P0089","trace_type":"Direct","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_QGOH_PT_5:ALT4","text":"Ontological/Temporal — The Mind affirms the actual existence of the body, while affective times are ordered through imagination and the absence of doubt does not pass into certainty concerning a particular thing.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_QGOH_PT_5:P0036","trace_type":"Composite","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"},{"id":"RAW_QGOH_PT_5:P0144","trace_type":"Composite","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]}],"original_name":"Altitude Levels"},{"number":"12","name":"Minimal Reconstruction Test","items":[{"id":"CH_QGOH_PT_5:MRT1","text":"The nucleus of the three affects, confused ideas, and change in bodily power suffices to restore the general law of reduction.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_QGOH_PT_5:P0139","trace_type":"Structural","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"},{"id":"RAW_QGOH_PT_5:P0141","trace_type":"Structural","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"},{"id":"RAW_QGOH_PT_5:P0143","trace_type":"Structural","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"},{"id":"RAW_QGOH_PT_5:P0145","trace_type":"Structural","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_QGOH_PT_5:MRT2","text":"The distinctions of cause, time, social imagination, and institution suffice to regenerate the principal families of derived definitions.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_QGOH_PT_5:P0006","trace_type":"Structural","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"},{"id":"RAW_QGOH_PT_5:P0026","trace_type":"Structural","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"},{"id":"RAW_QGOH_PT_5:P0042","trace_type":"Structural","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"},{"id":"RAW_QGOH_PT_5:P0074","trace_type":"Structural","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"},{"id":"RAW_QGOH_PT_5:P0095","trace_type":"Structural","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_QGOH_PT_5:MRT3","text":"The derivation of individual local definitions is not wholly autonomous, because several steps refer back to earlier propositions and scholia and are not demonstrated again here.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_QGOH_PT_5:P0013","trace_type":"Direct","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"},{"id":"RAW_QGOH_PT_5:P0020","trace_type":"Direct","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"},{"id":"RAW_QGOH_PT_5:P0024","trace_type":"Direct","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"},{"id":"RAW_QGOH_PT_5:P0074","trace_type":"Direct","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"},{"id":"RAW_QGOH_PT_5:P0097","trace_type":"Direct","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_QGOH_PT_5:MRT4","text":"A fragile joint remains between the general corporeal definition and the social or moral species, whose reduction to variation of power is here rather presupposed than explicitly carried out.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_QGOH_PT_5:P0074","trace_type":"Tension","confidence":"medium","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"},{"id":"RAW_QGOH_PT_5:P0143","trace_type":"Tension","confidence":"medium","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_QGOH_PT_5:MRT5","text":"A semantic tension remains between the principle of the nature of things and the retention of terms shaped by common usage, which the text resolves through pragmatic use rather than an exhaustive semantic rule.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_QGOH_PT_5:P0050","trace_type":"Tension","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"},{"id":"RAW_QGOH_PT_5:P0089","trace_type":"Tension","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_QGOH_PT_5:MRT6","text":"The fiction of free decree and the affect of Repentance cohere only if belief in freedom is understood as an efficacious imagined cause; this connection is affirmed here, but not fully re-deduced.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_QGOH_PT_5:P0009","trace_type":"Tension","confidence":"medium","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"},{"id":"RAW_QGOH_PT_5:P0070","trace_type":"Tension","confidence":"medium","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"},{"id":"RAW_QGOH_PT_5:P0072","trace_type":"Tension","confidence":"medium","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"},{"id":"RAW_QGOH_PT_5:P0074","trace_type":"Tension","confidence":"medium","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_QGOH_PT_5:MRT7","text":"The reconstruction preserves the true movement, because it proceeds from the exclusion of apparent primitives to the triad, from it to denominations, and finally to the general form of passion.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_QGOH_PT_5:P0001","trace_type":"Structural","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"},{"id":"RAW_QGOH_PT_5:P0002","trace_type":"Structural","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"},{"id":"RAW_QGOH_PT_5:P0139","trace_type":"Structural","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"},{"id":"RAW_QGOH_PT_5:P0141","trace_type":"Structural","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]}],"original_name":"Minimal Reconstruction Test"}],"revision":"CH_QGOH_PT_5@R001","source_variant_id":"canonical:CH_QGOH_PT_5:v1","source_language_code":"la"}],"identity":{"layer":"CH","kicker":"Chapter 13","title":"Part Three - On the Origin and Nature of the Affects · passage 5","context":"Chapter 13","original_title":"Pars tertia - De origine et natura affectuum · passage 5","original_context":"Chapter 13"},"original_title":"Pars tertia - De origine et natura affectuum · passage 5","title_language_code":"en"},{"id":"CH_QGOH_PQDS","layer":"CH","title":"Part Four - On Human Bondage, or the Powers of the Affects · passage 1","order":14,"parents":["RAW_QGOH_PQDS"],"revision":"CH_QGOH_PQDS@R001","sha256":"d56557aaf874e1d5384919538f18932c48978d8c1ec7a511370de9c17813ed18","sections":[{"number":"1","name":"Argumental Spine","items":[{"id":"CH_QGOH_PQDS:AS1","text":"Textus quaerit cur homo, etsi meliora cognoscere videatur, affectibus tamen regi atque deteriora sequi cogatur.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[]},{"id":"CH_QGOH_PQDS:AS2","text":"Argumentatio vocabula perfectionis, imperfectionis, boni et mali ex ordine naturæ removet et ad comparationes mentis atque usum humanum refert.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[]},{"id":"CH_QGOH_PQDS:AS3","text":"Homo constituitur pars finita Naturæ, cuius potentia causis externis infinito ordine superatur et quae se sola concipi non potest.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[]},{"id":"CH_QGOH_PQDS:AS4","text":"Affectus non error nudus, sed affirmatio positiva constitutionis corporis est; ideo veritas sola imaginationem eiusque vim non delet.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[]},{"id":"CH_QGOH_PQDS:AS5","text":"Vis passionis ex proportione causæ externæ ad potentiam hominis nascitur; hinc affectus actiones reliquas superare et pertinaciter manere potest.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[]},{"id":"CH_QGOH_PQDS:AS6","text":"Moderatio affectus non ex nuda cognitione, sed ex conflictu corporali-affectivo oritur, in quo affectus contrarius et fortior priorem excludit.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[]},{"id":"CH_QGOH_PQDS:AS7","text":"Gradus affectuum per praesentiam imaginatam, propinquitatem temporalem et modum quo existentia rei affirmatur vel secluditur ordinantur; vera cognitio boni et mali efficax fit tantum quatenus ipsa affectus est.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[]}],"original_name":"Argumental Spine"},{"number":"2","name":"Load-Bearing Axioms","items":[{"id":"CH_QGOH_PQDS:A1","text":"Deus sive Natura eadem necessitate agit qua existit, nec ullum finem externum in agendo habet.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[]},{"id":"CH_QGOH_PQDS:A2","text":"Causa finalis non est proprietas rerum, sed appetitus humanus ut principium actionis ab hominibus consideratus.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[]},{"id":"CH_QGOH_PQDS:A3","text":"Perfectio et imperfectio sunt modi cogitandi ex comparatione individuorum ad exemplaria universalia orti, non defectus reales Naturæ.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[]},{"id":"CH_QGOH_PQDS:A4","text":"Realitas et perfectio in genere idem significant; mutatio perfectionis intelligitur ut augmentum vel imminutio potentiæ agendi, non ut transitus essentiae in aliam essentiam.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[]},{"id":"CH_QGOH_PQDS:A5","text":"Bonum et malum nihil positivum rebus in se insunt, sed res secundum utilitatem vel impedimentum respectu humani exemplaris ordinant.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[]},{"id":"CH_QGOH_PQDS:A6","text":"Virtus hominis est ipsa eius essentia seu natura quatenus potentiam habet efficiendi ea quae ex solis ipsius legibus intelliguntur.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[]},{"id":"CH_QGOH_PQDS:A7","text":"Nulla res singularis ita potens est ut alia potentior dari non possit, a qua destrui aut superari possit.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[]},{"id":"CH_QGOH_PQDS:A8","text":"Homo patitur quatenus est pars Naturæ cuius effectuum tantum causa partialis est; propterea mutationes externas necessario patitur.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[]},{"id":"CH_QGOH_PQDS:A9","text":"Positivum quod idea falsa continet non tollitur praesentia veri quatenus verum est.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[]},{"id":"CH_QGOH_PQDS:A10","text":"Vis passionis determinatur potentia causæ externæ cum potentia hominis comparata, et hanc potentiam superare potest.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[]},{"id":"CH_QGOH_PQDS:A11","text":"Affectus coerceri aut tolli nequit nisi per affectum contrarium et fortiorem, qui existentiam prioris excludit.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[]},{"id":"CH_QGOH_PQDS:A12","text":"Cognitio boni et mali est affectus lætitiæ vel tristitiæ quatenus eius conscii sumus; ideo cognoscere affectum regere non est nisi cognitio ipsa vim affectivam obtineat.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[]}],"original_name":"Load-Bearing Axioms"},{"number":"3","name":"Apophatic Structure","items":[{"id":"CH_QGOH_PQDS:NX1","text":"Natura non agit propter finem, nec res naturales secundum scopum a Natura intentum perfectæ vel imperfectæ sunt.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[]},{"id":"CH_QGOH_PQDS:NX2","text":"Imperfectio non est privatio alicuius rei propriae nec peccatum Naturæ, sed nomen ex comparatione mentis ortum.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[]},{"id":"CH_QGOH_PQDS:NX3","text":"Bonum et malum non sunt qualitates positivas rerum in se spectatarum.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[]},{"id":"CH_QGOH_PQDS:NX4","text":"Diuturnitas existendi non constituit maiorem perfectionem rei singularis.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[]},{"id":"CH_QGOH_PQDS:NX5","text":"Homo sui iuris non est quatenus affectibus obnoxius est, nec a mutationibus externis segregari potest.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[]},{"id":"CH_QGOH_PQDS:NX6","text":"Verum, quatenus verum, imaginationem positivam nec affectum ex ea ortum per se non annihilat.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[]},{"id":"CH_QGOH_PQDS:NX7","text":"Affectus non vincitur contradictione logica, sed sola oppositione affectiva quae simul contraria et fortior est.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[]},{"id":"CH_QGOH_PQDS:NX8","text":"Distantia temporalis ultra terminum imaginationis distinctæ non servatur ut differentia affectiva proportionalis.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[]}],"original_name":"Apophatic Structure"},{"number":"4","name":"Derivative Propositions","items":[{"id":"CH_QGOH_PQDS:D1","text":"Exclusionem finalitatis sequitur iudicia de perfectione rerum naturalium indicare praeiudicium comparativum, non statum obiectivum Naturæ.","refs":["CH_QGOH_PQDS:A1","CH_QGOH_PQDS:A2","CH_QGOH_PQDS:A3"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"From","passages":[]},{"id":"CH_QGOH_PQDS:D2","text":"Vocabulum boni retineri potest, sed tantum ut index medii ad potentiam humanam seu ad exemplar humanæ naturæ augendam ordinati.","refs":["CH_QGOH_PQDS:A4","CH_QGOH_PQDS:A5","CH_QGOH_PQDS:A6"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"From","passages":[]},{"id":"CH_QGOH_PQDS:D3","text":"Servitus humana non est lapsus contingens, sed conditio necessaria entis finiti quod in ordine causarum externarum partialiter tantum agit.","refs":["CH_QGOH_PQDS:A7","CH_QGOH_PQDS:A8"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"From","passages":[]},{"id":"CH_QGOH_PQDS:D4","text":"Correctio erroris potest ignorantiam tollere, manente tamen imaginatione quae praesentem corporis constitutionem exprimit.","refs":["CH_QGOH_PQDS:A9"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"From","passages":[]},{"id":"CH_QGOH_PQDS:D5","text":"Affectus pertinax explicatur non ex defectu voluntatis, sed ex excessu causæ externæ supra potentiam hominis.","refs":["CH_QGOH_PQDS:A7","CH_QGOH_PQDS:A10"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"From","passages":[]},{"id":"CH_QGOH_PQDS:D6","text":"Omnis coërcitio affectiva requirit productionem corporis affectionis contrariæ et fortioris, cui respondet idea seu affectus contrarius in mente.","refs":["CH_QGOH_PQDS:A11"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"From","passages":[]},{"id":"CH_QGOH_PQDS:D7","text":"Affectus rei praesentis, rei temporaliter propinquæ, rei necessariæ, aut rei possibilis cuius existentia aliquo modo ponitur, ceteris paribus intenditur.","refs":["CH_QGOH_PQDS:A9","CH_QGOH_PQDS:A10"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"From","passages":[]},{"id":"CH_QGOH_PQDS:D8","text":"Cognitio boni et mali affectum regere potest solum si ipsa, ut lætitia vel tristitia conscia, vim maiorem et contrariam obtinet.","refs":["CH_QGOH_PQDS:A11","CH_QGOH_PQDS:A12"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"From","passages":[]}],"original_name":"Derivative Propositions"},{"number":"5","name":"Super-Derivatives","items":[{"id":"CH_QGOH_PQDS:SD1","text":"Ethica reconstituitur ut analysis immanens variationum potentiæ, non ut iudicium rerum ex fine transcendente aut defectu naturali.","refs":["CH_QGOH_PQDS:A1","CH_QGOH_PQDS:A3","CH_QGOH_PQDS:A4","CH_QGOH_PQDS:A5"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"From","passages":[]},{"id":"CH_QGOH_PQDS:SD2","text":"Virtus non opponitur causalitati Naturæ, sed designat gradum quo agere ex propria natura intelligi potest intra ordinem causarum qui hominem semper excedit.","refs":["CH_QGOH_PQDS:A6","CH_QGOH_PQDS:A7","CH_QGOH_PQDS:A8"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"From","passages":[]},{"id":"CH_QGOH_PQDS:SD3","text":"Veritas theoretica non habet imperium automaticum super vitam affectivam, quia verum privationem erroris tollit potius quam positivam corporis affectionem.","refs":["CH_QGOH_PQDS:A9","CH_QGOH_PQDS:A12"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"From","passages":[]},{"id":"CH_QGOH_PQDS:SD4","text":"Regimen affectuum describitur ut certamen potentiarum, in quo cognitio efficax esse debet causa affectiva et non mera repraesentatio vera.","refs":["CH_QGOH_PQDS:A10","CH_QGOH_PQDS:A11","CH_QGOH_PQDS:A12"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"From","passages":[]},{"id":"CH_QGOH_PQDS:SD5","text":"Tempus et modalitas non sunt hic tantum formae cognitionis, sed condiciones per quas imaginatio distributionem virium affectivarum componit.","refs":["CH_QGOH_PQDS:A9","CH_QGOH_PQDS:A10"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"From","passages":[]}],"original_name":"Super-Derivatives"},{"number":"6","name":"Deep Apophatics","items":[{"id":"CH_QGOH_PQDS:DNX1","text":"Falsum esse debet exemplar Naturæ quasi artificis qui fines concipit, exemplaria praestituit et defectus suos in rebus imperfectis relinquit.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[]},{"id":"CH_QGOH_PQDS:DNX2","text":"Falsum esse debet exemplar boni et mali ut proprietatum absolutarum quae rebus ante omnem relationem ad potentiam humanam insunt.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[]},{"id":"CH_QGOH_PQDS:DNX3","text":"Falsum esse debet exemplar mentis cuius cognitio vera corpus et imaginationem sine mediatione causali gubernat.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[]},{"id":"CH_QGOH_PQDS:DNX4","text":"Falsum esse debet exemplar individui per se sufficientis, cuius essentia omnium mutationum quas patitur causa adæquata est.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[]},{"id":"CH_QGOH_PQDS:DNX5","text":"Falsum esse debet exemplar passionis ut mera infirmitas voluntatis; passio est effectus determinatus causarum externarum cum potentia hominis comparatarum.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[]}],"original_name":"Deep Apophatics"},{"number":"7","name":"Super-Apophatics","items":[{"id":"CH_QGOH_PQDS:SNX1","text":"Argumentatio excludit possibilitatem ordinis normativi transcendentis in quo natura ipsa bonum, malum, perfectionem et finem obiective distribuat.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[]},{"id":"CH_QGOH_PQDS:SNX2","text":"Argumentatio excludit liberationem quae consisteret in absoluta exemptione hominis a causalitate externa et a passionibus.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[]},{"id":"CH_QGOH_PQDS:SNX3","text":"Argumentatio excludit rationem puram ut potentiam affectibus extrinsecam; ratio operari potest tantum cum in ordine affectuum vim obtinet.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[]},{"id":"CH_QGOH_PQDS:SNX4","text":"Argumentatio excludit praesentiam temporis ut mensuram neutram, quia praesentia imaginata et propinquitas temporalem vim affectivam constituunt.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[]}],"original_name":"Super-Apophatics"},{"number":"8","name":"Formal Generative Schema","items":[{"id":"CH_QGOH_PQDS:FGS1","text":"Necessitas Naturæ sine fine → causæ finales, perfectiones et defectus naturales ad modos cogitandi comparativos rediguntur.","refs":["CH_QGOH_PQDS:A1","CH_QGOH_PQDS:A2","CH_QGOH_PQDS:A3"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"From","passages":[]},{"id":"CH_QGOH_PQDS:FGS2","text":"Comparatio cum exemplari humano → bonum et malum functiones utilitatis et impedimenti fiunt, non entia moralia rerum.","refs":["CH_QGOH_PQDS:A4","CH_QGOH_PQDS:A5","CH_QGOH_PQDS:A6"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"From","passages":[]},{"id":"CH_QGOH_PQDS:FGS3","text":"Homo ut pars finita Naturæ → passio ut effectus cuius homo causa tantum partialis est → servitus necessaria.","refs":["CH_QGOH_PQDS:A7","CH_QGOH_PQDS:A8"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"From","passages":[]},{"id":"CH_QGOH_PQDS:FGS4","text":"Imaginatio ut affirmatio constitutionis corporis → veritas ignorantiam corrigit, sed positivam imaginationem non exstinguit.","refs":["CH_QGOH_PQDS:A9"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"From","passages":[]},{"id":"CH_QGOH_PQDS:FGS5","text":"Causa externa comparata cum potentia hominis + praesentia, propinquitas et affirmatio existentiæ imaginatæ → gradus virium affectivarum.","refs":["CH_QGOH_PQDS:A10"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"From","passages":[]},{"id":"CH_QGOH_PQDS:FGS6","text":"Affectus superans → affectus contrarius fortior → coërcitio vel sublatio prioris affectus.","refs":["CH_QGOH_PQDS:A11"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"From","passages":[]},{"id":"CH_QGOH_PQDS:FGS7","text":"Cognitio boni et mali → affectus conscius → efficacia solum si in conflictu affectuum vim contrariam maiorem habet.","refs":["CH_QGOH_PQDS:A12","CH_QGOH_PQDS:A11"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"From","passages":[]}],"original_name":"Formal Generative Schema"},{"number":"9","name":"Argument Flow Map","items":[{"id":"CH_QGOH_PQDS:AF1","text":"Praeiudicium finalitatis et defectus Naturæ dissolvitur in comparationes, exemplaria et appetitus humanos.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[]},{"id":"CH_QGOH_PQDS:AF2","text":"→ Bonum, malum, perfectio et virtus reordinantur secundum utilitatem et potentiam agendi.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[]},{"id":"CH_QGOH_PQDS:AF3","text":"→ Homo ut pars finita et causaliter partialis ponitur, unde obnoxietas passionibus necessario sequitur.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[]},{"id":"CH_QGOH_PQDS:AF4","text":"→ Passionum vis ad causas externas et ad imaginationes corporis refertur, non ad falsitatem simplicem aut ad liberum arbitrium.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[]},{"id":"CH_QGOH_PQDS:AF5","text":"→ Praesentia, tempus, necessitas, possibilitas et contingentia distribuunt intensitates affectuum.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[]},{"id":"CH_QGOH_PQDS:AF6","text":"→ Cognitio vera subordinatur legi conflictus affectivi: efficax est tantum quatenus affectus fortior et contrarius efficitur.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[]}],"original_name":"Argument Flow Map"},{"number":"10","name":"Map-Effects","items":[{"id":"CH_QGOH_PQDS:ME1","text":"Defectus Naturæ fit invisibilis ut proprietas rerum et visibilis ut effectus comparationis humanae ad exemplar conceptum.","refs":["CH_QGOH_PQDS:A1","CH_QGOH_PQDS:A3"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"From","passages":[]},{"id":"CH_QGOH_PQDS:ME2","text":"Bonum et malum reclassificantur ex praedicatis moralibus absolutis in relationes practicas utilitatis, impedimenti, lætitiæ et tristitiæ.","refs":["CH_QGOH_PQDS:A5","CH_QGOH_PQDS:A12"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"From","passages":[]},{"id":"CH_QGOH_PQDS:ME3","text":"Virtus reclassificatur ex merito morali in potentiam immanentem agendi quae ex natura hominis intelligi potest.","refs":["CH_QGOH_PQDS:A4","CH_QGOH_PQDS:A6"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"From","passages":[]},{"id":"CH_QGOH_PQDS:ME4","text":"Oppositio veri et falsi desinit esse schema sufficiens ad affectus explicandos; locus causalitatis corporalis et certaminis affectivi aperitur.","refs":["CH_QGOH_PQDS:A9","CH_QGOH_PQDS:A11","CH_QGOH_PQDS:A12"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"From","passages":[]},{"id":"CH_QGOH_PQDS:ME5","text":"Tempus futurum, praeteritum et praesens, una cum necessitate, possibilitate et contingentia, fiunt coordinatae intensitatis affectivae.","refs":["CH_QGOH_PQDS:A10"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"From","passages":[]}],"original_name":"Map-Effects"},{"number":"11","name":"Altitude Levels","items":[{"id":"CH_QGOH_PQDS:ALT1","text":"Surface — Homo affectibus obnoxius meliora cognoscere potest et tamen deteriora sequi, quia passiones eius actiones superare possunt.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[]},{"id":"CH_QGOH_PQDS:ALT2","text":"Structural — Passio oritur ubi homo causa partialis est, et vis eius ex proportione potentiae externae ad potentiam hominis determinatur.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[]},{"id":"CH_QGOH_PQDS:ALT3","text":"Metastrategic — Regimen affectuum mutatur ex praecepto cognitionis rectae in quaestionem de productione affectus contrarii fortioris.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[]},{"id":"CH_QGOH_PQDS:ALT4","text":"Ontological/Temporal — Natura necessario agit sine fine, dum imaginatio praesentiam, distantiam temporalem et modum existentiae in differentias virium affectivarum convertit.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[]}],"original_name":"Altitude Levels"},{"number":"12","name":"Minimal Reconstruction Test","items":[{"id":"CH_QGOH_PQDS:MRT1","text":"Series axiomatum sufficit ad restitutionem motus principalis, quia transitus a critica finalitatis ad theoriam servitutis et ad legem conflictus affectivi explicatur.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[]},{"id":"CH_QGOH_PQDS:MRT2","text":"Nodus firmissimus est connexio inter finitudinem hominis, excessum causarum externarum et necessitatem passionum.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[]},{"id":"CH_QGOH_PQDS:MRT3","text":"Exemplar naturae humanae ad quod bonum et malum referuntur functionem practicam obtinet, sed eius determinatio materialis in hoc loco non deducitur.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[]},{"id":"CH_QGOH_PQDS:MRT4","text":"Formula boni ut id quod certo utile scitur criterium certitudinis praesupponit quod hic non constituitur, licet vis cognitionis affectivae stricte limitetur.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[]},{"id":"CH_QGOH_PQDS:MRT5","text":"Tensio interna manet inter dissolutionem perfectionis obiectivae et retentionem exempli humani normativi; textus eam per distinctionem inter naturam rerum et usum humanum continet.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[]},{"id":"CH_QGOH_PQDS:MRT6","text":"Lex qua affectus sola vi affectus contrari et fortioris coercetur stabilitur, sed modus quo cognitio vera talem vim obtineat ultra conditionem generalem affectus non explicatur.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[]},{"id":"CH_QGOH_PQDS:MRT7","text":"Restitutio servat motum textus, quia vocabula moralia non abolentur simpliciter, sed ab ontologia finalistica ad calculus immanentem potentiarum transferuntur.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[]}],"original_name":"Minimal Reconstruction Test"}],"language_code":"la","language_name":"Latina","translations":[{"id":"CAREVAR_20261015T014645195035Z_88c3bb3a","language_code":"en","language_name":"English","title":"Part Four — On Human Bondage, or the Powers of the Affects · passage 1","source_sha256":"d56557aaf874e1d5384919538f18932c48978d8c1ec7a511370de9c17813ed18","sha256":"57f50829fcc006d3a4781a10ccfbe2fd17235ffd05d45fca2735468ec6be86b1","rendition_type":"machine","review_status":"machine","model":"gpt-5.6-terra","created":"2026-10-15T01:46:45.196151+00:00","sections":[{"number":"1","name":"Argumental Spine","items":[{"id":"CH_QGOH_PQDS:AS1","text":"The text asks why a human being, although seeming to know what is better, is nevertheless compelled to be governed by affects and to pursue what is worse.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[]},{"id":"CH_QGOH_PQDS:AS2","text":"The argument removes the terms perfection, imperfection, good, and evil from the order of Nature and refers them to comparisons of the mind and to human use.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[]},{"id":"CH_QGOH_PQDS:AS3","text":"The human being is constituted as a finite part of Nature, whose power is surpassed in an infinite order by external causes and which cannot be conceived through itself alone.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[]},{"id":"CH_QGOH_PQDS:AS4","text":"An affect is not bare error, but a positive affirmation of the constitution of the body; therefore truth alone does not destroy the imagination and its force.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[]},{"id":"CH_QGOH_PQDS:AS5","text":"The force of a passion arises from the proportion of the external cause to human power; hence an affect can overcome the remaining actions and persist obstinately.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[]},{"id":"CH_QGOH_PQDS:AS6","text":"The restraint of an affect arises not from bare cognition, but from a bodily-affective conflict in which a contrary and stronger affect excludes the prior one.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[]},{"id":"CH_QGOH_PQDS:AS7","text":"The degrees of affects are ordered by imagined presence, temporal proximity, and the manner in which the existence of a thing is affirmed or excluded; true knowledge of good and evil becomes effective only insofar as it is itself an affect.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[]}],"original_name":"Argumental Spine"},{"number":"2","name":"Load-Bearing Axioms","items":[{"id":"CH_QGOH_PQDS:A1","text":"God, or Nature, acts by the same necessity by which it exists, and has no external end in acting.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[]},{"id":"CH_QGOH_PQDS:A2","text":"A final cause is not a property of things, but human appetite considered by human beings as a principle of action.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[]},{"id":"CH_QGOH_PQDS:A3","text":"Perfection and imperfection are modes of thinking arising from the comparison of individuals with universal models, not real defects of Nature.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[]},{"id":"CH_QGOH_PQDS:A4","text":"Reality and perfection signify the same thing in kind; a change of perfection is understood as an increase or diminution of the power of acting, not as the transition of an essence into another essence.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[]},{"id":"CH_QGOH_PQDS:A5","text":"Good and evil contain nothing positive in things themselves, but order things according to utility or hindrance in relation to the human model.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[]},{"id":"CH_QGOH_PQDS:A6","text":"Human virtue is its very essence or nature insofar as it has the power to effect those things which are understood from its laws alone.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[]},{"id":"CH_QGOH_PQDS:A7","text":"No singular thing is so powerful that another more powerful thing cannot be given by which it may be destroyed or overcome.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[]},{"id":"CH_QGOH_PQDS:A8","text":"A human being suffers insofar as it is a part of Nature of whose effects it is only a partial cause; therefore it necessarily undergoes external changes.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[]},{"id":"CH_QGOH_PQDS:A9","text":"The positive content contained by a false idea is not removed by the presence of the true insofar as it is true.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[]},{"id":"CH_QGOH_PQDS:A10","text":"The force of a passion is determined by the power of the external cause compared with human power, and it can surpass this power.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[]},{"id":"CH_QGOH_PQDS:A11","text":"An affect cannot be restrained or removed except by a contrary and stronger affect which excludes the existence of the prior one.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[]},{"id":"CH_QGOH_PQDS:A12","text":"Knowledge of good and evil is an affect of joy or sadness insofar as we are conscious of it; therefore knowing is not governing an affect unless knowledge itself possesses affective force.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[]}],"original_name":"Load-Bearing Axioms"},{"number":"3","name":"Apophatic Structure","items":[{"id":"CH_QGOH_PQDS:NX1","text":"Nature does not act for the sake of an end, nor are natural things perfect or imperfect according to an aim intended by Nature.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[]},{"id":"CH_QGOH_PQDS:NX2","text":"Imperfection is not the privation of any proper thing nor a sin of Nature, but a name arising from a comparison of the mind.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[]},{"id":"CH_QGOH_PQDS:NX3","text":"Good and evil are not positive qualities of things considered in themselves.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[]},{"id":"CH_QGOH_PQDS:NX4","text":"Duration of existence does not constitute a greater perfection of a singular thing.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[]},{"id":"CH_QGOH_PQDS:NX5","text":"A human being is not its own master insofar as it is subject to affects, nor can it be separated from external changes.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[]},{"id":"CH_QGOH_PQDS:NX6","text":"The true, insofar as it is true, does not by itself annihilate positive imagination or the affect arising from it.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[]},{"id":"CH_QGOH_PQDS:NX7","text":"An affect is not conquered by logical contradiction, but only by an affective opposition which is at once contrary and stronger.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[]},{"id":"CH_QGOH_PQDS:NX8","text":"Temporal distance beyond the limit of distinct imagination is not retained as a proportional affective difference.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[]}],"original_name":"Apophatic Structure"},{"number":"4","name":"Derivative Propositions","items":[{"id":"CH_QGOH_PQDS:D1","text":"From the exclusion of finality it follows that judgments concerning the perfection of natural things indicate a comparative prejudice, not an objective state of Nature.","refs":["CH_QGOH_PQDS:A1","CH_QGOH_PQDS:A2","CH_QGOH_PQDS:A3"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"From","passages":[]},{"id":"CH_QGOH_PQDS:D2","text":"The term good may be retained, but only as an index of a means ordered toward increasing human power or the model of human nature.","refs":["CH_QGOH_PQDS:A4","CH_QGOH_PQDS:A5","CH_QGOH_PQDS:A6"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"From","passages":[]},{"id":"CH_QGOH_PQDS:D3","text":"Human bondage is not a contingent fall, but the necessary condition of a finite being which acts only partially within the order of external causes.","refs":["CH_QGOH_PQDS:A7","CH_QGOH_PQDS:A8"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"From","passages":[]},{"id":"CH_QGOH_PQDS:D4","text":"The correction of error can remove ignorance, while the imagination that expresses the present constitution of the body nevertheless remains.","refs":["CH_QGOH_PQDS:A9"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"From","passages":[]},{"id":"CH_QGOH_PQDS:D5","text":"A persistent affect is explained not by a defect of the will, but by the excess of the external cause over human power.","refs":["CH_QGOH_PQDS:A7","CH_QGOH_PQDS:A10"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"From","passages":[]},{"id":"CH_QGOH_PQDS:D6","text":"Every affective restraint requires the production of a contrary and stronger affection of the body, to which a contrary idea or affect in the mind corresponds.","refs":["CH_QGOH_PQDS:A11"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"From","passages":[]},{"id":"CH_QGOH_PQDS:D7","text":"An affect toward a present thing, a temporally proximate thing, a necessary thing, or a possible thing whose existence is in some manner posited, is intensified, other things being equal.","refs":["CH_QGOH_PQDS:A9","CH_QGOH_PQDS:A10"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"From","passages":[]},{"id":"CH_QGOH_PQDS:D8","text":"Knowledge of good and evil can govern an affect only if it itself, as conscious joy or sadness, attains a greater and contrary force.","refs":["CH_QGOH_PQDS:A11","CH_QGOH_PQDS:A12"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"From","passages":[]}],"original_name":"Derivative Propositions"},{"number":"5","name":"Super-Derivatives","items":[{"id":"CH_QGOH_PQDS:SD1","text":"Ethics is reconstituted as an immanent analysis of variations in power, not as a judgment of things by a transcendent end or natural defect.","refs":["CH_QGOH_PQDS:A1","CH_QGOH_PQDS:A3","CH_QGOH_PQDS:A4","CH_QGOH_PQDS:A5"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"From","passages":[]},{"id":"CH_QGOH_PQDS:SD2","text":"Virtue is not opposed to the causality of Nature, but designates the degree to which acting from one's own nature can be understood within an order of causes that always exceeds the human being.","refs":["CH_QGOH_PQDS:A6","CH_QGOH_PQDS:A7","CH_QGOH_PQDS:A8"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"From","passages":[]},{"id":"CH_QGOH_PQDS:SD3","text":"Theoretical truth has no automatic dominion over affective life, because the true removes the privation of error rather than the positive affection of the body.","refs":["CH_QGOH_PQDS:A9","CH_QGOH_PQDS:A12"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"From","passages":[]},{"id":"CH_QGOH_PQDS:SD4","text":"The governance of affects is described as a contest of powers, in which knowledge must be an affective cause and not a mere true representation in order to be effective.","refs":["CH_QGOH_PQDS:A10","CH_QGOH_PQDS:A11","CH_QGOH_PQDS:A12"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"From","passages":[]},{"id":"CH_QGOH_PQDS:SD5","text":"Time and modality are here not merely forms of cognition, but conditions through which imagination composes the distribution of affective forces.","refs":["CH_QGOH_PQDS:A9","CH_QGOH_PQDS:A10"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"From","passages":[]}],"original_name":"Super-Derivatives"},{"number":"6","name":"Deep Apophatics","items":[{"id":"CH_QGOH_PQDS:DNX1","text":"The model of Nature as an artificer who conceives ends, pre-establishes models, and leaves its defects in imperfect things must be false.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[]},{"id":"CH_QGOH_PQDS:DNX2","text":"The model of good and evil as absolute properties inherent in things prior to every relation to human power must be false.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[]},{"id":"CH_QGOH_PQDS:DNX3","text":"The model of a mind whose true knowledge governs the body and imagination without causal mediation must be false.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[]},{"id":"CH_QGOH_PQDS:DNX4","text":"The model of a self-sufficient individual whose essence is the adequate cause of all the changes it undergoes must be false.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[]},{"id":"CH_QGOH_PQDS:DNX5","text":"The model of passion as mere weakness of will must be false; passion is a determined effect of external causes compared with human power.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[]}],"original_name":"Deep Apophatics"},{"number":"7","name":"Super-Apophatics","items":[{"id":"CH_QGOH_PQDS:SNX1","text":"The argument excludes the possibility of a transcendent normative order in which nature itself objectively distributes good, evil, perfection, and end.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[]},{"id":"CH_QGOH_PQDS:SNX2","text":"The argument excludes liberation consisting in the absolute exemption of the human being from external causality and passions.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[]},{"id":"CH_QGOH_PQDS:SNX3","text":"The argument excludes pure reason as a power extrinsic to affects; reason can operate only when it obtains force within the order of affects.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[]},{"id":"CH_QGOH_PQDS:SNX4","text":"The argument excludes the presence of time as a neutral measure, because imagined presence and temporal proximity constitute affective force.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[]}],"original_name":"Super-Apophatics"},{"number":"8","name":"Formal Generative Schema","items":[{"id":"CH_QGOH_PQDS:FGS1","text":"Necessity of Nature without end → final causes, perfections, and natural defects are reduced to comparative modes of thinking.","refs":["CH_QGOH_PQDS:A1","CH_QGOH_PQDS:A2","CH_QGOH_PQDS:A3"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"From","passages":[]},{"id":"CH_QGOH_PQDS:FGS2","text":"Comparison with the human model → good and evil become functions of utility and hindrance, not moral entities of things.","refs":["CH_QGOH_PQDS:A4","CH_QGOH_PQDS:A5","CH_QGOH_PQDS:A6"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"From","passages":[]},{"id":"CH_QGOH_PQDS:FGS3","text":"Human being as a finite part of Nature → passion as an effect of which the human being is only a partial cause → necessary bondage.","refs":["CH_QGOH_PQDS:A7","CH_QGOH_PQDS:A8"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"From","passages":[]},{"id":"CH_QGOH_PQDS:FGS4","text":"Imagination as affirmation of the constitution of the body → truth corrects ignorance, but does not extinguish positive imagination.","refs":["CH_QGOH_PQDS:A9"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"From","passages":[]},{"id":"CH_QGOH_PQDS:FGS5","text":"External cause compared with human power + presence, proximity, and affirmation of imagined existence → degrees of affective forces.","refs":["CH_QGOH_PQDS:A10"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"From","passages":[]},{"id":"CH_QGOH_PQDS:FGS6","text":"Overcoming affect → contrary stronger affect → restraint or removal of the prior affect.","refs":["CH_QGOH_PQDS:A11"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"From","passages":[]},{"id":"CH_QGOH_PQDS:FGS7","text":"Knowledge of good and evil → conscious affect → effectiveness only if, in the conflict of affects, it has a greater contrary force.","refs":["CH_QGOH_PQDS:A12","CH_QGOH_PQDS:A11"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"From","passages":[]}],"original_name":"Formal Generative Schema"},{"number":"9","name":"Argument Flow Map","items":[{"id":"CH_QGOH_PQDS:AF1","text":"The prejudice of finality and of defect in Nature is dissolved into comparisons, models, and human appetites.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[]},{"id":"CH_QGOH_PQDS:AF2","text":"→ Good, evil, perfection, and virtue are reordered according to utility and the power of acting.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[]},{"id":"CH_QGOH_PQDS:AF3","text":"→ The human being is posited as finite and causally partial, whence subjection to passions necessarily follows.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[]},{"id":"CH_QGOH_PQDS:AF4","text":"→ The force of passions is referred to external causes and to imaginations of the body, not to simple falsity or free will.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[]},{"id":"CH_QGOH_PQDS:AF5","text":"→ Presence, time, necessity, possibility, and contingency distribute the intensities of affects.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[]},{"id":"CH_QGOH_PQDS:AF6","text":"→ True knowledge is subordinated to the law of affective conflict: it is effective only insofar as it becomes a stronger and contrary affect.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[]}],"original_name":"Argument Flow Map"},{"number":"10","name":"Map-Effects","items":[{"id":"CH_QGOH_PQDS:ME1","text":"Defect in Nature becomes invisible as a property of things and visible as an effect of human comparison with a conceived model.","refs":["CH_QGOH_PQDS:A1","CH_QGOH_PQDS:A3"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"From","passages":[]},{"id":"CH_QGOH_PQDS:ME2","text":"Good and evil are reclassified from absolute moral predicates into practical relations of utility, hindrance, joy, and sadness.","refs":["CH_QGOH_PQDS:A5","CH_QGOH_PQDS:A12"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"From","passages":[]},{"id":"CH_QGOH_PQDS:ME3","text":"Virtue is reclassified from moral merit into an immanent power of acting that can be understood from human nature.","refs":["CH_QGOH_PQDS:A4","CH_QGOH_PQDS:A6"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"From","passages":[]},{"id":"CH_QGOH_PQDS:ME4","text":"The opposition of true and false ceases to be a sufficient schema for explaining affects; the domain of bodily causality and affective contest is opened.","refs":["CH_QGOH_PQDS:A9","CH_QGOH_PQDS:A11","CH_QGOH_PQDS:A12"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"From","passages":[]},{"id":"CH_QGOH_PQDS:ME5","text":"Future, past, and present time, together with necessity, possibility, and contingency, become coordinates of affective intensity.","refs":["CH_QGOH_PQDS:A10"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"From","passages":[]}],"original_name":"Map-Effects"},{"number":"11","name":"Altitude Levels","items":[{"id":"CH_QGOH_PQDS:ALT1","text":"Surface — A human being subject to affects can know what is better and nevertheless pursue what is worse, because passions can overcome its actions.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[]},{"id":"CH_QGOH_PQDS:ALT2","text":"Structural — Passion arises where the human being is a partial cause, and its force is determined by the proportion of external power to human power.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[]},{"id":"CH_QGOH_PQDS:ALT3","text":"Metastrategic — The governance of affects changes from a precept of correct knowledge into a question of producing a stronger contrary affect.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[]},{"id":"CH_QGOH_PQDS:ALT4","text":"Ontological/Temporal — Nature acts necessarily without end, while imagination converts presence, temporal distance, and the mode of existence into differences of affective forces.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[]}],"original_name":"Altitude Levels"},{"number":"12","name":"Minimal Reconstruction Test","items":[{"id":"CH_QGOH_PQDS:MRT1","text":"The series of axioms suffices for restoring the principal movement, because the transition from the critique of finality to the theory of bondage and to the law of affective conflict is explained.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[]},{"id":"CH_QGOH_PQDS:MRT2","text":"The strongest node is the connection between human finitude, the excess of external causes, and the necessity of passions.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[]},{"id":"CH_QGOH_PQDS:MRT3","text":"The model of human nature to which good and evil are referred obtains a practical function, but its material determination is not deduced here.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[]},{"id":"CH_QGOH_PQDS:MRT4","text":"The formula of good as that which is certainly known to be useful presupposes a criterion of certainty that is not established here, although the affective force of knowledge is strictly limited.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[]},{"id":"CH_QGOH_PQDS:MRT5","text":"An internal tension remains between the dissolution of objective perfection and the retention of a normative human model; the text contains it through the distinction between the nature of things and human use.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[]},{"id":"CH_QGOH_PQDS:MRT6","text":"The law by which an affect is restrained solely by the force of a contrary and stronger affect is established, but the manner in which true knowledge might obtain such force is not explained beyond the general condition of affect.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[]},{"id":"CH_QGOH_PQDS:MRT7","text":"The reconstruction preserves the movement of the text, because moral terms are not simply abolished, but transferred from finalistic ontology to an immanent calculus of powers.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[]}],"original_name":"Minimal Reconstruction Test"}],"revision":"CH_QGOH_PQDS@R001","source_variant_id":"canonical:CH_QGOH_PQDS:v1","source_language_code":"la"}],"identity":{"layer":"CH","kicker":"Chapter 14","title":"Part Four - On Human Bondage, or the Powers of the Affects · passage 1","context":"Chapter 14","original_title":"Pars quarta - De servitute humana seu de affectuum viribus · passage 1","original_context":"Chapter 14"},"original_title":"Pars quarta - De servitute humana seu de affectuum viribus · passage 1","title_language_code":"en"},{"id":"CH_QGOH_PQ_2","layer":"CH","title":"Part Four - On Human Bondage, or the Powers of the Affects · passage 2","order":15,"parents":["RAW_QGOH_PQ_2"],"revision":"CH_QGOH_PQ_2@R001","sha256":"5ab57c0fb7abecb5382ce0f4b967ca87172b6f122d6d9c479b4070603cfe1a90","sections":[{"number":"1","name":"Argumental Spine","items":[{"id":"CH_QGOH_PQ_2:AS1","text":"Textus quaerit cur vera boni et mali cognitio, etiamsi cupiditatem activam generet, hominem a passionibus non necessario liberet.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_QGOH_PQ_2:P0001","trace_type":"Direct","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_QGOH_PQ_2:AS2","text":"Demonstratur cupiditatem ex cognitione ortam potentia humana tantum definiri, dum cupiditates passivae a causis externis potentioribus definiri possunt.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_QGOH_PQ_2:P0003","trace_type":"Composite","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"},{"id":"RAW_QGOH_PQ_2:P0004","trace_type":"Composite","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_QGOH_PQ_2:AS3","text":"Praesentia et contingentia futurae rei inaequalem affectuum vim efficiunt, ita ut cognitio futuri facilius a cupiditatibus praesentibus vincatur.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_QGOH_PQ_2:P0006","trace_type":"Composite","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"},{"id":"RAW_QGOH_PQ_2:P0008","trace_type":"Composite","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"},{"id":"RAW_QGOH_PQ_2:P0010","trace_type":"Composite","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_QGOH_PQ_2:AS4","text":"Diagnosis impotentiae ad reconstructionem transit: conatus sese conservandi constituit virtutem, felicitatem et fundamentum utilitatis rationalis.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_QGOH_PQ_2:P0020","trace_type":"Structural","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"},{"id":"RAW_QGOH_PQ_2:P0021","trace_type":"Structural","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"},{"id":"RAW_QGOH_PQ_2:P0030","trace_type":"Structural","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"},{"id":"RAW_QGOH_PQ_2:P0032","trace_type":"Structural","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_QGOH_PQ_2:AS5","text":"Ratio in intelligendo consistit; bonum certum ad intellectionem conducit, et summum Mentis bonum est Dei cognitio.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_QGOH_PQ_2:P0059","trace_type":"Composite","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"},{"id":"RAW_QGOH_PQ_2:P0062","trace_type":"Composite","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"},{"id":"RAW_QGOH_PQ_2:P0068","trace_type":"Composite","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_QGOH_PQ_2:AS6","text":"Conveniens natura utilitatem et compositionem potentiae producit; passiones homines dissociant, Ratio autem eos necessario in concordiam reducit.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_QGOH_PQ_2:P0088","trace_type":"Structural","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"},{"id":"RAW_QGOH_PQ_2:P0092","trace_type":"Structural","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"},{"id":"RAW_QGOH_PQ_2:P0096","trace_type":"Structural","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"},{"id":"RAW_QGOH_PQ_2:P0103","trace_type":"Structural","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"},{"id":"RAW_QGOH_PQ_2:P0106","trace_type":"Structural","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]}],"original_name":"Argumental Spine"},{"number":"2","name":"Load-Bearing Axioms","items":[{"id":"CH_QGOH_PQ_2:A1","text":"Cupiditas sive appetitus est ipsa hominis essentia, id est conatus quo homo in suo esse perseverat.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_QGOH_PQ_2:P0018","trace_type":"Direct","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_QGOH_PQ_2:A2","text":"Virtus est humana potentia ex solis legibus propriae naturae agendi, cuius primum et unicum fundamentum est conatus sese conservandi.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_QGOH_PQ_2:P0020","trace_type":"Composite","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"},{"id":"RAW_QGOH_PQ_2:P0021","trace_type":"Composite","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"},{"id":"RAW_QGOH_PQ_2:P0045","trace_type":"Composite","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_QGOH_PQ_2:A3","text":"Cognitio boni et mali est affectus Laetitiae vel Tristitiae, quatenus homo eius est conscius.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_QGOH_PQ_2:P0028","trace_type":"Direct","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_QGOH_PQ_2:A4","text":"Unusquisque ex legibus propriae naturae necessario appetit quod bonum, et aversatur quod malum esse iudicat.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_QGOH_PQ_2:P0026","trace_type":"Composite","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"},{"id":"RAW_QGOH_PQ_2:P0028","trace_type":"Composite","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_QGOH_PQ_2:A5","text":"Laetitia conatum et cupiditatem auget, Tristitia vero eadem minuit vel coercet.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_QGOH_PQ_2:P0018","trace_type":"Direct","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_QGOH_PQ_2:A6","text":"Cupiditates ex passionibus ortae vi causarum externarum definiuntur, quae potentiam humanam indefinite superare possunt.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_QGOH_PQ_2:P0003","trace_type":"Direct","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"},{"id":"RAW_QGOH_PQ_2:P0004","trace_type":"Direct","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_QGOH_PQ_2:A7","text":"Cupiditas ex vera boni et mali cognitione orta, quatenus ex intellectione procedit, ad agendi potentiam propriae essentiae pertinet.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_QGOH_PQ_2:P0003","trace_type":"Direct","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_QGOH_PQ_2:A8","text":"Affectus rei praesentis vehementior est affectu rei futurae imaginatae, et cognitio boni vel mali contingentis adhuc infirmius movet.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_QGOH_PQ_2:P0008","trace_type":"Composite","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"},{"id":"RAW_QGOH_PQ_2:P0010","trace_type":"Composite","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_QGOH_PQ_2:A9","text":"Homo ex ideis inadaequatis determinatus patitur; ex eo quod intelligit determinatus agit atque ex virtute procedit.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_QGOH_PQ_2:P0047","trace_type":"Direct","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"},{"id":"RAW_QGOH_PQ_2:P0049","trace_type":"Direct","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_QGOH_PQ_2:A10","text":"Ratio est Mens quatenus clare et distincte intelligit, nec utile certum iudicat nisi quod ad intelligendum conducit.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_QGOH_PQ_2:P0059","trace_type":"Composite","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"},{"id":"RAW_QGOH_PQ_2:P0061","trace_type":"Composite","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_QGOH_PQ_2:A11","text":"Nemo ex necessitate propriae naturae suum esse negare potest nec suum esse alterius rei causa conservare conatur.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_QGOH_PQ_2:P0035","trace_type":"Composite","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"},{"id":"RAW_QGOH_PQ_2:P0055","trace_type":"Composite","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"},{"id":"RAW_QGOH_PQ_2:P0057","trace_type":"Composite","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_QGOH_PQ_2:A12","text":"Homo sine rebus externis suum esse conservare nequit, et individua eiusdem naturae coniuncta maiorem potentiam compositam efficiunt.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_QGOH_PQ_2:P0022","trace_type":"Direct","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"},{"id":"RAW_QGOH_PQ_2:P0023","trace_type":"Direct","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_QGOH_PQ_2:A13","text":"Res cuius natura a natura humana prorsus diversa est humanam agendi potentiam neque iuvare neque coercere potest.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_QGOH_PQ_2:P0072","trace_type":"Direct","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"},{"id":"RAW_QGOH_PQ_2:P0074","trace_type":"Direct","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_QGOH_PQ_2:A14","text":"Id quod cum natura humana convenit necessario bonum est; malum est tantum quod agendi potentiam contrarie minuit vel coercet.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_QGOH_PQ_2:P0076","trace_type":"Composite","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"},{"id":"RAW_QGOH_PQ_2:P0078","trace_type":"Composite","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"},{"id":"RAW_QGOH_PQ_2:P0080","trace_type":"Composite","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"},{"id":"RAW_QGOH_PQ_2:P0082","trace_type":"Composite","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_QGOH_PQ_2:A15","text":"Natura convenire est positive in potentia convenire, non solam finitatem, impotentiam vel negationem communicare.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_QGOH_PQ_2:P0088","trace_type":"Direct","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"},{"id":"RAW_QGOH_PQ_2:P0090","trace_type":"Direct","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_QGOH_PQ_2:A16","text":"Passiones a causis externis definitae homines diversificant, inconstantes faciunt et invicem opponere possunt.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_QGOH_PQ_2:P0092","trace_type":"Composite","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"},{"id":"RAW_QGOH_PQ_2:P0094","trace_type":"Composite","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"},{"id":"RAW_QGOH_PQ_2:P0098","trace_type":"Composite","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_QGOH_PQ_2:A17","text":"Homines, quatenus ex ductu Rationis vivunt, ex natura humana ut causa proxima agunt atque necessario inter se conveniunt.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_QGOH_PQ_2:P0103","trace_type":"Direct","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"},{"id":"RAW_QGOH_PQ_2:P0105","trace_type":"Direct","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"},{"id":"RAW_QGOH_PQ_2:P0106","trace_type":"Direct","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_QGOH_PQ_2:A18","text":"Deus est Ens absolute infinitum, sine quo nihil esse neque concipi potest, ideoque summum obiectum Mentis intellegentis.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_QGOH_PQ_2:P0068","trace_type":"Direct","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"},{"id":"RAW_QGOH_PQ_2:P0070","trace_type":"Direct","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]}],"original_name":"Load-Bearing Axioms"},{"number":"3","name":"Apophatic Structure","items":[{"id":"CH_QGOH_PQ_2:NX1","text":"Vera boni et mali cognitio non est vis quae passiones per se vincit.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_QGOH_PQ_2:P0001","trace_type":"Negative","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"},{"id":"RAW_QGOH_PQ_2:P0003","trace_type":"Negative","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"},{"id":"RAW_QGOH_PQ_2:P0004","trace_type":"Negative","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_QGOH_PQ_2:NX2","text":"Cupiditas ex cognitione futuri vel contingentis orta non aequat vim cupiditatis rerum suavium praesentium.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_QGOH_PQ_2:P0006","trace_type":"Negative","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"},{"id":"RAW_QGOH_PQ_2:P0008","trace_type":"Negative","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"},{"id":"RAW_QGOH_PQ_2:P0010","trace_type":"Negative","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_QGOH_PQ_2:NX3","text":"Cognitio humanae impotentiae non probat ignorantiam cognitione praestantiorem neque stultum sapienti in moderandis affectibus parem esse.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_QGOH_PQ_2:P0014","trace_type":"Negative","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_QGOH_PQ_2:NX4","text":"Ratio nihil contra Naturam postulat, et virtus non in negatione propriae naturae consistit.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_QGOH_PQ_2:P0020","trace_type":"Negative","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_QGOH_PQ_2:NX5","text":"Bonum et malum res cui natura nostra prorsus externa est attribui non possunt.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_QGOH_PQ_2:P0072","trace_type":"Negative","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"},{"id":"RAW_QGOH_PQ_2:P0074","trace_type":"Negative","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_QGOH_PQ_2:NX6","text":"Communis finitas, imminutio vel passivitas non constituit veram naturarum convenientiam.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_QGOH_PQ_2:P0088","trace_type":"Negative","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"},{"id":"RAW_QGOH_PQ_2:P0090","trace_type":"Negative","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_QGOH_PQ_2:NX7","text":"Neglectus conservationis sui et imminutio vitae non sunt actus qui ex sola hominis natura necessario sequuntur.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_QGOH_PQ_2:P0034","trace_type":"Negative","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"},{"id":"RAW_QGOH_PQ_2:P0035","trace_type":"Negative","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_QGOH_PQ_2:NX8","text":"Idem obiectum amatum non est per se causa mutui damni; conflictus ex discrepantia affectuum et imaginationum oritur.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_QGOH_PQ_2:P0100","trace_type":"Negative","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"},{"id":"RAW_QGOH_PQ_2:P0101","trace_type":"Negative","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_QGOH_PQ_2:NX9","text":"Homines non simpliciter quatenus homines, sed tantum quatenus Ratione ducuntur, necessario conveniunt.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_QGOH_PQ_2:P0103","trace_type":"Negative","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"},{"id":"RAW_QGOH_PQ_2:P0105","trace_type":"Negative","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"},{"id":"RAW_QGOH_PQ_2:P0106","trace_type":"Negative","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]}],"original_name":"Apophatic Structure"},{"number":"4","name":"Derivative Propositions","items":[{"id":"CH_QGOH_PQ_2:D1","text":"Cupiditas ex vera boni et mali cognitione orta a cupiditatibus passionum extingui vel coerceri potest, quia hae potentia causarum externarum augeri possunt.","refs":["CH_QGOH_PQ_2:A6","CH_QGOH_PQ_2:A7"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"From","passages":[{"id":"RAW_QGOH_PQ_2:P0003","trace_type":"Direct","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"},{"id":"RAW_QGOH_PQ_2:P0004","trace_type":"Direct","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_QGOH_PQ_2:D2","text":"Cupiditas ex cognitione futuri orta facilius coercetur quam cupiditas ex cognitione rei praesentis, et cognitio contingentis adhuc facilius succumbit.","refs":["CH_QGOH_PQ_2:A6","CH_QGOH_PQ_2:A7","CH_QGOH_PQ_2:A8"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"From","passages":[{"id":"RAW_QGOH_PQ_2:P0006","trace_type":"Direct","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"},{"id":"RAW_QGOH_PQ_2:P0008","trace_type":"Direct","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"},{"id":"RAW_QGOH_PQ_2:P0010","trace_type":"Direct","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_QGOH_PQ_2:D3","text":"Hinc explicatur cur homines opinionibus et libidinibus magis quam vera Ratione saepe moveantur.","refs":["CH_QGOH_PQ_2:A6","CH_QGOH_PQ_2:A7","CH_QGOH_PQ_2:A8"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"From","passages":[{"id":"RAW_QGOH_PQ_2:P0014","trace_type":"Direct","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_QGOH_PQ_2:D4","text":"Cupiditas ex Laetitia orta, ceteris paribus, cupiditate ex Tristitia orta fortior est.","refs":["CH_QGOH_PQ_2:A1","CH_QGOH_PQ_2:A5"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"From","passages":[{"id":"RAW_QGOH_PQ_2:P0016","trace_type":"Direct","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"},{"id":"RAW_QGOH_PQ_2:P0018","trace_type":"Direct","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_QGOH_PQ_2:D5","text":"Ratio praecipit ut unusquisque se amet, verum utile quaerat, maiorem perfectionem appetat et suum esse, quantum potest, conservet.","refs":["CH_QGOH_PQ_2:A1","CH_QGOH_PQ_2:A2","CH_QGOH_PQ_2:A10","CH_QGOH_PQ_2:A11"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"From","passages":[{"id":"RAW_QGOH_PQ_2:P0020","trace_type":"Direct","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"},{"id":"RAW_QGOH_PQ_2:P0021","trace_type":"Direct","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_QGOH_PQ_2:D6","text":"Beate vivere, bene agere et bene vivere non possunt ab actu esse, agere et vivere separari.","refs":["CH_QGOH_PQ_2:A1","CH_QGOH_PQ_2:A2","CH_QGOH_PQ_2:A11"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"From","passages":[{"id":"RAW_QGOH_PQ_2:P0037","trace_type":"Direct","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"},{"id":"RAW_QGOH_PQ_2:P0039","trace_type":"Direct","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_QGOH_PQ_2:D7","text":"Ex virtute agere nihil aliud est quam ex ductu Rationis agere, vivere et suum esse conservare.","refs":["CH_QGOH_PQ_2:A2","CH_QGOH_PQ_2:A9","CH_QGOH_PQ_2:A10"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"From","passages":[{"id":"RAW_QGOH_PQ_2:P0047","trace_type":"Composite","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"},{"id":"RAW_QGOH_PQ_2:P0051","trace_type":"Composite","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"},{"id":"RAW_QGOH_PQ_2:P0053","trace_type":"Composite","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_QGOH_PQ_2:D8","text":"Nihil certo bonum esse scitur nisi quod ad intellegendum revera conducit, nec malum nisi quod intellectionem impedit.","refs":["CH_QGOH_PQ_2:A10"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"From","passages":[{"id":"RAW_QGOH_PQ_2:P0064","trace_type":"Direct","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"},{"id":"RAW_QGOH_PQ_2:P0066","trace_type":"Direct","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_QGOH_PQ_2:D9","text":"Dei cognitio est summum Mentis bonum et Dei cognitio summa Mentis virtus.","refs":["CH_QGOH_PQ_2:A10","CH_QGOH_PQ_2:A18"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"From","passages":[{"id":"RAW_QGOH_PQ_2:P0068","trace_type":"Direct","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"},{"id":"RAW_QGOH_PQ_2:P0070","trace_type":"Direct","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_QGOH_PQ_2:D10","text":"Quo res magis cum natura humana convenit, eo utilior et melior est; quo magis est contraria, eo magis mala est.","refs":["CH_QGOH_PQ_2:A13","CH_QGOH_PQ_2:A14"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"From","passages":[{"id":"RAW_QGOH_PQ_2:P0072","trace_type":"Composite","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"},{"id":"RAW_QGOH_PQ_2:P0076","trace_type":"Composite","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"},{"id":"RAW_QGOH_PQ_2:P0080","trace_type":"Composite","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"},{"id":"RAW_QGOH_PQ_2:P0084","trace_type":"Composite","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_QGOH_PQ_2:D11","text":"Passiones humanae diversitatem et oppositionem explicant, dum Ratio concordiam naturarum humanarum necessario explicat.","refs":["CH_QGOH_PQ_2:A15","CH_QGOH_PQ_2:A16","CH_QGOH_PQ_2:A17"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"From","passages":[{"id":"RAW_QGOH_PQ_2:P0092","trace_type":"Structural","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"},{"id":"RAW_QGOH_PQ_2:P0096","trace_type":"Structural","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"},{"id":"RAW_QGOH_PQ_2:P0103","trace_type":"Structural","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"},{"id":"RAW_QGOH_PQ_2:P0105","trace_type":"Structural","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"},{"id":"RAW_QGOH_PQ_2:P0106","trace_type":"Structural","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_QGOH_PQ_2:D12","text":"Homo ex ductu Rationis vivens est homini res singularis utilissima; utilitas propria rationalis in commune utile extenditur.","refs":["CH_QGOH_PQ_2:A12","CH_QGOH_PQ_2:A14","CH_QGOH_PQ_2:A17"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"From","passages":[{"id":"RAW_QGOH_PQ_2:P0022","trace_type":"Composite","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"},{"id":"RAW_QGOH_PQ_2:P0023","trace_type":"Composite","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"},{"id":"RAW_QGOH_PQ_2:P0103","trace_type":"Composite","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"},{"id":"RAW_QGOH_PQ_2:P0108","trace_type":"Composite","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]}],"original_name":"Derivative Propositions"},{"number":"5","name":"Super-Derivatives","items":[{"id":"CH_QGOH_PQ_2:SD1","text":"Servitus humana non est mera erroris conditio, sed asymmetria causalis inter potentiam finitam agentis et potentiam causarum externarum.","refs":["CH_QGOH_PQ_2:A6","CH_QGOH_PQ_2:A7","CH_QGOH_PQ_2:A9"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"From","passages":[{"id":"RAW_QGOH_PQ_2:P0003","trace_type":"Structural","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"},{"id":"RAW_QGOH_PQ_2:P0047","trace_type":"Structural","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"},{"id":"RAW_QGOH_PQ_2:P0049","trace_type":"Structural","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_QGOH_PQ_2:SD2","text":"Bonum et malum duplicem ordinem habent: affective indicant augmentum vel diminutionem potentiae, rationaliter vero certo definiuntur per relationem ad intellectionem.","refs":["CH_QGOH_PQ_2:A3","CH_QGOH_PQ_2:A5","CH_QGOH_PQ_2:A10","CH_QGOH_PQ_2:A14"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"From","passages":[{"id":"RAW_QGOH_PQ_2:P0018","trace_type":"Structural","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"},{"id":"RAW_QGOH_PQ_2:P0028","trace_type":"Structural","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"},{"id":"RAW_QGOH_PQ_2:P0064","trace_type":"Structural","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"},{"id":"RAW_QGOH_PQ_2:P0076","trace_type":"Structural","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"},{"id":"RAW_QGOH_PQ_2:P0080","trace_type":"Structural","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_QGOH_PQ_2:SD3","text":"Utilitas propria et commune utile non sunt termini contrarii, quia conservatio individui rationalis per convenientiam naturarum et compositionem potentiarum augetur.","refs":["CH_QGOH_PQ_2:A1","CH_QGOH_PQ_2:A12","CH_QGOH_PQ_2:A17"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"From","passages":[{"id":"RAW_QGOH_PQ_2:P0018","trace_type":"Structural","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"},{"id":"RAW_QGOH_PQ_2:P0022","trace_type":"Structural","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"},{"id":"RAW_QGOH_PQ_2:P0103","trace_type":"Structural","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"},{"id":"RAW_QGOH_PQ_2:P0108","trace_type":"Structural","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_QGOH_PQ_2:SD4","text":"Ordinatio socialis rationalis concipitur ut compositio individuorum in quasi unum Corpus et unam Mentem, non ut imminutio conatus singularis.","refs":["CH_QGOH_PQ_2:A1","CH_QGOH_PQ_2:A12","CH_QGOH_PQ_2:A17"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"From","passages":[{"id":"RAW_QGOH_PQ_2:P0022","trace_type":"Direct","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"},{"id":"RAW_QGOH_PQ_2:P0023","trace_type":"Direct","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_QGOH_PQ_2:SD5","text":"Humanitas bifariam ordinatur: quatenus patitur, variat et opponitur; quatenus intelligit, convenit et mutuam utilitatem producit.","refs":["CH_QGOH_PQ_2:A9","CH_QGOH_PQ_2:A16","CH_QGOH_PQ_2:A17"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"From","passages":[{"id":"RAW_QGOH_PQ_2:P0047","trace_type":"Structural","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"},{"id":"RAW_QGOH_PQ_2:P0092","trace_type":"Structural","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"},{"id":"RAW_QGOH_PQ_2:P0103","trace_type":"Structural","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_QGOH_PQ_2:SD6","text":"Ordo ethicus ascendit a conservatione sui per virtutem et intellectionem ad Dei cognitionem ut supremum obiectum Mentis.","refs":["CH_QGOH_PQ_2:A1","CH_QGOH_PQ_2:A2","CH_QGOH_PQ_2:A10","CH_QGOH_PQ_2:A18"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"From","passages":[{"id":"RAW_QGOH_PQ_2:P0020","trace_type":"Structural","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"},{"id":"RAW_QGOH_PQ_2:P0021","trace_type":"Structural","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"},{"id":"RAW_QGOH_PQ_2:P0059","trace_type":"Structural","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"},{"id":"RAW_QGOH_PQ_2:P0068","trace_type":"Structural","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]}],"original_name":"Super-Derivatives"},{"number":"6","name":"Deep Apophatics","items":[{"id":"CH_QGOH_PQ_2:DNX1","text":"Falsum esse debet cognitionem veram esse facultatem motivationalem a potentia affectuum et causis externis separatam.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_QGOH_PQ_2:P0003","trace_type":"Negative","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"},{"id":"RAW_QGOH_PQ_2:P0014","trace_type":"Negative","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_QGOH_PQ_2:DNX2","text":"Falsum esse debet ethicam fundari in mandato naturae humanae externo, in sacrificio sui, vel in recusatione amoris sui.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_QGOH_PQ_2:P0020","trace_type":"Negative","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"},{"id":"RAW_QGOH_PQ_2:P0021","trace_type":"Negative","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"},{"id":"RAW_QGOH_PQ_2:P0055","trace_type":"Negative","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"},{"id":"RAW_QGOH_PQ_2:P0057","trace_type":"Negative","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_QGOH_PQ_2:DNX3","text":"Falsum esse debet individuum humanum posse per se solum sufficienter subsistere aut sufficienter intelligere.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_QGOH_PQ_2:P0022","trace_type":"Negative","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_QGOH_PQ_2:DNX4","text":"Falsum esse debet solidarietatem humanam ex communi defectu, finitate vel passivitate positive constitui.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_QGOH_PQ_2:P0088","trace_type":"Negative","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"},{"id":"RAW_QGOH_PQ_2:P0090","trace_type":"Negative","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_QGOH_PQ_2:DNX5","text":"Falsum esse debet communionem obiecti amati ipsam esse fontem oppositionis; discrepantia affectiva, non convenientia, conflictum producit.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_QGOH_PQ_2:P0098","trace_type":"Negative","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"},{"id":"RAW_QGOH_PQ_2:P0100","trace_type":"Negative","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"},{"id":"RAW_QGOH_PQ_2:P0101","trace_type":"Negative","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_QGOH_PQ_2:DNX6","text":"Falsum esse debet voluntariam sui annihilationem ex sola natura hominis procedere.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_QGOH_PQ_2:P0034","trace_type":"Negative","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"},{"id":"RAW_QGOH_PQ_2:P0035","trace_type":"Negative","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_QGOH_PQ_2:DNX7","text":"Falsum esse debet bonum et malum esse proprietates rerum absolute a relatione ad humanam potentiam et intellectionem separatae.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_QGOH_PQ_2:P0072","trace_type":"Negative","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"},{"id":"RAW_QGOH_PQ_2:P0074","trace_type":"Negative","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"},{"id":"RAW_QGOH_PQ_2:P0076","trace_type":"Negative","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"},{"id":"RAW_QGOH_PQ_2:P0078","trace_type":"Negative","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]}],"original_name":"Deep Apophatics"},{"number":"7","name":"Super-Apophatics","items":[{"id":"CH_QGOH_PQ_2:SNX1","text":"Argumentatio libertatem absolutam, quae a nexu causarum externarum prorsus exempta sit, non admittit.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_QGOH_PQ_2:P0003","trace_type":"Negative","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"},{"id":"RAW_QGOH_PQ_2:P0004","trace_type":"Negative","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"},{"id":"RAW_QGOH_PQ_2:P0014","trace_type":"Negative","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_QGOH_PQ_2:SNX2","text":"Argumentatio beatitudinem quae ab actu existere, agere et perseverare separari possit indisponibilem facit.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_QGOH_PQ_2:P0037","trace_type":"Direct","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_QGOH_PQ_2:SNX3","text":"Argumentatio concordiam collectivam ex sola communi humanitate, sine Ratione et convenientia positiva, non permittit.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_QGOH_PQ_2:P0088","trace_type":"Composite","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"},{"id":"RAW_QGOH_PQ_2:P0090","trace_type":"Composite","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"},{"id":"RAW_QGOH_PQ_2:P0103","trace_type":"Composite","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"},{"id":"RAW_QGOH_PQ_2:P0106","trace_type":"Composite","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_QGOH_PQ_2:SNX4","text":"Argumentatio finem rationalem extra intellectionem et Dei cognitionem ultimum statuere non potest.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_QGOH_PQ_2:P0059","trace_type":"Composite","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"},{"id":"RAW_QGOH_PQ_2:P0068","trace_type":"Composite","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"},{"id":"RAW_QGOH_PQ_2:P0070","trace_type":"Composite","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]}],"original_name":"Super-Apophatics"},{"number":"8","name":"Formal Generative Schema","items":[{"id":"CH_QGOH_PQ_2:FGS1","text":"Potentia causarum externarum → passiones → cupiditates externis causis definitae → coercitio cupiditatis ex vera cognitione ortae → servitus humana.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_QGOH_PQ_2:P0003","trace_type":"Structural","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"},{"id":"RAW_QGOH_PQ_2:P0004","trace_type":"Structural","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_QGOH_PQ_2:FGS2","text":"Futurum vel contingens → affectus remissior → cupiditas cognitivi boni infirmior → maior vulnerabilitas ad suavitatem praesentem.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_QGOH_PQ_2:P0006","trace_type":"Structural","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"},{"id":"RAW_QGOH_PQ_2:P0008","trace_type":"Structural","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"},{"id":"RAW_QGOH_PQ_2:P0010","trace_type":"Structural","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_QGOH_PQ_2:FGS3","text":"Conatus sese conservandi → virtus → actio ex intellectu → Ratio ut intelligere → utile certum → Dei cognitio.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_QGOH_PQ_2:P0020","trace_type":"Structural","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"},{"id":"RAW_QGOH_PQ_2:P0021","trace_type":"Structural","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"},{"id":"RAW_QGOH_PQ_2:P0045","trace_type":"Structural","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"},{"id":"RAW_QGOH_PQ_2:P0053","trace_type":"Structural","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"},{"id":"RAW_QGOH_PQ_2:P0059","trace_type":"Structural","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"},{"id":"RAW_QGOH_PQ_2:P0068","trace_type":"Structural","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_QGOH_PQ_2:FGS4","text":"Indigentia rerum externarum + convenientia naturarum → compositio potentiarum → maius esse conservandum → commune utile.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_QGOH_PQ_2:P0022","trace_type":"Structural","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"},{"id":"RAW_QGOH_PQ_2:P0023","trace_type":"Structural","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_QGOH_PQ_2:FGS5","text":"Passio → discrepantia imaginativa et affectiva → inconstantia vel conflictus; Ratio → actio ex natura humana communi → necessaria convenientia.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_QGOH_PQ_2:P0092","trace_type":"Structural","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"},{"id":"RAW_QGOH_PQ_2:P0094","trace_type":"Structural","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"},{"id":"RAW_QGOH_PQ_2:P0096","trace_type":"Structural","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"},{"id":"RAW_QGOH_PQ_2:P0100","trace_type":"Structural","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"},{"id":"RAW_QGOH_PQ_2:P0103","trace_type":"Structural","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"},{"id":"RAW_QGOH_PQ_2:P0106","trace_type":"Structural","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]}],"original_name":"Formal Generative Schema"},{"number":"9","name":"Argument Flow Map","items":[{"id":"CH_QGOH_PQ_2:AF1","text":"Vera cognitio boni et mali → cupiditas activa finita → collisio cum cupiditatibus passionum.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_QGOH_PQ_2:P0001","trace_type":"Structural","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"},{"id":"RAW_QGOH_PQ_2:P0003","trace_type":"Structural","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"},{"id":"RAW_QGOH_PQ_2:P0004","trace_type":"Structural","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_QGOH_PQ_2:AF2","text":"Praesentia rerum suavium et imminutio futuri contingentis → superior vis cupiditatum praesentium → diagnosis impotentiae.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_QGOH_PQ_2:P0006","trace_type":"Structural","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"},{"id":"RAW_QGOH_PQ_2:P0008","trace_type":"Structural","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"},{"id":"RAW_QGOH_PQ_2:P0010","trace_type":"Structural","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"},{"id":"RAW_QGOH_PQ_2:P0014","trace_type":"Structural","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_QGOH_PQ_2:AF3","text":"Diagnosis impotentiae → recusatio ignorantiae → determinatio limitum Rationis in moderandis affectibus.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_QGOH_PQ_2:P0014","trace_type":"Direct","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_QGOH_PQ_2:AF4","text":"Conatus → virtus et felicitas → quaesitio veri utilitatis.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_QGOH_PQ_2:P0020","trace_type":"Structural","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"},{"id":"RAW_QGOH_PQ_2:P0021","trace_type":"Structural","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"},{"id":"RAW_QGOH_PQ_2:P0053","trace_type":"Structural","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_QGOH_PQ_2:AF5","text":"Ratio → intellegere → bonum certum → Dei cognitio ut summum bonum.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_QGOH_PQ_2:P0059","trace_type":"Structural","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"},{"id":"RAW_QGOH_PQ_2:P0064","trace_type":"Structural","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"},{"id":"RAW_QGOH_PQ_2:P0068","trace_type":"Structural","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"},{"id":"RAW_QGOH_PQ_2:P0070","trace_type":"Structural","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_QGOH_PQ_2:AF6","text":"Communitas naturae → utilitas et compositio; contrarietas naturae → malum et imminutio potentiae.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_QGOH_PQ_2:P0072","trace_type":"Structural","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"},{"id":"RAW_QGOH_PQ_2:P0080","trace_type":"Structural","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"},{"id":"RAW_QGOH_PQ_2:P0084","trace_type":"Structural","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_QGOH_PQ_2:AF7","text":"Passiones → dissensio humana; Ratio → concordia, fides, iustitia et commune utile.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_QGOH_PQ_2:P0096","trace_type":"Structural","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"},{"id":"RAW_QGOH_PQ_2:P0103","trace_type":"Structural","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"},{"id":"RAW_QGOH_PQ_2:P0106","trace_type":"Structural","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"},{"id":"RAW_QGOH_PQ_2:P0108","trace_type":"Structural","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]}],"original_name":"Argument Flow Map"},{"number":"10","name":"Map-Effects","items":[{"id":"CH_QGOH_PQ_2:ME1","text":"Bonum et malum a praedicatis moralibus abstractis in indices augmenti, diminutionis et ordinationis potentiae humanae transferuntur.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_QGOH_PQ_2:P0028","trace_type":"Structural","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"},{"id":"RAW_QGOH_PQ_2:P0064","trace_type":"Structural","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"},{"id":"RAW_QGOH_PQ_2:P0076","trace_type":"Structural","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"},{"id":"RAW_QGOH_PQ_2:P0084","trace_type":"Structural","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_QGOH_PQ_2:ME2","text":"Virtus a sacrificio vel obedientia externa ad conatum rationalem sese conservandi reclassificatur.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_QGOH_PQ_2:P0020","trace_type":"Structural","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"},{"id":"RAW_QGOH_PQ_2:P0021","trace_type":"Structural","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"},{"id":"RAW_QGOH_PQ_2:P0045","trace_type":"Structural","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_QGOH_PQ_2:ME3","text":"Distinctio inter cognitionem veram et efficaciam affectivam visibilis fit: veritas non ex se dominationem causalem importat.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_QGOH_PQ_2:P0001","trace_type":"Composite","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"},{"id":"RAW_QGOH_PQ_2:P0014","trace_type":"Composite","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_QGOH_PQ_2:ME4","text":"Convenientia naturarum ut relatio positiva potentiae, non ut communis defectus aut mera identitas negativa, definitur.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_QGOH_PQ_2:P0088","trace_type":"Structural","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"},{"id":"RAW_QGOH_PQ_2:P0090","trace_type":"Structural","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"},{"id":"RAW_QGOH_PQ_2:P0080","trace_type":"Structural","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_QGOH_PQ_2:ME5","text":"Alter homo ex possibili competitore in summam rem singularem utilem mutatur, sed tantum quatenus Ratione vivit.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_QGOH_PQ_2:P0103","trace_type":"Direct","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"},{"id":"RAW_QGOH_PQ_2:P0108","trace_type":"Direct","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_QGOH_PQ_2:ME6","text":"Tempus affectivum distinguitur: praesens vim immediatam habet, futurum et contingens vim imminutam.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_QGOH_PQ_2:P0006","trace_type":"Direct","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"},{"id":"RAW_QGOH_PQ_2:P0008","trace_type":"Direct","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"},{"id":"RAW_QGOH_PQ_2:P0010","trace_type":"Direct","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]}],"original_name":"Map-Effects"},{"number":"11","name":"Altitude Levels","items":[{"id":"CH_QGOH_PQ_2:ALT1","text":"Surface — Textus explicat cur homines cognitionem melioris sequi non valeant, et quae dictamina Ratio de utilitate, virtute et conservatione praecipiat.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_QGOH_PQ_2:P0014","trace_type":"Structural","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"},{"id":"RAW_QGOH_PQ_2:P0020","trace_type":"Structural","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"},{"id":"RAW_QGOH_PQ_2:P0021","trace_type":"Structural","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_QGOH_PQ_2:ALT2","text":"Structural — Affectus ordinantur per comparationem potentiae humanae cum causis externis, per praesentiam temporalem, et per convenientiam vel contrarietatem naturarum.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_QGOH_PQ_2:P0003","trace_type":"Structural","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"},{"id":"RAW_QGOH_PQ_2:P0008","trace_type":"Structural","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"},{"id":"RAW_QGOH_PQ_2:P0084","trace_type":"Structural","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"},{"id":"RAW_QGOH_PQ_2:P0094","trace_type":"Structural","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_QGOH_PQ_2:ALT3","text":"Metastrategic — Moderatio affectuum non per moralem increpationem, sed per cognitionem limitum causalium, ordinationem intellectionis et compositionem utilitatum reformulatur.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_QGOH_PQ_2:P0014","trace_type":"Structural","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"},{"id":"RAW_QGOH_PQ_2:P0059","trace_type":"Structural","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"},{"id":"RAW_QGOH_PQ_2:P0108","trace_type":"Structural","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_QGOH_PQ_2:ALT4","text":"Ontological/Temporal — Essentia est conatus actualis perseverandi; Deus est terminus supremus intellectionis; futurum contingens minus efficaciter movet quam praesens imaginatum.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_QGOH_PQ_2:P0018","trace_type":"Structural","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"},{"id":"RAW_QGOH_PQ_2:P0068","trace_type":"Structural","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"},{"id":"RAW_QGOH_PQ_2:P0070","trace_type":"Structural","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"},{"id":"RAW_QGOH_PQ_2:P0010","trace_type":"Structural","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]}],"original_name":"Altitude Levels"},{"number":"12","name":"Minimal Reconstruction Test","items":[{"id":"CH_QGOH_PQ_2:MRT1","text":"Axiomata sufficiunt ad restituendam seriem principalem: causae externae passionum, impotentia cupiditatis cognitivae, conatus ut virtutis fundamentum, Ratio ut intellectus, et concordia rationalis.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_QGOH_PQ_2:P0003","trace_type":"Structural","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"},{"id":"RAW_QGOH_PQ_2:P0021","trace_type":"Structural","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"},{"id":"RAW_QGOH_PQ_2:P0059","trace_type":"Structural","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"},{"id":"RAW_QGOH_PQ_2:P0106","trace_type":"Structural","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_QGOH_PQ_2:MRT2","text":"Firmissimum systematis nexus est inter conatum, virtutem, actionem ex intellectu et utilitatem quae ad intellegendum conducit.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_QGOH_PQ_2:P0021","trace_type":"Structural","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"},{"id":"RAW_QGOH_PQ_2:P0045","trace_type":"Structural","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"},{"id":"RAW_QGOH_PQ_2:P0053","trace_type":"Structural","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"},{"id":"RAW_QGOH_PQ_2:P0059","trace_type":"Structural","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_QGOH_PQ_2:MRT3","text":"Vis diagnosis pendet a distinctione inter cupiditatem ex cognitione ortam et cupiditates quas causae externae indefinitae augent.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_QGOH_PQ_2:P0003","trace_type":"Direct","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"},{"id":"RAW_QGOH_PQ_2:P0004","trace_type":"Direct","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_QGOH_PQ_2:MRT4","text":"Efficacia positiva Rationis ad affectus vincendos hic non demonstratur, sed ad tractationem separatam de potentia Rationis differtur.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_QGOH_PQ_2:P0014","trace_type":"Direct","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_QGOH_PQ_2:MRT5","text":"Transitus ab utilitate singulari ad commune utile compositionem naturarum affirmat, sed mechanismum quo homines passionales ad talem compositionem perveniant non explicat.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_QGOH_PQ_2:P0022","trace_type":"Structural","confidence":"medium","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"},{"id":"RAW_QGOH_PQ_2:P0023","trace_type":"Structural","confidence":"medium","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"},{"id":"RAW_QGOH_PQ_2:P0108","trace_type":"Structural","confidence":"medium","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_QGOH_PQ_2:MRT6","text":"Duplex definitio boni et mali, affectiva et rationaliter certa, ordines distinguit sed mediationem plenam inter eos non exhaurit.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_QGOH_PQ_2:P0028","trace_type":"Structural","confidence":"medium","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"},{"id":"RAW_QGOH_PQ_2:P0064","trace_type":"Structural","confidence":"medium","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"},{"id":"RAW_QGOH_PQ_2:P0066","trace_type":"Structural","confidence":"medium","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_QGOH_PQ_2:MRT7","text":"Concordia humana non universaliter asseritur, sed conditionaliter sub ratione vivendi; hac condicione servata, motus argumentationis interne cohaeret.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_QGOH_PQ_2:P0103","trace_type":"Direct","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"},{"id":"RAW_QGOH_PQ_2:P0105","trace_type":"Direct","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"},{"id":"RAW_QGOH_PQ_2:P0106","trace_type":"Direct","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]}],"original_name":"Minimal Reconstruction Test"}],"language_code":"la","language_name":"Latina","translations":[{"id":"CAREVAR_20261015T014711373690Z_48a21d67","language_code":"en","language_name":"English","title":"Part Four - On Human Bondage, or the Powers of the Affects · passage 2","source_sha256":"5ab57c0fb7abecb5382ce0f4b967ca87172b6f122d6d9c479b4070603cfe1a90","sha256":"2255cc30e016b7db98f8e15023de92c05ed5c17b20813d4397cdb3c57608b760","rendition_type":"machine","review_status":"machine","model":"gpt-5.6-terra","created":"2026-10-15T01:47:11.374735+00:00","sections":[{"number":"1","name":"Argumental Spine","items":[{"id":"CH_QGOH_PQ_2:AS1","text":"The text asks why true knowledge of good and evil, even if it generates an active desire, does not necessarily free a human being from the passions.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_QGOH_PQ_2:P0001","trace_type":"Direct","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_QGOH_PQ_2:AS2","text":"It is demonstrated that desire arising from knowledge is defined only by human power, whereas passive desires can be defined by more powerful external causes.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_QGOH_PQ_2:P0003","trace_type":"Composite","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"},{"id":"RAW_QGOH_PQ_2:P0004","trace_type":"Composite","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_QGOH_PQ_2:AS3","text":"The presence and contingency of a future thing produce unequal force in the affects, so that knowledge of the future is more easily overcome by present desires.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_QGOH_PQ_2:P0006","trace_type":"Composite","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"},{"id":"RAW_QGOH_PQ_2:P0008","trace_type":"Composite","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"},{"id":"RAW_QGOH_PQ_2:P0010","trace_type":"Composite","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_QGOH_PQ_2:AS4","text":"The diagnosis of impotence passes into reconstruction: the striving to preserve oneself constitutes virtue, happiness, and the foundation of rational utility.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_QGOH_PQ_2:P0020","trace_type":"Structural","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"},{"id":"RAW_QGOH_PQ_2:P0021","trace_type":"Structural","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"},{"id":"RAW_QGOH_PQ_2:P0030","trace_type":"Structural","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"},{"id":"RAW_QGOH_PQ_2:P0032","trace_type":"Structural","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_QGOH_PQ_2:AS5","text":"Reason consists in understanding; certain good conduces to understanding, and the highest good of the Mind is the knowledge of God.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_QGOH_PQ_2:P0059","trace_type":"Composite","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"},{"id":"RAW_QGOH_PQ_2:P0062","trace_type":"Composite","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"},{"id":"RAW_QGOH_PQ_2:P0068","trace_type":"Composite","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_QGOH_PQ_2:AS6","text":"Agreement in nature produces utility and the composition of power; passions dissociate human beings, whereas Reason necessarily brings them back into concord.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_QGOH_PQ_2:P0088","trace_type":"Structural","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"},{"id":"RAW_QGOH_PQ_2:P0092","trace_type":"Structural","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"},{"id":"RAW_QGOH_PQ_2:P0096","trace_type":"Structural","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"},{"id":"RAW_QGOH_PQ_2:P0103","trace_type":"Structural","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"},{"id":"RAW_QGOH_PQ_2:P0106","trace_type":"Structural","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]}],"original_name":"Argumental Spine"},{"number":"2","name":"Load-Bearing Axioms","items":[{"id":"CH_QGOH_PQ_2:A1","text":"Desire, or appetite, is the very essence of a human being, that is, the striving by which a human being perseveres in his being.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_QGOH_PQ_2:P0018","trace_type":"Direct","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_QGOH_PQ_2:A2","text":"Virtue is the human power of acting from the laws of one's own nature alone, whose first and sole foundation is the striving to preserve oneself.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_QGOH_PQ_2:P0020","trace_type":"Composite","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"},{"id":"RAW_QGOH_PQ_2:P0021","trace_type":"Composite","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"},{"id":"RAW_QGOH_PQ_2:P0045","trace_type":"Composite","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_QGOH_PQ_2:A3","text":"Knowledge of good and evil is an affect of Joy or Sadness, insofar as a human being is conscious of it.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_QGOH_PQ_2:P0028","trace_type":"Direct","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_QGOH_PQ_2:A4","text":"Everyone necessarily, from the laws of his own nature, desires what he judges to be good and is averse to what he judges to be evil.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_QGOH_PQ_2:P0026","trace_type":"Composite","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"},{"id":"RAW_QGOH_PQ_2:P0028","trace_type":"Composite","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_QGOH_PQ_2:A5","text":"Joy increases striving and desire, whereas Sadness diminishes or restrains them.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_QGOH_PQ_2:P0018","trace_type":"Direct","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_QGOH_PQ_2:A6","text":"Desires arising from passions are defined by the force of external causes, which can indefinitely surpass human power.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_QGOH_PQ_2:P0003","trace_type":"Direct","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"},{"id":"RAW_QGOH_PQ_2:P0004","trace_type":"Direct","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_QGOH_PQ_2:A7","text":"Desire arising from true knowledge of good and evil, insofar as it proceeds from understanding, pertains to the power of acting of one's own essence.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_QGOH_PQ_2:P0003","trace_type":"Direct","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_QGOH_PQ_2:A8","text":"An affect of a present thing is more vehement than an affect of an imagined future thing, and knowledge of a contingent good or evil moves still more weakly.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_QGOH_PQ_2:P0008","trace_type":"Composite","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"},{"id":"RAW_QGOH_PQ_2:P0010","trace_type":"Composite","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_QGOH_PQ_2:A9","text":"A human being determined by inadequate ideas is passive; determined by what he understands, he acts and proceeds from virtue.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_QGOH_PQ_2:P0047","trace_type":"Direct","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"},{"id":"RAW_QGOH_PQ_2:P0049","trace_type":"Direct","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_QGOH_PQ_2:A10","text":"Reason is the Mind insofar as it understands clearly and distinctly, and judges nothing certainly useful except what conduces to understanding.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_QGOH_PQ_2:P0059","trace_type":"Composite","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"},{"id":"RAW_QGOH_PQ_2:P0061","trace_type":"Composite","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_QGOH_PQ_2:A11","text":"No one can, from the necessity of his own nature, deny his own being, nor does anyone strive to preserve his own being for the sake of another thing.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_QGOH_PQ_2:P0035","trace_type":"Composite","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"},{"id":"RAW_QGOH_PQ_2:P0055","trace_type":"Composite","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"},{"id":"RAW_QGOH_PQ_2:P0057","trace_type":"Composite","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_QGOH_PQ_2:A12","text":"A human being cannot preserve his being without external things, and individuals of the same nature, joined together, produce a greater composite power.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_QGOH_PQ_2:P0022","trace_type":"Direct","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"},{"id":"RAW_QGOH_PQ_2:P0023","trace_type":"Direct","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_QGOH_PQ_2:A13","text":"A thing whose nature is wholly different from human nature can neither aid nor restrain the human power of acting.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_QGOH_PQ_2:P0072","trace_type":"Direct","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"},{"id":"RAW_QGOH_PQ_2:P0074","trace_type":"Direct","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_QGOH_PQ_2:A14","text":"What agrees with human nature is necessarily good; evil is only what contrarily diminishes or restrains the power of acting.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_QGOH_PQ_2:P0076","trace_type":"Composite","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"},{"id":"RAW_QGOH_PQ_2:P0078","trace_type":"Composite","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"},{"id":"RAW_QGOH_PQ_2:P0080","trace_type":"Composite","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"},{"id":"RAW_QGOH_PQ_2:P0082","trace_type":"Composite","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_QGOH_PQ_2:A15","text":"To agree in nature is to agree positively in power, not merely to share finitude, impotence, or negation.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_QGOH_PQ_2:P0088","trace_type":"Direct","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"},{"id":"RAW_QGOH_PQ_2:P0090","trace_type":"Direct","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_QGOH_PQ_2:A16","text":"Passions defined by external causes differentiate human beings, make them inconstant, and can set them against one another.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_QGOH_PQ_2:P0092","trace_type":"Composite","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"},{"id":"RAW_QGOH_PQ_2:P0094","trace_type":"Composite","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"},{"id":"RAW_QGOH_PQ_2:P0098","trace_type":"Composite","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_QGOH_PQ_2:A17","text":"Human beings, insofar as they live under the guidance of Reason, act from human nature as proximate cause and necessarily agree with one another.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_QGOH_PQ_2:P0103","trace_type":"Direct","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"},{"id":"RAW_QGOH_PQ_2:P0105","trace_type":"Direct","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"},{"id":"RAW_QGOH_PQ_2:P0106","trace_type":"Direct","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_QGOH_PQ_2:A18","text":"God is an absolutely infinite Being, without whom nothing can be or be conceived, and is therefore the highest object of the understanding Mind.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_QGOH_PQ_2:P0068","trace_type":"Direct","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"},{"id":"RAW_QGOH_PQ_2:P0070","trace_type":"Direct","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]}],"original_name":"Load-Bearing Axioms"},{"number":"3","name":"Apophatic Structure","items":[{"id":"CH_QGOH_PQ_2:NX1","text":"True knowledge of good and evil is not a force that overcomes the passions by itself.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_QGOH_PQ_2:P0001","trace_type":"Negative","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"},{"id":"RAW_QGOH_PQ_2:P0003","trace_type":"Negative","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"},{"id":"RAW_QGOH_PQ_2:P0004","trace_type":"Negative","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_QGOH_PQ_2:NX2","text":"Desire arising from knowledge of the future or the contingent does not equal the force of desire for pleasant present things.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_QGOH_PQ_2:P0006","trace_type":"Negative","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"},{"id":"RAW_QGOH_PQ_2:P0008","trace_type":"Negative","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"},{"id":"RAW_QGOH_PQ_2:P0010","trace_type":"Negative","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_QGOH_PQ_2:NX3","text":"Knowledge of human impotence does not prove ignorance superior to knowledge, nor that the fool equals the wise person in moderating the affects.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_QGOH_PQ_2:P0014","trace_type":"Negative","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_QGOH_PQ_2:NX4","text":"Reason demands nothing contrary to Nature, and virtue does not consist in the negation of one's own nature.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_QGOH_PQ_2:P0020","trace_type":"Negative","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_QGOH_PQ_2:NX5","text":"Good and evil cannot be attributed to a thing whose nature is wholly external to our nature.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_QGOH_PQ_2:P0072","trace_type":"Negative","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"},{"id":"RAW_QGOH_PQ_2:P0074","trace_type":"Negative","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_QGOH_PQ_2:NX6","text":"Common finitude, diminution, or passivity does not constitute a true agreement of natures.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_QGOH_PQ_2:P0088","trace_type":"Negative","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"},{"id":"RAW_QGOH_PQ_2:P0090","trace_type":"Negative","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_QGOH_PQ_2:NX7","text":"Neglect of self-preservation and diminution of life are not acts that necessarily follow from human nature alone.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_QGOH_PQ_2:P0034","trace_type":"Negative","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"},{"id":"RAW_QGOH_PQ_2:P0035","trace_type":"Negative","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_QGOH_PQ_2:NX8","text":"The same beloved object is not in itself a cause of mutual harm; conflict arises from the discrepancy of affects and imaginings.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_QGOH_PQ_2:P0100","trace_type":"Negative","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"},{"id":"RAW_QGOH_PQ_2:P0101","trace_type":"Negative","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_QGOH_PQ_2:NX9","text":"Human beings do not necessarily agree simply insofar as they are human beings, but only insofar as they are guided by Reason.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_QGOH_PQ_2:P0103","trace_type":"Negative","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"},{"id":"RAW_QGOH_PQ_2:P0105","trace_type":"Negative","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"},{"id":"RAW_QGOH_PQ_2:P0106","trace_type":"Negative","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]}],"original_name":"Apophatic Structure"},{"number":"4","name":"Derivative Propositions","items":[{"id":"CH_QGOH_PQ_2:D1","text":"Desire arising from true knowledge of good and evil can be extinguished or restrained by desires of the passions, because these can be increased by the power of external causes.","refs":["CH_QGOH_PQ_2:A6","CH_QGOH_PQ_2:A7"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"From","passages":[{"id":"RAW_QGOH_PQ_2:P0003","trace_type":"Direct","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"},{"id":"RAW_QGOH_PQ_2:P0004","trace_type":"Direct","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_QGOH_PQ_2:D2","text":"Desire arising from knowledge of the future is more easily restrained than desire arising from knowledge of a present thing, and knowledge of the contingent succumbs still more easily.","refs":["CH_QGOH_PQ_2:A6","CH_QGOH_PQ_2:A7","CH_QGOH_PQ_2:A8"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"From","passages":[{"id":"RAW_QGOH_PQ_2:P0006","trace_type":"Direct","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"},{"id":"RAW_QGOH_PQ_2:P0008","trace_type":"Direct","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"},{"id":"RAW_QGOH_PQ_2:P0010","trace_type":"Direct","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_QGOH_PQ_2:D3","text":"Hence it is explained why human beings are often moved more by opinions and lusts than by true Reason.","refs":["CH_QGOH_PQ_2:A6","CH_QGOH_PQ_2:A7","CH_QGOH_PQ_2:A8"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"From","passages":[{"id":"RAW_QGOH_PQ_2:P0014","trace_type":"Direct","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_QGOH_PQ_2:D4","text":"Desire arising from Joy is, other things being equal, stronger than desire arising from Sadness.","refs":["CH_QGOH_PQ_2:A1","CH_QGOH_PQ_2:A5"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"From","passages":[{"id":"RAW_QGOH_PQ_2:P0016","trace_type":"Direct","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"},{"id":"RAW_QGOH_PQ_2:P0018","trace_type":"Direct","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_QGOH_PQ_2:D5","text":"Reason commands that everyone love himself, seek what is truly useful, desire greater perfection, and preserve his being, insofar as he can.","refs":["CH_QGOH_PQ_2:A1","CH_QGOH_PQ_2:A2","CH_QGOH_PQ_2:A10","CH_QGOH_PQ_2:A11"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"From","passages":[{"id":"RAW_QGOH_PQ_2:P0020","trace_type":"Direct","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"},{"id":"RAW_QGOH_PQ_2:P0021","trace_type":"Direct","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_QGOH_PQ_2:D6","text":"To live blessedly, to act well, and to live well cannot be separated from the act of being, acting, and living.","refs":["CH_QGOH_PQ_2:A1","CH_QGOH_PQ_2:A2","CH_QGOH_PQ_2:A11"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"From","passages":[{"id":"RAW_QGOH_PQ_2:P0037","trace_type":"Direct","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"},{"id":"RAW_QGOH_PQ_2:P0039","trace_type":"Direct","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_QGOH_PQ_2:D7","text":"To act from virtue is nothing other than to act, live, and preserve one's being under the guidance of Reason.","refs":["CH_QGOH_PQ_2:A2","CH_QGOH_PQ_2:A9","CH_QGOH_PQ_2:A10"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"From","passages":[{"id":"RAW_QGOH_PQ_2:P0047","trace_type":"Composite","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"},{"id":"RAW_QGOH_PQ_2:P0051","trace_type":"Composite","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"},{"id":"RAW_QGOH_PQ_2:P0053","trace_type":"Composite","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_QGOH_PQ_2:D8","text":"Nothing is known certainly to be good except what truly conduces to understanding, nor evil except what impedes understanding.","refs":["CH_QGOH_PQ_2:A10"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"From","passages":[{"id":"RAW_QGOH_PQ_2:P0064","trace_type":"Direct","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"},{"id":"RAW_QGOH_PQ_2:P0066","trace_type":"Direct","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_QGOH_PQ_2:D9","text":"Knowledge of God is the highest good of the Mind, and knowledge of God is the highest virtue of the Mind.","refs":["CH_QGOH_PQ_2:A10","CH_QGOH_PQ_2:A18"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"From","passages":[{"id":"RAW_QGOH_PQ_2:P0068","trace_type":"Direct","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"},{"id":"RAW_QGOH_PQ_2:P0070","trace_type":"Direct","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_QGOH_PQ_2:D10","text":"The more a thing agrees with human nature, the more useful and better it is; the more contrary it is, the more evil it is.","refs":["CH_QGOH_PQ_2:A13","CH_QGOH_PQ_2:A14"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"From","passages":[{"id":"RAW_QGOH_PQ_2:P0072","trace_type":"Composite","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"},{"id":"RAW_QGOH_PQ_2:P0076","trace_type":"Composite","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"},{"id":"RAW_QGOH_PQ_2:P0080","trace_type":"Composite","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"},{"id":"RAW_QGOH_PQ_2:P0084","trace_type":"Composite","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_QGOH_PQ_2:D11","text":"Human passions explain diversity and opposition, whereas Reason necessarily explains the concord of human natures.","refs":["CH_QGOH_PQ_2:A15","CH_QGOH_PQ_2:A16","CH_QGOH_PQ_2:A17"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"From","passages":[{"id":"RAW_QGOH_PQ_2:P0092","trace_type":"Structural","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"},{"id":"RAW_QGOH_PQ_2:P0096","trace_type":"Structural","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"},{"id":"RAW_QGOH_PQ_2:P0103","trace_type":"Structural","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"},{"id":"RAW_QGOH_PQ_2:P0105","trace_type":"Structural","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"},{"id":"RAW_QGOH_PQ_2:P0106","trace_type":"Structural","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_QGOH_PQ_2:D12","text":"A human being living under the guidance of Reason is the most useful singular thing for a human being; rational self-interest extends into common utility.","refs":["CH_QGOH_PQ_2:A12","CH_QGOH_PQ_2:A14","CH_QGOH_PQ_2:A17"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"From","passages":[{"id":"RAW_QGOH_PQ_2:P0022","trace_type":"Composite","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"},{"id":"RAW_QGOH_PQ_2:P0023","trace_type":"Composite","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"},{"id":"RAW_QGOH_PQ_2:P0103","trace_type":"Composite","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"},{"id":"RAW_QGOH_PQ_2:P0108","trace_type":"Composite","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]}],"original_name":"Derivative Propositions"},{"number":"5","name":"Super-Derivatives","items":[{"id":"CH_QGOH_PQ_2:SD1","text":"Human bondage is not a mere condition of error, but a causal asymmetry between the finite power of the agent and the power of external causes.","refs":["CH_QGOH_PQ_2:A6","CH_QGOH_PQ_2:A7","CH_QGOH_PQ_2:A9"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"From","passages":[{"id":"RAW_QGOH_PQ_2:P0003","trace_type":"Structural","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"},{"id":"RAW_QGOH_PQ_2:P0047","trace_type":"Structural","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"},{"id":"RAW_QGOH_PQ_2:P0049","trace_type":"Structural","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_QGOH_PQ_2:SD2","text":"Good and evil have a twofold order: affectively they indicate an increase or diminution of power, but rationally they are certainly defined through their relation to understanding.","refs":["CH_QGOH_PQ_2:A3","CH_QGOH_PQ_2:A5","CH_QGOH_PQ_2:A10","CH_QGOH_PQ_2:A14"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"From","passages":[{"id":"RAW_QGOH_PQ_2:P0018","trace_type":"Structural","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"},{"id":"RAW_QGOH_PQ_2:P0028","trace_type":"Structural","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"},{"id":"RAW_QGOH_PQ_2:P0064","trace_type":"Structural","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"},{"id":"RAW_QGOH_PQ_2:P0076","trace_type":"Structural","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"},{"id":"RAW_QGOH_PQ_2:P0080","trace_type":"Structural","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_QGOH_PQ_2:SD3","text":"Self-interest and common utility are not contrary terms, because the preservation of the rational individual is increased through the agreement of natures and the composition of powers.","refs":["CH_QGOH_PQ_2:A1","CH_QGOH_PQ_2:A12","CH_QGOH_PQ_2:A17"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"From","passages":[{"id":"RAW_QGOH_PQ_2:P0018","trace_type":"Structural","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"},{"id":"RAW_QGOH_PQ_2:P0022","trace_type":"Structural","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"},{"id":"RAW_QGOH_PQ_2:P0103","trace_type":"Structural","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"},{"id":"RAW_QGOH_PQ_2:P0108","trace_type":"Structural","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_QGOH_PQ_2:SD4","text":"Rational social ordering is conceived as the composition of individuals into, as it were, one Body and one Mind, not as a diminution of singular striving.","refs":["CH_QGOH_PQ_2:A1","CH_QGOH_PQ_2:A12","CH_QGOH_PQ_2:A17"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"From","passages":[{"id":"RAW_QGOH_PQ_2:P0022","trace_type":"Direct","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"},{"id":"RAW_QGOH_PQ_2:P0023","trace_type":"Direct","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_QGOH_PQ_2:SD5","text":"Humanity is ordered in two ways: insofar as it is passive, it varies and is opposed; insofar as it understands, it agrees and produces mutual utility.","refs":["CH_QGOH_PQ_2:A9","CH_QGOH_PQ_2:A16","CH_QGOH_PQ_2:A17"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"From","passages":[{"id":"RAW_QGOH_PQ_2:P0047","trace_type":"Structural","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"},{"id":"RAW_QGOH_PQ_2:P0092","trace_type":"Structural","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"},{"id":"RAW_QGOH_PQ_2:P0103","trace_type":"Structural","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_QGOH_PQ_2:SD6","text":"The ethical order ascends from self-preservation through virtue and understanding to knowledge of God as the supreme object of the Mind.","refs":["CH_QGOH_PQ_2:A1","CH_QGOH_PQ_2:A2","CH_QGOH_PQ_2:A10","CH_QGOH_PQ_2:A18"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"From","passages":[{"id":"RAW_QGOH_PQ_2:P0020","trace_type":"Structural","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"},{"id":"RAW_QGOH_PQ_2:P0021","trace_type":"Structural","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"},{"id":"RAW_QGOH_PQ_2:P0059","trace_type":"Structural","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"},{"id":"RAW_QGOH_PQ_2:P0068","trace_type":"Structural","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]}],"original_name":"Super-Derivatives"},{"number":"6","name":"Deep Apophatics","items":[{"id":"CH_QGOH_PQ_2:DNX1","text":"It must be false that true knowledge is a motivational faculty separate from the power of the affects and from external causes.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_QGOH_PQ_2:P0003","trace_type":"Negative","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"},{"id":"RAW_QGOH_PQ_2:P0014","trace_type":"Negative","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_QGOH_PQ_2:DNX2","text":"It must be false that ethics is founded upon a command external to human nature, upon self-sacrifice, or upon the refusal of self-love.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_QGOH_PQ_2:P0020","trace_type":"Negative","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"},{"id":"RAW_QGOH_PQ_2:P0021","trace_type":"Negative","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"},{"id":"RAW_QGOH_PQ_2:P0055","trace_type":"Negative","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"},{"id":"RAW_QGOH_PQ_2:P0057","trace_type":"Negative","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_QGOH_PQ_2:DNX3","text":"It must be false that the human individual can by itself alone subsist sufficiently or understand sufficiently.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_QGOH_PQ_2:P0022","trace_type":"Negative","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_QGOH_PQ_2:DNX4","text":"It must be false that human solidarity is positively constituted from common defect, finitude, or passivity.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_QGOH_PQ_2:P0088","trace_type":"Negative","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"},{"id":"RAW_QGOH_PQ_2:P0090","trace_type":"Negative","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_QGOH_PQ_2:DNX5","text":"It must be false that sharing a beloved object is itself the source of opposition; affective discrepancy, not agreement, produces conflict.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_QGOH_PQ_2:P0098","trace_type":"Negative","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"},{"id":"RAW_QGOH_PQ_2:P0100","trace_type":"Negative","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"},{"id":"RAW_QGOH_PQ_2:P0101","trace_type":"Negative","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_QGOH_PQ_2:DNX6","text":"It must be false that voluntary self-annihilation proceeds from human nature alone.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_QGOH_PQ_2:P0034","trace_type":"Negative","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"},{"id":"RAW_QGOH_PQ_2:P0035","trace_type":"Negative","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_QGOH_PQ_2:DNX7","text":"It must be false that good and evil are properties of things absolutely separate from their relation to human power and understanding.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_QGOH_PQ_2:P0072","trace_type":"Negative","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"},{"id":"RAW_QGOH_PQ_2:P0074","trace_type":"Negative","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"},{"id":"RAW_QGOH_PQ_2:P0076","trace_type":"Negative","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"},{"id":"RAW_QGOH_PQ_2:P0078","trace_type":"Negative","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]}],"original_name":"Deep Apophatics"},{"number":"7","name":"Super-Apophatics","items":[{"id":"CH_QGOH_PQ_2:SNX1","text":"The argument does not admit absolute freedom wholly exempt from the nexus of external causes.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_QGOH_PQ_2:P0003","trace_type":"Negative","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"},{"id":"RAW_QGOH_PQ_2:P0004","trace_type":"Negative","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"},{"id":"RAW_QGOH_PQ_2:P0014","trace_type":"Negative","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_QGOH_PQ_2:SNX2","text":"The argument renders unavailable a blessedness that could be separated from the act of existing, acting, and persevering.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_QGOH_PQ_2:P0037","trace_type":"Direct","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_QGOH_PQ_2:SNX3","text":"The argument does not permit collective concord from common humanity alone, without Reason and positive agreement.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_QGOH_PQ_2:P0088","trace_type":"Composite","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"},{"id":"RAW_QGOH_PQ_2:P0090","trace_type":"Composite","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"},{"id":"RAW_QGOH_PQ_2:P0103","trace_type":"Composite","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"},{"id":"RAW_QGOH_PQ_2:P0106","trace_type":"Composite","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_QGOH_PQ_2:SNX4","text":"The argument cannot establish an ultimate rational end outside understanding and knowledge of God.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_QGOH_PQ_2:P0059","trace_type":"Composite","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"},{"id":"RAW_QGOH_PQ_2:P0068","trace_type":"Composite","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"},{"id":"RAW_QGOH_PQ_2:P0070","trace_type":"Composite","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]}],"original_name":"Super-Apophatics"},{"number":"8","name":"Formal Generative Schema","items":[{"id":"CH_QGOH_PQ_2:FGS1","text":"Power of external causes → passions → desires defined by external causes → restraint of desire arising from true knowledge → human bondage.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_QGOH_PQ_2:P0003","trace_type":"Structural","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"},{"id":"RAW_QGOH_PQ_2:P0004","trace_type":"Structural","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_QGOH_PQ_2:FGS2","text":"Future or contingent → weaker affect → weaker desire for cognitive good → greater vulnerability to present pleasantness.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_QGOH_PQ_2:P0006","trace_type":"Structural","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"},{"id":"RAW_QGOH_PQ_2:P0008","trace_type":"Structural","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"},{"id":"RAW_QGOH_PQ_2:P0010","trace_type":"Structural","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_QGOH_PQ_2:FGS3","text":"Striving to preserve oneself → virtue → action from understanding → Reason as understanding → certain utility → knowledge of God.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_QGOH_PQ_2:P0020","trace_type":"Structural","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"},{"id":"RAW_QGOH_PQ_2:P0021","trace_type":"Structural","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"},{"id":"RAW_QGOH_PQ_2:P0045","trace_type":"Structural","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"},{"id":"RAW_QGOH_PQ_2:P0053","trace_type":"Structural","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"},{"id":"RAW_QGOH_PQ_2:P0059","trace_type":"Structural","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"},{"id":"RAW_QGOH_PQ_2:P0068","trace_type":"Structural","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_QGOH_PQ_2:FGS4","text":"Need of external things + agreement of natures → composition of powers → greater being to be preserved → common utility.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_QGOH_PQ_2:P0022","trace_type":"Structural","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"},{"id":"RAW_QGOH_PQ_2:P0023","trace_type":"Structural","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_QGOH_PQ_2:FGS5","text":"Passion → imaginative and affective discrepancy → inconstancy or conflict; Reason → action from common human nature → necessary agreement.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_QGOH_PQ_2:P0092","trace_type":"Structural","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"},{"id":"RAW_QGOH_PQ_2:P0094","trace_type":"Structural","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"},{"id":"RAW_QGOH_PQ_2:P0096","trace_type":"Structural","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"},{"id":"RAW_QGOH_PQ_2:P0100","trace_type":"Structural","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"},{"id":"RAW_QGOH_PQ_2:P0103","trace_type":"Structural","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"},{"id":"RAW_QGOH_PQ_2:P0106","trace_type":"Structural","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]}],"original_name":"Formal Generative Schema"},{"number":"9","name":"Argument Flow Map","items":[{"id":"CH_QGOH_PQ_2:AF1","text":"True knowledge of good and evil → finite active desire → collision with desires of the passions.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_QGOH_PQ_2:P0001","trace_type":"Structural","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"},{"id":"RAW_QGOH_PQ_2:P0003","trace_type":"Structural","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"},{"id":"RAW_QGOH_PQ_2:P0004","trace_type":"Structural","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_QGOH_PQ_2:AF2","text":"Presence of pleasant things and diminution of the contingent future → superior force of present desires → diagnosis of impotence.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_QGOH_PQ_2:P0006","trace_type":"Structural","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"},{"id":"RAW_QGOH_PQ_2:P0008","trace_type":"Structural","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"},{"id":"RAW_QGOH_PQ_2:P0010","trace_type":"Structural","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"},{"id":"RAW_QGOH_PQ_2:P0014","trace_type":"Structural","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_QGOH_PQ_2:AF3","text":"Diagnosis of impotence → rejection of ignorance → determination of the limits of Reason in moderating the affects.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_QGOH_PQ_2:P0014","trace_type":"Direct","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_QGOH_PQ_2:AF4","text":"Striving → virtue and happiness → pursuit of true utility.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_QGOH_PQ_2:P0020","trace_type":"Structural","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"},{"id":"RAW_QGOH_PQ_2:P0021","trace_type":"Structural","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"},{"id":"RAW_QGOH_PQ_2:P0053","trace_type":"Structural","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_QGOH_PQ_2:AF5","text":"Reason → understanding → certain good → knowledge of God as the highest good.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_QGOH_PQ_2:P0059","trace_type":"Structural","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"},{"id":"RAW_QGOH_PQ_2:P0064","trace_type":"Structural","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"},{"id":"RAW_QGOH_PQ_2:P0068","trace_type":"Structural","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"},{"id":"RAW_QGOH_PQ_2:P0070","trace_type":"Structural","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_QGOH_PQ_2:AF6","text":"Community of nature → utility and composition; contrariety of nature → evil and diminution of power.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_QGOH_PQ_2:P0072","trace_type":"Structural","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"},{"id":"RAW_QGOH_PQ_2:P0080","trace_type":"Structural","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"},{"id":"RAW_QGOH_PQ_2:P0084","trace_type":"Structural","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_QGOH_PQ_2:AF7","text":"Passions → human dissension; Reason → concord, faith, justice, and common utility.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_QGOH_PQ_2:P0096","trace_type":"Structural","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"},{"id":"RAW_QGOH_PQ_2:P0103","trace_type":"Structural","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"},{"id":"RAW_QGOH_PQ_2:P0106","trace_type":"Structural","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"},{"id":"RAW_QGOH_PQ_2:P0108","trace_type":"Structural","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]}],"original_name":"Argument Flow Map"},{"number":"10","name":"Map-Effects","items":[{"id":"CH_QGOH_PQ_2:ME1","text":"Good and evil are shifted from abstract moral predicates into indices of increase, diminution, and ordering of human power.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_QGOH_PQ_2:P0028","trace_type":"Structural","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"},{"id":"RAW_QGOH_PQ_2:P0064","trace_type":"Structural","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"},{"id":"RAW_QGOH_PQ_2:P0076","trace_type":"Structural","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"},{"id":"RAW_QGOH_PQ_2:P0084","trace_type":"Structural","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_QGOH_PQ_2:ME2","text":"Virtue is reclassified from sacrifice or external obedience into rational striving to preserve oneself.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_QGOH_PQ_2:P0020","trace_type":"Structural","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"},{"id":"RAW_QGOH_PQ_2:P0021","trace_type":"Structural","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"},{"id":"RAW_QGOH_PQ_2:P0045","trace_type":"Structural","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_QGOH_PQ_2:ME3","text":"The distinction between true knowledge and affective efficacy becomes visible: truth does not of itself entail causal dominion.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_QGOH_PQ_2:P0001","trace_type":"Composite","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"},{"id":"RAW_QGOH_PQ_2:P0014","trace_type":"Composite","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_QGOH_PQ_2:ME4","text":"Agreement of natures is defined as a positive relation of power, not as common defect or mere negative identity.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_QGOH_PQ_2:P0088","trace_type":"Structural","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"},{"id":"RAW_QGOH_PQ_2:P0090","trace_type":"Structural","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"},{"id":"RAW_QGOH_PQ_2:P0080","trace_type":"Structural","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_QGOH_PQ_2:ME5","text":"Another human being is transformed from a possible competitor into the most useful singular thing, but only insofar as he lives by Reason.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_QGOH_PQ_2:P0103","trace_type":"Direct","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"},{"id":"RAW_QGOH_PQ_2:P0108","trace_type":"Direct","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_QGOH_PQ_2:ME6","text":"Affective time is distinguished: the present has immediate force, the future and contingent have diminished force.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_QGOH_PQ_2:P0006","trace_type":"Direct","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"},{"id":"RAW_QGOH_PQ_2:P0008","trace_type":"Direct","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"},{"id":"RAW_QGOH_PQ_2:P0010","trace_type":"Direct","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]}],"original_name":"Map-Effects"},{"number":"11","name":"Altitude Levels","items":[{"id":"CH_QGOH_PQ_2:ALT1","text":"Surface — The text explains why human beings cannot follow knowledge of the better, and what precepts Reason enjoins concerning utility, virtue, and preservation.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_QGOH_PQ_2:P0014","trace_type":"Structural","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"},{"id":"RAW_QGOH_PQ_2:P0020","trace_type":"Structural","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"},{"id":"RAW_QGOH_PQ_2:P0021","trace_type":"Structural","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_QGOH_PQ_2:ALT2","text":"Structural — The affects are ordered through the comparison of human power with external causes, through temporal presence, and through the agreement or contrariety of natures.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_QGOH_PQ_2:P0003","trace_type":"Structural","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"},{"id":"RAW_QGOH_PQ_2:P0008","trace_type":"Structural","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"},{"id":"RAW_QGOH_PQ_2:P0084","trace_type":"Structural","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"},{"id":"RAW_QGOH_PQ_2:P0094","trace_type":"Structural","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_QGOH_PQ_2:ALT3","text":"Metastrategic — The moderation of the affects is reformulated not through moral reproach, but through knowledge of causal limits, the ordering of understanding, and the composition of utilities.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_QGOH_PQ_2:P0014","trace_type":"Structural","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"},{"id":"RAW_QGOH_PQ_2:P0059","trace_type":"Structural","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"},{"id":"RAW_QGOH_PQ_2:P0108","trace_type":"Structural","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_QGOH_PQ_2:ALT4","text":"Ontological/Temporal — Essence is the actual striving to persevere; God is the supreme terminus of understanding; the contingent future moves less effectively than the imagined present.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_QGOH_PQ_2:P0018","trace_type":"Structural","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"},{"id":"RAW_QGOH_PQ_2:P0068","trace_type":"Structural","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"},{"id":"RAW_QGOH_PQ_2:P0070","trace_type":"Structural","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"},{"id":"RAW_QGOH_PQ_2:P0010","trace_type":"Structural","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]}],"original_name":"Altitude Levels"},{"number":"12","name":"Minimal Reconstruction Test","items":[{"id":"CH_QGOH_PQ_2:MRT1","text":"The axioms suffice to restore the principal sequence: external causes of passions, the impotence of cognitive desire, striving as the foundation of virtue, Reason as understanding, and rational concord.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_QGOH_PQ_2:P0003","trace_type":"Structural","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"},{"id":"RAW_QGOH_PQ_2:P0021","trace_type":"Structural","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"},{"id":"RAW_QGOH_PQ_2:P0059","trace_type":"Structural","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"},{"id":"RAW_QGOH_PQ_2:P0106","trace_type":"Structural","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_QGOH_PQ_2:MRT2","text":"The strongest nexus of the system is that between striving, virtue, action from understanding, and utility that conduces to understanding.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_QGOH_PQ_2:P0021","trace_type":"Structural","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"},{"id":"RAW_QGOH_PQ_2:P0045","trace_type":"Structural","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"},{"id":"RAW_QGOH_PQ_2:P0053","trace_type":"Structural","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"},{"id":"RAW_QGOH_PQ_2:P0059","trace_type":"Structural","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_QGOH_PQ_2:MRT3","text":"The force of the diagnosis depends on the distinction between desire arising from knowledge and desires increased by indefinite external causes.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_QGOH_PQ_2:P0003","trace_type":"Direct","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"},{"id":"RAW_QGOH_PQ_2:P0004","trace_type":"Direct","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_QGOH_PQ_2:MRT4","text":"The positive efficacy of Reason in overcoming the affects is not demonstrated here, but deferred to a separate treatment of the power of Reason.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_QGOH_PQ_2:P0014","trace_type":"Direct","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_QGOH_PQ_2:MRT5","text":"The transition from singular utility to common utility affirms the composition of natures, but does not explain the mechanism by which passionate human beings may arrive at such a composition.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_QGOH_PQ_2:P0022","trace_type":"Structural","confidence":"medium","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"},{"id":"RAW_QGOH_PQ_2:P0023","trace_type":"Structural","confidence":"medium","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"},{"id":"RAW_QGOH_PQ_2:P0108","trace_type":"Structural","confidence":"medium","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_QGOH_PQ_2:MRT6","text":"The twofold definition of good and evil, affective and rationally certain, distinguishes orders but does not exhaust the full mediation between them.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_QGOH_PQ_2:P0028","trace_type":"Structural","confidence":"medium","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"},{"id":"RAW_QGOH_PQ_2:P0064","trace_type":"Structural","confidence":"medium","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"},{"id":"RAW_QGOH_PQ_2:P0066","trace_type":"Structural","confidence":"medium","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_QGOH_PQ_2:MRT7","text":"Human concord is not asserted universally, but conditionally under the manner of living according to Reason; with this condition preserved, the movement of the argument is internally coherent.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_QGOH_PQ_2:P0103","trace_type":"Direct","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"},{"id":"RAW_QGOH_PQ_2:P0105","trace_type":"Direct","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"},{"id":"RAW_QGOH_PQ_2:P0106","trace_type":"Direct","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]}],"original_name":"Minimal Reconstruction Test"}],"revision":"CH_QGOH_PQ_2@R001","source_variant_id":"canonical:CH_QGOH_PQ_2:v1","source_language_code":"la"}],"identity":{"layer":"CH","kicker":"Chapter 15","title":"Part Four - On Human Bondage, or the Powers of the Affects · passage 2","context":"Chapter 15","original_title":"Pars quarta - De servitute humana seu de affectuum viribus · passage 2","original_context":"Chapter 15"},"original_title":"Pars quarta - De servitute humana seu de affectuum viribus · passage 2","title_language_code":"en"},{"id":"CH_QGOH_PQ_3","layer":"CH","title":"Part Four - On Human Bondage, or the Powers of the Affects · passage 3","order":16,"parents":["RAW_QGOH_PQ_3"],"revision":"CH_QGOH_PQ_3@R001","sha256":"fd0ed80268fe33a44007de91db4f998bfc7a345bad3494bbcadef78c63e82034","sections":[{"number":"1","name":"Argumental Spine","items":[{"id":"CH_QGOH_PQ_3:AS1","text":"Textus quaerit quomodo conatus unuscujusque suum utile quaerendi, qui speciem egoismi conflictuosi habere potest, in utilitatem mutuam et societatem humanam convertatur.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_QGOH_PQ_3:P0001","trace_type":"Structural","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"},{"id":"RAW_QGOH_PQ_3:P0003","trace_type":"Structural","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_QGOH_PQ_3:AS2","text":"Solutio prima ponit homines, quatenus Ratione ducuntur, natura convenire; inde virtus, utilitas propria, utilitas mutua et bonum commune eadem catena ordinantur.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_QGOH_PQ_3:P0001","trace_type":"Composite","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"},{"id":"RAW_QGOH_PQ_3:P0006","trace_type":"Composite","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"},{"id":"RAW_QGOH_PQ_3:P0012","trace_type":"Composite","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_QGOH_PQ_3:AS3","text":"Textus deinde ostendit hanc convenientiam rationalem non esse condicionem empirice dominantem, quia affectus homines externos, inconstantes et sibi contrarios efficiunt.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_QGOH_PQ_3:P0003","trace_type":"Tension","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"},{"id":"RAW_QGOH_PQ_3:P0024","trace_type":"Tension","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_QGOH_PQ_3:AS4","text":"Ex hac discrepantia inter normam Rationis et condicionem affectivam Civitas generatur ut apparatus securitatis, cessionis juris, legis communis et coactionis per minas.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_QGOH_PQ_3:P0024","trace_type":"Structural","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"},{"id":"RAW_QGOH_PQ_3:P0025","trace_type":"Structural","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"},{"id":"RAW_QGOH_PQ_3:P0026","trace_type":"Structural","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_QGOH_PQ_3:AS5","text":"Post institutionem civilem, bonum et malum ad potentiam corporis et mentis referuntur, atque affectus per effectum suum in capacitatem agendi, concordiam et stabilitatem dijudicantur.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_QGOH_PQ_3:P0030","trace_type":"Structural","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"},{"id":"RAW_QGOH_PQ_3:P0034","trace_type":"Structural","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"},{"id":"RAW_QGOH_PQ_3:P0042","trace_type":"Structural","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"},{"id":"RAW_QGOH_PQ_3:P0048","trace_type":"Structural","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_QGOH_PQ_3:AS6","text":"Argumentatio demonstrativo-reconstructiva procedit a convenientia rationali ad necessitatem civilem, et a constitutione corporis ad strategiam affectuum per amorem, generositatem et liberationem a metu.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_QGOH_PQ_3:P0001","trace_type":"Structural","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"},{"id":"RAW_QGOH_PQ_3:P0024","trace_type":"Structural","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"},{"id":"RAW_QGOH_PQ_3:P0036","trace_type":"Structural","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"},{"id":"RAW_QGOH_PQ_3:P0079","trace_type":"Structural","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"},{"id":"RAW_QGOH_PQ_3:P0089","trace_type":"Structural","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]}],"original_name":"Argumental Spine"},{"number":"2","name":"Load-Bearing Axioms","items":[{"id":"CH_QGOH_PQ_3:A1","text":"Virtus est potentia agendi ex ductu Rationis, et suum utile rationaliter quaerere est se conservare secundum leges propriae naturae.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_QGOH_PQ_3:P0001","trace_type":"Direct","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_QGOH_PQ_3:A2","text":"Homines, quatenus ex ductu Rationis vivunt, natura conveniunt; ideo utilitas rationaliter quaesita utilitatem mutuam auget.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_QGOH_PQ_3:P0001","trace_type":"Direct","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_QGOH_PQ_3:A3","text":"Summum bonum virtutem sectantium est cognitio Dei, omnibus hominibus communis et ab omnibus aeque possidenda.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_QGOH_PQ_3:P0006","trace_type":"Direct","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"},{"id":"RAW_QGOH_PQ_3:P0008","trace_type":"Direct","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_QGOH_PQ_3:A4","text":"Cupiditas qua vir rationalis summum bonum sibi appetit idem bonum reliquis cupit, atque haec cupiditas cum cognitione Dei crescit.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_QGOH_PQ_3:P0012","trace_type":"Direct","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"},{"id":"RAW_QGOH_PQ_3:P0014","trace_type":"Direct","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"},{"id":"RAW_QGOH_PQ_3:P0015","trace_type":"Direct","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_QGOH_PQ_3:A5","text":"Homines plerumque affectibus externis fortioribus patiuntur, et affectus non nisi affectu fortiore atque contrario coercetur; Ratio sola affectus coërcere non potest.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_QGOH_PQ_3:P0024","trace_type":"Direct","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"},{"id":"RAW_QGOH_PQ_3:P0025","trace_type":"Direct","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_QGOH_PQ_3:A6","text":"Jus naturale uniuscujusque ipsius potentia definitur, ita ut in statu naturali singulus ex suo ingenio bonum, malum, utile, vindictam et conservationem decernat.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_QGOH_PQ_3:P0024","trace_type":"Direct","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_QGOH_PQ_3:A7","text":"Concordia civilis requirit mutuam cessionem juris naturalis et potestatem communem leges ferentem easque minis firmantem.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_QGOH_PQ_3:P0025","trace_type":"Direct","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"},{"id":"RAW_QGOH_PQ_3:P0026","trace_type":"Direct","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_QGOH_PQ_3:A8","text":"Peccatum, meritum, justum, injustum et dominium ex consensu atque jure Civitatis oriuntur, non ex attributis intrinsecis Mentis vel Naturae.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_QGOH_PQ_3:P0027","trace_type":"Direct","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"},{"id":"RAW_QGOH_PQ_3:P0028","trace_type":"Direct","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_QGOH_PQ_3:A9","text":"Utile est quod Corpus aptius reddit ad pluribus modis afficiendum, alia corpora afficiendum et Mentem ad plura percipiendum.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_QGOH_PQ_3:P0030","trace_type":"Direct","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"},{"id":"RAW_QGOH_PQ_3:P0032","trace_type":"Direct","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_QGOH_PQ_3:A10","text":"Forma Corporis in certa ratione motus et quietis consistit; eius conservatio capacitatem corporis servat, eius mutatio destructionem inducit.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_QGOH_PQ_3:P0034","trace_type":"Direct","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"},{"id":"RAW_QGOH_PQ_3:P0036","trace_type":"Direct","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"},{"id":"RAW_QGOH_PQ_3:P0037","trace_type":"Direct","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"},{"id":"RAW_QGOH_PQ_3:P0039","trace_type":"Direct","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_QGOH_PQ_3:A11","text":"Bonitas affectuum per augmentum vel diminutionem potentiae totius Corporis, et per proportionem qua partes Corporis afficiuntur, dijudicatur.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_QGOH_PQ_3:P0048","trace_type":"Composite","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"},{"id":"RAW_QGOH_PQ_3:P0052","trace_type":"Composite","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"},{"id":"RAW_QGOH_PQ_3:P0054","trace_type":"Composite","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"},{"id":"RAW_QGOH_PQ_3:P0056","trace_type":"Composite","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_QGOH_PQ_3:A12","text":"Odium erga homines destructionem appetit atque malum est; Amor sive Generositas Odium extinguere et in contrarium affectum convertere potest.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_QGOH_PQ_3:P0066","trace_type":"Composite","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"},{"id":"RAW_QGOH_PQ_3:P0068","trace_type":"Composite","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"},{"id":"RAW_QGOH_PQ_3:P0079","trace_type":"Composite","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"},{"id":"RAW_QGOH_PQ_3:P0081","trace_type":"Composite","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_QGOH_PQ_3:A13","text":"Spes et Metus Tristitiam ac cognitionis defectum involvunt, atque per se boni esse non possunt.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_QGOH_PQ_3:P0085","trace_type":"Direct","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"},{"id":"RAW_QGOH_PQ_3:P0087","trace_type":"Direct","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_QGOH_PQ_3:A14","text":"Existimatio et Despectus Rationi repugnant, et Existimatio facile Superbiam generat.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_QGOH_PQ_3:P0091","trace_type":"Composite","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"},{"id":"RAW_QGOH_PQ_3:P0093","trace_type":"Composite","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"},{"id":"RAW_QGOH_PQ_3:P0095","trace_type":"Composite","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"},{"id":"RAW_QGOH_PQ_3:P0097","trace_type":"Composite","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_QGOH_PQ_3:A15","text":"Reciprocitas ethica ex convenientia naturae humanae procedit; bruta, natura et affectibus a hominibus diversa, in eandem communitatem amicitiae non includuntur.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_QGOH_PQ_3:P0021","trace_type":"Direct","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]}],"original_name":"Load-Bearing Axioms"},{"number":"3","name":"Apophatic Structure","items":[{"id":"CH_QGOH_PQ_3:NX1","text":"Utilitas propria rationalis non est egoismus competitivus, quia Rationis ductus homines natura conciliat.","refs":["CH_QGOH_PQ_3:A1","CH_QGOH_PQ_3:A2"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"From","passages":[{"id":"RAW_QGOH_PQ_3:P0001","trace_type":"Negative","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_QGOH_PQ_3:NX2","text":"Summum bonum non est res rara, exclusoria aut unius tantum possessoris capax.","refs":["CH_QGOH_PQ_3:A3"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"From","passages":[{"id":"RAW_QGOH_PQ_3:P0006","trace_type":"Negative","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"},{"id":"RAW_QGOH_PQ_3:P0008","trace_type":"Negative","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_QGOH_PQ_3:NX3","text":"Pietas et Honestitas non sunt conatus affectivus ut reliqui ex proprio ingenio vivent, sed communicatio boni communis per amicitiam.","refs":["CH_QGOH_PQ_3:A4"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"From","passages":[{"id":"RAW_QGOH_PQ_3:P0019","trace_type":"Negative","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"},{"id":"RAW_QGOH_PQ_3:P0020","trace_type":"Negative","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_QGOH_PQ_3:NX4","text":"Vita socialis empirica non praesupponitur iam rationalis, cum homines plerumque Invidia, contrarietate et affectuum imperio teneantur.","refs":["CH_QGOH_PQ_3:A5"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"From","passages":[{"id":"RAW_QGOH_PQ_3:P0003","trace_type":"Negative","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"},{"id":"RAW_QGOH_PQ_3:P0024","trace_type":"Negative","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_QGOH_PQ_3:NX5","text":"Lex civilis non fundatur in sola persuasione rationali, quia Ratio per se affectus coërcere nequit.","refs":["CH_QGOH_PQ_3:A5","CH_QGOH_PQ_3:A7"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"From","passages":[{"id":"RAW_QGOH_PQ_3:P0025","trace_type":"Negative","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"},{"id":"RAW_QGOH_PQ_3:P0026","trace_type":"Negative","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_QGOH_PQ_3:NX6","text":"In statu naturali non dantur peccatum, meritum, justum, injustum neque proprietas ex consensu definita.","refs":["CH_QGOH_PQ_3:A6","CH_QGOH_PQ_3:A8"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"From","passages":[{"id":"RAW_QGOH_PQ_3:P0024","trace_type":"Negative","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"},{"id":"RAW_QGOH_PQ_3:P0027","trace_type":"Negative","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"},{"id":"RAW_QGOH_PQ_3:P0028","trace_type":"Negative","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_QGOH_PQ_3:NX7","text":"Bonum non coincidit cum qualibet Laetitia, nec malum cum quolibet Dolore, quia affectus localis potest potentiam totius Corporis minuere, et Dolor excessum coercere.","refs":["CH_QGOH_PQ_3:A9","CH_QGOH_PQ_3:A11"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"From","passages":[{"id":"RAW_QGOH_PQ_3:P0048","trace_type":"Negative","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"},{"id":"RAW_QGOH_PQ_3:P0054","trace_type":"Negative","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"},{"id":"RAW_QGOH_PQ_3:P0056","trace_type":"Negative","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_QGOH_PQ_3:NX8","text":"Mors Corporis non restringitur ad cadaver aut ad cessationem sanguinis circulationis, sed intelligitur per mutationem rationis motus et quietis.","refs":["CH_QGOH_PQ_3:A10"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"From","passages":[{"id":"RAW_QGOH_PQ_3:P0039","trace_type":"Negative","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_QGOH_PQ_3:NX9","text":"Risus et jocus non sunt Irrisio, quia illi Laetitiam meram significare possunt, haec autem ad Odium pertinet.","refs":["CH_QGOH_PQ_3:A11","CH_QGOH_PQ_3:A12"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"From","passages":[{"id":"RAW_QGOH_PQ_3:P0072","trace_type":"Negative","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"},{"id":"RAW_QGOH_PQ_3:P0076","trace_type":"Negative","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_QGOH_PQ_3:NX10","text":"Odium non habet usum moralem proprium, quamvis vindicta vel imminutio alterius interdum utilia apparere possint.","refs":["CH_QGOH_PQ_3:A12"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"From","passages":[{"id":"RAW_QGOH_PQ_3:P0066","trace_type":"Negative","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"},{"id":"RAW_QGOH_PQ_3:P0068","trace_type":"Negative","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"},{"id":"RAW_QGOH_PQ_3:P0074","trace_type":"Negative","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_QGOH_PQ_3:NX11","text":"Spes, Metus, Securitas, Desperatio et Conscientiae morsus non sunt formae stabilis virtutis.","refs":["CH_QGOH_PQ_3:A13"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"From","passages":[{"id":"RAW_QGOH_PQ_3:P0085","trace_type":"Negative","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"},{"id":"RAW_QGOH_PQ_3:P0087","trace_type":"Negative","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"},{"id":"RAW_QGOH_PQ_3:P0089","trace_type":"Negative","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_QGOH_PQ_3:NX12","text":"Sentire non sufficit ut bruta participes sint societatis humanae vel amicitiae rationalis.","refs":["CH_QGOH_PQ_3:A15"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"From","passages":[{"id":"RAW_QGOH_PQ_3:P0021","trace_type":"Negative","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]}],"original_name":"Apophatic Structure"},{"number":"4","name":"Derivative Propositions","items":[{"id":"CH_QGOH_PQ_3:D1","text":"Quanto magis unusquisque suum utile ex Ratione quaerit, tanto magis reliquis hominibus utilis efficitur.","refs":["CH_QGOH_PQ_3:A1","CH_QGOH_PQ_3:A2"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"From","passages":[{"id":"RAW_QGOH_PQ_3:P0001","trace_type":"Direct","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_QGOH_PQ_3:D2","text":"Virtus non producit conflictum de summo bono, quia bonum quod quaeritur omnibus communicabile manet.","refs":["CH_QGOH_PQ_3:A3"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"From","passages":[{"id":"RAW_QGOH_PQ_3:P0006","trace_type":"Composite","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"},{"id":"RAW_QGOH_PQ_3:P0010","trace_type":"Composite","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_QGOH_PQ_3:D3","text":"Incrementum cognitionis Dei incrementum cupiditatis producere debet qua reliqui eodem bono fruantur.","refs":["CH_QGOH_PQ_3:A3","CH_QGOH_PQ_3:A4"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"From","passages":[{"id":"RAW_QGOH_PQ_3:P0012","trace_type":"Direct","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"},{"id":"RAW_QGOH_PQ_3:P0014","trace_type":"Direct","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"},{"id":"RAW_QGOH_PQ_3:P0015","trace_type":"Direct","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_QGOH_PQ_3:D4","text":"Conatus rationalis ad alios ducendos humaniter agere debet, quia impositio affectiva proprii ingenii contrarium impetum et odium suscitat.","refs":["CH_QGOH_PQ_3:A4","CH_QGOH_PQ_3:A5"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"From","passages":[{"id":"RAW_QGOH_PQ_3:P0019","trace_type":"Composite","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"},{"id":"RAW_QGOH_PQ_3:P0020","trace_type":"Composite","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_QGOH_PQ_3:D5","text":"Civitas stabilis non simpliciter consensu theoretico, sed per securitatem mutuam et metus majoris damni efficitur.","refs":["CH_QGOH_PQ_3:A5","CH_QGOH_PQ_3:A6","CH_QGOH_PQ_3:A7"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"From","passages":[{"id":"RAW_QGOH_PQ_3:P0024","trace_type":"Direct","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"},{"id":"RAW_QGOH_PQ_3:P0025","trace_type":"Direct","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"},{"id":"RAW_QGOH_PQ_3:P0026","trace_type":"Direct","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_QGOH_PQ_3:D6","text":"Poena peccatum et praemium meritum non detegunt ut res naturales, sed oboedientiam vel inoboedientiam erga ordinem civilem instituunt.","refs":["CH_QGOH_PQ_3:A7","CH_QGOH_PQ_3:A8"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"From","passages":[{"id":"RAW_QGOH_PQ_3:P0026","trace_type":"Direct","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"},{"id":"RAW_QGOH_PQ_3:P0027","trace_type":"Direct","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"},{"id":"RAW_QGOH_PQ_3:P0028","trace_type":"Direct","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_QGOH_PQ_3:D7","text":"Institutum vitae moderatae, quod varietatem alimentorum, recreationum et delectationum admittit, bonum est quatenus integritatem Corporis et multiplicitatem Mentis conservat.","refs":["CH_QGOH_PQ_3:A9","CH_QGOH_PQ_3:A10","CH_QGOH_PQ_3:A11"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"From","passages":[{"id":"RAW_QGOH_PQ_3:P0076","trace_type":"Direct","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"},{"id":"RAW_QGOH_PQ_3:P0077","trace_type":"Direct","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_QGOH_PQ_3:D8","text":"Concordia hominum est utilis quia conditiones vitae ex Ratione conformis producit; discordia mala est quia hanc conditionem destruit.","refs":["CH_QGOH_PQ_3:A2","CH_QGOH_PQ_3:A7"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"From","passages":[{"id":"RAW_QGOH_PQ_3:P0042","trace_type":"Direct","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"},{"id":"RAW_QGOH_PQ_3:P0044","trace_type":"Direct","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_QGOH_PQ_3:D9","text":"Generositas est strategia affectiva rationalis, qua Odium non reciproco Odio augetur sed Amore coercetur.","refs":["CH_QGOH_PQ_3:A5","CH_QGOH_PQ_3:A12"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"From","passages":[{"id":"RAW_QGOH_PQ_3:P0079","trace_type":"Direct","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"},{"id":"RAW_QGOH_PQ_3:P0081","trace_type":"Direct","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_QGOH_PQ_3:D10","text":"Vita ex Ratione tendit ad minuendam dependentiam a Spe, Metu et Fortuna, atque ad dirigendas actiones certo Rationis consilio.","refs":["CH_QGOH_PQ_3:A1","CH_QGOH_PQ_3:A13"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"From","passages":[{"id":"RAW_QGOH_PQ_3:P0089","trace_type":"Direct","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_QGOH_PQ_3:D11","text":"Relatio ad bruta non regitur eadem reciprocitate morali quae inter homines viget, sed potentia, utilitate et diversitate naturae.","refs":["CH_QGOH_PQ_3:A6","CH_QGOH_PQ_3:A15"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"From","passages":[{"id":"RAW_QGOH_PQ_3:P0021","trace_type":"Direct","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]}],"original_name":"Derivative Propositions"},{"number":"5","name":"Super-Derivatives","items":[{"id":"CH_QGOH_PQ_3:SD1","text":"Societas duplex fundamentum habet: normam internam convenientiae rationalis et mechanismum externum coercitionis affectivae.","refs":["CH_QGOH_PQ_3:A2","CH_QGOH_PQ_3:A5","CH_QGOH_PQ_3:A7"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"From","passages":[{"id":"RAW_QGOH_PQ_3:P0001","trace_type":"Structural","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"},{"id":"RAW_QGOH_PQ_3:P0025","trace_type":"Structural","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"},{"id":"RAW_QGOH_PQ_3:P0026","trace_type":"Structural","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_QGOH_PQ_3:SD2","text":"Systema tres ordines normativos distinguit: utile naturale ut potentiae augmentum, bonum rationale ut cognitio communis, et justum civile ut decretum publicum.","refs":["CH_QGOH_PQ_3:A1","CH_QGOH_PQ_3:A3","CH_QGOH_PQ_3:A8","CH_QGOH_PQ_3:A9"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"From","passages":[{"id":"RAW_QGOH_PQ_3:P0024","trace_type":"Structural","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"},{"id":"RAW_QGOH_PQ_3:P0028","trace_type":"Structural","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"},{"id":"RAW_QGOH_PQ_3:P0030","trace_type":"Structural","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_QGOH_PQ_3:SD3","text":"Ethica non ascetica fit, quia conservatio et multiplicatio corporis sunt conditio multiplicationis perceptionum Mentis.","refs":["CH_QGOH_PQ_3:A9","CH_QGOH_PQ_3:A10","CH_QGOH_PQ_3:A11"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"From","passages":[{"id":"RAW_QGOH_PQ_3:P0030","trace_type":"Structural","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"},{"id":"RAW_QGOH_PQ_3:P0036","trace_type":"Structural","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"},{"id":"RAW_QGOH_PQ_3:P0077","trace_type":"Structural","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_QGOH_PQ_3:SD4","text":"Self-preservatio, quamvis ab individuo incipiat, solitariam vitam excludit, quia potentia humana per societatem, auxilium et concordiam augetur.","refs":["CH_QGOH_PQ_3:A1","CH_QGOH_PQ_3:A2","CH_QGOH_PQ_3:A7"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"From","passages":[{"id":"RAW_QGOH_PQ_3:P0001","trace_type":"Structural","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"},{"id":"RAW_QGOH_PQ_3:P0004","trace_type":"Structural","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"},{"id":"RAW_QGOH_PQ_3:P0025","trace_type":"Structural","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_QGOH_PQ_3:SD5","text":"Ratio affectus non simpliciter eliminat, sed contrarios affectus ordinat, praecipue Amorem et Generositatem, ut vires destructivas superet.","refs":["CH_QGOH_PQ_3:A5","CH_QGOH_PQ_3:A12"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"From","passages":[{"id":"RAW_QGOH_PQ_3:P0025","trace_type":"Composite","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"},{"id":"RAW_QGOH_PQ_3:P0081","trace_type":"Composite","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_QGOH_PQ_3:SD6","text":"Identitas corporis non in sola apparentia vitae, memoria vel circulatione sanguinis, sed in perseverantia relationis structurae corporalis ponitur.","refs":["CH_QGOH_PQ_3:A10"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"From","passages":[{"id":"RAW_QGOH_PQ_3:P0039","trace_type":"Direct","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"},{"id":"RAW_QGOH_PQ_3:P0040","trace_type":"Direct","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]}],"original_name":"Super-Derivatives"},{"number":"6","name":"Deep Apophatics","items":[{"id":"CH_QGOH_PQ_3:DNX1","text":"Argumentatio tacite requirit bonum rationalem non esse structuram rivalitatis; aliter utilitas propria et utilitas mutua non coinciderent.","refs":["CH_QGOH_PQ_3:A2","CH_QGOH_PQ_3:A3"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"From","passages":[{"id":"RAW_QGOH_PQ_3:P0006","trace_type":"Negative","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"},{"id":"RAW_QGOH_PQ_3:P0010","trace_type":"Negative","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_QGOH_PQ_3:DNX2","text":"Argumentatio negat Intellectum nudum sufficientem esse ad regimen vitae affectivae; actio rationalis vim affectivam contrariam assumere debet.","refs":["CH_QGOH_PQ_3:A5","CH_QGOH_PQ_3:A12"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"From","passages":[{"id":"RAW_QGOH_PQ_3:P0025","trace_type":"Negative","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"},{"id":"RAW_QGOH_PQ_3:P0081","trace_type":"Negative","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_QGOH_PQ_3:DNX3","text":"Argumentatio excludit ordinem juridicum aut moralem naturaliter datum ante Civitatem, quia culpa, meritum, dominium et justitia institutionem publicam postulant.","refs":["CH_QGOH_PQ_3:A6","CH_QGOH_PQ_3:A7","CH_QGOH_PQ_3:A8"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"From","passages":[{"id":"RAW_QGOH_PQ_3:P0024","trace_type":"Negative","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"},{"id":"RAW_QGOH_PQ_3:P0027","trace_type":"Negative","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"},{"id":"RAW_QGOH_PQ_3:P0028","trace_type":"Negative","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_QGOH_PQ_3:DNX4","text":"Argumentatio excludit aestimationem affectuum per sola nomina positiva vel negativa, quia Laetitia localis nocere et Dolor limitate prodesse potest.","refs":["CH_QGOH_PQ_3:A9","CH_QGOH_PQ_3:A11"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"From","passages":[{"id":"RAW_QGOH_PQ_3:P0048","trace_type":"Negative","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"},{"id":"RAW_QGOH_PQ_3:P0054","trace_type":"Negative","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"},{"id":"RAW_QGOH_PQ_3:P0056","trace_type":"Negative","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_QGOH_PQ_3:DNX5","text":"Argumentatio excludit universalem communionem omnium viventium sentientium, quia convenientia naturae humanae, non sola sensibilitas, fundamentum relationis ethicae constituit.","refs":["CH_QGOH_PQ_3:A15"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"From","passages":[{"id":"RAW_QGOH_PQ_3:P0021","trace_type":"Negative","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_QGOH_PQ_3:DNX6","text":"Argumentatio excludit identitatem corporis absolute permanentem, quia mutatio relationis constitutivae corpus in naturam aliam transferre potest.","refs":["CH_QGOH_PQ_3:A10"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"From","passages":[{"id":"RAW_QGOH_PQ_3:P0039","trace_type":"Negative","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"},{"id":"RAW_QGOH_PQ_3:P0040","trace_type":"Negative","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]}],"original_name":"Deep Apophatics"},{"number":"7","name":"Super-Apophatics","items":[{"id":"CH_QGOH_PQ_3:SNX1","text":"Nulla justitia naturalis transcendentis ordinis admittitur inter jus ut potentiam et jus ut decretum Civitatis.","refs":["CH_QGOH_PQ_3:A6","CH_QGOH_PQ_3:A8"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"From","passages":[{"id":"RAW_QGOH_PQ_3:P0024","trace_type":"Negative","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"},{"id":"RAW_QGOH_PQ_3:P0028","trace_type":"Negative","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_QGOH_PQ_3:SNX2","text":"Nulla Civitas stabilis ex sola illuminatione rationali supponitur, quia affectuum potentia institutionem coactivam necessario interponit.","refs":["CH_QGOH_PQ_3:A5","CH_QGOH_PQ_3:A7"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"From","passages":[{"id":"RAW_QGOH_PQ_3:P0025","trace_type":"Negative","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"},{"id":"RAW_QGOH_PQ_3:P0026","trace_type":"Negative","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_QGOH_PQ_3:SNX3","text":"Nulla universalitas ethica indifferens ad differentias naturae ponitur, quia communitas boni et amicitiae ad convenientiam humanam limitatur.","refs":["CH_QGOH_PQ_3:A3","CH_QGOH_PQ_3:A15"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"From","passages":[{"id":"RAW_QGOH_PQ_3:P0008","trace_type":"Negative","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"},{"id":"RAW_QGOH_PQ_3:P0021","trace_type":"Negative","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_QGOH_PQ_3:SNX4","text":"Nulla definitio mortis per solum signum biologicum admittitur, quia destructio dicitur mutatio formae relationis corporalis.","refs":["CH_QGOH_PQ_3:A10"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"From","passages":[{"id":"RAW_QGOH_PQ_3:P0039","trace_type":"Negative","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]}],"original_name":"Super-Apophatics"},{"number":"8","name":"Formal Generative Schema","items":[{"id":"CH_QGOH_PQ_3:FGS1","text":"Conatus propriae utilitatis → Virtus sive potentia rationalis → Convenientia naturae humanae → Utilitas reciproca → Cognitio Dei communis → Cupiditas ut omnes eodem bono fruantur → Pietas et Honestitas.","refs":["CH_QGOH_PQ_3:A1","CH_QGOH_PQ_3:A2","CH_QGOH_PQ_3:A3","CH_QGOH_PQ_3:A4"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"From","passages":[{"id":"RAW_QGOH_PQ_3:P0001","trace_type":"Structural","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"},{"id":"RAW_QGOH_PQ_3:P0008","trace_type":"Structural","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"},{"id":"RAW_QGOH_PQ_3:P0012","trace_type":"Structural","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"},{"id":"RAW_QGOH_PQ_3:P0019","trace_type":"Structural","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"},{"id":"RAW_QGOH_PQ_3:P0020","trace_type":"Structural","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_QGOH_PQ_3:FGS2","text":"Heteronomia affectuum → Contrarietas inter homines mutuo indigentes → Necessitas securitatis → Cessio juris naturalis → Potestas communis → Leges minis firmatae → Civitas, Cives et normae juridicae.","refs":["CH_QGOH_PQ_3:A5","CH_QGOH_PQ_3:A6","CH_QGOH_PQ_3:A7","CH_QGOH_PQ_3:A8"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"From","passages":[{"id":"RAW_QGOH_PQ_3:P0024","trace_type":"Structural","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"},{"id":"RAW_QGOH_PQ_3:P0025","trace_type":"Structural","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"},{"id":"RAW_QGOH_PQ_3:P0026","trace_type":"Structural","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"},{"id":"RAW_QGOH_PQ_3:P0027","trace_type":"Structural","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_QGOH_PQ_3:FGS3","text":"Ratio motus et quietis → Integritas formae corporalis → Multiplex aptitudo ad afficiendum et afficiendum → Amplior perceptio Mentis → Discrimen affectuum bonorum et malorum → Moderatio, Laetitia integra, Amor et Generositas.","refs":["CH_QGOH_PQ_3:A9","CH_QGOH_PQ_3:A10","CH_QGOH_PQ_3:A11","CH_QGOH_PQ_3:A12"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"From","passages":[{"id":"RAW_QGOH_PQ_3:P0030","trace_type":"Structural","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"},{"id":"RAW_QGOH_PQ_3:P0034","trace_type":"Structural","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"},{"id":"RAW_QGOH_PQ_3:P0048","trace_type":"Structural","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"},{"id":"RAW_QGOH_PQ_3:P0077","trace_type":"Structural","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"},{"id":"RAW_QGOH_PQ_3:P0081","trace_type":"Structural","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_QGOH_PQ_3:FGS4","text":"Convenientia naturae humanae → Amicitia et reciprocitas → Communitas rationalis; diversitas naturae brutorum → Relatio instrumentaliter utilitatis, non societas ethica.","refs":["CH_QGOH_PQ_3:A2","CH_QGOH_PQ_3:A15"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"From","passages":[{"id":"RAW_QGOH_PQ_3:P0001","trace_type":"Composite","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"},{"id":"RAW_QGOH_PQ_3:P0021","trace_type":"Composite","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]}],"original_name":"Formal Generative Schema"},{"number":"9","name":"Argument Flow Map","items":[{"id":"CH_QGOH_PQ_3:AF1","text":"Utilitas propria rationalis → Virtus → Convenientia humana → Bonum commune non-rivale → Communicatio cognitionis Dei → Amicitia et Generositas.","refs":["CH_QGOH_PQ_3:A1","CH_QGOH_PQ_3:A2","CH_QGOH_PQ_3:A3","CH_QGOH_PQ_3:A4"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"From","passages":[{"id":"RAW_QGOH_PQ_3:P0001","trace_type":"Structural","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"},{"id":"RAW_QGOH_PQ_3:P0008","trace_type":"Structural","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"},{"id":"RAW_QGOH_PQ_3:P0012","trace_type":"Structural","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"},{"id":"RAW_QGOH_PQ_3:P0019","trace_type":"Structural","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_QGOH_PQ_3:AF2","text":"Imperium affectuum → Insecuritas et conflictus → Cessio juris → Coercitio communis → Civitas → Peccatum, meritum, justum et injustum ut effectus juridici.","refs":["CH_QGOH_PQ_3:A5","CH_QGOH_PQ_3:A6","CH_QGOH_PQ_3:A7","CH_QGOH_PQ_3:A8"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"From","passages":[{"id":"RAW_QGOH_PQ_3:P0024","trace_type":"Structural","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"},{"id":"RAW_QGOH_PQ_3:P0025","trace_type":"Structural","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"},{"id":"RAW_QGOH_PQ_3:P0026","trace_type":"Structural","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"},{"id":"RAW_QGOH_PQ_3:P0027","trace_type":"Structural","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_QGOH_PQ_3:AF3","text":"Conservatio formae corporis → Augmentum capacitatis → Laetitia ordinata → Reiectio Odii et affectuum impotentiae → Amor, moderatio et actio ex consilio Rationis.","refs":["CH_QGOH_PQ_3:A9","CH_QGOH_PQ_3:A10","CH_QGOH_PQ_3:A11","CH_QGOH_PQ_3:A12","CH_QGOH_PQ_3:A13"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"From","passages":[{"id":"RAW_QGOH_PQ_3:P0034","trace_type":"Structural","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"},{"id":"RAW_QGOH_PQ_3:P0048","trace_type":"Structural","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"},{"id":"RAW_QGOH_PQ_3:P0068","trace_type":"Structural","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"},{"id":"RAW_QGOH_PQ_3:P0079","trace_type":"Structural","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"},{"id":"RAW_QGOH_PQ_3:P0089","trace_type":"Structural","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]}],"original_name":"Argument Flow Map"},{"number":"10","name":"Map-Effects","items":[{"id":"CH_QGOH_PQ_3:ME1","text":"Oppositio inter utilitatem propriam et utilitatem communem dissolvitur pro hominibus quatenus Ratione ducuntur.","refs":["CH_QGOH_PQ_3:A1","CH_QGOH_PQ_3:A2"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"From","passages":[{"id":"RAW_QGOH_PQ_3:P0001","trace_type":"Direct","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_QGOH_PQ_3:ME2","text":"Summum bonum reclassificatur ut bonum communicabile, non ut obiectum possessionis exclusoriae.","refs":["CH_QGOH_PQ_3:A3"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"From","passages":[{"id":"RAW_QGOH_PQ_3:P0006","trace_type":"Direct","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"},{"id":"RAW_QGOH_PQ_3:P0008","trace_type":"Direct","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_QGOH_PQ_3:ME3","text":"Distinctio constituitur inter ductum rationalem aliorum et imperium affectivum quo quis reliquos ad proprium ingenium cogere conatur.","refs":["CH_QGOH_PQ_3:A4","CH_QGOH_PQ_3:A5"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"From","passages":[{"id":"RAW_QGOH_PQ_3:P0019","trace_type":"Direct","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"},{"id":"RAW_QGOH_PQ_3:P0020","trace_type":"Direct","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_QGOH_PQ_3:ME4","text":"Civitas describitur non ut simplex expressio moralis consensus, sed ut machina securitatis quae categorias juridicas efficit.","refs":["CH_QGOH_PQ_3:A6","CH_QGOH_PQ_3:A7","CH_QGOH_PQ_3:A8"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"From","passages":[{"id":"RAW_QGOH_PQ_3:P0026","trace_type":"Direct","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"},{"id":"RAW_QGOH_PQ_3:P0027","trace_type":"Direct","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"},{"id":"RAW_QGOH_PQ_3:P0028","trace_type":"Direct","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_QGOH_PQ_3:ME5","text":"Affectus ordinantur non secundum nomen, sed secundum effectum in potentiam integratam Corporis, aptitudinem Mentis et concordiam socialem.","refs":["CH_QGOH_PQ_3:A9","CH_QGOH_PQ_3:A10","CH_QGOH_PQ_3:A11"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"From","passages":[{"id":"RAW_QGOH_PQ_3:P0030","trace_type":"Composite","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"},{"id":"RAW_QGOH_PQ_3:P0048","trace_type":"Composite","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"},{"id":"RAW_QGOH_PQ_3:P0056","trace_type":"Composite","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_QGOH_PQ_3:ME6","text":"Vita bona reclassificatur ut moderata multiplicatio corporalis et mentalis capacitatis, non ut fuga delectationis vel cultus Tristitiae.","refs":["CH_QGOH_PQ_3:A9","CH_QGOH_PQ_3:A11"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"From","passages":[{"id":"RAW_QGOH_PQ_3:P0076","trace_type":"Direct","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"},{"id":"RAW_QGOH_PQ_3:P0077","trace_type":"Direct","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_QGOH_PQ_3:ME7","text":"Limes societatis ethicae inter homines et bruta collocatur in convenientia naturae et reciprocitate, non in sola facultate sentiendi.","refs":["CH_QGOH_PQ_3:A15"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"From","passages":[{"id":"RAW_QGOH_PQ_3:P0021","trace_type":"Direct","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]}],"original_name":"Map-Effects"},{"number":"11","name":"Altitude Levels","items":[{"id":"CH_QGOH_PQ_3:ALT1","text":"Surface — Textus bonum et malum, Laetitiam et Tristitiam, Odium et Amorem, Spem et Metum, justum et injustum formaliter distinguit.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_QGOH_PQ_3:P0046","trace_type":"Direct","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"},{"id":"RAW_QGOH_PQ_3:P0066","trace_type":"Direct","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"},{"id":"RAW_QGOH_PQ_3:P0085","trace_type":"Direct","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"},{"id":"RAW_QGOH_PQ_3:P0091","trace_type":"Direct","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_QGOH_PQ_3:ALT2","text":"Structural — Corpus per rationem motus et quietis, affectus per potentiam, et Civitas per securitatem atque minas describuntur.","refs":["CH_QGOH_PQ_3:A5","CH_QGOH_PQ_3:A7","CH_QGOH_PQ_3:A10"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"From","passages":[{"id":"RAW_QGOH_PQ_3:P0026","trace_type":"Structural","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"},{"id":"RAW_QGOH_PQ_3:P0034","trace_type":"Structural","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"},{"id":"RAW_QGOH_PQ_3:P0048","trace_type":"Structural","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_QGOH_PQ_3:ALT3","text":"Metastrategic — Ratio non solum veritatem cognoscit, sed affectuum regimen per Generositatem, institutionem civilem et ordinatam vitae moderationem constituit.","refs":["CH_QGOH_PQ_3:A4","CH_QGOH_PQ_3:A5","CH_QGOH_PQ_3:A12"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"From","passages":[{"id":"RAW_QGOH_PQ_3:P0019","trace_type":"Structural","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"},{"id":"RAW_QGOH_PQ_3:P0026","trace_type":"Structural","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"},{"id":"RAW_QGOH_PQ_3:P0079","trace_type":"Structural","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"},{"id":"RAW_QGOH_PQ_3:P0077","trace_type":"Structural","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_QGOH_PQ_3:ALT4","text":"Ontological/Temporal — Jus cum potentia, identitas corporis cum relatione structurae, et normae civiles cum institutione temporali Civitatis conectuntur.","refs":["CH_QGOH_PQ_3:A6","CH_QGOH_PQ_3:A8","CH_QGOH_PQ_3:A10"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"From","passages":[{"id":"RAW_QGOH_PQ_3:P0024","trace_type":"Structural","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"},{"id":"RAW_QGOH_PQ_3:P0028","trace_type":"Structural","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"},{"id":"RAW_QGOH_PQ_3:P0039","trace_type":"Structural","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]}],"original_name":"Altitude Levels"},{"number":"12","name":"Minimal Reconstruction Test","items":[{"id":"CH_QGOH_PQ_3:MRT1","text":"Axiomata sufficiunt ad restituendum transitum a convenientia rationali ad bonum commune, et ab affectuum conflictu ad necessitatem Civitatis.","refs":["CH_QGOH_PQ_3:A2","CH_QGOH_PQ_3:A3","CH_QGOH_PQ_3:A5","CH_QGOH_PQ_3:A7"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"From","passages":[{"id":"RAW_QGOH_PQ_3:P0001","trace_type":"Structural","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"},{"id":"RAW_QGOH_PQ_3:P0008","trace_type":"Structural","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"},{"id":"RAW_QGOH_PQ_3:P0024","trace_type":"Structural","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"},{"id":"RAW_QGOH_PQ_3:P0026","trace_type":"Structural","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_QGOH_PQ_3:MRT2","text":"Cardo firmissimus systematis est nexus inter bonum commune non-rivale et cupiditatem rationalem qua idem bonum reliquis communicatur.","refs":["CH_QGOH_PQ_3:A3","CH_QGOH_PQ_3:A4"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"From","passages":[{"id":"RAW_QGOH_PQ_3:P0006","trace_type":"Direct","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"},{"id":"RAW_QGOH_PQ_3:P0012","trace_type":"Direct","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_QGOH_PQ_3:MRT3","text":"Commissura fragilis manet inter Civitatem per minas firmatam et concordiam quae ex vita rationali definita est, quia coactio securitatem explicat sed rationem viventem non plene producit.","refs":["CH_QGOH_PQ_3:A2","CH_QGOH_PQ_3:A5","CH_QGOH_PQ_3:A7"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"From","passages":[{"id":"RAW_QGOH_PQ_3:P0001","trace_type":"Tension","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"},{"id":"RAW_QGOH_PQ_3:P0025","trace_type":"Tension","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"},{"id":"RAW_QGOH_PQ_3:P0026","trace_type":"Tension","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_QGOH_PQ_3:MRT4","text":"Systema postulat consensum vel cessionem juris naturalis, sed mechanismum quo singuli affectibus dominati hanc cessionem primitus stabilem efficiant non ulterius deducit.","refs":["CH_QGOH_PQ_3:A5","CH_QGOH_PQ_3:A6","CH_QGOH_PQ_3:A7"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"From","passages":[]},{"id":"CH_QGOH_PQ_3:MRT5","text":"Tensionem continet duplex normativitas: bonum naturale per potentiam definitur, dum justum et injustum sola institutione civili constituuntur.","refs":["CH_QGOH_PQ_3:A8","CH_QGOH_PQ_3:A9"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"From","passages":[{"id":"RAW_QGOH_PQ_3:P0024","trace_type":"Tension","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"},{"id":"RAW_QGOH_PQ_3:P0028","trace_type":"Tension","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"},{"id":"RAW_QGOH_PQ_3:P0030","trace_type":"Tension","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_QGOH_PQ_3:MRT6","text":"Limes inter homines et bruta structuram systematis servat, sed convenientiam naturae humanae tamquam condicionem decisivam assumit potius quam in hoc loco ulterius demonstrat.","refs":["CH_QGOH_PQ_3:A15"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"From","passages":[]},{"id":"CH_QGOH_PQ_3:MRT7","text":"Criterium mortis per mutationem rationis motus et quietis systematice cohaeret, sed gradum determinatum quo mutatio corpus in naturam aliam transfert indeterminatum relinquit.","refs":["CH_QGOH_PQ_3:A10"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"From","passages":[]},{"id":"CH_QGOH_PQ_3:MRT8","text":"Restitutio motum textus servat, si ordo duplicis machinae retinetur: Ratio bonum commune demonstrat, affectus autem necessitatem institutionis et contrarii affectus imponunt.","refs":["CH_QGOH_PQ_3:FGS1","CH_QGOH_PQ_3:FGS2","CH_QGOH_PQ_3:FGS3"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"From","passages":[{"id":"RAW_QGOH_PQ_3:P0001","trace_type":"Structural","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"},{"id":"RAW_QGOH_PQ_3:P0026","trace_type":"Structural","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"},{"id":"RAW_QGOH_PQ_3:P0034","trace_type":"Structural","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"},{"id":"RAW_QGOH_PQ_3:P0081","trace_type":"Structural","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]}],"original_name":"Minimal Reconstruction Test"}],"language_code":"la","language_name":"Latina","translations":[{"id":"CAREVAR_20261015T014740099398Z_bf891d6b","language_code":"en","language_name":"English","title":"Part Four - On Human Bondage, or the Powers of the Affects · passage 3","source_sha256":"fd0ed80268fe33a44007de91db4f998bfc7a345bad3494bbcadef78c63e82034","sha256":"db562474022462aa75fce2c13d1f30821436db52b065638b634a541e0f0b142b","rendition_type":"machine","review_status":"machine","model":"gpt-5.6-terra","created":"2026-10-15T01:47:40.101471+00:00","sections":[{"number":"1","name":"Argumental Spine","items":[{"id":"CH_QGOH_PQ_3:AS1","text":"The text asks how each person's striving to seek his own advantage, which may appear as a form of conflictual egoism, is transformed into mutual advantage and human society.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_QGOH_PQ_3:P0001","trace_type":"Structural","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"},{"id":"RAW_QGOH_PQ_3:P0003","trace_type":"Structural","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_QGOH_PQ_3:AS2","text":"The first solution posits that human beings, insofar as they are guided by Reason, agree by nature; hence virtue, self-advantage, mutual advantage, and the common good are ordered in the same chain.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_QGOH_PQ_3:P0001","trace_type":"Composite","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"},{"id":"RAW_QGOH_PQ_3:P0006","trace_type":"Composite","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"},{"id":"RAW_QGOH_PQ_3:P0012","trace_type":"Composite","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_QGOH_PQ_3:AS3","text":"The text then shows that this rational agreement is not an empirically dominant condition, because affects render human beings external, inconstant, and opposed to one another.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_QGOH_PQ_3:P0003","trace_type":"Tension","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"},{"id":"RAW_QGOH_PQ_3:P0024","trace_type":"Tension","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_QGOH_PQ_3:AS4","text":"From this discrepancy between the norm of Reason and the affective condition, the State is generated as an apparatus of security, surrender of right, common law, and coercion through threats.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_QGOH_PQ_3:P0024","trace_type":"Structural","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"},{"id":"RAW_QGOH_PQ_3:P0025","trace_type":"Structural","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"},{"id":"RAW_QGOH_PQ_3:P0026","trace_type":"Structural","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_QGOH_PQ_3:AS5","text":"After the civil institution, good and evil are referred to the power of body and mind, and affects are judged by their effect upon the capacity to act, concord, and stability.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_QGOH_PQ_3:P0030","trace_type":"Structural","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"},{"id":"RAW_QGOH_PQ_3:P0034","trace_type":"Structural","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"},{"id":"RAW_QGOH_PQ_3:P0042","trace_type":"Structural","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"},{"id":"RAW_QGOH_PQ_3:P0048","trace_type":"Structural","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_QGOH_PQ_3:AS6","text":"The argument proceeds demonstratively and reconstructively from rational agreement to civil necessity, and from the constitution of the body to a strategy of affects through love, generosity, and liberation from fear.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_QGOH_PQ_3:P0001","trace_type":"Structural","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"},{"id":"RAW_QGOH_PQ_3:P0024","trace_type":"Structural","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"},{"id":"RAW_QGOH_PQ_3:P0036","trace_type":"Structural","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"},{"id":"RAW_QGOH_PQ_3:P0079","trace_type":"Structural","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"},{"id":"RAW_QGOH_PQ_3:P0089","trace_type":"Structural","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]}],"original_name":"Argumental Spine"},{"number":"2","name":"Load-Bearing Axioms","items":[{"id":"CH_QGOH_PQ_3:A1","text":"Virtue is the power of acting under the guidance of Reason, and rationally seeking one's own advantage is preserving oneself according to the laws of one's own nature.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_QGOH_PQ_3:P0001","trace_type":"Direct","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_QGOH_PQ_3:A2","text":"Human beings, insofar as they live under the guidance of Reason, agree by nature; therefore advantage rationally sought increases mutual advantage.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_QGOH_PQ_3:P0001","trace_type":"Direct","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_QGOH_PQ_3:A3","text":"The supreme good of those who pursue virtue is the knowledge of God, common to all human beings and to be possessed equally by all.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_QGOH_PQ_3:P0006","trace_type":"Direct","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"},{"id":"RAW_QGOH_PQ_3:P0008","trace_type":"Direct","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_QGOH_PQ_3:A4","text":"The desire by which a rational person seeks the supreme good for himself desires the same good for others, and this desire increases with the knowledge of God.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_QGOH_PQ_3:P0012","trace_type":"Direct","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"},{"id":"RAW_QGOH_PQ_3:P0014","trace_type":"Direct","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"},{"id":"RAW_QGOH_PQ_3:P0015","trace_type":"Direct","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_QGOH_PQ_3:A5","text":"Human beings are for the most part acted upon by stronger external affects, and an affect is restrained only by a stronger and contrary affect; Reason alone cannot restrain affects.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_QGOH_PQ_3:P0024","trace_type":"Direct","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"},{"id":"RAW_QGOH_PQ_3:P0025","trace_type":"Direct","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_QGOH_PQ_3:A6","text":"The natural right of each person is defined by his own power, so that in the state of nature each individual decides, according to his own disposition, concerning good, evil, advantage, revenge, and preservation.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_QGOH_PQ_3:P0024","trace_type":"Direct","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_QGOH_PQ_3:A7","text":"Civil concord requires the mutual surrender of natural right and a common power that makes laws and upholds them through threats.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_QGOH_PQ_3:P0025","trace_type":"Direct","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"},{"id":"RAW_QGOH_PQ_3:P0026","trace_type":"Direct","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_QGOH_PQ_3:A8","text":"Sin, merit, justice, injustice, and ownership arise from the consent and right of the State, not from intrinsic attributes of Mind or Nature.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_QGOH_PQ_3:P0027","trace_type":"Direct","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"},{"id":"RAW_QGOH_PQ_3:P0028","trace_type":"Direct","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_QGOH_PQ_3:A9","text":"Useful is that which renders the Body more apt to be affected in many ways, to affect other bodies, and the Mind to perceive more things.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_QGOH_PQ_3:P0030","trace_type":"Direct","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"},{"id":"RAW_QGOH_PQ_3:P0032","trace_type":"Direct","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_QGOH_PQ_3:A10","text":"The form of the Body consists in a certain ratio of motion and rest; its preservation preserves the body's capacity, while its alteration induces destruction.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_QGOH_PQ_3:P0034","trace_type":"Direct","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"},{"id":"RAW_QGOH_PQ_3:P0036","trace_type":"Direct","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"},{"id":"RAW_QGOH_PQ_3:P0037","trace_type":"Direct","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"},{"id":"RAW_QGOH_PQ_3:P0039","trace_type":"Direct","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_QGOH_PQ_3:A11","text":"The goodness of affects is judged by the increase or decrease of the power of the whole Body, and by the proportion in which the parts of the Body are affected.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_QGOH_PQ_3:P0048","trace_type":"Composite","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"},{"id":"RAW_QGOH_PQ_3:P0052","trace_type":"Composite","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"},{"id":"RAW_QGOH_PQ_3:P0054","trace_type":"Composite","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"},{"id":"RAW_QGOH_PQ_3:P0056","trace_type":"Composite","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_QGOH_PQ_3:A12","text":"Hatred toward human beings seeks destruction and is evil; Love or Generosity can extinguish Hatred and transform it into a contrary affect.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_QGOH_PQ_3:P0066","trace_type":"Composite","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"},{"id":"RAW_QGOH_PQ_3:P0068","trace_type":"Composite","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"},{"id":"RAW_QGOH_PQ_3:P0079","trace_type":"Composite","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"},{"id":"RAW_QGOH_PQ_3:P0081","trace_type":"Composite","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_QGOH_PQ_3:A13","text":"Hope and Fear involve Sadness and a defect of knowledge, and cannot in themselves be good.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_QGOH_PQ_3:P0085","trace_type":"Direct","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"},{"id":"RAW_QGOH_PQ_3:P0087","trace_type":"Direct","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_QGOH_PQ_3:A14","text":"Esteem and Contempt are contrary to Reason, and Esteem easily produces Pride.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_QGOH_PQ_3:P0091","trace_type":"Composite","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"},{"id":"RAW_QGOH_PQ_3:P0093","trace_type":"Composite","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"},{"id":"RAW_QGOH_PQ_3:P0095","trace_type":"Composite","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"},{"id":"RAW_QGOH_PQ_3:P0097","trace_type":"Composite","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_QGOH_PQ_3:A15","text":"Ethical reciprocity proceeds from the agreement of human nature; beasts, differing from human beings in nature and affects, are not included in the same community of friendship.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_QGOH_PQ_3:P0021","trace_type":"Direct","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]}],"original_name":"Load-Bearing Axioms"},{"number":"3","name":"Apophatic Structure","items":[{"id":"CH_QGOH_PQ_3:NX1","text":"Rational self-advantage is not competitive egoism, because the guidance of Reason reconciles human beings by nature.","refs":["CH_QGOH_PQ_3:A1","CH_QGOH_PQ_3:A2"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"From","passages":[{"id":"RAW_QGOH_PQ_3:P0001","trace_type":"Negative","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_QGOH_PQ_3:NX2","text":"The supreme good is not a rare or exclusionary thing, or one capable of having only a single possessor.","refs":["CH_QGOH_PQ_3:A3"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"From","passages":[{"id":"RAW_QGOH_PQ_3:P0006","trace_type":"Negative","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"},{"id":"RAW_QGOH_PQ_3:P0008","trace_type":"Negative","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_QGOH_PQ_3:NX3","text":"Piety and Honesty are not an affective striving that others should live according to their own disposition, but the communication of the common good through friendship.","refs":["CH_QGOH_PQ_3:A4"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"From","passages":[{"id":"RAW_QGOH_PQ_3:P0019","trace_type":"Negative","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"},{"id":"RAW_QGOH_PQ_3:P0020","trace_type":"Negative","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_QGOH_PQ_3:NX4","text":"Empirical social life is not presupposed to be already rational, since human beings are for the most part held by Envy, contrariety, and the dominion of affects.","refs":["CH_QGOH_PQ_3:A5"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"From","passages":[{"id":"RAW_QGOH_PQ_3:P0003","trace_type":"Negative","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"},{"id":"RAW_QGOH_PQ_3:P0024","trace_type":"Negative","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_QGOH_PQ_3:NX5","text":"Civil law is not founded upon rational persuasion alone, because Reason by itself cannot restrain affects.","refs":["CH_QGOH_PQ_3:A5","CH_QGOH_PQ_3:A7"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"From","passages":[{"id":"RAW_QGOH_PQ_3:P0025","trace_type":"Negative","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"},{"id":"RAW_QGOH_PQ_3:P0026","trace_type":"Negative","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_QGOH_PQ_3:NX6","text":"In the state of nature there are no sin, merit, justice, injustice, or property defined by consent.","refs":["CH_QGOH_PQ_3:A6","CH_QGOH_PQ_3:A8"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"From","passages":[{"id":"RAW_QGOH_PQ_3:P0024","trace_type":"Negative","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"},{"id":"RAW_QGOH_PQ_3:P0027","trace_type":"Negative","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"},{"id":"RAW_QGOH_PQ_3:P0028","trace_type":"Negative","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_QGOH_PQ_3:NX7","text":"Good does not coincide with every Joy, nor evil with every Pain, because a local affect can diminish the power of the whole Body, and Pain can restrain excess.","refs":["CH_QGOH_PQ_3:A9","CH_QGOH_PQ_3:A11"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"From","passages":[{"id":"RAW_QGOH_PQ_3:P0048","trace_type":"Negative","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"},{"id":"RAW_QGOH_PQ_3:P0054","trace_type":"Negative","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"},{"id":"RAW_QGOH_PQ_3:P0056","trace_type":"Negative","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_QGOH_PQ_3:NX8","text":"The death of the Body is not restricted to a corpse or to the cessation of blood circulation, but is understood through a change in the ratio of motion and rest.","refs":["CH_QGOH_PQ_3:A10"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"From","passages":[{"id":"RAW_QGOH_PQ_3:P0039","trace_type":"Negative","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_QGOH_PQ_3:NX9","text":"Laughter and jest are not Mockery, because the former can signify pure Joy, whereas the latter pertains to Hatred.","refs":["CH_QGOH_PQ_3:A11","CH_QGOH_PQ_3:A12"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"From","passages":[{"id":"RAW_QGOH_PQ_3:P0072","trace_type":"Negative","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"},{"id":"RAW_QGOH_PQ_3:P0076","trace_type":"Negative","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_QGOH_PQ_3:NX10","text":"Hatred has no proper moral use, although revenge or the diminution of another may sometimes appear useful.","refs":["CH_QGOH_PQ_3:A12"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"From","passages":[{"id":"RAW_QGOH_PQ_3:P0066","trace_type":"Negative","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"},{"id":"RAW_QGOH_PQ_3:P0068","trace_type":"Negative","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"},{"id":"RAW_QGOH_PQ_3:P0074","trace_type":"Negative","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_QGOH_PQ_3:NX11","text":"Hope, Fear, Security, Despair, and remorse of conscience are not forms of stable virtue.","refs":["CH_QGOH_PQ_3:A13"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"From","passages":[{"id":"RAW_QGOH_PQ_3:P0085","trace_type":"Negative","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"},{"id":"RAW_QGOH_PQ_3:P0087","trace_type":"Negative","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"},{"id":"RAW_QGOH_PQ_3:P0089","trace_type":"Negative","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_QGOH_PQ_3:NX12","text":"Feeling is not sufficient for beasts to be participants in human society or rational friendship.","refs":["CH_QGOH_PQ_3:A15"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"From","passages":[{"id":"RAW_QGOH_PQ_3:P0021","trace_type":"Negative","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]}],"original_name":"Apophatic Structure"},{"number":"4","name":"Derivative Propositions","items":[{"id":"CH_QGOH_PQ_3:D1","text":"The more each person seeks his own advantage from Reason, the more useful he becomes to other human beings.","refs":["CH_QGOH_PQ_3:A1","CH_QGOH_PQ_3:A2"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"From","passages":[{"id":"RAW_QGOH_PQ_3:P0001","trace_type":"Direct","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_QGOH_PQ_3:D2","text":"Virtue does not produce conflict over the supreme good, because the good sought remains communicable to all.","refs":["CH_QGOH_PQ_3:A3"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"From","passages":[{"id":"RAW_QGOH_PQ_3:P0006","trace_type":"Composite","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"},{"id":"RAW_QGOH_PQ_3:P0010","trace_type":"Composite","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_QGOH_PQ_3:D3","text":"An increase in the knowledge of God must produce an increase in the desire that others enjoy the same good.","refs":["CH_QGOH_PQ_3:A3","CH_QGOH_PQ_3:A4"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"From","passages":[{"id":"RAW_QGOH_PQ_3:P0012","trace_type":"Direct","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"},{"id":"RAW_QGOH_PQ_3:P0014","trace_type":"Direct","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"},{"id":"RAW_QGOH_PQ_3:P0015","trace_type":"Direct","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_QGOH_PQ_3:D4","text":"The rational striving to lead others must act humanely, because affective imposition of one's own disposition arouses a contrary impulse and hatred.","refs":["CH_QGOH_PQ_3:A4","CH_QGOH_PQ_3:A5"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"From","passages":[{"id":"RAW_QGOH_PQ_3:P0019","trace_type":"Composite","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"},{"id":"RAW_QGOH_PQ_3:P0020","trace_type":"Composite","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_QGOH_PQ_3:D5","text":"A stable State is effected not simply by theoretical consent, but through mutual security and fear of greater harm.","refs":["CH_QGOH_PQ_3:A5","CH_QGOH_PQ_3:A6","CH_QGOH_PQ_3:A7"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"From","passages":[{"id":"RAW_QGOH_PQ_3:P0024","trace_type":"Direct","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"},{"id":"RAW_QGOH_PQ_3:P0025","trace_type":"Direct","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"},{"id":"RAW_QGOH_PQ_3:P0026","trace_type":"Direct","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_QGOH_PQ_3:D6","text":"Punishment does not disclose sin, nor reward merit, as natural things, but establishes obedience or disobedience toward the civil order.","refs":["CH_QGOH_PQ_3:A7","CH_QGOH_PQ_3:A8"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"From","passages":[{"id":"RAW_QGOH_PQ_3:P0026","trace_type":"Direct","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"},{"id":"RAW_QGOH_PQ_3:P0027","trace_type":"Direct","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"},{"id":"RAW_QGOH_PQ_3:P0028","trace_type":"Direct","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_QGOH_PQ_3:D7","text":"A regimen of moderate life, which admits a variety of foods, recreations, and pleasures, is good insofar as it preserves the integrity of the Body and the multiplicity of the Mind.","refs":["CH_QGOH_PQ_3:A9","CH_QGOH_PQ_3:A10","CH_QGOH_PQ_3:A11"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"From","passages":[{"id":"RAW_QGOH_PQ_3:P0076","trace_type":"Direct","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"},{"id":"RAW_QGOH_PQ_3:P0077","trace_type":"Direct","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_QGOH_PQ_3:D8","text":"Human concord is useful because it produces the conditions of a life conforming to Reason; discord is evil because it destroys this condition.","refs":["CH_QGOH_PQ_3:A2","CH_QGOH_PQ_3:A7"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"From","passages":[{"id":"RAW_QGOH_PQ_3:P0042","trace_type":"Direct","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"},{"id":"RAW_QGOH_PQ_3:P0044","trace_type":"Direct","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_QGOH_PQ_3:D9","text":"Generosity is a rational affective strategy by which Hatred is not increased by reciprocal Hatred but restrained by Love.","refs":["CH_QGOH_PQ_3:A5","CH_QGOH_PQ_3:A12"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"From","passages":[{"id":"RAW_QGOH_PQ_3:P0079","trace_type":"Direct","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"},{"id":"RAW_QGOH_PQ_3:P0081","trace_type":"Direct","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_QGOH_PQ_3:D10","text":"Life according to Reason tends to diminish dependence on Hope, Fear, and Fortune, and to direct actions by the certain counsel of Reason.","refs":["CH_QGOH_PQ_3:A1","CH_QGOH_PQ_3:A13"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"From","passages":[{"id":"RAW_QGOH_PQ_3:P0089","trace_type":"Direct","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_QGOH_PQ_3:D11","text":"The relation to beasts is not governed by the same moral reciprocity that prevails among human beings, but by power, advantage, and diversity of nature.","refs":["CH_QGOH_PQ_3:A6","CH_QGOH_PQ_3:A15"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"From","passages":[{"id":"RAW_QGOH_PQ_3:P0021","trace_type":"Direct","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]}],"original_name":"Derivative Propositions"},{"number":"5","name":"Super-Derivatives","items":[{"id":"CH_QGOH_PQ_3:SD1","text":"Society has a twofold foundation: the internal norm of rational agreement and the external mechanism of affective coercion.","refs":["CH_QGOH_PQ_3:A2","CH_QGOH_PQ_3:A5","CH_QGOH_PQ_3:A7"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"From","passages":[{"id":"RAW_QGOH_PQ_3:P0001","trace_type":"Structural","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"},{"id":"RAW_QGOH_PQ_3:P0025","trace_type":"Structural","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"},{"id":"RAW_QGOH_PQ_3:P0026","trace_type":"Structural","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_QGOH_PQ_3:SD2","text":"The system distinguishes three normative orders: natural usefulness as increase of power, rational good as common knowledge, and civil justice as public decree.","refs":["CH_QGOH_PQ_3:A1","CH_QGOH_PQ_3:A3","CH_QGOH_PQ_3:A8","CH_QGOH_PQ_3:A9"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"From","passages":[{"id":"RAW_QGOH_PQ_3:P0024","trace_type":"Structural","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"},{"id":"RAW_QGOH_PQ_3:P0028","trace_type":"Structural","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"},{"id":"RAW_QGOH_PQ_3:P0030","trace_type":"Structural","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_QGOH_PQ_3:SD3","text":"Ethics becomes non-ascetic, because the preservation and multiplication of the body are conditions for the multiplication of the Mind's perceptions.","refs":["CH_QGOH_PQ_3:A9","CH_QGOH_PQ_3:A10","CH_QGOH_PQ_3:A11"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"From","passages":[{"id":"RAW_QGOH_PQ_3:P0030","trace_type":"Structural","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"},{"id":"RAW_QGOH_PQ_3:P0036","trace_type":"Structural","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"},{"id":"RAW_QGOH_PQ_3:P0077","trace_type":"Structural","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_QGOH_PQ_3:SD4","text":"Self-preservation, although it begins with the individual, excludes a solitary life, because human power is increased through society, assistance, and concord.","refs":["CH_QGOH_PQ_3:A1","CH_QGOH_PQ_3:A2","CH_QGOH_PQ_3:A7"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"From","passages":[{"id":"RAW_QGOH_PQ_3:P0001","trace_type":"Structural","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"},{"id":"RAW_QGOH_PQ_3:P0004","trace_type":"Structural","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"},{"id":"RAW_QGOH_PQ_3:P0025","trace_type":"Structural","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_QGOH_PQ_3:SD5","text":"Reason does not simply eliminate affects, but orders contrary affects, especially Love and Generosity, so as to overcome destructive forces.","refs":["CH_QGOH_PQ_3:A5","CH_QGOH_PQ_3:A12"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"From","passages":[{"id":"RAW_QGOH_PQ_3:P0025","trace_type":"Composite","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"},{"id":"RAW_QGOH_PQ_3:P0081","trace_type":"Composite","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_QGOH_PQ_3:SD6","text":"The identity of the body is located not in the mere appearance of life, memory, or blood circulation, but in the persistence of the relation of bodily structure.","refs":["CH_QGOH_PQ_3:A10"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"From","passages":[{"id":"RAW_QGOH_PQ_3:P0039","trace_type":"Direct","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"},{"id":"RAW_QGOH_PQ_3:P0040","trace_type":"Direct","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]}],"original_name":"Super-Derivatives"},{"number":"6","name":"Deep Apophatics","items":[{"id":"CH_QGOH_PQ_3:DNX1","text":"The argument tacitly requires rational good not to be a structure of rivalry; otherwise self-advantage and mutual advantage would not coincide.","refs":["CH_QGOH_PQ_3:A2","CH_QGOH_PQ_3:A3"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"From","passages":[{"id":"RAW_QGOH_PQ_3:P0006","trace_type":"Negative","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"},{"id":"RAW_QGOH_PQ_3:P0010","trace_type":"Negative","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_QGOH_PQ_3:DNX2","text":"The argument denies that bare Intellect is sufficient for the governance of affective life; rational action must assume a contrary affective force.","refs":["CH_QGOH_PQ_3:A5","CH_QGOH_PQ_3:A12"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"From","passages":[{"id":"RAW_QGOH_PQ_3:P0025","trace_type":"Negative","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"},{"id":"RAW_QGOH_PQ_3:P0081","trace_type":"Negative","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_QGOH_PQ_3:DNX3","text":"The argument excludes a juridical or moral order naturally given prior to the State, because guilt, merit, ownership, and justice require public institution.","refs":["CH_QGOH_PQ_3:A6","CH_QGOH_PQ_3:A7","CH_QGOH_PQ_3:A8"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"From","passages":[{"id":"RAW_QGOH_PQ_3:P0024","trace_type":"Negative","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"},{"id":"RAW_QGOH_PQ_3:P0027","trace_type":"Negative","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"},{"id":"RAW_QGOH_PQ_3:P0028","trace_type":"Negative","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_QGOH_PQ_3:DNX4","text":"The argument excludes the evaluation of affects by positive or negative names alone, because local Joy can harm and Pain can beneficially limit.","refs":["CH_QGOH_PQ_3:A9","CH_QGOH_PQ_3:A11"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"From","passages":[{"id":"RAW_QGOH_PQ_3:P0048","trace_type":"Negative","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"},{"id":"RAW_QGOH_PQ_3:P0054","trace_type":"Negative","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"},{"id":"RAW_QGOH_PQ_3:P0056","trace_type":"Negative","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_QGOH_PQ_3:DNX5","text":"The argument excludes a universal communion of all sentient living beings, because agreement of human nature, not sensibility alone, constitutes the foundation of ethical relation.","refs":["CH_QGOH_PQ_3:A15"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"From","passages":[{"id":"RAW_QGOH_PQ_3:P0021","trace_type":"Negative","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_QGOH_PQ_3:DNX6","text":"The argument excludes an absolutely permanent identity of the body, because a change in its constitutive relation can transfer the body into another nature.","refs":["CH_QGOH_PQ_3:A10"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"From","passages":[{"id":"RAW_QGOH_PQ_3:P0039","trace_type":"Negative","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"},{"id":"RAW_QGOH_PQ_3:P0040","trace_type":"Negative","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]}],"original_name":"Deep Apophatics"},{"number":"7","name":"Super-Apophatics","items":[{"id":"CH_QGOH_PQ_3:SNX1","text":"No natural justice of a transcendent order is admitted between right as power and right as decree of the State.","refs":["CH_QGOH_PQ_3:A6","CH_QGOH_PQ_3:A8"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"From","passages":[{"id":"RAW_QGOH_PQ_3:P0024","trace_type":"Negative","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"},{"id":"RAW_QGOH_PQ_3:P0028","trace_type":"Negative","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_QGOH_PQ_3:SNX2","text":"No stable State is supposed to arise from rational illumination alone, because the power of affects necessarily interposes a coercive institution.","refs":["CH_QGOH_PQ_3:A5","CH_QGOH_PQ_3:A7"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"From","passages":[{"id":"RAW_QGOH_PQ_3:P0025","trace_type":"Negative","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"},{"id":"RAW_QGOH_PQ_3:P0026","trace_type":"Negative","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_QGOH_PQ_3:SNX3","text":"No ethical universality indifferent to differences of nature is posited, because the community of good and friendship is limited to human agreement.","refs":["CH_QGOH_PQ_3:A3","CH_QGOH_PQ_3:A15"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"From","passages":[{"id":"RAW_QGOH_PQ_3:P0008","trace_type":"Negative","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"},{"id":"RAW_QGOH_PQ_3:P0021","trace_type":"Negative","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_QGOH_PQ_3:SNX4","text":"No definition of death through a biological sign alone is admitted, because destruction is said to be a change in the form of bodily relation.","refs":["CH_QGOH_PQ_3:A10"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"From","passages":[{"id":"RAW_QGOH_PQ_3:P0039","trace_type":"Negative","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]}],"original_name":"Super-Apophatics"},{"number":"8","name":"Formal Generative Schema","items":[{"id":"CH_QGOH_PQ_3:FGS1","text":"Striving for one's own advantage → Virtue or rational power → Agreement of human nature → Reciprocal advantage → Common knowledge of God → Desire that all enjoy the same good → Piety and Honesty.","refs":["CH_QGOH_PQ_3:A1","CH_QGOH_PQ_3:A2","CH_QGOH_PQ_3:A3","CH_QGOH_PQ_3:A4"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"From","passages":[{"id":"RAW_QGOH_PQ_3:P0001","trace_type":"Structural","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"},{"id":"RAW_QGOH_PQ_3:P0008","trace_type":"Structural","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"},{"id":"RAW_QGOH_PQ_3:P0012","trace_type":"Structural","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"},{"id":"RAW_QGOH_PQ_3:P0019","trace_type":"Structural","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"},{"id":"RAW_QGOH_PQ_3:P0020","trace_type":"Structural","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_QGOH_PQ_3:FGS2","text":"Heteronomy of affects → Contrariety among mutually needy human beings → Necessity of security → Surrender of natural right → Common power → Laws upheld by threats → State, Citizens, and juridical norms.","refs":["CH_QGOH_PQ_3:A5","CH_QGOH_PQ_3:A6","CH_QGOH_PQ_3:A7","CH_QGOH_PQ_3:A8"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"From","passages":[{"id":"RAW_QGOH_PQ_3:P0024","trace_type":"Structural","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"},{"id":"RAW_QGOH_PQ_3:P0025","trace_type":"Structural","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"},{"id":"RAW_QGOH_PQ_3:P0026","trace_type":"Structural","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"},{"id":"RAW_QGOH_PQ_3:P0027","trace_type":"Structural","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_QGOH_PQ_3:FGS3","text":"Ratio of motion and rest → Integrity of bodily form → Multiple aptitude for affecting and being affected → Broader perception of the Mind → Distinction between good and evil affects → Moderation, integral Joy, Love, and Generosity.","refs":["CH_QGOH_PQ_3:A9","CH_QGOH_PQ_3:A10","CH_QGOH_PQ_3:A11","CH_QGOH_PQ_3:A12"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"From","passages":[{"id":"RAW_QGOH_PQ_3:P0030","trace_type":"Structural","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"},{"id":"RAW_QGOH_PQ_3:P0034","trace_type":"Structural","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"},{"id":"RAW_QGOH_PQ_3:P0048","trace_type":"Structural","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"},{"id":"RAW_QGOH_PQ_3:P0077","trace_type":"Structural","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"},{"id":"RAW_QGOH_PQ_3:P0081","trace_type":"Structural","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_QGOH_PQ_3:FGS4","text":"Agreement of human nature → Friendship and reciprocity → Rational community; diversity of the nature of beasts → Instrumentally useful relation, not ethical society.","refs":["CH_QGOH_PQ_3:A2","CH_QGOH_PQ_3:A15"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"From","passages":[{"id":"RAW_QGOH_PQ_3:P0001","trace_type":"Composite","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"},{"id":"RAW_QGOH_PQ_3:P0021","trace_type":"Composite","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]}],"original_name":"Formal Generative Schema"},{"number":"9","name":"Argument Flow Map","items":[{"id":"CH_QGOH_PQ_3:AF1","text":"Rational self-advantage → Virtue → Human agreement → Non-rival common good → Communication of the knowledge of God → Friendship and Generosity.","refs":["CH_QGOH_PQ_3:A1","CH_QGOH_PQ_3:A2","CH_QGOH_PQ_3:A3","CH_QGOH_PQ_3:A4"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"From","passages":[{"id":"RAW_QGOH_PQ_3:P0001","trace_type":"Structural","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"},{"id":"RAW_QGOH_PQ_3:P0008","trace_type":"Structural","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"},{"id":"RAW_QGOH_PQ_3:P0012","trace_type":"Structural","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"},{"id":"RAW_QGOH_PQ_3:P0019","trace_type":"Structural","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_QGOH_PQ_3:AF2","text":"Dominion of affects → Insecurity and conflict → Surrender of right → Common coercion → State → Sin, merit, justice, and injustice as juridical effects.","refs":["CH_QGOH_PQ_3:A5","CH_QGOH_PQ_3:A6","CH_QGOH_PQ_3:A7","CH_QGOH_PQ_3:A8"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"From","passages":[{"id":"RAW_QGOH_PQ_3:P0024","trace_type":"Structural","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"},{"id":"RAW_QGOH_PQ_3:P0025","trace_type":"Structural","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"},{"id":"RAW_QGOH_PQ_3:P0026","trace_type":"Structural","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"},{"id":"RAW_QGOH_PQ_3:P0027","trace_type":"Structural","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_QGOH_PQ_3:AF3","text":"Preservation of bodily form → Increase of capacity → Ordered Joy → Rejection of Hatred and affects of impotence → Love, moderation, and action from the counsel of Reason.","refs":["CH_QGOH_PQ_3:A9","CH_QGOH_PQ_3:A10","CH_QGOH_PQ_3:A11","CH_QGOH_PQ_3:A12","CH_QGOH_PQ_3:A13"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"From","passages":[{"id":"RAW_QGOH_PQ_3:P0034","trace_type":"Structural","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"},{"id":"RAW_QGOH_PQ_3:P0048","trace_type":"Structural","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"},{"id":"RAW_QGOH_PQ_3:P0068","trace_type":"Structural","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"},{"id":"RAW_QGOH_PQ_3:P0079","trace_type":"Structural","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"},{"id":"RAW_QGOH_PQ_3:P0089","trace_type":"Structural","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]}],"original_name":"Argument Flow Map"},{"number":"10","name":"Map-Effects","items":[{"id":"CH_QGOH_PQ_3:ME1","text":"The opposition between self-advantage and common advantage is dissolved for human beings insofar as they are guided by Reason.","refs":["CH_QGOH_PQ_3:A1","CH_QGOH_PQ_3:A2"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"From","passages":[{"id":"RAW_QGOH_PQ_3:P0001","trace_type":"Direct","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_QGOH_PQ_3:ME2","text":"The supreme good is reclassified as a communicable good, not as an object of exclusive possession.","refs":["CH_QGOH_PQ_3:A3"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"From","passages":[{"id":"RAW_QGOH_PQ_3:P0006","trace_type":"Direct","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"},{"id":"RAW_QGOH_PQ_3:P0008","trace_type":"Direct","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_QGOH_PQ_3:ME3","text":"A distinction is established between rational guidance of others and affective dominion by which someone seeks to compel others to conform to his own disposition.","refs":["CH_QGOH_PQ_3:A4","CH_QGOH_PQ_3:A5"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"From","passages":[{"id":"RAW_QGOH_PQ_3:P0019","trace_type":"Direct","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"},{"id":"RAW_QGOH_PQ_3:P0020","trace_type":"Direct","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_QGOH_PQ_3:ME4","text":"The State is described not as a simple expression of moral consensus, but as a machine of security that produces juridical categories.","refs":["CH_QGOH_PQ_3:A6","CH_QGOH_PQ_3:A7","CH_QGOH_PQ_3:A8"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"From","passages":[{"id":"RAW_QGOH_PQ_3:P0026","trace_type":"Direct","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"},{"id":"RAW_QGOH_PQ_3:P0027","trace_type":"Direct","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"},{"id":"RAW_QGOH_PQ_3:P0028","trace_type":"Direct","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_QGOH_PQ_3:ME5","text":"Affects are ordered not according to their name, but according to their effect on the integrated power of the Body, the aptitude of the Mind, and social concord.","refs":["CH_QGOH_PQ_3:A9","CH_QGOH_PQ_3:A10","CH_QGOH_PQ_3:A11"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"From","passages":[{"id":"RAW_QGOH_PQ_3:P0030","trace_type":"Composite","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"},{"id":"RAW_QGOH_PQ_3:P0048","trace_type":"Composite","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"},{"id":"RAW_QGOH_PQ_3:P0056","trace_type":"Composite","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_QGOH_PQ_3:ME6","text":"The good life is reclassified as the moderate multiplication of bodily and mental capacity, not as the flight from pleasure or the cult of Sadness.","refs":["CH_QGOH_PQ_3:A9","CH_QGOH_PQ_3:A11"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"From","passages":[{"id":"RAW_QGOH_PQ_3:P0076","trace_type":"Direct","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"},{"id":"RAW_QGOH_PQ_3:P0077","trace_type":"Direct","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_QGOH_PQ_3:ME7","text":"The boundary of ethical society between human beings and beasts is placed in agreement of nature and reciprocity, not in the mere faculty of feeling.","refs":["CH_QGOH_PQ_3:A15"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"From","passages":[{"id":"RAW_QGOH_PQ_3:P0021","trace_type":"Direct","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]}],"original_name":"Map-Effects"},{"number":"11","name":"Altitude Levels","items":[{"id":"CH_QGOH_PQ_3:ALT1","text":"Surface — The text formally distinguishes good and evil, Joy and Sadness, Hatred and Love, Hope and Fear, justice and injustice.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_QGOH_PQ_3:P0046","trace_type":"Direct","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"},{"id":"RAW_QGOH_PQ_3:P0066","trace_type":"Direct","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"},{"id":"RAW_QGOH_PQ_3:P0085","trace_type":"Direct","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"},{"id":"RAW_QGOH_PQ_3:P0091","trace_type":"Direct","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_QGOH_PQ_3:ALT2","text":"Structural — The Body is described through the ratio of motion and rest, affects through power, and the State through security and threats.","refs":["CH_QGOH_PQ_3:A5","CH_QGOH_PQ_3:A7","CH_QGOH_PQ_3:A10"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"From","passages":[{"id":"RAW_QGOH_PQ_3:P0026","trace_type":"Structural","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"},{"id":"RAW_QGOH_PQ_3:P0034","trace_type":"Structural","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"},{"id":"RAW_QGOH_PQ_3:P0048","trace_type":"Structural","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_QGOH_PQ_3:ALT3","text":"Metastrategic — Reason not only knows truth, but establishes the governance of affects through Generosity, civil institution, and the ordered moderation of life.","refs":["CH_QGOH_PQ_3:A4","CH_QGOH_PQ_3:A5","CH_QGOH_PQ_3:A12"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"From","passages":[{"id":"RAW_QGOH_PQ_3:P0019","trace_type":"Structural","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"},{"id":"RAW_QGOH_PQ_3:P0026","trace_type":"Structural","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"},{"id":"RAW_QGOH_PQ_3:P0079","trace_type":"Structural","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"},{"id":"RAW_QGOH_PQ_3:P0077","trace_type":"Structural","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_QGOH_PQ_3:ALT4","text":"Ontological/Temporal — Right is connected with power, the identity of the body with the relation of structure, and civil norms with the temporal institution of the State.","refs":["CH_QGOH_PQ_3:A6","CH_QGOH_PQ_3:A8","CH_QGOH_PQ_3:A10"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"From","passages":[{"id":"RAW_QGOH_PQ_3:P0024","trace_type":"Structural","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"},{"id":"RAW_QGOH_PQ_3:P0028","trace_type":"Structural","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"},{"id":"RAW_QGOH_PQ_3:P0039","trace_type":"Structural","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]}],"original_name":"Altitude Levels"},{"number":"12","name":"Minimal Reconstruction Test","items":[{"id":"CH_QGOH_PQ_3:MRT1","text":"The axioms suffice to reconstruct the transition from rational agreement to the common good, and from the conflict of affects to the necessity of the State.","refs":["CH_QGOH_PQ_3:A2","CH_QGOH_PQ_3:A3","CH_QGOH_PQ_3:A5","CH_QGOH_PQ_3:A7"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"From","passages":[{"id":"RAW_QGOH_PQ_3:P0001","trace_type":"Structural","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"},{"id":"RAW_QGOH_PQ_3:P0008","trace_type":"Structural","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"},{"id":"RAW_QGOH_PQ_3:P0024","trace_type":"Structural","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"},{"id":"RAW_QGOH_PQ_3:P0026","trace_type":"Structural","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_QGOH_PQ_3:MRT2","text":"The firmest hinge of the system is the connection between the non-rival common good and the rational desire by which the same good is communicated to others.","refs":["CH_QGOH_PQ_3:A3","CH_QGOH_PQ_3:A4"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"From","passages":[{"id":"RAW_QGOH_PQ_3:P0006","trace_type":"Direct","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"},{"id":"RAW_QGOH_PQ_3:P0012","trace_type":"Direct","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_QGOH_PQ_3:MRT3","text":"A fragile joint remains between the State upheld by threats and the concord defined by rational life, because coercion explains security but does not fully produce rational living.","refs":["CH_QGOH_PQ_3:A2","CH_QGOH_PQ_3:A5","CH_QGOH_PQ_3:A7"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"From","passages":[{"id":"RAW_QGOH_PQ_3:P0001","trace_type":"Tension","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"},{"id":"RAW_QGOH_PQ_3:P0025","trace_type":"Tension","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"},{"id":"RAW_QGOH_PQ_3:P0026","trace_type":"Tension","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_QGOH_PQ_3:MRT4","text":"The system requires consent or surrender of natural right, but does not further deduce the mechanism by which individuals dominated by affects first make this surrender stable.","refs":["CH_QGOH_PQ_3:A5","CH_QGOH_PQ_3:A6","CH_QGOH_PQ_3:A7"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"From","passages":[]},{"id":"CH_QGOH_PQ_3:MRT5","text":"It contains the tension of a twofold normativity: natural good is defined through power, while justice and injustice are constituted by civil institution alone.","refs":["CH_QGOH_PQ_3:A8","CH_QGOH_PQ_3:A9"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"From","passages":[{"id":"RAW_QGOH_PQ_3:P0024","trace_type":"Tension","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"},{"id":"RAW_QGOH_PQ_3:P0028","trace_type":"Tension","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"},{"id":"RAW_QGOH_PQ_3:P0030","trace_type":"Tension","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_QGOH_PQ_3:MRT6","text":"The boundary between human beings and beasts preserves the structure of the system, but assumes agreement of human nature as the decisive condition rather than further demonstrating it at this point.","refs":["CH_QGOH_PQ_3:A15"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"From","passages":[]},{"id":"CH_QGOH_PQ_3:MRT7","text":"The criterion of death through a change in the ratio of motion and rest is systematically coherent, but leaves indeterminate the determinate degree at which change transfers a body into another nature.","refs":["CH_QGOH_PQ_3:A10"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"From","passages":[]},{"id":"CH_QGOH_PQ_3:MRT8","text":"The reconstruction preserves the movement of the text if the order of the double machine is retained: Reason demonstrates the common good, whereas affects impose the necessity of institution and of a contrary affect.","refs":["CH_QGOH_PQ_3:FGS1","CH_QGOH_PQ_3:FGS2","CH_QGOH_PQ_3:FGS3"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"From","passages":[{"id":"RAW_QGOH_PQ_3:P0001","trace_type":"Structural","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"},{"id":"RAW_QGOH_PQ_3:P0026","trace_type":"Structural","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"},{"id":"RAW_QGOH_PQ_3:P0034","trace_type":"Structural","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"},{"id":"RAW_QGOH_PQ_3:P0081","trace_type":"Structural","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]}],"original_name":"Minimal Reconstruction Test"}],"revision":"CH_QGOH_PQ_3@R001","source_variant_id":"canonical:CH_QGOH_PQ_3:v1","source_language_code":"la"}],"identity":{"layer":"CH","kicker":"Chapter 16","title":"Part Four - On Human Bondage, or the Powers of the Affects · passage 3","context":"Chapter 16","original_title":"Pars quarta - De servitute humana seu de affectuum viribus · passage 3","original_context":"Chapter 16"},"original_title":"Pars quarta - De servitute humana seu de affectuum viribus · passage 3","title_language_code":"en"},{"id":"CH_QGOH_PQ_4","layer":"CH","title":"Part Four - On Human Bondage, or the Powers of the Affects · passage 4","order":17,"parents":["RAW_QGOH_PQ_4"],"revision":"CH_QGOH_PQ_4@R001","sha256":"0e7d334232028e3f6e7eb041375f5ee3f438ec815bcfce66bd00a8bff569017f","sections":[{"number":"1","name":"Argumental Spine","items":[{"id":"CH_QGOH_PQ_4:AS1","text":"Textus quaerit quibus affectibus homo ratione ductus uti possit, quos excludere debeat, et qua ratione eadem actio vel servilis vel libera fieri possit.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_QGOH_PQ_4:P0001","trace_type":"Structural","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"},{"id":"RAW_QGOH_PQ_4:P0062","trace_type":"Structural","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"},{"id":"RAW_QGOH_PQ_4:P0088","trace_type":"Structural","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"},{"id":"RAW_QGOH_PQ_4:P0118","trace_type":"Structural","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"},{"id":"RAW_QGOH_PQ_4:P0124","trace_type":"Structural","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_QGOH_PQ_4:AS2","text":"Thesis principalis statuit virtutem non ex tristitia, metu, paenitentia aut commiseratione oriri, sed ex ratione quae potentiam agendi intelligit et dirigit.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_QGOH_PQ_4:P0019","trace_type":"Composite","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"},{"id":"RAW_QGOH_PQ_4:P0025","trace_type":"Composite","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"},{"id":"RAW_QGOH_PQ_4:P0064","trace_type":"Composite","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"},{"id":"RAW_QGOH_PQ_4:P0088","trace_type":"Composite","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_QGOH_PQ_4:AS3","text":"Argumentatio simul diagnostica et reconstructiva est: passiones per earum dependentiam, partialitatem et temporalem caecitatem diagnoscit, deinde actionem rationalem sine passionibus restituit.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_QGOH_PQ_4:P0060","trace_type":"Structural","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"},{"id":"RAW_QGOH_PQ_4:P0062","trace_type":"Structural","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"},{"id":"RAW_QGOH_PQ_4:P0064","trace_type":"Structural","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"},{"id":"RAW_QGOH_PQ_4:P0069","trace_type":"Structural","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"},{"id":"RAW_QGOH_PQ_4:P0100","trace_type":"Structural","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"},{"id":"RAW_QGOH_PQ_4:P0102","trace_type":"Structural","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_QGOH_PQ_4:AS4","text":"Motus procedit ab affectuum moralium reclassificatione ad distinctionem inter actionem et causam actionis, inde ad utilitatem totius hominis et ad ordinem temporalem rationis.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_QGOH_PQ_4:P0015","trace_type":"Structural","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"},{"id":"RAW_QGOH_PQ_4:P0023","trace_type":"Structural","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"},{"id":"RAW_QGOH_PQ_4:P0028","trace_type":"Structural","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"},{"id":"RAW_QGOH_PQ_4:P0067","trace_type":"Structural","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"},{"id":"RAW_QGOH_PQ_4:P0071","trace_type":"Structural","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"},{"id":"RAW_QGOH_PQ_4:P0081","trace_type":"Structural","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"},{"id":"RAW_QGOH_PQ_4:P0118","trace_type":"Structural","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_QGOH_PQ_4:AS5","text":"Contrapositio finalis hominem affectu vel opinione actum servum, hominem ratione ductum liberum constituit; libertas est vita directe ex proprio utili et cognitione ducta.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_QGOH_PQ_4:P0118","trace_type":"Direct","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"},{"id":"RAW_QGOH_PQ_4:P0120","trace_type":"Direct","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"},{"id":"RAW_QGOH_PQ_4:P0122","trace_type":"Direct","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_QGOH_PQ_4:AS6","text":"Terminus argumenti tendit ad condicionem in qua libertas ipsa conceptus boni et mali superfluos reddit, quia hi ex comparatione et impedimento nascuntur.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_QGOH_PQ_4:P0104","trace_type":"Composite","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"},{"id":"RAW_QGOH_PQ_4:P0124","trace_type":"Composite","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]}],"original_name":"Argumental Spine"},{"number":"2","name":"Load-Bearing Axioms","items":[{"id":"CH_QGOH_PQ_4:A1","text":"Ex ratione agere est ex necessitate naturae humanae, quatenus per se et adequate intelligitur, agere.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_QGOH_PQ_4:P0064","trace_type":"Direct","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_QGOH_PQ_4:A2","text":"Vera hominis agendi potentia, seu virtus, est ipsa ratio.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_QGOH_PQ_4:P0019","trace_type":"Direct","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_QGOH_PQ_4:A3","text":"Tristitia potentiam agendi minuit vel coercet et, quatenus hominem ad minorem perfectionem transfert, passio est.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_QGOH_PQ_4:P0064","trace_type":"Direct","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_QGOH_PQ_4:A4","text":"Laetitia cum ratione convenit quatenus agendi potentiam auget; passio manet quatenus homo se suasque actiones adequate non concipit.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_QGOH_PQ_4:P0064","trace_type":"Direct","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_QGOH_PQ_4:A5","text":"Affectus passivi ad laetitiam, tristitiam aut cupiditatem reducuntur, et cupiditates ex passionibus ortae caecae sunt.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_QGOH_PQ_4:P0060","trace_type":"Composite","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"},{"id":"RAW_QGOH_PQ_4:P0069","trace_type":"Composite","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_QGOH_PQ_4:A6","text":"Commiseratio est tristitia ex alterius miseria orta.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_QGOH_PQ_4:P0003","trace_type":"Direct","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_QGOH_PQ_4:A7","text":"Actio in se spectata neque bona neque mala est; eius valor ex affectu vel cognitione qua determinatur pendet.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_QGOH_PQ_4:P0067","trace_type":"Direct","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_QGOH_PQ_4:A8","text":"Cupiditas ex affectu ad unam corporis partem relato utilitatem totius hominis non considerat.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_QGOH_PQ_4:P0071","trace_type":"Direct","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"},{"id":"RAW_QGOH_PQ_4:P0073","trace_type":"Direct","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_QGOH_PQ_4:A9","text":"Ratio res sub specie necessitatis et aeternitatis concipit, non differenter prout futurae, praeteritae vel praesentes imaginantur.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_QGOH_PQ_4:P0081","trace_type":"Direct","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"},{"id":"RAW_QGOH_PQ_4:P0083","trace_type":"Direct","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_QGOH_PQ_4:A10","text":"Cupiditas ex ratione orta essentiam humanam actu agentem exprimit et propterea excessum habere nequit.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_QGOH_PQ_4:P0077","trace_type":"Direct","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"},{"id":"RAW_QGOH_PQ_4:P0079","trace_type":"Direct","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_QGOH_PQ_4:A11","text":"Metus ad tristitiam pertinet; qui bonum agit ut malum fugiat, non ratione ducitur.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_QGOH_PQ_4:P0088","trace_type":"Direct","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"},{"id":"RAW_QGOH_PQ_4:P0090","trace_type":"Direct","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_QGOH_PQ_4:A12","text":"Cognitio mali est cognitio tristitiae conscia et ideo cognitio inadæquata.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_QGOH_PQ_4:P0100","trace_type":"Direct","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"},{"id":"RAW_QGOH_PQ_4:P0102","trace_type":"Direct","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_QGOH_PQ_4:A13","text":"Bonum et malum relationes comparativae sunt; ratio maius bonum et minus malum eligit.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_QGOH_PQ_4:P0106","trace_type":"Direct","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"},{"id":"RAW_QGOH_PQ_4:P0108","trace_type":"Direct","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_QGOH_PQ_4:A14","text":"Cognitio humana durationis et temporum existentiae est inadæquata atque imaginatione potius quam ratione ordinatur.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_QGOH_PQ_4:P0085","trace_type":"Direct","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"},{"id":"RAW_QGOH_PQ_4:P0086","trace_type":"Direct","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_QGOH_PQ_4:A15","text":"Homo ratione ductus bonum directe appetit, malum autem solum indirecte fugit.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_QGOH_PQ_4:P0094","trace_type":"Direct","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"},{"id":"RAW_QGOH_PQ_4:P0096","trace_type":"Direct","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_QGOH_PQ_4:A16","text":"Affectus non rationales, quamvis virtutes non sint, homines raro ratione viventes ad ordinem socialem et ad futuram libertatem disponere possunt.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_QGOH_PQ_4:P0032","trace_type":"Direct","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]}],"original_name":"Load-Bearing Axioms"},{"number":"3","name":"Apophatic Structure","items":[{"id":"CH_QGOH_PQ_4:NX1","text":"Commiseratio non est virtus rationalis, et liberatio alterius ex commiseratione orta non est ipsa causa rationalis beneficentiae.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_QGOH_PQ_4:P0001","trace_type":"Negative","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"},{"id":"RAW_QGOH_PQ_4:P0003","trace_type":"Negative","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"},{"id":"RAW_QGOH_PQ_4:P0007","trace_type":"Negative","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_QGOH_PQ_4:NX2","text":"Homo rationalis non definitur per insensibilitatem erga alios, quia auxilium alteri praestare potest ex solo rationis dictamine.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_QGOH_PQ_4:P0007","trace_type":"Direct","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_QGOH_PQ_4:NX3","text":"Humilitas et paenitentia non sunt formae virtutis, quia ex tristitia et impotentiae contemplatione oriuntur.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_QGOH_PQ_4:P0023","trace_type":"Negative","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"},{"id":"RAW_QGOH_PQ_4:P0025","trace_type":"Negative","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"},{"id":"RAW_QGOH_PQ_4:P0028","trace_type":"Negative","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_QGOH_PQ_4:NX4","text":"Indignatio non est iustitia rationalis; poena publica rationalis tantum est si ex amore salutis publicae, non ex odio, procedit.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_QGOH_PQ_4:P0015","trace_type":"Composite","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"},{"id":"RAW_QGOH_PQ_4:P0098","trace_type":"Composite","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_QGOH_PQ_4:NX5","text":"Gloria vulgi opinione sustentata non est vera acquiescentia, quia cum opinione externa perit.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_QGOH_PQ_4:P0058","trace_type":"Direct","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_QGOH_PQ_4:NX6","text":"Nulla actio passionem requirit ut fieri possit, quia eadem actio ex imaginibus confusis vel ideis adequatis determinari potest.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_QGOH_PQ_4:P0062","trace_type":"Direct","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"},{"id":"RAW_QGOH_PQ_4:P0069","trace_type":"Direct","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_QGOH_PQ_4:NX7","text":"Metus non est forma inferior prudentiae rationalis, sed signum actionis ab avoidance mali potius quam a cognitione boni motae.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_QGOH_PQ_4:P0088","trace_type":"Negative","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"},{"id":"RAW_QGOH_PQ_4:P0090","trace_type":"Negative","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_QGOH_PQ_4:NX8","text":"Bonum et malum non sunt proprietates absolutae rerum aut actionum.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_QGOH_PQ_4:P0067","trace_type":"Negative","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"},{"id":"RAW_QGOH_PQ_4:P0108","trace_type":"Negative","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_QGOH_PQ_4:NX9","text":"Cogitatio mortis non est centrum sapientiae liberae; sapientia libera meditationem vitae constituit.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_QGOH_PQ_4:P0120","trace_type":"Direct","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"},{"id":"RAW_QGOH_PQ_4:P0122","trace_type":"Direct","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]}],"original_name":"Apophatic Structure"},{"number":"4","name":"Derivative Propositions","items":[{"id":"CH_QGOH_PQ_4:D1","text":"Commiseratio in homine ratione ducto per se mala et inutilis est, quia tristitia est, dum beneficentia quam comitari videtur ex ratione sola produci potest.","refs":["CH_QGOH_PQ_4:A1","CH_QGOH_PQ_4:A3","CH_QGOH_PQ_4:A6"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"From","passages":[{"id":"RAW_QGOH_PQ_4:P0003","trace_type":"Direct","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_QGOH_PQ_4:D2","text":"Homo rationalis conatur ne commiseratione tangatur, non ut auxilium neget, sed ut bene agendi causam a tristitia ad rationem transferat.","refs":["CH_QGOH_PQ_4:A1","CH_QGOH_PQ_4:A6","CH_QGOH_PQ_4:A15"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"From","passages":[{"id":"RAW_QGOH_PQ_4:P0005","trace_type":"Composite","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"},{"id":"RAW_QGOH_PQ_4:P0007","trace_type":"Composite","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_QGOH_PQ_4:D3","text":"Favor et acquiescentia in se ipso cum ratione convenire possunt, quia ab intellectu propriae vel alienae potentiae agentis oriri possunt.","refs":["CH_QGOH_PQ_4:A1","CH_QGOH_PQ_4:A2","CH_QGOH_PQ_4:A4"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"From","passages":[{"id":"RAW_QGOH_PQ_4:P0009","trace_type":"Composite","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"},{"id":"RAW_QGOH_PQ_4:P0011","trace_type":"Composite","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"},{"id":"RAW_QGOH_PQ_4:P0017","trace_type":"Composite","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"},{"id":"RAW_QGOH_PQ_4:P0019","trace_type":"Composite","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_QGOH_PQ_4:D4","text":"Humilitas et paenitentia impotentiam duplicant: primum actum pravum non removent, deinde tristitiam superaddunt.","refs":["CH_QGOH_PQ_4:A3","CH_QGOH_PQ_4:A5"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"From","passages":[{"id":"RAW_QGOH_PQ_4:P0025","trace_type":"Composite","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"},{"id":"RAW_QGOH_PQ_4:P0028","trace_type":"Composite","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"},{"id":"RAW_QGOH_PQ_4:P0030","trace_type":"Composite","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_QGOH_PQ_4:D5","text":"Maxima superbia et maxima abjectio non sunt contrariae libertati diversis modis, sed communes formae maximae sui ignorantiae et animi impotentiae.","refs":["CH_QGOH_PQ_4:A2","CH_QGOH_PQ_4:A3"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"From","passages":[{"id":"RAW_QGOH_PQ_4:P0034","trace_type":"Direct","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"},{"id":"RAW_QGOH_PQ_4:P0038","trace_type":"Direct","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"},{"id":"RAW_QGOH_PQ_4:P0040","trace_type":"Direct","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_QGOH_PQ_4:D6","text":"Eadem corporalis actio ex ira, odio, ratione vel idea clara procedere potest; distinctio ethica igitur ad determinationem causalem, non ad motum externum, refertur.","refs":["CH_QGOH_PQ_4:A1","CH_QGOH_PQ_4:A7"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"From","passages":[{"id":"RAW_QGOH_PQ_4:P0067","trace_type":"Direct","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"},{"id":"RAW_QGOH_PQ_4:P0069","trace_type":"Direct","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_QGOH_PQ_4:D7","text":"Cupiditates passionum nullam actionem unice necessariam efficiunt, quia ratio ad omnes actiones quas passio determinat determinare potest.","refs":["CH_QGOH_PQ_4:A1","CH_QGOH_PQ_4:A4","CH_QGOH_PQ_4:A5","CH_QGOH_PQ_4:A7"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"From","passages":[{"id":"RAW_QGOH_PQ_4:P0062","trace_type":"Direct","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"},{"id":"RAW_QGOH_PQ_4:P0064","trace_type":"Direct","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"},{"id":"RAW_QGOH_PQ_4:P0065","trace_type":"Direct","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_QGOH_PQ_4:D8","text":"Cupiditas passionis plerumque praesentem corporis statum conservat sine ratione integrae valetudinis aut futuri.","refs":["CH_QGOH_PQ_4:A8","CH_QGOH_PQ_4:A14"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"From","passages":[{"id":"RAW_QGOH_PQ_4:P0071","trace_type":"Direct","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"},{"id":"RAW_QGOH_PQ_4:P0075","trace_type":"Direct","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_QGOH_PQ_4:D9","text":"Ratio, si sola spectatur, maius bonum futurum minori bono praesenti anteponit et minus malum praesens pro maiore bono futuro suscipit.","refs":["CH_QGOH_PQ_4:A9","CH_QGOH_PQ_4:A13"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"From","passages":[{"id":"RAW_QGOH_PQ_4:P0106","trace_type":"Composite","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"},{"id":"RAW_QGOH_PQ_4:P0110","trace_type":"Composite","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_QGOH_PQ_4:D10","text":"Cupiditas rationalis bonum prosequitur ex laetitia activa, non ex tristitia mali vitandi; propterea nec excessum nec formam metuendi habet.","refs":["CH_QGOH_PQ_4:A4","CH_QGOH_PQ_4:A10","CH_QGOH_PQ_4:A11","CH_QGOH_PQ_4:A15"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"From","passages":[{"id":"RAW_QGOH_PQ_4:P0077","trace_type":"Composite","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"},{"id":"RAW_QGOH_PQ_4:P0094","trace_type":"Composite","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"},{"id":"RAW_QGOH_PQ_4:P0096","trace_type":"Composite","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_QGOH_PQ_4:D11","text":"Cognitio mali, quia inadæquata est, rationem non instruit per malum ut obiectum positivum, sed tantum per comparationem bonorum et impedimentorum.","refs":["CH_QGOH_PQ_4:A12","CH_QGOH_PQ_4:A13"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"From","passages":[{"id":"RAW_QGOH_PQ_4:P0100","trace_type":"Composite","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"},{"id":"RAW_QGOH_PQ_4:P0102","trace_type":"Composite","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"},{"id":"RAW_QGOH_PQ_4:P0108","trace_type":"Composite","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_QGOH_PQ_4:D12","text":"Servitus est agere ea quae homo maxime ignorat ex affectu vel opinione; libertas est sibi soli obtemperare in iis quae ad vivendum et conservandum esse maxime cognoscuntur.","refs":["CH_QGOH_PQ_4:A1","CH_QGOH_PQ_4:A2","CH_QGOH_PQ_4:A15"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"From","passages":[{"id":"RAW_QGOH_PQ_4:P0118","trace_type":"Direct","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]}],"original_name":"Derivative Propositions"},{"number":"5","name":"Super-Derivatives","items":[{"id":"CH_QGOH_PQ_4:SD1","text":"Systema ethicum actiones non per species exteriores distribuit, sed per transitum a causis externis et imaginariis ad causam internam adequate intellectam.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_QGOH_PQ_4:P0067","trace_type":"Structural","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"},{"id":"RAW_QGOH_PQ_4:P0069","trace_type":"Structural","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_QGOH_PQ_4:SD2","text":"Virtus reorganizatur ut augmentum et ordinatio potentiae agendi, non ut tolerantia tristitiae, imminutio sui, metus poenae aut dolor paenitentialis.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_QGOH_PQ_4:P0019","trace_type":"Structural","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"},{"id":"RAW_QGOH_PQ_4:P0023","trace_type":"Structural","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"},{"id":"RAW_QGOH_PQ_4:P0028","trace_type":"Structural","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"},{"id":"RAW_QGOH_PQ_4:P0088","trace_type":"Structural","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_QGOH_PQ_4:SD3","text":"Ratio temporaliter totalizat utilitatem sub specie aeternitatis, dum passio localiter et praesentaneae parti corporis inservit.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_QGOH_PQ_4:P0071","trace_type":"Structural","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"},{"id":"RAW_QGOH_PQ_4:P0075","trace_type":"Structural","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"},{"id":"RAW_QGOH_PQ_4:P0081","trace_type":"Structural","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"},{"id":"RAW_QGOH_PQ_4:P0112","trace_type":"Structural","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_QGOH_PQ_4:SD4","text":"Ordo politicus affectibus uti potest ut homines ad disciplinam et tandem ad libertatem disponantur, sed affectus ipsi fundamentum libertatis fieri nequeunt.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_QGOH_PQ_4:P0032","trace_type":"Composite","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"},{"id":"RAW_QGOH_PQ_4:P0092","trace_type":"Composite","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_QGOH_PQ_4:SD5","text":"Conceptus boni et mali statum comparativum et imperfectum exprimunt; libertas perfecta tendit ad ordinem in quo haec vocabula formari desinunt.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_QGOH_PQ_4:P0100","trace_type":"Structural","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"},{"id":"RAW_QGOH_PQ_4:P0108","trace_type":"Structural","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"},{"id":"RAW_QGOH_PQ_4:P0124","trace_type":"Structural","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]}],"original_name":"Super-Derivatives"},{"number":"6","name":"Deep Apophatics","items":[{"id":"CH_QGOH_PQ_4:DNX1","text":"Falsum esse debet passionem irreducibilem fontem actionis utilis esse, quia ratio omnes actiones quas passio producit producere potest.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_QGOH_PQ_4:P0062","trace_type":"Negative","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"},{"id":"RAW_QGOH_PQ_4:P0069","trace_type":"Negative","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_QGOH_PQ_4:DNX2","text":"Falsum esse debet malum positivam rem adequate cognosci posse, quia cognitio mali ad tristitiam et ideam inadæquatam reducitur.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_QGOH_PQ_4:P0100","trace_type":"Negative","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"},{"id":"RAW_QGOH_PQ_4:P0102","trace_type":"Negative","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_QGOH_PQ_4:DNX3","text":"Falsum esse debet affectum localem spontaneam cognitionem utilitatis totius hominis praebere.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_QGOH_PQ_4:P0071","trace_type":"Negative","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"},{"id":"RAW_QGOH_PQ_4:P0073","trace_type":"Negative","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_QGOH_PQ_4:DNX4","text":"Falsum esse debet iudicium vulgi stabilem mensuram perfectionis propriae constituere.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_QGOH_PQ_4:P0019","trace_type":"Negative","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"},{"id":"RAW_QGOH_PQ_4:P0058","trace_type":"Negative","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_QGOH_PQ_4:DNX5","text":"Falsum esse debet libertatem consistere in fuga mali aut morte vitanda; libertas bonum et vitam directe affirmat.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_QGOH_PQ_4:P0094","trace_type":"Negative","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"},{"id":"RAW_QGOH_PQ_4:P0120","trace_type":"Negative","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"},{"id":"RAW_QGOH_PQ_4:P0122","trace_type":"Negative","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_QGOH_PQ_4:DNX6","text":"Falsum esse debet sui imminutio veram sui cognitionem exprimere, quia cognitio rationalis essentiam et potentiam, non impotentiam, percipit.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_QGOH_PQ_4:P0025","trace_type":"Negative","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"},{"id":"RAW_QGOH_PQ_4:P0026","trace_type":"Negative","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"},{"id":"RAW_QGOH_PQ_4:P0040","trace_type":"Negative","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]}],"original_name":"Deep Apophatics"},{"number":"7","name":"Super-Apophatics","items":[{"id":"CH_QGOH_PQ_4:SNX1","text":"Argumentatio excludit moralitatem cuius vis directiva in metu, prohibitione et fuga mali consistit.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_QGOH_PQ_4:P0088","trace_type":"Negative","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"},{"id":"RAW_QGOH_PQ_4:P0092","trace_type":"Negative","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_QGOH_PQ_4:SNX2","text":"Argumentatio excludit commiserationem ut supremam formam relationis humanae, quia rationalis communitas ex beneficentia activa et favore, non ex tristitia communicata, constituitur.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_QGOH_PQ_4:P0003","trace_type":"Negative","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"},{"id":"RAW_QGOH_PQ_4:P0007","trace_type":"Negative","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"},{"id":"RAW_QGOH_PQ_4:P0013","trace_type":"Negative","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_QGOH_PQ_4:SNX3","text":"Argumentatio excludit subiectum quod per defectum, culpam aut abiectionem se ipsummet vere cognoscere possit.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_QGOH_PQ_4:P0025","trace_type":"Negative","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"},{"id":"RAW_QGOH_PQ_4:P0034","trace_type":"Negative","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"},{"id":"RAW_QGOH_PQ_4:P0040","trace_type":"Negative","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_QGOH_PQ_4:SNX4","text":"Argumentatio circumagit condicionem libertatis in qua bonum et malum, ut conceptus comparativi servitutis, nullam amplius functionem cognitivam habent.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_QGOH_PQ_4:P0108","trace_type":"Structural","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"},{"id":"RAW_QGOH_PQ_4:P0124","trace_type":"Structural","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]}],"original_name":"Super-Apophatics"},{"number":"8","name":"Formal Generative Schema","items":[{"id":"CH_QGOH_PQ_4:FGS1","text":"Affectus → laetitia / tristitia / cupiditas → examen per augmentum vel diminutionem potentiae agendi.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_QGOH_PQ_4:P0064","trace_type":"Composite","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"},{"id":"RAW_QGOH_PQ_4:P0100","trace_type":"Composite","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_QGOH_PQ_4:FGS2","text":"Tristitia vel imaginatio partialis → passio → cognitio inadæquata → cupiditas caeca et praesentis temporis capta.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_QGOH_PQ_4:P0060","trace_type":"Structural","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"},{"id":"RAW_QGOH_PQ_4:P0071","trace_type":"Structural","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"},{"id":"RAW_QGOH_PQ_4:P0075","trace_type":"Structural","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"},{"id":"RAW_QGOH_PQ_4:P0086","trace_type":"Structural","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_QGOH_PQ_4:FGS3","text":"Actio exterior eadem → causae affectivae vel causae rationales diversae → valor ad modum determinationis, non ad figuram actus, refertur.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_QGOH_PQ_4:P0067","trace_type":"Direct","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"},{"id":"RAW_QGOH_PQ_4:P0069","trace_type":"Direct","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_QGOH_PQ_4:FGS4","text":"Ratio → cognitio adequata potentiae → laetitia activa → cupiditas sine excessu → bonum directe appetitum.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_QGOH_PQ_4:P0019","trace_type":"Composite","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"},{"id":"RAW_QGOH_PQ_4:P0077","trace_type":"Composite","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"},{"id":"RAW_QGOH_PQ_4:P0094","trace_type":"Composite","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_QGOH_PQ_4:FGS5","text":"Ratio sub specie aeternitatis → comparatio bonorum et malorum → electio maioris boni et minoris mali etiam trans tempora.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_QGOH_PQ_4:P0081","trace_type":"Composite","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"},{"id":"RAW_QGOH_PQ_4:P0106","trace_type":"Composite","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"},{"id":"RAW_QGOH_PQ_4:P0112","trace_type":"Composite","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_QGOH_PQ_4:FGS6","text":"Raritas vitae rationalis → usus transitorius affectuum ad disciplinam socialem → transitus possibilis ad libertatem → imminutio functionis boni et mali.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_QGOH_PQ_4:P0032","trace_type":"Structural","confidence":"medium","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"},{"id":"RAW_QGOH_PQ_4:P0118","trace_type":"Structural","confidence":"medium","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"},{"id":"RAW_QGOH_PQ_4:P0124","trace_type":"Structural","confidence":"medium","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]}],"original_name":"Formal Generative Schema"},{"number":"9","name":"Argument Flow Map","items":[{"id":"CH_QGOH_PQ_4:AF1","text":"Commiseratio ut tristitia → separatio auxilii ab affectu miserendi → virtus ut ratio et potentia agendi → reiectio humilitatis, paenitentiae et metus → actio a causa affectiva seiungitur.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_QGOH_PQ_4:P0003","trace_type":"Structural","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"},{"id":"RAW_QGOH_PQ_4:P0019","trace_type":"Structural","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"},{"id":"RAW_QGOH_PQ_4:P0023","trace_type":"Structural","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"},{"id":"RAW_QGOH_PQ_4:P0028","trace_type":"Structural","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"},{"id":"RAW_QGOH_PQ_4:P0062","trace_type":"Structural","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"},{"id":"RAW_QGOH_PQ_4:P0088","trace_type":"Structural","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_QGOH_PQ_4:AF2","text":"Partialitas corporis et praesentis imaginationis → cupiditas caeca → ratio aeternitatem et comparationem bonorum introducit → electio utilitatis integrae et futurae.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_QGOH_PQ_4:P0071","trace_type":"Structural","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"},{"id":"RAW_QGOH_PQ_4:P0075","trace_type":"Structural","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"},{"id":"RAW_QGOH_PQ_4:P0081","trace_type":"Structural","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"},{"id":"RAW_QGOH_PQ_4:P0112","trace_type":"Structural","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_QGOH_PQ_4:AF3","text":"Actio ex opinione et affectu → servitus → actio ex cognitione proprii utilis → libertas → vita potius quam mors meditanda.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_QGOH_PQ_4:P0118","trace_type":"Direct","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"},{"id":"RAW_QGOH_PQ_4:P0120","trace_type":"Direct","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"},{"id":"RAW_QGOH_PQ_4:P0122","trace_type":"Direct","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_QGOH_PQ_4:AF4","text":"Libertas continua → imminutio comparationum boni et mali → prospectus condicionis in qua haec concepta non formantur.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_QGOH_PQ_4:P0108","trace_type":"Composite","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"},{"id":"RAW_QGOH_PQ_4:P0124","trace_type":"Composite","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]}],"original_name":"Argument Flow Map"},{"number":"10","name":"Map-Effects","items":[{"id":"CH_QGOH_PQ_4:ME1","text":"Affectus reclassificantur non per approbationem moralem vulgarem, sed per relationem ad potentiam agendi, adecquationem cognitionis et utilitatem totius.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_QGOH_PQ_4:P0019","trace_type":"Structural","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"},{"id":"RAW_QGOH_PQ_4:P0064","trace_type":"Structural","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"},{"id":"RAW_QGOH_PQ_4:P0071","trace_type":"Structural","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"},{"id":"RAW_QGOH_PQ_4:P0102","trace_type":"Structural","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_QGOH_PQ_4:ME2","text":"Oppositio actus boni et actus mali dissolvitur, atque substituitur oppositio eiusdem actus ex passione vel ex ratione determinati.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_QGOH_PQ_4:P0067","trace_type":"Direct","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"},{"id":"RAW_QGOH_PQ_4:P0069","trace_type":"Direct","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_QGOH_PQ_4:ME3","text":"Misericordia, humilitas, paenitentia et metus e regione virtutis transferuntur ad passiones quae interdum disciplinarem, non autem rationalem, functionem habent.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_QGOH_PQ_4:P0023","trace_type":"Structural","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"},{"id":"RAW_QGOH_PQ_4:P0028","trace_type":"Structural","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"},{"id":"RAW_QGOH_PQ_4:P0032","trace_type":"Structural","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"},{"id":"RAW_QGOH_PQ_4:P0088","trace_type":"Structural","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_QGOH_PQ_4:ME4","text":"Utilitas individualis non iam cum satisfactione partis corporis aut cum commodo praesenti identificatur, sed cum conservatione et perfectione totius hominis per tempora ordinata.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_QGOH_PQ_4:P0071","trace_type":"Composite","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"},{"id":"RAW_QGOH_PQ_4:P0075","trace_type":"Composite","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"},{"id":"RAW_QGOH_PQ_4:P0112","trace_type":"Composite","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_QGOH_PQ_4:ME5","text":"Gloria et vituperium ab indicibus valoris intrinseci ad machinas opinionis externae et servitutis affectivae transferuntur.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_QGOH_PQ_4:P0021","trace_type":"Composite","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"},{"id":"RAW_QGOH_PQ_4:P0058","trace_type":"Composite","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_QGOH_PQ_4:ME6","text":"Libertas a facultate eligendi inter bona et mala ad activitatem ex sola ratione, vitae affirmationem et proprii utilis cognitionem reconfiguratur.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_QGOH_PQ_4:P0094","trace_type":"Structural","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"},{"id":"RAW_QGOH_PQ_4:P0118","trace_type":"Structural","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"},{"id":"RAW_QGOH_PQ_4:P0122","trace_type":"Structural","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]}],"original_name":"Map-Effects"},{"number":"11","name":"Altitude Levels","items":[{"id":"CH_QGOH_PQ_4:ALT1","text":"Surface — Textus iudicat commiserationem, favorem, acquiescentiam, humilitatem, paenitentiam, superbiam, gloriam, metum et cupiditatem.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_QGOH_PQ_4:P0001","trace_type":"Structural","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"},{"id":"RAW_QGOH_PQ_4:P0009","trace_type":"Structural","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"},{"id":"RAW_QGOH_PQ_4:P0017","trace_type":"Structural","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"},{"id":"RAW_QGOH_PQ_4:P0023","trace_type":"Structural","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"},{"id":"RAW_QGOH_PQ_4:P0028","trace_type":"Structural","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"},{"id":"RAW_QGOH_PQ_4:P0034","trace_type":"Structural","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"},{"id":"RAW_QGOH_PQ_4:P0054","trace_type":"Structural","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"},{"id":"RAW_QGOH_PQ_4:P0088","trace_type":"Structural","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"},{"id":"RAW_QGOH_PQ_4:P0077","trace_type":"Structural","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_QGOH_PQ_4:ALT2","text":"Structural — Affects distribuuntur secundum potentiam agendi, adequationem idearum, relationem corporis partis ad totum et dependentiam ab opinionibus externis.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_QGOH_PQ_4:P0019","trace_type":"Structural","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"},{"id":"RAW_QGOH_PQ_4:P0064","trace_type":"Structural","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"},{"id":"RAW_QGOH_PQ_4:P0071","trace_type":"Structural","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"},{"id":"RAW_QGOH_PQ_4:P0058","trace_type":"Structural","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_QGOH_PQ_4:ALT3","text":"Metastrategic — Textus separat virtutem rationalem a mechanismis quibus multitudo affectibus continetur, dum hos mechanismos transitui ad libertatem conditionaliter reservat.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_QGOH_PQ_4:P0032","trace_type":"Composite","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"},{"id":"RAW_QGOH_PQ_4:P0092","trace_type":"Composite","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_QGOH_PQ_4:ALT4","text":"Ontological/Temporal — Ratio res necessario et aeternaliter concipit, passio autem imaginatione temporali, praesentia et duratione inadæquate cognita ordinatur.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_QGOH_PQ_4:P0081","trace_type":"Direct","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"},{"id":"RAW_QGOH_PQ_4:P0085","trace_type":"Direct","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"},{"id":"RAW_QGOH_PQ_4:P0086","trace_type":"Direct","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_QGOH_PQ_4:ALT5","text":"Ontological/Temporal — Libertas significat hominem ut causam ex propria natura agentem; sub hoc horizonte bonum et malum status comparativi, non entia absoluta, fiunt.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_QGOH_PQ_4:P0064","trace_type":"Structural","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"},{"id":"RAW_QGOH_PQ_4:P0108","trace_type":"Structural","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"},{"id":"RAW_QGOH_PQ_4:P0122","trace_type":"Structural","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"},{"id":"RAW_QGOH_PQ_4:P0124","trace_type":"Structural","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]}],"original_name":"Altitude Levels"},{"number":"12","name":"Minimal Reconstruction Test","items":[{"id":"CH_QGOH_PQ_4:MRT1","text":"Axiomata de ratione ut actione adequata, de virtute ut potentia, de tristitia ut diminutione, et de actione ut causaliter qualificata sufficiunt ad nucleum argumenti restituendum.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_QGOH_PQ_4:P0019","trace_type":"Composite","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"},{"id":"RAW_QGOH_PQ_4:P0064","trace_type":"Composite","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"},{"id":"RAW_QGOH_PQ_4:P0067","trace_type":"Composite","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_QGOH_PQ_4:MRT2","text":"Axiomata de partialitate affectuum, de imaginatione temporali et de ratione sub specie aeternitatis explicant transitum a diagnostica passionum ad electionem rationalem bonorum.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_QGOH_PQ_4:P0071","trace_type":"Structural","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"},{"id":"RAW_QGOH_PQ_4:P0075","trace_type":"Structural","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"},{"id":"RAW_QGOH_PQ_4:P0081","trace_type":"Structural","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"},{"id":"RAW_QGOH_PQ_4:P0085","trace_type":"Structural","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"},{"id":"RAW_QGOH_PQ_4:P0106","trace_type":"Structural","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_QGOH_PQ_4:MRT3","text":"Systema maxime cohaeret ubi eandem actionem a causa passionis separat et sic beneficentiam a commiseratione, poenam publicam ab indignatione, atque salutem a metu distinguit.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_QGOH_PQ_4:P0003","trace_type":"Structural","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"},{"id":"RAW_QGOH_PQ_4:P0015","trace_type":"Structural","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"},{"id":"RAW_QGOH_PQ_4:P0067","trace_type":"Structural","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"},{"id":"RAW_QGOH_PQ_4:P0098","trace_type":"Structural","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_QGOH_PQ_4:MRT4","text":"Commissura fragilis manet inter normam rationis, quae bonum futurum aeque ac praesens ponderat, et conditionem humanam, quae durationem rerum necessario inadæquate cognoscit.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_QGOH_PQ_4:P0083","trace_type":"Tension","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"},{"id":"RAW_QGOH_PQ_4:P0085","trace_type":"Tension","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"},{"id":"RAW_QGOH_PQ_4:P0086","trace_type":"Tension","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"},{"id":"RAW_QGOH_PQ_4:P0114","trace_type":"Tension","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_QGOH_PQ_4:MRT5","text":"Tensio interna manet quia humilitas, paenitentia et metus rationi adversa declarantur, sed eadem affecta instrumenta utilia fiunt ad multitudinem ad rationem ducendam.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_QGOH_PQ_4:P0032","trace_type":"Tension","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"},{"id":"RAW_QGOH_PQ_4:P0023","trace_type":"Tension","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"},{"id":"RAW_QGOH_PQ_4:P0028","trace_type":"Tension","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"},{"id":"RAW_QGOH_PQ_4:P0088","trace_type":"Tension","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_QGOH_PQ_4:MRT6","text":"Distinctio inter virtutem et signum virtutis necessaria est ut pudor, tristitia et disciplina affectiva aliquo sensu utiles dicantur sine eo quod fontes rationales virtutis fiant.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_QGOH_PQ_4:P0023","trace_type":"Tension","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"},{"id":"RAW_QGOH_PQ_4:P0028","trace_type":"Tension","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"},{"id":"RAW_QGOH_PQ_4:P0032","trace_type":"Tension","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"},{"id":"RAW_QGOH_PQ_4:P0059","trace_type":"Tension","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_QGOH_PQ_4:MRT7","text":"Prospectus quo homines liberi nullum boni et mali conceptum formarent in fine tantum enuntiatur; demonstratio eius plena in hoc fonte nondum perficitur.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[]}],"original_name":"Minimal Reconstruction Test"}],"language_code":"la","language_name":"Latina","translations":[{"id":"CAREVAR_20261015T014808526442Z_3cb9202e","language_code":"en","language_name":"English","title":"Part Four - On Human Bondage, or the Powers of the Affects · passage 4","source_sha256":"0e7d334232028e3f6e7eb041375f5ee3f438ec815bcfce66bd00a8bff569017f","sha256":"e1444cc8e5f6e191061751b996043ad078d1d1f22d43300e88eb166c70cea7c1","rendition_type":"machine","review_status":"machine","model":"gpt-5.6-terra","created":"2026-10-15T01:48:08.527396+00:00","sections":[{"number":"1","name":"Argumental Spine","items":[{"id":"CH_QGOH_PQ_4:AS1","text":"The text asks which affects a person guided by reason may employ, which they ought to exclude, and how the same action may be either servile or free.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_QGOH_PQ_4:P0001","trace_type":"Structural","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"},{"id":"RAW_QGOH_PQ_4:P0062","trace_type":"Structural","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"},{"id":"RAW_QGOH_PQ_4:P0088","trace_type":"Structural","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"},{"id":"RAW_QGOH_PQ_4:P0118","trace_type":"Structural","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"},{"id":"RAW_QGOH_PQ_4:P0124","trace_type":"Structural","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_QGOH_PQ_4:AS2","text":"The principal thesis establishes that virtue arises not from sadness, fear, repentance, or pity, but from reason, which understands and directs the power of acting.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_QGOH_PQ_4:P0019","trace_type":"Composite","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"},{"id":"RAW_QGOH_PQ_4:P0025","trace_type":"Composite","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"},{"id":"RAW_QGOH_PQ_4:P0064","trace_type":"Composite","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"},{"id":"RAW_QGOH_PQ_4:P0088","trace_type":"Composite","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_QGOH_PQ_4:AS3","text":"The argument is at once diagnostic and reconstructive: it diagnoses passions through their dependence, partiality, and temporal blindness, then restores rational action without passions.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_QGOH_PQ_4:P0060","trace_type":"Structural","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"},{"id":"RAW_QGOH_PQ_4:P0062","trace_type":"Structural","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"},{"id":"RAW_QGOH_PQ_4:P0064","trace_type":"Structural","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"},{"id":"RAW_QGOH_PQ_4:P0069","trace_type":"Structural","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"},{"id":"RAW_QGOH_PQ_4:P0100","trace_type":"Structural","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"},{"id":"RAW_QGOH_PQ_4:P0102","trace_type":"Structural","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_QGOH_PQ_4:AS4","text":"The movement proceeds from the moral reclassification of affects to the distinction between action and the cause of action, thence to the utility of the whole person and to reason's temporal order.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_QGOH_PQ_4:P0015","trace_type":"Structural","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"},{"id":"RAW_QGOH_PQ_4:P0023","trace_type":"Structural","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"},{"id":"RAW_QGOH_PQ_4:P0028","trace_type":"Structural","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"},{"id":"RAW_QGOH_PQ_4:P0067","trace_type":"Structural","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"},{"id":"RAW_QGOH_PQ_4:P0071","trace_type":"Structural","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"},{"id":"RAW_QGOH_PQ_4:P0081","trace_type":"Structural","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"},{"id":"RAW_QGOH_PQ_4:P0118","trace_type":"Structural","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_QGOH_PQ_4:AS5","text":"The final opposition constitutes the person driven by affect or opinion as a slave, and the person guided by reason as free; freedom is life directed immediately by one's own advantage and knowledge.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_QGOH_PQ_4:P0118","trace_type":"Direct","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"},{"id":"RAW_QGOH_PQ_4:P0120","trace_type":"Direct","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"},{"id":"RAW_QGOH_PQ_4:P0122","trace_type":"Direct","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_QGOH_PQ_4:AS6","text":"The terminus of the argument tends toward a condition in which freedom itself renders the concepts of good and evil superfluous, because these arise from comparison and impediment.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_QGOH_PQ_4:P0104","trace_type":"Composite","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"},{"id":"RAW_QGOH_PQ_4:P0124","trace_type":"Composite","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]}],"original_name":"Argumental Spine"},{"number":"2","name":"Load-Bearing Axioms","items":[{"id":"CH_QGOH_PQ_4:A1","text":"To act from reason is to act from the necessity of human nature insofar as it is understood through itself and adequately.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_QGOH_PQ_4:P0064","trace_type":"Direct","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_QGOH_PQ_4:A2","text":"The true power of acting of a human being, or virtue, is reason itself.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_QGOH_PQ_4:P0019","trace_type":"Direct","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_QGOH_PQ_4:A3","text":"Sadness diminishes or restrains the power of acting and, insofar as it transfers a human being to lesser perfection, is a passion.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_QGOH_PQ_4:P0064","trace_type":"Direct","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_QGOH_PQ_4:A4","text":"Joy agrees with reason insofar as it increases the power of acting; it remains a passion insofar as a person does not adequately conceive himself and his actions.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_QGOH_PQ_4:P0064","trace_type":"Direct","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_QGOH_PQ_4:A5","text":"Passive affects are reduced to joy, sadness, or desire, and desires arising from passions are blind.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_QGOH_PQ_4:P0060","trace_type":"Composite","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"},{"id":"RAW_QGOH_PQ_4:P0069","trace_type":"Composite","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_QGOH_PQ_4:A6","text":"Pity is sadness arising from another's misery.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_QGOH_PQ_4:P0003","trace_type":"Direct","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_QGOH_PQ_4:A7","text":"An action considered in itself is neither good nor evil; its value depends on the affect or knowledge by which it is determined.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_QGOH_PQ_4:P0067","trace_type":"Direct","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_QGOH_PQ_4:A8","text":"Desire arising from an affect related to one part of the body does not consider the advantage of the whole person.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_QGOH_PQ_4:P0071","trace_type":"Direct","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"},{"id":"RAW_QGOH_PQ_4:P0073","trace_type":"Direct","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_QGOH_PQ_4:A9","text":"Reason conceives things under the aspect of necessity and eternity, not differently according as they are imagined as future, past, or present.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_QGOH_PQ_4:P0081","trace_type":"Direct","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"},{"id":"RAW_QGOH_PQ_4:P0083","trace_type":"Direct","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_QGOH_PQ_4:A10","text":"Desire arising from reason expresses human essence actually acting and therefore cannot have excess.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_QGOH_PQ_4:P0077","trace_type":"Direct","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"},{"id":"RAW_QGOH_PQ_4:P0079","trace_type":"Direct","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_QGOH_PQ_4:A11","text":"Fear belongs to sadness; one who does good in order to flee evil is not guided by reason.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_QGOH_PQ_4:P0088","trace_type":"Direct","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"},{"id":"RAW_QGOH_PQ_4:P0090","trace_type":"Direct","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_QGOH_PQ_4:A12","text":"Knowledge of evil is conscious knowledge of sadness and therefore inadequate knowledge.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_QGOH_PQ_4:P0100","trace_type":"Direct","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"},{"id":"RAW_QGOH_PQ_4:P0102","trace_type":"Direct","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_QGOH_PQ_4:A13","text":"Good and evil are comparative relations; reason chooses the greater good and the lesser evil.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_QGOH_PQ_4:P0106","trace_type":"Direct","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"},{"id":"RAW_QGOH_PQ_4:P0108","trace_type":"Direct","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_QGOH_PQ_4:A14","text":"Human knowledge of duration and of the temporal existence of things is inadequate and is ordered by imagination rather than reason.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_QGOH_PQ_4:P0085","trace_type":"Direct","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"},{"id":"RAW_QGOH_PQ_4:P0086","trace_type":"Direct","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_QGOH_PQ_4:A15","text":"A person guided by reason directly seeks good, but flees evil only indirectly.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_QGOH_PQ_4:P0094","trace_type":"Direct","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"},{"id":"RAW_QGOH_PQ_4:P0096","trace_type":"Direct","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_QGOH_PQ_4:A16","text":"Non-rational affects, although they are not virtues, can dispose persons rarely living by reason toward social order and future freedom.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_QGOH_PQ_4:P0032","trace_type":"Direct","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]}],"original_name":"Load-Bearing Axioms"},{"number":"3","name":"Apophatic Structure","items":[{"id":"CH_QGOH_PQ_4:NX1","text":"Pity is not a rational virtue, and another's deliverance arising from pity is not itself the rational cause of beneficence.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_QGOH_PQ_4:P0001","trace_type":"Negative","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"},{"id":"RAW_QGOH_PQ_4:P0003","trace_type":"Negative","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"},{"id":"RAW_QGOH_PQ_4:P0007","trace_type":"Negative","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_QGOH_PQ_4:NX2","text":"The rational person is not defined by insensibility toward others, because he can render assistance to another from the dictate of reason alone.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_QGOH_PQ_4:P0007","trace_type":"Direct","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_QGOH_PQ_4:NX3","text":"Humility and repentance are not forms of virtue, because they arise from sadness and the contemplation of impotence.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_QGOH_PQ_4:P0023","trace_type":"Negative","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"},{"id":"RAW_QGOH_PQ_4:P0025","trace_type":"Negative","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"},{"id":"RAW_QGOH_PQ_4:P0028","trace_type":"Negative","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_QGOH_PQ_4:NX4","text":"Indignation is not rational justice; public punishment is rational only if it proceeds from love of the public welfare, not from hatred.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_QGOH_PQ_4:P0015","trace_type":"Composite","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"},{"id":"RAW_QGOH_PQ_4:P0098","trace_type":"Composite","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_QGOH_PQ_4:NX5","text":"Glory sustained by popular opinion is not true self-contentment, because it perishes with external opinion.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_QGOH_PQ_4:P0058","trace_type":"Direct","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_QGOH_PQ_4:NX6","text":"No action requires a passion in order to be possible, because the same action can be determined by confused images or adequate ideas.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_QGOH_PQ_4:P0062","trace_type":"Direct","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"},{"id":"RAW_QGOH_PQ_4:P0069","trace_type":"Direct","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_QGOH_PQ_4:NX7","text":"Fear is not an inferior form of rational prudence, but a sign of action moved by the avoidance of evil rather than by knowledge of good.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_QGOH_PQ_4:P0088","trace_type":"Negative","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"},{"id":"RAW_QGOH_PQ_4:P0090","trace_type":"Negative","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_QGOH_PQ_4:NX8","text":"Good and evil are not absolute properties of things or actions.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_QGOH_PQ_4:P0067","trace_type":"Negative","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"},{"id":"RAW_QGOH_PQ_4:P0108","trace_type":"Negative","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_QGOH_PQ_4:NX9","text":"Thought of death is not the center of free wisdom; free wisdom consists in meditation on life.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_QGOH_PQ_4:P0120","trace_type":"Direct","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"},{"id":"RAW_QGOH_PQ_4:P0122","trace_type":"Direct","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]}],"original_name":"Apophatic Structure"},{"number":"4","name":"Derivative Propositions","items":[{"id":"CH_QGOH_PQ_4:D1","text":"Pity in a person guided by reason is in itself evil and useless, because it is sadness, whereas the beneficence it seems to accompany can be produced by reason alone.","refs":["CH_QGOH_PQ_4:A1","CH_QGOH_PQ_4:A3","CH_QGOH_PQ_4:A6"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"From","passages":[{"id":"RAW_QGOH_PQ_4:P0003","trace_type":"Direct","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_QGOH_PQ_4:D2","text":"The rational person strives not to be touched by pity, not in order to deny assistance, but in order to transfer the cause of doing good from sadness to reason.","refs":["CH_QGOH_PQ_4:A1","CH_QGOH_PQ_4:A6","CH_QGOH_PQ_4:A15"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"From","passages":[{"id":"RAW_QGOH_PQ_4:P0005","trace_type":"Composite","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"},{"id":"RAW_QGOH_PQ_4:P0007","trace_type":"Composite","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_QGOH_PQ_4:D3","text":"Favor and self-contentment can agree with reason, because they can arise from understanding one's own or another's active power.","refs":["CH_QGOH_PQ_4:A1","CH_QGOH_PQ_4:A2","CH_QGOH_PQ_4:A4"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"From","passages":[{"id":"RAW_QGOH_PQ_4:P0009","trace_type":"Composite","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"},{"id":"RAW_QGOH_PQ_4:P0011","trace_type":"Composite","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"},{"id":"RAW_QGOH_PQ_4:P0017","trace_type":"Composite","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"},{"id":"RAW_QGOH_PQ_4:P0019","trace_type":"Composite","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_QGOH_PQ_4:D4","text":"Humility and repentance double impotence: first, they do not remove the wrongful act; then, they add sadness.","refs":["CH_QGOH_PQ_4:A3","CH_QGOH_PQ_4:A5"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"From","passages":[{"id":"RAW_QGOH_PQ_4:P0025","trace_type":"Composite","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"},{"id":"RAW_QGOH_PQ_4:P0028","trace_type":"Composite","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"},{"id":"RAW_QGOH_PQ_4:P0030","trace_type":"Composite","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_QGOH_PQ_4:D5","text":"The greatest pride and the greatest abjection are not contrary to freedom in different ways, but are common forms of the greatest ignorance of oneself and impotence of mind.","refs":["CH_QGOH_PQ_4:A2","CH_QGOH_PQ_4:A3"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"From","passages":[{"id":"RAW_QGOH_PQ_4:P0034","trace_type":"Direct","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"},{"id":"RAW_QGOH_PQ_4:P0038","trace_type":"Direct","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"},{"id":"RAW_QGOH_PQ_4:P0040","trace_type":"Direct","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_QGOH_PQ_4:D6","text":"The same bodily action can proceed from anger, hatred, reason, or a clear idea; ethical distinction therefore refers to causal determination, not to external movement.","refs":["CH_QGOH_PQ_4:A1","CH_QGOH_PQ_4:A7"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"From","passages":[{"id":"RAW_QGOH_PQ_4:P0067","trace_type":"Direct","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"},{"id":"RAW_QGOH_PQ_4:P0069","trace_type":"Direct","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_QGOH_PQ_4:D7","text":"Desires of the passions render no action uniquely necessary, because reason can determine to all the actions to which passion determines.","refs":["CH_QGOH_PQ_4:A1","CH_QGOH_PQ_4:A4","CH_QGOH_PQ_4:A5","CH_QGOH_PQ_4:A7"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"From","passages":[{"id":"RAW_QGOH_PQ_4:P0062","trace_type":"Direct","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"},{"id":"RAW_QGOH_PQ_4:P0064","trace_type":"Direct","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"},{"id":"RAW_QGOH_PQ_4:P0065","trace_type":"Direct","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_QGOH_PQ_4:D8","text":"The desire of passion generally preserves the present state of the body without regard for integral health or the future.","refs":["CH_QGOH_PQ_4:A8","CH_QGOH_PQ_4:A14"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"From","passages":[{"id":"RAW_QGOH_PQ_4:P0071","trace_type":"Direct","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"},{"id":"RAW_QGOH_PQ_4:P0075","trace_type":"Direct","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_QGOH_PQ_4:D9","text":"Reason, if considered alone, prefers a greater future good to a lesser present good and accepts a lesser present evil for the sake of a greater future good.","refs":["CH_QGOH_PQ_4:A9","CH_QGOH_PQ_4:A13"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"From","passages":[{"id":"RAW_QGOH_PQ_4:P0106","trace_type":"Composite","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"},{"id":"RAW_QGOH_PQ_4:P0110","trace_type":"Composite","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_QGOH_PQ_4:D10","text":"Rational desire pursues good from active joy, not from the sadness of avoiding evil; therefore it has neither excess nor a form of fearing.","refs":["CH_QGOH_PQ_4:A4","CH_QGOH_PQ_4:A10","CH_QGOH_PQ_4:A11","CH_QGOH_PQ_4:A15"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"From","passages":[{"id":"RAW_QGOH_PQ_4:P0077","trace_type":"Composite","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"},{"id":"RAW_QGOH_PQ_4:P0094","trace_type":"Composite","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"},{"id":"RAW_QGOH_PQ_4:P0096","trace_type":"Composite","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_QGOH_PQ_4:D11","text":"Knowledge of evil, because it is inadequate, does not instruct reason through evil as a positive object, but only through comparison of goods and impediments.","refs":["CH_QGOH_PQ_4:A12","CH_QGOH_PQ_4:A13"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"From","passages":[{"id":"RAW_QGOH_PQ_4:P0100","trace_type":"Composite","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"},{"id":"RAW_QGOH_PQ_4:P0102","trace_type":"Composite","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"},{"id":"RAW_QGOH_PQ_4:P0108","trace_type":"Composite","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_QGOH_PQ_4:D12","text":"Bondage is acting from affect or opinion in those things that a person most greatly does not know; freedom is obeying oneself alone in those things that are most greatly known to pertain to living and preserving oneself.","refs":["CH_QGOH_PQ_4:A1","CH_QGOH_PQ_4:A2","CH_QGOH_PQ_4:A15"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"From","passages":[{"id":"RAW_QGOH_PQ_4:P0118","trace_type":"Direct","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]}],"original_name":"Derivative Propositions"},{"number":"5","name":"Super-Derivatives","items":[{"id":"CH_QGOH_PQ_4:SD1","text":"The ethical system distributes actions not according to external kinds, but according to the transition from external and imaginary causes to an internal cause adequately understood.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_QGOH_PQ_4:P0067","trace_type":"Structural","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"},{"id":"RAW_QGOH_PQ_4:P0069","trace_type":"Structural","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_QGOH_PQ_4:SD2","text":"Virtue is reorganized as the increase and ordering of the power of acting, not as endurance of sadness, diminution of oneself, fear of punishment, or penitential pain.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_QGOH_PQ_4:P0019","trace_type":"Structural","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"},{"id":"RAW_QGOH_PQ_4:P0023","trace_type":"Structural","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"},{"id":"RAW_QGOH_PQ_4:P0028","trace_type":"Structural","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"},{"id":"RAW_QGOH_PQ_4:P0088","trace_type":"Structural","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_QGOH_PQ_4:SD3","text":"Reason temporally totalizes utility under the aspect of eternity, whereas passion serves locally and the present part of the body.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_QGOH_PQ_4:P0071","trace_type":"Structural","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"},{"id":"RAW_QGOH_PQ_4:P0075","trace_type":"Structural","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"},{"id":"RAW_QGOH_PQ_4:P0081","trace_type":"Structural","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"},{"id":"RAW_QGOH_PQ_4:P0112","trace_type":"Structural","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_QGOH_PQ_4:SD4","text":"The political order can employ affects so that persons may be disposed toward discipline and finally toward freedom, but the affects themselves cannot become the foundation of freedom.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_QGOH_PQ_4:P0032","trace_type":"Composite","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"},{"id":"RAW_QGOH_PQ_4:P0092","trace_type":"Composite","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_QGOH_PQ_4:SD5","text":"The concepts of good and evil express a comparative and imperfect state; perfect freedom tends toward an order in which these terms cease to be formed.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_QGOH_PQ_4:P0100","trace_type":"Structural","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"},{"id":"RAW_QGOH_PQ_4:P0108","trace_type":"Structural","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"},{"id":"RAW_QGOH_PQ_4:P0124","trace_type":"Structural","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]}],"original_name":"Super-Derivatives"},{"number":"6","name":"Deep Apophatics","items":[{"id":"CH_QGOH_PQ_4:DNX1","text":"It must be false that passion is an irreducible source of useful action, because reason can produce all actions that passion produces.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_QGOH_PQ_4:P0062","trace_type":"Negative","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"},{"id":"RAW_QGOH_PQ_4:P0069","trace_type":"Negative","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_QGOH_PQ_4:DNX2","text":"It must be false that evil can be adequately known as a positive thing, because knowledge of evil is reduced to sadness and an inadequate idea.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_QGOH_PQ_4:P0100","trace_type":"Negative","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"},{"id":"RAW_QGOH_PQ_4:P0102","trace_type":"Negative","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_QGOH_PQ_4:DNX3","text":"It must be false that a local affect provides spontaneous knowledge of the advantage of the whole person.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_QGOH_PQ_4:P0071","trace_type":"Negative","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"},{"id":"RAW_QGOH_PQ_4:P0073","trace_type":"Negative","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_QGOH_PQ_4:DNX4","text":"It must be false that the judgment of the crowd constitutes a stable measure of one's own perfection.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_QGOH_PQ_4:P0019","trace_type":"Negative","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"},{"id":"RAW_QGOH_PQ_4:P0058","trace_type":"Negative","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_QGOH_PQ_4:DNX5","text":"It must be false that freedom consists in flight from evil or avoidance of death; freedom directly affirms good and life.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_QGOH_PQ_4:P0094","trace_type":"Negative","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"},{"id":"RAW_QGOH_PQ_4:P0120","trace_type":"Negative","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"},{"id":"RAW_QGOH_PQ_4:P0122","trace_type":"Negative","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_QGOH_PQ_4:DNX6","text":"It must be false that self-diminution expresses true self-knowledge, because rational knowledge perceives essence and power, not impotence.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_QGOH_PQ_4:P0025","trace_type":"Negative","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"},{"id":"RAW_QGOH_PQ_4:P0026","trace_type":"Negative","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"},{"id":"RAW_QGOH_PQ_4:P0040","trace_type":"Negative","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]}],"original_name":"Deep Apophatics"},{"number":"7","name":"Super-Apophatics","items":[{"id":"CH_QGOH_PQ_4:SNX1","text":"The argument excludes a morality whose directive force consists in fear, prohibition, and flight from evil.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_QGOH_PQ_4:P0088","trace_type":"Negative","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"},{"id":"RAW_QGOH_PQ_4:P0092","trace_type":"Negative","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_QGOH_PQ_4:SNX2","text":"The argument excludes pity as the supreme form of human relation, because rational community is constituted from active beneficence and favor, not from shared sadness.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_QGOH_PQ_4:P0003","trace_type":"Negative","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"},{"id":"RAW_QGOH_PQ_4:P0007","trace_type":"Negative","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"},{"id":"RAW_QGOH_PQ_4:P0013","trace_type":"Negative","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_QGOH_PQ_4:SNX3","text":"The argument excludes a subject who could truly know himself through defect, guilt, or abjection.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_QGOH_PQ_4:P0025","trace_type":"Negative","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"},{"id":"RAW_QGOH_PQ_4:P0034","trace_type":"Negative","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"},{"id":"RAW_QGOH_PQ_4:P0040","trace_type":"Negative","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_QGOH_PQ_4:SNX4","text":"The argument turns around a condition of freedom in which good and evil, as comparative concepts of bondage, no longer have any cognitive function.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_QGOH_PQ_4:P0108","trace_type":"Structural","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"},{"id":"RAW_QGOH_PQ_4:P0124","trace_type":"Structural","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]}],"original_name":"Super-Apophatics"},{"number":"8","name":"Formal Generative Schema","items":[{"id":"CH_QGOH_PQ_4:FGS1","text":"Affects → joy / sadness / desire → examination through increase or diminution of the power of acting.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_QGOH_PQ_4:P0064","trace_type":"Composite","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"},{"id":"RAW_QGOH_PQ_4:P0100","trace_type":"Composite","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_QGOH_PQ_4:FGS2","text":"Sadness or partial imagination → passion → inadequate knowledge → blind desire captive to the present time.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_QGOH_PQ_4:P0060","trace_type":"Structural","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"},{"id":"RAW_QGOH_PQ_4:P0071","trace_type":"Structural","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"},{"id":"RAW_QGOH_PQ_4:P0075","trace_type":"Structural","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"},{"id":"RAW_QGOH_PQ_4:P0086","trace_type":"Structural","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_QGOH_PQ_4:FGS3","text":"Same external action → different affective causes or rational causes → value refers to the mode of determination, not to the form of the act.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_QGOH_PQ_4:P0067","trace_type":"Direct","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"},{"id":"RAW_QGOH_PQ_4:P0069","trace_type":"Direct","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_QGOH_PQ_4:FGS4","text":"Reason → adequate knowledge of power → active joy → desire without excess → good directly sought.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_QGOH_PQ_4:P0019","trace_type":"Composite","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"},{"id":"RAW_QGOH_PQ_4:P0077","trace_type":"Composite","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"},{"id":"RAW_QGOH_PQ_4:P0094","trace_type":"Composite","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_QGOH_PQ_4:FGS5","text":"Reason under the aspect of eternity → comparison of goods and evils → choice of the greater good and the lesser evil even across times.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_QGOH_PQ_4:P0081","trace_type":"Composite","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"},{"id":"RAW_QGOH_PQ_4:P0106","trace_type":"Composite","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"},{"id":"RAW_QGOH_PQ_4:P0112","trace_type":"Composite","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_QGOH_PQ_4:FGS6","text":"Rarity of rational life → transitory use of affects for social discipline → possible transition to freedom → diminution of the function of good and evil.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_QGOH_PQ_4:P0032","trace_type":"Structural","confidence":"medium","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"},{"id":"RAW_QGOH_PQ_4:P0118","trace_type":"Structural","confidence":"medium","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"},{"id":"RAW_QGOH_PQ_4:P0124","trace_type":"Structural","confidence":"medium","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]}],"original_name":"Formal Generative Schema"},{"number":"9","name":"Argument Flow Map","items":[{"id":"CH_QGOH_PQ_4:AF1","text":"Pity as sadness → separation of assistance from the affect of pitying → virtue as reason and power of acting → rejection of humility, repentance, and fear → action separated from affective cause.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_QGOH_PQ_4:P0003","trace_type":"Structural","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"},{"id":"RAW_QGOH_PQ_4:P0019","trace_type":"Structural","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"},{"id":"RAW_QGOH_PQ_4:P0023","trace_type":"Structural","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"},{"id":"RAW_QGOH_PQ_4:P0028","trace_type":"Structural","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"},{"id":"RAW_QGOH_PQ_4:P0062","trace_type":"Structural","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"},{"id":"RAW_QGOH_PQ_4:P0088","trace_type":"Structural","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_QGOH_PQ_4:AF2","text":"Partiality of the body and of present imagination → blind desire → reason introduces eternity and comparison of goods → choice of integral and future advantage.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_QGOH_PQ_4:P0071","trace_type":"Structural","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"},{"id":"RAW_QGOH_PQ_4:P0075","trace_type":"Structural","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"},{"id":"RAW_QGOH_PQ_4:P0081","trace_type":"Structural","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"},{"id":"RAW_QGOH_PQ_4:P0112","trace_type":"Structural","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_QGOH_PQ_4:AF3","text":"Action from opinion and affect → bondage → action from knowledge of one's own advantage → freedom → life rather than death to be meditated upon.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_QGOH_PQ_4:P0118","trace_type":"Direct","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"},{"id":"RAW_QGOH_PQ_4:P0120","trace_type":"Direct","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"},{"id":"RAW_QGOH_PQ_4:P0122","trace_type":"Direct","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_QGOH_PQ_4:AF4","text":"Continuous freedom → diminution of comparisons of good and evil → prospect of a condition in which these concepts are not formed.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_QGOH_PQ_4:P0108","trace_type":"Composite","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"},{"id":"RAW_QGOH_PQ_4:P0124","trace_type":"Composite","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]}],"original_name":"Argument Flow Map"},{"number":"10","name":"Map-Effects","items":[{"id":"CH_QGOH_PQ_4:ME1","text":"Affects are reclassified not by ordinary moral approval, but by their relation to the power of acting, the adequacy of knowledge, and the advantage of the whole.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_QGOH_PQ_4:P0019","trace_type":"Structural","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"},{"id":"RAW_QGOH_PQ_4:P0064","trace_type":"Structural","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"},{"id":"RAW_QGOH_PQ_4:P0071","trace_type":"Structural","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"},{"id":"RAW_QGOH_PQ_4:P0102","trace_type":"Structural","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_QGOH_PQ_4:ME2","text":"The opposition of good action and evil action is dissolved, and is replaced by the opposition of the same action determined by passion or by reason.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_QGOH_PQ_4:P0067","trace_type":"Direct","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"},{"id":"RAW_QGOH_PQ_4:P0069","trace_type":"Direct","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_QGOH_PQ_4:ME3","text":"Mercy, humility, repentance, and fear are transferred from the region of virtue to passions that sometimes have a disciplinary, but not a rational, function.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_QGOH_PQ_4:P0023","trace_type":"Structural","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"},{"id":"RAW_QGOH_PQ_4:P0028","trace_type":"Structural","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"},{"id":"RAW_QGOH_PQ_4:P0032","trace_type":"Structural","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"},{"id":"RAW_QGOH_PQ_4:P0088","trace_type":"Structural","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_QGOH_PQ_4:ME4","text":"Individual advantage is no longer identified with the satisfaction of a part of the body or with present convenience, but with the preservation and perfection of the whole person ordered across time.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_QGOH_PQ_4:P0071","trace_type":"Composite","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"},{"id":"RAW_QGOH_PQ_4:P0075","trace_type":"Composite","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"},{"id":"RAW_QGOH_PQ_4:P0112","trace_type":"Composite","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_QGOH_PQ_4:ME5","text":"Glory and blame are transferred from indicators of intrinsic value to mechanisms of external opinion and affective bondage.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_QGOH_PQ_4:P0021","trace_type":"Composite","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"},{"id":"RAW_QGOH_PQ_4:P0058","trace_type":"Composite","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_QGOH_PQ_4:ME6","text":"Freedom is reconfigured from the faculty of choosing between goods and evils into activity from reason alone, affirmation of life, and knowledge of one's own advantage.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_QGOH_PQ_4:P0094","trace_type":"Structural","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"},{"id":"RAW_QGOH_PQ_4:P0118","trace_type":"Structural","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"},{"id":"RAW_QGOH_PQ_4:P0122","trace_type":"Structural","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]}],"original_name":"Map-Effects"},{"number":"11","name":"Altitude Levels","items":[{"id":"CH_QGOH_PQ_4:ALT1","text":"Surface — The text judges pity, favor, self-contentment, humility, repentance, pride, glory, fear, and desire.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_QGOH_PQ_4:P0001","trace_type":"Structural","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"},{"id":"RAW_QGOH_PQ_4:P0009","trace_type":"Structural","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"},{"id":"RAW_QGOH_PQ_4:P0017","trace_type":"Structural","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"},{"id":"RAW_QGOH_PQ_4:P0023","trace_type":"Structural","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"},{"id":"RAW_QGOH_PQ_4:P0028","trace_type":"Structural","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"},{"id":"RAW_QGOH_PQ_4:P0034","trace_type":"Structural","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"},{"id":"RAW_QGOH_PQ_4:P0054","trace_type":"Structural","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"},{"id":"RAW_QGOH_PQ_4:P0088","trace_type":"Structural","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"},{"id":"RAW_QGOH_PQ_4:P0077","trace_type":"Structural","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_QGOH_PQ_4:ALT2","text":"Structural — Affects are distributed according to the power of acting, the adequacy of ideas, the relation of a part of the body to the whole, and dependence on external opinions.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_QGOH_PQ_4:P0019","trace_type":"Structural","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"},{"id":"RAW_QGOH_PQ_4:P0064","trace_type":"Structural","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"},{"id":"RAW_QGOH_PQ_4:P0071","trace_type":"Structural","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"},{"id":"RAW_QGOH_PQ_4:P0058","trace_type":"Structural","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_QGOH_PQ_4:ALT3","text":"Metastrategic — The text separates rational virtue from the mechanisms by which the multitude is restrained through affects, while conditionally reserving these mechanisms for the transition to freedom.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_QGOH_PQ_4:P0032","trace_type":"Composite","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"},{"id":"RAW_QGOH_PQ_4:P0092","trace_type":"Composite","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_QGOH_PQ_4:ALT4","text":"Ontological/Temporal — Reason conceives things necessarily and eternally, whereas passion is ordered by temporal imagination, the present, and inadequately known duration.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_QGOH_PQ_4:P0081","trace_type":"Direct","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"},{"id":"RAW_QGOH_PQ_4:P0085","trace_type":"Direct","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"},{"id":"RAW_QGOH_PQ_4:P0086","trace_type":"Direct","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_QGOH_PQ_4:ALT5","text":"Ontological/Temporal — Freedom signifies a person as a cause acting from his own nature; under this horizon, good and evil become comparative states, not absolute entities.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_QGOH_PQ_4:P0064","trace_type":"Structural","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"},{"id":"RAW_QGOH_PQ_4:P0108","trace_type":"Structural","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"},{"id":"RAW_QGOH_PQ_4:P0122","trace_type":"Structural","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"},{"id":"RAW_QGOH_PQ_4:P0124","trace_type":"Structural","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]}],"original_name":"Altitude Levels"},{"number":"12","name":"Minimal Reconstruction Test","items":[{"id":"CH_QGOH_PQ_4:MRT1","text":"The axioms concerning reason as adequate action, virtue as power, sadness as diminution, and action as causally qualified suffice to reconstruct the core of the argument.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_QGOH_PQ_4:P0019","trace_type":"Composite","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"},{"id":"RAW_QGOH_PQ_4:P0064","trace_type":"Composite","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"},{"id":"RAW_QGOH_PQ_4:P0067","trace_type":"Composite","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_QGOH_PQ_4:MRT2","text":"The axioms concerning the partiality of affects, temporal imagination, and reason under the aspect of eternity explain the transition from the diagnosis of passions to the rational choice of goods.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_QGOH_PQ_4:P0071","trace_type":"Structural","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"},{"id":"RAW_QGOH_PQ_4:P0075","trace_type":"Structural","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"},{"id":"RAW_QGOH_PQ_4:P0081","trace_type":"Structural","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"},{"id":"RAW_QGOH_PQ_4:P0085","trace_type":"Structural","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"},{"id":"RAW_QGOH_PQ_4:P0106","trace_type":"Structural","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_QGOH_PQ_4:MRT3","text":"The system is most coherent where it separates the same action from the cause of passion and thus distinguishes beneficence from pity, public punishment from indignation, and welfare from fear.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_QGOH_PQ_4:P0003","trace_type":"Structural","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"},{"id":"RAW_QGOH_PQ_4:P0015","trace_type":"Structural","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"},{"id":"RAW_QGOH_PQ_4:P0067","trace_type":"Structural","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"},{"id":"RAW_QGOH_PQ_4:P0098","trace_type":"Structural","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_QGOH_PQ_4:MRT4","text":"A fragile joint remains between the norm of reason, which weighs future good equally with present good, and the human condition, which necessarily knows the duration of things inadequately.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_QGOH_PQ_4:P0083","trace_type":"Tension","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"},{"id":"RAW_QGOH_PQ_4:P0085","trace_type":"Tension","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"},{"id":"RAW_QGOH_PQ_4:P0086","trace_type":"Tension","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"},{"id":"RAW_QGOH_PQ_4:P0114","trace_type":"Tension","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_QGOH_PQ_4:MRT5","text":"An internal tension remains because humility, repentance, and fear are declared contrary to reason, yet the same affects become useful instruments for leading the multitude toward reason.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_QGOH_PQ_4:P0032","trace_type":"Tension","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"},{"id":"RAW_QGOH_PQ_4:P0023","trace_type":"Tension","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"},{"id":"RAW_QGOH_PQ_4:P0028","trace_type":"Tension","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"},{"id":"RAW_QGOH_PQ_4:P0088","trace_type":"Tension","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_QGOH_PQ_4:MRT6","text":"The distinction between virtue and a sign of virtue is necessary so that shame, sadness, and affective discipline may be called useful in some sense without thereby becoming rational sources of virtue.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_QGOH_PQ_4:P0023","trace_type":"Tension","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"},{"id":"RAW_QGOH_PQ_4:P0028","trace_type":"Tension","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"},{"id":"RAW_QGOH_PQ_4:P0032","trace_type":"Tension","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"},{"id":"RAW_QGOH_PQ_4:P0059","trace_type":"Tension","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_QGOH_PQ_4:MRT7","text":"The prospect in which free persons would form no concept of good and evil is stated only at the end; its full demonstration is not yet completed in this source.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[]}],"original_name":"Minimal Reconstruction Test"}],"revision":"CH_QGOH_PQ_4@R001","source_variant_id":"canonical:CH_QGOH_PQ_4:v1","source_language_code":"la"}],"identity":{"layer":"CH","kicker":"Chapter 17","title":"Part Four - On Human Bondage, or the Powers of the Affects · passage 4","context":"Chapter 17","original_title":"Pars quarta - De servitute humana seu de affectuum viribus · passage 4","original_context":"Chapter 17"},"original_title":"Pars quarta - De servitute humana seu de affectuum viribus · passage 4","title_language_code":"en"},{"id":"CH_QGOH_PQ_5","layer":"CH","title":"Part Four - On Human Bondage, or the Powers of the Affects · passage 5","order":18,"parents":["RAW_QGOH_PQ_5"],"revision":"CH_QGOH_PQ_5@R001","sha256":"ab1792b85ae794c8125a85d2c355f002ac21c5a6e1889ba64777539330dcc6fb","sections":[{"number":"1","name":"Argumental Spine","items":[{"id":"CH_QGOH_PQ_5:AS1","text":"Textus quaerit quomodo libertas humana, sub necessitate Naturae et inter homines plerumque affectibus ductos, effici possit sine exemptionis causalis fictione.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_QGOH_PQ_5:P0003","trace_type":"Structural","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"},{"id":"RAW_QGOH_PQ_5:P0041","trace_type":"Structural","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"},{"id":"RAW_QGOH_PQ_5:P0104","trace_type":"Structural","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_QGOH_PQ_5:AS2","text":"Libertas perfecte concepta statuitur ut terminus hypotheticus: homo sola Ratione ductus ideis adequatis fruitur atque boni malique conceptus transcendit.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_QGOH_PQ_5:P0001","trace_type":"Direct","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"},{"id":"RAW_QGOH_PQ_5:P0003","trace_type":"Direct","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_QGOH_PQ_5:AS3","text":"Argumentatio diagnostica servitutem ex cognitione mutilata, affectuum imperio et causarum externarum potentia derivat; libertatem ex actione rationali et affectuum moderatione restituit.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_QGOH_PQ_5:P0043","trace_type":"Composite","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"},{"id":"RAW_QGOH_PQ_5:P0051","trace_type":"Composite","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"},{"id":"RAW_QGOH_PQ_5:P0008","trace_type":"Composite","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"},{"id":"RAW_QGOH_PQ_5:P0037","trace_type":"Composite","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_QGOH_PQ_5:AS4","text":"Motus practicus a periculis, beneficiis, gratitudine et fide transit ad conclusionem politicam quod homo Ratione ductus in Civitate liberior sit quam in solitudine.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_QGOH_PQ_5:P0006","trace_type":"Structural","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"},{"id":"RAW_QGOH_PQ_5:P0014","trace_type":"Structural","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"},{"id":"RAW_QGOH_PQ_5:P0020","trace_type":"Structural","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"},{"id":"RAW_QGOH_PQ_5:P0026","trace_type":"Structural","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"},{"id":"RAW_QGOH_PQ_5:P0032","trace_type":"Structural","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_QGOH_PQ_5:AS5","text":"Appendix conatum, rationem boni, corporis conservationem, societatem, oeconomiam et acquiescentiam in ordine Naturae in unam rationem vitae rationalis conectit.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_QGOH_PQ_5:P0041","trace_type":"Structural","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"},{"id":"RAW_QGOH_PQ_5:P0047","trace_type":"Structural","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"},{"id":"RAW_QGOH_PQ_5:P0055","trace_type":"Structural","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"},{"id":"RAW_QGOH_PQ_5:P0063","trace_type":"Structural","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"},{"id":"RAW_QGOH_PQ_5:P0093","trace_type":"Structural","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"},{"id":"RAW_QGOH_PQ_5:P0104","trace_type":"Structural","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]}],"original_name":"Argumental Spine"},{"number":"2","name":"Load-Bearing Axioms","items":[{"id":"CH_QGOH_PQ_5:A1","text":"Homo liber eatenus est, quatenus sola Ratione ducitur et ideis adequatis agit.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_QGOH_PQ_5:P0001","trace_type":"Direct","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_QGOH_PQ_5:A2","text":"Libertas integra, qua homo libere nascitur et liber manet, homini ut parti Naturae considerato non competit, sed tantum hypothetice concipi potest.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_QGOH_PQ_5:P0003","trace_type":"Direct","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_QGOH_PQ_5:A3","text":"Omnis humanus conatus seu Cupiditas ex necessitate naturae humanae, sive ex ordine totius Naturae, sequitur.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_QGOH_PQ_5:P0041","trace_type":"Direct","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_QGOH_PQ_5:A4","text":"Cupiditates ex ideis adequatis ortae actiones sunt et potentiam humanam indicant; ex cognitione inadequata ortae passiones sunt et potentia externa definiuntur.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_QGOH_PQ_5:P0043","trace_type":"Direct","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_QGOH_PQ_5:A5","text":"Actiones Ratione definitae semper bonae sunt; reliquae Cupiditates bonitatem aut malitiam tantum relative habere possunt.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_QGOH_PQ_5:P0045","trace_type":"Direct","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_QGOH_PQ_5:A6","text":"Bonum est quod vitam rationalem et perfectionem intellectus promovet; beatitudo est acquiescentia ex cognitione Dei intuitiva orta.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_QGOH_PQ_5:P0047","trace_type":"Composite","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"},{"id":"RAW_QGOH_PQ_5:P0049","trace_type":"Composite","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_QGOH_PQ_5:A7","text":"Potentia causarum externarum humanam potentiam infinite superat; malum homini, quatenus patitur, ab externis causis provenit.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_QGOH_PQ_5:P0051","trace_type":"Composite","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"},{"id":"RAW_QGOH_PQ_5:P0104","trace_type":"Composite","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_QGOH_PQ_5:A8","text":"Affectus coerceri aut tolli non potest nisi affectu contrario et fortiore.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_QGOH_PQ_5:P0008","trace_type":"Direct","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_QGOH_PQ_5:A9","text":"Conservatio rationalis sui simul utilitatem communem postulat, quia homo Ratione ductus bonum sibi cupitum reliquis hominibus etiam cupit.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_QGOH_PQ_5:P0004","trace_type":"Composite","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"},{"id":"RAW_QGOH_PQ_5:P0022","trace_type":"Composite","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"},{"id":"RAW_QGOH_PQ_5:P0034","trace_type":"Composite","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_QGOH_PQ_5:A10","text":"Dolus malus non potest esse regula rationalis, quia, si universalis esset, fidem, foedera et iura communia destrueret.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_QGOH_PQ_5:P0028","trace_type":"Direct","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"},{"id":"RAW_QGOH_PQ_5:P0030","trace_type":"Direct","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_QGOH_PQ_5:A11","text":"Homo Ratione ductus libertatem suam amplius in communibus Civitatis iuribus quam in solitudine invenit.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_QGOH_PQ_5:P0032","trace_type":"Direct","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"},{"id":"RAW_QGOH_PQ_5:P0034","trace_type":"Direct","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_QGOH_PQ_5:A12","text":"Ius Naturae homini concedit removendi quae eius vitam rationalem impediunt et utendi rebus quas ad conservationem utileis iudicat.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_QGOH_PQ_5:P0055","trace_type":"Direct","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_QGOH_PQ_5:A13","text":"Corporis aptitudo ad plures affectiones et operationes Mentis aptitudinem ad plura concipienda auget; corpus variis alimentis et rebus indiget.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_QGOH_PQ_5:P0093","trace_type":"Direct","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"},{"id":"RAW_QGOH_PQ_5:P0094","trace_type":"Direct","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_QGOH_PQ_5:A14","text":"Laetitia potentiam humanam auget, sed, nisi Ratione et vigilantia moderetur, in excessum et in praesentis suavitatis praeiudicium cadit.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_QGOH_PQ_5:P0100","trace_type":"Composite","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"},{"id":"RAW_QGOH_PQ_5:P0102","trace_type":"Composite","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_QGOH_PQ_5:A15","text":"Homines plerumque non ex Ratione, sed ex varietate ingeniorum, invidia, odio, vindicta et aliis affectibus agunt.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_QGOH_PQ_5:P0059","trace_type":"Composite","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"},{"id":"RAW_QGOH_PQ_5:P0065","trace_type":"Composite","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"},{"id":"RAW_QGOH_PQ_5:P0067","trace_type":"Composite","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_QGOH_PQ_5:A16","text":"Concordia stabilis iustitia, aequitate, honestate, amore et generositate alitur; metus concordiam sine fide tantum parit.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_QGOH_PQ_5:P0069","trace_type":"Composite","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"},{"id":"RAW_QGOH_PQ_5:P0071","trace_type":"Composite","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"},{"id":"RAW_QGOH_PQ_5:P0089","trace_type":"Composite","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_QGOH_PQ_5:A17","text":"Intellegere omnia ex necessitate Naturae sequi partem meliorem Mentis ad acquiescentiam ordinatam disponit.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_QGOH_PQ_5:P0104","trace_type":"Direct","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"},{"id":"RAW_QGOH_PQ_5:P0105","trace_type":"Direct","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]}],"original_name":"Load-Bearing Axioms"},{"number":"3","name":"Apophatic Structure","items":[{"id":"CH_QGOH_PQ_5:NX1","text":"Libertas non est immunitas empirica ab affectibus, a causis externis aut ab ordine totius Naturae.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_QGOH_PQ_5:P0003","trace_type":"Negative","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"},{"id":"RAW_QGOH_PQ_5:P0051","trace_type":"Negative","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"},{"id":"RAW_QGOH_PQ_5:P0104","trace_type":"Negative","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_QGOH_PQ_5:NX2","text":"Bonum et malum non sunt proprietates absolutae rerum, sed relationes ad vitam rationalem, cognitionem et humanam utilitatem.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_QGOH_PQ_5:P0001","trace_type":"Composite","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"},{"id":"RAW_QGOH_PQ_5:P0045","trace_type":"Composite","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"},{"id":"RAW_QGOH_PQ_5:P0049","trace_type":"Composite","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_QGOH_PQ_5:NX3","text":"Fortitudo non est periculum semper aggredi; fuga tempestiva eandem animi praesentiam postulare potest ac pugna.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_QGOH_PQ_5:P0006","trace_type":"Direct","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"},{"id":"RAW_QGOH_PQ_5:P0008","trace_type":"Direct","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"},{"id":"RAW_QGOH_PQ_5:P0010","trace_type":"Direct","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_QGOH_PQ_5:NX4","text":"Beneficium ab ignaro acceptum non est officium simplex neque vinculum per se rationale, quia aestimatio affectiva benefactoris odium parere potest.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_QGOH_PQ_5:P0016","trace_type":"Direct","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"},{"id":"RAW_QGOH_PQ_5:P0018","trace_type":"Direct","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_QGOH_PQ_5:NX5","text":"Gratia non est mercatura, aucupium aut corruptibilitas per dona; imminutio donationum nocivarum non est ingratitudo.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_QGOH_PQ_5:P0024","trace_type":"Direct","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_QGOH_PQ_5:NX6","text":"Fides civilis non ex dolo malo, perfidia aut conventione verbali cum inimicitia reali constitui potest.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_QGOH_PQ_5:P0026","trace_type":"Direct","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"},{"id":"RAW_QGOH_PQ_5:P0030","trace_type":"Direct","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_QGOH_PQ_5:NX7","text":"Civitas rationalis non est mera coactio metu fundata, neque libertas rationalis est solitudo sibi soli obtemperans.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_QGOH_PQ_5:P0032","trace_type":"Tension","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"},{"id":"RAW_QGOH_PQ_5:P0071","trace_type":"Tension","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_QGOH_PQ_5:NX8","text":"Concordia non est adulatio, abiectio, falsa pietas, libido meretricia aut affectus tristitiae specie religiosus.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_QGOH_PQ_5:P0077","trace_type":"Negative","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"},{"id":"RAW_QGOH_PQ_5:P0081","trace_type":"Negative","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"},{"id":"RAW_QGOH_PQ_5:P0083","trace_type":"Negative","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"},{"id":"RAW_QGOH_PQ_5:P0085","trace_type":"Negative","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_QGOH_PQ_5:NX9","text":"Tristitia, superstitio, metus et commiseratio non sunt principia vitae rationalis, quamvis aliquando pietatis aut honestatis imaginem gerant.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_QGOH_PQ_5:P0071","trace_type":"Negative","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"},{"id":"RAW_QGOH_PQ_5:P0085","trace_type":"Negative","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"},{"id":"RAW_QGOH_PQ_5:P0102","trace_type":"Negative","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_QGOH_PQ_5:NX10","text":"Pecunia non est finis rationis vitae, sed instrumentum ad mutuas operas et corporis sustentationem reductibile.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_QGOH_PQ_5:P0096","trace_type":"Direct","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"},{"id":"RAW_QGOH_PQ_5:P0098","trace_type":"Direct","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_QGOH_PQ_5:NX11","text":"Acquiescentia in necessitate Naturae non est potestas externa omnia ad usum humanum redigendi.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_QGOH_PQ_5:P0104","trace_type":"Negative","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]}],"original_name":"Apophatic Structure"},{"number":"4","name":"Derivative Propositions","items":[{"id":"CH_QGOH_PQ_5:D1","text":"Eadem fortitudo requiritur ad periculum prudenter declinandum ac ad idem superandum, quia utrumque contra affectus caecos ordinari debet.","refs":["CH_QGOH_PQ_5:A1","CH_QGOH_PQ_5:A8"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"From","passages":[{"id":"RAW_QGOH_PQ_5:P0008","trace_type":"Direct","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"},{"id":"RAW_QGOH_PQ_5:P0010","trace_type":"Direct","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_QGOH_PQ_5:D2","text":"Homo liber beneficia ignarorum, quantum potest, declinat, sed ea recipere et secundum ingenium benefactoris recompensare potest, ubi necessitas humana aut cautio socialis id postulat.","refs":["CH_QGOH_PQ_5:A9","CH_QGOH_PQ_5:A15","CH_QGOH_PQ_5:A16"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"From","passages":[{"id":"RAW_QGOH_PQ_5:P0014","trace_type":"Composite","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"},{"id":"RAW_QGOH_PQ_5:P0016","trace_type":"Composite","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"},{"id":"RAW_QGOH_PQ_5:P0018","trace_type":"Composite","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_QGOH_PQ_5:D3","text":"Gratia vera inter homines liberos tantum stabilis est, quia eorum mutua utilitas, amicitia et beneficentia ex Ratione procedunt.","refs":["CH_QGOH_PQ_5:A1","CH_QGOH_PQ_5:A9","CH_QGOH_PQ_5:A10"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"From","passages":[{"id":"RAW_QGOH_PQ_5:P0020","trace_type":"Direct","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"},{"id":"RAW_QGOH_PQ_5:P0022","trace_type":"Direct","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_QGOH_PQ_5:D4","text":"Homo liber semper cum fide agit, quia dolus malus, si ratio conservationis esset, condicionem ipsam societatis rationalis destrueret.","refs":["CH_QGOH_PQ_5:A9","CH_QGOH_PQ_5:A10"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"From","passages":[{"id":"RAW_QGOH_PQ_5:P0026","trace_type":"Direct","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"},{"id":"RAW_QGOH_PQ_5:P0028","trace_type":"Direct","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"},{"id":"RAW_QGOH_PQ_5:P0030","trace_type":"Direct","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_QGOH_PQ_5:D5","text":"Obedientia legibus communibus, ubi ad communem vitam et utilitatem ordinantur, non libertatem minuit sed libertatem rationalem perficit.","refs":["CH_QGOH_PQ_5:A1","CH_QGOH_PQ_5:A9","CH_QGOH_PQ_5:A11"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"From","passages":[{"id":"RAW_QGOH_PQ_5:P0032","trace_type":"Direct","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"},{"id":"RAW_QGOH_PQ_5:P0034","trace_type":"Direct","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_QGOH_PQ_5:D6","text":"Cura egentium ad societatem integram pertinet, quia facultas privata neque universali necessitati neque stabili amicitiae sufficere potest.","refs":["CH_QGOH_PQ_5:A9","CH_QGOH_PQ_5:A13","CH_QGOH_PQ_5:A15"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"From","passages":[{"id":"RAW_QGOH_PQ_5:P0073","trace_type":"Direct","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_QGOH_PQ_5:D7","text":"Res non humanae conservari, destrui aut ad usum adaptari possunt, quatenus corpus conservant, Mentem adiuvent et vitam rationalem non impediant.","refs":["CH_QGOH_PQ_5:A5","CH_QGOH_PQ_5:A12","CH_QGOH_PQ_5:A13"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"From","passages":[{"id":"RAW_QGOH_PQ_5:P0055","trace_type":"Composite","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"},{"id":"RAW_QGOH_PQ_5:P0091","trace_type":"Composite","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"},{"id":"RAW_QGOH_PQ_5:P0093","trace_type":"Composite","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_QGOH_PQ_5:D8","text":"Mutuae operae et pecunia ex indigentia corporali et diversitate necessitatum oriuntur, sed pecunia mentem vulgi capere potest, ubi a vero usu separatur.","refs":["CH_QGOH_PQ_5:A13","CH_QGOH_PQ_5:A14","CH_QGOH_PQ_5:A15"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"From","passages":[{"id":"RAW_QGOH_PQ_5:P0093","trace_type":"Composite","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"},{"id":"RAW_QGOH_PQ_5:P0096","trace_type":"Composite","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"},{"id":"RAW_QGOH_PQ_5:P0098","trace_type":"Composite","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_QGOH_PQ_5:D9","text":"Inter homines affectibus obnoxios, virtus politica est affectus aliorum non imitari, vitia non exprobrare, et amorem ad vitam rationalem incitandam conciliare.","refs":["CH_QGOH_PQ_5:A8","CH_QGOH_PQ_5:A15","CH_QGOH_PQ_5:A16"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"From","passages":[{"id":"RAW_QGOH_PQ_5:P0065","trace_type":"Composite","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"},{"id":"RAW_QGOH_PQ_5:P0089","trace_type":"Composite","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_QGOH_PQ_5:D10","text":"Eventus externos inevitabiles aequo animo ferre licet, ubi intellectus cognoscit se officium suum fecisse et potentiam finitam ultra limites suos non extendi posse.","refs":["CH_QGOH_PQ_5:A7","CH_QGOH_PQ_5:A17"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"From","passages":[{"id":"RAW_QGOH_PQ_5:P0104","trace_type":"Direct","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"},{"id":"RAW_QGOH_PQ_5:P0105","trace_type":"Direct","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]}],"original_name":"Derivative Propositions"},{"number":"5","name":"Super-Derivatives","items":[{"id":"CH_QGOH_PQ_5:SD1","text":"Libertas non est proprietas interior individui solitarii, sed gradus agendi qui simul ex cognitione, affectionum dispositione, corporali potentia, societate et iure civili constituitur.","refs":["CH_QGOH_PQ_5:A1","CH_QGOH_PQ_5:A4","CH_QGOH_PQ_5:A9","CH_QGOH_PQ_5:A11","CH_QGOH_PQ_5:A13"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"From","passages":[{"id":"RAW_QGOH_PQ_5:P0001","trace_type":"Structural","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"},{"id":"RAW_QGOH_PQ_5:P0043","trace_type":"Structural","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"},{"id":"RAW_QGOH_PQ_5:P0057","trace_type":"Structural","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"},{"id":"RAW_QGOH_PQ_5:P0093","trace_type":"Structural","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"},{"id":"RAW_QGOH_PQ_5:P0032","trace_type":"Structural","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_QGOH_PQ_5:SD2","text":"Virtus reclassificatur ut potentia rationaliter ordinata ad conservandum, intelligendum et cum aliis concorditer agendum, non ut obedientia legi morali externae.","refs":["CH_QGOH_PQ_5:A4","CH_QGOH_PQ_5:A5","CH_QGOH_PQ_5:A6","CH_QGOH_PQ_5:A9"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"From","passages":[{"id":"RAW_QGOH_PQ_5:P0043","trace_type":"Structural","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"},{"id":"RAW_QGOH_PQ_5:P0045","trace_type":"Structural","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"},{"id":"RAW_QGOH_PQ_5:P0047","trace_type":"Structural","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"},{"id":"RAW_QGOH_PQ_5:P0057","trace_type":"Structural","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_QGOH_PQ_5:SD3","text":"Ordo politicus rationalis fidem, amorem, iustitiam et curam publicam indigentium requirit, quia beneficentia privata et metus neque sufficientiam materialem neque concordiam fidelem producunt.","refs":["CH_QGOH_PQ_5:A9","CH_QGOH_PQ_5:A10","CH_QGOH_PQ_5:A15","CH_QGOH_PQ_5:A16"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"From","passages":[{"id":"RAW_QGOH_PQ_5:P0026","trace_type":"Structural","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"},{"id":"RAW_QGOH_PQ_5:P0069","trace_type":"Structural","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"},{"id":"RAW_QGOH_PQ_5:P0071","trace_type":"Structural","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"},{"id":"RAW_QGOH_PQ_5:P0073","trace_type":"Structural","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_QGOH_PQ_5:SD4","text":"Corpus, cognitio et oeconomia constituunt unam infrastructuram libertatis: varietas corporalis postulat res externas, res externas mutua opera, mutuas operas media commutationis.","refs":["CH_QGOH_PQ_5:A13","CH_QGOH_PQ_5:A15"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"From","passages":[{"id":"RAW_QGOH_PQ_5:P0093","trace_type":"Structural","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"},{"id":"RAW_QGOH_PQ_5:P0094","trace_type":"Structural","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"},{"id":"RAW_QGOH_PQ_5:P0096","trace_type":"Structural","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_QGOH_PQ_5:SD5","text":"Ius Naturae usum rerum ex potentia et utilitate iudicata describit; Ratio autem hunc usum ad corporis conservationem, cognitionem et societatem stabilem ordinat.","refs":["CH_QGOH_PQ_5:A5","CH_QGOH_PQ_5:A9","CH_QGOH_PQ_5:A12","CH_QGOH_PQ_5:A13"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"From","passages":[{"id":"RAW_QGOH_PQ_5:P0055","trace_type":"Structural","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"},{"id":"RAW_QGOH_PQ_5:P0057","trace_type":"Structural","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"},{"id":"RAW_QGOH_PQ_5:P0091","trace_type":"Structural","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"},{"id":"RAW_QGOH_PQ_5:P0093","trace_type":"Structural","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_QGOH_PQ_5:SD6","text":"Necessitas Naturae libertatem non delet, sed libertatem ab imperio externorum ad intellectam participationem in ordine necessario transfert.","refs":["CH_QGOH_PQ_5:A2","CH_QGOH_PQ_5:A7","CH_QGOH_PQ_5:A17"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"From","passages":[{"id":"RAW_QGOH_PQ_5:P0003","trace_type":"Structural","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"},{"id":"RAW_QGOH_PQ_5:P0051","trace_type":"Structural","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"},{"id":"RAW_QGOH_PQ_5:P0104","trace_type":"Structural","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"},{"id":"RAW_QGOH_PQ_5:P0105","trace_type":"Structural","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]}],"original_name":"Super-Derivatives"},{"number":"6","name":"Deep Apophatics","items":[{"id":"CH_QGOH_PQ_5:DNX1","text":"Falsum esse debet hominem esse causam sui actus penitus indeterminatam, si omnis Cupiditas ex natura propria et ordine Naturae necessario sequitur.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_QGOH_PQ_5:P0041","trace_type":"Negative","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"},{"id":"RAW_QGOH_PQ_5:P0051","trace_type":"Negative","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"},{"id":"RAW_QGOH_PQ_5:P0104","trace_type":"Negative","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_QGOH_PQ_5:DNX2","text":"Falsum esse debet bonum et malum esse genera absoluta rerum, si eorum ratio a potentia, cognitione et vita rationali dependet.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_QGOH_PQ_5:P0045","trace_type":"Negative","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"},{"id":"RAW_QGOH_PQ_5:P0049","trace_type":"Negative","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_QGOH_PQ_5:DNX3","text":"Falsum esse debet individuum sibi sufficere posse, si humana utilitas maxima ex hominibus Ratione ductis, mutuis operis et iure communi provenit.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_QGOH_PQ_5:P0057","trace_type":"Negative","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"},{"id":"RAW_QGOH_PQ_5:P0063","trace_type":"Negative","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"},{"id":"RAW_QGOH_PQ_5:P0096","trace_type":"Negative","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_QGOH_PQ_5:DNX4","text":"Falsum esse debet Mentem rationalem a conditionibus corporis et rerum externarum separari posse, si corporis aptitudo mentis aptitudinem ad cogitandum auget.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_QGOH_PQ_5:P0093","trace_type":"Negative","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"},{"id":"RAW_QGOH_PQ_5:P0094","trace_type":"Negative","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_QGOH_PQ_5:DNX5","text":"Falsum esse debet metum, poenam, adulationem aut dolum fundamentum stabile societatis esse, si concordia rationalis fidem et generositatem requirit.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_QGOH_PQ_5:P0028","trace_type":"Negative","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"},{"id":"RAW_QGOH_PQ_5:P0069","trace_type":"Negative","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"},{"id":"RAW_QGOH_PQ_5:P0071","trace_type":"Negative","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"},{"id":"RAW_QGOH_PQ_5:P0081","trace_type":"Negative","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_QGOH_PQ_5:DNX6","text":"Falsum esse debet Ratio sine affectuum ordinatione operari posse, si affectus tantum per affectus contrarios et fortiores coerceri possunt.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_QGOH_PQ_5:P0008","trace_type":"Negative","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"},{"id":"RAW_QGOH_PQ_5:P0100","trace_type":"Negative","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_QGOH_PQ_5:DNX7","text":"Falsum esse debet societatem fidelem ex universali perfidia constitui posse, si perfidia generalis iura communia et cooperationem inanes facit.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_QGOH_PQ_5:P0028","trace_type":"Negative","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"},{"id":"RAW_QGOH_PQ_5:P0030","trace_type":"Negative","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]}],"original_name":"Deep Apophatics"},{"number":"7","name":"Super-Apophatics","items":[{"id":"CH_QGOH_PQ_5:SNX1","text":"Systema omnem prospectum humanum extra Naturam excludit: homo semper pars totius est, cuius ordinem sequi cogitur.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_QGOH_PQ_5:P0051","trace_type":"Negative","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"},{"id":"RAW_QGOH_PQ_5:P0053","trace_type":"Negative","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"},{"id":"RAW_QGOH_PQ_5:P0104","trace_type":"Negative","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_QGOH_PQ_5:SNX2","text":"Systema possibilitatem libertatis humanae absolutae et perpetuae excludit, dum figuram hominis perfecte liberi retinet ut terminum normativum.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_QGOH_PQ_5:P0001","trace_type":"Negative","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"},{"id":"RAW_QGOH_PQ_5:P0003","trace_type":"Negative","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_QGOH_PQ_5:SNX3","text":"Systema bonum ultimum mere privatum excludit, quia bonum rationaliter quaesitum simul aliis communicari et per societatem sustineri debet.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_QGOH_PQ_5:P0004","trace_type":"Negative","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"},{"id":"RAW_QGOH_PQ_5:P0022","trace_type":"Negative","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"},{"id":"RAW_QGOH_PQ_5:P0057","trace_type":"Negative","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_QGOH_PQ_5:SNX4","text":"Systema communitatem sine mediationibus affectivis, iuridicis et materialibus excludit, quia homines non plerumque ex sola Ratione vivunt.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_QGOH_PQ_5:P0059","trace_type":"Negative","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"},{"id":"RAW_QGOH_PQ_5:P0065","trace_type":"Negative","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"},{"id":"RAW_QGOH_PQ_5:P0071","trace_type":"Negative","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"},{"id":"RAW_QGOH_PQ_5:P0096","trace_type":"Negative","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]}],"original_name":"Super-Apophatics"},{"number":"8","name":"Formal Generative Schema","items":[{"id":"CH_QGOH_PQ_5:FGS1","text":"Necessitas Naturae → conatus et Cupiditates → distinctio actionum ex cognitione adequata et passionum ex cognitione inadequata.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_QGOH_PQ_5:P0041","trace_type":"Direct","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"},{"id":"RAW_QGOH_PQ_5:P0043","trace_type":"Direct","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_QGOH_PQ_5:FGS2","text":"Actiones rationales → bonum ut vita rationalis → perfectio intellectus → beatitudo ut acquiescentia cognitiva.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_QGOH_PQ_5:P0045","trace_type":"Composite","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"},{"id":"RAW_QGOH_PQ_5:P0047","trace_type":"Composite","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"},{"id":"RAW_QGOH_PQ_5:P0049","trace_type":"Composite","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_QGOH_PQ_5:FGS3","text":"Potentia externa superior + affectuum servitus → imminutio libertatis → necessitas affectuum contrariorum, fortitudinis et vigilantiae.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_QGOH_PQ_5:P0008","trace_type":"Composite","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"},{"id":"RAW_QGOH_PQ_5:P0051","trace_type":"Composite","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"},{"id":"RAW_QGOH_PQ_5:P0100","trace_type":"Composite","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_QGOH_PQ_5:FGS4","text":"Conservatio rationalis sui → bonum commune → amicitia, gratia, fides → exclusio doli mali.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_QGOH_PQ_5:P0020","trace_type":"Structural","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"},{"id":"RAW_QGOH_PQ_5:P0022","trace_type":"Structural","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"},{"id":"RAW_QGOH_PQ_5:P0026","trace_type":"Structural","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"},{"id":"RAW_QGOH_PQ_5:P0028","trace_type":"Structural","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_QGOH_PQ_5:FGS5","text":"Utilitas communis + pluralitas affectiva hominum → ius civile, iustitia, amor et generositas → libertas maior in Civitate.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_QGOH_PQ_5:P0032","trace_type":"Structural","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"},{"id":"RAW_QGOH_PQ_5:P0069","trace_type":"Structural","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"},{"id":"RAW_QGOH_PQ_5:P0071","trace_type":"Structural","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_QGOH_PQ_5:FGS6","text":"Corpus multiplex + rerum externarum necessitas → usus Naturae, mutua opera et pecunia → moderatio cupiditatis et Laetitiae.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_QGOH_PQ_5:P0093","trace_type":"Structural","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"},{"id":"RAW_QGOH_PQ_5:P0096","trace_type":"Structural","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"},{"id":"RAW_QGOH_PQ_5:P0098","trace_type":"Structural","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"},{"id":"RAW_QGOH_PQ_5:P0100","trace_type":"Structural","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_QGOH_PQ_5:FGS7","text":"Eventus inevitabiles extra potentiam humanam → cognitio necessitatis → acquiescentia Mentis in ordine totius Naturae.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_QGOH_PQ_5:P0104","trace_type":"Direct","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"},{"id":"RAW_QGOH_PQ_5:P0105","trace_type":"Direct","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]}],"original_name":"Formal Generative Schema"},{"number":"9","name":"Argument Flow Map","items":[{"id":"CH_QGOH_PQ_5:AF1","text":"Libertas hypothetica perfecta → fortitudo in periculis → prudentia circa beneficia ignarorum → gratia et fides inter liberos → libertas civilis maior quam solitudo.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_QGOH_PQ_5:P0001","trace_type":"Structural","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"},{"id":"RAW_QGOH_PQ_5:P0006","trace_type":"Structural","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"},{"id":"RAW_QGOH_PQ_5:P0014","trace_type":"Structural","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"},{"id":"RAW_QGOH_PQ_5:P0020","trace_type":"Structural","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"},{"id":"RAW_QGOH_PQ_5:P0026","trace_type":"Structural","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"},{"id":"RAW_QGOH_PQ_5:P0032","trace_type":"Structural","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_QGOH_PQ_5:AF2","text":"Conatus necessarius → actio/passio → bonum ut vita rationalis → conservatio corporis et Mentis → societas, ius et oeconomia → moderatio affectuum → acquiescentia in necessitate Naturae.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_QGOH_PQ_5:P0041","trace_type":"Structural","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"},{"id":"RAW_QGOH_PQ_5:P0043","trace_type":"Structural","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"},{"id":"RAW_QGOH_PQ_5:P0049","trace_type":"Structural","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"},{"id":"RAW_QGOH_PQ_5:P0093","trace_type":"Structural","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"},{"id":"RAW_QGOH_PQ_5:P0104","trace_type":"Structural","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]}],"original_name":"Argument Flow Map"},{"number":"10","name":"Map-Effects","items":[{"id":"CH_QGOH_PQ_5:ME1","text":"Libertas e figura arbitrii privati in modum agendi ex Ratione, legibus communibus et relationibus utilibus constitutum transfertur.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_QGOH_PQ_5:P0032","trace_type":"Structural","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"},{"id":"RAW_QGOH_PQ_5:P0034","trace_type":"Structural","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"},{"id":"RAW_QGOH_PQ_5:P0057","trace_type":"Structural","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_QGOH_PQ_5:ME2","text":"Fuga tempestiva e genere timiditatis in genus fortitudinis reclassificatur, si eadem animi praesentia qua pugna eligitur.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_QGOH_PQ_5:P0006","trace_type":"Direct","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"},{"id":"RAW_QGOH_PQ_5:P0010","trace_type":"Direct","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_QGOH_PQ_5:ME3","text":"Beneficium, gratia et ingratitudo a codice morali simplici ad structuram affectivam, rationalem et socialem distinguendam transferuntur.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_QGOH_PQ_5:P0016","trace_type":"Structural","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"},{"id":"RAW_QGOH_PQ_5:P0018","trace_type":"Structural","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"},{"id":"RAW_QGOH_PQ_5:P0022","trace_type":"Structural","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"},{"id":"RAW_QGOH_PQ_5:P0024","trace_type":"Structural","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_QGOH_PQ_5:ME4","text":"Civitas non opponitur libertati; Civitas rationaliter ordinata fit medium quo libertas singularis actu maior efficitur.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_QGOH_PQ_5:P0032","trace_type":"Direct","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"},{"id":"RAW_QGOH_PQ_5:P0034","trace_type":"Direct","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_QGOH_PQ_5:ME5","text":"Affectus sociales dividuntur in eos qui concordiam fidelem augent et eos qui tantum speciem concordiae per metum, captationem, servitutem aut tristitiam fingunt.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_QGOH_PQ_5:P0069","trace_type":"Composite","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"},{"id":"RAW_QGOH_PQ_5:P0071","trace_type":"Composite","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"},{"id":"RAW_QGOH_PQ_5:P0077","trace_type":"Composite","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"},{"id":"RAW_QGOH_PQ_5:P0081","trace_type":"Composite","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"},{"id":"RAW_QGOH_PQ_5:P0089","trace_type":"Composite","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_QGOH_PQ_5:ME6","text":"Corpus, res externae, nutritio, labor et pecunia non sunt adiuncta extrinseca, sed condiciones cognitivas et sociales vitae rationalis constituunt.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_QGOH_PQ_5:P0093","trace_type":"Structural","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"},{"id":"RAW_QGOH_PQ_5:P0096","trace_type":"Structural","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"},{"id":"RAW_QGOH_PQ_5:P0098","trace_type":"Structural","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_QGOH_PQ_5:ME7","text":"Necessitas Naturae non amplius apparet ut fatum libertati extrinsecum, sed ut ordo cui intellectus finitus consentiendo libertatem relativam exercet.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_QGOH_PQ_5:P0104","trace_type":"Direct","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"},{"id":"RAW_QGOH_PQ_5:P0105","trace_type":"Direct","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]}],"original_name":"Map-Effects"},{"number":"11","name":"Altitude Levels","items":[{"id":"CH_QGOH_PQ_5:ALT1","text":"Surface — Textus regulas de periculis, beneficiis, gratia, fide, civitate, matrimonio, pudore, pecunia et toleratione eventuum exponit.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_QGOH_PQ_5:P0006","trace_type":"Structural","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"},{"id":"RAW_QGOH_PQ_5:P0014","trace_type":"Structural","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"},{"id":"RAW_QGOH_PQ_5:P0020","trace_type":"Structural","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"},{"id":"RAW_QGOH_PQ_5:P0026","trace_type":"Structural","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"},{"id":"RAW_QGOH_PQ_5:P0032","trace_type":"Structural","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"},{"id":"RAW_QGOH_PQ_5:P0079","trace_type":"Structural","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"},{"id":"RAW_QGOH_PQ_5:P0096","trace_type":"Structural","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"},{"id":"RAW_QGOH_PQ_5:P0104","trace_type":"Structural","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_QGOH_PQ_5:ALT2","text":"Structural — Hae regulae ex distinctione inter actionem et passionem, ex superioritate causarum externarum et ex interdependentia corporali ac sociali procedunt.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_QGOH_PQ_5:P0041","trace_type":"Structural","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"},{"id":"RAW_QGOH_PQ_5:P0043","trace_type":"Structural","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"},{"id":"RAW_QGOH_PQ_5:P0051","trace_type":"Structural","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"},{"id":"RAW_QGOH_PQ_5:P0093","trace_type":"Structural","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"},{"id":"RAW_QGOH_PQ_5:P0096","trace_type":"Structural","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_QGOH_PQ_5:ALT3","text":"Metastrategic — Textus certamen morale in artem ordinandi affectus, instituendi fidem, conciliandi amorem et construendi condiciones cooperationis convertit.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_QGOH_PQ_5:P0063","trace_type":"Structural","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"},{"id":"RAW_QGOH_PQ_5:P0069","trace_type":"Structural","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"},{"id":"RAW_QGOH_PQ_5:P0071","trace_type":"Structural","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"},{"id":"RAW_QGOH_PQ_5:P0089","trace_type":"Structural","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_QGOH_PQ_5:ALT4","text":"Ontological/Temporal — Homo pars Naturae necessario ordinatae est; affectus praesentem suavitatem prae futuro aestimat, Ratio autem necessitatem intellegens stabilem Mentis acquiescentiam quaerit.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_QGOH_PQ_5:P0041","trace_type":"Structural","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"},{"id":"RAW_QGOH_PQ_5:P0100","trace_type":"Structural","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"},{"id":"RAW_QGOH_PQ_5:P0104","trace_type":"Structural","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"},{"id":"RAW_QGOH_PQ_5:P0105","trace_type":"Structural","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]}],"original_name":"Altitude Levels"},{"number":"12","name":"Minimal Reconstruction Test","items":[{"id":"CH_QGOH_PQ_5:MRT1","text":"Axiomata satis sunt ad seriem principalem restituendam: necessitas Naturae, adequatio, actio et passio, utilitas rationalis, societas civilis et acquiescentia.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_QGOH_PQ_5:P0041","trace_type":"Structural","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"},{"id":"RAW_QGOH_PQ_5:P0043","trace_type":"Structural","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"},{"id":"RAW_QGOH_PQ_5:P0045","trace_type":"Structural","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"},{"id":"RAW_QGOH_PQ_5:P0057","trace_type":"Structural","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"},{"id":"RAW_QGOH_PQ_5:P0032","trace_type":"Structural","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"},{"id":"RAW_QGOH_PQ_5:P0104","trace_type":"Structural","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_QGOH_PQ_5:MRT2","text":"Firmissimus nexus systematis est transitus ab impotentia affectiva ad necessitatem cooperationis rationalis: homo finitus nec solus nec dolo stabiliter conservari potest.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_QGOH_PQ_5:P0057","trace_type":"Structural","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"},{"id":"RAW_QGOH_PQ_5:P0063","trace_type":"Structural","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"},{"id":"RAW_QGOH_PQ_5:P0096","trace_type":"Structural","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"},{"id":"RAW_QGOH_PQ_5:P0028","trace_type":"Structural","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_QGOH_PQ_5:MRT3","text":"Figura hominis perfecte liberi simul norma argumenti et hypothesis falsa manet; transitus ab hoc termino hypothetico ad hominem actualem graduatim liberum non plene formalizatur.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_QGOH_PQ_5:P0001","trace_type":"Tension","confidence":"medium","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"},{"id":"RAW_QGOH_PQ_5:P0003","trace_type":"Tension","confidence":"medium","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"},{"id":"RAW_QGOH_PQ_5:P0104","trace_type":"Tension","confidence":"medium","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_QGOH_PQ_5:MRT4","text":"Systema Civitatem libertatis medium facit, sed discrimen inter Civitatem ex communi decreto viventem et Civitatem re vera metu aut cupiditate gubernatam hic non plene resolvit.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_QGOH_PQ_5:P0032","trace_type":"Tension","confidence":"medium","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"},{"id":"RAW_QGOH_PQ_5:P0034","trace_type":"Tension","confidence":"medium","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"},{"id":"RAW_QGOH_PQ_5:P0071","trace_type":"Tension","confidence":"medium","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_QGOH_PQ_5:MRT5","text":"Ius utendi rebus externis late definitur, dum eius moderatio per utilitatem rationalem affirmatur; criterium quo utilitas singularis et communis in casu conflictus componantur non explicatur.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[]},{"id":"CH_QGOH_PQ_5:MRT6","text":"Affectus per affectus coercendos esse textus statuit, sed vis concreta qua Ratio affectum contrarium efficacem producit in hoc loco praesupponitur potius quam demonstratur.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_QGOH_PQ_5:P0008","trace_type":"Tension","confidence":"medium","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"},{"id":"RAW_QGOH_PQ_5:P0100","trace_type":"Tension","confidence":"medium","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"},{"id":"RAW_QGOH_PQ_5:P0102","trace_type":"Tension","confidence":"medium","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_QGOH_PQ_5:MRT7","text":"Bonum et malum in figura hominis perfecte liberi evanescunt, sed in ratione vitae finitae necessaria manent; distinctio perspectivarum implicatur, non autem explicite sistematizatur.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_QGOH_PQ_5:P0001","trace_type":"Tension","confidence":"medium","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"},{"id":"RAW_QGOH_PQ_5:P0049","trace_type":"Tension","confidence":"medium","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"},{"id":"RAW_QGOH_PQ_5:P0102","trace_type":"Tension","confidence":"medium","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_QGOH_PQ_5:MRT8","text":"Restitutio motum verum servat, quia ab ideali libertatis ad fortitudinem, societatem, condiciones materiales et tandem ad intellectam necessitatem Naturae procedit.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_QGOH_PQ_5:P0001","trace_type":"Structural","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"},{"id":"RAW_QGOH_PQ_5:P0006","trace_type":"Structural","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"},{"id":"RAW_QGOH_PQ_5:P0032","trace_type":"Structural","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"},{"id":"RAW_QGOH_PQ_5:P0093","trace_type":"Structural","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"},{"id":"RAW_QGOH_PQ_5:P0104","trace_type":"Structural","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]}],"original_name":"Minimal Reconstruction Test"}],"language_code":"la","language_name":"Latina","translations":[{"id":"CAREVAR_20261015T014838249408Z_f5a0f661","language_code":"en","language_name":"English","title":"Part Four — On Human Bondage, or the Powers of the Affects · passage 5","source_sha256":"ab1792b85ae794c8125a85d2c355f002ac21c5a6e1889ba64777539330dcc6fb","sha256":"0ac6a7930d0b3f7875cef03bdda608b1355800f306616bb3a598cd64c96d639d","rendition_type":"machine","review_status":"machine","model":"gpt-5.6-terra","created":"2026-10-15T01:48:38.250377+00:00","sections":[{"number":"1","name":"Argumental Spine","items":[{"id":"CH_QGOH_PQ_5:AS1","text":"The text asks how human freedom can be achieved, under the necessity of Nature and among people for the most part led by affects, without the fiction of causal exemption.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_QGOH_PQ_5:P0003","trace_type":"Structural","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"},{"id":"RAW_QGOH_PQ_5:P0041","trace_type":"Structural","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"},{"id":"RAW_QGOH_PQ_5:P0104","trace_type":"Structural","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_QGOH_PQ_5:AS2","text":"Perfectly conceived freedom is posited as a hypothetical limit: a human being led by Reason alone enjoys adequate ideas and transcends the concepts of good and evil.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_QGOH_PQ_5:P0001","trace_type":"Direct","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"},{"id":"RAW_QGOH_PQ_5:P0003","trace_type":"Direct","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_QGOH_PQ_5:AS3","text":"The diagnostic argument derives bondage from mutilated cognition, the dominion of affects, and the power of external causes; it restores freedom through rational action and the moderation of affects.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_QGOH_PQ_5:P0043","trace_type":"Composite","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"},{"id":"RAW_QGOH_PQ_5:P0051","trace_type":"Composite","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"},{"id":"RAW_QGOH_PQ_5:P0008","trace_type":"Composite","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"},{"id":"RAW_QGOH_PQ_5:P0037","trace_type":"Composite","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_QGOH_PQ_5:AS4","text":"The practical movement passes from dangers, benefits, gratitude, and faith to the political conclusion that a human being led by Reason is freer in the State than in solitude.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_QGOH_PQ_5:P0006","trace_type":"Structural","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"},{"id":"RAW_QGOH_PQ_5:P0014","trace_type":"Structural","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"},{"id":"RAW_QGOH_PQ_5:P0020","trace_type":"Structural","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"},{"id":"RAW_QGOH_PQ_5:P0026","trace_type":"Structural","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"},{"id":"RAW_QGOH_PQ_5:P0032","trace_type":"Structural","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_QGOH_PQ_5:AS5","text":"The appendix connects conatus, the account of good, the preservation of the body, society, economy, and acquiescence in the order of Nature into a single account of rational life.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_QGOH_PQ_5:P0041","trace_type":"Structural","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"},{"id":"RAW_QGOH_PQ_5:P0047","trace_type":"Structural","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"},{"id":"RAW_QGOH_PQ_5:P0055","trace_type":"Structural","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"},{"id":"RAW_QGOH_PQ_5:P0063","trace_type":"Structural","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"},{"id":"RAW_QGOH_PQ_5:P0093","trace_type":"Structural","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"},{"id":"RAW_QGOH_PQ_5:P0104","trace_type":"Structural","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]}],"original_name":"Argumental Spine"},{"number":"2","name":"Load-Bearing Axioms","items":[{"id":"CH_QGOH_PQ_5:A1","text":"A free human being is free insofar as he is led by Reason alone and acts from adequate ideas.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_QGOH_PQ_5:P0001","trace_type":"Direct","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_QGOH_PQ_5:A2","text":"Complete freedom, by which a human being is born free and remains free, does not belong to a human being considered as a part of Nature, but can be conceived only hypothetically.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_QGOH_PQ_5:P0003","trace_type":"Direct","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_QGOH_PQ_5:A3","text":"Every human conatus or Desire follows from the necessity of human nature, or from the order of the whole of Nature.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_QGOH_PQ_5:P0041","trace_type":"Direct","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_QGOH_PQ_5:A4","text":"Desires arising from adequate ideas are actions and indicate human power; those arising from inadequate cognition are passions and are determined by external power.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_QGOH_PQ_5:P0043","trace_type":"Direct","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_QGOH_PQ_5:A5","text":"Actions determined by Reason are always good; the remaining Desires can possess goodness or badness only relatively.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_QGOH_PQ_5:P0045","trace_type":"Direct","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_QGOH_PQ_5:A6","text":"Good is what advances rational life and the perfection of the intellect; blessedness is acquiescence arising from the intuitive knowledge of God.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_QGOH_PQ_5:P0047","trace_type":"Composite","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"},{"id":"RAW_QGOH_PQ_5:P0049","trace_type":"Composite","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_QGOH_PQ_5:A7","text":"The power of external causes infinitely surpasses human power; evil befalls a human being, insofar as he suffers, from external causes.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_QGOH_PQ_5:P0051","trace_type":"Composite","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"},{"id":"RAW_QGOH_PQ_5:P0104","trace_type":"Composite","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_QGOH_PQ_5:A8","text":"An affect cannot be restrained or removed except by a contrary and stronger affect.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_QGOH_PQ_5:P0008","trace_type":"Direct","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_QGOH_PQ_5:A9","text":"Rational self-preservation at the same time demands common advantage, because a human being led by Reason also desires for other human beings the good he desires for himself.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_QGOH_PQ_5:P0004","trace_type":"Composite","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"},{"id":"RAW_QGOH_PQ_5:P0022","trace_type":"Composite","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"},{"id":"RAW_QGOH_PQ_5:P0034","trace_type":"Composite","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_QGOH_PQ_5:A10","text":"Malicious deceit cannot be a rational rule, because, if it were universal, it would destroy trust, agreements, and common rights.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_QGOH_PQ_5:P0028","trace_type":"Direct","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"},{"id":"RAW_QGOH_PQ_5:P0030","trace_type":"Direct","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_QGOH_PQ_5:A11","text":"A human being led by Reason finds his freedom more fully in the common rights of the State than in solitude.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_QGOH_PQ_5:P0032","trace_type":"Direct","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"},{"id":"RAW_QGOH_PQ_5:P0034","trace_type":"Direct","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_QGOH_PQ_5:A12","text":"Natural right grants a human being the right to remove what hinders his rational life and to use things which he judges useful for preservation.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_QGOH_PQ_5:P0055","trace_type":"Direct","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_QGOH_PQ_5:A13","text":"The body's aptitude for a greater number of affections and operations increases the Mind's aptitude for conceiving more things; the body requires diverse foods and things.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_QGOH_PQ_5:P0093","trace_type":"Direct","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"},{"id":"RAW_QGOH_PQ_5:P0094","trace_type":"Direct","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_QGOH_PQ_5:A14","text":"Joy increases human power, but unless it is moderated by Reason and vigilance, it falls into excess and into the prejudice of present sweetness.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_QGOH_PQ_5:P0100","trace_type":"Composite","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"},{"id":"RAW_QGOH_PQ_5:P0102","trace_type":"Composite","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_QGOH_PQ_5:A15","text":"People for the most part act not from Reason but from diversity of dispositions, envy, hatred, revenge, and other affects.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_QGOH_PQ_5:P0059","trace_type":"Composite","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"},{"id":"RAW_QGOH_PQ_5:P0065","trace_type":"Composite","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"},{"id":"RAW_QGOH_PQ_5:P0067","trace_type":"Composite","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_QGOH_PQ_5:A16","text":"Stable concord is nourished by justice, fairness, honesty, love, and generosity; fear produces only concord without trust.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_QGOH_PQ_5:P0069","trace_type":"Composite","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"},{"id":"RAW_QGOH_PQ_5:P0071","trace_type":"Composite","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"},{"id":"RAW_QGOH_PQ_5:P0089","trace_type":"Composite","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_QGOH_PQ_5:A17","text":"Understanding that all things follow from the necessity of Nature disposes the better part of the Mind toward ordered acquiescence.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_QGOH_PQ_5:P0104","trace_type":"Direct","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"},{"id":"RAW_QGOH_PQ_5:P0105","trace_type":"Direct","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]}],"original_name":"Load-Bearing Axioms"},{"number":"3","name":"Apophatic Structure","items":[{"id":"CH_QGOH_PQ_5:NX1","text":"Freedom is not empirical immunity from affects, external causes, or the order of the whole of Nature.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_QGOH_PQ_5:P0003","trace_type":"Negative","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"},{"id":"RAW_QGOH_PQ_5:P0051","trace_type":"Negative","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"},{"id":"RAW_QGOH_PQ_5:P0104","trace_type":"Negative","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_QGOH_PQ_5:NX2","text":"Good and evil are not absolute properties of things, but relations to rational life, knowledge, and human utility.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_QGOH_PQ_5:P0001","trace_type":"Composite","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"},{"id":"RAW_QGOH_PQ_5:P0045","trace_type":"Composite","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"},{"id":"RAW_QGOH_PQ_5:P0049","trace_type":"Composite","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_QGOH_PQ_5:NX3","text":"Fortitude is not always confronting danger; timely flight can require the same presence of mind as combat.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_QGOH_PQ_5:P0006","trace_type":"Direct","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"},{"id":"RAW_QGOH_PQ_5:P0008","trace_type":"Direct","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"},{"id":"RAW_QGOH_PQ_5:P0010","trace_type":"Direct","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_QGOH_PQ_5:NX4","text":"A benefit received from an ignorant person is neither a simple obligation nor in itself a rational bond, because the benefactor's affective estimation can generate hatred.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_QGOH_PQ_5:P0016","trace_type":"Direct","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"},{"id":"RAW_QGOH_PQ_5:P0018","trace_type":"Direct","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_QGOH_PQ_5:NX5","text":"Gratitude is not commerce, solicitation, or corruptibility through gifts; diminishing harmful gifts is not ingratitude.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_QGOH_PQ_5:P0024","trace_type":"Direct","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_QGOH_PQ_5:NX6","text":"Civil trust cannot be constituted from malicious deceit, perfidy, or verbal agreement with real hostility.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_QGOH_PQ_5:P0026","trace_type":"Direct","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"},{"id":"RAW_QGOH_PQ_5:P0030","trace_type":"Direct","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_QGOH_PQ_5:NX7","text":"A rational State is not mere coercion founded on fear, nor is rational freedom solitude obeying itself alone.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_QGOH_PQ_5:P0032","trace_type":"Tension","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"},{"id":"RAW_QGOH_PQ_5:P0071","trace_type":"Tension","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_QGOH_PQ_5:NX8","text":"Concord is not flattery, abjection, false piety, meretricious lust, or an affect religious in the guise of sadness.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_QGOH_PQ_5:P0077","trace_type":"Negative","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"},{"id":"RAW_QGOH_PQ_5:P0081","trace_type":"Negative","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"},{"id":"RAW_QGOH_PQ_5:P0083","trace_type":"Negative","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"},{"id":"RAW_QGOH_PQ_5:P0085","trace_type":"Negative","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_QGOH_PQ_5:NX9","text":"Sadness, superstition, fear, and compassion are not principles of rational life, even though they may at times bear the appearance of piety or honesty.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_QGOH_PQ_5:P0071","trace_type":"Negative","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"},{"id":"RAW_QGOH_PQ_5:P0085","trace_type":"Negative","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"},{"id":"RAW_QGOH_PQ_5:P0102","trace_type":"Negative","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_QGOH_PQ_5:NX10","text":"Money is not the end of rational life, but is reducible to an instrument for reciprocal services and bodily sustenance.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_QGOH_PQ_5:P0096","trace_type":"Direct","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"},{"id":"RAW_QGOH_PQ_5:P0098","trace_type":"Direct","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_QGOH_PQ_5:NX11","text":"Acquiescence in the necessity of Nature is not an external power to reduce all things to human use.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_QGOH_PQ_5:P0104","trace_type":"Negative","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]}],"original_name":"Apophatic Structure"},{"number":"4","name":"Derivative Propositions","items":[{"id":"CH_QGOH_PQ_5:D1","text":"The same fortitude is required to avoid danger prudently as to overcome it, because both must be ordered against blind affects.","refs":["CH_QGOH_PQ_5:A1","CH_QGOH_PQ_5:A8"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"From","passages":[{"id":"RAW_QGOH_PQ_5:P0008","trace_type":"Direct","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"},{"id":"RAW_QGOH_PQ_5:P0010","trace_type":"Direct","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_QGOH_PQ_5:D2","text":"A free human being avoids, as far as he can, the benefits of ignorant persons, but can receive them and recompense them according to the disposition of the benefactor where human necessity or social caution requires it.","refs":["CH_QGOH_PQ_5:A9","CH_QGOH_PQ_5:A15","CH_QGOH_PQ_5:A16"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"From","passages":[{"id":"RAW_QGOH_PQ_5:P0014","trace_type":"Composite","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"},{"id":"RAW_QGOH_PQ_5:P0016","trace_type":"Composite","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"},{"id":"RAW_QGOH_PQ_5:P0018","trace_type":"Composite","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_QGOH_PQ_5:D3","text":"True gratitude is stable only among free human beings, because their reciprocal utility, friendship, and beneficence proceed from Reason.","refs":["CH_QGOH_PQ_5:A1","CH_QGOH_PQ_5:A9","CH_QGOH_PQ_5:A10"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"From","passages":[{"id":"RAW_QGOH_PQ_5:P0020","trace_type":"Direct","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"},{"id":"RAW_QGOH_PQ_5:P0022","trace_type":"Direct","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_QGOH_PQ_5:D4","text":"A free human being always acts in good faith, because malicious deceit, if it were a principle of preservation, would destroy the very condition of rational society.","refs":["CH_QGOH_PQ_5:A9","CH_QGOH_PQ_5:A10"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"From","passages":[{"id":"RAW_QGOH_PQ_5:P0026","trace_type":"Direct","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"},{"id":"RAW_QGOH_PQ_5:P0028","trace_type":"Direct","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"},{"id":"RAW_QGOH_PQ_5:P0030","trace_type":"Direct","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_QGOH_PQ_5:D5","text":"Obedience to common laws, where they are ordered to common life and utility, does not diminish freedom but perfects rational freedom.","refs":["CH_QGOH_PQ_5:A1","CH_QGOH_PQ_5:A9","CH_QGOH_PQ_5:A11"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"From","passages":[{"id":"RAW_QGOH_PQ_5:P0032","trace_type":"Direct","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"},{"id":"RAW_QGOH_PQ_5:P0034","trace_type":"Direct","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_QGOH_PQ_5:D6","text":"Care for the needy belongs to society as a whole, because private means can suffice neither for universal necessity nor for stable friendship.","refs":["CH_QGOH_PQ_5:A9","CH_QGOH_PQ_5:A13","CH_QGOH_PQ_5:A15"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"From","passages":[{"id":"RAW_QGOH_PQ_5:P0073","trace_type":"Direct","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_QGOH_PQ_5:D7","text":"Non-human things can be preserved, destroyed, or adapted to use insofar as they preserve the body, assist the Mind, and do not hinder rational life.","refs":["CH_QGOH_PQ_5:A5","CH_QGOH_PQ_5:A12","CH_QGOH_PQ_5:A13"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"From","passages":[{"id":"RAW_QGOH_PQ_5:P0055","trace_type":"Composite","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"},{"id":"RAW_QGOH_PQ_5:P0091","trace_type":"Composite","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"},{"id":"RAW_QGOH_PQ_5:P0093","trace_type":"Composite","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_QGOH_PQ_5:D8","text":"Reciprocal services and money arise from bodily need and the diversity of necessities, but money can captivate the mind of the multitude where it is separated from true use.","refs":["CH_QGOH_PQ_5:A13","CH_QGOH_PQ_5:A14","CH_QGOH_PQ_5:A15"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"From","passages":[{"id":"RAW_QGOH_PQ_5:P0093","trace_type":"Composite","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"},{"id":"RAW_QGOH_PQ_5:P0096","trace_type":"Composite","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"},{"id":"RAW_QGOH_PQ_5:P0098","trace_type":"Composite","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_QGOH_PQ_5:D9","text":"Among people subject to affects, political virtue is not to imitate the affects of others, not to reproach vices, and to win over love for the encouragement of rational life.","refs":["CH_QGOH_PQ_5:A8","CH_QGOH_PQ_5:A15","CH_QGOH_PQ_5:A16"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"From","passages":[{"id":"RAW_QGOH_PQ_5:P0065","trace_type":"Composite","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"},{"id":"RAW_QGOH_PQ_5:P0089","trace_type":"Composite","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_QGOH_PQ_5:D10","text":"Inevitable external events may be borne with equanimity where the intellect knows that it has done its duty and that finite power cannot extend beyond its limits.","refs":["CH_QGOH_PQ_5:A7","CH_QGOH_PQ_5:A17"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"From","passages":[{"id":"RAW_QGOH_PQ_5:P0104","trace_type":"Direct","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"},{"id":"RAW_QGOH_PQ_5:P0105","trace_type":"Direct","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]}],"original_name":"Derivative Propositions"},{"number":"5","name":"Super-Derivatives","items":[{"id":"CH_QGOH_PQ_5:SD1","text":"Freedom is not an interior property of the solitary individual, but a degree of acting constituted simultaneously by knowledge, the disposition of affects, bodily power, society, and civil right.","refs":["CH_QGOH_PQ_5:A1","CH_QGOH_PQ_5:A4","CH_QGOH_PQ_5:A9","CH_QGOH_PQ_5:A11","CH_QGOH_PQ_5:A13"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"From","passages":[{"id":"RAW_QGOH_PQ_5:P0001","trace_type":"Structural","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"},{"id":"RAW_QGOH_PQ_5:P0043","trace_type":"Structural","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"},{"id":"RAW_QGOH_PQ_5:P0057","trace_type":"Structural","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"},{"id":"RAW_QGOH_PQ_5:P0093","trace_type":"Structural","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"},{"id":"RAW_QGOH_PQ_5:P0032","trace_type":"Structural","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_QGOH_PQ_5:SD2","text":"Virtue is reclassified as power rationally ordered toward preserving, understanding, and acting harmoniously with others, not as obedience to an external moral law.","refs":["CH_QGOH_PQ_5:A4","CH_QGOH_PQ_5:A5","CH_QGOH_PQ_5:A6","CH_QGOH_PQ_5:A9"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"From","passages":[{"id":"RAW_QGOH_PQ_5:P0043","trace_type":"Structural","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"},{"id":"RAW_QGOH_PQ_5:P0045","trace_type":"Structural","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"},{"id":"RAW_QGOH_PQ_5:P0047","trace_type":"Structural","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"},{"id":"RAW_QGOH_PQ_5:P0057","trace_type":"Structural","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_QGOH_PQ_5:SD3","text":"Rational political order requires trust, love, justice, and public care for the needy, because private beneficence and fear produce neither material sufficiency nor faithful concord.","refs":["CH_QGOH_PQ_5:A9","CH_QGOH_PQ_5:A10","CH_QGOH_PQ_5:A15","CH_QGOH_PQ_5:A16"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"From","passages":[{"id":"RAW_QGOH_PQ_5:P0026","trace_type":"Structural","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"},{"id":"RAW_QGOH_PQ_5:P0069","trace_type":"Structural","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"},{"id":"RAW_QGOH_PQ_5:P0071","trace_type":"Structural","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"},{"id":"RAW_QGOH_PQ_5:P0073","trace_type":"Structural","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_QGOH_PQ_5:SD4","text":"Body, knowledge, and economy constitute one infrastructure of freedom: bodily diversity demands external things, external things reciprocal service, reciprocal service means of exchange.","refs":["CH_QGOH_PQ_5:A13","CH_QGOH_PQ_5:A15"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"From","passages":[{"id":"RAW_QGOH_PQ_5:P0093","trace_type":"Structural","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"},{"id":"RAW_QGOH_PQ_5:P0094","trace_type":"Structural","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"},{"id":"RAW_QGOH_PQ_5:P0096","trace_type":"Structural","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_QGOH_PQ_5:SD5","text":"Natural right describes the use of things in terms of power and judged utility; Reason, however, orders this use toward bodily preservation, knowledge, and stable society.","refs":["CH_QGOH_PQ_5:A5","CH_QGOH_PQ_5:A9","CH_QGOH_PQ_5:A12","CH_QGOH_PQ_5:A13"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"From","passages":[{"id":"RAW_QGOH_PQ_5:P0055","trace_type":"Structural","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"},{"id":"RAW_QGOH_PQ_5:P0057","trace_type":"Structural","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"},{"id":"RAW_QGOH_PQ_5:P0091","trace_type":"Structural","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"},{"id":"RAW_QGOH_PQ_5:P0093","trace_type":"Structural","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_QGOH_PQ_5:SD6","text":"The necessity of Nature does not abolish freedom, but transfers freedom from the dominion of externals to understood participation in necessary order.","refs":["CH_QGOH_PQ_5:A2","CH_QGOH_PQ_5:A7","CH_QGOH_PQ_5:A17"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"From","passages":[{"id":"RAW_QGOH_PQ_5:P0003","trace_type":"Structural","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"},{"id":"RAW_QGOH_PQ_5:P0051","trace_type":"Structural","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"},{"id":"RAW_QGOH_PQ_5:P0104","trace_type":"Structural","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"},{"id":"RAW_QGOH_PQ_5:P0105","trace_type":"Structural","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]}],"original_name":"Super-Derivatives"},{"number":"6","name":"Deep Apophatics","items":[{"id":"CH_QGOH_PQ_5:DNX1","text":"It must be false that a human being is a wholly undetermined cause of his action, if every Desire necessarily follows from his own nature and the order of Nature.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_QGOH_PQ_5:P0041","trace_type":"Negative","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"},{"id":"RAW_QGOH_PQ_5:P0051","trace_type":"Negative","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"},{"id":"RAW_QGOH_PQ_5:P0104","trace_type":"Negative","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_QGOH_PQ_5:DNX2","text":"It must be false that good and evil are absolute genera of things, if their account depends on power, knowledge, and rational life.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_QGOH_PQ_5:P0045","trace_type":"Negative","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"},{"id":"RAW_QGOH_PQ_5:P0049","trace_type":"Negative","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_QGOH_PQ_5:DNX3","text":"It must be false that an individual can be self-sufficient, if the greatest human utility derives from human beings led by Reason, reciprocal services, and common right.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_QGOH_PQ_5:P0057","trace_type":"Negative","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"},{"id":"RAW_QGOH_PQ_5:P0063","trace_type":"Negative","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"},{"id":"RAW_QGOH_PQ_5:P0096","trace_type":"Negative","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_QGOH_PQ_5:DNX4","text":"It must be false that the rational Mind can be separated from the conditions of the body and external things, if the body's aptitude increases the Mind's aptitude for thinking.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_QGOH_PQ_5:P0093","trace_type":"Negative","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"},{"id":"RAW_QGOH_PQ_5:P0094","trace_type":"Negative","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_QGOH_PQ_5:DNX5","text":"It must be false that fear, punishment, flattery, or deceit can be the stable foundation of society, if rational concord requires trust and generosity.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_QGOH_PQ_5:P0028","trace_type":"Negative","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"},{"id":"RAW_QGOH_PQ_5:P0069","trace_type":"Negative","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"},{"id":"RAW_QGOH_PQ_5:P0071","trace_type":"Negative","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"},{"id":"RAW_QGOH_PQ_5:P0081","trace_type":"Negative","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_QGOH_PQ_5:DNX6","text":"It must be false that Reason can operate without the ordering of affects, if affects can be restrained only through contrary and stronger affects.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_QGOH_PQ_5:P0008","trace_type":"Negative","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"},{"id":"RAW_QGOH_PQ_5:P0100","trace_type":"Negative","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_QGOH_PQ_5:DNX7","text":"It must be false that a faithful society can be constituted from universal perfidy, if general perfidy renders common rights and cooperation empty.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_QGOH_PQ_5:P0028","trace_type":"Negative","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"},{"id":"RAW_QGOH_PQ_5:P0030","trace_type":"Negative","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]}],"original_name":"Deep Apophatics"},{"number":"7","name":"Super-Apophatics","items":[{"id":"CH_QGOH_PQ_5:SNX1","text":"The system excludes every human standpoint outside Nature: a human being is always a part of the whole, whose order he is compelled to follow.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_QGOH_PQ_5:P0051","trace_type":"Negative","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"},{"id":"RAW_QGOH_PQ_5:P0053","trace_type":"Negative","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"},{"id":"RAW_QGOH_PQ_5:P0104","trace_type":"Negative","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_QGOH_PQ_5:SNX2","text":"The system excludes the possibility of absolute and perpetual human freedom, while retaining the figure of the perfectly free human being as a normative limit.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_QGOH_PQ_5:P0001","trace_type":"Negative","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"},{"id":"RAW_QGOH_PQ_5:P0003","trace_type":"Negative","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_QGOH_PQ_5:SNX3","text":"The system excludes a merely private highest good, because the good rationally sought must at once be shared with others and sustained through society.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_QGOH_PQ_5:P0004","trace_type":"Negative","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"},{"id":"RAW_QGOH_PQ_5:P0022","trace_type":"Negative","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"},{"id":"RAW_QGOH_PQ_5:P0057","trace_type":"Negative","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_QGOH_PQ_5:SNX4","text":"The system excludes community without affective, juridical, and material mediations, because people do not for the most part live from Reason alone.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_QGOH_PQ_5:P0059","trace_type":"Negative","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"},{"id":"RAW_QGOH_PQ_5:P0065","trace_type":"Negative","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"},{"id":"RAW_QGOH_PQ_5:P0071","trace_type":"Negative","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"},{"id":"RAW_QGOH_PQ_5:P0096","trace_type":"Negative","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]}],"original_name":"Super-Apophatics"},{"number":"8","name":"Formal Generative Schema","items":[{"id":"CH_QGOH_PQ_5:FGS1","text":"Necessity of Nature → conatus and Desires → distinction of actions from adequate cognition and passions from inadequate cognition.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_QGOH_PQ_5:P0041","trace_type":"Direct","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"},{"id":"RAW_QGOH_PQ_5:P0043","trace_type":"Direct","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_QGOH_PQ_5:FGS2","text":"Rational actions → good as rational life → perfection of the intellect → blessedness as cognitive acquiescence.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_QGOH_PQ_5:P0045","trace_type":"Composite","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"},{"id":"RAW_QGOH_PQ_5:P0047","trace_type":"Composite","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"},{"id":"RAW_QGOH_PQ_5:P0049","trace_type":"Composite","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_QGOH_PQ_5:FGS3","text":"Superior external power + bondage to affects → diminution of freedom → necessity of contrary affects, fortitude, and vigilance.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_QGOH_PQ_5:P0008","trace_type":"Composite","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"},{"id":"RAW_QGOH_PQ_5:P0051","trace_type":"Composite","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"},{"id":"RAW_QGOH_PQ_5:P0100","trace_type":"Composite","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_QGOH_PQ_5:FGS4","text":"Rational self-preservation → common good → friendship, gratitude, trust → exclusion of malicious deceit.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_QGOH_PQ_5:P0020","trace_type":"Structural","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"},{"id":"RAW_QGOH_PQ_5:P0022","trace_type":"Structural","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"},{"id":"RAW_QGOH_PQ_5:P0026","trace_type":"Structural","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"},{"id":"RAW_QGOH_PQ_5:P0028","trace_type":"Structural","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_QGOH_PQ_5:FGS5","text":"Common utility + affective plurality of human beings → civil right, justice, love, and generosity → greater freedom in the State.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_QGOH_PQ_5:P0032","trace_type":"Structural","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"},{"id":"RAW_QGOH_PQ_5:P0069","trace_type":"Structural","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"},{"id":"RAW_QGOH_PQ_5:P0071","trace_type":"Structural","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_QGOH_PQ_5:FGS6","text":"Complex body + necessity of external things → use of Nature, reciprocal service, and money → moderation of Desire and Joy.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_QGOH_PQ_5:P0093","trace_type":"Structural","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"},{"id":"RAW_QGOH_PQ_5:P0096","trace_type":"Structural","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"},{"id":"RAW_QGOH_PQ_5:P0098","trace_type":"Structural","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"},{"id":"RAW_QGOH_PQ_5:P0100","trace_type":"Structural","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_QGOH_PQ_5:FGS7","text":"Inevitable events beyond human power → knowledge of necessity → acquiescence of the Mind in the order of the whole of Nature.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_QGOH_PQ_5:P0104","trace_type":"Direct","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"},{"id":"RAW_QGOH_PQ_5:P0105","trace_type":"Direct","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]}],"original_name":"Formal Generative Schema"},{"number":"9","name":"Argument Flow Map","items":[{"id":"CH_QGOH_PQ_5:AF1","text":"Perfect hypothetical freedom → fortitude in dangers → prudence concerning the benefits of ignorant persons → gratitude and trust among free persons → civil freedom greater than solitude.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_QGOH_PQ_5:P0001","trace_type":"Structural","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"},{"id":"RAW_QGOH_PQ_5:P0006","trace_type":"Structural","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"},{"id":"RAW_QGOH_PQ_5:P0014","trace_type":"Structural","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"},{"id":"RAW_QGOH_PQ_5:P0020","trace_type":"Structural","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"},{"id":"RAW_QGOH_PQ_5:P0026","trace_type":"Structural","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"},{"id":"RAW_QGOH_PQ_5:P0032","trace_type":"Structural","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_QGOH_PQ_5:AF2","text":"Necessary conatus → action/passion → good as rational life → preservation of body and Mind → society, right, and economy → moderation of affects → acquiescence in the necessity of Nature.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_QGOH_PQ_5:P0041","trace_type":"Structural","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"},{"id":"RAW_QGOH_PQ_5:P0043","trace_type":"Structural","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"},{"id":"RAW_QGOH_PQ_5:P0049","trace_type":"Structural","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"},{"id":"RAW_QGOH_PQ_5:P0093","trace_type":"Structural","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"},{"id":"RAW_QGOH_PQ_5:P0104","trace_type":"Structural","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]}],"original_name":"Argument Flow Map"},{"number":"10","name":"Map-Effects","items":[{"id":"CH_QGOH_PQ_5:ME1","text":"Freedom is transferred from the figure of private choice to a mode of acting constituted by Reason, common laws, and useful relations.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_QGOH_PQ_5:P0032","trace_type":"Structural","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"},{"id":"RAW_QGOH_PQ_5:P0034","trace_type":"Structural","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"},{"id":"RAW_QGOH_PQ_5:P0057","trace_type":"Structural","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_QGOH_PQ_5:ME2","text":"Timely flight is reclassified from the genus of timidity into that of fortitude, if it is chosen with the same presence of mind as combat.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_QGOH_PQ_5:P0006","trace_type":"Direct","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"},{"id":"RAW_QGOH_PQ_5:P0010","trace_type":"Direct","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_QGOH_PQ_5:ME3","text":"Benefit, gratitude, and ingratitude are transferred from a simple moral code to an affective, rational, and social structure requiring distinction.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_QGOH_PQ_5:P0016","trace_type":"Structural","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"},{"id":"RAW_QGOH_PQ_5:P0018","trace_type":"Structural","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"},{"id":"RAW_QGOH_PQ_5:P0022","trace_type":"Structural","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"},{"id":"RAW_QGOH_PQ_5:P0024","trace_type":"Structural","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_QGOH_PQ_5:ME4","text":"The State is not opposed to freedom; a rationally ordered State becomes the means through which individual freedom is actually made greater.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_QGOH_PQ_5:P0032","trace_type":"Direct","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"},{"id":"RAW_QGOH_PQ_5:P0034","trace_type":"Direct","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_QGOH_PQ_5:ME5","text":"Social affects are divided into those that increase faithful concord and those that merely feign the appearance of concord through fear, solicitation, bondage, or sadness.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_QGOH_PQ_5:P0069","trace_type":"Composite","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"},{"id":"RAW_QGOH_PQ_5:P0071","trace_type":"Composite","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"},{"id":"RAW_QGOH_PQ_5:P0077","trace_type":"Composite","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"},{"id":"RAW_QGOH_PQ_5:P0081","trace_type":"Composite","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"},{"id":"RAW_QGOH_PQ_5:P0089","trace_type":"Composite","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_QGOH_PQ_5:ME6","text":"The body, external things, nourishment, labor, and money are not extrinsic adjuncts, but constitute the cognitive and social conditions of rational life.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_QGOH_PQ_5:P0093","trace_type":"Structural","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"},{"id":"RAW_QGOH_PQ_5:P0096","trace_type":"Structural","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"},{"id":"RAW_QGOH_PQ_5:P0098","trace_type":"Structural","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_QGOH_PQ_5:ME7","text":"The necessity of Nature no longer appears as a fate extrinsic to freedom, but as an order to which the finite intellect, by assenting, exercises relative freedom.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_QGOH_PQ_5:P0104","trace_type":"Direct","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"},{"id":"RAW_QGOH_PQ_5:P0105","trace_type":"Direct","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]}],"original_name":"Map-Effects"},{"number":"11","name":"Altitude Levels","items":[{"id":"CH_QGOH_PQ_5:ALT1","text":"Surface — The text sets out rules concerning dangers, benefits, gratitude, trust, the State, marriage, modesty, money, and the endurance of events.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_QGOH_PQ_5:P0006","trace_type":"Structural","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"},{"id":"RAW_QGOH_PQ_5:P0014","trace_type":"Structural","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"},{"id":"RAW_QGOH_PQ_5:P0020","trace_type":"Structural","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"},{"id":"RAW_QGOH_PQ_5:P0026","trace_type":"Structural","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"},{"id":"RAW_QGOH_PQ_5:P0032","trace_type":"Structural","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"},{"id":"RAW_QGOH_PQ_5:P0079","trace_type":"Structural","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"},{"id":"RAW_QGOH_PQ_5:P0096","trace_type":"Structural","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"},{"id":"RAW_QGOH_PQ_5:P0104","trace_type":"Structural","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_QGOH_PQ_5:ALT2","text":"Structural — These rules proceed from the distinction between action and passion, from the superiority of external causes, and from bodily and social interdependence.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_QGOH_PQ_5:P0041","trace_type":"Structural","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"},{"id":"RAW_QGOH_PQ_5:P0043","trace_type":"Structural","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"},{"id":"RAW_QGOH_PQ_5:P0051","trace_type":"Structural","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"},{"id":"RAW_QGOH_PQ_5:P0093","trace_type":"Structural","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"},{"id":"RAW_QGOH_PQ_5:P0096","trace_type":"Structural","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_QGOH_PQ_5:ALT3","text":"Metastrategic — The text transforms moral struggle into an art of ordering affects, establishing trust, reconciling love, and constructing the conditions of cooperation.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_QGOH_PQ_5:P0063","trace_type":"Structural","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"},{"id":"RAW_QGOH_PQ_5:P0069","trace_type":"Structural","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"},{"id":"RAW_QGOH_PQ_5:P0071","trace_type":"Structural","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"},{"id":"RAW_QGOH_PQ_5:P0089","trace_type":"Structural","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_QGOH_PQ_5:ALT4","text":"Ontological/Temporal — A human being is a part of necessarily ordered Nature; affect values present sweetness above the future, whereas Reason, understanding necessity, seeks stable acquiescence of the Mind.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_QGOH_PQ_5:P0041","trace_type":"Structural","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"},{"id":"RAW_QGOH_PQ_5:P0100","trace_type":"Structural","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"},{"id":"RAW_QGOH_PQ_5:P0104","trace_type":"Structural","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"},{"id":"RAW_QGOH_PQ_5:P0105","trace_type":"Structural","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]}],"original_name":"Altitude Levels"},{"number":"12","name":"Minimal Reconstruction Test","items":[{"id":"CH_QGOH_PQ_5:MRT1","text":"The axioms are sufficient to reconstruct the principal sequence: necessity of Nature, adequacy, action and passion, rational utility, civil society, and acquiescence.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_QGOH_PQ_5:P0041","trace_type":"Structural","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"},{"id":"RAW_QGOH_PQ_5:P0043","trace_type":"Structural","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"},{"id":"RAW_QGOH_PQ_5:P0045","trace_type":"Structural","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"},{"id":"RAW_QGOH_PQ_5:P0057","trace_type":"Structural","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"},{"id":"RAW_QGOH_PQ_5:P0032","trace_type":"Structural","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"},{"id":"RAW_QGOH_PQ_5:P0104","trace_type":"Structural","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_QGOH_PQ_5:MRT2","text":"The system's strongest connection is the transition from affective impotence to the necessity of rational cooperation: a finite human being can be preserved stably neither alone nor by deceit.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_QGOH_PQ_5:P0057","trace_type":"Structural","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"},{"id":"RAW_QGOH_PQ_5:P0063","trace_type":"Structural","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"},{"id":"RAW_QGOH_PQ_5:P0096","trace_type":"Structural","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"},{"id":"RAW_QGOH_PQ_5:P0028","trace_type":"Structural","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_QGOH_PQ_5:MRT3","text":"The figure of the perfectly free human being remains at once the norm of the argument and a false hypothesis; the transition from this hypothetical limit to the actually gradually free human being is not fully formalized.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_QGOH_PQ_5:P0001","trace_type":"Tension","confidence":"medium","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"},{"id":"RAW_QGOH_PQ_5:P0003","trace_type":"Tension","confidence":"medium","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"},{"id":"RAW_QGOH_PQ_5:P0104","trace_type":"Tension","confidence":"medium","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_QGOH_PQ_5:MRT4","text":"The system makes the State a means of freedom, but does not fully resolve here the distinction between a State living by common decree and a State in fact governed by fear or Desire.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_QGOH_PQ_5:P0032","trace_type":"Tension","confidence":"medium","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"},{"id":"RAW_QGOH_PQ_5:P0034","trace_type":"Tension","confidence":"medium","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"},{"id":"RAW_QGOH_PQ_5:P0071","trace_type":"Tension","confidence":"medium","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_QGOH_PQ_5:MRT5","text":"The right to use external things is broadly defined, while its moderation through rational utility is affirmed; the criterion by which individual and common utility are to be reconciled in a case of conflict is not explained.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[]},{"id":"CH_QGOH_PQ_5:MRT6","text":"The text posits that affects must be restrained by affects, but the concrete force by which Reason produces an effective contrary affect is here presupposed rather than demonstrated.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_QGOH_PQ_5:P0008","trace_type":"Tension","confidence":"medium","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"},{"id":"RAW_QGOH_PQ_5:P0100","trace_type":"Tension","confidence":"medium","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"},{"id":"RAW_QGOH_PQ_5:P0102","trace_type":"Tension","confidence":"medium","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_QGOH_PQ_5:MRT7","text":"Good and evil vanish in the figure of the perfectly free human being, but remain necessary in the account of finite life; the distinction of perspectives is implied but not explicitly systematized.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_QGOH_PQ_5:P0001","trace_type":"Tension","confidence":"medium","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"},{"id":"RAW_QGOH_PQ_5:P0049","trace_type":"Tension","confidence":"medium","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"},{"id":"RAW_QGOH_PQ_5:P0102","trace_type":"Tension","confidence":"medium","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_QGOH_PQ_5:MRT8","text":"The reconstruction preserves the true movement, because it proceeds from the ideal of freedom to fortitude, society, material conditions, and finally to the understood necessity of Nature.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_QGOH_PQ_5:P0001","trace_type":"Structural","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"},{"id":"RAW_QGOH_PQ_5:P0006","trace_type":"Structural","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"},{"id":"RAW_QGOH_PQ_5:P0032","trace_type":"Structural","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"},{"id":"RAW_QGOH_PQ_5:P0093","trace_type":"Structural","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"},{"id":"RAW_QGOH_PQ_5:P0104","trace_type":"Structural","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]}],"original_name":"Minimal Reconstruction Test"}],"revision":"CH_QGOH_PQ_5@R001","source_variant_id":"canonical:CH_QGOH_PQ_5:v1","source_language_code":"la"}],"identity":{"layer":"CH","kicker":"Chapter 18","title":"Part Four - On Human Bondage, or the Powers of the Affects · passage 5","context":"Chapter 18","original_title":"Pars quarta - De servitute humana seu de affectuum viribus · passage 5","original_context":"Chapter 18"},"original_title":"Pars quarta - De servitute humana seu de affectuum viribus · passage 5","title_language_code":"en"},{"id":"CH_QGOH_PQDP","layer":"CH","title":"Part Five - On the Power of the Intellect, or Human Freedom · passage 1","order":19,"parents":["RAW_QGOH_PQDP"],"revision":"CH_QGOH_PQDP@R001","sha256":"bab3d19793ef850184df27b9dc1631c9e7edbeba2df701ce0f6cf8dcda453fda","sections":[{"number":"1","name":"Argumental Spine","items":[{"id":"CH_QGOH_PQDP:AS1","text":"Libertas humana quaeritur non ut imperium voluntatis absolutum in affectus, sed ut mensura qua Mens, per Rationem, minus ab affectibus patitur et eos moderatur.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_QGOH_PQDP:P0006","trace_type":"Composite","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"},{"id":"RAW_QGOH_PQDP:P0013","trace_type":"Composite","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"},{"id":"RAW_QGOH_PQDP:P0031","trace_type":"Composite","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_QGOH_PQDP:AS2","text":"Argumentatio polemica opinionem Stoicam et Cartesianam dissolvit, quia voluntas neque causam claram neque potentiam mensurabilem habet qua motus Corporis et passiones absolute regat.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_QGOH_PQDP:P0006","trace_type":"Structural","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"},{"id":"RAW_QGOH_PQDP:P0007","trace_type":"Structural","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"},{"id":"RAW_QGOH_PQDP:P0011","trace_type":"Structural","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"},{"id":"RAW_QGOH_PQDP:P0012","trace_type":"Structural","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"},{"id":"RAW_QGOH_PQDP:P0013","trace_type":"Structural","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_QGOH_PQDP:AS3","text":"Potentia Mentis in sola intelligentia reponitur; ideo affectus non voluntate reprimendi, sed ordine idearum reformandi sunt.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_QGOH_PQDP:P0013","trace_type":"Composite","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"},{"id":"RAW_QGOH_PQDP:P0039","trace_type":"Composite","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"},{"id":"RAW_QGOH_PQDP:P0041","trace_type":"Composite","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_QGOH_PQDP:AS4","text":"Cognitio clara, intellectus necessitatis, et affectuum ad causas plurimas ordinatio passivitatem minuunt atque affectus rationales stabiliores faciunt.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_QGOH_PQDP:P0027","trace_type":"Structural","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"},{"id":"RAW_QGOH_PQDP:P0031","trace_type":"Structural","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"},{"id":"RAW_QGOH_PQDP:P0047","trace_type":"Structural","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"},{"id":"RAW_QGOH_PQDP:P0049","trace_type":"Structural","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"},{"id":"RAW_QGOH_PQDP:P0053","trace_type":"Structural","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"},{"id":"RAW_QGOH_PQDP:P0055","trace_type":"Structural","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"},{"id":"RAW_QGOH_PQDP:P0056","trace_type":"Structural","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_QGOH_PQDP:AS5","text":"Ordinatio imaginum et memoria praeceptorum vitae efficiunt ut Mens affectus futuros anticipet, eosque ad actiones rationales et laetitiam dirigat.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_QGOH_PQDP:P0068","trace_type":"Structural","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"},{"id":"RAW_QGOH_PQDP:P0072","trace_type":"Structural","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"},{"id":"RAW_QGOH_PQDP:P0073","trace_type":"Structural","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"},{"id":"RAW_QGOH_PQDP:P0074","trace_type":"Structural","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"},{"id":"RAW_QGOH_PQDP:P0078","trace_type":"Structural","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"},{"id":"RAW_QGOH_PQDP:P0082","trace_type":"Structural","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"},{"id":"RAW_QGOH_PQDP:P0086","trace_type":"Structural","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_QGOH_PQDP:AS6","text":"Omnes Corporis affectiones ad ideam Dei referri possunt; ex intellectu sui affectuum nascitur Amor Dei, dum Deus ipse omni passione et amore reciproco caret.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_QGOH_PQDP:P0090","trace_type":"Structural","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"},{"id":"RAW_QGOH_PQDP:P0092","trace_type":"Structural","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"},{"id":"RAW_QGOH_PQDP:P0094","trace_type":"Structural","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"},{"id":"RAW_QGOH_PQDP:P0096","trace_type":"Structural","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"},{"id":"RAW_QGOH_PQDP:P0102","trace_type":"Structural","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"},{"id":"RAW_QGOH_PQDP:P0104","trace_type":"Structural","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"},{"id":"RAW_QGOH_PQDP:P0106","trace_type":"Structural","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_QGOH_PQDP:AS7","text":"Motus ad enuntiationem pervenit qua nemo Deum odio habere potest, sed demonstratio huius termini in fonte dato nondum exhibetur.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_QGOH_PQDP:P0108","trace_type":"Direct","confidence":"medium","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]}],"original_name":"Argumental Spine"},{"number":"2","name":"Load-Bearing Axioms","items":[{"id":"CH_QGOH_PQDP:A1","text":"Mentis potentia sola intelligentia definitur, id est cogitatione et formatione idearum adaequatarum.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_QGOH_PQDP:P0013","trace_type":"Direct","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"},{"id":"RAW_QGOH_PQDP:P0041","trace_type":"Direct","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_QGOH_PQDP:A2","text":"Ordo et concatenatio idearum in Mente ordine et concatenationi affectionum Corporis respondent.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_QGOH_PQDP:P0019","trace_type":"Direct","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"},{"id":"RAW_QGOH_PQDP:P0021","trace_type":"Direct","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_QGOH_PQDP:A3","text":"Affectus, quatenus passio est, idea confusa est; clara et distincta idea eius facit ut, quatenus ad Mentem refertur, passio esse desinat.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_QGOH_PQDP:P0027","trace_type":"Direct","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"},{"id":"RAW_QGOH_PQDP:P0029","trace_type":"Direct","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_QGOH_PQDP:A4","text":"Omnis Corporis affectio aliquem conceptum clarum et distinctum involvit; ideo omnis affectus saltem ex parte clare intelligi potest.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_QGOH_PQDP:P0033","trace_type":"Direct","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"},{"id":"RAW_QGOH_PQDP:P0035","trace_type":"Direct","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"},{"id":"RAW_QGOH_PQDP:P0037","trace_type":"Direct","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_QGOH_PQDP:A5","text":"Amor et Odium constant Laetitia vel Tristitia cum idea causae externae; remoto illo nexu causali, forma eorum destruitur.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_QGOH_PQDP:P0023","trace_type":"Direct","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"},{"id":"RAW_QGOH_PQDP:P0025","trace_type":"Direct","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_QGOH_PQDP:A6","text":"Res simpliciter vel libera imaginata affectum maximum excitat, quia causae quibus determinatur ignorantur.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_QGOH_PQDP:P0043","trace_type":"Direct","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"},{"id":"RAW_QGOH_PQDP:P0045","trace_type":"Direct","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_QGOH_PQDP:A7","text":"Mens, quatenus res ut necessarias et infinito causarum nexu determinatas intelligit, minus ab affectibus patitur.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_QGOH_PQDP:P0047","trace_type":"Direct","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"},{"id":"RAW_QGOH_PQDP:P0049","trace_type":"Direct","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_QGOH_PQDP:A8","text":"Affectus ex Ratione ortus ad proprietates communes semper praesentes refertur et ideo affectibus temporalibus contrariis stabilior est.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_QGOH_PQDP:P0053","trace_type":"Direct","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"},{"id":"RAW_QGOH_PQDP:P0055","trace_type":"Direct","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"},{"id":"RAW_QGOH_PQDP:P0056","trace_type":"Direct","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_QGOH_PQDP:A9","text":"Affectus a pluribus causis simul concurrentibus excitatus fortior est.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_QGOH_PQDP:P0058","trace_type":"Direct","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"},{"id":"RAW_QGOH_PQDP:P0060","trace_type":"Direct","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_QGOH_PQDP:A10","text":"Affectus ad plures causas vel obiecta relatus Mentem minus impedit, minus passivam facit, et erga singulas causas minus intense afficit.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_QGOH_PQDP:P0064","trace_type":"Direct","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"},{"id":"RAW_QGOH_PQDP:P0066","trace_type":"Direct","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_QGOH_PQDP:A11","text":"Imago pluribus imaginibus coniuncta frequentius excitatur; imagines ad res clare intellectas pertinentes facilius in tales nexus ingrediuntur.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_QGOH_PQDP:P0078","trace_type":"Composite","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"},{"id":"RAW_QGOH_PQDP:P0080","trace_type":"Composite","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"},{"id":"RAW_QGOH_PQDP:P0082","trace_type":"Composite","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"},{"id":"RAW_QGOH_PQDP:P0084","trace_type":"Composite","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"},{"id":"RAW_QGOH_PQDP:P0086","trace_type":"Composite","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"},{"id":"RAW_QGOH_PQDP:P0088","trace_type":"Composite","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_QGOH_PQDP:A12","text":"Dum affectus naturae contrarii Mentem non impediunt, Mens Corporis affectiones secundum ordinem ad intellectum ordinare et concatenare potest.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_QGOH_PQDP:P0068","trace_type":"Direct","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"},{"id":"RAW_QGOH_PQDP:P0070","trace_type":"Direct","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_QGOH_PQDP:A13","text":"Praecepta vitae memoriae mandata et imaginibus rerum particularium adiuncta parant affectus rationales ad occursus futuros.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_QGOH_PQDP:P0072","trace_type":"Direct","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"},{"id":"RAW_QGOH_PQDP:P0073","trace_type":"Direct","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"},{"id":"RAW_QGOH_PQDP:P0074","trace_type":"Direct","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_QGOH_PQDP:A14","text":"Mens omnes Corporis affectiones ad ideam Dei referre potest.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_QGOH_PQDP:P0090","trace_type":"Direct","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"},{"id":"RAW_QGOH_PQDP:P0092","trace_type":"Direct","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_QGOH_PQDP:A15","text":"Clara cognitio sui et affectuum Laetitiam cum idea Dei producit, atque hinc Amorem Dei; quia hic Amor omnibus affectionibus iungitur, Mentem maxime occupat.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_QGOH_PQDP:P0094","trace_type":"Composite","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"},{"id":"RAW_QGOH_PQDP:P0096","trace_type":"Composite","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"},{"id":"RAW_QGOH_PQDP:P0098","trace_type":"Composite","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"},{"id":"RAW_QGOH_PQDP:P0100","trace_type":"Composite","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_QGOH_PQDP:A16","text":"Deus expers est passionum, nec proprie loquendo amat aut odio habet.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_QGOH_PQDP:P0102","trace_type":"Direct","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"},{"id":"RAW_QGOH_PQDP:P0104","trace_type":"Direct","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"},{"id":"RAW_QGOH_PQDP:P0106","trace_type":"Direct","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_QGOH_PQDP:A17","text":"Eadem Cupiditas passio est si ex ideis inadaequatis oritur, virtus autem si ex ideis adaequatis generatur.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_QGOH_PQDP:P0040","trace_type":"Direct","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"},{"id":"RAW_QGOH_PQDP:P0041","trace_type":"Direct","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]}],"original_name":"Load-Bearing Axioms"},{"number":"3","name":"Apophatic Structure","items":[{"id":"CH_QGOH_PQDP:NX1","text":"Libertas non est imperium absolutum voluntatis in affectus.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_QGOH_PQDP:P0006","trace_type":"Direct","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_QGOH_PQDP:NX2","text":"Mens non explicatur ut res a Corpore separata quae glandulam pinealem voluntate moveat et sic passiones mechanice regat.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_QGOH_PQDP:P0007","trace_type":"Negative","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"},{"id":"RAW_QGOH_PQDP:P0011","trace_type":"Negative","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"},{"id":"RAW_QGOH_PQDP:P0012","trace_type":"Negative","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"},{"id":"RAW_QGOH_PQDP:P0013","trace_type":"Negative","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_QGOH_PQDP:NX3","text":"Remedium affectuum non est ars medica Corporis neque ars perficiendi Intellectum, sed cognitio qua Mens affectus intelligit et ordinat.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_QGOH_PQDP:P0006","trace_type":"Composite","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"},{"id":"RAW_QGOH_PQDP:P0013","trace_type":"Composite","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"},{"id":"RAW_QGOH_PQDP:P0041","trace_type":"Composite","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_QGOH_PQDP:NX4","text":"Affectus non est vis caeca cognitioni penitus externa, quia omnis affectus conceptum clarum et distinctum involvere dicitur.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_QGOH_PQDP:P0033","trace_type":"Direct","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"},{"id":"RAW_QGOH_PQDP:P0037","trace_type":"Direct","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_QGOH_PQDP:NX5","text":"Affectuum imminutio non procedit ex rerum contingentia vel libertate imaginata, sed ex intellectu necessitatis earum.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_QGOH_PQDP:P0045","trace_type":"Negative","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"},{"id":"RAW_QGOH_PQDP:P0047","trace_type":"Negative","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"},{"id":"RAW_QGOH_PQDP:P0049","trace_type":"Negative","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_QGOH_PQDP:NX6","text":"Moderatio affectuum non consistit in contemplatione vitiorum, obtrectatione hominum, aut falsa specie libertatis.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_QGOH_PQDP:P0074","trace_type":"Negative","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"},{"id":"RAW_QGOH_PQDP:P0075","trace_type":"Negative","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"},{"id":"RAW_QGOH_PQDP:P0076","trace_type":"Negative","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_QGOH_PQDP:NX7","text":"Amor Dei non est relatio reciproca inter Deum passibilem et hominem, quia Deus neque Laetitia neque Tristitia, amore neque odio afficitur.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_QGOH_PQDP:P0102","trace_type":"Negative","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"},{"id":"RAW_QGOH_PQDP:P0106","trace_type":"Negative","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]}],"original_name":"Apophatic Structure"},{"number":"4","name":"Derivative Propositions","items":[{"id":"CH_QGOH_PQDP:D1","text":"Cognitio affectus passivitatem minuit, quia affectus quatenus clare intelligitur desinit esse passio, et nullus affectus omnino cognitioni clarae subtrahitur.","refs":["CH_QGOH_PQDP:A3","CH_QGOH_PQDP:A4"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"From","passages":[{"id":"RAW_QGOH_PQDP:P0027","trace_type":"Composite","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"},{"id":"RAW_QGOH_PQDP:P0029","trace_type":"Composite","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"},{"id":"RAW_QGOH_PQDP:P0031","trace_type":"Composite","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"},{"id":"RAW_QGOH_PQDP:P0033","trace_type":"Composite","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"},{"id":"RAW_QGOH_PQDP:P0037","trace_type":"Composite","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_QGOH_PQDP:D2","text":"Amor, Odium, et fluctuationes ex eis ortae dissolvi possunt, si affectus ab idea causae externae segregatur et veris cogitationibus coniungitur.","refs":["CH_QGOH_PQDP:A3","CH_QGOH_PQDP:A4","CH_QGOH_PQDP:A5"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"From","passages":[{"id":"RAW_QGOH_PQDP:P0023","trace_type":"Composite","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"},{"id":"RAW_QGOH_PQDP:P0025","trace_type":"Composite","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"},{"id":"RAW_QGOH_PQDP:P0039","trace_type":"Composite","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_QGOH_PQDP:D3","text":"Intellectus necessitatis transformat dolorem rerum singularium amissarum, quia impedit ne res ut vitium Naturae, iniuria, vel casus evitabilis imaginetur.","refs":["CH_QGOH_PQDP:A6","CH_QGOH_PQDP:A7"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"From","passages":[{"id":"RAW_QGOH_PQDP:P0045","trace_type":"Composite","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"},{"id":"RAW_QGOH_PQDP:P0047","trace_type":"Composite","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"},{"id":"RAW_QGOH_PQDP:P0051","trace_type":"Composite","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_QGOH_PQDP:D4","text":"Affectus rationalis, quia ad communia semper praesentia refertur, affectibus contrariis externis non continuo sustentatis paulatim dominatur.","refs":["CH_QGOH_PQDP:A8"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"From","passages":[{"id":"RAW_QGOH_PQDP:P0053","trace_type":"Direct","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"},{"id":"RAW_QGOH_PQDP:P0055","trace_type":"Direct","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"},{"id":"RAW_QGOH_PQDP:P0056","trace_type":"Direct","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_QGOH_PQDP:D5","text":"Architectura affectuum tres operationes distinguit: concursus causarum affectum auget, distributio eius inter plura obiecta noxam minuit, et multiplicatio nexuum imaginativorum eius frequentiam auget.","refs":["CH_QGOH_PQDP:A9","CH_QGOH_PQDP:A10","CH_QGOH_PQDP:A11"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"From","passages":[{"id":"RAW_QGOH_PQDP:P0058","trace_type":"Composite","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"},{"id":"RAW_QGOH_PQDP:P0064","trace_type":"Composite","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"},{"id":"RAW_QGOH_PQDP:P0078","trace_type":"Composite","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"},{"id":"RAW_QGOH_PQDP:P0086","trace_type":"Composite","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_QGOH_PQDP:D6","text":"Praemeditatio praeceptorum rationalium fit dispositio corporalis et imaginativa, quia ideae memoriter firmatae affectiones Corporis secundum intellectum concatenant.","refs":["CH_QGOH_PQDP:A2","CH_QGOH_PQDP:A11","CH_QGOH_PQDP:A12","CH_QGOH_PQDP:A13"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"From","passages":[{"id":"RAW_QGOH_PQDP:P0019","trace_type":"Structural","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"},{"id":"RAW_QGOH_PQDP:P0068","trace_type":"Structural","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"},{"id":"RAW_QGOH_PQDP:P0072","trace_type":"Structural","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"},{"id":"RAW_QGOH_PQDP:P0082","trace_type":"Structural","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"},{"id":"RAW_QGOH_PQDP:P0086","trace_type":"Structural","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_QGOH_PQDP:D7","text":"Eadem actio vel Cupiditas non ex obiecto suo, sed ex genere ideae a qua oritur, ut passio aut virtus classificanda est.","refs":["CH_QGOH_PQDP:A3","CH_QGOH_PQDP:A17"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"From","passages":[{"id":"RAW_QGOH_PQDP:P0040","trace_type":"Direct","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"},{"id":"RAW_QGOH_PQDP:P0041","trace_type":"Direct","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_QGOH_PQDP:D8","text":"Amor Dei fit affectus maxime occupans, quia omnis affectio Corporis ad Deum referri potest et sic plures causae eundem amorem fovent.","refs":["CH_QGOH_PQDP:A9","CH_QGOH_PQDP:A14","CH_QGOH_PQDP:A15"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"From","passages":[{"id":"RAW_QGOH_PQDP:P0058","trace_type":"Composite","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"},{"id":"RAW_QGOH_PQDP:P0090","trace_type":"Composite","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"},{"id":"RAW_QGOH_PQDP:P0094","trace_type":"Composite","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"},{"id":"RAW_QGOH_PQDP:P0100","trace_type":"Composite","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]}],"original_name":"Derivative Propositions"},{"number":"5","name":"Super-Derivatives","items":[{"id":"CH_QGOH_PQDP:SD1","text":"Libert as non est imminutio causalitatis, sed imminutio passivitatis per reordinationem idearum, affectionum Corporis, memoriae, et imaginum.","refs":["CH_QGOH_PQDP:A1","CH_QGOH_PQDP:A2","CH_QGOH_PQDP:A3","CH_QGOH_PQDP:A12","CH_QGOH_PQDP:A13"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"From","passages":[{"id":"RAW_QGOH_PQDP:P0013","trace_type":"Structural","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"},{"id":"RAW_QGOH_PQDP:P0019","trace_type":"Structural","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"},{"id":"RAW_QGOH_PQDP:P0027","trace_type":"Structural","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"},{"id":"RAW_QGOH_PQDP:P0068","trace_type":"Structural","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"},{"id":"RAW_QGOH_PQDP:P0072","trace_type":"Structural","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_QGOH_PQDP:SD2","text":"Necessitas Naturae non parit fatalismum, sed rationem praevidendi et mitigandi affectus, quia intellectus causarum imminutam passivitatem cum praeparatione practica coniungit.","refs":["CH_QGOH_PQDP:A7","CH_QGOH_PQDP:A8","CH_QGOH_PQDP:A12","CH_QGOH_PQDP:A13"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"From","passages":[{"id":"RAW_QGOH_PQDP:P0047","trace_type":"Structural","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"},{"id":"RAW_QGOH_PQDP:P0053","trace_type":"Structural","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"},{"id":"RAW_QGOH_PQDP:P0068","trace_type":"Structural","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"},{"id":"RAW_QGOH_PQDP:P0072","trace_type":"Structural","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_QGOH_PQDP:SD3","text":"Ethica affectuum transit a iudicio de obiectis desideratis ad iudicium de causis ideationalibus ex quibus desiderium generatur.","refs":["CH_QGOH_PQDP:A3","CH_QGOH_PQDP:A17"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"From","passages":[{"id":"RAW_QGOH_PQDP:P0029","trace_type":"Composite","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"},{"id":"RAW_QGOH_PQDP:P0040","trace_type":"Composite","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_QGOH_PQDP:SD4","text":"Praxis rationalis non tantum affectus hostiles reprimit, sed laetitiam, virtutes, utilitatem communem, et imagines bonorum ita multiplicat ut contrarii affectus minus locum obtineant.","refs":["CH_QGOH_PQDP:A10","CH_QGOH_PQDP:A11","CH_QGOH_PQDP:A13"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"From","passages":[{"id":"RAW_QGOH_PQDP:P0064","trace_type":"Structural","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"},{"id":"RAW_QGOH_PQDP:P0072","trace_type":"Structural","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"},{"id":"RAW_QGOH_PQDP:P0074","trace_type":"Structural","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"},{"id":"RAW_QGOH_PQDP:P0078","trace_type":"Structural","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"},{"id":"RAW_QGOH_PQDP:P0086","trace_type":"Structural","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_QGOH_PQDP:SD5","text":"Theologia affectiva fit asymmetrica: Mens Deum amat quia affectus suos intelligit, sed Deus nec amorem nec odium erga homines habet.","refs":["CH_QGOH_PQDP:A14","CH_QGOH_PQDP:A15","CH_QGOH_PQDP:A16"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"From","passages":[{"id":"RAW_QGOH_PQDP:P0090","trace_type":"Composite","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"},{"id":"RAW_QGOH_PQDP:P0094","trace_type":"Composite","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"},{"id":"RAW_QGOH_PQDP:P0102","trace_type":"Composite","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"},{"id":"RAW_QGOH_PQDP:P0106","trace_type":"Composite","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]}],"original_name":"Super-Derivatives"},{"number":"6","name":"Deep Apophatics","items":[{"id":"CH_QGOH_PQDP:DNX1","text":"Falsum esse debet affectum esse residuum cognitioni impervium; aliter cognitio clara passionem mutare non posset.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_QGOH_PQDP:P0027","trace_type":"Negative","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"},{"id":"RAW_QGOH_PQDP:P0033","trace_type":"Negative","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"},{"id":"RAW_QGOH_PQDP:P0037","trace_type":"Negative","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_QGOH_PQDP:DNX2","text":"Falsum esse debet Mentem posse extra ordinem causarum stare atque voluntate nuda Corpore imperare.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_QGOH_PQDP:P0006","trace_type":"Negative","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"},{"id":"RAW_QGOH_PQDP:P0012","trace_type":"Negative","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"},{"id":"RAW_QGOH_PQDP:P0019","trace_type":"Negative","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"},{"id":"RAW_QGOH_PQDP:P0021","trace_type":"Negative","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_QGOH_PQDP:DNX3","text":"Falsum esse debet desideria per se virtutes aut vitia esse; eorum status ex adaequatione vel inadaequatione causae mentis pendet.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_QGOH_PQDP:P0040","trace_type":"Negative","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"},{"id":"RAW_QGOH_PQDP:P0041","trace_type":"Negative","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_QGOH_PQDP:DNX4","text":"Falsum esse debet necessitatem rerum omnem agendi potentiam destruere; eadem necessitas intellecta affectus mitigat et ordinationem rationalem permittit.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_QGOH_PQDP:P0047","trace_type":"Negative","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"},{"id":"RAW_QGOH_PQDP:P0068","trace_type":"Negative","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"},{"id":"RAW_QGOH_PQDP:P0072","trace_type":"Negative","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"},{"id":"RAW_QGOH_PQDP:P0074","trace_type":"Negative","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_QGOH_PQDP:DNX5","text":"Falsum esse debet Deum affectibus humanis analogis moveri; aliter Amor Dei in mutuam passionum commutationem redigeretur.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_QGOH_PQDP:P0102","trace_type":"Negative","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"},{"id":"RAW_QGOH_PQDP:P0106","trace_type":"Negative","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]}],"original_name":"Deep Apophatics"},{"number":"7","name":"Super-Apophatics","items":[{"id":"CH_QGOH_PQDP:SNX1","text":"Excluditur possibilitas libertatis ut absolutae autonomia singularis, quia Mentis imperium in affectus ex parte tantum obtinet et condicionibus conflictus affectivi subiacet.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_QGOH_PQDP:P0006","trace_type":"Negative","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"},{"id":"RAW_QGOH_PQDP:P0039","trace_type":"Negative","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"},{"id":"RAW_QGOH_PQDP:P0068","trace_type":"Negative","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_QGOH_PQDP:SNX2","text":"Excluditur punctum extra seriem causarum ex quo homo affectus suos libere eligere possit; cognitio liberans ipsa intellectus ordinis necessarii est.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_QGOH_PQDP:P0045","trace_type":"Negative","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"},{"id":"RAW_QGOH_PQDP:P0047","trace_type":"Negative","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"},{"id":"RAW_QGOH_PQDP:P0055","trace_type":"Negative","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_QGOH_PQDP:SNX3","text":"Excluditur forma religionis in qua Deus responderet odio, amore, praemio, aut offensa; relatio ad Deum est ordinatio Mentis, non mutua affectio Dei.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_QGOH_PQDP:P0090","trace_type":"Negative","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"},{"id":"RAW_QGOH_PQDP:P0094","trace_type":"Negative","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"},{"id":"RAW_QGOH_PQDP:P0102","trace_type":"Negative","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"},{"id":"RAW_QGOH_PQDP:P0106","trace_type":"Negative","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]}],"original_name":"Super-Apophatics"},{"number":"8","name":"Formal Generative Schema","items":[{"id":"CH_QGOH_PQDP:FGS1","text":"Ignorantia causarum → res ut simpliciter libera imaginatur → affectus maximus excitatur.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_QGOH_PQDP:P0043","trace_type":"Direct","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"},{"id":"RAW_QGOH_PQDP:P0045","trace_type":"Direct","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_QGOH_PQDP:FGS2","text":"Affectus confusus cum idea causae externae → passio, Amor, Odium, et fluctuationes animi producuntur.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_QGOH_PQDP:P0023","trace_type":"Composite","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"},{"id":"RAW_QGOH_PQDP:P0025","trace_type":"Composite","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"},{"id":"RAW_QGOH_PQDP:P0029","trace_type":"Composite","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_QGOH_PQDP:FGS3","text":"Conceptus clarus affectus + intellectus necessitatis → imminutio passivitatis et imminutio affectus erga causam singularem.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_QGOH_PQDP:P0027","trace_type":"Composite","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"},{"id":"RAW_QGOH_PQDP:P0031","trace_type":"Composite","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"},{"id":"RAW_QGOH_PQDP:P0047","trace_type":"Composite","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"},{"id":"RAW_QGOH_PQDP:P0049","trace_type":"Composite","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_QGOH_PQDP:FGS4","text":"Ordo idearum → ordo affectionum Corporis → nexus imaginum rationalium → memoria praeceptorum → responsio affectiva praeparata.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_QGOH_PQDP:P0019","trace_type":"Structural","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"},{"id":"RAW_QGOH_PQDP:P0068","trace_type":"Structural","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"},{"id":"RAW_QGOH_PQDP:P0072","trace_type":"Structural","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"},{"id":"RAW_QGOH_PQDP:P0082","trace_type":"Structural","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"},{"id":"RAW_QGOH_PQDP:P0086","trace_type":"Structural","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_QGOH_PQDP:FGS5","text":"Cupiditas ex idea inadaequata → passio; eadem Cupiditas ex idea adaequata → actio sive virtus.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_QGOH_PQDP:P0040","trace_type":"Direct","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"},{"id":"RAW_QGOH_PQDP:P0041","trace_type":"Direct","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_QGOH_PQDP:FGS6","text":"Omnes affectiones ad ideam Dei referuntur → Amor Dei omnibus affectibus fovetur → Mens maxime occupatur, sine reciproca Dei passione.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_QGOH_PQDP:P0090","trace_type":"Structural","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"},{"id":"RAW_QGOH_PQDP:P0094","trace_type":"Structural","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"},{"id":"RAW_QGOH_PQDP:P0098","trace_type":"Structural","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"},{"id":"RAW_QGOH_PQDP:P0100","trace_type":"Structural","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"},{"id":"RAW_QGOH_PQDP:P0102","trace_type":"Structural","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]}],"original_name":"Formal Generative Schema"},{"number":"9","name":"Argument Flow Map","items":[{"id":"CH_QGOH_PQDP:AF1","text":"Imperium voluntatis absolutum negatur → potentia Mentis in intelligentia collocatur → affectus ut ideae confusae intelliguntur → cognitio clara et necessitatis passivitatem minuit → imagines et praecepta in ordinem rationalem concatenantur → Amor Dei universaliter fovetur → impassibilitas Dei relationem theologicam non reciprocam constituit.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_QGOH_PQDP:P0006","trace_type":"Structural","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"},{"id":"RAW_QGOH_PQDP:P0013","trace_type":"Structural","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"},{"id":"RAW_QGOH_PQDP:P0019","trace_type":"Structural","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"},{"id":"RAW_QGOH_PQDP:P0027","trace_type":"Structural","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"},{"id":"RAW_QGOH_PQDP:P0047","trace_type":"Structural","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"},{"id":"RAW_QGOH_PQDP:P0068","trace_type":"Structural","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"},{"id":"RAW_QGOH_PQDP:P0072","trace_type":"Structural","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"},{"id":"RAW_QGOH_PQDP:P0090","trace_type":"Structural","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"},{"id":"RAW_QGOH_PQDP:P0102","trace_type":"Structural","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_QGOH_PQDP:AF2","text":"Amor Dei maxime occupans → enuntiatio impossibilitatis odii erga Deum, cuius demonstratio in segmento dato suspenditur.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_QGOH_PQDP:P0098","trace_type":"Tension","confidence":"medium","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"},{"id":"RAW_QGOH_PQDP:P0108","trace_type":"Tension","confidence":"medium","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]}],"original_name":"Argument Flow Map"},{"number":"10","name":"Map-Effects","items":[{"id":"CH_QGOH_PQDP:ME1","text":"Affectus reclassificatur ut structura ideationalis et corporalis ordinabilis, non ut impetus voluntati extrinsecus oppositus.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_QGOH_PQDP:P0019","trace_type":"Structural","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"},{"id":"RAW_QGOH_PQDP:P0027","trace_type":"Structural","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"},{"id":"RAW_QGOH_PQDP:P0068","trace_type":"Structural","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_QGOH_PQDP:ME2","text":"Libertas reclassificatur ut graduata imminutio passivitatis, non ut exemptio a necessitate causarum.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_QGOH_PQDP:P0045","trace_type":"Structural","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"},{"id":"RAW_QGOH_PQDP:P0047","trace_type":"Structural","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"},{"id":"RAW_QGOH_PQDP:P0053","trace_type":"Structural","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"},{"id":"RAW_QGOH_PQDP:P0068","trace_type":"Structural","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_QGOH_PQDP:ME3","text":"Distinctio passionis et virtutis transfertur ab obiecto Cupiditatis ad originem eius in ideis inadaequatis vel adaequatis.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_QGOH_PQDP:P0040","trace_type":"Direct","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"},{"id":"RAW_QGOH_PQDP:P0041","trace_type":"Direct","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_QGOH_PQDP:ME4","text":"Memoria, imaginatio, et habitus fiunt instrumenta Rationis, quia nexus imaginum affectus futuros vel fovent vel moderantur.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_QGOH_PQDP:P0072","trace_type":"Structural","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"},{"id":"RAW_QGOH_PQDP:P0073","trace_type":"Structural","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"},{"id":"RAW_QGOH_PQDP:P0078","trace_type":"Structural","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"},{"id":"RAW_QGOH_PQDP:P0082","trace_type":"Structural","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"},{"id":"RAW_QGOH_PQDP:P0086","trace_type":"Structural","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_QGOH_PQDP:ME5","text":"Amor Dei describitur ut effectus cognitionis universalis et nexus affectuum, non ut commercium cum Deo affective respondente.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_QGOH_PQDP:P0090","trace_type":"Composite","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"},{"id":"RAW_QGOH_PQDP:P0094","trace_type":"Composite","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"},{"id":"RAW_QGOH_PQDP:P0100","trace_type":"Composite","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"},{"id":"RAW_QGOH_PQDP:P0106","trace_type":"Composite","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]}],"original_name":"Map-Effects"},{"number":"11","name":"Altitude Levels","items":[{"id":"CH_QGOH_PQDP:ALT1","text":"Surface — Quaeritur quomodo affectus coerceri, moderari, et ad actiones vitae dirig i possint.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_QGOH_PQDP:P0006","trace_type":"Structural","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"},{"id":"RAW_QGOH_PQDP:P0068","trace_type":"Structural","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"},{"id":"RAW_QGOH_PQDP:P0072","trace_type":"Structural","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_QGOH_PQDP:ALT2","text":"Structural — Mechanismus operatur per correspondentiam ordinis idearum et affectionum Corporis, per claritatem idearum, et per concatenationem imaginum in memoria.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_QGOH_PQDP:P0019","trace_type":"Structural","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"},{"id":"RAW_QGOH_PQDP:P0027","trace_type":"Structural","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"},{"id":"RAW_QGOH_PQDP:P0068","trace_type":"Structural","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"},{"id":"RAW_QGOH_PQDP:P0072","trace_type":"Structural","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"},{"id":"RAW_QGOH_PQDP:P0082","trace_type":"Structural","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_QGOH_PQDP:ALT3","text":"Metastrategic — Certamen contra affectus transfertur a repulsione negativa ad praeoccupationem imaginativam per dogmata vitae, virtutes, Laetitiam, et cognitionem veri utilis.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_QGOH_PQDP:P0072","trace_type":"Structural","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"},{"id":"RAW_QGOH_PQDP:P0073","trace_type":"Structural","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"},{"id":"RAW_QGOH_PQDP:P0074","trace_type":"Structural","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"},{"id":"RAW_QGOH_PQDP:P0076","trace_type":"Structural","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_QGOH_PQDP:ALT4","text":"Ontological/Temporal — Rerum necessitas, concatenatio infinita causarum, constantia proprietatum communium, et impassibilitas Dei determinant condiciones sub quibus libertas graduata intelligitur.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_QGOH_PQDP:P0047","trace_type":"Structural","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"},{"id":"RAW_QGOH_PQDP:P0049","trace_type":"Structural","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"},{"id":"RAW_QGOH_PQDP:P0055","trace_type":"Structural","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"},{"id":"RAW_QGOH_PQDP:P0056","trace_type":"Structural","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"},{"id":"RAW_QGOH_PQDP:P0102","trace_type":"Structural","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]}],"original_name":"Altitude Levels"},{"number":"12","name":"Minimal Reconstruction Test","items":[{"id":"CH_QGOH_PQDP:MRT1","text":"Axiomata sufficiunt ad restituendum nucleum argumenti: Mentis potentia est intelligentia, affectus passivus est cognitio confusa, et libertas est imminutio passivitatis per ordinationem rationalem.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_QGOH_PQDP:P0013","trace_type":"Structural","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"},{"id":"RAW_QGOH_PQDP:P0019","trace_type":"Structural","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"},{"id":"RAW_QGOH_PQDP:P0027","trace_type":"Structural","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"},{"id":"RAW_QGOH_PQDP:P0068","trace_type":"Structural","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_QGOH_PQDP:MRT2","text":"Commissura firmissima est nexus inter ideam claram, intellectum necessitatis, et imminutam vim affectuum, quia hic nexus doctrinam, remedium, et praxim simul sustinet.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_QGOH_PQDP:P0027","trace_type":"Structural","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"},{"id":"RAW_QGOH_PQDP:P0031","trace_type":"Structural","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"},{"id":"RAW_QGOH_PQDP:P0047","trace_type":"Structural","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"},{"id":"RAW_QGOH_PQDP:P0049","trace_type":"Structural","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_QGOH_PQDP:MRT3","text":"Commissura fragilis manet inter propositionem quod omnis affectio aliquem conceptum clarum involvat et conclusionem quod affectus ipse clare et distincte intelligi possit; quantitas cognitionis requisitae non plene determinatur.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_QGOH_PQDP:P0033","trace_type":"Tension","confidence":"medium","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"},{"id":"RAW_QGOH_PQDP:P0037","trace_type":"Tension","confidence":"medium","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"},{"id":"RAW_QGOH_PQDP:P0039","trace_type":"Tension","confidence":"medium","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_QGOH_PQDP:MRT4","text":"Tensio interna inter augmentum affectus per plures causas et imminutam noxam affectus ad plures causas relati servanda est; textus eam distinguit per concursus causalis, distributionem obiectivam, et frequentiam imaginativam, sed distinctionem formaliter non universaliter explicat.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_QGOH_PQDP:P0058","trace_type":"Tension","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"},{"id":"RAW_QGOH_PQDP:P0064","trace_type":"Tension","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"},{"id":"RAW_QGOH_PQDP:P0078","trace_type":"Tension","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"},{"id":"RAW_QGOH_PQDP:P0080","trace_type":"Tension","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_QGOH_PQDP:MRT5","text":"Imperium Mentis non est absolutum: exercitium ordinationis valet dum affectus naturae contrarii Mentem non impediunt, et cognitio affectuum saepe tantum ex parte obtinetur.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_QGOH_PQDP:P0006","trace_type":"Tension","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"},{"id":"RAW_QGOH_PQDP:P0039","trace_type":"Tension","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"},{"id":"RAW_QGOH_PQDP:P0068","trace_type":"Tension","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_QGOH_PQDP:MRT6","text":"Transitus ad Amorem Dei nititur principio importato quo omnes affectiones ad ideam Dei referri possunt; intra segmentum datum haec relatio asseritur et demonstratur per remissum ad Partem I, non ex novo fundamento evolvitur.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_QGOH_PQDP:P0090","trace_type":"Direct","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"},{"id":"RAW_QGOH_PQDP:P0092","trace_type":"Direct","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_QGOH_PQDP:MRT7","text":"Enuntiatio finalis de impossibilitate odii erga Deum motum argumenti claudit, sed restitutio demonstrativa eius non potest compleri, quia demonstratio Prop. XVIII in fonte dato interrompitur.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[]}],"original_name":"Minimal Reconstruction Test"}],"language_code":"la","language_name":"Latina","translations":[{"id":"CAREVAR_20261015T014621352216Z_21beb733","language_code":"en","language_name":"English","title":"Part Five - On the Power of the Intellect, or on Human Freedom · passage 1","source_sha256":"bab3d19793ef850184df27b9dc1631c9e7edbeba2df701ce0f6cf8dcda453fda","sha256":"e08e79de68d32c193af1dc025bf6d35f2c3d2235a1bfc8b7d2383546599d191d","rendition_type":"machine","review_status":"machine","model":"gpt-5.6-terra","created":"2026-10-15T01:46:21.353107+00:00","sections":[{"number":"1","name":"Argumental Spine","items":[{"id":"CH_QGOH_PQDP:AS1","text":"Human freedom is sought not as the absolute dominion of the will over the affects, but as the measure by which the Mind, through Reason, suffers less from the affects and moderates them.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_QGOH_PQDP:P0006","trace_type":"Composite","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"},{"id":"RAW_QGOH_PQDP:P0013","trace_type":"Composite","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"},{"id":"RAW_QGOH_PQDP:P0031","trace_type":"Composite","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_QGOH_PQDP:AS2","text":"The polemical argument dismantles the Stoic and Cartesian opinion, because the will has neither a clear cause nor a measurable power by which it absolutely governs the motions of the Body and the passions.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_QGOH_PQDP:P0006","trace_type":"Structural","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"},{"id":"RAW_QGOH_PQDP:P0007","trace_type":"Structural","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"},{"id":"RAW_QGOH_PQDP:P0011","trace_type":"Structural","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"},{"id":"RAW_QGOH_PQDP:P0012","trace_type":"Structural","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"},{"id":"RAW_QGOH_PQDP:P0013","trace_type":"Structural","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_QGOH_PQDP:AS3","text":"The power of the Mind is situated in understanding alone; therefore affects are to be reformed not by the will, but by the ordering of ideas.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_QGOH_PQDP:P0013","trace_type":"Composite","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"},{"id":"RAW_QGOH_PQDP:P0039","trace_type":"Composite","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"},{"id":"RAW_QGOH_PQDP:P0041","trace_type":"Composite","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_QGOH_PQDP:AS4","text":"Clear knowledge, understanding of necessity, and the ordering of affects according to a plurality of causes diminish passivity and render rational affects more stable.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_QGOH_PQDP:P0027","trace_type":"Structural","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"},{"id":"RAW_QGOH_PQDP:P0031","trace_type":"Structural","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"},{"id":"RAW_QGOH_PQDP:P0047","trace_type":"Structural","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"},{"id":"RAW_QGOH_PQDP:P0049","trace_type":"Structural","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"},{"id":"RAW_QGOH_PQDP:P0053","trace_type":"Structural","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"},{"id":"RAW_QGOH_PQDP:P0055","trace_type":"Structural","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"},{"id":"RAW_QGOH_PQDP:P0056","trace_type":"Structural","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_QGOH_PQDP:AS5","text":"The ordering of images and the memory of precepts of life bring it about that the Mind anticipates future affects and directs them toward rational actions and joy.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_QGOH_PQDP:P0068","trace_type":"Structural","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"},{"id":"RAW_QGOH_PQDP:P0072","trace_type":"Structural","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"},{"id":"RAW_QGOH_PQDP:P0073","trace_type":"Structural","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"},{"id":"RAW_QGOH_PQDP:P0074","trace_type":"Structural","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"},{"id":"RAW_QGOH_PQDP:P0078","trace_type":"Structural","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"},{"id":"RAW_QGOH_PQDP:P0082","trace_type":"Structural","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"},{"id":"RAW_QGOH_PQDP:P0086","trace_type":"Structural","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_QGOH_PQDP:AS6","text":"All affections of the Body can be referred to the idea of God; from understanding oneself and one's affects there arises the Love of God, while God himself lacks every passion and reciprocal love.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_QGOH_PQDP:P0090","trace_type":"Structural","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"},{"id":"RAW_QGOH_PQDP:P0092","trace_type":"Structural","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"},{"id":"RAW_QGOH_PQDP:P0094","trace_type":"Structural","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"},{"id":"RAW_QGOH_PQDP:P0096","trace_type":"Structural","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"},{"id":"RAW_QGOH_PQDP:P0102","trace_type":"Structural","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"},{"id":"RAW_QGOH_PQDP:P0104","trace_type":"Structural","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"},{"id":"RAW_QGOH_PQDP:P0106","trace_type":"Structural","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_QGOH_PQDP:AS7","text":"The movement arrives at the proposition that no one can hate God, but the demonstration of this conclusion is not yet presented in the given source.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_QGOH_PQDP:P0108","trace_type":"Direct","confidence":"medium","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]}],"original_name":"Argumental Spine"},{"number":"2","name":"Load-Bearing Axioms","items":[{"id":"CH_QGOH_PQDP:A1","text":"The power of the Mind is defined by understanding alone, that is, by thought and the formation of adequate ideas.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_QGOH_PQDP:P0013","trace_type":"Direct","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"},{"id":"RAW_QGOH_PQDP:P0041","trace_type":"Direct","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_QGOH_PQDP:A2","text":"The order and connection of ideas in the Mind correspond to the order and connection of the affections of the Body.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_QGOH_PQDP:P0019","trace_type":"Direct","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"},{"id":"RAW_QGOH_PQDP:P0021","trace_type":"Direct","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_QGOH_PQDP:A3","text":"An affect, insofar as it is a passion, is a confused idea; a clear and distinct idea of it makes it, insofar as it is referred to the Mind, cease to be a passion.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_QGOH_PQDP:P0027","trace_type":"Direct","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"},{"id":"RAW_QGOH_PQDP:P0029","trace_type":"Direct","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_QGOH_PQDP:A4","text":"Every affection of the Body involves some clear and distinct conception; therefore every affect can at least in part be clearly understood.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_QGOH_PQDP:P0033","trace_type":"Direct","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"},{"id":"RAW_QGOH_PQDP:P0035","trace_type":"Direct","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"},{"id":"RAW_QGOH_PQDP:P0037","trace_type":"Direct","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_QGOH_PQDP:A5","text":"Love and Hate consist in Joy or Sadness together with the idea of an external cause; once that causal connection is removed, their form is destroyed.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_QGOH_PQDP:P0023","trace_type":"Direct","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"},{"id":"RAW_QGOH_PQDP:P0025","trace_type":"Direct","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_QGOH_PQDP:A6","text":"A thing imagined simply as free excites the greatest affect, because the causes by which it is determined are unknown.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_QGOH_PQDP:P0043","trace_type":"Direct","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"},{"id":"RAW_QGOH_PQDP:P0045","trace_type":"Direct","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_QGOH_PQDP:A7","text":"The Mind, insofar as it understands things as necessary and determined by an infinite nexus of causes, suffers less from the affects.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_QGOH_PQDP:P0047","trace_type":"Direct","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"},{"id":"RAW_QGOH_PQDP:P0049","trace_type":"Direct","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_QGOH_PQDP:A8","text":"An affect arising from Reason is referred to common properties that are always present and is therefore more stable than contrary temporal affects.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_QGOH_PQDP:P0053","trace_type":"Direct","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"},{"id":"RAW_QGOH_PQDP:P0055","trace_type":"Direct","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"},{"id":"RAW_QGOH_PQDP:P0056","trace_type":"Direct","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_QGOH_PQDP:A9","text":"An affect excited by several simultaneously concurrent causes is stronger.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_QGOH_PQDP:P0058","trace_type":"Direct","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"},{"id":"RAW_QGOH_PQDP:P0060","trace_type":"Direct","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_QGOH_PQDP:A10","text":"An affect referred to several causes or objects impedes the Mind less, makes it less passive, and affects it less intensely toward each individual cause.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_QGOH_PQDP:P0064","trace_type":"Direct","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"},{"id":"RAW_QGOH_PQDP:P0066","trace_type":"Direct","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_QGOH_PQDP:A11","text":"An image joined to several images is excited more frequently; images pertaining to things clearly understood enter such connections more easily.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_QGOH_PQDP:P0078","trace_type":"Composite","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"},{"id":"RAW_QGOH_PQDP:P0080","trace_type":"Composite","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"},{"id":"RAW_QGOH_PQDP:P0082","trace_type":"Composite","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"},{"id":"RAW_QGOH_PQDP:P0084","trace_type":"Composite","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"},{"id":"RAW_QGOH_PQDP:P0086","trace_type":"Composite","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"},{"id":"RAW_QGOH_PQDP:P0088","trace_type":"Composite","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_QGOH_PQDP:A12","text":"So long as affects contrary in nature do not impede the Mind, the Mind can order and connect the affections of the Body according to an order directed toward understanding.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_QGOH_PQDP:P0068","trace_type":"Direct","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"},{"id":"RAW_QGOH_PQDP:P0070","trace_type":"Direct","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_QGOH_PQDP:A13","text":"Precepts of life committed to memory and joined to images of particular things prepare rational affects for future occurrences.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_QGOH_PQDP:P0072","trace_type":"Direct","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"},{"id":"RAW_QGOH_PQDP:P0073","trace_type":"Direct","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"},{"id":"RAW_QGOH_PQDP:P0074","trace_type":"Direct","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_QGOH_PQDP:A14","text":"The Mind can refer all affections of the Body to the idea of God.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_QGOH_PQDP:P0090","trace_type":"Direct","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"},{"id":"RAW_QGOH_PQDP:P0092","trace_type":"Direct","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_QGOH_PQDP:A15","text":"Clear knowledge of oneself and one's affects produces Joy together with the idea of God, and hence the Love of God; because this Love is joined to all affections, it most greatly occupies the Mind.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_QGOH_PQDP:P0094","trace_type":"Composite","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"},{"id":"RAW_QGOH_PQDP:P0096","trace_type":"Composite","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"},{"id":"RAW_QGOH_PQDP:P0098","trace_type":"Composite","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"},{"id":"RAW_QGOH_PQDP:P0100","trace_type":"Composite","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_QGOH_PQDP:A16","text":"God is devoid of passions, and, properly speaking, neither loves nor hates.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_QGOH_PQDP:P0102","trace_type":"Direct","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"},{"id":"RAW_QGOH_PQDP:P0104","trace_type":"Direct","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"},{"id":"RAW_QGOH_PQDP:P0106","trace_type":"Direct","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_QGOH_PQDP:A17","text":"The same Desire is a passion if it arises from inadequate ideas, but a virtue if it is generated from adequate ideas.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_QGOH_PQDP:P0040","trace_type":"Direct","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"},{"id":"RAW_QGOH_PQDP:P0041","trace_type":"Direct","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]}],"original_name":"Load-Bearing Axioms"},{"number":"3","name":"Apophatic Structure","items":[{"id":"CH_QGOH_PQDP:NX1","text":"Freedom is not the absolute dominion of the will over the affects.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_QGOH_PQDP:P0006","trace_type":"Direct","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_QGOH_PQDP:NX2","text":"The Mind is not explained as a thing separate from the Body that moves the pineal gland by the will and thus mechanically governs the passions.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_QGOH_PQDP:P0007","trace_type":"Negative","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"},{"id":"RAW_QGOH_PQDP:P0011","trace_type":"Negative","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"},{"id":"RAW_QGOH_PQDP:P0012","trace_type":"Negative","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"},{"id":"RAW_QGOH_PQDP:P0013","trace_type":"Negative","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_QGOH_PQDP:NX3","text":"The remedy for the affects is neither the medical art of the Body nor the art of perfecting the Intellect, but the knowledge by which the Mind understands and orders the affects.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_QGOH_PQDP:P0006","trace_type":"Composite","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"},{"id":"RAW_QGOH_PQDP:P0013","trace_type":"Composite","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"},{"id":"RAW_QGOH_PQDP:P0041","trace_type":"Composite","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_QGOH_PQDP:NX4","text":"An affect is not a blind force wholly external to knowledge, because every affect is said to involve a clear and distinct conception.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_QGOH_PQDP:P0033","trace_type":"Direct","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"},{"id":"RAW_QGOH_PQDP:P0037","trace_type":"Direct","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_QGOH_PQDP:NX5","text":"The diminution of affects does not proceed from imagined contingency or freedom of things, but from the understanding of their necessity.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_QGOH_PQDP:P0045","trace_type":"Negative","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"},{"id":"RAW_QGOH_PQDP:P0047","trace_type":"Negative","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"},{"id":"RAW_QGOH_PQDP:P0049","trace_type":"Negative","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_QGOH_PQDP:NX6","text":"The moderation of affects does not consist in contemplation of vices, detraction of persons, or a false appearance of freedom.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_QGOH_PQDP:P0074","trace_type":"Negative","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"},{"id":"RAW_QGOH_PQDP:P0075","trace_type":"Negative","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"},{"id":"RAW_QGOH_PQDP:P0076","trace_type":"Negative","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_QGOH_PQDP:NX7","text":"The Love of God is not a reciprocal relation between a passible God and a human being, because God is affected neither by Joy nor by Sadness, neither by love nor by hate.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_QGOH_PQDP:P0102","trace_type":"Negative","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"},{"id":"RAW_QGOH_PQDP:P0106","trace_type":"Negative","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]}],"original_name":"Apophatic Structure"},{"number":"4","name":"Derivative Propositions","items":[{"id":"CH_QGOH_PQDP:D1","text":"Knowledge of an affect diminishes passivity, because an affect, insofar as it is clearly understood, ceases to be a passion, and no affect is wholly excluded from clear knowledge.","refs":["CH_QGOH_PQDP:A3","CH_QGOH_PQDP:A4"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"From","passages":[{"id":"RAW_QGOH_PQDP:P0027","trace_type":"Composite","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"},{"id":"RAW_QGOH_PQDP:P0029","trace_type":"Composite","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"},{"id":"RAW_QGOH_PQDP:P0031","trace_type":"Composite","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"},{"id":"RAW_QGOH_PQDP:P0033","trace_type":"Composite","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"},{"id":"RAW_QGOH_PQDP:P0037","trace_type":"Composite","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_QGOH_PQDP:D2","text":"Love, Hate, and the fluctuations arising from them can be dissolved if the affect is separated from the idea of an external cause and joined to true thoughts.","refs":["CH_QGOH_PQDP:A3","CH_QGOH_PQDP:A4","CH_QGOH_PQDP:A5"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"From","passages":[{"id":"RAW_QGOH_PQDP:P0023","trace_type":"Composite","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"},{"id":"RAW_QGOH_PQDP:P0025","trace_type":"Composite","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"},{"id":"RAW_QGOH_PQDP:P0039","trace_type":"Composite","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_QGOH_PQDP:D3","text":"Understanding necessity transforms the pain of lost singular things, because it prevents one from imagining a thing as a defect of Nature, an injury, or an avoidable accident.","refs":["CH_QGOH_PQDP:A6","CH_QGOH_PQDP:A7"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"From","passages":[{"id":"RAW_QGOH_PQDP:P0045","trace_type":"Composite","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"},{"id":"RAW_QGOH_PQDP:P0047","trace_type":"Composite","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"},{"id":"RAW_QGOH_PQDP:P0051","trace_type":"Composite","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_QGOH_PQDP:D4","text":"A rational affect, because it is referred to common things always present, gradually prevails over contrary external affects not continuously sustained.","refs":["CH_QGOH_PQDP:A8"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"From","passages":[{"id":"RAW_QGOH_PQDP:P0053","trace_type":"Direct","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"},{"id":"RAW_QGOH_PQDP:P0055","trace_type":"Direct","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"},{"id":"RAW_QGOH_PQDP:P0056","trace_type":"Direct","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_QGOH_PQDP:D5","text":"The architecture of affects distinguishes three operations: the concurrence of causes increases an affect, its distribution among several objects diminishes its harm, and the multiplication of imaginative connections increases its frequency.","refs":["CH_QGOH_PQDP:A9","CH_QGOH_PQDP:A10","CH_QGOH_PQDP:A11"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"From","passages":[{"id":"RAW_QGOH_PQDP:P0058","trace_type":"Composite","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"},{"id":"RAW_QGOH_PQDP:P0064","trace_type":"Composite","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"},{"id":"RAW_QGOH_PQDP:P0078","trace_type":"Composite","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"},{"id":"RAW_QGOH_PQDP:P0086","trace_type":"Composite","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_QGOH_PQDP:D6","text":"Premeditation of rational precepts becomes a bodily and imaginative disposition, because ideas firmly retained in memory connect the affections of the Body according to understanding.","refs":["CH_QGOH_PQDP:A2","CH_QGOH_PQDP:A11","CH_QGOH_PQDP:A12","CH_QGOH_PQDP:A13"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"From","passages":[{"id":"RAW_QGOH_PQDP:P0019","trace_type":"Structural","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"},{"id":"RAW_QGOH_PQDP:P0068","trace_type":"Structural","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"},{"id":"RAW_QGOH_PQDP:P0072","trace_type":"Structural","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"},{"id":"RAW_QGOH_PQDP:P0082","trace_type":"Structural","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"},{"id":"RAW_QGOH_PQDP:P0086","trace_type":"Structural","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_QGOH_PQDP:D7","text":"The same action or Desire is to be classified as a passion or a virtue not from its object, but from the kind of idea from which it arises.","refs":["CH_QGOH_PQDP:A3","CH_QGOH_PQDP:A17"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"From","passages":[{"id":"RAW_QGOH_PQDP:P0040","trace_type":"Direct","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"},{"id":"RAW_QGOH_PQDP:P0041","trace_type":"Direct","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_QGOH_PQDP:D8","text":"The Love of God becomes the affect that most occupies, because every affection of the Body can be referred to God and thus several causes foster the same love.","refs":["CH_QGOH_PQDP:A9","CH_QGOH_PQDP:A14","CH_QGOH_PQDP:A15"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"From","passages":[{"id":"RAW_QGOH_PQDP:P0058","trace_type":"Composite","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"},{"id":"RAW_QGOH_PQDP:P0090","trace_type":"Composite","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"},{"id":"RAW_QGOH_PQDP:P0094","trace_type":"Composite","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"},{"id":"RAW_QGOH_PQDP:P0100","trace_type":"Composite","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]}],"original_name":"Derivative Propositions"},{"number":"5","name":"Super-Derivatives","items":[{"id":"CH_QGOH_PQDP:SD1","text":"Freedom is not a diminution of causality, but a diminution of passivity through the reordering of ideas, affections of the Body, memory, and images.","refs":["CH_QGOH_PQDP:A1","CH_QGOH_PQDP:A2","CH_QGOH_PQDP:A3","CH_QGOH_PQDP:A12","CH_QGOH_PQDP:A13"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"From","passages":[{"id":"RAW_QGOH_PQDP:P0013","trace_type":"Structural","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"},{"id":"RAW_QGOH_PQDP:P0019","trace_type":"Structural","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"},{"id":"RAW_QGOH_PQDP:P0027","trace_type":"Structural","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"},{"id":"RAW_QGOH_PQDP:P0068","trace_type":"Structural","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"},{"id":"RAW_QGOH_PQDP:P0072","trace_type":"Structural","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_QGOH_PQDP:SD2","text":"The necessity of Nature does not produce fatalism, but a means of foreseeing and mitigating affects, because the understanding of causes joins diminished passivity with practical preparation.","refs":["CH_QGOH_PQDP:A7","CH_QGOH_PQDP:A8","CH_QGOH_PQDP:A12","CH_QGOH_PQDP:A13"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"From","passages":[{"id":"RAW_QGOH_PQDP:P0047","trace_type":"Structural","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"},{"id":"RAW_QGOH_PQDP:P0053","trace_type":"Structural","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"},{"id":"RAW_QGOH_PQDP:P0068","trace_type":"Structural","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"},{"id":"RAW_QGOH_PQDP:P0072","trace_type":"Structural","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_QGOH_PQDP:SD3","text":"The ethics of affects passes from judgment concerning desired objects to judgment concerning the ideational causes from which desire is generated.","refs":["CH_QGOH_PQDP:A3","CH_QGOH_PQDP:A17"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"From","passages":[{"id":"RAW_QGOH_PQDP:P0029","trace_type":"Composite","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"},{"id":"RAW_QGOH_PQDP:P0040","trace_type":"Composite","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_QGOH_PQDP:SD4","text":"Rational practice not only represses hostile affects, but multiplies joy, virtues, common utility, and images of goods in such a way that contrary affects have less room.","refs":["CH_QGOH_PQDP:A10","CH_QGOH_PQDP:A11","CH_QGOH_PQDP:A13"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"From","passages":[{"id":"RAW_QGOH_PQDP:P0064","trace_type":"Structural","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"},{"id":"RAW_QGOH_PQDP:P0072","trace_type":"Structural","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"},{"id":"RAW_QGOH_PQDP:P0074","trace_type":"Structural","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"},{"id":"RAW_QGOH_PQDP:P0078","trace_type":"Structural","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"},{"id":"RAW_QGOH_PQDP:P0086","trace_type":"Structural","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_QGOH_PQDP:SD5","text":"Affective theology becomes asymmetrical: the Mind loves God because it understands its affects, but God has neither love nor hate toward human beings.","refs":["CH_QGOH_PQDP:A14","CH_QGOH_PQDP:A15","CH_QGOH_PQDP:A16"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"From","passages":[{"id":"RAW_QGOH_PQDP:P0090","trace_type":"Composite","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"},{"id":"RAW_QGOH_PQDP:P0094","trace_type":"Composite","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"},{"id":"RAW_QGOH_PQDP:P0102","trace_type":"Composite","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"},{"id":"RAW_QGOH_PQDP:P0106","trace_type":"Composite","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]}],"original_name":"Super-Derivatives"},{"number":"6","name":"Deep Apophatics","items":[{"id":"CH_QGOH_PQDP:DNX1","text":"It must be false that an affect is a residue impervious to knowledge; otherwise clear knowledge could not transform passion.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_QGOH_PQDP:P0027","trace_type":"Negative","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"},{"id":"RAW_QGOH_PQDP:P0033","trace_type":"Negative","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"},{"id":"RAW_QGOH_PQDP:P0037","trace_type":"Negative","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_QGOH_PQDP:DNX2","text":"It must be false that the Mind can stand outside the order of causes and command the Body by bare will.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_QGOH_PQDP:P0006","trace_type":"Negative","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"},{"id":"RAW_QGOH_PQDP:P0012","trace_type":"Negative","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"},{"id":"RAW_QGOH_PQDP:P0019","trace_type":"Negative","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"},{"id":"RAW_QGOH_PQDP:P0021","trace_type":"Negative","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_QGOH_PQDP:DNX3","text":"It must be false that desires are in themselves virtues or vices; their status depends on the adequacy or inadequacy of the cause in the mind.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_QGOH_PQDP:P0040","trace_type":"Negative","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"},{"id":"RAW_QGOH_PQDP:P0041","trace_type":"Negative","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_QGOH_PQDP:DNX4","text":"It must be false that the necessity of things destroys every power of acting; that same necessity, when understood, mitigates affects and permits rational ordering.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_QGOH_PQDP:P0047","trace_type":"Negative","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"},{"id":"RAW_QGOH_PQDP:P0068","trace_type":"Negative","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"},{"id":"RAW_QGOH_PQDP:P0072","trace_type":"Negative","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"},{"id":"RAW_QGOH_PQDP:P0074","trace_type":"Negative","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_QGOH_PQDP:DNX5","text":"It must be false that God is moved by affects analogous to human ones; otherwise the Love of God would be reduced to a mutual exchange of passions.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_QGOH_PQDP:P0102","trace_type":"Negative","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"},{"id":"RAW_QGOH_PQDP:P0106","trace_type":"Negative","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]}],"original_name":"Deep Apophatics"},{"number":"7","name":"Super-Apophatics","items":[{"id":"CH_QGOH_PQDP:SNX1","text":"The possibility of freedom as the absolute autonomy of the individual is excluded, because the Mind's dominion over the affects obtains only in part and is subject to the conditions of affective conflict.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_QGOH_PQDP:P0006","trace_type":"Negative","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"},{"id":"RAW_QGOH_PQDP:P0039","trace_type":"Negative","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"},{"id":"RAW_QGOH_PQDP:P0068","trace_type":"Negative","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_QGOH_PQDP:SNX2","text":"A point outside the series of causes from which a human being could freely choose his affects is excluded; liberating knowledge itself is understanding of the necessary order.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_QGOH_PQDP:P0045","trace_type":"Negative","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"},{"id":"RAW_QGOH_PQDP:P0047","trace_type":"Negative","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"},{"id":"RAW_QGOH_PQDP:P0055","trace_type":"Negative","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_QGOH_PQDP:SNX3","text":"A form of religion in which God would respond with hate, love, reward, or offense is excluded; the relation to God is an ordering of the Mind, not a mutual affection of God.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_QGOH_PQDP:P0090","trace_type":"Negative","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"},{"id":"RAW_QGOH_PQDP:P0094","trace_type":"Negative","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"},{"id":"RAW_QGOH_PQDP:P0102","trace_type":"Negative","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"},{"id":"RAW_QGOH_PQDP:P0106","trace_type":"Negative","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]}],"original_name":"Super-Apophatics"},{"number":"8","name":"Formal Generative Schema","items":[{"id":"CH_QGOH_PQDP:FGS1","text":"Ignorance of causes → a thing is imagined as simply free → the greatest affect is excited.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_QGOH_PQDP:P0043","trace_type":"Direct","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"},{"id":"RAW_QGOH_PQDP:P0045","trace_type":"Direct","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_QGOH_PQDP:FGS2","text":"Confused affect together with the idea of an external cause → passion, Love, Hate, and fluctuations of mind are produced.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_QGOH_PQDP:P0023","trace_type":"Composite","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"},{"id":"RAW_QGOH_PQDP:P0025","trace_type":"Composite","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"},{"id":"RAW_QGOH_PQDP:P0029","trace_type":"Composite","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_QGOH_PQDP:FGS3","text":"Clear conception of an affect + understanding of necessity → diminution of passivity and diminution of the affect toward a singular cause.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_QGOH_PQDP:P0027","trace_type":"Composite","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"},{"id":"RAW_QGOH_PQDP:P0031","trace_type":"Composite","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"},{"id":"RAW_QGOH_PQDP:P0047","trace_type":"Composite","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"},{"id":"RAW_QGOH_PQDP:P0049","trace_type":"Composite","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_QGOH_PQDP:FGS4","text":"Order of ideas → order of affections of the Body → nexus of rational images → memory of precepts → prepared affective response.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_QGOH_PQDP:P0019","trace_type":"Structural","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"},{"id":"RAW_QGOH_PQDP:P0068","trace_type":"Structural","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"},{"id":"RAW_QGOH_PQDP:P0072","trace_type":"Structural","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"},{"id":"RAW_QGOH_PQDP:P0082","trace_type":"Structural","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"},{"id":"RAW_QGOH_PQDP:P0086","trace_type":"Structural","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_QGOH_PQDP:FGS5","text":"Desire from an inadequate idea → passion; the same Desire from an adequate idea → action or virtue.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_QGOH_PQDP:P0040","trace_type":"Direct","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"},{"id":"RAW_QGOH_PQDP:P0041","trace_type":"Direct","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_QGOH_PQDP:FGS6","text":"All affections are referred to the idea of God → the Love of God is fostered by all affects → the Mind is most greatly occupied, without reciprocal divine passion.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_QGOH_PQDP:P0090","trace_type":"Structural","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"},{"id":"RAW_QGOH_PQDP:P0094","trace_type":"Structural","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"},{"id":"RAW_QGOH_PQDP:P0098","trace_type":"Structural","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"},{"id":"RAW_QGOH_PQDP:P0100","trace_type":"Structural","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"},{"id":"RAW_QGOH_PQDP:P0102","trace_type":"Structural","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]}],"original_name":"Formal Generative Schema"},{"number":"9","name":"Argument Flow Map","items":[{"id":"CH_QGOH_PQDP:AF1","text":"Absolute dominion of the will is denied → the power of the Mind is located in understanding → affects are understood as confused ideas → clear knowledge and understanding of necessity diminish passivity → images and precepts are connected into a rational order → the Love of God is universally fostered → the impassibility of God establishes a non-reciprocal theological relation.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_QGOH_PQDP:P0006","trace_type":"Structural","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"},{"id":"RAW_QGOH_PQDP:P0013","trace_type":"Structural","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"},{"id":"RAW_QGOH_PQDP:P0019","trace_type":"Structural","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"},{"id":"RAW_QGOH_PQDP:P0027","trace_type":"Structural","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"},{"id":"RAW_QGOH_PQDP:P0047","trace_type":"Structural","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"},{"id":"RAW_QGOH_PQDP:P0068","trace_type":"Structural","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"},{"id":"RAW_QGOH_PQDP:P0072","trace_type":"Structural","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"},{"id":"RAW_QGOH_PQDP:P0090","trace_type":"Structural","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"},{"id":"RAW_QGOH_PQDP:P0102","trace_type":"Structural","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_QGOH_PQDP:AF2","text":"The Love of God most occupying → proposition of the impossibility of hatred toward God, the demonstration of which is suspended in the given segment.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_QGOH_PQDP:P0098","trace_type":"Tension","confidence":"medium","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"},{"id":"RAW_QGOH_PQDP:P0108","trace_type":"Tension","confidence":"medium","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]}],"original_name":"Argument Flow Map"},{"number":"10","name":"Map-Effects","items":[{"id":"CH_QGOH_PQDP:ME1","text":"An affect is reclassified as an orderable ideational and bodily structure, not as an impulse extrinsically opposed to the will.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_QGOH_PQDP:P0019","trace_type":"Structural","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"},{"id":"RAW_QGOH_PQDP:P0027","trace_type":"Structural","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"},{"id":"RAW_QGOH_PQDP:P0068","trace_type":"Structural","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_QGOH_PQDP:ME2","text":"Freedom is reclassified as a graduated diminution of passivity, not as exemption from the necessity of causes.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_QGOH_PQDP:P0045","trace_type":"Structural","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"},{"id":"RAW_QGOH_PQDP:P0047","trace_type":"Structural","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"},{"id":"RAW_QGOH_PQDP:P0053","trace_type":"Structural","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"},{"id":"RAW_QGOH_PQDP:P0068","trace_type":"Structural","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_QGOH_PQDP:ME3","text":"The distinction between passion and virtue is transferred from the object of Desire to its origin in inadequate or adequate ideas.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_QGOH_PQDP:P0040","trace_type":"Direct","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"},{"id":"RAW_QGOH_PQDP:P0041","trace_type":"Direct","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_QGOH_PQDP:ME4","text":"Memory, imagination, and habit become instruments of Reason, because connections of images either foster or moderate future affects.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_QGOH_PQDP:P0072","trace_type":"Structural","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"},{"id":"RAW_QGOH_PQDP:P0073","trace_type":"Structural","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"},{"id":"RAW_QGOH_PQDP:P0078","trace_type":"Structural","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"},{"id":"RAW_QGOH_PQDP:P0082","trace_type":"Structural","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"},{"id":"RAW_QGOH_PQDP:P0086","trace_type":"Structural","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_QGOH_PQDP:ME5","text":"The Love of God is described as an effect of universal knowledge and the nexus of affects, not as commerce with an affectively responsive God.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_QGOH_PQDP:P0090","trace_type":"Composite","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"},{"id":"RAW_QGOH_PQDP:P0094","trace_type":"Composite","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"},{"id":"RAW_QGOH_PQDP:P0100","trace_type":"Composite","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"},{"id":"RAW_QGOH_PQDP:P0106","trace_type":"Composite","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]}],"original_name":"Map-Effects"},{"number":"11","name":"Altitude Levels","items":[{"id":"CH_QGOH_PQDP:ALT1","text":"Surface — It is asked how affects can be restrained, moderated, and directed toward the actions of life.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_QGOH_PQDP:P0006","trace_type":"Structural","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"},{"id":"RAW_QGOH_PQDP:P0068","trace_type":"Structural","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"},{"id":"RAW_QGOH_PQDP:P0072","trace_type":"Structural","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_QGOH_PQDP:ALT2","text":"Structural — The mechanism operates through the correspondence of the order of ideas and affections of the Body, through the clarity of ideas, and through the connection of images in memory.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_QGOH_PQDP:P0019","trace_type":"Structural","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"},{"id":"RAW_QGOH_PQDP:P0027","trace_type":"Structural","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"},{"id":"RAW_QGOH_PQDP:P0068","trace_type":"Structural","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"},{"id":"RAW_QGOH_PQDP:P0072","trace_type":"Structural","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"},{"id":"RAW_QGOH_PQDP:P0082","trace_type":"Structural","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_QGOH_PQDP:ALT3","text":"Metastrategic — The contest against affects is transferred from negative repulsion to imaginative preoccupation through doctrines of life, virtues, Joy, and knowledge of the true useful.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_QGOH_PQDP:P0072","trace_type":"Structural","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"},{"id":"RAW_QGOH_PQDP:P0073","trace_type":"Structural","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"},{"id":"RAW_QGOH_PQDP:P0074","trace_type":"Structural","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"},{"id":"RAW_QGOH_PQDP:P0076","trace_type":"Structural","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_QGOH_PQDP:ALT4","text":"Ontological/Temporal — The necessity of things, the infinite connection of causes, the constancy of common properties, and the impassibility of God determine the conditions under which graduated freedom is understood.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_QGOH_PQDP:P0047","trace_type":"Structural","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"},{"id":"RAW_QGOH_PQDP:P0049","trace_type":"Structural","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"},{"id":"RAW_QGOH_PQDP:P0055","trace_type":"Structural","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"},{"id":"RAW_QGOH_PQDP:P0056","trace_type":"Structural","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"},{"id":"RAW_QGOH_PQDP:P0102","trace_type":"Structural","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]}],"original_name":"Altitude Levels"},{"number":"12","name":"Minimal Reconstruction Test","items":[{"id":"CH_QGOH_PQDP:MRT1","text":"The axioms suffice to reconstruct the core of the argument: the power of the Mind is understanding, a passive affect is confused knowledge, and freedom is the diminution of passivity through rational ordering.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_QGOH_PQDP:P0013","trace_type":"Structural","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"},{"id":"RAW_QGOH_PQDP:P0019","trace_type":"Structural","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"},{"id":"RAW_QGOH_PQDP:P0027","trace_type":"Structural","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"},{"id":"RAW_QGOH_PQDP:P0068","trace_type":"Structural","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_QGOH_PQDP:MRT2","text":"The strongest joint is the nexus between a clear idea, understanding of necessity, and the diminished force of affects, because this nexus simultaneously sustains doctrine, remedy, and practice.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_QGOH_PQDP:P0027","trace_type":"Structural","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"},{"id":"RAW_QGOH_PQDP:P0031","trace_type":"Structural","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"},{"id":"RAW_QGOH_PQDP:P0047","trace_type":"Structural","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"},{"id":"RAW_QGOH_PQDP:P0049","trace_type":"Structural","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_QGOH_PQDP:MRT3","text":"A fragile joint remains between the proposition that every affection involves some clear conception and the conclusion that the affect itself can be clearly and distinctly understood; the quantity of knowledge required is not fully determined.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_QGOH_PQDP:P0033","trace_type":"Tension","confidence":"medium","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"},{"id":"RAW_QGOH_PQDP:P0037","trace_type":"Tension","confidence":"medium","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"},{"id":"RAW_QGOH_PQDP:P0039","trace_type":"Tension","confidence":"medium","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_QGOH_PQDP:MRT4","text":"The internal tension between the increase of an affect through several causes and the diminished harm of an affect referred to several causes must be retained; the text distinguishes it through causal concurrence, objective distribution, and imaginative frequency, but does not formally explain the distinction universally.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_QGOH_PQDP:P0058","trace_type":"Tension","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"},{"id":"RAW_QGOH_PQDP:P0064","trace_type":"Tension","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"},{"id":"RAW_QGOH_PQDP:P0078","trace_type":"Tension","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"},{"id":"RAW_QGOH_PQDP:P0080","trace_type":"Tension","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_QGOH_PQDP:MRT5","text":"The dominion of the Mind is not absolute: the exercise of ordering is effective so long as affects contrary in nature do not impede the Mind, and knowledge of affects is often obtained only in part.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_QGOH_PQDP:P0006","trace_type":"Tension","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"},{"id":"RAW_QGOH_PQDP:P0039","trace_type":"Tension","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"},{"id":"RAW_QGOH_PQDP:P0068","trace_type":"Tension","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_QGOH_PQDP:MRT6","text":"The transition to the Love of God rests on the imported principle that all affections can be referred to the idea of God; within the given segment this relation is asserted and demonstrated by reference back to Part I, not developed from a new foundation.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_QGOH_PQDP:P0090","trace_type":"Direct","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"},{"id":"RAW_QGOH_PQDP:P0092","trace_type":"Direct","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_QGOH_PQDP:MRT7","text":"The final proposition concerning the impossibility of hatred toward God closes the movement of the argument, but its demonstrative reconstruction cannot be completed, because the demonstration of Prop. XVIII is interrupted in the given source.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[]}],"original_name":"Minimal Reconstruction Test"}],"revision":"CH_QGOH_PQDP@R001","source_variant_id":"canonical:CH_QGOH_PQDP:v1","source_language_code":"la"}],"identity":{"layer":"CH","kicker":"Chapter 19","title":"Part Five - On the Power of the Intellect, or Human Freedom · passage 1","context":"Chapter 19","original_title":"Pars quinta - De potentia intellectus seu de libertate humana · passage 1","original_context":"Chapter 19"},"original_title":"Pars quinta - De potentia intellectus seu de libertate humana · passage 1","title_language_code":"en"},{"id":"CH_QGOH_PQ_6","layer":"CH","title":"Part Five - On the Power of the Intellect, or Human Freedom · passage 2","order":20,"parents":["RAW_QGOH_PQ_6"],"revision":"CH_QGOH_PQ_6@R001","sha256":"09e4eb685f437b36e631b037bc0557a9b81cb9a4d114f4680428ca840dff446c","sections":[{"number":"1","name":"Argumental Spine","items":[{"id":"CH_QGOH_PQ_6:AS1","text":"Textus potentiam Mentis adversus affectus et libertatem quaerit, distinguens id quod tempori et Corpori adstrictum est ab eo quod Mentis essentiae aeternum manet.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_QGOH_PQ_6:P0022","trace_type":"Structural","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"},{"id":"RAW_QGOH_PQ_6:P0026","trace_type":"Structural","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"},{"id":"RAW_QGOH_PQ_6:P0030","trace_type":"Structural","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"},{"id":"RAW_QGOH_PQ_6:P0032","trace_type":"Structural","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"},{"id":"RAW_QGOH_PQ_6:P0034","trace_type":"Structural","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_QGOH_PQ_6:AS2","text":"Argumentatio demonstrativa et reconstructiva affectuum remedia ex gradu cognitionis derivat, non ex voluntaria affectuum repressione.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_QGOH_PQ_6:P0016","trace_type":"Structural","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"},{"id":"RAW_QGOH_PQ_6:P0018","trace_type":"Structural","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"},{"id":"RAW_QGOH_PQ_6:P0019","trace_type":"Structural","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_QGOH_PQ_6:AS3","text":"Idea Dei adaequata primum amorem divinum ab odio, tristitia, invidia et zelotypia segregat.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_QGOH_PQ_6:P0001","trace_type":"Composite","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"},{"id":"RAW_QGOH_PQ_6:P0003","trace_type":"Composite","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"},{"id":"RAW_QGOH_PQ_6:P0011","trace_type":"Composite","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"},{"id":"RAW_QGOH_PQ_6:P0013","trace_type":"Composite","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"},{"id":"RAW_QGOH_PQ_6:P0077","trace_type":"Composite","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_QGOH_PQ_6:AS4","text":"Potentia Mentis dein ad proportionem idearum adaequatarum et inadaequatarum reducitur atque per ordinationem affectuum explicatur.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_QGOH_PQ_6:P0016","trace_type":"Structural","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"},{"id":"RAW_QGOH_PQ_6:P0018","trace_type":"Structural","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"},{"id":"RAW_QGOH_PQ_6:P0019","trace_type":"Structural","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_QGOH_PQ_6:AS5","text":"Imaginatio, memoria et passiones ad durationem Corporis referuntur; essentia Corporis in Deo concepta ad aeternitatem Mentis refertur.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_QGOH_PQ_6:P0022","trace_type":"Composite","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"},{"id":"RAW_QGOH_PQ_6:P0024","trace_type":"Composite","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"},{"id":"RAW_QGOH_PQ_6:P0026","trace_type":"Composite","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"},{"id":"RAW_QGOH_PQ_6:P0032","trace_type":"Composite","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"},{"id":"RAW_QGOH_PQ_6:P0085","trace_type":"Composite","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_QGOH_PQ_6:AS6","text":"Cognitio tertii generis ex aeternitate Mentis procedens summam laetitiam et Amorem Dei intellectualem producit.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_QGOH_PQ_6:P0043","trace_type":"Composite","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"},{"id":"RAW_QGOH_PQ_6:P0051","trace_type":"Composite","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"},{"id":"RAW_QGOH_PQ_6:P0073","trace_type":"Composite","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"},{"id":"RAW_QGOH_PQ_6:P0075","trace_type":"Composite","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"},{"id":"RAW_QGOH_PQ_6:P0077","trace_type":"Composite","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_QGOH_PQ_6:AS7","text":"Amor intellectualis ut pars amoris infiniti Dei libertatem, beatitudinem, imminutam mortis metum et maiorem Mentis partem aeternam constituit.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_QGOH_PQ_6:P0077","trace_type":"Structural","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"},{"id":"RAW_QGOH_PQ_6:P0079","trace_type":"Structural","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"},{"id":"RAW_QGOH_PQ_6:P0083","trace_type":"Structural","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"},{"id":"RAW_QGOH_PQ_6:P0097","trace_type":"Structural","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"},{"id":"RAW_QGOH_PQ_6:P0103","trace_type":"Structural","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"},{"id":"RAW_QGOH_PQ_6:P0112","trace_type":"Structural","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"},{"id":"RAW_QGOH_PQ_6:P0116","trace_type":"Structural","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"},{"id":"RAW_QGOH_PQ_6:P0118","trace_type":"Structural","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"},{"id":"RAW_QGOH_PQ_6:P0120","trace_type":"Structural","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]}],"original_name":"Argumental Spine"},{"number":"2","name":"Load-Bearing Axioms","items":[{"id":"CH_QGOH_PQ_6:A1","text":"Contemplatio Dei ex idea in nobis adaequata est actio Mentis cui tristitia non concomitatur.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_QGOH_PQ_6:P0001","trace_type":"Direct","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"},{"id":"RAW_QGOH_PQ_6:P0005","trace_type":"Direct","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_QGOH_PQ_6:A2","text":"Deus, qui a finito amatur, ex humana cupiditate in naturam mutabilem vel non-divinam transire non potest.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_QGOH_PQ_6:P0007","trace_type":"Direct","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"},{"id":"RAW_QGOH_PQ_6:P0009","trace_type":"Direct","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_QGOH_PQ_6:A3","text":"Amor rationalis erga Deum est summum bonum omnibus commune et per imaginationem gaudii communis augetur.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_QGOH_PQ_6:P0011","trace_type":"Direct","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"},{"id":"RAW_QGOH_PQ_6:P0013","trace_type":"Direct","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_QGOH_PQ_6:A4","text":"Vis affectus ex comparatione causae externae cum nostra, potentia Mentis ex sola cognitione, passio autem ex cognitionis privatione definitur.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_QGOH_PQ_6:P0017","trace_type":"Direct","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"},{"id":"RAW_QGOH_PQ_6:P0018","trace_type":"Direct","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_QGOH_PQ_6:A5","text":"Mens cognitione affectus a confusa cogitatione causae externae separat eosque secundum ordinem intellectus ordinare et concatenare potest.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_QGOH_PQ_6:P0016","trace_type":"Direct","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_QGOH_PQ_6:A6","text":"Affectus ad res intellectas, proprietates communes vel Deum relati, pluribus causis fulti, affectus ex cognitione confusa ortos superant.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_QGOH_PQ_6:P0016","trace_type":"Direct","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"},{"id":"RAW_QGOH_PQ_6:P0019","trace_type":"Direct","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_QGOH_PQ_6:A7","text":"Imaginatio, memoria, conceptio rerum sub duratione et passiones ad actualem Corporis existentiam pertinent.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_QGOH_PQ_6:P0022","trace_type":"Composite","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"},{"id":"RAW_QGOH_PQ_6:P0024","trace_type":"Composite","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"},{"id":"RAW_QGOH_PQ_6:P0085","trace_type":"Composite","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"},{"id":"RAW_QGOH_PQ_6:P0087","trace_type":"Composite","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_QGOH_PQ_6:A8","text":"In Deo necessario datur idea essentiae cuiusque Corporis humani, quae ad essentiam Mentis humanae pertinet et aeterna est.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_QGOH_PQ_6:P0026","trace_type":"Composite","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"},{"id":"RAW_QGOH_PQ_6:P0028","trace_type":"Composite","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"},{"id":"RAW_QGOH_PQ_6:P0030","trace_type":"Composite","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"},{"id":"RAW_QGOH_PQ_6:P0032","trace_type":"Composite","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_QGOH_PQ_6:A9","text":"Tertium cognitionis genus procedit ab idea adaequata attributorum Dei ad cognitionem adaequatam essentiarum rerum singularium.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_QGOH_PQ_6:P0043","trace_type":"Direct","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"},{"id":"RAW_QGOH_PQ_6:P0069","trace_type":"Direct","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_QGOH_PQ_6:A10","text":"Cupiditas res tertio cognitionis genere cognoscendi ex primo cognitionis genere oriri non potest, sed ex secundo oritur.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_QGOH_PQ_6:P0053","trace_type":"Direct","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"},{"id":"RAW_QGOH_PQ_6:P0055","trace_type":"Direct","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_QGOH_PQ_6:A11","text":"Mens, quatenus aeternam Corporis essentiam concipit, Dei cognitionem habet et causa formalis cognitionis tertii generis est.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_QGOH_PQ_6:P0063","trace_type":"Composite","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"},{"id":"RAW_QGOH_PQ_6:P0067","trace_type":"Composite","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"},{"id":"RAW_QGOH_PQ_6:P0069","trace_type":"Composite","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_QGOH_PQ_6:A12","text":"Ex cognitione tertii generis oritur summa Mentis laetitia cum idea Dei tamquam causae.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_QGOH_PQ_6:P0073","trace_type":"Direct","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"},{"id":"RAW_QGOH_PQ_6:P0075","trace_type":"Direct","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_QGOH_PQ_6:A13","text":"Amor Dei intellectualis, qui ex tertio cognitionis genere oritur, est aeternus.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_QGOH_PQ_6:P0077","trace_type":"Direct","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"},{"id":"RAW_QGOH_PQ_6:P0079","trace_type":"Direct","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"},{"id":"RAW_QGOH_PQ_6:P0081","trace_type":"Direct","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_QGOH_PQ_6:A14","text":"Amor intellectualis Mentis erga Deum pars est amoris infiniti quo Deus se ipsum amat.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_QGOH_PQ_6:P0093","trace_type":"Composite","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"},{"id":"RAW_QGOH_PQ_6:P0097","trace_type":"Composite","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"},{"id":"RAW_QGOH_PQ_6:P0099","trace_type":"Composite","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_QGOH_PQ_6:A15","text":"Nihil in Natura datur quod Amori Dei intellectuali contrarium sit aut eum tollere possit.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_QGOH_PQ_6:P0106","trace_type":"Direct","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"},{"id":"RAW_QGOH_PQ_6:P0108","trace_type":"Direct","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_QGOH_PQ_6:A16","text":"Quo Mens plura secundo et tertio cognitionis genere intelligit, eo minus ab affectibus malis patitur et eo minus mortem timet.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_QGOH_PQ_6:P0112","trace_type":"Composite","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"},{"id":"RAW_QGOH_PQ_6:P0114","trace_type":"Composite","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"},{"id":"RAW_QGOH_PQ_6:P0116","trace_type":"Composite","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_QGOH_PQ_6:A17","text":"Corpus plurimis actionibus aptum Mentem disponit ad affectiones secundum intellectum ordinandas et ad maiorem Mentis partem aeternam constituendam.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_QGOH_PQ_6:P0118","trace_type":"Direct","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"},{"id":"RAW_QGOH_PQ_6:P0120","trace_type":"Direct","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]}],"original_name":"Load-Bearing Axioms"},{"number":"3","name":"Apophatic Structure","items":[{"id":"CH_QGOH_PQ_6:NX1","text":"Tristitia cum idea Dei consistere non potest; ideo Deus odio haberi nequit et Amor erga Deum in odium verti nequit.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_QGOH_PQ_6:P0001","trace_type":"Negative","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"},{"id":"RAW_QGOH_PQ_6:P0003","trace_type":"Negative","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_QGOH_PQ_6:NX2","text":"Amor Dei non includit conatum ut Deus amantem contra amet, quia talis cupiditas Deum non-Deum esse vellet.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_QGOH_PQ_6:P0007","trace_type":"Negative","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"},{"id":"RAW_QGOH_PQ_6:P0009","trace_type":"Negative","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_QGOH_PQ_6:NX3","text":"Amor Dei rationalis nec invidia nec zelotypia inquinatur, quia summum bonum commune non per participationem minuitur.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_QGOH_PQ_6:P0011","trace_type":"Negative","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"},{"id":"RAW_QGOH_PQ_6:P0013","trace_type":"Negative","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_QGOH_PQ_6:NX4","text":"Cognitio clara et distincta passiones non necessario omnes absolute tollit; eas tamen minimam Mentis partem efficit.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_QGOH_PQ_6:P0018","trace_type":"Negative","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"},{"id":"RAW_QGOH_PQ_6:P0019","trace_type":"Negative","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_QGOH_PQ_6:NX5","text":"Mens sine Corpore nihil imaginari, rerum praeteritarum recordari aut passionibus obnoxia esse potest.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_QGOH_PQ_6:P0022","trace_type":"Negative","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"},{"id":"RAW_QGOH_PQ_6:P0024","trace_type":"Negative","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"},{"id":"RAW_QGOH_PQ_6:P0085","trace_type":"Negative","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_QGOH_PQ_6:NX6","text":"Aeternitas per durationem explicari neque ad tempus referri potest.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_QGOH_PQ_6:P0034","trace_type":"Negative","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"},{"id":"RAW_QGOH_PQ_6:P0059","trace_type":"Negative","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"},{"id":"RAW_QGOH_PQ_6:P0065","trace_type":"Negative","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_QGOH_PQ_6:NX7","text":"Cognitio mutilata et confusa primi generis cupiditatem cognitionis tertii generis gignere non potest.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_QGOH_PQ_6:P0053","trace_type":"Negative","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"},{"id":"RAW_QGOH_PQ_6:P0055","trace_type":"Negative","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_QGOH_PQ_6:NX8","text":"Nullus Amor praeter Amorem intellectualem aeternus est.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_QGOH_PQ_6:P0079","trace_type":"Negative","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"},{"id":"RAW_QGOH_PQ_6:P0089","trace_type":"Negative","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_QGOH_PQ_6:NX9","text":"Nullum verum contrarium Amori intellectuali dari potest, quia eius destructio veritatem in falsitatem converteret.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_QGOH_PQ_6:P0106","trace_type":"Negative","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"},{"id":"RAW_QGOH_PQ_6:P0108","trace_type":"Negative","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]}],"original_name":"Apophatic Structure"},{"number":"4","name":"Derivative Propositions","items":[{"id":"CH_QGOH_PQ_6:D1","text":"Idea Dei adaequata Deum ab obiecto tristitiae et odii in obiectum actionis et amoris convertit.","refs":["CH_QGOH_PQ_6:A1"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"From","passages":[{"id":"RAW_QGOH_PQ_6:P0001","trace_type":"Composite","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"},{"id":"RAW_QGOH_PQ_6:P0005","trace_type":"Composite","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_QGOH_PQ_6:D2","text":"Qui Deum amat, non potest desiderare ut Deus ipsum reciproce amet, quia amor iste mutationem divinae naturae postularet.","refs":["CH_QGOH_PQ_6:A2"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"From","passages":[{"id":"RAW_QGOH_PQ_6:P0007","trace_type":"Direct","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"},{"id":"RAW_QGOH_PQ_6:P0009","trace_type":"Direct","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_QGOH_PQ_6:D3","text":"Communicatio Amoris Dei non est imminutio boni sed causa incrementi amoris; plures homines eodem bono fruentes amorem unius augent.","refs":["CH_QGOH_PQ_6:A3"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"From","passages":[{"id":"RAW_QGOH_PQ_6:P0011","trace_type":"Direct","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"},{"id":"RAW_QGOH_PQ_6:P0013","trace_type":"Direct","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_QGOH_PQ_6:D4","text":"Magnitudo affectus non per se indicat potentiam Mentis, quia affectuum conflictus a viribus externis, Mentis actio autem ab ideis adaequatis aestimatur.","refs":["CH_QGOH_PQ_6:A4"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"From","passages":[{"id":"RAW_QGOH_PQ_6:P0017","trace_type":"Composite","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"},{"id":"RAW_QGOH_PQ_6:P0018","trace_type":"Composite","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_QGOH_PQ_6:D5","text":"Animi aegritudines ex nimio amore rerum mutabilium et incompossibilium oriuntur; cognitio causalis affectus reordinat, et Amor in rem immutabilem atque vere possidendam transfert.","refs":["CH_QGOH_PQ_6:A3","CH_QGOH_PQ_6:A5","CH_QGOH_PQ_6:A6"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"From","passages":[{"id":"RAW_QGOH_PQ_6:P0018","trace_type":"Composite","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"},{"id":"RAW_QGOH_PQ_6:P0019","trace_type":"Composite","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_QGOH_PQ_6:D6","text":"Cum Corpore desinunt imaginatio, memoria et passiones; manet tamen id Mentis quod per aeternam ideam essentiae Corporis in Deo concipitur.","refs":["CH_QGOH_PQ_6:A7","CH_QGOH_PQ_6:A8"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"From","passages":[{"id":"RAW_QGOH_PQ_6:P0022","trace_type":"Composite","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"},{"id":"RAW_QGOH_PQ_6:P0026","trace_type":"Composite","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"},{"id":"RAW_QGOH_PQ_6:P0032","trace_type":"Composite","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"},{"id":"RAW_QGOH_PQ_6:P0085","trace_type":"Composite","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_QGOH_PQ_6:D7","text":"Actualitas bifariam concipitur: vel sub certo tempore et loco, vel in Deo ex necessitate divinae naturae; sola secunda conceptio res sub specie aeternitatis exhibet.","refs":["CH_QGOH_PQ_6:A7","CH_QGOH_PQ_6:A8","CH_QGOH_PQ_6:A11"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"From","passages":[{"id":"RAW_QGOH_PQ_6:P0057","trace_type":"Direct","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"},{"id":"RAW_QGOH_PQ_6:P0059","trace_type":"Direct","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"},{"id":"RAW_QGOH_PQ_6:P0061","trace_type":"Direct","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_QGOH_PQ_6:D8","text":"Quo Mens aptior est cognitioni tertii generis, eo magis res eodem cognitionis genere cognoscere cupit; progressio eius ab adequate cognito procedit, non ex confusione.","refs":["CH_QGOH_PQ_6:A9","CH_QGOH_PQ_6:A10"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"From","passages":[{"id":"RAW_QGOH_PQ_6:P0045","trace_type":"Composite","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"},{"id":"RAW_QGOH_PQ_6:P0053","trace_type":"Composite","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"},{"id":"RAW_QGOH_PQ_6:P0055","trace_type":"Composite","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_QGOH_PQ_6:D9","text":"Cognitio rerum singularium tertio genere Mentem efficacius afficit quam cognitio universalis secundi generis, quia ex ipsa singulari essentia dependentia a Deo concipitur.","refs":["CH_QGOH_PQ_6:A9","CH_QGOH_PQ_6:A11"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"From","passages":[{"id":"RAW_QGOH_PQ_6:P0103","trace_type":"Direct","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"},{"id":"RAW_QGOH_PQ_6:P0104","trace_type":"Direct","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_QGOH_PQ_6:D10","text":"Cognitio tertii generis summam laetitiam producit, quae cum idea Dei causae Amorem Dei intellectualem constituit atque aeternitatem eius sequitur.","refs":["CH_QGOH_PQ_6:A11","CH_QGOH_PQ_6:A12","CH_QGOH_PQ_6:A13"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"From","passages":[{"id":"RAW_QGOH_PQ_6:P0069","trace_type":"Structural","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"},{"id":"RAW_QGOH_PQ_6:P0073","trace_type":"Structural","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"},{"id":"RAW_QGOH_PQ_6:P0075","trace_type":"Structural","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"},{"id":"RAW_QGOH_PQ_6:P0077","trace_type":"Structural","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"},{"id":"RAW_QGOH_PQ_6:P0079","trace_type":"Structural","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_QGOH_PQ_6:D11","text":"Amor intellectualis Mentis erga Deum et Amor Dei erga homines idem Amor sunt, quatenus infinitus amor divinus per essentiam Mentis humanae explicatur.","refs":["CH_QGOH_PQ_6:A14"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"From","passages":[{"id":"RAW_QGOH_PQ_6:P0097","trace_type":"Direct","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"},{"id":"RAW_QGOH_PQ_6:P0099","trace_type":"Direct","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"},{"id":"RAW_QGOH_PQ_6:P0101","trace_type":"Direct","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_QGOH_PQ_6:D12","text":"Amor intellectualis, quia ex veritate aeterna necessario sequitur, non ab affectu contrario destrui potest; amoribus imaginativis et corporalibus sola temporalitas competit.","refs":["CH_QGOH_PQ_6:A7","CH_QGOH_PQ_6:A13","CH_QGOH_PQ_6:A15"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"From","passages":[{"id":"RAW_QGOH_PQ_6:P0085","trace_type":"Composite","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"},{"id":"RAW_QGOH_PQ_6:P0089","trace_type":"Composite","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"},{"id":"RAW_QGOH_PQ_6:P0106","trace_type":"Composite","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_QGOH_PQ_6:D13","text":"Incrementum cognitionis adaequatae et aptitudo Corporis ad plura agendum minuunt passiones malas, mortis metum et proportionem Mentis pereuntem.","refs":["CH_QGOH_PQ_6:A16","CH_QGOH_PQ_6:A17"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"From","passages":[{"id":"RAW_QGOH_PQ_6:P0112","trace_type":"Structural","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"},{"id":"RAW_QGOH_PQ_6:P0114","trace_type":"Structural","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"},{"id":"RAW_QGOH_PQ_6:P0118","trace_type":"Structural","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"},{"id":"RAW_QGOH_PQ_6:P0120","trace_type":"Structural","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]}],"original_name":"Derivative Propositions"},{"number":"5","name":"Super-Derivatives","items":[{"id":"CH_QGOH_PQ_6:SD1","text":"Libertas non est exemptio a causalitate neque vacuitas affectuum, sed stabilis praevalentia actionum cognitivae originis super passiones ex ideis inadaequatis ortas.","refs":["CH_QGOH_PQ_6:A4","CH_QGOH_PQ_6:A5","CH_QGOH_PQ_6:A6","CH_QGOH_PQ_6:A16"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"From","passages":[{"id":"RAW_QGOH_PQ_6:P0016","trace_type":"Structural","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"},{"id":"RAW_QGOH_PQ_6:P0018","trace_type":"Structural","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"},{"id":"RAW_QGOH_PQ_6:P0112","trace_type":"Structural","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_QGOH_PQ_6:SD2","text":"Aeternitas Mentis non est proprietas indivisa totius personae empiricae, sed proportio eius quod Mentem ad essentiam aeternam referentem constituit.","refs":["CH_QGOH_PQ_6:A7","CH_QGOH_PQ_6:A8","CH_QGOH_PQ_6:A16","CH_QGOH_PQ_6:A17"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"From","passages":[{"id":"RAW_QGOH_PQ_6:P0022","trace_type":"Structural","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"},{"id":"RAW_QGOH_PQ_6:P0032","trace_type":"Structural","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"},{"id":"RAW_QGOH_PQ_6:P0034","trace_type":"Structural","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"},{"id":"RAW_QGOH_PQ_6:P0114","trace_type":"Structural","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"},{"id":"RAW_QGOH_PQ_6:P0118","trace_type":"Structural","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_QGOH_PQ_6:SD3","text":"Summum bonum rationaliter intellectum structuram non-rivalem habet: communicatio eius non divisionem inducit, sed causam sui incrementi.","refs":["CH_QGOH_PQ_6:A3"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"From","passages":[{"id":"RAW_QGOH_PQ_6:P0011","trace_type":"Direct","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"},{"id":"RAW_QGOH_PQ_6:P0013","trace_type":"Direct","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_QGOH_PQ_6:SD4","text":"Cognitio intuitiva rerum singularium non est applicatio secundaria universalis cognitionis, sed locus in quo dependentia divina affective efficax fit.","refs":["CH_QGOH_PQ_6:A9","CH_QGOH_PQ_6:A11","CH_QGOH_PQ_6:D9"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"From","passages":[{"id":"RAW_QGOH_PQ_6:P0069","trace_type":"Composite","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"},{"id":"RAW_QGOH_PQ_6:P0103","trace_type":"Composite","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"},{"id":"RAW_QGOH_PQ_6:P0104","trace_type":"Composite","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_QGOH_PQ_6:SD5","text":"Corpus non est simplex impedimentum aeternitatis, sed condicio strategica qua Mens ad ordinationem affectionum et ad maiorem partem aeternam disponitur.","refs":["CH_QGOH_PQ_6:A7","CH_QGOH_PQ_6:A17"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"From","passages":[{"id":"RAW_QGOH_PQ_6:P0085","trace_type":"Structural","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"},{"id":"RAW_QGOH_PQ_6:P0118","trace_type":"Structural","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"},{"id":"RAW_QGOH_PQ_6:P0120","trace_type":"Structural","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]}],"original_name":"Super-Derivatives"},{"number":"6","name":"Deep Apophatics","items":[{"id":"CH_QGOH_PQ_6:DNX1","text":"Falsum esse debet libertatem consistere in imperio voluntatis quod omnes affectus omnino exstinguat; systema libertatem in mutatione proportionis inter actionem et passionem ponit.","refs":["CH_QGOH_PQ_6:A4","CH_QGOH_PQ_6:A5","CH_QGOH_PQ_6:D5"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"From","passages":[{"id":"RAW_QGOH_PQ_6:P0016","trace_type":"Negative","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"},{"id":"RAW_QGOH_PQ_6:P0018","trace_type":"Negative","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"},{"id":"RAW_QGOH_PQ_6:P0019","trace_type":"Negative","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_QGOH_PQ_6:DNX2","text":"Falsum esse debet aeternitatem esse continuationem memoriae, imaginationis aut narrationis personalis post mortem; haec omnia ad durationem Corporis pertinent.","refs":["CH_QGOH_PQ_6:A7","CH_QGOH_PQ_6:A8","CH_QGOH_PQ_6:D6"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"From","passages":[{"id":"RAW_QGOH_PQ_6:P0022","trace_type":"Negative","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"},{"id":"RAW_QGOH_PQ_6:P0034","trace_type":"Negative","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"},{"id":"RAW_QGOH_PQ_6:P0089","trace_type":"Negative","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"},{"id":"RAW_QGOH_PQ_6:P0091","trace_type":"Negative","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_QGOH_PQ_6:DNX3","text":"Falsum esse debet Deum esse alterum externum, cuius amor reciproce petitur aut cui tristitia passive imputatur; Deus intelligitur causa immanens, non obiectum mutuae negotiationis affectivae.","refs":["CH_QGOH_PQ_6:A1","CH_QGOH_PQ_6:A2","CH_QGOH_PQ_6:A14"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"From","passages":[{"id":"RAW_QGOH_PQ_6:P0005","trace_type":"Negative","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"},{"id":"RAW_QGOH_PQ_6:P0007","trace_type":"Negative","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"},{"id":"RAW_QGOH_PQ_6:P0097","trace_type":"Negative","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"},{"id":"RAW_QGOH_PQ_6:P0101","trace_type":"Negative","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_QGOH_PQ_6:DNX4","text":"Falsum esse debet omnes cognitionis gradus aequaliter ad cognitionem intuitivam ducere; primum genus est structuraliter sterile respectu cupiditatis tertii generis.","refs":["CH_QGOH_PQ_6:A9","CH_QGOH_PQ_6:A10"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"From","passages":[{"id":"RAW_QGOH_PQ_6:P0043","trace_type":"Negative","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"},{"id":"RAW_QGOH_PQ_6:P0053","trace_type":"Negative","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"},{"id":"RAW_QGOH_PQ_6:P0055","trace_type":"Negative","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_QGOH_PQ_6:DNX5","text":"Falsum esse debet beatitudinem esse bonum scarce distributum, cuius participatio alterius felicitatem minuere possit.","refs":["CH_QGOH_PQ_6:A3","CH_QGOH_PQ_6:D3"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"From","passages":[{"id":"RAW_QGOH_PQ_6:P0011","trace_type":"Negative","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"},{"id":"RAW_QGOH_PQ_6:P0013","trace_type":"Negative","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]}],"original_name":"Deep Apophatics"},{"number":"7","name":"Super-Apophatics","items":[{"id":"CH_QGOH_PQ_6:SNX1","text":"Praecluditur subiectum finitum extra Deum subsistens, quod Deum tamquam obiectum extrinsecum amet; Amor intellectualis est determinatio amoris divini ipsius.","refs":["CH_QGOH_PQ_6:A8","CH_QGOH_PQ_6:A14","CH_QGOH_PQ_6:D11"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"From","passages":[{"id":"RAW_QGOH_PQ_6:P0026","trace_type":"Negative","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"},{"id":"RAW_QGOH_PQ_6:P0097","trace_type":"Negative","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"},{"id":"RAW_QGOH_PQ_6:P0101","trace_type":"Negative","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_QGOH_PQ_6:SNX2","text":"Praecluditur mensura temporalis qua aeternitas, salus aut beatitudo aestimari possint; principium, duratio et recordatio ad ordinem aeternum non transferuntur.","refs":["CH_QGOH_PQ_6:A7","CH_QGOH_PQ_6:A8","CH_QGOH_PQ_6:A13"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"From","passages":[{"id":"RAW_QGOH_PQ_6:P0034","trace_type":"Negative","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"},{"id":"RAW_QGOH_PQ_6:P0059","trace_type":"Negative","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"},{"id":"RAW_QGOH_PQ_6:P0065","trace_type":"Negative","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"},{"id":"RAW_QGOH_PQ_6:P0079","trace_type":"Negative","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_QGOH_PQ_6:SNX3","text":"Praecluditur possibilitas ut veritas aeterna actionis intellectualis per vim oppositam naturalem annihiletur; destructio amoris intellectualis falsitatem veri postularet.","refs":["CH_QGOH_PQ_6:A13","CH_QGOH_PQ_6:A15"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"From","passages":[{"id":"RAW_QGOH_PQ_6:P0079","trace_type":"Negative","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"},{"id":"RAW_QGOH_PQ_6:P0106","trace_type":"Negative","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"},{"id":"RAW_QGOH_PQ_6:P0108","trace_type":"Negative","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]}],"original_name":"Super-Apophatics"},{"number":"8","name":"Formal Generative Schema","items":[{"id":"CH_QGOH_PQ_6:FGS1","text":"Idea inadaequata + imaginatio Corporis sub duratione + amor rei mutabilis → passio, anxietas, conflictus et vulnerabilitas affectiva.","refs":["CH_QGOH_PQ_6:A4","CH_QGOH_PQ_6:A7"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"From","passages":[{"id":"RAW_QGOH_PQ_6:P0018","trace_type":"Composite","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"},{"id":"RAW_QGOH_PQ_6:P0022","trace_type":"Composite","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"},{"id":"RAW_QGOH_PQ_6:P0087","trace_type":"Composite","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_QGOH_PQ_6:FGS2","text":"Cognitio causae + separatio ab externo confuse imaginato + ordinatio affectionum → imminutio passionis et praevalentia actionis Mentis.","refs":["CH_QGOH_PQ_6:A5","CH_QGOH_PQ_6:A6"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"From","passages":[{"id":"RAW_QGOH_PQ_6:P0016","trace_type":"Direct","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"},{"id":"RAW_QGOH_PQ_6:P0019","trace_type":"Direct","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_QGOH_PQ_6:FGS3","text":"Actualitas Corporis temporalis / essentia Corporis in Deo aeterna → distinctio memoriae pereuntis et partis Mentis aeternae.","refs":["CH_QGOH_PQ_6:A7","CH_QGOH_PQ_6:A8"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"From","passages":[{"id":"RAW_QGOH_PQ_6:P0022","trace_type":"Composite","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"},{"id":"RAW_QGOH_PQ_6:P0026","trace_type":"Composite","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"},{"id":"RAW_QGOH_PQ_6:P0032","trace_type":"Composite","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_QGOH_PQ_6:FGS4","text":"Mens quatenus aeterna → cognitio Dei → cognitio tertii generis → summa laetitia → Amor Dei intellectualis aeternus.","refs":["CH_QGOH_PQ_6:A9","CH_QGOH_PQ_6:A11","CH_QGOH_PQ_6:A12","CH_QGOH_PQ_6:A13"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"From","passages":[{"id":"RAW_QGOH_PQ_6:P0063","trace_type":"Structural","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"},{"id":"RAW_QGOH_PQ_6:P0069","trace_type":"Structural","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"},{"id":"RAW_QGOH_PQ_6:P0073","trace_type":"Structural","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"},{"id":"RAW_QGOH_PQ_6:P0075","trace_type":"Structural","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"},{"id":"RAW_QGOH_PQ_6:P0077","trace_type":"Structural","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"},{"id":"RAW_QGOH_PQ_6:P0079","trace_type":"Structural","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_QGOH_PQ_6:FGS5","text":"Amor intellectualis ut pars amoris Dei + absentia contrarii → beatitudo stabilis, imminutio mortis metus et libertas affectiva.","refs":["CH_QGOH_PQ_6:A14","CH_QGOH_PQ_6:A15","CH_QGOH_PQ_6:A16"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"From","passages":[{"id":"RAW_QGOH_PQ_6:P0097","trace_type":"Structural","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"},{"id":"RAW_QGOH_PQ_6:P0106","trace_type":"Structural","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"},{"id":"RAW_QGOH_PQ_6:P0112","trace_type":"Structural","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"},{"id":"RAW_QGOH_PQ_6:P0116","trace_type":"Structural","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_QGOH_PQ_6:FGS6","text":"Aptitudo Corporis ad plura agendum → ordinatio plurium affectionum → relatio earum ad ideam Dei → maior pars Mentis aeterna.","refs":["CH_QGOH_PQ_6:A17"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"From","passages":[{"id":"RAW_QGOH_PQ_6:P0118","trace_type":"Direct","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"},{"id":"RAW_QGOH_PQ_6:P0120","trace_type":"Direct","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]}],"original_name":"Formal Generative Schema"},{"number":"9","name":"Argument Flow Map","items":[{"id":"CH_QGOH_PQ_6:AF1","text":"Idea Dei adaequata → actio sine tristitia → impossibilitas odii et conversionis Amoris Dei in odium.","refs":["CH_QGOH_PQ_6:A1"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"From","passages":[{"id":"RAW_QGOH_PQ_6:P0001","trace_type":"Direct","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"},{"id":"RAW_QGOH_PQ_6:P0003","trace_type":"Direct","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_QGOH_PQ_6:AF2","text":"Summum bonum commune → imminutio invidiae et zelotypiae → Amor Dei per participationem aliorum auctus.","refs":["CH_QGOH_PQ_6:A3"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"From","passages":[{"id":"RAW_QGOH_PQ_6:P0011","trace_type":"Direct","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"},{"id":"RAW_QGOH_PQ_6:P0013","trace_type":"Direct","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_QGOH_PQ_6:AF3","text":"Potentia ut cognitio → remedia per cognitionem causalem et ordinationem affectuum → imminutio passionum, non absoluta earum abolitio.","refs":["CH_QGOH_PQ_6:A4","CH_QGOH_PQ_6:A5","CH_QGOH_PQ_6:A6"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"From","passages":[{"id":"RAW_QGOH_PQ_6:P0016","trace_type":"Structural","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"},{"id":"RAW_QGOH_PQ_6:P0018","trace_type":"Structural","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"},{"id":"RAW_QGOH_PQ_6:P0019","trace_type":"Structural","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_QGOH_PQ_6:AF4","text":"Corpus durans → imaginatio et memoria temporales; essentia Corporis in Deo → pars Mentis aeterna.","refs":["CH_QGOH_PQ_6:A7","CH_QGOH_PQ_6:A8"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"From","passages":[{"id":"RAW_QGOH_PQ_6:P0022","trace_type":"Composite","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"},{"id":"RAW_QGOH_PQ_6:P0026","trace_type":"Composite","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"},{"id":"RAW_QGOH_PQ_6:P0032","trace_type":"Composite","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_QGOH_PQ_6:AF5","text":"Mens aeterna → tertium cognitionis genus → summa laetitia → Amor Dei intellectualis aeternus.","refs":["CH_QGOH_PQ_6:A9","CH_QGOH_PQ_6:A10","CH_QGOH_PQ_6:A11","CH_QGOH_PQ_6:A12","CH_QGOH_PQ_6:A13"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"From","passages":[{"id":"RAW_QGOH_PQ_6:P0063","trace_type":"Structural","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"},{"id":"RAW_QGOH_PQ_6:P0069","trace_type":"Structural","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"},{"id":"RAW_QGOH_PQ_6:P0073","trace_type":"Structural","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"},{"id":"RAW_QGOH_PQ_6:P0077","trace_type":"Structural","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"},{"id":"RAW_QGOH_PQ_6:P0079","trace_type":"Structural","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_QGOH_PQ_6:AF6","text":"Amor intellectualis ut amor divinus partialiter explicatus → nulla destructio contraria → beatitudo, libertas et imminutio mortis metus.","refs":["CH_QGOH_PQ_6:A14","CH_QGOH_PQ_6:A15","CH_QGOH_PQ_6:A16"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"From","passages":[{"id":"RAW_QGOH_PQ_6:P0097","trace_type":"Structural","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"},{"id":"RAW_QGOH_PQ_6:P0106","trace_type":"Structural","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"},{"id":"RAW_QGOH_PQ_6:P0112","trace_type":"Structural","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"},{"id":"RAW_QGOH_PQ_6:P0116","trace_type":"Structural","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]}],"original_name":"Argument Flow Map"},{"number":"10","name":"Map-Effects","items":[{"id":"CH_QGOH_PQ_6:ME1","text":"Affectus a scala intensitatis psychologicae ad proportionem inter causas externas, cognitionem adaequatam et cognitionis privationem reclassificantur.","refs":["CH_QGOH_PQ_6:A4"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"From","passages":[{"id":"RAW_QGOH_PQ_6:P0017","trace_type":"Direct","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"},{"id":"RAW_QGOH_PQ_6:P0018","trace_type":"Direct","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_QGOH_PQ_6:ME2","text":"Actualitas dividitur in actualitatem temporalem imaginabilem et actualitatem aeternam in Deo conceptam.","refs":["CH_QGOH_PQ_6:A7","CH_QGOH_PQ_6:A8","CH_QGOH_PQ_6:D7"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"From","passages":[{"id":"RAW_QGOH_PQ_6:P0057","trace_type":"Direct","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"},{"id":"RAW_QGOH_PQ_6:P0059","trace_type":"Direct","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"},{"id":"RAW_QGOH_PQ_6:P0061","trace_type":"Direct","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_QGOH_PQ_6:ME3","text":"Amor Dei ab amore reciproco, possessivo et rivalitario ad laetitiam intellectualem causae divinae refertur.","refs":["CH_QGOH_PQ_6:A2","CH_QGOH_PQ_6:A3","CH_QGOH_PQ_6:A12"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"From","passages":[{"id":"RAW_QGOH_PQ_6:P0007","trace_type":"Composite","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"},{"id":"RAW_QGOH_PQ_6:P0013","trace_type":"Composite","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"},{"id":"RAW_QGOH_PQ_6:P0075","trace_type":"Composite","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"},{"id":"RAW_QGOH_PQ_6:P0077","trace_type":"Composite","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_QGOH_PQ_6:ME4","text":"Genera cognitionis in structuram asymmetricam disponuntur: primum genus tertium non generat, secundum transitum aperit, tertium essentias singulares intuetur.","refs":["CH_QGOH_PQ_6:A9","CH_QGOH_PQ_6:A10"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"From","passages":[{"id":"RAW_QGOH_PQ_6:P0043","trace_type":"Structural","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"},{"id":"RAW_QGOH_PQ_6:P0053","trace_type":"Structural","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"},{"id":"RAW_QGOH_PQ_6:P0055","trace_type":"Structural","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"},{"id":"RAW_QGOH_PQ_6:P0104","trace_type":"Structural","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_QGOH_PQ_6:ME5","text":"Corpus ab altero aeternitatis ad instrumentum dispositionis Mentis reclassificatur.","refs":["CH_QGOH_PQ_6:A7","CH_QGOH_PQ_6:A17"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"From","passages":[{"id":"RAW_QGOH_PQ_6:P0085","trace_type":"Structural","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"},{"id":"RAW_QGOH_PQ_6:P0118","trace_type":"Structural","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"},{"id":"RAW_QGOH_PQ_6:P0120","trace_type":"Structural","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]}],"original_name":"Map-Effects"},{"number":"11","name":"Altitude Levels","items":[{"id":"CH_QGOH_PQ_6:ALT1","text":"Surface — Deus odio haberi nequit, Amor Dei intellectualis aeternus est, et Mens quatenus Corpus actu durat imaginatur atque recordatur.","refs":["CH_QGOH_PQ_6:A1","CH_QGOH_PQ_6:A7","CH_QGOH_PQ_6:A13"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"From","passages":[{"id":"RAW_QGOH_PQ_6:P0001","trace_type":"Composite","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"},{"id":"RAW_QGOH_PQ_6:P0022","trace_type":"Composite","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"},{"id":"RAW_QGOH_PQ_6:P0079","trace_type":"Composite","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_QGOH_PQ_6:ALT2","text":"Structural — Potentia Mentis ex cognitione, passio ex ideis inadaequatis, et remedia ex causali affectuum reordinatione oriuntur.","refs":["CH_QGOH_PQ_6:A4","CH_QGOH_PQ_6:A5","CH_QGOH_PQ_6:A6"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"From","passages":[{"id":"RAW_QGOH_PQ_6:P0016","trace_type":"Structural","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"},{"id":"RAW_QGOH_PQ_6:P0018","trace_type":"Structural","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"},{"id":"RAW_QGOH_PQ_6:P0019","trace_type":"Structural","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_QGOH_PQ_6:ALT3","text":"Metastrategic — Libertatis quaestio a dominio voluntario affectuum ad productionem actionum intellectualium et ad mutationem proportionis Mentis reconstituitur.","refs":["CH_QGOH_PQ_6:A4","CH_QGOH_PQ_6:A5","CH_QGOH_PQ_6:A16"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"From","passages":[{"id":"RAW_QGOH_PQ_6:P0016","trace_type":"Structural","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"},{"id":"RAW_QGOH_PQ_6:P0018","trace_type":"Structural","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"},{"id":"RAW_QGOH_PQ_6:P0112","trace_type":"Structural","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_QGOH_PQ_6:ALT4","text":"Ontological/Temporal — Duratio pertinet ad existentiam actualem Corporis, aeternitas ad essentiam eius in Deo conceptam, et Amor intellectualis ad ordinem aeternum huius essentiae.","refs":["CH_QGOH_PQ_6:A7","CH_QGOH_PQ_6:A8","CH_QGOH_PQ_6:A11","CH_QGOH_PQ_6:A13"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"From","passages":[{"id":"RAW_QGOH_PQ_6:P0026","trace_type":"Structural","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"},{"id":"RAW_QGOH_PQ_6:P0032","trace_type":"Structural","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"},{"id":"RAW_QGOH_PQ_6:P0059","trace_type":"Structural","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"},{"id":"RAW_QGOH_PQ_6:P0079","trace_type":"Structural","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]}],"original_name":"Altitude Levels"},{"number":"12","name":"Minimal Reconstruction Test","items":[{"id":"CH_QGOH_PQ_6:MRT1","text":"Systema extractum argumentationem restituit, si definitiones externae idearum adaequatarum, passionis, conatus et generum cognitionis iam concessae sunt.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[]},{"id":"CH_QGOH_PQ_6:MRT2","text":"Catena firmissima est nexus inter ideam Dei adaequatam, actionem Mentis, cognitionem tertii generis, laetitiam et Amorem intellectualem aeternum.","refs":["CH_QGOH_PQ_6:A1","CH_QGOH_PQ_6:A11","CH_QGOH_PQ_6:A12","CH_QGOH_PQ_6:A13"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"From","passages":[{"id":"RAW_QGOH_PQ_6:P0001","trace_type":"Structural","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"},{"id":"RAW_QGOH_PQ_6:P0069","trace_type":"Structural","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"},{"id":"RAW_QGOH_PQ_6:P0073","trace_type":"Structural","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"},{"id":"RAW_QGOH_PQ_6:P0077","trace_type":"Structural","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"},{"id":"RAW_QGOH_PQ_6:P0079","trace_type":"Structural","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_QGOH_PQ_6:MRT3","text":"Compressio relinquit indeterminatum quomodo notiones adaequationis, inadaequationis et causae formalis intra hunc locum sola constitui possint, quia textus eas ex Partibus prioribus accipit.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[]},{"id":"CH_QGOH_PQ_6:MRT4","text":"Commissura fragilis manet inter absentiam memoriae ante Corpus et assertionem quod nos aeternos sentimus ac experimur; transitio a demonstratione ad experientiam hic non independenter mechanizatur.","refs":["CH_QGOH_PQ_6:D6","CH_QGOH_PQ_6:A11"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"From","passages":[{"id":"RAW_QGOH_PQ_6:P0022","trace_type":"Tension","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"},{"id":"RAW_QGOH_PQ_6:P0034","trace_type":"Tension","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_QGOH_PQ_6:MRT5","text":"Tensio temporalis manet inter Amorem intellectualem sine principio et narrationem qua Mens paulatim aptior ad cognitionem tertii generis fit; textus hanc tensionem per fictionem expositionis temporalis administrat, non removet.","refs":["CH_QGOH_PQ_6:A10","CH_QGOH_PQ_6:A13"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"From","passages":[{"id":"RAW_QGOH_PQ_6:P0045","trace_type":"Tension","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"},{"id":"RAW_QGOH_PQ_6:P0079","trace_type":"Tension","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"},{"id":"RAW_QGOH_PQ_6:P0083","trace_type":"Tension","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_QGOH_PQ_6:MRT6","text":"Commissura inter aptitudinem Corporis et maiorem partem Mentis aeternam pendet a longa catena ordinationis affectionum, relationis ad Deum et amoris crescentis, cuius nexus non hic separatim demonstratur.","refs":["CH_QGOH_PQ_6:A17"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"From","passages":[{"id":"RAW_QGOH_PQ_6:P0118","trace_type":"Structural","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"},{"id":"RAW_QGOH_PQ_6:P0120","trace_type":"Structural","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_QGOH_PQ_6:MRT7","text":"Restitutio motum textus servat: remedia affectuum → distinctio durationis et aeternitatis → cognitio intuitiva → Amor Dei intellectualis → libertas et imminutio mortis metus.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_QGOH_PQ_6:P0016","trace_type":"Structural","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"},{"id":"RAW_QGOH_PQ_6:P0032","trace_type":"Structural","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"},{"id":"RAW_QGOH_PQ_6:P0043","trace_type":"Structural","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"},{"id":"RAW_QGOH_PQ_6:P0077","trace_type":"Structural","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"},{"id":"RAW_QGOH_PQ_6:P0112","trace_type":"Structural","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"},{"id":"RAW_QGOH_PQ_6:P0120","trace_type":"Structural","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]}],"original_name":"Minimal Reconstruction Test"}],"language_code":"la","language_name":"Latina","translations":[{"id":"CAREVAR_20261015T014906525494Z_1d65db87","language_code":"en","language_name":"English","title":"Part Five - On the Power of the Intellect, or on Human Freedom · passage 2","source_sha256":"09e4eb685f437b36e631b037bc0557a9b81cb9a4d114f4680428ca840dff446c","sha256":"c2c4f5798e176991ae8e8864b30e8618f2d0801564accaa35ae0d7cc822dacb5","rendition_type":"machine","review_status":"machine","model":"gpt-5.6-terra","created":"2026-10-15T01:49:06.526448+00:00","sections":[{"number":"1","name":"Argumental Spine","items":[{"id":"CH_QGOH_PQ_6:AS1","text":"The text investigates the power of the Mind over the affects and freedom, distinguishing what is bound to time and the Body from what remains eternal to the essence of the Mind.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_QGOH_PQ_6:P0022","trace_type":"Structural","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"},{"id":"RAW_QGOH_PQ_6:P0026","trace_type":"Structural","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"},{"id":"RAW_QGOH_PQ_6:P0030","trace_type":"Structural","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"},{"id":"RAW_QGOH_PQ_6:P0032","trace_type":"Structural","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"},{"id":"RAW_QGOH_PQ_6:P0034","trace_type":"Structural","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_QGOH_PQ_6:AS2","text":"Demonstrative and reconstructive argument derives remedies for the affects from the degree of knowledge, not from voluntary repression of the affects.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_QGOH_PQ_6:P0016","trace_type":"Structural","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"},{"id":"RAW_QGOH_PQ_6:P0018","trace_type":"Structural","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"},{"id":"RAW_QGOH_PQ_6:P0019","trace_type":"Structural","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_QGOH_PQ_6:AS3","text":"An adequate idea of God first separates divine love from hatred, sadness, envy, and jealousy.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_QGOH_PQ_6:P0001","trace_type":"Composite","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"},{"id":"RAW_QGOH_PQ_6:P0003","trace_type":"Composite","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"},{"id":"RAW_QGOH_PQ_6:P0011","trace_type":"Composite","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"},{"id":"RAW_QGOH_PQ_6:P0013","trace_type":"Composite","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"},{"id":"RAW_QGOH_PQ_6:P0077","trace_type":"Composite","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_QGOH_PQ_6:AS4","text":"The power of the Mind is then reduced to the proportion of adequate and inadequate ideas and explained through the ordering of the affects.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_QGOH_PQ_6:P0016","trace_type":"Structural","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"},{"id":"RAW_QGOH_PQ_6:P0018","trace_type":"Structural","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"},{"id":"RAW_QGOH_PQ_6:P0019","trace_type":"Structural","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_QGOH_PQ_6:AS5","text":"Imagination, memory, and passions are referred to the duration of the Body; the essence of the Body conceived in God is referred to the eternity of the Mind.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_QGOH_PQ_6:P0022","trace_type":"Composite","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"},{"id":"RAW_QGOH_PQ_6:P0024","trace_type":"Composite","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"},{"id":"RAW_QGOH_PQ_6:P0026","trace_type":"Composite","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"},{"id":"RAW_QGOH_PQ_6:P0032","trace_type":"Composite","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"},{"id":"RAW_QGOH_PQ_6:P0085","trace_type":"Composite","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_QGOH_PQ_6:AS6","text":"Knowledge of the third kind, proceeding from the eternity of the Mind, produces the highest joy and the intellectual Love of God.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_QGOH_PQ_6:P0043","trace_type":"Composite","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"},{"id":"RAW_QGOH_PQ_6:P0051","trace_type":"Composite","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"},{"id":"RAW_QGOH_PQ_6:P0073","trace_type":"Composite","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"},{"id":"RAW_QGOH_PQ_6:P0075","trace_type":"Composite","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"},{"id":"RAW_QGOH_PQ_6:P0077","trace_type":"Composite","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_QGOH_PQ_6:AS7","text":"Intellectual Love, as a part of God's infinite love, constitutes freedom, blessedness, diminished fear of death, and the greater eternal part of the Mind.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_QGOH_PQ_6:P0077","trace_type":"Structural","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"},{"id":"RAW_QGOH_PQ_6:P0079","trace_type":"Structural","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"},{"id":"RAW_QGOH_PQ_6:P0083","trace_type":"Structural","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"},{"id":"RAW_QGOH_PQ_6:P0097","trace_type":"Structural","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"},{"id":"RAW_QGOH_PQ_6:P0103","trace_type":"Structural","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"},{"id":"RAW_QGOH_PQ_6:P0112","trace_type":"Structural","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"},{"id":"RAW_QGOH_PQ_6:P0116","trace_type":"Structural","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"},{"id":"RAW_QGOH_PQ_6:P0118","trace_type":"Structural","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"},{"id":"RAW_QGOH_PQ_6:P0120","trace_type":"Structural","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]}],"original_name":"Argumental Spine"},{"number":"2","name":"Load-Bearing Axioms","items":[{"id":"CH_QGOH_PQ_6:A1","text":"Contemplation of God from an adequate idea in us is an action of the Mind not accompanied by sadness.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_QGOH_PQ_6:P0001","trace_type":"Direct","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"},{"id":"RAW_QGOH_PQ_6:P0005","trace_type":"Direct","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_QGOH_PQ_6:A2","text":"God, who is loved by a finite being, cannot, through human desire, pass into a mutable or non-divine nature.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_QGOH_PQ_6:P0007","trace_type":"Direct","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"},{"id":"RAW_QGOH_PQ_6:P0009","trace_type":"Direct","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_QGOH_PQ_6:A3","text":"Rational love toward God is the highest good common to all and is increased through the imagination of common joy.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_QGOH_PQ_6:P0011","trace_type":"Direct","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"},{"id":"RAW_QGOH_PQ_6:P0013","trace_type":"Direct","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_QGOH_PQ_6:A4","text":"The force of an affect is defined by comparison of the external cause with our own, the power of the Mind by knowledge alone, and passion by the privation of knowledge.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_QGOH_PQ_6:P0017","trace_type":"Direct","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"},{"id":"RAW_QGOH_PQ_6:P0018","trace_type":"Direct","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_QGOH_PQ_6:A5","text":"By knowledge the Mind separates an affect from the confused thought of an external cause and can order and connect affects according to the order of the intellect.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_QGOH_PQ_6:P0016","trace_type":"Direct","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_QGOH_PQ_6:A6","text":"Affects referred to things understood, common properties, or God, supported by several causes, overcome affects arising from confused knowledge.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_QGOH_PQ_6:P0016","trace_type":"Direct","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"},{"id":"RAW_QGOH_PQ_6:P0019","trace_type":"Direct","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_QGOH_PQ_6:A7","text":"Imagination, memory, the conception of things under duration, and passions pertain to the actual existence of the Body.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_QGOH_PQ_6:P0022","trace_type":"Composite","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"},{"id":"RAW_QGOH_PQ_6:P0024","trace_type":"Composite","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"},{"id":"RAW_QGOH_PQ_6:P0085","trace_type":"Composite","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"},{"id":"RAW_QGOH_PQ_6:P0087","trace_type":"Composite","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_QGOH_PQ_6:A8","text":"In God there is necessarily given an idea of the essence of each human Body, which pertains to the essence of the human Mind and is eternal.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_QGOH_PQ_6:P0026","trace_type":"Composite","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"},{"id":"RAW_QGOH_PQ_6:P0028","trace_type":"Composite","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"},{"id":"RAW_QGOH_PQ_6:P0030","trace_type":"Composite","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"},{"id":"RAW_QGOH_PQ_6:P0032","trace_type":"Composite","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_QGOH_PQ_6:A9","text":"The third kind of knowledge proceeds from an adequate idea of God's attributes to the adequate knowledge of the essences of singular things.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_QGOH_PQ_6:P0043","trace_type":"Direct","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"},{"id":"RAW_QGOH_PQ_6:P0069","trace_type":"Direct","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_QGOH_PQ_6:A10","text":"The desire to know things by the third kind of knowledge cannot arise from the first kind of knowledge, but arises from the second.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_QGOH_PQ_6:P0053","trace_type":"Direct","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"},{"id":"RAW_QGOH_PQ_6:P0055","trace_type":"Direct","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_QGOH_PQ_6:A11","text":"The Mind, insofar as it conceives the eternal essence of the Body, has knowledge of God and is the formal cause of knowledge of the third kind.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_QGOH_PQ_6:P0063","trace_type":"Composite","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"},{"id":"RAW_QGOH_PQ_6:P0067","trace_type":"Composite","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"},{"id":"RAW_QGOH_PQ_6:P0069","trace_type":"Composite","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_QGOH_PQ_6:A12","text":"From knowledge of the third kind arises the Mind's highest joy, together with the idea of God as cause.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_QGOH_PQ_6:P0073","trace_type":"Direct","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"},{"id":"RAW_QGOH_PQ_6:P0075","trace_type":"Direct","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_QGOH_PQ_6:A13","text":"The intellectual Love of God, which arises from the third kind of knowledge, is eternal.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_QGOH_PQ_6:P0077","trace_type":"Direct","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"},{"id":"RAW_QGOH_PQ_6:P0079","trace_type":"Direct","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"},{"id":"RAW_QGOH_PQ_6:P0081","trace_type":"Direct","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_QGOH_PQ_6:A14","text":"The intellectual Love of the Mind toward God is a part of the infinite love by which God loves himself.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_QGOH_PQ_6:P0093","trace_type":"Composite","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"},{"id":"RAW_QGOH_PQ_6:P0097","trace_type":"Composite","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"},{"id":"RAW_QGOH_PQ_6:P0099","trace_type":"Composite","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_QGOH_PQ_6:A15","text":"Nothing is given in Nature that is contrary to the intellectual Love of God or can remove it.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_QGOH_PQ_6:P0106","trace_type":"Direct","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"},{"id":"RAW_QGOH_PQ_6:P0108","trace_type":"Direct","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_QGOH_PQ_6:A16","text":"The more the Mind understands by the second and third kinds of knowledge, the less it suffers from evil affects and the less it fears death.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_QGOH_PQ_6:P0112","trace_type":"Composite","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"},{"id":"RAW_QGOH_PQ_6:P0114","trace_type":"Composite","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"},{"id":"RAW_QGOH_PQ_6:P0116","trace_type":"Composite","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_QGOH_PQ_6:A17","text":"A Body suited to very many actions disposes the Mind to order affections according to the intellect and to constitute a greater eternal part of the Mind.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_QGOH_PQ_6:P0118","trace_type":"Direct","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"},{"id":"RAW_QGOH_PQ_6:P0120","trace_type":"Direct","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]}],"original_name":"Load-Bearing Axioms"},{"number":"3","name":"Apophatic Structure","items":[{"id":"CH_QGOH_PQ_6:NX1","text":"Sadness cannot coexist with the idea of God; therefore God cannot be hated, and Love toward God cannot be turned into hatred.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_QGOH_PQ_6:P0001","trace_type":"Negative","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"},{"id":"RAW_QGOH_PQ_6:P0003","trace_type":"Negative","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_QGOH_PQ_6:NX2","text":"Love of God does not include the striving that God should love the lover in return, because such desire would will God to be non-God.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_QGOH_PQ_6:P0007","trace_type":"Negative","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"},{"id":"RAW_QGOH_PQ_6:P0009","trace_type":"Negative","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_QGOH_PQ_6:NX3","text":"Rational Love of God is polluted by neither envy nor jealousy, because the common highest good is not diminished through participation.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_QGOH_PQ_6:P0011","trace_type":"Negative","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"},{"id":"RAW_QGOH_PQ_6:P0013","trace_type":"Negative","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_QGOH_PQ_6:NX4","text":"Clear and distinct knowledge does not necessarily abolish all passions absolutely; nevertheless, it makes them the smallest part of the Mind.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_QGOH_PQ_6:P0018","trace_type":"Negative","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"},{"id":"RAW_QGOH_PQ_6:P0019","trace_type":"Negative","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_QGOH_PQ_6:NX5","text":"Without the Body the Mind can imagine nothing, recall past things, or be subject to passions.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_QGOH_PQ_6:P0022","trace_type":"Negative","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"},{"id":"RAW_QGOH_PQ_6:P0024","trace_type":"Negative","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"},{"id":"RAW_QGOH_PQ_6:P0085","trace_type":"Negative","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_QGOH_PQ_6:NX6","text":"Eternity can neither be explained through duration nor referred to time.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_QGOH_PQ_6:P0034","trace_type":"Negative","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"},{"id":"RAW_QGOH_PQ_6:P0059","trace_type":"Negative","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"},{"id":"RAW_QGOH_PQ_6:P0065","trace_type":"Negative","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_QGOH_PQ_6:NX7","text":"Mutilated and confused knowledge of the first kind cannot generate the desire for knowledge of the third kind.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_QGOH_PQ_6:P0053","trace_type":"Negative","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"},{"id":"RAW_QGOH_PQ_6:P0055","trace_type":"Negative","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_QGOH_PQ_6:NX8","text":"No Love other than intellectual Love is eternal.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_QGOH_PQ_6:P0079","trace_type":"Negative","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"},{"id":"RAW_QGOH_PQ_6:P0089","trace_type":"Negative","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_QGOH_PQ_6:NX9","text":"No truth contrary to intellectual Love can be given, because its destruction would convert truth into falsehood.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_QGOH_PQ_6:P0106","trace_type":"Negative","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"},{"id":"RAW_QGOH_PQ_6:P0108","trace_type":"Negative","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]}],"original_name":"Apophatic Structure"},{"number":"4","name":"Derivative Propositions","items":[{"id":"CH_QGOH_PQ_6:D1","text":"An adequate idea of God transforms God from an object of sadness and hatred into an object of action and love.","refs":["CH_QGOH_PQ_6:A1"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"From","passages":[{"id":"RAW_QGOH_PQ_6:P0001","trace_type":"Composite","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"},{"id":"RAW_QGOH_PQ_6:P0005","trace_type":"Composite","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_QGOH_PQ_6:D2","text":"One who loves God cannot desire that God love him in return, because that love would require a change in divine nature.","refs":["CH_QGOH_PQ_6:A2"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"From","passages":[{"id":"RAW_QGOH_PQ_6:P0007","trace_type":"Direct","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"},{"id":"RAW_QGOH_PQ_6:P0009","trace_type":"Direct","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_QGOH_PQ_6:D3","text":"The sharing of Love of God is not a diminution of good but a cause of the increase of love; more people enjoying the same good increase the love of one.","refs":["CH_QGOH_PQ_6:A3"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"From","passages":[{"id":"RAW_QGOH_PQ_6:P0011","trace_type":"Direct","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"},{"id":"RAW_QGOH_PQ_6:P0013","trace_type":"Direct","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_QGOH_PQ_6:D4","text":"The magnitude of an affect does not in itself indicate the power of the Mind, because the conflict of affects is assessed from external forces, whereas the action of the Mind is assessed from adequate ideas.","refs":["CH_QGOH_PQ_6:A4"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"From","passages":[{"id":"RAW_QGOH_PQ_6:P0017","trace_type":"Composite","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"},{"id":"RAW_QGOH_PQ_6:P0018","trace_type":"Composite","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_QGOH_PQ_6:D5","text":"Ailments of the mind arise from excessive love of mutable and incompatible things; causal knowledge reorders affects and transfers Love to an immutable thing that can truly be possessed.","refs":["CH_QGOH_PQ_6:A3","CH_QGOH_PQ_6:A5","CH_QGOH_PQ_6:A6"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"From","passages":[{"id":"RAW_QGOH_PQ_6:P0018","trace_type":"Composite","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"},{"id":"RAW_QGOH_PQ_6:P0019","trace_type":"Composite","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_QGOH_PQ_6:D6","text":"With the Body, imagination, memory, and passions cease; yet there remains that of the Mind which is conceived through the eternal idea of the essence of the Body in God.","refs":["CH_QGOH_PQ_6:A7","CH_QGOH_PQ_6:A8"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"From","passages":[{"id":"RAW_QGOH_PQ_6:P0022","trace_type":"Composite","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"},{"id":"RAW_QGOH_PQ_6:P0026","trace_type":"Composite","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"},{"id":"RAW_QGOH_PQ_6:P0032","trace_type":"Composite","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"},{"id":"RAW_QGOH_PQ_6:P0085","trace_type":"Composite","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_QGOH_PQ_6:D7","text":"Actuality is conceived in two ways: either under a determinate time and place, or in God from the necessity of divine nature; only the second conception presents things under the aspect of eternity.","refs":["CH_QGOH_PQ_6:A7","CH_QGOH_PQ_6:A8","CH_QGOH_PQ_6:A11"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"From","passages":[{"id":"RAW_QGOH_PQ_6:P0057","trace_type":"Direct","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"},{"id":"RAW_QGOH_PQ_6:P0059","trace_type":"Direct","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"},{"id":"RAW_QGOH_PQ_6:P0061","trace_type":"Direct","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_QGOH_PQ_6:D8","text":"The more suited the Mind is to knowledge of the third kind, the more it desires to know things by the same kind of knowledge; its progress proceeds from what is adequately known, not from confusion.","refs":["CH_QGOH_PQ_6:A9","CH_QGOH_PQ_6:A10"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"From","passages":[{"id":"RAW_QGOH_PQ_6:P0045","trace_type":"Composite","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"},{"id":"RAW_QGOH_PQ_6:P0053","trace_type":"Composite","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"},{"id":"RAW_QGOH_PQ_6:P0055","trace_type":"Composite","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_QGOH_PQ_6:D9","text":"Knowledge of singular things by the third kind affects the Mind more efficaciously than universal knowledge of the second kind, because from the singular essence itself dependence on God is conceived.","refs":["CH_QGOH_PQ_6:A9","CH_QGOH_PQ_6:A11"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"From","passages":[{"id":"RAW_QGOH_PQ_6:P0103","trace_type":"Direct","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"},{"id":"RAW_QGOH_PQ_6:P0104","trace_type":"Direct","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_QGOH_PQ_6:D10","text":"Knowledge of the third kind produces the highest joy, which, together with the idea of God as cause, constitutes the intellectual Love of God and entails its eternity.","refs":["CH_QGOH_PQ_6:A11","CH_QGOH_PQ_6:A12","CH_QGOH_PQ_6:A13"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"From","passages":[{"id":"RAW_QGOH_PQ_6:P0069","trace_type":"Structural","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"},{"id":"RAW_QGOH_PQ_6:P0073","trace_type":"Structural","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"},{"id":"RAW_QGOH_PQ_6:P0075","trace_type":"Structural","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"},{"id":"RAW_QGOH_PQ_6:P0077","trace_type":"Structural","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"},{"id":"RAW_QGOH_PQ_6:P0079","trace_type":"Structural","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_QGOH_PQ_6:D11","text":"The intellectual Love of the Mind toward God and God's Love toward human beings are the same Love, insofar as infinite divine love is explained through the essence of the human Mind.","refs":["CH_QGOH_PQ_6:A14"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"From","passages":[{"id":"RAW_QGOH_PQ_6:P0097","trace_type":"Direct","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"},{"id":"RAW_QGOH_PQ_6:P0099","trace_type":"Direct","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"},{"id":"RAW_QGOH_PQ_6:P0101","trace_type":"Direct","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_QGOH_PQ_6:D12","text":"Intellectual Love, because it necessarily follows from eternal truth, cannot be destroyed by a contrary affect; imaginative and bodily loves belong to temporality alone.","refs":["CH_QGOH_PQ_6:A7","CH_QGOH_PQ_6:A13","CH_QGOH_PQ_6:A15"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"From","passages":[{"id":"RAW_QGOH_PQ_6:P0085","trace_type":"Composite","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"},{"id":"RAW_QGOH_PQ_6:P0089","trace_type":"Composite","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"},{"id":"RAW_QGOH_PQ_6:P0106","trace_type":"Composite","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_QGOH_PQ_6:D13","text":"The increase of adequate knowledge and the aptitude of the Body for doing more things diminish evil passions, fear of death, and the perishing proportion of the Mind.","refs":["CH_QGOH_PQ_6:A16","CH_QGOH_PQ_6:A17"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"From","passages":[{"id":"RAW_QGOH_PQ_6:P0112","trace_type":"Structural","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"},{"id":"RAW_QGOH_PQ_6:P0114","trace_type":"Structural","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"},{"id":"RAW_QGOH_PQ_6:P0118","trace_type":"Structural","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"},{"id":"RAW_QGOH_PQ_6:P0120","trace_type":"Structural","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]}],"original_name":"Derivative Propositions"},{"number":"5","name":"Super-Derivatives","items":[{"id":"CH_QGOH_PQ_6:SD1","text":"Freedom is neither exemption from causality nor emptiness of affects, but the stable predominance of actions of cognitive origin over passions arising from inadequate ideas.","refs":["CH_QGOH_PQ_6:A4","CH_QGOH_PQ_6:A5","CH_QGOH_PQ_6:A6","CH_QGOH_PQ_6:A16"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"From","passages":[{"id":"RAW_QGOH_PQ_6:P0016","trace_type":"Structural","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"},{"id":"RAW_QGOH_PQ_6:P0018","trace_type":"Structural","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"},{"id":"RAW_QGOH_PQ_6:P0112","trace_type":"Structural","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_QGOH_PQ_6:SD2","text":"The eternity of the Mind is not an undivided property of the entire empirical person, but the proportion of that which constitutes the Mind as referring to eternal essence.","refs":["CH_QGOH_PQ_6:A7","CH_QGOH_PQ_6:A8","CH_QGOH_PQ_6:A16","CH_QGOH_PQ_6:A17"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"From","passages":[{"id":"RAW_QGOH_PQ_6:P0022","trace_type":"Structural","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"},{"id":"RAW_QGOH_PQ_6:P0032","trace_type":"Structural","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"},{"id":"RAW_QGOH_PQ_6:P0034","trace_type":"Structural","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"},{"id":"RAW_QGOH_PQ_6:P0114","trace_type":"Structural","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"},{"id":"RAW_QGOH_PQ_6:P0118","trace_type":"Structural","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_QGOH_PQ_6:SD3","text":"The highest good, rationally understood, has a non-rivalrous structure: its sharing does not introduce division but the cause of its own increase.","refs":["CH_QGOH_PQ_6:A3"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"From","passages":[{"id":"RAW_QGOH_PQ_6:P0011","trace_type":"Direct","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"},{"id":"RAW_QGOH_PQ_6:P0013","trace_type":"Direct","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_QGOH_PQ_6:SD4","text":"Intuitive knowledge of singular things is not a secondary application of universal knowledge, but the locus in which divine dependence becomes affectively efficacious.","refs":["CH_QGOH_PQ_6:A9","CH_QGOH_PQ_6:A11","CH_QGOH_PQ_6:D9"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"From","passages":[{"id":"RAW_QGOH_PQ_6:P0069","trace_type":"Composite","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"},{"id":"RAW_QGOH_PQ_6:P0103","trace_type":"Composite","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"},{"id":"RAW_QGOH_PQ_6:P0104","trace_type":"Composite","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_QGOH_PQ_6:SD5","text":"The Body is not a mere impediment to eternity, but a strategic condition by which the Mind is disposed to the ordering of affections and to a greater eternal part.","refs":["CH_QGOH_PQ_6:A7","CH_QGOH_PQ_6:A17"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"From","passages":[{"id":"RAW_QGOH_PQ_6:P0085","trace_type":"Structural","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"},{"id":"RAW_QGOH_PQ_6:P0118","trace_type":"Structural","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"},{"id":"RAW_QGOH_PQ_6:P0120","trace_type":"Structural","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]}],"original_name":"Super-Derivatives"},{"number":"6","name":"Deep Apophatics","items":[{"id":"CH_QGOH_PQ_6:DNX1","text":"It must be false that freedom consists in a command of the will that wholly extinguishes all affects; the system places freedom in a change of proportion between action and passion.","refs":["CH_QGOH_PQ_6:A4","CH_QGOH_PQ_6:A5","CH_QGOH_PQ_6:D5"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"From","passages":[{"id":"RAW_QGOH_PQ_6:P0016","trace_type":"Negative","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"},{"id":"RAW_QGOH_PQ_6:P0018","trace_type":"Negative","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"},{"id":"RAW_QGOH_PQ_6:P0019","trace_type":"Negative","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_QGOH_PQ_6:DNX2","text":"It must be false that eternity is a continuation of memory, imagination, or personal narrative after death; all these pertain to the duration of the Body.","refs":["CH_QGOH_PQ_6:A7","CH_QGOH_PQ_6:A8","CH_QGOH_PQ_6:D6"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"From","passages":[{"id":"RAW_QGOH_PQ_6:P0022","trace_type":"Negative","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"},{"id":"RAW_QGOH_PQ_6:P0034","trace_type":"Negative","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"},{"id":"RAW_QGOH_PQ_6:P0089","trace_type":"Negative","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"},{"id":"RAW_QGOH_PQ_6:P0091","trace_type":"Negative","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_QGOH_PQ_6:DNX3","text":"It must be false that God is an external other, whose love is reciprocally sought or to whom sadness is passively imputed; God is understood as immanent cause, not as an object of mutual affective negotiation.","refs":["CH_QGOH_PQ_6:A1","CH_QGOH_PQ_6:A2","CH_QGOH_PQ_6:A14"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"From","passages":[{"id":"RAW_QGOH_PQ_6:P0005","trace_type":"Negative","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"},{"id":"RAW_QGOH_PQ_6:P0007","trace_type":"Negative","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"},{"id":"RAW_QGOH_PQ_6:P0097","trace_type":"Negative","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"},{"id":"RAW_QGOH_PQ_6:P0101","trace_type":"Negative","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_QGOH_PQ_6:DNX4","text":"It must be false that all degrees of knowledge equally lead to intuitive knowledge; the first kind is structurally sterile with respect to the desire of the third kind.","refs":["CH_QGOH_PQ_6:A9","CH_QGOH_PQ_6:A10"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"From","passages":[{"id":"RAW_QGOH_PQ_6:P0043","trace_type":"Negative","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"},{"id":"RAW_QGOH_PQ_6:P0053","trace_type":"Negative","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"},{"id":"RAW_QGOH_PQ_6:P0055","trace_type":"Negative","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_QGOH_PQ_6:DNX5","text":"It must be false that blessedness is a scarce-distributed good, participation in which could diminish another's happiness.","refs":["CH_QGOH_PQ_6:A3","CH_QGOH_PQ_6:D3"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"From","passages":[{"id":"RAW_QGOH_PQ_6:P0011","trace_type":"Negative","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"},{"id":"RAW_QGOH_PQ_6:P0013","trace_type":"Negative","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]}],"original_name":"Deep Apophatics"},{"number":"7","name":"Super-Apophatics","items":[{"id":"CH_QGOH_PQ_6:SNX1","text":"A finite subject subsisting outside God, which loves God as an extrinsic object, is precluded; intellectual Love is a determination of divine love itself.","refs":["CH_QGOH_PQ_6:A8","CH_QGOH_PQ_6:A14","CH_QGOH_PQ_6:D11"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"From","passages":[{"id":"RAW_QGOH_PQ_6:P0026","trace_type":"Negative","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"},{"id":"RAW_QGOH_PQ_6:P0097","trace_type":"Negative","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"},{"id":"RAW_QGOH_PQ_6:P0101","trace_type":"Negative","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_QGOH_PQ_6:SNX2","text":"A temporal measure by which eternity, salvation, or blessedness could be assessed is precluded; beginning, duration, and recollection are not transferred to the eternal order.","refs":["CH_QGOH_PQ_6:A7","CH_QGOH_PQ_6:A8","CH_QGOH_PQ_6:A13"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"From","passages":[{"id":"RAW_QGOH_PQ_6:P0034","trace_type":"Negative","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"},{"id":"RAW_QGOH_PQ_6:P0059","trace_type":"Negative","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"},{"id":"RAW_QGOH_PQ_6:P0065","trace_type":"Negative","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"},{"id":"RAW_QGOH_PQ_6:P0079","trace_type":"Negative","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_QGOH_PQ_6:SNX3","text":"The possibility that the eternal truth of intellectual action be annihilated by an opposed natural force is precluded; the destruction of intellectual Love would require the falsehood of truth.","refs":["CH_QGOH_PQ_6:A13","CH_QGOH_PQ_6:A15"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"From","passages":[{"id":"RAW_QGOH_PQ_6:P0079","trace_type":"Negative","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"},{"id":"RAW_QGOH_PQ_6:P0106","trace_type":"Negative","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"},{"id":"RAW_QGOH_PQ_6:P0108","trace_type":"Negative","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]}],"original_name":"Super-Apophatics"},{"number":"8","name":"Formal Generative Schema","items":[{"id":"CH_QGOH_PQ_6:FGS1","text":"Inadequate idea + imagination of the Body under duration + love of a mutable thing → passion, anxiety, conflict, and affective vulnerability.","refs":["CH_QGOH_PQ_6:A4","CH_QGOH_PQ_6:A7"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"From","passages":[{"id":"RAW_QGOH_PQ_6:P0018","trace_type":"Composite","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"},{"id":"RAW_QGOH_PQ_6:P0022","trace_type":"Composite","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"},{"id":"RAW_QGOH_PQ_6:P0087","trace_type":"Composite","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_QGOH_PQ_6:FGS2","text":"Knowledge of the cause + separation from the externally and confusedly imagined + ordering of affections → diminution of passion and predominance of the Mind's action.","refs":["CH_QGOH_PQ_6:A5","CH_QGOH_PQ_6:A6"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"From","passages":[{"id":"RAW_QGOH_PQ_6:P0016","trace_type":"Direct","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"},{"id":"RAW_QGOH_PQ_6:P0019","trace_type":"Direct","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_QGOH_PQ_6:FGS3","text":"Temporal actuality of the Body / eternal essence of the Body in God → distinction between perishing memory and the eternal part of the Mind.","refs":["CH_QGOH_PQ_6:A7","CH_QGOH_PQ_6:A8"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"From","passages":[{"id":"RAW_QGOH_PQ_6:P0022","trace_type":"Composite","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"},{"id":"RAW_QGOH_PQ_6:P0026","trace_type":"Composite","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"},{"id":"RAW_QGOH_PQ_6:P0032","trace_type":"Composite","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_QGOH_PQ_6:FGS4","text":"Mind insofar as eternal → knowledge of God → knowledge of the third kind → highest joy → eternal intellectual Love of God.","refs":["CH_QGOH_PQ_6:A9","CH_QGOH_PQ_6:A11","CH_QGOH_PQ_6:A12","CH_QGOH_PQ_6:A13"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"From","passages":[{"id":"RAW_QGOH_PQ_6:P0063","trace_type":"Structural","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"},{"id":"RAW_QGOH_PQ_6:P0069","trace_type":"Structural","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"},{"id":"RAW_QGOH_PQ_6:P0073","trace_type":"Structural","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"},{"id":"RAW_QGOH_PQ_6:P0075","trace_type":"Structural","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"},{"id":"RAW_QGOH_PQ_6:P0077","trace_type":"Structural","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"},{"id":"RAW_QGOH_PQ_6:P0079","trace_type":"Structural","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_QGOH_PQ_6:FGS5","text":"Intellectual Love as part of God's love + absence of a contrary → stable blessedness, diminution of fear of death, and affective freedom.","refs":["CH_QGOH_PQ_6:A14","CH_QGOH_PQ_6:A15","CH_QGOH_PQ_6:A16"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"From","passages":[{"id":"RAW_QGOH_PQ_6:P0097","trace_type":"Structural","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"},{"id":"RAW_QGOH_PQ_6:P0106","trace_type":"Structural","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"},{"id":"RAW_QGOH_PQ_6:P0112","trace_type":"Structural","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"},{"id":"RAW_QGOH_PQ_6:P0116","trace_type":"Structural","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_QGOH_PQ_6:FGS6","text":"Aptitude of the Body for doing more things → ordering of more affections → their relation to the idea of God → greater eternal part of the Mind.","refs":["CH_QGOH_PQ_6:A17"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"From","passages":[{"id":"RAW_QGOH_PQ_6:P0118","trace_type":"Direct","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"},{"id":"RAW_QGOH_PQ_6:P0120","trace_type":"Direct","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]}],"original_name":"Formal Generative Schema"},{"number":"9","name":"Argument Flow Map","items":[{"id":"CH_QGOH_PQ_6:AF1","text":"Adequate idea of God → action without sadness → impossibility of hatred and of the conversion of Love of God into hatred.","refs":["CH_QGOH_PQ_6:A1"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"From","passages":[{"id":"RAW_QGOH_PQ_6:P0001","trace_type":"Direct","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"},{"id":"RAW_QGOH_PQ_6:P0003","trace_type":"Direct","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_QGOH_PQ_6:AF2","text":"Common highest good → diminution of envy and jealousy → Love of God increased through the participation of others.","refs":["CH_QGOH_PQ_6:A3"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"From","passages":[{"id":"RAW_QGOH_PQ_6:P0011","trace_type":"Direct","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"},{"id":"RAW_QGOH_PQ_6:P0013","trace_type":"Direct","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_QGOH_PQ_6:AF3","text":"Power as knowledge → remedies through causal knowledge and ordering of affects → diminution of passions, not their absolute abolition.","refs":["CH_QGOH_PQ_6:A4","CH_QGOH_PQ_6:A5","CH_QGOH_PQ_6:A6"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"From","passages":[{"id":"RAW_QGOH_PQ_6:P0016","trace_type":"Structural","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"},{"id":"RAW_QGOH_PQ_6:P0018","trace_type":"Structural","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"},{"id":"RAW_QGOH_PQ_6:P0019","trace_type":"Structural","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_QGOH_PQ_6:AF4","text":"Durable Body → temporal imagination and memory; essence of the Body in God → eternal part of the Mind.","refs":["CH_QGOH_PQ_6:A7","CH_QGOH_PQ_6:A8"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"From","passages":[{"id":"RAW_QGOH_PQ_6:P0022","trace_type":"Composite","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"},{"id":"RAW_QGOH_PQ_6:P0026","trace_type":"Composite","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"},{"id":"RAW_QGOH_PQ_6:P0032","trace_type":"Composite","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_QGOH_PQ_6:AF5","text":"Eternal Mind → third kind of knowledge → highest joy → eternal intellectual Love of God.","refs":["CH_QGOH_PQ_6:A9","CH_QGOH_PQ_6:A10","CH_QGOH_PQ_6:A11","CH_QGOH_PQ_6:A12","CH_QGOH_PQ_6:A13"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"From","passages":[{"id":"RAW_QGOH_PQ_6:P0063","trace_type":"Structural","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"},{"id":"RAW_QGOH_PQ_6:P0069","trace_type":"Structural","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"},{"id":"RAW_QGOH_PQ_6:P0073","trace_type":"Structural","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"},{"id":"RAW_QGOH_PQ_6:P0077","trace_type":"Structural","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"},{"id":"RAW_QGOH_PQ_6:P0079","trace_type":"Structural","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_QGOH_PQ_6:AF6","text":"Intellectual Love as partially explained divine love → no contrary destruction → blessedness, freedom, and diminution of fear of death.","refs":["CH_QGOH_PQ_6:A14","CH_QGOH_PQ_6:A15","CH_QGOH_PQ_6:A16"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"From","passages":[{"id":"RAW_QGOH_PQ_6:P0097","trace_type":"Structural","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"},{"id":"RAW_QGOH_PQ_6:P0106","trace_type":"Structural","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"},{"id":"RAW_QGOH_PQ_6:P0112","trace_type":"Structural","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"},{"id":"RAW_QGOH_PQ_6:P0116","trace_type":"Structural","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]}],"original_name":"Argument Flow Map"},{"number":"10","name":"Map-Effects","items":[{"id":"CH_QGOH_PQ_6:ME1","text":"Affects are reclassified from a scale of psychological intensity to a proportion among external causes, adequate knowledge, and privation of knowledge.","refs":["CH_QGOH_PQ_6:A4"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"From","passages":[{"id":"RAW_QGOH_PQ_6:P0017","trace_type":"Direct","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"},{"id":"RAW_QGOH_PQ_6:P0018","trace_type":"Direct","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_QGOH_PQ_6:ME2","text":"Actuality is divided into imaginable temporal actuality and eternal actuality conceived in God.","refs":["CH_QGOH_PQ_6:A7","CH_QGOH_PQ_6:A8","CH_QGOH_PQ_6:D7"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"From","passages":[{"id":"RAW_QGOH_PQ_6:P0057","trace_type":"Direct","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"},{"id":"RAW_QGOH_PQ_6:P0059","trace_type":"Direct","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"},{"id":"RAW_QGOH_PQ_6:P0061","trace_type":"Direct","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_QGOH_PQ_6:ME3","text":"Love of God is referred away from reciprocal, possessive, and rivalrous love to intellectual joy in the divine cause.","refs":["CH_QGOH_PQ_6:A2","CH_QGOH_PQ_6:A3","CH_QGOH_PQ_6:A12"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"From","passages":[{"id":"RAW_QGOH_PQ_6:P0007","trace_type":"Composite","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"},{"id":"RAW_QGOH_PQ_6:P0013","trace_type":"Composite","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"},{"id":"RAW_QGOH_PQ_6:P0075","trace_type":"Composite","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"},{"id":"RAW_QGOH_PQ_6:P0077","trace_type":"Composite","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_QGOH_PQ_6:ME4","text":"The kinds of knowledge are arranged in an asymmetric structure: the first kind does not generate the third, the second opens the transition, and the third intuits singular essences.","refs":["CH_QGOH_PQ_6:A9","CH_QGOH_PQ_6:A10"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"From","passages":[{"id":"RAW_QGOH_PQ_6:P0043","trace_type":"Structural","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"},{"id":"RAW_QGOH_PQ_6:P0053","trace_type":"Structural","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"},{"id":"RAW_QGOH_PQ_6:P0055","trace_type":"Structural","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"},{"id":"RAW_QGOH_PQ_6:P0104","trace_type":"Structural","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_QGOH_PQ_6:ME5","text":"The Body is reclassified from the other of eternity to an instrument for disposing the Mind.","refs":["CH_QGOH_PQ_6:A7","CH_QGOH_PQ_6:A17"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"From","passages":[{"id":"RAW_QGOH_PQ_6:P0085","trace_type":"Structural","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"},{"id":"RAW_QGOH_PQ_6:P0118","trace_type":"Structural","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"},{"id":"RAW_QGOH_PQ_6:P0120","trace_type":"Structural","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]}],"original_name":"Map-Effects"},{"number":"11","name":"Altitude Levels","items":[{"id":"CH_QGOH_PQ_6:ALT1","text":"Surface — God cannot be hated, intellectual Love of God is eternal, and the Mind, insofar as the Body actually endures, imagines and remembers.","refs":["CH_QGOH_PQ_6:A1","CH_QGOH_PQ_6:A7","CH_QGOH_PQ_6:A13"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"From","passages":[{"id":"RAW_QGOH_PQ_6:P0001","trace_type":"Composite","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"},{"id":"RAW_QGOH_PQ_6:P0022","trace_type":"Composite","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"},{"id":"RAW_QGOH_PQ_6:P0079","trace_type":"Composite","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_QGOH_PQ_6:ALT2","text":"Structural — The power of the Mind arises from knowledge, passion from inadequate ideas, and remedies from the causal reordering of affects.","refs":["CH_QGOH_PQ_6:A4","CH_QGOH_PQ_6:A5","CH_QGOH_PQ_6:A6"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"From","passages":[{"id":"RAW_QGOH_PQ_6:P0016","trace_type":"Structural","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"},{"id":"RAW_QGOH_PQ_6:P0018","trace_type":"Structural","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"},{"id":"RAW_QGOH_PQ_6:P0019","trace_type":"Structural","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_QGOH_PQ_6:ALT3","text":"Metastrategic — The question of freedom is reconstituted from voluntary mastery of affects to the production of intellectual actions and to a change in the proportion of the Mind.","refs":["CH_QGOH_PQ_6:A4","CH_QGOH_PQ_6:A5","CH_QGOH_PQ_6:A16"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"From","passages":[{"id":"RAW_QGOH_PQ_6:P0016","trace_type":"Structural","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"},{"id":"RAW_QGOH_PQ_6:P0018","trace_type":"Structural","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"},{"id":"RAW_QGOH_PQ_6:P0112","trace_type":"Structural","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_QGOH_PQ_6:ALT4","text":"Ontological/Temporal — Duration pertains to the actual existence of the Body, eternity to its essence conceived in God, and intellectual Love to the eternal order of this essence.","refs":["CH_QGOH_PQ_6:A7","CH_QGOH_PQ_6:A8","CH_QGOH_PQ_6:A11","CH_QGOH_PQ_6:A13"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"From","passages":[{"id":"RAW_QGOH_PQ_6:P0026","trace_type":"Structural","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"},{"id":"RAW_QGOH_PQ_6:P0032","trace_type":"Structural","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"},{"id":"RAW_QGOH_PQ_6:P0059","trace_type":"Structural","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"},{"id":"RAW_QGOH_PQ_6:P0079","trace_type":"Structural","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]}],"original_name":"Altitude Levels"},{"number":"12","name":"Minimal Reconstruction Test","items":[{"id":"CH_QGOH_PQ_6:MRT1","text":"The extracted system restores the argumentation if the external definitions of adequate ideas, passion, conatus, and the kinds of knowledge have already been granted.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[]},{"id":"CH_QGOH_PQ_6:MRT2","text":"The firmest chain is the connection among the adequate idea of God, the action of the Mind, knowledge of the third kind, joy, and eternal intellectual Love.","refs":["CH_QGOH_PQ_6:A1","CH_QGOH_PQ_6:A11","CH_QGOH_PQ_6:A12","CH_QGOH_PQ_6:A13"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"From","passages":[{"id":"RAW_QGOH_PQ_6:P0001","trace_type":"Structural","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"},{"id":"RAW_QGOH_PQ_6:P0069","trace_type":"Structural","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"},{"id":"RAW_QGOH_PQ_6:P0073","trace_type":"Structural","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"},{"id":"RAW_QGOH_PQ_6:P0077","trace_type":"Structural","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"},{"id":"RAW_QGOH_PQ_6:P0079","trace_type":"Structural","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_QGOH_PQ_6:MRT3","text":"The compression leaves indeterminate how the notions of adequacy, inadequacy, and formal cause can be constituted solely within this passage, because the text receives them from the prior Parts.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[]},{"id":"CH_QGOH_PQ_6:MRT4","text":"A fragile joint remains between the absence of memory before the Body and the assertion that we feel and experience ourselves to be eternal; the transition from demonstration to experience is not independently mechanized here.","refs":["CH_QGOH_PQ_6:D6","CH_QGOH_PQ_6:A11"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"From","passages":[{"id":"RAW_QGOH_PQ_6:P0022","trace_type":"Tension","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"},{"id":"RAW_QGOH_PQ_6:P0034","trace_type":"Tension","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_QGOH_PQ_6:MRT5","text":"A temporal tension remains between intellectual Love without beginning and the narrative by which the Mind gradually becomes more suited to knowledge of the third kind; the text manages this tension through the fiction of temporal exposition, rather than removing it.","refs":["CH_QGOH_PQ_6:A10","CH_QGOH_PQ_6:A13"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"From","passages":[{"id":"RAW_QGOH_PQ_6:P0045","trace_type":"Tension","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"},{"id":"RAW_QGOH_PQ_6:P0079","trace_type":"Tension","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"},{"id":"RAW_QGOH_PQ_6:P0083","trace_type":"Tension","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_QGOH_PQ_6:MRT6","text":"The joint between the aptitude of the Body and the greater eternal part of the Mind depends upon a long chain of ordering affections, relation to God, and increasing love, whose connection is not separately demonstrated here.","refs":["CH_QGOH_PQ_6:A17"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"From","passages":[{"id":"RAW_QGOH_PQ_6:P0118","trace_type":"Structural","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"},{"id":"RAW_QGOH_PQ_6:P0120","trace_type":"Structural","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_QGOH_PQ_6:MRT7","text":"The restoration preserves the movement of the text: remedies for affects → distinction of duration and eternity → intuitive knowledge → intellectual Love of God → freedom and diminution of fear of death.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_QGOH_PQ_6:P0016","trace_type":"Structural","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"},{"id":"RAW_QGOH_PQ_6:P0032","trace_type":"Structural","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"},{"id":"RAW_QGOH_PQ_6:P0043","trace_type":"Structural","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"},{"id":"RAW_QGOH_PQ_6:P0077","trace_type":"Structural","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"},{"id":"RAW_QGOH_PQ_6:P0112","trace_type":"Structural","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"},{"id":"RAW_QGOH_PQ_6:P0120","trace_type":"Structural","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]}],"original_name":"Minimal Reconstruction Test"}],"revision":"CH_QGOH_PQ_6@R001","source_variant_id":"canonical:CH_QGOH_PQ_6:v1","source_language_code":"la"}],"identity":{"layer":"CH","kicker":"Chapter 20","title":"Part Five - On the Power of the Intellect, or Human Freedom · passage 2","context":"Chapter 20","original_title":"Pars quinta - De potentia intellectus seu de libertate humana · passage 2","original_context":"Chapter 20"},"original_title":"Pars quinta - De potentia intellectus seu de libertate humana · passage 2","title_language_code":"en"},{"id":"CH_QGOH_PQ_7","layer":"CH","title":"Part Five - On the Power of the Intellect, or Human Freedom · passage 3","order":21,"parents":["RAW_QGOH_PQ_7"],"revision":"CH_QGOH_PQ_7@R001","sha256":"1146b07ec20b8c04917f97b5883383525cbc6c04885a822173eb5b116d65cfb0","sections":[{"number":"1","name":"Argumental Spine","items":[{"id":"CH_QGOH_PQ_7:AS1","text":"Textus variationem corporis et mentis constituit ut condicionem ex qua felicitas, infelicitas et ordinatio vitae intelligendae sunt.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_QGOH_PQ_7:P0001","trace_type":"Composite","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"},{"id":"RAW_QGOH_PQ_7:P0002","trace_type":"Composite","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_QGOH_PQ_7:AS2","text":"Capacitas corporis ad plura agendum cum ampliore mentis conscientia sui, Dei et rerum conectitur, ita ut transformatio corporis ad intellectum dirigatur.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_QGOH_PQ_7:P0002","trace_type":"Direct","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_QGOH_PQ_7:AS3","text":"Perfectio ex gradu agendi et imminutis passionibus definitur; hinc intellectus aeternus imaginatione pereunte perfectior demonstratur.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_QGOH_PQ_7:P0004","trace_type":"Composite","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"},{"id":"RAW_QGOH_PQ_7:P0006","trace_type":"Composite","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"},{"id":"RAW_QGOH_PQ_7:P0008","trace_type":"Composite","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_QGOH_PQ_7:AS4","text":"Fundamentum virtutis ab cognitione immortalitatis removetur et in rationali quaesitione proprii utilis collocatur.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_QGOH_PQ_7:P0014","trace_type":"Direct","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_QGOH_PQ_7:AS5","text":"Beatitudo non consequens disciplinae affectuum, sed amor intellectualis Dei, virtus ipsa et fons potestatis affectus coercendi constituitur.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_QGOH_PQ_7:P0019","trace_type":"Composite","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"},{"id":"RAW_QGOH_PQ_7:P0021","trace_type":"Composite","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_QGOH_PQ_7:AS6","text":"Textus oppositionem inter ignarum externis causis et libidine actum atque sapientem ex necessitate aeterna intelligentem concludit.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_QGOH_PQ_7:P0023","trace_type":"Direct","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]}],"original_name":"Argumental Spine"},{"number":"2","name":"Load-Bearing Axioms","items":[{"id":"CH_QGOH_PQ_7:A1","text":"Res eo perfectior est quo magis agit et quo minus patitur; perfectio, realitas et potentia agendi eundem gradum exprimunt.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_QGOH_PQ_7:P0004","trace_type":"Direct","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"},{"id":"RAW_QGOH_PQ_7:P0006","trace_type":"Direct","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_QGOH_PQ_7:A2","text":"Intellectus est pars mentis aeterna, imaginatio autem pars quae perire dicitur.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_QGOH_PQ_7:P0008","trace_type":"Direct","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_QGOH_PQ_7:A3","text":"Mens per intellectum agit et per imaginationem patitur.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_QGOH_PQ_7:P0008","trace_type":"Direct","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_QGOH_PQ_7:A4","text":"Unicum fundamentum virtutis seu recte vivendi est proprium utile ratione quaerere.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_QGOH_PQ_7:P0014","trace_type":"Direct","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_QGOH_PQ_7:A5","text":"Praecepta rationis de pietate, religione, animositate et generositate ex cognitione aeternitatis mentis non dependent.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_QGOH_PQ_7:P0012","trace_type":"Composite","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"},{"id":"RAW_QGOH_PQ_7:P0014","trace_type":"Composite","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_QGOH_PQ_7:A6","text":"Beatitudo consistit in amore erga Deum qui ex tertio cognitionis genere oritur.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_QGOH_PQ_7:P0021","trace_type":"Direct","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_QGOH_PQ_7:A7","text":"Amor intellectualis Dei, quatenus ad mentem agentem refertur, ipsa virtus est.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_QGOH_PQ_7:P0021","trace_type":"Direct","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_QGOH_PQ_7:A8","text":"Quo mens beatitudine magis gaudet, eo plus intelligit, maiorem in affectus potentiam habet et minus ab affectibus malis patitur.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_QGOH_PQ_7:P0021","trace_type":"Direct","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_QGOH_PQ_7:A9","text":"Corpus ad plura aptum menti respondet quae amplius sui, Dei et rerum conscia est; vita igitur ad hanc aptitudinem, quantum natura fert, ordinari potest.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_QGOH_PQ_7:P0002","trace_type":"Direct","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_QGOH_PQ_7:A10","text":"Mens, quatenus intelligit, est modus cogitandi aeternus ab aliis modis aeternis in infinitum determinatus.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_QGOH_PQ_7:P0010","trace_type":"Direct","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]}],"original_name":"Load-Bearing Axioms"},{"number":"3","name":"Apophatic Structure","items":[{"id":"CH_QGOH_PQ_7:NX1","text":"Virtus non definitur per spem praemii post mortem nec per metum supplicii post mortem.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_QGOH_PQ_7:P0016","trace_type":"Negative","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_QGOH_PQ_7:NX2","text":"Pietas, religio, animositas et generositas non sunt onera servilia quae solum compensatione ultramortali tolerari possint.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_QGOH_PQ_7:P0016","trace_type":"Negative","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_QGOH_PQ_7:NX3","text":"Beatitudo non est praemium virtutis neque effectus posterior coercitionis libidinum.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_QGOH_PQ_7:P0019","trace_type":"Negative","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_QGOH_PQ_7:NX4","text":"Libertas non est licentia libidini parendi neque imminutio iuris ex sola observantia legis externa recepta.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_QGOH_PQ_7:P0016","trace_type":"Negative","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_QGOH_PQ_7:NX5","text":"Pars mentis imaginativa non est locus eius perfectionis quae post corporis existentiam considerari potest.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_QGOH_PQ_7:P0008","trace_type":"Negative","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_QGOH_PQ_7:NX6","text":"Sapientia non consistit in evadenda necessitate, sed in eius aeternae cognitionis forma.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_QGOH_PQ_7:P0010","trace_type":"Composite","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"},{"id":"RAW_QGOH_PQ_7:P0023","trace_type":"Composite","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]}],"original_name":"Apophatic Structure"},{"number":"4","name":"Derivative Propositions","items":[{"id":"CH_QGOH_PQ_7:D1","text":"Pars mentis quae manet perfectior est reliqua, quia per intellectum agit, dum imaginatio peritura passionum sedes manet.","refs":["CH_QGOH_PQ_7:A1","CH_QGOH_PQ_7:A2","CH_QGOH_PQ_7:A3"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"From","passages":[{"id":"RAW_QGOH_PQ_7:P0008","trace_type":"Direct","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_QGOH_PQ_7:D2","text":"Ratio pietatem et fortitudinem exigere potest etiam sub ignorantia aeternitatis mentis, quia earum norma est utile rationale, non destinatio post mortem.","refs":["CH_QGOH_PQ_7:A4","CH_QGOH_PQ_7:A5"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"From","passages":[{"id":"RAW_QGOH_PQ_7:P0014","trace_type":"Direct","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_QGOH_PQ_7:D3","text":"Coercitio libidinum non producit beatitudinem; beatitudo, intellectum augens, ipsam coercendi potentiam producit.","refs":["CH_QGOH_PQ_7:A6","CH_QGOH_PQ_7:A7","CH_QGOH_PQ_7:A8"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"From","passages":[{"id":"RAW_QGOH_PQ_7:P0019","trace_type":"Direct","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"},{"id":"RAW_QGOH_PQ_7:P0021","trace_type":"Direct","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_QGOH_PQ_7:D4","text":"Vita rationalis non requirit oppugnationem affectuum ut finem primarium, sed incrementum intellectus ex quo imminutio passionum sequitur.","refs":["CH_QGOH_PQ_7:A3","CH_QGOH_PQ_7:A7","CH_QGOH_PQ_7:A8"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"From","passages":[{"id":"RAW_QGOH_PQ_7:P0008","trace_type":"Composite","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"},{"id":"RAW_QGOH_PQ_7:P0021","trace_type":"Composite","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_QGOH_PQ_7:D5","text":"Institutio vitae tendit ad corpus aptius et mentem magis consciam, ut memoria et imaginatio respectu intellectus minorem vim obtineant.","refs":["CH_QGOH_PQ_7:A9","CH_QGOH_PQ_7:A3"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"From","passages":[{"id":"RAW_QGOH_PQ_7:P0002","trace_type":"Direct","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_QGOH_PQ_7:D6","text":"Stabilitas animi sapientis oritur ex intellectione necessitatis, dum ignorantia externarum causarum et libidinum imperio mentem fluctuantem relinquit.","refs":["CH_QGOH_PQ_7:A8","CH_QGOH_PQ_7:A10"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"From","passages":[{"id":"RAW_QGOH_PQ_7:P0021","trace_type":"Composite","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"},{"id":"RAW_QGOH_PQ_7:P0023","trace_type":"Composite","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]}],"original_name":"Derivative Propositions"},{"number":"5","name":"Super-Derivatives","items":[{"id":"CH_QGOH_PQ_7:SD1","text":"Ordo ethicus a ratione mercedis differtur ad immanentiam: virtus suum effectum constitutivum, beatitudinem, intra ipsum exercitium habet.","refs":["CH_QGOH_PQ_7:A4","CH_QGOH_PQ_7:A5","CH_QGOH_PQ_7:A6","CH_QGOH_PQ_7:A7"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"From","passages":[{"id":"RAW_QGOH_PQ_7:P0014","trace_type":"Structural","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"},{"id":"RAW_QGOH_PQ_7:P0019","trace_type":"Structural","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"},{"id":"RAW_QGOH_PQ_7:P0021","trace_type":"Structural","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_QGOH_PQ_7:SD2","text":"Ordo liberationis est epistemicus et affectivus simul: cognitio non affectibus extrinsece additur, sed potentiam activam qua affectus ordinantur efficit.","refs":["CH_QGOH_PQ_7:A1","CH_QGOH_PQ_7:A3","CH_QGOH_PQ_7:A6","CH_QGOH_PQ_7:A8"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"From","passages":[{"id":"RAW_QGOH_PQ_7:P0006","trace_type":"Structural","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"},{"id":"RAW_QGOH_PQ_7:P0008","trace_type":"Structural","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"},{"id":"RAW_QGOH_PQ_7:P0021","trace_type":"Structural","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_QGOH_PQ_7:SD3","text":"Discrimen sapientis et ignari non in possessione futuri praemii, sed in diverso modo praesentis existentiae consistit: alter agit et intelligit, alter patitur et quasi inscius vivit.","refs":["CH_QGOH_PQ_7:A1","CH_QGOH_PQ_7:A3","CH_QGOH_PQ_7:A8","CH_QGOH_PQ_7:A10"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"From","passages":[{"id":"RAW_QGOH_PQ_7:P0016","trace_type":"Composite","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"},{"id":"RAW_QGOH_PQ_7:P0023","trace_type":"Composite","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_QGOH_PQ_7:SD4","text":"Corpus non est mera sarcina mortalitatis, sed condicio practica qua mens ad ampliorem conscientiam et ad intellectus praevalentiam disponi potest.","refs":["CH_QGOH_PQ_7:A2","CH_QGOH_PQ_7:A3","CH_QGOH_PQ_7:A9"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"From","passages":[{"id":"RAW_QGOH_PQ_7:P0002","trace_type":"Composite","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"},{"id":"RAW_QGOH_PQ_7:P0008","trace_type":"Composite","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]}],"original_name":"Super-Derivatives"},{"number":"6","name":"Deep Apophatics","items":[{"id":"CH_QGOH_PQ_7:DNX1","text":"Identitas mentis perfectioris non cum memoria, imaginatione aut historia singulari corporis coincidit.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_QGOH_PQ_7:P0002","trace_type":"Negative","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"},{"id":"RAW_QGOH_PQ_7:P0008","trace_type":"Negative","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_QGOH_PQ_7:DNX2","text":"Bonum vivere non pendet ex promissione compensationis quae vitam praesentem post mortem iustificet.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_QGOH_PQ_7:P0014","trace_type":"Negative","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"},{"id":"RAW_QGOH_PQ_7:P0016","trace_type":"Negative","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_QGOH_PQ_7:DNX3","text":"Affectuum dominium non est actus voluntatis nudae, quia humana potentia ad eos coercendos in solo intellectu ponitur.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_QGOH_PQ_7:P0021","trace_type":"Direct","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_QGOH_PQ_7:DNX4","text":"Externae causae, quatenus mentem passive determinant, veram animi acquiescentiam producere non possunt.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_QGOH_PQ_7:P0023","trace_type":"Direct","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_QGOH_PQ_7:DNX5","text":"Necessitas causalis non destruit libertatem sapientis, sed arbitrium indeterminatum tamquam condicionem libertatis excludit.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_QGOH_PQ_7:P0010","trace_type":"Composite","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"},{"id":"RAW_QGOH_PQ_7:P0023","trace_type":"Composite","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]}],"original_name":"Deep Apophatics"},{"number":"7","name":"Super-Apophatics","items":[{"id":"CH_QGOH_PQ_7:SNX1","text":"Textus excludit oeconomiam moralem in qua virtus sine promisso praemio aut comminatione poenae caret ratione exercendi.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_QGOH_PQ_7:P0016","trace_type":"Negative","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_QGOH_PQ_7:SNX2","text":"Textus excludit conceptionem libertatis ut exemptio a causatione, quia mens intelligens in serie infinita modorum aeternorum determinatur.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_QGOH_PQ_7:P0010","trace_type":"Negative","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_QGOH_PQ_7:SNX3","text":"Textus excludit mortem corporis ut principium absolutum quo omnis esse mentis, omnis veritas vitae et omnis ratio virtutis aboleri debeant.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_QGOH_PQ_7:P0008","trace_type":"Composite","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"},{"id":"RAW_QGOH_PQ_7:P0014","trace_type":"Composite","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"},{"id":"RAW_QGOH_PQ_7:P0019","trace_type":"Composite","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"},{"id":"RAW_QGOH_PQ_7:P0023","trace_type":"Composite","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]}],"original_name":"Super-Apophatics"},{"number":"8","name":"Formal Generative Schema","items":[{"id":"CH_QGOH_PQ_7:FGS1","text":"Variatio corporis → diversus gradus aptitudinis → diversus gradus conscientiae mentis → directio practica ad corpus aptius et intellectum praevalentem.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_QGOH_PQ_7:P0001","trace_type":"Structural","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"},{"id":"RAW_QGOH_PQ_7:P0002","trace_type":"Structural","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_QGOH_PQ_7:FGS2","text":"Perfectio-realitas → maior actio et minor passio → intellectus ut pars activa → eius perfectio prae imaginatione peritura.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_QGOH_PQ_7:P0004","trace_type":"Structural","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"},{"id":"RAW_QGOH_PQ_7:P0006","trace_type":"Structural","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"},{"id":"RAW_QGOH_PQ_7:P0008","trace_type":"Structural","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_QGOH_PQ_7:FGS3","text":"Utile rationale → praecepta virtutis → independentia a doctrina immortalitatis → exclusio spei et metus ut fundamentorum moralium.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_QGOH_PQ_7:P0014","trace_type":"Structural","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"},{"id":"RAW_QGOH_PQ_7:P0016","trace_type":"Structural","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_QGOH_PQ_7:FGS4","text":"Cognitio tertii generis → amor Dei → beatitudo-virtus → augmentum intellectus → imminutio passionum et potentia coercendi libidines.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_QGOH_PQ_7:P0021","trace_type":"Direct","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_QGOH_PQ_7:FGS5","text":"Intellectus necessitatis aeternae → imminutio agitationis → vera animi acquiescentia → figura sapientis contra ignorantem.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_QGOH_PQ_7:P0010","trace_type":"Composite","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"},{"id":"RAW_QGOH_PQ_7:P0023","trace_type":"Composite","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]}],"original_name":"Formal Generative Schema"},{"number":"9","name":"Argument Flow Map","items":[{"id":"CH_QGOH_PQ_7:AF1","text":"Variatio corporis et mentis → aptitudo ad intelligendum → distinctio intellectus et imaginationis → perfectio ut actio → virtus sine praemio ultramortali → beatitudo ut virtus → libertas sapientis sub necessitate.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_QGOH_PQ_7:P0001","trace_type":"Structural","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"},{"id":"RAW_QGOH_PQ_7:P0002","trace_type":"Structural","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"},{"id":"RAW_QGOH_PQ_7:P0008","trace_type":"Structural","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"},{"id":"RAW_QGOH_PQ_7:P0014","trace_type":"Structural","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"},{"id":"RAW_QGOH_PQ_7:P0019","trace_type":"Structural","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"},{"id":"RAW_QGOH_PQ_7:P0023","trace_type":"Structural","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_QGOH_PQ_7:AF2","text":"Spes-metúsque vulgares → falsa ratio pietatis → separatio virtutis ab eschatologia → amor intellectualis Dei → imminutio passionum → animi acquiescentia.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_QGOH_PQ_7:P0016","trace_type":"Structural","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"},{"id":"RAW_QGOH_PQ_7:P0019","trace_type":"Structural","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"},{"id":"RAW_QGOH_PQ_7:P0021","trace_type":"Structural","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"},{"id":"RAW_QGOH_PQ_7:P0023","trace_type":"Structural","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]}],"original_name":"Argument Flow Map"},{"number":"10","name":"Map-Effects","items":[{"id":"CH_QGOH_PQ_7:ME1","text":"Perfectio a qualitate morali externa ad rationem activitatis et passionis reclassificatur.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_QGOH_PQ_7:P0004","trace_type":"Direct","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"},{"id":"RAW_QGOH_PQ_7:P0006","trace_type":"Direct","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_QGOH_PQ_7:ME2","text":"Pietas et religio a regimine praemii-poenae ad normam rationis et proprii utilis transferuntur.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_QGOH_PQ_7:P0014","trace_type":"Direct","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_QGOH_PQ_7:ME3","text":"Beatitudo a termino posteriore disciplinae ad causam immanentem virtutis et potentiae affectivae transfertur.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_QGOH_PQ_7:P0019","trace_type":"Direct","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"},{"id":"RAW_QGOH_PQ_7:P0021","trace_type":"Direct","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_QGOH_PQ_7:ME4","text":"Libertas a licentia libidinis et ab exemptionis causalitate imagine ad stabilitatem intellectus sub necessitate aeterna reordinatur.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_QGOH_PQ_7:P0016","trace_type":"Structural","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"},{"id":"RAW_QGOH_PQ_7:P0010","trace_type":"Structural","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"},{"id":"RAW_QGOH_PQ_7:P0023","trace_type":"Structural","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_QGOH_PQ_7:ME5","text":"Corpus a mero correlato mortalitatis ad condicionem mutabilem progressionis intellectualis reclassificatur.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_QGOH_PQ_7:P0001","trace_type":"Direct","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"},{"id":"RAW_QGOH_PQ_7:P0002","trace_type":"Direct","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]}],"original_name":"Map-Effects"},{"number":"11","name":"Altitude Levels","items":[{"id":"CH_QGOH_PQ_7:ALT1","text":"Surface — Textus declarat partem mentis aeternam perfectiorem esse, virtutem ex immortalitate non pendere, et beatitudinem ipsam virtutem esse.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_QGOH_PQ_7:P0008","trace_type":"Composite","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"},{"id":"RAW_QGOH_PQ_7:P0014","trace_type":"Composite","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"},{"id":"RAW_QGOH_PQ_7:P0019","trace_type":"Composite","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_QGOH_PQ_7:ALT2","text":"Structural — Intellectus, imaginatio, actio, passio, aptitudo corporis et causae externae constituunt mechanismum quo diversae formae vitae producuntur.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_QGOH_PQ_7:P0002","trace_type":"Structural","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"},{"id":"RAW_QGOH_PQ_7:P0008","trace_type":"Structural","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"},{"id":"RAW_QGOH_PQ_7:P0023","trace_type":"Structural","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_QGOH_PQ_7:ALT3","text":"Metastrategic — Argumentatio mutat modum affectus regendi: non metus aut imminutio voluntaria, sed cognitio et beatitudo sunt principium transformationis.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_QGOH_PQ_7:P0016","trace_type":"Composite","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"},{"id":"RAW_QGOH_PQ_7:P0021","trace_type":"Composite","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_QGOH_PQ_7:ALT4","text":"Ontological/Temporal — Mens intelligens in ordine aeterno modorum cogitandi ponitur, dum imaginatio, memoria et corporis existentia ad ordinem transitorium referuntur.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_QGOH_PQ_7:P0002","trace_type":"Composite","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"},{"id":"RAW_QGOH_PQ_7:P0008","trace_type":"Composite","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"},{"id":"RAW_QGOH_PQ_7:P0010","trace_type":"Composite","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]}],"original_name":"Altitude Levels"},{"number":"12","name":"Minimal Reconstruction Test","items":[{"id":"CH_QGOH_PQ_7:MRT1","text":"Axiomata sufficiunt ad restitutionem catenae principalis: perfectio → actio → intellectus → beatitudo → virtus → imminutio passionum.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_QGOH_PQ_7:P0004","trace_type":"Structural","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"},{"id":"RAW_QGOH_PQ_7:P0008","trace_type":"Structural","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"},{"id":"RAW_QGOH_PQ_7:P0014","trace_type":"Structural","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"},{"id":"RAW_QGOH_PQ_7:P0021","trace_type":"Structural","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_QGOH_PQ_7:MRT2","text":"Nexus inter aptitudinem corporis et ampliorem conscientiam mentis affirmatur, sed mechanismus eius intra hunc locum non plene explicatur.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_QGOH_PQ_7:P0001","trace_type":"Direct","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"},{"id":"RAW_QGOH_PQ_7:P0002","trace_type":"Direct","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_QGOH_PQ_7:MRT3","text":"Independentia virtutis ab immortalitate cohaeret cum fundamento utilitatis rationalis, quamvis textus simul cognitionem aeternitatis ad summam sapientis formam pertinere servet.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_QGOH_PQ_7:P0014","trace_type":"Tension","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"},{"id":"RAW_QGOH_PQ_7:P0023","trace_type":"Tension","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_QGOH_PQ_7:MRT4","text":"Structura firmissima est ubi beatitudo causaliter, non remunerative, explicatur ut principium potentiae in affectus.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_QGOH_PQ_7:P0019","trace_type":"Direct","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"},{"id":"RAW_QGOH_PQ_7:P0021","trace_type":"Direct","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_QGOH_PQ_7:MRT5","text":"Commissura fragilis manet inter partem mentis aeternam et singularitatem vitae imaginativae, quia quantitas et modus transitus inter has rationes hic non determinantur.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[]},{"id":"CH_QGOH_PQ_7:MRT6","text":"Restitutio motum textus servat, quia a conditionibus corporis et mentis ad fundamentum virtutis, deinde ad causam immanentem libertatis procedit.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_QGOH_PQ_7:P0001","trace_type":"Structural","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"},{"id":"RAW_QGOH_PQ_7:P0002","trace_type":"Structural","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"},{"id":"RAW_QGOH_PQ_7:P0014","trace_type":"Structural","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"},{"id":"RAW_QGOH_PQ_7:P0019","trace_type":"Structural","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"},{"id":"RAW_QGOH_PQ_7:P0023","trace_type":"Structural","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]}],"original_name":"Minimal Reconstruction Test"}],"language_code":"la","language_name":"Latina","translations":[{"id":"CAREVAR_20261015T014928083592Z_ddc18edb","language_code":"en","language_name":"English","title":"Part Five - On the Power of the Intellect or on Human Freedom · passage 3","source_sha256":"1146b07ec20b8c04917f97b5883383525cbc6c04885a822173eb5b116d65cfb0","sha256":"d9d306ab1ac2d29799232959e8b90f7f9eb6b78b193659675703cba33ff4de19","rendition_type":"machine","review_status":"machine","model":"gpt-5.6-terra","created":"2026-10-15T01:49:28.088591+00:00","sections":[{"number":"1","name":"Argumental Spine","items":[{"id":"CH_QGOH_PQ_7:AS1","text":"The text establishes the variation of body and mind as the condition from which happiness, unhappiness, and the ordering of life are to be understood.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_QGOH_PQ_7:P0001","trace_type":"Composite","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"},{"id":"RAW_QGOH_PQ_7:P0002","trace_type":"Composite","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_QGOH_PQ_7:AS2","text":"The body's capacity for doing more is connected with the mind's broader consciousness of itself, God, and things, so that the transformation of the body is directed toward the intellect.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_QGOH_PQ_7:P0002","trace_type":"Direct","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_QGOH_PQ_7:AS3","text":"Perfection is defined by the degree of acting and by diminished passions; hence the eternal intellect is shown to be more perfect than perishing imagination.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_QGOH_PQ_7:P0004","trace_type":"Composite","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"},{"id":"RAW_QGOH_PQ_7:P0006","trace_type":"Composite","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"},{"id":"RAW_QGOH_PQ_7:P0008","trace_type":"Composite","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_QGOH_PQ_7:AS4","text":"The foundation of virtue is removed from the knowledge of immortality and placed in the rational pursuit of one's own advantage.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_QGOH_PQ_7:P0014","trace_type":"Direct","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_QGOH_PQ_7:AS5","text":"Blessedness is constituted not as a consequence of the discipline of the affects, but as the intellectual love of God, virtue itself, and the source of the power to restrain the affects.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_QGOH_PQ_7:P0019","trace_type":"Composite","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"},{"id":"RAW_QGOH_PQ_7:P0021","trace_type":"Composite","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_QGOH_PQ_7:AS6","text":"The text concludes with the opposition between the ignorant person, driven by external causes and desire, and the wise person, who understands from eternal necessity.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_QGOH_PQ_7:P0023","trace_type":"Direct","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]}],"original_name":"Argumental Spine"},{"number":"2","name":"Load-Bearing Axioms","items":[{"id":"CH_QGOH_PQ_7:A1","text":"A thing is more perfect insofar as it acts more and suffers less; perfection, reality, and power of acting express the same degree.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_QGOH_PQ_7:P0004","trace_type":"Direct","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"},{"id":"RAW_QGOH_PQ_7:P0006","trace_type":"Direct","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_QGOH_PQ_7:A2","text":"The intellect is the eternal part of the mind, whereas imagination is the part said to perish.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_QGOH_PQ_7:P0008","trace_type":"Direct","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_QGOH_PQ_7:A3","text":"The mind acts through the intellect and suffers through imagination.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_QGOH_PQ_7:P0008","trace_type":"Direct","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_QGOH_PQ_7:A4","text":"The sole foundation of virtue, or of living rightly, is rationally to seek one's own advantage.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_QGOH_PQ_7:P0014","trace_type":"Direct","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_QGOH_PQ_7:A5","text":"The precepts of reason concerning piety, religion, courage, and generosity do not depend on knowledge of the eternity of the mind.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_QGOH_PQ_7:P0012","trace_type":"Composite","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"},{"id":"RAW_QGOH_PQ_7:P0014","trace_type":"Composite","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_QGOH_PQ_7:A6","text":"Blessedness consists in love toward God arising from the third kind of knowledge.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_QGOH_PQ_7:P0021","trace_type":"Direct","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_QGOH_PQ_7:A7","text":"The intellectual love of God, insofar as it is referred to the acting mind, is virtue itself.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_QGOH_PQ_7:P0021","trace_type":"Direct","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_QGOH_PQ_7:A8","text":"The more the mind enjoys blessedness, the more it understands, the greater power it has over the affects, and the less it suffers from evil affects.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_QGOH_PQ_7:P0021","trace_type":"Direct","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_QGOH_PQ_7:A9","text":"A body capable of more corresponds to a mind more conscious of itself, God, and things; therefore life can, insofar as nature permits, be ordered toward this capacity.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_QGOH_PQ_7:P0002","trace_type":"Direct","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_QGOH_PQ_7:A10","text":"The mind, insofar as it understands, is an eternal mode of thinking determined to infinity by other eternal modes.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_QGOH_PQ_7:P0010","trace_type":"Direct","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]}],"original_name":"Load-Bearing Axioms"},{"number":"3","name":"Apophatic Structure","items":[{"id":"CH_QGOH_PQ_7:NX1","text":"Virtue is not defined by hope of reward after death or by fear of punishment after death.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_QGOH_PQ_7:P0016","trace_type":"Negative","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_QGOH_PQ_7:NX2","text":"Piety, religion, courage, and generosity are not servile burdens that could be endured only by post-mortem compensation.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_QGOH_PQ_7:P0016","trace_type":"Negative","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_QGOH_PQ_7:NX3","text":"Blessedness is neither the reward of virtue nor a subsequent effect of restraining desires.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_QGOH_PQ_7:P0019","trace_type":"Negative","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_QGOH_PQ_7:NX4","text":"Freedom is neither the license to obey desire nor the diminution of right accepted merely through observance of external law.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_QGOH_PQ_7:P0016","trace_type":"Negative","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_QGOH_PQ_7:NX5","text":"The imaginative part of the mind is not the locus of that perfection which can be considered after the body's existence.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_QGOH_PQ_7:P0008","trace_type":"Negative","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_QGOH_PQ_7:NX6","text":"Wisdom does not consist in escaping necessity, but in its form of eternal knowledge.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_QGOH_PQ_7:P0010","trace_type":"Composite","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"},{"id":"RAW_QGOH_PQ_7:P0023","trace_type":"Composite","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]}],"original_name":"Apophatic Structure"},{"number":"4","name":"Derivative Propositions","items":[{"id":"CH_QGOH_PQ_7:D1","text":"The part of the mind that remains is more perfect than the rest, because it acts through the intellect, whereas perishing imagination remains the seat of passions.","refs":["CH_QGOH_PQ_7:A1","CH_QGOH_PQ_7:A2","CH_QGOH_PQ_7:A3"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"From","passages":[{"id":"RAW_QGOH_PQ_7:P0008","trace_type":"Direct","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_QGOH_PQ_7:D2","text":"Reason can require piety and fortitude even under ignorance of the eternity of the mind, because their norm is rational advantage, not a destiny after death.","refs":["CH_QGOH_PQ_7:A4","CH_QGOH_PQ_7:A5"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"From","passages":[{"id":"RAW_QGOH_PQ_7:P0014","trace_type":"Direct","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_QGOH_PQ_7:D3","text":"The restraint of desires does not produce blessedness; blessedness, by increasing the intellect, produces the very power of restraint.","refs":["CH_QGOH_PQ_7:A6","CH_QGOH_PQ_7:A7","CH_QGOH_PQ_7:A8"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"From","passages":[{"id":"RAW_QGOH_PQ_7:P0019","trace_type":"Direct","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"},{"id":"RAW_QGOH_PQ_7:P0021","trace_type":"Direct","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_QGOH_PQ_7:D4","text":"Rational life does not require the combating of the affects as its primary end, but the increase of the intellect from which the diminution of passions follows.","refs":["CH_QGOH_PQ_7:A3","CH_QGOH_PQ_7:A7","CH_QGOH_PQ_7:A8"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"From","passages":[{"id":"RAW_QGOH_PQ_7:P0008","trace_type":"Composite","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"},{"id":"RAW_QGOH_PQ_7:P0021","trace_type":"Composite","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_QGOH_PQ_7:D5","text":"The ordering of life tends toward a more capable body and a more conscious mind, so that memory and imagination acquire less force in relation to the intellect.","refs":["CH_QGOH_PQ_7:A9","CH_QGOH_PQ_7:A3"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"From","passages":[{"id":"RAW_QGOH_PQ_7:P0002","trace_type":"Direct","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_QGOH_PQ_7:D6","text":"The wise person's steadiness of mind arises from understanding necessity, whereas ignorance of external causes and the rule of desires leaves the mind wavering.","refs":["CH_QGOH_PQ_7:A8","CH_QGOH_PQ_7:A10"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"From","passages":[{"id":"RAW_QGOH_PQ_7:P0021","trace_type":"Composite","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"},{"id":"RAW_QGOH_PQ_7:P0023","trace_type":"Composite","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]}],"original_name":"Derivative Propositions"},{"number":"5","name":"Super-Derivatives","items":[{"id":"CH_QGOH_PQ_7:SD1","text":"The ethical order is shifted from the logic of reward to immanence: virtue has its constitutive effect, blessedness, within its own exercise.","refs":["CH_QGOH_PQ_7:A4","CH_QGOH_PQ_7:A5","CH_QGOH_PQ_7:A6","CH_QGOH_PQ_7:A7"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"From","passages":[{"id":"RAW_QGOH_PQ_7:P0014","trace_type":"Structural","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"},{"id":"RAW_QGOH_PQ_7:P0019","trace_type":"Structural","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"},{"id":"RAW_QGOH_PQ_7:P0021","trace_type":"Structural","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_QGOH_PQ_7:SD2","text":"The order of liberation is simultaneously epistemic and affective: knowledge is not added extrinsically to the affects, but produces the active power by which the affects are ordered.","refs":["CH_QGOH_PQ_7:A1","CH_QGOH_PQ_7:A3","CH_QGOH_PQ_7:A6","CH_QGOH_PQ_7:A8"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"From","passages":[{"id":"RAW_QGOH_PQ_7:P0006","trace_type":"Structural","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"},{"id":"RAW_QGOH_PQ_7:P0008","trace_type":"Structural","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"},{"id":"RAW_QGOH_PQ_7:P0021","trace_type":"Structural","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_QGOH_PQ_7:SD3","text":"The difference between the wise and the ignorant consists not in possession of a future reward, but in a different mode of present existence: the one acts and understands, the other suffers and lives as if unaware.","refs":["CH_QGOH_PQ_7:A1","CH_QGOH_PQ_7:A3","CH_QGOH_PQ_7:A8","CH_QGOH_PQ_7:A10"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"From","passages":[{"id":"RAW_QGOH_PQ_7:P0016","trace_type":"Composite","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"},{"id":"RAW_QGOH_PQ_7:P0023","trace_type":"Composite","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_QGOH_PQ_7:SD4","text":"The body is not a mere burden of mortality, but the practical condition through which the mind can be disposed toward broader consciousness and the predominance of the intellect.","refs":["CH_QGOH_PQ_7:A2","CH_QGOH_PQ_7:A3","CH_QGOH_PQ_7:A9"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"From","passages":[{"id":"RAW_QGOH_PQ_7:P0002","trace_type":"Composite","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"},{"id":"RAW_QGOH_PQ_7:P0008","trace_type":"Composite","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]}],"original_name":"Super-Derivatives"},{"number":"6","name":"Deep Apophatics","items":[{"id":"CH_QGOH_PQ_7:DNX1","text":"The identity of the more perfect mind does not coincide with memory, imagination, or the singular history of the body.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_QGOH_PQ_7:P0002","trace_type":"Negative","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"},{"id":"RAW_QGOH_PQ_7:P0008","trace_type":"Negative","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_QGOH_PQ_7:DNX2","text":"Living well does not depend on the promise of compensation that would justify present life after death.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_QGOH_PQ_7:P0014","trace_type":"Negative","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"},{"id":"RAW_QGOH_PQ_7:P0016","trace_type":"Negative","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_QGOH_PQ_7:DNX3","text":"Mastery of the affects is not an act of bare will, because the human power to restrain them is located in the intellect alone.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_QGOH_PQ_7:P0021","trace_type":"Direct","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_QGOH_PQ_7:DNX4","text":"External causes, insofar as they determine the mind passively, cannot produce true peace of mind.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_QGOH_PQ_7:P0023","trace_type":"Direct","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_QGOH_PQ_7:DNX5","text":"Causal necessity does not destroy the freedom of the wise person, but excludes indeterminate choice as a condition of freedom.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_QGOH_PQ_7:P0010","trace_type":"Composite","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"},{"id":"RAW_QGOH_PQ_7:P0023","trace_type":"Composite","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]}],"original_name":"Deep Apophatics"},{"number":"7","name":"Super-Apophatics","items":[{"id":"CH_QGOH_PQ_7:SNX1","text":"The text excludes a moral economy in which virtue lacks reason to be practiced without a promised reward or a threat of punishment.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_QGOH_PQ_7:P0016","trace_type":"Negative","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_QGOH_PQ_7:SNX2","text":"The text excludes a conception of freedom as exemption from causation, because the understanding mind is determined in an infinite series of eternal modes.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_QGOH_PQ_7:P0010","trace_type":"Negative","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_QGOH_PQ_7:SNX3","text":"The text excludes the death of the body as an absolute principle by which all being of the mind, all truth of life, and all ground of virtue must be abolished.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_QGOH_PQ_7:P0008","trace_type":"Composite","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"},{"id":"RAW_QGOH_PQ_7:P0014","trace_type":"Composite","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"},{"id":"RAW_QGOH_PQ_7:P0019","trace_type":"Composite","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"},{"id":"RAW_QGOH_PQ_7:P0023","trace_type":"Composite","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]}],"original_name":"Super-Apophatics"},{"number":"8","name":"Formal Generative Schema","items":[{"id":"CH_QGOH_PQ_7:FGS1","text":"Variation of the body → different degree of capacity → different degree of consciousness of the mind → practical direction toward a more capable body and a predominant intellect.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_QGOH_PQ_7:P0001","trace_type":"Structural","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"},{"id":"RAW_QGOH_PQ_7:P0002","trace_type":"Structural","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_QGOH_PQ_7:FGS2","text":"Perfection-reality → greater action and lesser passion → intellect as the active part → its perfection over perishing imagination.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_QGOH_PQ_7:P0004","trace_type":"Structural","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"},{"id":"RAW_QGOH_PQ_7:P0006","trace_type":"Structural","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"},{"id":"RAW_QGOH_PQ_7:P0008","trace_type":"Structural","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_QGOH_PQ_7:FGS3","text":"Rational advantage → precepts of virtue → independence from the doctrine of immortality → exclusion of hope and fear as moral foundations.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_QGOH_PQ_7:P0014","trace_type":"Structural","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"},{"id":"RAW_QGOH_PQ_7:P0016","trace_type":"Structural","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_QGOH_PQ_7:FGS4","text":"Knowledge of the third kind → love of God → blessedness-virtue → increase of the intellect → diminution of passions and power to restrain desires.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_QGOH_PQ_7:P0021","trace_type":"Direct","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_QGOH_PQ_7:FGS5","text":"Understanding of eternal necessity → diminution of agitation → true peace of mind → figure of the wise person against the ignorant person.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_QGOH_PQ_7:P0010","trace_type":"Composite","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"},{"id":"RAW_QGOH_PQ_7:P0023","trace_type":"Composite","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]}],"original_name":"Formal Generative Schema"},{"number":"9","name":"Argument Flow Map","items":[{"id":"CH_QGOH_PQ_7:AF1","text":"Variation of body and mind → capacity for understanding → distinction of intellect and imagination → perfection as action → virtue without post-mortem reward → blessedness as virtue → freedom of the wise under necessity.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_QGOH_PQ_7:P0001","trace_type":"Structural","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"},{"id":"RAW_QGOH_PQ_7:P0002","trace_type":"Structural","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"},{"id":"RAW_QGOH_PQ_7:P0008","trace_type":"Structural","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"},{"id":"RAW_QGOH_PQ_7:P0014","trace_type":"Structural","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"},{"id":"RAW_QGOH_PQ_7:P0019","trace_type":"Structural","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"},{"id":"RAW_QGOH_PQ_7:P0023","trace_type":"Structural","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_QGOH_PQ_7:AF2","text":"Common hope and fear → false ground of piety → separation of virtue from eschatology → intellectual love of God → diminution of passions → peace of mind.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_QGOH_PQ_7:P0016","trace_type":"Structural","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"},{"id":"RAW_QGOH_PQ_7:P0019","trace_type":"Structural","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"},{"id":"RAW_QGOH_PQ_7:P0021","trace_type":"Structural","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"},{"id":"RAW_QGOH_PQ_7:P0023","trace_type":"Structural","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]}],"original_name":"Argument Flow Map"},{"number":"10","name":"Map-Effects","items":[{"id":"CH_QGOH_PQ_7:ME1","text":"Perfection is reclassified from an external moral quality to the ratio of activity and passion.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_QGOH_PQ_7:P0004","trace_type":"Direct","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"},{"id":"RAW_QGOH_PQ_7:P0006","trace_type":"Direct","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_QGOH_PQ_7:ME2","text":"Piety and religion are transferred from the regime of reward and punishment to the norm of reason and one's own advantage.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_QGOH_PQ_7:P0014","trace_type":"Direct","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_QGOH_PQ_7:ME3","text":"Blessedness is transferred from the subsequent terminus of discipline to the immanent cause of virtue and affective power.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_QGOH_PQ_7:P0019","trace_type":"Direct","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"},{"id":"RAW_QGOH_PQ_7:P0021","trace_type":"Direct","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_QGOH_PQ_7:ME4","text":"Freedom is reordered from the image of the license of desire and causal exemption to the stability of the intellect under eternal necessity.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_QGOH_PQ_7:P0016","trace_type":"Structural","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"},{"id":"RAW_QGOH_PQ_7:P0010","trace_type":"Structural","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"},{"id":"RAW_QGOH_PQ_7:P0023","trace_type":"Structural","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_QGOH_PQ_7:ME5","text":"The body is reclassified from a mere correlate of mortality to a mutable condition of intellectual advancement.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_QGOH_PQ_7:P0001","trace_type":"Direct","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"},{"id":"RAW_QGOH_PQ_7:P0002","trace_type":"Direct","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]}],"original_name":"Map-Effects"},{"number":"11","name":"Altitude Levels","items":[{"id":"CH_QGOH_PQ_7:ALT1","text":"Surface — The text declares that the eternal part of the mind is more perfect, that virtue does not depend on immortality, and that blessedness is virtue itself.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_QGOH_PQ_7:P0008","trace_type":"Composite","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"},{"id":"RAW_QGOH_PQ_7:P0014","trace_type":"Composite","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"},{"id":"RAW_QGOH_PQ_7:P0019","trace_type":"Composite","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_QGOH_PQ_7:ALT2","text":"Structural — Intellect, imagination, action, passion, bodily capacity, and external causes constitute the mechanism through which different forms of life are produced.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_QGOH_PQ_7:P0002","trace_type":"Structural","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"},{"id":"RAW_QGOH_PQ_7:P0008","trace_type":"Structural","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"},{"id":"RAW_QGOH_PQ_7:P0023","trace_type":"Structural","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_QGOH_PQ_7:ALT3","text":"Metastrategic — The argument changes the mode of governing the affects: not fear or voluntary diminution, but knowledge and blessedness are the principle of transformation.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_QGOH_PQ_7:P0016","trace_type":"Composite","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"},{"id":"RAW_QGOH_PQ_7:P0021","trace_type":"Composite","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_QGOH_PQ_7:ALT4","text":"Ontological/Temporal — The understanding mind is placed in the eternal order of modes of thinking, whereas imagination, memory, and bodily existence are referred to the transitory order.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_QGOH_PQ_7:P0002","trace_type":"Composite","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"},{"id":"RAW_QGOH_PQ_7:P0008","trace_type":"Composite","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"},{"id":"RAW_QGOH_PQ_7:P0010","trace_type":"Composite","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]}],"original_name":"Altitude Levels"},{"number":"12","name":"Minimal Reconstruction Test","items":[{"id":"CH_QGOH_PQ_7:MRT1","text":"The axioms suffice for the reconstruction of the principal chain: perfection → action → intellect → blessedness → virtue → diminution of passions.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_QGOH_PQ_7:P0004","trace_type":"Structural","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"},{"id":"RAW_QGOH_PQ_7:P0008","trace_type":"Structural","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"},{"id":"RAW_QGOH_PQ_7:P0014","trace_type":"Structural","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"},{"id":"RAW_QGOH_PQ_7:P0021","trace_type":"Structural","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_QGOH_PQ_7:MRT2","text":"The connection between bodily capacity and the mind's broader consciousness is affirmed, but its mechanism is not fully explained within this passage.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_QGOH_PQ_7:P0001","trace_type":"Direct","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"},{"id":"RAW_QGOH_PQ_7:P0002","trace_type":"Direct","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_QGOH_PQ_7:MRT3","text":"The independence of virtue from immortality coheres with the foundation of rational advantage, although the text simultaneously preserves knowledge of eternity as pertaining to the highest form of the wise person.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_QGOH_PQ_7:P0014","trace_type":"Tension","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"},{"id":"RAW_QGOH_PQ_7:P0023","trace_type":"Tension","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_QGOH_PQ_7:MRT4","text":"The strongest structure is where blessedness is explained causally, not remuneratively, as the principle of power over the affects.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_QGOH_PQ_7:P0019","trace_type":"Direct","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"},{"id":"RAW_QGOH_PQ_7:P0021","trace_type":"Direct","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_QGOH_PQ_7:MRT5","text":"A fragile juncture remains between the eternal part of the mind and the singularity of imaginative life, because the quantity and mode of transition between these aspects are not determined here.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[]},{"id":"CH_QGOH_PQ_7:MRT6","text":"The reconstruction preserves the movement of the text, because it proceeds from the conditions of body and mind to the foundation of virtue, then to the immanent cause of freedom.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_QGOH_PQ_7:P0001","trace_type":"Structural","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"},{"id":"RAW_QGOH_PQ_7:P0002","trace_type":"Structural","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"},{"id":"RAW_QGOH_PQ_7:P0014","trace_type":"Structural","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"},{"id":"RAW_QGOH_PQ_7:P0019","trace_type":"Structural","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"},{"id":"RAW_QGOH_PQ_7:P0023","trace_type":"Structural","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]}],"original_name":"Minimal Reconstruction Test"}],"revision":"CH_QGOH_PQ_7@R001","source_variant_id":"canonical:CH_QGOH_PQ_7:v1","source_language_code":"la"}],"identity":{"layer":"CH","kicker":"Chapter 21","title":"Part Five - On the Power of the Intellect, or Human Freedom · passage 3","context":"Chapter 21","original_title":"Pars quinta - De potentia intellectus seu de libertate humana · passage 3","original_context":"Chapter 21"},"original_title":"Pars quinta - De potentia intellectus seu de libertate humana · passage 3","title_language_code":"en"},{"id":"CL_QGOH_01","layer":"CL","title":"Chapters 01–03","order":1,"parents":["CH_QGOH_PPDD","CH_QGOH_PP_2","CH_QGOH_PP_3"],"revision":"CL_QGOH_01@R001","sha256":"a5c1a517e3ebd985167a5b463355e8849573250fbe58469194a88fa2996de65e","sections":[{"number":"1","name":"Book-Scale Argumental Spine","items":[{"id":"CL_QGOH_01:BAS-01","text":"Quaestio centralis est quomodo substantia necessario existens simul principium immanens omnium modorum fiat, sine exteriori causa, arbitrio contingenti aut ordine rerum alternativo.","refs":["CH_QGOH_PPDD:AS1","CH_QGOH_PP_3:AS1"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[]},{"id":"CL_QGOH_01:BAS-02","text":"Thesis globalis affirmat unicam substantiam divinam, cuius essentia existentiam et potentiam involvit, omnia entia intra se continere atque necessario determinare.","refs":["CH_QGOH_PPDD:AS6","CH_QGOH_PP_3:A8"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[]},{"id":"CL_QGOH_01:BAS-03","text":"Argumentatio geometrica distinctiones ontologicas in exclusiones convertit, exclusiones in causalitatem necessariam, causalitatem autem in diagnosin errorum imaginationis et appetitus.","refs":["CH_QGOH_PPDD:AS3","CH_QGOH_PP_3:D12"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[]},{"id":"CL_QGOH_01:BAS-04","text":"Motus a substantiae improductibilitate ad causalitatem sui procedit, deinde infinitatem staticam in productivitatem infinitam naturae divinae transformat.","refs":["CH_QGOH_PPDD:AS4","CH_QGOH_PP_2:D3"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[]},{"id":"CL_QGOH_01:BAS-05","text":"Productivitas infinita per modos infinitos et concatenationes finitas distribuitur, donec necessitas ontologica in criticam contingentiae, voluntatis liberae et finalismi terminetur.","refs":["CH_QGOH_PP_3:AS3","CH_QGOH_PP_3:AS7"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[]},{"id":"CL_QGOH_01:BAS-06","text":"Motus est cumulativus et spiralis: omnis exclusio exterioritatis redit ut ulterior clausura causalis, modalis, epistemica vel teleologica.","refs":["CH_QGOH_PPDD:NX5","CH_QGOH_PP_3:NX4"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[]},{"id":"CL_QGOH_01:BAS-07","text":"Systema a constitutione entis ad unicitatem ordinis progreditur, tum ordinem ipsum contra interpretationes voluntaristicas et finales tuetur.","refs":["CH_QGOH_PP_3:AS6","CH_QGOH_PP_3:SD6"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[]},{"id":"CL_QGOH_01:BAS-08","text":"Clausura producta est immanens et actualistica: neque extra Deum, neque praeter ordinem actualem, neque intra contingentiam onticam spatium residuum relinquitur.","refs":["CH_QGOH_PP_2:SNX1","CH_QGOH_PP_3:SNX4"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[]}],"original_name":"Book-Scale Argumental Spine"},{"number":"2","name":"Global Load-Bearing Axioms","items":[{"id":"CL_QGOH_01:GA-01","text":"[Persistent] Omne ens aut substantia est aut modus; tertium genus ontologicum quo dependentia immanens effugiatur non datur.","refs":["CH_QGOH_PPDD:A1","CH_QGOH_PP_3:A6"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[]},{"id":"CL_QGOH_01:GA-02","text":"[Persistent] Deus est unica substantia absolute infinita, causa sui, cuius essentia ab existentia separari non potest.","refs":["CH_QGOH_PPDD:A11","CH_QGOH_PPDD:D15"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[]},{"id":"CL_QGOH_01:GA-03","text":"[Mutating] Attributa essentiam substantiae exprimunt sine eius divisione; multiplicitas eorum fit principium differentiationis internae, non pluralitatis substantialis.","refs":["CH_QGOH_PPDD:A5","CH_QGOH_PPDD:SD3"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[]},{"id":"CL_QGOH_01:GA-04","text":"[Persistent] Modi sunt affectiones substantiae quae in Deo sunt, per Deum concipiuntur atque ab eo quoad essentiam, existentiam et operationem dependent.","refs":["CH_QGOH_PPDD:A3","CH_QGOH_PP_3:A7"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[]},{"id":"CL_QGOH_01:GA-05","text":"[Persistent] Ex causa determinata effectus necessario sequitur; causalitas vera nullam indifferentiam inter causam datam et effectum admittit.","refs":["CH_QGOH_PPDD:A6","CH_QGOH_PP_2:A8"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[]},{"id":"CL_QGOH_01:GA-06","text":"[Mutating] Infinitas a negatione termini homogenei transit ad plenitudinem productivam qua infinita infinitis modis necessario consequuntur.","refs":["CH_QGOH_PPDD:SD2","CH_QGOH_PP_2:D3"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[]},{"id":"CL_QGOH_01:GA-07","text":"[Mutating] Libertas non est electio inter possibilia, sed actio ex sola necessitate propriae naturae sine determinatione externa.","refs":["CH_QGOH_PP_2:A9","CH_QGOH_PP_3:SD2"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[]},{"id":"CL_QGOH_01:GA-08","text":"[Emergent] Causalitas divina est immanens: effectus non extra causam constituuntur, sed intra eandem naturam ut expressiones attributorum permanent.","refs":["CH_QGOH_PP_2:D5","CH_QGOH_PP_3:SD1"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[]},{"id":"CL_QGOH_01:GA-09","text":"[Emergent] Productio modalis duplicem ordinem format: modi infiniti ex absoluta natura attributorum sequuntur, modi finiti per modificationes finitas mediantur.","refs":["CH_QGOH_PP_2:D7","CH_QGOH_PP_3:A4"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[]},{"id":"CL_QGOH_01:GA-10","text":"[Terminal] Omnis res finita ad existendum et operandum ab alia causa finita determinatur, nullo primo termino finito admisso.","refs":["CH_QGOH_PP_2:A12","CH_QGOH_PP_3:A5"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[]},{"id":"CL_QGOH_01:GA-11","text":"[Terminal] Potentia divina est ipsa essentia divina; ideo ordo actualis non est electus ex ordine possibilitatum non actualium.","refs":["CH_QGOH_PP_3:A15","CH_QGOH_PP_3:D11"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[]},{"id":"CL_QGOH_01:GA-12","text":"[Terminal] Intellectus et voluntas, etiam infinite concepta, sunt modi determinati, non facultates originariae supra naturam positae.","refs":["CH_QGOH_PP_3:A11","CH_QGOH_PP_3:D6"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[]},{"id":"CL_QGOH_01:GA-13","text":"[Emergent] Necessitas pertinet ad res et causas; contingentia, possibilitas et finalitas oriuntur ex cognitione causali defectiva.","refs":["CH_QGOH_PP_3:A14","CH_QGOH_PP_3:D8"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[]},{"id":"CL_QGOH_01:GA-14","text":"[Attractor] Intelligibilitas ad essentias, proprietates et causas convergit, dum imaginatio mensurans et appetitus conscius falsa exemplaria ontologica producunt.","refs":["CH_QGOH_PP_2:AS3","CH_QGOH_PP_3:A17","CH_QGOH_PP_3:A18"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[]}],"original_name":"Global Load-Bearing Axioms"},{"number":"3","name":"Recurrence, Mutation, and Displacement Matrix","items":[{"id":"CL_QGOH_01:RMD-01","text":"Substantia identitatem ontologicam retinet, sed a termino independentiae ad principium causaliter activum totius naturae promovetur.","refs":["CH_QGOH_PPDD:A2","CH_QGOH_PP_2:D4"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[]},{"id":"CL_QGOH_01:RMD-02","text":"Infinitas functionem mutat: primum terminum et negationem excludit, deinde productionem effectuum infinitis modis affirmat.","refs":["CH_QGOH_PPDD:SD2","CH_QGOH_PP_2:D3"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[]},{"id":"CL_QGOH_01:RMD-03","text":"Attributum ab indice essentiae ad canalem productivum transit, per quem modi infiniti et finiti ordinate consequuntur.","refs":["CH_QGOH_PPDD:A5","CH_QGOH_PP_2:D7"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[]},{"id":"CL_QGOH_01:RMD-04","text":"Formula qua omnia in Deo sunt amplificatur ab inexistentia ontologica ad clausuram causalitatis, conceptionis et determinationis.","refs":["CH_QGOH_PPDD:D16","CH_QGOH_PP_3:SD1"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[]},{"id":"CL_QGOH_01:RMD-05","text":"Libertas inversionem patitur: indifferentia repudiata est, dum necessitas sui ipsius nomen libertatis recipit.","refs":["CH_QGOH_PP_2:NX4","CH_QGOH_PP_3:SD2"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[]},{"id":"CL_QGOH_01:RMD-06","text":"Intellectus primum imaginationi opponitur ut modus rectae conceptionis, deinde ipse inter modos Naturae Naturatae reponitur.","refs":["CH_QGOH_PP_2:AS3","CH_QGOH_PP_3:A11"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[]},{"id":"CL_QGOH_01:RMD-07","text":"Aeternitas ab infinita duratione separata manet, sed eius oppositum nunc non tempus externum, verum series interna determinationum finitarum est.","refs":["CH_QGOH_PPDD:A13","CH_QGOH_PP_2:ME5"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[]},{"id":"CL_QGOH_01:RMD-08","text":"Contingentia ex categoria rei expellitur et ad statum epistemicum cognitionis incompletae transfertur.","refs":["CH_QGOH_PP_3:NX9","CH_QGOH_PP_3:ME2"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[]},{"id":"CL_QGOH_01:RMD-09","text":"Anthropomorphismus a simplici confusione naturae divinae cum affectibus humanis ad genealogiam finalismi et superstitionis extenditur.","refs":["CH_QGOH_PPDD:NX7","CH_QGOH_PP_3:D12"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[]}],"original_name":"Recurrence, Mutation, and Displacement Matrix"},{"number":"4","name":"Lower-Layer vs Current-Level Distinction","items":[{"id":"CL_QGOH_01:CGD-01","text":"Demonstrationes locales contra pluralitatem substantiarum hoc gradu absorbentur in propositionem latiorem nullam exterioritatem substantialem vel causalem dari.","refs":["CH_QGOH_PPDD:D5","CH_QGOH_PPDD:D15"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[]},{"id":"CL_QGOH_01:CGD-02","text":"Refutatio compositionis extensionis manet casus localis, sed distinctio imaginationis et intellectus fit mechanismus generalis ad pseudo-contradictiones ontologicas detegendas.","refs":["CH_QGOH_PP_2:D1","CH_QGOH_PP_2:ALT2"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[]},{"id":"CL_QGOH_01:CGD-03","text":"Distinctio modorum infinitarum et finitarum est architectura inferior mediationis; hoc gradu ea coniungit productivitatem aeternam cum determinatione singularium.","refs":["CH_QGOH_PP_2:D7","CH_QGOH_PP_3:D1"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[]},{"id":"CL_QGOH_01:CGD-04","text":"Distinctio Naturae Naturantis et Naturae Naturatae non est altera divisio substantiae, sed instrumentum praesentis gradus quo causalitas et dependentia sine transcendentia distinguuntur.","refs":["CH_QGOH_PP_3:A9","CH_QGOH_PP_3:D4"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[]},{"id":"CL_QGOH_01:CGD-05","text":"Difficultas localis transitus a non-productione substantiae ad causalitatem sui superatur structuraliter per clausuram immanentem, non per introductionem causae ulterioris.","refs":["CH_QGOH_PPDD:MRT3","CH_QGOH_PP_3:SD1"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[]},{"id":"CL_QGOH_01:CGD-06","text":"Quaestiones locales de intellectu et voluntate divina cedunt propositioni globali qua omnis facultas mentalis inter modos causatos collocatur.","refs":["CH_QGOH_PP_2:D9","CH_QGOH_PP_3:SD4"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[]},{"id":"CL_QGOH_01:CGD-07","text":"Genealogia finalismi, quamvis appendicis forma procedat, praesentem structuram claudit ostendendo quomodo contrarium causalitatis necessariae ab ipsa condicione cognitiva humana generetur.","refs":["CH_QGOH_PP_3:D12","CH_QGOH_PP_3:D13"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[]}],"original_name":"Lower-Layer vs Current-Level Distinction"},{"number":"5","name":"Global Apophatic Structure","items":[{"id":"CL_QGOH_01:GNX-01","text":"Nulla substantia, causa aut condicio intelligibilitatis extra Deum admittitur.","refs":["CH_QGOH_PPDD:SNX1","CH_QGOH_PP_2:SNX1"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"Negative","passages":[]},{"id":"CL_QGOH_01:GNX-02","text":"Substantia infinita nec ex partibus realibus componitur nec per vacuum, mensuram aut divisionem imaginationis explicatur.","refs":["CH_QGOH_PP_2:NX3","CH_QGOH_PP_2:DNX1"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"Negative","passages":[]},{"id":"CL_QGOH_01:GNX-03","text":"Deus non est artifex transiens cuius effectus extra ipsum consistant.","refs":["CH_QGOH_PP_2:NX6","CH_QGOH_PP_3:DNX2"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"Negative","passages":[]},{"id":"CL_QGOH_01:GNX-04","text":"Actio divina nec ex decreto contingenti nec ex deliberatione inter ordines possibiles oritur.","refs":["CH_QGOH_PP_2:NX5","CH_QGOH_PP_3:SNX1"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"Negative","passages":[]},{"id":"CL_QGOH_01:GNX-05","text":"Res finita neque causa sui neque subiectum voluntarium causaliter autonomum est.","refs":["CH_QGOH_PP_2:NX7","CH_QGOH_PP_3:DNX3"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"Negative","passages":[]},{"id":"CL_QGOH_01:GNX-06","text":"Contingentia ontica et finis humanus tamquam principia Naturae simul excluduntur.","refs":["CH_QGOH_PP_3:NX9","CH_QGOH_PP_3:NX10"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"Negative","passages":[]}],"original_name":"Global Apophatic Structure"},{"number":"6","name":"Deep Book Apophatics","items":[{"id":"CL_QGOH_01:DBN-01","text":"Systema requirit ut falsa sit separabilitas essentiae substantiae ab eius existentia; aliter necessitas Dei et tota dependentia modalis corruerent.","refs":["CH_QGOH_PPDD:DNX2"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"Negative","passages":[]},{"id":"CL_QGOH_01:DBN-02","text":"Falsa esse debet potentia divina quae possibilitates inexercitas reservet, quia hiatus inter essentiam, potentiam et actualitatem ordinem necessarium solveret.","refs":["CH_QGOH_PP_2:SNX2"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"Negative","passages":[]},{"id":"CL_QGOH_01:DBN-03","text":"Nulla prima causa finita admitti potest; eius admissio concatenationem modalem interrumperet et finitum in substantiam latentem converteret.","refs":["CH_QGOH_PP_3:DNX1"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"Negative","passages":[]},{"id":"CL_QGOH_01:DBN-04","text":"Facultas mentalis extra ordinem naturalem posita falsa esse debet, quia privilegium causale intellectus aut voluntatis clausuram immanentem frangeret.","refs":["CH_QGOH_PP_3:SD4"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"Negative","passages":[]},{"id":"CL_QGOH_01:DBN-05","text":"Utilitas, bonum et finis humanus normam veritatis naturae praebere non possunt; cognitio debet per essentias, proprietates et causas procedere.","refs":["CH_QGOH_PP_3:DNX6","CH_QGOH_PP_3:A18"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"Negative","passages":[]}],"original_name":"Deep Book Apophatics"},{"number":"7","name":"Super-Apophatics of the Whole","items":[{"id":"CL_QGOH_01:BSA-01","text":"Absentia ordinans est ipsum “extra”: systema nullum locum ontologicum, causalem vel epistemicum extra immanentiam concedere potest.","refs":["CH_QGOH_PP_2:SNX1"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"Negative","passages":[]},{"id":"CL_QGOH_01:BSA-02","text":"Possibilitas indisponibilis est mundus vere alternativus; alter ordo non variatio huius naturae, sed contradictoria substitutio alterius naturae divinae esset.","refs":["CH_QGOH_PP_3:DNX4"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"Negative","passages":[]},{"id":"CL_QGOH_01:BSA-03","text":"Nulla norma transcendens veritatis, causalitatis aut bonitatis super naturam stat, ex qua ordo actualis iudicari vel eligi possit.","refs":["CH_QGOH_PP_3:SNX2"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"Negative","passages":[]},{"id":"CL_QGOH_01:BSA-04","text":"Ruptura causalis ontica praecluditur; quod ut casus, possibilitas aut contingentia apparet defectum cognitionis, non hiatum realitatis, indicat.","refs":["CH_QGOH_PP_3:SNX4"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"Negative","passages":[]}],"original_name":"Super-Apophatics of the Whole"},{"number":"8","name":"Cross-Unit Derivative Propositions","items":[{"id":"CL_QGOH_01:XD-01","text":"Unicitas substantiae cum productivitate infinita coniuncta creationem ut eventum externum tollit et eam in expressionem aeternam naturae convertit.","refs":["CL_QGOH_01:GA-02","CL_QGOH_01:GA-06","CL_QGOH_01:GA-08","CH_QGOH_PPDD:D15","CH_QGOH_PP_2:D3"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"From","passages":[]},{"id":"CL_QGOH_01:XD-02","text":"Series causarum finitarum non est pons inter Deum et mundum, sed articulatio interna causalitatis immanentis.","refs":["CL_QGOH_01:GA-04","CL_QGOH_01:GA-08","CL_QGOH_01:GA-10","CH_QGOH_PPDD:D16","CH_QGOH_PP_2:D5","CH_QGOH_PP_3:D1"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"From","passages":[]},{"id":"CL_QGOH_01:XD-03","text":"Infinitas et omnipotentia convergunt in actualitatem exhaustivam: potentia divina neque mensuratur neque ut copia effectuum nondum productorum reservatur.","refs":["CL_QGOH_01:GA-06","CL_QGOH_01:GA-11","CH_QGOH_PPDD:SD2","CH_QGOH_PP_2:SD3","CH_QGOH_PP_3:A15"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"From","passages":[]},{"id":"CL_QGOH_01:XD-04","text":"Cum libertas per necessitatem propriae naturae definiatur et voluntas inter modos causatos reponatur, libertas originaria soli causae immanenti convenit, non facultati eligenti.","refs":["CL_QGOH_01:GA-07","CL_QGOH_01:GA-12","CH_QGOH_PP_2:A9","CH_QGOH_PP_3:D6"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"From","passages":[]},{"id":"CL_QGOH_01:XD-05","text":"Multiplicitas attributorum cum extensione divina coniuncta materiam ab ordine substantiae independentis removet et in differentiationem internam unius naturae reclassificat.","refs":["CL_QGOH_01:GA-03","CL_QGOH_01:GA-08","CH_QGOH_PPDD:D12","CH_QGOH_PP_2:NX2"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"From","passages":[]},{"id":"CL_QGOH_01:XD-06","text":"Asymmetria inter substantiam, cuius essentia existentiam involvit, et modos, quorum essentia eam non involvit, perseverantiam modalem in causalitatem continuam convertit.","refs":["CL_QGOH_01:GA-02","CL_QGOH_01:GA-04","CL_QGOH_01:GA-05","CH_QGOH_PPDD:SD6","CH_QGOH_PP_2:A10","CH_QGOH_PP_2:D6"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"From","passages":[]},{"id":"CL_QGOH_01:XD-07","text":"Exclusio ordinis alterius et translatio contingentiae ad cognitionem efficiunt ut possibilitas sit relatio ad ignorantiam causae, non status incompletus rei.","refs":["CL_QGOH_01:GA-11","CL_QGOH_01:GA-13","CH_QGOH_PP_3:A13","CH_QGOH_PP_3:D8"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"From","passages":[]},{"id":"CL_QGOH_01:XD-08","text":"Mensura partium et proiectio finium sunt formae eiusdem erroris: structurae modales imaginationis aut appetitus in naturam substantiae transferuntur.","refs":["CL_QGOH_01:GA-13","CL_QGOH_01:GA-14","CH_QGOH_PP_2:A5","CH_QGOH_PP_3:A17","CH_QGOH_PP_3:D13"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"From","passages":[]}],"original_name":"Cross-Unit Derivative Propositions"},{"number":"9","name":"Book-Scale Super-Derivatives","items":[{"id":"CL_QGOH_01:BSD-01","text":"Realitas praesentis systematis est actualismus immanens: possibilitas realis potentiam divinam non excedit, et potentia divina ab essentia actuali non separatur.","refs":["CL_QGOH_01:GA-08","CL_QGOH_01:GA-11","CH_QGOH_PP_2:SD3","CH_QGOH_PP_3:A15"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"From","passages":[]},{"id":"CL_QGOH_01:BSD-02","text":"Tempus non continet naturam divinam nec decretum ante productionem; aeternitas est necessitas essentiae, dum successio pertinet ad ordinem modorum finitorum.","refs":["CL_QGOH_01:GA-02","CL_QGOH_01:GA-09","CL_QGOH_01:GA-10","CH_QGOH_PPDD:SD6","CH_QGOH_PP_3:ALT4"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"From","passages":[]},{"id":"CL_QGOH_01:BSD-03","text":"Subiectum finitum fit nodus causalis intra seriem determinationum, non centrum transcendentale unde actus incipiant.","refs":["CL_QGOH_01:GA-10","CL_QGOH_01:GA-12","CH_QGOH_PP_3:D1","CH_QGOH_PP_3:SD4"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"From","passages":[]},{"id":"CL_QGOH_01:BSD-04","text":"Ignorantia non constituit indeterminationem rei, sed machinam quae necessitatem in contingentiam et causalitatem in finalitatem apparentem transformat.","refs":["CL_QGOH_01:GA-13","CL_QGOH_01:GA-14","CH_QGOH_PP_3:SD3","CH_QGOH_PP_3:SD6"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"From","passages":[]},{"id":"CL_QGOH_01:BSD-05","text":"Agere non significat seriem causarum absolute incipere, sed potentiam naturae sub certa determinatione exprimere et effectus ulteriores producere.","refs":["CL_QGOH_01:GA-04","CL_QGOH_01:GA-10","CL_QGOH_01:GA-11","CH_QGOH_PP_3:A16","CH_QGOH_PP_3:NX3"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"From","passages":[]},{"id":"CL_QGOH_01:BSD-06","text":"Methodus congrua huic ontologiae non quaerit cui fini res serviat, sed ex qua essentia, proprietate et concatenatione causarum necessario sequatur.","refs":["CL_QGOH_01:GA-05","CL_QGOH_01:GA-14","CH_QGOH_PP_3:A18","CH_QGOH_PP_3:D13"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"From","passages":[]}],"original_name":"Book-Scale Super-Derivatives"},{"number":"10","name":"Recursive Loops, Escalation Paths, and Conceptual Attractors","items":[{"id":"CL_QGOH_01:RLE-01","text":"Partitio entis in substantiam et modos dependentiam conceptualem instituit; dependentia conceptualis causalitatem immanentem requirit; causalitas immanens partitionem initialem claudit.","refs":["CH_QGOH_PPDD:A1","CH_QGOH_PPDD:D16"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[]},{"id":"CL_QGOH_01:RLE-02","text":"Exclusio substantiae exterioris causam externam tollit; causalitas sine exterioritate ordinem alternativum excludit; exclusio alternativae iterum unicitatem substantiae confirmat.","refs":["CH_QGOH_PPDD:D15","CH_QGOH_PP_3:SD1"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[]},{"id":"CL_QGOH_01:RLE-03","text":"Attributa per se concepta differentiam augent sine substantiam dividendo; eadem differentia productivitatem infinitam unius naturae amplificat.","refs":["CH_QGOH_PPDD:D12","CH_QGOH_PP_2:D3"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[]},{"id":"CL_QGOH_01:RLE-04","text":"Quaelibet causa finita ad aliam causam finitam remittit, unde regressus non defectum explicationis sed formam recursionis modalis efficit.","refs":["CH_QGOH_PP_3:A5","CH_QGOH_PP_3:D1"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[]},{"id":"CL_QGOH_01:RLE-05","text":"Imaginatio partes et fines fingit, ex fictionibus contradictiones vel utilitates elicit, deinde easdem fictiones pro structura naturae accipit.","refs":["CH_QGOH_PP_2:A5","CH_QGOH_PP_2:SD4"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[]},{"id":"CL_QGOH_01:RLE-06","text":"Ignorantia causarum opinionem libertatis parit; opinio libertatis finalismum generat; finalismus causas occultans ignorantiam initialem reproduci facit.","refs":["CH_QGOH_PP_3:A17","CH_QGOH_PP_3:D12"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[]}],"original_name":"Recursive Loops, Escalation Paths, and Conceptual Attractors"},{"number":"11","name":"Formal Generative Schema of the Whole Book","items":[{"id":"CL_QGOH_01:FGS-B01","text":"Exhaustio entis in substantiam aut modum → independentia substantiae / dependentia modi → impossibilitas tertii ordinis ontologici.","refs":["CH_QGOH_PPDD:A1","CH_QGOH_PPDD:A3"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[]},{"id":"CL_QGOH_01:FGS-B02","text":"Distinctio per attributa aut affectiones → exclusio pluralitatis substantiarum eiusdem naturae → unica substantia absolute infinita.","refs":["CH_QGOH_PPDD:D5","CH_QGOH_PPDD:D15"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[]},{"id":"CL_QGOH_01:FGS-B03","text":"Nulla productio substantiae ab alio → causa sui → essentia involvens existentiam → necessitas et aeternitas.","refs":["CH_QGOH_PPDD:D7","CH_QGOH_PPDD:D8"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[]},{"id":"CL_QGOH_01:FGS-B04","text":"Attributa essentiam exprimunt → natura infinita est interne differentiata → infinita infinitis modis necessario sequuntur.","refs":["CH_QGOH_PPDD:A5","CH_QGOH_PP_2:D3"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[]},{"id":"CL_QGOH_01:FGS-B05","text":"Causalitas immanens → modi infiniti ex absoluto / modi finiti per finitum → determinatio universalis intra Deum.","refs":["CH_QGOH_PP_2:D5","CH_QGOH_PP_3:A4"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[]},{"id":"CL_QGOH_01:FGS-B06","text":"Natura Naturans / Natura Naturata → intellectus et voluntas inter modos → exclusio arbitrii originarii → unicitas ordinis actualis.","refs":["CH_QGOH_PP_3:A9","CH_QGOH_PP_3:D6","CH_QGOH_PP_3:D11"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[]},{"id":"CL_QGOH_01:FGS-B07","text":"Ignorantia causarum + conscientia appetitus → libertas imaginaria → finalismus → correctio per cognitionem essentiarum et causarum.","refs":["CH_QGOH_PP_3:A17","CH_QGOH_PP_3:D13"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[]}],"original_name":"Formal Generative Schema of the Whole Book"},{"number":"12","name":"Whole-Book Argument Flow Map","items":[{"id":"CL_QGOH_01:AFM-B01","text":"Partitio in-se / in-alio → substantia / modus → dependentia conceptualis → exclusio substantiae exterioris.","refs":["CH_QGOH_PPDD:AS2","CH_QGOH_PPDD:AS6"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[]},{"id":"CL_QGOH_01:AFM-B02","text":"Improductibilitas substantiae → causa sui → existentia necessaria → infinitas → productivitas infinita.","refs":["CH_QGOH_PPDD:AS4","CH_QGOH_PP_2:D3"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[]},{"id":"CL_QGOH_01:AFM-B03","text":"Productivitas infinita → causalitas immanens → modi infiniti et finiti → concatenatio determinationum singularium.","refs":["CH_QGOH_PP_2:SD2","CH_QGOH_PP_3:AS3"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[]},{"id":"CL_QGOH_01:AFM-B04","text":"Determinatio universalis → Natura Naturans / Natura Naturata → demotio voluntatis → exclusio ordinis alternativi.","refs":["CH_QGOH_PP_3:AS4","CH_QGOH_PP_3:AS6"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[]},{"id":"CL_QGOH_01:AFM-B05","text":"Clausura ontologica → contingentia epistemica → genealogia finalismi → restitutio cognitionis per causas et essentias.","refs":["CH_QGOH_PP_3:D8","CH_QGOH_PP_3:D13"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[]}],"original_name":"Whole-Book Argument Flow Map"},{"number":"13","name":"Macro Map-Effects","items":[{"id":"CL_QGOH_01:MME-01","text":"Oppositio inter Deum et mundum corruit: Deus non est ens iuxta alia, iarum autem ordo est expressio modalis causalitatis divinae.","refs":["CH_QGOH_PPDD:ME3","CH_QGOH_PP_3:ME4"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[]},{"id":"CL_QGOH_01:MME-02","text":"Oppositio inter substantiam materialem et substantiam spiritualem dissolvitur, quia extensio et cogitatio ad attributa unius substantiae referuntur.","refs":["CH_QGOH_PPDD:SD5","CH_QGOH_PP_2:ME1"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[]},{"id":"CL_QGOH_01:MME-03","text":"Oppositio inter libertatem et necessitatem reordinatur: indifferentia libertati detrahitur, actio ex propria natura libertatem constituit.","refs":["CH_QGOH_PP_2:ME3","CH_QGOH_PP_3:SD2"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[]},{"id":"CL_QGOH_01:MME-04","text":"Oppositio inter possibile et actuale ex ontologia ad epistemologiam transfertur; ordo actualis non est unus inter plures ordines aeque reales.","refs":["CH_QGOH_PP_3:D8","CH_QGOH_PP_3:D11"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[]},{"id":"CL_QGOH_01:MME-05","text":"Explicatio per fines reclassificatur ut effectus ignorantiae, dum explicatio per causas, essentias et proprietates norma methodologica fit.","refs":["CH_QGOH_PP_3:SD6","CH_QGOH_PP_3:D13"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[]},{"id":"CL_QGOH_01:MME-06","text":"Agens singulare reclassificatur a principio sui iuris ad nodum activum sed determinatum intra seriem causalitatis immanentis.","refs":["CH_QGOH_PP_3:SD4","CH_QGOH_PP_3:ME1"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[]}],"original_name":"Macro Map-Effects"},{"number":"14","name":"Altitude Integration","items":[{"id":"CL_QGOH_01:ALT-B01","text":"Gradus superficiei affirmat Deum necessario existere, omnia in Deo esse, nihil contingens dari, voluntatem non esse liberam et naturam non agere propter fines.","refs":["CH_QGOH_PP_3:ALT1","CH_QGOH_PPDD:D16"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[]},{"id":"CL_QGOH_01:ALT-B02","text":"Gradus structuralis constituit campum unius substantiae interne per attributa et modos differentiatae, cuius causalitas per modos infinitos et concatenationes finitas distribuitur.","refs":["CH_QGOH_PP_3:ALT2","CH_QGOH_PP_3:SD1"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[]},{"id":"CL_QGOH_01:ALT-B03","text":"Gradus metastrategicus vocabula libertatis, possibilitatis, perfectionis et finalitatis ex theologia voluntaristica in analysin necessitatis, determinationis et ignorantiae transfert.","refs":["CH_QGOH_PP_3:ALT3","CH_QGOH_PP_2:ALT3"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[]},{"id":"CL_QGOH_01:ALT-B04","text":"Gradus ontologicus et temporalis distinguit aeternam necessitatem substantiae a successione interna modorum, nullo decreto temporali ante naturam posito.","refs":["CH_QGOH_PPDD:ALT4","CH_QGOH_PP_3:ALT4"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[]},{"id":"CL_QGOH_01:ALT-B05","text":"Gradus methodologicus et performativus negationes axiomatice cumulat, objectiones ad falsa schemata imaginationis reducit et finalismum genealogice producendo dissolvit.","refs":["CH_QGOH_PPDD:AS3","CH_QGOH_PP_3:A18","CH_QGOH_PP_3:D12"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[]}],"original_name":"Altitude Integration"},{"number":"15","name":"Internal Tensions, Mutation Points, and Fault Lines","items":[{"id":"CL_QGOH_01:FL-01","text":"Attributa per se concipiuntur et realiter distinguuntur, sed nexus positivus quo eadem attributa unam substantiam numericam constituant potius asseritur quam plene derivatur.","refs":["CH_QGOH_PPDD:MRT4"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"Tension","passages":[]},{"id":"CL_QGOH_01:FL-02","text":"Deus omnes modos determinat, sed res finita immediate ab alia causa finita determinatur; relatio exacta inter causalitatem divinam et mediationem finitam manet compressa.","refs":["CH_QGOH_PP_2:MRT3"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"Tension","passages":[]},{"id":"CL_QGOH_01:FL-03","text":"Productio aeterna modorum infinitarum et series causarum finitarum cohaerere debent, sed transitus inter hos ordines non plene formalizatur.","refs":["CH_QGOH_PP_2:MRT4"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"Tension","passages":[]},{"id":"CL_QGOH_01:FL-04","text":"Deus liber dicitur quia ex necessitate sua agit, voluntas autem necessaria vel coacta; differentia pendet ex distinctione inter necessitatem internam et determinationem ab alio.","refs":["CH_QGOH_PP_3:MRT5"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"Tension","passages":[]},{"id":"CL_QGOH_01:FL-05","text":"Transitus ab ordine necessario ad eius summam perfectionem notionem perfectionis postulat quae neque electione neque bono externo neque comparatione possibilitatum nitatur.","refs":["CH_QGOH_PP_3:MRT6"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"Tension","passages":[]},{"id":"CL_QGOH_01:MP-01","text":"Infinitas mutatur a pura exclusione termini in principium productionis inexhaustae.","refs":["CH_QGOH_PPDD:SD2","CH_QGOH_PP_2:D3"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"Tension","passages":[]},{"id":"CL_QGOH_01:MP-02","text":"Libertas mutatur ab indifferentia voluntatis in necessitatem activam propriae naturae.","refs":["CH_QGOH_PP_2:NX4","CH_QGOH_PP_3:SD2"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"Tension","passages":[]},{"id":"CL_QGOH_01:MP-03","text":"Possibilitas et contingentia mutantur a statibus rerum in indices cognitionis causalis incompletae.","refs":["CH_QGOH_PP_3:NX9","CH_QGOH_PP_3:ME2"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"Tension","passages":[]},{"id":"CL_QGOH_01:MP-04","text":"Intellectus et voluntas mutantur a facultatibus divinis vel humanis privilegiatis in modos Naturae Naturatae.","refs":["CH_QGOH_PP_2:D9","CH_QGOH_PP_3:SD4"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"Tension","passages":[]},{"id":"CL_QGOH_01:MP-05","text":"Causalitas mutatur ab imagine creationis transeuntis in dependentiam immanentem quae effectus intra causam retinet.","refs":["CH_QGOH_PP_2:SD1","CH_QGOH_PP_3:SD1"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"Tension","passages":[]}],"original_name":"Internal Tensions, Mutation Points, and Fault Lines"},{"number":"16","name":"Hidden System Image","items":[{"id":"CL_QGOH_01:HSI-01","text":"Realitas implicite est machina sui causalis, infinite differentiata et immanens, cuius effectus non ab ea exeunt sed eius potentiam exprimunt.","refs":["CH_QGOH_PPDD:SD1","CH_QGOH_PP_3:SD1"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[]},{"id":"CL_QGOH_01:HSI-02","text":"Res singularis est nodus temporalis determinationum, simul effectus causarum antecedentium et fons effectuum ulteriorum.","refs":["CH_QGOH_PP_3:ME1","CH_QGOH_PP_3:A16"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[]},{"id":"CL_QGOH_01:HSI-03","text":"Subiectum est modus conscius appetitus sed ignarus causarum, ideo pronus libertatem et fines fingere, non principium extra naturam.","refs":["CH_QGOH_PP_3:DNX3","CH_QGOH_PP_3:SD4"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[]},{"id":"CL_QGOH_01:HSI-04","text":"Tempus est series modalium determinationum intra aeternitatem quae ipsa neque duratione neque successione explicatur.","refs":["CH_QGOH_PPDD:SD6","CH_QGOH_PP_3:ALT4"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[]},{"id":"CL_QGOH_01:HSI-05","text":"Navigatio possibilis non est fuga ex causalitate, sed transitus ab imaginatione finium ad cognitionem essentiarum, proprietatum et causarum.","refs":["CH_QGOH_PP_3:D13","CH_QGOH_PP_3:A14"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[]}],"original_name":"Hidden System Image"},{"number":"17","name":"Minimal Whole-Book Reconstruction Test","items":[{"id":"CL_QGOH_01:MRT-B01","text":"Axiomata de substantia, modo, causa necessaria, attributis et mediatione finita sufficiunt ad motum praesentis gregis ab unitate substantiae ad necessitatem universalem restituendum.","refs":["CH_QGOH_PPDD:MRT1","CH_QGOH_PP_3:MRT1"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[]},{"id":"CL_QGOH_01:MRT-B02","text":"Structura firmissima est nexus inter immanentiam, determinationem modalem et negationem contingentiae onticae.","refs":["CH_QGOH_PP_3:MRT2"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[]},{"id":"CL_QGOH_01:MRT-B03","text":"Minus determinata manet mediatio inter productionem aeternam, modos infinitos et seriem causarum finitarum, praesertim ubi demonstratio regressus in fonte truncatur.","refs":["CH_QGOH_PP_2:MRT4","CH_QGOH_PP_2:MRT7"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[]},{"id":"CL_QGOH_01:MRT-B04","text":"Unitas substantiae sub pluralitate attributorum ad restitutionem necessaria est, sed eius coordinatio positiva ultra exclusiones pluralitatis adhuc resistit compressioni axiomaticae.","refs":["CH_QGOH_PPDD:MRT4"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[]},{"id":"CL_QGOH_01:MRT-B05","text":"Restitutio verum motum servat si seriem improductibilitas → causa sui → productivitas infinita → immanentia → determinatio → critica finalismi retinet; probationes scholasticae singulae non sunt totius structurae substitutae.","refs":["CH_QGOH_PPDD:MRT6","CH_QGOH_PP_3:MRT8"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[]}],"original_name":"Minimal Whole-Book Reconstruction Test"}],"language_code":"la","language_name":"Latina","translations":[{"id":"CAREVAR_20261015T015359814865Z_6185d690","language_code":"en","language_name":"English","title":"Chapters 01–03","source_sha256":"a5c1a517e3ebd985167a5b463355e8849573250fbe58469194a88fa2996de65e","sha256":"b6426d41a07ab424786cacbaffa48d1e1940b0ca1daab36313804542e0acf6e7","rendition_type":"machine","review_status":"machine","model":"gpt-5.6-terra","created":"2026-10-15T01:53:59.819263+00:00","sections":[{"number":"1","name":"Book-Scale Argumental Spine","items":[{"id":"CL_QGOH_01:BAS-01","text":"The central question is how a necessarily existing substance can at once become the immanent principle of all modes, without an external cause, contingent will, or alternative order of things.","refs":["CH_QGOH_PPDD:AS1","CH_QGOH_PP_3:AS1"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[]},{"id":"CL_QGOH_01:BAS-02","text":"The global thesis affirms a single divine substance, whose essence involves existence and power, to contain all beings within itself and determine them necessarily.","refs":["CH_QGOH_PPDD:AS6","CH_QGOH_PP_3:A8"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[]},{"id":"CL_QGOH_01:BAS-03","text":"The geometrical argumentation converts ontological distinctions into exclusions, exclusions into necessary causality, and causality into a diagnosis of the errors of imagination and appetite.","refs":["CH_QGOH_PPDD:AS3","CH_QGOH_PP_3:D12"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[]},{"id":"CL_QGOH_01:BAS-04","text":"The movement proceeds from the unproducibility of substance to self-causation, then transforms static infinity into the infinite productivity of divine nature.","refs":["CH_QGOH_PPDD:AS4","CH_QGOH_PP_2:D3"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[]},{"id":"CL_QGOH_01:BAS-05","text":"Infinite productivity is distributed through infinite modes and finite concatenations, until ontological necessity culminates in a critique of contingency, free will, and finalism.","refs":["CH_QGOH_PP_3:AS3","CH_QGOH_PP_3:AS7"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[]},{"id":"CL_QGOH_01:BAS-06","text":"The movement is cumulative and spiral: every exclusion of exteriority returns as a further causal, modal, epistemic, or teleological closure.","refs":["CH_QGOH_PPDD:NX5","CH_QGOH_PP_3:NX4"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[]},{"id":"CL_QGOH_01:BAS-07","text":"The system advances from the constitution of being to the uniqueness of order, then defends order itself against voluntaristic and final interpretations.","refs":["CH_QGOH_PP_3:AS6","CH_QGOH_PP_3:SD6"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[]},{"id":"CL_QGOH_01:BAS-08","text":"The produced closure is immanent and actualist: no residual space is left either outside God, beyond the actual order, or within ontic contingency.","refs":["CH_QGOH_PP_2:SNX1","CH_QGOH_PP_3:SNX4"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[]}],"original_name":"Book-Scale Argumental Spine"},{"number":"2","name":"Global Load-Bearing Axioms","items":[{"id":"CL_QGOH_01:GA-01","text":"[Persistent] Every being is either substance or mode; no third ontological kind by which immanent dependence may be escaped is given.","refs":["CH_QGOH_PPDD:A1","CH_QGOH_PP_3:A6"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[]},{"id":"CL_QGOH_01:GA-02","text":"[Persistent] God is the single absolutely infinite substance, cause of itself, whose essence cannot be separated from existence.","refs":["CH_QGOH_PPDD:A11","CH_QGOH_PPDD:D15"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[]},{"id":"CL_QGOH_01:GA-03","text":"[Mutating] Attributes express the essence of substance without dividing it; their multiplicity becomes a principle of internal differentiation, not of substantial plurality.","refs":["CH_QGOH_PPDD:A5","CH_QGOH_PPDD:SD3"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[]},{"id":"CL_QGOH_01:GA-04","text":"[Persistent] Modes are affections of substance which are in God, are conceived through God, and depend on him with respect to essence, existence, and operation.","refs":["CH_QGOH_PPDD:A3","CH_QGOH_PP_3:A7"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[]},{"id":"CL_QGOH_01:GA-05","text":"[Persistent] From a determinate cause an effect necessarily follows; true causality admits no indifference between a given cause and an effect.","refs":["CH_QGOH_PPDD:A6","CH_QGOH_PP_2:A8"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[]},{"id":"CL_QGOH_01:GA-06","text":"[Mutating] Infinity passes from the negation of a homogeneous limit to the productive fullness by which infinitely many things follow in infinitely many modes.","refs":["CH_QGOH_PPDD:SD2","CH_QGOH_PP_2:D3"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[]},{"id":"CL_QGOH_01:GA-07","text":"[Mutating] Freedom is not a choice among possibilities, but action from the sole necessity of one's own nature without external determination.","refs":["CH_QGOH_PP_2:A9","CH_QGOH_PP_3:SD2"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[]},{"id":"CL_QGOH_01:GA-08","text":"[Emergent] Divine causality is immanent: effects are not constituted outside the cause, but remain within the same nature as expressions of attributes.","refs":["CH_QGOH_PP_2:D5","CH_QGOH_PP_3:SD1"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[]},{"id":"CL_QGOH_01:GA-09","text":"[Emergent] Modal production forms a double order: infinite modes follow from the absolute nature of attributes, while finite modes are mediated through finite modifications.","refs":["CH_QGOH_PP_2:D7","CH_QGOH_PP_3:A4"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[]},{"id":"CL_QGOH_01:GA-10","text":"[Terminal] Every finite thing is determined to exist and operate by another finite cause, with no first finite term admitted.","refs":["CH_QGOH_PP_2:A12","CH_QGOH_PP_3:A5"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[]},{"id":"CL_QGOH_01:GA-11","text":"[Terminal] Divine power is divine essence itself; therefore the actual order is not chosen from an order of non-actual possibilities.","refs":["CH_QGOH_PP_3:A15","CH_QGOH_PP_3:D11"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[]},{"id":"CL_QGOH_01:GA-12","text":"[Terminal] Intellect and will, even when conceived as infinite, are determinate modes, not original faculties set above nature.","refs":["CH_QGOH_PP_3:A11","CH_QGOH_PP_3:D6"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[]},{"id":"CL_QGOH_01:GA-13","text":"[Emergent] Necessity pertains to things and causes; contingency, possibility, and finality arise from defective causal knowledge.","refs":["CH_QGOH_PP_3:A14","CH_QGOH_PP_3:D8"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[]},{"id":"CL_QGOH_01:GA-14","text":"[Attractor] Intelligibility converges upon essences, properties, and causes, while measuring imagination and conscious appetite produce false ontological models.","refs":["CH_QGOH_PP_2:AS3","CH_QGOH_PP_3:A17","CH_QGOH_PP_3:A18"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[]}],"original_name":"Global Load-Bearing Axioms"},{"number":"3","name":"Recurrence, Mutation, and Displacement Matrix","items":[{"id":"CL_QGOH_01:RMD-01","text":"Substance retains ontological identity, but is advanced from a term of independence to a causally active principle of the whole of nature.","refs":["CH_QGOH_PPDD:A2","CH_QGOH_PP_2:D4"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[]},{"id":"CL_QGOH_01:RMD-02","text":"Infinity changes function: first it excludes limit and negation, then it affirms the production of effects in infinitely many modes.","refs":["CH_QGOH_PPDD:SD2","CH_QGOH_PP_2:D3"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[]},{"id":"CL_QGOH_01:RMD-03","text":"Attribute passes from an index of essence to a productive channel through which infinite and finite modes follow in order.","refs":["CH_QGOH_PPDD:A5","CH_QGOH_PP_2:D7"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[]},{"id":"CL_QGOH_01:RMD-04","text":"The formula by which all things are in God is expanded from ontological inherence to the closure of causality, conception, and determination.","refs":["CH_QGOH_PPDD:D16","CH_QGOH_PP_3:SD1"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[]},{"id":"CL_QGOH_01:RMD-05","text":"Freedom undergoes an inversion: indifference is repudiated, while self-necessity receives the name of freedom.","refs":["CH_QGOH_PP_2:NX4","CH_QGOH_PP_3:SD2"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[]},{"id":"CL_QGOH_01:RMD-06","text":"Intellect is first opposed to imagination as the mode of correct conception, then is itself placed among the modes of Natura Naturata.","refs":["CH_QGOH_PP_2:AS3","CH_QGOH_PP_3:A11"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[]},{"id":"CL_QGOH_01:RMD-07","text":"Eternity remains separated from infinite duration, but its opposite is now not external time, but the internal series of finite determinations.","refs":["CH_QGOH_PPDD:A13","CH_QGOH_PP_2:ME5"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[]},{"id":"CL_QGOH_01:RMD-08","text":"Contingency is expelled from the category of the thing and transferred to the epistemic state of incomplete knowledge.","refs":["CH_QGOH_PP_3:NX9","CH_QGOH_PP_3:ME2"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[]},{"id":"CL_QGOH_01:RMD-09","text":"Anthropomorphism is extended from a simple confusion of divine nature with human affects to a genealogy of finalism and superstition.","refs":["CH_QGOH_PPDD:NX7","CH_QGOH_PP_3:D12"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[]}],"original_name":"Recurrence, Mutation, and Displacement Matrix"},{"number":"4","name":"Lower-Layer vs Current-Level Distinction","items":[{"id":"CL_QGOH_01:CGD-01","text":"Local demonstrations against a plurality of substances are at this level absorbed into the broader proposition that no substantial or causal exteriority is given.","refs":["CH_QGOH_PPDD:D5","CH_QGOH_PPDD:D15"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[]},{"id":"CL_QGOH_01:CGD-02","text":"The refutation of the composition of extension remains a local case, but the distinction between imagination and intellect becomes a general mechanism for detecting pseudo-ontological contradictions.","refs":["CH_QGOH_PP_2:D1","CH_QGOH_PP_2:ALT2"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[]},{"id":"CL_QGOH_01:CGD-03","text":"The distinction between infinite and finite modes is a lower architecture of mediation; at this level it joins eternal productivity with the determination of singular things.","refs":["CH_QGOH_PP_2:D7","CH_QGOH_PP_3:D1"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[]},{"id":"CL_QGOH_01:CGD-04","text":"The distinction of Natura Naturans and Natura Naturata is not a second division of substance, but an instrument of the present level by which causality and dependence are distinguished without transcendence.","refs":["CH_QGOH_PP_3:A9","CH_QGOH_PP_3:D4"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[]},{"id":"CL_QGOH_01:CGD-05","text":"The local difficulty of the transition from the non-production of substance to self-causation is overcome structurally through immanent closure, not through the introduction of a further cause.","refs":["CH_QGOH_PPDD:MRT3","CH_QGOH_PP_3:SD1"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[]},{"id":"CL_QGOH_01:CGD-06","text":"Local questions concerning divine intellect and will yield to the global proposition by which every mental faculty is placed among caused modes.","refs":["CH_QGOH_PP_2:D9","CH_QGOH_PP_3:SD4"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[]},{"id":"CL_QGOH_01:CGD-07","text":"The genealogy of finalism, although it proceeds in the form of an appendix, closes the present structure by showing how the contrary of necessary causality is generated by the very human cognitive condition.","refs":["CH_QGOH_PP_3:D12","CH_QGOH_PP_3:D13"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[]}],"original_name":"Lower-Layer vs Current-Level Distinction"},{"number":"5","name":"Global Apophatic Structure","items":[{"id":"CL_QGOH_01:GNX-01","text":"No substance, cause, or condition of intelligibility outside God is admitted.","refs":["CH_QGOH_PPDD:SNX1","CH_QGOH_PP_2:SNX1"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"Negative","passages":[]},{"id":"CL_QGOH_01:GNX-02","text":"Infinite substance is neither composed of real parts nor explained through the void, measure, or division of imagination.","refs":["CH_QGOH_PP_2:NX3","CH_QGOH_PP_2:DNX1"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"Negative","passages":[]},{"id":"CL_QGOH_01:GNX-03","text":"God is not a transitive craftsman whose effects subsist outside himself.","refs":["CH_QGOH_PP_2:NX6","CH_QGOH_PP_3:DNX2"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"Negative","passages":[]},{"id":"CL_QGOH_01:GNX-04","text":"Divine action arises neither from a contingent decree nor from deliberation among possible orders.","refs":["CH_QGOH_PP_2:NX5","CH_QGOH_PP_3:SNX1"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"Negative","passages":[]},{"id":"CL_QGOH_01:GNX-05","text":"A finite thing is neither cause of itself nor a voluntarily causally autonomous subject.","refs":["CH_QGOH_PP_2:NX7","CH_QGOH_PP_3:DNX3"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"Negative","passages":[]},{"id":"CL_QGOH_01:GNX-06","text":"Ontic contingency and human end are simultaneously excluded as principles of Nature.","refs":["CH_QGOH_PP_3:NX9","CH_QGOH_PP_3:NX10"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"Negative","passages":[]}],"original_name":"Global Apophatic Structure"},{"number":"6","name":"Deep Book Apophatics","items":[{"id":"CL_QGOH_01:DBN-01","text":"The system requires the separability of the essence of substance from its existence to be false; otherwise the necessity of God and all modal dependence would collapse.","refs":["CH_QGOH_PPDD:DNX2"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"Negative","passages":[]},{"id":"CL_QGOH_01:DBN-02","text":"A divine power that reserves unexercised possibilities must be false, because a gap between essence, power, and actuality would dissolve necessary order.","refs":["CH_QGOH_PP_2:SNX2"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"Negative","passages":[]},{"id":"CL_QGOH_01:DBN-03","text":"No first finite cause can be admitted; its admission would interrupt the modal concatenation and convert the finite into a latent substance.","refs":["CH_QGOH_PP_3:DNX1"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"Negative","passages":[]},{"id":"CL_QGOH_01:DBN-04","text":"A mental faculty placed outside the natural order must be false, because the causal privilege of intellect or will would break immanent closure.","refs":["CH_QGOH_PP_3:SD4"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"Negative","passages":[]},{"id":"CL_QGOH_01:DBN-05","text":"Human utility, good, and end cannot furnish a norm of the truth of nature; knowledge must proceed through essences, properties, and causes.","refs":["CH_QGOH_PP_3:DNX6","CH_QGOH_PP_3:A18"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"Negative","passages":[]}],"original_name":"Deep Book Apophatics"},{"number":"7","name":"Super-Apophatics of the Whole","items":[{"id":"CL_QGOH_01:BSA-01","text":"The ordering absence is “outside” itself: the system cannot concede any ontological, causal, or epistemic place outside immanence.","refs":["CH_QGOH_PP_2:SNX1"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"Negative","passages":[]},{"id":"CL_QGOH_01:BSA-02","text":"The unavailable possibility is a genuinely alternative world; another order would not be a variation of this nature, but the contradictory substitution of another divine nature.","refs":["CH_QGOH_PP_3:DNX4"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"Negative","passages":[]},{"id":"CL_QGOH_01:BSA-03","text":"No transcendent norm of truth, causality, or goodness stands above nature, from which the actual order might be judged or chosen.","refs":["CH_QGOH_PP_3:SNX2"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"Negative","passages":[]},{"id":"CL_QGOH_01:BSA-04","text":"An ontic causal rupture is precluded; what appears as chance, possibility, or contingency indicates a defect of knowledge, not a gap in reality.","refs":["CH_QGOH_PP_3:SNX4"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"Negative","passages":[]}],"original_name":"Super-Apophatics of the Whole"},{"number":"8","name":"Cross-Unit Derivative Propositions","items":[{"id":"CL_QGOH_01:XD-01","text":"The uniqueness of substance conjoined with infinite productivity abolishes creation as an external event and converts it into the eternal expression of nature.","refs":["CL_QGOH_01:GA-02","CL_QGOH_01:GA-06","CL_QGOH_01:GA-08","CH_QGOH_PPDD:D15","CH_QGOH_PP_2:D3"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"From","passages":[]},{"id":"CL_QGOH_01:XD-02","text":"The series of finite causes is not a bridge between God and the world, but an internal articulation of immanent causality.","refs":["CL_QGOH_01:GA-04","CL_QGOH_01:GA-08","CL_QGOH_01:GA-10","CH_QGOH_PPDD:D16","CH_QGOH_PP_2:D5","CH_QGOH_PP_3:D1"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"From","passages":[]},{"id":"CL_QGOH_01:XD-03","text":"Infinity and omnipotence converge in exhaustive actuality: divine power is neither measured nor reserved as a store of effects not yet produced.","refs":["CL_QGOH_01:GA-06","CL_QGOH_01:GA-11","CH_QGOH_PPDD:SD2","CH_QGOH_PP_2:SD3","CH_QGOH_PP_3:A15"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"From","passages":[]},{"id":"CL_QGOH_01:XD-04","text":"When freedom is defined through the necessity of one's own nature and will is placed among caused modes, original freedom belongs only to the immanent cause, not to a choosing faculty.","refs":["CL_QGOH_01:GA-07","CL_QGOH_01:GA-12","CH_QGOH_PP_2:A9","CH_QGOH_PP_3:D6"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"From","passages":[]},{"id":"CL_QGOH_01:XD-05","text":"The multiplicity of attributes conjoined with divine extension removes matter from the order of independent substance and reclassifies it as an internal differentiation of one nature.","refs":["CL_QGOH_01:GA-03","CL_QGOH_01:GA-08","CH_QGOH_PPDD:D12","CH_QGOH_PP_2:NX2"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"From","passages":[]},{"id":"CL_QGOH_01:XD-06","text":"The asymmetry between substance, whose essence involves existence, and modes, whose essence does not involve it, converts modal persistence into continuous causality.","refs":["CL_QGOH_01:GA-02","CL_QGOH_01:GA-04","CL_QGOH_01:GA-05","CH_QGOH_PPDD:SD6","CH_QGOH_PP_2:A10","CH_QGOH_PP_2:D6"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"From","passages":[]},{"id":"CL_QGOH_01:XD-07","text":"The exclusion of another order and the translation of contingency into knowledge make possibility a relation to ignorance of a cause, not an incomplete state of a thing.","refs":["CL_QGOH_01:GA-11","CL_QGOH_01:GA-13","CH_QGOH_PP_3:A13","CH_QGOH_PP_3:D8"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"From","passages":[]},{"id":"CL_QGOH_01:XD-08","text":"The measure of parts and the projection of ends are forms of the same error: modal structures of imagination or appetite are transferred into the nature of substance.","refs":["CL_QGOH_01:GA-13","CL_QGOH_01:GA-14","CH_QGOH_PP_2:A5","CH_QGOH_PP_3:A17","CH_QGOH_PP_3:D13"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"From","passages":[]}],"original_name":"Cross-Unit Derivative Propositions"},{"number":"9","name":"Book-Scale Super-Derivatives","items":[{"id":"CL_QGOH_01:BSD-01","text":"The reality of the present system is immanent actualism: real possibility does not exceed divine power, and divine power is not separated from actual essence.","refs":["CL_QGOH_01:GA-08","CL_QGOH_01:GA-11","CH_QGOH_PP_2:SD3","CH_QGOH_PP_3:A15"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"From","passages":[]},{"id":"CL_QGOH_01:BSD-02","text":"Time contains neither divine nature nor a decree before production; eternity is the necessity of essence, while succession belongs to the order of finite modes.","refs":["CL_QGOH_01:GA-02","CL_QGOH_01:GA-09","CL_QGOH_01:GA-10","CH_QGOH_PPDD:SD6","CH_QGOH_PP_3:ALT4"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"From","passages":[]},{"id":"CL_QGOH_01:BSD-03","text":"The finite subject becomes a causal node within the series of determinations, not a transcendental center from which acts begin.","refs":["CL_QGOH_01:GA-10","CL_QGOH_01:GA-12","CH_QGOH_PP_3:D1","CH_QGOH_PP_3:SD4"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"From","passages":[]},{"id":"CL_QGOH_01:BSD-04","text":"Ignorance does not constitute the indetermination of a thing, but the mechanism that transforms necessity into contingency and causality into apparent finality.","refs":["CL_QGOH_01:GA-13","CL_QGOH_01:GA-14","CH_QGOH_PP_3:SD3","CH_QGOH_PP_3:SD6"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"From","passages":[]},{"id":"CL_QGOH_01:BSD-05","text":"To act does not mean to begin a series of causes absolutely, but to express the power of nature under a certain determination and to produce further effects.","refs":["CL_QGOH_01:GA-04","CL_QGOH_01:GA-10","CL_QGOH_01:GA-11","CH_QGOH_PP_3:A16","CH_QGOH_PP_3:NX3"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"From","passages":[]},{"id":"CL_QGOH_01:BSD-06","text":"The method suited to this ontology does not ask for what end a thing serves, but from what essence, property, and concatenation of causes it necessarily follows.","refs":["CL_QGOH_01:GA-05","CL_QGOH_01:GA-14","CH_QGOH_PP_3:A18","CH_QGOH_PP_3:D13"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"From","passages":[]}],"original_name":"Book-Scale Super-Derivatives"},{"number":"10","name":"Recursive Loops, Escalation Paths, and Conceptual Attractors","items":[{"id":"CL_QGOH_01:RLE-01","text":"The partition of being into substance and modes establishes conceptual dependence; conceptual dependence requires immanent causality; immanent causality closes the initial partition.","refs":["CH_QGOH_PPDD:A1","CH_QGOH_PPDD:D16"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[]},{"id":"CL_QGOH_01:RLE-02","text":"The exclusion of external substance removes an external cause; causality without exteriority excludes an alternative order; the exclusion of the alternative again confirms the uniqueness of substance.","refs":["CH_QGOH_PPDD:D15","CH_QGOH_PP_3:SD1"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[]},{"id":"CL_QGOH_01:RLE-03","text":"Attributes conceived through themselves increase difference without dividing substance; this same difference amplifies the infinite productivity of one nature.","refs":["CH_QGOH_PPDD:D12","CH_QGOH_PP_2:D3"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[]},{"id":"CL_QGOH_01:RLE-04","text":"Every finite cause refers to another finite cause, whereby regress produces not a defect of explanation but a form of modal recursion.","refs":["CH_QGOH_PP_3:A5","CH_QGOH_PP_3:D1"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[]},{"id":"CL_QGOH_01:RLE-05","text":"Imagination invents parts and ends, derives contradictions or utilities from the fictions, and then takes these same fictions for the structure of nature.","refs":["CH_QGOH_PP_2:A5","CH_QGOH_PP_2:SD4"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[]},{"id":"CL_QGOH_01:RLE-06","text":"Ignorance of causes produces the opinion of freedom; the opinion of freedom generates finalism; finalism, by concealing causes, causes the initial ignorance to be reproduced.","refs":["CH_QGOH_PP_3:A17","CH_QGOH_PP_3:D12"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[]}],"original_name":"Recursive Loops, Escalation Paths, and Conceptual Attractors"},{"number":"11","name":"Formal Generative Schema of the Whole Book","items":[{"id":"CL_QGOH_01:FGS-B01","text":"Exhaustion of being into substance or mode → independence of substance / dependence of mode → impossibility of a third ontological order.","refs":["CH_QGOH_PPDD:A1","CH_QGOH_PPDD:A3"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[]},{"id":"CL_QGOH_01:FGS-B02","text":"Distinction through attributes or affections → exclusion of a plurality of substances of the same nature → one absolutely infinite substance.","refs":["CH_QGOH_PPDD:D5","CH_QGOH_PPDD:D15"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[]},{"id":"CL_QGOH_01:FGS-B03","text":"No production of substance by another → cause of itself → essence involving existence → necessity and eternity.","refs":["CH_QGOH_PPDD:D7","CH_QGOH_PPDD:D8"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[]},{"id":"CL_QGOH_01:FGS-B04","text":"Attributes express essence → infinite nature is internally differentiated → infinitely many things necessarily follow in infinitely many modes.","refs":["CH_QGOH_PPDD:A5","CH_QGOH_PP_2:D3"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[]},{"id":"CL_QGOH_01:FGS-B05","text":"Immanent causality → infinite modes from the absolute / finite modes through the finite → universal determination within God.","refs":["CH_QGOH_PP_2:D5","CH_QGOH_PP_3:A4"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[]},{"id":"CL_QGOH_01:FGS-B06","text":"Natura Naturans / Natura Naturata → intellect and will among modes → exclusion of original free will → uniqueness of the actual order.","refs":["CH_QGOH_PP_3:A9","CH_QGOH_PP_3:D6","CH_QGOH_PP_3:D11"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[]},{"id":"CL_QGOH_01:FGS-B07","text":"Ignorance of causes + consciousness of appetite → imaginary freedom → finalism → correction through knowledge of essences and causes.","refs":["CH_QGOH_PP_3:A17","CH_QGOH_PP_3:D13"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[]}],"original_name":"Formal Generative Schema of the Whole Book"},{"number":"12","name":"Whole-Book Argument Flow Map","items":[{"id":"CL_QGOH_01:AFM-B01","text":"Partition into-in-itself / in-another → substance / mode → conceptual dependence → exclusion of external substance.","refs":["CH_QGOH_PPDD:AS2","CH_QGOH_PPDD:AS6"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[]},{"id":"CL_QGOH_01:AFM-B02","text":"Unproducibility of substance → cause of itself → necessary existence → infinity → infinite productivity.","refs":["CH_QGOH_PPDD:AS4","CH_QGOH_PP_2:D3"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[]},{"id":"CL_QGOH_01:AFM-B03","text":"Infinite productivity → immanent causality → infinite and finite modes → concatenation of singular determinations.","refs":["CH_QGOH_PP_2:SD2","CH_QGOH_PP_3:AS3"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[]},{"id":"CL_QGOH_01:AFM-B04","text":"Universal determination → Natura Naturans / Natura Naturata → demotion of will → exclusion of an alternative order.","refs":["CH_QGOH_PP_3:AS4","CH_QGOH_PP_3:AS6"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[]},{"id":"CL_QGOH_01:AFM-B05","text":"Ontological closure → epistemic contingency → genealogy of finalism → restoration of knowledge through causes and essences.","refs":["CH_QGOH_PP_3:D8","CH_QGOH_PP_3:D13"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[]}],"original_name":"Whole-Book Argument Flow Map"},{"number":"13","name":"Macro Map-Effects","items":[{"id":"CL_QGOH_01:MME-01","text":"The opposition between God and world collapses: God is not a being alongside others, but their order is a modal expression of divine causality.","refs":["CH_QGOH_PPDD:ME3","CH_QGOH_PP_3:ME4"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[]},{"id":"CL_QGOH_01:MME-02","text":"The opposition between material substance and spiritual substance is dissolved, because extension and thought are referred to the attributes of one substance.","refs":["CH_QGOH_PPDD:SD5","CH_QGOH_PP_2:ME1"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[]},{"id":"CL_QGOH_01:MME-03","text":"The opposition between freedom and necessity is reordered: indifference is removed from freedom, while action from one's own nature constitutes freedom.","refs":["CH_QGOH_PP_2:ME3","CH_QGOH_PP_3:SD2"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[]},{"id":"CL_QGOH_01:MME-04","text":"The opposition between possible and actual is transferred from ontology to epistemology; the actual order is not one among several equally real orders.","refs":["CH_QGOH_PP_3:D8","CH_QGOH_PP_3:D11"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[]},{"id":"CL_QGOH_01:MME-05","text":"Explanation through ends is reclassified as an effect of ignorance, while explanation through causes, essences, and properties becomes the methodological norm.","refs":["CH_QGOH_PP_3:SD6","CH_QGOH_PP_3:D13"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[]},{"id":"CL_QGOH_01:MME-06","text":"The singular agent is reclassified from a self-governing principle to an active yet determined node within the series of immanent causality.","refs":["CH_QGOH_PP_3:SD4","CH_QGOH_PP_3:ME1"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[]}],"original_name":"Macro Map-Effects"},{"number":"14","name":"Altitude Integration","items":[{"id":"CL_QGOH_01:ALT-B01","text":"The surface level affirms that God necessarily exists, that all things are in God, that nothing contingent is given, that will is not free, and that nature does not act for ends.","refs":["CH_QGOH_PP_3:ALT1","CH_QGOH_PPDD:D16"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[]},{"id":"CL_QGOH_01:ALT-B02","text":"The structural level constitutes the field of one substance internally differentiated through attributes and modes, whose causality is distributed through infinite modes and finite concatenations.","refs":["CH_QGOH_PP_3:ALT2","CH_QGOH_PP_3:SD1"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[]},{"id":"CL_QGOH_01:ALT-B03","text":"The metastrategic level transfers the terms freedom, possibility, perfection, and finality from voluntaristic theology into an analysis of necessity, determination, and ignorance.","refs":["CH_QGOH_PP_3:ALT3","CH_QGOH_PP_2:ALT3"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[]},{"id":"CL_QGOH_01:ALT-B04","text":"The ontological and temporal level distinguishes the eternal necessity of substance from the internal succession of modes, with no temporal decree posited before nature.","refs":["CH_QGOH_PPDD:ALT4","CH_QGOH_PP_3:ALT4"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[]},{"id":"CL_QGOH_01:ALT-B05","text":"The methodological and performative level accumulates negations axiomatically, reduces objections to false schemas of imagination, and dissolves finalism by genealogically producing it.","refs":["CH_QGOH_PPDD:AS3","CH_QGOH_PP_3:A18","CH_QGOH_PP_3:D12"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[]}],"original_name":"Altitude Integration"},{"number":"15","name":"Internal Tensions, Mutation Points, and Fault Lines","items":[{"id":"CL_QGOH_01:FL-01","text":"Attributes are conceived through themselves and are really distinct, but the positive nexus by which the same attributes constitute one numerical substance is asserted rather than fully derived.","refs":["CH_QGOH_PPDD:MRT4"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"Tension","passages":[]},{"id":"CL_QGOH_01:FL-02","text":"God determines all modes, but a finite thing is immediately determined by another finite cause; the exact relation between divine causality and finite mediation remains compressed.","refs":["CH_QGOH_PP_2:MRT3"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"Tension","passages":[]},{"id":"CL_QGOH_01:FL-03","text":"The eternal production of infinite modes and the series of finite causes must cohere, but the transition between these orders is not fully formalized.","refs":["CH_QGOH_PP_2:MRT4"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"Tension","passages":[]},{"id":"CL_QGOH_01:FL-04","text":"God is called free because he acts from his own necessity, whereas will is necessary or compelled; the difference depends on the distinction between internal necessity and determination by another.","refs":["CH_QGOH_PP_3:MRT5"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"Tension","passages":[]},{"id":"CL_QGOH_01:FL-05","text":"The transition from necessary order to its highest perfection requires a notion of perfection resting neither on choice, nor on external good, nor on comparison of possibilities.","refs":["CH_QGOH_PP_3:MRT6"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"Tension","passages":[]},{"id":"CL_QGOH_01:MP-01","text":"Infinity changes from the pure exclusion of limit into a principle of inexhaustible production.","refs":["CH_QGOH_PPDD:SD2","CH_QGOH_PP_2:D3"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"Tension","passages":[]},{"id":"CL_QGOH_01:MP-02","text":"Freedom changes from indifference of will into the active necessity of one's own nature.","refs":["CH_QGOH_PP_2:NX4","CH_QGOH_PP_3:SD2"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"Tension","passages":[]},{"id":"CL_QGOH_01:MP-03","text":"Possibility and contingency change from states of things into indices of incomplete causal knowledge.","refs":["CH_QGOH_PP_3:NX9","CH_QGOH_PP_3:ME2"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"Tension","passages":[]},{"id":"CL_QGOH_01:MP-04","text":"Intellect and will change from privileged divine or human faculties into modes of Natura Naturata.","refs":["CH_QGOH_PP_2:D9","CH_QGOH_PP_3:SD4"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"Tension","passages":[]},{"id":"CL_QGOH_01:MP-05","text":"Causality changes from the image of transitive creation into immanent dependence that retains effects within the cause.","refs":["CH_QGOH_PP_2:SD1","CH_QGOH_PP_3:SD1"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"Tension","passages":[]}],"original_name":"Internal Tensions, Mutation Points, and Fault Lines"},{"number":"16","name":"Hidden System Image","items":[{"id":"CL_QGOH_01:HSI-01","text":"Reality is implicitly a self-causal, infinitely differentiated, and immanent machine, whose effects do not depart from it but express its power.","refs":["CH_QGOH_PPDD:SD1","CH_QGOH_PP_3:SD1"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[]},{"id":"CL_QGOH_01:HSI-02","text":"The singular thing is a temporal node of determinations, at once the effect of antecedent causes and the source of further effects.","refs":["CH_QGOH_PP_3:ME1","CH_QGOH_PP_3:A16"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[]},{"id":"CL_QGOH_01:HSI-03","text":"The subject is a mode conscious of appetite but ignorant of causes, and therefore prone to invent freedom and ends, not a principle outside nature.","refs":["CH_QGOH_PP_3:DNX3","CH_QGOH_PP_3:SD4"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[]},{"id":"CL_QGOH_01:HSI-04","text":"Time is a series of modal determinations within eternity, which itself is explained neither by duration nor by succession.","refs":["CH_QGOH_PPDD:SD6","CH_QGOH_PP_3:ALT4"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[]},{"id":"CL_QGOH_01:HSI-05","text":"Possible navigation is not an escape from causality, but a transition from the imagination of ends to knowledge of essences, properties, and causes.","refs":["CH_QGOH_PP_3:D13","CH_QGOH_PP_3:A14"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[]}],"original_name":"Hidden System Image"},{"number":"17","name":"Minimal Whole-Book Reconstruction Test","items":[{"id":"CL_QGOH_01:MRT-B01","text":"Axioms concerning substance, mode, necessary cause, attributes, and finite mediation suffice to reconstruct the movement of the present whole from the unity of substance to universal necessity.","refs":["CH_QGOH_PPDD:MRT1","CH_QGOH_PP_3:MRT1"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[]},{"id":"CL_QGOH_01:MRT-B02","text":"The firmest structure is the nexus between immanence, modal determination, and the negation of ontic contingency.","refs":["CH_QGOH_PP_3:MRT2"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[]},{"id":"CL_QGOH_01:MRT-B03","text":"Less determined remains the mediation between eternal production, infinite modes, and the series of finite causes, especially where the demonstration of regress is truncated in the source.","refs":["CH_QGOH_PP_2:MRT4","CH_QGOH_PP_2:MRT7"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[]},{"id":"CL_QGOH_01:MRT-B04","text":"The unity of substance beneath the plurality of attributes is necessary for reconstruction, but its positive coordination beyond the exclusions of plurality still resists axiomatic compression.","refs":["CH_QGOH_PPDD:MRT4"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[]},{"id":"CL_QGOH_01:MRT-B05","text":"Reconstruction preserves the true movement if it retains the sequence unproducibility → cause of itself → infinite productivity → immanence → determination → critique of finalism; individual scholastic proofs are not substitutes for the whole structure.","refs":["CH_QGOH_PPDD:MRT6","CH_QGOH_PP_3:MRT8"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[]}],"original_name":"Minimal Whole-Book Reconstruction Test"}],"revision":"CL_QGOH_01@R001","source_variant_id":"canonical:CL_QGOH_01:v1","source_language_code":"la","original_title":"Chapters 01–03"}],"identity":{"layer":"CL","kicker":"Cluster · Ethica","title":"Chapters 01–03","context":"Part One - Concerning God · passage 1 · Part One - Concerning God · passage 2 · Part One - Concerning God · passage 3","original_title":"Chapters 01–03","original_context":"Pars prima - De Deo · passage 1 · Pars prima - De Deo · passage 2 · Pars prima - De Deo · passage 3"},"original_title":"Chapters 01–03","title_language_code":"en"},{"id":"CL_QGOH_02","layer":"CL","title":"Chapters 04–06","order":2,"parents":["CH_QGOH_PP_4","CH_QGOH_PSDN","CH_QGOH_PS_2"],"revision":"CL_QGOH_02@R001","sha256":"c6ac6b016a86a222dbbc738d2e0f278f7bfd650e08fe5e4f8d750d073b0bcbf3","sections":[{"number":"1","name":"Book-Scale Argumental Spine","items":[{"id":"CL_QGOH_02:BAS-01","text":"Systema quaerit quomodo necessitas Naturae, constitutio Mentis ut idea Corporis et historia affectionum corporalium generationem finium imaginatorum ac iudiciorum aestimativorum explicent.","refs":["CH_QGOH_PP_4:AS1","CH_QGOH_PS_2:AS1"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[]},{"id":"CL_QGOH_02:BAS-02","text":"Thesis globalis est Naturam necessario et sine fine producere, dum Mens finita res per affectiones Corporis percipit atque dispositionem propriam in rerum structuram transfert.","refs":["CH_QGOH_PP_4:AS2","CH_QGOH_PSDN:AS2","CH_QGOH_PS_2:AS5"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[]},{"id":"CL_QGOH_02:BAS-03","text":"Argumentatio metaphysico-deductiva, physico-genetica et diagnostica progreditur a necessitate divina ad individuationem corporalem, inde ad imaginationem, memoriam et genealogiam teleologiae.","refs":["CH_QGOH_PSDN:AS3","CH_QGOH_PS_2:AS3","CH_QGOH_PP_4:AS3"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[]},{"id":"CL_QGOH_02:BAS-04","text":"Clausura causalis attributorum et identitas ordinis idearum cum ordine rerum simul dualismum Mentis et Corporis atque causalitatem finalem excludunt.","refs":["CH_QGOH_PSDN:AS4","CH_QGOH_PSDN:A11"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[]},{"id":"CL_QGOH_02:BAS-05","text":"Motus verus est recursivus: metaphysica Mentem ex Natura deducit, physica cognitionem ex Corpore constituit, deinde haec cognitio anthropocentricam metaphysicam ut projectionem corporis detegit.","refs":["CH_QGOH_PSDN:SD2","CH_QGOH_PP_4:SD2"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[]},{"id":"CL_QGOH_02:BAS-06","text":"Imaginatio mutatur ex nomine generali praeiudicii in mechanismum vestigiorum corporalium, quo absentia praesentantur, memoriae conectuntur et valores rebus attribuuntur.","refs":["CH_QGOH_PS_2:A5","CH_QGOH_PP_4:D5"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[]},{"id":"CL_QGOH_02:BAS-07","text":"Error non remanet mera carentia cognitionis, sed fit effectus positive generatus ex affectione, utilitate, memoria et defectu contextus causarum efficientium.","refs":["CH_QGOH_PP_4:A7","CH_QGOH_PS_2:D9"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[]},{"id":"CL_QGOH_02:BAS-08","text":"Clausura est diagnostica et praeparatoria, non beatifica: apparatus erroris restituitur, sed plena deductio cognitionis adaequatae et beatitudinis in posterum differtur.","refs":["CH_QGOH_PSDN:MRT6","CH_QGOH_PSDN:AF2"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[]}],"original_name":"Book-Scale Argumental Spine"},{"number":"2","name":"Global Load-Bearing Axioms","items":[{"id":"CL_QGOH_02:GA-01","text":"[Persistent] Omnia ex actuosa necessitate naturae divinae consequuntur; nullus finis externus productionem Naturae antecedit.","refs":["CH_QGOH_PP_4:A1","CH_QGOH_PSDN:A5"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[]},{"id":"CL_QGOH_02:GA-02","text":"[Persistent] Causa efficiens effectu prior est, et omnis mutatio finita per alias causas determinatur potius quam per effectum futurum.","refs":["CH_QGOH_PP_4:A3","CH_QGOH_PS_2:A1"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[]},{"id":"CL_QGOH_02:GA-03","text":"[Persistent] Cogitatio et Extensio unam eandemque substantiam exprimunt, sed modi cuiusque attributi intra propriam seriem causaliter producuntur.","refs":["CH_QGOH_PSDN:A8","CH_QGOH_PSDN:A9"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[]},{"id":"CL_QGOH_02:GA-04","text":"[Persistent] Mens humana non est substantia separata, sed idea Corporis actu existentis, cum quo unum idemque individuum constituit.","refs":["CH_QGOH_PSDN:AS2","CH_QGOH_PS_2:A7"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[]},{"id":"CL_QGOH_02:GA-05","text":"[Mutating] Corpus a modo Extensionis determinato in individuum dynamicum mutatur, cuius identitas in ratione motus et quietis, non in partibus permanentibus, consistit.","refs":["CH_QGOH_PSDN:A18","CH_QGOH_PS_2:A4"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[]},{"id":"CL_QGOH_02:GA-06","text":"[Emergent] Quaelibet affectio naturam corporis affecti et afficientis involvit, sed perceptio externi constitutionem Corporis percipientis potissimum indicat.","refs":["CH_QGOH_PS_2:A2","CH_QGOH_PS_2:A9"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[]},{"id":"CL_QGOH_02:GA-07","text":"[Mutating] Imaginatio est effectus mentalis affectionum et vestigiorum corporalium, non facultas a corpore separata nec mera imago rei externae.","refs":["CH_QGOH_PP_4:AS3","CH_QGOH_PS_2:A5"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[]},{"id":"CL_QGOH_02:GA-08","text":"[Persistent] Cognitio finita per ideas affectionum procedit atque, si earum contextum causaliter integratum non comprehendit, necessario partialis et inadaequata manet.","refs":["CH_QGOH_PS_2:AS2","CH_QGOH_PS_2:FGS8"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[]},{"id":"CL_QGOH_02:GA-09","text":"[Mutating] Perfectio non conformitas ad exemplar externum est, sed gradus naturae, potentiae et autonomiae causalis rei singularis.","refs":["CH_QGOH_PP_4:A11","CH_QGOH_PSDN:D11"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[]},{"id":"CL_QGOH_02:GA-10","text":"[Emergent] Error teleologicus et aestimativus oritur cum ignorantia causarum, utilitas corporis et ordo consuetudinarius imaginationis in iudicium universale de Natura coalescunt.","refs":["CH_QGOH_PP_4:A7","CH_QGOH_PP_4:A8","CH_QGOH_PS_2:NX5"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[]},{"id":"CL_QGOH_02:GA-11","text":"[Attractor] Adaequatio requirit ideam per contextum causarum intelligi; neque convenientia externa neque praesentia imaginata sufficit.","refs":["CH_QGOH_PSDN:SNX3","CH_QGOH_PS_2:A10"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[]},{"id":"CL_QGOH_02:GA-12","text":"[Emergent] Metaphysica immanens psychologiam corporis generat, quae vicissim teleologiam transcendentem ut effectum localem huius corporis explicat.","refs":["CH_QGOH_PSDN:AS3","CH_QGOH_PP_4:SD1"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[]},{"id":"CL_QGOH_02:GA-13","text":"[Terminal] Nullus locus extra Naturam neque ulla perspectiva finita extra historiam proprii Corporis admittitur.","refs":["CH_QGOH_PSDN:SNX1","CH_QGOH_PS_2:SNX1"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[]}],"original_name":"Global Load-Bearing Axioms"},{"number":"3","name":"Recurrence, Mutation, and Displacement Matrix","items":[{"id":"CL_QGOH_02:RMD-01","text":"Necessitas identitatem functionalem servat, sed ab exclusione finis ad productionem attributorum, corporum, affectionum et idearum extenditur.","refs":["CH_QGOH_PP_4:A1","CH_QGOH_PSDN:A5"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[]},{"id":"CL_QGOH_02:RMD-02","text":"Deus ab obiecto polemicae contra artificem indigentem in causam immanentem et actuosam omnium serierum causalium mutatur.","refs":["CH_QGOH_PP_4:NX2","CH_QGOH_PSDN:ME1"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[]},{"id":"CL_QGOH_02:RMD-03","text":"Causa efficiens a criterio refutandae teleologiae migrat ad regulam causalitatis attributivae et determinationis corporum ab aliis corporibus.","refs":["CH_QGOH_PP_4:A3","CH_QGOH_PSDN:A9","CH_QGOH_PS_2:A1"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[]},{"id":"CL_QGOH_02:RMD-04","text":"Ordo bifariam codificatur: ut attributum obiectivum rebus ad commodum imaginationis dispositis negatur, sed ut connexio causalis rerum et idearum retinetur.","refs":["CH_QGOH_PP_4:NX6","CH_QGOH_PSDN:A11"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[]},{"id":"CL_QGOH_02:RMD-05","text":"Imaginatio a fonte generali finium et valorum ad mechanismum corporeum praesentiae absentis, memoriae et consuetudinis specificatur.","refs":["CH_QGOH_PP_4:AS3","CH_QGOH_PS_2:A5"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[]},{"id":"CL_QGOH_02:RMD-06","text":"Perfectio ab immunitate divina ab indigentia transit ad potentiam singularis rei et deinde ad capacitatem Corporis pluribus modis agendi ac patiendi.","refs":["CH_QGOH_PP_4:A6","CH_QGOH_PP_4:A11","CH_QGOH_PSDN:D11"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[]},{"id":"CL_QGOH_02:RMD-07","text":"Unio Mentis et Corporis ab identitate abstracta sub duobus attributis ad historiam concretam affectionum eiusdem individui amplificatur.","refs":["CH_QGOH_PSDN:AS2","CH_QGOH_PS_2:A7"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[]},{"id":"CL_QGOH_02:RMD-08","text":"Individuum humanum, prius substantia negatum, positive restituitur ut ratio dynamica per substitutionem et variationem partium perseverans.","refs":["CH_QGOH_PSDN:NX4","CH_QGOH_PS_2:SD1"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[]},{"id":"CL_QGOH_02:RMD-09","text":"Ignorantia ab asylo rhetorico voluntatis divinae in statum formalem ideae affectionis sine sufficienti contextu causarum transfertur.","refs":["CH_QGOH_PP_4:D4","CH_QGOH_PS_2:FGS8"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[]},{"id":"CL_QGOH_02:RMD-10","text":"Beatitudo attractor directionis manet, sed postquam munus ordinandi transitum ad theoriam Mentis perfecit, ab hac clausura locali abscedit.","refs":["CH_QGOH_PSDN:AS1","CH_QGOH_PSDN:MRT6"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[]}],"original_name":"Recurrence, Mutation, and Displacement Matrix"},{"number":"4","name":"Lower-Layer vs Current-Level Distinction","items":[{"id":"CL_QGOH_02:CGD-01","text":"Gradatio perfectionis inter effectus immediate et mediate productos manet machina localis refutationis teleologiae neque structuram psychophysicam totius gregis per se ordinat.","refs":["CH_QGOH_PP_4:A4"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[]},{"id":"CL_QGOH_02:CGD-02","text":"Definitio essentiae per reciprocitatem strictam localiter tuetur distinctionem inter dependentiam a Deo et constitutionem rei, sed non fit principium dominante huius synthesis.","refs":["CH_QGOH_PSDN:A1"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[]},{"id":"CL_QGOH_02:CGD-03","text":"Vestigia in partibus fluidis mechanismum localem imaginationis praebent; gradus praesens retinet functionem geneticam, non exclusivitatem huius causae physicae.","refs":["CH_QGOH_PS_2:A5","CH_QGOH_PS_2:MRT5"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[]},{"id":"CL_QGOH_02:CGD-04","text":"Necessitas divina, causalitas attributiva et determinatio corporum sursum amplificantur in unam doctrinam causalitatis immanentis sine contingentia principiali.","refs":["CH_QGOH_PSDN:SD4"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[]},{"id":"CL_QGOH_02:CGD-05","text":"Reductio iudiciorum ad imaginationem et doctrina affectionis coniunguntur in propositionem superiorem secundum quam perceptio externi constitutionem corporis percipientis exprimit.","refs":["CH_QGOH_PP_4:D5","CH_QGOH_PS_2:A9"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[]},{"id":"CL_QGOH_02:CGD-06","text":"Transitus a corpore ut obiecto Mentis ad individuum relationale efficit ut cognitio, memoria et identitas personalis ex eadem structura dynamica deriventur.","refs":["CH_QGOH_PS_2:SD1"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[]},{"id":"CL_QGOH_02:CGD-07","text":"Negatio ordinis imaginarii non superat ordinem causalium connexionum; homonymia inferior duabus functionibus distinctis hoc gradu reordinatur.","refs":["CH_QGOH_PP_4:NX6","CH_QGOH_PSDN:A11"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[]},{"id":"CL_QGOH_02:CGD-08","text":"Promissio beatitudinis transitionem ad doctrinam Mentis movet, sed eius demonstratio extra clausuram praesentis gregis manet.","refs":["CH_QGOH_PSDN:MRT6"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[]}],"original_name":"Lower-Layer vs Current-Level Distinction"},{"number":"5","name":"Global Apophatic Structure","items":[{"id":"CL_QGOH_02:GNX-01","text":"Natura non agit propter fines futuros neque media ad exemplar extrinsecum disponit.","refs":["CH_QGOH_PP_4:A2"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"Negative","passages":[]},{"id":"CL_QGOH_02:GNX-02","text":"Potentia Dei non est voluntas regia quae res contingenter eligit, conservat aut destruit.","refs":["CH_QGOH_PSDN:NX1"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"Negative","passages":[]},{"id":"CL_QGOH_02:GNX-03","text":"Mens et Corpus non sunt substantiae realiter separatae quarum commercium causalitatem transversam requirat.","refs":["CH_QGOH_PSDN:NX3"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"Negative","passages":[]},{"id":"CL_QGOH_02:GNX-04","text":"Idea non causatur ab ideato ut modo alterius attributi; utraque expressio propriam seriem causalem servat.","refs":["CH_QGOH_PSDN:NX2","CH_QGOH_PSDN:A9"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"Negative","passages":[]},{"id":"CL_QGOH_02:GNX-05","text":"Mens Corpus, externa et se ipsam non cognitione immediata ac sui causa sufficienti apprehendit.","refs":["CH_QGOH_PS_2:NX6"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"Negative","passages":[]},{"id":"CL_QGOH_02:GNX-06","text":"Identitas individui non in permanentia partium, figurae, magnitudinis vel situs consistit.","refs":["CH_QGOH_PS_2:NX2"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"Negative","passages":[]},{"id":"CL_QGOH_02:GNX-07","text":"Imaginatio non est per se falsitas; error ex absentia ideae quae praesentiam imaginatam corrigat constituitur.","refs":["CH_QGOH_PS_2:NX4"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"Negative","passages":[]},{"id":"CL_QGOH_02:GNX-08","text":"Bonum, malum, ordo et pulchritudo non sunt naturae intrinsecae rerum quae independentem normam ontologicam exhibeant.","refs":["CH_QGOH_PP_4:NX7"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"Negative","passages":[]}],"original_name":"Global Apophatic Structure"},{"number":"6","name":"Deep Book Apophatics","items":[{"id":"CL_QGOH_02:DBN-01","text":"Falsum esse debet mundum propter hominem factum esse, quia haec positio affectionem localem in rationem universalis productionis convertit.","refs":["CH_QGOH_PP_4:SNX2"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"Negative","passages":[]},{"id":"CL_QGOH_02:DBN-02","text":"Falsum esse debet effectum futurum causam efficientem antecedentem regere posse, quia talis ordo causalitatem invertit.","refs":["CH_QGOH_PP_4:AS4"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"Negative","passages":[]},{"id":"CL_QGOH_02:DBN-03","text":"Falsum esse debet Mentem esse spectatorem sui et mundi a Corpore independentem atque sibi immediate transparentem.","refs":["CH_QGOH_PS_2:DNX1"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"Negative","passages":[]},{"id":"CL_QGOH_02:DBN-04","text":"Falsum esse debet perceptionem externi consistere in similitudine figurativa rei immediate recepta.","refs":["CH_QGOH_PS_2:DNX3"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"Negative","passages":[]},{"id":"CL_QGOH_02:DBN-05","text":"Falsum esse debet individuum per materiam permanentem definiri, quia identitas eius variationem partium tolerat.","refs":["CH_QGOH_PS_2:DNX2"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"Negative","passages":[]},{"id":"CL_QGOH_02:DBN-06","text":"Falsum esse debet nomen valoris vel ordinis proprietatem realem eo ipso significare.","refs":["CH_QGOH_PP_4:NX9"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"Negative","passages":[]},{"id":"CL_QGOH_02:DBN-07","text":"Falsum esse debet veritatem ideae sola comparatione externa cum obiecto constitui; aliter causalitas interna Cogitationis corrueret.","refs":["CH_QGOH_PSDN:NX8"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"Negative","passages":[]}],"original_name":"Deep Book Apophatics"},{"number":"7","name":"Super-Apophatics of the Whole","items":[{"id":"CL_QGOH_02:BSA-01","text":"Extra Naturam nullum tribunal ontologicum manet ex quo Deus, res, perfectiones et defectus communi norma iudicentur.","refs":["CH_QGOH_PSDN:SNX1"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"Negative","passages":[]},{"id":"CL_QGOH_02:BSA-02","text":"Perspectiva finita sine corpore, historia affectionum et ordine causarum ontologice indisponibilis est.","refs":["CH_QGOH_PS_2:SNX1"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"Negative","passages":[]},{"id":"CL_QGOH_02:BSA-03","text":"Praesentia pura rei externae Mentis humanae non datur; externum tantum per affectionem Corporis apparet.","refs":["CH_QGOH_PS_2:NX3","CH_QGOH_PS_2:D7"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"Negative","passages":[]},{"id":"CL_QGOH_02:BSA-04","text":"Norma finalis quae omnem varietatem rerum ad unum exemplar perfectionis redigat inadmissibilis est.","refs":["CH_QGOH_PP_4:SNX1"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"Negative","passages":[]},{"id":"CL_QGOH_02:BSA-05","text":"Ruptura contingens seriei causarum, sive in voluntate divina sive in motu corporis, systematis causalitatem dissolveret.","refs":["CH_QGOH_PSDN:SD4","CH_QGOH_PS_2:A1"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"Negative","passages":[]},{"id":"CL_QGOH_02:BSA-06","text":"Cognitio adaequata totius contextus manet possibilitas nondum exhibita circa quam diagnosis cognitionis confusae ordinatur.","refs":["CH_QGOH_PS_2:D9","CH_QGOH_PSDN:MRT6"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"Negative","passages":[]}],"original_name":"Super-Apophatics of the Whole"},{"number":"8","name":"Cross-Unit Derivative Propositions","items":[{"id":"CL_QGOH_02:XD-01","text":"Teleologia non tantum falsa doctrina, sed effectus naturaliter generatus Mentis corporeae fit: affectio perspectivam localem praebet, imaginatio autem eam in causam rerum transformat.","refs":["CL_QGOH_02:GA-06","CL_QGOH_02:GA-07","CL_QGOH_02:GA-10","CH_QGOH_PP_4:AS3","CH_QGOH_PS_2:AS5"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"From","passages":[]},{"id":"CL_QGOH_02:XD-02","text":"Anthropocentrismus est dilatatio illegitima indicii corporalis, qua id quod constitutionem corporis humani ostendit pro natura universali obiecti accipitur.","refs":["CL_QGOH_02:GA-06","CL_QGOH_02:GA-10","CH_QGOH_PP_4:SD2","CH_QGOH_PS_2:A9"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"From","passages":[]},{"id":"CL_QGOH_02:XD-03","text":"Ignorantia causalitatis non vacuum iners est: vestigia et consuetudines affectionum seriem imaginativam efficiunt quae finem fictum loco causae absentis collocat.","refs":["CL_QGOH_02:GA-02","CL_QGOH_02:GA-07","CL_QGOH_02:GA-08","CH_QGOH_PP_4:AS4","CH_QGOH_PS_2:A5","CH_QGOH_PS_2:NX5"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"From","passages":[]},{"id":"CL_QGOH_02:XD-04","text":"Correctio cognitionis non fit per fugam e Corpore, sed per integrationem ideae affectionis in ampliorem ordinem causarum et idearum.","refs":["CL_QGOH_02:GA-03","CL_QGOH_02:GA-04","CL_QGOH_02:GA-11","CH_QGOH_PSDN:A11","CH_QGOH_PS_2:A10"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"From","passages":[]},{"id":"CL_QGOH_02:XD-05","text":"Affectio corporalis et idea eius non sunt eventus causaliter coniungendi, sed idem eventus sub Extensione et Cogitatione expressus.","refs":["CL_QGOH_02:GA-03","CL_QGOH_02:GA-04","CL_QGOH_02:GA-07","CH_QGOH_PSDN:D4","CH_QGOH_PS_2:A7"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"From","passages":[]},{"id":"CL_QGOH_02:XD-06","text":"Negatio ordinis imaginarii causalitatem obiectivam non destruit; e contrario, distinctionem inter ordinem facilitatis memorialis et ordinem necessariae connexionis instituit.","refs":["CL_QGOH_02:GA-02","CL_QGOH_02:GA-11","CH_QGOH_PP_4:NX6","CH_QGOH_PSDN:A11"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"From","passages":[]},{"id":"CL_QGOH_02:XD-07","text":"Identitas dynamica Corporis memoriam personalem sine permanentia materiae possibilitatem reddit, quia ratio individui et vestigia affectionum per substitutiones partium perseverare possunt.","refs":["CL_QGOH_02:GA-05","CL_QGOH_02:GA-07","CH_QGOH_PS_2:SD1","CH_QGOH_PS_2:SD3"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"From","passages":[]},{"id":"CL_QGOH_02:XD-08","text":"Maior apertura Corporis ad affectiones bifurcationem producit: potentiam perceptivam auget, sed sine cognitione causarum materiam confusionis simul multiplicat.","refs":["CL_QGOH_02:GA-06","CL_QGOH_02:GA-08","CL_QGOH_02:GA-11","CH_QGOH_PS_2:SD2","CH_QGOH_PS_2:D9"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"From","passages":[]},{"id":"CL_QGOH_02:XD-09","text":"Auctoritas interpretis transcendentis ex eodem apparatu nascitur quo Mens absentia imaginatur: obscuritas causalis stabilitur per concatenationes affectionum donec cognitio causarum eas dissolvat.","refs":["CL_QGOH_02:GA-08","CL_QGOH_02:GA-10","CH_QGOH_PS_2:FGS8"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"From","passages":[]}],"original_name":"Cross-Unit Derivative Propositions"},{"number":"9","name":"Book-Scale Super-Derivatives","items":[{"id":"CL_QGOH_02:BSD-01","text":"Realitas fit rete immanens expressionum causalium cui nullum exterior est: eadem natura sub attributis diversis exprimitur atque per modos finitos determinatur.","refs":["CH_QGOH_PSDN:ME1","CH_QGOH_PSDN:SNX1"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[]},{"id":"CL_QGOH_02:BSD-02","text":"Subiectum humanum fit individuum distributum et historicum, cuius reflexio ex ideis affectionum Corporis relationaliter perseverantis oritur.","refs":["CH_QGOH_PS_2:SD4","CH_QGOH_PS_2:SD1"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[]},{"id":"CL_QGOH_02:BSD-03","text":"Error fit intelligibilis eventus naturae: imaginatio positive producitur, dum falsitas ex carentia ideae corrigentis et contextus causalis nascitur.","refs":["CH_QGOH_PS_2:NX4","CH_QGOH_PP_4:D4"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[]},{"id":"CL_QGOH_02:BSD-04","text":"Temporalitas duplicatur inter necessitatem aeternam productionis et historiam finitam vestigiorum, qua praeteritum velut praesens redit et futurum velut finis causaliter retroicitur.","refs":["CH_QGOH_PP_4:AS2","CH_QGOH_PS_2:SD3","CH_QGOH_PP_4:AS4"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[]},{"id":"CL_QGOH_02:BSD-05","text":"Quod velut finis futurus causam regere videtur reclassificatur ut effectus praesens imaginationis intra seriem causarum efficientium.","refs":["CH_QGOH_PP_4:AS3","CH_QGOH_PS_2:NX4"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[]},{"id":"CL_QGOH_02:BSD-06","text":"Adaequatio fit amplificatio contextus causalis, non abolitio corporis: idea localis corrigitur cum locus eius in connexione ampliori intelligitur.","refs":["CH_QGOH_PS_2:A10","CH_QGOH_PS_2:SD2"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[]},{"id":"CL_QGOH_02:BSD-07","text":"Potestas interpretativa quae causas occultas voluntati divinae attribuit pendet a reproductione sociali et memoriali ignorantiae corporalis.","refs":["CH_QGOH_PS_2:NX5"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[]},{"id":"CL_QGOH_02:BSD-08","text":"Methodus investigationis a quaestione finis et similitudinis ad generationem, determinationem, dispositionem corporis et ordinem causarum transfertur.","refs":["CH_QGOH_PP_4:ALT3"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[]}],"original_name":"Book-Scale Super-Derivatives"},{"number":"10","name":"Recursive Loops, Escalation Paths, and Conceptual Attractors","items":[{"id":"CL_QGOH_02:RLE-01","text":"Ignorantia causae efficientis quaestionem finis generat; finis imaginatus ignorantiam nomine voluntatis tegit; haec clausura investigationem causarum impedit atque ignorantiam reprodu­cit.","refs":["CH_QGOH_PP_4:A7","CH_QGOH_PP_4:AS4"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[]},{"id":"CL_QGOH_02:RLE-02","text":"Affectio externi vestigium relinquit, vestigium absentiam praesentat, memoria praesentias consuetudine conectit, utilitas autem hanc seriem in iudicia valoris stabilit.","refs":["CH_QGOH_PS_2:A5","CH_QGOH_PS_2:NX5","CH_QGOH_PP_4:A8"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[]},{"id":"CL_QGOH_02:RLE-03","text":"Complexitas Corporis plures affectiones et ideas producit; inde simul crescunt potentia percipiendi et periculum confusionis, donec cognitio causarum bifurcationem dirimat.","refs":["CH_QGOH_PS_2:D1","CH_QGOH_PS_2:SD2"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[]},{"id":"CL_QGOH_02:RLE-04","text":"Cognitio causarum circuitum teleologicum dissolvit, quia praesentiam imaginatam in ordinem determinationum reinserit et medium auctoritatis ex obscuritate ortae evacuat.","refs":["CH_QGOH_PS_2:A10"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[]},{"id":"CL_QGOH_02:RLE-05","text":"Immanentia est attractor principalis: Deus, attributa, corpora, ideae et iudicia ad diversas expressiones unius Naturae causaliter clausae reducuntur.","refs":["CH_QGOH_PSDN:A8","CH_QGOH_PSDN:ME1"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[]},{"id":"CL_QGOH_02:RLE-06","text":"Corpus relationale est attractor medians, quia identitatem, capacitatem perceptivam, memoriam, cognitionem sui et variationem mentium eadem ratione ordinat.","refs":["CH_QGOH_PS_2:A3","CH_QGOH_PSDN:ME5"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[]},{"id":"CL_QGOH_02:RLE-07","text":"Circuitus totius gregis est metaphysica necessitatis → constitutio Mentis corporeae → genesis imaginationis → diagnosis metaphysicae teleologicae → restitutio necessitatis.","refs":["CH_QGOH_PSDN:AS3","CH_QGOH_PS_2:AS6","CH_QGOH_PP_4:SD3"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[]},{"id":"CL_QGOH_02:RLE-08","text":"Finis subit transformationem terminalem: desinit esse causa Naturae et fit indicium mechanismi imaginativi qui causam cum effectu invertit.","refs":["CH_QGOH_PP_4:AS3","CH_QGOH_PP_4:AS4"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[]}],"original_name":"Recursive Loops, Escalation Paths, and Conceptual Attractors"},{"number":"11","name":"Formal Generative Schema of the Whole Book","items":[{"id":"CL_QGOH_02:FGS-B01","text":"Necessitas actuosa divina → productio sine fine → exclusio artificis indigentis et contingentiae principialis.","refs":["CH_QGOH_PP_4:AS2","CH_QGOH_PSDN:A5"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[]},{"id":"CL_QGOH_02:FGS-B02","text":"Attributa distincta + causalitas intra attributum + idem ordo → una res sub Cogitatione et Extensione dupliciter expressa.","refs":["CH_QGOH_PSDN:A9","CH_QGOH_PSDN:A11"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[]},{"id":"CL_QGOH_02:FGS-B03","text":"Ratio motus et quietis → individuum compositum → identitas per mutationem partium → Corpus humanum ut obiectum Mentis.","refs":["CH_QGOH_PS_2:A4","CH_QGOH_PSDN:AS2"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[]},{"id":"CL_QGOH_02:FGS-B04","text":"Occursus corporum → affectio naturam utriusque implicans → idea affectionis → externum per dispositionem proprii Corporis praesentatum.","refs":["CH_QGOH_PS_2:A2","CH_QGOH_PS_2:AS5"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[]},{"id":"CL_QGOH_02:FGS-B05","text":"Affectio perseverans → vestigium → praesentia rei absentis → concatenatio consuetudinaria → imaginatio et memoria.","refs":["CH_QGOH_PS_2:A5","CH_QGOH_PS_2:SD3"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[]},{"id":"CL_QGOH_02:FGS-B06","text":"Cognitio mediata + contextus causarum incompletus + utilitas → confusio → fines, valores et ordines imaginarii.","refs":["CH_QGOH_PS_2:FGS8","CH_QGOH_PP_4:A8"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[]},{"id":"CL_QGOH_02:FGS-B07","text":"Iudicium corporis localis universalizatum → Natura ad commodum humanum mensurata → teleologia et imperfectio fictae.","refs":["CH_QGOH_PP_4:SD2","CH_QGOH_PP_4:SNX1"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[]},{"id":"CL_QGOH_02:FGS-B08","text":"Reintegratio in ordinem causalium connexionum → finis reclassificatus ut effectus → valor restitutus ad relationem → perfectio mensurata ex potentia rei.","refs":["CH_QGOH_PP_4:AS3","CH_QGOH_PP_4:FGS5"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[]}],"original_name":"Formal Generative Schema of the Whole Book"},{"number":"12","name":"Whole-Book Argument Flow Map","items":[{"id":"CL_QGOH_02:AFM-B01","text":"Necessitas divina → causalitas attributiva → unitas Mentis et Corporis → individuum dynamicum → affectio → imaginatio et memoria → cognitio localis et confusa.","refs":["CH_QGOH_PSDN:A5","CH_QGOH_PS_2:A7","CH_QGOH_PS_2:AS6"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[]},{"id":"CL_QGOH_02:AFM-B02","text":"Ignorantia causarum → quaestio finis → praesentia imaginata → utilitas corporis → iudicium aestimativum → proiectio anthropocentrica → Natura falso ordinata.","refs":["CH_QGOH_PP_4:A7","CH_QGOH_PP_4:A8","CH_QGOH_PP_4:ME4"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[]},{"id":"CL_QGOH_02:AFM-B03","text":"Diagnosis projectionis → distinctio ordinis imaginarii ab ordine causali → ampliatio contextus idearum → perfectio ex natura et potentia → restitutio immanentiae.","refs":["CH_QGOH_PSDN:A11","CH_QGOH_PP_4:A11","CH_QGOH_PSDN:ME1"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[]}],"original_name":"Whole-Book Argument Flow Map"},{"number":"13","name":"Macro Map-Effects","items":[{"id":"CL_QGOH_02:MME-01","text":"Deus reclassificatur ab artifice voluntario in causam immanentem cuius potentia cum essentia actuosa coincidit.","refs":["CH_QGOH_PSDN:A5","CH_QGOH_PSDN:ME1"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[]},{"id":"CL_QGOH_02:MME-02","text":"Natura reclassificatur ab opere ad finem disposito in productionem necessariam sine exteriori et sine norma extrinseca.","refs":["CH_QGOH_PP_4:AS2","CH_QGOH_PP_4:SNX1"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[]},{"id":"CL_QGOH_02:MME-03","text":"Homo reclassificatur a substantia vel anima separata in modum finitum cuius Mens idea Corporis est.","refs":["CH_QGOH_PSDN:NX4","CH_QGOH_PSDN:D8"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[]},{"id":"CL_QGOH_02:MME-04","text":"Corpus reclassificatur ab aggregato materiali in relationem motus et quietis quae per mutationes componentium perseverat.","refs":["CH_QGOH_PS_2:FGS2"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[]},{"id":"CL_QGOH_02:MME-05","text":"Perceptio reclassificatur a receptione immediata obiecti in ideam affectionis quae externum per constitutionem percipientis exhibet.","refs":["CH_QGOH_PS_2:NX3","CH_QGOH_PS_2:FGS5"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[]},{"id":"CL_QGOH_02:MME-06","text":"Imaginatio et memoria reclassificantur a thesauro imaginum falsarum in effectus neutros vestigiorum et consuetudinum corporis.","refs":["CH_QGOH_PS_2:ME3","CH_QGOH_PS_2:ME4"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[]},{"id":"CL_QGOH_02:MME-07","text":"Ordo et valor dividuntur inter structuram causalem realem et modos imaginandi ex facilitate, utilitate atque affectione ortos.","refs":["CH_QGOH_PP_4:NX6","CH_QGOH_PP_4:A9","CH_QGOH_PSDN:A11"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[]},{"id":"CL_QGOH_02:MME-08","text":"Veritas perceptionis non amplius ex similitudine apparente, sed ex proprietatibus ideae et integratione eius in contextum causarum aestimatur.","refs":["CH_QGOH_PSDN:NX8"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[]}],"original_name":"Macro Map-Effects"},{"number":"14","name":"Altitude Integration","items":[{"id":"CL_QGOH_02:ALT-B01","text":"Gradus superficiei fines Naturae, unionem Mentis et Corporis, perceptionem externorum, imaginationem, memoriam et iudicia boni vel mali tractat.","refs":["CH_QGOH_PP_4:AS5","CH_QGOH_PS_2:AS6"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[]},{"id":"CL_QGOH_02:ALT-B02","text":"Gradus structuralis necessitatem divinam, clausuram causalem attributorum, rationem motus individui, affectiones et vestigia tamquam unum apparatum distribuit.","refs":["CH_QGOH_PSDN:AS4","CH_QGOH_PS_2:A4","CH_QGOH_PS_2:A5"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[]},{"id":"CL_QGOH_02:ALT-B03","text":"Gradus metastrategicus quaestionem a fine et qualitate apparente ad causas efficientes, condiciones corporales iudicii et ordinem generationis transfert.","refs":["CH_QGOH_PP_4:ALT3"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[]},{"id":"CL_QGOH_02:ALT-B04","text":"Gradus ontologicus et temporalis realitatem ut productionem immanentem, individuum ut relationem perseverantem et memoriam ut praeteritum corporaliter reactum constituit.","refs":["CH_QGOH_PS_2:SD1","CH_QGOH_PS_2:SD3"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[]},{"id":"CL_QGOH_02:ALT-B05","text":"Gradus methodologicus et performativus primum fines ac valores extrinsecos destruit, deinde machinam corporalem quae eos producit restituit, ita critique genealogiam causaliter perficiens.","refs":["CH_QGOH_PP_4:AS3","CH_QGOH_PS_2:ALT2"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[]}],"original_name":"Altitude Integration"},{"number":"15","name":"Internal Tensions, Mutation Points, and Fault Lines","items":[{"id":"CL_QGOH_02:FL-01","text":"Negatio ordinis ut attributi Naturae et affirmatio identitatis ordinis idearum ac rerum cohaerent tantum si ordo imaginativus ab ordine causali stricte distinguitur.","refs":["CH_QGOH_PP_4:NX6","CH_QGOH_PSDN:A11"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"Tension","passages":[]},{"id":"CL_QGOH_02:FL-02","text":"Identitas Mentis et Corporis ut unius individui cum clausura causarum intra attributa componenda est sine commercio causali neque mera duplicatione.","refs":["CH_QGOH_PSDN:MRT3","CH_QGOH_PSDN:DNX2"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"Tension","passages":[]},{"id":"CL_QGOH_02:FL-03","text":"Maior capacitas Corporis plura percipiendi cognitionem auget quantitative, sed adaequationem non praestat; transitus a pluralitate ad intellectum manet incompletus.","refs":["CH_QGOH_PS_2:SD2","CH_QGOH_PS_2:MRT6"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"Tension","passages":[]},{"id":"CL_QGOH_02:FL-04","text":"Idea affectionis naturam externi corporis involvit, sed constitutionem proprii Corporis potissimum indicat; mensura cognitionis externi inter praesentiam et inadaequationem instabilis manet.","refs":["CH_QGOH_PS_2:AS5","CH_QGOH_PS_2:A9"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"Tension","passages":[]},{"id":"CL_QGOH_02:FL-05","text":"Omnes ideae ex necessitate Naturae producuntur, sed distinctio inter adaequationem et errorem vim normativam retinere debet sine norma extrinseca.","refs":["CH_QGOH_PP_4:A1","CH_QGOH_PS_2:NX4","CH_QGOH_PSDN:SNX3"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"Tension","passages":[]},{"id":"CL_QGOH_02:FL-06","text":"Beatitudo motum argumenti dirigit, quamquam apparatus hic extractus tantum condiciones ontologicas et epistemicas eius ponit.","refs":["CH_QGOH_PSDN:AS1","CH_QGOH_PSDN:MRT6"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"Tension","passages":[]},{"id":"CL_QGOH_02:MP-01","text":"Causa finalis mutatur in effectum imaginationis et indicium ignorantiae causarum efficientium.","refs":["CH_QGOH_PP_4:AS3","CH_QGOH_PP_4:A7"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"Tension","passages":[]},{"id":"CL_QGOH_02:MP-02","text":"Corpus mutatur ab obiecto actu existente Mentis in individuum relationale cuius compositio gradus cognitionis determinat.","refs":["CH_QGOH_PSDN:D8","CH_QGOH_PS_2:SD1"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"Tension","passages":[]},{"id":"CL_QGOH_02:MP-03","text":"Idea mutatur a modo formaliter intra Cogitationem causato in registrum localem affectionum et historiae corporis.","refs":["CH_QGOH_PSDN:ME3","CH_QGOH_PS_2:SD3"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"Composite","passages":[]},{"id":"CL_QGOH_02:MP-04","text":"Imaginatio mutatur a cognitione simpliciter falsa in mechanismum neutralem, cui falsitas tantum per privationem correctionis accedit.","refs":["CH_QGOH_PS_2:NX4"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"Tension","passages":[]},{"id":"CL_QGOH_02:MP-05","text":"Perfectio mutatur a conformitate ad commodum vel exemplar in potentiam rei et capacitatem eius ex propria constitutione agendi.","refs":["CH_QGOH_PP_4:A11","CH_QGOH_PSDN:D11"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"Composite","passages":[]}],"original_name":"Internal Tensions, Mutation Points, and Fault Lines"},{"number":"16","name":"Hidden System Image","items":[{"id":"CL_QGOH_02:HSI-01","text":"Realitas implicite est machina infinita sui expressionis, cuius modi per necessarias series causarum producuntur neque ab exteriori artifice ordinantur.","refs":["CH_QGOH_PP_4:A12","CH_QGOH_PSDN:ME1"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[]},{"id":"CL_QGOH_02:HSI-02","text":"Individuum est vortex relationalis motus et quietis qui materiam permutat, figuram variat et tamen rationem constitutivam conservat.","refs":["CH_QGOH_PS_2:A4","CH_QGOH_PS_2:SD1"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[]},{"id":"CL_QGOH_02:HSI-03","text":"Mens est tabula activa occursuum Corporis, in qua externa simul apparent et secundum dispositionem recipientis refringuntur.","refs":["CH_QGOH_PS_2:AS5","CH_QGOH_PS_2:ME5"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[]},{"id":"CL_QGOH_02:HSI-04","text":"Tempus systematis aeternam necessitatem cum duratione finita coniungit: vestigia praeteritum in praesentiam revocant, dum finis imaginatus futurum in causam retrovertit.","refs":["CH_QGOH_PP_4:AS2","CH_QGOH_PS_2:SD3","CH_QGOH_PP_4:AS4"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[]},{"id":"CL_QGOH_02:HSI-05","text":"Captatio fit per utilitatem, memoriam et ignorantiam; fuga possibilis non est exitus e Natura vel e Corpore, sed navigatio ampliorem contextum causarum restituens.","refs":["CH_QGOH_PP_4:A8","CH_QGOH_PS_2:A10"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[]}],"original_name":"Hidden System Image"},{"number":"17","name":"Minimal Whole-Book Reconstruction Test","items":[{"id":"CL_QGOH_02:MRT-B01","text":"Axiomata necessitatis, identitatis Mentis et Corporis, individuationis relationalis, affectionis et imaginationis sufficiunt ad machinam principalem huius gregis restituendam.","refs":["CH_QGOH_PP_4:MRT6","CH_QGOH_PS_2:MRT1"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[]},{"id":"CL_QGOH_02:MRT-B02","text":"Structura firmissima est nexus inter causalitatem immanentem, Corpus compositum et ideam affectionis, ex quo perceptio localis atque teleologia proiectiva simul derivantur.","refs":["CH_QGOH_PS_2:MRT2","CH_QGOH_PP_4:SD2"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[]},{"id":"CL_QGOH_02:MRT-B03","text":"Nondum satis determinatur quomodo cognitio affectionum in cognitionem adaequatam et beatitudinem transeat.","refs":["CH_QGOH_PSDN:MRT6","CH_QGOH_PS_2:MRT7"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[]},{"id":"CL_QGOH_02:MRT-B04","text":"Explicatio imaginationis per vestigia causam sufficientem, non demonstratam causam exclusivam, praebet.","refs":["CH_QGOH_PS_2:MRT5"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[]},{"id":"CL_QGOH_02:MRT-B05","text":"Extensio doctrinae individui ad Naturam totam indicatur, sed demonstratio huius amplificationis suspenditur.","refs":["CH_QGOH_PS_2:MRT4"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[]},{"id":"CL_QGOH_02:MRT-B06","text":"Commissura maxime fragilis est distinctio inter ordinem imaginarium et ordinem causalem, una cum transitu a relationibus affectivis ad structuram obiectivam singularium rerum.","refs":["CH_QGOH_PP_4:MRT5","CH_QGOH_PSDN:A11"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[]},{"id":"CL_QGOH_02:MRT-B07","text":"Restitutio verum motum servat tantum si synthesis non ut series linearis, sed ut circuitus a necessitate ad Mentem corpoream et ab ea ad criticam teleologiae legitur.","refs":["CH_QGOH_PP_4:MRT6","CH_QGOH_PS_2:MRT3"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[]}],"original_name":"Minimal Whole-Book Reconstruction Test"}],"language_code":"la","language_name":"Latina","translations":[{"id":"CAREVAR_20261015T015439663661Z_97b59e61","language_code":"en","language_name":"English","title":"Chapters 04–06","source_sha256":"c6ac6b016a86a222dbbc738d2e0f278f7bfd650e08fe5e4f8d750d073b0bcbf3","sha256":"7b27bb289271268633268d6572d6b9e02b5b5d535b285fb90501e8a5717c7bd1","rendition_type":"machine","review_status":"machine","model":"gpt-5.6-terra","created":"2026-10-15T01:54:39.666971+00:00","sections":[{"number":"1","name":"Book-Scale Argumental Spine","items":[{"id":"CL_QGOH_02:BAS-01","text":"The system asks how the necessity of Nature, the constitution of Mind as the idea of Body, and the history of bodily affections explain the generation of imagined ends and evaluative judgments.","refs":["CH_QGOH_PP_4:AS1","CH_QGOH_PS_2:AS1"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[]},{"id":"CL_QGOH_02:BAS-02","text":"The global thesis is that Nature necessarily and without end produces, while the finite Mind perceives things through affections of the Body and transfers its own disposition into the structure of things.","refs":["CH_QGOH_PP_4:AS2","CH_QGOH_PSDN:AS2","CH_QGOH_PS_2:AS5"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[]},{"id":"CL_QGOH_02:BAS-03","text":"The metaphysical-deductive, physical-genetic, and diagnostic argument proceeds from divine necessity to bodily individuation, thence to imagination, memory, and the genealogy of teleology.","refs":["CH_QGOH_PSDN:AS3","CH_QGOH_PS_2:AS3","CH_QGOH_PP_4:AS3"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[]},{"id":"CL_QGOH_02:BAS-04","text":"The causal closure of attributes and the identity of the order of ideas with the order of things simultaneously exclude the dualism of Mind and Body and final causality.","refs":["CH_QGOH_PSDN:AS4","CH_QGOH_PSDN:A11"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[]},{"id":"CL_QGOH_02:BAS-05","text":"The true movement is recursive: metaphysics deduces Mind from Nature, physics constitutes cognition from Body, and then this cognition exposes anthropocentric metaphysics as a projection of the body.","refs":["CH_QGOH_PSDN:SD2","CH_QGOH_PP_4:SD2"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[]},{"id":"CL_QGOH_02:BAS-06","text":"Imagination is transformed from a general name for prejudice into a mechanism of bodily traces by which absences are presented, memories connected, and values attributed to things.","refs":["CH_QGOH_PS_2:A5","CH_QGOH_PP_4:D5"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[]},{"id":"CL_QGOH_02:BAS-07","text":"Error does not remain a mere lack of cognition, but becomes an effect positively generated from affection, utility, memory, and the lack of a context of efficient causes.","refs":["CH_QGOH_PP_4:A7","CH_QGOH_PS_2:D9"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[]},{"id":"CL_QGOH_02:BAS-08","text":"The closure is diagnostic and preparatory, not beatific: the apparatus of error is reconstructed, but the full deduction of adequate cognition and blessedness is deferred to the future.","refs":["CH_QGOH_PSDN:MRT6","CH_QGOH_PSDN:AF2"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[]}],"original_name":"Book-Scale Argumental Spine"},{"number":"2","name":"Global Load-Bearing Axioms","items":[{"id":"CL_QGOH_02:GA-01","text":"[Persistent] All things follow from the active necessity of the divine nature; no external end precedes the production of Nature.","refs":["CH_QGOH_PP_4:A1","CH_QGOH_PSDN:A5"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[]},{"id":"CL_QGOH_02:GA-02","text":"[Persistent] The efficient cause is prior to the effect, and every finite change is determined by other causes rather than by a future effect.","refs":["CH_QGOH_PP_4:A3","CH_QGOH_PS_2:A1"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[]},{"id":"CL_QGOH_02:GA-03","text":"[Persistent] Thought and Extension express one and the same substance, but the modes of each attribute are causally produced within their own series.","refs":["CH_QGOH_PSDN:A8","CH_QGOH_PSDN:A9"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[]},{"id":"CL_QGOH_02:GA-04","text":"[Persistent] The human Mind is not a separate substance, but the idea of a Body actually existing, with which it constitutes one and the same individual.","refs":["CH_QGOH_PSDN:AS2","CH_QGOH_PS_2:A7"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[]},{"id":"CL_QGOH_02:GA-05","text":"[Mutating] The Body is transformed from a determinate mode of Extension into a dynamic individual whose identity consists in a ratio of motion and rest, not in permanent parts.","refs":["CH_QGOH_PSDN:A18","CH_QGOH_PS_2:A4"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[]},{"id":"CL_QGOH_02:GA-06","text":"[Emergent] Every affection involves the nature of the affected and affecting body, but perception of the external chiefly indicates the constitution of the perceiving Body.","refs":["CH_QGOH_PS_2:A2","CH_QGOH_PS_2:A9"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[]},{"id":"CL_QGOH_02:GA-07","text":"[Mutating] Imagination is the mental effect of bodily affections and traces, not a faculty separate from the body nor a mere image of the external thing.","refs":["CH_QGOH_PP_4:AS3","CH_QGOH_PS_2:A5"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[]},{"id":"CL_QGOH_02:GA-08","text":"[Persistent] Finite cognition proceeds through ideas of affections and, if it does not comprehend their causally integrated context, necessarily remains partial and inadequate.","refs":["CH_QGOH_PS_2:AS2","CH_QGOH_PS_2:FGS8"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[]},{"id":"CL_QGOH_02:GA-09","text":"[Mutating] Perfection is not conformity to an external model, but the degree of the nature, power, and causal autonomy of a singular thing.","refs":["CH_QGOH_PP_4:A11","CH_QGOH_PSDN:D11"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[]},{"id":"CL_QGOH_02:GA-10","text":"[Emergent] Teleological and evaluative error arises when ignorance of causes, the utility of the body, and the customary order of imagination coalesce into a universal judgment concerning Nature.","refs":["CH_QGOH_PP_4:A7","CH_QGOH_PP_4:A8","CH_QGOH_PS_2:NX5"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[]},{"id":"CL_QGOH_02:GA-11","text":"[Attractor] Adequacy requires that an idea be understood through a context of causes; neither external correspondence nor imagined presence suffices.","refs":["CH_QGOH_PSDN:SNX3","CH_QGOH_PS_2:A10"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[]},{"id":"CL_QGOH_02:GA-12","text":"[Emergent] Immanent metaphysics generates a psychology of the body, which in turn explains transcendent teleology as a local effect of this body.","refs":["CH_QGOH_PSDN:AS3","CH_QGOH_PP_4:SD1"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[]},{"id":"CL_QGOH_02:GA-13","text":"[Terminal] No place outside Nature, nor any finite perspective outside the history of its own Body, is admitted.","refs":["CH_QGOH_PSDN:SNX1","CH_QGOH_PS_2:SNX1"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[]}],"original_name":"Global Load-Bearing Axioms"},{"number":"3","name":"Recurrence, Mutation, and Displacement Matrix","items":[{"id":"CL_QGOH_02:RMD-01","text":"Necessity preserves functional identity, but is extended from the exclusion of end to the production of attributes, bodies, affections, and ideas.","refs":["CH_QGOH_PP_4:A1","CH_QGOH_PSDN:A5"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[]},{"id":"CL_QGOH_02:RMD-02","text":"God is transformed from the object of polemic against a needy artificer into the immanent and active cause of all causal series.","refs":["CH_QGOH_PP_4:NX2","CH_QGOH_PSDN:ME1"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[]},{"id":"CL_QGOH_02:RMD-03","text":"The efficient cause migrates from a criterion for refuting teleology to a rule of attributive causality and the determination of bodies by other bodies.","refs":["CH_QGOH_PP_4:A3","CH_QGOH_PSDN:A9","CH_QGOH_PS_2:A1"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[]},{"id":"CL_QGOH_02:RMD-04","text":"Order is codified in two ways: it is denied as an objective attribute of things arranged for the convenience of imagination, but retained as the causal connection of things and ideas.","refs":["CH_QGOH_PP_4:NX6","CH_QGOH_PSDN:A11"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[]},{"id":"CL_QGOH_02:RMD-05","text":"Imagination is specified from a general source of ends and values into a bodily mechanism of absent presence, memory, and custom.","refs":["CH_QGOH_PP_4:AS3","CH_QGOH_PS_2:A5"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[]},{"id":"CL_QGOH_02:RMD-06","text":"Perfection passes from divine immunity from need to the power of the singular thing and then to the capacity of the Body to act and be acted upon in many ways.","refs":["CH_QGOH_PP_4:A6","CH_QGOH_PP_4:A11","CH_QGOH_PSDN:D11"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[]},{"id":"CL_QGOH_02:RMD-07","text":"The union of Mind and Body is amplified from abstract identity under two attributes to the concrete history of affections of the same individual.","refs":["CH_QGOH_PSDN:AS2","CH_QGOH_PS_2:A7"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[]},{"id":"CL_QGOH_02:RMD-08","text":"The human individual, previously denied as substance, is positively reconstructed as a dynamic ratio persisting through the substitution and variation of parts.","refs":["CH_QGOH_PSDN:NX4","CH_QGOH_PS_2:SD1"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[]},{"id":"CL_QGOH_02:RMD-09","text":"Ignorance is transferred from a rhetorical asylum of divine will into the formal state of an idea of affection without a sufficient context of causes.","refs":["CH_QGOH_PP_4:D4","CH_QGOH_PS_2:FGS8"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[]},{"id":"CL_QGOH_02:RMD-10","text":"Blessedness remains an attractor of direction, but after fulfilling the function of ordering the transition to the theory of Mind, it departs from this local closure.","refs":["CH_QGOH_PSDN:AS1","CH_QGOH_PSDN:MRT6"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[]}],"original_name":"Recurrence, Mutation, and Displacement Matrix"},{"number":"4","name":"Lower-Layer vs Current-Level Distinction","items":[{"id":"CL_QGOH_02:CGD-01","text":"The gradation of perfection among immediately and mediately produced effects remains a local engine for refuting teleology and does not by itself order the psychophysical structure of the whole group.","refs":["CH_QGOH_PP_4:A4"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[]},{"id":"CL_QGOH_02:CGD-02","text":"The definition of essence through strict reciprocity locally preserves the distinction between dependence on God and the constitution of a thing, but does not become the dominant principle of this synthesis.","refs":["CH_QGOH_PSDN:A1"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[]},{"id":"CL_QGOH_02:CGD-03","text":"Traces in fluid parts provide a local mechanism of imagination; the present level retains the genetic function, not the exclusivity of this physical cause.","refs":["CH_QGOH_PS_2:A5","CH_QGOH_PS_2:MRT5"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[]},{"id":"CL_QGOH_02:CGD-04","text":"Divine necessity, attributive causality, and the determination of bodies are amplified upward into a single doctrine of immanent causality without principial contingency.","refs":["CH_QGOH_PSDN:SD4"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[]},{"id":"CL_QGOH_02:CGD-05","text":"The reduction of judgments to imagination and the doctrine of affection are joined in the higher proposition according to which perception of the external expresses the constitution of the perceiving body.","refs":["CH_QGOH_PP_4:D5","CH_QGOH_PS_2:A9"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[]},{"id":"CL_QGOH_02:CGD-06","text":"The transition from body as object of Mind to relational individual makes cognition, memory, and personal identity derivable from the same dynamic structure.","refs":["CH_QGOH_PS_2:SD1"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[]},{"id":"CL_QGOH_02:CGD-07","text":"The negation of imaginary order does not supersede the order of causal connections; lower homonymy is reordered at this level into two distinct functions.","refs":["CH_QGOH_PP_4:NX6","CH_QGOH_PSDN:A11"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[]},{"id":"CL_QGOH_02:CGD-08","text":"The promise of blessedness moves the transition to the doctrine of Mind, but its demonstration remains outside the closure of the present group.","refs":["CH_QGOH_PSDN:MRT6"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[]}],"original_name":"Lower-Layer vs Current-Level Distinction"},{"number":"5","name":"Global Apophatic Structure","items":[{"id":"CL_QGOH_02:GNX-01","text":"Nature does not act for future ends, nor does it arrange means toward an extrinsic model.","refs":["CH_QGOH_PP_4:A2"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"Negative","passages":[]},{"id":"CL_QGOH_02:GNX-02","text":"God's power is not a royal will that contingently chooses, preserves, or destroys things.","refs":["CH_QGOH_PSDN:NX1"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"Negative","passages":[]},{"id":"CL_QGOH_02:GNX-03","text":"Mind and Body are not really separate substances whose commerce would require transverse causality.","refs":["CH_QGOH_PSDN:NX3"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"Negative","passages":[]},{"id":"CL_QGOH_02:GNX-04","text":"An idea is not caused by the ideate as a mode of another attribute; each expression preserves its own causal series.","refs":["CH_QGOH_PSDN:NX2","CH_QGOH_PSDN:A9"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"Negative","passages":[]},{"id":"CL_QGOH_02:GNX-05","text":"The Mind does not apprehend Body, external things, and itself by immediate cognition and through a cause sufficient in itself.","refs":["CH_QGOH_PS_2:NX6"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"Negative","passages":[]},{"id":"CL_QGOH_02:GNX-06","text":"The identity of the individual does not consist in the permanence of parts, figure, magnitude, or position.","refs":["CH_QGOH_PS_2:NX2"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"Negative","passages":[]},{"id":"CL_QGOH_02:GNX-07","text":"Imagination is not in itself falsity; error is constituted by the absence of an idea that corrects imagined presence.","refs":["CH_QGOH_PS_2:NX4"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"Negative","passages":[]},{"id":"CL_QGOH_02:GNX-08","text":"Good, evil, order, and beauty are not intrinsic natures of things that display an independent ontological norm.","refs":["CH_QGOH_PP_4:NX7"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"Negative","passages":[]}],"original_name":"Global Apophatic Structure"},{"number":"6","name":"Deep Book Apophatics","items":[{"id":"CL_QGOH_02:DBN-01","text":"It must be false that the world was made for man, because this position converts a local affection into the rationale of universal production.","refs":["CH_QGOH_PP_4:SNX2"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"Negative","passages":[]},{"id":"CL_QGOH_02:DBN-02","text":"It must be false that a future effect can govern a preceding efficient cause, because such an order inverts causality.","refs":["CH_QGOH_PP_4:AS4"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"Negative","passages":[]},{"id":"CL_QGOH_02:DBN-03","text":"It must be false that Mind is a spectator of itself and of the world, independent of Body and immediately transparent to itself.","refs":["CH_QGOH_PS_2:DNX1"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"Negative","passages":[]},{"id":"CL_QGOH_02:DBN-04","text":"It must be false that perception of the external consists in the figurative likeness of a thing immediately received.","refs":["CH_QGOH_PS_2:DNX3"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"Negative","passages":[]},{"id":"CL_QGOH_02:DBN-05","text":"It must be false that the individual is defined by permanent matter, because its identity tolerates variation of parts.","refs":["CH_QGOH_PS_2:DNX2"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"Negative","passages":[]},{"id":"CL_QGOH_02:DBN-06","text":"It must be false that a name of value or order thereby signifies a real property.","refs":["CH_QGOH_PP_4:NX9"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"Negative","passages":[]},{"id":"CL_QGOH_02:DBN-07","text":"It must be false that the truth of an idea is constituted by external comparison alone with the object; otherwise the internal causality of Thought would collapse.","refs":["CH_QGOH_PSDN:NX8"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"Negative","passages":[]}],"original_name":"Deep Book Apophatics"},{"number":"7","name":"Super-Apophatics of the Whole","items":[{"id":"CL_QGOH_02:BSA-01","text":"Outside Nature no ontological tribunal remains from which God, things, perfections, and defects may be judged by a common norm.","refs":["CH_QGOH_PSDN:SNX1"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"Negative","passages":[]},{"id":"CL_QGOH_02:BSA-02","text":"A finite perspective without body, history of affections, and order of causes is ontologically unavailable.","refs":["CH_QGOH_PS_2:SNX1"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"Negative","passages":[]},{"id":"CL_QGOH_02:BSA-03","text":"Pure presence of an external thing is not given to the human Mind; the external appears only through an affection of the Body.","refs":["CH_QGOH_PS_2:NX3","CH_QGOH_PS_2:D7"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"Negative","passages":[]},{"id":"CL_QGOH_02:BSA-04","text":"A final norm that reduces all the variety of things to one model of perfection is inadmissible.","refs":["CH_QGOH_PP_4:SNX1"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"Negative","passages":[]},{"id":"CL_QGOH_02:BSA-05","text":"A contingent rupture in the series of causes, whether in divine will or in bodily motion, would dissolve the system's causality.","refs":["CH_QGOH_PSDN:SD4","CH_QGOH_PS_2:A1"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"Negative","passages":[]},{"id":"CL_QGOH_02:BSA-06","text":"Adequate cognition of the whole context remains an as-yet unexhibited possibility around which the diagnosis of confused cognition is ordered.","refs":["CH_QGOH_PS_2:D9","CH_QGOH_PSDN:MRT6"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"Negative","passages":[]}],"original_name":"Super-Apophatics of the Whole"},{"number":"8","name":"Cross-Unit Derivative Propositions","items":[{"id":"CL_QGOH_02:XD-01","text":"Teleology becomes not merely a false doctrine, but a naturally generated effect of the embodied Mind: affection provides a local perspective, while imagination transforms it into a cause of things.","refs":["CL_QGOH_02:GA-06","CL_QGOH_02:GA-07","CL_QGOH_02:GA-10","CH_QGOH_PP_4:AS3","CH_QGOH_PS_2:AS5"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"From","passages":[]},{"id":"CL_QGOH_02:XD-02","text":"Anthropocentrism is the illegitimate expansion of a bodily indication, whereby that which shows the constitution of the human body is taken for the universal nature of the object.","refs":["CL_QGOH_02:GA-06","CL_QGOH_02:GA-10","CH_QGOH_PP_4:SD2","CH_QGOH_PS_2:A9"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"From","passages":[]},{"id":"CL_QGOH_02:XD-03","text":"Ignorance of causality is not an inert void: traces and habits of affections produce an imaginative series that places a fictitious end in the place of the absent cause.","refs":["CL_QGOH_02:GA-02","CL_QGOH_02:GA-07","CL_QGOH_02:GA-08","CH_QGOH_PP_4:AS4","CH_QGOH_PS_2:A5","CH_QGOH_PS_2:NX5"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"From","passages":[]},{"id":"CL_QGOH_02:XD-04","text":"The correction of cognition does not occur through flight from Body, but through integration of the idea of affection into a broader order of causes and ideas.","refs":["CL_QGOH_02:GA-03","CL_QGOH_02:GA-04","CL_QGOH_02:GA-11","CH_QGOH_PSDN:A11","CH_QGOH_PS_2:A10"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"From","passages":[]},{"id":"CL_QGOH_02:XD-05","text":"A bodily affection and its idea are not events to be causally connected, but the same event expressed under Extension and Thought.","refs":["CL_QGOH_02:GA-03","CL_QGOH_02:GA-04","CL_QGOH_02:GA-07","CH_QGOH_PSDN:D4","CH_QGOH_PS_2:A7"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"From","passages":[]},{"id":"CL_QGOH_02:XD-06","text":"The negation of imaginary order does not destroy objective causality; on the contrary, it establishes the distinction between the order of mnemonic facility and the order of necessary connection.","refs":["CL_QGOH_02:GA-02","CL_QGOH_02:GA-11","CH_QGOH_PP_4:NX6","CH_QGOH_PSDN:A11"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"From","passages":[]},{"id":"CL_QGOH_02:XD-07","text":"The dynamic identity of Body makes personal memory possible without permanence of matter, because the ratio of the individual and the traces of affections can persist through substitutions of parts.","refs":["CL_QGOH_02:GA-05","CL_QGOH_02:GA-07","CH_QGOH_PS_2:SD1","CH_QGOH_PS_2:SD3"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"From","passages":[]},{"id":"CL_QGOH_02:XD-08","text":"The greater openness of Body to affections produces a bifurcation: it increases perceptive power, but without cognition of causes simultaneously multiplies the material of confusion.","refs":["CL_QGOH_02:GA-06","CL_QGOH_02:GA-08","CL_QGOH_02:GA-11","CH_QGOH_PS_2:SD2","CH_QGOH_PS_2:D9"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"From","passages":[]},{"id":"CL_QGOH_02:XD-09","text":"The authority of the transcendent interpreter arises from the same apparatus by which Mind imagines absences: causal obscurity is stabilized through concatenations of affections until cognition of causes dissolves them.","refs":["CL_QGOH_02:GA-08","CL_QGOH_02:GA-10","CH_QGOH_PS_2:FGS8"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"From","passages":[]}],"original_name":"Cross-Unit Derivative Propositions"},{"number":"9","name":"Book-Scale Super-Derivatives","items":[{"id":"CL_QGOH_02:BSD-01","text":"Reality becomes an immanent network of causal expressions with no exterior: the same nature is expressed under diverse attributes and determined through finite modes.","refs":["CH_QGOH_PSDN:ME1","CH_QGOH_PSDN:SNX1"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[]},{"id":"CL_QGOH_02:BSD-02","text":"The human subject becomes a distributed and historical individual, whose reflection arises from ideas of affections of the relationally persisting Body.","refs":["CH_QGOH_PS_2:SD4","CH_QGOH_PS_2:SD1"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[]},{"id":"CL_QGOH_02:BSD-03","text":"Error becomes an intelligible event of nature: imagination is positively produced, while falsity arises from the lack of a correcting idea and causal context.","refs":["CH_QGOH_PS_2:NX4","CH_QGOH_PP_4:D4"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[]},{"id":"CL_QGOH_02:BSD-04","text":"Temporality is doubled between the eternal necessity of production and the finite history of traces, whereby the past returns as if present and the future is causally projected backward as if an end.","refs":["CH_QGOH_PP_4:AS2","CH_QGOH_PS_2:SD3","CH_QGOH_PP_4:AS4"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[]},{"id":"CL_QGOH_02:BSD-05","text":"That which seems as if a future end to govern a cause is reclassified as a present effect of imagination within the series of efficient causes.","refs":["CH_QGOH_PP_4:AS3","CH_QGOH_PS_2:NX4"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[]},{"id":"CL_QGOH_02:BSD-06","text":"Adequacy becomes an amplification of causal context, not an abolition of body: a local idea is corrected when its place in a broader connection is understood.","refs":["CH_QGOH_PS_2:A10","CH_QGOH_PS_2:SD2"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[]},{"id":"CL_QGOH_02:BSD-07","text":"Interpretative power that attributes hidden causes to divine will depends on the social and memorial reproduction of bodily ignorance.","refs":["CH_QGOH_PS_2:NX5"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[]},{"id":"CL_QGOH_02:BSD-08","text":"The method of inquiry is transferred from the question of end and likeness to generation, determination, the disposition of the body, and the order of causes.","refs":["CH_QGOH_PP_4:ALT3"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[]}],"original_name":"Book-Scale Super-Derivatives"},{"number":"10","name":"Recursive Loops, Escalation Paths, and Conceptual Attractors","items":[{"id":"CL_QGOH_02:RLE-01","text":"Ignorance of the efficient cause generates the question of end; the imagined end covers ignorance under the name of will; this closure impedes inquiry into causes and reproduces ignorance.","refs":["CH_QGOH_PP_4:A7","CH_QGOH_PP_4:AS4"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[]},{"id":"CL_QGOH_02:RLE-02","text":"An affection of the external leaves a trace, the trace presents absence, memory connects presences by custom, and utility stabilizes this series into value judgments.","refs":["CH_QGOH_PS_2:A5","CH_QGOH_PS_2:NX5","CH_QGOH_PP_4:A8"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[]},{"id":"CL_QGOH_02:RLE-03","text":"The complexity of Body produces more affections and ideas; thus the power of perceiving and the danger of confusion grow together, until cognition of causes resolves the bifurcation.","refs":["CH_QGOH_PS_2:D1","CH_QGOH_PS_2:SD2"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[]},{"id":"CL_QGOH_02:RLE-04","text":"Cognition of causes dissolves the teleological circuit, because it reinserts imagined presence into an order of determinations and empties the medium of authority arising from obscurity.","refs":["CH_QGOH_PS_2:A10"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[]},{"id":"CL_QGOH_02:RLE-05","text":"Immanence is the principal attractor: God, attributes, bodies, ideas, and judgments are reduced to diverse expressions of one causally closed Nature.","refs":["CH_QGOH_PSDN:A8","CH_QGOH_PSDN:ME1"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[]},{"id":"CL_QGOH_02:RLE-06","text":"The relational Body is an intermediate attractor, because it orders identity, perceptive capacity, memory, self-cognition, and the variation of minds according to the same ratio.","refs":["CH_QGOH_PS_2:A3","CH_QGOH_PSDN:ME5"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[]},{"id":"CL_QGOH_02:RLE-07","text":"The circuit of the whole group is metaphysics of necessity → constitution of embodied Mind → genesis of imagination → diagnosis of teleological metaphysics → restoration of necessity.","refs":["CH_QGOH_PSDN:AS3","CH_QGOH_PS_2:AS6","CH_QGOH_PP_4:SD3"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[]},{"id":"CL_QGOH_02:RLE-08","text":"End undergoes a terminal transformation: it ceases to be a cause of Nature and becomes an indication of an imaginative mechanism that inverts cause and effect.","refs":["CH_QGOH_PP_4:AS3","CH_QGOH_PP_4:AS4"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[]}],"original_name":"Recursive Loops, Escalation Paths, and Conceptual Attractors"},{"number":"11","name":"Formal Generative Schema of the Whole Book","items":[{"id":"CL_QGOH_02:FGS-B01","text":"Active divine necessity → production without end → exclusion of the needy artificer and principial contingency.","refs":["CH_QGOH_PP_4:AS2","CH_QGOH_PSDN:A5"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[]},{"id":"CL_QGOH_02:FGS-B02","text":"Distinct attributes + causality within the attribute + the same order → one thing expressed in two ways under Thought and Extension.","refs":["CH_QGOH_PSDN:A9","CH_QGOH_PSDN:A11"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[]},{"id":"CL_QGOH_02:FGS-B03","text":"Ratio of motion and rest → composite individual → identity through change of parts → human Body as object of Mind.","refs":["CH_QGOH_PS_2:A4","CH_QGOH_PSDN:AS2"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[]},{"id":"CL_QGOH_02:FGS-B04","text":"Encounter of bodies → affection implicating the nature of both → idea of affection → external presented through the disposition of one's own Body.","refs":["CH_QGOH_PS_2:A2","CH_QGOH_PS_2:AS5"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[]},{"id":"CL_QGOH_02:FGS-B05","text":"Persisting affection → trace → presence of an absent thing → customary concatenation → imagination and memory.","refs":["CH_QGOH_PS_2:A5","CH_QGOH_PS_2:SD3"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[]},{"id":"CL_QGOH_02:FGS-B06","text":"Mediated cognition + incomplete context of causes + utility → confusion → imagined ends, values, and orders.","refs":["CH_QGOH_PS_2:FGS8","CH_QGOH_PP_4:A8"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[]},{"id":"CL_QGOH_02:FGS-B07","text":"Universalized judgment of the local body → Nature measured for human convenience → fictitious teleology and imperfection.","refs":["CH_QGOH_PP_4:SD2","CH_QGOH_PP_4:SNX1"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[]},{"id":"CL_QGOH_02:FGS-B08","text":"Reintegration into the order of causal connections → end reclassified as effect → value restored to relation → perfection measured from the power of the thing.","refs":["CH_QGOH_PP_4:AS3","CH_QGOH_PP_4:FGS5"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[]}],"original_name":"Formal Generative Schema of the Whole Book"},{"number":"12","name":"Whole-Book Argument Flow Map","items":[{"id":"CL_QGOH_02:AFM-B01","text":"Divine necessity → attributive causality → unity of Mind and Body → dynamic individual → affection → imagination and memory → local and confused cognition.","refs":["CH_QGOH_PSDN:A5","CH_QGOH_PS_2:A7","CH_QGOH_PS_2:AS6"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[]},{"id":"CL_QGOH_02:AFM-B02","text":"Ignorance of causes → question of end → imagined presence → utility of body → evaluative judgment → anthropocentric projection → falsely ordered Nature.","refs":["CH_QGOH_PP_4:A7","CH_QGOH_PP_4:A8","CH_QGOH_PP_4:ME4"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[]},{"id":"CL_QGOH_02:AFM-B03","text":"Diagnosis of projection → distinction of imaginary order from causal order → expansion of the context of ideas → perfection from nature and power → restoration of immanence.","refs":["CH_QGOH_PSDN:A11","CH_QGOH_PP_4:A11","CH_QGOH_PSDN:ME1"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[]}],"original_name":"Whole-Book Argument Flow Map"},{"number":"13","name":"Macro Map-Effects","items":[{"id":"CL_QGOH_02:MME-01","text":"God is reclassified from a voluntary artificer into an immanent cause whose power coincides with active essence.","refs":["CH_QGOH_PSDN:A5","CH_QGOH_PSDN:ME1"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[]},{"id":"CL_QGOH_02:MME-02","text":"Nature is reclassified from a work arranged toward an end into necessary production without exterior and without extrinsic norm.","refs":["CH_QGOH_PP_4:AS2","CH_QGOH_PP_4:SNX1"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[]},{"id":"CL_QGOH_02:MME-03","text":"Man is reclassified from a separate substance or soul into a finite mode whose Mind is the idea of Body.","refs":["CH_QGOH_PSDN:NX4","CH_QGOH_PSDN:D8"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[]},{"id":"CL_QGOH_02:MME-04","text":"Body is reclassified from a material aggregate into a relation of motion and rest that persists through changes of components.","refs":["CH_QGOH_PS_2:FGS2"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[]},{"id":"CL_QGOH_02:MME-05","text":"Perception is reclassified from immediate reception of the object into an idea of affection that presents the external through the constitution of the perceiver.","refs":["CH_QGOH_PS_2:NX3","CH_QGOH_PS_2:FGS5"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[]},{"id":"CL_QGOH_02:MME-06","text":"Imagination and memory are reclassified from a storehouse of false images into neutral effects of bodily traces and habits.","refs":["CH_QGOH_PS_2:ME3","CH_QGOH_PS_2:ME4"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[]},{"id":"CL_QGOH_02:MME-07","text":"Order and value are divided between real causal structure and modes of imagining arising from facility, utility, and affection.","refs":["CH_QGOH_PP_4:NX6","CH_QGOH_PP_4:A9","CH_QGOH_PSDN:A11"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[]},{"id":"CL_QGOH_02:MME-08","text":"The truth of perception is no longer assessed from apparent likeness, but from the properties of the idea and its integration into a context of causes.","refs":["CH_QGOH_PSDN:NX8"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[]}],"original_name":"Macro Map-Effects"},{"number":"14","name":"Altitude Integration","items":[{"id":"CL_QGOH_02:ALT-B01","text":"The surface level treats the ends of Nature, the union of Mind and Body, perception of external things, imagination, memory, and judgments of good or evil.","refs":["CH_QGOH_PP_4:AS5","CH_QGOH_PS_2:AS6"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[]},{"id":"CL_QGOH_02:ALT-B02","text":"The structural level distributes divine necessity, the causal closure of attributes, the ratio of motion of the individual, affections, and traces as one apparatus.","refs":["CH_QGOH_PSDN:AS4","CH_QGOH_PS_2:A4","CH_QGOH_PS_2:A5"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[]},{"id":"CL_QGOH_02:ALT-B03","text":"The metastrategic level transfers the question from end and apparent quality to efficient causes, the bodily conditions of judgment, and the order of generation.","refs":["CH_QGOH_PP_4:ALT3"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[]},{"id":"CL_QGOH_02:ALT-B04","text":"The ontological and temporal level constitutes reality as immanent production, the individual as a persisting relation, and memory as a bodily reactivated past.","refs":["CH_QGOH_PS_2:SD1","CH_QGOH_PS_2:SD3"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[]},{"id":"CL_QGOH_02:ALT-B05","text":"The methodological and performative level first destroys extrinsic ends and values, then reconstructs the bodily machine that produces them, thereby causally completing the critique's genealogy.","refs":["CH_QGOH_PP_4:AS3","CH_QGOH_PS_2:ALT2"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[]}],"original_name":"Altitude Integration"},{"number":"15","name":"Internal Tensions, Mutation Points, and Fault Lines","items":[{"id":"CL_QGOH_02:FL-01","text":"The negation of order as an attribute of Nature and the affirmation of the identity of the order of ideas and things cohere only if imaginary order is strictly distinguished from causal order.","refs":["CH_QGOH_PP_4:NX6","CH_QGOH_PSDN:A11"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"Tension","passages":[]},{"id":"CL_QGOH_02:FL-02","text":"The identity of Mind and Body as one individual must be reconciled with the closure of causes within attributes without causal commerce or mere duplication.","refs":["CH_QGOH_PSDN:MRT3","CH_QGOH_PSDN:DNX2"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"Tension","passages":[]},{"id":"CL_QGOH_02:FL-03","text":"The greater capacity of Body to perceive more increases cognition quantitatively, but does not guarantee adequacy; the transition from plurality to understanding remains incomplete.","refs":["CH_QGOH_PS_2:SD2","CH_QGOH_PS_2:MRT6"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"Tension","passages":[]},{"id":"CL_QGOH_02:FL-04","text":"The idea of affection involves the nature of the external body, but chiefly indicates the constitution of one's own Body; the measure of cognition of the external remains unstable between presence and inadequacy.","refs":["CH_QGOH_PS_2:AS5","CH_QGOH_PS_2:A9"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"Tension","passages":[]},{"id":"CL_QGOH_02:FL-05","text":"All ideas are produced from the necessity of Nature, but the distinction between adequacy and error must retain normative force without an extrinsic norm.","refs":["CH_QGOH_PP_4:A1","CH_QGOH_PS_2:NX4","CH_QGOH_PSDN:SNX3"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"Tension","passages":[]},{"id":"CL_QGOH_02:FL-06","text":"Blessedness directs the movement of the argument, although the apparatus extracted here only lays down its ontological and epistemic conditions.","refs":["CH_QGOH_PSDN:AS1","CH_QGOH_PSDN:MRT6"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"Tension","passages":[]},{"id":"CL_QGOH_02:MP-01","text":"Final cause is transformed into an effect of imagination and an indication of ignorance of efficient causes.","refs":["CH_QGOH_PP_4:AS3","CH_QGOH_PP_4:A7"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"Tension","passages":[]},{"id":"CL_QGOH_02:MP-02","text":"Body is transformed from the actually existing object of Mind into a relational individual whose composition determines degrees of cognition.","refs":["CH_QGOH_PSDN:D8","CH_QGOH_PS_2:SD1"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"Tension","passages":[]},{"id":"CL_QGOH_02:MP-03","text":"Idea is transformed from a mode formally caused within Thought into a local record of the affections and history of the body.","refs":["CH_QGOH_PSDN:ME3","CH_QGOH_PS_2:SD3"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"Composite","passages":[]},{"id":"CL_QGOH_02:MP-04","text":"Imagination is transformed from cognition simply false into a neutral mechanism to which falsity accrues only through the privation of correction.","refs":["CH_QGOH_PS_2:NX4"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"Tension","passages":[]},{"id":"CL_QGOH_02:MP-05","text":"Perfection is transformed from conformity to convenience or a model into the power of the thing and its capacity to act from its own constitution.","refs":["CH_QGOH_PP_4:A11","CH_QGOH_PSDN:D11"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"Composite","passages":[]}],"original_name":"Internal Tensions, Mutation Points, and Fault Lines"},{"number":"16","name":"Hidden System Image","items":[{"id":"CL_QGOH_02:HSI-01","text":"Reality is implicitly an infinite machine of self-expression, whose modes are produced through necessary series of causes and are not ordered by an external artificer.","refs":["CH_QGOH_PP_4:A12","CH_QGOH_PSDN:ME1"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[]},{"id":"CL_QGOH_02:HSI-02","text":"The individual is a relational vortex of motion and rest that exchanges matter, varies figure, and yet preserves its constitutive ratio.","refs":["CH_QGOH_PS_2:A4","CH_QGOH_PS_2:SD1"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[]},{"id":"CL_QGOH_02:HSI-03","text":"Mind is an active tablet of encounters of Body, in which external things simultaneously appear and are refracted according to the disposition of the recipient.","refs":["CH_QGOH_PS_2:AS5","CH_QGOH_PS_2:ME5"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[]},{"id":"CL_QGOH_02:HSI-04","text":"The time of the system joins eternal necessity with finite duration: traces recall the past into presence, while the imagined end turns the future backward into a cause.","refs":["CH_QGOH_PP_4:AS2","CH_QGOH_PS_2:SD3","CH_QGOH_PP_4:AS4"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[]},{"id":"CL_QGOH_02:HSI-05","text":"Captivity occurs through utility, memory, and ignorance; possible escape is not an exit from Nature or Body, but navigation that restores a broader context of causes.","refs":["CH_QGOH_PP_4:A8","CH_QGOH_PS_2:A10"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[]}],"original_name":"Hidden System Image"},{"number":"17","name":"Minimal Whole-Book Reconstruction Test","items":[{"id":"CL_QGOH_02:MRT-B01","text":"The axioms of necessity, the identity of Mind and Body, relational individuation, affection, and imagination suffice to reconstruct the principal machine of this group.","refs":["CH_QGOH_PP_4:MRT6","CH_QGOH_PS_2:MRT1"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[]},{"id":"CL_QGOH_02:MRT-B02","text":"The firmest structure is the nexus between immanent causality, composite Body, and the idea of affection, from which local perception and projective teleology are simultaneously derived.","refs":["CH_QGOH_PS_2:MRT2","CH_QGOH_PP_4:SD2"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[]},{"id":"CL_QGOH_02:MRT-B03","text":"It is not yet sufficiently determined how cognition of affections passes into adequate cognition and blessedness.","refs":["CH_QGOH_PSDN:MRT6","CH_QGOH_PS_2:MRT7"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[]},{"id":"CL_QGOH_02:MRT-B04","text":"The explanation of imagination through traces provides a sufficient cause, not a demonstrated exclusive cause.","refs":["CH_QGOH_PS_2:MRT5"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[]},{"id":"CL_QGOH_02:MRT-B05","text":"The extension of the doctrine of the individual to the whole of Nature is indicated, but the demonstration of this amplification is suspended.","refs":["CH_QGOH_PS_2:MRT4"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[]},{"id":"CL_QGOH_02:MRT-B06","text":"The most fragile juncture is the distinction between imaginary order and causal order, together with the transition from affective relations to the objective structure of singular things.","refs":["CH_QGOH_PP_4:MRT5","CH_QGOH_PSDN:A11"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[]},{"id":"CL_QGOH_02:MRT-B07","text":"The reconstruction preserves the true movement only if the synthesis is read not as a linear series, but as a circuit from necessity to embodied Mind and from it to the critique of teleology.","refs":["CH_QGOH_PP_4:MRT6","CH_QGOH_PS_2:MRT3"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[]}],"original_name":"Minimal Whole-Book Reconstruction Test"}],"revision":"CL_QGOH_02@R001","source_variant_id":"canonical:CL_QGOH_02:v1","source_language_code":"la","original_title":"Chapters 04–06"}],"identity":{"layer":"CL","kicker":"Cluster · Ethica","title":"Chapters 04–06","context":"Part I - Concerning God · passage 4 · Part II - On the Nature and Origin of the Mind · passage 1 · Part II - On the Nature and Origin of the Mind · passage 2","original_title":"Chapters 04–06","original_context":"Pars prima - De Deo · passage 4 · Pars secunda - De natura et origine mentis · passage 1 · Pars secunda - De natura et origine mentis · passage 2"},"original_title":"Chapters 04–06","title_language_code":"en"},{"id":"CL_QGOH_03","layer":"CL","title":"Chapters 07–09","order":3,"parents":["CH_QGOH_PS_3","CH_QGOH_PS_4","CH_QGOH_PTDO"],"revision":"CL_QGOH_03@R001","sha256":"4f41461cf7de53b07aaf8084b25bbc3860185161713ac0e056faa3d056c9a320","sections":[{"number":"1","name":"Book-Scale Argumental Spine","items":[{"id":"CL_QGOH_03:AS-01","text":"Systema quaerit quibus condicionibus Mens finita, quae extra Naturam poni non potest, cognitionem, actionem et affectus suos intra eundem ordinem necessarium intelligere possit.","refs":["CH_QGOH_PS_3:AS1","CH_QGOH_PTDO:AS1"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[]},{"id":"CL_QGOH_03:AS-02","text":"Thesis globalis adæquationem facit principium commune veritatis et actionis: ideæ adæquatæ veram cognitionem atque causalitatem activam, inadæquatæ autem mutilationem et passionem constituunt.","refs":["CH_QGOH_PS_3:AS2","CH_QGOH_PTDO:A7"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[]},{"id":"CL_QGOH_03:AS-03","text":"Argumentatio diagnostica, reconstructiva et geometrica errorem, voluntatem liberam atque vitiorum moralizationem resolvit, deinde eorum loco structuram causalitatis graduatæ constituit.","refs":["CH_QGOH_PS_3:AS3","CH_QGOH_PTDO:AS3"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[]},{"id":"CL_QGOH_03:AS-04","text":"Motus procedit ab affectione corporis ad communitatem rationalem, cognitionem Dei, dissolutionem facultatis liberæ, conatum actualem atque formationem affectuum.","refs":["CH_QGOH_PS_3:SD4","CH_QGOH_PTDO:AS6"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[]},{"id":"CL_QGOH_03:AS-05","text":"Motus non vere linearis sed recursivus est, quia eadem realitas ideationalis successive ut perceptio, affirmatio, causalitas et variatio potentiæ redit.","refs":["CH_QGOH_PS_3:SD1","CH_QGOH_PTDO:SD2"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[]},{"id":"CL_QGOH_03:AS-06","text":"Mutatio principalis cognitionis vocabula in dynamicam agentiæ transfert: adæquatio et inadæquatio desinunt solum statum epistemicum designare et gradus agendi vel patiendi fiunt.","refs":["CH_QGOH_PTDO:A7","CH_QGOH_PTDO:SD1"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[]},{"id":"CL_QGOH_03:AS-07","text":"Clausura positiva libertatem non restituit ut exemptionem a necessitate, sed ut actionem ex intellecta necessitate et causalitate magis adæquata ortam.","refs":["CH_QGOH_PS_4:SD2","CH_QGOH_PTDO:SD5"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[]},{"id":"CL_QGOH_03:AS-08","text":"Clausura tamen provisionaliter aperta manet, quia principium conversionis cognitionis in potestatem datur, sed criterium practicum augmenti adæquationis et plena affectuum taxonomia nondum constituuntur.","refs":["CH_QGOH_PTDO:MRT3","CH_QGOH_PTDO:MRT7"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[]}],"original_name":"Book-Scale Argumental Spine"},{"number":"2","name":"Global Load-Bearing Axioms","items":[{"id":"CL_QGOH_03:GA-01","text":"[Persistent] Natura uno ordine necessario et eisdem legibus agit; homo, cognitio et affectus nullam regionem exceptionalem intra eam constituunt.","refs":["CH_QGOH_PTDO:A1","CH_QGOH_PTDO:NX1"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[]},{"id":"CL_QGOH_03:GA-02","text":"[Persistent] Mens finita per affectiones Corporis cognoscit, dum Mens et Corpus eandem rem sub diversis attributis exprimunt.","refs":["CH_QGOH_PS_3:A1","CH_QGOH_PTDO:A9"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[]},{"id":"CL_QGOH_03:GA-03","text":"[Mutating] Adæquatio ex principio veritatis in principium causalitatis activæ mutatur: quod ex ideis adæquatis sequitur simul cognitio et actio est.","refs":["CH_QGOH_PS_3:A11","CH_QGOH_PTDO:A7"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[]},{"id":"CL_QGOH_03:GA-04","text":"[Persistent] Inadæquatio ordinem positivum falsitatis non constituit, sed privationem cognitionis, partialitatem causæ et passionem exprimit.","refs":["CH_QGOH_PS_3:A7","CH_QGOH_PTDO:D4"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[]},{"id":"CL_QGOH_03:GA-05","text":"[Persistent] Quod commune et invariabile est adæquate concipitur, atque idea rei singularis per attributum divinum essentiam Dei implicat.","refs":["CH_QGOH_PS_3:A9","CH_QGOH_PS_4:A3"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[]},{"id":"CL_QGOH_03:GA-06","text":"[Terminal] Facultates universales voluntatis et intellectus causalitatem propriam amittunt, quia omnis idea affirmationem vel negationem intrinsecam involvit.","refs":["CH_QGOH_PS_4:A7","CH_QGOH_PS_4:A8"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[]},{"id":"CL_QGOH_03:GA-07","text":"[Emergent] Conatus est essentia actualis rei, cui nullus terminus temporalis internus assignatur.","refs":["CH_QGOH_PTDO:A13","CH_QGOH_PTDO:A14"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[]},{"id":"CL_QGOH_03:GA-08","text":"[Mutating] Voluntas ex facultate libera in affirmationem singularem mutatur, deinde ut conatus Mentis, Appetitus et Cupiditas dynamice redistribuitur.","refs":["CH_QGOH_PS_4:AS4","CH_QGOH_PTDO:A16"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[]},{"id":"CL_QGOH_03:GA-09","text":"[Emergent] Affectus est variatio potentiæ Corporis una cum idea eiusdem variationis, unde potentia agendi et cogitandi simul ordinantur.","refs":["CH_QGOH_PTDO:A4","CH_QGOH_PTDO:A17"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[]},{"id":"CL_QGOH_03:GA-10","text":"[Attractor] Libertas est maior adæquatio causalis intra necessitatem, non indifferentia eligendi aut imperium voluntatis.","refs":["CH_QGOH_PTDO:SD1","CH_QGOH_PS_4:SD2"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[]},{"id":"CL_QGOH_03:GA-11","text":"[Persistent] Conscientia actionis vel appetitus scientiam causarum non includit; reflexivitas sine causalitate cognita libertatem originariam non probat.","refs":["CH_QGOH_PS_3:A17","CH_QGOH_PTDO:DNX3"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[]},{"id":"CL_QGOH_03:GA-12","text":"[Mutating] Tempus dividitur inter contingentiam imaginativam, durationem conatus indefinitam et necessitatem quam ratio sub specie æternitatis percipit.","refs":["CH_QGOH_PS_3:SD5","CH_QGOH_PTDO:A14"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[]}],"original_name":"Global Load-Bearing Axioms"},{"number":"3","name":"Recurrence, Mutation, and Displacement Matrix","items":[{"id":"CL_QGOH_03:RMD-01","text":"Idea affectionis Corporis redit ut affectus: mediatio epistemica idem fundamentum retinet, sed nunc variationem potentiæ et statum agentiæ exprimit.","refs":["CH_QGOH_PS_3:A1","CH_QGOH_PTDO:A4"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[]},{"id":"CL_QGOH_03:RMD-02","text":"Inadæquatio ab origine falsitatis ad principium passionis migrat; privatio cognitionis fit etiam defectus causalitatis plenæ.","refs":["CH_QGOH_PS_3:A7","CH_QGOH_PTDO:NX5"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[]},{"id":"CL_QGOH_03:RMD-03","text":"Adæquatio functionem mutat ab autocertitudine ideæ ad productionem actionum quarum Mens causa sufficiens est.","refs":["CH_QGOH_PS_3:A14","CH_QGOH_PTDO:D1"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[]},{"id":"CL_QGOH_03:RMD-04","text":"Affirmatio intrinseca ideæ amplificatur in affirmationem existentiæ Corporis et tandem in conatum perseverandi.","refs":["CH_QGOH_PS_4:A8","CH_QGOH_PTDO:A18"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[]},{"id":"CL_QGOH_03:RMD-05","text":"Negatio voluntatis liberæ redit cum mutatione functionis: primo errorem de iudicio dissolvit, deinde causalitatem appetitus et affectuum aperit.","refs":["CH_QGOH_PS_3:NX9","CH_QGOH_PTDO:NX4"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[]},{"id":"CL_QGOH_03:RMD-06","text":"Corpus ex medio cognitionis in substratum dynamicum potentiæ mutatur; quod perceptionem mediat idem variationes agendi et cogitandi sustinet.","refs":["CH_QGOH_PS_3:AS2","CH_QGOH_PTDO:A17"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[]},{"id":"CL_QGOH_03:RMD-07","text":"Imaginatio non simpliciter eliminatur: ex fonte contingentiae et confusionis in operationem conatus, quæ imagines potentiæ faventes seligit, transfertur.","refs":["CH_QGOH_PS_3:D12","CH_QGOH_PTDO:D7"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[]},{"id":"CL_QGOH_03:RMD-08","text":"Cognitio Dei, initio terminus ascensus epistemici, in fundamentum tacitum naturalizationis affectuum absorbetur.","refs":["CH_QGOH_PS_3:AS7","CH_QGOH_PTDO:A1"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[]},{"id":"CL_QGOH_03:RMD-09","text":"Facultates universales evanescunt postquam actus singulares explicaverunt, sed doctrina de Deo et ideis adæquatis persistit ut infrastructura sectionis affectivæ.","refs":["CH_QGOH_PS_4:A7","CH_QGOH_PTDO:MRT2"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[]}],"original_name":"Recurrence, Mutation, and Displacement Matrix"},{"number":"4","name":"Lower-Layer vs Current-Level Distinction","items":[{"id":"CL_QGOH_03:CGD-01","text":"Doctrinæ locales de terminis transcendentalibus et tribus cognitionis generibus non integræ amplificantur; earum functio præsens est discrimen inter imaginationem confusam et adæquationem constituere.","refs":["CH_QGOH_PS_3:A12","CH_QGOH_PS_3:A13"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[]},{"id":"CL_QGOH_03:CGD-02","text":"Nexus ideæ singularis cum Deo supra gradum localem ascendit, quia immanentiam qua cognitionem et affectus legibus communibus subiici possunt constituit.","refs":["CH_QGOH_PS_4:SD1","CH_QGOH_PTDO:A1"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[]},{"id":"CL_QGOH_03:CGD-03","text":"Identitas voluntatis et intellectus fit commissura inter epistemologiam et affectuum doctrinam, cum affirmatio ideæ in Appetitum et Cupiditatem extenditur.","refs":["CH_QGOH_PS_4:D4","CH_QGOH_PTDO:A16"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[]},{"id":"CL_QGOH_03:CGD-04","text":"Taxonomia Cupiditatis, Lætitiæ et Tristitiæ manet mechanismus localis; structura huius gradus est conditio causalitatis qua talis taxonomia generatur.","refs":["CH_QGOH_PTDO:D6"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[]},{"id":"CL_QGOH_03:CGD-05","text":"Distinctio causæ adæquatæ et partialis unitates coniungit, quia distinctionem veri et falsi in discrimen agendi et patiendi convertit.","refs":["CH_QGOH_PTDO:A2","CH_QGOH_PTDO:A3"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[]},{"id":"CL_QGOH_03:CGD-06","text":"Exitus ethico-politicus directionem globalem indicat, sed efficacia practica qua cognitio necessitatis affectus et civium actiones mutet nondum demonstratur.","refs":["CH_QGOH_PS_4:SD3","CH_QGOH_PTDO:MRT3"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[]},{"id":"CL_QGOH_03:CGD-07","text":"Promissa taxonomia reliquorum affectuum ultra hunc gradum manet; hic restituitur tantum machina quæ affectus primarios producere potest.","refs":["CH_QGOH_PTDO:MRT7"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[]}],"original_name":"Lower-Layer vs Current-Level Distinction"},{"number":"5","name":"Global Apophatic Structure","items":[{"id":"CL_QGOH_03:GNX-01","text":"Mens cognitionem sui, Corporis et rerum externarum immediate atque sine affectionibus datam non habet.","refs":["CH_QGOH_PS_3:NX1"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"Negative","passages":[]},{"id":"CL_QGOH_03:GNX-02","text":"Falsitas non est forma positiva cogitationis, sed defectus relationis cognitivæ sufficientis.","refs":["CH_QGOH_PS_3:NX4"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"Negative","passages":[]},{"id":"CL_QGOH_03:GNX-03","text":"Voluntas nec causa libera nec potentia infinita supra ideas singulares est.","refs":["CH_QGOH_PS_4:NX1","CH_QGOH_PS_4:NX2"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"Negative","passages":[]},{"id":"CL_QGOH_03:GNX-04","text":"Mens et Corpus causalitatem transversam inter attributa non exercent.","refs":["CH_QGOH_PTDO:A8"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"Negative","passages":[]},{"id":"CL_QGOH_03:GNX-05","text":"Homo non est imperium intra imperium, et affectus non sunt vitia contra Naturæ ordinem insurgentia.","refs":["CH_QGOH_PTDO:NX1","CH_QGOH_PTDO:NX2"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"Negative","passages":[]},{"id":"CL_QGOH_03:GNX-06","text":"Nulla res principium internum propriæ destructionis continet.","refs":["CH_QGOH_PTDO:A10"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"Negative","passages":[]},{"id":"CL_QGOH_03:GNX-07","text":"Bonum appetitum non antecedit ut causa externa; iudicium boni ex appetitu iam operante sequitur.","refs":["CH_QGOH_PTDO:NX7"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"Negative","passages":[]}],"original_name":"Global Apophatic Structure"},{"number":"6","name":"Deep Book Apophatics","items":[{"id":"CL_QGOH_03:DBN-01","text":"Falsum esse debet exemplar subiecti sui iuris quod ante ideas, Corpus et causas suas constitutum sit.","refs":["CH_QGOH_PS_4:DNX1","CH_QGOH_PTDO:DNX1"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"Negative","passages":[]},{"id":"CL_QGOH_03:DBN-02","text":"Falsum esse debet exemplar duorum ordinum, quorum alter veritatis et libertatis, alter erroris et necessitatis sit.","refs":["CH_QGOH_PS_3:SNX2","CH_QGOH_PTDO:A9"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"Negative","passages":[]},{"id":"CL_QGOH_03:DBN-03","text":"Falsum esse debet exemplar rationis vel affectus a corporeis relationibus separabilis.","refs":["CH_QGOH_PS_3:DNX4","CH_QGOH_PTDO:SNX4"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"Negative","passages":[]},{"id":"CL_QGOH_03:DBN-04","text":"Falsum esse debet exemplar normæ externæ quæ veritatem ideæ aut bonitatem appetitus ab extra constituat.","refs":["CH_QGOH_PS_3:SNX4","CH_QGOH_PTDO:SD3"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"Negative","passages":[]},{"id":"CL_QGOH_03:DBN-05","text":"Falsum esse debet exemplar cognitionis causaliter inertis, quia differentia inter ideas adæquatas et inadæquatas differentiam inter actionem et passionem producit.","refs":["CH_QGOH_PTDO:A7","CH_QGOH_PTDO:SD5"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"Negative","passages":[]},{"id":"CL_QGOH_03:DBN-06","text":"Falsum esse debet exemplar temporis imaginati ut ultimi horizontis rerum, cum necessitas æterna et duratio conatus indefinita ad diversa tempora pertineant.","refs":["CH_QGOH_PS_3:SNX3","CH_QGOH_PTDO:A14"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"Negative","passages":[]}],"original_name":"Deep Book Apophatics"},{"number":"7","name":"Super-Apophatics of the Whole","items":[{"id":"CL_QGOH_03:BSA-01","text":"Nulla regio exterior Naturæ admittitur ex qua Deus, veritas, libertas aut affectus absolute iudicentur.","refs":["CH_QGOH_PTDO:SNX1","CH_QGOH_PS_4:SNX2"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"Negative","passages":[]},{"id":"CL_QGOH_03:BSA-02","text":"Nulla origo absoluta cogitationis vel actionis, a serie causarum immunis, ontologice disponibilis est.","refs":["CH_QGOH_PS_4:SNX1","CH_QGOH_PTDO:SNX2"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"Negative","passages":[]},{"id":"CL_QGOH_03:BSA-03","text":"Libertas ut pura indifferentia impossibilis est; sola libertas admissibilis per necessitatem intellectam et causalitatem auctam transit.","refs":["CH_QGOH_PS_4:SNX4","CH_QGOH_PTDO:SD1"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"Negative","passages":[]},{"id":"CL_QGOH_03:BSA-04","text":"Principium intrinsecum non-essendi excluditur, unde negatio, destructio et imminutio per relationes externas vel partialitatem explicandæ sunt.","refs":["CH_QGOH_PTDO:SNX3","CH_QGOH_PTDO:A10"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"Negative","passages":[]}],"original_name":"Super-Apophatics of the Whole"},{"number":"8","name":"Cross-Unit Derivative Propositions","items":[{"id":"CL_QGOH_03:XD-01","text":"Eadem differentia quæ ideam veram ab inadæquata separat actionem a passione distinguit; adæquatio epistemica fit potentia causalis.","refs":["CL_QGOH_03:GA-03","CL_QGOH_03:GA-04","CH_QGOH_PS_3:A11","CH_QGOH_PTDO:A7"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"From","passages":[]},{"id":"CL_QGOH_03:XD-02","text":"Mediatio corporalis non tantum cognitionem finitam limitat, sed etiam campum realem potentiæ constituit in quo cognitio et affectus variantur.","refs":["CL_QGOH_03:GA-02","CL_QGOH_03:GA-09","CH_QGOH_PS_3:AS2","CH_QGOH_PTDO:SD2"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"From","passages":[]},{"id":"CL_QGOH_03:XD-03","text":"Cum omnis idea affirmationem involvat et essentia actualis conatus sit, volitio fit expressio conscia perseverantiæ potius quam actus facultatis liberæ.","refs":["CL_QGOH_03:GA-06","CL_QGOH_03:GA-07","CL_QGOH_03:GA-08","CH_QGOH_PS_4:A8","CH_QGOH_PTDO:A13","CH_QGOH_PTDO:A16"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"From","passages":[]},{"id":"CL_QGOH_03:XD-04","text":"Quia idea singularis essentiam Dei implicat et Natura ubique iisdem legibus agit, affectus ex ipsa immanentia divina demonstrabiles fiunt.","refs":["CL_QGOH_03:GA-01","CL_QGOH_03:GA-05","CH_QGOH_PS_4:A3","CH_QGOH_PTDO:A1"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"From","passages":[]},{"id":"CL_QGOH_03:XD-05","text":"Credulitas libertatis ex conscientia sine scientia causarum oritur; eius substitutum non est fatalis inertia, sed maior causalitas adæquata.","refs":["CL_QGOH_03:GA-10","CL_QGOH_03:GA-11","CH_QGOH_PS_3:A17","CH_QGOH_PTDO:SD1"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"From","passages":[]},{"id":"CL_QGOH_03:XD-06","text":"Imaginatio functionem duplicem et instabilem habet: contingentiam atque errorem generat, sed simul imagines potentiæ faventes conative ordinat.","refs":["CL_QGOH_03:GA-02","CL_QGOH_03:GA-09","CH_QGOH_PS_3:D12","CH_QGOH_PTDO:D7"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"From","passages":[]},{"id":"CL_QGOH_03:XD-07","text":"Persistentia rei finitæ neque contingentia pura neque æternitas est: conatus durationem indefinitam habet, dum ratio eius necessitatem sub specie æternitatis intelligit.","refs":["CL_QGOH_03:GA-07","CL_QGOH_03:GA-12","CH_QGOH_PS_3:SD5","CH_QGOH_PTDO:A14","CH_QGOH_PS_3:A15"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"From","passages":[]},{"id":"CL_QGOH_03:XD-08","text":"Cum iudicium boni Cupiditatem sequatur sed libertas ex necessitate intellecta oriatur, normativitas fit ordinatio immanens conatus, non imperium externum appetitui oppositum.","refs":["CL_QGOH_03:GA-08","CL_QGOH_03:GA-10","CH_QGOH_PTDO:NX7","CH_QGOH_PS_4:SD2"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"From","passages":[]}],"original_name":"Cross-Unit Derivative Propositions"},{"number":"9","name":"Book-Scale Super-Derivatives","items":[{"id":"CL_QGOH_03:BSD-01","text":"Veritas, agentia et potentia non sunt regiones separatæ, sed tres mensuræ unius gradus expressionis causalis.","refs":["CH_QGOH_PS_3:SD1","CH_QGOH_PTDO:SD1"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[]},{"id":"CL_QGOH_03:BSD-02","text":"Subiectum humanum non est substantia clausum principiumque actuum, sed ratio mobilis inter causas externas, corporis affectiones et ideas plus minusve adæquatas.","refs":["CH_QGOH_PS_3:SD2","CH_QGOH_PTDO:DNX1"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[]},{"id":"CL_QGOH_03:BSD-03","text":"Libertas augetur non minuendo necessitatem, sed augendo proportionem effectuum qui per ipsius individui naturam adæquate intelliguntur.","refs":["CH_QGOH_PS_4:SD2","CH_QGOH_PTDO:D1"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[]},{"id":"CL_QGOH_03:BSD-04","text":"Systema tres temporalitates componit: successionem imaginativam contingentem, perseverantiam indefinitam singularis et necessitatem æternam ordinis divini.","refs":["CH_QGOH_PS_3:SD5","CH_QGOH_PTDO:A14","CH_QGOH_PS_3:A15"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[]},{"id":"CL_QGOH_03:BSD-05","text":"Deus non est obiectum remotum contemplationis, sed conditio immanens qua singularia, cognitiones et affectus sub uno ordine intelligibiles sunt.","refs":["CH_QGOH_PS_4:SD1","CH_QGOH_PTDO:A1"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[]},{"id":"CL_QGOH_03:BSD-06","text":"Interventio ethica vel politica causas, ideas et compositiones potentiæ mutare debet potius quam voluntati imperare; modus practicus huius mutationis adhuc incompletus est.","refs":["CH_QGOH_PS_4:ME6","CH_QGOH_PTDO:MRT3"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[]}],"original_name":"Book-Scale Super-Derivatives"},{"number":"10","name":"Recursive Loops, Escalation Paths, and Conceptual Attractors","items":[{"id":"CL_QGOH_03:RLE-01","text":"Affectio Corporis ideam generat; qualitas ideæ actionem vel passionem determinat; inde potentia Corporis et Mentis variatur atque novis affectionibus exponitur.","refs":["CH_QGOH_PTDO:A4","CH_QGOH_PTDO:A7"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[]},{"id":"CL_QGOH_03:RLE-02","text":"Fortuitus rerum occursus ideas inadæquatas producit, quæ partialem causalitatem et ulteriorem dependentiam ab ordine externo conservant.","refs":["CH_QGOH_PS_3:A3","CH_QGOH_PTDO:NX5"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[]},{"id":"CL_QGOH_03:RLE-03","text":"Communitates Corporis cum aliis corporibus ideas adæquatas multiplicare possunt; ideæ adæquatæ actiones augent, unde via amplificationis rationalis aperitur.","refs":["CH_QGOH_PS_3:D7","CH_QGOH_PTDO:D1"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[]},{"id":"CL_QGOH_03:RLE-04","text":"Conatus imagines potentiæ augentis retinet et contrarias excludere conatur, ita ut imaginatio circuitum conservativum ipsius perseverantiæ efficiat.","refs":["CH_QGOH_PTDO:A6","CH_QGOH_PTDO:D7"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[]},{"id":"CL_QGOH_03:RLE-05","text":"Conscientia actionum sine cognitione causarum opinionem libertatis parit; hæc opinio causalitatem occultans eandem ignorantiam interpretativam reproducit.","refs":["CH_QGOH_PS_3:A17","CH_QGOH_PTDO:D3"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[]},{"id":"CL_QGOH_03:RLE-06","text":"Idea singularis ad Dei essentiam ascendit, necessitas divina singularitatem reordinat, et ratio eandem rem sub specie æternitatis recipit.","refs":["CH_QGOH_PS_3:A16","CH_QGOH_PS_4:D1","CH_QGOH_PS_3:A15"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[]},{"id":"CL_QGOH_03:RLE-07","text":"Attractor totius structuræ est causalitas adæquata: cognitio, actio, libertas et moderatio affectuum circa eius augmentum convergunt.","refs":["CH_QGOH_PTDO:A2","CH_QGOH_PTDO:SD1"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[]}],"original_name":"Recursive Loops, Escalation Paths, and Conceptual Attractors"},{"number":"11","name":"Formal Generative Schema of the Whole Book","items":[{"id":"CL_QGOH_03:FGS-B01","text":"Natura una et necessaria → nullum privilegium humanum → cognitio et affectus eisdem legibus explicandi.","refs":["CH_QGOH_PTDO:A1","CH_QGOH_PTDO:NX1"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[]},{"id":"CL_QGOH_03:FGS-B02","text":"Corpus affectum → idea affectionis → cognitio et affectus tamquam duplex expressio unius processus.","refs":["CH_QGOH_PS_3:A1","CH_QGOH_PTDO:A4"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[]},{"id":"CL_QGOH_03:FGS-B03","text":"Occursus externus fortuitus → idea inadæquata; proprietas communis invariabilis → idea adæquata.","refs":["CH_QGOH_PS_3:A3","CH_QGOH_PS_3:A9"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[]},{"id":"CL_QGOH_03:FGS-B04","text":"Idea → affirmatio intrinseca → dissolutio facultatis voluntatis separatæ.","refs":["CH_QGOH_PS_4:A8","CH_QGOH_PS_4:A9"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[]},{"id":"CL_QGOH_03:FGS-B05","text":"Idea adæquata → causa adæquata → actio; idea inadæquata → causa partialis → passio.","refs":["CH_QGOH_PTDO:A7","CH_QGOH_PTDO:A3"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[]},{"id":"CL_QGOH_03:FGS-B06","text":"Essentia actualis → conatus → Appetitus conscius → Cupiditas → transitus potentiæ → Lætitia vel Tristitia.","refs":["CH_QGOH_PTDO:A13","CH_QGOH_PTDO:A16","CH_QGOH_PTDO:D6"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[]},{"id":"CL_QGOH_03:FGS-B07","text":"Necessitas intellecta + incrementum adæquationis → maior actio → libertas immanens sine imperio voluntatis.","refs":["CH_QGOH_PTDO:SD1","CH_QGOH_PTDO:SD5"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[]}],"original_name":"Formal Generative Schema of the Whole Book"},{"number":"12","name":"Whole-Book Argument Flow Map","items":[{"id":"CL_QGOH_03:AFM-B01","text":"Ordo naturalis necessarius → affectio Corporis et idea → bifurcatio inter imaginationem inadæquatam et communitatem adæquatam → veritas vel privatio → affirmatio sine facultate libera → actio vel passio → conatus → Cupiditas et variatio potentiæ → libertas ut causalitas adæquata.","refs":["CH_QGOH_PTDO:A1","CH_QGOH_PTDO:A4","CH_QGOH_PTDO:A7","CH_QGOH_PTDO:A13"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[]}],"original_name":"Whole-Book Argument Flow Map"},{"number":"13","name":"Macro Map-Effects","items":[{"id":"CL_QGOH_03:MME-01","text":"Ager cognitionis et ager agentiæ in unam tabulam graduum adæquationis causaliter connectuntur.","refs":["CH_QGOH_PTDO:A7","CH_QGOH_PTDO:SD1"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[]},{"id":"CL_QGOH_03:MME-02","text":"Subiectum reclassificatur ex centro mentali clauso in modum naturalem cuius autonomia variabilis est secundum rationem causalitatis plenæ et partialis.","refs":["CH_QGOH_PS_4:DNX1","CH_QGOH_PTDO:DNX1"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[]},{"id":"CL_QGOH_03:MME-03","text":"Oppositio Mentis imperantis et Corporis oboedientis corruit; loco eius ponitur parallelismus expressionum eiusdem rei.","refs":["CH_QGOH_PTDO:A8","CH_QGOH_PTDO:A9"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[]},{"id":"CL_QGOH_03:MME-04","text":"Deus reclassificatur ex obiecto externo vel imaginabili in immanentem condicionem intelligibilitatis omnium singularium.","refs":["CH_QGOH_PS_4:ME1","CH_QGOH_PS_4:SD1"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[]},{"id":"CL_QGOH_03:MME-05","text":"Imaginatio dividitur inter functionem epistemice confusivam et functionem affective conservativam; ideo nec ratio est nec merus defectus iners.","refs":["CH_QGOH_PS_3:NX6","CH_QGOH_PTDO:D7"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[]},{"id":"CL_QGOH_03:MME-06","text":"Ordo valoris invertitur: bonum appetitum non causat, sed conatus res appetitas ut bonas constituit.","refs":["CH_QGOH_PTDO:NX7","CH_QGOH_PTDO:SD3"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[]},{"id":"CL_QGOH_03:MME-07","text":"Oppositio simplicis temporis et æternitatis solvitur in distinctionem inter contingentiam imaginatam, durationem indefinitam et necessitatem rationalem.","refs":["CH_QGOH_PS_3:SD5","CH_QGOH_PTDO:A14"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[]}],"original_name":"Macro Map-Effects"},{"number":"14","name":"Altitude Integration","items":[{"id":"CL_QGOH_03:ALT-B01","text":"Gradus superficiei ideas veras et falsas, cognitionem Dei, voluntatem, Cupiditatem, Lætitiam atque Tristitiam explicat.","refs":["CH_QGOH_PS_4:ALT1","CH_QGOH_PTDO:ALT1"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[]},{"id":"CL_QGOH_03:ALT-B02","text":"Gradus structuralis affectiones Corporis, adæquationem causalitatis, parallelismum attributorum, conatum et vestigia imaginativa in unam machinam componit.","refs":["CH_QGOH_PTDO:ALT2","CH_QGOH_PTDO:A9"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[]},{"id":"CL_QGOH_03:ALT-B03","text":"Gradus metastrategicus vocabula experientiæ communis causaliter redéfinit et moralizationem affectuum intellectione geometrica substituit.","refs":["CH_QGOH_PS_4:ALT3","CH_QGOH_PTDO:ALT3"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[]},{"id":"CL_QGOH_03:ALT-B04","text":"Gradus ontologicus et temporalis realitatem ut ordinem necessarium concipit, cuius singularia conatu perseverant dum imaginatio contingentiam producit.","refs":["CH_QGOH_PS_3:SD5","CH_QGOH_PTDO:ALT4"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[]},{"id":"CL_QGOH_03:ALT-B05","text":"Gradus methodologicus et performativus ipsam thesim necessitatatis efficit, quia terminos psychologicos in definitiones et consequentias causales resolvit.","refs":["CH_QGOH_PTDO:AS3","CH_QGOH_PTDO:ALT3"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[]}],"original_name":"Altitude Integration"},{"number":"15","name":"Internal Tensions, Mutation Points, and Fault Lines","items":[{"id":"CL_QGOH_03:FL-01","text":"Cognitio Dei omnibus ideis singularibus communis dicitur, sed gradus claritatis, adæquationis et passionis inter homines inæquales manent.","refs":["CH_QGOH_PS_4:MRT4","CH_QGOH_PTDO:D1"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"Tension","passages":[]},{"id":"CL_QGOH_03:FL-02","text":"Causalitas directa inter Mentem et Corpus negatur, quamvis variatio potentiæ corporalis variationem potentiæ cogitandi simul inducere dicatur; parallelismus relationem servat, mechanismum autem non ulterius explicat.","refs":["CH_QGOH_PTDO:A8","CH_QGOH_PTDO:A17"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"Tension","passages":[]},{"id":"CL_QGOH_03:FL-03","text":"Necessitas universalis cum exhortatione ethica et directione politica coniungitur, sed transitus a cognitione necessitatis ad efficaciam practicam nondum plenus est.","refs":["CH_QGOH_PS_4:MRT5","CH_QGOH_PTDO:MRT3"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"Tension","passages":[]},{"id":"CL_QGOH_03:FL-04","text":"Ratio rem sub specie æternitatis percipit, dum essentia actualis singularis durationem indefinitam affirmat; relatio inter hos duos modos temporalitatis non omnino explicatur.","refs":["CH_QGOH_PS_3:A15","CH_QGOH_PTDO:A14"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"Tension","passages":[]},{"id":"CL_QGOH_03:FL-05","text":"Imaginatio simul unica falsitatis causa primi generis et instrumentum conservativum conatus est; eius status nec pure privativus nec simpliciter activus manet.","refs":["CH_QGOH_PS_3:D9","CH_QGOH_PTDO:D7"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"Tension","passages":[]},{"id":"CL_QGOH_03:MP-01","text":"Idea affectionis in affectum mutatur ubi mediatio cognitiva variationem potentiæ Corporis simul exprimere incipit.","refs":["CH_QGOH_PS_3:A1","CH_QGOH_PTDO:A4"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"Tension","passages":[]},{"id":"CL_QGOH_03:MP-02","text":"Inadæquatio mutatur ex privatione epistemica in causalitatem partialem et passionem.","refs":["CH_QGOH_PS_3:A7","CH_QGOH_PTDO:A7"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"Tension","passages":[]},{"id":"CL_QGOH_03:MP-03","text":"Affirmatio intrinseca ideæ mutatur in affirmationem existentiæ, conatum, Appetitum et Cupiditatem.","refs":["CH_QGOH_PS_4:A8","CH_QGOH_PTDO:A13","CH_QGOH_PTDO:A16"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"Tension","passages":[]},{"id":"CL_QGOH_03:MP-04","text":"Corpus mutatur ex medio cognitionis externæ in variabilem potentiam cuius transitus affectus constituunt.","refs":["CH_QGOH_PS_3:AS2","CH_QGOH_PTDO:A17"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"Tension","passages":[]},{"id":"CL_QGOH_03:MP-05","text":"Libertas mutatur ex facultate non determinata in gradum causalitatis adæquatæ intra necessitatem.","refs":["CH_QGOH_PS_3:NX9","CH_QGOH_PTDO:SD1"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"Tension","passages":[]}],"original_name":"Internal Tensions, Mutation Points, and Fault Lines"},{"number":"16","name":"Hidden System Image","items":[{"id":"CL_QGOH_03:HSI-01","text":"Realitas implicite est una machina causalis quæ se sub Cogitatione et Extensione dupliciter exprimit, non duo regna inter se imperantia.","refs":["CH_QGOH_PTDO:A9","CH_QGOH_PTDO:A1"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[]},{"id":"CL_QGOH_03:HSI-02","text":"Subiectum est nodus corporeus et mentalis conatu perseverans, cuius potentia secundum affectiones et gradum adæquationis variatur.","refs":["CH_QGOH_PTDO:A4","CH_QGOH_PTDO:A13"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[]},{"id":"CL_QGOH_03:HSI-03","text":"Tempus huius machinæ triplex est: successio contingens imaginationis, duratio indefinita perseverantiæ et æternitas ordinis intellecti.","refs":["CH_QGOH_PS_3:SD5","CH_QGOH_PTDO:A14","CH_QGOH_PS_3:A15"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[]},{"id":"CL_QGOH_03:HSI-04","text":"Navigatio possibilis non est fuga ex necessitate, sed transitus ab occursibus externis et causalitate partiali ad ideas, relationes et actiones magis adæquatas.","refs":["CH_QGOH_PS_3:A3","CH_QGOH_PTDO:D7","CH_QGOH_PTDO:SD1"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[]}],"original_name":"Hidden System Image"},{"number":"17","name":"Minimal Whole-Book Reconstruction Test","items":[{"id":"CL_QGOH_03:MRT-B01","text":"Axiomata de immanentia Naturæ, mediatione corporis, adæquatione, affirmatione ideæ, conatu et affectu sufficiunt ad nucleum huius gradus restituendum.","refs":["CH_QGOH_PTDO:MRT1","CH_QGOH_PS_4:MRT1"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[]},{"id":"CL_QGOH_03:MRT-B02","text":"Structura firmissima est continuatio inter distinctionem veritatis et privationis atque seriem destructionis externæ, conatus, essentiæ actualis et durationis indefinitæ.","refs":["CH_QGOH_PS_3:MRT3","CH_QGOH_PTDO:MRT4"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[]},{"id":"CL_QGOH_03:MRT-B03","text":"Minus determinatum manet quomodo cognitio necessitatis adæquationem augeat, affectus moderetur et directionem socialem efficacem producat.","refs":["CH_QGOH_PTDO:MRT3","CH_QGOH_PS_4:MRT5"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[]},{"id":"CL_QGOH_03:MRT-B04","text":"Restitutio doctrinis prioribus de Deo et attributis pendet, dum nexus terminalis inter rem singularem et essentiam Dei demonstratione locali incompletus manet.","refs":["CH_QGOH_PS_3:MRT2","CH_QGOH_PTDO:MRT2"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[]},{"id":"CL_QGOH_03:MRT-B05","text":"Motus verus servatur si epistemologia, dissolutio voluntatis, conatus et affectus ut una machina continua leguntur, quamvis plena taxonomia affectuum reductioni adhuc resistat.","refs":["CH_QGOH_PS_4:MRT6","CH_QGOH_PTDO:MRT7"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[]}],"original_name":"Minimal Whole-Book Reconstruction Test"}],"language_code":"la","language_name":"Latina","translations":[{"id":"CAREVAR_20261015T015515020137Z_e9a7ec32","language_code":"en","language_name":"English","title":"Chapters 07–09","source_sha256":"4f41461cf7de53b07aaf8084b25bbc3860185161713ac0e056faa3d056c9a320","sha256":"7785b7b3fced30850aee2cb8c7a9b66c860970b8e7a5a491c3e4dfbf9b0f7d81","rendition_type":"machine","review_status":"machine","model":"gpt-5.6-terra","created":"2026-10-15T01:55:15.021042+00:00","sections":[{"number":"1","name":"Book-Scale Argumental Spine","items":[{"id":"CL_QGOH_03:AS-01","text":"The system asks under what conditions the finite Mind, which cannot be placed outside Nature, can understand its cognition, action, and affects within the same necessary order.","refs":["CH_QGOH_PS_3:AS1","CH_QGOH_PTDO:AS1"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[]},{"id":"CL_QGOH_03:AS-02","text":"The global thesis makes adequacy the common principle of truth and action: adequate ideas constitute true cognition and active causality, whereas inadequate ideas constitute mutilation and passion.","refs":["CH_QGOH_PS_3:AS2","CH_QGOH_PTDO:A7"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[]},{"id":"CL_QGOH_03:AS-03","text":"Diagnostic, reconstructive, and geometrical argumentation dissolves error, free will, and the moralization of vices, then establishes in their place a structure of graded causality.","refs":["CH_QGOH_PS_3:AS3","CH_QGOH_PTDO:AS3"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[]},{"id":"CL_QGOH_03:AS-04","text":"The movement proceeds from the affection of the Body to rational commonality, knowledge of God, the dissolution of the free faculty, actual conatus, and the formation of affects.","refs":["CH_QGOH_PS_3:SD4","CH_QGOH_PTDO:AS6"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[]},{"id":"CL_QGOH_03:AS-05","text":"The movement is not truly linear but recursive, because the same ideational reality returns successively as perception, affirmation, causality, and variation of power.","refs":["CH_QGOH_PS_3:SD1","CH_QGOH_PTDO:SD2"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[]},{"id":"CL_QGOH_03:AS-06","text":"The principal change transfers the terms of cognition into a dynamics of agency: adequacy and inadequacy cease merely to designate an epistemic state and become degrees of acting or undergoing.","refs":["CH_QGOH_PTDO:A7","CH_QGOH_PTDO:SD1"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[]},{"id":"CL_QGOH_03:AS-07","text":"The positive closure restores freedom not as exemption from necessity, but as action arising from understood necessity and more adequate causality.","refs":["CH_QGOH_PS_4:SD2","CH_QGOH_PTDO:SD5"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[]},{"id":"CL_QGOH_03:AS-08","text":"The closure nevertheless remains provisionally open, because the principle of converting cognition into power is given, but the practical criterion of increased adequacy and a full taxonomy of affects are not yet established.","refs":["CH_QGOH_PTDO:MRT3","CH_QGOH_PTDO:MRT7"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[]}],"original_name":"Book-Scale Argumental Spine"},{"number":"2","name":"Global Load-Bearing Axioms","items":[{"id":"CL_QGOH_03:GA-01","text":"[Persistent] Nature acts in one necessary order and by the same laws; the human being, cognition, and affects constitute no exceptional region within it.","refs":["CH_QGOH_PTDO:A1","CH_QGOH_PTDO:NX1"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[]},{"id":"CL_QGOH_03:GA-02","text":"[Persistent] The finite Mind knows through affections of the Body, while Mind and Body express the same thing under different attributes.","refs":["CH_QGOH_PS_3:A1","CH_QGOH_PTDO:A9"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[]},{"id":"CL_QGOH_03:GA-03","text":"[Mutating] Adequacy changes from a principle of truth into a principle of active causality: what follows from adequate ideas is at once cognition and action.","refs":["CH_QGOH_PS_3:A11","CH_QGOH_PTDO:A7"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[]},{"id":"CL_QGOH_03:GA-04","text":"[Persistent] Inadequacy does not constitute a positive order of falsity, but expresses a privation of cognition, partiality of cause, and passion.","refs":["CH_QGOH_PS_3:A7","CH_QGOH_PTDO:D4"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[]},{"id":"CL_QGOH_03:GA-05","text":"[Persistent] What is common and invariable is adequately conceived, and the idea of a singular thing through a divine attribute involves the essence of God.","refs":["CH_QGOH_PS_3:A9","CH_QGOH_PS_4:A3"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[]},{"id":"CL_QGOH_03:GA-06","text":"[Terminal] The universal faculties of will and intellect lose their proper causality, because every idea involves an intrinsic affirmation or negation.","refs":["CH_QGOH_PS_4:A7","CH_QGOH_PS_4:A8"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[]},{"id":"CL_QGOH_03:GA-07","text":"[Emergent] Conatus is the actual essence of a thing, to which no internal temporal limit is assigned.","refs":["CH_QGOH_PTDO:A13","CH_QGOH_PTDO:A14"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[]},{"id":"CL_QGOH_03:GA-08","text":"[Mutating] Will changes from a free faculty into singular affirmation, then is dynamically redistributed as the conatus of the Mind, Appetite, and Desire.","refs":["CH_QGOH_PS_4:AS4","CH_QGOH_PTDO:A16"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[]},{"id":"CL_QGOH_03:GA-09","text":"[Emergent] An affect is a variation of the power of the Body together with the idea of that same variation, whence the power of acting and thinking are ordered together.","refs":["CH_QGOH_PTDO:A4","CH_QGOH_PTDO:A17"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[]},{"id":"CL_QGOH_03:GA-10","text":"[Attractor] Freedom is greater causal adequacy within necessity, not indifference of choosing or dominion of the will.","refs":["CH_QGOH_PTDO:SD1","CH_QGOH_PS_4:SD2"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[]},{"id":"CL_QGOH_03:GA-11","text":"[Persistent] Consciousness of action or appetite does not include knowledge of causes; reflexivity without known causality does not prove original freedom.","refs":["CH_QGOH_PS_3:A17","CH_QGOH_PTDO:DNX3"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[]},{"id":"CL_QGOH_03:GA-12","text":"[Mutating] Time is divided among imaginative contingency, the indefinite duration of conatus, and necessity which reason perceives sub specie æternitatis.","refs":["CH_QGOH_PS_3:SD5","CH_QGOH_PTDO:A14"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[]}],"original_name":"Global Load-Bearing Axioms"},{"number":"3","name":"Recurrence, Mutation, and Displacement Matrix","items":[{"id":"CL_QGOH_03:RMD-01","text":"The idea of an affection of the Body returns as affect: epistemic mediation retains the same foundation, but now expresses a variation of power and a state of agency.","refs":["CH_QGOH_PS_3:A1","CH_QGOH_PTDO:A4"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[]},{"id":"CL_QGOH_03:RMD-02","text":"Inadequacy migrates from the origin of falsity to the principle of passion; privation of cognition also becomes a defect of complete causality.","refs":["CH_QGOH_PS_3:A7","CH_QGOH_PTDO:NX5"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[]},{"id":"CL_QGOH_03:RMD-03","text":"Adequacy changes function from the self-certainty of the idea to the production of actions of which the Mind is the sufficient cause.","refs":["CH_QGOH_PS_3:A14","CH_QGOH_PTDO:D1"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[]},{"id":"CL_QGOH_03:RMD-04","text":"The intrinsic affirmation of the idea is amplified into affirmation of the existence of the Body and finally into the conatus of persevering.","refs":["CH_QGOH_PS_4:A8","CH_QGOH_PTDO:A18"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[]},{"id":"CL_QGOH_03:RMD-05","text":"The negation of free will returns with a change of function: first it dissolves error concerning judgment, then it opens the causality of appetite and affects.","refs":["CH_QGOH_PS_3:NX9","CH_QGOH_PTDO:NX4"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[]},{"id":"CL_QGOH_03:RMD-06","text":"The Body changes from a medium of cognition into the dynamic substrate of power; what mediates perception also sustains variations of acting and thinking.","refs":["CH_QGOH_PS_3:AS2","CH_QGOH_PTDO:A17"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[]},{"id":"CL_QGOH_03:RMD-07","text":"Imagination is not simply eliminated: it is transferred from a source of contingency and confusion into an operation of conatus that selects images favorable to power.","refs":["CH_QGOH_PS_3:D12","CH_QGOH_PTDO:D7"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[]},{"id":"CL_QGOH_03:RMD-08","text":"Knowledge of God, initially the terminus of epistemic ascent, is absorbed into the tacit foundation of the naturalization of affects.","refs":["CH_QGOH_PS_3:AS7","CH_QGOH_PTDO:A1"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[]},{"id":"CL_QGOH_03:RMD-09","text":"Universal faculties vanish after they have explained singular acts, but the doctrine of God and adequate ideas persists as the infrastructure of the affective section.","refs":["CH_QGOH_PS_4:A7","CH_QGOH_PTDO:MRT2"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[]}],"original_name":"Recurrence, Mutation, and Displacement Matrix"},{"number":"4","name":"Lower-Layer vs Current-Level Distinction","items":[{"id":"CL_QGOH_03:CGD-01","text":"Local doctrines concerning transcendental terms and the three kinds of cognition are not expanded integrally; their present function is to establish the distinction between confused imagination and adequacy.","refs":["CH_QGOH_PS_3:A12","CH_QGOH_PS_3:A13"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[]},{"id":"CL_QGOH_03:CGD-02","text":"The nexus of the singular idea with God rises above the local level, because it establishes the immanence by which cognition and affects can be subjected to common laws.","refs":["CH_QGOH_PS_4:SD1","CH_QGOH_PTDO:A1"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[]},{"id":"CL_QGOH_03:CGD-03","text":"The identity of will and intellect becomes the joint between epistemology and the doctrine of affects, when the affirmation of the idea is extended into Appetite and Desire.","refs":["CH_QGOH_PS_4:D4","CH_QGOH_PTDO:A16"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[]},{"id":"CL_QGOH_03:CGD-04","text":"The taxonomy of Desire, Joy, and Sadness remains a local mechanism; the structure of this level is the condition of causality by which such a taxonomy is generated.","refs":["CH_QGOH_PTDO:D6"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[]},{"id":"CL_QGOH_03:CGD-05","text":"The distinction between adequate and partial cause joins the units, because it converts the distinction between true and false into the difference between acting and undergoing.","refs":["CH_QGOH_PTDO:A2","CH_QGOH_PTDO:A3"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[]},{"id":"CL_QGOH_03:CGD-06","text":"The ethical-political outcome indicates the global direction, but the practical efficacy by which cognition of necessity may change affects and the actions of citizens is not yet demonstrated.","refs":["CH_QGOH_PS_4:SD3","CH_QGOH_PTDO:MRT3"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[]},{"id":"CL_QGOH_03:CGD-07","text":"The promised taxonomy of the remaining affects remains beyond this level; here only the machine capable of producing primary affects is reconstructed.","refs":["CH_QGOH_PTDO:MRT7"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[]}],"original_name":"Lower-Layer vs Current-Level Distinction"},{"number":"5","name":"Global Apophatic Structure","items":[{"id":"CL_QGOH_03:GNX-01","text":"The Mind does not possess cognition of itself, of the Body, and of external things given immediately and without affections.","refs":["CH_QGOH_PS_3:NX1"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"Negative","passages":[]},{"id":"CL_QGOH_03:GNX-02","text":"Falsity is not a positive form of thought, but a defect of sufficient cognitive relation.","refs":["CH_QGOH_PS_3:NX4"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"Negative","passages":[]},{"id":"CL_QGOH_03:GNX-03","text":"Will is neither a free cause nor an infinite power above singular ideas.","refs":["CH_QGOH_PS_4:NX1","CH_QGOH_PS_4:NX2"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"Negative","passages":[]},{"id":"CL_QGOH_03:GNX-04","text":"Mind and Body do not exercise transverse causality between attributes.","refs":["CH_QGOH_PTDO:A8"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"Negative","passages":[]},{"id":"CL_QGOH_03:GNX-05","text":"The human being is not an empire within an empire, and affects are not vices rising up against the order of Nature.","refs":["CH_QGOH_PTDO:NX1","CH_QGOH_PTDO:NX2"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"Negative","passages":[]},{"id":"CL_QGOH_03:GNX-06","text":"No thing contains an internal principle of its own destruction.","refs":["CH_QGOH_PTDO:A10"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"Negative","passages":[]},{"id":"CL_QGOH_03:GNX-07","text":"The good does not precede appetite as an external cause; the judgment of good follows from appetite already operating.","refs":["CH_QGOH_PTDO:NX7"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"Negative","passages":[]}],"original_name":"Global Apophatic Structure"},{"number":"6","name":"Deep Book Apophatics","items":[{"id":"CL_QGOH_03:DBN-01","text":"The model of a self-governing subject constituted prior to its ideas, Body, and causes must be false.","refs":["CH_QGOH_PS_4:DNX1","CH_QGOH_PTDO:DNX1"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"Negative","passages":[]},{"id":"CL_QGOH_03:DBN-02","text":"The model of two orders, one of truth and freedom, the other of error and necessity, must be false.","refs":["CH_QGOH_PS_3:SNX2","CH_QGOH_PTDO:A9"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"Negative","passages":[]},{"id":"CL_QGOH_03:DBN-03","text":"The model of reason or affect separable from bodily relations must be false.","refs":["CH_QGOH_PS_3:DNX4","CH_QGOH_PTDO:SNX4"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"Negative","passages":[]},{"id":"CL_QGOH_03:DBN-04","text":"The model of an external norm that establishes from without the truth of an idea or the goodness of appetite must be false.","refs":["CH_QGOH_PS_3:SNX4","CH_QGOH_PTDO:SD3"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"Negative","passages":[]},{"id":"CL_QGOH_03:DBN-05","text":"The model of causally inert cognition must be false, because the difference between adequate and inadequate ideas produces the difference between action and passion.","refs":["CH_QGOH_PTDO:A7","CH_QGOH_PTDO:SD5"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"Negative","passages":[]},{"id":"CL_QGOH_03:DBN-06","text":"The model of imagined time as the ultimate horizon of things must be false, since eternal necessity and the indefinite duration of conatus belong to different times.","refs":["CH_QGOH_PS_3:SNX3","CH_QGOH_PTDO:A14"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"Negative","passages":[]}],"original_name":"Deep Book Apophatics"},{"number":"7","name":"Super-Apophatics of the Whole","items":[{"id":"CL_QGOH_03:BSA-01","text":"No region external to Nature is admitted from which God, truth, freedom, or affects may be judged absolutely.","refs":["CH_QGOH_PTDO:SNX1","CH_QGOH_PS_4:SNX2"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"Negative","passages":[]},{"id":"CL_QGOH_03:BSA-02","text":"No absolute origin of thought or action, immune from the series of causes, is ontologically available.","refs":["CH_QGOH_PS_4:SNX1","CH_QGOH_PTDO:SNX2"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"Negative","passages":[]},{"id":"CL_QGOH_03:BSA-03","text":"Freedom as pure indifference is impossible; the only admissible freedom passes through understood necessity and increased causality.","refs":["CH_QGOH_PS_4:SNX4","CH_QGOH_PTDO:SD1"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"Negative","passages":[]},{"id":"CL_QGOH_03:BSA-04","text":"An intrinsic principle of non-being is excluded, whence negation, destruction, and diminution must be explained through external relations or partiality.","refs":["CH_QGOH_PTDO:SNX3","CH_QGOH_PTDO:A10"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"Negative","passages":[]}],"original_name":"Super-Apophatics of the Whole"},{"number":"8","name":"Cross-Unit Derivative Propositions","items":[{"id":"CL_QGOH_03:XD-01","text":"The same difference that separates a true idea from an inadequate one distinguishes action from passion; epistemic adequacy becomes causal power.","refs":["CL_QGOH_03:GA-03","CL_QGOH_03:GA-04","CH_QGOH_PS_3:A11","CH_QGOH_PTDO:A7"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"From","passages":[]},{"id":"CL_QGOH_03:XD-02","text":"Bodily mediation not only limits finite cognition, but also constitutes the real field of power in which cognition and affects vary.","refs":["CL_QGOH_03:GA-02","CL_QGOH_03:GA-09","CH_QGOH_PS_3:AS2","CH_QGOH_PTDO:SD2"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"From","passages":[]},{"id":"CL_QGOH_03:XD-03","text":"Since every idea involves affirmation and actual essence is conatus, volition becomes the conscious expression of perseverance rather than an act of a free faculty.","refs":["CL_QGOH_03:GA-06","CL_QGOH_03:GA-07","CL_QGOH_03:GA-08","CH_QGOH_PS_4:A8","CH_QGOH_PTDO:A13","CH_QGOH_PTDO:A16"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"From","passages":[]},{"id":"CL_QGOH_03:XD-04","text":"Because the singular idea involves the essence of God and Nature acts everywhere by the same laws, affects become demonstrable from divine immanence itself.","refs":["CL_QGOH_03:GA-01","CL_QGOH_03:GA-05","CH_QGOH_PS_4:A3","CH_QGOH_PTDO:A1"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"From","passages":[]},{"id":"CL_QGOH_03:XD-05","text":"The belief in freedom arises from consciousness without knowledge of causes; its substitute is not fatal inertia, but greater adequate causality.","refs":["CL_QGOH_03:GA-10","CL_QGOH_03:GA-11","CH_QGOH_PS_3:A17","CH_QGOH_PTDO:SD1"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"From","passages":[]},{"id":"CL_QGOH_03:XD-06","text":"Imagination has a double and unstable function: it generates contingency and error, but at the same time conatively orders images favorable to power.","refs":["CL_QGOH_03:GA-02","CL_QGOH_03:GA-09","CH_QGOH_PS_3:D12","CH_QGOH_PTDO:D7"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"From","passages":[]},{"id":"CL_QGOH_03:XD-07","text":"The persistence of a finite thing is neither pure contingency nor eternity: conatus has indefinite duration, while reason understands its necessity sub specie æternitatis.","refs":["CL_QGOH_03:GA-07","CL_QGOH_03:GA-12","CH_QGOH_PS_3:SD5","CH_QGOH_PTDO:A14","CH_QGOH_PS_3:A15"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"From","passages":[]},{"id":"CL_QGOH_03:XD-08","text":"Since the judgment of good follows Desire but freedom arises from understood necessity, normativity becomes the immanent ordering of conatus, not an external command opposed to appetite.","refs":["CL_QGOH_03:GA-08","CL_QGOH_03:GA-10","CH_QGOH_PTDO:NX7","CH_QGOH_PS_4:SD2"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"From","passages":[]}],"original_name":"Cross-Unit Derivative Propositions"},{"number":"9","name":"Book-Scale Super-Derivatives","items":[{"id":"CL_QGOH_03:BSD-01","text":"Truth, agency, and power are not separate regions, but three measures of one degree of causal expression.","refs":["CH_QGOH_PS_3:SD1","CH_QGOH_PTDO:SD1"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[]},{"id":"CL_QGOH_03:BSD-02","text":"The human subject is not a closed substance and principle of acts, but a mobile relation among external causes, bodily affections, and more or less adequate ideas.","refs":["CH_QGOH_PS_3:SD2","CH_QGOH_PTDO:DNX1"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[]},{"id":"CL_QGOH_03:BSD-03","text":"Freedom increases not by diminishing necessity, but by increasing the proportion of effects that are adequately understood through the nature of the individual itself.","refs":["CH_QGOH_PS_4:SD2","CH_QGOH_PTDO:D1"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[]},{"id":"CL_QGOH_03:BSD-04","text":"The system composes three temporalities: contingent imaginative succession, the indefinite perseverance of the singular, and the eternal necessity of the divine order.","refs":["CH_QGOH_PS_3:SD5","CH_QGOH_PTDO:A14","CH_QGOH_PS_3:A15"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[]},{"id":"CL_QGOH_03:BSD-05","text":"God is not a remote object of contemplation, but the immanent condition by which singular things, cognitions, and affects are intelligible under one order.","refs":["CH_QGOH_PS_4:SD1","CH_QGOH_PTDO:A1"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[]},{"id":"CL_QGOH_03:BSD-06","text":"Ethical or political intervention must change causes, ideas, and compositions of power rather than command the will; the practical mode of this change remains incomplete.","refs":["CH_QGOH_PS_4:ME6","CH_QGOH_PTDO:MRT3"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[]}],"original_name":"Book-Scale Super-Derivatives"},{"number":"10","name":"Recursive Loops, Escalation Paths, and Conceptual Attractors","items":[{"id":"CL_QGOH_03:RLE-01","text":"An affection of the Body generates an idea; the quality of the idea determines action or passion; thence the power of Body and Mind is varied and exposed to new affections.","refs":["CH_QGOH_PTDO:A4","CH_QGOH_PTDO:A7"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[]},{"id":"CL_QGOH_03:RLE-02","text":"The fortuitous encounter of things produces inadequate ideas, which preserve partial causality and further dependence on the external order.","refs":["CH_QGOH_PS_3:A3","CH_QGOH_PTDO:NX5"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[]},{"id":"CL_QGOH_03:RLE-03","text":"Communities of the Body with other bodies can multiply adequate ideas; adequate ideas increase actions, whence a path of rational amplification opens.","refs":["CH_QGOH_PS_3:D7","CH_QGOH_PTDO:D1"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[]},{"id":"CL_QGOH_03:RLE-04","text":"Conatus retains images of increasing power and attempts to exclude contrary ones, so that imagination produces a conservative circuit of perseverance itself.","refs":["CH_QGOH_PTDO:A6","CH_QGOH_PTDO:D7"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[]},{"id":"CL_QGOH_03:RLE-05","text":"Consciousness of actions without knowledge of causes gives rise to the opinion of freedom; this opinion, concealing causality, reproduces the same interpretive ignorance.","refs":["CH_QGOH_PS_3:A17","CH_QGOH_PTDO:D3"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[]},{"id":"CL_QGOH_03:RLE-06","text":"The singular idea ascends to the essence of God, divine necessity reorders singularity, and reason receives the same thing sub specie æternitatis.","refs":["CH_QGOH_PS_3:A16","CH_QGOH_PS_4:D1","CH_QGOH_PS_3:A15"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[]},{"id":"CL_QGOH_03:RLE-07","text":"The attractor of the whole structure is adequate causality: cognition, action, freedom, and the moderation of affects converge around its increase.","refs":["CH_QGOH_PTDO:A2","CH_QGOH_PTDO:SD1"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[]}],"original_name":"Recursive Loops, Escalation Paths, and Conceptual Attractors"},{"number":"11","name":"Formal Generative Schema of the Whole Book","items":[{"id":"CL_QGOH_03:FGS-B01","text":"One necessary Nature → no human privilege → cognition and affects to be explained by the same laws.","refs":["CH_QGOH_PTDO:A1","CH_QGOH_PTDO:NX1"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[]},{"id":"CL_QGOH_03:FGS-B02","text":"Affected Body → idea of affection → cognition and affects as a double expression of one process.","refs":["CH_QGOH_PS_3:A1","CH_QGOH_PTDO:A4"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[]},{"id":"CL_QGOH_03:FGS-B03","text":"Fortuitous external encounter → inadequate idea; invariable common property → adequate idea.","refs":["CH_QGOH_PS_3:A3","CH_QGOH_PS_3:A9"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[]},{"id":"CL_QGOH_03:FGS-B04","text":"Idea → intrinsic affirmation → dissolution of the faculty of separate will.","refs":["CH_QGOH_PS_4:A8","CH_QGOH_PS_4:A9"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[]},{"id":"CL_QGOH_03:FGS-B05","text":"Adequate idea → adequate cause → action; inadequate idea → partial cause → passion.","refs":["CH_QGOH_PTDO:A7","CH_QGOH_PTDO:A3"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[]},{"id":"CL_QGOH_03:FGS-B06","text":"Actual essence → conatus → conscious Appetite → Desire → transition of power → Joy or Sadness.","refs":["CH_QGOH_PTDO:A13","CH_QGOH_PTDO:A16","CH_QGOH_PTDO:D6"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[]},{"id":"CL_QGOH_03:FGS-B07","text":"Understood necessity + increase of adequacy → greater action → immanent freedom without dominion of the will.","refs":["CH_QGOH_PTDO:SD1","CH_QGOH_PTDO:SD5"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[]}],"original_name":"Formal Generative Schema of the Whole Book"},{"number":"12","name":"Whole-Book Argument Flow Map","items":[{"id":"CL_QGOH_03:AFM-B01","text":"Necessary natural order → affection of the Body and idea → bifurcation between inadequate imagination and adequate commonality → truth or privation → affirmation without free faculty → action or passion → conatus → Desire and variation of power → freedom as adequate causality.","refs":["CH_QGOH_PTDO:A1","CH_QGOH_PTDO:A4","CH_QGOH_PTDO:A7","CH_QGOH_PTDO:A13"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[]}],"original_name":"Whole-Book Argument Flow Map"},{"number":"13","name":"Macro Map-Effects","items":[{"id":"CL_QGOH_03:MME-01","text":"The field of cognition and the field of agency are causally connected in one map of degrees of adequacy.","refs":["CH_QGOH_PTDO:A7","CH_QGOH_PTDO:SD1"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[]},{"id":"CL_QGOH_03:MME-02","text":"The subject is reclassified from a closed mental center into a natural mode whose autonomy is variable according to the relation of complete and partial causality.","refs":["CH_QGOH_PS_4:DNX1","CH_QGOH_PTDO:DNX1"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[]},{"id":"CL_QGOH_03:MME-03","text":"The opposition of commanding Mind and obedient Body collapses; in its place is posited the parallelism of expressions of the same thing.","refs":["CH_QGOH_PTDO:A8","CH_QGOH_PTDO:A9"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[]},{"id":"CL_QGOH_03:MME-04","text":"God is reclassified from an external or imaginable object into the immanent condition of the intelligibility of all singular things.","refs":["CH_QGOH_PS_4:ME1","CH_QGOH_PS_4:SD1"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[]},{"id":"CL_QGOH_03:MME-05","text":"Imagination is divided between an epistemically confusing function and an affectively conservative function; therefore it is neither reason nor a mere inert defect.","refs":["CH_QGOH_PS_3:NX6","CH_QGOH_PTDO:D7"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[]},{"id":"CL_QGOH_03:MME-06","text":"The order of value is inverted: the good does not cause appetite, but conatus constitutes desired things as good.","refs":["CH_QGOH_PTDO:NX7","CH_QGOH_PTDO:SD3"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[]},{"id":"CL_QGOH_03:MME-07","text":"The opposition of simple time and eternity is dissolved into the distinction among imagined contingency, indefinite duration, and rational necessity.","refs":["CH_QGOH_PS_3:SD5","CH_QGOH_PTDO:A14"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[]}],"original_name":"Macro Map-Effects"},{"number":"14","name":"Altitude Integration","items":[{"id":"CL_QGOH_03:ALT-B01","text":"The surface level explains true and false ideas, knowledge of God, will, Desire, Joy, and Sadness.","refs":["CH_QGOH_PS_4:ALT1","CH_QGOH_PTDO:ALT1"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[]},{"id":"CL_QGOH_03:ALT-B02","text":"The structural level composes affections of the Body, adequacy of causality, parallelism of attributes, conatus, and imaginative traces into one machine.","refs":["CH_QGOH_PTDO:ALT2","CH_QGOH_PTDO:A9"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[]},{"id":"CL_QGOH_03:ALT-B03","text":"The metastrategic level causally redefines the terms of common experience and replaces the moralization of affects with geometrical understanding.","refs":["CH_QGOH_PS_4:ALT3","CH_QGOH_PTDO:ALT3"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[]},{"id":"CL_QGOH_03:ALT-B04","text":"The ontological and temporal level conceives reality as a necessary order, whose singular things persevere by conatus while imagination produces contingency.","refs":["CH_QGOH_PS_3:SD5","CH_QGOH_PTDO:ALT4"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[]},{"id":"CL_QGOH_03:ALT-B05","text":"The methodological and performative level effectuates the thesis of necessity itself, because it resolves psychological terms into causal definitions and consequences.","refs":["CH_QGOH_PTDO:AS3","CH_QGOH_PTDO:ALT3"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[]}],"original_name":"Altitude Integration"},{"number":"15","name":"Internal Tensions, Mutation Points, and Fault Lines","items":[{"id":"CL_QGOH_03:FL-01","text":"Knowledge of God is said to be common to all singular ideas, but degrees of clarity, adequacy, and passion remain unequal among human beings.","refs":["CH_QGOH_PS_4:MRT4","CH_QGOH_PTDO:D1"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"Tension","passages":[]},{"id":"CL_QGOH_03:FL-02","text":"Direct causality between Mind and Body is denied, although variation of bodily power is said simultaneously to induce variation of the power of thinking; parallelism preserves the relation but does not further explain the mechanism.","refs":["CH_QGOH_PTDO:A8","CH_QGOH_PTDO:A17"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"Tension","passages":[]},{"id":"CL_QGOH_03:FL-03","text":"Universal necessity is joined with ethical exhortation and political direction, but the transition from cognition of necessity to practical efficacy is not yet complete.","refs":["CH_QGOH_PS_4:MRT5","CH_QGOH_PTDO:MRT3"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"Tension","passages":[]},{"id":"CL_QGOH_03:FL-04","text":"Reason perceives a thing sub specie æternitatis, while the actual essence of the singular affirms indefinite duration; the relation between these two modes of temporality is not wholly explained.","refs":["CH_QGOH_PS_3:A15","CH_QGOH_PTDO:A14"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"Tension","passages":[]},{"id":"CL_QGOH_03:FL-05","text":"Imagination is at once the sole cause of falsity of the first kind and the conservative instrument of conatus; its status remains neither purely privative nor simply active.","refs":["CH_QGOH_PS_3:D9","CH_QGOH_PTDO:D7"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"Tension","passages":[]},{"id":"CL_QGOH_03:MP-01","text":"The idea of affection changes into affect where cognitive mediation simultaneously begins to express variation of the power of the Body.","refs":["CH_QGOH_PS_3:A1","CH_QGOH_PTDO:A4"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"Tension","passages":[]},{"id":"CL_QGOH_03:MP-02","text":"Inadequacy changes from epistemic privation into partial causality and passion.","refs":["CH_QGOH_PS_3:A7","CH_QGOH_PTDO:A7"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"Tension","passages":[]},{"id":"CL_QGOH_03:MP-03","text":"The intrinsic affirmation of the idea changes into affirmation of existence, conatus, Appetite, and Desire.","refs":["CH_QGOH_PS_4:A8","CH_QGOH_PTDO:A13","CH_QGOH_PTDO:A16"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"Tension","passages":[]},{"id":"CL_QGOH_03:MP-04","text":"The Body changes from the medium of external cognition into variable power, whose transitions constitute affects.","refs":["CH_QGOH_PS_3:AS2","CH_QGOH_PTDO:A17"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"Tension","passages":[]},{"id":"CL_QGOH_03:MP-05","text":"Freedom changes from an undetermined faculty into a degree of adequate causality within necessity.","refs":["CH_QGOH_PS_3:NX9","CH_QGOH_PTDO:SD1"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"Tension","passages":[]}],"original_name":"Internal Tensions, Mutation Points, and Fault Lines"},{"number":"16","name":"Hidden System Image","items":[{"id":"CL_QGOH_03:HSI-01","text":"Reality is implicitly one causal machine that expresses itself doubly under Thought and Extension, not two realms commanding one another.","refs":["CH_QGOH_PTDO:A9","CH_QGOH_PTDO:A1"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[]},{"id":"CL_QGOH_03:HSI-02","text":"The subject is a bodily and mental node persevering by conatus, whose power varies according to affections and the degree of adequacy.","refs":["CH_QGOH_PTDO:A4","CH_QGOH_PTDO:A13"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[]},{"id":"CL_QGOH_03:HSI-03","text":"The time of this machine is triple: the contingent succession of imagination, the indefinite duration of perseverance, and the eternity of the understood order.","refs":["CH_QGOH_PS_3:SD5","CH_QGOH_PTDO:A14","CH_QGOH_PS_3:A15"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[]},{"id":"CL_QGOH_03:HSI-04","text":"Possible navigation is not flight from necessity, but passage from external encounters and partial causality to more adequate ideas, relations, and actions.","refs":["CH_QGOH_PS_3:A3","CH_QGOH_PTDO:D7","CH_QGOH_PTDO:SD1"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[]}],"original_name":"Hidden System Image"},{"number":"17","name":"Minimal Whole-Book Reconstruction Test","items":[{"id":"CL_QGOH_03:MRT-B01","text":"The axioms concerning the immanence of Nature, bodily mediation, adequacy, affirmation of the idea, conatus, and affect suffice to reconstruct the nucleus of this level.","refs":["CH_QGOH_PTDO:MRT1","CH_QGOH_PS_4:MRT1"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[]},{"id":"CL_QGOH_03:MRT-B02","text":"The firmest structure is the continuity between the distinction of truth and privation and the series of external destruction, conatus, actual essence, and indefinite duration.","refs":["CH_QGOH_PS_3:MRT3","CH_QGOH_PTDO:MRT4"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[]},{"id":"CL_QGOH_03:MRT-B03","text":"Less determined remains how cognition of necessity increases adequacy, moderates affects, and produces effective social direction.","refs":["CH_QGOH_PTDO:MRT3","CH_QGOH_PS_4:MRT5"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[]},{"id":"CL_QGOH_03:MRT-B04","text":"Reconstruction depends on prior doctrines concerning God and attributes, while the terminal nexus between the singular thing and the essence of God remains incomplete in local demonstration.","refs":["CH_QGOH_PS_3:MRT2","CH_QGOH_PTDO:MRT2"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[]},{"id":"CL_QGOH_03:MRT-B05","text":"The true movement is preserved if epistemology, the dissolution of will, conatus, and affects are read as one continuous machine, although the full taxonomy of affects still resists reduction.","refs":["CH_QGOH_PS_4:MRT6","CH_QGOH_PTDO:MRT7"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[]}],"original_name":"Minimal Whole-Book Reconstruction Test"}],"revision":"CL_QGOH_03@R001","source_variant_id":"canonical:CL_QGOH_03:v1","source_language_code":"la","original_title":"Chapters 07–09"}],"identity":{"layer":"CL","kicker":"Cluster · Ethica","title":"Chapters 07–09","context":"Part II - On the Nature and Origin of the Mind · passage 3 · Part II - On the Nature and Origin of the Mind · passage 4 · Part III - On the Origin and Nature of the Emotions · passage 1","original_title":"Chapters 07–09","original_context":"Pars secunda - De natura et origine mentis · passage 3 · Pars secunda - De natura et origine mentis · passage 4 · Pars tertia - De origine et natura affectuum · passage 1"},"original_title":"Chapters 07–09","title_language_code":"en"},{"id":"CL_QGOH_04","layer":"CL","title":"Chapters 10–12","order":4,"parents":["CH_QGOH_PT_2","CH_QGOH_PT_3","CH_QGOH_PT_4"],"revision":"CL_QGOH_04@R001","sha256":"501b560ffa8f8abbba2cd33ff373532f7d667d3a1c0eac95f07d11cd877c4363","sections":[{"number":"1","name":"Book-Scale Argumental Spine","items":[{"id":"CL_QGOH_04:AS-01","text":"Hoc gradu quaeritur qua grammatica causalis conatus, affectiones Corporis et imagines rerum affectus privatos, relationes sociales atque actionem producant.","refs":["CH_QGOH_PT_2:AS1","CH_QGOH_PT_4:AS1"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[]},{"id":"CL_QGOH_04:AS-02","text":"Cupiditas essentiam individui operantem exprimit, Laetitia et Tristitia mutationes potentiae metiuntur, atque valor obiecti ex relatione affectiva potius quam ex proprietate intrinseca nascitur.","refs":["CH_QGOH_PT_4:A1","CH_QGOH_PT_4:A3","CH_QGOH_PT_2:SD1"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[]},{"id":"CL_QGOH_04:AS-03","text":"Argumentatio genetica et diagnostica affectus compositos ex paucis legibus deducit, dum vocabula psychologica et moralia vulgaria in mechanismos causales resolvit.","refs":["CH_QGOH_PT_2:AS3","CH_QGOH_PT_4:AS3","CH_QGOH_PT_4:SD1"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[]},{"id":"CL_QGOH_04:AS-04","text":"Motus procedit ab associatione imaginativa et causalitate per accidens ad imitationem, reciprocitatem, rivalitatem, collectivizationem et comparationem reflexivam.","refs":["CH_QGOH_PT_2:AS4","CH_QGOH_PT_3:AS3"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[]},{"id":"CL_QGOH_04:AS-05","text":"Affectus socialis fit circuitus recursivus: affectus alterius imaginatus potentiam propriam mutat, responsionem generat et condicionem affectivam alterius vicissim reordinat.","refs":["CH_QGOH_PT_3:SD1","CH_QGOH_PT_4:SD3"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[]},{"id":"CL_QGOH_04:AS-06","text":"Diversitas constitutionum Corporum et mutatio corporis per fruitionem prohibent ne idem obiectum valorem vel effectum affectivum stabilem retineat.","refs":["CH_QGOH_PT_3:A16","CH_QGOH_PT_4:A13","CH_QGOH_PT_4:SD5"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[]},{"id":"CL_QGOH_04:AS-07","text":"Punctum terminale mutat criterium affectus: inadequatio imaginationis passionem constituit, idea adaequata actionem, Laetitiam activam, Animositatem et Generositatem producit.","refs":["CH_QGOH_PT_4:AS6","CH_QGOH_PT_4:A11","CH_QGOH_PT_4:A14"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[]},{"id":"CL_QGOH_04:AS-08","text":"Motus totus est cumulativus, recursivus et mutatione actus; clausura non est catalogus ultimus affectuum, sed grammatica causalis quae transitum a passionibus ad actionem tractabilem reddit.","refs":["CH_QGOH_PT_4:SNX3","CH_QGOH_PT_4:MRT8","CH_QGOH_PT_4:D12"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[]}],"original_name":"Book-Scale Argumental Spine"},{"number":"2","name":"Global Load-Bearing Axioms","items":[{"id":"CL_QGOH_04:GA-01","text":"[Persistent] Cupiditas est conatus sive essentia individui, quatenus affectione determinata ad agendum dirigitur, sive huius determinationis conscia sit sive non.","refs":["CH_QGOH_PT_4:A1","CH_QGOH_PT_4:A2"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[]},{"id":"CL_QGOH_04:GA-02","text":"[Persistent] Laetitia et Tristitia non sunt status fixi, sed transitus ad maiorem vel minorem potentiam agendi.","refs":["CH_QGOH_PT_2:A2","CH_QGOH_PT_4:A3"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[]},{"id":"CL_QGOH_04:GA-03","text":"[Persistent] Imago est affectio Corporis quae rem absentem, praeteritam vel futuram affective praesentem reddere potest, quamdiu existentiam eius affirmat.","refs":["CH_QGOH_PT_3:A1","CH_QGOH_PT_2:A7"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[]},{"id":"CL_QGOH_04:GA-04","text":"[Persistent] Coaffectio, memoria, similitudo et associatio transferunt affectum inter obiecta, ita ut res causaliter neutra causa affectiva per accidens fieri possit.","refs":["CH_QGOH_PT_2:A3","CH_QGOH_PT_2:A4"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[]},{"id":"CL_QGOH_04:GA-05","text":"[Persistent] Laetitia vel Tristitia cum idea causae externae Amorem vel Odium constituit, unde conatus conservandi, promovendi, removendi aut destruendi sequitur.","refs":["CH_QGOH_PT_2:A5","CH_QGOH_PT_2:A6"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[]},{"id":"CL_QGOH_04:GA-06","text":"[Mutating] Idem obiectum affectus contrarios aut diversos producit secundum compositionem Corporis, tempus, memoriam et constitutionem ex fruitione mutatam.","refs":["CH_QGOH_PT_2:A8","CH_QGOH_PT_3:A16","CH_QGOH_PT_4:A13"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[]},{"id":"CL_QGOH_04:GA-07","text":"[Mutating] Similitudo affectum alienum in observantem transfert, sed Odium, differentia essentiarum et mutatio relationis signum vel intensionem translationis invertere possunt.","refs":["CH_QGOH_PT_2:A10","CH_QGOH_PT_3:A2","CH_QGOH_PT_4:A7"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[]},{"id":"CL_QGOH_04:GA-08","text":"[Emergent] Affectus interpersonales sunt circuitus reciproci in quibus Amor, Odium, Gloria, Vindicta et aestimatio sui per affectus imaginatos aliorum reproducuntur.","refs":["CH_QGOH_PT_3:SD1","CH_QGOH_PT_4:A9"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[]},{"id":"CL_QGOH_04:GA-09","text":"[Emergent] Attributio causae est operator directionis affectivae: causa individuata affectum intendit, distributa diluit, universalizata collectivizat.","refs":["CH_QGOH_PT_3:SD3","CH_QGOH_PT_3:A11"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[]},{"id":"CL_QGOH_04:GA-10","text":"[Emergent] Bonum et malum affectum et Cupiditatem non antecedunt, sed nomina sunt quibus res secundum desiderium, aversionem et mutationem potentiae aestimantur.","refs":["CH_QGOH_PT_3:A13","CH_QGOH_PT_4:SD2"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[]},{"id":"CL_QGOH_04:GA-11","text":"[Emergent] Trias Cupiditatis, Laetitiae et Tristitiae innumerabiles affectuum species generat, quamquam nulla taxonomia lexicalis ultimam earum clausuram praebet.","refs":["CH_QGOH_PT_4:SD1","CH_QGOH_PT_4:SNX3"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[]},{"id":"CL_QGOH_04:GA-12","text":"[Terminal] Passio et actio non exteriori forma sed modo cognitionis distinguuntur: idea inadequata passionem, idea adaequata actionem Mentis constituit.","refs":["CH_QGOH_PT_4:A5","CH_QGOH_PT_4:A11","CH_QGOH_PT_4:SD4"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[]},{"id":"CL_QGOH_04:GA-13","text":"[Terminal] Affectus activi ad Laetitiam et Cupiditatem pertinent; Tristitia activa excluditur, et actio rationalis in Animositatem atque Generositatem determinatur.","refs":["CH_QGOH_PT_4:A12","CH_QGOH_PT_4:D10","CH_QGOH_PT_4:A14"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[]},{"id":"CL_QGOH_04:GA-14","text":"[Attractor] Conservatio et augmentum potentiae agendi sunt attractor communis ad quem imaginatio, desiderium, cognitio et actio diversis viis diriguntur.","refs":["CH_QGOH_PT_2:A1","CH_QGOH_PT_3:A5"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[]}],"original_name":"Global Load-Bearing Axioms"},{"number":"3","name":"Recurrence, Mutation, and Displacement Matrix","items":[{"id":"CL_QGOH_04:RMD-01","text":"Conatus identitatem stabilem retinet: initio praesentias potentiam iuvantes quaerit, deinde ut essentia Mentis et Cupiditatis explicatur.","refs":["CH_QGOH_PT_2:A1","CH_QGOH_PT_4:A2"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[]},{"id":"CL_QGOH_04:RMD-02","text":"Laetitia et Tristitia redeunt functione mutata: primo mutationes potentiae mensurant, postea discrimen terminale constituunt quo sola Laetitia ad actionem referri potest.","refs":["CH_QGOH_PT_2:A2","CH_QGOH_PT_4:D10"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[]},{"id":"CL_QGOH_04:RMD-03","text":"Imaginatio migrat a praesentificatione temporali ad propagationem interpersonalem et denique ad reflexivam aestimationem sui per imagines affectuum aliorum.","refs":["CH_QGOH_PT_2:NX3","CH_QGOH_PT_3:AS1","CH_QGOH_PT_4:A9"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[]},{"id":"CL_QGOH_04:RMD-04","text":"Associatio per accidens amplificatur: ex causa locali Amoris vel Odii fit principium ominis, credulitatis asymmetricae et Superstitionis.","refs":["CH_QGOH_PT_2:A4","CH_QGOH_PT_3:D16"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[]},{"id":"CL_QGOH_04:RMD-05","text":"Similitudo mutatur ab imitatione affectus in conatum reciprocitatis et comparationis, sed differentia essentiarum eius universalitatem terminat.","refs":["CH_QGOH_PT_2:A10","CH_QGOH_PT_3:A6","CH_QGOH_PT_4:A7"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[]},{"id":"CL_QGOH_04:RMD-06","text":"Amor et Odium a relationibus dyadicis in structuram triadicam rivalitatis atque circuitus reciprocos migrant, deinde in generaliorem operationem attributionis causae absorbentur.","refs":["CH_QGOH_PT_2:A5","CH_QGOH_PT_3:SD2","CH_QGOH_PT_3:SD3"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[]},{"id":"CL_QGOH_04:RMD-07","text":"Approbatio imaginata primo actionem ad placendum et Gloriam producit; in clausura criterium sui a favore alieno ad contemplationem adaequatam propriae potentiae dislocatur.","refs":["CH_QGOH_PT_2:D10","CH_QGOH_PT_3:A8","CH_QGOH_PT_4:A11"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[]},{"id":"CL_QGOH_04:RMD-08","text":"Conflictus amplificatur a consensu affectivo impedito ad exclusivitatem, Zelotypiam et comparationem cum aequalibus potentioribus.","refs":["CH_QGOH_PT_2:D11","CH_QGOH_PT_3:D3","CH_QGOH_PT_4:D6"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[]},{"id":"CL_QGOH_04:RMD-09","text":"Identitas affectiva obiecti opere structurali peracto evanescit: corpus variabile et reconfigurabile efficit ut valor sit eventus relationis, non possessio rei.","refs":["CH_QGOH_PT_3:D17","CH_QGOH_PT_4:A13","CH_QGOH_PT_4:SD2"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[]}],"original_name":"Recurrence, Mutation, and Displacement Matrix"},{"number":"4","name":"Lower-Layer vs Current-Level Distinction","items":[{"id":"CL_QGOH_04:CGD-01","text":"Spes, Metus, Zelotypia, Admiratio et similes figurae manent compositiones locales; hoc gradu valent ut probationes grammaticae generativae, non ut principia globalia separata.","refs":["CH_QGOH_PT_2:D4","CH_QGOH_PT_3:NX1","CH_QGOH_PT_4:D3"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[]},{"id":"CL_QGOH_04:CGD-02","text":"Vindicta, Gratitudo et asymmetria earum sunt exitus locales reciprocitatis; sursum amplificatur forma circuitus qua responsio affectiva causam ulterioris responsionis fit.","refs":["CH_QGOH_PT_3:D9","CH_QGOH_PT_3:SD1"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[]},{"id":"CL_QGOH_04:CGD-03","text":"Omen et Superstitio manent ramus particularis associationis inter Spem et Metum; structura praesentis gradus servat mechanismum causalizationis accidentalis, non omnes formas sociales superstitionis.","refs":["CH_QGOH_PT_3:NX9","CH_QGOH_PT_3:D16"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[]},{"id":"CL_QGOH_04:CGD-04","text":"Memoria, imitatio et comparatio sursum amplificantur, quia affectum ex obiecto singulari in reticulum causale, interpersonalem et reflexivum distribuunt.","refs":["CH_QGOH_PT_2:SD1","CH_QGOH_PT_3:ME3","CH_QGOH_PT_4:SD3"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[]},{"id":"CL_QGOH_04:CGD-05","text":"Antagonismus non fit axioma universale: conflictus indiget affectibus oppositis, approbatione frustrata, comparatione coercitiva aut obiecto exclusive possidendo.","refs":["CH_QGOH_PT_2:MRT7","CH_QGOH_PT_2:A12"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[]},{"id":"CL_QGOH_04:CGD-06","text":"Transitus ab imaginatione inadequata ad ideam adaequatam est mutatio propria huius magnitudinis: taxonomias passionum concludit easque sub distinctione actionis et passionis reordinat.","refs":["CH_QGOH_PT_4:AS6","CH_QGOH_PT_4:MRT3"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[]}],"original_name":"Lower-Layer vs Current-Level Distinction"},{"number":"5","name":"Global Apophatic Structure","items":[{"id":"CL_QGOH_04:GNX-01","text":"Systema negat affectum ex proprietate intrinseca obiecti aut ex sola causa efficiente eius determinari.","refs":["CH_QGOH_PT_2:NX1","CH_QGOH_PT_4:AS2"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"Negative","passages":[]},{"id":"CL_QGOH_04:GNX-02","text":"Systema negat praesentiam actualem rei esse condicionem necessariam efficaciae affectivae, quia memoria et imaginatio absentiam praesentificant.","refs":["CH_QGOH_PT_2:NX3","CH_QGOH_PT_3:SD4"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"Negative","passages":[]},{"id":"CL_QGOH_04:GNX-03","text":"Systema negat subiectum affective sui iuris, immediate sibi transparens et a causis corporalibus vel socialibus immune.","refs":["CH_QGOH_PT_3:SNX1","CH_QGOH_PT_4:DNX1"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"Negative","passages":[]},{"id":"CL_QGOH_04:GNX-04","text":"Systema negat bonum, malum et iudicium morale tribunal obiectivum a desiderio et constitutione individui independens constituere.","refs":["CH_QGOH_PT_3:NX6","CH_QGOH_PT_3:SNX2"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"Negative","passages":[]},{"id":"CL_QGOH_04:GNX-05","text":"Systema negat consensum affectivum pacem spontaneam vel propagationem socialem responsionem uniformem necessario producere.","refs":["CH_QGOH_PT_2:SNX2","CH_QGOH_PT_3:SD6"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"Negative","passages":[]},{"id":"CL_QGOH_04:GNX-06","text":"Systema nec affectum purum, nec stabilitatem finalem, nec clausuram lexicalem specierum affectivarum admittit.","refs":["CH_QGOH_PT_4:SNX1","CH_QGOH_PT_4:SNX2","CH_QGOH_PT_4:SNX3"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"Negative","passages":[]}],"original_name":"Global Apophatic Structure"},{"number":"6","name":"Deep Book Apophatics","items":[{"id":"CL_QGOH_04:DBN-01","text":"Falsum esse oportet iudicium boni aut mali Cupiditatem causaliter antecedere; aliter tota reductio valoris ad affectum corrueret.","refs":["CH_QGOH_PT_3:A13","CH_QGOH_PT_4:SD2"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"Negative","passages":[]},{"id":"CL_QGOH_04:DBN-02","text":"Falsum esse oportet causam affective efficacem semper cum causa efficiente vere cognita coincidere; imago causae potest actionem dirigere quamvis eius veritas nondum constet.","refs":["CH_QGOH_PT_2:NX1","CH_QGOH_PT_3:MRT4"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"Negative","passages":[]},{"id":"CL_QGOH_04:DBN-03","text":"Falsum esse oportet relationem ad se ipsam immediatam esse, quia subiectum se per affectiones Corporis et affectus aliorum imaginatos cognoscit.","refs":["CH_QGOH_PT_2:SD5","CH_QGOH_PT_4:A4"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"Negative","passages":[]},{"id":"CL_QGOH_04:DBN-04","text":"Falsum esse oportet relationes sociales simpliciter dyadicas aut consensum ex se pacificum esse; tertii, rivales, collecta et obiecta exclusiva structuram medientur.","refs":["CH_QGOH_PT_3:DNX3","CH_QGOH_PT_2:DNX6"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"Negative","passages":[]},{"id":"CL_QGOH_04:DBN-05","text":"Falsum esse oportet universalitatem legum affectuum uniformitatem responsionum implicare, cum una grammatica per essentias et corpora diversa exitus diversos generet.","refs":["CH_QGOH_PT_3:SNX4","CH_QGOH_PT_4:A7"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"Negative","passages":[]},{"id":"CL_QGOH_04:DBN-06","text":"Falsum esse oportet Tristitiam principium actionis Mentis fieri posse, quia imminutio potentiae causaliter adversatur modo activo.","refs":["CH_QGOH_PT_4:NX9","CH_QGOH_PT_4:A12"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"Negative","passages":[]}],"original_name":"Deep Book Apophatics"},{"number":"7","name":"Super-Apophatics of the Whole","items":[{"id":"CL_QGOH_04:BSA-01","text":"Systema circa absentiam cuiuslibet loci extra causalitatem affectivam ordinatur: nullum subiectum purum affectus suos ab origine interna absoluta producit.","refs":["CH_QGOH_PT_3:SNX1","CH_QGOH_PT_4:SNX1"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"Negative","passages":[]},{"id":"CL_QGOH_04:BSA-02","text":"Indisponibile est tribunal transcendentale quod valorem rerum et rectitudinem affectuum ante motus potentiae definiat.","refs":["CH_QGOH_PT_3:SNX2","CH_QGOH_PT_4:DNX2"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"Negative","passages":[]},{"id":"CL_QGOH_04:BSA-03","text":"Impossibilis est identitas affectiva terminalis, quia corpora reconfigurantur, affectus transeunt et species eorum nullam enumerationem ultimam admittunt.","refs":["CH_QGOH_PT_4:SNX2","CH_QGOH_PT_4:SNX3"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"Negative","passages":[]},{"id":"CL_QGOH_04:BSA-04","text":"Libertas ut causa incausata ontologice inadmissibilis manet; actio possibilis est tantum ut augmentum intelligibilitatis causalis per ideam adaequatam.","refs":["CH_QGOH_PT_4:DNX1","CH_QGOH_PT_4:A11"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"Negative","passages":[]}],"original_name":"Super-Apophatics of the Whole"},{"number":"8","name":"Cross-Unit Derivative Propositions","items":[{"id":"CL_QGOH_04:XD-01","text":"Cum conjunctio imaginativa rem neutram causam per accidens faciat et imitatio affectum alienum in proprium traducat, causalitas socialis oriri potest sine causa efficienti obiecti cognita.","refs":["CL_QGOH_04:GA-03","CL_QGOH_04:GA-04","CL_QGOH_04:GA-07","CH_QGOH_PT_2:A4","CH_QGOH_PT_3:A2"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"From","passages":[]},{"id":"CL_QGOH_04:XD-02","text":"Attributio causae et imaginatio affectuum aliorum eandem operationem complent: affectus socialis dirigitur ad causam imaginatam, deinde ad aestimationem sui revertitur.","refs":["CL_QGOH_04:GA-08","CL_QGOH_04:GA-09","CH_QGOH_PT_3:SD3","CH_QGOH_PT_4:A9"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"From","passages":[]},{"id":"CL_QGOH_04:XD-03","text":"Affectus ad classem vel nationem universalizari potest, sed diversitas Corporum efficit ut collectivizatio sit distributio inaequalis, non identitas responsionum.","refs":["CL_QGOH_04:GA-06","CL_QGOH_04:GA-07","CL_QGOH_04:GA-09","CH_QGOH_PT_3:D13","CH_QGOH_PT_3:A16"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"From","passages":[]},{"id":"CL_QGOH_04:XD-04","text":"Possessio exclusiva, amor impeditus et comparatio cum aequali unam machinam rivalitatis efficiunt, in qua obiectum, rivalis et imago propriae potentiae mutuo se determinant.","refs":["CL_QGOH_04:GA-05","CL_QGOH_04:GA-08","CL_QGOH_04:GA-14","CH_QGOH_PT_2:A12","CH_QGOH_PT_3:D3","CH_QGOH_PT_4:D6"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"From","passages":[]},{"id":"CL_QGOH_04:XD-05","text":"Imaginatio temporalis eandem rem praesentificare potest, sed mutatio constitutionis Corporis prohibet ne praesentificatio eundem affectum restituat.","refs":["CL_QGOH_04:GA-03","CL_QGOH_04:GA-06","CH_QGOH_PT_2:A7","CH_QGOH_PT_4:A13"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"From","passages":[]},{"id":"CL_QGOH_04:XD-06","text":"Quia affectus affirmationem et negationem selectivas regit, Cupiditas valorem constituit et vocabula vulgaria mechanismum occultant, iudicium morale fit effectus epistemico-affectivus.","refs":["CL_QGOH_04:GA-10","CL_QGOH_04:GA-11","CH_QGOH_PT_2:SD2","CH_QGOH_PT_3:A13","CH_QGOH_PT_4:NX5"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"From","passages":[]},{"id":"CL_QGOH_04:XD-07","text":"Imaginata approbatio potentiam sui per Gloriam auget, sed idea adaequata criterium actionis a favore alieno ad causalitatem propriam transfert.","refs":["CL_QGOH_04:GA-08","CL_QGOH_04:GA-12","CL_QGOH_04:GA-13","CH_QGOH_PT_2:D10","CH_QGOH_PT_3:A8","CH_QGOH_PT_4:A11"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"From","passages":[]},{"id":"CL_QGOH_04:XD-08","text":"Amor passionem Odii dissolvere potest, sed Generositas non est simplex inversio Odii: ex alio modo causali, scilicet cognitione adaequata, procedit.","refs":["CL_QGOH_04:GA-05","CL_QGOH_04:GA-12","CL_QGOH_04:GA-13","CH_QGOH_PT_3:D11","CH_QGOH_PT_4:D10","CH_QGOH_PT_4:A12"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"From","passages":[]}],"original_name":"Cross-Unit Derivative Propositions"},{"number":"9","name":"Book-Scale Super-Derivatives","items":[{"id":"CL_QGOH_04:BSD-01","text":"Subiectum est processus nodalis qui se per affectiones Corporis, imagines causarum et responsiones aliorum constituit, non centrum sibi immediate datum.","refs":["CL_QGOH_04:GA-01","CL_QGOH_04:GA-03","CL_QGOH_04:GA-08","CH_QGOH_PT_2:SD5","CH_QGOH_PT_3:SNX1","CH_QGOH_PT_4:A4"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"From","passages":[]},{"id":"CL_QGOH_04:BSD-02","text":"Obiectum affectivum est eventus relationis inter corpus, memoriam, causam imaginatam et statum potentiae; mutata relatione, eius valor mutatur vel invertitur.","refs":["CL_QGOH_04:GA-04","CL_QGOH_04:GA-06","CL_QGOH_04:GA-10","CH_QGOH_PT_2:SD1","CH_QGOH_PT_4:A13","CH_QGOH_PT_4:SD2"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"From","passages":[]},{"id":"CL_QGOH_04:BSD-03","text":"Causalitas imaginata, quamvis a causalitate vera distinguenda sit, realem efficaciam habet quia intensionem, directionem et extensionem actionis affectivae ordinat.","refs":["CL_QGOH_04:GA-05","CL_QGOH_04:GA-09","CH_QGOH_PT_2:NX1","CH_QGOH_PT_3:SD3"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"From","passages":[]},{"id":"CL_QGOH_04:BSD-04","text":"Ordo socialis emergit ex propagatione et differentiatione affectuum: circuitus reciproci collecta constituunt, sed varietas Corporum eorum unitatem perpetuo frangit.","refs":["CL_QGOH_04:GA-06","CL_QGOH_04:GA-07","CL_QGOH_04:GA-08","CH_QGOH_PT_3:SD1","CH_QGOH_PT_3:SD6","CH_QGOH_PT_4:SD3"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"From","passages":[]},{"id":"CL_QGOH_04:BSD-05","text":"Tempus affectivum non est successio nuda: memoria praeteritum praesentificat, dubitatio futurum modulatur, et reconfiguratio corporis efficit ut repetitio temporalis mutationem status contineat.","refs":["CL_QGOH_04:GA-03","CL_QGOH_04:GA-06","CH_QGOH_PT_2:SD3","CH_QGOH_PT_3:SD4","CH_QGOH_PT_4:SD5"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"From","passages":[]},{"id":"CL_QGOH_04:BSD-06","text":"Interventio efficax non imperio voluntatis nudae, sed mutatione attributionis causae, condicionum imaginatarum et constitutionis corporalis operari debet.","refs":["CL_QGOH_04:GA-06","CL_QGOH_04:GA-09","CL_QGOH_04:GA-12","CH_QGOH_PT_3:AS6","CH_QGOH_PT_3:D15","CH_QGOH_PT_4:DNX1"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"From","passages":[]},{"id":"CL_QGOH_04:BSD-07","text":"Ethica fit transitus immanens a captura per causas inadequatas ad actionem ex cognitione adaequata, cuius forma socialis terminalis est Generositas potius quam oboedientia passioni contrariae.","refs":["CL_QGOH_04:GA-12","CL_QGOH_04:GA-13","CL_QGOH_04:GA-14","CH_QGOH_PT_4:SD4","CH_QGOH_PT_4:D10","CH_QGOH_PT_4:ME5"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"From","passages":[]}],"original_name":"Book-Scale Super-Derivatives"},{"number":"10","name":"Recursive Loops, Escalation Paths, and Conceptual Attractors","items":[{"id":"CL_QGOH_04:RLE-01","text":"Imago potentiam iuvans → Laetitia → Cupiditas conservandi imaginem vel obiectum → ulterior praesentificatio eiusdem auxilii.","refs":["CH_QGOH_PT_2:A1","CH_QGOH_PT_2:A6"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[]},{"id":"CL_QGOH_04:RLE-02","text":"Coaffectio → memoria conjunctiva → causalitas per accidens → Amor vel Odium → nova coaffectio obiecti cum affectu iam constituto.","refs":["CH_QGOH_PT_2:A3","CH_QGOH_PT_2:A4"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[]},{"id":"CL_QGOH_04:RLE-03","text":"Amor rei similis → conatus amoris reciproci → Amor imaginatus → Gloria → intensificatio conatus placendi et recipiendi.","refs":["CH_QGOH_PT_3:D2","CH_QGOH_PT_3:A8"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[]},{"id":"CL_QGOH_04:RLE-04","text":"Odium imaginatum → Odium reciprocum → malum illatum → Vindicta → confirmatio imaginis inimici et ulterior augmentatio Odii.","refs":["CH_QGOH_PT_3:D8","CH_QGOH_PT_3:D9","CH_QGOH_PT_3:D11"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[]},{"id":"CL_QGOH_04:RLE-05","text":"Approbatio imaginata → actio ad placendum → Gloria vel Acquiescentia → Ambitio conformandi alios → nova dependentia ab approbatione.","refs":["CH_QGOH_PT_2:D10","CH_QGOH_PT_4:SD3"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[]},{"id":"CL_QGOH_04:RLE-06","text":"Obiectum exclusivum → impedimentum appetitus → Zelotypia → Odium rivalis → conatus removendi impedimentum; hic conflictus attractorem triadicum format.","refs":["CH_QGOH_PT_2:A12","CH_QGOH_PT_3:D3"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[]},{"id":"CL_QGOH_04:RLE-07","text":"Imago universalis collecti affectum extendit, sed diversitas Corporum responsiones dividit; divergentia inde orta novas attributiones causae et novas collectivizationes excitat.","refs":["CH_QGOH_PT_3:D13","CH_QGOH_PT_3:SD6"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"Tension","passages":[]},{"id":"CL_QGOH_04:RLE-08","text":"Idea adaequata → contemplatio propriae potentiae → Laetitia et Cupiditas activae → Fortitudo → Animositas aut Generositas; cognitio causalis fit attractor clausurae activae.","refs":["CH_QGOH_PT_4:A11","CH_QGOH_PT_4:D10","CH_QGOH_PT_4:A14"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[]}],"original_name":"Recursive Loops, Escalation Paths, and Conceptual Attractors"},{"number":"11","name":"Formal Generative Schema of the Whole Book","items":[{"id":"CL_QGOH_04:FGS-B01","text":"Essentia individualis sive conatus + affectio determinans → Cupiditas ad agendum.","refs":["CH_QGOH_PT_4:A1"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[]},{"id":"CL_QGOH_04:FGS-B02","text":"Affectio Corporis + augmentum aut imminutio potentiae → Laetitia aut Tristitia.","refs":["CH_QGOH_PT_3:A1","CH_QGOH_PT_4:A3"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[]},{"id":"CL_QGOH_04:FGS-B03","text":"Coaffectio + memoria + similitudo + praesentificatio temporalis → translatio affectus et causa per accidens.","refs":["CH_QGOH_PT_2:A3","CH_QGOH_PT_2:A7"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[]},{"id":"CL_QGOH_04:FGS-B04","text":"Laetitia aut Tristitia + idea causae externae → Amor aut Odium → conservatio, promotio, remotio aut destructio.","refs":["CH_QGOH_PT_2:A5","CH_QGOH_PT_2:A6"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[]},{"id":"CL_QGOH_04:FGS-B05","text":"Imago affectus rei similis → imitatio → reciprocitas → circuitus Amoris, Odii, Gloriae, Vindictae et aestimationis sui.","refs":["CH_QGOH_PT_3:A2","CH_QGOH_PT_4:A9"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[]},{"id":"CL_QGOH_04:FGS-B06","text":"Exclusivitas + rivalis + comparatio + imago universalis → Zelotypia, Invidia, conflictus et collectivizatio affectuum.","refs":["CH_QGOH_PT_2:A12","CH_QGOH_PT_3:D13","CH_QGOH_PT_4:D6"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[]},{"id":"CL_QGOH_04:FGS-B07","text":"Diversitas et reconfiguratio Corporis → mutatio affectus erga idem obiectum → dissolutio valoris affectivi fixi.","refs":["CH_QGOH_PT_3:A16","CH_QGOH_PT_4:A13"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[]},{"id":"CL_QGOH_04:FGS-B08","text":"Idea inadequata → passio et captura causali-imaginativa; idea adaequata → actio → Laetitia et Cupiditas activae → Fortitudo.","refs":["CH_QGOH_PT_4:A5","CH_QGOH_PT_4:A11","CH_QGOH_PT_4:A14"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[]},{"id":"CL_QGOH_04:FGS-B09","text":"Iteratio huius schemae per obiecta et essentias diversas → species affectuum indefinitae; reductio ad primitivas causas → intelligibilitas sine catalogo clauso.","refs":["CH_QGOH_PT_4:SD1","CH_QGOH_PT_4:SNX3"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[]}],"original_name":"Formal Generative Schema of the Whole Book"},{"number":"12","name":"Whole-Book Argument Flow Map","items":[{"id":"CL_QGOH_04:AFM-B01","text":"Coaffectio corporalis → causalitas imaginativa → Amor et Odium → imitatio et reciprocitas → rivalitas et collectivizatio → variatio corporalis → dissolutio valorum fixorum → distinctio passionis et actionis → Fortitudo.","refs":["CH_QGOH_PT_2:AS4","CH_QGOH_PT_3:AS6","CH_QGOH_PT_4:AS6","CH_QGOH_PT_4:MRT8"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[]},{"id":"CL_QGOH_04:AFM-B02","text":"Effectus sociales → imago sui per alios → nova Laetitia vel Tristitia → nova Cupiditas → actio in alios → reconfiguratio Corporis et relationis; motus igitur spiraliter redit ad principium mutatum.","refs":["CH_QGOH_PT_3:SD1","CH_QGOH_PT_4:SD3","CH_QGOH_PT_4:A13"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[]}],"original_name":"Whole-Book Argument Flow Map"},{"number":"13","name":"Macro Map-Effects","items":[{"id":"CL_QGOH_04:MME-01","text":"Obiecta reclassificantur a portatoribus valoris intrinseci in nodos reticuli inter potentiam, memoriam, imaginationem et attributionem causae.","refs":["CH_QGOH_PT_2:ME1","CH_QGOH_PT_4:SD2"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[]},{"id":"CL_QGOH_04:MME-02","text":"Tempus reclassificatur a distantia chronologica in modum praesentificationis, dubitationis et reconfigurationis corporalis.","refs":["CH_QGOH_PT_2:ME3","CH_QGOH_PT_4:SD5"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[]},{"id":"CL_QGOH_04:MME-03","text":"Socialitas reclassificatur ab aggregatione individuorum in circulationem imaginum affectivarum, cuius propagatio simul connexionem et differentiam producit.","refs":["CH_QGOH_PT_3:ME3","CH_QGOH_PT_3:SD6"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[]},{"id":"CL_QGOH_04:MME-04","text":"Bonum, malum et iudicium morale transferuntur ex ordine proprietatum rerum in cartographiam desiderii et mutationum potentiae.","refs":["CH_QGOH_PT_4:ME1","CH_QGOH_PT_3:SD5"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[]},{"id":"CL_QGOH_04:MME-05","text":"Subiectum reclassificatur a causa libera et interiori in conatum corporaliter ac socialiter mediatum, qui tamen per ideam adaequatam activus fieri potest.","refs":["CH_QGOH_PT_2:SD5","CH_QGOH_PT_4:SD4","CH_QGOH_PT_4:ME5"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[]},{"id":"CL_QGOH_04:MME-06","text":"Methodus psychologica transit a catalogo nominum ad grammaticam causalium primitivorum, quae varietatem infinitam sine clausura lexicali tractat.","refs":["CH_QGOH_PT_4:ME4","CH_QGOH_PT_4:SNX3"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[]}],"original_name":"Macro Map-Effects"},{"number":"14","name":"Altitude Integration","items":[{"id":"CL_QGOH_04:ALT-B01","text":"In gradu superficiei definiuntur affectus singulares, relationes reciprocae, iudicia valoris, comparationes sociales atque figurae actionis rationalis.","refs":["CH_QGOH_PT_4:ALT1"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[]},{"id":"CL_QGOH_04:ALT-B02","text":"In gradu structurali conatus, transitus potentiae, affectiones Corporis, memoria, imitatio et causalitas imaginata unam machinam recurrentem componunt.","refs":["CH_QGOH_PT_4:ALT2","CH_QGOH_PT_2:SD1"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[]},{"id":"CL_QGOH_04:ALT-B03","text":"In gradu metastrategico certamen mutatur in pugnam pro attributione causarum, approbatione, directione imaginum et condicionibus quibus Cupiditas iuvatur aut coercetur.","refs":["CH_QGOH_PT_4:ALT3","CH_QGOH_PT_3:SD3"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[]},{"id":"CL_QGOH_04:ALT-B04","text":"In gradu ontologico et temporali individuum est potentia perseverans sed reconfigurabilis, res absentes affective adsunt, et tempus per transitiones potius quam per successionem nudam operatur.","refs":["CH_QGOH_PT_4:ALT4","CH_QGOH_PT_2:ALT4"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[]},{"id":"CL_QGOH_04:ALT-B05","text":"In gradu methodologico et performativo textus ipsa vocabula vulgaria dissolvendo et affectus recursive deducendo formam causalitatis quam asserit exercet.","refs":["CH_QGOH_PT_4:AS3","CH_QGOH_PT_2:AS3","CH_QGOH_PT_4:ME4"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[]}],"original_name":"Altitude Integration"},{"number":"15","name":"Internal Tensions, Mutation Points, and Fault Lines","items":[{"id":"CL_QGOH_04:FL-01","text":"Efficacia causae imaginatae clare demonstratur, sed criterium internum quo causa affective attributa a causa vere cognita discernatur incompletum manet.","refs":["CH_QGOH_PT_2:MRT5","CH_QGOH_PT_3:MRT4"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"Tension","passages":[]},{"id":"CL_QGOH_04:FL-02","text":"Extensio affectus ad classes vel nationes generalizationem requirit, dum diversitas Corporum uniformitatem illius generalizationis dissolvit.","refs":["CH_QGOH_PT_3:MRT5","CH_QGOH_PT_3:SD6"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"Tension","passages":[]},{"id":"CL_QGOH_04:FL-03","text":"Imago causae liberae affectum intendit, quamvis tota machina affectuum ex causis necessariis deducatur; vis imaginationis libertatis explicatur, non libertas metaphysica.","refs":["CH_QGOH_PT_3:MRT6","CH_QGOH_PT_3:D15"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"Tension","passages":[]},{"id":"CL_QGOH_04:FL-04","text":"Trias primitiva tractabilitatem promittit, sed varietas infinita obiectorum et essentiarum relinquit indeterminatum quantum definitiones generales de differentiis singularibus explicent.","refs":["CH_QGOH_PT_4:MRT4","CH_QGOH_PT_4:D12"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"Tension","passages":[]},{"id":"CL_QGOH_04:FL-05","text":"Transitus terminalis ad actionem ab principiis cognitionis adaequatae antecedentibus pendet, quae intra hunc gregem adhibentur sed non integre refondantur.","refs":["CH_QGOH_PT_4:MRT5","CH_QGOH_PT_4:AS6"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"Tension","passages":[]},{"id":"CL_QGOH_04:MP-01","text":"Res causaliter neutra, associatione affectiva adiuncta, in causam per accidens Amoris, Odii aut Cupiditatis mutatur.","refs":["CH_QGOH_PT_2:A4"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"Tension","passages":[]},{"id":"CL_QGOH_04:MP-02","text":"Similitudo quae affectum eodem signo propagat per Odium inverti potest, unde identitas relationis in contrarietatem mutatur.","refs":["CH_QGOH_PT_2:A10","CH_QGOH_PT_2:NX5"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"Tension","passages":[]},{"id":"CL_QGOH_04:MP-03","text":"Amor exclusivus, impedimento rivalis affectus, in Zelotypiam atque Odium maius quam affectus sine amore antecedente converti potest.","refs":["CH_QGOH_PT_3:D3"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"Tension","passages":[]},{"id":"CL_QGOH_04:MP-04","text":"Fruitio quae Cupiditatem implet Corpus ita reconfigurare potest ut obiectum antea appetitum in Fastidium vel Taedium vertatur.","refs":["CH_QGOH_PT_4:A13","CH_QGOH_PT_4:D11"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"Tension","passages":[]},{"id":"CL_QGOH_04:MP-05","text":"Mutatio ab idea inadequata ad ideam adaequatam statum affectus mutat: quod passio erat actio fit, non quia obiectum mutatur, sed quia modus causal-cognitivus mutatur.","refs":["CH_QGOH_PT_4:A5","CH_QGOH_PT_4:A11"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"Tension","passages":[]}],"original_name":"Internal Tensions, Mutation Points, and Fault Lines"},{"number":"16","name":"Hidden System Image","items":[{"id":"CL_QGOH_04:HSI-01","text":"Realitas implicite est reticulum corporum afficientium, imaginum praesentificantium et attributionum causalium, per quod potentia augetur, minuitur et redistribuitur.","refs":["CH_QGOH_PT_2:SD1","CH_QGOH_PT_3:A1"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[]},{"id":"CL_QGOH_04:HSI-02","text":"Subiectum intra hanc machinam est conatus nodalis qui se per alios et per affectiones Corporis cognoscit, sed cognitione adaequata modum activum acquirere potest.","refs":["CH_QGOH_PT_2:SD5","CH_QGOH_PT_4:A1","CH_QGOH_PT_4:A11"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[]},{"id":"CL_QGOH_04:HSI-03","text":"Tempus est campus praesentificationum et mutationum irreversibilium: praeteritum affective redit, sed corpus mutatum prohibet ne idem reditus eundem statum restituat.","refs":["CH_QGOH_PT_2:SD3","CH_QGOH_PT_3:SD4","CH_QGOH_PT_4:A13"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[]},{"id":"CL_QGOH_04:HSI-04","text":"Navigatio possibilis non est fuga a causalitate, sed transitus a captura imaginum causalium ad earum cognitionem adaequatam, cuius forma socialis est Generositas.","refs":["CH_QGOH_PT_3:SD3","CH_QGOH_PT_4:SD4","CH_QGOH_PT_4:D10"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[]}],"original_name":"Hidden System Image"},{"number":"17","name":"Minimal Whole-Book Reconstruction Test","items":[{"id":"CL_QGOH_04:MRT-B01","text":"Axiomata de conatu, transitu potentiae, imaginatione, associatione, imitatione, causalitate affectiva et cognitione adaequata sufficiunt ad machinam principalem huius gradus restituendam.","refs":["CH_QGOH_PT_2:MRT1","CH_QGOH_PT_3:MRT1","CH_QGOH_PT_4:MRT1"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[]},{"id":"CL_QGOH_04:MRT-B02","text":"Restitutio tamen pendet a principiis antecedentibus de imaginatione, parallelismo Mentis et Corporis, conatu et ideis adaequatis, quae hic assumuntur potius quam demonstrantur.","refs":["CH_QGOH_PT_2:MRT4","CH_QGOH_PT_3:MRT2","CH_QGOH_PT_4:MRT5"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[]},{"id":"CL_QGOH_04:MRT-B03","text":"Structura firmissima est ubi eadem regula mutationis potentiae et ideae causae affectus privatos, circuitus sociales et distinctionem actionis a passione ordinat.","refs":["CH_QGOH_PT_3:MRT3","CH_QGOH_PT_4:MRT2","CH_QGOH_PT_4:MRT3"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[]},{"id":"CL_QGOH_04:MRT-B04","text":"Minus determinata manent compositio affectuum contrariorum in actionem finalem, stabilitas socialis Superstitionis et mensura singularium differentiarum ex varietate infinita.","refs":["CH_QGOH_PT_2:MRT6","CH_QGOH_PT_3:MRT7","CH_QGOH_PT_4:MRT4"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[]},{"id":"CL_QGOH_04:MRT-B05","text":"Restitutio verum motum servat, quia a causalitate imaginativa et affectibus socialibus ad variationem corporalem, criticam valoris et mutationem terminalem a passionibus ad actionem progreditur.","refs":["CH_QGOH_PT_3:MRT8","CH_QGOH_PT_4:MRT8"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[]}],"original_name":"Minimal Whole-Book Reconstruction Test"}],"language_code":"la","language_name":"Latina","translations":[{"id":"CAREVAR_20261015T015552582558Z_31b196ae","language_code":"en","language_name":"English","title":"Chapters 10–12","source_sha256":"501b560ffa8f8abbba2cd33ff373532f7d667d3a1c0eac95f07d11cd877c4363","sha256":"878b1f8fee08084569b32f923ed66d2d4967598f904227e62a33c4b213b8e0da","rendition_type":"machine","review_status":"machine","model":"gpt-5.6-terra","created":"2026-10-15T01:55:52.586190+00:00","sections":[{"number":"1","name":"Book-Scale Argumental Spine","items":[{"id":"CL_QGOH_04:AS-01","text":"At this level it is asked by what causal grammar conatus, affections of the Body, and images of things produce private affects, social relations, and action.","refs":["CH_QGOH_PT_2:AS1","CH_QGOH_PT_4:AS1"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[]},{"id":"CL_QGOH_04:AS-02","text":"Cupiditas expresses the operative essence of the individual, Laetitia and Tristitia measure changes of power, and the value of an object arises from affective relation rather than from intrinsic property.","refs":["CH_QGOH_PT_4:A1","CH_QGOH_PT_4:A3","CH_QGOH_PT_2:SD1"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[]},{"id":"CL_QGOH_04:AS-03","text":"Genetic and diagnostic argumentation deduces composite affects from a few laws, while resolving common psychological and moral terms into causal mechanisms.","refs":["CH_QGOH_PT_2:AS3","CH_QGOH_PT_4:AS3","CH_QGOH_PT_4:SD1"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[]},{"id":"CL_QGOH_04:AS-04","text":"The movement proceeds from imaginative association and causality per accidens to imitation, reciprocity, rivalry, collectivization, and reflexive comparison.","refs":["CH_QGOH_PT_2:AS4","CH_QGOH_PT_3:AS3"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[]},{"id":"CL_QGOH_04:AS-05","text":"Social affect becomes a recursive circuit: the imagined affect of another modifies one's own power, generates a response, and in turn reorders the affective condition of the other.","refs":["CH_QGOH_PT_3:SD1","CH_QGOH_PT_4:SD3"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[]},{"id":"CL_QGOH_04:AS-06","text":"The diversity of the constitutions of Bodies and the alteration of the body through enjoyment prevent the same object from retaining stable value or affective effect.","refs":["CH_QGOH_PT_3:A16","CH_QGOH_PT_4:A13","CH_QGOH_PT_4:SD5"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[]},{"id":"CL_QGOH_04:AS-07","text":"The terminal point changes the criterion of affect: the inadequacy of imagination constitutes passion, an adequate idea produces action, active Laetitia, Animositas, and Generositas.","refs":["CH_QGOH_PT_4:AS6","CH_QGOH_PT_4:A11","CH_QGOH_PT_4:A14"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[]},{"id":"CL_QGOH_04:AS-08","text":"The whole movement is cumulative, recursive, and transformative of action; the closure is not a final catalogue of affects, but a causal grammar that renders the transition from passions to action tractable.","refs":["CH_QGOH_PT_4:SNX3","CH_QGOH_PT_4:MRT8","CH_QGOH_PT_4:D12"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[]}],"original_name":"Book-Scale Argumental Spine"},{"number":"2","name":"Global Load-Bearing Axioms","items":[{"id":"CL_QGOH_04:GA-01","text":"[Persistent] Cupiditas is conatus, or the essence of the individual, insofar as it is directed to acting by a determinate affection, whether it is conscious of this determination or not.","refs":["CH_QGOH_PT_4:A1","CH_QGOH_PT_4:A2"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[]},{"id":"CL_QGOH_04:GA-02","text":"[Persistent] Laetitia and Tristitia are not fixed states, but transitions to greater or lesser power of acting.","refs":["CH_QGOH_PT_2:A2","CH_QGOH_PT_4:A3"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[]},{"id":"CL_QGOH_04:GA-03","text":"[Persistent] An image is an affection of the Body that can render an absent, past, or future thing affectively present, so long as it affirms its existence.","refs":["CH_QGOH_PT_3:A1","CH_QGOH_PT_2:A7"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[]},{"id":"CL_QGOH_04:GA-04","text":"[Persistent] Co-affection, memory, similarity, and association transfer affect among objects, such that a causally neutral thing can become an affective cause per accidens.","refs":["CH_QGOH_PT_2:A3","CH_QGOH_PT_2:A4"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[]},{"id":"CL_QGOH_04:GA-05","text":"[Persistent] Laetitia or Tristitia together with the idea of an external cause constitutes Amor or Odium, from which follows the conatus to preserve, promote, remove, or destroy.","refs":["CH_QGOH_PT_2:A5","CH_QGOH_PT_2:A6"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[]},{"id":"CL_QGOH_04:GA-06","text":"[Mutating] The same object produces contrary or diverse affects according to the composition of the Body, time, memory, and constitution altered through enjoyment.","refs":["CH_QGOH_PT_2:A8","CH_QGOH_PT_3:A16","CH_QGOH_PT_4:A13"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[]},{"id":"CL_QGOH_04:GA-07","text":"[Mutating] Similarity transfers another's affect to the observer, but Odium, difference of essences, and alteration of relation can reverse the sign or intensity of the transfer.","refs":["CH_QGOH_PT_2:A10","CH_QGOH_PT_3:A2","CH_QGOH_PT_4:A7"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[]},{"id":"CL_QGOH_04:GA-08","text":"[Emergent] Interpersonal affects are reciprocal circuits in which Amor, Odium, Gloria, Vindicta, and self-estimation are reproduced through the imagined affects of others.","refs":["CH_QGOH_PT_3:SD1","CH_QGOH_PT_4:A9"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[]},{"id":"CL_QGOH_04:GA-09","text":"[Emergent] Attribution of cause is an operator of affective direction: an individuated cause intensifies affect, a distributed one dilutes it, a universalized one collectivizes it.","refs":["CH_QGOH_PT_3:SD3","CH_QGOH_PT_3:A11"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[]},{"id":"CL_QGOH_04:GA-10","text":"[Emergent] Good and evil do not precede affect and Cupiditas, but are names by which things are assessed according to desire, aversion, and change of power.","refs":["CH_QGOH_PT_3:A13","CH_QGOH_PT_4:SD2"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[]},{"id":"CL_QGOH_04:GA-11","text":"[Emergent] The triad of Cupiditas, Laetitia, and Tristitia generates innumerable species of affects, although no lexical taxonomy provides their final closure.","refs":["CH_QGOH_PT_4:SD1","CH_QGOH_PT_4:SNX3"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[]},{"id":"CL_QGOH_04:GA-12","text":"[Terminal] Passion and action are distinguished not by external form but by mode of cognition: an inadequate idea constitutes passion, an adequate idea action of the Mind.","refs":["CH_QGOH_PT_4:A5","CH_QGOH_PT_4:A11","CH_QGOH_PT_4:SD4"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[]},{"id":"CL_QGOH_04:GA-13","text":"[Terminal] Active affects pertain to Laetitia and Cupiditas; active Tristitia is excluded, and rational action is determined in Animositas and Generositas.","refs":["CH_QGOH_PT_4:A12","CH_QGOH_PT_4:D10","CH_QGOH_PT_4:A14"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[]},{"id":"CL_QGOH_04:GA-14","text":"[Attractor] Preservation and increase of the power of acting are the common attractor toward which imagination, desire, cognition, and action are directed by diverse paths.","refs":["CH_QGOH_PT_2:A1","CH_QGOH_PT_3:A5"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[]}],"original_name":"Global Load-Bearing Axioms"},{"number":"3","name":"Recurrence, Mutation, and Displacement Matrix","items":[{"id":"CL_QGOH_04:RMD-01","text":"Conatus retains a stable identity: at first it seeks presences aiding power, then it is explained as the essence of the Mind and of Cupiditas.","refs":["CH_QGOH_PT_2:A1","CH_QGOH_PT_4:A2"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[]},{"id":"CL_QGOH_04:RMD-02","text":"Laetitia and Tristitia return with an altered function: first they measure changes of power, later they constitute the terminal distinction by which Laetitia alone can be referred to action.","refs":["CH_QGOH_PT_2:A2","CH_QGOH_PT_4:D10"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[]},{"id":"CL_QGOH_04:RMD-03","text":"Imagination migrates from temporal presentification to interpersonal propagation and finally to reflexive self-estimation through images of the affects of others.","refs":["CH_QGOH_PT_2:NX3","CH_QGOH_PT_3:AS1","CH_QGOH_PT_4:A9"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[]},{"id":"CL_QGOH_04:RMD-04","text":"Association per accidens is amplified: from a local cause of Amor or Odium it becomes the principle of omen, asymmetric credulity, and Superstitio.","refs":["CH_QGOH_PT_2:A4","CH_QGOH_PT_3:D16"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[]},{"id":"CL_QGOH_04:RMD-05","text":"Similarity changes from imitation of affect into a conatus of reciprocity and comparison, but the difference of essences limits its universality.","refs":["CH_QGOH_PT_2:A10","CH_QGOH_PT_3:A6","CH_QGOH_PT_4:A7"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[]},{"id":"CL_QGOH_04:RMD-06","text":"Amor and Odium migrate from dyadic relations into the triadic structure of rivalry and reciprocal circuits, then are absorbed into the more general operation of attribution of cause.","refs":["CH_QGOH_PT_2:A5","CH_QGOH_PT_3:SD2","CH_QGOH_PT_3:SD3"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[]},{"id":"CL_QGOH_04:RMD-07","text":"Imagined approval first produces action aimed at pleasing and Gloria; in the closure, the criterion of self is displaced from others' favor to adequate contemplation of one's own power.","refs":["CH_QGOH_PT_2:D10","CH_QGOH_PT_3:A8","CH_QGOH_PT_4:A11"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[]},{"id":"CL_QGOH_04:RMD-08","text":"Conflict is amplified from obstructed affective agreement to exclusivity, Zelotypia, and comparison with more powerful equals.","refs":["CH_QGOH_PT_2:D11","CH_QGOH_PT_3:D3","CH_QGOH_PT_4:D6"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[]},{"id":"CL_QGOH_04:RMD-09","text":"The affective identity of the object vanishes once its structural work is completed: the variable and reconfigurable body makes value an event of relation, not a possession of the thing.","refs":["CH_QGOH_PT_3:D17","CH_QGOH_PT_4:A13","CH_QGOH_PT_4:SD2"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[]}],"original_name":"Recurrence, Mutation, and Displacement Matrix"},{"number":"4","name":"Lower-Layer vs Current-Level Distinction","items":[{"id":"CL_QGOH_04:CGD-01","text":"Spes, Metus, Zelotypia, Admiratio, and similar figures remain local compositions; at this level they serve as tests of the generative grammar, not as separate global principles.","refs":["CH_QGOH_PT_2:D4","CH_QGOH_PT_3:NX1","CH_QGOH_PT_4:D3"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[]},{"id":"CL_QGOH_04:CGD-02","text":"Vindicta, Gratitudo, and their asymmetry are local outcomes of reciprocity; upward, the form of circuit is amplified, whereby an affective response becomes the cause of a further response.","refs":["CH_QGOH_PT_3:D9","CH_QGOH_PT_3:SD1"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[]},{"id":"CL_QGOH_04:CGD-03","text":"Omen and Superstitio remain a particular branch of association between Spes and Metus; the structure of the present level retains the mechanism of accidental causalization, not all social forms of superstition.","refs":["CH_QGOH_PT_3:NX9","CH_QGOH_PT_3:D16"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[]},{"id":"CL_QGOH_04:CGD-04","text":"Memory, imitation, and comparison are amplified upward because they distribute affect from a singular object into a causal, interpersonal, and reflexive network.","refs":["CH_QGOH_PT_2:SD1","CH_QGOH_PT_3:ME3","CH_QGOH_PT_4:SD3"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[]},{"id":"CL_QGOH_04:CGD-05","text":"Antagonism does not become a universal axiom: conflict requires opposed affects, frustrated approval, coercive comparison, or an exclusively possessed object.","refs":["CH_QGOH_PT_2:MRT7","CH_QGOH_PT_2:A12"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[]},{"id":"CL_QGOH_04:CGD-06","text":"The transition from inadequate imagination to an adequate idea is a change proper to this magnitude: it concludes taxonomies of passions and reorders them under the distinction of action and passion.","refs":["CH_QGOH_PT_4:AS6","CH_QGOH_PT_4:MRT3"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[]}],"original_name":"Lower-Layer vs Current-Level Distinction"},{"number":"5","name":"Global Apophatic Structure","items":[{"id":"CL_QGOH_04:GNX-01","text":"The system denies that affect is determined by an intrinsic property of the object or by its efficient cause alone.","refs":["CH_QGOH_PT_2:NX1","CH_QGOH_PT_4:AS2"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"Negative","passages":[]},{"id":"CL_QGOH_04:GNX-02","text":"The system denies that the actual presence of a thing is a necessary condition of affective efficacy, because memory and imagination presentify absence.","refs":["CH_QGOH_PT_2:NX3","CH_QGOH_PT_3:SD4"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"Negative","passages":[]},{"id":"CL_QGOH_04:GNX-03","text":"The system denies an affectively self-governing subject, immediately transparent to itself and immune from bodily or social causes.","refs":["CH_QGOH_PT_3:SNX1","CH_QGOH_PT_4:DNX1"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"Negative","passages":[]},{"id":"CL_QGOH_04:GNX-04","text":"The system denies that good, evil, and moral judgment constitute an objective tribunal independent of the desire and constitution of the individual.","refs":["CH_QGOH_PT_3:NX6","CH_QGOH_PT_3:SNX2"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"Negative","passages":[]},{"id":"CL_QGOH_04:GNX-05","text":"The system denies that affective agreement necessarily produces spontaneous peace or that social propagation produces a uniform response.","refs":["CH_QGOH_PT_2:SNX2","CH_QGOH_PT_3:SD6"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"Negative","passages":[]},{"id":"CL_QGOH_04:GNX-06","text":"The system admits neither pure affect, nor final stability, nor lexical closure of affective species.","refs":["CH_QGOH_PT_4:SNX1","CH_QGOH_PT_4:SNX2","CH_QGOH_PT_4:SNX3"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"Negative","passages":[]}],"original_name":"Global Apophatic Structure"},{"number":"6","name":"Deep Book Apophatics","items":[{"id":"CL_QGOH_04:DBN-01","text":"It must be false that a judgment of good or evil causally precedes Cupiditas; otherwise the entire reduction of value to affect would collapse.","refs":["CH_QGOH_PT_3:A13","CH_QGOH_PT_4:SD2"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"Negative","passages":[]},{"id":"CL_QGOH_04:DBN-02","text":"It must be false that an affectively efficacious cause always coincides with a truly known efficient cause; the image of a cause can direct action although its truth has not yet been established.","refs":["CH_QGOH_PT_2:NX1","CH_QGOH_PT_3:MRT4"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"Negative","passages":[]},{"id":"CL_QGOH_04:DBN-03","text":"It must be false that the relation to oneself is immediate, because the subject knows itself through affections of the Body and imagined affects of others.","refs":["CH_QGOH_PT_2:SD5","CH_QGOH_PT_4:A4"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"Negative","passages":[]},{"id":"CL_QGOH_04:DBN-04","text":"It must be false that social relations are simply dyadic or that agreement is peaceful in itself; third parties, rivals, collectives, and exclusive objects mediate the structure.","refs":["CH_QGOH_PT_3:DNX3","CH_QGOH_PT_2:DNX6"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"Negative","passages":[]},{"id":"CL_QGOH_04:DBN-05","text":"It must be false that the universality of the laws of affects implies uniformity of responses, since one grammar generates diverse outcomes through diverse essences and bodies.","refs":["CH_QGOH_PT_3:SNX4","CH_QGOH_PT_4:A7"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"Negative","passages":[]},{"id":"CL_QGOH_04:DBN-06","text":"It must be false that Tristitia can become a principle of action of the Mind, because diminution of power causally opposes the active mode.","refs":["CH_QGOH_PT_4:NX9","CH_QGOH_PT_4:A12"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"Negative","passages":[]}],"original_name":"Deep Book Apophatics"},{"number":"7","name":"Super-Apophatics of the Whole","items":[{"id":"CL_QGOH_04:BSA-01","text":"The system is ordered around the absence of any place outside affective causality: no pure subject produces its affects from an absolute internal origin.","refs":["CH_QGOH_PT_3:SNX1","CH_QGOH_PT_4:SNX1"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"Negative","passages":[]},{"id":"CL_QGOH_04:BSA-02","text":"There is no available transcendental tribunal that defines the value of things and the rectitude of affects before movements of power.","refs":["CH_QGOH_PT_3:SNX2","CH_QGOH_PT_4:DNX2"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"Negative","passages":[]},{"id":"CL_QGOH_04:BSA-03","text":"A terminal affective identity is impossible, because bodies are reconfigured, affects pass, and their species admit no final enumeration.","refs":["CH_QGOH_PT_4:SNX2","CH_QGOH_PT_4:SNX3"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"Negative","passages":[]},{"id":"CL_QGOH_04:BSA-04","text":"Freedom as an uncaused cause remains ontologically inadmissible; action is possible only as an increase in causal intelligibility through an adequate idea.","refs":["CH_QGOH_PT_4:DNX1","CH_QGOH_PT_4:A11"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"Negative","passages":[]}],"original_name":"Super-Apophatics of the Whole"},{"number":"8","name":"Cross-Unit Derivative Propositions","items":[{"id":"CL_QGOH_04:XD-01","text":"Since imaginative conjunction makes a neutral thing a cause per accidens and imitation translates another's affect into one's own, social causality can arise without the object's efficient cause being known.","refs":["CL_QGOH_04:GA-03","CL_QGOH_04:GA-04","CL_QGOH_04:GA-07","CH_QGOH_PT_2:A4","CH_QGOH_PT_3:A2"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"From","passages":[]},{"id":"CL_QGOH_04:XD-02","text":"Attribution of cause and imagination of the affects of others complete the same operation: social affect is directed toward an imagined cause, then returns to self-estimation.","refs":["CL_QGOH_04:GA-08","CL_QGOH_04:GA-09","CH_QGOH_PT_3:SD3","CH_QGOH_PT_4:A9"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"From","passages":[]},{"id":"CL_QGOH_04:XD-03","text":"Affect can be universalized to a class or nation, but the diversity of Bodies makes collectivization an unequal distribution, not an identity of responses.","refs":["CL_QGOH_04:GA-06","CL_QGOH_04:GA-07","CL_QGOH_04:GA-09","CH_QGOH_PT_3:D13","CH_QGOH_PT_3:A16"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"From","passages":[]},{"id":"CL_QGOH_04:XD-04","text":"Exclusive possession, obstructed love, and comparison with an equal produce a single machine of rivalry, in which object, rival, and image of one's own power mutually determine one another.","refs":["CL_QGOH_04:GA-05","CL_QGOH_04:GA-08","CL_QGOH_04:GA-14","CH_QGOH_PT_2:A12","CH_QGOH_PT_3:D3","CH_QGOH_PT_4:D6"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"From","passages":[]},{"id":"CL_QGOH_04:XD-05","text":"Temporal imagination can presentify the same thing, but alteration of the constitution of the Body prevents the presentification from restoring the same affect.","refs":["CL_QGOH_04:GA-03","CL_QGOH_04:GA-06","CH_QGOH_PT_2:A7","CH_QGOH_PT_4:A13"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"From","passages":[]},{"id":"CL_QGOH_04:XD-06","text":"Because affect governs selective affirmation and negation, Cupiditas constitutes value and common terms conceal the mechanism, moral judgment becomes an epistemic-affective effect.","refs":["CL_QGOH_04:GA-10","CL_QGOH_04:GA-11","CH_QGOH_PT_2:SD2","CH_QGOH_PT_3:A13","CH_QGOH_PT_4:NX5"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"From","passages":[]},{"id":"CL_QGOH_04:XD-07","text":"Imagined approval increases self-power through Gloria, but an adequate idea transfers the criterion of action from others' favor to one's own causality.","refs":["CL_QGOH_04:GA-08","CL_QGOH_04:GA-12","CL_QGOH_04:GA-13","CH_QGOH_PT_2:D10","CH_QGOH_PT_3:A8","CH_QGOH_PT_4:A11"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"From","passages":[]},{"id":"CL_QGOH_04:XD-08","text":"Amor can dissolve the passion of Odium, but Generositas is not a simple inversion of Odium: it proceeds from another causal mode, namely adequate cognition.","refs":["CL_QGOH_04:GA-05","CL_QGOH_04:GA-12","CL_QGOH_04:GA-13","CH_QGOH_PT_3:D11","CH_QGOH_PT_4:D10","CH_QGOH_PT_4:A12"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"From","passages":[]}],"original_name":"Cross-Unit Derivative Propositions"},{"number":"9","name":"Book-Scale Super-Derivatives","items":[{"id":"CL_QGOH_04:BSD-01","text":"The subject is a nodal process that constitutes itself through affections of the Body, images of causes, and responses of others, not a center immediately given to itself.","refs":["CL_QGOH_04:GA-01","CL_QGOH_04:GA-03","CL_QGOH_04:GA-08","CH_QGOH_PT_2:SD5","CH_QGOH_PT_3:SNX1","CH_QGOH_PT_4:A4"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"From","passages":[]},{"id":"CL_QGOH_04:BSD-02","text":"The affective object is an event of relation among body, memory, imagined cause, and state of power; when the relation changes, its value changes or is inverted.","refs":["CL_QGOH_04:GA-04","CL_QGOH_04:GA-06","CL_QGOH_04:GA-10","CH_QGOH_PT_2:SD1","CH_QGOH_PT_4:A13","CH_QGOH_PT_4:SD2"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"From","passages":[]},{"id":"CL_QGOH_04:BSD-03","text":"Imagined causality, although it must be distinguished from true causality, has real efficacy because it orders the intensity, direction, and extension of affective action.","refs":["CL_QGOH_04:GA-05","CL_QGOH_04:GA-09","CH_QGOH_PT_2:NX1","CH_QGOH_PT_3:SD3"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"From","passages":[]},{"id":"CL_QGOH_04:BSD-04","text":"Social order emerges from the propagation and differentiation of affects: reciprocal circuits constitute collectives, but the variety of Bodies perpetually breaks their unity.","refs":["CL_QGOH_04:GA-06","CL_QGOH_04:GA-07","CL_QGOH_04:GA-08","CH_QGOH_PT_3:SD1","CH_QGOH_PT_3:SD6","CH_QGOH_PT_4:SD3"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"From","passages":[]},{"id":"CL_QGOH_04:BSD-05","text":"Affective time is not bare succession: memory presentifies the past, doubt modulates the future, and bodily reconfiguration makes temporal repetition contain a change of state.","refs":["CL_QGOH_04:GA-03","CL_QGOH_04:GA-06","CH_QGOH_PT_2:SD3","CH_QGOH_PT_3:SD4","CH_QGOH_PT_4:SD5"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"From","passages":[]},{"id":"CL_QGOH_04:BSD-06","text":"Effective intervention must operate not by the command of bare will, but by changing attribution of cause, imagined conditions, and bodily constitution.","refs":["CL_QGOH_04:GA-06","CL_QGOH_04:GA-09","CL_QGOH_04:GA-12","CH_QGOH_PT_3:AS6","CH_QGOH_PT_3:D15","CH_QGOH_PT_4:DNX1"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"From","passages":[]},{"id":"CL_QGOH_04:BSD-07","text":"Ethics becomes the immanent transition from capture by inadequate causes to action from adequate cognition, whose terminal social form is Generositas rather than obedience to a contrary passion.","refs":["CL_QGOH_04:GA-12","CL_QGOH_04:GA-13","CL_QGOH_04:GA-14","CH_QGOH_PT_4:SD4","CH_QGOH_PT_4:D10","CH_QGOH_PT_4:ME5"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"From","passages":[]}],"original_name":"Book-Scale Super-Derivatives"},{"number":"10","name":"Recursive Loops, Escalation Paths, and Conceptual Attractors","items":[{"id":"CL_QGOH_04:RLE-01","text":"Image aiding power → Laetitia → Cupiditas to preserve the image or object → further presentification of the same aid.","refs":["CH_QGOH_PT_2:A1","CH_QGOH_PT_2:A6"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[]},{"id":"CL_QGOH_04:RLE-02","text":"Co-affection → conjunctive memory → causality per accidens → Amor or Odium → new co-affection of the object with an already constituted affect.","refs":["CH_QGOH_PT_2:A3","CH_QGOH_PT_2:A4"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[]},{"id":"CL_QGOH_04:RLE-03","text":"Amor of a similar thing → conatus for reciprocal love → imagined Amor → Gloria → intensification of the conatus to please and to be received.","refs":["CH_QGOH_PT_3:D2","CH_QGOH_PT_3:A8"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[]},{"id":"CL_QGOH_04:RLE-04","text":"Imagined Odium → reciprocal Odium → harm inflicted → Vindicta → confirmation of the image of the enemy and further increase of Odium.","refs":["CH_QGOH_PT_3:D8","CH_QGOH_PT_3:D9","CH_QGOH_PT_3:D11"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[]},{"id":"CL_QGOH_04:RLE-05","text":"Imagined approval → action aimed at pleasing → Gloria or Acquiescentia → Ambitio to conform others → new dependence on approval.","refs":["CH_QGOH_PT_2:D10","CH_QGOH_PT_4:SD3"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[]},{"id":"CL_QGOH_04:RLE-06","text":"Exclusive object → obstruction of appetite → Zelotypia → Odium of the rival → conatus to remove the obstacle; this conflict forms a triadic attractor.","refs":["CH_QGOH_PT_2:A12","CH_QGOH_PT_3:D3"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[]},{"id":"CL_QGOH_04:RLE-07","text":"A universal image of the collective extends affect, but the diversity of Bodies divides responses; the divergence arising therefrom prompts new attributions of cause and new collectivizations.","refs":["CH_QGOH_PT_3:D13","CH_QGOH_PT_3:SD6"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"Tension","passages":[]},{"id":"CL_QGOH_04:RLE-08","text":"Adequate idea → contemplation of one's own power → active Laetitia and Cupiditas → Fortitudo → Animositas or Generositas; causal cognition becomes the attractor of active closure.","refs":["CH_QGOH_PT_4:A11","CH_QGOH_PT_4:D10","CH_QGOH_PT_4:A14"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[]}],"original_name":"Recursive Loops, Escalation Paths, and Conceptual Attractors"},{"number":"11","name":"Formal Generative Schema of the Whole Book","items":[{"id":"CL_QGOH_04:FGS-B01","text":"Individual essence or conatus + determining affection → Cupiditas to act.","refs":["CH_QGOH_PT_4:A1"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[]},{"id":"CL_QGOH_04:FGS-B02","text":"Affection of the Body + increase or diminution of power → Laetitia or Tristitia.","refs":["CH_QGOH_PT_3:A1","CH_QGOH_PT_4:A3"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[]},{"id":"CL_QGOH_04:FGS-B03","text":"Co-affection + memory + similarity + temporal presentification → transfer of affect and cause per accidens.","refs":["CH_QGOH_PT_2:A3","CH_QGOH_PT_2:A7"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[]},{"id":"CL_QGOH_04:FGS-B04","text":"Laetitia or Tristitia + idea of an external cause → Amor or Odium → preservation, promotion, removal, or destruction.","refs":["CH_QGOH_PT_2:A5","CH_QGOH_PT_2:A6"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[]},{"id":"CL_QGOH_04:FGS-B05","text":"Image of the affect of a similar thing → imitation → reciprocity → circuits of Amor, Odium, Gloria, Vindicta, and self-estimation.","refs":["CH_QGOH_PT_3:A2","CH_QGOH_PT_4:A9"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[]},{"id":"CL_QGOH_04:FGS-B06","text":"Exclusivity + rival + comparison + universal image → Zelotypia, Invidia, conflict, and collectivization of affects.","refs":["CH_QGOH_PT_2:A12","CH_QGOH_PT_3:D13","CH_QGOH_PT_4:D6"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[]},{"id":"CL_QGOH_04:FGS-B07","text":"Diversity and reconfiguration of the Body → alteration of affect toward the same object → dissolution of fixed affective value.","refs":["CH_QGOH_PT_3:A16","CH_QGOH_PT_4:A13"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[]},{"id":"CL_QGOH_04:FGS-B08","text":"Inadequate idea → passion and causal-imaginative capture; adequate idea → action → active Laetitia and Cupiditas → Fortitudo.","refs":["CH_QGOH_PT_4:A5","CH_QGOH_PT_4:A11","CH_QGOH_PT_4:A14"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[]},{"id":"CL_QGOH_04:FGS-B09","text":"Iteration of this schema through diverse objects and essences → indefinite species of affects; reduction to primitive causes → intelligibility without a closed catalogue.","refs":["CH_QGOH_PT_4:SD1","CH_QGOH_PT_4:SNX3"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[]}],"original_name":"Formal Generative Schema of the Whole Book"},{"number":"12","name":"Whole-Book Argument Flow Map","items":[{"id":"CL_QGOH_04:AFM-B01","text":"Bodily co-affection → imaginative causality → Amor and Odium → imitation and reciprocity → rivalry and collectivization → bodily variation → dissolution of fixed values → distinction of passion and action → Fortitudo.","refs":["CH_QGOH_PT_2:AS4","CH_QGOH_PT_3:AS6","CH_QGOH_PT_4:AS6","CH_QGOH_PT_4:MRT8"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[]},{"id":"CL_QGOH_04:AFM-B02","text":"Social effects → image of self through others → new Laetitia or Tristitia → new Cupiditas → action upon others → reconfiguration of Body and relation; the movement therefore returns spirally to a changed beginning.","refs":["CH_QGOH_PT_3:SD1","CH_QGOH_PT_4:SD3","CH_QGOH_PT_4:A13"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[]}],"original_name":"Whole-Book Argument Flow Map"},{"number":"13","name":"Macro Map-Effects","items":[{"id":"CL_QGOH_04:MME-01","text":"Objects are reclassified from bearers of intrinsic value into nodes of a network among power, memory, imagination, and attribution of cause.","refs":["CH_QGOH_PT_2:ME1","CH_QGOH_PT_4:SD2"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[]},{"id":"CL_QGOH_04:MME-02","text":"Time is reclassified from chronological distance into a mode of presentification, doubt, and bodily reconfiguration.","refs":["CH_QGOH_PT_2:ME3","CH_QGOH_PT_4:SD5"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[]},{"id":"CL_QGOH_04:MME-03","text":"Sociality is reclassified from an aggregation of individuals into a circulation of affective images, whose propagation produces connection and difference simultaneously.","refs":["CH_QGOH_PT_3:ME3","CH_QGOH_PT_3:SD6"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[]},{"id":"CL_QGOH_04:MME-04","text":"Good, evil, and moral judgment are transferred from the order of properties of things into a cartography of desire and changes of power.","refs":["CH_QGOH_PT_4:ME1","CH_QGOH_PT_3:SD5"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[]},{"id":"CL_QGOH_04:MME-05","text":"The subject is reclassified from a free and interior cause into a bodily and socially mediated conatus, which nevertheless can become active through an adequate idea.","refs":["CH_QGOH_PT_2:SD5","CH_QGOH_PT_4:SD4","CH_QGOH_PT_4:ME5"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[]},{"id":"CL_QGOH_04:MME-06","text":"Psychological method passes from a catalogue of names to a grammar of causal primitives, which treats infinite variety without lexical closure.","refs":["CH_QGOH_PT_4:ME4","CH_QGOH_PT_4:SNX3"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[]}],"original_name":"Macro Map-Effects"},{"number":"14","name":"Altitude Integration","items":[{"id":"CL_QGOH_04:ALT-B01","text":"At the level of the surface, singular affects, reciprocal relations, judgments of value, social comparisons, and figures of rational action are defined.","refs":["CH_QGOH_PT_4:ALT1"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[]},{"id":"CL_QGOH_04:ALT-B02","text":"At the structural level, conatus, transitions of power, affections of the Body, memory, imitation, and imagined causality compose one recurrent machine.","refs":["CH_QGOH_PT_4:ALT2","CH_QGOH_PT_2:SD1"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[]},{"id":"CL_QGOH_04:ALT-B03","text":"At the metastrategic level, contest is transformed into a struggle over attribution of causes, approval, direction of images, and the conditions by which Cupiditas is aided or constrained.","refs":["CH_QGOH_PT_4:ALT3","CH_QGOH_PT_3:SD3"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[]},{"id":"CL_QGOH_04:ALT-B04","text":"At the ontological and temporal level, the individual is a persevering but reconfigurable power, absent things are affectively present, and time operates through transitions rather than bare succession.","refs":["CH_QGOH_PT_4:ALT4","CH_QGOH_PT_2:ALT4"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[]},{"id":"CL_QGOH_04:ALT-B05","text":"At the methodological and performative level, the text itself exercises the form of causality it asserts by dissolving common terms and recursively deducing affects.","refs":["CH_QGOH_PT_4:AS3","CH_QGOH_PT_2:AS3","CH_QGOH_PT_4:ME4"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[]}],"original_name":"Altitude Integration"},{"number":"15","name":"Internal Tensions, Mutation Points, and Fault Lines","items":[{"id":"CL_QGOH_04:FL-01","text":"The efficacy of an imagined cause is clearly demonstrated, but the internal criterion by which an affectively attributed cause is distinguished from a truly known cause remains incomplete.","refs":["CH_QGOH_PT_2:MRT5","CH_QGOH_PT_3:MRT4"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"Tension","passages":[]},{"id":"CL_QGOH_04:FL-02","text":"The extension of affect to classes or nations requires generalization, while the diversity of Bodies dissolves the uniformity of that generalization.","refs":["CH_QGOH_PT_3:MRT5","CH_QGOH_PT_3:SD6"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"Tension","passages":[]},{"id":"CL_QGOH_04:FL-03","text":"The image of a free cause intensifies affect, although the whole machine of affects is deduced from necessary causes; the force of the imagination of freedom is explained, not metaphysical freedom.","refs":["CH_QGOH_PT_3:MRT6","CH_QGOH_PT_3:D15"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"Tension","passages":[]},{"id":"CL_QGOH_04:FL-04","text":"The primitive triad promises tractability, but the infinite variety of objects and essences leaves indeterminate how much general definitions explain singular differences.","refs":["CH_QGOH_PT_4:MRT4","CH_QGOH_PT_4:D12"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"Tension","passages":[]},{"id":"CL_QGOH_04:FL-05","text":"The terminal transition to action depends on prior principles of adequate cognition, which are employed within this cluster but are not fully re-grounded.","refs":["CH_QGOH_PT_4:MRT5","CH_QGOH_PT_4:AS6"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"Tension","passages":[]},{"id":"CL_QGOH_04:MP-01","text":"A causally neutral thing, conjoined by affective association, is transformed into a cause per accidens of Amor, Odium, or Cupiditas.","refs":["CH_QGOH_PT_2:A4"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"Tension","passages":[]},{"id":"CL_QGOH_04:MP-02","text":"Similarity that propagates affect with the same sign can be inverted through Odium, whence identity of relation is transformed into contrariety.","refs":["CH_QGOH_PT_2:A10","CH_QGOH_PT_2:NX5"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"Tension","passages":[]},{"id":"CL_QGOH_04:MP-03","text":"Exclusive Amor, affected by the obstacle of a rival, can be transformed into Zelotypia and into Odium greater than affect without preceding love.","refs":["CH_QGOH_PT_3:D3"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"Tension","passages":[]},{"id":"CL_QGOH_04:MP-04","text":"Enjoyment that fulfills Cupiditas can so reconfigure the Body that an object previously desired turns into Fastidium or Taedium.","refs":["CH_QGOH_PT_4:A13","CH_QGOH_PT_4:D11"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"Tension","passages":[]},{"id":"CL_QGOH_04:MP-05","text":"The change from an inadequate idea to an adequate idea changes the state of affect: what was passion becomes action, not because the object changes, but because the causal-cognitive mode changes.","refs":["CH_QGOH_PT_4:A5","CH_QGOH_PT_4:A11"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"Tension","passages":[]}],"original_name":"Internal Tensions, Mutation Points, and Fault Lines"},{"number":"16","name":"Hidden System Image","items":[{"id":"CL_QGOH_04:HSI-01","text":"Reality is implicitly a network of affecting bodies, presentifying images, and causal attributions, through which power is increased, diminished, and redistributed.","refs":["CH_QGOH_PT_2:SD1","CH_QGOH_PT_3:A1"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[]},{"id":"CL_QGOH_04:HSI-02","text":"Within this machine the subject is a nodal conatus that knows itself through others and through affections of the Body, but can acquire an active mode through adequate cognition.","refs":["CH_QGOH_PT_2:SD5","CH_QGOH_PT_4:A1","CH_QGOH_PT_4:A11"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[]},{"id":"CL_QGOH_04:HSI-03","text":"Time is a field of presentifications and irreversible changes: the past returns affectively, but the altered body prevents the same return from restoring the same state.","refs":["CH_QGOH_PT_2:SD3","CH_QGOH_PT_3:SD4","CH_QGOH_PT_4:A13"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[]},{"id":"CL_QGOH_04:HSI-04","text":"Navigation is possible not as an escape from causality, but as a transition from capture by causal images to their adequate cognition, whose social form is Generositas.","refs":["CH_QGOH_PT_3:SD3","CH_QGOH_PT_4:SD4","CH_QGOH_PT_4:D10"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[]}],"original_name":"Hidden System Image"},{"number":"17","name":"Minimal Whole-Book Reconstruction Test","items":[{"id":"CL_QGOH_04:MRT-B01","text":"Axioms concerning conatus, transition of power, imagination, association, imitation, affective causality, and adequate cognition suffice to reconstruct the principal machine of this level.","refs":["CH_QGOH_PT_2:MRT1","CH_QGOH_PT_3:MRT1","CH_QGOH_PT_4:MRT1"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[]},{"id":"CL_QGOH_04:MRT-B02","text":"The reconstruction nevertheless depends on prior principles concerning imagination, the parallelism of Mind and Body, conatus, and adequate ideas, which are assumed here rather than demonstrated.","refs":["CH_QGOH_PT_2:MRT4","CH_QGOH_PT_3:MRT2","CH_QGOH_PT_4:MRT5"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[]},{"id":"CL_QGOH_04:MRT-B03","text":"The firmest structure is where the same rule of change of power and idea of cause orders private affects, social circuits, and the distinction of action from passion.","refs":["CH_QGOH_PT_3:MRT3","CH_QGOH_PT_4:MRT2","CH_QGOH_PT_4:MRT3"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[]},{"id":"CL_QGOH_04:MRT-B04","text":"Less determined remain the composition of contrary affects into final action, the social stability of Superstitio, and the measure of singular differences arising from infinite variety.","refs":["CH_QGOH_PT_2:MRT6","CH_QGOH_PT_3:MRT7","CH_QGOH_PT_4:MRT4"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[]},{"id":"CL_QGOH_04:MRT-B05","text":"The reconstruction preserves the true movement, because it proceeds from imaginative causality and social affects to bodily variation, critique of value, and the terminal transition from passions to action.","refs":["CH_QGOH_PT_3:MRT8","CH_QGOH_PT_4:MRT8"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[]}],"original_name":"Minimal Whole-Book Reconstruction Test"}],"revision":"CL_QGOH_04@R001","source_variant_id":"canonical:CL_QGOH_04:v1","source_language_code":"la","original_title":"Chapters 10–12"}],"identity":{"layer":"CL","kicker":"Cluster · Ethica","title":"Chapters 10–12","context":"Part III - On the Origin and Nature of the Emotions · passage 2 · Part III - On the Origin and Nature of the Emotions · passage 3 · Part III - On the Origin and Nature of the Emotions · passage 4","original_title":"Chapters 10–12","original_context":"Pars tertia - De origine et natura affectuum · passage 2 · Pars tertia - De origine et natura affectuum · passage 3 · Pars tertia - De origine et natura affectuum · passage 4"},"original_title":"Chapters 10–12","title_language_code":"en"},{"id":"CL_QGOH_05","layer":"CL","title":"Chapters 13–15","order":5,"parents":["CH_QGOH_PT_5","CH_QGOH_PQDS","CH_QGOH_PQ_2"],"revision":"CL_QGOH_05@R001","sha256":"11d6128d0d97c2828e27014e6bfa224b2341e3ccf325f11b83616db8a9216aaa","sections":[{"number":"1","name":"Book-Scale Argumental Spine","items":[{"id":"CL_QGOH_05:BAS-01","text":"Systema quaerit quomodo pluralitas affectuum ex paucis motibus generetur et cur cognitio melioris hominem finitum a passionibus non necessario liberet.","refs":["CH_QGOH_PT_5:AS1","CH_QGOH_PQDS:AS1","CH_QGOH_PQ_2:AS1"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[]},{"id":"CL_QGOH_05:BAS-02","text":"Thesis globalis vitam affectivam constituit variationem potentiae corporis, cuius servitus ex asymmetria causarum oritur et cuius restitutio ethica per conatum immanentem incipit.","refs":["CH_QGOH_PT_5:A2","CH_QGOH_PQ_2:SD1","CH_QGOH_PQ_2:AS4"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[]},{"id":"CL_QGOH_05:BAS-03","text":"Argumentatio fit diagnostica et reconstructiva: species apparentes, fines transcendentes et imperium veritatis removet, deinde campum ut certamen potentiarum restituit.","refs":["CH_QGOH_PT_5:AS3","CH_QGOH_PQDS:SD1","CH_QGOH_PQ_2:ALT3"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[]},{"id":"CL_QGOH_05:BAS-04","text":"Motus procedit a reductione nominum affectivorum ad causalitatem passionum, inde ad servitutem finiti, postremo ad compositionem rationalem potentiarum.","refs":["CH_QGOH_PT_5:SD1","CH_QGOH_PQDS:D3","CH_QGOH_PQ_2:SD4"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[]},{"id":"CL_QGOH_05:BAS-05","text":"Progressio est cumulativa, recursiva et mutatione acta, quia affectus semel definiti redeunt ut vires certantes, normae practicae et gradus intellectionis.","refs":["CH_QGOH_PT_5:FGS7","CH_QGOH_PQDS:SD4","CH_QGOH_PQ_2:SD6"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[]},{"id":"CL_QGOH_05:BAS-06","text":"Clausura non liberationem absolutam sed directionem conditionalem producit: finita potentia per rationem, commune utile et Dei cognitionem ordinatur, manente causalitate externa.","refs":["CH_QGOH_PQDS:SNX2","CH_QGOH_PQ_2:D9","CH_QGOH_PQ_2:MRT7"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[]}],"original_name":"Book-Scale Argumental Spine"},{"number":"2","name":"Global Load-Bearing Axioms","items":[{"id":"CL_QGOH_05:GA-01","text":"[Persistent] Lætitia et Tristitia sunt directiones fundamentales mutationis potentiae, quae postea conatum augent vel minuunt atque ordinem boni et mali affectivum sustinent.","refs":["CH_QGOH_PT_5:A4","CH_QGOH_PQDS:A4","CH_QGOH_PQ_2:A5"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[]},{"id":"CL_QGOH_05:GA-02","text":"[Persistent] Passio oritur ubi mens ideis inadæquatis determinatur et homo effectus sui causa tantum partialis est.","refs":["CH_QGOH_PT_5:A1","CH_QGOH_PQDS:A8","CH_QGOH_PQ_2:A9"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[]},{"id":"CL_QGOH_05:GA-03","text":"[Mutating] Cupiditas ex determinatione affectiva in essentiam actualem hominis et deinde in potentiam activam ex intellectione procedentem mutatur.","refs":["CH_QGOH_PT_5:A5","CH_QGOH_PQ_2:A1","CH_QGOH_PQ_2:A7"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[]},{"id":"CL_QGOH_05:GA-04","text":"[Mutating] Imaginatio temporalis affectum rei absentis praesentem efficere potest, sed praesentia, propinquitas et contingentia gradum eius virium ordinant.","refs":["CH_QGOH_PT_5:A8","CH_QGOH_PQDS:AS7","CH_QGOH_PQ_2:A8"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[]},{"id":"CL_QGOH_05:GA-05","text":"[Emergent] Servitus est asymmetria necessaria inter potentiam hominis finiti et causas externas, quae cupiditates passivas ultra mensuram potentiae propriae augere possunt.","refs":["CH_QGOH_PQDS:AS3","CH_QGOH_PQDS:A10","CH_QGOH_PQ_2:A6"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[]},{"id":"CL_QGOH_05:GA-06","text":"[Persistent] Veritas sola affectionem positivam corporis non delet; cognitio efficax esse nequit nisi ordinem affectuum ingrediatur.","refs":["CH_QGOH_PQDS:AS4","CH_QGOH_PQDS:A9","CH_QGOH_PQ_2:NX1"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[]},{"id":"CL_QGOH_05:GA-07","text":"[Attractor] Affectus coercetur tantum per affectum contrarium et fortiorem, unde omnis moderatio realis fit redistributio potentiarum, non refutatio logica.","refs":["CH_QGOH_PQDS:A11","CH_QGOH_PQDS:D8","CH_QGOH_PQ_2:D1"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[]},{"id":"CL_QGOH_05:GA-08","text":"[Mutating] Bonum et malum ab institutione affectiva et praedicatis absolutis transferuntur ad duplicem ordinem: variationem potentiae et relationem certam ad intellectionem.","refs":["CH_QGOH_PT_5:A10","CH_QGOH_PQDS:A5","CH_QGOH_PQ_2:SD2"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[]},{"id":"CL_QGOH_05:GA-09","text":"[Terminal] Virtus est potentia propria quatenus conatus sese conservandi ex ductu Rationis agere potest.","refs":["CH_QGOH_PQDS:A6","CH_QGOH_PQ_2:A2","CH_QGOH_PQ_2:D7"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[]},{"id":"CL_QGOH_05:GA-10","text":"[Emergent] Individuum indiget rebus externis, sed convenientia positiva naturarum potest dependentiam in compositionem maioris potentiae convertere.","refs":["CH_QGOH_PQ_2:A12","CH_QGOH_PQ_2:SD3","CH_QGOH_PQ_2:SD4"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[]},{"id":"CL_QGOH_05:GA-11","text":"[Mutating] Relatio socialis affectus imitativos et conflictus generat quatenus imaginatione regitur, sed concordiam necessariam producit quatenus actio ex Ratione procedit.","refs":["CH_QGOH_PT_5:A9","CH_QGOH_PQ_2:A16","CH_QGOH_PQ_2:A17"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[]},{"id":"CL_QGOH_05:GA-12","text":"[Attractor] Intellegere est criterion utilitatis certae, cuius terminus supremus est Dei cognitio tamquam summum Mentis bonum.","refs":["CH_QGOH_PQ_2:A10","CH_QGOH_PQ_2:A18","CH_QGOH_PQ_2:D9"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[]},{"id":"CL_QGOH_05:GA-13","text":"[Persistent] Natura agit necessitate immanenti, non causa finali; Ratio igitur non contra Naturam imperat sed intra eius causalitatem potentiam ordinat.","refs":["CH_QGOH_PQDS:A1","CH_QGOH_PQDS:A2","CH_QGOH_PQ_2:NX4"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[]}],"original_name":"Global Load-Bearing Axioms"},{"number":"3","name":"Recurrence, Mutation, and Displacement Matrix","items":[{"id":"CL_QGOH_05:RMD-01","text":"Lætitia et Tristitia identitatem directionalem retinent, sed ex transitionibus affectivis in indices ethicos augmentationis et diminutionis conatus amplificantur.","refs":["CH_QGOH_PT_5:A4","CH_QGOH_PQ_2:A3"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[]},{"id":"CL_QGOH_05:RMD-02","text":"Cupiditas functionem mutat: primo determinatio Mentis est, deinde essentia perseverandi, tandem vector activus vel passivus secundum causam determinantem.","refs":["CH_QGOH_PT_5:A5","CH_QGOH_PQ_2:A1","CH_QGOH_PQ_2:A7"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[]},{"id":"CL_QGOH_05:RMD-03","text":"Idea causae externae e principio denominationis affectuum migrat in principium mensurandae servitutis, cum potentia causae potentiae hominis comparatur.","refs":["CH_QGOH_PT_5:A7","CH_QGOH_PQDS:A10"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[]},{"id":"CL_QGOH_05:RMD-04","text":"Tempus affectivum mutatur sine inversione: res absens eundem affectum genere movere potest, quamvis praesentia maiorem gradum intensitatis tribuat.","refs":["CH_QGOH_PT_5:A8","CH_QGOH_PQ_2:A8"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[]},{"id":"CL_QGOH_05:RMD-05","text":"Imaginatio alterius primo familias affectuum socialium producit, deinde dissensionem passionum explicat, postremo Rationi ut contrariae formae concordiae cedit.","refs":["CH_QGOH_PT_5:A9","CH_QGOH_PQ_2:A16","CH_QGOH_PQ_2:A17"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[]},{"id":"CL_QGOH_05:RMD-06","text":"Vocabula moralia ab effectibus institutionis ad praedicata non absoluta et tandem ad indices intellectionis transferuntur.","refs":["CH_QGOH_PT_5:SD4","CH_QGOH_PQDS:A5","CH_QGOH_PQ_2:D8"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[]},{"id":"CL_QGOH_05:RMD-07","text":"Veritas ab auctoritate explicativa contra sermonem vulgarem ad vim affective condicionatam transit: recta explicatio manet necessaria, sed sola non imperat.","refs":["CH_QGOH_PT_5:A12","CH_QGOH_PQDS:A9","CH_QGOH_PQDS:D8"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[]},{"id":"CL_QGOH_05:RMD-08","text":"Virtus, prius e classe passionum exclusa, redit ut potentia activa ipsius naturae humanae.","refs":["CH_QGOH_PT_5:NX7","CH_QGOH_PQDS:A6"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[]},{"id":"CL_QGOH_05:RMD-09","text":"Negatio liberi decreti in theoriam servitutis absorbetur: quod initio falsa causa imaginata erat, postea conditio finiti intra causas externas fit.","refs":["CH_QGOH_PT_5:A11","CH_QGOH_PQDS:D3"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[]},{"id":"CL_QGOH_05:RMD-10","text":"Nomina singularium affectuum opere taxonomico peracto recedunt; structura superior retinet relationes causales, non inventarium omnium compositorum.","refs":["CH_QGOH_PT_5:NX9","CH_QGOH_PT_5:D8"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[]}],"original_name":"Recurrence, Mutation, and Displacement Matrix"},{"number":"4","name":"Lower-Layer vs Current-Level Distinction","items":[{"id":"CL_QGOH_05:CGD-01","text":"Definitiones Amoris, Odii, Impudentiae aliorumque affectuum nominatorum manent mechanismi locales, non axiomata huius gregis.","refs":["CH_QGOH_PT_5:D1","CH_QGOH_PT_5:NX6"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[]},{"id":"CL_QGOH_05:CGD-02","text":"Exclusiones Admirationis et virtutum moderatricium e numero passionum limites taxonomicos inferiores constituunt, quorum effectus globalis est distinctio passionis a potentia activa.","refs":["CH_QGOH_PT_5:NX1","CH_QGOH_PT_5:NX7"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[]},{"id":"CL_QGOH_05:CGD-03","text":"Differentiae locales Spei, Metus, Securitatis et Desperationis sursum amplificantur in legem generalem qua imaginatio temporalis vires distribuit.","refs":["CH_QGOH_PT_5:FGS5","CH_QGOH_PQDS:SD5"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[]},{"id":"CL_QGOH_05:CGD-04","text":"Definitio passionis ut idea confusa mutationis corporis sursum amplificatur in theoriam servitutis tamquam causalitatis partialis.","refs":["CH_QGOH_PT_5:AS5","CH_QGOH_PQDS:D3"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[]},{"id":"CL_QGOH_05:CGD-05","text":"Trias affectuum et duplex ordo boni ac mali unitates coniungunt, quia mutationes potentiae fiunt materia iudicii ethici immanentis.","refs":["CH_QGOH_PT_5:SD1","CH_QGOH_PQ_2:SD2"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[]},{"id":"CL_QGOH_05:CGD-06","text":"Transitus maior efficitur ubi cognitio desinit esse mera veritas et fit cupiditas affectiva quae cum passionibus confligit.","refs":["CH_QGOH_PQDS:A12","CH_QGOH_PQ_2:D1"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[]},{"id":"CL_QGOH_05:CGD-07","text":"Affectus sociales inferiores in structuram praesentis gradus absorbentur ut bifurcatio inter conflictum imaginativum et compositionem rationalem.","refs":["CH_QGOH_PT_5:D5","CH_QGOH_PQ_2:SD4"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[]},{"id":"CL_QGOH_05:CGD-08","text":"Taxonomia psychologica fit secundario codificata, quia calculus potentiarum et certamen affectivum maiorem vim explicativam obtinent.","refs":["CH_QGOH_PT_5:ME1","CH_QGOH_PQDS:ME4"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[]}],"original_name":"Lower-Layer vs Current-Level Distinction"},{"number":"5","name":"Global Apophatic Structure","items":[{"id":"CL_QGOH_05:GNX-01","text":"Systema liberum decretum negat et servitutem non ut culpam voluntariam sed ut condicionem causaliter necessariam constituit.","refs":["CH_QGOH_PT_5:A11","CH_QGOH_PQDS:D3"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"Negative","passages":[]},{"id":"CL_QGOH_05:GNX-02","text":"Natura nec fines externos intendit nec appetitum humanum in causam finalem rerum convertit.","refs":["CH_QGOH_PQDS:A1","CH_QGOH_PQDS:A2"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"Negative","passages":[]},{"id":"CL_QGOH_05:GNX-03","text":"Actus, bonum et malum proprietates absolutas non continent, sed significationem ex institutione, potentia et intellectione accipiunt.","refs":["CH_QGOH_PT_5:DNX4","CH_QGOH_PQDS:A5","CH_QGOH_PQ_2:DNX7"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"Negative","passages":[]},{"id":"CL_QGOH_05:GNX-04","text":"Affectus a corpore separatus non admittitur, quia omnis passio constitutionem actualem corporis affirmat.","refs":["CH_QGOH_PT_5:SNX3","CH_QGOH_PQDS:AS4"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"Negative","passages":[]},{"id":"CL_QGOH_05:GNX-05","text":"Contradictio logica vel cognitio vera affectum non annihilat nisi ipsa contrariam vim affectivam producat.","refs":["CH_QGOH_PQDS:NX7","CH_QGOH_PQ_2:NX1"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"Negative","passages":[]},{"id":"CL_QGOH_05:GNX-06","text":"Individuum humanum per se sufficiens excluditur, quia finitum semper a potentiis exterioribus exceditur et rebus externis indiget.","refs":["CH_QGOH_PQDS:DNX4","CH_QGOH_PQ_2:DNX3"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"Negative","passages":[]},{"id":"CL_QGOH_05:GNX-07","text":"Communis humanitas, defectus vel finitas concordiam positivam non constituunt; convenientia requirit potentiam communem sub Ratione.","refs":["CH_QGOH_PQ_2:NX6","CH_QGOH_PQ_2:SNX3"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"Negative","passages":[]},{"id":"CL_QGOH_05:GNX-08","text":"Liberatio neque exemptio a causis externis neque actus voluntariae sui annihilationis esse potest.","refs":["CH_QGOH_PQDS:SNX2","CH_QGOH_PQ_2:DNX6"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"Negative","passages":[]}],"original_name":"Global Apophatic Structure"},{"number":"6","name":"Deep Book Apophatics","items":[{"id":"CL_QGOH_05:DBN-01","text":"Ut systema constet, falsum esse debet exemplar Naturae artificis vel legislatoris qui fines et valores ante causalitatem distribuit.","refs":["CH_QGOH_PQDS:DNX1","CH_QGOH_PQDS:SNX1"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"Negative","passages":[]},{"id":"CL_QGOH_05:DBN-02","text":"Falsum esse debet mentem veritate instructam posse corpus et imaginationem sine productione causae affectivae gubernare.","refs":["CH_QGOH_PQDS:DNX3","CH_QGOH_PQ_2:DNX1"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"Negative","passages":[]},{"id":"CL_QGOH_05:DBN-03","text":"Falsum esse debet individuum esse substantiam clausam, cuius potentia nulla re singulari superari possit.","refs":["CH_QGOH_PQDS:A7","CH_QGOH_PQ_2:DNX3"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"Negative","passages":[]},{"id":"CL_QGOH_05:DBN-04","text":"Falsum esse debet tempus esse mensuram affective neutralem, quia memoria, exspectatio, praesentia et contingentia ipsam vim passionum distribuunt.","refs":["CH_QGOH_PT_5:SD3","CH_QGOH_PQDS:SD5","CH_QGOH_PQ_2:ME6"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"Negative","passages":[]},{"id":"CL_QGOH_05:DBN-05","text":"Falsum esse debet virtutem in negatione naturae propriae, sacrificio sui vel obedientia mandato externo fundari.","refs":["CH_QGOH_PQ_2:NX4","CH_QGOH_PQ_2:DNX2"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"Negative","passages":[]},{"id":"CL_QGOH_05:DBN-06","text":"Falsum esse debet communitatem ex infirmitate communi nasci; sola convenientia positiva potentiae concordiam generare potest.","refs":["CH_QGOH_PQ_2:DNX4","CH_QGOH_PQ_2:A17"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"Negative","passages":[]}],"original_name":"Deep Book Apophatics"},{"number":"7","name":"Super-Apophatics of the Whole","items":[{"id":"CL_QGOH_05:BSA-01","text":"Possibilitas entis affectivi extra nexum causarum est ontologice indisponibilis, quia etiam actio rationalis intra necessitatem Naturae manet.","refs":["CH_QGOH_PQ_2:SNX1","CH_QGOH_PQDS:A1"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"Negative","passages":[]},{"id":"CL_QGOH_05:BSA-02","text":"Systema circum absentiam conversionis instantaneae veritatis in potentiam ordinatur; scientia sine transmutatione affectiva impotens manet.","refs":["CH_QGOH_PQDS:SD3","CH_QGOH_PQ_2:MRT4"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"Negative","passages":[]},{"id":"CL_QGOH_05:BSA-03","text":"Status terminalis in quo homo ab omni passione, mutatione externa et potentia superiore liber sit non admittitur.","refs":["CH_QGOH_PQDS:SNX2","CH_QGOH_PQDS:A7"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"Negative","passages":[]},{"id":"CL_QGOH_05:BSA-04","text":"Absentia principalis est mechanismus plene explicatus quo cognitio vera affectum fortiorem et societatem rationalem ex hominibus passionalibus producat.","refs":["CH_QGOH_PQDS:MRT6","CH_QGOH_PQ_2:MRT5"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"Negative","passages":[]},{"id":"CL_QGOH_05:BSA-05","text":"Finis transcendentis forma excluditur, quamvis Dei cognitio attractorem supremum constituat; attractio terminalis igitur causa finalis Naturae fieri non potest.","refs":["CH_QGOH_PQDS:A2","CH_QGOH_PQ_2:A18"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"Tension","passages":[]}],"original_name":"Super-Apophatics of the Whole"},{"number":"8","name":"Cross-Unit Derivative Propositions","items":[{"id":"CL_QGOH_05:XD-01","text":"Nomina affectuum non iam species psychologicae sunt, sed coordinatae quibus relationes causales in certamine potentiarum describuntur.","refs":["CL_QGOH_05:GA-01","CL_QGOH_05:GA-05","CH_QGOH_PT_5:D8","CH_QGOH_PQDS:SD4"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"From","passages":[]},{"id":"CL_QGOH_05:XD-02","text":"Cupiditas est commissura totius gregis, quia eadem forma transit a determinatione locali ad essentiam perseverandi et ad potentiam activam intellectionis.","refs":["CL_QGOH_05:GA-03","CL_QGOH_05:GA-09","CH_QGOH_PT_5:A5","CH_QGOH_PQ_2:A1","CH_QGOH_PQ_2:A7"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"From","passages":[]},{"id":"CL_QGOH_05:XD-03","text":"Quia passio affirmatio positiva corporis est et causae externae potentiam hominis superare possunt, veritas moderatrix debet fieri affectus contrarius fortior.","refs":["CL_QGOH_05:GA-02","CL_QGOH_05:GA-06","CL_QGOH_05:GA-07","CH_QGOH_PT_5:A2","CH_QGOH_PQDS:A9","CH_QGOH_PQDS:A11"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"From","passages":[]},{"id":"CL_QGOH_05:XD-04","text":"Res praeterita vel futura potest affectum eiusdem generis ac praesens producere, sed non necessario eiusdem gradus; identitas qualitatis et asymmetria intensitatis simul valent.","refs":["CL_QGOH_05:GA-04","CH_QGOH_PT_5:A8","CH_QGOH_PQ_2:A8"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"From","passages":[]},{"id":"CL_QGOH_05:XD-05","text":"Praedicata moralia oriuntur ex consuetudine affectiva, auctoritatem absolutam amittunt et legitime retinentur tantum ut indices utilitatis atque intellectionis.","refs":["CL_QGOH_05:GA-08","CL_QGOH_05:GA-13","CH_QGOH_PT_5:A10","CH_QGOH_PQDS:A5","CH_QGOH_PQ_2:D8"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"From","passages":[]},{"id":"CL_QGOH_05:XD-06","text":"Socialitas bifurcatur: imaginatio aliorum passionem et oppositionem amplificat, sed actio ex natura rationaliter communi potentias componit.","refs":["CL_QGOH_05:GA-10","CL_QGOH_05:GA-11","CH_QGOH_PT_5:A9","CH_QGOH_PQ_2:A16","CH_QGOH_PQ_2:A17"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"From","passages":[]},{"id":"CL_QGOH_05:XD-07","text":"Libertas non est decretum indeterminatum neque exemptio a Natura, sed gradus quo homo ex propria potentia intelligibili agit.","refs":["CL_QGOH_05:GA-05","CL_QGOH_05:GA-09","CH_QGOH_PT_5:A11","CH_QGOH_PQDS:A6","CH_QGOH_PQ_2:A9"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"From","passages":[]},{"id":"CL_QGOH_05:XD-08","text":"Ethica fit ars componendi affectiones contrarias, utilitates individuales et potentias communes, non tribunal quod passiones verbis condemnat.","refs":["CL_QGOH_05:GA-07","CL_QGOH_05:GA-10","CL_QGOH_05:GA-11","CH_QGOH_PQDS:D6","CH_QGOH_PQ_2:SD3","CH_QGOH_PQ_2:SD4"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"From","passages":[]},{"id":"CL_QGOH_05:XD-09","text":"Dei cognitio ordinem intellectionis claudit sine restitutione finalitatis, quia Deus obiectum supremum Mentis est, non finis ab artifice Naturae praestitutus.","refs":["CL_QGOH_05:GA-12","CL_QGOH_05:GA-13","CH_QGOH_PQDS:A2","CH_QGOH_PQ_2:A18"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"From","passages":[]}],"original_name":"Cross-Unit Derivative Propositions"},{"number":"9","name":"Book-Scale Super-Derivatives","items":[{"id":"CL_QGOH_05:BSD-01","text":"Ontologia huius gregis relationes potentiae primarias facit: res affectiva est status corporalis cuius vis tantum comparatione causalium potentiarum determinatur.","refs":["CH_QGOH_PT_5:SD2","CH_QGOH_PQ_2:SD1"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[]},{"id":"CL_QGOH_05:BSD-02","text":"Subiectum non est auctor clausus sed locus causalitatis partialis, qui tamen conatu actuali propriam perseverantiam ordinare potest.","refs":["CH_QGOH_PT_5:SD5","CH_QGOH_PQDS:A8","CH_QGOH_PQ_2:A1"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[]},{"id":"CL_QGOH_05:BSD-03","text":"Cognitio statum causaliter performativum obtinet: vera esse non sufficit, nisi ipsa in distributione virium affectivarum operetur.","refs":["CH_QGOH_PQDS:SD4","CH_QGOH_PQ_2:ME3"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[]},{"id":"CL_QGOH_05:BSD-04","text":"Tempus fit campus intensitatum affectivarum, in quo praesentia imaginata, memoria, exspectatio et contingentia efficaciam causaliter variant.","refs":["CH_QGOH_PT_5:SD3","CH_QGOH_PQDS:SD5","CH_QGOH_PQ_2:ME6"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[]},{"id":"CL_QGOH_05:BSD-05","text":"Ethica transit ab aestimatione actuum ad ordinationem potentiae, cuius criterium est quid conatum, intellectionem et compositionem naturarum augeat.","refs":["CH_QGOH_PQDS:SD1","CH_QGOH_PQ_2:A10","CH_QGOH_PQ_2:SD3"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[]},{"id":"CL_QGOH_05:BSD-06","text":"Politica implicita concordiam rationalem ut amplificationem potentiae singularis concipit, non ut suppressionem conatus individui.","refs":["CH_QGOH_PQ_2:SD4","CH_QGOH_PQ_2:MRT7"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[]},{"id":"CL_QGOH_05:BSD-07","text":"Libertas fit variabilis gradus causalitatis adæquatae: homo liberior est quatenus ex eo quod intelligit agit.","refs":["CH_QGOH_PQDS:A6","CH_QGOH_PQ_2:A9"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[]},{"id":"CL_QGOH_05:BSD-08","text":"Methodus vocabula recepta non simpliciter abolere sed ab ontologia vulgari ad functiones causales et practicas transferre debet.","refs":["CH_QGOH_PT_5:A12","CH_QGOH_PQDS:MRT7"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[]}],"original_name":"Book-Scale Super-Derivatives"},{"number":"10","name":"Recursive Loops, Escalation Paths, and Conceptual Attractors","items":[{"id":"CL_QGOH_05:RLE-01","text":"Mutatio corporis ideam affectivam producit, affectus cupiditatem determinat, iudicium boni directionem cupiditatis format, actio autem novam corporis constitutionem efficit.","refs":["CH_QGOH_PT_5:AS5","CH_QGOH_PT_5:A5","CH_QGOH_PQ_2:A4"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[]},{"id":"CL_QGOH_05:RLE-02","text":"Causa externa passionem auget, passio potentiam propriam minuit, et potentia imminuta facilius ulterioribus causis externis superatur.","refs":["CH_QGOH_PQDS:A10","CH_QGOH_PQ_2:A6"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[]},{"id":"CL_QGOH_05:RLE-03","text":"Institutio iudicium sociale affectibus coniungit, imaginatum iudicium Pudorem vel Gloriam producit, et affectus sic productus vim institutionis recreat.","refs":["CH_QGOH_PT_5:A10","CH_QGOH_PT_5:D6"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[]},{"id":"CL_QGOH_05:RLE-04","text":"Suavitas praesens affectum futuri coercet, inde ratio minus sequitur, et opinio vel libido praesentem vim denuo amplificat.","refs":["CH_QGOH_PQ_2:D2","CH_QGOH_PQ_2:D3"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[]},{"id":"CL_QGOH_05:RLE-05","text":"Circuitus moderationis incipit cum affectus contrarius producitur, maiorem vim acquirit et existentiam affectus prioris excludit.","refs":["CH_QGOH_PQDS:A11","CH_QGOH_PQDS:D8"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[]},{"id":"CL_QGOH_05:RLE-06","text":"Conatus est attractor immanens: perseverantia potentiam definit, Lætitia eam auget, et virtus formam rationalem eiusdem conatus exprimit.","refs":["CH_QGOH_PQ_2:A1","CH_QGOH_PQ_2:A2","CH_QGOH_PQ_2:A5"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[]},{"id":"CL_QGOH_05:RLE-07","text":"Convenientia rationalis potentias componit, compositio commune utile auget, et utilitas communis ulteriorem concordiam rationis confirmat.","refs":["CH_QGOH_PQ_2:A12","CH_QGOH_PQ_2:D12","CH_QGOH_PQ_2:SD4"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[]},{"id":"CL_QGOH_05:RLE-08","text":"Intellegere est attractor ascensionalis qui utile certum ordinat et in Dei cognitione terminum supremum invenit.","refs":["CH_QGOH_PQ_2:A10","CH_QGOH_PQ_2:A18","CH_QGOH_PQ_2:SD6"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[]}],"original_name":"Recursive Loops, Escalation Paths, and Conceptual Attractors"},{"number":"11","name":"Formal Generative Schema of the Whole Book","items":[{"id":"CL_QGOH_05:FGS-B01","text":"Necessitas Naturae sine fine causas finales excludit atque perfectionem et valorem absolutum in relationes comparativas convertit.","refs":["CH_QGOH_PQDS:A1","CH_QGOH_PQDS:A3","CH_QGOH_PQDS:A5"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[]},{"id":"CL_QGOH_05:FGS-B02","text":"Mutatio potentiae corporis per ideam affirmatur et, ubi idea confusa est, formam passionis accipit.","refs":["CH_QGOH_PT_5:A3","CH_QGOH_PT_5:A2"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[]},{"id":"CL_QGOH_05:FGS-B03","text":"Lætitia, Tristitia et Cupiditas motus primarios praebent, dum nomina plurima ex relationibus additis generantur.","refs":["CH_QGOH_PT_5:A6","CH_QGOH_PT_5:D8"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[]},{"id":"CL_QGOH_05:FGS-B04","text":"Causa imaginata, tempus et relatio socialis motum primarium determinant atque affectus denominatos producunt.","refs":["CH_QGOH_PT_5:A7","CH_QGOH_PT_5:A8","CH_QGOH_PT_5:A9"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[]},{"id":"CL_QGOH_05:FGS-B05","text":"Finitas hominis et excessus causae externae passionem in servitutem structuralem amplificant.","refs":["CH_QGOH_PQDS:AS3","CH_QGOH_PQDS:A10","CH_QGOH_PQDS:D3"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[]},{"id":"CL_QGOH_05:FGS-B06","text":"Moderatio requirit ut cognitio fiat affectus contrarius maioris potentiae, non mera correctio propositionis falsae.","refs":["CH_QGOH_PQDS:A11","CH_QGOH_PQDS:A12","CH_QGOH_PQ_2:D1"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[]},{"id":"CL_QGOH_05:FGS-B07","text":"Conatus servitutem non delet sed principium restitutionis praebet, cum virtus et Ratio potentiam propriam ordinant.","refs":["CH_QGOH_PQ_2:A1","CH_QGOH_PQ_2:A2","CH_QGOH_PQ_2:A10"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[]},{"id":"CL_QGOH_05:FGS-B08","text":"Ratio convenientiam naturarum componit et motum ad commune utile atque Dei cognitionem dirigit.","refs":["CH_QGOH_PQ_2:A12","CH_QGOH_PQ_2:A17","CH_QGOH_PQ_2:A18"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[]}],"original_name":"Formal Generative Schema of the Whole Book"},{"number":"12","name":"Whole-Book Argument Flow Map","items":[{"id":"CL_QGOH_05:AFM-B01","text":"Pluralitas affectuum nominalis → reductio ad variationes potentiae → modulatio per causam, tempus et societatem → dissolutio finalitatis et valorum absolutorum → finitas causalis et servitus → affectivizatio cognitionis → conatus ut virtus → compositio rationalis utilitatum → Dei cognitio ut attractor terminalis.","refs":["CH_QGOH_PT_5:SD1","CH_QGOH_PQDS:D3","CH_QGOH_PQ_2:SD6"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[]}],"original_name":"Whole-Book Argument Flow Map"},{"number":"13","name":"Macro Map-Effects","items":[{"id":"CL_QGOH_05:MME-01","text":"Psychologia affectuum reordinatur ex inventario specierum in tabulam motuum relationalium potentiae.","refs":["CH_QGOH_PT_5:ME1"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[]},{"id":"CL_QGOH_05:MME-02","text":"Moralia praedicata reclassificantur ut relationes practicae utilitatis, impedimenti, Lætitiae et Tristitiae.","refs":["CH_QGOH_PQDS:ME2"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[]},{"id":"CL_QGOH_05:MME-03","text":"Oppositio veri et falsi desinit sufficere ad vitam affectivam, quia efficacia cognitionis a vi causali distinguitur.","refs":["CH_QGOH_PQDS:ME4","CH_QGOH_PQ_2:ME3"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[]},{"id":"CL_QGOH_05:MME-04","text":"Tempora chronologica fiunt gradus praesentiae affectivae, quibus res absens vim praesentem vel imminutam accipit.","refs":["CH_QGOH_PT_5:ME3","CH_QGOH_PQ_2:ME6"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[]},{"id":"CL_QGOH_05:MME-05","text":"Agentia transfertur a subiecto libero ad conatum finitum, cuius virtus est potentia immanens agendi.","refs":["CH_QGOH_PT_5:SD5","CH_QGOH_PQDS:ME3"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[]},{"id":"CL_QGOH_05:MME-06","text":"Ager socialis mutatur ex scena iudicii et aemulationis in problema compositionis, ubi alter homo rationalis fit res singularis utilissima.","refs":["CH_QGOH_PQ_2:SD4","CH_QGOH_PQ_2:ME5"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[]},{"id":"CL_QGOH_05:MME-07","text":"Utilitas propria et commune utile desinunt esse opposita, quia concordia potest potentiam individui amplificare.","refs":["CH_QGOH_PQ_2:SD3","CH_QGOH_PQ_2:D12"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[]},{"id":"CL_QGOH_05:MME-08","text":"Lectio vocabularii affectivi debet functiones mutantes investigare, quia eadem verba ab usu vulgari ad calculum immanentem transferuntur.","refs":["CH_QGOH_PT_5:A12","CH_QGOH_PQDS:MRT7"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[]}],"original_name":"Macro Map-Effects"},{"number":"14","name":"Altitude Integration","items":[{"id":"CL_QGOH_05:ALT-B01","text":"In gradu superficiei agitur de nominibus affectuum, homine meliora cognoscente sed passionibus obnoxio, et dictaminibus Rationis de utilitate ac virtute.","refs":["CH_QGOH_PT_5:ALT1","CH_QGOH_PQDS:ALT1","CH_QGOH_PQ_2:ALT1"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[]},{"id":"CL_QGOH_05:ALT-B02","text":"In gradu structurali omnia ordinantur per mutationes corporis, causalitatem partialem, proportionem potentiarum et convenientiam vel contrarietatem naturarum.","refs":["CH_QGOH_PT_5:ALT2","CH_QGOH_PQDS:ALT2","CH_QGOH_PQ_2:ALT2"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[]},{"id":"CL_QGOH_05:ALT-B03","text":"In gradu metastrategico moderatio transit a vocabulario morali et cognitione recta ad productionem affectus contrarii atque compositionem utilitatum.","refs":["CH_QGOH_PT_5:ALT3","CH_QGOH_PQDS:ALT3","CH_QGOH_PQ_2:ALT3"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[]},{"id":"CL_QGOH_05:ALT-B04","text":"In gradu ontologico et temporali Natura necessario agit, subiectum est conatus corporalis finitus, et tempus efficaciam imaginativam variat.","refs":["CH_QGOH_PT_5:ALT4","CH_QGOH_PQDS:ALT4","CH_QGOH_PQ_2:ALT4"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[]},{"id":"CL_QGOH_05:ALT-B05","text":"In gradu methodologico et performativo textus ipse reductionem, exclusionem et mutationem terminorum peragit, atque diagnosis impotentiae in reconstructionem ethicam convertitur.","refs":["CH_QGOH_PT_5:AS3","CH_QGOH_PQDS:MRT7","CH_QGOH_PQ_2:AS4"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[]}],"original_name":"Altitude Integration"},{"number":"15","name":"Internal Tensions, Mutation Points, and Fault Lines","items":[{"id":"CL_QGOH_05:FL-01","text":"Reductio ad tres affectus primitivos cum multitudine compositorum sine nomine cohaeret tantum si primitivum principium generativum, non genus taxonomicum exhaustivum, significat.","refs":["CH_QGOH_PT_5:A6","CH_QGOH_PT_5:NX9"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"Tension","passages":[]},{"id":"CL_QGOH_05:FL-02","text":"Negatio perfectionis obiectivae tensionem cum retentione exemplaris humanae naturae et normarum utilitatis relinquit.","refs":["CH_QGOH_PQDS:A3","CH_QGOH_PQDS:MRT5"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"Tension","passages":[]},{"id":"CL_QGOH_05:FL-03","text":"Veritas imperium automaticum non habet, sed Ratio tamen directionem ethicam praebere debet; modus quo cognitio vim superiorem acquirit nondum perficitur.","refs":["CH_QGOH_PQDS:SD3","CH_QGOH_PQDS:MRT6","CH_QGOH_PQ_2:MRT4"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"Tension","passages":[]},{"id":"CL_QGOH_05:FL-04","text":"Aequalitas affectus praesentis et absentis secundum genus cum inaequalitate secundum vehementiam componitur, sed distinctio inter qualitatem et gradum tacite sustinetur.","refs":["CH_QGOH_PT_5:A8","CH_QGOH_PQ_2:A8"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"Tension","passages":[]},{"id":"CL_QGOH_05:FL-05","text":"Ascensus ad summum bonum Dei cognitionis formam quasi teleologicam inducit, quamquam Natura nullum finem externum intendit.","refs":["CH_QGOH_PQDS:A1","CH_QGOH_PQ_2:D9"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"Tension","passages":[]},{"id":"CL_QGOH_05:FL-06","text":"Concordia sub Ratione necessaria dicitur, sed transitus hominum passionalium ad talem compositionem causaliter indeterminatus manet.","refs":["CH_QGOH_PQ_2:A17","CH_QGOH_PQ_2:MRT5","CH_QGOH_PQ_2:MRT7"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"Tension","passages":[]},{"id":"CL_QGOH_05:FL-07","text":"Nexus inter definitionem corpoream affectus et ordines sociales, rationales ac normativos praesupponitur firmius quam singulis gradibus demonstratur.","refs":["CH_QGOH_PT_5:MRT4","CH_QGOH_PQ_2:MRT6"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"Tension","passages":[]},{"id":"CL_QGOH_05:MP-01","text":"Cupiditas punctum mutationis principale est, cum ex affectu primitivo in essentiam actualem et potentiam agendi convertitur.","refs":["CH_QGOH_PT_5:A5","CH_QGOH_PQ_2:A1"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"Tension","passages":[]},{"id":"CL_QGOH_05:MP-02","text":"Bonum et malum mutantur ab associationibus institutionis ad relationes potentiae et tandem ad criterion intellectionis.","refs":["CH_QGOH_PT_5:A10","CH_QGOH_PQDS:A5","CH_QGOH_PQ_2:SD2"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"Tension","passages":[]},{"id":"CL_QGOH_05:MP-03","text":"Socialitas sensum mutat ubi imaginatio alterius, quae affectus imitativos producit, cedit convenientiae hominum ex Ratione agentium.","refs":["CH_QGOH_PT_5:A9","CH_QGOH_PQ_2:A16","CH_QGOH_PQ_2:A17"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"Tension","passages":[]},{"id":"CL_QGOH_05:MP-04","text":"Virtus e termino negativo, quo passiones moderatrices distinguuntur, in principium positivum potentiae et conservationis sui mutatur.","refs":["CH_QGOH_PT_5:NX7","CH_QGOH_PQDS:A6","CH_QGOH_PQ_2:A2"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"Tension","passages":[]}],"original_name":"Internal Tensions, Mutation Points, and Fault Lines"},{"number":"16","name":"Hidden System Image","items":[{"id":"CL_QGOH_05:HSI-01","text":"Realitas implicite est rete virium finitarum, in quo nulla res singularis absolute dominatur et omnis status ex proportione potentiarum nascitur.","refs":["CH_QGOH_PQDS:AS3","CH_QGOH_PQDS:A10"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[]},{"id":"CL_QGOH_05:HSI-02","text":"Subiectum est nodus corporalis causarum externarum et conatus proprii, non centrum liberum quod affectiones ex nihilo instituit.","refs":["CH_QGOH_PT_5:SD5","CH_QGOH_PQ_2:A1"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[]},{"id":"CL_QGOH_05:HSI-03","text":"Tempus est topologia praesentiae affectivae, in qua memoria et futurum possunt ad praesens accedere sed non eandem semper vehementiam retinent.","refs":["CH_QGOH_PT_5:SD3","CH_QGOH_PQ_2:A8"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[]},{"id":"CL_QGOH_05:HSI-04","text":"Navigatio possibilis non est fuga e Natura, sed productio affectuum fortiorum, compositio potentiarum et directio conatus ad intellectionem.","refs":["CH_QGOH_PQDS:A11","CH_QGOH_PQ_2:SD4","CH_QGOH_PQ_2:A18"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[]}],"original_name":"Hidden System Image"},{"number":"17","name":"Minimal Whole-Book Reconstruction Test","items":[{"id":"CL_QGOH_05:MRT-B01","text":"Trias affectuum, idea confusa corporis, asymmetria causalium potentiarum, conatus et Ratio sufficiunt ad structuram minimam huius gregis restituendam.","refs":["CH_QGOH_PT_5:MRT1","CH_QGOH_PQDS:MRT1","CH_QGOH_PQ_2:MRT1"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[]},{"id":"CL_QGOH_05:MRT-B02","text":"Structura firmissima est connexio inter finitudinem, excessum causarum externarum, conatum, virtutem et actionem ex intellectu.","refs":["CH_QGOH_PQDS:MRT2","CH_QGOH_PQ_2:MRT2"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[]},{"id":"CL_QGOH_05:MRT-B03","text":"Nondum satis determinantur productio positiva affectus rationalis fortioris et mechanismus quo homines passionales ad compositionem communem transeant.","refs":["CH_QGOH_PQDS:MRT6","CH_QGOH_PQ_2:MRT4","CH_QGOH_PQ_2:MRT5"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[]},{"id":"CL_QGOH_05:MRT-B04","text":"Cohaerentia nititur reditu terminorum cum mutatione functionis; derivationes locales singulorum affectuum et regula semantica vocabularii reductioni plenae resistunt.","refs":["CH_QGOH_PT_5:MRT3","CH_QGOH_PT_5:MRT5","CH_QGOH_PQDS:MRT7"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[]},{"id":"CL_QGOH_05:MRT-B05","text":"Restitutio motum verum servat, quia a reductione affectuum ad diagnosin servitutis et inde ad reconstructionem rationalem procedit, manentibus mediationibus normativis incompletis.","refs":["CH_QGOH_PT_5:MRT7","CH_QGOH_PQ_2:MRT6","CH_QGOH_PQ_2:MRT7"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[]}],"original_name":"Minimal Whole-Book Reconstruction Test"}],"language_code":"la","language_name":"Latina","translations":[{"id":"CAREVAR_20261015T015629244862Z_29f39782","language_code":"en","language_name":"English","title":"Chapters 13–15","source_sha256":"11d6128d0d97c2828e27014e6bfa224b2341e3ccf325f11b83616db8a9216aaa","sha256":"b7f474564edaca2168db4b9040d8c54948822bf3f1a39e3b09711f7355c9584b","rendition_type":"machine","review_status":"machine","model":"gpt-5.6-terra","created":"2026-10-15T01:56:29.249320+00:00","sections":[{"number":"1","name":"Book-Scale Argumental Spine","items":[{"id":"CL_QGOH_05:BAS-01","text":"The system asks how a plurality of affects is generated from a few motions and why knowledge of the better does not necessarily free the finite human being from passions.","refs":["CH_QGOH_PT_5:AS1","CH_QGOH_PQDS:AS1","CH_QGOH_PQ_2:AS1"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[]},{"id":"CL_QGOH_05:BAS-02","text":"The global thesis constitutes affective life as variation of the body's power, whose servitude arises from an asymmetry of causes and whose ethical restoration begins through immanent conatus.","refs":["CH_QGOH_PT_5:A2","CH_QGOH_PQ_2:SD1","CH_QGOH_PQ_2:AS4"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[]},{"id":"CL_QGOH_05:BAS-03","text":"The argument is diagnostic and reconstructive: it removes apparent species, transcendent ends, and the sovereignty of truth, then restores the field as a contest of powers.","refs":["CH_QGOH_PT_5:AS3","CH_QGOH_PQDS:SD1","CH_QGOH_PQ_2:ALT3"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[]},{"id":"CL_QGOH_05:BAS-04","text":"The movement proceeds from the reduction of affective names to the causality of passions, thence to the servitude of the finite, and finally to the rational composition of powers.","refs":["CH_QGOH_PT_5:SD1","CH_QGOH_PQDS:D3","CH_QGOH_PQ_2:SD4"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[]},{"id":"CL_QGOH_05:BAS-05","text":"The progression is cumulative, recursive, and driven by mutation, because affects once defined return as competing forces, practical norms, and degrees of understanding.","refs":["CH_QGOH_PT_5:FGS7","CH_QGOH_PQDS:SD4","CH_QGOH_PQ_2:SD6"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[]},{"id":"CL_QGOH_05:BAS-06","text":"The closure produces not absolute liberation but conditional direction: finite power is ordered through reason, common utility, and knowledge of God, while external causality remains.","refs":["CH_QGOH_PQDS:SNX2","CH_QGOH_PQ_2:D9","CH_QGOH_PQ_2:MRT7"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[]}],"original_name":"Book-Scale Argumental Spine"},{"number":"2","name":"Global Load-Bearing Axioms","items":[{"id":"CL_QGOH_05:GA-01","text":"[Persistent] Joy and Sadness are fundamental directions of change in power, which subsequently increase or diminish conatus and sustain the affective order of good and evil.","refs":["CH_QGOH_PT_5:A4","CH_QGOH_PQDS:A4","CH_QGOH_PQ_2:A5"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[]},{"id":"CL_QGOH_05:GA-02","text":"[Persistent] Passion arises where the mind is determined by inadequate ideas and the human being is only a partial cause of its effect.","refs":["CH_QGOH_PT_5:A1","CH_QGOH_PQDS:A8","CH_QGOH_PQ_2:A9"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[]},{"id":"CL_QGOH_05:GA-03","text":"[Mutating] Desire changes from affective determination into the actual essence of the human being and then into active power proceeding from understanding.","refs":["CH_QGOH_PT_5:A5","CH_QGOH_PQ_2:A1","CH_QGOH_PQ_2:A7"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[]},{"id":"CL_QGOH_05:GA-04","text":"[Mutating] Temporal imagination can render the affect of an absent thing present, but presence, proximity, and contingency order the degree of its force.","refs":["CH_QGOH_PT_5:A8","CH_QGOH_PQDS:AS7","CH_QGOH_PQ_2:A8"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[]},{"id":"CL_QGOH_05:GA-05","text":"[Emergent] Servitude is the necessary asymmetry between the power of the finite human being and external causes, which can increase passive desires beyond the measure of one's own power.","refs":["CH_QGOH_PQDS:AS3","CH_QGOH_PQDS:A10","CH_QGOH_PQ_2:A6"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[]},{"id":"CL_QGOH_05:GA-06","text":"[Persistent] Truth alone does not destroy a positive affection of the body; knowledge cannot be effective unless it enters the order of affects.","refs":["CH_QGOH_PQDS:AS4","CH_QGOH_PQDS:A9","CH_QGOH_PQ_2:NX1"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[]},{"id":"CL_QGOH_05:GA-07","text":"[Attractor] An affect is restrained only by a contrary and stronger affect; hence all real moderation is a redistribution of powers, not logical refutation.","refs":["CH_QGOH_PQDS:A11","CH_QGOH_PQDS:D8","CH_QGOH_PQ_2:D1"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[]},{"id":"CL_QGOH_05:GA-08","text":"[Mutating] Good and evil are transferred from affective institution and absolute predicates to a double order: variation of power and a determinate relation to understanding.","refs":["CH_QGOH_PT_5:A10","CH_QGOH_PQDS:A5","CH_QGOH_PQ_2:SD2"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[]},{"id":"CL_QGOH_05:GA-09","text":"[Terminal] Virtue is one's own power insofar as the striving to preserve oneself can act under the guidance of Reason.","refs":["CH_QGOH_PQDS:A6","CH_QGOH_PQ_2:A2","CH_QGOH_PQ_2:D7"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[]},{"id":"CL_QGOH_05:GA-10","text":"[Emergent] The individual needs external things, but a positive compatibility of natures can transform dependence into a composition of greater power.","refs":["CH_QGOH_PQ_2:A12","CH_QGOH_PQ_2:SD3","CH_QGOH_PQ_2:SD4"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[]},{"id":"CL_QGOH_05:GA-11","text":"[Mutating] Social relation generates imitative affects and conflicts insofar as it is governed by imagination, but produces necessary concord insofar as action proceeds from Reason.","refs":["CH_QGOH_PT_5:A9","CH_QGOH_PQ_2:A16","CH_QGOH_PQ_2:A17"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[]},{"id":"CL_QGOH_05:GA-12","text":"[Attractor] To understand is the criterion of certain utility, whose supreme terminus is knowledge of God as the highest good of the Mind.","refs":["CH_QGOH_PQ_2:A10","CH_QGOH_PQ_2:A18","CH_QGOH_PQ_2:D9"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[]},{"id":"CL_QGOH_05:GA-13","text":"[Persistent] Nature acts by immanent necessity, not by final cause; Reason therefore does not command against Nature but orders power within its causality.","refs":["CH_QGOH_PQDS:A1","CH_QGOH_PQDS:A2","CH_QGOH_PQ_2:NX4"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[]}],"original_name":"Global Load-Bearing Axioms"},{"number":"3","name":"Recurrence, Mutation, and Displacement Matrix","items":[{"id":"CL_QGOH_05:RMD-01","text":"Joy and Sadness retain directional identity, but are amplified from affective transitions into ethical indices of the increase and diminution of conatus.","refs":["CH_QGOH_PT_5:A4","CH_QGOH_PQ_2:A3"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[]},{"id":"CL_QGOH_05:RMD-02","text":"Desire changes function: first it is a determination of the Mind, then the essence of persevering, finally an active or passive vector according to the determining cause.","refs":["CH_QGOH_PT_5:A5","CH_QGOH_PQ_2:A1","CH_QGOH_PQ_2:A7"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[]},{"id":"CL_QGOH_05:RMD-03","text":"The idea of an external cause migrates from the principle of denominating affects into the principle of measuring servitude, as the power of the cause is compared with human power.","refs":["CH_QGOH_PT_5:A7","CH_QGOH_PQDS:A10"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[]},{"id":"CL_QGOH_05:RMD-04","text":"Affective time changes without inversion: an absent thing can move the same affect in kind, although presence confers a greater degree of intensity.","refs":["CH_QGOH_PT_5:A8","CH_QGOH_PQ_2:A8"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[]},{"id":"CL_QGOH_05:RMD-05","text":"Imagination of another first produces families of social affects, then explains the dissension of passions, and finally yields to Reason as the contrary form of concord.","refs":["CH_QGOH_PT_5:A9","CH_QGOH_PQ_2:A16","CH_QGOH_PQ_2:A17"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[]},{"id":"CL_QGOH_05:RMD-06","text":"Moral terms are transferred from the effects of institution to non-absolute predicates and finally to indices of understanding.","refs":["CH_QGOH_PT_5:SD4","CH_QGOH_PQDS:A5","CH_QGOH_PQ_2:D8"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[]},{"id":"CL_QGOH_05:RMD-07","text":"Truth passes from explanatory authority against common speech to affectively conditioned force: correct explanation remains necessary, but does not rule by itself.","refs":["CH_QGOH_PT_5:A12","CH_QGOH_PQDS:A9","CH_QGOH_PQDS:D8"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[]},{"id":"CL_QGOH_05:RMD-08","text":"Virtue, previously excluded from the class of passions, returns as the active power of human nature itself.","refs":["CH_QGOH_PT_5:NX7","CH_QGOH_PQDS:A6"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[]},{"id":"CL_QGOH_05:RMD-09","text":"The negation of free decree is absorbed into the theory of servitude: what was initially an imagined false cause later becomes the condition of the finite within external causes.","refs":["CH_QGOH_PT_5:A11","CH_QGOH_PQDS:D3"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[]},{"id":"CL_QGOH_05:RMD-10","text":"The names of particular affects recede once the taxonomic work is completed; the higher structure retains causal relations, not an inventory of all compounds.","refs":["CH_QGOH_PT_5:NX9","CH_QGOH_PT_5:D8"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[]}],"original_name":"Recurrence, Mutation, and Displacement Matrix"},{"number":"4","name":"Lower-Layer vs Current-Level Distinction","items":[{"id":"CL_QGOH_05:CGD-01","text":"Definitions of Love, Hate, Shamelessness, and other named affects remain local mechanisms, not axioms of this group.","refs":["CH_QGOH_PT_5:D1","CH_QGOH_PT_5:NX6"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[]},{"id":"CL_QGOH_05:CGD-02","text":"The exclusions of Wonder and moderating virtues from the number of passions constitute lower taxonomic limits, whose global effect is to distinguish passion from active power.","refs":["CH_QGOH_PT_5:NX1","CH_QGOH_PT_5:NX7"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[]},{"id":"CL_QGOH_05:CGD-03","text":"Local differences of Hope, Fear, Security, and Despair are amplified upward into the general law by which temporal imagination distributes forces.","refs":["CH_QGOH_PT_5:FGS5","CH_QGOH_PQDS:SD5"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[]},{"id":"CL_QGOH_05:CGD-04","text":"The definition of passion as a confused idea of bodily change is amplified upward into a theory of servitude as partial causality.","refs":["CH_QGOH_PT_5:AS5","CH_QGOH_PQDS:D3"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[]},{"id":"CL_QGOH_05:CGD-05","text":"The triad of affects and the double order of good and evil join the units, because changes of power become the matter of immanent ethical judgment.","refs":["CH_QGOH_PT_5:SD1","CH_QGOH_PQ_2:SD2"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[]},{"id":"CL_QGOH_05:CGD-06","text":"A major transition occurs where knowledge ceases to be mere truth and becomes affective desire that conflicts with passions.","refs":["CH_QGOH_PQDS:A12","CH_QGOH_PQ_2:D1"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[]},{"id":"CL_QGOH_05:CGD-07","text":"Lower social affects are absorbed into the structure of the present level as a bifurcation between imaginative conflict and rational composition.","refs":["CH_QGOH_PT_5:D5","CH_QGOH_PQ_2:SD4"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[]},{"id":"CL_QGOH_05:CGD-08","text":"Psychological taxonomy becomes secondarily codified, because the calculus of powers and affective contest acquire greater explanatory force.","refs":["CH_QGOH_PT_5:ME1","CH_QGOH_PQDS:ME4"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[]}],"original_name":"Lower-Layer vs Current-Level Distinction"},{"number":"5","name":"Global Apophatic Structure","items":[{"id":"CL_QGOH_05:GNX-01","text":"The system denies free decree and constitutes servitude not as voluntary fault but as a causally necessary condition.","refs":["CH_QGOH_PT_5:A11","CH_QGOH_PQDS:D3"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"Negative","passages":[]},{"id":"CL_QGOH_05:GNX-02","text":"Nature neither intends external ends nor converts human appetite into the final cause of things.","refs":["CH_QGOH_PQDS:A1","CH_QGOH_PQDS:A2"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"Negative","passages":[]},{"id":"CL_QGOH_05:GNX-03","text":"Actions, good, and evil contain no absolute properties, but receive their meaning from institution, power, and understanding.","refs":["CH_QGOH_PT_5:DNX4","CH_QGOH_PQDS:A5","CH_QGOH_PQ_2:DNX7"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"Negative","passages":[]},{"id":"CL_QGOH_05:GNX-04","text":"An affect separated from the body is not admitted, because every passion affirms the actual constitution of the body.","refs":["CH_QGOH_PT_5:SNX3","CH_QGOH_PQDS:AS4"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"Negative","passages":[]},{"id":"CL_QGOH_05:GNX-05","text":"Logical contradiction or true knowledge does not annihilate an affect unless it itself produces a contrary affective force.","refs":["CH_QGOH_PQDS:NX7","CH_QGOH_PQ_2:NX1"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"Negative","passages":[]},{"id":"CL_QGOH_05:GNX-06","text":"The self-sufficient human individual is excluded, because the finite is always exceeded by external powers and needs external things.","refs":["CH_QGOH_PQDS:DNX4","CH_QGOH_PQ_2:DNX3"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"Negative","passages":[]},{"id":"CL_QGOH_05:GNX-07","text":"Common humanity, defect, or finitude do not constitute positive concord; compatibility requires common power under Reason.","refs":["CH_QGOH_PQ_2:NX6","CH_QGOH_PQ_2:SNX3"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"Negative","passages":[]},{"id":"CL_QGOH_05:GNX-08","text":"Liberation can be neither exemption from external causes nor an act of voluntary self-annihilation.","refs":["CH_QGOH_PQDS:SNX2","CH_QGOH_PQ_2:DNX6"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"Negative","passages":[]}],"original_name":"Global Apophatic Structure"},{"number":"6","name":"Deep Book Apophatics","items":[{"id":"CL_QGOH_05:DBN-01","text":"For the system to hold, the model of Nature as artificer or legislator who distributes ends and values prior to causality must be false.","refs":["CH_QGOH_PQDS:DNX1","CH_QGOH_PQDS:SNX1"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"Negative","passages":[]},{"id":"CL_QGOH_05:DBN-02","text":"It must be false that a mind furnished with truth can govern body and imagination without producing an affective cause.","refs":["CH_QGOH_PQDS:DNX3","CH_QGOH_PQ_2:DNX1"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"Negative","passages":[]},{"id":"CL_QGOH_05:DBN-03","text":"It must be false that the individual is a closed substance whose power can be surpassed by no singular thing.","refs":["CH_QGOH_PQDS:A7","CH_QGOH_PQ_2:DNX3"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"Negative","passages":[]},{"id":"CL_QGOH_05:DBN-04","text":"It must be false that time is an affectively neutral measure, because memory, expectation, presence, and contingency distribute the very force of passions.","refs":["CH_QGOH_PT_5:SD3","CH_QGOH_PQDS:SD5","CH_QGOH_PQ_2:ME6"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"Negative","passages":[]},{"id":"CL_QGOH_05:DBN-05","text":"It must be false that virtue is grounded in the negation of one's own nature, self-sacrifice, or obedience to an external command.","refs":["CH_QGOH_PQ_2:NX4","CH_QGOH_PQ_2:DNX2"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"Negative","passages":[]},{"id":"CL_QGOH_05:DBN-06","text":"It must be false that community arises from common weakness; only positive compatibility of power can generate concord.","refs":["CH_QGOH_PQ_2:DNX4","CH_QGOH_PQ_2:A17"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"Negative","passages":[]}],"original_name":"Deep Book Apophatics"},{"number":"7","name":"Super-Apophatics of the Whole","items":[{"id":"CL_QGOH_05:BSA-01","text":"The possibility of an affective being outside the nexus of causes is ontologically unavailable, because even rational action remains within the necessity of Nature.","refs":["CH_QGOH_PQ_2:SNX1","CH_QGOH_PQDS:A1"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"Negative","passages":[]},{"id":"CL_QGOH_05:BSA-02","text":"The system is ordered around the absence of an instantaneous conversion of truth into power; knowledge without affective transmutation remains powerless.","refs":["CH_QGOH_PQDS:SD3","CH_QGOH_PQ_2:MRT4"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"Negative","passages":[]},{"id":"CL_QGOH_05:BSA-03","text":"A terminal state in which the human being is free from every passion, external change, and superior power is not admitted.","refs":["CH_QGOH_PQDS:SNX2","CH_QGOH_PQDS:A7"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"Negative","passages":[]},{"id":"CL_QGOH_05:BSA-04","text":"The principal absence is a fully explained mechanism by which true knowledge produces a stronger affect and rational society from passionate human beings.","refs":["CH_QGOH_PQDS:MRT6","CH_QGOH_PQ_2:MRT5"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"Negative","passages":[]},{"id":"CL_QGOH_05:BSA-05","text":"The form of a transcendent end is excluded, although knowledge of God constitutes the supreme attractor; terminal attraction therefore cannot become Nature's final cause.","refs":["CH_QGOH_PQDS:A2","CH_QGOH_PQ_2:A18"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"Tension","passages":[]}],"original_name":"Super-Apophatics of the Whole"},{"number":"8","name":"Cross-Unit Derivative Propositions","items":[{"id":"CL_QGOH_05:XD-01","text":"Names of affects are no longer psychological species, but coordinates by which causal relations are described in the contest of powers.","refs":["CL_QGOH_05:GA-01","CL_QGOH_05:GA-05","CH_QGOH_PT_5:D8","CH_QGOH_PQDS:SD4"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"From","passages":[]},{"id":"CL_QGOH_05:XD-02","text":"Desire is the joint of the whole group, because the same form passes from local determination to the essence of persevering and to the active power of understanding.","refs":["CL_QGOH_05:GA-03","CL_QGOH_05:GA-09","CH_QGOH_PT_5:A5","CH_QGOH_PQ_2:A1","CH_QGOH_PQ_2:A7"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"From","passages":[]},{"id":"CL_QGOH_05:XD-03","text":"Because passion is a positive affirmation of the body and external causes can surpass human power, moderating truth must become a stronger contrary affect.","refs":["CL_QGOH_05:GA-02","CL_QGOH_05:GA-06","CL_QGOH_05:GA-07","CH_QGOH_PT_5:A2","CH_QGOH_PQDS:A9","CH_QGOH_PQDS:A11"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"From","passages":[]},{"id":"CL_QGOH_05:XD-04","text":"A past or future thing can produce an affect of the same kind as a present one, but not necessarily of the same degree; identity of quality and asymmetry of intensity hold together.","refs":["CL_QGOH_05:GA-04","CH_QGOH_PT_5:A8","CH_QGOH_PQ_2:A8"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"From","passages":[]},{"id":"CL_QGOH_05:XD-05","text":"Moral predicates arise from affective custom, lose absolute authority, and are legitimately retained only as indices of utility and understanding.","refs":["CL_QGOH_05:GA-08","CL_QGOH_05:GA-13","CH_QGOH_PT_5:A10","CH_QGOH_PQDS:A5","CH_QGOH_PQ_2:D8"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"From","passages":[]},{"id":"CL_QGOH_05:XD-06","text":"Sociality bifurcates: imagination of others amplifies passion and opposition, but action from rationally common nature composes powers.","refs":["CL_QGOH_05:GA-10","CL_QGOH_05:GA-11","CH_QGOH_PT_5:A9","CH_QGOH_PQ_2:A16","CH_QGOH_PQ_2:A17"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"From","passages":[]},{"id":"CL_QGOH_05:XD-07","text":"Freedom is neither indeterminate decree nor exemption from Nature, but the degree to which the human being acts from its own intelligible power.","refs":["CL_QGOH_05:GA-05","CL_QGOH_05:GA-09","CH_QGOH_PT_5:A11","CH_QGOH_PQDS:A6","CH_QGOH_PQ_2:A9"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"From","passages":[]},{"id":"CL_QGOH_05:XD-08","text":"Ethics becomes the art of composing contrary affections, individual utilities, and common powers, not a tribunal that condemns passions with words.","refs":["CL_QGOH_05:GA-07","CL_QGOH_05:GA-10","CL_QGOH_05:GA-11","CH_QGOH_PQDS:D6","CH_QGOH_PQ_2:SD3","CH_QGOH_PQ_2:SD4"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"From","passages":[]},{"id":"CL_QGOH_05:XD-09","text":"Knowledge of God closes the order of understanding without restoring finality, because God is the supreme object of the Mind, not an end pre-established by an artificer Nature.","refs":["CL_QGOH_05:GA-12","CL_QGOH_05:GA-13","CH_QGOH_PQDS:A2","CH_QGOH_PQ_2:A18"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"From","passages":[]}],"original_name":"Cross-Unit Derivative Propositions"},{"number":"9","name":"Book-Scale Super-Derivatives","items":[{"id":"CL_QGOH_05:BSD-01","text":"The ontology of this group makes relations of power primary: an affective thing is a bodily state whose force is determined only by comparison of causal powers.","refs":["CH_QGOH_PT_5:SD2","CH_QGOH_PQ_2:SD1"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[]},{"id":"CL_QGOH_05:BSD-02","text":"The subject is not a closed author but a site of partial causality, which can nevertheless order its own perseverance through actual conatus.","refs":["CH_QGOH_PT_5:SD5","CH_QGOH_PQDS:A8","CH_QGOH_PQ_2:A1"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[]},{"id":"CL_QGOH_05:BSD-03","text":"Knowledge obtains a causally performative status: being true is insufficient unless it itself operates in the distribution of affective forces.","refs":["CH_QGOH_PQDS:SD4","CH_QGOH_PQ_2:ME3"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[]},{"id":"CL_QGOH_05:BSD-04","text":"Time becomes a field of affective intensities, in which imagined presence, memory, expectation, and contingency causally vary efficacy.","refs":["CH_QGOH_PT_5:SD3","CH_QGOH_PQDS:SD5","CH_QGOH_PQ_2:ME6"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[]},{"id":"CL_QGOH_05:BSD-05","text":"Ethics passes from the evaluation of actions to the ordering of power, whose criterion is what increases conatus, understanding, and the composition of natures.","refs":["CH_QGOH_PQDS:SD1","CH_QGOH_PQ_2:A10","CH_QGOH_PQ_2:SD3"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[]},{"id":"CL_QGOH_05:BSD-06","text":"Implicit politics conceives rational concord as an amplification of singular power, not as suppression of individual conatus.","refs":["CH_QGOH_PQ_2:SD4","CH_QGOH_PQ_2:MRT7"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[]},{"id":"CL_QGOH_05:BSD-07","text":"Freedom becomes a variable degree of adequate causality: the human being is freer insofar as it acts from what it understands.","refs":["CH_QGOH_PQDS:A6","CH_QGOH_PQ_2:A9"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[]},{"id":"CL_QGOH_05:BSD-08","text":"Method must not simply abolish received terms but transfer them from common ontology to causal and practical functions.","refs":["CH_QGOH_PT_5:A12","CH_QGOH_PQDS:MRT7"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[]}],"original_name":"Book-Scale Super-Derivatives"},{"number":"10","name":"Recursive Loops, Escalation Paths, and Conceptual Attractors","items":[{"id":"CL_QGOH_05:RLE-01","text":"A bodily change produces an affective idea, affect determines desire, judgment of the good shapes the direction of desire, and action in turn produces a new bodily constitution.","refs":["CH_QGOH_PT_5:AS5","CH_QGOH_PT_5:A5","CH_QGOH_PQ_2:A4"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[]},{"id":"CL_QGOH_05:RLE-02","text":"An external cause increases passion, passion diminishes one's own power, and diminished power is more easily surpassed by further external causes.","refs":["CH_QGOH_PQDS:A10","CH_QGOH_PQ_2:A6"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[]},{"id":"CL_QGOH_05:RLE-03","text":"Institution joins social judgment to affects, imagined judgment produces Shame or Glory, and the affect thus produced recreates the force of institution.","refs":["CH_QGOH_PT_5:A10","CH_QGOH_PT_5:D6"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[]},{"id":"CL_QGOH_05:RLE-04","text":"Present sweetness restrains the affect of the future, hence reason is followed less, and opinion or lust again amplifies present force.","refs":["CH_QGOH_PQ_2:D2","CH_QGOH_PQ_2:D3"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[]},{"id":"CL_QGOH_05:RLE-05","text":"The circuit of moderation begins when a contrary affect is produced, acquires greater force, and excludes the existence of the prior affect.","refs":["CH_QGOH_PQDS:A11","CH_QGOH_PQDS:D8"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[]},{"id":"CL_QGOH_05:RLE-06","text":"Conatus is an immanent attractor: perseverance defines power, Joy increases it, and virtue expresses the rational form of that same conatus.","refs":["CH_QGOH_PQ_2:A1","CH_QGOH_PQ_2:A2","CH_QGOH_PQ_2:A5"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[]},{"id":"CL_QGOH_05:RLE-07","text":"Rational compatibility composes powers, composition increases common utility, and common utility confirms further concord of reason.","refs":["CH_QGOH_PQ_2:A12","CH_QGOH_PQ_2:D12","CH_QGOH_PQ_2:SD4"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[]},{"id":"CL_QGOH_05:RLE-08","text":"To understand is an ascending attractor that orders certain utility and finds its supreme terminus in knowledge of God.","refs":["CH_QGOH_PQ_2:A10","CH_QGOH_PQ_2:A18","CH_QGOH_PQ_2:SD6"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[]}],"original_name":"Recursive Loops, Escalation Paths, and Conceptual Attractors"},{"number":"11","name":"Formal Generative Schema of the Whole Book","items":[{"id":"CL_QGOH_05:FGS-B01","text":"The necessity of Nature without end excludes final causes and converts perfection and absolute value into comparative relations.","refs":["CH_QGOH_PQDS:A1","CH_QGOH_PQDS:A3","CH_QGOH_PQDS:A5"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[]},{"id":"CL_QGOH_05:FGS-B02","text":"A change in bodily power is affirmed through an idea and, where the idea is confused, takes the form of passion.","refs":["CH_QGOH_PT_5:A3","CH_QGOH_PT_5:A2"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[]},{"id":"CL_QGOH_05:FGS-B03","text":"Joy, Sadness, and Desire provide primary motions, while the many names are generated from added relations.","refs":["CH_QGOH_PT_5:A6","CH_QGOH_PT_5:D8"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[]},{"id":"CL_QGOH_05:FGS-B04","text":"Imagined cause, time, and social relation determine primary motion and produce denominated affects.","refs":["CH_QGOH_PT_5:A7","CH_QGOH_PT_5:A8","CH_QGOH_PT_5:A9"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[]},{"id":"CL_QGOH_05:FGS-B05","text":"Human finitude and the excess of external cause amplify passion into structural servitude.","refs":["CH_QGOH_PQDS:AS3","CH_QGOH_PQDS:A10","CH_QGOH_PQDS:D3"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[]},{"id":"CL_QGOH_05:FGS-B06","text":"Moderation requires that knowledge become a contrary affect of greater power, not the mere correction of a false proposition.","refs":["CH_QGOH_PQDS:A11","CH_QGOH_PQDS:A12","CH_QGOH_PQ_2:D1"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[]},{"id":"CL_QGOH_05:FGS-B07","text":"Conatus does not destroy servitude but provides the principle of restoration, when virtue and Reason order one's own power.","refs":["CH_QGOH_PQ_2:A1","CH_QGOH_PQ_2:A2","CH_QGOH_PQ_2:A10"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[]},{"id":"CL_QGOH_05:FGS-B08","text":"Reason composes the compatibility of natures and directs movement toward common utility and knowledge of God.","refs":["CH_QGOH_PQ_2:A12","CH_QGOH_PQ_2:A17","CH_QGOH_PQ_2:A18"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[]}],"original_name":"Formal Generative Schema of the Whole Book"},{"number":"12","name":"Whole-Book Argument Flow Map","items":[{"id":"CL_QGOH_05:AFM-B01","text":"Nominal plurality of affects → reduction to variations of power → modulation through cause, time, and society → dissolution of finality and absolute values → causal finitude and servitude → affectivization of knowledge → conatus as virtue → rational composition of utilities → knowledge of God as terminal attractor.","refs":["CH_QGOH_PT_5:SD1","CH_QGOH_PQDS:D3","CH_QGOH_PQ_2:SD6"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[]}],"original_name":"Whole-Book Argument Flow Map"},{"number":"13","name":"Macro Map-Effects","items":[{"id":"CL_QGOH_05:MME-01","text":"The psychology of affects is reordered from an inventory of species into a map of relational motions of power.","refs":["CH_QGOH_PT_5:ME1"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[]},{"id":"CL_QGOH_05:MME-02","text":"Moral predicates are reclassified as practical relations of utility, impediment, Joy, and Sadness.","refs":["CH_QGOH_PQDS:ME2"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[]},{"id":"CL_QGOH_05:MME-03","text":"The opposition of true and false ceases to suffice for affective life, because the efficacy of knowledge is distinguished from causal force.","refs":["CH_QGOH_PQDS:ME4","CH_QGOH_PQ_2:ME3"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[]},{"id":"CL_QGOH_05:MME-04","text":"Chronological times become degrees of affective presence, by which an absent thing receives present or diminished force.","refs":["CH_QGOH_PT_5:ME3","CH_QGOH_PQ_2:ME6"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[]},{"id":"CL_QGOH_05:MME-05","text":"Agency is transferred from the free subject to finite conatus, whose virtue is the immanent power of acting.","refs":["CH_QGOH_PT_5:SD5","CH_QGOH_PQDS:ME3"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[]},{"id":"CL_QGOH_05:MME-06","text":"The social field changes from a scene of judgment and emulation into a problem of composition, where another rational human being becomes the most useful singular thing.","refs":["CH_QGOH_PQ_2:SD4","CH_QGOH_PQ_2:ME5"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[]},{"id":"CL_QGOH_05:MME-07","text":"Self-interest and common utility cease to be opposed, because concord can amplify the power of the individual.","refs":["CH_QGOH_PQ_2:SD3","CH_QGOH_PQ_2:D12"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[]},{"id":"CL_QGOH_05:MME-08","text":"The reading of affective vocabulary must investigate changing functions, because the same words are transferred from common usage to immanent calculation.","refs":["CH_QGOH_PT_5:A12","CH_QGOH_PQDS:MRT7"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[]}],"original_name":"Macro Map-Effects"},{"number":"14","name":"Altitude Integration","items":[{"id":"CL_QGOH_05:ALT-B01","text":"At the surface level, the concern is with names of affects, with the human being knowing what is better yet subject to passions, and with the dictates of Reason concerning utility and virtue.","refs":["CH_QGOH_PT_5:ALT1","CH_QGOH_PQDS:ALT1","CH_QGOH_PQ_2:ALT1"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[]},{"id":"CL_QGOH_05:ALT-B02","text":"At the structural level, everything is ordered through bodily changes, partial causality, the proportion of powers, and the compatibility or contrariety of natures.","refs":["CH_QGOH_PT_5:ALT2","CH_QGOH_PQDS:ALT2","CH_QGOH_PQ_2:ALT2"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[]},{"id":"CL_QGOH_05:ALT-B03","text":"At the metastrategic level, moderation passes from moral vocabulary and correct knowledge to the production of a contrary affect and the composition of utilities.","refs":["CH_QGOH_PT_5:ALT3","CH_QGOH_PQDS:ALT3","CH_QGOH_PQ_2:ALT3"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[]},{"id":"CL_QGOH_05:ALT-B04","text":"At the ontological and temporal level, Nature acts necessarily, the subject is finite bodily conatus, and time varies imaginative efficacy.","refs":["CH_QGOH_PT_5:ALT4","CH_QGOH_PQDS:ALT4","CH_QGOH_PQ_2:ALT4"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[]},{"id":"CL_QGOH_05:ALT-B05","text":"At the methodological and performative level, the text itself performs reduction, exclusion, and mutation of terms, and diagnosis of impotence is converted into ethical reconstruction.","refs":["CH_QGOH_PT_5:AS3","CH_QGOH_PQDS:MRT7","CH_QGOH_PQ_2:AS4"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[]}],"original_name":"Altitude Integration"},{"number":"15","name":"Internal Tensions, Mutation Points, and Fault Lines","items":[{"id":"CL_QGOH_05:FL-01","text":"Reduction to three primitive affects coheres with a multitude of unnamed compounds only if primitive signifies a generative principle, not an exhaustive taxonomic genus.","refs":["CH_QGOH_PT_5:A6","CH_QGOH_PT_5:NX9"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"Tension","passages":[]},{"id":"CL_QGOH_05:FL-02","text":"The negation of objective perfection leaves a tension with the retention of the model of human nature and norms of utility.","refs":["CH_QGOH_PQDS:A3","CH_QGOH_PQDS:MRT5"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"Tension","passages":[]},{"id":"CL_QGOH_05:FL-03","text":"Truth has no automatic sovereignty, yet Reason must provide ethical direction; the manner in which knowledge acquires superior force is not yet completed.","refs":["CH_QGOH_PQDS:SD3","CH_QGOH_PQDS:MRT6","CH_QGOH_PQ_2:MRT4"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"Tension","passages":[]},{"id":"CL_QGOH_05:FL-04","text":"The equality in kind of present and absent affect is combined with inequality in vehemence, but the distinction between quality and degree is tacitly maintained.","refs":["CH_QGOH_PT_5:A8","CH_QGOH_PQ_2:A8"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"Tension","passages":[]},{"id":"CL_QGOH_05:FL-05","text":"The ascent to the highest good of knowledge of God introduces an almost teleological form, although Nature intends no external end.","refs":["CH_QGOH_PQDS:A1","CH_QGOH_PQ_2:D9"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"Tension","passages":[]},{"id":"CL_QGOH_05:FL-06","text":"Concord under Reason is said to be necessary, but the transition of passionate human beings to such composition remains causally indeterminate.","refs":["CH_QGOH_PQ_2:A17","CH_QGOH_PQ_2:MRT5","CH_QGOH_PQ_2:MRT7"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"Tension","passages":[]},{"id":"CL_QGOH_05:FL-07","text":"The connection between the bodily definition of affect and social, rational, and normative orders is presupposed more firmly than it is demonstrated at individual stages.","refs":["CH_QGOH_PT_5:MRT4","CH_QGOH_PQ_2:MRT6"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"Tension","passages":[]},{"id":"CL_QGOH_05:MP-01","text":"Desire is the principal point of mutation, as it is converted from a primitive affect into actual essence and the power of acting.","refs":["CH_QGOH_PT_5:A5","CH_QGOH_PQ_2:A1"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"Tension","passages":[]},{"id":"CL_QGOH_05:MP-02","text":"Good and evil change from associations of institution to relations of power and finally to the criterion of understanding.","refs":["CH_QGOH_PT_5:A10","CH_QGOH_PQDS:A5","CH_QGOH_PQ_2:SD2"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"Tension","passages":[]},{"id":"CL_QGOH_05:MP-03","text":"Sociality changes meaning where imagination of another, which produces imitative affects, yields to the compatibility of human beings acting from Reason.","refs":["CH_QGOH_PT_5:A9","CH_QGOH_PQ_2:A16","CH_QGOH_PQ_2:A17"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"Tension","passages":[]},{"id":"CL_QGOH_05:MP-04","text":"Virtue changes from a negative term, by which moderating passions are distinguished, into a positive principle of power and self-preservation.","refs":["CH_QGOH_PT_5:NX7","CH_QGOH_PQDS:A6","CH_QGOH_PQ_2:A2"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"Tension","passages":[]}],"original_name":"Internal Tensions, Mutation Points, and Fault Lines"},{"number":"16","name":"Hidden System Image","items":[{"id":"CL_QGOH_05:HSI-01","text":"Reality is implicitly a network of finite forces, in which no singular thing absolutely dominates and every state arises from a proportion of powers.","refs":["CH_QGOH_PQDS:AS3","CH_QGOH_PQDS:A10"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[]},{"id":"CL_QGOH_05:HSI-02","text":"The subject is a bodily node of external causes and its own conatus, not a free center that establishes affections from nothing.","refs":["CH_QGOH_PT_5:SD5","CH_QGOH_PQ_2:A1"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[]},{"id":"CL_QGOH_05:HSI-03","text":"Time is a topology of affective presence, in which memory and the future can approach the present but do not always retain the same vehemence.","refs":["CH_QGOH_PT_5:SD3","CH_QGOH_PQ_2:A8"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[]},{"id":"CL_QGOH_05:HSI-04","text":"Possible navigation is not flight from Nature, but the production of stronger affects, the composition of powers, and the direction of conatus toward understanding.","refs":["CH_QGOH_PQDS:A11","CH_QGOH_PQ_2:SD4","CH_QGOH_PQ_2:A18"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[]}],"original_name":"Hidden System Image"},{"number":"17","name":"Minimal Whole-Book Reconstruction Test","items":[{"id":"CL_QGOH_05:MRT-B01","text":"The triad of affects, the confused idea of the body, the asymmetry of causal powers, conatus, and Reason suffice to reconstruct the minimal structure of this group.","refs":["CH_QGOH_PT_5:MRT1","CH_QGOH_PQDS:MRT1","CH_QGOH_PQ_2:MRT1"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[]},{"id":"CL_QGOH_05:MRT-B02","text":"The strongest structure is the connection among finitude, the excess of external causes, conatus, virtue, and action from understanding.","refs":["CH_QGOH_PQDS:MRT2","CH_QGOH_PQ_2:MRT2"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[]},{"id":"CL_QGOH_05:MRT-B03","text":"The positive production of a stronger rational affect and the mechanism by which passionate human beings pass to common composition are not yet sufficiently determined.","refs":["CH_QGOH_PQDS:MRT6","CH_QGOH_PQ_2:MRT4","CH_QGOH_PQ_2:MRT5"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[]},{"id":"CL_QGOH_05:MRT-B04","text":"Coherence depends on the return of terms with change of function; local derivations of individual affects and the semantic rule of vocabulary resist complete reduction.","refs":["CH_QGOH_PT_5:MRT3","CH_QGOH_PT_5:MRT5","CH_QGOH_PQDS:MRT7"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[]},{"id":"CL_QGOH_05:MRT-B05","text":"The reconstruction preserves the true movement, because it proceeds from the reduction of affects to the diagnosis of servitude and thence to rational reconstruction, while incomplete normative mediations remain.","refs":["CH_QGOH_PT_5:MRT7","CH_QGOH_PQ_2:MRT6","CH_QGOH_PQ_2:MRT7"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[]}],"original_name":"Minimal Whole-Book Reconstruction Test"}],"revision":"CL_QGOH_05@R001","source_variant_id":"canonical:CL_QGOH_05:v1","source_language_code":"la","original_title":"Chapters 13–15"}],"identity":{"layer":"CL","kicker":"Cluster · Ethica","title":"Chapters 13–15","context":"Part III - On the Origin and Nature of the Emotions · passage 5 · Part IV - Of Human Bondage, or the Strength of the Emotions · passage 1 · Part IV - Of Human Bondage, or the Strength of the Emotions · passage 2","original_title":"Chapters 13–15","original_context":"Pars tertia - De origine et natura affectuum · passage 5 · Pars quarta - De servitute humana seu de affectuum viribus · passage 1 · Pars quarta - De servitute humana seu de affectuum viribus · passage 2"},"original_title":"Chapters 13–15","title_language_code":"en"},{"id":"CL_QGOH_06","layer":"CL","title":"Chapters 16–18","order":6,"parents":["CH_QGOH_PQ_3","CH_QGOH_PQ_4","CH_QGOH_PQ_5"],"revision":"CL_QGOH_06@R001","sha256":"59e2ad9e5b523055405c04d5fb866fcae13a722d45e4da0e01bda4b2c6849556","sections":[{"number":"1","name":"Book-Scale Argumental Spine","items":[{"id":"CL_QGOH_06:AS-B01","text":"Systema quaerit quomodo conatus propriae utilitatis sub necessitate Naturae et imperio affectuum in libertatem communem converti possit sine libero arbitrio aut altruismo sui negante.","refs":["CH_QGOH_PQ_3:AS1","CH_QGOH_PQ_5:AS1"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[]},{"id":"CL_QGOH_06:AS-B02","text":"Libertas constituitur non ut proprietas absoluta individui, sed ut gradus quo homo ex causis adequate intellectis agit intra corpus, societatem et Naturam.","refs":["CH_QGOH_PQ_5:A1","CH_QGOH_PQ_5:SD1"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[]},{"id":"CL_QGOH_06:AS-B03","text":"Fundamentum conversionis est convenientia rationalis qua conservatio sui utilitatem aliorum postulat et bonum communicabile efficit.","refs":["CH_QGOH_PQ_3:AS2","CH_QGOH_PQ_5:A9"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[]},{"id":"CL_QGOH_06:AS-B04","text":"Probatio negativa ostendit hanc convenientiam non sponte dominari, quia potentia causarum externarum homines in passiones, partialitatem et conflictum distribuit.","refs":["CH_QGOH_PQ_3:AS3","CH_QGOH_PQ_5:A7"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[]},{"id":"CL_QGOH_06:AS-B05","text":"Civitas propterea ab apparatu coactionis ad medium libertatis mutatur: primo conflictum continet, deinde condiciones cooperationis rationalis sustentat.","refs":["CH_QGOH_PQ_3:AS4","CH_QGOH_PQ_5:ME4"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[]},{"id":"CL_QGOH_06:AS-B06","text":"Motus argumenti est recursivus et amplificativus: actus a causa sua reclassificatur, causa ad totum corporis refertur, totum ad societatem, societas ad ordinem Naturae.","refs":["CH_QGOH_PQ_4:AS4","CH_QGOH_PQ_5:AS5"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[]},{"id":"CL_QGOH_06:AS-B07","text":"Clausura practica acquiescentiam producit, qua potentia finita suum officium agit et eventus inevitabiles ex necessitate Naturae intellegit.","refs":["CH_QGOH_PQ_5:A17","CH_QGOH_PQ_5:A6"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[]},{"id":"CL_QGOH_06:AS-B08","text":"Clausura absoluta differtur: homo perfecte liber manet terminus hypotheticus, dum libertas actualis per gradus corporales, affectivos, civiles et cognitivos efficitur.","refs":["CH_QGOH_PQ_5:AS2","CH_QGOH_PQ_5:A2"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[]}],"original_name":"Book-Scale Argumental Spine"},{"number":"2","name":"Global Load-Bearing Axioms","items":[{"id":"CL_QGOH_06:GA-01","text":"[Persistent] Conatus fit virtus quatenus conservatio sui ex natura proprie intellecta procedit et in actionem adequate causatam transit.","refs":["CH_QGOH_PQ_3:A1","CH_QGOH_PQ_5:A4"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[]},{"id":"CL_QGOH_06:GA-02","text":"[Persistent] Utilitas rationalis singularis structuram non-rivalem habet, quia potentiam propriam per utilitatem mutuam et bonum communicabile auget.","refs":["CH_QGOH_PQ_3:A2","CH_QGOH_PQ_3:A4"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[]},{"id":"CL_QGOH_06:GA-03","text":"[Mutating] Ratio ab intellectu normae in cupiditatem activam, amorem, generositatem et institutionem transit; tantum sic vim affectivam acquirit.","refs":["CH_QGOH_PQ_3:A5","CH_QGOH_PQ_5:DNX6"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[]},{"id":"CL_QGOH_06:GA-04","text":"[Persistent] Servitus oritur ubi determinatio externa, cognitio inadaequata aut affectus partialis potentiam agendi definit.","refs":["CH_QGOH_PQ_4:A5","CH_QGOH_PQ_5:A7"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[]},{"id":"CL_QGOH_06:GA-05","text":"[Persistent] Valor affectus aut rei ex effectu in potentiam integratam corporis, multiplicitatem perceptionis et conservationem totius hominis iudicatur.","refs":["CH_QGOH_PQ_3:A11","CH_QGOH_PQ_5:A13"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[]},{"id":"CL_QGOH_06:GA-06","text":"[Mutating] Civitas incipit ut securitas minis firmata, sed per iura communia, fidem et cooperationem in infrastructuram libertatis rationalis convertitur.","refs":["CH_QGOH_PQ_3:A7","CH_QGOH_PQ_5:A11"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[]},{"id":"CL_QGOH_06:GA-07","text":"[Mutating] Bonum et malum ab effectibus potentiae ad relationes comparativas vitae finitae transferuntur, dum iustum et iniustum institutionem civilem requirunt.","refs":["CH_QGOH_PQ_3:A8","CH_QGOH_PQ_4:A13"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[]},{"id":"CL_QGOH_06:GA-08","text":"[Emergent] Libertas est relatio immanens inter causalitatem propriam, corpus aptum, affectus ordinatos, societatem fidelem et necessitatem intellectam.","refs":["CH_QGOH_PQ_5:SD1","CH_QGOH_PQ_5:SD6"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[]},{"id":"CL_QGOH_06:GA-09","text":"[Emergent] Nutritio, res externae, labor, commutatio et cura publica non sunt adiuncta ethicae, sed condiciones materiales cognitionis et libertatis.","refs":["CH_QGOH_PQ_5:SD4","CH_QGOH_PQ_5:ME6"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[]},{"id":"CL_QGOH_06:GA-10","text":"[Attractor] Cognitio Dei et intellecta necessitas Naturae convergunt in acquiescentiam qua conatus finitus ordinem totius affirmative participat.","refs":["CH_QGOH_PQ_3:A3","CH_QGOH_PQ_5:A17"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[]},{"id":"CL_QGOH_06:GA-11","text":"[Terminal] In termino hominis perfecte liberi bonum et malum functionem amittunt, quamvis ad navigationem vitae finitae necessaria maneant.","refs":["CH_QGOH_PQ_4:AS6","CH_QGOH_PQ_5:SNX2"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[]}],"original_name":"Global Load-Bearing Axioms"},{"number":"3","name":"Recurrence, Mutation, and Displacement Matrix","items":[{"id":"CL_QGOH_06:RMD-01","text":"Conatus identitatem structuralem servat, sed ab auto-conservatione singulari ad cooperationem, fidem et conservationem communem amplificatur.","refs":["CH_QGOH_PQ_5:A3","CH_QGOH_PQ_5:A9"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[]},{"id":"CL_QGOH_06:RMD-02","text":"Ratio functionaliter redit: primo convenientiam demonstrat, deinde causam actionis qualificat, postremo affectus, institutiones et condiciones materiales ordinat.","refs":["CH_QGOH_PQ_3:AS2","CH_QGOH_PQ_4:SD1","CH_QGOH_PQ_5:SD2"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[]},{"id":"CL_QGOH_06:RMD-03","text":"Civitas mutatur ab exteriori coactione affectuum in medium internum libertatis, neque tamen originem coercitivam omnino deponit.","refs":["CH_QGOH_PQ_3:AS4","CH_QGOH_PQ_5:NX7"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[]},{"id":"CL_QGOH_06:RMD-04","text":"Bonum et malum a nominibus effectuum ad comparationes rationales migrant atque in libertate perfecta terminaliter evanescunt.","refs":["CH_QGOH_PQ_4:A13","CH_QGOH_PQ_4:SD5"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[]},{"id":"CL_QGOH_06:RMD-05","text":"Corpus ab identitate per rationem motus et quietis definita ad infrastructuram nutritionis, cognitionis, laboris et societatis extenditur.","refs":["CH_QGOH_PQ_3:A10","CH_QGOH_PQ_5:SD4"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[]},{"id":"CL_QGOH_06:RMD-06","text":"Metus ab instrumento initiali securitatis ad formam concordiae infidae deicitur; amor, generositas et fides eius functionem stabilientem absorbent.","refs":["CH_QGOH_PQ_3:A13","CH_QGOH_PQ_5:A16"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[]},{"id":"CL_QGOH_06:RMD-07","text":"Affectus particulares post reclassificationem evanescunt ut indices morales autonomi et redeunt ut modi causales augmenti, diminutionis vel transitionis potentiae.","refs":["CH_QGOH_PQ_3:AS5","CH_QGOH_PQ_4:ME1"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[]}],"original_name":"Recurrence, Mutation, and Displacement Matrix"},{"number":"4","name":"Lower-Layer vs Current-Level Distinction","items":[{"id":"CL_QGOH_06:CGD-01","text":"Discrimen inter homines et bruta localiter terminum reciprocitatis designat, sed structuram libertatis praesentis gradus non generat nisi ut exemplum utilitatis secundum naturae convenientiam.","refs":["CH_QGOH_PQ_3:A15","CH_QGOH_PQ_3:D11"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[]},{"id":"CL_QGOH_06:CGD-02","text":"Criterium mortis per mutationem formae corporis manet locale; sursum persistit tantum principium quod integritas relationalis, non species exterior, potentiam constituit.","refs":["CH_QGOH_PQ_3:A10","CH_QGOH_PQ_3:SD6"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[]},{"id":"CL_QGOH_06:CGD-03","text":"Casus risus, commiserationis, fugae, beneficii, gratiae et pecuniae non amplificantur singillatim; coniunguntur sub regula qua actus ex causa, toto et effectu aestimatur.","refs":["CH_QGOH_PQ_4:NX6","CH_QGOH_PQ_5:ME3"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[]},{"id":"CL_QGOH_06:CGD-04","text":"Doctrina affectus affectu fortiore coercendi transitum efficit inter diagnosin servitutis et constructionem cupiditatum activarum atque institutionum efficacium.","refs":["CH_QGOH_PQ_5:A8","CH_QGOH_PQ_4:D10"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[]},{"id":"CL_QGOH_06:CGD-05","text":"Institutio civilis unitates coniungit, quia eadem potestas quae conflictum coërcet iura, cooperationem materialem et libertatem gradualem sustinere potest.","refs":["CH_QGOH_PQ_3:D5","CH_QGOH_PQ_5:D5"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[]},{"id":"CL_QGOH_06:CGD-06","text":"Moralitas per tristitiam, culpam et opinionem obsolescit ut codex virtutis, sed conditionaliter servatur ut disciplina transitoria multitudinis nondum rationalis.","refs":["CH_QGOH_PQ_4:A16","CH_QGOH_PQ_4:MRT5"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[]}],"original_name":"Lower-Layer vs Current-Level Distinction"},{"number":"5","name":"Global Apophatic Structure","items":[{"id":"CL_QGOH_06:GNX-01","text":"Utilitas propria rationalis non est possessio competitiva, quia potentiam singularis per bonum communicabile et mutuum auxilium constituit.","refs":["CH_QGOH_PQ_3:NX1","CH_QGOH_PQ_5:SNX3"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"Negative","passages":[]},{"id":"CL_QGOH_06:GNX-02","text":"Virtus non oritur ex tristitia, metu, paenitentia, abiectione aut odio, quamvis haec affectus ad disciplinam transitoriam adhiberi possint.","refs":["CH_QGOH_PQ_4:AS2","CH_QGOH_PQ_5:NX9"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"Negative","passages":[]},{"id":"CL_QGOH_06:GNX-03","text":"Libertas non est arbitrium indeterminatum, solitudo aut exemptio a Naturae necessitate, sed actio relativa ex causis propriis intellectis.","refs":["CH_QGOH_PQ_5:NX1","CH_QGOH_PQ_5:ME1"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"Negative","passages":[]},{"id":"CL_QGOH_06:GNX-04","text":"Civitas rationalis non reducitur ad metum nec libertati opponitur, quia concordia fidelis iura communia et cooperationem requirit.","refs":["CH_QGOH_PQ_5:NX7","CH_QGOH_PQ_5:ME4"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"Negative","passages":[]},{"id":"CL_QGOH_06:GNX-05","text":"Bonum, malum, iustum et iniustum non sunt genera absoluta rerum, sed relationes potentiae, cognitionis aut institutionis.","refs":["CH_QGOH_PQ_3:A8","CH_QGOH_PQ_5:NX2"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"Negative","passages":[]}],"original_name":"Global Apophatic Structure"},{"number":"6","name":"Deep Book Apophatics","items":[{"id":"CL_QGOH_06:DBN-01","text":"Falsum esse oportet intellectum abstractum sine affectu activo aut mediatione institutionali posse passionum potentiam superare.","refs":["CH_QGOH_PQ_3:A5","CH_QGOH_PQ_5:DNX6"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"Negative","passages":[]},{"id":"CL_QGOH_06:DBN-02","text":"Falsum esse oportet individuum corpore, rebus externis et aliis hominibus carere posse quin facultas cognoscendi simul minuatur.","refs":["CH_QGOH_PQ_5:DNX3","CH_QGOH_PQ_5:DNX4"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"Negative","passages":[]},{"id":"CL_QGOH_06:DBN-03","text":"Falsum esse oportet speciem externam actus eius valorem determinare, quia servitus et libertas in modo causationis eiusdem actus distinguuntur.","refs":["CH_QGOH_PQ_4:A7","CH_QGOH_PQ_4:D6"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"Negative","passages":[]},{"id":"CL_QGOH_06:DBN-04","text":"Falsum esse oportet ordinem moralem transcendentem inter potentiam naturalem et institutionem civilem intervenire, quia normae ex relationibus immanentibus generantur.","refs":["CH_QGOH_PQ_3:A6","CH_QGOH_PQ_3:SNX1"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"Negative","passages":[]}],"original_name":"Deep Book Apophatics"},{"number":"7","name":"Super-Apophatics of the Whole","items":[{"id":"CL_QGOH_06:BSA-01","text":"Systema circa absentiam subiecti extra Naturam ordinatur: nulla potentia humana causationem totius transcendere aut sibi originem absolutam tribuere potest.","refs":["CH_QGOH_PQ_5:DNX1","CH_QGOH_PQ_5:SNX1"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"Negative","passages":[]},{"id":"CL_QGOH_06:BSA-02","text":"Possibilitas inadmissibilis est libertas perpetua sine passione, corpore, societate aut exterioribus causis; homo perfecte liber tantum attractor hypotheticus manet.","refs":["CH_QGOH_PQ_5:A2","CH_QGOH_PQ_5:SNX2"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"Negative","passages":[]},{"id":"CL_QGOH_06:BSA-03","text":"Communitas immediata hominum iam rationalium excluditur; omnis communitas actualis mediationes affectivas, iuridicas et materiales requirit.","refs":["CH_QGOH_PQ_5:A15","CH_QGOH_PQ_5:SNX4"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"Negative","passages":[]}],"original_name":"Super-Apophatics of the Whole"},{"number":"8","name":"Cross-Unit Derivative Propositions","items":[{"id":"CL_QGOH_06:XD-01","text":"Conservatio sui sub necessitate Naturae fit principium cooperationis, quia singulare potentiam suam non auget nisi bonum communicabile et auxilia communia producat.","refs":["CL_QGOH_06:GA-01","CL_QGOH_06:GA-02","CL_QGOH_06:GA-08","CH_QGOH_PQ_3:A2","CH_QGOH_PQ_5:A9"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"Composite","passages":[]},{"id":"CL_QGOH_06:XD-02","text":"Quoniam intellectus nudus affectum non coërcet, actio rationalis efficax debet formam cupiditatis activae, amoris, generositatis aut institutionis induere.","refs":["CL_QGOH_06:GA-03","CL_QGOH_06:GA-04","CH_QGOH_PQ_3:A5","CH_QGOH_PQ_5:A8"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"Tension","passages":[]},{"id":"CL_QGOH_06:XD-03","text":"Civitas est convertor causalis: vim externam ad securitatem adhibet, securitatem in cooperationem transfert, cooperationem autem in maiorem causalitatem propriam civium.","refs":["CL_QGOH_06:GA-06","CL_QGOH_06:GA-08","CH_QGOH_PQ_3:AS4","CH_QGOH_PQ_5:A11"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"Composite","passages":[]},{"id":"CL_QGOH_06:XD-04","text":"Libertas requirit ecologiam potentiae in qua integritas corporis, res externae, nutritio, labor, commutatio et iura communia mutuo se sustentant.","refs":["CL_QGOH_06:GA-05","CL_QGOH_06:GA-08","CL_QGOH_06:GA-09","CH_QGOH_PQ_5:A13","CH_QGOH_PQ_5:SD4"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"Composite","passages":[]},{"id":"CL_QGOH_06:XD-05","text":"Bonum et malum sunt instrumenta navigationis potentiae finitae: eo magis necessaria sunt quo minus homo liber est, atque attractore libertatis approximato recedunt.","refs":["CL_QGOH_06:GA-07","CL_QGOH_06:GA-11","CH_QGOH_PQ_4:SD5","CH_QGOH_PQ_5:MRT7"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"Composite","passages":[]},{"id":"CL_QGOH_06:XD-06","text":"Iustitia civiliter instituta potest rationem habere sine lege transcendente, si fides, aequitas et communis utilitas ipsas condiciones cooperationis conservant.","refs":["CL_QGOH_06:GA-02","CL_QGOH_06:GA-06","CL_QGOH_06:GA-07","CH_QGOH_PQ_3:A8","CH_QGOH_PQ_5:A10"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"Composite","passages":[]},{"id":"CL_QGOH_06:XD-07","text":"Cognitio Dei et cognitio necessitatis Naturae eundem attractorem practicalem efficiunt: cupiditatem communicandi bonum et acquiescentiam intra limites potentiae finitae.","refs":["CL_QGOH_06:GA-02","CL_QGOH_06:GA-10","CH_QGOH_PQ_3:A4","CH_QGOH_PQ_5:A17"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"Composite","passages":[]}],"original_name":"Cross-Unit Derivative Propositions"},{"number":"9","name":"Book-Scale Super-Derivatives","items":[{"id":"CL_QGOH_06:BSD-01","text":"Subiectum liberum non est substantia sui iuris, sed nexus relationum qui eo magis agit quo plures determinationes corporales, affectivas et sociales adequate ordinat.","refs":["CL_QGOH_06:GA-04","CL_QGOH_06:GA-05","CL_QGOH_06:GA-08","CH_QGOH_PQ_5:SD1","CH_QGOH_PQ_5:ALT2"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"Composite","passages":[]},{"id":"CL_QGOH_06:BSD-02","text":"Politica fit ars condiciones causalitatis propriae distribuendi, non simplex ars vetandi aut pacem metu imponendi.","refs":["CL_QGOH_06:GA-03","CL_QGOH_06:GA-06","CL_QGOH_06:GA-09","CH_QGOH_PQ_5:A16","CH_QGOH_PQ_5:SD3"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"Composite","passages":[]},{"id":"CL_QGOH_06:BSD-03","text":"Tempus ethicum duplicatur: imaginatio praesentia partialiter ponderat, dum Ratio utilitatem totius trans tempora ordinat quamvis durationem exacte cognoscere nequeat.","refs":["CL_QGOH_06:GA-04","CL_QGOH_06:GA-07","CH_QGOH_PQ_4:A14","CH_QGOH_PQ_4:SD3"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"Tension","passages":[]},{"id":"CL_QGOH_06:BSD-04","text":"Ethica fit diagnostica causalis: non quaerit quid actus externe significet, sed quae determinatio potentiam totius augeat et quae dependentiam externam reproducat.","refs":["CL_QGOH_06:GA-01","CL_QGOH_06:GA-04","CL_QGOH_06:GA-05","CH_QGOH_PQ_4:A7","CH_QGOH_PQ_4:ME2"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"Composite","passages":[]},{"id":"CL_QGOH_06:BSD-05","text":"Clausura systematis est resilientia rationalis potius quam dominium: libertas augetur per cooperationem et intellectum necessitatis, non per abolitionem exteriorum causarum.","refs":["CL_QGOH_06:GA-06","CL_QGOH_06:GA-08","CL_QGOH_06:GA-10","CH_QGOH_PQ_5:D10","CH_QGOH_PQ_5:SD6"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"Composite","passages":[]}],"original_name":"Book-Scale Super-Derivatives"},{"number":"10","name":"Recursive Loops, Escalation Paths, and Conceptual Attractors","items":[{"id":"CL_QGOH_06:RLE-01","text":"Conatus rationalis → utilitas mutua → societas → maior potentia singularis → amplificatio conatus rationalis.","refs":["CH_QGOH_PQ_3:A2","CH_QGOH_PQ_5:A9"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[]},{"id":"CL_QGOH_06:RLE-02","text":"Conflictus affectivus → metus et coactio → securitas civilis → cooperatio fidelis → imminutio necessitatis metus.","refs":["CH_QGOH_PQ_3:D5","CH_QGOH_PQ_5:A16"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[]},{"id":"CL_QGOH_06:RLE-03","text":"Aptitudo corporis → necessitas rerum externarum → labor et commutatio → interdependentia socialis → maior aptitudo corporis et Mentis.","refs":["CH_QGOH_PQ_5:A13","CH_QGOH_PQ_5:SD4"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[]},{"id":"CL_QGOH_06:RLE-04","text":"Idea adequata → laetitia activa → cupiditas rationalis → actio augens potentiam → maior facultas idearum adequatarum.","refs":["CH_QGOH_PQ_4:A10","CH_QGOH_PQ_4:FGS4"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[]},{"id":"CL_QGOH_06:RLE-05","text":"Comparatio boni et mali → electio utilitatis integrae → libertas maior → minor dependentia a comparatione → attractio ad terminum ultra bonum et malum.","refs":["CH_QGOH_PQ_4:A13","CH_QGOH_PQ_4:SD5"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[]}],"original_name":"Recursive Loops, Escalation Paths, and Conceptual Attractors"},{"number":"11","name":"Formal Generative Schema of the Whole Book","items":[{"id":"CL_QGOH_06:FGS-B01","text":"Necessitas Naturae generat conatum finitum; conatus ex cognitione adequata fit actio, ex cognitione inadaequata passio.","refs":["CH_QGOH_PQ_5:A3","CH_QGOH_PQ_5:A4"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[]},{"id":"CL_QGOH_06:FGS-B02","text":"Actio rationalis propriam conservationem quaerit; convenientia humana hanc conservationem in bonum commune non-rivale amplificat.","refs":["CH_QGOH_PQ_3:A1","CH_QGOH_PQ_3:A2"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[]},{"id":"CL_QGOH_06:FGS-B03","text":"Superioritas causarum externarum perturbat convenientiam; affectus contrarii et Civitas intervallum inter normam rationalem et multitudinem actualem mediantur.","refs":["CH_QGOH_PQ_5:A7","CH_QGOH_PQ_5:A8"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[]},{"id":"CL_QGOH_06:FGS-B04","text":"Civitas securitatem in iura communia, fidem et cooperationem convertit; hae structurae causalitatem propriam civium augent.","refs":["CH_QGOH_PQ_3:A7","CH_QGOH_PQ_5:D4"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[]},{"id":"CL_QGOH_06:FGS-B05","text":"Corpus multiplex res externas et mutuas operas postulat; oeconomia moderata libertatem cognitionis materialiter sustinet.","refs":["CH_QGOH_PQ_5:SD4","CH_QGOH_PQ_5:NX10"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[]},{"id":"CL_QGOH_06:FGS-B06","text":"Potentia sic aucta necessitatem suam intelligit et ad acquiescentiam tendit, dum libertas perfecta terminus regulativus potius quam status empiricus manet.","refs":["CH_QGOH_PQ_5:A17","CH_QGOH_PQ_5:MRT3"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[]}],"original_name":"Formal Generative Schema of the Whole Book"},{"number":"12","name":"Whole-Book Argument Flow Map","items":[{"id":"CL_QGOH_06:AFM-B01","text":"Conatus necessarius → utilitas propria → convenientia rationalis → bonum communicabile.","refs":["CH_QGOH_PQ_3:AS1","CH_QGOH_PQ_3:A3"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[]},{"id":"CL_QGOH_06:AFM-B02","text":"Imperium affectuum → conflictus et partialitas → affectus contrarii → securitas civilis.","refs":["CH_QGOH_PQ_3:AS3","CH_QGOH_PQ_5:A8"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[]},{"id":"CL_QGOH_06:AFM-B03","text":"Securitas civilis → iura communia → fides et cooperatio → libertas socialiter amplificata.","refs":["CH_QGOH_PQ_3:A7","CH_QGOH_PQ_5:A11"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[]},{"id":"CL_QGOH_06:AFM-B04","text":"Integritas corporis → res externae et oeconomia → potentia cognitiva → moderatio affectuum.","refs":["CH_QGOH_PQ_5:A13","CH_QGOH_PQ_5:ME6"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[]},{"id":"CL_QGOH_06:AFM-B05","text":"Moderatio affectuum → causalitas propria → intellectus necessitatis → acquiescentia → approximatio libertatis sine exemptione naturali.","refs":["CH_QGOH_PQ_5:AS3","CH_QGOH_PQ_5:ME7"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[]}],"original_name":"Whole-Book Argument Flow Map"},{"number":"13","name":"Macro Map-Effects","items":[{"id":"CL_QGOH_06:MME-01","text":"Oppositio inter utilitatem propriam et commune bonum corruit; discrimen verum ponitur inter conatum adequate ordinatum et conatum ab externis causis captum.","refs":["CH_QGOH_PQ_3:ME1","CH_QGOH_PQ_5:A4"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[]},{"id":"CL_QGOH_06:MME-02","text":"Oppositio inter Civitatem et libertatem corruit; discrimen transfertur ad Civitatem quae metum tantum administrat et Civitatem quae potentiam communem auget.","refs":["CH_QGOH_PQ_5:NX7","CH_QGOH_PQ_5:ME4"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[]},{"id":"CL_QGOH_06:MME-03","text":"Moralitas actuum exteriorum reordinatur in analysin causarum, adequationis, totius corporis et effectuum socialium.","refs":["CH_QGOH_PQ_4:D6","CH_QGOH_PQ_4:ME1"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[]},{"id":"CL_QGOH_06:MME-04","text":"Corpus et oeconomia ex adiunctis inferioribus in condiciones constitutivas intellectus, cooperationis et libertatis transferuntur.","refs":["CH_QGOH_PQ_5:SD4","CH_QGOH_PQ_5:ME6"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[]},{"id":"CL_QGOH_06:MME-05","text":"Necessitas reclassificatur ab externo limite libertatis in campum immanentem intra quem causalitas propria, prudentia et acquiescentia augeri possunt.","refs":["CH_QGOH_PQ_5:SD6","CH_QGOH_PQ_5:ME7"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[]}],"original_name":"Macro Map-Effects"},{"number":"14","name":"Altitude Integration","items":[{"id":"CL_QGOH_06:ALT-B01","text":"Gradus superficiei affectus, actiones, beneficia, pericula, leges, alimenta et pecuniam examinat ut casus vitae sub servitute et libertate.","refs":["CH_QGOH_PQ_4:ALT1","CH_QGOH_PQ_5:ALT1"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[]},{"id":"CL_QGOH_06:ALT-B02","text":"Gradus structuralis omnes casus secundum causalitatem actionis, potentiam corporis, adequationem cognitionis et mediationem socialem distribuit.","refs":["CH_QGOH_PQ_4:ALT2","CH_QGOH_PQ_5:ALT2"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[]},{"id":"CL_QGOH_06:ALT-B03","text":"Gradus metastrategicus certamen morale in artem producendi affectus activos, fidem, cooperationem et condiciones libertatis convertit.","refs":["CH_QGOH_PQ_4:ALT3","CH_QGOH_PQ_5:ALT3"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[]},{"id":"CL_QGOH_06:ALT-B04","text":"Gradus ontologicus et temporalis hominem ut partem Naturae necessario determinatam constituit, cuius Ratio utilitatem trans tempora ordinat.","refs":["CH_QGOH_PQ_5:A2","CH_QGOH_PQ_4:SD3"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[]},{"id":"CL_QGOH_06:ALT-B05","text":"Gradus methodologicus reclassificationes suas performat: eundem actum inter passionem et actionem transfert atque Civitatem ex limite in medium libertatis mutat.","refs":["CH_QGOH_PQ_4:NX6","CH_QGOH_PQ_5:ME4"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[]}],"original_name":"Altitude Integration"},{"number":"15","name":"Internal Tensions, Mutation Points, and Fault Lines","items":[{"id":"CL_QGOH_06:FL-01","text":"Ratio dicitur affectus sola coërcere non posse, sed omnes actiones passionum producere posse; medium quo idea adequata vim affectivam fortiorem acquirit imperfecte explicatur.","refs":["CH_QGOH_PQ_3:A5","CH_QGOH_PQ_4:D7"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"Tension","passages":[]},{"id":"CL_QGOH_06:FL-02","text":"Civitas simul minis fundatur et libertatem perficit; transitus a coactione externa ad cooperationem rationalem non ex sola securitate deducitur.","refs":["CH_QGOH_PQ_3:MRT3","CH_QGOH_PQ_5:MRT4"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"Tension","passages":[]},{"id":"CL_QGOH_06:FL-03","text":"Ratio bona futura ponderat, quamvis cognitio humana durationis necessario inadaequata sit; prudentia temporalis igitur certitudine quam non possidet operari debet.","refs":["CH_QGOH_PQ_4:D9","CH_QGOH_PQ_4:MRT4"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"Tension","passages":[]},{"id":"CL_QGOH_06:FL-04","text":"Actiones Ratione definitae semper bonae dicuntur, sed homo perfecte liber boni et mali conceptus non format; distinctio perspectivarum manet implicita.","refs":["CH_QGOH_PQ_5:A5","CH_QGOH_PQ_5:MRT7"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"Tension","passages":[]},{"id":"CL_QGOH_06:MP-01","text":"Utilitas mutatur a conservatione singularis in structuram communem quae corpus, cognitionem, cooperationem et iura simul conservat.","refs":["CH_QGOH_PQ_3:A1","CH_QGOH_PQ_5:SD4"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[]},{"id":"CL_QGOH_06:MP-02","text":"Libertas mutatur ab actione ex propria natura in gradum socialiter et materialiter sustentatum causalitatis adequatae.","refs":["CH_QGOH_PQ_4:A1","CH_QGOH_PQ_5:SD1"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[]},{"id":"CL_QGOH_06:MP-03","text":"Necessitas mutatur ab indice potentiae externae in obiectum intellectus quod acquiescentiam et libertatem relativam efficit.","refs":["CH_QGOH_PQ_5:A7","CH_QGOH_PQ_5:SD6"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[]},{"id":"CL_QGOH_06:MP-04","text":"Bonum et malum mutantur a criteriis potentiae in instrumenta comparativa et tandem in categorias terminaliter superfluas.","refs":["CH_QGOH_PQ_3:A11","CH_QGOH_PQ_4:SD5"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[]}],"original_name":"Internal Tensions, Mutation Points, and Fault Lines"},{"number":"16","name":"Hidden System Image","items":[{"id":"CL_QGOH_06:HSI-01","text":"Realitas implicite est reticulum virium in quo corpora, affectus, institutiones et res externae potentiam augent, minuunt aut ad novos circuitus transferunt.","refs":["CH_QGOH_PQ_3:A11","CH_QGOH_PQ_5:ALT2"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[]},{"id":"CL_QGOH_06:HSI-02","text":"Subiectum est nodus permeabilis qui libertatem non possidet, sed eam per ideas adequatas, affectus activos et relationes fideles componit.","refs":["CH_QGOH_PQ_5:A1","CH_QGOH_PQ_5:SD1"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[]},{"id":"CL_QGOH_06:HSI-03","text":"Tempus systematis duplex est: imaginatio praesentia partialiter amplificat, Ratio autem seriem utilitatum sub specie necessitatis ordinat.","refs":["CH_QGOH_PQ_4:A9","CH_QGOH_PQ_4:A14"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[]},{"id":"CL_QGOH_06:HSI-04","text":"Navigatio possibilis non est fuga extra Naturam, sed transitus inter determinationes quo homo conflictum in cooperationem et necessitatem in acquiescentiam convertit.","refs":["CH_QGOH_PQ_5:SNX1","CH_QGOH_PQ_5:ME7"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[]}],"original_name":"Hidden System Image"},{"number":"17","name":"Minimal Whole-Book Reconstruction Test","items":[{"id":"CL_QGOH_06:MRT-B01","text":"Axiomata de conatu, adequatione, potentia affectuum, corpore, Civitate et necessitate Naturae sufficiunt ad machinam huius gradus restituendam.","refs":["CH_QGOH_PQ_5:MRT1","CH_QGOH_PQ_4:MRT1"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[]},{"id":"CL_QGOH_06:MRT-B02","text":"Structura firmissima est circuitus quo utilitas rationalis cooperationem generat, cooperatio potentiam singularis auget, et potentia aucta maiorem actionem rationalem permittit.","refs":["CH_QGOH_PQ_3:A2","CH_QGOH_PQ_5:MRT2"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[]},{"id":"CL_QGOH_06:MRT-B03","text":"Minus determinata manent productio affectus rationalis efficacis et conversio Civitatis coercitivae in ordinem libertatem re vera sustinentem.","refs":["CH_QGOH_PQ_5:MRT6","CH_QGOH_PQ_5:MRT4"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[]},{"id":"CL_QGOH_06:MRT-B04","text":"Restitutio verum motum servat si libertatem non ut statum terminalem, sed ut amplificationem recursivam potentiae corporalis, socialis et cognitivae sub necessitate exhibet.","refs":["CH_QGOH_PQ_5:MRT8","CH_QGOH_PQ_5:SD6"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[]}],"original_name":"Minimal Whole-Book Reconstruction Test"}],"language_code":"la","language_name":"Latina","translations":[{"id":"CAREVAR_20261015T015659759902Z_2074c9d3","language_code":"en","language_name":"English","title":"Chapters 16–18","source_sha256":"59e2ad9e5b523055405c04d5fb866fcae13a722d45e4da0e01bda4b2c6849556","sha256":"500d03e693f000612710a4b2c67fd875ea6f92de25c43ab8512046db49a09b61","rendition_type":"machine","review_status":"machine","model":"gpt-5.6-terra","created":"2026-10-15T01:56:59.763250+00:00","sections":[{"number":"1","name":"Book-Scale Argumental Spine","items":[{"id":"CL_QGOH_06:AS-B01","text":"The system asks how the striving for one's own advantage, under the necessity of Nature and the dominion of the affects, can be converted into common freedom without free will or self-denying altruism.","refs":["CH_QGOH_PQ_3:AS1","CH_QGOH_PQ_5:AS1"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[]},{"id":"CL_QGOH_06:AS-B02","text":"Freedom is constituted not as an absolute property of the individual, but as the degree to which a human being acts from adequately understood causes within body, society, and Nature.","refs":["CH_QGOH_PQ_5:A1","CH_QGOH_PQ_5:SD1"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[]},{"id":"CL_QGOH_06:AS-B03","text":"The foundation of the conversion is rational agreement, by which self-preservation requires the advantage of others and produces a communicable good.","refs":["CH_QGOH_PQ_3:AS2","CH_QGOH_PQ_5:A9"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[]},{"id":"CL_QGOH_06:AS-B04","text":"The negative proof shows that this agreement does not spontaneously prevail, because the power of external causes distributes human beings into passions, partiality, and conflict.","refs":["CH_QGOH_PQ_3:AS3","CH_QGOH_PQ_5:A7"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[]},{"id":"CL_QGOH_06:AS-B05","text":"The State is therefore transformed from an apparatus of coercion into a means of freedom: first it contains conflict, then it sustains the conditions of rational cooperation.","refs":["CH_QGOH_PQ_3:AS4","CH_QGOH_PQ_5:ME4"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[]},{"id":"CL_QGOH_06:AS-B06","text":"The movement of the argument is recursive and amplificatory: an act is reclassified from its cause, the cause is referred to the whole of the body, the whole to society, society to the order of Nature.","refs":["CH_QGOH_PQ_4:AS4","CH_QGOH_PQ_5:AS5"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[]},{"id":"CL_QGOH_06:AS-B07","text":"The practical closure produces acquiescence, by which finite power performs its office and understands inevitable events from the necessity of Nature.","refs":["CH_QGOH_PQ_5:A17","CH_QGOH_PQ_5:A6"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[]},{"id":"CL_QGOH_06:AS-B08","text":"Absolute closure is deferred: the perfectly free human being remains a hypothetical limit, while actual freedom is effected through bodily, affective, civil, and cognitive degrees.","refs":["CH_QGOH_PQ_5:AS2","CH_QGOH_PQ_5:A2"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[]}],"original_name":"Book-Scale Argumental Spine"},{"number":"2","name":"Global Load-Bearing Axioms","items":[{"id":"CL_QGOH_06:GA-01","text":"[Persistent] Conatus becomes virtue insofar as self-preservation proceeds from properly understood nature and passes into adequately caused action.","refs":["CH_QGOH_PQ_3:A1","CH_QGOH_PQ_5:A4"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[]},{"id":"CL_QGOH_06:GA-02","text":"[Persistent] Rational individual advantage has a non-rival structure, because it increases its own power through mutual advantage and a communicable good.","refs":["CH_QGOH_PQ_3:A2","CH_QGOH_PQ_3:A4"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[]},{"id":"CL_QGOH_06:GA-03","text":"[Mutating] Reason passes from the understanding of a norm into active desire, love, generosity, and institution; only thus does it acquire affective force.","refs":["CH_QGOH_PQ_3:A5","CH_QGOH_PQ_5:DNX6"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[]},{"id":"CL_QGOH_06:GA-04","text":"[Persistent] Bondage arises where external determination, inadequate knowledge, or a partial affect defines the power of acting.","refs":["CH_QGOH_PQ_4:A5","CH_QGOH_PQ_5:A7"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[]},{"id":"CL_QGOH_06:GA-05","text":"[Persistent] The value of an affect or thing is judged from its effect upon the integrated power of the body, the multiplicity of perception, and the preservation of the whole human being.","refs":["CH_QGOH_PQ_3:A11","CH_QGOH_PQ_5:A13"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[]},{"id":"CL_QGOH_06:GA-06","text":"[Mutating] The State begins as security secured by threats, but through common rights, trust, and cooperation is converted into an infrastructure of rational freedom.","refs":["CH_QGOH_PQ_3:A7","CH_QGOH_PQ_5:A11"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[]},{"id":"CL_QGOH_06:GA-07","text":"[Mutating] Good and evil are transferred from effects of power to comparative relations of finite life, while just and unjust require civil institution.","refs":["CH_QGOH_PQ_3:A8","CH_QGOH_PQ_4:A13"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[]},{"id":"CL_QGOH_06:GA-08","text":"[Emergent] Freedom is an immanent relation among proper causality, an apt body, ordered affects, trustworthy society, and understood necessity.","refs":["CH_QGOH_PQ_5:SD1","CH_QGOH_PQ_5:SD6"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[]},{"id":"CL_QGOH_06:GA-09","text":"[Emergent] Nourishment, external things, labor, exchange, and public care are not adjuncts to ethics, but material conditions of knowledge and freedom.","refs":["CH_QGOH_PQ_5:SD4","CH_QGOH_PQ_5:ME6"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[]},{"id":"CL_QGOH_06:GA-10","text":"[Attractor] Knowledge of God and the understood necessity of Nature converge in the acquiescence by which finite conatus affirmatively participates in the order of the whole.","refs":["CH_QGOH_PQ_3:A3","CH_QGOH_PQ_5:A17"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[]},{"id":"CL_QGOH_06:GA-11","text":"[Terminal] At the limit of the perfectly free human being, good and evil lose their function, although they remain necessary for navigating finite life.","refs":["CH_QGOH_PQ_4:AS6","CH_QGOH_PQ_5:SNX2"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[]}],"original_name":"Global Load-Bearing Axioms"},{"number":"3","name":"Recurrence, Mutation, and Displacement Matrix","items":[{"id":"CL_QGOH_06:RMD-01","text":"Conatus preserves its structural identity, but is amplified from individual self-preservation into cooperation, trust, and common preservation.","refs":["CH_QGOH_PQ_5:A3","CH_QGOH_PQ_5:A9"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[]},{"id":"CL_QGOH_06:RMD-02","text":"Reason returns functionally: first it demonstrates agreement, then qualifies the cause of action, and finally orders affects, institutions, and material conditions.","refs":["CH_QGOH_PQ_3:AS2","CH_QGOH_PQ_4:SD1","CH_QGOH_PQ_5:SD2"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[]},{"id":"CL_QGOH_06:RMD-03","text":"The State is transformed from external coercion of the affects into an internal means of freedom, yet does not entirely lay aside its coercive origin.","refs":["CH_QGOH_PQ_3:AS4","CH_QGOH_PQ_5:NX7"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[]},{"id":"CL_QGOH_06:RMD-04","text":"Good and evil migrate from names of effects to rational comparisons and finally disappear in perfect freedom.","refs":["CH_QGOH_PQ_4:A13","CH_QGOH_PQ_4:SD5"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[]},{"id":"CL_QGOH_06:RMD-05","text":"The body is extended from an identity defined through a ratio of motion and rest into an infrastructure of nourishment, knowledge, labor, and society.","refs":["CH_QGOH_PQ_3:A10","CH_QGOH_PQ_5:SD4"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[]},{"id":"CL_QGOH_06:RMD-06","text":"Fear is cast down from an initial instrument of security to a form of untrustworthy concord; love, generosity, and trust absorb its stabilizing function.","refs":["CH_QGOH_PQ_3:A13","CH_QGOH_PQ_5:A16"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[]},{"id":"CL_QGOH_06:RMD-07","text":"Particular affects disappear after reclassification as autonomous moral indicators and return as causal modes of increase, decrease, or transition of power.","refs":["CH_QGOH_PQ_3:AS5","CH_QGOH_PQ_4:ME1"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[]}],"original_name":"Recurrence, Mutation, and Displacement Matrix"},{"number":"4","name":"Lower-Layer vs Current-Level Distinction","items":[{"id":"CL_QGOH_06:CGD-01","text":"The distinction between humans and brute animals locally marks the limit of reciprocity, but does not generate the structure of present freedom except as an example of advantage according to agreement of nature.","refs":["CH_QGOH_PQ_3:A15","CH_QGOH_PQ_3:D11"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[]},{"id":"CL_QGOH_06:CGD-02","text":"The criterion of death through a change in the form of the body remains local; upward there persists only the principle that relational integrity, not external appearance, constitutes power.","refs":["CH_QGOH_PQ_3:A10","CH_QGOH_PQ_3:SD6"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[]},{"id":"CL_QGOH_06:CGD-03","text":"The cases of laughter, compassion, flight, benefit, gratitude, and money are not individually amplified; they are joined under the rule by which an act is assessed from its cause, whole, and effect.","refs":["CH_QGOH_PQ_4:NX6","CH_QGOH_PQ_5:ME3"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[]},{"id":"CL_QGOH_06:CGD-04","text":"The doctrine of restraining an affect by a stronger affect effects the transition between the diagnosis of bondage and the construction of active desires and efficacious institutions.","refs":["CH_QGOH_PQ_5:A8","CH_QGOH_PQ_4:D10"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[]},{"id":"CL_QGOH_06:CGD-05","text":"Civil institution joins the units, because the same power that restrains conflict can sustain rights, material cooperation, and gradual freedom.","refs":["CH_QGOH_PQ_3:D5","CH_QGOH_PQ_5:D5"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[]},{"id":"CL_QGOH_06:CGD-06","text":"Morality through sadness, guilt, and opinion becomes obsolete as a code of virtue, but is conditionally preserved as a transitory discipline of the multitude not yet rational.","refs":["CH_QGOH_PQ_4:A16","CH_QGOH_PQ_4:MRT5"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[]}],"original_name":"Lower-Layer vs Current-Level Distinction"},{"number":"5","name":"Global Apophatic Structure","items":[{"id":"CL_QGOH_06:GNX-01","text":"Rational self-advantage is not competitive possession, because it constitutes the power of the individual through a communicable good and mutual aid.","refs":["CH_QGOH_PQ_3:NX1","CH_QGOH_PQ_5:SNX3"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"Negative","passages":[]},{"id":"CL_QGOH_06:GNX-02","text":"Virtue does not arise from sadness, fear, repentance, self-abjection, or hatred, although these affects may be employed for transitory discipline.","refs":["CH_QGOH_PQ_4:AS2","CH_QGOH_PQ_5:NX9"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"Negative","passages":[]},{"id":"CL_QGOH_06:GNX-03","text":"Freedom is not indeterminate choice, solitude, or exemption from the necessity of Nature, but relative action from understood proper causes.","refs":["CH_QGOH_PQ_5:NX1","CH_QGOH_PQ_5:ME1"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"Negative","passages":[]},{"id":"CL_QGOH_06:GNX-04","text":"The rational State is not reduced to fear nor opposed to freedom, because trustworthy concord requires common rights and cooperation.","refs":["CH_QGOH_PQ_5:NX7","CH_QGOH_PQ_5:ME4"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"Negative","passages":[]},{"id":"CL_QGOH_06:GNX-05","text":"Good, evil, just, and unjust are not absolute kinds of things, but relations of power, knowledge, or institution.","refs":["CH_QGOH_PQ_3:A8","CH_QGOH_PQ_5:NX2"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"Negative","passages":[]}],"original_name":"Global Apophatic Structure"},{"number":"6","name":"Deep Book Apophatics","items":[{"id":"CL_QGOH_06:DBN-01","text":"It must be false that abstract understanding without active affect or institutional mediation can overcome the power of the passions.","refs":["CH_QGOH_PQ_3:A5","CH_QGOH_PQ_5:DNX6"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"Negative","passages":[]},{"id":"CL_QGOH_06:DBN-02","text":"It must be false that an individual can lack body, external things, and other human beings without the faculty of knowing being diminished at the same time.","refs":["CH_QGOH_PQ_5:DNX3","CH_QGOH_PQ_5:DNX4"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"Negative","passages":[]},{"id":"CL_QGOH_06:DBN-03","text":"It must be false that the external appearance of an act determines its value, because bondage and freedom are distinguished in the mode of causation of the same act.","refs":["CH_QGOH_PQ_4:A7","CH_QGOH_PQ_4:D6"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"Negative","passages":[]},{"id":"CL_QGOH_06:DBN-04","text":"It must be false that a transcendent moral order intervenes between natural power and civil institution, because norms are generated from immanent relations.","refs":["CH_QGOH_PQ_3:A6","CH_QGOH_PQ_3:SNX1"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"Negative","passages":[]}],"original_name":"Deep Book Apophatics"},{"number":"7","name":"Super-Apophatics of the Whole","items":[{"id":"CL_QGOH_06:BSA-01","text":"The system is ordered around the absence of a subject outside Nature: no human power can transcend the causation of the whole or attribute an absolute origin to itself.","refs":["CH_QGOH_PQ_5:DNX1","CH_QGOH_PQ_5:SNX1"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"Negative","passages":[]},{"id":"CL_QGOH_06:BSA-02","text":"The inadmissible possibility is perpetual freedom without passion, body, society, or external causes; the perfectly free human being remains only a hypothetical attractor.","refs":["CH_QGOH_PQ_5:A2","CH_QGOH_PQ_5:SNX2"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"Negative","passages":[]},{"id":"CL_QGOH_06:BSA-03","text":"An immediate community of already rational human beings is excluded; every actual community requires affective, juridical, and material mediations.","refs":["CH_QGOH_PQ_5:A15","CH_QGOH_PQ_5:SNX4"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"Negative","passages":[]}],"original_name":"Super-Apophatics of the Whole"},{"number":"8","name":"Cross-Unit Derivative Propositions","items":[{"id":"CL_QGOH_06:XD-01","text":"Self-preservation under the necessity of Nature becomes a principle of cooperation, because the individual does not increase its power unless it produces a communicable good and common aids.","refs":["CL_QGOH_06:GA-01","CL_QGOH_06:GA-02","CL_QGOH_06:GA-08","CH_QGOH_PQ_3:A2","CH_QGOH_PQ_5:A9"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"Composite","passages":[]},{"id":"CL_QGOH_06:XD-02","text":"Since bare understanding does not restrain affect, efficacious rational action must assume the form of active desire, love, generosity, or institution.","refs":["CL_QGOH_06:GA-03","CL_QGOH_06:GA-04","CH_QGOH_PQ_3:A5","CH_QGOH_PQ_5:A8"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"Tension","passages":[]},{"id":"CL_QGOH_06:XD-03","text":"The State is a causal converter: it employs external force for security, transfers security into cooperation, and cooperation into greater proper causality of citizens.","refs":["CL_QGOH_06:GA-06","CL_QGOH_06:GA-08","CH_QGOH_PQ_3:AS4","CH_QGOH_PQ_5:A11"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"Composite","passages":[]},{"id":"CL_QGOH_06:XD-04","text":"Freedom requires an ecology of power in which bodily integrity, external things, nourishment, labor, exchange, and common rights mutually sustain one another.","refs":["CL_QGOH_06:GA-05","CL_QGOH_06:GA-08","CL_QGOH_06:GA-09","CH_QGOH_PQ_5:A13","CH_QGOH_PQ_5:SD4"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"Composite","passages":[]},{"id":"CL_QGOH_06:XD-05","text":"Good and evil are instruments for navigating finite power: they are more necessary the less free the human being is, and recede as the attractor of freedom is approached.","refs":["CL_QGOH_06:GA-07","CL_QGOH_06:GA-11","CH_QGOH_PQ_4:SD5","CH_QGOH_PQ_5:MRT7"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"Composite","passages":[]},{"id":"CL_QGOH_06:XD-06","text":"Civilly instituted justice can have reason without transcendent law, if trust, equity, and common advantage preserve the very conditions of cooperation.","refs":["CL_QGOH_06:GA-02","CL_QGOH_06:GA-06","CL_QGOH_06:GA-07","CH_QGOH_PQ_3:A8","CH_QGOH_PQ_5:A10"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"Composite","passages":[]},{"id":"CL_QGOH_06:XD-07","text":"Knowledge of God and knowledge of the necessity of Nature effect the same practical attractor: the desire to communicate good and acquiescence within the limits of finite power.","refs":["CL_QGOH_06:GA-02","CL_QGOH_06:GA-10","CH_QGOH_PQ_3:A4","CH_QGOH_PQ_5:A17"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"Composite","passages":[]}],"original_name":"Cross-Unit Derivative Propositions"},{"number":"9","name":"Book-Scale Super-Derivatives","items":[{"id":"CL_QGOH_06:BSD-01","text":"The free subject is not a substance in its own right, but a nexus of relations that acts all the more insofar as it adequately orders more bodily, affective, and social determinations.","refs":["CL_QGOH_06:GA-04","CL_QGOH_06:GA-05","CL_QGOH_06:GA-08","CH_QGOH_PQ_5:SD1","CH_QGOH_PQ_5:ALT2"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"Composite","passages":[]},{"id":"CL_QGOH_06:BSD-02","text":"Politics becomes the art of distributing the conditions of proper causality, not the simple art of forbidding or imposing peace through fear.","refs":["CL_QGOH_06:GA-03","CL_QGOH_06:GA-06","CL_QGOH_06:GA-09","CH_QGOH_PQ_5:A16","CH_QGOH_PQ_5:SD3"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"Composite","passages":[]},{"id":"CL_QGOH_06:BSD-03","text":"Ethical time is doubled: imagination weighs presents partially, while Reason orders the advantage of the whole across time, although it cannot know duration exactly.","refs":["CL_QGOH_06:GA-04","CL_QGOH_06:GA-07","CH_QGOH_PQ_4:A14","CH_QGOH_PQ_4:SD3"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"Tension","passages":[]},{"id":"CL_QGOH_06:BSD-04","text":"Ethics becomes causal diagnostics: it does not ask what an act externally signifies, but which determination increases the power of the whole and which reproduces external dependence.","refs":["CL_QGOH_06:GA-01","CL_QGOH_06:GA-04","CL_QGOH_06:GA-05","CH_QGOH_PQ_4:A7","CH_QGOH_PQ_4:ME2"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"Composite","passages":[]},{"id":"CL_QGOH_06:BSD-05","text":"The closure of the system is rational resilience rather than mastery: freedom is increased through cooperation and understanding necessity, not through the abolition of external causes.","refs":["CL_QGOH_06:GA-06","CL_QGOH_06:GA-08","CL_QGOH_06:GA-10","CH_QGOH_PQ_5:D10","CH_QGOH_PQ_5:SD6"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"Composite","passages":[]}],"original_name":"Book-Scale Super-Derivatives"},{"number":"10","name":"Recursive Loops, Escalation Paths, and Conceptual Attractors","items":[{"id":"CL_QGOH_06:RLE-01","text":"Rational conatus → mutual advantage → society → greater individual power → amplification of rational conatus.","refs":["CH_QGOH_PQ_3:A2","CH_QGOH_PQ_5:A9"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[]},{"id":"CL_QGOH_06:RLE-02","text":"Affective conflict → fear and coercion → civil security → trustworthy cooperation → diminution of the necessity of fear.","refs":["CH_QGOH_PQ_3:D5","CH_QGOH_PQ_5:A16"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[]},{"id":"CL_QGOH_06:RLE-03","text":"Aptitude of the body → necessity of external things → labor and exchange → social interdependence → greater aptitude of body and Mind.","refs":["CH_QGOH_PQ_5:A13","CH_QGOH_PQ_5:SD4"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[]},{"id":"CL_QGOH_06:RLE-04","text":"Adequate idea → active joy → rational desire → power-increasing action → greater capacity for adequate ideas.","refs":["CH_QGOH_PQ_4:A10","CH_QGOH_PQ_4:FGS4"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[]},{"id":"CL_QGOH_06:RLE-05","text":"Comparison of good and evil → choice of integral advantage → greater freedom → lesser dependence on comparison → attraction toward a limit beyond good and evil.","refs":["CH_QGOH_PQ_4:A13","CH_QGOH_PQ_4:SD5"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[]}],"original_name":"Recursive Loops, Escalation Paths, and Conceptual Attractors"},{"number":"11","name":"Formal Generative Schema of the Whole Book","items":[{"id":"CL_QGOH_06:FGS-B01","text":"The necessity of Nature generates finite conatus; conatus becomes action from adequate knowledge, passion from inadequate knowledge.","refs":["CH_QGOH_PQ_5:A3","CH_QGOH_PQ_5:A4"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[]},{"id":"CL_QGOH_06:FGS-B02","text":"Rational action seeks its own preservation; human agreement amplifies this preservation into a non-rival common good.","refs":["CH_QGOH_PQ_3:A1","CH_QGOH_PQ_3:A2"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[]},{"id":"CL_QGOH_06:FGS-B03","text":"The superiority of external causes disturbs agreement; contrary affects and the State mediate the interval between the rational norm and the actual multitude.","refs":["CH_QGOH_PQ_5:A7","CH_QGOH_PQ_5:A8"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[]},{"id":"CL_QGOH_06:FGS-B04","text":"The State converts security into common rights, trust, and cooperation; these structures increase the proper causality of citizens.","refs":["CH_QGOH_PQ_3:A7","CH_QGOH_PQ_5:D4"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[]},{"id":"CL_QGOH_06:FGS-B05","text":"The multiple body requires external things and mutual services; a regulated economy materially sustains the freedom of knowledge.","refs":["CH_QGOH_PQ_5:SD4","CH_QGOH_PQ_5:NX10"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[]},{"id":"CL_QGOH_06:FGS-B06","text":"Power thus increased understands its necessity and tends toward acquiescence, while perfect freedom remains a regulative limit rather than an empirical state.","refs":["CH_QGOH_PQ_5:A17","CH_QGOH_PQ_5:MRT3"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[]}],"original_name":"Formal Generative Schema of the Whole Book"},{"number":"12","name":"Whole-Book Argument Flow Map","items":[{"id":"CL_QGOH_06:AFM-B01","text":"Necessary conatus → self-advantage → rational agreement → communicable good.","refs":["CH_QGOH_PQ_3:AS1","CH_QGOH_PQ_3:A3"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[]},{"id":"CL_QGOH_06:AFM-B02","text":"Dominion of the affects → conflict and partiality → contrary affects → civil security.","refs":["CH_QGOH_PQ_3:AS3","CH_QGOH_PQ_5:A8"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[]},{"id":"CL_QGOH_06:AFM-B03","text":"Civil security → common rights → trust and cooperation → socially amplified freedom.","refs":["CH_QGOH_PQ_3:A7","CH_QGOH_PQ_5:A11"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[]},{"id":"CL_QGOH_06:AFM-B04","text":"Bodily integrity → external things and economy → cognitive power → moderation of the affects.","refs":["CH_QGOH_PQ_5:A13","CH_QGOH_PQ_5:ME6"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[]},{"id":"CL_QGOH_06:AFM-B05","text":"Moderation of the affects → proper causality → understanding of necessity → acquiescence → approximation of freedom without natural exemption.","refs":["CH_QGOH_PQ_5:AS3","CH_QGOH_PQ_5:ME7"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[]}],"original_name":"Whole-Book Argument Flow Map"},{"number":"13","name":"Macro Map-Effects","items":[{"id":"CL_QGOH_06:MME-01","text":"The opposition between self-advantage and the common good collapses; the true distinction is placed between adequately ordered conatus and conatus captured by external causes.","refs":["CH_QGOH_PQ_3:ME1","CH_QGOH_PQ_5:A4"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[]},{"id":"CL_QGOH_06:MME-02","text":"The opposition between the State and freedom collapses; the distinction is transferred to the State that merely administers fear and the State that increases common power.","refs":["CH_QGOH_PQ_5:NX7","CH_QGOH_PQ_5:ME4"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[]},{"id":"CL_QGOH_06:MME-03","text":"The morality of external acts is reordered into an analysis of causes, adequacy, the whole body, and social effects.","refs":["CH_QGOH_PQ_4:D6","CH_QGOH_PQ_4:ME1"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[]},{"id":"CL_QGOH_06:MME-04","text":"Body and economy are transferred from inferior adjuncts into constitutive conditions of understanding, cooperation, and freedom.","refs":["CH_QGOH_PQ_5:SD4","CH_QGOH_PQ_5:ME6"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[]},{"id":"CL_QGOH_06:MME-05","text":"Necessity is reclassified from an external limit of freedom into an immanent field within which proper causality, prudence, and acquiescence can be increased.","refs":["CH_QGOH_PQ_5:SD6","CH_QGOH_PQ_5:ME7"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[]}],"original_name":"Macro Map-Effects"},{"number":"14","name":"Altitude Integration","items":[{"id":"CL_QGOH_06:ALT-B01","text":"The surface level examines affects, actions, benefits, dangers, laws, food, and money as cases of life under bondage and freedom.","refs":["CH_QGOH_PQ_4:ALT1","CH_QGOH_PQ_5:ALT1"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[]},{"id":"CL_QGOH_06:ALT-B02","text":"The structural level distributes all cases according to the causality of action, the power of the body, the adequacy of knowledge, and social mediation.","refs":["CH_QGOH_PQ_4:ALT2","CH_QGOH_PQ_5:ALT2"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[]},{"id":"CL_QGOH_06:ALT-B03","text":"The metastrategic level converts moral contest into the art of producing active affects, trust, cooperation, and the conditions of freedom.","refs":["CH_QGOH_PQ_4:ALT3","CH_QGOH_PQ_5:ALT3"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[]},{"id":"CL_QGOH_06:ALT-B04","text":"The ontological and temporal level constitutes the human being as a necessarily determined part of Nature, whose Reason orders advantage across time.","refs":["CH_QGOH_PQ_5:A2","CH_QGOH_PQ_4:SD3"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[]},{"id":"CL_QGOH_06:ALT-B05","text":"The methodological level performs its reclassifications: it transfers the same act between passion and action and transforms the State from a limit into a means of freedom.","refs":["CH_QGOH_PQ_4:NX6","CH_QGOH_PQ_5:ME4"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[]}],"original_name":"Altitude Integration"},{"number":"15","name":"Internal Tensions, Mutation Points, and Fault Lines","items":[{"id":"CL_QGOH_06:FL-01","text":"Reason is said not to be able by itself to restrain affects, yet to be able to produce all actions of the passions; the means by which an adequate idea acquires stronger affective force is imperfectly explained.","refs":["CH_QGOH_PQ_3:A5","CH_QGOH_PQ_4:D7"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"Tension","passages":[]},{"id":"CL_QGOH_06:FL-02","text":"The State is at once founded on threats and perfects freedom; the transition from external coercion to rational cooperation is not deduced from security alone.","refs":["CH_QGOH_PQ_3:MRT3","CH_QGOH_PQ_5:MRT4"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"Tension","passages":[]},{"id":"CL_QGOH_06:FL-03","text":"Reason weighs future goods, although human knowledge of duration is necessarily inadequate; temporal prudence must therefore operate with a certainty that it does not possess.","refs":["CH_QGOH_PQ_4:D9","CH_QGOH_PQ_4:MRT4"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"Tension","passages":[]},{"id":"CL_QGOH_06:FL-04","text":"Actions defined by Reason are always called good, but the perfectly free human being does not form the concepts of good and evil; the distinction of perspectives remains implicit.","refs":["CH_QGOH_PQ_5:A5","CH_QGOH_PQ_5:MRT7"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"Tension","passages":[]},{"id":"CL_QGOH_06:MP-01","text":"Advantage is transformed from the preservation of the individual into a common structure that simultaneously preserves body, knowledge, cooperation, and rights.","refs":["CH_QGOH_PQ_3:A1","CH_QGOH_PQ_5:SD4"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[]},{"id":"CL_QGOH_06:MP-02","text":"Freedom is transformed from action from one's own nature into a socially and materially sustained degree of adequate causality.","refs":["CH_QGOH_PQ_4:A1","CH_QGOH_PQ_5:SD1"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[]},{"id":"CL_QGOH_06:MP-03","text":"Necessity is transformed from an index of external power into an object of understanding that effects acquiescence and relative freedom.","refs":["CH_QGOH_PQ_5:A7","CH_QGOH_PQ_5:SD6"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[]},{"id":"CL_QGOH_06:MP-04","text":"Good and evil are transformed from criteria of power into comparative instruments and finally into terminally superfluous categories.","refs":["CH_QGOH_PQ_3:A11","CH_QGOH_PQ_4:SD5"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[]}],"original_name":"Internal Tensions, Mutation Points, and Fault Lines"},{"number":"16","name":"Hidden System Image","items":[{"id":"CL_QGOH_06:HSI-01","text":"Reality is implicitly a network of forces in which bodies, affects, institutions, and external things increase, diminish, or transfer power into new circuits.","refs":["CH_QGOH_PQ_3:A11","CH_QGOH_PQ_5:ALT2"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[]},{"id":"CL_QGOH_06:HSI-02","text":"The subject is a permeable node that does not possess freedom, but composes it through adequate ideas, active affects, and trustworthy relations.","refs":["CH_QGOH_PQ_5:A1","CH_QGOH_PQ_5:SD1"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[]},{"id":"CL_QGOH_06:HSI-03","text":"The time of the system is double: imagination partially amplifies presents, but Reason orders the series of advantages under the aspect of necessity.","refs":["CH_QGOH_PQ_4:A9","CH_QGOH_PQ_4:A14"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[]},{"id":"CL_QGOH_06:HSI-04","text":"Possible navigation is not flight outside Nature, but passage among determinations by which the human being converts conflict into cooperation and necessity into acquiescence.","refs":["CH_QGOH_PQ_5:SNX1","CH_QGOH_PQ_5:ME7"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[]}],"original_name":"Hidden System Image"},{"number":"17","name":"Minimal Whole-Book Reconstruction Test","items":[{"id":"CL_QGOH_06:MRT-B01","text":"The axioms concerning conatus, adequacy, the power of the affects, body, State, and the necessity of Nature suffice to reconstruct the machinery of this level.","refs":["CH_QGOH_PQ_5:MRT1","CH_QGOH_PQ_4:MRT1"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[]},{"id":"CL_QGOH_06:MRT-B02","text":"The firmest structure is the circuit by which rational advantage generates cooperation, cooperation increases individual power, and increased power permits greater rational action.","refs":["CH_QGOH_PQ_3:A2","CH_QGOH_PQ_5:MRT2"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[]},{"id":"CL_QGOH_06:MRT-B03","text":"The production of efficacious rational affect and the conversion of the coercive State into an order truly sustaining freedom remain less determined.","refs":["CH_QGOH_PQ_5:MRT6","CH_QGOH_PQ_5:MRT4"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[]},{"id":"CL_QGOH_06:MRT-B04","text":"The reconstruction preserves the true movement if it presents freedom not as a terminal state, but as the recursive amplification of bodily, social, and cognitive power under necessity.","refs":["CH_QGOH_PQ_5:MRT8","CH_QGOH_PQ_5:SD6"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[]}],"original_name":"Minimal Whole-Book Reconstruction Test"}],"revision":"CL_QGOH_06@R001","source_variant_id":"canonical:CL_QGOH_06:v1","source_language_code":"la","original_title":"Chapters 16–18"}],"identity":{"layer":"CL","kicker":"Cluster · Ethica","title":"Chapters 16–18","context":"Part IV - Of Human Bondage, or the Strength of the Emotions · passage 3 · Part IV - Of Human Bondage, or the Strength of the Emotions · passage 4 · Part IV - Of Human Bondage, or the Strength of the Emotions · passage 5","original_title":"Chapters 16–18","original_context":"Pars quarta - De servitute humana seu de affectuum viribus · passage 3 · Pars quarta - De servitute humana seu de affectuum viribus · passage 4 · Pars quarta - De servitute humana seu de affectuum viribus · passage 5"},"original_title":"Chapters 16–18","title_language_code":"en"},{"id":"CL_QGOH_07","layer":"CL","title":"Chapters 19–21","order":7,"parents":["CH_QGOH_PQDP","CH_QGOH_PQ_6","CH_QGOH_PQ_7"],"revision":"CL_QGOH_07@R001","sha256":"82979d3790e86ac8110cdff861396fa6f23f5568f8f95ee07e7d56a68a9a240d","sections":[{"number":"1","name":"Book-Scale Argumental Spine","items":[{"id":"CL_QGOH_07:AS-B01","text":"Systema quaerit quomodo Mens necessaria, corpori coniuncta et causis externis obnoxia libertatem acquirat sine imperio voluntatis, sine exemptione a causalitate et sine oeconomia praemii post mortem.","refs":["CH_QGOH_PQDP:AS1","CH_QGOH_PQ_6:SD1","CH_QGOH_PQ_7:NX1"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[]},{"id":"CL_QGOH_07:AS-B02","text":"Thesis globalis potentiam Mentis in intelligentia collocat, quae affectus ordinando ad cognitionem intuitivam, Amorem Dei intellectualem et beatitudinem tamquam virtutem immanentem progreditur.","refs":["CH_QGOH_PQDP:A1","CH_QGOH_PQ_6:A13","CH_QGOH_PQ_7:A7"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[]},{"id":"CL_QGOH_07:AS-B03","text":"Argumentatio demonstrativa simul reconstructiva est: categorias voluntatis, passionis, amoris, aeternitatis et beatitudinis non tantum negat, sed secundum causalitatem idearum denuo constituit.","refs":["CH_QGOH_PQ_6:AS2","CH_QGOH_PQ_7:ALT3"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[]},{"id":"CL_QGOH_07:AS-B04","text":"Motus a confusa attributione affectus causae externae transit ad ordinationem intellectualem, deinde ad tertium cognitionis genus et tandem ad actionem aeternam quae suam laetitiam secum fert.","refs":["CH_QGOH_PQ_6:A5","CH_QGOH_PQ_6:A9","CH_QGOH_PQ_6:A12"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[]},{"id":"CL_QGOH_07:AS-B05","text":"Progressus verus spiralis est, quia cognitio passivitatem minuit, laetitia cognitionem auget, et beatitudo maiorem potentiam cognoscendi atque ordinandi affectus iterum producit.","refs":["CH_QGOH_PQ_7:A8","CH_QGOH_PQ_7:D3"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[]},{"id":"CL_QGOH_07:AS-B06","text":"Corpus ab sede durationis, imaginationis et passionum in condicionem strategicam mutatur, cuius aptitudo plures actiones, ampliorem cognitionem et maiorem partem Mentis aeternam sustinet.","refs":["CH_QGOH_PQ_6:A7","CH_QGOH_PQ_6:A17","CH_QGOH_PQ_6:SD5"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[]},{"id":"CL_QGOH_07:AS-B07","text":"Clausura figuram sapientis producit, cuius libertas non indeterminatio sed stabilitas sub necessitate intellecta est, contra ignorantem qui causis externis et libidinibus fluctuans determinatur.","refs":["CH_QGOH_PQ_7:AS6","CH_QGOH_PQ_7:D6"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[]},{"id":"CL_QGOH_07:AS-B08","text":"Terminus argumenti in causam convertitur: beatitudo, quae primo ut finis liberationis apparet, tandem ipsa virtus et principium potentiae in affectus fit.","refs":["CH_QGOH_PQ_7:AS5","CH_QGOH_PQ_7:D3"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[]}],"original_name":"Book-Scale Argumental Spine"},{"number":"2","name":"Global Load-Bearing Axioms","items":[{"id":"CL_QGOH_07:GA-01","text":"[Persistent] Potentia Mentis non voluntate separata, sed gradu intelligentiae et formationis idearum adaequatarum definitur.","refs":["CH_QGOH_PQDP:A1","CH_QGOH_PQ_6:ALT2"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[]},{"id":"CL_QGOH_07:GA-02","text":"[Persistent] Passio est affectus quatenus ex idea confusa oritur; claritas eius non obiectum delet, sed statum Mentis a pati ad agere transfert.","refs":["CH_QGOH_PQDP:A3","CH_QGOH_PQ_7:A3"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[]},{"id":"CL_QGOH_07:GA-03","text":"[Mutating] Libertas ab imminutione graduata passivitatis ad stabilem praevalentiam actionis intellectualis et tandem ad beatitudinem activam extenditur.","refs":["CH_QGOH_PQDP:ME2","CH_QGOH_PQ_6:SD1"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[]},{"id":"CL_QGOH_07:GA-04","text":"[Persistent] Necessitas causarum non est oppositum libertatis, sed ordo cuius intellectus affectum mitigat et actionem sapientis constituit.","refs":["CH_QGOH_PQDP:A7","CH_QGOH_PQ_7:DNX5"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[]},{"id":"CL_QGOH_07:GA-05","text":"[Persistent] Ordo idearum, affectionum Corporis, imaginum et memoriae unam machinam constituunt, per quam cognitio dispositio affectiva et corporalis fit.","refs":["CH_QGOH_PQDP:A2","CH_QGOH_PQDP:A12"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[]},{"id":"CL_QGOH_07:GA-06","text":"[Mutating] Corpus simul est condicio imaginationis temporalis et instrumentum aptitudinis, quo Mens ad plures actiones et ampliorem aeternitatis participationem disponitur.","refs":["CH_QGOH_PQ_6:A7","CH_QGOH_PQ_6:A17"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[]},{"id":"CL_QGOH_07:GA-07","text":"[Emergent] Genera cognitionis asymmetricam seriem formant: confusio primi generis tertium non parit, Ratio transitum aperit, intuitio autem essentias singulares ex Deo cognoscit.","refs":["CH_QGOH_PQ_6:A9","CH_QGOH_PQ_6:A10"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[]},{"id":"CL_QGOH_07:GA-08","text":"[Mutating] Amor Dei a relatione passiva et reciproca ad Amorem intellectualem mutatur, quo amor Mentis pars est activae et infinitae sui cognitionis divinae.","refs":["CH_QGOH_PQDP:A16","CH_QGOH_PQ_6:A14"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[]},{"id":"CL_QGOH_07:GA-09","text":"[Emergent] Aeternitas Mentis neque duratio indefinita neque conservatio memoriae personalis est, sed conceptio essentiae Corporis et Mentis sub necessitate divina.","refs":["CH_QGOH_PQ_6:NX6","CH_QGOH_PQ_6:DNX2"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[]},{"id":"CL_QGOH_07:GA-10","text":"[Attractor] Cognitio tertii generis, summa laetitia, Amor Dei intellectualis et beatitudo in unum attractorem convergunt qui affectus non reprimit sed activitate superat.","refs":["CH_QGOH_PQ_6:A12","CH_QGOH_PQ_7:A6"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[]},{"id":"CL_QGOH_07:GA-11","text":"[Emergent] Virtus proprium utile ratione quaerens a spe et metu separatur, atque beatitudo non merces virtutis sed eius exercitium constitutivum fit.","refs":["CH_QGOH_PQ_7:A4","CH_QGOH_PQ_7:NX3"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[]},{"id":"CL_QGOH_07:GA-12","text":"[Terminal] Discrimen inter sapientem et ignorantem est diversitas modorum existendi: sapientis stabilitas ex necessitate intellecta, ignorantia autem ex determinatione externa et fluctuatione oritur.","refs":["CH_QGOH_PQ_7:D6","CH_QGOH_PQ_7:AS6"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[]}],"original_name":"Global Load-Bearing Axioms"},{"number":"3","name":"Recurrence, Mutation, and Displacement Matrix","items":[{"id":"CL_QGOH_07:RMD-01","text":"Intelligentia identitatem functionis servat: ab initio ad finem unica mensura potentiae Mentis manet, quamvis ab affectuum remedio ad aeternam actionem extendatur.","refs":["CH_QGOH_PQDP:A1","CH_QGOH_PQ_6:ALT2"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[]},{"id":"CL_QGOH_07:RMD-02","text":"Affectus primum ut idea confusa, deinde ut proportio inter causas externas et cognitionem, postremo ut materia actionis beatificae reclassificatur.","refs":["CH_QGOH_PQDP:A3","CH_QGOH_PQ_6:ME1"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[]},{"id":"CL_QGOH_07:RMD-03","text":"Amor Dei inversionem patitur: amor passivus, reciprocus et anthropomorphicus excluditur, dum Amor intellectualis redit ut actus quo Deus per Mentem se ipsum amat.","refs":["CH_QGOH_PQDP:NX7","CH_QGOH_PQ_6:D11"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[]},{"id":"CL_QGOH_07:RMD-04","text":"Imaginatio et memoria primo instrumenta praeparationis rationalis sunt, sed in transitu ad aeternitatem structuram suam exhauriunt et cum duratione Corporis evanescunt.","refs":["CH_QGOH_PQDP:ME4","CH_QGOH_PQ_6:NX5"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[]},{"id":"CL_QGOH_07:RMD-05","text":"Corpus a termino polemico dualismi ad condicionem temporalem passionis, deinde ad instrumentum positivum ampliandae potentiae Mentis migrat.","refs":["CH_QGOH_PQDP:NX2","CH_QGOH_PQ_7:SD4"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[]},{"id":"CL_QGOH_07:RMD-06","text":"Necessitas ab argumento mitigandi dolorem in formam ontologicam libertatis sapientis amplificatur; eadem causalitas quae arbitrium excludit actionem intelligibilem constituit.","refs":["CH_QGOH_PQDP:SD2","CH_QGOH_PQ_7:DNX5"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[]},{"id":"CL_QGOH_07:RMD-07","text":"Libertas a moderamine partiali affectuum ad mutationem proportionis inter actionem et passionem, atque inde ad beatitudinem aeternam, transfertur.","refs":["CH_QGOH_PQDP:ME2","CH_QGOH_PQ_7:A8"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[]},{"id":"CL_QGOH_07:RMD-08","text":"Virtus primo ex origine adaequata Cupiditatis distinguitur, sed terminaliter cum Amore intellectuali et beatitudine ipsa identificatur.","refs":["CH_QGOH_PQDP:A17","CH_QGOH_PQ_7:A7"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[]},{"id":"CL_QGOH_07:RMD-09","text":"Mortis metus ex centro morali removetur et in effectum cognitionis inadaequatae absorbetur; aucta parte intelligente, momentum partis pereuntis minuitur.","refs":["CH_QGOH_PQ_6:A16","CH_QGOH_PQ_7:SNX3"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[]}],"original_name":"Recurrence, Mutation, and Displacement Matrix"},{"number":"4","name":"Lower-Layer vs Current-Level Distinction","items":[{"id":"CL_QGOH_07:CGD-01","text":"Mechanica concursus causarum, distributionis obiectivae et frequentiae imaginum localiter explicat regimen affectuum, sed hoc gradu sub ampliori transformatione actionis et passionis ordinatur.","refs":["CH_QGOH_PQDP:D5","CH_QGOH_PQDP:ALT2"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[]},{"id":"CL_QGOH_07:CGD-02","text":"Refutatio Stoicae et Cartesianae voluntatis munus fundamenti negativi retinet, sed non constituit clausuram, quae ex Amore intellectuali et beatitudine producitur.","refs":["CH_QGOH_PQDP:AS2","CH_QGOH_PQ_7:A6"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[]},{"id":"CL_QGOH_07:CGD-03","text":"Impossibilitas odii erga Deum, initio terminus demonstratione suspensus, sursum fit condicio transformationis amoris passivi in actionem intellectualem.","refs":["CH_QGOH_PQDP:AS7","CH_QGOH_PQ_6:NX1"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[]},{"id":"CL_QGOH_07:CGD-04","text":"Relatio omnium affectionum ad ideam Dei a remedio locali affectuum in fundamentum cognitionis tertii generis et amoris aeterni amplificatur.","refs":["CH_QGOH_PQDP:A14","CH_QGOH_PQ_6:A11"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[]},{"id":"CL_QGOH_07:CGD-05","text":"Distinctio actualitatis temporalis et aeternae unitates inferiores coniungit, quia eadem essentia Corporis imaginationem durantem ab intellectu aeterno separat.","refs":["CH_QGOH_PQ_6:D7","CH_QGOH_PQ_6:SD2"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[]},{"id":"CL_QGOH_07:CGD-06","text":"Sterilitas primi generis respectu cognitionis intuitivae localem doctrinam cognitionis in legem transitionis vertit: sola cognitio adaequata cognitionem altiorem generat.","refs":["CH_QGOH_PQ_6:NX7","CH_QGOH_PQ_6:ME4"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[]},{"id":"CL_QGOH_07:CGD-07","text":"Fundamentum virtutis in utili rationali ab immortalitate independens manet, sed hoc gradu cum cognitione aeternitatis coniungitur sine reductione unius ad alterum.","refs":["CH_QGOH_PQ_7:AS4","CH_QGOH_PQ_7:MRT3"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[]},{"id":"CL_QGOH_07:CGD-08","text":"Aptitudo Corporis excedit statum propositionis localis et fit commissura inter praxim temporalem, ordinationem affectuum et proportionem Mentis aeternam.","refs":["CH_QGOH_PQ_7:A9","CH_QGOH_PQ_6:SD5"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[]}],"original_name":"Lower-Layer vs Current-Level Distinction"},{"number":"5","name":"Global Apophatic Structure","items":[{"id":"CL_QGOH_07:GNX-01","text":"Libertas non est imperium absolutum voluntatis neque actus nudae coercitionis, sed effectus intelligentiae quae statum causalem affectuum mutat.","refs":["CH_QGOH_PQDP:NX1","CH_QGOH_PQ_7:DNX3"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[]},{"id":"CL_QGOH_07:GNX-02","text":"Libertas non requirit punctum extra seriem causarum, quia exemptio a determinatione naturalis ordinis ipsum fundamentum cognitionis destrueret.","refs":["CH_QGOH_PQDP:SNX2","CH_QGOH_PQ_7:SNX2"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[]},{"id":"CL_QGOH_07:GNX-03","text":"Affectus non est vis cognitioni penitus extrinseca aut residuum opacum, quoniam mutatio cognitionis eius formam passivam dissolvere potest.","refs":["CH_QGOH_PQDP:NX4","CH_QGOH_PQDP:DNX1"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[]},{"id":"CL_QGOH_07:GNX-04","text":"Deus non est particeps commercii affectivi reciproci, nec Amor Dei postulare potest ut natura divina ad cupiditatem finitam mutetur.","refs":["CH_QGOH_PQDP:NX7","CH_QGOH_PQ_6:NX2"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[]},{"id":"CL_QGOH_07:GNX-05","text":"Aeternitas non est continuatio durationis, memoriae, imaginationis aut narrationis empiricae personae.","refs":["CH_QGOH_PQ_6:NX6","CH_QGOH_PQ_6:DNX2"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[]},{"id":"CL_QGOH_07:GNX-06","text":"Virtus neque spe praemii neque metu supplicii fundatur; oeconomia moralis compensationis ad servitutem affectivam pertinet.","refs":["CH_QGOH_PQ_7:NX1","CH_QGOH_PQ_7:SNX1"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[]},{"id":"CL_QGOH_07:GNX-07","text":"Beatitudo non est praemium posterius victoriae in libidines, sed vis praesens ex qua potentia ordinandi affectus sequitur.","refs":["CH_QGOH_PQ_7:NX3","CH_QGOH_PQ_7:D3"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[]},{"id":"CL_QGOH_07:GNX-08","text":"Corpus non est adversarius aeternitatis, quia eius aptitudo temporalis dispositionem ad actionem intellectualem efficere potest.","refs":["CH_QGOH_PQ_6:SD5","CH_QGOH_PQ_7:SD4"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[]}],"original_name":"Global Apophatic Structure"},{"number":"6","name":"Deep Book Apophatics","items":[{"id":"CL_QGOH_07:DBN-01","text":"Falsum esse debet Mentem extra Naturam consistere et voluntate nuda ordinem Corporis infringere; aliter correspondentia idearum et affectionum vacillaret.","refs":["CH_QGOH_PQDP:DNX2","CH_QGOH_PQDP:A2"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[]},{"id":"CL_QGOH_07:DBN-02","text":"Falsum esse debet vocem amoris eandem passionem in homine et Deo significare; impassibilitas divina et amor divinus cohaerent tantum per mutationem ab affectu passivo ad actionem intellectualem.","refs":["CH_QGOH_PQDP:DNX5","CH_QGOH_PQ_6:D11"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[]},{"id":"CL_QGOH_07:DBN-03","text":"Falsum esse debet identitatem aeternam Mentis coincidere cum historia imaginativa singularis personae, quia memoria et imaginatio ad durationem Corporis restringuntur.","refs":["CH_QGOH_PQ_7:DNX1","CH_QGOH_PQ_6:DNX2"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[]},{"id":"CL_QGOH_07:DBN-04","text":"Falsum esse debet summum bonum structuram rivalem vel scarce distributam habere; eius communicatio Amorem auget potius quam partem cuiusquam minuit.","refs":["CH_QGOH_PQ_6:DNX5","CH_QGOH_PQ_6:SD3"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[]},{"id":"CL_QGOH_07:DBN-05","text":"Falsum esse debet cognitionem esse repraesentationem inertem, quoniam cognitio adaequata laetitiam, ordinationem corporalem et maiorem agendi potentiam efficit.","refs":["CH_QGOH_PQ_6:NX4","CH_QGOH_PQ_7:A8"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[]},{"id":"CL_QGOH_07:DBN-06","text":"Falsum esse debet mortalitatem Corporis omnem rationem virtutis abolere; recte vivere fundamentum immanens habet et mors solam structuram temporalem Mentis delet.","refs":["CH_QGOH_PQ_7:DNX2","CH_QGOH_PQ_7:SNX3"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[]}],"original_name":"Deep Book Apophatics"},{"number":"7","name":"Super-Apophatics of the Whole","items":[{"id":"CL_QGOH_07:BSA-01","text":"Ontologice inadmissibile est subiectum finitum extra Deum subsistens, quod ex exteriori loco causam totius contempletur aut amet.","refs":["CH_QGOH_PQ_6:SNX1"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[]},{"id":"CL_QGOH_07:BSA-02","text":"Indisponibilis est Deus personalis qui precibus, offensa, amore reciproco aut mutatione voluntatis respondeat; relatio divina intra actionem intelligendi reponitur.","refs":["CH_QGOH_PQ_6:DNX3","CH_QGOH_PQ_6:NX2"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[]},{"id":"CL_QGOH_07:BSA-03","text":"Inadmissibilis est mensura temporalis qua aeternitas quantificetur vel futura continuatio vitae personalis probetur.","refs":["CH_QGOH_PQ_6:SNX2"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[]},{"id":"CL_QGOH_07:BSA-04","text":"Systema circum absentiam iudicis remunerantis ordinatur: sine praemio, poena et compensatione ultramortali virtus tamen causam sufficientem habere debet.","refs":["CH_QGOH_PQ_7:SNX1","CH_QGOH_PQ_7:A4"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[]},{"id":"CL_QGOH_07:BSA-05","text":"Impossibilis habetur destructio veritatis intellectualis per affectum contrarium, quia talis destructio falsitatem eius quod necessario intelligitur postularet.","refs":["CH_QGOH_PQ_6:SNX3","CH_QGOH_PQ_6:NX9"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[]}],"original_name":"Super-Apophatics of the Whole"},{"number":"8","name":"Cross-Unit Derivative Propositions","items":[{"id":"CL_QGOH_07:XD-01","text":"Libertas duplicem sed continuam structuram accipit: in duratione est ordinatio imaginum et imminutio passionis; sub aeternitate est actio intuitiva cui beatitudo et Amor intellectualis insunt.","refs":["CL_QGOH_07:GA-03","CL_QGOH_07:GA-05","CL_QGOH_07:GA-07","CL_QGOH_07:GA-10","CH_QGOH_PQDP:D6","CH_QGOH_PQ_6:D10","CH_QGOH_PQ_7:A7"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"Composite","passages":[]},{"id":"CL_QGOH_07:XD-02","text":"Cognitio et libertas circuitum positivum efficiunt: cognitio passionem minuit, cognitio adaequata cupiditatem cognitionis altioris parit, et beatitudo facultatem intelligendi iterum auget.","refs":["CL_QGOH_07:GA-01","CL_QGOH_07:GA-02","CL_QGOH_07:GA-07","CL_QGOH_07:GA-10","CH_QGOH_PQDP:D1","CH_QGOH_PQ_6:D8","CH_QGOH_PQ_7:A8"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"Composite","passages":[]},{"id":"CL_QGOH_07:XD-03","text":"Negatio amoris divini et affirmatio amoris quo Deus homines amat cohaerere possunt tantum si nomen amoris a passione reciproca ad aeternam actionem sui cognitionis mutatur.","refs":["CL_QGOH_07:GA-08","CH_QGOH_PQDP:A16","CH_QGOH_PQ_6:D11"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"Tension","passages":[]},{"id":"CL_QGOH_07:XD-04","text":"Corpus est simul terminus partis Mentis pereuntis et condicio augendae partis activae; eius temporalitas igitur aeternitati non simpliciter opponitur, sed ad eam disponit.","refs":["CL_QGOH_07:GA-06","CL_QGOH_07:GA-09","CH_QGOH_PQ_6:A7","CH_QGOH_PQ_6:A17","CH_QGOH_PQ_7:SD4"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"Tension","passages":[]},{"id":"CL_QGOH_07:XD-05","text":"Intellectus necessitatis et praeparatio imaginativa coniuncti efficiunt ut actio non sit interruptio causalitatis, sed ordinata participatio nexus causarum.","refs":["CL_QGOH_07:GA-04","CL_QGOH_07:GA-05","CH_QGOH_PQDP:D6","CH_QGOH_PQ_7:DNX5","CH_QGOH_PQ_7:D6"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"Composite","passages":[]},{"id":"CL_QGOH_07:XD-06","text":"Communitas summi boni et independentia virtutis a praemio constituunt ethicam non-rivalem, in qua aliena beatitudo potentiam propriam augere potest.","refs":["CL_QGOH_07:GA-10","CL_QGOH_07:GA-11","CH_QGOH_PQ_6:D3","CH_QGOH_PQ_7:SD1"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"Composite","passages":[]},{"id":"CL_QGOH_07:XD-07","text":"Imaginatio est structura transitoria: intra durationem rationem corporaliter praeparat, sed ad statum aeternum perveniens propriam operationem et memoriam amittit.","refs":["CL_QGOH_07:GA-05","CL_QGOH_07:GA-06","CL_QGOH_07:GA-09","CH_QGOH_PQDP:ME4","CH_QGOH_PQ_6:NX5","CH_QGOH_PQ_7:NX5"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"Tension","passages":[]},{"id":"CL_QGOH_07:XD-08","text":"Valor Cupiditatis non ex obiecto sed ex origine ideationali pendet; eadem genealogia explicat cur cognitio confusa tertium genus parere nequeat et cur Ratio transitum ad intuitionem aperiat.","refs":["CL_QGOH_07:GA-02","CL_QGOH_07:GA-07","CL_QGOH_07:GA-11","CH_QGOH_PQDP:D7","CH_QGOH_PQ_6:A10","CH_QGOH_PQ_7:AS4"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"Composite","passages":[]},{"id":"CL_QGOH_07:XD-09","text":"Affectuum libertas non exstinctionem absolutam, sed mutationem proportionis inter pati et agere requirit; incrementum intellectus efficacius est quam directum bellum contra passiones.","refs":["CL_QGOH_07:GA-02","CL_QGOH_07:GA-03","CH_QGOH_PQ_6:NX4","CH_QGOH_PQ_7:A1","CH_QGOH_PQ_7:D4"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"Composite","passages":[]}],"original_name":"Cross-Unit Derivative Propositions"},{"number":"9","name":"Book-Scale Super-Derivatives","items":[{"id":"CL_QGOH_07:BSD-01","text":"Subiectum liberum non est substantia voluntaria indivisa, sed proportio mutabilis idearum adaequatarum et inadaequatarum, actionum et passionum, cuius identitas secundum potentiam ordinatur.","refs":["CL_QGOH_07:GA-01","CL_QGOH_07:GA-02","CL_QGOH_07:GA-03","CH_QGOH_PQ_6:A4","CH_QGOH_PQ_7:A1"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"Composite","passages":[]},{"id":"CL_QGOH_07:BSD-02","text":"Agendi potentia crescit non causalitate remota, sed causalitate intellecta; autonomia reclassificatur ut capacitas causas per ideas adaequatas interne explicandi.","refs":["CL_QGOH_07:GA-03","CL_QGOH_07:GA-04","CH_QGOH_PQDP:SD2","CH_QGOH_PQ_7:DNX5"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"Composite","passages":[]},{"id":"CL_QGOH_07:BSD-03","text":"Epistemologia fit technica affectiva, quia veritas non tantum cognitionem obiecti praestat sed ordinem Corporis, frequentiam imaginum et facultatem agendi immutat.","refs":["CL_QGOH_07:GA-01","CL_QGOH_07:GA-05","CH_QGOH_PQDP:ALT2","CH_QGOH_PQ_7:SD2"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"Composite","passages":[]},{"id":"CL_QGOH_07:BSD-04","text":"Aeternitas Mentis salutem personalem non promittit, sed identitatem empiricam dividit inter structuram memorativam pereuntem et intelligibilitatem essentiae quae tempori non subiacet.","refs":["CL_QGOH_07:GA-06","CL_QGOH_07:GA-09","CH_QGOH_PQ_6:D6","CH_QGOH_PQ_7:DNX1"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"Negative","passages":[]},{"id":"CL_QGOH_07:BSD-05","text":"Disciplina Corporis effectum ontologicum acquirit: multiplicata aptitudine ad agendum, Mens plura ordinate intelligit et minor pars eius a duratione imaginativa definitur.","refs":["CL_QGOH_07:GA-05","CL_QGOH_07:GA-06","CL_QGOH_07:GA-09","CH_QGOH_PQ_6:FGS6","CH_QGOH_PQ_7:SD4"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"Composite","passages":[]},{"id":"CL_QGOH_07:BSD-06","text":"Communicatio cognitionis et amoris non solum compatibilis cum libertate est, sed eam amplificat, quia summum bonum intellectum participatione aliena non consumitur.","refs":["CL_QGOH_07:GA-10","CL_QGOH_07:GA-11","CH_QGOH_PQ_6:A3","CH_QGOH_PQ_6:D3"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"Composite","passages":[]},{"id":"CL_QGOH_07:BSD-07","text":"Theologia in reflexivam ontologiam convertitur: Deus non alter affectivus est, sed causa immanens cuius infinita sui cognitio per Amorem intellectualem Mentis finite explicatur.","refs":["CL_QGOH_07:GA-08","CL_QGOH_07:GA-10","CH_QGOH_PQ_6:A14","CH_QGOH_PQ_6:D11"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"Composite","passages":[]}],"original_name":"Book-Scale Super-Derivatives"},{"number":"10","name":"Recursive Loops, Escalation Paths, and Conceptual Attractors","items":[{"id":"CL_QGOH_07:RLE-01","text":"Ignorantia causarum rem ut liberam et externam figit, haec fixatio affectum auget, affectus autem Mentem impedit atque ignorantiam causalem reproducingit.","refs":["CH_QGOH_PQDP:A6","CH_QGOH_PQ_6:D5"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[]},{"id":"CL_QGOH_07:RLE-02","text":"Claritas affectum a causa externa confuse imaginata separat, separatio ordinationem permittit, ordinatio actionem auget, et actio ulteriorem claritatem sustinet.","refs":["CH_QGOH_PQDP:D1","CH_QGOH_PQ_6:A5"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[]},{"id":"CL_QGOH_07:RLE-03","text":"Praeceptum memoriae mandatum imagines futuras ordinat; occursus ita praeparatus affectum rationalem excitat, qui eandem dispositionem memorativam confirmat.","refs":["CH_QGOH_PQDP:A13","CH_QGOH_PQDP:D6"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[]},{"id":"CL_QGOH_07:RLE-04","text":"Cognitio secundi generis cupiditatem intuitionis parit; intuitio laetitiam et Amorem intellectualem producit, quibus aptitudo ad ulteriorem intuitionem amplificatur.","refs":["CH_QGOH_PQ_6:D8","CH_QGOH_PQ_6:A12"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[]},{"id":"CL_QGOH_07:RLE-05","text":"Aptitudo Corporis plures actiones aperit, plures actiones ampliorem cognitionem sustentant, cognitio passiones minuit, et imminutio passionum Corpus ad ordinem intellectus expeditius disponit.","refs":["CH_QGOH_PQ_6:A17","CH_QGOH_PQ_7:A9"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[]},{"id":"CL_QGOH_07:RLE-06","text":"Amor boni communis participatione aliorum augetur; incrementum gaudii communis Amorem iterum nutrit, unde socialitas rationalis circuitum amplificationis format.","refs":["CH_QGOH_PQ_6:D3","CH_QGOH_PQ_6:A3"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[]},{"id":"CL_QGOH_07:RLE-07","text":"Beatitudo est attractor terminalis qui terminum in machinam vertit: quo magis adest, eo plus Mens intelligit, minus patitur et potentius eandem beatitudinem conservat.","refs":["CH_QGOH_PQ_7:A8","CH_QGOH_PQ_7:D3"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[]}],"original_name":"Recursive Loops, Escalation Paths, and Conceptual Attractors"},{"number":"11","name":"Formal Generative Schema of the Whole Book","items":[{"id":"CL_QGOH_07:FGS-B01","text":"Variatio corporis intra nexum necessarium causarum → diversus gradus aptitudinis et diversae condiciones initiales passionis atque cognitionis.","refs":["CH_QGOH_PQ_7:AS1","CH_QGOH_PQDP:A7"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[]},{"id":"CL_QGOH_07:FGS-B02","text":"Idea inadaequata + attributio causae externae → affectus passivus + fixatio imaginativa + dependentia a viribus externis.","refs":["CH_QGOH_PQDP:FGS2","CH_QGOH_PQ_6:A4"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[]},{"id":"CL_QGOH_07:FGS-B03","text":"Conceptus clarus affectus + intellectus necessitatis → separatio a causa confuse imaginata → imminutio passivitatis.","refs":["CH_QGOH_PQDP:D1","CH_QGOH_PQ_6:A5"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[]},{"id":"CL_QGOH_07:FGS-B04","text":"Ordo idearum → ordo affectionum Corporis → concatenatio imaginum → memoria praeceptorum → responsio rationalis anticipata.","refs":["CH_QGOH_PQDP:A13","CH_QGOH_PQDP:D6"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[]},{"id":"CL_QGOH_07:FGS-B05","text":"Ratio adaequata → cupiditas cognitionis tertii generis → intuitio essentiae singularis ex dependentia divina.","refs":["CH_QGOH_PQ_6:A10","CH_QGOH_PQ_6:D8"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[]},{"id":"CL_QGOH_07:FGS-B06","text":"Actualitas temporalis Corporis / essentia Corporis in Deo → imaginatio et memoria pereuntes / pars Mentis aeterna.","refs":["CH_QGOH_PQ_6:D7","CH_QGOH_PQ_6:NX6"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[]},{"id":"CL_QGOH_07:FGS-B07","text":"Cognitio intuitiva → summa laetitia cum idea Dei → Amor Dei intellectualis aeternus.","refs":["CH_QGOH_PQ_6:D10","CH_QGOH_PQ_6:A13"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[]},{"id":"CL_QGOH_07:FGS-B08","text":"Amor intellectualis ut explicatio amoris divini → beatitudo ut virtus → augmentum intellectus → imminutio passionum → libertas stabilis sub necessitate.","refs":["CH_QGOH_PQ_6:D11","CH_QGOH_PQ_7:A7","CH_QGOH_PQ_7:A8"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[]}],"original_name":"Formal Generative Schema of the Whole Book"},{"number":"12","name":"Whole-Book Argument Flow Map","items":[{"id":"CL_QGOH_07:AFM-B01","text":"Imperium voluntatis negatum → affectus ut idea confusa reconstitutus → necessitas intellecta → ordinatio imaginum et Corporis → cognitio tertii generis → Amor intellectualis → beatitudo activa → libertas sapientis.","refs":["CH_QGOH_PQDP:AF1","CH_QGOH_PQ_7:AF1"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[]},{"id":"CL_QGOH_07:AFM-B02","text":"Deus ut causa externa affective imaginata → impossibilitas odii et amoris reciproci → idea Dei adaequata → Amor intellectualis → amor Mentis ut explicatio amoris quo Deus se ipsum amat.","refs":["CH_QGOH_PQ_6:AS3","CH_QGOH_PQ_6:D11"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[]},{"id":"CL_QGOH_07:AFM-B03","text":"Corpus durans → imaginatio, memoria et praeparatio affectiva → Corpus ad plura aptum → amplius intelligere → maior proportio Mentis aeterna → imminutio mortis metus.","refs":["CH_QGOH_PQ_6:AS5","CH_QGOH_PQ_6:A17","CH_QGOH_PQ_6:D13"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[]}],"original_name":"Whole-Book Argument Flow Map"},{"number":"13","name":"Macro Map-Effects","items":[{"id":"CL_QGOH_07:MME-01","text":"Libertas a dominio voluntatis et exemptione causali ad transformationem graduatam proportionis inter actionem et passionem reordinatur.","refs":["CH_QGOH_PQDP:ME2","CH_QGOH_PQ_6:SD1"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[]},{"id":"CL_QGOH_07:MME-02","text":"Affectus ab impetu psychologico externo ad structuram ideationalem, corporalem et causaliter ordinabilem reclassificatur.","refs":["CH_QGOH_PQDP:ME1","CH_QGOH_PQ_6:ME1"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[]},{"id":"CL_QGOH_07:MME-03","text":"Corpus a mero correlato mortalitatis ad instrumentum dispositionis rationalis et ampliandae potentiae Mentis transfertur.","refs":["CH_QGOH_PQ_6:ME5","CH_QGOH_PQ_7:ME5"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[]},{"id":"CL_QGOH_07:MME-04","text":"Deus ab obiecto passionis reciprocae ad causam immanentem et structuram reflexivam Amoris intellectualis reclassificatur.","refs":["CH_QGOH_PQ_6:ME3","CH_QGOH_PQ_6:D11"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[]},{"id":"CL_QGOH_07:MME-05","text":"Aeternitas a continuatione temporali personae ad modum concipiendi essentiam sub necessitate divina transfertur.","refs":["CH_QGOH_PQ_6:ME2","CH_QGOH_PQ_6:SNX2"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[]},{"id":"CL_QGOH_07:MME-06","text":"Virtus a disciplina sustentata praemio et poena ad bonum immanens, non-rivale et causaliter beatificum convertitur.","refs":["CH_QGOH_PQ_7:ME2","CH_QGOH_PQ_7:ME3"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[]},{"id":"CL_QGOH_07:MME-07","text":"Cognitio ab inspectione rerum ad operationem transformantem transit, quae affectus, Corpus, temporalitatem et statum subiecti simul ordinat.","refs":["CH_QGOH_PQ_7:SD2","CH_QGOH_PQDP:ALT2"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[]}],"original_name":"Macro Map-Effects"},{"number":"14","name":"Altitude Integration","items":[{"id":"CL_QGOH_07:ALT-B01","text":"In gradu superficiei agitur de moderandis affectibus, intelligenda aeternitate Mentis, minuendo mortis metu et constituenda beatitudine.","refs":["CH_QGOH_PQDP:ALT1","CH_QGOH_PQ_6:ALT1","CH_QGOH_PQ_7:ALT1"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[]},{"id":"CL_QGOH_07:ALT-B02","text":"In gradu structurali ordines idearum, affectionum Corporis, imaginationis, generum cognitionis et actionis unam distributionem causalem efficiunt.","refs":["CH_QGOH_PQDP:ALT2","CH_QGOH_PQ_7:ALT2"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[]},{"id":"CL_QGOH_07:ALT-B03","text":"In gradu metastrategico certamen a repressione affectuum ad productionem activitatis, laetitiae et cognitionis aptae transfertur.","refs":["CH_QGOH_PQDP:ALT3","CH_QGOH_PQ_6:ALT3"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[]},{"id":"CL_QGOH_07:ALT-B04","text":"In gradu ontologico et temporali realitas est nexus necessarius et immanens, sub quo duratio imaginativa ab aeternitate intelligibili distinguitur sine oppositione substantialis Mentis et Corporis.","refs":["CH_QGOH_PQDP:ALT4","CH_QGOH_PQ_6:ALT4"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[]},{"id":"CL_QGOH_07:ALT-B05","text":"In gradu methodologico et performativo demonstratio ipsam transformationem quam docet exercet: terminos passionis ordinatim negat, reclassificat et in circuitum activum beatitudinis componit.","refs":["CH_QGOH_PQ_6:AS2","CH_QGOH_PQ_7:ALT3"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[]}],"original_name":"Altitude Integration"},{"number":"15","name":"Internal Tensions, Mutation Points, and Fault Lines","items":[{"id":"CL_QGOH_07:FL-01","text":"Assertio quod omnis affectio conceptum clarum involvat non plene determinat quantum claritatis sufficiat ut affectus ipse desinat esse passio.","refs":["CH_QGOH_PQDP:MRT3","CH_QGOH_PQDP:A4"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[]},{"id":"CL_QGOH_07:FL-02","text":"Pluralitas causarum affectum simul roborare et eius noxam minuere potest; systema distinctionem inter concursum, distributionem et frequentiam requirit, sed non universaliter formalizat.","refs":["CH_QGOH_PQDP:MRT4","CH_QGOH_PQDP:D5"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[]},{"id":"CL_QGOH_07:FL-03","text":"Impassibilitas Dei et identitas Amoris Mentis cum amore divino tensionem semanticam pariunt, quae sola mutatione notionis amoris administratur.","refs":["CH_QGOH_PQDP:A16","CH_QGOH_PQ_6:D11"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[]},{"id":"CL_QGOH_07:FL-04","text":"Amor intellectualis aeternus sine principio dicitur, sed accessus Mentis ad cognitionem tertii generis ut progressio gradatim acquisita narratur.","refs":["CH_QGOH_PQ_6:MRT5","CH_QGOH_PQ_6:D8"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[]},{"id":"CL_QGOH_07:FL-05","text":"Nexus inter aptitudinem Corporis et maiorem partem Mentis aeternam affirmatur potentius quam mechanismus proportionis demonstratur.","refs":["CH_QGOH_PQ_6:MRT6","CH_QGOH_PQ_7:MRT2"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[]},{"id":"CL_QGOH_07:FL-06","text":"Virtus ab cognitione aeternitatis independens est, quamvis summa figura sapientis per intellectum aeternum et Amorem intellectualem definiatur.","refs":["CH_QGOH_PQ_7:MRT3","CH_QGOH_PQ_7:A6"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[]},{"id":"CL_QGOH_07:MP-01","text":"Affectus mutatur ab idea confusa singularis passionis in indicem proportionis causalis inter determinationem externam et activitatem Mentis.","refs":["CH_QGOH_PQDP:A3","CH_QGOH_PQ_6:ME1"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[]},{"id":"CL_QGOH_07:MP-02","text":"Amor Dei sensum mutat ubi reciproca passio excluditur et idem nomen actioni intellectus atque divinae sui cognitioni assignatur.","refs":["CH_QGOH_PQ_6:NX2","CH_QGOH_PQ_6:A14"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[]},{"id":"CL_QGOH_07:MP-03","text":"Corpus functionem mutat ubi ex sede durationis et imaginationis fit dispositio positiva ad plures actiones et ampliorem cognitionem.","refs":["CH_QGOH_PQ_6:A7","CH_QGOH_PQ_6:A17"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[]},{"id":"CL_QGOH_07:MP-04","text":"Beatitudo statum mutat ubi ex fine disciplinae in ipsam virtutem et causam coercendi libidines invertitur.","refs":["CH_QGOH_PQ_7:NX3","CH_QGOH_PQ_7:D3"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[]},{"id":"CL_QGOH_07:MP-05","text":"Libertas mutatur ubi moderatio affectuum intra durationem in aeternam participationem actionis intellectualis et stabilitatem sub necessitate absorbetur.","refs":["CH_QGOH_PQDP:ME2","CH_QGOH_PQ_7:ME4"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[]}],"original_name":"Internal Tensions, Mutation Points, and Fault Lines"},{"number":"16","name":"Hidden System Image","items":[{"id":"CL_QGOH_07:HSI-01","text":"Realitas implicite est reticulum immanens causarum et idearum, in quo nulla voluntas extrinseca imperat, sed partes secundum gradum quo causas interne explicant agunt vel patiuntur.","refs":["CH_QGOH_PQDP:A2","CH_QGOH_PQ_7:SNX2"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[]},{"id":"CL_QGOH_07:HSI-02","text":"Subiectum est nodus proportionaliter mutabilis huius reticuli, non centrum suum; fit liber quatenus nexus qui ipsum determinant clarius per ipsum exprimuntur.","refs":["CH_QGOH_PQ_6:A4","CH_QGOH_PQ_7:A1"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[]},{"id":"CL_QGOH_07:HSI-03","text":"Tempus bifariam ordinatur: duratio imagines, memoriam et passionem sustinet, aeternitas autem essentiam sine principio, successione aut recordatione exhibet.","refs":["CH_QGOH_PQ_6:D7","CH_QGOH_PQ_6:SNX2"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[]},{"id":"CL_QGOH_07:HSI-04","text":"Certamen non est expulsio affectuum, sed conversio canalium causalium, qua fixatio externa in ordinem idearum, laetitiam activam et Amorem intellectualem traducitur.","refs":["CH_QGOH_PQ_6:A5","CH_QGOH_PQ_7:SD2"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[]},{"id":"CL_QGOH_07:HSI-05","text":"Navigatio possibilis est spiralis: Corpus aptius, Mens clarior, affectus minus passivus et beatitudo potentior invicem se amplificant, donec mors centrum ordinis ethici amittat.","refs":["CH_QGOH_PQ_7:A9","CH_QGOH_PQ_7:A8","CH_QGOH_PQ_6:A16"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[]}],"original_name":"Hidden System Image"},{"number":"17","name":"Minimal Whole-Book Reconstruction Test","items":[{"id":"CL_QGOH_07:MRT-B01","text":"Axiomata praesentia sufficiunt ad nucleum restituendum: intelligentia mutat passiones in actiones, necessitas intellecta libertatem constituit, et Amor intellectualis beatitudinem immanentem producit.","refs":["CH_QGOH_PQDP:MRT1","CH_QGOH_PQ_7:MRT1"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[]},{"id":"CL_QGOH_07:MRT-B02","text":"Structura firmissima est circuitus inter ideam adaequatam, intellectum necessitatis, imminutam passivitatem, laetitiam et auctam potentiam intelligendi.","refs":["CH_QGOH_PQDP:MRT2","CH_QGOH_PQ_7:MRT4"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[]},{"id":"CL_QGOH_07:MRT-B03","text":"Minus determinata manent quantitas cognitionis requisita ad passionem mutandam, mechanismus quo aptitudo Corporis maiorem partem Mentis aeternam efficit, et relatio experientiae aeternitatis ad absentiam memoriae.","refs":["CH_QGOH_PQ_6:MRT3","CH_QGOH_PQ_6:MRT4","CH_QGOH_PQ_7:MRT2"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[]},{"id":"CL_QGOH_07:MRT-B04","text":"Cohaerentia huius gradus non ex ordine lineari solo, sed ex reditu mutato eorundem terminorum constat: affectus, Corpus, amor, libertas et beatitudo singulis reditibus novam functionem accipiunt.","refs":["CH_QGOH_PQ_6:MRT7","CH_QGOH_PQ_7:MRT6"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[]},{"id":"CL_QGOH_07:MRT-B05","text":"Restitutio verum motum servat si remedia temporalia affectuum non a doctrina aeternitatis separantur, sed ut dispositio ad cognitionem intuitivam et beatitudinem leguntur.","refs":["CH_QGOH_PQ_6:MRT7","CH_QGOH_PQ_7:AF1"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[]},{"id":"CL_QGOH_07:MRT-B06","text":"Reductioni axiomaticae adhuc resistit transitus ab demonstratione aeternitatis ad affirmationem experientialem eius atque imago terminalis sapientis, cuius vis affectiva plus operatur quam catena demonstrativa sola explicat.","refs":["CH_QGOH_PQ_6:MRT4","CH_QGOH_PQ_7:AS6"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[]}],"original_name":"Minimal Whole-Book Reconstruction Test"}],"language_code":"la","language_name":"Latina","translations":[{"id":"CAREVAR_20261015T015742818396Z_796022d9","language_code":"en","language_name":"English","title":"Chapters 19–21","source_sha256":"82979d3790e86ac8110cdff861396fa6f23f5568f8f95ee07e7d56a68a9a240d","sha256":"98cdae7e1840daec1391c1e1b763dbb5cd85e277a5246c941a4aac4c4d897315","rendition_type":"machine","review_status":"machine","model":"gpt-5.6-terra","created":"2026-10-15T01:57:42.822264+00:00","sections":[{"number":"1","name":"Book-Scale Argumental Spine","items":[{"id":"CL_QGOH_07:AS-B01","text":"The system asks how the necessary Mind, conjoined to the Body and subject to external causes, acquires freedom without dominion of the will, without exemption from causality, and without an economy of reward after death.","refs":["CH_QGOH_PQDP:AS1","CH_QGOH_PQ_6:SD1","CH_QGOH_PQ_7:NX1"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[]},{"id":"CL_QGOH_07:AS-B02","text":"The global thesis locates the power of the Mind in understanding, which, by ordering the affects, advances toward intuitive knowledge, the intellectual Love of God, and blessedness as an immanent virtue.","refs":["CH_QGOH_PQDP:A1","CH_QGOH_PQ_6:A13","CH_QGOH_PQ_7:A7"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[]},{"id":"CL_QGOH_07:AS-B03","text":"The demonstrative argument is simultaneously reconstructive: it not only negates the categories of will, passion, love, eternity, and blessedness, but reconstitutes them according to the causality of ideas.","refs":["CH_QGOH_PQ_6:AS2","CH_QGOH_PQ_7:ALT3"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[]},{"id":"CL_QGOH_07:AS-B04","text":"The movement passes from the confused attribution of an affect to an external cause to intellectual ordering, then to the third kind of knowledge, and finally to eternal action that bears its own joy with it.","refs":["CH_QGOH_PQ_6:A5","CH_QGOH_PQ_6:A9","CH_QGOH_PQ_6:A12"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[]},{"id":"CL_QGOH_07:AS-B05","text":"True progress is spiral-shaped, because knowledge diminishes passivity, joy increases knowledge, and blessedness in turn produces a greater power of knowing and ordering the affects.","refs":["CH_QGOH_PQ_7:A8","CH_QGOH_PQ_7:D3"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[]},{"id":"CL_QGOH_07:AS-B06","text":"The Body is transformed from the seat of duration, imagination, and passions into a strategic condition, whose aptitude sustains more actions, broader knowledge, and a greater eternal part of the Mind.","refs":["CH_QGOH_PQ_6:A7","CH_QGOH_PQ_6:A17","CH_QGOH_PQ_6:SD5"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[]},{"id":"CL_QGOH_07:AS-B07","text":"The closure produces the figure of the wise person, whose freedom is not indeterminacy but stability under understood necessity, in contrast to the ignorant person, who is determined by external causes and fluctuating desires.","refs":["CH_QGOH_PQ_7:AS6","CH_QGOH_PQ_7:D6"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[]},{"id":"CL_QGOH_07:AS-B08","text":"The terminus of the argument turns into a cause: blessedness, which first appears as the end of liberation, ultimately becomes virtue itself and the principle of power over the affects.","refs":["CH_QGOH_PQ_7:AS5","CH_QGOH_PQ_7:D3"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[]}],"original_name":"Book-Scale Argumental Spine"},{"number":"2","name":"Global Load-Bearing Axioms","items":[{"id":"CL_QGOH_07:GA-01","text":"[Persistent] The power of the Mind is defined not by a separate will, but by the degree of understanding and the formation of adequate ideas.","refs":["CH_QGOH_PQDP:A1","CH_QGOH_PQ_6:ALT2"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[]},{"id":"CL_QGOH_07:GA-02","text":"[Persistent] A passion is an affect insofar as it arises from a confused idea; its clarification does not destroy the object, but transfers the state of the Mind from undergoing to acting.","refs":["CH_QGOH_PQDP:A3","CH_QGOH_PQ_7:A3"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[]},{"id":"CL_QGOH_07:GA-03","text":"[Mutating] Freedom extends from the gradual diminution of passivity to the stable predominance of intellectual action and finally to active blessedness.","refs":["CH_QGOH_PQDP:ME2","CH_QGOH_PQ_6:SD1"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[]},{"id":"CL_QGOH_07:GA-04","text":"[Persistent] The necessity of causes is not the opposite of freedom, but an order whose understanding mitigates affect and constitutes the action of the wise person.","refs":["CH_QGOH_PQDP:A7","CH_QGOH_PQ_7:DNX5"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[]},{"id":"CL_QGOH_07:GA-05","text":"[Persistent] The order of ideas, affections of the Body, images, and memory constitute one mechanism through which knowledge becomes affective and bodily disposition.","refs":["CH_QGOH_PQDP:A2","CH_QGOH_PQDP:A12"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[]},{"id":"CL_QGOH_07:GA-06","text":"[Mutating] The Body is at once the condition of temporal imagination and the instrument of aptitude, by which the Mind is disposed toward more actions and a broader participation in eternity.","refs":["CH_QGOH_PQ_6:A7","CH_QGOH_PQ_6:A17"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[]},{"id":"CL_QGOH_07:GA-07","text":"[Emergent] The kinds of knowledge form an asymmetric sequence: the confusion of the first kind does not produce the third; Reason opens the transition, whereas intuition knows singular essences from God.","refs":["CH_QGOH_PQ_6:A9","CH_QGOH_PQ_6:A10"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[]},{"id":"CL_QGOH_07:GA-08","text":"[Mutating] Love of God is transformed from a passive and reciprocal relation into intellectual Love, by which the Mind's love is part of God's active and infinite knowledge of himself.","refs":["CH_QGOH_PQDP:A16","CH_QGOH_PQ_6:A14"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[]},{"id":"CL_QGOH_07:GA-09","text":"[Emergent] The eternity of the Mind is neither indefinite duration nor preservation of personal memory, but conception of the essence of the Body and Mind under divine necessity.","refs":["CH_QGOH_PQ_6:NX6","CH_QGOH_PQ_6:DNX2"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[]},{"id":"CL_QGOH_07:GA-10","text":"[Attractor] Knowledge of the third kind, supreme joy, intellectual Love of God, and blessedness converge in one attractor that does not repress affects but surpasses them through activity.","refs":["CH_QGOH_PQ_6:A12","CH_QGOH_PQ_7:A6"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[]},{"id":"CL_QGOH_07:GA-11","text":"[Emergent] Virtue, seeking what is properly useful by reason, is separated from hope and fear, and blessedness becomes not the reward of virtue but its constitutive exercise.","refs":["CH_QGOH_PQ_7:A4","CH_QGOH_PQ_7:NX3"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[]},{"id":"CL_QGOH_07:GA-12","text":"[Terminal] The distinction between the wise and the ignorant is a diversity of modes of existing: the wise person's stability arises from understood necessity, whereas ignorance arises from external determination and fluctuation.","refs":["CH_QGOH_PQ_7:D6","CH_QGOH_PQ_7:AS6"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[]}],"original_name":"Global Load-Bearing Axioms"},{"number":"3","name":"Recurrence, Mutation, and Displacement Matrix","items":[{"id":"CL_QGOH_07:RMD-01","text":"Understanding preserves its functional identity: from beginning to end it remains the single measure of the Mind's power, although it extends from the remedy of affects to eternal action.","refs":["CH_QGOH_PQDP:A1","CH_QGOH_PQ_6:ALT2"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[]},{"id":"CL_QGOH_07:RMD-02","text":"The affect is reclassified first as a confused idea, then as a proportion between external causes and knowledge, and finally as the material of beatific action.","refs":["CH_QGOH_PQDP:A3","CH_QGOH_PQ_6:ME1"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[]},{"id":"CL_QGOH_07:RMD-03","text":"Love of God undergoes an inversion: passive, reciprocal, and anthropomorphic love is excluded, while intellectual Love returns as the act by which God loves himself through the Mind.","refs":["CH_QGOH_PQDP:NX7","CH_QGOH_PQ_6:D11"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[]},{"id":"CL_QGOH_07:RMD-04","text":"Imagination and memory are initially instruments of rational preparation, but in the transition to eternity they exhaust their structure and vanish with the duration of the Body.","refs":["CH_QGOH_PQDP:ME4","CH_QGOH_PQ_6:NX5"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[]},{"id":"CL_QGOH_07:RMD-05","text":"The Body migrates from the polemical term of dualism to the temporal condition of passion, then to a positive instrument for expanding the Mind's power.","refs":["CH_QGOH_PQDP:NX2","CH_QGOH_PQ_7:SD4"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[]},{"id":"CL_QGOH_07:RMD-06","text":"Necessity is enlarged from an argument for mitigating pain into the ontological form of the wise person's freedom; the same causality that excludes free choice constitutes intelligible action.","refs":["CH_QGOH_PQDP:SD2","CH_QGOH_PQ_7:DNX5"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[]},{"id":"CL_QGOH_07:RMD-07","text":"Freedom is transferred from partial governance of the affects to a change in the proportion between action and passion, and thence to eternal blessedness.","refs":["CH_QGOH_PQDP:ME2","CH_QGOH_PQ_7:A8"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[]},{"id":"CL_QGOH_07:RMD-08","text":"Virtue is first distinguished by the adequate origin of Desire, but terminally it is identified with intellectual Love and blessedness itself.","refs":["CH_QGOH_PQDP:A17","CH_QGOH_PQ_7:A7"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[]},{"id":"CL_QGOH_07:RMD-09","text":"Fear of death is removed from the moral center and absorbed into an effect of inadequate knowledge; as the understanding part increases, the importance of the perishing part diminishes.","refs":["CH_QGOH_PQ_6:A16","CH_QGOH_PQ_7:SNX3"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[]}],"original_name":"Recurrence, Mutation, and Displacement Matrix"},{"number":"4","name":"Lower-Layer vs Current-Level Distinction","items":[{"id":"CL_QGOH_07:CGD-01","text":"The mechanics of the concurrence of causes, objective distribution, and frequency of images locally explains the governance of affects, but at this level it is ordered under the broader transformation of action and passion.","refs":["CH_QGOH_PQDP:D5","CH_QGOH_PQDP:ALT2"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[]},{"id":"CL_QGOH_07:CGD-02","text":"The refutation of Stoic and Cartesian will retains the function of a negative foundation, but does not constitute the closure, which is produced by intellectual Love and blessedness.","refs":["CH_QGOH_PQDP:AS2","CH_QGOH_PQ_7:A6"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[]},{"id":"CL_QGOH_07:CGD-03","text":"The impossibility of hatred toward God, initially a terminus sustained by demonstration, becomes at a higher level the condition for transforming passive love into intellectual action.","refs":["CH_QGOH_PQDP:AS7","CH_QGOH_PQ_6:NX1"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[]},{"id":"CL_QGOH_07:CGD-04","text":"The relation of all affections to the idea of God is enlarged from a local remedy for affects into the foundation of knowledge of the third kind and eternal love.","refs":["CH_QGOH_PQDP:A14","CH_QGOH_PQ_6:A11"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[]},{"id":"CL_QGOH_07:CGD-05","text":"The distinction between temporal and eternal actuality joins lower unities, because the same essence of the Body separates enduring imagination from eternal intellect.","refs":["CH_QGOH_PQ_6:D7","CH_QGOH_PQ_6:SD2"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[]},{"id":"CL_QGOH_07:CGD-06","text":"The sterility of the first kind with respect to intuitive knowledge turns a local doctrine of knowledge into a law of transition: only adequate knowledge generates higher knowledge.","refs":["CH_QGOH_PQ_6:NX7","CH_QGOH_PQ_6:ME4"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[]},{"id":"CL_QGOH_07:CGD-07","text":"The foundation of virtue in rational advantage remains independent of immortality, but at this level it is joined to knowledge of eternity without reduction of one to the other.","refs":["CH_QGOH_PQ_7:AS4","CH_QGOH_PQ_7:MRT3"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[]},{"id":"CL_QGOH_07:CGD-08","text":"The aptitude of the Body exceeds the status of a local proposition and becomes the joint between temporal practice, the ordering of affects, and the eternal proportion of the Mind.","refs":["CH_QGOH_PQ_7:A9","CH_QGOH_PQ_6:SD5"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[]}],"original_name":"Lower-Layer vs Current-Level Distinction"},{"number":"5","name":"Global Apophatic Structure","items":[{"id":"CL_QGOH_07:GNX-01","text":"Freedom is neither absolute dominion of the will nor an act of naked coercion, but the effect of understanding that changes the causal state of affects.","refs":["CH_QGOH_PQDP:NX1","CH_QGOH_PQ_7:DNX3"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[]},{"id":"CL_QGOH_07:GNX-02","text":"Freedom does not require a point outside the series of causes, because exemption from the determination of the natural order would destroy the very foundation of knowledge.","refs":["CH_QGOH_PQDP:SNX2","CH_QGOH_PQ_7:SNX2"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[]},{"id":"CL_QGOH_07:GNX-03","text":"An affect is not a force wholly extrinsic to knowledge or an opaque residue, since a change in knowledge can dissolve its passive form.","refs":["CH_QGOH_PQDP:NX4","CH_QGOH_PQDP:DNX1"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[]},{"id":"CL_QGOH_07:GNX-04","text":"God is not a participant in reciprocal affective commerce, nor can Love of God require that the divine nature be transformed toward finite desire.","refs":["CH_QGOH_PQDP:NX7","CH_QGOH_PQ_6:NX2"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[]},{"id":"CL_QGOH_07:GNX-05","text":"Eternity is not the continuation of duration, memory, imagination, or the empirical narrative of a person.","refs":["CH_QGOH_PQ_6:NX6","CH_QGOH_PQ_6:DNX2"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[]},{"id":"CL_QGOH_07:GNX-06","text":"Virtue is founded neither on hope of reward nor fear of punishment; the moral economy of compensation belongs to affective servitude.","refs":["CH_QGOH_PQ_7:NX1","CH_QGOH_PQ_7:SNX1"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[]},{"id":"CL_QGOH_07:GNX-07","text":"Blessedness is not a later reward for victory over desires, but a present force from which the power of ordering affects follows.","refs":["CH_QGOH_PQ_7:NX3","CH_QGOH_PQ_7:D3"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[]},{"id":"CL_QGOH_07:GNX-08","text":"The Body is not the adversary of eternity, because its temporal aptitude can effect a disposition toward intellectual action.","refs":["CH_QGOH_PQ_6:SD5","CH_QGOH_PQ_7:SD4"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[]}],"original_name":"Global Apophatic Structure"},{"number":"6","name":"Deep Book Apophatics","items":[{"id":"CL_QGOH_07:DBN-01","text":"It must be false that the Mind stands outside Nature and by naked will breaks the order of the Body; otherwise the correspondence of ideas and affections would waver.","refs":["CH_QGOH_PQDP:DNX2","CH_QGOH_PQDP:A2"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[]},{"id":"CL_QGOH_07:DBN-02","text":"It must be false that the word love signifies the same passion in human beings and in God; divine impassibility and divine love cohere only through the transformation from passive affect to intellectual action.","refs":["CH_QGOH_PQDP:DNX5","CH_QGOH_PQ_6:D11"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[]},{"id":"CL_QGOH_07:DBN-03","text":"It must be false that the eternal identity of the Mind coincides with the imaginative history of a singular person, because memory and imagination are restricted to the duration of the Body.","refs":["CH_QGOH_PQ_7:DNX1","CH_QGOH_PQ_6:DNX2"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[]},{"id":"CL_QGOH_07:DBN-04","text":"It must be false that the highest good has a rivalrous or scarcely distributed structure; its communication increases Love rather than diminishing anyone's share.","refs":["CH_QGOH_PQ_6:DNX5","CH_QGOH_PQ_6:SD3"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[]},{"id":"CL_QGOH_07:DBN-05","text":"It must be false that knowledge is inert representation, since adequate knowledge effects joy, bodily ordering, and a greater power of acting.","refs":["CH_QGOH_PQ_6:NX4","CH_QGOH_PQ_7:A8"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[]},{"id":"CL_QGOH_07:DBN-06","text":"It must be false that the mortality of the Body abolishes every ground of virtue; living rightly has an immanent foundation, and death destroys only the temporal structure of the Mind.","refs":["CH_QGOH_PQ_7:DNX2","CH_QGOH_PQ_7:SNX3"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[]}],"original_name":"Deep Book Apophatics"},{"number":"7","name":"Super-Apophatics of the Whole","items":[{"id":"CL_QGOH_07:BSA-01","text":"A finite subject subsisting outside God, which contemplates or loves the cause of the whole from an external position, is ontologically inadmissible.","refs":["CH_QGOH_PQ_6:SNX1"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[]},{"id":"CL_QGOH_07:BSA-02","text":"A personal God who responds to prayers, offense, reciprocal love, or a change of will is unavailable; the divine relation is relocated within the action of understanding.","refs":["CH_QGOH_PQ_6:DNX3","CH_QGOH_PQ_6:NX2"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[]},{"id":"CL_QGOH_07:BSA-03","text":"A temporal measure by which eternity might be quantified or the future continuation of personal life proved is inadmissible.","refs":["CH_QGOH_PQ_6:SNX2"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[]},{"id":"CL_QGOH_07:BSA-04","text":"The system is ordered around the absence of a remunerating judge: without reward, punishment, and post-mortem compensation, virtue must nevertheless have a sufficient cause.","refs":["CH_QGOH_PQ_7:SNX1","CH_QGOH_PQ_7:A4"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[]},{"id":"CL_QGOH_07:BSA-05","text":"The destruction of intellectual truth by a contrary affect is held impossible, because such destruction would require the falsity of what is necessarily understood.","refs":["CH_QGOH_PQ_6:SNX3","CH_QGOH_PQ_6:NX9"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[]}],"original_name":"Super-Apophatics of the Whole"},{"number":"8","name":"Cross-Unit Derivative Propositions","items":[{"id":"CL_QGOH_07:XD-01","text":"Freedom receives a dual but continuous structure: in duration it is the ordering of images and diminution of passion; under eternity it is intuitive action containing blessedness and intellectual Love.","refs":["CL_QGOH_07:GA-03","CL_QGOH_07:GA-05","CL_QGOH_07:GA-07","CL_QGOH_07:GA-10","CH_QGOH_PQDP:D6","CH_QGOH_PQ_6:D10","CH_QGOH_PQ_7:A7"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"Composite","passages":[]},{"id":"CL_QGOH_07:XD-02","text":"Knowledge and freedom produce a positive circuit: knowledge diminishes passion, adequate knowledge generates the desire for higher knowledge, and blessedness in turn increases the faculty of understanding.","refs":["CL_QGOH_07:GA-01","CL_QGOH_07:GA-02","CL_QGOH_07:GA-07","CL_QGOH_07:GA-10","CH_QGOH_PQDP:D1","CH_QGOH_PQ_6:D8","CH_QGOH_PQ_7:A8"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"Composite","passages":[]},{"id":"CL_QGOH_07:XD-03","text":"The negation of divine love and the affirmation of the love by which God loves human beings can cohere only if the name of love is transformed from reciprocal passion into the eternal action of self-knowledge.","refs":["CL_QGOH_07:GA-08","CH_QGOH_PQDP:A16","CH_QGOH_PQ_6:D11"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"Tension","passages":[]},{"id":"CL_QGOH_07:XD-04","text":"The Body is at once the terminus of the perishing part of the Mind and the condition for increasing the active part; its temporality therefore is not simply opposed to eternity, but disposes toward it.","refs":["CL_QGOH_07:GA-06","CL_QGOH_07:GA-09","CH_QGOH_PQ_6:A7","CH_QGOH_PQ_6:A17","CH_QGOH_PQ_7:SD4"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"Tension","passages":[]},{"id":"CL_QGOH_07:XD-05","text":"Understanding necessity and imaginative preparation jointly bring it about that action is not an interruption of causality, but an ordered participation in the nexus of causes.","refs":["CL_QGOH_07:GA-04","CL_QGOH_07:GA-05","CH_QGOH_PQDP:D6","CH_QGOH_PQ_7:DNX5","CH_QGOH_PQ_7:D6"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"Composite","passages":[]},{"id":"CL_QGOH_07:XD-06","text":"The community of the highest good and the independence of virtue from reward constitute a non-rivalrous ethics, in which another's blessedness can increase one's own power.","refs":["CL_QGOH_07:GA-10","CL_QGOH_07:GA-11","CH_QGOH_PQ_6:D3","CH_QGOH_PQ_7:SD1"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"Composite","passages":[]},{"id":"CL_QGOH_07:XD-07","text":"Imagination is a transitory structure: within duration it bodily prepares reason, but upon reaching the eternal state it loses its own operation and memory.","refs":["CL_QGOH_07:GA-05","CL_QGOH_07:GA-06","CL_QGOH_07:GA-09","CH_QGOH_PQDP:ME4","CH_QGOH_PQ_6:NX5","CH_QGOH_PQ_7:NX5"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"Tension","passages":[]},{"id":"CL_QGOH_07:XD-08","text":"The value of Desire depends not on its object but on its ideational origin; the same genealogy explains why confused knowledge cannot produce the third kind and why Reason opens the transition to intuition.","refs":["CL_QGOH_07:GA-02","CL_QGOH_07:GA-07","CL_QGOH_07:GA-11","CH_QGOH_PQDP:D7","CH_QGOH_PQ_6:A10","CH_QGOH_PQ_7:AS4"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"Composite","passages":[]},{"id":"CL_QGOH_07:XD-09","text":"Freedom from affects requires not their absolute extinction, but a change in the proportion between undergoing and acting; the increase of intellect is more effective than direct war against passions.","refs":["CL_QGOH_07:GA-02","CL_QGOH_07:GA-03","CH_QGOH_PQ_6:NX4","CH_QGOH_PQ_7:A1","CH_QGOH_PQ_7:D4"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"Composite","passages":[]}],"original_name":"Cross-Unit Derivative Propositions"},{"number":"9","name":"Book-Scale Super-Derivatives","items":[{"id":"CL_QGOH_07:BSD-01","text":"The free subject is not an undivided voluntary substance, but a mutable proportion of adequate and inadequate ideas, actions and passions, whose identity is ordered according to power.","refs":["CL_QGOH_07:GA-01","CL_QGOH_07:GA-02","CL_QGOH_07:GA-03","CH_QGOH_PQ_6:A4","CH_QGOH_PQ_7:A1"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"Composite","passages":[]},{"id":"CL_QGOH_07:BSD-02","text":"The power of acting increases not through remote causality, but through understood causality; autonomy is reclassified as the capacity internally to explain causes through adequate ideas.","refs":["CL_QGOH_07:GA-03","CL_QGOH_07:GA-04","CH_QGOH_PQDP:SD2","CH_QGOH_PQ_7:DNX5"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"Composite","passages":[]},{"id":"CL_QGOH_07:BSD-03","text":"Epistemology becomes affective technique, because truth not only provides knowledge of an object but changes bodily order, the frequency of images, and the faculty of acting.","refs":["CL_QGOH_07:GA-01","CL_QGOH_07:GA-05","CH_QGOH_PQDP:ALT2","CH_QGOH_PQ_7:SD2"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"Composite","passages":[]},{"id":"CL_QGOH_07:BSD-04","text":"The eternity of the Mind does not promise personal salvation, but divides empirical identity between a perishing memorial structure and the intelligibility of essence that is not subject to time.","refs":["CL_QGOH_07:GA-06","CL_QGOH_07:GA-09","CH_QGOH_PQ_6:D6","CH_QGOH_PQ_7:DNX1"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"Negative","passages":[]},{"id":"CL_QGOH_07:BSD-05","text":"The discipline of the Body acquires an ontological effect: with multiplied aptitude for acting, the Mind understands more things in order and a lesser part of it is defined by imaginative duration.","refs":["CL_QGOH_07:GA-05","CL_QGOH_07:GA-06","CL_QGOH_07:GA-09","CH_QGOH_PQ_6:FGS6","CH_QGOH_PQ_7:SD4"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"Composite","passages":[]},{"id":"CL_QGOH_07:BSD-06","text":"The communication of knowledge and love is not only compatible with freedom, but amplifies it, because the highest good understood is not consumed by another's participation.","refs":["CL_QGOH_07:GA-10","CL_QGOH_07:GA-11","CH_QGOH_PQ_6:A3","CH_QGOH_PQ_6:D3"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"Composite","passages":[]},{"id":"CL_QGOH_07:BSD-07","text":"Theology is converted into reflexive ontology: God is not an affective other, but the immanent cause whose infinite self-knowledge is finitely explicated through the intellectual Love of the Mind.","refs":["CL_QGOH_07:GA-08","CL_QGOH_07:GA-10","CH_QGOH_PQ_6:A14","CH_QGOH_PQ_6:D11"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"Composite","passages":[]}],"original_name":"Book-Scale Super-Derivatives"},{"number":"10","name":"Recursive Loops, Escalation Paths, and Conceptual Attractors","items":[{"id":"CL_QGOH_07:RLE-01","text":"Ignorance of causes fixes a thing as free and external, this fixation increases affect, and affect impedes the Mind and reproduces causal ignorance.","refs":["CH_QGOH_PQDP:A6","CH_QGOH_PQ_6:D5"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[]},{"id":"CL_QGOH_07:RLE-02","text":"Clarity separates an affect from the confusedly imagined external cause, separation permits ordering, ordering increases action, and action sustains further clarity.","refs":["CH_QGOH_PQDP:D1","CH_QGOH_PQ_6:A5"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[]},{"id":"CL_QGOH_07:RLE-03","text":"The precept of memory orders future images; an encounter thus prepared arouses rational affect, which confirms the same memorial disposition.","refs":["CH_QGOH_PQDP:A13","CH_QGOH_PQDP:D6"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[]},{"id":"CL_QGOH_07:RLE-04","text":"Knowledge of the second kind generates the desire for intuition; intuition produces joy and intellectual Love, through which the aptitude for further intuition is amplified.","refs":["CH_QGOH_PQ_6:D8","CH_QGOH_PQ_6:A12"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[]},{"id":"CL_QGOH_07:RLE-05","text":"The aptitude of the Body opens more actions, more actions sustain broader knowledge, knowledge diminishes passions, and the diminution of passions disposes the Body more readily toward the order of intellect.","refs":["CH_QGOH_PQ_6:A17","CH_QGOH_PQ_7:A9"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[]},{"id":"CL_QGOH_07:RLE-06","text":"Love of the common good is increased by the participation of others; the increase of common joy nourishes Love in turn, whence rational sociability forms a circuit of amplification.","refs":["CH_QGOH_PQ_6:D3","CH_QGOH_PQ_6:A3"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[]},{"id":"CL_QGOH_07:RLE-07","text":"Blessedness is a terminal attractor that turns the terminus into a mechanism: the more it is present, the more the Mind understands, the less it undergoes, and the more powerfully it preserves that blessedness.","refs":["CH_QGOH_PQ_7:A8","CH_QGOH_PQ_7:D3"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[]}],"original_name":"Recursive Loops, Escalation Paths, and Conceptual Attractors"},{"number":"11","name":"Formal Generative Schema of the Whole Book","items":[{"id":"CL_QGOH_07:FGS-B01","text":"Variation of the body within the necessary nexus of causes → a different degree of aptitude and different initial conditions of passion and knowledge.","refs":["CH_QGOH_PQ_7:AS1","CH_QGOH_PQDP:A7"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[]},{"id":"CL_QGOH_07:FGS-B02","text":"Inadequate idea + attribution of an external cause → passive affect + imaginative fixation + dependence on external forces.","refs":["CH_QGOH_PQDP:FGS2","CH_QGOH_PQ_6:A4"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[]},{"id":"CL_QGOH_07:FGS-B03","text":"Clear conception of affect + understanding of necessity → separation from the confusedly imagined cause → diminution of passivity.","refs":["CH_QGOH_PQDP:D1","CH_QGOH_PQ_6:A5"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[]},{"id":"CL_QGOH_07:FGS-B04","text":"Order of ideas → order of affections of the Body → concatenation of images → memory of precepts → anticipated rational response.","refs":["CH_QGOH_PQDP:A13","CH_QGOH_PQDP:D6"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[]},{"id":"CL_QGOH_07:FGS-B05","text":"Adequate Reason → desire for knowledge of the third kind → intuition of singular essence from divine dependence.","refs":["CH_QGOH_PQ_6:A10","CH_QGOH_PQ_6:D8"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[]},{"id":"CL_QGOH_07:FGS-B06","text":"Temporal actuality of the Body / essence of the Body in God → perishing imagination and memory / eternal part of the Mind.","refs":["CH_QGOH_PQ_6:D7","CH_QGOH_PQ_6:NX6"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[]},{"id":"CL_QGOH_07:FGS-B07","text":"Intuitive knowledge → supreme joy with the idea of God → eternal intellectual Love of God.","refs":["CH_QGOH_PQ_6:D10","CH_QGOH_PQ_6:A13"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[]},{"id":"CL_QGOH_07:FGS-B08","text":"Intellectual Love as explication of divine love → blessedness as virtue → increase of intellect → diminution of passions → stable freedom under necessity.","refs":["CH_QGOH_PQ_6:D11","CH_QGOH_PQ_7:A7","CH_QGOH_PQ_7:A8"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[]}],"original_name":"Formal Generative Schema of the Whole Book"},{"number":"12","name":"Whole-Book Argument Flow Map","items":[{"id":"CL_QGOH_07:AFM-B01","text":"Dominion of the will negated → affect reconstituted as confused idea → understood necessity → ordering of images and Body → knowledge of the third kind → intellectual Love → active blessedness → freedom of the wise person.","refs":["CH_QGOH_PQDP:AF1","CH_QGOH_PQ_7:AF1"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[]},{"id":"CL_QGOH_07:AFM-B02","text":"God as affectively imagined external cause → impossibility of hatred and reciprocal love → adequate idea of God → intellectual Love → love of the Mind as explication of the love by which God loves himself.","refs":["CH_QGOH_PQ_6:AS3","CH_QGOH_PQ_6:D11"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[]},{"id":"CL_QGOH_07:AFM-B03","text":"Enduring Body → imagination, memory, and affective preparation → Body apt for more → understanding more → greater eternal proportion of the Mind → diminution of fear of death.","refs":["CH_QGOH_PQ_6:AS5","CH_QGOH_PQ_6:A17","CH_QGOH_PQ_6:D13"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[]}],"original_name":"Whole-Book Argument Flow Map"},{"number":"13","name":"Macro Map-Effects","items":[{"id":"CL_QGOH_07:MME-01","text":"Freedom is reordered from dominion of the will and causal exemption to the gradual transformation of the proportion between action and passion.","refs":["CH_QGOH_PQDP:ME2","CH_QGOH_PQ_6:SD1"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[]},{"id":"CL_QGOH_07:MME-02","text":"Affect is reclassified from an external psychological impulse into an ideational, bodily, and causally orderable structure.","refs":["CH_QGOH_PQDP:ME1","CH_QGOH_PQ_6:ME1"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[]},{"id":"CL_QGOH_07:MME-03","text":"The Body is transferred from a mere correlate of mortality into an instrument of rational disposition and of expanding the Mind's power.","refs":["CH_QGOH_PQ_6:ME5","CH_QGOH_PQ_7:ME5"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[]},{"id":"CL_QGOH_07:MME-04","text":"God is reclassified from the object of reciprocal passion into immanent cause and the reflexive structure of intellectual Love.","refs":["CH_QGOH_PQ_6:ME3","CH_QGOH_PQ_6:D11"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[]},{"id":"CL_QGOH_07:MME-05","text":"Eternity is transferred from the temporal continuation of the person to a mode of conceiving essence under divine necessity.","refs":["CH_QGOH_PQ_6:ME2","CH_QGOH_PQ_6:SNX2"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[]},{"id":"CL_QGOH_07:MME-06","text":"Virtue is converted from a discipline sustained by reward and punishment into an immanent, non-rivalrous, and causally beatifying good.","refs":["CH_QGOH_PQ_7:ME2","CH_QGOH_PQ_7:ME3"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[]},{"id":"CL_QGOH_07:MME-07","text":"Knowledge passes from inspection of things to a transformative operation that simultaneously orders affects, the Body, temporality, and the state of the subject.","refs":["CH_QGOH_PQ_7:SD2","CH_QGOH_PQDP:ALT2"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[]}],"original_name":"Macro Map-Effects"},{"number":"14","name":"Altitude Integration","items":[{"id":"CL_QGOH_07:ALT-B01","text":"At the surface level, the concern is with governing affects, understanding the eternity of the Mind, diminishing fear of death, and constituting blessedness.","refs":["CH_QGOH_PQDP:ALT1","CH_QGOH_PQ_6:ALT1","CH_QGOH_PQ_7:ALT1"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[]},{"id":"CL_QGOH_07:ALT-B02","text":"At the structural level, the orders of ideas, affections of the Body, imagination, kinds of knowledge, and action effect one causal distribution.","refs":["CH_QGOH_PQDP:ALT2","CH_QGOH_PQ_7:ALT2"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[]},{"id":"CL_QGOH_07:ALT-B03","text":"At the metastrategic level, the struggle is transferred from repression of affects to the production of activity, joy, and adequate knowledge.","refs":["CH_QGOH_PQDP:ALT3","CH_QGOH_PQ_6:ALT3"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[]},{"id":"CL_QGOH_07:ALT-B04","text":"At the ontological and temporal level, reality is a necessary and immanent nexus, under which imaginative duration is distinguished from intelligible eternity without substantial opposition of Mind and Body.","refs":["CH_QGOH_PQDP:ALT4","CH_QGOH_PQ_6:ALT4"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[]},{"id":"CL_QGOH_07:ALT-B05","text":"At the methodological and performative level, demonstration enacts the very transformation it teaches: it systematically negates, reclassifies, and composes the terms of passion into the active circuit of blessedness.","refs":["CH_QGOH_PQ_6:AS2","CH_QGOH_PQ_7:ALT3"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[]}],"original_name":"Altitude Integration"},{"number":"15","name":"Internal Tensions, Mutation Points, and Fault Lines","items":[{"id":"CL_QGOH_07:FL-01","text":"The assertion that every affection involves a clear conception does not fully determine how much clarity suffices for the affect itself to cease being a passion.","refs":["CH_QGOH_PQDP:MRT3","CH_QGOH_PQDP:A4"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[]},{"id":"CL_QGOH_07:FL-02","text":"A plurality of causes can simultaneously strengthen an affect and lessen its harm; the system requires a distinction among concurrence, distribution, and frequency, but does not universally formalize it.","refs":["CH_QGOH_PQDP:MRT4","CH_QGOH_PQDP:D5"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[]},{"id":"CL_QGOH_07:FL-03","text":"Divine impassibility and the identity of the Mind's Love with divine love produce a semantic tension, which is managed only by changing the notion of love.","refs":["CH_QGOH_PQDP:A16","CH_QGOH_PQ_6:D11"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[]},{"id":"CL_QGOH_07:FL-04","text":"Eternal intellectual Love is said to be without beginning, but the Mind's access to knowledge of the third kind is narrated as a gradually acquired progression.","refs":["CH_QGOH_PQ_6:MRT5","CH_QGOH_PQ_6:D8"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[]},{"id":"CL_QGOH_07:FL-05","text":"The nexus between the aptitude of the Body and the greater eternal part of the Mind is affirmed more strongly than the mechanism of proportion is demonstrated.","refs":["CH_QGOH_PQ_6:MRT6","CH_QGOH_PQ_7:MRT2"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[]},{"id":"CL_QGOH_07:FL-06","text":"Virtue is independent of knowledge of eternity, although the highest figure of the wise person is defined through eternal intellect and intellectual Love.","refs":["CH_QGOH_PQ_7:MRT3","CH_QGOH_PQ_7:A6"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[]},{"id":"CL_QGOH_07:MP-01","text":"The affect changes from the confused idea of a singular passion into an index of the causal proportion between external determination and activity of the Mind.","refs":["CH_QGOH_PQDP:A3","CH_QGOH_PQ_6:ME1"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[]},{"id":"CL_QGOH_07:MP-02","text":"Love of God changes its meaning where reciprocal passion is excluded and the same name is assigned to the action of intellect and divine self-knowledge.","refs":["CH_QGOH_PQ_6:NX2","CH_QGOH_PQ_6:A14"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[]},{"id":"CL_QGOH_07:MP-03","text":"The Body changes function where it becomes, from the seat of duration and imagination, a positive disposition toward more actions and broader knowledge.","refs":["CH_QGOH_PQ_6:A7","CH_QGOH_PQ_6:A17"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[]},{"id":"CL_QGOH_07:MP-04","text":"Blessedness changes status where it is inverted from the end of discipline into virtue itself and the cause of restraining desires.","refs":["CH_QGOH_PQ_7:NX3","CH_QGOH_PQ_7:D3"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[]},{"id":"CL_QGOH_07:MP-05","text":"Freedom changes where the governance of affects within duration is absorbed into eternal participation in intellectual action and stability under necessity.","refs":["CH_QGOH_PQDP:ME2","CH_QGOH_PQ_7:ME4"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[]}],"original_name":"Internal Tensions, Mutation Points, and Fault Lines"},{"number":"16","name":"Hidden System Image","items":[{"id":"CL_QGOH_07:HSI-01","text":"Reality is implicitly an immanent network of causes and ideas, in which no extrinsic will rules, but parts act or undergo according to the degree to which they internally explain causes.","refs":["CH_QGOH_PQDP:A2","CH_QGOH_PQ_7:SNX2"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[]},{"id":"CL_QGOH_07:HSI-02","text":"The subject is a proportionally mutable node of this network, not its center; it becomes free insofar as the nexuses that determine it are more clearly expressed through it.","refs":["CH_QGOH_PQ_6:A4","CH_QGOH_PQ_7:A1"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[]},{"id":"CL_QGOH_07:HSI-03","text":"Time is ordered in two ways: duration sustains images, memory, and passion, whereas eternity presents essence without beginning, succession, or recollection.","refs":["CH_QGOH_PQ_6:D7","CH_QGOH_PQ_6:SNX2"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[]},{"id":"CL_QGOH_07:HSI-04","text":"The struggle is not the expulsion of affects, but the conversion of causal channels, through which external fixation is transferred into the order of ideas, active joy, and intellectual Love.","refs":["CH_QGOH_PQ_6:A5","CH_QGOH_PQ_7:SD2"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[]},{"id":"CL_QGOH_07:HSI-05","text":"Possible navigation is spiral-shaped: a more apt Body, a clearer Mind, a less passive affect, and more powerful blessedness mutually amplify one another, until death loses the center of ethical order.","refs":["CH_QGOH_PQ_7:A9","CH_QGOH_PQ_7:A8","CH_QGOH_PQ_6:A16"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[]}],"original_name":"Hidden System Image"},{"number":"17","name":"Minimal Whole-Book Reconstruction Test","items":[{"id":"CL_QGOH_07:MRT-B01","text":"The present axioms suffice to restore the core: understanding transforms passions into actions, understood necessity constitutes freedom, and intellectual Love produces immanent blessedness.","refs":["CH_QGOH_PQDP:MRT1","CH_QGOH_PQ_7:MRT1"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[]},{"id":"CL_QGOH_07:MRT-B02","text":"The firmest structure is the circuit among adequate idea, understanding of necessity, diminished passivity, joy, and increased power of understanding.","refs":["CH_QGOH_PQDP:MRT2","CH_QGOH_PQ_7:MRT4"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[]},{"id":"CL_QGOH_07:MRT-B03","text":"What remain less determined are the quantity of knowledge required to transform a passion, the mechanism by which the aptitude of the Body effects a greater eternal part of the Mind, and the relation of the experience of eternity to the absence of memory.","refs":["CH_QGOH_PQ_6:MRT3","CH_QGOH_PQ_6:MRT4","CH_QGOH_PQ_7:MRT2"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[]},{"id":"CL_QGOH_07:MRT-B04","text":"The coherence of this level consists not in linear order alone, but in the altered return of the same terms: affect, Body, love, freedom, and blessedness receive a new function with each return.","refs":["CH_QGOH_PQ_6:MRT7","CH_QGOH_PQ_7:MRT6"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[]},{"id":"CL_QGOH_07:MRT-B05","text":"The reconstruction preserves the true movement if the temporal remedies for affects are not separated from the doctrine of eternity, but are read as a disposition toward intuitive knowledge and blessedness.","refs":["CH_QGOH_PQ_6:MRT7","CH_QGOH_PQ_7:AF1"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[]},{"id":"CL_QGOH_07:MRT-B06","text":"What still resists axiomatic reduction is the transition from the demonstration of eternity to its experiential affirmation, and the terminal image of the wise person, whose affective force accomplishes more than the demonstrative chain alone explains.","refs":["CH_QGOH_PQ_6:MRT4","CH_QGOH_PQ_7:AS6"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[]}],"original_name":"Minimal Whole-Book Reconstruction Test"}],"revision":"CL_QGOH_07@R001","source_variant_id":"canonical:CL_QGOH_07:v1","source_language_code":"la","original_title":"Chapters 19–21"}],"identity":{"layer":"CL","kicker":"Cluster · Ethica","title":"Chapters 19–21","context":"Part V - On the Power of the Intellect, or on Human Freedom · passage 1 · Part V - On the Power of the Intellect, or on Human Freedom · passage 2 · Part V - On the Power of the Intellect, or on Human Freedom · passage 3","original_title":"Chapters 19–21","original_context":"Pars quinta - De potentia intellectus seu de libertate humana · passage 1 · Pars quinta - De potentia intellectus seu de libertate humana · passage 2 · Pars quinta - De potentia intellectus seu de libertate humana · passage 3"},"original_title":"Chapters 19–21","title_language_code":"en"},{"id":"MC_QGOH_L1_01","layer":"MC","title":"Qgoh L1 01","order":1,"parents":["CL_QGOH_01","CL_QGOH_02","CL_QGOH_03","CL_QGOH_04"],"revision":"MC_QGOH_L1_01@R001","sha256":"ea42ac7aaa0615c7ea30a684a49863481dd2d110555392fb231d93f828ddcf15","sections":[{"number":"1","name":"Book-Scale Argumental Spine","items":[{"id":"MC_QGOH_L1_01:AS-01","text":"Systema quaerit quomodo una Natura necessario immanens corpora, mentes, cognitiones, conatus et relationes affectivas generet, dum intra eandem necessitatem transitus a determinatione externa ad actionem propriam possibilis manet.","refs":["CL_QGOH_01:BAS-01","CL_QGOH_02:BAS-01","CL_QGOH_03:AS-01","CL_QGOH_04:AS-01"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[]},{"id":"MC_QGOH_L1_01:AS-02","text":"Thesis globalis affirmat omne ens esse expressionem unius Naturae, cuius causalitas per individua corporea, ideas, imaginationes et affectus distribuitur atque secundum gradum adaequationis in passionem vel actionem mutatur.","refs":["CL_QGOH_01:BAS-02","CL_QGOH_02:BAS-02","CL_QGOH_03:AS-02","CL_QGOH_04:AS-02"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[]},{"id":"MC_QGOH_L1_01:AS-03","text":"Argumentatio geometrica fit genealogia recursiva: exclusio exterioritatis causalitatem necessariam constituit, causalitas constitutionem corporis et Mentis explicat, atque haec constitutio errores, valores et circuitus sociales tamquam effectus naturales restituit.","refs":["CL_QGOH_01:BAS-03","CL_QGOH_02:BAS-03","CL_QGOH_03:AS-03","CL_QGOH_04:AS-03"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[]},{"id":"MC_QGOH_L1_01:AS-04","text":"Motus procedit a substantia sui causa ad productionem modalem, a modo finito ad individuum corporeum, ab affectione ad ideam et affectum, atque a circuitibus passionum ad actionem causaliter adaequatam.","refs":["CL_QGOH_01:BAS-04","CL_QGOH_03:AS-04","CL_QGOH_04:AS-04"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[]},{"id":"MC_QGOH_L1_01:AS-05","text":"Motus verus est cumulativus et spiralis, quia psychologia corporis explicat errores metaphysicos iam exclusos, affectuum doctrina cognitionis structuram in socialitatem transfert, et quilibet reditus subiectum iam mutatum recipit.","refs":["CL_QGOH_01:BAS-06","CL_QGOH_02:BAS-05","CL_QGOH_03:AS-05","CL_QGOH_04:AS-05"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[]},{"id":"MC_QGOH_L1_01:AS-06","text":"Necessitas mutatur ab clausura ontologica in regulam individuationis, cognitionis et agentiae; quod initio omnem exterioritatem tollit, terminaliter libertatem ut gradum causalitatis propriae definit.","refs":["CL_QGOH_01:GA-06","CL_QGOH_03:AS-06","CL_QGOH_04:AS-07"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[]},{"id":"MC_QGOH_L1_01:AS-07","text":"Error, teleologia, voluntas libera et valor transcendens non extra Naturam cadunt, sed positive ex cognitione partiali, memoria, utilitate, attributione causae et circulatione affectuum producuntur.","refs":["CL_QGOH_02:BAS-07","CL_QGOH_02:BSD-03","CL_QGOH_04:DBN-02"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[]},{"id":"MC_QGOH_L1_01:AS-08","text":"Clausura est immanens sed provisionaliter aperta: fuga ex necessitate excluditur, actio et Generositas intra eam constituuntur, sed mechanismus plenus quo adaequatio passionum ordinem stabiliter transformat nondum absolvitur.","refs":["CL_QGOH_01:BAS-08","CL_QGOH_02:BAS-08","CL_QGOH_03:AS-07","CL_QGOH_04:AS-08"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[]}],"original_name":"Book-Scale Argumental Spine"},{"number":"2","name":"Global Load-Bearing Axioms","items":[{"id":"MC_QGOH_L1_01:GA-01","text":"[Persistent] Una Natura necessario existens est causa immanens omnium rerum; nullum ens, tribunal aut principium causale extra eam datur.","refs":["CL_QGOH_01:GA-08","CL_QGOH_02:GA-13","CL_QGOH_03:GA-01"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[]},{"id":"MC_QGOH_L1_01:GA-02","text":"[Persistent] Omne finitum est modus et nodus causaliter dependens; Mens humana et Corpus non substantiae separatae, sed eiusdem individui expressiones attributivae sunt.","refs":["CL_QGOH_01:GA-01","CL_QGOH_02:GA-04","CL_QGOH_03:BSD-02"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[]},{"id":"MC_QGOH_L1_01:GA-03","text":"[Mutating] Necessitas causalis a productione modorum ad ordinem cognitionis, actionis et propagationis socialis extenditur, sine ruptura inter ontologiam et psychologiam.","refs":["CL_QGOH_01:GA-05","CL_QGOH_02:GA-02","CL_QGOH_04:GA-09"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[]},{"id":"MC_QGOH_L1_01:GA-04","text":"[Mutating] Individuum corporeum est relatio dynamica perseverans, cuius affectiones simul perceptionem Mentis et variationes potentiae agendi constituunt.","refs":["CL_QGOH_02:GA-05","CL_QGOH_02:GA-06","CL_QGOH_03:GA-09"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[]},{"id":"MC_QGOH_L1_01:GA-05","text":"[Mutating] Imaginatio est mechanismus naturalis quo affectiones, vestigia, absentiae, similitudines et consuetudines praesentias affectivas atque nexus aestimativos producunt.","refs":["CL_QGOH_02:GA-07","CL_QGOH_04:GA-03","CL_QGOH_04:GA-04"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[]},{"id":"MC_QGOH_L1_01:GA-06","text":"[Mutating] Adaequatio et inadaequatio non tantum qualitates cognitionis sunt: prior causalitatem activam, altera causalitatem partialem, passionem et capturam per causas externas constituit.","refs":["CL_QGOH_03:GA-03","CL_QGOH_03:GA-04","CL_QGOH_04:GA-12"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[]},{"id":"MC_QGOH_L1_01:GA-07","text":"[Emergent] Conatus est essentia actualis individui, cuius operationes ad conservationem et augmentum potentiae agendi convergunt.","refs":["CL_QGOH_03:GA-07","CL_QGOH_04:GA-01","CL_QGOH_04:GA-14"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[]},{"id":"MC_QGOH_L1_01:GA-08","text":"[Emergent] Bonum, malum et perfectio non proprietates extrinsece normatae, sed indices relationis inter constitutionem rei, appetitum et variationem potentiae sunt.","refs":["CL_QGOH_02:GA-09","CL_QGOH_03:GNX-07","CL_QGOH_04:GA-10"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[]},{"id":"MC_QGOH_L1_01:GA-09","text":"[Emergent] Subiectum est processus corporeus, historicus et socialiter distributus, qui se per affectiones, ideas et responsiones imaginatas aliorum constituit.","refs":["CL_QGOH_02:BSD-02","CL_QGOH_03:BSD-02","CL_QGOH_04:BSD-01"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[]},{"id":"MC_QGOH_L1_01:GA-10","text":"[Terminal] Libertas est augmentum causalitatis adaequatae intra necessitatem, non indifferentia, exemptio causalis aut initium absolutum actionis.","refs":["CL_QGOH_01:GA-07","CL_QGOH_03:GA-10","CL_QGOH_04:BSA-04"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[]},{"id":"MC_QGOH_L1_01:GA-11","text":"[Persistent] Cogitatio et Extensio eundem ordinem exprimunt sine causalitate transversa; congruentia Mentis et Corporis identitatem processus, non commercium substantiarum, significat.","refs":["CL_QGOH_02:GA-03","CL_QGOH_02:XD-05","CL_QGOH_03:FL-02"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[]},{"id":"MC_QGOH_L1_01:GA-12","text":"[Emergent] Tempus systematis stratificatur in aeternam necessitatem Naturae, durationem indefinitam conatus et praesentificationes imaginativas praeteriti vel futuri.","refs":["CL_QGOH_03:GA-12","CL_QGOH_03:BSD-04","CL_QGOH_04:BSD-05"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[]},{"id":"MC_QGOH_L1_01:GA-13","text":"[Attractor] Intelligibilitas causalis est attractor communis: ideas locales in contextum ampliorem inserit, passionem in actionem reordinat et fictas exterioritates dissolvit.","refs":["CL_QGOH_01:GA-14","CL_QGOH_02:GA-11","CL_QGOH_03:RLE-07"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[]},{"id":"MC_QGOH_L1_01:GA-14","text":"[Terminal] Ethica est reconfiguratio immanens cognitionis, corporis et relationum, cuius forma activa socialis in Animositate et Generositate culminat, non in imperio voluntatis.","refs":["CL_QGOH_04:GA-13","CL_QGOH_04:BSD-07","CL_QGOH_04:HSI-04"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[]}],"original_name":"Global Load-Bearing Axioms"},{"number":"3","name":"Recurrence, Mutation, and Displacement Matrix","items":[{"id":"MC_QGOH_L1_01:RMD-01","text":"Substantia identitatem ontologicam retinet, sed postquam clausuram immanentem constituit, e proscenio recedit et ut infrastructura tacita cognitionis atque affectuum operatur.","refs":["CL_QGOH_01:RMD-01","CL_QGOH_03:RMD-08"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[]},{"id":"MC_QGOH_L1_01:RMD-02","text":"Immanentia ab relatione Dei et modorum ad constitutionem Mentis corporeae, demonstrabilitatem affectuum et circulationem socialem causalitatis amplificatur.","refs":["CL_QGOH_01:GA-08","CL_QGOH_02:GA-12","CL_QGOH_03:XD-04","CL_QGOH_02:RLE-05"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[]},{"id":"MC_QGOH_L1_01:RMD-03","text":"Necessitas functionem mutat: clausura modalitatis fit principium geneticum imaginationis, discrimen actionis et passionis atque condicio libertatis internae.","refs":["CL_QGOH_02:RMD-01","CL_QGOH_03:AS-06","CL_QGOH_04:AS-07"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[]},{"id":"MC_QGOH_L1_01:RMD-04","text":"Corpus a modo determinato in individuum relationale, deinde in substratum variabile potentiae et tandem in condicionem mutabilitatis valorum convertitur.","refs":["CL_QGOH_02:GA-05","CL_QGOH_03:RMD-06","CL_QGOH_04:AS-06"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[]},{"id":"MC_QGOH_L1_01:RMD-05","text":"Idea affectionis redit ut affectus: eadem mediatio quae externum per constitutionem Corporis exhibet nunc augmentum vel diminutionem potentiae exprimit.","refs":["CL_QGOH_03:RMD-01","CL_QGOH_03:MP-01"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[]},{"id":"MC_QGOH_L1_01:RMD-06","text":"Imaginatio mutatur a nomine generali erroris in mechanismum neutralem praesentificationis, deinde in operatorem conativum, interpersonalem et reflexivum.","refs":["CL_QGOH_02:BAS-06","CL_QGOH_03:RMD-07","CL_QGOH_04:RMD-03"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[]},{"id":"MC_QGOH_L1_01:RMD-07","text":"Libertas inversionem stabilem patitur: indifferentia evanescit, necessitas propriae naturae manet, et gradus libertatis ex proportione causalitatis adaequatae mensuratur.","refs":["CL_QGOH_01:RMD-05","CL_QGOH_03:MP-05"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[]},{"id":"MC_QGOH_L1_01:RMD-08","text":"Perfectio et bonum ab exemplaribus externis ad potentiam rei migrant; terminaliter etiam identitas valoris obiecti dissolvitur in eventum relationis mutabilis.","refs":["CL_QGOH_02:RMD-06","CL_QGOH_03:MME-06","CL_QGOH_04:RMD-09"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[]},{"id":"MC_QGOH_L1_01:RMD-09","text":"Conatus ab essentia actuali singularis ad principium selectionis imaginum, productionis Cupiditatis et conservationis socialiter mediatae amplificatur.","refs":["CL_QGOH_03:GA-07","CL_QGOH_04:RMD-01","CL_QGOH_04:GA-14"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[]},{"id":"MC_QGOH_L1_01:RMD-10","text":"Finis, postquam causalitas Naturae esse desinit, in indicem imaginationis transfertur et tandem in generaliorem operationem attributionis causae absorbetur.","refs":["CL_QGOH_02:RLE-08","CL_QGOH_04:GA-09"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[]}],"original_name":"Recurrence, Mutation, and Displacement Matrix"},{"number":"4","name":"Lower-Layer vs Current-Level Distinction","items":[{"id":"MC_QGOH_L1_01:CGD-01","text":"Demonstrationes locales de unicitate substantiae hoc gradu in clausuram generaliorem absorbentur qua omnis exterioritas ontologica, epistemica et practica excluditur.","refs":["CL_QGOH_01:CGD-01","CL_QGOH_01:BSA-01"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[]},{"id":"MC_QGOH_L1_01:CGD-02","text":"Modi infiniti, modi finiti et Natura Naturata architecturam mediationis praebent, sed praesentis gradus nexus est continuatio illius mediationis in corporibus, ideis et affectibus.","refs":["CL_QGOH_01:CGD-03","CL_QGOH_01:CGD-04"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[]},{"id":"MC_QGOH_L1_01:CGD-03","text":"Vestigia corporalium partium manent hypothesis localis imaginationis; sursum persistit tantum functio qua historia affectionum absentias et consuetudines efficaciter praesentat.","refs":["CL_QGOH_02:CGD-03","CL_QGOH_02:RLE-02"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[]},{"id":"MC_QGOH_L1_01:CGD-04","text":"Taxonomiae affectuum singularium ad gradum inferiorem pertinent; hoc gradu earum varietas in grammaticam conatus, potentiae, imaginationis et attributionis causae comprimitur.","refs":["CL_QGOH_03:CGD-04","CL_QGOH_04:CGD-01","CL_QGOH_04:GA-11"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[]},{"id":"MC_QGOH_L1_01:CGD-05","text":"Critica localis finalismi amplificatur in diagnosin generalem qua cognitiones partiales, valores et auctoritates transcendentes ex corporeis atque socialibus mechanismis producuntur.","refs":["CL_QGOH_01:CGD-07","CL_QGOH_02:XD-01","CL_QGOH_02:XD-09"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[]},{"id":"MC_QGOH_L1_01:CGD-06","text":"Parallelismus attributorum coniungit ontologiam, psychologiam et doctrinam affectuum, sed causalitatem transversam non introducit neque mechanismum congruentiae ulterius explicat.","refs":["CL_QGOH_02:BAS-04","CL_QGOH_02:FL-02","CL_QGOH_03:FL-02"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[]},{"id":"MC_QGOH_L1_01:CGD-07","text":"Distinctio causae adaequatae et partialis transitum maioris ordinis efficit, quia veritatem et errorem in actionem, passionem atque formas socialis potentiae convertit.","refs":["CL_QGOH_03:CGD-05","CL_QGOH_03:XD-01"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[]},{"id":"MC_QGOH_L1_01:CGD-08","text":"Beatitudo manet attractor non completus: directionem a necessitate ad libertatem ordinat, sed plena conversio cognitionis in stabilitatem affectivam extra determinationem praesentem manet.","refs":["CL_QGOH_02:CGD-08","CL_QGOH_02:FL-06","CL_QGOH_03:MRT-B03"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[]}],"original_name":"Lower-Layer vs Current-Level Distinction"},{"number":"5","name":"Global Apophatic Structure","items":[{"id":"MC_QGOH_L1_01:GNX-01","text":"Nullus locus extra Naturam admittitur ex quo causalitas, cognitio, valor aut actio originem vel normam accipiat.","refs":["CL_QGOH_01:BSA-01","CL_QGOH_02:BSA-01","CL_QGOH_03:BSA-01","CL_QGOH_04:BSA-01"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"Negative","passages":[]},{"id":"MC_QGOH_L1_01:GNX-02","text":"Nec mundus alternativus nec actus a serie causarum immunis possibilitatem realem constituunt.","refs":["CL_QGOH_01:BSA-02","CL_QGOH_03:BSA-02"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"Negative","passages":[]},{"id":"MC_QGOH_L1_01:GNX-03","text":"Finis futurus, arbitrium divinum et utilitas humana causalitatem Naturae regere non possunt.","refs":["CL_QGOH_01:GNX-06","CL_QGOH_02:GNX-01"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"Negative","passages":[]},{"id":"MC_QGOH_L1_01:GNX-04","text":"Mens et Corpus neque substantiae separatae neque agentes causaliter transversi sunt.","refs":["CL_QGOH_02:GNX-03","CL_QGOH_03:GNX-04"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"Negative","passages":[]},{"id":"MC_QGOH_L1_01:GNX-05","text":"Subiectum finitum nec sibi immediate transparens nec affective sui iuris est.","refs":["CL_QGOH_02:DBN-03","CL_QGOH_04:GNX-03"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"Negative","passages":[]},{"id":"MC_QGOH_L1_01:GNX-06","text":"Bonum, malum, ordo et perfectio proprietates intrinsecas rerum aut tribunal morale independente desiderio non constituunt.","refs":["CL_QGOH_02:GNX-08","CL_QGOH_04:GNX-04"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"Negative","passages":[]},{"id":"MC_QGOH_L1_01:GNX-07","text":"Identitas individui, valoris et affectus neque materia permanenti neque obiecto invariabili neque catalogo terminali stabilitur.","refs":["CL_QGOH_02:GNX-06","CL_QGOH_04:BSA-03"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"Negative","passages":[]}],"original_name":"Global Apophatic Structure"},{"number":"6","name":"Deep Book Apophatics","items":[{"id":"MC_QGOH_L1_01:DBN-01","text":"Separatio essentiae, existentiae, potentiae et actualitatis divinae falsa esse debet, quia hiatum ontologicum introduceret unde contingentia et exterioritas redirent.","refs":["CL_QGOH_01:DBN-01","CL_QGOH_01:DBN-02"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"Negative","passages":[]},{"id":"MC_QGOH_L1_01:DBN-02","text":"Cognitio causaliter iners falsa esse debet, quia differentia adaequationis eadem differentia est quae actionem a passione separat.","refs":["CL_QGOH_03:DBN-05","CL_QGOH_03:GA-03"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"Negative","passages":[]},{"id":"MC_QGOH_L1_01:DBN-03","text":"Universalitas legum affectuum uniformitatem responsionum implicare non potest; aliter diversitas corporum et singularitas essentiarum evanescerent.","refs":["CL_QGOH_04:DBN-05","CL_QGOH_04:GA-06"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"Negative","passages":[]},{"id":"MC_QGOH_L1_01:DBN-04","text":"Norma veritatis vel bonitatis ab exteriori imposita falsa esse debet, quia tam ideae quam appetitus ex ordine causali intrinseco aestimantur.","refs":["CL_QGOH_02:DBN-07","CL_QGOH_03:DBN-04"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"Negative","passages":[]},{"id":"MC_QGOH_L1_01:DBN-05","text":"Error tamquam mera irruptio irrationalis extra Naturam falsus esse debet; falsitas privativa est, sed condiciones eius corporales et sociales positive generantur.","refs":["CL_QGOH_02:BAS-07","CL_QGOH_03:GA-04","CL_QGOH_02:BSD-03"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"Negative","passages":[]},{"id":"MC_QGOH_L1_01:DBN-06","text":"Libertas quae necessitatem minuat aut causam incausatam postulet falsa esse debet; aliter actio ad exemptionem ontologicam, non ad adaequationem, reduceretur.","refs":["CL_QGOH_03:BSA-03","CL_QGOH_04:BSA-04"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"Negative","passages":[]}],"original_name":"Deep Book Apophatics"},{"number":"7","name":"Super-Apophatics of the Whole","items":[{"id":"MC_QGOH_L1_01:BSA-01","text":"Absentia suprema est ipsum extra, cuius exclusio substantiam, cognitionem, affectum et ethicam in eadem immanentia claudit.","refs":["CL_QGOH_01:BSA-01","CL_QGOH_03:BSA-01"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"Negative","passages":[]},{"id":"MC_QGOH_L1_01:BSA-02","text":"Origo absoluta cogitationis, appetitus aut actionis ontologice indisponibilis est; omnis initium apparens nexus causalis iam constituti est.","refs":["CL_QGOH_03:BSA-02","CL_QGOH_04:BSA-01"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"Negative","passages":[]},{"id":"MC_QGOH_L1_01:BSA-03","text":"Tribunal transcendens quod ordinem actualem, valores et affectus ante eorum generationem iudicet impossibile est.","refs":["CL_QGOH_01:BSA-03","CL_QGOH_04:BSA-02"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"Negative","passages":[]},{"id":"MC_QGOH_L1_01:BSA-04","text":"Praesentia rei externae vel subiecti sibi ipsi non mediata corpore, affectione et historia causali indisponibilis est.","refs":["CL_QGOH_02:BSA-02","CL_QGOH_02:BSA-03","CL_QGOH_04:DBN-03"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"Negative","passages":[]},{"id":"MC_QGOH_L1_01:BSA-05","text":"Possibilitas nondum exhibita est conversio integra cognitionis adaequatae in stabilitatem affectivam et socialem; haec absentia directionem ethicam ordinat sine clausura demonstrata.","refs":["CL_QGOH_02:BSA-06","CL_QGOH_03:MRT-B03","CL_QGOH_04:MRT-B04"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"Negative","passages":[]}],"original_name":"Super-Apophatics of the Whole"},{"number":"8","name":"Cross-Unit Derivative Propositions","items":[{"id":"MC_QGOH_L1_01:XD-01","text":"Quia immanentia ontologica Mentem ex Corpore constituit, error non exceptio a Natura sed circuitus endogenus est quo affectio localis in structuram universalem proiectatur.","refs":["MC_QGOH_L1_01:GA-01","MC_QGOH_L1_01:GA-04","MC_QGOH_L1_01:GA-05","MC_QGOH_L1_01:GA-06","CL_QGOH_01:GA-08","CL_QGOH_02:GA-12","CL_QGOH_02:BSD-03"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"From","passages":[]},{"id":"MC_QGOH_L1_01:XD-02","text":"Necessitas universalis cum causalitate partiali et imitatione affectuum coniuncta dominationem non ut violationem naturae, sed ut reproductionem naturalem dependentiae per circuitus sociales explicat.","refs":["MC_QGOH_L1_01:GA-03","MC_QGOH_L1_01:GA-06","MC_QGOH_L1_01:GA-09","CL_QGOH_01:GA-05","CL_QGOH_03:GA-04","CL_QGOH_04:GA-08"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"From","passages":[]},{"id":"MC_QGOH_L1_01:XD-03","text":"Critica finalismi et genesis valoris ex appetitu efficiunt ut normativitas sit cartographia compositionum quae potentiam augent vel minuunt, non lex supra desiderium posita.","refs":["MC_QGOH_L1_01:GA-07","MC_QGOH_L1_01:GA-08","MC_QGOH_L1_01:GA-14","CL_QGOH_01:DBN-05","CL_QGOH_03:GNX-07","CL_QGOH_04:GA-10"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"From","passages":[]},{"id":"MC_QGOH_L1_01:XD-04","text":"Individuum relationale, conatus indefinitus et reflexio per alios constituunt subiectum metastabile, cuius identitas perseverat tantum per continuam reconfigurationem corporalem atque socialem.","refs":["MC_QGOH_L1_01:GA-04","MC_QGOH_L1_01:GA-07","MC_QGOH_L1_01:GA-09","MC_QGOH_L1_01:GA-12","CL_QGOH_02:GA-05","CL_QGOH_03:GA-07","CL_QGOH_04:BSD-01"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"From","passages":[]},{"id":"MC_QGOH_L1_01:XD-05","text":"Cum contingentia ad ignorantiam transferatur et futurum imaginatum affectum praesenter ordinet, causalitas finalis fit retroiectio affectiva intra ordinem causarum efficientium.","refs":["MC_QGOH_L1_01:GA-03","MC_QGOH_L1_01:GA-05","MC_QGOH_L1_01:GA-12","CL_QGOH_01:RMD-08","CL_QGOH_02:BSD-05","CL_QGOH_04:BSD-05"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"From","passages":[]},{"id":"MC_QGOH_L1_01:XD-06","text":"Libertas adaequata potentiam relationalem habet: proprietates communes et Generositas actiones componere possunt, quamquam forma politica stabilis huius compositionis nondum deducitur.","refs":["MC_QGOH_L1_01:GA-06","MC_QGOH_L1_01:GA-10","MC_QGOH_L1_01:GA-13","MC_QGOH_L1_01:GA-14","CL_QGOH_03:RLE-03","CL_QGOH_04:GA-13","CL_QGOH_04:BSD-07"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"From","passages":[]},{"id":"MC_QGOH_L1_01:XD-07","text":"Novitas status non requirit ordinem possibilem extra actualitatem: intra ordinem necessarium emergit ex mutatione compositionis corporis, relationum et directionis affectivae.","refs":["MC_QGOH_L1_01:GA-01","MC_QGOH_L1_01:GA-04","MC_QGOH_L1_01:GA-12","CL_QGOH_01:GA-11","CL_QGOH_04:AS-06","CL_QGOH_04:MP-04"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"From","passages":[]},{"id":"MC_QGOH_L1_01:XD-08","text":"Imaginatio est commissura ambigua totius systematis: superstitionem et causalitatem fictam sustinet, sed etiam conatum conservat atque materiam praebet quam cognitio adaequata reordinat.","refs":["MC_QGOH_L1_01:GA-05","MC_QGOH_L1_01:GA-06","MC_QGOH_L1_01:GA-07","CL_QGOH_02:BAS-06","CL_QGOH_03:XD-06","CL_QGOH_04:RMD-04"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"From","passages":[]}],"original_name":"Cross-Unit Derivative Propositions"},{"number":"9","name":"Book-Scale Super-Derivatives","items":[{"id":"MC_QGOH_L1_01:BSD-01","text":"Ontologia, epistemologia et ethica fiunt tres altitudines unius integrationis causalis: realitas est expressio, veritas est adaequatio expressionis, et actio est eius causalitas sufficiens.","refs":["MC_QGOH_L1_01:GA-01","MC_QGOH_L1_01:GA-03","MC_QGOH_L1_01:GA-06","MC_QGOH_L1_01:GA-10","CL_QGOH_01:BSD-01","CL_QGOH_03:BSD-01"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"From","passages":[]},{"id":"MC_QGOH_L1_01:BSD-02","text":"Agentia subiecti non est proprietas binaria sed proportio variabilis inter causas externas, constitutionem corporis, ideas affectionum et effectus ex propria natura intelligibiles.","refs":["MC_QGOH_L1_01:GA-04","MC_QGOH_L1_01:GA-06","MC_QGOH_L1_01:GA-07","MC_QGOH_L1_01:GA-09","CL_QGOH_03:BSD-02","CL_QGOH_03:BSD-03","CL_QGOH_04:BSD-01"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"From","passages":[]},{"id":"MC_QGOH_L1_01:BSD-03","text":"Libertas crescit per cognitionem et recompositionem necessitatis, non per subtractionem causarum; determinatio externa minuitur quatenus nexus causales in operationem propriam integrantur.","refs":["MC_QGOH_L1_01:GA-06","MC_QGOH_L1_01:GA-10","MC_QGOH_L1_01:GA-13","CL_QGOH_03:XD-05","CL_QGOH_03:BSD-03"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"From","passages":[]},{"id":"MC_QGOH_L1_01:BSD-04","text":"Ordo socialis est circulatio inaequalis imaginum, attributionum et variationum potentiae, quae individua simul componit, distinguit et in circuitus reciprocos implicat.","refs":["MC_QGOH_L1_01:GA-05","MC_QGOH_L1_01:GA-08","MC_QGOH_L1_01:GA-09","CL_QGOH_04:GA-08","CL_QGOH_04:BSD-04"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"From","passages":[]},{"id":"MC_QGOH_L1_01:BSD-05","text":"Tempus non est continens uniforme, sed relatio inter necessitatem aeternam, durationem perseverantiae et reditus imaginativos qui corpus mutatum numquam ad statum omnino eundem reducunt.","refs":["MC_QGOH_L1_01:GA-04","MC_QGOH_L1_01:GA-07","MC_QGOH_L1_01:GA-12","CL_QGOH_02:HSI-04","CL_QGOH_03:BSD-04","CL_QGOH_04:HSI-03"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"From","passages":[]},{"id":"MC_QGOH_L1_01:BSD-06","text":"Interventio efficax condiciones compositionis, attributiones causae et ordines imaginativos mutare debet; imperium voluntatis sine tali reconfiguratione eandem causalitatem partialem reproducit.","refs":["MC_QGOH_L1_01:GA-05","MC_QGOH_L1_01:GA-06","MC_QGOH_L1_01:GA-14","CL_QGOH_03:BSD-06","CL_QGOH_04:BSD-06"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"From","passages":[]},{"id":"MC_QGOH_L1_01:BSD-07","text":"Methodus geometrica naturalizationem quam affirmat performat: vocabula transcendentalia et moralia in definitiones, nexus causales, mutationes potentiae et circuitus recursivos resolvit.","refs":["MC_QGOH_L1_01:GA-03","MC_QGOH_L1_01:GA-08","MC_QGOH_L1_01:GA-13","CL_QGOH_01:RMD-08","CL_QGOH_03:ALT-B05","CL_QGOH_04:ALT-B05"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"From","passages":[]}],"original_name":"Book-Scale Super-Derivatives"},{"number":"10","name":"Recursive Loops, Escalation Paths, and Conceptual Attractors","items":[{"id":"MC_QGOH_L1_01:RLE-01","text":"Partitio entis in substantiam et modos dependentiam constituit; dependentia immanentiam requirit; immanentia psychologiam naturalizat; psychologia vicissim fictionem exterioritatis explicat.","refs":["CL_QGOH_01:RLE-01","CL_QGOH_02:GA-12","CL_QGOH_02:RLE-07"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[]},{"id":"MC_QGOH_L1_01:RLE-02","text":"Affectio imaginem generat, imago valorem et Cupiditatem dirigit, Cupiditas actionem producit, actio corpus et relationes mutat, atque mutatum corpus novas affectiones recipit.","refs":["CL_QGOH_02:RLE-02","CL_QGOH_03:RLE-01","CL_QGOH_04:AS-05"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[]},{"id":"MC_QGOH_L1_01:RLE-03","text":"Ignorantia causarum opinionem libertatis et finium parit; hae imagines causas occultant, auctoritatem fictam confirmant et ignorantiam initialem reproducunt.","refs":["CL_QGOH_01:RLE-06","CL_QGOH_02:RLE-01","CL_QGOH_03:RLE-05"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[]},{"id":"MC_QGOH_L1_01:RLE-04","text":"Proprietates communes ideas adaequatas multiplicant; adaequatio actionem auget; actio compositiones potentiores efficit, unde amplificatio rationalis et socialis possibilis fit.","refs":["CL_QGOH_03:RLE-03","CL_QGOH_04:BSD-07"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[]},{"id":"MC_QGOH_L1_01:RLE-05","text":"Conatus imagines potentiae faventes retinet, Laetitiam et Cupiditatem auget, atque per hanc selectionem condiciones propriae perseverantiae recursive confirmat.","refs":["CL_QGOH_03:RLE-04","CL_QGOH_04:RMD-01"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[]},{"id":"MC_QGOH_L1_01:RLE-06","text":"Affectus imaginatus alterius responsionem excitat; responsio novam causam affectivam praebet; reciprocitas inde in Amorem, Gloriam, Odium, Vindictam vel rivalitatem escalat.","refs":["CL_QGOH_04:GA-08","CL_QGOH_04:CGD-02"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[]},{"id":"MC_QGOH_L1_01:RLE-07","text":"Repetitio affectiva numquam mera identitas est, quia corpus per fruitionem et occursus mutatur; eadem imago rediens affectum diversum aut contrarium producere potest.","refs":["CL_QGOH_04:AS-06","CL_QGOH_04:XD-05"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[]},{"id":"MC_QGOH_L1_01:RLE-08","text":"Attractor principalis est causalitas adaequata, circa quam intelligibilitas, libertas, conservatio potentiae et Generositas convergunt sine egressu ex necessitate.","refs":["CL_QGOH_03:RLE-07","CL_QGOH_04:GA-14","CL_QGOH_04:BSD-07"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[]}],"original_name":"Recursive Loops, Escalation Paths, and Conceptual Attractors"},{"number":"11","name":"Formal Generative Schema of the Whole Book","items":[{"id":"MC_QGOH_L1_01:FGS-B01","text":"Una Natura necessario existens → nulla exterioritas substantialis → omnia entia ut modi causaliter dependentes.","refs":["CL_QGOH_01:GA-01","CL_QGOH_01:GA-08"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[]},{"id":"MC_QGOH_L1_01:FGS-B02","text":"Expressio per attributa → idem individuum sub Cogitatione et Extensione → series causales distinctae sine dualismo substantiali.","refs":["CL_QGOH_01:GA-09","CL_QGOH_02:GA-03"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[]},{"id":"MC_QGOH_L1_01:FGS-B03","text":"Occursus corporum → affectio relationem afficientis et affecti involvens → idea affectionis → perceptio perspectivalis et variatio potentiae.","refs":["CL_QGOH_02:GA-06","CL_QGOH_03:RMD-01"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[]},{"id":"MC_QGOH_L1_01:FGS-B04","text":"Contextus causarum incompletus → vestigium et praesentificatio → associatio imaginativa → contingentia, finis, valor et causa per accidens.","refs":["CL_QGOH_02:GA-08","CL_QGOH_02:XD-03"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[]},{"id":"MC_QGOH_L1_01:FGS-B05","text":"Essentia actualis → conatus → Cupiditas → augmentum aut imminutio potentiae → Laetitia aut Tristitia → actio vel passio secundum qualitatem causae.","refs":["CL_QGOH_03:GA-07","CL_QGOH_03:GA-09","CL_QGOH_04:GA-02"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[]},{"id":"MC_QGOH_L1_01:FGS-B06","text":"Imago affectus alieni → imitatio et attributio causae → responsio reciproca → circuitus socialis → reconfiguratio identitatis et potentiae.","refs":["CL_QGOH_04:GA-04","CL_QGOH_04:GA-08"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[]},{"id":"MC_QGOH_L1_01:FGS-B07","text":"Integratio ideae in contextum causarum → causa magis adaequata → actio → Laetitia et Cupiditas activae → Fortitudo atque Generositas.","refs":["CL_QGOH_03:GA-03","CL_QGOH_04:GA-12","CL_QGOH_04:BSD-07"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[]},{"id":"MC_QGOH_L1_01:FGS-B08","text":"Metaphysica immanentiae → constitutio Mentis corporeae → genesis erroris et affectus → explicatio projectionis transcendentis → reditus ad immanentiam sub forma libertatis causalis.","refs":["CL_QGOH_02:GA-12","CL_QGOH_02:RLE-07"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[]}],"original_name":"Formal Generative Schema of the Whole Book"},{"number":"12","name":"Whole-Book Argument Flow Map","items":[{"id":"MC_QGOH_L1_01:AFM-B01","text":"Substantia sui causa → causalitas immanens → modi finiti → individuum corporeum → Mens ut idea Corporis → affectio → imaginatio → conatus → affectus → socialitas.","refs":["CL_QGOH_01:BAS-04","CL_QGOH_02:GA-04","CL_QGOH_03:GA-07","CL_QGOH_04:GA-08"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[]},{"id":"MC_QGOH_L1_01:AFM-B02","text":"Ignorantia causarum → contingentia et finis imaginati → utilitas et valor proiecti → auctoritas transcendentis → ulterior occultatio causalitatis.","refs":["CL_QGOH_01:RMD-09","CL_QGOH_02:XD-03","CL_QGOH_02:XD-09"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[]},{"id":"MC_QGOH_L1_01:AFM-B03","text":"Affectio → praesentificatio → imitatio → reciprocitas → rivalitas vel compositio → mutatio Corporis → novus circuitus affectivus.","refs":["CL_QGOH_04:RMD-03","CL_QGOH_04:RMD-05","CL_QGOH_04:RMD-08"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[]},{"id":"MC_QGOH_L1_01:AFM-B04","text":"Cognitio causalis → adaequatio aucta → passio imminuta → actio ex propria natura → Fortitudo et Generositas → libertas intra necessitatem.","refs":["CL_QGOH_03:AS-07","CL_QGOH_04:GA-13","CL_QGOH_04:BSD-07"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[]}],"original_name":"Whole-Book Argument Flow Map"},{"number":"13","name":"Macro Map-Effects","items":[{"id":"MC_QGOH_L1_01:MME-01","text":"Oppositio Dei et mundi corruit; Deus non est artifex iuxta res, sed causalitas immanens cuius modi totum campum realitatis constituunt.","refs":["CL_QGOH_01:GNX-03","CL_QGOH_02:MME-01"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[]},{"id":"MC_QGOH_L1_01:MME-02","text":"Oppositio Mentis et Corporis reordinatur in duplicem expressionem unius individui, unde subiectum mentale clausum locum processui corporeo-relationali cedit.","refs":["CL_QGOH_02:GNX-03","CL_QGOH_03:MME-03","CL_QGOH_04:MME-05"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[]},{"id":"MC_QGOH_L1_01:MME-03","text":"Ager cognitionis et ager agentiae in unam scalam causalitatis plenae vel partialis coalescunt.","refs":["CL_QGOH_03:XD-01","CL_QGOH_03:MME-01"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[]},{"id":"MC_QGOH_L1_01:MME-04","text":"Valor reclassificatur a proprietate obiecti in indicem desiderii, compositionis corporis et mutationis potentiae.","refs":["CL_QGOH_03:MME-06","CL_QGOH_04:MME-01","CL_QGOH_04:MME-04"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[]},{"id":"MC_QGOH_L1_01:MME-05","text":"Socialitas reclassificatur ab aggregatione subiectorum praeconstitutorum in circulationem imaginum, attributionum causarum et affectuum reciprocorum.","refs":["CL_QGOH_04:BSD-04","CL_QGOH_04:MME-03"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[]},{"id":"MC_QGOH_L1_01:MME-06","text":"Tempus reclassificatur a successione homogena in coordinationem aeternitatis, durationis, memoriae, expectationis et mutationis irreversibilis corporis.","refs":["CL_QGOH_03:BSD-04","CL_QGOH_04:MME-02","CL_QGOH_04:HSI-03"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[]},{"id":"MC_QGOH_L1_01:MME-07","text":"Methodus interventionis transit ab imperio, iudicio et catalogo ad analysin geneticam compositionum, contextuum causalium et circuituum amplificationis.","refs":["CL_QGOH_02:BSD-08","CL_QGOH_04:BSD-06","CL_QGOH_04:MME-06"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[]}],"original_name":"Macro Map-Effects"},{"number":"14","name":"Altitude Integration","items":[{"id":"MC_QGOH_L1_01:ALT-B01","text":"In gradu superficiei agunt Deus, Natura, Mens, Corpus, cognitio, voluntas, Cupiditas, Laetitia, Tristitia et relationes sociales.","refs":["CL_QGOH_01:ALT-B01","CL_QGOH_03:ALT-B01","CL_QGOH_04:ALT-B01"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[]},{"id":"MC_QGOH_L1_01:ALT-B02","text":"In gradu structurali omnia haec obiecta distribuuntur per causalitatem immanentem, individuationem relationalem, affectiones, conatum, adaequationem et circuitus affectivos.","refs":["CL_QGOH_01:ALT-B02","CL_QGOH_02:ALT-B02","CL_QGOH_03:ALT-B02","CL_QGOH_04:ALT-B02"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[]},{"id":"MC_QGOH_L1_01:ALT-B03","text":"In gradu metastrategico quaestio transfertur ab electione, fine et iudicio ad generationem, attributionem causae, compositionem potentiae et condiciones interventionis.","refs":["CL_QGOH_01:ALT-B03","CL_QGOH_02:ALT-B03","CL_QGOH_04:ALT-B03"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[]},{"id":"MC_QGOH_L1_01:ALT-B04","text":"In gradu ontologico et temporali realitas est una productio necessaria, singularia sunt relationes perseverantes, et tempus est nexus aeternitatis cum duratione atque praesentificatione.","refs":["CL_QGOH_01:ALT-B04","CL_QGOH_02:ALT-B04","CL_QGOH_04:ALT-B04"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[]},{"id":"MC_QGOH_L1_01:ALT-B05","text":"In gradu methodologico et performativo systema negationibus exterioritatis, deductionibus geneticis et redefinitionibus causalibus ipsam naturalizationem quam asserit efficit.","refs":["CL_QGOH_01:ALT-B05","CL_QGOH_03:ALT-B05","CL_QGOH_04:ALT-B05"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[]}],"original_name":"Altitude Integration"},{"number":"15","name":"Internal Tensions, Mutation Points, and Fault Lines","items":[{"id":"MC_QGOH_L1_01:FL-01","text":"Attributa per se concipiuntur, sed eorum distinctio cum unitate numerica substantiae positive coordinanda manet; exclusio pluralitatis nexum plene non substituit.","refs":["CL_QGOH_01:GA-03","CL_QGOH_01:FL-01"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"Tension","passages":[]},{"id":"MC_QGOH_L1_01:FL-02","text":"Causalitas divina omnes modos determinat, dum finita immediate per finita producuntur; mediatio inter productivitatem aeternam et series singulares manet compressa.","refs":["CL_QGOH_01:FL-02","CL_QGOH_01:FL-03"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"Tension","passages":[]},{"id":"MC_QGOH_L1_01:FL-03","text":"Parallelismus identitatem processus Mentis et Corporis servat sine commercio causali, sed modum positivum huius congruentiae ultra duplicem expressionem non explicat.","refs":["CL_QGOH_02:FL-02","CL_QGOH_03:FL-02"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"Tension","passages":[]},{"id":"MC_QGOH_L1_01:FL-04","text":"Falsitas privatio cognitionis dicitur, quamquam error mechanismis corporalibus et socialibus positive producitur; privatio normativam formam, genesis causalitatem rei explicat, sed nexus earum manet delicatus.","refs":["CL_QGOH_02:BAS-07","CL_QGOH_03:GA-04","CL_QGOH_02:BSD-03"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"Tension","passages":[]},{"id":"MC_QGOH_L1_01:FL-05","text":"Necessitas omnium idearum cum discrimine normativo inter adaequationem et inadaequationem componenda est sine tribunali externo.","refs":["CL_QGOH_02:FL-05","CL_QGOH_03:XD-08"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"Tension","passages":[]},{"id":"MC_QGOH_L1_01:FL-06","text":"Directio ethica et politica cognitioni adaequatae innititur, sed transitus ab intellecta necessitate ad transformationem stabilem affectuum et collectivorum incompletus manet.","refs":["CL_QGOH_03:FL-03","CL_QGOH_04:FL-05"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"Tension","passages":[]},{"id":"MC_QGOH_L1_01:MP-01","text":"Infinitas mutatur ab exclusione termini in potentiam productivam cuius effectus necessario actuantur.","refs":["CL_QGOH_01:RMD-02"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"Tension","passages":[]},{"id":"MC_QGOH_L1_01:MP-02","text":"Corpus mutatur ab obiecto Mentis in individuum relationale, deinde in variabilem potentiam quae cognitionem, affectum et valorem ordinat.","refs":["CL_QGOH_02:MP-02","CL_QGOH_03:MP-04"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"Tension","passages":[]},{"id":"MC_QGOH_L1_01:MP-03","text":"Imaginatio mutatur a praeiudicio generali in mechanismum neutrum, deinde in operatorem conativum et socialem qui tam capturam quam conservationem sustinet.","refs":["CL_QGOH_02:BAS-06","CL_QGOH_02:MP-04","CL_QGOH_04:RMD-03"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"Tension","passages":[]},{"id":"MC_QGOH_L1_01:MP-04","text":"Adaequatio mutatur a qualitate veritatis in causalitatem activam, dum inadaequatio a partialitate epistemica in passionem et dependentiam socialem transit.","refs":["CL_QGOH_03:GA-03","CL_QGOH_03:MP-02","CL_QGOH_04:MP-05"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"Tension","passages":[]},{"id":"MC_QGOH_L1_01:MP-05","text":"Libertas mutatur ab facultate indeterminata in necessitatem propriae naturae et tandem in gradum variablem causalitatis adaequatae.","refs":["CL_QGOH_01:RMD-05","CL_QGOH_03:MP-05"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"Tension","passages":[]},{"id":"MC_QGOH_L1_01:MP-06","text":"Perfectio et valor mutantur ab conformitate externa in potentiam singularis, deinde in relationem affectivam cuius obiectum ipsum stabilitatem axiologicam amittit.","refs":["CL_QGOH_02:GA-09","CL_QGOH_03:MME-06","CL_QGOH_04:RMD-09"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"Tension","passages":[]}],"original_name":"Internal Tensions, Mutation Points, and Fault Lines"},{"number":"16","name":"Hidden System Image","items":[{"id":"MC_QGOH_L1_01:HSI-01","text":"Realitas est machina immanens sui expressionis, reticulum causarum et corporum in quo effectus non exeunt ex Natura sed eius potentiam localiter redistribuunt.","refs":["CL_QGOH_01:HSI-01","CL_QGOH_02:HSI-01","CL_QGOH_04:HSI-01"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[]},{"id":"MC_QGOH_L1_01:HSI-02","text":"Subiectum est vortex corporeus et mentalis conatu perseverans, permeabilis causis externis et imaginibus aliorum, cuius autonomia secundum adaequationem variatur.","refs":["CL_QGOH_02:HSI-02","CL_QGOH_03:HSI-02","CL_QGOH_04:HSI-02"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[]},{"id":"MC_QGOH_L1_01:HSI-03","text":"Tempus est machina stratificata in qua necessitas aeterna singularia durare facit, memoria absentia reducit, et corporis mutatio identitatem reditus impedit.","refs":["CL_QGOH_03:HSI-03","CL_QGOH_04:HSI-03"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[]},{"id":"MC_QGOH_L1_01:HSI-04","text":"Captura est circuitus quo ignorantia, utilitas, imago causae et affectus reciproci individuum ad causas externas alligant dum speciem libertatis producunt.","refs":["CL_QGOH_02:HSI-05","CL_QGOH_03:RLE-05","CL_QGOH_04:BSD-03"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[]},{"id":"MC_QGOH_L1_01:HSI-05","text":"Navigatio possibilis non est fuga e machina, sed transitus per eam ab affectionibus partialiter intellectis ad compositiones causales adaequatas, quarum forma socialis est Generositas.","refs":["CL_QGOH_01:HSI-05","CL_QGOH_03:HSI-04","CL_QGOH_04:HSI-04"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[]}],"original_name":"Hidden System Image"},{"number":"17","name":"Minimal Whole-Book Reconstruction Test","items":[{"id":"MC_QGOH_L1_01:MRT-B01","text":"Axiomata immanentiae, individuationis corporeae, imaginationis, adaequationis, conatus et recursionis affectivae sufficiunt ad machinam huius metagregis restituendam.","refs":["CL_QGOH_01:MRT-B01","CL_QGOH_02:MRT-B01","CL_QGOH_03:MRT-B01","CL_QGOH_04:MRT-B01"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[]},{"id":"MC_QGOH_L1_01:MRT-B02","text":"Structura firmissima est continuatio qua causalitas immanens affectionem producit, affectio cognitionem et potentiam variat, atque variatio in actiones et circuitus sociales distribuitur.","refs":["CL_QGOH_01:MRT-B02","CL_QGOH_02:MRT-B02","CL_QGOH_04:MRT-B03"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[]},{"id":"MC_QGOH_L1_01:MRT-B03","text":"Minus determinata manet conversio cognitionis adaequatae in moderationem stabilem affectuum, compositionem socialem et beatitudinem.","refs":["CL_QGOH_02:MRT-B03","CL_QGOH_03:MRT-B03","CL_QGOH_04:MRT-B04"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[]},{"id":"MC_QGOH_L1_01:MRT-B04","text":"Systema hac magnitudine cohaeret tantum si ordo non ut series doctrinarum, sed ut circuitus a metaphysica ad corpus, ab corpore ad imaginationem et ab imaginatione ad criticam metaphysicae legitur.","refs":["CL_QGOH_02:MRT-B07","CL_QGOH_03:MRT-B05"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[]},{"id":"MC_QGOH_L1_01:MRT-B05","text":"Restitutio motum generativum servat, sed pluralitas singularium affectuum, differentiae corporum et forma terminalis vitae activae compressioni axiomaticae adhuc resistunt.","refs":["CL_QGOH_03:MRT-B05","CL_QGOH_04:FL-04","CL_QGOH_04:MRT-B04"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[]}],"original_name":"Minimal Whole-Book Reconstruction Test"}],"language_code":"la","language_name":"Latina","translations":[{"id":"CAREVAR_20261015T015824682785Z_9e7f60c4","language_code":"en","language_name":"English","title":"Qgoh L1 01","source_sha256":"ea42ac7aaa0615c7ea30a684a49863481dd2d110555392fb231d93f828ddcf15","sha256":"0749be68eddb8861ce01967a6e9f180029d5e6f77e43f264d7cef013db875fb9","rendition_type":"machine","review_status":"machine","model":"gpt-5.6-terra","created":"2026-10-15T01:58:24.683773+00:00","sections":[{"number":"1","name":"Book-Scale Argumental Spine","items":[{"id":"MC_QGOH_L1_01:AS-01","text":"The system asks how one necessarily immanent Nature generates bodies, minds, cognitions, conatus, and affective relations, while within that same necessity the passage from external determination to proper action remains possible.","refs":["CL_QGOH_01:BAS-01","CL_QGOH_02:BAS-01","CL_QGOH_03:AS-01","CL_QGOH_04:AS-01"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[]},{"id":"MC_QGOH_L1_01:AS-02","text":"The global thesis affirms that every being is an expression of one Nature, whose causality is distributed through corporeal individuals, ideas, imaginations, and affects, and is transformed into passion or action according to the degree of adequacy.","refs":["CL_QGOH_01:BAS-02","CL_QGOH_02:BAS-02","CL_QGOH_03:AS-02","CL_QGOH_04:AS-02"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[]},{"id":"MC_QGOH_L1_01:AS-03","text":"Geometrical argumentation becomes a recursive genealogy: the exclusion of exteriority establishes necessary causality, causality explains the constitution of Body and Mind, and this constitution restores errors, values, and social circuits as natural effects.","refs":["CL_QGOH_01:BAS-03","CL_QGOH_02:BAS-03","CL_QGOH_03:AS-03","CL_QGOH_04:AS-03"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[]},{"id":"MC_QGOH_L1_01:AS-04","text":"The movement proceeds from substance that is its own cause to modal production, from finite mode to corporeal individual, from affection to idea and affect, and from circuits of passions to causally adequate action.","refs":["CL_QGOH_01:BAS-04","CL_QGOH_03:AS-04","CL_QGOH_04:AS-04"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[]},{"id":"MC_QGOH_L1_01:AS-05","text":"The true movement is cumulative and spiral, because the psychology of the body explains metaphysical errors already excluded, the doctrine of affects transfers the structure of cognition into sociality, and each return receives a subject already transformed.","refs":["CL_QGOH_01:BAS-06","CL_QGOH_02:BAS-05","CL_QGOH_03:AS-05","CL_QGOH_04:AS-05"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[]},{"id":"MC_QGOH_L1_01:AS-06","text":"Necessity changes from ontological closure into a rule of individuation, cognition, and agency; what initially removes all exteriority ultimately defines freedom as a degree of proper causality.","refs":["CL_QGOH_01:GA-06","CL_QGOH_03:AS-06","CL_QGOH_04:AS-07"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[]},{"id":"MC_QGOH_L1_01:AS-07","text":"Error, teleology, free will, and transcendent value do not fall outside Nature, but are positively produced from partial cognition, memory, utility, attribution of cause, and the circulation of affects.","refs":["CL_QGOH_02:BAS-07","CL_QGOH_02:BSD-03","CL_QGOH_04:DBN-02"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[]},{"id":"MC_QGOH_L1_01:AS-08","text":"Closure is immanent yet provisionally open: escape from necessity is excluded, action and Generosity are constituted within it, but the full mechanism by which adequacy stably transforms the order of passions is not yet completed.","refs":["CL_QGOH_01:BAS-08","CL_QGOH_02:BAS-08","CL_QGOH_03:AS-07","CL_QGOH_04:AS-08"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[]}],"original_name":"Book-Scale Argumental Spine"},{"number":"2","name":"Global Load-Bearing Axioms","items":[{"id":"MC_QGOH_L1_01:GA-01","text":"[Persistent] One necessarily existing Nature is the immanent cause of all things; no being, tribunal, or causal principle outside it is given.","refs":["CL_QGOH_01:GA-08","CL_QGOH_02:GA-13","CL_QGOH_03:GA-01"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[]},{"id":"MC_QGOH_L1_01:GA-02","text":"[Persistent] Everything finite is a mode and a causally dependent node; the human Mind and Body are not separate substances, but attributive expressions of the same individual.","refs":["CL_QGOH_01:GA-01","CL_QGOH_02:GA-04","CL_QGOH_03:BSD-02"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[]},{"id":"MC_QGOH_L1_01:GA-03","text":"[Mutating] Causal necessity extends from the production of modes to the order of cognition, action, and social propagation, without rupture between ontology and psychology.","refs":["CL_QGOH_01:GA-05","CL_QGOH_02:GA-02","CL_QGOH_04:GA-09"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[]},{"id":"MC_QGOH_L1_01:GA-04","text":"[Mutating] The corporeal individual is a persisting dynamic relation, whose affections simultaneously constitute the perception of the Mind and variations of the power of acting.","refs":["CL_QGOH_02:GA-05","CL_QGOH_02:GA-06","CL_QGOH_03:GA-09"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[]},{"id":"MC_QGOH_L1_01:GA-05","text":"[Mutating] Imagination is the natural mechanism by which affections, traces, absences, similarities, and habits produce affective presences and evaluative connections.","refs":["CL_QGOH_02:GA-07","CL_QGOH_04:GA-03","CL_QGOH_04:GA-04"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[]},{"id":"MC_QGOH_L1_01:GA-06","text":"[Mutating] Adequacy and inadequacy are not merely qualities of cognition: the former constitutes active causality, the latter partial causality, passion, and capture by external causes.","refs":["CL_QGOH_03:GA-03","CL_QGOH_03:GA-04","CL_QGOH_04:GA-12"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[]},{"id":"MC_QGOH_L1_01:GA-07","text":"[Emergent] Conatus is the actual essence of the individual, whose operations converge upon the preservation and increase of the power of acting.","refs":["CL_QGOH_03:GA-07","CL_QGOH_04:GA-01","CL_QGOH_04:GA-14"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[]},{"id":"MC_QGOH_L1_01:GA-08","text":"[Emergent] Good, evil, and perfection are not extrinsically normed properties, but indices of the relation between a thing's constitution, appetite, and variation of power.","refs":["CL_QGOH_02:GA-09","CL_QGOH_03:GNX-07","CL_QGOH_04:GA-10"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[]},{"id":"MC_QGOH_L1_01:GA-09","text":"[Emergent] The subject is a corporeal, historical, and socially distributed process, which constitutes itself through affections, ideas, and imagined responses of others.","refs":["CL_QGOH_02:BSD-02","CL_QGOH_03:BSD-02","CL_QGOH_04:BSD-01"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[]},{"id":"MC_QGOH_L1_01:GA-10","text":"[Terminal] Freedom is an increase of adequate causality within necessity, not indifference, causal exemption, or the absolute beginning of action.","refs":["CL_QGOH_01:GA-07","CL_QGOH_03:GA-10","CL_QGOH_04:BSA-04"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[]},{"id":"MC_QGOH_L1_01:GA-11","text":"[Persistent] Thought and Extension express the same order without transverse causality; the congruence of Mind and Body signifies the identity of process, not an interaction of substances.","refs":["CL_QGOH_02:GA-03","CL_QGOH_02:XD-05","CL_QGOH_03:FL-02"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[]},{"id":"MC_QGOH_L1_01:GA-12","text":"[Emergent] The system's time is stratified into the eternal necessity of Nature, the indefinite duration of conatus, and imaginative presentifications of past or future.","refs":["CL_QGOH_03:GA-12","CL_QGOH_03:BSD-04","CL_QGOH_04:BSD-05"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[]},{"id":"MC_QGOH_L1_01:GA-13","text":"[Attractor] Causal intelligibility is a common attractor: it inserts local ideas into a broader context, reorders passion into action, and dissolves fictive exteriorities.","refs":["CL_QGOH_01:GA-14","CL_QGOH_02:GA-11","CL_QGOH_03:RLE-07"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[]},{"id":"MC_QGOH_L1_01:GA-14","text":"[Terminal] Ethics is the immanent reconfiguration of cognition, body, and relations, whose active social form culminates in Courage and Generosity, not in the command of the will.","refs":["CL_QGOH_04:GA-13","CL_QGOH_04:BSD-07","CL_QGOH_04:HSI-04"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[]}],"original_name":"Global Load-Bearing Axioms"},{"number":"3","name":"Recurrence, Mutation, and Displacement Matrix","items":[{"id":"MC_QGOH_L1_01:RMD-01","text":"Substance retains ontological identity, but after establishing immanent closure, it recedes from the foreground and operates as the tacit infrastructure of cognition and affects.","refs":["CL_QGOH_01:RMD-01","CL_QGOH_03:RMD-08"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[]},{"id":"MC_QGOH_L1_01:RMD-02","text":"Immanence is expanded from the relation of God and modes to the constitution of the corporeal Mind, the demonstrability of affects, and the social circulation of causality.","refs":["CL_QGOH_01:GA-08","CL_QGOH_02:GA-12","CL_QGOH_03:XD-04","CL_QGOH_02:RLE-05"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[]},{"id":"MC_QGOH_L1_01:RMD-03","text":"Necessity changes function: the closure of modality becomes the genetic principle of imagination, the distinction between action and passion, and the condition of internal freedom.","refs":["CL_QGOH_02:RMD-01","CL_QGOH_03:AS-06","CL_QGOH_04:AS-07"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[]},{"id":"MC_QGOH_L1_01:RMD-04","text":"The Body is transformed from a determined mode into a relational individual, then into a variable substratum of power, and finally into the condition of the mutability of values.","refs":["CL_QGOH_02:GA-05","CL_QGOH_03:RMD-06","CL_QGOH_04:AS-06"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[]},{"id":"MC_QGOH_L1_01:RMD-05","text":"The idea of affection returns as affect: the same mediation that presents the external through the constitution of the Body now expresses increase or diminution of power.","refs":["CL_QGOH_03:RMD-01","CL_QGOH_03:MP-01"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[]},{"id":"MC_QGOH_L1_01:RMD-06","text":"Imagination changes from a general name for error into a neutral mechanism of presentification, then into a conative, interpersonal, and reflexive operator.","refs":["CL_QGOH_02:BAS-06","CL_QGOH_03:RMD-07","CL_QGOH_04:RMD-03"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[]},{"id":"MC_QGOH_L1_01:RMD-07","text":"Freedom undergoes a stable inversion: indifference disappears, the necessity of one's own nature remains, and the degree of freedom is measured by the proportion of adequate causality.","refs":["CL_QGOH_01:RMD-05","CL_QGOH_03:MP-05"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[]},{"id":"MC_QGOH_L1_01:RMD-08","text":"Perfection and good migrate from external models to the power of the thing; ultimately, even the identity of the value-object dissolves into the event of a mutable relation.","refs":["CL_QGOH_02:RMD-06","CL_QGOH_03:MME-06","CL_QGOH_04:RMD-09"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[]},{"id":"MC_QGOH_L1_01:RMD-09","text":"Conatus is expanded from the actual essence of the singular into a principle for the selection of images, the production of Desire, and socially mediated preservation.","refs":["CL_QGOH_03:GA-07","CL_QGOH_04:RMD-01","CL_QGOH_04:GA-14"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[]},{"id":"MC_QGOH_L1_01:RMD-10","text":"End, after ceasing to be the causality of Nature, is transferred into an index of imagination and finally absorbed into the more general operation of attribution of cause.","refs":["CL_QGOH_02:RLE-08","CL_QGOH_04:GA-09"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[]}],"original_name":"Recurrence, Mutation, and Displacement Matrix"},{"number":"4","name":"Lower-Layer vs Current-Level Distinction","items":[{"id":"MC_QGOH_L1_01:CGD-01","text":"Local demonstrations of the uniqueness of substance are absorbed at this level into the more general closure by which all ontological, epistemic, and practical exteriority is excluded.","refs":["CL_QGOH_01:CGD-01","CL_QGOH_01:BSA-01"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[]},{"id":"MC_QGOH_L1_01:CGD-02","text":"Infinite modes, finite modes, and Natura Naturata provide the architecture of mediation, but the connection at the present level is the continuation of that mediation in bodies, ideas, and affects.","refs":["CL_QGOH_01:CGD-03","CL_QGOH_01:CGD-04"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[]},{"id":"MC_QGOH_L1_01:CGD-03","text":"Traces of corporeal parts remain a local hypothesis of imagination; at the higher level, only the function persists by which the history of affections effectively presents absences and habits.","refs":["CL_QGOH_02:CGD-03","CL_QGOH_02:RLE-02"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[]},{"id":"MC_QGOH_L1_01:CGD-04","text":"Taxonomies of singular affects belong to the lower level; at this level their variety is compressed into a grammar of conatus, power, imagination, and attribution of cause.","refs":["CL_QGOH_03:CGD-04","CL_QGOH_04:CGD-01","CL_QGOH_04:GA-11"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[]},{"id":"MC_QGOH_L1_01:CGD-05","text":"The local critique of finalism is expanded into a general diagnosis by which partial cognitions, values, and transcendent authorities are produced from corporeal and social mechanisms.","refs":["CL_QGOH_01:CGD-07","CL_QGOH_02:XD-01","CL_QGOH_02:XD-09"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[]},{"id":"MC_QGOH_L1_01:CGD-06","text":"The parallelism of attributes connects ontology, psychology, and the doctrine of affects, but introduces no transverse causality and does not further explain the mechanism of congruence.","refs":["CL_QGOH_02:BAS-04","CL_QGOH_02:FL-02","CL_QGOH_03:FL-02"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[]},{"id":"MC_QGOH_L1_01:CGD-07","text":"The distinction between adequate and partial cause produces a transition of higher order, because it transforms truth and error into action, passion, and forms of social power.","refs":["CL_QGOH_03:CGD-05","CL_QGOH_03:XD-01"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[]},{"id":"MC_QGOH_L1_01:CGD-08","text":"Blessedness remains an incomplete attractor: it orders the direction from necessity to freedom, but the full conversion of cognition into affective stability remains beyond the present determination.","refs":["CL_QGOH_02:CGD-08","CL_QGOH_02:FL-06","CL_QGOH_03:MRT-B03"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[]}],"original_name":"Lower-Layer vs Current-Level Distinction"},{"number":"5","name":"Global Apophatic Structure","items":[{"id":"MC_QGOH_L1_01:GNX-01","text":"No place outside Nature is admitted from which causality, cognition, value, or action might receive origin or norm.","refs":["CL_QGOH_01:BSA-01","CL_QGOH_02:BSA-01","CL_QGOH_03:BSA-01","CL_QGOH_04:BSA-01"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"Negative","passages":[]},{"id":"MC_QGOH_L1_01:GNX-02","text":"Neither an alternative world nor an act immune from the series of causes constitutes a real possibility.","refs":["CL_QGOH_01:BSA-02","CL_QGOH_03:BSA-02"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"Negative","passages":[]},{"id":"MC_QGOH_L1_01:GNX-03","text":"A future end, divine choice, and human utility cannot govern the causality of Nature.","refs":["CL_QGOH_01:GNX-06","CL_QGOH_02:GNX-01"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"Negative","passages":[]},{"id":"MC_QGOH_L1_01:GNX-04","text":"Mind and Body are neither separate substances nor causally transverse agents.","refs":["CL_QGOH_02:GNX-03","CL_QGOH_03:GNX-04"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"Negative","passages":[]},{"id":"MC_QGOH_L1_01:GNX-05","text":"The finite subject is neither immediately transparent to itself nor affectively autonomous.","refs":["CL_QGOH_02:DBN-03","CL_QGOH_04:GNX-03"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"Negative","passages":[]},{"id":"MC_QGOH_L1_01:GNX-06","text":"Good, evil, order, and perfection do not constitute intrinsic properties of things or a moral tribunal independent of desire.","refs":["CL_QGOH_02:GNX-08","CL_QGOH_04:GNX-04"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"Negative","passages":[]},{"id":"MC_QGOH_L1_01:GNX-07","text":"The identity of the individual, value, and affect is stabilized neither by permanent matter, nor by an invariant object, nor by a terminal catalogue.","refs":["CL_QGOH_02:GNX-06","CL_QGOH_04:BSA-03"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"Negative","passages":[]}],"original_name":"Global Apophatic Structure"},{"number":"6","name":"Deep Book Apophatics","items":[{"id":"MC_QGOH_L1_01:DBN-01","text":"The separation of divine essence, existence, power, and actuality must be false, because it would introduce an ontological gap through which contingency and exteriority would return.","refs":["CL_QGOH_01:DBN-01","CL_QGOH_01:DBN-02"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"Negative","passages":[]},{"id":"MC_QGOH_L1_01:DBN-02","text":"Causally inert cognition must be false, because the difference of adequacy is the same difference that separates action from passion.","refs":["CL_QGOH_03:DBN-05","CL_QGOH_03:GA-03"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"Negative","passages":[]},{"id":"MC_QGOH_L1_01:DBN-03","text":"The universality of the laws of affects cannot imply uniformity of responses; otherwise the diversity of bodies and the singularity of essences would vanish.","refs":["CL_QGOH_04:DBN-05","CL_QGOH_04:GA-06"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"Negative","passages":[]},{"id":"MC_QGOH_L1_01:DBN-04","text":"A norm of truth or goodness imposed from outside must be false, because both ideas and appetites are assessed from an intrinsic causal order.","refs":["CL_QGOH_02:DBN-07","CL_QGOH_03:DBN-04"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"Negative","passages":[]},{"id":"MC_QGOH_L1_01:DBN-05","text":"Error as a mere irrational irruption outside Nature must be false; falsity is privative, but its corporeal and social conditions are positively generated.","refs":["CL_QGOH_02:BAS-07","CL_QGOH_03:GA-04","CL_QGOH_02:BSD-03"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"Negative","passages":[]},{"id":"MC_QGOH_L1_01:DBN-06","text":"Freedom that diminishes necessity or requires an uncaused cause must be false; otherwise action would be reduced to ontological exemption, not adequacy.","refs":["CL_QGOH_03:BSA-03","CL_QGOH_04:BSA-04"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"Negative","passages":[]}],"original_name":"Deep Book Apophatics"},{"number":"7","name":"Super-Apophatics of the Whole","items":[{"id":"MC_QGOH_L1_01:BSA-01","text":"The supreme absence is the outside itself, whose exclusion encloses substance, cognition, affect, and ethics within the same immanence.","refs":["CL_QGOH_01:BSA-01","CL_QGOH_03:BSA-01"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"Negative","passages":[]},{"id":"MC_QGOH_L1_01:BSA-02","text":"An absolute origin of thought, appetite, or action is ontologically unavailable; every apparent beginning is a connection of an already constituted causal nexus.","refs":["CL_QGOH_03:BSA-02","CL_QGOH_04:BSA-01"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"Negative","passages":[]},{"id":"MC_QGOH_L1_01:BSA-03","text":"A transcendent tribunal judging the actual order, values, and affects before their generation is impossible.","refs":["CL_QGOH_01:BSA-03","CL_QGOH_04:BSA-02"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"Negative","passages":[]},{"id":"MC_QGOH_L1_01:BSA-04","text":"The presence of an external thing or of a subject to itself, unmediated by body, affection, and causal history, is unavailable.","refs":["CL_QGOH_02:BSA-02","CL_QGOH_02:BSA-03","CL_QGOH_04:DBN-03"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"Negative","passages":[]},{"id":"MC_QGOH_L1_01:BSA-05","text":"The possibility not yet exhibited is the integral conversion of adequate cognition into affective and social stability; this absence orders ethical direction without demonstrated closure.","refs":["CL_QGOH_02:BSA-06","CL_QGOH_03:MRT-B03","CL_QGOH_04:MRT-B04"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"Negative","passages":[]}],"original_name":"Super-Apophatics of the Whole"},{"number":"8","name":"Cross-Unit Derivative Propositions","items":[{"id":"MC_QGOH_L1_01:XD-01","text":"Because ontological immanence constitutes Mind from Body, error is not an exception to Nature but an endogenous circuit by which a local affection is projected into a universal structure.","refs":["MC_QGOH_L1_01:GA-01","MC_QGOH_L1_01:GA-04","MC_QGOH_L1_01:GA-05","MC_QGOH_L1_01:GA-06","CL_QGOH_01:GA-08","CL_QGOH_02:GA-12","CL_QGOH_02:BSD-03"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"From","passages":[]},{"id":"MC_QGOH_L1_01:XD-02","text":"Universal necessity joined with partial causality and imitation of affects explains domination not as a violation of nature, but as the natural reproduction of dependence through social circuits.","refs":["MC_QGOH_L1_01:GA-03","MC_QGOH_L1_01:GA-06","MC_QGOH_L1_01:GA-09","CL_QGOH_01:GA-05","CL_QGOH_03:GA-04","CL_QGOH_04:GA-08"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"From","passages":[]},{"id":"MC_QGOH_L1_01:XD-03","text":"The critique of finalism and the genesis of value from appetite entail that normativity is a cartography of compositions that increase or diminish power, not a law placed above desire.","refs":["MC_QGOH_L1_01:GA-07","MC_QGOH_L1_01:GA-08","MC_QGOH_L1_01:GA-14","CL_QGOH_01:DBN-05","CL_QGOH_03:GNX-07","CL_QGOH_04:GA-10"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"From","passages":[]},{"id":"MC_QGOH_L1_01:XD-04","text":"The relational individual, indefinite conatus, and reflection through others constitute a metastable subject, whose identity persists only through continuous corporeal and social reconfiguration.","refs":["MC_QGOH_L1_01:GA-04","MC_QGOH_L1_01:GA-07","MC_QGOH_L1_01:GA-09","MC_QGOH_L1_01:GA-12","CL_QGOH_02:GA-05","CL_QGOH_03:GA-07","CL_QGOH_04:BSD-01"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"From","passages":[]},{"id":"MC_QGOH_L1_01:XD-05","text":"When contingency is transferred to ignorance and the imagined future orders affect in the present, final causality becomes affective retrojection within the order of efficient causes.","refs":["MC_QGOH_L1_01:GA-03","MC_QGOH_L1_01:GA-05","MC_QGOH_L1_01:GA-12","CL_QGOH_01:RMD-08","CL_QGOH_02:BSD-05","CL_QGOH_04:BSD-05"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"From","passages":[]},{"id":"MC_QGOH_L1_01:XD-06","text":"Adequate freedom has relational power: common properties and Generosity can compose actions, although the stable political form of this composition is not yet deduced.","refs":["MC_QGOH_L1_01:GA-06","MC_QGOH_L1_01:GA-10","MC_QGOH_L1_01:GA-13","MC_QGOH_L1_01:GA-14","CL_QGOH_03:RLE-03","CL_QGOH_04:GA-13","CL_QGOH_04:BSD-07"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"From","passages":[]},{"id":"MC_QGOH_L1_01:XD-07","text":"Novelty of state does not require an order of possibility outside actuality: within the necessary order it emerges from a change in the composition of body, relations, and affective direction.","refs":["MC_QGOH_L1_01:GA-01","MC_QGOH_L1_01:GA-04","MC_QGOH_L1_01:GA-12","CL_QGOH_01:GA-11","CL_QGOH_04:AS-06","CL_QGOH_04:MP-04"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"From","passages":[]},{"id":"MC_QGOH_L1_01:XD-08","text":"Imagination is the ambiguous seam of the whole system: it sustains superstition and fictive causality, but also preserves conatus and provides the material that adequate cognition reorders.","refs":["MC_QGOH_L1_01:GA-05","MC_QGOH_L1_01:GA-06","MC_QGOH_L1_01:GA-07","CL_QGOH_02:BAS-06","CL_QGOH_03:XD-06","CL_QGOH_04:RMD-04"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"From","passages":[]}],"original_name":"Cross-Unit Derivative Propositions"},{"number":"9","name":"Book-Scale Super-Derivatives","items":[{"id":"MC_QGOH_L1_01:BSD-01","text":"Ontology, epistemology, and ethics become three heights of one causal integration: reality is expression, truth is adequacy of expression, and action is its sufficient causality.","refs":["MC_QGOH_L1_01:GA-01","MC_QGOH_L1_01:GA-03","MC_QGOH_L1_01:GA-06","MC_QGOH_L1_01:GA-10","CL_QGOH_01:BSD-01","CL_QGOH_03:BSD-01"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"From","passages":[]},{"id":"MC_QGOH_L1_01:BSD-02","text":"The agency of the subject is not a binary property but a variable proportion among external causes, the constitution of the body, ideas of affections, and effects intelligible from one's own nature.","refs":["MC_QGOH_L1_01:GA-04","MC_QGOH_L1_01:GA-06","MC_QGOH_L1_01:GA-07","MC_QGOH_L1_01:GA-09","CL_QGOH_03:BSD-02","CL_QGOH_03:BSD-03","CL_QGOH_04:BSD-01"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"From","passages":[]},{"id":"MC_QGOH_L1_01:BSD-03","text":"Freedom grows through cognition and recomposition of necessity, not through subtraction of causes; external determination is diminished insofar as causal connections are integrated into proper operation.","refs":["MC_QGOH_L1_01:GA-06","MC_QGOH_L1_01:GA-10","MC_QGOH_L1_01:GA-13","CL_QGOH_03:XD-05","CL_QGOH_03:BSD-03"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"From","passages":[]},{"id":"MC_QGOH_L1_01:BSD-04","text":"Social order is an unequal circulation of images, attributions, and variations of power, which simultaneously composes and distinguishes individuals and entangles them in reciprocal circuits.","refs":["MC_QGOH_L1_01:GA-05","MC_QGOH_L1_01:GA-08","MC_QGOH_L1_01:GA-09","CL_QGOH_04:GA-08","CL_QGOH_04:BSD-04"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"From","passages":[]},{"id":"MC_QGOH_L1_01:BSD-05","text":"Time is not a uniform container, but a relation between eternal necessity, the duration of perseverance, and imaginative returns that never reduce the transformed body to an altogether identical state.","refs":["MC_QGOH_L1_01:GA-04","MC_QGOH_L1_01:GA-07","MC_QGOH_L1_01:GA-12","CL_QGOH_02:HSI-04","CL_QGOH_03:BSD-04","CL_QGOH_04:HSI-03"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"From","passages":[]},{"id":"MC_QGOH_L1_01:BSD-06","text":"Effective intervention must change conditions of composition, attributions of cause, and imaginative orders; command of the will without such reconfiguration reproduces the same partial causality.","refs":["MC_QGOH_L1_01:GA-05","MC_QGOH_L1_01:GA-06","MC_QGOH_L1_01:GA-14","CL_QGOH_03:BSD-06","CL_QGOH_04:BSD-06"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"From","passages":[]},{"id":"MC_QGOH_L1_01:BSD-07","text":"The geometrical method performs the naturalization that it affirms: it resolves transcendental and moral terms into definitions, causal connections, variations of power, and recursive circuits.","refs":["MC_QGOH_L1_01:GA-03","MC_QGOH_L1_01:GA-08","MC_QGOH_L1_01:GA-13","CL_QGOH_01:RMD-08","CL_QGOH_03:ALT-B05","CL_QGOH_04:ALT-B05"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"From","passages":[]}],"original_name":"Book-Scale Super-Derivatives"},{"number":"10","name":"Recursive Loops, Escalation Paths, and Conceptual Attractors","items":[{"id":"MC_QGOH_L1_01:RLE-01","text":"The division of being into substance and modes establishes dependence; dependence requires immanence; immanence naturalizes psychology; psychology in turn explains the fiction of exteriority.","refs":["CL_QGOH_01:RLE-01","CL_QGOH_02:GA-12","CL_QGOH_02:RLE-07"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[]},{"id":"MC_QGOH_L1_01:RLE-02","text":"Affection generates image, image directs value and Desire, Desire produces action, action changes body and relations, and the changed body receives new affections.","refs":["CL_QGOH_02:RLE-02","CL_QGOH_03:RLE-01","CL_QGOH_04:AS-05"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[]},{"id":"MC_QGOH_L1_01:RLE-03","text":"Ignorance of causes produces the opinion of freedom and ends; these images conceal causes, confirm fictive authority, and reproduce the initial ignorance.","refs":["CL_QGOH_01:RLE-06","CL_QGOH_02:RLE-01","CL_QGOH_03:RLE-05"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[]},{"id":"MC_QGOH_L1_01:RLE-04","text":"Common properties multiply adequate ideas; adequacy increases action; action produces more powerful compositions, whence rational and social amplification becomes possible.","refs":["CL_QGOH_03:RLE-03","CL_QGOH_04:BSD-07"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[]},{"id":"MC_QGOH_L1_01:RLE-05","text":"Conatus retains images favorable to power, increases Joy and Desire, and through this selection recursively confirms the conditions of its own perseverance.","refs":["CL_QGOH_03:RLE-04","CL_QGOH_04:RMD-01"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[]},{"id":"MC_QGOH_L1_01:RLE-06","text":"The imagined affect of another provokes a response; the response provides a new affective cause; reciprocity thereby escalates into Love, Glory, Hatred, Vengeance, or rivalry.","refs":["CL_QGOH_04:GA-08","CL_QGOH_04:CGD-02"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[]},{"id":"MC_QGOH_L1_01:RLE-07","text":"Affective repetition is never mere identity, because the body is changed through enjoyment and encounters; the same returning image can produce a different or contrary affect.","refs":["CL_QGOH_04:AS-06","CL_QGOH_04:XD-05"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[]},{"id":"MC_QGOH_L1_01:RLE-08","text":"The principal attractor is adequate causality, around which intelligibility, freedom, preservation of power, and Generosity converge without exit from necessity.","refs":["CL_QGOH_03:RLE-07","CL_QGOH_04:GA-14","CL_QGOH_04:BSD-07"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[]}],"original_name":"Recursive Loops, Escalation Paths, and Conceptual Attractors"},{"number":"11","name":"Formal Generative Schema of the Whole Book","items":[{"id":"MC_QGOH_L1_01:FGS-B01","text":"One necessarily existing Nature → no substantial exteriority → all beings as causally dependent modes.","refs":["CL_QGOH_01:GA-01","CL_QGOH_01:GA-08"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[]},{"id":"MC_QGOH_L1_01:FGS-B02","text":"Expression through attributes → the same individual under Thought and Extension → distinct causal series without substantial dualism.","refs":["CL_QGOH_01:GA-09","CL_QGOH_02:GA-03"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[]},{"id":"MC_QGOH_L1_01:FGS-B03","text":"Encounter of bodies → affection involving the relation of affecting and affected → idea of affection → perspectival perception and variation of power.","refs":["CL_QGOH_02:GA-06","CL_QGOH_03:RMD-01"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[]},{"id":"MC_QGOH_L1_01:FGS-B04","text":"Incomplete context of causes → trace and presentification → imaginative association → contingency, end, value, and accidental cause.","refs":["CL_QGOH_02:GA-08","CL_QGOH_02:XD-03"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[]},{"id":"MC_QGOH_L1_01:FGS-B05","text":"Actual essence → conatus → Desire → increase or diminution of power → Joy or Sadness → action or passion according to the quality of the cause.","refs":["CL_QGOH_03:GA-07","CL_QGOH_03:GA-09","CL_QGOH_04:GA-02"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[]},{"id":"MC_QGOH_L1_01:FGS-B06","text":"Image of another's affect → imitation and attribution of cause → reciprocal response → social circuit → reconfiguration of identity and power.","refs":["CL_QGOH_04:GA-04","CL_QGOH_04:GA-08"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[]},{"id":"MC_QGOH_L1_01:FGS-B07","text":"Integration of an idea into the context of causes → more adequate cause → action → active Joy and Desire → Courage and Generosity.","refs":["CL_QGOH_03:GA-03","CL_QGOH_04:GA-12","CL_QGOH_04:BSD-07"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[]},{"id":"MC_QGOH_L1_01:FGS-B08","text":"Metaphysics of immanence → constitution of the corporeal Mind → genesis of error and affect → explanation of transcendent projection → return to immanence in the form of causal freedom.","refs":["CL_QGOH_02:GA-12","CL_QGOH_02:RLE-07"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[]}],"original_name":"Formal Generative Schema of the Whole Book"},{"number":"12","name":"Whole-Book Argument Flow Map","items":[{"id":"MC_QGOH_L1_01:AFM-B01","text":"Substance its own cause → immanent causality → finite modes → corporeal individual → Mind as idea of the Body → affection → imagination → conatus → affects → sociality.","refs":["CL_QGOH_01:BAS-04","CL_QGOH_02:GA-04","CL_QGOH_03:GA-07","CL_QGOH_04:GA-08"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[]},{"id":"MC_QGOH_L1_01:AFM-B02","text":"Ignorance of causes → imagined contingency and end → projected utility and value → authority of the transcendent → further concealment of causality.","refs":["CL_QGOH_01:RMD-09","CL_QGOH_02:XD-03","CL_QGOH_02:XD-09"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[]},{"id":"MC_QGOH_L1_01:AFM-B03","text":"Affection → presentification → imitation → reciprocity → rivalry or composition → change of Body → new affective circuit.","refs":["CL_QGOH_04:RMD-03","CL_QGOH_04:RMD-05","CL_QGOH_04:RMD-08"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[]},{"id":"MC_QGOH_L1_01:AFM-B04","text":"Causal cognition → increased adequacy → diminished passion → action from one's own nature → Courage and Generosity → freedom within necessity.","refs":["CL_QGOH_03:AS-07","CL_QGOH_04:GA-13","CL_QGOH_04:BSD-07"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[]}],"original_name":"Whole-Book Argument Flow Map"},{"number":"13","name":"Macro Map-Effects","items":[{"id":"MC_QGOH_L1_01:MME-01","text":"The opposition of God and world collapses; God is not an artisan alongside things, but immanent causality whose modes constitute the whole field of reality.","refs":["CL_QGOH_01:GNX-03","CL_QGOH_02:MME-01"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[]},{"id":"MC_QGOH_L1_01:MME-02","text":"The opposition of Mind and Body is reordered into the double expression of one individual, whence the closed mental subject yields place to a corporeal-relational process.","refs":["CL_QGOH_02:GNX-03","CL_QGOH_03:MME-03","CL_QGOH_04:MME-05"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[]},{"id":"MC_QGOH_L1_01:MME-03","text":"The field of cognition and the field of agency coalesce into one scale of full or partial causality.","refs":["CL_QGOH_03:XD-01","CL_QGOH_03:MME-01"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[]},{"id":"MC_QGOH_L1_01:MME-04","text":"Value is reclassified from a property of the object into an index of desire, composition of the body, and change of power.","refs":["CL_QGOH_03:MME-06","CL_QGOH_04:MME-01","CL_QGOH_04:MME-04"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[]},{"id":"MC_QGOH_L1_01:MME-05","text":"Sociality is reclassified from an aggregation of preconstituted subjects into a circulation of images, attributions of causes, and reciprocal affects.","refs":["CL_QGOH_04:BSD-04","CL_QGOH_04:MME-03"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[]},{"id":"MC_QGOH_L1_01:MME-06","text":"Time is reclassified from homogeneous succession into the coordination of eternity, duration, memory, expectation, and irreversible change of the body.","refs":["CL_QGOH_03:BSD-04","CL_QGOH_04:MME-02","CL_QGOH_04:HSI-03"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[]},{"id":"MC_QGOH_L1_01:MME-07","text":"The method of intervention passes from command, judgment, and catalogue to genetic analysis of compositions, causal contexts, and circuits of amplification.","refs":["CL_QGOH_02:BSD-08","CL_QGOH_04:BSD-06","CL_QGOH_04:MME-06"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[]}],"original_name":"Macro Map-Effects"},{"number":"14","name":"Altitude Integration","items":[{"id":"MC_QGOH_L1_01:ALT-B01","text":"At the level of surface, God, Nature, Mind, Body, cognition, will, Desire, Joy, Sadness, and social relations operate.","refs":["CL_QGOH_01:ALT-B01","CL_QGOH_03:ALT-B01","CL_QGOH_04:ALT-B01"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[]},{"id":"MC_QGOH_L1_01:ALT-B02","text":"At the structural level, all these objects are distributed through immanent causality, relational individuation, affections, conatus, adequacy, and affective circuits.","refs":["CL_QGOH_01:ALT-B02","CL_QGOH_02:ALT-B02","CL_QGOH_03:ALT-B02","CL_QGOH_04:ALT-B02"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[]},{"id":"MC_QGOH_L1_01:ALT-B03","text":"At the metastrategic level, the question is transferred from choice, end, and judgment to generation, attribution of cause, composition of power, and conditions of intervention.","refs":["CL_QGOH_01:ALT-B03","CL_QGOH_02:ALT-B03","CL_QGOH_04:ALT-B03"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[]},{"id":"MC_QGOH_L1_01:ALT-B04","text":"At the ontological and temporal level, reality is one necessary production, singular things are persisting relations, and time is the nexus of eternity with duration and presentification.","refs":["CL_QGOH_01:ALT-B04","CL_QGOH_02:ALT-B04","CL_QGOH_04:ALT-B04"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[]},{"id":"MC_QGOH_L1_01:ALT-B05","text":"At the methodological and performative level, the system effectuates the very naturalization it asserts through negations of exteriority, genetic deductions, and causal redefinitions.","refs":["CL_QGOH_01:ALT-B05","CL_QGOH_03:ALT-B05","CL_QGOH_04:ALT-B05"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[]}],"original_name":"Altitude Integration"},{"number":"15","name":"Internal Tensions, Mutation Points, and Fault Lines","items":[{"id":"MC_QGOH_L1_01:FL-01","text":"Attributes are conceived through themselves, but their distinction remains to be positively coordinated with the numerical unity of substance; the exclusion of plurality does not fully replace the connection.","refs":["CL_QGOH_01:GA-03","CL_QGOH_01:FL-01"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"Tension","passages":[]},{"id":"MC_QGOH_L1_01:FL-02","text":"Divine causality determines all modes, while finite things are immediately produced by finite things; the mediation between eternal productivity and singular series remains compressed.","refs":["CL_QGOH_01:FL-02","CL_QGOH_01:FL-03"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"Tension","passages":[]},{"id":"MC_QGOH_L1_01:FL-03","text":"Parallelism preserves the identity of the process of Mind and Body without causal interaction, but does not explain the positive mode of this congruence beyond double expression.","refs":["CL_QGOH_02:FL-02","CL_QGOH_03:FL-02"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"Tension","passages":[]},{"id":"MC_QGOH_L1_01:FL-04","text":"Falsity is called a privation of cognition, although error is positively produced by corporeal and social mechanisms; privation explains the normative form, genesis the causality of the thing, but their connection remains delicate.","refs":["CL_QGOH_02:BAS-07","CL_QGOH_03:GA-04","CL_QGOH_02:BSD-03"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"Tension","passages":[]},{"id":"MC_QGOH_L1_01:FL-05","text":"The necessity of all ideas must be composed with the normative distinction between adequacy and inadequacy without an external tribunal.","refs":["CL_QGOH_02:FL-05","CL_QGOH_03:XD-08"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"Tension","passages":[]},{"id":"MC_QGOH_L1_01:FL-06","text":"Ethical and political direction rests on adequate cognition, but the transition from understood necessity to the stable transformation of affects and collectives remains incomplete.","refs":["CL_QGOH_03:FL-03","CL_QGOH_04:FL-05"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"Tension","passages":[]},{"id":"MC_QGOH_L1_01:MP-01","text":"Infinity changes from the exclusion of a limit into productive power whose effects are necessarily actualized.","refs":["CL_QGOH_01:RMD-02"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"Tension","passages":[]},{"id":"MC_QGOH_L1_01:MP-02","text":"The Body changes from the object of the Mind into a relational individual, then into variable power that orders cognition, affect, and value.","refs":["CL_QGOH_02:MP-02","CL_QGOH_03:MP-04"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"Tension","passages":[]},{"id":"MC_QGOH_L1_01:MP-03","text":"Imagination changes from general prejudice into a neutral mechanism, then into a conative and social operator that sustains both capture and preservation.","refs":["CL_QGOH_02:BAS-06","CL_QGOH_02:MP-04","CL_QGOH_04:RMD-03"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"Tension","passages":[]},{"id":"MC_QGOH_L1_01:MP-04","text":"Adequacy changes from a quality of truth into active causality, while inadequacy passes from epistemic partiality into passion and social dependence.","refs":["CL_QGOH_03:GA-03","CL_QGOH_03:MP-02","CL_QGOH_04:MP-05"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"Tension","passages":[]},{"id":"MC_QGOH_L1_01:MP-05","text":"Freedom changes from an indeterminate faculty into the necessity of one's own nature and finally into a variable degree of adequate causality.","refs":["CL_QGOH_01:RMD-05","CL_QGOH_03:MP-05"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"Tension","passages":[]},{"id":"MC_QGOH_L1_01:MP-06","text":"Perfection and value change from external conformity into the power of the singular, then into an affective relation whose object itself loses axiological stability.","refs":["CL_QGOH_02:GA-09","CL_QGOH_03:MME-06","CL_QGOH_04:RMD-09"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"Tension","passages":[]}],"original_name":"Internal Tensions, Mutation Points, and Fault Lines"},{"number":"16","name":"Hidden System Image","items":[{"id":"MC_QGOH_L1_01:HSI-01","text":"Reality is an immanent machine of self-expression, a network of causes and bodies in which effects do not leave Nature but locally redistribute its power.","refs":["CL_QGOH_01:HSI-01","CL_QGOH_02:HSI-01","CL_QGOH_04:HSI-01"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[]},{"id":"MC_QGOH_L1_01:HSI-02","text":"The subject is a corporeal and mental vortex persisting through conatus, permeable to external causes and images of others, whose autonomy varies according to adequacy.","refs":["CL_QGOH_02:HSI-02","CL_QGOH_03:HSI-02","CL_QGOH_04:HSI-02"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[]},{"id":"MC_QGOH_L1_01:HSI-03","text":"Time is a stratified machine in which eternal necessity makes singular things endure, memory brings back absences, and change of the body prevents the identity of return.","refs":["CL_QGOH_03:HSI-03","CL_QGOH_04:HSI-03"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[]},{"id":"MC_QGOH_L1_01:HSI-04","text":"Capture is the circuit by which ignorance, utility, the image of a cause, and reciprocal affects bind the individual to external causes while producing the appearance of freedom.","refs":["CL_QGOH_02:HSI-05","CL_QGOH_03:RLE-05","CL_QGOH_04:BSD-03"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[]},{"id":"MC_QGOH_L1_01:HSI-05","text":"Possible navigation is not escape from the machine, but passage through it from partially understood affections to adequate causal compositions, whose social form is Generosity.","refs":["CL_QGOH_01:HSI-05","CL_QGOH_03:HSI-04","CL_QGOH_04:HSI-04"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[]}],"original_name":"Hidden System Image"},{"number":"17","name":"Minimal Whole-Book Reconstruction Test","items":[{"id":"MC_QGOH_L1_01:MRT-B01","text":"The axioms of immanence, corporeal individuation, imagination, adequacy, conatus, and affective recursion suffice to reconstruct the machine of this metagregis.","refs":["CL_QGOH_01:MRT-B01","CL_QGOH_02:MRT-B01","CL_QGOH_03:MRT-B01","CL_QGOH_04:MRT-B01"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[]},{"id":"MC_QGOH_L1_01:MRT-B02","text":"The firmest structure is the continuation by which immanent causality produces affection, affection varies cognition and power, and variation is distributed into actions and social circuits.","refs":["CL_QGOH_01:MRT-B02","CL_QGOH_02:MRT-B02","CL_QGOH_04:MRT-B03"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[]},{"id":"MC_QGOH_L1_01:MRT-B03","text":"The conversion of adequate cognition into stable moderation of affects, social composition, and blessedness remains less determined.","refs":["CL_QGOH_02:MRT-B03","CL_QGOH_03:MRT-B03","CL_QGOH_04:MRT-B04"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[]},{"id":"MC_QGOH_L1_01:MRT-B04","text":"The system coheres at this magnitude only if order is read not as a series of doctrines, but as a circuit from metaphysics to body, from body to imagination, and from imagination to the critique of metaphysics.","refs":["CL_QGOH_02:MRT-B07","CL_QGOH_03:MRT-B05"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[]},{"id":"MC_QGOH_L1_01:MRT-B05","text":"The reconstruction preserves the generative movement, but the plurality of singular affects, differences of bodies, and the terminal form of active life still resist axiomatic compression.","refs":["CL_QGOH_03:MRT-B05","CL_QGOH_04:FL-04","CL_QGOH_04:MRT-B04"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[]}],"original_name":"Minimal Whole-Book Reconstruction Test"}],"revision":"MC_QGOH_L1_01@R001","source_variant_id":"canonical:MC_QGOH_L1_01:v1","source_language_code":"la","original_title":"Qgoh L1 01"}],"identity":{"layer":"MC","kicker":"Meta-cluster · Ethica","title":"Qgoh L1 01","context":"Chapters 01–12 · Part I - Concerning God · passage 1 · Part I - Concerning God · passage 2 · Part I - Concerning God · passage 3 · Part I - Concerning God · passage 4 · Part II - On the Nature and Origin of the Mind · passage 1 · Part II - On the Nature and Origin of the Mind · passage 2 · Part II - On the Nature and Origin of the Mind · passage 3 · Part II - On the Nature and Origin of the Mind · passage 4 · Part III - On the Origin and Nature of the Emotions · passage 1 · Part III - On the Origin and Nature of the Emotions · passage 2 · Part III - On the Origin and Nature of the Emotions · passage 3 · Part III - On the Origin and Nature of the Emotions · passage 4","original_title":"Qgoh L1 01","original_context":"Chapters 01–12 · Pars prima - De Deo · passage 1 · Pars prima - De Deo · passage 2 · Pars prima - De Deo · passage 3 · Pars prima - De Deo · passage 4 · Pars secunda - De natura et origine mentis · passage 1 · Pars secunda - De natura et origine mentis · passage 2 · Pars secunda - De natura et origine mentis · passage 3 · Pars secunda - De natura et origine mentis · passage 4 · Pars tertia - De origine et natura affectuum · passage 1 · Pars tertia - De origine et natura affectuum · passage 2 · Pars tertia - De origine et natura affectuum · passage 3 · Pars tertia - De origine et natura affectuum · passage 4"},"original_title":"Qgoh L1 01","title_language_code":"en"},{"id":"MC_QGOH_L1_02","layer":"MC","title":"Qgoh L1 02","order":2,"parents":["CL_QGOH_05","CL_QGOH_06","CL_QGOH_07"],"revision":"MC_QGOH_L1_02@R001","sha256":"5750f3da2da410966e0c557330decf54635b7405cf3c028f6357868076961c43","sections":[{"number":"1","name":"Book-Scale Argumental Spine","items":[{"id":"MC_QGOH_L1_02:AS-01","text":"Systema quaerit quomodo homo finitus, ex affectibus et causis externis compositus, libertatem communem et intellectualem acquirere possit sine imperio voluntatis aut exemptione a Natura.","refs":["CL_QGOH_05:BAS-01","CL_QGOH_06:AS-B01","CL_QGOH_07:AS-B01"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[]},{"id":"MC_QGOH_L1_02:AS-02","text":"Thesis globalis libertatem constituit ut amplificationem graduatam causalitatis propriae, quae servitutem affectivam per compositionem corporalem, civilem et cognitivam in beatitudinem activam transformat.","refs":["CL_QGOH_05:GA-05","CL_QGOH_06:GA-08","CL_QGOH_07:GA-10"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[]},{"id":"MC_QGOH_L1_02:AS-03","text":"Argumentatio est apophatica et reconstructiva: falsas autonomiae, moralitatis et salutis formas excludit, deinde terminos negatos intra causalitatem immanentem denuo constituit.","refs":["CL_QGOH_05:BAS-03","CL_QGOH_07:AS-B03"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[]},{"id":"MC_QGOH_L1_02:AS-04","text":"Motus procedit a reductione affectuum ad servitutem, a servitute ad compositionem socialem potentiarum, atque ab hac compositione ad cognitionem intuitivam et Amorem intellectualem.","refs":["CL_QGOH_05:BAS-04","CL_QGOH_06:XD-03","CL_QGOH_07:AS-B04"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[]},{"id":"MC_QGOH_L1_02:AS-05","text":"Progressio est cumulativa, recursiva, amplificativa et spiralis, quia quisque gradus causam efficaciae gradus sequentis producit et terminus ad operationem priorum retrovertitur.","refs":["CL_QGOH_05:BAS-05","CL_QGOH_06:AS-B06","CL_QGOH_07:AS-B05"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[]},{"id":"MC_QGOH_L1_02:AS-06","text":"Clausura non liberationem absolutam sed duplicem stabilitatem praebet: acquiescentiam finitam intra eventus necessarios et beatitudinem quae, ex fine apparente, in causam permanentem activitatis convertitur.","refs":["CL_QGOH_05:BAS-06","CL_QGOH_06:AS-B07","CL_QGOH_07:AS-B08"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[]}],"original_name":"Book-Scale Argumental Spine"},{"number":"2","name":"Global Load-Bearing Axioms","items":[{"id":"MC_QGOH_L1_02:GA-01","text":"[Persistent] Natura ex necessitate immanenti agit; libertas igitur non causalitatem tollit, sed modum quo necessitas per rem finitam explicatur mutat.","refs":["CL_QGOH_05:GA-13","CL_QGOH_07:GA-04"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[]},{"id":"MC_QGOH_L1_02:GA-02","text":"[Persistent] Passio est determinatio corporalis et ideationalis cuius causa a subiecto tantum partialiter explicatur; actio crescit prout idea causae adaequatur.","refs":["CL_QGOH_05:GA-02","CL_QGOH_07:GA-02"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[]},{"id":"MC_QGOH_L1_02:GA-03","text":"[Mutating] Conatus transit ab essentia perseverandi ad virtutem rationalem, compositionem communem et actionem intellectualem beatificam, identitate directionis servata.","refs":["CL_QGOH_05:GA-09","CL_QGOH_06:GA-01","CL_QGOH_07:RMD-08"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[]},{"id":"MC_QGOH_L1_02:GA-04","text":"[Mutating] Libertas est gradus causalitatis adaequatae qui primum affectus ordinat, deinde condiciones corporales et civiles componit, postremo in stabilitatem intellectualem extenditur.","refs":["CL_QGOH_05:BSD-07","CL_QGOH_06:GA-08","CL_QGOH_07:GA-03"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[]},{"id":"MC_QGOH_L1_02:GA-05","text":"[Persistent] Cognitio efficax repraesentatio iners esse nequit: veritas tantum potentiam auget ubi affectum, corpus, institutionem aut ordinem imaginum causaliter immutat.","refs":["CL_QGOH_05:GA-06","CL_QGOH_06:DBN-01","CL_QGOH_07:DBN-05"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[]},{"id":"MC_QGOH_L1_02:GA-06","text":"[Emergent] Finitas, quae causis externis servitutem exponit, eadem dependentia est ex qua compositio corporum, mutuum auxilium et potentia communis generantur.","refs":["CL_QGOH_05:GA-10","CL_QGOH_06:XD-01"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[]},{"id":"MC_QGOH_L1_02:GA-07","text":"[Mutating] Corpus ex sede affectionum in infrastructuram materialem libertatis et tandem in dispositionem positivam ad ampliorem actionem Mentis mutatur.","refs":["CL_QGOH_05:GNX-04","CL_QGOH_06:GA-09","CL_QGOH_07:GA-06"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[]},{"id":"MC_QGOH_L1_02:GA-08","text":"[Mutating] Ratio vim obtinet tantum cum ex norma intelligibili in affectum contrarium, cupiditatem activam, institutionem stabilem et transitum ad intuitionem convertitur.","refs":["CL_QGOH_05:GA-07","CL_QGOH_06:GA-03","CL_QGOH_07:GA-07"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[]},{"id":"MC_QGOH_L1_02:GA-09","text":"[Mutating] Socialitas ab imitatione conflictiva ad Civitatem coercitivam et inde ad infrastructuram cooperationis rationalis progreditur, origine passionali numquam omnino deleta.","refs":["CL_QGOH_05:GA-11","CL_QGOH_06:GA-06"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[]},{"id":"MC_QGOH_L1_02:GA-10","text":"[Emergent] Bonum, malum, iustum et iniustum sunt instrumenta comparativa potentiae finitae; in libertate perfecta functionem terminaliter amittunt.","refs":["CL_QGOH_05:GA-08","CL_QGOH_06:GA-07","CL_QGOH_06:GA-11"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[]},{"id":"MC_QGOH_L1_02:GA-11","text":"[Mutating] Necessitas ex signo dominationis externae in obiectum intellectus et formam ontologicam libertatis sapientis invertitur.","refs":["CL_QGOH_06:MP-03","CL_QGOH_07:RMD-06"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[]},{"id":"MC_QGOH_L1_02:GA-12","text":"[Attractor] Intellegere, cognitio Dei, acquiescentia, intuitio, Amor intellectualis et beatitudo constituunt unum attractorem ascensionalem potentiae non-rivalis.","refs":["CL_QGOH_05:GA-12","CL_QGOH_06:GA-10","CL_QGOH_07:GA-10"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[]},{"id":"MC_QGOH_L1_02:GA-13","text":"[Emergent] Tempus affectivum bifurcatur inter durationem imaginativam, quae praesentiam et intensitatem distribuit, et aeternitatem intelligibilem, quae successionem et memoriam non continuat.","refs":["CL_QGOH_05:BSD-04","CL_QGOH_06:HSI-03","CL_QGOH_07:GA-09"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[]},{"id":"MC_QGOH_L1_02:GA-14","text":"[Terminal] Sapientis beatitudo non est praemium virtutis sed exercitium eius, quo positio terminalis in causam conservandae libertatis convertitur.","refs":["CL_QGOH_07:GA-11","CL_QGOH_07:GA-12","CL_QGOH_07:AS-B08"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[]}],"original_name":"Global Load-Bearing Axioms"},{"number":"3","name":"Recurrence, Mutation, and Displacement Matrix","items":[{"id":"MC_QGOH_L1_02:RMD-01","text":"Lætitia et Tristitia identitatem directionalem servant, sed ex indicibus mutationis affectivae in criterios ethicos et tandem in differentiam inter laetitiam activam et fluctuationem passivam amplificantur.","refs":["CL_QGOH_05:RMD-01","CL_QGOH_07:DBN-05"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[]},{"id":"MC_QGOH_L1_02:RMD-02","text":"Cupiditas ex affectu primitivo in essentiam conatus, cupiditatem rationalem et genealogiam actionis transit; obiectum eius causalitati originis subordinatur.","refs":["CL_QGOH_05:RMD-02","CL_QGOH_06:GA-03","CL_QGOH_07:XD-08"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[]},{"id":"MC_QGOH_L1_02:RMD-03","text":"Veritas functionem mutat: ex auctoritate polemica fit vis affective condicionata, deinde technica corporalis et intellectualis transformationis.","refs":["CL_QGOH_05:RMD-07","CL_QGOH_06:XD-02","CL_QGOH_07:BSD-03"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[]},{"id":"MC_QGOH_L1_02:RMD-04","text":"Corpus ex correlato passionis in infrastructuram nutritionis et laboris, deinde in instrumentum ampliandae potentiae Mentis migrat.","refs":["CL_QGOH_06:RMD-05","CL_QGOH_07:RMD-05"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[]},{"id":"MC_QGOH_L1_02:RMD-05","text":"Socialitas redit functione mutata: imaginatio alterius conflictum parit, Civitas eum coercet, cooperatio rationalis dependentiam in amplificationem convertit.","refs":["CL_QGOH_05:RMD-05","CL_QGOH_06:RMD-03"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[]},{"id":"MC_QGOH_L1_02:RMD-06","text":"Libertas ab exclusione decreti indeterminati ad ecologiam materialiter sustentatam et tandem ad mutationem proportionis inter actionem et passionem transit.","refs":["CL_QGOH_05:XD-07","CL_QGOH_06:MP-02","CL_QGOH_07:RMD-07"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[]},{"id":"MC_QGOH_L1_02:RMD-07","text":"Necessitas, initio negatio liberi decreti, in condicionem acquiescentiae et postremo in formam libertatis intellectualis absorbetur.","refs":["CL_QGOH_05:RMD-09","CL_QGOH_06:MP-03","CL_QGOH_07:RMD-06"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[]},{"id":"MC_QGOH_L1_02:RMD-08","text":"Vocabula moralia ab institutione affectiva ad comparationem rationalem migrant et, libertate perfecta approximata, opere navigationis peracto recedunt.","refs":["CL_QGOH_05:RMD-06","CL_QGOH_06:RMD-04"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[]},{"id":"MC_QGOH_L1_02:RMD-09","text":"Dei cognitio ab attractore intellectionis ad acquiescentiam et Amorem intellectualem progreditur, dum amor reciprocus et anthropomorphicus invertitur in actionem reflexivam.","refs":["CL_QGOH_05:XD-09","CL_QGOH_07:GA-08","CL_QGOH_07:RMD-03"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[]},{"id":"MC_QGOH_L1_02:RMD-10","text":"Imaginatio res absentes affective praesentat et rationem praeparat, sed in aeternitate memorativa operatione sua exhausta terminaliter evanescit.","refs":["CL_QGOH_05:RMD-04","CL_QGOH_07:RMD-04"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[]}],"original_name":"Recurrence, Mutation, and Displacement Matrix"},{"number":"4","name":"Lower-Layer vs Current-Level Distinction","items":[{"id":"MC_QGOH_L1_02:CGD-01","text":"Taxonomiae affectuum et casus actuum manent machinae locales; hoc gradu servantur tantum ut probationes legis qua causa, intensitas et totum potentiae valorem constituunt.","refs":["CL_QGOH_05:CGD-01","CL_QGOH_06:CGD-03"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[]},{"id":"MC_QGOH_L1_02:CGD-02","text":"Differentiae temporales et concursus imaginum sursum amplificantur in doctrinam generalem de distributione praesentiae affectivae, sed nondum ipsae aeternitatem intelligibilem constituunt.","refs":["CL_QGOH_05:CGD-03","CL_QGOH_07:CGD-01"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[]},{"id":"MC_QGOH_L1_02:CGD-03","text":"Aestimatio actuum singulorum absorbetur in genealogiam causae: eadem species exterior potest passionem, actionem rationalem aut virtutem exprimere.","refs":["CL_QGOH_06:CGD-03","CL_QGOH_07:XD-08"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[]},{"id":"MC_QGOH_L1_02:CGD-04","text":"Trias affectuum, doctrina affectus fortioris et relatio affectionum ad Deum unitates inferiores coniungunt, quia mutationem potentiae in transitum cognitivum convertunt.","refs":["CL_QGOH_05:CGD-05","CL_QGOH_06:CGD-04","CL_QGOH_07:CGD-04"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[]},{"id":"MC_QGOH_L1_02:CGD-05","text":"Transitus praesentis gradus fit ubi cognitio desinit esse mera veritas et fit affectus activus, dispositio corporalis atque causa laetitiae intellectualis.","refs":["CL_QGOH_05:CGD-06","CL_QGOH_06:XD-02","CL_QGOH_07:DBN-05"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[]},{"id":"MC_QGOH_L1_02:CGD-06","text":"Civitas transitum maiorem efficit cum potestas coercitiva in iura, cooperationem materialem et causalitatem propriam civium convertitur.","refs":["CL_QGOH_06:CGD-05","CL_QGOH_06:XD-03"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[]},{"id":"MC_QGOH_L1_02:CGD-07","text":"Distinctio durationis et aeternitatis unitates superiores coniungit, dum aptitudo Corporis fit commissura inter praxim temporalem et cognitionem non successivam.","refs":["CL_QGOH_07:CGD-05","CL_QGOH_07:CGD-08"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[]},{"id":"MC_QGOH_L1_02:CGD-08","text":"Moralitas tristitiae, inventarium affectuum et refutatio voluntatis fiunt secundaria, quia eorum opera negativa in machinam positivam cooperationis et beatitudinis absorbetur.","refs":["CL_QGOH_06:CGD-06","CL_QGOH_07:CGD-02","CL_QGOH_05:RMD-10"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[]}],"original_name":"Lower-Layer vs Current-Level Distinction"},{"number":"5","name":"Global Apophatic Structure","items":[{"id":"MC_QGOH_L1_02:GNX-01","text":"Libertas neque decretum indeterminatum neque imperium voluntatis est; nulla actio humana nexum causarum naturalium interrumpit.","refs":["CL_QGOH_05:GNX-01","CL_QGOH_06:GNX-03","CL_QGOH_07:GNX-01"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"Negative","passages":[]},{"id":"MC_QGOH_L1_02:GNX-02","text":"Natura nec fines externos nec ordinem moralem transcendentem nec Deum voluntate mutabilem admittit.","refs":["CL_QGOH_05:GNX-02","CL_QGOH_06:DBN-04","CL_QGOH_07:BSA-02"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"Negative","passages":[]},{"id":"MC_QGOH_L1_02:GNX-03","text":"Cognitio vera, si affectum et ordinem Corporis non mutat, passionem sola contradictione logica superare nequit.","refs":["CL_QGOH_05:GNX-05","CL_QGOH_06:DBN-01","CL_QGOH_07:DBN-05"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"Negative","passages":[]},{"id":"MC_QGOH_L1_02:GNX-04","text":"Individuum sibi sufficiens excluditur, quia corpus, res externae, alii homines et Deus intra ipsam constitutionem potentiae agunt.","refs":["CL_QGOH_05:GNX-06","CL_QGOH_06:DBN-02"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"Negative","passages":[]},{"id":"MC_QGOH_L1_02:GNX-05","text":"Utilitas propria et commune bonum non sunt contraria, nisi conatus sub imaginatione competitiva et causalitate inadaequata consideretur.","refs":["CL_QGOH_06:GNX-01","CL_QGOH_06:MME-01"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"Negative","passages":[]},{"id":"MC_QGOH_L1_02:GNX-06","text":"Civitas et libertas non sunt oppositiones absolutae; discrimen verum est inter administrationem metus et productionem potentiae communis.","refs":["CL_QGOH_06:GNX-04","CL_QGOH_06:MME-02"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"Negative","passages":[]},{"id":"MC_QGOH_L1_02:GNX-07","text":"Corpus non est adversarius Mentis aut aeternitatis, sed condicio sine qua affectus ordinari et intellectus amplificari nequeunt.","refs":["CL_QGOH_05:GNX-04","CL_QGOH_07:GNX-08"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"Negative","passages":[]},{"id":"MC_QGOH_L1_02:GNX-08","text":"Aeternitas non est continuatio memoriae personalis, neque virtus ex spe praemii, metu poenae aut iudicio post mortem pendet.","refs":["CL_QGOH_07:GNX-05","CL_QGOH_07:GNX-06","CL_QGOH_07:BSA-04"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"Negative","passages":[]}],"original_name":"Global Apophatic Structure"},{"number":"6","name":"Deep Book Apophatics","items":[{"id":"MC_QGOH_L1_02:DBN-01","text":"Falsum esse oportet Naturam a legislatore finali vel Deo personali ordinari, quia omnis norma valida ex relationibus immanentibus potentiae generatur.","refs":["CL_QGOH_06:DBN-04","CL_QGOH_07:BSA-02"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"Negative","passages":[]},{"id":"MC_QGOH_L1_02:DBN-02","text":"Falsum esse oportet mentem abstractam sine affectu et corpore veritatem inertem in imperium practicum convertere posse.","refs":["CL_QGOH_05:DBN-02","CL_QGOH_06:DBN-01","CL_QGOH_07:DBN-05"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"Negative","passages":[]},{"id":"MC_QGOH_L1_02:DBN-03","text":"Falsum esse oportet subiectum esse substantiam clausam aut extra Deum subsistere, quia libertas ex relationibus adaequate compositis nascitur.","refs":["CL_QGOH_05:DBN-03","CL_QGOH_06:DBN-02","CL_QGOH_07:BSA-01"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"Negative","passages":[]},{"id":"MC_QGOH_L1_02:DBN-04","text":"Falsum esse oportet speciem externam actus valorem eius continere; passio et actio discernuntur ex modo causationis, non ex similitudine operis.","refs":["CL_QGOH_06:DBN-03","CL_QGOH_06:ALT-B05"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"Negative","passages":[]},{"id":"MC_QGOH_L1_02:DBN-05","text":"Falsum esse oportet coactionem securitatemque per se cooperationem rationalem producere; communitas actualis mediationes affectivas, iuridicas et materiales postulat.","refs":["CL_QGOH_06:BSA-03","CL_QGOH_06:FL-02"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"Negative","passages":[]},{"id":"MC_QGOH_L1_02:DBN-06","text":"Falsum esse oportet aeternam Mentis identitatem cum historia memorativa personae coincidere aut mensura temporali definiri posse.","refs":["CL_QGOH_07:DBN-03","CL_QGOH_07:BSA-03"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"Negative","passages":[]}],"original_name":"Deep Book Apophatics"},{"number":"7","name":"Super-Apophatics of the Whole","items":[{"id":"MC_QGOH_L1_02:BSA-01","text":"Ontologice inadmissibilis est locus exterior Naturae ex quo subiectum finitum causas totius sine participatione contemplaretur.","refs":["CL_QGOH_05:BSA-01","CL_QGOH_06:BSA-01","CL_QGOH_07:BSA-01"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"Negative","passages":[]},{"id":"MC_QGOH_L1_02:BSA-02","text":"Systema circum absentiam conversionis instantaneae veritatis in potentiam ordinatur; nulla claritas minima per se transmutationem passionis praestat.","refs":["CL_QGOH_05:BSA-02","CL_QGOH_06:DBN-01","CL_QGOH_07:FL-01"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"Negative","passages":[]},{"id":"MC_QGOH_L1_02:BSA-03","text":"Impossibilis est status hominis finiti omni passione, corpore, societate et causa externa absolute liberati.","refs":["CL_QGOH_05:BSA-03","CL_QGOH_06:BSA-02"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"Negative","passages":[]},{"id":"MC_QGOH_L1_02:BSA-04","text":"Absentia iudicis remunerantis et Dei affective reciproci cogit virtutem causam immanentem atque sufficientem sui ipsius fieri.","refs":["CL_QGOH_07:BSA-02","CL_QGOH_07:BSA-04"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"Negative","passages":[]},{"id":"MC_QGOH_L1_02:BSA-05","text":"Systema circum pontem incompletum inter passionem actualem et libertatem perfectam laborat: affectus rationalis, Civitas cooperativa et experientia aeternitatis causaliter nondum plene nectuntur.","refs":["CL_QGOH_05:BSA-04","CL_QGOH_06:MRT-B03","CL_QGOH_07:MRT-B03"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"Tension","passages":[]}],"original_name":"Super-Apophatics of the Whole"},{"number":"8","name":"Cross-Unit Derivative Propositions","items":[{"id":"MC_QGOH_L1_02:XD-01","text":"Libertas est ecologia potentiae, quia asymmetria causarum externarum tantum per relationem corporalis aptitudinis, cooperationis socialis et intelligentiae adaequatae convertitur.","refs":["MC_QGOH_L1_02:GA-04","MC_QGOH_L1_02:GA-06","MC_QGOH_L1_02:GA-07","MC_QGOH_L1_02:GA-09","CL_QGOH_05:GA-05","CL_QGOH_06:GA-08","CL_QGOH_07:GA-06"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"From","passages":[]},{"id":"MC_QGOH_L1_02:XD-02","text":"Veritas vim practicam obtinet cum fit affectus contrarius, cupiditas activa, dispositio institutionalis et laetitia ex cognitione adaequata.","refs":["MC_QGOH_L1_02:GA-05","MC_QGOH_L1_02:GA-08","CL_QGOH_05:XD-03","CL_QGOH_06:XD-02","CL_QGOH_07:DBN-05"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"From","passages":[]},{"id":"MC_QGOH_L1_02:XD-03","text":"Conatus propriae conservationis structuram non-rivalem acquirit, quia auxilium commune et aliena beatitudo potentiam singularis minuere non debent sed augere possunt.","refs":["MC_QGOH_L1_02:GA-03","MC_QGOH_L1_02:GA-06","MC_QGOH_L1_02:GA-12","CL_QGOH_05:GA-09","CL_QGOH_06:XD-01","CL_QGOH_07:XD-06"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"From","passages":[]},{"id":"MC_QGOH_L1_02:XD-04","text":"Corpus est simul terminus partis memorativae pereuntis et medium materiale quo cognitio, libertas et maior participatio actionis intellectualis producuntur.","refs":["MC_QGOH_L1_02:GA-07","MC_QGOH_L1_02:GA-13","CL_QGOH_06:GA-09","CL_QGOH_07:XD-04"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"From","passages":[]},{"id":"MC_QGOH_L1_02:XD-05","text":"Necessitas vim inversivam exercet: eadem causalitas quae libertatem arbitrii negat campum immanentem constituit in quo causalitas propria et libertas sapientis crescunt.","refs":["MC_QGOH_L1_02:GA-01","MC_QGOH_L1_02:GA-04","MC_QGOH_L1_02:GA-11","CL_QGOH_05:BSA-01","CL_QGOH_06:MME-05","CL_QGOH_07:RMD-06"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"From","passages":[]},{"id":"MC_QGOH_L1_02:XD-06","text":"Civitas est convertor inter servitutem et libertatem, non terminus liberationis: vim externam in cooperationem mutat, dum Ratio transitum ad actionem intellectualem aperit.","refs":["MC_QGOH_L1_02:GA-08","MC_QGOH_L1_02:GA-09","CL_QGOH_06:XD-03","CL_QGOH_07:GA-07"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"From","passages":[]},{"id":"MC_QGOH_L1_02:XD-07","text":"Categoriae boni et mali necessariae sunt dum potentia finita navigat, sed attractore beatitudinis approximato recedunt, quia beatitudo iam non merces sed virtus est.","refs":["MC_QGOH_L1_02:GA-10","MC_QGOH_L1_02:GA-14","CL_QGOH_05:GA-08","CL_QGOH_06:XD-05","CL_QGOH_07:GA-11"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"From","passages":[]},{"id":"MC_QGOH_L1_02:XD-08","text":"Tempus ethicorum operum duplicem rationem habet: praesentia imaginativa intensitates distribuit, Ratio utilitates trans durationem ordinat, aeternitas autem successionem omnino suspendit.","refs":["MC_QGOH_L1_02:GA-13","CL_QGOH_05:BSD-04","CL_QGOH_06:HSI-03","CL_QGOH_07:GA-09"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"From","passages":[]},{"id":"MC_QGOH_L1_02:XD-09","text":"Beatitudo totam seriem retroactive ordinat, quia finis apparens in machinam fit quae intellectum auget, passiones minuit et propriam praesentiam conservat.","refs":["MC_QGOH_L1_02:GA-12","MC_QGOH_L1_02:GA-14","CL_QGOH_07:AS-B08","CL_QGOH_07:RLE-07"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"From","passages":[]},{"id":"MC_QGOH_L1_02:XD-10","text":"Subiectum est proportio multiscalaris causalitatum partialium, relationum socialium et idearum adaequatarum, cuius identitas ex potentia agendi potius quam clausura substantialis constat.","refs":["MC_QGOH_L1_02:GA-02","MC_QGOH_L1_02:GA-04","MC_QGOH_L1_02:GA-06","CL_QGOH_05:BSD-02","CL_QGOH_06:BSD-01","CL_QGOH_07:BSD-01"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"From","passages":[]}],"original_name":"Cross-Unit Derivative Propositions"},{"number":"9","name":"Book-Scale Super-Derivatives","items":[{"id":"MC_QGOH_L1_02:BSD-01","text":"Ontologia fit ecologia multiscalaris potentiae, in qua corpora, affectus, institutiones et ideae sunt modi distribuendi causalitatem immanentem.","refs":["MC_QGOH_L1_02:GA-01","MC_QGOH_L1_02:GA-02","MC_QGOH_L1_02:GA-06","CL_QGOH_05:BSD-01","CL_QGOH_06:HSI-01","CL_QGOH_07:HSI-01"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"From","passages":[]},{"id":"MC_QGOH_L1_02:BSD-02","text":"Libertas neque proprietas individui neque status politicus solus est, sed relatio distributa qua corpus, societas et intellectus causam actionis sufficientius exprimunt.","refs":["MC_QGOH_L1_02:GA-04","MC_QGOH_L1_02:GA-07","MC_QGOH_L1_02:GA-09","CL_QGOH_06:GA-08","CL_QGOH_06:HSI-02","CL_QGOH_07:HSI-02"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"From","passages":[]},{"id":"MC_QGOH_L1_02:BSD-03","text":"Epistemologia obtinet statum technologiae causalis, quia cognitio vera affectus, ordinem Corporis et facultatem ulterius cognoscendi simul producit.","refs":["MC_QGOH_L1_02:GA-05","MC_QGOH_L1_02:GA-08","CL_QGOH_05:BSD-03","CL_QGOH_07:BSD-03"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"From","passages":[]},{"id":"MC_QGOH_L1_02:BSD-04","text":"Politica fit distributio condicionum causalitatis propriae, cuius mensura est utrum communicatio boni potentiam singularem et communem simul amplificet.","refs":["MC_QGOH_L1_02:GA-06","MC_QGOH_L1_02:GA-09","MC_QGOH_L1_02:GA-12","CL_QGOH_06:BSD-02","CL_QGOH_07:BSD-06"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"From","passages":[]},{"id":"MC_QGOH_L1_02:BSD-05","text":"Tempus non est unum medium continuum: affectus inhabitant topologiam praesentiae, deliberatio seriem utilitatum, intellectus autem essentiam non durationalem.","refs":["MC_QGOH_L1_02:GA-13","CL_QGOH_05:BSD-04","CL_QGOH_07:GA-09"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"From","passages":[]},{"id":"MC_QGOH_L1_02:BSD-06","text":"Theologia in ontologiam reflexivam convertitur, quia Deus obiectum supremum manet sine persona affective reciproca, iar Amor intellectualis fit explicatio immanentis sui cognitionis.","refs":["MC_QGOH_L1_02:GA-01","MC_QGOH_L1_02:GA-12","CL_QGOH_05:XD-09","CL_QGOH_07:BSD-07"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"From","passages":[]},{"id":"MC_QGOH_L1_02:BSD-07","text":"Ethica est navigatio finitudinis quae ordinat potentiam donec instrumenta comparativa boni et mali in virtute beatifica propriam necessitatem amittant.","refs":["MC_QGOH_L1_02:GA-10","MC_QGOH_L1_02:GA-14","CL_QGOH_05:BSD-05","CL_QGOH_06:XD-05","CL_QGOH_07:GNX-07"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"From","passages":[]}],"original_name":"Book-Scale Super-Derivatives"},{"number":"10","name":"Recursive Loops, Escalation Paths, and Conceptual Attractors","items":[{"id":"MC_QGOH_L1_02:RLE-01","text":"Causa externa passionem auget, potentiam propriam minuit et ignorantiam causae stabilit; ignorantia autem rem externam iterum quasi dominam affectus figit.","refs":["CL_QGOH_05:RLE-02","CL_QGOH_07:RLE-01"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"Composite","passages":[]},{"id":"MC_QGOH_L1_02:RLE-02","text":"Affectus contrarius ordinationem incipit, ordinatio actionem auget, actio claritatem causae firmat, et claritas ulteriorem passivitatem minuit.","refs":["CL_QGOH_05:RLE-05","CL_QGOH_07:RLE-02"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"Composite","passages":[]},{"id":"MC_QGOH_L1_02:RLE-03","text":"Convenientia rationalis potentias componit, cooperatio potentiam singularis auget, et potentia aucta maiorem cooperationem rationalem efficit.","refs":["CL_QGOH_05:RLE-07","CL_QGOH_06:MRT-B02"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"Composite","passages":[]},{"id":"MC_QGOH_L1_02:RLE-04","text":"Conflictus ad coercitionem, coercitio ad securitatem, securitas ad cooperationem tendit; circuitus tamen regreditur si metus fundamentum solum institutionis manet.","refs":["CL_QGOH_06:XD-03","CL_QGOH_06:FL-02"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"Tension","passages":[]},{"id":"MC_QGOH_L1_02:RLE-05","text":"Oeconomia Corporis libertatem cognitionis sustinet, cognitio aptitudinem actionis auget, et Corpus ad plures actiones aptum novam cognitionem recipit.","refs":["CL_QGOH_06:FGS-B05","CL_QGOH_07:RLE-05"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"Composite","passages":[]},{"id":"MC_QGOH_L1_02:RLE-06","text":"Cognitio et libertas circuitum positivum efficiunt: Ratio cupiditatem intuitionis parit, intuitio laetitiam auget, et laetitia ulteriorem intelligentiam sustentat.","refs":["CL_QGOH_07:XD-02","CL_QGOH_07:RLE-04"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"Composite","passages":[]},{"id":"MC_QGOH_L1_02:RLE-07","text":"Communicatio boni non-rivalis Amorem auget, auctus Amor participationem aliorum desiderat, et participatio potentiam communem iterum amplificat.","refs":["CL_QGOH_07:BSD-06","CL_QGOH_07:RLE-06"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"Composite","passages":[]},{"id":"MC_QGOH_L1_02:RLE-08","text":"Beatitudo est attractor terminalis et circuitus sui conservativus: quo magis adest, eo plus Mens intelligit et minus ab affectibus externis regitur.","refs":["CL_QGOH_07:AS-B08","CL_QGOH_07:RLE-07"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"Composite","passages":[]}],"original_name":"Recursive Loops, Escalation Paths, and Conceptual Attractors"},{"number":"11","name":"Formal Generative Schema of the Whole Book","items":[{"id":"MC_QGOH_L1_02:FGS-B01","text":"Necessitas immanens + potentia finita → campus relationum in quo omnis res agit quatenus causas suas exprimit et patitur quatenus ab externis exceditur.","refs":["CL_QGOH_05:GA-13","CL_QGOH_05:GA-05"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[]},{"id":"MC_QGOH_L1_02:FGS-B02","text":"Mutatio Corporis + idea inadaequata → affectus passivus + causalitas partialis + attribuenda vis causae externae.","refs":["CL_QGOH_05:GA-02","CL_QGOH_07:GA-02"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[]},{"id":"MC_QGOH_L1_02:FGS-B03","text":"Causa externa superior → imminutio potentiae → maior receptivitas passionis → servitus affectiva se ipsam amplificans.","refs":["CL_QGOH_05:RLE-02","CL_QGOH_06:GA-04"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[]},{"id":"MC_QGOH_L1_02:FGS-B04","text":"Conatus + affectus contrarius fortior + cupiditas activa → initium causalitatis propriae sine interruptione necessitatis.","refs":["CL_QGOH_05:GA-07","CL_QGOH_06:GA-03"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[]},{"id":"MC_QGOH_L1_02:FGS-B05","text":"Convenientia rationalis + auxilia communia + condiciones materiales → compositio socialis potentiarum → maior aptitudo corporis et intellectus.","refs":["CL_QGOH_06:XD-01","CL_QGOH_06:XD-04"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[]},{"id":"MC_QGOH_L1_02:FGS-B06","text":"Intellectus necessitatis + ordinatio affectuum + causalitas adaequata → libertas relativa ut participatio intelligibilis nexus causarum.","refs":["CL_QGOH_06:MME-05","CL_QGOH_07:BSD-02"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[]},{"id":"MC_QGOH_L1_02:FGS-B07","text":"Ratio adaequata → cupiditas cognitionis tertii generis → intuitio essentiae singularis → Amor intellectualis.","refs":["CL_QGOH_07:GA-07","CL_QGOH_07:FGS-B05"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[]},{"id":"MC_QGOH_L1_02:FGS-B08","text":"Cognitio intuitiva + laetitia activa + Amor intellectualis → beatitudo → augmentum intellectus → stabilitas libertatis sub necessitate.","refs":["CL_QGOH_07:GA-10","CL_QGOH_07:RLE-07"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[]}],"original_name":"Formal Generative Schema of the Whole Book"},{"number":"12","name":"Whole-Book Argument Flow Map","items":[{"id":"MC_QGOH_L1_02:AFM-B01","text":"Pluralitas affectuum → reductio ad mutationes potentiae → asymmetria causalium virium → servitus → affectus rationalis → compositio communis → intelligentia adaequata → beatitudo activa.","refs":["CL_QGOH_05:BAS-04","CL_QGOH_06:BSD-05","CL_QGOH_07:RLE-07"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[]},{"id":"MC_QGOH_L1_02:AFM-B02","text":"Conflictus imaginativus → Civitas coercitiva → securitas → iura et cooperatio → causalitas propria civium → acquiescentia sub necessitate.","refs":["CL_QGOH_06:AS-B05","CL_QGOH_06:XD-03","CL_QGOH_06:FGS-B06"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[]},{"id":"MC_QGOH_L1_02:AFM-B03","text":"Corpus affectum et durans → aptitudo materialiter aucta → ordinatio imaginum → plures actiones → cognitio intuitiva → maior proportio Mentis sub aeternitate intellecta.","refs":["CL_QGOH_07:GA-06","CL_QGOH_07:CGD-08","CL_QGOH_07:AFM-B03"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[]}],"original_name":"Whole-Book Argument Flow Map"},{"number":"13","name":"Macro Map-Effects","items":[{"id":"MC_QGOH_L1_02:MME-01","text":"Psychologia affectuum in ecologiam potentiae reordinatur, ubi corpora, institutiones et ideae non regiones separatae sed modi unius distributionis causalis sunt.","refs":["CL_QGOH_05:MME-01","CL_QGOH_06:HSI-01","CL_QGOH_07:MME-02"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[]},{"id":"MC_QGOH_L1_02:MME-02","text":"Moralitas actuum exteriorum corruit; valor transfertur ad modum causationis, effectum in potentiam totius et originem affectivam vel intellectualem.","refs":["CL_QGOH_05:MME-03","CL_QGOH_06:MME-03","CL_QGOH_07:MME-06"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[]},{"id":"MC_QGOH_L1_02:MME-03","text":"Corpus et oeconomia ex adiunctis ethicae in condiciones constitutivas cognitionis, libertatis et dispositionis rationalis transferuntur.","refs":["CL_QGOH_06:MME-04","CL_QGOH_07:MME-03"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[]},{"id":"MC_QGOH_L1_02:MME-04","text":"Oppositio inter Civitatem et libertatem reponitur oppositione inter regimen metus et ordinem qui proportionem actionis ad passionem collective mutat.","refs":["CL_QGOH_06:MME-02","CL_QGOH_07:MME-01"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[]},{"id":"MC_QGOH_L1_02:MME-05","text":"Necessitas reclassificatur ab externo limite agentiae in campum immanentem autonomiae relationalis et intelligentiae causalium nexuum.","refs":["CL_QGOH_06:MME-05","CL_QGOH_07:BSD-02"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[]},{"id":"MC_QGOH_L1_02:MME-06","text":"Deus et aeternitas a personis vel continuationibus temporalibus transferuntur ad structuram reflexivam causalitatis immanentis et conceptionem essentiae sub necessitate.","refs":["CL_QGOH_07:MME-04","CL_QGOH_07:MME-05"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[]},{"id":"MC_QGOH_L1_02:MME-07","text":"Lectio systematis debet functiones mutantes terminorum sequi, quia actus, affectus, Corpus, libertas et beatitudo identitatem nominalem servant dum statum structuralem mutant.","refs":["CL_QGOH_05:MME-08","CL_QGOH_06:ALT-B05","CL_QGOH_07:MRT-B04"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[]}],"original_name":"Macro Map-Effects"},{"number":"14","name":"Altitude Integration","items":[{"id":"MC_QGOH_L1_02:ALT-B01","text":"In gradu superficiei systema nomina affectuum, utilitates, leges, corpus, mortem et beatitudinem tractat ut quaestiones vitae humanae sub servitute et libertate.","refs":["CL_QGOH_05:ALT-B01","CL_QGOH_06:ALT-B01","CL_QGOH_07:ALT-B01"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[]},{"id":"MC_QGOH_L1_02:ALT-B02","text":"In gradu structurali casus omnes per mutationes corporis, causalitatem partialem, mediationem socialem et ordinem idearum distribuuntur.","refs":["CL_QGOH_05:ALT-B02","CL_QGOH_06:ALT-B02","CL_QGOH_07:ALT-B02"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[]},{"id":"MC_QGOH_L1_02:ALT-B03","text":"In gradu metastrategico moderatio a condemnatione passionum ad productionem affectuum activorum, cooperationis et laetitiae cognitivae transfertur.","refs":["CL_QGOH_05:ALT-B03","CL_QGOH_06:ALT-B03","CL_QGOH_07:ALT-B03"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[]},{"id":"MC_QGOH_L1_02:ALT-B04","text":"In gradu ontologico et temporali subiectum est pars Naturae necessario determinata, cuius duratio imaginativa ab aeternitate intelligibili distinguitur.","refs":["CL_QGOH_05:ALT-B04","CL_QGOH_06:ALT-B04","CL_QGOH_07:ALT-B04"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[]},{"id":"MC_QGOH_L1_02:ALT-B05","text":"In gradu methodologico et performativo demonstratio reductionem, reclassificationem et mutationem quam describit ipsa exercet, donec negatio in circuitum positivum beatitudinis convertatur.","refs":["CL_QGOH_05:ALT-B05","CL_QGOH_06:ALT-B05","CL_QGOH_07:ALT-B05"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[]}],"original_name":"Altitude Integration"},{"number":"15","name":"Internal Tensions, Mutation Points, and Fault Lines","items":[{"id":"MC_QGOH_L1_02:FL-01","text":"Veritas imperium automaticum non habet, sed libertas terminalis ab intelligentia pendet; quantitas et forma cognitionis quae vim affectivam sufficientem producit indeterminatae manent.","refs":["CL_QGOH_06:FL-01","CL_QGOH_07:FL-01"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"Tension","passages":[]},{"id":"MC_QGOH_L1_02:FL-02","text":"Concordia rationalis necessaria dicitur, sed Civitas minis fundata cooperationem rationalem ex sola securitate deducere nequit.","refs":["CL_QGOH_05:FL-06","CL_QGOH_06:FL-02","CL_QGOH_06:MRT-B03"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"Tension","passages":[]},{"id":"MC_QGOH_L1_02:FL-03","text":"Corpus est temporale et pars structurae Mentis pereuntis, sed eius aptitudo maiorem participationem aeternitatis producere dicitur sine mechanismi proportionis plena demonstratione.","refs":["CL_QGOH_07:XD-04","CL_QGOH_07:FL-05"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"Tension","passages":[]},{"id":"MC_QGOH_L1_02:FL-04","text":"Negatio finalitatis cum attractore Dei cognitionis et beatitudinis cohaeret tantum si attractor causa immanens, non finis Naturae praestitutus, intelligitur.","refs":["CL_QGOH_05:FL-05","CL_QGOH_05:BSA-05","CL_QGOH_07:GA-10"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"Tension","passages":[]},{"id":"MC_QGOH_L1_02:FL-05","text":"Virtus ab cognitione aeternitatis independens manet, quamvis figura summa sapientis per intellectum aeternum et Amorem intellectualem definiatur.","refs":["CL_QGOH_07:CGD-07","CL_QGOH_07:FL-06"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"Tension","passages":[]},{"id":"MC_QGOH_L1_02:FL-06","text":"Amor intellectualis aeternus sine principio ponitur, sed accessus ad cognitionem intuitivam progressionis gradatim acquisitae forma narratur.","refs":["CL_QGOH_07:FL-04","CL_QGOH_07:GA-09"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"Tension","passages":[]},{"id":"MC_QGOH_L1_02:MP-01","text":"Cupiditas punctum mutationis constituit ubi ex affectu primitivo in essentiam actualem, cupiditatem activam et principium cognitionis altioris transit.","refs":["CL_QGOH_05:MP-01","CL_QGOH_06:GA-03"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[]},{"id":"MC_QGOH_L1_02:MP-02","text":"Civitas functionem mutat ubi ex exteriori coercitione in convertorem securitatis, cooperationis et causalitatis propriae civium transit.","refs":["CL_QGOH_06:RMD-03","CL_QGOH_06:XD-03"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[]},{"id":"MC_QGOH_L1_02:MP-03","text":"Corpus functionem mutat ubi ex sede durationis et passionis fit dispositio positiva ad actionem, cognitionem et aeternitatem intelligibilem.","refs":["CL_QGOH_07:RMD-05","CL_QGOH_07:MP-03"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[]},{"id":"MC_QGOH_L1_02:MP-04","text":"Necessitas sensum mutat ubi ex indice excessus externi fit obiectum acquiescentiae et forma libertatis sapientis.","refs":["CL_QGOH_06:MP-03","CL_QGOH_07:RMD-06"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[]},{"id":"MC_QGOH_L1_02:MP-05","text":"Beatitudo statum terminalem amittit ubi ex fine disciplinae in ipsam virtutem et causam minuendarum passionum invertitur.","refs":["CL_QGOH_07:AS-B08","CL_QGOH_07:MP-04"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[]}],"original_name":"Internal Tensions, Mutation Points, and Fault Lines"},{"number":"16","name":"Hidden System Image","items":[{"id":"MC_QGOH_L1_02:HSI-01","text":"Realitas implicite est rete virium finitarum, corporum, institutionum et idearum, in quo causalitas perpetuo distribuitur, componitur aut captatur.","refs":["CL_QGOH_05:HSI-01","CL_QGOH_06:HSI-01","CL_QGOH_07:HSI-01"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[]},{"id":"MC_QGOH_L1_02:HSI-02","text":"Subiectum est nodus permeabilis, corporaliter et socialiter compositus, qui liber fit quatenus nexus determinantes clarius per ipsum exprimuntur.","refs":["CL_QGOH_05:HSI-02","CL_QGOH_06:HSI-02","CL_QGOH_07:HSI-02"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[]},{"id":"MC_QGOH_L1_02:HSI-03","text":"Tempus est topologia bifurcata: duratio praesentiam, memoriam et passionem distribuit, aeternitas autem essentiam sine successione aut recordatione exhibet.","refs":["CL_QGOH_05:HSI-03","CL_QGOH_06:HSI-03","CL_QGOH_07:HSI-03"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[]},{"id":"MC_QGOH_L1_02:HSI-04","text":"Navigatio possibilis est spiralis conversio canalium causalium: conflictus in cooperationem, fixatio externa in intelligentiam et mortalitatis metus in beatitudinem praesentem transferuntur.","refs":["CL_QGOH_05:HSI-04","CL_QGOH_06:HSI-04","CL_QGOH_07:HSI-05"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[]}],"original_name":"Hidden System Image"},{"number":"17","name":"Minimal Whole-Book Reconstruction Test","items":[{"id":"MC_QGOH_L1_02:MRT-B01","text":"Axiomata necessitatis, affectus, conatus, compositionis socialis, aptitudinis Corporis et intelligentiae sufficiunt ad machinam minimam huius gradus restituendam.","refs":["CL_QGOH_05:MRT-B01","CL_QGOH_06:MRT-B01","CL_QGOH_07:MRT-B01"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[]},{"id":"MC_QGOH_L1_02:MRT-B02","text":"Nondum satis determinantur productio affectus rationalis fortioris, conversio Civitatis coercitivae et relatio experientiae aeternitatis ad Mentem memoriam amittentem.","refs":["CL_QGOH_05:MRT-B03","CL_QGOH_06:MRT-B03","CL_QGOH_07:MRT-B03"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[]},{"id":"MC_QGOH_L1_02:MRT-B03","text":"Structura firmissima est circuitus qui conatum, cooperationem, ideam adaequatam, intellectum necessitatis et auctam potentiam cognoscendi connectit.","refs":["CL_QGOH_05:MRT-B02","CL_QGOH_06:MRT-B02","CL_QGOH_07:MRT-B02"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[]},{"id":"MC_QGOH_L1_02:MRT-B04","text":"Restitutio verum motum servat si libertatem ut amplificationem recursivam potentiae et remedia temporalia ut dispositionem ad cognitionem intuitivam exhibet, non ut liberationem instantaneam.","refs":["CL_QGOH_05:MRT-B05","CL_QGOH_06:MRT-B04","CL_QGOH_07:MRT-B05"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[]},{"id":"MC_QGOH_L1_02:MRT-B05","text":"Systema hoc gradu cohaeret per reditum terminorum cum mutatione functionis, sed nexus inter demonstrationem, experientiam aeternitatis et vim imaginis sapientis reductioni plenae resistit.","refs":["CL_QGOH_05:MRT-B04","CL_QGOH_07:MRT-B06"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[]}],"original_name":"Minimal Whole-Book Reconstruction Test"}],"language_code":"la","language_name":"Latina","translations":[{"id":"CAREVAR_20261015T015905984040Z_89f91e9d","language_code":"en","language_name":"English","title":"Qgoh L1 02","source_sha256":"5750f3da2da410966e0c557330decf54635b7405cf3c028f6357868076961c43","sha256":"270d852a8ce2a8b998642e3328c06b83e27ebaaefbe7cf3c731676c68c59d6c0","rendition_type":"machine","review_status":"machine","model":"gpt-5.6-terra","created":"2026-10-15T01:59:05.984933+00:00","sections":[{"number":"1","name":"Book-Scale Argumental Spine","items":[{"id":"MC_QGOH_L1_02:AS-01","text":"The system asks how a finite human being, composed of affects and external causes, can acquire common and intellectual freedom without dominion of the will or exemption from Nature.","refs":["CL_QGOH_05:BAS-01","CL_QGOH_06:AS-B01","CL_QGOH_07:AS-B01"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[]},{"id":"MC_QGOH_L1_02:AS-02","text":"The global thesis establishes freedom as the graduated amplification of one's own causality, which transforms affective servitude into active blessedness through bodily, civil, and cognitive composition.","refs":["CL_QGOH_05:GA-05","CL_QGOH_06:GA-08","CL_QGOH_07:GA-10"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[]},{"id":"MC_QGOH_L1_02:AS-03","text":"The argument is apophatic and reconstructive: it excludes false forms of autonomy, morality, and salvation, then reconstitutes the negated terms within immanent causality.","refs":["CL_QGOH_05:BAS-03","CL_QGOH_07:AS-B03"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[]},{"id":"MC_QGOH_L1_02:AS-04","text":"The movement proceeds from the reduction of affects to servitude, from servitude to the social composition of powers, and from this composition to intuitive knowledge and intellectual Love.","refs":["CL_QGOH_05:BAS-04","CL_QGOH_06:XD-03","CL_QGOH_07:AS-B04"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[]},{"id":"MC_QGOH_L1_02:AS-05","text":"The progression is cumulative, recursive, amplificatory, and spiral, because each stage produces the cause of the efficacy of the following stage and the terminus turns back to the operation of the preceding ones.","refs":["CL_QGOH_05:BAS-05","CL_QGOH_06:AS-B06","CL_QGOH_07:AS-B05"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[]},{"id":"MC_QGOH_L1_02:AS-06","text":"The closure offers not absolute liberation but a twofold stability: finite acquiescence within necessary events and blessedness which, from an apparent end, is converted into a permanent cause of activity.","refs":["CL_QGOH_05:BAS-06","CL_QGOH_06:AS-B07","CL_QGOH_07:AS-B08"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[]}],"original_name":"Book-Scale Argumental Spine"},{"number":"2","name":"Global Load-Bearing Axioms","items":[{"id":"MC_QGOH_L1_02:GA-01","text":"[Persistent] Nature acts from immanent necessity; freedom therefore does not remove causality, but changes the mode in which necessity is explicated through a finite thing.","refs":["CL_QGOH_05:GA-13","CL_QGOH_07:GA-04"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[]},{"id":"MC_QGOH_L1_02:GA-02","text":"[Persistent] Passion is a bodily and ideational determination whose cause is explained by the subject only partially; action increases insofar as the idea of the cause is adequate.","refs":["CL_QGOH_05:GA-02","CL_QGOH_07:GA-02"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[]},{"id":"MC_QGOH_L1_02:GA-03","text":"[Mutating] Conatus passes from the essence of persevering to rational virtue, common composition, and blessed intellectual action, with directional identity preserved.","refs":["CL_QGOH_05:GA-09","CL_QGOH_06:GA-01","CL_QGOH_07:RMD-08"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[]},{"id":"MC_QGOH_L1_02:GA-04","text":"[Mutating] Freedom is a degree of adequate causality that first orders the affects, then composes bodily and civil conditions, and finally extends into intellectual stability.","refs":["CL_QGOH_05:BSD-07","CL_QGOH_06:GA-08","CL_QGOH_07:GA-03"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[]},{"id":"MC_QGOH_L1_02:GA-05","text":"[Persistent] Effective knowledge cannot be inert representation: truth increases power only where it causally changes an affect, body, institution, or order of images.","refs":["CL_QGOH_05:GA-06","CL_QGOH_06:DBN-01","CL_QGOH_07:DBN-05"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[]},{"id":"MC_QGOH_L1_02:GA-06","text":"[Emergent] Finitude, which exposes one to servitude under external causes, is the same dependence from which the composition of bodies, mutual aid, and common power are generated.","refs":["CL_QGOH_05:GA-10","CL_QGOH_06:XD-01"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[]},{"id":"MC_QGOH_L1_02:GA-07","text":"[Mutating] The Body is transformed from the seat of affections into the material infrastructure of freedom and finally into a positive disposition toward the greater action of the Mind.","refs":["CL_QGOH_05:GNX-04","CL_QGOH_06:GA-09","CL_QGOH_07:GA-06"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[]},{"id":"MC_QGOH_L1_02:GA-08","text":"[Mutating] Reason obtains force only when it is converted from an intelligible norm into a contrary affect, active desire, stable institution, and transition to intuition.","refs":["CL_QGOH_05:GA-07","CL_QGOH_06:GA-03","CL_QGOH_07:GA-07"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[]},{"id":"MC_QGOH_L1_02:GA-09","text":"[Mutating] Sociality progresses from conflictual imitation to the coercive State and thence to the infrastructure of rational cooperation, its passional origin never wholly erased.","refs":["CL_QGOH_05:GA-11","CL_QGOH_06:GA-06"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[]},{"id":"MC_QGOH_L1_02:GA-10","text":"[Emergent] Good, evil, just, and unjust are comparative instruments of finite power; in perfect freedom they ultimately lose their function.","refs":["CL_QGOH_05:GA-08","CL_QGOH_06:GA-07","CL_QGOH_06:GA-11"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[]},{"id":"MC_QGOH_L1_02:GA-11","text":"[Mutating] Necessity is inverted from a sign of external domination into an object of intellect and the ontological form of the freedom of the wise.","refs":["CL_QGOH_06:MP-03","CL_QGOH_07:RMD-06"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[]},{"id":"MC_QGOH_L1_02:GA-12","text":"[Attractor] Understanding, knowledge of God, acquiescence, intuition, intellectual Love, and blessedness constitute one ascending attractor of non-rivalrous power.","refs":["CL_QGOH_05:GA-12","CL_QGOH_06:GA-10","CL_QGOH_07:GA-10"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[]},{"id":"MC_QGOH_L1_02:GA-13","text":"[Emergent] Affective time bifurcates between imaginative duration, which distributes presence and intensity, and intelligible eternity, which does not continue succession and memory.","refs":["CL_QGOH_05:BSD-04","CL_QGOH_06:HSI-03","CL_QGOH_07:GA-09"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[]},{"id":"MC_QGOH_L1_02:GA-14","text":"[Terminal] The blessedness of the wise is not the reward of virtue but its exercise, whereby a terminal position is converted into a cause of preserving freedom.","refs":["CL_QGOH_07:GA-11","CL_QGOH_07:GA-12","CL_QGOH_07:AS-B08"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[]}],"original_name":"Global Load-Bearing Axioms"},{"number":"3","name":"Recurrence, Mutation, and Displacement Matrix","items":[{"id":"MC_QGOH_L1_02:RMD-01","text":"Joy and Sadness preserve directional identity, but are amplified from indicators of affective change into ethical criteria and finally into the difference between active joy and passive fluctuation.","refs":["CL_QGOH_05:RMD-01","CL_QGOH_07:DBN-05"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[]},{"id":"MC_QGOH_L1_02:RMD-02","text":"Desire passes from primitive affect into the essence of conatus, rational desire, and the genealogy of action; its object is subordinated to the causality of its origin.","refs":["CL_QGOH_05:RMD-02","CL_QGOH_06:GA-03","CL_QGOH_07:XD-08"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[]},{"id":"MC_QGOH_L1_02:RMD-03","text":"Truth changes function: from polemical authority it becomes affectively conditioned force, then a technique of bodily and intellectual transformation.","refs":["CL_QGOH_05:RMD-07","CL_QGOH_06:XD-02","CL_QGOH_07:BSD-03"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[]},{"id":"MC_QGOH_L1_02:RMD-04","text":"The Body migrates from the correlate of passion to the infrastructure of nourishment and labor, then to an instrument for amplifying the power of the Mind.","refs":["CL_QGOH_06:RMD-05","CL_QGOH_07:RMD-05"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[]},{"id":"MC_QGOH_L1_02:RMD-05","text":"Sociality returns with altered function: imagination of another produces conflict, the State restrains it, rational cooperation converts dependence into amplification.","refs":["CL_QGOH_05:RMD-05","CL_QGOH_06:RMD-03"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[]},{"id":"MC_QGOH_L1_02:RMD-06","text":"Freedom passes from the exclusion of an indeterminate decree to a materially sustained ecology and finally to a change in the proportion between action and passion.","refs":["CL_QGOH_05:XD-07","CL_QGOH_06:MP-02","CL_QGOH_07:RMD-07"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[]},{"id":"MC_QGOH_L1_02:RMD-07","text":"Necessity, initially the negation of free decree, is absorbed into the condition of acquiescence and finally into the form of intellectual freedom.","refs":["CL_QGOH_05:RMD-09","CL_QGOH_06:MP-03","CL_QGOH_07:RMD-06"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[]},{"id":"MC_QGOH_L1_02:RMD-08","text":"Moral terms migrate from affective institution to rational comparison and, with perfect freedom approximated, withdraw once their work of navigation is completed.","refs":["CL_QGOH_05:RMD-06","CL_QGOH_06:RMD-04"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[]},{"id":"MC_QGOH_L1_02:RMD-09","text":"Knowledge of God progresses from an attractor of understanding to acquiescence and intellectual Love, while reciprocal and anthropomorphic love is inverted into reflexive action.","refs":["CL_QGOH_05:XD-09","CL_QGOH_07:GA-08","CL_QGOH_07:RMD-03"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[]},{"id":"MC_QGOH_L1_02:RMD-10","text":"Imagination affectively presents absent things and prepares reason, but in memorial eternity it ultimately vanishes, its operation exhausted.","refs":["CL_QGOH_05:RMD-04","CL_QGOH_07:RMD-04"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[]}],"original_name":"Recurrence, Mutation, and Displacement Matrix"},{"number":"4","name":"Lower-Layer vs Current-Level Distinction","items":[{"id":"MC_QGOH_L1_02:CGD-01","text":"Taxonomies of affects and cases of acts remain local machines; at this level they are preserved only as proofs of the law by which cause, intensity, and the whole of power constitute value.","refs":["CL_QGOH_05:CGD-01","CL_QGOH_06:CGD-03"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[]},{"id":"MC_QGOH_L1_02:CGD-02","text":"Temporal differences and the concurrence of images are amplified upward into a general doctrine of the distribution of affective presence, but do not yet themselves constitute intelligible eternity.","refs":["CL_QGOH_05:CGD-03","CL_QGOH_07:CGD-01"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[]},{"id":"MC_QGOH_L1_02:CGD-03","text":"The evaluation of individual acts is absorbed into the genealogy of cause: the same outward species can express passion, rational action, or virtue.","refs":["CL_QGOH_06:CGD-03","CL_QGOH_07:XD-08"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[]},{"id":"MC_QGOH_L1_02:CGD-04","text":"The triad of affects, the doctrine of the stronger affect, and the relation of affections to God join the lower units, because they convert a change of power into a cognitive transition.","refs":["CL_QGOH_05:CGD-05","CL_QGOH_06:CGD-04","CL_QGOH_07:CGD-04"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[]},{"id":"MC_QGOH_L1_02:CGD-05","text":"The transition of the present level occurs where knowledge ceases to be mere truth and becomes an active affect, bodily disposition, and cause of intellectual joy.","refs":["CL_QGOH_05:CGD-06","CL_QGOH_06:XD-02","CL_QGOH_07:DBN-05"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[]},{"id":"MC_QGOH_L1_02:CGD-06","text":"The State effects the greater transition when coercive power is converted into rights, material cooperation, and the citizens' own causality.","refs":["CL_QGOH_06:CGD-05","CL_QGOH_06:XD-03"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[]},{"id":"MC_QGOH_L1_02:CGD-07","text":"The distinction between duration and eternity joins the higher units, while the aptitude of the Body becomes the joint between temporal praxis and non-successive knowledge.","refs":["CL_QGOH_07:CGD-05","CL_QGOH_07:CGD-08"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[]},{"id":"MC_QGOH_L1_02:CGD-08","text":"The morality of sadness, the inventory of affects, and the refutation of the will become secondary, because their negative work is absorbed into the positive machine of cooperation and blessedness.","refs":["CL_QGOH_06:CGD-06","CL_QGOH_07:CGD-02","CL_QGOH_05:RMD-10"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[]}],"original_name":"Lower-Layer vs Current-Level Distinction"},{"number":"5","name":"Global Apophatic Structure","items":[{"id":"MC_QGOH_L1_02:GNX-01","text":"Freedom is neither an indeterminate decree nor dominion of the will; no human action interrupts the nexus of natural causes.","refs":["CL_QGOH_05:GNX-01","CL_QGOH_06:GNX-03","CL_QGOH_07:GNX-01"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"Negative","passages":[]},{"id":"MC_QGOH_L1_02:GNX-02","text":"Nature admits neither external ends nor a transcendent moral order nor a God mutable by will.","refs":["CL_QGOH_05:GNX-02","CL_QGOH_06:DBN-04","CL_QGOH_07:BSA-02"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"Negative","passages":[]},{"id":"MC_QGOH_L1_02:GNX-03","text":"True knowledge, if it does not change affect and the order of the Body, cannot overcome passion by logical contradiction alone.","refs":["CL_QGOH_05:GNX-05","CL_QGOH_06:DBN-01","CL_QGOH_07:DBN-05"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"Negative","passages":[]},{"id":"MC_QGOH_L1_02:GNX-04","text":"The self-sufficient individual is excluded, because body, external things, other humans, and God act within the very constitution of power.","refs":["CL_QGOH_05:GNX-06","CL_QGOH_06:DBN-02"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"Negative","passages":[]},{"id":"MC_QGOH_L1_02:GNX-05","text":"Self-interest and the common good are not contrary, unless conatus is considered under competitive imagination and inadequate causality.","refs":["CL_QGOH_06:GNX-01","CL_QGOH_06:MME-01"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"Negative","passages":[]},{"id":"MC_QGOH_L1_02:GNX-06","text":"The State and freedom are not absolute oppositions; the true distinction is between the administration of fear and the production of common power.","refs":["CL_QGOH_06:GNX-04","CL_QGOH_06:MME-02"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"Negative","passages":[]},{"id":"MC_QGOH_L1_02:GNX-07","text":"The Body is not the adversary of the Mind or of eternity, but the condition without which affects cannot be ordered and intellect cannot be amplified.","refs":["CL_QGOH_05:GNX-04","CL_QGOH_07:GNX-08"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"Negative","passages":[]},{"id":"MC_QGOH_L1_02:GNX-08","text":"Eternity is not the continuation of personal memory, nor does virtue depend on hope of reward, fear of punishment, or judgment after death.","refs":["CL_QGOH_07:GNX-05","CL_QGOH_07:GNX-06","CL_QGOH_07:BSA-04"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"Negative","passages":[]}],"original_name":"Global Apophatic Structure"},{"number":"6","name":"Deep Book Apophatics","items":[{"id":"MC_QGOH_L1_02:DBN-01","text":"It must be false that Nature is ordered by a final legislator or personal God, because every valid norm is generated from immanent relations of power.","refs":["CL_QGOH_06:DBN-04","CL_QGOH_07:BSA-02"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"Negative","passages":[]},{"id":"MC_QGOH_L1_02:DBN-02","text":"It must be false that an abstract mind without affect and body can convert inert truth into practical dominion.","refs":["CL_QGOH_05:DBN-02","CL_QGOH_06:DBN-01","CL_QGOH_07:DBN-05"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"Negative","passages":[]},{"id":"MC_QGOH_L1_02:DBN-03","text":"It must be false that the subject is a closed substance or subsists outside God, because freedom is born from adequately composed relations.","refs":["CL_QGOH_05:DBN-03","CL_QGOH_06:DBN-02","CL_QGOH_07:BSA-01"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"Negative","passages":[]},{"id":"MC_QGOH_L1_02:DBN-04","text":"It must be false that the external appearance of an act contains its value; passion and action are distinguished by the mode of causation, not by similarity of work.","refs":["CL_QGOH_06:DBN-03","CL_QGOH_06:ALT-B05"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"Negative","passages":[]},{"id":"MC_QGOH_L1_02:DBN-05","text":"It must be false that coercion and security in themselves produce rational cooperation; actual community requires affective, juridical, and material mediations.","refs":["CL_QGOH_06:BSA-03","CL_QGOH_06:FL-02"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"Negative","passages":[]},{"id":"MC_QGOH_L1_02:DBN-06","text":"It must be false that the eternal identity of the Mind coincides with the memorial history of the person or can be defined by temporal measure.","refs":["CL_QGOH_07:DBN-03","CL_QGOH_07:BSA-03"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"Negative","passages":[]}],"original_name":"Deep Book Apophatics"},{"number":"7","name":"Super-Apophatics of the Whole","items":[{"id":"MC_QGOH_L1_02:BSA-01","text":"An external place outside Nature is ontologically inadmissible, from which a finite subject might contemplate the causes of the whole without participation.","refs":["CL_QGOH_05:BSA-01","CL_QGOH_06:BSA-01","CL_QGOH_07:BSA-01"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"Negative","passages":[]},{"id":"MC_QGOH_L1_02:BSA-02","text":"The system is ordered around the absence of an instantaneous conversion of truth into power; no minimal clarity by itself secures the transmutation of passion.","refs":["CL_QGOH_05:BSA-02","CL_QGOH_06:DBN-01","CL_QGOH_07:FL-01"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"Negative","passages":[]},{"id":"MC_QGOH_L1_02:BSA-03","text":"A state of finite humanity absolutely liberated from every passion, body, society, and external cause is impossible.","refs":["CL_QGOH_05:BSA-03","CL_QGOH_06:BSA-02"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"Negative","passages":[]},{"id":"MC_QGOH_L1_02:BSA-04","text":"The absence of a rewarding judge and an affectively reciprocal God compels virtue to become the immanent and sufficient cause of itself.","refs":["CL_QGOH_07:BSA-02","CL_QGOH_07:BSA-04"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"Negative","passages":[]},{"id":"MC_QGOH_L1_02:BSA-05","text":"The system labors around an incomplete bridge between actual passion and perfect freedom: rational affect, the cooperative State, and the experience of eternity are not yet fully causally connected.","refs":["CL_QGOH_05:BSA-04","CL_QGOH_06:MRT-B03","CL_QGOH_07:MRT-B03"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"Tension","passages":[]}],"original_name":"Super-Apophatics of the Whole"},{"number":"8","name":"Cross-Unit Derivative Propositions","items":[{"id":"MC_QGOH_L1_02:XD-01","text":"Freedom is an ecology of power, because the asymmetry of external causes is converted only through the relation of bodily aptitude, social cooperation, and adequate intelligence.","refs":["MC_QGOH_L1_02:GA-04","MC_QGOH_L1_02:GA-06","MC_QGOH_L1_02:GA-07","MC_QGOH_L1_02:GA-09","CL_QGOH_05:GA-05","CL_QGOH_06:GA-08","CL_QGOH_07:GA-06"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"From","passages":[]},{"id":"MC_QGOH_L1_02:XD-02","text":"Truth obtains practical force when it becomes a contrary affect, active desire, institutional disposition, and joy from adequate knowledge.","refs":["MC_QGOH_L1_02:GA-05","MC_QGOH_L1_02:GA-08","CL_QGOH_05:XD-03","CL_QGOH_06:XD-02","CL_QGOH_07:DBN-05"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"From","passages":[]},{"id":"MC_QGOH_L1_02:XD-03","text":"The conatus of self-preservation acquires a non-rivalrous structure, because common aid and another's blessedness need not diminish but can increase an individual's power.","refs":["MC_QGOH_L1_02:GA-03","MC_QGOH_L1_02:GA-06","MC_QGOH_L1_02:GA-12","CL_QGOH_05:GA-09","CL_QGOH_06:XD-01","CL_QGOH_07:XD-06"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"From","passages":[]},{"id":"MC_QGOH_L1_02:XD-04","text":"The Body is at once the terminus of the perishing memorial part and the material medium through which knowledge, freedom, and greater participation in intellectual action are produced.","refs":["MC_QGOH_L1_02:GA-07","MC_QGOH_L1_02:GA-13","CL_QGOH_06:GA-09","CL_QGOH_07:XD-04"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"From","passages":[]},{"id":"MC_QGOH_L1_02:XD-05","text":"Necessity exercises an inversive force: the same causality that denies freedom of choice constitutes the immanent field in which one's own causality and the freedom of the wise increase.","refs":["MC_QGOH_L1_02:GA-01","MC_QGOH_L1_02:GA-04","MC_QGOH_L1_02:GA-11","CL_QGOH_05:BSA-01","CL_QGOH_06:MME-05","CL_QGOH_07:RMD-06"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"From","passages":[]},{"id":"MC_QGOH_L1_02:XD-06","text":"The State is a converter between servitude and freedom, not the terminus of liberation: it changes external force into cooperation, while Reason opens the transition to intellectual action.","refs":["MC_QGOH_L1_02:GA-08","MC_QGOH_L1_02:GA-09","CL_QGOH_06:XD-03","CL_QGOH_07:GA-07"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"From","passages":[]},{"id":"MC_QGOH_L1_02:XD-07","text":"The categories of good and evil are necessary while finite power navigates, but withdraw as the attractor of blessedness is approached, because blessedness is no longer a reward but virtue.","refs":["MC_QGOH_L1_02:GA-10","MC_QGOH_L1_02:GA-14","CL_QGOH_05:GA-08","CL_QGOH_06:XD-05","CL_QGOH_07:GA-11"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"From","passages":[]},{"id":"MC_QGOH_L1_02:XD-08","text":"The time of ethical works has a double account: imaginative presence distributes intensities, Reason orders utilities across duration, but eternity wholly suspends succession.","refs":["MC_QGOH_L1_02:GA-13","CL_QGOH_05:BSD-04","CL_QGOH_06:HSI-03","CL_QGOH_07:GA-09"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"From","passages":[]},{"id":"MC_QGOH_L1_02:XD-09","text":"Blessedness retroactively orders the entire series, because the apparent end becomes a machine that increases intellect, diminishes passions, and preserves its own presence.","refs":["MC_QGOH_L1_02:GA-12","MC_QGOH_L1_02:GA-14","CL_QGOH_07:AS-B08","CL_QGOH_07:RLE-07"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"From","passages":[]},{"id":"MC_QGOH_L1_02:XD-10","text":"The subject is a multiscalar proportion of partial causalities, social relations, and adequate ideas, whose identity consists in power of acting rather than substantial closure.","refs":["MC_QGOH_L1_02:GA-02","MC_QGOH_L1_02:GA-04","MC_QGOH_L1_02:GA-06","CL_QGOH_05:BSD-02","CL_QGOH_06:BSD-01","CL_QGOH_07:BSD-01"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"From","passages":[]}],"original_name":"Cross-Unit Derivative Propositions"},{"number":"9","name":"Book-Scale Super-Derivatives","items":[{"id":"MC_QGOH_L1_02:BSD-01","text":"Ontology becomes a multiscalar ecology of power, in which bodies, affects, institutions, and ideas are modes of distributing immanent causality.","refs":["MC_QGOH_L1_02:GA-01","MC_QGOH_L1_02:GA-02","MC_QGOH_L1_02:GA-06","CL_QGOH_05:BSD-01","CL_QGOH_06:HSI-01","CL_QGOH_07:HSI-01"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"From","passages":[]},{"id":"MC_QGOH_L1_02:BSD-02","text":"Freedom is neither a property of the individual nor political status alone, but a distributed relation by which body, society, and intellect more sufficiently express the cause of action.","refs":["MC_QGOH_L1_02:GA-04","MC_QGOH_L1_02:GA-07","MC_QGOH_L1_02:GA-09","CL_QGOH_06:GA-08","CL_QGOH_06:HSI-02","CL_QGOH_07:HSI-02"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"From","passages":[]},{"id":"MC_QGOH_L1_02:BSD-03","text":"Epistemology acquires the status of causal technology, because true knowledge simultaneously produces affects, the order of the Body, and the capacity for further knowing.","refs":["MC_QGOH_L1_02:GA-05","MC_QGOH_L1_02:GA-08","CL_QGOH_05:BSD-03","CL_QGOH_07:BSD-03"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"From","passages":[]},{"id":"MC_QGOH_L1_02:BSD-04","text":"Politics becomes the distribution of conditions of one's own causality, whose measure is whether the communication of good simultaneously amplifies individual and common power.","refs":["MC_QGOH_L1_02:GA-06","MC_QGOH_L1_02:GA-09","MC_QGOH_L1_02:GA-12","CL_QGOH_06:BSD-02","CL_QGOH_07:BSD-06"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"From","passages":[]},{"id":"MC_QGOH_L1_02:BSD-05","text":"Time is not one continuous medium: affects inhabit a topology of presence, deliberation a series of utilities, but intellect non-durational essence.","refs":["MC_QGOH_L1_02:GA-13","CL_QGOH_05:BSD-04","CL_QGOH_07:GA-09"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"From","passages":[]},{"id":"MC_QGOH_L1_02:BSD-06","text":"Theology is converted into reflexive ontology, because God remains the supreme object without an affectively reciprocal person, and intellectual Love becomes the explication of immanent self-knowledge.","refs":["MC_QGOH_L1_02:GA-01","MC_QGOH_L1_02:GA-12","CL_QGOH_05:XD-09","CL_QGOH_07:BSD-07"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"From","passages":[]},{"id":"MC_QGOH_L1_02:BSD-07","text":"Ethics is the navigation of finitude that orders power until the comparative instruments of good and evil lose their own necessity in blessed virtue.","refs":["MC_QGOH_L1_02:GA-10","MC_QGOH_L1_02:GA-14","CL_QGOH_05:BSD-05","CL_QGOH_06:XD-05","CL_QGOH_07:GNX-07"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"From","passages":[]}],"original_name":"Book-Scale Super-Derivatives"},{"number":"10","name":"Recursive Loops, Escalation Paths, and Conceptual Attractors","items":[{"id":"MC_QGOH_L1_02:RLE-01","text":"An external cause increases passion, diminishes one's own power, and stabilizes ignorance of the cause; ignorance in turn fixes the external thing again as though mistress of affect.","refs":["CL_QGOH_05:RLE-02","CL_QGOH_07:RLE-01"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"Composite","passages":[]},{"id":"MC_QGOH_L1_02:RLE-02","text":"A contrary affect begins ordering, ordering increases action, action strengthens clarity of cause, and clarity diminishes further passivity.","refs":["CL_QGOH_05:RLE-05","CL_QGOH_07:RLE-02"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"Composite","passages":[]},{"id":"MC_QGOH_L1_02:RLE-03","text":"Rational agreement composes powers, cooperation increases individual power, and increased power produces greater rational cooperation.","refs":["CL_QGOH_05:RLE-07","CL_QGOH_06:MRT-B02"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"Composite","passages":[]},{"id":"MC_QGOH_L1_02:RLE-04","text":"Conflict tends toward coercion, coercion toward security, security toward cooperation; yet the circuit regresses if fear remains the sole foundation of institution.","refs":["CL_QGOH_06:XD-03","CL_QGOH_06:FL-02"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"Tension","passages":[]},{"id":"MC_QGOH_L1_02:RLE-05","text":"The economy of the Body sustains freedom of knowledge, knowledge increases aptitude for action, and a Body suited to more actions receives new knowledge.","refs":["CL_QGOH_06:FGS-B05","CL_QGOH_07:RLE-05"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"Composite","passages":[]},{"id":"MC_QGOH_L1_02:RLE-06","text":"Knowledge and freedom produce a positive circuit: Reason generates desire for intuition, intuition increases joy, and joy sustains further intelligence.","refs":["CL_QGOH_07:XD-02","CL_QGOH_07:RLE-04"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"Composite","passages":[]},{"id":"MC_QGOH_L1_02:RLE-07","text":"The communication of non-rivalrous good increases Love, increased Love desires the participation of others, and participation again amplifies common power.","refs":["CL_QGOH_07:BSD-06","CL_QGOH_07:RLE-06"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"Composite","passages":[]},{"id":"MC_QGOH_L1_02:RLE-08","text":"Blessedness is a terminal attractor and self-preserving circuit: the more it is present, the more the Mind understands and the less it is governed by external affects.","refs":["CL_QGOH_07:AS-B08","CL_QGOH_07:RLE-07"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"Composite","passages":[]}],"original_name":"Recursive Loops, Escalation Paths, and Conceptual Attractors"},{"number":"11","name":"Formal Generative Schema of the Whole Book","items":[{"id":"MC_QGOH_L1_02:FGS-B01","text":"Immanent necessity + finite power → a field of relations in which every thing acts insofar as it expresses its causes and suffers insofar as it is exceeded by externals.","refs":["CL_QGOH_05:GA-13","CL_QGOH_05:GA-05"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[]},{"id":"MC_QGOH_L1_02:FGS-B02","text":"Change of the Body + inadequate idea → passive affect + partial causality + force attributed to an external cause.","refs":["CL_QGOH_05:GA-02","CL_QGOH_07:GA-02"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[]},{"id":"MC_QGOH_L1_02:FGS-B03","text":"Superior external cause → diminution of power → greater receptivity to passion → self-amplifying affective servitude.","refs":["CL_QGOH_05:RLE-02","CL_QGOH_06:GA-04"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[]},{"id":"MC_QGOH_L1_02:FGS-B04","text":"Conatus + stronger contrary affect + active desire → beginning of one's own causality without interruption of necessity.","refs":["CL_QGOH_05:GA-07","CL_QGOH_06:GA-03"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[]},{"id":"MC_QGOH_L1_02:FGS-B05","text":"Rational agreement + common aids + material conditions → social composition of powers → greater aptitude of body and intellect.","refs":["CL_QGOH_06:XD-01","CL_QGOH_06:XD-04"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[]},{"id":"MC_QGOH_L1_02:FGS-B06","text":"Intellect of necessity + ordering of affects + adequate causality → relative freedom as intelligible participation in the nexus of causes.","refs":["CL_QGOH_06:MME-05","CL_QGOH_07:BSD-02"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[]},{"id":"MC_QGOH_L1_02:FGS-B07","text":"Adequate Reason → desire for knowledge of the third kind → intuition of singular essence → intellectual Love.","refs":["CL_QGOH_07:GA-07","CL_QGOH_07:FGS-B05"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[]},{"id":"MC_QGOH_L1_02:FGS-B08","text":"Intuitive knowledge + active joy + intellectual Love → blessedness → increase of intellect → stability of freedom under necessity.","refs":["CL_QGOH_07:GA-10","CL_QGOH_07:RLE-07"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[]}],"original_name":"Formal Generative Schema of the Whole Book"},{"number":"12","name":"Whole-Book Argument Flow Map","items":[{"id":"MC_QGOH_L1_02:AFM-B01","text":"Plurality of affects → reduction to changes of power → asymmetry of causal forces → servitude → rational affect → common composition → adequate intelligence → active blessedness.","refs":["CL_QGOH_05:BAS-04","CL_QGOH_06:BSD-05","CL_QGOH_07:RLE-07"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[]},{"id":"MC_QGOH_L1_02:AFM-B02","text":"Imaginative conflict → coercive State → security → rights and cooperation → citizens' own causality → acquiescence under necessity.","refs":["CL_QGOH_06:AS-B05","CL_QGOH_06:XD-03","CL_QGOH_06:FGS-B06"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[]},{"id":"MC_QGOH_L1_02:AFM-B03","text":"Affective and enduring Body → materially increased aptitude → ordering of images → more actions → intuitive knowledge → greater proportion of Mind understood under eternity.","refs":["CL_QGOH_07:GA-06","CL_QGOH_07:CGD-08","CL_QGOH_07:AFM-B03"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[]}],"original_name":"Whole-Book Argument Flow Map"},{"number":"13","name":"Macro Map-Effects","items":[{"id":"MC_QGOH_L1_02:MME-01","text":"The psychology of affects is reordered into an ecology of power, where bodies, institutions, and ideas are not separate regions but modes of one causal distribution.","refs":["CL_QGOH_05:MME-01","CL_QGOH_06:HSI-01","CL_QGOH_07:MME-02"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[]},{"id":"MC_QGOH_L1_02:MME-02","text":"The morality of external acts collapses; value is transferred to the mode of causation, the effect upon the power of the whole, and affective or intellectual origin.","refs":["CL_QGOH_05:MME-03","CL_QGOH_06:MME-03","CL_QGOH_07:MME-06"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[]},{"id":"MC_QGOH_L1_02:MME-03","text":"Body and economy are transferred from adjuncts of ethics into constitutive conditions of knowledge, freedom, and rational disposition.","refs":["CL_QGOH_06:MME-04","CL_QGOH_07:MME-03"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[]},{"id":"MC_QGOH_L1_02:MME-04","text":"The opposition between the State and freedom is replaced by the opposition between the regime of fear and an order that collectively changes the proportion of action to passion.","refs":["CL_QGOH_06:MME-02","CL_QGOH_07:MME-01"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[]},{"id":"MC_QGOH_L1_02:MME-05","text":"Necessity is reclassified from an external limit of agency into the immanent field of relational autonomy and intelligence of causal nexuses.","refs":["CL_QGOH_06:MME-05","CL_QGOH_07:BSD-02"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[]},{"id":"MC_QGOH_L1_02:MME-06","text":"God and eternity are transferred from persons or temporal continuations to the reflexive structure of immanent causality and the conception of essence under necessity.","refs":["CL_QGOH_07:MME-04","CL_QGOH_07:MME-05"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[]},{"id":"MC_QGOH_L1_02:MME-07","text":"A reading of the system must follow the changing functions of terms, because act, affect, Body, freedom, and blessedness preserve nominal identity while changing structural status.","refs":["CL_QGOH_05:MME-08","CL_QGOH_06:ALT-B05","CL_QGOH_07:MRT-B04"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[]}],"original_name":"Macro Map-Effects"},{"number":"14","name":"Altitude Integration","items":[{"id":"MC_QGOH_L1_02:ALT-B01","text":"At the surface level, the system treats names of affects, utilities, laws, body, death, and blessedness as questions of human life under servitude and freedom.","refs":["CL_QGOH_05:ALT-B01","CL_QGOH_06:ALT-B01","CL_QGOH_07:ALT-B01"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[]},{"id":"MC_QGOH_L1_02:ALT-B02","text":"At the structural level, all cases are distributed through changes of body, partial causality, social mediation, and the order of ideas.","refs":["CL_QGOH_05:ALT-B02","CL_QGOH_06:ALT-B02","CL_QGOH_07:ALT-B02"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[]},{"id":"MC_QGOH_L1_02:ALT-B03","text":"At the metastrategic level, governance passes from condemnation of passions to production of active affects, cooperation, and cognitive joy.","refs":["CL_QGOH_05:ALT-B03","CL_QGOH_06:ALT-B03","CL_QGOH_07:ALT-B03"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[]},{"id":"MC_QGOH_L1_02:ALT-B04","text":"At the ontological and temporal level, the subject is a necessarily determined part of Nature, whose imaginative duration is distinguished from intelligible eternity.","refs":["CL_QGOH_05:ALT-B04","CL_QGOH_06:ALT-B04","CL_QGOH_07:ALT-B04"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[]},{"id":"MC_QGOH_L1_02:ALT-B05","text":"At the methodological and performative level, demonstration itself performs the reduction, reclassification, and change that it describes, until negation is converted into the positive circuit of blessedness.","refs":["CL_QGOH_05:ALT-B05","CL_QGOH_06:ALT-B05","CL_QGOH_07:ALT-B05"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[]}],"original_name":"Altitude Integration"},{"number":"15","name":"Internal Tensions, Mutation Points, and Fault Lines","items":[{"id":"MC_QGOH_L1_02:FL-01","text":"Truth has no automatic dominion, yet terminal freedom depends on intelligence; the quantity and form of knowledge that produce sufficient affective force remain undetermined.","refs":["CL_QGOH_06:FL-01","CL_QGOH_07:FL-01"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"Tension","passages":[]},{"id":"MC_QGOH_L1_02:FL-02","text":"Rational concord is said to be necessary, but a State founded on threats cannot derive rational cooperation from security alone.","refs":["CL_QGOH_05:FL-06","CL_QGOH_06:FL-02","CL_QGOH_06:MRT-B03"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"Tension","passages":[]},{"id":"MC_QGOH_L1_02:FL-03","text":"The Body is temporal and a part of the perishing structure of the Mind, but its aptitude is said to produce greater participation in eternity without full demonstration of the mechanism of proportion.","refs":["CL_QGOH_07:XD-04","CL_QGOH_07:FL-05"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"Tension","passages":[]},{"id":"MC_QGOH_L1_02:FL-04","text":"The negation of finality coheres with the attractor of knowledge of God and blessedness only if the attractor is understood as an immanent cause, not as a preordained end of Nature.","refs":["CL_QGOH_05:FL-05","CL_QGOH_05:BSA-05","CL_QGOH_07:GA-10"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"Tension","passages":[]},{"id":"MC_QGOH_L1_02:FL-05","text":"Virtue remains independent of knowledge of eternity, although the highest figure of the wise is defined through eternal intellect and intellectual Love.","refs":["CL_QGOH_07:CGD-07","CL_QGOH_07:FL-06"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"Tension","passages":[]},{"id":"MC_QGOH_L1_02:FL-06","text":"Eternal intellectual Love is posited without beginning, but access to intuitive knowledge is narrated in the form of gradually acquired progression.","refs":["CL_QGOH_07:FL-04","CL_QGOH_07:GA-09"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"Tension","passages":[]},{"id":"MC_QGOH_L1_02:MP-01","text":"Desire constitutes a point of mutation where it passes from primitive affect into actual essence, active desire, and the principle of higher knowledge.","refs":["CL_QGOH_05:MP-01","CL_QGOH_06:GA-03"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[]},{"id":"MC_QGOH_L1_02:MP-02","text":"The State changes function where it passes from external coercion into a converter of security, cooperation, and the citizens' own causality.","refs":["CL_QGOH_06:RMD-03","CL_QGOH_06:XD-03"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[]},{"id":"MC_QGOH_L1_02:MP-03","text":"The Body changes function where it passes from the seat of duration and passion into a positive disposition toward action, knowledge, and intelligible eternity.","refs":["CL_QGOH_07:RMD-05","CL_QGOH_07:MP-03"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[]},{"id":"MC_QGOH_L1_02:MP-04","text":"Necessity changes meaning where it passes from an indicator of external excess into an object of acquiescence and the form of the freedom of the wise.","refs":["CL_QGOH_06:MP-03","CL_QGOH_07:RMD-06"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[]},{"id":"MC_QGOH_L1_02:MP-05","text":"Blessedness loses terminal status where it is inverted from the end of discipline into virtue itself and the cause of diminishing passions.","refs":["CL_QGOH_07:AS-B08","CL_QGOH_07:MP-04"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[]}],"original_name":"Internal Tensions, Mutation Points, and Fault Lines"},{"number":"16","name":"Hidden System Image","items":[{"id":"MC_QGOH_L1_02:HSI-01","text":"Reality is implicitly a network of finite forces, bodies, institutions, and ideas, in which causality is continuously distributed, composed, or captured.","refs":["CL_QGOH_05:HSI-01","CL_QGOH_06:HSI-01","CL_QGOH_07:HSI-01"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[]},{"id":"MC_QGOH_L1_02:HSI-02","text":"The subject is a permeable, bodily and socially composed node, which becomes free insofar as determining nexuses are more clearly expressed through it.","refs":["CL_QGOH_05:HSI-02","CL_QGOH_06:HSI-02","CL_QGOH_07:HSI-02"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[]},{"id":"MC_QGOH_L1_02:HSI-03","text":"Time is a bifurcated topology: duration distributes presence, memory, and passion, but eternity displays essence without succession or recollection.","refs":["CL_QGOH_05:HSI-03","CL_QGOH_06:HSI-03","CL_QGOH_07:HSI-03"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[]},{"id":"MC_QGOH_L1_02:HSI-04","text":"Navigation is possible as a spiral conversion of causal channels: conflict is transferred into cooperation, external fixation into intelligence, and fear of mortality into present blessedness.","refs":["CL_QGOH_05:HSI-04","CL_QGOH_06:HSI-04","CL_QGOH_07:HSI-05"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[]}],"original_name":"Hidden System Image"},{"number":"17","name":"Minimal Whole-Book Reconstruction Test","items":[{"id":"MC_QGOH_L1_02:MRT-B01","text":"The axioms of necessity, affect, conatus, social composition, aptitude of the Body, and intelligence suffice to restore the minimal machine of this level.","refs":["CL_QGOH_05:MRT-B01","CL_QGOH_06:MRT-B01","CL_QGOH_07:MRT-B01"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[]},{"id":"MC_QGOH_L1_02:MRT-B02","text":"The production of a stronger rational affect, the conversion of the coercive State, and the relation of the experience of eternity to the Mind losing memory are not yet sufficiently determined.","refs":["CL_QGOH_05:MRT-B03","CL_QGOH_06:MRT-B03","CL_QGOH_07:MRT-B03"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[]},{"id":"MC_QGOH_L1_02:MRT-B03","text":"The firmest structure is the circuit connecting conatus, cooperation, adequate idea, intellect of necessity, and increased power of knowing.","refs":["CL_QGOH_05:MRT-B02","CL_QGOH_06:MRT-B02","CL_QGOH_07:MRT-B02"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[]},{"id":"MC_QGOH_L1_02:MRT-B04","text":"The reconstruction preserves the true movement if it presents freedom as recursive amplification of power and temporal remedies as a disposition toward intuitive knowledge, not as instantaneous liberation.","refs":["CL_QGOH_05:MRT-B05","CL_QGOH_06:MRT-B04","CL_QGOH_07:MRT-B05"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[]},{"id":"MC_QGOH_L1_02:MRT-B05","text":"At this level the system coheres through the return of terms with altered function, but the nexus between demonstration, the experience of eternity, and the force of the image of the wise resists full reduction.","refs":["CL_QGOH_05:MRT-B04","CL_QGOH_07:MRT-B06"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[]}],"original_name":"Minimal Whole-Book Reconstruction Test"}],"revision":"MC_QGOH_L1_02@R001","source_variant_id":"canonical:MC_QGOH_L1_02:v1","source_language_code":"la","original_title":"Qgoh L1 02"}],"identity":{"layer":"MC","kicker":"Meta-cluster · Ethica","title":"Qgoh L1 02","context":"Chapters 13–21 · Part III - On the Origin and Nature of the Emotions · passage 5 · Part IV - Of Human Bondage, or the Strength of the Emotions · passage 1 · Part IV - Of Human Bondage, or the Strength of the Emotions · passage 2 · Part IV - Of Human Bondage, or the Strength of the Emotions · passage 3 · Part IV - Of Human Bondage, or the Strength of the Emotions · passage 4 · Part IV - Of Human Bondage, or the Strength of the Emotions · passage 5 · Part V - On the Power of the Intellect, or on Human Freedom · passage 1 · Part V - On the Power of the Intellect, or on Human Freedom · passage 2 · Part V - On the Power of the Intellect, or on Human Freedom · passage 3","original_title":"Qgoh L1 02","original_context":"Chapters 13–21 · Pars tertia - De origine et natura affectuum · passage 5 · Pars quarta - De servitute humana seu de affectuum viribus · passage 1 · Pars quarta - De servitute humana seu de affectuum viribus · passage 2 · Pars quarta - De servitute humana seu de affectuum viribus · passage 3 · Pars quarta - De servitute humana seu de affectuum viribus · passage 4 · Pars quarta - De servitute humana seu de affectuum viribus · passage 5 · Pars quinta - De potentia intellectus seu de libertate humana · passage 1 · Pars quinta - De potentia intellectus seu de libertate humana · passage 2 · Pars quinta - De potentia intellectus seu de libertate humana · passage 3"},"original_title":"Qgoh L1 02","title_language_code":"en"},{"id":"WB_QGOH","layer":"WB","title":"Ethics","order":1,"parents":["MC_QGOH_L1_01","MC_QGOH_L1_02"],"revision":"WB_QGOH@R002","sha256":"3a66f1b8e4bf427fe9acda2d8524af1f52a3306d87597b8996760c553e29594a","sections":[{"number":"1","name":"Book-Scale Argumental Spine","items":[{"id":"WB_QGOH:BAS-01","text":"Totum systema quaerit quomodo Natura necessario immanens subiecta finita, passionibus et causis externis implicata, libertatem communem atque intellectualem sine exemptione causali generare possit.","refs":["MC_QGOH_L1_01:AS-01","MC_QGOH_L1_02:AS-01"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[]},{"id":"WB_QGOH:BAS-02","text":"Thesis globalis libertatem constituit ut amplificationem causalitatis propriae qua expressio finita Naturae a determinatione partiali ad actionem corpoream, socialem et intellectualem transit.","refs":["MC_QGOH_L1_01:AS-02","MC_QGOH_L1_02:AS-02"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[]},{"id":"WB_QGOH:BAS-03","text":"Argumentatio geometrica simul genealogica, apophatica et reconstructiva fit: exterioritates fictas dissolvit, earum genesim naturalem explicat, deinde functiones ab illis occupatas intra immanentiam restituit.","refs":["MC_QGOH_L1_01:AS-03","MC_QGOH_L1_02:AS-03"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[]},{"id":"WB_QGOH:BAS-04","text":"Motus procedit a causalitate immanenti ad individuationem corpoream, imaginationem et conatum, inde per servitutem affectivam et compositionem civilem ad cognitionem intuitivam atque Amorem intellectualem.","refs":["MC_QGOH_L1_01:AS-04","MC_QGOH_L1_02:AS-04"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[]},{"id":"WB_QGOH:BAS-05","text":"Progressio est cumulativa et spiralis, quia quisque gradus mechanismos priorum retinet sed eorum functionem mutat, atque terminus in condiciones propriae productionis retroagit.","refs":["MC_QGOH_L1_01:AS-05","MC_QGOH_L1_02:AS-05"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[]},{"id":"WB_QGOH:BAS-06","text":"Necessitas ab clausura quae omnem exterioritatem excludit in medium individuationis, interventionis et tandem libertatis sapientis invertitur.","refs":["MC_QGOH_L1_01:AS-06","MC_QGOH_L1_02:GA-11"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[]},{"id":"WB_QGOH:BAS-07","text":"Clausura terminalis non finem remunerativum sed beatitudinem activam producit, quae ex termino apparente in causam permanentem virtutis et intelligentiae convertitur.","refs":["MC_QGOH_L1_02:AS-06","MC_QGOH_L1_02:GA-14"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[]},{"id":"WB_QGOH:BAS-08","text":"Systema immanenter clauditur, sed pons inter cognitionem causalem, transformationem affectuum, cooperationem civilem et stabilitatem beatificam demonstrative incompletus manet.","refs":["MC_QGOH_L1_01:AS-08","MC_QGOH_L1_02:BSA-05"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[]}],"original_name":"Book-Scale Argumental Spine"},{"number":"2","name":"Global Load-Bearing Axioms","items":[{"id":"WB_QGOH:GA-01","text":"[Persistent] Una Natura ex necessitate propria existit et agit ut causa immanens omnium rerum; nulla causalitas primaria extra eam stat.","refs":["MC_QGOH_L1_01:GA-01","MC_QGOH_L1_02:GA-01"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[]},{"id":"WB_QGOH:GA-02","text":"[Persistent] Omne finitum est modus, iar Mens et Corpus sunt expressiones eiusdem individui, non substantiae separatae aut causaliter transversae.","refs":["MC_QGOH_L1_01:GA-02","MC_QGOH_L1_01:GA-11"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[]},{"id":"WB_QGOH:GA-03","text":"[Mutating] Individuum est relatio corporea perseverans, cuius essentia actualis conatus est et cuius identitas per continuas compositiones conservatur.","refs":["MC_QGOH_L1_01:GA-04","MC_QGOH_L1_01:GA-07"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[]},{"id":"WB_QGOH:GA-04","text":"[Mutating] Affectio, idea affectionis et variatio potentiae sunt tres functiones unius occursus, qui secundum causalitatem partialem vel adaequatam passionem aut actionem producit.","refs":["MC_QGOH_L1_01:RMD-05","MC_QGOH_L1_02:GA-02"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[]},{"id":"WB_QGOH:GA-05","text":"[Persistent] Adaequatio causalitatem activam designat, inadaequatio autem dependentiam qua effectus singularis tantum partialiter per propriam naturam explicatur.","refs":["MC_QGOH_L1_01:GA-06","MC_QGOH_L1_02:GA-02"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[]},{"id":"WB_QGOH:GA-06","text":"[Mutating] Imaginatio est mechanismus naturalis praesentificationis et associationis, qui simul capturam affectivam, conservationem conatus et materiam reordinationis rationalis praebet.","refs":["MC_QGOH_L1_01:RMD-06","MC_QGOH_L1_02:RMD-10"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[]},{"id":"WB_QGOH:GA-07","text":"[Emergent] Bonum, malum et perfectio sunt indices comparativi relationis inter appetitum, compositionem et potentiam finitam, non proprietates transcendentes rerum.","refs":["MC_QGOH_L1_01:GA-08","MC_QGOH_L1_02:GA-10"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[]},{"id":"WB_QGOH:GA-08","text":"[Mutating] Subiectum socialiter distribuitur: imitatio affectiva potest conflictum amplificare, sed eadem dependentia per Civitatem et cooperationem in potentiam communem converti potest.","refs":["MC_QGOH_L1_01:GA-09","MC_QGOH_L1_02:GA-09"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[]},{"id":"WB_QGOH:GA-09","text":"[Terminal] Libertas est gradus causalitatis adaequatae intra necessitatem, non indifferentia, decretum absolutum aut interruptio nexus naturalis.","refs":["MC_QGOH_L1_01:GA-10","MC_QGOH_L1_02:GA-04"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[]},{"id":"WB_QGOH:GA-10","text":"[Mutating] Cognitio efficax repraesentatio iners esse nequit, sed affectum, ordinem Corporis, institutiones vel facultatem ulterius intelligendi causaliter mutare debet.","refs":["MC_QGOH_L1_02:GA-05","MC_QGOH_L1_02:CGD-05"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[]},{"id":"WB_QGOH:GA-11","text":"[Mutating] Corpus ex obiecto affectionum in infrastructuram materialem libertatis et instrumentum ampliandae potentiae Mentis mutatur.","refs":["MC_QGOH_L1_02:GA-07","MC_QGOH_L1_02:RMD-04"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[]},{"id":"WB_QGOH:GA-12","text":"[Emergent] Civitas est convertor inter servitutem et libertatem quatenus coercitionem et securitatem in iura, cooperationem et maiorem causalitatem civium traducit.","refs":["MC_QGOH_L1_02:CGD-06","MC_QGOH_L1_02:XD-06"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[]},{"id":"WB_QGOH:GA-13","text":"[Emergent] Tempus stratificatur in necessitatem aeternam, durationem conatus, praesentificationem imaginativam et intellectionem essentiae sine successione.","refs":["MC_QGOH_L1_01:GA-12","MC_QGOH_L1_02:GA-13"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[]},{"id":"WB_QGOH:GA-14","text":"[Attractor] Intelligibilitas causalis, cognitio Dei, acquiescentia et Amor intellectualis constituunt attractorem ascensionalem potentiae activae et non-rivalis.","refs":["MC_QGOH_L1_01:GA-13","MC_QGOH_L1_02:GA-12"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[]},{"id":"WB_QGOH:GA-15","text":"[Terminal] Beatitudo non est praemium futurum, sed exercitium virtutis quod, ex fine apparente, in machinam sui conservationis convertitur.","refs":["MC_QGOH_L1_02:GA-14","MC_QGOH_L1_02:XD-09"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[]}],"original_name":"Global Load-Bearing Axioms"},{"number":"3","name":"Recurrence, Mutation, and Displacement Matrix","items":[{"id":"WB_QGOH:RMD-01","text":"Substantia identitatem ontologicam retinet, sed post constitutam immanentiam e proscenio recedit et terminaliter ut ecologia multiscalaris potentiae reapparet.","refs":["MC_QGOH_L1_01:RMD-01","MC_QGOH_L1_02:BSD-01"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[]},{"id":"WB_QGOH:RMD-02","text":"Necessitas a clausura modalitatis ad genesim passionis, ordinationem affectuum, acquiescentiam et formam libertatis intellectualis migrat.","refs":["MC_QGOH_L1_01:RMD-03","MC_QGOH_L1_02:RMD-07"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[]},{"id":"WB_QGOH:RMD-03","text":"Corpus a modo determinato in individuum relationale, substratum variationis potentiae, infrastructuram materialem et instrumentum intellectuale transformatur.","refs":["MC_QGOH_L1_01:RMD-04","MC_QGOH_L1_02:RMD-04"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[]},{"id":"WB_QGOH:RMD-04","text":"Imaginatio ab errore generali in mechanismum neutralem et socialem mutatur, sed sub specie aeternitatis functio eius memorativa terminaliter exhauritur.","refs":["MC_QGOH_L1_01:RMD-06","MC_QGOH_L1_02:RMD-10"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[]},{"id":"WB_QGOH:RMD-05","text":"Conatus ab essentia perseverandi ad selectionem imaginum, virtutem rationalem, cooperationem communem et actionem intellectualem amplificatur.","refs":["MC_QGOH_L1_01:RMD-09","MC_QGOH_L1_02:GA-03"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[]},{"id":"WB_QGOH:RMD-06","text":"Socialitas redit functione mutata: affectus alienus reciprocitatem conflictivam excitat, Civitas eam coercet, iar cooperatio rationalis in amplificationem communem vertit.","refs":["MC_QGOH_L1_01:RLE-06","MC_QGOH_L1_02:RMD-05"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[]},{"id":"WB_QGOH:RMD-07","text":"Valor ab exemplari externo ad indicem potentiae migrat, deinde ut instrumentum navigationis finitae adhibetur et in beatitudine perfectiore recedit.","refs":["MC_QGOH_L1_01:RMD-08","MC_QGOH_L1_02:RMD-08"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[]},{"id":"WB_QGOH:RMD-08","text":"Libertas inversionem duplicem patitur: primum indifferentiam amittit, deinde ex causalitate singulari in ecologiam corporalem, civilem atque intellectualem extenditur.","refs":["MC_QGOH_L1_01:RMD-07","MC_QGOH_L1_02:RMD-06"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[]},{"id":"WB_QGOH:RMD-09","text":"Cognitio Dei ab attractore intellectionis ad Amorem intellectualem progreditur, dum beatitudo statum terminalem amittit et fit causa activa totius progressionis.","refs":["MC_QGOH_L1_02:RMD-09","MC_QGOH_L1_02:MP-05"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[]}],"original_name":"Recurrence, Mutation, and Displacement Matrix"},{"number":"4","name":"Lower-Layer vs Current-Level Distinction","items":[{"id":"WB_QGOH:CGD-01","text":"Demonstrationes de unicitate substantiae et distinctiones modorum inferiores architecturam praebent; hoc gradu earum functio est clausura exterioritatis per totum campum ethicum servata.","refs":["MC_QGOH_L1_01:CGD-01","MC_QGOH_L1_01:CGD-02"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[]},{"id":"WB_QGOH:CGD-02","text":"Taxonomiae affectuum et casus actuum inferiores manent; gradus totius libri eos in leges mutationis potentiae, causalitatis et compositionis comprimit.","refs":["MC_QGOH_L1_01:CGD-04","MC_QGOH_L1_02:CGD-01"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[]},{"id":"WB_QGOH:CGD-03","text":"Vestigia corporalia et concursus imaginum sunt mechanismi locales; sursum fiunt infrastructura qua memoria, expectatio et praesentia affectiva durationem distribuunt.","refs":["MC_QGOH_L1_01:CGD-03","MC_QGOH_L1_02:CGD-02"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[]},{"id":"WB_QGOH:CGD-04","text":"Aestimatio actuum singularium ex specie externa obsolescit, quia totius libri genealogia valorem ad causam, proportionem actionis et effectum in potentiam transfert.","refs":["MC_QGOH_L1_02:CGD-03","MC_QGOH_L1_02:DBN-04"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[]},{"id":"WB_QGOH:CGD-05","text":"Parallelismus attributorum ontologiam cum psychologia coniungit, sed manet commissura inferior cuius congruentia positiva ultra identitatem expressionis non explicatur.","refs":["MC_QGOH_L1_01:CGD-06","MC_QGOH_L1_01:FL-03"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[]},{"id":"WB_QGOH:CGD-06","text":"Nutritio, labor, lex et Civitas sunt media localia; hoc gradu in condiciones materiales distribuendae causalitatis propriae absorbentur.","refs":["MC_QGOH_L1_02:CGD-06","MC_QGOH_L1_02:MME-03"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[]},{"id":"WB_QGOH:CGD-07","text":"Beatitudo, quae in priore structura attractor nondum completus erat, clausuram activam accipit, quamquam nexus affectus rationalis, cooperationis et intuitionis adhuc non plene demonstratur.","refs":["MC_QGOH_L1_01:CGD-08","MC_QGOH_L1_02:BSA-05"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[]}],"original_name":"Lower-Layer vs Current-Level Distinction"},{"number":"5","name":"Global Apophatic Structure","items":[{"id":"WB_QGOH:GNX-01","text":"Nullus locus extra Naturam admittitur ex quo causae, fines, normae aut libertas originem accipiant.","refs":["MC_QGOH_L1_01:GNX-01","MC_QGOH_L1_02:GNX-02"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"Negative","passages":[]},{"id":"WB_QGOH:GNX-02","text":"Nulla actio humana seriem causarum interrumpit; libertas arbitrii et actus incausatus excluduntur.","refs":["MC_QGOH_L1_01:GNX-02","MC_QGOH_L1_02:GNX-01"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"Negative","passages":[]},{"id":"WB_QGOH:GNX-03","text":"Mens et Corpus neque substantiae separatae neque adversarii ethici sunt; libertas Mentis per aptitudinem Corporis producitur.","refs":["MC_QGOH_L1_01:GNX-04","MC_QGOH_L1_02:GNX-07"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"Negative","passages":[]},{"id":"WB_QGOH:GNX-04","text":"Nullum tribunal morale transcendens aut finis externus ordinem Naturae, valorem rerum vel rectitudinem actionis antecedit.","refs":["MC_QGOH_L1_01:GNX-06","MC_QGOH_L1_02:GNX-02"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"Negative","passages":[]},{"id":"WB_QGOH:GNX-05","text":"Subiectum nec sibi transparens nec affective sui iuris nec relationibus externis sufficiens est.","refs":["MC_QGOH_L1_01:GNX-05","MC_QGOH_L1_02:GNX-04"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"Negative","passages":[]},{"id":"WB_QGOH:GNX-06","text":"Cognitio causaliter iners passionem superare non potest; contradictio logica sine mutatione affectus et Corporis insufficiens est.","refs":["MC_QGOH_L1_01:DBN-02","MC_QGOH_L1_02:GNX-03"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"Negative","passages":[]},{"id":"WB_QGOH:GNX-07","text":"Aeternitas neque continuatio memoriae personalis neque spatium remunerationis post mortem est.","refs":["MC_QGOH_L1_02:GNX-08"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"Negative","passages":[]}],"original_name":"Global Apophatic Structure"},{"number":"6","name":"Deep Book Apophatics","items":[{"id":"WB_QGOH:DBN-01","text":"Falsum esse oportet ontologiam, cognitionem, politicam et ethicam ordines causaliter separatos constituere, quia omnes distributiones unius immanentiae sunt.","refs":["MC_QGOH_L1_01:GA-03","MC_QGOH_L1_02:BSD-01"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"Negative","passages":[]},{"id":"WB_QGOH:DBN-02","text":"Falsum esse oportet errorem et passionem irruptiones irrationales extra Naturam esse; eorum ignorantia mechanismis corporalibus, imaginativis et socialibus stabilitur.","refs":["MC_QGOH_L1_01:DBN-05","MC_QGOH_L1_02:RLE-01"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"Negative","passages":[]},{"id":"WB_QGOH:DBN-03","text":"Falsum esse oportet dependentiam finiti meram privationem esse, quia eadem relatio quae servitutem permittit mutuum auxilium et potentiam communem generat.","refs":["MC_QGOH_L1_02:GA-06","MC_QGOH_L1_02:XD-03"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"Negative","passages":[]},{"id":"WB_QGOH:DBN-04","text":"Falsum esse oportet speciem exteriorem actus eius valorem continere, cum idem opus passionem, rationem vel virtutem exprimere possit.","refs":["MC_QGOH_L1_02:DBN-04","MC_QGOH_L1_02:CGD-03"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"Negative","passages":[]},{"id":"WB_QGOH:DBN-05","text":"Falsum esse oportet coercitionem et securitatem per se cooperationem rationalem efficere; regimen metus circuitum servitutis recreare potest.","refs":["MC_QGOH_L1_02:DBN-05","MC_QGOH_L1_02:RLE-04"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"Negative","passages":[]},{"id":"WB_QGOH:DBN-06","text":"Falsum esse oportet beatitudinem mercedem futuram aut virtutem calculatione praemii sustentatam esse.","refs":["MC_QGOH_L1_02:GA-14","MC_QGOH_L1_02:GNX-08"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"Negative","passages":[]}],"original_name":"Deep Book Apophatics"},{"number":"7","name":"Super-Apophatics of the Whole","items":[{"id":"WB_QGOH:BSA-01","text":"Absentia suprema est exterioritas absoluta, locus unde Naturae totalitas sine participatione causali spectari aut gubernari posset.","refs":["MC_QGOH_L1_01:BSA-01","MC_QGOH_L1_02:BSA-01"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"Negative","passages":[]},{"id":"WB_QGOH:BSA-02","text":"Origo absoluta cogitationis, appetitus vel actionis ontologice indisponibilis est; omne initium nexus iam constituti modulatio est.","refs":["MC_QGOH_L1_01:BSA-02","MC_QGOH_L1_02:GNX-01"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"Negative","passages":[]},{"id":"WB_QGOH:BSA-03","text":"Praesentia immediata rei aut sui, corporali et historica mediatione carens, excluditur una cum prospectu causas totius sine participatione cognoscente.","refs":["MC_QGOH_L1_01:BSA-04","MC_QGOH_L1_02:BSA-01"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"Negative","passages":[]},{"id":"WB_QGOH:BSA-04","text":"Systema circum impossibilitatem liberationis instantaneae et absolutae ordinatur: veritas non statim fit potentia, iar homo finitus numquam omnibus causis externis vacat.","refs":["MC_QGOH_L1_02:BSA-02","MC_QGOH_L1_02:BSA-03"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"Negative","passages":[]}],"original_name":"Super-Apophatics of the Whole"},{"number":"8","name":"Cross-Unit Derivative Propositions","items":[{"id":"WB_QGOH:XD-01","text":"Quia ontologia iam veritatem et actionem ut gradus unius integrationis causalis constituit, remedia ethica non secundum imperium additum sed secundum redistributionem eiusdem causalitatis operantur.","refs":["WB_QGOH:GA-01","WB_QGOH:GA-05","WB_QGOH:GA-09","MC_QGOH_L1_01:BSD-01","MC_QGOH_L1_02:BSD-01"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"From","passages":[]},{"id":"WB_QGOH:XD-02","text":"Cum imaginatio simul capturam et conservationem sustineat, veritas vim practicam obtinet tantum ubi imagines, affectus et dispositiones corporales ab intra reordinat.","refs":["WB_QGOH:GA-06","WB_QGOH:GA-10","WB_QGOH:GA-11","MC_QGOH_L1_01:XD-08","MC_QGOH_L1_02:XD-02"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"From","passages":[]},{"id":"WB_QGOH:XD-03","text":"Individuum relationale et subiectum metastabile efficiunt ut libertas sit ecologia distributa corporis, societatis et intellectus, non possessio subiecti clausi.","refs":["WB_QGOH:GA-03","WB_QGOH:GA-08","WB_QGOH:GA-09","WB_QGOH:GA-11","MC_QGOH_L1_01:XD-04","MC_QGOH_L1_02:XD-01"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"From","passages":[]},{"id":"WB_QGOH:XD-04","text":"Normativitas est cartographia temporaria compositionum: bonum et malum necessaria sunt navigationi finitae, sed opere beatitudinis peracto functionem amittunt.","refs":["WB_QGOH:GA-07","WB_QGOH:GA-15","MC_QGOH_L1_01:XD-03","MC_QGOH_L1_02:XD-07"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"From","passages":[]},{"id":"WB_QGOH:XD-05","text":"Politica est conversio circuituum affectivorum, qua circulatio imaginum et attributionum ex reciprocitate conflictiva in cooperationem potentiam amplificantem traducitur.","refs":["WB_QGOH:GA-08","WB_QGOH:GA-12","MC_QGOH_L1_01:BSD-04","MC_QGOH_L1_02:XD-06"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"From","passages":[]},{"id":"WB_QGOH:XD-06","text":"Praxis ethica non in tempore uno progreditur, sed praesentiam imaginativam, durationem deliberativam et aeternitatem intelligibilem sine earum reductione coordinat.","refs":["WB_QGOH:GA-13","MC_QGOH_L1_01:BSD-05","MC_QGOH_L1_02:XD-08"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"From","passages":[]},{"id":"WB_QGOH:XD-07","text":"Generositas et Amor intellectualis sunt duae scalae potentiae non-rivalis, quarum prima relationes humanas componit et altera ipsam immanentiam reflexive intelligit.","refs":["WB_QGOH:GA-08","WB_QGOH:GA-14","WB_QGOH:GA-15","MC_QGOH_L1_01:XD-06","MC_QGOH_L1_02:RLE-07"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"From","passages":[]},{"id":"WB_QGOH:XD-08","text":"Transitus a cognitione ad stabilitatem affectivam requirit affectum rationalem, Civitatem cooperativam et dispositionem corporalem, sed nexus causaliter sufficiens horum mediorum incompletus manet.","refs":["WB_QGOH:GA-10","WB_QGOH:GA-11","WB_QGOH:GA-12","WB_QGOH:GA-15","MC_QGOH_L1_01:FL-06","MC_QGOH_L1_02:BSA-05"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"From","passages":[]}],"original_name":"Cross-Unit Derivative Propositions"},{"number":"9","name":"Book-Scale Super-Derivatives","items":[{"id":"WB_QGOH:BSD-01","text":"Realitas fit ecologia sui modulationis, in qua substantia, corpora, affectus, institutiones et ideae sunt altitudines unius causalitatis immanentis.","refs":["MC_QGOH_L1_01:BSD-01","MC_QGOH_L1_02:BSD-01"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"Composite","passages":[]},{"id":"WB_QGOH:BSD-02","text":"Subiectum est proportio multiscalaris causalitatum, cuius agentia crescit vel decrescit secundum modum quo nexus determinantes per ipsum exprimuntur.","refs":["MC_QGOH_L1_01:BSD-02","MC_QGOH_L1_02:XD-10"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"Composite","passages":[]},{"id":"WB_QGOH:BSD-03","text":"Epistemologia fit technologia causalis, quia intelligere significat condiciones compositionis, affectionis et ulterioris cognitionis reordinare.","refs":["MC_QGOH_L1_01:BSD-06","MC_QGOH_L1_02:BSD-03"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"Composite","passages":[]},{"id":"WB_QGOH:BSD-04","text":"Politica fit distributio condicionum causalitatis propriae, non administratio voluntatum iam formatarum aut mera suppressio conflictus.","refs":["MC_QGOH_L1_01:BSD-04","MC_QGOH_L1_02:BSD-04"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"Composite","passages":[]},{"id":"WB_QGOH:BSD-05","text":"Ethica est conversio capturarum necessariarum in compositiones non-rivales, qua finitas non tollitur sed potentiam cognoscendi et agendi auget.","refs":["MC_QGOH_L1_01:BSD-03","MC_QGOH_L1_02:BSD-07"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"Composite","passages":[]},{"id":"WB_QGOH:BSD-06","text":"Historia subiecti non in tempore homogeno continetur, sed ex coordinatione durationis, praesentificationis, deliberationis et intellectionis aeternae constat.","refs":["MC_QGOH_L1_01:BSD-05","MC_QGOH_L1_02:BSD-05"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"Composite","passages":[]},{"id":"WB_QGOH:BSD-07","text":"Methodus geometrica performative naturalizat: negationes transcendentes in geneses causales resolvit et geneses in circuitus positivos libertatis convertit.","refs":["MC_QGOH_L1_01:BSD-07","MC_QGOH_L1_02:ALT-B05"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"Composite","passages":[]}],"original_name":"Book-Scale Super-Derivatives"},{"number":"10","name":"Recursive Loops, Escalation Paths, and Conceptual Attractors","items":[{"id":"WB_QGOH:RLE-01","text":"Immanentia psychologiam naturalizat; psychologia fictionem exterioritatis explicat; explicata exterioritas vicissim clausuram immanentiae confirmat.","refs":["MC_QGOH_L1_01:RLE-01","MC_QGOH_L1_01:RLE-03"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[]},{"id":"WB_QGOH:RLE-02","text":"Affectio imaginem generat, imago Cupiditatem dirigit, Cupiditas corpus et relationes mutat, iar mutatum corpus novas affectiones sub alia proportione recipit.","refs":["MC_QGOH_L1_01:RLE-02","MC_QGOH_L1_02:RLE-01"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[]},{"id":"WB_QGOH:RLE-03","text":"Adaequatio actionem auget, actio clariorem causae intellectionem firmat, iar claritas aucta ulteriorem passivitatem minuit.","refs":["MC_QGOH_L1_01:RLE-04","MC_QGOH_L1_02:RLE-02"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[]},{"id":"WB_QGOH:RLE-04","text":"Affectus reciprocus conflictum amplificat; coercitio securitatem inducere potest, sed circuitus ad servitutem regreditur si metus fundamentum institutionis manet.","refs":["MC_QGOH_L1_01:RLE-06","MC_QGOH_L1_02:RLE-04"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"Tension","passages":[]},{"id":"WB_QGOH:RLE-05","text":"Convenientia rationalis cooperationem auget, cooperatio potentiam singularem amplificat, iar potentia aucta maiorem communicationem boni non-rivalis efficit.","refs":["MC_QGOH_L1_02:RLE-03","MC_QGOH_L1_02:RLE-07"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[]},{"id":"WB_QGOH:RLE-06","text":"Oeconomia Corporis libertatem cognitionis sustinet, cognitio aptitudinem agendi auget, iar corpus aptius plures ideas adaequatas recipit.","refs":["MC_QGOH_L1_02:RLE-05"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[]},{"id":"WB_QGOH:RLE-07","text":"Causalitas adaequata est attractor medius, beatitudo attractor terminalis; prior transitum ordinat, altera transitum in circuitum sui conservationis claudit.","refs":["MC_QGOH_L1_01:RLE-08","MC_QGOH_L1_02:RLE-08"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[]}],"original_name":"Recursive Loops, Escalation Paths, and Conceptual Attractors"},{"number":"11","name":"Formal Generative Schema of the Whole Book","items":[{"id":"WB_QGOH:FGS-B01","text":"Natura necessario existens → exclusio exterioritatis → causalitas immanens → modi finiti causaliter dependentes.","refs":["MC_QGOH_L1_01:FGS-B01"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[]},{"id":"WB_QGOH:FGS-B02","text":"Modus finitus → individuum corporeum relationale → idem processus sub Cogitatione et Extensione → Mens ut idea Corporis.","refs":["MC_QGOH_L1_01:GA-04","MC_QGOH_L1_01:GA-11"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[]},{"id":"WB_QGOH:FGS-B03","text":"Occursus corporalis → affectio → idea affectionis → vestigium et praesentificatio → imaginatio perspectivalis.","refs":["MC_QGOH_L1_01:RMD-05","MC_QGOH_L1_01:GA-05"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[]},{"id":"WB_QGOH:FGS-B04","text":"Conatus → Cupiditas → mutatio potentiae → Laetitia aut Tristitia → actio vel passio secundum adaequationem causae.","refs":["MC_QGOH_L1_01:GA-07","MC_QGOH_L1_01:GA-06"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[]},{"id":"WB_QGOH:FGS-B05","text":"Imaginatio alterius → imitatio affectus → reciprocitas → conflictus, rivalitas aut compositio socialis.","refs":["MC_QGOH_L1_01:RLE-06","MC_QGOH_L1_02:GA-09"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[]},{"id":"WB_QGOH:FGS-B06","text":"Affectus contrarius + aptitudo Corporis + convenientia rationalis + auxilia communia → maior causalitas propria.","refs":["MC_QGOH_L1_02:XD-01","MC_QGOH_L1_02:RLE-03"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[]},{"id":"WB_QGOH:FGS-B07","text":"Ratio adaequata → cupiditas cognitionis tertii generis → intuitio essentiae singularis → Amor intellectualis.","refs":["MC_QGOH_L1_02:FGS-B07"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[]},{"id":"WB_QGOH:FGS-B08","text":"Amor intellectualis + laetitia activa → beatitudo → augmentum intellectus → imminutio passionum → stabilitas libertatis.","refs":["MC_QGOH_L1_02:RLE-08"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[]}],"original_name":"Formal Generative Schema of the Whole Book"},{"number":"12","name":"Whole-Book Argument Flow Map","items":[{"id":"WB_QGOH:AFM-B01","text":"Immanentia → individuum corporeum → Mens → affectio → imaginatio → conatus → mutatio potentiae → servitus affectiva.","refs":["MC_QGOH_L1_01:AFM-B01","MC_QGOH_L1_02:AFM-B01"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[]},{"id":"WB_QGOH:AFM-B02","text":"Ignorantia causarum → fines et valores proiecti → fixatio causae externae → imminutio potentiae → ulterior ignorantia.","refs":["MC_QGOH_L1_01:AFM-B02","MC_QGOH_L1_02:RLE-01"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[]},{"id":"WB_QGOH:AFM-B03","text":"Imitatio conflictiva → Civitas coercitiva → securitas → convenientia rationalis → cooperatio → potentia communis.","refs":["MC_QGOH_L1_02:XD-06","MC_QGOH_L1_02:RLE-03"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[]},{"id":"WB_QGOH:AFM-B04","text":"Cognitio causalis → affectus activus → causalitas propria → intuitio → Amor intellectualis → beatitudo ut exercitium virtutis.","refs":["MC_QGOH_L1_01:BSD-03","MC_QGOH_L1_02:GA-14"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[]}],"original_name":"Whole-Book Argument Flow Map"},{"number":"13","name":"Macro Map-Effects","items":[{"id":"WB_QGOH:MME-01","text":"Oppositio Dei et mundi corruit, iar theologia in ontologiam reflexivam causalitatis immanentis convertitur.","refs":["MC_QGOH_L1_01:MME-01","MC_QGOH_L1_02:MME-06"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[]},{"id":"WB_QGOH:MME-02","text":"Oppositio Mentis et Corporis cedit duplici expressioni unius individui, cuius oeconomia corporea cognitionem constitutive condicionat.","refs":["MC_QGOH_L1_01:MME-02","MC_QGOH_L1_02:MME-03"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[]},{"id":"WB_QGOH:MME-03","text":"Cognitio et agentia in unam scalam causalitatis coalescunt, unde epistemologia statum technologiae transformationis accipit.","refs":["MC_QGOH_L1_01:MME-03","MC_QGOH_L1_02:BSD-03"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[]},{"id":"WB_QGOH:MME-04","text":"Moralitas actuum exteriorum corruit, iar valor fit index causae, compositionis et mutationis potentiae.","refs":["MC_QGOH_L1_01:MME-04","MC_QGOH_L1_02:MME-02"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[]},{"id":"WB_QGOH:MME-05","text":"Socialitas non iam aggregatio subiectorum praeconstitutorum est, iar Civitas non opponitur libertati nisi regimen metus causalitatem civium minuit.","refs":["MC_QGOH_L1_01:MME-05","MC_QGOH_L1_02:MME-04"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[]},{"id":"WB_QGOH:MME-06","text":"Tempus reclassificatur a successione homogena in pluralitatem durationis, praesentiae affectivae et aeternitatis intelligibilis.","refs":["MC_QGOH_L1_01:MME-06","MC_QGOH_L1_02:BSD-05"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[]},{"id":"WB_QGOH:MME-07","text":"Interventio transit ab imperio et iudicio ad analysin geneticam, iar lectio debet mutationes functionales terminorum potius quam identitatem nominalem sequi.","refs":["MC_QGOH_L1_01:MME-07","MC_QGOH_L1_02:MME-07"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[]}],"original_name":"Macro Map-Effects"},{"number":"14","name":"Altitude Integration","items":[{"id":"WB_QGOH:ALT-B01","text":"In gradu superficiei Deus, Natura, Mens, Corpus, affectus, lex, mors et beatitudo sunt obiecta declarata per quae quaestio servitutis et libertatis tractatur.","refs":["MC_QGOH_L1_01:ALT-B01","MC_QGOH_L1_02:ALT-B01"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[]},{"id":"WB_QGOH:ALT-B02","text":"In gradu structurali obiecta distribuuntur per causalitatem immanentem, individuationem relationalem, mediationem socialem, conatum et proportiones causae partialis vel adaequatae.","refs":["MC_QGOH_L1_01:ALT-B02","MC_QGOH_L1_02:ALT-B02"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[]},{"id":"WB_QGOH:ALT-B03","text":"In gradu metastrategico quaestio ab electione et condemnatione ad generationem affectuum activorum, compositionem potentiae et condiciones interventionis transfertur.","refs":["MC_QGOH_L1_01:ALT-B03","MC_QGOH_L1_02:ALT-B03"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[]},{"id":"WB_QGOH:ALT-B04","text":"In gradu ontologico et temporali realitas est productio necessaria, subiectum pars relationalis Naturae, iar duratio imaginativa ab aeternitate intelligibili distinguitur.","refs":["MC_QGOH_L1_01:ALT-B04","MC_QGOH_L1_02:ALT-B04"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[]},{"id":"WB_QGOH:ALT-B05","text":"In gradu methodologico et performativo deductio genetica exterioritates dissolvit et negationem ipsam in circuitum positivum libertatis convertit.","refs":["MC_QGOH_L1_01:ALT-B05","MC_QGOH_L1_02:ALT-B05"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[]}],"original_name":"Altitude Integration"},{"number":"15","name":"Internal Tensions, Mutation Points, and Fault Lines","items":[{"id":"WB_QGOH:FL-01","text":"Attributa per se concipiuntur, sed earum distinctio cum unitate substantiae per exclusionem pluralitatis potius quam per coordinationem positivam stabilitur.","refs":["MC_QGOH_L1_01:FL-01"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"Tension","passages":[]},{"id":"WB_QGOH:FL-02","text":"Causalitas divina omnes modos determinat, sed mediatio inter productivitatem aeternam et series finitas immediate causantes nimis compressa manet.","refs":["MC_QGOH_L1_01:FL-02"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"Tension","passages":[]},{"id":"WB_QGOH:FL-03","text":"Parallelismus identitatem processus Mentis et Corporis servat, sed principium positivum congruentiae ultra duplicem expressionem non explicat.","refs":["MC_QGOH_L1_01:FL-03"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"Tension","passages":[]},{"id":"WB_QGOH:FL-04","text":"Falsitas privatio cognitionis dicitur, quamquam condiciones erroris positive producuntur; statutum normativum et genesis causalis eodem vocabulo non plene reconciliantur.","refs":["MC_QGOH_L1_01:FL-04","MC_QGOH_L1_01:DBN-05"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"Tension","passages":[]},{"id":"WB_QGOH:FL-05","text":"Veritas imperium automaticum non habet, sed libertas terminalis ab intelligentia pendet; limen quo intellecta necessitas vim affectivam sufficientem acquirit indeterminatum manet.","refs":["MC_QGOH_L1_02:FL-01","MC_QGOH_L1_01:FL-06"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"Tension","passages":[]},{"id":"WB_QGOH:FL-06","text":"Concordia rationalis finem politicum ordinat, sed Civitas metu fundata cooperationem ex securitate sola deducere nequit.","refs":["MC_QGOH_L1_02:FL-02","MC_QGOH_L1_02:RLE-04"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"Tension","passages":[]},{"id":"WB_QGOH:FL-07","text":"Corpus temporale maiorem participationem intellectionis aeternae parare dicitur, sed proportio inter aptitudinem corporalem et aeternitatem Mentis non plene demonstratur.","refs":["MC_QGOH_L1_02:FL-03","MC_QGOH_L1_02:CGD-07"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"Tension","passages":[]},{"id":"WB_QGOH:FL-08","text":"Negatio finalitatis cum attractore beatitudinis cohaeret tantum si beatitudo causa immanens progressionis, non finis Naturae praestitutus, intelligitur.","refs":["MC_QGOH_L1_02:FL-04","MC_QGOH_L1_02:XD-09"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"Tension","passages":[]},{"id":"WB_QGOH:MP-01","text":"Necessitas punctum mutationis attingit ubi ex clausura contingentiae et signo dominationis in obiectum acquiescentiae atque formam libertatis transit.","refs":["MC_QGOH_L1_01:RMD-03","MC_QGOH_L1_02:MP-04"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"Tension","passages":[]},{"id":"WB_QGOH:MP-02","text":"Imaginatio punctum mutationis attingit ubi ex praeiudicio fit mechanismus neutralis et conservativus, deinde sub aeternitate operationem memorativam amittit.","refs":["MC_QGOH_L1_01:MP-03","MC_QGOH_L1_02:RMD-10"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"Tension","passages":[]},{"id":"WB_QGOH:MP-03","text":"Corpus punctum mutationis attingit ubi ex obiecto Mentis et sede passionis in dispositionem positivam ad actionem, cognitionem et intellectum aeternitatis transit.","refs":["MC_QGOH_L1_01:MP-02","MC_QGOH_L1_02:MP-03"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"Tension","passages":[]},{"id":"WB_QGOH:MP-04","text":"Adaequatio punctum mutationis attingit ubi ex qualitate veritatis in affectum activum et causalitatem transformativam convertitur.","refs":["MC_QGOH_L1_01:MP-04","MC_QGOH_L1_02:CGD-05"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"Tension","passages":[]},{"id":"WB_QGOH:MP-05","text":"Civitas punctum mutationis attingit ubi exterior coercitio in securitatem, cooperationem et causalitatem propriam civium convertitur.","refs":["MC_QGOH_L1_02:MP-02"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"Tension","passages":[]},{"id":"WB_QGOH:MP-06","text":"Beatitudo punctum mutationis terminale constituit ubi statum finis amittit et fit exercitium virtutis atque causa conservandae libertatis.","refs":["MC_QGOH_L1_02:MP-05","MC_QGOH_L1_02:GA-14"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"Tension","passages":[]}],"original_name":"Internal Tensions, Mutation Points, and Fault Lines"},{"number":"16","name":"Hidden System Image","items":[{"id":"WB_QGOH:HSI-01","text":"Realitas est machina immanens et rete virium, in quo causalitas non exit ex Natura sed inter corpora, institutiones et ideas perpetuo redistribuitur.","refs":["MC_QGOH_L1_01:HSI-01","MC_QGOH_L1_02:HSI-01"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[]},{"id":"WB_QGOH:HSI-02","text":"Subiectum est vortex corporeus et nodus permeabilis, cuius autonomia non clausura sed clarior expressio nexuum per ipsum agentium est.","refs":["MC_QGOH_L1_01:HSI-02","MC_QGOH_L1_02:HSI-02"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[]},{"id":"WB_QGOH:HSI-03","text":"Tempus est machina stratificata et topologia bifurcata, in qua duratio mutationes memorativas distribuit, aeternitas autem essentiam sine successione exhibet.","refs":["MC_QGOH_L1_01:HSI-03","MC_QGOH_L1_02:HSI-03"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[]},{"id":"WB_QGOH:HSI-04","text":"Captura est circuitus quo causa externa potentiam minuit, ignorantiam stabilit et speciem libertatis sub ipsa dependentia producit.","refs":["MC_QGOH_L1_01:HSI-04","MC_QGOH_L1_02:RLE-01"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[]},{"id":"WB_QGOH:HSI-05","text":"Navigatio possibilis non est fuga e machina, sed conversio spiralis canalium causalium qua conflictus, fixatio externa et metus in cooperationem, intelligentiam et beatitudinem transferuntur.","refs":["MC_QGOH_L1_01:HSI-05","MC_QGOH_L1_02:HSI-04"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[]}],"original_name":"Hidden System Image"},{"number":"17","name":"Minimal Whole-Book Reconstruction Test","items":[{"id":"WB_QGOH:MRT-B01","text":"Axiomata immanentiae, individuationis corporeae, conatus, imaginationis, adaequationis, compositionis socialis et beatitudinis activae sufficiunt ad machinam minimam totius libri restituendam.","refs":["MC_QGOH_L1_01:MRT-B01","MC_QGOH_L1_02:MRT-B01"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[]},{"id":"WB_QGOH:MRT-B02","text":"Structura firmissima est circuitus continuus quo causalitas immanens affectionem, affectio potentiam, cooperatio adaequationem et adaequatio ulteriorem potentiam cognoscendi generat.","refs":["MC_QGOH_L1_01:MRT-B02","MC_QGOH_L1_02:MRT-B03"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[]},{"id":"WB_QGOH:MRT-B03","text":"Minus determinata manet productio affectus rationalis fortioris, conversio Civitatis coercitivae et nexus inter aptitudinem Corporis, intuitionem atque experientiam aeternitatis.","refs":["MC_QGOH_L1_01:MRT-B03","MC_QGOH_L1_02:MRT-B02"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[]},{"id":"WB_QGOH:MRT-B04","text":"Systema hac magnitudine cohaeret si non ut series doctrinarum sed ut circuitus a metaphysica ad corpus, ab imaginatione ad servitutem et a servitute ad beatitudinem legitur.","refs":["MC_QGOH_L1_01:MRT-B04","MC_QGOH_L1_02:MRT-B04"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[]},{"id":"WB_QGOH:MRT-B05","text":"Restitutio motum generativum servat, sed singularitas affectuum, differentiae corporum et vis performativa imaginis sapientis reductioni axiomaticae plenae resistunt.","refs":["MC_QGOH_L1_01:MRT-B05","MC_QGOH_L1_02:MRT-B05"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[]}],"original_name":"Minimal Whole-Book Reconstruction Test"}],"language_code":"la","language_name":"Latina","translations":[{"id":"CAREVAR_20261014T203545250250Z_96902771","language_code":"en","language_name":"English","title":"Ethics","source_sha256":"3a66f1b8e4bf427fe9acda2d8524af1f52a3306d87597b8996760c553e29594a","sha256":"6f3e573b51fded7e0f3d0fab10f0cdeb03d797788cdd53e474b6897037b0ef0a","rendition_type":"machine","review_status":"machine","model":"gpt-5.6-terra","created":"2026-10-14T20:35:45.253851+00:00","sections":[{"number":"1","name":"Book-Scale Argumental Spine","items":[{"id":"WB_QGOH:BAS-01","text":"The whole system asks how finite subjects necessarily immanent in Nature, implicated in passions and external causes, can generate common and intellectual freedom without causal exemption.","refs":["MC_QGOH_L1_01:AS-01","MC_QGOH_L1_02:AS-01"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[]},{"id":"WB_QGOH:BAS-02","text":"The global thesis establishes freedom as the amplification of one's own causality through which a finite expression of Nature passes from partial determination to bodily, social, and intellectual action.","refs":["MC_QGOH_L1_01:AS-02","MC_QGOH_L1_02:AS-02"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[]},{"id":"WB_QGOH:BAS-03","text":"The geometrical argumentation becomes at once genealogical, apophatic, and reconstructive: it dissolves fictitious exteriorities, explains their natural genesis, then restores within immanence the functions occupied by them.","refs":["MC_QGOH_L1_01:AS-03","MC_QGOH_L1_02:AS-03"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[]},{"id":"WB_QGOH:BAS-04","text":"The movement proceeds from immanent causality to bodily individuation, imagination, and conatus, thence through affective servitude and civil composition to intuitive knowledge and intellectual Love.","refs":["MC_QGOH_L1_01:AS-04","MC_QGOH_L1_02:AS-04"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[]},{"id":"WB_QGOH:BAS-05","text":"The progression is cumulative and spiral, because each stage retains the mechanisms of the preceding ones but changes their function, and the terminus acts back upon the conditions of its own production.","refs":["MC_QGOH_L1_01:AS-05","MC_QGOH_L1_02:AS-05"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[]},{"id":"WB_QGOH:BAS-06","text":"Necessity is inverted from a closure excluding all exteriority into a medium of individuation, intervention, and finally wise freedom.","refs":["MC_QGOH_L1_01:AS-06","MC_QGOH_L1_02:GA-11"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[]},{"id":"WB_QGOH:BAS-07","text":"Terminal closure produces not a remunerative end but active blessedness, which is converted from an apparent terminus into a permanent cause of virtue and intelligence.","refs":["MC_QGOH_L1_02:AS-06","MC_QGOH_L1_02:GA-14"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[]},{"id":"WB_QGOH:BAS-08","text":"The system closes immanently, but the bridge between causal knowledge, transformation of the affects, civil cooperation, and beatific stability remains demonstratively incomplete.","refs":["MC_QGOH_L1_01:AS-08","MC_QGOH_L1_02:BSA-05"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[]}],"original_name":"Book-Scale Argumental Spine"},{"number":"2","name":"Global Load-Bearing Axioms","items":[{"id":"WB_QGOH:GA-01","text":"[Persistent] One Nature exists and acts from its own necessity as the immanent cause of all things; no primary causality stands outside it.","refs":["MC_QGOH_L1_01:GA-01","MC_QGOH_L1_02:GA-01"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[]},{"id":"WB_QGOH:GA-02","text":"[Persistent] Everything finite is a mode, and Mind and Body are expressions of the same individual, not separate or causally transverse substances.","refs":["MC_QGOH_L1_01:GA-02","MC_QGOH_L1_01:GA-11"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[]},{"id":"WB_QGOH:GA-03","text":"[Mutating] The individual is a persisting bodily relation, whose actual essence is conatus and whose identity is preserved through continuous compositions.","refs":["MC_QGOH_L1_01:GA-04","MC_QGOH_L1_01:GA-07"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[]},{"id":"WB_QGOH:GA-04","text":"[Mutating] An affection, the idea of an affection, and a variation of power are three functions of one occurrence, which according to partial or adequate causality produces passion or action.","refs":["MC_QGOH_L1_01:RMD-05","MC_QGOH_L1_02:GA-02"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[]},{"id":"WB_QGOH:GA-05","text":"[Persistent] Adequacy designates active causality, whereas inadequacy designates the dependence by which a singular effect is only partially explained through its own nature.","refs":["MC_QGOH_L1_01:GA-06","MC_QGOH_L1_02:GA-02"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[]},{"id":"WB_QGOH:GA-06","text":"[Mutating] Imagination is a natural mechanism of making-present and association, which simultaneously provides affective capture, preservation of conatus, and material for rational reordering.","refs":["MC_QGOH_L1_01:RMD-06","MC_QGOH_L1_02:RMD-10"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[]},{"id":"WB_QGOH:GA-07","text":"[Emergent] Good, evil, and perfection are comparative indices of the relation between appetite, composition, and finite power, not transcendent properties of things.","refs":["MC_QGOH_L1_01:GA-08","MC_QGOH_L1_02:GA-10"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[]},{"id":"WB_QGOH:GA-08","text":"[Mutating] The subject is socially distributed: affective imitation can amplify conflict, but the same dependence can be converted through the State and cooperation into common power.","refs":["MC_QGOH_L1_01:GA-09","MC_QGOH_L1_02:GA-09"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[]},{"id":"WB_QGOH:GA-09","text":"[Terminal] Freedom is a degree of adequate causality within necessity, not indifference, absolute decree, or interruption of the natural nexus.","refs":["MC_QGOH_L1_01:GA-10","MC_QGOH_L1_02:GA-04"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[]},{"id":"WB_QGOH:GA-10","text":"[Mutating] Effective knowledge cannot be inert representation, but must causally alter affect, the order of the Body, institutions, or the capacity to understand further.","refs":["MC_QGOH_L1_02:GA-05","MC_QGOH_L1_02:CGD-05"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[]},{"id":"WB_QGOH:GA-11","text":"[Mutating] The Body is transformed from an object of affections into the material infrastructure of freedom and an instrument for amplifying the power of the Mind.","refs":["MC_QGOH_L1_02:GA-07","MC_QGOH_L1_02:RMD-04"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[]},{"id":"WB_QGOH:GA-12","text":"[Emergent] The State is a converter between servitude and freedom insofar as it translates coercion and security into rights, cooperation, and the greater causality of citizens.","refs":["MC_QGOH_L1_02:CGD-06","MC_QGOH_L1_02:XD-06"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[]},{"id":"WB_QGOH:GA-13","text":"[Emergent] Time is stratified into eternal necessity, the duration of conatus, imaginative making-present, and the understanding of essence without succession.","refs":["MC_QGOH_L1_01:GA-12","MC_QGOH_L1_02:GA-13"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[]},{"id":"WB_QGOH:GA-14","text":"[Attractor] Causal intelligibility, knowledge of God, acquiescence, and intellectual Love constitute an ascending attractor of active and non-rivalrous power.","refs":["MC_QGOH_L1_01:GA-13","MC_QGOH_L1_02:GA-12"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[]},{"id":"WB_QGOH:GA-15","text":"[Terminal] Blessedness is not a future reward, but an exercise of virtue which, from an apparent end, is converted into a mechanism of its own preservation.","refs":["MC_QGOH_L1_02:GA-14","MC_QGOH_L1_02:XD-09"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[]}],"original_name":"Global Load-Bearing Axioms"},{"number":"3","name":"Recurrence, Mutation, and Displacement Matrix","items":[{"id":"WB_QGOH:RMD-01","text":"Substance retains its ontological identity, but after immanence has been established it recedes from the foreground and terminally reappears as a multiscalar ecology of power.","refs":["MC_QGOH_L1_01:RMD-01","MC_QGOH_L1_02:BSD-01"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[]},{"id":"WB_QGOH:RMD-02","text":"Necessity migrates from the closure of modality to the genesis of passion, the ordering of affects, acquiescence, and the form of intellectual freedom.","refs":["MC_QGOH_L1_01:RMD-03","MC_QGOH_L1_02:RMD-07"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[]},{"id":"WB_QGOH:RMD-03","text":"The Body is transformed from a determined mode into a relational individual, a substrate of variation in power, material infrastructure, and an intellectual instrument.","refs":["MC_QGOH_L1_01:RMD-04","MC_QGOH_L1_02:RMD-04"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[]},{"id":"WB_QGOH:RMD-04","text":"Imagination changes from a general error into a neutral and social mechanism, but under the aspect of eternity its memorial function is terminally exhausted.","refs":["MC_QGOH_L1_01:RMD-06","MC_QGOH_L1_02:RMD-10"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[]},{"id":"WB_QGOH:RMD-05","text":"Conatus is amplified from the essence of persevering into the selection of images, rational virtue, common cooperation, and intellectual action.","refs":["MC_QGOH_L1_01:RMD-09","MC_QGOH_L1_02:GA-03"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[]},{"id":"WB_QGOH:RMD-06","text":"Sociality returns with altered function: another's affect excites conflictual reciprocity, the State restrains it, and rational cooperation converts it into common amplification.","refs":["MC_QGOH_L1_01:RLE-06","MC_QGOH_L1_02:RMD-05"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[]},{"id":"WB_QGOH:RMD-07","text":"Value migrates from an external model to an index of power, is then used as an instrument of finite navigation, and recedes in more perfect blessedness.","refs":["MC_QGOH_L1_01:RMD-08","MC_QGOH_L1_02:RMD-08"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[]},{"id":"WB_QGOH:RMD-08","text":"Freedom undergoes a double inversion: first it loses indifference, then it extends from singular causality into a bodily, civil, and intellectual ecology.","refs":["MC_QGOH_L1_01:RMD-07","MC_QGOH_L1_02:RMD-06"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[]},{"id":"WB_QGOH:RMD-09","text":"Knowledge of God advances from the attractor of understanding to intellectual Love, while blessedness loses its terminal status and becomes the active cause of the whole progression.","refs":["MC_QGOH_L1_02:RMD-09","MC_QGOH_L1_02:MP-05"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[]}],"original_name":"Recurrence, Mutation, and Displacement Matrix"},{"number":"4","name":"Lower-Layer vs Current-Level Distinction","items":[{"id":"WB_QGOH:CGD-01","text":"Demonstrations of the uniqueness of substance and distinctions of modes provide the lower architecture; at this level their function is the closure of exteriority maintained throughout the whole ethical field.","refs":["MC_QGOH_L1_01:CGD-01","MC_QGOH_L1_01:CGD-02"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[]},{"id":"WB_QGOH:CGD-02","text":"Taxonomies of affects and cases of actions remain lower; the level of the whole book compresses them into laws of the mutation of power, causality, and composition.","refs":["MC_QGOH_L1_01:CGD-04","MC_QGOH_L1_02:CGD-01"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[]},{"id":"WB_QGOH:CGD-03","text":"Bodily traces and the concurrence of images are local mechanisms; at a higher level they become the infrastructure through which memory, expectation, and affective presence distribute duration.","refs":["MC_QGOH_L1_01:CGD-03","MC_QGOH_L1_02:CGD-02"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[]},{"id":"WB_QGOH:CGD-04","text":"The evaluation of singular acts from their external appearance becomes obsolete, because the genealogy of the whole book transfers value to cause, the proportion of action, and the effect upon power.","refs":["MC_QGOH_L1_02:CGD-03","MC_QGOH_L1_02:DBN-04"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[]},{"id":"WB_QGOH:CGD-05","text":"The parallelism of attributes joins ontology to psychology, but remains a lower joint whose positive congruence beyond identity of expression is not explained.","refs":["MC_QGOH_L1_01:CGD-06","MC_QGOH_L1_01:FL-03"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[]},{"id":"WB_QGOH:CGD-06","text":"Nutrition, labor, law, and the State are local media; at this level they are absorbed into the material conditions for distributing one's own causality.","refs":["MC_QGOH_L1_02:CGD-06","MC_QGOH_L1_02:MME-03"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[]},{"id":"WB_QGOH:CGD-07","text":"Blessedness, which in the prior structure was an attractor not yet complete, receives active closure, although the nexus of rational affect, cooperation, and intuition is still not fully demonstrated.","refs":["MC_QGOH_L1_01:CGD-08","MC_QGOH_L1_02:BSA-05"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[]}],"original_name":"Lower-Layer vs Current-Level Distinction"},{"number":"5","name":"Global Apophatic Structure","items":[{"id":"WB_QGOH:GNX-01","text":"No place outside Nature is admitted from which causes, ends, norms, or freedom might receive their origin.","refs":["MC_QGOH_L1_01:GNX-01","MC_QGOH_L1_02:GNX-02"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"Negative","passages":[]},{"id":"WB_QGOH:GNX-02","text":"No human action interrupts the series of causes; freedom of the will and uncaused act are excluded.","refs":["MC_QGOH_L1_01:GNX-02","MC_QGOH_L1_02:GNX-01"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"Negative","passages":[]},{"id":"WB_QGOH:GNX-03","text":"Mind and Body are neither separate substances nor ethical adversaries; the freedom of the Mind is produced through the aptitude of the Body.","refs":["MC_QGOH_L1_01:GNX-04","MC_QGOH_L1_02:GNX-07"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"Negative","passages":[]},{"id":"WB_QGOH:GNX-04","text":"No transcendent moral tribunal or external end precedes the order of Nature, the value of things, or the rectitude of action.","refs":["MC_QGOH_L1_01:GNX-06","MC_QGOH_L1_02:GNX-02"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"Negative","passages":[]},{"id":"WB_QGOH:GNX-05","text":"The subject is neither transparent to itself, nor affectively self-governing, nor sufficient unto itself in relation to external relations.","refs":["MC_QGOH_L1_01:GNX-05","MC_QGOH_L1_02:GNX-04"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"Negative","passages":[]},{"id":"WB_QGOH:GNX-06","text":"Causally inert knowledge cannot overcome passion; logical contradiction without a change in affect and Body is insufficient.","refs":["MC_QGOH_L1_01:DBN-02","MC_QGOH_L1_02:GNX-03"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"Negative","passages":[]},{"id":"WB_QGOH:GNX-07","text":"Eternity is neither the continuation of personal memory nor a space of remuneration after death.","refs":["MC_QGOH_L1_02:GNX-08"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"Negative","passages":[]}],"original_name":"Global Apophatic Structure"},{"number":"6","name":"Deep Book Apophatics","items":[{"id":"WB_QGOH:DBN-01","text":"It must be false that ontology, knowledge, politics, and ethics constitute causally separate orders, because they are all distributions of one immanence.","refs":["MC_QGOH_L1_01:GA-03","MC_QGOH_L1_02:BSD-01"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"Negative","passages":[]},{"id":"WB_QGOH:DBN-02","text":"It must be false that error and passion are irrational irruptions outside Nature; their ignorance is stabilized by bodily, imaginative, and social mechanisms.","refs":["MC_QGOH_L1_01:DBN-05","MC_QGOH_L1_02:RLE-01"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"Negative","passages":[]},{"id":"WB_QGOH:DBN-03","text":"It must be false that the dependence of the finite is mere privation, because the same relation that permits servitude generates mutual aid and common power.","refs":["MC_QGOH_L1_02:GA-06","MC_QGOH_L1_02:XD-03"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"Negative","passages":[]},{"id":"WB_QGOH:DBN-04","text":"It must be false that the external appearance of an act contains its value, since the same deed can express passion, reason, or virtue.","refs":["MC_QGOH_L1_02:DBN-04","MC_QGOH_L1_02:CGD-03"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"Negative","passages":[]},{"id":"WB_QGOH:DBN-05","text":"It must be false that coercion and security by themselves effect rational cooperation; a regime of fear can recreate the circuit of servitude.","refs":["MC_QGOH_L1_02:DBN-05","MC_QGOH_L1_02:RLE-04"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"Negative","passages":[]},{"id":"WB_QGOH:DBN-06","text":"It must be false that blessedness is a future recompense or that virtue is sustained by calculation of reward.","refs":["MC_QGOH_L1_02:GA-14","MC_QGOH_L1_02:GNX-08"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"Negative","passages":[]}],"original_name":"Deep Book Apophatics"},{"number":"7","name":"Super-Apophatics of the Whole","items":[{"id":"WB_QGOH:BSA-01","text":"The supreme absence is absolute exteriority, a place from which the totality of Nature could be contemplated or governed without causal participation.","refs":["MC_QGOH_L1_01:BSA-01","MC_QGOH_L1_02:BSA-01"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"Negative","passages":[]},{"id":"WB_QGOH:BSA-02","text":"An absolute origin of thought, appetite, or action is ontologically unavailable; every beginning is a modulation of an already established nexus.","refs":["MC_QGOH_L1_01:BSA-02","MC_QGOH_L1_02:GNX-01"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"Negative","passages":[]},{"id":"WB_QGOH:BSA-03","text":"Immediate presence of a thing or of oneself, lacking bodily and historical mediation, is excluded together with the prospect of knowing the causes of the whole without participation.","refs":["MC_QGOH_L1_01:BSA-04","MC_QGOH_L1_02:BSA-01"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"Negative","passages":[]},{"id":"WB_QGOH:BSA-04","text":"The system is ordered around the impossibility of instantaneous and absolute liberation: truth does not immediately become power, and the finite human being is never free of all external causes.","refs":["MC_QGOH_L1_02:BSA-02","MC_QGOH_L1_02:BSA-03"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"Negative","passages":[]}],"original_name":"Super-Apophatics of the Whole"},{"number":"8","name":"Cross-Unit Derivative Propositions","items":[{"id":"WB_QGOH:XD-01","text":"Because ontology already constitutes truth and action as degrees of one causal integration, ethical remedies operate not according to an added command but according to a redistribution of the same causality.","refs":["WB_QGOH:GA-01","WB_QGOH:GA-05","WB_QGOH:GA-09","MC_QGOH_L1_01:BSD-01","MC_QGOH_L1_02:BSD-01"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"From","passages":[]},{"id":"WB_QGOH:XD-02","text":"Since imagination simultaneously sustains capture and preservation, truth obtains practical force only where it reorders images, affects, and bodily dispositions from within.","refs":["WB_QGOH:GA-06","WB_QGOH:GA-10","WB_QGOH:GA-11","MC_QGOH_L1_01:XD-08","MC_QGOH_L1_02:XD-02"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"From","passages":[]},{"id":"WB_QGOH:XD-03","text":"The relational individual and the metastable subject entail that freedom is a distributed ecology of body, society, and intellect, not the possession of a closed subject.","refs":["WB_QGOH:GA-03","WB_QGOH:GA-08","WB_QGOH:GA-09","WB_QGOH:GA-11","MC_QGOH_L1_01:XD-04","MC_QGOH_L1_02:XD-01"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"From","passages":[]},{"id":"WB_QGOH:XD-04","text":"Normativity is a temporary cartography of compositions: good and evil are necessary for finite navigation, but lose their function once the work of blessedness has been accomplished.","refs":["WB_QGOH:GA-07","WB_QGOH:GA-15","MC_QGOH_L1_01:XD-03","MC_QGOH_L1_02:XD-07"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"From","passages":[]},{"id":"WB_QGOH:XD-05","text":"Politics is the conversion of affective circuits, through which the circulation of images and attributions is translated from conflictual reciprocity into power-amplifying cooperation.","refs":["WB_QGOH:GA-08","WB_QGOH:GA-12","MC_QGOH_L1_01:BSD-04","MC_QGOH_L1_02:XD-06"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"From","passages":[]},{"id":"WB_QGOH:XD-06","text":"Ethical praxis does not progress in a single time, but coordinates imaginative presence, deliberative duration, and intelligible eternity without reducing them.","refs":["WB_QGOH:GA-13","MC_QGOH_L1_01:BSD-05","MC_QGOH_L1_02:XD-08"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"From","passages":[]},{"id":"WB_QGOH:XD-07","text":"Generosity and intellectual Love are two scales of non-rivalrous power, the former composing human relations and the latter reflexively understanding immanence itself.","refs":["WB_QGOH:GA-08","WB_QGOH:GA-14","WB_QGOH:GA-15","MC_QGOH_L1_01:XD-06","MC_QGOH_L1_02:RLE-07"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"From","passages":[]},{"id":"WB_QGOH:XD-08","text":"The transition from knowledge to affective stability requires rational affect, a cooperative State, and bodily disposition, but the causally sufficient nexus of these means remains incomplete.","refs":["WB_QGOH:GA-10","WB_QGOH:GA-11","WB_QGOH:GA-12","WB_QGOH:GA-15","MC_QGOH_L1_01:FL-06","MC_QGOH_L1_02:BSA-05"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"From","passages":[]}],"original_name":"Cross-Unit Derivative Propositions"},{"number":"9","name":"Book-Scale Super-Derivatives","items":[{"id":"WB_QGOH:BSD-01","text":"Reality becomes an ecology of self-modulation, in which substance, bodies, affects, institutions, and ideas are levels of one immanent causality.","refs":["MC_QGOH_L1_01:BSD-01","MC_QGOH_L1_02:BSD-01"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"Composite","passages":[]},{"id":"WB_QGOH:BSD-02","text":"The subject is a multiscalar proportion of causalities, whose agency increases or decreases according to the manner in which determining nexuses are expressed through it.","refs":["MC_QGOH_L1_01:BSD-02","MC_QGOH_L1_02:XD-10"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"Composite","passages":[]},{"id":"WB_QGOH:BSD-03","text":"Epistemology becomes causal technology, because to understand means to reorder the conditions of composition, affection, and further knowledge.","refs":["MC_QGOH_L1_01:BSD-06","MC_QGOH_L1_02:BSD-03"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"Composite","passages":[]},{"id":"WB_QGOH:BSD-04","text":"Politics becomes the distribution of the conditions of one's own causality, not the administration of already formed wills or the mere suppression of conflict.","refs":["MC_QGOH_L1_01:BSD-04","MC_QGOH_L1_02:BSD-04"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"Composite","passages":[]},{"id":"WB_QGOH:BSD-05","text":"Ethics is the conversion of necessary captures into non-rivalrous compositions, through which finitude is not removed but the power of knowing and acting is increased.","refs":["MC_QGOH_L1_01:BSD-03","MC_QGOH_L1_02:BSD-07"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"Composite","passages":[]},{"id":"WB_QGOH:BSD-06","text":"The history of the subject is not contained in homogeneous time, but consists in the coordination of duration, making-present, deliberation, and eternal understanding.","refs":["MC_QGOH_L1_01:BSD-05","MC_QGOH_L1_02:BSD-05"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"Composite","passages":[]},{"id":"WB_QGOH:BSD-07","text":"The geometrical method naturalizes performatively: it resolves transcendent negations into causal geneses and converts geneses into positive circuits of freedom.","refs":["MC_QGOH_L1_01:BSD-07","MC_QGOH_L1_02:ALT-B05"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"Composite","passages":[]}],"original_name":"Book-Scale Super-Derivatives"},{"number":"10","name":"Recursive Loops, Escalation Paths, and Conceptual Attractors","items":[{"id":"WB_QGOH:RLE-01","text":"Immanence naturalizes psychology; psychology explains the fiction of exteriority; explained exteriority in turn confirms the closure of immanence.","refs":["MC_QGOH_L1_01:RLE-01","MC_QGOH_L1_01:RLE-03"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[]},{"id":"WB_QGOH:RLE-02","text":"Affection generates an image, the image directs Desire, Desire changes the body and relations, and the changed body receives new affections under another proportion.","refs":["MC_QGOH_L1_01:RLE-02","MC_QGOH_L1_02:RLE-01"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[]},{"id":"WB_QGOH:RLE-03","text":"Adequacy increases action, action confirms a clearer understanding of the cause, and increased clarity diminishes further passivity.","refs":["MC_QGOH_L1_01:RLE-04","MC_QGOH_L1_02:RLE-02"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[]},{"id":"WB_QGOH:RLE-04","text":"Reciprocal affect amplifies conflict; coercion can induce security, but the circuit regresses into servitude if fear remains the foundation of the institution.","refs":["MC_QGOH_L1_01:RLE-06","MC_QGOH_L1_02:RLE-04"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"Tension","passages":[]},{"id":"WB_QGOH:RLE-05","text":"Rational agreement increases cooperation, cooperation amplifies singular power, and increased power effects greater communication of non-rivalrous good.","refs":["MC_QGOH_L1_02:RLE-03","MC_QGOH_L1_02:RLE-07"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[]},{"id":"WB_QGOH:RLE-06","text":"The economy of the Body sustains freedom of knowledge, knowledge increases the aptitude for acting, and a more apt body receives more adequate ideas.","refs":["MC_QGOH_L1_02:RLE-05"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[]},{"id":"WB_QGOH:RLE-07","text":"Adequate causality is the intermediate attractor, blessedness the terminal attractor; the former orders the transition, the latter closes the transition into a circuit of self-preservation.","refs":["MC_QGOH_L1_01:RLE-08","MC_QGOH_L1_02:RLE-08"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[]}],"original_name":"Recursive Loops, Escalation Paths, and Conceptual Attractors"},{"number":"11","name":"Formal Generative Schema of the Whole Book","items":[{"id":"WB_QGOH:FGS-B01","text":"Necessarily existing Nature → exclusion of exteriority → immanent causality → causally dependent finite modes.","refs":["MC_QGOH_L1_01:FGS-B01"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[]},{"id":"WB_QGOH:FGS-B02","text":"Finite mode → relational bodily individual → same process under Thought and Extension → Mind as idea of the Body.","refs":["MC_QGOH_L1_01:GA-04","MC_QGOH_L1_01:GA-11"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[]},{"id":"WB_QGOH:FGS-B03","text":"Bodily occurrence → affection → idea of affection → trace and making-present → perspectival imagination.","refs":["MC_QGOH_L1_01:RMD-05","MC_QGOH_L1_01:GA-05"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[]},{"id":"WB_QGOH:FGS-B04","text":"Conatus → Desire → variation of power → Joy or Sadness → action or passion according to adequacy of cause.","refs":["MC_QGOH_L1_01:GA-07","MC_QGOH_L1_01:GA-06"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[]},{"id":"WB_QGOH:FGS-B05","text":"Imagination of another → imitation of affect → reciprocity → conflict, rivalry, or social composition.","refs":["MC_QGOH_L1_01:RLE-06","MC_QGOH_L1_02:GA-09"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[]},{"id":"WB_QGOH:FGS-B06","text":"Contrary affect + aptitude of the Body + rational agreement + common aids → greater causality of one's own.","refs":["MC_QGOH_L1_02:XD-01","MC_QGOH_L1_02:RLE-03"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[]},{"id":"WB_QGOH:FGS-B07","text":"Adequate reason → desire for knowledge of the third kind → intuition of singular essence → intellectual Love.","refs":["MC_QGOH_L1_02:FGS-B07"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[]},{"id":"WB_QGOH:FGS-B08","text":"Intellectual Love + active joy → blessedness → increase of intellect → diminution of passions → stability of freedom.","refs":["MC_QGOH_L1_02:RLE-08"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[]}],"original_name":"Formal Generative Schema of the Whole Book"},{"number":"12","name":"Whole-Book Argument Flow Map","items":[{"id":"WB_QGOH:AFM-B01","text":"Immanence → bodily individual → Mind → affection → imagination → conatus → variation of power → affective servitude.","refs":["MC_QGOH_L1_01:AFM-B01","MC_QGOH_L1_02:AFM-B01"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[]},{"id":"WB_QGOH:AFM-B02","text":"Ignorance of causes → projected ends and values → fixation upon an external cause → diminution of power → further ignorance.","refs":["MC_QGOH_L1_01:AFM-B02","MC_QGOH_L1_02:RLE-01"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[]},{"id":"WB_QGOH:AFM-B03","text":"Conflictual imitation → coercive State → security → rational agreement → cooperation → common power.","refs":["MC_QGOH_L1_02:XD-06","MC_QGOH_L1_02:RLE-03"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[]},{"id":"WB_QGOH:AFM-B04","text":"Causal knowledge → active affect → one's own causality → intuition → intellectual Love → blessedness as exercise of virtue.","refs":["MC_QGOH_L1_01:BSD-03","MC_QGOH_L1_02:GA-14"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[]}],"original_name":"Whole-Book Argument Flow Map"},{"number":"13","name":"Macro Map-Effects","items":[{"id":"WB_QGOH:MME-01","text":"The opposition of God and world collapses, and theology is converted into a reflexive ontology of immanent causality.","refs":["MC_QGOH_L1_01:MME-01","MC_QGOH_L1_02:MME-06"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[]},{"id":"WB_QGOH:MME-02","text":"The opposition of Mind and Body gives way to the double expression of one individual, whose bodily economy constitutively conditions knowledge.","refs":["MC_QGOH_L1_01:MME-02","MC_QGOH_L1_02:MME-03"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[]},{"id":"WB_QGOH:MME-03","text":"Knowledge and agency coalesce into one scale of causality, whence epistemology assumes the status of a technology of transformation.","refs":["MC_QGOH_L1_01:MME-03","MC_QGOH_L1_02:BSD-03"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[]},{"id":"WB_QGOH:MME-04","text":"The morality of external acts collapses, and value becomes an index of cause, composition, and variation of power.","refs":["MC_QGOH_L1_01:MME-04","MC_QGOH_L1_02:MME-02"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[]},{"id":"WB_QGOH:MME-05","text":"Sociality is no longer an aggregation of preconstituted subjects, and the State is not opposed to freedom unless a regime of fear diminishes the causality of citizens.","refs":["MC_QGOH_L1_01:MME-05","MC_QGOH_L1_02:MME-04"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[]},{"id":"WB_QGOH:MME-06","text":"Time is reclassified from homogeneous succession into a plurality of duration, affective presence, and intelligible eternity.","refs":["MC_QGOH_L1_01:MME-06","MC_QGOH_L1_02:BSD-05"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[]},{"id":"WB_QGOH:MME-07","text":"Intervention passes from command and judgment to genetic analysis, and reading must follow the functional mutations of terms rather than nominal identity.","refs":["MC_QGOH_L1_01:MME-07","MC_QGOH_L1_02:MME-07"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[]}],"original_name":"Macro Map-Effects"},{"number":"14","name":"Altitude Integration","items":[{"id":"WB_QGOH:ALT-B01","text":"At the level of the surface, God, Nature, Mind, Body, affects, law, death, and blessedness are declared objects through which the question of servitude and freedom is treated.","refs":["MC_QGOH_L1_01:ALT-B01","MC_QGOH_L1_02:ALT-B01"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[]},{"id":"WB_QGOH:ALT-B02","text":"At the structural level, objects are distributed through immanent causality, relational individuation, social mediation, conatus, and the proportions of partial or adequate cause.","refs":["MC_QGOH_L1_01:ALT-B02","MC_QGOH_L1_02:ALT-B02"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[]},{"id":"WB_QGOH:ALT-B03","text":"At the metastrategic level, the question is transferred from choice and condemnation to the generation of active affects, the composition of power, and the conditions of intervention.","refs":["MC_QGOH_L1_01:ALT-B03","MC_QGOH_L1_02:ALT-B03"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[]},{"id":"WB_QGOH:ALT-B04","text":"At the ontological and temporal level, reality is necessary production, the subject a relational part of Nature, and imaginative duration is distinguished from intelligible eternity.","refs":["MC_QGOH_L1_01:ALT-B04","MC_QGOH_L1_02:ALT-B04"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[]},{"id":"WB_QGOH:ALT-B05","text":"At the methodological and performative level, genetic deduction dissolves exteriorities and converts negation itself into a positive circuit of freedom.","refs":["MC_QGOH_L1_01:ALT-B05","MC_QGOH_L1_02:ALT-B05"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[]}],"original_name":"Altitude Integration"},{"number":"15","name":"Internal Tensions, Mutation Points, and Fault Lines","items":[{"id":"WB_QGOH:FL-01","text":"Attributes are conceived through themselves, but their distinction from the unity of substance is stabilized through exclusion of plurality rather than through positive coordination.","refs":["MC_QGOH_L1_01:FL-01"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"Tension","passages":[]},{"id":"WB_QGOH:FL-02","text":"Divine causality determines all modes, but the mediation between eternal productivity and immediately causing finite series remains overly compressed.","refs":["MC_QGOH_L1_01:FL-02"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"Tension","passages":[]},{"id":"WB_QGOH:FL-03","text":"Parallelism preserves the identity of the process of Mind and Body, but does not explain a positive principle of congruence beyond double expression.","refs":["MC_QGOH_L1_01:FL-03"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"Tension","passages":[]},{"id":"WB_QGOH:FL-04","text":"Falsity is said to be a privation of knowledge, although the conditions of error are positively produced; normative status and causal genesis are not fully reconciled in the same term.","refs":["MC_QGOH_L1_01:FL-04","MC_QGOH_L1_01:DBN-05"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"Tension","passages":[]},{"id":"WB_QGOH:FL-05","text":"Truth has no automatic command, but terminal freedom depends on intelligence; the threshold at which understood necessity acquires sufficient affective force remains indeterminate.","refs":["MC_QGOH_L1_02:FL-01","MC_QGOH_L1_01:FL-06"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"Tension","passages":[]},{"id":"WB_QGOH:FL-06","text":"Rational concord orders the political end, but a State founded on fear cannot derive cooperation from security alone.","refs":["MC_QGOH_L1_02:FL-02","MC_QGOH_L1_02:RLE-04"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"Tension","passages":[]},{"id":"WB_QGOH:FL-07","text":"The temporal Body is said to prepare greater participation in eternal understanding, but the proportion between bodily aptitude and the eternity of the Mind is not fully demonstrated.","refs":["MC_QGOH_L1_02:FL-03","MC_QGOH_L1_02:CGD-07"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"Tension","passages":[]},{"id":"WB_QGOH:FL-08","text":"The negation of finality coheres with the attractor of blessedness only if blessedness is understood as the immanent cause of progression, not as a preordained end of Nature.","refs":["MC_QGOH_L1_02:FL-04","MC_QGOH_L1_02:XD-09"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"Tension","passages":[]},{"id":"WB_QGOH:MP-01","text":"Necessity reaches a point of mutation where it passes from the closure of contingency and a sign of domination into an object of acquiescence and a form of freedom.","refs":["MC_QGOH_L1_01:RMD-03","MC_QGOH_L1_02:MP-04"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"Tension","passages":[]},{"id":"WB_QGOH:MP-02","text":"Imagination reaches a point of mutation where it changes from prejudice into a neutral and preservative mechanism, then under eternity loses its memorial operation.","refs":["MC_QGOH_L1_01:MP-03","MC_QGOH_L1_02:RMD-10"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"Tension","passages":[]},{"id":"WB_QGOH:MP-03","text":"The Body reaches a point of mutation where it passes from an object of the Mind and seat of passion into a positive disposition for action, knowledge, and understanding of eternity.","refs":["MC_QGOH_L1_01:MP-02","MC_QGOH_L1_02:MP-03"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"Tension","passages":[]},{"id":"WB_QGOH:MP-04","text":"Adequacy reaches a point of mutation where it is converted from a quality of truth into active affect and transformative causality.","refs":["MC_QGOH_L1_01:MP-04","MC_QGOH_L1_02:CGD-05"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"Tension","passages":[]},{"id":"WB_QGOH:MP-05","text":"The State reaches a point of mutation where external coercion is converted into security, cooperation, and the citizens' own causality.","refs":["MC_QGOH_L1_02:MP-02"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"Tension","passages":[]},{"id":"WB_QGOH:MP-06","text":"Blessedness constitutes the terminal point of mutation where it loses the status of an end and becomes an exercise of virtue and cause of preserving freedom.","refs":["MC_QGOH_L1_02:MP-05","MC_QGOH_L1_02:GA-14"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"Tension","passages":[]}],"original_name":"Internal Tensions, Mutation Points, and Fault Lines"},{"number":"16","name":"Hidden System Image","items":[{"id":"WB_QGOH:HSI-01","text":"Reality is an immanent machine and network of forces, in which causality does not exit Nature but is perpetually redistributed among bodies, institutions, and ideas.","refs":["MC_QGOH_L1_01:HSI-01","MC_QGOH_L1_02:HSI-01"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[]},{"id":"WB_QGOH:HSI-02","text":"The subject is a bodily vortex and permeable node, whose autonomy is not closure but a clearer expression of the nexuses acting through it.","refs":["MC_QGOH_L1_01:HSI-02","MC_QGOH_L1_02:HSI-02"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[]},{"id":"WB_QGOH:HSI-03","text":"Time is a stratified machine and bifurcated topology, in which duration distributes memorial changes, whereas eternity presents essence without succession.","refs":["MC_QGOH_L1_01:HSI-03","MC_QGOH_L1_02:HSI-03"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[]},{"id":"WB_QGOH:HSI-04","text":"Capture is a circuit through which an external cause diminishes power, stabilizes ignorance, and produces the appearance of freedom under dependence itself.","refs":["MC_QGOH_L1_01:HSI-04","MC_QGOH_L1_02:RLE-01"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[]},{"id":"WB_QGOH:HSI-05","text":"Possible navigation is not flight from the machine, but the spiral conversion of causal channels through which conflict, external fixation, and fear are transferred into cooperation, intelligence, and blessedness.","refs":["MC_QGOH_L1_01:HSI-05","MC_QGOH_L1_02:HSI-04"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[]}],"original_name":"Hidden System Image"},{"number":"17","name":"Minimal Whole-Book Reconstruction Test","items":[{"id":"WB_QGOH:MRT-B01","text":"The axioms of immanence, bodily individuation, conatus, imagination, adequacy, social composition, and active blessedness suffice to reconstruct the minimal machine of the whole book.","refs":["MC_QGOH_L1_01:MRT-B01","MC_QGOH_L1_02:MRT-B01"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[]},{"id":"WB_QGOH:MRT-B02","text":"The firmest structure is the continuous circuit through which immanent causality generates affection, affection power, cooperation adequacy, and adequacy a further power of knowing.","refs":["MC_QGOH_L1_01:MRT-B02","MC_QGOH_L1_02:MRT-B03"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[]},{"id":"WB_QGOH:MRT-B03","text":"Less determined remain the production of a stronger rational affect, the conversion of the coercive State, and the nexus between the aptitude of the Body, intuition, and the experience of eternity.","refs":["MC_QGOH_L1_01:MRT-B03","MC_QGOH_L1_02:MRT-B02"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[]},{"id":"WB_QGOH:MRT-B04","text":"The system coheres at this magnitude if it is read not as a series of doctrines but as a circuit from metaphysics to body, from imagination to servitude, and from servitude to blessedness.","refs":["MC_QGOH_L1_01:MRT-B04","MC_QGOH_L1_02:MRT-B04"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[]},{"id":"WB_QGOH:MRT-B05","text":"The reconstruction preserves the generative movement, but the singularity of affects, the differences of bodies, and the performative force of the image of the wise person resist complete axiomatic reduction.","refs":["MC_QGOH_L1_01:MRT-B05","MC_QGOH_L1_02:MRT-B05"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[]}],"original_name":"Minimal Whole-Book Reconstruction Test"}],"revision":"WB_QGOH@R002","source_variant_id":"canonical:WB_QGOH:WB_QGOH@R002","source_language_code":"la","original_title":"Ethica"}],"identity":{"layer":"WB","kicker":"Whole book","title":"Ethics","context":"","original_title":"Ethica"},"original_title":"Ethica","title_language_code":"en"}],"seal":"SEAL_QGOH_8C0F7838B4BC9950507A","version_work_id":"WORK_STORE_EUROPEAN_SPINOZA_ETHICA","version_label":"Added 14 Oct 2026 · Run 14 Oct 2026 · gpt-5.6-terra / gpt-5.6-sol","version_revision":"0d6379417fc7f66cd105d5b63ce1e1e72000deb7b840e0a4c344282efe4e8cfc","original_title":"Ethica","title_language_code":"en","title_translation":{"schema":"care-title-heading-translation/1","language_code":"en","rendition_type":"machine","originals_preserved":true}},{"id":"WORK_STORE_PERSEUS_GREEK_TLG0086_TLG025_PERSEUS_GRC2","slug":"beta-223fa1fddd7cddd2","title":"Metaphysics","author":"Aristotle","edition":"William David Ross","year":"350 BCE","description":"","cover_url":"./assets/covers/beta-223fa1fddd7cddd2-8bbd0ab3c2fb0bf2.png","source_url":"https://github.com/PerseusDL/canonical-greekLit/blob/01b725d835e6e733062ffd79e0efdbae1ba06e5c/data/tlg0086/tlg025/tlg0086.tlg025.perseus-grc2.xml","source_notices":{"provider":"Perseus Greek","source_url":"https://github.com/PerseusDL/canonical-greekLit/blob/01b725d835e6e733062ffd79e0efdbae1ba06e5c/data/tlg0086/tlg025/tlg0086.tlg025.perseus-grc2.xml","licence":"https://creativecommons.org/licenses/by-sa/4.0/","statement":"Available under a Creative Commons Attribution-ShareAlike 4.0 International License"},"language_code":"grc","language_name":"Ἑλληνική","sources":[{"id":"RAW_DAZY_BOO1","title":"Book 1","order":1,"chapter_id":"CH_DAZY_BOO1","text":"πάντες ἄνθρωποι τοῦ εἰδέναι ὀρέγονται φύσει. σημεῖον δʼ ἡ τῶν αἰσθήσεων ἀγάπησις· καὶ γὰρ χωρὶς τῆς χρείας ἀγαπῶνται διʼ αὑτάς, καὶ μάλιστα τῶν ἄλλων ἡ διὰ τῶν ὀμμάτων. οὐ γὰρ μόνον ἵνα πράττωμεν ἀλλὰ καὶ μηθὲν μέλλοντες πράττειν τὸ ὁρᾶν αἱρούμεθα ἀντὶ πάντων ὡς εἰπεῖν τῶν ἄλλων. αἴτιον δʼ ὅτι μάλιστα ποιεῖ γνωρίζειν ἡμᾶς αὕτη τῶν αἰσθήσεων καὶ πολλὰς δηλοῖ διαφοράς. φύσει μὲν οὖν αἴσθησιν ἔχοντα γίγνεται τὰ ζῷα, ἐκ δὲ ταύτης τοῖς μὲν αὐτῶν οὐκ ἐγγίγνεται μνήμη, τοῖς δʼ ἐγγίγνεται.  καὶ διὰ τοῦτο ταῦτα φρονιμώτερα καὶ μαθητικώτερα τῶν μὴ δυναμένων μνημονεύειν ἐστί, φρόνιμα μὲν ἄνευ τοῦ μανθάνειν ὅσα μὴ δύναται τῶν ψόφων ἀκούειν (οἷον μέλιττα κἂν εἴ τι τοιοῦτον ἄλλο γένος ζῴων ἔστι), μανθάνει δʼ ὅσα πρὸς τῇ μνήμῃ καὶ ταύτην ἔχει τὴν αἴσθησιν. τὰ μὲν οὖν ἄλλα ταῖς φαντασίαις ζῇ καὶ ταῖς μνήμαις, ἐμπειρίας δὲ μετέχει μικρόν· τὸ δὲ τῶν ἀνθρώπων γένος καὶ τέχνῃ καὶ λογισμοῖς. γίγνεται δʼ ἐκ τῆς μνήμης ἐμπειρία τοῖς ἀνθρώποις· αἱ γὰρ πολλαὶ μνῆμαι τοῦ αὐτοῦ πράγματος μιᾶς ἐμπειρίας δύναμιν ἀποτελοῦσιν.  καὶ δοκεῖ σχεδὸν ἐπιστήμῃ καὶ τέχνῃ ὅμοιον εἶναι καὶ ἐμπειρία, ἀποβαίνει δʼ ἐπιστήμη καὶ τέχνη διὰ τῆς ἐμπειρίας τοῖς ἀνθρώποις· ἡ μὲν γὰρ ἐμπειρία τέχνην ἐποίησεν, ὡς φησὶ Πῶλος, ἡ δʼ ἀπειρία τύχην. γίγνεται δὲ τέχνη ὅταν ἐκ πολλῶν τῆς ἐμπειρίας ἐννοημάτων μία καθόλου γένηται περὶ τῶν ὁμοίων ὑπόληψις. τὸ μὲν γὰρ ἔχειν ὑπόληψιν ὅτι Καλλίᾳ κάμνοντι τηνδὶ τὴν νόσον τοδὶ συνήνεγκε καὶ Σωκράτει καὶ καθʼ ἕκαστον οὕτω πολλοῖς, ἐμπειρίας ἐστίν· τὸ δʼ ὅτι πᾶσι τοῖς τοιοῖσδε κατʼ εἶδος ἓν ἀφορισθεῖσι, κάμνουσι τηνδὶ τὴν νόσον, συνήνεγκεν, οἷον τοῖς φλεγματώδεσιν ἢ χολώδεσι  πυρέττουσι καύσῳ, τέχνης.—πρὸς μὲν οὖν τὸ πράττειν ἐμπειρία τέχνης οὐδὲν δοκεῖ διαφέρειν, ἀλλὰ καὶ μᾶλλον ἐπιτυγχάνουσιν οἱ ἔμπειροι τῶν ἄνευ τῆς ἐμπειρίας λόγον ἐχόντων (αἴτιον δʼ ὅτι ἡ μὲν ἐμπειρία τῶν καθʼ ἕκαστόν ἐστι γνῶσις ἡ δὲ τέχνη τῶν καθόλου, αἱ δὲ πράξεις καὶ αἱ γενέσεις πᾶσαι περὶ τὸ καθʼ ἕκαστόν εἰσιν· οὐ γὰρ ἄνθρωπον ὑγιάζει ὁ ἰατρεύων ἀλλʼ ἢ κατὰ συμβεβηκός, ἀλλὰ Καλλίαν ἢ Σωκράτην ἢ τῶν ἄλλων τινὰ τῶν οὕτω λεγομένων ᾧ συμβέβηκεν ἀνθρώπῳ εἶναι· ἐὰν οὖν ἄνευ τῆς ἐμπειρίας ἔχῃ τις τὸν λόγον, καὶ τὸ καθόλου μὲν γνωρίζῃ τὸ δʼ ἐν τούτῳ καθʼ ἕκαστον ἀγνοῇ, πολλάκις διαμαρτήσεται τῆς θεραπείας· θεραπευτὸν γὰρ τὸ καθʼ ἕκαστον)· ἀλλʼ ὅμως τό γε εἰδέναι καὶ τὸ ἐπαΐειν τῇ τέχνῃ τῆς ἐμπειρίας ὑπάρχειν οἰόμεθα μᾶλλον, καὶ σοφωτέρους τοὺς τεχνίτας τῶν ἐμπείρων ὑπολαμβάνομεν, ὡς κατὰ τὸ εἰδέναι μᾶλλον ἀκολουθοῦσαν τὴν σοφίαν πᾶσι· τοῦτο δʼ ὅτι οἱ μὲν τὴν αἰτίαν ἴσασιν οἱ δʼ οὔ. οἱ μὲν γὰρ ἔμπειροι τὸ ὅτι μὲν ἴσασι, διότι δʼ οὐκ ἴσασιν· οἱ δὲ τὸ διότι καὶ τὴν αἰτίαν γνωρίζουσιν. διὸ καὶ τοὺς ἀρχιτέκτονας περὶ ἕκαστον τιμιωτέρους καὶ μᾶλλον εἰδέναι νομίζομεν τῶν χειροτεχνῶν καὶ σοφωτέρους,  ὅτι τὰς αἰτίας τῶν ποιουμένων ἴσασιν (τοὺς δʼ, ὥσπερ καὶ τῶν ἀψύχων ἔνια ποιεῖ μέν, οὐκ εἰδότα δὲ ποιεῖ ἃ ποιεῖ, οἷον καίει τὸ πῦρ—τὰ μὲν οὖν ἄψυχα φύσει τινὶ ποιεῖν τούτων ἕκαστον τοὺς δὲ χειροτέχνας διʼ ἔθος), ὡς οὐ κατὰ τὸ πρακτικοὺς εἶναι σοφωτέρους ὄντας ἀλλὰ κατὰ τὸ λόγον ἔχειν αὐτοὺς καὶ τὰς αἰτίας γνωρίζειν. ὅλως τε σημεῖον τοῦ εἰδότος καὶ μὴ εἰδότος τὸ δύνασθαι διδάσκειν ἐστίν, καὶ διὰ τοῦτο τὴν τέχνην τῆς ἐμπειρίας ἡγούμεθα μᾶλλον ἐπιστήμην εἶναι· δύνανται γάρ, οἱ δὲ οὐ δύνανται διδάσκειν. ἔτι δὲ τῶν αἰσθήσεων οὐδεμίαν ἡγούμεθα εἶναι σοφίαν· καίτοι κυριώταταί γʼ εἰσὶν αὗται τῶν καθʼ ἕκαστα γνώσεις· ἀλλʼ οὐ λέγουσι τὸ διὰ τί περὶ οὐδενός, οἷον διὰ τί θερμὸν τὸ πῦρ, ἀλλὰ μόνον ὅτι θερμόν. τὸ μὲν οὖν πρῶτον εἰκὸς τὸν ὁποιανοῦν εὑρόντα τέχνην παρὰ τὰς κοινὰς αἰσθήσεις θαυμάζεσθαι ὑπὸ τῶν ἀνθρώπων μὴ μόνον διὰ τὸ χρήσιμον εἶναί τι τῶν εὑρεθέντων ἀλλʼ ὡς σοφὸν καὶ διαφέροντα τῶν ἄλλων· πλειόνων δʼ εὑρισκομένων τεχνῶν καὶ τῶν μὲν πρὸς τἀναγκαῖα τῶν δὲ πρὸς διαγωγὴν οὐσῶν, ἀεὶ σοφωτέρους τοὺς τοιούτους ἐκείνων ὑπολαμβάνεσθαι διὰ τὸ μὴ πρὸς χρῆσιν εἶναι τὰς ἐπιστήμας αὐτῶν. ὅθεν ἤδη πάντων τῶν τοιούτων κατεσκευασμένων αἱ μὴ πρὸς ἡδονὴν μηδὲ πρὸς τἀναγκαῖα τῶν ἐπιστημῶν εὑρέθησαν, καὶ πρῶτον ἐν τούτοις τοῖς τόποις οὗ πρῶτον ἐσχόλασαν· διὸ περὶ Αἴγυπτον αἱ μαθηματικαὶ πρῶτον τέχναι συνέστησαν, ἐκεῖ γὰρ ἀφείθη σχολάζειν τὸ τῶν ἱερέων ἔθνος. εἴρηται μὲν οὖν ἐν τοῖς ἠθικοῖς τίς διαφορὰ τέχνης καὶ ἐπιστήμης καὶ τῶν ἄλλων τῶν ὁμογενῶν· οὗ δʼ ἕνεκα νῦν ποιούμεθα τὸν λόγον τοῦτʼ ἐστίν, ὅτι τὴν ὀνομαζομένην σοφίαν περὶ τὰ πρῶτα αἴτια καὶ τὰς ἀρχὰς ὑπολαμβάνουσι πάντες· ὥστε, καθάπερ εἴρηται πρότερον, ὁ μὲν ἔμπειρος τῶν ὁποιανοῦν ἐχόντων αἴσθησιν εἶναι δοκεῖ σοφώτερος, ὁ δὲ τεχνίτης τῶν ἐμπείρων, χειροτέχνου δὲ ἀρχιτέκτων, αἱ δὲ θεωρητικαὶ τῶν ποιητικῶν μᾶλλον.  ὅτι μὲν οὖν ἡ σοφία περί τινας ἀρχὰς καὶ αἰτίας ἐστὶν ἐπιστήμη, δῆλον.\n\n \n\n \n\n \n\nἐπεὶ δὲ ταύτην τὴν ἐπιστήμην ζητοῦμεν, τοῦτʼ ἂν εἴη σκεπτέον, ἡ περὶ ποίας αἰτίας καὶ περὶ ποίας ἀρχὰς ἐπιστήμη σοφία ἐστίν. εἰ δὴ λάβοι τις τὰς ὑπολήψεις ἃς ἔχομεν περὶ τοῦ σοφοῦ, τάχʼ ἂν ἐκ τούτου φανερὸν γένοιτο μᾶλλον. ὑπολαμβάνομεν δὴ πρῶτον μὲν ἐπίστασθαι πάντα τὸν σοφὸν ὡς ἐνδέχεται, μὴ καθʼ ἕκαστον ἔχοντα ἐπιστήμην αὐτῶν· εἶτα τὸν τὰ χαλεπὰ γνῶναι δυνάμενον καὶ μὴ ῥᾴδια ἀνθρώπῳ γιγνώσκειν, τοῦτον σοφόν (τὸ γὰρ αἰσθάνεσθαι πάντων κοινόν, διὸ ῥᾴδιον καὶ οὐδὲν σοφόν)· ἔτι τὸν ἀκριβέστερον καὶ τὸν διδασκαλικώτερον τῶν αἰτιῶν σοφώτερον εἶναι περὶ πᾶσαν ἐπιστήμην· καὶ τῶν ἐπιστημῶν δὲ τὴν αὑτῆς ἕνεκεν καὶ τοῦ εἰδέναι χάριν αἱρετὴν οὖσαν μᾶλλον εἶναι σοφίαν ἢ τὴν τῶν ἀποβαινόντων ἕνεκεν, καὶ τὴν ἀρχικωτέραν τῆς ὑπηρετούσης μᾶλλον σοφίαν· οὐ γὰρ δεῖν ἐπιτάττεσθαι τὸν σοφὸν ἀλλʼ ἐπιτάττειν, καὶ οὐ τοῦτον ἑτέρῳ πείθεσθαι, ἀλλὰ τούτῳ τὸν ἧττον σοφόν. —τὰς μὲν οὖν ὑπολήψεις τοιαύτας καὶ τοσαύτας ἔχομεν περὶ τῆς σοφίας καὶ τῶν σοφῶν· τούτων δὲ τὸ μὲν πάντα ἐπίστασθαι τῷ μάλιστα ἔχοντι τὴν καθόλου ἐπιστήμην ἀναγκαῖον ὑπάρχειν (οὗτος γὰρ οἶδέ πως πάντα τὰ ὑποκείμενα), σχεδὸν δὲ καὶ χαλεπώτατα ταῦτα γνωρίζειν τοῖς ἀνθρώποις, τὰ μάλιστα καθόλου (πορρωτάτω γὰρ τῶν αἰσθήσεών ἐστιν), ἀκριβέσταται δὲ τῶν ἐπιστημῶν αἳ μάλιστα τῶν πρώτων εἰσίν (αἱ γὰρ ἐξ ἐλαττόνων ἀκριβέστεραι τῶν ἐκ προσθέσεως λεγομένων, οἷον ἀριθμητικὴ γεωμετρίας)· ἀλλὰ μὴν καὶ διδασκαλική γε ἡ τῶν αἰτιῶν θεωρητικὴ μᾶλλον (οὗτοι γὰρ διδάσκουσιν, οἱ τὰς αἰτίας λέγοντες περὶ ἑκάστου), τὸ δʼ εἰδέναι καὶ τὸ ἐπίστασθαι αὐτῶν ἕνεκα μάλισθʼ ὑπάρχει τῇ τοῦ μάλιστα ἐπιστητοῦ ἐπιστήμῃ (ὁ γὰρ τὸ ἐπίστασθαι διʼ αὑτὸ αἱρούμενος τὴν μάλιστα ἐπιστήμην μάλιστα αἱρήσεται,  τοιαύτη δʼ ἐστὶν ἡ τοῦ μάλιστα ἐπιστητου), μάλιστα δʼ ἐπιστητὰ τὰ πρῶτα καὶ τὰ αἴτια (διὰ γὰρ ταῦτα καὶ ἐκ τούτων τἆλλα γνωρίζεται ἀλλʼ οὐ ταῦτα διὰ τῶν ὑποκειμένων), ἀρχικωτάτη δὲ τῶν ἐπιστημῶν, καὶ μᾶλλον ἀρχικὴ τῆς ὑπηρετούσης, ἡ γνωρίζουσα τίνος ἕνεκέν ἐστι πρακτέον ἕκαστον· τοῦτο δʼ ἐστὶ τἀγαθὸν ἑκάστου, ὅλως δὲ τὸ ἄριστον ἐν τῇ φύσει πάσῃ. ἐξ ἁπάντων οὖν τῶν εἰρημένων ἐπὶ τὴν αὐτὴν ἐπιστήμην πίπτει τὸ ζητούμενον ὄνομα· δεῖ γὰρ ταύτην τῶν πρώτων ἀρχῶν καὶ αἰτιῶν εἶναι θεωρητικήν· καὶ γὰρ τἀγαθὸν καὶ τὸ οὗ ἕνεκα ἓν τῶν αἰτίων ἐστίν.\n\n \n\nὅτι δʼ οὐ ποιητική, δῆλον καὶ ἐκ τῶν πρώτων φιλοσοφησάντων· διὰ γὰρ τὸ θαυμάζειν οἱ ἄνθρωποι καὶ νῦν καὶ τὸ πρῶτον ἤρξαντο φιλοσοφεῖν, ἐξ ἀρχῆς μὲν τὰ πρόχειρα τῶν ἀτόπων θαυμάσαντες, εἶτα κατὰ μικρὸν οὕτω προϊόντες καὶ περὶ τῶν μειζόνων διαπορήσαντες, οἷον περί τε τῶν τῆς σελήνης παθημάτων καὶ τῶν περὶ τὸν ἥλιον καὶ ἄστρα καὶ περὶ τῆς τοῦ παντὸς γενέσεως. ὁ δʼ ἀπορῶν καὶ θαυμάζων οἴεται ἀγνοεῖν (διὸ καὶ ὁ φιλόμυθος φιλόσοφός πώς ἐστιν· ὁ γὰρ μῦθος σύγκειται ἐκ θαυμασίων)· ὥστʼ εἴπερ διὰ τὸ φεύγειν τὴν ἄγνοιαν ἐφιλοσόφησαν, φανερὸν ὅτι διὰ τὸ εἰδέναι τὸ ἐπίστασθαι ἐδίωκον καὶ οὐ χρήσεώς τινος ἕνεκεν. μαρτυρεῖ δὲ αὐτὸ τὸ συμβεβηκός· σχεδὸν γὰρ πάντων ὑπαρχόντων τῶν ἀναγκαίων καὶ πρὸς ῥᾳστώνην καὶ διαγωγὴν ἡ τοιαύτη φρόνησις ἤρξατο ζητεῖσθαι. δῆλον οὖν ὡς διʼ οὐδεμίαν αὐτὴν ζητοῦμεν χρείαν ἑτέραν, ἀλλʼ ὥσπερ ἄνθρωπος, φαμέν, ἐλεύθερος ὁ αὑτοῦ ἕνεκα καὶ μὴ ἄλλου ὤν, οὕτω καὶ αὐτὴν ὡς μόνην οὖσαν ἐλευθέραν τῶν ἐπιστημῶν· μόνη γὰρ αὕτη αὑτῆς ἕνεκέν ἐστιν. διὸ καὶ δικαίως ἂν οὐκ ἀνθρωπίνη νομίζοιτο αὐτῆς ἡ κτῆσις· πολλαχῇ γὰρ ἡ φύσις δούλη τῶν ἀνθρώπων ἐστίν, ὥστε κατὰ Σιμωνίδην  \nθεὸς ἂν μόνος τοῦτʼ ἔχοι γέρας, ἄνδρα δʼ οὐκ ἄξιον μὴ οὐ ζητεῖν τὴν καθʼ αὑτὸν ἐπιστήμην. εἰ δὴ λέγουσί τι οἱ ποιηταὶ καὶ πέφυκε φθονεῖν τὸ θεῖον,  ἐπὶ τούτου συμβῆναι μάλιστα εἰκὸς καὶ δυστυχεῖς εἶναι πάντας τοὺς περιττούς. ἀλλʼ οὔτε τὸ θεῖον φθονερὸν ἐνδέχεται εἶναι, ἀλλὰ κατὰ τὴν παροιμίαν πολλὰ ψεύδονται ἀοιδοί, οὔτε τῆς τοιαύτης ἄλλην χρὴ νομίζειν τιμιωτέραν.  ἡ γὰρ θειοτάτη καὶ τιμιωτάτη· τοιαύτη δὲ διχῶς ἂν εἴη μόνη· ἥν τε γὰρ μάλιστʼ ἂν ὁ θεὸς ἔχοι, θεία τῶν ἐπιστημῶν ἐστί, κἂν εἴ τις τῶν θείων εἴη. μόνη δʼ αὕτη τούτων ἀμφοτέρων τετύχηκεν· ὅ τε γὰρ θεὸς δοκεῖ τῶν αἰτίων πᾶσιν εἶναι καὶ ἀρχή τις, καὶ τὴν τοιαύτην ἢ μόνος ἢ μάλιστʼ ἂν ἔχοι ὁ θεός. ἀναγκαιότεραι μὲν οὖν πᾶσαι ταύτης, ἀμείνων δʼ οὐδεμία.—δεῖ μέντοι πως καταστῆναι τὴν κτῆσιν αὐτῆς εἰς τοὐναντίον ἡμῖν τῶν ἐξ ἀρχῆς ζητήσεων. ἄρχονται μὲν γάρ, ὥσπερ εἴπομεν, ἀπὸ τοῦ θαυμάζειν πάντες εἰ οὕτως ἔχει, καθάπερ περὶ τῶν θαυμάτων ταὐτόματα  ἢ περὶ τὰς τοῦ ἡλίου τροπὰς ἢ τὴν τῆς διαμέτρου ἀσυμμετρίαν (θαυμαστὸν γὰρ εἶναι δοκεῖ πᾶσι τοῖς μήπω τεθεωρηκόσι τὴν αἰτίαν εἴ τι τῷ ἐλαχίστῳ μὴ μετρεῖται)· δεῖ δὲ εἰς τοὐναντίον καὶ τὸ ἄμεινον κατὰ τὴν παροιμίαν ἀποτελευτῆσαι, καθάπερ καὶ ἐν τούτοις ὅταν μάθωσιν· οὐθὲν γὰρ ἂν οὕτως θαυμάσειεν ἀνὴρ γεωμετρικὸς ὡς εἰ γένοιτο ἡ διάμετρος μετρητή. τίς μὲν οὖν ἡ φύσις τῆς ἐπιστήμης τῆς ζητουμένης, εἴρηται, καὶ τίς ὁ σκοπὸς οὗ δεῖ τυγχάνειν τὴν ζήτησιν καὶ τὴν ὅλην μέθοδον.\n\n \n\n \n\n \n\nἐπεὶ δὲ φανερὸν ὅτι τῶν ἐξ ἀρχῆς αἰτίων δεῖ λαβεῖν ἐπιστήμην (τότε γὰρ εἰδέναι φαμὲν ἕκαστον, ὅταν τὴν πρώτην αἰτίαν οἰώμεθα γνωρίζειν), τὰ δʼ αἴτια λέγεται τετραχῶς, ὧν μίαν μὲν αἰτίαν φαμὲν εἶναι τὴν οὐσίαν καὶ τὸ τί ἦν εἶναι (ἀνάγεται γὰρ τὸ διὰ τί εἰς τὸν λόγον ἔσχατον, αἴτιον δὲ καὶ ἀρχὴ τὸ διὰ τί πρῶτον), ἑτέραν δὲ τὴν ὕλην καὶ τὸ ὑποκείμενον, τρίτην δὲ ὅθεν ἡ ἀρχὴ τῆς κινήσεως, τετάρτην δὲ τὴν ἀντικειμένην αἰτίαν ταύτῃ, τὸ οὗ ἕνεκα καὶ τἀγαθόν (τέλος γὰρ γενέσεως καὶ κινήσεως πάσης τοῦτʼ ἐστίν), τεθεώρηται μὲν οὖν ἱκανῶς περὶ αὐτῶν ἡμῖν ἐν τοῖς περὶ φύσεως,  ὅμως δὲ παραλάβωμεν καὶ τοὺς πρότερον ἡμῶν εἰς ἐπίσκεψιν τῶν ὄντων ἐλθόντας καὶ φιλοσοφήσαντας περὶ τῆς ἀληθείας. δῆλον γὰρ ὅτι κἀκεῖνοι λέγουσιν ἀρχάς τινας καὶ αἰτίας· ἐπελθοῦσιν οὖν ἔσται τι προὔργου τῇ μεθόδῳ τῇ νῦν· ἢ γὰρ ἕτερόν τι γένος εὑρήσομεν αἰτίας ἢ ταῖς νῦν λεγομέναις μᾶλλον πιστεύσομεν.—τῶν δὴ πρώτων φιλοσοφησάντων οἱ πλεῖστοι τὰς ἐν ὕλης εἴδει μόνας ᾠήθησαν ἀρχὰς εἶναι πάντων· ἐξ οὗ γὰρ ἔστιν ἅπαντα τὰ ὄντα καὶ ἐξ οὗ γίγνεται πρώτου καὶ εἰς ὃ φθείρεται τελευταῖον, τῆς μὲν οὐσίας ὑπομενούσης τοῖς δὲ πάθεσι μεταβαλλούσης, τοῦτο στοιχεῖον καὶ ταύτην ἀρχήν φασιν εἶναι τῶν ὄντων, καὶ διὰ τοῦτο οὔτε γίγνεσθαι οὐθὲν οἴονται οὔτε ἀπόλλυσθαι, ὡς τῆς τοιαύτης φύσεως ἀεὶ σωζομένης, ὥσπερ οὐδὲ τὸν Σωκράτην φαμὲν οὔτε γίγνεσθαι ἁπλῶς ὅταν γίγνηται καλὸς ἢ μουσικὸς οὔτε ἀπόλλυσθαι ὅταν ἀποβάλλῃ ταύτας τὰς ἕξεις, διὰ τὸ ὑπομένειν τὸ ὑποκείμενον τὸν Σωκράτην αὐτόν, οὕτως οὐδὲ τῶν ἄλλων οὐδέν· ἀεὶ γὰρ εἶναί τινα φύσιν ἢ μίαν ἢ πλείους μιᾶς ἐξ ὧν γίγνεται τἆλλα σωζομένης ἐκείνης. τὸ μέντοι πλῆθος καὶ τὸ εἶδος τῆς τοιαύτης ἀρχῆς οὐ τὸ αὐτὸ πάντες λέγουσιν, ἀλλὰ Θαλῆς μὲν ὁ τῆς τοιαύτης ἀρχηγὸς φιλοσοφίας ὕδωρ φησὶν εἶναι (διὸ καὶ τὴν γῆν ἐφʼ ὕδατος ἀπεφήνατο εἶναι), λαβὼν ἴσως τὴν ὑπόληψιν ταύτην ἐκ τοῦ πάντων ὁρᾶν τὴν τροφὴν ὑγρὰν οὖσαν καὶ αὐτὸ τὸ θερμὸν ἐκ τούτου γιγνόμενον καὶ τούτῳ ζῶν (τὸ δʼ ἐξ οὗ γίγνεται, τοῦτʼ ἐστὶν ἀρχὴ πάντων)—διά τε δὴ τοῦτο τὴν ὑπόληψιν λαβὼν ταύτην καὶ διὰ τὸ πάντων τὰ σπέρματα τὴν φύσιν ὑγρὰν ἔχειν, τὸ δʼ ὕδωρ ἀρχὴν τῆς φύσεως εἶναι τοῖς ὑγροῖς. εἰσὶ δέ τινες οἳ καὶ τοὺς παμπαλαίους καὶ πολὺ πρὸ τῆς νῦν γενέσεως καὶ πρώτους θεολογήσαντας οὕτως οἴονται περὶ τῆς φύσεως ὑπολαβεῖν· Ὠκεανόν τε γὰρ καὶ Τηθὺν ἐποίησαν τῆς γενέσεως πατέρας, καὶ τὸν ὅρκον τῶν θεῶν ὕδωρ, τὴν καλουμένην ὑπʼ αὐτῶν Στύγα · τιμιώτατον μὲν γὰρ τὸ πρεσβύτατον, ὅρκος δὲ τὸ τιμιώτατόν ἐστιν.  εἰ μὲν οὖν ἀρχαία τις αὕτη καὶ παλαιὰ τετύχηκεν οὖσα περὶ τῆς φύσεως ἡ δόξα, τάχʼ ἂν ἄδηλον εἴη, Θαλῆς μέντοι λέγεται οὕτως ἀποφήνασθαι περὶ τῆς πρώτης αἰτίας (Ἵππωνα γὰρ οὐκ ἄν τις ἀξιώσειε θεῖναι μετὰ τούτων διὰ τὴν εὐτέλειαν αὐτοῦ τῆς διανοίας)· Ἀναξιμένης δὲ ἀέρα καὶ Διογένης πρότερον ὕδατος καὶ μάλιστʼ ἀρχὴν τιθέασι τῶν ἁπλῶν σωμάτων, Ἵππασος δὲ πῦρ ὁ Μεταποντῖνος καὶ Ἡράκλειτος ὁ Ἐφέσιος, Ἐμπεδοκλῆς δὲ τὰ τέτταρα, πρὸς τοῖς εἰρημένοις γῆν προστιθεὶς τέταρτον (ταῦτα γὰρ ἀεὶ διαμένειν καὶ οὐ γίγνεσθαι ἀλλʼ ἢ πλήθει καὶ ὀλιγότητι, συγκρινόμενα καὶ διακρινόμενα εἰς ἕν τε καὶ ἐξ ἑνός)· Ἀναξαγόρας δὲ ὁ Κλαζομένιος τῇ μὲν ἡλικίᾳ πρότερος ὢν τούτου τοῖς δʼ ἔργοις ὕστερος ἀπείρους εἶναί φησι τὰς ἀρχάς· σχεδὸν γὰρ ἅπαντα τὰ ὁμοιομερῆ καθάπερ ὕδωρ ἢ πῦρ οὕτω γίγνεσθαι καὶ ἀπόλλυσθαί φησι, συγκρίσει καὶ διακρίσει μόνον, ἄλλως δʼ οὔτε γίγνεσθαι οὔτʼ ἀπόλλυσθαι ἀλλὰ διαμένειν ἀΐδια.—ἐκ μὲν οὖν τούτων μόνην τις αἰτίαν νομίσειεν ἂν τὴν ἐν ὕλης εἴδει λεγομένην· προϊόντων δʼ οὕτως, αὐτὸ τὸ πρᾶγμα ὡδοποίησεν αὐτοῖς καὶ συνηνάγκασε ζητεῖν· εἰ γὰρ ὅτι μάλιστα πᾶσα γένεσις καὶ φθορὰ ἔκ τινος ἑνὸς ἢ καὶ πλειόνων ἐστίν, διὰ τί τοῦτο συμβαίνει καὶ τί τὸ αἴτιον; οὐ γὰρ δὴ τό γε ὑποκείμενον αὐτὸ ποιεῖ μεταβάλλειν ἑαυτό· λέγω δʼ οἷον οὔτε τὸ ξύλον οὔτε ὁ χαλκὸς αἴτιος τοῦ μεταβάλλειν ἑκάτερον αὐτῶν, οὐδὲ ποιεῖ τὸ μὲν ξύλον κλίνην ὁ δὲ χαλκὸς ἀνδριάντα, ἀλλʼ ἕτερόν τι τῆς μεταβολῆς αἴτιον. τὸ δὲ τοῦτο ζητεῖν ἐστὶ τὸ τὴν ἑτέραν ἀρχὴν ζητεῖν, ὡς ἂν ἡμεῖς φαίημεν, ὅθεν ἡ ἀρχὴ τῆς κινήσεως. οἱ μὲν οὖν πάμπαν ἐξ ἀρχῆς ἁψάμενοι τῆς μεθόδου τῆς τοιαύτης καὶ ἓν φάσκοντες εἶναι τὸ ὑποκείμενον οὐθὲν ἐδυσχέραναν ἑαυτοῖς, ἀλλʼ ἔνιοί γε τῶν ἓν λεγόντων, ὥσπερ ἡττηθέντες ὑπὸ ταύτης τῆς ζητήσεως, τὸ ἓν ἀκίνητόν φασιν εἶναι καὶ τὴν φύσιν ὅλην οὐ μόνον κατὰ γένεσιν καὶ φθοράν (τοῦτο μὲν γὰρ ἀρχαῖόν τε καὶ πάντες ὡμολόγησαν) ἀλλὰ καὶ κατὰ τὴν ἄλλην μεταβολὴν πᾶσαν· καὶ τοῦτο αὐτῶν ἴδιόν ἐστιν.  τῶν μὲν οὖν ἓν φασκόντων εἶναι τὸ πᾶν οὐθενὶ συνέβη τὴν τοιαύτην συνιδεῖν αἰτίαν πλὴν εἰ ἄρα Παρμενίδῃ, καὶ τούτῳ κατὰ τοσοῦτον ὅσον οὐ μόνον ἓν ἀλλὰ καὶ δύο πως τίθησιν αἰτίας εἶναι· τοῖς δὲ δὴ πλείω ποιοῦσι μᾶλλον ἐνδέχεται λέγειν, οἷον τοῖς θερμὸν καὶ ψυχρὸν ἢ πῦρ καὶ γῆν· χρῶνται γὰρ ὡς κινητικὴν ἔχοντι τῷ πυρὶ τὴν φύσιν, ὕδατι δὲ καὶ γῇ καὶ τοῖς τοιούτοις τοὐναντίον.—μετὰ δὲ τούτους καὶ τὰς τοιαύτας ἀρχάς, ὡς οὐχ ἱκανῶν οὐσῶν γεννῆσαι τὴν τῶν ὄντων φύσιν, πάλιν ὑπʼ αὐτῆς τῆς ἀληθείας, ὥσπερ εἴπομεν, ἀναγκαζόμενοι τὴν ἐχομένην ἐζήτησαν ἀρχήν. τοῦ γὰρ εὖ καὶ καλῶς τὰ μὲν ἔχειν τὰ δὲ γίγνεσθαι τῶν ὄντων ἴσως οὔτε πῦρ οὔτε γῆν οὔτʼ ἄλλο τῶν τοιούτων οὐθὲν οὔτʼ εἰκὸς αἴτιον εἶναι οὔτʼ ἐκείνους οἰηθῆναι· οὐδʼ αὖ τῷ αὐτομάτῳ καὶ τύχῃ τοσοῦτον ἐπιτρέψαι πρᾶγμα καλῶς εἶχεν. νοῦν δή τις εἰπὼν ἐνεῖναι, καθάπερ ἐν τοῖς ζῴοις, καὶ ἐν τῇ φύσει τὸν αἴτιον τοῦ κόσμου καὶ τῆς τάξεως πάσης οἷον νήφων ἐφάνη παρʼ εἰκῇ λέγοντας τοὺς πρότερον. φανερῶς μὲν οὖν Ἀναξαγόραν ἴσμεν ἁψάμενον τούτων τῶν λόγων, αἰτίαν δʼ ἔχει πρότερον Ἑρμότιμος ὁ Κλαζομένιος εἰπεῖν. οἱ μὲν οὖν οὕτως ὑπολαμβάνοντες ἅμα τοῦ καλῶς τὴν αἰτίαν ἀρχὴν εἶναι τῶν ὄντων ἔθεσαν, καὶ τὴν τοιαύτην ὅθεν ἡ κίνησις ὑπάρχει τοῖς οὖσιν·\n\n\n\n \n\n \n\nὑποπτεύσειε δʼ ἄν τις Ἡσίοδον πρῶτον ζητῆσαι τὸ τοιοῦτον, κἂν εἴ τις ἄλλος ἔρωτα ἢ ἐπιθυμίαν ἐν τοῖς οὖσιν ἔθηκεν ὡς ἀρχήν, οἷον καὶ Παρμενίδης· καὶ γὰρ οὗτος κατασκευάζων τὴν τοῦ παντὸς γένεσιν  \n\nπρώτιστον μέν (φησιν) ἔρωτα θεῶν μητίσατο πάντων\n\n  Parmenides Fr. 13 (Diels), Ἡσίοδος δὲ  \n\nπάντων μὲν πρώτιστα χάος γένετʼ, αὐτὰρ ἔπειτα\n\n \n\nγαῖʼ εὐρύστερνος [Gap in source] \n\n \n\nἠδʼ ἔρος, ὃς πάντεσσι μεταπρέπει ἀθανάτοισιν,\n\n  Hes. Th. 116-20 ὡς δέον ἐν τοῖς οὖσιν ὑπάρχειν τινʼ αἰτίαν ἥτις κινήσει καὶ συνάξει τὰ πράγματα. τούτους μὲν οὖν πῶς χρὴ διανεῖμαι περὶ τοῦ τίς πρῶτος, ἐξέστω κρίνειν ὕστερον· ἐπεὶ δὲ καὶ τἀναντία τοῖς ἀγαθοῖς ἐνόντα ἐφαίνετο ἐν τῇ φύσει, καὶ οὐ μόνον τάξις καὶ τὸ καλὸν ἀλλὰ καὶ ἀταξία καὶ τὸ αἰσχρόν,  καὶ πλείω τὰ κακὰ τῶν ἀγαθῶν καὶ τὰ φαῦλα τῶν καλῶν, οὕτως ἄλλος τις φιλίαν εἰσήνεγκε καὶ νεῖκος, ἑκάτερον ἑκατέρων αἴτιον τούτων. εἰ γάρ τις ἀκολουθοίη καὶ λαμβάνοι πρὸς τὴν διάνοιαν καὶ μὴ πρὸς ἃ ψελλίζεται λέγων Ἐμπεδοκλῆς, εὑρήσει τὴν μὲν φιλίαν αἰτίαν οὖσαν τῶν ἀγαθῶν τὸ δὲ νεῖκος τῶν κακῶν· ὥστʼ εἴ τις φαίη τρόπον τινὰ καὶ λέγειν καὶ πρῶτον λέγειν τὸ κακὸν καὶ τὸ ἀγαθὸν ἀρχὰς Ἐμπεδοκλέα, τάχʼ ἂν λέγοι καλῶς, εἴπερ τὸ τῶν ἀγαθῶν ἁπάντων αἴτιον αὐτὸ τἀγαθόν ἐστι .—οὗτοι μὲν οὖν, ὥσπερ λέγομεν, καὶ μέχρι τούτου δυοῖν αἰτίαιν ὧν ἡμεῖς διωρίσαμεν ἐν τοῖς περὶ φύσεως ἡμμένοι φαίνονται, τῆς τε ὕλης καὶ τοῦ ὅθεν ἡ κίνησις, ἀμυδρῶς μέντοι καὶ οὐθὲν σαφῶς ἀλλʼ οἷον ἐν ταῖς μάχαις οἱ ἀγύμναστοι ποιοῦσιν· καὶ γὰρ ἐκεῖνοι περιφερόμενοι τύπτουσι πολλάκις καλὰς πληγάς, ἀλλʼ οὔτε ἐκεῖνοι ἀπὸ ἐπιστήμης οὔτε οὗτοι ἐοίκασιν εἰδέναι ὅ τι λέγουσιν· σχεδὸν γὰρ οὐθὲν χρώμενοι φαίνονται τούτοις ἀλλʼ ἢ κατὰ μικρόν. Ἀναξαγόρας τε γὰρ μηχανῇ χρῆται τῷ νῷ πρὸς τὴν κοσμοποιίαν, καὶ ὅταν ἀπορήσῃ διὰ τίνʼ αἰτίαν ἐξ ἀνάγκης ἐστί, τότε παρέλκει αὐτόν, ἐν δὲ τοῖς ἄλλοις πάντα μᾶλλον αἰτιᾶται τῶν γιγνομένων ἢ νοῦν, καὶ Ἐμπεδοκλῆς ἐπὶ πλέον μὲν τούτου χρῆται τοῖς αἰτίοις, οὐ μὴν οὔθʼ ἱκανῶς, οὔτʼ ἐν τούτοις εὑρίσκει τὸ ὁμολογούμενον. πολλαχοῦ γοῦν αὐτῷ ἡ μὲν φιλία διακρίνει τὸ δὲ νεῖκος συγκρίνει. ὅταν μὲν γὰρ εἰς τὰ στοιχεῖα διίστηται τὸ πᾶν ὑπὸ τοῦ νείκους, τότε τὸ πῦρ εἰς ἓν συγκρίνεται καὶ τῶν ἄλλων στοιχείων ἕκαστον· ὅταν δὲ πάλιν ὑπὸ τῆς φιλίας συνίωσιν εἰς τὸ ἕν, ἀναγκαῖον ἐξ ἑκάστου τὰ μόρια διακρίνεσθαι πάλιν.—Ἐμπεδοκλῆς μὲν οὖν παρὰ τοὺς πρότερον πρῶτος τὸ τὴν αἰτίαν διελεῖν εἰσήνεγκεν, οὐ μίαν ποιήσας τὴν τῆς κινήσεως ἀρχὴν ἀλλʼ ἑτέρας τε καὶ ἐναντίας, ἔτι δὲ τὰ ὡς ἐν ὕλης εἴδει λεγόμενα στοιχεῖα τέτταρα πρῶτος εἶπεν (οὐ μὴν χρῆταί γε τέτταρσιν ἀλλʼ ὡς δυσὶν οὖσι μόνοις,  πυρὶ μὲν καθʼ αὑτὸ τοῖς δʼ ἀντικειμένοις ὡς μιᾷ φύσει, γῇ τε καὶ ἀέρι καὶ ὕδατι· λάβοι δʼ ἄν τις αὐτὸ θεωρῶν ἐκ τῶν ἐπῶν)·—οὗτος μὲν οὖν, ὥσπερ λέγομεν, οὕτω τε καὶ τοσαύτας εἴρηκε τὰς ἀρχάς· Λεύκιππος δὲ καὶ ὁ ἑταῖρος αὐτοῦ Δημόκριτος στοιχεῖα μὲν τὸ πλῆρες καὶ τὸ κενὸν εἶναί φασι, λέγοντες τὸ μὲν ὂν τὸ δὲ μὴ ὄν, τούτων δὲ τὸ μὲν πλῆρες καὶ στερεὸν τὸ ὄν, τὸ δὲ κενὸν τὸ μὴ ὄν (διὸ καὶ οὐθὲν μᾶλλον τὸ ὂν τοῦ μὴ ὄντος εἶναί φασιν, ὅτι οὐδὲ τοῦ κενοῦ τὸ σῶμα), αἴτια δὲ τῶν ὄντων ταῦτα ὡς ὕλην. καὶ καθάπερ οἱ ἓν ποιοῦντες τὴν ὑποκειμένην οὐσίαν τἆλλα τοῖς πάθεσιν αὐτῆς γεννῶσι, τὸ μανὸν καὶ τὸ πυκνὸν ἀρχὰς τιθέμενοι τῶν παθημάτων, τὸν αὐτὸν τρόπον καὶ οὗτοι τὰς διαφορὰς αἰτίας τῶν ἄλλων εἶναί φασιν. ταύτας μέντοι τρεῖς εἶναι λέγουσι, σχῆμά τε καὶ τάξιν καὶ θέσιν· διαφέρειν γάρ φασι τὸ ὂν ῥυσμῷ καὶ διαθιγῇ καὶ τροπῇ μόνον· τούτων δὲ ὁ μὲν ῥυσμὸς σχῆμά ἐστιν ἡ δὲ διαθιγὴ τάξις ἡ δὲ τροπὴ θέσις· διαφέρει γὰρ τὸ μὲν Α τοῦ Ν σχήματι τὸ δὲ ΑΝ τοῦ ΝΑ τάξει τὸ δὲ Ζ τοῦ Η θέσει. περὶ δὲ κινήσεως, ὅθεν ἢ πῶς ὑπάρξει τοῖς οὖσι, καὶ οὗτοι παραπλησίως τοῖς ἄλλοις ῥᾳθύμως ἀφεῖσαν. περὶ μὲν οὖν τῶν δύο αἰτιῶν, ὥσπερ λέγομεν, ἐπὶ τοσοῦτον ἔοικεν ἐζητῆσθαι παρὰ τῶν πρότερον.\n\n \n\n \n\n \n\nἐν δὲ τούτοις καὶ πρὸ τούτων οἱ καλούμενοι Πυθαγόρειοι τῶν μαθημάτων ἁψάμενοι πρῶτοι ταῦτά τε προήγαγον, καὶ ἐντραφέντες ἐν αὐτοῖς τὰς τούτων ἀρχὰς τῶν ὄντων ἀρχὰς ᾠήθησαν εἶναι πάντων. ἐπεὶ δὲ τούτων οἱ ἀριθμοὶ φύσει πρῶτοι, ἐν δὲ τούτοις ἐδόκουν θεωρεῖν ὁμοιώματα πολλὰ τοῖς οὖσι καὶ γιγνομένοις, μᾶλλον ἢ ἐν πυρὶ καὶ γῇ καὶ ὕδατι, ὅτι τὸ μὲν τοιονδὶ τῶν ἀριθμῶν πάθος δικαιοσύνη τὸ δὲ τοιονδὶ ψυχή τε καὶ νοῦς ἕτερον δὲ καιρὸς καὶ τῶν ἄλλων ὡς εἰπεῖν ἕκαστον ὁμοίως, ἔτι δὲ τῶν ἁρμονιῶν ἐν ἀριθμοῖς ὁρῶντες τὰ πάθη καὶ τοὺς λόγους,—ἐπεὶ δὴ τὰ μὲν ἄλλα τοῖς ἀριθμοῖς ἐφαίνοντο τὴν φύσιν ἀφωμοιῶσθαι πᾶσαν, οἱ δʼ ἀριθμοὶ πάσης τῆς φύσεως πρῶτοι,  τὰ τῶν ἀριθμῶν στοιχεῖα τῶν ὄντων στοιχεῖα πάντων ὑπέλαβον εἶναι, καὶ τὸν ὅλον οὐρανὸν ἁρμονίαν εἶναι καὶ ἀριθμόν· καὶ ὅσα εἶχον ὁμολογούμενα ἔν τε τοῖς ἀριθμοῖς καὶ ταῖς ἁρμονίαις πρὸς τὰ τοῦ οὐρανοῦ πάθη καὶ μέρη καὶ πρὸς τὴν ὅλην διακόσμησιν, ταῦτα συνάγοντες ἐφήρμοττον. κἂν εἴ τί που διέλειπε, προσεγλίχοντο τοῦ συνειρομένην πᾶσαν αὐτοῖς εἶναι τὴν πραγματείαν· λέγω δʼ οἷον, ἐπειδὴ τέλειον ἡ δεκὰς εἶναι δοκεῖ καὶ πᾶσαν περιειληφέναι τὴν τῶν ἀριθμῶν φύσιν, καὶ τὰ φερόμενα κατὰ τὸν οὐρανὸν δέκα μὲν εἶναί φασιν, ὄντων δὲ ἐννέα μόνον τῶν φανερῶν διὰ τοῦτο δεκάτην τὴν ἀντίχθονα ποιοῦσιν. διώρισται δὲ περὶ τούτων ἐν ἑτέροις ἡμῖν ἀκριβέστερον. ἀλλʼ οὗ δὴ χάριν ἐπερχόμεθα, τοῦτό ἐστιν ὅπως λάβωμεν καὶ παρὰ τούτων τίνας εἶναι τιθέασι τὰς ἀρχὰς καὶ πῶς εἰς τὰς εἰρημένας ἐμπίπτουσιν αἰτίας. φαίνονται δὴ καὶ οὗτοι τὸν ἀριθμὸν νομίζοντες ἀρχὴν εἶναι καὶ ὡς ὕλην τοῖς οὖσι καὶ ὡς πάθη τε καὶ ἕξεις, τοῦ δὲ ἀριθμοῦ στοιχεῖα τό τε ἄρτιον καὶ τὸ περιττόν, τούτων δὲ τὸ μὲν πεπερασμένον τὸ δὲ ἄπειρον, τὸ δʼ ἓν ἐξ ἀμφοτέρων εἶναι τούτων (καὶ γὰρ ἄρτιον εἶναι καὶ περιττόν), τὸν δʼ ἀριθμὸν ἐκ τοῦ ἑνός, ἀριθμοὺς δέ, καθάπερ εἴρηται, τὸν ὅλον οὐρανόν.—ἕτεροι δὲ τῶν αὐτῶν τούτων τὰς ἀρχὰς δέκα λέγουσιν εἶναι τὰς κατὰ συστοιχίαν λεγομένας, πέρας  ἄπειρον, περιττὸν  ἄρτιον, ἓν  πλῆθος, δεξιὸν  ἀριστερόν, ἄρρεν   θῆλυ, ἠρεμοῦν  κινούμενον, εὐθὺ  καμπύλον, φῶς  σκότος, ἀγαθὸν  κακόν, τετράγωνον  ἑτερόμηκες· ὅνπερ τρόπον ἔοικε καὶ Ἀλκμαίων ὁ Κροτωνιάτης ὑπολαβεῖν, καὶ ἤτοι οὗτος παρʼ ἐκείνων ἢ ἐκεῖνοι παρὰ τούτου παρέλαβον τὸν λόγον τοῦτον· καὶ γὰρ  Ἀλκμαίων   ἀπεφήνατο  παραπλησίως τούτοις· φησὶ γὰρ εἶναι δύο τὰ πολλὰ τῶν ἀνθρωπίνων, λέγων τὰς ἐναντιότητας οὐχ ὥσπερ οὗτοι διωρισμένας ἀλλὰ τὰς τυχούσας, οἷον λευκὸν μέλαν, γλυκὺ πικρόν, ἀγαθὸν κακόν, μέγα μικρόν. οὗτος μὲν οὖν ἀδιορίστως ἀπέρριψε περὶ τῶν λοιπῶν,  οἱ δὲ Πυθαγόρειοι καὶ πόσαι καὶ τίνες αἱ ἐναντιώσεις ἀπεφήναντο. παρὰ μὲν οὖν τούτων ἀμφοῖν τοσοῦτον ἔστι λαβεῖν, ὅτι τἀναντία ἀρχαὶ τῶν ὄντων· τὸ δʼ ὅσαι παρὰ τῶν ἑτέρων, καὶ τίνες αὗταί εἰσιν. πῶς μέντοι πρὸς τὰς εἰρημένας αἰτίας ἐνδέχεται συνάγειν, σαφῶς μὲν οὐ διήρθρωται παρʼ ἐκείνων, ἐοίκασι δʼ ὡς ἐν ὕλης εἴδει τὰ στοιχεῖα τάττειν· ἐκ τούτων γὰρ ὡς ἐνυπαρχόντων συνεστάναι καὶ πεπλάσθαι φασὶ τὴν οὐσίαν.—τῶν μὲν οὖν παλαιῶν καὶ πλείω λεγόντων τὰ στοιχεῖα τῆς φύσεως ἐκ τούτων ἱκανόν ἐστι θεωρῆσαι τὴν διάνοιαν· εἰσὶ δέ τινες οἳ περὶ τοῦ παντὸς ὡς μιᾶς οὔσης φύσεως ἀπεφήναντο, τρόπον δὲ οὐ τὸν αὐτὸν πάντες οὔτε τοῦ καλῶς οὔτε τοῦ κατὰ τὴν φύσιν. εἰς μὲν οὖν τὴν νῦν σκέψιν τῶν αἰτίων οὐδαμῶς συναρμόττει περὶ αὐτῶν ὁ λόγος (οὐ γὰρ ὥσπερ ἔνιοι τῶν φυσιολόγων ἓν ὑποθέμενοι τὸ ὂν ὅμως γεννῶσιν ὡς ἐξ ὕλης τοῦ ἑνός, ἀλλʼ ἕτερον τρόπον οὗτοι λέγουσιν· ἐκεῖνοι μὲν γὰρ προστιθέασι κίνησιν, γεννῶντές γε τὸ πᾶν, οὗτοι δὲ ἀκίνητον εἶναί φασιν)· οὐ μὴν ἀλλὰ τοσοῦτόν γε οἰκεῖόν ἐστι τῇ νῦν σκέψει. Παρμενίδης μὲν γὰρ ἔοικε τοῦ κατὰ τὸν λόγον ἑνὸς ἅπτεσθαι, Μέλισσος δὲ τοῦ κατὰ τὴν ὕλην (διὸ καὶ ὁ μὲν πεπερασμένον ὁ δʼ ἄπειρόν φησιν εἶναι αὐτό)· Ξενοφάνης δὲ πρῶτος τούτων ἑνίσας (ὁ γὰρ Παρμενίδης τούτου λέγεται γενέσθαι μαθητής) οὐθὲν διεσαφήνισεν, οὐδὲ τῆς φύσεως τούτων οὐδετέρας ἔοικε θιγεῖν, ἀλλʼ εἰς τὸν ὅλον οὐρανὸν ἀποβλέψας τὸ ἓν εἶναί φησι τὸν θεόν. οὗτοι μὲν οὖν, καθάπερ εἴπομεν, ἀφετέοι πρὸς τὴν νῦν ζήτησιν, οἱ μὲν δύο καὶ πάμπαν ὡς ὄντες μικρὸν ἀγροικότεροι, Ξενοφάνης καὶ Μέλισσος· Παρμενίδης δὲ μᾶλλον βλέπων ἔοικέ που λέγειν· παρὰ γὰρ τὸ ὂν τὸ μὴ ὂν οὐθὲν ἀξιῶν εἶναι, ἐξ ἀνάγκης ἓν οἴεται εἶναι, τὸ ὄν, καὶ ἄλλο οὐθέν (περὶ οὗ σαφέστερον ἐν τοῖς περὶ φύσεως εἰρήκαμεν), ἀναγκαζόμενος δʼ ἀκολουθεῖν τοῖς φαινομένοις, καὶ τὸ ἓν μὲν κατὰ τὸν λόγον πλείω δὲ κατὰ τὴν αἴσθησιν ὑπολαμβάνων εἶναι, δύο τὰς αἰτίας καὶ δύο τὰς ἀρχὰς πάλιν τίθησι, θερμὸν καὶ ψυχρόν, οἷον πῦρ καὶ γῆν λέγων·  τούτων δὲ κατὰ μὲν τὸ ὂν τὸ θερμὸν τάττει θάτερον δὲ κατὰ τὸ μὴ ὄν.—ἐκ μὲν οὖν τῶν εἰρημένων καὶ παρὰ τῶν συνηδρευκότων ἤδη τῷ λόγῳ σοφῶν ταῦτα παρειλήφαμεν, παρὰ μὲν τῶν πρώτων σωματικήν τε τὴν ἀρχήν (ὕδωρ γὰρ καὶ πῦρ καὶ τὰ τοιαῦτα σώματά ἐστιν), καὶ τῶν μὲν μίαν τῶν δὲ πλείους τὰς ἀρχὰς τὰς σωματικάς, ἀμφοτέρων μέντοι ταύτας ὡς ἐν ὕλης εἴδει τιθέντων, παρὰ δέ τινων ταύτην τε τὴν αἰτίαν τιθέντων καὶ πρὸς ταύτῃ τὴν ὅθεν ἡ κίνησις, καὶ ταύτην παρὰ τῶν μὲν μίαν παρὰ τῶν δὲ δύο. μέχρι μὲν οὖν τῶν Ἰταλικῶν καὶ χωρὶς ἐκείνων μορυχώτερον εἰρήκασιν οἱ ἄλλοι περὶ αὐτῶν, πλὴν ὥσπερ εἴπομεν δυοῖν τε αἰτίαιν τυγχάνουσι κεχρημένοι, καὶ τούτων τὴν ἑτέραν οἱ μὲν μίαν οἱ δὲ δύο ποιοῦσι, τὴν ὅθεν ἡ κίνησις· οἱ δὲ Πυθαγόρειοι δύο μὲν τὰς ἀρχὰς κατὰ τὸν αὐτὸν εἰρήκασι τρόπον, τοσοῦτον δὲ προσεπέθεσαν ὃ καὶ ἴδιόν ἐστιν αὐτῶν, ὅτι τὸ πεπερασμένον καὶ τὸ ἄπειρον  οὐχ ἑτέρας τινὰς ᾠήθησαν εἶναι φύσεις, οἷον πῦρ ἢ γῆν ἤ τι τοιοῦτον ἕτερον, ἀλλʼ αὐτὸ τὸ ἄπειρον καὶ αὐτὸ τὸ ἓν οὐσίαν εἶναι τούτων ὧν κατηγοροῦνται, διὸ καὶ ἀριθμὸν εἶναι τὴν οὐσίαν πάντων. περί τε τούτων οὖν τοῦτον ἀπεφήναντο τὸν τρόπον, καὶ περὶ τοῦ τί ἐστιν ἤρξαντο μὲν λέγειν καὶ ὁρίζεσθαι, λίαν δʼ ἁπλῶς ἐπραγματεύθησαν. ὡρίζοντό τε γὰρ ἐπιπολαίως, καὶ ᾧ πρώτῳ ὑπάρξειεν ὁ λεχθεὶς ὅρος, τοῦτʼ εἶναι τὴν οὐσίαν τοῦ πράγματος ἐνόμιζον, ὥσπερ εἴ τις οἴοιτο ταὐτὸν εἶναι διπλάσιον καὶ τὴν δυάδα διότι πρῶτον ὑπάρχει τοῖς δυσὶ τὸ διπλάσιον. ἀλλʼ οὐ ταὐτὸν ἴσως ἐστὶ τὸ εἶναι διπλασίῳ καὶ δυάδι· εἰ δὲ μή, πολλὰ τὸ ἓν ἔσται, ὃ κἀκείνοις συνέβαινεν. παρὰ μὲν οὖν τῶν πρότερον καὶ τῶν ἄλλων τοσαῦτα ἔστι λαβεῖν.\n\n \n\n \n\n \n\nμετὰ δὲ τὰς εἰρημένας φιλοσοφίας ἡ Πλάτωνος ἐπεγένετο πραγματεία, τὰ μὲν πολλὰ τούτοις ἀκολουθοῦσα, τὰ δὲ καὶ ἴδια παρὰ τὴν τῶν Ἰταλικῶν ἔχουσα φιλοσοφίαν. ἐκ νέου τε γὰρ συνήθης γενόμενος πρῶτον Κρατύλῳ καὶ ταῖς Ἡρακλειτείοις δόξαις, ὡς ἁπάντων τῶν αἰσθητῶν ἀεὶ ῥεόντων καὶ ἐπιστήμης περὶ αὐτῶν οὐκ οὔσης, ταῦτα μὲν καὶ ὕστερον οὕτως ὑπέλαβεν·  Σωκράτους δὲ περὶ μὲν τὰ ἠθικὰ πραγματευομένου περὶ δὲ τῆς ὅλης φύσεως οὐθέν, ἐν μέντοι τούτοις τὸ καθόλου ζητοῦντος καὶ περὶ ὁρισμῶν ἐπιστήσαντος πρώτου τὴν διάνοιαν, ἐκεῖνον ἀποδεξάμενος διὰ τὸ τοιοῦτον ὑπέλαβεν ὡς περὶ ἑτέρων τοῦτο γιγνόμενον καὶ οὐ τῶν αἰσθητῶν· ἀδύνατον γὰρ εἶναι τὸν κοινὸν ὅρον τῶν αἰσθητῶν τινός, ἀεί γε μεταβαλλόντων. οὗτος οὖν τὰ μὲν τοιαῦτα τῶν ὄντων ἰδέας προσηγόρευσε, τὰ δʼ αἰσθητὰ παρὰ ταῦτα καὶ κατὰ ταῦτα λέγεσθαι πάντα· κατὰ μέθεξιν γὰρ εἶναι τὰ πολλὰ ὁμώνυμα τοῖς εἴδεσιν. τὴν δὲ μέθεξιν τοὔνομα μόνον μετέβαλεν· οἱ μὲν γὰρ Πυθαγόρειοι μιμήσει τὰ ὄντα φασὶν εἶναι τῶν ἀριθμῶν, Πλάτων δὲ μεθέξει, τοὔνομα μεταβαλών. τὴν μέντοι γε μέθεξιν ἢ τὴν μίμησιν ἥτις ἂν εἴη τῶν εἰδῶν ἀφεῖσαν ἐν κοινῷ ζητεῖν. ἔτι δὲ παρὰ τὰ αἰσθητὰ καὶ τὰ εἴδη τὰ μαθηματικὰ τῶν πραγμάτων εἶναί φησι μεταξύ, διαφέροντα τῶν μὲν αἰσθητῶν τῷ ἀΐδια καὶ ἀκίνητα εἶναι, τῶν δʼ εἰδῶν τῷ τὰ μὲν πόλλʼ ἄττα ὅμοια εἶναι τὸ δὲ εἶδος αὐτὸ ἓν ἕκαστον μόνον. ἐπεὶ δʼ αἴτια τὰ εἴδη τοῖς ἄλλοις, τἀκείνων στοιχεῖα πάντων ᾠήθη τῶν ὄντων εἶναι στοιχεῖα. ὡς μὲν οὖν ὕλην τὸ μέγα καὶ τὸ μικρὸν εἶναι ἀρχάς, ὡς δʼ οὐσίαν τὸ ἕν· ἐξ ἐκείνων γὰρ κατὰ μέθεξιν τοῦ ἑνὸς  εἶναι τοὺς ἀριθμούς. τὸ μέντοι γε ἓν οὐσίαν εἶναι, καὶ μὴ ἕτερόν γέ τι ὂν λέγεσθαι ἕν, παραπλησίως τοῖς Πυθαγορείοις ἔλεγε, καὶ τὸ τοὺς ἀριθμοὺς αἰτίους εἶναι τοῖς ἄλλοις τῆς οὐσίας ὡσαύτως ἐκείνοις· τὸ δὲ ἀντὶ τοῦ ἀπείρου ὡς ἑνὸς δυάδα ποιῆσαι, τὸ δʼ ἄπειρον ἐκ μεγάλου καὶ μικροῦ, τοῦτʼ ἴδιον· καὶ ἔτι ὁ μὲν τοὺς ἀριθμοὺς παρὰ τὰ αἰσθητά, οἱ δʼ ἀριθμοὺς εἶναί φασιν αὐτὰ τὰ πράγματα, καὶ τὰ μαθηματικὰ μεταξὺ τούτων οὐ τιθέασιν. τὸ μὲν οὖν τὸ ἓν καὶ τοὺς ἀριθμοὺς παρὰ τὰ πράγματα ποιῆσαι, καὶ μὴ ὥσπερ οἱ Πυθαγόρειοι, καὶ ἡ τῶν εἰδῶν εἰσαγωγὴ διὰ τὴν ἐν τοῖς λόγοις ἐγένετο σκέψιν (οἱ γὰρ πρότεροι διαλεκτικῆς οὐ μετεῖχον), τὸ δὲ δυάδα ποιῆσαι τὴν ἑτέραν φύσιν διὰ τὸ τοὺς ἀριθμοὺς ἔξω τῶν πρώτων εὐφυῶς ἐξ αὐτῆς γεννᾶσθαι ὥσπερ ἔκ τινος ἐκμαγείου.  καίτοι συμβαίνει γʼ ἐναντίως· οὐ γὰρ εὔλογον οὕτως. οἱ μὲν γὰρ ἐκ τῆς ὕλης πολλὰ ποιοῦσιν, τὸ δʼ εἶδος ἅπαξ γεννᾷ μόνον, φαίνεται δʼ ἐκ μιᾶς ὕλης μία τράπεζα, ὁ δὲ τὸ εἶδος ἐπιφέρων εἷς ὢν πολλὰς ποιεῖ. ὁμοίως δʼ ἔχει καὶ τὸ ἄρρεν πρὸς τὸ θῆλυ· τὸ μὲν γὰρ ὑπὸ μιᾶς πληροῦται ὀχείας, τὸ δʼ ἄρρεν πολλὰ πληροῖ· καίτοι ταῦτα μιμήματα τῶν ἀρχῶν ἐκείνων ἐστίν. Πλάτων μὲν οὖν περὶ τῶν ζητουμένων οὕτω διώρισεν· φανερὸν δʼ ἐκ τῶν εἰρημένων ὅτι δυοῖν αἰτίαιν μόνον κέχρηται, τῇ τε τοῦ τί ἐστι καὶ τῇ κατὰ τὴν ὕλην (τὰ γὰρ εἴδη τοῦ τί ἐστιν αἴτια τοῖς ἄλλοις, τοῖς δʼ εἴδεσι τὸ ἕν), καὶ τίς ἡ ὕλη ἡ ὑποκειμένη καθʼ ἧς τὰ εἴδη μὲν ἐπὶ τῶν αἰσθητῶν τὸ δʼ ἓν ἐν τοῖς εἴδεσι λέγεται, ὅτι αὕτη δυάς ἐστι, τὸ μέγα καὶ τὸ μικρόν, ἔτι δὲ τὴν τοῦ εὖ καὶ τοῦ κακῶς αἰτίαν τοῖς στοιχείοις ἀπέδωκεν ἑκατέροις ἑκατέραν, ὥσπερ φαμὲν καὶ τῶν προτέρων ἐπιζητῆσαί τινας φιλοσόφων, οἷον Ἐμπεδοκλέα καὶ Ἀναξαγόραν. \n\n\n\n \n\n \n\nσυντόμως μὲν οὖν καὶ κεφαλαιωδῶς ἐπεληλύθαμεν τίνες τε καὶ πῶς τυγχάνουσιν εἰρηκότες περί τε τῶν ἀρχῶν καὶ τῆς ἀληθείας· ὅμως δὲ τοσοῦτόν γʼ ἔχομεν ἐξ αὐτῶν, ὅτι τῶν λεγόντων περὶ ἀρχῆς καὶ αἰτίας οὐθεὶς ἔξω τῶν ἐν τοῖς περὶ φύσεως ἡμῖν διωρισμένων εἴρηκεν, ἀλλὰ πάντες ἀμυδρῶς μὲν ἐκείνων δέ πως φαίνονται θιγγάνοντες. οἱ μὲν γὰρ ὡς ὕλην τὴν ἀρχὴν λέγουσιν, ἄν τε μίαν ἄν τε πλείους ὑποθῶσι, καὶ ἐάν τε σῶμα ἐάν τε ἀσώματον τοῦτο τιθῶσιν (οἷον Πλάτων μὲν τὸ μέγα καὶ τὸ μικρὸν λέγων, οἱ δʼ Ἰταλικοὶ τὸ ἄπειρον, Ἐμπεδοκλῆς δὲ πῦρ καὶ γῆν καὶ ὕδωρ καὶ ἀέρα, Ἀναξαγόρας δὲ τὴν τῶν ὁμοιομερῶν ἀπειρίαν· οὗτοί τε δὴ πάντες τῆς τοιαύτης αἰτίας ἡμμένοι εἰσί, καὶ ἔτι ὅσοι ἀέρα ἢ πῦρ ἢ ὕδωρ ἢ πυρὸς μὲν πυκνότερον ἀέρος δὲ λεπτότερον· καὶ γὰρ τοιοῦτόν τινες εἰρήκασιν εἶναι τὸ πρῶτον στοιχεῖον)·—οὗτοι μὲν οὖν ταύτης τῆς αἰτίας ἥψαντο μόνον, ἕτεροι δέ τινες ὅθεν ἡ ἀρχὴ τῆς κινήσεως (οἷον ὅσοι φιλίαν καὶ νεῖκος ἢ νοῦν ἢ ἔρωτα ποιοῦσιν ἀρχήν)· τὸ δὲ τί ἦν εἶναι καὶ τὴν οὐσίαν σαφῶς μὲν οὐθεὶς ἀποδέδωκε,  μάλιστα δʼ οἱ τὰ εἴδη τιθέντες λέγουσιν (οὔτε γὰρ ὡς ὕλην τοῖς αἰσθητοῖς τὰ εἴδη καὶ τὸ ἓν τοῖς εἴδεσιν οὔθʼ ὡς ἐντεῦθεν τὴν ἀρχὴν τῆς κινήσεως γιγνομένην ὑπολαμβάνουσιν—ἀκινησίας γὰρ αἴτια μᾶλλον καὶ τοῦ ἐν ἠρεμίᾳ εἶναι φασιν—ἀλλὰ τὸ τί ἦν εἶναι ἑκάστῳ τῶν ἄλλων τὰ εἴδη παρέχονται, τοῖς δʼ εἴδεσι τὸ ἕν)· τὸ δʼ οὗ ἕνεκα αἱ πράξεις καὶ αἱ μεταβολαὶ καὶ αἱ κινήσεις τρόπον μέν τινα λέγουσιν αἴτιον, οὕτω δὲ οὐ λέγουσιν οὐδʼ ὅνπερ πέφυκεν. οἱ μὲν γὰρ νοῦν λέγοντες ἢ φιλίαν ὡς ἀγαθὸν μὲν ταύτας τὰς αἰτίας τιθέασιν, οὐ μὴν ὡς ἕνεκά γε τούτων ἢ ὂν ἢ γιγνόμενόν τι τῶν ὄντων ἀλλʼ ὡς ἀπὸ τούτων τὰς κινήσεις οὔσας λέγουσιν· ὡς δʼ αὔτως καὶ οἱ τὸ ἓν ἢ τὸ ὂν φάσκοντες εἶναι τὴν τοιαύτην φύσιν τῆς μὲν οὐσίας αἴτιόν φασιν εἶναι, οὐ μὴν τούτου γε ἕνεκα ἢ εἶναι ἢ γίγνεσθαι, ὥστε λέγειν τε καὶ μὴ λέγειν πως συμβαίνει αὐτοῖς τἀγαθὸν αἴτιον· οὐ γὰρ ἁπλῶς ἀλλὰ κατὰ συμβεβηκὸς λέγουσιν.—ὅτι μὲν οὖν ὀρθῶς διώρισται περὶ τῶν αἰτίων καὶ πόσα καὶ ποῖα, μαρτυρεῖν ἐοίκασιν ἡμῖν καὶ οὗτοι πάντες, οὐ δυνάμενοι θιγεῖν ἄλλης αἰτίας, πρὸς δὲ τούτοις ὅτι ζητητέαι αἱ ἀρχαὶ ἢ οὕτως ἅπασαι ἢ τινὰ τρόπον τοιοῦτον, δῆλον· πῶς δὲ τούτων ἕκαστος εἴρηκε καὶ πῶς ἔχει περὶ τῶν ἀρχῶν, τὰς ἐνδεχομένας ἀπορίας μετὰ τοῦτο διέλθωμεν περὶ αὐτῶν.\n\n\n\n \n\n \n\nὅσοι μὲν οὖν ἕν τε τὸ πᾶν καὶ μίαν τινὰ φύσιν ὡς ὕλην τιθέασι, καὶ ταύτην σωματικὴν καὶ μέγεθος ἔχουσαν, δῆλον ὅτι πολλαχῶς ἁμαρτάνουσιν. τῶν γὰρ σωμάτων τὰ στοιχεῖα τιθέασι μόνον, τῶν δʼ ἀσωμάτων οὔ, ὄντων καὶ ἀσωμάτων. καὶ περὶ γενέσεως καὶ φθορᾶς ἐπιχειροῦντες τὰς αἰτίας λέγειν, καὶ περὶ πάντων φυσιολογοῦντες, τὸ τῆς κινήσεως αἴτιον ἀναιροῦσιν. ἔτι δὲ τῷ τὴν οὐσίαν μηθενὸς αἰτίαν τιθέναι μηδὲ τὸ τί ἐστι, καὶ πρὸς τούτοις τῷ ῥᾳδίως τῶν ἁπλῶν σωμάτων λέγειν ἀρχὴν ὁτιοῦν πλὴν γῆς, οὐκ ἐπισκεψάμενοι τὴν ἐξ ἀλλήλων γένεσιν πῶς ποιοῦνται, λέγω δὲ πῦρ καὶ ὕδωρ καὶ γῆν καὶ ἀέρα. τὰ μὲν γὰρ συγκρίσει τὰ δὲ διακρίσει ἐξ ἀλλήλων γίγνεται, τοῦτο δὲ πρὸς τὸ πρότερον εἶναι καὶ ὕστερον διαφέρει πλεῖστον. τῇ μὲν γὰρ ἂν δόξειε στοιχειωδέστατον εἶναι πάντων ἐξ οὗ γίγνονται συγκρίσει πρώτου,  τοιοῦτον δὲ τὸ μικρομερέστατον καὶ λεπτότατον ἂν εἴη τῶν σωμάτων (διόπερ ὅσοι πῦρ ἀρχὴν τιθέασι, μάλιστα ὁμολογουμένως ἂν τῷ λόγῳ τούτῳ λέγοιεν· τοιοῦτον δὲ καὶ τῶν ἄλλων ἕκαστος ὁμολογεῖ τὸ στοιχεῖον εἶναι τὸ τῶν σωμάτων· οὐθεὶς γοῦν ἠξίωσε τῶν ἓν λεγόντων γῆν εἶναι στοιχεῖον, δηλονότι διὰ τὴν μεγαλομέρειαν, τῶν δὲ τριῶν ἕκαστον στοιχείων εἴληφέ τινα κριτήν, οἱ μὲν γὰρ πῦρ οἱ δʼ ὕδωρ οἱ δʼ ἀέρα τοῦτʼ εἶναί φασιν· καίτοι διὰ τί ποτʼ οὐ καὶ τὴν γῆν λέγουσιν, ὥσπερ οἱ πολλοὶ τῶν ἀνθρώπων; πάντα γὰρ εἶναί φασι γῆν, φησὶ δὲ καὶ Ἡσίοδος τὴν γῆν πρώτην γενέσθαι τῶν σωμάτων· οὕτως ἀρχαίαν καὶ δημοτικὴν συμβέβηκεν εἶναι τὴν ὑπόληψιν)·—κατὰ μὲν οὖν τοῦτον τὸν λόγον οὔτʼ εἴ τις τούτων τι λέγει πλὴν πυρός, οὔτʼ εἴ τις ἀέρος μὲν πυκνότερον τοῦτο τίθησιν ὕδατος δὲ λεπτότερον, οὐκ ὀρθῶς ἂν λέγοι· εἰ δʼ ἔστι τὸ τῇ γενέσει ὕστερον τῇ φύσει πρότερον, τὸ δὲ πεπεμμένον καὶ συγκεκριμένον ὕστερον τῇ γενέσει, τοὐναντίον ἂν εἴη τούτων, ὕδωρ μὲν ἀέρος πρότερον γῆ δὲ ὕδατος.—περὶ μὲν οὖν τῶν μίαν τιθεμένων αἰτίαν οἵαν εἴπομεν, ἔστω ταῦτʼ εἰρημένα· τὸ δʼ αὐτὸ κἂν εἴ τις ταῦτα πλείω τίθησιν, οἷον Ἐμπεδοκλῆς τέτταρά φησιν εἶναι σώματα τὴν ὕλην. καὶ γὰρ τούτῳ τὰ μὲν ταὐτὰ τὰ δʼ ἴδια συμβαίνειν ἀνάγκη. γιγνόμενά τε γὰρ ἐξ ἀλλήλων ὁρῶμεν ὡς οὐκ ἀεὶ διαμένοντος πυρὸς καὶ γῆς τοῦ αὐτοῦ σώματος (εἴρηται δὲ ἐν τοῖς περὶ φύσεως περὶ αὐτῶν), καὶ περὶ τῆς τῶν κινουμένων αἰτίας, πότερον ἓν ἢ δύο θετέον, οὔτʼ ὀρθῶς οὔτε εὐλόγως οἰητέον εἰρῆσθαι παντελῶς. ὅλως τε ἀλλοίωσιν ἀναιρεῖσθαι ἀνάγκη τοῖς οὕτω λέγουσιν· οὐ γὰρ ἐκ θερμοῦ ψυχρὸν οὐδὲ ἐκ ψυχροῦ θερμὸν ἔσται. τὶ γὰρ αὐτὰ ἂν πάσχοι τἀναντία, καὶ τὶς εἴη ἂν μία φύσις ἡ γιγνομένη πῦρ καὶ ὕδωρ, ὃ ἐκεῖνος οὔ φησιν. Ἀναξαγόραν δʼ εἴ τις ὑπολάβοι δύο λέγειν στοιχεῖα, μάλιστʼ ἂν ὑπολάβοι κατὰ λόγον, ὃν ἐκεῖνος αὐτὸς μὲν οὐ διήρθρωσεν, ἠκολούθησε μέντʼ ἂν ἐξ ἀνάγκης τοῖς ἐπάγουσιν αὐτόν. ἀτόπου γὰρ ὄντος καὶ ἄλλως τοῦ φάσκειν μεμῖχθαι τὴν ἀρχὴν πάντα,  καὶ διὰ τὸ συμβαίνειν ἄμικτα δεῖν προϋπάρχειν καὶ διὰ τὸ μὴ πεφυκέναι τῷ τυχόντι μίγνυσθαι τὸ τυχόν, πρὸς δὲ τούτοις ὅτι τὰ πάθη καὶ τὰ συμβεβηκότα χωρίζοιτʼ ἂν τῶν οὐσιῶν (τῶν γὰρ αὐτῶν μῖξίς ἐστι καὶ χωρισμός), ὅμως εἴ τις ἀκολουθήσειε συνδιαρθρῶν ἃ βούλεται λέγειν, ἴσως ἂν φανείη καινοπρεπεστέρως λέγων. ὅτε γὰρ οὐθὲν ἦν ἀποκεκριμένον, δῆλον ὡς οὐθὲν ἦν ἀληθὲς εἰπεῖν κατὰ τῆς οὐσίας ἐκείνης, λέγω δʼ οἷον ὅτι οὔτε λευκὸν οὔτε μέλαν ἢ φαιὸν ἢ ἄλλο χρῶμα, ἀλλʼ ἄχρων ἦν ἐξ ἀνάγκης· εἶχε γὰρ ἄν τι τούτων τῶν χρωμάτων· ὁμοίως δὲ καὶ ἄχυμον τῷ αὐτῷ λόγῳ τούτῳ, οὐδὲ ἄλλο τῶν ὁμοίων οὐθέν· οὔτε γὰρ ποιόν τι οἷόν τε αὐτὸ εἶναι οὔτε ποσὸν οὔτε τί. τῶν γὰρ ἐν μέρει τι λεγομένων εἰδῶν ὑπῆρχεν ἂν αὐτῷ, τοῦτο δὲ ἀδύνατον μεμιγμένων γε πάντων· ἤδη γὰρ ἂν ἀπεκέκριτο, φησὶ δʼ εἶναι μεμιγμένα πάντα πλὴν τοῦ νοῦ, τοῦτον δὲ ἀμιγῆ μόνον καὶ καθαρόν. ἐκ δὴ τούτων συμβαίνει λέγειν αὐτῷ τὰς ἀρχὰς τό τε ἕν (τοῦτο γὰρ ἁπλοῦν καὶ ἀμιγές) καὶ θάτερον, οἷον τίθεμεν τὸ ἀόριστον πρὶν ὁρισθῆναι καὶ μετασχεῖν εἴδους τινός, ὥστε λέγει μὲν οὔτʼ ὀρθῶς οὔτε σαφῶς, βούλεται μέντοι τι παραπλήσιον τοῖς τε ὕστερον λέγουσι καὶ τοῖς νῦν φαινομένοις μᾶλλον.—ἀλλὰ γὰρ οὗτοι μὲν τοῖς περὶ γένεσιν λόγοις καὶ φθορὰν καὶ κίνησιν οἰκεῖοι τυγχάνουσι μόνον (σχεδὸν γὰρ περὶ τῆς τοιαύτης οὐσίας καὶ τὰς ἀρχὰς καὶ τὰς αἰτίας ζητοῦσι μόνης)· ὅσοι δὲ περὶ μὲν ἁπάντων τῶν ὄντων ποιοῦνται τὴν θεωρίαν, τῶν δʼ ὄντων τὰ μὲν αἰσθητὰ τὰ δʼ οὐκ αἰσθητὰ τιθέασι, δῆλον ὡς περὶ ἀμφοτέρων τῶν γενῶν ποιοῦνται τὴν ἐπίσκεψιν· διὸ μᾶλλον ἄν τις ἐνδιατρίψειε περὶ αὐτῶν, τί καλῶς ἢ μὴ καλῶς λέγουσιν εἰς τὴν τῶν νῦν ἡμῖν προκειμένων σκέψιν. οἱ μὲν οὖν καλούμενοι Πυθαγόρειοι ταῖς μὲν ἀρχαῖς καὶ τοῖς στοιχείοις ἐκτοπωτέροις χρῶνται τῶν φυσιολόγων (τὸ δʼ αἴτιον ὅτι παρέλαβον αὐτὰς οὐκ ἐξ αἰσθητῶν· τὰ γὰρ μαθηματικὰ τῶν ὄντων ἄνευ κινήσεώς ἐστιν ἔξω τῶν περὶ τὴν ἀστρολογίαν), διαλέγονται μέντοι καὶ πραγματεύονται περὶ φύσεως πάντα· γεννῶσί τε γὰρ τὸν οὐρανόν,  καὶ περὶ τὰ τούτου μέρη καὶ τὰ πάθη καὶ τὰ ἔργα διατηροῦσι τὸ συμβαῖνον, καὶ τὰς ἀρχὰς καὶ τὰ αἴτια εἰς ταῦτα καταναλίσκουσιν, ὡς ὁμολογοῦντες τοῖς ἄλλοις φυσιολόγοις ὅτι τό γε ὂν τοῦτʼ ἐστὶν ὅσον αἰσθητόν ἐστι καὶ περιείληφεν ὁ καλούμενος οὐρανός. τὰς δʼ αἰτίας καὶ τὰς ἀρχάς, ὥσπερ εἴπομεν, ἱκανὰς λέγουσιν ἐπαναβῆναι καὶ ἐπὶ τὰ ἀνωτέρω τῶν ὄντων, καὶ μᾶλλον ἢ τοῖς περὶ φύσεως λόγοις ἁρμοττούσας. ἐκ τίνος μέντοι τρόπου κίνησις ἔσται πέρατος καὶ ἀπείρου μόνων ὑποκειμένων καὶ περιττοῦ καὶ ἀρτίου, οὐθὲν λέγουσιν, ἢ πῶς δυνατὸν ἄνευ κινήσεως καὶ μεταβολῆς γένεσιν εἶναι καὶ φθορὰν ἢ τὰ τῶν φερομένων ἔργα κατὰ τὸν οὐρανόν. ἔτι δὲ εἴτε δοίη τις αὐτοῖς ἐκ τούτων εἶναι μέγεθος εἴτε δειχθείη τοῦτο, ὅμως τίνα τρόπον ἔσται τὰ μὲν κοῦφα τὰ δὲ βάρος ἔχοντα τῶν σωμάτων; ἐξ ὧν γὰρ ὑποτίθενται καὶ λέγουσιν, οὐθὲν μᾶλλον περὶ τῶν μαθηματικῶν λέγουσι σωμάτων ἢ τῶν αἰσθητῶν· διὸ περὶ πυρὸς ἢ γῆς ἢ τῶν ἄλλων τῶν τοιούτων σωμάτων οὐδʼ ὁτιοῦν εἰρήκασιν, ἅτε οὐθὲν περὶ τῶν αἰσθητῶν οἶμαι λέγοντες ἴδιον. ἔτι δὲ πῶς δεῖ λαβεῖν αἴτια μὲν εἶναι τὰ τοῦ ἀριθμοῦ πάθη καὶ τὸν ἀριθμὸν τῶν κατὰ τὸν οὐρανὸν ὄντων καὶ γιγνομένων καὶ ἐξ ἀρχῆς καὶ νῦν, ἀριθμὸν δʼ ἄλλον μηθένα εἶναι παρὰ τὸν ἀριθμὸν τοῦτον ἐξ οὗ συνέστηκεν ὁ κόσμος; ὅταν γὰρ ἐν τῳδὶ μὲν τῷ μέρει δόξα καὶ καιρὸς αὐτοῖς ᾖ, μικρὸν δὲ ἄνωθεν ἢ κάτωθεν ἀδικία καὶ κρίσις ἢ μῖξις, ἀπόδειξιν δὲ λέγωσιν ὅτι τούτων μὲν ἕκαστον ἀριθμός ἐστι, συμβαίνει δὲ κατὰ τὸν τόπον τοῦτον ἤδη πλῆθος εἶναι τῶν συνισταμένων μεγεθῶν διὰ τὸ τὰ πάθη ταῦτα ἀκολουθεῖν τοῖς τόποις ἑκάστοις, πότερον οὗτος ὁ αὐτός ἐστιν ἀριθμός, ὁ ἐν τῷ οὐρανῷ, ὃν δεῖ λαβεῖν ὅτι τούτων ἕκαστόν ἐστιν, ἢ παρὰ τοῦτον ἄλλος; ὁ μὲν γὰρ Πλάτων ἕτερον εἶναί φησιν· καίτοι κἀκεῖνος ἀριθμοὺς οἴεται καὶ ταῦτα εἶναι καὶ τὰς τούτων αἰτίας, ἀλλὰ τοὺς μὲν νοητοὺς αἰτίους τούτους δὲ αἰσθητούς.\n\n\n\n \n\n \n\nπερὶ μὲν οὖν τῶν Πυθαγορείων ἀφείσθω τὰ νῦν (ἱκανὸν γὰρ αὐτῶν ἅψασθαι τοσοῦτον)·  οἱ δὲ τὰς ἰδέας αἰτίας τιθέμενοι πρῶτον μὲν ζητοῦντες τωνδὶ τῶν ὄντων λαβεῖν τὰς αἰτίας ἕτερα τούτοις ἴσα τὸν ἀριθμὸν ἐκόμισαν, ὥσπερ εἴ τις ἀριθμῆσαι βουλόμενος ἐλαττόνων μὲν ὄντων οἴοιτο μὴ δυνήσεσθαι, πλείω δὲ ποιήσας ἀριθμοίη (σχεδὸν γὰρ ἴσα—ἢ οὐκ ἐλάττω—ἐστὶ τὰ εἴδη τούτοις περὶ ὧν ζητοῦντες τὰς αἰτίας ἐκ τούτων ἐπʼ ἐκεῖνα προῆλθον· καθʼ ἕκαστον γὰρ ὁμώνυμόν τι ἔστι καὶ παρὰ τὰς οὐσίας, τῶν τε ἄλλων ἔστιν ἓν ἐπὶ πολλῶν, καὶ ἐπὶ τοῖσδε καὶ ἐπὶ τοῖς ἀϊδίοις)· ἔτι δὲ καθʼ οὓς τρόπους δείκνυμεν ὅτι ἔστι τὰ εἴδη, κατʼ οὐθένα φαίνεται τούτων· ἐξ ἐνίων μὲν γὰρ οὐκ ἀνάγκη γίγνεσθαι συλλογισμόν, ἐξ ἐνίων δὲ καὶ οὐχ ὧν οἰόμεθα τούτων εἴδη γίγνεται. κατά τε γὰρ τοὺς λόγους τοὺς ἐκ τῶν ἐπιστημῶν εἴδη ἔσται πάντων ὅσων ἐπιστῆμαι εἰσί, καὶ κατὰ τὸ ἓν ἐπὶ πολλῶν καὶ τῶν ἀποφάσεων, κατὰ δὲ τὸ νοεῖν τι φθαρέντος τῶν φθαρτῶν· φάντασμα γάρ τι τούτων ἔστιν. ἔτι δὲ οἱ ἀκριβέστεροι τῶν λόγων οἱ μὲν τῶν πρός τι ποιοῦσιν ἰδέας, ὧν οὔ φαμεν εἶναι καθʼ αὑτὸ γένος, οἱ δὲ τὸν τρίτον ἄνθρωπον λέγουσιν. ὅλως τε ἀναιροῦσιν οἱ περὶ τῶν εἰδῶν λόγοι ἃ μᾶλλον εἶναι βουλόμεθα  τοῦ τὰς ἰδέας εἶναι· συμβαίνει γὰρ μὴ εἶναι τὴν δυάδα πρώτην ἀλλὰ τὸν ἀριθμόν, καὶ τὸ πρός τι τοῦ καθʼ αὑτό, καὶ πάνθʼ ὅσα τινὲς ἀκολουθήσαντες ταῖς περὶ τῶν ἰδεῶν δόξαις ἠναντιώθησαν ταῖς ἀρχαῖς.—ἔτι κατὰ μὲν τὴν ὑπόληψιν καθʼ ἣν εἶναί φαμεν τὰς ἰδέας οὐ μόνον τῶν οὐσιῶν ἔσται εἴδη ἀλλὰ πολλῶν καὶ ἑτέρων (καὶ γὰρ τὸ νόημα ἓν οὐ μόνον περὶ τὰς οὐσίας ἀλλὰ καὶ κατὰ τῶν ἄλλων ἐστί, καὶ ἐπιστῆμαι οὐ μόνον τῆς οὐσίας εἰσὶν ἀλλὰ καὶ ἑτέρων, καὶ ἄλλα δὲ μυρία συμβαίνει τοιαῦτα)· κατὰ δὲ τὸ ἀναγκαῖον καὶ τὰς δόξας τὰς περὶ αὐτῶν, εἰ ἔστι μεθεκτὰ τὰ εἴδη, τῶν οὐσιῶν ἀναγκαῖον ἰδέας εἶναι μόνον. οὐ γὰρ κατὰ συμβεβηκὸς μετέχονται ἀλλὰ δεῖ ταύτῃ ἑκάστου μετέχειν ᾗ μὴ καθʼ ὑποκειμένου λέγεται (λέγω δʼ οἷον, εἴ τι αὐτοδιπλασίου μετέχει, τοῦτο καὶ ἀϊδίου μετέχει, ἀλλὰ κατὰ συμβεβηκός· συμβέβηκε γὰρ τῷ διπλασίῳ ἀϊδίῳ εἶναι), ὥστʼ ἔσται οὐσία τὰ εἴδη· ταὐτὰ δὲ ἐνταῦθα οὐσίαν σημαίνει κἀκεῖ·  ἢ τί ἔσται τὸ εἶναι τι παρὰ ταῦτα, τὸ ἓν ἐπὶ πολλῶν; καὶ εἰ μὲν ταὐτὸ εἶδος τῶν ἰδεῶν καὶ τῶν μετεχόντων, ἔσται τι κοινόν (τί γὰρ μᾶλλον ἐπὶ τῶν φθαρτῶν δυάδων, καὶ τῶν πολλῶν μὲν ἀϊδίων δέ, τὸ δυὰς ἓν καὶ ταὐτόν, ἢ ἐπί τʼ αὐτῆς καὶ τῆς τινός;)· εἰ δὲ μὴ τὸ αὐτὸ εἶδος, ὁμώνυμα ἂν εἴη, καὶ ὅμοιον ὥσπερ ἂν εἴ τις καλοῖ ἄνθρωπον τόν τε Καλλίαν καὶ τὸ ξύλον, μηδεμίαν κοινωνίαν ἐπιβλέψας αὐτῶν.—πάντων δὲ μάλιστα διαπορήσειεν ἄν τις τί ποτε συμβάλλεται τὰ εἴδη τοῖς ἀϊδίοις τῶν αἰσθητῶν ἢ τοῖς γιγνομένοις καὶ φθειρομένοις· οὔτε γὰρ κινήσεως οὔτε μεταβολῆς οὐδεμιᾶς ἐστὶν αἴτια αὐτοῖς. ἀλλὰ μὴν οὔτε πρὸς τὴν ἐπιστήμην οὐθὲν βοηθεῖ τὴν τῶν ἄλλων (οὐδὲ γὰρ οὐσία ἐκεῖνα τούτων· ἐν τούτοις γὰρ ἂν ἦν), οὔτε εἰς τὸ εἶναι, μὴ ἐνυπάρχοντά γε τοῖς μετέχουσιν· οὕτω μὲν γὰρ ἂν ἴσως αἴτια δόξειεν εἶναι ὡς τὸ λευκὸν μεμιγμένον τῷ λευκῷ, ἀλλʼ οὗτος μὲν ὁ λόγος λίαν εὐκίνητος, ὃν Ἀναξαγόρας μὲν πρῶτος Εὔδοξος δʼ ὕστερον καὶ ἄλλοι τινὲς ἔλεγον (ῥᾴδιον γὰρ συναγαγεῖν πολλὰ καὶ ἀδύνατα πρὸς τὴν τοιαύτην δόξαν)· ἀλλὰ μὴν οὐδʼ ἐκ τῶν εἰδῶν ἐστὶ τἆλλα κατʼ οὐθένα τρόπον τῶν εἰωθότων λέγεσθαι. τὸ δὲ λέγειν παραδείγματα αὐτὰ εἶναι καὶ μετέχειν αὐτῶν τἆλλα κενολογεῖν ἐστὶ καὶ μεταφορὰς λέγειν ποιητικάς. τί γάρ ἐστι τὸ ἐργαζόμενον πρὸς τὰς ἰδέας ἀποβλέπον; ἐνδέχεταί τε καὶ εἶναι καὶ γίγνεσθαι ὅμοιον ὁτιοῦν καὶ μὴ εἰκαζόμενον πρὸς ἐκεῖνο, ὥστε καὶ ὄντος Σωκράτους καὶ μὴ ὄντος γένοιτʼ ἂν οἷος Σωκράτης· ὁμοίως δὲ δῆλον ὅτι κἂν εἰ ἦν ὁ Σωκράτης ἀΐδιος. ἔσται τε πλείω παραδείγματα τοῦ αὐτοῦ, ὥστε καὶ εἴδη, οἷον τοῦ ἀνθρώπου τὸ ζῷον καὶ τὸ δίπουν, ἅμα δὲ καὶ τὸ αὐτοάνθρωπος. ἔτι οὐ μόνον τῶν αἰσθητῶν παραδείγματα τὰ εἴδη ἀλλὰ καὶ αὐτῶν, οἷον τὸ γένος, ὡς γένος εἰδῶν· ὥστε τὸ αὐτὸ ἔσται παράδειγμα καὶ εἰκών.  ἔτι δόξειεν ἂν ἀδύνατον εἶναι χωρὶς τὴν οὐσίαν καὶ οὗ ἡ οὐσία· ὥστε πῶς ἂν αἱ ἰδέαι οὐσίαι τῶν πραγμάτων οὖσαι χωρὶς εἶεν; ἐν δὲ τῷ Φαίδωνι οὕτω λέγεται, ὡς καὶ τοῦ εἶναι καὶ τοῦ γίγνεσθαι αἴτια τὰ εἴδη ἐστίν· καίτοι τῶν εἰδῶν ὄντων ὅμως οὐ γίγνεται τὰ μετέχοντα ἂν μὴ ᾖ τὸ κινῆσον, καὶ πολλὰ γίγνεται ἕτερα, οἷον οἰκία καὶ δακτύλιος, ὧν οὔ φαμεν εἴδη εἶναι· ὥστε δῆλον ὅτι ἐνδέχεται καὶ τἆλλα καὶ εἶναι καὶ γίγνεσθαι διὰ τοιαύτας αἰτίας οἵας καὶ τὰ ῥηθέντα νῦν.—ἔτι εἴπερ εἰσὶν ἀριθμοὶ τὰ εἴδη, πῶς αἴτιοι ἔσονται; πότερον ὅτι ἕτεροι ἀριθμοί εἰσι τὰ ὄντα, οἷον ὁδὶ μὲν ὁ ἀριθμὸς ἄνθρωπος ὁδὶ δὲ Σωκράτης ὁδὶ δὲ Καλλίας; τί οὖν ἐκεῖνοι τούτοις αἴτιοί εἰσιν; οὐδὲ γὰρ εἰ οἱ μὲν ἀΐδιοι οἱ δὲ μή, οὐδὲν διοίσει. εἰ δʼ ὅτι λόγοι ἀριθμῶν τἀνταῦθα, οἷον ἡ συμφωνία, δῆλον ὅτι ἐστὶν ἕν γέ τι ὧν εἰσὶ λόγοι. εἰ δή τι τοῦτο, ἡ ὕλη, φανερὸν ὅτι καὶ αὐτοὶ οἱ ἀριθμοὶ λόγοι τινὲς ἔσονται ἑτέρου πρὸς ἕτερον. λέγω δʼ οἷον, εἰ ἔστιν ὁ Καλλίας λόγος ἐν ἀριθμοῖς πυρὸς καὶ γῆς καὶ ὕδατος καὶ ἀέρος, καὶ ἄλλων τινῶν ὑποκειμένων ἔσται καὶ ἡ ἰδέα ἀριθμός· καὶ αὐτοάνθρωπος, εἴτʼ ἀριθμός τις ὢν εἴτε μή, ὅμως ἔσται λόγος ἐν ἀριθμοῖς τινῶν καὶ οὐκ ἀριθμός, οὐδʼ ἔσται τις διὰ ταῦτα ἀριθμός. ἔτι ἐκ πολλῶν ἀριθμῶν εἷς ἀριθμὸς γίγνεται, ἐξ εἰδῶν δὲ ἓν εἶδος πῶς; εἰ δὲ μὴ ἐξ αὐτῶν ἀλλʼ ἐκ τῶν ἐν τῷ ἀριθμῷ, οἷον ἐν τῇ μυριάδι, πῶς ἔχουσιν αἱ μονάδες; εἴτε γὰρ ὁμοειδεῖς, πολλὰ συμβήσεται ἄτοπα, εἴτε μὴ ὁμοειδεῖς, μήτε αὐταὶ ἀλλήλαις μήτε αἱ ἄλλαι πᾶσαι πάσαις· τίνι γὰρ διοίσουσιν ἀπαθεῖς οὖσαι; οὔτε γὰρ εὔλογα ταῦτα οὔτε ὁμολογούμενα τῇ νοήσει. ἔτι δʼ ἀναγκαῖον ἕτερον γένος ἀριθμοῦ κατασκευάζειν περὶ ὃ ἡ ἀριθμητική, καὶ πάντα τὰ μεταξὺ λεγόμενα ὑπό τινων, ἃ πῶς ἢ ἐκ τίνων ἐστὶν ἀρχῶν; ἢ διὰ τί μεταξὺ τῶν δεῦρό τʼ ἔσται καὶ αὐτῶν; ἔτι αἱ μονάδες αἱ ἐν τῇ δυάδι ἑκατέρα ἔκ τινος προτέρας δυάδος· καίτοι ἀδύνατον.  ἔτι διὰ τί ἓν ὁ ἀριθμὸς συλλαμβανόμενος; ἔτι δὲ πρὸς τοῖς εἰρημένοις, εἴπερ εἰσὶν αἱ μονάδες διάφοροι, ἐχρῆν οὕτω λέγειν ὥσπερ καὶ ὅσοι τὰ στοιχεῖα τέτταρα ἢ δύο λέγουσιν· καὶ γὰρ τούτων ἕκαστος οὐ τὸ κοινὸν λέγει στοιχεῖον, οἷον τὸ σῶμα, ἀλλὰ πῦρ καὶ γῆν, εἴτʼ ἔστι τι κοινόν, τὸ σῶμα, εἴτε μή. νῦν δὲ λέγεται ὡς ὄντος τοῦ ἑνὸς ὥσπερ πυρὸς ἢ ὕδατος ὁμοιομεροῦς· εἰ δʼ οὕτως, οὐκ ἔσονται οὐσίαι οἱ ἀριθμοί, ἀλλὰ δῆλον ὅτι, εἴπερ ἐστί τι ἓν αὐτὸ καὶ τοῦτό ἐστιν ἀρχή, πλεοναχῶς λέγεται τὸ ἕν· ἄλλως γὰρ ἀδύνατον.—βουλόμενοι δὲ τὰς οὐσίας ἀνάγειν εἰς τὰς ἀρχὰς μήκη μὲν τίθεμεν ἐκ βραχέος καὶ μακροῦ, ἔκ τινος μικροῦ καὶ μεγάλου, καὶ ἐπίπεδον ἐκ πλατέος καὶ στενοῦ, σῶμα δʼ ἐκ βαθέος καὶ ταπεινοῦ. καίτοι πῶς ἕξει ἢ τὸ ἐπίπεδον γραμμὴν ἢ τὸ στερεὸν γραμμὴν καὶ ἐπίπεδον; ἄλλο γὰρ γένος τὸ πλατὺ καὶ στενὸν καὶ βαθὺ καὶ ταπεινόν· ὥσπερ οὖν οὐδʼ ἀριθμὸς ὑπάρχει ἐν αὐτοῖς, ὅτι τὸ πολὺ καὶ ὀλίγον ἕτερον τούτων, δῆλον ὅτι οὐδʼ ἄλλο οὐθὲν τῶν ἄνω ὑπάρξει τοῖς κάτω. ἀλλὰ μὴν οὐδὲ γένος τὸ πλατὺ τοῦ βαθέος· ἦν γὰρ ἂν ἐπίπεδόν τι τὸ σῶμα. ἔτι αἱ στιγμαὶ ἐκ τίνος ἐνυπάρξουσιν; τούτῳ μὲν οὖν τῷ γένει καὶ διεμάχετο Πλάτων ὡς ὄντι γεωμετρικῷ δόγματι, ἀλλʼ ἐκάλει ἀρχὴν γραμμῆς—τοῦτο δὲ πολλάκις ἐτίθει—τὰς ἀτόμους γραμμάς. καίτοι ἀνάγκη τούτων εἶναί τι πέρας· ὥστʼ ἐξ οὗ λόγου γραμμὴ ἔστι, καὶ στιγμὴ ἔστιν.—ὅλως δὲ ζητούσης τῆς σοφίας περὶ τῶν φανερῶν τὸ αἴτιον, τοῦτο μὲν εἰάκαμεν (οὐθὲν γὰρ λέγομεν περὶ τῆς αἰτίας ὅθεν ἡ ἀρχὴ τῆς μεταβολῆς) , τὴν δʼ οὐσίαν οἰόμενοι λέγειν αὐτῶν ἑτέρας μὲν οὐσίας εἶναί φαμεν, ὅπως δʼ ἐκεῖναι τούτων οὐσίαι, διὰ κενῆς λέγομεν· τὸ γὰρ μετέχειν, ὥσπερ καὶ πρότερον εἴπομεν, οὐθέν ἐστιν. οὐδὲ δὴ ὅπερ ταῖς ἐπιστήμαις ὁρῶμεν ὂν αἴτιον, διʼ ὃ καὶ πᾶς νοῦς καὶ πᾶσα φύσις ποιεῖ, οὐδὲ ταύτης τῆς αἰτίας, ἥν φαμεν εἶναι μίαν τῶν ἀρχῶν, οὐθὲν ἅπτεται τὰ εἴδη, ἀλλὰ γέγονε τὰ μαθήματα τοῖς νῦν ἡ φιλοσοφία, φασκόντων ἄλλων χάριν αὐτὰ δεῖν πραγματεύεσθαι.  ἔτι δὲ τὴν ὑποκειμένην οὐσίαν ὡς ὕλην μαθηματικωτέραν ἄν τις ὑπολάβοι, καὶ μᾶλλον κατηγορεῖσθαι καὶ διαφορὰν εἶναι τῆς οὐσίας καὶ τῆς ὕλης ἢ ὕλην, οἷον τὸ μέγα καὶ τὸ μικρόν, ὥσπερ καὶ οἱ φυσιολόγοι φασὶ τὸ μανὸν καὶ τὸ πυκνόν, πρώτας τοῦ ὑποκειμένου φάσκοντες εἶναι διαφορὰς ταύτας· ταῦτα γάρ ἐστιν ὑπεροχή τις καὶ ἔλλειψις. περί τε κινήσεως, εἰ μὲν ἔσται ταῦτα κίνησις, δῆλον ὅτι κινήσεται τὰ εἴδη· εἰ δὲ μή, πόθεν ἦλθεν; ὅλη γὰρ ἡ περὶ φύσεως ἀνῄρηται σκέψις. ὅ τε δοκεῖ ῥᾴδιον εἶναι, τὸ δεῖξαι ὅτι ἓν ἅπαντα, οὐ γίγνεται· τῇ γὰρ ἐκθέσει οὐ γίγνεται πάντα ἓν ἀλλʼ αὐτό τι ἕν, ἂν διδῷ τις πάντα· καὶ οὐδὲ τοῦτο, εἰ μὴ γένος δώσει τὸ καθόλου εἶναι· τοῦτο δʼ ἐν ἐνίοις ἀδύνατον. οὐθένα δʼ ἔχει λόγον οὐδὲ τὰ μετὰ τοὺς ἀριθμοὺς μήκη τε καὶ ἐπίπεδα καὶ στερεά, οὔτε ὅπως ἔστιν ἢ ἔσται οὔτε τίνα ἔχει δύναμιν· ταῦτα γὰρ οὔτε εἴδη οἷόν τε εἶναι (οὐ γάρ εἰσιν ἀριθμοί) οὔτε τὰ μεταξύ (μαθηματικὰ γὰρ ἐκεῖνα) οὔτε τὰ φθαρτά, ἀλλὰ πάλιν τέταρτον ἄλλο φαίνεται τοῦτό τι γένος. ὅλως τε τὸ τῶν ὄντων ζητεῖν στοιχεῖα μὴ διελόντας, πολλαχῶς λεγομένων, ἀδύνατον εὑρεῖν, ἄλλως τε καὶ τοῦτον τὸν τρόπον ζητοῦντας ἐξ οἵων ἐστὶ στοιχείων. ἐκ τίνων γὰρ τὸ ποιεῖν ἢ πάσχειν ἢ τὸ εὐθύ, οὐκ ἔστι δήπου λαβεῖν, ἀλλʼ εἴπερ, τῶν οὐσιῶν μόνον ἐνδέχεται· ὥστε τὸ τῶν ὄντων ἁπάντων τὰ στοιχεῖα ἢ ζητεῖν ἢ οἴεσθαι ἔχειν οὐκ ἀληθές. πῶς δʼ ἄν τις καὶ μάθοι τὰ τῶν πάντων στοιχεῖα; δῆλον γὰρ ὡς οὐθὲν οἷόν τε προϋπάρχειν γνωρίζοντα πρότερον. ὥσπερ γὰρ τῷ γεωμετρεῖν μανθάνοντι ἄλλα μὲν ἐνδέχεται προειδέναι, ὧν δὲ ἡ ἐπιστήμη καὶ περὶ ὧν μέλλει μανθάνειν οὐθὲν προγιγνώσκει, οὕτω δὴ καὶ ἐπὶ τῶν ἄλλων, ὥστʼ εἴ τις τῶν πάντων ἔστιν ἐπιστήμη, οἵαν δή τινές φασιν, οὐθὲν ἂν προϋπάρχοι γνωρίζων οὗτος. καίτοι πᾶσα μάθησις διὰ προγιγνωσκομένων ἢ πάντων ἢ τινῶν ἐστί, καὶ ἡ διʼ ἀποδείξεως καὶ ἡ διʼ ὁρισμῶν (δεῖ γὰρ ἐξ ὧν ὁ ὁρισμὸς προειδέναι καὶ εἶναι γνώριμα)· ὁμοίως δὲ καὶ ἡ διʼ ἐπαγωγῆς. ἀλλὰ μὴν εἰ καὶ τυγχάνοι σύμφυτος οὖσα,  θαυμαστὸν πῶς λανθάνομεν ἔχοντες τὴν κρατίστην τῶν ἐπιστημῶν. ἔτι πῶς τις γνωριεῖ ἐκ τίνων ἐστί, καὶ πῶς ἔσται δῆλον; καὶ γὰρ τοῦτʼ ἔχει ἀπορίαν· ἀμφισβητήσειε γὰρ ἄν τις ὥσπερ καὶ περὶ ἐνίας συλλαβάς· οἱ μὲν γὰρ τὸ ζα ἐκ τοῦ ς καὶ δ καὶ α φασὶν εἶναι, οἱ δέ τινες ἕτερον φθόγγον φασὶν εἶναι καὶ οὐθένα τῶν γνωρίμων. ἔτι δὲ ὧν ἐστὶν αἴσθησις, ταῦτα πῶς ἄν τις μὴ ἔχων τὴν αἴσθησιν γνοίη; καίτοι ἔδει, εἴγε πάντων ταὐτὰ στοιχεῖά ἐστιν ἐξ ὧν, ὥσπερ αἱ σύνθετοι φωναί εἰσιν ἐκ τῶν οἰκείων στοιχείων.\n\n\n\n \n\n \n\nὅτι μὲν οὖν τὰς εἰρημένας ἐν τοῖς φυσικοῖς αἰτίας ζητεῖν ἐοίκασι πάντες, καὶ τούτων ἐκτὸς οὐδεμίαν ἔχοιμεν ἂν εἰπεῖν, δῆλον καὶ ἐκ τῶν πρότερον εἰρημένων· ἀλλʼ ἀμυδρῶς ταύτας, καὶ τρόπον μέν τινα πᾶσαι πρότερον εἴρηνται τρόπον δέ τινα οὐδαμῶς. ψελλιζομένῃ γὰρ ἔοικεν ἡ πρώτη φιλοσοφία περὶ πάντων, ἅτε νέα τε καὶ κατʼ ἀρχὰς οὖσα , ἐπεὶ καὶ Ἐμπεδοκλῆς ὀστοῦν τῷ λόγῳ φησὶν εἶναι, τοῦτο δʼ ἐστὶ τὸ τί ἦν εἶναι καὶ ἡ οὐσία τοῦ πράγματες. ἀλλὰ μὴν ὁμοίως ἀναγκαῖον καὶ σάρκας καὶ τῶν ἄλλων ἕκαστον εἶναι τὸν λόγον, ἢ μηδὲ ἕν· διὰ τοῦτο γὰρ καὶ σὰρξ καὶ ὀστοῦν ἔσται καὶ τῶν ἄλλων ἕκαστον καὶ οὐ διὰ τὴν ὕλην, ἣν ἐκεῖνος λέγει, πῦρ καὶ γῆν καὶ ὕδωρ καὶ ἀέρα. ἀλλὰ ταῦτα ἄλλου μὲν λέγοντος συνέφησεν ἂν ἐξ ἀνάγκης, σαφῶς δὲ οὐκ εἴρηκεν. περὶ μὲν οὖν τούτων δεδήλωται καὶ πρότερον· ὅσα δὲ περὶ τῶν αὐτῶν τούτων ἀπορήσειεν ἄν τις, ἐπανέλθωμεν πάλιν· τάχα γὰρ ἂν ἐξ αὐτῶν εὐπορήσαιμέν τι πρὸς τὰς ὕστερον ἀπορίας.","paragraphs":[{"id":"RAW_DAZY_BOO1:P0001","text":"πάντες ἄνθρωποι τοῦ εἰδέναι ὀρέγονται φύσει. σημεῖον δʼ ἡ τῶν αἰσθήσεων ἀγάπησις· καὶ γὰρ χωρὶς τῆς χρείας ἀγαπῶνται διʼ αὑτάς, καὶ μάλιστα τῶν ἄλλων ἡ διὰ τῶν ὀμμάτων. οὐ γὰρ μόνον ἵνα πράττωμεν ἀλλὰ καὶ μηθὲν μέλλοντες πράττειν τὸ ὁρᾶν αἱρούμεθα ἀντὶ πάντων ὡς εἰπεῖν τῶν ἄλλων. αἴτιον δʼ ὅτι μάλιστα ποιεῖ γνωρίζειν ἡμᾶς αὕτη τῶν αἰσθήσεων καὶ πολλὰς δηλοῖ διαφοράς. φύσει μὲν οὖν αἴσθησιν ἔχοντα γίγνεται τὰ ζῷα, ἐκ δὲ ταύτης τοῖς μὲν αὐτῶν οὐκ ἐγγίγνεται μνήμη, τοῖς δʼ ἐγγίγνεται.  καὶ διὰ τοῦτο ταῦτα φρονιμώτερα καὶ μαθητικώτερα τῶν μὴ δυναμένων μνημονεύειν ἐστί, φρόνιμα μὲν ἄνευ τοῦ μανθάνειν ὅσα μὴ δύναται τῶν ψόφων ἀκούειν (οἷον μέλιττα κἂν εἴ τι τοιοῦτον ἄλλο γένος ζῴων ἔστι), μανθάνει δʼ ὅσα πρὸς τῇ μνήμῃ καὶ ταύτην ἔχει τὴν αἴσθησιν. τὰ μὲν οὖν ἄλλα ταῖς φαντασίαις ζῇ καὶ ταῖς μνήμαις, ἐμπειρίας δὲ μετέχει μικρόν· τὸ δὲ τῶν ἀνθρώπων γένος καὶ τέχνῃ καὶ λογισμοῖς.","kind":"passage"},{"id":"RAW_DAZY_BOO1:P0002","text":"γίγνεται δʼ ἐκ τῆς μνήμης ἐμπειρία τοῖς ἀνθρώποις· αἱ γὰρ πολλαὶ μνῆμαι τοῦ αὐτοῦ πράγματος μιᾶς ἐμπειρίας δύναμιν ἀποτελοῦσιν.  καὶ δοκεῖ σχεδὸν ἐπιστήμῃ καὶ τέχνῃ ὅμοιον εἶναι καὶ ἐμπειρία, ἀποβαίνει δʼ ἐπιστήμη καὶ τέχνη διὰ τῆς ἐμπειρίας τοῖς ἀνθρώποις· ἡ μὲν γὰρ ἐμπειρία τέχνην ἐποίησεν, ὡς φησὶ Πῶλος, ἡ δʼ ἀπειρία τύχην. γίγνεται δὲ τέχνη ὅταν ἐκ πολλῶν τῆς ἐμπειρίας ἐννοημάτων μία καθόλου γένηται περὶ τῶν ὁμοίων ὑπόληψις.","kind":"passage"},{"id":"RAW_DAZY_BOO1:P0003","text":"τὸ μὲν γὰρ ἔχειν ὑπόληψιν ὅτι Καλλίᾳ κάμνοντι τηνδὶ τὴν νόσον τοδὶ συνήνεγκε καὶ Σωκράτει καὶ καθʼ ἕκαστον οὕτω πολλοῖς, ἐμπειρίας ἐστίν· τὸ δʼ ὅτι πᾶσι τοῖς τοιοῖσδε κατʼ εἶδος ἓν ἀφορισθεῖσι, κάμνουσι τηνδὶ τὴν νόσον, συνήνεγκεν, οἷον τοῖς φλεγματώδεσιν ἢ χολώδεσι  πυρέττουσι καύσῳ, τέχνης.—πρὸς μὲν οὖν τὸ πράττειν ἐμπειρία τέχνης οὐδὲν δοκεῖ διαφέρειν, ἀλλὰ καὶ μᾶλλον ἐπιτυγχάνουσιν οἱ ἔμπειροι τῶν ἄνευ τῆς ἐμπειρίας λόγον ἐχόντων (αἴτιον δʼ ὅτι ἡ μὲν ἐμπειρία τῶν καθʼ ἕκαστόν ἐστι γνῶσις ἡ δὲ τέχνη τῶν καθόλου, αἱ δὲ πράξεις καὶ αἱ γενέσεις πᾶσαι περὶ τὸ καθʼ ἕκαστόν εἰσιν· οὐ γὰρ ἄνθρωπον ὑγιάζει ὁ ἰατρεύων ἀλλʼ ἢ κατὰ συμβεβηκός, ἀλλὰ Καλλίαν ἢ Σωκράτην ἢ τῶν ἄλλων τινὰ τῶν οὕτω λεγομένων ᾧ συμβέβηκεν ἀνθρώπῳ εἶναι· ἐὰν οὖν ἄνευ τῆς ἐμπειρίας ἔχῃ τις τὸν λόγον, καὶ τὸ καθόλου μὲν γνωρίζῃ τὸ δʼ ἐν τούτῳ καθʼ ἕκαστον ἀγνοῇ, πολλάκις διαμαρτήσεται τῆς θεραπείας· θεραπευτὸν γὰρ τὸ","kind":"passage"},{"id":"RAW_DAZY_BOO1:P0004","text":"καθʼ ἕκαστον)· ἀλλʼ ὅμως τό γε εἰδέναι καὶ τὸ ἐπαΐειν τῇ τέχνῃ τῆς ἐμπειρίας ὑπάρχειν οἰόμεθα μᾶλλον, καὶ σοφωτέρους τοὺς τεχνίτας τῶν ἐμπείρων ὑπολαμβάνομεν, ὡς κατὰ τὸ εἰδέναι μᾶλλον ἀκολουθοῦσαν τὴν σοφίαν πᾶσι· τοῦτο δʼ ὅτι οἱ μὲν τὴν αἰτίαν ἴσασιν οἱ δʼ οὔ. οἱ μὲν γὰρ ἔμπειροι τὸ ὅτι μὲν ἴσασι, διότι δʼ οὐκ ἴσασιν· οἱ δὲ τὸ διότι καὶ τὴν αἰτίαν γνωρίζουσιν. διὸ καὶ τοὺς ἀρχιτέκτονας περὶ ἕκαστον τιμιωτέρους καὶ μᾶλλον εἰδέναι νομίζομεν τῶν χειροτεχνῶν καὶ σοφωτέρους,  ὅτι τὰς αἰτίας τῶν ποιουμένων ἴσασιν (τοὺς δʼ, ὥσπερ καὶ τῶν ἀψύχων ἔνια ποιεῖ μέν, οὐκ εἰδότα δὲ ποιεῖ ἃ ποιεῖ, οἷον καίει τὸ πῦρ—τὰ μὲν οὖν ἄψυχα φύσει τινὶ ποιεῖν τούτων ἕκαστον τοὺς δὲ χειροτέχνας διʼ ἔθος), ὡς οὐ κατὰ τὸ πρακτικοὺς εἶναι σοφωτέρους ὄντας ἀλλὰ κατὰ τὸ λόγον ἔχειν αὐτοὺς καὶ τὰς αἰτίας γνωρίζειν.","kind":"passage"},{"id":"RAW_DAZY_BOO1:P0005","text":"ὅλως τε σημεῖον τοῦ εἰδότος καὶ μὴ εἰδότος τὸ δύνασθαι διδάσκειν ἐστίν, καὶ διὰ τοῦτο τὴν τέχνην τῆς ἐμπειρίας ἡγούμεθα μᾶλλον ἐπιστήμην εἶναι· δύνανται γάρ, οἱ δὲ οὐ δύνανται διδάσκειν. ἔτι δὲ τῶν αἰσθήσεων οὐδεμίαν ἡγούμεθα εἶναι σοφίαν· καίτοι κυριώταταί γʼ εἰσὶν αὗται τῶν καθʼ ἕκαστα γνώσεις· ἀλλʼ οὐ λέγουσι τὸ διὰ τί περὶ οὐδενός, οἷον διὰ τί θερμὸν τὸ πῦρ, ἀλλὰ μόνον ὅτι θερμόν. τὸ μὲν οὖν πρῶτον εἰκὸς τὸν ὁποιανοῦν εὑρόντα τέχνην παρὰ τὰς κοινὰς αἰσθήσεις θαυμάζεσθαι ὑπὸ τῶν ἀνθρώπων μὴ μόνον διὰ τὸ χρήσιμον εἶναί τι τῶν εὑρεθέντων ἀλλʼ ὡς σοφὸν καὶ διαφέροντα τῶν ἄλλων· πλειόνων δʼ εὑρισκομένων τεχνῶν καὶ τῶν μὲν πρὸς τἀναγκαῖα τῶν δὲ πρὸς διαγωγὴν οὐσῶν, ἀεὶ σοφωτέρους τοὺς τοιούτους ἐκείνων ὑπολαμβάνεσθαι διὰ τὸ μὴ πρὸς χρῆσιν εἶναι τὰς ἐπιστήμας αὐτῶν.","kind":"passage"},{"id":"RAW_DAZY_BOO1:P0006","text":"ὅθεν ἤδη πάντων τῶν τοιούτων κατεσκευασμένων αἱ μὴ πρὸς ἡδονὴν μηδὲ πρὸς τἀναγκαῖα τῶν ἐπιστημῶν εὑρέθησαν, καὶ πρῶτον ἐν τούτοις τοῖς τόποις οὗ πρῶτον ἐσχόλασαν· διὸ περὶ Αἴγυπτον αἱ μαθηματικαὶ πρῶτον τέχναι συνέστησαν, ἐκεῖ γὰρ ἀφείθη σχολάζειν τὸ τῶν ἱερέων ἔθνος. εἴρηται μὲν οὖν ἐν τοῖς ἠθικοῖς τίς διαφορὰ τέχνης καὶ ἐπιστήμης καὶ τῶν ἄλλων τῶν ὁμογενῶν· οὗ δʼ ἕνεκα νῦν ποιούμεθα τὸν λόγον τοῦτʼ ἐστίν, ὅτι τὴν ὀνομαζομένην σοφίαν περὶ τὰ πρῶτα αἴτια καὶ τὰς ἀρχὰς ὑπολαμβάνουσι πάντες· ὥστε, καθάπερ εἴρηται πρότερον, ὁ μὲν ἔμπειρος τῶν ὁποιανοῦν ἐχόντων αἴσθησιν εἶναι δοκεῖ σοφώτερος, ὁ δὲ τεχνίτης τῶν ἐμπείρων, χειροτέχνου δὲ ἀρχιτέκτων, αἱ δὲ θεωρητικαὶ τῶν ποιητικῶν μᾶλλον.  ὅτι μὲν οὖν ἡ σοφία περί τινας ἀρχὰς καὶ αἰτίας ἐστὶν ἐπιστήμη, δῆλον.","kind":"passage"},{"id":"RAW_DAZY_BOO1:P0007","text":"ἐπεὶ δὲ ταύτην τὴν ἐπιστήμην ζητοῦμεν, τοῦτʼ ἂν εἴη σκεπτέον, ἡ περὶ ποίας αἰτίας καὶ περὶ ποίας ἀρχὰς ἐπιστήμη σοφία ἐστίν. εἰ δὴ λάβοι τις τὰς ὑπολήψεις ἃς ἔχομεν περὶ τοῦ σοφοῦ, τάχʼ ἂν ἐκ τούτου φανερὸν γένοιτο μᾶλλον. ὑπολαμβάνομεν δὴ πρῶτον μὲν ἐπίστασθαι πάντα τὸν σοφὸν ὡς ἐνδέχεται, μὴ καθʼ ἕκαστον ἔχοντα ἐπιστήμην αὐτῶν· εἶτα τὸν τὰ χαλεπὰ γνῶναι δυνάμενον καὶ μὴ ῥᾴδια ἀνθρώπῳ γιγνώσκειν, τοῦτον σοφόν (τὸ γὰρ αἰσθάνεσθαι πάντων κοινόν, διὸ ῥᾴδιον καὶ οὐδὲν σοφόν)· ἔτι τὸν ἀκριβέστερον καὶ τὸν διδασκαλικώτερον τῶν αἰτιῶν σοφώτερον εἶναι περὶ πᾶσαν ἐπιστήμην· καὶ τῶν ἐπιστημῶν δὲ τὴν αὑτῆς ἕνεκεν καὶ τοῦ εἰδέναι χάριν αἱρετὴν οὖσαν μᾶλλον εἶναι σοφίαν ἢ τὴν τῶν ἀποβαινόντων ἕνεκεν, καὶ τὴν ἀρχικωτέραν τῆς ὑπηρετούσης μᾶλλον σοφίαν· οὐ γὰρ δεῖν ἐπιτάττεσθαι τὸν σοφὸν ἀλλʼ ἐπιτάττειν, καὶ οὐ τοῦτον ἑτέρῳ πείθεσθαι, ἀλλὰ τούτῳ τὸν ἧττον σοφόν.","kind":"passage"},{"id":"RAW_DAZY_BOO1:P0008","text":"—τὰς μὲν οὖν ὑπολήψεις τοιαύτας καὶ τοσαύτας ἔχομεν περὶ τῆς σοφίας καὶ τῶν σοφῶν· τούτων δὲ τὸ μὲν πάντα ἐπίστασθαι τῷ μάλιστα ἔχοντι τὴν καθόλου ἐπιστήμην ἀναγκαῖον ὑπάρχειν (οὗτος γὰρ οἶδέ πως πάντα τὰ ὑποκείμενα), σχεδὸν δὲ καὶ χαλεπώτατα ταῦτα γνωρίζειν τοῖς ἀνθρώποις, τὰ μάλιστα καθόλου (πορρωτάτω γὰρ τῶν αἰσθήσεών ἐστιν), ἀκριβέσταται δὲ τῶν ἐπιστημῶν αἳ μάλιστα τῶν πρώτων εἰσίν (αἱ γὰρ ἐξ ἐλαττόνων ἀκριβέστεραι τῶν ἐκ προσθέσεως λεγομένων, οἷον ἀριθμητικὴ γεωμετρίας)· ἀλλὰ μὴν καὶ διδασκαλική γε ἡ τῶν αἰτιῶν θεωρητικὴ μᾶλλον (οὗτοι γὰρ διδάσκουσιν, οἱ τὰς αἰτίας λέγοντες περὶ ἑκάστου), τὸ δʼ εἰδέναι καὶ τὸ ἐπίστασθαι αὐτῶν ἕνεκα μάλισθʼ ὑπάρχει τῇ τοῦ μάλιστα ἐπιστητοῦ ἐπιστήμῃ (ὁ γὰρ τὸ ἐπίστασθαι διʼ αὑτὸ αἱρούμενος τὴν μάλιστα ἐπιστήμην μάλιστα αἱρήσεται,  τοιαύτη δʼ ἐστὶν ἡ τοῦ μάλιστα ἐπιστητου), μάλιστα δʼ ἐπιστητὰ τὰ πρῶτα καὶ τὰ αἴτια (διὰ γὰρ ταῦτα καὶ ἐκ τούτων τἆλλα","kind":"passage"},{"id":"RAW_DAZY_BOO1:P0009","text":"γνωρίζεται ἀλλʼ οὐ ταῦτα διὰ τῶν ὑποκειμένων), ἀρχικωτάτη δὲ τῶν ἐπιστημῶν, καὶ μᾶλλον ἀρχικὴ τῆς ὑπηρετούσης, ἡ γνωρίζουσα τίνος ἕνεκέν ἐστι πρακτέον ἕκαστον· τοῦτο δʼ ἐστὶ τἀγαθὸν ἑκάστου, ὅλως δὲ τὸ ἄριστον ἐν τῇ φύσει πάσῃ. ἐξ ἁπάντων οὖν τῶν εἰρημένων ἐπὶ τὴν αὐτὴν ἐπιστήμην πίπτει τὸ ζητούμενον ὄνομα· δεῖ γὰρ ταύτην τῶν πρώτων ἀρχῶν καὶ αἰτιῶν εἶναι θεωρητικήν· καὶ γὰρ τἀγαθὸν καὶ τὸ οὗ ἕνεκα ἓν τῶν αἰτίων ἐστίν.","kind":"passage"},{"id":"RAW_DAZY_BOO1:P0010","text":"ὅτι δʼ οὐ ποιητική, δῆλον καὶ ἐκ τῶν πρώτων φιλοσοφησάντων· διὰ γὰρ τὸ θαυμάζειν οἱ ἄνθρωποι καὶ νῦν καὶ τὸ πρῶτον ἤρξαντο φιλοσοφεῖν, ἐξ ἀρχῆς μὲν τὰ πρόχειρα τῶν ἀτόπων θαυμάσαντες, εἶτα κατὰ μικρὸν οὕτω προϊόντες καὶ περὶ τῶν μειζόνων διαπορήσαντες, οἷον περί τε τῶν τῆς σελήνης παθημάτων καὶ τῶν περὶ τὸν ἥλιον καὶ ἄστρα καὶ περὶ τῆς τοῦ παντὸς γενέσεως. ὁ δʼ ἀπορῶν καὶ θαυμάζων οἴεται ἀγνοεῖν (διὸ καὶ ὁ φιλόμυθος φιλόσοφός πώς ἐστιν· ὁ γὰρ μῦθος σύγκειται ἐκ θαυμασίων)· ὥστʼ εἴπερ διὰ τὸ φεύγειν τὴν ἄγνοιαν ἐφιλοσόφησαν, φανερὸν ὅτι διὰ τὸ εἰδέναι τὸ ἐπίστασθαι ἐδίωκον καὶ οὐ χρήσεώς τινος ἕνεκεν. μαρτυρεῖ δὲ αὐτὸ τὸ συμβεβηκός· σχεδὸν γὰρ πάντων ὑπαρχόντων τῶν ἀναγκαίων καὶ πρὸς ῥᾳστώνην καὶ διαγωγὴν ἡ τοιαύτη φρόνησις ἤρξατο ζητεῖσθαι.","kind":"passage"},{"id":"RAW_DAZY_BOO1:P0011","text":"δῆλον οὖν ὡς διʼ οὐδεμίαν αὐτὴν ζητοῦμεν χρείαν ἑτέραν, ἀλλʼ ὥσπερ ἄνθρωπος, φαμέν, ἐλεύθερος ὁ αὑτοῦ ἕνεκα καὶ μὴ ἄλλου ὤν, οὕτω καὶ αὐτὴν ὡς μόνην οὖσαν ἐλευθέραν τῶν ἐπιστημῶν· μόνη γὰρ αὕτη αὑτῆς ἕνεκέν ἐστιν. διὸ καὶ δικαίως ἂν οὐκ ἀνθρωπίνη νομίζοιτο αὐτῆς ἡ κτῆσις· πολλαχῇ γὰρ ἡ φύσις δούλη τῶν ἀνθρώπων ἐστίν, ὥστε κατὰ Σιμωνίδην  \nθεὸς ἂν μόνος τοῦτʼ ἔχοι γέρας, ἄνδρα δʼ οὐκ ἄξιον μὴ οὐ ζητεῖν τὴν καθʼ αὑτὸν ἐπιστήμην. εἰ δὴ λέγουσί τι οἱ ποιηταὶ καὶ πέφυκε φθονεῖν τὸ θεῖον,  ἐπὶ τούτου συμβῆναι μάλιστα εἰκὸς καὶ δυστυχεῖς εἶναι πάντας τοὺς περιττούς. ἀλλʼ οὔτε τὸ θεῖον φθονερὸν ἐνδέχεται εἶναι, ἀλλὰ κατὰ τὴν παροιμίαν πολλὰ ψεύδονται ἀοιδοί, οὔτε τῆς τοιαύτης ἄλλην χρὴ νομίζειν τιμιωτέραν.  ἡ γὰρ θειοτάτη καὶ τιμιωτάτη· τοιαύτη δὲ διχῶς ἂν εἴη μόνη· ἥν τε γὰρ μάλιστʼ ἂν ὁ θεὸς ἔχοι, θεία τῶν ἐπιστημῶν ἐστί, κἂν εἴ τις τῶν θείων εἴη.","kind":"passage"},{"id":"RAW_DAZY_BOO1:P0012","text":"μόνη δʼ αὕτη τούτων ἀμφοτέρων τετύχηκεν· ὅ τε γὰρ θεὸς δοκεῖ τῶν αἰτίων πᾶσιν εἶναι καὶ ἀρχή τις, καὶ τὴν τοιαύτην ἢ μόνος ἢ μάλιστʼ ἂν ἔχοι ὁ θεός. ἀναγκαιότεραι μὲν οὖν πᾶσαι ταύτης, ἀμείνων δʼ οὐδεμία.—δεῖ μέντοι πως καταστῆναι τὴν κτῆσιν αὐτῆς εἰς τοὐναντίον ἡμῖν τῶν ἐξ ἀρχῆς ζητήσεων. ἄρχονται μὲν γάρ, ὥσπερ εἴπομεν, ἀπὸ τοῦ θαυμάζειν πάντες εἰ οὕτως ἔχει, καθάπερ περὶ τῶν θαυμάτων ταὐτόματα  ἢ περὶ τὰς τοῦ ἡλίου τροπὰς ἢ τὴν τῆς διαμέτρου ἀσυμμετρίαν (θαυμαστὸν γὰρ εἶναι δοκεῖ πᾶσι τοῖς μήπω τεθεωρηκόσι τὴν αἰτίαν εἴ τι τῷ ἐλαχίστῳ μὴ μετρεῖται)· δεῖ δὲ εἰς τοὐναντίον καὶ τὸ ἄμεινον κατὰ τὴν παροιμίαν ἀποτελευτῆσαι, καθάπερ καὶ ἐν τούτοις ὅταν μάθωσιν· οὐθὲν γὰρ ἂν οὕτως θαυμάσειεν ἀνὴρ γεωμετρικὸς ὡς εἰ γένοιτο ἡ διάμετρος μετρητή. τίς μὲν οὖν ἡ φύσις τῆς ἐπιστήμης τῆς ζητουμένης, εἴρηται, καὶ τίς ὁ σκοπὸς οὗ δεῖ τυγχάνειν τὴν ζήτησιν καὶ τὴν ὅλην μέθοδον.","kind":"passage"},{"id":"RAW_DAZY_BOO1:P0013","text":"ἐπεὶ δὲ φανερὸν ὅτι τῶν ἐξ ἀρχῆς αἰτίων δεῖ λαβεῖν ἐπιστήμην (τότε γὰρ εἰδέναι φαμὲν ἕκαστον, ὅταν τὴν πρώτην αἰτίαν οἰώμεθα γνωρίζειν), τὰ δʼ αἴτια λέγεται τετραχῶς, ὧν μίαν μὲν αἰτίαν φαμὲν εἶναι τὴν οὐσίαν καὶ τὸ τί ἦν εἶναι (ἀνάγεται γὰρ τὸ διὰ τί εἰς τὸν λόγον ἔσχατον, αἴτιον δὲ καὶ ἀρχὴ τὸ διὰ τί πρῶτον), ἑτέραν δὲ τὴν ὕλην καὶ τὸ ὑποκείμενον, τρίτην δὲ ὅθεν ἡ ἀρχὴ τῆς κινήσεως, τετάρτην δὲ τὴν ἀντικειμένην αἰτίαν ταύτῃ, τὸ οὗ ἕνεκα καὶ τἀγαθόν (τέλος γὰρ γενέσεως καὶ κινήσεως πάσης τοῦτʼ ἐστίν), τεθεώρηται μὲν οὖν ἱκανῶς περὶ αὐτῶν ἡμῖν ἐν τοῖς περὶ φύσεως,  ὅμως δὲ παραλάβωμεν καὶ τοὺς πρότερον ἡμῶν εἰς ἐπίσκεψιν τῶν ὄντων ἐλθόντας καὶ φιλοσοφήσαντας περὶ τῆς ἀληθείας.","kind":"passage"},{"id":"RAW_DAZY_BOO1:P0014","text":"δῆλον γὰρ ὅτι κἀκεῖνοι λέγουσιν ἀρχάς τινας καὶ αἰτίας· ἐπελθοῦσιν οὖν ἔσται τι προὔργου τῇ μεθόδῳ τῇ νῦν· ἢ γὰρ ἕτερόν τι γένος εὑρήσομεν αἰτίας ἢ ταῖς νῦν λεγομέναις μᾶλλον πιστεύσομεν.—τῶν δὴ πρώτων φιλοσοφησάντων οἱ πλεῖστοι τὰς ἐν ὕλης εἴδει μόνας ᾠήθησαν ἀρχὰς εἶναι πάντων· ἐξ οὗ γὰρ ἔστιν ἅπαντα τὰ ὄντα καὶ ἐξ οὗ γίγνεται πρώτου καὶ εἰς ὃ φθείρεται τελευταῖον, τῆς μὲν οὐσίας ὑπομενούσης τοῖς δὲ πάθεσι μεταβαλλούσης, τοῦτο στοιχεῖον καὶ ταύτην ἀρχήν φασιν εἶναι τῶν ὄντων, καὶ διὰ τοῦτο οὔτε γίγνεσθαι οὐθὲν οἴονται οὔτε ἀπόλλυσθαι, ὡς τῆς τοιαύτης φύσεως ἀεὶ σωζομένης, ὥσπερ οὐδὲ τὸν Σωκράτην φαμὲν οὔτε γίγνεσθαι ἁπλῶς ὅταν γίγνηται καλὸς ἢ μουσικὸς οὔτε ἀπόλλυσθαι ὅταν ἀποβάλλῃ ταύτας τὰς ἕξεις, διὰ τὸ ὑπομένειν τὸ ὑποκείμενον τὸν Σωκράτην αὐτόν, οὕτως οὐδὲ τῶν ἄλλων οὐδέν· ἀεὶ γὰρ εἶναί τινα φύσιν ἢ μίαν ἢ πλείους μιᾶς ἐξ ὧν γίγνεται τἆλλα σωζομένης ἐκείνης.","kind":"passage"},{"id":"RAW_DAZY_BOO1:P0015","text":"τὸ μέντοι πλῆθος καὶ τὸ εἶδος τῆς τοιαύτης ἀρχῆς οὐ τὸ αὐτὸ πάντες λέγουσιν, ἀλλὰ Θαλῆς μὲν ὁ τῆς τοιαύτης ἀρχηγὸς φιλοσοφίας ὕδωρ φησὶν εἶναι (διὸ καὶ τὴν γῆν ἐφʼ ὕδατος ἀπεφήνατο εἶναι), λαβὼν ἴσως τὴν ὑπόληψιν ταύτην ἐκ τοῦ πάντων ὁρᾶν τὴν τροφὴν ὑγρὰν οὖσαν καὶ αὐτὸ τὸ θερμὸν ἐκ τούτου γιγνόμενον καὶ τούτῳ ζῶν (τὸ δʼ ἐξ οὗ γίγνεται, τοῦτʼ ἐστὶν ἀρχὴ πάντων)—διά τε δὴ τοῦτο τὴν ὑπόληψιν λαβὼν ταύτην καὶ διὰ τὸ πάντων τὰ σπέρματα τὴν φύσιν ὑγρὰν ἔχειν, τὸ δʼ ὕδωρ ἀρχὴν τῆς φύσεως εἶναι τοῖς ὑγροῖς. εἰσὶ δέ τινες οἳ καὶ τοὺς παμπαλαίους καὶ πολὺ πρὸ τῆς νῦν γενέσεως καὶ πρώτους θεολογήσαντας οὕτως οἴονται περὶ τῆς φύσεως ὑπολαβεῖν· Ὠκεανόν τε γὰρ καὶ Τηθὺν ἐποίησαν τῆς γενέσεως πατέρας, καὶ τὸν ὅρκον τῶν θεῶν ὕδωρ, τὴν καλουμένην ὑπʼ αὐτῶν Στύγα · τιμιώτατον μὲν γὰρ τὸ πρεσβύτατον, ὅρκος δὲ τὸ τιμιώτατόν ἐστιν.","kind":"passage"},{"id":"RAW_DAZY_BOO1:P0016","text":"εἰ μὲν οὖν ἀρχαία τις αὕτη καὶ παλαιὰ τετύχηκεν οὖσα περὶ τῆς φύσεως ἡ δόξα, τάχʼ ἂν ἄδηλον εἴη, Θαλῆς μέντοι λέγεται οὕτως ἀποφήνασθαι περὶ τῆς πρώτης αἰτίας (Ἵππωνα γὰρ οὐκ ἄν τις ἀξιώσειε θεῖναι μετὰ τούτων διὰ τὴν εὐτέλειαν αὐτοῦ τῆς διανοίας)· Ἀναξιμένης δὲ ἀέρα καὶ Διογένης πρότερον ὕδατος καὶ μάλιστʼ ἀρχὴν τιθέασι τῶν ἁπλῶν σωμάτων, Ἵππασος δὲ πῦρ ὁ Μεταποντῖνος καὶ Ἡράκλειτος ὁ Ἐφέσιος, Ἐμπεδοκλῆς δὲ τὰ τέτταρα, πρὸς τοῖς εἰρημένοις γῆν προστιθεὶς τέταρτον (ταῦτα γὰρ ἀεὶ διαμένειν καὶ οὐ γίγνεσθαι ἀλλʼ ἢ πλήθει καὶ ὀλιγότητι, συγκρινόμενα καὶ διακρινόμενα εἰς ἕν τε καὶ ἐξ ἑνός)· Ἀναξαγόρας δὲ ὁ Κλαζομένιος τῇ μὲν ἡλικίᾳ πρότερος ὢν τούτου τοῖς δʼ ἔργοις ὕστερος ἀπείρους εἶναί φησι τὰς ἀρχάς· σχεδὸν γὰρ ἅπαντα τὰ ὁμοιομερῆ καθάπερ ὕδωρ ἢ πῦρ οὕτω γίγνεσθαι καὶ ἀπόλλυσθαί φησι, συγκρίσει καὶ διακρίσει μόνον, ἄλλως δʼ οὔτε γίγνεσθαι οὔτʼ ἀπόλλυσθαι ἀλλὰ διαμένειν ἀΐδια.—ἐκ μὲν οὖν","kind":"passage"},{"id":"RAW_DAZY_BOO1:P0017","text":"τούτων μόνην τις αἰτίαν νομίσειεν ἂν τὴν ἐν ὕλης εἴδει λεγομένην· προϊόντων δʼ οὕτως, αὐτὸ τὸ πρᾶγμα ὡδοποίησεν αὐτοῖς καὶ συνηνάγκασε ζητεῖν· εἰ γὰρ ὅτι μάλιστα πᾶσα γένεσις καὶ φθορὰ ἔκ τινος ἑνὸς ἢ καὶ πλειόνων ἐστίν, διὰ τί τοῦτο συμβαίνει καὶ τί τὸ αἴτιον; οὐ γὰρ δὴ τό γε ὑποκείμενον αὐτὸ ποιεῖ μεταβάλλειν ἑαυτό· λέγω δʼ οἷον οὔτε τὸ ξύλον οὔτε ὁ χαλκὸς αἴτιος τοῦ μεταβάλλειν ἑκάτερον αὐτῶν, οὐδὲ ποιεῖ τὸ μὲν ξύλον κλίνην ὁ δὲ χαλκὸς ἀνδριάντα, ἀλλʼ ἕτερόν τι τῆς μεταβολῆς αἴτιον. τὸ δὲ τοῦτο ζητεῖν ἐστὶ τὸ τὴν ἑτέραν ἀρχὴν ζητεῖν, ὡς ἂν ἡμεῖς φαίημεν, ὅθεν ἡ ἀρχὴ τῆς κινήσεως.","kind":"passage"},{"id":"RAW_DAZY_BOO1:P0018","text":"οἱ μὲν οὖν πάμπαν ἐξ ἀρχῆς ἁψάμενοι τῆς μεθόδου τῆς τοιαύτης καὶ ἓν φάσκοντες εἶναι τὸ ὑποκείμενον οὐθὲν ἐδυσχέραναν ἑαυτοῖς, ἀλλʼ ἔνιοί γε τῶν ἓν λεγόντων, ὥσπερ ἡττηθέντες ὑπὸ ταύτης τῆς ζητήσεως, τὸ ἓν ἀκίνητόν φασιν εἶναι καὶ τὴν φύσιν ὅλην οὐ μόνον κατὰ γένεσιν καὶ φθοράν (τοῦτο μὲν γὰρ ἀρχαῖόν τε καὶ πάντες ὡμολόγησαν) ἀλλὰ καὶ κατὰ τὴν ἄλλην μεταβολὴν πᾶσαν· καὶ τοῦτο αὐτῶν ἴδιόν ἐστιν.","kind":"passage"},{"id":"RAW_DAZY_BOO1:P0019","text":"τῶν μὲν οὖν ἓν φασκόντων εἶναι τὸ πᾶν οὐθενὶ συνέβη τὴν τοιαύτην συνιδεῖν αἰτίαν πλὴν εἰ ἄρα Παρμενίδῃ, καὶ τούτῳ κατὰ τοσοῦτον ὅσον οὐ μόνον ἓν ἀλλὰ καὶ δύο πως τίθησιν αἰτίας εἶναι· τοῖς δὲ δὴ πλείω ποιοῦσι μᾶλλον ἐνδέχεται λέγειν, οἷον τοῖς θερμὸν καὶ ψυχρὸν ἢ πῦρ καὶ γῆν· χρῶνται γὰρ ὡς κινητικὴν ἔχοντι τῷ πυρὶ τὴν φύσιν, ὕδατι δὲ καὶ γῇ καὶ τοῖς τοιούτοις τοὐναντίον.—μετὰ δὲ τούτους καὶ τὰς τοιαύτας ἀρχάς, ὡς οὐχ ἱκανῶν οὐσῶν γεννῆσαι τὴν τῶν ὄντων φύσιν, πάλιν ὑπʼ αὐτῆς τῆς ἀληθείας, ὥσπερ εἴπομεν, ἀναγκαζόμενοι τὴν ἐχομένην ἐζήτησαν ἀρχήν. τοῦ γὰρ εὖ καὶ καλῶς τὰ μὲν ἔχειν τὰ δὲ γίγνεσθαι τῶν ὄντων ἴσως οὔτε πῦρ οὔτε γῆν οὔτʼ ἄλλο τῶν τοιούτων οὐθὲν οὔτʼ εἰκὸς αἴτιον εἶναι οὔτʼ ἐκείνους οἰηθῆναι· οὐδʼ αὖ τῷ αὐτομάτῳ καὶ τύχῃ τοσοῦτον ἐπιτρέψαι πρᾶγμα καλῶς εἶχεν.","kind":"passage"},{"id":"RAW_DAZY_BOO1:P0020","text":"νοῦν δή τις εἰπὼν ἐνεῖναι, καθάπερ ἐν τοῖς ζῴοις, καὶ ἐν τῇ φύσει τὸν αἴτιον τοῦ κόσμου καὶ τῆς τάξεως πάσης οἷον νήφων ἐφάνη παρʼ εἰκῇ λέγοντας τοὺς πρότερον. φανερῶς μὲν οὖν Ἀναξαγόραν ἴσμεν ἁψάμενον τούτων τῶν λόγων, αἰτίαν δʼ ἔχει πρότερον Ἑρμότιμος ὁ Κλαζομένιος εἰπεῖν. οἱ μὲν οὖν οὕτως ὑπολαμβάνοντες ἅμα τοῦ καλῶς τὴν αἰτίαν ἀρχὴν εἶναι τῶν ὄντων ἔθεσαν, καὶ τὴν τοιαύτην ὅθεν ἡ κίνησις ὑπάρχει τοῖς οὖσιν·","kind":"passage"},{"id":"RAW_DAZY_BOO1:P0021","text":"ὑποπτεύσειε δʼ ἄν τις Ἡσίοδον πρῶτον ζητῆσαι τὸ τοιοῦτον, κἂν εἴ τις ἄλλος ἔρωτα ἢ ἐπιθυμίαν ἐν τοῖς οὖσιν ἔθηκεν ὡς ἀρχήν, οἷον καὶ Παρμενίδης· καὶ γὰρ οὗτος κατασκευάζων τὴν τοῦ παντὸς γένεσιν","kind":"passage"},{"id":"RAW_DAZY_BOO1:P0022","text":"πρώτιστον μέν (φησιν) ἔρωτα θεῶν μητίσατο πάντων","kind":"passage"},{"id":"RAW_DAZY_BOO1:P0023","text":"Parmenides Fr. 13 (Diels), Ἡσίοδος δὲ","kind":"passage"},{"id":"RAW_DAZY_BOO1:P0024","text":"πάντων μὲν πρώτιστα χάος γένετʼ, αὐτὰρ ἔπειτα","kind":"passage"},{"id":"RAW_DAZY_BOO1:P0025","text":"γαῖʼ εὐρύστερνος [Gap in source]","kind":"passage"},{"id":"RAW_DAZY_BOO1:P0026","text":"ἠδʼ ἔρος, ὃς πάντεσσι μεταπρέπει ἀθανάτοισιν,","kind":"passage"},{"id":"RAW_DAZY_BOO1:P0027","text":"Hes. Th. 116-20 ὡς δέον ἐν τοῖς οὖσιν ὑπάρχειν τινʼ αἰτίαν ἥτις κινήσει καὶ συνάξει τὰ πράγματα. τούτους μὲν οὖν πῶς χρὴ διανεῖμαι περὶ τοῦ τίς πρῶτος, ἐξέστω κρίνειν ὕστερον· ἐπεὶ δὲ καὶ τἀναντία τοῖς ἀγαθοῖς ἐνόντα ἐφαίνετο ἐν τῇ φύσει, καὶ οὐ μόνον τάξις καὶ τὸ καλὸν ἀλλὰ καὶ ἀταξία καὶ τὸ αἰσχρόν,  καὶ πλείω τὰ κακὰ τῶν ἀγαθῶν καὶ τὰ φαῦλα τῶν καλῶν, οὕτως ἄλλος τις φιλίαν εἰσήνεγκε καὶ νεῖκος, ἑκάτερον ἑκατέρων αἴτιον τούτων.","kind":"passage"},{"id":"RAW_DAZY_BOO1:P0028","text":"εἰ γάρ τις ἀκολουθοίη καὶ λαμβάνοι πρὸς τὴν διάνοιαν καὶ μὴ πρὸς ἃ ψελλίζεται λέγων Ἐμπεδοκλῆς, εὑρήσει τὴν μὲν φιλίαν αἰτίαν οὖσαν τῶν ἀγαθῶν τὸ δὲ νεῖκος τῶν κακῶν· ὥστʼ εἴ τις φαίη τρόπον τινὰ καὶ λέγειν καὶ πρῶτον λέγειν τὸ κακὸν καὶ τὸ ἀγαθὸν ἀρχὰς Ἐμπεδοκλέα, τάχʼ ἂν λέγοι καλῶς, εἴπερ τὸ τῶν ἀγαθῶν ἁπάντων αἴτιον αὐτὸ τἀγαθόν ἐστι .—οὗτοι μὲν οὖν, ὥσπερ λέγομεν, καὶ μέχρι τούτου δυοῖν αἰτίαιν ὧν ἡμεῖς διωρίσαμεν ἐν τοῖς περὶ φύσεως ἡμμένοι φαίνονται, τῆς τε ὕλης καὶ τοῦ ὅθεν ἡ κίνησις, ἀμυδρῶς μέντοι καὶ οὐθὲν σαφῶς ἀλλʼ οἷον ἐν ταῖς μάχαις οἱ ἀγύμναστοι ποιοῦσιν· καὶ γὰρ ἐκεῖνοι περιφερόμενοι τύπτουσι πολλάκις καλὰς πληγάς, ἀλλʼ οὔτε ἐκεῖνοι ἀπὸ ἐπιστήμης οὔτε οὗτοι ἐοίκασιν εἰδέναι ὅ τι λέγουσιν· σχεδὸν γὰρ οὐθὲν χρώμενοι φαίνονται τούτοις ἀλλʼ ἢ κατὰ μικρόν.","kind":"passage"},{"id":"RAW_DAZY_BOO1:P0029","text":"Ἀναξαγόρας τε γὰρ μηχανῇ χρῆται τῷ νῷ πρὸς τὴν κοσμοποιίαν, καὶ ὅταν ἀπορήσῃ διὰ τίνʼ αἰτίαν ἐξ ἀνάγκης ἐστί, τότε παρέλκει αὐτόν, ἐν δὲ τοῖς ἄλλοις πάντα μᾶλλον αἰτιᾶται τῶν γιγνομένων ἢ νοῦν, καὶ Ἐμπεδοκλῆς ἐπὶ πλέον μὲν τούτου χρῆται τοῖς αἰτίοις, οὐ μὴν οὔθʼ ἱκανῶς, οὔτʼ ἐν τούτοις εὑρίσκει τὸ ὁμολογούμενον. πολλαχοῦ γοῦν αὐτῷ ἡ μὲν φιλία διακρίνει τὸ δὲ νεῖκος συγκρίνει.","kind":"passage"},{"id":"RAW_DAZY_BOO1:P0030","text":"ὅταν μὲν γὰρ εἰς τὰ στοιχεῖα διίστηται τὸ πᾶν ὑπὸ τοῦ νείκους, τότε τὸ πῦρ εἰς ἓν συγκρίνεται καὶ τῶν ἄλλων στοιχείων ἕκαστον· ὅταν δὲ πάλιν ὑπὸ τῆς φιλίας συνίωσιν εἰς τὸ ἕν, ἀναγκαῖον ἐξ ἑκάστου τὰ μόρια διακρίνεσθαι πάλιν.—Ἐμπεδοκλῆς μὲν οὖν παρὰ τοὺς πρότερον πρῶτος τὸ τὴν αἰτίαν διελεῖν εἰσήνεγκεν, οὐ μίαν ποιήσας τὴν τῆς κινήσεως ἀρχὴν ἀλλʼ ἑτέρας τε καὶ ἐναντίας, ἔτι δὲ τὰ ὡς ἐν ὕλης εἴδει λεγόμενα στοιχεῖα τέτταρα πρῶτος εἶπεν (οὐ μὴν χρῆταί γε τέτταρσιν ἀλλʼ ὡς δυσὶν οὖσι μόνοις,  πυρὶ μὲν καθʼ αὑτὸ τοῖς δʼ ἀντικειμένοις ὡς μιᾷ φύσει, γῇ τε καὶ ἀέρι καὶ ὕδατι· λάβοι δʼ ἄν τις αὐτὸ θεωρῶν ἐκ τῶν ἐπῶν)·—οὗτος μὲν οὖν, ὥσπερ λέγομεν, οὕτω τε καὶ τοσαύτας εἴρηκε τὰς ἀρχάς· Λεύκιππος δὲ καὶ ὁ ἑταῖρος αὐτοῦ Δημόκριτος στοιχεῖα μὲν τὸ πλῆρες καὶ τὸ κενὸν εἶναί φασι, λέγοντες τὸ μὲν ὂν τὸ δὲ μὴ ὄν, τούτων δὲ τὸ μὲν πλῆρες καὶ στερεὸν τὸ ὄν, τὸ δὲ κενὸν τὸ μὴ ὄν (διὸ καὶ οὐθὲν μᾶλλον τὸ","kind":"passage"},{"id":"RAW_DAZY_BOO1:P0031","text":"ὂν τοῦ μὴ ὄντος εἶναί φασιν, ὅτι οὐδὲ τοῦ κενοῦ τὸ σῶμα), αἴτια δὲ τῶν ὄντων ταῦτα ὡς ὕλην. καὶ καθάπερ οἱ ἓν ποιοῦντες τὴν ὑποκειμένην οὐσίαν τἆλλα τοῖς πάθεσιν αὐτῆς γεννῶσι, τὸ μανὸν καὶ τὸ πυκνὸν ἀρχὰς τιθέμενοι τῶν παθημάτων, τὸν αὐτὸν τρόπον καὶ οὗτοι τὰς διαφορὰς αἰτίας τῶν ἄλλων εἶναί φασιν. ταύτας μέντοι τρεῖς εἶναι λέγουσι, σχῆμά τε καὶ τάξιν καὶ θέσιν· διαφέρειν γάρ φασι τὸ ὂν ῥυσμῷ καὶ διαθιγῇ καὶ τροπῇ μόνον· τούτων δὲ ὁ μὲν ῥυσμὸς σχῆμά ἐστιν ἡ δὲ διαθιγὴ τάξις ἡ δὲ τροπὴ θέσις· διαφέρει γὰρ τὸ μὲν Α τοῦ Ν σχήματι τὸ δὲ ΑΝ τοῦ ΝΑ τάξει τὸ δὲ Ζ τοῦ Η θέσει. περὶ δὲ κινήσεως, ὅθεν ἢ πῶς ὑπάρξει τοῖς οὖσι, καὶ οὗτοι παραπλησίως τοῖς ἄλλοις ῥᾳθύμως ἀφεῖσαν. περὶ μὲν οὖν τῶν δύο αἰτιῶν, ὥσπερ λέγομεν, ἐπὶ τοσοῦτον ἔοικεν ἐζητῆσθαι παρὰ τῶν πρότερον.","kind":"passage"},{"id":"RAW_DAZY_BOO1:P0032","text":"ἐν δὲ τούτοις καὶ πρὸ τούτων οἱ καλούμενοι Πυθαγόρειοι τῶν μαθημάτων ἁψάμενοι πρῶτοι ταῦτά τε προήγαγον, καὶ ἐντραφέντες ἐν αὐτοῖς τὰς τούτων ἀρχὰς τῶν ὄντων ἀρχὰς ᾠήθησαν εἶναι πάντων. ἐπεὶ δὲ τούτων οἱ ἀριθμοὶ φύσει πρῶτοι, ἐν δὲ τούτοις ἐδόκουν θεωρεῖν ὁμοιώματα πολλὰ τοῖς οὖσι καὶ γιγνομένοις, μᾶλλον ἢ ἐν πυρὶ καὶ γῇ καὶ ὕδατι, ὅτι τὸ μὲν τοιονδὶ τῶν ἀριθμῶν πάθος δικαιοσύνη τὸ δὲ τοιονδὶ ψυχή τε καὶ νοῦς ἕτερον δὲ καιρὸς καὶ τῶν ἄλλων ὡς εἰπεῖν ἕκαστον ὁμοίως, ἔτι δὲ τῶν ἁρμονιῶν ἐν ἀριθμοῖς ὁρῶντες τὰ πάθη καὶ τοὺς λόγους,—ἐπεὶ δὴ τὰ μὲν ἄλλα τοῖς ἀριθμοῖς ἐφαίνοντο τὴν φύσιν ἀφωμοιῶσθαι πᾶσαν, οἱ δʼ ἀριθμοὶ πάσης τῆς φύσεως πρῶτοι,  τὰ τῶν ἀριθμῶν στοιχεῖα τῶν ὄντων στοιχεῖα πάντων ὑπέλαβον εἶναι, καὶ τὸν ὅλον οὐρανὸν ἁρμονίαν εἶναι καὶ ἀριθμόν· καὶ ὅσα εἶχον ὁμολογούμενα ἔν τε τοῖς ἀριθμοῖς καὶ ταῖς ἁρμονίαις πρὸς τὰ τοῦ οὐρανοῦ πάθη καὶ μέρη καὶ πρὸς τὴν ὅλην διακόσμησιν, ταῦτα","kind":"passage"},{"id":"RAW_DAZY_BOO1:P0033","text":"συνάγοντες ἐφήρμοττον. κἂν εἴ τί που διέλειπε, προσεγλίχοντο τοῦ συνειρομένην πᾶσαν αὐτοῖς εἶναι τὴν πραγματείαν· λέγω δʼ οἷον, ἐπειδὴ τέλειον ἡ δεκὰς εἶναι δοκεῖ καὶ πᾶσαν περιειληφέναι τὴν τῶν ἀριθμῶν φύσιν, καὶ τὰ φερόμενα κατὰ τὸν οὐρανὸν δέκα μὲν εἶναί φασιν, ὄντων δὲ ἐννέα μόνον τῶν φανερῶν διὰ τοῦτο δεκάτην τὴν ἀντίχθονα ποιοῦσιν. διώρισται δὲ περὶ τούτων ἐν ἑτέροις ἡμῖν ἀκριβέστερον. ἀλλʼ οὗ δὴ χάριν ἐπερχόμεθα, τοῦτό ἐστιν ὅπως λάβωμεν καὶ παρὰ τούτων τίνας εἶναι τιθέασι τὰς ἀρχὰς καὶ πῶς εἰς τὰς εἰρημένας ἐμπίπτουσιν αἰτίας.","kind":"passage"},{"id":"RAW_DAZY_BOO1:P0034","text":"φαίνονται δὴ καὶ οὗτοι τὸν ἀριθμὸν νομίζοντες ἀρχὴν εἶναι καὶ ὡς ὕλην τοῖς οὖσι καὶ ὡς πάθη τε καὶ ἕξεις, τοῦ δὲ ἀριθμοῦ στοιχεῖα τό τε ἄρτιον καὶ τὸ περιττόν, τούτων δὲ τὸ μὲν πεπερασμένον τὸ δὲ ἄπειρον, τὸ δʼ ἓν ἐξ ἀμφοτέρων εἶναι τούτων (καὶ γὰρ ἄρτιον εἶναι καὶ περιττόν), τὸν δʼ ἀριθμὸν ἐκ τοῦ ἑνός, ἀριθμοὺς δέ, καθάπερ εἴρηται, τὸν ὅλον οὐρανόν.—ἕτεροι δὲ τῶν αὐτῶν τούτων τὰς ἀρχὰς δέκα λέγουσιν εἶναι τὰς κατὰ συστοιχίαν λεγομένας, πέρας  ἄπειρον, περιττὸν  ἄρτιον, ἓν  πλῆθος, δεξιὸν  ἀριστερόν, ἄρρεν   θῆλυ, ἠρεμοῦν  κινούμενον, εὐθὺ  καμπύλον, φῶς  σκότος, ἀγαθὸν  κακόν, τετράγωνον  ἑτερόμηκες· ὅνπερ τρόπον ἔοικε καὶ Ἀλκμαίων ὁ Κροτωνιάτης ὑπολαβεῖν, καὶ ἤτοι οὗτος παρʼ ἐκείνων ἢ ἐκεῖνοι παρὰ τούτου παρέλαβον τὸν λόγον τοῦτον· καὶ γὰρ  Ἀλκμαίων   ἀπεφήνατο  παραπλησίως τούτοις· φησὶ γὰρ εἶναι δύο τὰ πολλὰ τῶν ἀνθρωπίνων, λέγων τὰς ἐναντιότητας οὐχ ὥσπερ οὗτοι διωρισμένας ἀλλὰ τὰς","kind":"passage"},{"id":"RAW_DAZY_BOO1:P0035","text":"τυχούσας, οἷον λευκὸν μέλαν, γλυκὺ πικρόν, ἀγαθὸν κακόν, μέγα μικρόν. οὗτος μὲν οὖν ἀδιορίστως ἀπέρριψε περὶ τῶν λοιπῶν,  οἱ δὲ Πυθαγόρειοι καὶ πόσαι καὶ τίνες αἱ ἐναντιώσεις ἀπεφήναντο. παρὰ μὲν οὖν τούτων ἀμφοῖν τοσοῦτον ἔστι λαβεῖν, ὅτι τἀναντία ἀρχαὶ τῶν ὄντων· τὸ δʼ ὅσαι παρὰ τῶν ἑτέρων, καὶ τίνες αὗταί εἰσιν. πῶς μέντοι πρὸς τὰς εἰρημένας αἰτίας ἐνδέχεται συνάγειν, σαφῶς μὲν οὐ διήρθρωται παρʼ ἐκείνων, ἐοίκασι δʼ ὡς ἐν ὕλης εἴδει τὰ στοιχεῖα τάττειν· ἐκ τούτων γὰρ ὡς ἐνυπαρχόντων συνεστάναι καὶ πεπλάσθαι φασὶ τὴν οὐσίαν.—τῶν μὲν οὖν παλαιῶν καὶ πλείω λεγόντων τὰ στοιχεῖα τῆς φύσεως ἐκ τούτων ἱκανόν ἐστι θεωρῆσαι τὴν διάνοιαν· εἰσὶ δέ τινες οἳ περὶ τοῦ παντὸς ὡς μιᾶς οὔσης φύσεως ἀπεφήναντο, τρόπον δὲ οὐ τὸν αὐτὸν πάντες οὔτε τοῦ καλῶς οὔτε τοῦ κατὰ τὴν φύσιν.","kind":"passage"},{"id":"RAW_DAZY_BOO1:P0036","text":"εἰς μὲν οὖν τὴν νῦν σκέψιν τῶν αἰτίων οὐδαμῶς συναρμόττει περὶ αὐτῶν ὁ λόγος (οὐ γὰρ ὥσπερ ἔνιοι τῶν φυσιολόγων ἓν ὑποθέμενοι τὸ ὂν ὅμως γεννῶσιν ὡς ἐξ ὕλης τοῦ ἑνός, ἀλλʼ ἕτερον τρόπον οὗτοι λέγουσιν· ἐκεῖνοι μὲν γὰρ προστιθέασι κίνησιν, γεννῶντές γε τὸ πᾶν, οὗτοι δὲ ἀκίνητον εἶναί φασιν)· οὐ μὴν ἀλλὰ τοσοῦτόν γε οἰκεῖόν ἐστι τῇ νῦν σκέψει. Παρμενίδης μὲν γὰρ ἔοικε τοῦ κατὰ τὸν λόγον ἑνὸς ἅπτεσθαι, Μέλισσος δὲ τοῦ κατὰ τὴν ὕλην (διὸ καὶ ὁ μὲν πεπερασμένον ὁ δʼ ἄπειρόν φησιν εἶναι αὐτό)· Ξενοφάνης δὲ πρῶτος τούτων ἑνίσας (ὁ γὰρ Παρμενίδης τούτου λέγεται γενέσθαι μαθητής) οὐθὲν διεσαφήνισεν, οὐδὲ τῆς φύσεως τούτων οὐδετέρας ἔοικε θιγεῖν, ἀλλʼ εἰς τὸν ὅλον οὐρανὸν ἀποβλέψας τὸ ἓν εἶναί φησι τὸν θεόν.","kind":"passage"},{"id":"RAW_DAZY_BOO1:P0037","text":"οὗτοι μὲν οὖν, καθάπερ εἴπομεν, ἀφετέοι πρὸς τὴν νῦν ζήτησιν, οἱ μὲν δύο καὶ πάμπαν ὡς ὄντες μικρὸν ἀγροικότεροι, Ξενοφάνης καὶ Μέλισσος· Παρμενίδης δὲ μᾶλλον βλέπων ἔοικέ που λέγειν· παρὰ γὰρ τὸ ὂν τὸ μὴ ὂν οὐθὲν ἀξιῶν εἶναι, ἐξ ἀνάγκης ἓν οἴεται εἶναι, τὸ ὄν, καὶ ἄλλο οὐθέν (περὶ οὗ σαφέστερον ἐν τοῖς περὶ φύσεως εἰρήκαμεν), ἀναγκαζόμενος δʼ ἀκολουθεῖν τοῖς φαινομένοις, καὶ τὸ ἓν μὲν κατὰ τὸν λόγον πλείω δὲ κατὰ τὴν αἴσθησιν ὑπολαμβάνων εἶναι, δύο τὰς αἰτίας καὶ δύο τὰς ἀρχὰς πάλιν τίθησι, θερμὸν καὶ ψυχρόν, οἷον πῦρ καὶ γῆν λέγων·  τούτων δὲ κατὰ μὲν τὸ ὂν τὸ θερμὸν τάττει θάτερον δὲ κατὰ τὸ μὴ ὄν.—ἐκ μὲν οὖν τῶν εἰρημένων καὶ παρὰ τῶν συνηδρευκότων ἤδη τῷ λόγῳ σοφῶν ταῦτα παρειλήφαμεν, παρὰ μὲν τῶν πρώτων σωματικήν τε τὴν ἀρχήν (ὕδωρ γὰρ καὶ πῦρ καὶ τὰ τοιαῦτα σώματά ἐστιν), καὶ τῶν μὲν μίαν τῶν δὲ πλείους τὰς ἀρχὰς τὰς σωματικάς, ἀμφοτέρων μέντοι ταύτας ὡς ἐν ὕλης εἴδει τιθέντων,","kind":"passage"},{"id":"RAW_DAZY_BOO1:P0038","text":"παρὰ δέ τινων ταύτην τε τὴν αἰτίαν τιθέντων καὶ πρὸς ταύτῃ τὴν ὅθεν ἡ κίνησις, καὶ ταύτην παρὰ τῶν μὲν μίαν παρὰ τῶν δὲ δύο. μέχρι μὲν οὖν τῶν Ἰταλικῶν καὶ χωρὶς ἐκείνων μορυχώτερον εἰρήκασιν οἱ ἄλλοι περὶ αὐτῶν, πλὴν ὥσπερ εἴπομεν δυοῖν τε αἰτίαιν τυγχάνουσι κεχρημένοι, καὶ τούτων τὴν ἑτέραν οἱ μὲν μίαν οἱ δὲ δύο ποιοῦσι, τὴν ὅθεν ἡ κίνησις· οἱ δὲ Πυθαγόρειοι δύο μὲν τὰς ἀρχὰς κατὰ τὸν αὐτὸν εἰρήκασι τρόπον, τοσοῦτον δὲ προσεπέθεσαν ὃ καὶ ἴδιόν ἐστιν αὐτῶν, ὅτι τὸ πεπερασμένον καὶ τὸ ἄπειρον  οὐχ ἑτέρας τινὰς ᾠήθησαν εἶναι φύσεις, οἷον πῦρ ἢ γῆν ἤ τι τοιοῦτον ἕτερον, ἀλλʼ αὐτὸ τὸ ἄπειρον καὶ αὐτὸ τὸ ἓν οὐσίαν εἶναι τούτων ὧν κατηγοροῦνται, διὸ καὶ ἀριθμὸν εἶναι τὴν οὐσίαν πάντων. περί τε τούτων οὖν τοῦτον ἀπεφήναντο τὸν τρόπον, καὶ περὶ τοῦ τί ἐστιν ἤρξαντο μὲν λέγειν καὶ ὁρίζεσθαι, λίαν δʼ ἁπλῶς ἐπραγματεύθησαν.","kind":"passage"},{"id":"RAW_DAZY_BOO1:P0039","text":"ὡρίζοντό τε γὰρ ἐπιπολαίως, καὶ ᾧ πρώτῳ ὑπάρξειεν ὁ λεχθεὶς ὅρος, τοῦτʼ εἶναι τὴν οὐσίαν τοῦ πράγματος ἐνόμιζον, ὥσπερ εἴ τις οἴοιτο ταὐτὸν εἶναι διπλάσιον καὶ τὴν δυάδα διότι πρῶτον ὑπάρχει τοῖς δυσὶ τὸ διπλάσιον. ἀλλʼ οὐ ταὐτὸν ἴσως ἐστὶ τὸ εἶναι διπλασίῳ καὶ δυάδι· εἰ δὲ μή, πολλὰ τὸ ἓν ἔσται, ὃ κἀκείνοις συνέβαινεν. παρὰ μὲν οὖν τῶν πρότερον καὶ τῶν ἄλλων τοσαῦτα ἔστι λαβεῖν.","kind":"passage"},{"id":"RAW_DAZY_BOO1:P0040","text":"μετὰ δὲ τὰς εἰρημένας φιλοσοφίας ἡ Πλάτωνος ἐπεγένετο πραγματεία, τὰ μὲν πολλὰ τούτοις ἀκολουθοῦσα, τὰ δὲ καὶ ἴδια παρὰ τὴν τῶν Ἰταλικῶν ἔχουσα φιλοσοφίαν. ἐκ νέου τε γὰρ συνήθης γενόμενος πρῶτον Κρατύλῳ καὶ ταῖς Ἡρακλειτείοις δόξαις, ὡς ἁπάντων τῶν αἰσθητῶν ἀεὶ ῥεόντων καὶ ἐπιστήμης περὶ αὐτῶν οὐκ οὔσης, ταῦτα μὲν καὶ ὕστερον οὕτως ὑπέλαβεν·  Σωκράτους δὲ περὶ μὲν τὰ ἠθικὰ πραγματευομένου περὶ δὲ τῆς ὅλης φύσεως οὐθέν, ἐν μέντοι τούτοις τὸ καθόλου ζητοῦντος καὶ περὶ ὁρισμῶν ἐπιστήσαντος πρώτου τὴν διάνοιαν, ἐκεῖνον ἀποδεξάμενος διὰ τὸ τοιοῦτον ὑπέλαβεν ὡς περὶ ἑτέρων τοῦτο γιγνόμενον καὶ οὐ τῶν αἰσθητῶν· ἀδύνατον γὰρ εἶναι τὸν κοινὸν ὅρον τῶν αἰσθητῶν τινός, ἀεί γε μεταβαλλόντων. οὗτος οὖν τὰ μὲν τοιαῦτα τῶν ὄντων ἰδέας προσηγόρευσε, τὰ δʼ αἰσθητὰ παρὰ ταῦτα καὶ κατὰ ταῦτα λέγεσθαι πάντα· κατὰ μέθεξιν γὰρ εἶναι τὰ πολλὰ ὁμώνυμα τοῖς εἴδεσιν.","kind":"passage"},{"id":"RAW_DAZY_BOO1:P0041","text":"τὴν δὲ μέθεξιν τοὔνομα μόνον μετέβαλεν· οἱ μὲν γὰρ Πυθαγόρειοι μιμήσει τὰ ὄντα φασὶν εἶναι τῶν ἀριθμῶν, Πλάτων δὲ μεθέξει, τοὔνομα μεταβαλών. τὴν μέντοι γε μέθεξιν ἢ τὴν μίμησιν ἥτις ἂν εἴη τῶν εἰδῶν ἀφεῖσαν ἐν κοινῷ ζητεῖν. ἔτι δὲ παρὰ τὰ αἰσθητὰ καὶ τὰ εἴδη τὰ μαθηματικὰ τῶν πραγμάτων εἶναί φησι μεταξύ, διαφέροντα τῶν μὲν αἰσθητῶν τῷ ἀΐδια καὶ ἀκίνητα εἶναι, τῶν δʼ εἰδῶν τῷ τὰ μὲν πόλλʼ ἄττα ὅμοια εἶναι τὸ δὲ εἶδος αὐτὸ ἓν ἕκαστον μόνον. ἐπεὶ δʼ αἴτια τὰ εἴδη τοῖς ἄλλοις, τἀκείνων στοιχεῖα πάντων ᾠήθη τῶν ὄντων εἶναι στοιχεῖα. ὡς μὲν οὖν ὕλην τὸ μέγα καὶ τὸ μικρὸν εἶναι ἀρχάς, ὡς δʼ οὐσίαν τὸ ἕν· ἐξ ἐκείνων γὰρ κατὰ μέθεξιν τοῦ ἑνὸς  εἶναι τοὺς ἀριθμούς.","kind":"passage"},{"id":"RAW_DAZY_BOO1:P0042","text":"τὸ μέντοι γε ἓν οὐσίαν εἶναι, καὶ μὴ ἕτερόν γέ τι ὂν λέγεσθαι ἕν, παραπλησίως τοῖς Πυθαγορείοις ἔλεγε, καὶ τὸ τοὺς ἀριθμοὺς αἰτίους εἶναι τοῖς ἄλλοις τῆς οὐσίας ὡσαύτως ἐκείνοις· τὸ δὲ ἀντὶ τοῦ ἀπείρου ὡς ἑνὸς δυάδα ποιῆσαι, τὸ δʼ ἄπειρον ἐκ μεγάλου καὶ μικροῦ, τοῦτʼ ἴδιον· καὶ ἔτι ὁ μὲν τοὺς ἀριθμοὺς παρὰ τὰ αἰσθητά, οἱ δʼ ἀριθμοὺς εἶναί φασιν αὐτὰ τὰ πράγματα, καὶ τὰ μαθηματικὰ μεταξὺ τούτων οὐ τιθέασιν. τὸ μὲν οὖν τὸ ἓν καὶ τοὺς ἀριθμοὺς παρὰ τὰ πράγματα ποιῆσαι, καὶ μὴ ὥσπερ οἱ Πυθαγόρειοι, καὶ ἡ τῶν εἰδῶν εἰσαγωγὴ διὰ τὴν ἐν τοῖς λόγοις ἐγένετο σκέψιν (οἱ γὰρ πρότεροι διαλεκτικῆς οὐ μετεῖχον), τὸ δὲ δυάδα ποιῆσαι τὴν ἑτέραν φύσιν διὰ τὸ τοὺς ἀριθμοὺς ἔξω τῶν πρώτων εὐφυῶς ἐξ αὐτῆς γεννᾶσθαι ὥσπερ ἔκ τινος ἐκμαγείου.  καίτοι συμβαίνει γʼ ἐναντίως· οὐ γὰρ εὔλογον οὕτως.","kind":"passage"},{"id":"RAW_DAZY_BOO1:P0043","text":"οἱ μὲν γὰρ ἐκ τῆς ὕλης πολλὰ ποιοῦσιν, τὸ δʼ εἶδος ἅπαξ γεννᾷ μόνον, φαίνεται δʼ ἐκ μιᾶς ὕλης μία τράπεζα, ὁ δὲ τὸ εἶδος ἐπιφέρων εἷς ὢν πολλὰς ποιεῖ. ὁμοίως δʼ ἔχει καὶ τὸ ἄρρεν πρὸς τὸ θῆλυ· τὸ μὲν γὰρ ὑπὸ μιᾶς πληροῦται ὀχείας, τὸ δʼ ἄρρεν πολλὰ πληροῖ· καίτοι ταῦτα μιμήματα τῶν ἀρχῶν ἐκείνων ἐστίν. Πλάτων μὲν οὖν περὶ τῶν ζητουμένων οὕτω διώρισεν· φανερὸν δʼ ἐκ τῶν εἰρημένων ὅτι δυοῖν αἰτίαιν μόνον κέχρηται, τῇ τε τοῦ τί ἐστι καὶ τῇ κατὰ τὴν ὕλην (τὰ γὰρ εἴδη τοῦ τί ἐστιν αἴτια τοῖς ἄλλοις, τοῖς δʼ εἴδεσι τὸ ἕν), καὶ τίς ἡ ὕλη ἡ ὑποκειμένη καθʼ ἧς τὰ εἴδη μὲν ἐπὶ τῶν αἰσθητῶν τὸ δʼ ἓν ἐν τοῖς εἴδεσι λέγεται, ὅτι αὕτη δυάς ἐστι, τὸ μέγα καὶ τὸ μικρόν, ἔτι δὲ τὴν τοῦ εὖ καὶ τοῦ κακῶς αἰτίαν τοῖς στοιχείοις ἀπέδωκεν ἑκατέροις ἑκατέραν, ὥσπερ φαμὲν καὶ τῶν προτέρων ἐπιζητῆσαί τινας φιλοσόφων, οἷον Ἐμπεδοκλέα καὶ Ἀναξαγόραν.","kind":"passage"},{"id":"RAW_DAZY_BOO1:P0044","text":"συντόμως μὲν οὖν καὶ κεφαλαιωδῶς ἐπεληλύθαμεν τίνες τε καὶ πῶς τυγχάνουσιν εἰρηκότες περί τε τῶν ἀρχῶν καὶ τῆς ἀληθείας· ὅμως δὲ τοσοῦτόν γʼ ἔχομεν ἐξ αὐτῶν, ὅτι τῶν λεγόντων περὶ ἀρχῆς καὶ αἰτίας οὐθεὶς ἔξω τῶν ἐν τοῖς περὶ φύσεως ἡμῖν διωρισμένων εἴρηκεν, ἀλλὰ πάντες ἀμυδρῶς μὲν ἐκείνων δέ πως φαίνονται θιγγάνοντες.","kind":"passage"},{"id":"RAW_DAZY_BOO1:P0045","text":"οἱ μὲν γὰρ ὡς ὕλην τὴν ἀρχὴν λέγουσιν, ἄν τε μίαν ἄν τε πλείους ὑποθῶσι, καὶ ἐάν τε σῶμα ἐάν τε ἀσώματον τοῦτο τιθῶσιν (οἷον Πλάτων μὲν τὸ μέγα καὶ τὸ μικρὸν λέγων, οἱ δʼ Ἰταλικοὶ τὸ ἄπειρον, Ἐμπεδοκλῆς δὲ πῦρ καὶ γῆν καὶ ὕδωρ καὶ ἀέρα, Ἀναξαγόρας δὲ τὴν τῶν ὁμοιομερῶν ἀπειρίαν· οὗτοί τε δὴ πάντες τῆς τοιαύτης αἰτίας ἡμμένοι εἰσί, καὶ ἔτι ὅσοι ἀέρα ἢ πῦρ ἢ ὕδωρ ἢ πυρὸς μὲν πυκνότερον ἀέρος δὲ λεπτότερον· καὶ γὰρ τοιοῦτόν τινες εἰρήκασιν εἶναι τὸ πρῶτον στοιχεῖον)·—οὗτοι μὲν οὖν ταύτης τῆς αἰτίας ἥψαντο μόνον, ἕτεροι δέ τινες ὅθεν ἡ ἀρχὴ τῆς κινήσεως (οἷον ὅσοι φιλίαν καὶ νεῖκος ἢ νοῦν ἢ ἔρωτα ποιοῦσιν ἀρχήν)· τὸ δὲ τί ἦν εἶναι καὶ τὴν οὐσίαν σαφῶς μὲν οὐθεὶς ἀποδέδωκε,  μάλιστα δʼ οἱ τὰ εἴδη τιθέντες λέγουσιν (οὔτε γὰρ ὡς ὕλην τοῖς αἰσθητοῖς τὰ εἴδη καὶ τὸ ἓν τοῖς εἴδεσιν οὔθʼ ὡς ἐντεῦθεν τὴν ἀρχὴν τῆς κινήσεως γιγνομένην ὑπολαμβάνουσιν—ἀκινησίας γὰρ αἴτια μᾶλλον καὶ τοῦ ἐν ἠρεμίᾳ εἶναι","kind":"passage"},{"id":"RAW_DAZY_BOO1:P0046","text":"φασιν—ἀλλὰ τὸ τί ἦν εἶναι ἑκάστῳ τῶν ἄλλων τὰ εἴδη παρέχονται, τοῖς δʼ εἴδεσι τὸ ἕν)· τὸ δʼ οὗ ἕνεκα αἱ πράξεις καὶ αἱ μεταβολαὶ καὶ αἱ κινήσεις τρόπον μέν τινα λέγουσιν αἴτιον, οὕτω δὲ οὐ λέγουσιν οὐδʼ ὅνπερ πέφυκεν.","kind":"passage"},{"id":"RAW_DAZY_BOO1:P0047","text":"οἱ μὲν γὰρ νοῦν λέγοντες ἢ φιλίαν ὡς ἀγαθὸν μὲν ταύτας τὰς αἰτίας τιθέασιν, οὐ μὴν ὡς ἕνεκά γε τούτων ἢ ὂν ἢ γιγνόμενόν τι τῶν ὄντων ἀλλʼ ὡς ἀπὸ τούτων τὰς κινήσεις οὔσας λέγουσιν· ὡς δʼ αὔτως καὶ οἱ τὸ ἓν ἢ τὸ ὂν φάσκοντες εἶναι τὴν τοιαύτην φύσιν τῆς μὲν οὐσίας αἴτιόν φασιν εἶναι, οὐ μὴν τούτου γε ἕνεκα ἢ εἶναι ἢ γίγνεσθαι, ὥστε λέγειν τε καὶ μὴ λέγειν πως συμβαίνει αὐτοῖς τἀγαθὸν αἴτιον· οὐ γὰρ ἁπλῶς ἀλλὰ κατὰ συμβεβηκὸς λέγουσιν.—ὅτι μὲν οὖν ὀρθῶς διώρισται περὶ τῶν αἰτίων καὶ πόσα καὶ ποῖα, μαρτυρεῖν ἐοίκασιν ἡμῖν καὶ οὗτοι πάντες, οὐ δυνάμενοι θιγεῖν ἄλλης αἰτίας, πρὸς δὲ τούτοις ὅτι ζητητέαι αἱ ἀρχαὶ ἢ οὕτως ἅπασαι ἢ τινὰ τρόπον τοιοῦτον, δῆλον· πῶς δὲ τούτων ἕκαστος εἴρηκε καὶ πῶς ἔχει περὶ τῶν ἀρχῶν, τὰς ἐνδεχομένας ἀπορίας μετὰ τοῦτο διέλθωμεν περὶ αὐτῶν.","kind":"passage"},{"id":"RAW_DAZY_BOO1:P0048","text":"ὅσοι μὲν οὖν ἕν τε τὸ πᾶν καὶ μίαν τινὰ φύσιν ὡς ὕλην τιθέασι, καὶ ταύτην σωματικὴν καὶ μέγεθος ἔχουσαν, δῆλον ὅτι πολλαχῶς ἁμαρτάνουσιν. τῶν γὰρ σωμάτων τὰ στοιχεῖα τιθέασι μόνον, τῶν δʼ ἀσωμάτων οὔ, ὄντων καὶ ἀσωμάτων. καὶ περὶ γενέσεως καὶ φθορᾶς ἐπιχειροῦντες τὰς αἰτίας λέγειν, καὶ περὶ πάντων φυσιολογοῦντες, τὸ τῆς κινήσεως αἴτιον ἀναιροῦσιν. ἔτι δὲ τῷ τὴν οὐσίαν μηθενὸς αἰτίαν τιθέναι μηδὲ τὸ τί ἐστι, καὶ πρὸς τούτοις τῷ ῥᾳδίως τῶν ἁπλῶν σωμάτων λέγειν ἀρχὴν ὁτιοῦν πλὴν γῆς, οὐκ ἐπισκεψάμενοι τὴν ἐξ ἀλλήλων γένεσιν πῶς ποιοῦνται, λέγω δὲ πῦρ καὶ ὕδωρ καὶ γῆν καὶ ἀέρα. τὰ μὲν γὰρ συγκρίσει τὰ δὲ διακρίσει ἐξ ἀλλήλων γίγνεται, τοῦτο δὲ πρὸς τὸ πρότερον εἶναι καὶ ὕστερον διαφέρει πλεῖστον.","kind":"passage"},{"id":"RAW_DAZY_BOO1:P0049","text":"τῇ μὲν γὰρ ἂν δόξειε στοιχειωδέστατον εἶναι πάντων ἐξ οὗ γίγνονται συγκρίσει πρώτου,  τοιοῦτον δὲ τὸ μικρομερέστατον καὶ λεπτότατον ἂν εἴη τῶν σωμάτων (διόπερ ὅσοι πῦρ ἀρχὴν τιθέασι, μάλιστα ὁμολογουμένως ἂν τῷ λόγῳ τούτῳ λέγοιεν· τοιοῦτον δὲ καὶ τῶν ἄλλων ἕκαστος ὁμολογεῖ τὸ στοιχεῖον εἶναι τὸ τῶν σωμάτων· οὐθεὶς γοῦν ἠξίωσε τῶν ἓν λεγόντων γῆν εἶναι στοιχεῖον, δηλονότι διὰ τὴν μεγαλομέρειαν, τῶν δὲ τριῶν ἕκαστον στοιχείων εἴληφέ τινα κριτήν, οἱ μὲν γὰρ πῦρ οἱ δʼ ὕδωρ οἱ δʼ ἀέρα τοῦτʼ εἶναί φασιν· καίτοι διὰ τί ποτʼ οὐ καὶ τὴν γῆν λέγουσιν, ὥσπερ οἱ πολλοὶ τῶν ἀνθρώπων; πάντα γὰρ εἶναί φασι γῆν, φησὶ δὲ καὶ Ἡσίοδος τὴν γῆν πρώτην γενέσθαι τῶν σωμάτων· οὕτως ἀρχαίαν καὶ δημοτικὴν συμβέβηκεν εἶναι τὴν ὑπόληψιν)·—κατὰ μὲν οὖν τοῦτον τὸν λόγον οὔτʼ εἴ τις τούτων τι λέγει πλὴν πυρός, οὔτʼ εἴ τις ἀέρος μὲν πυκνότερον τοῦτο τίθησιν ὕδατος δὲ λεπτότερον, οὐκ ὀρθῶς ἂν λέγοι· εἰ δʼ ἔστι τὸ τῇ","kind":"passage"},{"id":"RAW_DAZY_BOO1:P0050","text":"γενέσει ὕστερον τῇ φύσει πρότερον, τὸ δὲ πεπεμμένον καὶ συγκεκριμένον ὕστερον τῇ γενέσει, τοὐναντίον ἂν εἴη τούτων, ὕδωρ μὲν ἀέρος πρότερον γῆ δὲ ὕδατος.—περὶ μὲν οὖν τῶν μίαν τιθεμένων αἰτίαν οἵαν εἴπομεν, ἔστω ταῦτʼ εἰρημένα· τὸ δʼ αὐτὸ κἂν εἴ τις ταῦτα πλείω τίθησιν, οἷον Ἐμπεδοκλῆς τέτταρά φησιν εἶναι σώματα τὴν ὕλην. καὶ γὰρ τούτῳ τὰ μὲν ταὐτὰ τὰ δʼ ἴδια συμβαίνειν ἀνάγκη. γιγνόμενά τε γὰρ ἐξ ἀλλήλων ὁρῶμεν ὡς οὐκ ἀεὶ διαμένοντος πυρὸς καὶ γῆς τοῦ αὐτοῦ σώματος (εἴρηται δὲ ἐν τοῖς περὶ φύσεως περὶ αὐτῶν), καὶ περὶ τῆς τῶν κινουμένων αἰτίας, πότερον ἓν ἢ δύο θετέον, οὔτʼ ὀρθῶς οὔτε εὐλόγως οἰητέον εἰρῆσθαι παντελῶς. ὅλως τε ἀλλοίωσιν ἀναιρεῖσθαι ἀνάγκη τοῖς οὕτω λέγουσιν· οὐ γὰρ ἐκ θερμοῦ ψυχρὸν οὐδὲ ἐκ ψυχροῦ θερμὸν ἔσται. τὶ γὰρ αὐτὰ ἂν πάσχοι τἀναντία, καὶ τὶς εἴη ἂν μία φύσις ἡ γιγνομένη πῦρ καὶ ὕδωρ, ὃ ἐκεῖνος οὔ φησιν.","kind":"passage"},{"id":"RAW_DAZY_BOO1:P0051","text":"Ἀναξαγόραν δʼ εἴ τις ὑπολάβοι δύο λέγειν στοιχεῖα, μάλιστʼ ἂν ὑπολάβοι κατὰ λόγον, ὃν ἐκεῖνος αὐτὸς μὲν οὐ διήρθρωσεν, ἠκολούθησε μέντʼ ἂν ἐξ ἀνάγκης τοῖς ἐπάγουσιν αὐτόν. ἀτόπου γὰρ ὄντος καὶ ἄλλως τοῦ φάσκειν μεμῖχθαι τὴν ἀρχὴν πάντα,  καὶ διὰ τὸ συμβαίνειν ἄμικτα δεῖν προϋπάρχειν καὶ διὰ τὸ μὴ πεφυκέναι τῷ τυχόντι μίγνυσθαι τὸ τυχόν, πρὸς δὲ τούτοις ὅτι τὰ πάθη καὶ τὰ συμβεβηκότα χωρίζοιτʼ ἂν τῶν οὐσιῶν (τῶν γὰρ αὐτῶν μῖξίς ἐστι καὶ χωρισμός), ὅμως εἴ τις ἀκολουθήσειε συνδιαρθρῶν ἃ βούλεται λέγειν, ἴσως ἂν φανείη καινοπρεπεστέρως λέγων. ὅτε γὰρ οὐθὲν ἦν ἀποκεκριμένον, δῆλον ὡς οὐθὲν ἦν ἀληθὲς εἰπεῖν κατὰ τῆς οὐσίας ἐκείνης, λέγω δʼ οἷον ὅτι οὔτε λευκὸν οὔτε μέλαν ἢ φαιὸν ἢ ἄλλο χρῶμα, ἀλλʼ ἄχρων ἦν ἐξ ἀνάγκης· εἶχε γὰρ ἄν τι τούτων τῶν χρωμάτων· ὁμοίως δὲ καὶ ἄχυμον τῷ αὐτῷ λόγῳ τούτῳ, οὐδὲ ἄλλο τῶν ὁμοίων οὐθέν· οὔτε γὰρ ποιόν τι οἷόν τε αὐτὸ εἶναι οὔτε ποσὸν οὔτε τί.","kind":"passage"},{"id":"RAW_DAZY_BOO1:P0052","text":"τῶν γὰρ ἐν μέρει τι λεγομένων εἰδῶν ὑπῆρχεν ἂν αὐτῷ, τοῦτο δὲ ἀδύνατον μεμιγμένων γε πάντων· ἤδη γὰρ ἂν ἀπεκέκριτο, φησὶ δʼ εἶναι μεμιγμένα πάντα πλὴν τοῦ νοῦ, τοῦτον δὲ ἀμιγῆ μόνον καὶ καθαρόν. ἐκ δὴ τούτων συμβαίνει λέγειν αὐτῷ τὰς ἀρχὰς τό τε ἕν (τοῦτο γὰρ ἁπλοῦν καὶ ἀμιγές) καὶ θάτερον, οἷον τίθεμεν τὸ ἀόριστον πρὶν ὁρισθῆναι καὶ μετασχεῖν εἴδους τινός, ὥστε λέγει μὲν οὔτʼ ὀρθῶς οὔτε σαφῶς, βούλεται μέντοι τι παραπλήσιον τοῖς τε ὕστερον λέγουσι καὶ τοῖς νῦν φαινομένοις μᾶλλον.—ἀλλὰ γὰρ οὗτοι μὲν τοῖς περὶ γένεσιν λόγοις καὶ φθορὰν καὶ κίνησιν οἰκεῖοι τυγχάνουσι μόνον (σχεδὸν γὰρ περὶ τῆς τοιαύτης οὐσίας καὶ τὰς ἀρχὰς καὶ τὰς αἰτίας ζητοῦσι μόνης)· ὅσοι δὲ περὶ μὲν ἁπάντων τῶν ὄντων ποιοῦνται τὴν θεωρίαν, τῶν δʼ ὄντων τὰ μὲν αἰσθητὰ τὰ δʼ οὐκ αἰσθητὰ τιθέασι, δῆλον ὡς περὶ ἀμφοτέρων τῶν γενῶν ποιοῦνται τὴν ἐπίσκεψιν· διὸ μᾶλλον ἄν τις ἐνδιατρίψειε περὶ αὐτῶν, τί καλῶς ἢ μὴ καλῶς","kind":"passage"},{"id":"RAW_DAZY_BOO1:P0053","text":"λέγουσιν εἰς τὴν τῶν νῦν ἡμῖν προκειμένων σκέψιν. οἱ μὲν οὖν καλούμενοι Πυθαγόρειοι ταῖς μὲν ἀρχαῖς καὶ τοῖς στοιχείοις ἐκτοπωτέροις χρῶνται τῶν φυσιολόγων (τὸ δʼ αἴτιον ὅτι παρέλαβον αὐτὰς οὐκ ἐξ αἰσθητῶν· τὰ γὰρ μαθηματικὰ τῶν ὄντων ἄνευ κινήσεώς ἐστιν ἔξω τῶν περὶ τὴν ἀστρολογίαν), διαλέγονται μέντοι καὶ πραγματεύονται περὶ φύσεως πάντα· γεννῶσί τε γὰρ τὸν οὐρανόν,  καὶ περὶ τὰ τούτου μέρη καὶ τὰ πάθη καὶ τὰ ἔργα διατηροῦσι τὸ συμβαῖνον, καὶ τὰς ἀρχὰς καὶ τὰ αἴτια εἰς ταῦτα καταναλίσκουσιν, ὡς ὁμολογοῦντες τοῖς ἄλλοις φυσιολόγοις ὅτι τό γε ὂν τοῦτʼ ἐστὶν ὅσον αἰσθητόν ἐστι καὶ περιείληφεν ὁ καλούμενος οὐρανός. τὰς δʼ αἰτίας καὶ τὰς ἀρχάς, ὥσπερ εἴπομεν, ἱκανὰς λέγουσιν ἐπαναβῆναι καὶ ἐπὶ τὰ ἀνωτέρω τῶν ὄντων, καὶ μᾶλλον ἢ τοῖς περὶ φύσεως λόγοις ἁρμοττούσας.","kind":"passage"},{"id":"RAW_DAZY_BOO1:P0054","text":"ἐκ τίνος μέντοι τρόπου κίνησις ἔσται πέρατος καὶ ἀπείρου μόνων ὑποκειμένων καὶ περιττοῦ καὶ ἀρτίου, οὐθὲν λέγουσιν, ἢ πῶς δυνατὸν ἄνευ κινήσεως καὶ μεταβολῆς γένεσιν εἶναι καὶ φθορὰν ἢ τὰ τῶν φερομένων ἔργα κατὰ τὸν οὐρανόν. ἔτι δὲ εἴτε δοίη τις αὐτοῖς ἐκ τούτων εἶναι μέγεθος εἴτε δειχθείη τοῦτο, ὅμως τίνα τρόπον ἔσται τὰ μὲν κοῦφα τὰ δὲ βάρος ἔχοντα τῶν σωμάτων; ἐξ ὧν γὰρ ὑποτίθενται καὶ λέγουσιν, οὐθὲν μᾶλλον περὶ τῶν μαθηματικῶν λέγουσι σωμάτων ἢ τῶν αἰσθητῶν· διὸ περὶ πυρὸς ἢ γῆς ἢ τῶν ἄλλων τῶν τοιούτων σωμάτων οὐδʼ ὁτιοῦν εἰρήκασιν, ἅτε οὐθὲν περὶ τῶν αἰσθητῶν οἶμαι λέγοντες ἴδιον.","kind":"passage"},{"id":"RAW_DAZY_BOO1:P0055","text":"ἔτι δὲ πῶς δεῖ λαβεῖν αἴτια μὲν εἶναι τὰ τοῦ ἀριθμοῦ πάθη καὶ τὸν ἀριθμὸν τῶν κατὰ τὸν οὐρανὸν ὄντων καὶ γιγνομένων καὶ ἐξ ἀρχῆς καὶ νῦν, ἀριθμὸν δʼ ἄλλον μηθένα εἶναι παρὰ τὸν ἀριθμὸν τοῦτον ἐξ οὗ συνέστηκεν ὁ κόσμος; ὅταν γὰρ ἐν τῳδὶ μὲν τῷ μέρει δόξα καὶ καιρὸς αὐτοῖς ᾖ, μικρὸν δὲ ἄνωθεν ἢ κάτωθεν ἀδικία καὶ κρίσις ἢ μῖξις, ἀπόδειξιν δὲ λέγωσιν ὅτι τούτων μὲν ἕκαστον ἀριθμός ἐστι, συμβαίνει δὲ κατὰ τὸν τόπον τοῦτον ἤδη πλῆθος εἶναι τῶν συνισταμένων μεγεθῶν διὰ τὸ τὰ πάθη ταῦτα ἀκολουθεῖν τοῖς τόποις ἑκάστοις, πότερον οὗτος ὁ αὐτός ἐστιν ἀριθμός, ὁ ἐν τῷ οὐρανῷ, ὃν δεῖ λαβεῖν ὅτι τούτων ἕκαστόν ἐστιν, ἢ παρὰ τοῦτον ἄλλος; ὁ μὲν γὰρ Πλάτων ἕτερον εἶναί φησιν· καίτοι κἀκεῖνος ἀριθμοὺς οἴεται καὶ ταῦτα εἶναι καὶ τὰς τούτων αἰτίας, ἀλλὰ τοὺς μὲν νοητοὺς αἰτίους τούτους δὲ αἰσθητούς.","kind":"passage"},{"id":"RAW_DAZY_BOO1:P0056","text":"περὶ μὲν οὖν τῶν Πυθαγορείων ἀφείσθω τὰ νῦν (ἱκανὸν γὰρ αὐτῶν ἅψασθαι τοσοῦτον)·  οἱ δὲ τὰς ἰδέας αἰτίας τιθέμενοι πρῶτον μὲν ζητοῦντες τωνδὶ τῶν ὄντων λαβεῖν τὰς αἰτίας ἕτερα τούτοις ἴσα τὸν ἀριθμὸν ἐκόμισαν, ὥσπερ εἴ τις ἀριθμῆσαι βουλόμενος ἐλαττόνων μὲν ὄντων οἴοιτο μὴ δυνήσεσθαι, πλείω δὲ ποιήσας ἀριθμοίη (σχεδὸν γὰρ ἴσα—ἢ οὐκ ἐλάττω—ἐστὶ τὰ εἴδη τούτοις περὶ ὧν ζητοῦντες τὰς αἰτίας ἐκ τούτων ἐπʼ ἐκεῖνα προῆλθον· καθʼ ἕκαστον γὰρ ὁμώνυμόν τι ἔστι καὶ παρὰ τὰς οὐσίας, τῶν τε ἄλλων ἔστιν ἓν ἐπὶ πολλῶν, καὶ ἐπὶ τοῖσδε καὶ ἐπὶ τοῖς ἀϊδίοις)· ἔτι δὲ καθʼ οὓς τρόπους δείκνυμεν ὅτι ἔστι τὰ εἴδη, κατʼ οὐθένα φαίνεται τούτων· ἐξ ἐνίων μὲν γὰρ οὐκ ἀνάγκη γίγνεσθαι συλλογισμόν, ἐξ ἐνίων δὲ καὶ οὐχ ὧν οἰόμεθα τούτων εἴδη γίγνεται.","kind":"passage"},{"id":"RAW_DAZY_BOO1:P0057","text":"κατά τε γὰρ τοὺς λόγους τοὺς ἐκ τῶν ἐπιστημῶν εἴδη ἔσται πάντων ὅσων ἐπιστῆμαι εἰσί, καὶ κατὰ τὸ ἓν ἐπὶ πολλῶν καὶ τῶν ἀποφάσεων, κατὰ δὲ τὸ νοεῖν τι φθαρέντος τῶν φθαρτῶν· φάντασμα γάρ τι τούτων ἔστιν. ἔτι δὲ οἱ ἀκριβέστεροι τῶν λόγων οἱ μὲν τῶν πρός τι ποιοῦσιν ἰδέας, ὧν οὔ φαμεν εἶναι καθʼ αὑτὸ γένος, οἱ δὲ τὸν τρίτον ἄνθρωπον λέγουσιν.","kind":"passage"},{"id":"RAW_DAZY_BOO1:P0058","text":"ὅλως τε ἀναιροῦσιν οἱ περὶ τῶν εἰδῶν λόγοι ἃ μᾶλλον εἶναι βουλόμεθα  τοῦ τὰς ἰδέας εἶναι· συμβαίνει γὰρ μὴ εἶναι τὴν δυάδα πρώτην ἀλλὰ τὸν ἀριθμόν, καὶ τὸ πρός τι τοῦ καθʼ αὑτό, καὶ πάνθʼ ὅσα τινὲς ἀκολουθήσαντες ταῖς περὶ τῶν ἰδεῶν δόξαις ἠναντιώθησαν ταῖς ἀρχαῖς.—ἔτι κατὰ μὲν τὴν ὑπόληψιν καθʼ ἣν εἶναί φαμεν τὰς ἰδέας οὐ μόνον τῶν οὐσιῶν ἔσται εἴδη ἀλλὰ πολλῶν καὶ ἑτέρων (καὶ γὰρ τὸ νόημα ἓν οὐ μόνον περὶ τὰς οὐσίας ἀλλὰ καὶ κατὰ τῶν ἄλλων ἐστί, καὶ ἐπιστῆμαι οὐ μόνον τῆς οὐσίας εἰσὶν ἀλλὰ καὶ ἑτέρων, καὶ ἄλλα δὲ μυρία συμβαίνει τοιαῦτα)· κατὰ δὲ τὸ ἀναγκαῖον καὶ τὰς δόξας τὰς περὶ αὐτῶν, εἰ ἔστι μεθεκτὰ τὰ εἴδη, τῶν οὐσιῶν ἀναγκαῖον ἰδέας εἶναι μόνον.","kind":"passage"},{"id":"RAW_DAZY_BOO1:P0059","text":"οὐ γὰρ κατὰ συμβεβηκὸς μετέχονται ἀλλὰ δεῖ ταύτῃ ἑκάστου μετέχειν ᾗ μὴ καθʼ ὑποκειμένου λέγεται (λέγω δʼ οἷον, εἴ τι αὐτοδιπλασίου μετέχει, τοῦτο καὶ ἀϊδίου μετέχει, ἀλλὰ κατὰ συμβεβηκός· συμβέβηκε γὰρ τῷ διπλασίῳ ἀϊδίῳ εἶναι), ὥστʼ ἔσται οὐσία τὰ εἴδη· ταὐτὰ δὲ ἐνταῦθα οὐσίαν σημαίνει κἀκεῖ·  ἢ τί ἔσται τὸ εἶναι τι παρὰ ταῦτα, τὸ ἓν ἐπὶ πολλῶν; καὶ εἰ μὲν ταὐτὸ εἶδος τῶν ἰδεῶν καὶ τῶν μετεχόντων, ἔσται τι κοινόν (τί γὰρ μᾶλλον ἐπὶ τῶν φθαρτῶν δυάδων, καὶ τῶν πολλῶν μὲν ἀϊδίων δέ, τὸ δυὰς ἓν καὶ ταὐτόν, ἢ ἐπί τʼ αὐτῆς καὶ τῆς τινός;)· εἰ δὲ μὴ τὸ αὐτὸ εἶδος, ὁμώνυμα ἂν εἴη, καὶ ὅμοιον ὥσπερ ἂν εἴ τις καλοῖ ἄνθρωπον τόν τε Καλλίαν καὶ τὸ ξύλον, μηδεμίαν κοινωνίαν ἐπιβλέψας αὐτῶν.—πάντων δὲ μάλιστα διαπορήσειεν ἄν τις τί ποτε συμβάλλεται τὰ εἴδη τοῖς ἀϊδίοις τῶν αἰσθητῶν ἢ τοῖς γιγνομένοις καὶ φθειρομένοις· οὔτε γὰρ κινήσεως οὔτε μεταβολῆς οὐδεμιᾶς ἐστὶν αἴτια αὐτοῖς.","kind":"passage"},{"id":"RAW_DAZY_BOO1:P0060","text":"ἀλλὰ μὴν οὔτε πρὸς τὴν ἐπιστήμην οὐθὲν βοηθεῖ τὴν τῶν ἄλλων (οὐδὲ γὰρ οὐσία ἐκεῖνα τούτων· ἐν τούτοις γὰρ ἂν ἦν), οὔτε εἰς τὸ εἶναι, μὴ ἐνυπάρχοντά γε τοῖς μετέχουσιν· οὕτω μὲν γὰρ ἂν ἴσως αἴτια δόξειεν εἶναι ὡς τὸ λευκὸν μεμιγμένον τῷ λευκῷ, ἀλλʼ οὗτος μὲν ὁ λόγος λίαν εὐκίνητος, ὃν Ἀναξαγόρας μὲν πρῶτος Εὔδοξος δʼ ὕστερον καὶ ἄλλοι τινὲς ἔλεγον (ῥᾴδιον γὰρ συναγαγεῖν πολλὰ καὶ ἀδύνατα πρὸς τὴν τοιαύτην δόξαν)· ἀλλὰ μὴν οὐδʼ ἐκ τῶν εἰδῶν ἐστὶ τἆλλα κατʼ οὐθένα τρόπον τῶν εἰωθότων λέγεσθαι. τὸ δὲ λέγειν παραδείγματα αὐτὰ εἶναι καὶ μετέχειν αὐτῶν τἆλλα κενολογεῖν ἐστὶ καὶ μεταφορὰς λέγειν ποιητικάς. τί γάρ ἐστι τὸ ἐργαζόμενον πρὸς τὰς ἰδέας ἀποβλέπον; ἐνδέχεταί τε καὶ εἶναι καὶ γίγνεσθαι ὅμοιον ὁτιοῦν καὶ μὴ εἰκαζόμενον πρὸς ἐκεῖνο, ὥστε καὶ ὄντος Σωκράτους καὶ μὴ ὄντος γένοιτʼ ἂν οἷος Σωκράτης· ὁμοίως δὲ δῆλον ὅτι κἂν εἰ ἦν ὁ Σωκράτης ἀΐδιος.","kind":"passage"},{"id":"RAW_DAZY_BOO1:P0061","text":"ἔσται τε πλείω παραδείγματα τοῦ αὐτοῦ, ὥστε καὶ εἴδη, οἷον τοῦ ἀνθρώπου τὸ ζῷον καὶ τὸ δίπουν, ἅμα δὲ καὶ τὸ αὐτοάνθρωπος. ἔτι οὐ μόνον τῶν αἰσθητῶν παραδείγματα τὰ εἴδη ἀλλὰ καὶ αὐτῶν, οἷον τὸ γένος, ὡς γένος εἰδῶν· ὥστε τὸ αὐτὸ ἔσται παράδειγμα καὶ εἰκών.","kind":"passage"},{"id":"RAW_DAZY_BOO1:P0062","text":"ἔτι δόξειεν ἂν ἀδύνατον εἶναι χωρὶς τὴν οὐσίαν καὶ οὗ ἡ οὐσία· ὥστε πῶς ἂν αἱ ἰδέαι οὐσίαι τῶν πραγμάτων οὖσαι χωρὶς εἶεν; ἐν δὲ τῷ Φαίδωνι οὕτω λέγεται, ὡς καὶ τοῦ εἶναι καὶ τοῦ γίγνεσθαι αἴτια τὰ εἴδη ἐστίν· καίτοι τῶν εἰδῶν ὄντων ὅμως οὐ γίγνεται τὰ μετέχοντα ἂν μὴ ᾖ τὸ κινῆσον, καὶ πολλὰ γίγνεται ἕτερα, οἷον οἰκία καὶ δακτύλιος, ὧν οὔ φαμεν εἴδη εἶναι· ὥστε δῆλον ὅτι ἐνδέχεται καὶ τἆλλα καὶ εἶναι καὶ γίγνεσθαι διὰ τοιαύτας αἰτίας οἵας καὶ τὰ ῥηθέντα νῦν.—ἔτι εἴπερ εἰσὶν ἀριθμοὶ τὰ εἴδη, πῶς αἴτιοι ἔσονται; πότερον ὅτι ἕτεροι ἀριθμοί εἰσι τὰ ὄντα, οἷον ὁδὶ μὲν ὁ ἀριθμὸς ἄνθρωπος ὁδὶ δὲ Σωκράτης ὁδὶ δὲ Καλλίας; τί οὖν ἐκεῖνοι τούτοις αἴτιοί εἰσιν; οὐδὲ γὰρ εἰ οἱ μὲν ἀΐδιοι οἱ δὲ μή, οὐδὲν διοίσει. εἰ δʼ ὅτι λόγοι ἀριθμῶν τἀνταῦθα, οἷον ἡ συμφωνία, δῆλον ὅτι ἐστὶν ἕν γέ τι ὧν εἰσὶ λόγοι. εἰ δή τι τοῦτο, ἡ ὕλη, φανερὸν ὅτι καὶ αὐτοὶ οἱ ἀριθμοὶ λόγοι τινὲς ἔσονται ἑτέρου πρὸς ἕτερον.","kind":"passage"},{"id":"RAW_DAZY_BOO1:P0063","text":"λέγω δʼ οἷον, εἰ ἔστιν ὁ Καλλίας λόγος ἐν ἀριθμοῖς πυρὸς καὶ γῆς καὶ ὕδατος καὶ ἀέρος, καὶ ἄλλων τινῶν ὑποκειμένων ἔσται καὶ ἡ ἰδέα ἀριθμός· καὶ αὐτοάνθρωπος, εἴτʼ ἀριθμός τις ὢν εἴτε μή, ὅμως ἔσται λόγος ἐν ἀριθμοῖς τινῶν καὶ οὐκ ἀριθμός, οὐδʼ ἔσται τις διὰ ταῦτα ἀριθμός. ἔτι ἐκ πολλῶν ἀριθμῶν εἷς ἀριθμὸς γίγνεται, ἐξ εἰδῶν δὲ ἓν εἶδος πῶς; εἰ δὲ μὴ ἐξ αὐτῶν ἀλλʼ ἐκ τῶν ἐν τῷ ἀριθμῷ, οἷον ἐν τῇ μυριάδι, πῶς ἔχουσιν αἱ μονάδες; εἴτε γὰρ ὁμοειδεῖς, πολλὰ συμβήσεται ἄτοπα, εἴτε μὴ ὁμοειδεῖς, μήτε αὐταὶ ἀλλήλαις μήτε αἱ ἄλλαι πᾶσαι πάσαις· τίνι γὰρ διοίσουσιν ἀπαθεῖς οὖσαι; οὔτε γὰρ εὔλογα ταῦτα οὔτε ὁμολογούμενα τῇ νοήσει. ἔτι δʼ ἀναγκαῖον ἕτερον γένος ἀριθμοῦ κατασκευάζειν περὶ ὃ ἡ ἀριθμητική, καὶ πάντα τὰ μεταξὺ λεγόμενα ὑπό τινων, ἃ πῶς ἢ ἐκ τίνων ἐστὶν ἀρχῶν; ἢ διὰ τί μεταξὺ τῶν δεῦρό τʼ ἔσται καὶ αὐτῶν; ἔτι αἱ μονάδες αἱ ἐν τῇ δυάδι ἑκατέρα ἔκ τινος προτέρας δυάδος· καίτοι ἀδύνατον.","kind":"passage"},{"id":"RAW_DAZY_BOO1:P0064","text":"ἔτι διὰ τί ἓν ὁ ἀριθμὸς συλλαμβανόμενος; ἔτι δὲ πρὸς τοῖς εἰρημένοις, εἴπερ εἰσὶν αἱ μονάδες διάφοροι, ἐχρῆν οὕτω λέγειν ὥσπερ καὶ ὅσοι τὰ στοιχεῖα τέτταρα ἢ δύο λέγουσιν· καὶ γὰρ τούτων ἕκαστος οὐ τὸ κοινὸν λέγει στοιχεῖον, οἷον τὸ σῶμα, ἀλλὰ πῦρ καὶ γῆν, εἴτʼ ἔστι τι κοινόν, τὸ σῶμα, εἴτε μή. νῦν δὲ λέγεται ὡς ὄντος τοῦ ἑνὸς ὥσπερ πυρὸς ἢ ὕδατος ὁμοιομεροῦς· εἰ δʼ οὕτως, οὐκ ἔσονται οὐσίαι οἱ ἀριθμοί, ἀλλὰ δῆλον ὅτι, εἴπερ ἐστί τι ἓν αὐτὸ καὶ τοῦτό ἐστιν ἀρχή, πλεοναχῶς λέγεται τὸ ἕν· ἄλλως γὰρ ἀδύνατον.—βουλόμενοι δὲ τὰς οὐσίας ἀνάγειν εἰς τὰς ἀρχὰς μήκη μὲν τίθεμεν ἐκ βραχέος καὶ μακροῦ, ἔκ τινος μικροῦ καὶ μεγάλου, καὶ ἐπίπεδον ἐκ πλατέος καὶ στενοῦ, σῶμα δʼ ἐκ βαθέος καὶ ταπεινοῦ.","kind":"passage"},{"id":"RAW_DAZY_BOO1:P0065","text":"καίτοι πῶς ἕξει ἢ τὸ ἐπίπεδον γραμμὴν ἢ τὸ στερεὸν γραμμὴν καὶ ἐπίπεδον; ἄλλο γὰρ γένος τὸ πλατὺ καὶ στενὸν καὶ βαθὺ καὶ ταπεινόν· ὥσπερ οὖν οὐδʼ ἀριθμὸς ὑπάρχει ἐν αὐτοῖς, ὅτι τὸ πολὺ καὶ ὀλίγον ἕτερον τούτων, δῆλον ὅτι οὐδʼ ἄλλο οὐθὲν τῶν ἄνω ὑπάρξει τοῖς κάτω. ἀλλὰ μὴν οὐδὲ γένος τὸ πλατὺ τοῦ βαθέος· ἦν γὰρ ἂν ἐπίπεδόν τι τὸ σῶμα. ἔτι αἱ στιγμαὶ ἐκ τίνος ἐνυπάρξουσιν; τούτῳ μὲν οὖν τῷ γένει καὶ διεμάχετο Πλάτων ὡς ὄντι γεωμετρικῷ δόγματι, ἀλλʼ ἐκάλει ἀρχὴν γραμμῆς—τοῦτο δὲ πολλάκις ἐτίθει—τὰς ἀτόμους γραμμάς.","kind":"passage"},{"id":"RAW_DAZY_BOO1:P0066","text":"καίτοι ἀνάγκη τούτων εἶναί τι πέρας· ὥστʼ ἐξ οὗ λόγου γραμμὴ ἔστι, καὶ στιγμὴ ἔστιν.—ὅλως δὲ ζητούσης τῆς σοφίας περὶ τῶν φανερῶν τὸ αἴτιον, τοῦτο μὲν εἰάκαμεν (οὐθὲν γὰρ λέγομεν περὶ τῆς αἰτίας ὅθεν ἡ ἀρχὴ τῆς μεταβολῆς) , τὴν δʼ οὐσίαν οἰόμενοι λέγειν αὐτῶν ἑτέρας μὲν οὐσίας εἶναί φαμεν, ὅπως δʼ ἐκεῖναι τούτων οὐσίαι, διὰ κενῆς λέγομεν· τὸ γὰρ μετέχειν, ὥσπερ καὶ πρότερον εἴπομεν, οὐθέν ἐστιν. οὐδὲ δὴ ὅπερ ταῖς ἐπιστήμαις ὁρῶμεν ὂν αἴτιον, διʼ ὃ καὶ πᾶς νοῦς καὶ πᾶσα φύσις ποιεῖ, οὐδὲ ταύτης τῆς αἰτίας, ἥν φαμεν εἶναι μίαν τῶν ἀρχῶν, οὐθὲν ἅπτεται τὰ εἴδη, ἀλλὰ γέγονε τὰ μαθήματα τοῖς νῦν ἡ φιλοσοφία, φασκόντων ἄλλων χάριν αὐτὰ δεῖν πραγματεύεσθαι.","kind":"passage"},{"id":"RAW_DAZY_BOO1:P0067","text":"ἔτι δὲ τὴν ὑποκειμένην οὐσίαν ὡς ὕλην μαθηματικωτέραν ἄν τις ὑπολάβοι, καὶ μᾶλλον κατηγορεῖσθαι καὶ διαφορὰν εἶναι τῆς οὐσίας καὶ τῆς ὕλης ἢ ὕλην, οἷον τὸ μέγα καὶ τὸ μικρόν, ὥσπερ καὶ οἱ φυσιολόγοι φασὶ τὸ μανὸν καὶ τὸ πυκνόν, πρώτας τοῦ ὑποκειμένου φάσκοντες εἶναι διαφορὰς ταύτας· ταῦτα γάρ ἐστιν ὑπεροχή τις καὶ ἔλλειψις. περί τε κινήσεως, εἰ μὲν ἔσται ταῦτα κίνησις, δῆλον ὅτι κινήσεται τὰ εἴδη· εἰ δὲ μή, πόθεν ἦλθεν; ὅλη γὰρ ἡ περὶ φύσεως ἀνῄρηται σκέψις. ὅ τε δοκεῖ ῥᾴδιον εἶναι, τὸ δεῖξαι ὅτι ἓν ἅπαντα, οὐ γίγνεται· τῇ γὰρ ἐκθέσει οὐ γίγνεται πάντα ἓν ἀλλʼ αὐτό τι ἕν, ἂν διδῷ τις πάντα· καὶ οὐδὲ τοῦτο, εἰ μὴ γένος δώσει τὸ καθόλου εἶναι· τοῦτο δʼ ἐν ἐνίοις ἀδύνατον.","kind":"passage"},{"id":"RAW_DAZY_BOO1:P0068","text":"οὐθένα δʼ ἔχει λόγον οὐδὲ τὰ μετὰ τοὺς ἀριθμοὺς μήκη τε καὶ ἐπίπεδα καὶ στερεά, οὔτε ὅπως ἔστιν ἢ ἔσται οὔτε τίνα ἔχει δύναμιν· ταῦτα γὰρ οὔτε εἴδη οἷόν τε εἶναι (οὐ γάρ εἰσιν ἀριθμοί) οὔτε τὰ μεταξύ (μαθηματικὰ γὰρ ἐκεῖνα) οὔτε τὰ φθαρτά, ἀλλὰ πάλιν τέταρτον ἄλλο φαίνεται τοῦτό τι γένος. ὅλως τε τὸ τῶν ὄντων ζητεῖν στοιχεῖα μὴ διελόντας, πολλαχῶς λεγομένων, ἀδύνατον εὑρεῖν, ἄλλως τε καὶ τοῦτον τὸν τρόπον ζητοῦντας ἐξ οἵων ἐστὶ στοιχείων. ἐκ τίνων γὰρ τὸ ποιεῖν ἢ πάσχειν ἢ τὸ εὐθύ, οὐκ ἔστι δήπου λαβεῖν, ἀλλʼ εἴπερ, τῶν οὐσιῶν μόνον ἐνδέχεται· ὥστε τὸ τῶν ὄντων ἁπάντων τὰ στοιχεῖα ἢ ζητεῖν ἢ οἴεσθαι ἔχειν οὐκ ἀληθές. πῶς δʼ ἄν τις καὶ μάθοι τὰ τῶν πάντων στοιχεῖα; δῆλον γὰρ ὡς οὐθὲν οἷόν τε προϋπάρχειν γνωρίζοντα πρότερον.","kind":"passage"},{"id":"RAW_DAZY_BOO1:P0069","text":"ὥσπερ γὰρ τῷ γεωμετρεῖν μανθάνοντι ἄλλα μὲν ἐνδέχεται προειδέναι, ὧν δὲ ἡ ἐπιστήμη καὶ περὶ ὧν μέλλει μανθάνειν οὐθὲν προγιγνώσκει, οὕτω δὴ καὶ ἐπὶ τῶν ἄλλων, ὥστʼ εἴ τις τῶν πάντων ἔστιν ἐπιστήμη, οἵαν δή τινές φασιν, οὐθὲν ἂν προϋπάρχοι γνωρίζων οὗτος. καίτοι πᾶσα μάθησις διὰ προγιγνωσκομένων ἢ πάντων ἢ τινῶν ἐστί, καὶ ἡ διʼ ἀποδείξεως καὶ ἡ διʼ ὁρισμῶν (δεῖ γὰρ ἐξ ὧν ὁ ὁρισμὸς προειδέναι καὶ εἶναι γνώριμα)· ὁμοίως δὲ καὶ ἡ διʼ ἐπαγωγῆς. ἀλλὰ μὴν εἰ καὶ τυγχάνοι σύμφυτος οὖσα,  θαυμαστὸν πῶς λανθάνομεν ἔχοντες τὴν κρατίστην τῶν ἐπιστημῶν. ἔτι πῶς τις γνωριεῖ ἐκ τίνων ἐστί, καὶ πῶς ἔσται δῆλον; καὶ γὰρ τοῦτʼ ἔχει ἀπορίαν· ἀμφισβητήσειε γὰρ ἄν τις ὥσπερ καὶ περὶ ἐνίας συλλαβάς· οἱ μὲν γὰρ τὸ ζα ἐκ τοῦ ς καὶ δ καὶ α φασὶν εἶναι, οἱ δέ τινες ἕτερον φθόγγον φασὶν εἶναι καὶ οὐθένα τῶν γνωρίμων.","kind":"passage"},{"id":"RAW_DAZY_BOO1:P0070","text":"ἔτι δὲ ὧν ἐστὶν αἴσθησις, ταῦτα πῶς ἄν τις μὴ ἔχων τὴν αἴσθησιν γνοίη; καίτοι ἔδει, εἴγε πάντων ταὐτὰ στοιχεῖά ἐστιν ἐξ ὧν, ὥσπερ αἱ σύνθετοι φωναί εἰσιν ἐκ τῶν οἰκείων στοιχείων.","kind":"passage"},{"id":"RAW_DAZY_BOO1:P0071","text":"ὅτι μὲν οὖν τὰς εἰρημένας ἐν τοῖς φυσικοῖς αἰτίας ζητεῖν ἐοίκασι πάντες, καὶ τούτων ἐκτὸς οὐδεμίαν ἔχοιμεν ἂν εἰπεῖν, δῆλον καὶ ἐκ τῶν πρότερον εἰρημένων· ἀλλʼ ἀμυδρῶς ταύτας, καὶ τρόπον μέν τινα πᾶσαι πρότερον εἴρηνται τρόπον δέ τινα οὐδαμῶς. ψελλιζομένῃ γὰρ ἔοικεν ἡ πρώτη φιλοσοφία περὶ πάντων, ἅτε νέα τε καὶ κατʼ ἀρχὰς οὖσα , ἐπεὶ καὶ Ἐμπεδοκλῆς ὀστοῦν τῷ λόγῳ φησὶν εἶναι, τοῦτο δʼ ἐστὶ τὸ τί ἦν εἶναι καὶ ἡ οὐσία τοῦ πράγματες. ἀλλὰ μὴν ὁμοίως ἀναγκαῖον καὶ σάρκας καὶ τῶν ἄλλων ἕκαστον εἶναι τὸν λόγον, ἢ μηδὲ ἕν· διὰ τοῦτο γὰρ καὶ σὰρξ καὶ ὀστοῦν ἔσται καὶ τῶν ἄλλων ἕκαστον καὶ οὐ διὰ τὴν ὕλην, ἣν ἐκεῖνος λέγει, πῦρ καὶ γῆν καὶ ὕδωρ καὶ ἀέρα. ἀλλὰ ταῦτα ἄλλου μὲν λέγοντος συνέφησεν ἂν ἐξ ἀνάγκης, σαφῶς δὲ οὐκ εἴρηκεν.","kind":"passage"},{"id":"RAW_DAZY_BOO1:P0072","text":"περὶ μὲν οὖν τούτων δεδήλωται καὶ πρότερον· ὅσα δὲ περὶ τῶν αὐτῶν τούτων ἀπορήσειεν ἄν τις, ἐπανέλθωμεν πάλιν· τάχα γὰρ ἂν ἐξ αὐτῶν εὐπορήσαιμέν τι πρὸς τὰς ὕστερον ἀπορίας.","kind":"passage"}],"sha256":"fab1025ce199f8b55f202897ca777c5b31106c494a7f7c221cd7582d6329a67c","language_code":"grc","language_name":"Ἑλληνική","translations":[{"id":"SOURCE_TR_20261015T172106523127Z_5ea33425","language_code":"en","language_name":"English","title":"Book 1","source_sha256":"fab1025ce199f8b55f202897ca777c5b31106c494a7f7c221cd7582d6329a67c","sha256":"a9968b60a67236397e78a6c53fac7539da9bfc1439c10b288607531792317ebc","rendition_type":"machine","review_status":"machine","model":"gpt-5.6-terra","created":"2026-10-15T17:21:06.523177+00:00","paragraphs":[{"id":"RAW_DAZY_BOO1:P0001","text":"All human beings by nature reach out for knowing. A sign of this is the love of the senses; for apart from their usefulness they are loved for themselves, and most of all the sense through the eyes. For not only in order that we may act, but even when we are about to do nothing, we choose seeing, so to speak, in preference to all the other senses. The cause is that this sense, more than the others, makes us know and reveals many differences.\n\nThus animals are born by nature possessing sensation, but from this sensation memory does not arise in some of them, while in others it does arise. And for this reason the latter are more prudent and more capable of learning than those unable to remember: prudent without learning are those which cannot hear sounds (for example, the bee, and whatever other kind of animal there may be of this sort), while those learn which, in addition to memory, also possess this sense. The other animals, then, live by appearances and memories, and partake little in experience; but the human kind lives also by art and reasonings.","kind":"passage","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_DAZY_BOO1:P0001"]},{"id":"RAW_DAZY_BOO1:P0002","text":"Experience comes to human beings from memory; for the many memories of the same thing produce the capacity of a single experience. And experience seems to be nearly like science and art, but science and art come to human beings through experience. For experience made art, as Polus says, whereas inexperience made chance. Art arises whenever, from many conceptions derived from experience, one universal supposition about similar things comes to be.","kind":"passage","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_DAZY_BOO1:P0002"]},{"id":"RAW_DAZY_BOO1:P0003","text":"For to have the supposition that this remedy benefited Callias when suffering from this disease, and Socrates, and similarly many individuals, belongs to experience; but that it benefited all such persons, defined as one in kind, suffering from this disease—for example, phlegmatic or bilious persons feverish with burning fever—belongs to art.—With a view to acting, then, experience seems to differ in no way from art; indeed, experienced people succeed more than those who possess an account without experience. (The cause is that experience is knowledge of particulars, whereas art is knowledge of universals; but all actions and all generations concern particulars. For the physician does not heal a human being except incidentally, but Callias or Socrates or some other of those so called, to whom it happens to be a human being. If, then, someone possesses the account without experience, and knows the universal but is ignorant of the particular included in it, he will often fail in the treatment; for it is the","kind":"passage","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_DAZY_BOO1:P0003"]},{"id":"RAW_DAZY_BOO1:P0004","text":"particular that is treatable.) Yet nevertheless we think that knowing and understanding belong more to art than to experience, and we suppose craftsmen to be wiser than experienced persons, on the ground that wisdom follows everyone more according to knowing. This is because the former know the cause, while the latter do not. For experienced people know the that, but do not know the why; whereas the others know the why and the cause. Hence we also consider master-craftsmen in each case more honorable, and to know more, than manual workers, and wiser, because they know the causes of the things made (whereas the latter, just as some lifeless things produce effects without knowing what they do—for example, fire burns; lifeless things, then, produce each of these effects by a certain nature, while manual workers do so by habit), as though they were wiser not insofar as they are practical, but insofar as they possess an account and know the causes.","kind":"passage","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_DAZY_BOO1:P0004"]},{"id":"RAW_DAZY_BOO1:P0005","text":"In general, the sign of one who knows and one who does not know is the ability to teach; and therefore we think art to be science more than experience is. For the former can teach, whereas the latter cannot. Further, we do not think any of the senses to be wisdom, although these are the most authoritative cognitions of particulars; yet they state the why concerning nothing—for example, why fire is hot—but only that it is hot. It is likely, then, that at first whoever discovered any art beyond the common sensations was admired by human beings, not only because something of what was discovered was useful, but as wise and distinguished from the others. And when more arts had been discovered, some directed toward necessities and others toward recreation, those concerned with the latter were always supposed wiser than those concerned with the former, because their sciences were not for use.","kind":"passage","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_DAZY_BOO1:P0005"]},{"id":"RAW_DAZY_BOO1:P0006","text":"Hence, once all such things had been provided, those sciences that are directed neither toward pleasure nor toward necessities were discovered, and first in those places where people first had leisure. Therefore the mathematical arts first arose around Egypt; for there the class of priests was allowed to have leisure. It has been stated in the ethical works what the difference is between art and science and the other cognate states; but the reason we are now making this inquiry is this: everyone supposes that what is called wisdom concerns the first causes and principles. Hence, as was said before, the experienced person seems wiser than anyone whatever who has sensation, the craftsman than the experienced person, the master-craftsman than the manual worker, and the theoretical sciences more so than the productive ones. That wisdom, then, is a science concerning certain principles and causes is clear.","kind":"passage","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_DAZY_BOO1:P0006"]},{"id":"RAW_DAZY_BOO1:P0007","text":"Since we seek this science, it would be necessary to consider what sort of causes and what sort of principles wisdom is a science of. If one were to take the suppositions we hold concerning the wise person, perhaps this would make it clearer. First, then, we suppose that the wise person knows all things as far as possible, without possessing knowledge of each particular thing; next, that one capable of knowing difficult things, and things not easy for a human being to know, is wise (for sensing is common to all, and therefore easy and not at all wise); further, that in every science the more exact and the more capable of teaching the causes is wiser; and, among the sciences, that the one chosen for its own sake and for the sake of knowing is more wisdom than that chosen for the sake of its results, and that the more ruling science is more wisdom than the subordinate one. For the wise person ought not to be commanded, but to command; nor ought he to obey another, but the less wise person ought to obey him.","kind":"passage","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_DAZY_BOO1:P0007"]},{"id":"RAW_DAZY_BOO1:P0008","text":"—Such and so many are the suppositions we hold concerning wisdom and the wise. Of these, knowing all things must belong to the one who possesses universal knowledge most fully (for this person somehow knows all the things subject to it); and these universals are also almost the most difficult things for human beings to know (for they are furthest removed from the senses). The most exact sciences are those which are most concerned with first things (for those proceeding from fewer principles are more exact than those called sciences by addition, as arithmetic is more exact than geometry). Moreover, the science that contemplates causes is more capable of teaching (for those teach who state the causes in each case); and knowing and understanding for their own sake belong most of all to the science of what is most knowable (for one who chooses knowing for its own sake will choose most of all the science that is most so; and such is the science of what is most knowable). But the first things and the causes are most knowable (for through these and from these the other things","kind":"passage","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_DAZY_BOO1:P0008"]},{"id":"RAW_DAZY_BOO1:P0009","text":"are known, but not these through the things subject to them), and the most ruling of the sciences, and more ruling than the subordinate science, is that which knows for the sake of what each thing is to be done. This is the good of each thing, and in general the best in all nature. From all that has been said, then, the name sought falls upon the same science; for it must be contemplative of the first principles and causes. For the good and that for the sake of which are one of the causes.","kind":"passage","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_DAZY_BOO1:P0009"]},{"id":"RAW_DAZY_BOO1:P0010","text":"That it is not productive is clear also from the first philosophers. For it was through wonder that human beings, both now and at first, began to philosophize: at first wondering at the strange things close at hand, then progressing little by little in this way and being at a loss about greater matters—for example, about the affections of the moon, and those concerning the sun and stars, and about the coming-to-be of the whole. But one who is at a loss and wonders thinks himself ignorant (which is why the lover of myth is in a way a philosopher, for myth is composed of wonders). Hence, if they philosophized in order to escape ignorance, it is clear that they pursued knowing for the sake of knowing and not for the sake of any use. And the actual occurrence bears witness to this: for it was when nearly all the necessities, and things for ease and recreation, were already available that this kind of understanding began to be sought.","kind":"passage","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_DAZY_BOO1:P0010"]},{"id":"RAW_DAZY_BOO1:P0011","text":"It is clear, then, that we seek it for no other need, but that, just as we say a human being is free who is for his own sake and not for another's, so we seek this science as alone free among the sciences; for it alone is for its own sake. Hence its possession might justly be thought not human; for in many ways human nature is enslaved, so that, according to Simonides,\nonly a god might possess this prerogative,\nand it is unworthy of a man not to seek the science in accordance with himself. If what the poets say has any truth, and the divine is by nature envious, it is likely that this would occur especially in this case, and that all who excel would be unfortunate. But it is neither possible for the divine to be envious—rather, according to the proverb, poets tell many lies—nor should any science be thought more honorable than this one. For it is the most divine and most honorable; and it alone could be such in two ways. For that science which a god would most possess is divine among the sciences, as is also any science concerned with divine things.","kind":"passage","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_DAZY_BOO1:P0011"]},{"id":"RAW_DAZY_BOO1:P0012","text":"This science alone has obtained both of these characteristics. For god is thought to be among the causes of all things and to be a certain principle, and god alone, or most of all, would possess such a science. All the others, then, are more necessary than this, but none is better.—Yet in a way its acquisition must end for us in the opposite of the inquiries from which we began. For, as we said, all begin from wondering whether something is so, as with the self-moving things among marvels, or with the turnings of the sun, or the incommensurability of the diagonal (for it seems marvelous to all who have not yet contemplated the cause if something is not measured by the least unit). But one must end in the opposite and the better, according to the proverb, as also in these cases when they have learned; for a geometrician would not wonder at anything so much as if the diagonal became commensurable. What, then, is the nature of the science sought, and what is the aim which the inquiry and the whole method must attain, has been stated.","kind":"passage","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_DAZY_BOO1:P0012"]},{"id":"RAW_DAZY_BOO1:P0013","text":"Since it is clear that we must acquire knowledge of the original causes (for we say that we know each thing when we suppose that we know its first cause), and causes are spoken of in four ways: one cause, we say, is the substance and the what-it-was-to-be (for the why is reduced ultimately to the account, and the first why is cause and principle); another is the matter and the underlying subject; a third is that from which the principle of motion comes; and a fourth is the cause opposed to this, that for the sake of which and the good (for this is the end of every coming-to-be and motion). Although we have considered these sufficiently in the works on nature, nevertheless let us also take up for examination those who before us came to investigate beings and philosophized about truth.","kind":"passage","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_DAZY_BOO1:P0013"]},{"id":"RAW_DAZY_BOO1:P0014","text":"For it is clear that they too speak of certain principles and causes. Our survey of them, then, will be of some service to the present method: for either we shall discover some other kind of cause, or we shall have more confidence in those now spoken of.—Of those who first philosophized, most supposed that the principles of all things were only those in the form of matter. For that from which all beings are, and from which they first come to be, and into which they are finally destroyed, while the substance remains but its attributes change—this, they say, is the element and this the principle of beings. And therefore they suppose that nothing comes to be or is destroyed, since such a nature is always preserved; just as we do not say that Socrates simply comes to be when he becomes beautiful or musical, nor is destroyed when he loses these states, because the underlying subject, Socrates himself, remains. So too with none of the other things; for there must always be some nature, either one or more than one, from which the other things come to be, while that nature is preserved.","kind":"passage","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_DAZY_BOO1:P0014"]},{"id":"RAW_DAZY_BOO1:P0015","text":"Yet not all state the same number and form of such a principle. Thales, the founder of this kind of philosophy, says that it is water (which is why he also declared that the earth rests upon water), perhaps taking this supposition from seeing that the nourishment of all things is moist, and that heat itself comes to be from this and lives by this (and that from which something comes to be is the principle of all things)—taking this supposition, then, both for this reason and because the seeds of all things have a moist nature, water being the principle of the nature of moist things. There are some who think that even the very ancient persons, who long before the present generation and first theologized, held this view about nature. For they made Ocean and Tethys the parents of generation, and water, called by them Styx, the oath of the gods. For the most ancient is most honorable, and an oath is the most honorable thing.","kind":"passage","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_DAZY_BOO1:P0015"]},{"id":"RAW_DAZY_BOO1:P0016","text":"Whether, then, this opinion about nature happens to be ancient and old is perhaps unclear; but Thales is said to have declared himself in this way concerning the first cause (for no one would deem Hippo worthy of being placed among these because of the poverty of his thought). Anaximenes and Diogenes posit air as prior to water and as the most fundamental principle of simple bodies; Hippasus of Metapontum and Heraclitus of Ephesus posit fire; Empedocles posits the four, adding earth as a fourth to those mentioned (for these always remain and do not come to be, except in quantity and scarcity, being combined and separated into one and from one); while Anaxagoras of Clazomenae, earlier than Empedocles in age but later in his works, says that the principles are infinite. For he says that nearly all homoeomerous things, such as water or fire, come to be and perish in this way, by combination and separation only; otherwise they neither come to be nor perish, but remain eternal.—From these, then,","kind":"passage","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_DAZY_BOO1:P0016"]},{"id":"RAW_DAZY_BOO1:P0017","text":"one might suppose that the only cause is the one spoken of in the form of matter. But as they proceeded in this way, the matter itself made a road for them and compelled them to seek further. For even if every coming-to-be and perishing is, as much as possible, from some one thing or even from several things, why does this happen, and what is the cause? Surely the underlying subject itself does not make itself change. I mean, for example, neither wood nor bronze is the cause of either's changing; nor does the wood make a bed, nor the bronze a statue, but something else is the cause of the change. To seek this is to seek the other principle, as we would say, that from which comes the principle of motion.","kind":"passage","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_DAZY_BOO1:P0017"]},{"id":"RAW_DAZY_BOO1:P0018","text":"Those, then, who from the beginning wholly embraced this method and said that the underlying subject is one did not make any difficulty for themselves; indeed, some of those who say it is one, as though defeated by this inquiry, say that the one is unmoved and that the whole of nature is unmoved not only with respect to coming-to-be and perishing (for this is ancient and all agreed on it), but also with respect to every other change. And this is peculiar to them.","kind":"passage","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_DAZY_BOO1:P0018"]},{"id":"RAW_DAZY_BOO1:P0019","text":"Of those who say that the all is one, no one succeeded in discerning a cause of this kind, except perhaps Parmenides—and he only insofar as he posits not merely one cause but, in a way, two. But for those who make them more numerous, it is more possible to speak of it, for example for those who make them hot and cold, or fire and earth; for they employ fire as having a nature capable of causing motion, and water and earth and such things as having the contrary nature.—But after these, and after principles of this kind, since these were insufficient to generate the nature of beings, they were again, as we said, compelled by truth itself to seek the next principle. For perhaps neither fire nor earth nor any other such thing is likely to be the cause of some beings being, and others coming to be, well and beautifully; nor is it likely that those men thought so. Nor again was it fitting to entrust so great a matter to spontaneity and chance.","kind":"passage","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_DAZY_BOO1:P0019"]},{"id":"RAW_DAZY_BOO1:P0020","text":"Someone who said that intellect is present—as in animals—also in nature, and that it is the cause of the world and of all order, appeared like a sober man beside those who had spoken at random before him. We know that Anaxagoras manifestly touched upon these arguments, though Hermotimus of Clazomenae has a prior claim to have said it. Those, then, who held this view posited at once the good as a cause, as a principle of beings, and also that kind of cause from which motion belongs to beings;","kind":"passage","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_DAZY_BOO1:P0020"]},{"id":"RAW_DAZY_BOO1:P0021","text":"One might suppose that Hesiod was the first to seek such a thing, or anyone else who posited love or desire among beings as a principle, as Parmenides also did. For he too, in constructing the generation of the all,","kind":"passage","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_DAZY_BOO1:P0021"]},{"id":"RAW_DAZY_BOO1:P0022","text":"“First of all, he devised Love of all the gods.”","kind":"passage","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_DAZY_BOO1:P0022"]},{"id":"RAW_DAZY_BOO1:P0023","text":"Parmenides Fr. 13 (Diels), but Hesiod:","kind":"passage","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_DAZY_BOO1:P0023"]},{"id":"RAW_DAZY_BOO1:P0024","text":"“First of all Chaos came to be, but then thereafter”","kind":"passage","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_DAZY_BOO1:P0024"]},{"id":"RAW_DAZY_BOO1:P0025","text":"“broad-bosomed Earth [Gap in source]”","kind":"passage","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_DAZY_BOO1:P0025"]},{"id":"RAW_DAZY_BOO1:P0026","text":"“and Love, who stands out among all the immortals,”","kind":"passage","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_DAZY_BOO1:P0026"]},{"id":"RAW_DAZY_BOO1:P0027","text":"Hes. Th. 116–20, as though there must be present among beings some cause which will move and bring things together. How, then, these men should be apportioned with regard to who was first, may be decided later. But since contraries to goods also appeared to be present in nature, and not only order and the beautiful but also disorder and the ugly, and since evils were more numerous than goods and base things than beautiful ones, someone else introduced Love and Strife, each the cause of each of these respectively.","kind":"passage","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_DAZY_BOO1:P0027"]},{"id":"RAW_DAZY_BOO1:P0028","text":"For if one were to follow and grasp Empedocles' thought, rather than what he stammers in speaking, one would find that Love is the cause of goods and Strife of evils. Thus, if someone were to say that in a way Empedocles both says, and is the first to say, that the bad and the good are principles, he would perhaps speak correctly, if indeed the Good itself is the cause of all goods.—These men, then, as we say, appear to have touched upon two of the causes which we distinguished in the writings on nature, namely matter and that from which motion comes; though dimly and not at all clearly, but as untrained men do in battles. For they too, moving about, often strike fine blows, but neither do those do so from knowledge nor do these seem to know what they are saying; for they appear to make almost no use of these principles, except to a small extent.","kind":"passage","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_DAZY_BOO1:P0028"]},{"id":"RAW_DAZY_BOO1:P0029","text":"For Anaxagoras employs intellect as a device for world-making, and whenever he is at a loss through what cause something is necessarily so, then he drags it in; but in other matters he assigns every cause of what comes to be rather than intellect. And Empedocles uses these causes more than he does, yet neither sufficiently nor does he find in them what is consistent. At any rate, in many places with him Love separates, while Strife combines.","kind":"passage","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_DAZY_BOO1:P0029"]},{"id":"RAW_DAZY_BOO1:P0030","text":"For whenever the all is divided into the elements by Strife, then fire is combined into one, and likewise each of the other elements; but whenever they again come together into the one under Love, the parts must again be separated out from each.—Empedocles, then, beyond his predecessors, was the first to introduce a division of the cause, making the principle of motion not one but different and contrary ones; further, he was the first to say that the elements spoken of as in the form of matter are four (though in fact he does not employ four, but as though there were only two: fire by itself, and the opposed things as one nature—earth, air, and water; one might infer this by considering his verses).—He, then, as we say, stated the principles in this way and in this number. But Leucippus and his associate Democritus say that the full and the void are elements, calling the one being and the other non-being; of these, the full and solid is being, while the void is non-being (whence they also say that being is no more","kind":"passage","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_DAZY_BOO1:P0030"]},{"id":"RAW_DAZY_BOO1:P0031","text":"than non-being, because body is no more than the void), and they take these as causes of beings in the manner of matter. And just as those who make the underlying substance one generate the other things by its affections, positing the rare and the dense as principles of the affections, so these too say that the differences are causes of the other things. They say that these are three: shape, arrangement, and position. For they say that being differs only by rhythm, contact, and turning; and of these rhythm is shape, contact arrangement, and turning position. For A differs from N in shape, AN from NA in arrangement, and Z from H in position. But concerning motion—whence or how it will belong to beings—these too, like the others, carelessly let the matter go. Thus far, then, it seems that the earlier thinkers investigated the two causes of which we speak.","kind":"passage","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_DAZY_BOO1:P0031"]},{"id":"RAW_DAZY_BOO1:P0032","text":"Among these, and before them, the so-called Pythagoreans, having first taken up mathematics and advanced it, and having been brought up in it, supposed its principles to be the principles of all beings. Since numbers are by nature first among these things, and since in numbers they seemed to discern many likenesses to beings and things that come to be, more than in fire and earth and water—because one such affection of numbers was justice, another soul and intellect, another opportunity, and similarly, so to speak, each of the other things; and further, since they saw in numbers the affections and ratios of harmonies—since, then, all other things seemed to have their whole nature assimilated to numbers, while numbers were first in the whole of nature, they supposed the elements of numbers to be the elements of all beings, and the whole heaven to be harmony and number. And whatever correspondences they had in numbers and harmonies with the affections and parts of heaven and with the whole ordering of things, these","kind":"passage","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_DAZY_BOO1:P0032"]},{"id":"RAW_DAZY_BOO1:P0033","text":"they collected and fitted together. And if anything was lacking anywhere, they sought to supply it, so that their whole account might be connected. I mean, for example, since the decad seems to be perfect and to comprise the whole nature of numbers, they say that the bodies moving in heaven are ten; but since only nine are visible, for this reason they make the counter-earth the tenth. We have distinguished these matters more precisely elsewhere. But the reason we are reviewing them is this: that we may learn from these men also what principles they posit and how they fall under the causes stated.","kind":"passage","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_DAZY_BOO1:P0033"]},{"id":"RAW_DAZY_BOO1:P0034","text":"These too, then, evidently suppose number to be a principle, both as matter for beings and as their affections and states; and the elements of number are the even and the odd, of which the one is limited and the other unlimited; while the one is composed of both of these (for it is both even and odd), and number is from the one; and, as has been said, the whole heaven is numbers.—But others among these same people say that the principles are the ten spoken of in pairs of opposites: \nlimit  unlimited,\nodd  even,\none  plurality,\nright  left,\nmale  female,\nat rest  moving,\nstraight  curved,\nlight  darkness,\ngood  bad,\nsquare  oblong.\nAlcmaeon of Croton seems to have held the same view; and either he received this account from them or they from him. For Alcmaeon also declared things in a manner similar to these. He says that the many human things are two, speaking of contrarieties not determined as these are, but of whatever ones occur, such as","kind":"passage","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_DAZY_BOO1:P0034"]},{"id":"RAW_DAZY_BOO1:P0035","text":"white and black, sweet and bitter, good and bad, great and small. This man, then, threw out his remarks about the remaining opposites without determination; but the Pythagoreans declared both how many and which the oppositions are. From both of these, then, one can gather this much: that contraries are principles of beings; from the latter, how many they are and what they are. But how they can be brought under the stated causes has not been clearly articulated by them; yet they seem to arrange the elements as in the form of matter. For they say that substance is composed and fashioned from these as things present within it.—From these considerations it is sufficient to discern the thought of the ancients who speak of more than one element of nature. But there are some who have declared concerning the all as though its nature were one, though not all in the same way, either in correctness or in accordance with nature.","kind":"passage","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_DAZY_BOO1:P0035"]},{"id":"RAW_DAZY_BOO1:P0036","text":"For the account concerning them does not at all fit the present inquiry into causes (for they do not, like some natural philosophers, posit being as one and yet generate all things from the one as from matter; rather, these men speak in another way. For the former add motion, since they generate the all, whereas these say that it is motionless). Yet it is relevant to the present inquiry to this extent. For Parmenides seems to touch upon the one according to account, Melissus upon the one according to matter (and hence the former says that it is limited, the latter unlimited). Xenophanes, however, who first unified among these—for Parmenides is said to have been his pupil—made nothing clear, nor does he seem to touch either of these two natures; rather, looking to the whole heaven, he says that the one is god.","kind":"passage","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_DAZY_BOO1:P0036"]},{"id":"RAW_DAZY_BOO1:P0037","text":"These men, then, as we said, must be set aside for the present inquiry—two of them altogether, as being somewhat too rustic, Xenophanes and Melissus. But Parmenides seems in some respect to speak with greater insight. For, judging that beside being non-being is nothing, he thinks that necessarily being is one, and nothing else (concerning which we have spoken more clearly in the writings on nature). But, compelled to follow the appearances, and supposing that the one is one according to account but many according to perception, he again posits two causes and two principles, hot and cold, calling them fire and earth; and of these he assigns the hot to being, and the other to non-being.—From what has been said, then, and from the wise men who have already taken their seats with our argument, we have received these things: from the first, that the principle is bodily (for water and fire and such things are bodies), and that some posit one bodily principle and others several, though both posit these as in the form of matter;","kind":"passage","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_DAZY_BOO1:P0037"]},{"id":"RAW_DAZY_BOO1:P0038","text":"and from certain others, that they posit both this cause and, in addition to it, that from which motion comes—one such cause according to some, two according to others. Up to the Italians, then, and apart from them, the others have spoken of these matters more confusedly; except that, as we said, they happen to have employed two causes, and of these they make one, some one and others two: the cause from which motion comes. The Pythagoreans too have spoken of two principles in the same manner; but they added something which is peculiar to them: they did not think that the limited and the unlimited were certain other natures, such as fire or earth or some other such thing, but that the unlimited itself and the one itself were the substance of those things of which they are predicated; hence they held number to be the substance of all things. They declared themselves thus about these matters; and they began to speak and define concerning what something is, but their treatment was exceedingly simple.","kind":"passage","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_DAZY_BOO1:P0038"]},{"id":"RAW_DAZY_BOO1:P0039","text":"For they defined superficially, and whatever thing first had the stated definition, this they thought to be the substance of the thing—as if someone thought that double and two were the same because doubleness first belongs to two. But perhaps being double and being two are not the same; otherwise the one will be many, which also happened to them. Such, then, is what may be gathered from the earlier thinkers and the others.","kind":"passage","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_DAZY_BOO1:P0039"]},{"id":"RAW_DAZY_BOO1:P0040","text":"After the philosophies mentioned, Plato's inquiry arose, in many respects following these, but also possessing things peculiar to itself beyond the philosophy of the Italians. For in his youth he first became acquainted with Cratylus and the Heraclitean opinions, that all sensible things are always flowing and that there is no knowledge concerning them; and he retained these views later as well. But since Socrates was occupied with ethical matters and not at all with the whole of nature, while in these matters he was seeking the universal and was the first to fix his thought upon definitions, Plato, accepting this, supposed that this concerned other things and not sensibles. For it was impossible, he thought, for the common definition to be of any sensible thing, since they are always changing. Such beings, then, he called Ideas; and he said that all sensibles are named apart from these and in accordance with these. For the many are homonymous with the Forms by participation.","kind":"passage","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_DAZY_BOO1:P0040"]},{"id":"RAW_DAZY_BOO1:P0041","text":"He changed only the name of participation. For the Pythagoreans say that beings exist by imitation of numbers, whereas Plato says by participation, changing the name. But what the participation or imitation of Forms might be, they left open to common inquiry. Further, besides sensible things and the Forms, he says that the mathematical objects of things are intermediate, differing from sensibles in being eternal and motionless, and from Forms in that there are many things alike, whereas each Form itself is one alone. And since Forms are causes for the other things, he thought that their elements were elements of all beings. As matter, the great and the small are principles; as substance, the one. For numbers are from those, through participation in the one.","kind":"passage","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_DAZY_BOO1:P0041"]},{"id":"RAW_DAZY_BOO1:P0042","text":"Now, that the one is substance, and is not called one while being some other thing, he said similarly to the Pythagoreans; likewise, that numbers are causes of the substance of other things. But making a dyad, instead of the unlimited as one, and making the unlimited out of the great and the small—this is peculiar to him. Further, he places numbers apart from sensibles, whereas they say that the things themselves are numbers and do not posit mathematical objects as intermediate between these. Thus, his making the one and numbers separate from things, unlike the Pythagoreans, and his introduction of the Forms arose from inquiry in accounts (for the earlier thinkers did not participate in dialectic); while making the other nature a dyad arose because numbers, apart from the first, are naturally generated from it as from some mould. Yet the contrary happens; for this is not reasonable.","kind":"passage","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_DAZY_BOO1:P0042"]},{"id":"RAW_DAZY_BOO1:P0043","text":"For those who proceed from matter make many things, whereas the form generates only once; yet from one matter one table appears to come to be, while he who imposes the form, though being one, makes many. The male also stands similarly in relation to the female: the female is filled by one intercourse, whereas the male fills many; and yet these are imitations of those principles. Plato, then, thus distinguished concerning the matters under inquiry. But it is clear from what has been said that he employed only two causes, that of what-it-is and that according to matter (for the Forms are causes of what-it-is for other things, and the One for the Forms), and [said] what the underlying matter is, according to which the Forms are predicated in the case of sensibles and the One among the Forms: that it is the dyad, the Great and the Small. Further, he assigned to the elements, respectively, the cause of well and ill, as we say that certain of the earlier philosophers also sought to do, such as Empedocles and Anaxagoras.","kind":"passage","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_DAZY_BOO1:P0043"]},{"id":"RAW_DAZY_BOO1:P0044","text":"Briefly, then, and in outline, we have reviewed who they are and in what manner they happen to have spoken concerning principles and truth. Yet we gain at least this much from them: that none of those who speak concerning principle and cause has said anything outside the [causes] distinguished by us in the writings on nature, but all appear, though obscurely, somehow to touch upon these.","kind":"passage","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_DAZY_BOO1:P0044"]},{"id":"RAW_DAZY_BOO1:P0045","text":"For some speak of the principle as matter, whether they posit one [such principle] or more, and whether they posit it as body or as incorporeal (for example, Plato in speaking of the Great and the Small; the Italians, of the Unlimited; Empedocles, of fire and earth and water and air; Anaxagoras, of the infinity of homoeomeries; all these, then, have laid hold of this kind of cause; as have also all those who [posit] air or fire or water, or something denser than fire but finer than air—for some have said the first element to be of this sort). These, then, have touched upon this cause alone; but certain others [have touched upon] that whence the principle of motion comes (for example, all who make friendship and strife, or intellect, or love, a principle). But no one has clearly rendered the what-it-was-to-be and the substance, though those who posit the Forms speak of it most of all (for they do not suppose the Forms to be matter for sensibles, nor the One for the Forms, nor [suppose] the principle of motion to arise from these—for they say","kind":"passage","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_DAZY_BOO1:P0045"]},{"id":"RAW_DAZY_BOO1:P0046","text":"that they are rather causes of immobility and of being at rest—but the Forms provide the what-it-was-to-be for each of the other things, and the One for the Forms). As for that for the sake of which actions and changes and motions [occur], they in a way call it a cause, but they do not call it so in this way, nor in the way in which it naturally is [a cause].","kind":"passage","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_DAZY_BOO1:P0046"]},{"id":"RAW_DAZY_BOO1:P0047","text":"For those who speak of intellect or friendship posit these causes as good, but not as that for the sake of which something among beings either is or comes to be; rather, they say that motions proceed from them. Similarly too, those who say that the One or Being is such a nature say that it is a cause of substance, but not that it is for the sake of this that things either are or come to be. Thus it happens to them, in a way, both to say and not to say that the good is a cause; for they do not say it simply, but incidentally. That the causes have been correctly distinguished, both how many and what sorts they are, all these men too seem to bear witness for us, since they are unable to touch upon any other cause. And, in addition to these, it is clear that the principles must be sought either all in this way or in some such manner. But how each of these has spoken concerning them, and what is the situation regarding the principles, let us next go through the possible difficulties concerning them.","kind":"passage","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_DAZY_BOO1:P0047"]},{"id":"RAW_DAZY_BOO1:P0048","text":"All, then, who posit both that the whole is one and some one nature as matter, and posit this as bodily and as having magnitude, plainly err in many ways. For they posit elements only of bodies, not of incorporeals, though there are incorporeals also. And while undertaking to state the causes concerning coming-to-be and passing-away, and while theorizing about all things concerning nature, they eliminate the cause of motion. Further, [they err] by positing neither substance nor what-it-is as the cause of anything; and, besides these things, by readily declaring any one whatsoever of the simple bodies, except earth, to be a principle, without considering how they come to be from one another—I mean fire and water and earth and air. For some come to be from one another by combination, others by separation; and this makes the greatest difference with respect to being prior and posterior.","kind":"passage","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_DAZY_BOO1:P0048"]},{"id":"RAW_DAZY_BOO1:P0049","text":"For, on the one hand, that from which things first come to be by combination would seem to be most elemental of all; and such would be the most fine-parted and thinnest of bodies. Hence those who posit fire as a principle would speak most consistently according to this account. And each of the others too agrees that the element of bodies is of this sort; at any rate, none of those who say that [all is] one thought fit to make earth an element, evidently because of its large parts, whereas each of the other three elements has found some advocate: some say that it is fire, others water, others air. And yet why do they not also speak of earth, as most human beings do? For they say that all things are earth; and Hesiod too says that earth came to be first among bodies—so ancient and popular has this supposition happened to be. According to this account, then, neither if someone says any one of these except fire, nor if someone posits it as denser than air but finer than water, would he speak correctly. But if what is posterior in","kind":"passage","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_DAZY_BOO1:P0049"]},{"id":"RAW_DAZY_BOO1:P0050","text":"generation is prior by nature, and what has been concocted and combined is posterior in generation, the opposite of these [claims] would hold: water would be prior to air, and earth to water. Let this, then, have been said concerning those who posit one cause of the sort we have mentioned. But the same [objections apply] also if someone posits more of these, as Empedocles says that matter consists of four bodies. For to him it is necessary that some consequences be the same and others peculiar. For we observe them coming to be from one another, since fire and earth do not always remain the same body (and concerning these things it has been spoken in the writings on nature); and concerning the cause of things in motion, whether one or two should be posited, one must not think that he has spoken altogether either correctly or reasonably. In general, those who speak in this way must eliminate alteration; for there will not be cold from hot, nor hot from cold. For what would the contraries undergo, and what one nature would there be that became fire and water—which he does not assert.","kind":"passage","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_DAZY_BOO1:P0050"]},{"id":"RAW_DAZY_BOO1:P0051","text":"If one were to suppose that Anaxagoras says there are two elements, one would most especially suppose this in accordance with an argument which he himself did not articulate, but which he would necessarily have followed if others had led him [to it]. For it is absurd in other ways too to say that all things were mixed at the beginning: both because it follows that the unmixed must pre-exist, and because one random thing is not naturally fitted to mix with another random thing; and besides, because affections and accidents would be separable from substances (for mixing and separation belong to the same things). Nevertheless, if someone followed him by articulating jointly what he wishes to say, he might perhaps appear to speak more originally. For when nothing was separated off, plainly nothing true could be said of that substance; I mean, for example, that it was neither white nor black nor gray nor any other colour, but was necessarily colourless—for it would otherwise have had some one of these colours. Likewise, by this same argument, it was tasteless, and none of the other similar things; for it could not be either a something-qualified or a quantity or a what.","kind":"passage","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_DAZY_BOO1:P0051"]},{"id":"RAW_DAZY_BOO1:P0052","text":"For some of the forms spoken of in part would belong to it; but this is impossible if all things are mixed, for they would already have been separated off. Yet he says that all things are mixed except intellect, and that intellect alone is unmixed and pure. From these [claims], then, it follows for him to say that the principles are both the One (for this is simple and unmixed) and the Other, such as we posit the indefinite before it has been determined and participated in some form. Thus he speaks neither correctly nor clearly, though he intends something similar to what those who speak later, and those now apparent [to us], say rather. But these men are at home only in arguments concerning coming-to-be and passing-away and motion (for they investigate principles and causes almost exclusively concerning this kind of substance); whereas those who make their inquiry concerning all beings, and posit that of beings some are sensible and others not sensible, plainly conduct their examination concerning both kinds. Hence one might dwell more upon them, [examining] what they say well or not well","kind":"passage","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_DAZY_BOO1:P0052"]},{"id":"RAW_DAZY_BOO1:P0053","text":"for the inquiry now before us. Those called Pythagoreans, then, employ principles and elements more remote from those of the natural philosophers (the reason being that they took them not from sensibles; for mathematical objects among beings are without motion, except those concerned with astronomy); nevertheless, they discourse and concern themselves wholly with nature. For they generate the heaven, and observe what happens in its parts, affections, and works, and expend their principles and causes upon these, as though agreeing with the other natural philosophers that being is just whatever is sensible and embraced by what is called heaven. But the causes and principles, as we have said, they state to be sufficient for ascending even to the higher regions of beings, and more fitting than for accounts concerning nature.","kind":"passage","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_DAZY_BOO1:P0053"]},{"id":"RAW_DAZY_BOO1:P0054","text":"Yet they say nothing about how motion will arise when only limit and unlimited, odd and even, are posited; or how, without motion and change, coming-to-be and passing-away can occur, or the works of the bodies borne along in heaven. Further, even if one were to grant them that magnitude is composed out of these, or if this were demonstrated, nevertheless how will some bodies be light and others have weight? For from what they posit and say, they speak no more about mathematical bodies than about sensible ones. Hence they have said nothing whatever about fire or earth or any other such bodies, since, I think, they say nothing peculiar about sensibles.","kind":"passage","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_DAZY_BOO1:P0054"]},{"id":"RAW_DAZY_BOO1:P0055","text":"Further, how are we to understand that the affections of number and number are causes of the things that are and come to be in heaven, both from the beginning and now, while there is no number other than this number from which the cosmos has been constituted? For whenever in this part there are, for them, opinion and occasion, and a little higher or lower injustice and judgment or mixture, and they say as proof that each of these is a number, but it happens that already in this place there is a multitude of constituted magnitudes because these affections follow each of the places respectively—is this the same number, the number in heaven, which must be taken to be each of these things, or is it another besides this? Plato says it is another. Yet he too thinks that these things and their causes are numbers, but the former are intelligible causes and the latter sensible.","kind":"passage","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_DAZY_BOO1:P0055"]},{"id":"RAW_DAZY_BOO1:P0056","text":"Let the Pythagoreans, then, be dismissed for the present (for it is sufficient to have touched upon them to this extent). But those who posit the Ideas as causes, first, in seeking to grasp the causes of these beings here, brought in other [beings] equal in number to these, as though someone wishing to count, and thinking he would be unable to do so with fewer things, should make them more numerous and then count them. For the Forms are nearly equal—or not fewer—in number to the things from which, while seeking their causes, they proceeded to the Forms. For corresponding to each thing there is something homonymous, both apart from substances and, among other things, one over many, both in the case of these here and in the case of eternal things. Further, by none of the ways through which we demonstrate that the Forms exist do they appear [to exist]. For from some [arguments] no necessary inference results, while from others Forms result even of things of which we do not think [there are Forms].","kind":"passage","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_DAZY_BOO1:P0056"]},{"id":"RAW_DAZY_BOO1:P0057","text":"For according to the arguments from the sciences, there will be Forms of all things of which there are sciences; and according to the one-over-many, there will be Forms even of negations; and according to thinking of something that has perished, [there will be Forms] of perishables, for there is some phantasm of these. Further, of the more precise arguments, some make Ideas of relatives, though we say that there is no genus of what is by itself; while others state the Third Man.","kind":"passage","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_DAZY_BOO1:P0057"]},{"id":"RAW_DAZY_BOO1:P0058","text":"In general, the arguments concerning the Forms eliminate things which we wish to exist more than the existence of the Ideas. For it follows that the dyad is not first, but number; and that the relative is prior to what is by itself; and all the other things which certain men, following the opinions concerning the Ideas, have held contrary to the principles. Further, according to the supposition by which we say that the Ideas exist, there will be Forms not only of substances but also of many other things (for the conception is one not only concerning substances but also concerning other things, and there are sciences not only of substance but also of other things, and ten thousand other such consequences follow); but according to necessity and the opinions concerning them, if the Forms are participable, there must be Ideas of substances only.","kind":"passage","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_DAZY_BOO1:P0058"]},{"id":"RAW_DAZY_BOO1:P0059","text":"For things do not participate incidentally, but each must participate in that in which it is not predicated of a subject (I mean, for example, if something participates in Double-itself, it also participates in Eternal, but incidentally; for it is incidental to the double to be eternal). Thus the Forms will be substances. But the same things signify substance both here and there; or what will being something apart from these be, the one over many? And if the Ideas and the participants have the same form, there will be something common (for why, in the case of perishable dyads and the many that are eternal, is the dyad one and the same rather than in the case of itself and some particular dyad?). But if they do not have the same form, they will be homonymous, much as if someone called both Callias and a piece of wood “human,” without regarding any community between them. But above all one might be puzzled as to what contribution the Forms make to sensibles, whether to eternal sensibles or to things that come to be and perish. For they are causes neither of motion nor of any change for them.","kind":"passage","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_DAZY_BOO1:P0059"]},{"id":"RAW_DAZY_BOO1:P0060","text":"Nor indeed do they help at all toward knowledge of the other things (for they are not the substance of these things; otherwise they would be in them), nor toward their being, since they are not present in the participants. In that way they might perhaps seem to be causes, as white mixed with white; but this argument is very easily moved, which Anaxagoras first, and Eudoxus later, and certain others, advanced (for it is easy to gather many absurdities against such an opinion). Yet neither do the other things derive from the Forms in any of the customary senses of “from.” And to say that they are paradigms and that the other things participate in them is empty talk and poetic metaphor. For what is it that works while looking to the Ideas? Anything whatever can both be and come to be like [something else] without being likened to that [Idea], so that, whether Socrates exists or does not exist, someone like Socrates could come to be; and similarly, plainly, even if Socrates were eternal.","kind":"passage","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_DAZY_BOO1:P0060"]},{"id":"RAW_DAZY_BOO1:P0061","text":"There will also be several paradigms of the same thing, and hence several Forms; for example, of human being there will be animal and two-footed, and at the same time Human-being-itself. Further, the Forms are paradigms not only of sensibles but also of themselves, as the genus is a genus of Forms; hence the same thing will be paradigm and image.","kind":"passage","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_DAZY_BOO1:P0061"]},{"id":"RAW_DAZY_BOO1:P0062","text":"Again, it would seem impossible for the substance and that of which it is the substance to exist separately; so how could the Ideas, being substances of things, exist separately? In the Phaedo it is stated thus, that the Forms are causes both of being and of coming to be; yet, although the Forms exist, the things which participate in them do not come to be unless there is that which causes motion, and many other things come to be, such as a house and a ring, of which we say that there are no Forms. Hence it is clear that the other things also can both be and come to be through such causes as those now mentioned.—Again, if the Forms are numbers, how will they be causes? Is it because the things that are are other numbers—for example, this number is man, this is Socrates, and this Callias? Why then are the former causes of the latter? For even if the former are eternal and the latter are not, it will make no difference. But if it is because the things here are ratios of numbers, as a concord is, it is clear that there is some one thing of which they are ratios. And if this is something, namely matter, it is evident that the numbers themselves too will be certain ratios of one thing to another.","kind":"passage","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_DAZY_BOO1:P0062"]},{"id":"RAW_DAZY_BOO1:P0063","text":"I mean, for example, if Callias is a ratio in numbers of fire, earth, water, and air, the Idea too will be a number of certain other underlying things; and Man-itself, whether it is some number or not, will nevertheless be a ratio in numbers of certain things, and not a number, nor will anything be a number on account of these things. Again, from many numbers one number comes to be; but how can one Form come to be from Forms? And if it is not from them but from the units in the number, as in ten thousand, how are the units related? For if they are of the same kind, many absurdities will result; and if they are not of the same kind, neither will they themselves be alike to one another nor will all the others be alike to all. For in what will they differ, if they are without affections? These things are neither reasonable nor consonant with thought. Again, it is necessary to construct another kind of number, with which arithmetic is concerned, and all the things called intermediates by some people: from what principles are these, or out of what? Or why will they be intermediate between the things here and the Forms themselves? Again, each of the units in the dyad is from some prior dyad; yet this is impossible.","kind":"passage","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_DAZY_BOO1:P0063"]},{"id":"RAW_DAZY_BOO1:P0064","text":"Again, why is a number, when taken together, one? Again, in addition to what has been said, if the units are different, they ought to speak in the same way as those who posit four or two elements. For each of these too does not call the common thing, such as body, an element, but fire and earth, whether there is some common thing, body, or not. But now the One is spoken of as though it were something homogeneous, like fire or water. If this is so, numbers will not be substances; but it is clear that, if there is some One itself and this is a principle, “one” is said in several ways; for otherwise it is impossible.—But, wishing to reduce substances to principles, we posit lengths from short and long, from a certain small and great, and a plane from broad and narrow, and a solid from deep and shallow.","kind":"passage","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_DAZY_BOO1:P0064"]},{"id":"RAW_DAZY_BOO1:P0065","text":"Yet how will either the plane contain a line, or the solid a line and a plane? For broad and narrow, and deep and shallow, are another genus. Therefore, just as number is not present in them, because much and little are different from these, it is clear that none of the other higher things will be present in the lower. But neither is broad a genus of deep; for body would then be some plane. Again, from what will points be present? Plato indeed disputed against this genus as being a geometrical doctrine, but he called the principle of a line—this he repeatedly posited—indivisible lines.","kind":"passage","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_DAZY_BOO1:P0065"]},{"id":"RAW_DAZY_BOO1:P0066","text":"Yet there must be some limit of these; hence by the same argument by which there is a line, there is also a point.—But, in general, while wisdom seeks the cause concerning the things that are manifest, we have passed this over (for we say nothing concerning the cause from which the principle of change comes); and, thinking that we are stating their substance, we say that there are other substances, but we speak emptily as to how those are the substances of these. For participation, as we said before, is nothing. Nor do the Forms touch at all upon that which we see to be a cause in the sciences, for the sake of which both every intellect and all nature acts—nor upon this cause, which we say is one of the principles; rather, philosophy has become mathematics for the present people, although they say that mathematics must be cultivated for the sake of other things.","kind":"passage","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_DAZY_BOO1:P0066"]},{"id":"RAW_DAZY_BOO1:P0067","text":"Again, one might suppose that the underlying substance, as matter, is rather mathematical, and that it is predicated of substance and matter, and is a difference of them rather than matter—for example, the great and the small—just as the natural philosophers also say of rare and dense, asserting that these are the primary differences of the underlying subject. For these are a kind of excess and deficiency. And concerning motion: if these will be motion, it is clear that the Forms will be moved; but if not, whence did motion come? For the whole inquiry concerning nature is abolished. And what seems easy, namely to show that all things are one, does not come about; for by the exposition all things do not become one, but some one thing itself, if one grants everything; and not even this, unless one grants that the universal is a genus; but in some cases this is impossible.","kind":"passage","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_DAZY_BOO1:P0067"]},{"id":"RAW_DAZY_BOO1:P0068","text":"Nor is there any account of the lengths, planes, and solids after the numbers: neither how they are or will be, nor what power they possess. For these can be neither Forms (for they are not numbers), nor the intermediates (for those are mathematical), nor perishable things; rather, this appears again to be a fourth different genus. In general, to seek the elements of beings without having distinguished them, though they are spoken of in many ways, is impossible; especially when one seeks in this manner the elements of which they consist. For from what elements acting, being acted upon, or straightness consist, it is surely not possible to discover; but, if at all, it is possible only in the case of substances. Hence it is not true either to seek or to think one has the elements of all beings. And how could anyone even learn the elements of all things? For clearly one cannot previously possess any knowledge of them.","kind":"passage","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_DAZY_BOO1:P0068"]},{"id":"RAW_DAZY_BOO1:P0069","text":"For just as one who is learning geometry can know other things beforehand, but has no previous knowledge of the things of which the science is concerned and which he is about to learn, so too in the case of the other sciences; hence, if there is a science of all things, such as some assert, this person would have no prior knowledge at all. Yet every learning is through things previously known, either all or some, both learning by demonstration and learning by definitions (for the things from which a definition is composed must be previously known and familiar); and likewise learning by induction. But indeed, even if it should happen to be innate, it is strange that we are unaware that we possess the most authoritative of the sciences. Again, how will one know from what things it consists, and how will this be clear? For this too presents a difficulty. One might dispute about it as about certain syllables: some say that “za” consists of s, d, and a, while others say that it is another sound and none of the familiar ones.","kind":"passage","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_DAZY_BOO1:P0069"]},{"id":"RAW_DAZY_BOO1:P0070","text":"Again, how could anyone know things of which there is perception without possessing the perception? Yet this ought to be possible, if indeed all things have the same elements from which they consist, just as compound sounds consist of their proper elements.","kind":"passage","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_DAZY_BOO1:P0070"]},{"id":"RAW_DAZY_BOO1:P0071","text":"That all seem to seek the causes stated in the Physics, and that we could name no cause apart from these, is clear also from what has been said before; but they have stated these obscurely, and in one way all have been stated before, but in another way not at all. For first philosophy seems to stammer concerning all things, since it is young and at its beginning. For Empedocles too says that bone exists by its ratio; and this is the what-it-was-to-be and the substance of the thing. But it is equally necessary that flesh and each of the other things should be their ratio, or else not one of them should be. For it is because of this that flesh and bone and each of the other things will be, and not because of the matter which he speaks of—fire, earth, water, and air. But if another had said these things, he would necessarily have agreed; yet he has not stated them clearly.","kind":"passage","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_DAZY_BOO1:P0071"]},{"id":"RAW_DAZY_BOO1:P0072","text":"What concerns these matters has been made clear before; but let us return again to the difficulties that one might raise concerning these same things. For perhaps from them we may obtain some resource for the difficulties that follow.","kind":"passage","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_DAZY_BOO1:P0072"]}],"source_digest":"2ae056b9081da7847cbe02d69171d70f0d916edfa4fd07b2f25df1877ef2713f","source_language_code":"grc","original_title":"Book 1"}],"passage_basis":"frozen-grounding","original_title":"Book 1","title_language_code":"en"},{"id":"RAW_DAZY_BOO2","title":"Book 2","order":2,"chapter_id":"CH_DAZY_BOO2","text":"ἡ περὶ τῆς ἀληθείας θεωρία τῇ μὲν χαλεπὴ τῇ δὲ ῥᾳδία. σημεῖον δὲ τὸ μήτʼ ἀξίως μηδένα δύνασθαι θιγεῖν αὐτῆς μήτε πάντας ἀποτυγχάνειν,  ἀλλʼ ἕκαστον λέγειν τι περὶ τῆς φύσεως, καὶ καθʼ ἕνα μὲν ἢ μηθὲν ἢ μικρὸν ἐπιβάλλειν αὐτῇ, ἐκ πάντων δὲ συναθροιζομένων γίγνεσθαί τι μέγεθος· ὥστʼ εἴπερ ἔοικεν ἔχειν καθάπερ τυγχάνομεν παροιμιαζόμενοι, τίς ἂν θύρας ἁμάρτοι; ταύτῃ μὲν ἂν εἴη ῥᾳδία, τὸ δʼ ὅλον τι ἔχειν καὶ μέρος μὴ δύνασθαι δηλοῖ τὸ χαλεπὸν αὐτῆς. ἴσως δὲ καὶ τῆς χαλεπότητος οὔσης κατὰ δύο τρόπους, οὐκ ἐν τοῖς πράγμασιν ἀλλʼ ἐν ἡμῖν τὸ αἴτιον αὐτῆς· ὥσπερ γὰρ τὰ τῶν νυκτερίδων ὄμματα πρὸς τὸ φέγγος ἔχει τὸ μεθʼ ἡμέραν, οὕτω καὶ τῆς ἡμετέρας ψυχῆς ὁ νοῦς πρὸς τὰ τῇ φύσει φανερώτατα πάντων. οὐ μόνον δὲ χάριν ἔχειν δίκαιον τούτοις ὧν ἄν τις κοινώσαιτο ταῖς δόξαις, ἀλλὰ καὶ τοῖς ἐπιπολαιότερον ἀποφηναμένοις· καὶ γὰρ οὗτοι συνεβάλοντό τι· τὴν γὰρ ἕξιν προήσκησαν ἡμῶν· εἰ μὲν γὰρ Τιμόθεος μὴ ἐγένετο, πολλὴν ἂν μελοποιίαν οὐκ εἴχομεν· εἰ δὲ μὴ Φρῦνις, Τιμόθεος οὐκ ἂν ἐγένετο. τὸν αὐτὸν δὲ τρόπον καὶ ἐπὶ τῶν περὶ τῆς ἀληθείας ἀποφηναμένων· παρὰ μὲν γὰρ ἐνίων παρειλήφαμέν τινας δόξας, οἱ δὲ τοῦ γενέσθαι τούτους αἴτιοι γεγόνασιν. ὀρθῶς δʼ ἔχει καὶ τὸ καλεῖσθαι τὴν φιλοσοφίαν ἐπιστήμην τῆς ἀληθείας. θεωρητικῆς μὲν γὰρ τέλος ἀλήθεια πρακτικῆς δʼ ἔργον· καὶ γὰρ ἂν τὸ πῶς ἔχει σκοπῶσιν, οὐ τὸ ἀΐδιον ἀλλʼ ὃ πρός τι καὶ νῦν θεωροῦσιν οἱ πρακτικοί. οὐκ ἴσμεν δὲ τὸ ἀληθὲς ἄνευ τῆς αἰτίας· ἕκαστον δὲ μάλιστα αὐτὸ τῶν ἄλλων καθʼ ὃ καὶ τοῖς ἄλλοις ὑπάρχει τὸ συνώνυμον (οἷον τὸ πῦρ θερμότατον· καὶ γὰρ τοῖς ἄλλοις τὸ αἴτιον τοῦτο τῆς θερμότητος)· ὥστε καὶ ἀληθέστατον τὸ τοῖς ὑστέροις αἴτιον τοῦ ἀληθέσιν εἶναι. διὸ τὰς τῶν ἀεὶ ὄντων ἀρχὰς ἀναγκαῖον ἀεὶ εἶναι ἀληθεστάτας (οὐ γάρ ποτε ἀληθεῖς, οὐδʼ ἐκείναις αἴτιόν τί ἐστι τοῦ εἶναι, ἀλλʼ ἐκεῖναι τοῖς ἄλλοις) , ὥσθʼ ἕκαστον ὡς ἔχει τοῦ εἶναι, οὕτω καὶ τῆς ἀληθείας. \n\n\n\n \n\n \n\n ἀλλὰ μὴν ὅτι γʼ ἔστιν ἀρχή τις καὶ οὐκ ἄπειρα τὰ αἴτια τῶν ὄντων οὔτʼ εἰς εὐθυωρίαν οὔτε κατʼ εἶδος, δῆλον. οὔτε γὰρ ὡς ἐξ ὕλης τόδʼ ἐκ τοῦδε δυνατὸν ἰέναι εἰς ἄπειρον (οἷον σάρκα μὲν ἐκ γῆς, γῆν δʼ ἐξ ἀέρος, ἀέρα δʼ ἐκ πυρός, καὶ τοῦτο μὴ ἵστασθαι), οὔτε ὅθεν ἡ ἀρχὴ τῆς κινήσεως (οἷον τὸν μὲν ἄνθρωπον ὑπὸ τοῦ ἀέρος κινηθῆναι, τοῦτον δʼ ὑπὸ τοῦ ἡλίου, τὸν δὲ ἥλιον ὑπὸ τοῦ νείκους, καὶ τούτου μηδὲν εἶναι πέρας)· ὁμοίως δὲ οὐδὲ τὸ οὗ ἕνεκα εἰς ἄπειρον οἷόν τε ἰέναι, βάδισιν μὲν ὑγιείας ἕνεκα, ταύτην δʼ εὐδαιμονίας, τὴν δʼ εὐδαιμονίαν ἄλλου, καὶ οὕτως ἀεὶ ἄλλο ἄλλου ἕνεκεν εἶναι· καὶ ἐπὶ τοῦ τί ἦν εἶναι δʼ ὡσαύτως. τῶν γὰρ μέσων, ὧν ἐστί τι ἔσχατον καὶ πρότερον, ἀναγκαῖον εἶναι τὸ πρότερον αἴτιον τῶν μετʼ αὐτό. εἰ γὰρ εἰπεῖν ἡμᾶς δέοι τί τῶν τριῶν αἴτιον, τὸ πρῶτον ἐροῦμεν· οὐ γὰρ δὴ τό γʼ ἔσχατον, οὐδενὸς γὰρ τὸ τελευταῖον· ἀλλὰ μὴν οὐδὲ τὸ μέσον, ἑνὸς γάρ (οὐθὲν δὲ διαφέρει ἓν ἢ πλείω εἶναι, οὐδʼ ἄπειρα ἢ πεπερασμένα). τῶν δʼ ἀπείρων τοῦτον τὸν τρόπον καὶ ὅλως τοῦ ἀπείρου πάντα τὰ μόρια μέσα ὁμοίως μέχρι τοῦ νῦν· ὥστʼ εἴπερ μηδέν ἐστι πρῶτον, ὅλως αἴτιον οὐδέν ἐστιν.—ἀλλὰ μὴν οὐδʼ ἐπὶ τὸ κάτω οἷόν τε εἰς ἄπειρον ἰέναι, τοῦ ἄνω ἔχοντος ἀρχήν, ὥστʼ ἐκ πυρὸς μὲν ὕδωρ, ἐκ δὲ τούτου γῆν, καὶ οὕτως ἀεὶ ἄλλο τι γίγνεσθαι γένος. διχῶς γὰρ γίγνεται τόδε ἐκ τοῦδε—μὴ ὡς τόδε λέγεται μετὰ τόδε, οἷον ἐξ Ἰσθμίων Ὀλύμπια, ἀλλʼ ἢ ὡς ἐκ παιδὸς ἀνὴρ μεταβάλλοντος ἢ ὡς ἐξ ὕδατος ἀήρ. ὡς μὲν οὖν ἐκ παιδὸς ἄνδρα γίγνεσθαί φαμεν, ὡς ἐκ τοῦ γιγνομένου τὸ γεγονὸς ἢ ἐκ τοῦ ἐπιτελουμένου τὸ τετελεσμένον (ἀεὶ γάρ ἐστι μεταξύ, ὥσπερ τοῦ εἶναι καὶ μὴ εἶναι γένεσις, οὕτω καὶ τὸ γιγνόμενον τοῦ ὄντος καὶ μὴ ὄντος· ἔστι γὰρ ὁ μανθάνων γιγνόμενος ἐπιστήμων, καὶ τοῦτʼ ἐστὶν ὃ λέγεται, ὅτι γίγνεται ἐκ μανθάνοντος ἐπιστήμων)· τὸ δʼ ὡς ἐξ ἀέρος ὕδωρ, φθειρομένου θατέρου. διὸ ἐκεῖνα μὲν οὐκ ἀνακάμπτει εἰς ἄλληλα,  οὐδὲ γίγνεται ἐξ ἀνδρὸς παῖς (οὐ γὰρ γίγνεται ἐκ τῆς γενέσεως τὸ γιγνόμενον ἀλλʼ ὃ ἔστι μετὰ τὴν γένεσιν· οὕτω γὰρ καὶ ἡμέρα ἐκ τοῦ πρωΐ, ὅτι μετὰ τοῦτο· διὸ οὐδὲ τὸ πρωῒ ἐξ ἡμέρας)· θάτερα δὲ ἀνακάμπτει. ἀμφοτέρως δὲ ἀδύνατον εἰς ἄπειρον ἰέναι· τῶν μὲν γὰρ ὄντων μεταξὺ ἀνάγκη τέλος εἶναι, τὰ δʼ εἰς ἄλληλα ἀνακάμπτει· ἡ γὰρ θατέρου φθορὰ θατέρου ἐστὶ γένεσις.—ἅμα δὲ καὶ ἀδύνατον τὸ πρῶτον ἀΐδιον ὂν φθαρῆναι· ἐπεὶ γὰρ οὐκ ἄπειρος ἡ γένεσις ἐπὶ τὸ ἄνω, ἀνάγκη ἐξ οὗ φθαρέντος πρώτου τι ἐγένετο μὴ ἀΐδιον εἶναι. ἔτι δὲ τὸ οὗ ἕνεκα τέλος, τοιοῦτον δὲ ὃ μὴ ἄλλου ἕνεκα ἀλλὰ τἆλλα ἐκείνου, ὥστʼ εἰ μὲν ἔσται τοιοῦτόν τι ἔσχατον, οὐκ ἔσται ἄπειρον, εἰ δὲ μηθὲν τοιοῦτον, οὐκ ἔσται τὸ οὗ ἕνεκα, ἀλλʼ οἱ τὸ ἄπειρον ποιοῦντες λανθάνουσιν ἐξαιροῦντες τὴν τοῦ ἀγαθοῦ φύσιν (καίτοι οὐθεὶς ἂν ἐγχειρήσειεν οὐδὲν πράττειν μὴ μέλλων ἐπὶ πέρας ἥξειν)· οὐδʼ ἂν εἴη νοῦς ἐν τοῖς οὖσιν· ἕνεκα γάρ τινος ἀεὶ πράττει ὅ γε νοῦν ἔχων, τοῦτο δέ ἐστι πέρας· τὸ γὰρ τέλος πέρας ἐστίν. ἀλλὰ μὴν οὐδὲ τὸ τί ἦν εἶναι ἐνδέχεται ἀνάγεσθαι εἰς ἄλλον ὁρισμὸν πλεονάζοντα τῷ λόγῳ· ἀεί τε γὰρ ἔστιν ὁ ἔμπροσθεν μᾶλλον, ὁ δʼ ὕστερος οὐκ ἔστιν, οὗ δὲ τὸ πρῶτον μὴ ἔστιν, οὐδὲ τὸ ἐχόμενον· ἔτι τὸ ἐπίστασθαι ἀναιροῦσιν οἱ οὕτως λέγοντες, οὐ γὰρ οἷόν τε εἰδέναι πρὶν εἰς τὰ ἄτομα ἐλθεῖν· καὶ τὸ γιγνώσκειν οὐκ ἔστιν, τὰ γὰρ οὕτως ἄπειρα πῶς ἐνδέχεται νοεῖν; οὐ γὰρ ὅμοιον ἐπὶ τῆς γραμμῆς, ἣ κατὰ τὰς διαιρέσεις μὲν οὐχ ἵσταται, νοῆσαι δʼ οὐκ ἔστι μὴ στήσαντα (διόπερ οὐκ ἀριθμήσει τὰς τομὰς ὁ τὴν ἄπειρον διεξιών), ἀλλὰ καὶ τὴν ὅλην οὐ κινουμένῳ νοεῖν ἀνάγκη. καὶ ἀπείρῳ οὐδενὶ ἔστιν εἶναι· εἰ δὲ μή, οὐκ ἄπειρόν γʼ ἐστὶ τὸ ἀπείρῳ εἶναι.—ἀλλὰ μὴν καὶ εἰ ἄπειρά γʼ ἦσαν πλήθει τὰ εἴδη τῶν αἰτίων, οὐκ ἂν ἦν οὐδʼ οὕτω τὸ γιγνώσκειν· τότε γὰρ εἰδέναι οἰόμεθα ὅταν τὰ αἴτια γνωρίσωμεν· τὸ δʼ ἄπειρον κατὰ τὴν πρόσθεσιν οὐκ ἔστιν ἐν πεπερασμένῳ διεξελθεῖν. \n\n\n\n \n\n \n\nαἱ δʼ ἀκροάσεις κατὰ τὰ ἔθη συμβαίνουσιν· ὡς γὰρ εἰώθαμεν οὕτως ἀξιοῦμεν λέγεσθαι,  καὶ τὰ παρὰ ταῦτα οὐχ ὅμοια φαίνεται ἀλλὰ διὰ τὴν ἀσυνήθειαν ἀγνωστότερα καὶ ξενικώτερα· τὸ γὰρ σύνηθες γνώριμον. ἡλίκην δὲ ἰσχὺν ἔχει τὸ σύνηθες οἱ νόμοι δηλοῦσιν, ἐν οἷς τὰ μυθώδη καὶ παιδαριώδη μεῖζον ἰσχύει τοῦ γινώσκειν περὶ αὐτῶν διὰ τὸ ἔθος. οἱ μὲν οὖν ἐὰν μὴ μαθηματικῶς λέγῃ τις οὐκ ἀποδέχονται τῶν λεγόντων, οἱ δʼ ἂν μὴ παραδειγματικῶς, οἱ δὲ μάρτυρα ἀξιοῦσιν ἐπάγεσθαι ποιητήν. καὶ οἱ μὲν πάντα ἀκριβῶς, τοὺς δὲ λυπεῖ τὸ ἀκριβὲς ἢ διὰ τὸ μὴ δύνασθαι συνείρειν ἢ διὰ τὴν μικρολογίαν· ἔχει γάρ τι τὸ ἀκριβὲς τοιοῦτον, ὥστε, καθάπερ ἐπὶ τῶν συμβολαίων, καὶ ἐπὶ τῶν λόγων ἀνελεύθερον εἶναί τισι δοκεῖ. διὸ δεῖ πεπαιδεῦσθαι πῶς ἕκαστα ἀποδεκτέον, ὡς ἄτοπον ἅμα ζητεῖν ἐπιστήμην καὶ τρόπον ἐπιστήμης· ἔστι δʼ οὐδὲ θάτερον ῥᾴδιον λαβεῖν. τὴν δʼ ἀκριβολογίαν τὴν μαθηματικὴν οὐκ ἐν ἅπασιν ἀπαιτητέον, ἀλλʼ ἐν τοῖς μὴ ἔχουσιν ὕλην. διόπερ οὐ φυσικὸς ὁ τρόπος· ἅπασα γὰρ ἴσως ἡ φύσις ἔχει ὕλην. διὸ σκεπτέον πρῶτον τί ἐστιν ἡ φύσις· οὕτω γὰρ καὶ περὶ τίνων ἡ φυσικὴ δῆλον ἔσται .","paragraphs":[{"id":"RAW_DAZY_BOO2:P0001","text":"ἡ περὶ τῆς ἀληθείας θεωρία τῇ μὲν χαλεπὴ τῇ δὲ ῥᾳδία. σημεῖον δὲ τὸ μήτʼ ἀξίως μηδένα δύνασθαι θιγεῖν αὐτῆς μήτε πάντας ἀποτυγχάνειν,  ἀλλʼ ἕκαστον λέγειν τι περὶ τῆς φύσεως, καὶ καθʼ ἕνα μὲν ἢ μηθὲν ἢ μικρὸν ἐπιβάλλειν αὐτῇ, ἐκ πάντων δὲ συναθροιζομένων γίγνεσθαί τι μέγεθος· ὥστʼ εἴπερ ἔοικεν ἔχειν καθάπερ τυγχάνομεν παροιμιαζόμενοι, τίς ἂν θύρας ἁμάρτοι; ταύτῃ μὲν ἂν εἴη ῥᾳδία, τὸ δʼ ὅλον τι ἔχειν καὶ μέρος μὴ δύνασθαι δηλοῖ τὸ χαλεπὸν αὐτῆς. ἴσως δὲ καὶ τῆς χαλεπότητος οὔσης κατὰ δύο τρόπους, οὐκ ἐν τοῖς πράγμασιν ἀλλʼ ἐν ἡμῖν τὸ αἴτιον αὐτῆς· ὥσπερ γὰρ τὰ τῶν νυκτερίδων ὄμματα πρὸς τὸ φέγγος ἔχει τὸ μεθʼ ἡμέραν, οὕτω καὶ τῆς ἡμετέρας ψυχῆς ὁ νοῦς πρὸς τὰ τῇ φύσει φανερώτατα πάντων.","kind":"passage"},{"id":"RAW_DAZY_BOO2:P0002","text":"οὐ μόνον δὲ χάριν ἔχειν δίκαιον τούτοις ὧν ἄν τις κοινώσαιτο ταῖς δόξαις, ἀλλὰ καὶ τοῖς ἐπιπολαιότερον ἀποφηναμένοις· καὶ γὰρ οὗτοι συνεβάλοντό τι· τὴν γὰρ ἕξιν προήσκησαν ἡμῶν· εἰ μὲν γὰρ Τιμόθεος μὴ ἐγένετο, πολλὴν ἂν μελοποιίαν οὐκ εἴχομεν· εἰ δὲ μὴ Φρῦνις, Τιμόθεος οὐκ ἂν ἐγένετο. τὸν αὐτὸν δὲ τρόπον καὶ ἐπὶ τῶν περὶ τῆς ἀληθείας ἀποφηναμένων· παρὰ μὲν γὰρ ἐνίων παρειλήφαμέν τινας δόξας, οἱ δὲ τοῦ γενέσθαι τούτους αἴτιοι γεγόνασιν. ὀρθῶς δʼ ἔχει καὶ τὸ καλεῖσθαι τὴν φιλοσοφίαν ἐπιστήμην τῆς ἀληθείας. θεωρητικῆς μὲν γὰρ τέλος ἀλήθεια πρακτικῆς δʼ ἔργον· καὶ γὰρ ἂν τὸ πῶς ἔχει σκοπῶσιν, οὐ τὸ ἀΐδιον ἀλλʼ ὃ πρός τι καὶ νῦν θεωροῦσιν οἱ πρακτικοί.","kind":"passage"},{"id":"RAW_DAZY_BOO2:P0003","text":"οὐκ ἴσμεν δὲ τὸ ἀληθὲς ἄνευ τῆς αἰτίας· ἕκαστον δὲ μάλιστα αὐτὸ τῶν ἄλλων καθʼ ὃ καὶ τοῖς ἄλλοις ὑπάρχει τὸ συνώνυμον (οἷον τὸ πῦρ θερμότατον· καὶ γὰρ τοῖς ἄλλοις τὸ αἴτιον τοῦτο τῆς θερμότητος)· ὥστε καὶ ἀληθέστατον τὸ τοῖς ὑστέροις αἴτιον τοῦ ἀληθέσιν εἶναι. διὸ τὰς τῶν ἀεὶ ὄντων ἀρχὰς ἀναγκαῖον ἀεὶ εἶναι ἀληθεστάτας (οὐ γάρ ποτε ἀληθεῖς, οὐδʼ ἐκείναις αἴτιόν τί ἐστι τοῦ εἶναι, ἀλλʼ ἐκεῖναι τοῖς ἄλλοις) , ὥσθʼ ἕκαστον ὡς ἔχει τοῦ εἶναι, οὕτω καὶ τῆς ἀληθείας.","kind":"passage"},{"id":"RAW_DAZY_BOO2:P0004","text":"ἀλλὰ μὴν ὅτι γʼ ἔστιν ἀρχή τις καὶ οὐκ ἄπειρα τὰ αἴτια τῶν ὄντων οὔτʼ εἰς εὐθυωρίαν οὔτε κατʼ εἶδος, δῆλον. οὔτε γὰρ ὡς ἐξ ὕλης τόδʼ ἐκ τοῦδε δυνατὸν ἰέναι εἰς ἄπειρον (οἷον σάρκα μὲν ἐκ γῆς, γῆν δʼ ἐξ ἀέρος, ἀέρα δʼ ἐκ πυρός, καὶ τοῦτο μὴ ἵστασθαι), οὔτε ὅθεν ἡ ἀρχὴ τῆς κινήσεως (οἷον τὸν μὲν ἄνθρωπον ὑπὸ τοῦ ἀέρος κινηθῆναι, τοῦτον δʼ ὑπὸ τοῦ ἡλίου, τὸν δὲ ἥλιον ὑπὸ τοῦ νείκους, καὶ τούτου μηδὲν εἶναι πέρας)· ὁμοίως δὲ οὐδὲ τὸ οὗ ἕνεκα εἰς ἄπειρον οἷόν τε ἰέναι, βάδισιν μὲν ὑγιείας ἕνεκα, ταύτην δʼ εὐδαιμονίας, τὴν δʼ εὐδαιμονίαν ἄλλου, καὶ οὕτως ἀεὶ ἄλλο ἄλλου ἕνεκεν εἶναι· καὶ ἐπὶ τοῦ τί ἦν εἶναι δʼ ὡσαύτως. τῶν γὰρ μέσων, ὧν ἐστί τι ἔσχατον καὶ πρότερον, ἀναγκαῖον εἶναι τὸ πρότερον αἴτιον τῶν μετʼ αὐτό.","kind":"passage"},{"id":"RAW_DAZY_BOO2:P0005","text":"εἰ γὰρ εἰπεῖν ἡμᾶς δέοι τί τῶν τριῶν αἴτιον, τὸ πρῶτον ἐροῦμεν· οὐ γὰρ δὴ τό γʼ ἔσχατον, οὐδενὸς γὰρ τὸ τελευταῖον· ἀλλὰ μὴν οὐδὲ τὸ μέσον, ἑνὸς γάρ (οὐθὲν δὲ διαφέρει ἓν ἢ πλείω εἶναι, οὐδʼ ἄπειρα ἢ πεπερασμένα). τῶν δʼ ἀπείρων τοῦτον τὸν τρόπον καὶ ὅλως τοῦ ἀπείρου πάντα τὰ μόρια μέσα ὁμοίως μέχρι τοῦ νῦν· ὥστʼ εἴπερ μηδέν ἐστι πρῶτον, ὅλως αἴτιον οὐδέν ἐστιν.—ἀλλὰ μὴν οὐδʼ ἐπὶ τὸ κάτω οἷόν τε εἰς ἄπειρον ἰέναι, τοῦ ἄνω ἔχοντος ἀρχήν, ὥστʼ ἐκ πυρὸς μὲν ὕδωρ, ἐκ δὲ τούτου γῆν, καὶ οὕτως ἀεὶ ἄλλο τι γίγνεσθαι γένος. διχῶς γὰρ γίγνεται τόδε ἐκ τοῦδε—μὴ ὡς τόδε λέγεται μετὰ τόδε, οἷον ἐξ Ἰσθμίων Ὀλύμπια, ἀλλʼ ἢ ὡς ἐκ παιδὸς ἀνὴρ μεταβάλλοντος ἢ ὡς ἐξ ὕδατος ἀήρ.","kind":"passage"},{"id":"RAW_DAZY_BOO2:P0006","text":"ὡς μὲν οὖν ἐκ παιδὸς ἄνδρα γίγνεσθαί φαμεν, ὡς ἐκ τοῦ γιγνομένου τὸ γεγονὸς ἢ ἐκ τοῦ ἐπιτελουμένου τὸ τετελεσμένον (ἀεὶ γάρ ἐστι μεταξύ, ὥσπερ τοῦ εἶναι καὶ μὴ εἶναι γένεσις, οὕτω καὶ τὸ γιγνόμενον τοῦ ὄντος καὶ μὴ ὄντος· ἔστι γὰρ ὁ μανθάνων γιγνόμενος ἐπιστήμων, καὶ τοῦτʼ ἐστὶν ὃ λέγεται, ὅτι γίγνεται ἐκ μανθάνοντος ἐπιστήμων)· τὸ δʼ ὡς ἐξ ἀέρος ὕδωρ, φθειρομένου θατέρου. διὸ ἐκεῖνα μὲν οὐκ ἀνακάμπτει εἰς ἄλληλα,  οὐδὲ γίγνεται ἐξ ἀνδρὸς παῖς (οὐ γὰρ γίγνεται ἐκ τῆς γενέσεως τὸ γιγνόμενον ἀλλʼ ὃ ἔστι μετὰ τὴν γένεσιν· οὕτω γὰρ καὶ ἡμέρα ἐκ τοῦ πρωΐ, ὅτι μετὰ τοῦτο· διὸ οὐδὲ τὸ πρωῒ ἐξ ἡμέρας)· θάτερα δὲ ἀνακάμπτει.","kind":"passage"},{"id":"RAW_DAZY_BOO2:P0007","text":"ἀμφοτέρως δὲ ἀδύνατον εἰς ἄπειρον ἰέναι· τῶν μὲν γὰρ ὄντων μεταξὺ ἀνάγκη τέλος εἶναι, τὰ δʼ εἰς ἄλληλα ἀνακάμπτει· ἡ γὰρ θατέρου φθορὰ θατέρου ἐστὶ γένεσις.—ἅμα δὲ καὶ ἀδύνατον τὸ πρῶτον ἀΐδιον ὂν φθαρῆναι· ἐπεὶ γὰρ οὐκ ἄπειρος ἡ γένεσις ἐπὶ τὸ ἄνω, ἀνάγκη ἐξ οὗ φθαρέντος πρώτου τι ἐγένετο μὴ ἀΐδιον εἶναι. ἔτι δὲ τὸ οὗ ἕνεκα τέλος, τοιοῦτον δὲ ὃ μὴ ἄλλου ἕνεκα ἀλλὰ τἆλλα ἐκείνου, ὥστʼ εἰ μὲν ἔσται τοιοῦτόν τι ἔσχατον, οὐκ ἔσται ἄπειρον, εἰ δὲ μηθὲν τοιοῦτον, οὐκ ἔσται τὸ οὗ ἕνεκα, ἀλλʼ οἱ τὸ ἄπειρον ποιοῦντες λανθάνουσιν ἐξαιροῦντες τὴν τοῦ ἀγαθοῦ φύσιν (καίτοι οὐθεὶς ἂν ἐγχειρήσειεν οὐδὲν πράττειν μὴ μέλλων ἐπὶ πέρας ἥξειν)· οὐδʼ ἂν εἴη νοῦς ἐν τοῖς οὖσιν· ἕνεκα γάρ τινος ἀεὶ πράττει ὅ γε νοῦν ἔχων, τοῦτο δέ ἐστι πέρας· τὸ γὰρ τέλος πέρας ἐστίν.","kind":"passage"},{"id":"RAW_DAZY_BOO2:P0008","text":"ἀλλὰ μὴν οὐδὲ τὸ τί ἦν εἶναι ἐνδέχεται ἀνάγεσθαι εἰς ἄλλον ὁρισμὸν πλεονάζοντα τῷ λόγῳ· ἀεί τε γὰρ ἔστιν ὁ ἔμπροσθεν μᾶλλον, ὁ δʼ ὕστερος οὐκ ἔστιν, οὗ δὲ τὸ πρῶτον μὴ ἔστιν, οὐδὲ τὸ ἐχόμενον· ἔτι τὸ ἐπίστασθαι ἀναιροῦσιν οἱ οὕτως λέγοντες, οὐ γὰρ οἷόν τε εἰδέναι πρὶν εἰς τὰ ἄτομα ἐλθεῖν· καὶ τὸ γιγνώσκειν οὐκ ἔστιν, τὰ γὰρ οὕτως ἄπειρα πῶς ἐνδέχεται νοεῖν; οὐ γὰρ ὅμοιον ἐπὶ τῆς γραμμῆς, ἣ κατὰ τὰς διαιρέσεις μὲν οὐχ ἵσταται, νοῆσαι δʼ οὐκ ἔστι μὴ στήσαντα (διόπερ οὐκ ἀριθμήσει τὰς τομὰς ὁ τὴν ἄπειρον διεξιών), ἀλλὰ καὶ τὴν ὅλην οὐ κινουμένῳ νοεῖν ἀνάγκη. καὶ ἀπείρῳ οὐδενὶ ἔστιν εἶναι· εἰ δὲ μή, οὐκ ἄπειρόν γʼ ἐστὶ τὸ ἀπείρῳ εἶναι.—ἀλλὰ μὴν καὶ εἰ ἄπειρά γʼ ἦσαν πλήθει τὰ εἴδη τῶν αἰτίων, οὐκ ἂν ἦν οὐδʼ οὕτω τὸ γιγνώσκειν· τότε γὰρ εἰδέναι οἰόμεθα ὅταν τὰ αἴτια γνωρίσωμεν· τὸ δʼ ἄπειρον κατὰ τὴν πρόσθεσιν οὐκ ἔστιν ἐν πεπερασμένῳ διεξελθεῖν.","kind":"passage"},{"id":"RAW_DAZY_BOO2:P0009","text":"αἱ δʼ ἀκροάσεις κατὰ τὰ ἔθη συμβαίνουσιν· ὡς γὰρ εἰώθαμεν οὕτως ἀξιοῦμεν λέγεσθαι,  καὶ τὰ παρὰ ταῦτα οὐχ ὅμοια φαίνεται ἀλλὰ διὰ τὴν ἀσυνήθειαν ἀγνωστότερα καὶ ξενικώτερα· τὸ γὰρ σύνηθες γνώριμον. ἡλίκην δὲ ἰσχὺν ἔχει τὸ σύνηθες οἱ νόμοι δηλοῦσιν, ἐν οἷς τὰ μυθώδη καὶ παιδαριώδη μεῖζον ἰσχύει τοῦ γινώσκειν περὶ αὐτῶν διὰ τὸ ἔθος. οἱ μὲν οὖν ἐὰν μὴ μαθηματικῶς λέγῃ τις οὐκ ἀποδέχονται τῶν λεγόντων, οἱ δʼ ἂν μὴ παραδειγματικῶς, οἱ δὲ μάρτυρα ἀξιοῦσιν ἐπάγεσθαι ποιητήν. καὶ οἱ μὲν πάντα ἀκριβῶς, τοὺς δὲ λυπεῖ τὸ ἀκριβὲς ἢ διὰ τὸ μὴ δύνασθαι συνείρειν ἢ διὰ τὴν μικρολογίαν· ἔχει γάρ τι τὸ ἀκριβὲς τοιοῦτον, ὥστε, καθάπερ ἐπὶ τῶν συμβολαίων, καὶ ἐπὶ τῶν λόγων ἀνελεύθερον εἶναί τισι δοκεῖ. διὸ δεῖ πεπαιδεῦσθαι πῶς ἕκαστα ἀποδεκτέον, ὡς ἄτοπον ἅμα ζητεῖν ἐπιστήμην καὶ τρόπον ἐπιστήμης· ἔστι δʼ οὐδὲ θάτερον ῥᾴδιον λαβεῖν.","kind":"passage"},{"id":"RAW_DAZY_BOO2:P0010","text":"τὴν δʼ ἀκριβολογίαν τὴν μαθηματικὴν οὐκ ἐν ἅπασιν ἀπαιτητέον, ἀλλʼ ἐν τοῖς μὴ ἔχουσιν ὕλην. διόπερ οὐ φυσικὸς ὁ τρόπος· ἅπασα γὰρ ἴσως ἡ φύσις ἔχει ὕλην. διὸ σκεπτέον πρῶτον τί ἐστιν ἡ φύσις· οὕτω γὰρ καὶ περὶ τίνων ἡ φυσικὴ δῆλον ἔσται .","kind":"passage"}],"sha256":"96799994ae6ff08037ade926ff712ffd2bbfc419d5ffa1c0a81b8848e84dd5d6","language_code":"grc","language_name":"Ἑλληνική","translations":[{"id":"SOURCE_TR_20261015T172128793779Z_ed9f8bfd","language_code":"en","language_name":"English","title":"Book 2","source_sha256":"96799994ae6ff08037ade926ff712ffd2bbfc419d5ffa1c0a81b8848e84dd5d6","sha256":"93f58615bab0a28e4a213c4bc75d320023d081507f69e100c2cc4f74dc99e5aa","rendition_type":"machine","review_status":"machine","model":"gpt-5.6-terra","created":"2026-10-15T17:21:28.793846+00:00","paragraphs":[{"id":"RAW_DAZY_BOO2:P0001","text":"The inquiry concerning truth is in one respect difficult, in another easy. A sign of this is that no one is able to attain it worthily, nor do all fail, but each says something about nature; and individually they contribute either nothing or little to it, but from all gathered together some magnitude results. Thus, if it is as we happen proverbially to say, “Who could miss a door?”, in this respect it would be easy; but the fact that one can have the whole and not be able to have a part indicates its difficulty. Perhaps, too, since the difficulty is in two ways, its cause lies not in the things but in us: for just as the eyes of bats stand toward the light of day, so too the intellect of our soul stands toward the things which are by nature most evident of all.","kind":"passage","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_DAZY_BOO2:P0001"]},{"id":"RAW_DAZY_BOO2:P0002","text":"It is just to be grateful not only to those whose opinions one might share, but also to those who have made pronouncements more superficially; for these too have contributed something: they have exercised our habit of mind beforehand. For if Timotheus had not existed, we should not have had much music; and if Phrynis had not existed, Timotheus would not have existed. The same is true also of those who have made pronouncements concerning truth: from some we have received certain opinions, while others have been causes of the coming-to-be of these men.\n\nAnd it is right that philosophy should be called knowledge of truth. For the end of theoretical knowledge is truth, whereas that of practical knowledge is action; for even if practical men investigate how something stands, they contemplate not the eternal but what is relative to something and now.","kind":"passage","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_DAZY_BOO2:P0002"]},{"id":"RAW_DAZY_BOO2:P0003","text":"But we do not know what is true without its cause; and each thing is most of all that by which the synonymous attribute also belongs to the other things (for example, fire is hottest; for it is also the cause of heat in the other things). Thus what is the cause for subsequent things of being true is also most true. Therefore the principles of things that are always are necessarily always most true (for they are not true at one time only, nor is there some cause for them of being, but they are causes for the other things); so that each thing is as it is in respect of being, so also in respect of truth.","kind":"passage","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_DAZY_BOO2:P0003"]},{"id":"RAW_DAZY_BOO2:P0004","text":"But indeed, that there is some principle, and that the causes of beings are not infinite either in a straight succession or in kind, is evident. For it is not possible, as from matter, for this to proceed from this to infinity—for example, flesh from earth, earth from air, air from fire, and for this not to stop—nor is it possible for the source of motion to proceed thus: for example, for man to be moved by air, this by the sun, the sun by Strife, and for there to be no limit to this. Likewise, it is not possible for the final cause to proceed to infinity: walking for the sake of health, health for the sake of happiness, happiness for the sake of something else, and thus always one thing for the sake of another. The same holds also for the what-it-was-to-be. For among intermediates, where there is some last and prior term, the prior must be the cause of what comes after it.","kind":"passage","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_DAZY_BOO2:P0004"]},{"id":"RAW_DAZY_BOO2:P0005","text":"For if we had to say which of three is the cause, we shall say the first. For surely it is not the last, since the last is the cause of nothing; nor indeed is it the middle, for it is the cause of one thing (and it makes no difference whether it is one or many, or infinite or finite). But in infinities of this kind, and in an infinity generally, all the parts are alike intermediate, down to the present point. Therefore, if there is nothing first, there is no cause at all.—But neither is it possible to proceed to infinity downward, if what is above has a principle, so that from fire comes water, and from this earth, and thus some other kind always comes to be. For this comes to be from that in two ways—not in the sense in which this is said to come after that, as the Olympic games after the Isthmian games, but either as a man from a changing child, or as air from water.","kind":"passage","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_DAZY_BOO2:P0005"]},{"id":"RAW_DAZY_BOO2:P0006","text":"Now when we say that a man comes to be from a child, this is as the completed from what is coming to be, or the finished from what is being completed (for there is always something intermediate; and, just as generation is intermediate between being and not-being, so what is coming to be is intermediate between what is and what is not. For the learner is becoming knowledgeable, and this is what is meant by saying that the knowledgeable comes to be from the learner). But water comes from air as one of the two is perishing. Therefore the former do not turn back into one another, nor does a child come to be from a man (for what is coming to be does not come to be from the coming-to-be, but from what is after the coming-to-be; thus day comes from morning because it is after it; therefore morning does not come from day); but the latter do turn back into one another.","kind":"passage","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_DAZY_BOO2:P0006"]},{"id":"RAW_DAZY_BOO2:P0007","text":"But in both ways it is impossible to proceed to infinity. For among things that are intermediate there must be an end, while the things that turn into one another return; for the perishing of one is the coming-to-be of the other.—At the same time, it is impossible for the first thing, being eternal, to perish. For since generation is not infinite upward, that from whose first perishing something came to be must not be eternal. Further, that for the sake of which is an end; and such is that which is not for the sake of another, but the other things are for its sake. Thus, if there will be some such last thing, there will not be an infinity; but if there is nothing such, there will not be that for the sake of which. Yet those who posit the infinite unwittingly abolish the nature of the good (although no one would undertake to do anything if he were not going to arrive at an end); nor would there be intellect among beings, for one who has intellect always acts for the sake of something, and this is a limit; for the end is a limit.","kind":"passage","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_DAZY_BOO2:P0007"]},{"id":"RAW_DAZY_BOO2:P0008","text":"But neither can the what-it-was-to-be be referred back to another definition increasing in account; for the preceding term is always more so, while the subsequent term is not; and where the first is not, neither is what follows. Further, those who speak thus abolish knowledge, for it is not possible to know before reaching the indivisibles; and knowing is impossible, for how can things infinite in this way be thought? For it is not like a line, which does not stop in its divisions, but cannot be thought unless one stops it (which is why one traversing the infinite will not count the cuts); rather, it is necessary to think even the whole without moving through it. And for no infinite thing is there being; if not, then being-infinite is not infinite.—But indeed, even if the kinds of causes were infinite in number, knowing would not exist even so; for we think that we know when we have come to know the causes, whereas what is infinite by addition cannot be gone through in a finite time.","kind":"passage","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_DAZY_BOO2:P0008"]},{"id":"RAW_DAZY_BOO2:P0009","text":"Lectures accord with habits. For as we are accustomed, so we demand things be said; and what is contrary to these habits does not appear similar, but through unfamiliarity appears more unknown and more foreign. For what is customary is familiar. The laws make clear how great the force of what is customary is: in them, mythical and childish matters prevail more than knowledge about them, because of habit. Thus, some do not accept speakers unless they speak mathematically; others, unless they speak by examples; and others demand that a poet be adduced as a witness. Some demand precision in everything, whereas precision distresses others, either because they cannot connect the points or because of pettiness; for precision has something of this character, so that, as in contracts, it seems to some to be ungenerous also in arguments. Therefore one must have been educated in how each kind of thing is to be accepted, since it is absurd to seek at the same time knowledge and the method of knowledge; yet neither of the two is easy to acquire.","kind":"passage","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_DAZY_BOO2:P0009"]},{"id":"RAW_DAZY_BOO2:P0010","text":"Mathematical exactness is not to be demanded in all matters, but in those which do not have matter. Hence this method is not that of natural science; for perhaps all nature has matter. Therefore one must first investigate what nature is; for thus it will also be clear what things natural science concerns.","kind":"passage","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_DAZY_BOO2:P0010"]}],"source_digest":"e711b53586c9f7efc542a2d3fce9863bdfb9ddce0b0cd9d7f7a1bfaaab4bc7df","source_language_code":"grc","original_title":"Book 2"}],"passage_basis":"frozen-grounding","original_title":"Book 2","title_language_code":"en"},{"id":"RAW_DAZY_BOO3","title":"Book 3","order":3,"chapter_id":"CH_DAZY_BOO3","text":"ἀνάγκη πρὸς τὴν ἐπιζητουμένην ἐπιστήμην ἐπελθεῖν ἡμᾶς πρῶτον περὶ ὧν ἀπορῆσαι δεῖ πρῶτον· ταῦτα δʼ ἐστὶν ὅσα τε περὶ αὐτῶν ἄλλως ὑπειλήφασί τινες, κἂν εἴ τι χωρὶς τούτων τυγχάνει παρεωραμένον. ἔστι δὲ τοῖς εὐπορῆσαι βουλομένοις προὔργου τὸ διαπορῆσαι καλῶς· ἡ γὰρ ὕστερον εὐπορία λύσις τῶν πρότερον ἀπορουμένων ἐστί, λύειν δʼ οὐκ ἔστιν ἀγνοοῦντας τὸν δεσμόν, ἀλλʼ ἡ τῆς διανοίας ἀπορία δηλοῖ τοῦτο περὶ τοῦ πράγματος· ᾗ γὰρ ἀπορεῖ, ταύτῃ παραπλήσιον πέπονθε τοῖς δεδεμένοις· ἀδύνατον γὰρ ἀμφοτέρως προελθεῖν εἰς τὸ πρόσθεν. διὸ δεῖ τὰς δυσχερείας τεθεωρηκέναι πάσας πρότερον, τούτων τε χάριν καὶ διὰ τὸ τοὺς ζητοῦντας ἄνευ τοῦ διαπορῆσαι πρῶτον ὁμοίους εἶναι τοῖς ποῖ δεῖ βαδίζειν ἀγνοοῦσι, καὶ πρὸς τούτοις οὐδʼ εἴ ποτε τὸ ζητούμενον εὕρηκεν ἢ μὴ γιγνώσκειν·  τὸ γὰρ τέλος τούτῳ μὲν οὐ δῆλον τῷ δὲ προηπορηκότι δῆλον. ἔτι δὲ βέλτιον ἀνάγκη ἔχειν πρὸς τὸ κρῖναι τὸν ὥσπερ ἀντιδίκων καὶ τῶν ἀμφισβητούντων λόγων ἀκηκοότα πάντων.—ἔστι δʼ ἀπορία πρώτη μὲν περὶ ὧν ἐν τοῖς πεφροιμιασμένοις διηπορήσαμεν, πότερον μιᾶς ἢ πολλῶν ἐπιστημῶν θεωρῆσαι τὰς αἰτίας· καὶ πότερον τὰς τῆς οὐσίας ἀρχὰς τὰς πρώτας ἐστὶ τῆς ἐπιστήμης ἰδεῖν μόνον ἢ καὶ περὶ τῶν ἀρχῶν ἐξ ὧν δεικνύουσι πάντες, οἷον πότερον ἐνδέχεται ταὐτὸ καὶ ἓν ἅμα φάναι καὶ ἀποφάναι ἢ οὔ, καὶ περὶ τῶν ἄλλων τῶν τοιούτων· εἴ τʼ ἐστι περὶ τὴν οὐσίαν, πότερον μία περὶ πάσας ἢ πλείονές εἰσι, κἂν εἰ πλείονες πότερον ἅπασαι συγγενεῖς ἢ τὰς μὲν σοφίας τὰς δὲ ἄλλο τι λεκτέον αὐτῶν. καὶ τοῦτο δʼ αὐτὸ τῶν ἀναγκαίων ἐστὶ ζητῆσαι, πότερον τὰς αἰσθητὰς οὐσίας εἶναι μόνον φατέον ἢ καὶ παρὰ ταύτας ἄλλας, καὶ πότερον μοναχῶς ἢ πλείονα γένη τῶν οὐσιῶν, οἷον οἱ ποιοῦντες τά τε εἴδη καὶ τὰ μαθηματικὰ μεταξὺ τούτων τε καὶ τῶν αἰσθητῶν. περί τε τούτων οὖν, καθάπερ φαμέν, ἐπισκεπτέον, καὶ πότερον περὶ τὰς οὐσίας ἡ θεωρία μόνον ἐστὶν ἢ καὶ περὶ τὰ συμβεβηκότα καθʼ αὑτὰ ταῖς οὐσίαις, πρὸς δὲ τούτοις περὶ ταὐτοῦ καὶ ἑτέρου καὶ ὁμοίου καὶ ἀνομοίου καὶ ἐναντιότητος, καὶ περὶ προτέρου καὶ ὑστέρου καὶ τῶν ἄλλων ἁπάντων τῶν τοιούτων περὶ ὅσων οἱ διαλεκτικοὶ πειρῶνται σκοπεῖν ἐκ τῶν ἐνδόξων μόνων ποιούμενοι τὴν σκέψιν, τίνος ἐστὶ θεωρῆσαι περὶ πάντων· ἔτι δὲ τούτοις αὐτοῖς ὅσα καθʼ αὑτὰ συμβέβηκεν, καὶ μὴ μόνον τί ἐστι τούτων ἕκαστον ἀλλὰ καὶ ἆρα ἓν ἑνὶ ἐναντίον· καὶ πότερον αἱ ἀρχαὶ καὶ τὰ στοιχεῖα τὰ γένη ἐστὶν ἢ εἰς ἃ διαιρεῖται ἐνυπάρχοντα ἕκαστον· καὶ εἰ τὰ γένη, πότερον ὅσα ἐπὶ τοῖς ἀτόμοις λέγεται  τελευταῖα ἢ τὰ πρῶτα, οἷον πότερον ζῷον ἢ ἄνθρωπος ἀρχή τε καὶ μᾶλλον ἔστι παρὰ τὸ καθʼ ἕκαστον. μάλιστα δὲ ζητητέον καὶ πραγματευτέον πότερον ἔστι τι παρὰ τὴν ὕλην αἴτιον καθʼ αὑτὸ ἢ οὔ, καὶ τοῦτο χωριστὸν ἢ οὔ, καὶ πότερον ἓν ἢ πλείω τὸν ἀριθμόν, καὶ πότερον ἔστι τι παρὰ τὸ σύνολον (λέγω δὲ τὸ σύνολον, ὅταν κατηγορηθῇ τι τῆς ὕλης) ἢ οὐθέν, ἢ τῶν μὲν τῶν δʼ οὔ, καὶ ποῖα τοιαῦτα τῶν ὄντων.  ἔτι αἱ ἀρχαὶ πότερον ἀριθμῷ ἢ εἴδει ὡρισμέναι, καὶ αἱ ἐν τοῖς λόγοις καὶ αἱ ἐν τῷ ὑποκειμένῳ; καὶ πότερον τῶν φθαρτῶν καὶ ἀφθάρτων αἱ αὐταὶ ἢ ἕτεραι, καὶ πότερον ἄφθαρτοι πᾶσαι ἢ τῶν φθαρτῶν φθαρταί; ἔτι δὲ τὸ πάντων χαλεπώτατον καὶ πλείστην ἀπορίαν ἔχον, πότερον τὸ ἓν καὶ τὸ ὄν, καθάπερ οἱ Πυθαγόρειοι καὶ Πλάτων ἔλεγεν, οὐχ ἕτερόν τί ἐστιν ἀλλʼ οὐσία τῶν ὄντων; ἢ οὔ, ἀλλʼ ἕτερόν τι τὸ ὑποκείμενον, ὥσπερ Ἐμπεδοκλῆς φησὶ φιλίαν ἄλλος δέ τις πῦρ ὁ δὲ ὕδωρ ἢ ἀέρα· καὶ πότερον αἱ ἀρχαὶ καθόλου εἰσὶν ἢ ὡς τὰ καθʼ ἕκαστα τῶν πραγμάτων, καὶ δυνάμει ἢ ἐνεργείᾳ· ἔτι πότερον ἄλλως ἢ κατὰ κίνησιν· καὶ γὰρ ταῦτα ἀπορίαν ἂν παράσχοι πολλήν. πρὸς δὲ τούτοις πότερον οἱ ἀριθμοὶ καὶ τὰ μήκη καὶ τὰ σχήματα καὶ αἱ στιγμαὶ οὐσίαι τινές εἰσιν ἢ οὔ, κἂν εἰ οὐσίαι πότερον κεχωρισμέναι τῶν αἰσθητῶν ἢ ἐνυπάρχουσαι ἐν τούτοις; περὶ γὰρ τούτων ἁπάντων οὐ μόνον χαλεπὸν τὸ εὐπορῆσαι τῆς ἀληθείας ἀλλʼ οὐδὲ τὸ διαπορῆσαι τῷ λόγῳ ῥᾴδιον καλῶς. \n\n\n\n \n\n \n\nπρῶτον μὲν οὖν περὶ ὧν πρῶτον εἴπομεν, πότερον μιᾶς ἢ πλειόνων ἐστὶν ἐπιστημῶν θεωρῆσαι πάντα τὰ γένη τῶν αἰτίων. μιᾶς μὲν γὰρ ἐπιστήμης πῶς ἂν εἴη μὴ ἐναντίας οὔσας τὰς ἀρχὰς γνωρίζειν; ἔτι δὲ πολλοῖς τῶν ὄντων οὐχ ὑπάρχουσι πᾶσαι· τίνα γὰρ τρόπον οἷόν τε κινήσεως ἀρχὴν εἶναι τοῖς ἀκινήτοις ἢ τὴν τἀγαθοῦ φύσιν, εἴπερ ἅπαν ὃ ἂν ᾖ ἀγαθὸν καθʼ αὑτὸ καὶ διὰ τὴν αὑτοῦ φύσιν τέλος ἐστὶν καὶ οὕτως αἴτιον ὅτι ἐκείνου ἕνεκα καὶ γίγνεται καὶ ἔστι τἆλλα, τὸ δὲ τέλος καὶ τὸ οὗ ἕνεκα πράξεώς τινός ἐστι τέλος, αἱ δὲ πράξεις πᾶσαι μετὰ κινήσεως; ὥστʼ ἐν τοῖς ἀκινήτοις οὐκ ἂν ἐνδέχοιτο ταύτην εἶναι τὴν ἀρχὴν οὐδʼ εἶναί τι αὐτοαγαθόν. διὸ καὶ ἐν τοῖς μαθήμασιν οὐθὲν δείκνυται διὰ ταύτης τῆς αἰτίας, οὐδʼ ἔστιν ἀπόδειξις οὐδεμία διότι βέλτιον ἢ χεῖρον, ἀλλʼ οὐδὲ τὸ παράπαν μέμνηται οὐθεὶς οὐθενὸς τῶν τοιούτων, ὥστε διὰ ταῦτα τῶν σοφιστῶν τινὲς οἷον Ἀρίστιππος προεπηλάκιζεν αὐτάς· ἐν μὲν γὰρ ταῖς ἄλλαις τέχναις, καὶ ταῖς βαναύσοις, οἷον ἐν τεκτονικῇ καὶ σκυτικῇ, διότι βέλτιον ἢ χεῖρον λέγεσθαι πάντα, τὰς δὲ μαθηματικὰς οὐθένα ποιεῖσθαι λόγον περὶ ἀγαθῶν καὶ κακῶν.  —ἀλλὰ μὴν εἴ γε πλείους ἐπιστῆμαι τῶν αἰτίων εἰσὶ καὶ ἑτέρα ἑτέρας ἀρχῆς, τίνα τούτων φατέον εἶναι τὴν ζητουμένην, ἢ τίνα μάλιστα τοῦ πράγματος τοῦ ζητουμένου ἐπιστήμονα τῶν ἐχόντων αὐτάς; ἐνδέχεται γὰρ τῷ αὐτῷ πάντας τοὺς τρόπους τοὺς τῶν αἰτίων ὑπάρχειν, οἷον οἰκίας ὅθεν μὲν ἡ κίνησις ἡ τέχνη καὶ ὁ οἰκοδόμος, οὗ δʼ ἕνεκα τὸ ἔργον, ὕλη δὲ γῆ καὶ λίθοι, τὸ δʼ εἶδος ὁ λόγος. ἐκ μὲν οὖν τῶν πάλαι διωρισμένων τίνα χρὴ καλεῖν τῶν ἐπιστημῶν σοφίαν ἔχει λόγον ἑκάστην προσαγορεύειν· ᾗ μὲν γὰρ ἀρχικωτάτη καὶ ἡγεμονικωτάτη καὶ ᾗ ὥσπερ δούλας οὐδʼ ἀντειπεῖν τὰς ἄλλας ἐπιστήμας δίκαιον, ἡ τοῦ τέλους καὶ τἀγαθοῦ τοιαύτη (τούτου γὰρ ἕνεκα τἆλλα), ᾗ δὲ τῶν πρώτων αἰτίων καὶ τοῦ μάλιστα ἐπιστητοῦ διωρίσθη εἶναι, ἡ τῆς οὐσίας ἂν εἴη τοιαύτη· πολλαχῶς γὰρ ἐπισταμένων τὸ αὐτὸ μᾶλλον μὲν εἰδέναι φαμὲν τὸν τῷ εἶναι γνωρίζοντα τί τὸ πρᾶγμα ἢ τῷ μὴ εἶναι, αὐτῶν δὲ τούτων ἕτερον ἑτέρου μᾶλλον, καὶ μάλιστα τὸν τί ἐστιν ἀλλʼ οὐ τὸν πόσον ἢ ποῖον ἢ τί ποιεῖν ἢ πάσχειν πέφυκεν. ἔτι δὲ καὶ ἐν τοῖς ἄλλοις τὸ εἰδέναι ἕκαστον καὶ ὧν ἀποδείξεις εἰσί, τότʼ οἰόμεθα ὑπάρχειν ὅταν εἰδῶμεν τί ἐστιν (οἷον τί ἐστι τὸ τετραγωνίζειν, ὅτι μέσης εὕρεσις· ὁμοίως δὲ καὶ ἐπὶ τῶν ἄλλων), περὶ δὲ τὰς γενέσεις καὶ τὰς πράξεις καὶ περὶ πᾶσαν μεταβολὴν ὅταν εἰδῶμεν τὴν ἀρχὴν τῆς κινήσεως· τοῦτο δʼ ἕτερον καὶ ἀντικείμενον τῷ τέλει, ὥστʼ ἄλλης ἂν δόξειεν ἐπιστήμης εἶναι τὸ θεωρῆσαι τῶν αἰτίων τούτων ἕκαστον.—ἀλλὰ μὴν καὶ περὶ τῶν ἀποδεικτικῶν ἀρχῶν, πότερον μιᾶς ἐστὶν ἐπιστήμης ἢ πλειόνων, ἀμφισβητήσιμόν ἐστιν (λέγω δὲ ἀποδεικτικὰς τὰς κοινὰς δόξας ἐξ ὧν ἅπαντες δεικνύουσιν) οἷον ὅτι πᾶν ἀναγκαῖον ἢ φάναι ἢ ἀποφάναι, καὶ ἀδύνατον ἅμα εἶναι καὶ μὴ εἶναι, καὶ ὅσαι ἄλλαι τοιαῦται προτάσεις, πότερον μία τούτων ἐπιστήμη καὶ τῆς οὐσίας ἢ ἑτέρα, κἂν εἰ μὴ μία, ποτέραν χρὴ προσαγορεύειν τὴν ζητουμένην νῦν. μιᾶς μὲν οὖν οὐκ εὔλογον εἶναι· τί γὰρ μᾶλλον γεωμετρίας ἢ ὁποιασοῦν περὶ τούτων ἐστὶν ἴδιον τὸ ἐπαΐειν; εἴπερ οὖν ὁμοίως μὲν ὁποιασοῦν ἐστίν, ἁπασῶν δὲ μὴ ἐνδέχεται,  ὥσπερ οὐδὲ τῶν ἄλλων οὕτως οὐδὲ τῆς γνωριζούσης τὰς οὐσίας ἴδιόν ἐστι τὸ γιγνώσκειν περὶ αὐτῶν. ἅμα δὲ καὶ τίνα τρόπον ἔσται αὐτῶν ἐπιστήμη; τί μὲν γὰρ ἕκαστον τούτων τυγχάνει ὂν καὶ νῦν γνωρίζομεν (χρῶνται γοῦν ὡς γιγνωσκομένοις αὐτοῖς καὶ ἄλλαι τέχναι)· εἰ δὲ ἀποδεικτικὴ περὶ αὐτῶν ἐστί, δεήσει τι γένος εἶναι ὑποκείμενον καὶ τὰ μὲν πάθη τὰ δʼ ἀξιώματʼ αὐτῶν (περὶ πάντων γὰρ ἀδύνατον ἀπόδειξιν εἶναι), ἀνάγκη γὰρ ἔκ τινων εἶναι καὶ περί τι καὶ τινῶν τὴν ἀπόδειξιν· ὥστε συμβαίνει πάντων εἶναι γένος ἕν τι τῶν δεικνυμένων, πᾶσαι γὰρ αἱ ἀποδεικτικαὶ χρῶνται τοῖς ἀξιώμασιν.—ἀλλὰ μὴν εἰ ἑτέρα ἡ τῆς οὐσίας καὶ ἡ περὶ τούτων, ποτέρα κυριωτέρα καὶ προτέρα πέφυκεν αὐτῶν; καθόλου γὰρ μάλιστα καὶ πάντων ἀρχαὶ τὰ ἀξιώματά ἐστιν, εἴ τʼ ἐστὶ μὴ τοῦ φιλοσόφου, τίνος ἔσται περὶ αὐτῶν ἄλλου τὸ θεωρῆσαι τὸ ἀληθὲς καὶ ψεῦδος;—ὅλως τε τῶν οὐσιῶν πότερον μία πασῶν ἐστὶν ἢ πλείους ἐπιστῆμαι; εἰ μὲν οὖν μὴ μία, ποίας οὐσίας θετέον τὴν ἐπιστήμην ταύτην; τὸ δὲ μίαν πασῶν οὐκ εὔλογον· καὶ γὰρ ἂν ἀποδεικτικὴ μία περὶ πάντων εἴη τῶν συμβεβηκότων, εἴπερ πᾶσα ἀποδεικτικὴ περί τι ὑποκείμενον θεωρεῖ τὰ καθʼ αὑτὰ συμβεβηκότα ἐκ τῶν κοινῶν δοξῶν. περὶ οὖν τὸ αὐτὸ γένος τὰ συμβεβηκότα καθʼ αὑτὰ τῆς αὐτῆς ἐστὶ θεωρῆσαι ἐκ τῶν αὐτῶν δοξῶν. περί τε γὰρ ὃ μιᾶς καὶ ἐξ ὧν μιᾶς, εἴτε τῆς αὐτῆς εἴτε ἄλλης, ὥστε καὶ τὰ συμβεβηκότα, εἴθʼ αὗται θεωροῦσιν εἴτʼ ἐκ τούτων μία.—ἔτι δὲ πότερον περὶ τὰς οὐσίας μόνον ἡ θεωρία ἐστὶν ἢ καὶ περὶ τὰ συμβεβηκότα ταύταις; λέγω δʼ οἷον, εἰ τὸ στερεὸν οὐσία τίς ἐστι καὶ γραμμαὶ καὶ ἐπίπεδα, πότερον τῆς αὐτῆς ταῦτα γνωρίζειν ἐστὶν ἐπιστήμης καὶ τὰ συμβεβηκότα περὶ ἕκαστον γένος περὶ ὧν αἱ μαθηματικαὶ  δεικνύουσιν, ἢ ἄλλης. εἰ μὲν γὰρ τῆς αὐτῆς, ἀποδεικτική τις ἂν εἴη καὶ ἡ τῆς οὐσίας, οὐ δοκεῖ δὲ τοῦ τί ἐστιν ἀπόδειξις εἶναι· εἰ δʼ ἑτέρας, τίς ἔσται ἡ θεωροῦσα περὶ τὴν οὐσίαν τὰ συμβεβηκότα; τοῦτο γὰρ ἀποδοῦναι παγχάλεπον.—ἔτι δὲ πότερον τὰς αἰσθητὰς οὐσίας μόνας εἶναι φατέον ἢ καὶ παρὰ ταύτας ἄλλας, καὶ πότερον μοναχῶς ἢ πλείω γένη τετύχηκεν ὄντα τῶν οὐσιῶν,  οἷον οἱ λέγοντες τά τε εἴδη καὶ τὰ μεταξύ, περὶ ἃ τὰς μαθηματικὰς εἶναί φασιν ἐπιστήμας; ὡς μὲν οὖν λέγομεν τὰ εἴδη αἴτιά τε καὶ οὐσίας εἶναι καθʼ ἑαυτὰς εἴρηται ἐν τοῖς πρώτοις λόγοις περὶ αὐτῶν· πολλαχῇ δὲ ἐχόντων δυσκολίαν, οὐθενὸς ἧττον ἄτοπον τὸ φάναι μὲν εἶναί τινας φύσεις παρὰ τὰς ἐν τῷ οὐρανῷ, ταύτας δὲ τὰς αὐτὰς φάναι τοῖς αἰσθητοῖς πλὴν ὅτι τὰ μὲν ἀΐδια τὰ δὲ φθαρτά. αὐτὸ γὰρ ἄνθρωπόν φασιν εἶναι καὶ ἵππον καὶ ὑγίειαν, ἄλλο δʼ οὐδέν, παραπλήσιον ποιοῦντες τοῖς θεοὺς μὲν εἶναι φάσκουσιν ἀνθρωποειδεῖς δέ· οὔτε γὰρ ἐκεῖνοι οὐδὲν ἄλλο ἐποίουν ἢ ἀνθρώπους ἀϊδίους, οὔθʼ οὗτοι τὰ εἴδη ἄλλʼ ἢ αἰσθητὰ ἀΐδια. ἔτι δὲ εἴ τις παρὰ τὰ εἴδη καὶ τὰ αἰσθητὰ τὰ μεταξὺ θήσεται, πολλὰς ἀπορίας ἕξει· δῆλον γὰρ ὡς ὁμοίως γραμμαί τε παρά τʼ αὐτὰς καὶ τὰς αἰσθητὰς ἔσονται καὶ ἕκαστον τῶν ἄλλων γενῶν· ὥστʼ ἐπείπερ ἡ ἀστρολογία μία τούτων ἐστίν, ἔσται τις καὶ οὐρανὸς παρὰ τὸν αἰσθητὸν οὐρανὸν καὶ ἥλιός τε καὶ σελήνη καὶ τἆλλα ὁμοίως τὰ κατὰ τὸν οὐρανόν. καίτοι πῶς δεῖ πιστεῦσαι τούτοις; οὐδὲ γὰρ ἀκίνητον εὔλογον εἶναι, κινούμενον δὲ καὶ παντελῶς ἀδύνατον· ὁμοίως δὲ καὶ περὶ ὧν ἡ ὀπτικὴ πραγματεύεται καὶ ἡ ἐν τοῖς μαθήμασιν ἁρμονική· καὶ γὰρ ταῦτα ἀδύνατον εἶναι παρὰ τὰ αἰσθητὰ διὰ τὰς αὐτὰς αἰτίας· εἰ γὰρ ἔστιν αἰσθητὰ μεταξὺ καὶ αἰσθήσεις, δῆλον ὅτι καὶ ζῷα ἔσονται μεταξὺ αὐτῶν τε καὶ τῶν φθαρτῶν. ἀπορήσειε δʼ ἄν τις καὶ περὶ ποῖα τῶν ὄντων δεῖ ζητεῖν ταύτας τὰς ἐπιστήμας. εἰ γὰρ τούτῳ διοίσει τῆς γεωδαισίας ἡ γεωμετρία μόνον, ὅτι ἡ μὲν τούτων ἐστὶν ὧν αἰσθανόμεθα ἡ δʼ οὐκ αἰσθητῶν, δῆλον ὅτι καὶ παρʼ ἰατρικὴν ἔσται τις ἐπιστήμη καὶ παρʼ ἑκάστην τῶν ἄλλων μεταξὺ αὐτῆς τε ἰατρικῆς καὶ τῆσδε τῆς ἰατρικῆς· καίτοι πῶς τοῦτο δυνατόν; καὶ γὰρ ἂν ὑγιείνʼ ἄττα εἴη παρὰ τὰ αἰσθητὰ καὶ αὐτὸ τὸ ὑγιεινόν. ἅμα δὲ οὐδὲ τοῦτο ἀληθές, ὡς ἡ γεωδαισία τῶν αἰσθητῶν ἐστὶ μεγεθῶν καὶ φθαρτῶν· ἐφθείρετο γὰρ ἂν φθειρομένων.—ἀλλὰ μὴν οὐδὲ τῶν αἰσθητῶν ἂν εἴη μεγεθῶν οὐδὲ περὶ τὸν οὐρανὸν ἡ ἀστρολογία τόνδε.  οὔτε γὰρ αἱ αἰσθηταὶ γραμμαὶ τοιαῦταί εἰσιν οἵας λέγει ὁ γεωμέτρης (οὐθὲν γὰρ εὐθὺ τῶν αἰσθητῶν οὕτως οὐδὲ στρογγύλον· ἅπτεται γὰρ τοῦ κανόνος οὐ κατὰ στιγμὴν ὁ κύκλος ἀλλʼ ὥσπερ Πρωταγόρας ἔλεγεν ἐλέγχων τοὺς γεωμέτρας) , οὔθʼ αἱ κινήσεις καὶ ἕλικες τοῦ οὐρανοῦ ὅμοιαι περὶ ὧν ἡ ἀστρολογία ποιεῖται τοὺς λόγους, οὔτε τὰ σημεῖα τοῖς ἄστροις τὴν αὐτὴν ἔχει φύσιν. εἰσὶ δέ τινες οἵ φασιν εἶναι μὲν τὰ μεταξὺ ταῦτα λεγόμενα τῶν τε εἰδῶν καὶ τῶν αἰσθητῶν, οὐ μὴν χωρίς γε τῶν αἰσθητῶν ἀλλʼ ἐν τούτοις· οἷς τὰ συμβαίνοντα ἀδύνατα πάντα μὲν πλείονος λόγου διελθεῖν, ἱκανὸν δὲ καὶ τὰ τοιαῦτα θεωρῆσαι. οὔτε γὰρ ἐπὶ τούτων εὔλογον ἔχειν οὕτω μόνον, ἀλλὰ δῆλον ὅτι καὶ τὰ εἴδη ἐνδέχοιτʼ ἂν ἐν τοῖς αἰσθητοῖς εἶναι (τοῦ γὰρ αὐτοῦ λόγου ἀμφότερα ταῦτά ἐστιν), ἔτι δὲ δύο στερεὰ ἐν τῷ αὐτῷ ἀναγκαῖον εἶναι τόπῳ, καὶ μὴ εἶναι ἀκίνητα ἐν κινουμένοις γε ὄντα τοῖς αἰσθητοῖς. ὅλως δὲ τίνος ἕνεκʼ ἄν τις θείη εἶναι μὲν αὐτά, εἶναι δʼ ἐν τοῖς αἰσθητοῖς; ταὐτὰ γὰρ συμβήσεται ἄτοπα τοῖς προειρημένοις· ἔσται γὰρ οὐρανός τις παρὰ τὸν οὐρανόν, πλήν γʼ οὐ χωρὶς ἀλλʼ ἐν τῷ αὐτῷ τόπῳ· ὅπερ ἐστὶν ἀδυνατώτερον. \n\n\n\n \n\n \n\nπερί τε τούτων οὖν ἀπορία πολλὴ πῶς δεῖ θέμενον τυχεῖν τῆς ἀληθείας, καὶ περὶ τῶν ἀρχῶν πότερον δεῖ τὰ γένη στοιχεῖα καὶ ἀρχὰς ὑπολαμβάνειν ἢ μᾶλλον ἐξ ὧν ἐνυπαρχόντων ἐστὶν ἕκαστον πρώτων, οἷον φωνῆς στοιχεῖα καὶ ἀρχαὶ δοκοῦσιν εἶναι ταῦτʼ ἐξ ὧν σύγκεινται αἱ φωναὶ πρώτων, ἀλλʼ οὐ τὸ κοινὸν ἡ φωνή· καὶ τῶν διαγραμμάτων ταῦτα στοιχεῖα λέγομεν ὧν αἱ ἀποδείξεις ἐνυπάρχουσιν ἐν ταῖς τῶν ἄλλων ἀποδείξεσιν ἢ πάντων ἢ τῶν πλείστων, ἔτι δὲ τῶν σωμάτων καὶ οἱ πλείω λέγοντες εἶναι στοιχεῖα καὶ οἱ ἕν, ἐξ ὧν σύγκειται καὶ ἐξ ὧν συνέστηκεν ἀρχὰς λέγουσιν εἶναι, οἷον Ἐμπεδοκλῆς πῦρ καὶ ὕδωρ καὶ τὰ μετὰ τούτων στοιχεῖά φησιν εἶναι ἐξ ὧν ἐστὶ τὰ ὄντα ἐνυπαρχόντων, ἀλλʼ οὐχ ὡς γένη λέγει ταῦτα τῶν ὄντων. πρὸς δὲ τούτοις καὶ τῶν ἄλλων εἴ τις ἐθέλει τὴν φύσιν ἀθρεῖν,  οἷον κλίνην ἐξ ὧν μορίων συνέστηκε καὶ πῶς συγκειμένων, τότε γνωρίζει τὴν φύσιν αὐτῆς.—ἐκ μὲν οὖν τούτων τῶν λόγων οὐκ ἂν εἴησαν αἱ ἀρχαὶ τὰ γένη τῶν ὄντων· εἰ δʼ ἕκαστον μὲν γνωρίζομεν διὰ τῶν ὁρισμῶν, ἀρχαὶ δὲ τὰ γένη τῶν ὁρισμῶν εἰσίν, ἀνάγκη καὶ τῶν ὁριστῶν ἀρχὰς εἶναι τὰ γένη. κἂν εἰ ἔστι τὴν τῶν ὄντων λαβεῖν ἐπιστήμην τὸ τῶν εἰδῶν λαβεῖν καθʼ ἃ λέγονται τὰ ὄντα, τῶν γε εἰδῶν ἀρχαὶ τὰ γένη εἰσίν. φαίνονται δέ τινες καὶ τῶν λεγόντων στοιχεῖα τῶν ὄντων τὸ ἓν ἢ τὸ ὂν ἢ τὸ μέγα καὶ μικρὸν ὡς γένεσιν αὐτοῖς χρῆσθαι.—ἀλλὰ μὴν οὐδὲ ἀμφοτέρως γε οἷόν τε λέγειν τὰς ἀρχάς. ὁ μὲν γὰρ λόγος τῆς οὐσίας εἷς· ἕτερος δʼ ἔσται ὁ διὰ τῶν γενῶν ὁρισμὸς καὶ ὁ λέγων ἐξ ὧν ἔστιν ἐνυπαρχόντων.—πρὸς δὲ τούτοις εἰ καὶ ὅτι μάλιστα ἀρχαὶ τὰ γένη εἰσί, πότερον δεῖ νομίζειν τὰ πρῶτα τῶν γενῶν ἀρχὰς ἢ τὰ ἔσχατα κατηγορούμενα ἐπὶ τῶν ἀτόμων; καὶ γὰρ τοῦτο ἔχει ἀμφισβήτησιν. εἰ μὲν γὰρ ἀεὶ τὰ καθόλου μᾶλλον ἀρχαί, φανερὸν ὅτι τὰ ἀνωτάτω τῶν γενῶν· ταῦτα γὰρ λέγεται κατὰ πάντων. τοσαῦται οὖν ἔσονται ἀρχαὶ τῶν ὄντων ὅσαπερ τὰ πρῶτα γένη, ὥστʼ ἔσται τό τε ὂν καὶ τὸ ἓν ἀρχαὶ καὶ οὐσίαι· ταῦτα γὰρ κατὰ πάντων μάλιστα λέγεται τῶν ὄντων. οὐχ οἷόν τε δὲ τῶν ὄντων ἓν εἶναι γένος οὔτε τὸ ἓν οὔτε τὸ ὄν· ἀνάγκη μὲν γὰρ τὰς διαφορὰς ἑκάστου γένους καὶ εἶναι καὶ μίαν εἶναι ἑκάστην, ἀδύνατον δὲ κατηγορεῖσθαι ἢ τὰ εἴδη τοῦ γένους ἐπὶ τῶν οἰκείων διαφορῶν ἢ τὸ γένος ἄνευ τῶν αὐτοῦ εἰδῶν, ὥστʼ εἴπερ τὸ ἓν γένος ἢ τὸ ὄν, οὐδεμία διαφορὰ οὔτε ὂν οὔτε ἓν ἔσται. ἀλλὰ μὴν εἰ μὴ γένη, οὐδʼ ἀρχαὶ ἔσονται, εἴπερ ἀρχαὶ τὰ γένη. ἔτι καὶ τὰ μεταξὺ συλλαμβανόμενα μετὰ τῶν διαφορῶν ἔσται γένη μέχρι τῶν ἀτόμων (νῦν δὲ τὰ μὲν δοκεῖ τὰ δʼ οὐ δοκει)· πρὸς δὲ τούτοις ἔτι μᾶλλον αἱ διαφοραὶ ἀρχαὶ ἢ τὰ γένη· εἰ δὲ καὶ αὗται ἀρχαί, ἄπειροι ὡς εἰπεῖν ἀρχαὶ γίγνονται, ἄλλως τε κἄν τις τὸ πρῶτον γένος ἀρχὴν τιθῇ.  ἀλλὰ μὴν καὶ εἰ μᾶλλόν γε ἀρχοειδὲς τὸ ἕν ἐστιν, ἓν δὲ τὸ ἀδιαίρετον, ἀδιαίρετον δὲ ἅπαν ἢ κατὰ τὸ ποσὸν ἢ κατʼ εἶδος, πρότερον δὲ τὸ κατʼ εἶδος, τὰ δὲ γένη διαιρετὰ εἰς εἴδη, μᾶλλον ἂν ἓν τὸ ἔσχατον εἴη κατηγορούμενον· οὐ γάρ ἐστι γένος ἅνθρωπος τῶν τινῶν ἀνθρώπων. ἔτι ἐν οἷς τὸ πρότερον καὶ ὕστερόν ἐστιν, οὐχ οἷόν τε τὸ ἐπὶ τούτων εἶναί τι παρὰ ταῦτα (οἷον εἰ πρώτη τῶν ἀριθμῶν ἡ δυάς, οὐκ ἔσται τις ἀριθμὸς παρὰ τὰ εἴδη τῶν ἀριθμῶν· ὁμοίως δὲ οὐδὲ σχῆμα παρὰ τὰ εἴδη τῶν σχημάτων· εἰ δὲ μὴ τούτων, σχολῇ τῶν γε ἄλλων ἔσται τὰ γένη παρὰ τὰ εἴδη· τούτων γὰρ δοκεῖ μάλιστα εἶναι γένη)· ἐν δὲ τοῖς ἀτόμοις οὐκ ἔστι τὸ μὲν πρότερον τὸ δʼ ὕστερον. ἔτι ὅπου τὸ μὲν βέλτιον τὸ δὲ χεῖρον, ἀεὶ τὸ βέλτιον πρότερον· ὥστʼ οὐδὲ τούτων ἂν εἴη γένος.—ἐκ μὲν οὖν τούτων μᾶλλον φαίνεται τὰ ἐπὶ τῶν ἀτόμων κατηγορούμενα ἀρχαὶ εἶναι τῶν γενῶν· πάλιν δὲ πῶς αὖ δεῖ ταύτας ἀρχὰς ὑπολαβεῖν οὐ ῥᾴδιον εἰπεῖν. τὴν μὲν γὰρ ἀρχὴν δεῖ καὶ τὴν αἰτίαν εἶναι παρὰ τὰ πράγματα ὧν ἀρχή, καὶ δύνασθαι εἶναι χωριζομένην αὐτῶν· τοιοῦτον δέ τι παρὰ τὸ καθʼ ἕκαστον εἶναι διὰ τί ἄν τις ὑπολάβοι, πλὴν ὅτι καθόλου κατηγορεῖται καὶ κατὰ πάντων; ἀλλὰ μὴν εἰ διὰ τοῦτο, τὰ μᾶλλον καθόλου μᾶλλον θετέον ἀρχάς· ὥστε ἀρχαὶ τὰ πρῶτʼ ἂν εἴησαν γένη. \n\n\n\n \n\n \n\nἔστι δʼ ἐχομένη τε τούτων ἀπορία καὶ πασῶν χαλεπωτάτη καὶ ἀναγκαιοτάτη θεωρῆσαι, περὶ ἧς ὁ λόγος ἐφέστηκε νῦν. εἴτε γὰρ μὴ ἔστι τι παρὰ τὰ καθʼ ἕκαστα, τὰ δὲ καθʼ ἕκαστα ἄπειρα, τῶν δʼ ἀπείρων πῶς ἐνδέχεται λαβεῖν ἐπιστήμην; ᾗ γὰρ ἕν τι καὶ ταὐτόν, καὶ ᾗ καθόλου τι ὑπάρχει, ταύτῃ πάντα γνωρίζομεν.—ἀλλὰ μὴν εἰ τοῦτο ἀναγκαῖόν ἐστι καὶ δεῖ τι εἶναι παρὰ τὰ καθʼ ἕκαστα, ἀναγκαῖον ἂν εἴη τὰ γένη εἶναι παρὰ τὰ καθʼ ἕκαστα, ἤτοι τὰ ἔσχατα ἢ τὰ πρῶτα· τοῦτο δʼ ὅτι ἀδύνατον ἄρτι διηπορήσαμεν.—ἔτι εἰ ὅτι μάλιστα ἔστι τι παρὰ τὸ σύνολον ὅταν κατηγορηθῇ τι τῆς ὕλης, πότερον, εἰ ἔστι, παρὰ πάντα δεῖ εἶναί τι, ἢ παρὰ μὲν ἔνια εἶναι παρὰ δʼ ἔνια μὴ εἶναι, ἢ παρʼ οὐδέν;  εἰ μὲν οὖν μηδέν ἐστι παρὰ τὰ καθʼ ἕκαστα, οὐθὲν ἂν εἴη νοητὸν ἀλλὰ πάντα αἰσθητὰ καὶ ἐπιστήμη οὐδενός, εἰ μή τις εἶναι λέγει τὴν αἴσθησιν ἐπιστήμην. ἔτι δʼ οὐδʼ ἀΐδιον οὐθὲν οὐδʼ ἀκίνητον (τὰ γὰρ αἰσθητὰ πάντα φθείρεται καὶ ἐν κινήσει ἐστίν)· ἀλλὰ μὴν εἴ γε ἀΐδιον μηθέν ἐστιν, οὐδὲ γένεσιν εἶναι δυνατόν. ἀνάγκη γὰρ εἶναί τι τὸ γιγνόμενον καὶ ἐξ οὗ γίγνεται καὶ τούτων τὸ ἔσχατον ἀγένητον, εἴπερ ἵσταταί τε καὶ ἐκ μὴ ὄντος γενέσθαι ἀδύνατον· ἔτι δὲ γενέσεως οὔσης καὶ κινήσεως ἀνάγκη καὶ πέρας εἶναι (οὔτε γὰρ ἄπειρός ἐστιν οὐδεμία κίνησις ἀλλὰ πάσης ἔστι τέλος, γίγνεσθαί τε οὐχ οἷόν τε τὸ ἀδύνατον γενέσθαι· τὸ δὲ γεγονὸς ἀνάγκη εἶναι ὅτε πρῶτον γέγονεν)· ἔτι δʼ εἴπερ ἡ ὕλη ἔστι διὰ τὸ ἀγένητος εἶναι, πολὺ ἔτι μᾶλλον εὔλογον εἶναι τὴν οὐσίαν, ὅ ποτε ἐκείνη γίγνεται· εἰ γὰρ μήτε τοῦτο ἔσται μήτε ἐκείνη, οὐθὲν ἔσται τὸ παράπαν, εἰ δὲ τοῦτο ἀδύνατον, ἀνάγκη τι εἶναι παρὰ τὸ σύνολον, τὴν μορφὴν καὶ τὸ εἶδος.—εἰ δʼ αὖ τις τοῦτο θήσει, ἀπορία ἐπὶ τίνων τε θήσει τοῦτο καὶ ἐπὶ τίνων οὔ. ὅτι μὲν γὰρ ἐπὶ πάντων οὐχ οἷόν τε, φανερόν· οὐ γὰρ ἂν θείημεν εἶναί τινα οἰκίαν παρὰ τὰς τινὰς  οἰκίας. πρὸς δὲ τούτοις πότερον ἡ οὐσία μία πάντων ἔσται, οἷον τῶν ἀνθρώπων; ἀλλʼ ἄτοπον· ἓν γὰρ πάντα ὧν ἡ οὐσία μία. ἀλλὰ πολλὰ καὶ διάφορα; ἀλλὰ καὶ τοῦτο ἄλογον. ἅμα δὲ καὶ πῶς γίγνεται ἡ ὕλη τούτων ἕκαστον καὶ ἔστι τὸ σύνολον ἄμφω ταῦτα;—ἔτι δὲ περὶ τῶν ἀρχῶν καὶ τόδε ἀπορήσειεν ἄν τις. εἰ μὲν γὰρ εἴδει εἰσὶν ἕν, οὐθὲν ἔσται ἀριθμῷ ἕν, οὐδʼ αὐτὸ τὸ ἓν καὶ τὸ ὄν· καὶ τὸ ἐπίστασθαι πῶς ἔσται, εἰ μή τι ἔσται ἓν ἐπὶ πάντων;—ἀλλὰ μὴν εἰ ἀριθμῷ ἓν καὶ μία ἑκάστη τῶν ἀρχῶν, καὶ μὴ ὥσπερ ἐπὶ τῶν αἰσθητῶν ἄλλαι ἄλλων (οἷον τῆσδε τῆς συλλαβῆς τῷ εἴδει τῆς αὐτῆς οὔσης καὶ αἱ ἀρχαὶ εἴδει αἱ αὐταί· καὶ γὰρ αὗται ὑπάρχουσιν ἀριθμῷ ἕτεραι),—εἰ δὲ μὴ οὕτως ἀλλʼ αἱ τῶν ὄντων ἀρχαὶ ἀριθμῷ ἕν εἰσιν, οὐκ ἔσται παρὰ τὰ στοιχεῖα οὐθὲν ἕτερον· τὸ γὰρ ἀριθμῷ ἓν ἢ τὸ καθʼ ἕκαστον λέγειν διαφέρει οὐθέν· οὕτω γὰρ λέγομεν τὸ καθʼ ἕκαστον, τὸ ἀριθμῷ ἕν, καθόλου δὲ τὸ ἐπὶ τούτων.  ὥσπερ οὖν εἰ τὰ τῆς φωνῆς ἀριθμῷ ἦν στοιχεῖα ὡρισμένα, ἀναγκαῖον ἦν ἂν τοσαῦτα εἶναι τὰ πάντα γράμματα ὅσαπερ τὰ στοιχεῖα, μὴ ὄντων γε δύο τῶν αὐτῶν μηδὲ πλειόνων.\n\n \n\nοὐθενὸς δʼ ἐλάττων ἀπορία παραλέλειπται καὶ τοῖς νῦν καὶ τοῖς πρότερον, πότερον αἱ αὐταὶ τῶν φθαρτῶν καὶ τῶν ἀφθάρτων ἀρχαί εἰσιν ἢ ἕτεραι. εἰ μὲν γὰρ αἱ αὐταί, πῶς τὰ μὲν φθαρτὰ τὰ δὲ ἄφθαρτα, καὶ διὰ τίνʼ αἰτίαν; οἱ μὲν οὖν περὶ Ἡσίοδον καὶ πάντες ὅσοι θεολόγοι μόνον ἐφρόντισαν τοῦ πιθανοῦ τοῦ πρὸς αὑτούς, ἡμῶν δʼ ὠλιγώρησαν (θεοὺς γὰρ ποιοῦντες τὰς ἀρχὰς καὶ ἐκ θεῶν γεγονέναι, τὰ μὴ γευσάμενα τοῦ νέκταρος καὶ τῆς ἀμβροσίας θνητὰ γενέσθαι φασίν, δῆλον ὡς ταῦτα τὰ ὀνόματα γνώριμα λέγοντες αὑτοῖς· καίτοι περὶ αὐτῆς τῆς προσφορᾶς τῶν αἰτίων τούτων ὑπὲρ ἡμᾶς εἰρήκασιν· εἰ μὲν γὰρ χάριν ἡδονῆς αὐτῶν θιγγάνουσιν, οὐθὲν αἴτια τοῦ εἶναι τὸ νέκταρ καὶ ἡ ἀμβροσία, εἰ δὲ τοῦ εἶναι, πῶς ἂν εἶεν ἀΐδιοι δεόμενοι τροφῆς)·—ἀλλὰ περὶ μὲν τῶν μυθικῶς σοφιζομένων οὐκ ἄξιον μετὰ σπουδῆς σκοπεῖν· παρὰ δὲ τῶν διʼ ἀποδείξεως λεγόντων δεῖ πυνθάνεσθαι διερωτῶντας τί δή ποτʼ ἐκ τῶν αὐτῶν ὄντα τὰ μὲν ἀΐδια τὴν φύσιν ἐστὶ τὰ δὲ φθείρεται τῶν ὄντων. ἐπεὶ δὲ οὔτε αἰτίαν λέγουσιν οὔτε εὔλογον οὕτως ἔχειν, δῆλον ὡς οὐχ αἱ αὐταὶ ἀρχαὶ οὐδὲ αἰτίαι αὐτῶν ἂν εἶεν. καὶ γὰρ ὅνπερ οἰηθείη λέγειν ἄν τις μάλιστα ὁμολογουμένως αὑτῷ, Ἐμπεδοκλῆς, καὶ οὗτος ταὐτὸν πέπονθεν· τίθησι μὲν γὰρ ἀρχήν τινα αἰτίαν τῆς φθορᾶς τὸ νεῖκος, δόξειε δʼ ἂν οὐθὲν ἧττον καὶ τοῦτο γεννᾶν ἔξω τοῦ ἑνός· ἅπαντα γὰρ ἐκ τούτου τἆλλά ἐστι πλὴν ὁ θεός. λέγει γοῦν  \n\nἐξ ὧν πάνθʼ ὅσα τʼ ἦν ὅσα τʼ ἔσθʼ ὅσα τʼ ἔσται ὀπίσσω,\n\n \n\nδένδρεά τʼ ἐβλάστησε καὶ ἀνέρες ἠδὲ γυναῖκες,\n\n \n\nθῆρές τʼ οἰωνοί τε καὶ ὑδατοθρέμμονες ἰχθῦς,\n\n \n\nκαί τε θεοὶ δολιχαίωνες.\n\n  Empedocles Fr. 21.9-12 καὶ χωρὶς δὲ τούτων δῆλον·  εἰ γὰρ μὴ ἦν ἐν τοῖς πράγμασιν, ἓν ἂν ἦν ἅπαντα, ὡς φησίν· ὅταν γὰρ συνέλθῃ,  \n\nτότε δʼ ἔσχατον ἵστατο νεῖκος.\n\n  Empedocles Fr. 36.7 διὸ καὶ συμβαίνει αὐτῷ τὸν εὐδαιμονέστατον θεὸν ἧττον φρόνιμον εἶναι τῶν ἄλλων· οὐ γὰρ γνωρίζει ἅπαντα· τὸ γὰρ νεῖκος οὐκ ἔχει, ἡ δὲ γνῶσις τοῦ ὁμοίου τῷ ὁμοίῳ.  \n\nγαίῃ μὲν γάρ, (φησί,) γαῖαν ὀπώπαμεν, ὕδατι δʼ ὕδωρ,\n\n \n\nαἰθέρι δʼ αἰθέρα δῖον, ἀτὰρ πυρὶ πῦρ ἀΐδηλον,\n\n \n\nστοργὴν δὲ στοργῇ, νεῖκος δέ τε νείκεϊ λυγρῷ.\n\n  Empedocles Fr. 109 ἀλλʼ ὅθεν δὴ ὁ λόγος, τοῦτό γε φανερόν, ὅτι συμβαίνει αὐτῷ τὸ νεῖκος μηθὲν μᾶλλον φθορᾶς ἢ τοῦ εἶναι αἴτιον· ὁμοίως δʼ οὐδʼ ἡ φιλότης τοῦ εἶναι, συνάγουσα γὰρ εἰς τὸ ἓν φθείρει τὰ ἄλλα. καὶ ἅμα δὲ αὐτῆς τῆς μεταβολῆς αἴτιον οὐθὲν λέγει ἀλλʼ ἢ ὅτι οὕτως πέφυκεν· \n\nἀλλʼ ὅτε δὴ μέγα νεῖκος ἐνὶ μελέεσσιν ἐθρέφθη,\n\n \n\nεἰς τιμάς τʼ ἀνόρουσε τελειομένοιο χρόνοιο\n\n \n\nὅς σφιν ἀμοιβαῖος πλατέος παρʼ ἐλήλαται ὅρκου·\n\n  Empedocles Fr. 30 ὡς ἀναγκαῖον μὲν ὂν μεταβάλλειν· αἰτίαν δὲ τῆς ἀνάγκης οὐδεμίαν δηλοῖ. ἀλλʼ ὅμως τοσοῦτόν γε μόνος λέγει ὁμολογουμένως· οὐ γὰρ τὰ μὲν φθαρτὰ τὰ δὲ ἄφθαρτα ποιεῖ τῶν ὄντων ἀλλὰ πάντα φθαρτὰ πλὴν τῶν στοιχείων. ἡ δὲ νῦν λεγομένη ἀπορία ἐστὶ διὰ τί τὰ μὲν τὰ δʼ οὔ, εἴπερ ἐκ τῶν αὐτῶν ἐστίν.—ὅτι μὲν οὖν οὐκ ἂν εἴησαν αἱ αὐταὶ ἀρχαί, τοσαῦτα εἰρήσθω· εἰ δὲ ἕτεραι ἀρχαί, μία μὲν ἀπορία πότερον ἄφθαρτοι καὶ αὗται ἔσονται ἢ φθαρταί· εἰ μὲν γὰρ φθαρταί, δῆλον ὡς ἀναγκαῖον καὶ ταύτας ἔκ τινων εἶναι (πάντα γὰρ φθείρεται εἰς ταῦτʼ ἐξ ὧν ἔστιν), ὥστε συμβαίνει τῶν ἀρχῶν ἑτέρας ἀρχὰς εἶναι προτέρας, τοῦτο δʼ ἀδύνατον, καὶ εἰ ἵσταται καὶ εἰ βαδίζει εἰς ἄπειρον· ἔτι δὲ πῶς ἔσται τὰ φθαρτά, εἰ αἱ ἀρχαὶ ἀναιρεθήσονται; εἰ δὲ ἄφθαρτοι, διὰ τί ἐκ μὲν τούτων ἀφθάρτων οὐσῶν φθαρτὰ ἔσται, ἐκ δὲ τῶν ἑτέρων ἄφθαρτα; τοῦτο γὰρ οὐκ εὔλογον, ἀλλʼ ἢ ἀδύνατον ἢ πολλοῦ λόγου δεῖται. ἔτι δὲ οὐδʼ ἐγκεχείρηκεν οὐδεὶς ἑτέρας, ἀλλὰ τὰς αὐτὰς ἁπάντων λέγουσιν ἀρχάς.  ἀλλὰ τὸ πρῶτον ἀπορηθὲν ἀποτρώγουσιν ὥσπερ τοῦτο μικρόν τι λαμβάνοντες.\n\n \n\nπάντων δὲ καὶ θεωρῆσαι χαλεπώτατον καὶ πρὸς τὸ γνῶναι τἀληθὲς ἀναγκαιότατον πότερόν ποτε τὸ ὂν καὶ τὸ ἓν οὐσίαι τῶν ὄντων εἰσί, καὶ ἑκάτερον αὐτῶν οὐχ ἕτερόν τι ὂν τὸ μὲν ἓν τὸ δὲ ὄν ἐστιν, ἢ δεῖ ζητεῖν τί ποτʼ ἐστὶ τὸ ὂν καὶ τὸ ἓν ὡς ὑποκειμένης ἄλλης φύσεως. οἱ μὲν γὰρ ἐκείνως οἱ δʼ οὕτως οἴονται τὴν φύσιν ἔχειν. Πλάτων μὲν γὰρ καὶ οἱ Πυθαγόρειοι οὐχ ἕτερόν τι τὸ ὂν οὐδὲ τὸ ἓν ἀλλὰ τοῦτο αὐτῶν τὴν φύσιν εἶναι, ὡς οὔσης τῆς οὐσίας αὐτοῦ τοῦ ἑνὶ εἶναι καὶ ὄντι· οἱ δὲ περὶ φύσεως, οἷον Ἐμπεδοκλῆς ὡς εἰς γνωριμώτερον ἀνάγων λέγει ὅ τι τὸ ἕν ἐστιν· δόξειε γὰρ ἂν λέγειν τοῦτο τὴν φιλίαν εἶναι (αἰτία γοῦν ἐστὶν αὕτη τοῦ ἓν εἶναι πᾶσιν), ἕτεροι δὲ πῦρ, οἱ δʼ ἀέρα φασὶν εἶναι τὸ ἓν τοῦτο καὶ τὸ ὄν, ἐξ οὗ τὰ ὄντα εἶναί τε καὶ γεγονέναι. ὣς δʼ αὔτως καὶ οἱ πλείω τὰ στοιχεῖα τιθέμενοι· ἀνάγκη γὰρ καὶ τούτοις τοσαῦτα λέγειν τὸ ἓν καὶ τὸ ὂν ὅσας περ ἀρχὰς εἶναί φασιν. συμβαίνει δέ, εἰ μέν τις μὴ θήσεται εἶναί τινα οὐσίαν τὸ ἓν καὶ τὸ ὄν, μηδὲ τῶν ἄλλων εἶναι τῶν καθόλου μηθέν (ταῦτα γάρ ἐστι καθόλου μάλιστα πάντων, εἰ δὲ μὴ ἔστι τι ἓν αὐτὸ μηδʼ αὐτὸ ὄν, σχολῇ τῶν γε ἄλλων τι ἂν εἴη παρὰ τὰ λεγόμενα καθʼ ἕκαστα), ἔτι δὲ μὴ ὄντος τοῦ ἑνὸς οὐσίας, δῆλον ὅτι οὐδʼ ἂν ἀριθμὸς εἴη ὡς κεχωρισμένη τις φύσις τῶν ὄντων (ὁ μὲν γὰρ ἀριθμὸς μονάδες, ἡ δὲ μονὰς ὅπερ ἕν τί ἐστιν)· εἰ δʼ ἔστι τι αὐτὸ ἓν καὶ ὄν, ἀναγκαῖον οὐσίαν αὐτῶν εἶναι τὸ ἓν καὶ τὸ ὄν· οὐ γὰρ ἕτερόν τι καθόλου κατηγορεῖται ἀλλὰ ταῦτα αὐτά.—ἀλλὰ μὴν εἴ γʼ ἔσται τι αὐτὸ ὂν καὶ αὐτὸ ἕν, πολλὴ ἀπορία πῶς ἔσται τι παρὰ ταῦτα ἕτερον, λέγω δὲ πῶς ἔσται πλείω ἑνὸς τὰ ὄντα. τὸ γὰρ ἕτερον τοῦ ὄντος οὐκ ἔστιν, ὥστε κατὰ τὸν Παρμενίδου συμβαίνειν ἀνάγκη λόγον ἓν ἅπαντα εἶναι τὰ ὄντα καὶ τοῦτο εἶναι τὸ ὄν.  ἀμφοτέρως δὲ δύσκολον· ἄν τε γὰρ μὴ ᾖ τὸ ἓν οὐσία ἄν τε ᾖ τὸ αὐτὸ ἕν, ἀδύνατον τὸν ἀριθμὸν οὐσίαν εἶναι. ἐὰν μὲν οὖν μὴ ᾖ, εἴρηται πρότερον διʼ ὅ· ἐὰν δὲ ᾖ, ἡ αὐτὴ ἀπορία καὶ περὶ τοῦ ὄντος. ἐκ τίνος γὰρ παρὰ τὸ ἓν ἔσται αὐτὸ ἄλλο ἕν; ἀνάγκη γὰρ μὴ ἓν εἶναι· ἅπαντα δὲ τὰ ὄντα ἢ ἓν ἢ πολλὰ ὧν ἓν ἕκαστον. ἔτι εἰ ἀδιαίρετον αὐτὸ τὸ ἕν, κατὰ μὲν τὸ Ζήνωνος ἀξίωμα οὐθὲν ἂν εἴη (ὃ γὰρ μήτε προστιθέμενον μήτε ἀφαιρούμενον ποιεῖ μεῖζον μηδὲ ἔλαττον, οὔ φησιν εἶναι τοῦτο τῶν ὄντων, ὡς δηλονότι ὄντος μεγέθους τοῦ ὄντος· καὶ εἰ μέγεθος, σωματικόν· τοῦτο γὰρ πάντῃ ὄν· τὰ δὲ ἄλλα πὼς μὲν προστιθέμενα ποιήσει μεῖζον, πὼς δʼ οὐθέν, οἷον ἐπίπεδον καὶ γραμμή, στιγμὴ δὲ καὶ μονὰς οὐδαμῶς)· ἀλλʼ ἐπειδὴ οὗτος θεωρεῖ φορτικῶς, καὶ ἐνδέχεται εἶναι ἀδιαίρετόν τι ὥστε  καὶ πρὸς ἐκεῖνόν τινʼ ἀπολογίαν ἔχειν (μεῖζον μὲν γὰρ οὐ ποιήσει πλεῖον δὲ προστιθέμενον τὸ τοιοῦτον)·—ἀλλὰ πῶς δὴ ἐξ ἑνὸς τοιούτου ἢ πλειόνων τοιούτων ἔσται μέγεθος; ὅμοιον γὰρ καὶ τὴν γραμμὴν ἐκ στιγμῶν εἶναι φάσκειν. ἀλλὰ μὴν καὶ εἴ τις οὕτως ὑπολαμβάνει ὥστε γενέσθαι, καθάπερ λέγουσί τινες, ἐκ τοῦ ἑνὸς αὐτοῦ καὶ ἄλλου μὴ ἑνός τινος τὸν ἀριθμόν, οὐθὲν ἧττον ζητητέον διὰ τί καὶ πῶς ὁτὲ μὲν ἀριθμὸς ὁτὲ δὲ μέγεθος ἔσται τὸ γενόμενον, εἴπερ τὸ μὴ ἓν ἡ ἀνισότης καὶ ἡ αὐτὴ φύσις ἦν. οὔτε γὰρ ὅπως ἐξ ἑνὸς καὶ ταύτης οὔτε ὅπως ἐξ ἀριθμοῦ τινὸς καὶ ταύτης γένοιτʼ ἂν τὰ μεγέθη, δῆλον. \n\n\n\n \n\n \n\nτούτων δʼ ἐχομένη ἀπορία πότερον οἱ ἀριθμοὶ καὶ τὰ σώματα καὶ τὰ ἐπίπεδα καὶ αἱ στιγμαὶ οὐσίαι τινές εἰσιν ἢ οὔ. εἰ μὲν γὰρ μή εἰσιν, διαφεύγει τί τὸ ὂν καὶ τίνες αἱ οὐσίαι τῶν ὄντων· τὰ μὲν γὰρ πάθη καὶ αἱ κινήσεις καὶ τὰ πρός τι καὶ αἱ διαθέσεις καὶ οἱ λόγοι οὐθενὸς δοκοῦσιν οὐσίαν σημαίνειν (λέγονται γὰρ πάντα καθʼ ὑποκειμένου τινός, καὶ οὐθὲν τόδε τι)· ἃ δὲ μάλιστʼ ἂν δόξειε σημαίνειν οὐσίαν, ὕδωρ καὶ γῆ καὶ πῦρ καὶ ἀήρ, ἐξ ὧν τὰ σύνθετα σώματα συνέστηκε,  τούτων θερμότητες μὲν καὶ ψυχρότητες καὶ τὰ τοιαῦτα πάθη, οὐκ οὐσίαι, τὸ δὲ σῶμα τὸ ταῦτα πεπονθὸς μόνον ὑπομένει ὡς ὄν τι καὶ οὐσία τις οὖσα. ἀλλὰ μὴν τό γε σῶμα ἧττον οὐσία τῆς ἐπιφανείας, καὶ αὕτη τῆς γραμμῆς, καὶ αὕτη τῆς μονάδος καὶ τῆς στιγμῆς· τούτοις γὰρ ὥρισται τὸ σῶμα, καὶ τὰ μὲν ἄνευ σώματος ἐνδέχεσθαι δοκεῖ εἶναι τὸ δὲ σῶμα ἄνευ τούτων ἀδύνατον. διόπερ οἱ μὲν πολλοὶ καὶ οἱ πρότερον τὴν οὐσίαν καὶ τὸ ὂν ᾤοντο τὸ σῶμα εἶναι τὰ δὲ ἄλλα τούτου πάθη, ὥστε καὶ τὰς ἀρχὰς τὰς τῶν σωμάτων τῶν ὄντων εἶναι ἀρχάς· οἱ δʼ ὕστεροι καὶ σοφώτεροι τούτων εἶναι δόξαντες ἀριθμούς. καθάπερ οὖν εἴπομεν, εἰ μὴ ἔστιν οὐσία ταῦτα, ὅλως οὐδὲν ἐστὶν οὐσία οὐδὲ ὂν οὐθέν· οὐ γὰρ δὴ τά γε συμβεβηκότα τούτοις ἄξιον ὄντα καλεῖν. —ἀλλὰ μὴν εἰ τοῦτο μὲν ὁμολογεῖται, ὅτι μᾶλλον οὐσία τὰ μήκη τῶν σωμάτων καὶ αἱ στιγμαί, ταῦτα δὲ μὴ ὁρῶμεν ποίων ἂν εἶεν σωμάτων (ἐν γὰρ τοῖς αἰσθητοῖς ἀδύνατον εἶναι), οὐκ ἂν εἴη οὐσία οὐδεμία. ἔτι δὲ φαίνεται ταῦτα πάντα διαιρέσεις ὄντα τοῦ σώματος, τὸ μὲν εἰς πλάτος τὸ δʼ εἰς βάθος τὸ δʼ εἰς μῆκος. πρὸς δὲ τούτοις ὁμοίως ἔνεστιν ἐν τῷ στερεῷ ὁποιονοῦν σχῆμα· ὥστʼ εἰ μηδʼ ἐν τῷ λίθῳ Ἑρμῆς, οὐδὲ τὸ ἥμισυ τοῦ κύβου ἐν τῷ κύβῳ οὕτως ὡς ἀφωρισμένον· οὐκ ἄρα οὐδʼ ἐπιφάνεια (εἰ γὰρ ὁποιαοῦν, κἂν αὕτη ἂν ἦν ἡ ἀφορίζουσα τὸ ἥμισυ), ὁ δʼ αὐτὸς λόγος καὶ ἐπὶ γραμμῆς καὶ στιγμῆς καὶ μονάδος, ὥστʼ εἰ μάλιστα μὲν οὐσία τὸ σῶμα, τούτου δὲ μᾶλλον ταῦτα, μὴ ἔστι δὲ ταῦτα μηδὲ οὐσίαι τινές, διαφεύγει τί τὸ ὂν καὶ τίς ἡ οὐσία τῶν ὄντων. πρὸς γὰρ τοῖς εἰρημένοις καὶ τὰ περὶ τὴν γένεσιν καὶ τὴν φθορὰν συμβαίνει ἄλογα. δοκεῖ μὲν γὰρ ἡ οὐσία, ἐὰν μὴ οὖσα πρότερον νῦν ᾖ ἢ πρότερον οὖσα ὕστερον μὴ ᾖ, μετὰ τοῦ γίγνεσθαι καὶ φθείρεσθαι ταῦτα πάσχειν· τὰς δὲ στιγμὰς καὶ τὰς γραμμὰς καὶ τὰς ἐπιφανείας οὐκ ἐνδέχεται οὔτε γίγνεσθαι οὔτε φθείρεσθαι, ὁτὲ μὲν οὔσας ὁτὲ δὲ οὐκ οὔσας. ὅταν γὰρ ἅπτηται ἢ διαιρῆται τὰ σώματα,  ἅμα ὁτὲ μὲν μία ἁπτομένων ὁτὲ δὲ δύο διαιρουμένων γίγνονται· ὥστʼ οὔτε συγκειμένων ἔστιν ἀλλʼ ἔφθαρται, διῃρημένων τε εἰσὶν αἱ πρότερον οὐκ οὖσαι (οὐ γὰρ δὴ ἥ γʼ ἀδιαίρετος στιγμὴ διῃρέθη εἰς δύο), εἴ τε γίγνονται καὶ φθείρονται, ἐκ τίνος γίγνονται; παραπλησίως δʼ ἔχει καὶ περὶ τὸ νῦν τὸ ἐν τῷ χρόνῳ· οὐδὲ γὰρ τοῦτο ἐνδέχεται γίγνεσθαι καὶ φθείρεσθαι, ἀλλʼ ὅμως ἕτερον ἀεὶ δοκεῖ εἶναι, οὐκ οὐσία τις οὖσα. ὁμοίως δὲ δῆλον ὅτι ἔχει καὶ περὶ τὰς στιγμὰς καὶ τὰς γραμμὰς καὶ τὰ ἐπίπεδα· ὁ γὰρ αὐτὸς λόγος· ἅπαντα γὰρ ὁμοίως ἢ πέρατα ἢ διαιρέσεις εἰσίν.\n\n\n\n \n\n \n\nὅλως δʼ ἀπορήσειεν ἄν τις διὰ τί καὶ δεῖ ζητεῖν ἄλλʼ ἄττα παρά τε τὰ αἰσθητὰ καὶ τὰ μεταξύ, οἷον ἃ τίθεμεν εἴδη. εἰ γὰρ διὰ τοῦτο, ὅτι τὰ μὲν μαθηματικὰ τῶν δεῦρο ἄλλῳ μέν τινι διαφέρει, τῷ δὲ πόλλʼ ἄττα ὁμοειδῆ εἶναι οὐθὲν διαφέρει, ὥστʼ οὐκ ἔσονται αὐτῶν αἱ ἀρχαὶ ἀριθμῷ ἀφωρισμέναι (ὥσπερ οὐδὲ τῶν ἐνταῦθα γραμμάτων ἀριθμῷ μὲν πάντων οὐκ εἰσὶν αἱ ἀρχαὶ ὡρισμέναι, εἴδει δέ, ἐὰν μὴ λαμβάνῃ τις τησδὶ τῆς συλλαβῆς ἢ τησδὶ τῆς φωνῆς· τούτων δʼ ἔσονται καὶ ἀριθμῷ ὡρισμέναι—ὁμοίως δὲ καὶ ἐπὶ τῶν μεταξύ· ἄπειρα γὰρ κἀκεῖ τὰ ὁμοειδῆ), ὥστʼ εἰ μὴ ἔστι παρὰ τὰ αἰσθητὰ καὶ τὰ μαθηματικὰ ἕτερʼ ἄττα οἷα λέγουσι τὰ εἴδη τινές, οὐκ ἔσται μία ἀριθμῷ ἀλλʼ εἴδει οὐσία, οὐδʼ αἱ ἀρχαὶ τῶν ὄντων ἀριθμῷ ἔσονται ποσαί τινες ἀλλὰ εἴδει·—εἰ οὖν τοῦτο ἀναγκαῖον, καὶ τὰ εἴδη ἀναγκαῖον διὰ τοῦτο εἶναι τιθέναι. καὶ γὰρ εἰ μὴ καλῶς διαρθροῦσιν οἱ λέγοντες, ἀλλʼ ἔστι γε τοῦθʼ ὃ βούλονται, καὶ ἀνάγκη ταῦτα λέγειν αὐτοῖς, ὅτι τῶν εἰδῶν οὐσία τις ἕκαστόν ἐστι καὶ οὐθὲν κατὰ συμβεβηκός.—ἀλλὰ μὴν εἴ γε θήσομεν τά τε εἴδη εἶναι καὶ ἓν ἀριθμῷ τὰς ἀρχὰς ἀλλὰ μὴ εἴδει, εἰρήκαμεν ἃ συμβαίνειν ἀναγκαῖον ἀδύνατα.—σύνεγγυς δὲ τούτων ἐστὶ τὸ διαπορῆσαι πότερον δυνάμει ἔστι τὰ στοιχεῖα ἤ τινʼ ἕτερον τρόπον. εἰ μὲν γὰρ ἄλλως πως, πρότερόν τι ἔσται τῶν ἀρχῶν ἄλλο (πρότερον  γὰρ ἡ δύναμις ἐκείνης τῆς αἰτίας, τὸ δὲ δυνατὸν οὐκ ἀναγκαῖον ἐκείνως πᾶν ἔχειν)· εἰ δʼ ἔστι δυνάμει τὰ στοιχεῖα, ἐνδέχεται μηθὲν εἶναι τῶν ὄντων· δυνατὸν γὰρ εἶναι καὶ τὸ μήπω ὄν· γίγνεται μὲν γὰρ τὸ μὴ ὄν, οὐθὲν δὲ γίγνεται τῶν εἶναι ἀδυνάτων.—ταύτας τε οὖν τὰς ἀπορίας ἀναγκαῖον ἀπορῆσαι περὶ τῶν ἀρχῶν, καὶ πότερον καθόλου εἰσὶν ἢ ὡς λέγομεν τὰ καθʼ ἕκαστα. εἰ μὲν γὰρ καθόλου, οὐκ ἔσονται οὐσίαι (οὐθὲν γὰρ τῶν κοινῶν τόδε τι σημαίνει ἀλλὰ τοιόνδε, ἡ δʼ οὐσία τόδε τι· εἰ δʼ ἔσται τόδε τι καὶ ἓν θέσθαι τὸ κοινῇ κατηγορούμενον, πολλὰ ἔσται ζῷα ὁ Σωκράτης, αὐτός τε καὶ ὁ ἄνθρωπος καὶ τὸ ζῷον, εἴπερ σημαίνει ἕκαστον τόδε τι καὶ ἕν)·—εἰ μὲν οὖν καθόλου αἱ ἀρχαί, ταῦτα συμβαίνει· εἰ δὲ μὴ καθόλου ἀλλʼ ὡς τὰ καθʼ ἕκαστα, οὐκ ἔσονται ἐπιστηταί (καθόλου γὰρ ἡ ἐπιστήμη πάντων), ὥστʼ ἔσονται ἀρχαὶ ἕτεραι πρότεραι τῶν ἀρχῶν αἱ καθόλου κατηγορούμεναι, ἄνπερ μέλλῃ ἔσεσθαι αὐτῶν ἐπιστήμη.","paragraphs":[{"id":"RAW_DAZY_BOO3:P0001","text":"ἀνάγκη πρὸς τὴν ἐπιζητουμένην ἐπιστήμην ἐπελθεῖν ἡμᾶς πρῶτον περὶ ὧν ἀπορῆσαι δεῖ πρῶτον· ταῦτα δʼ ἐστὶν ὅσα τε περὶ αὐτῶν ἄλλως ὑπειλήφασί τινες, κἂν εἴ τι χωρὶς τούτων τυγχάνει παρεωραμένον. ἔστι δὲ τοῖς εὐπορῆσαι βουλομένοις προὔργου τὸ διαπορῆσαι καλῶς· ἡ γὰρ ὕστερον εὐπορία λύσις τῶν πρότερον ἀπορουμένων ἐστί, λύειν δʼ οὐκ ἔστιν ἀγνοοῦντας τὸν δεσμόν, ἀλλʼ ἡ τῆς διανοίας ἀπορία δηλοῖ τοῦτο περὶ τοῦ πράγματος· ᾗ γὰρ ἀπορεῖ, ταύτῃ παραπλήσιον πέπονθε τοῖς δεδεμένοις· ἀδύνατον γὰρ ἀμφοτέρως προελθεῖν εἰς τὸ πρόσθεν. διὸ δεῖ τὰς δυσχερείας τεθεωρηκέναι πάσας πρότερον, τούτων τε χάριν καὶ διὰ τὸ τοὺς ζητοῦντας ἄνευ τοῦ διαπορῆσαι πρῶτον ὁμοίους εἶναι τοῖς ποῖ δεῖ βαδίζειν ἀγνοοῦσι, καὶ πρὸς τούτοις οὐδʼ εἴ ποτε τὸ ζητούμενον εὕρηκεν ἢ μὴ γιγνώσκειν·  τὸ γὰρ τέλος τούτῳ μὲν οὐ δῆλον τῷ δὲ προηπορηκότι δῆλον.","kind":"passage"},{"id":"RAW_DAZY_BOO3:P0002","text":"ἔτι δὲ βέλτιον ἀνάγκη ἔχειν πρὸς τὸ κρῖναι τὸν ὥσπερ ἀντιδίκων καὶ τῶν ἀμφισβητούντων λόγων ἀκηκοότα πάντων.—ἔστι δʼ ἀπορία πρώτη μὲν περὶ ὧν ἐν τοῖς πεφροιμιασμένοις διηπορήσαμεν, πότερον μιᾶς ἢ πολλῶν ἐπιστημῶν θεωρῆσαι τὰς αἰτίας· καὶ πότερον τὰς τῆς οὐσίας ἀρχὰς τὰς πρώτας ἐστὶ τῆς ἐπιστήμης ἰδεῖν μόνον ἢ καὶ περὶ τῶν ἀρχῶν ἐξ ὧν δεικνύουσι πάντες, οἷον πότερον ἐνδέχεται ταὐτὸ καὶ ἓν ἅμα φάναι καὶ ἀποφάναι ἢ οὔ, καὶ περὶ τῶν ἄλλων τῶν τοιούτων· εἴ τʼ ἐστι περὶ τὴν οὐσίαν, πότερον μία περὶ πάσας ἢ πλείονές εἰσι, κἂν εἰ πλείονες πότερον ἅπασαι συγγενεῖς ἢ τὰς μὲν σοφίας τὰς δὲ ἄλλο τι λεκτέον αὐτῶν. καὶ τοῦτο δʼ αὐτὸ τῶν ἀναγκαίων ἐστὶ ζητῆσαι, πότερον τὰς αἰσθητὰς οὐσίας εἶναι μόνον φατέον ἢ καὶ παρὰ ταύτας ἄλλας, καὶ πότερον μοναχῶς ἢ πλείονα γένη τῶν οὐσιῶν, οἷον οἱ ποιοῦντες τά τε εἴδη καὶ τὰ μαθηματικὰ μεταξὺ τούτων τε καὶ τῶν αἰσθητῶν.","kind":"passage"},{"id":"RAW_DAZY_BOO3:P0003","text":"περί τε τούτων οὖν, καθάπερ φαμέν, ἐπισκεπτέον, καὶ πότερον περὶ τὰς οὐσίας ἡ θεωρία μόνον ἐστὶν ἢ καὶ περὶ τὰ συμβεβηκότα καθʼ αὑτὰ ταῖς οὐσίαις, πρὸς δὲ τούτοις περὶ ταὐτοῦ καὶ ἑτέρου καὶ ὁμοίου καὶ ἀνομοίου καὶ ἐναντιότητος, καὶ περὶ προτέρου καὶ ὑστέρου καὶ τῶν ἄλλων ἁπάντων τῶν τοιούτων περὶ ὅσων οἱ διαλεκτικοὶ πειρῶνται σκοπεῖν ἐκ τῶν ἐνδόξων μόνων ποιούμενοι τὴν σκέψιν, τίνος ἐστὶ θεωρῆσαι περὶ πάντων· ἔτι δὲ τούτοις αὐτοῖς ὅσα καθʼ αὑτὰ συμβέβηκεν, καὶ μὴ μόνον τί ἐστι τούτων ἕκαστον ἀλλὰ καὶ ἆρα ἓν ἑνὶ ἐναντίον· καὶ πότερον αἱ ἀρχαὶ καὶ τὰ στοιχεῖα τὰ γένη ἐστὶν ἢ εἰς ἃ διαιρεῖται ἐνυπάρχοντα ἕκαστον· καὶ εἰ τὰ γένη, πότερον ὅσα ἐπὶ τοῖς ἀτόμοις λέγεται  τελευταῖα ἢ τὰ πρῶτα, οἷον πότερον ζῷον ἢ ἄνθρωπος ἀρχή τε καὶ μᾶλλον ἔστι παρὰ τὸ καθʼ ἕκαστον.","kind":"passage"},{"id":"RAW_DAZY_BOO3:P0004","text":"μάλιστα δὲ ζητητέον καὶ πραγματευτέον πότερον ἔστι τι παρὰ τὴν ὕλην αἴτιον καθʼ αὑτὸ ἢ οὔ, καὶ τοῦτο χωριστὸν ἢ οὔ, καὶ πότερον ἓν ἢ πλείω τὸν ἀριθμόν, καὶ πότερον ἔστι τι παρὰ τὸ σύνολον (λέγω δὲ τὸ σύνολον, ὅταν κατηγορηθῇ τι τῆς ὕλης) ἢ οὐθέν, ἢ τῶν μὲν τῶν δʼ οὔ, καὶ ποῖα τοιαῦτα τῶν ὄντων.","kind":"passage"},{"id":"RAW_DAZY_BOO3:P0005","text":"ἔτι αἱ ἀρχαὶ πότερον ἀριθμῷ ἢ εἴδει ὡρισμέναι, καὶ αἱ ἐν τοῖς λόγοις καὶ αἱ ἐν τῷ ὑποκειμένῳ; καὶ πότερον τῶν φθαρτῶν καὶ ἀφθάρτων αἱ αὐταὶ ἢ ἕτεραι, καὶ πότερον ἄφθαρτοι πᾶσαι ἢ τῶν φθαρτῶν φθαρταί; ἔτι δὲ τὸ πάντων χαλεπώτατον καὶ πλείστην ἀπορίαν ἔχον, πότερον τὸ ἓν καὶ τὸ ὄν, καθάπερ οἱ Πυθαγόρειοι καὶ Πλάτων ἔλεγεν, οὐχ ἕτερόν τί ἐστιν ἀλλʼ οὐσία τῶν ὄντων; ἢ οὔ, ἀλλʼ ἕτερόν τι τὸ ὑποκείμενον, ὥσπερ Ἐμπεδοκλῆς φησὶ φιλίαν ἄλλος δέ τις πῦρ ὁ δὲ ὕδωρ ἢ ἀέρα· καὶ πότερον αἱ ἀρχαὶ καθόλου εἰσὶν ἢ ὡς τὰ καθʼ ἕκαστα τῶν πραγμάτων, καὶ δυνάμει ἢ ἐνεργείᾳ· ἔτι πότερον ἄλλως ἢ κατὰ κίνησιν· καὶ γὰρ ταῦτα ἀπορίαν ἂν παράσχοι πολλήν.","kind":"passage"},{"id":"RAW_DAZY_BOO3:P0006","text":"πρὸς δὲ τούτοις πότερον οἱ ἀριθμοὶ καὶ τὰ μήκη καὶ τὰ σχήματα καὶ αἱ στιγμαὶ οὐσίαι τινές εἰσιν ἢ οὔ, κἂν εἰ οὐσίαι πότερον κεχωρισμέναι τῶν αἰσθητῶν ἢ ἐνυπάρχουσαι ἐν τούτοις; περὶ γὰρ τούτων ἁπάντων οὐ μόνον χαλεπὸν τὸ εὐπορῆσαι τῆς ἀληθείας ἀλλʼ οὐδὲ τὸ διαπορῆσαι τῷ λόγῳ ῥᾴδιον καλῶς.","kind":"passage"},{"id":"RAW_DAZY_BOO3:P0007","text":"πρῶτον μὲν οὖν περὶ ὧν πρῶτον εἴπομεν, πότερον μιᾶς ἢ πλειόνων ἐστὶν ἐπιστημῶν θεωρῆσαι πάντα τὰ γένη τῶν αἰτίων. μιᾶς μὲν γὰρ ἐπιστήμης πῶς ἂν εἴη μὴ ἐναντίας οὔσας τὰς ἀρχὰς γνωρίζειν; ἔτι δὲ πολλοῖς τῶν ὄντων οὐχ ὑπάρχουσι πᾶσαι· τίνα γὰρ τρόπον οἷόν τε κινήσεως ἀρχὴν εἶναι τοῖς ἀκινήτοις ἢ τὴν τἀγαθοῦ φύσιν, εἴπερ ἅπαν ὃ ἂν ᾖ ἀγαθὸν καθʼ αὑτὸ καὶ διὰ τὴν αὑτοῦ φύσιν τέλος ἐστὶν καὶ οὕτως αἴτιον ὅτι ἐκείνου ἕνεκα καὶ γίγνεται καὶ ἔστι τἆλλα, τὸ δὲ τέλος καὶ τὸ οὗ ἕνεκα πράξεώς τινός ἐστι τέλος, αἱ δὲ πράξεις πᾶσαι μετὰ κινήσεως; ὥστʼ ἐν τοῖς ἀκινήτοις οὐκ ἂν ἐνδέχοιτο ταύτην εἶναι τὴν ἀρχὴν οὐδʼ εἶναί τι αὐτοαγαθόν.","kind":"passage"},{"id":"RAW_DAZY_BOO3:P0008","text":"διὸ καὶ ἐν τοῖς μαθήμασιν οὐθὲν δείκνυται διὰ ταύτης τῆς αἰτίας, οὐδʼ ἔστιν ἀπόδειξις οὐδεμία διότι βέλτιον ἢ χεῖρον, ἀλλʼ οὐδὲ τὸ παράπαν μέμνηται οὐθεὶς οὐθενὸς τῶν τοιούτων, ὥστε διὰ ταῦτα τῶν σοφιστῶν τινὲς οἷον Ἀρίστιππος προεπηλάκιζεν αὐτάς· ἐν μὲν γὰρ ταῖς ἄλλαις τέχναις, καὶ ταῖς βαναύσοις, οἷον ἐν τεκτονικῇ καὶ σκυτικῇ, διότι βέλτιον ἢ χεῖρον λέγεσθαι πάντα, τὰς δὲ μαθηματικὰς οὐθένα ποιεῖσθαι λόγον περὶ ἀγαθῶν καὶ κακῶν.  —ἀλλὰ μὴν εἴ γε πλείους ἐπιστῆμαι τῶν αἰτίων εἰσὶ καὶ ἑτέρα ἑτέρας ἀρχῆς, τίνα τούτων φατέον εἶναι τὴν ζητουμένην, ἢ τίνα μάλιστα τοῦ πράγματος τοῦ ζητουμένου ἐπιστήμονα τῶν ἐχόντων αὐτάς; ἐνδέχεται γὰρ τῷ αὐτῷ πάντας τοὺς τρόπους τοὺς τῶν αἰτίων ὑπάρχειν, οἷον οἰκίας ὅθεν μὲν ἡ κίνησις ἡ τέχνη καὶ ὁ οἰκοδόμος, οὗ δʼ ἕνεκα τὸ ἔργον, ὕλη δὲ γῆ καὶ λίθοι, τὸ δʼ εἶδος ὁ λόγος.","kind":"passage"},{"id":"RAW_DAZY_BOO3:P0009","text":"ἐκ μὲν οὖν τῶν πάλαι διωρισμένων τίνα χρὴ καλεῖν τῶν ἐπιστημῶν σοφίαν ἔχει λόγον ἑκάστην προσαγορεύειν· ᾗ μὲν γὰρ ἀρχικωτάτη καὶ ἡγεμονικωτάτη καὶ ᾗ ὥσπερ δούλας οὐδʼ ἀντειπεῖν τὰς ἄλλας ἐπιστήμας δίκαιον, ἡ τοῦ τέλους καὶ τἀγαθοῦ τοιαύτη (τούτου γὰρ ἕνεκα τἆλλα), ᾗ δὲ τῶν πρώτων αἰτίων καὶ τοῦ μάλιστα ἐπιστητοῦ διωρίσθη εἶναι, ἡ τῆς οὐσίας ἂν εἴη τοιαύτη· πολλαχῶς γὰρ ἐπισταμένων τὸ αὐτὸ μᾶλλον μὲν εἰδέναι φαμὲν τὸν τῷ εἶναι γνωρίζοντα τί τὸ πρᾶγμα ἢ τῷ μὴ εἶναι, αὐτῶν δὲ τούτων ἕτερον ἑτέρου μᾶλλον, καὶ μάλιστα τὸν τί ἐστιν ἀλλʼ οὐ τὸν πόσον ἢ ποῖον ἢ τί ποιεῖν ἢ πάσχειν πέφυκεν.","kind":"passage"},{"id":"RAW_DAZY_BOO3:P0010","text":"ἔτι δὲ καὶ ἐν τοῖς ἄλλοις τὸ εἰδέναι ἕκαστον καὶ ὧν ἀποδείξεις εἰσί, τότʼ οἰόμεθα ὑπάρχειν ὅταν εἰδῶμεν τί ἐστιν (οἷον τί ἐστι τὸ τετραγωνίζειν, ὅτι μέσης εὕρεσις· ὁμοίως δὲ καὶ ἐπὶ τῶν ἄλλων), περὶ δὲ τὰς γενέσεις καὶ τὰς πράξεις καὶ περὶ πᾶσαν μεταβολὴν ὅταν εἰδῶμεν τὴν ἀρχὴν τῆς κινήσεως· τοῦτο δʼ ἕτερον καὶ ἀντικείμενον τῷ τέλει, ὥστʼ ἄλλης ἂν δόξειεν ἐπιστήμης εἶναι τὸ θεωρῆσαι τῶν αἰτίων τούτων ἕκαστον.—ἀλλὰ μὴν καὶ περὶ τῶν ἀποδεικτικῶν ἀρχῶν, πότερον μιᾶς ἐστὶν ἐπιστήμης ἢ πλειόνων, ἀμφισβητήσιμόν ἐστιν (λέγω δὲ ἀποδεικτικὰς τὰς κοινὰς δόξας ἐξ ὧν ἅπαντες δεικνύουσιν) οἷον ὅτι πᾶν ἀναγκαῖον ἢ φάναι ἢ ἀποφάναι, καὶ ἀδύνατον ἅμα εἶναι καὶ μὴ εἶναι, καὶ ὅσαι ἄλλαι τοιαῦται προτάσεις, πότερον μία τούτων ἐπιστήμη καὶ τῆς οὐσίας ἢ ἑτέρα, κἂν εἰ μὴ μία, ποτέραν χρὴ προσαγορεύειν τὴν ζητουμένην νῦν.","kind":"passage"},{"id":"RAW_DAZY_BOO3:P0011","text":"μιᾶς μὲν οὖν οὐκ εὔλογον εἶναι· τί γὰρ μᾶλλον γεωμετρίας ἢ ὁποιασοῦν περὶ τούτων ἐστὶν ἴδιον τὸ ἐπαΐειν; εἴπερ οὖν ὁμοίως μὲν ὁποιασοῦν ἐστίν, ἁπασῶν δὲ μὴ ἐνδέχεται,  ὥσπερ οὐδὲ τῶν ἄλλων οὕτως οὐδὲ τῆς γνωριζούσης τὰς οὐσίας ἴδιόν ἐστι τὸ γιγνώσκειν περὶ αὐτῶν.","kind":"passage"},{"id":"RAW_DAZY_BOO3:P0012","text":"ἅμα δὲ καὶ τίνα τρόπον ἔσται αὐτῶν ἐπιστήμη; τί μὲν γὰρ ἕκαστον τούτων τυγχάνει ὂν καὶ νῦν γνωρίζομεν (χρῶνται γοῦν ὡς γιγνωσκομένοις αὐτοῖς καὶ ἄλλαι τέχναι)· εἰ δὲ ἀποδεικτικὴ περὶ αὐτῶν ἐστί, δεήσει τι γένος εἶναι ὑποκείμενον καὶ τὰ μὲν πάθη τὰ δʼ ἀξιώματʼ αὐτῶν (περὶ πάντων γὰρ ἀδύνατον ἀπόδειξιν εἶναι), ἀνάγκη γὰρ ἔκ τινων εἶναι καὶ περί τι καὶ τινῶν τὴν ἀπόδειξιν· ὥστε συμβαίνει πάντων εἶναι γένος ἕν τι τῶν δεικνυμένων, πᾶσαι γὰρ αἱ ἀποδεικτικαὶ χρῶνται τοῖς ἀξιώμασιν.—ἀλλὰ μὴν εἰ ἑτέρα ἡ τῆς οὐσίας καὶ ἡ περὶ τούτων, ποτέρα κυριωτέρα καὶ προτέρα πέφυκεν αὐτῶν; καθόλου γὰρ μάλιστα καὶ πάντων ἀρχαὶ τὰ ἀξιώματά ἐστιν, εἴ τʼ ἐστὶ μὴ τοῦ φιλοσόφου, τίνος ἔσται περὶ αὐτῶν ἄλλου τὸ θεωρῆσαι τὸ ἀληθὲς καὶ ψεῦδος;—ὅλως τε τῶν οὐσιῶν πότερον μία πασῶν ἐστὶν ἢ πλείους ἐπιστῆμαι; εἰ μὲν οὖν μὴ μία, ποίας οὐσίας θετέον τὴν ἐπιστήμην ταύτην; τὸ δὲ μίαν πασῶν οὐκ εὔλογον· καὶ γὰρ ἂν ἀποδεικτικὴ","kind":"passage"},{"id":"RAW_DAZY_BOO3:P0013","text":"μία περὶ πάντων εἴη τῶν συμβεβηκότων, εἴπερ πᾶσα ἀποδεικτικὴ περί τι ὑποκείμενον θεωρεῖ τὰ καθʼ αὑτὰ συμβεβηκότα ἐκ τῶν κοινῶν δοξῶν. περὶ οὖν τὸ αὐτὸ γένος τὰ συμβεβηκότα καθʼ αὑτὰ τῆς αὐτῆς ἐστὶ θεωρῆσαι ἐκ τῶν αὐτῶν δοξῶν. περί τε γὰρ ὃ μιᾶς καὶ ἐξ ὧν μιᾶς, εἴτε τῆς αὐτῆς εἴτε ἄλλης, ὥστε καὶ τὰ συμβεβηκότα, εἴθʼ αὗται θεωροῦσιν εἴτʼ ἐκ τούτων μία.—ἔτι δὲ πότερον περὶ τὰς οὐσίας μόνον ἡ θεωρία ἐστὶν ἢ καὶ περὶ τὰ συμβεβηκότα ταύταις; λέγω δʼ οἷον, εἰ τὸ στερεὸν οὐσία τίς ἐστι καὶ γραμμαὶ καὶ ἐπίπεδα, πότερον τῆς αὐτῆς ταῦτα γνωρίζειν ἐστὶν ἐπιστήμης καὶ τὰ συμβεβηκότα περὶ ἕκαστον γένος περὶ ὧν αἱ μαθηματικαὶ  δεικνύουσιν, ἢ ἄλλης.","kind":"passage"},{"id":"RAW_DAZY_BOO3:P0014","text":"εἰ μὲν γὰρ τῆς αὐτῆς, ἀποδεικτική τις ἂν εἴη καὶ ἡ τῆς οὐσίας, οὐ δοκεῖ δὲ τοῦ τί ἐστιν ἀπόδειξις εἶναι· εἰ δʼ ἑτέρας, τίς ἔσται ἡ θεωροῦσα περὶ τὴν οὐσίαν τὰ συμβεβηκότα; τοῦτο γὰρ ἀποδοῦναι παγχάλεπον.—ἔτι δὲ πότερον τὰς αἰσθητὰς οὐσίας μόνας εἶναι φατέον ἢ καὶ παρὰ ταύτας ἄλλας, καὶ πότερον μοναχῶς ἢ πλείω γένη τετύχηκεν ὄντα τῶν οὐσιῶν,  οἷον οἱ λέγοντες τά τε εἴδη καὶ τὰ μεταξύ, περὶ ἃ τὰς μαθηματικὰς εἶναί φασιν ἐπιστήμας; ὡς μὲν οὖν λέγομεν τὰ εἴδη αἴτιά τε καὶ οὐσίας εἶναι καθʼ ἑαυτὰς εἴρηται ἐν τοῖς πρώτοις λόγοις περὶ αὐτῶν· πολλαχῇ δὲ ἐχόντων δυσκολίαν, οὐθενὸς ἧττον ἄτοπον τὸ φάναι μὲν εἶναί τινας φύσεις παρὰ τὰς ἐν τῷ οὐρανῷ, ταύτας δὲ τὰς αὐτὰς φάναι τοῖς αἰσθητοῖς πλὴν ὅτι τὰ μὲν ἀΐδια τὰ δὲ φθαρτά.","kind":"passage"},{"id":"RAW_DAZY_BOO3:P0015","text":"αὐτὸ γὰρ ἄνθρωπόν φασιν εἶναι καὶ ἵππον καὶ ὑγίειαν, ἄλλο δʼ οὐδέν, παραπλήσιον ποιοῦντες τοῖς θεοὺς μὲν εἶναι φάσκουσιν ἀνθρωποειδεῖς δέ· οὔτε γὰρ ἐκεῖνοι οὐδὲν ἄλλο ἐποίουν ἢ ἀνθρώπους ἀϊδίους, οὔθʼ οὗτοι τὰ εἴδη ἄλλʼ ἢ αἰσθητὰ ἀΐδια. ἔτι δὲ εἴ τις παρὰ τὰ εἴδη καὶ τὰ αἰσθητὰ τὰ μεταξὺ θήσεται, πολλὰς ἀπορίας ἕξει· δῆλον γὰρ ὡς ὁμοίως γραμμαί τε παρά τʼ αὐτὰς καὶ τὰς αἰσθητὰς ἔσονται καὶ ἕκαστον τῶν ἄλλων γενῶν· ὥστʼ ἐπείπερ ἡ ἀστρολογία μία τούτων ἐστίν, ἔσται τις καὶ οὐρανὸς παρὰ τὸν αἰσθητὸν οὐρανὸν καὶ ἥλιός τε καὶ σελήνη καὶ τἆλλα ὁμοίως τὰ κατὰ τὸν οὐρανόν.","kind":"passage"},{"id":"RAW_DAZY_BOO3:P0016","text":"καίτοι πῶς δεῖ πιστεῦσαι τούτοις; οὐδὲ γὰρ ἀκίνητον εὔλογον εἶναι, κινούμενον δὲ καὶ παντελῶς ἀδύνατον· ὁμοίως δὲ καὶ περὶ ὧν ἡ ὀπτικὴ πραγματεύεται καὶ ἡ ἐν τοῖς μαθήμασιν ἁρμονική· καὶ γὰρ ταῦτα ἀδύνατον εἶναι παρὰ τὰ αἰσθητὰ διὰ τὰς αὐτὰς αἰτίας· εἰ γὰρ ἔστιν αἰσθητὰ μεταξὺ καὶ αἰσθήσεις, δῆλον ὅτι καὶ ζῷα ἔσονται μεταξὺ αὐτῶν τε καὶ τῶν φθαρτῶν. ἀπορήσειε δʼ ἄν τις καὶ περὶ ποῖα τῶν ὄντων δεῖ ζητεῖν ταύτας τὰς ἐπιστήμας. εἰ γὰρ τούτῳ διοίσει τῆς γεωδαισίας ἡ γεωμετρία μόνον, ὅτι ἡ μὲν τούτων ἐστὶν ὧν αἰσθανόμεθα ἡ δʼ οὐκ αἰσθητῶν, δῆλον ὅτι καὶ παρʼ ἰατρικὴν ἔσται τις ἐπιστήμη καὶ παρʼ ἑκάστην τῶν ἄλλων μεταξὺ αὐτῆς τε ἰατρικῆς καὶ τῆσδε τῆς ἰατρικῆς· καίτοι πῶς τοῦτο δυνατόν; καὶ γὰρ ἂν ὑγιείνʼ ἄττα εἴη παρὰ τὰ αἰσθητὰ καὶ αὐτὸ τὸ ὑγιεινόν.","kind":"passage"},{"id":"RAW_DAZY_BOO3:P0017","text":"ἅμα δὲ οὐδὲ τοῦτο ἀληθές, ὡς ἡ γεωδαισία τῶν αἰσθητῶν ἐστὶ μεγεθῶν καὶ φθαρτῶν· ἐφθείρετο γὰρ ἂν φθειρομένων.—ἀλλὰ μὴν οὐδὲ τῶν αἰσθητῶν ἂν εἴη μεγεθῶν οὐδὲ περὶ τὸν οὐρανὸν ἡ ἀστρολογία τόνδε.  οὔτε γὰρ αἱ αἰσθηταὶ γραμμαὶ τοιαῦταί εἰσιν οἵας λέγει ὁ γεωμέτρης (οὐθὲν γὰρ εὐθὺ τῶν αἰσθητῶν οὕτως οὐδὲ στρογγύλον· ἅπτεται γὰρ τοῦ κανόνος οὐ κατὰ στιγμὴν ὁ κύκλος ἀλλʼ ὥσπερ Πρωταγόρας ἔλεγεν ἐλέγχων τοὺς γεωμέτρας) , οὔθʼ αἱ κινήσεις καὶ ἕλικες τοῦ οὐρανοῦ ὅμοιαι περὶ ὧν ἡ ἀστρολογία ποιεῖται τοὺς λόγους, οὔτε τὰ σημεῖα τοῖς ἄστροις τὴν αὐτὴν ἔχει φύσιν. εἰσὶ δέ τινες οἵ φασιν εἶναι μὲν τὰ μεταξὺ ταῦτα λεγόμενα τῶν τε εἰδῶν καὶ τῶν αἰσθητῶν, οὐ μὴν χωρίς γε τῶν αἰσθητῶν ἀλλʼ ἐν τούτοις· οἷς τὰ συμβαίνοντα ἀδύνατα πάντα μὲν πλείονος λόγου διελθεῖν, ἱκανὸν δὲ καὶ τὰ τοιαῦτα θεωρῆσαι.","kind":"passage"},{"id":"RAW_DAZY_BOO3:P0018","text":"οὔτε γὰρ ἐπὶ τούτων εὔλογον ἔχειν οὕτω μόνον, ἀλλὰ δῆλον ὅτι καὶ τὰ εἴδη ἐνδέχοιτʼ ἂν ἐν τοῖς αἰσθητοῖς εἶναι (τοῦ γὰρ αὐτοῦ λόγου ἀμφότερα ταῦτά ἐστιν), ἔτι δὲ δύο στερεὰ ἐν τῷ αὐτῷ ἀναγκαῖον εἶναι τόπῳ, καὶ μὴ εἶναι ἀκίνητα ἐν κινουμένοις γε ὄντα τοῖς αἰσθητοῖς. ὅλως δὲ τίνος ἕνεκʼ ἄν τις θείη εἶναι μὲν αὐτά, εἶναι δʼ ἐν τοῖς αἰσθητοῖς; ταὐτὰ γὰρ συμβήσεται ἄτοπα τοῖς προειρημένοις· ἔσται γὰρ οὐρανός τις παρὰ τὸν οὐρανόν, πλήν γʼ οὐ χωρὶς ἀλλʼ ἐν τῷ αὐτῷ τόπῳ· ὅπερ ἐστὶν ἀδυνατώτερον.","kind":"passage"},{"id":"RAW_DAZY_BOO3:P0019","text":"περί τε τούτων οὖν ἀπορία πολλὴ πῶς δεῖ θέμενον τυχεῖν τῆς ἀληθείας, καὶ περὶ τῶν ἀρχῶν πότερον δεῖ τὰ γένη στοιχεῖα καὶ ἀρχὰς ὑπολαμβάνειν ἢ μᾶλλον ἐξ ὧν ἐνυπαρχόντων ἐστὶν ἕκαστον πρώτων, οἷον φωνῆς στοιχεῖα καὶ ἀρχαὶ δοκοῦσιν εἶναι ταῦτʼ ἐξ ὧν σύγκεινται αἱ φωναὶ πρώτων, ἀλλʼ οὐ τὸ κοινὸν ἡ φωνή· καὶ τῶν διαγραμμάτων ταῦτα στοιχεῖα λέγομεν ὧν αἱ ἀποδείξεις ἐνυπάρχουσιν ἐν ταῖς τῶν ἄλλων ἀποδείξεσιν ἢ πάντων ἢ τῶν πλείστων, ἔτι δὲ τῶν σωμάτων καὶ οἱ πλείω λέγοντες εἶναι στοιχεῖα καὶ οἱ ἕν, ἐξ ὧν σύγκειται καὶ ἐξ ὧν συνέστηκεν ἀρχὰς λέγουσιν εἶναι, οἷον Ἐμπεδοκλῆς πῦρ καὶ ὕδωρ καὶ τὰ μετὰ τούτων στοιχεῖά φησιν εἶναι ἐξ ὧν ἐστὶ τὰ ὄντα ἐνυπαρχόντων, ἀλλʼ οὐχ ὡς γένη λέγει ταῦτα τῶν ὄντων.","kind":"passage"},{"id":"RAW_DAZY_BOO3:P0020","text":"πρὸς δὲ τούτοις καὶ τῶν ἄλλων εἴ τις ἐθέλει τὴν φύσιν ἀθρεῖν,  οἷον κλίνην ἐξ ὧν μορίων συνέστηκε καὶ πῶς συγκειμένων, τότε γνωρίζει τὴν φύσιν αὐτῆς.—ἐκ μὲν οὖν τούτων τῶν λόγων οὐκ ἂν εἴησαν αἱ ἀρχαὶ τὰ γένη τῶν ὄντων· εἰ δʼ ἕκαστον μὲν γνωρίζομεν διὰ τῶν ὁρισμῶν, ἀρχαὶ δὲ τὰ γένη τῶν ὁρισμῶν εἰσίν, ἀνάγκη καὶ τῶν ὁριστῶν ἀρχὰς εἶναι τὰ γένη. κἂν εἰ ἔστι τὴν τῶν ὄντων λαβεῖν ἐπιστήμην τὸ τῶν εἰδῶν λαβεῖν καθʼ ἃ λέγονται τὰ ὄντα, τῶν γε εἰδῶν ἀρχαὶ τὰ γένη εἰσίν. φαίνονται δέ τινες καὶ τῶν λεγόντων στοιχεῖα τῶν ὄντων τὸ ἓν ἢ τὸ ὂν ἢ τὸ μέγα καὶ μικρὸν ὡς γένεσιν αὐτοῖς χρῆσθαι.—ἀλλὰ μὴν οὐδὲ ἀμφοτέρως γε οἷόν τε λέγειν τὰς ἀρχάς.","kind":"passage"},{"id":"RAW_DAZY_BOO3:P0021","text":"ὁ μὲν γὰρ λόγος τῆς οὐσίας εἷς· ἕτερος δʼ ἔσται ὁ διὰ τῶν γενῶν ὁρισμὸς καὶ ὁ λέγων ἐξ ὧν ἔστιν ἐνυπαρχόντων.—πρὸς δὲ τούτοις εἰ καὶ ὅτι μάλιστα ἀρχαὶ τὰ γένη εἰσί, πότερον δεῖ νομίζειν τὰ πρῶτα τῶν γενῶν ἀρχὰς ἢ τὰ ἔσχατα κατηγορούμενα ἐπὶ τῶν ἀτόμων; καὶ γὰρ τοῦτο ἔχει ἀμφισβήτησιν. εἰ μὲν γὰρ ἀεὶ τὰ καθόλου μᾶλλον ἀρχαί, φανερὸν ὅτι τὰ ἀνωτάτω τῶν γενῶν· ταῦτα γὰρ λέγεται κατὰ πάντων. τοσαῦται οὖν ἔσονται ἀρχαὶ τῶν ὄντων ὅσαπερ τὰ πρῶτα γένη, ὥστʼ ἔσται τό τε ὂν καὶ τὸ ἓν ἀρχαὶ καὶ οὐσίαι· ταῦτα γὰρ κατὰ πάντων μάλιστα λέγεται τῶν ὄντων. οὐχ οἷόν τε δὲ τῶν ὄντων ἓν εἶναι γένος οὔτε τὸ ἓν οὔτε τὸ ὄν· ἀνάγκη μὲν γὰρ τὰς διαφορὰς ἑκάστου γένους καὶ εἶναι καὶ μίαν εἶναι ἑκάστην, ἀδύνατον δὲ κατηγορεῖσθαι ἢ τὰ εἴδη τοῦ γένους ἐπὶ τῶν οἰκείων διαφορῶν ἢ τὸ γένος ἄνευ τῶν αὐτοῦ εἰδῶν, ὥστʼ εἴπερ τὸ ἓν γένος ἢ τὸ ὄν, οὐδεμία διαφορὰ οὔτε ὂν οὔτε ἓν ἔσται.","kind":"passage"},{"id":"RAW_DAZY_BOO3:P0022","text":"ἀλλὰ μὴν εἰ μὴ γένη, οὐδʼ ἀρχαὶ ἔσονται, εἴπερ ἀρχαὶ τὰ γένη. ἔτι καὶ τὰ μεταξὺ συλλαμβανόμενα μετὰ τῶν διαφορῶν ἔσται γένη μέχρι τῶν ἀτόμων (νῦν δὲ τὰ μὲν δοκεῖ τὰ δʼ οὐ δοκει)· πρὸς δὲ τούτοις ἔτι μᾶλλον αἱ διαφοραὶ ἀρχαὶ ἢ τὰ γένη· εἰ δὲ καὶ αὗται ἀρχαί, ἄπειροι ὡς εἰπεῖν ἀρχαὶ γίγνονται, ἄλλως τε κἄν τις τὸ πρῶτον γένος ἀρχὴν τιθῇ.  ἀλλὰ μὴν καὶ εἰ μᾶλλόν γε ἀρχοειδὲς τὸ ἕν ἐστιν, ἓν δὲ τὸ ἀδιαίρετον, ἀδιαίρετον δὲ ἅπαν ἢ κατὰ τὸ ποσὸν ἢ κατʼ εἶδος, πρότερον δὲ τὸ κατʼ εἶδος, τὰ δὲ γένη διαιρετὰ εἰς εἴδη, μᾶλλον ἂν ἓν τὸ ἔσχατον εἴη κατηγορούμενον· οὐ γάρ ἐστι γένος ἅνθρωπος τῶν τινῶν ἀνθρώπων.","kind":"passage"},{"id":"RAW_DAZY_BOO3:P0023","text":"ἔτι ἐν οἷς τὸ πρότερον καὶ ὕστερόν ἐστιν, οὐχ οἷόν τε τὸ ἐπὶ τούτων εἶναί τι παρὰ ταῦτα (οἷον εἰ πρώτη τῶν ἀριθμῶν ἡ δυάς, οὐκ ἔσται τις ἀριθμὸς παρὰ τὰ εἴδη τῶν ἀριθμῶν· ὁμοίως δὲ οὐδὲ σχῆμα παρὰ τὰ εἴδη τῶν σχημάτων· εἰ δὲ μὴ τούτων, σχολῇ τῶν γε ἄλλων ἔσται τὰ γένη παρὰ τὰ εἴδη· τούτων γὰρ δοκεῖ μάλιστα εἶναι γένη)· ἐν δὲ τοῖς ἀτόμοις οὐκ ἔστι τὸ μὲν πρότερον τὸ δʼ ὕστερον. ἔτι ὅπου τὸ μὲν βέλτιον τὸ δὲ χεῖρον, ἀεὶ τὸ βέλτιον πρότερον· ὥστʼ οὐδὲ τούτων ἂν εἴη γένος.—ἐκ μὲν οὖν τούτων μᾶλλον φαίνεται τὰ ἐπὶ τῶν ἀτόμων κατηγορούμενα ἀρχαὶ εἶναι τῶν γενῶν· πάλιν δὲ πῶς αὖ δεῖ ταύτας ἀρχὰς ὑπολαβεῖν οὐ ῥᾴδιον εἰπεῖν.","kind":"passage"},{"id":"RAW_DAZY_BOO3:P0024","text":"τὴν μὲν γὰρ ἀρχὴν δεῖ καὶ τὴν αἰτίαν εἶναι παρὰ τὰ πράγματα ὧν ἀρχή, καὶ δύνασθαι εἶναι χωριζομένην αὐτῶν· τοιοῦτον δέ τι παρὰ τὸ καθʼ ἕκαστον εἶναι διὰ τί ἄν τις ὑπολάβοι, πλὴν ὅτι καθόλου κατηγορεῖται καὶ κατὰ πάντων; ἀλλὰ μὴν εἰ διὰ τοῦτο, τὰ μᾶλλον καθόλου μᾶλλον θετέον ἀρχάς· ὥστε ἀρχαὶ τὰ πρῶτʼ ἂν εἴησαν γένη.","kind":"passage"},{"id":"RAW_DAZY_BOO3:P0025","text":"ἔστι δʼ ἐχομένη τε τούτων ἀπορία καὶ πασῶν χαλεπωτάτη καὶ ἀναγκαιοτάτη θεωρῆσαι, περὶ ἧς ὁ λόγος ἐφέστηκε νῦν. εἴτε γὰρ μὴ ἔστι τι παρὰ τὰ καθʼ ἕκαστα, τὰ δὲ καθʼ ἕκαστα ἄπειρα, τῶν δʼ ἀπείρων πῶς ἐνδέχεται λαβεῖν ἐπιστήμην; ᾗ γὰρ ἕν τι καὶ ταὐτόν, καὶ ᾗ καθόλου τι ὑπάρχει, ταύτῃ πάντα γνωρίζομεν.—ἀλλὰ μὴν εἰ τοῦτο ἀναγκαῖόν ἐστι καὶ δεῖ τι εἶναι παρὰ τὰ καθʼ ἕκαστα, ἀναγκαῖον ἂν εἴη τὰ γένη εἶναι παρὰ τὰ καθʼ ἕκαστα, ἤτοι τὰ ἔσχατα ἢ τὰ πρῶτα· τοῦτο δʼ ὅτι ἀδύνατον ἄρτι διηπορήσαμεν.—ἔτι εἰ ὅτι μάλιστα ἔστι τι παρὰ τὸ σύνολον ὅταν κατηγορηθῇ τι τῆς ὕλης, πότερον, εἰ ἔστι, παρὰ πάντα δεῖ εἶναί τι, ἢ παρὰ μὲν ἔνια εἶναι παρὰ δʼ ἔνια μὴ εἶναι, ἢ παρʼ οὐδέν;  εἰ μὲν οὖν μηδέν ἐστι παρὰ τὰ καθʼ ἕκαστα, οὐθὲν ἂν εἴη νοητὸν ἀλλὰ πάντα αἰσθητὰ καὶ ἐπιστήμη οὐδενός, εἰ μή τις εἶναι λέγει τὴν αἴσθησιν ἐπιστήμην.","kind":"passage"},{"id":"RAW_DAZY_BOO3:P0026","text":"ἔτι δʼ οὐδʼ ἀΐδιον οὐθὲν οὐδʼ ἀκίνητον (τὰ γὰρ αἰσθητὰ πάντα φθείρεται καὶ ἐν κινήσει ἐστίν)· ἀλλὰ μὴν εἴ γε ἀΐδιον μηθέν ἐστιν, οὐδὲ γένεσιν εἶναι δυνατόν. ἀνάγκη γὰρ εἶναί τι τὸ γιγνόμενον καὶ ἐξ οὗ γίγνεται καὶ τούτων τὸ ἔσχατον ἀγένητον, εἴπερ ἵσταταί τε καὶ ἐκ μὴ ὄντος γενέσθαι ἀδύνατον· ἔτι δὲ γενέσεως οὔσης καὶ κινήσεως ἀνάγκη καὶ πέρας εἶναι (οὔτε γὰρ ἄπειρός ἐστιν οὐδεμία κίνησις ἀλλὰ πάσης ἔστι τέλος, γίγνεσθαί τε οὐχ οἷόν τε τὸ ἀδύνατον γενέσθαι· τὸ δὲ γεγονὸς ἀνάγκη εἶναι ὅτε πρῶτον γέγονεν)· ἔτι δʼ εἴπερ ἡ ὕλη ἔστι διὰ τὸ ἀγένητος εἶναι, πολὺ ἔτι μᾶλλον εὔλογον εἶναι τὴν οὐσίαν, ὅ ποτε ἐκείνη γίγνεται· εἰ γὰρ μήτε τοῦτο ἔσται μήτε ἐκείνη, οὐθὲν ἔσται τὸ παράπαν, εἰ δὲ τοῦτο ἀδύνατον, ἀνάγκη τι εἶναι παρὰ τὸ σύνολον, τὴν μορφὴν καὶ τὸ εἶδος.—εἰ δʼ αὖ τις τοῦτο θήσει, ἀπορία ἐπὶ τίνων τε θήσει τοῦτο καὶ ἐπὶ τίνων οὔ.","kind":"passage"},{"id":"RAW_DAZY_BOO3:P0027","text":"ὅτι μὲν γὰρ ἐπὶ πάντων οὐχ οἷόν τε, φανερόν· οὐ γὰρ ἂν θείημεν εἶναί τινα οἰκίαν παρὰ τὰς τινὰς  οἰκίας. πρὸς δὲ τούτοις πότερον ἡ οὐσία μία πάντων ἔσται, οἷον τῶν ἀνθρώπων; ἀλλʼ ἄτοπον· ἓν γὰρ πάντα ὧν ἡ οὐσία μία. ἀλλὰ πολλὰ καὶ διάφορα; ἀλλὰ καὶ τοῦτο ἄλογον. ἅμα δὲ καὶ πῶς γίγνεται ἡ ὕλη τούτων ἕκαστον καὶ ἔστι τὸ σύνολον ἄμφω ταῦτα;—ἔτι δὲ περὶ τῶν ἀρχῶν καὶ τόδε ἀπορήσειεν ἄν τις.","kind":"passage"},{"id":"RAW_DAZY_BOO3:P0028","text":"εἰ μὲν γὰρ εἴδει εἰσὶν ἕν, οὐθὲν ἔσται ἀριθμῷ ἕν, οὐδʼ αὐτὸ τὸ ἓν καὶ τὸ ὄν· καὶ τὸ ἐπίστασθαι πῶς ἔσται, εἰ μή τι ἔσται ἓν ἐπὶ πάντων;—ἀλλὰ μὴν εἰ ἀριθμῷ ἓν καὶ μία ἑκάστη τῶν ἀρχῶν, καὶ μὴ ὥσπερ ἐπὶ τῶν αἰσθητῶν ἄλλαι ἄλλων (οἷον τῆσδε τῆς συλλαβῆς τῷ εἴδει τῆς αὐτῆς οὔσης καὶ αἱ ἀρχαὶ εἴδει αἱ αὐταί· καὶ γὰρ αὗται ὑπάρχουσιν ἀριθμῷ ἕτεραι),—εἰ δὲ μὴ οὕτως ἀλλʼ αἱ τῶν ὄντων ἀρχαὶ ἀριθμῷ ἕν εἰσιν, οὐκ ἔσται παρὰ τὰ στοιχεῖα οὐθὲν ἕτερον· τὸ γὰρ ἀριθμῷ ἓν ἢ τὸ καθʼ ἕκαστον λέγειν διαφέρει οὐθέν· οὕτω γὰρ λέγομεν τὸ καθʼ ἕκαστον, τὸ ἀριθμῷ ἕν, καθόλου δὲ τὸ ἐπὶ τούτων.  ὥσπερ οὖν εἰ τὰ τῆς φωνῆς ἀριθμῷ ἦν στοιχεῖα ὡρισμένα, ἀναγκαῖον ἦν ἂν τοσαῦτα εἶναι τὰ πάντα γράμματα ὅσαπερ τὰ στοιχεῖα, μὴ ὄντων γε δύο τῶν αὐτῶν μηδὲ πλειόνων.","kind":"passage"},{"id":"RAW_DAZY_BOO3:P0029","text":"οὐθενὸς δʼ ἐλάττων ἀπορία παραλέλειπται καὶ τοῖς νῦν καὶ τοῖς πρότερον, πότερον αἱ αὐταὶ τῶν φθαρτῶν καὶ τῶν ἀφθάρτων ἀρχαί εἰσιν ἢ ἕτεραι. εἰ μὲν γὰρ αἱ αὐταί, πῶς τὰ μὲν φθαρτὰ τὰ δὲ ἄφθαρτα, καὶ διὰ τίνʼ αἰτίαν; οἱ μὲν οὖν περὶ Ἡσίοδον καὶ πάντες ὅσοι θεολόγοι μόνον ἐφρόντισαν τοῦ πιθανοῦ τοῦ πρὸς αὑτούς, ἡμῶν δʼ ὠλιγώρησαν (θεοὺς γὰρ ποιοῦντες τὰς ἀρχὰς καὶ ἐκ θεῶν γεγονέναι, τὰ μὴ γευσάμενα τοῦ νέκταρος καὶ τῆς ἀμβροσίας θνητὰ γενέσθαι φασίν, δῆλον ὡς ταῦτα τὰ ὀνόματα γνώριμα λέγοντες αὑτοῖς· καίτοι περὶ αὐτῆς τῆς προσφορᾶς τῶν αἰτίων τούτων ὑπὲρ ἡμᾶς εἰρήκασιν· εἰ μὲν γὰρ χάριν ἡδονῆς αὐτῶν θιγγάνουσιν, οὐθὲν αἴτια τοῦ εἶναι τὸ νέκταρ καὶ ἡ ἀμβροσία, εἰ δὲ τοῦ εἶναι, πῶς ἂν εἶεν ἀΐδιοι δεόμενοι τροφῆς)·—ἀλλὰ περὶ μὲν τῶν μυθικῶς σοφιζομένων οὐκ ἄξιον μετὰ σπουδῆς σκοπεῖν· παρὰ δὲ τῶν διʼ ἀποδείξεως λεγόντων δεῖ πυνθάνεσθαι διερωτῶντας τί δή ποτʼ ἐκ τῶν αὐτῶν ὄντα τὰ μὲν ἀΐδια τὴν","kind":"passage"},{"id":"RAW_DAZY_BOO3:P0030","text":"φύσιν ἐστὶ τὰ δὲ φθείρεται τῶν ὄντων. ἐπεὶ δὲ οὔτε αἰτίαν λέγουσιν οὔτε εὔλογον οὕτως ἔχειν, δῆλον ὡς οὐχ αἱ αὐταὶ ἀρχαὶ οὐδὲ αἰτίαι αὐτῶν ἂν εἶεν. καὶ γὰρ ὅνπερ οἰηθείη λέγειν ἄν τις μάλιστα ὁμολογουμένως αὑτῷ, Ἐμπεδοκλῆς, καὶ οὗτος ταὐτὸν πέπονθεν· τίθησι μὲν γὰρ ἀρχήν τινα αἰτίαν τῆς φθορᾶς τὸ νεῖκος, δόξειε δʼ ἂν οὐθὲν ἧττον καὶ τοῦτο γεννᾶν ἔξω τοῦ ἑνός· ἅπαντα γὰρ ἐκ τούτου τἆλλά ἐστι πλὴν ὁ θεός. λέγει γοῦν","kind":"passage"},{"id":"RAW_DAZY_BOO3:P0031","text":"ἐξ ὧν πάνθʼ ὅσα τʼ ἦν ὅσα τʼ ἔσθʼ ὅσα τʼ ἔσται ὀπίσσω,","kind":"passage"},{"id":"RAW_DAZY_BOO3:P0032","text":"δένδρεά τʼ ἐβλάστησε καὶ ἀνέρες ἠδὲ γυναῖκες,","kind":"passage"},{"id":"RAW_DAZY_BOO3:P0033","text":"θῆρές τʼ οἰωνοί τε καὶ ὑδατοθρέμμονες ἰχθῦς,","kind":"passage"},{"id":"RAW_DAZY_BOO3:P0034","text":"καί τε θεοὶ δολιχαίωνες.","kind":"passage"},{"id":"RAW_DAZY_BOO3:P0035","text":"Empedocles Fr. 21.9-12 καὶ χωρὶς δὲ τούτων δῆλον·  εἰ γὰρ μὴ ἦν ἐν τοῖς πράγμασιν, ἓν ἂν ἦν ἅπαντα, ὡς φησίν· ὅταν γὰρ συνέλθῃ,","kind":"passage"},{"id":"RAW_DAZY_BOO3:P0036","text":"τότε δʼ ἔσχατον ἵστατο νεῖκος.","kind":"passage"},{"id":"RAW_DAZY_BOO3:P0037","text":"Empedocles Fr. 36.7 διὸ καὶ συμβαίνει αὐτῷ τὸν εὐδαιμονέστατον θεὸν ἧττον φρόνιμον εἶναι τῶν ἄλλων· οὐ γὰρ γνωρίζει ἅπαντα· τὸ γὰρ νεῖκος οὐκ ἔχει, ἡ δὲ γνῶσις τοῦ ὁμοίου τῷ ὁμοίῳ.","kind":"passage"},{"id":"RAW_DAZY_BOO3:P0038","text":"γαίῃ μὲν γάρ, (φησί,) γαῖαν ὀπώπαμεν, ὕδατι δʼ ὕδωρ,","kind":"passage"},{"id":"RAW_DAZY_BOO3:P0039","text":"αἰθέρι δʼ αἰθέρα δῖον, ἀτὰρ πυρὶ πῦρ ἀΐδηλον,","kind":"passage"},{"id":"RAW_DAZY_BOO3:P0040","text":"στοργὴν δὲ στοργῇ, νεῖκος δέ τε νείκεϊ λυγρῷ.","kind":"passage"},{"id":"RAW_DAZY_BOO3:P0041","text":"Empedocles Fr. 109 ἀλλʼ ὅθεν δὴ ὁ λόγος, τοῦτό γε φανερόν, ὅτι συμβαίνει αὐτῷ τὸ νεῖκος μηθὲν μᾶλλον φθορᾶς ἢ τοῦ εἶναι αἴτιον· ὁμοίως δʼ οὐδʼ ἡ φιλότης τοῦ εἶναι, συνάγουσα γὰρ εἰς τὸ ἓν φθείρει τὰ ἄλλα. καὶ ἅμα δὲ αὐτῆς τῆς μεταβολῆς αἴτιον οὐθὲν λέγει ἀλλʼ ἢ ὅτι οὕτως πέφυκεν·","kind":"passage"},{"id":"RAW_DAZY_BOO3:P0042","text":"ἀλλʼ ὅτε δὴ μέγα νεῖκος ἐνὶ μελέεσσιν ἐθρέφθη,","kind":"passage"},{"id":"RAW_DAZY_BOO3:P0043","text":"εἰς τιμάς τʼ ἀνόρουσε τελειομένοιο χρόνοιο","kind":"passage"},{"id":"RAW_DAZY_BOO3:P0044","text":"ὅς σφιν ἀμοιβαῖος πλατέος παρʼ ἐλήλαται ὅρκου·","kind":"passage"},{"id":"RAW_DAZY_BOO3:P0045","text":"Empedocles Fr. 30 ὡς ἀναγκαῖον μὲν ὂν μεταβάλλειν· αἰτίαν δὲ τῆς ἀνάγκης οὐδεμίαν δηλοῖ. ἀλλʼ ὅμως τοσοῦτόν γε μόνος λέγει ὁμολογουμένως· οὐ γὰρ τὰ μὲν φθαρτὰ τὰ δὲ ἄφθαρτα ποιεῖ τῶν ὄντων ἀλλὰ πάντα φθαρτὰ πλὴν τῶν στοιχείων. ἡ δὲ νῦν λεγομένη ἀπορία ἐστὶ διὰ τί τὰ μὲν τὰ δʼ οὔ, εἴπερ ἐκ τῶν αὐτῶν ἐστίν.—ὅτι μὲν οὖν οὐκ ἂν εἴησαν αἱ αὐταὶ ἀρχαί, τοσαῦτα εἰρήσθω· εἰ δὲ ἕτεραι ἀρχαί, μία μὲν ἀπορία πότερον ἄφθαρτοι καὶ αὗται ἔσονται ἢ φθαρταί· εἰ μὲν γὰρ φθαρταί, δῆλον ὡς ἀναγκαῖον καὶ ταύτας ἔκ τινων εἶναι (πάντα γὰρ φθείρεται εἰς ταῦτʼ ἐξ ὧν ἔστιν), ὥστε συμβαίνει τῶν ἀρχῶν ἑτέρας ἀρχὰς εἶναι προτέρας, τοῦτο δʼ ἀδύνατον, καὶ εἰ ἵσταται καὶ εἰ βαδίζει εἰς ἄπειρον· ἔτι δὲ πῶς ἔσται τὰ φθαρτά, εἰ αἱ ἀρχαὶ ἀναιρεθήσονται; εἰ δὲ ἄφθαρτοι, διὰ τί ἐκ μὲν τούτων ἀφθάρτων οὐσῶν φθαρτὰ ἔσται, ἐκ δὲ τῶν ἑτέρων ἄφθαρτα; τοῦτο γὰρ οὐκ εὔλογον, ἀλλʼ ἢ ἀδύνατον ἢ πολλοῦ λόγου δεῖται.","kind":"passage"},{"id":"RAW_DAZY_BOO3:P0046","text":"ἔτι δὲ οὐδʼ ἐγκεχείρηκεν οὐδεὶς ἑτέρας, ἀλλὰ τὰς αὐτὰς ἁπάντων λέγουσιν ἀρχάς.  ἀλλὰ τὸ πρῶτον ἀπορηθὲν ἀποτρώγουσιν ὥσπερ τοῦτο μικρόν τι λαμβάνοντες.","kind":"passage"},{"id":"RAW_DAZY_BOO3:P0047","text":"πάντων δὲ καὶ θεωρῆσαι χαλεπώτατον καὶ πρὸς τὸ γνῶναι τἀληθὲς ἀναγκαιότατον πότερόν ποτε τὸ ὂν καὶ τὸ ἓν οὐσίαι τῶν ὄντων εἰσί, καὶ ἑκάτερον αὐτῶν οὐχ ἕτερόν τι ὂν τὸ μὲν ἓν τὸ δὲ ὄν ἐστιν, ἢ δεῖ ζητεῖν τί ποτʼ ἐστὶ τὸ ὂν καὶ τὸ ἓν ὡς ὑποκειμένης ἄλλης φύσεως. οἱ μὲν γὰρ ἐκείνως οἱ δʼ οὕτως οἴονται τὴν φύσιν ἔχειν. Πλάτων μὲν γὰρ καὶ οἱ Πυθαγόρειοι οὐχ ἕτερόν τι τὸ ὂν οὐδὲ τὸ ἓν ἀλλὰ τοῦτο αὐτῶν τὴν φύσιν εἶναι, ὡς οὔσης τῆς οὐσίας αὐτοῦ τοῦ ἑνὶ εἶναι καὶ ὄντι· οἱ δὲ περὶ φύσεως, οἷον Ἐμπεδοκλῆς ὡς εἰς γνωριμώτερον ἀνάγων λέγει ὅ τι τὸ ἕν ἐστιν· δόξειε γὰρ ἂν λέγειν τοῦτο τὴν φιλίαν εἶναι (αἰτία γοῦν ἐστὶν αὕτη τοῦ ἓν εἶναι πᾶσιν), ἕτεροι δὲ πῦρ, οἱ δʼ ἀέρα φασὶν εἶναι τὸ ἓν τοῦτο καὶ τὸ ὄν, ἐξ οὗ τὰ ὄντα εἶναί τε καὶ γεγονέναι. ὣς δʼ αὔτως καὶ οἱ πλείω τὰ στοιχεῖα τιθέμενοι· ἀνάγκη γὰρ καὶ τούτοις τοσαῦτα λέγειν τὸ ἓν καὶ τὸ ὂν ὅσας περ ἀρχὰς εἶναί φασιν.","kind":"passage"},{"id":"RAW_DAZY_BOO3:P0048","text":"συμβαίνει δέ, εἰ μέν τις μὴ θήσεται εἶναί τινα οὐσίαν τὸ ἓν καὶ τὸ ὄν, μηδὲ τῶν ἄλλων εἶναι τῶν καθόλου μηθέν (ταῦτα γάρ ἐστι καθόλου μάλιστα πάντων, εἰ δὲ μὴ ἔστι τι ἓν αὐτὸ μηδʼ αὐτὸ ὄν, σχολῇ τῶν γε ἄλλων τι ἂν εἴη παρὰ τὰ λεγόμενα καθʼ ἕκαστα), ἔτι δὲ μὴ ὄντος τοῦ ἑνὸς οὐσίας, δῆλον ὅτι οὐδʼ ἂν ἀριθμὸς εἴη ὡς κεχωρισμένη τις φύσις τῶν ὄντων (ὁ μὲν γὰρ ἀριθμὸς μονάδες, ἡ δὲ μονὰς ὅπερ ἕν τί ἐστιν)· εἰ δʼ ἔστι τι αὐτὸ ἓν καὶ ὄν, ἀναγκαῖον οὐσίαν αὐτῶν εἶναι τὸ ἓν καὶ τὸ ὄν· οὐ γὰρ ἕτερόν τι καθόλου κατηγορεῖται ἀλλὰ ταῦτα αὐτά.—ἀλλὰ μὴν εἴ γʼ ἔσται τι αὐτὸ ὂν καὶ αὐτὸ ἕν, πολλὴ ἀπορία πῶς ἔσται τι παρὰ ταῦτα ἕτερον, λέγω δὲ πῶς ἔσται πλείω ἑνὸς τὰ ὄντα. τὸ γὰρ ἕτερον τοῦ ὄντος οὐκ ἔστιν, ὥστε κατὰ τὸν Παρμενίδου συμβαίνειν ἀνάγκη λόγον ἓν ἅπαντα εἶναι τὰ ὄντα καὶ τοῦτο εἶναι τὸ ὄν.","kind":"passage"},{"id":"RAW_DAZY_BOO3:P0049","text":"ἀμφοτέρως δὲ δύσκολον· ἄν τε γὰρ μὴ ᾖ τὸ ἓν οὐσία ἄν τε ᾖ τὸ αὐτὸ ἕν, ἀδύνατον τὸν ἀριθμὸν οὐσίαν εἶναι. ἐὰν μὲν οὖν μὴ ᾖ, εἴρηται πρότερον διʼ ὅ· ἐὰν δὲ ᾖ, ἡ αὐτὴ ἀπορία καὶ περὶ τοῦ ὄντος. ἐκ τίνος γὰρ παρὰ τὸ ἓν ἔσται αὐτὸ ἄλλο ἕν; ἀνάγκη γὰρ μὴ ἓν εἶναι· ἅπαντα δὲ τὰ ὄντα ἢ ἓν ἢ πολλὰ ὧν ἓν ἕκαστον.","kind":"passage"},{"id":"RAW_DAZY_BOO3:P0050","text":"ἔτι εἰ ἀδιαίρετον αὐτὸ τὸ ἕν, κατὰ μὲν τὸ Ζήνωνος ἀξίωμα οὐθὲν ἂν εἴη (ὃ γὰρ μήτε προστιθέμενον μήτε ἀφαιρούμενον ποιεῖ μεῖζον μηδὲ ἔλαττον, οὔ φησιν εἶναι τοῦτο τῶν ὄντων, ὡς δηλονότι ὄντος μεγέθους τοῦ ὄντος· καὶ εἰ μέγεθος, σωματικόν· τοῦτο γὰρ πάντῃ ὄν· τὰ δὲ ἄλλα πὼς μὲν προστιθέμενα ποιήσει μεῖζον, πὼς δʼ οὐθέν, οἷον ἐπίπεδον καὶ γραμμή, στιγμὴ δὲ καὶ μονὰς οὐδαμῶς)· ἀλλʼ ἐπειδὴ οὗτος θεωρεῖ φορτικῶς, καὶ ἐνδέχεται εἶναι ἀδιαίρετόν τι ὥστε  καὶ πρὸς ἐκεῖνόν τινʼ ἀπολογίαν ἔχειν (μεῖζον μὲν γὰρ οὐ ποιήσει πλεῖον δὲ προστιθέμενον τὸ τοιοῦτον)·—ἀλλὰ πῶς δὴ ἐξ ἑνὸς τοιούτου ἢ πλειόνων τοιούτων ἔσται μέγεθος; ὅμοιον γὰρ καὶ τὴν γραμμὴν ἐκ στιγμῶν εἶναι φάσκειν.","kind":"passage"},{"id":"RAW_DAZY_BOO3:P0051","text":"ἀλλὰ μὴν καὶ εἴ τις οὕτως ὑπολαμβάνει ὥστε γενέσθαι, καθάπερ λέγουσί τινες, ἐκ τοῦ ἑνὸς αὐτοῦ καὶ ἄλλου μὴ ἑνός τινος τὸν ἀριθμόν, οὐθὲν ἧττον ζητητέον διὰ τί καὶ πῶς ὁτὲ μὲν ἀριθμὸς ὁτὲ δὲ μέγεθος ἔσται τὸ γενόμενον, εἴπερ τὸ μὴ ἓν ἡ ἀνισότης καὶ ἡ αὐτὴ φύσις ἦν. οὔτε γὰρ ὅπως ἐξ ἑνὸς καὶ ταύτης οὔτε ὅπως ἐξ ἀριθμοῦ τινὸς καὶ ταύτης γένοιτʼ ἂν τὰ μεγέθη, δῆλον.","kind":"passage"},{"id":"RAW_DAZY_BOO3:P0052","text":"τούτων δʼ ἐχομένη ἀπορία πότερον οἱ ἀριθμοὶ καὶ τὰ σώματα καὶ τὰ ἐπίπεδα καὶ αἱ στιγμαὶ οὐσίαι τινές εἰσιν ἢ οὔ. εἰ μὲν γὰρ μή εἰσιν, διαφεύγει τί τὸ ὂν καὶ τίνες αἱ οὐσίαι τῶν ὄντων· τὰ μὲν γὰρ πάθη καὶ αἱ κινήσεις καὶ τὰ πρός τι καὶ αἱ διαθέσεις καὶ οἱ λόγοι οὐθενὸς δοκοῦσιν οὐσίαν σημαίνειν (λέγονται γὰρ πάντα καθʼ ὑποκειμένου τινός, καὶ οὐθὲν τόδε τι)· ἃ δὲ μάλιστʼ ἂν δόξειε σημαίνειν οὐσίαν, ὕδωρ καὶ γῆ καὶ πῦρ καὶ ἀήρ, ἐξ ὧν τὰ σύνθετα σώματα συνέστηκε,  τούτων θερμότητες μὲν καὶ ψυχρότητες καὶ τὰ τοιαῦτα πάθη, οὐκ οὐσίαι, τὸ δὲ σῶμα τὸ ταῦτα πεπονθὸς μόνον ὑπομένει ὡς ὄν τι καὶ οὐσία τις οὖσα. ἀλλὰ μὴν τό γε σῶμα ἧττον οὐσία τῆς ἐπιφανείας, καὶ αὕτη τῆς γραμμῆς, καὶ αὕτη τῆς μονάδος καὶ τῆς στιγμῆς· τούτοις γὰρ ὥρισται τὸ σῶμα, καὶ τὰ μὲν ἄνευ σώματος ἐνδέχεσθαι δοκεῖ εἶναι τὸ δὲ σῶμα ἄνευ τούτων ἀδύνατον.","kind":"passage"},{"id":"RAW_DAZY_BOO3:P0053","text":"διόπερ οἱ μὲν πολλοὶ καὶ οἱ πρότερον τὴν οὐσίαν καὶ τὸ ὂν ᾤοντο τὸ σῶμα εἶναι τὰ δὲ ἄλλα τούτου πάθη, ὥστε καὶ τὰς ἀρχὰς τὰς τῶν σωμάτων τῶν ὄντων εἶναι ἀρχάς· οἱ δʼ ὕστεροι καὶ σοφώτεροι τούτων εἶναι δόξαντες ἀριθμούς. καθάπερ οὖν εἴπομεν, εἰ μὴ ἔστιν οὐσία ταῦτα, ὅλως οὐδὲν ἐστὶν οὐσία οὐδὲ ὂν οὐθέν· οὐ γὰρ δὴ τά γε συμβεβηκότα τούτοις ἄξιον ὄντα καλεῖν. —ἀλλὰ μὴν εἰ τοῦτο μὲν ὁμολογεῖται, ὅτι μᾶλλον οὐσία τὰ μήκη τῶν σωμάτων καὶ αἱ στιγμαί, ταῦτα δὲ μὴ ὁρῶμεν ποίων ἂν εἶεν σωμάτων (ἐν γὰρ τοῖς αἰσθητοῖς ἀδύνατον εἶναι), οὐκ ἂν εἴη οὐσία οὐδεμία. ἔτι δὲ φαίνεται ταῦτα πάντα διαιρέσεις ὄντα τοῦ σώματος, τὸ μὲν εἰς πλάτος τὸ δʼ εἰς βάθος τὸ δʼ εἰς μῆκος.","kind":"passage"},{"id":"RAW_DAZY_BOO3:P0054","text":"πρὸς δὲ τούτοις ὁμοίως ἔνεστιν ἐν τῷ στερεῷ ὁποιονοῦν σχῆμα· ὥστʼ εἰ μηδʼ ἐν τῷ λίθῳ Ἑρμῆς, οὐδὲ τὸ ἥμισυ τοῦ κύβου ἐν τῷ κύβῳ οὕτως ὡς ἀφωρισμένον· οὐκ ἄρα οὐδʼ ἐπιφάνεια (εἰ γὰρ ὁποιαοῦν, κἂν αὕτη ἂν ἦν ἡ ἀφορίζουσα τὸ ἥμισυ), ὁ δʼ αὐτὸς λόγος καὶ ἐπὶ γραμμῆς καὶ στιγμῆς καὶ μονάδος, ὥστʼ εἰ μάλιστα μὲν οὐσία τὸ σῶμα, τούτου δὲ μᾶλλον ταῦτα, μὴ ἔστι δὲ ταῦτα μηδὲ οὐσίαι τινές, διαφεύγει τί τὸ ὂν καὶ τίς ἡ οὐσία τῶν ὄντων. πρὸς γὰρ τοῖς εἰρημένοις καὶ τὰ περὶ τὴν γένεσιν καὶ τὴν φθορὰν συμβαίνει ἄλογα. δοκεῖ μὲν γὰρ ἡ οὐσία, ἐὰν μὴ οὖσα πρότερον νῦν ᾖ ἢ πρότερον οὖσα ὕστερον μὴ ᾖ, μετὰ τοῦ γίγνεσθαι καὶ φθείρεσθαι ταῦτα πάσχειν· τὰς δὲ στιγμὰς καὶ τὰς γραμμὰς καὶ τὰς ἐπιφανείας οὐκ ἐνδέχεται οὔτε γίγνεσθαι οὔτε φθείρεσθαι, ὁτὲ μὲν οὔσας ὁτὲ δὲ οὐκ οὔσας.","kind":"passage"},{"id":"RAW_DAZY_BOO3:P0055","text":"ὅταν γὰρ ἅπτηται ἢ διαιρῆται τὰ σώματα,  ἅμα ὁτὲ μὲν μία ἁπτομένων ὁτὲ δὲ δύο διαιρουμένων γίγνονται· ὥστʼ οὔτε συγκειμένων ἔστιν ἀλλʼ ἔφθαρται, διῃρημένων τε εἰσὶν αἱ πρότερον οὐκ οὖσαι (οὐ γὰρ δὴ ἥ γʼ ἀδιαίρετος στιγμὴ διῃρέθη εἰς δύο), εἴ τε γίγνονται καὶ φθείρονται, ἐκ τίνος γίγνονται; παραπλησίως δʼ ἔχει καὶ περὶ τὸ νῦν τὸ ἐν τῷ χρόνῳ· οὐδὲ γὰρ τοῦτο ἐνδέχεται γίγνεσθαι καὶ φθείρεσθαι, ἀλλʼ ὅμως ἕτερον ἀεὶ δοκεῖ εἶναι, οὐκ οὐσία τις οὖσα. ὁμοίως δὲ δῆλον ὅτι ἔχει καὶ περὶ τὰς στιγμὰς καὶ τὰς γραμμὰς καὶ τὰ ἐπίπεδα· ὁ γὰρ αὐτὸς λόγος· ἅπαντα γὰρ ὁμοίως ἢ πέρατα ἢ διαιρέσεις εἰσίν.","kind":"passage"},{"id":"RAW_DAZY_BOO3:P0056","text":"ὅλως δʼ ἀπορήσειεν ἄν τις διὰ τί καὶ δεῖ ζητεῖν ἄλλʼ ἄττα παρά τε τὰ αἰσθητὰ καὶ τὰ μεταξύ, οἷον ἃ τίθεμεν εἴδη. εἰ γὰρ διὰ τοῦτο, ὅτι τὰ μὲν μαθηματικὰ τῶν δεῦρο ἄλλῳ μέν τινι διαφέρει, τῷ δὲ πόλλʼ ἄττα ὁμοειδῆ εἶναι οὐθὲν διαφέρει, ὥστʼ οὐκ ἔσονται αὐτῶν αἱ ἀρχαὶ ἀριθμῷ ἀφωρισμέναι (ὥσπερ οὐδὲ τῶν ἐνταῦθα γραμμάτων ἀριθμῷ μὲν πάντων οὐκ εἰσὶν αἱ ἀρχαὶ ὡρισμέναι, εἴδει δέ, ἐὰν μὴ λαμβάνῃ τις τησδὶ τῆς συλλαβῆς ἢ τησδὶ τῆς φωνῆς· τούτων δʼ ἔσονται καὶ ἀριθμῷ ὡρισμέναι—ὁμοίως δὲ καὶ ἐπὶ τῶν μεταξύ· ἄπειρα γὰρ κἀκεῖ τὰ ὁμοειδῆ), ὥστʼ εἰ μὴ ἔστι παρὰ τὰ αἰσθητὰ καὶ τὰ μαθηματικὰ ἕτερʼ ἄττα οἷα λέγουσι τὰ εἴδη τινές, οὐκ ἔσται μία ἀριθμῷ ἀλλʼ εἴδει οὐσία, οὐδʼ αἱ ἀρχαὶ τῶν ὄντων ἀριθμῷ ἔσονται ποσαί τινες ἀλλὰ εἴδει·—εἰ οὖν τοῦτο ἀναγκαῖον, καὶ τὰ εἴδη ἀναγκαῖον διὰ τοῦτο εἶναι τιθέναι.","kind":"passage"},{"id":"RAW_DAZY_BOO3:P0057","text":"καὶ γὰρ εἰ μὴ καλῶς διαρθροῦσιν οἱ λέγοντες, ἀλλʼ ἔστι γε τοῦθʼ ὃ βούλονται, καὶ ἀνάγκη ταῦτα λέγειν αὐτοῖς, ὅτι τῶν εἰδῶν οὐσία τις ἕκαστόν ἐστι καὶ οὐθὲν κατὰ συμβεβηκός.—ἀλλὰ μὴν εἴ γε θήσομεν τά τε εἴδη εἶναι καὶ ἓν ἀριθμῷ τὰς ἀρχὰς ἀλλὰ μὴ εἴδει, εἰρήκαμεν ἃ συμβαίνειν ἀναγκαῖον ἀδύνατα.—σύνεγγυς δὲ τούτων ἐστὶ τὸ διαπορῆσαι πότερον δυνάμει ἔστι τὰ στοιχεῖα ἤ τινʼ ἕτερον τρόπον. εἰ μὲν γὰρ ἄλλως πως, πρότερόν τι ἔσται τῶν ἀρχῶν ἄλλο (πρότερον  γὰρ ἡ δύναμις ἐκείνης τῆς αἰτίας, τὸ δὲ δυνατὸν οὐκ ἀναγκαῖον ἐκείνως πᾶν ἔχειν)· εἰ δʼ ἔστι δυνάμει τὰ στοιχεῖα, ἐνδέχεται μηθὲν εἶναι τῶν ὄντων· δυνατὸν γὰρ εἶναι καὶ τὸ μήπω ὄν· γίγνεται μὲν γὰρ τὸ μὴ ὄν, οὐθὲν δὲ γίγνεται τῶν εἶναι ἀδυνάτων.—ταύτας τε οὖν τὰς ἀπορίας ἀναγκαῖον ἀπορῆσαι περὶ τῶν ἀρχῶν, καὶ πότερον καθόλου εἰσὶν ἢ ὡς λέγομεν τὰ καθʼ ἕκαστα.","kind":"passage"},{"id":"RAW_DAZY_BOO3:P0058","text":"εἰ μὲν γὰρ καθόλου, οὐκ ἔσονται οὐσίαι (οὐθὲν γὰρ τῶν κοινῶν τόδε τι σημαίνει ἀλλὰ τοιόνδε, ἡ δʼ οὐσία τόδε τι· εἰ δʼ ἔσται τόδε τι καὶ ἓν θέσθαι τὸ κοινῇ κατηγορούμενον, πολλὰ ἔσται ζῷα ὁ Σωκράτης, αὐτός τε καὶ ὁ ἄνθρωπος καὶ τὸ ζῷον, εἴπερ σημαίνει ἕκαστον τόδε τι καὶ ἕν)·—εἰ μὲν οὖν καθόλου αἱ ἀρχαί, ταῦτα συμβαίνει· εἰ δὲ μὴ καθόλου ἀλλʼ ὡς τὰ καθʼ ἕκαστα, οὐκ ἔσονται ἐπιστηταί (καθόλου γὰρ ἡ ἐπιστήμη πάντων), ὥστʼ ἔσονται ἀρχαὶ ἕτεραι πρότεραι τῶν ἀρχῶν αἱ καθόλου κατηγορούμεναι, ἄνπερ μέλλῃ ἔσεσθαι αὐτῶν ἐπιστήμη.","kind":"passage"}],"sha256":"a9c358a637e3cdb14551d64d92cdd526cb00f009a11fe4d3c48d03fe5f7b9098","language_code":"grc","language_name":"Ἑλληνική","translations":[{"id":"SOURCE_TR_20261015T172307601383Z_8b91a914","language_code":"en","language_name":"English","title":"Book 3","source_sha256":"a9c358a637e3cdb14551d64d92cdd526cb00f009a11fe4d3c48d03fe5f7b9098","sha256":"448784094dc86ad6fa65bee2de5cd59e802de7997bde43e054f9a2f9d73b7faa","rendition_type":"machine","review_status":"machine","model":"gpt-5.6-terra","created":"2026-10-15T17:23:07.601400+00:00","paragraphs":[{"id":"RAW_DAZY_BOO3:P0001","text":"It is necessary for us, with a view to the knowledge sought, first to go through the matters concerning which we must first raise difficulties; these are both all the things which some have otherwise supposed concerning them, and whatever besides these happens to have been overlooked. For those who wish to find a way through, it is advantageous first to work through the difficulties well; for the subsequent finding of a way through is a resolution of the things previously at a loss, and it is impossible to resolve without knowing the bond. Rather, the perplexity of thought makes this clear with regard to the matter; for insofar as it is perplexed, it is in a condition similar to those who are bound; for it is impossible in either case to proceed forward. Hence one must have surveyed all the difficulties beforehand, both for these reasons and because those who inquire without first having worked through the difficulties are like men ignorant of where they ought to walk, and moreover do not even know whether they have at some time found what is sought or not; for the end is not manifest to such a person, whereas to one who has previously been at a loss it is manifest.","kind":"passage","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_DAZY_BOO3:P0001"]},{"id":"RAW_DAZY_BOO3:P0002","text":"Further, one is necessarily better equipped for judging if one has heard all the arguments, as though of opposing litigants and disputants.—The first difficulty is concerning the things about which we raised difficulties in the introductory discussions: whether it belongs to one science or to many to study the causes; and whether it belongs to the science to investigate only the first principles of substance, or also the principles from which all demonstrate—for example, whether it is possible to affirm and deny the same thing at the same time or not, and the other such matters. And if it is concerned with substance, whether there is one science of all substances or more than one; and if more than one, whether all are akin, or whether some of them should be called wisdoms and others something else. And this very question also must necessarily be investigated: whether one should say that there are only perceptible substances, or others besides these; and whether there is one kind of substance or several kinds, as is held by those who posit both Forms and mathematical objects as intermediates between these and perceptible things.","kind":"passage","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_DAZY_BOO3:P0002"]},{"id":"RAW_DAZY_BOO3:P0003","text":"Concerning these matters, then, as we say, inquiry must be made; and whether the study is concerned only with substances, or also with the attributes which belong to substances in their own right; and in addition, concerning same and other, like and unlike, and contrariety, and concerning prior and posterior, and all the other such things which dialecticians attempt to examine, making their inquiry from reputable opinions alone—whose task it is to study all these. Further, concerning all the things which belong to these themselves in their own right, and not only what each of them is, but also whether one thing is contrary to one thing. And whether principles and elements are the genera, or those into which each thing is divided as immanent constituents; and if genera are principles, whether the last genera predicated of individuals, or the first—for example, whether animal or man is a principle and is more so than the particular individual.","kind":"passage","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_DAZY_BOO3:P0003"]},{"id":"RAW_DAZY_BOO3:P0004","text":"But above all one must inquire and investigate whether there is some cause in its own right besides matter or not, and whether this is separable or not, and whether it is one or more in number; and whether there is anything besides the composite (by “the composite” I mean when something is predicated of matter) or nothing, or in some cases there is and in others not, and which beings are such.","kind":"passage","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_DAZY_BOO3:P0004"]},{"id":"RAW_DAZY_BOO3:P0005","text":"Further, whether principles are determinate in number or in form, both those in the accounts and those in the substrate; and whether the principles of perishable and imperishable things are the same or different, and whether all are imperishable, or whether those of perishable things are perishable. Further, the most difficult question of all and that involving the greatest perplexity: whether the one and being, as the Pythagoreans and Plato said, are not something else but the substance of beings; or not, but the substrate is something else, as Empedocles says it is Love, and another says fire, another water or air. And whether principles are universal or as the particular things, and whether they are potentially or actually; further, whether they are otherwise than in accordance with motion; for these questions too would produce much perplexity.","kind":"passage","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_DAZY_BOO3:P0005"]},{"id":"RAW_DAZY_BOO3:P0006","text":"In addition, whether numbers and lengths and figures and points are certain substances or not, and if they are substances, whether they are separate from perceptible things or immanent in these. For concerning all these matters not only is it difficult to find a way through to the truth, but it is not even easy to work through the difficulties well in argument.","kind":"passage","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_DAZY_BOO3:P0006"]},{"id":"RAW_DAZY_BOO3:P0007","text":"First, then, concerning the matters which we mentioned first: whether it belongs to one science or to several to study all the kinds of causes. For how could it belong to one science to know principles that are not contrary? Further, not all the causes belong to many beings. For how can a principle of motion belong to immovable things, or the nature of the good, if everything that is good in itself and through its own nature is an end and in this way a cause, because for the sake of it the other things both come to be and are; whereas the end, and that for the sake of which, is the end of some action, and all actions are accompanied by motion? Thus, among immovable things it could not be possible for this principle to exist, nor for there to be anything self-good.","kind":"passage","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_DAZY_BOO3:P0007"]},{"id":"RAW_DAZY_BOO3:P0008","text":"Hence even in mathematics nothing is demonstrated through this cause, nor is there any demonstration that something is better or worse; indeed no one at all makes any mention whatever of anything of this sort. Thus for these reasons certain sophists, such as Aristippus, used to deride them; for in the other arts, even the banausic ones, such as carpentry and shoemaking, all things are said to be so because they are better or worse, whereas the mathematical sciences make no account of goods and evils at all.\n\n—But if there are several sciences of causes, and one of one principle and another of another, which of these are we to say is the science sought, or which of those possessing them is most knowledgeable of the thing sought? For all the modes of causes may belong to the same thing; for example, in the case of a house, the source of motion is the art and the builder, that for the sake of which is the work, the matter is earth and stones, and the form is the account.","kind":"passage","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_DAZY_BOO3:P0008"]},{"id":"RAW_DAZY_BOO3:P0009","text":"From the distinctions made long ago, there is reason to call each of these sciences wisdom. Insofar as it is most commanding and ruling, and insofar as it is just that the other sciences, like slaves, should not even gainsay it, the science of the end and the good is such (for the sake of this are the other things). But insofar as wisdom was determined to be concerned with the first causes and with what is most knowable, the science of substance would be such. For when several persons know the same thing in several ways, we say that one who knows what the thing is by its being knows more than one who knows it by its not being; and even among the former, one knows more than another, especially one who knows what it is, rather than how much or of what sort it is, or what it is naturally fitted to do or suffer.","kind":"passage","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_DAZY_BOO3:P0009"]},{"id":"RAW_DAZY_BOO3:P0010","text":"Further, even in other cases, in each of the things for which there are demonstrations, we think knowledge is present when we know what it is—for example, what squaring is: the finding of a mean; and similarly in the other cases. But in cases of generations and actions and every change, knowledge is present when we know the principle of motion. This is different from and opposed to the end; so it would seem that the study of each of these causes belongs to a different science.—But then, concerning the demonstrative principles too, whether they belong to one science or to more than one is disputable (by demonstrative principles I mean the common opinions from which all demonstrate), such as that it is necessary either to affirm or deny everything, and that it is impossible for the same thing to be and not to be at the same time, and however many other such propositions there are: whether there is one science of these and of substance, or another; and if not one, which ought we to call the science now sought?","kind":"passage","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_DAZY_BOO3:P0010"]},{"id":"RAW_DAZY_BOO3:P0011","text":"It is not reasonable that it should be one science; for why is understanding these matters more proper to geometry than to any other science? If, then, it belongs alike to any science whatsoever, yet cannot belong to all, then, as it is not proper to the other sciences, so neither is it proper to the science which knows substances to know about them.","kind":"passage","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_DAZY_BOO3:P0011"]},{"id":"RAW_DAZY_BOO3:P0012","text":"At the same time, how will there be a science of them? For what each of these happens to be we already know (at any rate, the other arts use them as things known). But if there is a demonstrative science concerning them, there must be some underlying genus, with some things as its attributes and others as its axioms (for it is impossible for there to be demonstration of everything); for demonstration must proceed from certain things, be concerning something, and be of certain things. Hence it follows that there is some one genus of all things demonstrated, for all demonstrative sciences use the axioms.—But if the science of substance and the science concerning these are different, which of them is naturally more authoritative and prior? For axioms are most universal and are principles of all things; and if it is not the philosopher's task, whose else will it be to study the truth and falsehood concerning them?—And in general, whether there is one science of all substances or several. If not one, of what kind of substance should this science be posited? But that there is one science of all is not reasonable; for then there would be a demonstrative","kind":"passage","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_DAZY_BOO3:P0012"]},{"id":"RAW_DAZY_BOO3:P0013","text":"science of all attributes, if every demonstrative science studies, concerning some one substrate, the attributes belonging in their own right from common opinions. Therefore it belongs to the same science to study the attributes belonging in their own right to the same genus, from the same opinions. For that concerning which the science is one, and that from which it proceeds, are each the concern of one science, whether the same or different; and thus the attributes also, whether these sciences study them or one science from these principles.—Further, is the study concerned only with substances, or also with the attributes belonging to them? I mean, for example, if solid is a certain substance, and lines and planes, whether it belongs to the same science to know these and the attributes pertaining to each genus, concerning which the mathematical sciences demonstrate, or to another.","kind":"passage","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_DAZY_BOO3:P0013"]},{"id":"RAW_DAZY_BOO3:P0014","text":"For if it belongs to the same science, then the science of substance too would be a sort of demonstrative science; but there does not seem to be demonstration of what a thing is. But if it belongs to another science, what science will study the attributes concerning substance? For it is exceedingly difficult to give an answer to this.—Further, should one say that there are only perceptible substances, or also others besides these; and is there one kind of substance or are there several kinds, as those say who posit both Forms and the intermediates, concerning which they say that the mathematical sciences are concerned? In what sense we say that Forms are causes and substances in their own right has been stated in the first discussions concerning them. But although there are difficulties in many ways, it is no less absurd to say that there are certain natures besides those in the heaven, and to say that these are the same as perceptible things except that the former are eternal and the latter perishable.","kind":"passage","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_DAZY_BOO3:P0014"]},{"id":"RAW_DAZY_BOO3:P0015","text":"For they say that there is Man-itself and Horse-itself and Health-itself, but nothing else, doing something similar to those who say that the gods exist but are anthropomorphic; for neither did the latter make anything other than eternal humans, nor do the former make the Forms anything other than eternal perceptible things. Further, if one posits intermediates besides Forms and perceptible things, there will be many difficulties; for it is clear that there will similarly be lines besides the lines themselves and perceptible lines, and likewise with each of the other genera. Hence, since astronomy is one of these sciences, there will also be some heaven besides the perceptible heaven, and a sun and moon, and likewise the other things in the heaven.","kind":"passage","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_DAZY_BOO3:P0015"]},{"id":"RAW_DAZY_BOO3:P0016","text":"Yet how is one to believe these things? For it is not reasonable that it should be immovable, but that it should be in motion is altogether impossible. Likewise also with the objects treated by optics and by harmonics in mathematics; for these too cannot exist besides perceptible things, for the same reasons. For if there are intermediate perceptible things and perceptions, it is clear that there will also be animals intermediate between them and perishable animals. One might also raise the question: among what beings must these sciences be sought? For if geometry differs from land-measuring only in this, that the latter is of things we perceive while the former is of non-perceptible things, it is clear that there will be some science besides medicine and beside each of the other sciences, intermediate between medicine itself and this medicine. Yet how is this possible? For then there would also be certain healths besides perceptible health and Health-itself.","kind":"passage","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_DAZY_BOO3:P0016"]},{"id":"RAW_DAZY_BOO3:P0017","text":"At the same time, it is not true that land-measuring is concerned with perceptible and perishable magnitudes; for it would perish when they perish.—But neither would astronomy be concerned with perceptible magnitudes nor with this heaven. For perceptible lines are not such as the geometer speaks of (for nothing perceptible is straight or round in that way; a circle does not touch a ruler at a point, but as Protagoras said in criticizing geometers), nor are the motions and revolutions of the heaven like those concerning which astronomy gives its accounts, nor have points the same nature as stars. Yet some say that these so-called intermediates between Forms and perceptible things do exist, not separately from perceptible things but in them. To go through all the impossibilities that follow from this would require a longer discussion, but it is sufficient to consider such points as these.","kind":"passage","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_DAZY_BOO3:P0017"]},{"id":"RAW_DAZY_BOO3:P0018","text":"For it is not reasonable that only in these cases things should be so; rather, it is clear that Forms too could be in perceptible things (for both assertions rest on the same account). Further, it is necessary that two solids be in the same place, and that they not be immovable, since they are in perceptible things which are in motion. And in general, for what reason would anyone posit that they exist, but exist in perceptible things? For the same absurdities as those mentioned before will result; there will be some heaven besides the heaven, except that it is not separate but in the same place—which is still more impossible.","kind":"passage","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_DAZY_BOO3:P0018"]},{"id":"RAW_DAZY_BOO3:P0019","text":"Concerning these matters, then, there is much perplexity as to how one must posit things in order to attain the truth; and concerning principles, whether one should suppose genera to be elements and principles, or rather those primary immanent constituents from which each thing is composed. For example, the elements and principles of speech seem to be the things from which utterances are first composed, and not speech in common. And we call those things elements of diagrams whose demonstrations are immanent in the demonstrations of the other diagrams, either all or most of them. Further, concerning bodies, both those who say there are several elements and those who say there is one call principles the things from which they are composed and constituted. Thus Empedocles says that fire and water and the elements along with these are elements from which beings consist as immanent constituents, but he does not call these genera of beings.","kind":"passage","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_DAZY_BOO3:P0019"]},{"id":"RAW_DAZY_BOO3:P0020","text":"Moreover, if anyone wishes to observe the nature of any other thing—for example, of a bed, from what parts it is constituted and how they are put together—then he knows its nature.—On the basis of these arguments, then, the genera of beings would not be principles. But if we know each thing through its definitions, and the genera are principles of definitions, then the genera must also be principles of the things defined. And if to acquire knowledge of beings is to acquire knowledge of the Forms according to which beings are spoken of, the genera are principles of the Forms. Yet some of those who say that the one or being or the great and small are elements of beings also seem to use them as genera.—But it is not possible to state the principles in both ways.","kind":"passage","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_DAZY_BOO3:P0020"]},{"id":"RAW_DAZY_BOO3:P0021","text":"For the account of substance is one; but the definition through genera and the account stating the immanent constituents from which it consists will be different.—Moreover, even if genera are principles in the highest degree, should one suppose the first of the genera to be principles, or the last genera predicated of individuals? For this too is disputable. If universals are always more principles, clearly the highest genera are; for these are predicated of all things. There will then be as many principles of beings as there are first genera, so that both being and one will be principles and substances; for these are predicated most of all of all beings. But neither one nor being can be a genus of beings. For it is necessary that the differentiae of each genus both exist and each be one; but it is impossible either for the species of a genus to be predicated of their proper differentiae or for the genus to be predicated without its species. Hence, if one or being is a genus, no differentia will be either being or one.","kind":"passage","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_DAZY_BOO3:P0021"]},{"id":"RAW_DAZY_BOO3:P0022","text":"But surely, if there are no genera, there will not be principles either, if indeed the genera are principles. Further, the intermediates taken together with the differentiae will be genera down to the individuals (but as it is, some seem to be so and others do not seem to be so); and besides these, the differentiae are even more principles than the genera; but if these too are principles, the principles become, so to speak, infinite, especially if one posits the first genus as a principle. But surely, if the one is rather of the nature of a principle, and the indivisible is one, and everything indivisible is indivisible either in quantity or in form, and that which is indivisible in form is prior, while the genera are divisible into species, then the last predicate would rather be one; for man is not a genus of the particular men.","kind":"passage","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_DAZY_BOO3:P0022"]},{"id":"RAW_DAZY_BOO3:P0023","text":"Further, in cases in which there is prior and posterior, it is impossible that there should be anything besides these among them (for example, if two is the first of the numbers, there will not be any number besides the species of numbers; likewise, neither will there be any figure besides the species of figures; and if not in these cases, still less will there be genera besides the species in the other cases; for these seem most of all to have genera). But among individuals there is no prior and posterior. Further, where one thing is better and another worse, the better is always prior; so that there could not be a genus of these either.—From these considerations, then, it appears rather that the things predicated of individuals are principles than that the genera are; but again, it is not easy to say how one ought in turn to conceive these as principles.","kind":"passage","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_DAZY_BOO3:P0023"]},{"id":"RAW_DAZY_BOO3:P0024","text":"For the principle and the cause must exist apart from the things of which it is the principle, and must be capable of existing separately from them; but why should one suppose that there is something of this sort apart from the particular, except because it is predicated universally and of all things? Yet surely, if this is why, the more universal things must be posited as more truly principles; so that the primary genera would be principles.","kind":"passage","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_DAZY_BOO3:P0024"]},{"id":"RAW_DAZY_BOO3:P0025","text":"There is an aporia consequent upon these, and the most difficult and most necessary of all to consider, concerning which the discussion now stands. For if there is nothing besides the particulars, and the particulars are infinite, how is it possible to acquire knowledge of infinite things? For we know all things insofar as there is something one and the same, and insofar as something belongs universally.—But surely, if this is necessary and there must be something besides the particulars, it would be necessary for the genera to exist besides the particulars, either the lowest or the highest; but we have just raised the aporia that this is impossible.—Further, if there is most of all something besides the composite whenever something is predicated of the matter, must there, if it exists, be something besides all things, or besides some but not besides others, or besides none? If, then, there is nothing besides the particulars, there would be nothing intelligible, but all things would be perceptible, and there would be knowledge of nothing, unless someone says that perception is knowledge.","kind":"passage","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_DAZY_BOO3:P0025"]},{"id":"RAW_DAZY_BOO3:P0026","text":"Further, there would be nothing eternal or unmoved either (for all perceptible things perish and are in motion). Yet surely, if nothing is eternal, not even coming-to-be can be possible. For there must be something that comes to be and something from which it comes to be, and the last of these must be ungenerated, if the process comes to a stop and if it is impossible for something to come to be from what is not. Further, if there is coming-to-be and motion, there must also be an end (for no motion is infinite, but every motion has an end; and what is incapable of coming to be cannot come to be; and what has come to be must exist when it has first come to be). Further, if matter exists because it is ungenerated, it is still much more reasonable that the substance should exist, whatever it is that that matter becomes; for if neither this nor that will exist, nothing at all will exist; but if this is impossible, there must be something besides the composite, namely the form and the species.—But if someone in turn posits this, there is an aporia as to in what cases he will posit it and in what cases he will not.","kind":"passage","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_DAZY_BOO3:P0026"]},{"id":"RAW_DAZY_BOO3:P0027","text":"For that it is impossible in all cases is evident; for we should not posit some house existing besides the particular houses. Besides, will the substance be one for all things, for example for human beings? But this is absurd; for all things whose substance is one are one. Or will there be many and different substances? But this too is unreasonable. At the same time, how does matter become each of these things, and how is the composite both of these?—Further, one might also raise this aporia concerning the principles.","kind":"passage","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_DAZY_BOO3:P0027"]},{"id":"RAW_DAZY_BOO3:P0028","text":"For if they are one in species, nothing will be one in number, not even the one itself or being; and how will knowledge be possible, if there is not to be something one over all things?—But surely, if they are one in number and each of the principles is one, and not, as in perceptible things, different principles of different things (for example, of this syllable, though it is the same in species, the principles too are the same in species; and these are also different in number),—if, then, this is not so, but the principles of beings are one in number, there will be nothing other than the elements; for to say “one in number” or “particular” makes no difference. For this is how we speak of the particular: as that which is one in number; and the universal as that which is over these. Just as, then, if the elements of speech were determinate in number, all letters would necessarily be as many as the elements, since there could not be two of the same or more.","kind":"passage","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_DAZY_BOO3:P0028"]},{"id":"RAW_DAZY_BOO3:P0029","text":"No less an aporia has been left aside, both by the present thinkers and by their predecessors: whether the principles of perishable things and of imperishable things are the same or different. For if they are the same, how are some things perishable and others imperishable, and for what cause? Those around Hesiod, and all the theologians, considered only what was persuasive to themselves, and took no thought for us. For, making the principles gods and saying that things have come to be from gods, they say that those which have not tasted nectar and ambrosia become mortal—clearly using these names as familiar to themselves. Yet concerning the very application of these causes they have spoken beyond us; for if they partake of them for the sake of pleasure, nectar and ambrosia are in no way causes of being, whereas if for the sake of being, how can they be eternal while needing nourishment?—But it is not worth examining with seriousness those who speak sophisticially in myths; rather, from those who speak by demonstration we must inquire, asking why on earth, though they are from the same things, some beings are eternal in","kind":"passage","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_DAZY_BOO3:P0029"]},{"id":"RAW_DAZY_BOO3:P0030","text":"nature while others perish. Since they state no cause, nor is it reasonable that things should be so, it is clear that their principles and causes cannot be the same. For even Empedocles, whom one might suppose speaks most consistently with himself, has suffered the same thing. For he posits as a principle a certain cause of destruction, Strife; yet this too would seem no less to generate, apart from the One. For all the other things, except the god, are from this. At any rate, he says:","kind":"passage","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_DAZY_BOO3:P0030"]},{"id":"RAW_DAZY_BOO3:P0031","text":"From which all things, whatever were and are and will be hereafter,","kind":"passage","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_DAZY_BOO3:P0031"]},{"id":"RAW_DAZY_BOO3:P0032","text":"trees sprang forth, and men and women,","kind":"passage","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_DAZY_BOO3:P0032"]},{"id":"RAW_DAZY_BOO3:P0033","text":"beasts and birds and water-nourished fish,","kind":"passage","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_DAZY_BOO3:P0033"]},{"id":"RAW_DAZY_BOO3:P0034","text":"and long-lived gods.","kind":"passage","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_DAZY_BOO3:P0034"]},{"id":"RAW_DAZY_BOO3:P0035","text":"Empedocles Fr. 21.9-12 And apart from these things it is evident. For if it were not present in things, all things would be one, as he says; for whenever they come together,","kind":"passage","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_DAZY_BOO3:P0035"]},{"id":"RAW_DAZY_BOO3:P0036","text":"then Strife stood at the utmost limit.","kind":"passage","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_DAZY_BOO3:P0036"]},{"id":"RAW_DAZY_BOO3:P0037","text":"Empedocles Fr. 36.7 Hence it also follows for him that the most blessed god is less intelligent than the others; for he does not know all things, since he does not have Strife, and knowledge is of like by like.","kind":"passage","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_DAZY_BOO3:P0037"]},{"id":"RAW_DAZY_BOO3:P0038","text":"For by earth, he says, we behold earth, and by water, water,","kind":"passage","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_DAZY_BOO3:P0038"]},{"id":"RAW_DAZY_BOO3:P0039","text":"by aether divine aether, but by fire destructive fire,","kind":"passage","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_DAZY_BOO3:P0039"]},{"id":"RAW_DAZY_BOO3:P0040","text":"love by love, and Strife by baneful Strife.","kind":"passage","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_DAZY_BOO3:P0040"]},{"id":"RAW_DAZY_BOO3:P0041","text":"Empedocles Fr. 109 But, to return to the argument, this at least is clear: it follows for him that Strife is no more a cause of destruction than of being; and likewise Love is not a cause of being, for in bringing things together into the One it destroys the other things. And at the same time he states no cause of the change itself, except that it is so by nature:","kind":"passage","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_DAZY_BOO3:P0041"]},{"id":"RAW_DAZY_BOO3:P0042","text":"But when great Strife was nurtured in the limbs,","kind":"passage","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_DAZY_BOO3:P0042"]},{"id":"RAW_DAZY_BOO3:P0043","text":"and sprang up into its honours as the time was being fulfilled,","kind":"passage","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_DAZY_BOO3:P0043"]},{"id":"RAW_DAZY_BOO3:P0044","text":"which has been fixed for them in alternation by a broad oath:","kind":"passage","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_DAZY_BOO3:P0044"]},{"id":"RAW_DAZY_BOO3:P0045","text":"Empedocles Fr. 30 as though change were necessary; but he indicates no cause of the necessity. Yet he alone at least speaks consistently in this respect: for he does not make some of beings perishable and others imperishable, but all perishable except the elements. But the aporia now being stated is why some things are so and others not, if they are from the same things.—Let so much be said to show that the principles could not be the same. But if the principles are different, one aporia is whether these too will be imperishable or perishable. For if they are perishable, it is evident that these too must necessarily be from certain things (for everything perishes into those things from which it is); so that it follows that there are other principles prior to the principles. But this is impossible, whether the process comes to a stop or proceeds to infinity. Further, how will perishable things exist if their principles are destroyed? But if they are imperishable, why will perishable things arise from these imperishable things, while imperishable things arise from the others? For this is not reasonable, but either impossible or in need of much argument.","kind":"passage","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_DAZY_BOO3:P0045"]},{"id":"RAW_DAZY_BOO3:P0046","text":"Moreover, no one has even undertaken others, but they all speak of the same principles. But they nibble away at the first difficulty, as though taking it to be something small.","kind":"passage","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_DAZY_BOO3:P0046"]},{"id":"RAW_DAZY_BOO3:P0047","text":"But, of all questions, the most difficult to contemplate and the most necessary for knowing the truth is whether being and unity are substances of the things that are, and whether each of them is not something else that is, the one unity and the other being; or whether one must inquire what being and unity are, on the supposition that another nature underlies them. For some think that their nature is thus, others otherwise. Plato, indeed, and the Pythagoreans think that neither being nor unity is something else, but that this is their nature, their substance being precisely to be one and to be being. But those concerned with nature, such as Empedocles, speak as though reducing it to something more familiar, saying what the one is; for he would seem to say that it is Friendship (for this is at any rate the cause of all things being one), while others say that this one and being, from which the things that are both are and have come to be, are fire, and others air. And similarly too those who posit more elements; for they too must say that unity and being are as many as the principles which they assert to exist.","kind":"passage","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_DAZY_BOO3:P0047"]},{"id":"RAW_DAZY_BOO3:P0048","text":"The consequence is that, if one does not posit unity and being to be some substance, none of the other universals will be either (for these are the most universal of all things; and if there is no unity itself nor being itself, there will scarcely be anything else besides the things said of particulars); and further, if unity is not a substance, evidently number too will not be some separate nature among things that are (for number consists of units, and a unit is precisely a certain one). But if there is something that is unity itself and being itself, unity and being must be their substance; for no other universal is predicated of them, but these themselves are.—Yet if there is to be something that is being itself and unity itself, there is a great difficulty as to how there will be something other than these—I mean, how the things that are will be more than one. For what is other than being is not; so that, according to Parmenides' argument, it necessarily follows that all things that are are one, and that this is being.","kind":"passage","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_DAZY_BOO3:P0048"]},{"id":"RAW_DAZY_BOO3:P0049","text":"But in either way there is a difficulty; for whether unity is not substance or whether unity itself is substance, it is impossible for number to be substance. If it is not, the reason why has been stated before; but if it is, the same difficulty arises also concerning being. For from what, besides the one, will there be another one itself? For it must be not one; yet all things that are are either one or many, each of which is one.","kind":"passage","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_DAZY_BOO3:P0049"]},{"id":"RAW_DAZY_BOO3:P0050","text":"Further, if unity itself is indivisible, according to Zeno's axiom it would be nothing. For what, when added or subtracted, makes neither greater nor smaller, he says is not among things that are, plainly on the assumption that what is is magnitude; and if it is magnitude, it is bodily, for this is being in every dimension. The other things, however, in one way when added will make greater, in another way not at all—for example, a surface and a line; but a point and a unit in no way.—But since he considers this crudely, and since it is possible for there to be something indivisible, so as to have some defence even against that argument (for such a thing, when added, will not make greater, but will make more)—how then will magnitude arise from one such thing or from several such things? For it is like asserting that a line is composed of points.","kind":"passage","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_DAZY_BOO3:P0050"]},{"id":"RAW_DAZY_BOO3:P0051","text":"But further, even if someone supposes in this way that number comes to be, as some say, from unity itself and from some other thing that is not one, one must no less inquire why and how what comes to be will sometimes be number and sometimes magnitude, if the not-one was inequality and the same nature. For it is not clear either how magnitudes could arise from the one and this, or how from some number and this.","kind":"passage","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_DAZY_BOO3:P0051"]},{"id":"RAW_DAZY_BOO3:P0052","text":"A difficulty consequent upon these is whether numbers and bodies and surfaces and points are certain substances or not. For if they are not, it escapes us what being is and what the substances of things that are are. For affections, motions, relatives, dispositions, and accounts seem not to signify the substance of anything (for they are all said of some underlying subject, and none is a this); while the things that would seem most of all to signify substance—water, earth, fire, and air, from which composite bodies are constituted—have heats and colds and such affections which are not substances; only the body that has undergone these affections remains as something that is and as a certain substance. Yet body is less substance than surface, and surface than line, and line than unit and point; for body is bounded by these, and while these seem capable of existing without body, body without these is impossible.","kind":"passage","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_DAZY_BOO3:P0052"]},{"id":"RAW_DAZY_BOO3:P0053","text":"Therefore most people, and the earlier thinkers, thought that substance and being were body, and that the other things were affections of this, so that the principles of bodies were the principles of the things that are; but the later thinkers, seeming to be wiser than these, thought they were numbers. As we said, then, if these are not substances, there is no substance at all and nothing is; for surely the accidents of these are not worthy to be called beings.—Yet if this is agreed, that lengths and points are more substance than bodies, but we do not see of what bodies they could be (for they cannot be among sensibles), there would be no substance at all. Further, all these appear to be divisions of body, one in breadth, another in depth, another in length.","kind":"passage","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_DAZY_BOO3:P0053"]},{"id":"RAW_DAZY_BOO3:P0054","text":"Moreover, every kind of figure alike is present in a solid; so that if Hermes is not in the stone, neither is half of the cube in the cube as something marked off in this way; and therefore neither is a surface (for if any surface were, so too would be the one marking off the half). The same argument applies also to line, point, and unit. Hence, if body is substance in the highest degree, and these are more so than body, but these do not exist and are not certain substances, it escapes us what being is and what the substance of things that are is. For in addition to what has been said, absurd consequences also follow concerning coming-to-be and passing-away. For substance seems, if it was not previously and now is, or if it previously was and later is not, to undergo these changes along with coming-to-be and passing-away; but points and lines and surfaces cannot either come to be or pass away, being at one time and not being at another.","kind":"passage","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_DAZY_BOO3:P0054"]},{"id":"RAW_DAZY_BOO3:P0055","text":"For when bodies touch or are divided, at the same time points become one when they touch and two when they are divided. Thus when they are compounded they do not exist but have perished, while when they are divided the points that previously did not exist are; for surely the indivisible point was not divided into two. And if they come to be and pass away, from what do they come to be? The case is similar also with the now in time; for this too cannot come to be or pass away, yet it always seems to be different, though it is not a certain substance. Likewise it is clear that the case is the same with points, lines, and surfaces; for the same argument applies, since all alike are either limits or divisions.","kind":"passage","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_DAZY_BOO3:P0055"]},{"id":"RAW_DAZY_BOO3:P0056","text":"In general, one might raise the difficulty why it is necessary to seek certain other things besides sensibles and the intermediates, such as the Forms which we posit. For if it is because mathematical objects differ from the things here in this respect, that there are many things alike in kind, then their principles will not be determinate in number (just as neither are the principles of all the letters here determinate in number, though they are determinate in kind—unless one takes the principles of this particular syllable or this particular sound; in that case they will also be determinate in number). The same applies to the intermediates; for there too the things alike in kind are infinite. Thus, if besides sensibles and mathematical objects there are no other things of the kind which some call Forms, there will not be one substance numerically but only specifically, nor will the principles of things that are be a certain number in number but in kind.—If, then, this is necessary, it is necessary on this account to posit that the Forms exist.","kind":"passage","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_DAZY_BOO3:P0056"]},{"id":"RAW_DAZY_BOO3:P0057","text":"For even if those who speak thus do not articulate the matter well, still this is what they intend, and they must say that each of the Forms is a certain substance and that none is so accidentally.—Yet if we posit both that the Forms exist and that the principles are one in number and not in kind, we have stated the impossibilities that necessarily follow.—Closely connected with these is the question whether the elements exist potentially or in some other way. For if in some other way, there will be something else prior to the principles (for potentiality is prior to that cause, and not everything potential necessarily is in that way); but if the elements exist potentially, it is possible that none of the things that are exists. For even what does not yet exist can be possible; for what is not comes to be, but nothing comes to be of what is impossible to be.—It is necessary, then, to raise these difficulties concerning the principles, and whether they are universal or like what we call particulars.","kind":"passage","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_DAZY_BOO3:P0057"]},{"id":"RAW_DAZY_BOO3:P0058","text":"For if they are universal, they will not be substances (for none of the common predicates signifies a this, but rather such-and-such, whereas substance is a this; and if what is predicated in common is to be posited as a this and one, Socrates will be many animals—himself, man, and animal—if each signifies a this and one). If, then, the principles are universal, these consequences follow; but if they are not universal, but like particulars, they will not be knowable (for knowledge of all things is universal), so that there will be other principles prior to the principles, universally predicated, if there is to be knowledge of them.","kind":"passage","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_DAZY_BOO3:P0058"]}],"source_digest":"a6a284cdbcc6e98e6c31afb1f6dfbfc166f4653d98012aa49ccf8e7fe2b9db75","source_language_code":"grc","original_title":"Book 3"}],"passage_basis":"frozen-grounding","original_title":"Book 3","title_language_code":"en"},{"id":"RAW_DAZY_BOO4","title":"Book 4","order":4,"chapter_id":"CH_DAZY_BOO4","text":"ἔστιν ἐπιστήμη τις ἣ θεωρεῖ τὸ ὂν ᾗ ὂν καὶ τὰ τούτῳ ὑπάρχοντα καθʼ αὑτό. αὕτη δʼ ἐστὶν οὐδεμιᾷ τῶν ἐν μέρει λεγομένων ἡ αὐτή· οὐδεμία γὰρ τῶν ἄλλων ἐπισκοπεῖ καθόλου περὶ τοῦ ὄντος ᾗ ὄν, ἀλλὰ μέρος αὐτοῦ τι ἀποτεμόμεναι περὶ τούτου θεωροῦσι τὸ συμβεβηκός, οἷον αἱ μαθηματικαὶ τῶν ἐπιστημῶν. ἐπεὶ δὲ τὰς ἀρχὰς καὶ τὰς ἀκροτάτας αἰτίας ζητοῦμεν, δῆλον ὡς φύσεώς τινος αὐτὰς ἀναγκαῖον εἶναι καθʼ αὑτήν. εἰ οὖν καὶ οἱ τὰ στοιχεῖα τῶν ὄντων ζητοῦντες ταύτας τὰς ἀρχὰς ἐζήτουν, ἀνάγκη καὶ τὰ στοιχεῖα τοῦ ὄντος εἶναι μὴ κατὰ συμβεβηκὸς ἀλλʼ ᾗ ὄν· διὸ καὶ ἡμῖν τοῦ ὄντος ᾗ ὂν τὰς πρώτας αἰτίας ληπτέον. \n\n\n\n \n\n \n\nτὸ δὲ ὂν λέγεται μὲν πολλαχῶς, ἀλλὰ πρὸς ἓν καὶ μίαν τινὰ φύσιν καὶ οὐχ ὁμωνύμως ἀλλʼ ὥσπερ καὶ τὸ ὑγιεινὸν ἅπαν πρὸς ὑγίειαν, τὸ μὲν τῷ φυλάττειν τὸ δὲ τῷ ποιεῖν τὸ δὲ τῷ σημεῖον εἶναι τῆς ὑγιείας τὸ δʼ ὅτι δεκτικὸν αὐτῆς,  καὶ τὸ ἰατρικὸν πρὸς ἰατρικήν (τὸ μὲν γὰρ τῷ ἔχειν ἰατρικὴν λέγεται ἰατρικὸν τὸ δὲ τῷ εὐφυὲς εἶναι πρὸς αὐτὴν τὸ δὲ τῷ ἔργον εἶναι τῆς ἰατρικῆς), ὁμοιοτρόπως δὲ καὶ ἄλλα ληψόμεθα λεγόμενα τούτοις,—οὕτω δὲ καὶ τὸ ὂν λέγεται πολλαχῶς μὲν ἀλλʼ ἅπαν πρὸς μίαν ἀρχήν· τὰ μὲν γὰρ ὅτι οὐσίαι, ὄντα λέγεται, τὰ δʼ ὅτι πάθη οὐσίας, τὰ δʼ ὅτι ὁδὸς εἰς οὐσίαν ἢ φθοραὶ ἢ στερήσεις ἢ ποιότητες ἢ ποιητικὰ ἢ γεννητικὰ οὐσίας ἢ τῶν πρὸς τὴν οὐσίαν λεγομένων, ἢ τούτων τινὸς ἀποφάσεις ἢ οὐσίας· διὸ καὶ τὸ μὴ ὂν εἶναι μὴ ὄν φαμεν. καθάπερ οὖν καὶ τῶν ὑγιεινῶν ἁπάντων μία ἐπιστήμη ἔστιν, ὁμοίως τοῦτο καὶ ἐπὶ τῶν ἄλλων. οὐ γὰρ μόνον τῶν καθʼ ἓν λεγομένων ἐπιστήμης ἐστὶ θεωρῆσαι μιᾶς ἀλλὰ καὶ τῶν πρὸς μίαν λεγομένων φύσιν· καὶ γὰρ ταῦτα τρόπον τινὰ λέγονται καθʼ ἕν. δῆλον οὖν ὅτι καὶ τὰ ὄντα μιᾶς θεωρῆσαι ᾗ ὄντα. πανταχοῦ δὲ κυρίως τοῦ πρώτου ἡ ἐπιστήμη, καὶ ἐξ οὗ τὰ ἄλλα ἤρτηται, καὶ διʼ ὃ λέγονται. εἰ οὖν τοῦτʼ ἐστὶν ἡ οὐσία, τῶν οὐσιῶν ἂν δέοι τὰς ἀρχὰς καὶ τὰς αἰτίας ἔχειν τὸν φιλόσοφον.—ἅπαντος δὲ γένους καὶ αἴσθησις μία ἑνὸς καὶ ἐπιστήμη, οἷον γραμματικὴ μία οὖσα πάσας θεωρεῖ τὰς φωνάς· διὸ καὶ τοῦ ὄντος ᾗ ὂν ὅσα εἴδη θεωρῆσαι μιᾶς ἐστὶν ἐπιστήμης τῷ γένει, τά τε εἴδη τῶν εἰδῶν. εἰ δὴ τὸ ὂν καὶ τὸ ἓν ταὐτὸν καὶ μία φύσις τῷ ἀκολουθεῖν ἀλλήλοις ὥσπερ ἀρχὴ καὶ αἴτιον, ἀλλʼ οὐχ ὡς ἑνὶ λόγῳ δηλούμενα (διαφέρει δὲ οὐθὲν οὐδʼ ἂν ὁμοίως ὑπολάβωμεν, ἀλλὰ καὶ πρὸ ἔργου μᾶλλον)· ταὐτὸ γὰρ εἷς ἄνθρωπος καὶ ἄνθρωπος, καὶ ὢν ἄνθρωπος καὶ ἄνθρωπος, καὶ οὐχ ἕτερόν τι δηλοῖ κατὰ τὴν λέξιν ἐπαναδιπλούμενον τὸ εἷς ἄνθρωπος καὶ εἷς ὢν ἄνθρωπος (δῆλον δʼ ὅτι οὐ χωρίζεται οὔτʼ ἐπὶ γενέσεως οὔτʼ ἐπὶ φθορᾶς) , ὁμοίως δὲ καὶ ἐπὶ τοῦ ἑνός, ὥστε φανερὸν ὅτι ἡ πρόσθεσις ἐν τούτοις ταὐτὸ δηλοῖ, καὶ οὐδὲν ἕτερον τὸ ἓν παρὰ τὸ ὄν, ἔτι δʼ ἡ ἑκάστου οὐσία ἕν ἐστιν οὐ κατὰ συμβεβηκός, ὁμοίως δὲ καὶ ὅπερ ὄν τι·—ὥσθʼ ὅσα περ τοῦ ἑνὸς εἴδη, τοσαῦτα καὶ τοῦ ὄντος· περὶ ὧν τὸ τί ἐστι τῆς αὐτῆς ἐπιστήμης τῷ γένει θεωρῆσαι, λέγω δʼ οἷον περὶ ταὐτοῦ καὶ ὁμοίου καὶ τῶν ἄλλων τῶν τοιούτων. σχεδὸν δὲ πάντα ἀνάγεται τἀναντία εἰς τὴν ἀρχὴν ταύτην·  τεθεωρήσθω δʼ ἡμῖν ταῦτα ἐν τῇ ἐκλογῇ τῶν ἐναντίων. καὶ τοσαῦτα μέρη φιλοσοφίας ἔστιν ὅσαι περ αἱ οὐσίαι· ὥστε ἀναγκαῖον εἶναί τινα πρώτην καὶ ἐχομένην αὐτῶν. ὑπάρχει γὰρ εὐθὺς γένη ἔχον τὸ ὂν · διὸ καὶ αἱ ἐπιστῆμαι ἀκολουθήσουσι τούτοις. ἔστι γὰρ ὁ φιλόσοφος ὥσπερ ὁ μαθηματικὸς λεγόμενος· καὶ γὰρ αὕτη ἔχει μέρη, καὶ πρώτη τις καὶ δευτέρα ἔστιν ἐπιστήμη καὶ ἄλλαι ἐφεξῆς ἐν τοῖς μαθήμασιν.—ἐπεὶ δὲ μιᾶς τἀντικείμενα θεωρῆσαι, τῷ δὲ ἑνὶ ἀντίκειται πλῆθος—ἀπόφασιν δὲ καὶ στέρησιν μιᾶς ἐστὶ θεωρῆσαι διὰ τὸ ἀμφοτέρως θεωρεῖσθαι τὸ ἓν οὗ ἡ ἀπόφασις ἢ ἡ στέρησις (ἢ γὰρ ἁπλῶς λέγομεν ὅτι οὐχ ὑπάρχει ἐκεῖνο, ἤ τινι γένει· ἔνθα μὲν οὖν †τῷ ἑνὶ ἡ διαφορὰ πρόσεστι παρὰ τὸ ἐν τῇ ἀποφάσει†, ἀπουσία γὰρ ἡ ἀπόφασις ἐκείνου ἐστίν, ἐν δὲ τῇ στερήσει καὶ ὑποκειμένη τις φύσις γίγνεται καθʼ ἧς λέγεται ἡ στέρησις) —ὥστε καὶ τἀντικείμενα τοῖς εἰρημένοις, τό τε ἕτερον καὶ ἀνόμοιον καὶ ἄνισον καὶ ὅσα ἄλλα λέγεται ἢ κατὰ ταῦτα ἢ κατὰ πλῆθος καὶ τὸ ἕν, τῆς εἰρημένης γνωρίζειν ἐπιστήμης· ὧν ἐστὶ καὶ ἡ ἐναντιότης· διαφορὰ γάρ τις ἡ ἐναντιότης, ἡ δὲ διαφορὰ ἑτερότης. ὥστʼ ἐπειδὴ πολλαχῶς τὸ ἓν λέγεται, καὶ ταῦτα πολλαχῶς μὲν λεχθήσεται, ὅμως δὲ μιᾶς ἅπαντά ἐστι γνωρίζειν· οὐ γὰρ εἰ πολλαχῶς, ἑτέρας, ἀλλʼ εἰ μήτε καθʼ ἓν μήτε πρὸς ἓν οἱ λόγοι ἀναφέρονται. ἐπεὶ δὲ πάντα πρὸς τὸ πρῶτον ἀναφέρεται, οἷον ὅσα ἓν λέγεται πρὸς τὸ πρῶτον ἕν, ὡσαύτως φατέον καὶ περὶ ταὐτοῦ καὶ ἑτέρου καὶ τῶν ἐναντίων ἔχειν· ὥστε διελόμενον ποσαχῶς λέγεται ἕκαστον, οὕτως ἀποδοτέον πρὸς τὸ πρῶτον ἐν ἑκάστῃ κατηγορίᾳ πῶς πρὸς ἐκεῖνο λέγεται· τὰ μὲν γὰρ τῷ ἔχειν ἐκεῖνο τὰ δὲ τῷ ποιεῖν τὰ δὲ κατʼ ἄλλους λεχθήσεται τοιούτους τρόπους.—φανερὸν οὖν  ὅτι μιᾶς περὶ τούτων καὶ τῆς οὐσίας ἐστὶ λόγον ἔχειν (τοῦτο δʼ ἦν ἓν τῶν ἐν τοῖς ἀπορήμασιν), καὶ ἔστι τοῦ φιλοσόφου περὶ πάντων δύνασθαι θεωρεῖν.  εἰ γὰρ μὴ τοῦ φιλοσόφου, τίς ἔσται ὁ ἐπισκεψόμενος εἰ ταὐτὸ Σωκράτης καὶ Σωκράτης καθήμενος, ἢ εἰ ἓν ἑνὶ ἐναντίον, ἢ τί ἐστι τὸ ἐναντίον ἢ ποσαχῶς λέγεται; ὁμοίως δὲ καὶ περὶ τῶν ἄλλων τῶν τοιούτων. ἐπεὶ οὖν τοῦ ἑνὸς ᾗ ἓν καὶ τοῦ ὄντος ᾗ ὂν ταῦτα καθʼ αὑτά ἐστι πάθη, ἀλλʼ οὐχ ᾗ ἀριθμοὶ ἢ γραμμαὶ ἢ πῦρ, δῆλον ὡς ἐκείνης τῆς ἐπιστήμης καὶ τί ἐστι γνωρίσαι καὶ τὰ συμβεβηκότʼ αὐτοῖς. καὶ οὐ ταύτῃ ἁμαρτάνουσιν οἱ περὶ αὐτῶν σκοπούμενοι ὡς οὐ φιλοσοφοῦντες, ἀλλʼ ὅτι πρότερον ἡ οὐσία, περὶ ἧς οὐθὲν ἐπαΐουσιν, ἐπεὶ ὥσπερ ἔστι καὶ ἀριθμοῦ ᾗ ἀριθμὸς ἴδια πάθη, οἷον περιττότης ἀρτιότης, συμμετρία ἰσότης, ὑπεροχὴ ἔλλειψις, καὶ ταῦτα καὶ καθʼ αὑτοὺς καὶ πρὸς ἀλλήλους ὑπάρχει τοῖς ἀριθμοῖς (ὁμοίως δὲ καὶ στερεῷ καὶ ἀκινήτῳ καὶ κινουμένῳ ἀβαρεῖ τε καὶ βάρος ἔχοντι ἔστιν ἕτερα ἴδια), οὕτω καὶ τῷ ὄντι ᾗ ὂν ἔστι τινὰ ἴδια, καὶ ταῦτʼ ἐστὶ περὶ ὧν τοῦ φιλοσόφου ἐπισκέψασθαι τὸ ἀληθές. σημεῖον δέ· οἱ γὰρ διαλεκτικοὶ καὶ σοφισταὶ τὸ αὐτὸ μὲν ὑποδύονται σχῆμα τῷ φιλοσόφῳ· ἡ γὰρ σοφιστικὴ φαινομένη μόνον σοφία ἐστί, καὶ οἱ διαλεκτικοὶ διαλέγονται περὶ ἁπάντων, κοινὸν δὲ πᾶσι τὸ ὄν ἐστιν, διαλέγονται δὲ περὶ τούτων δῆλον ὅτι διὰ τὸ τῆς φιλοσοφίας ταῦτα εἶναι οἰκεῖα. περὶ μὲν γὰρ τὸ αὐτὸ γένος στρέφεται ἡ σοφιστικὴ καὶ ἡ διαλεκτικὴ τῇ φιλοσοφίᾳ, ἀλλὰ διαφέρει τῆς μὲν τῷ τρόπῳ τῆς δυνάμεως, τῆς δὲ τοῦ βίου τῇ προαιρέσει· ἔστι δὲ ἡ διαλεκτικὴ πειραστικὴ περὶ ὧν ἡ φιλοσοφία γνωριστική, ἡ δὲ σοφιστικὴ φαινομένη, οὖσα δʼ οὔ.\n\n \n\nἔτι τῶν ἐναντίων ἡ ἑτέρα συστοιχία στέρησις, καὶ πάντα ἀνάγεται εἰς τὸ ὂν καὶ τὸ μὴ ὄν, καὶ εἰς ἓν καὶ πλῆθος, οἷον στάσις τοῦ ἑνὸς κίνησις δὲ τοῦ πλήθους· τὰ δʼ ὄντα καὶ τὴν οὐσίαν ὁμολογοῦσιν ἐξ ἐναντίων σχεδὸν ἅπαντες συγκεῖσθαι· πάντες γοῦν τὰς ἀρχὰς ἐναντίας λέγουσιν· οἱ μὲν γὰρ περιττὸν καὶ ἄρτιον, οἱ δὲ θερμὸν καὶ ψυχρόν, οἱ δὲ πέρας καὶ ἄπειρον, οἱ δὲ φιλίαν καὶ νεῖκος. πάντα δὲ καὶ τἆλλα ἀναγόμενα φαίνεται εἰς τὸ ἓν καὶ πλῆθος (εἰλήφθω γὰρ ἡ ἀναγωγὴ ἡμῖν),  αἱ δʼ ἀρχαὶ καὶ παντελῶς αἱ παρὰ τῶν ἄλλων ὡς εἰς γένη ταῦτα πίπτουσιν. φανερὸν οὖν καὶ ἐκ τούτων ὅτι μιᾶς ἐπιστήμης τὸ ὂν ᾗ ὂν θεωρῆσαι. πάντα γὰρ ἢ ἐναντία ἢ ἐξ ἐναντίων, ἀρχαὶ δὲ τῶν ἐναντίων τὸ ἓν καὶ πλῆθος. ταῦτα δὲ μιᾶς ἐπιστήμης, εἴτε καθʼ ἓν λέγεται εἴτε μή, ὥσπερ ἴσως ἔχει καὶ τἀληθές. ἀλλʼ ὅμως εἰ καὶ πολλαχῶς λέγεται τὸ ἕν, πρὸς τὸ πρῶτον τἆλλα λεχθήσεται καὶ τὰ ἐναντία ὁμοίως,  καὶ εἰ μὴ ἔστι τὸ ὂν ἢ τὸ ἓν καθόλου καὶ ταὐτὸ ἐπὶ πάντων ἢ χωριστόν, ὥσπερ ἴσως οὐκ ἔστιν ἀλλὰ τὰ μὲν πρὸς ἓν τὰ δὲ τῷ ἐφεξῆς. καὶ διὰ τοῦτο οὐ τοῦ γεωμέτρου θεωρῆσαι τί τὸ ἐναντίον ἢ τέλειον ἢ ἓν ἢ ὂν ἢ ταὐτὸν ἢ ἕτερον, ἀλλʼ ἢ ἐξ ὑποθέσεως. ὅτι μὲν οὖν μιᾶς ἐπιστήμης τὸ ὂν ᾗ ὂν θεωρῆσαι καὶ τὰ ὑπάρχοντα αὐτῷ ᾗ ὄν, δῆλον, καὶ ὅτι οὐ μόνον τῶν οὐσιῶν ἀλλὰ καὶ τῶν ὑπαρχόντων ἡ αὐτὴ θεωρητική, τῶν τε εἰρημένων καὶ περὶ προτέρου καὶ ὑστέρου, καὶ γένους καὶ εἴδους, καὶ ὅλου καὶ μέρους καὶ τῶν ἄλλων τῶν τοιούτων.\n\n \n\n \n\n \n\nλεκτέον δὲ πότερον μιᾶς ἢ ἑτέρας ἐπιστήμης περί τε τῶν ἐν τοῖς μαθήμασι καλουμένων ἀξιωμάτων καὶ περὶ τῆς οὐσίας. φανερὸν δὴ ὅτι μιᾶς τε καὶ τῆς τοῦ φιλοσόφου καὶ ἡ περὶ τούτων ἐστὶ σκέψις· ἅπασι γὰρ ὑπάρχει τοῖς οὖσιν ἀλλʼ οὐ γένει τινὶ χωρὶς ἰδίᾳ τῶν ἄλλων. καὶ χρῶνται μὲν πάντες, ὅτι τοῦ ὄντος ἐστὶν ᾗ ὄν, ἕκαστον δὲ τὸ γένος ὄν· ἐπὶ τοσοῦτον δὲ χρῶνται ἐφʼ ὅσον αὐτοῖς ἱκανόν, τοῦτο δʼ ἔστιν ὅσον ἐπέχει τὸ γένος περὶ οὗ φέρουσι τὰς ἀποδείξεις· ὥστʼ ἐπεὶ δῆλον ὅτι ᾗ ὄντα ὑπάρχει πᾶσι (τοῦτο γὰρ αὐτοῖς τὸ κοινόν), τοῦ περὶ τὸ ὂν ᾗ ὂν γνωρίζοντος καὶ περὶ τούτων ἐστὶν ἡ θεωρία. διόπερ οὐθεὶς τῶν κατὰ μέρος ἐπισκοπούντων ἐγχειρεῖ λέγειν τι περὶ αὐτῶν, εἰ ἀληθῆ ἢ μή, οὔτε γεωμέτρης οὔτʼ ἀριθμητικός, ἀλλὰ τῶν φυσικῶν ἔνιοι, εἰκότως τοῦτο δρῶντες· μόνοι γὰρ ᾤοντο περί τε τῆς ὅλης φύσεως σκοπεῖν καὶ περὶ τοῦ ὄντος. ἐπεὶ δʼ ἔστιν ἔτι τοῦ φυσικοῦ τις ἀνωτέρω (ἓν γάρ τι γένος τοῦ ὄντος ἡ φύσις) , τοῦ καθόλου καὶ τοῦ περὶ τὴν πρώτην οὐσίαν θεωρητικοῦ καὶ ἡ περὶ τούτων ἂν εἴη σκέψις·  ἔστι δὲ σοφία τις καὶ ἡ φυσική, ἀλλʼ οὐ πρώτη. ὅσα δʼ ἐγχειροῦσι τῶν λεγόντων τινὲς περὶ τῆς ἀληθείας ὃν τρόπον δεῖ ἀποδέχεσθαι, διʼ ἀπαιδευσίαν τῶν ἀναλυτικῶν τοῦτο δρῶσιν· δεῖ γὰρ περὶ τούτων ἥκειν προεπισταμένους ἀλλὰ μὴ ἀκούοντας ζητεῖν.—ὅτι μὲν οὖν τοῦ φιλοσόφου, καὶ τοῦ περὶ πάσης τῆς οὐσίας θεωροῦντος ᾗ πέφυκεν, καὶ περὶ τῶν συλλογιστικῶν ἀρχῶν ἐστὶν ἐπισκέψασθαι, δῆλον· προσήκει δὲ τὸν μάλιστα γνωρίζοντα περὶ ἕκαστον γένος ἔχειν λέγειν τὰς βεβαιοτάτας ἀρχὰς τοῦ πράγματος, ὥστε καὶ τὸν περὶ τῶν ὄντων ᾗ ὄντα τὰς πάντων βεβαιοτάτας. ἔστι δʼ οὗτος ὁ φιλόσοφος. βεβαιοτάτη δʼ ἀρχὴ πασῶν περὶ ἣν διαψευσθῆναι ἀδύνατον· γνωριμωτάτην τε γὰρ ἀναγκαῖον εἶναι τὴν τοιαύτην (περὶ γὰρ ἃ μὴ γνωρίζουσιν ἀπατῶνται πάντες) καὶ ἀνυπόθετον. ἣν γὰρ ἀναγκαῖον ἔχειν τὸν ὁτιοῦν ξυνιέντα τῶν ὄντων, τοῦτο οὐχ ὑπόθεσις· ὃ δὲ γνωρίζειν ἀναγκαῖον τῷ ὁτιοῦν γνωρίζοντι, καὶ ἥκειν ἔχοντα ἀναγκαῖον. ὅτι μὲν οὖν βεβαιοτάτη ἡ τοιαύτη πασῶν ἀρχή, δῆλον· τίς δʼ ἔστιν αὕτη, μετὰ ταῦτα λέγωμεν. τὸ γὰρ αὐτὸ ἅμα ὑπάρχειν τε καὶ μὴ ὑπάρχειν ἀδύνατον τῷ αὐτῷ καὶ κατὰ τὸ αὐτό (καὶ ὅσα ἄλλα προσδιορισαίμεθʼ ἄν, ἔστω προσδιωρισμένα πρὸς τὰς λογικὰς δυσχερείας)· αὕτη δὴ πασῶν ἐστὶ βεβαιοτάτη τῶν ἀρχῶν· ἔχει γὰρ τὸν εἰρημένον διορισμόν. ἀδύνατον γὰρ ὁντινοῦν ταὐτὸν ὑπολαμβάνειν εἶναι καὶ μὴ εἶναι, καθάπερ τινὲς οἴονται λέγειν Ἡράκλειτον. οὐκ ἔστι γὰρ ἀναγκαῖον, ἅ τις λέγει, ταῦτα καὶ ὑπολαμβάνειν· εἰ δὲ μὴ ἐνδέχεται ἅμα ὑπάρχειν τῷ αὐτῷ τἀναντία (προσδιωρίσθω δʼ ἡμῖν καὶ ταύτῃ τῇ προτάσει τὰ εἰωθότα), ἐναντία δʼ ἐστὶ δόξα δόξῃ ἡ τῆς ἀντιφάσεως, φανερὸν ὅτι ἀδύνατον ἅμα ὑπολαμβάνειν τὸν αὐτὸν εἶναι καὶ μὴ εἶναι τὸ αὐτό· ἅμα γὰρ ἂν ἔχοι τὰς ἐναντίας δόξας ὁ διεψευσμένος περὶ τούτου. διὸ πάντες οἱ ἀποδεικνύντες εἰς ταύτην ἀνάγουσιν ἐσχάτην δόξαν· φύσει γὰρ ἀρχὴ καὶ τῶν ἄλλων ἀξιωμάτων αὕτη πάντων.\n\n\n\n \n\n \n\nεἰσὶ δέ τινες οἵ, καθάπερ εἴπομεν, αὐτοί τε ἐνδέχεσθαί φασι τὸ αὐτὸ εἶναι καὶ μὴ εἶναι,  καὶ ὑπολαμβάνειν οὕτως. χρῶνται δὲ τῷ λόγῳ τούτῳ πολλοὶ καὶ τῶν περὶ φύσεως. ἡμεῖς δὲ νῦν εἰλήφαμεν ὡς ἀδυνάτου ὄντος ἅμα εἶναι καὶ μὴ εἶναι, καὶ διὰ τούτου ἐδείξαμεν ὅτι βεβαιοτάτη  αὕτη τῶν ἀρχῶν πασῶν. ἀξιοῦσι δὴ καὶ τοῦτο ἀποδεικνύναι τινὲς διʼ ἀπαιδευσίαν· ἔστι γὰρ ἀπαιδευσία τὸ μὴ γιγνώσκειν τίνων δεῖ ζητεῖν ἀπόδειξιν καὶ τίνων οὐ δεῖ· ὅλως μὲν γὰρ ἁπάντων ἀδύνατον ἀπόδειξιν εἶναι (εἰς ἄπειρον γὰρ ἂν βαδίζοι, ὥστε μηδʼ οὕτως εἶναι ἀπόδειξιν), εἰ δέ τινων μὴ δεῖ ζητεῖν ἀπόδειξιν, τίνα ἀξιοῦσιν εἶναι μᾶλλον τοιαύτην ἀρχὴν οὐκ ἂν ἔχοιεν εἰπεῖν. ἔστι δʼ ἀποδεῖξαι ἐλεγκτικῶς καὶ περὶ τούτου ὅτι ἀδύνατον, ἂν μόνον τι λέγῃ ὁ ἀμφισβητῶν· ἂν δὲ μηθέν, γελοῖον τὸ ζητεῖν λόγον πρὸς τὸν μηθενὸς ἔχοντα λόγον, ᾗ μὴ ἔχει· ὅμοιος γὰρ φυτῷ ὁ τοιοῦτος ᾗ τοιοῦτος ἤδη. τὸ δʼ ἐλεγκτικῶς ἀποδεῖξαι λέγω διαφέρειν καὶ τὸ ἀποδεῖξαι, ὅτι ἀποδεικνύων μὲν ἂν δόξειεν αἰτεῖσθαι τὸ ἐν ἀρχῇ, ἄλλου δὲ τοῦ τοιούτου αἰτίου ὄντος ἔλεγχος ἂν εἴη καὶ οὐκ ἀπόδειξις. ἀρχὴ δὲ πρὸς ἅπαντα τὰ τοιαῦτα οὐ τὸ ἀξιοῦν ἢ εἶναί τι λέγειν ἢ μὴ εἶναι (τοῦτο μὲν γὰρ τάχʼ ἄν τις ὑπολάβοι τὸ ἐξ ἀρχῆς αἰτεῖν), ἀλλὰ σημαίνειν γέ τι καὶ αὑτῷ καὶ ἄλλῳ· τοῦτο γὰρ ἀνάγκη, εἴπερ λέγοι τι. εἰ γὰρ μή, οὐκ ἂν εἴη τῷ τοιούτῳ λόγος, οὔτʼ αὐτῷ πρὸς αὑτὸν οὔτε πρὸς ἄλλον. ἂν δέ τις τοῦτο διδῷ, ἔσται ἀπόδειξις· ἤδη γάρ τι ἔσται ὡρισμένον. ἀλλʼ αἴτιος οὐχ ὁ ἀποδεικνὺς ἀλλʼ ὁ ὑπομένων· ἀναιρῶν γὰρ λόγον ὑπομένει λόγον. ἔτι δὲ ὁ τοῦτο συγχωρήσας συγκεχώρηκέ τι ἀληθὲς εἶναι χωρὶς ἀποδείξεως .—πρῶτον μὲν οὖν δῆλον ὡς τοῦτό γʼ αὐτὸ ἀληθές, ὅτι σημαίνει τὸ ὄνομα τὸ εἶναι ἢ μὴ εἶναι τοδί, ὥστʼ οὐκ ἂν πᾶν οὕτως καὶ οὐχ οὕτως ἔχοι· ἔτι εἰ τὸ ἄνθρωπος σημαίνει ἕν, ἔστω τοῦτο τὸ ζῷον δίπουν. λέγω δὲ τὸ ἓν σημαίνειν τοῦτο· εἰ τοῦτʼ ἔστιν ἄνθρωπος, ἂν ᾖ τι ἄνθρωπος, τοῦτʼ ἔσται τὸ ἀνθρώπῳ εἶναι (διαφέρει δʼ οὐθὲν οὐδʼ εἰ πλείω τις φαίη σημαίνειν μόνον δὲ ὡρισμένα,  τεθείη γὰρ ἂν ἐφʼ ἑκάστῳ λόγῳ ἕτερον ὄνομα· λέγω δʼ οἷον, εἰ μὴ φαίη τὸ ἄνθρωπος ἓν σημαίνειν, πολλὰ δέ, ὧν ἑνὸς μὲν εἷς λόγος τὸ ζῷον δίπουν, εἶεν δὲ καὶ ἕτεροι πλείους, ὡρισμένοι δὲ τὸν ἀριθμόν· τεθείη γὰρ ἂν ἴδιον ὄνομα καθʼ ἕκαστον τὸν λόγον· εἰ δὲ μή , ἀλλʼ ἄπειρα σημαίνειν φαίη, φανερὸν ὅτι οὐκ ἂν εἴη λόγος· τὸ γὰρ μὴ ἓν σημαίνειν οὐθὲν σημαίνειν ἐστίν, μὴ σημαινόντων δὲ τῶν ὀνομάτων ἀνῄρηται τὸ διαλέγεσθαι πρὸς ἀλλήλους, κατὰ δὲ τὴν ἀλήθειαν καὶ πρὸς αὑτόν· οὐθὲν γὰρ ἐνδέχεται νοεῖν μὴ νοοῦντα ἕν, εἰ δʼ ἐνδέχεται, τεθείη ἂν ὄνομα τούτῳ τῷ πράγματι ἕν).—ἔστω δή, ὥσπερ ἐλέχθη κατʼ ἀρχάς, σημαῖνόν τι τὸ ὄνομα καὶ σημαῖνον ἕν· οὐ δὴ ἐνδέχεται τὸ ἀνθρώπῳ εἶναι σημαίνειν ὅπερ ἀνθρώπῳ μὴ εἶναι, εἰ τὸ ἄνθρωπος σημαίνει μὴ μόνον καθʼ ἑνὸς ἀλλὰ καὶ ἕν (οὐ γὰρ τοῦτο ἀξιοῦμεν τὸ ἓν σημαίνειν, τὸ καθʼ ἑνός, ἐπεὶ οὕτω γε κἂν τὸ μουσικὸν καὶ τὸ λευκὸν καὶ τὸ ἄνθρωπος ἓν ἐσήμαινεν, ὥστε ἓν ἅπαντα ἔσται· συνώνυμα γάρ). καὶ οὐκ ἔσται εἶναι καὶ μὴ εἶναι τὸ αὐτὸ ἀλλʼ ἢ καθʼ ὁμωνυμίαν, ὥσπερ ἂν εἰ ὃν ἡμεῖς ἄνθρωπον καλοῦμεν, ἄλλοι μὴ ἄνθρωπον καλοῖεν· τὸ δʼ ἀπορούμενον οὐ τοῦτό ἐστιν, εἰ ἐνδέχεται τὸ αὐτὸ ἅμα εἶναι καὶ μὴ εἶναι ἄνθρωπον τὸ ὄνομα, ἀλλὰ τὸ πρᾶγμα. εἰ δὲ μὴ σημαίνει ἕτερον τὸ ἄνθρωπος καὶ τὸ μὴ ἄνθρωπος, δῆλον ὅτι καὶ τὸ μὴ εἶναι ἀνθρώπῳ τοῦ εἶναι ἀνθρώπῳ· ὥστʼ ἔσται τὸ ἀνθρώπῳ  εἶναι μὴ ἀνθρώπῳ εἶναι· ἓν γὰρ ἔσται. τοῦτο γὰρ σημαίνει τὸ εἶναι ἕν, τὸ ὡς λώπιον καὶ ἱμάτιον, εἰ ὁ λόγος εἷς· εἰ δὲ ἔσται ἕν, ἓν σημανεῖ τὸ ἀνθρώπῳ εἶναι καὶ μὴ ἀνθρώπῳ. ἀλλʼ ἐδέδεικτο ὅτι ἕτερον σημαίνει. ἀνάγκη τοίνυν, εἴ τί ἐστιν ἀληθὲς εἰπεῖν ὅτι ἄνθρωπος, ζῷον εἶναι δίπουν (τοῦτο γὰρ ἦν ὃ ἐσήμαινε τὸ ἄνθρωπος)· εἰ δʼ ἀνάγκη τοῦτο, οὐκ ἐνδέχεται μὴ εἶναι τότε τὸ αὐτὸ ζῷον δίπουν (τοῦτο γὰρ σημαίνει τὸ ἀνάγκη εἶναι, τὸ ἀδύνατον εἶναι μὴ εἶναι )· οὐκ ἄρα ἐνδέχεται ἅμα ἀληθὲς εἶναι εἰπεῖν τὸ αὐτὸ ἄνθρωπον εἶναι καὶ μὴ εἶναι ἄνθρωπον. ὁ δʼ αὐτὸς λόγος καὶ ἐπὶ τοῦ μὴ εἶναι ἄνθρωπον·  τὸ γὰρ ἀνθρώπῳ εἶναι καὶ τὸ μὴ ἀνθρώπῳ εἶναι ἕτερον σημαίνει, εἴπερ καὶ τὸ λευκὸν εἶναι καὶ τὸ ἄνθρωπον εἶναι ἕτερον· πολὺ γὰρ ἀντίκειται ἐκεῖνο μᾶλλον, ὥστε σημαίνειν ἕτερον. εἰ δὲ καὶ τὸ λευκὸν φήσει τὸ αὐτὸ καὶ ἓν σημαίνειν, πάλιν τὸ αὐτὸ ἐροῦμεν ὅπερ καὶ πρότερον ἐλέχθη, ὅτι ἓν πάντα ἔσται καὶ οὐ μόνον τὰ ἀντικείμενα. εἰ δὲ μὴ ἐνδέχεται τοῦτο, συμβαίνει τὸ λεχθέν, ἂν ἀποκρίνηται τὸ ἐρωτώμενον. ἐὰν δὲ προστιθῇ ἐρωτῶντος ἁπλῶς καὶ τὰς ἀποφάσεις, οὐκ ἀποκρίνεται τὸ ἐρωτώμενον. οὐθὲν γὰρ κωλύει εἶναι τὸ αὐτὸ καὶ ἄνθρωπον καὶ λευκὸν καὶ ἄλλα μυρία τὸ πλῆθος· ἀλλʼ ὅμως ἐρομένου εἰ ἀληθὲς εἰπεῖν ἄνθρωπον τοῦτο εἶναι ἢ οὔ, ἀποκριτέον τὸ ἓν σημαῖνον καὶ οὐ προσθετέον ὅτι καὶ λευκὸν καὶ μέγα. καὶ γὰρ ἀδύνατον ἄπειρά γʼ ὄντα τὰ συμβεβηκότα διελθεῖν· ἢ οὖν ἅπαντα διελθέτω ἢ μηθέν. ὁμοίως τοίνυν εἰ καὶ μυριάκις ἐστὶ τὸ αὐτὸ ἄνθρωπος καὶ οὐκ ἄνθρωπος, οὐ προσαποκριτέον τῷ ἐρομένῳ εἰ ἔστιν ἄνθρωπος, ὅτι ἐστὶν ἅμα καὶ οὐκ ἄνθρωπος, εἰ μὴ καὶ τἆλλα ὅσα συμβέβηκε προσαποκριτέον, ὅσα ἐστὶν ἢ μὴ ἔστιν· ἐὰν δὲ τοῦτο ποιῇ, οὐ διαλέγεται.—ὅλως δʼ ἀναιροῦσιν οἱ τοῦτο λέγοντες οὐσίαν καὶ τὸ τί ἦν εἶναι. πάντα γὰρ ἀνάγκη συμβεβηκέναι φάσκειν αὐτοῖς, καὶ τὸ ὅπερ ἀνθρώπῳ εἶναι ἢ ζῴῳ εἶναι μὴ εἶναι. εἰ γὰρ ἔσται τι ὅπερ ἀνθρώπῳ εἶναι, τοῦτο οὐκ ἔσται μὴ ἀνθρώπῳ εἶναι ἢ μὴ εἶναι ἀνθρώπῳ (καίτοι αὗται ἀποφάσεις τούτου)· ἓν γὰρ ἦν ὃ ἐσήμαινε, καὶ ἦν τοῦτό τινος οὐσία. τὸ δʼ οὐσίαν σημαίνειν ἐστὶν ὅτι οὐκ ἄλλο τι τὸ εἶναι αὐτῷ. εἰ δʼ ἔσται αὐτῷ τὸ ὅπερ ἀνθρώπῳ εἶναι ἢ ὅπερ μὴ ἀνθρώπῳ εἶναι ἢ ὅπερ μὴ εἶναι ἀνθρώπῳ, ἄλλο ἔσται, ὥστʼ ἀναγκαῖον αὐτοῖς λέγειν ὅτι οὐθενὸς ἔσται τοιοῦτος λόγος, ἀλλὰ πάντα κατὰ συμβεβηκός· τούτῳ γὰρ διώρισται οὐσία καὶ τὸ συμβεβηκός· τὸ γὰρ λευκὸν τῷ ἀνθρώπῳ συμβέβηκεν ὅτι ἔστι μὲν λευκὸς ἀλλʼ οὐχ ὅπερ λευκόν. εἰ δὲ πάντα κατὰ συμβεβηκὸς λέγεται, οὐθὲν ἔσται πρῶτον τὸ καθʼ οὗ, εἰ ἀεὶ τὸ συμβεβηκὸς καθʼ ὑποκειμένου τινὸς σημαίνει τὴν κατηγορίαν.  ἀνάγκη ἄρα εἰς ἄπειρον ἰέναι. ἀλλʼ ἀδύνατον· οὐδὲ γὰρ πλείω συμπλέκεται δυοῖν· τὸ γὰρ συμβεβηκὸς οὐ συμβεβηκότι συμβεβηκός, εἰ μὴ ὅτι ἄμφω συμβέβηκε ταὐτῷ, λέγω δʼ οἷον τὸ λευκὸν μουσικὸν καὶ τοῦτο λευκὸν ὅτι ἄμφω τῷ ἀνθρώπῳ συμβέβηκεν. ἀλλʼ οὐχ ὁ Σωκράτης μουσικὸς οὕτως, ὅτι ἄμφω συμβέβηκεν ἑτέρῳ τινί. ἐπεὶ τοίνυν τὰ μὲν οὕτως τὰ δʼ ἐκείνως λέγεται συμβεβηκότα, ὅσα οὕτως λέγεται ὡς τὸ λευκὸν τῷ Σωκράτει, οὐκ ἐνδέχεται ἄπειρα εἶναι ἐπὶ τὸ ἄνω, οἷον τῷ Σωκράτει τῷ λευκῷ ἕτερόν τι συμβεβηκός· οὐ γὰρ γίγνεταί τι ἓν ἐξ ἁπάντων. οὐδὲ δὴ τῷ λευκῷ ἕτερόν τι ἔσται συμβεβηκός, οἷον τὸ μουσικόν· οὐθέν τε γὰρ μᾶλλον τοῦτο ἐκείνῳ ἢ ἐκεῖνο τούτῳ συμβέβηκεν, καὶ ἅμα διώρισται ὅτι τὰ μὲν οὕτω συμβέβηκε τὰ δʼ ὡς τὸ μουσικὸν Σωκράτει· ὅσα δʼ οὕτως, οὐ συμβεβηκότι συμβέβηκε συμβεβηκός, ἀλλʼ ὅσα ἐκείνως, ὥστʼ οὐ πάντα κατὰ συμβεβηκὸς λεχθήσεται. ἔσται ἄρα τι καὶ ὣς οὐσίαν σημαῖνον. εἰ δὲ τοῦτο, δέδεικται ὅτι ἀδύνατον ἅμα κατηγορεῖσθαι τὰς ἀντιφάσεις.—ἔτι εἰ ἀληθεῖς αἱ ἀντιφάσεις ἅμα κατὰ τοῦ αὐτοῦ πᾶσαι, δῆλον ὡς ἅπαντα ἔσται ἕν. ἔσται γὰρ τὸ αὐτὸ καὶ τριήρης καὶ τοῖχος καὶ ἄνθρωπος, εἰ κατὰ παντός τι ἢ καταφῆσαι ἢ ἀποφῆσαι ἐνδέχεται, καθάπερ ἀνάγκη τοῖς τὸν Πρωταγόρου λέγουσι λόγον. εἰ γάρ τῳ δοκεῖ μὴ εἶναι τριήρης ὁ ἄνθρωπος, δῆλον ὡς οὐκ ἔστι τριήρης· ὥστε καὶ ἔστιν, εἴπερ ἡ ἀντίφασις ἀληθής. καὶ γίγνεται δὴ τὸ τοῦ Ἀναξαγόρου, ὁμοῦ πάντα χρήματα· ὥστε μηθὲν ἀληθῶς ὑπάρχειν. τὸ ἀόριστον οὖν ἐοίκασι λέγειν, καὶ οἰόμενοι τὸ ὂν λέγειν περὶ τοῦ μὴ ὄντος λέγουσιν· τὸ γὰρ δυνάμει ὂν καὶ μὴ ἐντελεχείᾳ τὸ ἀόριστόν ἐστιν. ἀλλὰ μὴν λεκτέον γʼ αὐτοῖς κατὰ παντὸς παντὸς τὴν κατάφασιν ἢ τὴν ἀπόφασιν· ἄτοπον γὰρ εἰ ἑκάστῳ ἡ μὲν αὐτοῦ ἀπόφασις ὑπάρξει, ἡ δʼ ἑτέρου ὃ μὴ ὑπάρχει αὐτῷ οὐχ ὑπάρξει· λέγω δʼ οἷον εἰ ὀληθὲς εἰπεῖν τὸν ἄνθρωπον ὅτι οὐκ ἄνθρωπος, δῆλον ὅτι καὶ ἢ τριήρης ἢ οὐ τριήρης. εἰ μὲν οὖν ἡ κατάφασις, ἀνάγκη καὶ τὴν ἀπόφασιν· εἰ δὲ μὴ ὑπάρχει ἡ κατάφασις, ἥ γε ἀπόφασις ὑπάρξει μᾶλλον ἢ ἡ αὐτοῦ.  εἰ οὖν κἀκείνη ὑπάρχει, ὑπάρξει καὶ ἡ τῆς τριήρους· εἰ δʼ αὕτη, καὶ ἡ κατάφασις.—ταῦτά τε οὖν συμβαίνει τοῖς λέγουσι τὸν λόγον τοῦτον, καὶ ὅτι οὐκ ἀνάγκη ἢ φάναι ἢ ἀποφάναι. εἰ γὰρ ἀληθὲς ὅτι ἄνθρωπος καὶ οὐκ ἄνθρωπος, δῆλον ὅτι καὶ οὔτʼ ἄνθρωπος οὔτʼ οὐκ ἄνθρωπος ἔσται· τοῖν γὰρ δυοῖν δύο ἀποφάσεις, εἰ δὲ μία ἐξ ἀμφοῖν ἐκείνη, καὶ αὕτη μία ἂν εἴη ἀντικειμένη.—ἔτι ἤτοι περὶ ἅπαντα οὕτως ἔχει, καὶ ἔστι καὶ λευκὸν καὶ οὐ λευκὸν καὶ ὂν καὶ οὐκ ὄν, καὶ περὶ τὰς ἄλλας φάσεις καὶ ἀποφάσεις ὁμοιοτρόπως, ἢ οὒ ἀλλὰ περὶ μέν τινας, περί τινας δʼ οὔ. καὶ εἰ μὲν μὴ περὶ πάσας, αὗται ἂν εἶεν ὁμολογούμεναι· εἰ δὲ περὶ πάσας, πάλιν ἤτοι καθʼ ὅσων τὸ φῆσαι καὶ ἀποφῆσαι καὶ καθʼ ὅσων ἀποφῆσαι καὶ φῆσαι, ἢ κατὰ μὲν ὧν φῆσαι καὶ ἀποφῆσαι, καθʼ ὅσων δὲ ἀποφῆσαι οὐ πάντων φῆσαι. καὶ εἰ μὲν οὕτως, εἴη ἄν τι παγίως οὐκ ὄν, καὶ αὕτη βεβαία δόξα, καὶ εἰ τὸ μὴ εἶναι βέβαιόν τι καὶ γνώριμον, γνωριμωτέρα ἂν εἴη ἡ φάσις ἡ ἀντικειμένη· εἰ δὲ ὁμοίως καὶ ὅσα ἀποφῆσαι φάναι, ἀνάγκη ἤτοι ἀληθὲς διαιροῦντα λέγειν, οἷον ὅτι λευκὸν καὶ πάλιν ὅτι οὐ λευκόν, ἢ οὔ. καὶ εἰ μὲν μὴ ἀληθὲς διαιροῦντα λέγειν, οὐ λέγει τε ταῦτα καὶ οὐκ ἔστιν οὐθέν (τὰ δὲ μὴ ὄντα πῶς ἂν φθέγξαιτο ἢ βαδίσειεν;), καὶ πάντα δʼ ἂν εἴη ἕν, ὥσπερ καὶ πρότερον εἴρηται, καὶ ταὐτὸν ἔσται καὶ ἄνθρωπος καὶ θεὸς καὶ τριήρης καὶ αἱ ἀντιφάσεις αὐτῶν (εἰ γὰρ ὁμοίως καθʼ ἑκάστου, οὐδὲν διοίσει ἕτερον ἑτέρου· εἰ γὰρ διοίσει, τοῦτʼ ἔσται ἀληθὲς καὶ ἴδιον)· ὁμοίως δὲ καὶ εἰ διαιροῦντα ἐνδέχεται ἀληθεύειν, συμβαίνει τὸ λεχθέν, πρὸς δὲ τούτῳ ὅτι πάντες ἂν ἀληθεύοιεν καὶ πάντες ἂν ψεύδοιντο, καὶ αὐτὸς αὑτὸν ὁμολογεῖ  ψεύδεσθαι. ἅμα δὲ φανερὸν ὅτι περὶ οὐθενός ἐστι πρὸς τοῦτον ἡ σκέψις· οὐθὲν γὰρ λέγει. οὔτε γὰρ οὕτως οὔτʼ οὐχ οὕτως λέγει, ἀλλʼ οὕτως τε καὶ οὐχ οὕτως· καὶ πάλιν γε ταῦτα ἀπόφησιν ἄμφω, ὅτι οὔθʼ οὕτως οὔτε οὐχ οὕτως· εἰ γὰρ μή, ἤδη ἄν τι εἴη ὡρισμένον.—ἔτι εἰ ὅταν ἡ φάσις ἀληθὴς ᾖ, ἡ ἀπόφασις ψευδής, κἂν αὕτη ἀληθὴς ᾖ, ἡ κατάφασις ψευδής, οὐκ ἂν εἴη τὸ αὐτὸ ἅμα φάναι καὶ ἀποφάναι ἀληθῶς.  ἀλλʼ ἴσως φαῖεν ἂν τοῦτʼ εἶναι τὸ ἐξ ἀρχῆς κείμενον.—ἔτι ἆρα ὁ μὲν ἢ ἔχειν πως ὑπολαμβάνων ἢ μὴ ἔχειν διέψευσται, ὁ δὲ ἄμφω ἀληθεύει; εἰ γὰρ ἀληθεύει, τί ἂν εἴη τὸ λεγόμενον ὅτι τοιαύτη τῶν ὄντων ἡ φύσις; εἰ δὲ μὴ ἀληθεύει, ἀλλὰ μᾶλλον ἀληθεύει ἢ ὁ ἐκείνως ὑπολαμβάνων, ἤδη πως ἔχοι ἂν τὰ ὄντα, καὶ τοῦτʼ ἀληθὲς ἂν εἴη, καὶ οὐχ ἅμα καὶ οὐκ ἀληθές. εἰ δὲ ὁμοίως ἅπαντες καὶ ψεύδονται καὶ ἀληθῆ λέγουσιν, οὔτε φθέγξασθαι οὔτʼ εἰπεῖν τῷ τοιούτῳ ἔσται· ἅμα γὰρ ταῦτά τε καὶ οὐ ταῦτα λέγει. εἰ δὲ μηθὲν ὑπολαμβάνει ἀλλʼ ὁμοίως οἴεται καὶ οὐκ οἴεται, τί ἂν διαφερόντως ἔχοι τῶν γε φυτῶν; ὅθεν καὶ μάλιστα φανερόν ἐστιν ὅτι οὐδεὶς οὕτω διάκειται οὔτε τῶν ἄλλων οὔτε τῶν λεγόντων τὸν λόγον τοῦτον. διὰ τί γὰρ βαδίζει Μέγαράδε ἀλλʼ οὐχ ἡσυχάζει, οἰόμενος βαδίζειν δεῖν; οὐδʼ εὐθέως ἕωθεν πορεύεται εἰς φρέαρ ἢ εἰς φάραγγα, ἐὰν τύχῃ, ἀλλὰ φαίνεται εὐλαβούμενος, ὡς οὐχ ὁμοίως οἰόμενος μὴ ἀγαθὸν εἶναι τὸ ἐμπεσεῖν καὶ ἀγαθόν; δῆλον ἄρα ὅτι τὸ μὲν βέλτιον ὑπολαμβάνει τὸ δʼ οὐ βέλτιον. εἰ δὲ τοῦτο, καὶ τὸ μὲν ἄνθρωπον τὸ δʼ οὐκ ἄνθρωπον καὶ τὸ μὲν γλυκὺ τὸ δʼ οὐ γλυκὺ ἀνάγκη ὑπολαμβάνειν. οὐ γὰρ ἐξ ἴσου ἅπαντα ζητεῖ καὶ ὑπολαμβάνει, ὅταν οἰηθεὶς βέλτιον εἶναι τὸ πιεῖν ὕδωρ καὶ ἰδεῖν ἄνθρωπον εἶτα ζητῇ αὐτά· καίτοι ἔδει γε, εἰ ταὐτὸν ἦν ὁμοίως καὶ ἄνθρωπος καὶ οὐκ ἄνθρωπος. ἀλλʼ ὅπερ ἐλέχθη, οὐθεὶς ὃς οὐ φαίνεται τὰ μὲν εὐλαβούμενος τὰ δʼ οὔ· ὥστε, ὡς ἔοικε, πάντες ὑπολαμβάνουσιν ἔχειν ἁπλῶς, εἰ μὴ περὶ ἅπαντα, ἀλλὰ περὶ τὸ ἄμεινον καὶ χεῖρον. εἰ δὲ μὴ ἐπιστάμενοι ἀλλὰ δοξάζοντες, πολὺ μᾶλλον ἐπιμελητέον ἂν εἴη τῆς ἀληθείας, ὥσπερ καὶ νοσώδει ὄντι ἢ ὑγιεινῷ τῆς ὑγιείας· καὶ γὰρ ὁ δοξάζων πρὸς τὸν ἐπιστάμενον οὐχ ὑγιεινῶς διάκειται πρὸς τὴν ἀλήθειαν.—ἔτι εἰ ὅτι μάλιστα πάντα οὕτως ἔχει καὶ οὐχ οὕτως, ἀλλὰ τό γε μᾶλλον καὶ ἧττον ἔνεστιν ἐν τῇ φύσει τῶν ὄντων· οὐ γὰρ ἂν ὁμοίως φήσαιμεν εἶναι τὰ δύο ἄρτια καὶ τὰ τρία, οὐδʼ ὁμοίως διέψευσται ὁ τὰ τέτταρα πέντε οἰόμενος καὶ ὁ χίλια. εἰ οὖν μὴ ὁμοίως, δῆλον ὅτι ἅτερος ἧττον, ὥστε μᾶλλον ἀληθεύει. εἰ οὖν τὸ μᾶλλον ἐγγύτερον,  εἴη γε ἄν τι ἀληθὲς οὗ ἐγγύτερον τὸ μᾶλλον ἀληθές. κἂν εἰ μὴ ἔστιν, ἀλλʼ ἤδη γέ τι ἔστι βεβαιότερον καὶ ἀληθινώτερον, καὶ τοῦ λόγου ἀπηλλαγμένοι ἂν εἴημεν τοῦ ἀκράτου καὶ κωλύοντός τι τῇ διανοίᾳ ὁρίσαι. \n\n\n\n \n\n \n\nἔστι δʼ ἀπὸ τῆς αὐτῆς δόξης καὶ ὁ Πρωταγόρου λόγος, καὶ ἀνάγκη ὁμοίως αὐτοὺς ἄμφω ἢ εἶναι ἢ μὴ εἶναι· εἴτε γὰρ τὰ δοκοῦντα πάντα ἐστὶν ἀληθῆ καὶ τὰ φαινόμενα, ἀνάγκη εἶναι πάντα ἅμα ἀληθῆ καὶ ψευδῆ (πολλοὶ γὰρ τἀναντία ὑπολαμβάνουσιν ἀλλήλοις, καὶ τοὺς μὴ ταὐτὰ δοξάζοντας ἑαυτοῖς διεψεῦσθαι νομίζουσιν· ὥστʼ ἀνάγκη τὸ αὐτὸ εἶναί τε καὶ μὴ εἶναι), καὶ εἰ τοῦτʼ ἔστιν, ἀνάγκη τὰ δοκοῦντα εἶναι πάντʼ ἀληθῆ (τὰ ἀντικείμενα γὰρ δοξάζουσιν ἀλλήλοις οἱ διεψευσμένοι καὶ ἀληθεύοντες· εἰ οὖν ἔχει τὰ ὄντα οὕτως, ἀληθεύσουσι πάντες) . ὅτι μὲν οὖν ἀπὸ τῆς αὐτῆς εἰσὶ διανοίας ἀμφότεροι οἱ λόγοι, δῆλον· ἔστι δʼ οὐχ ὁ αὐτὸς τρόπος πρὸς ἅπαντας τῆς ἐντεύξεως· οἱ μὲν γὰρ πειθοῦς δέονται οἱ δὲ βίας. ὅσοι μὲν γὰρ ἐκ τοῦ ἀπορῆσαι ὑπέλαβον οὕτως, τούτων εὐΐατος ἡ ἄγνοια (οὐ γὰρ πρὸς τὸν λόγον ἀλλὰ πρὸς τὴν διάνοιαν ἡ ἀπάντησις αὐτῶν)· ὅσοι δὲ λόγου χάριν λέγουσι, τούτων δʼ ἔλεγχος ἴασις τοῦ ἐν τῇ φωνῇ λόγου καὶ τοῦ ἐν τοῖς ὀνόμασιν. ἐλήλυθε δὲ τοῖς διαποροῦσιν αὕτη ἡ δόξα ἐκ τῶν αἰσθητῶν, ἡ μὲν τοῦ ἅμα τὰς ἀντιφάσεις καὶ τἀναντία ὑπάρχειν ὁρῶσιν ἐκ ταὐτοῦ γιγνόμενα τἀναντία· εἰ οὖν μὴ ἐνδέχεται γίγνεσθαι τὸ μὴ ὄν, προϋπῆρχεν ὁμοίως τὸ πρᾶγμα ἄμφω ὄν, ὥσπερ καὶ Ἀναξαγόρας μεμῖχθαι πᾶν ἐν παντί φησι καὶ Δημόκριτος· καὶ γὰρ οὗτος τὸ κενὸν καὶ τὸ πλῆρες ὁμοίως καθʼ ὁτιοῦν ὑπάρχειν μέρος, καίτοι τὸ μὲν ὂν τούτων εἶναι τὸ δὲ μὴ ὄν. πρὸς μὲν οὖν τοὺς ἐκ τούτων ὑπολαμβάνοντας ἐροῦμεν ὅτι τρόπον μέν τινα ὀρθῶς λέγουσι τρόπον δέ τινα ἀγνοοῦσιν· τὸ γὰρ ὂν λέγεται διχῶς, ὥστʼ ἔστιν ὃν τρόπον ἐνδέχεται γίγνεσθαί τι ἐκ τοῦ μὴ ὄντος, ἔστι δʼ ὃν οὔ, καὶ ἅμα τὸ αὐτὸ εἶναι καὶ ὂν καὶ μὴ ὄν, ἀλλʼ οὐ κατὰ ταὐτὸ · δυνάμει  μὲν γὰρ ἐνδέχεται ἅμα ταὐτὸ εἶναι τὰ ἐναντία, ἐντελεχείᾳ δʼ οὔ. ἔτι δʼ ἀξιώσομεν αὐτοὺς ὑπολαμβάνειν καὶ ἄλλην τινὰ οὐσίαν εἶναι τῶν ὄντων ᾗ οὔτε κίνησις ὑπάρχει οὔτε φθορὰ οὔτε γένεσις τὸ παράπαν.  —ὅμοιως δὲ καὶ ἡ περὶ τὰ φαινόμενα ἀλήθεια ἐνίοις ἐκ τῶν αἰσθητῶν ἐλήλυθεν. τὸ μὲν γὰρ ἀληθὲς οὐ πλήθει κρίνεσθαι οἴονται προσήκειν οὐδὲ ὀλιγότητι, τὸ δʼ αὐτὸ τοῖς μὲν γλυκὺ γευομένοις δοκεῖν εἶναι τοῖς δὲ πικρόν, ὥστʼ εἰ πάντες ἔκαμνον ἢ πάντες παρεφρόνουν, δύο δʼ ἢ τρεῖς ὑγίαινον ἢ νοῦν εἶχον, δοκεῖν ἂν τούτους κάμνειν καὶ παραφρονεῖν τοὺς δʼ ἄλλους οὔ· ἔτι δὲ καὶ πολλοῖς τῶν ἄλλων ζῴων τἀναντία  φαίνεσθαι καὶ ἡμῖν, καὶ αὐτῷ δὲ ἑκάστῳ πρὸς αὑτὸν οὐ ταὐτὰ κατὰ τὴν αἴσθησιν ἀεὶ δοκεῖν. ποῖα οὖν τούτων ἀληθῆ ἢ ψευδῆ, ἄδηλον· οὐθὲν γὰρ μᾶλλον τάδε ἢ τάδε ἀληθῆ, ἀλλʼ ὁμοίως. διὸ Δημόκριτός γέ φησιν ἤτοι οὐθὲν εἶναι ἀληθὲς ἢ ἡμῖν γʼ ἄδηλον. ὅλως δὲ διὰ τὸ ὑπολαμβάνειν φρόνησιν μὲν τὴν αἴσθησιν, ταύτην δʼ εἶναι ἀλλοίωσιν, τὸ φαινόμενον κατὰ τὴν αἴσθησιν ἐξ ἀνάγκης ἀληθὲς εἶναί φασιν· ἐκ τούτων γὰρ καὶ Ἐμπεδοκλῆς καὶ Δημόκριτος καὶ τῶν ἄλλων ὡς ἔπος εἰπεῖν ἕκαστος τοιαύταις δόξαις γεγένηνται ἔνοχοι. καὶ γὰρ Ἐμπεδοκλῆς μεταβάλλοντας τὴν ἕξιν μεταβάλλειν φησὶ τὴν φρόνησιν·  \n\nπρὸς παρεὸν γὰρ μῆτις ἐναύξεται ἀνθρώποισιν.\n\n  Empedocles Fr. 106καὶ ἐν ἑτέροις δὲ λέγει ὅτι  \n\nὅσσον δʼ ἀλλοῖοι μετέφυν, τόσον ἄρ σφισιν αἰεὶ\n\n \n\nκαὶ τὸ φρονεῖν ἀλλοῖα παρίστατο.\n\n  Empedocles Fr. 108 καὶ Παρμενίδης δὲ ἀποφαίνεται τὸν αὐτὸν τρόπον·  \n\nὡς γὰρ ἑκάστοτʼ ἔχει κρᾶσιν μελέων πολυκάμπτων,\n\n \n\nτὼς νόος ἀνθρώποισι παρίσταται· τὸ γὰρ αὐτὸ\n\n \n\nἔστιν ὅπερ φρονέει, μελέων φύσις ἀνθρώποισιν\n\n \n\n καὶ πᾶσιν καὶ παντί· τὸ γὰρ πλέον ἐστὶ νόημα.\n\n  Parmenides Fr. 16 Ἀναξαγόρου δὲ καὶ ἀπόφθεγμα μνημονεύεται πρὸς τῶν ἑταίρων τινάς, ὅτι τοιαῦτʼ αὐτοῖς ἔσται τὰ ὄντα οἷα ἂν ὑπολάβωσιν. φασὶ δὲ καὶ τὸν Ὅμηρον ταύτην ἔχοντα φαίνεσθαι τὴν δόξαν, ὅτι ἐποίησε τὸν Ἕκτορα, ὡς ἐξέστη ὑπὸ τῆς πληγῆς, κεῖσθαι ἀλλοφρονέοντα, ὡς φρονοῦντας μὲν καὶ τοὺς παραφρονοῦντας ἀλλʼ οὐ ταὐτά. δῆλον οὖν ὅτι, εἰ ἀμφότεραι φρονήσεις, καὶ τὰ ὄντα ἅμα οὕτω τε καὶ οὐχ οὕτως ἔχει. ᾗ καὶ χαλεπώτατον τὸ συμβαῖνόν ἐστιν· εἰ γὰρ οἱ μάλιστα τὸ ἐνδεχόμενον ἀληθὲς ἑωρακότες—οὗτοι δʼ εἰσὶν οἱ μάλιστα ζητοῦντες αὐτὸ καὶ φιλοῦντες—οὗτοι τοιαύτας ἔχουσι τὰς δόξας καὶ ταῦτα ἀποφαίνονται περὶ τῆς ἀληθείας, πῶς οὐκ ἄξιον ἀθυμῆσαι τοὺς φιλοσοφεῖν ἐγχειροῦντας; τὸ γὰρ τὰ πετόμενα διώκειν τὸ ζητεῖν ἂν εἴη τὴν ἀλήθειαν.  —αἴτιον δὲ τῆς δόξης τούτοις ὅτι περὶ τῶν ὄντων μὲν τὴν ἀλήθειαν ἐσκόπουν, τὰ δʼ ὄντα ὑπέλαβον εἶναι τὰ αἰσθητὰ μόνον· ἐν δὲ τούτοις πολλὴ ἡ τοῦ ἀορίστου φύσις ἐνυπάρχει καὶ ἡ τοῦ ὄντος οὕτως ὥσπερ εἴπομεν· διὸ εἰκότως μὲν λέγουσιν, οὐκ ἀληθῆ δὲ λέγουσιν (οὕτω γὰρ ἁρμόττει μᾶλλον εἰπεῖν ἢ ὥσπερ Ἐπίχαρμος εἰς Ξενοφάνην). ἔτι δὲ πᾶσαν ὁρῶντες ταύτην κινουμένην τὴν φύσιν, κατὰ δὲ τοῦ μεταβάλλοντος οὐθὲν ἀληθευόμενον, περί γε τὸ πάντῃ πάντως μεταβάλλον οὐκ ἐνδέχεσθαι ἀληθεύειν. ἐκ γὰρ ταύτης τῆς ὑπολήψεως ἐξήνθησεν ἡ ἀκροτάτη δόξα τῶν εἰρημένων, ἡ τῶν φασκόντων ἡρακλειτίζειν καὶ οἵαν Κρατύλος εἶχεν, ὃς τὸ τελευταῖον οὐθὲν ᾤετο δεῖν λέγειν ἀλλὰ τὸν δάκτυλον ἐκίνει μόνον, καὶ Ἡρακλείτῳ ἐπετίμα εἰπόντι ὅτι δὶς τῷ αὐτῷ ποταμῷ οὐκ ἔστιν ἐμβῆναι· αὐτὸς γὰρ ᾤετο οὐδʼ ἅπαξ. ἡμεῖς δὲ καὶ πρὸς τοῦτον τὸν λόγον ἐροῦμεν ὅτι τὸ μὲν μεταβάλλον ὅτε μεταβάλλει ἔχει τινὰ αὐτοῖς λόγον μὴ οἴεσθαι εἶναι, καίτοι ἔστι γε ἀμφισβητήσιμον· τό τε γὰρ ἀποβάλλον ἔχει τι τοῦ ἀποβαλλομένου, καὶ τοῦ γιγνομένου ἤδη ἀνάγκη τι εἶναι, ὅλως τε εἰ φθείρεται, ὑπάρξει τι ὄν, καὶ εἰ γίγνεται, ἐξ οὗ γίγνεται καὶ ὑφʼ οὗ γεννᾶται ἀναγκαῖον εἶναι, καὶ τοῦτο μὴ ἰέναι εἰς ἄπειρον. ἀλλὰ ταῦτα παρέντες ἐκεῖνα λέγωμεν, ὅτι οὐ ταὐτό ἐστι τὸ μεταβάλλειν κατὰ τὸ ποσὸν καὶ κατὰ τὸ ποιόν· κατὰ μὲν οὖν τὸ ποσὸν ἔστω μὴ μένον, ἀλλὰ κατὰ τὸ εἶδος ἅπαντα γιγνώσκομεν. ἔτι δʼ ἄξιον ἐπιτιμῆσαι τοῖς οὕτως ὑπολαμβάνουσιν, ὅτι καὶ αὐτῶν τῶν αἰσθητῶν ἐπὶ τῶν ἐλαττόνων τὸν ἀριθμὸν ἰδόντες οὕτως ἔχοντα περὶ ὅλου τοῦ οὐρανοῦ ὁμοίως ἀπεφήναντο· ὁ γὰρ περὶ ἡμᾶς τοῦ αἰσθητοῦ τόπος ἐν φθορᾷ καὶ γενέσει διατελεῖ μόνος ὤν, ἀλλʼ οὗτος οὐθὲν ὡς εἰπεῖν μόριον τοῦ παντός ἐστιν, ὥστε δικαιότερον ἂν διʼ ἐκεῖνα τούτων ἀπεψηφίσαντο ἢ διὰ ταῦτα ἐκείνων κατεψηφίσαντο. ἔτι δὲ δῆλον ὅτι καὶ πρὸς τούτους ταὐτὰ τοῖς πάλαι λεχθεῖσιν ἐροῦμεν· ὅτι γὰρ ἔστιν ἀκίνητός τις φύσις δεικτέον αὐτοῖς καὶ πειστέον αὐτούς. καίτοι γε συμβαίνει τοῖς ἅμα φάσκουσιν εἶναι καὶ μὴ εἶναι ἠρεμεῖν μᾶλλον φάναι πάντα ἢ κινεῖσθαι· οὐ γὰρ ἔστιν εἰς ὅ τι μεταβαλεῖ· ἅπαντα γὰρ ὑπάρχει πᾶσιν.  —περὶ δὲ τῆς ἀληθείας, ὡς οὐ πᾶν τὸ φαινόμενον ἀληθές, πρῶτον μὲν ὅτι οὐδʼ εἰ ἡ αἴσθησις μὴ ψευδὴς τοῦ γε ἰδίου ἐστίν, ἀλλʼ ἡ φαντασία οὐ ταὐτὸν τῇ αἰσθήσει. εἶτʼ ἄξιον θαυμάσαι εἰ τοῦτʼ ἀποροῦσι, πότερον τηλικαῦτά ἐστι τὰ μεγέθη καὶ τὰ χρώματα τοιαῦτα οἷα τοῖς ἄπωθεν φαίνεται ἢ οἷα τοῖς ἐγγύθεν, καὶ πότερον οἷα τοῖς ὑγιαίνουσιν ἢ οἷα τοῖς κάμνουσιν, καὶ βαρύτερα πότερον ἃ τοῖς ἀσθενοῦσιν ἢ ἃ τοῖς ἰσχύουσιν, καὶ ἀληθῆ πότερον ἃ τοῖς καθεύδουσιν ἢ ἃ τοῖς ἐγρηγορόσιν. ὅτι μὲν γὰρ οὐκ οἴονταί γε, φανερόν· οὐθεὶς γοῦν, ἐὰν ὑπολάβῃ νύκτωρ Ἀθήνῃσιν εἶναι ὢν ἐν Λιβύῃ, πορεύεται εἰς τὸ ᾠδεῖον. ἔτι δὲ περὶ τοῦ μέλλοντος, ὥσπερ καὶ Πλάτων λέγει, οὐ δήπου ὁμοίως κυρία ἡ τοῦ ἰατροῦ δόξα καὶ ἡ τοῦ ἀγνοοῦντος, οἷον περὶ τοῦ μέλλοντος ἔσεσθαι ὑγιοῦς ἢ μὴ μέλλοντος. ἔτι δὲ ἐπʼ αὐτῶν τῶν αἰσθήσεων οὐχ ὁμοίως κυρία ἡ τοῦ ἀλλοτρίου καὶ ἰδίου ἢ τοῦ πλησίον καὶ τοῦ αὑτῆς, ἀλλὰ περὶ μὲν χρώματος ὄψις, οὐ γεῦσις, περὶ δὲ χυμοῦ γεῦσις, οὐκ ὄψις· ὧν ἑκάστη ἐν τῷ αὐτῷ χρόνῳ περὶ τὸ αὐτὸ οὐδέποτε φησιν ἅμα οὕτω καὶ οὐχ οὕτως ἔχειν. ἀλλʼ οὐδὲ ἐν ἑτέρῳ χρόνῳ περί γε τὸ πάθος ἠμφισβήτησεν, ἀλλὰ περὶ τὸ ᾧ συμβέβηκε τὸ πάθος. λέγω δʼ οἷον ὁ μὲν αὐτὸς οἶνος δόξειεν ἂν ἢ μεταβαλὼν ἢ τοῦ σώματος μεταβαλόντος ὁτὲ μὲν εἶναι γλυκὺς ὁτὲ δὲ οὐ γλυκύς· ἀλλʼ οὐ τό γε γλυκύ, οἷόν ἐστιν ὅταν ᾖ, οὐδεπώποτε μετέβαλεν, ἀλλʼ ἀεὶ ἀληθεύει περὶ αὐτοῦ, καὶ ἔστιν ἐξ ἀνάγκης τὸ ἐσόμενον γλυκὺ τοιοῦτον. καίτοι τοῦτο ἀναιροῦσιν οὗτοι οἱ λόγοι ἅπαντες, ὥσπερ καὶ οὐσίαν μὴ εἶναι μηθενός, οὕτω μηδʼ ἐξ ἀνάγκης μηθέν· τὸ γὰρ ἀναγκαῖον οὐκ ἐνδέχεται ἄλλως καὶ ἄλλως ἔχειν, ὥστʼ εἴ τι ἔστιν ἐξ ἀνάγκης, οὐχ ἕξει οὕτω τε καὶ οὐχ οὕτως.—ὅλως τʼ εἴπερ ἔστι τὸ αἰσθητὸν μόνον, οὐθὲν ἂν εἴη μὴ ὄντων τῶν ἐμψύχων· αἴσθησις γὰρ οὐκ ἂν εἴη. τὸ μὲν οὖν μήτε τὰ αἰσθητὰ εἶναι μήτε τὰ αἰσθήματα ἴσως ἀληθές (τοῦ γὰρ αἰσθανομένου πάθος τοῦτό ἐστι), τὸ δὲ τὰ ὑποκείμενα μὴ εἶναι, ἃ ποιεῖ τὴν αἴσθησιν, καὶ ἄνευ αἰσθήσεως, ἀδύνατον. οὐ γὰρ δὴ ἥ γʼ αἴσθησις αὐτὴ ἑαυτῆς ἐστίν, ἀλλʼ ἔστι τι καὶ ἕτερον παρὰ τὴν αἴσθησιν, ὃ ἀνάγκη πρότερον εἶναι τῆς αἰσθήσεως·  τὸ γὰρ κινοῦν τοῦ κινουμένου φύσει πρότερόν ἐστι, κἂν εἰ λέγεται πρὸς ἄλληλα ταῦτα, οὐθὲν ἧττον. \n\n\n\n \n\n \n\nεἰσὶ δέ τινες οἳ ἀποροῦσι καὶ τῶν ταῦτα πεπεισμένων καὶ τῶν τοὺς λόγους τούτους μόνον λεγόντων· ζητοῦσι γὰρ τίς ὁ κρινῶν τὸν ὑγιαίνοντα καὶ ὅλως τὸν περὶ ἕκαστα κρινοῦντα ὀρθῶς. τὰ δὲ τοιαῦτα ἀπορήματα ὅμοιά ἐστι τῷ ἀπορεῖν πότερον καθεύδομεν νῦν ἢ ἐγρηγόραμεν, δύνανται δʼ αἱ ἀπορίαι αἱ τοιαῦται πᾶσαι τὸ αὐτό· πάντων γὰρ λόγον ἀξιοῦσιν εἶναι οὗτοι· ἀρχὴν γὰρ ζητοῦσι, καὶ ταύτην διʼ ἀποδείξεως λαμβάνειν, ἐπεὶ ὅτι γε πεπεισμένοι οὐκ εἰσί, φανεροί εἰσιν ἐν ταῖς πράξεσιν. ἀλλʼ ὅπερ εἴπομεν, τοῦτο αὐτῶν τὸ πάθος ἐστίν· λόγον γὰρ ζητοῦσιν ὧν οὐκ ἔστι λόγος· ἀποδείξεως γὰρ ἀρχὴ οὐκ ἀπόδειξίς ἐστιν. οὗτοι μὲν οὖν ῥᾳδίως ἂν τοῦτο πεισθεῖεν (ἔστι γὰρ οὐ χαλεπὸν λαβεῖν)· οἱ δʼ ἐν τῷ λόγῳ τὴν βίαν μόνον ζητοῦντες ἀδύνατον ζητοῦσιν· ἐναντία γὰρ εἰπεῖν ἀξιοῦσιν, εὐθὺς ἐναντία λέγοντες. εἰ δὲ μὴ ἔστι πάντα πρός τι, ἀλλʼ ἔνιά ἐστι καὶ αὐτὰ καθʼ αὑτά, οὐκ ἂν εἴη πᾶν τὸ φαινόμενον ἀληθές· τὸ γὰρ φαινόμενον τινί ἐστι φαινόμενον· ὥστε ὁ λέγων ἅπαντα τὰ φαινόμενα εἶναι ἀληθῆ ἅπαντα ποιεῖ τὰ ὄντα πρός τι. διὸ καὶ φυλακτέον τοῖς τὴν βίαν ἐν τῷ λόγῳ ζητοῦσιν, ἅμα δὲ καὶ ὑπέχειν λόγον ἀξιοῦσιν, ὅτι οὐ τὸ φαινόμενον ἔστιν ἀλλὰ τὸ φαινόμενον ᾧ φαίνεται καὶ ὅτε φαίνεται καὶ ᾗ καὶ ὥς. ἂν δʼ ὑπέχωσι μὲν λόγον, μὴ οὕτω δʼ ὑπέχωσι, συμβήσεται αὑτοῖς τἀναντία ταχὺ λέγειν. ἐνδέχεται γὰρ τὸ αὐτὸ κατὰ μὲν τὴν ὄψιν μέλι φαίνεσθαι τῇ δὲ γεύσει μή, καὶ τῶν ὀφθαλμῶν δυοῖν ὄντοιν μὴ ταὐτὰ ἑκατέρᾳ τῇ ὄψει, ἂν ὦσιν ἀνόμοιαι· ἐπεὶ πρός γε τοὺς διὰ τὰς πάλαι εἰρημένας αἰτίας τὸ φαινόμενον φάσκοντας ἀληθὲς εἶναι, καὶ διὰ τοῦτο πάνθʼ ὁμοίως εἶναι ψευδῆ καὶ ἀληθῆ· οὔτε γὰρ ἅπασι ταὐτὰ φαίνεσθαι οὔτε ταὐτῷ ἀεὶ ταὐτά, ἀλλὰ πολλάκις τἀναντία κατὰ τὸν αὐτὸν χρόνον (ἡ μὲν γὰρ ἁφὴ δύο λέγει ἐν τῇ ἐπαλλάξει τῶν δακτύλων ἡ δʼ ὄψις ἕν)·—ἀλλʼ οὔ τι τῇ αὐτῇ γε καὶ κατὰ τὸ αὐτὸ αἰσθήσει καὶ ὡσαύτως καὶ ἐν τῷ αὐτῷ χρόνῳ, ὥστε τοῦτʼ ἂν εἴη ἀληθές.  ἀλλʼ ἴσως διὰ τοῦτʼ ἀνάγκη λέγειν τοῖς μὴ διʼ ἀπορίαν ἀλλὰ λόγου χάριν λέγουσιν, ὅτι οὐκ ἔστιν ἀληθὲς τοῦτο ἀλλὰ τούτῳ ἀληθές. καὶ ὥσπερ δὴ πρότερον εἴρηται, ἀνάγκη πρός τι ποιεῖν ἅπαντα καὶ πρὸς δόξαν καὶ αἴσθησιν, ὥστʼ οὔτε γέγονεν οὔτʼ ἔσται οὐθὲν μηθενὸς προδοξάσαντος. εἰ δὲ γέγονεν ἢ ἔσται, δῆλον ὅτι οὐκ ἂν εἴη ἅπαντα πρὸς δόξαν. ἔτι εἰ ἕν, πρὸς ἓν ἢ πρὸς ὡρισμένον· καὶ εἰ τὸ αὐτὸ καὶ ἥμισυ καὶ ἴσον, ἀλλʼ οὐ πρὸς τὸ διπλάσιόν γε τὸ ἴσον. πρὸς δὴ τὸ δοξάζον εἰ ταὐτὸ ἄνθρωπος καὶ τὸ δοξαζόμενον, οὐκ ἔσται ἄνθρωπος τὸ δοξάζον ἀλλὰ τὸ δοξαζόμενον. εἰ δʼ ἕκαστον ἔσται πρὸς τὸ δοξάζον, πρὸς ἄπειρα ἔσται τῷ εἴδει τὸ δοξάζον. ὅτι μὲν οὖν βεβαιοτάτη δόξα πασῶν τὸ μὴ εἶναι ἀληθεῖς ἅμα τὰς ἀντικειμένας φάσεις, καὶ τί συμβαίνει τοῖς οὕτω λέγουσι, καὶ διὰ τί οὕτω λέγουσι, τοσαῦτα εἰρήσθω· ἐπεὶ δʼ ἀδύνατον τὴν ἀντίφασιν ἅμα ἀληθεύεσθαι κατὰ τοῦ αὐτοῦ, φανερὸν ὅτι οὐδὲ τἀναντία ἅμα ὑπάρχειν ἐνδέχεται τῷ αὐτῷ· τῶν μὲν γὰρ ἐναντίων θάτερον στέρησίς ἐστιν οὐχ ἧττον, οὐσίας δὲ στέρησις· ἡ δὲ στέρησις ἀπόφασίς ἐστιν ἀπό τινος ὡρισμένου γένους· εἰ οὖν ἀδύνατον ἅμα καταφάναι καὶ ἀποφάναι ἀληθῶς, ἀδύνατον καὶ τἀναντία ὑπάρχειν ἅμα, ἀλλʼ ἢ πῇ ἄμφω ἢ θάτερον μὲν πῇ θάτερον δὲ ἁπλῶς. \n\n\n\n \n\n \n\nἀλλὰ μὴν οὐδὲ μεταξὺ ἀντιφάσεως ἐνδέχεται εἶναι οὐθέν, ἀλλʼ ἀνάγκη ἢ φάναι ἢ ἀποφάναι ἓν καθʼ ἑνὸς ὁτιοῦν. δῆλον δὲ πρῶτον μὲν ὁρισαμένοις τί τὸ ἀληθὲς καὶ ψεῦδος. τὸ μὲν γὰρ λέγειν τὸ ὂν μὴ εἶναι ἢ τὸ μὴ ὂν εἶναι ψεῦδος, τὸ δὲ τὸ ὂν εἶναι καὶ τὸ μὴ ὂν μὴ εἶναι ἀληθές, ὥστε καὶ ὁ λέγων εἶναι ἢ μὴ ἀληθεύσει ἢ ψεύσεται· ἀλλʼ οὔτε τὸ ὂν λέγεται μὴ εἶναι ἢ εἶναι οὔτε τὸ μὴ ὄν. ἔτι ἤτοι μεταξὺ ἔσται τῆς ἀντιφάσεως ὥσπερ τὸ φαιὸν μέλανος καὶ λευκοῦ, ἢ ὡς τὸ μηδέτερον ἀνθρώπου καὶ ἵππου. εἰ μὲν οὖν οὕτως, οὐκ ἂν μεταβάλλοι (ἐκ μὴ ἀγαθοῦ γὰρ εἰς ἀγαθὸν μεταβάλλει ἢ ἐκ τούτου εἰς μὴ ἀγαθόν), νῦν δʼ ἀεὶ φαίνεται (οὐ γὰρ ἔστι μεταβολὴ ἀλλʼ ἢ εἰς τὰ ἀντικείμενα καὶ μεταξύ)· εἰ δʼ ἔστι μεταξύ, καὶ οὕτως εἴη ἄν τις εἰς λευκὸν οὐκ ἐκ μὴ λευκοῦ γένεσις, νῦν δʼ οὐχ ὁρᾶται.  ἔτι πᾶν τὸ διανοητὸν καὶ νοητὸν ἡ διάνοια ἢ κατάφησιν ἢ ἀπόφησιν—τοῦτο δʼ ἐξ ὁρισμοῦ δῆλον—ὅταν ἀληθεύῃ ἢ ψεύδηται· ὅταν μὲν ὡδὶ συνθῇ φᾶσα ἢ ἀποφᾶσα, ἀληθεύει, ὅταν δὲ ὡδί, ψεύδεται. ἔτι παρὰ πάσας δεῖ εἶναι τὰς ἀντιφάσεις, εἰ μὴ λόγου ἕνεκα λέγεται· ὥστε καὶ οὔτε ἀληθεύσει τις οὔτʼ οὐκ ἀληθεύσει, καὶ παρὰ τὸ ὂν καὶ τὸ μὴ ὂν ἔσται, ὥστε καὶ παρὰ γένεσιν καὶ φθορὰν μεταβολή τις ἔσται. ἔτι ἐν ὅσοις γένεσιν ἡ ἀπόφασις τὸ ἐναντίον ἐπιφέρει, καὶ ἐν τούτοις ἔσται, οἷον ἐν ἀριθμοῖς οὔτε περιττὸς οὔτε οὐ περιττὸς ἀριθμός· ἀλλʼ ἀδύνατον· ἐκ τοῦ ὁρισμοῦ δὲ δῆλον. ἔτι εἰς ἄπειρον βαδιεῖται, καὶ οὐ μόνον ἡμιόλια τὰ ὄντα ἔσται ἀλλὰ πλείω. πάλιν γὰρ ἔσται ἀποφῆσαι τοῦτο πρὸς τὴν φάσιν καὶ τὴν ἀπόφασιν, καὶ τοῦτʼ ἔσται τι· ἡ γὰρ οὐσία ἐστί τις αὐτοῦ ἄλλη. ἔτι ὅταν ἐρομένου εἰ λευκόν ἐστιν εἴπῃ ὅτι οὔ, οὐθὲν ἄλλο ἀποπέφηκεν ἢ τὸ εἶναι· ἀπόφασις δὲ τὸ μὴ εἶναι. ἐλήλυθε δʼ ἐνίοις αὕτη ἡ δόξα ὥσπερ καὶ ἄλλαι τῶν παραδόξων· ὅταν γὰρ λύειν μὴ δύνωνται λόγους ἐριστικούς, ἐνδόντες τῷ λόγῳ σύμφασιν ἀληθὲς εἶναι τὸ συλλογισθέν. οἱ μὲν οὖν διὰ τοιαύτην αἰτίαν λέγουσιν, οἱ δὲ διὰ τὸ πάντων ζητεῖν λόγον. ἀρχὴ δὲ πρὸς ἅπαντας τούτους ἐξ ὁρισμοῦ. ὁρισμὸς δὲ γίγνεται ἐκ τοῦ σημαίνειν τι ἀναγκαῖον εἶναι αὐτούς· ὁ γὰρ λόγος οὗ τὸ ὄνομα σημεῖον ὁρισμὸς ἔσται. ἔοικε δʼ ὁ μὲν Ἡρακλείτου λόγος, λέγων πάντα εἶναι καὶ μὴ εἶναι, ἅπαντα ἀληθῆ ποιεῖν, ὁ δʼ Ἀναξαγόρου, εἶναί τι μεταξὺ τῆς ἀντιφάσεως, πάντα ψευδῆ· ὅταν γὰρ μιχθῇ, οὔτε ἀγαθὸν οὔτε οὐκ ἀγαθὸν τὸ μῖγμα, ὥστʼ οὐδὲν εἰπεῖν ἀληθές.\n\n\n\n \n\n \n\nδιωρισμένων δὲ τούτων φανερὸν ὅτι καὶ τὰ μοναχῶς λεγόμενα καὶ κατὰ πάντων ἀδύνατον ὑπάρχειν ὥσπερ τινὲς λέγουσιν, οἱ μὲν οὐθὲν φάσκοντες ἀληθὲς εἶναι (οὐθὲν γὰρ κωλύειν φασὶν οὕτως ἅπαντα εἶναι ὥσπερ τὸ τὴν διάμετρον σύμμετρον εἶναι), οἱ δὲ πάντʼ ἀληθῆ. σχεδὸν γὰρ οὗτοι οἱ λόγοι οἱ αὐτοὶ τῷ Ἡρακλείτου· ὁ γὰρ λέγων ὅτι πάντʼ ἀληθῆ καὶ πάντα ψευδῆ, καὶ χωρὶς λέγει τῶν λόγων ἑκάτερον τούτων,  ὥστʼ εἴπερ ἀδύνατα ἐκεῖνα, καὶ ταῦτα ἀδύνατον εἶναι. ἔτι δὲ φανερῶς ἀντιφάσεις εἰσὶν ἃς οὐχ οἷόν τε ἅμα ἀληθεῖς εἶναι—οὐδὲ δὴ ψευδεῖς πάσας· καίτοι δόξειέ γʼ ἂν μᾶλλον ἐνδέχεσθαι ἐκ τῶν εἰρημένων. ἀλλὰ πρὸς πάντας τοὺς τοιούτους λόγους αἰτεῖσθαι δεῖ, καθάπερ ἐλέχθη καὶ ἐν τοῖς ἐπάνω λόγοις, οὐχὶ εἶναί τι ἢ μὴ εἶναι ἀλλὰ σημαίνειν τι, ὥστε ἐξ ὁρισμοῦ διαλεκτέον λαβόντας τί σημαίνει τὸ ψεῦδος ἢ τὸ ἀληθές. εἰ δὲ μηθὲν ἄλλο τὸ ἀληθὲς φάναι ἢ ὃ ἀποφάναι ψεῦδός ἐστιν, ἀδύνατον  πάντα ψευδῆ εἶναι· ἀνάγκη γὰρ τῆς ἀντιφάσεως θάτερον εἶναι μόριον ἀληθές. ἔτι εἰ πᾶν ἢ φάναι ἢ ἀποφάναι ἀναγκαῖον, ἀδύνατον ἀμφότερα ψευδῆ εἶναι· θάτερον γὰρ μόριον τῆς ἀντιφάσεως ψεῦδός ἐστιν. συμβαίνει δὴ καὶ τὸ θρυλούμενον πᾶσι τοῖς τοιούτοις λόγοις, αὐτοὺς ἑαυτοὺς ἀναιρεῖν. ὁ μὲν γὰρ πάντα ἀληθῆ λέγων καὶ τὸν ἐναντίον αὑτοῦ λόγον ἀληθῆ ποιεῖ, ὥστε τὸν ἑαυτοῦ οὐκ ἀληθῆ (ὁ γὰρ ἐναντίος οὔ φησιν αὐτὸν ἀληθῆ), ὁ δὲ πάντα ψευδῆ καὶ αὐτὸς αὑτόν. ἐὰν δʼ ἐξαιρῶνται ὁ μὲν τὸν ἐναντίον ὡς οὐκ ἀληθὴς μόνος ἐστίν, ὁ δὲ τὸν αὑτοῦ ὡς οὐ ψευδής, οὐδὲν ἧττον ἀπείρους συμβαίνει αὐτοῖς αἰτεῖσθαι λόγους ἀληθεῖς καὶ ψευδεῖς· ὁ γὰρ λέγων τὸν ἀληθῆ λόγον ἀληθῆ ἀληθής, τοῦτο δʼ εἰς ἄπειρον βαδιεῖται.—φανερὸν δʼ ὅτι οὐδʼ οἱ πάντα ἠρεμεῖν λέγοντες ἀληθῆ λέγουσιν οὐδʼ οἱ πάντα κινεῖσθαι. εἰ μὲν γὰρ ἠρεμεῖ πάντα, ἀεὶ ταὐτὰ ἀληθῆ καὶ ψευδῆ ἔσται, φαίνεται δὲ τοῦτο μεταβάλλον (ὁ γὰρ λέγων ποτὲ αὐτὸς οὐκ ἦν καὶ πάλιν οὐκ ἔσται)· εἰ δὲ πάντα κινεῖται, οὐθὲν ἔσται ἀληθές· πάντα ἄρα ψευδῆ· ἀλλὰ δέδεικται ὅτι ἀδύνατον. ἔτι ἀνάγκη τὸ ὂν μεταβάλλειν· ἔκ τινος γὰρ εἴς τι ἡ μεταβολή. ἀλλὰ μὴν οὐδὲ πάντα ἠρεμεῖ ἢ κινεῖται ποτέ, ἀεὶ δʼ οὐθέν· ἔστι γάρ τι ὃ ἀεὶ κινεῖ τὰ κινούμενα, καὶ τὸ πρῶτον κινοῦν ἀκίνητον αὐτό.","paragraphs":[{"id":"RAW_DAZY_BOO4:P0001","text":"ἔστιν ἐπιστήμη τις ἣ θεωρεῖ τὸ ὂν ᾗ ὂν καὶ τὰ τούτῳ ὑπάρχοντα καθʼ αὑτό. αὕτη δʼ ἐστὶν οὐδεμιᾷ τῶν ἐν μέρει λεγομένων ἡ αὐτή· οὐδεμία γὰρ τῶν ἄλλων ἐπισκοπεῖ καθόλου περὶ τοῦ ὄντος ᾗ ὄν, ἀλλὰ μέρος αὐτοῦ τι ἀποτεμόμεναι περὶ τούτου θεωροῦσι τὸ συμβεβηκός, οἷον αἱ μαθηματικαὶ τῶν ἐπιστημῶν. ἐπεὶ δὲ τὰς ἀρχὰς καὶ τὰς ἀκροτάτας αἰτίας ζητοῦμεν, δῆλον ὡς φύσεώς τινος αὐτὰς ἀναγκαῖον εἶναι καθʼ αὑτήν. εἰ οὖν καὶ οἱ τὰ στοιχεῖα τῶν ὄντων ζητοῦντες ταύτας τὰς ἀρχὰς ἐζήτουν, ἀνάγκη καὶ τὰ στοιχεῖα τοῦ ὄντος εἶναι μὴ κατὰ συμβεβηκὸς ἀλλʼ ᾗ ὄν· διὸ καὶ ἡμῖν τοῦ ὄντος ᾗ ὂν τὰς πρώτας αἰτίας ληπτέον.","kind":"passage"},{"id":"RAW_DAZY_BOO4:P0002","text":"τὸ δὲ ὂν λέγεται μὲν πολλαχῶς, ἀλλὰ πρὸς ἓν καὶ μίαν τινὰ φύσιν καὶ οὐχ ὁμωνύμως ἀλλʼ ὥσπερ καὶ τὸ ὑγιεινὸν ἅπαν πρὸς ὑγίειαν, τὸ μὲν τῷ φυλάττειν τὸ δὲ τῷ ποιεῖν τὸ δὲ τῷ σημεῖον εἶναι τῆς ὑγιείας τὸ δʼ ὅτι δεκτικὸν αὐτῆς,  καὶ τὸ ἰατρικὸν πρὸς ἰατρικήν (τὸ μὲν γὰρ τῷ ἔχειν ἰατρικὴν λέγεται ἰατρικὸν τὸ δὲ τῷ εὐφυὲς εἶναι πρὸς αὐτὴν τὸ δὲ τῷ ἔργον εἶναι τῆς ἰατρικῆς), ὁμοιοτρόπως δὲ καὶ ἄλλα ληψόμεθα λεγόμενα τούτοις,—οὕτω δὲ καὶ τὸ ὂν λέγεται πολλαχῶς μὲν ἀλλʼ ἅπαν πρὸς μίαν ἀρχήν· τὰ μὲν γὰρ ὅτι οὐσίαι, ὄντα λέγεται, τὰ δʼ ὅτι πάθη οὐσίας, τὰ δʼ ὅτι ὁδὸς εἰς οὐσίαν ἢ φθοραὶ ἢ στερήσεις ἢ ποιότητες ἢ ποιητικὰ ἢ γεννητικὰ οὐσίας ἢ τῶν πρὸς τὴν οὐσίαν λεγομένων, ἢ τούτων τινὸς ἀποφάσεις ἢ οὐσίας· διὸ καὶ τὸ μὴ ὂν εἶναι μὴ ὄν φαμεν. καθάπερ οὖν καὶ τῶν ὑγιεινῶν ἁπάντων μία ἐπιστήμη ἔστιν, ὁμοίως τοῦτο καὶ ἐπὶ τῶν ἄλλων.","kind":"passage"},{"id":"RAW_DAZY_BOO4:P0003","text":"οὐ γὰρ μόνον τῶν καθʼ ἓν λεγομένων ἐπιστήμης ἐστὶ θεωρῆσαι μιᾶς ἀλλὰ καὶ τῶν πρὸς μίαν λεγομένων φύσιν· καὶ γὰρ ταῦτα τρόπον τινὰ λέγονται καθʼ ἕν. δῆλον οὖν ὅτι καὶ τὰ ὄντα μιᾶς θεωρῆσαι ᾗ ὄντα. πανταχοῦ δὲ κυρίως τοῦ πρώτου ἡ ἐπιστήμη, καὶ ἐξ οὗ τὰ ἄλλα ἤρτηται, καὶ διʼ ὃ λέγονται. εἰ οὖν τοῦτʼ ἐστὶν ἡ οὐσία, τῶν οὐσιῶν ἂν δέοι τὰς ἀρχὰς καὶ τὰς αἰτίας ἔχειν τὸν φιλόσοφον.—ἅπαντος δὲ γένους καὶ αἴσθησις μία ἑνὸς καὶ ἐπιστήμη, οἷον γραμματικὴ μία οὖσα πάσας θεωρεῖ τὰς φωνάς· διὸ καὶ τοῦ ὄντος ᾗ ὂν ὅσα εἴδη θεωρῆσαι μιᾶς ἐστὶν ἐπιστήμης τῷ γένει, τά τε εἴδη τῶν εἰδῶν.","kind":"passage"},{"id":"RAW_DAZY_BOO4:P0004","text":"εἰ δὴ τὸ ὂν καὶ τὸ ἓν ταὐτὸν καὶ μία φύσις τῷ ἀκολουθεῖν ἀλλήλοις ὥσπερ ἀρχὴ καὶ αἴτιον, ἀλλʼ οὐχ ὡς ἑνὶ λόγῳ δηλούμενα (διαφέρει δὲ οὐθὲν οὐδʼ ἂν ὁμοίως ὑπολάβωμεν, ἀλλὰ καὶ πρὸ ἔργου μᾶλλον)· ταὐτὸ γὰρ εἷς ἄνθρωπος καὶ ἄνθρωπος, καὶ ὢν ἄνθρωπος καὶ ἄνθρωπος, καὶ οὐχ ἕτερόν τι δηλοῖ κατὰ τὴν λέξιν ἐπαναδιπλούμενον τὸ εἷς ἄνθρωπος καὶ εἷς ὢν ἄνθρωπος (δῆλον δʼ ὅτι οὐ χωρίζεται οὔτʼ ἐπὶ γενέσεως οὔτʼ ἐπὶ φθορᾶς) , ὁμοίως δὲ καὶ ἐπὶ τοῦ ἑνός, ὥστε φανερὸν ὅτι ἡ πρόσθεσις ἐν τούτοις ταὐτὸ δηλοῖ, καὶ οὐδὲν ἕτερον τὸ ἓν παρὰ τὸ ὄν, ἔτι δʼ ἡ ἑκάστου οὐσία ἕν ἐστιν οὐ κατὰ συμβεβηκός, ὁμοίως δὲ καὶ ὅπερ ὄν τι·—ὥσθʼ ὅσα περ τοῦ ἑνὸς εἴδη, τοσαῦτα καὶ τοῦ ὄντος· περὶ ὧν τὸ τί ἐστι τῆς αὐτῆς ἐπιστήμης τῷ γένει θεωρῆσαι, λέγω δʼ οἷον περὶ ταὐτοῦ καὶ ὁμοίου καὶ τῶν ἄλλων τῶν τοιούτων. σχεδὸν δὲ πάντα ἀνάγεται τἀναντία εἰς τὴν ἀρχὴν ταύτην·  τεθεωρήσθω δʼ ἡμῖν ταῦτα ἐν τῇ ἐκλογῇ τῶν ἐναντίων.","kind":"passage"},{"id":"RAW_DAZY_BOO4:P0005","text":"καὶ τοσαῦτα μέρη φιλοσοφίας ἔστιν ὅσαι περ αἱ οὐσίαι· ὥστε ἀναγκαῖον εἶναί τινα πρώτην καὶ ἐχομένην αὐτῶν. ὑπάρχει γὰρ εὐθὺς γένη ἔχον τὸ ὂν · διὸ καὶ αἱ ἐπιστῆμαι ἀκολουθήσουσι τούτοις. ἔστι γὰρ ὁ φιλόσοφος ὥσπερ ὁ μαθηματικὸς λεγόμενος· καὶ γὰρ αὕτη ἔχει μέρη, καὶ πρώτη τις καὶ δευτέρα ἔστιν ἐπιστήμη καὶ ἄλλαι ἐφεξῆς ἐν τοῖς μαθήμασιν.—ἐπεὶ δὲ μιᾶς τἀντικείμενα θεωρῆσαι, τῷ δὲ ἑνὶ ἀντίκειται πλῆθος—ἀπόφασιν δὲ καὶ στέρησιν μιᾶς ἐστὶ θεωρῆσαι διὰ τὸ ἀμφοτέρως θεωρεῖσθαι τὸ ἓν οὗ ἡ ἀπόφασις ἢ ἡ στέρησις (ἢ γὰρ ἁπλῶς λέγομεν ὅτι οὐχ ὑπάρχει ἐκεῖνο, ἤ τινι γένει· ἔνθα μὲν οὖν †τῷ ἑνὶ ἡ διαφορὰ πρόσεστι παρὰ τὸ ἐν τῇ ἀποφάσει†, ἀπουσία γὰρ ἡ ἀπόφασις ἐκείνου ἐστίν, ἐν δὲ τῇ στερήσει καὶ ὑποκειμένη τις φύσις γίγνεται καθʼ ἧς λέγεται ἡ στέρησις) —ὥστε καὶ τἀντικείμενα τοῖς εἰρημένοις, τό τε ἕτερον καὶ ἀνόμοιον καὶ ἄνισον καὶ ὅσα ἄλλα λέγεται ἢ κατὰ ταῦτα ἢ κατὰ πλῆθος καὶ τὸ ἕν, τῆς","kind":"passage"},{"id":"RAW_DAZY_BOO4:P0006","text":"εἰρημένης γνωρίζειν ἐπιστήμης· ὧν ἐστὶ καὶ ἡ ἐναντιότης· διαφορὰ γάρ τις ἡ ἐναντιότης, ἡ δὲ διαφορὰ ἑτερότης. ὥστʼ ἐπειδὴ πολλαχῶς τὸ ἓν λέγεται, καὶ ταῦτα πολλαχῶς μὲν λεχθήσεται, ὅμως δὲ μιᾶς ἅπαντά ἐστι γνωρίζειν· οὐ γὰρ εἰ πολλαχῶς, ἑτέρας, ἀλλʼ εἰ μήτε καθʼ ἓν μήτε πρὸς ἓν οἱ λόγοι ἀναφέρονται. ἐπεὶ δὲ πάντα πρὸς τὸ πρῶτον ἀναφέρεται, οἷον ὅσα ἓν λέγεται πρὸς τὸ πρῶτον ἕν, ὡσαύτως φατέον καὶ περὶ ταὐτοῦ καὶ ἑτέρου καὶ τῶν ἐναντίων ἔχειν· ὥστε διελόμενον ποσαχῶς λέγεται ἕκαστον, οὕτως ἀποδοτέον πρὸς τὸ πρῶτον ἐν ἑκάστῃ κατηγορίᾳ πῶς πρὸς ἐκεῖνο λέγεται· τὰ μὲν γὰρ τῷ ἔχειν ἐκεῖνο τὰ δὲ τῷ ποιεῖν τὰ δὲ κατʼ ἄλλους λεχθήσεται τοιούτους τρόπους.—φανερὸν οὖν  ὅτι μιᾶς περὶ τούτων καὶ τῆς οὐσίας ἐστὶ λόγον ἔχειν (τοῦτο δʼ ἦν ἓν τῶν ἐν τοῖς ἀπορήμασιν), καὶ ἔστι τοῦ φιλοσόφου περὶ πάντων δύνασθαι θεωρεῖν.","kind":"passage"},{"id":"RAW_DAZY_BOO4:P0007","text":"εἰ γὰρ μὴ τοῦ φιλοσόφου, τίς ἔσται ὁ ἐπισκεψόμενος εἰ ταὐτὸ Σωκράτης καὶ Σωκράτης καθήμενος, ἢ εἰ ἓν ἑνὶ ἐναντίον, ἢ τί ἐστι τὸ ἐναντίον ἢ ποσαχῶς λέγεται; ὁμοίως δὲ καὶ περὶ τῶν ἄλλων τῶν τοιούτων. ἐπεὶ οὖν τοῦ ἑνὸς ᾗ ἓν καὶ τοῦ ὄντος ᾗ ὂν ταῦτα καθʼ αὑτά ἐστι πάθη, ἀλλʼ οὐχ ᾗ ἀριθμοὶ ἢ γραμμαὶ ἢ πῦρ, δῆλον ὡς ἐκείνης τῆς ἐπιστήμης καὶ τί ἐστι γνωρίσαι καὶ τὰ συμβεβηκότʼ αὐτοῖς. καὶ οὐ ταύτῃ ἁμαρτάνουσιν οἱ περὶ αὐτῶν σκοπούμενοι ὡς οὐ φιλοσοφοῦντες, ἀλλʼ ὅτι πρότερον ἡ οὐσία, περὶ ἧς οὐθὲν ἐπαΐουσιν, ἐπεὶ ὥσπερ ἔστι καὶ ἀριθμοῦ ᾗ ἀριθμὸς ἴδια πάθη, οἷον περιττότης ἀρτιότης, συμμετρία ἰσότης, ὑπεροχὴ ἔλλειψις, καὶ ταῦτα καὶ καθʼ αὑτοὺς καὶ πρὸς ἀλλήλους ὑπάρχει τοῖς ἀριθμοῖς (ὁμοίως δὲ καὶ στερεῷ καὶ ἀκινήτῳ καὶ κινουμένῳ ἀβαρεῖ τε καὶ βάρος ἔχοντι ἔστιν ἕτερα ἴδια), οὕτω καὶ τῷ ὄντι ᾗ ὂν ἔστι τινὰ ἴδια, καὶ ταῦτʼ ἐστὶ περὶ ὧν τοῦ φιλοσόφου ἐπισκέψασθαι τὸ ἀληθές.","kind":"passage"},{"id":"RAW_DAZY_BOO4:P0008","text":"σημεῖον δέ· οἱ γὰρ διαλεκτικοὶ καὶ σοφισταὶ τὸ αὐτὸ μὲν ὑποδύονται σχῆμα τῷ φιλοσόφῳ· ἡ γὰρ σοφιστικὴ φαινομένη μόνον σοφία ἐστί, καὶ οἱ διαλεκτικοὶ διαλέγονται περὶ ἁπάντων, κοινὸν δὲ πᾶσι τὸ ὄν ἐστιν, διαλέγονται δὲ περὶ τούτων δῆλον ὅτι διὰ τὸ τῆς φιλοσοφίας ταῦτα εἶναι οἰκεῖα. περὶ μὲν γὰρ τὸ αὐτὸ γένος στρέφεται ἡ σοφιστικὴ καὶ ἡ διαλεκτικὴ τῇ φιλοσοφίᾳ, ἀλλὰ διαφέρει τῆς μὲν τῷ τρόπῳ τῆς δυνάμεως, τῆς δὲ τοῦ βίου τῇ προαιρέσει· ἔστι δὲ ἡ διαλεκτικὴ πειραστικὴ περὶ ὧν ἡ φιλοσοφία γνωριστική, ἡ δὲ σοφιστικὴ φαινομένη, οὖσα δʼ οὔ.","kind":"passage"},{"id":"RAW_DAZY_BOO4:P0009","text":"ἔτι τῶν ἐναντίων ἡ ἑτέρα συστοιχία στέρησις, καὶ πάντα ἀνάγεται εἰς τὸ ὂν καὶ τὸ μὴ ὄν, καὶ εἰς ἓν καὶ πλῆθος, οἷον στάσις τοῦ ἑνὸς κίνησις δὲ τοῦ πλήθους· τὰ δʼ ὄντα καὶ τὴν οὐσίαν ὁμολογοῦσιν ἐξ ἐναντίων σχεδὸν ἅπαντες συγκεῖσθαι· πάντες γοῦν τὰς ἀρχὰς ἐναντίας λέγουσιν· οἱ μὲν γὰρ περιττὸν καὶ ἄρτιον, οἱ δὲ θερμὸν καὶ ψυχρόν, οἱ δὲ πέρας καὶ ἄπειρον, οἱ δὲ φιλίαν καὶ νεῖκος. πάντα δὲ καὶ τἆλλα ἀναγόμενα φαίνεται εἰς τὸ ἓν καὶ πλῆθος (εἰλήφθω γὰρ ἡ ἀναγωγὴ ἡμῖν),  αἱ δʼ ἀρχαὶ καὶ παντελῶς αἱ παρὰ τῶν ἄλλων ὡς εἰς γένη ταῦτα πίπτουσιν. φανερὸν οὖν καὶ ἐκ τούτων ὅτι μιᾶς ἐπιστήμης τὸ ὂν ᾗ ὂν θεωρῆσαι. πάντα γὰρ ἢ ἐναντία ἢ ἐξ ἐναντίων, ἀρχαὶ δὲ τῶν ἐναντίων τὸ ἓν καὶ πλῆθος. ταῦτα δὲ μιᾶς ἐπιστήμης, εἴτε καθʼ ἓν λέγεται εἴτε μή, ὥσπερ ἴσως ἔχει καὶ τἀληθές.","kind":"passage"},{"id":"RAW_DAZY_BOO4:P0010","text":"ἀλλʼ ὅμως εἰ καὶ πολλαχῶς λέγεται τὸ ἕν, πρὸς τὸ πρῶτον τἆλλα λεχθήσεται καὶ τὰ ἐναντία ὁμοίως,  καὶ εἰ μὴ ἔστι τὸ ὂν ἢ τὸ ἓν καθόλου καὶ ταὐτὸ ἐπὶ πάντων ἢ χωριστόν, ὥσπερ ἴσως οὐκ ἔστιν ἀλλὰ τὰ μὲν πρὸς ἓν τὰ δὲ τῷ ἐφεξῆς. καὶ διὰ τοῦτο οὐ τοῦ γεωμέτρου θεωρῆσαι τί τὸ ἐναντίον ἢ τέλειον ἢ ἓν ἢ ὂν ἢ ταὐτὸν ἢ ἕτερον, ἀλλʼ ἢ ἐξ ὑποθέσεως. ὅτι μὲν οὖν μιᾶς ἐπιστήμης τὸ ὂν ᾗ ὂν θεωρῆσαι καὶ τὰ ὑπάρχοντα αὐτῷ ᾗ ὄν, δῆλον, καὶ ὅτι οὐ μόνον τῶν οὐσιῶν ἀλλὰ καὶ τῶν ὑπαρχόντων ἡ αὐτὴ θεωρητική, τῶν τε εἰρημένων καὶ περὶ προτέρου καὶ ὑστέρου, καὶ γένους καὶ εἴδους, καὶ ὅλου καὶ μέρους καὶ τῶν ἄλλων τῶν τοιούτων.","kind":"passage"},{"id":"RAW_DAZY_BOO4:P0011","text":"λεκτέον δὲ πότερον μιᾶς ἢ ἑτέρας ἐπιστήμης περί τε τῶν ἐν τοῖς μαθήμασι καλουμένων ἀξιωμάτων καὶ περὶ τῆς οὐσίας. φανερὸν δὴ ὅτι μιᾶς τε καὶ τῆς τοῦ φιλοσόφου καὶ ἡ περὶ τούτων ἐστὶ σκέψις· ἅπασι γὰρ ὑπάρχει τοῖς οὖσιν ἀλλʼ οὐ γένει τινὶ χωρὶς ἰδίᾳ τῶν ἄλλων. καὶ χρῶνται μὲν πάντες, ὅτι τοῦ ὄντος ἐστὶν ᾗ ὄν, ἕκαστον δὲ τὸ γένος ὄν· ἐπὶ τοσοῦτον δὲ χρῶνται ἐφʼ ὅσον αὐτοῖς ἱκανόν, τοῦτο δʼ ἔστιν ὅσον ἐπέχει τὸ γένος περὶ οὗ φέρουσι τὰς ἀποδείξεις· ὥστʼ ἐπεὶ δῆλον ὅτι ᾗ ὄντα ὑπάρχει πᾶσι (τοῦτο γὰρ αὐτοῖς τὸ κοινόν), τοῦ περὶ τὸ ὂν ᾗ ὂν γνωρίζοντος καὶ περὶ τούτων ἐστὶν ἡ θεωρία. διόπερ οὐθεὶς τῶν κατὰ μέρος ἐπισκοπούντων ἐγχειρεῖ λέγειν τι περὶ αὐτῶν, εἰ ἀληθῆ ἢ μή, οὔτε γεωμέτρης οὔτʼ ἀριθμητικός, ἀλλὰ τῶν φυσικῶν ἔνιοι, εἰκότως τοῦτο δρῶντες· μόνοι γὰρ ᾤοντο περί τε τῆς ὅλης φύσεως σκοπεῖν καὶ περὶ τοῦ ὄντος.","kind":"passage"},{"id":"RAW_DAZY_BOO4:P0012","text":"ἐπεὶ δʼ ἔστιν ἔτι τοῦ φυσικοῦ τις ἀνωτέρω (ἓν γάρ τι γένος τοῦ ὄντος ἡ φύσις) , τοῦ καθόλου καὶ τοῦ περὶ τὴν πρώτην οὐσίαν θεωρητικοῦ καὶ ἡ περὶ τούτων ἂν εἴη σκέψις·  ἔστι δὲ σοφία τις καὶ ἡ φυσική, ἀλλʼ οὐ πρώτη. ὅσα δʼ ἐγχειροῦσι τῶν λεγόντων τινὲς περὶ τῆς ἀληθείας ὃν τρόπον δεῖ ἀποδέχεσθαι, διʼ ἀπαιδευσίαν τῶν ἀναλυτικῶν τοῦτο δρῶσιν· δεῖ γὰρ περὶ τούτων ἥκειν προεπισταμένους ἀλλὰ μὴ ἀκούοντας ζητεῖν.—ὅτι μὲν οὖν τοῦ φιλοσόφου, καὶ τοῦ περὶ πάσης τῆς οὐσίας θεωροῦντος ᾗ πέφυκεν, καὶ περὶ τῶν συλλογιστικῶν ἀρχῶν ἐστὶν ἐπισκέψασθαι, δῆλον· προσήκει δὲ τὸν μάλιστα γνωρίζοντα περὶ ἕκαστον γένος ἔχειν λέγειν τὰς βεβαιοτάτας ἀρχὰς τοῦ πράγματος, ὥστε καὶ τὸν περὶ τῶν ὄντων ᾗ ὄντα τὰς πάντων βεβαιοτάτας. ἔστι δʼ οὗτος ὁ φιλόσοφος.","kind":"passage"},{"id":"RAW_DAZY_BOO4:P0013","text":"βεβαιοτάτη δʼ ἀρχὴ πασῶν περὶ ἣν διαψευσθῆναι ἀδύνατον· γνωριμωτάτην τε γὰρ ἀναγκαῖον εἶναι τὴν τοιαύτην (περὶ γὰρ ἃ μὴ γνωρίζουσιν ἀπατῶνται πάντες) καὶ ἀνυπόθετον. ἣν γὰρ ἀναγκαῖον ἔχειν τὸν ὁτιοῦν ξυνιέντα τῶν ὄντων, τοῦτο οὐχ ὑπόθεσις· ὃ δὲ γνωρίζειν ἀναγκαῖον τῷ ὁτιοῦν γνωρίζοντι, καὶ ἥκειν ἔχοντα ἀναγκαῖον. ὅτι μὲν οὖν βεβαιοτάτη ἡ τοιαύτη πασῶν ἀρχή, δῆλον· τίς δʼ ἔστιν αὕτη, μετὰ ταῦτα λέγωμεν. τὸ γὰρ αὐτὸ ἅμα ὑπάρχειν τε καὶ μὴ ὑπάρχειν ἀδύνατον τῷ αὐτῷ καὶ κατὰ τὸ αὐτό (καὶ ὅσα ἄλλα προσδιορισαίμεθʼ ἄν, ἔστω προσδιωρισμένα πρὸς τὰς λογικὰς δυσχερείας)· αὕτη δὴ πασῶν ἐστὶ βεβαιοτάτη τῶν ἀρχῶν· ἔχει γὰρ τὸν εἰρημένον διορισμόν. ἀδύνατον γὰρ ὁντινοῦν ταὐτὸν ὑπολαμβάνειν εἶναι καὶ μὴ εἶναι, καθάπερ τινὲς οἴονται λέγειν Ἡράκλειτον.","kind":"passage"},{"id":"RAW_DAZY_BOO4:P0014","text":"οὐκ ἔστι γὰρ ἀναγκαῖον, ἅ τις λέγει, ταῦτα καὶ ὑπολαμβάνειν· εἰ δὲ μὴ ἐνδέχεται ἅμα ὑπάρχειν τῷ αὐτῷ τἀναντία (προσδιωρίσθω δʼ ἡμῖν καὶ ταύτῃ τῇ προτάσει τὰ εἰωθότα), ἐναντία δʼ ἐστὶ δόξα δόξῃ ἡ τῆς ἀντιφάσεως, φανερὸν ὅτι ἀδύνατον ἅμα ὑπολαμβάνειν τὸν αὐτὸν εἶναι καὶ μὴ εἶναι τὸ αὐτό· ἅμα γὰρ ἂν ἔχοι τὰς ἐναντίας δόξας ὁ διεψευσμένος περὶ τούτου. διὸ πάντες οἱ ἀποδεικνύντες εἰς ταύτην ἀνάγουσιν ἐσχάτην δόξαν· φύσει γὰρ ἀρχὴ καὶ τῶν ἄλλων ἀξιωμάτων αὕτη πάντων.","kind":"passage"},{"id":"RAW_DAZY_BOO4:P0015","text":"εἰσὶ δέ τινες οἵ, καθάπερ εἴπομεν, αὐτοί τε ἐνδέχεσθαί φασι τὸ αὐτὸ εἶναι καὶ μὴ εἶναι,  καὶ ὑπολαμβάνειν οὕτως. χρῶνται δὲ τῷ λόγῳ τούτῳ πολλοὶ καὶ τῶν περὶ φύσεως. ἡμεῖς δὲ νῦν εἰλήφαμεν ὡς ἀδυνάτου ὄντος ἅμα εἶναι καὶ μὴ εἶναι, καὶ διὰ τούτου ἐδείξαμεν ὅτι βεβαιοτάτη  αὕτη τῶν ἀρχῶν πασῶν. ἀξιοῦσι δὴ καὶ τοῦτο ἀποδεικνύναι τινὲς διʼ ἀπαιδευσίαν· ἔστι γὰρ ἀπαιδευσία τὸ μὴ γιγνώσκειν τίνων δεῖ ζητεῖν ἀπόδειξιν καὶ τίνων οὐ δεῖ· ὅλως μὲν γὰρ ἁπάντων ἀδύνατον ἀπόδειξιν εἶναι (εἰς ἄπειρον γὰρ ἂν βαδίζοι, ὥστε μηδʼ οὕτως εἶναι ἀπόδειξιν), εἰ δέ τινων μὴ δεῖ ζητεῖν ἀπόδειξιν, τίνα ἀξιοῦσιν εἶναι μᾶλλον τοιαύτην ἀρχὴν οὐκ ἂν ἔχοιεν εἰπεῖν. ἔστι δʼ ἀποδεῖξαι ἐλεγκτικῶς καὶ περὶ τούτου ὅτι ἀδύνατον, ἂν μόνον τι λέγῃ ὁ ἀμφισβητῶν· ἂν δὲ μηθέν, γελοῖον τὸ ζητεῖν λόγον πρὸς τὸν μηθενὸς ἔχοντα λόγον, ᾗ μὴ ἔχει· ὅμοιος γὰρ φυτῷ ὁ τοιοῦτος ᾗ τοιοῦτος ἤδη.","kind":"passage"},{"id":"RAW_DAZY_BOO4:P0016","text":"τὸ δʼ ἐλεγκτικῶς ἀποδεῖξαι λέγω διαφέρειν καὶ τὸ ἀποδεῖξαι, ὅτι ἀποδεικνύων μὲν ἂν δόξειεν αἰτεῖσθαι τὸ ἐν ἀρχῇ, ἄλλου δὲ τοῦ τοιούτου αἰτίου ὄντος ἔλεγχος ἂν εἴη καὶ οὐκ ἀπόδειξις. ἀρχὴ δὲ πρὸς ἅπαντα τὰ τοιαῦτα οὐ τὸ ἀξιοῦν ἢ εἶναί τι λέγειν ἢ μὴ εἶναι (τοῦτο μὲν γὰρ τάχʼ ἄν τις ὑπολάβοι τὸ ἐξ ἀρχῆς αἰτεῖν), ἀλλὰ σημαίνειν γέ τι καὶ αὑτῷ καὶ ἄλλῳ· τοῦτο γὰρ ἀνάγκη, εἴπερ λέγοι τι. εἰ γὰρ μή, οὐκ ἂν εἴη τῷ τοιούτῳ λόγος, οὔτʼ αὐτῷ πρὸς αὑτὸν οὔτε πρὸς ἄλλον. ἂν δέ τις τοῦτο διδῷ, ἔσται ἀπόδειξις· ἤδη γάρ τι ἔσται ὡρισμένον. ἀλλʼ αἴτιος οὐχ ὁ ἀποδεικνὺς ἀλλʼ ὁ ὑπομένων· ἀναιρῶν γὰρ λόγον ὑπομένει λόγον. ἔτι δὲ ὁ τοῦτο συγχωρήσας συγκεχώρηκέ τι ἀληθὲς εἶναι χωρὶς ἀποδείξεως .—πρῶτον μὲν οὖν δῆλον ὡς τοῦτό γʼ αὐτὸ ἀληθές, ὅτι σημαίνει τὸ ὄνομα τὸ εἶναι ἢ μὴ εἶναι τοδί, ὥστʼ οὐκ ἂν πᾶν οὕτως καὶ οὐχ οὕτως ἔχοι· ἔτι εἰ τὸ ἄνθρωπος σημαίνει ἕν, ἔστω τοῦτο τὸ ζῷον δίπουν.","kind":"passage"},{"id":"RAW_DAZY_BOO4:P0017","text":"λέγω δὲ τὸ ἓν σημαίνειν τοῦτο· εἰ τοῦτʼ ἔστιν ἄνθρωπος, ἂν ᾖ τι ἄνθρωπος, τοῦτʼ ἔσται τὸ ἀνθρώπῳ εἶναι (διαφέρει δʼ οὐθὲν οὐδʼ εἰ πλείω τις φαίη σημαίνειν μόνον δὲ ὡρισμένα,  τεθείη γὰρ ἂν ἐφʼ ἑκάστῳ λόγῳ ἕτερον ὄνομα· λέγω δʼ οἷον, εἰ μὴ φαίη τὸ ἄνθρωπος ἓν σημαίνειν, πολλὰ δέ, ὧν ἑνὸς μὲν εἷς λόγος τὸ ζῷον δίπουν, εἶεν δὲ καὶ ἕτεροι πλείους, ὡρισμένοι δὲ τὸν ἀριθμόν· τεθείη γὰρ ἂν ἴδιον ὄνομα καθʼ ἕκαστον τὸν λόγον· εἰ δὲ μή , ἀλλʼ ἄπειρα σημαίνειν φαίη, φανερὸν ὅτι οὐκ ἂν εἴη λόγος· τὸ γὰρ μὴ ἓν σημαίνειν οὐθὲν σημαίνειν ἐστίν, μὴ σημαινόντων δὲ τῶν ὀνομάτων ἀνῄρηται τὸ διαλέγεσθαι πρὸς ἀλλήλους, κατὰ δὲ τὴν ἀλήθειαν καὶ πρὸς αὑτόν· οὐθὲν γὰρ ἐνδέχεται νοεῖν μὴ νοοῦντα ἕν, εἰ δʼ ἐνδέχεται, τεθείη ἂν ὄνομα τούτῳ τῷ πράγματι ἕν).—ἔστω δή, ὥσπερ ἐλέχθη κατʼ ἀρχάς, σημαῖνόν τι τὸ ὄνομα καὶ σημαῖνον ἕν· οὐ δὴ ἐνδέχεται τὸ ἀνθρώπῳ εἶναι σημαίνειν ὅπερ ἀνθρώπῳ μὴ εἶναι, εἰ τὸ ἄνθρωπος","kind":"passage"},{"id":"RAW_DAZY_BOO4:P0018","text":"σημαίνει μὴ μόνον καθʼ ἑνὸς ἀλλὰ καὶ ἕν (οὐ γὰρ τοῦτο ἀξιοῦμεν τὸ ἓν σημαίνειν, τὸ καθʼ ἑνός, ἐπεὶ οὕτω γε κἂν τὸ μουσικὸν καὶ τὸ λευκὸν καὶ τὸ ἄνθρωπος ἓν ἐσήμαινεν, ὥστε ἓν ἅπαντα ἔσται· συνώνυμα γάρ). καὶ οὐκ ἔσται εἶναι καὶ μὴ εἶναι τὸ αὐτὸ ἀλλʼ ἢ καθʼ ὁμωνυμίαν, ὥσπερ ἂν εἰ ὃν ἡμεῖς ἄνθρωπον καλοῦμεν, ἄλλοι μὴ ἄνθρωπον καλοῖεν· τὸ δʼ ἀπορούμενον οὐ τοῦτό ἐστιν, εἰ ἐνδέχεται τὸ αὐτὸ ἅμα εἶναι καὶ μὴ εἶναι ἄνθρωπον τὸ ὄνομα, ἀλλὰ τὸ πρᾶγμα. εἰ δὲ μὴ σημαίνει ἕτερον τὸ ἄνθρωπος καὶ τὸ μὴ ἄνθρωπος, δῆλον ὅτι καὶ τὸ μὴ εἶναι ἀνθρώπῳ τοῦ εἶναι ἀνθρώπῳ· ὥστʼ ἔσται τὸ ἀνθρώπῳ  εἶναι μὴ ἀνθρώπῳ εἶναι· ἓν γὰρ ἔσται. τοῦτο γὰρ σημαίνει τὸ εἶναι ἕν, τὸ ὡς λώπιον καὶ ἱμάτιον, εἰ ὁ λόγος εἷς· εἰ δὲ ἔσται ἕν, ἓν σημανεῖ τὸ ἀνθρώπῳ εἶναι καὶ μὴ ἀνθρώπῳ. ἀλλʼ ἐδέδεικτο ὅτι ἕτερον σημαίνει.","kind":"passage"},{"id":"RAW_DAZY_BOO4:P0019","text":"ἀνάγκη τοίνυν, εἴ τί ἐστιν ἀληθὲς εἰπεῖν ὅτι ἄνθρωπος, ζῷον εἶναι δίπουν (τοῦτο γὰρ ἦν ὃ ἐσήμαινε τὸ ἄνθρωπος)· εἰ δʼ ἀνάγκη τοῦτο, οὐκ ἐνδέχεται μὴ εἶναι τότε τὸ αὐτὸ ζῷον δίπουν (τοῦτο γὰρ σημαίνει τὸ ἀνάγκη εἶναι, τὸ ἀδύνατον εἶναι μὴ εἶναι )· οὐκ ἄρα ἐνδέχεται ἅμα ἀληθὲς εἶναι εἰπεῖν τὸ αὐτὸ ἄνθρωπον εἶναι καὶ μὴ εἶναι ἄνθρωπον. ὁ δʼ αὐτὸς λόγος καὶ ἐπὶ τοῦ μὴ εἶναι ἄνθρωπον·  τὸ γὰρ ἀνθρώπῳ εἶναι καὶ τὸ μὴ ἀνθρώπῳ εἶναι ἕτερον σημαίνει, εἴπερ καὶ τὸ λευκὸν εἶναι καὶ τὸ ἄνθρωπον εἶναι ἕτερον· πολὺ γὰρ ἀντίκειται ἐκεῖνο μᾶλλον, ὥστε σημαίνειν ἕτερον. εἰ δὲ καὶ τὸ λευκὸν φήσει τὸ αὐτὸ καὶ ἓν σημαίνειν, πάλιν τὸ αὐτὸ ἐροῦμεν ὅπερ καὶ πρότερον ἐλέχθη, ὅτι ἓν πάντα ἔσται καὶ οὐ μόνον τὰ ἀντικείμενα. εἰ δὲ μὴ ἐνδέχεται τοῦτο, συμβαίνει τὸ λεχθέν, ἂν ἀποκρίνηται τὸ ἐρωτώμενον. ἐὰν δὲ προστιθῇ ἐρωτῶντος ἁπλῶς καὶ τὰς ἀποφάσεις, οὐκ ἀποκρίνεται τὸ ἐρωτώμενον.","kind":"passage"},{"id":"RAW_DAZY_BOO4:P0020","text":"οὐθὲν γὰρ κωλύει εἶναι τὸ αὐτὸ καὶ ἄνθρωπον καὶ λευκὸν καὶ ἄλλα μυρία τὸ πλῆθος· ἀλλʼ ὅμως ἐρομένου εἰ ἀληθὲς εἰπεῖν ἄνθρωπον τοῦτο εἶναι ἢ οὔ, ἀποκριτέον τὸ ἓν σημαῖνον καὶ οὐ προσθετέον ὅτι καὶ λευκὸν καὶ μέγα. καὶ γὰρ ἀδύνατον ἄπειρά γʼ ὄντα τὰ συμβεβηκότα διελθεῖν· ἢ οὖν ἅπαντα διελθέτω ἢ μηθέν. ὁμοίως τοίνυν εἰ καὶ μυριάκις ἐστὶ τὸ αὐτὸ ἄνθρωπος καὶ οὐκ ἄνθρωπος, οὐ προσαποκριτέον τῷ ἐρομένῳ εἰ ἔστιν ἄνθρωπος, ὅτι ἐστὶν ἅμα καὶ οὐκ ἄνθρωπος, εἰ μὴ καὶ τἆλλα ὅσα συμβέβηκε προσαποκριτέον, ὅσα ἐστὶν ἢ μὴ ἔστιν· ἐὰν δὲ τοῦτο ποιῇ, οὐ διαλέγεται.—ὅλως δʼ ἀναιροῦσιν οἱ τοῦτο λέγοντες οὐσίαν καὶ τὸ τί ἦν εἶναι. πάντα γὰρ ἀνάγκη συμβεβηκέναι φάσκειν αὐτοῖς, καὶ τὸ ὅπερ ἀνθρώπῳ εἶναι ἢ ζῴῳ εἶναι μὴ εἶναι. εἰ γὰρ ἔσται τι ὅπερ ἀνθρώπῳ εἶναι, τοῦτο οὐκ ἔσται μὴ ἀνθρώπῳ εἶναι ἢ μὴ εἶναι ἀνθρώπῳ (καίτοι αὗται ἀποφάσεις τούτου)· ἓν γὰρ ἦν ὃ ἐσήμαινε, καὶ ἦν τοῦτό τινος οὐσία.","kind":"passage"},{"id":"RAW_DAZY_BOO4:P0021","text":"τὸ δʼ οὐσίαν σημαίνειν ἐστὶν ὅτι οὐκ ἄλλο τι τὸ εἶναι αὐτῷ. εἰ δʼ ἔσται αὐτῷ τὸ ὅπερ ἀνθρώπῳ εἶναι ἢ ὅπερ μὴ ἀνθρώπῳ εἶναι ἢ ὅπερ μὴ εἶναι ἀνθρώπῳ, ἄλλο ἔσται, ὥστʼ ἀναγκαῖον αὐτοῖς λέγειν ὅτι οὐθενὸς ἔσται τοιοῦτος λόγος, ἀλλὰ πάντα κατὰ συμβεβηκός· τούτῳ γὰρ διώρισται οὐσία καὶ τὸ συμβεβηκός· τὸ γὰρ λευκὸν τῷ ἀνθρώπῳ συμβέβηκεν ὅτι ἔστι μὲν λευκὸς ἀλλʼ οὐχ ὅπερ λευκόν. εἰ δὲ πάντα κατὰ συμβεβηκὸς λέγεται, οὐθὲν ἔσται πρῶτον τὸ καθʼ οὗ, εἰ ἀεὶ τὸ συμβεβηκὸς καθʼ ὑποκειμένου τινὸς σημαίνει τὴν κατηγορίαν.  ἀνάγκη ἄρα εἰς ἄπειρον ἰέναι. ἀλλʼ ἀδύνατον· οὐδὲ γὰρ πλείω συμπλέκεται δυοῖν· τὸ γὰρ συμβεβηκὸς οὐ συμβεβηκότι συμβεβηκός, εἰ μὴ ὅτι ἄμφω συμβέβηκε ταὐτῷ, λέγω δʼ οἷον τὸ λευκὸν μουσικὸν καὶ τοῦτο λευκὸν ὅτι ἄμφω τῷ ἀνθρώπῳ συμβέβηκεν. ἀλλʼ οὐχ ὁ Σωκράτης μουσικὸς οὕτως, ὅτι ἄμφω συμβέβηκεν ἑτέρῳ τινί.","kind":"passage"},{"id":"RAW_DAZY_BOO4:P0022","text":"ἐπεὶ τοίνυν τὰ μὲν οὕτως τὰ δʼ ἐκείνως λέγεται συμβεβηκότα, ὅσα οὕτως λέγεται ὡς τὸ λευκὸν τῷ Σωκράτει, οὐκ ἐνδέχεται ἄπειρα εἶναι ἐπὶ τὸ ἄνω, οἷον τῷ Σωκράτει τῷ λευκῷ ἕτερόν τι συμβεβηκός· οὐ γὰρ γίγνεταί τι ἓν ἐξ ἁπάντων. οὐδὲ δὴ τῷ λευκῷ ἕτερόν τι ἔσται συμβεβηκός, οἷον τὸ μουσικόν· οὐθέν τε γὰρ μᾶλλον τοῦτο ἐκείνῳ ἢ ἐκεῖνο τούτῳ συμβέβηκεν, καὶ ἅμα διώρισται ὅτι τὰ μὲν οὕτω συμβέβηκε τὰ δʼ ὡς τὸ μουσικὸν Σωκράτει· ὅσα δʼ οὕτως, οὐ συμβεβηκότι συμβέβηκε συμβεβηκός, ἀλλʼ ὅσα ἐκείνως, ὥστʼ οὐ πάντα κατὰ συμβεβηκὸς λεχθήσεται. ἔσται ἄρα τι καὶ ὣς οὐσίαν σημαῖνον. εἰ δὲ τοῦτο, δέδεικται ὅτι ἀδύνατον ἅμα κατηγορεῖσθαι τὰς ἀντιφάσεις.—ἔτι εἰ ἀληθεῖς αἱ ἀντιφάσεις ἅμα κατὰ τοῦ αὐτοῦ πᾶσαι, δῆλον ὡς ἅπαντα ἔσται ἕν. ἔσται γὰρ τὸ αὐτὸ καὶ τριήρης καὶ τοῖχος καὶ ἄνθρωπος, εἰ κατὰ παντός τι ἢ καταφῆσαι ἢ ἀποφῆσαι ἐνδέχεται, καθάπερ ἀνάγκη τοῖς τὸν Πρωταγόρου λέγουσι λόγον.","kind":"passage"},{"id":"RAW_DAZY_BOO4:P0023","text":"εἰ γάρ τῳ δοκεῖ μὴ εἶναι τριήρης ὁ ἄνθρωπος, δῆλον ὡς οὐκ ἔστι τριήρης· ὥστε καὶ ἔστιν, εἴπερ ἡ ἀντίφασις ἀληθής. καὶ γίγνεται δὴ τὸ τοῦ Ἀναξαγόρου, ὁμοῦ πάντα χρήματα· ὥστε μηθὲν ἀληθῶς ὑπάρχειν. τὸ ἀόριστον οὖν ἐοίκασι λέγειν, καὶ οἰόμενοι τὸ ὂν λέγειν περὶ τοῦ μὴ ὄντος λέγουσιν· τὸ γὰρ δυνάμει ὂν καὶ μὴ ἐντελεχείᾳ τὸ ἀόριστόν ἐστιν. ἀλλὰ μὴν λεκτέον γʼ αὐτοῖς κατὰ παντὸς παντὸς τὴν κατάφασιν ἢ τὴν ἀπόφασιν· ἄτοπον γὰρ εἰ ἑκάστῳ ἡ μὲν αὐτοῦ ἀπόφασις ὑπάρξει, ἡ δʼ ἑτέρου ὃ μὴ ὑπάρχει αὐτῷ οὐχ ὑπάρξει· λέγω δʼ οἷον εἰ ὀληθὲς εἰπεῖν τὸν ἄνθρωπον ὅτι οὐκ ἄνθρωπος, δῆλον ὅτι καὶ ἢ τριήρης ἢ οὐ τριήρης. εἰ μὲν οὖν ἡ κατάφασις, ἀνάγκη καὶ τὴν ἀπόφασιν· εἰ δὲ μὴ ὑπάρχει ἡ κατάφασις, ἥ γε ἀπόφασις ὑπάρξει μᾶλλον ἢ ἡ αὐτοῦ.","kind":"passage"},{"id":"RAW_DAZY_BOO4:P0024","text":"εἰ οὖν κἀκείνη ὑπάρχει, ὑπάρξει καὶ ἡ τῆς τριήρους· εἰ δʼ αὕτη, καὶ ἡ κατάφασις.—ταῦτά τε οὖν συμβαίνει τοῖς λέγουσι τὸν λόγον τοῦτον, καὶ ὅτι οὐκ ἀνάγκη ἢ φάναι ἢ ἀποφάναι. εἰ γὰρ ἀληθὲς ὅτι ἄνθρωπος καὶ οὐκ ἄνθρωπος, δῆλον ὅτι καὶ οὔτʼ ἄνθρωπος οὔτʼ οὐκ ἄνθρωπος ἔσται· τοῖν γὰρ δυοῖν δύο ἀποφάσεις, εἰ δὲ μία ἐξ ἀμφοῖν ἐκείνη, καὶ αὕτη μία ἂν εἴη ἀντικειμένη.—ἔτι ἤτοι περὶ ἅπαντα οὕτως ἔχει, καὶ ἔστι καὶ λευκὸν καὶ οὐ λευκὸν καὶ ὂν καὶ οὐκ ὄν, καὶ περὶ τὰς ἄλλας φάσεις καὶ ἀποφάσεις ὁμοιοτρόπως, ἢ οὒ ἀλλὰ περὶ μέν τινας, περί τινας δʼ οὔ. καὶ εἰ μὲν μὴ περὶ πάσας, αὗται ἂν εἶεν ὁμολογούμεναι· εἰ δὲ περὶ πάσας, πάλιν ἤτοι καθʼ ὅσων τὸ φῆσαι καὶ ἀποφῆσαι καὶ καθʼ ὅσων ἀποφῆσαι καὶ φῆσαι, ἢ κατὰ μὲν ὧν φῆσαι καὶ ἀποφῆσαι, καθʼ ὅσων δὲ ἀποφῆσαι οὐ πάντων φῆσαι.","kind":"passage"},{"id":"RAW_DAZY_BOO4:P0025","text":"καὶ εἰ μὲν οὕτως, εἴη ἄν τι παγίως οὐκ ὄν, καὶ αὕτη βεβαία δόξα, καὶ εἰ τὸ μὴ εἶναι βέβαιόν τι καὶ γνώριμον, γνωριμωτέρα ἂν εἴη ἡ φάσις ἡ ἀντικειμένη· εἰ δὲ ὁμοίως καὶ ὅσα ἀποφῆσαι φάναι, ἀνάγκη ἤτοι ἀληθὲς διαιροῦντα λέγειν, οἷον ὅτι λευκὸν καὶ πάλιν ὅτι οὐ λευκόν, ἢ οὔ. καὶ εἰ μὲν μὴ ἀληθὲς διαιροῦντα λέγειν, οὐ λέγει τε ταῦτα καὶ οὐκ ἔστιν οὐθέν (τὰ δὲ μὴ ὄντα πῶς ἂν φθέγξαιτο ἢ βαδίσειεν;), καὶ πάντα δʼ ἂν εἴη ἕν, ὥσπερ καὶ πρότερον εἴρηται, καὶ ταὐτὸν ἔσται καὶ ἄνθρωπος καὶ θεὸς καὶ τριήρης καὶ αἱ ἀντιφάσεις αὐτῶν (εἰ γὰρ ὁμοίως καθʼ ἑκάστου, οὐδὲν διοίσει ἕτερον ἑτέρου· εἰ γὰρ διοίσει, τοῦτʼ ἔσται ἀληθὲς καὶ ἴδιον)· ὁμοίως δὲ καὶ εἰ διαιροῦντα ἐνδέχεται ἀληθεύειν, συμβαίνει τὸ λεχθέν, πρὸς δὲ τούτῳ ὅτι πάντες ἂν ἀληθεύοιεν καὶ πάντες ἂν ψεύδοιντο, καὶ αὐτὸς αὑτὸν ὁμολογεῖ  ψεύδεσθαι. ἅμα δὲ φανερὸν ὅτι περὶ οὐθενός ἐστι πρὸς τοῦτον ἡ σκέψις· οὐθὲν γὰρ λέγει.","kind":"passage"},{"id":"RAW_DAZY_BOO4:P0026","text":"οὔτε γὰρ οὕτως οὔτʼ οὐχ οὕτως λέγει, ἀλλʼ οὕτως τε καὶ οὐχ οὕτως· καὶ πάλιν γε ταῦτα ἀπόφησιν ἄμφω, ὅτι οὔθʼ οὕτως οὔτε οὐχ οὕτως· εἰ γὰρ μή, ἤδη ἄν τι εἴη ὡρισμένον.—ἔτι εἰ ὅταν ἡ φάσις ἀληθὴς ᾖ, ἡ ἀπόφασις ψευδής, κἂν αὕτη ἀληθὴς ᾖ, ἡ κατάφασις ψευδής, οὐκ ἂν εἴη τὸ αὐτὸ ἅμα φάναι καὶ ἀποφάναι ἀληθῶς.  ἀλλʼ ἴσως φαῖεν ἂν τοῦτʼ εἶναι τὸ ἐξ ἀρχῆς κείμενον.—ἔτι ἆρα ὁ μὲν ἢ ἔχειν πως ὑπολαμβάνων ἢ μὴ ἔχειν διέψευσται, ὁ δὲ ἄμφω ἀληθεύει; εἰ γὰρ ἀληθεύει, τί ἂν εἴη τὸ λεγόμενον ὅτι τοιαύτη τῶν ὄντων ἡ φύσις; εἰ δὲ μὴ ἀληθεύει, ἀλλὰ μᾶλλον ἀληθεύει ἢ ὁ ἐκείνως ὑπολαμβάνων, ἤδη πως ἔχοι ἂν τὰ ὄντα, καὶ τοῦτʼ ἀληθὲς ἂν εἴη, καὶ οὐχ ἅμα καὶ οὐκ ἀληθές. εἰ δὲ ὁμοίως ἅπαντες καὶ ψεύδονται καὶ ἀληθῆ λέγουσιν, οὔτε φθέγξασθαι οὔτʼ εἰπεῖν τῷ τοιούτῳ ἔσται· ἅμα γὰρ ταῦτά τε καὶ οὐ ταῦτα λέγει.","kind":"passage"},{"id":"RAW_DAZY_BOO4:P0027","text":"εἰ δὲ μηθὲν ὑπολαμβάνει ἀλλʼ ὁμοίως οἴεται καὶ οὐκ οἴεται, τί ἂν διαφερόντως ἔχοι τῶν γε φυτῶν; ὅθεν καὶ μάλιστα φανερόν ἐστιν ὅτι οὐδεὶς οὕτω διάκειται οὔτε τῶν ἄλλων οὔτε τῶν λεγόντων τὸν λόγον τοῦτον. διὰ τί γὰρ βαδίζει Μέγαράδε ἀλλʼ οὐχ ἡσυχάζει, οἰόμενος βαδίζειν δεῖν; οὐδʼ εὐθέως ἕωθεν πορεύεται εἰς φρέαρ ἢ εἰς φάραγγα, ἐὰν τύχῃ, ἀλλὰ φαίνεται εὐλαβούμενος, ὡς οὐχ ὁμοίως οἰόμενος μὴ ἀγαθὸν εἶναι τὸ ἐμπεσεῖν καὶ ἀγαθόν; δῆλον ἄρα ὅτι τὸ μὲν βέλτιον ὑπολαμβάνει τὸ δʼ οὐ βέλτιον. εἰ δὲ τοῦτο, καὶ τὸ μὲν ἄνθρωπον τὸ δʼ οὐκ ἄνθρωπον καὶ τὸ μὲν γλυκὺ τὸ δʼ οὐ γλυκὺ ἀνάγκη ὑπολαμβάνειν. οὐ γὰρ ἐξ ἴσου ἅπαντα ζητεῖ καὶ ὑπολαμβάνει, ὅταν οἰηθεὶς βέλτιον εἶναι τὸ πιεῖν ὕδωρ καὶ ἰδεῖν ἄνθρωπον εἶτα ζητῇ αὐτά· καίτοι ἔδει γε, εἰ ταὐτὸν ἦν ὁμοίως καὶ ἄνθρωπος καὶ οὐκ ἄνθρωπος.","kind":"passage"},{"id":"RAW_DAZY_BOO4:P0028","text":"ἀλλʼ ὅπερ ἐλέχθη, οὐθεὶς ὃς οὐ φαίνεται τὰ μὲν εὐλαβούμενος τὰ δʼ οὔ· ὥστε, ὡς ἔοικε, πάντες ὑπολαμβάνουσιν ἔχειν ἁπλῶς, εἰ μὴ περὶ ἅπαντα, ἀλλὰ περὶ τὸ ἄμεινον καὶ χεῖρον. εἰ δὲ μὴ ἐπιστάμενοι ἀλλὰ δοξάζοντες, πολὺ μᾶλλον ἐπιμελητέον ἂν εἴη τῆς ἀληθείας, ὥσπερ καὶ νοσώδει ὄντι ἢ ὑγιεινῷ τῆς ὑγιείας· καὶ γὰρ ὁ δοξάζων πρὸς τὸν ἐπιστάμενον οὐχ ὑγιεινῶς διάκειται πρὸς τὴν ἀλήθειαν.—ἔτι εἰ ὅτι μάλιστα πάντα οὕτως ἔχει καὶ οὐχ οὕτως, ἀλλὰ τό γε μᾶλλον καὶ ἧττον ἔνεστιν ἐν τῇ φύσει τῶν ὄντων· οὐ γὰρ ἂν ὁμοίως φήσαιμεν εἶναι τὰ δύο ἄρτια καὶ τὰ τρία, οὐδʼ ὁμοίως διέψευσται ὁ τὰ τέτταρα πέντε οἰόμενος καὶ ὁ χίλια. εἰ οὖν μὴ ὁμοίως, δῆλον ὅτι ἅτερος ἧττον, ὥστε μᾶλλον ἀληθεύει. εἰ οὖν τὸ μᾶλλον ἐγγύτερον,  εἴη γε ἄν τι ἀληθὲς οὗ ἐγγύτερον τὸ μᾶλλον ἀληθές.","kind":"passage"},{"id":"RAW_DAZY_BOO4:P0029","text":"κἂν εἰ μὴ ἔστιν, ἀλλʼ ἤδη γέ τι ἔστι βεβαιότερον καὶ ἀληθινώτερον, καὶ τοῦ λόγου ἀπηλλαγμένοι ἂν εἴημεν τοῦ ἀκράτου καὶ κωλύοντός τι τῇ διανοίᾳ ὁρίσαι.","kind":"passage"},{"id":"RAW_DAZY_BOO4:P0030","text":"ἔστι δʼ ἀπὸ τῆς αὐτῆς δόξης καὶ ὁ Πρωταγόρου λόγος, καὶ ἀνάγκη ὁμοίως αὐτοὺς ἄμφω ἢ εἶναι ἢ μὴ εἶναι· εἴτε γὰρ τὰ δοκοῦντα πάντα ἐστὶν ἀληθῆ καὶ τὰ φαινόμενα, ἀνάγκη εἶναι πάντα ἅμα ἀληθῆ καὶ ψευδῆ (πολλοὶ γὰρ τἀναντία ὑπολαμβάνουσιν ἀλλήλοις, καὶ τοὺς μὴ ταὐτὰ δοξάζοντας ἑαυτοῖς διεψεῦσθαι νομίζουσιν· ὥστʼ ἀνάγκη τὸ αὐτὸ εἶναί τε καὶ μὴ εἶναι), καὶ εἰ τοῦτʼ ἔστιν, ἀνάγκη τὰ δοκοῦντα εἶναι πάντʼ ἀληθῆ (τὰ ἀντικείμενα γὰρ δοξάζουσιν ἀλλήλοις οἱ διεψευσμένοι καὶ ἀληθεύοντες· εἰ οὖν ἔχει τὰ ὄντα οὕτως, ἀληθεύσουσι πάντες) . ὅτι μὲν οὖν ἀπὸ τῆς αὐτῆς εἰσὶ διανοίας ἀμφότεροι οἱ λόγοι, δῆλον· ἔστι δʼ οὐχ ὁ αὐτὸς τρόπος πρὸς ἅπαντας τῆς ἐντεύξεως· οἱ μὲν γὰρ πειθοῦς δέονται οἱ δὲ βίας.","kind":"passage"},{"id":"RAW_DAZY_BOO4:P0031","text":"ὅσοι μὲν γὰρ ἐκ τοῦ ἀπορῆσαι ὑπέλαβον οὕτως, τούτων εὐΐατος ἡ ἄγνοια (οὐ γὰρ πρὸς τὸν λόγον ἀλλὰ πρὸς τὴν διάνοιαν ἡ ἀπάντησις αὐτῶν)· ὅσοι δὲ λόγου χάριν λέγουσι, τούτων δʼ ἔλεγχος ἴασις τοῦ ἐν τῇ φωνῇ λόγου καὶ τοῦ ἐν τοῖς ὀνόμασιν. ἐλήλυθε δὲ τοῖς διαποροῦσιν αὕτη ἡ δόξα ἐκ τῶν αἰσθητῶν, ἡ μὲν τοῦ ἅμα τὰς ἀντιφάσεις καὶ τἀναντία ὑπάρχειν ὁρῶσιν ἐκ ταὐτοῦ γιγνόμενα τἀναντία· εἰ οὖν μὴ ἐνδέχεται γίγνεσθαι τὸ μὴ ὄν, προϋπῆρχεν ὁμοίως τὸ πρᾶγμα ἄμφω ὄν, ὥσπερ καὶ Ἀναξαγόρας μεμῖχθαι πᾶν ἐν παντί φησι καὶ Δημόκριτος· καὶ γὰρ οὗτος τὸ κενὸν καὶ τὸ πλῆρες ὁμοίως καθʼ ὁτιοῦν ὑπάρχειν μέρος, καίτοι τὸ μὲν ὂν τούτων εἶναι τὸ δὲ μὴ ὄν.","kind":"passage"},{"id":"RAW_DAZY_BOO4:P0032","text":"πρὸς μὲν οὖν τοὺς ἐκ τούτων ὑπολαμβάνοντας ἐροῦμεν ὅτι τρόπον μέν τινα ὀρθῶς λέγουσι τρόπον δέ τινα ἀγνοοῦσιν· τὸ γὰρ ὂν λέγεται διχῶς, ὥστʼ ἔστιν ὃν τρόπον ἐνδέχεται γίγνεσθαί τι ἐκ τοῦ μὴ ὄντος, ἔστι δʼ ὃν οὔ, καὶ ἅμα τὸ αὐτὸ εἶναι καὶ ὂν καὶ μὴ ὄν, ἀλλʼ οὐ κατὰ ταὐτὸ · δυνάμει  μὲν γὰρ ἐνδέχεται ἅμα ταὐτὸ εἶναι τὰ ἐναντία, ἐντελεχείᾳ δʼ οὔ. ἔτι δʼ ἀξιώσομεν αὐτοὺς ὑπολαμβάνειν καὶ ἄλλην τινὰ οὐσίαν εἶναι τῶν ὄντων ᾗ οὔτε κίνησις ὑπάρχει οὔτε φθορὰ οὔτε γένεσις τὸ παράπαν.  —ὅμοιως δὲ καὶ ἡ περὶ τὰ φαινόμενα ἀλήθεια ἐνίοις ἐκ τῶν αἰσθητῶν ἐλήλυθεν.","kind":"passage"},{"id":"RAW_DAZY_BOO4:P0033","text":"τὸ μὲν γὰρ ἀληθὲς οὐ πλήθει κρίνεσθαι οἴονται προσήκειν οὐδὲ ὀλιγότητι, τὸ δʼ αὐτὸ τοῖς μὲν γλυκὺ γευομένοις δοκεῖν εἶναι τοῖς δὲ πικρόν, ὥστʼ εἰ πάντες ἔκαμνον ἢ πάντες παρεφρόνουν, δύο δʼ ἢ τρεῖς ὑγίαινον ἢ νοῦν εἶχον, δοκεῖν ἂν τούτους κάμνειν καὶ παραφρονεῖν τοὺς δʼ ἄλλους οὔ· ἔτι δὲ καὶ πολλοῖς τῶν ἄλλων ζῴων τἀναντία  φαίνεσθαι καὶ ἡμῖν, καὶ αὐτῷ δὲ ἑκάστῳ πρὸς αὑτὸν οὐ ταὐτὰ κατὰ τὴν αἴσθησιν ἀεὶ δοκεῖν. ποῖα οὖν τούτων ἀληθῆ ἢ ψευδῆ, ἄδηλον· οὐθὲν γὰρ μᾶλλον τάδε ἢ τάδε ἀληθῆ, ἀλλʼ ὁμοίως. διὸ Δημόκριτός γέ φησιν ἤτοι οὐθὲν εἶναι ἀληθὲς ἢ ἡμῖν γʼ ἄδηλον. ὅλως δὲ διὰ τὸ ὑπολαμβάνειν φρόνησιν μὲν τὴν αἴσθησιν, ταύτην δʼ εἶναι ἀλλοίωσιν, τὸ φαινόμενον κατὰ τὴν αἴσθησιν ἐξ ἀνάγκης ἀληθὲς εἶναί φασιν· ἐκ τούτων γὰρ καὶ Ἐμπεδοκλῆς καὶ Δημόκριτος καὶ τῶν ἄλλων ὡς ἔπος εἰπεῖν ἕκαστος τοιαύταις δόξαις γεγένηνται ἔνοχοι.","kind":"passage"},{"id":"RAW_DAZY_BOO4:P0034","text":"καὶ γὰρ Ἐμπεδοκλῆς μεταβάλλοντας τὴν ἕξιν μεταβάλλειν φησὶ τὴν φρόνησιν·","kind":"passage"},{"id":"RAW_DAZY_BOO4:P0035","text":"πρὸς παρεὸν γὰρ μῆτις ἐναύξεται ἀνθρώποισιν.","kind":"passage"},{"id":"RAW_DAZY_BOO4:P0036","text":"Empedocles Fr. 106καὶ ἐν ἑτέροις δὲ λέγει ὅτι","kind":"passage"},{"id":"RAW_DAZY_BOO4:P0037","text":"ὅσσον δʼ ἀλλοῖοι μετέφυν, τόσον ἄρ σφισιν αἰεὶ","kind":"passage"},{"id":"RAW_DAZY_BOO4:P0038","text":"καὶ τὸ φρονεῖν ἀλλοῖα παρίστατο.","kind":"passage"},{"id":"RAW_DAZY_BOO4:P0039","text":"Empedocles Fr. 108 καὶ Παρμενίδης δὲ ἀποφαίνεται τὸν αὐτὸν τρόπον·","kind":"passage"},{"id":"RAW_DAZY_BOO4:P0040","text":"ὡς γὰρ ἑκάστοτʼ ἔχει κρᾶσιν μελέων πολυκάμπτων,","kind":"passage"},{"id":"RAW_DAZY_BOO4:P0041","text":"τὼς νόος ἀνθρώποισι παρίσταται· τὸ γὰρ αὐτὸ","kind":"passage"},{"id":"RAW_DAZY_BOO4:P0042","text":"ἔστιν ὅπερ φρονέει, μελέων φύσις ἀνθρώποισιν","kind":"passage"},{"id":"RAW_DAZY_BOO4:P0043","text":"καὶ πᾶσιν καὶ παντί· τὸ γὰρ πλέον ἐστὶ νόημα.","kind":"passage"},{"id":"RAW_DAZY_BOO4:P0044","text":"Parmenides Fr. 16 Ἀναξαγόρου δὲ καὶ ἀπόφθεγμα μνημονεύεται πρὸς τῶν ἑταίρων τινάς, ὅτι τοιαῦτʼ αὐτοῖς ἔσται τὰ ὄντα οἷα ἂν ὑπολάβωσιν. φασὶ δὲ καὶ τὸν Ὅμηρον ταύτην ἔχοντα φαίνεσθαι τὴν δόξαν, ὅτι ἐποίησε τὸν Ἕκτορα, ὡς ἐξέστη ὑπὸ τῆς πληγῆς, κεῖσθαι ἀλλοφρονέοντα, ὡς φρονοῦντας μὲν καὶ τοὺς παραφρονοῦντας ἀλλʼ οὐ ταὐτά. δῆλον οὖν ὅτι, εἰ ἀμφότεραι φρονήσεις, καὶ τὰ ὄντα ἅμα οὕτω τε καὶ οὐχ οὕτως ἔχει. ᾗ καὶ χαλεπώτατον τὸ συμβαῖνόν ἐστιν· εἰ γὰρ οἱ μάλιστα τὸ ἐνδεχόμενον ἀληθὲς ἑωρακότες—οὗτοι δʼ εἰσὶν οἱ μάλιστα ζητοῦντες αὐτὸ καὶ φιλοῦντες—οὗτοι τοιαύτας ἔχουσι τὰς δόξας καὶ ταῦτα ἀποφαίνονται περὶ τῆς ἀληθείας, πῶς οὐκ ἄξιον ἀθυμῆσαι τοὺς φιλοσοφεῖν ἐγχειροῦντας; τὸ γὰρ τὰ πετόμενα διώκειν τὸ ζητεῖν ἂν εἴη τὴν ἀλήθειαν.","kind":"passage"},{"id":"RAW_DAZY_BOO4:P0045","text":"—αἴτιον δὲ τῆς δόξης τούτοις ὅτι περὶ τῶν ὄντων μὲν τὴν ἀλήθειαν ἐσκόπουν, τὰ δʼ ὄντα ὑπέλαβον εἶναι τὰ αἰσθητὰ μόνον· ἐν δὲ τούτοις πολλὴ ἡ τοῦ ἀορίστου φύσις ἐνυπάρχει καὶ ἡ τοῦ ὄντος οὕτως ὥσπερ εἴπομεν· διὸ εἰκότως μὲν λέγουσιν, οὐκ ἀληθῆ δὲ λέγουσιν (οὕτω γὰρ ἁρμόττει μᾶλλον εἰπεῖν ἢ ὥσπερ Ἐπίχαρμος εἰς Ξενοφάνην). ἔτι δὲ πᾶσαν ὁρῶντες ταύτην κινουμένην τὴν φύσιν, κατὰ δὲ τοῦ μεταβάλλοντος οὐθὲν ἀληθευόμενον, περί γε τὸ πάντῃ πάντως μεταβάλλον οὐκ ἐνδέχεσθαι ἀληθεύειν. ἐκ γὰρ ταύτης τῆς ὑπολήψεως ἐξήνθησεν ἡ ἀκροτάτη δόξα τῶν εἰρημένων, ἡ τῶν φασκόντων ἡρακλειτίζειν καὶ οἵαν Κρατύλος εἶχεν, ὃς τὸ τελευταῖον οὐθὲν ᾤετο δεῖν λέγειν ἀλλὰ τὸν δάκτυλον ἐκίνει μόνον, καὶ Ἡρακλείτῳ ἐπετίμα εἰπόντι ὅτι δὶς τῷ αὐτῷ ποταμῷ οὐκ ἔστιν ἐμβῆναι· αὐτὸς γὰρ ᾤετο οὐδʼ ἅπαξ.","kind":"passage"},{"id":"RAW_DAZY_BOO4:P0046","text":"ἡμεῖς δὲ καὶ πρὸς τοῦτον τὸν λόγον ἐροῦμεν ὅτι τὸ μὲν μεταβάλλον ὅτε μεταβάλλει ἔχει τινὰ αὐτοῖς λόγον μὴ οἴεσθαι εἶναι, καίτοι ἔστι γε ἀμφισβητήσιμον· τό τε γὰρ ἀποβάλλον ἔχει τι τοῦ ἀποβαλλομένου, καὶ τοῦ γιγνομένου ἤδη ἀνάγκη τι εἶναι, ὅλως τε εἰ φθείρεται, ὑπάρξει τι ὄν, καὶ εἰ γίγνεται, ἐξ οὗ γίγνεται καὶ ὑφʼ οὗ γεννᾶται ἀναγκαῖον εἶναι, καὶ τοῦτο μὴ ἰέναι εἰς ἄπειρον. ἀλλὰ ταῦτα παρέντες ἐκεῖνα λέγωμεν, ὅτι οὐ ταὐτό ἐστι τὸ μεταβάλλειν κατὰ τὸ ποσὸν καὶ κατὰ τὸ ποιόν· κατὰ μὲν οὖν τὸ ποσὸν ἔστω μὴ μένον, ἀλλὰ κατὰ τὸ εἶδος ἅπαντα γιγνώσκομεν.","kind":"passage"},{"id":"RAW_DAZY_BOO4:P0047","text":"ἔτι δʼ ἄξιον ἐπιτιμῆσαι τοῖς οὕτως ὑπολαμβάνουσιν, ὅτι καὶ αὐτῶν τῶν αἰσθητῶν ἐπὶ τῶν ἐλαττόνων τὸν ἀριθμὸν ἰδόντες οὕτως ἔχοντα περὶ ὅλου τοῦ οὐρανοῦ ὁμοίως ἀπεφήναντο· ὁ γὰρ περὶ ἡμᾶς τοῦ αἰσθητοῦ τόπος ἐν φθορᾷ καὶ γενέσει διατελεῖ μόνος ὤν, ἀλλʼ οὗτος οὐθὲν ὡς εἰπεῖν μόριον τοῦ παντός ἐστιν, ὥστε δικαιότερον ἂν διʼ ἐκεῖνα τούτων ἀπεψηφίσαντο ἢ διὰ ταῦτα ἐκείνων κατεψηφίσαντο. ἔτι δὲ δῆλον ὅτι καὶ πρὸς τούτους ταὐτὰ τοῖς πάλαι λεχθεῖσιν ἐροῦμεν· ὅτι γὰρ ἔστιν ἀκίνητός τις φύσις δεικτέον αὐτοῖς καὶ πειστέον αὐτούς. καίτοι γε συμβαίνει τοῖς ἅμα φάσκουσιν εἶναι καὶ μὴ εἶναι ἠρεμεῖν μᾶλλον φάναι πάντα ἢ κινεῖσθαι· οὐ γὰρ ἔστιν εἰς ὅ τι μεταβαλεῖ· ἅπαντα γὰρ ὑπάρχει πᾶσιν.  —περὶ δὲ τῆς ἀληθείας, ὡς οὐ πᾶν τὸ φαινόμενον ἀληθές, πρῶτον μὲν ὅτι οὐδʼ εἰ ἡ αἴσθησις μὴ ψευδὴς τοῦ γε ἰδίου ἐστίν, ἀλλʼ ἡ φαντασία οὐ ταὐτὸν τῇ αἰσθήσει.","kind":"passage"},{"id":"RAW_DAZY_BOO4:P0048","text":"εἶτʼ ἄξιον θαυμάσαι εἰ τοῦτʼ ἀποροῦσι, πότερον τηλικαῦτά ἐστι τὰ μεγέθη καὶ τὰ χρώματα τοιαῦτα οἷα τοῖς ἄπωθεν φαίνεται ἢ οἷα τοῖς ἐγγύθεν, καὶ πότερον οἷα τοῖς ὑγιαίνουσιν ἢ οἷα τοῖς κάμνουσιν, καὶ βαρύτερα πότερον ἃ τοῖς ἀσθενοῦσιν ἢ ἃ τοῖς ἰσχύουσιν, καὶ ἀληθῆ πότερον ἃ τοῖς καθεύδουσιν ἢ ἃ τοῖς ἐγρηγορόσιν. ὅτι μὲν γὰρ οὐκ οἴονταί γε, φανερόν· οὐθεὶς γοῦν, ἐὰν ὑπολάβῃ νύκτωρ Ἀθήνῃσιν εἶναι ὢν ἐν Λιβύῃ, πορεύεται εἰς τὸ ᾠδεῖον. ἔτι δὲ περὶ τοῦ μέλλοντος, ὥσπερ καὶ Πλάτων λέγει, οὐ δήπου ὁμοίως κυρία ἡ τοῦ ἰατροῦ δόξα καὶ ἡ τοῦ ἀγνοοῦντος, οἷον περὶ τοῦ μέλλοντος ἔσεσθαι ὑγιοῦς ἢ μὴ μέλλοντος. ἔτι δὲ ἐπʼ αὐτῶν τῶν αἰσθήσεων οὐχ ὁμοίως κυρία ἡ τοῦ ἀλλοτρίου καὶ ἰδίου ἢ τοῦ πλησίον καὶ τοῦ αὑτῆς, ἀλλὰ περὶ μὲν χρώματος ὄψις, οὐ γεῦσις, περὶ δὲ χυμοῦ γεῦσις, οὐκ ὄψις· ὧν ἑκάστη ἐν τῷ αὐτῷ χρόνῳ περὶ τὸ αὐτὸ οὐδέποτε φησιν ἅμα οὕτω καὶ οὐχ οὕτως ἔχειν.","kind":"passage"},{"id":"RAW_DAZY_BOO4:P0049","text":"ἀλλʼ οὐδὲ ἐν ἑτέρῳ χρόνῳ περί γε τὸ πάθος ἠμφισβήτησεν, ἀλλὰ περὶ τὸ ᾧ συμβέβηκε τὸ πάθος. λέγω δʼ οἷον ὁ μὲν αὐτὸς οἶνος δόξειεν ἂν ἢ μεταβαλὼν ἢ τοῦ σώματος μεταβαλόντος ὁτὲ μὲν εἶναι γλυκὺς ὁτὲ δὲ οὐ γλυκύς· ἀλλʼ οὐ τό γε γλυκύ, οἷόν ἐστιν ὅταν ᾖ, οὐδεπώποτε μετέβαλεν, ἀλλʼ ἀεὶ ἀληθεύει περὶ αὐτοῦ, καὶ ἔστιν ἐξ ἀνάγκης τὸ ἐσόμενον γλυκὺ τοιοῦτον. καίτοι τοῦτο ἀναιροῦσιν οὗτοι οἱ λόγοι ἅπαντες, ὥσπερ καὶ οὐσίαν μὴ εἶναι μηθενός, οὕτω μηδʼ ἐξ ἀνάγκης μηθέν· τὸ γὰρ ἀναγκαῖον οὐκ ἐνδέχεται ἄλλως καὶ ἄλλως ἔχειν, ὥστʼ εἴ τι ἔστιν ἐξ ἀνάγκης, οὐχ ἕξει οὕτω τε καὶ οὐχ οὕτως.—ὅλως τʼ εἴπερ ἔστι τὸ αἰσθητὸν μόνον, οὐθὲν ἂν εἴη μὴ ὄντων τῶν ἐμψύχων· αἴσθησις γὰρ οὐκ ἂν εἴη. τὸ μὲν οὖν μήτε τὰ αἰσθητὰ εἶναι μήτε τὰ αἰσθήματα ἴσως ἀληθές (τοῦ γὰρ αἰσθανομένου πάθος τοῦτό ἐστι), τὸ δὲ τὰ ὑποκείμενα μὴ εἶναι, ἃ ποιεῖ τὴν αἴσθησιν, καὶ ἄνευ αἰσθήσεως, ἀδύνατον.","kind":"passage"},{"id":"RAW_DAZY_BOO4:P0050","text":"οὐ γὰρ δὴ ἥ γʼ αἴσθησις αὐτὴ ἑαυτῆς ἐστίν, ἀλλʼ ἔστι τι καὶ ἕτερον παρὰ τὴν αἴσθησιν, ὃ ἀνάγκη πρότερον εἶναι τῆς αἰσθήσεως·  τὸ γὰρ κινοῦν τοῦ κινουμένου φύσει πρότερόν ἐστι, κἂν εἰ λέγεται πρὸς ἄλληλα ταῦτα, οὐθὲν ἧττον.","kind":"passage"},{"id":"RAW_DAZY_BOO4:P0051","text":"εἰσὶ δέ τινες οἳ ἀποροῦσι καὶ τῶν ταῦτα πεπεισμένων καὶ τῶν τοὺς λόγους τούτους μόνον λεγόντων· ζητοῦσι γὰρ τίς ὁ κρινῶν τὸν ὑγιαίνοντα καὶ ὅλως τὸν περὶ ἕκαστα κρινοῦντα ὀρθῶς. τὰ δὲ τοιαῦτα ἀπορήματα ὅμοιά ἐστι τῷ ἀπορεῖν πότερον καθεύδομεν νῦν ἢ ἐγρηγόραμεν, δύνανται δʼ αἱ ἀπορίαι αἱ τοιαῦται πᾶσαι τὸ αὐτό· πάντων γὰρ λόγον ἀξιοῦσιν εἶναι οὗτοι· ἀρχὴν γὰρ ζητοῦσι, καὶ ταύτην διʼ ἀποδείξεως λαμβάνειν, ἐπεὶ ὅτι γε πεπεισμένοι οὐκ εἰσί, φανεροί εἰσιν ἐν ταῖς πράξεσιν. ἀλλʼ ὅπερ εἴπομεν, τοῦτο αὐτῶν τὸ πάθος ἐστίν· λόγον γὰρ ζητοῦσιν ὧν οὐκ ἔστι λόγος· ἀποδείξεως γὰρ ἀρχὴ οὐκ ἀπόδειξίς ἐστιν. οὗτοι μὲν οὖν ῥᾳδίως ἂν τοῦτο πεισθεῖεν (ἔστι γὰρ οὐ χαλεπὸν λαβεῖν)· οἱ δʼ ἐν τῷ λόγῳ τὴν βίαν μόνον ζητοῦντες ἀδύνατον ζητοῦσιν· ἐναντία γὰρ εἰπεῖν ἀξιοῦσιν, εὐθὺς ἐναντία λέγοντες.","kind":"passage"},{"id":"RAW_DAZY_BOO4:P0052","text":"εἰ δὲ μὴ ἔστι πάντα πρός τι, ἀλλʼ ἔνιά ἐστι καὶ αὐτὰ καθʼ αὑτά, οὐκ ἂν εἴη πᾶν τὸ φαινόμενον ἀληθές· τὸ γὰρ φαινόμενον τινί ἐστι φαινόμενον· ὥστε ὁ λέγων ἅπαντα τὰ φαινόμενα εἶναι ἀληθῆ ἅπαντα ποιεῖ τὰ ὄντα πρός τι. διὸ καὶ φυλακτέον τοῖς τὴν βίαν ἐν τῷ λόγῳ ζητοῦσιν, ἅμα δὲ καὶ ὑπέχειν λόγον ἀξιοῦσιν, ὅτι οὐ τὸ φαινόμενον ἔστιν ἀλλὰ τὸ φαινόμενον ᾧ φαίνεται καὶ ὅτε φαίνεται καὶ ᾗ καὶ ὥς. ἂν δʼ ὑπέχωσι μὲν λόγον, μὴ οὕτω δʼ ὑπέχωσι, συμβήσεται αὑτοῖς τἀναντία ταχὺ λέγειν.","kind":"passage"},{"id":"RAW_DAZY_BOO4:P0053","text":"ἐνδέχεται γὰρ τὸ αὐτὸ κατὰ μὲν τὴν ὄψιν μέλι φαίνεσθαι τῇ δὲ γεύσει μή, καὶ τῶν ὀφθαλμῶν δυοῖν ὄντοιν μὴ ταὐτὰ ἑκατέρᾳ τῇ ὄψει, ἂν ὦσιν ἀνόμοιαι· ἐπεὶ πρός γε τοὺς διὰ τὰς πάλαι εἰρημένας αἰτίας τὸ φαινόμενον φάσκοντας ἀληθὲς εἶναι, καὶ διὰ τοῦτο πάνθʼ ὁμοίως εἶναι ψευδῆ καὶ ἀληθῆ· οὔτε γὰρ ἅπασι ταὐτὰ φαίνεσθαι οὔτε ταὐτῷ ἀεὶ ταὐτά, ἀλλὰ πολλάκις τἀναντία κατὰ τὸν αὐτὸν χρόνον (ἡ μὲν γὰρ ἁφὴ δύο λέγει ἐν τῇ ἐπαλλάξει τῶν δακτύλων ἡ δʼ ὄψις ἕν)·—ἀλλʼ οὔ τι τῇ αὐτῇ γε καὶ κατὰ τὸ αὐτὸ αἰσθήσει καὶ ὡσαύτως καὶ ἐν τῷ αὐτῷ χρόνῳ, ὥστε τοῦτʼ ἂν εἴη ἀληθές.  ἀλλʼ ἴσως διὰ τοῦτʼ ἀνάγκη λέγειν τοῖς μὴ διʼ ἀπορίαν ἀλλὰ λόγου χάριν λέγουσιν, ὅτι οὐκ ἔστιν ἀληθὲς τοῦτο ἀλλὰ τούτῳ ἀληθές. καὶ ὥσπερ δὴ πρότερον εἴρηται, ἀνάγκη πρός τι ποιεῖν ἅπαντα καὶ πρὸς δόξαν καὶ αἴσθησιν, ὥστʼ οὔτε γέγονεν οὔτʼ ἔσται οὐθὲν μηθενὸς προδοξάσαντος. εἰ δὲ γέγονεν ἢ ἔσται, δῆλον ὅτι οὐκ ἂν εἴη ἅπαντα πρὸς δόξαν.","kind":"passage"},{"id":"RAW_DAZY_BOO4:P0054","text":"ἔτι εἰ ἕν, πρὸς ἓν ἢ πρὸς ὡρισμένον· καὶ εἰ τὸ αὐτὸ καὶ ἥμισυ καὶ ἴσον, ἀλλʼ οὐ πρὸς τὸ διπλάσιόν γε τὸ ἴσον. πρὸς δὴ τὸ δοξάζον εἰ ταὐτὸ ἄνθρωπος καὶ τὸ δοξαζόμενον, οὐκ ἔσται ἄνθρωπος τὸ δοξάζον ἀλλὰ τὸ δοξαζόμενον. εἰ δʼ ἕκαστον ἔσται πρὸς τὸ δοξάζον, πρὸς ἄπειρα ἔσται τῷ εἴδει τὸ δοξάζον. ὅτι μὲν οὖν βεβαιοτάτη δόξα πασῶν τὸ μὴ εἶναι ἀληθεῖς ἅμα τὰς ἀντικειμένας φάσεις, καὶ τί συμβαίνει τοῖς οὕτω λέγουσι, καὶ διὰ τί οὕτω λέγουσι, τοσαῦτα εἰρήσθω· ἐπεὶ δʼ ἀδύνατον τὴν ἀντίφασιν ἅμα ἀληθεύεσθαι κατὰ τοῦ αὐτοῦ, φανερὸν ὅτι οὐδὲ τἀναντία ἅμα ὑπάρχειν ἐνδέχεται τῷ αὐτῷ· τῶν μὲν γὰρ ἐναντίων θάτερον στέρησίς ἐστιν οὐχ ἧττον, οὐσίας δὲ στέρησις· ἡ δὲ στέρησις ἀπόφασίς ἐστιν ἀπό τινος ὡρισμένου γένους· εἰ οὖν ἀδύνατον ἅμα καταφάναι καὶ ἀποφάναι ἀληθῶς, ἀδύνατον καὶ τἀναντία ὑπάρχειν ἅμα, ἀλλʼ ἢ πῇ ἄμφω ἢ θάτερον μὲν πῇ θάτερον δὲ ἁπλῶς.","kind":"passage"},{"id":"RAW_DAZY_BOO4:P0055","text":"ἀλλὰ μὴν οὐδὲ μεταξὺ ἀντιφάσεως ἐνδέχεται εἶναι οὐθέν, ἀλλʼ ἀνάγκη ἢ φάναι ἢ ἀποφάναι ἓν καθʼ ἑνὸς ὁτιοῦν. δῆλον δὲ πρῶτον μὲν ὁρισαμένοις τί τὸ ἀληθὲς καὶ ψεῦδος. τὸ μὲν γὰρ λέγειν τὸ ὂν μὴ εἶναι ἢ τὸ μὴ ὂν εἶναι ψεῦδος, τὸ δὲ τὸ ὂν εἶναι καὶ τὸ μὴ ὂν μὴ εἶναι ἀληθές, ὥστε καὶ ὁ λέγων εἶναι ἢ μὴ ἀληθεύσει ἢ ψεύσεται· ἀλλʼ οὔτε τὸ ὂν λέγεται μὴ εἶναι ἢ εἶναι οὔτε τὸ μὴ ὄν. ἔτι ἤτοι μεταξὺ ἔσται τῆς ἀντιφάσεως ὥσπερ τὸ φαιὸν μέλανος καὶ λευκοῦ, ἢ ὡς τὸ μηδέτερον ἀνθρώπου καὶ ἵππου. εἰ μὲν οὖν οὕτως, οὐκ ἂν μεταβάλλοι (ἐκ μὴ ἀγαθοῦ γὰρ εἰς ἀγαθὸν μεταβάλλει ἢ ἐκ τούτου εἰς μὴ ἀγαθόν), νῦν δʼ ἀεὶ φαίνεται (οὐ γὰρ ἔστι μεταβολὴ ἀλλʼ ἢ εἰς τὰ ἀντικείμενα καὶ μεταξύ)· εἰ δʼ ἔστι μεταξύ, καὶ οὕτως εἴη ἄν τις εἰς λευκὸν οὐκ ἐκ μὴ λευκοῦ γένεσις, νῦν δʼ οὐχ ὁρᾶται.","kind":"passage"},{"id":"RAW_DAZY_BOO4:P0056","text":"ἔτι πᾶν τὸ διανοητὸν καὶ νοητὸν ἡ διάνοια ἢ κατάφησιν ἢ ἀπόφησιν—τοῦτο δʼ ἐξ ὁρισμοῦ δῆλον—ὅταν ἀληθεύῃ ἢ ψεύδηται· ὅταν μὲν ὡδὶ συνθῇ φᾶσα ἢ ἀποφᾶσα, ἀληθεύει, ὅταν δὲ ὡδί, ψεύδεται. ἔτι παρὰ πάσας δεῖ εἶναι τὰς ἀντιφάσεις, εἰ μὴ λόγου ἕνεκα λέγεται· ὥστε καὶ οὔτε ἀληθεύσει τις οὔτʼ οὐκ ἀληθεύσει, καὶ παρὰ τὸ ὂν καὶ τὸ μὴ ὂν ἔσται, ὥστε καὶ παρὰ γένεσιν καὶ φθορὰν μεταβολή τις ἔσται. ἔτι ἐν ὅσοις γένεσιν ἡ ἀπόφασις τὸ ἐναντίον ἐπιφέρει, καὶ ἐν τούτοις ἔσται, οἷον ἐν ἀριθμοῖς οὔτε περιττὸς οὔτε οὐ περιττὸς ἀριθμός· ἀλλʼ ἀδύνατον· ἐκ τοῦ ὁρισμοῦ δὲ δῆλον. ἔτι εἰς ἄπειρον βαδιεῖται, καὶ οὐ μόνον ἡμιόλια τὰ ὄντα ἔσται ἀλλὰ πλείω. πάλιν γὰρ ἔσται ἀποφῆσαι τοῦτο πρὸς τὴν φάσιν καὶ τὴν ἀπόφασιν, καὶ τοῦτʼ ἔσται τι· ἡ γὰρ οὐσία ἐστί τις αὐτοῦ ἄλλη. ἔτι ὅταν ἐρομένου εἰ λευκόν ἐστιν εἴπῃ ὅτι οὔ, οὐθὲν ἄλλο ἀποπέφηκεν ἢ τὸ εἶναι· ἀπόφασις δὲ τὸ μὴ εἶναι.","kind":"passage"},{"id":"RAW_DAZY_BOO4:P0057","text":"ἐλήλυθε δʼ ἐνίοις αὕτη ἡ δόξα ὥσπερ καὶ ἄλλαι τῶν παραδόξων· ὅταν γὰρ λύειν μὴ δύνωνται λόγους ἐριστικούς, ἐνδόντες τῷ λόγῳ σύμφασιν ἀληθὲς εἶναι τὸ συλλογισθέν. οἱ μὲν οὖν διὰ τοιαύτην αἰτίαν λέγουσιν, οἱ δὲ διὰ τὸ πάντων ζητεῖν λόγον. ἀρχὴ δὲ πρὸς ἅπαντας τούτους ἐξ ὁρισμοῦ. ὁρισμὸς δὲ γίγνεται ἐκ τοῦ σημαίνειν τι ἀναγκαῖον εἶναι αὐτούς· ὁ γὰρ λόγος οὗ τὸ ὄνομα σημεῖον ὁρισμὸς ἔσται. ἔοικε δʼ ὁ μὲν Ἡρακλείτου λόγος, λέγων πάντα εἶναι καὶ μὴ εἶναι, ἅπαντα ἀληθῆ ποιεῖν, ὁ δʼ Ἀναξαγόρου, εἶναί τι μεταξὺ τῆς ἀντιφάσεως, πάντα ψευδῆ· ὅταν γὰρ μιχθῇ, οὔτε ἀγαθὸν οὔτε οὐκ ἀγαθὸν τὸ μῖγμα, ὥστʼ οὐδὲν εἰπεῖν ἀληθές.","kind":"passage"},{"id":"RAW_DAZY_BOO4:P0058","text":"διωρισμένων δὲ τούτων φανερὸν ὅτι καὶ τὰ μοναχῶς λεγόμενα καὶ κατὰ πάντων ἀδύνατον ὑπάρχειν ὥσπερ τινὲς λέγουσιν, οἱ μὲν οὐθὲν φάσκοντες ἀληθὲς εἶναι (οὐθὲν γὰρ κωλύειν φασὶν οὕτως ἅπαντα εἶναι ὥσπερ τὸ τὴν διάμετρον σύμμετρον εἶναι), οἱ δὲ πάντʼ ἀληθῆ. σχεδὸν γὰρ οὗτοι οἱ λόγοι οἱ αὐτοὶ τῷ Ἡρακλείτου· ὁ γὰρ λέγων ὅτι πάντʼ ἀληθῆ καὶ πάντα ψευδῆ, καὶ χωρὶς λέγει τῶν λόγων ἑκάτερον τούτων,  ὥστʼ εἴπερ ἀδύνατα ἐκεῖνα, καὶ ταῦτα ἀδύνατον εἶναι. ἔτι δὲ φανερῶς ἀντιφάσεις εἰσὶν ἃς οὐχ οἷόν τε ἅμα ἀληθεῖς εἶναι—οὐδὲ δὴ ψευδεῖς πάσας· καίτοι δόξειέ γʼ ἂν μᾶλλον ἐνδέχεσθαι ἐκ τῶν εἰρημένων. ἀλλὰ πρὸς πάντας τοὺς τοιούτους λόγους αἰτεῖσθαι δεῖ, καθάπερ ἐλέχθη καὶ ἐν τοῖς ἐπάνω λόγοις, οὐχὶ εἶναί τι ἢ μὴ εἶναι ἀλλὰ σημαίνειν τι, ὥστε ἐξ ὁρισμοῦ διαλεκτέον λαβόντας τί σημαίνει τὸ ψεῦδος ἢ τὸ ἀληθές.","kind":"passage"},{"id":"RAW_DAZY_BOO4:P0059","text":"εἰ δὲ μηθὲν ἄλλο τὸ ἀληθὲς φάναι ἢ ὃ ἀποφάναι ψεῦδός ἐστιν, ἀδύνατον  πάντα ψευδῆ εἶναι· ἀνάγκη γὰρ τῆς ἀντιφάσεως θάτερον εἶναι μόριον ἀληθές. ἔτι εἰ πᾶν ἢ φάναι ἢ ἀποφάναι ἀναγκαῖον, ἀδύνατον ἀμφότερα ψευδῆ εἶναι· θάτερον γὰρ μόριον τῆς ἀντιφάσεως ψεῦδός ἐστιν. συμβαίνει δὴ καὶ τὸ θρυλούμενον πᾶσι τοῖς τοιούτοις λόγοις, αὐτοὺς ἑαυτοὺς ἀναιρεῖν. ὁ μὲν γὰρ πάντα ἀληθῆ λέγων καὶ τὸν ἐναντίον αὑτοῦ λόγον ἀληθῆ ποιεῖ, ὥστε τὸν ἑαυτοῦ οὐκ ἀληθῆ (ὁ γὰρ ἐναντίος οὔ φησιν αὐτὸν ἀληθῆ), ὁ δὲ πάντα ψευδῆ καὶ αὐτὸς αὑτόν. ἐὰν δʼ ἐξαιρῶνται ὁ μὲν τὸν ἐναντίον ὡς οὐκ ἀληθὴς μόνος ἐστίν, ὁ δὲ τὸν αὑτοῦ ὡς οὐ ψευδής, οὐδὲν ἧττον ἀπείρους συμβαίνει αὐτοῖς αἰτεῖσθαι λόγους ἀληθεῖς καὶ ψευδεῖς· ὁ γὰρ λέγων τὸν ἀληθῆ λόγον ἀληθῆ ἀληθής, τοῦτο δʼ εἰς ἄπειρον βαδιεῖται.—φανερὸν δʼ ὅτι οὐδʼ οἱ πάντα ἠρεμεῖν λέγοντες ἀληθῆ λέγουσιν οὐδʼ οἱ πάντα κινεῖσθαι.","kind":"passage"},{"id":"RAW_DAZY_BOO4:P0060","text":"εἰ μὲν γὰρ ἠρεμεῖ πάντα, ἀεὶ ταὐτὰ ἀληθῆ καὶ ψευδῆ ἔσται, φαίνεται δὲ τοῦτο μεταβάλλον (ὁ γὰρ λέγων ποτὲ αὐτὸς οὐκ ἦν καὶ πάλιν οὐκ ἔσται)· εἰ δὲ πάντα κινεῖται, οὐθὲν ἔσται ἀληθές· πάντα ἄρα ψευδῆ· ἀλλὰ δέδεικται ὅτι ἀδύνατον. ἔτι ἀνάγκη τὸ ὂν μεταβάλλειν· ἔκ τινος γὰρ εἴς τι ἡ μεταβολή. ἀλλὰ μὴν οὐδὲ πάντα ἠρεμεῖ ἢ κινεῖται ποτέ, ἀεὶ δʼ οὐθέν· ἔστι γάρ τι ὃ ἀεὶ κινεῖ τὰ κινούμενα, καὶ τὸ πρῶτον κινοῦν ἀκίνητον αὐτό.","kind":"passage"}],"sha256":"2e8f01087b76cfe7c14308047e1b831d489f93c4142bc878b4e69221219cfee1","language_code":"grc","language_name":"Ἑλληνική","translations":[{"id":"SOURCE_TR_20261015T172518842637Z_46bbde8e","language_code":"en","language_name":"English","title":"Book 4","source_sha256":"2e8f01087b76cfe7c14308047e1b831d489f93c4142bc878b4e69221219cfee1","sha256":"0fb0dc514cc49d5b3298d63566df41e5086d6dad3f78a4db4a9c02a61cc1c5d7","rendition_type":"machine","review_status":"machine","model":"gpt-5.6-terra","created":"2026-10-15T17:25:18.842655+00:00","paragraphs":[{"id":"RAW_DAZY_BOO4:P0001","text":"There is a certain science which studies being qua being, and the attributes belonging to it in itself. This is the same as none of the sciences said to be particular; for none of the others investigates universally concerning being qua being, but, cutting off some part of it, studies the attribute belonging to this, as do the mathematical sciences. And since we seek the principles and the highest causes, it is clear that these must necessarily be causes of some nature in itself. If, then, those who seek the elements of beings were also seeking these principles, the elements of being too must necessarily be not accidentally but qua being; hence we too must grasp the first causes of being qua being.","kind":"passage","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_DAZY_BOO4:P0001"]},{"id":"RAW_DAZY_BOO4:P0002","text":"But being is said in many ways, yet in relation to one thing and one certain nature, and not homonymously, but as everything healthy is in relation to health: one thing by preserving it, another by producing it, another by being a sign of health, and another because it is receptive of it; and as what is medical is in relation to medicine (for one thing is called medical by possessing medicine, another by being naturally suited to it, and another by being a work of medicine); and in like manner we shall find other things spoken of similarly to these. Thus too being is said in many ways, but all in relation to one principle: some things are called beings because they are substances, others because they are affections of substance, others because they are a way toward substance, or destructions or privations or qualities or productive or generative of substance, or of things spoken of in relation to substance, or negations of some one of these or of substance; hence we say that not-being is not-being. Therefore, just as there is one science of all healthy things, similarly this is so in the other cases as well.","kind":"passage","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_DAZY_BOO4:P0002"]},{"id":"RAW_DAZY_BOO4:P0003","text":"For it belongs to one science to study not only things said according to one thing, but also things said in relation to one nature; for these too are in a way said according to one thing. It is clear, then, that it belongs to one science also to study beings qua beings. And everywhere the science is principally of the first thing, that upon which the others depend and through which they are named. If, then, this is substance, the philosopher must possess the principles and causes of substances.—But of every genus there is one sense and one science, as, for example, grammar, being one, studies all spoken sounds; hence it belongs to one science generically to study however many species there are of being qua being, and the species of the species.","kind":"passage","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_DAZY_BOO4:P0003"]},{"id":"RAW_DAZY_BOO4:P0004","text":"If, then, being and one are the same and one nature, by following one another as principle and cause do, but not as being made clear by one account (and it makes no difference even if we suppose them similarly, but this rather serves our purpose): for one man and man are the same, and being-a-man and man; nor does the repetition in speech, “one man” and “one being a man,” signify anything different (and it is clear that they are not separated either in coming-to-be or in perishing); and similarly in the case of one: so that it is clear that the addition signifies the same thing in these cases, and that one is nothing different from being. Further, the substance of each thing is one not accidentally, and similarly is some being.—Thus, however many are the species of one, so many also are those of being; and it belongs to the same science generically to study what each of them is—I mean, for example, sameness and likeness and the other such things. Nearly all contraries are reduced to this principle; let these matters have been considered by us in the selection of contraries.","kind":"passage","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_DAZY_BOO4:P0004"]},{"id":"RAW_DAZY_BOO4:P0005","text":"And there are as many parts of philosophy as there are substances; thus there must necessarily be some first and some succeeding science among them. For being at once has genera; hence the sciences too will follow these. For the philosopher is as the person called a mathematician; for mathematics too has parts, and there is some first and second science, and others in succession among the mathematical disciplines.—And since it belongs to one science to study opposites, while multitude is opposed to one—and it belongs to one science to study negation and privation, because in both cases the one of which there is negation or privation is studied (for either we say simply that that thing is not present, or that it is not present in some genus; in the former case †there is an added difference in relation to the one beyond that in the negation†, for negation is an absence of that thing, whereas in privation there comes to be also some underlying nature of which the privation is said)—thus it belongs to the","kind":"passage","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_DAZY_BOO4:P0005"]},{"id":"RAW_DAZY_BOO4:P0006","text":"science mentioned to know also the opposites of those spoken of: other, unlike, unequal, and however many other things are said either according to these or according to multitude and one. Contrariety too is among these; for contrariety is a kind of difference, and difference is otherness. Therefore, since one is said in many ways, these too will be said in many ways, but nevertheless it belongs to one science to know them all; for it is not if they are said in many ways that they belong to different sciences, but if their accounts are referred neither to one thing nor in relation to one thing. But since all things are referred to the first, as all things called one are referred to the first one, the same must be said concerning sameness and otherness and contraries; therefore, after distinguishing in how many ways each thing is said, one must state, in each category, how it is said in relation to the first: for some things will be said by possessing it, others by producing it, and others in such other ways.—It is clear, then, that it belongs to one science to give an account of these things and of substance (and this was one of the problems), and that it belongs to the philosopher to be able to study all things.","kind":"passage","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_DAZY_BOO4:P0006"]},{"id":"RAW_DAZY_BOO4:P0007","text":"For if it does not belong to the philosopher, who will examine whether Socrates and seated Socrates are the same, or whether one is opposed to one, or what the contrary is or in how many ways it is said? Similarly also concerning the other such things. Since, then, these are attributes belonging in themselves to one qua one and to being qua being, but not qua numbers or lines or fire, it is clear that it belongs to that science both to know what they are and the attributes belonging to them. And those who investigate them err not in this, that they are not philosophizing, but because substance is prior, concerning which they understand nothing. For just as number qua number has its proper attributes, such as oddness, evenness, commensurability, equality, excess, deficiency—and these belong to numbers both in themselves and in relation to one another (and similarly solid, motionless, moving, weightless, and weight-bearing things have other proper attributes)—so being qua being also has certain proper attributes, and these are the things concerning which it belongs to the philosopher to investigate the truth.","kind":"passage","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_DAZY_BOO4:P0007"]},{"id":"RAW_DAZY_BOO4:P0008","text":"A sign of this is that dialecticians and sophists assume the same guise as the philosopher; for sophistry is wisdom only in appearance, and dialecticians argue about all things, while being is common to all things; and they evidently argue about these matters because they belong properly to philosophy. For sophistry and dialectic turn upon the same genus as philosophy, but they differ from philosophy, the one in the mode of its capacity, the other in the choice of its life; dialectic is tentative concerning those things of which philosophy is cognizant, while sophistry is apparent, but is not so.","kind":"passage","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_DAZY_BOO4:P0008"]},{"id":"RAW_DAZY_BOO4:P0009","text":"Further, one of the two coordinated series of contraries is privation, and all things are reduced to being and not-being, and to one and multitude—for example, rest belongs to one and motion to multitude. And almost all agree that beings and substance are composed from contraries; at any rate, all call the principles contrary: some odd and even, others hot and cold, others limit and unlimited, others friendship and strife. And all the rest too appear to be reduced to one and multitude (let this reduction be granted us), and the principles, even those altogether given by others, fall into these as into genera. It is clear, then, from these considerations too, that it belongs to one science to study being qua being. For all things are either contraries or from contraries, and the principles of contraries are one and multitude. These belong to one science, whether they are said according to one thing or not, as perhaps the truth is also.","kind":"passage","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_DAZY_BOO4:P0009"]},{"id":"RAW_DAZY_BOO4:P0010","text":"Yet even if one is said in many ways, the other things will be said in relation to the first, and likewise the contraries; and even if being or one is not universal and the same over all things, or separate—as perhaps it is not—but some things are in relation to one and others by succession. And for this reason it does not belong to the geometer to study what contrary or complete or one or being or same or other is, except from a hypothesis. That it belongs to one science to study being qua being and the things belonging to it qua being is clear, and that this science studies not only substances but also the attributes belonging to them, both those mentioned and those concerning prior and posterior, genus and species, whole and part, and the other such things.","kind":"passage","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_DAZY_BOO4:P0010"]},{"id":"RAW_DAZY_BOO4:P0011","text":"It must be said whether the study both of the things called axioms in the mathematical disciplines and of substance belongs to one science or to another. It is clear, then, that the inquiry concerning these belongs to one science, namely that of the philosopher; for they belong to all beings, but not separately to some one genus distinct from the others. And all employ them, because they belong to being qua being, and each genus is a being; but they employ them only insofar as is sufficient for them, that is, as far as extends the genus concerning which they bring their demonstrations. Therefore, since it is clear that they belong to all things qua beings (for this is what they have in common), the study of these too belongs to the person who knows being qua being. Hence none of those who investigate particular fields attempts to say anything about them, whether they are true or not—neither geometer nor arithmetician—but some of the natural philosophers, reasonably doing this; for they alone thought that they were investigating both the whole of nature and being.","kind":"passage","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_DAZY_BOO4:P0011"]},{"id":"RAW_DAZY_BOO4:P0012","text":"But since there is some one higher than the natural philosopher (for nature is one genus of being), the inquiry concerning these too will belong to the student of the universal and of primary substance. Natural science too is a kind of wisdom, but not the first. And whatever some of those who discourse concerning truth undertake about how it ought to be accepted, they do this through lack of training in analytics; for they ought to come to these matters knowing them beforehand, rather than seek them while listening.—That it belongs to the philosopher, and to the one who studies every substance in accordance with its nature, to investigate the principles of syllogistic reasoning too, is clear. It is fitting that the person who knows each genus most fully should be able to state the most certain principles of the object in question; hence the one who knows beings qua beings should state the most certain principles of all things. And this person is the philosopher.","kind":"passage","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_DAZY_BOO4:P0012"]},{"id":"RAW_DAZY_BOO4:P0013","text":"The most certain principle of all is that concerning which it is impossible to be mistaken; for such a principle must necessarily be the most knowable (since all are deceived concerning things they do not know), and unhypothetical. For that which anyone who understands anything whatsoever among beings must possess is not a hypothesis; and that which it is necessary for anyone who knows anything whatsoever to know, he must necessarily possess upon arrival. That such a principle is the most certain of all is clear; what it is, let us now state. For it is impossible for the same thing to belong and not to belong at the same time to the same thing and in the same respect (and let whatever further qualifications we might add be taken as specified against logical difficulties). This, then, is the most certain of all principles, for it has the qualification stated. For it is impossible for anyone to suppose that the same thing is and is not, as some think Heraclitus says.","kind":"passage","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_DAZY_BOO4:P0013"]},{"id":"RAW_DAZY_BOO4:P0014","text":"For it is not necessary that the things someone says are also the things he supposes. But if contraries cannot belong at the same time to the same thing (and let the customary qualifications be specified by us also for this proposition), and the opinion of contradiction is contrary to an opinion, it is clear that it is impossible for the same person to suppose at the same time that the same thing is and is not; for one who was deceived on this point would at the same time hold contrary opinions. Hence all those who demonstrate reduce things to this as the ultimate opinion; for by nature this is the principle of all the other axioms as well.","kind":"passage","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_DAZY_BOO4:P0014"]},{"id":"RAW_DAZY_BOO4:P0015","text":"There are some who, as we said, themselves declare that the same thing can be and not be, and that one may suppose it so. Many of those concerned with nature employ this argument too. But we have now assumed it to be impossible for a thing to be and not be at the same time, and through this have shown that this is the most certain of all principles. Some demand that even this be demonstrated, through lack of training; for it is lack of training not to know of what things one must seek a demonstration and of what one must not. For in general it is impossible for there to be a demonstration of everything (for one would proceed to infinity, so that there would not be demonstration even in this way); but if of some things one should not seek a demonstration, they would not be able to say what principle they deem more fitting to be such a one. Yet it is possible to demonstrate even concerning this, by refutation, that it is impossible, if only the disputant says something. But if he says nothing, it is ridiculous to seek an account against one who has no account of anything, insofar as he has none; for such a person, insofar as he is such, is already like a plant.","kind":"passage","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_DAZY_BOO4:P0015"]},{"id":"RAW_DAZY_BOO4:P0016","text":"By demonstrating by refutation I mean something different from demonstrating, because one who demonstrates might seem to beg the question; but if another person is the cause of such a thing, it would be refutation and not demonstration. The starting point for all such cases is not to demand that he say that something is or is not (for this perhaps one might suppose to be begging the question from the outset), but that he signify something both to himself and to another; for this is necessary, if indeed he says anything. For if not, there would be no discourse for such a person, neither with himself nor with another. But if someone grants this, there will be demonstration; for something will already be determined. Yet the cause is not the demonstrator but the person who submits; for in destroying discourse, he submits to discourse. Further, he who has conceded this has conceded that something is true without demonstration.—First, then, it is clear that this very thing is true: that the name signifies being or not being this particular thing, so that not everything can be so and not so. Further, if “man” signifies one thing, let this be “two-footed animal.”","kind":"passage","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_DAZY_BOO4:P0016"]},{"id":"RAW_DAZY_BOO4:P0017","text":"And by signifying one thing I mean this: if this is man, then, if something is a man, this will be what it is to be a man. (It makes no difference if someone says that it signifies several things, provided only that they are determinate; for a different name could be assigned to each account. I mean, for example, if he were to say that “man” does not signify one thing but many, one of which has the one account “two-footed animal,” while there are also several other accounts, determinate in number; for a proper name could be assigned to each account. But if he were to say that it signifies infinitely many things, it is clear that there could be no discourse; for not to signify one thing is to signify nothing, and if names signify nothing, discourse with one another is abolished, and in truth discourse with oneself as well; for it is impossible to think anything without thinking one thing, but if it is possible, one name could be assigned to this thing.)—Let the name, then, as was said at the beginning, signify something and signify one thing. It is not possible, then, for being a man to signify the same as not being a man, if “man”","kind":"passage","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_DAZY_BOO4:P0017"]},{"id":"RAW_DAZY_BOO4:P0018","text":"signifies not only of one thing but also one thing (for by signifying one thing we do not mean signifying of one thing, since in that case “musical,” “white,” and “man” too would signify one thing, so that all things would be one; for they would be synonymous). And being and not being will not be the same except homonymously, as if, for example, those whom we call men others were to call not-men. But the problem is not whether it is possible for the same name to be man and not-man at the same time, but whether the thing is so. But if “man” and “not-man” do not signify different things, it is clear that not being a man is also not different from being a man; hence being a man will be being a not-man, for they will be one. For this is what it means to be one: as “garment” and “cloak” are one, if their account is one. But if they are one, being a man and being a not-man will signify one thing. Yet it has been demonstrated that they signify different things.","kind":"passage","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_DAZY_BOO4:P0018"]},{"id":"RAW_DAZY_BOO4:P0019","text":"It is necessary, then, if it is true to say that a man is a two-footed animal (for this was what “man” signified), that he be a two-footed animal; and if this is necessary, it is impossible for the same thing not then to be a two-footed animal (for this is what “being necessary” signifies: being impossible not to be); therefore it is not possible for it to be true at the same time to say that the same thing is a man and is not a man. The same account applies also to not being a man; for being for a man and not being for a man signify something different, if indeed being white and being a man also signify something different; for the latter is much more opposed, so that it signifies something different. But if he will say that “white” too signifies the same thing and one thing, we shall again say the same as was said before, namely that everything will be one, and not only the opposites. But if this is not possible, the stated result follows, if he answers what is asked. But if, when the questioner asks simply, he adds the negations as well, he is not answering what is asked.","kind":"passage","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_DAZY_BOO4:P0019"]},{"id":"RAW_DAZY_BOO4:P0020","text":"For nothing prevents the same thing from being both a man and white and innumerable other things in multitude; but nevertheless, when one asks whether it is true to say that this is a man or not, one must answer what signifies one thing, and not add that it is also white and large. For it is impossible to go through the attributes, being infinite. Therefore let him either go through them all or none. Likewise, then, even if the same thing is a man and not a man ten thousand times over, one must not add in answering the question whether it is a man that it is at the same time also not a man, unless one must also add in answer all the other things that have happened to belong to it, both what is and what is not; but if he does this, he is not engaging in discussion.—In general, those who say this do away with substance and the what-it-was-to-be. For they are forced to say that everything belongs accidentally, and that there is no such thing as what it is to be for a man or what it is to be for an animal. For if there will be something that is what it is to be for a man, this will not be what it is to be not for a man or what it is to be not a man (though these are negations of it); for what it signified was one thing, and this was the substance of something.","kind":"passage","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_DAZY_BOO4:P0020"]},{"id":"RAW_DAZY_BOO4:P0021","text":"And to signify substance is to signify that its being is not something else. But if what it is to be for a man, or what it is to be not for a man, or what it is to be not a man, will be its being, it will be something else; so that they are forced to say that there will be no account of this sort for anything, but that everything is accidental. For by this substance and the accidental are distinguished: white belongs accidentally to a man because he is white, but not what white is. But if everything is said accidentally, there will be no primary subject, if an accident always signifies its predication of some subject. Thus one must proceed to infinity. But this is impossible; for no more than two things are combined. For an accident is not an accident of an accident, except because both belong accidentally to the same thing—I mean, for example, white is musical and this is white because both belong accidentally to man. But Socrates is not musical in this way, because both belong accidentally to some other thing.","kind":"passage","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_DAZY_BOO4:P0021"]},{"id":"RAW_DAZY_BOO4:P0022","text":"Since, then, some things are said to belong accidentally in this way and others in that way, those which are said in this way, as white belongs to Socrates, cannot be infinite upwards—for example, some other thing belonging accidentally to white Socrates; for no one thing comes to be out of all of them. Nor, indeed, will some other thing belong accidentally to white, for example musical; for this no more belongs accidentally to that than that to this; and at the same time it has been distinguished that some things belong accidentally in this way, others as musical belongs to Socrates. Those which belong in this way do not belong accidentally to an accident, but those which belong in that way do; hence not everything will be said accidentally. There will therefore be something that signifies substance. But if this is so, it has been shown that it is impossible for contradictions to be predicated at the same time.—Again, if all contradictions are true at the same time of the same thing, it is clear that everything will be one. For the same thing will be both a trireme and a wall and a man, if it is possible either to affirm or deny something of everything, as must be the case for those who state the doctrine of Protagoras.","kind":"passage","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_DAZY_BOO4:P0022"]},{"id":"RAW_DAZY_BOO4:P0023","text":"For if it seems to someone that a man is not a trireme, clearly he is not a trireme; and so he is also one, if the contradiction is true. And thus Anaxagoras’ doctrine comes about: all things together; so that nothing belongs truly. They seem, then, to speak of the indeterminate, and, thinking that they are speaking of what is, they speak about what is not; for what is potentially and not actually is indeterminate. But they must say of everything either the affirmation or the negation. For it is absurd if, while each thing’s own negation belongs to it, the negation of another thing which does not belong to it will not belong to it. I mean, for example, if it is true to say of the man that he is not a man, clearly he is also either a trireme or not a trireme. If it is the affirmation, the negation must also belong; but if the affirmation does not belong, its negation will belong more than his own.","kind":"passage","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_DAZY_BOO4:P0023"]},{"id":"RAW_DAZY_BOO4:P0024","text":"If, then, that too belongs, the negation of trireme will also belong; and if this does, the affirmation too.—These consequences, then, attend those who state this doctrine, and also that it is not necessary either to affirm or to deny. For if it is true that a thing is a man and not a man, clearly it will be neither a man nor not a man; for there are two negations corresponding to the two, but if the one compounded from both is one, this too would be one opposite.—Again, either things stand in this way concerning everything, and it is both white and not white, and being and not being, and similarly concerning the other affirmations and negations; or they do not, but concerning some things they do and concerning others they do not. If not concerning all, these would be agreed upon; but if concerning all, then again either, of as many things as one can affirm and deny, one can also deny and affirm, or, of as many things as one can affirm and deny, one cannot affirm all those of which one can deny.","kind":"passage","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_DAZY_BOO4:P0024"]},{"id":"RAW_DAZY_BOO4:P0025","text":"And if it is thus, there would be something stably not being, and this would be a firm belief; and if not-being is something firm and knowable, the opposing affirmation would be more knowable. But if it is similarly possible to affirm all that one can deny, it is necessary either that one speak truly while separating—for example, that it is white and again that it is not white—or not. And if it is not true to speak while separating, he neither says these things nor is anything; for how could things that are not speak or walk? And all things would be one, as was said before, and man and god and trireme and their contradictions would be the same (for if the same applies to each, nothing will differ from anything else; but if it differs, this will be true and peculiar). Likewise, if it is possible to speak truly while separating, the stated result follows, and in addition everyone would speak truly and everyone would speak falsely, and he himself admits that he is speaking falsely. At the same time it is clear that the inquiry with such a person is about nothing; for he says nothing.","kind":"passage","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_DAZY_BOO4:P0025"]},{"id":"RAW_DAZY_BOO4:P0026","text":"For he says neither thus nor not thus, but both thus and not thus; and again he denies both of these, that it is neither thus nor not thus. For otherwise something would already be determinate.—Again, if, whenever the affirmation is true, the negation is false, and whenever this is true, the affirmation is false, it would not be possible truly to affirm and deny the same thing at the same time. But perhaps they might say that this is what was laid down from the beginning.—Again, is the person who supposes either that things are somehow or that they are not somehow mistaken, while the person who supposes both speaks truly? For if he speaks truly, what could be meant by the assertion that the nature of beings is such? But if he does not speak truly, but speaks more truly than the person who supposes in that way, beings would already be somehow disposed, and this would be true, and not at the same time also not true. But if everyone alike both speaks falsely and speaks truth, such a person will be unable either to utter a sound or to say anything; for he says these things and not these things at the same time.","kind":"passage","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_DAZY_BOO4:P0026"]},{"id":"RAW_DAZY_BOO4:P0027","text":"But if he supposes nothing, but alike thinks and does not think, how would he differ at all from plants? Hence it is especially clear that no one, either of the others or of those who state this doctrine, is so disposed. For why does he walk to Megara rather than stay still, thinking that he ought to walk? Nor does he go straightway at dawn into a well or a ravine, if he happens upon one; rather, he plainly takes care, as though he did not equally suppose falling in to be not good and good. Clearly, then, he supposes the one to be better and the other not better. But if this is so, he must also suppose the one to be man and the other not man, and the one sweet and the other not sweet. For he does not seek and suppose all things equally, when, thinking it better to drink water and to see a man, he then seeks these things; though he ought to do so, if man and not-man were alike the same.","kind":"passage","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_DAZY_BOO4:P0027"]},{"id":"RAW_DAZY_BOO4:P0028","text":"But, as was said, there is no one who does not plainly take care about some things and not about others. Hence, as it seems, all suppose things to be simply so, if not concerning everything, at least concerning the better and the worse. But if they do not know but opine, they ought much more to take care for truth, just as one who is sick rather than healthy ought to take care for health; for one who opines is not soundly disposed toward truth in comparison with one who knows.—Again, even if everything is as much thus as not thus, nevertheless the more and the less are present in the nature of beings. For we would not say that two is even and that three is even in the same degree, nor is the person who thinks four to be five mistaken to the same degree as the person who thinks it to be a thousand. If, then, they are not mistaken to the same degree, clearly one is less mistaken, and therefore speaks more truly. If, then, what is more is nearer, there would be something true to which what is more true is nearer.","kind":"passage","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_DAZY_BOO4:P0028"]},{"id":"RAW_DAZY_BOO4:P0029","text":"And even if there is not, still there is already something firmer and truer, and we would be rid of the unqualified doctrine that prevents the mind from determining anything.","kind":"passage","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_DAZY_BOO4:P0029"]},{"id":"RAW_DAZY_BOO4:P0030","text":"The doctrine of Protagoras too comes from the same opinion, and both must alike either be or not be. For if all things that seem and appear are true, everything must be at the same time true and false (for many hold contrary opinions to one another, and think that those who do not opine the same things as themselves are mistaken; so that the same thing must both be and not be); and if this is so, all things that seem must be true (for those who are mistaken and those who speak truly hold contrary opinions to one another; if, then, beings are so disposed, all will speak truly). That both doctrines arise from the same thought is clear; but the manner of dealing with all persons is not the same. For some need persuasion, others force.","kind":"passage","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_DAZY_BOO4:P0030"]},{"id":"RAW_DAZY_BOO4:P0031","text":"For those who have supposed thus as a result of being perplexed, their ignorance is easily cured (for the response to them is directed not at their argument but at their thought); but those who speak for the sake of argument, their cure is refutation of the argument in their voice and in their names. This opinion has come to those who are perplexed from sensible things: the opinion that contradictions and contraries belong at the same time, when they see contraries coming to be from the same thing. If, then, what is not cannot come to be, the thing previously existed alike as both, just as Anaxagoras too says that everything is mixed in everything, and Democritus; for the latter too holds that the void and the full belong alike to every part, although one of these is being and the other not-being.","kind":"passage","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_DAZY_BOO4:P0031"]},{"id":"RAW_DAZY_BOO4:P0032","text":"To those who suppose on these grounds, then, we shall say that in one way they speak correctly, but in another way they are ignorant. For being is spoken of in two ways, so that in one way something can come to be from what is not, and in another it cannot; and the same thing can at the same time be both being and not-being, but not in the same respect. For the contraries can be the same thing at the same time potentially, but not actually. Further, we shall require them to suppose that there is some other substance among beings to which neither motion nor destruction nor coming-to-be belongs at all.—Likewise, the truth concerning appearances has come to some from sensible things.","kind":"passage","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_DAZY_BOO4:P0032"]},{"id":"RAW_DAZY_BOO4:P0033","text":"For they think it proper that truth be judged neither by multitude nor by smallness of number, while the same thing appears sweet to those tasting it and bitter to others; so that if all were sick or all mad, while two or three were healthy or had intelligence, these would seem to be sick and mad, and the others not. Further, contrary things appear to us and to many of the other animals; and even to each person himself, the same things do not always appear the same according to perception. Which of these, then, are true or false is unclear; for these are no more true than those, but equally so. Hence Democritus at least says either that nothing is true or that it is unclear to us. In general, because they suppose perception to be thought, and this to be alteration, they say that what appears according to perception is necessarily true. For from these considerations Empedocles and Democritus and, so to speak, every one of the others have become subject to such opinions.","kind":"passage","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_DAZY_BOO4:P0033"]},{"id":"RAW_DAZY_BOO4:P0034","text":"For Empedocles says that, as their condition changes, their thought changes:","kind":"passage","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_DAZY_BOO4:P0034"]},{"id":"RAW_DAZY_BOO4:P0035","text":"For understanding increases in men toward what is present.","kind":"passage","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_DAZY_BOO4:P0035"]},{"id":"RAW_DAZY_BOO4:P0036","text":"Empedocles Fr. 106. And elsewhere too he says that","kind":"passage","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_DAZY_BOO4:P0036"]},{"id":"RAW_DAZY_BOO4:P0037","text":"But insofar as they became different, so much there always","kind":"passage","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_DAZY_BOO4:P0037"]},{"id":"RAW_DAZY_BOO4:P0038","text":"presented itself to them as different in thought.","kind":"passage","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_DAZY_BOO4:P0038"]},{"id":"RAW_DAZY_BOO4:P0039","text":"Empedocles Fr. 108. And Parmenides too declares in the same manner:","kind":"passage","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_DAZY_BOO4:P0039"]},{"id":"RAW_DAZY_BOO4:P0040","text":"For as at each time he has the mixture of much-wandering limbs,","kind":"passage","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_DAZY_BOO4:P0040"]},{"id":"RAW_DAZY_BOO4:P0041","text":"so mind is present to men; for the same thing","kind":"passage","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_DAZY_BOO4:P0041"]},{"id":"RAW_DAZY_BOO4:P0042","text":"is that which thinks: the nature of limbs in men.","kind":"passage","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_DAZY_BOO4:P0042"]},{"id":"RAW_DAZY_BOO4:P0043","text":"And in all cases and in every case; for the more is thought.","kind":"passage","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_DAZY_BOO4:P0043"]},{"id":"RAW_DAZY_BOO4:P0044","text":"Parmenides Fr. 16. And an utterance of Anaxagoras is recalled to certain of his companions, that for them things that are will be such as they suppose them to be. They also say that Homer appears to hold this opinion, because he represented Hector, when he was beside himself from the blow, as lying there thinking otherwise, as though both those who are in their right mind and those who are out of their mind think, but not the same things. Clearly, then, if both are acts of thought, things that are are at the same time both thus and not thus. Hence the consequence is most difficult. For if those who have most seen the truth that is possible—these are those who most seek and love it—hold such opinions and make these pronouncements about truth, how is it not fitting that those undertaking philosophy should lose heart? For to seek truth would be to chase flying things.","kind":"passage","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_DAZY_BOO4:P0044"]},{"id":"RAW_DAZY_BOO4:P0045","text":"—The cause of this opinion for them is that they were investigating the truth concerning beings, but supposed that the only beings were sensible things; and in these there is present much of the nature of the indeterminate and of being in the way we have stated. Hence they speak plausibly, but not truly (for it is more fitting to put it this way than as Epicharmus did against Xenophanes). Further, seeing that this whole nature is in motion, and that nothing true can be asserted of what is changing, they held that concerning what changes altogether in every way it is impossible to speak truth. For from this supposition there flowered the most extreme opinion of those mentioned: that of those who profess Heracliteanism, and such as Cratylus held, who in the end thought that one ought to say nothing, but only moved his finger, and reproached Heraclitus for saying that it is not possible to step twice into the same river; for he himself thought that it was not possible even once.","kind":"passage","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_DAZY_BOO4:P0045"]},{"id":"RAW_DAZY_BOO4:P0046","text":"But against this argument too we shall say that, while what is changing, when it changes, gives them some ground for thinking that it is not, nevertheless this is disputable. For what is shedding something retains something of what is shed, and it is necessary that something of what is coming to be already be; and in general, if something is perishing, there will be something that is, and if it is coming to be, it is necessary that there be that from which it comes to be and that by which it is generated, and that this not proceed to infinity. But leaving these matters aside, let us say this: changing in quantity is not the same as changing in quality. So let it not remain in respect of quantity; but in respect of form we know all things.","kind":"passage","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_DAZY_BOO4:P0046"]},{"id":"RAW_DAZY_BOO4:P0047","text":"Further, it is fitting to rebuke those who suppose in this way, because, having seen that even among sensible things those fewer in number are so disposed, they made the same pronouncement concerning the whole heaven. For the region of the sensible world around us, being alone, continually persists in destruction and generation, but it is, so to speak, no part of the whole; so that it would have been more just for them, because of those things, to reject these than, because of these, to reject those. Further, it is clear that against these too we shall make the same statements as those made before: that one must show them, and persuade them, that there is a certain motionless nature. And yet it follows for those who say that things are and are not simultaneously that they should say that all things are at rest rather than in motion; for there is nothing into which they can change, since all things belong to all things.\n\n—Concerning truth, that not everything which appears is true: first, because even if perception is not false concerning its proper object, appearance is not the same as perception.","kind":"passage","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_DAZY_BOO4:P0047"]},{"id":"RAW_DAZY_BOO4:P0048","text":"Then it is worth wondering if they are perplexed about this: whether magnitudes are of such a size and colors such as they appear to those far away or such as they appear to those nearby; and whether they are such as they appear to the healthy or such as they appear to the sick; and whether things are heavier as they appear to the weak or as they appear to the strong; and whether things are true as they appear to those asleep or as they appear to those awake. For it is clear that they do not in fact think so. At any rate, no one, if he supposes at night that he is in Athens while being in Libya, sets out for the Odeon. Further, concerning the future, as Plato too says, surely the opinion of the physician and that of the ignorant person do not have equal authority—for example, concerning whether someone is going to be healthy or not. Further, even among the perceptions themselves, the perception of what belongs to another and of what is proper, or of what is adjacent and of its own object, does not have equal authority; rather, sight judges color, not taste, and taste judges flavor, not sight. And no one of these ever says, at the same time and concerning the same thing, that it is both thus and not thus.","kind":"passage","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_DAZY_BOO4:P0048"]},{"id":"RAW_DAZY_BOO4:P0049","text":"Nor has it disputed at another time concerning the affection itself, but concerning that to which the affection has occurred. I mean, for example, that the same wine, either because it has changed or because the body has changed, may at one time seem sweet and at another not sweet; but sweetness itself, such as it is when it is present, has never changed, but one always speaks truth about it, and what is going to be sweet is necessarily such. Yet all these arguments do away with this, just as they do away with there being the substance of anything, and thus with anything being necessary; for what is necessary cannot be otherwise and otherwise. Hence, if anything is necessary, it will not be both thus and not thus.—And in general, if only the sensible exists, there would be nothing if ensouled beings did not exist, for there would not be perception. Thus, perhaps it is true that there are neither sensible things nor perceptions—for this is an affection of the perceiver—but that the underlying things which produce perception do not exist even apart from perception is impossible.","kind":"passage","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_DAZY_BOO4:P0049"]},{"id":"RAW_DAZY_BOO4:P0050","text":"For perception itself surely is not of itself; rather, there is something else besides perception, which must be prior to perception. For what moves is by nature prior to what is moved, even if these are spoken of in relation to one another, nonetheless.","kind":"passage","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_DAZY_BOO4:P0050"]},{"id":"RAW_DAZY_BOO4:P0051","text":"There are some who raise a difficulty both among those persuaded of these things and among those who merely utter these arguments. For they ask who is to judge the healthy person, and in general who is to judge correctly concerning each matter. But difficulties of this sort are like asking whether we are now asleep or awake; and all such difficulties aim at the same thing. For these people demand an account of everything: they seek a principle, and seek to obtain it by demonstration, though it is clear from their actions that they are not persuaded. But, as we said, this is their condition: they seek an account of things for which there is no account; for the principle of demonstration is not demonstration. These people, then, could readily be persuaded of this (for it is not difficult to grasp); but those who seek only compulsion in argument seek what is impossible. For they demand to state contraries while immediately stating contraries.","kind":"passage","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_DAZY_BOO4:P0051"]},{"id":"RAW_DAZY_BOO4:P0052","text":"But if not all things are relative, and some things are also themselves in themselves, not everything that appears can be true. For what appears appears to someone. Hence whoever says that all appearances are true makes all beings relative. Therefore one must be on guard against those who seek compulsion in argument while at the same time demand that an account be rendered: that it is not simply what appears, but what appears to that to whom it appears, and when it appears, and in what respect, and how. But if they render an account, yet do not render it in this way, they will quickly find themselves saying contraries.","kind":"passage","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_DAZY_BOO4:P0052"]},{"id":"RAW_DAZY_BOO4:P0053","text":"For it is possible that the same thing should appear to sight to be honey but not to taste, and, when there are two eyes, not the same to each sight, if they are unlike. And against those who, for the reasons stated earlier, say that what appears is true, and therefore that all things are equally false and true: neither do the same things appear to everyone nor always the same things to the same person; rather, often contraries appear at the same time (for touch says there are two in the crossing of the fingers, while sight says there is one)—but not, at least, to the same perception and in the same respect and in the same way and at the same time; hence this would be true.\n\nBut perhaps for this reason it is necessary for those who speak not from perplexity but for the sake of argument to say that this is not true, but true for this person. And, as was said before, it is necessary to make all things relative and relative to opinion and perception, so that nothing has come to be or will be unless someone has previously held an opinion. But if something has come to be or will be, clearly not all things would be relative to opinion.","kind":"passage","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_DAZY_BOO4:P0053"]},{"id":"RAW_DAZY_BOO4:P0054","text":"Further, if something is one, it is relative to one thing or to something determinate; and if the same thing is both half and equal, the equal is nevertheless not relative to the double. Now if man who holds an opinion and the object of opinion are the same in relation to the opinion-holder, the opinion-holder will not be man but the object of opinion. And if each thing will be relative to the opinion-holder, the opinion-holder will be relative in form to infinitely many things. Let this much have been said to show that the most certain opinion of all is that contradictory assertions are not simultaneously true, and to show what follows for those who speak thus and why they speak thus. But since it is impossible for contradiction to be true simultaneously of the same thing, it is clear that contraries too cannot belong simultaneously to the same thing. For one of two contraries is no less a privation, and a privation is a privation of substance; and privation is a negation from a determinate genus. Therefore, if it is impossible truly to affirm and deny simultaneously, it is impossible also for contraries to belong simultaneously, except that both belong in some respect, or one in some respect and the other without qualification.","kind":"passage","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_DAZY_BOO4:P0054"]},{"id":"RAW_DAZY_BOO4:P0055","text":"But neither can there be anything intermediate between the members of a contradiction; rather, it is necessary, concerning any one thing, either to affirm or to deny one predicate. This is clear first if we define what truth and falsehood are. For to say that what is is not, or that what is not is, is false; but to say that what is is, and that what is not is not, is true. Hence one who says either that it is or that it is not will speak truly or falsely; but neither what is nor what is not is said to be or not to be. Further, either there will be something intermediate between the members of a contradiction, as gray is between black and white, or as what is neither is between man and horse. If it is so in the latter way, there could be no change (for change is from not-good to good or from this to not-good); but now it always appears otherwise, for there is no change except into opposites and intermediates. But if there is an intermediate, then in this way too there would be a coming-to-be into white not from not-white; but this is not observed.","kind":"passage","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_DAZY_BOO4:P0055"]},{"id":"RAW_DAZY_BOO4:P0056","text":"Further, thought either affirms or denies everything that is thinkable and intelligible—this is clear from the definition—whenever it speaks truly or falsely: when, in affirming or denying, it combines in this way, it speaks truly; when it does so in that way, falsely. Further, there must be an intermediate alongside every contradiction, unless this is said merely for the sake of argument; hence someone will neither speak truly nor not speak truly, and there will be something besides being and not-being, and thus there will be a change besides generation and destruction. Further, in all cases in which negation brings in the contrary as a genus, it will be so in these cases too: for example, among numbers there will be a number that is neither odd nor not odd. But this is impossible, as is clear from the definition. Further, one will proceed to infinity, and not only will beings be increased by half again, but by more. For it will again be possible to deny this in relation to the affirmation and the negation, and this will be something; for its substance is something else. Further, when, in answer to the question whether something is white, one says no, one has denied nothing other than its being; and negation is its not being.","kind":"passage","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_DAZY_BOO4:P0056"]},{"id":"RAW_DAZY_BOO4:P0057","text":"This opinion has come to some people, as have other paradoxical opinions. For when they are unable to resolve eristic arguments, yielding to the argument they accept the conclusion as true. Some, then, speak for such a reason; others because they seek an account of everything. But the starting-point against all these people is from definition. Definition arises from its being necessary for them to signify something; for the account of which the name is a sign will be a definition. Heraclitus' argument, saying that all things are and are not, seems to make all things true; but Anaxagoras' argument, that there is something intermediate between contradiction, seems to make all things false. For when the mixture is mixed, it is neither good nor not good; hence nothing true is said.","kind":"passage","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_DAZY_BOO4:P0057"]},{"id":"RAW_DAZY_BOO4:P0058","text":"Once these matters have been distinguished, it is clear that propositions stated in one way and universally cannot hold as some say: some saying that nothing is true (for they say that nothing prevents all things from being in the way in which the diagonal is commensurable), and others that all things are true. For these arguments are nearly the same as Heraclitus' argument. For the person who says that all things are true and all things are false states each of these arguments separately, so that, if those things are impossible, these too must be impossible. Further, there plainly are contradictions which cannot simultaneously be true—nor, indeed, can they all be false, although from what has been said this might seem more possible. But against all such arguments one must demand, as was said also in the arguments above, not that something be or not be, but that something be signified; so one must argue from a definition, having taken what falsehood or truth signifies.","kind":"passage","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_DAZY_BOO4:P0058"]},{"id":"RAW_DAZY_BOO4:P0059","text":"But if to affirm truth is nothing other than that whose denial is false, it is impossible that all things be false; for one member of a contradiction must be true. Further, if it is necessary in every case either to affirm or to deny, it is impossible for both to be false; for one member of the contradiction is false. And the familiar result follows for all such arguments: they destroy themselves. For the person who says that all things are true also makes the argument opposed to his own true, and thus makes his own not true (for the opposing argument says that it is not true); while the person who says that all things are false does the same to himself. And if they make exceptions—the former excepting the opposing argument as alone not true, and the latter excepting his own as not false—nonetheless it follows for them to posit infinitely many true and false arguments. For the person who says that the true argument is true is true, and this proceeds to infinity.—It is clear too that neither those who say that all things are at rest speak truly nor those who say that all things are in motion.","kind":"passage","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_DAZY_BOO4:P0059"]},{"id":"RAW_DAZY_BOO4:P0060","text":"For if all things are at rest, the same things will always be true and false; but this plainly changes (for the person making the assertion himself once did not exist and again will not exist). But if all things are in motion, nothing will be true; therefore all things are false. But it has been shown that this is impossible. Further, what is must change; for change is from something into something. But indeed neither are all things ever at rest or in motion, nor is nothing always so; for there is something which always moves the things moved, and the first mover is itself unmoved.","kind":"passage","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_DAZY_BOO4:P0060"]}],"source_digest":"0620196ba549b0b978674ae36217a335b7040bbfebbbaa1c8ef8be14447f3741","source_language_code":"grc","original_title":"Book 4"}],"passage_basis":"frozen-grounding","original_title":"Book 4","title_language_code":"en"},{"id":"RAW_DAZY_BOO5","title":"Book 5","order":5,"chapter_id":"CH_DAZY_BOO5","text":"ἀρχὴ λέγεται ἡ μὲν ὅθεν ἄν τις τοῦ πράγματος κινηθείη πρῶτον, οἷον τοῦ μήκους καὶ ὁδοῦ ἐντεῦθεν μὲν αὕτη ἀρχή, ἐξ ἐναντίας δὲ ἑτέρα·  ἡ δὲ ὅθεν ἂν κάλλιστα ἕκαστον γένοιτο, οἷον καὶ μαθήσεως οὐκ ἀπὸ τοῦ πρώτου καὶ τῆς τοῦ πράγματος ἀρχῆς ἐνίοτε ἀρκτέον ἀλλʼ ὅθεν ῥᾷστʼ ἂν μάθοι· ἡ δὲ ὅθεν πρῶτον γίγνεται ἐνυπάρχοντος, οἷον ὡς πλοίου τρόπις καὶ οἰκίας θεμέλιος, καὶ τῶν ζῴων οἱ μὲν καρδίαν οἱ δὲ ἐγκέφαλον οἱ δʼ ὅ τι ἂν τύχωσι τοιοῦτον ὑπολαμβάνουσιν· ἡ δὲ ὅθεν γίγνεται πρῶτον μὴ ἐνυπάρχοντος καὶ ὅθεν πρῶτον ἡ κίνησις πέφυκεν ἄρχεσθαι καὶ ἡ μεταβολή, οἷον τὸ τέκνον ἐκ τοῦ πατρὸς καὶ τῆς μητρὸς καὶ ἡ μάχη ἐκ τῆς λοιδορίας· ἡ δὲ οὗ κατὰ προαίρεσιν κινεῖται τὰ κινούμενα καὶ μεταβάλλει τὰ μεταβάλλοντα, ὥσπερ αἵ τε κατὰ πόλεις ἀρχαὶ καὶ αἱ δυναστεῖαι καὶ αἱ βασιλεῖαι καὶ τυραννίδες ἀρχαὶ λέγονται καὶ αἱ τέχναι, καὶ τούτων αἱ ἀρχιτεκτονικαὶ μάλιστα. ἔτι ὅθεν γνωστὸν τὸ πρᾶγμα πρῶτον, καὶ αὕτη ἀρχὴ λέγεται τοῦ πράγματος, οἷον τῶν ἀποδείξεων αἱ ὑποθέσεις. ἰσαχῶς δὲ καὶ τὰ αἴτια λέγεται· πάντα γὰρ τὰ αἴτια ἀρχαί. πασῶν μὲν οὖν κοινὸν τῶν ἀρχῶν τὸ πρῶτον εἶναι ὅθεν ἢ ἔστιν ἢ γίγνεται ἢ γιγνώσκεται· τούτων δὲ αἱ μὲν ἐνυπάρχουσαί εἰσιν αἱ δὲ ἐκτός. διὸ ἥ τε φύσις ἀρχὴ καὶ τὸ στοιχεῖον καὶ ἡ διάνοια καὶ ἡ προαίρεσις καὶ οὐσία καὶ τὸ οὗ ἕνεκα· πολλῶν γὰρ καὶ τοῦ γνῶναι καὶ τῆς κινήσεως ἀρχὴ τἀγαθὸν καὶ τὸ καλόν.\n\n\n\n \n\n \n\nαἴτιον λέγεται ἕνα μὲν τρόπον ἐξ οὗ γίγνεταί τι ἐνυπάρχοντος, οἷον ὁ χαλκὸς τοῦ ἀνδριάντος καὶ ὁ ἄργυρος τῆς φιάλης καὶ τὰ τούτων γένη· ἄλλον δὲ τὸ εἶδος καὶ τὸ παράδειγμα, τοῦτο δʼ ἐστὶν ὁ λόγος τοῦ τί ἦν εἶναι καὶ τὰ τούτου γένη (οἷον τοῦ διὰ πασῶν τὸ δύο πρὸς ἓν καὶ ὅλως ὁ ἀριθμός) καὶ τὰ μέρη τὰ ἐν τῷ λόγῳ. ἔτι ὅθεν ἡ ἀρχὴ τῆς μεταβολῆς ἡ πρώτη ἢ τῆς ἠρεμήσεως, οἷον ὁ βουλεύσας αἴτιος, καὶ ὁ πατὴρ τοῦ τέκνου καὶ ὅλως τὸ ποιοῦν τοῦ ποιουμένου καὶ τὸ μεταβλητικὸν τοῦ μεταβάλλοντος. ἔτι ὡς τὸ τέλος· τοῦτο δʼ ἐστὶ τὸ οὗ ἕνεκα, οἷον τοῦ περιπατεῖν ἡ ὑγίεια. διὰ τί γὰρ περιπατεῖ; φαμέν. ἵνα ὑγιαίνῃ. καὶ εἰπόντες οὕτως οἰόμεθα ἀποδεδωκέναι τὸ αἴτιον. καὶ ὅσα δὴ κινήσαντος ἄλλου μεταξὺ γίγνεται τοῦ τέλους,  οἷον τῆς ὑγιείας ἡ ἰσχνασία ἢ ἡ κάθαρσις ἢ τὰ φάρμακα ἢ τὰ ὄργανα· πάντα γὰρ ταῦτα τοῦ τέλους ἕνεκά ἐστι, διαφέρει δὲ ἀλλήλων ὡς ὄντα τὰ μὲν ὄργανα τὰ δʼ ἔργα. τὰ μὲν οὖν αἴτια σχεδὸν τοσαυταχῶς λέγεται, συμβαίνει δὲ πολλαχῶς λεγομένων τῶν αἰτίων καὶ πολλὰ τοῦ αὐτοῦ αἴτια εἶναι οὐ κατὰ συμβεβηκός (οἷον τοῦ ἀνδριάντος καὶ ἡ ἀνδριαντοποιητικὴ καὶ ὁ χαλκὸς οὐ καθʼ ἕτερόν τι ἀλλʼ ᾗ ἀνδριάς· ἀλλʼ οὐ τὸν αὐτὸν τρόπον ἀλλὰ τὸ μὲν ὡς ὕλη τὸ δʼ ὡς ὅθεν ἡ κίνησις), καὶ ἀλλήλων αἴτια (οἷον τὸ πονεῖν τῆς εὐεξίας καὶ αὕτη τοῦ πονεῖν· ἀλλʼ οὐ τὸν αὐτὸν τρόπον ἀλλὰ τὸ μὲν ὡς τέλος τὸ δʼ ὡς ἀρχὴ κινήσεως). ἔτι δὲ ταὐτὸ τῶν ἐναντίων ἐστίν· ὃ γὰρ παρὸν αἴτιον τουδί, τοῦτʼ ἀπὸν αἰτιώμεθα ἐνίοτε τοῦ ἐναντίου, οἷον τὴν ἀπουσίαν τοῦ κυβερνήτου τῆς ἀνατροπῆς, οὗ ἦν ἡ παρουσία αἰτία τῆς σωτηρίας· ἄμφω δέ, καὶ ἡ παρουσία καὶ ἡ στέρησις, αἴτια ὡς κινοῦντα.—ἅπαντα δὲ τὰ νῦν εἰρημένα αἴτια εἰς τέτταρας τρόπους πίπτει τοὺς φανερωτάτους. τὰ μὲν γὰρ στοιχεῖα τῶν συλλαβῶν καὶ ἡ ὕλη τῶν σκευαστῶν καὶ τὸ πῦρ καὶ ἡ γῆ καὶ τὰ τοιαῦτα πάντα τῶν σωμάτων καὶ τὰ μέρη τοῦ ὅλου καὶ αἱ ὑποθέσεις τοῦ συμπεράσματος ὡς τὸ ἐξ οὗ αἴτιά ἐστιν· τούτων δὲ τὰ μὲν ὡς τὸ ὑποκείμενον, οἷον τὰ μέρη, τὰ δὲ ὡς τὸ τί ἦν εἶναι, τό τε ὅλον καὶ ἡ σύνθεσις καὶ τὸ εἶδος. τὸ δὲ σπέρμα καὶ ὁ ἰατρὸς καὶ ὁ βουλεύσας καὶ ὅλως τὸ ποιοῦν, πάντα ὅθεν ἡ ἀρχὴ τῆς μεταβολῆς ἢ στάσεως. τὰ δʼ ὡς τὸ τέλος καὶ τἀγαθὸν τῶν ἄλλων· τὸ γὰρ οὗ ἕνεκα βέλτιστον καὶ τέλος τῶν ἄλλων ἐθέλει εἶναι· διαφερέτω δὲ μηδὲν αὐτὸ εἰπεῖν ἀγαθὸν ἢ φαινόμενον ἀγαθόν.—τὰ μὲν οὖν αἴτια ταῦτα καὶ τοσαῦτά ἐστι τῷ εἴδει, τρόποι δὲ τῶν αἰτίων ἀριθμῷ μέν εἰσι πολλοί, κεφαλαιούμενοι δὲ καὶ οὗτοι ἐλάττους. λέγονται γὰρ αἴτια πολλαχῶς, καὶ αὐτῶν τῶν ὁμοειδῶν προτέρως καὶ ὑστέρως ἄλλο ἄλλου, οἷον ὑγιείας ὁ ἰατρὸς καὶ ὁ τεχνίτης, καὶ τοῦ διὰ πασῶν τὸ διπλάσιον καὶ ἀριθμός, καὶ ἀεὶ τὰ περιέχοντα ὁτιοῦν τῶν καθʼ ἕκαστα. ἔτι δʼ ὡς τὸ συμβεβηκὸς καὶ τὰ τούτων γένη, οἷον ἀνδριάντος ἄλλως Πολύκλειτος καὶ ἄλλως ἀνδριαντοποιός, ὅτι συμβέβηκε τῷ ἀνδριαντοποιῷ Πολυκλείτῳ εἶναι·  καὶ τὰ περιέχοντα δὲ τὸ συμβεβηκός, οἷον ἄνθρωπος αἴτιος ἀνδριάντος, ἢ καὶ ὅλως ζῷον, ὅτι ὁ Πολύκλειτος ἄνθρωπος ὁ δὲ ἄνθρωπος ζῷον. ἔστι δὲ καὶ τῶν συμβεβηκότων ἄλλα ἄλλων πορρώτερον καὶ ἐγγύτερον, οἷον εἰ ὁ λευκὸς καὶ ὁ μουσικὸς αἴτιος λέγοιτο τοῦ ἀνδριάντος, ἀλλὰ μὴ μόνον Πολύκλειτος ἢ ἄνθρωπος. παρὰ πάντα δὲ καὶ τὰ οἰκείως λεγόμενα καὶ τὰ κατὰ συμβεβηκός, τὰ μὲν ὡς δυνάμενα λέγεται τὰ δʼ ὡς ἐνεργοῦντα, οἷον τοῦ οἰκοδομεῖσθαι οἰκοδόμος ἢ οἰκοδομῶν οἰκοδόμος.  ὁμοίως δὲ λεχθήσεται καὶ ἐφʼ ὧν αἴτια τὰ αἴτια τοῖς εἰρημένοις, οἷον τοῦδε τοῦ ἀνδριάντος ἢ ἀνδριάντος ἢ ὅλως εἰκόνος, καὶ χαλκοῦ τοῦδε ἢ χαλκοῦ ἢ ὅλως ὕλης· καὶ ἐπὶ τῶν συμβεβηκότων ὡσαύτως. ἔτι δὲ συμπλεκόμενα καὶ ταῦτα κἀκεῖνα λεχθήσεται, οἷον οὐ Πολύκλειτος οὐδὲ ἀνδριαντοποιὸς ἀλλὰ Πολύκλειτος ἀνδριαντοποιός. ἀλλʼ ὅμως ἅπαντά γε ταῦτʼ ἐστὶ τὸ μὲν πλῆθος ἕξ, λεγόμενα δὲ διχῶς· ἢ γὰρ ὡς τὸ καθʼ ἕκαστον ἢ ὡς τὸ γένος, ἢ ὡς τὸ συμβεβηκὸς ἢ ὡς τὸ γένος τοῦ συμβεβηκότος, ἢ ὡς συμπλεκόμενα ταῦτα ἢ ὡς ἁπλῶς λεγόμενα, πάντα δὲ ἢ ὡς ἐνεργοῦντα ἢ κατὰ δύναμιν. διαφέρει δὲ τοσοῦτον, ὅτι τὰ μὲν ἐνεργοῦντα καὶ τὰ καθʼ ἕκαστον ἅμα ἔστι καὶ οὐκ ἔστι καὶ ὧν αἴτια, οἷον ὅδε ὁ ἰατρεύων τῷδε τῷ ὑγιαζομένῳ καὶ ὅδε ὁ οἰκοδόμος τῷδε τῷ οἰκοδομουμένῳ, τὰ δὲ κατὰ δύναμιν οὐκ ἀεί· φθείρεται γὰρ οὐχ ἅμα ἡ οἰκία καὶ ὁ οἰκοδόμος.\n\n\n\n \n\n \n\nστοιχεῖον λέγεται ἐξ οὗ σύγκειται πρώτου ἐνυπάρχοντος ἀδιαιρέτου τῷ εἴδει εἰς ἕτερον εἶδος, οἷον φωνῆς στοιχεῖα ἐξ ὧν σύγκειται ἡ φωνὴ καὶ εἰς ἃ διαιρεῖται ἔσχατα, ἐκεῖνα δὲ μηκέτʼ εἰς ἄλλας φωνὰς ἑτέρας τῷ εἴδει αὐτῶν, ἀλλὰ κἂν διαιρῆται, τὰ μόρια ὁμοειδῆ, οἷον ὕδατος τὸ μόριον ὕδωρ, ἀλλʼ οὐ τῆς συλλαβῆς. ὁμοίως δὲ καὶ τὰ τῶν σωμάτων στοιχεῖα λέγουσιν οἱ λέγοντες εἰς ἃ διαιρεῖται τὰ σώματα ἔσχατα, ἐκεῖνα δὲ μηκέτʼ εἰς ἄλλα εἴδει διαφέροντα· καὶ εἴτε ἓν εἴτε πλείω τὰ τοιαῦτα, ταῦτα στοιχεῖα λέγουσιν. παραπλησίως δὲ καὶ τὰ τῶν διαγραμμάτων στοιχεῖα λέγεται, καὶ ὅλως τὰ τῶν ἀποδείξεων· αἱ γὰρ πρῶται ἀποδείξεις καὶ ἐν πλείοσιν ἀποδείξεσιν ἐνυπάρχουσαι,  αὗται στοιχεῖα τῶν ἀποδείξεων λέγονται· εἰσὶ δὲ τοιοῦτοι συλλογισμοὶ οἱ πρῶτοι ἐκ τῶν τριῶν διʼ ἑνὸς μέσου. καὶ μεταφέροντες δὲ στοιχεῖον καλοῦσιν ἐντεῦθεν ὃ ἂν ἓν ὂν καὶ μικρὸν ἐπὶ πολλὰ ᾖ χρήσιμον, διὸ καὶ τὸ μικρὸν καὶ ἁπλοῦν καὶ ἀδιαίρετον στοιχεῖον λέγεται. ὅθεν ἐλήλυθε τὰ μάλιστα καθόλου στοιχεῖα εἶναι, ὅτι ἕκαστον αὐτῶν ἓν ὂν καὶ ἁπλοῦν ἐν πολλοῖς ὑπάρχει ἢ πᾶσιν ἢ ὅτι πλείστοις, καὶ τὸ ἓν καὶ τὴν στιγμὴν ἀρχάς τισι δοκεῖν εἶναι. ἐπεὶ οὖν τὰ καλούμενα γένη καθόλου καὶ ἀδιαίρετα (οὐ γὰρ ἔστι λόγος αὐτῶν), στοιχεῖα τὰ γένη λέγουσί τινες, καὶ μᾶλλον ἢ τὴν διαφορὰν ὅτι καθόλου μᾶλλον τὸ γένος· ᾧ μὲν γὰρ ἡ διαφορὰ ὑπάρχει, καὶ τὸ γένος ἀκολουθεῖ, ᾧ δὲ τὸ γένος, οὐ παντὶ ἡ διαφορά. ἁπάντων δὲ κοινὸν τὸ εἶναι στοιχεῖον ἑκάστου τὸ πρῶτον ἐνυπάρχον ἑκάστῳ.\n\n\n\n \n\n \n\nφύσις λέγεται ἕνα μὲν τρόπον ἡ τῶν φυομένων γένεσις, οἷον εἴ τις ἐπεκτείνας λέγοι τὸ υ, ἕνα δὲ ἐξ οὗ φύεται πρώτου τὸ φυόμενον ἐνυπάρχοντος· ἔτι ὅθεν ἡ κίνησις ἡ πρώτη ἐν ἑκάστῳ τῶν φύσει ὄντων ἐν αὐτῷ ᾗ αὐτὸ ὑπάρχει· φύεσθαι δὲ λέγεται ὅσα αὔξησιν ἔχει διʼ ἑτέρου τῷ ἅπτεσθαι καὶ συμπεφυκέναι ἢ προσπεφυκέναι ὥσπερ τὰ ἔμβρυα· διαφέρει δὲ σύμφυσις ἁφῆς, ἔνθα μὲν γὰρ οὐδὲν παρὰ τὴν ἁφὴν ἕτερον ἀνάγκη εἶναι, ἐν δὲ τοῖς συμπεφυκόσιν ἔστι τι ἓν τὸ αὐτὸ ἐν ἀμφοῖν ὃ ποιεῖ ἀντὶ τοῦ ἅπτεσθαι συμπεφυκέναι καὶ εἶναι ἓν κατὰ τὸ συνεχὲς καὶ ποσόν, ἀλλὰ μὴ κατὰ τὸ ποιόν. ἔτι δὲ φύσις λέγεται ἐξ οὗ πρώτου ἢ ἔστιν ἢ γίγνεταί τι τῶν φύσει ὄντων, ἀρρυθμίστου ὄντος καὶ ἀμεταβλήτου ἐκ τῆς δυνάμεως τῆς αὑτοῦ, οἷον ἀνδριάντος καὶ τῶν σκευῶν τῶν χαλκῶν ὁ χαλκὸς ἡ φύσις λέγεται, τῶν δὲ ξυλίνων ξύλον· ὁμοίως δὲ καὶ ἐπὶ τῶν ἄλλων· ἐκ τούτων γάρ ἐστιν ἕκαστον διασωζομένης τῆς πρώτης ὕλης· τοῦτον γὰρ τὸν τρόπον καὶ τῶν φύσει ὄντων τὰ στοιχεῖά φασιν εἶναι φύσιν, οἱ μὲν πῦρ οἱ δὲ γῆν οἱ δʼ ἀέρα οἱ δʼ ὕδωρ οἱ δʼ ἄλλο τι τοιοῦτον λέγοντες, οἱ δʼ ἔνια τούτων οἱ δὲ πάντα ταῦτα. ἔτι δʼ ἄλλον τρόπον λέγεται ἡ φύσις ἡ τῶν φύσει ὄντων οὐσία, οἷον οἱ λέγοντες τὴν φύσιν εἶναι τὴν πρώτην σύνθεσιν,  ἢ ὥσπερ Ἐμπεδοκλῆς λέγει ὅτι  \n\nφύσις οὐδενὸς ἔστιν ἐόντων,\n\n \n\nἀλλὰ μόνον μῖξίς τε διάλλαξίς τε μιγέντων\n\n \n\nἔστι, φύσις δʼ ἐπὶ τοῖς ὀνομάζεται ἀνθρώποισιν.\n\n  Empedocles Fr. 8 διὸ καὶ ὅσα φύσει ἔστιν ἢ γίγνεται, ἤδη ὑπάρχοντος ἐξ οὗ πέφυκε γίγνεσθαι ἢ εἶναι, οὔπω φαμὲν τὴν φύσιν ἔχειν ἐὰν μὴ ἔχῃ τὸ εἶδος καὶ τὴν μορφήν. φύσει μὲν οὖν τὸ ἐξ ἀμφοτέρων τούτων ἐστίν, οἷον τὰ ζῷα καὶ τὰ μόρια αὐτῶν· φύσις δὲ ἥ τε πρώτη ὕλη (καὶ αὕτη διχῶς, ἢ ἡ πρὸς αὐτὸ πρώτη ἢ ἡ ὅλως πρώτη, οἷον τῶν χαλκῶν ἔργων πρὸς αὐτὰ μὲν πρῶτος ὁ χαλκός, ὅλως δʼ ἴσως ὕδωρ, εἰ πάντα τὰ τηκτὰ ὕδωρ) καὶ τὸ εἶδος καὶ ἡ οὐσία· τοῦτο δʼ ἐστὶ τὸ τέλος τῆς γενέσεως. μεταφορᾷ δʼ ἤδη καὶ ὅλως πᾶσα οὐσία φύσις λέγεται διὰ ταύτην, ὅτι καὶ ἡ φύσις οὐσία τίς ἐστιν. ἐκ δὴ τῶν εἰρημένων ἡ πρώτη φύσις καὶ κυρίως λεγομένη ἐστὶν ἡ οὐσία ἡ τῶν ἐχόντων ἀρχὴν κινήσεως ἐν αὑτοῖς ᾗ αὐτά· ἡ γὰρ ὕλη τῷ ταύτης δεκτικὴ εἶναι λέγεται φύσις, καὶ αἱ γενέσεις καὶ τὸ φύεσθαι τῷ ἀπὸ ταύτης εἶναι κινήσεις. καὶ ἡ ἀρχὴ τῆς κινήσεως τῶν φύσει ὄντων αὕτη ἐστίν, ἐνυπάρχουσά πως ἢ δυνάμει ἢ ἐντελεχείᾳ.\n\n\n\n \n\n \n\nἀναγκαῖον λέγεται οὗ ἄνευ οὐκ ἐνδέχεται ζῆν ὡς συναιτίου (οἷον τὸ ἀναπνεῖν καὶ ἡ τροφὴ τῷ ζῴῳ ἀναγκαῖον, ἀδύνατον γὰρ ἄνευ τούτων εἶναι), καὶ ὧν ἄνευ τὸ ἀγαθὸν μὴ ἐνδέχεται ἢ εἶναι ἢ γενέσθαι, ἢ τὸ κακὸν ἀποβαλεῖν ἢ στερηθῆναι (οἷον τὸ πιεῖν τὸ φάρμακον ἀναγκαῖον ἵνα μὴ κάμνῃ, καὶ τὸ πλεῦσαι εἰς Αἴγιναν ἵνα ἀπολάβῃ τὰ χρήματα). ἔτι τὸ βίαιον καὶ ἡ βία· τοῦτο δʼ ἐστὶ τὸ παρὰ τὴν ὁρμὴν καὶ τὴν προαίρεσιν ἐμποδίζον καὶ κωλυτικόν, τὸ γὰρ βίαιον ἀναγκαῖον λέγεται, διὸ καὶ λυπηρόν (ὥσπερ καὶ Εὔηνός φησι πᾶν γὰρ ἀναγκαῖον πρᾶγμʼ ἀνιαρὸν ἔφυ), καὶ ἡ βία ἀνάγκη τις (ὥσπερ καὶ Σοφοκλῆς λέγει  ἀλλʼ ἡ βία με ταῦτʼ ἀναγκάζει ποιεῖν), καὶ δοκεῖ ἡ ἀνάγκη ἀμετάπειστόν τι εἶναι, ὀρθῶς· ἐναντίον γὰρ τῇ κατὰ τὴν προαίρεσιν κινήσει καὶ κατὰ τὸν λογισμόν. ἔτι τὸ μὴ ἐνδεχόμενον ἄλλως ἔχειν ἀναγκαῖόν φαμεν οὕτως ἔχειν· καὶ κατὰ τοῦτο τὸ ἀναγκαῖον καὶ τἆλλα λέγεταί πως ἅπαντα ἀναγκαῖα· τό τε γὰρ βίαιον ἀναγκαῖον λέγεται ἢ ποιεῖν ἢ πάσχειν τότε,  ὅταν μὴ ἐνδέχηται κατὰ τὴν ὁρμὴν διὰ τὸ βιαζόμενον, ὡς ταύτην ἀνάγκην οὖσαν διʼ ἣν μὴ ἐνδέχεται ἄλλως, καὶ ἐπὶ τῶν συναιτίων τοῦ ζῆν καὶ τοῦ ἀγαθοῦ ὡσαύτως· ὅταν γὰρ μὴ ἐνδέχηται ἔνθα μὲν τὸ ἀγαθὸν ἔνθα δὲ τὸ ζῆν καὶ τὸ εἶναι ἄνευ τινῶν, ταῦτα ἀναγκαῖα καὶ ἡ αἰτία ἀνάγκη τίς ἐστιν αὕτη. ἔτι ἡ ἀπόδειξις τῶν ἀναγκαίων, ὅτι οὐκ ἐνδέχεται ἄλλως ἔχειν, εἰ ἀποδέδεικται ἁπλῶς· τούτου δʼ αἴτια τὰ πρῶτα, εἰ ἀδύνατον ἄλλως ἔχειν ἐξ ὧν ὁ συλλογισμός. τῶν μὲν δὴ ἕτερον αἴτιον τοῦ ἀναγκαῖα εἶναι, τῶν δὲ οὐδέν, ἀλλὰ διὰ ταῦτα ἕτερά ἐστιν ἐξ ἀνάγκης. ὥστε τὸ πρῶτον καὶ κυρίως ἀναγκαῖον τὸ ἁπλοῦν ἐστίν· τοῦτο γὰρ οὐκ ἐνδέχεται πλεοναχῶς ἔχειν, ὥστʼ οὐδὲ ἄλλως καὶ ἄλλως· ἤδη γὰρ πλεοναχῶς ἂν ἔχοι. εἰ ἄρα ἔστιν ἄττα ἀΐδια καὶ ἀκίνητα, οὐδὲν ἐκείνοις ἐστὶ βίαιον οὐδὲ παρὰ φύσιν. \n\n\n\n \n\n \n\nἓν λέγεται τὸ μὲν κατὰ συμβεβηκὸς τὸ δὲ καθʼ αὑτό, κατὰ συμβεβηκὸς μὲν οἷον Κορίσκος καὶ τὸ μουσικόν, καὶ Κορίσκος μουσικός (ταὐτὸ γὰρ εἰπεῖν Κορίσκος καὶ τὸ μουσικόν, καὶ Κορίσκος μουσικός), καὶ τὸ μουσικὸν καὶ τὸ δίκαιον, καὶ μουσικὸς Κορίσκος καὶ δίκαιος Κορίσκος· πάντα γὰρ ταῦτα ἓν λέγεται κατὰ συμβεβηκός, τὸ μὲν δίκαιον καὶ τὸ μουσικὸν ὅτι μιᾷ οὐσίᾳ συμβέβηκεν, τὸ δὲ μουσικὸν καὶ Κορίσκος ὅτι θάτερον θατέρῳ συμβέβηκεν· ὁμοίως δὲ τρόπον τινὰ καὶ ὁ μουσικὸς Κορίσκος τῷ Κορίσκῳ ἓν ὅτι θάτερον τῶν μορίων θατέρῳ συμβέβηκε τῶν ἐν τῷ λόγῳ, οἷον τὸ μουσικὸν τῷ Κορίσκῳ· καὶ ὁ μουσικὸς Κορίσκος δικαίῳ Κορίσκῳ ὅτι ἑκατέρου μέρος τῷ αὐτῷ ἑνὶ συμβέβηκεν ἕν. ὡσαύτως δὲ κἂν ἐπὶ γένους κἂν ἐπὶ τῶν καθόλου τινὸς ὀνομάτων λέγηται τὸ συμβεβηκός, οἷον ὅτι ἄνθρωπος τὸ αὐτὸ καὶ μουσικὸς ἄνθρωπος· ἢ γὰρ ὅτι τῷ ἀνθρώπῳ μιᾷ οὔσῃ οὐσίᾳ συμβέβηκε τὸ μουσικόν, ἢ ὅτι ἄμφω τῶν καθʼ ἕκαστόν τινι συμβέβηκεν, οἷον Κορίσκῳ. πλὴν οὐ τὸν αὐτὸν τρόπον ἄμφω ὑπάρχει, ἀλλὰ τὸ μὲν ἴσως ὡς γένος καὶ ἐν τῇ οὐσίᾳ τὸ δὲ ὡς ἕξις ἢ πάθος τῆς οὐσίας.—ὅσα μὲν οὖν κατὰ συμβεβηκὸς λέγεται ἕν, τοῦτον τὸν τρόπον λέγεται· τῶν δὲ καθʼ ἑαυτὰ ἓν λεγομένων τὰ μὲν λέγεται τῷ συνεχῆ εἶναι, οἷον φάκελος δεσμῷ καὶ ξύλα κόλλῃ·  καὶ γραμμή, κἂν κεκαμμένη ᾖ, συνεχὴς δέ, μία λέγεται, ὥσπερ καὶ τῶν μερῶν ἕκαστον, οἷον σκέλος καὶ βραχίων. αὐτῶν δὲ τούτων μᾶλλον ἓν τὰ φύσει συνεχῆ ἢ τέχνῃ. συνεχὲς δὲ λέγεται οὗ κίνησις μία καθʼ αὑτὸ καὶ μὴ οἷόν τε ἄλλως· μία δʼ οὗ ἀδιαίρετος, ἀδιαίρετος δὲ κατὰ χρόνον. καθʼ αὑτὰ δὲ συνεχῆ ὅσα μὴ ἁφῇ ἕν· εἰ γὰρ θείης ἁπτόμενα ἀλλήλων ξύλα, οὐ φήσεις ταῦτα εἶναι ἓν οὔτε ξύλον οὔτε σῶμα οὔτʼ ἄλλο συνεχὲς οὐδέν. τά τε δὴ ὅλως συνεχῆ ἓν λέγεται κἂν ἔχῃ κάμψιν, καὶ ἔτι μᾶλλον τὰ μὴ ἔχοντα κάμψιν, οἷον κνήμη ἢ μηρὸς σκέλους, ὅτι ἐνδέχεται μὴ μίαν εἶναι τὴν κίνησιν τοῦ σκέλους. καὶ ἡ εὐθεῖα τῆς κεκαμμένης μᾶλλον ἕν· τὴν δὲ κεκαμμένην καὶ ἔχουσαν γωνίαν καὶ μίαν καὶ οὐ μίαν λέγομεν, ὅτι ἐνδέχεται καὶ μὴ ἅμα τὴν κίνησιν αὐτῆς εἶναι καὶ ἅμα· τῆς δʼ εὐθείας ἀεὶ ἅμα, καὶ οὐδὲν μόριον ἔχον μέγεθος τὸ μὲν ἠρεμεῖ τὸ δὲ κινεῖται, ὥσπερ τῆς κεκαμμένης. ἔτι ἄλλον τρόπον ἓν λέγεται τῷ τὸ ὑποκείμενον τῷ εἴδει εἶναι ἀδιάφορον· ἀδιάφορον δʼ ὧν ἀδιαίρετον τὸ εἶδος κατὰ τὴν αἴσθησιν· τὸ δʼ ὑποκείμενον ἢ τὸ πρῶτον ἢ τὸ τελευταῖον πρὸς τὸ τέλος· καὶ γὰρ οἶνος εἷς λέγεται καὶ ὕδωρ ἕν, ᾗ ἀδιαίρετον κατὰ τὸ εἶδος, καὶ οἱ χυμοὶ πάντες λέγονται ἕν (οἷον ἔλαιον οἶνος) καὶ τὰ τηκτά, ὅτι πάντων τὸ ἔσχατον ὑποκείμενον τὸ αὐτό· ὕδωρ γὰρ ἢ ἀὴρ πάντα ταῦτα. λέγεται δʼ ἓν καὶ ὧν τὸ γένος ἓν διαφέρον ταῖς ἀντικειμέναις διαφοραῖς—καὶ ταῦτα λέγεται πάντα ἓν ὅτι τὸ γένος ἓν τὸ ὑποκείμενον ταῖς διαφοραῖς (οἷον ἵππος ἄνθρωπος κύων ἕν τι ὅτι πάντα ζῷα), καὶ τρόπον δὴ παραπλήσιον ὥσπερ ἡ ὕλη μία. ταῦτα δὲ ὁτὲ μὲν οὕτως ἓν λέγεται, ὁτὲ δὲ τὸ ἄνω γένος ταὐτὸν λέγεται —ἂν ᾖ τελευταῖα τοῦ γένους εἴδη—τὸ ἀνωτέρω τούτων, οἷον τὸ ἰσοσκελὲς καὶ τὸ ἰσόπλευρον ταὐτὸ καὶ ἓν σχῆμα ὅτι ἄμφω τρίγωνα· τρίγωνα δʼ οὐ ταὐτά. ἔτι δὲ ἓν λέγεται ὅσων ὁ λόγος ὁ τὸ τί ἦν εἶναι λέγων ἀδιαίρετος πρὸς ἄλλον τὸν δηλοῦντα  τὸ πρᾶγμα (αὐτὸς γὰρ καθʼ αὑτὸν πᾶς λόγος διαιρετός). οὕτω γὰρ καὶ τὸ ηὐξημένον καὶ φθῖνον ἕν ἐστιν, ὅτι ὁ λόγος εἷς, ὥσπερ ἐπὶ τῶν ἐπιπέδων ὁ τοῦ εἴδους.  ὅλως δὲ ὧν ἡ νόησις ἀδιαίρετος ἡ νοοῦσα τὸ τί ἦν εἶναι, καὶ μὴ δύναται χωρίσαι μήτε χρόνῳ μήτε τόπῳ μήτε λόγῳ, μάλιστα ταῦτα ἕν, καὶ τούτων ὅσα οὐσίαι· καθόλου γὰρ ὅσα μὴ ἔχει διαίρεσιν, ᾗ μὴ ἔχει, ταύτῃ ἓν λέγεται, οἷον εἰ ᾗ ἄνθρωπος μὴ ἔχει διαίρεσιν, εἷς ἄνθρωπος, εἰ δʼ ᾗ ζῷον, ἓν ζῷον, εἰ δὲ ᾗ μέγεθος, ἓν μέγεθος. τὰ μὲν οὖν πλεῖστα ἓν λέγεται τῷ ἕτερόν τι ἢ ποιεῖν ἢ ἔχειν ἢ πάσχειν ἢ πρός τι εἶναι ἕν, τὰ δὲ πρώτως λεγόμενα ἓν ὧν ἡ οὐσία μία, μία δὲ ἢ συνεχείᾳ ἢ εἴδει ἢ λόγῳ· καὶ γὰρ ἀριθμοῦμεν ὡς πλείω ἢ τὰ μὴ συνεχῆ ἢ ὧν μὴ ἓν τὸ εἶδος ἢ ὧν ὁ λόγος μὴ εἷς. ἔτι δʼ ἔστι μὲν ὡς ὁτιοῦν ἕν φαμεν εἶναι ἂν ᾖ ποσὸν καὶ συνεχές, ἔστι δʼ ὡς οὔ, ἂν μή τι ὅλον ᾖ, τοῦτο δὲ ἂν μὴ τὸ εἶδος ἔχῃ ἕν· οἷον οὐκ ἂν φαῖμεν ὁμοίως ἓν ἰδόντες ὁπωσοῦν τὰ μέρη συγκείμενα τοῦ ὑποδήματος, ἐὰν μὴ διὰ τὴν συνέχειαν, ἀλλʼ ἐὰν οὕτως ὥστε ὑπόδημα εἶναι καὶ εἶδός τι ἔχειν ἤδη ἕν· διὸ καὶ ἡ τοῦ κύκλου μάλιστα μία τῶν γραμμῶν, ὅτι ὅλη καὶ τέλειός ἐστιν.—τὸ δὲ ἑνὶ εἶναι ἀρχῇ τινί ἐστιν ἀριθμοῦ εἶναι· τὸ γὰρ πρῶτον μέτρον ἀρχή, ᾧ γὰρ πρώτῳ γνωρίζομεν, τοῦτο πρῶτον μέτρον ἑκάστου γένους· ἀρχὴ οὖν τοῦ γνωστοῦ περὶ ἕκαστον τὸ ἕν. οὐ ταὐτὸ δὲ ἐν πᾶσι τοῖς γένεσι τὸ ἕν. ἔνθα μὲν γὰρ δίεσις ἔνθα δὲ τὸ φωνῆεν ἢ ἄφωνον· βάρους δὲ ἕτερον καὶ κινήσεως ἄλλο. πανταχοῦ δὲ τὸ ἓν ἢ τῷ ποσῷ ἢ τῷ εἴδει ἀδιαίρετον. τὸ μὲν οὖν κατὰ τὸ ποσὸν ἀδιαίρετον, τὸ μὲν πάντῃ καὶ ἄθετον λέγεται μονάς, τὸ δὲ πάντῃ καὶ θέσιν ἔχον στιγμή, τὸ δὲ μοναχῇ γραμμή, τὸ δὲ διχῇ ἐπίπεδον, τὸ δὲ πάντῃ καὶ τριχῇ διαιρετὸν κατὰ τὸ ποσὸν σῶμα· καὶ ἀντιστρέψαντι δὴ τὸ μὲν διχῇ διαιρετὸν ἐπίπεδον, τὸ δὲ μοναχῇ γραμμή, τὸ δὲ μηδαμῇ διαιρετὸν κατὰ τὸ ποσὸν στιγμὴ καὶ μονάς, ἡ μὲν ἄθετος μονὰς ἡ δὲ θετὸς στιγμή. ἔτι δὲ τὰ μὲν κατʼ ἀριθμόν ἐστιν ἕν, τὰ δὲ κατʼ εἶδος, τὰ δὲ κατὰ γένος, τὰ δὲ κατʼ ἀναλογίαν, ἀριθμῷ μὲν ὧν ἡ ὕλη μία, εἴδει δʼ ὧν ὁ λόγος εἷς, γένει δʼ ὧν τὸ αὐτὸ σχῆμα τῆς κατηγορίας, κατʼ ἀναλογίαν δὲ ὅσα ἔχει ὡς ἄλλο πρὸς ἄλλο. ἀεὶ δὲ τὰ ὕστερα τοῖς ἔμπροσθεν ἀκολουθεῖ, οἷον ὅσα ἀριθμῷ καὶ εἴδει ἕν, ὅσα δʼ εἴδει οὐ πάντα ἀριθμῷ·  ἀλλὰ γένει πάντα ἓν ὅσαπερ καὶ εἴδει, ὅσα δὲ γένει οὐ πάντα εἴδει ἀλλʼ ἀναλογίᾳ· ὅσα δὲ ἀνολογίᾳ οὐ πάντα γένει. φανερὸν δὲ καὶ ὅτι τὰ πολλὰ ἀντικειμένως λεχθήσεται τῷ ἑνί· τὰ μὲν γὰρ τῷ μὴ συνεχῆ εἶναι, τὰ δὲ τῷ διαιρετὴν  ἔχειν τὴν ὕλην κατὰ τὸ εἶδος, ἢ τὴν πρώτην ἢ τὴν τελευταίαν, τὰ δὲ τῷ τοὺς λόγους πλείους τοὺς τί ἦν εἶναι λέγοντας. \n\n\n\n \n\n \n\nτὸ ὂν λέγεται τὸ μὲν κατὰ συμβεβηκὸς τὸ δὲ καθʼ αὑτό, κατὰ συμβεβηκὸς μέν, οἷον τὸν δίκαιον μουσικὸν εἶναί φαμεν καὶ τὸν ἄνθρωπον μουσικὸν καὶ τὸν μουσικὸν ἄνθρωπον, παραπλησίως λέγοντες ὡσπερεὶ τὸν μουσικὸν οἰκοδομεῖν ὅτι συμβέβηκε τῷ οἰκοδόμῳ μουσικῷ εἶναι ἢ τῷ μουσικῷ οἰκοδόμῳ (τὸ γὰρ τόδε εἶναι τόδε σημαίνει τὸ συμβεβηκέναι τῷδε τόδε),—οὕτω δὲ καὶ ἐπὶ τῶν εἰρημένων· τὸν γὰρ ἄνθρωπον ὅταν μουσικὸν λέγωμεν καὶ τὸν μουσικὸν ἄνθρωπον, ἢ τὸν λευκὸν μουσικὸν ἢ τοῦτον λευκόν, τὸ μὲν ὅτι ἄμφω τῷ αὐτῷ συμβεβήκασι, τὸ δʼ ὅτι τῷ ὄντι συμβέβηκε, τὸ δὲ μουσικὸν ἄνθρωπον ὅτι τούτῳ τὸ μουσικὸν συμβέβηκεν (οὕτω δὲ λέγεται καὶ τὸ μὴ λευκὸν εἶναι, ὅτι ᾧ συμβέβηκεν, ἐκεῖνο ἔστιν)·—τὰ μὲν οὖν κατὰ συμβεβηκὸς εἶναι λεγόμενα οὕτω λέγεται ἢ διότι τῷ αὐτῷ ὄντι ἄμφω ὑπάρχει, ἢ ὅτι ὄντι ἐκείνῳ ὑπάρχει, ἢ ὅτι αὐτὸ ἔστιν ᾧ ὑπάρχει οὗ αὐτὸ κατηγορεῖται· καθʼ αὑτὰ δὲ εἶναι λέγεται ὅσαπερ σημαίνει τὰ σχήματα τῆς κατηγορίας· ὁσαχῶς γὰρ λέγεται, τοσαυταχῶς τὸ εἶναι σημαίνει. ἐπεὶ οὖν τῶν κατηγορουμένων τὰ μὲν τί ἐστι σημαίνει, τὰ δὲ ποιόν, τὰ δὲ ποσόν, τὰ δὲ πρός τι, τὰ δὲ ποιεῖν ἢ πάσχειν, τὰ δὲ πού, τὰ δὲ ποτέ, ἑκάστῳ τούτων τὸ εἶναι ταὐτὸ σημαίνει· οὐθὲν γὰρ διαφέρει τὸ ἄνθρωπος ὑγιαίνων ἐστὶν ἢ τὸ ἄνθρωπος ὑγιαίνει, οὐδὲ τὸ ἄνθρωπος βαδίζων ἐστὶν ἢ τέμνων τοῦ ἄνθρωπος βαδίζει ἢ τέμνει, ὁμοίως δὲ καὶ ἐπὶ τῶν ἄλλων. ἔτι τὸ εἶναι σημαίνει καὶ τὸ ἔστιν ὅτι ἀληθές, τὸ δὲ μὴ εἶναι ὅτι οὐκ ἀληθὲς ἀλλὰ ψεῦδος, ὁμοίως ἐπὶ καταφάσεως καὶ ἀποφάσεως, οἷον ὅτι ἔστι Σωκράτης μουσικός, ὅτι ἀληθὲς τοῦτο, ἢ ὅτι ἔστι Σωκράτης οὐ λευκός, ὅτι ἀληθές· τὸ δʼ οὐκ ἔστιν ἡ διάμετρος σύμμετρος, ὅτι ψεῦδος.  ἔτι τὸ εἶναι σημαίνει καὶ τὸ ὂν τὸ μὲν δυνάμει ῥητὸν τὸ δʼ ἐντελεχείᾳ τῶν εἰρημένων τούτων· ὁρῶν τε γὰρ εἶναί φαμεν καὶ τὸ δυνάμει ὁρῶν καὶ τὸ ἐντελεχείᾳ, καὶ  ἐπίστασθαι ὡσαύτως καὶ τὸ δυνάμενον χρῆσθαι τῇ ἐπιστήμῃ καὶ τὸ χρώμενον, καὶ ἠρεμοῦν καὶ ᾧ ἤδη ὑπάρχει ἠρεμία καὶ τὸ δυνάμενον ἠρεμεῖν. ὁμοίως δὲ καὶ ἐπὶ τῶν οὐσιῶν· καὶ γὰρ Ἑρμῆν ἐν τῷ λίθῳ φαμὲν εἶναι, καὶ τὸ ἥμισυ τῆς γραμμῆς, καὶ σῖτον τὸν μήπω ἁδρόν. πότε δὲ δυνατὸν καὶ πότε οὔπω, ἐν ἄλλοις διοριστέον.\n\n\n\n \n\n \n\nοὐσία λέγεται τά τε ἁπλᾶ σώματα, οἷον γῆ καὶ πῦρ καὶ ὕδωρ καὶ ὅσα τοιαῦτα, καὶ ὅλως σώματα καὶ τὰ ἐκ τούτων συνεστῶτα ζῷά τε καὶ δαιμόνια καὶ τὰ μόρια τούτων· ἅπαντα δὲ ταῦτα λέγεται οὐσία ὅτι οὐ καθʼ ὑποκειμένου λέγεται ἀλλὰ κατὰ τούτων τὰ ἄλλα. ἄλλον δὲ τρόπον ὃ ἂν ᾖ αἴτιον τοῦ εἶναι, ἐνυπάρχον ἐν τοῖς τοιούτοις ὅσα μὴ λέγεται καθʼ ὑποκειμένου, οἷον ἡ ψυχὴ τῷ ζῴῳ. ἔτι ὅσα μόρια ἐνυπάρχοντά ἐστιν ἐν τοῖς τοιούτοις ὁρίζοντά τε καὶ τόδε τι σημαίνοντα, ὧν ἀναιρουμένων ἀναιρεῖται τὸ ὅλον, οἷον ἐπιπέδου σῶμα, ὥς φασί τινες, καὶ ἐπίπεδον γραμμῆς· καὶ ὅλως ὁ ἀριθμὸς δοκεῖ εἶναί τισι τοιοῦτος (ἀναιρουμένου τε γὰρ οὐδὲν εἶναι, καὶ ὁρίζειν πάντα)· ἔτι τὸ τί ἦν εἶναι, οὗ ὁ λόγος ὁρισμός, καὶ τοῦτο οὐσία λέγεται ἑκάστου. συμβαίνει δὴ κατὰ δύο τρόπους τὴν οὐσίαν λέγεσθαι, τό θʼ ὑποκείμενον ἔσχατον, ὃ μηκέτι κατʼ ἄλλου λέγεται, καὶ ὃ ἂν τόδε τι ὂν καὶ χωριστὸν ᾖ· τοιοῦτον δὲ ἑκάστου ἡ μορφὴ καὶ τὸ εἶδος.\n\n\n\n \n\n \n\nταὐτὰ λέγεται τὰ μὲν κατὰ συμβεβηκός, οἷον τὸ λευκὸν καὶ τὸ μουσικὸν τὸ αὐτὸ ὅτι τῷ αὐτῷ συμβέβηκε, καὶ ἄνθρωπος καὶ μουσικὸν ὅτι θάτερον θατέρῳ συμβέβηκεν, τὸ δὲ μουσικὸν ἄνθρωπος ὅτι τῷ ἀνθρώπῳ συμβέβηκεν· ἑκατέρῳ δὲ τοῦτο καὶ τούτῳ ἑκάτερον ἐκείνων, καὶ γὰρ τῷ ἀνθρώπῳ τῷ μουσικῷ καὶ ὁ ἄνθρωπος καὶ τὸ μουσικὸν ταὐτὸ λέγεται, καὶ τούτοις ἐκεῖνο (διὸ καὶ πάντα ταῦτα καθόλου οὐ λέγεται· οὐ γὰρ ἀληθὲς εἰπεῖν ὅτι πᾶς ἄνθρωπος ταὐτὸ καὶ τὸ μουσικόν· τὰ γὰρ καθόλου καθʼ αὑτὰ ὑπάρχει, τὰ δὲ συμβεβηκότα οὐ καθʼ αὑτά·  ἀλλʼ ἐπὶ τῶν καθʼ ἕκαστα ἁπλῶς λέγεται· ταὐτὸ γὰρ δοκεῖ Σωκράτης καὶ Σωκράτης εἶναι μουσικός· τὸ δὲ Σωκράτης οὐκ ἐπὶ πολλῶν, διὸ οὐ πᾶς Σωκράτης λέγεται ὥσπερ πᾶς ἄνθρωπος)·—καὶ τὰ μὲν οὕτως λέγεται ταὐτά, τὰ δὲ καθʼ αὑτὰ ὁσαχῶσπερ καὶ τὸ ἕν· καὶ γὰρ ὧν ἡ ὕλη μία ἢ εἴδει ἢ ἀριθμῷ ταὐτὰ λέγεται καὶ ὧν ἡ οὐσία μία, ὥστε φανερὸν ὅτι ἡ ταυτότης ἑνότης τίς ἐστιν ἢ πλειόνων τοῦ εἶναι ἢ ὅταν χρῆται ὡς πλείοσιν, οἷον ὅταν λέγῃ αὐτὸ αὑτῷ ταὐτόν· ὡς δυσὶ γὰρ χρῆται αὐτῷ.—ἕτερα δὲ λέγεται ὧν ἢ τὰ εἴδη πλείω ἢ ἡ ὕλη ἢ ὁ λόγος τῆς οὐσίας· καὶ ὅλως ἀντικειμένως τῷ ταὐτῷ λέγεται τὸ ἕτερον.\n\n \n\nδιάφορα δὲ λέγεται ὅσʼ ἕτερά ἐστι τὸ αὐτό τι ὄντα, μὴ μόνον ἀριθμῷ ἀλλʼ ἢ εἴδει ἢ γένει ἢ ἀναλογίᾳ· ἔτι ὧν ἕτερον τὸ γένος, καὶ τὰ ἐναντία, καὶ ὅσα ἔχει ἐν τῇ οὐσίᾳ τὴν ἑτερότητα. ὅμοια λέγεται τά τε πάντῃ ταὐτὸ πεπονθότα, καὶ τὰ πλείω ταὐτὰ πεπονθότα ἢ ἕτερα, καὶ ὧν ἡ ποιότης μία· καὶ καθʼ ὅσα ἀλλοιοῦσθαι ἐνδέχεται τῶν ἐναντίων, τούτων τὸ πλείω ἔχον ἢ κυριώτερα ὅμοιον τούτῳ. ἀντικειμένως δὲ τοῖς ὁμοίοις τὰ ἀνόμοια.\n\n\n\n \n\n \n\nἀντικείμενα λέγεται ἀντίφασις καὶ τἀναντία καὶ τὰ πρός τι καὶ στέρησις καὶ ἕξις καὶ ἐξ ὧν καὶ εἰς ἃ ἔσχατα αἱ γενέσεις καὶ φθοραί· καὶ ὅσα μὴ ἐνδέχεται ἅμα παρεῖναι τῷ ἀμφοῖν δεκτικῷ, ταῦτα ἀντικεῖσθαι λέγεται ἢ αὐτὰ ἢ ἐξ ὧν ἐστίν. φαιὸν γὰρ καὶ λευκὸν ἅμα τῷ αὐτῷ οὐχ ὑπάρχει· διὸ ἐξ ὧν ἐστὶν ἀντίκειται. ἐναντία λέγεται τά τε μὴ δυνατὰ ἅμα τῷ αὐτῷ παρεῖναι τῶν διαφερόντων κατὰ γένος, καὶ τὰ πλεῖστον διαφέροντα τῶν ἐν τῷ αὐτῷ γένει, καὶ τὰ πλεῖστον διαφέροντα τῶν ἐν ταὐτῷ δεκτικῷ, καὶ τὰ πλεῖστον διαφέροντα τῶν ὑπὸ τὴν αὐτὴν δύναμιν, καὶ ὧν ἡ διαφορὰ μεγίστη ἢ ἁπλῶς ἢ κατὰ γένος ἢ κατʼ εἶδος. τὰ δʼ ἄλλα ἐναντία λέγεται τὰ μὲν τῷ τὰ τοιαῦτα ἔχειν, τὰ δὲ τῷ δεκτικὰ εἶναι τῶν τοιούτων, τὰ δὲ τῷ ποιητικὰ ἢ παθητικὰ εἶναι τῶν τοιούτων, ἢ ποιοῦντα ἢ πάσχοντα, ἢ ἀποβολαὶ ἢ λήψεις, ἢ ἕξεις ἢ στερήσεις εἶναι τῶν τοιούτων. ἐπεὶ δὲ τὸ ἓν καὶ τὸ ὂν πολλαχῶς λέγεται, ἀκολουθεῖν ἀνάγκη καὶ τἆλλα ὅσα κατὰ ταῦτα λέγεται, ὥστε καὶ τὸ ταὐτὸν καὶ τὸ ἕτερον καὶ τὸ ἐναντίον, ὥστʼ εἶναι ἕτερον καθʼ ἑκάστην κατηγορίαν.—ἕτερα δὲ τῷ εἴδει λέγεται ὅσα τε ταὐτοῦ γένους ὄντα μὴ ὑπάλληλά ἐστι,  καὶ ὅσα ἐν τῷ αὐτῷ γένει ὄντα διαφορὰν ἔχει, καὶ ὅσα ἐν τῇ οὐσίᾳ ἐναντίωσιν ἔχει· καὶ τὰ ἐναντία ἕτερα τῷ εἴδει ἀλλήλων ἢ πάντα ἢ τὰ λεγόμενα πρώτως, καὶ ὅσων ἐν τῷ τελευταίῳ τοῦ γένους εἴδει οἱ λόγοι ἕτεροι (οἷον ἄνθρωπος καὶ ἵππος ἄτομα τῷ γένει οἱ δὲ λόγοι ἕτεροι αὐτῶν), καὶ ὅσα ἐν τῇ αὐτῇ οὐσίᾳ ὄντα ἔχει διαφοράν. ταὐτὰ δὲ τῷ εἴδει τὰ ἀντικειμένως λεγόμενα τούτοις.\n\n\n\n \n\n \n\nπρότερα καὶ ὕστερα λέγεται ἔνια μέν, ὡς ὄντος τινὸς πρώτου καὶ ἀρχῆς ἐν ἑκάστῳ γένει, τῷ ἐγγύτερον εἶναι ἀρχῆς τινὸς ὡρισμένης ἢ ἁπλῶς καὶ τῇ φύσει ἢ πρός τι ἢ ποὺ ἢ ὑπό τινων, οἷον τὰ μὲν κατὰ τόπον τῷ εἶναι ἐγγύτερον ἢ φύσει τινὸς τόπου ὡρισμένου (οἷον τοῦ μέσου ἢ τοῦ ἐσχάτου) ἢ πρὸς τὸ τυχόν, τὸ δὲ πορρώτερον ὕστερον· τὰ δὲ κατὰ χρόνον (τὰ μὲν γὰρ τῷ πορρώτερον τοῦ νῦν, οἷον ἐπὶ τῶν γενομένων, πρότερον γὰρ τὰ Τρωϊκὰ τῶν Μηδικῶν ὅτι πορρώτερον ἀπέχει τοῦ νῦν· τὰ δὲ τῷ ἐγγύτερον τοῦ νῦν, οἷον ἐπὶ τῶν μελλόντων, πρότερον γὰρ Νέμεα Πυθίων ὅτι ἐγγύτερον τοῦ νῦν τῷ νῦν ὡς ἀρχῇ καὶ πρώτῳ χρησαμένων)· τὰ δὲ κατὰ κίνησιν (τὸ γὰρ ἐγγύτερον τοῦ πρώτου κινήσαντος πρότερον, οἷον παῖς ἀνδρός· ἀρχὴ δὲ καὶ αὕτη τις ἁπλῶς)· τὰ δὲ κατὰ δύναμιν (τὸ γὰρ ὑπερέχον τῇ δυνάμει πρότερον, καὶ τὸ δυνατώτερον· τοιοῦτον δʼ ἐστὶν οὗ κατὰ τὴν προαίρεσιν ἀνάγκη ἀκολουθεῖν θάτερον καὶ τὸ ὕστερον, ὥστε μὴ κινοῦντός τε ἐκείνου μὴ κινεῖσθαι καὶ κινοῦντος κινεῖσθαι· ἡ δὲ προαίρεσις ἀρχή)· τὰ δὲ κατὰ τάξιν (ταῦτα δʼ ἐστὶν ὅσα πρός τι ἓν ὡρισμένον διέστηκε κατά τινα λόγον, οἷον παραστάτης τριτοστάτου πρότερον καὶ παρανήτη νήτης· ἔνθα μὲν γὰρ ὁ κορυφαῖος ἔνθα δὲ ἡ μέση ἀρχή)·—ταῦτα μὲν οὖν πρότερα τοῦτον λέγεται τὸν τρόπον, ἄλλον δὲ τρόπον τὸ τῇ γνώσει πρότερον ὡς καὶ ἁπλῶς πρότερον. τούτων δὲ ἄλλως τὰ κατὰ τὸν λόγον καὶ τὰ κατὰ τὴν αἴσθησιν. κατὰ μὲν γὰρ τὸν λόγον τὰ καθόλου πρότερα κατὰ δὲ τὴν αἴσθησιν τὰ καθʼ ἕκαστα· καὶ κατὰ τὸν λόγον δὲ τὸ συμβεβηκὸς τοῦ ὅλου πρότερον, οἷον τὸ μουσικὸν τοῦ μουσικοῦ ἀνθρώπου· οὐ γὰρ ἔσται ὁ λόγος ὅλος ἄνευ τοῦ μέρους· καίτοι οὐκ ἐνδέχεται μουσικὸν εἶναι μὴ ὄντος μουσικοῦ τινός. ἔτι πρότερα λέγεται τὰ τῶν προτέρων πάθη, οἷον εὐθύτης λειότητος· τὸ μὲν γὰρ γραμμῆς καθʼ αὑτὴν πάθος τὸ δὲ ἐπιφανείας.  τὰ μὲν δὴ οὕτω λέγεται πρότερα καὶ ὕστερα, τὰ δὲ κατὰ φύσιν καὶ οὐσίαν, ὅσα ἐνδέχεται εἶναι ἄνευ ἄλλων, ἐκεῖνα δὲ ἄνευ ἐκείνων μή· ᾗ διαιρέσει ἐχρήσατο Πλάτων. (ἐπεὶ δὲ τὸ εἶναι πολλαχῶς, πρῶτον μὲν τὸ ὑποκείμενον πρότερον, διὸ ἡ οὐσία πρότερον, ἔπειτα ἄλλως τὰ κατὰ δύναμιν καὶ κατʼ ἐντελέχειαν· τὰ μὲν γὰρ κατὰ δύναμιν πρότερά ἐστι τὰ δὲ κατὰ ἐντελέχειαν, οἷον κατὰ δύναμιν μὲν ἡ ἡμίσεια τῆς ὅλης καὶ τὸ μόριον τοῦ ὅλου καὶ ἡ ὕλη τῆς οὐσίας, κατʼ ἐντελέχειαν δʼ ὕστερον· διαλυθέντος γὰρ κατʼ ἐντελέχειαν ἔσται.) τρόπον δή τινα πάντα τὰ πρότερον καὶ ὕστερον λεγόμενα κατὰ ταῦτα λέγεται· τὰ μὲν γὰρ κατὰ γένεσιν ἐνδέχεται ἄνευ τῶν ἑτέρων εἶναι, οἷον τὸ ὅλον τῶν μορίων, τὰ δὲ κατὰ φθοράν, οἷον τὸ μόριον τοῦ ὅλου. ὁμοίως δὲ καὶ τἆλλα.\n\n\n\n \n\n \n\nδύναμις λέγεται ἡ μὲν ἀρχὴ κινήσεως ἢ μεταβολῆς ἡ ἐν ἑτέρῳ ἢ ᾗ ἕτερον, οἷον ἡ οἰκοδομικὴ δύναμίς ἐστιν ἣ οὐχ ὑπάρχει ἐν τῷ οἰκοδομουμένῳ, ἀλλʼ ἡ ἰατρικὴ δύναμις οὖσα ὑπάρχοι ἂν ἐν τῷ ἰατρευομένῳ, ἀλλʼ οὐχ ᾗ ἰατρευόμενος. ἡ μὲν οὖν ὅλως ἀρχὴ μεταβολῆς ἢ κινήσεως λέγεται δύναμις ἐν ἑτέρῳ ἢ ᾗ ἕτερον, ἡ δʼ ὑφʼ ἑτέρου ἢ ᾗ ἕτερον (καθʼ ἣν γὰρ τὸ πάσχον πάσχει τι, ὁτὲ μὲν ἐὰν ὁτιοῦν, δυνατὸν αὐτό φαμεν εἶναι παθεῖν, ὁτὲ δʼ οὐ κατὰ πᾶν πάθος ἀλλʼ ἂν ἐπὶ τὸ βέλτιον)· ἔτι ἡ τοῦ καλῶς τοῦτʼ ἐπιτελεῖν ἢ κατὰ προαίρεσιν· ἐνίοτε γὰρ τοὺς μόνον ἂν πορευθέντας ἢ εἰπόντας, μὴ καλῶς δὲ ἢ μὴ ὡς προείλοντο, οὔ φαμεν δύνασθαι λέγειν ἢ βαδίζειν· ὁμοίως δὲ καὶ ἐπὶ τοῦ πάσχειν. ἔτι ὅσαι ἕξεις καθʼ ἃς ἀπαθῆ ὅλως ἢ ἀμετάβλητα ἢ μὴ ῥᾳδίως ἐπὶ τὸ χεῖρον εὐμετακίνητα, δυνάμεις λέγονται· κλᾶται μὲν γὰρ καὶ συντρίβεται καὶ κάμπτεται καὶ ὅλως φθείρεται οὐ τῷ δύνασθαι ἀλλὰ τῷ μὴ δύνασθαι καὶ ἐλλείπειν τινός· ἀπαθῆ δὲ τῶν τοιούτων ἃ μόλις καὶ ἠρέμα πάσχει διὰ δύναμιν καὶ τῷ δύνασθαι καὶ τῷ ἔχειν πώς. λεγομένης δὲ τῆς δυνάμεως τοσαυταχῶς, καὶ τὸ δυνατὸν ἕνα μὲν τρόπον λεχθήσεται τὸ ἔχον κινήσεως ἀρχὴν ἢ μεταβολῆς (καὶ γὰρ τὸ στατικὸν δυνατόν τι) ἐν ἑτέρῳ ἢ ᾗ ἕτερον, ἕνα δʼ ἐὰν ἔχῃ τι αὐτοῦ ἄλλο δύναμιν τοιαύτην,  ἕνα δʼ ἐὰν ἔχῃ μεταβάλλειν ἐφʼ ὁτιοῦν δύναμιν, εἴτʼ ἐπὶ τὸ χεῖρον εἴτʼ ἐπὶ τὸ βέλτιον (καὶ γὰρ τὸ φθειρόμενον δοκεῖ δυνατὸν εἶναι φθείρεσθαι, ἢ οὐκ ἂν φθαρῆναι εἰ ἦν ἀδύνατον· νῦν δὲ ἔχει τινὰ διάθεσιν καὶ αἰτίαν καὶ ἀρχὴν τοῦ τοιούτου πάθους· ὁτὲ μὲν δὴ τῷ ἔχειν τι δοκεῖ, ὁτὲ δὲ τῷ ἐστερῆσθαι τοιοῦτον εἶναι· εἰ δʼ ἡ στέρησίς ἐστιν ἕξις πως, πάντα τῷ ἔχειν ἂν εἴη τι,  ὥστε τῷ τε ἔχειν ἕξιν τινὰ καὶ ἀρχήν ἐστι δυνατὸν  καὶ τῷ ἔχειν τὴν τούτου στέρησιν, εἰ ἐνδέχεται ἔχειν στέρησιν· εἰ δὲ μή, ὁμωνύμως)· ἕνα δὲ τῷ μὴ ἔχειν αὐτοῦ δύναμιν ἢ ἀρχὴν ἄλλο ἢ ᾗ ἄλλο φθαρτικήν. ἔτι δὲ ταῦτα πάντα ἢ τῷ μόνον ἂν συμβῆναι γενέσθαι ἢ μὴ γενέσθαι, ἢ τῷ καλῶς. καὶ γὰρ ἐν τοῖς ἀψύχοις ἔνεστιν ἡ τοιαύτη δύναμις, οἷον ἐν τοῖς ὀργάνοις· τὴν μὲν γὰρ δύνασθαί φασι φθέγγεσθαι λύραν, τὴν δʼ οὐδέν, ἂν ᾖ μὴ εὔφωνος. ἀδυναμία δὲ ἐστὶ στέρησις δυνάμεως καὶ τῆς τοιαύτης ἀρχῆς οἵα εἴρηται, ἢ ὅλως ἢ τῷ πεφυκότι ἔχειν, ἢ καὶ ὅτε πέφυκεν ἤδη ἔχειν· οὐ γὰρ ὁμοίως ἂν φαῖεν ἀδύνατον εἶναι γεννᾶν παῖδα καὶ ἄνδρα καὶ εὐνοῦχον. ἔτι δὲ καθʼ ἑκατέραν δύναμιν ἔστιν ἀδυναμία ἀντικειμένη, τῇ τε μόνον κινητικῇ καὶ τῇ καλῶς κινητικῇ. καὶ ἀδύνατα δὴ τὰ μὲν κατὰ τὴν ἀδυναμίαν ταύτην λέγεται, τὰ δὲ ἄλλον τρόπον, οἷον δυνατόν τε καὶ ἀδύνατον, ἀδύνατον μὲν οὗ τὸ ἐναντίον ἐξ ἀνάγκης ἀληθές (οἷον τὸ τὴν διάμετρον σύμμετρον εἶναι ἀδύνατον ὅτι ψεῦδος τὸ τοιοῦτον οὗ τὸ ἐναντίον οὐ μόνον ἀληθὲς ἀλλὰ καὶ ἀνάγκη · τὸ ἄρα σύμμετρον οὐ μόνον ψεῦδος ἀλλὰ καὶ ἐξ ἀνάγκης ψεῦδος)· τὸ δʼ ἐναντίον τούτῳ, τὸ δυνατόν, ὅταν μὴ ἀναγκαῖον ᾖ τὸ ἐναντίον ψεῦδος εἶναι, οἷον τὸ καθῆσθαι ἄνθρωπον δυνατόν· οὐ γὰρ ἐξ ἀνάγκης τὸ μὴ καθῆσθαι ψεῦδος. τὸ μὲν οὖν δυνατὸν ἕνα μὲν τρόπον, ὥσπερ εἴρηται, τὸ μὴ ἐξ ἀνάγκης ψεῦδος σημαίνει, ἕνα δὲ τὸ ἀληθές , ἕνα δὲ τὸ ἐνδεχόμενον ἀληθὲς εἶναι. κατὰ μεταφορὰν δὲ ἡ ἐν γεωμετρίᾳ λέγεται δύναμις. ταῦτα μὲν οὖν τὰ δυνατὰ οὐ κατὰ δύναμιν· τὰ δὲ λεγόμενα κατὰ δύναμιν πάντα λέγεται πρὸς τὴν πρώτην ·  αὕτη δʼ ἐστὶν ἀρχὴ μεταβολῆς ἐν ἄλλῳ ἢ ᾗ ἄλλο. τὰ γὰρ ἄλλα λέγεται δυνατὰ τῷ τὰ μὲν ἔχειν αὐτῶν ἄλλο τι τοιαύτην δύναμιν τὰ δὲ μὴ ἔχειν τὰ δὲ ὡδὶ ἔχειν. ὁμοίως δὲ καὶ τὰ ἀδύνατα. ὥστε ὁ κύριος ὅρος τῆς πρώτης δυνάμεως ἂν εἴη ἀρχὴ μεταβλητικὴ ἐν ἄλλῳ ἢ ᾗ ἄλλο. \n\n\n\n \n\n \n\nποσὸν λέγεται τὸ διαιρετὸν εἰς ἐνυπάρχοντα ὧν ἑκάτερον ἢ ἕκαστον ἕν τι καὶ τόδε τι πέφυκεν εἶναι. πλῆθος μὲν οὖν ποσόν τι ἐὰν ἀριθμητὸν ᾖ, μέγεθος δὲ ἂν μετρητὸν ᾖ. λέγεται δὲ πλῆθος μὲν τὸ διαιρετὸν δυνάμει εἰς μὴ συνεχῆ, μέγεθος δὲ τὸ εἰς συνεχῆ· μεγέθους δὲ τὸ μὲν ἐφʼ ἓν συνεχὲς μῆκος τὸ δʼ ἐπὶ δύο πλάτος τὸ δʼ ἐπὶ τρία βάθος. τούτων δὲ πλῆθος μὲν τὸ πεπερασμένον ἀριθμὸς μῆκος δὲ γραμμὴ πλάτος δὲ ἐπιφάνεια βάθος δὲ σῶμα. ἔτι τὰ μὲν λέγεται καθʼ αὑτὰ ποσά, τὰ δὲ κατὰ συμβεβηκός, οἷον ἡ μὲν γραμμὴ ποσόν τι καθʼ ἑαυτό, τὸ δὲ μουσικὸν κατὰ συμβεβηκός. τῶν δὲ καθʼ αὑτὰ τὰ μὲν κατʼ οὐσίαν ἐστίν, οἷον ἡ γραμμὴ ποσόν τι (ἐν γὰρ τῷ λόγῳ τῷ τί ἐστι λέγοντι τὸ ποσόν τι ὑπάρχει), τὰ δὲ πάθη καὶ ἕξεις τῆς τοιαύτης ἐστὶν οὐσίας, οἷον τὸ πολὺ καὶ τὸ ὀλίγον, καὶ μακρὸν καὶ βραχύ, καὶ πλατὺ καὶ στενόν, καὶ βαθὺ καὶ ταπεινόν, καὶ βαρὺ καὶ κοῦφον, καὶ τὰ ἄλλα τὰ τοιαῦτα. ἔστι δὲ καὶ τὸ μέγα καὶ τὸ μικρὸν καὶ μεῖζον καὶ ἔλαττον, καὶ καθʼ αὑτὰ καὶ πρὸς ἄλληλα λεγόμενα, τοῦ ποσοῦ πάθη καθʼ αὑτά· μεταφέρονται μέντοι καὶ ἐπʼ ἄλλα ταῦτα τὰ ὀνόματα. τῶν δὲ κατὰ συμβεβηκὸς λεγομένων ποσῶν τὰ μὲν οὕτως λέγεται ὥσπερ ἐλέχθη ὅτι τὸ μουσικὸν ποσὸν καὶ τὸ λευκὸν τῷ εἶναι ποσόν τι ᾧ ὑπάρχουσι, τὰ δὲ ὡς κίνησις καὶ χρόνος· καὶ γὰρ ταῦτα πόσʼ ἄττα λέγεται καὶ συνεχῆ τῷ ἐκεῖνα διαιρετὰ εἶναι ὧν ἐστὶ ταῦτα πάθη. λέγω δὲ οὐ τὸ κινούμενον ἀλλʼ ὃ ἐκινήθη· τῷ γὰρ ποσὸν εἶναι ἐκεῖνο καὶ ἡ κίνησις ποσή, ὁ δὲ χρόνος τῷ ταύτην. \n\n\n\n \n\n \n\n ποιὸν λέγεται ἕνα μὲν τρόπον ἡ διαφορὰ τῆς οὐσίας, οἷον ποιόν τι ἄνθρωπος ζῷον ὅτι δίπουν, ἵππος δὲ τετράπουν, καὶ κύκλος ποιόν τι σχῆμα ὅτι ἀγώνιον, ὡς τῆς διαφορᾶς τῆς κατὰ τὴν οὐσίαν ποιότητος οὔσης·  —ἕνα μὲν δὴ τρόπον τοῦτον λέγεται ἡ ποιότης διαφορὰ οὐσίας, ἕνα δὲ ὡς τὰ ἀκίνητα καὶ τὰ μαθηματικά, ὥσπερ οἱ ἀριθμοὶ ποιοί τινες, οἷον οἱ σύνθετοι καὶ μὴ μόνον ἐφʼ ἓν ὄντες ἀλλʼ ὧν μίμημα τὸ ἐπίπεδον καὶ τὸ στερεόν (οὗτοι δʼ εἰσὶν οἱ ποσάκις ποσοὶ ἢ ποσάκις ποσάκις ποσοί), καὶ ὅλως ὃ παρὰ τὸ ποσὸν ὑπάρχει ἐν τῇ οὐσίᾳ· οὐσία γὰρ ἑκάστου ὃ ἅπαξ, οἷον τῶν ἓξ οὐχ ὃ δὶς ἢ τρὶς εἰσὶν ἀλλʼ ὃ ἅπαξ· ἓξ γὰρ ἅπαξ ἕξ. ἔτι ὅσα πάθη τῶν κινουμένων οὐσιῶν, οἷον θερμότης καὶ ψυχρότης, καὶ λευκότης καὶ μελανία, καὶ βαρύτης καὶ κουφότης, καὶ ὅσα τοιαῦτα, καθʼ ἃ λέγονται καὶ ἀλλοιοῦσθαι τὰ σώματα μεταβαλλόντων. ἔτι κατʼ ἀρετὴν καὶ κακίαν καὶ ὅλως τὸ κακὸν καὶ ἀγαθόν. σχεδὸν δὴ κατὰ δύο τρόπους λέγοιτʼ ἂν τὸ ποιόν, καὶ τούτων ἕνα τὸν κυριώτατον· πρώτη μὲν γὰρ ποιότης ἡ τῆς οὐσίας διαφορά (ταύτης δέ τι καὶ ἡ ἐν τοῖς ἀριθμοῖς ποιότης μέρος· διαφορὰ γάρ τις οὐσιῶν, ἀλλʼ ἢ οὐ κινουμένων ἢ οὐχ ᾗ κινούμενα), τὰ δὲ πάθη τῶν κινουμένων ᾗ κινούμενα, καὶ αἱ τῶν κινήσεων διαφοραί. ἀρετὴ δὲ καὶ κακία τῶν παθημάτων μέρος τι· διαφορὰς γὰρ δηλοῦσι τῆς κινήσεως καὶ τῆς ἐνεργείας, καθʼ ἃς ποιοῦσιν ἢ πάσχουσι καλῶς ἢ φαύλως τὰ ἐν κινήσει ὄντα· τὸ μὲν γὰρ ὡδὶ δυνάμενον κινεῖσθαι ἢ ἐνεργεῖν ἀγαθὸν τὸ δʼ ὡδὶ καὶ ἐναντίως μοχθηρόν. μάλιστα δὲ τὸ ἀγαθὸν καὶ τὸ κακὸν σημαίνει τὸ ποιὸν ἐπὶ τῶν ἐμψύχων, καὶ τούτων μάλιστα ἐπὶ τοῖς ἔχουσι προαίρεσιν. \n\n\n\n \n\n \n\nπρός τι λέγεται τὰ μὲν ὡς διπλάσιον πρὸς ἥμισυ καὶ τριπλάσιον πρὸς τριτημόριον, καὶ ὅλως πολλαπλάσιον πρὸς πολλοστημόριον καὶ ὑπερέχον πρὸς ὑπερεχόμενον· τὰ δʼ ὡς τὸ θερμαντικὸν πρὸς τὸ θερμαντὸν καὶ τὸ τμητικὸν πρὸς τὸ τμητόν, καὶ ὅλως τὸ ποιητικὸν πρὸς τὸ παθητικόν· τὰ δʼ ὡς τὸ μετρητὸν πρὸς τὸ μέτρον καὶ ἐπιστητὸν πρὸς ἐπιστήμην καὶ αἰσθητὸν πρὸς αἴσθησιν. λέγεται δὲ τὰ μὲν πρῶτα κατʼ ἀριθμὸν ἢ ἁπλῶς ἢ ὡρισμένως, πρὸς αὐτοὺς ἢ πρὸς ἕν (οἷον τὸ μὲν διπλάσιον πρὸς ἓν ἀριθμὸς ὡρισμένος, τὸ δὲ πολλαπλάσιον κατʼ ἀριθμὸν πρὸς ἕν, οὐχ ὡρισμένον δέ, οἷον τόνδε ἢ τόνδε·  τὸ δὲ ἡμιόλιον πρὸς τὸ ὑφημιόλιον κατʼ ἀριθμὸν πρὸς ἀριθμὸν ὡρισμένον· τὸ δʼ ἐπιμόριον πρὸς τὸ ὑπεπιμόριον κατὰ ἀόριστον, ὥσπερ τὸ πολλαπλάσιον πρὸς τὸ ἕν· τὸ δʼ ὑπερέχον πρὸς τὸ ὑπερεχόμενον ὅλως ἀόριστον κατʼ ἀριθμόν· ὁ γὰρ ἀριθμὸς σύμμετρος, κατὰ μὴ συμμέτρου δὲ ἀριθμὸς οὐ λέγεται, τὸ δὲ ὑπερέχον πρὸς τὸ ὑπερεχόμενον τοσοῦτόν τέ ἐστι καὶ ἔτι, τοῦτο δʼ ἀόριστον· ὁπότερον γὰρ ἔτυχέν ἐστιν, ἢ ἴσον ἢ οὐκ ἴσον)· ταῦτά τε οὖν τὰ πρός τι πάντα κατʼ ἀριθμὸν λέγεται καὶ ἀριθμοῦ πάθη, καὶ ἔτι τὸ ἴσον καὶ ὅμοιον καὶ ταὐτὸ κατʼ ἄλλον τρόπον (κατὰ γὰρ τὸ ἓν λέγεται πάντα, ταὐτὰ μὲν γὰρ ὧν μία ἡ οὐσία, ὅμοια δʼ ὧν ἡ ποιότης μία, ἴσα δὲ ὧν τὸ ποσὸν ἕν· τὸ δʼ ἓν τοῦ ἀριθμοῦ ἀρχὴ καὶ μέτρον, ὥστε ταῦτα πάντα πρός τι λέγεται κατʼ ἀριθμὸν μέν, οὐ τὸν αὐτὸν δὲ τρόπον)· τὰ δὲ ποιητικὰ καὶ παθητικὰ κατὰ δύναμιν ποιητικὴν καὶ παθητικὴν καὶ ἐνεργείας τὰς τῶν δυνάμεων, οἷον τὸ θερμαντικὸν πρὸς τὸ θερμαντὸν ὅτι δύναται, καὶ πάλιν τὸ θερμαῖνον πρὸς τὸ θερμαινόμενον καὶ τὸ τέμνον πρὸς τὸ τεμνόμενον ὡς ἐνεργοῦντα. τῶν δὲ κατʼ ἀριθμὸν οὐκ εἰσὶν ἐνέργειαι ἀλλʼ ἢ ὃν τρόπον ἐν ἑτέροις εἴρηται· αἱ δὲ κατὰ κίνησιν ἐνέργειαι οὐχ ὑπάρχουσιν. τῶν δὲ κατὰ δύναμιν καὶ κατὰ χρόνους ἤδη λέγονται πρός τι οἷον τὸ πεποιηκὸς πρὸς τὸ πεποιημένον καὶ τὸ ποιῆσον πρὸς τὸ ποιησόμενον. οὕτω γὰρ καὶ πατὴρ υἱοῦ λέγεται πατήρ· τὸ μὲν γὰρ πεποιηκὸς τὸ δὲ πεπονθός τί ἐστιν. ἔτι ἔνια κατὰ στέρησιν δυνάμεως, ὥσπερ τὸ ἀδύνατον καὶ ὅσα οὕτω λέγεται, οἷον τὸ ἀόρατον. τὰ μὲν οὖν κατʼ ἀριθμὸν καὶ δύναμιν λεγόμενα πρός τι πάντα ἐστὶ πρός τι τῷ ὅπερ ἐστὶν ἄλλου λέγεσθαι αὐτὸ ὅ ἐστιν, ἀλλὰ μὴ τῷ ἄλλο πρὸς ἐκεῖνο· τὸ δὲ μετρητὸν καὶ τὸ ἐπιστητὸν καὶ τὸ διανοητὸν τῷ ἄλλο πρὸς αὐτὸ λέγεσθαι πρός τι λέγονται. τό τε γὰρ διανοητὸν σημαίνει ὅτι ἔστιν αὐτοῦ διάνοια, οὐκ ἔστι δʼ ἡ διάνοια πρὸς τοῦτο οὗ ἐστὶ διάνοια (δὶς γὰρ ταὐτὸν εἰρημένον ἂν εἴη), ὁμοίως δὲ καὶ τινός ἐστιν ἡ ὄψις ὄψις, οὐχ οὗ ἐστὶν ὄψις (καίτοι γʼ ἀληθὲς τοῦτο εἰπεῖν) ἀλλὰ πρὸς χρῶμα ἢ πρὸς ἄλλο τι τοιοῦτον. ἐκείνως δὲ δὶς τὸ αὐτὸ λεχθήσεται, ὅτι ἐστὶν οὗ ἐστὶν ἡ ὄψις.  τὰ μὲν οὖν καθʼ ἑαυτὰ λεγόμενα πρός τι τὰ μὲν οὕτω λέγεται, τὰ δὲ ἂν τὰ γένη αὐτῶν ᾖ τοιαῦτα, οἷον ἡ ἰατρικὴ τῶν πρός τι ὅτι τὸ γένος αὐτῆς ἡ ἐπιστήμη δοκεῖ εἶναι πρός τι· ἔτι καθʼ ὅσα τὰ ἔχοντα λέγεται πρός τι, οἷον ἰσότης ὅτι τὸ ἴσον καὶ ὁμοιότης ὅτι τὸ ὅμοιον· τὰ δὲ κατὰ συμβεβηκός, οἷον ἅνθρωπος πρός τι ὅτι συμβέβηκεν αὐτῷ διπλασίῳ εἶναι, τοῦτο δʼ ἐστὶ τῶν πρός τι· ἢ τὸ λευκόν, εἰ τῷ αὐτῷ συμβέβηκε διπλασίῳ καὶ λευκῷ εἶναι.\n\n\n\n \n\n \n\nτέλειον λέγεται ἓν μὲν οὗ μὴ ἔστιν ἔξω τι λαβεῖν μηδὲ ἓν μόριον (οἷον χρόνος τέλειος ἑκάστου οὗτος οὗ μὴ ἔστιν ἔξω λαβεῖν χρόνον τινὰ ὃς τούτου μέρος ἐστὶ τοῦ χρόνου), καὶ τὸ κατʼ ἀρετὴν καὶ τὸ εὖ μὴ ἔχον ὑπερβολὴν πρὸς τὸ γένος, οἷον τέλειος ἰατρὸς καὶ τέλειος αὐλητὴς ὅταν κατὰ τὸ εἶδος τῆς οἰκείας ἀρετῆς μηθὲν ἐλλείπωσιν (οὕτω δὲ μεταφέροντες καὶ ἐπὶ τῶν κακῶν λέγομεν συκοφάντην τέλειον καὶ κλέπτην τέλειον, ἐπειδὴ καὶ ἀγαθοὺς λέγομεν αὐτούς, οἷον κλέπτην ἀγαθὸν καὶ συκοφάντην ἀγαθόν· καὶ ἡ ἀρετὴ τελείωσίς τις· ἕκαστον γὰρ τότε τέλειον καὶ οὐσία πᾶσα τότε τελεία, ὅταν κατὰ τὸ εἶδος τῆς οἰκείας ἀρετῆς μηδὲν ἐλλείπῃ μόριον τοῦ κατὰ φύσιν μεγέθους)· ἔτι οἷς ὑπάρχει τὸ τέλος, σπουδαῖον ὄν, ταῦτα λέγεται τέλεια· κατὰ γὰρ τὸ ἔχειν τὸ τέλος τέλεια, ὥστʼ ἐπεὶ τὸ τέλος τῶν ἐσχάτων τί ἐστι, καὶ ἐπὶ τὰ φαῦλα μεταφέροντες λέγομεν τελείως ἀπολωλέναι καὶ τελείως ἐφθάρθαι, ὅταν μηδὲν ἐλλείπῃ τῆς φθορᾶς καὶ τοῦ κακοῦ ἀλλʼ ἐπὶ τῷ ἐσχάτῳ ᾖ· διὸ καὶ ἡ τελευτὴ κατὰ μεταφορὰν λέγεται τέλος, ὅτι ἄμφω ἔσχατα· τέλος δὲ καὶ τὸ οὗ ἕνεκα ἔσχατον. τὰ μὲν οὖν καθʼ αὑτὰ λεγόμενα τέλεια τοσαυταχῶς λέγεται, τὰ μὲν τῷ κατὰ τὸ εὖ μηδὲν ἐλλείπειν μηδʼ ἔχειν ὑπερβολὴν μηδὲ ἔξω τι λαβεῖν, τὰ δʼ ὅλως κατὰ τὸ μὴ ἔχειν ὑπερβολὴν ἐν ἑκάστῳ γένει μηδʼ εἶναί τι ἔξω·  τὰ δὲ ἄλλα ἤδη κατὰ ταῦτα τῷ ἢ ποιεῖν τι τοιοῦτον ἢ ἔχειν ἢ ἁρμόττειν τούτῳ ἢ ἁμῶς γέ πως λέγεσθαι πρὸς τὰ πρώτως λεγόμενα τέλεια.\n\n\n\n \n\n \n\nπέρας λέγεται τό τε ἔσχατον ἑκάστου καὶ οὗ ἔξω μηδὲν ἔστι λαβεῖν πρώτου καὶ οὗ ἔσω πάντα πρώτου, καὶ ὃ ἂν ᾖ εἶδος μεγέθους ἢ ἔχοντος μέγεθος, καὶ τὸ τέλος ἑκάστου (τοιοῦτον δʼ ἐφʼ ὃ ἡ κίνησις καὶ ἡ πρᾶξις, καὶ οὐκ ἀφʼ οὗ—ὁτὲ δὲ ἄμφω, καὶ ἀφʼ οὗ καὶ ἐφʼ ὃ καὶ τὸ οὗ ἕνεκα), καὶ ἡ οὐσία ἡ ἑκάστου καὶ τὸ τί ἦν εἶναι ἑκάστῳ· τῆς γνώσεως γὰρ τοῦτο πέρας· εἰ δὲ τῆς γνώσεως, καὶ τοῦ πράγματος. ὥστε φανερὸν ὅτι ὁσαχῶς τε ἡ ἀρχὴ λέγεται, τοσαυταχῶς καὶ τὸ πέρας, καὶ ἔτι πλεοναχῶς· ἡ μὲν γὰρ ἀρχὴ πέρας τι, τὸ δὲ πέρας οὐ πᾶν ἀρχή. \n\n\n\n \n\n \n\nτὸ καθʼ ὃ λέγεται πολλαχῶς, ἕνα μὲν τρόπον τὸ εἶδος καὶ ἡ οὐσία ἑκάστου πράγματος, οἷον καθʼ ὃ ἀγαθός, αὐτὸ ἀγαθόν, ἕνα δὲ ἐν ᾧ πρώτῳ πέφυκε γίγνεσθαι, οἷον τὸ χρῶμα ἐν τῇ ἐπιφανείᾳ. τὸ μὲν οὖν πρώτως λεγόμενον καθʼ ὃ τὸ εἶδός ἐστι, δευτέρως δὲ ὡς ἡ ὕλη ἑκάστου καὶ τὸ ὑποκείμενον ἑκάστῳ πρῶτον. ὅλως δὲ τὸ καθʼ ὃ ἰσαχῶς καὶ τὸ αἴτιον ὑπάρξει· κατὰ τί γὰρ ἐλήλυθεν ἢ οὗ ἕνεκα ἐλήλυθε λέγεται, καὶ κατὰ τί παραλελόγισται ἢ συλλελόγισται, ἢ τί τὸ αἴτιον τοῦ συλλογισμοῦ ἢ παραλογισμοῦ. ἔτι δὲ τὸ καθʼ ὃ τὸ κατὰ θέσιν λέγεται, καθʼ ὃ ἕστηκεν ἢ καθʼ ὃ βαδίζει· πάντα γὰρ ταῦτα τόπον σημαίνει καὶ θέσιν. ὥστε καὶ τὸ καθʼ αὑτὸ πολλαχῶς ἀνάγκη λέγεσθαι. ἓν μὲν γὰρ καθʼ αὑτὸ τὸ τί ἦν εἶναι ἑκάστῳ, οἷον ὁ Καλλίας καθʼ αὑτὸν Καλλίας καὶ τὸ τί ἦν εἶναι Καλλίᾳ· ἓν δὲ ὅσα ἐν τῷ τί ἐστιν ὑπάρχει, οἷον ζῷον ὁ Καλλίας καθʼ αὑτόν· ἐν γὰρ τῷ λόγῳ ἐνυπάρχει τὸ ζῷον· ζῷον γάρ τι ὁ Καλλίας. ἔτι δὲ εἰ ἐν αὑτῷ δέδεκται πρώτῳ ἢ τῶν αὑτοῦ τινί, οἷον ἡ ἐπιφάνεια λευκὴ καθʼ ἑαυτήν, καὶ ζῇ ὁ ἄνθρωπος καθʼ αὑτόν· ἡ γὰρ ψυχὴ μέρος τι τοῦ ἀνθρώπου, ἐν ᾗ πρώτῃ τὸ ζῆν. ἔτι οὗ μὴ ἔστιν ἄλλο αἴτιον· τοῦ γὰρ ἀνθρώπου πολλὰ αἴτια, τὸ ζῷον, τὸ δίπουν, ἀλλʼ ὅμως καθʼ αὑτὸν ἄνθρωπος ὁ ἄνθρωπός ἐστιν. ἔτι ὅσα μόνῳ ὑπάρχει καὶ ᾗ μόνον διʼ αὐτὸ κεχωρισμένον καθʼ αὑτό.\n\n\n\n \n\n \n\n διάθεσις λέγεται τοῦ ἔχοντος μέρη τάξις ἢ κατὰ τόπον ἢ κατὰ δύναμιν ἢ κατʼ εἶδος· θέσιν γὰρ δεῖ τινὰ εἶναι, ὥσπερ καὶ τοὔνομα δηλοῖ ἡ διάθεσις.\n\n\n\n \n\n \n\nἕξις δὲ λέγεται ἕνα μὲν τρόπον οἷον ἐνέργειά τις τοῦ ἔχοντος καὶ ἐχομένου, ὥσπερ πρᾶξίς τις ἢ κίνησις (ὅταν γὰρ τὸ μὲν ποιῇ τὸ δὲ ποιῆται, ἔστι ποίησις μεταξύ· οὕτω καὶ τοῦ ἔχοντος ἐσθῆτα καὶ τῆς ἐχομένης ἐσθῆτος ἔστι μεταξὺ ἕξις)·—ταύτην μὲν οὖν φανερὸν ὅτι οὐκ ἐνδέχεται ἔχειν ἕξιν (εἰς ἄπειρον γὰρ βαδιεῖται, εἰ τοῦ ἐχομένου ἔσται ἔχειν τὴν ἕξιν), ἄλλον δὲ τρόπον ἕξις λέγεται διάθεσις καθʼ ἣν ἢ εὖ ἢ κακῶς διάκειται τὸ διακείμενον, καὶ ἢ καθʼ αὑτὸ ἢ πρὸς ἄλλο, οἷον ἡ ὑγίεια ἕξις τις· διάθεσις γάρ ἐστι τοιαύτη. ἔτι ἕξις λέγεται ἂν ᾖ μόριον διαθέσεως τοιαύτης· διὸ καὶ ἡ τῶν μερῶν ἀρετὴ ἕξις τίς ἐστιν.\n\n\n\n \n\n \n\nπάθος λέγεται ἕνα μὲν τρόπον ποιότης καθʼ ἣν ἀλλοιοῦσθαι ἐνδέχεται, οἷον τὸ λευκὸν καὶ τὸ μέλαν, καὶ γλυκὺ καὶ πικρόν, καὶ βαρύτης καὶ κουφότης, καὶ ὅσα ἄλλα τοιαῦτα· ἕνα δὲ αἱ τούτων ἐνέργειαι καὶ ἀλλοιώσεις ἤδη. ἔτι τούτων μᾶλλον αἱ βλαβεραὶ ἀλλοιώσεις καὶ κινήσεις, καὶ μάλιστα αἱ λυπηραὶ βλάβαι. ἔτι τὰ μεγέθη τῶν συμφορῶν καὶ λυπηρῶν πάθη λέγεται. \n\n\n\n \n\n \n\nστέρησις λέγεται ἕνα μὲν τρόπον ἂν μὴ ἔχῃ τι τῶν πεφυκότων ἔχεσθαι, κἂν μὴ αὐτὸ ᾖ πεφυκὸς ἔχειν, οἷον φυτὸν ὀμμάτων ἐστερῆσθαι λέγεται· ἕνα δὲ ἂν πεφυκὸς ἔχειν, ἢ αὐτὸ ἢ τὸ γένος, μὴ ἔχῃ, οἷον ἄλλως ἄνθρωπος ὁ τυφλὸς ὄψεως ἐστέρηται καὶ ἀσπάλαξ, τὸ μὲν κατὰ τὸ γένος τὸ δὲ καθʼ αὑτό. ἔτι ἂν πεφυκὸς καὶ ὅτε πέφυκεν ἔχειν μὴ ἔχῃ· ἡ γὰρ τυφλότης στέρησίς τις, τυφλὸς δʼ οὐ κατὰ πᾶσαν ἡλικίαν, ἀλλʼ ἐν ᾗ πέφυκεν ἔχειν, ἂν μὴ ἔχῃ. ὁμοίως δὲ καὶ ἐν ᾧ ἂν ᾖ πεφυκὸς καὶ καθʼ ὃ καὶ πρὸς ὃ καὶ ὥς, ἂν μὴ ἔχῃ . ἔτι ἡ βιαία ἑκάστου ἀφαίρεσις στέρησις λέγεται. καὶ ὁσαχῶς δὲ αἱ ἀπὸ τοῦ α ἀποφάσεις λέγονται, τοσαυταχῶς καὶ αἱ στερήσεις λέγονται· ἄνισον μὲν γὰρ τῷ μὴ ἔχειν ἰσότητα πεφυκὸς λέγεται, ἀόρατον δὲ καὶ τῷ ὅλως μὴ ἔχειν χρῶμα καὶ τῷ φαύλως, καὶ ἄπουν καὶ τῷ μὴ ἔχειν ὅλως πόδας καὶ τῷ φαύλους. ἔτι καὶ τῷ μικρὸν ἔχειν, οἷον τὸ ἀπύρηνον·  τοῦτο δʼ ἐστὶ τὸ φαύλως πως ἔχειν. ἔτι τῷ μὴ ῥᾳδίως ἢ τῷ μὴ καλῶς, οἷον τὸ ἄτμητον οὐ μόνον τῷ μὴ τέμνεσθαι ἀλλὰ καὶ τῷ μὴ ῥᾳδίως ἢ μὴ καλῶς. ἔτι τῷ πάντῃ μὴ ἔχειν· τυφλὸς γὰρ οὐ λέγεται ὁ ἑτερόφθαλμος ἀλλʼ ὁ ἐν ἀμφοῖν μὴ ἔχων ὄψιν· διὸ οὐ πᾶς ἀγαθὸς ἢ κακός, ἢ δίκαιος ἢ ἄδικος, ἀλλὰ καὶ τὸ μεταξύ. \n\n\n\n \n\n \n\nτὸ ἔχειν λέγεται πολλαχῶς, ἕνα μὲν τρόπον τὸ ἄγειν κατὰ τὴν αὑτοῦ φύσιν ἢ κατὰ τὴν αὑτοῦ ὁρμήν, διὸ λέγεται πυρετός τε ἔχειν τὸν ἄνθρωπον καὶ οἱ τύραννοι τὰς πόλεις καὶ τὴν ἐσθῆτα οἱ ἀμπεχόμενοι· ἕνα δʼ ἐν ᾧ ἄν τι ὑπάρχῃ ὡς δεκτικῷ, οἷον ὁ χαλκὸς ἔχει τὸ εἶδος τοῦ ἀνδριάντος καὶ τὴν νόσον τὸ σῶμα· ἕνα δὲ ὡς τὸ περιέχον τὰ περιεχόμενα· ἐν ᾧ γάρ ἐστι περιέχοντι, ἔχεσθαι ὑπὸ τούτου λέγεται, οἷον τὸ ἀγγεῖον ἔχειν τὸ ὑγρόν φαμεν καὶ τὴν πόλιν ἀνθρώπους καὶ τὴν ναῦν ναύτας, οὕτω δὲ καὶ τὸ ὅλον ἔχειν τὰ μέρη. ἔτι τὸ κωλῦον κατὰ τὴν αὑτοῦ ὁρμήν τι κινεῖσθαι ἢ πράττειν ἔχειν λέγεται τοῦτο αὐτό, οἷον καὶ οἱ κίονες τὰ ἐπικείμενα βάρη, καὶ ὡς οἱ ποιηταὶ τὸν Ἄτλαντα ποιοῦσι τὸν οὐρανὸν ἔχειν ὡς συμπεσόντʼ ἂν ἐπὶ τὴν γῆν, ὥσπερ καὶ τῶν φυσιολόγων τινές φασιν· τοῦτον δὲ τὸν τρόπον καὶ τὸ συνέχον λέγεται ἃ συνέχει ἔχειν, ὡς διαχωρισθέντα ἂν κατὰ τὴν αὑτοῦ ὁρμὴν ἕκαστον. καὶ τὸ ἔν τινι δὲ εἶναι ὁμοτρόπως λέγεται καὶ ἑπομένως τῷ ἔχειν. \n\n\n\n \n\n \n\nτὸ ἔκ τινος εἶναι λέγεται ἕνα μὲν τρόπον ἐξ οὗ ἐστὶν ὡς ὕλης, καὶ τοῦτο διχῶς, ἢ κατὰ τὸ πρῶτον γένος ἢ κατὰ τὸ ὕστατον εἶδος, οἷον ἔστι μὲν ὡς ἅπαντα τὰ τηκτὰ ἐξ ὕδατος, ἔστι δʼ ὡς ἐκ χαλκοῦ ὁ ἀνδριάς· ἕνα δʼ ὡς ἐκ τῆς πρώτης κινησάσης ἀρχῆς (οἷον ἐκ τίνος ἡ μάχη; ἐκ λοιδορίας, ὅτι αὕτη ἀρχὴ τῆς μάχης)· ἕνα δʼ ἐκ τοῦ συνθέτου ἐκ τῆς ὕλης καὶ τῆς μορφῆς, ὥσπερ ἐκ τοῦ ὅλου τὰ μέρη καὶ ἐκ τῆς Ἰλιάδος τὸ ἔπος καὶ ἐκ τῆς οἰκίας οἱ λίθοι· τέλος μὲν γάρ ἐστιν ἡ μορφή, τέλειον δὲ τὸ ἔχον τέλος. τὰ δὲ ὡς ἐκ τοῦ μέρους τὸ εἶδος, οἷον ἅνθρωπος ἐκ τοῦ δίποδος καὶ ἡ συλλαβὴ ἐκ τοῦ στοιχείου· ἄλλως γὰρ τοῦτο καὶ ὁ ἀνδριὰς ἐκ χαλκοῦ·  ἐκ τῆς αἰσθητῆς γὰρ ὕλης ἡ συνθετὴ οὐσία, ἀλλὰ καὶ τὸ εἶδος ἐκ τῆς τοῦ εἴδους ὕλης. τὰ μὲν οὖν οὕτω λέγεται, τὰ δʼ ἐὰν κατὰ μέρος τι τούτων τις ὑπάρχῃ τῶν τρόπων, οἷον ἐκ πατρὸς καὶ μητρὸς τὸ τέκνον καὶ ἐκ γῆς τὰ φυτά, ὅτι ἔκ τινος μέρους αὐτῶν. ἕνα δὲ μεθʼ ὃ τῷ χρόνῳ, οἷον ἐξ ἡμέρας νὺξ καὶ ἐξ εὐδίας χειμών, ὅτι τοῦτο μετὰ τοῦτο· τούτων δὲ τὰ μὲν τῷ ἔχειν μεταβολὴν εἰς ἄλληλα οὕτω λέγεται, ὥσπερ καὶ τὰ νῦν εἰρημένα, τὰ δὲ τῷ κατὰ τὸν χρόνον ἐφεξῆς μόνον, οἷον ἐξ ἰσημερίας ἐγένετο ὁ πλοῦς ὅτι μετʼ ἰσημερίαν ἐγένετο, καὶ ἐκ Διονυσίων Θαργήλια ὅτι μετὰ τὰ Διονύσια. \n\n\n\n \n\n \n\nμέρος λέγεται ἕνα μὲν τρόπον εἰς ὃ διαιρεθείη ἂν τὸ ποσὸν ὁπωσοῦν (ἀεὶ γὰρ τὸ ἀφαιρούμενον τοῦ ποσοῦ ᾗ ποσὸν μέρος λέγεται ἐκείνου, οἷον τῶν τριῶν τὰ δύο μέρος λέγεταί πως), ἄλλον δὲ τρόπον τὰ καταμετροῦντα τῶν τοιούτων μόνον· διὸ τὰ δύο τῶν τριῶν ἔστι μὲν ὡς λέγεται μέρος, ἔστι δʼ ὡς οὔ. ἔτι εἰς ἃ τὸ εἶδος διαιρεθείη ἂν ἄνευ τοῦ ποσοῦ, καὶ ταῦτα μόρια λέγεται τούτου· διὸ τὰ εἴδη τοῦ γένους φασὶν εἶναι μόρια. ἔτι εἰς ἃ διαιρεῖται ἢ ἐξ ὧν σύγκειται τὸ ὅλον, ἢ τὸ εἶδος ἢ τὸ ἔχον τὸ εἶδος, οἷον τῆς σφαίρας τῆς χαλκῆς ἢ τοῦ κύβου τοῦ χαλκοῦ καὶ ὁ χαλκὸς μέρος (τοῦτο δʼ ἐστὶν ἡ ὕλη ἐν ᾗ τὸ εἶδος) καὶ ἡ γωνία μέρος. ἔτι τὰ ἐν τῷ λόγῳ τῷ δηλοῦντι ἕκαστον, καὶ ταῦτα μόρια τοῦ ὅλου· διὸ τὸ γένος τοῦ εἴδους καὶ μέρος λέγεται, ἄλλως δὲ τὸ εἶδος τοῦ γένους μέρος. \n\n\n\n \n\n \n\nὅλον λέγεται οὗ τε μηθὲν ἄπεστι μέρος ἐξ ὧν λέγεται ὅλον φύσει, καὶ τὸ περιέχον τὰ περιεχόμενα ὥστε ἕν τι εἶναι ἐκεῖνα· τοῦτο δὲ διχῶς· ἢ γὰρ ὡς ἕκαστον ἓν ἢ ὡς ἐκ τούτων τὸ ἕν. τὸ μὲν γὰρ καθόλου, καὶ τὸ ὅλως λεγόμενον  ὡς ὅλον τι ὄν, οὕτως ἐστὶ καθόλου ὡς πολλὰ περιέχον τῷ κατηγορεῖσθαι καθʼ ἑκάστου καὶ ἓν ἅπαντα εἶναι ὡς ἕκαστον, οἷον ἄνθρωπον ἵππον θεόν, διότι ἅπαντα ζῷα· τὸ δὲ συνεχὲς καὶ πεπερασμένον, ὅταν ἕν τι ἐκ πλειόνων ᾖ, ἐνυπαρχόντων μάλιστα μὲν δυνάμει, εἰ δὲ μή, ἐνεργείᾳ. τούτων δʼ αὐτῶν μᾶλλον τὰ φύσει ἢ τέχνῃ τοιαῦτα, ὥσπερ καὶ ἐπὶ τοῦ ἑνὸς ἐλέγομεν, ὡς οὔσης τῆς ὁλότητος ἑνότητός τινος.  ἔτι τοῦ ποσοῦ ἔχοντος δὲ ἀρχὴν καὶ μέσον καὶ ἔσχατον, ὅσων μὲν μὴ ποιεῖ ἡ θέσις διαφοράν, πᾶν λέγεται, ὅσων δὲ ποιεῖ, ὅλον. ὅσα δὲ ἄμφω ἐνδέχεται, καὶ ὅλα καὶ πάντα· ἔστι δὲ ταῦτα ὅσων ἡ μὲν φύσις ἡ αὐτὴ μένει τῇ μεταθέσει, ἡ δὲ μορφὴ οὔ, οἷον κηρὸς καὶ ἱμάτιον· καὶ γὰρ ὅλον καὶ πᾶν λέγεται· ἔχει γὰρ ἄμφω. ὕδωρ δὲ καὶ ὅσα ὑγρὰ καὶ ἀριθμὸς πᾶν μὲν λέγεται, ὅλος δʼ ἀριθμὸς καὶ ὅλον ὕδωρ οὐ λέγεται, ἂν μὴ μεταφορᾷ. πάντα δὲ λέγεται ἐφʼ οἷς τὸ πᾶν ὡς ἐφʼ ἑνί, ἐπὶ τούτοις τὸ πάντα ὡς ἐπὶ διῃρημένοις· πᾶς οὗτος ὁ ἀριθμός, πᾶσαι αὗται αἱ μονάδες.\n\n\n\n \n\n \n\nκολοβὸν δὲ λέγεται τῶν ποσῶν οὐ τὸ τυχόν, ἀλλὰ μεριστόν τε δεῖ αὐτὸ εἶναι καὶ ὅλον. τά τε γὰρ δύο οὐ κολοβὰ θατέρου ἀφαιρουμένου ἑνός (οὐ γὰρ ἴσον τὸ καλόβωμα καὶ τὸ λοιπὸν οὐδέποτʼ ἐστίν) οὐδʼ ὅλως ἀριθμὸς οὐδείς· καὶ γὰρ τὴν οὐσίαν δεῖ μένειν· εἰ κύλιξ κολοβός, ἔτι εἶναι κύλικα· ὁ δὲ ἀριθμὸς οὐκέτι ὁ αὐτός. πρὸς δὲ τούτοις κἂν ἀνομοιομερῆ ᾖ, οὐδὲ ταῦτα πάντα (ὁ γὰρ ἀριθμὸς ἔστιν ὡς καὶ ἀνόμοια ἔχει μέρη, οἷον δυάδα τριάδα), ἀλλʼ ὅλως ὧν μὴ ποιεῖ ἡ θέσις διαφορὰν οὐδὲν κολοβόν, οἷον ὕδωρ ἢ πῦρ, ἀλλὰ δεῖ τοιαῦτα εἶναι ἃ κατὰ τὴν οὐσίαν θέσιν ἔχει. ἔτι συνεχῆ· ἡ γὰρ ἁρμονία ἐξ ἀνομοίων μὲν καὶ θέσιν ἔχει, κολοβὸς δὲ οὐ γίγνεται. πρὸς δὲ τούτοις οὐδʼ ὅσα ὅλα, οὐδὲ ταῦτα ὁτουοῦν μορίου στερήσει κολοβά. οὐ γὰρ δεῖ οὔτε τὰ κύρια τῆς οὐσίας οὔτε τὰ ὁπουοῦν ὄντα· οἷον ἂν τρυπηθῇ ἡ κύλιξ, οὐ κολοβός, ἀλλʼ ἂν τὸ οὖς ἢ ἀκρωτήριόν τι, καὶ ὁ ἄνθρωπος οὐκ ἐὰν σάρκα ἢ τὸν σπλῆνα, ἀλλʼ ἐὰν ἀκρωτήριόν τι, καὶ τοῦτο οὐ πᾶν ἀλλʼ ὃ μὴ ἔχει γένεσιν ἀφαιρεθὲν ὅλον. διὰ τοῦτο οἱ φαλακροὶ οὐ κολοβοί. \n\n\n\n \n\n \n\nγένος λέγεται τὸ μὲν ἐὰν ᾖ ἡ γένεσις συνεχὴς τῶν τὸ εἶδος ἐχόντων τὸ αὐτό, οἷον λέγεται ἕως ἂν ἀνθρώπων γένος ᾖ, ὅτι ἕως ἂν ᾖ ἡ γένεσις συνεχὴς αὐτῶν· τὸ δὲ ἀφʼ οὗ ἂν ὦσι πρώτου κινήσαντος εἰς τὸ εἶναι· οὕτω γὰρ λέγονται Ἕλληνες τὸ γένος οἱ δὲ Ἴωνες, τῷ οἱ μὲν ἀπὸ Ἕλληνος οἱ δὲ ἀπὸ Ἴωνος εἶναι πρώτου γεννήσαντος· καὶ μᾶλλον οἱ ἀπὸ τοῦ γεννήσαντος ἢ τῆς ὕλης (λέγονται γὰρ καὶ ἀπὸ τοῦ θήλεος τὸ γένος, οἷον οἱ ἀπὸ Πύρρας).  ἔτι δὲ ὡς τὸ ἐπίπεδον τῶν σχημάτων γένος τῶν ἐπιπέδων καὶ τὸ στερεὸν τῶν στερεῶν· ἕκαστον γὰρ τῶν σχημάτων τὸ μὲν ἐπίπεδον τοιονδὶ τὸ δὲ στερεόν ἐστι τοιονδί· τοῦτο δʼ ἐστὶ τὸ ὑποκείμενον ταῖς διαφοραῖς. ἔτι ὡς ἐν τοῖς λόγοις τὸ πρῶτον ἐνυπάρχον, ὃ λέγεται ἐν τῷ τί ἐστι, τοῦτο γένος, οὗ διαφοραὶ λέγονται αἱ ποιότητες. τὸ μὲν οὖν γένος τοσαυταχῶς λέγεται, τὸ μὲν κατὰ γένεσιν συνεχῆ τοῦ αὐτοῦ εἴδους, τὸ δὲ κατὰ τὸ πρῶτον κινῆσαν ὁμοειδές, τὸ δʼ ὡς ὕλη· οὗ γὰρ ἡ διαφορὰ καὶ ἡ ποιότης ἐστί, τοῦτʼ ἔστι τὸ ὑποκείμενον, ὃ λέγομεν ὕλην. ἕτερα δὲ τῷ γένει λέγεται ὧν ἕτερον τὸ πρῶτον ὑποκείμενον καὶ μὴ ἀναλύεται θάτερον εἰς θάτερον μηδʼ ἄμφω εἰς ταὐτόν, οἷον τὸ εἶδος καὶ ἡ ὕλη ἕτερον τῷ γένει, καὶ ὅσα καθʼ ἕτερον σχῆμα κατηγορίας τοῦ ὄντος λέγεται (τὰ μὲν γὰρ τί ἐστι σημαίνει τῶν ὄντων τὰ δὲ ποιόν τι τὰ δʼ ὡς διῄρηται πρότερον)· οὐδὲ γὰρ ταῦτα ἀναλύεται οὔτʼ εἰς ἄλληλα οὔτʼ εἰς ἕν τι. \n\n\n\n \n\n \n\nτὸ ψεῦδος λέγεται ἄλλον μὲν τρόπον ὡς πρᾶγμα ψεῦδος, καὶ τούτου τὸ μὲν τῷ μὴ συγκεῖσθαι ἢ ἀδύνατον εἶναι συντεθῆναι (ὥσπερ λέγεται τὸ τὴν διάμετρον εἶναι σύμμετρον ἢ τὸ σὲ καθῆσθαι· τούτων γὰρ ψεῦδος τὸ μὲν ἀεὶ τὸ δὲ ποτέ· οὕτω γὰρ οὐκ ὄντα ταῦτἀ, τὰ δὲ ὅσα ἔστι μὲν ὄντα, πέφυκε μέντοι φαίνεσθαι ἢ μὴ οἷα) ἐστιν ἢ ἃ μὴ ἔστιν (οἷον ἡ σκιαγραφία καὶ τὰ ἐνύπνια· ταῦτα γὰρ ἔστι μέν τι, ἀλλʼ οὐχ ὧν ἐμποιεῖ τὴν φαντασίαν)·—πράγματα μὲν οὖν ψευδῆ οὕτω λέγεται, ἢ τῷ μὴ εἶναι αὐτὰ ἢ τῷ τὴν ἀπʼ αὐτῶν φαντασίαν μὴ ὄντος εἶναι· λόγος δὲ ψευδὴς ὁ τῶν μὴ ὄντων, ᾗ ψευδής, διὸ πᾶς λόγος ψευδὴς ἑτέρου ἢ οὗ ἐστὶν ἀληθής, οἷον ὁ τοῦ κύκλου ψευδὴς τριγώνου. ἑκάστου δὲ λόγος ἔστι μὲν ὡς εἷς, ὁ τοῦ τί ἦν εἶναι, ἔστι δʼ ὡς πολλοί, ἐπεὶ ταὐτό πως αὐτὸ καὶ αὐτὸ πεπονθός, οἷον Σωκράτης καὶ Σωκράτης μουσικός (ὁ δὲ ψευδὴς λόγος οὐθενός ἐστιν ἁπλῶς λόγος)· διὸ Ἀντισθένης ᾤετο εὐήθως μηθὲν ἀξιῶν λέγεσθαι πλὴν τῷ οἰκείῳ λόγῳ, ἓν ἐφʼ ἑνός· ἐξ ὧν συνέβαινε μὴ εἶναι ἀντιλέγειν, σχεδὸν δὲ μηδὲ ψεύδεσθαι. ἔστι δʼ ἕκαστον λέγειν οὐ μόνον τῷ αὐτοῦ λόγῳ ἀλλὰ καὶ τῷ ἑτέρου, ψευδῶς μὲν καὶ παντελῶς, ἔστι δʼ ὡς καὶ ἀληθῶς, ὥσπερ τὰ ὀκτὼ διπλάσια τῷ τῆς δυάδος λόγῳ.  τὰ μὲν οὖν οὕτω λέγεται ψευδῆ, ἄνθρωπος δὲ ψευδὴς ὁ εὐχερὴς καὶ προαιρετικὸς τῶν τοιούτων λόγων, μὴ διʼ ἕτερόν τι ἀλλὰ διʼ αὐτό, καὶ ὁ ἄλλοις ἐμποιητικὸς τῶν τοιούτων λόγων, ὥσπερ καὶ τὰ πράγματά φαμεν ψευδῆ εἶναι ὅσα ἐμποιεῖ φαντασίαν ψευδῆ. διὸ ὁ ἐν τῷ Ἱππίᾳ λόγος παρακρούεται ὡς ὁ αὐτὸς ψευδὴς καὶ ἀληθής. τὸν δυνάμενον γὰρ ψεύσασθαι λαμβάνει ψευδῆ (οὗτος δʼ ὁ εἰδὼς καὶ ὁ φρόνιμος)· ἔτι τὸν ἑκόντα φαῦλον βελτίω. τοῦτο δὲ ψεῦδος λαμβάνει διὰ τῆς ἐπαγωγῆς—ὁ γὰρ ἑκὼν χωλαίνων τοῦ ἄκοντος κρείττων—τὸ χωλαίνειν τὸ μιμεῖσθαι λέγων, ἐπεὶ εἴ γε χωλὸς ἑκών, χείρων ἴσως, ὥσπερ ἐπὶ τοῦ ἤθους, καὶ οὗτος. \n\n\n\n \n\n \n\nσυμβεβηκὸς λέγεται ὃ ὑπάρχει μέν τινι καὶ ἀληθὲς εἰπεῖν, οὐ μέντοι οὔτʼ ἐξ ἀνάγκης οὔτε ὡς ἐπὶ τὸ πολύ, οἷον εἴ τις ὀρύττων φυτῷ βόθρον εὗρε θησαυρόν. τοῦτο τοίνυν συμβεβηκὸς τῷ ὀρύττοντι τὸν βόθρον, τὸ εὑρεῖν θησαυρόν· οὔτε γὰρ ἐξ ἀνάγκης τοῦτο ἐκ τούτου ἢ μετὰ τοῦτο, οὔθʼ ὡς ἐπὶ τὸ πολὺ ἄν τις φυτεύῃ θησαυρὸν εὑρίσκει. καὶ μουσικός γʼ ἄν τις εἴη λευκός· ἀλλʼ ἐπεὶ οὔτε ἐξ ἀνάγκης οὔθʼ ὡς ἐπὶ τὸ πολὺ τοῦτο γίγνεται, συμβεβηκὸς αὐτὸ λέγομεν. ὥστʼ ἐπεὶ ἔστιν ὑπάρχον τι καὶ τινί, καὶ ἔνια τούτων καὶ ποὺ καὶ ποτέ, ὅ τι ἂν ὑπάρχῃ μέν, ἀλλὰ μὴ διότι τοδὶ ἦν ἢ νῦν ἢ ἐνταῦθα, συμβεβηκὸς ἔσται. οὐδὲ δὴ αἴτιον ὡρισμένον οὐδὲν τοῦ συμβεβηκότος ἀλλὰ τὸ τυχόν· τοῦτο δʼ ἀόριστον. συνέβη τῳ εἰς Αἴγιναν ἐλθεῖν, εἰ μὴ διὰ τοῦτο ἀφίκετο ὅπως ἐκεῖ ἔλθῃ, ἀλλʼ ὑπὸ χειμῶνος ἐξωσθεὶς ἢ ὑπὸ λῃστῶν ληφθείς. γέγονε μὲν δὴ ἢ ἔστι τὸ συμβεβηκός, ἀλλʼ οὐχ ᾗ αὐτὸ ἀλλʼ ᾗ ἕτερον· ὁ γὰρ χειμὼν αἴτιος τοῦ μὴ ὅπου ἔπλει ἐλθεῖν, τοῦτο δʼ ἦν Αἴγινα. λέγεται δὲ καὶ ἄλλως συμβεβηκός, οἷον ὅσα ὑπάρχει ἑκάστῳ καθʼ αὑτὸ μὴ ἐν τῇ οὐσίᾳ ὄντα, οἷον τῷ τριγώνῳ τὸ δύο ὀρθὰς ἔχειν. καὶ ταῦτα μὲν ἐνδέχεται ἀΐδια εἶναι, ἐκείνων δὲ οὐδέν. λόγος δὲ τούτου ἐν ἑτέροις.","paragraphs":[{"id":"RAW_DAZY_BOO5:P0001","text":"ἀρχὴ λέγεται ἡ μὲν ὅθεν ἄν τις τοῦ πράγματος κινηθείη πρῶτον, οἷον τοῦ μήκους καὶ ὁδοῦ ἐντεῦθεν μὲν αὕτη ἀρχή, ἐξ ἐναντίας δὲ ἑτέρα·  ἡ δὲ ὅθεν ἂν κάλλιστα ἕκαστον γένοιτο, οἷον καὶ μαθήσεως οὐκ ἀπὸ τοῦ πρώτου καὶ τῆς τοῦ πράγματος ἀρχῆς ἐνίοτε ἀρκτέον ἀλλʼ ὅθεν ῥᾷστʼ ἂν μάθοι· ἡ δὲ ὅθεν πρῶτον γίγνεται ἐνυπάρχοντος, οἷον ὡς πλοίου τρόπις καὶ οἰκίας θεμέλιος, καὶ τῶν ζῴων οἱ μὲν καρδίαν οἱ δὲ ἐγκέφαλον οἱ δʼ ὅ τι ἂν τύχωσι τοιοῦτον ὑπολαμβάνουσιν· ἡ δὲ ὅθεν γίγνεται πρῶτον μὴ ἐνυπάρχοντος καὶ ὅθεν πρῶτον ἡ κίνησις πέφυκεν ἄρχεσθαι καὶ ἡ μεταβολή, οἷον τὸ τέκνον ἐκ τοῦ πατρὸς καὶ τῆς μητρὸς καὶ ἡ μάχη ἐκ τῆς λοιδορίας· ἡ δὲ οὗ κατὰ προαίρεσιν κινεῖται τὰ κινούμενα καὶ μεταβάλλει τὰ μεταβάλλοντα, ὥσπερ αἵ τε κατὰ πόλεις ἀρχαὶ καὶ αἱ δυναστεῖαι καὶ αἱ βασιλεῖαι καὶ τυραννίδες ἀρχαὶ λέγονται καὶ αἱ τέχναι, καὶ τούτων αἱ ἀρχιτεκτονικαὶ μάλιστα.","kind":"passage"},{"id":"RAW_DAZY_BOO5:P0002","text":"ἔτι ὅθεν γνωστὸν τὸ πρᾶγμα πρῶτον, καὶ αὕτη ἀρχὴ λέγεται τοῦ πράγματος, οἷον τῶν ἀποδείξεων αἱ ὑποθέσεις. ἰσαχῶς δὲ καὶ τὰ αἴτια λέγεται· πάντα γὰρ τὰ αἴτια ἀρχαί. πασῶν μὲν οὖν κοινὸν τῶν ἀρχῶν τὸ πρῶτον εἶναι ὅθεν ἢ ἔστιν ἢ γίγνεται ἢ γιγνώσκεται· τούτων δὲ αἱ μὲν ἐνυπάρχουσαί εἰσιν αἱ δὲ ἐκτός. διὸ ἥ τε φύσις ἀρχὴ καὶ τὸ στοιχεῖον καὶ ἡ διάνοια καὶ ἡ προαίρεσις καὶ οὐσία καὶ τὸ οὗ ἕνεκα· πολλῶν γὰρ καὶ τοῦ γνῶναι καὶ τῆς κινήσεως ἀρχὴ τἀγαθὸν καὶ τὸ καλόν.","kind":"passage"},{"id":"RAW_DAZY_BOO5:P0003","text":"αἴτιον λέγεται ἕνα μὲν τρόπον ἐξ οὗ γίγνεταί τι ἐνυπάρχοντος, οἷον ὁ χαλκὸς τοῦ ἀνδριάντος καὶ ὁ ἄργυρος τῆς φιάλης καὶ τὰ τούτων γένη· ἄλλον δὲ τὸ εἶδος καὶ τὸ παράδειγμα, τοῦτο δʼ ἐστὶν ὁ λόγος τοῦ τί ἦν εἶναι καὶ τὰ τούτου γένη (οἷον τοῦ διὰ πασῶν τὸ δύο πρὸς ἓν καὶ ὅλως ὁ ἀριθμός) καὶ τὰ μέρη τὰ ἐν τῷ λόγῳ. ἔτι ὅθεν ἡ ἀρχὴ τῆς μεταβολῆς ἡ πρώτη ἢ τῆς ἠρεμήσεως, οἷον ὁ βουλεύσας αἴτιος, καὶ ὁ πατὴρ τοῦ τέκνου καὶ ὅλως τὸ ποιοῦν τοῦ ποιουμένου καὶ τὸ μεταβλητικὸν τοῦ μεταβάλλοντος. ἔτι ὡς τὸ τέλος· τοῦτο δʼ ἐστὶ τὸ οὗ ἕνεκα, οἷον τοῦ περιπατεῖν ἡ ὑγίεια. διὰ τί γὰρ περιπατεῖ; φαμέν. ἵνα ὑγιαίνῃ. καὶ εἰπόντες οὕτως οἰόμεθα ἀποδεδωκέναι τὸ αἴτιον. καὶ ὅσα δὴ κινήσαντος ἄλλου μεταξὺ γίγνεται τοῦ τέλους,  οἷον τῆς ὑγιείας ἡ ἰσχνασία ἢ ἡ κάθαρσις ἢ τὰ φάρμακα ἢ τὰ ὄργανα· πάντα γὰρ ταῦτα τοῦ τέλους ἕνεκά ἐστι, διαφέρει δὲ ἀλλήλων ὡς ὄντα τὰ μὲν ὄργανα τὰ δʼ ἔργα.","kind":"passage"},{"id":"RAW_DAZY_BOO5:P0004","text":"τὰ μὲν οὖν αἴτια σχεδὸν τοσαυταχῶς λέγεται, συμβαίνει δὲ πολλαχῶς λεγομένων τῶν αἰτίων καὶ πολλὰ τοῦ αὐτοῦ αἴτια εἶναι οὐ κατὰ συμβεβηκός (οἷον τοῦ ἀνδριάντος καὶ ἡ ἀνδριαντοποιητικὴ καὶ ὁ χαλκὸς οὐ καθʼ ἕτερόν τι ἀλλʼ ᾗ ἀνδριάς· ἀλλʼ οὐ τὸν αὐτὸν τρόπον ἀλλὰ τὸ μὲν ὡς ὕλη τὸ δʼ ὡς ὅθεν ἡ κίνησις), καὶ ἀλλήλων αἴτια (οἷον τὸ πονεῖν τῆς εὐεξίας καὶ αὕτη τοῦ πονεῖν· ἀλλʼ οὐ τὸν αὐτὸν τρόπον ἀλλὰ τὸ μὲν ὡς τέλος τὸ δʼ ὡς ἀρχὴ κινήσεως). ἔτι δὲ ταὐτὸ τῶν ἐναντίων ἐστίν· ὃ γὰρ παρὸν αἴτιον τουδί, τοῦτʼ ἀπὸν αἰτιώμεθα ἐνίοτε τοῦ ἐναντίου, οἷον τὴν ἀπουσίαν τοῦ κυβερνήτου τῆς ἀνατροπῆς, οὗ ἦν ἡ παρουσία αἰτία τῆς σωτηρίας· ἄμφω δέ, καὶ ἡ παρουσία καὶ ἡ στέρησις, αἴτια ὡς κινοῦντα.—ἅπαντα δὲ τὰ νῦν εἰρημένα αἴτια εἰς τέτταρας τρόπους πίπτει τοὺς φανερωτάτους.","kind":"passage"},{"id":"RAW_DAZY_BOO5:P0005","text":"τὰ μὲν γὰρ στοιχεῖα τῶν συλλαβῶν καὶ ἡ ὕλη τῶν σκευαστῶν καὶ τὸ πῦρ καὶ ἡ γῆ καὶ τὰ τοιαῦτα πάντα τῶν σωμάτων καὶ τὰ μέρη τοῦ ὅλου καὶ αἱ ὑποθέσεις τοῦ συμπεράσματος ὡς τὸ ἐξ οὗ αἴτιά ἐστιν· τούτων δὲ τὰ μὲν ὡς τὸ ὑποκείμενον, οἷον τὰ μέρη, τὰ δὲ ὡς τὸ τί ἦν εἶναι, τό τε ὅλον καὶ ἡ σύνθεσις καὶ τὸ εἶδος. τὸ δὲ σπέρμα καὶ ὁ ἰατρὸς καὶ ὁ βουλεύσας καὶ ὅλως τὸ ποιοῦν, πάντα ὅθεν ἡ ἀρχὴ τῆς μεταβολῆς ἢ στάσεως. τὰ δʼ ὡς τὸ τέλος καὶ τἀγαθὸν τῶν ἄλλων· τὸ γὰρ οὗ ἕνεκα βέλτιστον καὶ τέλος τῶν ἄλλων ἐθέλει εἶναι· διαφερέτω δὲ μηδὲν αὐτὸ εἰπεῖν ἀγαθὸν ἢ φαινόμενον ἀγαθόν.—τὰ μὲν οὖν αἴτια ταῦτα καὶ τοσαῦτά ἐστι τῷ εἴδει, τρόποι δὲ τῶν αἰτίων ἀριθμῷ μέν εἰσι πολλοί, κεφαλαιούμενοι δὲ καὶ οὗτοι ἐλάττους.","kind":"passage"},{"id":"RAW_DAZY_BOO5:P0006","text":"λέγονται γὰρ αἴτια πολλαχῶς, καὶ αὐτῶν τῶν ὁμοειδῶν προτέρως καὶ ὑστέρως ἄλλο ἄλλου, οἷον ὑγιείας ὁ ἰατρὸς καὶ ὁ τεχνίτης, καὶ τοῦ διὰ πασῶν τὸ διπλάσιον καὶ ἀριθμός, καὶ ἀεὶ τὰ περιέχοντα ὁτιοῦν τῶν καθʼ ἕκαστα. ἔτι δʼ ὡς τὸ συμβεβηκὸς καὶ τὰ τούτων γένη, οἷον ἀνδριάντος ἄλλως Πολύκλειτος καὶ ἄλλως ἀνδριαντοποιός, ὅτι συμβέβηκε τῷ ἀνδριαντοποιῷ Πολυκλείτῳ εἶναι·  καὶ τὰ περιέχοντα δὲ τὸ συμβεβηκός, οἷον ἄνθρωπος αἴτιος ἀνδριάντος, ἢ καὶ ὅλως ζῷον, ὅτι ὁ Πολύκλειτος ἄνθρωπος ὁ δὲ ἄνθρωπος ζῷον. ἔστι δὲ καὶ τῶν συμβεβηκότων ἄλλα ἄλλων πορρώτερον καὶ ἐγγύτερον, οἷον εἰ ὁ λευκὸς καὶ ὁ μουσικὸς αἴτιος λέγοιτο τοῦ ἀνδριάντος, ἀλλὰ μὴ μόνον Πολύκλειτος ἢ ἄνθρωπος. παρὰ πάντα δὲ καὶ τὰ οἰκείως λεγόμενα καὶ τὰ κατὰ συμβεβηκός, τὰ μὲν ὡς δυνάμενα λέγεται τὰ δʼ ὡς ἐνεργοῦντα, οἷον τοῦ οἰκοδομεῖσθαι οἰκοδόμος ἢ οἰκοδομῶν οἰκοδόμος.","kind":"passage"},{"id":"RAW_DAZY_BOO5:P0007","text":"ὁμοίως δὲ λεχθήσεται καὶ ἐφʼ ὧν αἴτια τὰ αἴτια τοῖς εἰρημένοις, οἷον τοῦδε τοῦ ἀνδριάντος ἢ ἀνδριάντος ἢ ὅλως εἰκόνος, καὶ χαλκοῦ τοῦδε ἢ χαλκοῦ ἢ ὅλως ὕλης· καὶ ἐπὶ τῶν συμβεβηκότων ὡσαύτως. ἔτι δὲ συμπλεκόμενα καὶ ταῦτα κἀκεῖνα λεχθήσεται, οἷον οὐ Πολύκλειτος οὐδὲ ἀνδριαντοποιὸς ἀλλὰ Πολύκλειτος ἀνδριαντοποιός. ἀλλʼ ὅμως ἅπαντά γε ταῦτʼ ἐστὶ τὸ μὲν πλῆθος ἕξ, λεγόμενα δὲ διχῶς· ἢ γὰρ ὡς τὸ καθʼ ἕκαστον ἢ ὡς τὸ γένος, ἢ ὡς τὸ συμβεβηκὸς ἢ ὡς τὸ γένος τοῦ συμβεβηκότος, ἢ ὡς συμπλεκόμενα ταῦτα ἢ ὡς ἁπλῶς λεγόμενα, πάντα δὲ ἢ ὡς ἐνεργοῦντα ἢ κατὰ δύναμιν. διαφέρει δὲ τοσοῦτον, ὅτι τὰ μὲν ἐνεργοῦντα καὶ τὰ καθʼ ἕκαστον ἅμα ἔστι καὶ οὐκ ἔστι καὶ ὧν αἴτια, οἷον ὅδε ὁ ἰατρεύων τῷδε τῷ ὑγιαζομένῳ καὶ ὅδε ὁ οἰκοδόμος τῷδε τῷ οἰκοδομουμένῳ, τὰ δὲ κατὰ δύναμιν οὐκ ἀεί· φθείρεται γὰρ οὐχ ἅμα ἡ οἰκία καὶ ὁ οἰκοδόμος.","kind":"passage"},{"id":"RAW_DAZY_BOO5:P0008","text":"στοιχεῖον λέγεται ἐξ οὗ σύγκειται πρώτου ἐνυπάρχοντος ἀδιαιρέτου τῷ εἴδει εἰς ἕτερον εἶδος, οἷον φωνῆς στοιχεῖα ἐξ ὧν σύγκειται ἡ φωνὴ καὶ εἰς ἃ διαιρεῖται ἔσχατα, ἐκεῖνα δὲ μηκέτʼ εἰς ἄλλας φωνὰς ἑτέρας τῷ εἴδει αὐτῶν, ἀλλὰ κἂν διαιρῆται, τὰ μόρια ὁμοειδῆ, οἷον ὕδατος τὸ μόριον ὕδωρ, ἀλλʼ οὐ τῆς συλλαβῆς. ὁμοίως δὲ καὶ τὰ τῶν σωμάτων στοιχεῖα λέγουσιν οἱ λέγοντες εἰς ἃ διαιρεῖται τὰ σώματα ἔσχατα, ἐκεῖνα δὲ μηκέτʼ εἰς ἄλλα εἴδει διαφέροντα· καὶ εἴτε ἓν εἴτε πλείω τὰ τοιαῦτα, ταῦτα στοιχεῖα λέγουσιν. παραπλησίως δὲ καὶ τὰ τῶν διαγραμμάτων στοιχεῖα λέγεται, καὶ ὅλως τὰ τῶν ἀποδείξεων· αἱ γὰρ πρῶται ἀποδείξεις καὶ ἐν πλείοσιν ἀποδείξεσιν ἐνυπάρχουσαι,  αὗται στοιχεῖα τῶν ἀποδείξεων λέγονται· εἰσὶ δὲ τοιοῦτοι συλλογισμοὶ οἱ πρῶτοι ἐκ τῶν τριῶν διʼ ἑνὸς μέσου.","kind":"passage"},{"id":"RAW_DAZY_BOO5:P0009","text":"καὶ μεταφέροντες δὲ στοιχεῖον καλοῦσιν ἐντεῦθεν ὃ ἂν ἓν ὂν καὶ μικρὸν ἐπὶ πολλὰ ᾖ χρήσιμον, διὸ καὶ τὸ μικρὸν καὶ ἁπλοῦν καὶ ἀδιαίρετον στοιχεῖον λέγεται. ὅθεν ἐλήλυθε τὰ μάλιστα καθόλου στοιχεῖα εἶναι, ὅτι ἕκαστον αὐτῶν ἓν ὂν καὶ ἁπλοῦν ἐν πολλοῖς ὑπάρχει ἢ πᾶσιν ἢ ὅτι πλείστοις, καὶ τὸ ἓν καὶ τὴν στιγμὴν ἀρχάς τισι δοκεῖν εἶναι. ἐπεὶ οὖν τὰ καλούμενα γένη καθόλου καὶ ἀδιαίρετα (οὐ γὰρ ἔστι λόγος αὐτῶν), στοιχεῖα τὰ γένη λέγουσί τινες, καὶ μᾶλλον ἢ τὴν διαφορὰν ὅτι καθόλου μᾶλλον τὸ γένος· ᾧ μὲν γὰρ ἡ διαφορὰ ὑπάρχει, καὶ τὸ γένος ἀκολουθεῖ, ᾧ δὲ τὸ γένος, οὐ παντὶ ἡ διαφορά. ἁπάντων δὲ κοινὸν τὸ εἶναι στοιχεῖον ἑκάστου τὸ πρῶτον ἐνυπάρχον ἑκάστῳ.","kind":"passage"},{"id":"RAW_DAZY_BOO5:P0010","text":"φύσις λέγεται ἕνα μὲν τρόπον ἡ τῶν φυομένων γένεσις, οἷον εἴ τις ἐπεκτείνας λέγοι τὸ υ, ἕνα δὲ ἐξ οὗ φύεται πρώτου τὸ φυόμενον ἐνυπάρχοντος· ἔτι ὅθεν ἡ κίνησις ἡ πρώτη ἐν ἑκάστῳ τῶν φύσει ὄντων ἐν αὐτῷ ᾗ αὐτὸ ὑπάρχει· φύεσθαι δὲ λέγεται ὅσα αὔξησιν ἔχει διʼ ἑτέρου τῷ ἅπτεσθαι καὶ συμπεφυκέναι ἢ προσπεφυκέναι ὥσπερ τὰ ἔμβρυα· διαφέρει δὲ σύμφυσις ἁφῆς, ἔνθα μὲν γὰρ οὐδὲν παρὰ τὴν ἁφὴν ἕτερον ἀνάγκη εἶναι, ἐν δὲ τοῖς συμπεφυκόσιν ἔστι τι ἓν τὸ αὐτὸ ἐν ἀμφοῖν ὃ ποιεῖ ἀντὶ τοῦ ἅπτεσθαι συμπεφυκέναι καὶ εἶναι ἓν κατὰ τὸ συνεχὲς καὶ ποσόν, ἀλλὰ μὴ κατὰ τὸ ποιόν.","kind":"passage"},{"id":"RAW_DAZY_BOO5:P0011","text":"ἔτι δὲ φύσις λέγεται ἐξ οὗ πρώτου ἢ ἔστιν ἢ γίγνεταί τι τῶν φύσει ὄντων, ἀρρυθμίστου ὄντος καὶ ἀμεταβλήτου ἐκ τῆς δυνάμεως τῆς αὑτοῦ, οἷον ἀνδριάντος καὶ τῶν σκευῶν τῶν χαλκῶν ὁ χαλκὸς ἡ φύσις λέγεται, τῶν δὲ ξυλίνων ξύλον· ὁμοίως δὲ καὶ ἐπὶ τῶν ἄλλων· ἐκ τούτων γάρ ἐστιν ἕκαστον διασωζομένης τῆς πρώτης ὕλης· τοῦτον γὰρ τὸν τρόπον καὶ τῶν φύσει ὄντων τὰ στοιχεῖά φασιν εἶναι φύσιν, οἱ μὲν πῦρ οἱ δὲ γῆν οἱ δʼ ἀέρα οἱ δʼ ὕδωρ οἱ δʼ ἄλλο τι τοιοῦτον λέγοντες, οἱ δʼ ἔνια τούτων οἱ δὲ πάντα ταῦτα. ἔτι δʼ ἄλλον τρόπον λέγεται ἡ φύσις ἡ τῶν φύσει ὄντων οὐσία, οἷον οἱ λέγοντες τὴν φύσιν εἶναι τὴν πρώτην σύνθεσιν,  ἢ ὥσπερ Ἐμπεδοκλῆς λέγει ὅτι","kind":"passage"},{"id":"RAW_DAZY_BOO5:P0012","text":"φύσις οὐδενὸς ἔστιν ἐόντων,","kind":"passage"},{"id":"RAW_DAZY_BOO5:P0013","text":"ἀλλὰ μόνον μῖξίς τε διάλλαξίς τε μιγέντων","kind":"passage"},{"id":"RAW_DAZY_BOO5:P0014","text":"ἔστι, φύσις δʼ ἐπὶ τοῖς ὀνομάζεται ἀνθρώποισιν.","kind":"passage"},{"id":"RAW_DAZY_BOO5:P0015","text":"Empedocles Fr. 8 διὸ καὶ ὅσα φύσει ἔστιν ἢ γίγνεται, ἤδη ὑπάρχοντος ἐξ οὗ πέφυκε γίγνεσθαι ἢ εἶναι, οὔπω φαμὲν τὴν φύσιν ἔχειν ἐὰν μὴ ἔχῃ τὸ εἶδος καὶ τὴν μορφήν. φύσει μὲν οὖν τὸ ἐξ ἀμφοτέρων τούτων ἐστίν, οἷον τὰ ζῷα καὶ τὰ μόρια αὐτῶν· φύσις δὲ ἥ τε πρώτη ὕλη (καὶ αὕτη διχῶς, ἢ ἡ πρὸς αὐτὸ πρώτη ἢ ἡ ὅλως πρώτη, οἷον τῶν χαλκῶν ἔργων πρὸς αὐτὰ μὲν πρῶτος ὁ χαλκός, ὅλως δʼ ἴσως ὕδωρ, εἰ πάντα τὰ τηκτὰ ὕδωρ) καὶ τὸ εἶδος καὶ ἡ οὐσία· τοῦτο δʼ ἐστὶ τὸ τέλος τῆς γενέσεως. μεταφορᾷ δʼ ἤδη καὶ ὅλως πᾶσα οὐσία φύσις λέγεται διὰ ταύτην, ὅτι καὶ ἡ φύσις οὐσία τίς ἐστιν. ἐκ δὴ τῶν εἰρημένων ἡ πρώτη φύσις καὶ κυρίως λεγομένη ἐστὶν ἡ οὐσία ἡ τῶν ἐχόντων ἀρχὴν κινήσεως ἐν αὑτοῖς ᾗ αὐτά· ἡ γὰρ ὕλη τῷ ταύτης δεκτικὴ εἶναι λέγεται φύσις, καὶ αἱ γενέσεις καὶ τὸ φύεσθαι τῷ ἀπὸ ταύτης εἶναι κινήσεις. καὶ ἡ ἀρχὴ τῆς κινήσεως τῶν φύσει ὄντων αὕτη ἐστίν, ἐνυπάρχουσά πως ἢ δυνάμει ἢ ἐντελεχείᾳ.","kind":"passage"},{"id":"RAW_DAZY_BOO5:P0016","text":"ἀναγκαῖον λέγεται οὗ ἄνευ οὐκ ἐνδέχεται ζῆν ὡς συναιτίου (οἷον τὸ ἀναπνεῖν καὶ ἡ τροφὴ τῷ ζῴῳ ἀναγκαῖον, ἀδύνατον γὰρ ἄνευ τούτων εἶναι), καὶ ὧν ἄνευ τὸ ἀγαθὸν μὴ ἐνδέχεται ἢ εἶναι ἢ γενέσθαι, ἢ τὸ κακὸν ἀποβαλεῖν ἢ στερηθῆναι (οἷον τὸ πιεῖν τὸ φάρμακον ἀναγκαῖον ἵνα μὴ κάμνῃ, καὶ τὸ πλεῦσαι εἰς Αἴγιναν ἵνα ἀπολάβῃ τὰ χρήματα). ἔτι τὸ βίαιον καὶ ἡ βία· τοῦτο δʼ ἐστὶ τὸ παρὰ τὴν ὁρμὴν καὶ τὴν προαίρεσιν ἐμποδίζον καὶ κωλυτικόν, τὸ γὰρ βίαιον ἀναγκαῖον λέγεται, διὸ καὶ λυπηρόν (ὥσπερ καὶ Εὔηνός φησι πᾶν γὰρ ἀναγκαῖον πρᾶγμʼ ἀνιαρὸν ἔφυ), καὶ ἡ βία ἀνάγκη τις (ὥσπερ καὶ Σοφοκλῆς λέγει  ἀλλʼ ἡ βία με ταῦτʼ ἀναγκάζει ποιεῖν), καὶ δοκεῖ ἡ ἀνάγκη ἀμετάπειστόν τι εἶναι, ὀρθῶς· ἐναντίον γὰρ τῇ κατὰ τὴν προαίρεσιν κινήσει καὶ κατὰ τὸν λογισμόν.","kind":"passage"},{"id":"RAW_DAZY_BOO5:P0017","text":"ἔτι τὸ μὴ ἐνδεχόμενον ἄλλως ἔχειν ἀναγκαῖόν φαμεν οὕτως ἔχειν· καὶ κατὰ τοῦτο τὸ ἀναγκαῖον καὶ τἆλλα λέγεταί πως ἅπαντα ἀναγκαῖα· τό τε γὰρ βίαιον ἀναγκαῖον λέγεται ἢ ποιεῖν ἢ πάσχειν τότε,  ὅταν μὴ ἐνδέχηται κατὰ τὴν ὁρμὴν διὰ τὸ βιαζόμενον, ὡς ταύτην ἀνάγκην οὖσαν διʼ ἣν μὴ ἐνδέχεται ἄλλως, καὶ ἐπὶ τῶν συναιτίων τοῦ ζῆν καὶ τοῦ ἀγαθοῦ ὡσαύτως· ὅταν γὰρ μὴ ἐνδέχηται ἔνθα μὲν τὸ ἀγαθὸν ἔνθα δὲ τὸ ζῆν καὶ τὸ εἶναι ἄνευ τινῶν, ταῦτα ἀναγκαῖα καὶ ἡ αἰτία ἀνάγκη τίς ἐστιν αὕτη. ἔτι ἡ ἀπόδειξις τῶν ἀναγκαίων, ὅτι οὐκ ἐνδέχεται ἄλλως ἔχειν, εἰ ἀποδέδεικται ἁπλῶς· τούτου δʼ αἴτια τὰ πρῶτα, εἰ ἀδύνατον ἄλλως ἔχειν ἐξ ὧν ὁ συλλογισμός. τῶν μὲν δὴ ἕτερον αἴτιον τοῦ ἀναγκαῖα εἶναι, τῶν δὲ οὐδέν, ἀλλὰ διὰ ταῦτα ἕτερά ἐστιν ἐξ ἀνάγκης. ὥστε τὸ πρῶτον καὶ κυρίως ἀναγκαῖον τὸ ἁπλοῦν ἐστίν· τοῦτο γὰρ οὐκ ἐνδέχεται πλεοναχῶς ἔχειν, ὥστʼ οὐδὲ ἄλλως καὶ ἄλλως· ἤδη γὰρ πλεοναχῶς ἂν ἔχοι.","kind":"passage"},{"id":"RAW_DAZY_BOO5:P0018","text":"εἰ ἄρα ἔστιν ἄττα ἀΐδια καὶ ἀκίνητα, οὐδὲν ἐκείνοις ἐστὶ βίαιον οὐδὲ παρὰ φύσιν.","kind":"passage"},{"id":"RAW_DAZY_BOO5:P0019","text":"ἓν λέγεται τὸ μὲν κατὰ συμβεβηκὸς τὸ δὲ καθʼ αὑτό, κατὰ συμβεβηκὸς μὲν οἷον Κορίσκος καὶ τὸ μουσικόν, καὶ Κορίσκος μουσικός (ταὐτὸ γὰρ εἰπεῖν Κορίσκος καὶ τὸ μουσικόν, καὶ Κορίσκος μουσικός), καὶ τὸ μουσικὸν καὶ τὸ δίκαιον, καὶ μουσικὸς Κορίσκος καὶ δίκαιος Κορίσκος· πάντα γὰρ ταῦτα ἓν λέγεται κατὰ συμβεβηκός, τὸ μὲν δίκαιον καὶ τὸ μουσικὸν ὅτι μιᾷ οὐσίᾳ συμβέβηκεν, τὸ δὲ μουσικὸν καὶ Κορίσκος ὅτι θάτερον θατέρῳ συμβέβηκεν· ὁμοίως δὲ τρόπον τινὰ καὶ ὁ μουσικὸς Κορίσκος τῷ Κορίσκῳ ἓν ὅτι θάτερον τῶν μορίων θατέρῳ συμβέβηκε τῶν ἐν τῷ λόγῳ, οἷον τὸ μουσικὸν τῷ Κορίσκῳ· καὶ ὁ μουσικὸς Κορίσκος δικαίῳ Κορίσκῳ ὅτι ἑκατέρου μέρος τῷ αὐτῷ ἑνὶ συμβέβηκεν ἕν.","kind":"passage"},{"id":"RAW_DAZY_BOO5:P0020","text":"ὡσαύτως δὲ κἂν ἐπὶ γένους κἂν ἐπὶ τῶν καθόλου τινὸς ὀνομάτων λέγηται τὸ συμβεβηκός, οἷον ὅτι ἄνθρωπος τὸ αὐτὸ καὶ μουσικὸς ἄνθρωπος· ἢ γὰρ ὅτι τῷ ἀνθρώπῳ μιᾷ οὔσῃ οὐσίᾳ συμβέβηκε τὸ μουσικόν, ἢ ὅτι ἄμφω τῶν καθʼ ἕκαστόν τινι συμβέβηκεν, οἷον Κορίσκῳ. πλὴν οὐ τὸν αὐτὸν τρόπον ἄμφω ὑπάρχει, ἀλλὰ τὸ μὲν ἴσως ὡς γένος καὶ ἐν τῇ οὐσίᾳ τὸ δὲ ὡς ἕξις ἢ πάθος τῆς οὐσίας.—ὅσα μὲν οὖν κατὰ συμβεβηκὸς λέγεται ἕν, τοῦτον τὸν τρόπον λέγεται· τῶν δὲ καθʼ ἑαυτὰ ἓν λεγομένων τὰ μὲν λέγεται τῷ συνεχῆ εἶναι, οἷον φάκελος δεσμῷ καὶ ξύλα κόλλῃ·  καὶ γραμμή, κἂν κεκαμμένη ᾖ, συνεχὴς δέ, μία λέγεται, ὥσπερ καὶ τῶν μερῶν ἕκαστον, οἷον σκέλος καὶ βραχίων. αὐτῶν δὲ τούτων μᾶλλον ἓν τὰ φύσει συνεχῆ ἢ τέχνῃ. συνεχὲς δὲ λέγεται οὗ κίνησις μία καθʼ αὑτὸ καὶ μὴ οἷόν τε ἄλλως· μία δʼ οὗ ἀδιαίρετος, ἀδιαίρετος δὲ κατὰ χρόνον.","kind":"passage"},{"id":"RAW_DAZY_BOO5:P0021","text":"καθʼ αὑτὰ δὲ συνεχῆ ὅσα μὴ ἁφῇ ἕν· εἰ γὰρ θείης ἁπτόμενα ἀλλήλων ξύλα, οὐ φήσεις ταῦτα εἶναι ἓν οὔτε ξύλον οὔτε σῶμα οὔτʼ ἄλλο συνεχὲς οὐδέν. τά τε δὴ ὅλως συνεχῆ ἓν λέγεται κἂν ἔχῃ κάμψιν, καὶ ἔτι μᾶλλον τὰ μὴ ἔχοντα κάμψιν, οἷον κνήμη ἢ μηρὸς σκέλους, ὅτι ἐνδέχεται μὴ μίαν εἶναι τὴν κίνησιν τοῦ σκέλους. καὶ ἡ εὐθεῖα τῆς κεκαμμένης μᾶλλον ἕν· τὴν δὲ κεκαμμένην καὶ ἔχουσαν γωνίαν καὶ μίαν καὶ οὐ μίαν λέγομεν, ὅτι ἐνδέχεται καὶ μὴ ἅμα τὴν κίνησιν αὐτῆς εἶναι καὶ ἅμα· τῆς δʼ εὐθείας ἀεὶ ἅμα, καὶ οὐδὲν μόριον ἔχον μέγεθος τὸ μὲν ἠρεμεῖ τὸ δὲ κινεῖται, ὥσπερ τῆς κεκαμμένης.","kind":"passage"},{"id":"RAW_DAZY_BOO5:P0022","text":"ἔτι ἄλλον τρόπον ἓν λέγεται τῷ τὸ ὑποκείμενον τῷ εἴδει εἶναι ἀδιάφορον· ἀδιάφορον δʼ ὧν ἀδιαίρετον τὸ εἶδος κατὰ τὴν αἴσθησιν· τὸ δʼ ὑποκείμενον ἢ τὸ πρῶτον ἢ τὸ τελευταῖον πρὸς τὸ τέλος· καὶ γὰρ οἶνος εἷς λέγεται καὶ ὕδωρ ἕν, ᾗ ἀδιαίρετον κατὰ τὸ εἶδος, καὶ οἱ χυμοὶ πάντες λέγονται ἕν (οἷον ἔλαιον οἶνος) καὶ τὰ τηκτά, ὅτι πάντων τὸ ἔσχατον ὑποκείμενον τὸ αὐτό· ὕδωρ γὰρ ἢ ἀὴρ πάντα ταῦτα. λέγεται δʼ ἓν καὶ ὧν τὸ γένος ἓν διαφέρον ταῖς ἀντικειμέναις διαφοραῖς—καὶ ταῦτα λέγεται πάντα ἓν ὅτι τὸ γένος ἓν τὸ ὑποκείμενον ταῖς διαφοραῖς (οἷον ἵππος ἄνθρωπος κύων ἕν τι ὅτι πάντα ζῷα), καὶ τρόπον δὴ παραπλήσιον ὥσπερ ἡ ὕλη μία. ταῦτα δὲ ὁτὲ μὲν οὕτως ἓν λέγεται, ὁτὲ δὲ τὸ ἄνω γένος ταὐτὸν λέγεται —ἂν ᾖ τελευταῖα τοῦ γένους εἴδη—τὸ ἀνωτέρω τούτων, οἷον τὸ ἰσοσκελὲς καὶ τὸ ἰσόπλευρον ταὐτὸ καὶ ἓν σχῆμα ὅτι ἄμφω τρίγωνα· τρίγωνα δʼ οὐ ταὐτά.","kind":"passage"},{"id":"RAW_DAZY_BOO5:P0023","text":"ἔτι δὲ ἓν λέγεται ὅσων ὁ λόγος ὁ τὸ τί ἦν εἶναι λέγων ἀδιαίρετος πρὸς ἄλλον τὸν δηλοῦντα  τὸ πρᾶγμα (αὐτὸς γὰρ καθʼ αὑτὸν πᾶς λόγος διαιρετός). οὕτω γὰρ καὶ τὸ ηὐξημένον καὶ φθῖνον ἕν ἐστιν, ὅτι ὁ λόγος εἷς, ὥσπερ ἐπὶ τῶν ἐπιπέδων ὁ τοῦ εἴδους.  ὅλως δὲ ὧν ἡ νόησις ἀδιαίρετος ἡ νοοῦσα τὸ τί ἦν εἶναι, καὶ μὴ δύναται χωρίσαι μήτε χρόνῳ μήτε τόπῳ μήτε λόγῳ, μάλιστα ταῦτα ἕν, καὶ τούτων ὅσα οὐσίαι· καθόλου γὰρ ὅσα μὴ ἔχει διαίρεσιν, ᾗ μὴ ἔχει, ταύτῃ ἓν λέγεται, οἷον εἰ ᾗ ἄνθρωπος μὴ ἔχει διαίρεσιν, εἷς ἄνθρωπος, εἰ δʼ ᾗ ζῷον, ἓν ζῷον, εἰ δὲ ᾗ μέγεθος, ἓν μέγεθος. τὰ μὲν οὖν πλεῖστα ἓν λέγεται τῷ ἕτερόν τι ἢ ποιεῖν ἢ ἔχειν ἢ πάσχειν ἢ πρός τι εἶναι ἕν, τὰ δὲ πρώτως λεγόμενα ἓν ὧν ἡ οὐσία μία, μία δὲ ἢ συνεχείᾳ ἢ εἴδει ἢ λόγῳ· καὶ γὰρ ἀριθμοῦμεν ὡς πλείω ἢ τὰ μὴ συνεχῆ ἢ ὧν μὴ ἓν τὸ εἶδος ἢ ὧν ὁ λόγος μὴ εἷς.","kind":"passage"},{"id":"RAW_DAZY_BOO5:P0024","text":"ἔτι δʼ ἔστι μὲν ὡς ὁτιοῦν ἕν φαμεν εἶναι ἂν ᾖ ποσὸν καὶ συνεχές, ἔστι δʼ ὡς οὔ, ἂν μή τι ὅλον ᾖ, τοῦτο δὲ ἂν μὴ τὸ εἶδος ἔχῃ ἕν· οἷον οὐκ ἂν φαῖμεν ὁμοίως ἓν ἰδόντες ὁπωσοῦν τὰ μέρη συγκείμενα τοῦ ὑποδήματος, ἐὰν μὴ διὰ τὴν συνέχειαν, ἀλλʼ ἐὰν οὕτως ὥστε ὑπόδημα εἶναι καὶ εἶδός τι ἔχειν ἤδη ἕν· διὸ καὶ ἡ τοῦ κύκλου μάλιστα μία τῶν γραμμῶν, ὅτι ὅλη καὶ τέλειός ἐστιν.—τὸ δὲ ἑνὶ εἶναι ἀρχῇ τινί ἐστιν ἀριθμοῦ εἶναι· τὸ γὰρ πρῶτον μέτρον ἀρχή, ᾧ γὰρ πρώτῳ γνωρίζομεν, τοῦτο πρῶτον μέτρον ἑκάστου γένους· ἀρχὴ οὖν τοῦ γνωστοῦ περὶ ἕκαστον τὸ ἕν. οὐ ταὐτὸ δὲ ἐν πᾶσι τοῖς γένεσι τὸ ἕν. ἔνθα μὲν γὰρ δίεσις ἔνθα δὲ τὸ φωνῆεν ἢ ἄφωνον· βάρους δὲ ἕτερον καὶ κινήσεως ἄλλο. πανταχοῦ δὲ τὸ ἓν ἢ τῷ ποσῷ ἢ τῷ εἴδει ἀδιαίρετον.","kind":"passage"},{"id":"RAW_DAZY_BOO5:P0025","text":"τὸ μὲν οὖν κατὰ τὸ ποσὸν ἀδιαίρετον, τὸ μὲν πάντῃ καὶ ἄθετον λέγεται μονάς, τὸ δὲ πάντῃ καὶ θέσιν ἔχον στιγμή, τὸ δὲ μοναχῇ γραμμή, τὸ δὲ διχῇ ἐπίπεδον, τὸ δὲ πάντῃ καὶ τριχῇ διαιρετὸν κατὰ τὸ ποσὸν σῶμα· καὶ ἀντιστρέψαντι δὴ τὸ μὲν διχῇ διαιρετὸν ἐπίπεδον, τὸ δὲ μοναχῇ γραμμή, τὸ δὲ μηδαμῇ διαιρετὸν κατὰ τὸ ποσὸν στιγμὴ καὶ μονάς, ἡ μὲν ἄθετος μονὰς ἡ δὲ θετὸς στιγμή. ἔτι δὲ τὰ μὲν κατʼ ἀριθμόν ἐστιν ἕν, τὰ δὲ κατʼ εἶδος, τὰ δὲ κατὰ γένος, τὰ δὲ κατʼ ἀναλογίαν, ἀριθμῷ μὲν ὧν ἡ ὕλη μία, εἴδει δʼ ὧν ὁ λόγος εἷς, γένει δʼ ὧν τὸ αὐτὸ σχῆμα τῆς κατηγορίας, κατʼ ἀναλογίαν δὲ ὅσα ἔχει ὡς ἄλλο πρὸς ἄλλο. ἀεὶ δὲ τὰ ὕστερα τοῖς ἔμπροσθεν ἀκολουθεῖ, οἷον ὅσα ἀριθμῷ καὶ εἴδει ἕν, ὅσα δʼ εἴδει οὐ πάντα ἀριθμῷ·  ἀλλὰ γένει πάντα ἓν ὅσαπερ καὶ εἴδει, ὅσα δὲ γένει οὐ πάντα εἴδει ἀλλʼ ἀναλογίᾳ· ὅσα δὲ ἀνολογίᾳ οὐ πάντα γένει.","kind":"passage"},{"id":"RAW_DAZY_BOO5:P0026","text":"φανερὸν δὲ καὶ ὅτι τὰ πολλὰ ἀντικειμένως λεχθήσεται τῷ ἑνί· τὰ μὲν γὰρ τῷ μὴ συνεχῆ εἶναι, τὰ δὲ τῷ διαιρετὴν  ἔχειν τὴν ὕλην κατὰ τὸ εἶδος, ἢ τὴν πρώτην ἢ τὴν τελευταίαν, τὰ δὲ τῷ τοὺς λόγους πλείους τοὺς τί ἦν εἶναι λέγοντας.","kind":"passage"},{"id":"RAW_DAZY_BOO5:P0027","text":"τὸ ὂν λέγεται τὸ μὲν κατὰ συμβεβηκὸς τὸ δὲ καθʼ αὑτό, κατὰ συμβεβηκὸς μέν, οἷον τὸν δίκαιον μουσικὸν εἶναί φαμεν καὶ τὸν ἄνθρωπον μουσικὸν καὶ τὸν μουσικὸν ἄνθρωπον, παραπλησίως λέγοντες ὡσπερεὶ τὸν μουσικὸν οἰκοδομεῖν ὅτι συμβέβηκε τῷ οἰκοδόμῳ μουσικῷ εἶναι ἢ τῷ μουσικῷ οἰκοδόμῳ (τὸ γὰρ τόδε εἶναι τόδε σημαίνει τὸ συμβεβηκέναι τῷδε τόδε),—οὕτω δὲ καὶ ἐπὶ τῶν εἰρημένων· τὸν γὰρ ἄνθρωπον ὅταν μουσικὸν λέγωμεν καὶ τὸν μουσικὸν ἄνθρωπον, ἢ τὸν λευκὸν μουσικὸν ἢ τοῦτον λευκόν, τὸ μὲν ὅτι ἄμφω τῷ αὐτῷ συμβεβήκασι, τὸ δʼ ὅτι τῷ ὄντι συμβέβηκε, τὸ δὲ μουσικὸν ἄνθρωπον ὅτι τούτῳ τὸ μουσικὸν συμβέβηκεν (οὕτω δὲ λέγεται καὶ τὸ μὴ λευκὸν εἶναι, ὅτι ᾧ συμβέβηκεν, ἐκεῖνο ἔστιν)·—τὰ μὲν οὖν κατὰ συμβεβηκὸς εἶναι λεγόμενα οὕτω λέγεται ἢ διότι τῷ αὐτῷ ὄντι ἄμφω ὑπάρχει, ἢ ὅτι ὄντι ἐκείνῳ ὑπάρχει, ἢ ὅτι αὐτὸ ἔστιν ᾧ ὑπάρχει οὗ αὐτὸ κατηγορεῖται· καθʼ αὑτὰ δὲ εἶναι λέγεται ὅσαπερ σημαίνει τὰ σχήματα τῆς","kind":"passage"},{"id":"RAW_DAZY_BOO5:P0028","text":"κατηγορίας· ὁσαχῶς γὰρ λέγεται, τοσαυταχῶς τὸ εἶναι σημαίνει. ἐπεὶ οὖν τῶν κατηγορουμένων τὰ μὲν τί ἐστι σημαίνει, τὰ δὲ ποιόν, τὰ δὲ ποσόν, τὰ δὲ πρός τι, τὰ δὲ ποιεῖν ἢ πάσχειν, τὰ δὲ πού, τὰ δὲ ποτέ, ἑκάστῳ τούτων τὸ εἶναι ταὐτὸ σημαίνει· οὐθὲν γὰρ διαφέρει τὸ ἄνθρωπος ὑγιαίνων ἐστὶν ἢ τὸ ἄνθρωπος ὑγιαίνει, οὐδὲ τὸ ἄνθρωπος βαδίζων ἐστὶν ἢ τέμνων τοῦ ἄνθρωπος βαδίζει ἢ τέμνει, ὁμοίως δὲ καὶ ἐπὶ τῶν ἄλλων. ἔτι τὸ εἶναι σημαίνει καὶ τὸ ἔστιν ὅτι ἀληθές, τὸ δὲ μὴ εἶναι ὅτι οὐκ ἀληθὲς ἀλλὰ ψεῦδος, ὁμοίως ἐπὶ καταφάσεως καὶ ἀποφάσεως, οἷον ὅτι ἔστι Σωκράτης μουσικός, ὅτι ἀληθὲς τοῦτο, ἢ ὅτι ἔστι Σωκράτης οὐ λευκός, ὅτι ἀληθές· τὸ δʼ οὐκ ἔστιν ἡ διάμετρος σύμμετρος, ὅτι ψεῦδος.","kind":"passage"},{"id":"RAW_DAZY_BOO5:P0029","text":"ἔτι τὸ εἶναι σημαίνει καὶ τὸ ὂν τὸ μὲν δυνάμει ῥητὸν τὸ δʼ ἐντελεχείᾳ τῶν εἰρημένων τούτων· ὁρῶν τε γὰρ εἶναί φαμεν καὶ τὸ δυνάμει ὁρῶν καὶ τὸ ἐντελεχείᾳ, καὶ  ἐπίστασθαι ὡσαύτως καὶ τὸ δυνάμενον χρῆσθαι τῇ ἐπιστήμῃ καὶ τὸ χρώμενον, καὶ ἠρεμοῦν καὶ ᾧ ἤδη ὑπάρχει ἠρεμία καὶ τὸ δυνάμενον ἠρεμεῖν. ὁμοίως δὲ καὶ ἐπὶ τῶν οὐσιῶν· καὶ γὰρ Ἑρμῆν ἐν τῷ λίθῳ φαμὲν εἶναι, καὶ τὸ ἥμισυ τῆς γραμμῆς, καὶ σῖτον τὸν μήπω ἁδρόν. πότε δὲ δυνατὸν καὶ πότε οὔπω, ἐν ἄλλοις διοριστέον.","kind":"passage"},{"id":"RAW_DAZY_BOO5:P0030","text":"οὐσία λέγεται τά τε ἁπλᾶ σώματα, οἷον γῆ καὶ πῦρ καὶ ὕδωρ καὶ ὅσα τοιαῦτα, καὶ ὅλως σώματα καὶ τὰ ἐκ τούτων συνεστῶτα ζῷά τε καὶ δαιμόνια καὶ τὰ μόρια τούτων· ἅπαντα δὲ ταῦτα λέγεται οὐσία ὅτι οὐ καθʼ ὑποκειμένου λέγεται ἀλλὰ κατὰ τούτων τὰ ἄλλα. ἄλλον δὲ τρόπον ὃ ἂν ᾖ αἴτιον τοῦ εἶναι, ἐνυπάρχον ἐν τοῖς τοιούτοις ὅσα μὴ λέγεται καθʼ ὑποκειμένου, οἷον ἡ ψυχὴ τῷ ζῴῳ. ἔτι ὅσα μόρια ἐνυπάρχοντά ἐστιν ἐν τοῖς τοιούτοις ὁρίζοντά τε καὶ τόδε τι σημαίνοντα, ὧν ἀναιρουμένων ἀναιρεῖται τὸ ὅλον, οἷον ἐπιπέδου σῶμα, ὥς φασί τινες, καὶ ἐπίπεδον γραμμῆς· καὶ ὅλως ὁ ἀριθμὸς δοκεῖ εἶναί τισι τοιοῦτος (ἀναιρουμένου τε γὰρ οὐδὲν εἶναι, καὶ ὁρίζειν πάντα)· ἔτι τὸ τί ἦν εἶναι, οὗ ὁ λόγος ὁρισμός, καὶ τοῦτο οὐσία λέγεται ἑκάστου.","kind":"passage"},{"id":"RAW_DAZY_BOO5:P0031","text":"συμβαίνει δὴ κατὰ δύο τρόπους τὴν οὐσίαν λέγεσθαι, τό θʼ ὑποκείμενον ἔσχατον, ὃ μηκέτι κατʼ ἄλλου λέγεται, καὶ ὃ ἂν τόδε τι ὂν καὶ χωριστὸν ᾖ· τοιοῦτον δὲ ἑκάστου ἡ μορφὴ καὶ τὸ εἶδος.","kind":"passage"},{"id":"RAW_DAZY_BOO5:P0032","text":"ταὐτὰ λέγεται τὰ μὲν κατὰ συμβεβηκός, οἷον τὸ λευκὸν καὶ τὸ μουσικὸν τὸ αὐτὸ ὅτι τῷ αὐτῷ συμβέβηκε, καὶ ἄνθρωπος καὶ μουσικὸν ὅτι θάτερον θατέρῳ συμβέβηκεν, τὸ δὲ μουσικὸν ἄνθρωπος ὅτι τῷ ἀνθρώπῳ συμβέβηκεν· ἑκατέρῳ δὲ τοῦτο καὶ τούτῳ ἑκάτερον ἐκείνων, καὶ γὰρ τῷ ἀνθρώπῳ τῷ μουσικῷ καὶ ὁ ἄνθρωπος καὶ τὸ μουσικὸν ταὐτὸ λέγεται, καὶ τούτοις ἐκεῖνο (διὸ καὶ πάντα ταῦτα καθόλου οὐ λέγεται· οὐ γὰρ ἀληθὲς εἰπεῖν ὅτι πᾶς ἄνθρωπος ταὐτὸ καὶ τὸ μουσικόν· τὰ γὰρ καθόλου καθʼ αὑτὰ ὑπάρχει, τὰ δὲ συμβεβηκότα οὐ καθʼ αὑτά·  ἀλλʼ ἐπὶ τῶν καθʼ ἕκαστα ἁπλῶς λέγεται· ταὐτὸ γὰρ δοκεῖ Σωκράτης καὶ Σωκράτης εἶναι μουσικός· τὸ δὲ Σωκράτης οὐκ ἐπὶ πολλῶν, διὸ οὐ πᾶς Σωκράτης λέγεται ὥσπερ πᾶς ἄνθρωπος)·—καὶ τὰ μὲν οὕτως λέγεται ταὐτά, τὰ δὲ καθʼ αὑτὰ ὁσαχῶσπερ καὶ τὸ ἕν· καὶ γὰρ ὧν ἡ ὕλη μία ἢ εἴδει ἢ ἀριθμῷ ταὐτὰ λέγεται καὶ ὧν ἡ οὐσία μία, ὥστε φανερὸν ὅτι ἡ ταυτότης ἑνότης τίς ἐστιν ἢ πλειόνων τοῦ εἶναι ἢ","kind":"passage"},{"id":"RAW_DAZY_BOO5:P0033","text":"ὅταν χρῆται ὡς πλείοσιν, οἷον ὅταν λέγῃ αὐτὸ αὑτῷ ταὐτόν· ὡς δυσὶ γὰρ χρῆται αὐτῷ.—ἕτερα δὲ λέγεται ὧν ἢ τὰ εἴδη πλείω ἢ ἡ ὕλη ἢ ὁ λόγος τῆς οὐσίας· καὶ ὅλως ἀντικειμένως τῷ ταὐτῷ λέγεται τὸ ἕτερον.","kind":"passage"},{"id":"RAW_DAZY_BOO5:P0034","text":"διάφορα δὲ λέγεται ὅσʼ ἕτερά ἐστι τὸ αὐτό τι ὄντα, μὴ μόνον ἀριθμῷ ἀλλʼ ἢ εἴδει ἢ γένει ἢ ἀναλογίᾳ· ἔτι ὧν ἕτερον τὸ γένος, καὶ τὰ ἐναντία, καὶ ὅσα ἔχει ἐν τῇ οὐσίᾳ τὴν ἑτερότητα. ὅμοια λέγεται τά τε πάντῃ ταὐτὸ πεπονθότα, καὶ τὰ πλείω ταὐτὰ πεπονθότα ἢ ἕτερα, καὶ ὧν ἡ ποιότης μία· καὶ καθʼ ὅσα ἀλλοιοῦσθαι ἐνδέχεται τῶν ἐναντίων, τούτων τὸ πλείω ἔχον ἢ κυριώτερα ὅμοιον τούτῳ. ἀντικειμένως δὲ τοῖς ὁμοίοις τὰ ἀνόμοια.","kind":"passage"},{"id":"RAW_DAZY_BOO5:P0035","text":"ἀντικείμενα λέγεται ἀντίφασις καὶ τἀναντία καὶ τὰ πρός τι καὶ στέρησις καὶ ἕξις καὶ ἐξ ὧν καὶ εἰς ἃ ἔσχατα αἱ γενέσεις καὶ φθοραί· καὶ ὅσα μὴ ἐνδέχεται ἅμα παρεῖναι τῷ ἀμφοῖν δεκτικῷ, ταῦτα ἀντικεῖσθαι λέγεται ἢ αὐτὰ ἢ ἐξ ὧν ἐστίν. φαιὸν γὰρ καὶ λευκὸν ἅμα τῷ αὐτῷ οὐχ ὑπάρχει· διὸ ἐξ ὧν ἐστὶν ἀντίκειται. ἐναντία λέγεται τά τε μὴ δυνατὰ ἅμα τῷ αὐτῷ παρεῖναι τῶν διαφερόντων κατὰ γένος, καὶ τὰ πλεῖστον διαφέροντα τῶν ἐν τῷ αὐτῷ γένει, καὶ τὰ πλεῖστον διαφέροντα τῶν ἐν ταὐτῷ δεκτικῷ, καὶ τὰ πλεῖστον διαφέροντα τῶν ὑπὸ τὴν αὐτὴν δύναμιν, καὶ ὧν ἡ διαφορὰ μεγίστη ἢ ἁπλῶς ἢ κατὰ γένος ἢ κατʼ εἶδος. τὰ δʼ ἄλλα ἐναντία λέγεται τὰ μὲν τῷ τὰ τοιαῦτα ἔχειν, τὰ δὲ τῷ δεκτικὰ εἶναι τῶν τοιούτων, τὰ δὲ τῷ ποιητικὰ ἢ παθητικὰ εἶναι τῶν τοιούτων, ἢ ποιοῦντα ἢ πάσχοντα, ἢ ἀποβολαὶ ἢ λήψεις, ἢ ἕξεις ἢ στερήσεις εἶναι τῶν τοιούτων.","kind":"passage"},{"id":"RAW_DAZY_BOO5:P0036","text":"ἐπεὶ δὲ τὸ ἓν καὶ τὸ ὂν πολλαχῶς λέγεται, ἀκολουθεῖν ἀνάγκη καὶ τἆλλα ὅσα κατὰ ταῦτα λέγεται, ὥστε καὶ τὸ ταὐτὸν καὶ τὸ ἕτερον καὶ τὸ ἐναντίον, ὥστʼ εἶναι ἕτερον καθʼ ἑκάστην κατηγορίαν.—ἕτερα δὲ τῷ εἴδει λέγεται ὅσα τε ταὐτοῦ γένους ὄντα μὴ ὑπάλληλά ἐστι,  καὶ ὅσα ἐν τῷ αὐτῷ γένει ὄντα διαφορὰν ἔχει, καὶ ὅσα ἐν τῇ οὐσίᾳ ἐναντίωσιν ἔχει· καὶ τὰ ἐναντία ἕτερα τῷ εἴδει ἀλλήλων ἢ πάντα ἢ τὰ λεγόμενα πρώτως, καὶ ὅσων ἐν τῷ τελευταίῳ τοῦ γένους εἴδει οἱ λόγοι ἕτεροι (οἷον ἄνθρωπος καὶ ἵππος ἄτομα τῷ γένει οἱ δὲ λόγοι ἕτεροι αὐτῶν), καὶ ὅσα ἐν τῇ αὐτῇ οὐσίᾳ ὄντα ἔχει διαφοράν. ταὐτὰ δὲ τῷ εἴδει τὰ ἀντικειμένως λεγόμενα τούτοις.","kind":"passage"},{"id":"RAW_DAZY_BOO5:P0037","text":"πρότερα καὶ ὕστερα λέγεται ἔνια μέν, ὡς ὄντος τινὸς πρώτου καὶ ἀρχῆς ἐν ἑκάστῳ γένει, τῷ ἐγγύτερον εἶναι ἀρχῆς τινὸς ὡρισμένης ἢ ἁπλῶς καὶ τῇ φύσει ἢ πρός τι ἢ ποὺ ἢ ὑπό τινων, οἷον τὰ μὲν κατὰ τόπον τῷ εἶναι ἐγγύτερον ἢ φύσει τινὸς τόπου ὡρισμένου (οἷον τοῦ μέσου ἢ τοῦ ἐσχάτου) ἢ πρὸς τὸ τυχόν, τὸ δὲ πορρώτερον ὕστερον· τὰ δὲ κατὰ χρόνον (τὰ μὲν γὰρ τῷ πορρώτερον τοῦ νῦν, οἷον ἐπὶ τῶν γενομένων, πρότερον γὰρ τὰ Τρωϊκὰ τῶν Μηδικῶν ὅτι πορρώτερον ἀπέχει τοῦ νῦν· τὰ δὲ τῷ ἐγγύτερον τοῦ νῦν, οἷον ἐπὶ τῶν μελλόντων, πρότερον γὰρ Νέμεα Πυθίων ὅτι ἐγγύτερον τοῦ νῦν τῷ νῦν ὡς ἀρχῇ καὶ πρώτῳ χρησαμένων)· τὰ δὲ κατὰ κίνησιν (τὸ γὰρ ἐγγύτερον τοῦ πρώτου κινήσαντος πρότερον, οἷον παῖς ἀνδρός· ἀρχὴ δὲ καὶ αὕτη τις ἁπλῶς)· τὰ δὲ κατὰ δύναμιν (τὸ γὰρ ὑπερέχον τῇ δυνάμει πρότερον, καὶ τὸ δυνατώτερον· τοιοῦτον δʼ ἐστὶν οὗ κατὰ τὴν προαίρεσιν ἀνάγκη ἀκολουθεῖν θάτερον καὶ τὸ ὕστερον, ὥστε μὴ κινοῦντός","kind":"passage"},{"id":"RAW_DAZY_BOO5:P0038","text":"τε ἐκείνου μὴ κινεῖσθαι καὶ κινοῦντος κινεῖσθαι· ἡ δὲ προαίρεσις ἀρχή)· τὰ δὲ κατὰ τάξιν (ταῦτα δʼ ἐστὶν ὅσα πρός τι ἓν ὡρισμένον διέστηκε κατά τινα λόγον, οἷον παραστάτης τριτοστάτου πρότερον καὶ παρανήτη νήτης· ἔνθα μὲν γὰρ ὁ κορυφαῖος ἔνθα δὲ ἡ μέση ἀρχή)·—ταῦτα μὲν οὖν πρότερα τοῦτον λέγεται τὸν τρόπον, ἄλλον δὲ τρόπον τὸ τῇ γνώσει πρότερον ὡς καὶ ἁπλῶς πρότερον. τούτων δὲ ἄλλως τὰ κατὰ τὸν λόγον καὶ τὰ κατὰ τὴν αἴσθησιν. κατὰ μὲν γὰρ τὸν λόγον τὰ καθόλου πρότερα κατὰ δὲ τὴν αἴσθησιν τὰ καθʼ ἕκαστα· καὶ κατὰ τὸν λόγον δὲ τὸ συμβεβηκὸς τοῦ ὅλου πρότερον, οἷον τὸ μουσικὸν τοῦ μουσικοῦ ἀνθρώπου· οὐ γὰρ ἔσται ὁ λόγος ὅλος ἄνευ τοῦ μέρους· καίτοι οὐκ ἐνδέχεται μουσικὸν εἶναι μὴ ὄντος μουσικοῦ τινός. ἔτι πρότερα λέγεται τὰ τῶν προτέρων πάθη, οἷον εὐθύτης λειότητος· τὸ μὲν γὰρ γραμμῆς καθʼ αὑτὴν πάθος τὸ δὲ ἐπιφανείας.","kind":"passage"},{"id":"RAW_DAZY_BOO5:P0039","text":"τὰ μὲν δὴ οὕτω λέγεται πρότερα καὶ ὕστερα, τὰ δὲ κατὰ φύσιν καὶ οὐσίαν, ὅσα ἐνδέχεται εἶναι ἄνευ ἄλλων, ἐκεῖνα δὲ ἄνευ ἐκείνων μή· ᾗ διαιρέσει ἐχρήσατο Πλάτων. (ἐπεὶ δὲ τὸ εἶναι πολλαχῶς, πρῶτον μὲν τὸ ὑποκείμενον πρότερον, διὸ ἡ οὐσία πρότερον, ἔπειτα ἄλλως τὰ κατὰ δύναμιν καὶ κατʼ ἐντελέχειαν· τὰ μὲν γὰρ κατὰ δύναμιν πρότερά ἐστι τὰ δὲ κατὰ ἐντελέχειαν, οἷον κατὰ δύναμιν μὲν ἡ ἡμίσεια τῆς ὅλης καὶ τὸ μόριον τοῦ ὅλου καὶ ἡ ὕλη τῆς οὐσίας, κατʼ ἐντελέχειαν δʼ ὕστερον· διαλυθέντος γὰρ κατʼ ἐντελέχειαν ἔσται.) τρόπον δή τινα πάντα τὰ πρότερον καὶ ὕστερον λεγόμενα κατὰ ταῦτα λέγεται· τὰ μὲν γὰρ κατὰ γένεσιν ἐνδέχεται ἄνευ τῶν ἑτέρων εἶναι, οἷον τὸ ὅλον τῶν μορίων, τὰ δὲ κατὰ φθοράν, οἷον τὸ μόριον τοῦ ὅλου. ὁμοίως δὲ καὶ τἆλλα.","kind":"passage"},{"id":"RAW_DAZY_BOO5:P0040","text":"δύναμις λέγεται ἡ μὲν ἀρχὴ κινήσεως ἢ μεταβολῆς ἡ ἐν ἑτέρῳ ἢ ᾗ ἕτερον, οἷον ἡ οἰκοδομικὴ δύναμίς ἐστιν ἣ οὐχ ὑπάρχει ἐν τῷ οἰκοδομουμένῳ, ἀλλʼ ἡ ἰατρικὴ δύναμις οὖσα ὑπάρχοι ἂν ἐν τῷ ἰατρευομένῳ, ἀλλʼ οὐχ ᾗ ἰατρευόμενος. ἡ μὲν οὖν ὅλως ἀρχὴ μεταβολῆς ἢ κινήσεως λέγεται δύναμις ἐν ἑτέρῳ ἢ ᾗ ἕτερον, ἡ δʼ ὑφʼ ἑτέρου ἢ ᾗ ἕτερον (καθʼ ἣν γὰρ τὸ πάσχον πάσχει τι, ὁτὲ μὲν ἐὰν ὁτιοῦν, δυνατὸν αὐτό φαμεν εἶναι παθεῖν, ὁτὲ δʼ οὐ κατὰ πᾶν πάθος ἀλλʼ ἂν ἐπὶ τὸ βέλτιον)· ἔτι ἡ τοῦ καλῶς τοῦτʼ ἐπιτελεῖν ἢ κατὰ προαίρεσιν· ἐνίοτε γὰρ τοὺς μόνον ἂν πορευθέντας ἢ εἰπόντας, μὴ καλῶς δὲ ἢ μὴ ὡς προείλοντο, οὔ φαμεν δύνασθαι λέγειν ἢ βαδίζειν· ὁμοίως δὲ καὶ ἐπὶ τοῦ πάσχειν.","kind":"passage"},{"id":"RAW_DAZY_BOO5:P0041","text":"ἔτι ὅσαι ἕξεις καθʼ ἃς ἀπαθῆ ὅλως ἢ ἀμετάβλητα ἢ μὴ ῥᾳδίως ἐπὶ τὸ χεῖρον εὐμετακίνητα, δυνάμεις λέγονται· κλᾶται μὲν γὰρ καὶ συντρίβεται καὶ κάμπτεται καὶ ὅλως φθείρεται οὐ τῷ δύνασθαι ἀλλὰ τῷ μὴ δύνασθαι καὶ ἐλλείπειν τινός· ἀπαθῆ δὲ τῶν τοιούτων ἃ μόλις καὶ ἠρέμα πάσχει διὰ δύναμιν καὶ τῷ δύνασθαι καὶ τῷ ἔχειν πώς.","kind":"passage"},{"id":"RAW_DAZY_BOO5:P0042","text":"λεγομένης δὲ τῆς δυνάμεως τοσαυταχῶς, καὶ τὸ δυνατὸν ἕνα μὲν τρόπον λεχθήσεται τὸ ἔχον κινήσεως ἀρχὴν ἢ μεταβολῆς (καὶ γὰρ τὸ στατικὸν δυνατόν τι) ἐν ἑτέρῳ ἢ ᾗ ἕτερον, ἕνα δʼ ἐὰν ἔχῃ τι αὐτοῦ ἄλλο δύναμιν τοιαύτην,  ἕνα δʼ ἐὰν ἔχῃ μεταβάλλειν ἐφʼ ὁτιοῦν δύναμιν, εἴτʼ ἐπὶ τὸ χεῖρον εἴτʼ ἐπὶ τὸ βέλτιον (καὶ γὰρ τὸ φθειρόμενον δοκεῖ δυνατὸν εἶναι φθείρεσθαι, ἢ οὐκ ἂν φθαρῆναι εἰ ἦν ἀδύνατον· νῦν δὲ ἔχει τινὰ διάθεσιν καὶ αἰτίαν καὶ ἀρχὴν τοῦ τοιούτου πάθους· ὁτὲ μὲν δὴ τῷ ἔχειν τι δοκεῖ, ὁτὲ δὲ τῷ ἐστερῆσθαι τοιοῦτον εἶναι· εἰ δʼ ἡ στέρησίς ἐστιν ἕξις πως, πάντα τῷ ἔχειν ἂν εἴη τι,  ὥστε τῷ τε ἔχειν ἕξιν τινὰ καὶ ἀρχήν ἐστι δυνατὸν  καὶ τῷ ἔχειν τὴν τούτου στέρησιν, εἰ ἐνδέχεται ἔχειν στέρησιν· εἰ δὲ μή, ὁμωνύμως)· ἕνα δὲ τῷ μὴ ἔχειν αὐτοῦ δύναμιν ἢ ἀρχὴν ἄλλο ἢ ᾗ ἄλλο φθαρτικήν. ἔτι δὲ ταῦτα πάντα ἢ τῷ μόνον ἂν συμβῆναι γενέσθαι ἢ μὴ γενέσθαι, ἢ τῷ καλῶς.","kind":"passage"},{"id":"RAW_DAZY_BOO5:P0043","text":"καὶ γὰρ ἐν τοῖς ἀψύχοις ἔνεστιν ἡ τοιαύτη δύναμις, οἷον ἐν τοῖς ὀργάνοις· τὴν μὲν γὰρ δύνασθαί φασι φθέγγεσθαι λύραν, τὴν δʼ οὐδέν, ἂν ᾖ μὴ εὔφωνος. ἀδυναμία δὲ ἐστὶ στέρησις δυνάμεως καὶ τῆς τοιαύτης ἀρχῆς οἵα εἴρηται, ἢ ὅλως ἢ τῷ πεφυκότι ἔχειν, ἢ καὶ ὅτε πέφυκεν ἤδη ἔχειν· οὐ γὰρ ὁμοίως ἂν φαῖεν ἀδύνατον εἶναι γεννᾶν παῖδα καὶ ἄνδρα καὶ εὐνοῦχον. ἔτι δὲ καθʼ ἑκατέραν δύναμιν ἔστιν ἀδυναμία ἀντικειμένη, τῇ τε μόνον κινητικῇ καὶ τῇ καλῶς κινητικῇ.","kind":"passage"},{"id":"RAW_DAZY_BOO5:P0044","text":"καὶ ἀδύνατα δὴ τὰ μὲν κατὰ τὴν ἀδυναμίαν ταύτην λέγεται, τὰ δὲ ἄλλον τρόπον, οἷον δυνατόν τε καὶ ἀδύνατον, ἀδύνατον μὲν οὗ τὸ ἐναντίον ἐξ ἀνάγκης ἀληθές (οἷον τὸ τὴν διάμετρον σύμμετρον εἶναι ἀδύνατον ὅτι ψεῦδος τὸ τοιοῦτον οὗ τὸ ἐναντίον οὐ μόνον ἀληθὲς ἀλλὰ καὶ ἀνάγκη · τὸ ἄρα σύμμετρον οὐ μόνον ψεῦδος ἀλλὰ καὶ ἐξ ἀνάγκης ψεῦδος)· τὸ δʼ ἐναντίον τούτῳ, τὸ δυνατόν, ὅταν μὴ ἀναγκαῖον ᾖ τὸ ἐναντίον ψεῦδος εἶναι, οἷον τὸ καθῆσθαι ἄνθρωπον δυνατόν· οὐ γὰρ ἐξ ἀνάγκης τὸ μὴ καθῆσθαι ψεῦδος. τὸ μὲν οὖν δυνατὸν ἕνα μὲν τρόπον, ὥσπερ εἴρηται, τὸ μὴ ἐξ ἀνάγκης ψεῦδος σημαίνει, ἕνα δὲ τὸ ἀληθές , ἕνα δὲ τὸ ἐνδεχόμενον ἀληθὲς εἶναι. κατὰ μεταφορὰν δὲ ἡ ἐν γεωμετρίᾳ λέγεται δύναμις. ταῦτα μὲν οὖν τὰ δυνατὰ οὐ κατὰ δύναμιν· τὰ δὲ λεγόμενα κατὰ δύναμιν πάντα λέγεται πρὸς τὴν πρώτην ·  αὕτη δʼ ἐστὶν ἀρχὴ μεταβολῆς ἐν ἄλλῳ ἢ ᾗ ἄλλο.","kind":"passage"},{"id":"RAW_DAZY_BOO5:P0045","text":"τὰ γὰρ ἄλλα λέγεται δυνατὰ τῷ τὰ μὲν ἔχειν αὐτῶν ἄλλο τι τοιαύτην δύναμιν τὰ δὲ μὴ ἔχειν τὰ δὲ ὡδὶ ἔχειν. ὁμοίως δὲ καὶ τὰ ἀδύνατα. ὥστε ὁ κύριος ὅρος τῆς πρώτης δυνάμεως ἂν εἴη ἀρχὴ μεταβλητικὴ ἐν ἄλλῳ ἢ ᾗ ἄλλο.","kind":"passage"},{"id":"RAW_DAZY_BOO5:P0046","text":"ποσὸν λέγεται τὸ διαιρετὸν εἰς ἐνυπάρχοντα ὧν ἑκάτερον ἢ ἕκαστον ἕν τι καὶ τόδε τι πέφυκεν εἶναι. πλῆθος μὲν οὖν ποσόν τι ἐὰν ἀριθμητὸν ᾖ, μέγεθος δὲ ἂν μετρητὸν ᾖ. λέγεται δὲ πλῆθος μὲν τὸ διαιρετὸν δυνάμει εἰς μὴ συνεχῆ, μέγεθος δὲ τὸ εἰς συνεχῆ· μεγέθους δὲ τὸ μὲν ἐφʼ ἓν συνεχὲς μῆκος τὸ δʼ ἐπὶ δύο πλάτος τὸ δʼ ἐπὶ τρία βάθος. τούτων δὲ πλῆθος μὲν τὸ πεπερασμένον ἀριθμὸς μῆκος δὲ γραμμὴ πλάτος δὲ ἐπιφάνεια βάθος δὲ σῶμα. ἔτι τὰ μὲν λέγεται καθʼ αὑτὰ ποσά, τὰ δὲ κατὰ συμβεβηκός, οἷον ἡ μὲν γραμμὴ ποσόν τι καθʼ ἑαυτό, τὸ δὲ μουσικὸν κατὰ συμβεβηκός. τῶν δὲ καθʼ αὑτὰ τὰ μὲν κατʼ οὐσίαν ἐστίν, οἷον ἡ γραμμὴ ποσόν τι (ἐν γὰρ τῷ λόγῳ τῷ τί ἐστι λέγοντι τὸ ποσόν τι ὑπάρχει), τὰ δὲ πάθη καὶ ἕξεις τῆς τοιαύτης ἐστὶν οὐσίας, οἷον τὸ πολὺ καὶ τὸ ὀλίγον, καὶ μακρὸν καὶ βραχύ, καὶ πλατὺ καὶ στενόν, καὶ βαθὺ καὶ ταπεινόν, καὶ βαρὺ καὶ κοῦφον, καὶ τὰ ἄλλα τὰ τοιαῦτα.","kind":"passage"},{"id":"RAW_DAZY_BOO5:P0047","text":"ἔστι δὲ καὶ τὸ μέγα καὶ τὸ μικρὸν καὶ μεῖζον καὶ ἔλαττον, καὶ καθʼ αὑτὰ καὶ πρὸς ἄλληλα λεγόμενα, τοῦ ποσοῦ πάθη καθʼ αὑτά· μεταφέρονται μέντοι καὶ ἐπʼ ἄλλα ταῦτα τὰ ὀνόματα. τῶν δὲ κατὰ συμβεβηκὸς λεγομένων ποσῶν τὰ μὲν οὕτως λέγεται ὥσπερ ἐλέχθη ὅτι τὸ μουσικὸν ποσὸν καὶ τὸ λευκὸν τῷ εἶναι ποσόν τι ᾧ ὑπάρχουσι, τὰ δὲ ὡς κίνησις καὶ χρόνος· καὶ γὰρ ταῦτα πόσʼ ἄττα λέγεται καὶ συνεχῆ τῷ ἐκεῖνα διαιρετὰ εἶναι ὧν ἐστὶ ταῦτα πάθη. λέγω δὲ οὐ τὸ κινούμενον ἀλλʼ ὃ ἐκινήθη· τῷ γὰρ ποσὸν εἶναι ἐκεῖνο καὶ ἡ κίνησις ποσή, ὁ δὲ χρόνος τῷ ταύτην.","kind":"passage"},{"id":"RAW_DAZY_BOO5:P0048","text":"ποιὸν λέγεται ἕνα μὲν τρόπον ἡ διαφορὰ τῆς οὐσίας, οἷον ποιόν τι ἄνθρωπος ζῷον ὅτι δίπουν, ἵππος δὲ τετράπουν, καὶ κύκλος ποιόν τι σχῆμα ὅτι ἀγώνιον, ὡς τῆς διαφορᾶς τῆς κατὰ τὴν οὐσίαν ποιότητος οὔσης·  —ἕνα μὲν δὴ τρόπον τοῦτον λέγεται ἡ ποιότης διαφορὰ οὐσίας, ἕνα δὲ ὡς τὰ ἀκίνητα καὶ τὰ μαθηματικά, ὥσπερ οἱ ἀριθμοὶ ποιοί τινες, οἷον οἱ σύνθετοι καὶ μὴ μόνον ἐφʼ ἓν ὄντες ἀλλʼ ὧν μίμημα τὸ ἐπίπεδον καὶ τὸ στερεόν (οὗτοι δʼ εἰσὶν οἱ ποσάκις ποσοὶ ἢ ποσάκις ποσάκις ποσοί), καὶ ὅλως ὃ παρὰ τὸ ποσὸν ὑπάρχει ἐν τῇ οὐσίᾳ· οὐσία γὰρ ἑκάστου ὃ ἅπαξ, οἷον τῶν ἓξ οὐχ ὃ δὶς ἢ τρὶς εἰσὶν ἀλλʼ ὃ ἅπαξ· ἓξ γὰρ ἅπαξ ἕξ. ἔτι ὅσα πάθη τῶν κινουμένων οὐσιῶν, οἷον θερμότης καὶ ψυχρότης, καὶ λευκότης καὶ μελανία, καὶ βαρύτης καὶ κουφότης, καὶ ὅσα τοιαῦτα, καθʼ ἃ λέγονται καὶ ἀλλοιοῦσθαι τὰ σώματα μεταβαλλόντων. ἔτι κατʼ ἀρετὴν καὶ κακίαν καὶ ὅλως τὸ κακὸν καὶ ἀγαθόν.","kind":"passage"},{"id":"RAW_DAZY_BOO5:P0049","text":"σχεδὸν δὴ κατὰ δύο τρόπους λέγοιτʼ ἂν τὸ ποιόν, καὶ τούτων ἕνα τὸν κυριώτατον· πρώτη μὲν γὰρ ποιότης ἡ τῆς οὐσίας διαφορά (ταύτης δέ τι καὶ ἡ ἐν τοῖς ἀριθμοῖς ποιότης μέρος· διαφορὰ γάρ τις οὐσιῶν, ἀλλʼ ἢ οὐ κινουμένων ἢ οὐχ ᾗ κινούμενα), τὰ δὲ πάθη τῶν κινουμένων ᾗ κινούμενα, καὶ αἱ τῶν κινήσεων διαφοραί. ἀρετὴ δὲ καὶ κακία τῶν παθημάτων μέρος τι· διαφορὰς γὰρ δηλοῦσι τῆς κινήσεως καὶ τῆς ἐνεργείας, καθʼ ἃς ποιοῦσιν ἢ πάσχουσι καλῶς ἢ φαύλως τὰ ἐν κινήσει ὄντα· τὸ μὲν γὰρ ὡδὶ δυνάμενον κινεῖσθαι ἢ ἐνεργεῖν ἀγαθὸν τὸ δʼ ὡδὶ καὶ ἐναντίως μοχθηρόν. μάλιστα δὲ τὸ ἀγαθὸν καὶ τὸ κακὸν σημαίνει τὸ ποιὸν ἐπὶ τῶν ἐμψύχων, καὶ τούτων μάλιστα ἐπὶ τοῖς ἔχουσι προαίρεσιν.","kind":"passage"},{"id":"RAW_DAZY_BOO5:P0050","text":"πρός τι λέγεται τὰ μὲν ὡς διπλάσιον πρὸς ἥμισυ καὶ τριπλάσιον πρὸς τριτημόριον, καὶ ὅλως πολλαπλάσιον πρὸς πολλοστημόριον καὶ ὑπερέχον πρὸς ὑπερεχόμενον· τὰ δʼ ὡς τὸ θερμαντικὸν πρὸς τὸ θερμαντὸν καὶ τὸ τμητικὸν πρὸς τὸ τμητόν, καὶ ὅλως τὸ ποιητικὸν πρὸς τὸ παθητικόν· τὰ δʼ ὡς τὸ μετρητὸν πρὸς τὸ μέτρον καὶ ἐπιστητὸν πρὸς ἐπιστήμην καὶ αἰσθητὸν πρὸς αἴσθησιν.","kind":"passage"},{"id":"RAW_DAZY_BOO5:P0051","text":"λέγεται δὲ τὰ μὲν πρῶτα κατʼ ἀριθμὸν ἢ ἁπλῶς ἢ ὡρισμένως, πρὸς αὐτοὺς ἢ πρὸς ἕν (οἷον τὸ μὲν διπλάσιον πρὸς ἓν ἀριθμὸς ὡρισμένος, τὸ δὲ πολλαπλάσιον κατʼ ἀριθμὸν πρὸς ἕν, οὐχ ὡρισμένον δέ, οἷον τόνδε ἢ τόνδε·  τὸ δὲ ἡμιόλιον πρὸς τὸ ὑφημιόλιον κατʼ ἀριθμὸν πρὸς ἀριθμὸν ὡρισμένον· τὸ δʼ ἐπιμόριον πρὸς τὸ ὑπεπιμόριον κατὰ ἀόριστον, ὥσπερ τὸ πολλαπλάσιον πρὸς τὸ ἕν· τὸ δʼ ὑπερέχον πρὸς τὸ ὑπερεχόμενον ὅλως ἀόριστον κατʼ ἀριθμόν· ὁ γὰρ ἀριθμὸς σύμμετρος, κατὰ μὴ συμμέτρου δὲ ἀριθμὸς οὐ λέγεται, τὸ δὲ ὑπερέχον πρὸς τὸ ὑπερεχόμενον τοσοῦτόν τέ ἐστι καὶ ἔτι, τοῦτο δʼ ἀόριστον· ὁπότερον γὰρ ἔτυχέν ἐστιν, ἢ ἴσον ἢ οὐκ ἴσον)· ταῦτά τε οὖν τὰ πρός τι πάντα κατʼ ἀριθμὸν λέγεται καὶ ἀριθμοῦ πάθη, καὶ ἔτι τὸ ἴσον καὶ ὅμοιον καὶ ταὐτὸ κατʼ ἄλλον τρόπον (κατὰ γὰρ τὸ ἓν λέγεται πάντα, ταὐτὰ μὲν γὰρ ὧν μία ἡ οὐσία, ὅμοια δʼ ὧν ἡ ποιότης μία, ἴσα δὲ ὧν τὸ ποσὸν ἕν· τὸ δʼ ἓν τοῦ ἀριθμοῦ ἀρχὴ καὶ μέτρον,","kind":"passage"},{"id":"RAW_DAZY_BOO5:P0052","text":"ὥστε ταῦτα πάντα πρός τι λέγεται κατʼ ἀριθμὸν μέν, οὐ τὸν αὐτὸν δὲ τρόπον)· τὰ δὲ ποιητικὰ καὶ παθητικὰ κατὰ δύναμιν ποιητικὴν καὶ παθητικὴν καὶ ἐνεργείας τὰς τῶν δυνάμεων, οἷον τὸ θερμαντικὸν πρὸς τὸ θερμαντὸν ὅτι δύναται, καὶ πάλιν τὸ θερμαῖνον πρὸς τὸ θερμαινόμενον καὶ τὸ τέμνον πρὸς τὸ τεμνόμενον ὡς ἐνεργοῦντα. τῶν δὲ κατʼ ἀριθμὸν οὐκ εἰσὶν ἐνέργειαι ἀλλʼ ἢ ὃν τρόπον ἐν ἑτέροις εἴρηται· αἱ δὲ κατὰ κίνησιν ἐνέργειαι οὐχ ὑπάρχουσιν. τῶν δὲ κατὰ δύναμιν καὶ κατὰ χρόνους ἤδη λέγονται πρός τι οἷον τὸ πεποιηκὸς πρὸς τὸ πεποιημένον καὶ τὸ ποιῆσον πρὸς τὸ ποιησόμενον. οὕτω γὰρ καὶ πατὴρ υἱοῦ λέγεται πατήρ· τὸ μὲν γὰρ πεποιηκὸς τὸ δὲ πεπονθός τί ἐστιν. ἔτι ἔνια κατὰ στέρησιν δυνάμεως, ὥσπερ τὸ ἀδύνατον καὶ ὅσα οὕτω λέγεται, οἷον τὸ ἀόρατον.","kind":"passage"},{"id":"RAW_DAZY_BOO5:P0053","text":"τὰ μὲν οὖν κατʼ ἀριθμὸν καὶ δύναμιν λεγόμενα πρός τι πάντα ἐστὶ πρός τι τῷ ὅπερ ἐστὶν ἄλλου λέγεσθαι αὐτὸ ὅ ἐστιν, ἀλλὰ μὴ τῷ ἄλλο πρὸς ἐκεῖνο· τὸ δὲ μετρητὸν καὶ τὸ ἐπιστητὸν καὶ τὸ διανοητὸν τῷ ἄλλο πρὸς αὐτὸ λέγεσθαι πρός τι λέγονται. τό τε γὰρ διανοητὸν σημαίνει ὅτι ἔστιν αὐτοῦ διάνοια, οὐκ ἔστι δʼ ἡ διάνοια πρὸς τοῦτο οὗ ἐστὶ διάνοια (δὶς γὰρ ταὐτὸν εἰρημένον ἂν εἴη), ὁμοίως δὲ καὶ τινός ἐστιν ἡ ὄψις ὄψις, οὐχ οὗ ἐστὶν ὄψις (καίτοι γʼ ἀληθὲς τοῦτο εἰπεῖν) ἀλλὰ πρὸς χρῶμα ἢ πρὸς ἄλλο τι τοιοῦτον. ἐκείνως δὲ δὶς τὸ αὐτὸ λεχθήσεται, ὅτι ἐστὶν οὗ ἐστὶν ἡ ὄψις.","kind":"passage"},{"id":"RAW_DAZY_BOO5:P0054","text":"τὰ μὲν οὖν καθʼ ἑαυτὰ λεγόμενα πρός τι τὰ μὲν οὕτω λέγεται, τὰ δὲ ἂν τὰ γένη αὐτῶν ᾖ τοιαῦτα, οἷον ἡ ἰατρικὴ τῶν πρός τι ὅτι τὸ γένος αὐτῆς ἡ ἐπιστήμη δοκεῖ εἶναι πρός τι· ἔτι καθʼ ὅσα τὰ ἔχοντα λέγεται πρός τι, οἷον ἰσότης ὅτι τὸ ἴσον καὶ ὁμοιότης ὅτι τὸ ὅμοιον· τὰ δὲ κατὰ συμβεβηκός, οἷον ἅνθρωπος πρός τι ὅτι συμβέβηκεν αὐτῷ διπλασίῳ εἶναι, τοῦτο δʼ ἐστὶ τῶν πρός τι· ἢ τὸ λευκόν, εἰ τῷ αὐτῷ συμβέβηκε διπλασίῳ καὶ λευκῷ εἶναι.","kind":"passage"},{"id":"RAW_DAZY_BOO5:P0055","text":"τέλειον λέγεται ἓν μὲν οὗ μὴ ἔστιν ἔξω τι λαβεῖν μηδὲ ἓν μόριον (οἷον χρόνος τέλειος ἑκάστου οὗτος οὗ μὴ ἔστιν ἔξω λαβεῖν χρόνον τινὰ ὃς τούτου μέρος ἐστὶ τοῦ χρόνου), καὶ τὸ κατʼ ἀρετὴν καὶ τὸ εὖ μὴ ἔχον ὑπερβολὴν πρὸς τὸ γένος, οἷον τέλειος ἰατρὸς καὶ τέλειος αὐλητὴς ὅταν κατὰ τὸ εἶδος τῆς οἰκείας ἀρετῆς μηθὲν ἐλλείπωσιν (οὕτω δὲ μεταφέροντες καὶ ἐπὶ τῶν κακῶν λέγομεν συκοφάντην τέλειον καὶ κλέπτην τέλειον, ἐπειδὴ καὶ ἀγαθοὺς λέγομεν αὐτούς, οἷον κλέπτην ἀγαθὸν καὶ συκοφάντην ἀγαθόν· καὶ ἡ ἀρετὴ τελείωσίς τις· ἕκαστον γὰρ τότε τέλειον καὶ οὐσία πᾶσα τότε τελεία, ὅταν κατὰ τὸ εἶδος τῆς οἰκείας ἀρετῆς μηδὲν ἐλλείπῃ μόριον τοῦ κατὰ φύσιν μεγέθους)· ἔτι οἷς ὑπάρχει τὸ τέλος, σπουδαῖον ὄν, ταῦτα λέγεται τέλεια· κατὰ γὰρ τὸ ἔχειν τὸ τέλος τέλεια, ὥστʼ ἐπεὶ τὸ τέλος τῶν ἐσχάτων τί ἐστι, καὶ ἐπὶ τὰ φαῦλα μεταφέροντες λέγομεν τελείως ἀπολωλέναι καὶ τελείως ἐφθάρθαι, ὅταν μηδὲν ἐλλείπῃ τῆς","kind":"passage"},{"id":"RAW_DAZY_BOO5:P0056","text":"φθορᾶς καὶ τοῦ κακοῦ ἀλλʼ ἐπὶ τῷ ἐσχάτῳ ᾖ· διὸ καὶ ἡ τελευτὴ κατὰ μεταφορὰν λέγεται τέλος, ὅτι ἄμφω ἔσχατα· τέλος δὲ καὶ τὸ οὗ ἕνεκα ἔσχατον. τὰ μὲν οὖν καθʼ αὑτὰ λεγόμενα τέλεια τοσαυταχῶς λέγεται, τὰ μὲν τῷ κατὰ τὸ εὖ μηδὲν ἐλλείπειν μηδʼ ἔχειν ὑπερβολὴν μηδὲ ἔξω τι λαβεῖν, τὰ δʼ ὅλως κατὰ τὸ μὴ ἔχειν ὑπερβολὴν ἐν ἑκάστῳ γένει μηδʼ εἶναί τι ἔξω·  τὰ δὲ ἄλλα ἤδη κατὰ ταῦτα τῷ ἢ ποιεῖν τι τοιοῦτον ἢ ἔχειν ἢ ἁρμόττειν τούτῳ ἢ ἁμῶς γέ πως λέγεσθαι πρὸς τὰ πρώτως λεγόμενα τέλεια.","kind":"passage"},{"id":"RAW_DAZY_BOO5:P0057","text":"πέρας λέγεται τό τε ἔσχατον ἑκάστου καὶ οὗ ἔξω μηδὲν ἔστι λαβεῖν πρώτου καὶ οὗ ἔσω πάντα πρώτου, καὶ ὃ ἂν ᾖ εἶδος μεγέθους ἢ ἔχοντος μέγεθος, καὶ τὸ τέλος ἑκάστου (τοιοῦτον δʼ ἐφʼ ὃ ἡ κίνησις καὶ ἡ πρᾶξις, καὶ οὐκ ἀφʼ οὗ—ὁτὲ δὲ ἄμφω, καὶ ἀφʼ οὗ καὶ ἐφʼ ὃ καὶ τὸ οὗ ἕνεκα), καὶ ἡ οὐσία ἡ ἑκάστου καὶ τὸ τί ἦν εἶναι ἑκάστῳ· τῆς γνώσεως γὰρ τοῦτο πέρας· εἰ δὲ τῆς γνώσεως, καὶ τοῦ πράγματος. ὥστε φανερὸν ὅτι ὁσαχῶς τε ἡ ἀρχὴ λέγεται, τοσαυταχῶς καὶ τὸ πέρας, καὶ ἔτι πλεοναχῶς· ἡ μὲν γὰρ ἀρχὴ πέρας τι, τὸ δὲ πέρας οὐ πᾶν ἀρχή.","kind":"passage"},{"id":"RAW_DAZY_BOO5:P0058","text":"τὸ καθʼ ὃ λέγεται πολλαχῶς, ἕνα μὲν τρόπον τὸ εἶδος καὶ ἡ οὐσία ἑκάστου πράγματος, οἷον καθʼ ὃ ἀγαθός, αὐτὸ ἀγαθόν, ἕνα δὲ ἐν ᾧ πρώτῳ πέφυκε γίγνεσθαι, οἷον τὸ χρῶμα ἐν τῇ ἐπιφανείᾳ. τὸ μὲν οὖν πρώτως λεγόμενον καθʼ ὃ τὸ εἶδός ἐστι, δευτέρως δὲ ὡς ἡ ὕλη ἑκάστου καὶ τὸ ὑποκείμενον ἑκάστῳ πρῶτον. ὅλως δὲ τὸ καθʼ ὃ ἰσαχῶς καὶ τὸ αἴτιον ὑπάρξει· κατὰ τί γὰρ ἐλήλυθεν ἢ οὗ ἕνεκα ἐλήλυθε λέγεται, καὶ κατὰ τί παραλελόγισται ἢ συλλελόγισται, ἢ τί τὸ αἴτιον τοῦ συλλογισμοῦ ἢ παραλογισμοῦ. ἔτι δὲ τὸ καθʼ ὃ τὸ κατὰ θέσιν λέγεται, καθʼ ὃ ἕστηκεν ἢ καθʼ ὃ βαδίζει· πάντα γὰρ ταῦτα τόπον σημαίνει καὶ θέσιν. ὥστε καὶ τὸ καθʼ αὑτὸ πολλαχῶς ἀνάγκη λέγεσθαι. ἓν μὲν γὰρ καθʼ αὑτὸ τὸ τί ἦν εἶναι ἑκάστῳ, οἷον ὁ Καλλίας καθʼ αὑτὸν Καλλίας καὶ τὸ τί ἦν εἶναι Καλλίᾳ· ἓν δὲ ὅσα ἐν τῷ τί ἐστιν ὑπάρχει, οἷον ζῷον ὁ Καλλίας καθʼ αὑτόν· ἐν γὰρ τῷ λόγῳ ἐνυπάρχει τὸ ζῷον· ζῷον γάρ τι ὁ Καλλίας.","kind":"passage"},{"id":"RAW_DAZY_BOO5:P0059","text":"ἔτι δὲ εἰ ἐν αὑτῷ δέδεκται πρώτῳ ἢ τῶν αὑτοῦ τινί, οἷον ἡ ἐπιφάνεια λευκὴ καθʼ ἑαυτήν, καὶ ζῇ ὁ ἄνθρωπος καθʼ αὑτόν· ἡ γὰρ ψυχὴ μέρος τι τοῦ ἀνθρώπου, ἐν ᾗ πρώτῃ τὸ ζῆν. ἔτι οὗ μὴ ἔστιν ἄλλο αἴτιον· τοῦ γὰρ ἀνθρώπου πολλὰ αἴτια, τὸ ζῷον, τὸ δίπουν, ἀλλʼ ὅμως καθʼ αὑτὸν ἄνθρωπος ὁ ἄνθρωπός ἐστιν. ἔτι ὅσα μόνῳ ὑπάρχει καὶ ᾗ μόνον διʼ αὐτὸ κεχωρισμένον καθʼ αὑτό.","kind":"passage"},{"id":"RAW_DAZY_BOO5:P0060","text":"διάθεσις λέγεται τοῦ ἔχοντος μέρη τάξις ἢ κατὰ τόπον ἢ κατὰ δύναμιν ἢ κατʼ εἶδος· θέσιν γὰρ δεῖ τινὰ εἶναι, ὥσπερ καὶ τοὔνομα δηλοῖ ἡ διάθεσις.","kind":"passage"},{"id":"RAW_DAZY_BOO5:P0061","text":"ἕξις δὲ λέγεται ἕνα μὲν τρόπον οἷον ἐνέργειά τις τοῦ ἔχοντος καὶ ἐχομένου, ὥσπερ πρᾶξίς τις ἢ κίνησις (ὅταν γὰρ τὸ μὲν ποιῇ τὸ δὲ ποιῆται, ἔστι ποίησις μεταξύ· οὕτω καὶ τοῦ ἔχοντος ἐσθῆτα καὶ τῆς ἐχομένης ἐσθῆτος ἔστι μεταξὺ ἕξις)·—ταύτην μὲν οὖν φανερὸν ὅτι οὐκ ἐνδέχεται ἔχειν ἕξιν (εἰς ἄπειρον γὰρ βαδιεῖται, εἰ τοῦ ἐχομένου ἔσται ἔχειν τὴν ἕξιν), ἄλλον δὲ τρόπον ἕξις λέγεται διάθεσις καθʼ ἣν ἢ εὖ ἢ κακῶς διάκειται τὸ διακείμενον, καὶ ἢ καθʼ αὑτὸ ἢ πρὸς ἄλλο, οἷον ἡ ὑγίεια ἕξις τις· διάθεσις γάρ ἐστι τοιαύτη. ἔτι ἕξις λέγεται ἂν ᾖ μόριον διαθέσεως τοιαύτης· διὸ καὶ ἡ τῶν μερῶν ἀρετὴ ἕξις τίς ἐστιν.","kind":"passage"},{"id":"RAW_DAZY_BOO5:P0062","text":"πάθος λέγεται ἕνα μὲν τρόπον ποιότης καθʼ ἣν ἀλλοιοῦσθαι ἐνδέχεται, οἷον τὸ λευκὸν καὶ τὸ μέλαν, καὶ γλυκὺ καὶ πικρόν, καὶ βαρύτης καὶ κουφότης, καὶ ὅσα ἄλλα τοιαῦτα· ἕνα δὲ αἱ τούτων ἐνέργειαι καὶ ἀλλοιώσεις ἤδη. ἔτι τούτων μᾶλλον αἱ βλαβεραὶ ἀλλοιώσεις καὶ κινήσεις, καὶ μάλιστα αἱ λυπηραὶ βλάβαι. ἔτι τὰ μεγέθη τῶν συμφορῶν καὶ λυπηρῶν πάθη λέγεται.","kind":"passage"},{"id":"RAW_DAZY_BOO5:P0063","text":"στέρησις λέγεται ἕνα μὲν τρόπον ἂν μὴ ἔχῃ τι τῶν πεφυκότων ἔχεσθαι, κἂν μὴ αὐτὸ ᾖ πεφυκὸς ἔχειν, οἷον φυτὸν ὀμμάτων ἐστερῆσθαι λέγεται· ἕνα δὲ ἂν πεφυκὸς ἔχειν, ἢ αὐτὸ ἢ τὸ γένος, μὴ ἔχῃ, οἷον ἄλλως ἄνθρωπος ὁ τυφλὸς ὄψεως ἐστέρηται καὶ ἀσπάλαξ, τὸ μὲν κατὰ τὸ γένος τὸ δὲ καθʼ αὑτό. ἔτι ἂν πεφυκὸς καὶ ὅτε πέφυκεν ἔχειν μὴ ἔχῃ· ἡ γὰρ τυφλότης στέρησίς τις, τυφλὸς δʼ οὐ κατὰ πᾶσαν ἡλικίαν, ἀλλʼ ἐν ᾗ πέφυκεν ἔχειν, ἂν μὴ ἔχῃ. ὁμοίως δὲ καὶ ἐν ᾧ ἂν ᾖ πεφυκὸς καὶ καθʼ ὃ καὶ πρὸς ὃ καὶ ὥς, ἂν μὴ ἔχῃ . ἔτι ἡ βιαία ἑκάστου ἀφαίρεσις στέρησις λέγεται. καὶ ὁσαχῶς δὲ αἱ ἀπὸ τοῦ α ἀποφάσεις λέγονται, τοσαυταχῶς καὶ αἱ στερήσεις λέγονται· ἄνισον μὲν γὰρ τῷ μὴ ἔχειν ἰσότητα πεφυκὸς λέγεται, ἀόρατον δὲ καὶ τῷ ὅλως μὴ ἔχειν χρῶμα καὶ τῷ φαύλως, καὶ ἄπουν καὶ τῷ μὴ ἔχειν ὅλως πόδας καὶ τῷ φαύλους. ἔτι καὶ τῷ μικρὸν ἔχειν, οἷον τὸ ἀπύρηνον·  τοῦτο δʼ ἐστὶ τὸ φαύλως πως ἔχειν.","kind":"passage"},{"id":"RAW_DAZY_BOO5:P0064","text":"ἔτι τῷ μὴ ῥᾳδίως ἢ τῷ μὴ καλῶς, οἷον τὸ ἄτμητον οὐ μόνον τῷ μὴ τέμνεσθαι ἀλλὰ καὶ τῷ μὴ ῥᾳδίως ἢ μὴ καλῶς. ἔτι τῷ πάντῃ μὴ ἔχειν· τυφλὸς γὰρ οὐ λέγεται ὁ ἑτερόφθαλμος ἀλλʼ ὁ ἐν ἀμφοῖν μὴ ἔχων ὄψιν· διὸ οὐ πᾶς ἀγαθὸς ἢ κακός, ἢ δίκαιος ἢ ἄδικος, ἀλλὰ καὶ τὸ μεταξύ.","kind":"passage"},{"id":"RAW_DAZY_BOO5:P0065","text":"τὸ ἔχειν λέγεται πολλαχῶς, ἕνα μὲν τρόπον τὸ ἄγειν κατὰ τὴν αὑτοῦ φύσιν ἢ κατὰ τὴν αὑτοῦ ὁρμήν, διὸ λέγεται πυρετός τε ἔχειν τὸν ἄνθρωπον καὶ οἱ τύραννοι τὰς πόλεις καὶ τὴν ἐσθῆτα οἱ ἀμπεχόμενοι· ἕνα δʼ ἐν ᾧ ἄν τι ὑπάρχῃ ὡς δεκτικῷ, οἷον ὁ χαλκὸς ἔχει τὸ εἶδος τοῦ ἀνδριάντος καὶ τὴν νόσον τὸ σῶμα· ἕνα δὲ ὡς τὸ περιέχον τὰ περιεχόμενα· ἐν ᾧ γάρ ἐστι περιέχοντι, ἔχεσθαι ὑπὸ τούτου λέγεται, οἷον τὸ ἀγγεῖον ἔχειν τὸ ὑγρόν φαμεν καὶ τὴν πόλιν ἀνθρώπους καὶ τὴν ναῦν ναύτας, οὕτω δὲ καὶ τὸ ὅλον ἔχειν τὰ μέρη. ἔτι τὸ κωλῦον κατὰ τὴν αὑτοῦ ὁρμήν τι κινεῖσθαι ἢ πράττειν ἔχειν λέγεται τοῦτο αὐτό, οἷον καὶ οἱ κίονες τὰ ἐπικείμενα βάρη, καὶ ὡς οἱ ποιηταὶ τὸν Ἄτλαντα ποιοῦσι τὸν οὐρανὸν ἔχειν ὡς συμπεσόντʼ ἂν ἐπὶ τὴν γῆν, ὥσπερ καὶ τῶν φυσιολόγων τινές φασιν· τοῦτον δὲ τὸν τρόπον καὶ τὸ συνέχον λέγεται ἃ συνέχει ἔχειν, ὡς διαχωρισθέντα ἂν κατὰ τὴν αὑτοῦ ὁρμὴν ἕκαστον.","kind":"passage"},{"id":"RAW_DAZY_BOO5:P0066","text":"καὶ τὸ ἔν τινι δὲ εἶναι ὁμοτρόπως λέγεται καὶ ἑπομένως τῷ ἔχειν.","kind":"passage"},{"id":"RAW_DAZY_BOO5:P0067","text":"τὸ ἔκ τινος εἶναι λέγεται ἕνα μὲν τρόπον ἐξ οὗ ἐστὶν ὡς ὕλης, καὶ τοῦτο διχῶς, ἢ κατὰ τὸ πρῶτον γένος ἢ κατὰ τὸ ὕστατον εἶδος, οἷον ἔστι μὲν ὡς ἅπαντα τὰ τηκτὰ ἐξ ὕδατος, ἔστι δʼ ὡς ἐκ χαλκοῦ ὁ ἀνδριάς· ἕνα δʼ ὡς ἐκ τῆς πρώτης κινησάσης ἀρχῆς (οἷον ἐκ τίνος ἡ μάχη; ἐκ λοιδορίας, ὅτι αὕτη ἀρχὴ τῆς μάχης)· ἕνα δʼ ἐκ τοῦ συνθέτου ἐκ τῆς ὕλης καὶ τῆς μορφῆς, ὥσπερ ἐκ τοῦ ὅλου τὰ μέρη καὶ ἐκ τῆς Ἰλιάδος τὸ ἔπος καὶ ἐκ τῆς οἰκίας οἱ λίθοι· τέλος μὲν γάρ ἐστιν ἡ μορφή, τέλειον δὲ τὸ ἔχον τέλος. τὰ δὲ ὡς ἐκ τοῦ μέρους τὸ εἶδος, οἷον ἅνθρωπος ἐκ τοῦ δίποδος καὶ ἡ συλλαβὴ ἐκ τοῦ στοιχείου· ἄλλως γὰρ τοῦτο καὶ ὁ ἀνδριὰς ἐκ χαλκοῦ·  ἐκ τῆς αἰσθητῆς γὰρ ὕλης ἡ συνθετὴ οὐσία, ἀλλὰ καὶ τὸ εἶδος ἐκ τῆς τοῦ εἴδους ὕλης. τὰ μὲν οὖν οὕτω λέγεται, τὰ δʼ ἐὰν κατὰ μέρος τι τούτων τις ὑπάρχῃ τῶν τρόπων, οἷον ἐκ πατρὸς καὶ μητρὸς τὸ τέκνον καὶ ἐκ γῆς τὰ φυτά, ὅτι ἔκ τινος μέρους αὐτῶν.","kind":"passage"},{"id":"RAW_DAZY_BOO5:P0068","text":"ἕνα δὲ μεθʼ ὃ τῷ χρόνῳ, οἷον ἐξ ἡμέρας νὺξ καὶ ἐξ εὐδίας χειμών, ὅτι τοῦτο μετὰ τοῦτο· τούτων δὲ τὰ μὲν τῷ ἔχειν μεταβολὴν εἰς ἄλληλα οὕτω λέγεται, ὥσπερ καὶ τὰ νῦν εἰρημένα, τὰ δὲ τῷ κατὰ τὸν χρόνον ἐφεξῆς μόνον, οἷον ἐξ ἰσημερίας ἐγένετο ὁ πλοῦς ὅτι μετʼ ἰσημερίαν ἐγένετο, καὶ ἐκ Διονυσίων Θαργήλια ὅτι μετὰ τὰ Διονύσια.","kind":"passage"},{"id":"RAW_DAZY_BOO5:P0069","text":"μέρος λέγεται ἕνα μὲν τρόπον εἰς ὃ διαιρεθείη ἂν τὸ ποσὸν ὁπωσοῦν (ἀεὶ γὰρ τὸ ἀφαιρούμενον τοῦ ποσοῦ ᾗ ποσὸν μέρος λέγεται ἐκείνου, οἷον τῶν τριῶν τὰ δύο μέρος λέγεταί πως), ἄλλον δὲ τρόπον τὰ καταμετροῦντα τῶν τοιούτων μόνον· διὸ τὰ δύο τῶν τριῶν ἔστι μὲν ὡς λέγεται μέρος, ἔστι δʼ ὡς οὔ. ἔτι εἰς ἃ τὸ εἶδος διαιρεθείη ἂν ἄνευ τοῦ ποσοῦ, καὶ ταῦτα μόρια λέγεται τούτου· διὸ τὰ εἴδη τοῦ γένους φασὶν εἶναι μόρια. ἔτι εἰς ἃ διαιρεῖται ἢ ἐξ ὧν σύγκειται τὸ ὅλον, ἢ τὸ εἶδος ἢ τὸ ἔχον τὸ εἶδος, οἷον τῆς σφαίρας τῆς χαλκῆς ἢ τοῦ κύβου τοῦ χαλκοῦ καὶ ὁ χαλκὸς μέρος (τοῦτο δʼ ἐστὶν ἡ ὕλη ἐν ᾗ τὸ εἶδος) καὶ ἡ γωνία μέρος. ἔτι τὰ ἐν τῷ λόγῳ τῷ δηλοῦντι ἕκαστον, καὶ ταῦτα μόρια τοῦ ὅλου· διὸ τὸ γένος τοῦ εἴδους καὶ μέρος λέγεται, ἄλλως δὲ τὸ εἶδος τοῦ γένους μέρος.","kind":"passage"},{"id":"RAW_DAZY_BOO5:P0070","text":"ὅλον λέγεται οὗ τε μηθὲν ἄπεστι μέρος ἐξ ὧν λέγεται ὅλον φύσει, καὶ τὸ περιέχον τὰ περιεχόμενα ὥστε ἕν τι εἶναι ἐκεῖνα· τοῦτο δὲ διχῶς· ἢ γὰρ ὡς ἕκαστον ἓν ἢ ὡς ἐκ τούτων τὸ ἕν. τὸ μὲν γὰρ καθόλου, καὶ τὸ ὅλως λεγόμενον  ὡς ὅλον τι ὄν, οὕτως ἐστὶ καθόλου ὡς πολλὰ περιέχον τῷ κατηγορεῖσθαι καθʼ ἑκάστου καὶ ἓν ἅπαντα εἶναι ὡς ἕκαστον, οἷον ἄνθρωπον ἵππον θεόν, διότι ἅπαντα ζῷα· τὸ δὲ συνεχὲς καὶ πεπερασμένον, ὅταν ἕν τι ἐκ πλειόνων ᾖ, ἐνυπαρχόντων μάλιστα μὲν δυνάμει, εἰ δὲ μή, ἐνεργείᾳ. τούτων δʼ αὐτῶν μᾶλλον τὰ φύσει ἢ τέχνῃ τοιαῦτα, ὥσπερ καὶ ἐπὶ τοῦ ἑνὸς ἐλέγομεν, ὡς οὔσης τῆς ὁλότητος ἑνότητός τινος.  ἔτι τοῦ ποσοῦ ἔχοντος δὲ ἀρχὴν καὶ μέσον καὶ ἔσχατον, ὅσων μὲν μὴ ποιεῖ ἡ θέσις διαφοράν, πᾶν λέγεται, ὅσων δὲ ποιεῖ, ὅλον.","kind":"passage"},{"id":"RAW_DAZY_BOO5:P0071","text":"ὅσα δὲ ἄμφω ἐνδέχεται, καὶ ὅλα καὶ πάντα· ἔστι δὲ ταῦτα ὅσων ἡ μὲν φύσις ἡ αὐτὴ μένει τῇ μεταθέσει, ἡ δὲ μορφὴ οὔ, οἷον κηρὸς καὶ ἱμάτιον· καὶ γὰρ ὅλον καὶ πᾶν λέγεται· ἔχει γὰρ ἄμφω. ὕδωρ δὲ καὶ ὅσα ὑγρὰ καὶ ἀριθμὸς πᾶν μὲν λέγεται, ὅλος δʼ ἀριθμὸς καὶ ὅλον ὕδωρ οὐ λέγεται, ἂν μὴ μεταφορᾷ. πάντα δὲ λέγεται ἐφʼ οἷς τὸ πᾶν ὡς ἐφʼ ἑνί, ἐπὶ τούτοις τὸ πάντα ὡς ἐπὶ διῃρημένοις· πᾶς οὗτος ὁ ἀριθμός, πᾶσαι αὗται αἱ μονάδες.","kind":"passage"},{"id":"RAW_DAZY_BOO5:P0072","text":"κολοβὸν δὲ λέγεται τῶν ποσῶν οὐ τὸ τυχόν, ἀλλὰ μεριστόν τε δεῖ αὐτὸ εἶναι καὶ ὅλον. τά τε γὰρ δύο οὐ κολοβὰ θατέρου ἀφαιρουμένου ἑνός (οὐ γὰρ ἴσον τὸ καλόβωμα καὶ τὸ λοιπὸν οὐδέποτʼ ἐστίν) οὐδʼ ὅλως ἀριθμὸς οὐδείς· καὶ γὰρ τὴν οὐσίαν δεῖ μένειν· εἰ κύλιξ κολοβός, ἔτι εἶναι κύλικα· ὁ δὲ ἀριθμὸς οὐκέτι ὁ αὐτός. πρὸς δὲ τούτοις κἂν ἀνομοιομερῆ ᾖ, οὐδὲ ταῦτα πάντα (ὁ γὰρ ἀριθμὸς ἔστιν ὡς καὶ ἀνόμοια ἔχει μέρη, οἷον δυάδα τριάδα), ἀλλʼ ὅλως ὧν μὴ ποιεῖ ἡ θέσις διαφορὰν οὐδὲν κολοβόν, οἷον ὕδωρ ἢ πῦρ, ἀλλὰ δεῖ τοιαῦτα εἶναι ἃ κατὰ τὴν οὐσίαν θέσιν ἔχει. ἔτι συνεχῆ· ἡ γὰρ ἁρμονία ἐξ ἀνομοίων μὲν καὶ θέσιν ἔχει, κολοβὸς δὲ οὐ γίγνεται. πρὸς δὲ τούτοις οὐδʼ ὅσα ὅλα, οὐδὲ ταῦτα ὁτουοῦν μορίου στερήσει κολοβά.","kind":"passage"},{"id":"RAW_DAZY_BOO5:P0073","text":"οὐ γὰρ δεῖ οὔτε τὰ κύρια τῆς οὐσίας οὔτε τὰ ὁπουοῦν ὄντα· οἷον ἂν τρυπηθῇ ἡ κύλιξ, οὐ κολοβός, ἀλλʼ ἂν τὸ οὖς ἢ ἀκρωτήριόν τι, καὶ ὁ ἄνθρωπος οὐκ ἐὰν σάρκα ἢ τὸν σπλῆνα, ἀλλʼ ἐὰν ἀκρωτήριόν τι, καὶ τοῦτο οὐ πᾶν ἀλλʼ ὃ μὴ ἔχει γένεσιν ἀφαιρεθὲν ὅλον. διὰ τοῦτο οἱ φαλακροὶ οὐ κολοβοί.","kind":"passage"},{"id":"RAW_DAZY_BOO5:P0074","text":"γένος λέγεται τὸ μὲν ἐὰν ᾖ ἡ γένεσις συνεχὴς τῶν τὸ εἶδος ἐχόντων τὸ αὐτό, οἷον λέγεται ἕως ἂν ἀνθρώπων γένος ᾖ, ὅτι ἕως ἂν ᾖ ἡ γένεσις συνεχὴς αὐτῶν· τὸ δὲ ἀφʼ οὗ ἂν ὦσι πρώτου κινήσαντος εἰς τὸ εἶναι· οὕτω γὰρ λέγονται Ἕλληνες τὸ γένος οἱ δὲ Ἴωνες, τῷ οἱ μὲν ἀπὸ Ἕλληνος οἱ δὲ ἀπὸ Ἴωνος εἶναι πρώτου γεννήσαντος· καὶ μᾶλλον οἱ ἀπὸ τοῦ γεννήσαντος ἢ τῆς ὕλης (λέγονται γὰρ καὶ ἀπὸ τοῦ θήλεος τὸ γένος, οἷον οἱ ἀπὸ Πύρρας).  ἔτι δὲ ὡς τὸ ἐπίπεδον τῶν σχημάτων γένος τῶν ἐπιπέδων καὶ τὸ στερεὸν τῶν στερεῶν· ἕκαστον γὰρ τῶν σχημάτων τὸ μὲν ἐπίπεδον τοιονδὶ τὸ δὲ στερεόν ἐστι τοιονδί· τοῦτο δʼ ἐστὶ τὸ ὑποκείμενον ταῖς διαφοραῖς. ἔτι ὡς ἐν τοῖς λόγοις τὸ πρῶτον ἐνυπάρχον, ὃ λέγεται ἐν τῷ τί ἐστι, τοῦτο γένος, οὗ διαφοραὶ λέγονται αἱ ποιότητες.","kind":"passage"},{"id":"RAW_DAZY_BOO5:P0075","text":"τὸ μὲν οὖν γένος τοσαυταχῶς λέγεται, τὸ μὲν κατὰ γένεσιν συνεχῆ τοῦ αὐτοῦ εἴδους, τὸ δὲ κατὰ τὸ πρῶτον κινῆσαν ὁμοειδές, τὸ δʼ ὡς ὕλη· οὗ γὰρ ἡ διαφορὰ καὶ ἡ ποιότης ἐστί, τοῦτʼ ἔστι τὸ ὑποκείμενον, ὃ λέγομεν ὕλην. ἕτερα δὲ τῷ γένει λέγεται ὧν ἕτερον τὸ πρῶτον ὑποκείμενον καὶ μὴ ἀναλύεται θάτερον εἰς θάτερον μηδʼ ἄμφω εἰς ταὐτόν, οἷον τὸ εἶδος καὶ ἡ ὕλη ἕτερον τῷ γένει, καὶ ὅσα καθʼ ἕτερον σχῆμα κατηγορίας τοῦ ὄντος λέγεται (τὰ μὲν γὰρ τί ἐστι σημαίνει τῶν ὄντων τὰ δὲ ποιόν τι τὰ δʼ ὡς διῄρηται πρότερον)· οὐδὲ γὰρ ταῦτα ἀναλύεται οὔτʼ εἰς ἄλληλα οὔτʼ εἰς ἕν τι.","kind":"passage"},{"id":"RAW_DAZY_BOO5:P0076","text":"τὸ ψεῦδος λέγεται ἄλλον μὲν τρόπον ὡς πρᾶγμα ψεῦδος, καὶ τούτου τὸ μὲν τῷ μὴ συγκεῖσθαι ἢ ἀδύνατον εἶναι συντεθῆναι (ὥσπερ λέγεται τὸ τὴν διάμετρον εἶναι σύμμετρον ἢ τὸ σὲ καθῆσθαι· τούτων γὰρ ψεῦδος τὸ μὲν ἀεὶ τὸ δὲ ποτέ· οὕτω γὰρ οὐκ ὄντα ταῦτἀ, τὰ δὲ ὅσα ἔστι μὲν ὄντα, πέφυκε μέντοι φαίνεσθαι ἢ μὴ οἷα) ἐστιν ἢ ἃ μὴ ἔστιν (οἷον ἡ σκιαγραφία καὶ τὰ ἐνύπνια· ταῦτα γὰρ ἔστι μέν τι, ἀλλʼ οὐχ ὧν ἐμποιεῖ τὴν φαντασίαν)·—πράγματα μὲν οὖν ψευδῆ οὕτω λέγεται, ἢ τῷ μὴ εἶναι αὐτὰ ἢ τῷ τὴν ἀπʼ αὐτῶν φαντασίαν μὴ ὄντος εἶναι· λόγος δὲ ψευδὴς ὁ τῶν μὴ ὄντων, ᾗ ψευδής, διὸ πᾶς λόγος ψευδὴς ἑτέρου ἢ οὗ ἐστὶν ἀληθής, οἷον ὁ τοῦ κύκλου ψευδὴς τριγώνου.","kind":"passage"},{"id":"RAW_DAZY_BOO5:P0077","text":"ἑκάστου δὲ λόγος ἔστι μὲν ὡς εἷς, ὁ τοῦ τί ἦν εἶναι, ἔστι δʼ ὡς πολλοί, ἐπεὶ ταὐτό πως αὐτὸ καὶ αὐτὸ πεπονθός, οἷον Σωκράτης καὶ Σωκράτης μουσικός (ὁ δὲ ψευδὴς λόγος οὐθενός ἐστιν ἁπλῶς λόγος)· διὸ Ἀντισθένης ᾤετο εὐήθως μηθὲν ἀξιῶν λέγεσθαι πλὴν τῷ οἰκείῳ λόγῳ, ἓν ἐφʼ ἑνός· ἐξ ὧν συνέβαινε μὴ εἶναι ἀντιλέγειν, σχεδὸν δὲ μηδὲ ψεύδεσθαι. ἔστι δʼ ἕκαστον λέγειν οὐ μόνον τῷ αὐτοῦ λόγῳ ἀλλὰ καὶ τῷ ἑτέρου, ψευδῶς μὲν καὶ παντελῶς, ἔστι δʼ ὡς καὶ ἀληθῶς, ὥσπερ τὰ ὀκτὼ διπλάσια τῷ τῆς δυάδος λόγῳ.  τὰ μὲν οὖν οὕτω λέγεται ψευδῆ, ἄνθρωπος δὲ ψευδὴς ὁ εὐχερὴς καὶ προαιρετικὸς τῶν τοιούτων λόγων, μὴ διʼ ἕτερόν τι ἀλλὰ διʼ αὐτό, καὶ ὁ ἄλλοις ἐμποιητικὸς τῶν τοιούτων λόγων, ὥσπερ καὶ τὰ πράγματά φαμεν ψευδῆ εἶναι ὅσα ἐμποιεῖ φαντασίαν ψευδῆ. διὸ ὁ ἐν τῷ Ἱππίᾳ λόγος παρακρούεται ὡς ὁ αὐτὸς ψευδὴς καὶ ἀληθής.","kind":"passage"},{"id":"RAW_DAZY_BOO5:P0078","text":"τὸν δυνάμενον γὰρ ψεύσασθαι λαμβάνει ψευδῆ (οὗτος δʼ ὁ εἰδὼς καὶ ὁ φρόνιμος)· ἔτι τὸν ἑκόντα φαῦλον βελτίω. τοῦτο δὲ ψεῦδος λαμβάνει διὰ τῆς ἐπαγωγῆς—ὁ γὰρ ἑκὼν χωλαίνων τοῦ ἄκοντος κρείττων—τὸ χωλαίνειν τὸ μιμεῖσθαι λέγων, ἐπεὶ εἴ γε χωλὸς ἑκών, χείρων ἴσως, ὥσπερ ἐπὶ τοῦ ἤθους, καὶ οὗτος.","kind":"passage"},{"id":"RAW_DAZY_BOO5:P0079","text":"συμβεβηκὸς λέγεται ὃ ὑπάρχει μέν τινι καὶ ἀληθὲς εἰπεῖν, οὐ μέντοι οὔτʼ ἐξ ἀνάγκης οὔτε ὡς ἐπὶ τὸ πολύ, οἷον εἴ τις ὀρύττων φυτῷ βόθρον εὗρε θησαυρόν. τοῦτο τοίνυν συμβεβηκὸς τῷ ὀρύττοντι τὸν βόθρον, τὸ εὑρεῖν θησαυρόν· οὔτε γὰρ ἐξ ἀνάγκης τοῦτο ἐκ τούτου ἢ μετὰ τοῦτο, οὔθʼ ὡς ἐπὶ τὸ πολὺ ἄν τις φυτεύῃ θησαυρὸν εὑρίσκει. καὶ μουσικός γʼ ἄν τις εἴη λευκός· ἀλλʼ ἐπεὶ οὔτε ἐξ ἀνάγκης οὔθʼ ὡς ἐπὶ τὸ πολὺ τοῦτο γίγνεται, συμβεβηκὸς αὐτὸ λέγομεν. ὥστʼ ἐπεὶ ἔστιν ὑπάρχον τι καὶ τινί, καὶ ἔνια τούτων καὶ ποὺ καὶ ποτέ, ὅ τι ἂν ὑπάρχῃ μέν, ἀλλὰ μὴ διότι τοδὶ ἦν ἢ νῦν ἢ ἐνταῦθα, συμβεβηκὸς ἔσται. οὐδὲ δὴ αἴτιον ὡρισμένον οὐδὲν τοῦ συμβεβηκότος ἀλλὰ τὸ τυχόν· τοῦτο δʼ ἀόριστον. συνέβη τῳ εἰς Αἴγιναν ἐλθεῖν, εἰ μὴ διὰ τοῦτο ἀφίκετο ὅπως ἐκεῖ ἔλθῃ, ἀλλʼ ὑπὸ χειμῶνος ἐξωσθεὶς ἢ ὑπὸ λῃστῶν ληφθείς.","kind":"passage"},{"id":"RAW_DAZY_BOO5:P0080","text":"γέγονε μὲν δὴ ἢ ἔστι τὸ συμβεβηκός, ἀλλʼ οὐχ ᾗ αὐτὸ ἀλλʼ ᾗ ἕτερον· ὁ γὰρ χειμὼν αἴτιος τοῦ μὴ ὅπου ἔπλει ἐλθεῖν, τοῦτο δʼ ἦν Αἴγινα. λέγεται δὲ καὶ ἄλλως συμβεβηκός, οἷον ὅσα ὑπάρχει ἑκάστῳ καθʼ αὑτὸ μὴ ἐν τῇ οὐσίᾳ ὄντα, οἷον τῷ τριγώνῳ τὸ δύο ὀρθὰς ἔχειν. καὶ ταῦτα μὲν ἐνδέχεται ἀΐδια εἶναι, ἐκείνων δὲ οὐδέν. λόγος δὲ τούτου ἐν ἑτέροις.","kind":"passage"}],"sha256":"9f7477af9406f2a90e3ee10212e695a2c05914cf021a3f7410b51633bedd6786","language_code":"grc","language_name":"Ἑλληνική","translations":[{"id":"SOURCE_TR_20261015T172801410820Z_9c9342bb","language_code":"en","language_name":"English","title":"Book 5","source_sha256":"9f7477af9406f2a90e3ee10212e695a2c05914cf021a3f7410b51633bedd6786","sha256":"abe9df6f93621789e30db04ff2ba7b8e3d932bdd2d2bd1c7e68f050a4e2b6ff5","rendition_type":"machine","review_status":"machine","model":"gpt-5.6-terra","created":"2026-10-15T17:28:01.410846+00:00","paragraphs":[{"id":"RAW_DAZY_BOO5:P0001","text":"A principle is called, in one sense, that from which one might first move in respect of the thing, as, for example, this is a principle of length and of a road from this end, while from the opposite end another. In another sense, that from which each thing might come about best; thus, even in learning, one must sometimes begin not from what is first and from the principle of the thing, but from where one might learn most easily. In another sense, that from which a thing first comes to be, being present in it, as the keel of a ship and the foundation of a house; and among animals some suppose it to be the heart, others the brain, and others whatever thing of this sort they happen upon. In another sense, that from which a thing first comes to be, not being present in it, and from which motion and change are naturally first initiated, as a child from father and mother, and a fight from abuse. In another sense, that by whose choice things moved are moved, and things changing change; thus the offices in cities, and dynasties, kingships, and tyrannies are called principles, and so are the arts, especially the architectonic among them.","kind":"passage","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_DAZY_BOO5:P0001"]},{"id":"RAW_DAZY_BOO5:P0002","text":"Further, that from which a thing is first known is called a principle of the thing also, as the hypotheses of demonstrations. Causes too are spoken of in as many ways; for all causes are principles. It is common, then, to all principles to be that from which a thing either is, comes to be, or is known; and of these, some are present in the thing, others external. Hence nature is a principle, as are an element, thought, choice, substance, and that for the sake of which; for in many cases the good and the beautiful are principles both of knowing and of motion.","kind":"passage","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_DAZY_BOO5:P0002"]},{"id":"RAW_DAZY_BOO5:P0003","text":"A cause is called, in one sense, that from which something comes to be, being present in it, as bronze is the cause of a statue and silver of a bowl, and the genera of these. In another sense, the form and the paradigm; and this is the account of what-it-was-to-be, and the genera of this (as, for the octave, the ratio of two to one, and in general number), and the parts in the account. Further, that from which the first principle of change or of rest comes, as the one who deliberated is a cause, and the father is cause of the child, and in general the maker of what is made and the changer of what is changed. Further, as the end; this is that for the sake of which, as health is the end of walking. For why does he walk? we say. In order that he may be healthy. And having spoken thus, we think that we have given the cause. And whatever comes about intermediate to the end through the motion of another, as, for health, emaciation or purgation or drugs or instruments: for all these are for the sake of the end, though they differ from one another in that some are instruments and others works.","kind":"passage","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_DAZY_BOO5:P0003"]},{"id":"RAW_DAZY_BOO5:P0004","text":"Causes, then, are spoken of in approximately this many ways; but, since causes are spoken of in many ways, it happens that there are also many causes of the same thing, and not accidentally (as both the art of statue-making and bronze are causes of a statue, not in respect of something else but insofar as it is a statue; though not in the same way, but the one as matter and the other as that from which the motion comes). And things are causes of one another (as exertion is cause of good condition, and this of exertion; though not in the same way, but the one as end and the other as a principle of motion). Further, the same thing is cause of contraries; for what, when present, is cause of this, we sometimes blame, when absent, as cause of the contrary—for example, the absence of the steersman for the capsizing, whose presence was cause of preservation. Both, presence and privation, are causes as movers.—But all the causes now mentioned fall under the four most evident modes.","kind":"passage","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_DAZY_BOO5:P0004"]},{"id":"RAW_DAZY_BOO5:P0005","text":"For the elements of syllables, the matter of manufactured things, fire and earth and all such things among bodies, the parts of a whole, and the hypotheses of a conclusion, are causes as that from which a thing is. Of these, some are as the underlying subject, such as the parts, and others as the what-it-was-to-be, namely the whole, the composition, and the form. Seed, physician, deliberator, and in general the maker, are all that from which comes the principle of change or rest. Others are as the end and the good of the others; for that for the sake of which tends to be the best and the end of the others; and let it make no difference whether one calls it good or apparent good.—Such, and so many in form, are the causes; but the modes of causes are many in number, though when summed up these too are fewer.","kind":"passage","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_DAZY_BOO5:P0005"]},{"id":"RAW_DAZY_BOO5:P0006","text":"For causes are spoken of in many ways, and even among those of the same form one is prior and another posterior: for example, for health, the physician and the expert; and for the octave, the double and number; and always the encompassing universals in relation to any particular. Further, they are spoken of as the accidental and the genera of these: for a statue, Polycleitus in one way and statue-maker in another, because it is accidental to the statue-maker to be Polycleitus; and also the things encompassing the accident, as man is cause of the statue, or indeed animal in general, because Polycleitus is a man and man an animal. Among accidents too, some are more remote and others nearer than others, as if the white man and the musical man were said to be causes of the statue, and not merely Polycleitus or man. Besides all the causes spoken of properly and accidentally, some are spoken of as capable and others as active: for example, of the house's being built, a builder, or a builder building.","kind":"passage","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_DAZY_BOO5:P0006"]},{"id":"RAW_DAZY_BOO5:P0007","text":"Similarly, the things of which the causes are causes will be spoken of in the ways stated: for example, of this statue, or of a statue, or of an image in general; and of this bronze, or bronze, or matter in general; and likewise in the case of accidents. Further, both these and those will be spoken of in combination: for example, not Polycleitus nor statue-maker, but Polycleitus the statue-maker. Yet all these are six in number, and are spoken of in two ways: for either as the particular or as the genus, or as the accident or as the genus of the accident, or as these combined or as spoken of simply; and all either as active or in potentiality. They differ to this extent: active causes and particular causes exist and do not exist simultaneously with the things of which they are causes, as this physician treating this patient being healed, and this builder with this thing being built; but potential causes do not always do so, for the house and the builder do not perish at the same time.","kind":"passage","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_DAZY_BOO5:P0007"]},{"id":"RAW_DAZY_BOO5:P0008","text":"An element is called that first constituent thing, present in a compound, which is indivisible in form into another form; for example, the elements of speech are those from which speech is composed and into which it is ultimately divided, while they are no longer divisible into other sounds differing in form from them; but even if they are divided, their parts are of the same form, as a part of water is water, but not so a part of a syllable. Similarly, those who speak of the elements of bodies mean those into which bodies are ultimately divided, while these are no longer divided into others differing in form; and whether there be one or more such things, these they call elements. In a similar way, the elements of diagrams, and in general of demonstrations, are spoken of; for the first demonstrations, which are present in several demonstrations, are called elements of demonstrations; and such are the primary syllogisms from three terms through one middle term.","kind":"passage","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_DAZY_BOO5:P0008"]},{"id":"RAW_DAZY_BOO5:P0009","text":"By transference from this, they call an element whatever, being one and small, is useful for many things; hence even what is small and simple and indivisible is called an element. From this it has followed that the most universal things are elements, because each of them, being one and simple, is present in many things, either in all or in the greatest number; and the one and the point seem to some to be principles. Since, then, the things called genera are universal and indivisible (for there is no account of them), some call the genera elements, more so than the differentia, because the genus is more universal; for where the differentia belongs, the genus follows, but where the genus belongs, the differentia does not belong in every case. Common to all is that the element of each thing is the first constituent present in each thing.","kind":"passage","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_DAZY_BOO5:P0009"]},{"id":"RAW_DAZY_BOO5:P0010","text":"Nature is called, in one sense, the coming-to-be of growing things, as if one were to pronounce the υ with an extended sound; in another, that from which what grows first grows, being present in it. Further, that from which the first motion in each of the things that are by nature is present in it, insofar as it is itself. Things are said to grow when they have increase through another by touching and growing together or adhering, as embryos do. Growing together differs from touching: in the latter case there need be nothing other than the touch, but in things that have grown together there is some one and the same thing in both which makes them, instead of merely touching, grow together and be one in respect of continuity and quantity, but not in respect of quality.","kind":"passage","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_DAZY_BOO5:P0010"]},{"id":"RAW_DAZY_BOO5:P0011","text":"Further, nature is called that from which something that is by nature either is or comes to be first, it being unarranged and unchangeable from its own potentiality; for example, bronze is called the nature of a statue and of bronze implements, and wood that of wooden things; and likewise in the other cases. For each thing is from these, the primary matter being preserved. In this way they also say that the elements of things that are by nature are nature, some naming fire, others earth, others air, others water, others something else of this sort; some naming certain of these, others all of them. Further, in another way nature is called the substance of things that are by nature, as those who say that nature is the first composition, or as Empedocles says that","kind":"passage","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_DAZY_BOO5:P0011"]},{"id":"RAW_DAZY_BOO5:P0012","text":"there is no nature of any of the things that are,","kind":"passage","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_DAZY_BOO5:P0012"]},{"id":"RAW_DAZY_BOO5:P0013","text":"but only mixture and separation of things mixed","kind":"passage","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_DAZY_BOO5:P0013"]},{"id":"RAW_DAZY_BOO5:P0014","text":"exist; and ‘nature’ is a name applied to them by men.","kind":"passage","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_DAZY_BOO5:P0014"]},{"id":"RAW_DAZY_BOO5:P0015","text":"Empedocles Fr. 8 Therefore, even things that are or come to be by nature, when that from which they are naturally fitted to come to be or be is already present, we do not yet say have their nature unless they have form and shape. What is by nature, then, is that which is from both of these, as animals and their parts are. Nature is both the primary matter (and this in two ways: either what is primary in relation to the thing itself, or what is primary without qualification—for example, for works in bronze, bronze is first in relation to them, but perhaps water is first without qualification, if all meltable things are water) and the form and substance; and this is the end of coming-to-be. By transference, moreover, every substance is called nature because of this, since nature too is a kind of substance. From what has been said, then, the first nature, and that called nature in the primary sense, is the substance of things that have in themselves a principle of motion insofar as they are themselves. For matter is called nature because it is receptive of this, and generations and growing are called nature because they are motions from this. And this is the principle of motion of things that are by nature, being present in them somehow, either in potentiality or in actuality.","kind":"passage","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_DAZY_BOO5:P0015"]},{"id":"RAW_DAZY_BOO5:P0016","text":"Necessary is called that without which it is impossible to live, as a joint cause (for example, breathing and nourishment are necessary for an animal, since it is impossible for it to be without them); and those things without which the good cannot either be or come to be, or evil be cast off or removed (for example, drinking the medicine is necessary in order not to be ill, and sailing to Aegina in order to recover one's money). Further, the compulsory and compulsion; and this is what impedes and prevents contrary to impulse and choice. For the compulsory is called necessary, and hence painful (as Evenus says, “For every necessary thing is naturally grievous”), and compulsion is a kind of necessity (as Sophocles says, “But force compels me to do these things”); and necessity seems to be something not open to persuasion, rightly so, for it is contrary to motion according to choice and according to reasoning.","kind":"passage","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_DAZY_BOO5:P0016"]},{"id":"RAW_DAZY_BOO5:P0017","text":"Further, we say that what cannot be otherwise is necessarily so; and according to this necessary, all the other things too are in some way called necessary. For the compulsory is said to be necessary to do or suffer when one cannot act according to one's impulse because of what compels one, as though this were a necessity through which it cannot be otherwise; and similarly in the case of the joint causes of living and of the good. For when the good in one case, and life and being in another, cannot be without certain things, these things are necessary, and this cause is a kind of necessity. Further, demonstration concerns necessary things, because, if it has been demonstrated without qualification, it cannot be otherwise; and the causes of this are the first things, if the things from which the syllogism proceeds cannot be otherwise. For some things have another cause of their being necessary, but others have none; rather, because of these others are from necessity. Therefore, what is first and properly necessary is the simple; for this cannot be in more than one way, and hence not otherwise and otherwise; for it would then already be in more than one way.","kind":"passage","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_DAZY_BOO5:P0017"]},{"id":"RAW_DAZY_BOO5:P0018","text":"If, then, there are certain eternal and unmoved things, nothing is compulsory or contrary to nature for them.","kind":"passage","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_DAZY_BOO5:P0018"]},{"id":"RAW_DAZY_BOO5:P0019","text":"One is called either accidentally or in itself. Accidentally, for example, Coriscus and the musical, and musical Coriscus (for it is the same thing to say Coriscus and the musical, and musical Coriscus); and the musical and the just, and musical Coriscus and just Coriscus. For all these are called one accidentally: the just and the musical because they are accidents of one substance, and the musical and Coriscus because one is an accident of the other. In a certain way, similarly, musical Coriscus is one with Coriscus because one of the parts in the account is an accident of the other, as the musical is of Coriscus; and musical Coriscus is one with just Coriscus because a part of each is an accident of the same one thing.","kind":"passage","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_DAZY_BOO5:P0019"]},{"id":"RAW_DAZY_BOO5:P0020","text":"Similarly, even if the accident is said of a genus or of some universal name, as when man is said to be the same as musical man: either because the musical is an accident of the one substance, man, or because both are accidents of some particular, as of Coriscus. Yet the two do not belong in the same way: one perhaps as genus and in the substance, the other as a state or affection of the substance.—Such, then, is the way in which things are called one accidentally. Of things called one in themselves, some are so called because they are continuous, as a bundle by a binding and pieces of wood by glue; and a line, even if it is bent, provided it is continuous, is called one, as is each of the parts, such as a leg or an arm. Of these themselves, things continuous by nature are more one than those continuous by art. A continuous thing is one whose motion is one in itself and cannot be otherwise; and a motion is one if it is indivisible, and indivisible in time.","kind":"passage","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_DAZY_BOO5:P0020"]},{"id":"RAW_DAZY_BOO5:P0021","text":"Things are continuous in themselves when they are one not by contact; for if one were to place pieces of wood touching one another, one would not say that they are one—neither one piece of wood nor one body nor anything else continuous. Things wholly continuous, then, are called one even if they have a bend, and still more those without a bend, as shin or thigh are parts of a leg, because the motion of the leg may fail to be one. And the straight is more one than the bent. We say that the bent thing having an angle is both one and not one, because its motion can be both not simultaneous and simultaneous; but that of the straight is always simultaneous, and no part having magnitude is at rest while another moves, as happens with the bent.","kind":"passage","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_DAZY_BOO5:P0021"]},{"id":"RAW_DAZY_BOO5:P0022","text":"Further, one is called in another way because the underlying subject is undifferentiated in form; and things are undifferentiated whose form is indivisible to perception. The underlying subject is either the first or the last in relation to the end. For wine is called one, and water one, insofar as they are indivisible in form; and all liquids are called one (as oil and wine), as are all meltable things, because the ultimate underlying subject of all is the same; for all these are water or air. Things are also called one when their genus is one, differing by opposed differentiae—and all these are called one because the genus underlying the differentiae is one (as horse, man, and dog are one thing because all are animals), and in a manner similar to matter being one. These are sometimes called one in this way; but sometimes the higher genus is said to be the same—when they are ultimate species of the genus—as that which is above them: for example, isosceles and equilateral are the same and one figure because both are triangles; but they are not the same triangles.","kind":"passage","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_DAZY_BOO5:P0022"]},{"id":"RAW_DAZY_BOO5:P0023","text":"Further, one is said of those things whose account stating the what-it-was-to-be is indivisible in relation to another account declaring the thing (for every account is in itself divisible). For in this way too the increasing and diminishing thing is one, because the account is one, just as in the case of planes the account of the species is one. And in general, those things whose thought, in thinking the what-it-was-to-be, is indivisible and cannot separate them either in time or in place or in account, are most of all one, and among these, as many as are substances. For universally, as many things as have no division, insofar as they do not have it, are said to be one: for example, if, insofar as it is a man, it has no division, it is one man; if insofar as it is an animal, one animal; and if insofar as it is a magnitude, one magnitude. Most things, then, are said to be one by doing or having or undergoing something else that is one, or by being relative to something one; but those primarily said to be one are those whose substance is one, and it is one either by continuity or by species or by account. For we count as many either things that are not continuous, or things whose species is not one, or things whose account is not one.","kind":"passage","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_DAZY_BOO5:P0023"]},{"id":"RAW_DAZY_BOO5:P0024","text":"Further, in one way we say that anything whatever is one if it is a quantity and continuous, but in another way not, if it is not some whole; and this will be so if it does not have one species. For example, we would not say in the same way, on seeing the parts of a shoe put together in any fashion, that they are one, unless on account of continuity; rather, only if they are so arranged as to be a shoe and already have some one species. Hence also the circular line is most of all one among lines, because it is whole and complete.—To be one is to be a principle of some number; for the first measure is a principle, since that by which we first know is the first measure of each genus. Thus the one is the principle, in each case, of what is known. But the one is not the same in all genera: in one case it is a diesis, in another the vowel or the mute; and there is one for weight and another for motion. But everywhere the one is indivisible either in quantity or in species.","kind":"passage","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_DAZY_BOO5:P0024"]},{"id":"RAW_DAZY_BOO5:P0025","text":"Of what is indivisible in respect of quantity, that which is indivisible in every way and has no position is called a unit; that which is indivisible in every way and has position, a point; that which is divisible in one way, a line; that which is divisible in two ways, a plane; and that which is divisible in every way and in three ways in respect of quantity, a body. And, reversing the order, what is divisible in two ways is a plane, what is divisible in one way a line, and what is in no way divisible in respect of quantity a point and a unit—the unit being without position, and the point having position. Further, some things are one in number, others in species, others in genus, and others by analogy: in number, those whose matter is one; in species, those whose account is one; in genus, those having the same figure of category; and by analogy, all things which stand as one thing to another. The later always follow upon the earlier: things one in number are also one in species, but not all things one in species are one in number; yet all things one in species are one in genus, but not all things one in genus are one in species, but by analogy; and not all things one by analogy are one in genus.","kind":"passage","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_DAZY_BOO5:P0025"]},{"id":"RAW_DAZY_BOO5:P0026","text":"It is clear also that the many will be spoken of in opposition to the one: some by not being continuous, some by having matter divisible in species, whether the first or the last, and some by having several accounts stating the what-it-was-to-be.","kind":"passage","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_DAZY_BOO5:P0026"]},{"id":"RAW_DAZY_BOO5:P0027","text":"Being is said in one way accidentally, and in another in itself. Accidentally, for example, when we say that the just man is musical, and that the man is musical, and the musical man a man; speaking in a manner similar to saying that the musical man builds because it has happened that the builder is musical or the musical man a builder (for this thing's being that thing signifies that that has happened to this). So too in the cases mentioned: when we call the man musical and the musical man a man, or the white thing musical or this thing white, in one case it is because both have happened to the same thing, in another because it has happened to what is, and in another because musical has happened to this man (and in this way too non-white is said to be, because that to which it has happened is). Thus things said to be accidentally are so said either because both belong to the same being, or because one belongs to that being, or because it is itself that to which belongs that of which it is predicated. Things are said to be in themselves in as many ways as the figures of the","kind":"passage","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_DAZY_BOO5:P0027"]},{"id":"RAW_DAZY_BOO5:P0028","text":"categories signify; for in however many ways they are spoken of, in so many ways does being signify. Since, then, of predicates some signify what a thing is, others quality, others quantity, others relation, others acting or being acted upon, others where, and others when, being signifies the same thing as each of these. For it makes no difference whether one says “a man is healthy” or “a man is in health,” nor whether “a man is walking” or cutting and “a man walks” or cuts; and likewise in the other cases. Further, being and is signify that something is true, and not being that it is not true but false, alike in affirmation and negation: for example, “Socrates is musical,” because this is true; or “Socrates is not white,” because this is true; but “the diagonal is not commensurable,” because it is false.","kind":"passage","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_DAZY_BOO5:P0028"]},{"id":"RAW_DAZY_BOO5:P0029","text":"Further, being and what is signify, among the things stated, what is potential in one way and what is in actuality in another. For we say that both what sees potentially and what sees actually see; likewise, that both what can use knowledge and what is using it know; and that both that in which rest already belongs and that which can be at rest are at rest. Likewise also in the case of substances: for we say that Hermes is in the stone, and the half of the line, and that grain not yet ripe is grain. But when a thing is potential and when it is not yet so must be determined elsewhere.","kind":"passage","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_DAZY_BOO5:P0029"]},{"id":"RAW_DAZY_BOO5:P0030","text":"Substance is said of the simple bodies, such as earth and fire and water and things of this kind; and in general of bodies and of the things composed of these—animals and divine beings and the parts of these. All these are called substances because they are not said of a subject, but other things are said of them. In another way, it is said of whatever is the cause of being, present in such things as are not said of a subject, for example the soul in an animal. Further, it is said of whatever parts are present in such things, delimit them, and signify a this-something, upon whose removal the whole is removed—for example, a body in relation to a plane, as some say, and a plane in relation to a line. And in general number seems to some to be such a thing (for when it is removed, they say, nothing exists, and it delimits all things). Further, the what-it-was-to-be, whose account is a definition, is also called the substance of each thing.","kind":"passage","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_DAZY_BOO5:P0030"]},{"id":"RAW_DAZY_BOO5:P0031","text":"It follows, then, that substance is said in two ways: both the ultimate subject, which is no longer said of another, and whatever, being a this-something, is separable; and such is the form and species of each thing.","kind":"passage","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_DAZY_BOO5:P0031"]},{"id":"RAW_DAZY_BOO5:P0032","text":"The same is said accidentally in some cases: for example, the white and the musical are the same because both have happened to the same thing; and man and musical, because one of them has happened to the other; and the musical is a man because musical has happened to the man. And each of these is the same as the other and as this: for both man and musical are said to be the same as the musical man, and that is said to be the same as these. Hence none of all these is said universally; for it is not true to say that every man is the same as the musical. For universals belong in themselves, whereas accidents do not belong in themselves; but in the case of particulars it is said without qualification, since Socrates and musical Socrates seem to be the same. But “Socrates” is not said of many, and hence “every Socrates” is not said as “every man” is.—Some things, then, are said to be the same in this way; others in themselves, in as many ways as the one is said. For things whose matter is one either in species or in number, and things whose substance is one, are called the same; so that it is clear that sameness is a certain unity either of more than one thing in being or","kind":"passage","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_DAZY_BOO5:P0032"]},{"id":"RAW_DAZY_BOO5:P0033","text":"when it is treated as more than one, for example when one says that a thing is the same as itself; for one treats it as two.—Other things are said to be different when either their species or their matter or the account of their substance is more than one; and in general the different is spoken of in opposition to the same.","kind":"passage","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_DAZY_BOO5:P0033"]},{"id":"RAW_DAZY_BOO5:P0034","text":"Things are called different when, being other things, they are the same in some respect, not only in number but also in species or genus or analogy; further, things whose genus is different, contraries, and things that have otherness in their substance. Things are called like both when they have undergone the same thing in every respect, and when they have undergone more of the same things than different things, and when their quality is one. And among contraries in respect of which alteration is possible, that which has more, or the more dominant, is like that other. Unlike things are spoken of in opposition to like things.","kind":"passage","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_DAZY_BOO5:P0034"]},{"id":"RAW_DAZY_BOO5:P0035","text":"Opposites are called contradiction, contraries, relatives, privation and possession, and the terminal points from which and into which generations and corruptions occur; and whatever cannot be present together in that which is receptive of both is said to be opposed, either themselves or the things from which they are. For grey and white do not belong to the same thing at the same time; hence the things from which they are are opposed. Contraries are called both things, differing in genus, that cannot be present together in the same thing, and things differing most among those in the same genus, and things differing most among those in the same recipient, and things differing most among those under the same capacity, and things whose difference is greatest either without qualification or in genus or in species. Other things are called contraries, some because they have such things, some because they are receptive of such things, some because they are productive or affected in respect of such things, or produce or undergo them, or are losses or acquisitions, or are possessions or privations of such things.","kind":"passage","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_DAZY_BOO5:P0035"]},{"id":"RAW_DAZY_BOO5:P0036","text":"Since the one and being are said in many ways, it is necessary that the other things said in accordance with these also follow; thus the same, the different, and the contrary are different in each category.—Things are said to be different in species when, being of the same genus, they are not subordinate to one another; and when, being in the same genus, they have a difference; and when they have contrariety in substance. Contraries too are different in species from one another, either all contraries or those primarily so called; and so are things whose accounts are different in the ultimate species of the genus—for example, man and horse are indivisible in genus, but their accounts are different—and things which, being in the same substance, have a difference. The same in species are things spoken of oppositely to these.","kind":"passage","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_DAZY_BOO5:P0036"]},{"id":"RAW_DAZY_BOO5:P0037","text":"Prior and posterior are said in some cases by being nearer to some first thing and principle in each genus, whether to a principle determined simply and by nature, or relatively, or somewhere, or by certain people. For some things are prior in place by being nearer either by nature to some determined place, such as the middle or the extremity, or to a chance point, while what is farther away is posterior; others are prior in time—for things farther from the now, as in the case of things that have occurred, are prior, since the Trojan events are prior to the Median because they are more distant from the now; while things nearer the now, as in the case of future things, are prior, since the Nemean games are prior to the Pythian because they are nearer the now, using the now as principle and first; others are prior in motion, for what is nearer to the first mover is prior, as a boy is prior to a man; and this too is in some way a principle simply; others in capacity, for what exceeds in capacity and is more capable is prior. Such is that upon whose choice the other, the posterior, necessarily follows, so that, if it does not move, the other does not move, and if it moves, the other moves; and choice is a principle. Others are prior in order: these are things that are arranged at different distances from some one determined thing according to a certain account, for example, the second-from-last is prior to the third-from-last, and the paranētē to the nētē; for in one case the leader, and in the other the middle, is the principle.—These, then, are called prior in this way; but in another way, what is prior in knowledge is also prior without qualification. Of these, things prior in account and things prior in perception differ. For in account universals are prior, but in perception particulars; and in account the accident is prior to the whole, for example musical to musical man, since the whole account will not exist without the part, although musical cannot exist if no musical thing exists. Further, the attributes of prior things are called prior, for example straightness is prior to smoothness; for the former is an attribute of a line in itself, the latter of a surface.","kind":"passage","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_DAZY_BOO5:P0037"]},{"id":"RAW_DAZY_BOO5:P0038","text":"and if it moves, the other moves; and choice is a principle. Others are prior in order: these are things that are arranged at different distances from some one determined thing according to a certain account, for example, the second-from-last is prior to the third-from-last, and the paranētē to the nētē; for in one case the leader, and in the other the middle, is the principle.—These, then, are called prior in this way; but in another way, what is prior in knowledge is also prior without qualification. Of these, things prior in account and things prior in perception differ. For in account universals are prior, but in perception particulars; and in account the accident is prior to the whole, for example musical to musical man, since the whole account will not exist without the part, although musical cannot exist if no musical thing exists. Further, the attributes of prior things are called prior, for example straightness is prior to smoothness; for the former is an attribute of a line in itself, the latter of a surface.","kind":"passage","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_DAZY_BOO5:P0038"]},{"id":"RAW_DAZY_BOO5:P0039","text":"Some things, then, are called prior and posterior in these ways; others are so called in nature and substance: those which can be without other things, while those other things cannot be without them—a division which Plato employed. (Since being is said in many ways, first the subject is prior, and hence substance is prior; then, in another way, things are prior in potentiality and in actuality: for some are prior potentially, others actually—for example, potentially the half is prior to the whole, the part to the whole, and matter to substance, but actually they are posterior; for only when the whole has been dissolved will they be actually.) In a certain way, all things called prior and posterior are so called in accordance with these. For some can be without others in generation, for example the whole without the parts, while others can be without others in destruction, for example the part without the whole. Likewise also the rest.","kind":"passage","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_DAZY_BOO5:P0039"]},{"id":"RAW_DAZY_BOO5:P0040","text":"Capacity is called, in one sense, a principle of motion or change in another thing or insofar as it is another; for the capacity for building is one that is not present in the thing being built, whereas the medical capacity, though present in the thing being treated, may be present in it, but not insofar as it is being treated. Thus capacity is called, in general, a principle of change or motion in another thing or insofar as it is another; and also a principle of being changed by another thing or insofar as it is another (for in virtue of that by which the patient undergoes something, we sometimes say that it is capable of undergoing it if it undergoes anything whatever, but sometimes not in respect of every affection, but only if it is toward the better). Further, it is the capacity for accomplishing this well or according to choice; for we sometimes do not say that people who merely walk or speak, but do not do so well or as they chose, are able to speak or walk. Likewise in the case of being affected.","kind":"passage","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_DAZY_BOO5:P0040"]},{"id":"RAW_DAZY_BOO5:P0041","text":"Further, such states as render things altogether unaffected or unchangeable, or not readily changeable for the worse, are called capacities. For things are broken, crushed, bent, and in general destroyed not through being capable but through not being capable and through lacking something; and things are unaffected by such things when they are affected only with difficulty and slightly, through capacity and through being capable and being disposed in some way.","kind":"passage","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_DAZY_BOO5:P0041"]},{"id":"RAW_DAZY_BOO5:P0042","text":"Since capacity is spoken of in so many ways, the capable too will be spoken of in one way as that which has a principle of motion or change (for even what is capable of being at rest is something capable) in another thing or insofar as it is another; in another way, if something else has in it such a capacity; in another, if it has a capacity to change into anything whatever, whether for the worse or for the better. For even what is perishing seems capable of perishing, since it would not have perished if it were incapable; but as it is, it has some disposition and cause and principle of such an affection. Sometimes it seems to be such by having something, sometimes by being deprived of something; but if privation is in some way a state, everything would be so by having something, so that a thing is capable both by having some state and principle and by having the privation of this, if it is possible to have privation; but if not, homonymously. In another way, it is capable by not having another, or another insofar as it is another, capacity or principle destructive of it. Further, all these are capable either because the event may merely occur or not occur, or because it may occur well.","kind":"passage","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_DAZY_BOO5:P0042"]},{"id":"RAW_DAZY_BOO5:P0043","text":"For such a capacity is present even in inanimate things, for example in instruments: they say that one lyre can sound, but another cannot, if it is not tuneful. Incapacity is a privation of capacity and of such a principle as has been stated, either absolutely or in what is naturally fitted to have it, or even when it is already naturally fitted to have it. For they would not say in the same way that a child, a man, and a eunuch are incapable of begetting. Further, there is an opposed incapacity corresponding to each capacity, both to the merely motive capacity and to the capacity to move well.","kind":"passage","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_DAZY_BOO5:P0043"]},{"id":"RAW_DAZY_BOO5:P0044","text":"And “impossible” is said in one way in accordance with this impossibility, and in another way as are “possible” and “impossible”: impossible is that whose contrary is necessarily true (for example, that the diagonal is commensurable is impossible, because such a thing is false, whose contrary is not only true but also necessary; therefore, being commensurable is not only false but necessarily false); and its contrary, the possible, is when the contrary is not necessarily false, for example, that a human being is seated is possible; for it is not necessarily false that he is not seated. Thus the possible, in one way, as has been said, signifies what is not necessarily false; in another, what is true; and in another, what admits of being true. By metaphor, power is also spoken of in geometry. These, then, are possibles not in respect of power; but all things said in respect of power are said in relation to the primary sense; and this is a principle of change in another thing, or in the same thing qua other.","kind":"passage","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_DAZY_BOO5:P0044"]},{"id":"RAW_DAZY_BOO5:P0045","text":"For the other things are called capable because some of them have something else possessing such a power, others do not have it, and others have it in such-and-such a way. Likewise also the incapables. Hence the proper definition of the primary power would be: a principle productive of change in another thing, or in the same thing qua other.","kind":"passage","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_DAZY_BOO5:P0045"]},{"id":"RAW_DAZY_BOO5:P0046","text":"Quantity is said to be that which is divisible into inherent things, each or either of which is naturally one and a this. A plurality, then, is a quantity if it is numerable, and a magnitude if it is measurable. Plurality is said to be what is potentially divisible into non-continuous things, magnitude what is divisible into continuous things; and of magnitude, what is continuous in one direction is length, in two breadth, and in three depth. Of these, finite plurality is number, length is line, breadth surface, and depth body. Further, some things are called quantities in themselves, others incidentally; for example, a line is a quantity in itself, but the musical is so incidentally. Of things that are quantities in themselves, some are so according to substance, for example, a line is a quantity (for quantity belongs in the account stating what it is); others are affections and states of such substance, for example, much and little, long and short, broad and narrow, deep and shallow, heavy and light, and the other such things.","kind":"passage","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_DAZY_BOO5:P0046"]},{"id":"RAW_DAZY_BOO5:P0047","text":"Further, great and small, greater and lesser, are affections of quantity both when said in themselves and when said in relation to one another; these names, however, are also transferred to other things. Of quantities said incidentally, some are so called in the way stated, because the musical and the white are quantities through the thing in which they inhere being a quantity; others are so called as motion and time are. For these too are called quantities of a sort and continuous, because those things of which they are affections are divisible. I mean not the thing in motion, but that which was moved; for motion is quantitative because that is quantitative, and time because of this.","kind":"passage","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_DAZY_BOO5:P0047"]},{"id":"RAW_DAZY_BOO5:P0048","text":"Quality is said in one way to be the difference of substance: for example, a human being is an animal of a certain quality because it is two-footed, a horse because it is four-footed, and a circle is a figure of a certain quality because it is without angles, since the difference according to substance is a quality. —Thus, then, quality is said in this one way as a difference of substance; in another way, as the immovable things and mathematical objects are, as numbers are certain qualities, for example composite numbers and numbers not merely existing in one dimension, but of which the plane and the solid are imitations (these are numbers-times-numbers, or numbers-times-numbers-times-numbers), and in general whatever belongs in substance besides quantity. For the substance of each is what it is once, for example, in the case of six, not what they are twice or three times, but what it is once; for six is once six. Further, there are all the affections of moving substances, such as heat and coldness, whiteness and blackness, heaviness and lightness, and all such things, in accordance with which bodies, when they change, are said also to be altered. Further, there is quality according to virtue and vice, and in general the bad and the good.","kind":"passage","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_DAZY_BOO5:P0048"]},{"id":"RAW_DAZY_BOO5:P0049","text":"Thus quality might be said in approximately two ways, and of these one is the most proper. For the primary quality is the difference of substance (and the quality in numbers is in some way a part of this; for it is a certain difference of substances, but either of things not moved or not qua moved); the others are affections of moving things qua moving, and differences of motions. Virtue and vice are a certain part of affections; for they signify differences of motion and activity, in accordance with which things in motion act or are acted upon well or badly. For what is capable of being moved or acting in this way is good, and what is so capable in this way and contrariwise is bad. But quality signifies the good and bad most especially in living things, and among these especially in those having choice.","kind":"passage","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_DAZY_BOO5:P0049"]},{"id":"RAW_DAZY_BOO5:P0050","text":"Relatives are said in one way as double to half, and triple to a third; and in general multiple to submultiple, and exceeding to exceeded; in another, as what heats to what is heated, and what cuts to what is cut, and in general what produces to what is affected; and in another, as what is measurable to measure, what is knowable to knowledge, and what is perceptible to perception.","kind":"passage","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_DAZY_BOO5:P0050"]},{"id":"RAW_DAZY_BOO5:P0051","text":"The first of these are said according to number, either simply or determinate, in relation either to themselves or to one: for example, double is a determinate number in relation to one, while multiple is according to number in relation to one, but not a determinate number, such as this or that; sesquialteral is according to a determinate number in relation to a determinate number, while superparticular is according to an indeterminate number, as multiple is in relation to one; and exceeding in relation to exceeded is wholly indeterminate according to number. For number is commensurate, but number is not spoken of in relation to the incommensurate; whereas the exceeding in relation to the exceeded is so much and more, and this is indeterminate; for whichever happens to be the case, it is either equal or unequal. Thus all these relatives are said according to number and are affections of number; and further, equal, similar, and same are said in another way (for all are said according to one: things are the same whose substance is one, similar whose quality is one, and equal whose quantity is one; and one is the principle and measure of number,","kind":"passage","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_DAZY_BOO5:P0051"]},{"id":"RAW_DAZY_BOO5:P0052","text":"so that all these are called relatives according to number, though not in the same way). Productive and affective things, on the other hand, are relatives according to productive and affective power and the activities of the powers: for example, what heats is relative to what can be heated because it is capable, and again what is heating is relative to what is being heated, and what is cutting to what is being cut, as being active. But among things according to number there are no activities, except in the way stated in the other cases; activities according to motion are not present. Of things according to power, some are already called relatives also according to times, such as what has made in relation to what has been made, and what will make in relation to what will be made. For it is thus that a father is called father of a son; for the one is something that has acted, the other something that has been affected. Further, some are relatives according to privation of power, such as the incapable and whatever is so spoken of, for example the invisible.","kind":"passage","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_DAZY_BOO5:P0052"]},{"id":"RAW_DAZY_BOO5:P0053","text":"Things called relatives according to number and power are all relatives because what they are is said of another, but not because another is said in relation to them. But the measurable, the knowable, and the thinkable are called relatives because another is said in relation to them. For the thinkable signifies that there is thought of it, but thought is not said in relation to that of which it is thought (for then the same thing would have been said twice); similarly too sight is sight of something, not of that of which it is sight (although it is true to say this), but of colour or something else of that sort. In that other way, however, the same thing will be said twice: that it is that of which sight is sight.","kind":"passage","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_DAZY_BOO5:P0053"]},{"id":"RAW_DAZY_BOO5:P0054","text":"Of things called relatives in themselves, some are called so in this way, while others are so if their genera are such; for example, medicine is among relatives because its genus, knowledge, seems to be a relative. Further, they are so according to those things which possess them are called relatives; for example, equality, because the equal is relative, and similarity, because the similar is relative. Others are relatives incidentally, for example, a human being is a relative because it happens to be double, and this is among relatives; or the white, if it happens that the same thing is both double and white.","kind":"passage","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_DAZY_BOO5:P0054"]},{"id":"RAW_DAZY_BOO5:P0055","text":"Complete is said in one way of that outside which it is not possible to take even one part: for example, this is the complete time of each thing, outside which it is not possible to take any time that is a part of this time. It is also said of what, in respect of virtue and excellence, has no excess in relation to its kind: for example, a complete physician and a complete flute-player, when they lack nothing in the form of their proper virtue. Thus, by transference, we also speak of a complete informer and a complete thief in the case of bad things, since we call them good too, for example, a good thief and a good informer; and virtue is a kind of completion. For each thing is then complete, and every substance is then complete, when according to the form of its proper virtue it lacks no part of its natural magnitude. Further, things to which the end belongs, being something serious, are called complete; for they are complete by having the end. Hence, since the end is something ultimate, by transference we also say that things have been completely destroyed and completely corrupted when they lack nothing of","kind":"passage","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_DAZY_BOO5:P0055"]},{"id":"RAW_DAZY_BOO5:P0056","text":"corruption and evil but are at the ultimate point. Hence too death is metaphorically called an end, because both are ultimate; and the final cause is also ultimate. Thus things called complete in themselves are said in these many ways: some because they lack nothing in respect of excellence and have neither excess nor anything outside; others generally because they have no excess in each kind and there is nothing outside. The other things are now called complete in accordance with these, because they either produce something such, possess it, are fitted to it, or are somehow or other spoken of in relation to things primarily called complete.","kind":"passage","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_DAZY_BOO5:P0056"]},{"id":"RAW_DAZY_BOO5:P0057","text":"Limit is said to be both the extremity of each thing, and that outside of which nothing of the first thing can be taken and inside of which is everything of the first thing; and whatever is the form of a magnitude or of something having magnitude; and the end of each thing (such is that toward which motion and action are directed, and not that from which they proceed—though sometimes it is both, both that from which and that toward which, as well as that for the sake of which); and the substance of each thing and the what-it-was-to-be for each thing. For this is the limit of knowledge; and if of knowledge, then also of the thing. Hence it is clear that limit is said in as many ways as principle, and in still more ways; for principle is a certain limit, but not every limit is a principle.","kind":"passage","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_DAZY_BOO5:P0057"]},{"id":"RAW_DAZY_BOO5:P0058","text":"That according to which is said in many ways: in one way, the form and substance of each thing, for example, that according to which something is good is the good itself; in another, that in which it is naturally fitted first to come to be, for example, colour in a surface. Thus what is primarily called that according to which is the form, and secondarily the matter of each thing and what is first underlying each thing. In general, that according to which will belong in as many ways as cause; for “on account of what did he come?” or “for the sake of what did he come?” is said, and “on account of what has he reasoned falsely or reasoned syllogistically?” or “what is the cause of the syllogism or false inference?” Further, that according to which also signifies position: that according to which something stands or that according to which it walks; for all these signify place and position. Hence also per se must be said in many ways. For in one way, per se is the what-it-was-to-be for each thing: for example, Callias is per se Callias, and the what-it-was-to-be for Callias. In another way, it is whatever belongs in what a thing is: for example, Callias is an animal per se; for animal is present in the account, since Callias is a certain animal.","kind":"passage","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_DAZY_BOO5:P0058"]},{"id":"RAW_DAZY_BOO5:P0059","text":"Further, it is what has been received first in itself or in some part of itself: for example, a surface is white per se, and a human being lives per se; for the soul is a certain part of the human being, in which life is first present. Further, it is that of which there is no other cause. For there are many causes of a human being—animal and two-footed—but nevertheless the human being is per se human. Further, whatever belongs to a thing alone, and qua alone is separated through itself, is per se.","kind":"passage","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_DAZY_BOO5:P0059"]},{"id":"RAW_DAZY_BOO5:P0060","text":"Disposition is said to be the arrangement of that which has parts, whether according to place, power, or form; for there must be some positioning, as the name disposition also indicates.","kind":"passage","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_DAZY_BOO5:P0060"]},{"id":"RAW_DAZY_BOO5:P0061","text":"Having is said in one way to be a kind of actuality of what has and what is had, as a kind of action or motion (for when the one acts and the other is acted upon, there is production between them; similarly, between the one having a garment and the garment that is had, there is having). It is clear, then, that it is not possible to have this sort of having (for there will be an infinite regress if there is to be a having of what is had). In another way, having is called a disposition in accordance with which the thing disposed is disposed either well or badly, either in itself or in relation to another, as health is a certain having; for it is such a disposition. Further, having is said if it is a part of such a disposition; hence the virtue of the parts too is a certain having.","kind":"passage","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_DAZY_BOO5:P0061"]},{"id":"RAW_DAZY_BOO5:P0062","text":"Affection is said in one way to be a quality in accordance with which alteration is possible, such as white and black, sweet and bitter, heaviness and lightness, and all other such things; in another, the actualities and already actual alterations of these. Further, more especially, it is harmful alterations and motions, and most especially painful injuries. Further, the magnitudes of misfortunes and painful things are called affections.","kind":"passage","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_DAZY_BOO5:P0062"]},{"id":"RAW_DAZY_BOO5:P0063","text":"Privation is said in one way when a thing does not have something which things are naturally fitted to have, even if it itself is not naturally fitted to have it, as a plant is said to be deprived of eyes. In another way, it is when either the thing itself or its genus is naturally fitted to have something but does not have it; for example, a human being and a mole are deprived of sight in different ways, the latter according to its genus, the former in itself. Further, it is when a thing naturally fitted to have something does not have it at the time when it is naturally fitted to have it; for blindness is a certain privation, but one is not blind at every age, but when one is naturally fitted to have sight and does not have it. Likewise also in that in which, according to which, in relation to which, and in the way in which it is naturally fitted to have something, if it does not have it. Further, the forcible removal of anything is called privation. And in as many ways as negations formed with a- are spoken of, privations too are spoken of in as many ways. For unequal is said of what, though naturally fitted to have equality, does not have it; invisible is said both of what has no colour at all and of what has it badly; and footless is said both of what has no feet at all and of what has bad feet. Further, it is said also of what has something only slightly, as seedless; and this is to be in some way badly disposed.","kind":"passage","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_DAZY_BOO5:P0063"]},{"id":"RAW_DAZY_BOO5:P0064","text":"Further, it is said of what is not easy or not good: for example, uncuttable is said not only of what cannot be cut but also of what cannot be cut easily or well. Further, it is said of what does not have something in every respect; for one-eyed is not called blind, but only what has no sight in both eyes. Hence not everything is either good or bad, or just or unjust, but there is also the intermediate.","kind":"passage","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_DAZY_BOO5:P0064"]},{"id":"RAW_DAZY_BOO5:P0065","text":"Having is said in many ways. In one way, it is to lead according to one's own nature or impulse; hence fever is said to have a human being, tyrants cities, and those who are clothed a garment. In another, it is that in which something is present as in a recipient: for example, bronze has the form of a statue, and the body has disease. In another, it is as the containing has what is contained; for that which is in a container is said to be held by it: for example, we say that a vessel has liquid, a city has human beings, and a ship has sailors; and likewise the whole has its parts. Further, that which prevents something from being moved or acting according to its own impulse is said to have that thing, as columns have the weights laid upon them, and as poets make Atlas hold the heaven, which otherwise would fall upon the earth, as some of the natural philosophers also say. In this way too, what holds together is said to have the things which it holds together, since each would be separated according to its own impulse if they were separated.","kind":"passage","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_DAZY_BOO5:P0065"]},{"id":"RAW_DAZY_BOO5:P0066","text":"And being in something is said in a similar way and consequently to having.","kind":"passage","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_DAZY_BOO5:P0066"]},{"id":"RAW_DAZY_BOO5:P0067","text":"“To be from something” is said in one way of that from which a thing is as from matter, and this in two ways: either according to the first genus or according to the ultimate species; for example, all fusible things are from water in one way, while the statue is from bronze in another. In one way it is said with reference to the first moving principle (for example, from what is a battle? From abuse, because this is a principle of the battle). In one way it is said from the composite of matter and form, as the parts are from the whole, the verse from the Iliad, and the stones from the house; for the form is an end, and that which has an end is complete. Some things are said to be from the part as the species, for example, man from the two-footed and a syllable from an element; for this is otherwise than the statue from bronze. For the composite substance is from sensible matter, but the form too is from the matter of the form. Some things, then, are said thus; others, if someone of these modes belongs to them in part—for example, the child is from father and mother, and plants from earth, because they are from some part of them.","kind":"passage","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_DAZY_BOO5:P0067"]},{"id":"RAW_DAZY_BOO5:P0068","text":"In one way it is said of that which is after something in time, as night from day and winter from fair weather, because this is after that. Of these, some are so said because they have change into one another, as also the things just mentioned; others only because of temporal succession, as a voyage took place from the equinox because it took place after the equinox, and the Thargelia from the Dionysia because they are after the Dionysia.","kind":"passage","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_DAZY_BOO5:P0068"]},{"id":"RAW_DAZY_BOO5:P0069","text":"A part is said, in one way, to be that into which a quantity may be divided in any manner whatsoever (for what is taken away from a quantity, qua quantity, is always called a part of it; for example, two of three is in some way called a part). In another way, it is said only of those things which measure out such quantities; hence two of three is in one sense said to be a part, and in another sense not.\n\nAgain, those into which the form may be divided apart from quantity are called parts of it; hence they say that the species are parts of the genus. Again, the things into which the whole, or the form, or that which has the form, is divided, or from which it is composed; for example, of the bronze sphere or the bronze cube, both bronze is a part (this is the matter in which the form is) and the angle is a part. Again, the things in the account which makes each thing clear are also parts of the whole; hence the genus too is called a part of the species, though in another way the species is a part of the genus.","kind":"passage","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_DAZY_BOO5:P0069"]},{"id":"RAW_DAZY_BOO5:P0070","text":"A whole is said to be that from which no part is absent of those things from which it is naturally called a whole; and that which contains the things contained so that they are some one thing. This is in two ways: either as each is one, or as the one is from these. For the universal, and that which is spoken of simply as being some whole, is universal in this way: as containing many by being predicated of each, and as all being one as each—for example, man, horse, god, because all are animals. But the continuous and limited is a whole whenever some one thing is from several, especially when they are present potentially, or, if not, actually. Of these things themselves, those which are such by nature are more so than those which are such by art, as we said also in the case of the one, since wholeness is a certain unity.\n\nAgain, among quantities having a beginning, middle, and end, those in which position makes no difference are called “all,” while those in which it does make a difference are called “whole.”","kind":"passage","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_DAZY_BOO5:P0070"]},{"id":"RAW_DAZY_BOO5:P0071","text":"Whatever admits of both is called both whole and all. Such are those whose nature remains the same upon transposition, but whose form does not, for example wax and a garment; for both “whole” and “all” are said of them, since they possess both. Water, and all liquids, and number are called “all,” but “whole number” and “whole water” are not said, except metaphorically. But where “all” is said as of one thing, in these cases “all things” is said as of things divided: this whole number, these all units.","kind":"passage","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_DAZY_BOO5:P0071"]},{"id":"RAW_DAZY_BOO5:P0072","text":"Mutilated is said not of any quantity whatever, but it must both be divisible and be a whole. For two is not mutilated if one of the two is removed (for the mutilation and what remains are never equal), nor is any number whatever mutilated; for the substance too must remain: if a cup is mutilated, it is still a cup, but the number is no longer the same. Further, even if things have unlike parts, not all such things are mutilated (for number too has, as it were, unlike parts, for example a two and a three); rather, in general, nothing is mutilated among things in which position makes no difference, such as water or fire, but they must be such things as have position in accordance with their substance. Again, they must be continuous; for harmony consists of unlike things and has position, yet does not become mutilated. Further, not even all wholes are mutilated by the privation of any part whatever.","kind":"passage","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_DAZY_BOO5:P0072"]},{"id":"RAW_DAZY_BOO5:P0073","text":"For the missing parts must be neither the principal parts of the substance nor parts of any sort whatsoever. For example, if a cup is pierced, it is not mutilated; but it is if its handle or some projection is removed. And a man is not mutilated if he loses flesh or the spleen, but if he loses some extremity; and not every extremity, but one which, when removed entire, does not grow again. Therefore bald men are not mutilated.","kind":"passage","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_DAZY_BOO5:P0073"]},{"id":"RAW_DAZY_BOO5:P0074","text":"Genus is said, in one way, when the generation of things having the same species is continuous—for example, it is said that there is a genus of men so long as their generation is continuous. In another way, it is that from which they come as from the first mover into being; for thus the Greeks and the Ionians are called a genus, because the former are from Hellen and the latter from Ion, as the first begetter; and they are so called more from the begetter than from the matter (for they are also called a genus from the female, for example those from Pyrrha).\n\nAgain, plane is the genus of plane figures and solid of solid figures; for each figure is either such-and-such a plane figure or such-and-such a solid figure; and this is the substrate of the differences. Again, as that which is first present in the accounts, which is stated in the what-it-is, is genus, whose differences are said to be the qualities.","kind":"passage","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_DAZY_BOO5:P0074"]},{"id":"RAW_DAZY_BOO5:P0075","text":"Thus genus is said in as many ways: according to the continuous generation of the same species, according to the first mover of like kind, and as matter; for that of which the difference and quality are, this is the substrate, which we call matter. Things are said to differ in genus whose first substrates are different and neither is resolved into the other nor both into the same thing; for example, form and matter differ in genus, as do all things spoken of according to a different figure of the categories of being (for some beings signify what a thing is, others a certain quality, and others according to the divisions previously made); for neither are these resolved either into one another or into some one thing.","kind":"passage","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_DAZY_BOO5:P0075"]},{"id":"RAW_DAZY_BOO5:P0076","text":"The false is said in one way as a false thing; and of this, one kind is false because it is not compounded or is impossible to compound (as is said that the diagonal is commensurable, or that you are sitting; for of these, one is false always and the other sometimes, since in this way these are not beings); another kind consists in things which are, but are naturally apt to appear either not as they are or as things which are not—for example, a painting in shadow-colours and dreams; for these are something, but not the things of which they produce an appearance. Things, then, are called false in these ways: either because they do not exist, or because the appearance from them is of what does not exist. A false account is an account of things that are not, insofar as it is false; hence every false account is false of something other than that of which it is true—for example, the account of a circle is false of a triangle.","kind":"passage","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_DAZY_BOO5:P0076"]},{"id":"RAW_DAZY_BOO5:P0077","text":"The account of each thing is in one way one, namely the account of its what-it-was-to-be, but in another way many, since in a way the thing itself and the thing itself as affected are the same—for example, Socrates and musical Socrates (but a false account is, simply, the account of nothing). Hence Antisthenes thought, naively, that nothing should be predicated except by its proper account, one account of one thing; from which it followed that there is no contradiction, and almost that it is impossible to speak falsely. But it is possible to state each thing not only by its own account but also by that of another, falsely indeed and altogether, but in a way also truly, as eight is double by the account of two.\n\nThus, then, false things are so called; but a false man is one readily and deliberately disposed to such accounts, not because of anything else but for their own sake, and one who produces such accounts in others, just as we call false those things which produce a false appearance. Hence the argument in the Hippias is deceptive in taking the same man to be false and true.","kind":"passage","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_DAZY_BOO5:P0077"]},{"id":"RAW_DAZY_BOO5:P0078","text":"For it takes as false the one capable of speaking falsely (and this is the one who knows and is prudent); again, it takes the voluntarily bad man to be better. But this is false by induction—for the man who limps voluntarily is superior to one who limps involuntarily—understanding limping to mean imitation; since, if he were voluntarily lame, he would perhaps be worse, as in the case of character, and so would this man.","kind":"passage","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_DAZY_BOO5:P0078"]},{"id":"RAW_DAZY_BOO5:P0079","text":"An accident is said to be what belongs to something and may truly be asserted, yet neither of necessity nor for the most part; for example, if someone digging a hole for a plant finds a treasure. Thus, finding a treasure is an accident to the one digging the hole; for this does not follow from that, or after that, by necessity, nor does one for the most part find a treasure when planting. And a musician may be white; but since this occurs neither necessarily nor for the most part, we call it an accident. Consequently, since there is something that belongs, and belongs to something, and some of these belong somewhere and at some time, whatever belongs but not because this was this thing, or now, or here, will be an accident. There is no determinate cause of an accident, but only the chance one; and this is indeterminate. It happened to someone to come to Aegina if he did not arrive for the purpose of getting there, but was driven out by a storm or captured by pirates.","kind":"passage","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_DAZY_BOO5:P0079"]},{"id":"RAW_DAZY_BOO5:P0080","text":"An accident has come to be or is, then, but not qua itself, rather qua something else; for the storm is the cause of his arriving not where he was sailing, and that was Aegina. Accident is also said in another way, namely, of whatever belongs to each thing in itself without being in its substance—for example, having angles equal to two right angles belongs to a triangle. And these may be eternal, whereas none of the former may be. The account of this is elsewhere.","kind":"passage","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_DAZY_BOO5:P0080"]}],"source_digest":"d5fd30d54252c723c6eea3c40feb5e100328fcbfc048eae72ee8777b1557731d","source_language_code":"grc","original_title":"Book 5"}],"passage_basis":"frozen-grounding","original_title":"Book 5","title_language_code":"en"},{"id":"RAW_DAZY_BOO6","title":"Book 6","order":6,"chapter_id":"CH_DAZY_BOO6","text":"αἱ ἀρχαὶ καὶ τὰ αἴτια ζητεῖται τῶν ὄντων, δῆλον δὲ ὅτι ᾗ ὄντα. ἔστι γάρ τι αἴτιον ὑγιείας καὶ εὐεξίας, καὶ τῶν μαθηματικῶν εἰσὶν ἀρχαὶ καὶ στοιχεῖα καὶ αἴτια, καὶ ὅλως δὲ πᾶσα ἐπιστήμη διανοητικὴ ἢ μετέχουσά τι διανοίας περὶ αἰτίας καὶ ἀρχάς ἐστιν ἢ ἀκριβεστέρας ἢ ἁπλουστέρας. ἀλλὰ πᾶσαι αὗται περὶ ὄν τι καὶ γένος τι περιγραψάμεναι περὶ τούτου πραγματεύονται, ἀλλʼ οὐχὶ περὶ ὄντος ἁπλῶς οὐδὲ ᾗ ὄν, οὐδὲ τοῦ τί ἐστιν οὐθένα λόγον ποιοῦνται, ἀλλʼ ἐκ τούτου, αἱ μὲν αἰσθήσει ποιήσασαι αὐτὸ δῆλον αἱ δʼ ὑπόθεσιν λαβοῦσαι τὸ τί ἐστιν, οὕτω τὰ καθʼ αὑτὰ ὑπάρχοντα τῷ γένει περὶ ὅ εἰσιν ἀποδεικνύουσιν ἢ ἀναγκαιότερον ἢ μαλακώτερον· διόπερ φανερὸν ὅτι οὐκ ἔστιν ἀπόδειξις οὐσίας οὐδὲ τοῦ τί ἐστιν ἐκ τῆς τοιαύτης ἐπαγωγῆς, ἀλλά τις ἄλλος τρόπος τῆς δηλώσεως. ὁμοίως δὲ οὐδʼ εἰ ἔστιν ἢ μὴ ἔστι τὸ γένος περὶ ὃ πραγματεύονται οὐδὲν λέγουσι, διὰ τὸ τῆς αὐτῆς εἶναι διανοίας τό τε τί ἐστι δῆλον ποιεῖν καὶ εἰ ἔστιν.—ἐπεὶ δὲ καὶ ἡ φυσικὴ ἐπιστήμη τυγχάνει οὖσα περὶ γένος τι τοῦ ὄντος (περὶ γὰρ τὴν τοιαύτην ἐστὶν οὐσίαν ἐν ᾗ ἡ ἀρχὴ τῆς κινήσεως καὶ στάσεως ἐν αὐτᾖ, δῆλον ὅτι οὔτε πρακτική ἐστιν οὔτε ποιητική (τῶν μὲν γὰρ ποιητῶν ἐν τῷ ποιοῦντι ἡ ἀρχή, ἢ νοῦς ἢ τέχνη ἢ δύναμίς τις, τῶν δὲ πρακτῶν ἐν τῷ πράττοντι, ἡ προαίρεσις· τὸ αὐτὸ γὰρ τὸ πρακτὸν καὶ προαιρετόν), ὥστε εἰ πᾶσα διάνοια ἢ πρακτικὴ ἢ ποιητικὴ ἢ θεωρητική, ἡ φυσικὴ θεωρητική τις ἂν εἴη, ἀλλὰ θεωρητικὴ περὶ τοιοῦτον ὂν ὅ ἐστι δυνατὸν κινεῖσθαι, καὶ περὶ οὐσίαν τὴν κατὰ τὸν λόγον ὡς ἐπὶ τὸ πολὺ ὡς οὐ χωριστὴν μόνον. δεῖ δὲ τὸ τί ἦν εἶναι καὶ τὸν λόγον πῶς ἐστὶ μὴ λανθάνειν, ὡς ἄνευ γε τούτου τὸ ζητεῖν μηδέν ἐστι ποιεῖν. ἔστι δὲ τῶν ὁριζομένων καὶ τῶν τί ἐστι τὰ μὲν ὡς τὸ σιμὸν τὰ δʼ ὡς τὸ κοῖλον. διαφέρει δὲ ταῦτα ὅτι τὸ μὲν σιμὸν συνειλημμένον ἐστὶ μετὰ τῆς ὕλης (ἔστι γὰρ τὸ σιμὸν κοίλη ῥίς), ἡ δὲ κοιλότης ἄνευ ὕλης αἰσθητῆς.  εἰ δὴ πάντα τὰ φυσικὰ ὁμοίως τῷ σιμῷ λέγονται, οἷον ῥὶς ὀφθαλμὸς πρόσωπον σὰρξ ὀστοῦν, ὅλως ζῷον, φύλλον ῥίζα φλοιός, ὅλως φυτόν (οὐθενὸς γὰρ ἄνευ κινήσεως ὁ λόγος αὐτῶν, ἀλλʼ ἀεὶ ἔχει ὕλην), δῆλον πῶς δεῖ ἐν τοῖς φυσικοῖς τὸ τί ἐστι ζητεῖν καὶ ὁρίζεσθαι, καὶ διότι καὶ περὶ ψυχῆς ἐνίας θεωρῆσαι τοῦ φυσικοῦ, ὅση μὴ ἄνευ τῆς ὕλης ἐστίν. ὅτι μὲν οὖν ἡ φυσικὴ θεωρητική ἐστι, φανερὸν ἐκ τούτων· ἀλλʼ ἔστι καὶ ἡ μαθηματικὴ θεωρητική· ἀλλʼ εἰ ἀκινήτων καὶ χωριστῶν ἐστί, νῦν ἄδηλον, ὅτι μέντοι ἔνια μαθήματα ᾗ ἀκίνητα καὶ ᾗ χωριστὰ θεωρεῖ, δῆλον. εἰ δέ τί ἐστιν ἀΐδιον καὶ ἀκίνητον καὶ χωριστόν, φανερὸν ὅτι θεωρητικῆς τὸ γνῶναι, οὐ μέντοι φυσικῆς γε (περὶ κινητῶν γάρ τινων ἡ φυσικἤ οὐδὲ μαθηματικῆς, ἀλλὰ προτέρας ἀμφοῖν. ἡ μὲν γὰρ φυσικὴ περὶ χωριστὰ μὲν ἀλλʼ οὐκ ἀκίνητα, τῆς δὲ μαθηματικῆς ἔνια περὶ ἀκίνητα μὲν οὐ χωριστὰ δὲ ἴσως ἀλλʼ ὡς ἐν ὕλῃ· ἡ δὲ πρώτη καὶ περὶ χωριστὰ καὶ ἀκίνητα. ἀνάγκη δὲ πάντα μὲν τὰ αἴτια ἀΐδια εἶναι, μάλιστα δὲ ταῦτα· ταῦτα γὰρ αἴτια τοῖς φανεροῖς τῶν θείων. ὥστε τρεῖς ἂν εἶεν φιλοσοφίαι θεωρητικαί, μαθηματική, φυσική, θεολογική (οὐ γὰρ ἄδηλον ὅτι εἴ που τὸ θεῖον ὑπάρχει, ἐν τῇ τοιαύτῃ φύσει ὑπάρχεἰ, καὶ τὴν τιμιωτάτην δεῖ περὶ τὸ τιμιώτατον γένος εἶναι. αἱ μὲν οὖν θεωρητικαὶ τῶν ἄλλων ἐπιστημῶν αἱρετώταται, αὕτη δὲ τῶν θεωρητικῶν. ἀπορήσειε γὰρ ἄν τις πότερόν ποθʼ ἡ πρώτη φιλοσοφία καθόλου ἐστὶν ἢ περί τι γένος  καὶ φύσιν τινὰ μίαν (οὐ γὰρ ὁ αὐτὸς τρόπος οὐδʼ ἐν ταῖς μαθηματικαῖς, ἀλλʼ ἡ μὲν γεωμετρία καὶ ἀστρολογία περί τινα φύσιν εἰσίν, ἡ δὲ καθόλου πασῶν κοινἤ· εἰ μὲν οὖν μὴ ἔστι τις ἑτέρα οὐσία παρὰ τὰς φύσει συνεστηκυίας, ἡ φυσικὴ ἂν εἴη πρώτη ἐπιστήμη· εἰ δʼ ἔστι τις οὐσία ἀκίνητος, αὕτη προτέρα καὶ φιλοσοφία πρώτη, καὶ καθόλου οὕτως ὅτι πρώτη· καὶ περὶ τοῦ ὄντος ᾗ ὂν ταύτης ἂν εἴη θεωρῆσαι, καὶ τί ἐστι καὶ τὰ ὑπάρχοντα ᾗ ὄν. \n\n\n\n \n\n \n\nἀλλʼ ἐπεὶ τὸ ὂν τὸ ἁπλῶς λεγόμενον λέγεται πολλαχῶς, ὧν ἓν μὲν ἦν τὸ κατὰ συμβεβηκός, ἕτερον δὲ τὸ ὡς ἀληθές, καὶ τὸ μὴ ὂν ὡς τὸ ψεῦδος, παρὰ ταῦτα δʼ ἐστὶ τὰ σχήματα τῆς κατηγορίας (οἷον τὸ μὲν τί, τὸ δὲ ποιόν, τὸ δὲ ποσόν, τὸ δὲ πού, τὸ δὲ ποτέ, καὶ εἴ τι ἄλλο σημαίνει τὸν τρόπον τοῦτον),  ἔτι παρὰ ταῦτα πάντα τὸ δυνάμει καὶ ἐνεργείᾳ·—ἐπεὶ δὴ πολλαχῶς λέγεται τὸ ὄν, πρῶτον περὶ τοῦ κατὰ συμβεβηκὸς λεκτέον, ὅτι οὐδεμία ἐστὶ περὶ αὐτὸ θεωρία. σημεῖον δέ· οὐδεμιᾷ γὰρ ἐπιστήμῃ ἐπιμελὲς περὶ αὐτοῦ οὔτε πρακτικῇ οὔτε ποιητικῇ οὔτε θεωρητικῇ. οὔτε γὰρ ὁ ποιῶν οἰκίαν ποιεῖ ὅσα συμβαίνει ἅμα τῇ οἰκίᾳ γιγνομένῃ (ἄπειρα γάρ ἐστιν· τοῖς μὲν γὰρ ἡδεῖαν τοῖς δὲ βλαβερὰν τοῖς δʼ ὠφέλιμον οὐθὲν εἶναι κωλύει τὴν ποιηθεῖσαν, καὶ ἑτέραν ὡς εἰπεῖν πάντων τῶν ὄντων· ὧν οὐθενός ἐστιν ἡ οἰκοδομικὴ ποιητικἤ, τὸν αὐτὸν δὲ τρόπον οὐδʼ ὁ γεωμέτρης θεωρεῖ τὰ οὕτω συμβεβηκότα τοῖς σχήμασιν, οὐδʼ εἰ ἕτερόν ἐστι τρίγωνον καὶ τρίγωνον δύο ὀρθὰς ἔχον. καὶ τοῦτʼ εὐλόγως συμπίπτει· ὥσπερ γὰρ ὄνομά τι μόνον τὸ συμβεβηκός ἐστιν. διὸ Πλάτων τρόπον τινὰ οὐ κακῶς τὴν σοφιστικὴν περὶ τὸ μὴ ὂν ἔταξεν. εἰσὶ γὰρ οἱ τῶν σοφιστῶν λόγοι περὶ τὸ συμβεβηκὸς ὡς εἰπεῖν μάλιστα πάντων, πότερον ἕτερον ἢ ταὐτὸν μουσικὸν καὶ γραμματικόν, καὶ μουσικὸς Κορίσκος καὶ Κορίσκος, καὶ εἰ πᾶν ὃ ἂν ᾖ, μὴ ἀεὶ δέ, γέγονεν, ὥστʼ εἰ μουσικὸς ὢν γραμματικὸς γέγονε, καὶ γραμματικὸς ὢν μουσικός, καὶ ὅσοι δὴ ἄλλοι τοιοῦτοι τῶν λόγων εἰσίν· φαίνεται γὰρ τὸ συμβεβηκὸς ἐγγύς τι τοῦ μὴ ὄντος. δῆλον δὲ καὶ ἐκ τῶν τοιούτων λόγων· τῶν μὲν γὰρ ἄλλον τρόπον ὄντων ἔστι γένεσις καὶ φθορά, τῶν δὲ κατὰ συμβεβηκὸς οὐκ ἔστιν. ἀλλʼ ὅμως λεκτέον ἔτι περὶ τοῦ συμβεβηκότος ἐφʼ ὅσον ἐνδέχεται, τίς ἡ φύσις αὐτοῦ καὶ διὰ τίνʼ αἰτίαν ἔστιν· ἅμα γὰρ δῆλον ἴσως ἔσται καὶ διὰ τί ἐπιστήμη οὐκ ἔστιν αὐτοῦ.—ἐπεὶ οὖν ἐστὶν ἐν τοῖς οὖσι τὰ μὲν ἀεὶ ὡσαύτως ἔχοντα καὶ ἐξ ἀνάγκης, οὐ τῆς κατὰ τὸ βίαιον λεγομένης ἀλλʼ ἣν λέγομεν τῷ μὴ ἐνδέχεσθαι ἄλλως, τὰ δʼ ἐξ ἀνάγκης μὲν οὐκ ἔστιν οὐδʼ ἀεί, ὡς δʼ ἐπὶ τὸ πολύ, αὕτη ἀρχὴ καὶ αὕτη αἰτία ἐστὶ τοῦ εἶναι τὸ συμβεβηκός· ὃ γὰρ ἂν ᾖ μήτʼ ἀεὶ μήθʼ ὡς ἐπὶ τὸ πολύ, τοῦτό φαμεν συμβεβηκὸς εἶναι. οἷον ἐπὶ κυνὶ ἂν χειμὼν γένηται καὶ ψῦχος, τοῦτο συμβῆναί φαμεν, ἀλλʼ οὐκ ἂν πνῖγος καὶ ἀλέα, ὅτι τὸ μὲν ἀεὶ ἢ ὡς ἐπὶ τὸ πολὺ τὸ δʼ οὔ. καὶ τὸν ἄνθρωπον λευκὸν εἶναι συμβέβηκεν (οὔτε γὰρ ἀεὶ οὔθʼ ὡς ἐπὶ τὸ πολὔ, ζῷον δʼ οὐ κατὰ συμβεβηκός. καὶ τὸ ὑγιάζειν δὲ τὸν οἰκοδόμον συμβεβηκός,  ὅτι οὐ πέφυκε τοῦτο ποιεῖν οἰκοδόμος ἀλλὰ ἰατρός, ἀλλὰ συνέβη ἰατρὸν εἶναι τὸν οἰκοδόμον. καὶ ὀψοποιὸς ἡδονῆς στοχαζόμενος ποιήσειεν ἄν τι ὑγιεινόν, ἀλλʼ οὐ κατὰ τὴν ὀψοποιητικήν· διὸ συνέβη, φαμέν, καὶ ἔστιν ὡς ποιεῖ, ἁπλῶς δʼ οὔ. τῶν μὲν γὰρ ἄλλων  δυνάμεις εἰσὶν αἱ ποιητικαί, τῶν δʼ οὐδεμία τέχνη οὐδὲ δύναμις ὡρισμένη· τῶν γὰρ κατὰ συμβεβηκὸς ὄντων ἢ γιγνομένων καὶ τὸ αἴτιόν ἐστι κατὰ συμβεβηκός. ὥστʼ ἐπεὶ οὐ πάντα ἐστὶν ἐξ ἀνάγκης καὶ ἀεὶ ἢ ὄντα ἢ γιγνόμενα, ἀλλὰ τὰ πλεῖστα ὡς ἐπὶ τὸ πολύ, ἀνάγκη εἶναι τὸ κατὰ συμβεβηκὸς ὄν· οἷον οὔτʼ ἀεὶ οὔθʼ ὡς ἐπὶ τὸ πολὺ ὁ λευκὸς μουσικός ἐστιν, ἐπεὶ δὲ γίγνεταί ποτε, κατὰ συμβεβηκὸς ἔσται (εἰ δὲ μή, πάντʼ ἔσται ἐξ ἀνάγκης)· ὥστε ἡ ὕλη ἔσται αἰτία ἡ ἐνδεχομένη παρὰ τὸ ὡς ἐπὶ τὸ πολὺ ἄλλως τοῦ συμβεβηκότος.  ἀρχὴν δὲ τηνδὶ ληπτέον, πότερον οὐδέν ἐστιν οὔτʼ αἰεὶ οὔθʼ ὡς ἐπὶ τὸ πολύ. ἢ τοῦτο ἀδύνατον; ἔστιν ἄρα τι παρὰ ταῦτα τὸ ὁπότερʼ ἔτυχε καὶ κατὰ συμβεβηκός. ἀλλὰ πότερον τὸ ὡς ἐπὶ τὸ πολύ, τὸ δʼ ἀεὶ οὐθενὶ ὑπάρχει, ἢ ἔστιν ἄττα ἀΐδια; περὶ μὲν οὖν τούτων ὕστερον σκεπτέον, ὅτι δʼ ἐπιστήμη οὐκ ἔστι τοῦ συμβεβηκότος φανερόν· ἐπιστήμη μὲν γὰρ πᾶσα ἢ τοῦ ἀεὶ ἢ τοῦ ὡς ἐπὶ τὸ πολύ—πῶς γὰρ ἢ μαθήσεται ἢ διδάξει ἄλλον; δεῖ γὰρ ὡρίσθαι ἢ τῷ ἀεὶ ἢ τῷ ὡς ἐπὶ τὸ πολύ, οἷον ὅτι ὠφέλιμον τὸ μελίκρατον τῷ πυρέττοντι ὡς ἐπὶ τὸ πολύ—τὸ δὲ παρὰ τοῦτο οὐχ ἕξει λέγειν, πότε οὔ, οἷον νουμηνίᾳ· ἢ γὰρ ἀεὶ ἢ ὡς ἐπὶ τὸ πολὺ καὶ τὸ τῇ νουμηνίᾳ· τὸ δὲ συμβεβηκός ἐστι παρὰ ταῦτα. τί μὲν οὖν ἐστὶ τὸ συμβεβηκὸς καὶ διὰ τίνʼ αἰτίαν καὶ ὅτι ἐπιστήμη οὐκ ἔστιν αὐτοῦ, εἴρηται. \n\n\n\n \n\n \n\nὅτι δʼ εἰσὶν ἀρχαὶ καὶ αἴτια γενητὰ καὶ φθαρτὰ ἄνευ τοῦ γίγνεσθαι καὶ φθείρεσθαι, φανερόν. εἰ γὰρ μὴ τοῦτʼ, ἐξ ἀνάγκης πάντʼ ἔσται, εἰ τοῦ γιγνομένου καὶ φθειρομένου μὴ κατὰ συμβεβηκὸς αἴτιόν τι ἀνάγκη εἶναι. πότερον γὰρ ἔσται τοδὶ ἢ οὔ; ἐάν γε τοδὶ γένηται· εἰ δὲ μή, οὔ. τοῦτο δὲ ἐὰν ἄλλο. καὶ οὕτω δῆλον ὅτι ἀεὶ χρόνου ἀφαιρουμένου ἀπὸ πεπερασμένου χρόνου ἥξει ἐπὶ τὸ νῦν,  ὥστε ὁδὶ ἀποθανεῖται  βίᾳ, ἐάν γε ἐξέλθῃ· τοῦτο δὲ ἐὰν διψήσῃ· τοῦτο δὲ ἐὰν ἄλλο· καὶ οὕτως ἥξει εἰς ὃ νῦν ὑπάρχει, ἢ εἰς τῶν γεγονότων τι. οἷον ἐὰν διψήσῃ· τοῦτο δὲ εἰ ἐσθίει δριμέα· τοῦτο δʼ ἤτοι ὑπάρχει ἢ οὔ· ὥστʼ ἐξ ἀνάγκης ἀποθανεῖται ἢ οὐκ ἀποθανεῖται. ὁμοίως δὲ κἂν ὑπερπηδήσῃ τις εἰς τὰ γενόμενα, ὁ αὐτὸς λόγος· ἤδη γὰρ ὑπάρχει τοῦτο ἔν τινι, λέγω δὲ τὸ γεγονός· ἐξ ἀνάγκης ἄρα πάντα ἔσται τὰ ἐσόμενα, οἷον τὸ ἀποθανεῖν τὸν ζῶντα· ἤδη γάρ τι γέγονεν, οἷον τὰ ἐναντία ἐν τῷ αὐτῷ. ἀλλʼ εἰ νόσῳ ἢ βίᾳ, οὔπω, ἀλλʼ ἐὰν τοδὶ γένηται. δῆλον ἄρα ὅτι μέχρι τινὸς βαδίζει ἀρχῆς, αὕτη δʼ οὐκέτι εἰς ἄλλο. ἔσται οὖν ἡ τοῦ ὁπότερʼ ἔτυχεν αὕτη, καὶ αἴτιον τῆς γενέσεως αὐτῆς ἄλλο οὐθέν. ἀλλʼ εἰς ἀρχὴν ποίαν καὶ αἴτιον ποῖον ἡ ἀναγωγὴ ἡ τοιαύτη, πότερον ὡς εἰς ὕλην ἢ ὡς εἰς τὸ οὗ ἕνεκα ἢ ὡς εἰς τὸ κινῆσαν, μάλιστα σκεπτέον. \n\n\n\n \n\n \n\nπερὶ μὲν οὖν τοῦ κατὰ συμβεβηκὸς ὄντος ἀφείσθω (διώρισται γὰρ ἱκανῶς)· τὸ δὲ ὡς ἀληθὲς ὄν, καὶ μὴ ὂν ὡς ψεῦδος, ἐπειδὴ παρὰ σύνθεσίν ἐστι καὶ διαίρεσιν, τὸ δὲ σύνολον περὶ μερισμὸν ἀντιφάσεως (τὸ μὲν γὰρ ἀληθὲς τὴν κατάφασιν ἐπὶ τῷ συγκειμένῳ ἔχει τὴν δʼ ἀπόφασιν ἐπὶ τῷ διῃρημένῳ, τὸ δὲ ψεῦδος τούτου τοῦ μερισμοῦ τὴν ἀντίφασιν· πῶς δὲ τὸ ἅμα ἢ τὸ χωρὶς νοεῖν συμβαίνει, ἄλλος λόγος, λέγω δὲ τὸ ἅμα καὶ τὸ χωρὶς ὥστε μὴ τὸ ἐφεξῆς ἀλλʼ ἕν τι γίγνεσθαἰ· οὐ γάρ ἐστι τὸ ψεῦδος καὶ τὸ ἀληθὲς ἐν τοῖς πράγμασιν, οἷον τὸ μὲν ἀγαθὸν ἀληθὲς τὸ δὲ κακὸν εὐθὺς ψεῦδος, ἀλλʼ ἐν διανοίᾳ, περὶ δὲ τὰ ἁπλᾶ καὶ τὰ τί ἐστιν οὐδʼ ἐν διανοίᾳ·—ὅσα μὲν οὖν δεῖ θεωρῆσαι περὶ τὸ οὕτως ὂν καὶ μὴ ὄν, ὕστερον ἐπισκεπτέον· ἐπεὶ δὲ ἡ συμπλοκή ἐστιν καὶ ἡ διαίρεσις ἐν διανοίᾳ ἀλλʼ οὐκ ἐν τοῖς πράγμασι, τὸ δʼ οὕτως ὂν ἕτερον ὂν τῶν κυρίως (ἢ γὰρ τὸ τί ἐστιν ἢ ὅτι ποιὸν ἢ ὅτι ποσὸν ἤ τι ἄλλο συνάπτει ἢ ἀφαιρεῖ ἡ διάνοιἀ, τὸ μὲν ὡς συμβεβηκὸς καὶ τὸ ὡς ἀληθὲς ὂν ἀφετέον—τὸ γὰρ αἴτιον τοῦ μὲν ἀόριστον τοῦ δὲ τῆς διανοίας τι πάθος,  καὶ ἀμφότερα περὶ τὸ λοιπὸν γένος τοῦ ὄντος, καὶ οὐκ ἔξω δηλοῦσιν οὖσάν τινα φύσιν τοῦ ὄντος—διὸ ταῦτα μὲν ἀφείσθω, σκεπτέον δὲ τοῦ ὄντος αὐτοῦ τὰ αἴτια καὶ τὰς ἀρχὰς ᾗ ὄν.","paragraphs":[{"id":"RAW_DAZY_BOO6:P0001","text":"αἱ ἀρχαὶ καὶ τὰ αἴτια ζητεῖται τῶν ὄντων, δῆλον δὲ ὅτι ᾗ ὄντα. ἔστι γάρ τι αἴτιον ὑγιείας καὶ εὐεξίας, καὶ τῶν μαθηματικῶν εἰσὶν ἀρχαὶ καὶ στοιχεῖα καὶ αἴτια, καὶ ὅλως δὲ πᾶσα ἐπιστήμη διανοητικὴ ἢ μετέχουσά τι διανοίας περὶ αἰτίας καὶ ἀρχάς ἐστιν ἢ ἀκριβεστέρας ἢ ἁπλουστέρας. ἀλλὰ πᾶσαι αὗται περὶ ὄν τι καὶ γένος τι περιγραψάμεναι περὶ τούτου πραγματεύονται, ἀλλʼ οὐχὶ περὶ ὄντος ἁπλῶς οὐδὲ ᾗ ὄν, οὐδὲ τοῦ τί ἐστιν οὐθένα λόγον ποιοῦνται, ἀλλʼ ἐκ τούτου, αἱ μὲν αἰσθήσει ποιήσασαι αὐτὸ δῆλον αἱ δʼ ὑπόθεσιν λαβοῦσαι τὸ τί ἐστιν, οὕτω τὰ καθʼ αὑτὰ ὑπάρχοντα τῷ γένει περὶ ὅ εἰσιν ἀποδεικνύουσιν ἢ ἀναγκαιότερον ἢ μαλακώτερον· διόπερ φανερὸν ὅτι οὐκ ἔστιν ἀπόδειξις οὐσίας οὐδὲ τοῦ τί ἐστιν ἐκ τῆς τοιαύτης ἐπαγωγῆς, ἀλλά τις ἄλλος τρόπος τῆς δηλώσεως.","kind":"passage"},{"id":"RAW_DAZY_BOO6:P0002","text":"ὁμοίως δὲ οὐδʼ εἰ ἔστιν ἢ μὴ ἔστι τὸ γένος περὶ ὃ πραγματεύονται οὐδὲν λέγουσι, διὰ τὸ τῆς αὐτῆς εἶναι διανοίας τό τε τί ἐστι δῆλον ποιεῖν καὶ εἰ ἔστιν.—ἐπεὶ δὲ καὶ ἡ φυσικὴ ἐπιστήμη τυγχάνει οὖσα περὶ γένος τι τοῦ ὄντος (περὶ γὰρ τὴν τοιαύτην ἐστὶν οὐσίαν ἐν ᾗ ἡ ἀρχὴ τῆς κινήσεως καὶ στάσεως ἐν αὐτᾖ, δῆλον ὅτι οὔτε πρακτική ἐστιν οὔτε ποιητική (τῶν μὲν γὰρ ποιητῶν ἐν τῷ ποιοῦντι ἡ ἀρχή, ἢ νοῦς ἢ τέχνη ἢ δύναμίς τις, τῶν δὲ πρακτῶν ἐν τῷ πράττοντι, ἡ προαίρεσις· τὸ αὐτὸ γὰρ τὸ πρακτὸν καὶ προαιρετόν), ὥστε εἰ πᾶσα διάνοια ἢ πρακτικὴ ἢ ποιητικὴ ἢ θεωρητική, ἡ φυσικὴ θεωρητική τις ἂν εἴη, ἀλλὰ θεωρητικὴ περὶ τοιοῦτον ὂν ὅ ἐστι δυνατὸν κινεῖσθαι, καὶ περὶ οὐσίαν τὴν κατὰ τὸν λόγον ὡς ἐπὶ τὸ πολὺ ὡς οὐ χωριστὴν μόνον. δεῖ δὲ τὸ τί ἦν εἶναι καὶ τὸν λόγον πῶς ἐστὶ μὴ λανθάνειν, ὡς ἄνευ γε τούτου τὸ ζητεῖν μηδέν ἐστι ποιεῖν.","kind":"passage"},{"id":"RAW_DAZY_BOO6:P0003","text":"ἔστι δὲ τῶν ὁριζομένων καὶ τῶν τί ἐστι τὰ μὲν ὡς τὸ σιμὸν τὰ δʼ ὡς τὸ κοῖλον. διαφέρει δὲ ταῦτα ὅτι τὸ μὲν σιμὸν συνειλημμένον ἐστὶ μετὰ τῆς ὕλης (ἔστι γὰρ τὸ σιμὸν κοίλη ῥίς), ἡ δὲ κοιλότης ἄνευ ὕλης αἰσθητῆς.  εἰ δὴ πάντα τὰ φυσικὰ ὁμοίως τῷ σιμῷ λέγονται, οἷον ῥὶς ὀφθαλμὸς πρόσωπον σὰρξ ὀστοῦν, ὅλως ζῷον, φύλλον ῥίζα φλοιός, ὅλως φυτόν (οὐθενὸς γὰρ ἄνευ κινήσεως ὁ λόγος αὐτῶν, ἀλλʼ ἀεὶ ἔχει ὕλην), δῆλον πῶς δεῖ ἐν τοῖς φυσικοῖς τὸ τί ἐστι ζητεῖν καὶ ὁρίζεσθαι, καὶ διότι καὶ περὶ ψυχῆς ἐνίας θεωρῆσαι τοῦ φυσικοῦ, ὅση μὴ ἄνευ τῆς ὕλης ἐστίν. ὅτι μὲν οὖν ἡ φυσικὴ θεωρητική ἐστι, φανερὸν ἐκ τούτων· ἀλλʼ ἔστι καὶ ἡ μαθηματικὴ θεωρητική· ἀλλʼ εἰ ἀκινήτων καὶ χωριστῶν ἐστί, νῦν ἄδηλον, ὅτι μέντοι ἔνια μαθήματα ᾗ ἀκίνητα καὶ ᾗ χωριστὰ θεωρεῖ, δῆλον.","kind":"passage"},{"id":"RAW_DAZY_BOO6:P0004","text":"εἰ δέ τί ἐστιν ἀΐδιον καὶ ἀκίνητον καὶ χωριστόν, φανερὸν ὅτι θεωρητικῆς τὸ γνῶναι, οὐ μέντοι φυσικῆς γε (περὶ κινητῶν γάρ τινων ἡ φυσικἤ οὐδὲ μαθηματικῆς, ἀλλὰ προτέρας ἀμφοῖν. ἡ μὲν γὰρ φυσικὴ περὶ χωριστὰ μὲν ἀλλʼ οὐκ ἀκίνητα, τῆς δὲ μαθηματικῆς ἔνια περὶ ἀκίνητα μὲν οὐ χωριστὰ δὲ ἴσως ἀλλʼ ὡς ἐν ὕλῃ· ἡ δὲ πρώτη καὶ περὶ χωριστὰ καὶ ἀκίνητα. ἀνάγκη δὲ πάντα μὲν τὰ αἴτια ἀΐδια εἶναι, μάλιστα δὲ ταῦτα· ταῦτα γὰρ αἴτια τοῖς φανεροῖς τῶν θείων. ὥστε τρεῖς ἂν εἶεν φιλοσοφίαι θεωρητικαί, μαθηματική, φυσική, θεολογική (οὐ γὰρ ἄδηλον ὅτι εἴ που τὸ θεῖον ὑπάρχει, ἐν τῇ τοιαύτῃ φύσει ὑπάρχεἰ, καὶ τὴν τιμιωτάτην δεῖ περὶ τὸ τιμιώτατον γένος εἶναι. αἱ μὲν οὖν θεωρητικαὶ τῶν ἄλλων ἐπιστημῶν αἱρετώταται, αὕτη δὲ τῶν θεωρητικῶν.","kind":"passage"},{"id":"RAW_DAZY_BOO6:P0005","text":"ἀπορήσειε γὰρ ἄν τις πότερόν ποθʼ ἡ πρώτη φιλοσοφία καθόλου ἐστὶν ἢ περί τι γένος  καὶ φύσιν τινὰ μίαν (οὐ γὰρ ὁ αὐτὸς τρόπος οὐδʼ ἐν ταῖς μαθηματικαῖς, ἀλλʼ ἡ μὲν γεωμετρία καὶ ἀστρολογία περί τινα φύσιν εἰσίν, ἡ δὲ καθόλου πασῶν κοινἤ· εἰ μὲν οὖν μὴ ἔστι τις ἑτέρα οὐσία παρὰ τὰς φύσει συνεστηκυίας, ἡ φυσικὴ ἂν εἴη πρώτη ἐπιστήμη· εἰ δʼ ἔστι τις οὐσία ἀκίνητος, αὕτη προτέρα καὶ φιλοσοφία πρώτη, καὶ καθόλου οὕτως ὅτι πρώτη· καὶ περὶ τοῦ ὄντος ᾗ ὂν ταύτης ἂν εἴη θεωρῆσαι, καὶ τί ἐστι καὶ τὰ ὑπάρχοντα ᾗ ὄν.","kind":"passage"},{"id":"RAW_DAZY_BOO6:P0006","text":"ἀλλʼ ἐπεὶ τὸ ὂν τὸ ἁπλῶς λεγόμενον λέγεται πολλαχῶς, ὧν ἓν μὲν ἦν τὸ κατὰ συμβεβηκός, ἕτερον δὲ τὸ ὡς ἀληθές, καὶ τὸ μὴ ὂν ὡς τὸ ψεῦδος, παρὰ ταῦτα δʼ ἐστὶ τὰ σχήματα τῆς κατηγορίας (οἷον τὸ μὲν τί, τὸ δὲ ποιόν, τὸ δὲ ποσόν, τὸ δὲ πού, τὸ δὲ ποτέ, καὶ εἴ τι ἄλλο σημαίνει τὸν τρόπον τοῦτον),  ἔτι παρὰ ταῦτα πάντα τὸ δυνάμει καὶ ἐνεργείᾳ·—ἐπεὶ δὴ πολλαχῶς λέγεται τὸ ὄν, πρῶτον περὶ τοῦ κατὰ συμβεβηκὸς λεκτέον, ὅτι οὐδεμία ἐστὶ περὶ αὐτὸ θεωρία. σημεῖον δέ· οὐδεμιᾷ γὰρ ἐπιστήμῃ ἐπιμελὲς περὶ αὐτοῦ οὔτε πρακτικῇ οὔτε ποιητικῇ οὔτε θεωρητικῇ.","kind":"passage"},{"id":"RAW_DAZY_BOO6:P0007","text":"οὔτε γὰρ ὁ ποιῶν οἰκίαν ποιεῖ ὅσα συμβαίνει ἅμα τῇ οἰκίᾳ γιγνομένῃ (ἄπειρα γάρ ἐστιν· τοῖς μὲν γὰρ ἡδεῖαν τοῖς δὲ βλαβερὰν τοῖς δʼ ὠφέλιμον οὐθὲν εἶναι κωλύει τὴν ποιηθεῖσαν, καὶ ἑτέραν ὡς εἰπεῖν πάντων τῶν ὄντων· ὧν οὐθενός ἐστιν ἡ οἰκοδομικὴ ποιητικἤ, τὸν αὐτὸν δὲ τρόπον οὐδʼ ὁ γεωμέτρης θεωρεῖ τὰ οὕτω συμβεβηκότα τοῖς σχήμασιν, οὐδʼ εἰ ἕτερόν ἐστι τρίγωνον καὶ τρίγωνον δύο ὀρθὰς ἔχον. καὶ τοῦτʼ εὐλόγως συμπίπτει· ὥσπερ γὰρ ὄνομά τι μόνον τὸ συμβεβηκός ἐστιν. διὸ Πλάτων τρόπον τινὰ οὐ κακῶς τὴν σοφιστικὴν περὶ τὸ μὴ ὂν ἔταξεν. εἰσὶ γὰρ οἱ τῶν σοφιστῶν λόγοι περὶ τὸ συμβεβηκὸς ὡς εἰπεῖν μάλιστα πάντων, πότερον ἕτερον ἢ ταὐτὸν μουσικὸν καὶ γραμματικόν, καὶ μουσικὸς Κορίσκος καὶ Κορίσκος, καὶ εἰ πᾶν ὃ ἂν ᾖ, μὴ ἀεὶ δέ, γέγονεν, ὥστʼ εἰ μουσικὸς ὢν γραμματικὸς γέγονε, καὶ γραμματικὸς ὢν μουσικός, καὶ ὅσοι δὴ ἄλλοι τοιοῦτοι τῶν λόγων εἰσίν· φαίνεται γὰρ τὸ συμβεβηκὸς ἐγγύς τι τοῦ μὴ ὄντος.","kind":"passage"},{"id":"RAW_DAZY_BOO6:P0008","text":"δῆλον δὲ καὶ ἐκ τῶν τοιούτων λόγων· τῶν μὲν γὰρ ἄλλον τρόπον ὄντων ἔστι γένεσις καὶ φθορά, τῶν δὲ κατὰ συμβεβηκὸς οὐκ ἔστιν. ἀλλʼ ὅμως λεκτέον ἔτι περὶ τοῦ συμβεβηκότος ἐφʼ ὅσον ἐνδέχεται, τίς ἡ φύσις αὐτοῦ καὶ διὰ τίνʼ αἰτίαν ἔστιν· ἅμα γὰρ δῆλον ἴσως ἔσται καὶ διὰ τί ἐπιστήμη οὐκ ἔστιν αὐτοῦ.—ἐπεὶ οὖν ἐστὶν ἐν τοῖς οὖσι τὰ μὲν ἀεὶ ὡσαύτως ἔχοντα καὶ ἐξ ἀνάγκης, οὐ τῆς κατὰ τὸ βίαιον λεγομένης ἀλλʼ ἣν λέγομεν τῷ μὴ ἐνδέχεσθαι ἄλλως, τὰ δʼ ἐξ ἀνάγκης μὲν οὐκ ἔστιν οὐδʼ ἀεί, ὡς δʼ ἐπὶ τὸ πολύ, αὕτη ἀρχὴ καὶ αὕτη αἰτία ἐστὶ τοῦ εἶναι τὸ συμβεβηκός· ὃ γὰρ ἂν ᾖ μήτʼ ἀεὶ μήθʼ ὡς ἐπὶ τὸ πολύ, τοῦτό φαμεν συμβεβηκὸς εἶναι. οἷον ἐπὶ κυνὶ ἂν χειμὼν γένηται καὶ ψῦχος, τοῦτο συμβῆναί φαμεν, ἀλλʼ οὐκ ἂν πνῖγος καὶ ἀλέα, ὅτι τὸ μὲν ἀεὶ ἢ ὡς ἐπὶ τὸ πολὺ τὸ δʼ οὔ. καὶ τὸν ἄνθρωπον λευκὸν εἶναι συμβέβηκεν (οὔτε γὰρ ἀεὶ οὔθʼ ὡς ἐπὶ τὸ πολὔ, ζῷον δʼ οὐ κατὰ συμβεβηκός.","kind":"passage"},{"id":"RAW_DAZY_BOO6:P0009","text":"καὶ τὸ ὑγιάζειν δὲ τὸν οἰκοδόμον συμβεβηκός,  ὅτι οὐ πέφυκε τοῦτο ποιεῖν οἰκοδόμος ἀλλὰ ἰατρός, ἀλλὰ συνέβη ἰατρὸν εἶναι τὸν οἰκοδόμον. καὶ ὀψοποιὸς ἡδονῆς στοχαζόμενος ποιήσειεν ἄν τι ὑγιεινόν, ἀλλʼ οὐ κατὰ τὴν ὀψοποιητικήν· διὸ συνέβη, φαμέν, καὶ ἔστιν ὡς ποιεῖ, ἁπλῶς δʼ οὔ. τῶν μὲν γὰρ ἄλλων  δυνάμεις εἰσὶν αἱ ποιητικαί, τῶν δʼ οὐδεμία τέχνη οὐδὲ δύναμις ὡρισμένη· τῶν γὰρ κατὰ συμβεβηκὸς ὄντων ἢ γιγνομένων καὶ τὸ αἴτιόν ἐστι κατὰ συμβεβηκός. ὥστʼ ἐπεὶ οὐ πάντα ἐστὶν ἐξ ἀνάγκης καὶ ἀεὶ ἢ ὄντα ἢ γιγνόμενα, ἀλλὰ τὰ πλεῖστα ὡς ἐπὶ τὸ πολύ, ἀνάγκη εἶναι τὸ κατὰ συμβεβηκὸς ὄν· οἷον οὔτʼ ἀεὶ οὔθʼ ὡς ἐπὶ τὸ πολὺ ὁ λευκὸς μουσικός ἐστιν, ἐπεὶ δὲ γίγνεταί ποτε, κατὰ συμβεβηκὸς ἔσται (εἰ δὲ μή, πάντʼ ἔσται ἐξ ἀνάγκης)· ὥστε ἡ ὕλη ἔσται αἰτία ἡ ἐνδεχομένη παρὰ τὸ ὡς ἐπὶ τὸ πολὺ ἄλλως τοῦ συμβεβηκότος.  ἀρχὴν δὲ τηνδὶ ληπτέον, πότερον οὐδέν ἐστιν οὔτʼ αἰεὶ οὔθʼ ὡς ἐπὶ τὸ πολύ.","kind":"passage"},{"id":"RAW_DAZY_BOO6:P0010","text":"ἢ τοῦτο ἀδύνατον; ἔστιν ἄρα τι παρὰ ταῦτα τὸ ὁπότερʼ ἔτυχε καὶ κατὰ συμβεβηκός. ἀλλὰ πότερον τὸ ὡς ἐπὶ τὸ πολύ, τὸ δʼ ἀεὶ οὐθενὶ ὑπάρχει, ἢ ἔστιν ἄττα ἀΐδια; περὶ μὲν οὖν τούτων ὕστερον σκεπτέον, ὅτι δʼ ἐπιστήμη οὐκ ἔστι τοῦ συμβεβηκότος φανερόν· ἐπιστήμη μὲν γὰρ πᾶσα ἢ τοῦ ἀεὶ ἢ τοῦ ὡς ἐπὶ τὸ πολύ—πῶς γὰρ ἢ μαθήσεται ἢ διδάξει ἄλλον; δεῖ γὰρ ὡρίσθαι ἢ τῷ ἀεὶ ἢ τῷ ὡς ἐπὶ τὸ πολύ, οἷον ὅτι ὠφέλιμον τὸ μελίκρατον τῷ πυρέττοντι ὡς ἐπὶ τὸ πολύ—τὸ δὲ παρὰ τοῦτο οὐχ ἕξει λέγειν, πότε οὔ, οἷον νουμηνίᾳ· ἢ γὰρ ἀεὶ ἢ ὡς ἐπὶ τὸ πολὺ καὶ τὸ τῇ νουμηνίᾳ· τὸ δὲ συμβεβηκός ἐστι παρὰ ταῦτα. τί μὲν οὖν ἐστὶ τὸ συμβεβηκὸς καὶ διὰ τίνʼ αἰτίαν καὶ ὅτι ἐπιστήμη οὐκ ἔστιν αὐτοῦ, εἴρηται.","kind":"passage"},{"id":"RAW_DAZY_BOO6:P0011","text":"ὅτι δʼ εἰσὶν ἀρχαὶ καὶ αἴτια γενητὰ καὶ φθαρτὰ ἄνευ τοῦ γίγνεσθαι καὶ φθείρεσθαι, φανερόν. εἰ γὰρ μὴ τοῦτʼ, ἐξ ἀνάγκης πάντʼ ἔσται, εἰ τοῦ γιγνομένου καὶ φθειρομένου μὴ κατὰ συμβεβηκὸς αἴτιόν τι ἀνάγκη εἶναι. πότερον γὰρ ἔσται τοδὶ ἢ οὔ; ἐάν γε τοδὶ γένηται· εἰ δὲ μή, οὔ. τοῦτο δὲ ἐὰν ἄλλο. καὶ οὕτω δῆλον ὅτι ἀεὶ χρόνου ἀφαιρουμένου ἀπὸ πεπερασμένου χρόνου ἥξει ἐπὶ τὸ νῦν,  ὥστε ὁδὶ ἀποθανεῖται  βίᾳ, ἐάν γε ἐξέλθῃ· τοῦτο δὲ ἐὰν διψήσῃ· τοῦτο δὲ ἐὰν ἄλλο· καὶ οὕτως ἥξει εἰς ὃ νῦν ὑπάρχει, ἢ εἰς τῶν γεγονότων τι. οἷον ἐὰν διψήσῃ· τοῦτο δὲ εἰ ἐσθίει δριμέα· τοῦτο δʼ ἤτοι ὑπάρχει ἢ οὔ· ὥστʼ ἐξ ἀνάγκης ἀποθανεῖται ἢ οὐκ ἀποθανεῖται. ὁμοίως δὲ κἂν ὑπερπηδήσῃ τις εἰς τὰ γενόμενα, ὁ αὐτὸς λόγος· ἤδη γὰρ ὑπάρχει τοῦτο ἔν τινι, λέγω δὲ τὸ γεγονός· ἐξ ἀνάγκης ἄρα πάντα ἔσται τὰ ἐσόμενα, οἷον τὸ ἀποθανεῖν τὸν ζῶντα· ἤδη γάρ τι γέγονεν, οἷον τὰ ἐναντία ἐν τῷ αὐτῷ.","kind":"passage"},{"id":"RAW_DAZY_BOO6:P0012","text":"ἀλλʼ εἰ νόσῳ ἢ βίᾳ, οὔπω, ἀλλʼ ἐὰν τοδὶ γένηται. δῆλον ἄρα ὅτι μέχρι τινὸς βαδίζει ἀρχῆς, αὕτη δʼ οὐκέτι εἰς ἄλλο. ἔσται οὖν ἡ τοῦ ὁπότερʼ ἔτυχεν αὕτη, καὶ αἴτιον τῆς γενέσεως αὐτῆς ἄλλο οὐθέν. ἀλλʼ εἰς ἀρχὴν ποίαν καὶ αἴτιον ποῖον ἡ ἀναγωγὴ ἡ τοιαύτη, πότερον ὡς εἰς ὕλην ἢ ὡς εἰς τὸ οὗ ἕνεκα ἢ ὡς εἰς τὸ κινῆσαν, μάλιστα σκεπτέον.","kind":"passage"},{"id":"RAW_DAZY_BOO6:P0013","text":"περὶ μὲν οὖν τοῦ κατὰ συμβεβηκὸς ὄντος ἀφείσθω (διώρισται γὰρ ἱκανῶς)· τὸ δὲ ὡς ἀληθὲς ὄν, καὶ μὴ ὂν ὡς ψεῦδος, ἐπειδὴ παρὰ σύνθεσίν ἐστι καὶ διαίρεσιν, τὸ δὲ σύνολον περὶ μερισμὸν ἀντιφάσεως (τὸ μὲν γὰρ ἀληθὲς τὴν κατάφασιν ἐπὶ τῷ συγκειμένῳ ἔχει τὴν δʼ ἀπόφασιν ἐπὶ τῷ διῃρημένῳ, τὸ δὲ ψεῦδος τούτου τοῦ μερισμοῦ τὴν ἀντίφασιν· πῶς δὲ τὸ ἅμα ἢ τὸ χωρὶς νοεῖν συμβαίνει, ἄλλος λόγος, λέγω δὲ τὸ ἅμα καὶ τὸ χωρὶς ὥστε μὴ τὸ ἐφεξῆς ἀλλʼ ἕν τι γίγνεσθαἰ· οὐ γάρ ἐστι τὸ ψεῦδος καὶ τὸ ἀληθὲς ἐν τοῖς πράγμασιν, οἷον τὸ μὲν ἀγαθὸν ἀληθὲς τὸ δὲ κακὸν εὐθὺς ψεῦδος, ἀλλʼ ἐν διανοίᾳ, περὶ δὲ τὰ ἁπλᾶ καὶ τὰ τί ἐστιν οὐδʼ ἐν διανοίᾳ·—ὅσα μὲν οὖν δεῖ θεωρῆσαι περὶ τὸ οὕτως ὂν καὶ μὴ ὄν, ὕστερον ἐπισκεπτέον· ἐπεὶ δὲ ἡ συμπλοκή ἐστιν καὶ ἡ διαίρεσις ἐν διανοίᾳ ἀλλʼ οὐκ ἐν τοῖς πράγμασι, τὸ δʼ οὕτως ὂν ἕτερον ὂν τῶν κυρίως (ἢ γὰρ τὸ τί ἐστιν ἢ ὅτι ποιὸν ἢ ὅτι ποσὸν ἤ τι ἄλλο συνάπτει ἢ ἀφαιρεῖ ἡ διάνοιἀ,","kind":"passage"},{"id":"RAW_DAZY_BOO6:P0014","text":"τὸ μὲν ὡς συμβεβηκὸς καὶ τὸ ὡς ἀληθὲς ὂν ἀφετέον—τὸ γὰρ αἴτιον τοῦ μὲν ἀόριστον τοῦ δὲ τῆς διανοίας τι πάθος,  καὶ ἀμφότερα περὶ τὸ λοιπὸν γένος τοῦ ὄντος, καὶ οὐκ ἔξω δηλοῦσιν οὖσάν τινα φύσιν τοῦ ὄντος—διὸ ταῦτα μὲν ἀφείσθω, σκεπτέον δὲ τοῦ ὄντος αὐτοῦ τὰ αἴτια καὶ τὰς ἀρχὰς ᾗ ὄν.","kind":"passage"}],"sha256":"b0114ab74edfd4457cf256687e8039affd9297485a3bdf43c21bba649f0990ae","language_code":"grc","language_name":"Ἑλληνική","translations":[{"id":"SOURCE_TR_20261015T172836334853Z_b680f5b2","language_code":"en","language_name":"English","title":"Book 6","source_sha256":"b0114ab74edfd4457cf256687e8039affd9297485a3bdf43c21bba649f0990ae","sha256":"4684f678c19740404ae9d660f01ecb7800d5503cfe97e3c577a25a317fc593ec","rendition_type":"machine","review_status":"machine","model":"gpt-5.6-terra","created":"2026-10-15T17:28:36.334865+00:00","paragraphs":[{"id":"RAW_DAZY_BOO6:P0001","text":"The principles and causes of beings are sought, and clearly of beings qua beings. For there is some cause of health and good condition, and mathematical objects have principles and elements and causes; and in general every intellectual science, or one that partakes in some way of understanding, is concerned with causes and principles, either more exact or simpler. But all these sciences, having marked off some being and some genus, concern themselves with this, but not with being simply nor with being qua being; nor do they give any account of what it is, but, beginning from this—some making it evident by perception, others taking as a hypothesis what it is—they demonstrate, either more necessarily or more weakly, the attributes belonging in themselves to the genus about which they are concerned. Hence it is clear that from such an induction there is no demonstration of substance or of what it is, but some other manner of making evident.","kind":"passage","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_DAZY_BOO6:P0001"]},{"id":"RAW_DAZY_BOO6:P0002","text":"Likewise, they say nothing about whether the genus with which they are concerned exists or does not exist, because it belongs to the same act of thought both to make evident what it is and whether it is.—But since natural science happens to be concerned with a certain genus of being (for it is concerned with such substance as has within itself the principle of motion and rest), it is clear that it is neither practical nor productive. For in the productive arts the principle is in the producer, whether intellect or art or some capacity; while in practical matters it is in the agent, namely choice—for the practicable and the choiceworthy are the same. Thus, if every act of thought is either practical or productive or theoretical, natural science would be a kind of theoretical science; but it is theoretical concerning such being as is capable of motion, and concerning substance according to its account, for the most part only as not separable. But it must not escape notice how the essence and the account are; for without this, inquiry accomplishes nothing.","kind":"passage","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_DAZY_BOO6:P0002"]},{"id":"RAW_DAZY_BOO6:P0003","text":"Among things defined, and among what things are, some are like the snub and others like the concave. These differ because the snub is taken together with matter (for the snub is a concave nose), whereas concavity is without perceptible matter. If, then, all natural things are spoken of similarly to the snub, for example nose, eye, face, flesh, bone, in general animal, leaf, root, bark, in general plant (for the account of none of these is without motion, but it always has matter), it is clear how one must seek and define what it is in natural things, and why it belongs to the natural philosopher also to study a certain kind of soul, insofar as it is not without matter. That natural science is theoretical, then, is clear from these considerations; but mathematics too is theoretical. Whether it is concerned with things unmoved and separable is now unclear; yet it is clear that certain mathematical sciences study their objects qua unmoved and qua separable.","kind":"passage","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_DAZY_BOO6:P0003"]},{"id":"RAW_DAZY_BOO6:P0004","text":"But if there is something eternal and unmoved and separable, it is clear that knowledge of it belongs to a theoretical science, though not to natural science (for natural science is concerned with certain moving things), nor to mathematics, but to a science prior to both. For natural science is concerned with things separable but not unmoved; and some parts of mathematics are concerned with things unmoved but perhaps not separable, rather as in matter; whereas the first science is concerned both with things separable and with things unmoved. Now all causes must be eternal, but these especially; for these are causes for the manifest things among divine things. Thus there would be three theoretical philosophies: mathematics, natural science, and theology. For it is not unclear that, if the divine exists anywhere, it exists in such a nature; and the most honorable science must be concerned with the most honorable genus. The theoretical sciences, then, are more choiceworthy than the other sciences, and this one than the theoretical sciences.","kind":"passage","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_DAZY_BOO6:P0004"]},{"id":"RAW_DAZY_BOO6:P0005","text":"For one might be perplexed whether first philosophy is universal or concerned with some genus and one certain nature (for the manner is not the same even in the mathematical sciences: geometry and astronomy are concerned with a certain nature, whereas the universal science is common to all). If, then, there is no substance other than those constituted by nature, natural science would be the first science; but if there is some unmoved substance, this is prior and is first philosophy, and universal in this way, because it is first. And it would belong to this science to study being qua being, both what it is and the attributes belonging to it qua being.","kind":"passage","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_DAZY_BOO6:P0005"]},{"id":"RAW_DAZY_BOO6:P0006","text":"But since being in the unqualified sense is said in many ways, one of which was being according to accident, another being as true, and non-being as false, and besides these there are the figures of predication (for example, what-it-is, what sort, how much, where, when, and whatever else signifies in this manner), and besides all these, being in potentiality and in actuality:—since being is said in many ways, first one must speak of being according to accident, that there is no inquiry concerning it. A sign of this is that no science, whether practical or productive or theoretical, is concerned with it.","kind":"passage","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_DAZY_BOO6:P0006"]},{"id":"RAW_DAZY_BOO6:P0007","text":"For neither does the maker of a house make all the things that happen concomitantly when the house comes to be (for they are infinite: nothing prevents the house that has been made from being pleasant to some, harmful to others, useful to others, and, so to speak, different from all beings; and housebuilding is productive of none of these); and in the same way neither does the geometer study the accidents that belong to figures in this way, nor whether a triangle is different from a triangle having two right angles. And this reasonably follows; for an accident is, as it were, only a name. Therefore Plato was in a way not wrong in placing sophistry around non-being. For the arguments of the sophists are concerned, more than anything else, so to speak, with accident: whether musical and literate are different or the same, and whether musical Coriscus and Coriscus are the same; and whether everything that is, but is not always, has come to be; so that, if someone has become literate while being musical, he has also become musical while being literate; and however many other such arguments there are. For the accidental appears to be something close to non-being.","kind":"passage","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_DAZY_BOO6:P0007"]},{"id":"RAW_DAZY_BOO6:P0008","text":"This is also clear from arguments of the following sort. Of things that are in another way there is coming-to-be and passing-away, but of things according to accident there is not. Yet one must still speak, insofar as possible, about accident—what its nature is and through what cause it exists; for at the same time perhaps it will also become clear why there is no science of it.—Since, then, among beings some are always in the same state and of necessity—not necessity said in the sense of compulsion, but that which we speak of in saying that it cannot be otherwise—while others exist neither of necessity nor always, but for the most part, this is the principle and this is the cause of the being of accident. For whatever is neither always nor for the most part, this we call accidental. For example, if winter and cold should occur during the Dog days, we say that this has happened accidentally; but not if sultriness and heat should occur, because the latter is always or for the most part, while the former is not. And it is accidental for a human being to be white (for this is neither always nor for the most part), but not for him to be an animal.","kind":"passage","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_DAZY_BOO6:P0008"]},{"id":"RAW_DAZY_BOO6:P0009","text":"And for the builder to cure is accidental, because it is not the nature of a builder to do this, but of a doctor; yet it happened that the builder was a doctor. And a cook, aiming at pleasure, might produce something healthful, but not according to the culinary art; therefore, we say, it happened accidentally, and in a way he produces it, but simply speaking he does not. For in other cases there are productive capacities, but for these there is no determinate art or capacity. For of beings or things that come to be according to accident, the cause too is according to accident. Therefore, since not all things either are or come to be of necessity and always, but most things are for the most part, there must be being according to accident. For example, the white man is neither always nor for the most part musical; but since he sometimes comes to be so, it will be accidental (otherwise all things will be of necessity). Thus matter, capable of being otherwise than for the most part, will be the cause of accident. But one must take this as a starting-point: whether nothing exists neither always nor for the most part.","kind":"passage","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_DAZY_BOO6:P0009"]},{"id":"RAW_DAZY_BOO6:P0010","text":"Or is this impossible? There is, then, something besides these: what happens by chance and according to accident. But does what is for the most part belong to nothing, while the always belongs to something, or are there some eternal things? Concerning these matters one must inquire later; but that there is no science of accident is clear. For every science is of what is either always or for the most part—for how else will one learn or teach another? For it must be determined either by what is always or by what is for the most part, for example that honey-water is beneficial to one with a fever for the most part. But it will not be able to say what is outside this, when it is not so, for example at the new moon; for either what is at the new moon is always so or for the most part, whereas accident is contrary to these. What accident is, then, and through what cause, and that there is no science of it, has been stated.","kind":"passage","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_DAZY_BOO6:P0010"]},{"id":"RAW_DAZY_BOO6:P0011","text":"That there are principles and causes which are generated and perishable without themselves coming to be and perishing is clear. For if this were not so, all things would be of necessity, if there must be some cause, not according to accident, of what comes to be and perishes. For will this thing be or not? It will, if this other thing comes to be; if not, it will not. And this will occur if another thing does. And thus it is clear that, if time is always subtracted from a finite time, one will arrive at the now, so that this man will die violently if he goes out; and this if he thirsts; and this if another thing happens; and thus one will arrive at what now exists, or at something among things that have happened. For example, if he thirsts; and this if he eats pungent food; and this either exists or does not exist. Thus he will necessarily die or not die. Likewise, if someone should leap over into things that have come to be, the same account applies. For this already exists in something—I mean the thing that has happened. Therefore all future things will be of necessity, for example that a living person will die; for something has already happened, for example the contraries in the same thing.","kind":"passage","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_DAZY_BOO6:P0011"]},{"id":"RAW_DAZY_BOO6:P0012","text":"But whether by disease or by violence is not yet determined, but it will be if this thing comes to be. It is clear, then, that the regress proceeds as far as some principle, but this no longer proceeds to another. This, then, will be the principle of what happens by chance, and there is no other cause of its coming-to-be. But to what sort of principle and what sort of cause such a regress leads—whether to matter or to that for the sake of which or to that which moved—is what must especially be considered.","kind":"passage","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_DAZY_BOO6:P0012"]},{"id":"RAW_DAZY_BOO6:P0013","text":"Let being according to accident, then, be set aside (for it has been sufficiently distinguished). As for being as true, and non-being as false, since these depend on combination and division, and the whole concerns a division of contradiction (for the true has affirmation in the case of what is combined and negation in the case of what is divided, while the false has the contradiction of this division; but how thinking simultaneously or separately comes about is another account—I mean by simultaneously and separately, not that it occurs successively but that it comes to be one thing; for the false and the true are not in things, as though the good were true and the bad immediately false, but in thought; while in the case of simple things and what they are, they are not even in thought):—whatever must be studied concerning being and non-being in this sense must be examined later. But since combination and division are in thought and not in things, and being in this sense is a different kind of being from beings in the proper sense (for thought either combines or removes what-it-is, or that it is of a certain sort, or that it is of a certain quantity, or something else),","kind":"passage","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_DAZY_BOO6:P0013"]},{"id":"RAW_DAZY_BOO6:P0014","text":"being as accidental and being as true must be set aside—for the cause of the one is indeterminate, and that of the other is some affection of thought, and both concern the remaining genus of being and do not indicate any nature of being existing outside it. Therefore let these be set aside, and let us investigate the causes and principles of being itself qua being.","kind":"passage","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_DAZY_BOO6:P0014"]}],"source_digest":"2953ba3b2a57dd0aeb4dda34dfbe75d06be51ef99bb804de15f0c78b6c69e496","source_language_code":"grc","original_title":"Book 6"}],"passage_basis":"frozen-grounding","original_title":"Book 6","title_language_code":"en"},{"id":"RAW_DAZY_BOO7","title":"Book 7","order":7,"chapter_id":"CH_DAZY_BOO7","text":"τὸ ὂν λέγεται πολλαχῶς, καθάπερ διειλόμεθα πρότερον ἐν τοῖς περὶ τοῦ ποσαχῶς· σημαίνει γὰρ τὸ μὲν τί ἐστι καὶ τόδε τι, τὸ δὲ ποιὸν ἢ ποσὸν ἢ τῶν ἄλλων ἕκαστον τῶν οὕτω κατηγορουμένων. τοσαυταχῶς δὲ λεγομένου τοῦ ὄντος φανερὸν ὅτι τούτων πρῶτον ὂν τὸ τί ἐστιν, ὅπερ σημαίνει τὴν οὐσίαν (ὅταν μὲν γὰρ εἴπωμεν ποῖόν τι τόδε, ἢ ἀγαθὸν λέγομεν ἢ κακόν, ἀλλʼ οὐ τρίπηχυ ἢ ἄνθρωπον· ὅταν δὲ τί ἐστιν, οὐ λευκὸν οὐδὲ θερμὸν οὐδὲ τρίπηχυ, ἀλλὰ ἄνθρωπον ἢ θεόν), τὰ δʼ ἄλλα λέγεται ὄντα τῷ τοῦ οὕτως ὄντος τὰ μὲν ποσότητες εἶναι, τὰ δὲ ποιότητες, τὰ δὲ πάθη, τὰ δὲ ἄλλο τι. διὸ κἂν ἀπορήσειέ τις πότερον τὸ βαδίζειν καὶ τὸ ὑγιαίνειν καὶ τὸ καθῆσθαι ἕκαστον αὐτῶν ὂν σημαίνει, ὁμοίως δὲ καὶ ἐπὶ τῶν ἄλλων ὁτουοῦν τῶν τοιούτων· οὐδὲν γὰρ αὐτῶν ἐστὶν οὔτε καθʼ αὑτὸ πεφυκὸς οὔτε χωρίζεσθαι δυνατὸν τῆς οὐσίας, ἀλλὰ μᾶλλον, εἴπερ, τὸ βαδίζον τῶν ὄντων καὶ τὸ καθήμενον καὶ τὸ ὑγιαῖνον. ταῦτα δὲ μᾶλλον φαίνεται ὄντα, διότι ἔστι τι τὸ ὑποκείμενον αὐτοῖς ὡρισμένον (τοῦτο δʼ ἐστὶν ἡ οὐσία καὶ τὸ καθʼ ἕκαστον), ὅπερ ἐμφαίνεται ἐν τῇ κατηγορίᾳ τῇ τοιαύτῃ· τὸ ἀγαθὸν γὰρ ἢ τὸ καθήμενον οὐκ ἄνευ τούτου λέγεται. δῆλον οὖν ὅτι διὰ ταύτην κἀκείνων ἕκαστον ἔστιν, ὥστε τὸ πρώτως ὂν καὶ οὐ τὶ ὂν ἀλλʼ ὂν ἁπλῶς ἡ οὐσία ἂν εἴη. πολλαχῶς μὲν οὖν λέγεται τὸ πρῶτον· ὅμως δὲ πάντως ἡ οὐσία πρῶτον, καὶ λόγῳ καὶ γνώσει καὶ χρόνῳ. τῶν μὲν γὰρ ἄλλων κατηγορημάτων οὐθὲν χωριστόν, αὕτη δὲ μόνη· καὶ τῷ λόγῳ δὲ τοῦτο πρῶτον (ἀνάγκη γὰρ ἐν τῷ ἑκάστου λόγῳ τὸν τῆς οὐσίας ἐνυπάρχειν)· καὶ εἰδέναι δὲ τότʼ οἰόμεθα ἕκαστον μάλιστα, ὅταν τί ἐστιν ὁ ἄνθρωπος γνῶμεν ἢ τὸ πῦρ,  μᾶλλον ἢ τὸ ποιὸν ἢ τὸ ποσὸν ἢ τὸ πού, ἐπεὶ καὶ αὐτῶν τούτων τότε ἕκαστον ἴσμεν, ὅταν τί ἐστι τὸ ποσὸν ἢ τὸ ποιὸν γνῶμεν. καὶ δὴ καὶ τὸ πάλαι τε καὶ νῦν καὶ ἀεὶ ζητούμενον καὶ ἀεὶ ἀπορούμενον, τί τὸ ὄν, τοῦτό ἐστι τίς ἡ οὐσία (τοῦτο γὰρ οἱ μὲν ἓν εἶναί φασιν οἱ δὲ πλείω ἢ ἕν, καὶ οἱ μὲν πεπερασμένα οἱ δὲ ἄπειρα), διὸ καὶ ἡμῖν καὶ μάλιστα καὶ πρῶτον καὶ μόνον ὡς εἰπεῖν περὶ τοῦ οὕτως ὄντος θεωρητέον τί ἐστιν. \n\n\n\n \n\n \n\nδοκεῖ δʼ ἡ οὐσία ὑπάρχειν φανερώτατα μὲν τοῖς σώμασιν (διὸ τά τε ζῷα καὶ τὰ φυτὰ καὶ τὰ μόρια αὐτῶν οὐσίας εἶναί φαμεν, καὶ τὰ φυσικὰ σώματα, οἷον πῦρ καὶ ὕδωρ καὶ γῆν καὶ τῶν τοιούτων ἕκαστον, καὶ ὅσα ἢ μόρια τούτων ἢ ἐκ τούτων ἐστίν, ἢ μορίων ἢ πάντων, οἷον ὅ τε οὐρανὸς καὶ τὰ μόρια αὐτοῦ, ἄστρα καὶ σελήνη καὶ ἥλιος)· πότερον δὲ αὗται μόναι οὐσίαι εἰσὶν ἢ καὶ ἄλλαι, ἢ τούτων τινὲς ἢ καὶ ἄλλαι, ἢ τούτων μὲν οὐθὲν ἕτεραι δέ τινες, σκεπτέον. δοκεῖ δέ τισι τὰ τοῦ σώματος πέρατα, οἷον ἐπιφάνεια καὶ γραμμὴ καὶ στιγμὴ καὶ μονάς, εἶναι οὐσίαι, καὶ μᾶλλον ἢ τὸ σῶμα καὶ τὸ στερεόν. ἔτι παρὰ τὰ αἰσθητὰ οἱ μὲν οὐκ οἴονται εἶναι οὐδὲν τοιοῦτον, οἱ δὲ πλείω καὶ μᾶλλον ὄντα ἀΐδια, ὥσπερ Πλάτων τά τε εἴδη καὶ τὰ μαθηματικὰ δύο οὐσίας, τρίτην δὲ τὴν τῶν αἰσθητῶν σωμάτων οὐσίαν, Σπεύσιππος δὲ καὶ πλείους οὐσίας ἀπὸ τοῦ ἑνὸς ἀρξάμενος, καὶ ἀρχὰς ἑκάστης οὐσίας, ἄλλην μὲν ἀριθμῶν ἄλλην δὲ μεγεθῶν, ἔπειτα ψυχῆς· καὶ τοῦτον δὴ τὸν τρόπον ἐπεκτείνει τὰς οὐσίας. ἔνιοι δὲ τὰ μὲν εἴδη καὶ τοὺς ἀριθμοὺς τὴν αὐτὴν ἔχειν φασὶ φύσιν, τὰ δὲ ἄλλα ἐχόμενα, γραμμὰς καὶ ἐπίπεδα, μέχρι πρὸς τὴν τοῦ οὐρανοῦ οὐσίαν καὶ τὰ αἰσθητά. περὶ δὴ τούτων τί λέγεται καλῶς ἢ μὴ καλῶς, καὶ τίνες εἰσὶν οὐσίαι, καὶ πότερον εἰσί τινες παρὰ τὰς αἰσθητὰς ἢ οὐκ εἰσί, καὶ αὗται πῶς εἰσί, καὶ πότερον ἔστι τις χωριστὴ οὐσία, καὶ διὰ τί καὶ πῶς, ἢ οὐδεμία, παρὰ τὰς αἰσθητάς, σκεπτέον, ὑποτυπωσαμένοις τὴν οὐσίαν πρῶτον τί ἐστιν. \n\n\n\n \n\n \n\nλέγεται δʼ ἡ οὐσία, εἰ μὴ πλεοναχῶς, ἀλλʼ ἐν τέτταρσί γε μάλιστα· καὶ γὰρ τὸ τί ἦν εἶναι καὶ τὸ καθόλου καὶ τὸ γένος οὐσία δοκεῖ εἶναι ἑκάστου, καὶ τέταρτον τούτων τὸ ὑποκείμενον. τὸ δʼ ὑποκείμενόν ἐστι καθʼ οὗ τὰ ἄλλα λέγεται, ἐκεῖνο δὲ αὐτὸ μηκέτι κατʼ ἄλλου· διὸ πρῶτον περὶ τούτου διοριστέον·  μάλιστα γὰρ δοκεῖ εἶναι οὐσία τὸ ὑποκείμενον πρῶτον. τοιοῦτον δὲ τρόπον μέν τινα ἡ ὕλη λέγεται, ἄλλον δὲ τρόπον ἡ μορφή, τρίτον δὲ τὸ ἐκ τούτων (λέγω δὲ τὴν μὲν ὕλην οἷον τὸν χαλκόν, τὴν δὲ μορφὴν τὸ σχῆμα τῆς ἰδέας, τὸ δʼ ἐκ τούτων τὸν ἀνδριάντα τὸ σύνολον), ὥστε εἰ τὸ εἶδος τῆς ὕλης πρότερον καὶ μᾶλλον ὄν, καὶ τοῦ ἐξ ἀμφοῖν πρότερον ἔσται διὰ τὸν αὐτὸν λόγον. νῦν μὲν οὖν τύπῳ εἴρηται τί ποτʼ ἐστὶν ἡ οὐσία, ὅτι τὸ μὴ καθʼ ὑποκειμένου ἀλλὰ καθʼ οὗ τὰ ἄλλα· δεῖ δὲ μὴ μόνον οὕτως· οὐ γὰρ ἱκανόν· αὐτὸ γὰρ τοῦτο ἄδηλον, καὶ ἔτι ἡ ὕλη οὐσία γίγνεται. εἰ γὰρ μὴ αὕτη οὐσία, τίς ἐστιν ἄλλη διαφεύγει· περιαιρουμένων γὰρ τῶν ἄλλων οὐ φαίνεται οὐδὲν ὑπομένον· τὰ μὲν γὰρ ἄλλα τῶν σωμάτων πάθη καὶ ποιήματα καὶ δυνάμεις, τὸ δὲ μῆκος καὶ πλάτος καὶ βάθος ποσότητές τινες ἀλλʼ οὐκ οὐσίαι (τὸ γὰρ ποσὸν οὐκ οὐσία), ἀλλὰ μᾶλλον ᾧ ὑπάρχει ταῦτα πρώτῳ, ἐκεῖνό ἐστιν οὐσία. ἀλλὰ μὴν ἀφαιρουμένου μήκους καὶ πλάτους καὶ βάθους οὐδὲν ὁρῶμεν ὑπολειπόμενον, πλὴν εἴ τί ἐστι τὸ ὁριζόμενον ὑπὸ τούτων, ὥστε τὴν ὕλην ἀνάγκη φαίνεσθαι μόνην οὐσίαν οὕτω σκοπουμένοις. λέγω δʼ ὕλην ἣ καθʼ αὑτὴν μήτε τὶ μήτε ποσὸν μήτε ἄλλο μηδὲν λέγεται οἷς ὥρισται τὸ ὄν. ἔστι γάρ τι καθʼ οὗ κατηγορεῖται τούτων ἕκαστον, ᾧ τὸ εἶναι ἕτερον καὶ τῶν κατηγοριῶν ἑκάστῃ (τὰ μὲν γὰρ ἄλλα τῆς οὐσίας κατηγορεῖται, αὕτη δὲ τῆς ὕλης), ὥστε τὸ ἔσχατον καθʼ αὑτὸ οὔτε τὶ οὔτε ποσὸν οὔτε ἄλλο οὐδέν ἐστιν· οὐδὲ δὴ αἱ ἀποφάσεις, καὶ γὰρ αὗται ὑπάρξουσι κατὰ συμβεβηκός. ἐκ μὲν οὖν τούτων θεωροῦσι συμβαίνει οὐσίαν εἶναι τὴν ὕλην· ἀδύνατον δέ· καὶ γὰρ τὸ χωριστὸν καὶ τὸ τόδε τι ὑπάρχειν δοκεῖ μάλιστα τῇ οὐσίᾳ, διὸ τὸ εἶδος καὶ τὸ ἐξ ἀμφοῖν οὐσία δόξειεν ἂν εἶναι μᾶλλον τῆς ὕλης. τὴν μὲν τοίνυν ἐξ ἀμφοῖν οὐσίαν, λέγω δὲ τὴν ἔκ τε τῆς ὕλης καὶ τῆς μορφῆς, ἀφετέον, ὑστέρα γὰρ καὶ δήλη· φανερὰ δέ πως καὶ ἡ ὕλη· περὶ δὲ τῆς τρίτης σκεπτέον, αὕτη γὰρ ἀπορωτάτη. ὁμολογοῦνται δʼ οὐσίαι εἶναι τῶν αἰσθητῶν τινές, ὥστε ἐν ταύταις ζητητέον πρῶτον.  πρὸ ἔργου γὰρ τὸ μεταβαίνειν εἰς τὸ γνωριμώτερον. ἡ γὰρ μάθησις οὕτω γίγνεται πᾶσι διὰ τῶν ἧττον γνωρίμων φύσει εἰς τὰ γνώριμα μᾶλλον· καὶ τοῦτο ἔργον ἐστίν, ὥσπερ ἐν ταῖς πράξεσι τὸ ποιῆσαι ἐκ τῶν ἑκάστῳ ἀγαθῶν τὰ ὅλως ἀγαθὰ ἑκάστῳ ἀγαθά, οὕτως ἐκ τῶν αὐτῷ γνωριμωτέρων τὰ τῇ φύσει γνώριμα αὐτῷ γνώριμα. τὰ δʼ ἑκάστοις γνώριμα καὶ πρῶτα πολλάκις ἠρέμα ἐστὶ γνώριμα, καὶ μικρὸν ἢ οὐθὲν ἔχει τοῦ ὄντος· ἀλλʼ ὅμως ἐκ τῶν φαύλως μὲν γνωστῶν αὐτῷ δὲ γνωστῶν τὰ ὅλως γνωστὰ γνῶναι πειρατέον, μεταβαίνοντας, ὥσπερ εἴρηται, διὰ τούτων αὐτῶν.\n\n\n\n \n\n \n\nἐπεὶ δʼ ἐν ἀρχῇ διειλόμεθα πόσοις ὁρίζομεν τὴν οὐσίαν, καὶ τούτων ἕν τι ἐδόκει εἶναι τὸ τί ἦν εἶναι, θεωρητέον περὶ αὐτοῦ. καὶ πρῶτον εἴπωμεν ἔνια περὶ αὐτοῦ λογικῶς, ὅτι ἐστὶ τὸ τί ἦν εἶναι ἑκάστου ὃ λέγεται καθʼ αὑτό. οὐ γάρ ἐστι τὸ σοὶ εἶναι τὸ μουσικῷ εἶναι· οὐ γὰρ κατὰ σαυτὸν εἶ μουσικός. ὃ ἄρα κατὰ σαυτόν. οὐδὲ δὴ τοῦτο πᾶν· οὐ γὰρ τὸ οὕτως καθʼ αὑτὸ ὡς ἐπιφανείᾳ λευκόν, ὅτι οὐκ ἔστι τὸ ἐπιφανείᾳ εἶναι τὸ λευκῷ εἶναι. ἀλλὰ μὴν οὐδὲ τὸ ἐξ ἀμφοῖν, τὸ ἐπιφανείᾳ λευκῇ, ὅτι πρόσεστιν αὐτό. ἐν ᾧ ἄρα μὴ ἐνέσται λόγῳ αὐτό, λέγοντι αὐτό, οὗτος ὁ λόγος τοῦ τί ἦν εἶναι ἑκάστῳ, ὥστʼ εἰ τὸ ἐπιφανείᾳ λευκῇ εἶναί ἐστι τὸ ἐπιφανείᾳ εἶναι λείᾳ, τὸ λευκῷ καὶ λείῳ εἶναι τὸ αὐτὸ καὶ ἕν. ἐπεὶ δʼ ἔστι καὶ κατὰ τὰς ἄλλας κατηγορίας σύνθετα (ἔστι γάρ τι ὑποκείμενον ἑκάστῳ, οἷον τῷ ποιῷ καὶ τῷ ποσῷ καὶ τῷ ποτὲ καὶ τῷ ποὺ καὶ τῇ κινήσει), σκεπτέον ἆρʼ ἔστι λόγος τοῦ τί ἦν εἶναι ἑκάστῳ αὐτῶν, καὶ ὑπάρχει καὶ τούτοις τὸ τί ἦν εἶναι, οἷον λευκῷ ἀνθρώπῳ . ἔστω δὴ ὄνομα αὐτῷ ἱμάτιον. τί ἐστι τὸ ἱματίῳ εἶναι; ἀλλὰ μὴν οὐδὲ τῶν καθʼ αὑτὸ λεγομένων οὐδὲ τοῦτο. ἢ τὸ οὐ καθʼ αὑτὸ λέγεται διχῶς, καὶ τούτου ἐστὶ τὸ μὲν ἐκ προσθέσεως τὸ δὲ οὔ. τὸ μὲν γὰρ τῷ αὐτὸ ἄλλῳ προσκεῖσθαι λέγεται ὃ ὁρίζεται, οἷον εἰ τὸ λευκῷ εἶναι ὁριζόμενος λέγοι λευκοῦ ἀνθρώπου λόγον· τὸ δὲ τῷ ἄλλο αὐτῷ, οἷον εἰ σημαίνοι τὸ ἱμάτιον λευκὸν ἄνθρωπον, ὁ δὲ ὁρίζοιτο ἱμάτιον ὡς λευκόν. τὸ δὴ λευκὸς ἄνθρωπος ἔστι μὲν λευκόν,  οὐ μέντοι τὸ τί ἦν εἶναι λευκῷ εἶναι.—ἀλλὰ τὸ ἱματίῳ εἶναι ἆρά ἐστι τί ἦν εἶναί τι  ὅλως; ἢ οὔ; ὅπερ γάρ τί ἐστι τὸ τί ἦν εἶναι· ὅταν δʼ ἄλλο κατʼ ἄλλου λέγηται, οὐκ ἔστιν ὅπερ τόδε τι, οἷον ὁ λευκὸς ἄνθρωπος οὐκ ἔστιν ὅπερ τόδε τι, εἴπερ τὸ τόδε ταῖς οὐσίαις ὑπάρχει μόνον· ὥστε τὸ τί ἦν εἶναί ἐστιν ὅσων ὁ λόγος ἐστὶν ὁρισμός. ὁρισμὸς δʼ ἐστὶν οὐκ ἂν ὄνομα λόγῳ ταὐτὸ σημαίνῃ (πάντες γὰρ ἂν εἶεν οἱ λόγοι ὅροι· ἔσται γὰρ ὄνομα ὁτῳοῦν λόγῳ, ὥστε καὶ ἡ Ἰλιὰς ὁρισμὸς ἔσται), ἀλλʼ ἐὰν πρώτου τινὸς ᾖ· τοιαῦτα δʼ ἐστὶν ὅσα λέγεται μὴ τῷ ἄλλο κατʼ ἄλλου λέγεσθαι. οὐκ ἔσται ἄρα οὐδενὶ τῶν μὴ γένους εἰδῶν ὑπάρχον τὸ τί ἦν εἶναι, ἀλλὰ τούτοις μόνον (ταῦτα γὰρ δοκεῖ οὐ κατὰ μετοχὴν λέγεσθαι καὶ πάθος οὐδʼ ὡς συμβεβηκός)· ἀλλὰ λόγος μὲν ἔσται ἑκάστου καὶ τῶν ἄλλων τί σημαίνει, ἐὰν ᾖ ὄνομα, ὅτι τόδε τῷδε ὑπάρχει, ἢ ἀντὶ λόγου ἁπλοῦ ἀκριβέστερος· ὁρισμὸς δʼ οὐκ ἔσται οὐδὲ τὸ τί ἦν εἶναι. ἢ καὶ ὁ ὁρισμὸς ὥσπερ καὶ τὸ τί ἐστι πλεοναχῶς λέγεται; καὶ γὰρ τὸ τί ἐστιν ἕνα μὲν τρόπον σημαίνει τὴν οὐσίαν καὶ τὸ τόδε τι, ἄλλον δὲ ἕκαστον τῶν κατηγορουμένων, ποσὸν ποιὸν καὶ ὅσα ἄλλα τοιαῦτα. ὥσπερ γὰρ καὶ τὸ ἔστιν ὑπάρχει πᾶσιν, ἀλλʼ οὐχ ὁμοίως ἀλλὰ τῷ μὲν πρώτως τοῖς δʼ ἑπομένως, οὕτω καὶ τὸ τί ἐστιν ἁπλῶς μὲν τῇ οὐσίᾳ πὼς δὲ τοῖς ἄλλοις· καὶ γὰρ τὸ ποιὸν ἐροίμεθʼ ἂν τί ἐστιν, ὥστε καὶ τὸ ποιὸν τῶν τί ἐστιν, ἀλλʼ οὐχ ἁπλῶς, ἀλλʼ ὥσπερ ἐπὶ τοῦ μὴ ὄντος λογικῶς φασί τινες εἶναι τὸ μὴ ὄν, οὐχ ἁπλῶς ἀλλὰ μὴ ὄν, οὕτω καὶ τὸ ποιόν.—δεῖ μὲν οὖν σκοπεῖν καὶ τὸ πῶς δεῖ λέγειν περὶ ἕκαστον, οὐ μὴν μᾶλλόν γε ἢ τὸ πῶς ἔχει· διὸ καὶ νῦν ἐπεὶ τὸ λεγόμενον φανερόν, καὶ τὸ τί ἦν εἶναι ὁμοίως ὑπάρξει πρώτως μὲν καὶ ἁπλῶς τῇ οὐσίᾳ, εἶτα καὶ τοῖς ἄλλοις, ὥσπερ καὶ τὸ τί ἐστιν, οὐχ ἁπλῶς τί ἦν εἶναι ἀλλὰ ποιῷ ἢ ποσῷ τί ἦν εἶναι. δεῖ γὰρ ἢ ὁμωνύμως ταῦτα φάναι εἶναι ὄντα, ἢ προστιθέντας καὶ ἀφαιροῦντας, ὥσπερ καὶ τὸ μὴ ἐπιστητὸν ἐπιστητόν, ἐπεὶ τό γε ὀρθόν ἐστι μήτε ὁμωνύμως φάναι μήτε ὡσαύτως ἀλλʼ ὥσπερ τὸ ἰατρικὸν τῷ πρὸς τὸ αὐτὸ μὲν καὶ ἕν, οὐ τὸ αὐτὸ δὲ καὶ ἕν, οὐ μέντοι οὐδὲ ὁμωνύμως·  οὐδὲ γὰρ ἰατρικὸν σῶμα καὶ ἔργον καὶ σκεῦος λέγεται οὔτε ὁμωνύμως οὔτε καθʼ ἓν ἀλλὰ πρὸς ἕν. ἀλλὰ ταῦτα μὲν ὁποτέρως τις ἐθέλει λέγειν διαφέρει οὐδέν· ἐκεῖνο δὲ φανερὸν ὅτι ὁ πρώτως καὶ ἁπλῶς ὁρισμὸς καὶ τὸ τί ἦν εἶναι τῶν οὐσιῶν ἐστίν. οὐ μὴν ἀλλὰ καὶ τῶν ἄλλων ὁμοίως ἐστί, πλὴν οὐ πρώτως. οὐ γὰρ ἀνάγκη, ἂν τοῦτο τιθῶμεν, τούτου ὁρισμὸν εἶναι ὃ ἂν λόγῳ τὸ αὐτὸ σημαίνῃ, ἀλλὰ τινὶ λόγῳ· τοῦτο δὲ ἐὰν ἑνὸς ᾖ, μὴ τῷ συνεχεῖ ὥσπερ ἡ Ἰλιὰς ἢ ὅσα συνδέσμω ι,ι ἀλλʼ ἐὰν ὁσαχῶς λέγεται τὸ ἕν· τὸ δʼ ἓν λέγεται ὥσπερ τὸ ὄν· τὸ δὲ ὂν τὸ μὲν τόδε τι τὸ δὲ ποσὸν τὸ δὲ ποιόν τι σημαίνει. διὸ καὶ λευκοῦ ἀνθρώπου ἔσται λόγος καὶ ὁρισμός, ἄλλον δὲ τρόπον καὶ τοῦ λευκοῦ καὶ οὐσίας. \n\n\n\n \n\n \n\nἔχει δʼ ἀπορίαν, ἐάν τις μὴ φῇ ὁρισμὸν εἶναι τὸν ἐκ προσθέσεως λόγον, τίνος ἔσται ὁρισμὸς τῶν οὐχ ἁπλῶν ἀλλὰ συνδεδυασμένων· ἐκ προσθέσεως γὰρ ἀνάγκη δηλοῦν. λέγω δὲ οἷον ἔστι ῥὶς καὶ κοιλότης, καὶ σιμότης τὸ ἐκ τῶν δυοῖν λεγόμενον τῷ τόδε ἐν τῷδε, καὶ οὐ κατὰ συμβεβηκός γε οὔθʼ ἡ κοιλότης οὔθʼ ἡ σιμότης πάθος τῆς ῥινός, ἀλλὰ καθʼ αὑτήν· οὐδʼ ὡς τὸ λευκὸν Καλλίᾳ, ἢ ἀνθρώπῳ, ὅτι Καλλίας λευκὸς ᾧ συμβέβηκεν ἀνθρώπῳ εἶναι, ἀλλʼ ὡς τὸ ἄρρεν τῷ ζῴῳ καὶ τὸ ἴσον τῷ ποσῷ καὶ πάντα ὅσα λέγεται καθʼ αὑτὰ ὑπάρχειν. ταῦτα δʼ ἐστὶν ἐν ὅσοις ὑπάρχει ἢ ὁ λόγος ἢ τοὔνομα οὗ ἐστὶ τοῦτο τὸ πάθος, καὶ μὴ ἐνδέχεται δηλῶσαι χωρίς, ὥσπερ τὸ λευκὸν ἄνευ τοῦ ἀνθρώπου ἐνδέχεται ἀλλʼ οὐ τὸ θῆλυ ἄνευ τοῦ ζῴου· ὥστε τούτων τὸ τί ἦν εἶναι καὶ ὁρισμὸς ἢ οὐκ ἔστιν οὐδενὸς ἤ, εἰ ἔστιν, ἄλλως, καθάπερ εἰρήκαμεν. ἔστι δὲ ἀπορία καὶ ἑτέρα περὶ αὐτῶν. εἰ μὲν γὰρ τὸ αὐτό ἐστι σιμὴ ῥὶς καὶ κοίλη ῥίς, τὸ αὐτὸ ἔσται τὸ σιμὸν καὶ τὸ κοῖλον· εἰ δὲ μή, διὰ τὸ ἀδύνατον εἶναι εἰπεῖν τὸ σιμὸν ἄνευ τοῦ πράγματος οὗ ἐστὶ πάθος καθʼ αὑτό (ἔστι γὰρ τὸ σιμὸν κοιλότης ἐν ῥινί), τὸ ῥῖνα σιμὴν εἰπεῖν ἢ οὐκ ἔστιν ἢ δὶς τὸ αὐτὸ ἔσται εἰρημένον, ῥὶς ῥὶς κοίλη (ἡ γὰρ ῥὶς ἡ σιμὴ ῥὶς ῥὶς κοίλη ἔσται), διὸ ἄτοπον τὸ ὑπάρχειν τοῖς τοιούτοις τὸ τί ἦν εἶναι· εἰ δὲ μή, εἰς ἄπειρον εἶσιν· ῥινὶ γὰρ ῥινὶ σιμῇ ἔτι ἄλλο ἐνέσται.  δῆλον τοίνυν ὅτι μόνης τῆς οὐσίας ἐστὶν ὁ ὁρισμός. εἰ γὰρ καὶ τῶν ἄλλων κατηγοριῶν, ἀνάγκη ἐκ προσθέσεως εἶναι, οἷον τοῦ †ποιοῦ† καὶ περιττοῦ· οὐ γὰρ ἄνευ ἀριθμοῦ, οὐδὲ τὸ θῆλυ ἄνευ ζῴου (τὸ δὲ ἐκ προσθέσεως λέγω ἐν οἷς συμβαίνει δὶς τὸ αὐτὸ λέγειν ὥσπερ ἐν τούτοις). εἰ δὲ τοῦτο ἀληθές, οὐδὲ συνδυαζομένων ἔσται, οἷον ἀριθμοῦ περιττοῦ· ἀλλὰ λανθάνει ὅτι οὐκ ἀκριβῶς λέγονται οἱ λόγοι. εἰ δʼ εἰσὶ καὶ τούτων ὅροι, ἤτοι ἄλλον τρόπον εἰσὶν ἢ καθάπερ ἐλέχθη πολλαχῶς λεκτέον εἶναι τὸν ὁρισμὸν καὶ τὸ τί ἦν εἶναι, ὥστε ὡδὶ μὲν οὐδενὸς ἔσται ὁρισμὸς οὐδὲ τὸ τί ἦν εἶναι οὐδενὶ ὑπάρξει πλὴν ταῖς οὐσίαις, ὡδὶ δʼ ἔσται. ὅτι μὲν οὖν ἐστὶν ὁ ὁρισμὸς ὁ τοῦ τί ἦν εἶναι λόγος, καὶ τὸ τί ἦν εἶναι ἢ μόνων τῶν οὐσιῶν ἐστὶν ἢ μάλιστα καὶ πρώτως καὶ ἁπλῶς, δῆλον.\n\n\n\n \n\n \n\nπότερον δὲ ταὐτόν ἐστιν ἢ ἕτερον τὸ τί ἦν εἶναι καὶ ἕκαστον, σκεπτέον. ἔστι γάρ τι πρὸ ἔργου πρὸς τὴν περὶ τῆς οὐσίας σκέψιν· ἕκαστόν τε γὰρ οὐκ ἄλλο δοκεῖ εἶναι τῆς ἑαυτοῦ οὐσίας, καὶ τὸ τί ἦν εἶναι λέγεται εἶναι ἡ ἑκάστου οὐσία. ἐπὶ μὲν δὴ τῶν λεγομένων κατὰ συμβεβηκὸς δόξειεν ἂν ἕτερον εἶναι, οἷον λευκὸς ἄνθρωπος ἕτερον καὶ τὸ λευκῷ ἀνθρώπῳ εἶναι (εἰ γὰρ τὸ αὐτό, καὶ τὸ ἀνθρώπῳ εἶναι καὶ τὸ λευκῷ ἀνθρώπῳ τὸ αὐτό· τὸ αὐτὸ γὰρ ἄνθρωπος καὶ λευκὸς ἄνθρωπος, ὡς φασίν, ὥστε καὶ τὸ λευκῷ ἀνθρώπῳ καὶ τὸ ἀνθρώπῳ· ἢ οὐκ ἀνάγκη ὅσα κατὰ συμβεβηκὸς εἶναι ταὐτά, οὐ γὰρ ὡσαύτως τὰ ἄκρα γίγνεται ταὐτά· ἀλλʼ ἴσως γε ἐκεῖνο δόξειεν ἂν συμβαίνειν, τὰ ἄκρα γίγνεσθαι ταὐτὰ τὰ κατὰ συμβεβηκός, οἷον τὸ λευκῷ εἶναι καὶ τὸ μουσικῷ· δοκεῖ δὲ οὔ)· ἐπὶ δὲ τῶν καθʼ αὑτὰ λεγομένων ἆρʼ ἀνάγκη ταὐτὸ εἶναι, οἷον εἴ τινες εἰσὶν οὐσίαι ὧν ἕτεραι μὴ εἰσὶν οὐσίαι μηδὲ φύσεις ἕτεραι πρότεραι, οἵας φασὶ τὰς ἰδέας εἶναί τινες; εἰ γὰρ ἔσται ἕτερον αὐτὸ τὸ ἀγαθὸν καὶ τὸ ἀγαθῷ εἶναι, καὶ ζῷον καὶ τὸ ζῴῳ, καὶ τὸ ὄντι καὶ τὸ ὄν,  ἔσονται ἄλλαι τε οὐσίαι καὶ φύσεις καὶ ἰδέαι παρὰ τὰς λεγομένας, καὶ πρότεραι οὐσίαι ἐκεῖναι, εἰ τὸ τί ἦν εἶναι οὐσία ἐστίν. καὶ εἰ μὲν ἀπολελυμέναι ἀλλήλων, τῶν μὲν οὐκ ἔσται ἐπιστήμη τὰ δʼ οὐκ ἔσται ὄντα (λέγω δὲ τὸ ἀπολελύσθαι εἰ μήτε τῷ ἀγαθῷ αὐτῷ ὑπάρχει τὸ εἶναι ἀγαθῷ μήτε τούτῳ τὸ εἶναι ἀγαθόν)· ἐπιστήμη τε γὰρ ἑκάστου ἔστιν ὅταν τὸ τί ἦν ἐκείνῳ εἶναι γνῶμεν, καὶ ἐπὶ ἀγαθοῦ καὶ τῶν ἄλλων ὁμοίως ἔχει, ὥστε εἰ μηδὲ τὸ ἀγαθῷ εἶναι ἀγαθόν, οὐδὲ τὸ ὄντι ὂν οὐδὲ τὸ ἑνὶ ἕν· ὁμοίως δὲ πάντα ἔστιν ἢ οὐθὲν τὰ τί ἦν εἶναι, ὥστʼ εἰ μηδὲ τὸ ὄντι ὄν, οὐδὲ τῶν ἄλλων οὐδέν. ἔτι ᾧ μὴ ὑπάρχει ἀγαθῷ εἶναι, οὐκ ἀγαθόν. ἀνάγκη ἄρα ἓν εἶναι τὸ ἀγαθὸν καὶ ἀγαθῷ εἶναι καὶ καλὸν καὶ καλῷ εἶναι, καὶ ὅσα μὴ κατʼ ἄλλο λέγεται, ἀλλὰ καθʼ αὑτὰ καὶ πρῶτα· καὶ γὰρ τοῦτο ἱκανὸν ἂν ὑπάρχῃ, κἂν μὴ ᾖ εἴδη, μᾶλλον δʼ ἴσως κἂν ᾖ εἴδη (ἅμα δὲ δῆλον καὶ ὅτι εἴπερ εἰσὶν αἱ ἰδέαι οἵας τινές φασιν, οὐκ ἔσται τὸ ὑποκείμενον οὐσία· ταύτας γὰρ οὐσίας μὲν ἀναγκαῖον εἶναι, μὴ καθʼ ὑποκειμένου δέ· ἔσονται γὰρ κατὰ μέθεξιν).—ἔκ τε δὴ τούτων τῶν λόγων ἓν καὶ ταὐτὸ οὐ κατὰ συμβεβηκὸς αὐτὸ ἕκαστον καὶ τὸ τί ἦν εἶναι, καὶ ὅτι γε τὸ ἐπίστασθαι ἕκαστον τοῦτό ἐστι, τὸ τί ἦν εἶναι ἐπίστασθαι, ὥστε καὶ κατὰ τὴν ἔκθεσιν ἀνάγκη ἕν τι εἶναι ἄμφω (τὸ δὲ κατὰ συμβεβηκὸς λεγόμενον, οἷον τὸ μουσικὸν ἢ λευκόν, διὰ τὸ διττὸν σημαίνειν οὐκ ἀληθὲς εἰπεῖν ὡς ταὐτὸ τὸ τί ἦν εἶναι καὶ αὐτό· καὶ γὰρ ᾧ συμβέβηκε λευκὸν καὶ τὸ συμβεβηκός, ὥστʼ ἔστι μὲν ὡς ταὐτόν, ἔστι δὲ ὡς οὐ ταὐτὸ τὸ τί ἦν εἶναι καὶ αὐτό· τῷ μὲν γὰρ ἀνθρώπῳ καὶ τῷ λευκῷ ἀνθρώπῳ οὐ ταὐτό, τῷ πάθει δὲ ταὐτό). ἄτοπον δʼ ἂν φανείη κἂν εἴ τις ἑκάστῳ ὄνομα θεῖτο τῶν τί ἦν εἶναι· ἔσται γὰρ καὶ παρʼ ἐκεῖνο ἄλλο, οἷον τῷ τί ἦν εἶναι ἵππῳ τί ἦν εἶναι  ἕτερον. καίτοι τί κωλύει καὶ νῦν εἶναι ἔνια εὐθὺς τί ἦν εἶναι, εἴπερ οὐσία τὸ τί ἦν εἶναι; ἀλλὰ μὴν οὐ μόνον ἕν, ἀλλὰ καὶ ὁ λόγος ὁ αὐτὸς αὐτῶν, ὡς δῆλον καὶ ἐκ τῶν εἰρημένων·  οὐ γὰρ κατὰ συμβεβηκὸς ἓν τὸ ἑνὶ εἶναι καὶ ἕν. ἔτι εἰ ἄλλο ἔσται, εἰς ἄπειρον εἶσιν· τὸ μὲν γὰρ ἔσται τί ἦν εἶναι τοῦ ἑνὸς τὸ δὲ τὸ ἕν, ὥστε καὶ ἐπʼ ἐκείνων ὁ αὐτὸς ἔσται λόγος. ὅτι μὲν οὖν ἐπὶ τῶν πρώτων καὶ καθʼ αὑτὰ λεγομένων τὸ ἑκάστῳ εἶναι καὶ ἕκαστον τὸ αὐτὸ καὶ ἕν ἐστι, δῆλον· οἱ δὲ σοφιστικοὶ ἔλεγχοι πρὸς τὴν θέσιν ταύτην φανερὸν ὅτι τῇ αὐτῇ λύονται λύσει καὶ εἰ ταὐτὸ Σωκράτης καὶ Σωκράτει εἶναι· οὐδὲν γὰρ διαφέρει οὔτε ἐξ ὧν ἐρωτήσειεν ἄν τις οὔτε ἐξ ὧν λύων ἐπιτύχοι. πῶς μὲν οὖν τὸ τί ἦν εἶναι ταὐτὸν καὶ πῶς οὐ ταὐτὸν ἑκάστῳ, εἴρηται.\n\n\n\n \n\n \n\nτῶν δὲ γιγνομένων τὰ μὲν φύσει γίγνεται τὰ δὲ τέχνῃ τὰ δὲ ἀπὸ ταὐτομάτου, πάντα δὲ τὰ γιγνόμενα ὑπό τέ τινος γίγνεται καὶ ἔκ τινος καὶ τί· τὸ δὲ τὶ λέγω καθʼ ἑκάστην κατηγορίαν· ἢ γὰρ τόδε ἢ ποσὸν ἢ ποιὸν ἢ πού. αἱ δὲ γενέσεις αἱ μὲν φυσικαὶ αὗταί εἰσιν ὧν ἡ γένεσις ἐκ φύσεώς ἐστιν, τὸ δʼ ἐξ οὗ γίγνεται, ἣν λέγομεν ὕλην, τὸ δὲ ὑφʼ οὗ τῶν φύσει τι ὄντων, τὸ δὲ τὶ ἄνθρωπος ἢ φυτὸν ἢ ἄλλο τι τῶν τοιούτων, ἃ δὴ μάλιστα λέγομεν οὐσίας εἶναι —ἅπαντα δὲ τὰ γιγνόμενα ἢ φύσει ἢ τέχνῃ ἔχει ὕλην· δυνατὸν γὰρ καὶ εἶναι καὶ μὴ εἶναι ἕκαστον αὐτῶν, τοῦτο δʼ ἐστὶν ἡ ἐν ἑκάστῳ ὕλη—καθόλου δὲ καὶ ἐξ οὗ φύσις καὶ καθʼ ὃ φύσις (τὸ γὰρ γιγνόμενον ἔχει φύσιν, οἷον φυτὸν ἢ ζῷον) καὶ ὑφʼ οὗ ἡ κατὰ τὸ εἶδος λεγομένη φύσις ἡ ὁμοειδής (ἁὕτη δὲ ἐν ἄλλῳ)· ἄνθρωπος γὰρ ἄνθρωπον γεννᾷ·—οὕτω μὲν οὖν γίγνεται τὰ γιγνόμενα διὰ τὴν φύσιν, αἱ δʼ ἄλλαι γενέσεις λέγονται ποιήσεις. πᾶσαι δὲ εἰσὶν αἱ ποιήσεις ἢ ἀπὸ τέχνης ἢ ἀπὸ δυνάμεως ἢ ἀπὸ διανοίας. τούτων δέ τινες γίγνονται καὶ ἀπὸ ταὐτομάτου καὶ ἀπὸ τύχης παραπλησίως ὥσπερ ἐν τοῖς ἀπὸ φύσεως γιγνομένοις· ἔνια γὰρ κἀκεῖ ταὐτὰ καὶ ἐκ σπέρματος γίγνεται καὶ ἄνευ σπέρματος. περὶ μὲν οὖν τούτων ὕστερον ἐπισκεπτέον,  ἀπὸ τέχνης δὲ γίγνεται ὅσων τὸ εἶδος ἐν τῇ ψυχῇ (εἶδος δὲ λέγω τὸ τί ἦν εἶναι ἑκάστου καὶ τὴν πρώτην οὐσίαν)· καὶ γὰρ τῶν ἐναντίων τρόπον τινὰ τὸ αὐτὸ εἶδος· τῆς γὰρ στερήσεως οὐσία ἡ οὐσία ἡ ἀντικειμένη, οἷον ὑγίεια νόσου, ἐκείνης γὰρ ἀπουσία ἡ νόσος, ἡ δὲ ὑγίεια ὁ ἐν τῇ ψυχῇ λόγος καὶ ἡ ἐπιστήμη. γίγνεται δὲ τὸ ὑγιὲς νοήσαντος οὕτως· ἐπειδὴ τοδὶ ὑγίεια, ἀνάγκη εἰ ὑγιὲς ἔσται τοδὶ ὑπάρξαι, οἷον ὁμαλότητα, εἰ δὲ τοῦτο, θερμότητα· καὶ οὕτως ἀεὶ νοεῖ, ἕως ἂν ἀγάγῃ εἰς τοῦτο ὃ αὐτὸς δύναται ἔσχατον ποιεῖν. εἶτα ἤδη ἡ ἀπὸ τούτου κίνησις ποίησις καλεῖται, ἡ ἐπὶ τὸ ὑγιαίνειν. ὥστε συμβαίνει τρόπον τινὰ τὴν ὑγίειαν ἐξ ὑγιείας γίγνεσθαι καὶ τὴν οἰκίαν ἐξ οἰκίας, τῆς ἄνευ ὕλης τὴν ἔχουσαν ὕλην· ἡ γὰρ ἰατρική ἐστι καὶ ἡ οἰκοδομικὴ τὸ εἶδος τῆς ὑγιείας καὶ τῆς οἰκίας, λέγω δὲ οὐσίαν ἄνευ ὕλης τὸ τί ἦν εἶναι. \n\n \n\nτῶν δὴ γενέσεων καὶ κινήσεων ἡ μὲν νόησις καλεῖται ἡ δὲ ποίησις, ἡ μὲν ἀπὸ τῆς ἀρχῆς καὶ τοῦ εἴδους νόησις ἡ δʼ ἀπὸ τοῦ τελευταίου τῆς νοήσεως ποίησις. ὁμοίως δὲ καὶ τῶν ἄλλων τῶν μεταξὺ ἕκαστον γίγνεται. λέγω δʼ οἷον εἰ ὑγιανεῖ, δέοι ἂν ὁμαλυνθῆναι. τί οὖν ἐστὶ τὸ ὁμαλυνθῆναι; τοδί, τοῦτο δʼ ἔσται εἰ θερμανθήσεται. τοῦτο δὲ τί ἐστι; τοδί. ὑπάρχει δὲ τοδὶ δυνάμει· τοῦτο δὲ ἤδη ἐπʼ αὐτῷ. τὸ δὴ ποιοῦν καὶ ὅθεν ἄρχεται ἡ κίνησις τοῦ ὑγιαίνειν, ἂν μὲν ἀπὸ τέχνης, τὸ εἶδός ἐστι τὸ ἐν τῇ ψυχῇ, ἐὰν δʼ ἀπὸ ταὐτομάτου, ἀπὸ τούτου ὅ ποτε τοῦ ποιεῖν ἄρχει τῷ ποιοῦντι ἀπὸ τέχνης, ὥσπερ καὶ ἐν τῷ ἰατρεύειν ἴσως ἀπὸ τοῦ θερμαίνειν ἡ ἀρχή (τοῦτο δὲ ποιεῖ τῇ τρίψει)· ἡ θερμότης τοίνυν ἡ ἐν τῷ σώματι ἢ μέρος τῆς ὑγιείας ἢ ἕπεταί τι αὐτῇ τοιοῦτον ὅ ἐστι μέρος τῆς ὑγιείας, ἢ διὰ πλειόνων· τοῦτο δʼ ἔσχατόν ἐστι, τὸ ποιοῦν τὸ μέρος τῆς ὑγιείας,—καὶ τῆς οἰκίας (ὁἷον οἱ λίθοι) καὶ τῶν ἄλλων· ὥστε, καθάπερ λέγεται, ἀδύνατον γενέσθαι εἰ μηδὲν προϋπάρχοι. ὅτι μὲν οὖν τι μέρος ἐξ ἀνάγκης ὑπάρξει φανερόν· ἡ γὰρ ὕλη μέρος (ἐνυπάρχει γὰρ καὶ γίγνεται αὕτη).  ἀλλʼ ἆρα καὶ τῶν ἐν τῷ λόγῳ; ἀμφοτέρως δὴ λέγομεν τοὺς χαλκοῦς κύκλους τί εἰσι, καὶ τὴν ὕλην λέγοντες ὅτι χαλκός, καὶ τὸ εἶδος ὅτι σχῆμα τοιόνδε, καὶ τοῦτό ἐστι τὸ γένος εἰς ὃ πρῶτον τίθεται. ὁ δὴ χαλκοῦς κύκλος ἔχει ἐν τῷ λόγῳ τὴν ὕλην.—ἐξ οὗ δὲ ὡς ὕλης γίγνεται ἔνια λέγεται, ὅταν γένηται, οὐκ ἐκεῖνο ἀλλʼ ἐκείνινον, οἷον ὁ ἀνδριὰς οὐ λίθος ἀλλὰ λίθινος, ὁ δὲ ἄνθρωπος ὁ ὑγιαίνων οὐ λέγεται ἐκεῖνο ἐξ οὗ· αἴτιον δὲ ὅτι γίγνεται ἐκ τῆς στερήσεως καὶ τοῦ ὑποκειμένου, ὃ λέγομεν τὴν ὕλην (οἷον καὶ ὁ ἄνθρωπος καὶ ὁ κάμνων γίγνεται ὑγιής), μᾶλλον μέντοι λέγεται γίγνεσθαι ἐκ τῆς στερήσεως, οἷον ἐκ κάμνοντος ὑγιὴς ἢ ἐξ ἀνθρώπου, διὸ κάμνων μὲν ὁ ὑγιὴς οὐ λέγεται, ἄνθρωπος δέ, καὶ ὁ ἄνθρωπος ὑγιής· ὧν δʼ ἡ στέρησις ἄδηλος καὶ ἀνώνυμος, οἷον ἐν χαλκῷ σχήματος ὁποιουοῦν ἢ ἐν πλίνθοις καὶ ξύλοις οἰκίας, ἐκ τούτων δοκεῖ γίγνεσθαι ὡς ἐκεῖ ἐκ κάμνοντος· διὸ ὥσπερ οὐδʼ ἐκεῖ ἐξ οὗ τοῦτο, ἐκεῖνο οὐ λέγεται, οὐδʼ ἐνταῦθα ὁ ἀνδριὰς ξύλον, ἀλλὰ παράγεται ξύλινος,  καὶ χαλκοῦς ἀλλʼ οὐ χαλκός, καὶ λίθινος ἀλλʼ οὐ λίθος, καὶ ἡ οἰκία πλινθίνη ἀλλʼ οὐ πλίνθοι, ἐπεὶ οὐδὲ ὡς ἐκ ξύλου γίγνεται ἀνδριὰς ἢ ἐκ πλίνθων οἰκία, ἐάν τις ἐπιβλέπῃ σφόδρα, οὐκ ἂν ἁπλῶς εἴπειεν, διὰ τὸ δεῖν μεταβάλλοντος γίγνεσθαι ἐξ οὗ, ἀλλʼ οὐχ ὑπομένοντος. διὰ μὲν οὖν τοῦτο οὕτως λέγεται. \n\n\n\n \n\n \n\nἐπεὶ δὲ ὑπό τινός τε γίγνεται τὸ γιγνόμενον (τοῦτο δὲ λέγω ὅθεν ἡ ἀρχὴ τῆς γενέσεώς ἐστι) καὶ ἔκ τινος (ἔστω δὲ μὴ ἡ στέρησις τοῦτο ἀλλʼ ἡ ὕλη· ἤδη γὰρ διώρισται ὃν τρόπον τοῦτο λέγομεν) καὶ τὶ γίγνεται (τοῦτο δʼ ἐστὶν ἢ σφαῖρα ἢ κύκλος ἢ ὅ τι ἔτυχε τῶν ἄλλων), ὥσπερ οὐδὲ τὸ ὑποκείμενον ποιεῖ, τὸν χαλκόν, οὕτως οὐδὲ τὴν σφαῖραν, εἰ μὴ κατὰ συμβεβηκὸς ὅτι ἡ χαλκῆ σφαῖρα σφαῖρά ἐστιν ἐκείνην δὲ ποιεῖ. τὸ γὰρ τόδε τι ποιεῖν ἐκ τοῦ ὅλως ὑποκειμένου τόδε τι ποιεῖν ἐστίν (λέγω δʼ ὅτι τὸν χαλκὸν στρογγύλον ποιεῖν ἐστὶν οὐ τὸ στρογγύλον ἢ τὴν σφαῖραν ποιεῖν ἀλλʼ ἕτερόν τι, οἷον τὸ εἶδος τοῦτο ἐν ἄλλῳ· εἰ γὰρ ποιεῖ, ἔκ τινος ἂν ποιοίη ἄλλου, τοῦτο γὰρ ὑπέκειτο·  οἷον ποιεῖ χαλκῆν σφαῖραν, τοῦτο δὲ οὕτως ὅτι ἐκ τουδί, ὅ ἐστι χαλκός, τοδὶ ποιεῖ, ὅ ἐστι σφαῖρα)· εἰ οὖν καὶ τοῦτο ποιεῖ αὐτό, δῆλον ὅτι ὡσαύτως ποιήσει, καὶ βαδιοῦνται αἱ γενέσεις εἰς ἄπειρον. φανερὸν ἄρα ὅτι οὐδὲ τὸ εἶδος, ἢ ὁτιδήποτε χρὴ καλεῖν τὴν ἐν τῷ αἰσθητῷ μορφήν, οὐ γίγνεται, οὐδʼ ἔστιν αὐτοῦ γένεσις, οὐδὲ τὸ τί ἦν εἶναι (τοῦτο γάρ ἐστιν ὃ ἐν ἄλλῳ γίγνεται ἢ ὑπὸ τέχνης ἢ ὑπὸ φύσεως ἢ δυνάμεως). τὸ δὲ χαλκῆν σφαῖραν εἶναι ποιεῖ· ποιεῖ γὰρ ἐκ χαλκοῦ καὶ σφαίρας· εἰς τοδὶ γὰρ τὸ εἶδος ποιεῖ, καὶ ἔστι τοῦτο σφαῖρα χαλκῆ. τοῦ δὲ σφαίρᾳ εἶναι ὅλως εἰ ἔσται γένεσις, ἔκ τινος τὶ ἔσται. δεήσει γὰρ διαιρετὸν εἶναι ἀεὶ τὸ γιγνόμενον, καὶ εἶναι τὸ μὲν τόδε τὸ δὲ τόδε, λέγω δʼ ὅτι τὸ μὲν ὕλην τὸ δὲ εἶδος. εἰ δή ἐστι σφαῖρα τὸ ἐκ τοῦ μέσου σχῆμα ἴσον, τούτου τὸ μὲν ἐν ᾧ ἔσται ὃ ποιεῖ, τὸ δʼ ἐν ἐκείνῳ, τὸ δὲ ἅπαν τὸ γεγονός, οἷον ἡ χαλκῆ σφαῖρα. φανερὸν δὴ ἐκ τῶν εἰρημένων ὅτι τὸ μὲν ὡς εἶδος ἢ οὐσία λεγόμενον οὐ γίγνεται, ἡ δὲ σύνολος ἡ κατὰ ταύτην λεγομένη γίγνεται, καὶ ὅτι ἐν παντὶ τῷ γεννωμένῳ ὕλη ἔνεστι, καὶ ἔστι τὸ μὲν τόδε τὸ δὲ τόδε.—πότερον οὖν ἔστι τις σφαῖρα παρὰ τάσδε ἢ οἰκία παρὰ τὰς πλίνθους; ἢ οὐδʼ ἄν ποτε ἐγίγνετο, εἰ οὕτως ἦν, τόδε τι, ἀλλὰ τὸ τοιόνδε σημαίνει, τόδε δὲ καὶ ὡρισμένον οὐκ ἔστιν, ἀλλὰ ποιεῖ καὶ γεννᾷ ἐκ τοῦδε τοιόνδε, καὶ ὅταν γεννηθῇ, ἔστι τόδε τοιόνδε; τὸ δὲ ἅπαν τόδε, Καλλίας ἢ Σωκράτης, ἐστὶν ὥσπερ ἡ σφαῖρα ἡ χαλκῆ ἡδί, ὁ δʼ ἄνθρωπος καὶ τὸ ζῷον ὥσπερ σφαῖρα χαλκῆ ὅλως. φανερὸν ἄρα ὅτι ἡ τῶν εἰδῶν αἰτία, ὡς εἰώθασί τινες λέγειν τὰ εἴδη, εἰ ἔστιν ἄττα παρὰ τὰ καθʼ ἕκαστα, πρός γε τὰς γενέσεις καὶ τὰς οὐσίας οὐθὲν χρησίμη· οὐδʼ ἂν εἶεν διά γε ταῦτα οὐσίαι καθʼ αὑτάς. ἐπὶ μὲν δή τινων καὶ φανερὸν ὅτι τὸ γεννῶν τοιοῦτον μὲν οἷον τὸ γεννώμενον, οὐ μέντοι τὸ αὐτό γε, οὐδὲ ἓν τῷ ἀριθμῷ ἀλλὰ τῷ εἴδει, οἷον ἐν τοῖς φυσικοῖς—ἄνθρωπος γὰρ ἄνθρωπον γεννᾷ—ἂν μή τι παρὰ φύσιν γένηται, οἷον ἵππος ἡμίονον (καὶ ταῦτα δὲ ὁμοίως· ὃ γὰρ ἂν κοινὸν εἴη ἐφʼ ἵππου καὶ ὄνου οὐκ ὠνόμασται, τὸ ἐγγύτατα γένος, εἴη δʼ ἂν ἄμφω ἴσως, οἷον ἡμίονος)·  ὥστε φανερὸν ὅτι οὐθὲν δεῖ ὡς παράδειγμα εἶδος κατασκευάζειν (μάλιστα γὰρ ἂν ἐν τούτοις ἐπεζητοῦντο· οὐσίαι γὰρ αἱ μάλιστα αὗται) ἀλλὰ ἱκανὸν τὸ γεννῶν ποιῆσαι καὶ τοῦ εἴδους αἴτιον εἶναι ἐν τῇ ὕλῃ. τὸ δʼ ἅπαν ἤδη, τὸ τοιόνδε εἶδος ἐν ταῖσδε ταῖς σαρξὶ καὶ ὀστοῖς, Καλλίας καὶ Σωκράτης· καὶ ἕτερον μὲν διὰ τὴν ὕλην (ἑτέρα γάρ), ταὐτὸ δὲ τῷ εἴδει (ἄτομον γὰρ τὸ εἶδος). \n\n\n\n \n\n \n\nἀπορήσειε δʼ ἄν τις διὰ τί τὰ μὲν γίγνεται καὶ τέχνῃ καὶ ἀπὸ ταὐτομάτου, οἷον ὑγίεια, τὰ δʼ οὔ, οἷον οἰκία. αἴτιον δὲ ὅτι τῶν μὲν ἡ ὕλη ἡ ἄρχουσα τῆς γενέσεως ἐν τῷ ποιεῖν καὶ γίγνεσθαί τι τῶν ἀπὸ τέχνης, ἐν ᾗ ὑπάρχει τι μέρος τοῦ πράγματος,—ἡ μὲν τοιαύτη ἐστὶν οἵα κινεῖσθαι ὑφʼ αὑτῆς ἡ δʼ οὔ, καὶ ταύτης ἡ μὲν ὡδὶ οἵα τε ἡ δὲ ἀδύνατος· πολλὰ γὰρ δυνατὰ μὲν ὑφʼ αὑτῶν κινεῖσθαι ἀλλʼ οὐχ ὡδί, οἷον ὀρχήσασθαι. ὅσων οὖν τοιαύτη ἡ ὕλη, οἷον οἱ λίθοι, ἀδύνατον ὡδὶ κινηθῆναι εἰ μὴ ὑπʼ ἄλλου, ὡδὶ μέντοι ναί—καὶ τὸ πῦρ. διὰ τοῦτο τὰ μὲν οὐκ ἔσται ἄνευ τοῦ ἔχοντος τὴν τέχνην τὰ δὲ ἔσται· ὑπὸ γὰρ τούτων κινηθήσεται τῶν οὐκ ἐχόντων τὴν τέχνην, κινεῖσθαι δὲ δυναμένων αὐτῶν ὑπʼ ἄλλων οὐκ ἐχόντων τὴν τέχνην ἢ ἐκ μέρους. δῆλον δʼ ἐκ τῶν εἰρημένων καὶ ὅτι τρόπον τινὰ πάντα γίγνεται ἐξ ὁμωνύμου, ὥσπερ τὰ φύσει, ἢ ἐκ μέρους ὁμωνύμου (οἷον ἡ οἰκία ἐξ οἰκίας, ᾗ ὑπὸ νοῦ· ἡ γὰρ τέχνη τὸ εἶδος)  ἢ ἔχοντός τι μέρος,—ἐὰν μὴ κατὰ συμβεβηκὸς γίγνηται· τὸ γὰρ αἴτιον τοῦ ποιεῖν πρῶτον καθʼ αὑτὸ μέρος. θερμότης γὰρ ἡ ἐν τῇ κινήσει θερμότητα ἐν τῷ σώματι ἐποίησεν· αὕτη δὲ ἐστὶν ἢ ὑγίεια ἢ μέρος, ἢ ἀκολουθεῖ αὐτῇ μέρος τι τῆς ὑγιείας ἢ αὐτὴ ἡ ὑγίεια· διὸ καὶ λέγεται ποιεῖν, ὅτι ἐκεῖνο ποιεῖ  ᾧ ἀκολουθεῖ καὶ συμβέβηκε . ὥστε, ὥσπερ ἐν τοῖς συλλογισμοῖς, πάντων ἀρχὴ ἡ οὐσία· ἐκ γὰρ τοῦ τί ἐστιν οἱ συλλογισμοί εἰσιν, ἐνταῦθα δὲ αἱ γενέσεις. ὁμοίως δὲ καὶ τὰ φύσει συνιστάμενα τούτοις ἔχει. τὸ μὲν γὰρ σπέρμα ποιεῖ ὥσπερ τὰ ἀπὸ τέχνης (ἔχει γὰρ δυνάμει τὸ εἶδος,  καὶ ἀφʼ οὗ τὸ σπέρμα, ἐστί πως ὁμώνυμον—οὐ γὰρ πάντα οὕτω δεῖ ζητεῖν ὡς ἐξ ἀνθρώπου ἄνθρωπος· καὶ γὰρ γυνὴ ἐξ ἀνδρός—ἐὰν μὴ πήρωμα ᾖ· διὸ ἡμίονος οὐκ ἐξ ἡμιόνου)· ὅσα δὲ ἀπὸ ταὐτομάτου ὥσπερ ἐκεῖ γίγνεται, ὅσων ἡ ὕλη δύναται καὶ ὑφʼ αὑτῆς κινεῖσθαι ταύτην τὴν κίνησιν ἣν τὸ σπέρμα κινεῖ· ὅσων δὲ μή, ταῦτα ἀδύνατα γίγνεσθαι ἄλλως πως ἢ ἐξ αὐτῶν.—οὐ μόνον δὲ περὶ τῆς οὐσίας ὁ λόγος δηλοῖ τὸ μὴ γίγνεσθαι τὸ εἶδος, ἀλλὰ περὶ πάντων ὁμοίως τῶν πρώτων κοινὸς ὁ λόγος, οἷον ποσοῦ ποιοῦ καὶ τῶν ἄλλων κατηγοριῶν. γίγνεται γὰρ ὥσπερ ἡ χαλκῆ σφαῖρα ἀλλʼ οὐ σφαῖρα οὐδὲ χαλκός, καὶ ἐπὶ χαλκοῦ, εἰ γίγνεται (ἀεὶ γὰρ δεῖ προϋπάρχειν τὴν ὕλην καὶ τὸ εἶδος), οὕτως καὶ ἐπὶ τοῦ τί ἐστι καὶ ἐπὶ τοῦ ποιοῦ καὶ ποσοῦ καὶ τῶν ἄλλων ὁμοίως κατηγοριῶν· οὐ γὰρ γίγνεται τὸ ποιὸν ἀλλὰ τὸ ποιὸν ξύλον, οὐδὲ τὸ ποσὸν ἀλλὰ τὸ ποσὸν ξύλον ἢ ζῷον. ἀλλʼ ἴδιον τῆς οὐσίας ἐκ τούτων λαβεῖν ἔστιν ὅτι ἀναγκαῖον προϋπάρχειν ἑτέραν οὐσίαν ἐντελεχείᾳ οὖσαν ἣ ποιεῖ, οἷον ζῷον εἰ γίγνεται ζῷον· ποιὸν δʼ ἢ ποσὸν οὐκ ἀνάγκη ἀλλʼ ἢ δυνάμει μόνον. \n\n\n\n \n\n \n\nἐπεὶ δὲ ὁ ὁρισμὸς λόγος ἐστί, πᾶς δὲ λόγος μέρη ἔχει, ὡς δὲ ὁ λόγος πρὸς τὸ πρᾶγμα, καὶ τὸ μέρος τοῦ λόγου πρὸς τὸ μέρος τοῦ πράγματος ὁμοίως ἔχει, ἀπορεῖται ἤδη πότερον δεῖ τὸν τῶν μερῶν λόγον ἐνυπάρχειν ἐν τῷ τοῦ ὅλου λόγῳ ἢ οὔ. ἐπʼ ἐνίων μὲν γὰρ φαίνονται ἐνόντες ἐνίων δʼ οὔ. τοῦ μὲν γὰρ κύκλου ὁ λόγος οὐκ ἔχει τὸν τῶν τμημάτων, ὁ δὲ τῆς συλλαβῆς ἔχει τὸν τῶν στοιχείων· καίτοι διαιρεῖται καὶ ὁ κύκλος εἰς τὰ τμήματα ὥσπερ καὶ ἡ συλλαβὴ εἰς τὰ στοιχεῖα. ἔτι δὲ εἰ πρότερα τὰ μέρη τοῦ ὅλου, τῆς δὲ ὀρθῆς ἡ ὀξεῖα μέρος καὶ ὁ δάκτυλος τοῦ ζῴου, πρότερον ἂν εἴη ἡ ὀξεῖα τῆς ὀρθῆς καὶ ὁ δάκτυλος τοῦ ἀνθρώπου. δοκεῖ δʼ ἐκεῖνα εἶναι πρότερα· τῷ λόγῳ γὰρ λέγονται ἐξ ἐκείνων, καὶ τῷ εἶναι δὲ ἄνευ ἀλλήλων πρότερα.—ἢ πολλαχῶς λέγεται τὸ μέρος, ὧν εἷς μὲν τρόπος τὸ μετροῦν κατὰ τὸ ποσόν—ἀλλὰ τοῦτο μὲν ἀφείσθω· ἐξ ὧν δὲ ἡ οὐσία ὡς μερῶν, τοῦτο σκεπτέον.  εἰ οὖν ἐστὶ τὸ μὲν ὕλη τὸ δὲ εἶδος τὸ δʼ ἐκ τούτων, καὶ οὐσία ἥ τε ὕλη καὶ τὸ εἶδος καὶ τὸ ἐκ τούτων, ἔστι μὲν ὡς καὶ ἡ ὕλη μέρος τινὸς λέγεται, ἔστι δʼ ὡς οὔ, ἀλλʼ ἐξ ὧν ὁ τοῦ εἴδους λόγος. οἷον τῆς μὲν κοιλότητος οὐκ ἔστι μέρος ἡ σάρξ (αὕτη γὰρ ἡ ὕλη ἐφʼ ἧς γίγνεται), τῆς δὲ σιμότητος μέρος· καὶ τοῦ μὲν συνόλου ἀνδριάντος μέρος ὁ χαλκὸς τοῦ δʼ ὡς εἴδους λεγομένου ἀνδριάντος οὔ (λεκτέον γὰρ τὸ εἶδος καὶ ᾗ εἶδος ἔχει ἕκαστον, τὸ δʼ ὑλικὸν οὐδέποτε καθʼ αὑτὸ λεκτέον)· διὸ ὁ μὲν τοῦ κύκλου λόγος οὐκ ἔχει τὸν τῶν τμημάτων, ὁ δὲ τῆς συλλαβῆς ἔχει τὸν τῶν στοιχείων· τὰ μὲν γὰρ στοιχεῖα τοῦ λόγου μέρη τοῦ εἴδους καὶ οὐχ ὕλη, τὰ δὲ τμήματα οὕτως μέρη ὡς ὕλη ἐφʼ ἧς ἐπιγίγνεται· ἐγγυτέρω μέντοι τοῦ εἴδους ἢ ὁ χαλκὸς ὅταν ἐν χαλκῷ ἡ στρογγυλότης ἐγγένηται. ἔστι δʼ ὡς οὐδὲ τὰ στοιχεῖα πάντα τῆς συλλαβῆς ἐν τῷ λόγῳ ἐνέσται, οἷον ταδὶ τὰ κήρινα ἢ τὰ ἐν τῷ ἀέρι· ἤδη γὰρ καὶ ταῦτα μέρος τῆς συλλαβῆς ὡς ὕλη αἰσθητή. καὶ γὰρ ἡ γραμμὴ οὐκ εἰ διαιρουμένη εἰς τὰ ἡμίση φθείρεται, ἢ ὁ ἄνθρωπος εἰς τὰ ὀστᾶ καὶ νεῦρα καὶ σάρκας, διὰ τοῦτο καὶ εἰσὶν ἐκ τούτων οὕτως ὡς ὄντων τῆς οὐσίας μερῶν, ἀλλʼ ὡς ἐξ ὕλης, καὶ τοῦ μὲν συνόλου μέρη, τοῦ εἴδους δὲ καὶ οὗ ὁ λόγος οὐκέτι· διόπερ οὐδʼ ἐν τοῖς λόγοις. τῷ μὲν οὖν ἐνέσται ὁ τῶν τοιούτων μερῶν λόγος, τῷ δʼ οὐ δεῖ ἐνεῖναι, ἂν μὴ ᾖ τοῦ συνειλημμένου· διὰ γὰρ τοῦτο ἔνια μὲν ἐκ τούτων ὡς ἀρχῶν ἐστὶν εἰς ἃ φθείρονται, ἔνια δὲ οὐκ ἔστιν. ὅσα μὲν οὖν συνειλημμένα τὸ εἶδος καὶ ἡ ὕλη ἐστίν, οἷον τὸ σιμὸν ἢ ὁ χαλκοῦς κύκλος, ταῦτα μὲν φθείρεται εἰς ταῦτα καὶ μέρος αὐτῶν ἡ ὕλη· ὅσα δὲ μὴ συνείληπται τῇ ὕλῃ ἀλλὰ ἄνευ ὕλης, ὧν οἱ λόγοι τοῦ εἴδους μόνον, ταῦτα δʼ οὐ φθείρεται, ἢ ὅλως ἢ οὔτοι οὕτω γε· ὥστʼ ἐκείνων μὲν ἀρχαὶ καὶ μέρη ταῦτα τοῦ δὲ εἴδους οὔτε μέρη οὔτε ἀρχαί. καὶ διὰ τοῦτο φθείρεται ὁ πήλινος ἀνδριὰς εἰς πηλὸν καὶ ἡ σφαῖρα εἰς χαλκὸν καὶ ὁ Καλλίας εἰς σάρκα καὶ ὀστᾶ, ἔτι δὲ ὁ κύκλος εἰς τὰ τμήματα· ἔστι γάρ τις ὃς συνείληπται τῇ ὕλῃ·  ὁμωνύμως γὰρ λέγεται κύκλος ὅ τε ἁπλῶς λεγόμενος καὶ ὁ καθʼ ἕκαστα διὰ τὸ μὴ εἶναι ἴδιον ὄνομα τοῖς καθʼ ἕκαστον.—εἴρηται μὲν οὖν καὶ νῦν τὸ ἀληθές, ὅμως δʼ ἔτι σαφέστερον εἴπωμεν ἐπαναλαβόντες. ὅσα μὲν γὰρ τοῦ λόγου μέρη καὶ εἰς ἃ διαιρεῖται ὁ λόγος, ταῦτα πρότερα ἢ πάντα ἢ ἔνια· ὁ δὲ τῆς ὀρθῆς λόγος οὐ διαιρεῖται εἰς ὀξείας λόγον, ἀλλʼ ὁ τῆς ὀξείας εἰς ὀρθήν· χρῆται γὰρ ὁ ὁριζόμενος τὴν ὀξεῖαν τῇ ὀρθῇ· ἐλάττων γὰρ ὀρθῆς ἡ ὀξεῖα. ὁμοίως δὲ καὶ ὁ κύκλος καὶ τὸ ἡμικύκλιον ἔχουσιν· τὸ γὰρ ἡμικύκλιον τῷ κύκλῳ ὁρίζεται καὶ ὁ δάκτυλος τῷ ὅλῳ· τὸ γὰρ τοιόνδε μέρος ἀνθρώπου δάκτυλος. ὥσθʼ ὅσα μὲν μέρη ὡς ὕλη καὶ εἰς ἃ διαιρεῖται ὡς ὕλην, ὕστερα· ὅσα δὲ ὡς τοῦ λόγου καὶ τῆς οὐσίας τῆς κατὰ τὸν λόγον, πρότερα ἢ πάντα ἢ ἔνια. ἐπεὶ δὲ ἡ τῶν ζῴων ψυχή (τοῦτο γὰρ οὐσία τοῦ ἐμψύχου) ἡ κατὰ τὸν λόγον οὐσία καὶ τὸ εἶδος καὶ τὸ τί ἦν εἶναι τῷ τοιῷδε σώματι (ἕκαστον γοῦν τὸ μέρος ἐὰν ὁρίζηται καλῶς, οὐκ ἄνευ τοῦ ἔργου ὁριεῖται, ὃ οὐχ ὑπάρξει ἄνευ αἰσθήσεως), ὥστε τὰ ταύτης μέρη πρότερα ἢ πάντα ἢ ἔνια τοῦ συνόλου ζῴου, καὶ καθʼ ἕκαστον δὴ ὁμοίως, τὸ δὲ σῶμα καὶ τὰ τούτου μόρια ὕστερα ταύτης τῆς οὐσίας, καὶ διαιρεῖται εἰς ταῦτα ὡς εἰς ὕλην οὐχ ἡ οὐσία ἀλλὰ τὸ σύνολον,—τοῦ μὲν οὖν συνόλου πρότερα ταῦτʼ ἔστιν ὥς, ἔστι δʼ ὡς οὔ (οὐδὲ γὰρ εἶναι δύναται χωριζόμενα· οὐ γὰρ ὁ πάντως ἔχων δάκτυλος ζῴου, ἀλλʼ ὁμώνυμος ὁ τεθνεώς)· ἔνια δὲ ἅμα, ὅσα κύρια καὶ ἐν ᾧ πρώτῳ ὁ λόγος καὶ ἡ οὐσία, οἷον εἰ τοῦτο καρδία ἢ ἐγκέφαλος· διαφέρει γὰρ οὐθὲν πότερον τοιοῦτον. ὁ δʼ ἄνθρωπος καὶ ὁ ἵππος καὶ τὰ οὕτως ἐπὶ τῶν καθʼ ἕκαστα, καθόλου δέ, οὐκ ἔστιν οὐσία ἀλλὰ σύνολόν τι ἐκ τουδὶ τοῦ λόγου καὶ τησδὶ τῆς ὕλης ὡς καθόλου· καθʼ ἕκαστον δʼ ἐκ τῆς ἐσχάτης ὕλης ὁ Σωκράτης ἤδη ἐστίν, καὶ ἐπὶ τῶν ἄλλων ὁμοίως.—μέρος μὲν οὖν ἐστὶ καὶ τοῦ εἴδους (εἶδος δὲ λέγω τὸ τί ἦν εἶναι) καὶ τοῦ συνόλου τοῦ ἐκ τοῦ εἴδους καὶ τῆς ὕλης καὶ τῆς ὕλης αὐτῆς. ἀλλὰ τοῦ λόγου μέρη τὰ τοῦ εἴδους μόνον ἐστίν, ὁ δὲ λόγος ἐστὶ τοῦ καθόλου·  τὸ γὰρ κύκλῳ εἶναι καὶ κύκλος καὶ ψυχῇ εἶναι καὶ ψυχὴ ταὐτό. τοῦ δὲ συνόλου ἤδη, οἷον κύκλου τουδὶ καὶ τῶν καθʼ ἕκαστά τινος ἢ αἰσθητοῦ ἢ νοητοῦ—λέγω δὲ νοητοὺς μὲν οἷον τοὺς μαθηματικούς, αἰσθητοὺς δὲ οἷον τοὺς χαλκοῦς καὶ τοὺς ξυλίνους—τούτων δὲ οὐκ ἔστιν ὁρισμός, ἀλλὰ μετὰ νοήσεως ἢ αἰσθήσεως γνωρίζονται, ἀπελθόντες δὲ ἐκ τῆς ἐντελεχείας οὐ δῆλον πότερον εἰσὶν ἢ οὐκ εἰσίν· ἀλλʼ ἀεὶ λέγονται καὶ γνωρίζονται τῷ καθόλου λόγῳ. ἡ δʼ ὕλη ἄγνωστος καθʼ αὑτήν. ὕλη δὲ ἡ μὲν αἰσθητή ἐστιν ἡ δὲ νοητή, αἰσθητὴ μὲν οἷον χαλκὸς καὶ ξύλον καὶ ὅση κινητὴ ὕλη, νοητὴ δὲ ἡ ἐν τοῖς αἰσθητοῖς ὑπάρχουσα μὴ ᾗ αἰσθητά, οἷον τὰ μαθηματικά. πῶς μὲν οὖν ἔχει περὶ ὅλου καὶ μέρους καὶ περὶ τοῦ προτέρου καὶ ὑστέρου, εἴρηται· πρὸς δὲ τὴν ἐρώτησιν ἀνάγκη ἀπαντᾶν, ὅταν τις ἔρηται πότερον ἡ ὀρθὴ καὶ ὁ κύκλος καὶ τὸ ζῷον πρότερον ἢ εἰς ἃ διαιροῦνται καὶ ἐξ ὧν εἰσί, τὰ μέρη, ὅτι οὐχ ἁπλῶς. εἰ μὲν γάρ ἐστι καὶ ἡ ψυχὴ ζῷον ἢ ἔμψυχον, ἢ ἕκαστον ἡ ἑκάστου, καὶ κύκλος τὸ κύκλῳ εἶναι, καὶ ὀρθὴ τὸ ὀρθῇ εἶναι καὶ ἡ οὐσία ἡ τῆς ὀρθῆς, τὶ μὲν καὶ τινὸς φατέον ὕστερον, οἷον τῶν ἐν τῷ λόγῳ καὶ τινὸς ὀρθῆς (καὶ γὰρ ἡ μετὰ τῆς ὕλης, ἡ χαλκῆ ὀρθή, καὶ ἡ ἐν ταῖς γραμμαῖς ταῖς καθʼ ἕκαστα), ἡ δʼ ἄνευ ὕλης τῶν μὲν ἐν τῷ λόγῳ ὑστέρα τῶν δʼ ἐν τῷ καθʼ ἕκαστα μορίων προτέρα, ἁπλῶς δʼ οὐ φατέον· εἰ δʼ ἑτέρα καὶ μὴ ἔστιν ἡ ψυχὴ ζῷον, καὶ οὕτω τὰ μὲν φατέον τὰ δʼ οὐ φατέον, ὥσπερ εἴρηται.\n\n\n\n \n\n \n\nἀπορεῖται δὲ εἰκότως καὶ ποῖα τοῦ εἴδους μέρη καὶ ποῖα οὔ, ἀλλὰ τοῦ συνειλημμένου. καίτοι τούτου μὴ δήλου ὄντος οὐκ ἔστιν ὁρίσασθαι ἕκαστον· τοῦ γὰρ καθόλου καὶ τοῦ εἴδους ὁ ὁρισμός· ποῖα οὖν ἐστὶ τῶν μερῶν ὡς ὕλη καὶ ποῖα οὔ, ἐὰν μὴ ᾖ φανερά, οὐδὲ ὁ λόγος ἔσται φανερὸς ὁ τοῦ πράγματος. ὅσα μὲν οὖν φαίνεται ἐπιγιγνόμενα ἐφʼ ἑτέρων τῷ εἴδει, οἷον κύκλος ἐν χαλκῷ καὶ λίθῳ καὶ ξύλῳ, ταῦτα μὲν δῆλα εἶναι δοκεῖ ὅτι οὐδὲν τῆς τοῦ κύκλου οὐσίας ὁ χαλκὸς οὐδʼ ὁ λίθος διὰ τὸ χωρίζεσθαι αὐτῶν· ὅσα δὲ μὴ ὁρᾶται χωριζόμενα, οὐδὲν μὲν κωλύει ὁμοίως ἔχειν τούτοις, ὥσπερ κἂν εἰ οἱ κύκλοι πάντες ἑωρῶντο χαλκοῖ·  οὐδὲν γὰρ ἂν ἧττον ἦν ὁ χαλκὸς οὐδὲν τοῦ εἴδους· χαλεπὸν δὲ ἀφελεῖν τοῦτον τῇ διανοίᾳ. οἷον τὸ τοῦ ἀνθρώπου εἶδος ἀεὶ ἐν σαρξὶ φαίνεται καὶ ὀστοῖς καὶ τοῖς τοιούτοις μέρεσιν· ἆρʼ οὖν καὶ ἐστὶ ταῦτα μέρη τοῦ εἴδους καὶ τοῦ λόγου; ἢ οὔ, ἀλλʼ ὕλη, ἀλλὰ διὰ τὸ μὴ καὶ ἐπʼ ἄλλων ἐπιγίγνεσθαι ἀδυνατοῦμεν χωρίσαι; ἐπεὶ δὲ τοῦτο δοκεῖ μὲν ἐνδέχεσθαι ἄδηλον δὲ πότε, ἀποροῦσί τινες ἤδη καὶ ἐπὶ τοῦ κύκλου καὶ τοῦ τριγώνου ὡς οὐ προσῆκον γραμμαῖς ὁρίζεσθαι καὶ τῷ συνεχεῖ, ἀλλὰ πάντα καὶ ταῦτα ὁμοίως λέγεσθαι ὡσανεὶ σάρκες καὶ ὀστᾶ τοῦ ἀνθρώπου καὶ χαλκὸς καὶ λίθος τοῦ ἀνδριάντος· καὶ ἀνάγουσι πάντα εἰς τοὺς ἀριθμούς, καὶ γραμμῆς τὸν λόγον τὸν τῶν δύο εἶναί φασιν. καὶ τῶν τὰς ἰδέας λεγόντων οἱ μὲν αὐτογραμμὴν τὴν δυάδα, οἱ δὲ τὸ εἶδος τῆς γραμμῆς, ἔνια μὲν γὰρ εἶναι τὸ αὐτὸ τὸ εἶδος καὶ οὗ τὸ εἶδος (οἷον δυάδα καὶ τὸ εἶδος δυάδος), ἐπὶ γραμμῆς δὲ οὐκέτι. συμβαίνει δὴ ἕν τε πολλῶν εἶδος εἶναι ὧν τὸ εἶδος φαίνεται ἕτερον (ὅπερ καὶ τοῖς Πυθαγορείοις συνέβαινεν), καὶ ἐνδέχεται ἓν πάντων ποιεῖν αὐτὸ εἶδος, τὰ δʼ ἄλλα μὴ εἴδη· καίτοι οὕτως ἓν πάντα ἔσται.\n\n \n\nὅτι μὲν οὖν ἔχει τινὰ ἀπορίαν τὰ περὶ τοὺς ὁρισμούς, καὶ διὰ τίνʼ αἰτίαν, εἴρηται· διὸ καὶ τὸ πάντα ἀνάγειν οὕτω καὶ ἀφαιρεῖν τὴν ὕλην περίεργον· ἔνια γὰρ ἴσως τόδʼ ἐν τῷδʼ ἐστὶν ἢ ὡδὶ ταδὶ ἔχοντα. καὶ ἡ παραβολὴ ἡ ἐπὶ τοῦ ζῴου, ἣν εἰώθει λέγειν Σωκράτης ὁ νεώτερος, οὐ καλῶς ἔχει· ἀπάγει γὰρ ἀπὸ τοῦ ἀληθοῦς, καὶ ποιεῖ ὑπολαμβάνειν ὡς ἐνδεχόμενον εἶναι τὸν ἄνθρωπον ἄνευ τῶν μερῶν, ὥσπερ ἄνευ τοῦ χαλκοῦ τὸν κύκλον. τὸ δʼ οὐχ ὅμοιον· αἰσθητὸν γάρ τι τὸ ζῷον, καὶ ἄνευ κινήσεως οὐκ ἔστιν ὁρίσασθαι, διὸ οὐδʼ ἄνευ τῶν μερῶν ἐχόντων πώς. οὐ γὰρ πάντως τοῦ ἀνθρώπου μέρος ἡ χείρ, ἀλλʼ ἢ δυναμένη τὸ ἔργον ἀποτελεῖν, ὥστε ἔμψυχος οὖσα· μὴ ἔμψυχος δὲ οὐ μέρος. περὶ δὲ τὰ μαθηματικὰ διὰ τί οὐκ εἰσὶ μέρη οἱ λόγοι τῶν λόγων, οἷον τοῦ κύκλου τὰ ἡμικύκλια; οὐ γάρ ἐστιν αἰσθητὰ ταῦτα. ἢ οὐθὲν διαφέρει; ἔσται γὰρ ὕλη ἐνίων καὶ μὴ αἰσθητῶν·  καὶ παντὸς γὰρ ὕλη τις ἔστιν ὃ μὴ ἔστι τί ἦν εἶναι καὶ εἶδος αὐτὸ καθʼ αὑτὸ ἀλλὰ τόδε τι. κύκλου μὲν οὖν οὐκ ἔσται τοῦ καθόλου, τῶν δὲ καθʼ ἕκαστα ἔσται μέρη ταῦτα, ὥσπερ εἴρηται πρότερον· ἔστι γὰρ ὕλη ἡ μὲν αἰσθητὴ ἡ δὲ νοητή. δῆλον δὲ καὶ ὅτι ἡ μὲν ψυχὴ οὐσία ἡ πρώτη, τὸ δὲ σῶμα ὕλη, ὁ δʼ ἄνθρωπος ἢ τὸ ζῷον τὸ ἐξ ἀμφοῖν ὡς καθόλου· Σωκράτης δὲ καὶ Κορίσκος, εἰ μὲν καὶ ἡ ψυχὴ Σωκράτης, διττόν (οἱ μὲν γὰρ ὡς ψυχὴν οἱ δʼ ὡς τὸ σύνολον), εἰ δʼ ἁπλῶς ἡ ψυχὴ ἥδε καὶ τὸ σῶμα τόδε, ὥσπερ τὸ καθόλου  καὶ τὸ καθʼ ἕκαστον. πότερον δὲ ἔστι παρὰ τὴν ὕλην τῶν τοιούτων οὐσιῶν τις ἄλλη, καὶ δεῖ ζητεῖν οὐσίαν ἑτέραν τινὰ οἷον ἀριθμοὺς ἤ τι τοιοῦτον, σκεπτέον ὕστερον. τούτου γὰρ χάριν καὶ περὶ τῶν αἰσθητῶν οὐσιῶν πειρώμεθα διορίζειν, ἐπεὶ τρόπον τινὰ τῆς φυσικῆς καὶ δευτέρας φιλοσοφίας ἔργον ἡ περὶ τὰς αἰσθητὰς οὐσίας θεωρία· οὐ γὰρ μόνον περὶ τῆς ὕλης δεῖ γνωρίζειν τὸν φυσικὸν ἀλλὰ καὶ τῆς κατὰ τὸν λόγον, καὶ μᾶλλον. ἐπὶ δὲ τῶν ὁρισμῶν πῶς μέρη τὰ ἐν τῷ λόγῳ, καὶ διὰ τί εἷς λόγος ὁ ὁρισμός (δῆλον γὰρ ὅτι τὸ πρᾶγμα ἕν, τὸ δὲ πρᾶγμα τίνι ἕν, μέρη γε ἔχον;), σκεπτέον ὕστερον.\n\n \n\nτί μὲν οὖν ἐστὶ τὸ τί ἦν εἶναι καὶ πῶς αὐτὸ καθʼ αὑτό, καθόλου περὶ παντὸς εἴρηται, καὶ διὰ τί τῶν μὲν ὁ λόγος ὁ τοῦ τί ἦν εἶναι ἔχει τὰ μόρια τοῦ ὁριζομένου τῶν δʼ οὔ, καὶ ὅτι ἐν μὲν τῷ τῆς οὐσίας λόγῳ τὰ οὕτω μόρια ὡς ὕλη οὐκ ἐνέσται—οὐδὲ γὰρ ἔστιν ἐκείνης μόρια τῆς οὐσίας ἀλλὰ τῆς συνόλου, ταύτης δέ γʼ ἔστι πως λόγος καὶ οὐκ ἔστιν· μετὰ μὲν γὰρ τῆς ὕλης οὐκ ἔστιν (ἀόριστον γάρ), κατὰ τὴν πρώτην δʼ οὐσίαν ἔστιν, οἷον ἀνθρώπου ὁ τῆς ψυχῆς λόγος· ἡ γὰρ οὐσία ἐστὶ τὸ εἶδος τὸ ἐνόν, ἐξ οὗ καὶ τῆς ὕλης ἡ σύνολος λέγεται οὐσία, οἷον ἡ κοιλότης (ἐκ γὰρ ταύτης καὶ τῆς ῥινὸς σιμὴ ῥὶς καὶ ἡ σιμότης ἐστί )—ἐν δὲ τῇ συνόλῳ οὐσίᾳ, οἷον ῥινὶ σιμῇ ἢ Καλλίᾳ, ἐνέσται καὶ ἡ ὕλη· καὶ ὅτι τὸ τί ἦν εἶναι καὶ ἕκαστον ἐπὶ τινῶν μὲν ταὐτό,  ὥσπερ ἐπὶ τῶν πρώτων οὐσιῶν, οἷον καμπυλότης καὶ καμπυλότητι εἶναι, εἰ πρώτη ἐστίν (λέγω δὲ πρώτην ἣ μὴ λέγεται τῷ ἄλλο ἐν ἄλλῳ εἶναι καὶ ὑποκειμένῳ ὡς ὕλῃ), ὅσα δὲ ὡς ὕλη ἢ ὡς συνειλημμένα τῇ ὕλῃ, οὐ ταὐτό, οὐδʼ εἰ κατὰ συμβεβηκὸς ἕν, οἷον Σωκράτης καὶ τὸ μουσικόν· ταῦτα γὰρ ταὐτὰ κατὰ συμβεβηκός. \n\n\n\n \n\n \n\nνῦν δὲ λέγωμεν πρῶτον ἐφʼ ὅσον ἐν τοῖς ἀναλυτικοῖς περὶ ὁρισμοῦ μὴ εἴρηται· ἡ γὰρ ἐν ἐκείνοις ἀπορία λεχθεῖσα πρὸ ἔργου τοῖς περὶ τῆς οὐσίας ἐστὶ λόγοις. λέγω δὲ ταύτην τὴν ἀπορίαν, διὰ τί ποτε ἕν ἐστιν οὗ τὸν λόγον ὁρισμὸν εἶναί φαμεν, οἷον τοῦ ἀνθρώπου τὸ ζῷον δίπουν· ἔστω γὰρ οὗτος αὐτοῦ λόγος. διὰ τί δὴ τοῦτο ἕν ἐστιν ἀλλʼ οὐ πολλά, ζῷον καὶ δίπουν· ἐπὶ μὲν γὰρ τοῦ ἄνθρωπος καὶ λευκὸν πολλὰ μέν ἐστιν ὅταν μὴ ὑπάρχῃ θατέρῳ θάτερον, ἓν δὲ ὅταν ὑπάρχῃ καὶ πάθῃ τι τὸ ὑποκείμενον, ὁ ἄνθρωπος (τότε γὰρ ἓν γίγνεται καὶ ἔστιν ὁ λευκὸς ἄνθρωπος)· ἐνταῦθα δʼ οὐ μετέχει θατέρου θάτερον· τὸ γὰρ γένος οὐ δοκεῖ μετέχειν τῶν διαφορῶν (ἅμα γὰρ ἂν τῶν ἐναντίων τὸ αὐτὸ μετεῖχεν· αἱ γὰρ διαφοραὶ ἐναντίαι αἷς διαφέρει τὸ γένος). εἰ δὲ καὶ μετέχει, ὁ αὐτὸς λόγος, εἴπερ εἰσὶν αἱ διαφοραὶ πλείους, οἷον πεζὸν δίπουν ἄπτερον. διὰ τί γὰρ ταῦθʼ ἓν ἀλλʼ οὐ πολλά; οὐ γὰρ ὅτι ἐνυπάρχει· οὕτω μὲν γὰρ ἐξ ἁπάντων ἔσται ἕν. δεῖ δέ γε ἓν εἶναι ὅσα ἐν τῷ ὁρισμῷ· ὁ γὰρ ὁρισμὸς λόγος τίς ἐστιν εἷς καὶ οὐσίας, ὥστε ἑνός τινος δεῖ αὐτὸν εἶναι λόγον· καὶ γὰρ ἡ οὐσία ἕν τι καὶ τόδε τι σημαίνει, ὡς φαμέν.—δεῖ δὲ ἐπισκοπεῖν πρῶτον περὶ τῶν κατὰ τὰς διαιρέσεις ὁρισμῶν. οὐδὲν γὰρ ἕτερόν ἐστιν ἐν τῷ ὁρισμῷ πλὴν τὸ πρῶτον λεγόμενον γένος καὶ αἱ διαφοραί· τὰ δʼ ἄλλα γένη ἐστὶ τό τε πρῶτον καὶ μετὰ τούτου αἱ συλλαμβανόμεναι διαφοραί, οἷον τὸ πρῶτον ζῷον, τὸ δὲ ἐχόμενον ζῷον δίπουν, καὶ πάλιν ζῷον δίπουν ἄπτερον· ὁμοίως δὲ κἂν διὰ πλειόνων λέγηται.  ὅλως δʼ οὐδὲν διαφέρει διὰ πολλῶν ἢ διʼ ὀλίγων λέγεσθαι, ὥστʼ οὐδὲ διʼ ὀλίγων ἢ διὰ δυοῖν· τοῖν δυοῖν δὲ τὸ μὲν διαφορὰ τὸ δὲ γένος, οἷον τοῦ ζῷον δίπουν τὸ μὲν ζῷον γένος διαφορὰ δὲ θάτερον. εἰ οὖν τὸ γένος ἁπλῶς μὴ ἔστι παρὰ τὰ ὡς γένους εἴδη, ἢ εἰ ἔστι μὲν ὡς ὕλη δʼ ἐστίν (ἡ μὲν γὰρ φωνὴ γένος καὶ ὕλη, αἱ δὲ διαφοραὶ τὰ εἴδη καὶ τὰ στοιχεῖα ἐκ ταύτης ποιοῦσιν), φανερὸν ὅτι ὁ ὁρισμός ἐστιν ὁ ἐκ τῶν διαφορῶν λόγος. ἀλλὰ μὴν καὶ δεῖ γε διαιρεῖσθαι τῇ τῆς διαφορᾶς διαφορᾷ, οἷον ζῴου διαφορὰ τὸ ὑπόπουν· πάλιν τοῦ ζῴου τοῦ ὑπόποδος τὴν διαφορὰν δεῖ εἶναι ᾗ ὑπόπουν, ὥστʼ οὐ λεκτέον τοῦ ὑπόποδος τὸ μὲν πτερωτὸν τὸ δὲ ἄπτερον, ἐάνπερ λέγῃ καλῶς (ἀλλὰ διὰ τὸ ἀδυνατεῖν ποιήσει τοῦτο), ἀλλʼ ἢ τὸ μὲν σχιζόπουν τὸ δʼ ἄσχιστον· αὗται γὰρ διαφοραὶ ποδός· ἡ γὰρ σχιζοποδία ποδότης τις. καὶ οὕτως ἀεὶ βούλεται βαδίζειν ἕως ἂν ἔλθῃ εἰς τὰ ἀδιάφορα· τότε δʼ ἔσονται τοσαῦτα εἴδη ποδὸς ὅσαιπερ αἱ διαφοραί, καὶ τὰ ὑπόποδα ζῷα ἴσα ταῖς διαφοραῖς. εἰ δὴ ταῦτα οὕτως ἔχει, φανερὸν ὅτι ἡ τελευταία διαφορὰ ἡ οὐσία τοῦ πράγματος ἔσται καὶ ὁ ὁρισμός, εἴπερ μὴ δεῖ πολλάκις ταὐτὰ λέγειν ἐν τοῖς ὅροις· περίεργον γάρ. συμβαίνει δέ γε τοῦτο· ὅταν γὰρ εἴπῃ ζῷον ὑπόπουν δίπουν, οὐδὲν ἄλλο εἴρηκεν ἢ ζῷον πόδας ἔχον, δύο πόδας ἔχον· κἂν τοῦτο διαιρῇ τῇ οἰκείᾳ διαιρέσει, πλεονάκις ἐρεῖ καὶ ἰσάκις ταῖς διαφοραῖς. ἐὰν μὲν δὴ διαφορᾶς διαφορὰ γίγνηται, μία ἔσται ἡ τελευταία τὸ εἶδος καὶ ἡ οὐσία· ἐὰν δὲ κατὰ συμβεβηκός, οἷον εἰ διαιροῖ τοῦ ὑπόποδος τὸ μὲν λευκὸν τὸ δὲ μέλαν, τοσαῦται ὅσαι ἂν αἱ τομαὶ ὦσιν. ὥστε φανερὸν ὅτι ὁ ὁρισμὸς λόγος ἐστὶν ὁ ἐκ τῶν διαφορῶν, καὶ τούτων τῆς τελευταίας κατά γε τὸ ὀρθόν. δῆλον δʼ ἂν εἴη, εἴ τις μετατάξειε τοὺς τοιούτους ὁρισμούς, οἷον τὸν τοῦ ἀνθρώπου, λέγων ζῷον δίπουν ὑπόπουν· περίεργον γὰρ τὸ ὑπόπουν εἰρημένου τοῦ δίποδος. τάξις δʼ οὐκ ἔστιν ἐν τῇ οὐσίᾳ· πῶς γὰρ δεῖ νοῆσαι τὸ μὲν ὕστερον τὸ δὲ πρότερον; περὶ μὲν οὖν τῶν κατὰ τὰς διαιρέσεις ὁρισμῶν τοσαῦτα εἰρήσθω τὴν πρώτην, ποῖοί τινές εἰσιν. \n\n\n\n \n\n \n\n ἐπεὶ δὲ περὶ τῆς οὐσίας ἡ σκέψις ἐστί, πάλιν ἐπανέλθωμεν. λέγεται δʼ ὥσπερ τὸ ὑποκείμενον οὐσία εἶναι καὶ τὸ τί ἦν εἶναι καὶ τὸ ἐκ τούτων, καὶ τὸ καθόλου. περὶ μὲν οὖν τοῖν δυοῖν εἴρηται (καὶ γὰρ περὶ τοῦ τί ἦν εἶναι καὶ τοῦ ὑποκειμένου, ὅτι διχῶς ὑπόκειται, ἢ τόδε τι ὄν, ὥσπερ τὸ ζῷον τοῖς πάθεσιν, ἢ ὡς ἡ ὕλη τῇ ἐντελεχείᾳ), δοκεῖ δὲ καὶ τὸ καθόλου αἴτιόν τισιν εἶναι μάλιστα, καὶ εἶναι ἀρχὴ τὸ καθόλου· διὸ ἐπέλθωμεν καὶ περὶ τούτου. ἔοικε γὰρ ἀδύνατον εἶναι οὐσίαν εἶναι ὁτιοῦν τῶν καθόλου λεγομένων. πρῶτον μὲν γὰρ οὐσία ἑκάστου ἡ ἴδιος ἑκάστῳ, ἣ οὐχ ὑπάρχει ἄλλῳ, τὸ δὲ καθόλου κοινόν· τοῦτο γὰρ λέγεται καθόλου ὃ πλείοσιν ὑπάρχειν πέφυκεν. τίνος οὖν οὐσία τοῦτʼ ἔσται; ἢ γὰρ πάντων ἢ οὐδενός, πάντων δʼ οὐχ οἷόν τε· ἑνὸς δʼ εἰ ἔσται, καὶ τἆλλα τοῦτʼ ἔσται· ὧν γὰρ μία ἡ οὐσία καὶ τὸ τί ἦν εἶναι ἕν, καὶ αὐτὰ ἕν. ἔτι οὐσία λέγεται τὸ μὴ καθʼ ὑποκειμένου, τὸ δὲ καθόλου καθʼ ὑποκειμένου τινὸς λέγεται ἀεί. ἀλλʼ ἆρα οὕτω μὲν οὐκ ἐνδέχεται ὡς τὸ τί ἦν εἶναι, ἐν τούτῳ δὲ ἐνυπάρχειν, οἷον τὸ ζῷον ἐν τῷ ἀνθρώπῳ καὶ ἵππῳ; οὐκοῦν δῆλον ὅτι ἔστι τις αὐτοῦ λόγος. διαφέρει δʼ οὐθὲν οὐδʼ εἰ μὴ πάντων λόγος ἔστι τῶν ἐν τῇ οὐσίᾳ· οὐδὲν γὰρ ἧττον οὐσία τοῦτʼ ἔσται τινός, ὡς ὁ ἄνθρωπος τοῦ ἀνθρώπου ἐν ᾧ ὑπάρχει, ὥστε τὸ αὐτὸ συμβήσεται πάλιν· ἔσται γὰρ ἐκείνου οὐσία, οἷον τὸ ζῷον, ἐν ᾧ ὡς ἴδιον ὑπάρχει. ἔτι δὲ καὶ ἀδύνατον καὶ ἄτοπον τὸ τόδε καὶ οὐσίαν, εἰ ἔστιν ἔκ τινων, μὴ ἐξ οὐσιῶν εἶναι μηδʼ ἐκ τοῦ τόδε τι ἀλλʼ ἐκ ποιοῦ· πρότερον γὰρ ἔσται μὴ οὐσία τε καὶ τὸ ποιὸν οὐσίας τε καὶ τοῦ τόδε. ὅπερ ἀδύνατον· οὔτε λόγῳ γὰρ οὔτε χρόνῳ οὔτε γενέσει οἷόν τε τὰ πάθη τῆς οὐσίας εἶναι πρότερα· ἔσται γὰρ καὶ χωριστά. ἔτι τῷ Σωκράτει ἐνυπάρξει οὐσία οὐσίᾳ, ὥστε δυοῖν ἔσται οὐσία. ὅλως δὲ συμβαίνει, εἰ ἔστιν οὐσία ὁ ἄνθρωπος καὶ ὅσα οὕτω λέγεται, μηθὲν τῶν ἐν τῷ λόγῳ εἶναι μηδενὸς οὐσίαν μηδὲ χωρὶς ὑπάρχειν αὐτῶν μηδʼ ἐν ἄλλῳ, λέγω δʼ οἷον οὐκ εἶναί τι ζῷον παρὰ τὰ τινά, οὐδʼ ἄλλο τῶν ἐν τοῖς λόγοις οὐδέν. ἔκ τε δὴ τούτων θεωροῦσι φανερὸν ὅτι οὐδὲν τῶν καθόλου ὑπαρχόντων οὐσία ἐστί, καὶ ὅτι οὐδὲν σημαίνει τῶν κοινῇ κατηγορουμένων τόδε τι,  ἀλλὰ τοιόνδε. εἰ δὲ μή, ἄλλα τε πολλὰ συμβαίνει καὶ ὁ τρίτος ἄνθρωπος. ἔτι δὲ καὶ ὧδε δῆλον. ἀδύνατον γὰρ οὐσίαν ἐξ οὐσιῶν εἶναι ἐνυπαρχουσῶν ὡς ἐντελεχείᾳ· τὰ γὰρ δύο οὕτως ἐντελεχείᾳ οὐδέποτε ἓν ἐντελεχείᾳ, ἀλλʼ ἐὰν δυνάμει δύο ᾖ, ἔσται ἕν (οἷον ἡ διπλασία ἐκ δύο ἡμίσεων δυνάμει γε· ἡ γὰρ ἐντελέχεια χωρίζει), ὥστʼ εἰ ἡ οὐσία ἕν, οὐκ ἔσται ἐξ οὐσιῶν ἐνυπαρχουσῶν καὶ κατὰ τοῦτον τὸν τρόπον, ὃν λέγει Δημόκριτος ὀρθῶς· ἀδύνατον γὰρ εἶναί φησιν ἐκ δύο ἓν ἢ ἐξ ἑνὸς δύο γενέσθαι· τὰ γὰρ μεγέθη τὰ ἄτομα τὰς οὐσίας ποιεῖ. ὁμοίως τοίνυν δῆλον ὅτι καὶ ἐπʼ ἀριθμοῦ ἕξει, εἴπερ ἐστὶν ὁ ἀριθμὸς σύνθεσις μονάδων, ὥσπερ λέγεται ὑπό τινων· ἢ γὰρ οὐχ ἓν ἡ δυὰς ἢ οὐκ ἔστι μονὰς ἐν αὐτῇ ἐντελεχείᾳ.—ἔχει δὲ τὸ συμβαῖνον ἀπορίαν. εἰ γὰρ μήτε ἐκ τῶν καθόλου οἷόν τʼ εἶναι μηδεμίαν οὐσίαν διὰ τὸ τοιόνδε ἀλλὰ μὴ τόδε τι σημαίνειν, μήτʼ ἐξ οὐσιῶν ἐνδέχεται ἐντελεχείᾳ εἶναι μηδεμίαν οὐσίαν σύνθετον, ἀσύνθετον ἂν εἴη οὐσία πᾶσα, ὥστʼ οὐδὲ λόγος ἂν εἴη οὐδεμιᾶς οὐσίας. ἀλλὰ μὴν δοκεῖ γε πᾶσι καὶ ἐλέχθη πάλαι ἢ μόνον οὐσίας εἶναι ὅρον ἢ μάλιστα· νῦν δʼ οὐδὲ ταύτης. οὐδενὸς ἄρʼ ἔσται ὁρισμός· ἢ τρόπον μέν τινα ἔσται τρόπον δέ τινα οὔ. δῆλον δʼ ἔσται τὸ λεγόμενον ἐκ τῶν ὕστερον μᾶλλον. \n\n\n\n \n\n \n\nφανερὸν δʼ ἐξ αὐτῶν τούτων τὸ συμβαῖνον καὶ τοῖς τὰς ἰδέας λέγουσιν οὐσίας τε χωριστὰς εἶναι καὶ ἅμα τὸ εἶδος ἐκ τοῦ γένους ποιοῦσι καὶ τῶν διαφορῶν. εἰ γὰρ ἔστι τὰ εἴδη, καὶ τὸ ζῷον ἐν τῷ ἀνθρώπῳ καὶ ἵππῳ, ἤτοι ἓν καὶ ταὐτὸν τῷ ἀριθμῷ ἐστὶν ἢ ἕτερον· τῷ μὲν γὰρ λόγῳ δῆλον ὅτι ἕν· τὸν γὰρ αὐτὸν διέξεισι λόγον ὁ λέγων ἐν ἑκατέρῳ. εἰ οὖν ἐστί τις ἄνθρωπος αὐτὸς καθʼ αὑτὸν τόδε τι καὶ κεχωρισμένον, ἀνάγκη καὶ ἐξ ὧν, οἷον τὸ ζῷον καὶ τὸ δίπουν, τόδε τι σημαίνειν καὶ εἶναι χωριστὰ καὶ οὐσίας· ὥστε καὶ τὸ ζῷον. εἰ μὲν οὖν τὸ αὐτὸ καὶ ἓν τὸ ἐν τῷ ἵππῳ καὶ τῷ ἀνθρώπῳ, ὥσπερ σὺ σαυτῷ, πῶς τὸ ἓν ἐν τοῖς οὖσι χωρὶς ἓν ἔσται,  καὶ διὰ τί οὐ καὶ χωρὶς αὑτοῦ ἔσται τὸ ζῷον τοῦτο; ἔπειτα εἰ μὲν μεθέξει τοῦ δίποδος καὶ τοῦ πολύποδος, ἀδύνατόν τι συμβαίνει, τἀναντία γὰρ ἅμα ὑπάρξει αὐτῷ ἑνὶ καὶ τῷδέ τινι ὄντι· εἰ δὲ μή, τίς ὁ τρόπος ὅταν εἴπῃ τις τὸ ζῷον εἶναι δίπουν ἢ πεζόν; ἀλλʼ ἴσως σύγκειται καὶ ἅπτεται ἢ μέμικται· ἀλλὰ πάντα ἄτοπα. ἀλλʼ ἕτερον ἐν ἑκάστῳ· οὐκοῦν ἄπειρα ὡς ἔπος εἰπεῖν ἔσται ὧν ἡ οὐσία ζῷον· οὐ γὰρ κατὰ συμβεβηκὸς ἐκ ζῴου ἅνθρωπος. ἔτι πολλὰ ἔσται αὐτὸ τὸ ζῷον· οὐσία τε γὰρ τὸ ἐν ἑκάστῳ ζῷον (οὐ γὰρ κατʼ ἄλλο λέγεται· εἰ δὲ μή, ἐξ ἐκείνου ἔσται ὁ ἄνθρωπος καὶ γένος αὐτοῦ ἐκεῖνο), καὶ ἔτι ἰδέαι ἅπαντα ἐξ ὧν ὁ ἄνθρωπος· οὐκοῦν οὐκ ἄλλου μὲν ἰδέα ἔσται ἄλλου δʼ οὐσία (ἀδύνατον γάρ)· αὐτὸ ἄρα ζῷον ἓν ἕκαστον ἔσται τῶν ἐν τοῖς ζῴοις. ἔτι ἐκ τίνος τοῦτο, καὶ πῶς ἐξ αὐτοῦ ζῴου; ἢ πῶς οἷόν τε εἶναι τὸ ζῷον, ᾧ οὐσία τοῦτο αὐτό, παρʼ αὐτὸ τὸ ζῷον; ἔτι δʼ ἐπὶ τῶν αἰσθητῶν ταῦτά τε συμβαίνει καὶ τούτων ἀτοπώτερα. εἰ δὴ ἀδύνατον οὕτως ἔχειν, δῆλον ὅτι οὐκ ἔστιν εἴδη αὐτῶν οὕτως ὥς τινές φασιν.\n\n\n\n \n\n \n\nἐπεὶ δʼ ἡ οὐσία ἑτέρα, τό τε σύνολον καὶ ὁ λόγος (λέγω δʼ ὅτι ἡ μὲν οὕτως ἐστὶν οὐσία, σὺν τῇ ὕλῃ συνειλημμένος ὁ λόγος, ἡ δʼ ὁ λόγος ὅλως), ὅσαι μὲν οὖν οὕτω λέγονται, τούτων μὲν ἔστι φθορά (καὶ γὰρ γένεσις), τοῦ δὲ λόγου οὐκ ἔστιν οὕτως ὥστε φθείρεσθαι (οὐδὲ γὰρ γένεσις, οὐ γὰρ γίγνεται τὸ οἰκίᾳ εἶναι ἀλλὰ τὸ τῇδε τῇ οἰκίᾳ), ἀλλʼ ἄνευ γενέσεως καὶ φθορᾶς εἰσὶ καὶ οὐκ εἰσίν· δέδεικται γὰρ ὅτι οὐδεὶς ταῦτα γεννᾷ οὐδὲ ποιεῖ. διὰ τοῦτο δὲ καὶ τῶν οὐσιῶν τῶν αἰσθητῶν τῶν καθʼ ἕκαστα οὔτε ὁρισμὸς οὔτε ἀπόδειξις ἔστιν, ὅτι ἔχουσιν ὕλην ἧς ἡ φύσις τοιαύτη ὥστʼ ἐνδέχεσθαι καὶ εἶναι καὶ μή· διὸ φθαρτὰ πάντα τὰ καθʼ ἕκαστα αὐτῶν. εἰ οὖν ἥ τʼ ἀπόδειξις τῶν ἀναγκαίων καὶ ὁ ὁρισμὸς ἐπιστημονικόν, καὶ οὐκ ἐνδέχεται, ὥσπερ οὐδʼ ἐπιστήμην ὁτὲ μὲν ἐπιστήμην ὁτὲ δʼ ἄγνοιαν εἶναι, ἀλλὰ δόξα τὸ τοιοῦτόν ἐστιν, οὕτως οὐδʼ ἀπόδειξιν οὐδʼ ὁρισμόν, ἀλλὰ δόξα ἐστὶ τοῦ ἐνδεχομένου ἄλλως ἔχειν,  δῆλον ὅτι οὐκ ἂν εἴη αὐτῶν οὔτε ὁρισμὸς οὔτε ἀπόδειξις. ἄδηλά τε γὰρ τὰ φθειρόμενα τοῖς ἔχουσι τὴν ἐπιστήμην, ὅταν ἐκ τῆς αἰσθήσεως ἀπέλθῃ, καὶ σωζομένων τῶν λόγων ἐν τῇ ψυχῇ τῶν αὐτῶν οὐκ ἔσται οὔτε ὁρισμὸς ἔτι οὔτε ἀπόδειξις. διὸ δεῖ, τῶν πρὸς ὅρον ὅταν τις ὁρίζηταί τι τῶν καθʼ ἕκαστον, μὴ ἀγνοεῖν ὅτι ἀεὶ ἀναιρεῖν ἔστιν· οὐ γὰρ ἐνδέχεται ὁρίσασθαι.\n\n \n\nοὐδὲ δὴ ἰδέαν οὐδεμίαν ἔστιν ὁρίσασθαι. τῶν γὰρ καθʼ ἕκαστον ἡ ἰδέα, ὡς φασί, καὶ χωριστή· ἀναγκαῖον δὲ ἐξ ὀνομάτων εἶναι τὸν λόγον, ὄνομα δʼ οὐ ποιήσει ὁ ὁριζόμενος (ἄγνωστον γὰρ ἔσται), τὰ δὲ κείμενα κοινὰ πᾶσιν· ἀνάγκη ἄρα ὑπάρχειν καὶ ἄλλῳ ταῦτα· οἷον εἴ τις σὲ ὁρίσαιτο, ζῷον ἐρεῖ ἰσχνὸν ἢ λευκὸν ἢ ἕτερόν τι ὃ καὶ ἄλλῳ ὑπάρξει. εἰ δέ τις φαίη μηδὲν κωλύειν χωρὶς μὲν πάντα πολλοῖς ἅμα δὲ μόνῳ τούτῳ ὑπάρχειν, λεκτέον πρῶτον μὲν ὅτι καὶ ἀμφοῖν, οἷον τὸ ζῷον δίπουν τῷ ζῴῳ καὶ τῷ δίποδι (καὶ τοῦτο ἐπὶ μὲν τῶν ἀϊδίων καὶ ἀνάγκη εἶναι, πρότερά γʼ ὄντα καὶ μέρη τοῦ συνθέτου· ἀλλὰ μὴν καὶ χωριστά, εἴπερ τὸ ἄνθρωπος χωριστόν· ἢ γὰρ οὐθὲν ἢ ἄμφω· εἰ μὲν οὖν μηθέν, οὐκ ἔσται τὸ γένος παρὰ τὰ εἴδη, εἰ δʼ ἔσται, καὶ ἡ διαφορά)· εἶθʼ ὅτι πρότερα τῷ εἶναι· ταῦτα δὲ οὐκ ἀνταναιρεῖται. ἔπειτα εἰ ἐξ ἰδεῶν αἱ ἰδέαι (ἁσυνθετώτερα γὰρ τὰ ἐξ ὧν), ἔτι ἐπὶ πολλῶν δεήσει κἀκεῖνα κατηγορεῖσθαι ἐξ ὧν ἡ ἰδέα, οἷον τὸ ζῷον καὶ τὸ δίπουν. εἰ δὲ μή, πῶς γνωρισθήσεται; ἔσται γὰρ ἰδέα τις ἣν ἀδύνατον ἐπὶ πλειόνων κατηγορῆσαι ἢ ἑνός. οὐ δοκεῖ δέ, ἀλλὰ πᾶσα ἰδέα εἶναι μεθεκτή. ὥσπερ οὖν εἴρηται, λανθάνει ὅτι ἀδύνατον ὁρίσασθαι ἐν τοῖς ἀϊδίοις, μάλιστα δὲ ὅσα μοναχά, οἷον ἥλιος ἢ σελήνη. οὐ μόνον γὰρ διαμαρτάνουσι τῷ προστιθέναι τοιαῦτα ὧν ἀφαιρουμένων ἔτι ἔσται ἥλιος, ὥσπερ τὸ περὶ γῆν ἰὸν ἢ νυκτικρυφές (ἂν γὰρ στῇ ἢ φανῇ, οὐκέτι ἔσται ἥλιος· ἀλλʼ ἄτοπον εἰ μή· ὁ γὰρ ἥλιος οὐσίαν τινὰ σημαίνεi)· ἔτι ὅσα ἐπʼ ἄλλου ἐνδέχεται, οἷον ἐὰν ἕτερος γένηται τοιοῦτος, δῆλον ὅτι ἥλιος ἔσται· κοινὸς ἄρα ὁ λόγος·  ἀλλʼ ἦν τῶν καθʼ ἕκαστα ὁ ἥλιος, ὥσπερ Κλέων ἢ Σωκράτης· ἐπεὶ διὰ τί οὐδεὶς ὅρον ἐκφέρει αὐτῶν ἰδέας; γένοιτο γὰρ ἂν δῆλον πειρωμένων ὅτι ἀληθὲς τὸ νῦν εἰρημένον.\n\n\n\n \n\n \n\nφανερὸν δὲ ὅτι καὶ τῶν δοκουσῶν εἶναι οὐσιῶν αἱ πλεῖσται δυνάμεις εἰσί, τά τε μόρια τῶν ζῴων (οὐθὲν γὰρ κεχωρισμένον αὐτῶν ἐστίν· ὅταν δὲ χωρισθῇ, καὶ τότε ὄντα ὡς ὕλη πάντα) καὶ γῆ καὶ πῦρ καὶ ἀήρ· οὐδὲν γὰρ αὐτῶν ἕν ἐστιν, ἀλλʼ οἷον σωρός, πρὶν ἢ πεφθῇ καὶ γένηταί τι ἐξ αὐτῶν ἕν. μάλιστα δʼ ἄν τις τὰ τῶν ἐμψύχων ὑπολάβοι μόρια καὶ τὰ τῆς ψυχῆς πάρεγγυς ἄμφω γίγνεσθαι, ὄντα καὶ ἐντελεχείᾳ καὶ δυνάμει, τῷ ἀρχὰς ἔχειν κινήσεως ἀπό τινος ἐν ταῖς καμπαῖς· διὸ ἔνια ζῷα διαιρούμενα ζῇ. ἀλλʼ ὅμως δυνάμει πάντʼ ἔσται, ὅταν ᾖ ἓν καὶ συνεχὲς φύσει, ἀλλὰ μὴ βίᾳ ἢ συμφύσει· τὸ γὰρ τοιοῦτον πήρωσις. ἐπεὶ δὲ τὸ ἓν λέγεται ὥσπερ καὶ τὸ ὄν, καὶ ἡ οὐσία ἡ τοῦ ἑνὸς μία, καὶ ὧν μία ἀριθμῷ ἓν ἀριθμῷ, φανερὸν ὅτι οὔτε τὸ ἓν οὔτε τὸ ὂν ἐνδέχεται οὐσίαν εἶναι τῶν πραγμάτων, ὥσπερ οὐδὲ τὸ στοιχείῳ εἶναι ἢ ἀρχῇ· ἀλλὰ ζητοῦμεν τίς οὖν ἡ ἀρχή, ἵνα εἰς γνωριμώτερον ἀναγάγωμεν. μᾶλλον μὲν οὖν τούτων οὐσία τὸ ὂν καὶ ἓν ἢ ἥ τε ἀρχὴ καὶ τὸ στοιχεῖον καὶ τὸ αἴτιον, οὔπω δὲ οὐδὲ ταῦτα, εἴπερ μηδʼ ἄλλο κοινὸν μηδὲν οὐσία· οὐδενὶ γὰρ ὑπάρχει ἡ οὐσία ἀλλʼ ἢ αὑτῇ τε καὶ τῷ ἔχοντι αὐτήν, οὗ ἐστὶν οὐσία. ἔτι τὸ ἓν πολλαχῇ οὐκ ἂν εἴη ἅμα, τὸ δὲ κοινὸν ἅμα πολλαχῇ ὑπάρχει· ὥστε δῆλον ὅτι οὐδὲν τῶν καθόλου ὑπάρχει παρὰ τὰ καθʼ ἕκαστα χωρίς. ἀλλʼ οἱ τὰ εἴδη λέγοντες τῇ μὲν ὀρθῶς λέγουσι χωρίζοντες αὐτά, εἴπερ οὐσίαι εἰσί, τῇ δʼ οὐκ ὀρθῶς, ὅτι τὸ ἓν ἐπὶ πολλῶν εἶδος λέγουσιν. αἴτιον δʼ ὅτι οὐκ ἔχουσιν ἀποδοῦναι τίνες αἱ τοιαῦται οὐσίαι αἱ ἄφθαρτοι παρὰ τὰς καθʼ ἕκαστα καὶ αἰσθητάς· ποιοῦσιν οὖν τὰς αὐτὰς τῷ εἴδει τοῖς φθαρτοῖς (ταύτας γὰρ ἴσμεν), αὐτοάνθρωπον καὶ αὐτόϊππον, προστιθέντες τοῖς αἰσθητοῖς τὸ ῥῆμα τὸ αὐτό. καίτοι κἂν εἰ μὴ ἑωράκειμεν τὰ ἄστρα,  οὐδὲν ἂν ἧττον, οἶμαι, ἦσαν οὐσίαι ἀΐδιοι παρʼ ἃς ἡμεῖς ᾔδειμεν· ὥστε καὶ νῦν εἰ μὴ ἔχομεν τίνες εἰσίν, ἀλλʼ εἶναί γέ τινας ἴσως ἀναγκαῖον. ὅτι μὲν οὖν οὔτε τῶν καθόλου λεγομένων οὐδὲν οὐσία οὔτʼ ἐστὶν οὐσία οὐδεμία ἐξ οὐσιῶν, δῆλον. \n\n\n\n \n\n \n\nτί δὲ χρὴ λέγειν καὶ ὁποῖόν τι τὴν οὐσίαν, πάλιν ἄλλην οἷον ἀρχὴν ποιησάμενοι λέγωμεν· ἴσως γὰρ ἐκ τούτων ἔσται δῆλον καὶ περὶ ἐκείνης τῆς οὐσίας ἥτις ἐστὶ κεχωρισμένη τῶν αἰσθητῶν οὐσιῶν. ἐπεὶ οὖν ἡ οὐσία ἀρχὴ καὶ αἰτία τις ἐστίν, ἐντεῦθεν μετιτέον. ζητεῖται δὲ τὸ διὰ τί ἀεὶ οὕτως, διὰ τί ἄλλο ἄλλῳ τινὶ ὑπάρχει. τὸ γὰρ ζητεῖν διὰ τί ὁ μουσικὸς ἄνθρωπος μουσικὸς ἄνθρωπός ἐστιν, ἤτοι ἐστὶ τὸ εἰρημένον ζητεῖν, διὰ τί ὁ ἄνθρωπος μουσικός ἐστιν, ἢ ἄλλο. τὸ μὲν οὖν διὰ τί αὐτό ἐστιν αὐτό, οὐδέν ἐστι ζητεῖν (δεῖ γὰρ τὸ ὅτι καὶ τὸ εἶναι ὑπάρχειν δῆλα ὄντα—λέγω δʼ οἷον ὅτι ἡ σελήνη ἐκλείπει—, αὐτὸ δὲ ὅτι αὐτό, εἷς λόγος καὶ μία αἰτία ἐπὶ πάντων, διὰ τί ὁ ἄνθρωπος ἄνθρωπος ἢ ὁ μουσικὸς μουσικός, πλὴν εἴ τις λέγοι ὅτι ἀδιαίρετον πρὸς αὑτὸ ἕκαστον, τοῦτο δʼ ἦν τὸ ἑνὶ εἶναι· ἀλλὰ τοῦτο κοινόν γε κατὰ πάντων καὶ σύντομον)· ζητήσειε δʼ ἄν τις διὰ τί ἅνθρωπός ἐστι ζῷον τοιονδί. τοῦτο μὲν τοίνυν δῆλον, ὅτι οὐ ζητεῖ διὰ τί ὅς ἐστιν ἄνθρωπος ἄνθρωπός ἐστιν· τὶ ἄρα κατά τινος ζητεῖ διὰ τί ὑπάρχει (ὅτι δʼ ὑπάρχει, δεῖ δῆλον εἶναι· εἰ γὰρ μὴ οὕτως, οὐδὲν ζητεῖ), οἷον διὰ τί βροντᾷ; διὰ τί ψόφος γίγνεται ἐν τοῖς νέφεσιν; ἄλλο γὰρ οὕτω κατʼ ἄλλου ἐστὶ τὸ ζητούμενον. καὶ διὰ τί ταδί, οἷον πλίνθοι καὶ λίθοι, οἰκία ἐστίν; φανερὸν τοίνυν ὅτι ζητεῖ τὸ αἴτιον· τοῦτο δʼ ἐστὶ τὸ τί ἦν εἶναι, ὡς εἰπεῖν λογικῶς, ὃ ἐπʼ ἐνίων μέν ἐστι τίνος ἕνεκα, οἷον ἴσως ἐπʼ οἰκίας ἢ κλίνης, ἐπʼ ἐνίων δὲ τί ἐκίνησε πρῶτον· αἴτιον γὰρ καὶ τοῦτο. ἀλλὰ τὸ μὲν τοιοῦτον αἴτιον ἐπὶ τοῦ γίγνεσθαι ζητεῖται καὶ φθείρεσθαι, θάτερον δὲ καὶ ἐπὶ τοῦ εἶναι. λανθάνει δὲ μάλιστα τὸ ζητούμενον ἐν τοῖς μὴ κατʼ ἀλλήλων λεγομένοις,  οἷον ἄνθρωπος τί ἐστι ζητεῖται διὰ τὸ ἁπλῶς λέγεσθαι ἀλλὰ μὴ διορίζειν ὅτι τάδε τόδε. ἀλλὰ δεῖ διαρθρώσαντας ζητεῖν· εἰ δὲ μή, κοινὸν τοῦ μηθὲν ζητεῖν καὶ τοῦ ζητεῖν τι γίγνεται. ἐπεὶ δὲ δεῖ ἔχειν τε καὶ ὑπάρχειν τὸ εἶναι, δῆλον δὴ ὅτι τὴν ὕλην ζητεῖ διὰ τί τί ἐστιν· οἷον οἰκία ταδὶ διὰ τί; ὅτι ὑπάρχει ὃ ἦν οἰκίᾳ εἶναι. καὶ ἄνθρωπος τοδί, ἢ τὸ σῶμα τοῦτο τοδὶ ἔχον. ὥστε τὸ αἴτιον ζητεῖται τῆς ὕλης (τοῦτο δʼ ἐστὶ τὸ εἶδος) ᾧ τί ἐστιν· τοῦτο δʼ ἡ οὐσία. φανερὸν τοίνυν ὅτι ἐπὶ τῶν ἁπλῶν οὐκ ἔστι ζήτησις οὐδὲ δίδαξις, ἀλλʼ ἕτερος τρόπος τῆς ζητήσεως τῶν τοιούτων.—ἐπεὶ δὲ τὸ ἔκ τινος σύνθετον οὕτως ὥστε ἓν εἶναι τὸ πᾶν,  μὴ ὡς σωρὸς ἀλλʼ ὡς ἡ συλλαβή—ἡ δὲ συλλαβὴ οὐκ ἔστι τὰ στοιχεῖα, οὐδὲ τῷ βα ταὐτὸ τὸ β καὶ α, οὐδʼ ἡ σὰρξ πῦρ καὶ γῆ (διαλυθέντων γὰρ τὰ μὲν οὐκέτι ἔστιν, οἷον ἡ σὰρξ καὶ ἡ συλλαβή, τὰ δὲ στοιχεῖα ἔστι, καὶ τὸ πῦρ καὶ ἡ γῆ)· ἔστιν ἄρα τι ἡ συλλαβή, οὐ μόνον τὰ στοιχεῖα τὸ φωνῆεν καὶ ἄφωνον ἀλλὰ καὶ ἕτερόν τι, καὶ ἡ σὰρξ οὐ μόνον πῦρ καὶ γῆ ἢ τὸ θερμὸν καὶ ψυχρὸν ἀλλὰ καὶ ἕτερόν τι—εἰ τοίνυν ἀνάγκη κἀκεῖνο ἢ στοιχεῖον ἢ ἐκ στοιχείων εἶναι, εἰ μὲν στοιχεῖον, πάλιν ὁ αὐτὸς ἔσται λόγος (ἐκ τούτου γὰρ καὶ πυρὸς καὶ γῆς ἔσται ἡ σὰρξ καὶ ἔτι ἄλλου, ὥστʼ εἰς ἄπειρον βαδιεῖταi)· εἰ δὲ ἐκ στοιχείου, δῆλον ὅτι οὐχ ἑνὸς ἀλλὰ πλειόνων, ἢ ἐκεῖνο αὐτὸ ἔσται, ὥστε πάλιν ἐπὶ τούτου τὸν αὐτὸν ἐροῦμεν λόγον καὶ ἐπὶ τῆς σαρκὸς ἢ συλλαβῆς. δόξειε δʼ ἂν εἶναι τὶ τοῦτο καὶ οὐ στοιχεῖον, καὶ αἴτιόν γε τοῦ εἶναι τοδὶ μὲν σάρκα τοδὶ δὲ συλλαβήν· ὁμοίως δὲ καὶ ἐπὶ τῶν ἄλλων. οὐσία δὲ ἑκάστου μὲν τοῦτο (τοῦτο γὰρ αἴτιον πρῶτον τοῦ εἶναι)—ἐπεὶ δʼ ἔνια οὐκ οὐσίαι τῶν πραγμάτων, ἀλλʼ ὅσαι οὐσίαι, κατὰ φύσιν καὶ φύσει συνεστήκασι, φανείη ἂν  αὕτη ἡ φύσις οὐσία, ἥ ἐστιν οὐ στοιχεῖον ἀλλʼ ἀρχή—· στοιχεῖον δʼ ἐστὶν εἰς ὃ διαιρεῖται ἐνυπάρχον ὡς ὕλην, οἷον τῆς συλλαβῆς τὸ α καὶ τὸ β.","paragraphs":[{"id":"RAW_DAZY_BOO7:P0001","text":"τὸ ὂν λέγεται πολλαχῶς, καθάπερ διειλόμεθα πρότερον ἐν τοῖς περὶ τοῦ ποσαχῶς· σημαίνει γὰρ τὸ μὲν τί ἐστι καὶ τόδε τι, τὸ δὲ ποιὸν ἢ ποσὸν ἢ τῶν ἄλλων ἕκαστον τῶν οὕτω κατηγορουμένων. τοσαυταχῶς δὲ λεγομένου τοῦ ὄντος φανερὸν ὅτι τούτων πρῶτον ὂν τὸ τί ἐστιν, ὅπερ σημαίνει τὴν οὐσίαν (ὅταν μὲν γὰρ εἴπωμεν ποῖόν τι τόδε, ἢ ἀγαθὸν λέγομεν ἢ κακόν, ἀλλʼ οὐ τρίπηχυ ἢ ἄνθρωπον· ὅταν δὲ τί ἐστιν, οὐ λευκὸν οὐδὲ θερμὸν οὐδὲ τρίπηχυ, ἀλλὰ ἄνθρωπον ἢ θεόν), τὰ δʼ ἄλλα λέγεται ὄντα τῷ τοῦ οὕτως ὄντος τὰ μὲν ποσότητες εἶναι, τὰ δὲ ποιότητες, τὰ δὲ πάθη, τὰ δὲ ἄλλο τι. διὸ κἂν ἀπορήσειέ τις πότερον τὸ βαδίζειν καὶ τὸ ὑγιαίνειν καὶ τὸ καθῆσθαι ἕκαστον αὐτῶν ὂν σημαίνει, ὁμοίως δὲ καὶ ἐπὶ τῶν ἄλλων ὁτουοῦν τῶν τοιούτων· οὐδὲν γὰρ αὐτῶν ἐστὶν οὔτε καθʼ αὑτὸ πεφυκὸς οὔτε χωρίζεσθαι δυνατὸν τῆς οὐσίας, ἀλλὰ μᾶλλον, εἴπερ, τὸ βαδίζον τῶν ὄντων καὶ τὸ καθήμενον καὶ τὸ ὑγιαῖνον.","kind":"passage"},{"id":"RAW_DAZY_BOO7:P0002","text":"ταῦτα δὲ μᾶλλον φαίνεται ὄντα, διότι ἔστι τι τὸ ὑποκείμενον αὐτοῖς ὡρισμένον (τοῦτο δʼ ἐστὶν ἡ οὐσία καὶ τὸ καθʼ ἕκαστον), ὅπερ ἐμφαίνεται ἐν τῇ κατηγορίᾳ τῇ τοιαύτῃ· τὸ ἀγαθὸν γὰρ ἢ τὸ καθήμενον οὐκ ἄνευ τούτου λέγεται. δῆλον οὖν ὅτι διὰ ταύτην κἀκείνων ἕκαστον ἔστιν, ὥστε τὸ πρώτως ὂν καὶ οὐ τὶ ὂν ἀλλʼ ὂν ἁπλῶς ἡ οὐσία ἂν εἴη. πολλαχῶς μὲν οὖν λέγεται τὸ πρῶτον· ὅμως δὲ πάντως ἡ οὐσία πρῶτον, καὶ λόγῳ καὶ γνώσει καὶ χρόνῳ. τῶν μὲν γὰρ ἄλλων κατηγορημάτων οὐθὲν χωριστόν, αὕτη δὲ μόνη· καὶ τῷ λόγῳ δὲ τοῦτο πρῶτον (ἀνάγκη γὰρ ἐν τῷ ἑκάστου λόγῳ τὸν τῆς οὐσίας ἐνυπάρχειν)· καὶ εἰδέναι δὲ τότʼ οἰόμεθα ἕκαστον μάλιστα, ὅταν τί ἐστιν ὁ ἄνθρωπος γνῶμεν ἢ τὸ πῦρ,  μᾶλλον ἢ τὸ ποιὸν ἢ τὸ ποσὸν ἢ τὸ πού, ἐπεὶ καὶ αὐτῶν τούτων τότε ἕκαστον ἴσμεν, ὅταν τί ἐστι τὸ ποσὸν ἢ τὸ ποιὸν γνῶμεν.","kind":"passage"},{"id":"RAW_DAZY_BOO7:P0003","text":"καὶ δὴ καὶ τὸ πάλαι τε καὶ νῦν καὶ ἀεὶ ζητούμενον καὶ ἀεὶ ἀπορούμενον, τί τὸ ὄν, τοῦτό ἐστι τίς ἡ οὐσία (τοῦτο γὰρ οἱ μὲν ἓν εἶναί φασιν οἱ δὲ πλείω ἢ ἕν, καὶ οἱ μὲν πεπερασμένα οἱ δὲ ἄπειρα), διὸ καὶ ἡμῖν καὶ μάλιστα καὶ πρῶτον καὶ μόνον ὡς εἰπεῖν περὶ τοῦ οὕτως ὄντος θεωρητέον τί ἐστιν.","kind":"passage"},{"id":"RAW_DAZY_BOO7:P0004","text":"δοκεῖ δʼ ἡ οὐσία ὑπάρχειν φανερώτατα μὲν τοῖς σώμασιν (διὸ τά τε ζῷα καὶ τὰ φυτὰ καὶ τὰ μόρια αὐτῶν οὐσίας εἶναί φαμεν, καὶ τὰ φυσικὰ σώματα, οἷον πῦρ καὶ ὕδωρ καὶ γῆν καὶ τῶν τοιούτων ἕκαστον, καὶ ὅσα ἢ μόρια τούτων ἢ ἐκ τούτων ἐστίν, ἢ μορίων ἢ πάντων, οἷον ὅ τε οὐρανὸς καὶ τὰ μόρια αὐτοῦ, ἄστρα καὶ σελήνη καὶ ἥλιος)· πότερον δὲ αὗται μόναι οὐσίαι εἰσὶν ἢ καὶ ἄλλαι, ἢ τούτων τινὲς ἢ καὶ ἄλλαι, ἢ τούτων μὲν οὐθὲν ἕτεραι δέ τινες, σκεπτέον. δοκεῖ δέ τισι τὰ τοῦ σώματος πέρατα, οἷον ἐπιφάνεια καὶ γραμμὴ καὶ στιγμὴ καὶ μονάς, εἶναι οὐσίαι, καὶ μᾶλλον ἢ τὸ σῶμα καὶ τὸ στερεόν.","kind":"passage"},{"id":"RAW_DAZY_BOO7:P0005","text":"ἔτι παρὰ τὰ αἰσθητὰ οἱ μὲν οὐκ οἴονται εἶναι οὐδὲν τοιοῦτον, οἱ δὲ πλείω καὶ μᾶλλον ὄντα ἀΐδια, ὥσπερ Πλάτων τά τε εἴδη καὶ τὰ μαθηματικὰ δύο οὐσίας, τρίτην δὲ τὴν τῶν αἰσθητῶν σωμάτων οὐσίαν, Σπεύσιππος δὲ καὶ πλείους οὐσίας ἀπὸ τοῦ ἑνὸς ἀρξάμενος, καὶ ἀρχὰς ἑκάστης οὐσίας, ἄλλην μὲν ἀριθμῶν ἄλλην δὲ μεγεθῶν, ἔπειτα ψυχῆς· καὶ τοῦτον δὴ τὸν τρόπον ἐπεκτείνει τὰς οὐσίας. ἔνιοι δὲ τὰ μὲν εἴδη καὶ τοὺς ἀριθμοὺς τὴν αὐτὴν ἔχειν φασὶ φύσιν, τὰ δὲ ἄλλα ἐχόμενα, γραμμὰς καὶ ἐπίπεδα, μέχρι πρὸς τὴν τοῦ οὐρανοῦ οὐσίαν καὶ τὰ αἰσθητά. περὶ δὴ τούτων τί λέγεται καλῶς ἢ μὴ καλῶς, καὶ τίνες εἰσὶν οὐσίαι, καὶ πότερον εἰσί τινες παρὰ τὰς αἰσθητὰς ἢ οὐκ εἰσί, καὶ αὗται πῶς εἰσί, καὶ πότερον ἔστι τις χωριστὴ οὐσία, καὶ διὰ τί καὶ πῶς, ἢ οὐδεμία, παρὰ τὰς αἰσθητάς, σκεπτέον, ὑποτυπωσαμένοις τὴν οὐσίαν πρῶτον τί ἐστιν.","kind":"passage"},{"id":"RAW_DAZY_BOO7:P0006","text":"λέγεται δʼ ἡ οὐσία, εἰ μὴ πλεοναχῶς, ἀλλʼ ἐν τέτταρσί γε μάλιστα· καὶ γὰρ τὸ τί ἦν εἶναι καὶ τὸ καθόλου καὶ τὸ γένος οὐσία δοκεῖ εἶναι ἑκάστου, καὶ τέταρτον τούτων τὸ ὑποκείμενον. τὸ δʼ ὑποκείμενόν ἐστι καθʼ οὗ τὰ ἄλλα λέγεται, ἐκεῖνο δὲ αὐτὸ μηκέτι κατʼ ἄλλου· διὸ πρῶτον περὶ τούτου διοριστέον·  μάλιστα γὰρ δοκεῖ εἶναι οὐσία τὸ ὑποκείμενον πρῶτον. τοιοῦτον δὲ τρόπον μέν τινα ἡ ὕλη λέγεται, ἄλλον δὲ τρόπον ἡ μορφή, τρίτον δὲ τὸ ἐκ τούτων (λέγω δὲ τὴν μὲν ὕλην οἷον τὸν χαλκόν, τὴν δὲ μορφὴν τὸ σχῆμα τῆς ἰδέας, τὸ δʼ ἐκ τούτων τὸν ἀνδριάντα τὸ σύνολον), ὥστε εἰ τὸ εἶδος τῆς ὕλης πρότερον καὶ μᾶλλον ὄν, καὶ τοῦ ἐξ ἀμφοῖν πρότερον ἔσται διὰ τὸν αὐτὸν λόγον. νῦν μὲν οὖν τύπῳ εἴρηται τί ποτʼ ἐστὶν ἡ οὐσία, ὅτι τὸ μὴ καθʼ ὑποκειμένου ἀλλὰ καθʼ οὗ τὰ ἄλλα· δεῖ δὲ μὴ μόνον οὕτως· οὐ γὰρ ἱκανόν· αὐτὸ γὰρ τοῦτο ἄδηλον, καὶ ἔτι ἡ ὕλη οὐσία γίγνεται.","kind":"passage"},{"id":"RAW_DAZY_BOO7:P0007","text":"εἰ γὰρ μὴ αὕτη οὐσία, τίς ἐστιν ἄλλη διαφεύγει· περιαιρουμένων γὰρ τῶν ἄλλων οὐ φαίνεται οὐδὲν ὑπομένον· τὰ μὲν γὰρ ἄλλα τῶν σωμάτων πάθη καὶ ποιήματα καὶ δυνάμεις, τὸ δὲ μῆκος καὶ πλάτος καὶ βάθος ποσότητές τινες ἀλλʼ οὐκ οὐσίαι (τὸ γὰρ ποσὸν οὐκ οὐσία), ἀλλὰ μᾶλλον ᾧ ὑπάρχει ταῦτα πρώτῳ, ἐκεῖνό ἐστιν οὐσία. ἀλλὰ μὴν ἀφαιρουμένου μήκους καὶ πλάτους καὶ βάθους οὐδὲν ὁρῶμεν ὑπολειπόμενον, πλὴν εἴ τί ἐστι τὸ ὁριζόμενον ὑπὸ τούτων, ὥστε τὴν ὕλην ἀνάγκη φαίνεσθαι μόνην οὐσίαν οὕτω σκοπουμένοις. λέγω δʼ ὕλην ἣ καθʼ αὑτὴν μήτε τὶ μήτε ποσὸν μήτε ἄλλο μηδὲν λέγεται οἷς ὥρισται τὸ ὄν. ἔστι γάρ τι καθʼ οὗ κατηγορεῖται τούτων ἕκαστον, ᾧ τὸ εἶναι ἕτερον καὶ τῶν κατηγοριῶν ἑκάστῃ (τὰ μὲν γὰρ ἄλλα τῆς οὐσίας κατηγορεῖται, αὕτη δὲ τῆς ὕλης), ὥστε τὸ ἔσχατον καθʼ αὑτὸ οὔτε τὶ οὔτε ποσὸν οὔτε ἄλλο οὐδέν ἐστιν· οὐδὲ δὴ αἱ ἀποφάσεις, καὶ γὰρ αὗται ὑπάρξουσι κατὰ συμβεβηκός.","kind":"passage"},{"id":"RAW_DAZY_BOO7:P0008","text":"ἐκ μὲν οὖν τούτων θεωροῦσι συμβαίνει οὐσίαν εἶναι τὴν ὕλην· ἀδύνατον δέ· καὶ γὰρ τὸ χωριστὸν καὶ τὸ τόδε τι ὑπάρχειν δοκεῖ μάλιστα τῇ οὐσίᾳ, διὸ τὸ εἶδος καὶ τὸ ἐξ ἀμφοῖν οὐσία δόξειεν ἂν εἶναι μᾶλλον τῆς ὕλης. τὴν μὲν τοίνυν ἐξ ἀμφοῖν οὐσίαν, λέγω δὲ τὴν ἔκ τε τῆς ὕλης καὶ τῆς μορφῆς, ἀφετέον, ὑστέρα γὰρ καὶ δήλη· φανερὰ δέ πως καὶ ἡ ὕλη· περὶ δὲ τῆς τρίτης σκεπτέον, αὕτη γὰρ ἀπορωτάτη. ὁμολογοῦνται δʼ οὐσίαι εἶναι τῶν αἰσθητῶν τινές, ὥστε ἐν ταύταις ζητητέον πρῶτον.  πρὸ ἔργου γὰρ τὸ μεταβαίνειν εἰς τὸ γνωριμώτερον. ἡ γὰρ μάθησις οὕτω γίγνεται πᾶσι διὰ τῶν ἧττον γνωρίμων φύσει εἰς τὰ γνώριμα μᾶλλον· καὶ τοῦτο ἔργον ἐστίν, ὥσπερ ἐν ταῖς πράξεσι τὸ ποιῆσαι ἐκ τῶν ἑκάστῳ ἀγαθῶν τὰ ὅλως ἀγαθὰ ἑκάστῳ ἀγαθά, οὕτως ἐκ τῶν αὐτῷ γνωριμωτέρων τὰ τῇ φύσει γνώριμα αὐτῷ γνώριμα.","kind":"passage"},{"id":"RAW_DAZY_BOO7:P0009","text":"τὰ δʼ ἑκάστοις γνώριμα καὶ πρῶτα πολλάκις ἠρέμα ἐστὶ γνώριμα, καὶ μικρὸν ἢ οὐθὲν ἔχει τοῦ ὄντος· ἀλλʼ ὅμως ἐκ τῶν φαύλως μὲν γνωστῶν αὐτῷ δὲ γνωστῶν τὰ ὅλως γνωστὰ γνῶναι πειρατέον, μεταβαίνοντας, ὥσπερ εἴρηται, διὰ τούτων αὐτῶν.","kind":"passage"},{"id":"RAW_DAZY_BOO7:P0010","text":"ἐπεὶ δʼ ἐν ἀρχῇ διειλόμεθα πόσοις ὁρίζομεν τὴν οὐσίαν, καὶ τούτων ἕν τι ἐδόκει εἶναι τὸ τί ἦν εἶναι, θεωρητέον περὶ αὐτοῦ. καὶ πρῶτον εἴπωμεν ἔνια περὶ αὐτοῦ λογικῶς, ὅτι ἐστὶ τὸ τί ἦν εἶναι ἑκάστου ὃ λέγεται καθʼ αὑτό. οὐ γάρ ἐστι τὸ σοὶ εἶναι τὸ μουσικῷ εἶναι· οὐ γὰρ κατὰ σαυτὸν εἶ μουσικός. ὃ ἄρα κατὰ σαυτόν. οὐδὲ δὴ τοῦτο πᾶν· οὐ γὰρ τὸ οὕτως καθʼ αὑτὸ ὡς ἐπιφανείᾳ λευκόν, ὅτι οὐκ ἔστι τὸ ἐπιφανείᾳ εἶναι τὸ λευκῷ εἶναι. ἀλλὰ μὴν οὐδὲ τὸ ἐξ ἀμφοῖν, τὸ ἐπιφανείᾳ λευκῇ, ὅτι πρόσεστιν αὐτό. ἐν ᾧ ἄρα μὴ ἐνέσται λόγῳ αὐτό, λέγοντι αὐτό, οὗτος ὁ λόγος τοῦ τί ἦν εἶναι ἑκάστῳ, ὥστʼ εἰ τὸ ἐπιφανείᾳ λευκῇ εἶναί ἐστι τὸ ἐπιφανείᾳ εἶναι λείᾳ, τὸ λευκῷ καὶ λείῳ εἶναι τὸ αὐτὸ καὶ ἕν.","kind":"passage"},{"id":"RAW_DAZY_BOO7:P0011","text":"ἐπεὶ δʼ ἔστι καὶ κατὰ τὰς ἄλλας κατηγορίας σύνθετα (ἔστι γάρ τι ὑποκείμενον ἑκάστῳ, οἷον τῷ ποιῷ καὶ τῷ ποσῷ καὶ τῷ ποτὲ καὶ τῷ ποὺ καὶ τῇ κινήσει), σκεπτέον ἆρʼ ἔστι λόγος τοῦ τί ἦν εἶναι ἑκάστῳ αὐτῶν, καὶ ὑπάρχει καὶ τούτοις τὸ τί ἦν εἶναι, οἷον λευκῷ ἀνθρώπῳ . ἔστω δὴ ὄνομα αὐτῷ ἱμάτιον. τί ἐστι τὸ ἱματίῳ εἶναι; ἀλλὰ μὴν οὐδὲ τῶν καθʼ αὑτὸ λεγομένων οὐδὲ τοῦτο. ἢ τὸ οὐ καθʼ αὑτὸ λέγεται διχῶς, καὶ τούτου ἐστὶ τὸ μὲν ἐκ προσθέσεως τὸ δὲ οὔ. τὸ μὲν γὰρ τῷ αὐτὸ ἄλλῳ προσκεῖσθαι λέγεται ὃ ὁρίζεται, οἷον εἰ τὸ λευκῷ εἶναι ὁριζόμενος λέγοι λευκοῦ ἀνθρώπου λόγον· τὸ δὲ τῷ ἄλλο αὐτῷ, οἷον εἰ σημαίνοι τὸ ἱμάτιον λευκὸν ἄνθρωπον, ὁ δὲ ὁρίζοιτο ἱμάτιον ὡς λευκόν.","kind":"passage"},{"id":"RAW_DAZY_BOO7:P0012","text":"τὸ δὴ λευκὸς ἄνθρωπος ἔστι μὲν λευκόν,  οὐ μέντοι τὸ τί ἦν εἶναι λευκῷ εἶναι.—ἀλλὰ τὸ ἱματίῳ εἶναι ἆρά ἐστι τί ἦν εἶναί τι  ὅλως; ἢ οὔ; ὅπερ γάρ τί ἐστι τὸ τί ἦν εἶναι· ὅταν δʼ ἄλλο κατʼ ἄλλου λέγηται, οὐκ ἔστιν ὅπερ τόδε τι, οἷον ὁ λευκὸς ἄνθρωπος οὐκ ἔστιν ὅπερ τόδε τι, εἴπερ τὸ τόδε ταῖς οὐσίαις ὑπάρχει μόνον· ὥστε τὸ τί ἦν εἶναί ἐστιν ὅσων ὁ λόγος ἐστὶν ὁρισμός. ὁρισμὸς δʼ ἐστὶν οὐκ ἂν ὄνομα λόγῳ ταὐτὸ σημαίνῃ (πάντες γὰρ ἂν εἶεν οἱ λόγοι ὅροι· ἔσται γὰρ ὄνομα ὁτῳοῦν λόγῳ, ὥστε καὶ ἡ Ἰλιὰς ὁρισμὸς ἔσται), ἀλλʼ ἐὰν πρώτου τινὸς ᾖ· τοιαῦτα δʼ ἐστὶν ὅσα λέγεται μὴ τῷ ἄλλο κατʼ ἄλλου λέγεσθαι.","kind":"passage"},{"id":"RAW_DAZY_BOO7:P0013","text":"οὐκ ἔσται ἄρα οὐδενὶ τῶν μὴ γένους εἰδῶν ὑπάρχον τὸ τί ἦν εἶναι, ἀλλὰ τούτοις μόνον (ταῦτα γὰρ δοκεῖ οὐ κατὰ μετοχὴν λέγεσθαι καὶ πάθος οὐδʼ ὡς συμβεβηκός)· ἀλλὰ λόγος μὲν ἔσται ἑκάστου καὶ τῶν ἄλλων τί σημαίνει, ἐὰν ᾖ ὄνομα, ὅτι τόδε τῷδε ὑπάρχει, ἢ ἀντὶ λόγου ἁπλοῦ ἀκριβέστερος· ὁρισμὸς δʼ οὐκ ἔσται οὐδὲ τὸ τί ἦν εἶναι. ἢ καὶ ὁ ὁρισμὸς ὥσπερ καὶ τὸ τί ἐστι πλεοναχῶς λέγεται; καὶ γὰρ τὸ τί ἐστιν ἕνα μὲν τρόπον σημαίνει τὴν οὐσίαν καὶ τὸ τόδε τι, ἄλλον δὲ ἕκαστον τῶν κατηγορουμένων, ποσὸν ποιὸν καὶ ὅσα ἄλλα τοιαῦτα.","kind":"passage"},{"id":"RAW_DAZY_BOO7:P0014","text":"ὥσπερ γὰρ καὶ τὸ ἔστιν ὑπάρχει πᾶσιν, ἀλλʼ οὐχ ὁμοίως ἀλλὰ τῷ μὲν πρώτως τοῖς δʼ ἑπομένως, οὕτω καὶ τὸ τί ἐστιν ἁπλῶς μὲν τῇ οὐσίᾳ πὼς δὲ τοῖς ἄλλοις· καὶ γὰρ τὸ ποιὸν ἐροίμεθʼ ἂν τί ἐστιν, ὥστε καὶ τὸ ποιὸν τῶν τί ἐστιν, ἀλλʼ οὐχ ἁπλῶς, ἀλλʼ ὥσπερ ἐπὶ τοῦ μὴ ὄντος λογικῶς φασί τινες εἶναι τὸ μὴ ὄν, οὐχ ἁπλῶς ἀλλὰ μὴ ὄν, οὕτω καὶ τὸ ποιόν.—δεῖ μὲν οὖν σκοπεῖν καὶ τὸ πῶς δεῖ λέγειν περὶ ἕκαστον, οὐ μὴν μᾶλλόν γε ἢ τὸ πῶς ἔχει· διὸ καὶ νῦν ἐπεὶ τὸ λεγόμενον φανερόν, καὶ τὸ τί ἦν εἶναι ὁμοίως ὑπάρξει πρώτως μὲν καὶ ἁπλῶς τῇ οὐσίᾳ, εἶτα καὶ τοῖς ἄλλοις, ὥσπερ καὶ τὸ τί ἐστιν, οὐχ ἁπλῶς τί ἦν εἶναι ἀλλὰ ποιῷ ἢ ποσῷ τί ἦν εἶναι.","kind":"passage"},{"id":"RAW_DAZY_BOO7:P0015","text":"δεῖ γὰρ ἢ ὁμωνύμως ταῦτα φάναι εἶναι ὄντα, ἢ προστιθέντας καὶ ἀφαιροῦντας, ὥσπερ καὶ τὸ μὴ ἐπιστητὸν ἐπιστητόν, ἐπεὶ τό γε ὀρθόν ἐστι μήτε ὁμωνύμως φάναι μήτε ὡσαύτως ἀλλʼ ὥσπερ τὸ ἰατρικὸν τῷ πρὸς τὸ αὐτὸ μὲν καὶ ἕν, οὐ τὸ αὐτὸ δὲ καὶ ἕν, οὐ μέντοι οὐδὲ ὁμωνύμως·  οὐδὲ γὰρ ἰατρικὸν σῶμα καὶ ἔργον καὶ σκεῦος λέγεται οὔτε ὁμωνύμως οὔτε καθʼ ἓν ἀλλὰ πρὸς ἕν. ἀλλὰ ταῦτα μὲν ὁποτέρως τις ἐθέλει λέγειν διαφέρει οὐδέν· ἐκεῖνο δὲ φανερὸν ὅτι ὁ πρώτως καὶ ἁπλῶς ὁρισμὸς καὶ τὸ τί ἦν εἶναι τῶν οὐσιῶν ἐστίν. οὐ μὴν ἀλλὰ καὶ τῶν ἄλλων ὁμοίως ἐστί, πλὴν οὐ πρώτως. οὐ γὰρ ἀνάγκη, ἂν τοῦτο τιθῶμεν, τούτου ὁρισμὸν εἶναι ὃ ἂν λόγῳ τὸ αὐτὸ σημαίνῃ, ἀλλὰ τινὶ λόγῳ· τοῦτο δὲ ἐὰν ἑνὸς ᾖ, μὴ τῷ συνεχεῖ ὥσπερ ἡ Ἰλιὰς ἢ ὅσα συνδέσμω ι,ι ἀλλʼ ἐὰν ὁσαχῶς λέγεται τὸ ἕν· τὸ δʼ ἓν λέγεται ὥσπερ τὸ ὄν· τὸ δὲ ὂν τὸ μὲν τόδε τι τὸ δὲ ποσὸν τὸ δὲ ποιόν τι σημαίνει.","kind":"passage"},{"id":"RAW_DAZY_BOO7:P0016","text":"διὸ καὶ λευκοῦ ἀνθρώπου ἔσται λόγος καὶ ὁρισμός, ἄλλον δὲ τρόπον καὶ τοῦ λευκοῦ καὶ οὐσίας.","kind":"passage"},{"id":"RAW_DAZY_BOO7:P0017","text":"ἔχει δʼ ἀπορίαν, ἐάν τις μὴ φῇ ὁρισμὸν εἶναι τὸν ἐκ προσθέσεως λόγον, τίνος ἔσται ὁρισμὸς τῶν οὐχ ἁπλῶν ἀλλὰ συνδεδυασμένων· ἐκ προσθέσεως γὰρ ἀνάγκη δηλοῦν. λέγω δὲ οἷον ἔστι ῥὶς καὶ κοιλότης, καὶ σιμότης τὸ ἐκ τῶν δυοῖν λεγόμενον τῷ τόδε ἐν τῷδε, καὶ οὐ κατὰ συμβεβηκός γε οὔθʼ ἡ κοιλότης οὔθʼ ἡ σιμότης πάθος τῆς ῥινός, ἀλλὰ καθʼ αὑτήν· οὐδʼ ὡς τὸ λευκὸν Καλλίᾳ, ἢ ἀνθρώπῳ, ὅτι Καλλίας λευκὸς ᾧ συμβέβηκεν ἀνθρώπῳ εἶναι, ἀλλʼ ὡς τὸ ἄρρεν τῷ ζῴῳ καὶ τὸ ἴσον τῷ ποσῷ καὶ πάντα ὅσα λέγεται καθʼ αὑτὰ ὑπάρχειν. ταῦτα δʼ ἐστὶν ἐν ὅσοις ὑπάρχει ἢ ὁ λόγος ἢ τοὔνομα οὗ ἐστὶ τοῦτο τὸ πάθος, καὶ μὴ ἐνδέχεται δηλῶσαι χωρίς, ὥσπερ τὸ λευκὸν ἄνευ τοῦ ἀνθρώπου ἐνδέχεται ἀλλʼ οὐ τὸ θῆλυ ἄνευ τοῦ ζῴου· ὥστε τούτων τὸ τί ἦν εἶναι καὶ ὁρισμὸς ἢ οὐκ ἔστιν οὐδενὸς ἤ, εἰ ἔστιν, ἄλλως, καθάπερ εἰρήκαμεν. ἔστι δὲ ἀπορία καὶ ἑτέρα περὶ αὐτῶν.","kind":"passage"},{"id":"RAW_DAZY_BOO7:P0018","text":"εἰ μὲν γὰρ τὸ αὐτό ἐστι σιμὴ ῥὶς καὶ κοίλη ῥίς, τὸ αὐτὸ ἔσται τὸ σιμὸν καὶ τὸ κοῖλον· εἰ δὲ μή, διὰ τὸ ἀδύνατον εἶναι εἰπεῖν τὸ σιμὸν ἄνευ τοῦ πράγματος οὗ ἐστὶ πάθος καθʼ αὑτό (ἔστι γὰρ τὸ σιμὸν κοιλότης ἐν ῥινί), τὸ ῥῖνα σιμὴν εἰπεῖν ἢ οὐκ ἔστιν ἢ δὶς τὸ αὐτὸ ἔσται εἰρημένον, ῥὶς ῥὶς κοίλη (ἡ γὰρ ῥὶς ἡ σιμὴ ῥὶς ῥὶς κοίλη ἔσται), διὸ ἄτοπον τὸ ὑπάρχειν τοῖς τοιούτοις τὸ τί ἦν εἶναι· εἰ δὲ μή, εἰς ἄπειρον εἶσιν· ῥινὶ γὰρ ῥινὶ σιμῇ ἔτι ἄλλο ἐνέσται.  δῆλον τοίνυν ὅτι μόνης τῆς οὐσίας ἐστὶν ὁ ὁρισμός. εἰ γὰρ καὶ τῶν ἄλλων κατηγοριῶν, ἀνάγκη ἐκ προσθέσεως εἶναι, οἷον τοῦ †ποιοῦ† καὶ περιττοῦ· οὐ γὰρ ἄνευ ἀριθμοῦ, οὐδὲ τὸ θῆλυ ἄνευ ζῴου (τὸ δὲ ἐκ προσθέσεως λέγω ἐν οἷς συμβαίνει δὶς τὸ αὐτὸ λέγειν ὥσπερ ἐν τούτοις). εἰ δὲ τοῦτο ἀληθές, οὐδὲ συνδυαζομένων ἔσται, οἷον ἀριθμοῦ περιττοῦ· ἀλλὰ λανθάνει ὅτι οὐκ ἀκριβῶς λέγονται οἱ λόγοι.","kind":"passage"},{"id":"RAW_DAZY_BOO7:P0019","text":"εἰ δʼ εἰσὶ καὶ τούτων ὅροι, ἤτοι ἄλλον τρόπον εἰσὶν ἢ καθάπερ ἐλέχθη πολλαχῶς λεκτέον εἶναι τὸν ὁρισμὸν καὶ τὸ τί ἦν εἶναι, ὥστε ὡδὶ μὲν οὐδενὸς ἔσται ὁρισμὸς οὐδὲ τὸ τί ἦν εἶναι οὐδενὶ ὑπάρξει πλὴν ταῖς οὐσίαις, ὡδὶ δʼ ἔσται. ὅτι μὲν οὖν ἐστὶν ὁ ὁρισμὸς ὁ τοῦ τί ἦν εἶναι λόγος, καὶ τὸ τί ἦν εἶναι ἢ μόνων τῶν οὐσιῶν ἐστὶν ἢ μάλιστα καὶ πρώτως καὶ ἁπλῶς, δῆλον.","kind":"passage"},{"id":"RAW_DAZY_BOO7:P0020","text":"πότερον δὲ ταὐτόν ἐστιν ἢ ἕτερον τὸ τί ἦν εἶναι καὶ ἕκαστον, σκεπτέον. ἔστι γάρ τι πρὸ ἔργου πρὸς τὴν περὶ τῆς οὐσίας σκέψιν· ἕκαστόν τε γὰρ οὐκ ἄλλο δοκεῖ εἶναι τῆς ἑαυτοῦ οὐσίας, καὶ τὸ τί ἦν εἶναι λέγεται εἶναι ἡ ἑκάστου οὐσία.","kind":"passage"},{"id":"RAW_DAZY_BOO7:P0021","text":"ἐπὶ μὲν δὴ τῶν λεγομένων κατὰ συμβεβηκὸς δόξειεν ἂν ἕτερον εἶναι, οἷον λευκὸς ἄνθρωπος ἕτερον καὶ τὸ λευκῷ ἀνθρώπῳ εἶναι (εἰ γὰρ τὸ αὐτό, καὶ τὸ ἀνθρώπῳ εἶναι καὶ τὸ λευκῷ ἀνθρώπῳ τὸ αὐτό· τὸ αὐτὸ γὰρ ἄνθρωπος καὶ λευκὸς ἄνθρωπος, ὡς φασίν, ὥστε καὶ τὸ λευκῷ ἀνθρώπῳ καὶ τὸ ἀνθρώπῳ· ἢ οὐκ ἀνάγκη ὅσα κατὰ συμβεβηκὸς εἶναι ταὐτά, οὐ γὰρ ὡσαύτως τὰ ἄκρα γίγνεται ταὐτά· ἀλλʼ ἴσως γε ἐκεῖνο δόξειεν ἂν συμβαίνειν, τὰ ἄκρα γίγνεσθαι ταὐτὰ τὰ κατὰ συμβεβηκός, οἷον τὸ λευκῷ εἶναι καὶ τὸ μουσικῷ· δοκεῖ δὲ οὔ)· ἐπὶ δὲ τῶν καθʼ αὑτὰ λεγομένων ἆρʼ ἀνάγκη ταὐτὸ εἶναι, οἷον εἴ τινες εἰσὶν οὐσίαι ὧν ἕτεραι μὴ εἰσὶν οὐσίαι μηδὲ φύσεις ἕτεραι πρότεραι, οἵας φασὶ τὰς ἰδέας εἶναί τινες; εἰ γὰρ ἔσται ἕτερον αὐτὸ τὸ ἀγαθὸν καὶ τὸ ἀγαθῷ εἶναι, καὶ ζῷον καὶ τὸ ζῴῳ, καὶ τὸ ὄντι καὶ τὸ ὄν,  ἔσονται ἄλλαι τε οὐσίαι καὶ φύσεις καὶ ἰδέαι παρὰ τὰς λεγομένας, καὶ πρότεραι οὐσίαι ἐκεῖναι, εἰ τὸ τί ἦν εἶναι οὐσία ἐστίν.","kind":"passage"},{"id":"RAW_DAZY_BOO7:P0022","text":"καὶ εἰ μὲν ἀπολελυμέναι ἀλλήλων, τῶν μὲν οὐκ ἔσται ἐπιστήμη τὰ δʼ οὐκ ἔσται ὄντα (λέγω δὲ τὸ ἀπολελύσθαι εἰ μήτε τῷ ἀγαθῷ αὐτῷ ὑπάρχει τὸ εἶναι ἀγαθῷ μήτε τούτῳ τὸ εἶναι ἀγαθόν)· ἐπιστήμη τε γὰρ ἑκάστου ἔστιν ὅταν τὸ τί ἦν ἐκείνῳ εἶναι γνῶμεν, καὶ ἐπὶ ἀγαθοῦ καὶ τῶν ἄλλων ὁμοίως ἔχει, ὥστε εἰ μηδὲ τὸ ἀγαθῷ εἶναι ἀγαθόν, οὐδὲ τὸ ὄντι ὂν οὐδὲ τὸ ἑνὶ ἕν· ὁμοίως δὲ πάντα ἔστιν ἢ οὐθὲν τὰ τί ἦν εἶναι, ὥστʼ εἰ μηδὲ τὸ ὄντι ὄν, οὐδὲ τῶν ἄλλων οὐδέν. ἔτι ᾧ μὴ ὑπάρχει ἀγαθῷ εἶναι, οὐκ ἀγαθόν.","kind":"passage"},{"id":"RAW_DAZY_BOO7:P0023","text":"ἀνάγκη ἄρα ἓν εἶναι τὸ ἀγαθὸν καὶ ἀγαθῷ εἶναι καὶ καλὸν καὶ καλῷ εἶναι, καὶ ὅσα μὴ κατʼ ἄλλο λέγεται, ἀλλὰ καθʼ αὑτὰ καὶ πρῶτα· καὶ γὰρ τοῦτο ἱκανὸν ἂν ὑπάρχῃ, κἂν μὴ ᾖ εἴδη, μᾶλλον δʼ ἴσως κἂν ᾖ εἴδη (ἅμα δὲ δῆλον καὶ ὅτι εἴπερ εἰσὶν αἱ ἰδέαι οἵας τινές φασιν, οὐκ ἔσται τὸ ὑποκείμενον οὐσία· ταύτας γὰρ οὐσίας μὲν ἀναγκαῖον εἶναι, μὴ καθʼ ὑποκειμένου δέ· ἔσονται γὰρ κατὰ μέθεξιν).—ἔκ τε δὴ τούτων τῶν λόγων ἓν καὶ ταὐτὸ οὐ κατὰ συμβεβηκὸς αὐτὸ ἕκαστον καὶ τὸ τί ἦν εἶναι, καὶ ὅτι γε τὸ ἐπίστασθαι ἕκαστον τοῦτό ἐστι, τὸ τί ἦν εἶναι ἐπίστασθαι, ὥστε καὶ κατὰ τὴν ἔκθεσιν ἀνάγκη ἕν τι εἶναι ἄμφω (τὸ δὲ κατὰ συμβεβηκὸς λεγόμενον, οἷον τὸ μουσικὸν ἢ λευκόν, διὰ τὸ διττὸν σημαίνειν οὐκ ἀληθὲς εἰπεῖν ὡς ταὐτὸ τὸ τί ἦν εἶναι καὶ αὐτό· καὶ γὰρ ᾧ συμβέβηκε λευκὸν καὶ τὸ συμβεβηκός, ὥστʼ ἔστι μὲν ὡς ταὐτόν, ἔστι δὲ ὡς οὐ ταὐτὸ τὸ τί ἦν εἶναι καὶ αὐτό· τῷ μὲν γὰρ ἀνθρώπῳ καὶ τῷ λευκῷ ἀνθρώπῳ οὐ ταὐτό,","kind":"passage"},{"id":"RAW_DAZY_BOO7:P0024","text":"τῷ πάθει δὲ ταὐτό). ἄτοπον δʼ ἂν φανείη κἂν εἴ τις ἑκάστῳ ὄνομα θεῖτο τῶν τί ἦν εἶναι· ἔσται γὰρ καὶ παρʼ ἐκεῖνο ἄλλο, οἷον τῷ τί ἦν εἶναι ἵππῳ τί ἦν εἶναι  ἕτερον. καίτοι τί κωλύει καὶ νῦν εἶναι ἔνια εὐθὺς τί ἦν εἶναι, εἴπερ οὐσία τὸ τί ἦν εἶναι; ἀλλὰ μὴν οὐ μόνον ἕν, ἀλλὰ καὶ ὁ λόγος ὁ αὐτὸς αὐτῶν, ὡς δῆλον καὶ ἐκ τῶν εἰρημένων·  οὐ γὰρ κατὰ συμβεβηκὸς ἓν τὸ ἑνὶ εἶναι καὶ ἕν. ἔτι εἰ ἄλλο ἔσται, εἰς ἄπειρον εἶσιν· τὸ μὲν γὰρ ἔσται τί ἦν εἶναι τοῦ ἑνὸς τὸ δὲ τὸ ἕν, ὥστε καὶ ἐπʼ ἐκείνων ὁ αὐτὸς ἔσται λόγος. ὅτι μὲν οὖν ἐπὶ τῶν πρώτων καὶ καθʼ αὑτὰ λεγομένων τὸ ἑκάστῳ εἶναι καὶ ἕκαστον τὸ αὐτὸ καὶ ἕν ἐστι, δῆλον· οἱ δὲ σοφιστικοὶ ἔλεγχοι πρὸς τὴν θέσιν ταύτην φανερὸν ὅτι τῇ αὐτῇ λύονται λύσει καὶ εἰ ταὐτὸ Σωκράτης καὶ Σωκράτει εἶναι· οὐδὲν γὰρ διαφέρει οὔτε ἐξ ὧν ἐρωτήσειεν ἄν τις οὔτε ἐξ ὧν λύων ἐπιτύχοι. πῶς μὲν οὖν τὸ τί ἦν εἶναι ταὐτὸν καὶ πῶς οὐ ταὐτὸν ἑκάστῳ, εἴρηται.","kind":"passage"},{"id":"RAW_DAZY_BOO7:P0025","text":"τῶν δὲ γιγνομένων τὰ μὲν φύσει γίγνεται τὰ δὲ τέχνῃ τὰ δὲ ἀπὸ ταὐτομάτου, πάντα δὲ τὰ γιγνόμενα ὑπό τέ τινος γίγνεται καὶ ἔκ τινος καὶ τί· τὸ δὲ τὶ λέγω καθʼ ἑκάστην κατηγορίαν· ἢ γὰρ τόδε ἢ ποσὸν ἢ ποιὸν ἢ πού. αἱ δὲ γενέσεις αἱ μὲν φυσικαὶ αὗταί εἰσιν ὧν ἡ γένεσις ἐκ φύσεώς ἐστιν, τὸ δʼ ἐξ οὗ γίγνεται, ἣν λέγομεν ὕλην, τὸ δὲ ὑφʼ οὗ τῶν φύσει τι ὄντων, τὸ δὲ τὶ ἄνθρωπος ἢ φυτὸν ἢ ἄλλο τι τῶν τοιούτων, ἃ δὴ μάλιστα λέγομεν οὐσίας εἶναι —ἅπαντα δὲ τὰ γιγνόμενα ἢ φύσει ἢ τέχνῃ ἔχει ὕλην· δυνατὸν γὰρ καὶ εἶναι καὶ μὴ εἶναι ἕκαστον αὐτῶν, τοῦτο δʼ ἐστὶν ἡ ἐν ἑκάστῳ ὕλη—καθόλου δὲ καὶ ἐξ οὗ φύσις καὶ καθʼ ὃ φύσις (τὸ γὰρ γιγνόμενον ἔχει φύσιν, οἷον φυτὸν ἢ ζῷον) καὶ ὑφʼ οὗ ἡ κατὰ τὸ εἶδος λεγομένη φύσις ἡ ὁμοειδής (ἁὕτη δὲ ἐν ἄλλῳ)· ἄνθρωπος γὰρ ἄνθρωπον γεννᾷ·—οὕτω μὲν οὖν γίγνεται τὰ γιγνόμενα διὰ τὴν φύσιν, αἱ δʼ ἄλλαι γενέσεις λέγονται ποιήσεις.","kind":"passage"},{"id":"RAW_DAZY_BOO7:P0026","text":"πᾶσαι δὲ εἰσὶν αἱ ποιήσεις ἢ ἀπὸ τέχνης ἢ ἀπὸ δυνάμεως ἢ ἀπὸ διανοίας. τούτων δέ τινες γίγνονται καὶ ἀπὸ ταὐτομάτου καὶ ἀπὸ τύχης παραπλησίως ὥσπερ ἐν τοῖς ἀπὸ φύσεως γιγνομένοις· ἔνια γὰρ κἀκεῖ ταὐτὰ καὶ ἐκ σπέρματος γίγνεται καὶ ἄνευ σπέρματος. περὶ μὲν οὖν τούτων ὕστερον ἐπισκεπτέον,  ἀπὸ τέχνης δὲ γίγνεται ὅσων τὸ εἶδος ἐν τῇ ψυχῇ (εἶδος δὲ λέγω τὸ τί ἦν εἶναι ἑκάστου καὶ τὴν πρώτην οὐσίαν)· καὶ γὰρ τῶν ἐναντίων τρόπον τινὰ τὸ αὐτὸ εἶδος· τῆς γὰρ στερήσεως οὐσία ἡ οὐσία ἡ ἀντικειμένη, οἷον ὑγίεια νόσου, ἐκείνης γὰρ ἀπουσία ἡ νόσος, ἡ δὲ ὑγίεια ὁ ἐν τῇ ψυχῇ λόγος καὶ ἡ ἐπιστήμη. γίγνεται δὲ τὸ ὑγιὲς νοήσαντος οὕτως· ἐπειδὴ τοδὶ ὑγίεια, ἀνάγκη εἰ ὑγιὲς ἔσται τοδὶ ὑπάρξαι, οἷον ὁμαλότητα, εἰ δὲ τοῦτο, θερμότητα· καὶ οὕτως ἀεὶ νοεῖ, ἕως ἂν ἀγάγῃ εἰς τοῦτο ὃ αὐτὸς δύναται ἔσχατον ποιεῖν. εἶτα ἤδη ἡ ἀπὸ τούτου κίνησις ποίησις καλεῖται, ἡ ἐπὶ τὸ ὑγιαίνειν.","kind":"passage"},{"id":"RAW_DAZY_BOO7:P0027","text":"ὥστε συμβαίνει τρόπον τινὰ τὴν ὑγίειαν ἐξ ὑγιείας γίγνεσθαι καὶ τὴν οἰκίαν ἐξ οἰκίας, τῆς ἄνευ ὕλης τὴν ἔχουσαν ὕλην· ἡ γὰρ ἰατρική ἐστι καὶ ἡ οἰκοδομικὴ τὸ εἶδος τῆς ὑγιείας καὶ τῆς οἰκίας, λέγω δὲ οὐσίαν ἄνευ ὕλης τὸ τί ἦν εἶναι.","kind":"passage"},{"id":"RAW_DAZY_BOO7:P0028","text":"τῶν δὴ γενέσεων καὶ κινήσεων ἡ μὲν νόησις καλεῖται ἡ δὲ ποίησις, ἡ μὲν ἀπὸ τῆς ἀρχῆς καὶ τοῦ εἴδους νόησις ἡ δʼ ἀπὸ τοῦ τελευταίου τῆς νοήσεως ποίησις. ὁμοίως δὲ καὶ τῶν ἄλλων τῶν μεταξὺ ἕκαστον γίγνεται. λέγω δʼ οἷον εἰ ὑγιανεῖ, δέοι ἂν ὁμαλυνθῆναι. τί οὖν ἐστὶ τὸ ὁμαλυνθῆναι; τοδί, τοῦτο δʼ ἔσται εἰ θερμανθήσεται. τοῦτο δὲ τί ἐστι; τοδί. ὑπάρχει δὲ τοδὶ δυνάμει· τοῦτο δὲ ἤδη ἐπʼ αὐτῷ.","kind":"passage"},{"id":"RAW_DAZY_BOO7:P0029","text":"τὸ δὴ ποιοῦν καὶ ὅθεν ἄρχεται ἡ κίνησις τοῦ ὑγιαίνειν, ἂν μὲν ἀπὸ τέχνης, τὸ εἶδός ἐστι τὸ ἐν τῇ ψυχῇ, ἐὰν δʼ ἀπὸ ταὐτομάτου, ἀπὸ τούτου ὅ ποτε τοῦ ποιεῖν ἄρχει τῷ ποιοῦντι ἀπὸ τέχνης, ὥσπερ καὶ ἐν τῷ ἰατρεύειν ἴσως ἀπὸ τοῦ θερμαίνειν ἡ ἀρχή (τοῦτο δὲ ποιεῖ τῇ τρίψει)· ἡ θερμότης τοίνυν ἡ ἐν τῷ σώματι ἢ μέρος τῆς ὑγιείας ἢ ἕπεταί τι αὐτῇ τοιοῦτον ὅ ἐστι μέρος τῆς ὑγιείας, ἢ διὰ πλειόνων· τοῦτο δʼ ἔσχατόν ἐστι, τὸ ποιοῦν τὸ μέρος τῆς ὑγιείας,—καὶ τῆς οἰκίας (ὁἷον οἱ λίθοι) καὶ τῶν ἄλλων· ὥστε, καθάπερ λέγεται, ἀδύνατον γενέσθαι εἰ μηδὲν προϋπάρχοι. ὅτι μὲν οὖν τι μέρος ἐξ ἀνάγκης ὑπάρξει φανερόν· ἡ γὰρ ὕλη μέρος (ἐνυπάρχει γὰρ καὶ γίγνεται αὕτη).  ἀλλʼ ἆρα καὶ τῶν ἐν τῷ λόγῳ; ἀμφοτέρως δὴ λέγομεν τοὺς χαλκοῦς κύκλους τί εἰσι, καὶ τὴν ὕλην λέγοντες ὅτι χαλκός, καὶ τὸ εἶδος ὅτι σχῆμα τοιόνδε, καὶ τοῦτό ἐστι τὸ γένος εἰς ὃ πρῶτον τίθεται.","kind":"passage"},{"id":"RAW_DAZY_BOO7:P0030","text":"ὁ δὴ χαλκοῦς κύκλος ἔχει ἐν τῷ λόγῳ τὴν ὕλην.—ἐξ οὗ δὲ ὡς ὕλης γίγνεται ἔνια λέγεται, ὅταν γένηται, οὐκ ἐκεῖνο ἀλλʼ ἐκείνινον, οἷον ὁ ἀνδριὰς οὐ λίθος ἀλλὰ λίθινος, ὁ δὲ ἄνθρωπος ὁ ὑγιαίνων οὐ λέγεται ἐκεῖνο ἐξ οὗ· αἴτιον δὲ ὅτι γίγνεται ἐκ τῆς στερήσεως καὶ τοῦ ὑποκειμένου, ὃ λέγομεν τὴν ὕλην (οἷον καὶ ὁ ἄνθρωπος καὶ ὁ κάμνων γίγνεται ὑγιής), μᾶλλον μέντοι λέγεται γίγνεσθαι ἐκ τῆς στερήσεως, οἷον ἐκ κάμνοντος ὑγιὴς ἢ ἐξ ἀνθρώπου, διὸ κάμνων μὲν ὁ ὑγιὴς οὐ λέγεται, ἄνθρωπος δέ, καὶ ὁ ἄνθρωπος ὑγιής· ὧν δʼ ἡ στέρησις ἄδηλος καὶ ἀνώνυμος, οἷον ἐν χαλκῷ σχήματος ὁποιουοῦν ἢ ἐν πλίνθοις καὶ ξύλοις οἰκίας, ἐκ τούτων δοκεῖ γίγνεσθαι ὡς ἐκεῖ ἐκ κάμνοντος· διὸ ὥσπερ οὐδʼ ἐκεῖ ἐξ οὗ τοῦτο, ἐκεῖνο οὐ λέγεται, οὐδʼ ἐνταῦθα ὁ ἀνδριὰς ξύλον, ἀλλὰ παράγεται ξύλινος,  καὶ χαλκοῦς ἀλλʼ οὐ χαλκός, καὶ λίθινος ἀλλʼ οὐ λίθος, καὶ ἡ οἰκία πλινθίνη ἀλλʼ οὐ πλίνθοι, ἐπεὶ οὐδὲ ὡς ἐκ ξύλου γίγνεται ἀνδριὰς ἢ","kind":"passage"},{"id":"RAW_DAZY_BOO7:P0031","text":"ἐκ πλίνθων οἰκία, ἐάν τις ἐπιβλέπῃ σφόδρα, οὐκ ἂν ἁπλῶς εἴπειεν, διὰ τὸ δεῖν μεταβάλλοντος γίγνεσθαι ἐξ οὗ, ἀλλʼ οὐχ ὑπομένοντος. διὰ μὲν οὖν τοῦτο οὕτως λέγεται.","kind":"passage"},{"id":"RAW_DAZY_BOO7:P0032","text":"ἐπεὶ δὲ ὑπό τινός τε γίγνεται τὸ γιγνόμενον (τοῦτο δὲ λέγω ὅθεν ἡ ἀρχὴ τῆς γενέσεώς ἐστι) καὶ ἔκ τινος (ἔστω δὲ μὴ ἡ στέρησις τοῦτο ἀλλʼ ἡ ὕλη· ἤδη γὰρ διώρισται ὃν τρόπον τοῦτο λέγομεν) καὶ τὶ γίγνεται (τοῦτο δʼ ἐστὶν ἢ σφαῖρα ἢ κύκλος ἢ ὅ τι ἔτυχε τῶν ἄλλων), ὥσπερ οὐδὲ τὸ ὑποκείμενον ποιεῖ, τὸν χαλκόν, οὕτως οὐδὲ τὴν σφαῖραν, εἰ μὴ κατὰ συμβεβηκὸς ὅτι ἡ χαλκῆ σφαῖρα σφαῖρά ἐστιν ἐκείνην δὲ ποιεῖ. τὸ γὰρ τόδε τι ποιεῖν ἐκ τοῦ ὅλως ὑποκειμένου τόδε τι ποιεῖν ἐστίν (λέγω δʼ ὅτι τὸν χαλκὸν στρογγύλον ποιεῖν ἐστὶν οὐ τὸ στρογγύλον ἢ τὴν σφαῖραν ποιεῖν ἀλλʼ ἕτερόν τι, οἷον τὸ εἶδος τοῦτο ἐν ἄλλῳ· εἰ γὰρ ποιεῖ, ἔκ τινος ἂν ποιοίη ἄλλου, τοῦτο γὰρ ὑπέκειτο·  οἷον ποιεῖ χαλκῆν σφαῖραν, τοῦτο δὲ οὕτως ὅτι ἐκ τουδί, ὅ ἐστι χαλκός, τοδὶ ποιεῖ, ὅ ἐστι σφαῖρα)· εἰ οὖν καὶ τοῦτο ποιεῖ αὐτό, δῆλον ὅτι ὡσαύτως ποιήσει, καὶ βαδιοῦνται αἱ γενέσεις εἰς ἄπειρον.","kind":"passage"},{"id":"RAW_DAZY_BOO7:P0033","text":"φανερὸν ἄρα ὅτι οὐδὲ τὸ εἶδος, ἢ ὁτιδήποτε χρὴ καλεῖν τὴν ἐν τῷ αἰσθητῷ μορφήν, οὐ γίγνεται, οὐδʼ ἔστιν αὐτοῦ γένεσις, οὐδὲ τὸ τί ἦν εἶναι (τοῦτο γάρ ἐστιν ὃ ἐν ἄλλῳ γίγνεται ἢ ὑπὸ τέχνης ἢ ὑπὸ φύσεως ἢ δυνάμεως). τὸ δὲ χαλκῆν σφαῖραν εἶναι ποιεῖ· ποιεῖ γὰρ ἐκ χαλκοῦ καὶ σφαίρας· εἰς τοδὶ γὰρ τὸ εἶδος ποιεῖ, καὶ ἔστι τοῦτο σφαῖρα χαλκῆ. τοῦ δὲ σφαίρᾳ εἶναι ὅλως εἰ ἔσται γένεσις, ἔκ τινος τὶ ἔσται. δεήσει γὰρ διαιρετὸν εἶναι ἀεὶ τὸ γιγνόμενον, καὶ εἶναι τὸ μὲν τόδε τὸ δὲ τόδε, λέγω δʼ ὅτι τὸ μὲν ὕλην τὸ δὲ εἶδος. εἰ δή ἐστι σφαῖρα τὸ ἐκ τοῦ μέσου σχῆμα ἴσον, τούτου τὸ μὲν ἐν ᾧ ἔσται ὃ ποιεῖ, τὸ δʼ ἐν ἐκείνῳ, τὸ δὲ ἅπαν τὸ γεγονός, οἷον ἡ χαλκῆ σφαῖρα.","kind":"passage"},{"id":"RAW_DAZY_BOO7:P0034","text":"φανερὸν δὴ ἐκ τῶν εἰρημένων ὅτι τὸ μὲν ὡς εἶδος ἢ οὐσία λεγόμενον οὐ γίγνεται, ἡ δὲ σύνολος ἡ κατὰ ταύτην λεγομένη γίγνεται, καὶ ὅτι ἐν παντὶ τῷ γεννωμένῳ ὕλη ἔνεστι, καὶ ἔστι τὸ μὲν τόδε τὸ δὲ τόδε.—πότερον οὖν ἔστι τις σφαῖρα παρὰ τάσδε ἢ οἰκία παρὰ τὰς πλίνθους; ἢ οὐδʼ ἄν ποτε ἐγίγνετο, εἰ οὕτως ἦν, τόδε τι, ἀλλὰ τὸ τοιόνδε σημαίνει, τόδε δὲ καὶ ὡρισμένον οὐκ ἔστιν, ἀλλὰ ποιεῖ καὶ γεννᾷ ἐκ τοῦδε τοιόνδε, καὶ ὅταν γεννηθῇ, ἔστι τόδε τοιόνδε; τὸ δὲ ἅπαν τόδε, Καλλίας ἢ Σωκράτης, ἐστὶν ὥσπερ ἡ σφαῖρα ἡ χαλκῆ ἡδί, ὁ δʼ ἄνθρωπος καὶ τὸ ζῷον ὥσπερ σφαῖρα χαλκῆ ὅλως. φανερὸν ἄρα ὅτι ἡ τῶν εἰδῶν αἰτία, ὡς εἰώθασί τινες λέγειν τὰ εἴδη, εἰ ἔστιν ἄττα παρὰ τὰ καθʼ ἕκαστα, πρός γε τὰς γενέσεις καὶ τὰς οὐσίας οὐθὲν χρησίμη· οὐδʼ ἂν εἶεν διά γε ταῦτα οὐσίαι καθʼ αὑτάς.","kind":"passage"},{"id":"RAW_DAZY_BOO7:P0035","text":"ἐπὶ μὲν δή τινων καὶ φανερὸν ὅτι τὸ γεννῶν τοιοῦτον μὲν οἷον τὸ γεννώμενον, οὐ μέντοι τὸ αὐτό γε, οὐδὲ ἓν τῷ ἀριθμῷ ἀλλὰ τῷ εἴδει, οἷον ἐν τοῖς φυσικοῖς—ἄνθρωπος γὰρ ἄνθρωπον γεννᾷ—ἂν μή τι παρὰ φύσιν γένηται, οἷον ἵππος ἡμίονον (καὶ ταῦτα δὲ ὁμοίως· ὃ γὰρ ἂν κοινὸν εἴη ἐφʼ ἵππου καὶ ὄνου οὐκ ὠνόμασται, τὸ ἐγγύτατα γένος, εἴη δʼ ἂν ἄμφω ἴσως, οἷον ἡμίονος)·  ὥστε φανερὸν ὅτι οὐθὲν δεῖ ὡς παράδειγμα εἶδος κατασκευάζειν (μάλιστα γὰρ ἂν ἐν τούτοις ἐπεζητοῦντο· οὐσίαι γὰρ αἱ μάλιστα αὗται) ἀλλὰ ἱκανὸν τὸ γεννῶν ποιῆσαι καὶ τοῦ εἴδους αἴτιον εἶναι ἐν τῇ ὕλῃ. τὸ δʼ ἅπαν ἤδη, τὸ τοιόνδε εἶδος ἐν ταῖσδε ταῖς σαρξὶ καὶ ὀστοῖς, Καλλίας καὶ Σωκράτης· καὶ ἕτερον μὲν διὰ τὴν ὕλην (ἑτέρα γάρ), ταὐτὸ δὲ τῷ εἴδει (ἄτομον γὰρ τὸ εἶδος).","kind":"passage"},{"id":"RAW_DAZY_BOO7:P0036","text":"ἀπορήσειε δʼ ἄν τις διὰ τί τὰ μὲν γίγνεται καὶ τέχνῃ καὶ ἀπὸ ταὐτομάτου, οἷον ὑγίεια, τὰ δʼ οὔ, οἷον οἰκία. αἴτιον δὲ ὅτι τῶν μὲν ἡ ὕλη ἡ ἄρχουσα τῆς γενέσεως ἐν τῷ ποιεῖν καὶ γίγνεσθαί τι τῶν ἀπὸ τέχνης, ἐν ᾗ ὑπάρχει τι μέρος τοῦ πράγματος,—ἡ μὲν τοιαύτη ἐστὶν οἵα κινεῖσθαι ὑφʼ αὑτῆς ἡ δʼ οὔ, καὶ ταύτης ἡ μὲν ὡδὶ οἵα τε ἡ δὲ ἀδύνατος· πολλὰ γὰρ δυνατὰ μὲν ὑφʼ αὑτῶν κινεῖσθαι ἀλλʼ οὐχ ὡδί, οἷον ὀρχήσασθαι. ὅσων οὖν τοιαύτη ἡ ὕλη, οἷον οἱ λίθοι, ἀδύνατον ὡδὶ κινηθῆναι εἰ μὴ ὑπʼ ἄλλου, ὡδὶ μέντοι ναί—καὶ τὸ πῦρ. διὰ τοῦτο τὰ μὲν οὐκ ἔσται ἄνευ τοῦ ἔχοντος τὴν τέχνην τὰ δὲ ἔσται· ὑπὸ γὰρ τούτων κινηθήσεται τῶν οὐκ ἐχόντων τὴν τέχνην, κινεῖσθαι δὲ δυναμένων αὐτῶν ὑπʼ ἄλλων οὐκ ἐχόντων τὴν τέχνην ἢ ἐκ μέρους.","kind":"passage"},{"id":"RAW_DAZY_BOO7:P0037","text":"δῆλον δʼ ἐκ τῶν εἰρημένων καὶ ὅτι τρόπον τινὰ πάντα γίγνεται ἐξ ὁμωνύμου, ὥσπερ τὰ φύσει, ἢ ἐκ μέρους ὁμωνύμου (οἷον ἡ οἰκία ἐξ οἰκίας, ᾗ ὑπὸ νοῦ· ἡ γὰρ τέχνη τὸ εἶδος)  ἢ ἔχοντός τι μέρος,—ἐὰν μὴ κατὰ συμβεβηκὸς γίγνηται· τὸ γὰρ αἴτιον τοῦ ποιεῖν πρῶτον καθʼ αὑτὸ μέρος. θερμότης γὰρ ἡ ἐν τῇ κινήσει θερμότητα ἐν τῷ σώματι ἐποίησεν· αὕτη δὲ ἐστὶν ἢ ὑγίεια ἢ μέρος, ἢ ἀκολουθεῖ αὐτῇ μέρος τι τῆς ὑγιείας ἢ αὐτὴ ἡ ὑγίεια· διὸ καὶ λέγεται ποιεῖν, ὅτι ἐκεῖνο ποιεῖ  ᾧ ἀκολουθεῖ καὶ συμβέβηκε . ὥστε, ὥσπερ ἐν τοῖς συλλογισμοῖς, πάντων ἀρχὴ ἡ οὐσία· ἐκ γὰρ τοῦ τί ἐστιν οἱ συλλογισμοί εἰσιν, ἐνταῦθα δὲ αἱ γενέσεις. ὁμοίως δὲ καὶ τὰ φύσει συνιστάμενα τούτοις ἔχει.","kind":"passage"},{"id":"RAW_DAZY_BOO7:P0038","text":"τὸ μὲν γὰρ σπέρμα ποιεῖ ὥσπερ τὰ ἀπὸ τέχνης (ἔχει γὰρ δυνάμει τὸ εἶδος,  καὶ ἀφʼ οὗ τὸ σπέρμα, ἐστί πως ὁμώνυμον—οὐ γὰρ πάντα οὕτω δεῖ ζητεῖν ὡς ἐξ ἀνθρώπου ἄνθρωπος· καὶ γὰρ γυνὴ ἐξ ἀνδρός—ἐὰν μὴ πήρωμα ᾖ· διὸ ἡμίονος οὐκ ἐξ ἡμιόνου)· ὅσα δὲ ἀπὸ ταὐτομάτου ὥσπερ ἐκεῖ γίγνεται, ὅσων ἡ ὕλη δύναται καὶ ὑφʼ αὑτῆς κινεῖσθαι ταύτην τὴν κίνησιν ἣν τὸ σπέρμα κινεῖ· ὅσων δὲ μή, ταῦτα ἀδύνατα γίγνεσθαι ἄλλως πως ἢ ἐξ αὐτῶν.—οὐ μόνον δὲ περὶ τῆς οὐσίας ὁ λόγος δηλοῖ τὸ μὴ γίγνεσθαι τὸ εἶδος, ἀλλὰ περὶ πάντων ὁμοίως τῶν πρώτων κοινὸς ὁ λόγος, οἷον ποσοῦ ποιοῦ καὶ τῶν ἄλλων κατηγοριῶν. γίγνεται γὰρ ὥσπερ ἡ χαλκῆ σφαῖρα ἀλλʼ οὐ σφαῖρα οὐδὲ χαλκός, καὶ ἐπὶ χαλκοῦ, εἰ γίγνεται (ἀεὶ γὰρ δεῖ προϋπάρχειν τὴν ὕλην καὶ τὸ εἶδος), οὕτως καὶ ἐπὶ τοῦ τί ἐστι καὶ ἐπὶ τοῦ ποιοῦ καὶ ποσοῦ καὶ τῶν ἄλλων ὁμοίως κατηγοριῶν· οὐ γὰρ γίγνεται τὸ ποιὸν ἀλλὰ τὸ ποιὸν ξύλον, οὐδὲ τὸ ποσὸν ἀλλὰ τὸ ποσὸν ξύλον ἢ ζῷον.","kind":"passage"},{"id":"RAW_DAZY_BOO7:P0039","text":"ἀλλʼ ἴδιον τῆς οὐσίας ἐκ τούτων λαβεῖν ἔστιν ὅτι ἀναγκαῖον προϋπάρχειν ἑτέραν οὐσίαν ἐντελεχείᾳ οὖσαν ἣ ποιεῖ, οἷον ζῷον εἰ γίγνεται ζῷον· ποιὸν δʼ ἢ ποσὸν οὐκ ἀνάγκη ἀλλʼ ἢ δυνάμει μόνον.","kind":"passage"},{"id":"RAW_DAZY_BOO7:P0040","text":"ἐπεὶ δὲ ὁ ὁρισμὸς λόγος ἐστί, πᾶς δὲ λόγος μέρη ἔχει, ὡς δὲ ὁ λόγος πρὸς τὸ πρᾶγμα, καὶ τὸ μέρος τοῦ λόγου πρὸς τὸ μέρος τοῦ πράγματος ὁμοίως ἔχει, ἀπορεῖται ἤδη πότερον δεῖ τὸν τῶν μερῶν λόγον ἐνυπάρχειν ἐν τῷ τοῦ ὅλου λόγῳ ἢ οὔ. ἐπʼ ἐνίων μὲν γὰρ φαίνονται ἐνόντες ἐνίων δʼ οὔ. τοῦ μὲν γὰρ κύκλου ὁ λόγος οὐκ ἔχει τὸν τῶν τμημάτων, ὁ δὲ τῆς συλλαβῆς ἔχει τὸν τῶν στοιχείων· καίτοι διαιρεῖται καὶ ὁ κύκλος εἰς τὰ τμήματα ὥσπερ καὶ ἡ συλλαβὴ εἰς τὰ στοιχεῖα. ἔτι δὲ εἰ πρότερα τὰ μέρη τοῦ ὅλου, τῆς δὲ ὀρθῆς ἡ ὀξεῖα μέρος καὶ ὁ δάκτυλος τοῦ ζῴου, πρότερον ἂν εἴη ἡ ὀξεῖα τῆς ὀρθῆς καὶ ὁ δάκτυλος τοῦ ἀνθρώπου. δοκεῖ δʼ ἐκεῖνα εἶναι πρότερα· τῷ λόγῳ γὰρ λέγονται ἐξ ἐκείνων, καὶ τῷ εἶναι δὲ ἄνευ ἀλλήλων πρότερα.—ἢ πολλαχῶς λέγεται τὸ μέρος, ὧν εἷς μὲν τρόπος τὸ μετροῦν κατὰ τὸ ποσόν—ἀλλὰ τοῦτο μὲν ἀφείσθω· ἐξ ὧν δὲ ἡ οὐσία ὡς μερῶν, τοῦτο σκεπτέον.","kind":"passage"},{"id":"RAW_DAZY_BOO7:P0041","text":"εἰ οὖν ἐστὶ τὸ μὲν ὕλη τὸ δὲ εἶδος τὸ δʼ ἐκ τούτων, καὶ οὐσία ἥ τε ὕλη καὶ τὸ εἶδος καὶ τὸ ἐκ τούτων, ἔστι μὲν ὡς καὶ ἡ ὕλη μέρος τινὸς λέγεται, ἔστι δʼ ὡς οὔ, ἀλλʼ ἐξ ὧν ὁ τοῦ εἴδους λόγος. οἷον τῆς μὲν κοιλότητος οὐκ ἔστι μέρος ἡ σάρξ (αὕτη γὰρ ἡ ὕλη ἐφʼ ἧς γίγνεται), τῆς δὲ σιμότητος μέρος· καὶ τοῦ μὲν συνόλου ἀνδριάντος μέρος ὁ χαλκὸς τοῦ δʼ ὡς εἴδους λεγομένου ἀνδριάντος οὔ (λεκτέον γὰρ τὸ εἶδος καὶ ᾗ εἶδος ἔχει ἕκαστον, τὸ δʼ ὑλικὸν οὐδέποτε καθʼ αὑτὸ λεκτέον)· διὸ ὁ μὲν τοῦ κύκλου λόγος οὐκ ἔχει τὸν τῶν τμημάτων, ὁ δὲ τῆς συλλαβῆς ἔχει τὸν τῶν στοιχείων· τὰ μὲν γὰρ στοιχεῖα τοῦ λόγου μέρη τοῦ εἴδους καὶ οὐχ ὕλη, τὰ δὲ τμήματα οὕτως μέρη ὡς ὕλη ἐφʼ ἧς ἐπιγίγνεται· ἐγγυτέρω μέντοι τοῦ εἴδους ἢ ὁ χαλκὸς ὅταν ἐν χαλκῷ ἡ στρογγυλότης ἐγγένηται.","kind":"passage"},{"id":"RAW_DAZY_BOO7:P0042","text":"ἔστι δʼ ὡς οὐδὲ τὰ στοιχεῖα πάντα τῆς συλλαβῆς ἐν τῷ λόγῳ ἐνέσται, οἷον ταδὶ τὰ κήρινα ἢ τὰ ἐν τῷ ἀέρι· ἤδη γὰρ καὶ ταῦτα μέρος τῆς συλλαβῆς ὡς ὕλη αἰσθητή. καὶ γὰρ ἡ γραμμὴ οὐκ εἰ διαιρουμένη εἰς τὰ ἡμίση φθείρεται, ἢ ὁ ἄνθρωπος εἰς τὰ ὀστᾶ καὶ νεῦρα καὶ σάρκας, διὰ τοῦτο καὶ εἰσὶν ἐκ τούτων οὕτως ὡς ὄντων τῆς οὐσίας μερῶν, ἀλλʼ ὡς ἐξ ὕλης, καὶ τοῦ μὲν συνόλου μέρη, τοῦ εἴδους δὲ καὶ οὗ ὁ λόγος οὐκέτι· διόπερ οὐδʼ ἐν τοῖς λόγοις. τῷ μὲν οὖν ἐνέσται ὁ τῶν τοιούτων μερῶν λόγος, τῷ δʼ οὐ δεῖ ἐνεῖναι, ἂν μὴ ᾖ τοῦ συνειλημμένου· διὰ γὰρ τοῦτο ἔνια μὲν ἐκ τούτων ὡς ἀρχῶν ἐστὶν εἰς ἃ φθείρονται, ἔνια δὲ οὐκ ἔστιν.","kind":"passage"},{"id":"RAW_DAZY_BOO7:P0043","text":"ὅσα μὲν οὖν συνειλημμένα τὸ εἶδος καὶ ἡ ὕλη ἐστίν, οἷον τὸ σιμὸν ἢ ὁ χαλκοῦς κύκλος, ταῦτα μὲν φθείρεται εἰς ταῦτα καὶ μέρος αὐτῶν ἡ ὕλη· ὅσα δὲ μὴ συνείληπται τῇ ὕλῃ ἀλλὰ ἄνευ ὕλης, ὧν οἱ λόγοι τοῦ εἴδους μόνον, ταῦτα δʼ οὐ φθείρεται, ἢ ὅλως ἢ οὔτοι οὕτω γε· ὥστʼ ἐκείνων μὲν ἀρχαὶ καὶ μέρη ταῦτα τοῦ δὲ εἴδους οὔτε μέρη οὔτε ἀρχαί. καὶ διὰ τοῦτο φθείρεται ὁ πήλινος ἀνδριὰς εἰς πηλὸν καὶ ἡ σφαῖρα εἰς χαλκὸν καὶ ὁ Καλλίας εἰς σάρκα καὶ ὀστᾶ, ἔτι δὲ ὁ κύκλος εἰς τὰ τμήματα· ἔστι γάρ τις ὃς συνείληπται τῇ ὕλῃ·  ὁμωνύμως γὰρ λέγεται κύκλος ὅ τε ἁπλῶς λεγόμενος καὶ ὁ καθʼ ἕκαστα διὰ τὸ μὴ εἶναι ἴδιον ὄνομα τοῖς καθʼ ἕκαστον.—εἴρηται μὲν οὖν καὶ νῦν τὸ ἀληθές, ὅμως δʼ ἔτι σαφέστερον εἴπωμεν ἐπαναλαβόντες.","kind":"passage"},{"id":"RAW_DAZY_BOO7:P0044","text":"ὅσα μὲν γὰρ τοῦ λόγου μέρη καὶ εἰς ἃ διαιρεῖται ὁ λόγος, ταῦτα πρότερα ἢ πάντα ἢ ἔνια· ὁ δὲ τῆς ὀρθῆς λόγος οὐ διαιρεῖται εἰς ὀξείας λόγον, ἀλλʼ ὁ τῆς ὀξείας εἰς ὀρθήν· χρῆται γὰρ ὁ ὁριζόμενος τὴν ὀξεῖαν τῇ ὀρθῇ· ἐλάττων γὰρ ὀρθῆς ἡ ὀξεῖα. ὁμοίως δὲ καὶ ὁ κύκλος καὶ τὸ ἡμικύκλιον ἔχουσιν· τὸ γὰρ ἡμικύκλιον τῷ κύκλῳ ὁρίζεται καὶ ὁ δάκτυλος τῷ ὅλῳ· τὸ γὰρ τοιόνδε μέρος ἀνθρώπου δάκτυλος. ὥσθʼ ὅσα μὲν μέρη ὡς ὕλη καὶ εἰς ἃ διαιρεῖται ὡς ὕλην, ὕστερα· ὅσα δὲ ὡς τοῦ λόγου καὶ τῆς οὐσίας τῆς κατὰ τὸν λόγον, πρότερα ἢ πάντα ἢ ἔνια.","kind":"passage"},{"id":"RAW_DAZY_BOO7:P0045","text":"ἐπεὶ δὲ ἡ τῶν ζῴων ψυχή (τοῦτο γὰρ οὐσία τοῦ ἐμψύχου) ἡ κατὰ τὸν λόγον οὐσία καὶ τὸ εἶδος καὶ τὸ τί ἦν εἶναι τῷ τοιῷδε σώματι (ἕκαστον γοῦν τὸ μέρος ἐὰν ὁρίζηται καλῶς, οὐκ ἄνευ τοῦ ἔργου ὁριεῖται, ὃ οὐχ ὑπάρξει ἄνευ αἰσθήσεως), ὥστε τὰ ταύτης μέρη πρότερα ἢ πάντα ἢ ἔνια τοῦ συνόλου ζῴου, καὶ καθʼ ἕκαστον δὴ ὁμοίως, τὸ δὲ σῶμα καὶ τὰ τούτου μόρια ὕστερα ταύτης τῆς οὐσίας, καὶ διαιρεῖται εἰς ταῦτα ὡς εἰς ὕλην οὐχ ἡ οὐσία ἀλλὰ τὸ σύνολον,—τοῦ μὲν οὖν συνόλου πρότερα ταῦτʼ ἔστιν ὥς, ἔστι δʼ ὡς οὔ (οὐδὲ γὰρ εἶναι δύναται χωριζόμενα· οὐ γὰρ ὁ πάντως ἔχων δάκτυλος ζῴου, ἀλλʼ ὁμώνυμος ὁ τεθνεώς)· ἔνια δὲ ἅμα, ὅσα κύρια καὶ ἐν ᾧ πρώτῳ ὁ λόγος καὶ ἡ οὐσία, οἷον εἰ τοῦτο καρδία ἢ ἐγκέφαλος· διαφέρει γὰρ οὐθὲν πότερον τοιοῦτον.","kind":"passage"},{"id":"RAW_DAZY_BOO7:P0046","text":"ὁ δʼ ἄνθρωπος καὶ ὁ ἵππος καὶ τὰ οὕτως ἐπὶ τῶν καθʼ ἕκαστα, καθόλου δέ, οὐκ ἔστιν οὐσία ἀλλὰ σύνολόν τι ἐκ τουδὶ τοῦ λόγου καὶ τησδὶ τῆς ὕλης ὡς καθόλου· καθʼ ἕκαστον δʼ ἐκ τῆς ἐσχάτης ὕλης ὁ Σωκράτης ἤδη ἐστίν, καὶ ἐπὶ τῶν ἄλλων ὁμοίως.—μέρος μὲν οὖν ἐστὶ καὶ τοῦ εἴδους (εἶδος δὲ λέγω τὸ τί ἦν εἶναι) καὶ τοῦ συνόλου τοῦ ἐκ τοῦ εἴδους καὶ τῆς ὕλης καὶ τῆς ὕλης αὐτῆς. ἀλλὰ τοῦ λόγου μέρη τὰ τοῦ εἴδους μόνον ἐστίν, ὁ δὲ λόγος ἐστὶ τοῦ καθόλου·  τὸ γὰρ κύκλῳ εἶναι καὶ κύκλος καὶ ψυχῇ εἶναι καὶ ψυχὴ ταὐτό. τοῦ δὲ συνόλου ἤδη, οἷον κύκλου τουδὶ καὶ τῶν καθʼ ἕκαστά τινος ἢ αἰσθητοῦ ἢ νοητοῦ—λέγω δὲ νοητοὺς μὲν οἷον τοὺς μαθηματικούς, αἰσθητοὺς δὲ οἷον τοὺς χαλκοῦς καὶ τοὺς ξυλίνους—τούτων δὲ οὐκ ἔστιν ὁρισμός, ἀλλὰ μετὰ νοήσεως ἢ αἰσθήσεως γνωρίζονται, ἀπελθόντες δὲ ἐκ τῆς ἐντελεχείας οὐ δῆλον πότερον εἰσὶν ἢ οὐκ εἰσίν· ἀλλʼ ἀεὶ λέγονται καὶ γνωρίζονται τῷ καθόλου λόγῳ.","kind":"passage"},{"id":"RAW_DAZY_BOO7:P0047","text":"ἡ δʼ ὕλη ἄγνωστος καθʼ αὑτήν. ὕλη δὲ ἡ μὲν αἰσθητή ἐστιν ἡ δὲ νοητή, αἰσθητὴ μὲν οἷον χαλκὸς καὶ ξύλον καὶ ὅση κινητὴ ὕλη, νοητὴ δὲ ἡ ἐν τοῖς αἰσθητοῖς ὑπάρχουσα μὴ ᾗ αἰσθητά, οἷον τὰ μαθηματικά. πῶς μὲν οὖν ἔχει περὶ ὅλου καὶ μέρους καὶ περὶ τοῦ προτέρου καὶ ὑστέρου, εἴρηται· πρὸς δὲ τὴν ἐρώτησιν ἀνάγκη ἀπαντᾶν, ὅταν τις ἔρηται πότερον ἡ ὀρθὴ καὶ ὁ κύκλος καὶ τὸ ζῷον πρότερον ἢ εἰς ἃ διαιροῦνται καὶ ἐξ ὧν εἰσί, τὰ μέρη, ὅτι οὐχ ἁπλῶς.","kind":"passage"},{"id":"RAW_DAZY_BOO7:P0048","text":"εἰ μὲν γάρ ἐστι καὶ ἡ ψυχὴ ζῷον ἢ ἔμψυχον, ἢ ἕκαστον ἡ ἑκάστου, καὶ κύκλος τὸ κύκλῳ εἶναι, καὶ ὀρθὴ τὸ ὀρθῇ εἶναι καὶ ἡ οὐσία ἡ τῆς ὀρθῆς, τὶ μὲν καὶ τινὸς φατέον ὕστερον, οἷον τῶν ἐν τῷ λόγῳ καὶ τινὸς ὀρθῆς (καὶ γὰρ ἡ μετὰ τῆς ὕλης, ἡ χαλκῆ ὀρθή, καὶ ἡ ἐν ταῖς γραμμαῖς ταῖς καθʼ ἕκαστα), ἡ δʼ ἄνευ ὕλης τῶν μὲν ἐν τῷ λόγῳ ὑστέρα τῶν δʼ ἐν τῷ καθʼ ἕκαστα μορίων προτέρα, ἁπλῶς δʼ οὐ φατέον· εἰ δʼ ἑτέρα καὶ μὴ ἔστιν ἡ ψυχὴ ζῷον, καὶ οὕτω τὰ μὲν φατέον τὰ δʼ οὐ φατέον, ὥσπερ εἴρηται.","kind":"passage"},{"id":"RAW_DAZY_BOO7:P0049","text":"ἀπορεῖται δὲ εἰκότως καὶ ποῖα τοῦ εἴδους μέρη καὶ ποῖα οὔ, ἀλλὰ τοῦ συνειλημμένου. καίτοι τούτου μὴ δήλου ὄντος οὐκ ἔστιν ὁρίσασθαι ἕκαστον· τοῦ γὰρ καθόλου καὶ τοῦ εἴδους ὁ ὁρισμός· ποῖα οὖν ἐστὶ τῶν μερῶν ὡς ὕλη καὶ ποῖα οὔ, ἐὰν μὴ ᾖ φανερά, οὐδὲ ὁ λόγος ἔσται φανερὸς ὁ τοῦ πράγματος. ὅσα μὲν οὖν φαίνεται ἐπιγιγνόμενα ἐφʼ ἑτέρων τῷ εἴδει, οἷον κύκλος ἐν χαλκῷ καὶ λίθῳ καὶ ξύλῳ, ταῦτα μὲν δῆλα εἶναι δοκεῖ ὅτι οὐδὲν τῆς τοῦ κύκλου οὐσίας ὁ χαλκὸς οὐδʼ ὁ λίθος διὰ τὸ χωρίζεσθαι αὐτῶν· ὅσα δὲ μὴ ὁρᾶται χωριζόμενα, οὐδὲν μὲν κωλύει ὁμοίως ἔχειν τούτοις, ὥσπερ κἂν εἰ οἱ κύκλοι πάντες ἑωρῶντο χαλκοῖ·  οὐδὲν γὰρ ἂν ἧττον ἦν ὁ χαλκὸς οὐδὲν τοῦ εἴδους· χαλεπὸν δὲ ἀφελεῖν τοῦτον τῇ διανοίᾳ.","kind":"passage"},{"id":"RAW_DAZY_BOO7:P0050","text":"οἷον τὸ τοῦ ἀνθρώπου εἶδος ἀεὶ ἐν σαρξὶ φαίνεται καὶ ὀστοῖς καὶ τοῖς τοιούτοις μέρεσιν· ἆρʼ οὖν καὶ ἐστὶ ταῦτα μέρη τοῦ εἴδους καὶ τοῦ λόγου; ἢ οὔ, ἀλλʼ ὕλη, ἀλλὰ διὰ τὸ μὴ καὶ ἐπʼ ἄλλων ἐπιγίγνεσθαι ἀδυνατοῦμεν χωρίσαι; ἐπεὶ δὲ τοῦτο δοκεῖ μὲν ἐνδέχεσθαι ἄδηλον δὲ πότε, ἀποροῦσί τινες ἤδη καὶ ἐπὶ τοῦ κύκλου καὶ τοῦ τριγώνου ὡς οὐ προσῆκον γραμμαῖς ὁρίζεσθαι καὶ τῷ συνεχεῖ, ἀλλὰ πάντα καὶ ταῦτα ὁμοίως λέγεσθαι ὡσανεὶ σάρκες καὶ ὀστᾶ τοῦ ἀνθρώπου καὶ χαλκὸς καὶ λίθος τοῦ ἀνδριάντος· καὶ ἀνάγουσι πάντα εἰς τοὺς ἀριθμούς, καὶ γραμμῆς τὸν λόγον τὸν τῶν δύο εἶναί φασιν. καὶ τῶν τὰς ἰδέας λεγόντων οἱ μὲν αὐτογραμμὴν τὴν δυάδα, οἱ δὲ τὸ εἶδος τῆς γραμμῆς, ἔνια μὲν γὰρ εἶναι τὸ αὐτὸ τὸ εἶδος καὶ οὗ τὸ εἶδος (οἷον δυάδα καὶ τὸ εἶδος δυάδος), ἐπὶ γραμμῆς δὲ οὐκέτι.","kind":"passage"},{"id":"RAW_DAZY_BOO7:P0051","text":"συμβαίνει δὴ ἕν τε πολλῶν εἶδος εἶναι ὧν τὸ εἶδος φαίνεται ἕτερον (ὅπερ καὶ τοῖς Πυθαγορείοις συνέβαινεν), καὶ ἐνδέχεται ἓν πάντων ποιεῖν αὐτὸ εἶδος, τὰ δʼ ἄλλα μὴ εἴδη· καίτοι οὕτως ἓν πάντα ἔσται.","kind":"passage"},{"id":"RAW_DAZY_BOO7:P0052","text":"ὅτι μὲν οὖν ἔχει τινὰ ἀπορίαν τὰ περὶ τοὺς ὁρισμούς, καὶ διὰ τίνʼ αἰτίαν, εἴρηται· διὸ καὶ τὸ πάντα ἀνάγειν οὕτω καὶ ἀφαιρεῖν τὴν ὕλην περίεργον· ἔνια γὰρ ἴσως τόδʼ ἐν τῷδʼ ἐστὶν ἢ ὡδὶ ταδὶ ἔχοντα. καὶ ἡ παραβολὴ ἡ ἐπὶ τοῦ ζῴου, ἣν εἰώθει λέγειν Σωκράτης ὁ νεώτερος, οὐ καλῶς ἔχει· ἀπάγει γὰρ ἀπὸ τοῦ ἀληθοῦς, καὶ ποιεῖ ὑπολαμβάνειν ὡς ἐνδεχόμενον εἶναι τὸν ἄνθρωπον ἄνευ τῶν μερῶν, ὥσπερ ἄνευ τοῦ χαλκοῦ τὸν κύκλον. τὸ δʼ οὐχ ὅμοιον· αἰσθητὸν γάρ τι τὸ ζῷον, καὶ ἄνευ κινήσεως οὐκ ἔστιν ὁρίσασθαι, διὸ οὐδʼ ἄνευ τῶν μερῶν ἐχόντων πώς. οὐ γὰρ πάντως τοῦ ἀνθρώπου μέρος ἡ χείρ, ἀλλʼ ἢ δυναμένη τὸ ἔργον ἀποτελεῖν, ὥστε ἔμψυχος οὖσα· μὴ ἔμψυχος δὲ οὐ μέρος. περὶ δὲ τὰ μαθηματικὰ διὰ τί οὐκ εἰσὶ μέρη οἱ λόγοι τῶν λόγων, οἷον τοῦ κύκλου τὰ ἡμικύκλια; οὐ γάρ ἐστιν αἰσθητὰ ταῦτα.","kind":"passage"},{"id":"RAW_DAZY_BOO7:P0053","text":"ἢ οὐθὲν διαφέρει; ἔσται γὰρ ὕλη ἐνίων καὶ μὴ αἰσθητῶν·  καὶ παντὸς γὰρ ὕλη τις ἔστιν ὃ μὴ ἔστι τί ἦν εἶναι καὶ εἶδος αὐτὸ καθʼ αὑτὸ ἀλλὰ τόδε τι. κύκλου μὲν οὖν οὐκ ἔσται τοῦ καθόλου, τῶν δὲ καθʼ ἕκαστα ἔσται μέρη ταῦτα, ὥσπερ εἴρηται πρότερον· ἔστι γὰρ ὕλη ἡ μὲν αἰσθητὴ ἡ δὲ νοητή. δῆλον δὲ καὶ ὅτι ἡ μὲν ψυχὴ οὐσία ἡ πρώτη, τὸ δὲ σῶμα ὕλη, ὁ δʼ ἄνθρωπος ἢ τὸ ζῷον τὸ ἐξ ἀμφοῖν ὡς καθόλου· Σωκράτης δὲ καὶ Κορίσκος, εἰ μὲν καὶ ἡ ψυχὴ Σωκράτης, διττόν (οἱ μὲν γὰρ ὡς ψυχὴν οἱ δʼ ὡς τὸ σύνολον), εἰ δʼ ἁπλῶς ἡ ψυχὴ ἥδε καὶ τὸ σῶμα τόδε, ὥσπερ τὸ καθόλου  καὶ τὸ καθʼ ἕκαστον. πότερον δὲ ἔστι παρὰ τὴν ὕλην τῶν τοιούτων οὐσιῶν τις ἄλλη, καὶ δεῖ ζητεῖν οὐσίαν ἑτέραν τινὰ οἷον ἀριθμοὺς ἤ τι τοιοῦτον, σκεπτέον ὕστερον.","kind":"passage"},{"id":"RAW_DAZY_BOO7:P0054","text":"τούτου γὰρ χάριν καὶ περὶ τῶν αἰσθητῶν οὐσιῶν πειρώμεθα διορίζειν, ἐπεὶ τρόπον τινὰ τῆς φυσικῆς καὶ δευτέρας φιλοσοφίας ἔργον ἡ περὶ τὰς αἰσθητὰς οὐσίας θεωρία· οὐ γὰρ μόνον περὶ τῆς ὕλης δεῖ γνωρίζειν τὸν φυσικὸν ἀλλὰ καὶ τῆς κατὰ τὸν λόγον, καὶ μᾶλλον. ἐπὶ δὲ τῶν ὁρισμῶν πῶς μέρη τὰ ἐν τῷ λόγῳ, καὶ διὰ τί εἷς λόγος ὁ ὁρισμός (δῆλον γὰρ ὅτι τὸ πρᾶγμα ἕν, τὸ δὲ πρᾶγμα τίνι ἕν, μέρη γε ἔχον;), σκεπτέον ὕστερον.","kind":"passage"},{"id":"RAW_DAZY_BOO7:P0055","text":"τί μὲν οὖν ἐστὶ τὸ τί ἦν εἶναι καὶ πῶς αὐτὸ καθʼ αὑτό, καθόλου περὶ παντὸς εἴρηται, καὶ διὰ τί τῶν μὲν ὁ λόγος ὁ τοῦ τί ἦν εἶναι ἔχει τὰ μόρια τοῦ ὁριζομένου τῶν δʼ οὔ, καὶ ὅτι ἐν μὲν τῷ τῆς οὐσίας λόγῳ τὰ οὕτω μόρια ὡς ὕλη οὐκ ἐνέσται—οὐδὲ γὰρ ἔστιν ἐκείνης μόρια τῆς οὐσίας ἀλλὰ τῆς συνόλου, ταύτης δέ γʼ ἔστι πως λόγος καὶ οὐκ ἔστιν· μετὰ μὲν γὰρ τῆς ὕλης οὐκ ἔστιν (ἀόριστον γάρ), κατὰ τὴν πρώτην δʼ οὐσίαν ἔστιν, οἷον ἀνθρώπου ὁ τῆς ψυχῆς λόγος· ἡ γὰρ οὐσία ἐστὶ τὸ εἶδος τὸ ἐνόν, ἐξ οὗ καὶ τῆς ὕλης ἡ σύνολος λέγεται οὐσία, οἷον ἡ κοιλότης (ἐκ γὰρ ταύτης καὶ τῆς ῥινὸς σιμὴ ῥὶς καὶ ἡ σιμότης ἐστί )—ἐν δὲ τῇ συνόλῳ οὐσίᾳ, οἷον ῥινὶ σιμῇ ἢ Καλλίᾳ, ἐνέσται καὶ ἡ ὕλη· καὶ ὅτι τὸ τί ἦν εἶναι καὶ ἕκαστον ἐπὶ τινῶν μὲν ταὐτό,  ὥσπερ ἐπὶ τῶν πρώτων οὐσιῶν, οἷον καμπυλότης καὶ καμπυλότητι εἶναι, εἰ πρώτη ἐστίν (λέγω δὲ πρώτην ἣ μὴ λέγεται τῷ ἄλλο ἐν ἄλλῳ εἶναι καὶ ὑποκειμένῳ ὡς ὕλῃ), ὅσα δὲ ὡς","kind":"passage"},{"id":"RAW_DAZY_BOO7:P0056","text":"ὕλη ἢ ὡς συνειλημμένα τῇ ὕλῃ, οὐ ταὐτό, οὐδʼ εἰ κατὰ συμβεβηκὸς ἕν, οἷον Σωκράτης καὶ τὸ μουσικόν· ταῦτα γὰρ ταὐτὰ κατὰ συμβεβηκός.","kind":"passage"},{"id":"RAW_DAZY_BOO7:P0057","text":"νῦν δὲ λέγωμεν πρῶτον ἐφʼ ὅσον ἐν τοῖς ἀναλυτικοῖς περὶ ὁρισμοῦ μὴ εἴρηται· ἡ γὰρ ἐν ἐκείνοις ἀπορία λεχθεῖσα πρὸ ἔργου τοῖς περὶ τῆς οὐσίας ἐστὶ λόγοις. λέγω δὲ ταύτην τὴν ἀπορίαν, διὰ τί ποτε ἕν ἐστιν οὗ τὸν λόγον ὁρισμὸν εἶναί φαμεν, οἷον τοῦ ἀνθρώπου τὸ ζῷον δίπουν· ἔστω γὰρ οὗτος αὐτοῦ λόγος. διὰ τί δὴ τοῦτο ἕν ἐστιν ἀλλʼ οὐ πολλά, ζῷον καὶ δίπουν· ἐπὶ μὲν γὰρ τοῦ ἄνθρωπος καὶ λευκὸν πολλὰ μέν ἐστιν ὅταν μὴ ὑπάρχῃ θατέρῳ θάτερον, ἓν δὲ ὅταν ὑπάρχῃ καὶ πάθῃ τι τὸ ὑποκείμενον, ὁ ἄνθρωπος (τότε γὰρ ἓν γίγνεται καὶ ἔστιν ὁ λευκὸς ἄνθρωπος)· ἐνταῦθα δʼ οὐ μετέχει θατέρου θάτερον· τὸ γὰρ γένος οὐ δοκεῖ μετέχειν τῶν διαφορῶν (ἅμα γὰρ ἂν τῶν ἐναντίων τὸ αὐτὸ μετεῖχεν· αἱ γὰρ διαφοραὶ ἐναντίαι αἷς διαφέρει τὸ γένος). εἰ δὲ καὶ μετέχει, ὁ αὐτὸς λόγος, εἴπερ εἰσὶν αἱ διαφοραὶ πλείους, οἷον πεζὸν δίπουν ἄπτερον.","kind":"passage"},{"id":"RAW_DAZY_BOO7:P0058","text":"διὰ τί γὰρ ταῦθʼ ἓν ἀλλʼ οὐ πολλά; οὐ γὰρ ὅτι ἐνυπάρχει· οὕτω μὲν γὰρ ἐξ ἁπάντων ἔσται ἕν. δεῖ δέ γε ἓν εἶναι ὅσα ἐν τῷ ὁρισμῷ· ὁ γὰρ ὁρισμὸς λόγος τίς ἐστιν εἷς καὶ οὐσίας, ὥστε ἑνός τινος δεῖ αὐτὸν εἶναι λόγον· καὶ γὰρ ἡ οὐσία ἕν τι καὶ τόδε τι σημαίνει, ὡς φαμέν.—δεῖ δὲ ἐπισκοπεῖν πρῶτον περὶ τῶν κατὰ τὰς διαιρέσεις ὁρισμῶν. οὐδὲν γὰρ ἕτερόν ἐστιν ἐν τῷ ὁρισμῷ πλὴν τὸ πρῶτον λεγόμενον γένος καὶ αἱ διαφοραί· τὰ δʼ ἄλλα γένη ἐστὶ τό τε πρῶτον καὶ μετὰ τούτου αἱ συλλαμβανόμεναι διαφοραί, οἷον τὸ πρῶτον ζῷον, τὸ δὲ ἐχόμενον ζῷον δίπουν, καὶ πάλιν ζῷον δίπουν ἄπτερον· ὁμοίως δὲ κἂν διὰ πλειόνων λέγηται.  ὅλως δʼ οὐδὲν διαφέρει διὰ πολλῶν ἢ διʼ ὀλίγων λέγεσθαι, ὥστʼ οὐδὲ διʼ ὀλίγων ἢ διὰ δυοῖν· τοῖν δυοῖν δὲ τὸ μὲν διαφορὰ τὸ δὲ γένος, οἷον τοῦ ζῷον δίπουν τὸ μὲν ζῷον γένος διαφορὰ δὲ θάτερον.","kind":"passage"},{"id":"RAW_DAZY_BOO7:P0059","text":"εἰ οὖν τὸ γένος ἁπλῶς μὴ ἔστι παρὰ τὰ ὡς γένους εἴδη, ἢ εἰ ἔστι μὲν ὡς ὕλη δʼ ἐστίν (ἡ μὲν γὰρ φωνὴ γένος καὶ ὕλη, αἱ δὲ διαφοραὶ τὰ εἴδη καὶ τὰ στοιχεῖα ἐκ ταύτης ποιοῦσιν), φανερὸν ὅτι ὁ ὁρισμός ἐστιν ὁ ἐκ τῶν διαφορῶν λόγος. ἀλλὰ μὴν καὶ δεῖ γε διαιρεῖσθαι τῇ τῆς διαφορᾶς διαφορᾷ, οἷον ζῴου διαφορὰ τὸ ὑπόπουν· πάλιν τοῦ ζῴου τοῦ ὑπόποδος τὴν διαφορὰν δεῖ εἶναι ᾗ ὑπόπουν, ὥστʼ οὐ λεκτέον τοῦ ὑπόποδος τὸ μὲν πτερωτὸν τὸ δὲ ἄπτερον, ἐάνπερ λέγῃ καλῶς (ἀλλὰ διὰ τὸ ἀδυνατεῖν ποιήσει τοῦτο), ἀλλʼ ἢ τὸ μὲν σχιζόπουν τὸ δʼ ἄσχιστον· αὗται γὰρ διαφοραὶ ποδός· ἡ γὰρ σχιζοποδία ποδότης τις. καὶ οὕτως ἀεὶ βούλεται βαδίζειν ἕως ἂν ἔλθῃ εἰς τὰ ἀδιάφορα· τότε δʼ ἔσονται τοσαῦτα εἴδη ποδὸς ὅσαιπερ αἱ διαφοραί, καὶ τὰ ὑπόποδα ζῷα ἴσα ταῖς διαφοραῖς.","kind":"passage"},{"id":"RAW_DAZY_BOO7:P0060","text":"εἰ δὴ ταῦτα οὕτως ἔχει, φανερὸν ὅτι ἡ τελευταία διαφορὰ ἡ οὐσία τοῦ πράγματος ἔσται καὶ ὁ ὁρισμός, εἴπερ μὴ δεῖ πολλάκις ταὐτὰ λέγειν ἐν τοῖς ὅροις· περίεργον γάρ. συμβαίνει δέ γε τοῦτο· ὅταν γὰρ εἴπῃ ζῷον ὑπόπουν δίπουν, οὐδὲν ἄλλο εἴρηκεν ἢ ζῷον πόδας ἔχον, δύο πόδας ἔχον· κἂν τοῦτο διαιρῇ τῇ οἰκείᾳ διαιρέσει, πλεονάκις ἐρεῖ καὶ ἰσάκις ταῖς διαφοραῖς. ἐὰν μὲν δὴ διαφορᾶς διαφορὰ γίγνηται, μία ἔσται ἡ τελευταία τὸ εἶδος καὶ ἡ οὐσία· ἐὰν δὲ κατὰ συμβεβηκός, οἷον εἰ διαιροῖ τοῦ ὑπόποδος τὸ μὲν λευκὸν τὸ δὲ μέλαν, τοσαῦται ὅσαι ἂν αἱ τομαὶ ὦσιν. ὥστε φανερὸν ὅτι ὁ ὁρισμὸς λόγος ἐστὶν ὁ ἐκ τῶν διαφορῶν, καὶ τούτων τῆς τελευταίας κατά γε τὸ ὀρθόν. δῆλον δʼ ἂν εἴη, εἴ τις μετατάξειε τοὺς τοιούτους ὁρισμούς, οἷον τὸν τοῦ ἀνθρώπου, λέγων ζῷον δίπουν ὑπόπουν· περίεργον γὰρ τὸ ὑπόπουν εἰρημένου τοῦ δίποδος.","kind":"passage"},{"id":"RAW_DAZY_BOO7:P0061","text":"τάξις δʼ οὐκ ἔστιν ἐν τῇ οὐσίᾳ· πῶς γὰρ δεῖ νοῆσαι τὸ μὲν ὕστερον τὸ δὲ πρότερον; περὶ μὲν οὖν τῶν κατὰ τὰς διαιρέσεις ὁρισμῶν τοσαῦτα εἰρήσθω τὴν πρώτην, ποῖοί τινές εἰσιν.","kind":"passage"},{"id":"RAW_DAZY_BOO7:P0062","text":"ἐπεὶ δὲ περὶ τῆς οὐσίας ἡ σκέψις ἐστί, πάλιν ἐπανέλθωμεν. λέγεται δʼ ὥσπερ τὸ ὑποκείμενον οὐσία εἶναι καὶ τὸ τί ἦν εἶναι καὶ τὸ ἐκ τούτων, καὶ τὸ καθόλου. περὶ μὲν οὖν τοῖν δυοῖν εἴρηται (καὶ γὰρ περὶ τοῦ τί ἦν εἶναι καὶ τοῦ ὑποκειμένου, ὅτι διχῶς ὑπόκειται, ἢ τόδε τι ὄν, ὥσπερ τὸ ζῷον τοῖς πάθεσιν, ἢ ὡς ἡ ὕλη τῇ ἐντελεχείᾳ), δοκεῖ δὲ καὶ τὸ καθόλου αἴτιόν τισιν εἶναι μάλιστα, καὶ εἶναι ἀρχὴ τὸ καθόλου· διὸ ἐπέλθωμεν καὶ περὶ τούτου. ἔοικε γὰρ ἀδύνατον εἶναι οὐσίαν εἶναι ὁτιοῦν τῶν καθόλου λεγομένων. πρῶτον μὲν γὰρ οὐσία ἑκάστου ἡ ἴδιος ἑκάστῳ, ἣ οὐχ ὑπάρχει ἄλλῳ, τὸ δὲ καθόλου κοινόν· τοῦτο γὰρ λέγεται καθόλου ὃ πλείοσιν ὑπάρχειν πέφυκεν. τίνος οὖν οὐσία τοῦτʼ ἔσται; ἢ γὰρ πάντων ἢ οὐδενός, πάντων δʼ οὐχ οἷόν τε· ἑνὸς δʼ εἰ ἔσται, καὶ τἆλλα τοῦτʼ ἔσται· ὧν γὰρ μία ἡ οὐσία καὶ τὸ τί ἦν εἶναι ἕν, καὶ αὐτὰ ἕν.","kind":"passage"},{"id":"RAW_DAZY_BOO7:P0063","text":"ἔτι οὐσία λέγεται τὸ μὴ καθʼ ὑποκειμένου, τὸ δὲ καθόλου καθʼ ὑποκειμένου τινὸς λέγεται ἀεί. ἀλλʼ ἆρα οὕτω μὲν οὐκ ἐνδέχεται ὡς τὸ τί ἦν εἶναι, ἐν τούτῳ δὲ ἐνυπάρχειν, οἷον τὸ ζῷον ἐν τῷ ἀνθρώπῳ καὶ ἵππῳ; οὐκοῦν δῆλον ὅτι ἔστι τις αὐτοῦ λόγος. διαφέρει δʼ οὐθὲν οὐδʼ εἰ μὴ πάντων λόγος ἔστι τῶν ἐν τῇ οὐσίᾳ· οὐδὲν γὰρ ἧττον οὐσία τοῦτʼ ἔσται τινός, ὡς ὁ ἄνθρωπος τοῦ ἀνθρώπου ἐν ᾧ ὑπάρχει, ὥστε τὸ αὐτὸ συμβήσεται πάλιν· ἔσται γὰρ ἐκείνου οὐσία, οἷον τὸ ζῷον, ἐν ᾧ ὡς ἴδιον ὑπάρχει. ἔτι δὲ καὶ ἀδύνατον καὶ ἄτοπον τὸ τόδε καὶ οὐσίαν, εἰ ἔστιν ἔκ τινων, μὴ ἐξ οὐσιῶν εἶναι μηδʼ ἐκ τοῦ τόδε τι ἀλλʼ ἐκ ποιοῦ· πρότερον γὰρ ἔσται μὴ οὐσία τε καὶ τὸ ποιὸν οὐσίας τε καὶ τοῦ τόδε. ὅπερ ἀδύνατον· οὔτε λόγῳ γὰρ οὔτε χρόνῳ οὔτε γενέσει οἷόν τε τὰ πάθη τῆς οὐσίας εἶναι πρότερα· ἔσται γὰρ καὶ χωριστά. ἔτι τῷ Σωκράτει ἐνυπάρξει οὐσία οὐσίᾳ, ὥστε δυοῖν ἔσται οὐσία.","kind":"passage"},{"id":"RAW_DAZY_BOO7:P0064","text":"ὅλως δὲ συμβαίνει, εἰ ἔστιν οὐσία ὁ ἄνθρωπος καὶ ὅσα οὕτω λέγεται, μηθὲν τῶν ἐν τῷ λόγῳ εἶναι μηδενὸς οὐσίαν μηδὲ χωρὶς ὑπάρχειν αὐτῶν μηδʼ ἐν ἄλλῳ, λέγω δʼ οἷον οὐκ εἶναί τι ζῷον παρὰ τὰ τινά, οὐδʼ ἄλλο τῶν ἐν τοῖς λόγοις οὐδέν. ἔκ τε δὴ τούτων θεωροῦσι φανερὸν ὅτι οὐδὲν τῶν καθόλου ὑπαρχόντων οὐσία ἐστί, καὶ ὅτι οὐδὲν σημαίνει τῶν κοινῇ κατηγορουμένων τόδε τι,  ἀλλὰ τοιόνδε. εἰ δὲ μή, ἄλλα τε πολλὰ συμβαίνει καὶ ὁ τρίτος ἄνθρωπος. ἔτι δὲ καὶ ὧδε δῆλον. ἀδύνατον γὰρ οὐσίαν ἐξ οὐσιῶν εἶναι ἐνυπαρχουσῶν ὡς ἐντελεχείᾳ· τὰ γὰρ δύο οὕτως ἐντελεχείᾳ οὐδέποτε ἓν ἐντελεχείᾳ, ἀλλʼ ἐὰν δυνάμει δύο ᾖ, ἔσται ἕν (οἷον ἡ διπλασία ἐκ δύο ἡμίσεων δυνάμει γε· ἡ γὰρ ἐντελέχεια χωρίζει), ὥστʼ εἰ ἡ οὐσία ἕν, οὐκ ἔσται ἐξ οὐσιῶν ἐνυπαρχουσῶν καὶ κατὰ τοῦτον τὸν τρόπον, ὃν λέγει Δημόκριτος ὀρθῶς· ἀδύνατον γὰρ εἶναί φησιν ἐκ δύο ἓν ἢ ἐξ ἑνὸς δύο γενέσθαι· τὰ γὰρ μεγέθη τὰ ἄτομα τὰς οὐσίας ποιεῖ.","kind":"passage"},{"id":"RAW_DAZY_BOO7:P0065","text":"ὁμοίως τοίνυν δῆλον ὅτι καὶ ἐπʼ ἀριθμοῦ ἕξει, εἴπερ ἐστὶν ὁ ἀριθμὸς σύνθεσις μονάδων, ὥσπερ λέγεται ὑπό τινων· ἢ γὰρ οὐχ ἓν ἡ δυὰς ἢ οὐκ ἔστι μονὰς ἐν αὐτῇ ἐντελεχείᾳ.—ἔχει δὲ τὸ συμβαῖνον ἀπορίαν. εἰ γὰρ μήτε ἐκ τῶν καθόλου οἷόν τʼ εἶναι μηδεμίαν οὐσίαν διὰ τὸ τοιόνδε ἀλλὰ μὴ τόδε τι σημαίνειν, μήτʼ ἐξ οὐσιῶν ἐνδέχεται ἐντελεχείᾳ εἶναι μηδεμίαν οὐσίαν σύνθετον, ἀσύνθετον ἂν εἴη οὐσία πᾶσα, ὥστʼ οὐδὲ λόγος ἂν εἴη οὐδεμιᾶς οὐσίας. ἀλλὰ μὴν δοκεῖ γε πᾶσι καὶ ἐλέχθη πάλαι ἢ μόνον οὐσίας εἶναι ὅρον ἢ μάλιστα· νῦν δʼ οὐδὲ ταύτης. οὐδενὸς ἄρʼ ἔσται ὁρισμός· ἢ τρόπον μέν τινα ἔσται τρόπον δέ τινα οὔ. δῆλον δʼ ἔσται τὸ λεγόμενον ἐκ τῶν ὕστερον μᾶλλον.","kind":"passage"},{"id":"RAW_DAZY_BOO7:P0066","text":"φανερὸν δʼ ἐξ αὐτῶν τούτων τὸ συμβαῖνον καὶ τοῖς τὰς ἰδέας λέγουσιν οὐσίας τε χωριστὰς εἶναι καὶ ἅμα τὸ εἶδος ἐκ τοῦ γένους ποιοῦσι καὶ τῶν διαφορῶν. εἰ γὰρ ἔστι τὰ εἴδη, καὶ τὸ ζῷον ἐν τῷ ἀνθρώπῳ καὶ ἵππῳ, ἤτοι ἓν καὶ ταὐτὸν τῷ ἀριθμῷ ἐστὶν ἢ ἕτερον· τῷ μὲν γὰρ λόγῳ δῆλον ὅτι ἕν· τὸν γὰρ αὐτὸν διέξεισι λόγον ὁ λέγων ἐν ἑκατέρῳ. εἰ οὖν ἐστί τις ἄνθρωπος αὐτὸς καθʼ αὑτὸν τόδε τι καὶ κεχωρισμένον, ἀνάγκη καὶ ἐξ ὧν, οἷον τὸ ζῷον καὶ τὸ δίπουν, τόδε τι σημαίνειν καὶ εἶναι χωριστὰ καὶ οὐσίας· ὥστε καὶ τὸ ζῷον.","kind":"passage"},{"id":"RAW_DAZY_BOO7:P0067","text":"εἰ μὲν οὖν τὸ αὐτὸ καὶ ἓν τὸ ἐν τῷ ἵππῳ καὶ τῷ ἀνθρώπῳ, ὥσπερ σὺ σαυτῷ, πῶς τὸ ἓν ἐν τοῖς οὖσι χωρὶς ἓν ἔσται,  καὶ διὰ τί οὐ καὶ χωρὶς αὑτοῦ ἔσται τὸ ζῷον τοῦτο; ἔπειτα εἰ μὲν μεθέξει τοῦ δίποδος καὶ τοῦ πολύποδος, ἀδύνατόν τι συμβαίνει, τἀναντία γὰρ ἅμα ὑπάρξει αὐτῷ ἑνὶ καὶ τῷδέ τινι ὄντι· εἰ δὲ μή, τίς ὁ τρόπος ὅταν εἴπῃ τις τὸ ζῷον εἶναι δίπουν ἢ πεζόν; ἀλλʼ ἴσως σύγκειται καὶ ἅπτεται ἢ μέμικται· ἀλλὰ πάντα ἄτοπα. ἀλλʼ ἕτερον ἐν ἑκάστῳ· οὐκοῦν ἄπειρα ὡς ἔπος εἰπεῖν ἔσται ὧν ἡ οὐσία ζῷον· οὐ γὰρ κατὰ συμβεβηκὸς ἐκ ζῴου ἅνθρωπος. ἔτι πολλὰ ἔσται αὐτὸ τὸ ζῷον· οὐσία τε γὰρ τὸ ἐν ἑκάστῳ ζῷον (οὐ γὰρ κατʼ ἄλλο λέγεται· εἰ δὲ μή, ἐξ ἐκείνου ἔσται ὁ ἄνθρωπος καὶ γένος αὐτοῦ ἐκεῖνο), καὶ ἔτι ἰδέαι ἅπαντα ἐξ ὧν ὁ ἄνθρωπος· οὐκοῦν οὐκ ἄλλου μὲν ἰδέα ἔσται ἄλλου δʼ οὐσία (ἀδύνατον γάρ)· αὐτὸ ἄρα ζῷον ἓν ἕκαστον ἔσται τῶν ἐν τοῖς ζῴοις.","kind":"passage"},{"id":"RAW_DAZY_BOO7:P0068","text":"ἔτι ἐκ τίνος τοῦτο, καὶ πῶς ἐξ αὐτοῦ ζῴου; ἢ πῶς οἷόν τε εἶναι τὸ ζῷον, ᾧ οὐσία τοῦτο αὐτό, παρʼ αὐτὸ τὸ ζῷον; ἔτι δʼ ἐπὶ τῶν αἰσθητῶν ταῦτά τε συμβαίνει καὶ τούτων ἀτοπώτερα. εἰ δὴ ἀδύνατον οὕτως ἔχειν, δῆλον ὅτι οὐκ ἔστιν εἴδη αὐτῶν οὕτως ὥς τινές φασιν.","kind":"passage"},{"id":"RAW_DAZY_BOO7:P0069","text":"ἐπεὶ δʼ ἡ οὐσία ἑτέρα, τό τε σύνολον καὶ ὁ λόγος (λέγω δʼ ὅτι ἡ μὲν οὕτως ἐστὶν οὐσία, σὺν τῇ ὕλῃ συνειλημμένος ὁ λόγος, ἡ δʼ ὁ λόγος ὅλως), ὅσαι μὲν οὖν οὕτω λέγονται, τούτων μὲν ἔστι φθορά (καὶ γὰρ γένεσις), τοῦ δὲ λόγου οὐκ ἔστιν οὕτως ὥστε φθείρεσθαι (οὐδὲ γὰρ γένεσις, οὐ γὰρ γίγνεται τὸ οἰκίᾳ εἶναι ἀλλὰ τὸ τῇδε τῇ οἰκίᾳ), ἀλλʼ ἄνευ γενέσεως καὶ φθορᾶς εἰσὶ καὶ οὐκ εἰσίν· δέδεικται γὰρ ὅτι οὐδεὶς ταῦτα γεννᾷ οὐδὲ ποιεῖ. διὰ τοῦτο δὲ καὶ τῶν οὐσιῶν τῶν αἰσθητῶν τῶν καθʼ ἕκαστα οὔτε ὁρισμὸς οὔτε ἀπόδειξις ἔστιν, ὅτι ἔχουσιν ὕλην ἧς ἡ φύσις τοιαύτη ὥστʼ ἐνδέχεσθαι καὶ εἶναι καὶ μή· διὸ φθαρτὰ πάντα τὰ καθʼ ἕκαστα αὐτῶν.","kind":"passage"},{"id":"RAW_DAZY_BOO7:P0070","text":"εἰ οὖν ἥ τʼ ἀπόδειξις τῶν ἀναγκαίων καὶ ὁ ὁρισμὸς ἐπιστημονικόν, καὶ οὐκ ἐνδέχεται, ὥσπερ οὐδʼ ἐπιστήμην ὁτὲ μὲν ἐπιστήμην ὁτὲ δʼ ἄγνοιαν εἶναι, ἀλλὰ δόξα τὸ τοιοῦτόν ἐστιν, οὕτως οὐδʼ ἀπόδειξιν οὐδʼ ὁρισμόν, ἀλλὰ δόξα ἐστὶ τοῦ ἐνδεχομένου ἄλλως ἔχειν,  δῆλον ὅτι οὐκ ἂν εἴη αὐτῶν οὔτε ὁρισμὸς οὔτε ἀπόδειξις. ἄδηλά τε γὰρ τὰ φθειρόμενα τοῖς ἔχουσι τὴν ἐπιστήμην, ὅταν ἐκ τῆς αἰσθήσεως ἀπέλθῃ, καὶ σωζομένων τῶν λόγων ἐν τῇ ψυχῇ τῶν αὐτῶν οὐκ ἔσται οὔτε ὁρισμὸς ἔτι οὔτε ἀπόδειξις. διὸ δεῖ, τῶν πρὸς ὅρον ὅταν τις ὁρίζηταί τι τῶν καθʼ ἕκαστον, μὴ ἀγνοεῖν ὅτι ἀεὶ ἀναιρεῖν ἔστιν· οὐ γὰρ ἐνδέχεται ὁρίσασθαι.","kind":"passage"},{"id":"RAW_DAZY_BOO7:P0071","text":"οὐδὲ δὴ ἰδέαν οὐδεμίαν ἔστιν ὁρίσασθαι. τῶν γὰρ καθʼ ἕκαστον ἡ ἰδέα, ὡς φασί, καὶ χωριστή· ἀναγκαῖον δὲ ἐξ ὀνομάτων εἶναι τὸν λόγον, ὄνομα δʼ οὐ ποιήσει ὁ ὁριζόμενος (ἄγνωστον γὰρ ἔσται), τὰ δὲ κείμενα κοινὰ πᾶσιν· ἀνάγκη ἄρα ὑπάρχειν καὶ ἄλλῳ ταῦτα· οἷον εἴ τις σὲ ὁρίσαιτο, ζῷον ἐρεῖ ἰσχνὸν ἢ λευκὸν ἢ ἕτερόν τι ὃ καὶ ἄλλῳ ὑπάρξει. εἰ δέ τις φαίη μηδὲν κωλύειν χωρὶς μὲν πάντα πολλοῖς ἅμα δὲ μόνῳ τούτῳ ὑπάρχειν, λεκτέον πρῶτον μὲν ὅτι καὶ ἀμφοῖν, οἷον τὸ ζῷον δίπουν τῷ ζῴῳ καὶ τῷ δίποδι (καὶ τοῦτο ἐπὶ μὲν τῶν ἀϊδίων καὶ ἀνάγκη εἶναι, πρότερά γʼ ὄντα καὶ μέρη τοῦ συνθέτου· ἀλλὰ μὴν καὶ χωριστά, εἴπερ τὸ ἄνθρωπος χωριστόν· ἢ γὰρ οὐθὲν ἢ ἄμφω· εἰ μὲν οὖν μηθέν, οὐκ ἔσται τὸ γένος παρὰ τὰ εἴδη, εἰ δʼ ἔσται, καὶ ἡ διαφορά)· εἶθʼ ὅτι πρότερα τῷ εἶναι· ταῦτα δὲ οὐκ ἀνταναιρεῖται.","kind":"passage"},{"id":"RAW_DAZY_BOO7:P0072","text":"ἔπειτα εἰ ἐξ ἰδεῶν αἱ ἰδέαι (ἁσυνθετώτερα γὰρ τὰ ἐξ ὧν), ἔτι ἐπὶ πολλῶν δεήσει κἀκεῖνα κατηγορεῖσθαι ἐξ ὧν ἡ ἰδέα, οἷον τὸ ζῷον καὶ τὸ δίπουν. εἰ δὲ μή, πῶς γνωρισθήσεται; ἔσται γὰρ ἰδέα τις ἣν ἀδύνατον ἐπὶ πλειόνων κατηγορῆσαι ἢ ἑνός. οὐ δοκεῖ δέ, ἀλλὰ πᾶσα ἰδέα εἶναι μεθεκτή. ὥσπερ οὖν εἴρηται, λανθάνει ὅτι ἀδύνατον ὁρίσασθαι ἐν τοῖς ἀϊδίοις, μάλιστα δὲ ὅσα μοναχά, οἷον ἥλιος ἢ σελήνη. οὐ μόνον γὰρ διαμαρτάνουσι τῷ προστιθέναι τοιαῦτα ὧν ἀφαιρουμένων ἔτι ἔσται ἥλιος, ὥσπερ τὸ περὶ γῆν ἰὸν ἢ νυκτικρυφές (ἂν γὰρ στῇ ἢ φανῇ, οὐκέτι ἔσται ἥλιος· ἀλλʼ ἄτοπον εἰ μή· ὁ γὰρ ἥλιος οὐσίαν τινὰ σημαίνεi)· ἔτι ὅσα ἐπʼ ἄλλου ἐνδέχεται, οἷον ἐὰν ἕτερος γένηται τοιοῦτος, δῆλον ὅτι ἥλιος ἔσται· κοινὸς ἄρα ὁ λόγος·  ἀλλʼ ἦν τῶν καθʼ ἕκαστα ὁ ἥλιος, ὥσπερ Κλέων ἢ Σωκράτης· ἐπεὶ διὰ τί οὐδεὶς ὅρον ἐκφέρει αὐτῶν ἰδέας; γένοιτο γὰρ ἂν δῆλον πειρωμένων ὅτι ἀληθὲς τὸ νῦν εἰρημένον.","kind":"passage"},{"id":"RAW_DAZY_BOO7:P0073","text":"φανερὸν δὲ ὅτι καὶ τῶν δοκουσῶν εἶναι οὐσιῶν αἱ πλεῖσται δυνάμεις εἰσί, τά τε μόρια τῶν ζῴων (οὐθὲν γὰρ κεχωρισμένον αὐτῶν ἐστίν· ὅταν δὲ χωρισθῇ, καὶ τότε ὄντα ὡς ὕλη πάντα) καὶ γῆ καὶ πῦρ καὶ ἀήρ· οὐδὲν γὰρ αὐτῶν ἕν ἐστιν, ἀλλʼ οἷον σωρός, πρὶν ἢ πεφθῇ καὶ γένηταί τι ἐξ αὐτῶν ἕν. μάλιστα δʼ ἄν τις τὰ τῶν ἐμψύχων ὑπολάβοι μόρια καὶ τὰ τῆς ψυχῆς πάρεγγυς ἄμφω γίγνεσθαι, ὄντα καὶ ἐντελεχείᾳ καὶ δυνάμει, τῷ ἀρχὰς ἔχειν κινήσεως ἀπό τινος ἐν ταῖς καμπαῖς· διὸ ἔνια ζῷα διαιρούμενα ζῇ. ἀλλʼ ὅμως δυνάμει πάντʼ ἔσται, ὅταν ᾖ ἓν καὶ συνεχὲς φύσει, ἀλλὰ μὴ βίᾳ ἢ συμφύσει· τὸ γὰρ τοιοῦτον πήρωσις. ἐπεὶ δὲ τὸ ἓν λέγεται ὥσπερ καὶ τὸ ὄν, καὶ ἡ οὐσία ἡ τοῦ ἑνὸς μία, καὶ ὧν μία ἀριθμῷ ἓν ἀριθμῷ, φανερὸν ὅτι οὔτε τὸ ἓν οὔτε τὸ ὂν ἐνδέχεται οὐσίαν εἶναι τῶν πραγμάτων, ὥσπερ οὐδὲ τὸ στοιχείῳ εἶναι ἢ ἀρχῇ· ἀλλὰ ζητοῦμεν τίς οὖν ἡ ἀρχή, ἵνα εἰς γνωριμώτερον ἀναγάγωμεν.","kind":"passage"},{"id":"RAW_DAZY_BOO7:P0074","text":"μᾶλλον μὲν οὖν τούτων οὐσία τὸ ὂν καὶ ἓν ἢ ἥ τε ἀρχὴ καὶ τὸ στοιχεῖον καὶ τὸ αἴτιον, οὔπω δὲ οὐδὲ ταῦτα, εἴπερ μηδʼ ἄλλο κοινὸν μηδὲν οὐσία· οὐδενὶ γὰρ ὑπάρχει ἡ οὐσία ἀλλʼ ἢ αὑτῇ τε καὶ τῷ ἔχοντι αὐτήν, οὗ ἐστὶν οὐσία. ἔτι τὸ ἓν πολλαχῇ οὐκ ἂν εἴη ἅμα, τὸ δὲ κοινὸν ἅμα πολλαχῇ ὑπάρχει· ὥστε δῆλον ὅτι οὐδὲν τῶν καθόλου ὑπάρχει παρὰ τὰ καθʼ ἕκαστα χωρίς. ἀλλʼ οἱ τὰ εἴδη λέγοντες τῇ μὲν ὀρθῶς λέγουσι χωρίζοντες αὐτά, εἴπερ οὐσίαι εἰσί, τῇ δʼ οὐκ ὀρθῶς, ὅτι τὸ ἓν ἐπὶ πολλῶν εἶδος λέγουσιν. αἴτιον δʼ ὅτι οὐκ ἔχουσιν ἀποδοῦναι τίνες αἱ τοιαῦται οὐσίαι αἱ ἄφθαρτοι παρὰ τὰς καθʼ ἕκαστα καὶ αἰσθητάς· ποιοῦσιν οὖν τὰς αὐτὰς τῷ εἴδει τοῖς φθαρτοῖς (ταύτας γὰρ ἴσμεν), αὐτοάνθρωπον καὶ αὐτόϊππον, προστιθέντες τοῖς αἰσθητοῖς τὸ ῥῆμα τὸ αὐτό.","kind":"passage"},{"id":"RAW_DAZY_BOO7:P0075","text":"καίτοι κἂν εἰ μὴ ἑωράκειμεν τὰ ἄστρα,  οὐδὲν ἂν ἧττον, οἶμαι, ἦσαν οὐσίαι ἀΐδιοι παρʼ ἃς ἡμεῖς ᾔδειμεν· ὥστε καὶ νῦν εἰ μὴ ἔχομεν τίνες εἰσίν, ἀλλʼ εἶναί γέ τινας ἴσως ἀναγκαῖον. ὅτι μὲν οὖν οὔτε τῶν καθόλου λεγομένων οὐδὲν οὐσία οὔτʼ ἐστὶν οὐσία οὐδεμία ἐξ οὐσιῶν, δῆλον.","kind":"passage"},{"id":"RAW_DAZY_BOO7:P0076","text":"τί δὲ χρὴ λέγειν καὶ ὁποῖόν τι τὴν οὐσίαν, πάλιν ἄλλην οἷον ἀρχὴν ποιησάμενοι λέγωμεν· ἴσως γὰρ ἐκ τούτων ἔσται δῆλον καὶ περὶ ἐκείνης τῆς οὐσίας ἥτις ἐστὶ κεχωρισμένη τῶν αἰσθητῶν οὐσιῶν. ἐπεὶ οὖν ἡ οὐσία ἀρχὴ καὶ αἰτία τις ἐστίν, ἐντεῦθεν μετιτέον. ζητεῖται δὲ τὸ διὰ τί ἀεὶ οὕτως, διὰ τί ἄλλο ἄλλῳ τινὶ ὑπάρχει. τὸ γὰρ ζητεῖν διὰ τί ὁ μουσικὸς ἄνθρωπος μουσικὸς ἄνθρωπός ἐστιν, ἤτοι ἐστὶ τὸ εἰρημένον ζητεῖν, διὰ τί ὁ ἄνθρωπος μουσικός ἐστιν, ἢ ἄλλο. τὸ μὲν οὖν διὰ τί αὐτό ἐστιν αὐτό, οὐδέν ἐστι ζητεῖν (δεῖ γὰρ τὸ ὅτι καὶ τὸ εἶναι ὑπάρχειν δῆλα ὄντα—λέγω δʼ οἷον ὅτι ἡ σελήνη ἐκλείπει—, αὐτὸ δὲ ὅτι αὐτό, εἷς λόγος καὶ μία αἰτία ἐπὶ πάντων, διὰ τί ὁ ἄνθρωπος ἄνθρωπος ἢ ὁ μουσικὸς μουσικός, πλὴν εἴ τις λέγοι ὅτι ἀδιαίρετον πρὸς αὑτὸ ἕκαστον, τοῦτο δʼ ἦν τὸ ἑνὶ εἶναι· ἀλλὰ τοῦτο κοινόν γε κατὰ πάντων καὶ σύντομον)· ζητήσειε δʼ ἄν τις διὰ τί ἅνθρωπός ἐστι ζῷον τοιονδί.","kind":"passage"},{"id":"RAW_DAZY_BOO7:P0077","text":"τοῦτο μὲν τοίνυν δῆλον, ὅτι οὐ ζητεῖ διὰ τί ὅς ἐστιν ἄνθρωπος ἄνθρωπός ἐστιν· τὶ ἄρα κατά τινος ζητεῖ διὰ τί ὑπάρχει (ὅτι δʼ ὑπάρχει, δεῖ δῆλον εἶναι· εἰ γὰρ μὴ οὕτως, οὐδὲν ζητεῖ), οἷον διὰ τί βροντᾷ; διὰ τί ψόφος γίγνεται ἐν τοῖς νέφεσιν; ἄλλο γὰρ οὕτω κατʼ ἄλλου ἐστὶ τὸ ζητούμενον. καὶ διὰ τί ταδί, οἷον πλίνθοι καὶ λίθοι, οἰκία ἐστίν; φανερὸν τοίνυν ὅτι ζητεῖ τὸ αἴτιον· τοῦτο δʼ ἐστὶ τὸ τί ἦν εἶναι, ὡς εἰπεῖν λογικῶς, ὃ ἐπʼ ἐνίων μέν ἐστι τίνος ἕνεκα, οἷον ἴσως ἐπʼ οἰκίας ἢ κλίνης, ἐπʼ ἐνίων δὲ τί ἐκίνησε πρῶτον· αἴτιον γὰρ καὶ τοῦτο. ἀλλὰ τὸ μὲν τοιοῦτον αἴτιον ἐπὶ τοῦ γίγνεσθαι ζητεῖται καὶ φθείρεσθαι, θάτερον δὲ καὶ ἐπὶ τοῦ εἶναι. λανθάνει δὲ μάλιστα τὸ ζητούμενον ἐν τοῖς μὴ κατʼ ἀλλήλων λεγομένοις,  οἷον ἄνθρωπος τί ἐστι ζητεῖται διὰ τὸ ἁπλῶς λέγεσθαι ἀλλὰ μὴ διορίζειν ὅτι τάδε τόδε. ἀλλὰ δεῖ διαρθρώσαντας ζητεῖν· εἰ δὲ μή, κοινὸν τοῦ μηθὲν ζητεῖν καὶ τοῦ ζητεῖν τι γίγνεται.","kind":"passage"},{"id":"RAW_DAZY_BOO7:P0078","text":"ἐπεὶ δὲ δεῖ ἔχειν τε καὶ ὑπάρχειν τὸ εἶναι, δῆλον δὴ ὅτι τὴν ὕλην ζητεῖ διὰ τί τί ἐστιν· οἷον οἰκία ταδὶ διὰ τί; ὅτι ὑπάρχει ὃ ἦν οἰκίᾳ εἶναι. καὶ ἄνθρωπος τοδί, ἢ τὸ σῶμα τοῦτο τοδὶ ἔχον. ὥστε τὸ αἴτιον ζητεῖται τῆς ὕλης (τοῦτο δʼ ἐστὶ τὸ εἶδος) ᾧ τί ἐστιν· τοῦτο δʼ ἡ οὐσία.","kind":"passage"},{"id":"RAW_DAZY_BOO7:P0079","text":"φανερὸν τοίνυν ὅτι ἐπὶ τῶν ἁπλῶν οὐκ ἔστι ζήτησις οὐδὲ δίδαξις, ἀλλʼ ἕτερος τρόπος τῆς ζητήσεως τῶν τοιούτων.—ἐπεὶ δὲ τὸ ἔκ τινος σύνθετον οὕτως ὥστε ἓν εἶναι τὸ πᾶν,  μὴ ὡς σωρὸς ἀλλʼ ὡς ἡ συλλαβή—ἡ δὲ συλλαβὴ οὐκ ἔστι τὰ στοιχεῖα, οὐδὲ τῷ βα ταὐτὸ τὸ β καὶ α, οὐδʼ ἡ σὰρξ πῦρ καὶ γῆ (διαλυθέντων γὰρ τὰ μὲν οὐκέτι ἔστιν, οἷον ἡ σὰρξ καὶ ἡ συλλαβή, τὰ δὲ στοιχεῖα ἔστι, καὶ τὸ πῦρ καὶ ἡ γῆ)· ἔστιν ἄρα τι ἡ συλλαβή, οὐ μόνον τὰ στοιχεῖα τὸ φωνῆεν καὶ ἄφωνον ἀλλὰ καὶ ἕτερόν τι, καὶ ἡ σὰρξ οὐ μόνον πῦρ καὶ γῆ ἢ τὸ θερμὸν καὶ ψυχρὸν ἀλλὰ καὶ ἕτερόν τι—εἰ τοίνυν ἀνάγκη κἀκεῖνο ἢ στοιχεῖον ἢ ἐκ στοιχείων εἶναι, εἰ μὲν στοιχεῖον, πάλιν ὁ αὐτὸς ἔσται λόγος (ἐκ τούτου γὰρ καὶ πυρὸς καὶ γῆς ἔσται ἡ σὰρξ καὶ ἔτι ἄλλου, ὥστʼ εἰς ἄπειρον βαδιεῖταi)· εἰ δὲ ἐκ στοιχείου, δῆλον ὅτι οὐχ ἑνὸς ἀλλὰ πλειόνων, ἢ ἐκεῖνο αὐτὸ ἔσται, ὥστε πάλιν ἐπὶ τούτου τὸν αὐτὸν ἐροῦμεν λόγον καὶ ἐπὶ τῆς σαρκὸς ἢ συλλαβῆς.","kind":"passage"},{"id":"RAW_DAZY_BOO7:P0080","text":"δόξειε δʼ ἂν εἶναι τὶ τοῦτο καὶ οὐ στοιχεῖον, καὶ αἴτιόν γε τοῦ εἶναι τοδὶ μὲν σάρκα τοδὶ δὲ συλλαβήν· ὁμοίως δὲ καὶ ἐπὶ τῶν ἄλλων. οὐσία δὲ ἑκάστου μὲν τοῦτο (τοῦτο γὰρ αἴτιον πρῶτον τοῦ εἶναι)—ἐπεὶ δʼ ἔνια οὐκ οὐσίαι τῶν πραγμάτων, ἀλλʼ ὅσαι οὐσίαι, κατὰ φύσιν καὶ φύσει συνεστήκασι, φανείη ἂν  αὕτη ἡ φύσις οὐσία, ἥ ἐστιν οὐ στοιχεῖον ἀλλʼ ἀρχή—· στοιχεῖον δʼ ἐστὶν εἰς ὃ διαιρεῖται ἐνυπάρχον ὡς ὕλην, οἷον τῆς συλλαβῆς τὸ α καὶ τὸ β.","kind":"passage"}],"sha256":"383a2cdbd945faca91ae7ecad47f111924740914e115d86acd2178998a3e1e9d","language_code":"grc","language_name":"Ἑλληνική","translations":[{"id":"SOURCE_TR_20261015T181052495638Z_6d7d2dfc","language_code":"en","language_name":"English","title":"Book 7","source_sha256":"383a2cdbd945faca91ae7ecad47f111924740914e115d86acd2178998a3e1e9d","sha256":"ecc8c04ed6029281ff05d76786f3c563c0ce8a4da82cdd908f4fb18385fd8834","rendition_type":"machine","review_status":"machine","model":"gpt-5.6-terra","created":"2026-10-15T18:10:52.495650+00:00","paragraphs":[{"id":"RAW_DAZY_BOO7:P0001","text":"Being is said in many ways, just as we distinguished earlier in the discussion of the ways in which things are said; for it signifies, in one way, what a thing is and a this-something, and in another, a quality or quantity or each of the other things so predicated. Since being is said in so many ways, it is clear that the first among these is what a thing is, which signifies substance (for when we say what sort of a thing this is, we say either good or bad, but not three-cubit or human; but when we say what it is, we say not white nor hot nor three-cubit, but human or god); while the others are called beings because, of what is thus a being, some are quantities, others qualities, others affections, and others something else. Hence one might even be puzzled whether walking and being healthy and sitting signify, each of them, a being, and similarly in the case of any of the other such things; for none of them is either naturally capable of existing by itself or capable of being separated from substance, but rather, if anything is, it is the walking thing and the sitting thing and the healthy thing that are among beings.","kind":"passage","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_DAZY_BOO7:P0001"]},{"id":"RAW_DAZY_BOO7:P0002","text":"These appear more to be beings because there is some determinate underlying subject for them (and this is substance and the particular), which is made evident in such predication; for good or sitting is not said without this. It is clear, then, that each of those too is by reason of this, so that what is being primarily, and not a certain being but being without qualification, would be substance. Now “first” is said in many ways; nevertheless substance is first in every way, both in account and in knowledge and in time. For none of the other predicates is separable, but this alone is; and this is first also in account (for it is necessary that the account of substance be present in the account of each thing); and we think that we know each thing most of all when we know what a human being or fire is, rather than its quality or quantity or where, since we then know each of these things themselves too, when we know what the quantitative or the qualitative is.","kind":"passage","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_DAZY_BOO7:P0002"]},{"id":"RAW_DAZY_BOO7:P0003","text":"And indeed what was sought long ago and is now and always sought, and always a matter of perplexity—what being is—is this: what substance is (for this is what some say is one, others more than one, and some limited, others unlimited). Therefore it is also for us to consider, most of all and first and, so to speak, solely, what that which is in this way is.","kind":"passage","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_DAZY_BOO7:P0003"]},{"id":"RAW_DAZY_BOO7:P0004","text":"Substance seems to belong most evidently to bodies (and therefore we say that animals and plants and their parts are substances, and natural bodies such as fire and water and earth and each of such things, and whatever either are parts of these or are composed from these, either from parts or from all of them, such as the heaven and its parts, stars and moon and sun). But whether these alone are substances, or there are others too, or some of these and others too, or none of these but certain others, must be considered. To some it seems that the limits of body, such as surface and line and point and unit, are substances, and more so than body and solid.","kind":"passage","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_DAZY_BOO7:P0004"]},{"id":"RAW_DAZY_BOO7:P0005","text":"Further, besides perceptible things, some think that there is nothing of this sort, while others think there are more, and more eternal, beings: Plato, for example, makes the Forms and mathematical objects two substances, and the substance of perceptible bodies a third; Speusippus makes still more substances, beginning from the One, and principles for each substance—a different one for numbers, another for magnitudes, and then for soul—and in this way extends substances. Some say that Forms and numbers have the same nature, with the other things following upon them, lines and planes, down to the substance of heaven and perceptible things. Concerning these matters, then—what is said well or not well, what things are substances, whether there are any besides perceptible substances or not, how these exist, and whether there is some separable substance, and why and how, or none, besides perceptible things—we must consider, after first sketching what substance is.","kind":"passage","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_DAZY_BOO7:P0005"]},{"id":"RAW_DAZY_BOO7:P0006","text":"Substance is said, if not in more ways, at least chiefly in four; for the what-it-was-to-be, the universal, and the genus seem to be substance of each thing, and fourth among these is the underlying subject. The underlying subject is that of which the other things are said, while it itself is no longer said of another; therefore it must first be determined concerning this. For the primary underlying subject seems most of all to be substance. In one manner matter is said to be such, in another manner form, and in a third what is composed of these (by matter I mean, for example, bronze; by form, the shape of the figure; and by what is composed of these, the composite statue). Thus, if the form is prior to matter and is more a being, it will also be prior, for the same reason, to that composed of both. Now, then, what substance is has been stated in outline: namely, that which is not said of an underlying subject, but that of which the other things are said. But it must not be understood only in this way; for that is not sufficient. For this itself is unclear, and further, matter comes to be substance.","kind":"passage","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_DAZY_BOO7:P0006"]},{"id":"RAW_DAZY_BOO7:P0007","text":"For if this is not substance, what other thing is escapes us; for when the other things are removed, nothing appears to remain. The other things are affections, products, and powers of bodies; and length and breadth and depth are certain quantities, not substances (for quantity is not substance), but rather that to which these belong primarily, that is substance. Yet when length and breadth and depth are removed, we see nothing remaining, unless there is something delimited by these; hence, to those who consider the matter in this way, matter necessarily appears to be the only substance. By matter I mean that which, in itself, is said to be neither a something nor a quantity nor anything else by which being has been delimited. For there is something of which each of these is predicated, whose being is different from that of each of the categories (for the other things are predicated of substance, whereas this is predicated of matter); thus the ultimate thing is in itself neither a something nor a quantity nor anything else whatsoever. Nor indeed are the negations, for these too will belong accidentally.","kind":"passage","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_DAZY_BOO7:P0007"]},{"id":"RAW_DAZY_BOO7:P0008","text":"From these considerations, then, it follows for those who inquire thus that matter is substance. But this is impossible; for separability and being a this-something seem to belong most of all to substance. Hence form and what is composed of both would seem to be substance more than matter. The substance composed of both, namely that from matter and form, must then be set aside, for it is posterior and evident; and matter too is in a way evident. But the third must be investigated, for this is most perplexing. Some perceptible things are agreed to be substances, so that inquiry must first be made among these. For it is useful to proceed to what is more knowable. For learning comes about in this way for everyone: through what is less knowable by nature to what is more knowable. And this is a task, just as in actions it is a task to make things good for each person without qualification from things good for each person, so too to make things knowable to oneself by nature from things more knowable to oneself.","kind":"passage","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_DAZY_BOO7:P0008"]},{"id":"RAW_DAZY_BOO7:P0009","text":"But the things knowable and primary to each person are often only slightly knowable, and possess little or nothing of being; nevertheless, from what are poorly known without qualification but known to oneself, one must attempt to know what is known without qualification, proceeding, as was said, through these very things.","kind":"passage","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_DAZY_BOO7:P0009"]},{"id":"RAW_DAZY_BOO7:P0010","text":"Since at the beginning we distinguished how many ways we define substance, and one among these seemed to be the what-it-was-to-be, we must investigate this. First, let us say certain things about it logically: the what-it-was-to-be of each thing is what is said of it in itself. For being you is not being musical; for you are not musical in yourself. Therefore, what you are in yourself. Yet not even all of this; for it is not what is in itself in the way that white is in itself for a surface, because being a surface is not being white. But neither is it the compound of both, being a white surface, because the surface is added to it. Therefore, the account in which the thing itself will not be present, when the account states the thing itself, is the account of the what-it-was-to-be for each thing; so that if being a white surface is being a smooth surface, being white and smooth are the same and one.","kind":"passage","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_DAZY_BOO7:P0010"]},{"id":"RAW_DAZY_BOO7:P0011","text":"Since there are also compounds according to the other categories (for there is some underlying subject for each, for example for the qualitative, the quantitative, the when, the where, and motion), we must investigate whether there is an account of the what-it-was-to-be for each of them, and whether the what-it-was-to-be belongs to these too—for example, to white human. Let “cloak” be its name. What is being a cloak? Yet surely this too is not among the things said in themselves. Or is what is not said in itself said in two ways, one by addition and the other not? For in one way it is said because what is being defined is added to another thing—for example, if one defining being white were to give an account of a white human being; in the other because another thing is added to it—for example, if “cloak” signified a white human being, but one were to define cloak as white.","kind":"passage","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_DAZY_BOO7:P0011"]},{"id":"RAW_DAZY_BOO7:P0012","text":"A white human being is, to be sure, white, but being a white human being is not the what-it-was-to-be of white.—But is being a cloak perhaps the what-it-was-to-be of anything at all? Or not? For the what-it-was-to-be is what a thing is; but when one thing is said of another, it is not what is a this-something, for example, a white human being is not what is a this-something, if this-something belongs only to substances. Thus the what-it-was-to-be belongs to those whose account is a definition. But a definition does not arise if a name signifies the same thing as an account (for then all accounts would be definitions, since there will be a name for any account whatever, and thus even the Iliad will be a definition), but if it is an account of some primary thing. Such things are those that are said not by one thing's being said of another.","kind":"passage","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_DAZY_BOO7:P0012"]},{"id":"RAW_DAZY_BOO7:P0013","text":"Therefore the what-it-was-to-be will not belong to any things that are not species of a genus, but only to these (for these seem not to be said by participation or affection or as an accident). Yet there will be an account of each of the other things too, if it has a name, of what it signifies—that this belongs to that—or an account more precise than a simple account; but there will be no definition, nor what-it-was-to-be. Or are definition, like what a thing is, also said in many ways? For what a thing is signifies in one way substance and a this-something, and in another way each of the predicates—quantity, quality, and the other such things.","kind":"passage","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_DAZY_BOO7:P0013"]},{"id":"RAW_DAZY_BOO7:P0014","text":"For just as “is” belongs to all things, though not in the same way, but primarily to one and consequently to the others, so too what a thing is belongs without qualification to substance, but in a way to the others. For we might also ask what the qualitative is, so that the qualitative too is among what-a-thing-is; but not without qualification. Rather, just as some say, logically, that not-being is, not without qualification but as not-being, so too with the qualitative.—One must indeed consider also how one ought to speak about each thing, though not more than how it is. Therefore now, since what is meant is clear, the what-it-was-to-be will likewise belong primarily and without qualification to substance, and then also to the others, just as what a thing is does—not what-it-was-to-be without qualification, but the what-it-was-to-be of a qualitative or a quantitative.","kind":"passage","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_DAZY_BOO7:P0014"]},{"id":"RAW_DAZY_BOO7:P0015","text":"For we must either say that these are beings homonymously, or by adding and subtracting, as also the non-knowable is knowable; since what is correct is to say neither that they are homonymous nor that they are in the same way, but as “medical” is said in relation to one and the same thing, though not as one and the same thing, yet not homonymously either. For body and work and instrument are called medical neither homonymously nor in reference to one thing, but in relation to one thing. Yet it makes no difference in which of these ways someone wishes to speak. But this is clear: the definition primarily and without qualification, and the what-it-was-to-be, belong to substances. Yet they belong similarly to the other things too, though not primarily. For if we posit this, it is not necessary that there be a definition of that which signifies the same thing in an account, but rather in some account; and this occurs if it is an account of one thing—not by continuity, as the Iliad is, or whatever things are joined by a connective ι,ι, but if it is one in however many ways “one” is said. And “one” is said as “being” is; and being signifies, in one way, a this-something, in another a quantity, in another a certain quality.","kind":"passage","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_DAZY_BOO7:P0015"]},{"id":"RAW_DAZY_BOO7:P0016","text":"Hence there will be an account and a definition of white human too, but in a different way from those of white and of substance.","kind":"passage","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_DAZY_BOO7:P0016"]},{"id":"RAW_DAZY_BOO7:P0017","text":"There is a difficulty, however, if one denies that an account by addition is a definition: of which things that are not simple but combined will there be a definition? For they must be made clear by addition. I mean, for example, that there are nose and concavity, and snubness, which is said from the two together, by the presence of this in this, and not accidentally; for neither concavity nor snubness is an affection of nose accidentally, but in itself. Nor is this like white belonging to Callias, or to human, because Callias is white and it happens to him to be human; rather, it is as male belongs to animal and equal to quantity, and all things said to belong in themselves. These are cases in which either the account or the name of that of which this affection is belongs is present, and it cannot be made clear separately: just as white can be stated without human, but female cannot be stated without animal. Thus for these things there is either no what-it-was-to-be and no definition at all, or, if there is, it is otherwise, as we have said. But there is another difficulty concerning them as well.","kind":"passage","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_DAZY_BOO7:P0017"]},{"id":"RAW_DAZY_BOO7:P0018","text":"For if snub nose and concave nose are the same, snub and concave will be the same. But if not, because it is impossible to state snub without the thing of which it is an affection in itself (for snub is concavity in a nose), then either it is impossible to say “snub nose,” or the same thing will have been said twice, “nose nose concave” (for snub nose will be nose nose concave). Therefore it is absurd that such things should have a what-it-was-to-be; but if they do not, an infinite regress ensues, for something else will again be present in snub nose nose. It is clear, then, that definition belongs only to substance. For if it belongs also to the other categories, it must be by addition, for example, of †quality† and odd; for odd is not without number, nor female without animal (and by “by addition” I mean cases in which it happens that the same thing is said twice, as in these cases). But if this is true, there will be no definition even of combined things, for example of odd number. Yet it escapes notice that the accounts are not stated precisely.","kind":"passage","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_DAZY_BOO7:P0018"]},{"id":"RAW_DAZY_BOO7:P0019","text":"But if there are definitions of these things too, either they are in another manner, or, as was said, “definition” and “what-it-was-to-be” must be said in many ways. Thus in one way there will be no definition of anything, nor will the what-it-was-to-be belong to anything except substances; but in another way there will be. It is clear, then, that definition is the account of the what-it-was-to-be, and that the what-it-was-to-be belongs either only to substances or at least chiefly and primarily and without qualification to them.","kind":"passage","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_DAZY_BOO7:P0019"]},{"id":"RAW_DAZY_BOO7:P0020","text":"Whether the what-it-was-to-be and each thing are the same or different must be considered. For this is useful for the inquiry concerning substance: each thing seems not to be other than its own substance, and the what-it-was-to-be is said to be the substance of each thing.","kind":"passage","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_DAZY_BOO7:P0020"]},{"id":"RAW_DAZY_BOO7:P0021","text":"In the case of things said accidentally, the thing and its what-it-was-to-be would seem to be different, for example, white human and being a white human (for if they were the same, being human and being a white human would also be the same; for human and white human are the same, as they say, and therefore being a white human and being human too. Or is it not necessary that things which are the same accidentally are the same in this way? For the extremes do not become the same in the same manner. But perhaps that consequence would seem to follow: that the accidental extremes become the same, for example being white and being musical; yet this does not seem so). But in the case of things said in themselves, must they be the same? For example, if there are certain substances of which there are no other substances or prior natures, such as some say the Forms are? For if the Good itself and being good are different, and animal and being animal, and being and being-a-being, there will be other substances and natures and Forms besides those spoken of, and those will be prior substances, if the what-it-was-to-be is substance.","kind":"passage","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_DAZY_BOO7:P0021"]},{"id":"RAW_DAZY_BOO7:P0022","text":"And if they are separated from one another, there will be no knowledge of some, while the others will not be beings (by “separated” I mean if neither being good belongs to the Good itself nor being good to that). For there is knowledge of each thing when we know what it was for that thing to be; and it is likewise so in the case of the good and the others. Thus if being good is not good, neither is being-a-being being, nor being-one one; and all the what-it-was-to-bes are alike either beings or none is. Further, that to which being good does not belong is not good.","kind":"passage","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_DAZY_BOO7:P0022"]},{"id":"RAW_DAZY_BOO7:P0023","text":"It is necessary, then, that the good be one, and that being-good and being-fine be [one], and that the things which are said not in virtue of something else but in themselves and primarily [be one]. For this would be sufficient even if there were no Forms, or rather perhaps even if there are Forms. (At the same time it is also clear that, if the Ideas are such as certain people say, the underlying subject will not be substance; for these must be substances, but not said of an underlying subject, since they will be so by participation.)—And from these arguments it follows that, in each case, the thing itself and its what-it-was-to-be are one and the same not accidentally; and also that to know each thing is to know its what-it-was-to-be, so that even by exposition it is necessary that both be some one thing. (But what is said accidentally, such as the musical or the white, because it signifies in two ways, it is not true to say that its what-it-was-to-be and the thing itself are the same; for both that to which white has happened and the accident are [called white], so that in one way the what-it-was-to-be and the thing itself are the same, but in another way they are not; for [they are] not the same for man and white man,","kind":"passage","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_DAZY_BOO7:P0023"]},{"id":"RAW_DAZY_BOO7:P0024","text":"but they are the same for the affection.) And it would seem absurd if someone were to assign a name to each what-it-was-to-be; for there will be something else besides that, for example, another what-it-was-to-be for the what-it-was-to-be of horse. Yet what prevents some things even now from being immediately what-it-was-to-be, if what-it-was-to-be is substance? But indeed they are not only one, but their account too is the same, as is clear also from what has been said; for being-one and one are not one accidentally. Further, if they will be different, [the process] goes on to infinity; for the one will be the what-it-was-to-be of the one, and the other the one, so that the same account will hold of those too. It is clear, then, that in the case of primary things and things said in themselves, the being of each thing and each thing are the same and one. It is clear too that the sophistical refutations directed against this position are resolved by the same solution as [the question] whether Socrates and being-Socrates are the same; for there is no difference either in the questions one might ask or in the answers by which one might successfully solve [them]. In what way, then, the what-it-was-to-be is the same as each thing, and in what way it is not the same, has been stated.","kind":"passage","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_DAZY_BOO7:P0024"]},{"id":"RAW_DAZY_BOO7:P0025","text":"Of things that come to be, some come to be by nature, others by art, and others spontaneously; and all things that come to be come to be by something, from something, and something. By “something” I mean according to each category: either a this, or a quantity, or a quality, or where. Natural comings-to-be are those whose coming-to-be is from nature; that from which they come to be is what we call matter; that by which [they come to be] is one of the things that are by nature; and the something is man or plant or something else of that sort, which indeed we most of all call substances. And all things that come to be either by nature or by art have matter; for each of them can both be and not be, and this is the matter in each.—And universally, both that from which [a thing comes to be] is nature and that according to which [it comes to be] is nature (for what comes to be has a nature, for example a plant or an animal), and that by which [it comes to be] is the nature spoken of according to form, the same in kind (though this is in another); for a man begets a man.—Thus, then, things that come to be come to be through nature; the other comings-to-be are called productions.","kind":"passage","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_DAZY_BOO7:P0025"]},{"id":"RAW_DAZY_BOO7:P0026","text":"All productions are either from art, or from capacity, or from thought. Some of these also come to be spontaneously and by chance, in a manner similar to things that come to be from nature; for in those cases too some of the same things come to be both from seed and without seed. These matters must be considered later. But things whose form is in the soul come to be from art (by form I mean the what-it-was-to-be of each thing and the primary substance); for in a way the same form belongs also to contraries. For the substance of a privation is the opposed substance, as health [is the substance] of disease; for disease is the absence of that, while health is the account in the soul and the knowledge. The healthy thing comes to be when [the practitioner] has thought as follows: since this is health, if [the patient] is to be healthy, this must belong, for example uniformity; and if this, warmth. And thus he thinks continually, until he leads [the process] to that which he himself is able to make last. Then the motion from this point onward is called production, [the motion] toward being healthy.","kind":"passage","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_DAZY_BOO7:P0026"]},{"id":"RAW_DAZY_BOO7:P0027","text":"Consequently, in a way it follows that health comes to be from health, and a house from a house—the one having matter from the one without matter; for medicine and housebuilding are the form of health and of house. By substance without matter I mean the what-it-was-to-be.","kind":"passage","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_DAZY_BOO7:P0027"]},{"id":"RAW_DAZY_BOO7:P0028","text":"Of comings-to-be and motions, one is called thought and the other production: thought begins from the principle and the form, production from the last point of the thought. Likewise each of the other intermediate [stages] comes to be. I mean, for example, if [someone] is to be healthy, he must be made uniform. What then is being made uniform? This; and this will be if he is heated. And what is this? This. But this is present potentially; and this is already up to him.","kind":"passage","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_DAZY_BOO7:P0028"]},{"id":"RAW_DAZY_BOO7:P0029","text":"The producing cause, then, and that from which the motion toward being healthy begins, if it is from art, is the form in the soul; but if it is spontaneous, it begins from that which at any time initiates production for the producer acting from art, just as in medical treatment perhaps the beginning is from heating (and this it produces by rubbing). Thus the warmth in the body is either a part of health, or something of such a sort follows upon it which is a part of health, whether through several intervening steps or not. This is the last [stage], that which produces the part of health—and likewise [what produces] the part of a house (for example, the stones) and of the other things. Hence, as is said, it is impossible for something to come to be if nothing pre-exists. That some part must necessarily pre-exist is clear; for matter is a part (for it is present and comes to be). But is it also [a part] of the account? We speak in both ways of what bronze circles are, stating both the matter, that it is bronze, and the form, that it is such-and-such a shape; and this is the genus into which it is first placed.","kind":"passage","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_DAZY_BOO7:P0029"]},{"id":"RAW_DAZY_BOO7:P0030","text":"The bronze circle, then, has matter in its account.—But from that out of which some things come to be as matter, when they have come to be they are said to be not that thing but made of that thing; for example, a statue is not stone but stone-made. But the man who is becoming healthy is not said to be that from which [he comes to be]. The reason is that he comes to be both from the privation and from the underlying subject, which we call matter (for both the man and the sick man become healthy); yet he is said rather to come to be from the privation, for example, healthy from sick rather than from man. Hence the healthy [man] is not called sick, but man, and the man is healthy. But where the privation is obscure and nameless, as [the privation] of any sort of shape in bronze, or of a house in bricks and timber, [the product] seems to come to be from these, as there [the healthy man seems to come to be] from sick. Hence, just as in the former case that from which this [comes to be] is not called that [thing], so here too the statue is not timber, but is derivatively called wooden; and bronze, but not bronze [itself]; and stone-made, but not stone; and the house brick-made, but not bricks. For neither would one say without qualification, if one looked closely, that a statue comes to be from timber or a house","kind":"passage","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_DAZY_BOO7:P0030"]},{"id":"RAW_DAZY_BOO7:P0031","text":"from bricks, because that from which [a thing comes to be] must change, not remain. It is for this reason, then, that it is so spoken of.","kind":"passage","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_DAZY_BOO7:P0031"]},{"id":"RAW_DAZY_BOO7:P0032","text":"Since what comes to be comes to be by something (by which I mean that from which the principle of its coming-to-be derives), and from something (let this not be the privation but the matter—for the way in which we use this expression has already been distinguished), and something comes to be (and this is either a sphere or a circle or any of the other things whatsoever), just as [the producer] does not make the underlying subject, the bronze, so neither does he make the sphere, except accidentally, because the bronze sphere is a sphere and he makes that. For to make this particular thing out of the underlying subject simply is to make this particular thing. I mean that making the bronze round is not making the round or the sphere, but something else, namely this form in something else. For if he makes [the form], he will make it out of something else; for this was the underlying subject. For example, he makes a bronze sphere, and he does this in the sense that from this, which is bronze, he makes this, which is a sphere. If, then, he also makes this itself, it is clear that he will make [it] in the same way, and comings-to-be will proceed to infinity.","kind":"passage","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_DAZY_BOO7:P0032"]},{"id":"RAW_DAZY_BOO7:P0033","text":"It is clear, then, that neither the form—nor whatever one ought to call the shape in the perceptible thing—comes to be, nor is there a coming-to-be of it, nor [of] the what-it-was-to-be (for this is what comes to be in something else, either by art or by nature or by capacity). But he makes [the thing] be a bronze sphere; for he makes it from bronze and sphere. For he makes [it] into this form, and this is a bronze sphere. But if there is to be a coming-to-be of being-a-sphere simpliciter, something will come to be from something. For what comes to be must always be divisible, and one part must be this and another that—I mean, the one matter and the other form. If, then, a sphere is a figure equal [in all directions] from the middle, one part of it will be that in which what produces will be, another that in the former, and the whole will be the thing that has come to be, for example the bronze sphere.","kind":"passage","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_DAZY_BOO7:P0033"]},{"id":"RAW_DAZY_BOO7:P0034","text":"It is clear from what has been said, then, that what is spoken of as form or substance does not come to be, but the concrete whole spoken of in accordance with it comes to be; and that in everything generated matter is present, and one part is this and another that.—Is there then some sphere besides these [particular spheres], or some house besides the bricks? Or rather, if that were so, a particular thing would never come to be; but [the form] signifies such-and-such, and is not a particular and determinate thing; rather, [one] makes and generates from this such-and-such a thing, and when it has been generated, it is a particular such-and-such thing. But the whole particular thing, Callias or Socrates, is as this particular bronze sphere, while man and animal are as bronze sphere simpliciter. It is clear, then, that the cause of the Forms, as some are accustomed to call the Forms, if there are any besides particulars, is of no use with regard to comings-to-be and substances; nor would they, at least for this reason, be substances in themselves.","kind":"passage","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_DAZY_BOO7:P0034"]},{"id":"RAW_DAZY_BOO7:P0035","text":"In some cases it is also clear that what begets is such as what is begotten, though it is not the same thing nor one in number, but one in form—as among natural things; for man begets man—unless something comes to be contrary to nature, as a horse begets a mule. (And these cases are similar too; for whatever would be common to horse and ass, the nearest genus, has not been named; but perhaps it would be both, for example mule.) Hence it is clear that there is no need to posit a Form as a paradigm (for they would most of all be sought in these cases; for these are substances most of all); rather, the begetter is sufficient to make [the offspring] and to be the cause of the form in the matter. And the whole [thing] is now such-and-such a form in these flesh and bones, Callias and Socrates; and they are different because of their matter (for it is different), but the same in form (for the form is indivisible).","kind":"passage","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_DAZY_BOO7:P0035"]},{"id":"RAW_DAZY_BOO7:P0036","text":"One might raise the difficulty why some things come to be both by art and spontaneously, for example health, while others do not, for example a house. The reason is that, among the matters which govern the coming-to-be in producing and coming-to-be something by art, and in which there is present some part of the thing, one kind is such as to be moved by itself, while another is not; and of the former, one is capable of being moved in this way, while another is incapable. For many things are capable of being moved by themselves, but not in this way—for example, of dancing. Thus things whose matter is of such a kind, for example stones, cannot be moved in this way unless by another, though in another way they can; and likewise fire. For this reason, some things will not be [produced] without one who possesses the art, while others will be; for they will be moved by things that do not possess the art, though [those things] are capable of being moved by other things not possessing the art, or by a part [of themselves].","kind":"passage","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_DAZY_BOO7:P0036"]},{"id":"RAW_DAZY_BOO7:P0037","text":"It is also clear from what has been said that, in a way, all things come to be from a homonymous thing, as natural things do, or from a part which is homonymous—for example, a house from a house, insofar as [it is produced] by intellect; for the art is the form—or from something having some part [of it], unless it comes to be accidentally. For the cause of producing is, primarily and in itself, a part. For the warmth in the motion produced warmth in the body; and this is either health or a part [of health], or some part of health or health itself follows upon it. Hence it too is said to produce, because it produces that upon which [health] follows and to which it has happened. Therefore, just as in syllogisms, substance is the principle of all things; for syllogisms proceed from what a thing is, and here comings-to-be [proceed from it]. Natural compounds are similarly disposed.","kind":"passage","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_DAZY_BOO7:P0037"]},{"id":"RAW_DAZY_BOO7:P0038","text":"For seed produces as things from art do (for it has the form potentially, and that from which the seed comes is in a way homonymous—not that one must always seek [the relation] in the way that man [comes] from man; for woman too [comes] from man—unless it is a mutilation; hence a mule does not come from a mule). Things that come to be spontaneously, as in the former case, are those whose matter can also move itself with the motion which the seed moves; but those whose [matter] cannot [do so] cannot come to be otherwise than from themselves.—And the argument shows not only concerning substance that the form does not come to be, but the same account applies commonly and similarly to all the primary things, such as quantity, quality, and the other categories. For a bronze sphere comes to be, but not sphere or bronze; and similarly in the case of bronze, if it comes to be (for matter and form must always pre-exist); and likewise in the case of what a thing is, and of quality and quantity and the other categories alike. For it is not the quality that comes to be, but qualified wood; nor the quantity, but quantified wood or animal.","kind":"passage","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_DAZY_BOO7:P0038"]},{"id":"RAW_DAZY_BOO7:P0039","text":"But from these points one may grasp a peculiarity of substance: it is necessary that another substance, being in actuality, pre-exist and produce, for example an animal if an animal comes to be; but in the case of a quality or quantity this is not necessary, but only [something] potentially [such].","kind":"passage","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_DAZY_BOO7:P0039"]},{"id":"RAW_DAZY_BOO7:P0040","text":"Since a definition is an account, and every account has parts, and as the account stands to the thing so the part of the account likewise stands to the part of the thing, there is now a difficulty whether the account of the parts must be present in the account of the whole or not. For in some cases they seem to be present, and in others not. For the account of the circle does not contain that of the segments, whereas that of the syllable contains that of the letters; yet the circle too is divided into segments just as the syllable is into letters. Further, if the parts are prior to the whole, and the acute angle is a part of the right angle and the finger a part of the animal, the acute angle would be prior to the right angle and the finger to man. Yet those [wholes] seem to be prior; for [the parts] are defined from those, and they are prior in being also, since they can be without one another.—Or is “part” said in many ways, one way being what measures in respect of quantity? But let this be set aside; we must consider the things from which substance consists as parts.","kind":"passage","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_DAZY_BOO7:P0040"]},{"id":"RAW_DAZY_BOO7:P0041","text":"If, then, one thing is matter, another form, and another that which is from these, and if both matter and form and that which is from these are substance, then in one way matter too is said to be a part of something, while in another way it is not; rather, [the parts are] those from which the account of the form consists. For flesh is not a part of concavity (for it is the matter in which it comes to be), but it is a part of snubness; and bronze is a part of the concrete statue, but not of the statue spoken of as form (for one must speak of the form, and each thing insofar as it has form; but the material thing must never be spoken of in itself). Hence the account of the circle does not contain that of the segments, whereas that of the syllable contains that of the letters; for the letters are parts of the account, parts of the form, and not matter, whereas the segments are parts as matter in which [the form] supervenes; though they are closer to the form than bronze is when roundness comes to be in bronze.","kind":"passage","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_DAZY_BOO7:P0041"]},{"id":"RAW_DAZY_BOO7:P0042","text":"Yet in one way not all the letters of the syllable will be present in the account, for example these particular letters in wax or those in air; for these too are already parts of the syllable as perceptible matter. Nor is it because a line, if divided into its halves, is destroyed, or a man into bones and sinews and flesh, that they are for this reason composed of these as parts that are parts of the substance; rather, [they are composed] as of matter, and these are parts of the concrete whole, but no longer of the form and of that of which there is an account; hence they are not in the accounts either. Thus, in some accounts the account of such parts will be present, while in others it need not be present, unless [the account] is of the compound. For this reason some things consist of these as principles into which they perish, while others do not.","kind":"passage","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_DAZY_BOO7:P0042"]},{"id":"RAW_DAZY_BOO7:P0043","text":"Whatever things are jointly taken as form and matter, such as the snub or the bronze circle, are perishable into these, and matter is a part of them; but whatever things are not jointly taken with matter but are without matter, whose accounts are of the form alone, these are not perishable, either without qualification or at least not in this way. Thus these are principles and parts of the former, but neither parts nor principles of the form. And for this reason the clay statue is perishable into clay, and the sphere into bronze, and Callias into flesh and bones, and further the circle into its segments; for there is a certain circle which is jointly taken with matter. For “circle” is said homonymously both of that spoken of simply and of the particular circle, because there is no special name for the particulars.—What is true has now been stated; nevertheless, let us state it still more clearly by taking it up again.","kind":"passage","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_DAZY_BOO7:P0043"]},{"id":"RAW_DAZY_BOO7:P0044","text":"For whatever are parts of the account, and into which the account is divided, are prior, either all or some of them; but the account of the right angle is not divided into an account of the acute angle, but that of the acute angle into the right angle; for one defining the acute employs the right angle, since the acute is less than a right angle. Similarly also with the circle and the semicircle; for the semicircle is defined by the circle, and the finger by the whole, since such-and-such a part of a human being is a finger. Hence whatever are parts as matter and into which things are divided as into matter are posterior; but whatever are parts as parts of the account and of the substance according to the account are prior, either all or some of them.","kind":"passage","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_DAZY_BOO7:P0044"]},{"id":"RAW_DAZY_BOO7:P0045","text":"And since the soul of animals (for this is the substance of the animate thing) is the substance according to the account, and the form and the what-it-was-to-be for such-and-such a body (at any rate, if each part is to be defined well, it will not be defined without its function, which will not belong to it without perception), it follows that the parts of this are prior, either all or some of them, to the composite animal, and similarly in each particular case; but the body and its parts are posterior to this substance, and not the substance but the composite is divided into these as into matter.—Thus in one way these are prior to the composite, but in another way they are not (for they cannot exist when separated; for it is not a finger in every condition that is a finger of an animal, but the dead one is homonymous); while some are simultaneous, namely those which are dominant and in which the account and the substance are primarily present, for example, whether this be the heart or the brain; for it makes no difference which such thing it is.","kind":"passage","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_DAZY_BOO7:P0045"]},{"id":"RAW_DAZY_BOO7:P0046","text":"But man and horse and things thus spoken of in the case of particulars, though universally, are not substance but some composite of this account and this matter taken universally; whereas Socrates, already, is from the ultimate matter a particular, and similarly in the other cases.—There is, then, a part both of the form (and by form I mean the what-it-was-to-be) and of the composite composed of form and matter, and of matter itself. But only the parts of the form are parts of the account, and the account is of the universal; for being-a-circle and circle are the same, and being-a-soul and soul are the same. But of the composite, for example this particular circle and any particular thing, whether sensible or intelligible—I call intelligible such things as mathematical objects, and sensible such things as bronze and wooden ones—of these there is no definition; rather, they are known with thought or perception, and when they have departed from actuality it is not clear whether they are or are not; but they are always spoken of and known by the universal account.","kind":"passage","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_DAZY_BOO7:P0046"]},{"id":"RAW_DAZY_BOO7:P0047","text":"Matter in itself is unknowable. Matter is of two kinds, sensible and intelligible: sensible matter, for example, bronze and wood and all movable matter; intelligible matter, that which belongs to sensibles not insofar as they are sensible, for example mathematical objects. How things stand concerning whole and part, and concerning prior and posterior, has been stated; but in answer to the question, when someone asks whether the right angle and the circle and the animal are prior, or the parts into which they are divided and from which they consist, one must answer that it is not so without qualification.","kind":"passage","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_DAZY_BOO7:P0047"]},{"id":"RAW_DAZY_BOO7:P0048","text":"For if the soul too is an animal or an animate thing, or each thing is its own essence, and if circle is being-a-circle, and right angle is being-a-right-angle and the substance of the right angle, then one must say that something is posterior to something, for example to the things in the account and to some right angle (for there is both the right angle with matter, the bronze right angle, and that in particular lines); but the one without matter is posterior to the things in the account and prior to the particular parts, though it must not be said simply. But if the soul is different and is not an animal, then in this way too some things must be affirmed and others not, as has been stated.","kind":"passage","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_DAZY_BOO7:P0048"]},{"id":"RAW_DAZY_BOO7:P0049","text":"It is reasonably disputed which parts are parts of the form and which are not, but are parts of the composite. Yet unless this is clear, it is not possible to define each thing; for the definition is of the universal and the form. Therefore, unless it is evident which parts are as matter and which are not, neither will the account of the thing be evident. Now whatever appear to come to be upon things differing in form, such as a circle in bronze, stone, and wood, these seem clearly not to be anything of the substance of the circle—neither bronze nor stone—because they are separable from it. But regarding whatever are not seen as separable, nothing prevents their being similarly related to these, just as if all circles that were seen were bronze; for bronze would be none the less nothing of the form. But it is difficult to remove it in thought.","kind":"passage","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_DAZY_BOO7:P0049"]},{"id":"RAW_DAZY_BOO7:P0050","text":"For example, the form of a human being always appears in flesh and bones and such parts. Are these then also parts of the form and of the account? Or are they not, but matter; yet because it does not also come to be in other things, are we unable to separate them? And since this seems possible but it is unclear when, some already raise the difficulty even concerning the circle and triangle, as though it were not proper to define them by lines and the continuous, but as though all these too were spoken of in the same way as flesh and bones are of a human being, and bronze and stone of a statue. And they reduce all things to numbers, and say that the account of a line is that of two. And among those who posit the Forms, some say that the Dyad itself is the line itself, while others say it is the form of the line; for in some cases the form and that of which it is the form are the same, such as the Dyad and the form of the Dyad, but no longer in the case of a line.","kind":"passage","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_DAZY_BOO7:P0050"]},{"id":"RAW_DAZY_BOO7:P0051","text":"It follows, then, that there is one form of many things whose form appears different—which was also the consequence for the Pythagoreans—and that it is possible to make one thing itself the form of all things, with the others not being forms; yet in this way all things will be one.","kind":"passage","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_DAZY_BOO7:P0051"]},{"id":"RAW_DAZY_BOO7:P0052","text":"That matters concerning definitions involve a certain difficulty, and for what cause, has been stated. Hence it is superfluous to reduce all things in this way and remove matter; for perhaps some things are this in this, or are such-and-such things disposed in such-and-such a way. And the comparison concerning the animal which the younger Socrates used to make is not sound; for it leads away from the truth and makes one suppose it possible for man to be without his parts, just as a circle is without bronze. But the case is not similar; for an animal is something sensible, and it cannot be defined without motion, and therefore not without parts disposed in a certain way. For a hand is not in every condition a part of a human being, but only if it is capable of accomplishing its function, and thus is animate; if not animate, it is not a part. But why, in mathematical things, are the accounts not parts of the accounts, for example the semicircles of the circle? Is it because these are not sensible?","kind":"passage","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_DAZY_BOO7:P0052"]},{"id":"RAW_DAZY_BOO7:P0053","text":"Or does it make no difference? For there will be matter even of some things that are not sensible; indeed, there is some matter of everything which is not the what-it-was-to-be and form itself in itself, but a this-something. Thus there will not be parts of the universal circle, but these will be parts of particular circles, as was stated before; for matter is of two kinds, sensible and intelligible. It is clear also that the soul is primary substance, the body matter, and man or animal, taken universally, that which is from both; but Socrates and Coriscus, if the soul too is Socrates, are twofold (for some take them as soul, others as the composite), whereas if they are simply this soul and this body, they are as the universal and the particular. Whether there is, besides matter, some other substance of such substances, and whether one should seek some different substance, such as numbers or something of that sort, must be examined later.","kind":"passage","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_DAZY_BOO7:P0053"]},{"id":"RAW_DAZY_BOO7:P0054","text":"For it is for this reason that we are attempting to determine the matter concerning sensible substances, since inquiry concerning sensible substances is, in a way, the work of natural philosophy and second philosophy. For the natural philosopher must know not only about matter but also, and more so, about the substance according to the account. But how the items in the account are parts in definitions, and why the definition is one account—for it is clear that the thing is one, but by what is the thing one, since it has parts?—must be examined later.","kind":"passage","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_DAZY_BOO7:P0054"]},{"id":"RAW_DAZY_BOO7:P0055","text":"What the what-it-was-to-be is, then, and how it is in itself, has been stated universally concerning everything; and also why, in the case of some things, the account of the what-it-was-to-be contains the parts of the thing defined, while in other cases it does not; and that in the account of substance the parts that are thus as matter will not be present—for they are not parts of that substance, but of the composite substance. Of this composite there is in a way an account and in a way there is not: with matter there is not, for it is indeterminate, but according to the primary substance there is, for example the account of the soul in the case of man. For substance is the form that is present, from which and matter the composite substance is called substance, for example concavity (for from this and the nose there are a snub nose and snubness). But in the composite substance, for example a snub nose or Callias, matter too will be present. And the what-it-was-to-be and each thing are the same in some cases, namely in the case of primary substances, such as curvature and being-curvature, if it is primary (and by primary I mean that which is not said by being-other-in-other and in a subject as matter); but whatever are as","kind":"passage","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_DAZY_BOO7:P0055"]},{"id":"RAW_DAZY_BOO7:P0056","text":"matter or as jointly taken with matter are not the same, not even if they are one accidentally, such as Socrates and the musical; for these are the same accidentally.","kind":"passage","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_DAZY_BOO7:P0056"]},{"id":"RAW_DAZY_BOO7:P0057","text":"But now let us speak first to the extent that definition has not been discussed in the Analytics; for the difficulty stated there is useful for the discussions concerning substance. I mean this difficulty: why is that whose account we say is a definition one, for example, in the case of man, animal two-footed? For let this be his account. Why then is this one and not many, animal and two-footed? For in the case of man and white they are many when neither belongs to the other, but one when it belongs and the subject, man, undergoes something—for then one thing comes to be, the white man. But here neither partakes of the other; for the genus does not seem to partake of the differentiae (for then the same thing would at once partake of contraries, since the differentiae by which the genus differs are contraries). But even if it does partake, the same account applies, if the differentiae are more than one, for example footed, two-footed, wingless.","kind":"passage","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_DAZY_BOO7:P0057"]},{"id":"RAW_DAZY_BOO7:P0058","text":"For why are these one and not many? Not because they are inherent; for in that way one thing will be made out of all things. But all the items in the definition must be one; for definition is some one account and an account of substance, so it must be an account of something one; for substance signifies something one and a this-something, as we say.—But one must first examine definitions according to divisions. For there is nothing else in the definition besides the first genus spoken and the differentiae; the other genera are the first genus and, together with it, the differentiae taken in combination, for example the first is animal, the next animal two-footed, and again animal two-footed wingless; and similarly even if it is spoken through more terms. And in general it makes no difference whether it is spoken through many or through few, and consequently neither through few nor through two. Of the two, one is the differentia and the other the genus; for example, in “animal two-footed,” animal is genus and the other is differentia.","kind":"passage","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_DAZY_BOO7:P0058"]},{"id":"RAW_DAZY_BOO7:P0059","text":"If, then, the genus simply is not apart from the species as species of a genus, or if it is but is as matter (for sound is genus and matter, while the differentiae make the forms and elements from it), it is clear that the definition is the account composed of the differentiae. Yet one must divide by the differentia of the differentia, for example the differentia of animal is footed; again, the differentia of the footed animal must be that by which it is footed. Thus one must not say that of the footed, one kind is winged and another wingless, if one is to speak correctly—though because of inability one will do this—but rather that one kind is cloven-footed and another uncloven-footed; for these are differentiae of foot: cloven-footedness is a certain kind of footedness. And in this way one always wishes to proceed until one comes to what is without difference; then there will be as many species of foot as there are differentiae, and the footed animals will be equal in number to the differentiae.","kind":"passage","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_DAZY_BOO7:P0059"]},{"id":"RAW_DAZY_BOO7:P0060","text":"If this is so, it is clear that the last differentia will be the substance of the thing and the definition, if one must not say the same things repeatedly in the definitions; for that is superfluous. Yet this does happen: when one says “animal footed two-footed,” one has said nothing other than “animal having feet, having two feet”; and if one divides this by the proper division, one will say it more often, and as many times as there are differentiae. Thus, if there comes to be a differentia of a differentia, the last will be one, the form and the substance; but if it is accidental, for example if one divides the footed into white and black, there will be as many as there are divisions. Hence it is clear that definition is an account composed of differentiae, and, correctly speaking, of the last of these. This would be evident if someone transposed such definitions, for example that of man, saying “animal two-footed footed”; for “footed” is superfluous when “two-footed” has been said.","kind":"passage","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_DAZY_BOO7:P0060"]},{"id":"RAW_DAZY_BOO7:P0061","text":"But there is no order in substance; for how must one conceive the one as later and the other as prior? Let this suffice, then, as a first account of definitions according to divisions, of what sort they are.","kind":"passage","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_DAZY_BOO7:P0061"]},{"id":"RAW_DAZY_BOO7:P0062","text":"Since the inquiry is concerning substance, let us return again. Substance is said to be, as it were, the subject, the what-it-was-to-be, that which is from these, and the universal. Now concerning two of these we have spoken—for concerning both the what-it-was-to-be and the subject: that the subject underlies in two ways, either as a this-something, as the animal underlies its affections, or as matter underlies actuality. But the universal also seems to some to be especially a cause and to be a principle; therefore let us consider this too. For it seems impossible that any of the things spoken of universally should be substance. First, the substance of each thing is its own, belonging to that thing, and does not belong to another; but the universal is common, for that is called universal which is naturally fitted to belong to many things. Of what, then, will this be the substance? Either of all or of none; but it cannot be of all. And if it is of one, the others will also be that one; for things whose substance is one and whose what-it-was-to-be is one are themselves one.","kind":"passage","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_DAZY_BOO7:P0062"]},{"id":"RAW_DAZY_BOO7:P0063","text":"Further, substance is said to be what is not said of a subject, whereas the universal is always said of some subject. But perhaps it is not possible in this way, as the what-it-was-to-be, but it is present in this, for example animal in man and horse? Then clearly there is some account of it. And it makes no difference even if there is not an account of all the things in the substance; for it will no less be the substance of something, as man is the substance of the man in whom it is present, so that the same consequence will occur again: for it will be the substance of that, for example animal, in which it is present as its own. Further, it is both impossible and absurd that a this-something and a substance, if it is composed of certain things, should not be composed of substances or of this-somethings, but of a quality; for then non-substance and quality will be prior to substance and this-something. But this is impossible; for neither in account nor in time nor in generation can affections be prior to substance, since they would then be separable too. Further, there will be substance in Socrates as substance, so that there will be a substance of two things.","kind":"passage","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_DAZY_BOO7:P0063"]},{"id":"RAW_DAZY_BOO7:P0064","text":"In general, it follows that, if man and whatever is spoken of in this way is substance, none of the things in the account is the substance of anything, nor does it exist separately from them or in something else; I mean, for example, that there is no animal apart from the particular animals, nor anything else among the things in the accounts. From these considerations it is evident to those who examine them that none of the universals that belong is substance, and that none of the things commonly predicated signifies a this something, but rather a such something. Otherwise, many other consequences follow, including the Third Man.\n\nFurther, it is clear also in this way. For it is impossible for a substance to be composed of substances existing in actuality; for two things that are thus actual are never one thing in actuality, though if they are two potentially, they will be one (for example, the double is potentially composed of two halves; for actuality separates them). Hence, if substance is one, it will not be composed of substances existing in it, and in this way Democritus speaks correctly; for he says that it is impossible for one thing to come from two, or two from one; for he makes the indivisible magnitudes the substances.","kind":"passage","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_DAZY_BOO7:P0064"]},{"id":"RAW_DAZY_BOO7:P0065","text":"Similarly, then, it is clear that the case will be the same with number, if number is a composition of units, as some say; for either the dyad is not one, or there is not a unit in it in actuality.—But the consequence involves a difficulty. For if no substance can be composed from universals, because they signify a such something and not a this something, and if no composite substance can be composed of substances in actuality, every substance would be non-composite; hence there would be no account of any substance. Yet it seems to everyone, and it was said long ago, that either substance alone has a definition, or has one most of all; but now not even substance does. Therefore there will be a definition of nothing; or in one way there will be and in another way there will not. What is being said will become clearer from what follows.","kind":"passage","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_DAZY_BOO7:P0065"]},{"id":"RAW_DAZY_BOO7:P0066","text":"From these very considerations the consequence is also evident for those who say that the Forms are separate substances and at the same time make the Form out of the genus and the differentiae. For if there are Forms, and animal is in man and horse, then it is either one and the same numerically, or different. For in account it is clearly one; since the speaker will set out the same account in each case. If, then, there is some man himself, in his own right, a this something and separate, necessarily the things from which he is composed, such as animal and biped, also signify a this something and are separate and substances; and hence so too is animal.","kind":"passage","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_DAZY_BOO7:P0066"]},{"id":"RAW_DAZY_BOO7:P0067","text":"If, then, what is in horse and in man is the same and one, as you are one with yourself, how will the one be separately one in things that are, and why will this animal not also be separate from itself? Further, if it will partake of biped and many-footed, something impossible follows; for contraries will belong to it simultaneously, though it is one and a this something. But if not, what is the manner in which someone will say that animal is biped or footed? Perhaps, then, it is compounded, or connected, or mixed; but all these alternatives are absurd. But it is different in each case. Then there will be, so to speak, infinitely many things whose substance is animal; for man is not from animal accidentally. Further, animal-itself will be many things. For the animal in each case is substance (for it is not spoken of in virtue of something else; otherwise man will be from that, and that will be his genus), and again all the things out of which man is composed will be Forms. Therefore there will not be a Form of one thing and a substance of another (for that is impossible); consequently, animal-itself will be one thing in each of the animals.","kind":"passage","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_DAZY_BOO7:P0067"]},{"id":"RAW_DAZY_BOO7:P0068","text":"Further, from what is this, and how is it from animal-itself? Or how can animal, whose substance is animal-itself, exist apart from animal-itself? Further, in the case of sensibles these consequences occur, and consequences more absurd than these. If, then, it is impossible for things to be so, it is clear that there are no Forms of them in the way some assert.","kind":"passage","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_DAZY_BOO7:P0068"]},{"id":"RAW_DAZY_BOO7:P0069","text":"But since substance is one thing, and the concrete whole and the account another (by the former I mean substance when the account is taken together with the matter, and by the latter the account simply), those spoken of in the former way are subject to destruction—for they also come to be—whereas the account is not subject to destruction in this way (for neither does it come to be: it is not being-a-house that comes to be, but being this particular house); rather, they are and are not without coming to be and passing away; for it has been shown that no one generates or makes these.\n\nFor this reason there is neither definition nor demonstration of the particular sensible substances, because they have matter whose nature is such as to admit both of being and of not being; hence all their particulars are perishable.","kind":"passage","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_DAZY_BOO7:P0069"]},{"id":"RAW_DAZY_BOO7:P0070","text":"If, then, demonstration is of necessary things and definition is scientific, and if, just as knowledge cannot at one time be knowledge and at another time ignorance—such a condition belongs rather to opinion—so too neither demonstration nor definition can be such, but opinion is of what admits of being otherwise, it is clear that there could be neither definition nor demonstration of these things. For perishable things are unclear to those who possess knowledge, when they have departed from perception; and even if the same accounts are preserved in the soul, there will no longer be either a definition or a demonstration. Therefore, when someone defines any particular among things directed toward a definition, he must not fail to recognize that it is always possible to destroy it; for it is not possible to define it.","kind":"passage","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_DAZY_BOO7:P0070"]},{"id":"RAW_DAZY_BOO7:P0071","text":"Nor indeed is it possible to define any Form. For the Form is of particulars, as they say, and is separate; but the account must consist of names, and the definer will not coin a name (for it would be unknown), while the established names are common to all. Therefore these names must also belong to something else. For example, if someone were to define you, he will say “a thin animal,” or “a white animal,” or something else that will also belong to another. But if someone should say that nothing prevents all these predicates from belonging separately to many things, but together to this one thing alone, it must first be said that they belong to both, for example, “biped animal” belongs to animal and biped (and in the case of eternal things this must be so, since they are prior and parts of the composite; indeed, they are also separate, if man is separate—for either neither is separate, or both are; if neither, the genus will not exist apart from the Forms, while if it does, the differentia will too); and then, that they are prior in being. But these are not reciprocally destroyed.","kind":"passage","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_DAZY_BOO7:P0071"]},{"id":"RAW_DAZY_BOO7:P0072","text":"Further, if Forms are composed of Forms (for the things out of which they are composed are more non-composite), then the things out of which the Form is composed, such as animal and biped, must also be predicated of many things. Otherwise, how will they be known? For there will be some Form which it is impossible to predicate of more than one thing. But this does not seem so; rather, every Form seems to be participable.\n\nThus, as has been said, it escapes notice that it is impossible to define eternal things, especially those that are unique, such as the sun or moon. For they err not only by adding such things that, if removed, the sun will still be sun, such as “moving around the earth” or “concealed at night” (for if it stands still or is visible, it will no longer be sun; but this is absurd, if it is not sun; for sun signifies a certain substance); further, they add things that can belong to another, for example, if another thing should come to be such, it is clear that it will be a sun. The account, then, is common; but the sun was one of the particulars, like Cleon or Socrates. Again, why does none of them offer a definition of their Form? For if they tried, it would become clear that what has now been said is true.","kind":"passage","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_DAZY_BOO7:P0072"]},{"id":"RAW_DAZY_BOO7:P0073","text":"It is also clear that most of the things thought to be substances are potentials: the parts of animals (for none of them exists separately; and when they are separated, then too they all exist as matter), and earth, fire, and air. For none of these is one, but rather like a heap, before it has been concocted and some one thing has come to be from them. One might especially suppose that the parts of living things and the things close to soul are both actual and potential, because they have principles of motion from some source in their joints; hence some animals live when divided. But nevertheless all will be potentially such whenever they are one and naturally continuous, and not by force or by growing together; for such a condition is a mutilation.\n\nBut since one is spoken of in the same ways as being, and the substance of the one is one, and things whose substance is one numerically are one numerically, it is clear that neither one nor being can be the substance of things, just as neither can being an element or a principle. Rather, we seek what the principle is, in order that we may reduce it to something more knowable.","kind":"passage","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_DAZY_BOO7:P0073"]},{"id":"RAW_DAZY_BOO7:P0074","text":"Being and one, then, are substance to a greater degree than principle, element, and cause; but not even these are substance, if indeed no other common thing is substance either. For substance belongs to nothing except itself and that which has it, of which it is the substance. Further, the one could not be simultaneously in many places, whereas the common belongs simultaneously in many places. Hence it is clear that none of the universals exists separately apart from particulars.\n\nBut those who posit the Forms speak correctly in separating them, if they are substances; but incorrectly in calling a Form one over many. The reason is that they cannot say what such imperishable substances are, apart from the particular and sensible ones. They therefore make them the same in form as the perishable things (for these are familiar to us), man-itself and horse-itself, adding to sensibles the word “itself.”","kind":"passage","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_DAZY_BOO7:P0074"]},{"id":"RAW_DAZY_BOO7:P0075","text":"And yet, even if we had not seen the stars, there would nonetheless, I think, be eternal substances besides those we knew. Thus, even now, if we do not know what they are, it is perhaps necessary that there be some. It is clear, then, that none of the things spoken of universally is substance, and that no substance is composed of substances.","kind":"passage","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_DAZY_BOO7:P0075"]},{"id":"RAW_DAZY_BOO7:P0076","text":"But what we ought to say substance is, and what sort of thing it is, let us say again, making, as it were, another beginning; for perhaps from these considerations it will be clear also concerning that substance which is separate from sensible substances. Since, then, substance is a certain principle and cause, we must proceed from this point. For the question “why?” is always sought in this way: why does one thing belong to another? For to ask why a musical man is a musical man is either to ask the question already stated, why a man is musical, or something else. Now, to ask why a thing itself is itself is to ask nothing (for the that and the being must be present and manifest—I mean, for example, that the moon is eclipsed); but why it is itself has one account and one cause in all cases—why man is man, or the musical is musical—unless someone says that each thing is indivisible in relation to itself, and that this was being one; but this is common to all things and is a short answer. One might rather ask why man is an animal of such-and-such a kind.","kind":"passage","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_DAZY_BOO7:P0076"]},{"id":"RAW_DAZY_BOO7:P0077","text":"This, then, is clear: he is not asking why whoever is a man is a man. He asks, then, why something belongs to something (and that it belongs must be clear; for if not, he asks nothing); for example, why does it thunder? Why does a noise arise in the clouds? For what is sought in this way is one thing of another. And why are these things—bricks and stones, for example—a house? It is clear, then, that he seeks the cause. This is, to speak logically, the what-it-was-to-be, which in some cases is that for the sake of which, perhaps in the case of a house or a bed, and in other cases what first moved; for this too is a cause. But the latter sort of cause is sought in connection with coming to be and passing away, the other also in connection with being.\n\nWhat is sought escapes notice especially in things not spoken of one of another; for example, “what is man?” is sought because it is spoken simply and does not distinguish that this is this. But one must articulate the question before seeking; otherwise, there comes to be no difference between seeking nothing and seeking something.","kind":"passage","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_DAZY_BOO7:P0077"]},{"id":"RAW_DAZY_BOO7:P0078","text":"Since being must be present and belong, it is clear that he seeks why the matter is some particular thing. For example, why are these things a house? Because that which was being a house belongs to them. And why is this a man, or why is this body, having this character, a man? Thus the cause of the matter is sought (and this is the form), by which it is something; and this is the substance.","kind":"passage","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_DAZY_BOO7:P0078"]},{"id":"RAW_DAZY_BOO7:P0079","text":"It is clear, then, that in the case of simple things there is no inquiry or teaching, but another manner of inquiry concerning such things.—But since a thing composed of something is such that the whole is one, not like a heap but like a syllable—and a syllable is not its elements, nor is ba the same as b and a, nor is flesh fire and earth (for when they are dissolved, the former things, such as flesh and the syllable, no longer exist, whereas the elements do exist, both fire and earth)—there is, then, something that the syllable is, not merely its elements, vowel and consonant, but something else; and flesh is not merely fire and earth, or hot and cold, but something else.\n\nIf, then, that other thing must itself be either an element or composed of elements, if it is an element, the same argument will recur (for flesh will be composed of this and fire and earth and yet another thing, and so the process will proceed to infinity); but if it is composed of elements, it is clear that it will not be composed of one but of several, or else it will be that thing itself, so that again we shall give the same account in its case as in that of flesh or syllable.","kind":"passage","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_DAZY_BOO7:P0079"]},{"id":"RAW_DAZY_BOO7:P0080","text":"This would seem to be something and not an element, and indeed the cause of this being flesh and that being a syllable; and similarly in the other cases. And this is the substance of each thing (for this is the first cause of being). But since some things are not substances, whereas those things are substances which are constituted according to nature and by nature, this nature would appear to be substance, which is not an element but a principle.—An element is that into which a thing is divided, being present in it as matter, as a and b are elements of a syllable.","kind":"passage","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_DAZY_BOO7:P0080"]}],"source_digest":"904c8f440b7137c7761b9e021d2973ae297dc9f3a827d694e2412f3a44fe0e78","source_language_code":"grc","original_title":"Book 7"}],"passage_basis":"frozen-grounding","original_title":"Book 7","title_language_code":"en"},{"id":"RAW_DAZY_BOO8","title":"Book 8","order":8,"chapter_id":"CH_DAZY_BOO8","text":"ἐκ δὴ τῶν εἰρημένων συλλογίσασθαι δεῖ καὶ συναγαγόντας τὸ κεφάλαιον τέλος ἐπιθεῖναι. εἴρηται δὴ ὅτι τῶν οὐσιῶν ζητεῖται τὰ αἴτια καὶ αἱ ἀρχαὶ καὶ τὰ στοιχεῖα. οὐσίαι δὲ αἱ μὲν ὁμολογούμεναί εἰσιν ὑπὸ πάντων, περὶ δὲ ἐνίων ἰδίᾳ τινὲς ἀπεφήναντο· ὁμολογούμεναι μὲν αἱ φυσικαί, οἷον πῦρ γῆ ὕδωρ ἀὴρ καὶ τἆλλα τὰ ἁπλᾶ σώματα, ἔπειτα τὰ φυτὰ καὶ τὰ μόρια αὐτῶν, καὶ τὰ ζῷα καὶ τὰ μόρια τῶν ζῴων, καὶ τέλος ὁ οὐρανὸς καὶ τὰ μόρια τοῦ οὐρανοῦ· ἰδίᾳ δέ τινες οὐσίας λέγουσιν εἶναι τά τʼ εἴδη καὶ τὰ μαθηματικά. ἄλλας δὲ δὴ συμβαίνει ἐκ τῶν λόγων οὐσίας εἶναι, τὸ τί ἦν εἶναι καὶ τὸ ὑποκείμενον· ἔτι ἄλλως τὸ γένος μᾶλλον τῶν εἰδῶν καὶ τὸ καθόλου τῶν καθʼ ἕκαστα· τῷ δὲ καθόλου καὶ τῷ γένει καὶ αἱ ἰδέαι συνάπτουσιν (κατὰ τὸν αὐτὸν γὰρ λόγον οὐσίαι δοκοῦσιν εἶναι). ἐπεὶ δὲ τὸ τί ἦν εἶναι οὐσία, τούτου δὲ λόγος ὁ ὁρισμός, διὰ τοῦτο περὶ ὁρισμοῦ καὶ περὶ τοῦ καθʼ αὑτὸ διώρισται· ἐπεὶ δὲ ὁ ὁρισμὸς λόγος, ὁ δὲ λόγος μέρη ἔχει, ἀναγκαῖον καὶ περὶ μέρους ἦν ἰδεῖν, ποῖα τῆς οὐσίας μέρη καὶ ποῖα οὔ, καὶ εἰ ταῦτα καὶ τοῦ ὁρισμοῦ. ἔτι τοίνυν οὔτε τὸ καθόλου οὐσία οὔτε τὸ γένος· περὶ δὲ τῶν ἰδεῶν καὶ τῶν μαθηματικῶν ὕστερον σκεπτέον· παρὰ γὰρ τὰς αἰσθητὰς οὐσίας ταύτας λέγουσί τινες εἶναι.—νῦν δὲ περὶ τῶν ὁμολογουμένων οὐσιῶν ἐπέλθωμεν. αὗται δʼ εἰσὶν αἱ αἰσθηταί· αἱ δʼ αἰσθηταὶ οὐσίαι πᾶσαι ὕλην ἔχουσιν. ἔστι δʼ οὐσία τὸ ὑποκείμενον, ἄλλως μὲν ἡ ὕλη (ὕλην δὲ λέγω ἣ μὴ τόδε τι οὖσα ἐνεργείᾳ δυνάμει ἐστὶ τόδε τι), ἄλλως δʼ ὁ λόγος καὶ ἡ μορφή, ὃ τόδε τι ὂν τῷ λόγῳ χωριστόν ἐστιν· τρίτον δὲ τὸ ἐκ τούτων, οὗ γένεσις μόνου καὶ φθορά ἐστι, καὶ χωριστὸν ἁπλῶς· τῶν γὰρ κατὰ τὸν λόγον οὐσιῶν αἱ μὲν αἱ δʼ οὔ. ὅτι δʼ ἐστὶν οὐσία καὶ ἡ ὕλη, δῆλον· ἐν πάσαις γὰρ ταῖς ἀντικειμέναις μεταβολαῖς ἐστί τι τὸ ὑποκείμενον ταῖς μεταβολαῖς, οἷον κατὰ τόπον τὸ νῦν μὲν ἐνταῦθα πάλιν δʼ ἄλλοθι, καὶ κατʼ αὔξησιν ὃ νῦν μὲν τηλικόνδε πάλιν δʼ ἔλαττον ἢ μεῖζον, καὶ κατʼ ἀλλοίωσιν ὃ νῦν μὲν ὑγιὲς πάλιν δὲ κάμνον·  ὁμοίως δὲ καὶ κατʼ οὐσίαν ὃ νῦν μὲν ἐν γενέσει πάλιν δʼ ἐν φθορᾷ, καὶ νῦν μὲν ὑποκείμενον ὡς τόδε τι πάλιν δʼ ὑποκείμενον ὡς κατὰ στέρησιν. καὶ ἀκολουθοῦσι δὴ ταύτῃ αἱ ἄλλαι μεταβολαί, τῶν δʼ ἄλλων ἢ μιᾷ ἢ δυοῖν αὕτη οὐκ ἀκολουθεῖ· οὐ γὰρ ἀνάγκη, εἴ τι ὕλην ἔχει τοπικήν, τοῦτο καὶ γεννητὴν καὶ φθαρτὴν ἔχειν. τίς μὲν οὖν διαφορὰ τοῦ ἁπλῶς γίγνεσθαι καὶ μὴ ἁπλῶς, ἐν τοῖς φυσικοῖς εἴρηται. \n\n\n\n \n\n \n\nἐπεὶ δʼ ἡ μὲν ὡς ὑποκειμένη καὶ ὡς ὕλη οὐσία ὁμολογεῖται,  αὕτη δʼ ἐστὶν ἡ δυνάμει, λοιπὸν τὴν ὡς ἐνέργειαν οὐσίαν τῶν αἰσθητῶν εἰπεῖν τίς ἐστιν. Δημόκριτος μὲν οὖν τρεῖς διαφορὰς ἔοικεν οἰομένῳ εἶναι (τὸ μὲν γὰρ ὑποκείμενον σῶμα, τὴν ὕλην, ἓν καὶ ταὐτόν, διαφέρειν δὲ ἢ ῥυσμῷ, ὅ ἐστι σχῆμα, ἢ τροπῇ, ὅ ἐστι θέσις, ἢ διαθιγῇ, ὅ ἐστι τάξις)· φαίνονται δὲ πολλαὶ διαφοραὶ οὖσαι, οἷον τὰ μὲν συνθέσει λέγεται τῆς ὕλης, ὥσπερ ὅσα κράσει καθάπερ μελίκρατον, τὰ δὲ δεσμῷ οἷον φάκελος, τὰ δὲ κόλλῃ οἷον βιβλίον, τὰ δὲ γόμφῳ οἷον κιβώτιον, τὰ δὲ πλείοσι τούτων, τὰ δὲ θέσει οἷον οὐδὸς καὶ ὑπέρθυρον (ταῦτα γὰρ τῷ κεῖσθαί πως διαφέρει), τὰ δὲ χρόνῳ οἷον δεῖπνον καὶ ἄριστον, τὰ δὲ τόπῳ οἷον τὰ πνεύματα· τὰ δὲ τοῖς τῶν αἰσθητῶν πάθεσιν οἷον σκληρότητι καὶ μαλακότητι, καὶ πυκνότητι καὶ ἀραιότητι, καὶ ξηρότητι καὶ ὑγρότητι, καὶ τὰ μὲν ἐνίοις τούτων τὰ δὲ πᾶσι τούτοις, καὶ ὅλως τὰ μὲν ὑπεροχῇ τὰ δὲ ἐλλείψει. ὥστε δῆλον ὅτι καὶ τὸ ἔστι τοσαυταχῶς λέγεται· οὐδὸς γὰρ ἔστιν ὅτι οὕτως κεῖται, καὶ τὸ εἶναι τὸ οὕτως αὐτὸ κεῖσθαι σημαίνει, καὶ τὸ κρύσταλλον εἶναι τὸ οὕτω πεπυκνῶσθαι. ἐνίων δὲ τὸ εἶναι καὶ πᾶσι τούτοις ὁρισθήσεται, τῷ τὰ μὲν μεμῖχθαι, τὰ δὲ κεκρᾶσθαι, τὰ δὲ δεδέσθαι, τὰ δὲ πεπυκνῶσθαι, τὰ δὲ ταῖς ἄλλαις διαφοραῖς κεχρῆσθαι, ὥσπερ χεὶρ ἢ πούς. ληπτέα οὖν τὰ γένη τῶν διαφορῶν (αὗται γὰρ ἀρχαὶ ἔσονται τοῦ εἶναι), οἷον τὰ τῷ μᾶλλον καὶ ἧττον ἢ πυκνῷ καὶ μανῷ καὶ τοῖς ἄλλοις τοῖς τοιούτοις· πάντα γὰρ ταῦτα ὑπεροχὴ καὶ ἔλλειψίς ἐστιν. εἰ δέ τι σχήματι ἢ λειότητι καὶ τραχύτητι, πάντα εὐθεῖ καὶ καμπύλῳ. τοῖς δὲ τὸ εἶναι τὸ μεμῖχθαι ἔσται, ἀντικειμένως δὲ τὸ μὴ εἶναι.  φανερὸν δὴ ἐκ τούτων ὅτι εἴπερ ἡ οὐσία αἰτία τοῦ εἶναι ἕκαστον, ὅτι ἐν τούτοις ζητητέον τί τὸ αἴτιον τοῦ εἶναι τούτων ἕκαστον. οὐσία μὲν οὖν οὐδὲν τούτων οὐδὲ συνδυαζόμενον, ὅμως δὲ τὸ ἀνάλογον ἐν ἑκάστῳ· καὶ ὡς ἐν ταῖς οὐσίαις τὸ τῆς ὕλης κατηγορούμενον αὐτὴ ἡ ἐνέργεια, καὶ ἐν τοῖς ἄλλοις ὁρισμοῖς μάλιστα. οἷον εἰ οὐδὸν δέοι ὁρίσασθαι, ξύλον ἢ λίθον ὡδὶ κείμενον ἐροῦμεν, καὶ οἰκίαν πλίνθους καὶ ξύλα ὡδὶ κείμενα (ἢ ἔτι καὶ τὸ οὗ ἕνεκα ἐπʼ ἐνίων ἔστιν), εἰ δὲ κρύσταλλον, ὕδωρ πεπηγὸς ἢ πεπυκνωμένον ὡδί· συμφωνία δὲ ὀξέος καὶ βαρέος μῖξις τοιαδί· τὸν αὐτὸν δὲ τρόπον καὶ ἐπὶ τῶν ἄλλων. φανερὸν δὴ ἐκ τούτων ὅτι ἡ ἐνέργεια ἄλλη ἄλλης ὕλης καὶ ὁ λόγος· τῶν μὲν γὰρ ἡ σύνθεσις τῶν δʼ ἡ μῖξις τῶν δὲ ἄλλο τι τῶν εἰρημένων. διὸ τῶν ὁριζομένων οἱ μὲν λέγοντες τί ἐστιν οἰκία, ὅτι λίθοι πλίνθοι ξύλα, τὴν δυνάμει οἰκίαν λέγουσιν, ὕλη γὰρ ταῦτα· οἱ δὲ ἀγγεῖον σκεπαστικὸν χρημάτων καὶ σωμάτων ἤ τι ἄλλο τοιοῦτον προτιθέντες, τὴν ἐνέργειαν λέγουσιν· οἱ δʼ ἄμφω ταῦτα συντιθέντες τὴν τρίτην καὶ τὴν ἐκ τούτων οὐσίαν (ἔοικε γὰρ ὁ μὲν διὰ τῶν διαφορῶν λόγος τοῦ εἴδους καὶ τῆς ἐνεργείας εἶναι, ὁ δʼ ἐκ τῶν ἐνυπαρχόντων τῆς ὕλης μᾶλλον)· ὁμοίως δὲ καὶ οἵους Ἀρχύτας ἀπεδέχετο ὅρους· τοῦ συνάμφω γάρ εἰσιν. οἷον τί ἐστι νηνεμία; ἠρεμία ἐν πλήθει ἀέρος· ὕλη μὲν γὰρ ὁ ἀήρ, ἐνέργεια δὲ καὶ οὐσία ἡ ἠρεμία. τί ἐστι γαλήνη; ὁμαλότης θαλάττης· τὸ μὲν ὑποκείμενον ὡς ὕλη ἡ θάλαττα, ἡ δὲ ἐνέργεια καὶ ἡ μορφὴ ἡ ὁμαλότης. φανερὸν δὴ ἐκ τῶν εἰρημένων τίς ἡ αἰσθητὴ οὐσία ἐστὶ καὶ πῶς· ἡ μὲν γὰρ ὡς ὕλη, ἡ δʼ ὡς μορφὴ καὶ ἐνέργεια, ἡ δὲ τρίτη ἡ ἐκ τούτων. \n\n\n\n \n\n \n\nδεῖ δὲ μὴ ἀγνοεῖν ὅτι ἐνίοτε λανθάνει πότερον σημαίνει τὸ ὄνομα τὴν σύνθετον οὐσίαν ἢ τὴν ἐνέργειαν καὶ τὴν μορφήν, οἷον ἡ οἰκία πότερον σημεῖον τοῦ κοινοῦ ὅτι σκέπασμα ἐκ πλίνθων καὶ λίθων ὡδὶ κειμένων, ἢ τῆς ἐνεργείας καὶ τοῦ εἴδους ὅτι σκέπασμα, καὶ γραμμὴ πότερον δυὰς ἐν μήκει ἢ  δυάς, καὶ ζῷον πότερον ψυχὴ ἐν σώματι ἢ ψυχή· αὕτη γὰρ οὐσία καὶ ἐνέργεια σώματός τινος. εἴη δʼ ἂν καὶ ἐπʼ ἀμφοτέροις τὸ ζῷον, οὐχ ὡς ἑνὶ λόγῳ λεγόμενον ἀλλʼ ὡς πρὸς ἕν. ἀλλὰ ταῦτα πρὸς μέν τι ἄλλο διαφέρει, πρὸς δὲ τὴν ζήτησιν τῆς οὐσίας τῆς αἰσθητῆς οὐδέν·  τὸ γὰρ τί ἦν εἶναι τῷ εἴδει καὶ τῇ ἐνεργείᾳ ὑπάρχει. ψυχὴ μὲν γὰρ καὶ ψυχῇ εἶναι ταὐτόν, ἀνθρώπῳ δὲ καὶ ἄνθρωπος οὐ ταὐτόν, εἰ μὴ καὶ ἡ ψυχὴ ἄνθρωπος λεχθήσεται· οὕτω δὲ τινὶ μὲν τινὶ δʼ οὔ.—οὐ φαίνεται δὴ ζητοῦσιν ἡ συλλαβὴ ἐκ τῶν στοιχείων οὖσα καὶ συνθέσεως, οὐδʼ ἡ οἰκία πλίνθοι τε καὶ σύνθεσις. καὶ τοῦτο ὀρθῶς· οὐ γάρ ἐστιν ἡ σύνθεσις οὐδʼ ἡ μῖξις ἐκ τούτων ὧν ἐστὶ σύνθεσις ἢ μῖξις. ὁμοίως δὲ οὐδὲ τῶν ἄλλων οὐθέν, οἷον εἰ ὁ οὐδὸς θέσει, οὐκ ἐκ τοῦ οὐδοῦ ἡ θέσις ἀλλὰ μᾶλλον οὗτος ἐξ ἐκείνης. οὐδὲ δὴ ὁ ἄνθρωπός ἐστι τὸ ζῷον καὶ δίπουν, ἀλλά τι δεῖ εἶναι ὃ παρὰ ταῦτά ἐστιν, εἰ ταῦθʼ ὕλη, οὔτε δὲ στοιχεῖον οὔτʼ ἐκ στοιχείου, ἀλλʼ ἡ οὐσία· ὃ ἐξαιροῦντες τὴν ὕλην λέγουσιν. εἰ οὖν τοῦτʼ αἴτιον τοῦ εἶναι, καὶ οὐσία τοῦτο, αὐτὴν ἂν τὴν οὐσίαν οὐ λέγοιεν. (ἀνάγκη δὴ ταύτην ἢ ἀΐδιον εἶναι ἢ φθαρτὴν ἄνευ τοῦ φθείρεσθαι καὶ γεγονέναι ἄνευ τοῦ γίγνεσθαι. δέδεικται δὲ καὶ δεδήλωται ἐν ἄλλοις ὅτι τὸ εἶδος οὐθεὶς ποιεῖ οὐδὲ γεννᾷ, ἀλλὰ ποιεῖται τόδε, γίγνεται δὲ τὸ ἐκ τούτων. εἰ δʼ εἰσὶ τῶν φθαρτῶν αἱ οὐσίαι χωρισταί, οὐδέν πω δῆλον· πλὴν ὅτι γʼ ἐνίων οὐκ ἐνδέχεται δῆλον, ὅσα μὴ οἷόν τε παρὰ τὰ τινὰ εἶναι, οἷον οἰκίαν ἢ σκεῦος. ἴσως μὲν οὖν οὐδʼ οὐσίαι εἰσὶν οὔτʼ αὐτὰ ταῦτα οὔτε τι τῶν ἄλλων ὅσα μὴ φύσει συνέστηκεν· τὴν γὰρ φύσιν μόνην ἄν τις θείη τὴν ἐν τοῖς φθαρτοῖς οὐσίαν.) ὥστε ἡ ἀπορία ἣν οἱ Ἀντισθένειοι καὶ οἱ οὕτως ἀπαίδευτοι ἠπόρουν ἔχει τινὰ καιρόν, ὅτι οὐκ ἔστι τὸ τί ἔστιν ὁρίσασθαι (τὸν γὰρ ὅρον λόγον εἶναι μακρόν), ἀλλὰ ποῖον μέν τί ἐστιν ἐνδέχεται καὶ διδάξαι, ὥσπερ ἄργυρον, τί μέν ἐστιν οὔ, ὅτι δʼ οἷον καττίτερος· ὥστʼ οὐσίας ἔστι μὲν ἧς ἐνδέχεται εἶναι ὅρον καὶ λόγον, οἷον τῆς συνθέτου, ἐάν τε αἰσθητὴ ἐάν τε νοητὴ ᾖ· ἐξ ὧν δʼ αὕτη πρώτων, οὐκέτι, εἴπερ τὶ κατὰ τινὸς σημαίνει ὁ λόγος ὁ ὁριστικὸς καὶ δεῖ τὸ μὲν ὥσπερ ὕλην εἶναι τὸ δὲ ὡς μορφήν.—φανερὸν δὲ καὶ διότι, εἴπερ εἰσί πως ἀριθμοὶ αἱ οὐσίαι, οὕτως εἰσὶ καὶ οὐχ ὥς τινες λέγουσι μονάδων· ὅ τε γὰρ ὁρισμὸς ἀριθμός τις· διαιρετός τε γὰρ καὶ εἰς ἀδιαίρετα (οὐ γὰρ ἄπειροι οἱ λόγοι), καὶ ὁ ἀριθμὸς δὲ τοιοῦτον. καὶ ὥσπερ οὐδʼ ἀπʼ ἀριθμοῦ ἀφαιρεθέντος τινὸς ἢ προστεθέντος ἐξ ὧν ὁ ἀριθμός ἐστιν, οὐκέτι ὁ αὐτὸς ἀριθμός ἐστιν ἀλλʼ ἕτερος, κἂν τοὐλάχιστον ἀφαιρεθῇ ἢ προστεθῇ,  οὕτως οὐδὲ ὁ ὁρισμὸς οὐδὲ τὸ τί ἦν εἶναι οὐκέτι ἔσται ἀφαιρεθέντος τινὸς ἢ προστεθέντος. καὶ τὸν ἀριθμὸν δεῖ εἶναί τι ᾧ εἷς, ὃ νῦν οὐκ ἔχουσι λέγειν τίνι εἷς, εἴπερ ἐστὶν εἷς (ἢ γὰρ οὐκ ἔστιν ἀλλʼ οἷον σωρός, ἢ εἴπερ ἐστί, λεκτέον τί τὸ ποιοῦν ἓν ἐκ πολλῶν)· καὶ ὁ ὁρισμὸς εἷς ἐστίν, ὁμοίως δὲ οὐδὲ τοῦτον ἔχουσι λέγειν. καὶ τοῦτο εἰκότως συμβαίνει· τοῦ αὐτοῦ γὰρ λόγου, καὶ ἡ οὐσία ἓν οὕτως, ἀλλʼ οὐχ ὡς λέγουσί τινες οἷον μονάς τις οὖσα ἢ στιγμή, ἀλλʼ ἐντελέχεια καὶ φύσις τις ἑκάστη. καὶ ὥσπερ οὐδὲ ὁ ἀριθμὸς ἔχει τὸ μᾶλλον καὶ ἧττον, οὐδʼ ἡ κατὰ τὸ εἶδος οὐσία, ἀλλʼ εἴπερ, ἡ μετὰ τῆς ὕλης. περὶ μὲν οὖν γενέσεως καὶ φθορᾶς τῶν λεγομένων οὐσιῶν, πῶς τʼ ἐνδέχεται καὶ πῶς ἀδύνατον, καὶ περὶ τῆς εἰς τὸν ἀριθμὸν ἀναγωγῆς, ἔστω μέχρι τούτων διωρισμένον.\n\n\n\n \n\n \n\nπερὶ δὲ τῆς ὑλικῆς οὐσίας δεῖ μὴ λανθάνειν ὅτι εἰ καὶ ἐκ τοῦ αὐτοῦ πάντα πρώτου ἢ τῶν αὐτῶν ὡς πρώτων καὶ ἡ αὐτὴ ὕλη ὡς ἀρχὴ τοῖς γιγνομένοις, ὅμως ἔστι τις οἰκεία ἑκάστου, οἷον φλέγματος  τὰ γλυκέα ἢ λιπαρά, χολῆς δὲ τὰ πικρὰ ἢ ἄλλʼ ἄττα· ἴσως δὲ ταῦτα ἐκ τοῦ αὐτοῦ. γίγνονται δὲ πλείους ὗλαι τοῦ αὐτοῦ ὅταν θατέρου ἡ ἑτέρα ᾖ, οἷον φλέγμα ἐκ λιπαροῦ καὶ γλυκέος εἰ τὸ λιπαρὸν ἐκ τοῦ γλυκέος, ἐκ δὲ χολῆς τῷ ἀναλύεσθαι εἰς τὴν πρώτην ὕλην τὴν χολήν. διχῶς γὰρ τόδʼ ἐκ τοῦδε, ἢ ὅτι πρὸ ὁδοῦ ἔσται ἢ ὅτι ἀναλυθέντος εἰς τὴν ἀρχήν. ἐνδέχεται δὲ μιᾶς τῆς ὕλης οὔσης ἕτερα γίγνεσθαι διὰ τὴν κινοῦσαν αἰτίαν, οἷον ἐκ ξύλου καὶ κιβωτὸς καὶ κλίνη. ἐνίων δʼ ἑτέρα ἡ ὕλη ἐξ ἀνάγκης ἑτέρων ὄντων, οἷον πρίων οὐκ ἂν γένοιτο ἐκ ξύλου, οὐδʼ ἐπὶ τῇ κινούσῃ αἰτίᾳ τοῦτο· οὐ γὰρ ποιήσει πρίονα ἐξ ἐρίου ἢ ξύλου. εἰ δʼ ἄρα τὸ αὐτὸ ἐνδέχεται ἐξ ἄλλης ὕλης ποιῆσαι, δῆλον ὅτι ἡ τέχνη καὶ ἡ ἀρχὴ ἡ ὡς κινοῦσα ἡ αὐτή· εἰ γὰρ καὶ ἡ ὕλη ἑτέρα καὶ τὸ κινοῦν, καὶ τὸ γεγονός.—ὅταν δή τις ζητῇ τὸ αἴτιον, ἐπεὶ πλεοναχῶς τὰ αἴτια λέγεται, πάσας δεῖ λέγειν τὰς ἐνδεχομένας αἰτίας. οἷον ἀνθρώπου τίς αἰτία ὡς ὕλη; ἆρα τὰ καταμήνια; τί δʼ ὡς κινοῦν; ἆρα τὸ σπέρμα; τί δʼ ὡς τὸ εἶδος; τὸ τί ἦν εἶναι. τί δʼ ὡς οὗ ἕνεκα; τὸ τέλος.  ἴσως δὲ ταῦτα ἄμφω τὸ αὐτό. δεῖ δὲ τὰ ἐγγύτατα αἴτια λέγειν. τίς ἡ ὕλη; μὴ πῦρ ἢ γῆν ἀλλὰ τὴν ἴδιον. περὶ μὲν οὖν τὰς φυσικὰς οὐσίας καὶ γενητὰς ἀνάγκη οὕτω μετιέναι εἴ τις μέτεισιν ὀρθῶς, εἴπερ ἄρα αἴτιά τε ταῦτα καὶ τοσαῦτα καὶ δεῖ τὰ αἴτια γνωρίζειν· ἐπὶ δὲ τῶν φυσικῶν μὲν ἀϊδίων δὲ οὐσιῶν ἄλλος λόγος. ἴσως γὰρ ἔνια οὐκ ἔχει ὕλην, ἢ οὐ τοιαύτην ἀλλὰ μόνον κατὰ τόπον κινητήν. οὐδʼ ὅσα δὴ φύσει μέν, μὴ οὐσίαι δέ, οὐκ ἔστι τούτοις ὕλη, ἀλλὰ τὸ ὑποκείμενον ἡ οὐσία. οἷον τί αἴτιον ἐκλείψεως, τίς ὕλη; οὐ γὰρ ἔστιν, ἀλλʼ ἡ σελήνη τὸ πάσχον. τί δʼ αἴτιον ὡς κινῆσαν καὶ φθεῖραν τὸ φῶς; ἡ γῆ. τὸ δʼ οὗ ἕνεκα ἴσως οὐκ ἔστιν. τὸ δʼ ὡς εἶδος ὁ λόγος, ἀλλὰ ἄδηλος ἐὰν μὴ μετὰ τῆς αἰτίας ᾖ ὁ λόγος. οἷον τί ἔκλειψις; στέρησις φωτός. ἐὰν δὲ προστεθῇ τὸ ὑπὸ γῆς ἐν μέσῳ γιγνομένης, ὁ σὺν τῷ αἰτίῳ λόγος οὗτος. ὕπνου δʼ ἄδηλον τί τὸ πρῶτον πάσχον. ἀλλʼ ὅτι τὸ ζῷον; ναί, ἀλλὰ τοῦτο κατὰ τί, καὶ τί πρῶτον; καρδία ἢ ἄλλο τι. εἶτα ὑπὸ τίνος; εἶτα τί τὸ πάθος, τὸ ἐκείνου καὶ μὴ τοῦ ὅλου; ὅτι ἀκινησία τοιαδί; ναί, ἀλλʼ αὕτη τῷ τί πάσχειν τὸ πρῶτον; \n\n\n\n \n\n \n\nἐπεὶ δʼ ἔνια ἄνευ γενέσεως καὶ φθορᾶς ἔστι καὶ οὐκ ἔστιν, οἷον αἱ στιγμαί, εἴπερ εἰσί, καὶ ὅλως τὰ εἴδη (οὐ γὰρ τὸ λευκὸν γίγνεται ἀλλὰ τὸ ξύλον λευκόν, εἰ ἔκ τινος καὶ τὶ πᾶν τὸ γιγνόμενον γίγνεται), οὐ πάντα ἂν τἀναντία γίγνοιτο ἐξ ἀλλήλων, ἀλλʼ ἑτέρως λευκὸς ἄνθρωπος ἐκ μέλανος ἀνθρώπου καὶ λευκὸν ἐκ μέλανος· οὐδὲ παντὸς ὕλη ἔστιν ἀλλʼ ὅσων γένεσις ἔστι καὶ μεταβολὴ εἰς ἄλληλα· ὅσα δʼ ἄνευ τοῦ μεταβάλλειν ἔστιν ἢ μή, οὐκ ἔστι τούτων ὕλη.—ἔχει δʼ ἀπορίαν πῶς πρὸς τἀναντία ἡ ὕλη ἡ ἑκάστου ἔχει. οἷον εἰ τὸ σῶμα δυνάμει ὑγιεινόν, ἐναντίον δὲ νόσος ὑγιείᾳ, ἆρα ἄμφω δυνάμει; καὶ τὸ ὕδωρ δυνάμει οἶνος καὶ ὄξος; ἢ τοῦ μὲν καθʼ ἕξιν καὶ κατὰ τὸ εἶδος ὕλη, τοῦ δὲ κατὰ στέρησιν καὶ φθορὰν τὴν παρὰ φύσιν; ἀπορία δέ τις ἔστι καὶ διὰ τί ὁ οἶνος οὐχ ὕλη τοῦ ὄξους οὐδὲ δυνάμει ὄξος (καίτοι γίγνεται ἐξ αὐτοῦ ὄξος) καὶ ὁ ζῶν δυνάμει νεκρός. ἢ οὔ, ἀλλὰ κατὰ συμβεβηκὸς αἱ φθοραί,  ἡ δὲ τοῦ ζῴου ὕλη αὐτὴ κατὰ φθορὰν νεκροῦ δύναμις καὶ ὕλη, καὶ τὸ ὕδωρ ὄξους· γίγνεται γὰρ ἐκ τούτων ὥσπερ ἐξ ἡμέρας νύξ. καὶ ὅσα δὴ οὕτω μεταβάλλει εἰς ἄλληλα, εἰς τὴν ὕλην δεῖ ἐπανελθεῖν, οἷον εἰ ἐκ νεκροῦ ζῷον, εἰς τὴν ὕλην πρῶτον, εἶθʼ οὕτω ζῷον· καὶ τὸ ὄξος εἰς ὕδωρ, εἶθʼ οὕτως οἶνος. \n\n\n\n \n\n \n\nπερὶ δὲ τῆς ἀπορίας τῆς εἰρημένης περί τε τοὺς ὁρισμοὺς καὶ περὶ τοὺς ἀριθμούς, τί αἴτιον τοῦ ἓν εἶναι; πάντων γὰρ ὅσα πλείω μέρη ἔχει καὶ μὴ ἔστιν οἷον σωρὸς τὸ πᾶν ἀλλʼ ἔστι τι τὸ ὅλον παρὰ τὰ μόρια, ἔστι τι αἴτιον, ἐπεὶ καὶ ἐν τοῖς σώμασι τοῖς μὲν ἁφὴ αἰτία τοῦ ἓν εἶναι τοῖς δὲ γλισχρότης ἤ τι πάθος ἕτερον τοιοῦτον. ὁ δʼ ὁρισμὸς λόγος ἐστὶν εἷς οὐ συνδέσμῳ καθάπερ ἡ Ἰλιὰς ἀλλὰ τῷ ἑνὸς εἶναι. τί οὖν ἐστὶν ὃ ποιεῖ ἓν τὸν ἄνθρωπον, καὶ διὰ τί ἓν ἀλλʼ οὐ πολλά, οἷον τό τε ζῷον καὶ τὸ δίπουν, ἄλλως τε δὴ καὶ εἰ ἔστιν, ὥσπερ φασί τινες, αὐτό τι ζῷον καὶ αὐτὸ δίπουν; διὰ τί γὰρ οὐκ ἐκεῖνα αὐτὰ ὁ ἄνθρωπός ἐστι, καὶ ἔσονται κατὰ μέθεξιν οἱ ἄνθρωποι οὐκ ἀνθρώπου οὐδʼ ἑνὸς ἀλλὰ δυοῖν, ζῴου καὶ δίποδος, καὶ ὅλως δὴ οὐκ ἂν εἴη ὁ ἄνθρωπος ἓν ἀλλὰ πλείω, ζῷον καὶ δίπουν; φανερὸν δὴ ὅτι οὕτω μὲν μετιοῦσιν ὡς εἰώθασιν ὁρίζεσθαι καὶ λέγειν, οὐκ ἐνδέχεται ἀποδοῦναι καὶ λῦσαι τὴν ἀπορίαν· εἰ δʼ ἐστίν, ὥσπερ λέγομεν, τὸ μὲν ὕλη τὸ δὲ μορφή, καὶ τὸ μὲν δυνάμει τὸ δὲ ἐνεργείᾳ, οὐκέτι ἀπορία δόξειεν ἂν εἶναι τὸ ζητούμενον. ἔστι γὰρ αὕτη ἡ ἀπορία ἡ αὐτὴ κἂν εἰ ὁ ὅρος εἴη ἱματίου στρογγύλος χαλκός· εἴη γὰρ ἂν σημεῖον τοὔνομα τοῦτο τοῦ λόγου, ὥστε τὸ ζητούμενόν ἐστι τί αἴτιον τοῦ ἓν εἶναι τὸ στρογγύλον καὶ τὸν χαλκόν. οὐκέτι δὴ ἀπορία φαίνεται, ὅτι τὸ μὲν ὕλη τὸ δὲ μορφή. τί οὖν τούτου αἴτιον, τοῦ τὸ δυνάμει ὂν ἐνεργείᾳ εἶναι, παρὰ τὸ ποιῆσαν, ἐν ὅσοις ἔστι γένεσις; οὐθὲν γάρ ἐστιν αἴτιον ἕτερον τοῦ τὴν δυνάμει σφαῖραν ἐνεργείᾳ εἶναι σφαῖραν, ἀλλὰ τοῦτʼ ἦν τὸ τί ἦν εἶναι ἑκατέρῳ. ἔστι δὲ τῆς ὕλης ἡ μὲν νοητὴ ἡ δʼ αἰσθητή, καὶ ἀεὶ τοῦ λόγου τὸ μὲν ὕλη τὸ δὲ ἐνέργειά ἐστιν, οἷον ὁ κύκλος σχῆμα ἐπίπεδον. ὅσα δὲ μὴ ἔχει ὕλην μήτε νοητὴν μήτε αἰσθητήν, εὐθὺς ὅπερ ἕν τί  ἐστιν ἕκαστον,  ὥσπερ καὶ ὅπερ ὄν τι, τὸ τόδε, τὸ ποιόν, τὸ ποσόν—διὸ καὶ οὐκ ἔνεστιν ἐν τοῖς ὁρισμοῖς οὔτε τὸ ὂν οὔτε τὸ ἕν—, καὶ τὸ τί ἦν εἶναι εὐθὺς ἕν τί ἐστιν ὥσπερ καὶ ὄν τι—διὸ καὶ οὐκ ἔστιν ἕτερόν τι αἴτιον τοῦ ἓν εἶναι οὐθενὶ τούτων οὐδὲ τοῦ ὄν τι εἶναι· εὐθὺς γὰρ ἕκαστόν ἐστιν ὄν τι καὶ ἕν τι, οὐχ ὡς ἐν γένει τῷ ὄντι καὶ τῷ ἑνί, οὐδʼ ὡς χωριστῶν ὄντων παρὰ τὰ καθʼ ἕκαστα. διὰ ταύτην δὲ τὴν ἀπορίαν οἱ μὲν μέθεξιν λέγουσι, καὶ αἴτιον τί τῆς μεθέξεως καὶ τί τὸ μετέχειν ἀποροῦσιν· οἱ δὲ συνουσίαν  , ὥσπερ Λυκόφρων φησὶν εἶναι τὴν ἐπιστήμην τοῦ ἐπίστασθαι καὶ ψυχῆς· οἱ δὲ σύνθεσιν ἢ σύνδεσμον ψυχῆς σώματι τὸ ζῆν. καίτοι ὁ αὐτὸς λόγος ἐπὶ πάντων· καὶ γὰρ τὸ ὑγιαίνειν ἔσται ἢ συνουσία ἢ σύνδεσμος ἢ σύνθεσις ψυχῆς καὶ ὑγιείας, καὶ τὸ τὸν χαλκὸν εἶναι τρίγωνον σύνθεσις χαλκοῦ καὶ τριγώνου, καὶ τὸ λευκὸν εἶναι σύνθεσις ἐπιφανείας καὶ λευκότητος. αἴτιον δʼ ὅτι δυνάμεως καὶ ἐντελεχείας ζητοῦσι λόγον ἑνοποιὸν καὶ διαφοράν. ἔστι δʼ, ὥσπερ εἴρηται, ἡ ἐσχάτη ὕλη καὶ ἡ μορφὴ ταὐτὸ καὶ ἕν, δυνάμει, τὸ δὲ ἐνεργείᾳ, ὥστε ὅμοιον τὸ ζητεῖν τοῦ ἑνὸς τί αἴτιον καὶ τοῦ ἓν εἶναι· ἓν γάρ τι ἕκαστον, καὶ τὸ δυνάμει καὶ τὸ ἐνεργείᾳ ἕν πώς ἐστιν, ὥστε αἴτιον οὐθὲν ἄλλο πλὴν εἴ τι ὡς κινῆσαν ἐκ δυνάμεως εἰς ἐνέργειαν. ὅσα δὲ μὴ ἔχει ὕλην, πάντα ἁπλῶς ὅπερ ἕν τι.","paragraphs":[{"id":"RAW_DAZY_BOO8:P0001","text":"ἐκ δὴ τῶν εἰρημένων συλλογίσασθαι δεῖ καὶ συναγαγόντας τὸ κεφάλαιον τέλος ἐπιθεῖναι. εἴρηται δὴ ὅτι τῶν οὐσιῶν ζητεῖται τὰ αἴτια καὶ αἱ ἀρχαὶ καὶ τὰ στοιχεῖα. οὐσίαι δὲ αἱ μὲν ὁμολογούμεναί εἰσιν ὑπὸ πάντων, περὶ δὲ ἐνίων ἰδίᾳ τινὲς ἀπεφήναντο· ὁμολογούμεναι μὲν αἱ φυσικαί, οἷον πῦρ γῆ ὕδωρ ἀὴρ καὶ τἆλλα τὰ ἁπλᾶ σώματα, ἔπειτα τὰ φυτὰ καὶ τὰ μόρια αὐτῶν, καὶ τὰ ζῷα καὶ τὰ μόρια τῶν ζῴων, καὶ τέλος ὁ οὐρανὸς καὶ τὰ μόρια τοῦ οὐρανοῦ· ἰδίᾳ δέ τινες οὐσίας λέγουσιν εἶναι τά τʼ εἴδη καὶ τὰ μαθηματικά. ἄλλας δὲ δὴ συμβαίνει ἐκ τῶν λόγων οὐσίας εἶναι, τὸ τί ἦν εἶναι καὶ τὸ ὑποκείμενον· ἔτι ἄλλως τὸ γένος μᾶλλον τῶν εἰδῶν καὶ τὸ καθόλου τῶν καθʼ ἕκαστα· τῷ δὲ καθόλου καὶ τῷ γένει καὶ αἱ ἰδέαι συνάπτουσιν (κατὰ τὸν αὐτὸν γὰρ λόγον οὐσίαι δοκοῦσιν εἶναι).","kind":"passage"},{"id":"RAW_DAZY_BOO8:P0002","text":"ἐπεὶ δὲ τὸ τί ἦν εἶναι οὐσία, τούτου δὲ λόγος ὁ ὁρισμός, διὰ τοῦτο περὶ ὁρισμοῦ καὶ περὶ τοῦ καθʼ αὑτὸ διώρισται· ἐπεὶ δὲ ὁ ὁρισμὸς λόγος, ὁ δὲ λόγος μέρη ἔχει, ἀναγκαῖον καὶ περὶ μέρους ἦν ἰδεῖν, ποῖα τῆς οὐσίας μέρη καὶ ποῖα οὔ, καὶ εἰ ταῦτα καὶ τοῦ ὁρισμοῦ. ἔτι τοίνυν οὔτε τὸ καθόλου οὐσία οὔτε τὸ γένος· περὶ δὲ τῶν ἰδεῶν καὶ τῶν μαθηματικῶν ὕστερον σκεπτέον· παρὰ γὰρ τὰς αἰσθητὰς οὐσίας ταύτας λέγουσί τινες εἶναι.—νῦν δὲ περὶ τῶν ὁμολογουμένων οὐσιῶν ἐπέλθωμεν. αὗται δʼ εἰσὶν αἱ αἰσθηταί· αἱ δʼ αἰσθηταὶ οὐσίαι πᾶσαι ὕλην ἔχουσιν. ἔστι δʼ οὐσία τὸ ὑποκείμενον, ἄλλως μὲν ἡ ὕλη (ὕλην δὲ λέγω ἣ μὴ τόδε τι οὖσα ἐνεργείᾳ δυνάμει ἐστὶ τόδε τι), ἄλλως δʼ ὁ λόγος καὶ ἡ μορφή, ὃ τόδε τι ὂν τῷ λόγῳ χωριστόν ἐστιν· τρίτον δὲ τὸ ἐκ τούτων, οὗ γένεσις μόνου καὶ φθορά ἐστι, καὶ χωριστὸν ἁπλῶς· τῶν γὰρ κατὰ τὸν λόγον οὐσιῶν αἱ μὲν αἱ δʼ οὔ.","kind":"passage"},{"id":"RAW_DAZY_BOO8:P0003","text":"ὅτι δʼ ἐστὶν οὐσία καὶ ἡ ὕλη, δῆλον· ἐν πάσαις γὰρ ταῖς ἀντικειμέναις μεταβολαῖς ἐστί τι τὸ ὑποκείμενον ταῖς μεταβολαῖς, οἷον κατὰ τόπον τὸ νῦν μὲν ἐνταῦθα πάλιν δʼ ἄλλοθι, καὶ κατʼ αὔξησιν ὃ νῦν μὲν τηλικόνδε πάλιν δʼ ἔλαττον ἢ μεῖζον, καὶ κατʼ ἀλλοίωσιν ὃ νῦν μὲν ὑγιὲς πάλιν δὲ κάμνον·  ὁμοίως δὲ καὶ κατʼ οὐσίαν ὃ νῦν μὲν ἐν γενέσει πάλιν δʼ ἐν φθορᾷ, καὶ νῦν μὲν ὑποκείμενον ὡς τόδε τι πάλιν δʼ ὑποκείμενον ὡς κατὰ στέρησιν. καὶ ἀκολουθοῦσι δὴ ταύτῃ αἱ ἄλλαι μεταβολαί, τῶν δʼ ἄλλων ἢ μιᾷ ἢ δυοῖν αὕτη οὐκ ἀκολουθεῖ· οὐ γὰρ ἀνάγκη, εἴ τι ὕλην ἔχει τοπικήν, τοῦτο καὶ γεννητὴν καὶ φθαρτὴν ἔχειν. τίς μὲν οὖν διαφορὰ τοῦ ἁπλῶς γίγνεσθαι καὶ μὴ ἁπλῶς, ἐν τοῖς φυσικοῖς εἴρηται.","kind":"passage"},{"id":"RAW_DAZY_BOO8:P0004","text":"ἐπεὶ δʼ ἡ μὲν ὡς ὑποκειμένη καὶ ὡς ὕλη οὐσία ὁμολογεῖται,  αὕτη δʼ ἐστὶν ἡ δυνάμει, λοιπὸν τὴν ὡς ἐνέργειαν οὐσίαν τῶν αἰσθητῶν εἰπεῖν τίς ἐστιν. Δημόκριτος μὲν οὖν τρεῖς διαφορὰς ἔοικεν οἰομένῳ εἶναι (τὸ μὲν γὰρ ὑποκείμενον σῶμα, τὴν ὕλην, ἓν καὶ ταὐτόν, διαφέρειν δὲ ἢ ῥυσμῷ, ὅ ἐστι σχῆμα, ἢ τροπῇ, ὅ ἐστι θέσις, ἢ διαθιγῇ, ὅ ἐστι τάξις)· φαίνονται δὲ πολλαὶ διαφοραὶ οὖσαι, οἷον τὰ μὲν συνθέσει λέγεται τῆς ὕλης, ὥσπερ ὅσα κράσει καθάπερ μελίκρατον, τὰ δὲ δεσμῷ οἷον φάκελος, τὰ δὲ κόλλῃ οἷον βιβλίον, τὰ δὲ γόμφῳ οἷον κιβώτιον, τὰ δὲ πλείοσι τούτων, τὰ δὲ θέσει οἷον οὐδὸς καὶ ὑπέρθυρον (ταῦτα γὰρ τῷ κεῖσθαί πως διαφέρει), τὰ δὲ χρόνῳ οἷον δεῖπνον καὶ ἄριστον, τὰ δὲ τόπῳ οἷον τὰ πνεύματα· τὰ δὲ τοῖς τῶν αἰσθητῶν πάθεσιν οἷον σκληρότητι καὶ μαλακότητι, καὶ πυκνότητι καὶ ἀραιότητι, καὶ ξηρότητι καὶ ὑγρότητι, καὶ τὰ μὲν ἐνίοις τούτων τὰ δὲ πᾶσι τούτοις, καὶ ὅλως τὰ μὲν ὑπεροχῇ τὰ δὲ ἐλλείψει.","kind":"passage"},{"id":"RAW_DAZY_BOO8:P0005","text":"ὥστε δῆλον ὅτι καὶ τὸ ἔστι τοσαυταχῶς λέγεται· οὐδὸς γὰρ ἔστιν ὅτι οὕτως κεῖται, καὶ τὸ εἶναι τὸ οὕτως αὐτὸ κεῖσθαι σημαίνει, καὶ τὸ κρύσταλλον εἶναι τὸ οὕτω πεπυκνῶσθαι. ἐνίων δὲ τὸ εἶναι καὶ πᾶσι τούτοις ὁρισθήσεται, τῷ τὰ μὲν μεμῖχθαι, τὰ δὲ κεκρᾶσθαι, τὰ δὲ δεδέσθαι, τὰ δὲ πεπυκνῶσθαι, τὰ δὲ ταῖς ἄλλαις διαφοραῖς κεχρῆσθαι, ὥσπερ χεὶρ ἢ πούς. ληπτέα οὖν τὰ γένη τῶν διαφορῶν (αὗται γὰρ ἀρχαὶ ἔσονται τοῦ εἶναι), οἷον τὰ τῷ μᾶλλον καὶ ἧττον ἢ πυκνῷ καὶ μανῷ καὶ τοῖς ἄλλοις τοῖς τοιούτοις· πάντα γὰρ ταῦτα ὑπεροχὴ καὶ ἔλλειψίς ἐστιν. εἰ δέ τι σχήματι ἢ λειότητι καὶ τραχύτητι, πάντα εὐθεῖ καὶ καμπύλῳ. τοῖς δὲ τὸ εἶναι τὸ μεμῖχθαι ἔσται, ἀντικειμένως δὲ τὸ μὴ εἶναι.  φανερὸν δὴ ἐκ τούτων ὅτι εἴπερ ἡ οὐσία αἰτία τοῦ εἶναι ἕκαστον, ὅτι ἐν τούτοις ζητητέον τί τὸ αἴτιον τοῦ εἶναι τούτων ἕκαστον.","kind":"passage"},{"id":"RAW_DAZY_BOO8:P0006","text":"οὐσία μὲν οὖν οὐδὲν τούτων οὐδὲ συνδυαζόμενον, ὅμως δὲ τὸ ἀνάλογον ἐν ἑκάστῳ· καὶ ὡς ἐν ταῖς οὐσίαις τὸ τῆς ὕλης κατηγορούμενον αὐτὴ ἡ ἐνέργεια, καὶ ἐν τοῖς ἄλλοις ὁρισμοῖς μάλιστα. οἷον εἰ οὐδὸν δέοι ὁρίσασθαι, ξύλον ἢ λίθον ὡδὶ κείμενον ἐροῦμεν, καὶ οἰκίαν πλίνθους καὶ ξύλα ὡδὶ κείμενα (ἢ ἔτι καὶ τὸ οὗ ἕνεκα ἐπʼ ἐνίων ἔστιν), εἰ δὲ κρύσταλλον, ὕδωρ πεπηγὸς ἢ πεπυκνωμένον ὡδί· συμφωνία δὲ ὀξέος καὶ βαρέος μῖξις τοιαδί· τὸν αὐτὸν δὲ τρόπον καὶ ἐπὶ τῶν ἄλλων. φανερὸν δὴ ἐκ τούτων ὅτι ἡ ἐνέργεια ἄλλη ἄλλης ὕλης καὶ ὁ λόγος· τῶν μὲν γὰρ ἡ σύνθεσις τῶν δʼ ἡ μῖξις τῶν δὲ ἄλλο τι τῶν εἰρημένων.","kind":"passage"},{"id":"RAW_DAZY_BOO8:P0007","text":"διὸ τῶν ὁριζομένων οἱ μὲν λέγοντες τί ἐστιν οἰκία, ὅτι λίθοι πλίνθοι ξύλα, τὴν δυνάμει οἰκίαν λέγουσιν, ὕλη γὰρ ταῦτα· οἱ δὲ ἀγγεῖον σκεπαστικὸν χρημάτων καὶ σωμάτων ἤ τι ἄλλο τοιοῦτον προτιθέντες, τὴν ἐνέργειαν λέγουσιν· οἱ δʼ ἄμφω ταῦτα συντιθέντες τὴν τρίτην καὶ τὴν ἐκ τούτων οὐσίαν (ἔοικε γὰρ ὁ μὲν διὰ τῶν διαφορῶν λόγος τοῦ εἴδους καὶ τῆς ἐνεργείας εἶναι, ὁ δʼ ἐκ τῶν ἐνυπαρχόντων τῆς ὕλης μᾶλλον)· ὁμοίως δὲ καὶ οἵους Ἀρχύτας ἀπεδέχετο ὅρους· τοῦ συνάμφω γάρ εἰσιν. οἷον τί ἐστι νηνεμία; ἠρεμία ἐν πλήθει ἀέρος· ὕλη μὲν γὰρ ὁ ἀήρ, ἐνέργεια δὲ καὶ οὐσία ἡ ἠρεμία. τί ἐστι γαλήνη; ὁμαλότης θαλάττης· τὸ μὲν ὑποκείμενον ὡς ὕλη ἡ θάλαττα, ἡ δὲ ἐνέργεια καὶ ἡ μορφὴ ἡ ὁμαλότης. φανερὸν δὴ ἐκ τῶν εἰρημένων τίς ἡ αἰσθητὴ οὐσία ἐστὶ καὶ πῶς· ἡ μὲν γὰρ ὡς ὕλη, ἡ δʼ ὡς μορφὴ καὶ ἐνέργεια, ἡ δὲ τρίτη ἡ ἐκ τούτων.","kind":"passage"},{"id":"RAW_DAZY_BOO8:P0008","text":"δεῖ δὲ μὴ ἀγνοεῖν ὅτι ἐνίοτε λανθάνει πότερον σημαίνει τὸ ὄνομα τὴν σύνθετον οὐσίαν ἢ τὴν ἐνέργειαν καὶ τὴν μορφήν, οἷον ἡ οἰκία πότερον σημεῖον τοῦ κοινοῦ ὅτι σκέπασμα ἐκ πλίνθων καὶ λίθων ὡδὶ κειμένων, ἢ τῆς ἐνεργείας καὶ τοῦ εἴδους ὅτι σκέπασμα, καὶ γραμμὴ πότερον δυὰς ἐν μήκει ἢ  δυάς, καὶ ζῷον πότερον ψυχὴ ἐν σώματι ἢ ψυχή· αὕτη γὰρ οὐσία καὶ ἐνέργεια σώματός τινος. εἴη δʼ ἂν καὶ ἐπʼ ἀμφοτέροις τὸ ζῷον, οὐχ ὡς ἑνὶ λόγῳ λεγόμενον ἀλλʼ ὡς πρὸς ἕν. ἀλλὰ ταῦτα πρὸς μέν τι ἄλλο διαφέρει, πρὸς δὲ τὴν ζήτησιν τῆς οὐσίας τῆς αἰσθητῆς οὐδέν·  τὸ γὰρ τί ἦν εἶναι τῷ εἴδει καὶ τῇ ἐνεργείᾳ ὑπάρχει. ψυχὴ μὲν γὰρ καὶ ψυχῇ εἶναι ταὐτόν, ἀνθρώπῳ δὲ καὶ ἄνθρωπος οὐ ταὐτόν, εἰ μὴ καὶ ἡ ψυχὴ ἄνθρωπος λεχθήσεται· οὕτω δὲ τινὶ μὲν τινὶ δʼ οὔ.—οὐ φαίνεται δὴ ζητοῦσιν ἡ συλλαβὴ ἐκ τῶν στοιχείων οὖσα καὶ συνθέσεως, οὐδʼ ἡ οἰκία πλίνθοι τε καὶ σύνθεσις.","kind":"passage"},{"id":"RAW_DAZY_BOO8:P0009","text":"καὶ τοῦτο ὀρθῶς· οὐ γάρ ἐστιν ἡ σύνθεσις οὐδʼ ἡ μῖξις ἐκ τούτων ὧν ἐστὶ σύνθεσις ἢ μῖξις. ὁμοίως δὲ οὐδὲ τῶν ἄλλων οὐθέν, οἷον εἰ ὁ οὐδὸς θέσει, οὐκ ἐκ τοῦ οὐδοῦ ἡ θέσις ἀλλὰ μᾶλλον οὗτος ἐξ ἐκείνης. οὐδὲ δὴ ὁ ἄνθρωπός ἐστι τὸ ζῷον καὶ δίπουν, ἀλλά τι δεῖ εἶναι ὃ παρὰ ταῦτά ἐστιν, εἰ ταῦθʼ ὕλη, οὔτε δὲ στοιχεῖον οὔτʼ ἐκ στοιχείου, ἀλλʼ ἡ οὐσία· ὃ ἐξαιροῦντες τὴν ὕλην λέγουσιν. εἰ οὖν τοῦτʼ αἴτιον τοῦ εἶναι, καὶ οὐσία τοῦτο, αὐτὴν ἂν τὴν οὐσίαν οὐ λέγοιεν. (ἀνάγκη δὴ ταύτην ἢ ἀΐδιον εἶναι ἢ φθαρτὴν ἄνευ τοῦ φθείρεσθαι καὶ γεγονέναι ἄνευ τοῦ γίγνεσθαι. δέδεικται δὲ καὶ δεδήλωται ἐν ἄλλοις ὅτι τὸ εἶδος οὐθεὶς ποιεῖ οὐδὲ γεννᾷ, ἀλλὰ ποιεῖται τόδε, γίγνεται δὲ τὸ ἐκ τούτων. εἰ δʼ εἰσὶ τῶν φθαρτῶν αἱ οὐσίαι χωρισταί, οὐδέν πω δῆλον· πλὴν ὅτι γʼ ἐνίων οὐκ ἐνδέχεται δῆλον, ὅσα μὴ οἷόν τε παρὰ τὰ τινὰ εἶναι, οἷον οἰκίαν ἢ σκεῦος.","kind":"passage"},{"id":"RAW_DAZY_BOO8:P0010","text":"ἴσως μὲν οὖν οὐδʼ οὐσίαι εἰσὶν οὔτʼ αὐτὰ ταῦτα οὔτε τι τῶν ἄλλων ὅσα μὴ φύσει συνέστηκεν· τὴν γὰρ φύσιν μόνην ἄν τις θείη τὴν ἐν τοῖς φθαρτοῖς οὐσίαν.) ὥστε ἡ ἀπορία ἣν οἱ Ἀντισθένειοι καὶ οἱ οὕτως ἀπαίδευτοι ἠπόρουν ἔχει τινὰ καιρόν, ὅτι οὐκ ἔστι τὸ τί ἔστιν ὁρίσασθαι (τὸν γὰρ ὅρον λόγον εἶναι μακρόν), ἀλλὰ ποῖον μέν τί ἐστιν ἐνδέχεται καὶ διδάξαι, ὥσπερ ἄργυρον, τί μέν ἐστιν οὔ, ὅτι δʼ οἷον καττίτερος· ὥστʼ οὐσίας ἔστι μὲν ἧς ἐνδέχεται εἶναι ὅρον καὶ λόγον, οἷον τῆς συνθέτου, ἐάν τε αἰσθητὴ ἐάν τε νοητὴ ᾖ· ἐξ ὧν δʼ αὕτη πρώτων, οὐκέτι, εἴπερ τὶ κατὰ τινὸς σημαίνει ὁ λόγος ὁ ὁριστικὸς καὶ δεῖ τὸ μὲν ὥσπερ ὕλην εἶναι τὸ δὲ ὡς μορφήν.—φανερὸν δὲ καὶ διότι, εἴπερ εἰσί πως ἀριθμοὶ αἱ οὐσίαι, οὕτως εἰσὶ καὶ οὐχ ὥς τινες λέγουσι μονάδων· ὅ τε γὰρ ὁρισμὸς ἀριθμός τις· διαιρετός τε γὰρ καὶ εἰς ἀδιαίρετα (οὐ γὰρ ἄπειροι οἱ λόγοι), καὶ ὁ ἀριθμὸς δὲ τοιοῦτον.","kind":"passage"},{"id":"RAW_DAZY_BOO8:P0011","text":"καὶ ὥσπερ οὐδʼ ἀπʼ ἀριθμοῦ ἀφαιρεθέντος τινὸς ἢ προστεθέντος ἐξ ὧν ὁ ἀριθμός ἐστιν, οὐκέτι ὁ αὐτὸς ἀριθμός ἐστιν ἀλλʼ ἕτερος, κἂν τοὐλάχιστον ἀφαιρεθῇ ἢ προστεθῇ,  οὕτως οὐδὲ ὁ ὁρισμὸς οὐδὲ τὸ τί ἦν εἶναι οὐκέτι ἔσται ἀφαιρεθέντος τινὸς ἢ προστεθέντος. καὶ τὸν ἀριθμὸν δεῖ εἶναί τι ᾧ εἷς, ὃ νῦν οὐκ ἔχουσι λέγειν τίνι εἷς, εἴπερ ἐστὶν εἷς (ἢ γὰρ οὐκ ἔστιν ἀλλʼ οἷον σωρός, ἢ εἴπερ ἐστί, λεκτέον τί τὸ ποιοῦν ἓν ἐκ πολλῶν)· καὶ ὁ ὁρισμὸς εἷς ἐστίν, ὁμοίως δὲ οὐδὲ τοῦτον ἔχουσι λέγειν. καὶ τοῦτο εἰκότως συμβαίνει· τοῦ αὐτοῦ γὰρ λόγου, καὶ ἡ οὐσία ἓν οὕτως, ἀλλʼ οὐχ ὡς λέγουσί τινες οἷον μονάς τις οὖσα ἢ στιγμή, ἀλλʼ ἐντελέχεια καὶ φύσις τις ἑκάστη. καὶ ὥσπερ οὐδὲ ὁ ἀριθμὸς ἔχει τὸ μᾶλλον καὶ ἧττον, οὐδʼ ἡ κατὰ τὸ εἶδος οὐσία, ἀλλʼ εἴπερ, ἡ μετὰ τῆς ὕλης.","kind":"passage"},{"id":"RAW_DAZY_BOO8:P0012","text":"περὶ μὲν οὖν γενέσεως καὶ φθορᾶς τῶν λεγομένων οὐσιῶν, πῶς τʼ ἐνδέχεται καὶ πῶς ἀδύνατον, καὶ περὶ τῆς εἰς τὸν ἀριθμὸν ἀναγωγῆς, ἔστω μέχρι τούτων διωρισμένον.","kind":"passage"},{"id":"RAW_DAZY_BOO8:P0013","text":"περὶ δὲ τῆς ὑλικῆς οὐσίας δεῖ μὴ λανθάνειν ὅτι εἰ καὶ ἐκ τοῦ αὐτοῦ πάντα πρώτου ἢ τῶν αὐτῶν ὡς πρώτων καὶ ἡ αὐτὴ ὕλη ὡς ἀρχὴ τοῖς γιγνομένοις, ὅμως ἔστι τις οἰκεία ἑκάστου, οἷον φλέγματος  τὰ γλυκέα ἢ λιπαρά, χολῆς δὲ τὰ πικρὰ ἢ ἄλλʼ ἄττα· ἴσως δὲ ταῦτα ἐκ τοῦ αὐτοῦ. γίγνονται δὲ πλείους ὗλαι τοῦ αὐτοῦ ὅταν θατέρου ἡ ἑτέρα ᾖ, οἷον φλέγμα ἐκ λιπαροῦ καὶ γλυκέος εἰ τὸ λιπαρὸν ἐκ τοῦ γλυκέος, ἐκ δὲ χολῆς τῷ ἀναλύεσθαι εἰς τὴν πρώτην ὕλην τὴν χολήν. διχῶς γὰρ τόδʼ ἐκ τοῦδε, ἢ ὅτι πρὸ ὁδοῦ ἔσται ἢ ὅτι ἀναλυθέντος εἰς τὴν ἀρχήν. ἐνδέχεται δὲ μιᾶς τῆς ὕλης οὔσης ἕτερα γίγνεσθαι διὰ τὴν κινοῦσαν αἰτίαν, οἷον ἐκ ξύλου καὶ κιβωτὸς καὶ κλίνη. ἐνίων δʼ ἑτέρα ἡ ὕλη ἐξ ἀνάγκης ἑτέρων ὄντων, οἷον πρίων οὐκ ἂν γένοιτο ἐκ ξύλου, οὐδʼ ἐπὶ τῇ κινούσῃ αἰτίᾳ τοῦτο· οὐ γὰρ ποιήσει πρίονα ἐξ ἐρίου ἢ ξύλου.","kind":"passage"},{"id":"RAW_DAZY_BOO8:P0014","text":"εἰ δʼ ἄρα τὸ αὐτὸ ἐνδέχεται ἐξ ἄλλης ὕλης ποιῆσαι, δῆλον ὅτι ἡ τέχνη καὶ ἡ ἀρχὴ ἡ ὡς κινοῦσα ἡ αὐτή· εἰ γὰρ καὶ ἡ ὕλη ἑτέρα καὶ τὸ κινοῦν, καὶ τὸ γεγονός.—ὅταν δή τις ζητῇ τὸ αἴτιον, ἐπεὶ πλεοναχῶς τὰ αἴτια λέγεται, πάσας δεῖ λέγειν τὰς ἐνδεχομένας αἰτίας. οἷον ἀνθρώπου τίς αἰτία ὡς ὕλη; ἆρα τὰ καταμήνια; τί δʼ ὡς κινοῦν; ἆρα τὸ σπέρμα; τί δʼ ὡς τὸ εἶδος; τὸ τί ἦν εἶναι. τί δʼ ὡς οὗ ἕνεκα; τὸ τέλος.  ἴσως δὲ ταῦτα ἄμφω τὸ αὐτό. δεῖ δὲ τὰ ἐγγύτατα αἴτια λέγειν. τίς ἡ ὕλη; μὴ πῦρ ἢ γῆν ἀλλὰ τὴν ἴδιον. περὶ μὲν οὖν τὰς φυσικὰς οὐσίας καὶ γενητὰς ἀνάγκη οὕτω μετιέναι εἴ τις μέτεισιν ὀρθῶς, εἴπερ ἄρα αἴτιά τε ταῦτα καὶ τοσαῦτα καὶ δεῖ τὰ αἴτια γνωρίζειν· ἐπὶ δὲ τῶν φυσικῶν μὲν ἀϊδίων δὲ οὐσιῶν ἄλλος λόγος. ἴσως γὰρ ἔνια οὐκ ἔχει ὕλην, ἢ οὐ τοιαύτην ἀλλὰ μόνον κατὰ τόπον κινητήν. οὐδʼ ὅσα δὴ φύσει μέν, μὴ οὐσίαι δέ, οὐκ ἔστι τούτοις ὕλη, ἀλλὰ τὸ ὑποκείμενον ἡ οὐσία.","kind":"passage"},{"id":"RAW_DAZY_BOO8:P0015","text":"οἷον τί αἴτιον ἐκλείψεως, τίς ὕλη; οὐ γὰρ ἔστιν, ἀλλʼ ἡ σελήνη τὸ πάσχον. τί δʼ αἴτιον ὡς κινῆσαν καὶ φθεῖραν τὸ φῶς; ἡ γῆ. τὸ δʼ οὗ ἕνεκα ἴσως οὐκ ἔστιν. τὸ δʼ ὡς εἶδος ὁ λόγος, ἀλλὰ ἄδηλος ἐὰν μὴ μετὰ τῆς αἰτίας ᾖ ὁ λόγος. οἷον τί ἔκλειψις; στέρησις φωτός. ἐὰν δὲ προστεθῇ τὸ ὑπὸ γῆς ἐν μέσῳ γιγνομένης, ὁ σὺν τῷ αἰτίῳ λόγος οὗτος. ὕπνου δʼ ἄδηλον τί τὸ πρῶτον πάσχον. ἀλλʼ ὅτι τὸ ζῷον; ναί, ἀλλὰ τοῦτο κατὰ τί, καὶ τί πρῶτον; καρδία ἢ ἄλλο τι. εἶτα ὑπὸ τίνος; εἶτα τί τὸ πάθος, τὸ ἐκείνου καὶ μὴ τοῦ ὅλου; ὅτι ἀκινησία τοιαδί; ναί, ἀλλʼ αὕτη τῷ τί πάσχειν τὸ πρῶτον;","kind":"passage"},{"id":"RAW_DAZY_BOO8:P0016","text":"ἐπεὶ δʼ ἔνια ἄνευ γενέσεως καὶ φθορᾶς ἔστι καὶ οὐκ ἔστιν, οἷον αἱ στιγμαί, εἴπερ εἰσί, καὶ ὅλως τὰ εἴδη (οὐ γὰρ τὸ λευκὸν γίγνεται ἀλλὰ τὸ ξύλον λευκόν, εἰ ἔκ τινος καὶ τὶ πᾶν τὸ γιγνόμενον γίγνεται), οὐ πάντα ἂν τἀναντία γίγνοιτο ἐξ ἀλλήλων, ἀλλʼ ἑτέρως λευκὸς ἄνθρωπος ἐκ μέλανος ἀνθρώπου καὶ λευκὸν ἐκ μέλανος· οὐδὲ παντὸς ὕλη ἔστιν ἀλλʼ ὅσων γένεσις ἔστι καὶ μεταβολὴ εἰς ἄλληλα· ὅσα δʼ ἄνευ τοῦ μεταβάλλειν ἔστιν ἢ μή, οὐκ ἔστι τούτων ὕλη.—ἔχει δʼ ἀπορίαν πῶς πρὸς τἀναντία ἡ ὕλη ἡ ἑκάστου ἔχει. οἷον εἰ τὸ σῶμα δυνάμει ὑγιεινόν, ἐναντίον δὲ νόσος ὑγιείᾳ, ἆρα ἄμφω δυνάμει; καὶ τὸ ὕδωρ δυνάμει οἶνος καὶ ὄξος; ἢ τοῦ μὲν καθʼ ἕξιν καὶ κατὰ τὸ εἶδος ὕλη, τοῦ δὲ κατὰ στέρησιν καὶ φθορὰν τὴν παρὰ φύσιν; ἀπορία δέ τις ἔστι καὶ διὰ τί ὁ οἶνος οὐχ ὕλη τοῦ ὄξους οὐδὲ δυνάμει ὄξος (καίτοι γίγνεται ἐξ αὐτοῦ ὄξος) καὶ ὁ ζῶν δυνάμει νεκρός.","kind":"passage"},{"id":"RAW_DAZY_BOO8:P0017","text":"ἢ οὔ, ἀλλὰ κατὰ συμβεβηκὸς αἱ φθοραί,  ἡ δὲ τοῦ ζῴου ὕλη αὐτὴ κατὰ φθορὰν νεκροῦ δύναμις καὶ ὕλη, καὶ τὸ ὕδωρ ὄξους· γίγνεται γὰρ ἐκ τούτων ὥσπερ ἐξ ἡμέρας νύξ. καὶ ὅσα δὴ οὕτω μεταβάλλει εἰς ἄλληλα, εἰς τὴν ὕλην δεῖ ἐπανελθεῖν, οἷον εἰ ἐκ νεκροῦ ζῷον, εἰς τὴν ὕλην πρῶτον, εἶθʼ οὕτω ζῷον· καὶ τὸ ὄξος εἰς ὕδωρ, εἶθʼ οὕτως οἶνος.","kind":"passage"},{"id":"RAW_DAZY_BOO8:P0018","text":"περὶ δὲ τῆς ἀπορίας τῆς εἰρημένης περί τε τοὺς ὁρισμοὺς καὶ περὶ τοὺς ἀριθμούς, τί αἴτιον τοῦ ἓν εἶναι; πάντων γὰρ ὅσα πλείω μέρη ἔχει καὶ μὴ ἔστιν οἷον σωρὸς τὸ πᾶν ἀλλʼ ἔστι τι τὸ ὅλον παρὰ τὰ μόρια, ἔστι τι αἴτιον, ἐπεὶ καὶ ἐν τοῖς σώμασι τοῖς μὲν ἁφὴ αἰτία τοῦ ἓν εἶναι τοῖς δὲ γλισχρότης ἤ τι πάθος ἕτερον τοιοῦτον. ὁ δʼ ὁρισμὸς λόγος ἐστὶν εἷς οὐ συνδέσμῳ καθάπερ ἡ Ἰλιὰς ἀλλὰ τῷ ἑνὸς εἶναι.","kind":"passage"},{"id":"RAW_DAZY_BOO8:P0019","text":"τί οὖν ἐστὶν ὃ ποιεῖ ἓν τὸν ἄνθρωπον, καὶ διὰ τί ἓν ἀλλʼ οὐ πολλά, οἷον τό τε ζῷον καὶ τὸ δίπουν, ἄλλως τε δὴ καὶ εἰ ἔστιν, ὥσπερ φασί τινες, αὐτό τι ζῷον καὶ αὐτὸ δίπουν; διὰ τί γὰρ οὐκ ἐκεῖνα αὐτὰ ὁ ἄνθρωπός ἐστι, καὶ ἔσονται κατὰ μέθεξιν οἱ ἄνθρωποι οὐκ ἀνθρώπου οὐδʼ ἑνὸς ἀλλὰ δυοῖν, ζῴου καὶ δίποδος, καὶ ὅλως δὴ οὐκ ἂν εἴη ὁ ἄνθρωπος ἓν ἀλλὰ πλείω, ζῷον καὶ δίπουν; φανερὸν δὴ ὅτι οὕτω μὲν μετιοῦσιν ὡς εἰώθασιν ὁρίζεσθαι καὶ λέγειν, οὐκ ἐνδέχεται ἀποδοῦναι καὶ λῦσαι τὴν ἀπορίαν· εἰ δʼ ἐστίν, ὥσπερ λέγομεν, τὸ μὲν ὕλη τὸ δὲ μορφή, καὶ τὸ μὲν δυνάμει τὸ δὲ ἐνεργείᾳ, οὐκέτι ἀπορία δόξειεν ἂν εἶναι τὸ ζητούμενον. ἔστι γὰρ αὕτη ἡ ἀπορία ἡ αὐτὴ κἂν εἰ ὁ ὅρος εἴη ἱματίου στρογγύλος χαλκός· εἴη γὰρ ἂν σημεῖον τοὔνομα τοῦτο τοῦ λόγου, ὥστε τὸ ζητούμενόν ἐστι τί αἴτιον τοῦ ἓν εἶναι τὸ στρογγύλον καὶ τὸν χαλκόν. οὐκέτι δὴ ἀπορία φαίνεται, ὅτι τὸ μὲν ὕλη τὸ δὲ μορφή.","kind":"passage"},{"id":"RAW_DAZY_BOO8:P0020","text":"τί οὖν τούτου αἴτιον, τοῦ τὸ δυνάμει ὂν ἐνεργείᾳ εἶναι, παρὰ τὸ ποιῆσαν, ἐν ὅσοις ἔστι γένεσις; οὐθὲν γάρ ἐστιν αἴτιον ἕτερον τοῦ τὴν δυνάμει σφαῖραν ἐνεργείᾳ εἶναι σφαῖραν, ἀλλὰ τοῦτʼ ἦν τὸ τί ἦν εἶναι ἑκατέρῳ. ἔστι δὲ τῆς ὕλης ἡ μὲν νοητὴ ἡ δʼ αἰσθητή, καὶ ἀεὶ τοῦ λόγου τὸ μὲν ὕλη τὸ δὲ ἐνέργειά ἐστιν, οἷον ὁ κύκλος σχῆμα ἐπίπεδον. ὅσα δὲ μὴ ἔχει ὕλην μήτε νοητὴν μήτε αἰσθητήν, εὐθὺς ὅπερ ἕν τί  ἐστιν ἕκαστον,  ὥσπερ καὶ ὅπερ ὄν τι, τὸ τόδε, τὸ ποιόν, τὸ ποσόν—διὸ καὶ οὐκ ἔνεστιν ἐν τοῖς ὁρισμοῖς οὔτε τὸ ὂν οὔτε τὸ ἕν—, καὶ τὸ τί ἦν εἶναι εὐθὺς ἕν τί ἐστιν ὥσπερ καὶ ὄν τι—διὸ καὶ οὐκ ἔστιν ἕτερόν τι αἴτιον τοῦ ἓν εἶναι οὐθενὶ τούτων οὐδὲ τοῦ ὄν τι εἶναι· εὐθὺς γὰρ ἕκαστόν ἐστιν ὄν τι καὶ ἕν τι, οὐχ ὡς ἐν γένει τῷ ὄντι καὶ τῷ ἑνί, οὐδʼ ὡς χωριστῶν ὄντων παρὰ τὰ καθʼ ἕκαστα.","kind":"passage"},{"id":"RAW_DAZY_BOO8:P0021","text":"διὰ ταύτην δὲ τὴν ἀπορίαν οἱ μὲν μέθεξιν λέγουσι, καὶ αἴτιον τί τῆς μεθέξεως καὶ τί τὸ μετέχειν ἀποροῦσιν· οἱ δὲ συνουσίαν  , ὥσπερ Λυκόφρων φησὶν εἶναι τὴν ἐπιστήμην τοῦ ἐπίστασθαι καὶ ψυχῆς· οἱ δὲ σύνθεσιν ἢ σύνδεσμον ψυχῆς σώματι τὸ ζῆν. καίτοι ὁ αὐτὸς λόγος ἐπὶ πάντων· καὶ γὰρ τὸ ὑγιαίνειν ἔσται ἢ συνουσία ἢ σύνδεσμος ἢ σύνθεσις ψυχῆς καὶ ὑγιείας, καὶ τὸ τὸν χαλκὸν εἶναι τρίγωνον σύνθεσις χαλκοῦ καὶ τριγώνου, καὶ τὸ λευκὸν εἶναι σύνθεσις ἐπιφανείας καὶ λευκότητος. αἴτιον δʼ ὅτι δυνάμεως καὶ ἐντελεχείας ζητοῦσι λόγον ἑνοποιὸν καὶ διαφοράν. ἔστι δʼ, ὥσπερ εἴρηται, ἡ ἐσχάτη ὕλη καὶ ἡ μορφὴ ταὐτὸ καὶ ἕν, δυνάμει, τὸ δὲ ἐνεργείᾳ, ὥστε ὅμοιον τὸ ζητεῖν τοῦ ἑνὸς τί αἴτιον καὶ τοῦ ἓν εἶναι· ἓν γάρ τι ἕκαστον, καὶ τὸ δυνάμει καὶ τὸ ἐνεργείᾳ ἕν πώς ἐστιν, ὥστε αἴτιον οὐθὲν ἄλλο πλὴν εἴ τι ὡς κινῆσαν ἐκ δυνάμεως εἰς ἐνέργειαν. ὅσα δὲ μὴ ἔχει ὕλην, πάντα ἁπλῶς ὅπερ ἕν τι.","kind":"passage"}],"sha256":"1450e8673334120bfc4122e47f32e8a4f2e1ce4fb0b62b12b7e93ec54b559318","language_code":"grc","language_name":"Ἑλληνική","translations":[{"id":"SOURCE_TR_20261015T173157146258Z_58f8e01a","language_code":"en","language_name":"English","title":"Book 8","source_sha256":"1450e8673334120bfc4122e47f32e8a4f2e1ce4fb0b62b12b7e93ec54b559318","sha256":"3e5b360eab93b169368074f4980f433a060948964fb526cbba3bf1dc141bfdec","rendition_type":"machine","review_status":"machine","model":"gpt-5.6-terra","created":"2026-10-15T17:31:57.146300+00:00","paragraphs":[{"id":"RAW_DAZY_BOO8:P0001","text":"From what has been said, then, one must draw an inference, and, having brought the main point together, set an end upon it. It has been said, then, that the causes and principles and elements of substances are being sought. Now some substances are agreed upon by all, while concerning some others certain people have made pronouncements of their own. The agreed-upon ones are the natural substances, such as fire, earth, water, air, and the other simple bodies; then plants and their parts; and animals and the parts of animals; and finally the heaven and the parts of heaven. But some say, in a special way, that the Forms and mathematical objects are substances. From the arguments it further follows that there are other substances: the what-it-was-to-be and the substrate. Again, in another way, the genus is more substance than the species, and the universal than particulars; and with the universal and the genus they connect the Ideas too (for by the same account they seem to be substances).","kind":"passage","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_DAZY_BOO8:P0001"]},{"id":"RAW_DAZY_BOO8:P0002","text":"And since the what-it-was-to-be is substance, and its account is the definition, it is for this reason that there has been a determination concerning definition and concerning what belongs in itself. And since a definition is an account, and an account has parts, it was necessary also to examine part: which things are parts of the substance and which are not, and whether these are also parts of the definition. Further, neither the universal nor the genus is substance. But concerning the Ideas and the mathematical objects inquiry must be made later; for some say that these exist besides sensible substances.—But now let us proceed to the agreed-upon substances. These are the sensible ones; and all sensible substances have matter. Substance is the substrate: in one way, matter (and by matter I mean that which, not being a this in actuality, is potentially a this); in another way, the account and form, which, being a this, is separable in account; and thirdly, that composed of these, of which alone there is generation and destruction, and which is separable without qualification. For among substances according to the account, some are separable and some are not.","kind":"passage","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_DAZY_BOO8:P0002"]},{"id":"RAW_DAZY_BOO8:P0003","text":"That matter too is substance is clear. For in all opposed changes there is something underlying the changes: for example, in respect of place, what is now here and again elsewhere; and in respect of increase, what is now of this size and again smaller or larger; and in respect of alteration, what is now healthy and again diseased. Similarly also in respect of substance, what is now in generation and again in destruction, and now underlies as a this and again underlies as according to privation. And the other changes follow upon this one, whereas this does not follow upon one or two of the others. For it is not necessary that, if something has matter capable of local motion, it also have matter capable of generation and destruction. What the difference is between coming-to-be without qualification and not without qualification has been stated in the physical works.","kind":"passage","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_DAZY_BOO8:P0003"]},{"id":"RAW_DAZY_BOO8:P0004","text":"Since substance as substrate and as matter is agreed upon, and this is that which is potentially, it remains to say what substance as actuality is in the case of sensible things. Democritus, then, seems to have thought that there are three differences (for he takes the underlying body, matter, to be one and the same, but differing either in rhythm, which is shape, or in turning, which is position, or in contact, which is arrangement). Yet there appear to be many differences. For some things are spoken of by composition of the matter, as things produced by mixture, such as honey-water; some by binding, such as a bundle; some by gluing, such as a book; some by nails, such as a chest; some by several of these; some by position, such as a threshold and lintel (for these differ by being placed somehow); some by time, such as dinner and breakfast; some by place, such as winds; and some by the affections of sensible things, such as hardness and softness, density and rarity, dryness and wetness. Some are so by some of these, others by all of them; and in general some by excess and others by deficiency.","kind":"passage","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_DAZY_BOO8:P0004"]},{"id":"RAW_DAZY_BOO8:P0005","text":"So it is clear that “is” too is spoken of in as many ways. For a threshold is because it lies thus, and its being signifies its lying thus; and to be ice is to have been condensed thus. But the being of some things will be defined also by all these, in that some are mixed, others blended, others bound, others condensed, and others make use of the other differences, as a hand or a foot does. One must therefore take the genera of the differences (for these will be principles of being), such as those involving more and less, or dense and rare, and the other such things; for all these are excess and deficiency. And if something is by shape, or by smoothness and roughness, all are reducible to straight and curved. For some, being will be being mixed, and, oppositely, not being will be not being mixed. It is clear, then, from these things that if substance is the cause of each thing's being, one must seek among these what is the cause of the being of each of these things.","kind":"passage","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_DAZY_BOO8:P0005"]},{"id":"RAW_DAZY_BOO8:P0006","text":"None of these, then, nor any combination of them, is substance; nevertheless, what is analogous is present in each case. And just as in substances what is predicated of the matter is actuality itself, so too it is so most of all in the other definitions. For example, if one must define a threshold, we shall say: wood or stone lying thus; and a house: bricks and timbers lying thus (or in some cases also that for the sake of which it is); and if ice: water congealed or condensed thus; and concord: a mixture of high and low notes of such a kind. And in the same way in the other cases. It is clear, then, from these things that the actuality and the account are different for different matter: for in some cases it is composition, in others mixture, and in others something else among the things stated.","kind":"passage","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_DAZY_BOO8:P0006"]},{"id":"RAW_DAZY_BOO8:P0007","text":"Hence, among those who define, some, in saying what a house is—that it is stones, bricks, and timbers—state the house potentially, for these are matter. But those who put forward “a covering vessel for possessions and bodies,” or something else of this sort, state the actuality. And those who combine both state the third substance, that composed of these (for the account through the differences seems to be of the form and actuality, whereas that from the constituents is rather of the matter). Likewise also are the definitions which Archytas accepted; for they are definitions of both together. For example, what is windlessness? Stillness in a large quantity of air: for the air is the matter, while the stillness is actuality and substance. What is calm? Smoothness of sea: the underlying thing as matter is the sea, while actuality and form are the smoothness. It is clear, then, from what has been said what sensible substance is and how: one as matter, another as form and actuality, and the third as that composed of these.","kind":"passage","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_DAZY_BOO8:P0007"]},{"id":"RAW_DAZY_BOO8:P0008","text":"But one must not fail to notice that sometimes it escapes us whether the name signifies the composite substance or the actuality and form: for example, whether house is a sign of the composite, namely a covering made from bricks and stones lying thus, or of the actuality and form, namely a covering; and whether line is a dyad in length or a dyad; and whether animal is soul in a body or soul. For this is the substance and actuality of a certain body. “Animal” may indeed apply to both, not as spoken in one account but as related to one thing. Yet these matters make a difference for something else, but none for the inquiry concerning sensible substance. For the what-it-was-to-be belongs to the form and actuality. For soul and being a soul are the same, whereas man and being a man are not the same—unless soul too is to be called man; and thus in one way it is so, but in another it is not.—Now, to those who inquire, the syllable does not appear to be composed of the elements and their composition, nor the house to be bricks and composition.","kind":"passage","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_DAZY_BOO8:P0008"]},{"id":"RAW_DAZY_BOO8:P0009","text":"And this is correct. For the composition or mixture is not composed of those things of which it is the composition or mixture. Similarly, neither is anything else: for example, if a threshold is by position, position is not from the threshold, but rather this is from that. Nor is man animal and biped; rather, there must be something which is besides these, if these are matter—neither an element nor from elements, but the substance, which they state when they remove the matter. If, then, this is the cause of being, and this is substance, they would not be stating the substance itself. (It is necessary that this either be eternal, or be destructible without being destroyed, and have come to be without coming-to-be. It has been demonstrated and made clear elsewhere that no one makes or generates the form, but this particular thing is made, and what is composed of these comes to be. But whether the substances of destructible things are separable is not yet clear—except that it is clear that this is impossible in certain cases, namely all those which cannot exist apart from particular things, such as a house or an implement.","kind":"passage","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_DAZY_BOO8:P0009"]},{"id":"RAW_DAZY_BOO8:P0010","text":"Perhaps, then, neither these things themselves nor any of the other things which have not been constituted by nature are substances; for one might posit nature alone as substance among destructible things.) Thus the puzzle raised by the Antistheneans and others so uneducated has some appropriateness: that it is not possible to define what a thing is (for the definition, they say, is a long account), but it is possible both to teach and to state what sort of thing something is; for example, not what silver is, but that it is like tin. Thus there is a substance of which it is possible to have a definition and account, namely the composite substance, whether sensible or intelligible; but of the primary things from which it is composed there is not, if indeed the defining account signifies something of something, and one thing must be as matter and the other as form.—It is also clear why, if substances are in some manner numbers, they are numbers in this way and not as some say, of units. For definition is a certain number: it is divisible into indivisibles (for accounts are not infinite), and number is of this sort too.","kind":"passage","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_DAZY_BOO8:P0010"]},{"id":"RAW_DAZY_BOO8:P0011","text":"And just as, if something is taken away from or added to the things of which a number consists, it is no longer the same number but another, even if the least thing is taken away or added, so too neither the definition nor the what-it-was-to-be will remain if something is taken away or added. And there must be something by which the number is one, which they now cannot say—if it is one at all (for either it is not one, but like a heap, or, if it is, one must say what makes one out of many); and the definition is one, but similarly they cannot say this either. And this happens reasonably; for the account is the same, and substance too is one in this way, but not as some say, as though it were some unit or point; rather, each is a certain actuality and nature. And just as number does not admit of more and less, neither does substance according to the form; but if anything does, it is that which is with matter.","kind":"passage","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_DAZY_BOO8:P0011"]},{"id":"RAW_DAZY_BOO8:P0012","text":"Let what has been determined concerning the generation and destruction of the substances so called—how it is possible and how impossible—and concerning reduction to number, extend thus far.","kind":"passage","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_DAZY_BOO8:P0012"]},{"id":"RAW_DAZY_BOO8:P0013","text":"But concerning material substance, one must not fail to notice that even if all things are from the same first thing, or from the same things as first things, and even if the same matter is a principle for things that come to be, nevertheless there is some matter proper to each thing: for example, sweet or fatty things are matter for phlegm, bitter things or certain others for bile. Perhaps these are from the same thing. There come to be several matters of the same thing whenever one is matter of the other: for example, phlegm from fatty and sweet, if the fatty is from the sweet; and from bile, by the bile's being dissolved into the first matter. For in two ways is this from that: either because it will be before it in the path, or because it has been dissolved into its principle. It is possible, when the matter is one, for different things to come to be on account of the moving cause: for example, from wood both a chest and a bed. But for some things the matter is necessarily different because the things are different: for a saw could not come to be from wood, nor does this lie within the power of the moving cause; for it will not make a saw from wool or wood.","kind":"passage","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_DAZY_BOO8:P0013"]},{"id":"RAW_DAZY_BOO8:P0014","text":"But if it is possible to make the same thing from different matter, it is clear that the art and the principle as mover are the same; for if both the matter and the mover are different, the thing that has come to be is different too.—Whenever someone seeks the cause, since causes are spoken of in many ways, one must state all the possible causes. For example, what is the cause of man as matter? Is it the menstrual fluids? And what as mover? Is it the seed? And what as form? The what-it-was-to-be. And what as that for the sake of which? The end. Perhaps these latter two are the same. One must state the nearest causes. What is the matter? Not fire or earth, but the proper matter. Thus concerning natural and generable substances one must proceed in this way, if one proceeds correctly, if indeed these are causes and are so many, and one must know the causes. But in the case of natural yet eternal substances there is another account. For perhaps some do not have matter, or not such matter, but only matter movable in place. Nor do those things which are by nature but are not substances have matter; rather, their substrate is substance.","kind":"passage","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_DAZY_BOO8:P0014"]},{"id":"RAW_DAZY_BOO8:P0015","text":"For example, what is the cause of an eclipse? What is its matter? There is none; rather, the moon is what undergoes the affection. And what is the cause as moving and destroying the light? The earth. Perhaps there is no that for the sake of which. The cause as form is the account, but the account is unclear unless it is together with the cause. For example, what is an eclipse? Privation of light. But if “when the earth comes to be in the middle” is added, this is the account together with the cause. In the case of sleep it is unclear what is the first thing affected. But is it the animal? Yes, but in respect of what, and what is first? The heart, or something else? Then by what? Then what is the affection, of that thing and not of the whole? Is it such-and-such an immobility? Yes, but this is present through what affection of the first thing affected?","kind":"passage","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_DAZY_BOO8:P0015"]},{"id":"RAW_DAZY_BOO8:P0016","text":"Since some things are and are not without generation and destruction, such as points, if they exist, and in general Forms (for it is not the white that comes to be, but the wood white, if everything that comes to be comes to be from something and as something), not all contraries could come to be from one another; rather, a white man comes from a dark man in a different way from that in which white comes from dark. Nor is there matter of everything, but only of those things which have generation and change into one another. But of those things which are or are not without changing, there is no matter.—There is a puzzle as to how the matter of each thing is related to contraries. For example, if body is potentially healthy, and disease is contrary to health, is it potentially both? And is water potentially wine and vinegar? Or is it matter of the one according to the possession and according to the form, but of the other according to privation and destruction contrary to nature? There is also a puzzle why wine is not matter of vinegar nor potentially vinegar (though vinegar comes to be from it), and why the living is potentially dead.","kind":"passage","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_DAZY_BOO8:P0016"]},{"id":"RAW_DAZY_BOO8:P0017","text":"Or is this not so? Rather, destructions are incidental; and the matter of the animal itself is, according to destruction, potentiality and matter of a corpse, and water is matter of vinegar. For these come to be from them as night from day. And all things which thus change into one another must return to matter: for example, if an animal is to come from a corpse, first into matter and then thus into an animal; and vinegar first into water and then thus into wine.","kind":"passage","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_DAZY_BOO8:P0017"]},{"id":"RAW_DAZY_BOO8:P0018","text":"Concerning the puzzle stated about definitions and numbers, what is the cause of their being one? For in all things which have several parts, and in which the whole is not like a heap but is something besides the parts, there is some cause. Indeed, in bodies contact is the cause of being one in some cases, while in others viscosity or some other such affection is. But a definition is one account, not by conjunction, like the Iliad, but by being of one thing.","kind":"passage","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_DAZY_BOO8:P0018"]},{"id":"RAW_DAZY_BOO8:P0019","text":"What, then, is it that makes man one, and why is he one and not many—for example, animal and biped—especially if, as some say, there is Animal-itself and Biped-itself? For why is man not those things themselves, so that men will be men by participation not in man nor in one thing, but in two, animal and biped, and in general man will not be one but many, animal and biped? Clearly, then, for those who proceed in the way in which they are accustomed to define and speak, it is not possible to give an answer and resolve the puzzle. But if, as we say, one thing is matter and another form, and one is potentially and the other actually, what is sought will no longer seem to be a puzzle. For this puzzle would be the same even if the definition of a cloak were “round bronze.” For this name would be a sign of the account, so that what is sought is what is the cause of round and bronze being one. It no longer appears to be a puzzle, because one is matter and the other form.","kind":"passage","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_DAZY_BOO8:P0019"]},{"id":"RAW_DAZY_BOO8:P0020","text":"What, then, apart from that which made it, is the cause of what is potentially being actually, in cases where there is coming-to-be? For there is no other cause of the potentially spherical being actually a sphere; rather, this was the essence for each of them. Of matter, one kind is intelligible and another perceptible; and in every account one part is matter and another actuality, for example, the circle is a plane figure. But whatever things have neither intelligible nor perceptible matter are immediately each precisely some one thing, just as they are also precisely some being—a this, a qualified thing, a quantified thing (and therefore neither being nor one is present in the definitions)—and the essence is immediately some one thing, just as it is also some being. Hence there is no other cause for any of these either of being one or of being some being; for each is immediately some being and some one, not as though being and one were genera, nor as though they existed separately apart from particulars.","kind":"passage","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_DAZY_BOO8:P0020"]},{"id":"RAW_DAZY_BOO8:P0021","text":"Because of this difficulty, some speak of participation, and inquire what the cause of participation is and what it is to participate; others speak of association, as Lycophron says that knowledge is an association of knowing and soul; others say that living is a composition or conjunction of soul with body. Yet the same account applies in all cases: for being healthy will be either an association, conjunction, or composition of soul and health; and the bronze being triangular, a composition of bronze and triangle; and being white, a composition of surface and whiteness. The reason is that they seek a unifying account and a difference for potency and actuality. But, as has been said, the ultimate matter and the form are the same and one, the one potentially, the other actually; so that to seek what is the cause of the one is like seeking what is the cause of being one. For each thing is some one thing, and what is potentially one and what is actually one are in a way one; so there is no other cause, unless there is something that moves from potency into actuality. But all things that have no matter are simply precisely some one thing.","kind":"passage","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_DAZY_BOO8:P0021"]}],"source_digest":"0f0ce4a57f1d0efb24af84b946207a5726888ddbeaee5645ab16b87cc5f0b327","source_language_code":"grc","original_title":"Book 8"}],"passage_basis":"frozen-grounding","original_title":"Book 8","title_language_code":"en"},{"id":"RAW_DAZY_BOO9","title":"Book 9","order":9,"chapter_id":"CH_DAZY_BOO9","text":"περὶ μὲν οὖν τοῦ πρώτως ὄντος καὶ πρὸς ὃ πᾶσαι αἱ ἄλλαι κατηγορίαι τοῦ ὄντος ἀναφέρονται εἴρηται, περὶ τῆς οὐσίας (κατὰ γὰρ τὸν τῆς οὐσίας λόγον λέγεται τἆλλα ὄντα, τό τε ποσὸν καὶ τὸ ποιὸν καὶ τἆλλα τὰ οὕτω λεγόμενα· πάντα γὰρ ἕξει τὸν τῆς οὐσίας λόγον, ὥσπερ εἴπομεν ἐν τοῖς πρώτοις λόγοις)· ἐπεὶ δὲ λέγεται τὸ ὂν τὸ μὲν τὸ τὶ ἢ ποιὸν ἢ ποσόν, τὸ δὲ κατὰ δύναμιν καὶ ἐντελέχειαν καὶ κατὰ τὸ ἔργον, διορίσωμεν καὶ περὶ δυνάμεως καὶ ἐντελεχείας, καὶ πρῶτον περὶ δυνάμεως ἣ λέγεται μὲν μάλιστα κυρίως, οὐ μὴν χρησιμωτάτη γέ ἐστι πρὸς ὃ βουλόμεθα νῦν·  ἐπὶ πλέον γάρ ἐστιν ἡ δύναμις καὶ ἡ ἐνέργεια τῶν μόνον λεγομένων κατὰ κίνησιν. ἀλλʼ εἰπόντες περὶ ταύτης, ἐν τοῖς περὶ τῆς ἐνεργείας διορισμοῖς δηλώσομεν καὶ περὶ τῶν ἄλλων. ὅτι μὲν οὖν λέγεται πολλαχῶς ἡ δύναμις καὶ τὸ δύνασθαι, διώρισται ἡμῖν ἐν ἄλλοις· τούτων δʼ ὅσαι μὲν ὁμωνύμως λέγονται δυνάμεις ἀφείσθωσαν (ἔνιαι γὰρ ὁμοιότητί τινι λέγονται, καθάπερ ἐν γεωμετρίᾳ καὶ δυνατὰ καὶ ἀδύνατα λέγομεν τῷ εἶναί πως ἢ μὴ εἶναι), ὅσαι δὲ πρὸς τὸ αὐτὸ εἶδος, πᾶσαι ἀρχαί τινές εἰσι, καὶ πρὸς πρώτην μίαν λέγονται, ἥ ἐστιν ἀρχὴ μεταβολῆς ἐν ἄλλῳ ἢ ᾗ ἄλλο. ἡ μὲν γὰρ τοῦ παθεῖν ἐστὶ δύναμις, ἡ ἐν αὐτῷ τῷ πάσχοντι ἀρχὴ μεταβολῆς παθητικῆς ὑπʼ ἄλλου ἢ ᾗ ἄλλο· ἡ δʼ ἕξις ἀπαθείας τῆς ἐπὶ τὸ χεῖρον καὶ φθορᾶς τῆς ὑπʼ ἄλλου ἢ ᾗ ἄλλο ὑπʼ ἀρχῆς μεταβλητικῆς. ἐν γὰρ τούτοις ἔνεστι πᾶσι τοῖς ὅροις ὁ τῆς πρώτης δυνάμεως λόγος. πάλιν δʼ αὗται δυνάμεις λέγονται ἢ τοῦ μόνον ποιῆσαι ἢ  παθεῖν ἢ τοῦ καλῶς, ὥστε καὶ ἐν τοῖς τούτων λόγοις ἐνυπάρχουσί πως οἱ τῶν προτέρων δυνάμεων λόγοι.—φανερὸν οὖν ὅτι ἔστι μὲν ὡς μία δύναμις τοῦ ποιεῖν καὶ πάσχειν (δυνατὸν γάρ ἐστι καὶ τῷ ἔχειν αὐτὸ δύναμιν τοῦ παθεῖν καὶ τῷ ἄλλο ὑπʼ αὐτοῦ), ἔστι δὲ ὡς ἄλλη. ἡ μὲν γὰρ ἐν τῷ πάσχοντι (διὰ γὰρ τὸ ἔχειν τινὰ ἀρχήν, καὶ εἶναι καὶ τὴν ὕλην ἀρχήν τινα, πάσχει τὸ πάσχον, καὶ ἄλλο ὑπʼ ἄλλου· τὸ λιπαρὸν μὲν γὰρ καυστὸν τὸ δʼ ὑπεῖκον ὡδὶ θλαστόν, ὁμοίως δὲ καὶ ἐπὶ τῶν ἄλλων), ἡ δʼ ἐν τῷ ποιοῦντι, οἷον τὸ θερμὸν καὶ ἡ οἰκοδομική, ἡ μὲν ἐν τῷ θερμαντικῷ ἡ δʼ ἐν τῷ οἰκοδομικῷ· διὸ ᾗ συμπέφυκεν, οὐθὲν πάσχει αὐτὸ ὑφʼ ἑαυτοῦ· ἓν γὰρ καὶ οὐκ ἄλλο. καὶ ἡ ἀδυναμία καὶ τὸ ἀδύνατον ἡ τῇ τοιαύτῃ δυνάμει ἐναντία στέρησίς ἐστιν, ὥστε τοῦ αὐτοῦ καὶ κατὰ τὸ αὐτὸ πᾶσα δύναμις ἀδυναμίᾳ. ἡ δὲ στέρησις λέγεται πολλαχῶς· καὶ γὰρ τὸ μὴ ἔχον καὶ τὸ πεφυκὸς ἂν μὴ ἔχῃ, ἢ ὅλως ἢ ὅτε πέφυκεν, καὶ ἢ ὡδί, οἷον παντελῶς, ἢ κἂν ὁπωσοῦν. ἐπʼ ἐνίων δέ, ἂν πεφυκότα ἔχειν μὴ ἔχῃ βίᾳ, ἐστερῆσθαι ταῦτα λέγομεν. \n\n\n\n \n\n \n\nἐπεὶ δʼ αἱ μὲν ἐν τοῖς ἀψύχοις ἐνυπάρχουσιν ἀρχαὶ τοιαῦται, αἱ δʼ ἐν τοῖς ἐμψύχοις καὶ ἐν ψυχῇ καὶ τῆς ψυχῆς ἐν τῷ λόγον ἔχοντι,  δῆλον ὅτι καὶ τῶν δυνάμεων αἱ μὲν ἔσονται ἄλογοι αἱ δὲ μετὰ λόγου· διὸ πᾶσαι αἱ τέχναι καὶ αἱ ποιητικαὶ ἐπιστῆμαι δυνάμεις εἰσίν· ἀρχαὶ γὰρ μεταβλητικαί εἰσιν ἐν ἄλλῳ ἢ ᾗ ἄλλο. καὶ αἱ μὲν μετὰ λόγου πᾶσαι τῶν ἐναντίων αἱ αὐταί, αἱ δὲ ἄλογοι μία ἑνός, οἷον τὸ θερμὸν τοῦ θερμαίνειν μόνονʼ ἡ δὲ ἰατρικὴ νόσου καὶ ὑγιείας. αἴτιον δὲ ὅτι λόγος ἐστὶν ἡ ἐπιστήμη, ὁ δὲ λόγος ὁ αὐτὸς δηλοῖ τὸ πρᾶγμα καὶ τὴν στέρησιν, πλὴν οὐχ ὡσαύτως, καὶ ἔστιν ὡς ἀμφοῖν ἔστι δʼ ὡς τοῦ ὑπάρχοντος μᾶλλον, ὥστʼ ἀνάγκη καὶ τὰς τοιαύτας ἐπιστήμας εἶναι μὲν τῶν ἐναντίων, εἶναι δὲ τοῦ μὲν καθʼ αὑτὰς τοῦ δὲ μὴ καθʼ αὑτάς· καὶ γὰρ ὁ λόγος τοῦ μὲν καθʼ αὑτὸ τοῦ δὲ τρόπον τινὰ κατὰ συμβεβηκός· ἀποφάσει γὰρ καὶ ἀποφορᾷ δηλοῖ τὸ ἐναντίον· ἡ γὰρ στέρησις ἡ πρώτη τὸ ἐναντίον, αὕτη δὲ ἀποφορὰ θατέρου. ἐπεὶ δὲ τὰ ἐναντία οὐκ ἐγγίγνεται ἐν τῷ αὐτῷ, ἡ δʼ ἐπιστήμη δύναμις τῷ λόγον ἔχειν, καὶ ἡ ψυχὴ κινήσεως ἔχει ἀρχήν, τὸ μὲν ὑγιεινὸν ὑγίειαν μόνον ποιεῖ καὶ τὸ θερμαντικὸν θερμότητα καὶ τὸ ψυκτικὸν ψυχρότητα, ὁ δʼ ἐπιστήμων ἄμφω. λόγος γάρ ἐστιν ἀμφοῖν μέν, οὐχ ὁμοίως δέ, καὶ ἐν ψυχῇ ἣ ἔχει κινήσεως ἀρχήν· ὥστε ἄμφω ἀπὸ τῆς αὐτῆς ἀρχῆς κινήσει πρὸς ταὐτὸ συνάψασα· διὸ τὰ κατὰ λόγον δυνατὰ τοῖς ἄνευ λόγου δυνατοῖς ποιεῖ τἀναντία· μιᾷ γὰρ ἀρχῇ περιέχεται, τῷ λόγῳ. φανερὸν δὲ καὶ ὅτι τῇ μὲν τοῦ εὖ δυνάμει ἀκολουθεῖ ἡ τοῦ μόνον ποιῆσαι ἢ παθεῖν δύναμις, ταύτῃ δʼ ἐκείνη οὐκ ἀεί· ἀνάγκη γὰρ τὸν εὖ ποιοῦντα καὶ ποιεῖν, τὸν δὲ μόνον ποιοῦντα οὐκ ἀνάγκη καὶ εὖ ποιεῖν. \n\n\n\n \n\n \n\nεἰσὶ δέ τινες οἵ φασιν, οἷον οἱ Μεγαρικοί, ὅταν ἐνεργῇ μόνον δύνασθαι, ὅταν δὲ μὴ ἐνεργῇ οὐ δύνασθαι, οἷον τὸν μὴ οἰκοδομοῦντα οὐ δύνασθαι οἰκοδομεῖν, ἀλλὰ τὸν οἰκοδομοῦντα ὅταν οἰκοδομῇ· ὁμοίως δὲ καὶ ἐπὶ τῶν ἄλλων. οἷς τὰ συμβαίνοντα ἄτοπα οὐ χαλεπὸν ἰδεῖν. δῆλον γὰρ ὅτι οὔτʼ οἰκοδόμος ἔσται ἐὰν μὴ οἰκοδομῇ (τὸ γὰρ οἰκοδόμῳ εἶναι τὸ δυνατῷ εἶναί ἐστιν οἰκοδομεῖν), ὁμοίως δὲ καὶ ἐπὶ τῶν ἄλλων τεχνῶν. εἰ οὖν ἀδύνατον τὰς τοιαύτας ἔχειν τέχνας μὴ μαθόντα ποτὲ καὶ λαβόντα,  καὶ μὴ ἔχειν μὴ ἀποβαλόντα ποτέ (ἢ γὰρ λήθῃ ἢ πάθει τινὶ ἢ χρόνῳ· οὐ γὰρ δὴ τοῦ γε πράγματος φθαρέντος, ἀεὶ γὰρ ἔστιν), ὅταν παύσηται, οὐχ ἕξει τὴν τέχνην, πάλιν δʼ εὐθὺς οἰκοδομήσει πῶς λαβών; καὶ τὰ ἄψυχα δὴ ὁμοίως· οὔτε γὰρ ψυχρὸν οὔτε θερμὸν οὔτε γλυκὺ οὔτε ὅλως αἰσθητὸν οὐθὲν ἔσται μὴ αἰσθανομένων· ὥστε τὸν Πρωταγόρου λόγον συμβήσεται λέγειν αὐτοῖς. ἀλλὰ μὴν οὐδʼ αἴσθησιν ἕξει οὐδὲν ἂν μὴ αἰσθάνηται μηδʼ ἐνεργῇ. εἰ οὖν τυφλὸν τὸ μὴ ἔχον ὄψιν, πεφυκὸς δὲ καὶ ὅτε πέφυκε καὶ ἔτι ὄν, οἱ αὐτοὶ τυφλοὶ ἔσονται πολλάκις τῆς ἡμέρας, καὶ κωφοί. ἔτι εἰ ἀδύνατον τὸ ἐστερημένον δυνάμεως, τὸ μὴ γιγνόμενον ἀδύνατον ἔσται γενέσθαι· τὸ δʼ ἀδύνατον γενέσθαι ὁ λέγων ἢ εἶναι ἢ ἔσεσθαι ψεύσεται (τὸ γὰρ ἀδύνατον τοῦτο ἐσήμαινεν), ὥστε οὗτοι οἱ λόγοι ἐξαιροῦσι καὶ κίνησιν καὶ γένεσιν. ἀεὶ γὰρ τό τε ἑστηκὸς ἑστήξεται καὶ τὸ καθήμενον καθεδεῖται· οὐ γὰρ ἀναστήσεται ἂν καθέζηται· ἀδύνατον γὰρ ἔσται ἀναστῆναι ὅ γε μὴ δύναται ἀναστῆναι. εἰ οὖν μὴ ἐνδέχεται ταῦτα λέγειν, φανερὸν ὅτι δύναμις καὶ ἐνέργεια ἕτερόν ἐστιν (ἐκεῖνοι δʼ οἱ λόγοι δύναμιν καὶ ἐνέργειαν ταὐτὸ ποιοῦσιν, διὸ καὶ οὐ μικρόν τι ζητοῦσιν ἀναιρεῖν), ὥστε ἐνδέχεται δυνατὸν μέν τι εἶναι μὴ εἶναι δέ, καὶ δυνατὸν μὴ εἶναι εἶναι δέ, ὁμοίως δὲ καὶ ἐπὶ τῶν ἄλλων κατηγοριῶν δυνατὸν βαδίζειν ὂν μὴ βαδίζειν, καὶ μὴ βαδίζειν δυνατὸν ὂν βαδίζειν. ἔστι δὲ δυνατὸν τοῦτο ᾧ ἐὰν ὑπάρξῃ ἡ ἐνέργεια οὗ λέγεται ἔχειν τὴν δύναμιν, οὐθὲν ἔσται ἀδύνατον. λέγω δὲ οἷον, εἰ δυνατὸν καθῆσθαι καὶ ἐνδέχεται καθῆσθαι, τούτῳ ἐὰν ὑπάρξῃ τὸ καθῆσθαι, οὐδὲν ἔσται ἀδύνατον· καὶ εἰ κινηθῆναι ἢ κινῆσαι ἢ στῆναι ἢ στῆσαι ἢ εἶναι ἢ γίγνεσθαι ἢ μὴ εἶναι ἢ μὴ γίγνεσθαι, ὁμοίως. ἐλήλυθε δʼ ἡ ἐνέργεια τοὔνομα, ἡ πρὸς τὴν ἐντελέχειαν συντιθεμένη, καὶ ἐπὶ τὰ ἄλλα ἐκ τῶν κινήσεων μάλιστα· δοκεῖ γὰρ ἡ ἐνέργεια μάλιστα ἡ κίνησις εἶναι, διὸ καὶ τοῖς μὴ οὖσιν οὐκ ἀποδιδόασι τὸ κινεῖσθαι, ἄλλας δέ τινας κατηγορίας, οἷον διανοητὰ καὶ ἐπιθυμητὰ εἶναι τὰ μὴ ὄντα, κινούμενα δὲ οὔ, τοῦτο δὲ ὅτι οὐκ ὄντα ἐνεργείᾳ ἔσονται ἐνεργείᾳ.  τῶν γὰρ μὴ ὄντων ἔνια δυνάμει ἐστίν· οὐκ ἔστι δέ, ὅτι οὐκ ἐντελεχείᾳ ἐστίν. \n\n\n\n \n\n \n\nεἰ δέ ἐστι τὸ εἰρημένον τὸ δυνατὸν ἢ ἀκολουθεῖ, φανερὸν ὅτι οὐκ ἐνδέχεται ἀληθὲς εἶναι τὸ εἰπεῖν ὅτι δυνατὸν μὲν τοδί, οὐκ ἔσται δέ, ὥστε τὰ ἀδύνατα εἶναι ταύτῃ διαφεύγειν· λέγω δὲ οἷον εἴ τις φαίη δυνατὸν τὴν διάμετρον μετρηθῆναι οὐ μέντοι μετρηθήσεσθαι—ὁ μὴ λογιζόμενος τὸ ἀδύνατον εἶναι—ὅτι οὐθὲν κωλύει δυνατόν τι ὂν εἶναι ἢ γενέσθαι μὴ εἶναι μηδʼ ἔσεσθαι. ἀλλʼ ἐκεῖνο ἀνάγκη ἐκ τῶν κειμένων, εἰ καὶ ὑποθοίμεθα εἶναι ἢ γεγονέναι ὃ οὐκ ἔστι μὲν δυνατὸν δέ, ὅτι οὐθὲν ἔσται ἀδύνατον· συμβήσεται δέ γε, τὸ γὰρ μετρεῖσθαι ἀδύνατον. οὐ γὰρ δή ἐστι ταὐτὸ τὸ ψεῦδος καὶ τὸ ἀδύνατον· τὸ γάρ σε ἑστάναι νῦν ψεῦδος μέν, οὐκ ἀδύνατον δέ. ἅμα δὲ δῆλον καὶ ὅτι, εἰ τοῦ Α ὄντος ἀνάγκη τὸ Β εἶναι, καὶ δυνατοῦ ὄντος εἶναι τοῦ Α καὶ τὸ Β ἀνάγκη εἶναι δυνατόν· εἰ γὰρ μὴ ἀνάγκη δυνατὸν εἶναι, οὐθὲν κωλύει μὴ εἶναι δυνατὸν εἶναι. ἔστω δὴ τὸ Α δυνατόν. οὐκοῦν ὅτε τὸ Α δυνατὸν εἴη εἶναι, εἰ τεθείη τὸ Α, οὐθὲν ἀδύνατον εἶναι συνέβαινεν· τὸ δέ γε Β ἀνάγκη εἶναι. ἀλλʼ ἦν ἀδύνατον. ἔστω δὴ ἀδύνατον. εἰ δὴ ἀδύνατον  εἶναι τὸ Β, ἀνάγκη καὶ τὸ Α εἶναι. ἀλλʼ ἦν ἄρα τὸ πρῶτον ἀδύνατον· καὶ τὸ δεύτερον ἄρα. ἂν ἄρα ᾖ τὸ Α δυνατόν, καὶ τὸ Β ἔσται δυνατόν, εἴπερ οὕτως εἶχον ὥστε τοῦ Α ὄντος ἀνάγκη εἶναι τὸ Β. ἐὰν δὴ οὕτως ἐχόντων  τῶν Α Β μὴ ᾖ δυνατὸν τὸ Β οὕτως, οὐδὲ τὰ Α Β ἕξει ὡς ἐτέθη· καὶ εἰ τοῦ Α δυνατοῦ ὄντος ἀνάγκη τὸ Β δυνατὸν εἶναι, εἰ ἔστι τὸ Α ἀνάγκη εἶναι καὶ τὸ Β. τὸ γὰρ δυνατὸν εἶναι ἐξ ἀνάγκης τὸ Β εἶναι, εἰ τὸ Α δυνατόν, τοῦτο σημαίνει, ἐὰν ᾖ τὸ Α καὶ ὅτε καὶ ὡς ἦν δυνατὸν εἶναι, κἀκεῖνο τότε καὶ οὕτως εἶναι ἀναγκαῖον. \n\n\n\n \n\n \n\nἁπασῶν δὲ τῶν δυνάμεων οὐσῶν τῶν μὲν συγγενῶν οἷον τῶν αἰσθήσεων, τῶν δὲ ἔθει οἷον τῆς τοῦ αὐλεῖν, τῶν δὲ μαθήσει οἷον τῆς τῶν τεχνῶν, τὰς μὲν ἀνάγκη προενεργήσαντας ἔχειν, ὅσαι ἔθει καὶ λόγῳ, τὰς δὲ μὴ τοιαύτας καὶ τὰς ἐπὶ τοῦ πάσχειν οὐκ ἀνάγκη.  ἐπεὶ δὲ τὸ δυνατὸν τὶ δυνατὸν καὶ ποτὲ καὶ πὼς καὶ ὅσα ἄλλα ἀνάγκη προσεῖναι ἐν τῷ διορισμῷ, καὶ τὰ μὲν κατὰ λόγον δύναται κινεῖν καὶ αἱ δυνάμεις αὐτῶν μετὰ λόγου, τὰ δὲ ἄλογα καὶ αἱ δυνάμεις ἄλογοι, κἀκείνας μὲν ἀνάγκη ἐν ἐμψύχῳ εἶναι ταύτας δὲ ἐν ἀμφοῖν, τὰς μὲν τοιαύτας δυνάμεις ἀνάγκη, ὅταν ὡς δύνανται τὸ ποιητικὸν καὶ τὸ παθητικὸν πλησιάζωσι, τὸ μὲν ποιεῖν τὸ δὲ πάσχειν, ἐκείνας δʼ οὐκ ἀνάγκη· αὗται μὲν γὰρ πᾶσαι μία ἑνὸς ποιητική, ἐκεῖναι δὲ τῶν ἐναντίων, ὥστε ἅμα ποιήσει τὰ ἐναντία· τοῦτο δὲ ἀδύνατον. ἀνάγκη ἄρα ἕτερόν τι εἶναι τὸ κύριον· λέγω δὲ τοῦτο ὄρεξιν ἢ προαίρεσιν. ὁποτέρου γὰρ ἂν ὀρέγηται κυρίως, τοῦτο ποιήσει ὅταν ὡς δύναται ὑπάρχῃ καὶ πλησιάζῃ τῷ παθητικῷ· ὥστε τὸ δυνατὸν κατὰ λόγον ἅπαν ἀνάγκη, ὅταν ὀρέγηται οὗ ἔχει τὴν δύναμιν καὶ ὡς ἔχει, τοῦτο ποιεῖν· ἔχει δὲ παρόντος τοῦ παθητικοῦ καὶ ὡδὶ ἔχοντος · εἰ δὲ μή, ποιεῖν οὐ δυνήσεται (τὸ γὰρ μηθενὸς τῶν ἔξω κωλύοντος προσδιορίζεσθαι οὐθὲν ἔτι δεῖ· τὴν γὰρ δύναμιν ἔχει ὡς ἔστι δύναμις τοῦ ποιεῖν, ἔστι δʼ οὐ πάντως ἀλλʼ ἐχόντων πῶς, ἐν οἷς ἀφορισθήσεται καὶ τὰ ἔξω κωλύοντα· ἀφαιρεῖται γὰρ ταῦτα τῶν ἐν τῷ διορισμῷ προσόντων ἔνια)· διὸ οὐδʼ ἐὰν ἅμα βούληται ἢ ἐπιθυμῇ ποιεῖν δύο ἢ τὰ ἐναντία, οὐ ποιήσει· οὐ γὰρ οὕτως ἔχει αὐτῶν τὴν δύναμιν οὐδʼ ἔστι τοῦ ἅμα ποιεῖν ἡ δύναμις, ἐπεὶ ὧν ἐστὶν οὕτως ποιήσει.\n\n\n\n \n\n \n\n ἐπεὶ δὲ περὶ τῆς κατὰ κίνησιν λεγομένης δυνάμεως εἴρηται, περὶ ἐνεργείας διορίσωμεν τί τέ ἐστιν ἡ ἐνέργεια καὶ ποῖόν τι. καὶ γὰρ τὸ δυνατὸν ἅμα δῆλον ἔσται διαιροῦσιν, ὅτι οὐ μόνον τοῦτο λέγομεν δυνατὸν ὃ πέφυκε κινεῖν ἄλλο ἢ κινεῖσθαι ὑπʼ ἄλλου ἢ ἁπλῶς ἢ τρόπον τινά, ἀλλὰ καὶ ἑτέρως, διὸ ζητοῦντες καὶ περὶ τούτων διήλθομεν. ἔστι δὴ ἐνέργεια τὸ ὑπάρχειν τὸ πρᾶγμα μὴ οὕτως ὥσπερ λέγομεν δυνάμει· λέγομεν δὲ δυνάμει οἷον ἐν τῷ ξύλῳ Ἑρμῆν καὶ ἐν τῇ ὅλῃ τὴν ἡμίσειαν, ὅτι ἀφαιρεθείη ἄν, καὶ ἐπιστήμονα καὶ τὸν μὴ θεωροῦντα, ἂν δυνατὸς ᾖ θεωρῆσαι· τὸ δὲ ἐνεργείᾳ. δῆλον δʼ ἐπὶ τῶν καθʼ ἕκαστα τῇ ἐπαγωγῇ ὃ βουλόμεθα λέγειν, καὶ οὐ δεῖ παντὸς ὅρον ζητεῖν ἀλλὰ καὶ τὸ ἀνάλογον συνορᾶν, ὅτι ὡς τὸ οἰκοδομοῦν πρὸς τὸ οἰκοδομικόν,  καὶ τὸ ἐγρηγορὸς πρὸς τὸ καθεῦδον, καὶ τὸ ὁρῶν πρὸς τὸ μῦον μὲν ὄψιν δὲ ἔχον, καὶ τὸ ἀποκεκριμένον ἐκ τῆς ὕλης πρὸς τὴν ὕλην, καὶ τὸ ἀπειργασμένον πρὸς τὸ ἀνέργαστον. ταύτης δὲ τῆς διαφορᾶς θατέρῳ μορίῳ ἔστω ἡ ἐνέργεια ἀφωρισμένη θατέρῳ δὲ τὸ δυνατόν. λέγεται δὲ ἐνεργείᾳ οὐ πάντα ὁμοίως ἀλλʼ ἢ τῷ ἀνάλογον, ὡς τοῦτο ἐν τούτῳ ἢ πρὸς τοῦτο, τόδʼ ἐν τῷδε ἢ πρὸς τόδε· τὰ μὲν γὰρ ὡς κίνησις πρὸς δύναμιν τὰ δʼ ὡς οὐσία πρός τινα ὕλην. ἄλλως δὲ καὶ τὸ ἄπειρον καὶ τὸ κενόν, καὶ ὅσα τοιαῦτα, λέγεται δυνάμει καὶ ἐνεργείᾳ ἢ πολλοῖς τῶν ὄντων, οἷον τῷ ὁρῶντι καὶ βαδίζοντι καὶ ὁρωμένῳ. ταῦτα μὲν γὰρ ἐνδέχεται καὶ ἁπλῶς ἀληθεύεσθαί ποτε (τὸ μὲν γὰρ ὁρώμενον ὅτι ὁρᾶται, τὸ δὲ ὅτι ὁρᾶσθαι δυνατόν)· τὸ δʼ ἄπειρον οὐχ οὕτω δυνάμει ἔστιν ὡς ἐνεργείᾳ ἐσόμενον χωριστόν, ἀλλὰ γνώσει. τὸ γὰρ μὴ ὑπολείπειν τὴν διαίρεσιν ἀποδίδωσι τὸ εἶναι δυνάμει ταύτην τὴν ἐνέργειαν, τὸ δὲ χωρίζεσθαι οὔ.\n\n \n\nἐπεὶ δὲ τῶν πράξεων ὧν ἔστι πέρας οὐδεμία τέλος ἀλλὰ τῶν περὶ τὸ τέλος, οἷον τὸ ἰσχναίνειν ἢ ἰσχνασία  , αὐτὰ δὲ ὅταν ἰσχναίνῃ οὕτως ἐστὶν ἐν κινήσει, μὴ ὑπάρχοντα ὧν ἕνεκα ἡ κίνησις, οὐκ ἔστι ταῦτα πρᾶξις ἢ οὐ τελεία γε (οὐ γὰρ τέλος)· ἀλλʼ ἐκείνη ᾗ ἐνυπάρχει τὸ τέλος καὶ  πρᾶξις. οἷον ὁρᾷ ἅμα καὶ ἑώρακε, καὶ φρονεῖ καὶ πεφρόνηκε, καὶ νοεῖ καὶ νενόηκεν, ἀλλʼ οὐ μανθάνει καὶ μεμάθηκεν οὐδʼ ὑγιάζεται καὶ ὑγίασται· εὖ ζῇ καὶ εὖ ἔζηκεν ἅμα, καὶ εὐδαιμονεῖ καὶ εὐδαιμόνηκεν. εἰ δὲ μή, ἔδει ἄν ποτε παύεσθαι ὥσπερ ὅταν ἰσχναίνῃ, νῦν δʼ οὔ, ἀλλὰ ζῇ καὶ ἔζηκεν. τούτων δὴ δεῖ τὰς μὲν κινήσεις λέγειν, τὰς δʼ ἐνεργείας. πᾶσα γὰρ κίνησις ἀτελής, ἰσχνασία μάθησις βάδισις οἰκοδόμησις· αὗται δὴ κινήσεις, καὶ ἀτελεῖς γε. οὐ γὰρ ἅμα βαδίζει καὶ βεβάδικεν, οὐδʼ οἰκοδομεῖ καὶ ᾠκοδόμηκεν, οὐδὲ γίγνεται καὶ γέγονεν ἢ κινεῖται καὶ κεκίνηται, ἀλλʼ ἕτερον, καὶ κινεῖ καὶ κεκίνηκεν· ἑώρακε δὲ καὶ ὁρᾷ ἅμα τὸ αὐτό, καὶ νοεῖ καὶ νενόηκεν. τὴν μὲν οὖν τοιαύτην ἐνέργειαν λέγω, ἐκείνην δὲ κίνησιν. τὸ μὲν οὖν ἐνεργείᾳ τί τέ ἐστι καὶ ποῖον, ἐκ τούτων καὶ τῶν τοιούτων δῆλον ἡμῖν ἔστω. \n\n\n\n \n\n \n\nπότε δὲ δυνάμει ἔστιν ἕκαστον καὶ πότε οὔ, διοριστέον· οὐ γὰρ ὁποτεοῦν.  οἷον ἡ γῆ ἆρʼ ἐστὶ δυνάμει ἄνθρωπος; ἢ οὔ, ἀλλὰ μᾶλλον ὅταν ἤδη γένηται σπέρμα, καὶ οὐδὲ τότε ἴσως; ὥσπερ οὖν οὐδʼ ὑπὸ ἰατρικῆς ἅπαν ἂν ὑγιασθείη οὐδʼ ἀπὸ τύχης, ἀλλʼ ἔστι τι ὃ δυνατόν ἐστι, καὶ τοῦτʼ ἔστιν ὑγιαῖνον δυνάμει. ὅρος δὲ τοῦ μὲν ἀπὸ διανοίας ἐντελεχείᾳ γιγνομένου ἐκ τοῦ δυνάμει ὄντος, ὅταν βουληθέντος γίγνηται μηθενὸς κωλύοντος τῶν ἐκτός, ἐκεῖ δʼ ἐν τῷ ὑγιαζομένῳ, ὅταν μηθὲν κωλύῃ τῶν ἐν αὐτῷ· ὁμοίως δὲ δυνάμει καὶ οἰκία· εἰ μηθὲν κωλύει τῶν ἐν τούτῳ καὶ τῇ ὕλῃ τοῦ γίγνεσθαι οἰκίαν, οὐδʼ ἔστιν ὃ δεῖ προσγενέσθαι ἢ ἀπογενέσθαι ἢ μεταβαλεῖν, τοῦτο δυνάμει οἰκία· καὶ ἐπὶ τῶν ἄλλων ὡσαύτως ὅσων ἔξωθεν ἡ ἀρχὴ τῆς γενέσεως. καὶ ὅσων δὴ ἐν αὐτῷ τῷ ἔχοντι, ὅσα μηθενὸς τῶν ἔξωθεν ἐμποδίζοντος ἔσται διʼ αὐτοῦ· οἷον τὸ σπέρμα οὔπω (δεῖ γὰρ ἐν ἄλλῳ πεσεῖν καὶ μεταβάλλειν), ὅταν δʼ ἤδη διὰ τῆς αὑτοῦ ἀρχῆς ᾖ τοιοῦτον, ἤδη τοῦτο δυνάμει· ἐκεῖνο δὲ ἑτέρας ἀρχῆς δεῖται, ὥσπερ ἡ γῆ οὔπω ἀνδριὰς δυνάμει (μεταβαλοῦσα γὰρ ἔσται χαλκός). ἔοικε δὲ ὃ λέγομεν εἶναι οὐ τόδε ἀλλʼ ἐκείνινον—οἷον τὸ κιβώτιον οὐ ξύλον ἀλλὰ ξύλινον, οὐδὲ τὸ ξύλον γῆ ἀλλὰ γήϊνον, πάλιν ἡ γῆ εἰ οὕτως μὴ ἄλλο ἀλλὰ ἐκείνινον—ἀεὶ ἐκεῖνο δυνάμει ἁπλῶς τὸ ὕστερόν ἐστιν. οἷον τὸ κιβώτιον οὐ γήϊνον οὐδὲ γῆ ἀλλὰ ξύλινον· τοῦτο γὰρ δυνάμει κιβώτιον καὶ ὕλη κιβωτίου αὕτη, ἁπλῶς μὲν τοῦ ἁπλῶς τουδὶ δὲ τοδὶ τὸ ξύλον. εἰ δέ τί ἐστι πρῶτον ὃ μηκέτι κατʼ ἄλλο λέγεται ἐκείνινον, τοῦτο πρώτη ὕλη· οἷον εἰ ἡ γῆ ἀερίνη, ὁ δʼ ἀὴρ μὴ πῦρ ἀλλὰ πύρινος, τὸ πῦρ ὕλη πρώτη οὐ τόδε τι οὖσα. τούτῳ γὰρ διαφέρει τὸ καθʼ οὗ καὶ τὸ ὑποκείμενον, τῷ εἶναι τόδε τι ἢ μὴ εἶναι· οἷον τοῖς πάθεσι τὸ ὑποκείμενον ἄνθρωπος καὶ σῶμα καὶ ψυχή, πάθος δὲ τὸ μουσικὸν καὶ λευκόν (λέγεται δὲ τῆς μουσικῆς ἐγγενομένης ἐκεῖνο οὐ μουσικὴ ἀλλὰ μουσικόν, καὶ οὐ λευκότης ὁ ἄνθρωπος ἀλλὰ λευκόν, οὐδὲ βάδισις ἢ κίνησις ἀλλὰ βαδίζον ἢ κινούμενον, ὡς τὸ ἐκείνινον)·—ὅσα μὲν οὖν οὕτω, τὸ ἔσχατον οὐσία· ὅσα δὲ μὴ οὕτως ἀλλʼ εἶδός τι καὶ τόδε τι τὸ κατηγορούμενον, τὸ ἔσχατον ὕλη καὶ οὐσία ὑλική. καὶ ὀρθῶς δὴ συμβαίνει τὸ ἐκείνινον λέγεσθαι κατὰ τὴν ὕλην καὶ τὰ πάθη·  ἄμφω γὰρ ἀόριστα. πότε μὲν οὖν λεκτέον δυνάμει καὶ πότε οὔ, εἴρηται.\n\n\n\n \n\n \n\nἐπεὶ δὲ τὸ πρότερον διώρισται ποσαχῶς λέγεται, φανερὸν ὅτι πρότερον ἐνέργεια δυνάμεώς ἐστιν. λέγω δὲ δυνάμεως οὐ μόνον τῆς ὡρισμένης ἣ λέγεται ἀρχὴ μεταβλητικὴ ἐν ἄλλῳ ἢ ᾗ ἄλλο, ἀλλʼ ὅλως πάσης ἀρχῆς κινητικῆς ἢ στατικῆς. καὶ γὰρ ἡ φύσις ἐν ταὐτῷ  γένει τῇ δυνάμει· ἀρχὴ γὰρ κινητική, ἀλλʼ οὐκ ἐν ἄλλῳ ἀλλʼ ἐν αὐτῷ ᾗ αὐτό.—πάσης δὴ τῆς τοιαύτης προτέρα ἐστὶν ἡ ἐνέργεια καὶ λόγῳ καὶ τῇ οὐσίᾳ· χρόνῳ δʼ ἔστι μὲν ὥς, ἔστι δὲ ὡς οὔ. τῷ λόγῳ μὲν οὖν ὅτι προτέρα, δῆλον (τῷ γὰρ ἐνδέχεσθαι ἐνεργῆσαι δυνατόν ἐστι τὸ πρώτως δυνατόν, οἷον λέγω οἰκοδομικὸν τὸ δυνάμενον οἰκοδομεῖν, καὶ ὁρατικὸν τὸ ὁρᾶν, καὶ ὁρατὸν τὸ δυνατὸν ὁρᾶσθαι· ὁ δʼ αὐτὸς λόγος καὶ ἐπὶ τῶν ἄλλων, ὥστʼ ἀνάγκη τὸν λόγον προϋπάρχειν καὶ τὴν γνῶσιν τῆς γνώσεως)· τῷ δὲ χρόνῳ πρότερον ὧδε· τὸ τῷ εἴδει τὸ αὐτὸ ἐνεργοῦν πρότερον, ἀριθμῷ δʼ οὔ. λέγω δὲ τοῦτο ὅτι τοῦδε μὲν τοῦ ἀνθρώπου τοῦ ἤδη ὄντος κατʼ ἐνέργειαν καὶ τοῦ σίτου καὶ τοῦ ὁρῶντος πρότερον τῷ χρόνῳ ἡ ὕλη καὶ τὸ σπέρμα καὶ τὸ ὁρατικόν, ἃ δυνάμει μέν ἐστιν ἄνθρωπος καὶ σῖτος καὶ ὁρῶν, ἐνεργείᾳ δʼ οὔπω· ἀλλὰ τούτων πρότερα τῷ χρόνῳ ἕτερα ὄντα ἐνεργείᾳ ἐξ ὧν ταῦτα ἐγένετο· ἀεὶ γὰρ ἐκ τοῦ δυνάμει ὄντος γίγνεται τὸ ἐνεργείᾳ ὂν ὑπὸ ἐνεργείᾳ ὄντος, οἷον ἄνθρωπος ἐξ ἀνθρώπου, μουσικὸς ὑπὸ μουσικοῦ, ἀεὶ κινοῦντός τινος πρώτου· τὸ δὲ κινοῦν ἐνεργείᾳ ἤδη ἔστιν. εἴρηται δὲ ἐν τοῖς περὶ τῆς οὐσίας λόγοις ὅτι πᾶν τὸ γιγνόμενον γίγνεται ἔκ τινος τι καὶ ὑπό τινος, καὶ τοῦτο τῷ εἴδει τὸ αὐτό. διὸ καὶ δοκεῖ ἀδύνατον εἶναι οἰκοδόμον εἶναι μὴ οἰκοδομήσαντα μηθὲν ἢ κιθαριστὴν μηθὲν κιθαρίσαντα· ὁ γὰρ μανθάνων κιθαρίζειν κιθαρίζων μανθάνει κιθαρίζειν, ὁμοίως δὲ καὶ οἱ ἄλλοι. ὅθεν ὁ σοφιστικὸς ἔλεγχος ἐγίγνετο ὅτι οὐκ ἔχων τις τὴν ἐπιστήμην ποιήσει οὗ ἡ ἐπιστήμη· ὁ γὰρ μανθάνων οὐκ ἔχει. ἀλλὰ διὰ τὸ τοῦ γιγνομένου γεγενῆσθαί τι καὶ τοῦ ὅλως κινουμένου κεκινῆσθαί τι (δῆλον δʼ ἐν τοῖς περὶ κινήσεως τοῦτο)  καὶ τὸν μανθάνοντα ἀνάγκη ἔχειν τι τῆς ἐπιστήμης ἴσως. ἀλλʼ οὖν καὶ ταύτῃ γε δῆλον ὅτι ἡ ἐνέργεια καὶ οὕτω προτέρα τῆς δυνάμεως κατὰ γένεσιν καὶ χρόνον. ἀλλὰ μὴν καὶ οὐσίᾳ γε, πρῶτον μὲν ὅτι τὰ τῇ γενέσει ὕστερα τῷ εἴδει καὶ τῇ οὐσίᾳ πρότερα (οἷον ἀνὴρ παιδὸς καὶ ἄνθρωπος σπέρματος· τὸ μὲν γὰρ ἤδη ἔχει τὸ εἶδος τὸ δʼ οὔ), καὶ ὅτι ἅπαν ἐπʼ ἀρχὴν βαδίζει τὸ γιγνόμενον καὶ τέλος (ἀρχὴ γὰρ τὸ οὗ ἕνεκα, τοῦ τέλους δὲ ἕνεκα ἡ γένεσις), τέλος δʼ ἡ ἐνέργεια, καὶ τούτου χάριν ἡ δύναμις λαμβάνεται. οὐ γὰρ ἵνα ὄψιν ἔχωσιν ὁρῶσι τὰ ζῷα ἀλλʼ ὅπως ὁρῶσιν ὄψιν ἔχουσιν, ὁμοίως δὲ καὶ οἰκοδομικὴν ἵνα οἰκοδομῶσι καὶ τὴν θεωρητικὴν ἵνα θεωρῶσιν· ἀλλʼ οὐ θεωροῦσιν ἵνα θεωρητικὴν ἔχωσιν, εἰ μὴ οἱ μελετῶντες· οὗτοι δὲ οὐχὶ θεωροῦσιν ἀλλʼ ἢ ὡδί, †ἢ ὅτι οὐδὲν δέονται θεωρεῖν†. ἔτι ἡ ὕλη ἔστι δυνάμει ὅτι ἔλθοι ἂν εἰς τὸ εἶδος· ὅταν δέ γε ἐνεργείᾳ ᾖ, τότε ἐν τῷ εἴδει ἐστίν. ὁμοίως δὲ καὶ ἐπὶ τῶν ἄλλων, καὶ ὧν κίνησις τὸ τέλος, διὸ ὥσπερ οἱ διδάσκοντες ἐνεργοῦντα ἐπιδείξαντες οἴονται τὸ τέλος ἀποδεδωκέναι, καὶ ἡ φύσις ὁμοίως. εἰ γὰρ μὴ οὕτω γίγνεται, ὁ Παύσωνος ἔσται Ἑρμῆς· ἄδηλος γὰρ καὶ ἡ ἐπιστήμη εἰ ἔσω ἢ ἔξω, ὥσπερ κἀκεῖνος. τὸ γὰρ ἔργον τέλος, ἡ δὲ ἐνέργεια τὸ ἔργον, διὸ καὶ τοὔνομα ἐνέργεια λέγεται κατὰ τὸ ἔργον καὶ συντείνει πρὸς τὴν ἐντελέχειαν. ἐπεὶ δʼ ἐστὶ τῶν μὲν ἔσχατον ἡ χρῆσις (οἷον ὄψεως ἡ ὅρασις, καὶ οὐθὲν γίγνεται παρὰ ταύτην ἕτερον ἀπὸ τῆς ὄψεως), ἀπʼ ἐνίων δὲ γίγνεταί τι (οἷον ἀπὸ τῆς οἰκοδομικῆς οἰκία παρὰ τὴν οἰκοδόμησιν), ὅμως οὐθὲν ἧττον ἔνθα μὲν τέλος, ἔνθα δὲ μᾶλλον τέλος τῆς δυνάμεώς ἐστιν· ἡ γὰρ οἰκοδόμησις ἐν τῷ οἰκοδομουμένῳ, καὶ ἅμα γίγνεται καὶ ἔστι τῇ οἰκίᾳ. ὅσων μὲν οὖν ἕτερόν τί ἐστι παρὰ τὴν χρῆσιν τὸ γιγνόμενον, τούτων μὲν ἡ ἐνέργεια ἐν τῷ ποιουμένῳ ἐστίν (οἷον ἥ τε οἰκοδόμησις ἐν τῷ οἰκοδομουμένῳ καὶ ἡ ὕφανσις ἐν τῷ ὑφαινομένῳ, ὁμοίως δὲ καὶ ἐπὶ τῶν ἄλλων, καὶ ὅλως ἡ κίνησις ἐν τῷ κινουμένῳ)· ὅσων δὲ μὴ ἔστιν ἄλλο τι ἔργον παρὰ τὴν ἐνέργειαν, ἐν αὐτοῖς ὑπάρχει ἡ ἐνέργεια (οἷον ἡ ὅρασις ἐν τῷ ὁρῶντι καὶ ἡ θεωρία ἐν τῷ θεωροῦντι καὶ ἡ ζωὴ ἐν τῇ ψυχῇ, διὸ καὶ ἡ εὐδαιμονία·  ζωὴ γὰρ ποιά τίς ἐστιν). ὥστε φανερὸν ὅτι ἡ οὐσία καὶ τὸ εἶδος ἐνέργειά ἐστιν. κατά τε δὴ τοῦτον τὸν λόγον φανερὸν ὅτι πρότερον τῇ οὐσίᾳ ἐνέργεια δυνάμεως, καὶ ὥσπερ εἴπομεν, τοῦ χρόνου ἀεὶ προλαμβάνει ἐνέργεια ἑτέρα πρὸ ἑτέρας ἕως τῆς τοῦ ἀεὶ κινοῦντος πρώτως.—ἀλλὰ μὴν καὶ κυριωτέρως· τὰ μὲν γὰρ ἀΐδια πρότερα τῇ οὐσίᾳ τῶν φθαρτῶν, ἔστι δʼ οὐθὲν δυνάμει ἀΐδιον. λόγος δὲ ὅδε· πᾶσα δύναμις ἅμα τῆς ἀντιφάσεώς ἐστιν· τὸ μὲν γὰρ μὴ δυνατὸν ὑπάρχειν οὐκ ἂν ὑπάρξειεν οὐθενί, τὸ δυνατὸν δὲ πᾶν ἐνδέχεται μὴ ἐνεργεῖν. τὸ ἄρα δυνατὸν εἶναι ἐνδέχεται καὶ εἶναι καὶ μὴ εἶναι· τὸ αὐτὸ ἄρα δυνατὸν καὶ εἶναι καὶ μὴ εἶναι. τὸ δὲ δυνατὸν μὴ εἶναι ἐνδέχεται μὴ εἶναι· τὸ δὲ ἐνδεχόμενον μὴ εἶναι φθαρτόν, ἢ ἁπλῶς ἢ τοῦτο αὐτὸ ὃ λέγεται ἐνδέχεσθαι μὴ εἶναι, ἢ κατὰ τόπον ἢ κατὰ τὸ ποσὸν ἢ ποιόν· ἁπλῶς δὲ τὸ κατʼ οὐσίαν. οὐθὲν ἄρα τῶν ἀφθάρτων ἁπλῶς δυνάμει ἔστιν ἁπλῶς (κατά τι δὲ οὐδὲν κωλύει, οἷον ποιὸν ἢ πού)· ἐνεργείᾳ ἄρα πάντα· οὐδὲ τῶν ἐξ ἀνάγκης ὄντων (καίτοι ταῦτα πρῶτα· εἰ γὰρ ταῦτα μὴ ἦν, οὐθὲν ἂν ἦν)· οὐδὲ δὴ κίνησις, εἴ τίς ἐστιν ἀΐδιος· οὐδʼ εἴ τι κινούμενον ἀΐδιον, οὐκ ἔστι κατὰ δύναμιν κινούμενον ἀλλʼ ἢ ποθὲν ποί (τούτου δʼ ὕλην οὐδὲν κωλύει ὑπάρχειν), διὸ ἀεὶ ἐνεργεῖ ἥλιος καὶ ἄστρα καὶ ὅλος ὁ οὐρανός, καὶ οὐ φοβερὸν μή ποτε στῇ, ὃ φοβοῦνται οἱ περὶ φύσεως. οὐδὲ κάμνει τοῦτο δρῶντα· οὐ γὰρ περὶ τὴν δύναμιν τῆς ἀντιφάσεως αὐτοῖς, οἷον τοῖς φθαρτοῖς, ἡ κίνησις, ὥστε ἐπίπονον εἶναι τὴν συνέχειαν τῆς κινήσεως· ἡ γὰρ οὐσία ὕλη καὶ δύναμις οὖσα, οὐκ ἐνέργεια, αἰτία τούτου. μιμεῖται δὲ τὰ ἄφθαρτα καὶ τὰ ἐν μεταβολῇ ὄντα, οἷον γῆ καὶ πῦρ. καὶ γὰρ ταῦτα ἀεὶ ἐνεργεῖ· καθʼ αὑτὰ γὰρ καὶ ἐν αὑτοῖς ἔχει τὴν κίνησιν. αἱ δὲ ἄλλαι δυνάμεις, ἐξ ὧν διώρισται, πᾶσαι τῆς ἀντιφάσεώς εἰσιν· τὸ γὰρ δυνάμενον ὡδὶ κινεῖν δύναται καὶ μὴ ὡδί, ὅσα γε κατὰ λόγον· αἱ δʼ ἄλογοι τῷ παρεῖναι καὶ μὴ τῆς ἀντιφάσεως ἔσονται αἱ αὐταί. εἰ ἄρα τινὲς εἰσὶ φύσεις τοιαῦται ἢ οὐσίαι οἵας λέγουσιν οἱ ἐν τοῖς λόγοις τὰς ἰδέας, πολὺ μᾶλλον ἐπιστῆμον ἄν τι εἴη ἢ αὐτὸ ἐπιστήμη καὶ κινούμενον ἢ κίνησις·  ταῦτα γὰρ ἐνέργειαι μᾶλλον, ἐκεῖναι δὲ δυνάμεις τούτων. ὅτι μὲν οὖν πρότερον ἡ ἐνέργεια καὶ δυνάμεως καὶ πάσης ἀρχῆς μεταβλητικῆς, φανερόν. \n\n\n\n \n\n \n\nὅτι δὲ καὶ βελτίων καὶ τιμιωτέρα τῆς σπουδαίας δυνάμεως ἡ ἐνέργεια, ἐκ τῶνδε δῆλον. ὅσα γὰρ κατὰ τὸ δύνασθαι λέγεται, ταὐτόν ἐστι δυνατὸν τἀναντία, οἷον τὸ δύνασθαι λεγόμενον ὑγιαίνειν ταὐτόν ἐστι καὶ τὸ νοσεῖν, καὶ ἅμα· ἡ αὐτὴ γὰρ δύναμις τοῦ ὑγιαίνειν καὶ κάμνειν, καὶ ἠρεμεῖν καὶ κινεῖσθαι, καὶ οἰκοδομεῖν καὶ καταβάλλειν, καὶ οἰκοδομεῖσθαι καὶ καταπίπτειν. τὸ μὲν οὖν δύνασθαι τἀναντία ἅμα ὑπάρχει· τὰ δʼ ἐναντία ἅμα ἀδύνατον, καὶ τὰς ἐνεργείας δὲ ἅμα ἀδύνατον ὑπάρχειν (οἷον ὑγιαίνειν καὶ κάμνειν), ὥστʼ ἀνάγκη τούτων θάτερον εἶναι τἀγαθόν, τὸ δὲ δύνασθαι ὁμοίως ἀμφότερον ἢ οὐδέτερον· ἡ ἄρα ἐνέργεια βελτίων. ἀνάγκη δὲ καὶ ἐπὶ τῶν κακῶν τὸ τέλος καὶ τὴν ἐνέργειαν εἶναι χεῖρον τῆς δυνάμεως· τὸ γὰρ δυνάμενον ταὐτὸ ἄμφω τἀναντία. δῆλον ἄρα ὅτι οὐκ ἔστι τὸ κακὸν παρὰ τὰ πράγματα· ὕστερον γὰρ τῇ φύσει τὸ κακὸν τῆς δυνάμεως. οὐκ ἄρα οὐδʼ ἐν τοῖς ἐξ ἀρχῆς καὶ τοῖς ἀϊδίοις οὐθὲν ἔστιν οὔτε κακὸν οὔτε ἁμάρτημα οὔτε διεφθαρμένον (καὶ γὰρ ἡ διαφθορὰ τῶν κακῶν ἐστίν). εὑρίσκεται δὲ καὶ τὰ διαγράμματα ἐνεργείᾳ· διαιροῦντες γὰρ εὑρίσκουσιν. εἰ δʼ ἦν διῃρημένα, φανερὰ ἂν ἦν· νῦν δʼ ἐνυπάρχει δυνάμει. διὰ τί δύο ὀρθαὶ τὸ τρίγωνον; ὅτι αἱ περὶ μίαν στιγμὴν γωνίαι ἴσαι δύο ὀρθαῖς. εἰ οὖν ἀνῆκτο ἡ παρὰ τὴν πλευράν, ἰδόντι ἂν ἦν εὐθὺς δῆλον διὰ τί. ἐν ἡμικυκλίῳ ὀρθὴ καθόλου διὰ τί; ἐὰν ἴσαι τρεῖς, ἥ τε βάσις δύο καὶ ἡ ἐκ μέσου ἐπισταθεῖσα ὀρθή, ἰδόντι δῆλον τῷ ἐκεῖνο εἰδότι. ὥστε φανερὸν ὅτι τὰ δυνάμει ὄντα εἰς ἐνέργειαν ἀγόμενα εὑρίσκεται· αἴτιον δὲ ὅτι ἡ νόησις ἐνέργεια· ὥστʼ ἐξ ἐνεργείας ἡ δύναμις, καὶ διὰ τοῦτο ποιοῦντες γιγνώσκουσιν (ὕστερον γὰρ γενέσει ἡ ἐνέργεια ἡ κατʼ ἀριθμόν). \n\n\n\n \n\n \n\nἐπεὶ δὲ τὸ ὂν λέγεται καὶ τὸ μὴ ὂν τὸ μὲν κατὰ τὰ σχήματα τῶν κατηγοριῶν, τὸ δὲ κατὰ δύναμιν ἢ ἐνέργειαν τούτων ἢ τἀναντία,  τὸ δὲ  ἀληθὲς ἢ ψεῦδος, τοῦτο δʼ ἐπὶ τῶν πραγμάτων ἐστὶ τῷ συγκεῖσθαι ἢ διῃρῆσθαι, ὥστε ἀληθεύει μὲν ὁ τὸ διῃρημένον οἰόμενος διῃρῆσθαι καὶ τὸ συγκείμενον συγκεῖσθαι, ἔψευσται δὲ ὁ ἐναντίως ἔχων ἢ τὰ πράγματα, πότʼ ἔστιν ἢ οὐκ ἔστι τὸ ἀληθὲς λεγόμενον ἢ ψεῦδος; τοῦτο γὰρ σκεπτέον τί λέγομεν. οὐ γὰρ διὰ τὸ ἡμᾶς οἴεσθαι ἀληθῶς σε λευκὸν εἶναι εἶ σὺ λευκός, ἀλλὰ διὰ τὸ σὲ εἶναι λευκὸν ἡμεῖς οἱ φάντες τοῦτο ἀληθεύομεν. εἰ δὴ τὰ μὲν ἀεὶ σύγκειται καὶ ἀδύνατα διαιρεθῆναι, τὰ δʼ ἀεὶ διῄρηται καὶ ἀδύνατα συντεθῆναι, τὰ δʼ ἐνδέχεται τἀναντία, τὸ μὲν εἶναί ἐστι τὸ συγκεῖσθαι καὶ ἓν εἶναι, τὸ δὲ μὴ εἶναι τὸ μὴ συγκεῖσθαι ἀλλὰ πλείω εἶναι· περὶ μὲν οὖν τὰ ἐνδεχόμενα ἡ αὐτὴ γίγνεται ψευδὴς καὶ ἀληθὴς δόξα καὶ ὁ λόγος ὁ αὐτός, καὶ ἐνδέχεται ὁτὲ μὲν ἀληθεύειν ὁτὲ δὲ ψεύδεσθαι· περὶ δὲ τὰ ἀδύνατα ἄλλως ἔχειν οὐ γίγνεται ὁτὲ μὲν ἀληθὲς ὁτὲ δὲ ψεῦδος, ἀλλʼ ἀεὶ ταὐτὰ ἀληθῆ καὶ ψευδῆ.—περὶ δὲ δὴ τὰ ἀσύνθετα τί τὸ εἶναι ἢ μὴ εἶναι καὶ τὸ ἀληθὲς καὶ τὸ ψεῦδος; οὐ γάρ ἐστι σύνθετον, ὥστε εἶναι μὲν ὅταν συγκέηται, μὴ εἶναι δὲ ἐὰν διῃρημένον ᾖ, ὥσπερ τὸ λευκὸν τὸ ξύλον ἢ τὸ ἀσύμμετρον τὴν διάμετρον· οὐδὲ τὸ ἀληθὲς καὶ τὸ ψεῦδος ὁμοίως ἔτι ὑπάρξει καὶ ἐπʼ ἐκείνων. ἢ ὥσπερ οὐδὲ τὸ ἀληθὲς ἐπὶ τούτων τὸ αὐτό, οὕτως οὐδὲ τὸ εἶναι, ἀλλʼ ἔστι τὸ μὲν ἀληθὲς ἢ ψεῦδος, τὸ μὲν θιγεῖν καὶ φάναι ἀληθές (οὐ γὰρ ταὐτὸ κατάφασις καὶ φάσις), τὸ δʼ ἀγνοεῖν μὴ θιγγάνειν (ἀπατηθῆναι γὰρ περὶ τὸ τί ἐστιν οὐκ ἔστιν ἀλλʼ ἢ κατὰ συμβεβηκός· ὁμοίως δὲ καὶ περὶ τὰς μὴ συνθετὰς οὐσίας, οὐ γὰρ ἔστιν ἀπατηθῆναι· καὶ πᾶσαι εἰσὶν ἐνεργείᾳ, οὐ δυνάμει, ἐγίγνοντο γὰρ ἂν καὶ ἐφθείροντο, νῦν δὲ τὸ ὂν αὐτὸ οὐ γίγνεται οὐδὲ φθείρεται, ἔκ τινος γὰρ ἂν ἐγίγνετο·—ὅσα δή ἐστιν ὅπερ εἶναί τι καὶ ἐνέργειαι, περὶ ταῦτα οὐκ ἔστιν ἀπατηθῆναι ἀλλʼ ἢ νοεῖν ἢ μή· ἀλλὰ τὸ τί ἐστι ζητεῖται περὶ αὐτῶν, εἰ τοιαῦτά ἐστιν ἢ μή)· τὸ δὲ εἶναι ὡς τὸ ἀληθές, καὶ τὸ μὴ εἶναι τὸ ὡς τὸ ψεῦδος, ἓν μέν ἐστιν, εἰ σύγκειται, ἀληθές, τὸ δʼ εἰ μὴ σύγκειται, ψεῦδος· τὸ δὲ ἕν, εἴπερ ὄν, οὕτως ἐστίν, εἰ δὲ μὴ οὕτως, οὐκ ἔστιν·  τὸ δὲ ἀληθὲς τὸ νοεῖν ταῦτα· τὸ δὲ ψεῦδος οὐκ ἔστιν, οὐδὲ ἀπάτη, ἀλλὰ ἄγνοια, οὐχ οἵα ἡ τυφλότης· ἡ μὲν γὰρ τυφλότης ἐστὶν ὡς ἂν εἰ τὸ νοητικὸν ὅλως μὴ ἔχοι τις. φανερὸν δὲ καὶ ὅτι περὶ τῶν ἀκινήτων οὐκ ἔστιν ἀπάτη κατὰ τὸ ποτέ, εἴ τις ὑπολαμβάνει ἀκίνητα. οἷον τὸ τρίγωνον εἰ μὴ μεταβάλλειν οἴεται, οὐκ οἰήσεται ποτὲ μὲν δύο ὀρθὰς ἔχειν ποτὲ δὲ οὔ (μεταβάλλοι γὰρ ἄν), ἀλλὰ τὶ μὲν τὶ δʼ οὔ, οἷον ἄρτιον ἀριθμὸν πρῶτον εἶναι μηθένα, ἢ τινὰς μὲν τινὰς δʼ οὔ· ἀριθμῷ δὲ περὶ ἕνα οὐδὲ τοῦτο· οὐ γὰρ ἔτι τινὰ μὲν τινὰ δὲ οὒ οἰήσεται, ἀλλʼ ἀληθεύσει ἢ ψεύσεται ὡς ἀεὶ οὕτως ἔχοντος.","paragraphs":[{"id":"RAW_DAZY_BOO9:P0001","text":"περὶ μὲν οὖν τοῦ πρώτως ὄντος καὶ πρὸς ὃ πᾶσαι αἱ ἄλλαι κατηγορίαι τοῦ ὄντος ἀναφέρονται εἴρηται, περὶ τῆς οὐσίας (κατὰ γὰρ τὸν τῆς οὐσίας λόγον λέγεται τἆλλα ὄντα, τό τε ποσὸν καὶ τὸ ποιὸν καὶ τἆλλα τὰ οὕτω λεγόμενα· πάντα γὰρ ἕξει τὸν τῆς οὐσίας λόγον, ὥσπερ εἴπομεν ἐν τοῖς πρώτοις λόγοις)· ἐπεὶ δὲ λέγεται τὸ ὂν τὸ μὲν τὸ τὶ ἢ ποιὸν ἢ ποσόν, τὸ δὲ κατὰ δύναμιν καὶ ἐντελέχειαν καὶ κατὰ τὸ ἔργον, διορίσωμεν καὶ περὶ δυνάμεως καὶ ἐντελεχείας, καὶ πρῶτον περὶ δυνάμεως ἣ λέγεται μὲν μάλιστα κυρίως, οὐ μὴν χρησιμωτάτη γέ ἐστι πρὸς ὃ βουλόμεθα νῦν·  ἐπὶ πλέον γάρ ἐστιν ἡ δύναμις καὶ ἡ ἐνέργεια τῶν μόνον λεγομένων κατὰ κίνησιν. ἀλλʼ εἰπόντες περὶ ταύτης, ἐν τοῖς περὶ τῆς ἐνεργείας διορισμοῖς δηλώσομεν καὶ περὶ τῶν ἄλλων.","kind":"passage"},{"id":"RAW_DAZY_BOO9:P0002","text":"ὅτι μὲν οὖν λέγεται πολλαχῶς ἡ δύναμις καὶ τὸ δύνασθαι, διώρισται ἡμῖν ἐν ἄλλοις· τούτων δʼ ὅσαι μὲν ὁμωνύμως λέγονται δυνάμεις ἀφείσθωσαν (ἔνιαι γὰρ ὁμοιότητί τινι λέγονται, καθάπερ ἐν γεωμετρίᾳ καὶ δυνατὰ καὶ ἀδύνατα λέγομεν τῷ εἶναί πως ἢ μὴ εἶναι), ὅσαι δὲ πρὸς τὸ αὐτὸ εἶδος, πᾶσαι ἀρχαί τινές εἰσι, καὶ πρὸς πρώτην μίαν λέγονται, ἥ ἐστιν ἀρχὴ μεταβολῆς ἐν ἄλλῳ ἢ ᾗ ἄλλο. ἡ μὲν γὰρ τοῦ παθεῖν ἐστὶ δύναμις, ἡ ἐν αὐτῷ τῷ πάσχοντι ἀρχὴ μεταβολῆς παθητικῆς ὑπʼ ἄλλου ἢ ᾗ ἄλλο· ἡ δʼ ἕξις ἀπαθείας τῆς ἐπὶ τὸ χεῖρον καὶ φθορᾶς τῆς ὑπʼ ἄλλου ἢ ᾗ ἄλλο ὑπʼ ἀρχῆς μεταβλητικῆς. ἐν γὰρ τούτοις ἔνεστι πᾶσι τοῖς ὅροις ὁ τῆς πρώτης δυνάμεως λόγος.","kind":"passage"},{"id":"RAW_DAZY_BOO9:P0003","text":"πάλιν δʼ αὗται δυνάμεις λέγονται ἢ τοῦ μόνον ποιῆσαι ἢ  παθεῖν ἢ τοῦ καλῶς, ὥστε καὶ ἐν τοῖς τούτων λόγοις ἐνυπάρχουσί πως οἱ τῶν προτέρων δυνάμεων λόγοι.—φανερὸν οὖν ὅτι ἔστι μὲν ὡς μία δύναμις τοῦ ποιεῖν καὶ πάσχειν (δυνατὸν γάρ ἐστι καὶ τῷ ἔχειν αὐτὸ δύναμιν τοῦ παθεῖν καὶ τῷ ἄλλο ὑπʼ αὐτοῦ), ἔστι δὲ ὡς ἄλλη. ἡ μὲν γὰρ ἐν τῷ πάσχοντι (διὰ γὰρ τὸ ἔχειν τινὰ ἀρχήν, καὶ εἶναι καὶ τὴν ὕλην ἀρχήν τινα, πάσχει τὸ πάσχον, καὶ ἄλλο ὑπʼ ἄλλου· τὸ λιπαρὸν μὲν γὰρ καυστὸν τὸ δʼ ὑπεῖκον ὡδὶ θλαστόν, ὁμοίως δὲ καὶ ἐπὶ τῶν ἄλλων), ἡ δʼ ἐν τῷ ποιοῦντι, οἷον τὸ θερμὸν καὶ ἡ οἰκοδομική, ἡ μὲν ἐν τῷ θερμαντικῷ ἡ δʼ ἐν τῷ οἰκοδομικῷ· διὸ ᾗ συμπέφυκεν, οὐθὲν πάσχει αὐτὸ ὑφʼ ἑαυτοῦ· ἓν γὰρ καὶ οὐκ ἄλλο. καὶ ἡ ἀδυναμία καὶ τὸ ἀδύνατον ἡ τῇ τοιαύτῃ δυνάμει ἐναντία στέρησίς ἐστιν, ὥστε τοῦ αὐτοῦ καὶ κατὰ τὸ αὐτὸ πᾶσα δύναμις ἀδυναμίᾳ.","kind":"passage"},{"id":"RAW_DAZY_BOO9:P0004","text":"ἡ δὲ στέρησις λέγεται πολλαχῶς· καὶ γὰρ τὸ μὴ ἔχον καὶ τὸ πεφυκὸς ἂν μὴ ἔχῃ, ἢ ὅλως ἢ ὅτε πέφυκεν, καὶ ἢ ὡδί, οἷον παντελῶς, ἢ κἂν ὁπωσοῦν. ἐπʼ ἐνίων δέ, ἂν πεφυκότα ἔχειν μὴ ἔχῃ βίᾳ, ἐστερῆσθαι ταῦτα λέγομεν.","kind":"passage"},{"id":"RAW_DAZY_BOO9:P0005","text":"ἐπεὶ δʼ αἱ μὲν ἐν τοῖς ἀψύχοις ἐνυπάρχουσιν ἀρχαὶ τοιαῦται, αἱ δʼ ἐν τοῖς ἐμψύχοις καὶ ἐν ψυχῇ καὶ τῆς ψυχῆς ἐν τῷ λόγον ἔχοντι,  δῆλον ὅτι καὶ τῶν δυνάμεων αἱ μὲν ἔσονται ἄλογοι αἱ δὲ μετὰ λόγου· διὸ πᾶσαι αἱ τέχναι καὶ αἱ ποιητικαὶ ἐπιστῆμαι δυνάμεις εἰσίν· ἀρχαὶ γὰρ μεταβλητικαί εἰσιν ἐν ἄλλῳ ἢ ᾗ ἄλλο. καὶ αἱ μὲν μετὰ λόγου πᾶσαι τῶν ἐναντίων αἱ αὐταί, αἱ δὲ ἄλογοι μία ἑνός, οἷον τὸ θερμὸν τοῦ θερμαίνειν μόνονʼ ἡ δὲ ἰατρικὴ νόσου καὶ ὑγιείας.","kind":"passage"},{"id":"RAW_DAZY_BOO9:P0006","text":"αἴτιον δὲ ὅτι λόγος ἐστὶν ἡ ἐπιστήμη, ὁ δὲ λόγος ὁ αὐτὸς δηλοῖ τὸ πρᾶγμα καὶ τὴν στέρησιν, πλὴν οὐχ ὡσαύτως, καὶ ἔστιν ὡς ἀμφοῖν ἔστι δʼ ὡς τοῦ ὑπάρχοντος μᾶλλον, ὥστʼ ἀνάγκη καὶ τὰς τοιαύτας ἐπιστήμας εἶναι μὲν τῶν ἐναντίων, εἶναι δὲ τοῦ μὲν καθʼ αὑτὰς τοῦ δὲ μὴ καθʼ αὑτάς· καὶ γὰρ ὁ λόγος τοῦ μὲν καθʼ αὑτὸ τοῦ δὲ τρόπον τινὰ κατὰ συμβεβηκός· ἀποφάσει γὰρ καὶ ἀποφορᾷ δηλοῖ τὸ ἐναντίον· ἡ γὰρ στέρησις ἡ πρώτη τὸ ἐναντίον, αὕτη δὲ ἀποφορὰ θατέρου. ἐπεὶ δὲ τὰ ἐναντία οὐκ ἐγγίγνεται ἐν τῷ αὐτῷ, ἡ δʼ ἐπιστήμη δύναμις τῷ λόγον ἔχειν, καὶ ἡ ψυχὴ κινήσεως ἔχει ἀρχήν, τὸ μὲν ὑγιεινὸν ὑγίειαν μόνον ποιεῖ καὶ τὸ θερμαντικὸν θερμότητα καὶ τὸ ψυκτικὸν ψυχρότητα, ὁ δʼ ἐπιστήμων ἄμφω.","kind":"passage"},{"id":"RAW_DAZY_BOO9:P0007","text":"λόγος γάρ ἐστιν ἀμφοῖν μέν, οὐχ ὁμοίως δέ, καὶ ἐν ψυχῇ ἣ ἔχει κινήσεως ἀρχήν· ὥστε ἄμφω ἀπὸ τῆς αὐτῆς ἀρχῆς κινήσει πρὸς ταὐτὸ συνάψασα· διὸ τὰ κατὰ λόγον δυνατὰ τοῖς ἄνευ λόγου δυνατοῖς ποιεῖ τἀναντία· μιᾷ γὰρ ἀρχῇ περιέχεται, τῷ λόγῳ. φανερὸν δὲ καὶ ὅτι τῇ μὲν τοῦ εὖ δυνάμει ἀκολουθεῖ ἡ τοῦ μόνον ποιῆσαι ἢ παθεῖν δύναμις, ταύτῃ δʼ ἐκείνη οὐκ ἀεί· ἀνάγκη γὰρ τὸν εὖ ποιοῦντα καὶ ποιεῖν, τὸν δὲ μόνον ποιοῦντα οὐκ ἀνάγκη καὶ εὖ ποιεῖν.","kind":"passage"},{"id":"RAW_DAZY_BOO9:P0008","text":"εἰσὶ δέ τινες οἵ φασιν, οἷον οἱ Μεγαρικοί, ὅταν ἐνεργῇ μόνον δύνασθαι, ὅταν δὲ μὴ ἐνεργῇ οὐ δύνασθαι, οἷον τὸν μὴ οἰκοδομοῦντα οὐ δύνασθαι οἰκοδομεῖν, ἀλλὰ τὸν οἰκοδομοῦντα ὅταν οἰκοδομῇ· ὁμοίως δὲ καὶ ἐπὶ τῶν ἄλλων. οἷς τὰ συμβαίνοντα ἄτοπα οὐ χαλεπὸν ἰδεῖν. δῆλον γὰρ ὅτι οὔτʼ οἰκοδόμος ἔσται ἐὰν μὴ οἰκοδομῇ (τὸ γὰρ οἰκοδόμῳ εἶναι τὸ δυνατῷ εἶναί ἐστιν οἰκοδομεῖν), ὁμοίως δὲ καὶ ἐπὶ τῶν ἄλλων τεχνῶν. εἰ οὖν ἀδύνατον τὰς τοιαύτας ἔχειν τέχνας μὴ μαθόντα ποτὲ καὶ λαβόντα,  καὶ μὴ ἔχειν μὴ ἀποβαλόντα ποτέ (ἢ γὰρ λήθῃ ἢ πάθει τινὶ ἢ χρόνῳ· οὐ γὰρ δὴ τοῦ γε πράγματος φθαρέντος, ἀεὶ γὰρ ἔστιν), ὅταν παύσηται, οὐχ ἕξει τὴν τέχνην, πάλιν δʼ εὐθὺς οἰκοδομήσει πῶς λαβών; καὶ τὰ ἄψυχα δὴ ὁμοίως· οὔτε γὰρ ψυχρὸν οὔτε θερμὸν οὔτε γλυκὺ οὔτε ὅλως αἰσθητὸν οὐθὲν ἔσται μὴ αἰσθανομένων· ὥστε τὸν Πρωταγόρου λόγον συμβήσεται λέγειν αὐτοῖς. ἀλλὰ μὴν οὐδʼ αἴσθησιν ἕξει οὐδὲν ἂν μὴ αἰσθάνηται μηδʼ ἐνεργῇ.","kind":"passage"},{"id":"RAW_DAZY_BOO9:P0009","text":"εἰ οὖν τυφλὸν τὸ μὴ ἔχον ὄψιν, πεφυκὸς δὲ καὶ ὅτε πέφυκε καὶ ἔτι ὄν, οἱ αὐτοὶ τυφλοὶ ἔσονται πολλάκις τῆς ἡμέρας, καὶ κωφοί. ἔτι εἰ ἀδύνατον τὸ ἐστερημένον δυνάμεως, τὸ μὴ γιγνόμενον ἀδύνατον ἔσται γενέσθαι· τὸ δʼ ἀδύνατον γενέσθαι ὁ λέγων ἢ εἶναι ἢ ἔσεσθαι ψεύσεται (τὸ γὰρ ἀδύνατον τοῦτο ἐσήμαινεν), ὥστε οὗτοι οἱ λόγοι ἐξαιροῦσι καὶ κίνησιν καὶ γένεσιν. ἀεὶ γὰρ τό τε ἑστηκὸς ἑστήξεται καὶ τὸ καθήμενον καθεδεῖται· οὐ γὰρ ἀναστήσεται ἂν καθέζηται· ἀδύνατον γὰρ ἔσται ἀναστῆναι ὅ γε μὴ δύναται ἀναστῆναι. εἰ οὖν μὴ ἐνδέχεται ταῦτα λέγειν, φανερὸν ὅτι δύναμις καὶ ἐνέργεια ἕτερόν ἐστιν (ἐκεῖνοι δʼ οἱ λόγοι δύναμιν καὶ ἐνέργειαν ταὐτὸ ποιοῦσιν, διὸ καὶ οὐ μικρόν τι ζητοῦσιν ἀναιρεῖν), ὥστε ἐνδέχεται δυνατὸν μέν τι εἶναι μὴ εἶναι δέ, καὶ δυνατὸν μὴ εἶναι εἶναι δέ, ὁμοίως δὲ καὶ ἐπὶ τῶν ἄλλων κατηγοριῶν δυνατὸν βαδίζειν ὂν μὴ βαδίζειν, καὶ μὴ βαδίζειν δυνατὸν ὂν βαδίζειν.","kind":"passage"},{"id":"RAW_DAZY_BOO9:P0010","text":"ἔστι δὲ δυνατὸν τοῦτο ᾧ ἐὰν ὑπάρξῃ ἡ ἐνέργεια οὗ λέγεται ἔχειν τὴν δύναμιν, οὐθὲν ἔσται ἀδύνατον. λέγω δὲ οἷον, εἰ δυνατὸν καθῆσθαι καὶ ἐνδέχεται καθῆσθαι, τούτῳ ἐὰν ὑπάρξῃ τὸ καθῆσθαι, οὐδὲν ἔσται ἀδύνατον· καὶ εἰ κινηθῆναι ἢ κινῆσαι ἢ στῆναι ἢ στῆσαι ἢ εἶναι ἢ γίγνεσθαι ἢ μὴ εἶναι ἢ μὴ γίγνεσθαι, ὁμοίως. ἐλήλυθε δʼ ἡ ἐνέργεια τοὔνομα, ἡ πρὸς τὴν ἐντελέχειαν συντιθεμένη, καὶ ἐπὶ τὰ ἄλλα ἐκ τῶν κινήσεων μάλιστα· δοκεῖ γὰρ ἡ ἐνέργεια μάλιστα ἡ κίνησις εἶναι, διὸ καὶ τοῖς μὴ οὖσιν οὐκ ἀποδιδόασι τὸ κινεῖσθαι, ἄλλας δέ τινας κατηγορίας, οἷον διανοητὰ καὶ ἐπιθυμητὰ εἶναι τὰ μὴ ὄντα, κινούμενα δὲ οὔ, τοῦτο δὲ ὅτι οὐκ ὄντα ἐνεργείᾳ ἔσονται ἐνεργείᾳ.  τῶν γὰρ μὴ ὄντων ἔνια δυνάμει ἐστίν· οὐκ ἔστι δέ, ὅτι οὐκ ἐντελεχείᾳ ἐστίν.","kind":"passage"},{"id":"RAW_DAZY_BOO9:P0011","text":"εἰ δέ ἐστι τὸ εἰρημένον τὸ δυνατὸν ἢ ἀκολουθεῖ, φανερὸν ὅτι οὐκ ἐνδέχεται ἀληθὲς εἶναι τὸ εἰπεῖν ὅτι δυνατὸν μὲν τοδί, οὐκ ἔσται δέ, ὥστε τὰ ἀδύνατα εἶναι ταύτῃ διαφεύγειν· λέγω δὲ οἷον εἴ τις φαίη δυνατὸν τὴν διάμετρον μετρηθῆναι οὐ μέντοι μετρηθήσεσθαι—ὁ μὴ λογιζόμενος τὸ ἀδύνατον εἶναι—ὅτι οὐθὲν κωλύει δυνατόν τι ὂν εἶναι ἢ γενέσθαι μὴ εἶναι μηδʼ ἔσεσθαι. ἀλλʼ ἐκεῖνο ἀνάγκη ἐκ τῶν κειμένων, εἰ καὶ ὑποθοίμεθα εἶναι ἢ γεγονέναι ὃ οὐκ ἔστι μὲν δυνατὸν δέ, ὅτι οὐθὲν ἔσται ἀδύνατον· συμβήσεται δέ γε, τὸ γὰρ μετρεῖσθαι ἀδύνατον. οὐ γὰρ δή ἐστι ταὐτὸ τὸ ψεῦδος καὶ τὸ ἀδύνατον· τὸ γάρ σε ἑστάναι νῦν ψεῦδος μέν, οὐκ ἀδύνατον δέ. ἅμα δὲ δῆλον καὶ ὅτι, εἰ τοῦ Α ὄντος ἀνάγκη τὸ Β εἶναι, καὶ δυνατοῦ ὄντος εἶναι τοῦ Α καὶ τὸ Β ἀνάγκη εἶναι δυνατόν· εἰ γὰρ μὴ ἀνάγκη δυνατὸν εἶναι, οὐθὲν κωλύει μὴ εἶναι δυνατὸν εἶναι. ἔστω δὴ τὸ Α δυνατόν.","kind":"passage"},{"id":"RAW_DAZY_BOO9:P0012","text":"οὐκοῦν ὅτε τὸ Α δυνατὸν εἴη εἶναι, εἰ τεθείη τὸ Α, οὐθὲν ἀδύνατον εἶναι συνέβαινεν· τὸ δέ γε Β ἀνάγκη εἶναι. ἀλλʼ ἦν ἀδύνατον. ἔστω δὴ ἀδύνατον. εἰ δὴ ἀδύνατον  εἶναι τὸ Β, ἀνάγκη καὶ τὸ Α εἶναι. ἀλλʼ ἦν ἄρα τὸ πρῶτον ἀδύνατον· καὶ τὸ δεύτερον ἄρα. ἂν ἄρα ᾖ τὸ Α δυνατόν, καὶ τὸ Β ἔσται δυνατόν, εἴπερ οὕτως εἶχον ὥστε τοῦ Α ὄντος ἀνάγκη εἶναι τὸ Β. ἐὰν δὴ οὕτως ἐχόντων  τῶν Α Β μὴ ᾖ δυνατὸν τὸ Β οὕτως, οὐδὲ τὰ Α Β ἕξει ὡς ἐτέθη· καὶ εἰ τοῦ Α δυνατοῦ ὄντος ἀνάγκη τὸ Β δυνατὸν εἶναι, εἰ ἔστι τὸ Α ἀνάγκη εἶναι καὶ τὸ Β. τὸ γὰρ δυνατὸν εἶναι ἐξ ἀνάγκης τὸ Β εἶναι, εἰ τὸ Α δυνατόν, τοῦτο σημαίνει, ἐὰν ᾖ τὸ Α καὶ ὅτε καὶ ὡς ἦν δυνατὸν εἶναι, κἀκεῖνο τότε καὶ οὕτως εἶναι ἀναγκαῖον.","kind":"passage"},{"id":"RAW_DAZY_BOO9:P0013","text":"ἁπασῶν δὲ τῶν δυνάμεων οὐσῶν τῶν μὲν συγγενῶν οἷον τῶν αἰσθήσεων, τῶν δὲ ἔθει οἷον τῆς τοῦ αὐλεῖν, τῶν δὲ μαθήσει οἷον τῆς τῶν τεχνῶν, τὰς μὲν ἀνάγκη προενεργήσαντας ἔχειν, ὅσαι ἔθει καὶ λόγῳ, τὰς δὲ μὴ τοιαύτας καὶ τὰς ἐπὶ τοῦ πάσχειν οὐκ ἀνάγκη.  ἐπεὶ δὲ τὸ δυνατὸν τὶ δυνατὸν καὶ ποτὲ καὶ πὼς καὶ ὅσα ἄλλα ἀνάγκη προσεῖναι ἐν τῷ διορισμῷ, καὶ τὰ μὲν κατὰ λόγον δύναται κινεῖν καὶ αἱ δυνάμεις αὐτῶν μετὰ λόγου, τὰ δὲ ἄλογα καὶ αἱ δυνάμεις ἄλογοι, κἀκείνας μὲν ἀνάγκη ἐν ἐμψύχῳ εἶναι ταύτας δὲ ἐν ἀμφοῖν, τὰς μὲν τοιαύτας δυνάμεις ἀνάγκη, ὅταν ὡς δύνανται τὸ ποιητικὸν καὶ τὸ παθητικὸν πλησιάζωσι, τὸ μὲν ποιεῖν τὸ δὲ πάσχειν, ἐκείνας δʼ οὐκ ἀνάγκη· αὗται μὲν γὰρ πᾶσαι μία ἑνὸς ποιητική, ἐκεῖναι δὲ τῶν ἐναντίων, ὥστε ἅμα ποιήσει τὰ ἐναντία· τοῦτο δὲ ἀδύνατον. ἀνάγκη ἄρα ἕτερόν τι εἶναι τὸ κύριον· λέγω δὲ τοῦτο ὄρεξιν ἢ προαίρεσιν.","kind":"passage"},{"id":"RAW_DAZY_BOO9:P0014","text":"ὁποτέρου γὰρ ἂν ὀρέγηται κυρίως, τοῦτο ποιήσει ὅταν ὡς δύναται ὑπάρχῃ καὶ πλησιάζῃ τῷ παθητικῷ· ὥστε τὸ δυνατὸν κατὰ λόγον ἅπαν ἀνάγκη, ὅταν ὀρέγηται οὗ ἔχει τὴν δύναμιν καὶ ὡς ἔχει, τοῦτο ποιεῖν· ἔχει δὲ παρόντος τοῦ παθητικοῦ καὶ ὡδὶ ἔχοντος · εἰ δὲ μή, ποιεῖν οὐ δυνήσεται (τὸ γὰρ μηθενὸς τῶν ἔξω κωλύοντος προσδιορίζεσθαι οὐθὲν ἔτι δεῖ· τὴν γὰρ δύναμιν ἔχει ὡς ἔστι δύναμις τοῦ ποιεῖν, ἔστι δʼ οὐ πάντως ἀλλʼ ἐχόντων πῶς, ἐν οἷς ἀφορισθήσεται καὶ τὰ ἔξω κωλύοντα· ἀφαιρεῖται γὰρ ταῦτα τῶν ἐν τῷ διορισμῷ προσόντων ἔνια)· διὸ οὐδʼ ἐὰν ἅμα βούληται ἢ ἐπιθυμῇ ποιεῖν δύο ἢ τὰ ἐναντία, οὐ ποιήσει· οὐ γὰρ οὕτως ἔχει αὐτῶν τὴν δύναμιν οὐδʼ ἔστι τοῦ ἅμα ποιεῖν ἡ δύναμις, ἐπεὶ ὧν ἐστὶν οὕτως ποιήσει.","kind":"passage"},{"id":"RAW_DAZY_BOO9:P0015","text":"ἐπεὶ δὲ περὶ τῆς κατὰ κίνησιν λεγομένης δυνάμεως εἴρηται, περὶ ἐνεργείας διορίσωμεν τί τέ ἐστιν ἡ ἐνέργεια καὶ ποῖόν τι. καὶ γὰρ τὸ δυνατὸν ἅμα δῆλον ἔσται διαιροῦσιν, ὅτι οὐ μόνον τοῦτο λέγομεν δυνατὸν ὃ πέφυκε κινεῖν ἄλλο ἢ κινεῖσθαι ὑπʼ ἄλλου ἢ ἁπλῶς ἢ τρόπον τινά, ἀλλὰ καὶ ἑτέρως, διὸ ζητοῦντες καὶ περὶ τούτων διήλθομεν. ἔστι δὴ ἐνέργεια τὸ ὑπάρχειν τὸ πρᾶγμα μὴ οὕτως ὥσπερ λέγομεν δυνάμει· λέγομεν δὲ δυνάμει οἷον ἐν τῷ ξύλῳ Ἑρμῆν καὶ ἐν τῇ ὅλῃ τὴν ἡμίσειαν, ὅτι ἀφαιρεθείη ἄν, καὶ ἐπιστήμονα καὶ τὸν μὴ θεωροῦντα, ἂν δυνατὸς ᾖ θεωρῆσαι· τὸ δὲ ἐνεργείᾳ. δῆλον δʼ ἐπὶ τῶν καθʼ ἕκαστα τῇ ἐπαγωγῇ ὃ βουλόμεθα λέγειν, καὶ οὐ δεῖ παντὸς ὅρον ζητεῖν ἀλλὰ καὶ τὸ ἀνάλογον συνορᾶν, ὅτι ὡς τὸ οἰκοδομοῦν πρὸς τὸ οἰκοδομικόν,  καὶ τὸ ἐγρηγορὸς πρὸς τὸ καθεῦδον, καὶ τὸ ὁρῶν πρὸς τὸ μῦον μὲν ὄψιν δὲ ἔχον, καὶ τὸ ἀποκεκριμένον ἐκ τῆς ὕλης πρὸς τὴν ὕλην, καὶ τὸ ἀπειργασμένον πρὸς τὸ ἀνέργαστον.","kind":"passage"},{"id":"RAW_DAZY_BOO9:P0016","text":"ταύτης δὲ τῆς διαφορᾶς θατέρῳ μορίῳ ἔστω ἡ ἐνέργεια ἀφωρισμένη θατέρῳ δὲ τὸ δυνατόν. λέγεται δὲ ἐνεργείᾳ οὐ πάντα ὁμοίως ἀλλʼ ἢ τῷ ἀνάλογον, ὡς τοῦτο ἐν τούτῳ ἢ πρὸς τοῦτο, τόδʼ ἐν τῷδε ἢ πρὸς τόδε· τὰ μὲν γὰρ ὡς κίνησις πρὸς δύναμιν τὰ δʼ ὡς οὐσία πρός τινα ὕλην. ἄλλως δὲ καὶ τὸ ἄπειρον καὶ τὸ κενόν, καὶ ὅσα τοιαῦτα, λέγεται δυνάμει καὶ ἐνεργείᾳ ἢ πολλοῖς τῶν ὄντων, οἷον τῷ ὁρῶντι καὶ βαδίζοντι καὶ ὁρωμένῳ. ταῦτα μὲν γὰρ ἐνδέχεται καὶ ἁπλῶς ἀληθεύεσθαί ποτε (τὸ μὲν γὰρ ὁρώμενον ὅτι ὁρᾶται, τὸ δὲ ὅτι ὁρᾶσθαι δυνατόν)· τὸ δʼ ἄπειρον οὐχ οὕτω δυνάμει ἔστιν ὡς ἐνεργείᾳ ἐσόμενον χωριστόν, ἀλλὰ γνώσει. τὸ γὰρ μὴ ὑπολείπειν τὴν διαίρεσιν ἀποδίδωσι τὸ εἶναι δυνάμει ταύτην τὴν ἐνέργειαν, τὸ δὲ χωρίζεσθαι οὔ.","kind":"passage"},{"id":"RAW_DAZY_BOO9:P0017","text":"ἐπεὶ δὲ τῶν πράξεων ὧν ἔστι πέρας οὐδεμία τέλος ἀλλὰ τῶν περὶ τὸ τέλος, οἷον τὸ ἰσχναίνειν ἢ ἰσχνασία  , αὐτὰ δὲ ὅταν ἰσχναίνῃ οὕτως ἐστὶν ἐν κινήσει, μὴ ὑπάρχοντα ὧν ἕνεκα ἡ κίνησις, οὐκ ἔστι ταῦτα πρᾶξις ἢ οὐ τελεία γε (οὐ γὰρ τέλος)· ἀλλʼ ἐκείνη ᾗ ἐνυπάρχει τὸ τέλος καὶ  πρᾶξις. οἷον ὁρᾷ ἅμα καὶ ἑώρακε, καὶ φρονεῖ καὶ πεφρόνηκε, καὶ νοεῖ καὶ νενόηκεν, ἀλλʼ οὐ μανθάνει καὶ μεμάθηκεν οὐδʼ ὑγιάζεται καὶ ὑγίασται· εὖ ζῇ καὶ εὖ ἔζηκεν ἅμα, καὶ εὐδαιμονεῖ καὶ εὐδαιμόνηκεν. εἰ δὲ μή, ἔδει ἄν ποτε παύεσθαι ὥσπερ ὅταν ἰσχναίνῃ, νῦν δʼ οὔ, ἀλλὰ ζῇ καὶ ἔζηκεν. τούτων δὴ δεῖ τὰς μὲν κινήσεις λέγειν, τὰς δʼ ἐνεργείας. πᾶσα γὰρ κίνησις ἀτελής, ἰσχνασία μάθησις βάδισις οἰκοδόμησις· αὗται δὴ κινήσεις, καὶ ἀτελεῖς γε.","kind":"passage"},{"id":"RAW_DAZY_BOO9:P0018","text":"οὐ γὰρ ἅμα βαδίζει καὶ βεβάδικεν, οὐδʼ οἰκοδομεῖ καὶ ᾠκοδόμηκεν, οὐδὲ γίγνεται καὶ γέγονεν ἢ κινεῖται καὶ κεκίνηται, ἀλλʼ ἕτερον, καὶ κινεῖ καὶ κεκίνηκεν· ἑώρακε δὲ καὶ ὁρᾷ ἅμα τὸ αὐτό, καὶ νοεῖ καὶ νενόηκεν. τὴν μὲν οὖν τοιαύτην ἐνέργειαν λέγω, ἐκείνην δὲ κίνησιν. τὸ μὲν οὖν ἐνεργείᾳ τί τέ ἐστι καὶ ποῖον, ἐκ τούτων καὶ τῶν τοιούτων δῆλον ἡμῖν ἔστω.","kind":"passage"},{"id":"RAW_DAZY_BOO9:P0019","text":"πότε δὲ δυνάμει ἔστιν ἕκαστον καὶ πότε οὔ, διοριστέον· οὐ γὰρ ὁποτεοῦν.  οἷον ἡ γῆ ἆρʼ ἐστὶ δυνάμει ἄνθρωπος; ἢ οὔ, ἀλλὰ μᾶλλον ὅταν ἤδη γένηται σπέρμα, καὶ οὐδὲ τότε ἴσως; ὥσπερ οὖν οὐδʼ ὑπὸ ἰατρικῆς ἅπαν ἂν ὑγιασθείη οὐδʼ ἀπὸ τύχης, ἀλλʼ ἔστι τι ὃ δυνατόν ἐστι, καὶ τοῦτʼ ἔστιν ὑγιαῖνον δυνάμει. ὅρος δὲ τοῦ μὲν ἀπὸ διανοίας ἐντελεχείᾳ γιγνομένου ἐκ τοῦ δυνάμει ὄντος, ὅταν βουληθέντος γίγνηται μηθενὸς κωλύοντος τῶν ἐκτός, ἐκεῖ δʼ ἐν τῷ ὑγιαζομένῳ, ὅταν μηθὲν κωλύῃ τῶν ἐν αὐτῷ· ὁμοίως δὲ δυνάμει καὶ οἰκία· εἰ μηθὲν κωλύει τῶν ἐν τούτῳ καὶ τῇ ὕλῃ τοῦ γίγνεσθαι οἰκίαν, οὐδʼ ἔστιν ὃ δεῖ προσγενέσθαι ἢ ἀπογενέσθαι ἢ μεταβαλεῖν, τοῦτο δυνάμει οἰκία· καὶ ἐπὶ τῶν ἄλλων ὡσαύτως ὅσων ἔξωθεν ἡ ἀρχὴ τῆς γενέσεως.","kind":"passage"},{"id":"RAW_DAZY_BOO9:P0020","text":"καὶ ὅσων δὴ ἐν αὐτῷ τῷ ἔχοντι, ὅσα μηθενὸς τῶν ἔξωθεν ἐμποδίζοντος ἔσται διʼ αὐτοῦ· οἷον τὸ σπέρμα οὔπω (δεῖ γὰρ ἐν ἄλλῳ πεσεῖν καὶ μεταβάλλειν), ὅταν δʼ ἤδη διὰ τῆς αὑτοῦ ἀρχῆς ᾖ τοιοῦτον, ἤδη τοῦτο δυνάμει· ἐκεῖνο δὲ ἑτέρας ἀρχῆς δεῖται, ὥσπερ ἡ γῆ οὔπω ἀνδριὰς δυνάμει (μεταβαλοῦσα γὰρ ἔσται χαλκός). ἔοικε δὲ ὃ λέγομεν εἶναι οὐ τόδε ἀλλʼ ἐκείνινον—οἷον τὸ κιβώτιον οὐ ξύλον ἀλλὰ ξύλινον, οὐδὲ τὸ ξύλον γῆ ἀλλὰ γήϊνον, πάλιν ἡ γῆ εἰ οὕτως μὴ ἄλλο ἀλλὰ ἐκείνινον—ἀεὶ ἐκεῖνο δυνάμει ἁπλῶς τὸ ὕστερόν ἐστιν. οἷον τὸ κιβώτιον οὐ γήϊνον οὐδὲ γῆ ἀλλὰ ξύλινον· τοῦτο γὰρ δυνάμει κιβώτιον καὶ ὕλη κιβωτίου αὕτη, ἁπλῶς μὲν τοῦ ἁπλῶς τουδὶ δὲ τοδὶ τὸ ξύλον. εἰ δέ τί ἐστι πρῶτον ὃ μηκέτι κατʼ ἄλλο λέγεται ἐκείνινον, τοῦτο πρώτη ὕλη· οἷον εἰ ἡ γῆ ἀερίνη, ὁ δʼ ἀὴρ μὴ πῦρ ἀλλὰ πύρινος, τὸ πῦρ ὕλη πρώτη οὐ τόδε τι οὖσα.","kind":"passage"},{"id":"RAW_DAZY_BOO9:P0021","text":"τούτῳ γὰρ διαφέρει τὸ καθʼ οὗ καὶ τὸ ὑποκείμενον, τῷ εἶναι τόδε τι ἢ μὴ εἶναι· οἷον τοῖς πάθεσι τὸ ὑποκείμενον ἄνθρωπος καὶ σῶμα καὶ ψυχή, πάθος δὲ τὸ μουσικὸν καὶ λευκόν (λέγεται δὲ τῆς μουσικῆς ἐγγενομένης ἐκεῖνο οὐ μουσικὴ ἀλλὰ μουσικόν, καὶ οὐ λευκότης ὁ ἄνθρωπος ἀλλὰ λευκόν, οὐδὲ βάδισις ἢ κίνησις ἀλλὰ βαδίζον ἢ κινούμενον, ὡς τὸ ἐκείνινον)·—ὅσα μὲν οὖν οὕτω, τὸ ἔσχατον οὐσία· ὅσα δὲ μὴ οὕτως ἀλλʼ εἶδός τι καὶ τόδε τι τὸ κατηγορούμενον, τὸ ἔσχατον ὕλη καὶ οὐσία ὑλική. καὶ ὀρθῶς δὴ συμβαίνει τὸ ἐκείνινον λέγεσθαι κατὰ τὴν ὕλην καὶ τὰ πάθη·  ἄμφω γὰρ ἀόριστα. πότε μὲν οὖν λεκτέον δυνάμει καὶ πότε οὔ, εἴρηται.","kind":"passage"},{"id":"RAW_DAZY_BOO9:P0022","text":"ἐπεὶ δὲ τὸ πρότερον διώρισται ποσαχῶς λέγεται, φανερὸν ὅτι πρότερον ἐνέργεια δυνάμεώς ἐστιν. λέγω δὲ δυνάμεως οὐ μόνον τῆς ὡρισμένης ἣ λέγεται ἀρχὴ μεταβλητικὴ ἐν ἄλλῳ ἢ ᾗ ἄλλο, ἀλλʼ ὅλως πάσης ἀρχῆς κινητικῆς ἢ στατικῆς. καὶ γὰρ ἡ φύσις ἐν ταὐτῷ  γένει τῇ δυνάμει· ἀρχὴ γὰρ κινητική, ἀλλʼ οὐκ ἐν ἄλλῳ ἀλλʼ ἐν αὐτῷ ᾗ αὐτό.—πάσης δὴ τῆς τοιαύτης προτέρα ἐστὶν ἡ ἐνέργεια καὶ λόγῳ καὶ τῇ οὐσίᾳ· χρόνῳ δʼ ἔστι μὲν ὥς, ἔστι δὲ ὡς οὔ. τῷ λόγῳ μὲν οὖν ὅτι προτέρα, δῆλον (τῷ γὰρ ἐνδέχεσθαι ἐνεργῆσαι δυνατόν ἐστι τὸ πρώτως δυνατόν, οἷον λέγω οἰκοδομικὸν τὸ δυνάμενον οἰκοδομεῖν, καὶ ὁρατικὸν τὸ ὁρᾶν, καὶ ὁρατὸν τὸ δυνατὸν ὁρᾶσθαι· ὁ δʼ αὐτὸς λόγος καὶ ἐπὶ τῶν ἄλλων, ὥστʼ ἀνάγκη τὸν λόγον προϋπάρχειν καὶ τὴν γνῶσιν τῆς γνώσεως)· τῷ δὲ χρόνῳ πρότερον ὧδε· τὸ τῷ εἴδει τὸ αὐτὸ ἐνεργοῦν πρότερον, ἀριθμῷ δʼ οὔ.","kind":"passage"},{"id":"RAW_DAZY_BOO9:P0023","text":"λέγω δὲ τοῦτο ὅτι τοῦδε μὲν τοῦ ἀνθρώπου τοῦ ἤδη ὄντος κατʼ ἐνέργειαν καὶ τοῦ σίτου καὶ τοῦ ὁρῶντος πρότερον τῷ χρόνῳ ἡ ὕλη καὶ τὸ σπέρμα καὶ τὸ ὁρατικόν, ἃ δυνάμει μέν ἐστιν ἄνθρωπος καὶ σῖτος καὶ ὁρῶν, ἐνεργείᾳ δʼ οὔπω· ἀλλὰ τούτων πρότερα τῷ χρόνῳ ἕτερα ὄντα ἐνεργείᾳ ἐξ ὧν ταῦτα ἐγένετο· ἀεὶ γὰρ ἐκ τοῦ δυνάμει ὄντος γίγνεται τὸ ἐνεργείᾳ ὂν ὑπὸ ἐνεργείᾳ ὄντος, οἷον ἄνθρωπος ἐξ ἀνθρώπου, μουσικὸς ὑπὸ μουσικοῦ, ἀεὶ κινοῦντός τινος πρώτου· τὸ δὲ κινοῦν ἐνεργείᾳ ἤδη ἔστιν. εἴρηται δὲ ἐν τοῖς περὶ τῆς οὐσίας λόγοις ὅτι πᾶν τὸ γιγνόμενον γίγνεται ἔκ τινος τι καὶ ὑπό τινος, καὶ τοῦτο τῷ εἴδει τὸ αὐτό. διὸ καὶ δοκεῖ ἀδύνατον εἶναι οἰκοδόμον εἶναι μὴ οἰκοδομήσαντα μηθὲν ἢ κιθαριστὴν μηθὲν κιθαρίσαντα· ὁ γὰρ μανθάνων κιθαρίζειν κιθαρίζων μανθάνει κιθαρίζειν, ὁμοίως δὲ καὶ οἱ ἄλλοι. ὅθεν ὁ σοφιστικὸς ἔλεγχος ἐγίγνετο ὅτι οὐκ ἔχων τις τὴν ἐπιστήμην ποιήσει οὗ ἡ ἐπιστήμη· ὁ γὰρ μανθάνων οὐκ ἔχει.","kind":"passage"},{"id":"RAW_DAZY_BOO9:P0024","text":"ἀλλὰ διὰ τὸ τοῦ γιγνομένου γεγενῆσθαί τι καὶ τοῦ ὅλως κινουμένου κεκινῆσθαί τι (δῆλον δʼ ἐν τοῖς περὶ κινήσεως τοῦτο)  καὶ τὸν μανθάνοντα ἀνάγκη ἔχειν τι τῆς ἐπιστήμης ἴσως. ἀλλʼ οὖν καὶ ταύτῃ γε δῆλον ὅτι ἡ ἐνέργεια καὶ οὕτω προτέρα τῆς δυνάμεως κατὰ γένεσιν καὶ χρόνον. ἀλλὰ μὴν καὶ οὐσίᾳ γε, πρῶτον μὲν ὅτι τὰ τῇ γενέσει ὕστερα τῷ εἴδει καὶ τῇ οὐσίᾳ πρότερα (οἷον ἀνὴρ παιδὸς καὶ ἄνθρωπος σπέρματος· τὸ μὲν γὰρ ἤδη ἔχει τὸ εἶδος τὸ δʼ οὔ), καὶ ὅτι ἅπαν ἐπʼ ἀρχὴν βαδίζει τὸ γιγνόμενον καὶ τέλος (ἀρχὴ γὰρ τὸ οὗ ἕνεκα, τοῦ τέλους δὲ ἕνεκα ἡ γένεσις), τέλος δʼ ἡ ἐνέργεια, καὶ τούτου χάριν ἡ δύναμις λαμβάνεται. οὐ γὰρ ἵνα ὄψιν ἔχωσιν ὁρῶσι τὰ ζῷα ἀλλʼ ὅπως ὁρῶσιν ὄψιν ἔχουσιν, ὁμοίως δὲ καὶ οἰκοδομικὴν ἵνα οἰκοδομῶσι καὶ τὴν θεωρητικὴν ἵνα θεωρῶσιν· ἀλλʼ οὐ θεωροῦσιν ἵνα θεωρητικὴν ἔχωσιν, εἰ μὴ οἱ μελετῶντες· οὗτοι δὲ οὐχὶ θεωροῦσιν ἀλλʼ ἢ ὡδί, †ἢ ὅτι οὐδὲν δέονται θεωρεῖν†.","kind":"passage"},{"id":"RAW_DAZY_BOO9:P0025","text":"ἔτι ἡ ὕλη ἔστι δυνάμει ὅτι ἔλθοι ἂν εἰς τὸ εἶδος· ὅταν δέ γε ἐνεργείᾳ ᾖ, τότε ἐν τῷ εἴδει ἐστίν. ὁμοίως δὲ καὶ ἐπὶ τῶν ἄλλων, καὶ ὧν κίνησις τὸ τέλος, διὸ ὥσπερ οἱ διδάσκοντες ἐνεργοῦντα ἐπιδείξαντες οἴονται τὸ τέλος ἀποδεδωκέναι, καὶ ἡ φύσις ὁμοίως. εἰ γὰρ μὴ οὕτω γίγνεται, ὁ Παύσωνος ἔσται Ἑρμῆς· ἄδηλος γὰρ καὶ ἡ ἐπιστήμη εἰ ἔσω ἢ ἔξω, ὥσπερ κἀκεῖνος. τὸ γὰρ ἔργον τέλος, ἡ δὲ ἐνέργεια τὸ ἔργον, διὸ καὶ τοὔνομα ἐνέργεια λέγεται κατὰ τὸ ἔργον καὶ συντείνει πρὸς τὴν ἐντελέχειαν. ἐπεὶ δʼ ἐστὶ τῶν μὲν ἔσχατον ἡ χρῆσις (οἷον ὄψεως ἡ ὅρασις, καὶ οὐθὲν γίγνεται παρὰ ταύτην ἕτερον ἀπὸ τῆς ὄψεως), ἀπʼ ἐνίων δὲ γίγνεταί τι (οἷον ἀπὸ τῆς οἰκοδομικῆς οἰκία παρὰ τὴν οἰκοδόμησιν), ὅμως οὐθὲν ἧττον ἔνθα μὲν τέλος, ἔνθα δὲ μᾶλλον τέλος τῆς δυνάμεώς ἐστιν· ἡ γὰρ οἰκοδόμησις ἐν τῷ οἰκοδομουμένῳ, καὶ ἅμα γίγνεται καὶ ἔστι τῇ οἰκίᾳ.","kind":"passage"},{"id":"RAW_DAZY_BOO9:P0026","text":"ὅσων μὲν οὖν ἕτερόν τί ἐστι παρὰ τὴν χρῆσιν τὸ γιγνόμενον, τούτων μὲν ἡ ἐνέργεια ἐν τῷ ποιουμένῳ ἐστίν (οἷον ἥ τε οἰκοδόμησις ἐν τῷ οἰκοδομουμένῳ καὶ ἡ ὕφανσις ἐν τῷ ὑφαινομένῳ, ὁμοίως δὲ καὶ ἐπὶ τῶν ἄλλων, καὶ ὅλως ἡ κίνησις ἐν τῷ κινουμένῳ)· ὅσων δὲ μὴ ἔστιν ἄλλο τι ἔργον παρὰ τὴν ἐνέργειαν, ἐν αὐτοῖς ὑπάρχει ἡ ἐνέργεια (οἷον ἡ ὅρασις ἐν τῷ ὁρῶντι καὶ ἡ θεωρία ἐν τῷ θεωροῦντι καὶ ἡ ζωὴ ἐν τῇ ψυχῇ, διὸ καὶ ἡ εὐδαιμονία·  ζωὴ γὰρ ποιά τίς ἐστιν). ὥστε φανερὸν ὅτι ἡ οὐσία καὶ τὸ εἶδος ἐνέργειά ἐστιν. κατά τε δὴ τοῦτον τὸν λόγον φανερὸν ὅτι πρότερον τῇ οὐσίᾳ ἐνέργεια δυνάμεως, καὶ ὥσπερ εἴπομεν, τοῦ χρόνου ἀεὶ προλαμβάνει ἐνέργεια ἑτέρα πρὸ ἑτέρας ἕως τῆς τοῦ ἀεὶ κινοῦντος πρώτως.—ἀλλὰ μὴν καὶ κυριωτέρως· τὰ μὲν γὰρ ἀΐδια πρότερα τῇ οὐσίᾳ τῶν φθαρτῶν, ἔστι δʼ οὐθὲν δυνάμει ἀΐδιον.","kind":"passage"},{"id":"RAW_DAZY_BOO9:P0027","text":"λόγος δὲ ὅδε· πᾶσα δύναμις ἅμα τῆς ἀντιφάσεώς ἐστιν· τὸ μὲν γὰρ μὴ δυνατὸν ὑπάρχειν οὐκ ἂν ὑπάρξειεν οὐθενί, τὸ δυνατὸν δὲ πᾶν ἐνδέχεται μὴ ἐνεργεῖν. τὸ ἄρα δυνατὸν εἶναι ἐνδέχεται καὶ εἶναι καὶ μὴ εἶναι· τὸ αὐτὸ ἄρα δυνατὸν καὶ εἶναι καὶ μὴ εἶναι. τὸ δὲ δυνατὸν μὴ εἶναι ἐνδέχεται μὴ εἶναι· τὸ δὲ ἐνδεχόμενον μὴ εἶναι φθαρτόν, ἢ ἁπλῶς ἢ τοῦτο αὐτὸ ὃ λέγεται ἐνδέχεσθαι μὴ εἶναι, ἢ κατὰ τόπον ἢ κατὰ τὸ ποσὸν ἢ ποιόν· ἁπλῶς δὲ τὸ κατʼ οὐσίαν.","kind":"passage"},{"id":"RAW_DAZY_BOO9:P0028","text":"οὐθὲν ἄρα τῶν ἀφθάρτων ἁπλῶς δυνάμει ἔστιν ἁπλῶς (κατά τι δὲ οὐδὲν κωλύει, οἷον ποιὸν ἢ πού)· ἐνεργείᾳ ἄρα πάντα· οὐδὲ τῶν ἐξ ἀνάγκης ὄντων (καίτοι ταῦτα πρῶτα· εἰ γὰρ ταῦτα μὴ ἦν, οὐθὲν ἂν ἦν)· οὐδὲ δὴ κίνησις, εἴ τίς ἐστιν ἀΐδιος· οὐδʼ εἴ τι κινούμενον ἀΐδιον, οὐκ ἔστι κατὰ δύναμιν κινούμενον ἀλλʼ ἢ ποθὲν ποί (τούτου δʼ ὕλην οὐδὲν κωλύει ὑπάρχειν), διὸ ἀεὶ ἐνεργεῖ ἥλιος καὶ ἄστρα καὶ ὅλος ὁ οὐρανός, καὶ οὐ φοβερὸν μή ποτε στῇ, ὃ φοβοῦνται οἱ περὶ φύσεως. οὐδὲ κάμνει τοῦτο δρῶντα· οὐ γὰρ περὶ τὴν δύναμιν τῆς ἀντιφάσεως αὐτοῖς, οἷον τοῖς φθαρτοῖς, ἡ κίνησις, ὥστε ἐπίπονον εἶναι τὴν συνέχειαν τῆς κινήσεως· ἡ γὰρ οὐσία ὕλη καὶ δύναμις οὖσα, οὐκ ἐνέργεια, αἰτία τούτου. μιμεῖται δὲ τὰ ἄφθαρτα καὶ τὰ ἐν μεταβολῇ ὄντα, οἷον γῆ καὶ πῦρ. καὶ γὰρ ταῦτα ἀεὶ ἐνεργεῖ· καθʼ αὑτὰ γὰρ καὶ ἐν αὑτοῖς ἔχει τὴν κίνησιν.","kind":"passage"},{"id":"RAW_DAZY_BOO9:P0029","text":"αἱ δὲ ἄλλαι δυνάμεις, ἐξ ὧν διώρισται, πᾶσαι τῆς ἀντιφάσεώς εἰσιν· τὸ γὰρ δυνάμενον ὡδὶ κινεῖν δύναται καὶ μὴ ὡδί, ὅσα γε κατὰ λόγον· αἱ δʼ ἄλογοι τῷ παρεῖναι καὶ μὴ τῆς ἀντιφάσεως ἔσονται αἱ αὐταί. εἰ ἄρα τινὲς εἰσὶ φύσεις τοιαῦται ἢ οὐσίαι οἵας λέγουσιν οἱ ἐν τοῖς λόγοις τὰς ἰδέας, πολὺ μᾶλλον ἐπιστῆμον ἄν τι εἴη ἢ αὐτὸ ἐπιστήμη καὶ κινούμενον ἢ κίνησις·  ταῦτα γὰρ ἐνέργειαι μᾶλλον, ἐκεῖναι δὲ δυνάμεις τούτων. ὅτι μὲν οὖν πρότερον ἡ ἐνέργεια καὶ δυνάμεως καὶ πάσης ἀρχῆς μεταβλητικῆς, φανερόν.","kind":"passage"},{"id":"RAW_DAZY_BOO9:P0030","text":"ὅτι δὲ καὶ βελτίων καὶ τιμιωτέρα τῆς σπουδαίας δυνάμεως ἡ ἐνέργεια, ἐκ τῶνδε δῆλον. ὅσα γὰρ κατὰ τὸ δύνασθαι λέγεται, ταὐτόν ἐστι δυνατὸν τἀναντία, οἷον τὸ δύνασθαι λεγόμενον ὑγιαίνειν ταὐτόν ἐστι καὶ τὸ νοσεῖν, καὶ ἅμα· ἡ αὐτὴ γὰρ δύναμις τοῦ ὑγιαίνειν καὶ κάμνειν, καὶ ἠρεμεῖν καὶ κινεῖσθαι, καὶ οἰκοδομεῖν καὶ καταβάλλειν, καὶ οἰκοδομεῖσθαι καὶ καταπίπτειν. τὸ μὲν οὖν δύνασθαι τἀναντία ἅμα ὑπάρχει· τὰ δʼ ἐναντία ἅμα ἀδύνατον, καὶ τὰς ἐνεργείας δὲ ἅμα ἀδύνατον ὑπάρχειν (οἷον ὑγιαίνειν καὶ κάμνειν), ὥστʼ ἀνάγκη τούτων θάτερον εἶναι τἀγαθόν, τὸ δὲ δύνασθαι ὁμοίως ἀμφότερον ἢ οὐδέτερον· ἡ ἄρα ἐνέργεια βελτίων. ἀνάγκη δὲ καὶ ἐπὶ τῶν κακῶν τὸ τέλος καὶ τὴν ἐνέργειαν εἶναι χεῖρον τῆς δυνάμεως· τὸ γὰρ δυνάμενον ταὐτὸ ἄμφω τἀναντία. δῆλον ἄρα ὅτι οὐκ ἔστι τὸ κακὸν παρὰ τὰ πράγματα· ὕστερον γὰρ τῇ φύσει τὸ κακὸν τῆς δυνάμεως.","kind":"passage"},{"id":"RAW_DAZY_BOO9:P0031","text":"οὐκ ἄρα οὐδʼ ἐν τοῖς ἐξ ἀρχῆς καὶ τοῖς ἀϊδίοις οὐθὲν ἔστιν οὔτε κακὸν οὔτε ἁμάρτημα οὔτε διεφθαρμένον (καὶ γὰρ ἡ διαφθορὰ τῶν κακῶν ἐστίν). εὑρίσκεται δὲ καὶ τὰ διαγράμματα ἐνεργείᾳ· διαιροῦντες γὰρ εὑρίσκουσιν. εἰ δʼ ἦν διῃρημένα, φανερὰ ἂν ἦν· νῦν δʼ ἐνυπάρχει δυνάμει. διὰ τί δύο ὀρθαὶ τὸ τρίγωνον; ὅτι αἱ περὶ μίαν στιγμὴν γωνίαι ἴσαι δύο ὀρθαῖς. εἰ οὖν ἀνῆκτο ἡ παρὰ τὴν πλευράν, ἰδόντι ἂν ἦν εὐθὺς δῆλον διὰ τί. ἐν ἡμικυκλίῳ ὀρθὴ καθόλου διὰ τί; ἐὰν ἴσαι τρεῖς, ἥ τε βάσις δύο καὶ ἡ ἐκ μέσου ἐπισταθεῖσα ὀρθή, ἰδόντι δῆλον τῷ ἐκεῖνο εἰδότι. ὥστε φανερὸν ὅτι τὰ δυνάμει ὄντα εἰς ἐνέργειαν ἀγόμενα εὑρίσκεται· αἴτιον δὲ ὅτι ἡ νόησις ἐνέργεια· ὥστʼ ἐξ ἐνεργείας ἡ δύναμις, καὶ διὰ τοῦτο ποιοῦντες γιγνώσκουσιν (ὕστερον γὰρ γενέσει ἡ ἐνέργεια ἡ κατʼ ἀριθμόν).","kind":"passage"},{"id":"RAW_DAZY_BOO9:P0032","text":"ἐπεὶ δὲ τὸ ὂν λέγεται καὶ τὸ μὴ ὂν τὸ μὲν κατὰ τὰ σχήματα τῶν κατηγοριῶν, τὸ δὲ κατὰ δύναμιν ἢ ἐνέργειαν τούτων ἢ τἀναντία,  τὸ δὲ  ἀληθὲς ἢ ψεῦδος, τοῦτο δʼ ἐπὶ τῶν πραγμάτων ἐστὶ τῷ συγκεῖσθαι ἢ διῃρῆσθαι, ὥστε ἀληθεύει μὲν ὁ τὸ διῃρημένον οἰόμενος διῃρῆσθαι καὶ τὸ συγκείμενον συγκεῖσθαι, ἔψευσται δὲ ὁ ἐναντίως ἔχων ἢ τὰ πράγματα, πότʼ ἔστιν ἢ οὐκ ἔστι τὸ ἀληθὲς λεγόμενον ἢ ψεῦδος; τοῦτο γὰρ σκεπτέον τί λέγομεν. οὐ γὰρ διὰ τὸ ἡμᾶς οἴεσθαι ἀληθῶς σε λευκὸν εἶναι εἶ σὺ λευκός, ἀλλὰ διὰ τὸ σὲ εἶναι λευκὸν ἡμεῖς οἱ φάντες τοῦτο ἀληθεύομεν.","kind":"passage"},{"id":"RAW_DAZY_BOO9:P0033","text":"εἰ δὴ τὰ μὲν ἀεὶ σύγκειται καὶ ἀδύνατα διαιρεθῆναι, τὰ δʼ ἀεὶ διῄρηται καὶ ἀδύνατα συντεθῆναι, τὰ δʼ ἐνδέχεται τἀναντία, τὸ μὲν εἶναί ἐστι τὸ συγκεῖσθαι καὶ ἓν εἶναι, τὸ δὲ μὴ εἶναι τὸ μὴ συγκεῖσθαι ἀλλὰ πλείω εἶναι· περὶ μὲν οὖν τὰ ἐνδεχόμενα ἡ αὐτὴ γίγνεται ψευδὴς καὶ ἀληθὴς δόξα καὶ ὁ λόγος ὁ αὐτός, καὶ ἐνδέχεται ὁτὲ μὲν ἀληθεύειν ὁτὲ δὲ ψεύδεσθαι· περὶ δὲ τὰ ἀδύνατα ἄλλως ἔχειν οὐ γίγνεται ὁτὲ μὲν ἀληθὲς ὁτὲ δὲ ψεῦδος, ἀλλʼ ἀεὶ ταὐτὰ ἀληθῆ καὶ ψευδῆ.—περὶ δὲ δὴ τὰ ἀσύνθετα τί τὸ εἶναι ἢ μὴ εἶναι καὶ τὸ ἀληθὲς καὶ τὸ ψεῦδος; οὐ γάρ ἐστι σύνθετον, ὥστε εἶναι μὲν ὅταν συγκέηται, μὴ εἶναι δὲ ἐὰν διῃρημένον ᾖ, ὥσπερ τὸ λευκὸν τὸ ξύλον ἢ τὸ ἀσύμμετρον τὴν διάμετρον· οὐδὲ τὸ ἀληθὲς καὶ τὸ ψεῦδος ὁμοίως ἔτι ὑπάρξει καὶ ἐπʼ ἐκείνων.","kind":"passage"},{"id":"RAW_DAZY_BOO9:P0034","text":"ἢ ὥσπερ οὐδὲ τὸ ἀληθὲς ἐπὶ τούτων τὸ αὐτό, οὕτως οὐδὲ τὸ εἶναι, ἀλλʼ ἔστι τὸ μὲν ἀληθὲς ἢ ψεῦδος, τὸ μὲν θιγεῖν καὶ φάναι ἀληθές (οὐ γὰρ ταὐτὸ κατάφασις καὶ φάσις), τὸ δʼ ἀγνοεῖν μὴ θιγγάνειν (ἀπατηθῆναι γὰρ περὶ τὸ τί ἐστιν οὐκ ἔστιν ἀλλʼ ἢ κατὰ συμβεβηκός· ὁμοίως δὲ καὶ περὶ τὰς μὴ συνθετὰς οὐσίας, οὐ γὰρ ἔστιν ἀπατηθῆναι· καὶ πᾶσαι εἰσὶν ἐνεργείᾳ, οὐ δυνάμει, ἐγίγνοντο γὰρ ἂν καὶ ἐφθείροντο, νῦν δὲ τὸ ὂν αὐτὸ οὐ γίγνεται οὐδὲ φθείρεται, ἔκ τινος γὰρ ἂν ἐγίγνετο·—ὅσα δή ἐστιν ὅπερ εἶναί τι καὶ ἐνέργειαι, περὶ ταῦτα οὐκ ἔστιν ἀπατηθῆναι ἀλλʼ ἢ νοεῖν ἢ μή· ἀλλὰ τὸ τί ἐστι ζητεῖται περὶ αὐτῶν, εἰ τοιαῦτά ἐστιν ἢ μή)· τὸ δὲ εἶναι ὡς τὸ ἀληθές, καὶ τὸ μὴ εἶναι τὸ ὡς τὸ ψεῦδος, ἓν μέν ἐστιν, εἰ σύγκειται, ἀληθές, τὸ δʼ εἰ μὴ σύγκειται, ψεῦδος· τὸ δὲ ἕν, εἴπερ ὄν, οὕτως ἐστίν, εἰ δὲ μὴ οὕτως, οὐκ ἔστιν·  τὸ δὲ ἀληθὲς τὸ νοεῖν ταῦτα· τὸ δὲ ψεῦδος οὐκ ἔστιν, οὐδὲ ἀπάτη, ἀλλὰ ἄγνοια, οὐχ οἵα ἡ","kind":"passage"},{"id":"RAW_DAZY_BOO9:P0035","text":"τυφλότης· ἡ μὲν γὰρ τυφλότης ἐστὶν ὡς ἂν εἰ τὸ νοητικὸν ὅλως μὴ ἔχοι τις. φανερὸν δὲ καὶ ὅτι περὶ τῶν ἀκινήτων οὐκ ἔστιν ἀπάτη κατὰ τὸ ποτέ, εἴ τις ὑπολαμβάνει ἀκίνητα. οἷον τὸ τρίγωνον εἰ μὴ μεταβάλλειν οἴεται, οὐκ οἰήσεται ποτὲ μὲν δύο ὀρθὰς ἔχειν ποτὲ δὲ οὔ (μεταβάλλοι γὰρ ἄν), ἀλλὰ τὶ μὲν τὶ δʼ οὔ, οἷον ἄρτιον ἀριθμὸν πρῶτον εἶναι μηθένα, ἢ τινὰς μὲν τινὰς δʼ οὔ· ἀριθμῷ δὲ περὶ ἕνα οὐδὲ τοῦτο· οὐ γὰρ ἔτι τινὰ μὲν τινὰ δὲ οὒ οἰήσεται, ἀλλʼ ἀληθεύσει ἢ ψεύσεται ὡς ἀεὶ οὕτως ἔχοντος.","kind":"passage"}],"sha256":"89f119b222ccc8c5bcca6cad554eba3339170df3f5f1100b19d662ed1df4fd80","language_code":"grc","language_name":"Ἑλληνική","translations":[{"id":"SOURCE_TR_20261015T173321110974Z_6aba52de","language_code":"en","language_name":"English","title":"Book 9","source_sha256":"89f119b222ccc8c5bcca6cad554eba3339170df3f5f1100b19d662ed1df4fd80","sha256":"3c8e2118677dd00ba4f77f2f1f70804e931ded916d4796004bd06cb15250260c","rendition_type":"machine","review_status":"machine","model":"gpt-5.6-terra","created":"2026-10-15T17:33:21.111003+00:00","paragraphs":[{"id":"RAW_DAZY_BOO9:P0001","text":"Then, concerning what is primarily being and that to which all the other categories of being are referred, it has been said—concerning substance (for the other beings, both quantity and quality and the other things so called, are said in accordance with the account of substance; for all will contain the account of substance, as we said in the first discussions). But since being is said in one way as what-it-is, or quality, or quantity, and in another according to potentiality and entelechy and according to activity, let us distinguish also concerning potentiality and entelechy; and first concerning the potentiality which is spoken of most properly in the primary sense, though it is not the most useful for what we now intend. For potentiality and actuality extend further than those things which are spoken of only in relation to motion. But, having spoken about this, in our distinctions concerning actuality we shall make clear also the other matters.","kind":"passage","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_DAZY_BOO9:P0001"]},{"id":"RAW_DAZY_BOO9:P0002","text":"That potentiality and being able are spoken of in many ways has been distinguished by us elsewhere. Of these, let those potentialities which are spoken of homonymously be set aside (for some are so called by a certain likeness, as in geometry we speak of things as possible and impossible by their somehow being or not being); but those which are directed toward the same form are all certain principles, and are spoken of in reference to one primary principle, which is a principle of change in another thing, or in the same thing insofar as it is another. For the potentiality for being affected is the principle in the affected thing itself of passive change by another thing, or by itself insofar as it is another; while the disposition of immunity from being affected for the worse, and of perishing, is [a principle] by another thing, or by itself insofar as it is another, through a principle capable of change. For in all these definitions there is contained the account of the primary potentiality.","kind":"passage","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_DAZY_BOO9:P0002"]},{"id":"RAW_DAZY_BOO9:P0003","text":"Again, these potentialities are spoken of either in respect of merely doing or being affected, or of doing so well; hence, in the accounts of these too, the accounts of the prior potentialities are somehow present.—It is clear, then, that in one sense the potentiality of acting and of being affected is one (for a thing is capable both because it possesses itself a potentiality for being affected and because another thing is affected by it), but in another sense it is different. For the one is in the thing affected (for, because it has a certain principle, and matter too is a certain principle, the thing affected is affected, one thing by another; thus the oily is combustible, and what yields in this way is crushable, and likewise in the other cases); but the other is in the acting thing, such as heat and the art of building—the former in what heats, the latter in the builder. Hence, insofar as a thing is naturally united with itself, it suffers nothing from itself; for it is one and not another. And incapacity and the impossible are the privation contrary to such potentiality, so that every potentiality is relative to an incapacity of the same thing and in the same respect.","kind":"passage","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_DAZY_BOO9:P0003"]},{"id":"RAW_DAZY_BOO9:P0004","text":"Privation too is spoken of in many ways. For it applies both to what does not have something and to what, though naturally fitted to have it, does not have it—either altogether or when it is naturally fitted to have it—and either in this manner, for example completely, or in whatever manner. In some cases, when things naturally fitted to have something do not have it through force, we say that they have been deprived of it.","kind":"passage","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_DAZY_BOO9:P0004"]},{"id":"RAW_DAZY_BOO9:P0005","text":"But since some such principles are present in non-living things, while others are present in living things and in soul, and in that part of soul which possesses reason, it is clear that of potentialities too some will be non-rational and others accompanied by reason. Hence all arts and productive sciences are potentialities; for they are principles productive of change in another thing, or in the same thing insofar as it is another. And all those accompanied by reason are the same potentialities for contraries, whereas the non-rational ones are each for one thing—for example, the hot is capable only of heating, whereas medicine is capable of disease and health.","kind":"passage","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_DAZY_BOO9:P0005"]},{"id":"RAW_DAZY_BOO9:P0006","text":"The cause is that knowledge is an account, and the same account makes clear both the thing and the privation, though not in the same way; and in one sense it is the account of both, but in another more of what is present. Hence such sciences too must be sciences of contraries, but of the one in themselves and of the other not in themselves; for the account too is of the one in itself, and of the other in a certain manner accidentally, since it makes the contrary clear by negation and removal. For the primary privation is the contrary, and this is the removal of the other. But since contraries do not come to be in the same thing, while knowledge is a potentiality by possessing an account, and soul possesses a principle of motion, the healthy produces health only, the heating produces heat, and the cooling produces cold, but the knowledgeable person produces both.","kind":"passage","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_DAZY_BOO9:P0006"]},{"id":"RAW_DAZY_BOO9:P0007","text":"For the account is of both, though not in the same way, and it is in a soul which possesses a principle of motion; so that, having joined both to the same principle, it will move toward the same thing. Hence things capable according to reason produce contraries to things capable without reason; for they are contained under one principle, the account. It is also clear that the potentiality for doing well is accompanied by the potentiality merely to do or be affected, but the latter is not always accompanied by the former. For one who does well must also act, but one who merely acts need not also act well.","kind":"passage","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_DAZY_BOO9:P0007"]},{"id":"RAW_DAZY_BOO9:P0008","text":"There are some who say, such as the Megarians, that one is capable only when one is active, and when one is not active one is not capable; for example, that one who is not building is unable to build, but the one who is building is able when he is building; and similarly in the other cases. It is not difficult to see the absurd consequences for them. For clearly a person will not be a builder unless he is building (for being a builder is being capable of building), and similarly in the case of the other arts. If, then, it is impossible to possess such arts without ever having learned and acquired them, and impossible not to possess them without ever having lost them (whether through forgetfulness, some affection, or time; certainly not because the object has perished, for it always exists), then, whenever one ceases, one will not possess the art; and how, having acquired it again, will one immediately build? Likewise also with non-living things: nothing will be cold or hot or sweet or in general perceptible unless it is perceived. Thus they will find themselves asserting the doctrine of Protagoras. Yet nothing will possess sensation either, unless it is perceiving and active.","kind":"passage","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_DAZY_BOO9:P0008"]},{"id":"RAW_DAZY_BOO9:P0009","text":"If, then, blind is what does not possess sight, though naturally fitted to possess it, both when it is naturally fitted and while it still exists, the same persons will be blind many times in a day, and deaf as well. Further, if what is deprived of potentiality is impossible, what does not come to be will be impossible to come to be; and whoever says that what is impossible to come to be either is, or will be, will speak falsely (for this is what “impossible” signified). Thus these arguments abolish both motion and coming-to-be. For what is standing will always stand, and what is sitting will remain seated; for it will not rise while it is sitting, since what is unable to rise will be unable to rise. If, then, these things cannot be said, it is clear that potentiality and actuality are different (but those arguments make potentiality and actuality the same, and hence they seek to abolish no small thing); so that something may be capable of being and yet not be, and capable of not being and yet be; and similarly in the other categories, something capable of walking may not walk, and something not walking may be capable of walking.","kind":"passage","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_DAZY_BOO9:P0009"]},{"id":"RAW_DAZY_BOO9:P0010","text":"That is possible for which, if the actuality of which it is said to have the potentiality is present, nothing will be impossible. I mean, for example, if sitting is possible and it is possible for a thing to sit, then if sitting is present in it, nothing will be impossible; and similarly if being moved or moving, standing or causing to stand, being or coming to be, not being or not coming to be, is possible. The name actuality, compounded with entelechy, has come from motions chiefly to the other things as well; for actuality seems chiefly to be motion. Hence they do not attribute being moved to non-beings, though they attribute certain other categories to them—for example, they say that non-beings are thinkable and desirable, but not moved—because, if they were moved, they would be actual non-beings. For some non-beings are potentially; but they are not, because they are not in entelechy.","kind":"passage","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_DAZY_BOO9:P0010"]},{"id":"RAW_DAZY_BOO9:P0011","text":"If what has been said is possible, or follows from it, it is clear that it cannot be true to say, “This is possible, but it will not be”; for thus impossible things escape being impossible. I mean, for example, if someone were to say that it is possible for the diagonal to be measured, but that it will not be measured—without reckoning that it is impossible—on the grounds that nothing prevents something possible from being or coming to be from not being and not being going to be. But from the premises it necessarily follows, if we suppose that there is or has come to be what is not possible but is possible, that nothing will be impossible. Yet that consequence will follow, for being measured is impossible. For falsehood and impossibility are not the same: that you are standing now is false, but not impossible. At the same time it is also clear that, if B must be when A is, then if A is capable of being, B too must be capable of being. For if B need not be capable of being, nothing prevents its not being capable of being. Let A, then, be possible.","kind":"passage","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_DAZY_BOO9:P0011"]},{"id":"RAW_DAZY_BOO9:P0012","text":"Then, when A is capable of being, if A were posited, nothing impossible would result; but B must be. Yet B was impossible. Let it then be impossible. If B is impossible to be, A too must necessarily be impossible. But the first was impossible; therefore the second is so too. Therefore, if A is possible, B too will be possible, if things were so related that, when A is, B must be. If, when A and B are so related, B is not possible in this way, A and B will not be as they were posited. And if, when A is possible, B must be possible, then, if A is, B too must be. For that B necessarily is possible, if A is possible, signifies this: if A is, and when and in the way in which it was possible for it to be, then that too necessarily is then and in that way.","kind":"passage","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_DAZY_BOO9:P0012"]},{"id":"RAW_DAZY_BOO9:P0013","text":"Since all potentialities are either congenital, such as the senses, or acquired by habituation, such as that of playing the flute, or by learning, such as those of the arts, it is necessary to acquire by prior activity those which arise by habituation and by reason, but not those which are not of this kind, nor those concerned with being affected. But since what is capable is capable of something, and at some time, and in some way, and whatever else must be present in the definition; and since some things can move according to reason and their potentialities are accompanied by reason, while others are non-rational and their potentialities non-rational; and since the former must be in a living thing, but the latter may be in both kinds: in the case of potentialities of the latter kind, when the active and passive things approach one another in the way in which they can, the one must act and the other be affected; but this is not necessary in the former case. For all the former are productive each of one thing, whereas the latter are productive of contraries, so that they would produce contraries at the same time; but this is impossible. Therefore something else must be sovereign; I mean desire or choice.","kind":"passage","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_DAZY_BOO9:P0013"]},{"id":"RAW_DAZY_BOO9:P0014","text":"For whichever of the two it primarily desires, this it will do when it is present in the way in which it can be and approaches the passive thing. Hence everything capable according to reason must, when it desires that for which it has the potentiality, and in the way in which it has it, do this. And it has the potentiality when the passive thing is present and is in such a condition; otherwise it will not be able to act. (There is no longer any need to add the qualification “when nothing external prevents it”; for it possesses the potentiality as a potentiality for acting, and it is not so without qualification but when things are in a certain condition, among which conditions the external impediments will be excluded; for these remove some of the conditions present in the definition.) Hence, even if it wishes or desires to do two things or contraries at the same time, it will not do so; for it does not possess the potentiality for them in this way, nor is its potentiality a potentiality for doing them simultaneously, since it will act in the way in which it is a potentiality for them.","kind":"passage","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_DAZY_BOO9:P0014"]},{"id":"RAW_DAZY_BOO9:P0015","text":"Since, then, we have spoken concerning the potentiality spoken of in relation to motion, let us distinguish concerning actuality—what actuality is and what sort of thing it is. For in making the distinction, the possible too will at the same time become clear, because we speak of the possible not only as what is naturally fitted to move another thing or be moved by another, either simply or in some particular way, but also otherwise; which is why, in seeking these matters, we went through those as well. Actuality, then, is the being-present of the thing not in the way in which we say it is potentially. We say potentially, for example, that Hermes is in the wood and half of the whole is in the whole, because it might be removed; and that one who does not contemplate is knowing, if he is capable of contemplating. The actuality is the other. What we wish to say is clear by induction from particular cases, and one should not seek a definition of everything, but also discern the analogy: as what is building is to what is capable of building, and what is awake to what is asleep, and what sees to what has its eyes closed but possesses sight, and what has been separated from matter to matter, and what has been worked out to what is unworked.","kind":"passage","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_DAZY_BOO9:P0015"]},{"id":"RAW_DAZY_BOO9:P0016","text":"Let actuality be marked off by one part of this difference, and the possible by the other. But not everything is said to be actual in the same way, but by analogy: as this is in this or relative to this, so that is in that or relative to that. For some are as motion is to potentiality, others as substance is to some matter. Again, the infinite and the void, and other such things, are said to be potentially and actually in a different way from many beings, such as what sees, walks, and is seen. For of the latter it is possible at some time to speak truly without qualification (what is seen, in one sense because it is being seen, and in another because it can be seen); but the infinite is not potentially so that it will be actual as a separately existing thing, but in knowledge. For the fact that division does not run out supplies its being this actuality potentially, but not its being separated.","kind":"passage","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_DAZY_BOO9:P0016"]},{"id":"RAW_DAZY_BOO9:P0017","text":"Since, among actions which have a limit, none is an end but all are concerned with the end—for example, losing weight or weight-loss—and these themselves, when a thing is losing weight, are in motion in this way, not being that for the sake of which the motion is, these are not an action, or at least not a complete action (for they are not an end). But that in which the end is present is also an action. For example, one sees and has seen at the same time, thinks and has thought, and understands and has understood; but one does not learn and have learned, nor is one being healed and has been healed. One lives well and has lived well at the same time, and is flourishing and has flourished. Otherwise it would at some time have to cease, as when one is losing weight; but now this is not so: one lives and has lived. Of these, then, some must be called motions, others actualities. For every motion is incomplete: weight-loss, learning, walking, building. These, then, are motions, and indeed incomplete ones.","kind":"passage","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_DAZY_BOO9:P0017"]},{"id":"RAW_DAZY_BOO9:P0018","text":"For one does not at the same time walk and have walked, nor build and have built, nor come to be and have come to be, nor be moved and have been moved; rather, these are different, as are moving and having moved. But one has seen and sees the same thing at the same time, and understands and has understood. The latter sort of thing, then, I call actuality, and the former motion. From these and similar cases, let it be clear to us what and what sort of thing being in actuality is.","kind":"passage","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_DAZY_BOO9:P0018"]},{"id":"RAW_DAZY_BOO9:P0019","text":"But when each thing is potentially, and when it is not, must be distinguished; for it is not so at any random time. For example, is earth potentially a human being? Or is it not, but rather when it has already become seed—and perhaps not even then? Just as not everything could be healed either by medicine or by chance, but there is something which is capable, and this is what is potentially healthy. And the definition of what comes to be in entelechy from what is potentially, by thought, is: when, if it is wished, it comes to be with nothing external preventing it; while in the thing being healed, it is when nothing internal prevents it. Similarly, a house is potentially a house: if nothing in the thing or in its matter prevents its becoming a house, and there is nothing which must be added, removed, or changed, this is potentially a house; and similarly with all things whose principle of coming-to-be is external.","kind":"passage","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_DAZY_BOO9:P0019"]},{"id":"RAW_DAZY_BOO9:P0020","text":"And in the case of things whose principle is in the thing possessing it itself, whatever will be through itself when nothing external prevents it: for example, seed is not yet [potentially such] (for it must fall into another thing and change); but when it is already such through its own principle, then it is already potentially such. But the former requires another principle, just as earth is not yet potentially a statue (for when changed it will be bronze). What we call a thing seems not to be this, but to be made of this—for example, a box is not wood but wooden, nor is wood earth but earthy; and again, if earth in this way is not another thing but made of that other thing—then that other thing is always, without qualification, potentially the later thing. For example, a box is not earthy or earth but wooden; for this is potentially a box and this is the matter of a box—the matter, without qualification, of a box without qualification, but in this particular case the wood of this particular box. But if there is some first thing which is no longer said to be made of another thing, this is first matter; for example, if earth is airy, but air is not fire but fiery, fire is first matter, not being a this-something.","kind":"passage","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_DAZY_BOO9:P0020"]},{"id":"RAW_DAZY_BOO9:P0021","text":"For this is the difference between that of which something is predicated and the underlying subject: being a this-something or not being so. For example, the underlying subjects for affections are a human being, body, and soul, while the affection is the musical or white. (When musicality has come to be in it, that thing is called not “musicality” but “musical”; and the human being is not called whiteness but white, nor walking or motion, but walking or being moved, as the thing derived from those.)—In cases thus constituted, then, the ultimate subject is substance; but where this is not so, and what is predicated is some form and a this-something, the ultimate subject is matter and material substance. And it rightly follows that the thing derived from it is spoken of in accordance with matter, as are also the affections; for both are indeterminate. When, then, one should speak of something as being potentially, and when not, has been stated.","kind":"passage","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_DAZY_BOO9:P0021"]},{"id":"RAW_DAZY_BOO9:P0022","text":"Since the ways in which “prior” is spoken of have been distinguished, it is clear that actuality is prior to potentiality. And by potentiality I mean not only the determinate potentiality which is called a principle of change in another thing or insofar as it is another, but in general every principle of motion or rest. For nature too belongs to the same genus as potentiality; for it is a principle of motion, though not in another thing but in the thing itself insofar as it is itself.—Actuality, then, is prior to every such potentiality both in account and in substance; in time it is prior in one way, but not in another. That it is prior in account is clear: for what is primarily potential is potential by being capable of being actual—for example, I call “capable of building” that which can build, “capable of seeing” that which can see, and “visible” that which can be seen; and the same account applies to the other cases, so that the account and the knowledge must pre-exist the knowledge [of the thing]. In time it is prior in this way: what is actual and the same in form is prior, though not what is the same in number.","kind":"passage","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_DAZY_BOO9:P0022"]},{"id":"RAW_DAZY_BOO9:P0023","text":"What I mean is this: before this particular human being, already actual, and before the grain and the seeing thing, there are in time matter, seed, and the capacity for sight, which are potentially a human being, grain, and a seeing thing, but are not yet so actually. But before these there are other things, actual in time, from which these came to be; for what is actual always comes to be from what is potential by what is actual—for example, a human being from a human being, a musician by a musician—there always being something first that moves. But the mover is already actual. And it has been stated in the discussions concerning substance that everything which comes to be comes to be from something, as something, and by something, this being the same in form. Hence it also seems impossible to be a builder without having built anything, or a cithara-player without having played the cithara at all; for one who is learning to play the cithara learns to play it by playing it, and likewise in the other cases. Whence there arose the sophistical refutation that someone will do that of which he has knowledge without possessing the knowledge; for the learner does not possess it.","kind":"passage","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_DAZY_BOO9:P0023"]},{"id":"RAW_DAZY_BOO9:P0024","text":"But because something of what is coming to be has come to be, and something of what is in motion generally has been moved (this is clear in the discussions on motion), the learner too must perhaps possess some part of the knowledge. Yet even in this way it is clear that actuality is prior to potentiality in coming-to-be and in time as well. But indeed it is prior in substance too: first, because things posterior in coming-to-be are prior in form and substance—for example, a man is prior to a child and a human being to seed; for the one already has the form, while the other does not—and because everything that comes to be proceeds toward a principle and an end (for the principle is that for the sake of which, and coming-to-be is for the sake of the end), and actuality is the end, and it is for the sake of this that potentiality is acquired. For animals do not see in order that they may have sight, but have sight in order that they may see; likewise they have the art of building in order that they may build, and the theoretical capacity in order that they may contemplate. But they do not contemplate in order that they may have the theoretical capacity, except those who are practising; and these do not contemplate, except in this way, †or because they have no need to contemplate†.","kind":"passage","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_DAZY_BOO9:P0024"]},{"id":"RAW_DAZY_BOO9:P0025","text":"Further, matter is potentially, because it may come into form; but when it is actually, then it is in the form. Likewise also in the other cases, including those whose end is motion. Hence, just as teachers think they have rendered the end when they have displayed someone in activity, so too does nature. For if it does not come about in this way, there will be a Hermes of Pauson; for whether knowledge is inward or outward is unclear, just as that figure is. For the work is the end, and actuality is the work; hence the name actuality is spoken in relation to work and tends toward complete actuality. Since in some cases use is the ultimate thing—for example, sight in the case of vision, and nothing else comes to be from sight besides this—whereas from some things something is produced—for example, from the art of building a house is produced besides the building—nevertheless, in the former case it is an end, and in the latter it is more an end of the potentiality. For building is in what is being built, and comes to be and exists simultaneously with the house.","kind":"passage","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_DAZY_BOO9:P0025"]},{"id":"RAW_DAZY_BOO9:P0026","text":"In cases, then, where what comes to be is something other than the use, actuality is in the thing being made—for example, building in what is being built and weaving in what is being woven, and likewise in the other cases, and motion generally in what is moved. But where there is no other work besides the actuality, actuality is present in the things themselves—for example, seeing is in the one who sees, contemplation in the one who contemplates, and life in the soul; hence happiness too, for it is a certain kind of life. Thus it is clear that substance and form are actuality. By this account, then, it is clear that actuality is prior in substance to potentiality; and, as we said, one actuality always precedes another in time, up to that of what first moves eternally.—But indeed this is so in a more authoritative sense as well: for eternal things are prior in substance to perishable things, and nothing eternal exists potentially.","kind":"passage","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_DAZY_BOO9:P0026"]},{"id":"RAW_DAZY_BOO9:P0027","text":"The argument is this. Every potentiality is at the same time a potentiality for the contradiction. For what is not capable of being present could not be present in anything, whereas everything capable is capable of not being actual. Therefore, what is capable of being may both be and not be; the same thing, then, is capable both of being and of not being. But what is capable of not being may not be; and what may not be is perishable, either without qualification or in that very respect in which it is said to be capable of not being—whether in place, quantity, or quality; and without qualification, in substance.","kind":"passage","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_DAZY_BOO9:P0027"]},{"id":"RAW_DAZY_BOO9:P0028","text":"Therefore, nothing imperishable exists potentially without qualification, though nothing prevents it from doing so in some respect, such as in quality or place; therefore all such things are actual. Nor does anything that exists of necessity exist potentially (though these are primary; for if they did not exist, nothing would exist). Nor does any eternal motion; nor, if anything moved is eternal, is it moved potentially, except from somewhere to somewhere (and nothing prevents there being matter for this). Hence the sun and the stars and the whole heaven are always active, and there is no cause to fear that they may ever stand still, as those concerned with nature fear. Nor do they tire in performing this activity; for their motion does not concern the potentiality for contradiction, as does that of perishable things, so that the continuity of their motion should be laborious. For substance that is matter and potentiality, not actuality, is the cause of this. Things in change, such as earth and fire, imitate the imperishable things. For these too are always active, since they have motion in themselves and through themselves.","kind":"passage","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_DAZY_BOO9:P0028"]},{"id":"RAW_DAZY_BOO9:P0029","text":"But all the other potentialities, according to the distinctions made, are potentialities for contradiction. For that which can move in this way can also move not in this way, at least in cases according to reason; and the non-rational potentialities will be the same potentialities for contradiction by being present and not being present. If, then, there are certain natures or substances such as those which the proponents of Forms say the Ideas are, something much more knowing than knowledge itself, and more moved than motion, would exist; for these are actualities more than those, whereas the latter are potentialities for these. That actuality is prior both to potentiality and to every principle of change is therefore clear.","kind":"passage","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_DAZY_BOO9:P0029"]},{"id":"RAW_DAZY_BOO9:P0030","text":"That actuality is also better and more honorable than good potentiality is clear from the following. For in all cases spoken of in terms of capacity, the same thing is capable of opposites: for example, what is said to be capable of being healthy is also capable of being sick, and at the same time; for the same potentiality is for being healthy and being ill, for being at rest and being moved, for building and tearing down, and for being built and falling down. Thus, the capacity for opposites belongs simultaneously; but it is impossible for opposites to belong simultaneously, and it is also impossible for the actualities to belong simultaneously—for example, being healthy and being ill. Hence one of these must be good, while the capacity is alike for both or for neither; therefore actuality is better. And in the case of bad things too, the end and actuality must be worse than the potentiality; for what is capable is the same for both opposites. It is clear, then, that evil does not exist apart from things; for evil is posterior by nature to potentiality.","kind":"passage","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_DAZY_BOO9:P0030"]},{"id":"RAW_DAZY_BOO9:P0031","text":"Therefore neither in things that are from a principle nor in eternal things is there anything bad, erroneous, or corrupted (for corruption too is among bad things). Geometrical figures too are discovered in actuality; for it is by dividing that they discover them. If they had been divided, they would be evident; but as it is, they are present potentially. Why does a triangle have two right angles? Because the angles around one point are equal to two right angles. If, then, the line alongside the side were drawn upward, it would be immediately evident to one who saw why. Why is the angle in a semicircle universally right? If the three are equal—the base’s two and the right angle set up from the middle—it is evident to one who sees, if he knows that. Thus it is clear that things which are potentially are discovered when brought into actuality; and the cause is that thinking is actuality. Hence potentiality comes from actuality, and for this reason they know by doing (for actuality in number is later in coming-to-be).","kind":"passage","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_DAZY_BOO9:P0031"]},{"id":"RAW_DAZY_BOO9:P0032","text":"Since being and not-being are spoken of, in one way according to the figures of predication, in another according to potentiality or actuality of these or their opposites, and in another way as truth or falsity—and this, in the case of things, consists in their being combined or divided—so that one speaks truly who judges what is divided to be divided and what is combined to be combined, while one is false whose judgment is contrary to the things: when is what is called true or false, or not? For this is what must be considered: what do we mean? For you are not white because we truly judge you to be white; rather, because you are white, we who affirm this speak truly.","kind":"passage","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_DAZY_BOO9:P0032"]},{"id":"RAW_DAZY_BOO9:P0033","text":"If, then, some things are always combined and incapable of being divided, others are always divided and incapable of being combined, while others admit of opposites, being is combination and being one, while not-being is not being combined but being many. In the case of contingent things, then, the same belief and the same statement become false and true, and it is possible at one time to speak truly and at another falsely. But in the case of things incapable of being otherwise, it does not become at one time true and at another false; rather, the same things are always true or false.—But in the case of incomposites, what are being or not-being, and truth or falsity? For it is not composite, so as to be when it is combined and not to be when it is divided, like wood being white or the diagonal being incommensurable. Nor will truth and falsity any longer belong to those things in the same way.","kind":"passage","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_DAZY_BOO9:P0033"]},{"id":"RAW_DAZY_BOO9:P0034","text":"Or rather, just as truth is not the same in their case, so neither is being; but truth or falsity is as follows: truth is touching and affirming (for affirmation and assertion are not the same), while ignorance is not touching. For it is not possible to be deceived as to what a thing is, except incidentally; and likewise concerning non-composite substances, for it is not possible to be deceived about them. And all of them are actual, not potential; for otherwise they would come to be and perish, whereas being itself neither comes to be nor perishes, since it would come to be from something.—In the case, then, of all things that are what it is to be something and are actualities, it is not possible to be deceived; one either thinks them or does not. But concerning them it is investigated what they are, whether they are of such a kind or not. Being as truth, and not-being as falsity, is, in one case, if things are combined, truth, and if they are not combined, falsity; but the one, if indeed it is, is thus, and if not thus, it is not. And truth is thinking these things; but falsity does not exist, nor deception, but ignorance—not ignorance such as","kind":"passage","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_DAZY_BOO9:P0034"]},{"id":"RAW_DAZY_BOO9:P0035","text":"blindness; for blindness would be as if one did not possess the faculty of thinking at all. It is also clear that concerning immovable things there is no deception with respect to when, if one supposes them immovable. For example, if one thinks that a triangle does not change, one will not think that at one time it has two right angles and at another it does not—for it would then change—but that some have this property and others do not; for example, that no even number is prime, or that some are and some are not. But with respect to one thing in number, not even this is possible; for one will no longer think that some have the property and others do not, but will speak truly or falsely of what always has the same state.","kind":"passage","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_DAZY_BOO9:P0035"]}],"source_digest":"5a1c2f60e8a6e0a2c210ebd816e1426def223b1e2e083b4e7600597f514e3afd","source_language_code":"grc","original_title":"Book 9"}],"passage_basis":"frozen-grounding","original_title":"Book 9","title_language_code":"en"},{"id":"RAW_DAZY_BO_2","title":"Book 10","order":10,"chapter_id":"CH_DAZY_BO_2","text":"τὸ ἓν ὅτι μὲν λέγεται πολλαχῶς, ἐν τοῖς περὶ τοῦ ποσαχῶς διῃρημένοις εἴρηται πρότερον· πλεοναχῶς δὲ λεγομένου οἱ συγκεφαλαιούμενοι τρόποι εἰσὶ τέτταρες τῶν πρώτων καὶ καθʼ αὑτὰ λεγομένων ἓν ἀλλὰ μὴ κατὰ συμβεβηκός. τό τε γὰρ συνεχὲς ἢ ἁπλῶς ἢ μάλιστά γε τὸ φύσει καὶ μὴ ἁφῇ μηδὲ δεσμῷ (καὶ τούτων μᾶλλον ἓν καὶ πρότερον οὗ ἀδιαιρετωτέρα ἡ κίνησις καὶ μᾶλλον ἁπλῆ)· ἔτι τοιοῦτον καὶ μᾶλλον τὸ ὅλον καὶ ἔχον τινὰ μορφὴν καὶ εἶδος, μάλιστα δʼ εἴ τι φύσει τοιοῦτον καὶ μὴ βίᾳ, ὥσπερ ὅσα κόλλῃ ἢ γόμφῳ ἢ συνδέσμῳ, ἀλλὰ ἔχει ἐν αὑτῷ τὸ αἴτιον αὐτῷ τοῦ συνεχὲς εἶναι. τοιοῦτον δὲ τῷ μίαν τὴν κίνησιν εἶναι καὶ ἀδιαίρετον τόπῳ καὶ χρόνῳ, ὥστε φανερόν, εἴ τι φύσει κινήσεως ἀρχὴν ἔχει τῆς πρώτης τὴν πρώτην, οἷον λέγω φορᾶς κυκλοφορίαν, ὅτι τοῦτο πρῶτον μέγεθος ἕν. τὰ μὲν δὴ οὕτως ἓν ᾗ συνεχὲς ἢ ὅλον, τὰ δὲ ὧν ἂν ὁ λόγος  εἷς ᾖ, τοιαῦτα δὲ ὧν ἡ νόησις μία, τοιαῦτα δὲ ὧν ἀδιαίρετος, ἀδιαίρετος δὲ τοῦ ἀδιαιρέτου εἴδει ἢ ἀριθμῷ· ἀριθμῷ μὲν οὖν τὸ καθʼ ἕκαστον ἀδιαίρετον, εἴδει δὲ τὸ τῷ γνωστῷ καὶ τῇ ἐπιστήμῃ, ὥσθʼ ἓν ἂν εἴη πρῶτον τὸ ταῖς οὐσίαις αἴτιον τοῦ ἑνός. λέγεται μὲν οὖν τὸ ἓν τοσαυταχῶς, τό τε συνεχὲς φύσει καὶ τὸ ὅλον, καὶ τὸ καθʼ ἕκαστον καὶ τὸ καθόλου, πάντα δὲ ταῦτα ἓν τῷ ἀδιαίρετον εἶναι τῶν μὲν τὴν κίνησιν τῶν δὲ τὴν νόησιν ἢ τὸν λόγον.  —δεῖ δὲ κατανοεῖν ὅτι οὐχ ὡσαύτως ληπτέον λέγεσθαι ποῖά τε ἓν λέγεται, καὶ τί ἐστι τὸ ἑνὶ εἶναι καὶ τίς αὐτοῦ λόγος. λέγεται μὲν γὰρ τὸ ἓν τοσαυταχῶς, καὶ ἕκαστον ἔσται ἓν τούτων, ᾧ ἂν ὑπάρχῃ τις τούτων τῶν τρόπων· τὸ δὲ ἑνὶ εἶναι ὁτὲ μὲν τούτων τινὶ ἔσται, ὁτὲ δὲ ἄλλῳ ὃ καὶ μᾶλλον ἐγγὺς τῷ ὀνόματί ἐστι, τῇ δυνάμει δʼ ἐκεῖνα, ὥσπερ καὶ περὶ στοιχείου καὶ αἰτίου εἰ δέοι λέγειν ἐπί τε τοῖς πράγμασι διορίζοντα καὶ τοῦ ὀνόματος ὅρον ἀποδιδόντα. ἔστι μὲν γὰρ ὡς στοιχεῖον τὸ πῦρ (ἔστι δʼ ἴσως καθʼ αὑτὸ καὶ τὸ ἄπειρον ἤ τι ἄλλο τοιοῦτον), ἔστι δʼ ὡς οὔ· οὐ γὰρ τὸ αὐτὸ πυρὶ καὶ στοιχείῳ εἶναι, ἀλλʼ ὡς μὲν πρᾶγμά τι καὶ φύσις τὸ πῦρ στοιχεῖον, τὸ δὲ ὄνομα σημαίνει τὸ τοδὶ συμβεβηκέναι αὐτῷ, ὅτι ἐστί τι ἐκ τούτου ὡς πρώτου ἐνυπάρχοντος. οὕτω καὶ ἐπὶ αἰτίου καὶ ἑνὸς καὶ τῶν τοιούτων ἁπάντων, διὸ καὶ τὸ ἑνὶ εἶναι τὸ ἀδιαιρέτῳ ἐστὶν εἶναι, ὅπερ τόδε ὄντι καὶ ἰδίᾳ χωριστῷ ἢ τόπῳ ἢ εἴδει ἢ διανοίᾳ, ἢ καὶ τὸ ὅλῳ καὶ ἀδιαιρέτῳ, μάλιστα δὲ τὸ μέτρῳ εἶναι πρώτῳ ἑκάστου γένους καὶ κυριώτατα τοῦ ποσοῦ· ἐντεῦθεν γὰρ ἐπὶ τὰ ἄλλα ἐλήλυθεν. μέτρον γάρ ἐστιν ᾧ τὸ ποσὸν γιγνώσκεται· γιγνώσκεται δὲ ἢ ἑνὶ ἢ ἀριθμῷ τὸ ποσὸν ᾗ ποσόν, ὁ δὲ ἀριθμὸς ἅπας ἑνί, ὥστε πᾶν τὸ ποσὸν γιγνώσκεται ᾗ ποσὸν τῷ ἑνί, καὶ ᾧ πρώτῳ ποσὰ γιγνώσκεται, τοῦτο αὐτὸ ἕν· διὸ τὸ ἓν ἀριθμοῦ ἀρχὴ ᾗ ἀριθμός. ἐντεῦθεν δὲ καὶ ἐν τοῖς ἄλλοις λέγεται μέτρον τε ᾧ ἕκαστον πρώτῳ γιγνώσκεται, καὶ τὸ μέτρον ἑκάστου ἕν, ἐν μήκει, ἐν πλάτει, ἐν βάθει, ἐν βάρει, ἐν τάχει (τὸ γὰρ βάρος καὶ τάχος κοινὸν ἐν τοῖς ἐναντίοις· διττὸν γὰρ ἑκάτερον αὐτῶν, οἷον βάρος τό τε ὁποσηνοῦν ἔχον ῥοπὴν καὶ τὸ ἔχον ὑπεροχὴν ῥοπῆς, καὶ τάχος τό τε ὁποσηνοῦν κίνησιν ἔχον καὶ τὸ ὑπεροχὴν κινήσεως· ἔστι γάρ τι τάχος καὶ τοῦ βραδέος καὶ βάρος τοῦ κουφοτέρου). ἐν πᾶσι δὴ τούτοις μέτρον καὶ ἀρχὴ ἕν τι καὶ ἀδιαίρετον, ἐπεὶ καὶ ἐν ταῖς γραμμαῖς χρῶνται ὡς ἀτόμῳ τῇ ποδιαίᾳ. πανταχοῦ γὰρ τὸ μέτρον ἕν τι ζητοῦσι καὶ ἀδιαίρετον· τοῦτο δὲ τὸ ἁπλοῦν ἢ τῷ ποιῷ ἢ τῷ ποσῷ. ὅπου μὲν οὖν δοκεῖ μὴ εἶναι ἀφελεῖν ἢ προσθεῖναι, τοῦτο ἀκριβὲς τὸ μέτρον (διὸ τὸ τοῦ ἀριθμοῦ ἀκριβέστατον·  τὴν γὰρ μονάδα τιθέασι πάντῃ ἀδιαίρετον)· ἐν δὲ τοῖς ἄλλοις μιμοῦνται τὸ τοιοῦτον· ἀπὸ γὰρ σταδίου καὶ ταλάντου καὶ ἀεὶ τοῦ μείζονος λάθοι ἂν καὶ προστεθέν τι καὶ ἀφαιρεθὲν μᾶλλον ἢ ἀπὸ ἐλάττονος· ὥστε ἀφʼ οὗ πρώτου κατὰ τὴν αἴσθησιν μὴ ἐνδέχεται, τοῦτο πάντες ποιοῦνται μέτρον καὶ ὑγρῶν καὶ ξηρῶν καὶ βάρους καὶ μεγέθους· καὶ τότʼ οἴονται εἰδέναι τὸ ποσόν, ὅταν εἰδῶσι διὰ τούτου τοῦ μέτρου. καὶ δὴ καὶ κίνησιν τῇ ἁπλῇ κινήσει καὶ τῇ ταχίστῃ (ὀλίγιστον γὰρ αὕτη ἔχει χρόνον)· διὸ ἐν τῇ ἀστρολογίᾳ τὸ τοιοῦτον ἓν ἀρχὴ καὶ μέτρον (τὴν κίνησιν γὰρ ὁμαλὴν ὑποτίθενται καὶ ταχίστην τὴν τοῦ οὐρανοῦ, πρὸς ἣν κρίνουσι τὰς ἄλλας), καὶ ἐν μουσικῇ δίεσις, ὅτι ἐλάχιστον, καὶ ἐν φωνῇ στοιχεῖον. καὶ ταῦτα πάντα ἕν τι οὕτως, οὐχ ὡς κοινόν τι τὸ ἓν ἀλλʼ ὥσπερ εἴρηται.—οὐκ ἀεὶ δὲ τῷ ἀριθμῷ ἓν τὸ μέτρον ἀλλʼ ἐνίοτε πλείω, οἷον αἱ διέσεις δύο, αἱ μὴ κατὰ τὴν ἀκοὴν ἀλλʼ ἐν τοῖς λόγοις, καὶ αἱ φωναὶ πλείους αἷς μετροῦμεν, καὶ ἡ διάμετρος δυσὶ μετρεῖται καὶ ἡ πλευρά, καὶ τὰ μεγέθη πάντα. οὕτω δὴ πάντων μέτρον τὸ ἕν, ὅτι γνωρίζομεν ἐξ ὧν ἐστὶν ἡ οὐσία διαιροῦντες ἢ κατὰ τὸ ποσὸν ἢ κατὰ τὸ εἶδος. καὶ διὰ τοῦτο τὸ ἓν ἀδιαίρετον, ὅτι τὸ πρῶτον ἑκάστων ἀδιαίρετον. οὐχ ὁμοίως δὲ πᾶν ἀδιαίρετον, οἷον ποὺς καὶ μονάς, ἀλλὰ τὸ μὲν πάντῃ, τὸ δʼ εἰς ἀδιαίρετα πρὸς τὴν αἴσθησιν θετέον, ὥσπερ εἴρηται ἤδη· ἴσως γὰρ πᾶν συνεχὲς διαιρετόν. ἀεὶ δὲ συγγενὲς τὸ μέτρον· μεγεθῶν μὲν γὰρ μέγεθος, καὶ καθʼ ἕκαστον μήκους μῆκος, πλάτους πλάτος, φωνῆς φωνή, βάρους βάρος, μονάδων μονάς. οὕτω γὰρ δεῖ λαμβάνειν, ἀλλʼ οὐχ ὅτι ἀριθμῶν ἀριθμός· καίτοι ἔδει, εἰ ὁμοίως· ἀλλʼ οὐχ ὁμοίως ἀξιοῖ ἀλλʼ ὥσπερ εἰ μονάδων μονάδας ἀξιώσειε μέτρον ἀλλὰ μὴ μονάδα· ὁ δʼ ἀριθμὸς πλῆθος μονάδων. καὶ τὴν ἐπιστήμην δὲ μέτρον τῶν πραγμάτων λέγομεν καὶ τὴν αἴσθησιν διὰ τὸ αὐτό, ὅτι γνωρίζομέν τι αὐταῖς, ἐπεὶ μετροῦνται μᾶλλον ἢ μετροῦσιν. ἀλλὰ συμβαίνει ἡμῖν ὥσπερ ἂν εἰ ἄλλου ἡμᾶς μετροῦντος ἐγνωρίσαμεν πηλίκοι ἐσμὲν τῷ τὸν πῆχυν ἐπὶ τοσοῦτον ἡμῶν ἐπιβάλλειν. Πρωταγόρας δʼ ἄνθρωπόν φησι πάντων εἶναι μέτρον, ὥσπερ ἂν εἰ τὸν ἐπιστήμονα εἰπὼν ἢ τὸν αἰσθανόμενον·  τούτους δʼ ὅτι ἔχουσιν ὁ μὲν αἴσθησιν ὁ δὲ ἐπιστήμην, ἅ φαμεν εἶναι μέτρα τῶν ὑποκειμένων. οὐθὲν δὴ λέγοντες περιττὸν φαίνονταί τι λέγειν. ὅτι μὲν οὖν τὸ ἑνὶ εἶναι μάλιστά ἐστι κατὰ τὸ ὄνομα ἀφορίζοντι μέτρον τι, καὶ κυριώτατα τοῦ ποσοῦ, εἶτα τοῦ ποιοῦ, φανερόν· ἔσται δὲ τοιοῦτον τὸ μὲν ἂν ᾖ ἀδιαίρετον κατὰ τὸ ποσόν, τὸ δὲ ἂν κατὰ τὸ ποιόν· διόπερ ἀδιαίρετον τὸ ἓν ἢ ἁπλῶς ἢ ᾗ ἕν.\n\n\n\n \n\n \n\nκατὰ δὲ τὴν οὐσίαν καὶ τὴν φύσιν ζητητέον ποτέρως ἔχει, καθάπερ ἐν τοῖς διαπορήμασιν ἐπήλθομεν τί τὸ ἕν ἐστι καὶ πῶς δεῖ περὶ αὐτοῦ λαβεῖν, πότερον ὡς οὐσίας τινὸς οὔσης αὐτοῦ τοῦ ἑνός, καθάπερ οἵ τε Πυθαγόρειοί φασι πρότερον καὶ Πλάτων ὕστερον, ἢ μᾶλλον ὑπόκειταί τις φύσις καὶ  δεῖ γνωριμωτέρως λεχθῆναι καὶ μᾶλλον ὥσπερ οἱ περὶ φύσεως· ἐκείνων γὰρ ὁ μέν τις φιλίαν εἶναί φησι τὸ ἓν ὁ δʼ ἀέρα ὁ δὲ τὸ ἄπειρον. εἰ δὴ μηδὲν τῶν καθόλου δυνατὸν οὐσίαν εἶναι, καθάπερ ἐν τοῖς περὶ οὐσίας καὶ περὶ τοῦ ὄντος εἴρηται λόγοις, οὐδʼ αὐτὸ τοῦτο οὐσίαν ὡς ἕν τι παρὰ τὰ πολλὰ δυνατὸν εἶναι (κοινὸν γάρ) ἀλλʼ ἢ κατηγόρημα μόνον, δῆλον ὡς οὐδὲ τὸ ἕν· τὸ γὰρ ὂν καὶ τὸ ἓν καθόλου κατηγορεῖται μάλιστα πάντων. ὥστε οὔτε τὰ γένη φύσεις τινὲς καὶ οὐσίαι χωρισταὶ τῶν ἄλλων εἰσίν, οὔτε τὸ ἓν γένος ἐνδέχεται εἶναι διὰ τὰς αὐτὰς αἰτίας διʼ ἅσπερ οὐδὲ τὸ ὂν οὐδὲ τὴν οὐσίαν. ἔτι δʼ ὁμοίως ἐπὶ πάντων ἀναγκαῖον ἔχειν· λέγεται δʼ ἰσαχῶς τὸ ὂν καὶ τὸ ἕν· ὥστʼ ἐπείπερ ἐν τοῖς ποιοῖς ἐστί τι τὸ ἓν καί τις φύσις, ὁμοίως δὲ καὶ ἐν τοῖς ποσοῖς, δῆλον ὅτι καὶ ὅλως ζητητέον τί τὸ ἕν, ὥσπερ καὶ τί τὸ ὄν, ὡς οὐχ ἱκανὸν ὅτι τοῦτο αὐτὸ ἡ φύσις αὐτοῦ. ἀλλὰ μὴν ἔν γε χρώμασίν ἐστι τὸ ἓν χρῶμα, οἷον τὸ λευκόν, εἶτα τὰ ἄλλα ἐκ τούτου καὶ τοῦ μέλανος φαίνεται γιγνόμενα, τὸ δὲ μέλαν στέρησις λευκοῦ ὥσπερ καὶ φωτὸς σκότος · ὥστε εἰ τὰ ὄντα ἦν χρώματα, ἦν ἂν ἀριθμός τις τὰ ὄντα, ἀλλὰ τίνων; δῆλον δὴ ὅτι χρωμάτων, καὶ τὸ ἓν ἦν ἄν τι ἕν, οἷον τὸ λευκόν. ὁμοίως δὲ καὶ εἰ μέλη τὰ ὄντα ἦν, ἀριθμὸς ἂν ἦν, διέσεων μέντοι, ἀλλʼ οὐκ ἀριθμὸς ἡ οὐσία αὐτῶν· καὶ τὸ ἓν ἦν ἄν τι οὗ ἡ οὐσία οὐ τὸ ἓν ἀλλὰ δίεσις.  ὁμοίως δὲ καὶ ἐπὶ τῶν φθόγγων στοιχείων ἂν ἦν τὰ ὄντα ἀριθμός, καὶ τὸ ἓν στοιχεῖον φωνῆεν. καὶ εἰ σχήματα εὐθύγραμμα, σχημάτων ἂν ἦν ἀριθμός, καὶ τὸ ἓν τὸ τρίγωνον. ὁ δʼ αὐτὸς λόγος καὶ ἐπὶ τῶν ἄλλων γενῶν, ὥστʼ εἴπερ καὶ ἐν τοῖς πάθεσι καὶ ἐν τοῖς ποιοῖς καὶ ἐν τοῖς ποσοῖς καὶ ἐν κινήσει ἀριθμῶν ὄντων καὶ ἑνός τινος ἐν ἅπασιν ὅ τε ἀριθμὸς τινῶν καὶ τὸ ἓν τὶ ἕν, ἀλλʼ οὐχὶ τοῦτο αὐτὸ ἡ οὐσία, καὶ ἐπὶ τῶν οὐσιῶν ἀνάγκη ὡσαύτως ἔχειν· ὁμοίως γὰρ ἔχει ἐπὶ πάντων.—ὅτι μὲν οὖν τὸ ἓν ἐν ἅπαντι γένει ἐστί τις φύσις, καὶ οὐδενὸς τοῦτό γʼ αὐτὸ ἡ φύσις τὸ ἕν, φανερόν, ἀλλʼ ὥσπερ ἐν χρώμασι χρῶμα ἓν ζητητέον αὐτὸ τὸ ἕν, οὕτω καὶ ἐν οὐσίᾳ οὐσίαν μίαν αὐτὸ τὸ ἕν· ὅτι δὲ ταὐτὸ σημαίνει πως τὸ ἓν καὶ τὸ ὄν, δῆλον τῷ τε παρακολουθεῖν ἰσαχῶς ταῖς κατηγορίαις καὶ μὴ εἶναι ἐν μηδεμιᾷ (οἷον οὔτʼ ἐν τῇ τί ἐστιν οὔτʼ ἐν τῇ ποῖον, ἀλλʼ ὁμοίως ἔχει ὥσπερ τὸ ὄν) καὶ τῷ μὴ προσκατηγορεῖσθαι ἕτερόν τι τὸ εἷς ἄνθρωπος τοῦ ἄνθρωπος (ὥσπερ οὐδὲ τὸ εἶναι παρὰ τὸ τί ἢ ποῖον ἢ πόσον) καὶ τῷ εἶναι τὸ ἑνὶ εἶναι τὸ ἑκάστῳ εἶναι.\n\n\n\n \n\n \n\nἀντίκειται δὲ τὸ ἓν καὶ τὰ πολλὰ κατὰ πλείους τρόπους, ὧν ἕνα τὸ ἓν καὶ τὸ πλῆθος ὡς ἀδιαίρετον καὶ διαιρετόν· τὸ μὲν γὰρ ἢ διῃρημένον ἢ διαιρετὸν πλῆθός τι λέγεται, τὸ δὲ ἀδιαίρετον ἢ μὴ διῃρημένον ἕν. ἐπεὶ οὖν αἱ ἀντιθέσεις τετραχῶς, καὶ τούτων κατὰ στέρησιν λέγεται θάτερον, ἐναντία ἂν εἴη καὶ οὔτε ὡς ἀντίφασις οὔτε ὡς τὰ πρός τι λεγόμενα. λέγεται δὲ ἐκ τοῦ ἐναντίου καὶ δηλοῦται τὸ ἕν, ἐκ τοῦ διαιρετοῦ τὸ ἀδιαίρετον, διὰ τὸ μᾶλλον αἰσθητὸν τὸ πλῆθος εἶναι καὶ τὸ διαιρετὸν ἢ τὸ ἀδιαίρετον, ὥστε τῷ λόγῳ πρότερον τὸ πλῆθος τοῦ ἀδιαιρέτου διὰ τὴν αἴσθησιν. ἔστι δὲ τοῦ μὲν ἑνός, ὥσπερ καὶ ἐν τῇ διαιρέσει τῶν ἐναντίων διεγράψαμεν, τὸ ταὐτὸ καὶ ὅμοιον καὶ ἴσον, τοῦ δὲ πλήθους τὸ ἕτερον καὶ ἀνόμοιον καὶ ἄνισον. λεγομένου δὲ τοῦ ταὐτοῦ πολλαχῶς, ἕνα μὲν τρόπον κατʼ ἀριθμὸν λέγομεν ἐνίοτε αὐτό, τὸ δʼ ἐὰν καὶ λόγῳ καὶ ἀριθμῷ ἓν ᾖ, οἷον σὺ σαυτῷ καὶ τῷ εἴδει καὶ τῇ ὕλῃ ἕν· ἔτι δʼ ἐὰν ὁ λόγος ὁ τῆς πρώτης οὐσίας εἷς ᾖ,  οἷον αἱ ἴσαι γραμμαὶ εὐθεῖαι αἱ αὐταί, καὶ τὰ ἴσα καὶ ἰσογώνια τετράγωνα, καίτοι πλείω· ἀλλʼ ἐν τούτοις ἡ ἰσότης ἑνότης. ὅμοια δὲ ἐὰν μὴ ταὐτὰ ἁπλῶς ὄντα, μηδὲ κατὰ τὴν οὐσίαν ἀδιάφορα τὴν συγκειμένην, κατὰ τὸ εἶδος ταὐτὰ ᾖ, ὥσπερ τὸ μεῖζον τετράγωνον τῷ μικρῷ ὅμοιον, καὶ αἱ ἄνισοι εὐθεῖαι· αὗται γὰρ ὅμοιαι μέν, αἱ αὐταὶ δὲ ἁπλῶς οὔ. τὰ δὲ ἐὰν τὸ αὐτὸ εἶδος ἔχοντα, ἐν οἷς τὸ μᾶλλον καὶ ἧττον ἐγγίγνεται, μήτε μᾶλλον ᾖ μήτε ἧττον. τὰ δὲ ἐὰν ᾖ τὸ αὐτὸ πάθος καὶ ἓν τῷ εἴδει, οἷον τὸ λευκόν, σφόδρα καὶ ἧττον, ὅμοιά φασιν εἶναι ὅτι ἓν τὸ εἶδος αὐτῶν. τὰ δὲ ἐὰν πλείω ἔχῃ ταὐτὰ ἢ ἕτερα, ἢ ἁπλῶς ἢ τὰ πρόχειρα, οἷον καττίτερος ἀργύρῳ ᾗ λευκόν, χρυσὸς δὲ πυρὶ ᾗ ξανθὸν καὶ πυρρόν. ὥστε δῆλον ὅτι καὶ τὸ ἕτερον καὶ τὸ ἀνόμοιον πολλαχῶς λέγεται. καὶ τὸ μὲν ἄλλο ἀντικειμένως καὶ τὸ ταὐτό, διὸ ἅπαν πρὸς ἅπαν ἢ ταὐτὸ ἢ ἄλλο· τὸ δʼ ἐὰν μὴ καὶ ἡ ὕλη καὶ ὁ λόγος εἷς, διὸ σὺ καὶ ὁ πλησίον ἕτερος· τὸ δὲ τρίτον ὡς τὰ ἐν τοῖς μαθηματικοῖς. τὸ μὲν οὖν ἕτερον ἢ ταὐτὸ διὰ τοῦτο πᾶν πρὸς πᾶν λέγεται, ὅσα λέγεται ἓν καὶ ὄν· οὐ γὰρ ἀντίφασίς ἐστι τοῦ ταὐτοῦ, διὸ οὐ λέγεται ἐπὶ τῶν μὴ ὄντων (τὸ δὲ μὴ ταὐτὸ λέγεται), ἐπὶ δὲ τῶν ὄντων πάντων· ἢ γὰρ ἓν ἢ οὐχ ἓν πέφυχʼ ὅσα ὂν καὶ ἕν. τὸ μὲν οὖν ἕτερον καὶ ταὐτὸν οὕτως ἀντίκειται, διαφορὰ δὲ καὶ ἑτερότης ἄλλο. τὸ μὲν γὰρ ἕτερον καὶ οὗ ἕτερον οὐκ ἀνάγκη εἶναι τινὶ ἕτερον· πᾶν γὰρ ἢ ἕτερον ἢ ταὐτὸ ὅ τι ἂν ᾖ ὄν· τὸ δὲ διάφορον τινὸς τινὶ διάφορον, ὥστε ἀνάγκη ταὐτό τι εἶναι ᾧ διαφέρουσιν. τοῦτο δὲ τὸ ταὐτὸ γένος ἢ εἶδος· πᾶν γὰρ τὸ διαφέρον διαφέρει ἢ γένει ἢ εἴδει, γένει μὲν ὧν μὴ ἔστι κοινὴ ἡ ὕλη μηδὲ γένεσις εἰς ἄλληλα, οἷον ὅσων ἄλλο σχῆμα τῆς κατηγορίας, εἴδει δὲ ὧν τὸ αὐτὸ γένος (λέγεται δὲ γένος ὃ ἄμφω τὸ αὐτὸ λέγονται κατὰ τὴν οὐσίαν τὰ διάφορα). τὰ δʼ ἐναντία διάφορα, καὶ ἡ ἐναντίωσις διαφορά τις. ὅτι δὲ καλῶς τοῦτο ὑποτιθέμεθα, δῆλον ἐκ τῆς ἐπαγωγῆς· πάντα γὰρ διαφέροντα φαίνεται καὶ ταῦτα, οὐ μόνον ἕτερα ὄντα ἀλλὰ τὰ μὲν τὸ γένος ἕτερα τὰ δʼ ἐν τῇ αὐτῇ συστοιχίᾳ τῆς κατηγορίας,  ὥστʼ ἐν ταὐτῷ γένει καὶ ταὐτὰ τῷ γένει. διώρισται δʼ ἐν ἄλλοις ποῖα τῷ γένει ταὐτὰ ἢ ἕτερα. \n\n\n\n \n\n \n\nἐπεὶ δὲ διαφέρειν ἐνδέχεται ἀλλήλων τὰ διαφέροντα πλεῖον καὶ ἔλαττον, ἔστι τις καὶ μεγίστη διαφορά, καὶ ταύτην λέγω ἐναντίωσιν. ὅτι δʼ ἡ μεγίστη ἐστὶ διαφορά, δῆλον ἐκ τῆς ἐπαγωγῆς. τὰ μὲν γὰρ γένει διαφέροντα οὐκ ἔχει ὁδὸν εἰς ἄλληλα, ἀλλʼ ἀπέχει πλέον καὶ ἀσύμβλητα· τοῖς δʼ εἴδει διαφέρουσιν αἱ γενέσεις ἐκ τῶν ἐναντίων εἰσὶν ὡς ἐσχάτων, τὸ δὲ τῶν ἐσχάτων διάστημα μέγιστον, ὥστε καὶ τὸ τῶν ἐναντίων. ἀλλὰ μὴν τό γε μέγιστον ἐν ἑκάστῳ γένει τέλειον. μέγιστόν τε γὰρ οὗ μὴ ἔστιν ὑπερβολή, καὶ τέλειον οὗ μὴ ἔστιν ἔξω λαβεῖν τι δυνατόν· τέλος γὰρ ἔχει ἡ τελεία διαφορά (ὥσπερ καὶ τἆλλα τῷ τέλος ἔχειν λέγεται τέλεια), τοῦ δὲ τέλους οὐθὲν ἔξω· ἔσχατον γὰρ ἐν παντὶ καὶ περιέχει, διὸ οὐδὲν ἔξω τοῦ τέλους, οὐδὲ προσδεῖται οὐδενὸς τὸ τέλειον. ὅτι μὲν οὖν ἡ ἐναντιότης ἐστὶ διαφορὰ τέλειος, ἐκ τούτων δῆλον· πολλαχῶς δὲ λεγομένων τῶν ἐναντίων, ἀκολουθήσει τὸ τελείως οὕτως ὡς ἂν καὶ τὸ ἐναντίοις εἶναι ὑπάρχῃ αὐτοῖς. τούτων δὲ ὄντων φανερὸν ὅτι οὐκ ἐνδέχεται ἑνὶ πλείω ἐναντία εἶναι (οὔτε γὰρ τοῦ ἐσχάτου ἐσχατώτερον εἴη ἄν τι, οὔτε τοῦ ἑνὸς διαστήματος πλείω δυοῖν ἔσχατα), ὅλως τε εἰ ἔστιν ἡ ἐναντιότης διαφορά, ἡ δὲ διαφορὰ δυοῖν, ὥστε καὶ ἡ τέλειος. ἀνάγκη δὲ καὶ τοὺς ἄλλους ὅρους ἀληθεῖς εἶναι τῶν ἐναντίων. καὶ γὰρ πλεῖστον διαφέρει ἡ τέλειος διαφορά (τῶν τε γὰρ γένει διαφερόντων οὐκ ἔστιν ἐξωτέρω λαβεῖν καὶ τῶν εἴδει· δέδεικται γὰρ ὅτι πρὸς τὰ ἔξω τοῦ γένους οὐκ ἔστι διαφορά, τούτων δʼ αὕτη μεγίστη), καὶ τὰ ἐν ταὐτῷ γένει πλεῖστον διαφέροντα ἐναντία (μεγίστη γὰρ διαφορὰ τούτων ἡ τέλειος), καὶ τὰ ἐν τῷ αὐτῷ δεκτικῷ πλεῖστον διαφέροντα ἐναντία (ἡ γὰρ ὕλη ἡ αὐτὴ τοῖς ἐναντίοις) καὶ τὰ ὑπὸ τὴν αὐτὴν δύναμιν πλεῖστον διαφέροντα (καὶ γὰρ ἡ ἐπιστήμη περὶ ἓν γένος ἡ μία)· ἐν οἷς ἡ τελεία διαφορὰ μεγίστη.—πρώτη δὲ ἐναντίωσις ἕξις καὶ στέρησίς ἐστιν· οὐ πᾶσα δὲ στέρησις (πολλαχῶς γὰρ λέγεται ἡ στέρησις) ἀλλʼ ἥτις ἂν τελεία ᾖ. τὰ δʼ ἄλλα ἐναντία κατὰ ταῦτα λεχθήσεται, τὰ μὲν τῷ ἔχειν τὰ δὲ τῷ ποιεῖν ἢ ποιητικὰ εἶναι τὰ δὲ τῷ λήψεις εἶναι καὶ ἀποβολαὶ τούτων ἢ ἄλλων ἐναντίων. εἰ δὴ ἀντίκειται μὲν ἀντίφασις καὶ στέρησις καὶ ἐναντιότης καὶ τὰ πρός τι,  τούτων δὲ πρῶτον ἀντίφασις, ἀντιφάσεως δὲ μηδέν ἐστι μεταξύ, τῶν δὲ ἐναντίων ἐνδέχεται, ὅτι μὲν οὐ ταὐτὸν ἀντίφασις καὶ τἀναντία δῆλον· ἡ δὲ στέρησις ἀντίφασίς τίς ἐστιν· ἢ γὰρ τὸ ἀδύνατον ὅλως ἔχειν, ἢ ὃ ἂν πεφυκὸς ἔχειν μὴ ἔχῃ, ἐστέρηται ἢ ὅλως ἢ πὼς ἀφορισθέν (πολλαχῶς γὰρ ἤδη τοῦτο λέγομεν, ὥσπερ διῄρηται ἡμῖν ἐν ἄλλοις), ὥστʼ ἐστὶν ἡ στέρησις ἀντίφασίς τις ἢ ἀδυναμία διορισθεῖσα ἢ συνειλημμένη τῷ δεκτικῷ· διὸ ἀντιφάσεως μὲν οὐκ ἔστι μεταξύ, στερήσεως δέ τινος ἔστιν· ἴσον μὲν γὰρ ἢ οὐκ ἴσον πᾶν, ἴσον δʼ ἢ ἄνισον οὐ πᾶν, ἀλλʼ εἴπερ, μόνον ἐν τῷ δεκτικῷ τοῦ ἴσου. εἰ δὴ αἱ γενέσεις τῇ ὕλῃ ἐκ τῶν ἐναντίων, γίγνονται δὲ ἢ ἐκ τοῦ εἴδους καὶ τῆς τοῦ εἴδους ἕξεως ἢ ἐκ στερήσεώς τινος τοῦ εἴδους καὶ τῆς μορφῆς, δῆλον ὅτι ἡ μὲν ἐναντίωσις στέρησις ἂν εἴη πᾶσα, ἡ δὲ στέρησις ἴσως οὐ πᾶσα ἐναντιότης (αἴτιον δʼ ὅτι πολλαχῶς ἐνδέχεται ἐστερῆσθαι τὸ ἐστερημένον)· ἐξ ὧν γὰρ αἱ μεταβολαὶ ἐσχάτων, ἐναντία ταῦτα. φανερὸν δὲ καὶ διὰ τῆς ἐπαγωγῆς. πᾶσα γὰρ ἐναντίωσις ἔχει στέρησιν θάτερον τῶν ἐναντίων, ἀλλʼ οὐχ ὁμοίως πάντα· ἀνισότης μὲν γὰρ ἰσότητος ἀνομοιότης δὲ ὁμοιότητος κακία δὲ ἀρετῆς, διαφέρει δὲ ὥσπερ εἴρηται· τὸ μὲν γὰρ ἐὰν μόνον ᾖ ἐστερημένον, τὸ δʼ ἐὰν ἢ ποτὲ ἢ ἔν τινι, οἷον ἂν ἐν ἡλικίᾳ τινὶ ἢ τῷ κυρίῳ, ἢ πάντῃ· διὸ τῶν μὲν ἔστι μεταξύ, καὶ ἔστιν οὔτε ἀγαθὸς ἄνθρωπος οὔτε κακός, τῶν δὲ οὐκ ἔστιν, ἀλλʼ ἀνάγκη εἶναι ἢ περιττὸν ἢ ἄρτιον. ἔτι τὰ μὲν ἔχει τὸ ὑποκείμενον ὡρισμένον, τὰ δʼ οὔ. ὥστε φανερὸν ὅτι ἀεὶ θάτερον τῶν ἐναντίων λέγεται κατὰ στέρησιν· ἀπόχρη δὲ κἂν τὰ πρῶτα καὶ τὰ γένη τῶν ἐναντίων, οἷον τὸ ἓν καὶ τὰ πολλά· τὰ γὰρ ἄλλα εἰς ταῦτα ἀνάγεται.\n\n\n\n \n\n \n\nἐπεὶ δὲ ἓν ἑνὶ ἐναντίον, ἀπορήσειεν ἄν τις πῶς ἀντίκειται τὸ ἓν καὶ τὰ πολλά, καὶ τὸ ἴσον τῷ μεγάλῳ καὶ τῷ μικρῷ. εἰ γὰρ τὸ πότερον ἀεὶ ἐν ἀντιθέσει λέγομεν, οἷον πότερον λευκὸν ἢ μέλαν, καὶ πότερον λευκὸν ἢ οὐ λευκόν (πότερον δὲ ἄνθρωπος ἢ λευκὸν οὐ λέγομεν, ἐὰν μὴ ἐξ ὑποθέσεως καὶ ζητοῦντες οἷον πότερον ἦλθε Κλέων ἢ Σωκράτης—ἀλλʼ οὐκ ἀνάγκη ἐν οὐδενὶ γένει τοῦτο· ἀλλὰ καὶ τοῦτο ἐκεῖθεν ἐλήλυθεν· τὰ γὰρ ἀντικείμενα μόνα οὐκ ἐνδέχεται ἅμα ὑπάρχειν, ᾧ καὶ ἐνταῦθα χρῆται ἐν τῷ πότερος ἦλθεν·  εἰ γὰρ ἅμα ἐνεδέχετο, γελοῖον τὸ ἐρώτημα· εἰ δέ, καὶ οὕτως ὁμοίως ἐμπίπτει εἰς ἀντίθεσιν, εἰς τὸ ἓν ἢ πολλά, οἷον πότερον ἀμφότεροι ἦλθον ἢ ἅτερος)·—εἰ δὴ ἐν τοῖς ἀντικειμένοις ἀεὶ τοῦ ποτέρου ἡ ζήτησις, λέγεται δὲ πότερον μεῖζον ἢ ἔλαττον ἢ ἴσον, τίς ἐστιν ἡ ἀντίθεσις πρὸς ταῦτα τοῦ ἴσου; οὔτε γὰρ θατέρῳ μόνῳ ἐναντίον οὔτʼ ἀμφοῖν· τί γὰρ μᾶλλον τῷ μείζονι ἢ τῷ ἐλάττονι; ἔτι τῷ ἀνίσῳ ἐναντίον τὸ ἴσον, ὥστε πλείοσιν ἔσται ἢ ἑνί. εἰ δὲ τὸ ἄνισον σημαίνει τὸ αὐτὸ ἅμα ἀμφοῖν, εἴη μὲν ἂν ἀντικείμενον ἀμφοῖν (καὶ ἡ ἀπορία βοηθεῖ τοῖς φάσκουσι τὸ ἄνισον δυάδα εἶναι), ἀλλὰ συμβαίνει ἓν δυοῖν ἐναντίον· ὅπερ ἀδύνατον. ἔτι τὸ μὲν ἴσον μεταξὺ φαίνεται μεγάλου καὶ μικροῦ, ἐναντίωσις δὲ μεταξὺ οὐδεμία οὔτε φαίνεται οὔτε ἐκ τοῦ ὁρισμοῦ δυνατόν· οὐ γὰρ ἂν εἴη τελεία μεταξύ τινος οὖσα, ἀλλὰ μᾶλλον ἔχει ἀεὶ ἑαυτῆς τι μεταξύ. λείπεται δὴ ἢ ὡς ἀπόφασιν ἀντικεῖσθαι ἢ ὡς στέρησιν. θατέρου μὲν δὴ οὐκ ἐνδέχεται (τί γὰρ μᾶλλον τοῦ μεγάλου ἢ μικροῦ;)· ἀμφοῖν ἄρα ἀπόφασις στερητική, διὸ καὶ πρὸς ἀμφότερα τὸ πότερον λέγεται, πρὸς δὲ θάτερον οὔ (οἷον πότερον μεῖζον ἢ ἴσον, ἢ πότερον ἴσον ἢ ἔλαττον), ἀλλʼ ἀεὶ τρία. οὐ στέρησις δὲ ἐξ ἀνάγκης· οὐ γὰρ πᾶν ἴσον ὃ μὴ μεῖζον ἢ ἔλαττον, ἀλλʼ ἐν οἷς πέφυκεν ἐκεῖνα.—ἔστι δὴ τὸ ἴσον τὸ μήτε μέγα μήτε μικρόν, πεφυκὸς δὲ ἢ μέγα ἢ μικρὸν εἶναι· καὶ ἀντίκειται ἀμφοῖν ὡς ἀπόφασις στερητική, διὸ καὶ μεταξύ ἐστιν. καὶ τὸ μήτε ἀγαθὸν μήτε κακὸν ἀντίκειται ἀμφοῖν, ἀλλʼ ἀνώνυμον· πολλαχῶς γὰρ λέγεται ἑκάτερον καὶ οὐκ ἔστιν ἓν τὸ δεκτικόν, ἀλλὰ μᾶλλον τὸ μήτε λευκὸν μήτε μέλαν. ἓν δὲ οὐδὲ τοῦτο λέγεται, ἀλλʼ ὡρισμένα πως ἐφʼ ὧν λέγεται στερητικῶς ἡ ἀπόφασις αὕτη· ἀνάγκη γὰρ ἢ φαιὸν ἢ ὠχρὸν εἶναι ἢ τοιοῦτόν τι ἄλλο. ὥστε οὐκ ὀρθῶς ἐπιτιμῶσιν οἱ νομίζοντες ὁμοίως λέγεσθαι πάντα, ὥστε ἔσεσθαι ὑποδήματος καὶ χειρὸς μεταξὺ τὸ μήτε ὑπόδημα μήτε χεῖρα, ἔπειπερ καὶ τὸ μήτε ἀγαθὸν μήτε κακὸν τοῦ ἀγαθοῦ καὶ τοῦ κακοῦ, ὡς πάντων ἐσομένου τινὸς μεταξύ. οὐκ ἀνάγκη δὲ τοῦτο συμβαίνειν. ἡ μὲν γὰρ ἀντικειμένων συναπόφασίς ἐστιν ὧν ἔστι μεταξύ τι καὶ διάστημά τι πέφυκεν εἶναι·  τῶν δʼ οὐκ ἔστι διαφορά· ἐν ἄλλῳ γὰρ γένει ὧν αἱ συναποφάσεις, ὥστʼ οὐχ ἓν τὸ ὑποκείμενον.\n\n\n\n \n\n \n\nὁμοίως δὲ καὶ περὶ τοῦ ἑνὸς καὶ τῶν πολλῶν ἀπορήσειεν ἄν τις. εἰ γὰρ τὰ πολλὰ τῷ ἑνὶ ἁπλῶς ἀντίκειται, συμβαίνει ἔνια ἀδύνατα. τὸ γὰρ ἓν ὀλίγον ἢ ὀλίγα ἔσται· τὰ γὰρ πολλὰ καὶ τοῖς ὀλίγοις ἀντίκειται. ἔτι τὰ δύο πολλά, εἴπερ τὸ διπλάσιον πολλαπλάσιον λέγεται δὲ κατὰ τὰ δύο· ὥστε τὸ ἓν ὀλίγον· πρὸς τί γὰρ πολλὰ τὰ δύο εἰ μὴ πρὸς ἕν τε καὶ τὸ ὀλίγον; οὐθὲν γάρ ἐστιν ἔλαττον. ἔτι εἰ ὡς ἐν μήκει τὸ μακρὸν καὶ βραχύ, οὕτως ἐν πλήθει τὸ πολὺ καὶ ὀλίγον, καὶ ὃ ἂν ᾖ πολὺ καὶ πολλά, καὶ τὰ πολλὰ πολύ (εἰ μή τι ἄρα διαφέρει ἐν συνεχεῖ εὐορίστῳ), τὸ ὀλίγον πλῆθός τι ἔσται. ὥστε τὸ ἓν πλῆθός τι, εἴπερ καὶ ὀλίγον· τοῦτο δʼ ἀνάγκη, εἰ τὰ δύο πολλά. ἀλλʼ ἴσως τὰ πολλὰ λέγεται μέν πως καὶ  πολύ, ἀλλʼ ὡς διαφέρον, οἷον ὕδωρ πολύ, πολλὰ δʼ οὔ. ἀλλʼ ὅσα διαιρετά, ἐν τούτοις λέγεται, ἕνα μὲν τρόπον ἐὰν ᾖ πλῆθος ἔχον ὑπεροχὴν ἢ ἁπλῶς ἢ πρός τι (καὶ τὸ ὀλίγον ὡσαύτως πλῆθος ἔχον ἔλλειψιν), τὸ δὲ ὡς ἀριθμός, ὃ καὶ ἀντίκειται τῷ ἑνὶ μόνον. οὕτως γὰρ λέγομεν ἓν ἢ πολλά, ὥσπερ εἴ τις εἴποι ἓν καὶ ἕνα ἢ λευκὸν καὶ λευκά, καὶ τὰ μεμετρημένα πρὸς τὸ μέτρον · οὕτως καὶ τὰ πολλαπλάσια λέγεται· πολλὰ γὰρ ἕκαστος ὁ ἀριθμὸς ὅτι ἕνα καὶ ὅτι μετρητὸς ἑνὶ ἕκαστος, καὶ ὡς τὸ ἀντικείμενον τῷ ἑνί, οὐ τῷ ὀλίγῳ. οὕτω μὲν οὖν ἐστὶ πολλὰ καὶ τὰ δύο, ὡς δὲ πλῆθος ἔχον ὑπεροχὴν ἢ πρός τι ἢ ἁπλῶς οὐκ ἔστιν, ἀλλὰ πρῶτον. ὀλίγα δʼ ἁπλῶς τὰ δύο· πλῆθος γάρ ἐστιν ἔλλειψιν ἔχον πρῶτον (διὸ καὶ οὐκ ὀρθῶς ἀπέστη Ἀναξαγόρας εἰπὼν ὅτι ὁμοῦ πάντα χρήματα ἦν ἄπειρα καὶ πλήθει καὶ μικρότητι, ἔδει δʼ εἰπεῖν ἀντὶ τοῦ καὶ μικρότητι καὶ ὀλιγότητι· οὐ γὰρ ἄπειρα), ἐπεὶ τὸ ὀλίγον οὐ διὰ τὸ ἕν, ὥσπερ τινές φασιν, ἀλλὰ διὰ τὰ δύο.—ἀντίκειται δὴ τὸ ἓν καὶ τὰ πολλὰ τὰ ἐν ἀριθμοῖς ὡς μέτρον μετρητῷ· ταῦτα δὲ ὡς τὰ πρός τι, ὅσα μὴ καθʼ αὑτὰ τῶν πρός τι. διῄρηται δʼ ἡμῖν ἐν ἄλλοις ὅτι διχῶς λέγεται τὰ πρός τι, τὰ μὲν ὡς ἐναντία, τὰ δʼ ὡς ἐπιστήμη πρὸς ἐπιστητόν, τῷ λέγεσθαί τι ἄλλο πρὸς αὐτό.  τὸ δὲ ἓν ἔλαττον εἶναι τινός, οἷον τοῖν δυοῖν, οὐδὲν κωλύει· οὐ γάρ, εἰ ἔλαττον, καὶ ὀλίγον. τὸ δὲ πλῆθος οἷον γένος ἐστὶ τοῦ ἀριθμοῦ· ἔστι γὰρ ἀριθμὸς πλῆθος ἑνὶ μετρητόν, καὶ ἀντίκειταί πως τὸ ἓν καὶ ἀριθμός, οὐχ ὡς ἐναντίον ἀλλʼ ὥσπερ εἴρηται τῶν πρός τι ἔνια· ᾗ γὰρ μέτρον τὸ δὲ μετρητόν, ταύτῃ ἀντίκειται, διὸ οὐ πᾶν ὃ ἂν ᾖ ἓν ἀριθμός ἐστιν, οἷον εἴ τι ἀδιαίρετόν ἐστιν. ὁμοίως δὲ λεγομένη ἡ ἐπιστήμη πρὸς τὸ ἐπιστητὸν οὐχ ὁμοίως ἀποδίδωσιν. δόξειε μὲν γὰρ ἂν μέτρον ἡ ἐπιστήμη εἶναι τὸ δὲ ἐπιστητὸν τὸ μετρούμενον, συμβαίνει δὲ ἐπιστήμην μὲν πᾶσαν ἐπιστητὸν εἶναι τὸ δὲ ἐπιστητὸν μὴ πᾶν ἐπιστήμην, ὅτι τρόπον τινὰ ἡ ἐπιστήμη μετρεῖται τῷ ἐπιστητῷ. τὸ δὲ πλῆθος οὔτε τῷ ὀλίγῳ ἐναντίον—ἀλλὰ τούτῳ μὲν τὸ πολὺ ὡς ὑπερέχον πλῆθος ὑπερεχομένῳ πλήθει—οὔτε τῷ ἑνὶ πάντως· ἀλλὰ τὸ μὲν ὥσπερ εἴρηται, ὅτι διαιρετὸν τὸ δʼ ἀδιαίρετον, τὸ δʼ ὡς πρός τι ὥσπερ ἡ ἐπιστήμη ἐπιστητῷ, ἐὰν ᾖ ἀριθμὸς τὸ δʼ ἓν μέτρον.\n\n\n\n \n\n \n\nἐπεὶ δὲ τῶν ἐναντίων ἐνδέχεται εἶναί τι μεταξὺ καὶ ἐνίων ἔστιν, ἀνάγκη ἐκ τῶν ἐναντίων εἶναι τὰ μεταξύ. πάντα γὰρ τὰ μεταξὺ ἐν τῷ αὐτῷ γένει ἐστὶ καὶ ὧν ἐστὶ μεταξύ. μεταξὺ μὲν γὰρ ταῦτα λέγομεν εἰς ὅσα μεταβάλλειν ἀνάγκη πρότερον τὸ μεταβάλλον (οἷον ἀπὸ τῆς ὑπάτης ἐπὶ τὴν νήτην εἰ μεταβαίνοι τῷ ὀλιγίστῳ, ἥξει πρότερον εἰς τοὺς μεταξὺ φθόγγους, καὶ ἐν χρώμασιν εἰ  ἐκ τοῦ λευκοῦ εἰς τὸ μέλαν, πρότερον ἥξει εἰς τὸ φοινικοῦν καὶ φαιὸν ἢ εἰς τὸ μέλαν· ὁμοίως δὲ καὶ ἐπὶ τῶν ἄλλων)· μεταβάλλειν δʼ ἐξ ἄλλου γένους εἰς ἄλλο γένος οὐκ ἔστιν ἀλλʼ ἢ κατὰ συμβεβηκός, οἷον ἐκ χρώματος εἰς σχῆμα. ἀνάγκη ἄρα τὰ μεταξὺ καὶ αὑτοῖς καὶ ὧν μεταξύ εἰσιν ἐν τῷ αὐτῷ γένει εἶναι. ἀλλὰ μὴν πάντα γε τὰ μεταξύ ἐστιν ἀντικειμένων τινῶν· ἐκ τούτων γὰρ μόνων καθʼ αὑτὰ ἔστι μεταβάλλειν (διὸ ἀδύνατον εἶναι μεταξὺ μὴ ἀντικειμένων· εἴη γὰρ ἂν μεταβολὴ καὶ μὴ ἐξ ἀντικειμένων). τῶν δʼ ἀντικειμένων ἀντιφάσεως μὲν οὐκ ἔστι μεταξύ (τοῦτο γάρ ἐστιν ἀντίφασις, ἀντίθεσις ἧς ὁτῳοῦν θάτερον μόριον πάρεστιν, οὐκ ἐχούσης οὐθὲν μεταξύ), τῶν δὲ λοιπῶν τὰ μὲν πρός τι τὰ δὲ στέρησις τὰ δὲ ἐναντία ἐστίν. τῶν δὲ πρός τι ὅσα μὴ ἐναντία, οὐκ ἔχει μεταξύ· αἴτιον δʼ ὅτι οὐκ ἐν τῷ αὐτῷ γένει ἐστίν.  τί γὰρ ἐπιστήμης καὶ ἐπιστητοῦ μεταξύ; ἀλλὰ μεγάλου καὶ μικροῦ. εἰ δʼ ἐστὶν ἐν ταὐτῷ γένει τὰ μεταξύ, ὥσπερ δέδεικται, καὶ μεταξὺ ἐναντίων, ἀνάγκη αὐτὰ συγκεῖσθαι ἐκ τούτων τῶν ἐναντίων. ἢ γὰρ ἔσται τι γένος αὐτῶν ἢ οὐθέν. καὶ εἰ μὲν γένος ἔσται οὕτως ὥστʼ εἶναι πρότερόν τι τῶν ἐναντίων, αἱ διαφοραὶ πρότεραι ἐναντίαι ἔσονται αἱ ποιήσουσαι τὰ ἐναντία εἴδη ὡς γένους· ἐκ γὰρ τοῦ γένους καὶ τῶν διαφορῶν τὰ εἴδη (οἷον εἰ τὸ λευκὸν καὶ μέλαν ἐναντία, ἔστι δὲ τὸ μὲν διακριτικὸν χρῶμα τὸ δὲ συγκριτικὸν χρῶμα, αὗται αἱ διαφοραί, τὸ διακριτικὸν καὶ συγκριτικόν, πρότεραι· ὥστε ταῦτα ἐναντία ἀλλήλοις πρότερα). ἀλλὰ μὴν τά γε ἐναντίως διαφέροντα μᾶλλον ἐναντίἀ· καὶ τὰ λοιπὰ καὶ τὰ μεταξὺ ἐκ τοῦ γένους ἔσται καὶ τῶν διαφορῶν (οἷον ὅσα χρώματα τοῦ λευκοῦ καὶ μέλανός ἐστι μεταξύ, ταῦτα δεῖ ἔκ τε τοῦ γένους λέγεσθαι —ἔστι δὲ γένος τὸ χρῶμα—καὶ ἐκ διαφορῶν τινῶν· αὗται δὲ οὐκ ἔσονται τὰ πρῶτα ἐναντία· εἰ δὲ μή, ἔσται ἕκαστον ἢ λευκὸν ἢ μέλαν· ἕτεραι ἄρα· μεταξὺ ἄρα τῶν πρώτων ἐναντίων αὗται ἔσονται, αἱ πρῶται δὲ διαφοραὶ τὸ διακριτικὸν καὶ συγκριτικόν)· ὥστε ταῦτα πρῶτα ζητητέον ὅσα ἐναντία μὴ ἐν γένει, ἐκ τίνος τὰ μεταξὺ αὐτῶν (ἀνάγκη γὰρ τὰ ἐν τῷ αὐτῷ γένει ἐκ τῶν ἀσυνθέτων τῷ γένει συγκεῖσθαι ἢ ἀσύνθετα εἶναι). τὰ μὲν οὖν ἐναντία ἀσύνθετα ἐξ ἀλλήλων, ὥστε ἀρχαί· τὰ δὲ μεταξὺ ἢ πάντα ἢ οὐθέν. ἐκ δὲ τῶν ἐναντίων γίγνεταί τι, ὥστʼ ἔσται μεταβολὴ εἰς τοῦτο πρὶν ἢ εἰς αὐτά· ἑκατέρου γὰρ καὶ ἧττον ἔσται καὶ μᾶλλον. μεταξὺ ἄρα ἔσται καὶ τοῦτο τῶν ἐναντίων. καὶ τἆλλα ἄρα πάντα σύνθετα τὰ μεταξύ· τὸ γὰρ τοῦ μὲν μᾶλλον τοῦ δʼ ἧττον σύνθετόν πως ἐξ ἐκείνων ὧν λέγεται εἶναι τοῦ μὲν μᾶλλον τοῦ δʼ ἧττον. ἐπεὶ δʼ οὐκ ἔστιν ἕτερα πρότερα ὁμογενῆ τῶν ἐναντίων, ἅπαντʼ ἂν ἐκ τῶν ἐναντίων εἴη τὰ μεταξύ, ὥστε καὶ τὰ κάτω πάντα, καὶ τἀναντία καὶ τὰ μεταξύ, ἐκ τῶν πρώτων ἐναντίων ἔσονται. ὅτι μὲν οὖν τὰ μεταξὺ ἔν τε ταὐτῷ γένει πάντα καὶ μεταξὺ ἐναντίων καὶ σύγκειται ἐκ τῶν ἐναντίων πάντα, δῆλον. \n\n\n\n \n\n \n\nτὸ δʼ ἕτερον τῷ εἴδει τινὸς τὶ ἕτερόν ἐστι, καὶ δεῖ τοῦτο ἀμφοῖν ὑπάρχειν· οἷον εἰ ζῷον ἕτερον τῷ εἴδει, ἄμφω ζῷα. ἀνάγκη ἄρα ἐν γένει τῷ αὐτῷ εἶναι τὰ ἕτερα τῷ εἴδει· τὸ γὰρ τοιοῦτο γένος καλῶ ὃ ἄμφω ἓν ταὐτὸ λέγεται, μὴ κατὰ συμβεβηκὸς ἔχον διαφοράν,  εἴτε ὡς ὕλη ὂν εἴτε ἄλλως. οὐ μόνον γὰρ δεῖ τὸ κοινὸν ὑπάρχειν, οἷον ἄμφω ζῷα, ἀλλὰ καὶ ἕτερον ἑκατέρῳ τοῦτο αὐτὸ τὸ ζῷον, οἷον τὸ μὲν ἵππον τὸ δὲ ἄνθρωπον, διὸ τοῦτο τὸ κοινὸν ἕτερον ἀλλήλων ἐστὶ τῷ εἴδει. ἔσται δὴ καθʼ αὑτὰ τὸ μὲν τοιονδὶ ζῷον τὸ δὲ τοιονδί, οἷον τὸ μὲν ἵππος τὸ δʼ ἄνθρωπος. ἀνάγκη ἄρα τὴν διαφορὰν ταύτην ἑτερότητα τοῦ γένους εἶναι. λέγω γὰρ γένους διαφορὰν ἑτερότητα ἣ ἕτερον ποιεῖ τοῦτο αὐτό. ἐναντίωσις τοίνυν ἔσται αὕτη (δῆλον δὲ καὶ ἐκ τῆς ἐπαγωγῆς)· πάντα γὰρ διαιρεῖται τοῖς ἀντικειμένοις, καὶ ὅτι τὰ ἐναντία ἐν ταὐτῷ γένει, δέδεικται· ἡ γὰρ ἐναντιότης ἦν διαφορὰ τελεία, ἡ δὲ διαφορὰ ἡ εἴδει πᾶσα τινὸς τί, ὥστε τοῦτο τὸ αὐτό τε καὶ γένος ἐπʼ ἀμφοῖν (διὸ καὶ ἐν τῇ αὐτῇ συστοιχίᾳ πάντα τὰ ἐναντία τῆς κατηγορίας ὅσα εἴδει διάφορα καὶ μὴ γένει, ἕτερά τε ἀλλήλων μάλιστα—τελεία γὰρ ἡ διαφορά—καὶ ἅμα ἀλλήλοις οὐ γίγνεται). ἡ ἄρα διαφορὰ ἐναντίωσίς ἐστιν. τοῦτο ἄρα ἐστὶ τὸ ἑτέροις εἶναι τῷ εἴδει, τὸ ἐν ταὐτῷ γένει ὄντα ἐναντίωσιν ἔχειν ἄτομα ὄντα (ταὐτὰ δὲ τῷ εἴδει ὅσα μὴ ἔχει ἐναντίωσιν ἄτομα ὄντα)· ἐν γὰρ τῇ διαιρέσει καὶ ἐν τοῖς μεταξὺ γίγνονται ἐναντιώσεις πρὶν εἰς τὰ ἄτομα ἐλθεῖν· ὥστε φανερὸν ὅτι πρὸς τὸ καλούμενον γένος οὔτε ταὐτὸν οὔτε ἕτερον τῷ εἴδει οὐθέν ἐστι τῶν ὡς γένους εἰδῶν (προσηκόντως· ἡ γὰρ ὕλη ἀποφάσει δηλοῦται, τὸ δὲ γένος ὕλη οὗ λέγεται γένος—μὴ ὡς τὸ τῶν Ἡρακλειδῶν ἀλλʼ ὡς τὸ ἐν τῇ φύσει), οὐδὲ πρὸς τὰ μὴ ἐν ταὐτῷ γένει, ἀλλὰ διοίσει τῷ γένει ἐκείνων, εἴδει δὲ τῶν ἐν ταὐτῷ γένει. ἐναντίωσιν γὰρ ἀνάγκη εἶναι τὴν διαφορὰν οὗ διαφέρει εἴδει· αὕτη δὲ ὑπάρχει τοῖς ἐν ταὐτῷ γένει οὖσι μόνοις.\n\n\n\n \n\n \n\nἀπορήσειε δʼ ἄν τις διὰ τί γυνὴ ἀνδρὸς οὐκ εἴδει διαφέρει, ἐναντίου τοῦ θήλεος καὶ τοῦ ἄρρενος ὄντος τῆς δὲ διαφορᾶς ἐναντιώσεως, οὐδὲ ζῷον θῆλυ καὶ ἄρρεν ἕτερον τῷ εἴδει· καίτοι καθʼ αὑτὸ τοῦ ζῴου αὕτη ἡ διαφορὰ καὶ οὐχ ὡς λευκότης ἢ μελανία ἀλλʼ ᾗ ζῷον καὶ τὸ θῆλυ καὶ τὸ ἄρρεν ὑπάρχει. ἔστι δʼ ἡ ἀπορία αὕτη σχεδὸν ἡ αὐτὴ καὶ διὰ τί ἡ μὲν ποιεῖ τῷ εἴδει ἕτερα ἐναντίωσις ἡ δʼ οὔ, οἷον τὸ πεζὸν καὶ τὸ πτερωτόν, λευκότης δὲ καὶ μελανία οὔ. ἢ ὅτι τὰ μὲν οἰκεῖα πάθη τοῦ γένους τὰ δʼ ἧττον; καὶ ἐπειδή ἐστι τὸ μὲν λόγος τὸ δʼ ὕλη,  ὅσαι μὲν ἐν τῷ λόγῳ εἰσὶν ἐναντιότητες εἴδει ποιοῦσι διαφοράν, ὅσαι δʼ ἐν τῷ συνειλημμένῳ τῇ ὕλῃ οὐ ποιοῦσιν. διὸ ἀνθρώπου λευκότης οὐ ποιεῖ οὐδὲ μελανία, οὐδὲ τοῦ λευκοῦ ἀνθρώπου ἔστι διαφορὰ κατʼ εἶδος πρὸς μέλανα ἄνθρωπον, οὐδʼ ἂν ὄνομα ἓν τεθῇ. ὡς ὕλη γὰρ ὁ ἄνθρωπος, οὐ ποιεῖ δὲ διαφορὰν ἡ ὕλη· οὐδʼ ἀνθρώπου γὰρ εἴδη εἰσὶν οἱ ἄνθρωποι διὰ τοῦτο, καίτοι ἕτεραι αἱ σάρκες καὶ τὰ ὀστᾶ ἐξ ὧν ὅδε καὶ ὅδε· ἀλλὰ τὸ σύνολον ἕτερον μέν, εἴδει δʼ οὐχ ἕτερον, ὅτι ἐν τῷ λόγῳ οὐκ ἔστιν ἐναντίωσις. τοῦτο δʼ ἐστὶ τὸ ἔσχατον ἄτομον· ὁ δὲ Καλλίας ἐστὶν ὁ λόγος μετὰ τῆς ὕλης· καὶ ὁ λευκὸς δὴ ἄνθρωπος, ὅτι Καλλίας λευκός· κατὰ συμβεβηκὸς οὖν ὁ ἄνθρωπος. οὐδὲ χαλκοῦς δὴ κύκλος καὶ ξύλινος· οὐδὲ τρίγωνον χαλκοῦν καὶ κύκλος ξύλινος, οὐ διὰ τὴν ὕλην εἴδει διαφέρουσιν ἀλλʼ ὅτι ἐν τῷ λόγῳ ἔνεστιν ἐναντίωσις. πότερον δʼ ἡ ὕλη οὐ ποιεῖ ἕτερα τῷ εἴδει, οὖσά πως ἑτέρα, ἢ ἔστιν ὡς ποιεῖ; διὰ τί γὰρ ὁδὶ ὁ ἵππος τουδὶ τοῦ ἀνθρώπου ἕτερος τῷ εἴδει; καίτοι σὺν τῇ ὕλῃ οἱ λόγοι αὐτῶν. ἢ ὅτι ἔνεστιν ἐν τῷ λόγῳ ἐναντίωσις; καὶ γὰρ τοῦ λευκοῦ ἀνθρώπου καὶ μέλανος ἵππου, καὶ ἔστι γε εἴδει, ἀλλʼ οὐχ ᾗ ὁ μὲν λευκὸς ὁ δὲ μέλας, ἐπεὶ καὶ εἰ ἄμφω λευκὰ ἦν, ὅμως ἂν ἦν εἴδει ἕτερα. τὸ δὲ ἄρρεν καὶ θῆλυ τοῦ ζῴου οἰκεῖα μὲν πάθη, ἀλλʼ οὐ κατὰ τὴν οὐσίαν ἀλλʼ ἐν τῇ ὕλῃ καὶ τῷ σώματι, διὸ τὸ αὐτὸ σπέρμα θῆλυ ἢ ἄρρεν γίγνεται παθόν τι πάθος. τί μὲν οὖν ἐστὶ τὸ τῷ εἴδει ἕτερον εἶναι, καὶ διὰ τί τὰ μὲν διαφέρει εἴδει τὰ δʼ οὔ, εἴρηται. \n\n\n\n \n\n \n\nἐπειδὴ δὲ τὰ ἐναντία ἕτερα τῷ εἴδει, τὸ δὲ φθαρτὸν καὶ τὸ ἄφθαρτον ἐναντία (στέρησις γὰρ ἀδυναμία διωρισμένη), ἀνάγκη ἕτερον εἶναι τῷ γένει τὸ φθαρτὸν καὶ τὸ ἄφθαρτον. νῦν μὲν οὖν ἐπʼ αὐτῶν εἰρήκαμεν τῶν καθόλου ὀνομάτων, ὥστε δόξειεν ἂν οὐκ ἀναγκαῖον εἶναι ὁτιοῦν ἄφθαρτον καὶ φθαρτὸν ἕτερα εἶναι τῷ εἴδει, ὥσπερ οὐδὲ λευκὸν καὶ μέλαν (τὸ γὰρ αὐτὸ ἐνδέχεται εἶναι, καὶ ἅμα, ἐὰν ᾖ τῶν καθόλου, ὥσπερ ὁ ἄνθρωπος εἴη ἂν καὶ λευκὸς καὶ μέλας, καὶ τῶν καθʼ ἕκαστον· εἴη γὰρ ἄν, μὴ ἅμα, ὁ αὐτὸς λευκὸς καὶ μέλας· καίτοι ἐναντίον τὸ λευκὸν τῷ μέλανι)· ἀλλὰ τῶν ἐναντίων τὰ μὲν κατὰ συμβεβηκὸς ὑπάρχει ἐνίοις, οἷον καὶ τὰ νῦν εἰρημένα καὶ ἄλλα πολλά, τὰ δὲ ἀδύνατον, ὧν ἐστὶ καὶ τὸ φθαρτὸν καὶ τὸ ἄφθαρτον·  οὐδὲν γάρ ἐστι φθαρτὸν κατὰ συμβεβηκός· τὸ μὲν γὰρ συμβεβηκὸς ἐνδέχεται μὴ ὑπάρχειν, τὸ δὲ φθαρτὸν τῶν ἐξ ἀνάγκης ὑπαρχόντων ἐστὶν οἷς ὑπάρχει· ἢ ἔσται τὸ αὐτὸ καὶ ἓν φθαρτὸν καὶ ἄφθαρτον, εἰ ἐνδέχεται μὴ ὑπάρχειν αὐτῷ τὸ φθαρτόν. ἢ τὴν οὐσίαν ἄρα ἢ ἐν τῇ οὐσίᾳ ἀνάγκη ὑπάρχειν τὸ φθαρτὸν ἑκάστῳ τῶν φθαρτῶν. ὁ δʼ αὐτὸς λόγος καὶ περὶ τοῦ ἀφθάρτου· τῶν γὰρ ἐξ ἀνάγκης ὑπαρχόντων ἄμφω. ᾗ ἄρα καὶ καθʼ ὃ πρῶτον τὸ μὲν φθαρτὸν τὸ δʼ ἄφθαρτον, ἔχει ἀντίθεσιν, ὥστε ἀνάγκη γένει ἕτερα εἶναι. φανερὸν τοίνυν ὅτι οὐκ ἐνδέχεται εἶναι εἴδη τοιαῦτα οἷα λέγουσί τινες· ἔσται γὰρ καὶ ἄνθρωπος ὁ μὲν φθαρτὸς ὁ δʼ ἄφθαρτος. καίτοι τῷ εἴδει ταὐτὰ λέγεται εἶναι τὰ εἴδη τοῖς τισὶ καὶ οὐχ ὁμώνυμα· τὰ δὲ γένει ἕτερα πλεῖον διέστηκεν ἢ τὰ εἴδει.","paragraphs":[{"id":"RAW_DAZY_BO_2:P0001","text":"τὸ ἓν ὅτι μὲν λέγεται πολλαχῶς, ἐν τοῖς περὶ τοῦ ποσαχῶς διῃρημένοις εἴρηται πρότερον· πλεοναχῶς δὲ λεγομένου οἱ συγκεφαλαιούμενοι τρόποι εἰσὶ τέτταρες τῶν πρώτων καὶ καθʼ αὑτὰ λεγομένων ἓν ἀλλὰ μὴ κατὰ συμβεβηκός. τό τε γὰρ συνεχὲς ἢ ἁπλῶς ἢ μάλιστά γε τὸ φύσει καὶ μὴ ἁφῇ μηδὲ δεσμῷ (καὶ τούτων μᾶλλον ἓν καὶ πρότερον οὗ ἀδιαιρετωτέρα ἡ κίνησις καὶ μᾶλλον ἁπλῆ)· ἔτι τοιοῦτον καὶ μᾶλλον τὸ ὅλον καὶ ἔχον τινὰ μορφὴν καὶ εἶδος, μάλιστα δʼ εἴ τι φύσει τοιοῦτον καὶ μὴ βίᾳ, ὥσπερ ὅσα κόλλῃ ἢ γόμφῳ ἢ συνδέσμῳ, ἀλλὰ ἔχει ἐν αὑτῷ τὸ αἴτιον αὐτῷ τοῦ συνεχὲς εἶναι. τοιοῦτον δὲ τῷ μίαν τὴν κίνησιν εἶναι καὶ ἀδιαίρετον τόπῳ καὶ χρόνῳ, ὥστε φανερόν, εἴ τι φύσει κινήσεως ἀρχὴν ἔχει τῆς πρώτης τὴν πρώτην, οἷον λέγω φορᾶς κυκλοφορίαν, ὅτι τοῦτο πρῶτον μέγεθος ἕν.","kind":"passage"},{"id":"RAW_DAZY_BO_2:P0002","text":"τὰ μὲν δὴ οὕτως ἓν ᾗ συνεχὲς ἢ ὅλον, τὰ δὲ ὧν ἂν ὁ λόγος  εἷς ᾖ, τοιαῦτα δὲ ὧν ἡ νόησις μία, τοιαῦτα δὲ ὧν ἀδιαίρετος, ἀδιαίρετος δὲ τοῦ ἀδιαιρέτου εἴδει ἢ ἀριθμῷ· ἀριθμῷ μὲν οὖν τὸ καθʼ ἕκαστον ἀδιαίρετον, εἴδει δὲ τὸ τῷ γνωστῷ καὶ τῇ ἐπιστήμῃ, ὥσθʼ ἓν ἂν εἴη πρῶτον τὸ ταῖς οὐσίαις αἴτιον τοῦ ἑνός. λέγεται μὲν οὖν τὸ ἓν τοσαυταχῶς, τό τε συνεχὲς φύσει καὶ τὸ ὅλον, καὶ τὸ καθʼ ἕκαστον καὶ τὸ καθόλου, πάντα δὲ ταῦτα ἓν τῷ ἀδιαίρετον εἶναι τῶν μὲν τὴν κίνησιν τῶν δὲ τὴν νόησιν ἢ τὸν λόγον.  —δεῖ δὲ κατανοεῖν ὅτι οὐχ ὡσαύτως ληπτέον λέγεσθαι ποῖά τε ἓν λέγεται, καὶ τί ἐστι τὸ ἑνὶ εἶναι καὶ τίς αὐτοῦ λόγος.","kind":"passage"},{"id":"RAW_DAZY_BO_2:P0003","text":"λέγεται μὲν γὰρ τὸ ἓν τοσαυταχῶς, καὶ ἕκαστον ἔσται ἓν τούτων, ᾧ ἂν ὑπάρχῃ τις τούτων τῶν τρόπων· τὸ δὲ ἑνὶ εἶναι ὁτὲ μὲν τούτων τινὶ ἔσται, ὁτὲ δὲ ἄλλῳ ὃ καὶ μᾶλλον ἐγγὺς τῷ ὀνόματί ἐστι, τῇ δυνάμει δʼ ἐκεῖνα, ὥσπερ καὶ περὶ στοιχείου καὶ αἰτίου εἰ δέοι λέγειν ἐπί τε τοῖς πράγμασι διορίζοντα καὶ τοῦ ὀνόματος ὅρον ἀποδιδόντα. ἔστι μὲν γὰρ ὡς στοιχεῖον τὸ πῦρ (ἔστι δʼ ἴσως καθʼ αὑτὸ καὶ τὸ ἄπειρον ἤ τι ἄλλο τοιοῦτον), ἔστι δʼ ὡς οὔ· οὐ γὰρ τὸ αὐτὸ πυρὶ καὶ στοιχείῳ εἶναι, ἀλλʼ ὡς μὲν πρᾶγμά τι καὶ φύσις τὸ πῦρ στοιχεῖον, τὸ δὲ ὄνομα σημαίνει τὸ τοδὶ συμβεβηκέναι αὐτῷ, ὅτι ἐστί τι ἐκ τούτου ὡς πρώτου ἐνυπάρχοντος.","kind":"passage"},{"id":"RAW_DAZY_BO_2:P0004","text":"οὕτω καὶ ἐπὶ αἰτίου καὶ ἑνὸς καὶ τῶν τοιούτων ἁπάντων, διὸ καὶ τὸ ἑνὶ εἶναι τὸ ἀδιαιρέτῳ ἐστὶν εἶναι, ὅπερ τόδε ὄντι καὶ ἰδίᾳ χωριστῷ ἢ τόπῳ ἢ εἴδει ἢ διανοίᾳ, ἢ καὶ τὸ ὅλῳ καὶ ἀδιαιρέτῳ, μάλιστα δὲ τὸ μέτρῳ εἶναι πρώτῳ ἑκάστου γένους καὶ κυριώτατα τοῦ ποσοῦ· ἐντεῦθεν γὰρ ἐπὶ τὰ ἄλλα ἐλήλυθεν. μέτρον γάρ ἐστιν ᾧ τὸ ποσὸν γιγνώσκεται· γιγνώσκεται δὲ ἢ ἑνὶ ἢ ἀριθμῷ τὸ ποσὸν ᾗ ποσόν, ὁ δὲ ἀριθμὸς ἅπας ἑνί, ὥστε πᾶν τὸ ποσὸν γιγνώσκεται ᾗ ποσὸν τῷ ἑνί, καὶ ᾧ πρώτῳ ποσὰ γιγνώσκεται, τοῦτο αὐτὸ ἕν· διὸ τὸ ἓν ἀριθμοῦ ἀρχὴ ᾗ ἀριθμός.","kind":"passage"},{"id":"RAW_DAZY_BO_2:P0005","text":"ἐντεῦθεν δὲ καὶ ἐν τοῖς ἄλλοις λέγεται μέτρον τε ᾧ ἕκαστον πρώτῳ γιγνώσκεται, καὶ τὸ μέτρον ἑκάστου ἕν, ἐν μήκει, ἐν πλάτει, ἐν βάθει, ἐν βάρει, ἐν τάχει (τὸ γὰρ βάρος καὶ τάχος κοινὸν ἐν τοῖς ἐναντίοις· διττὸν γὰρ ἑκάτερον αὐτῶν, οἷον βάρος τό τε ὁποσηνοῦν ἔχον ῥοπὴν καὶ τὸ ἔχον ὑπεροχὴν ῥοπῆς, καὶ τάχος τό τε ὁποσηνοῦν κίνησιν ἔχον καὶ τὸ ὑπεροχὴν κινήσεως· ἔστι γάρ τι τάχος καὶ τοῦ βραδέος καὶ βάρος τοῦ κουφοτέρου). ἐν πᾶσι δὴ τούτοις μέτρον καὶ ἀρχὴ ἕν τι καὶ ἀδιαίρετον, ἐπεὶ καὶ ἐν ταῖς γραμμαῖς χρῶνται ὡς ἀτόμῳ τῇ ποδιαίᾳ. πανταχοῦ γὰρ τὸ μέτρον ἕν τι ζητοῦσι καὶ ἀδιαίρετον· τοῦτο δὲ τὸ ἁπλοῦν ἢ τῷ ποιῷ ἢ τῷ ποσῷ.","kind":"passage"},{"id":"RAW_DAZY_BO_2:P0006","text":"ὅπου μὲν οὖν δοκεῖ μὴ εἶναι ἀφελεῖν ἢ προσθεῖναι, τοῦτο ἀκριβὲς τὸ μέτρον (διὸ τὸ τοῦ ἀριθμοῦ ἀκριβέστατον·  τὴν γὰρ μονάδα τιθέασι πάντῃ ἀδιαίρετον)· ἐν δὲ τοῖς ἄλλοις μιμοῦνται τὸ τοιοῦτον· ἀπὸ γὰρ σταδίου καὶ ταλάντου καὶ ἀεὶ τοῦ μείζονος λάθοι ἂν καὶ προστεθέν τι καὶ ἀφαιρεθὲν μᾶλλον ἢ ἀπὸ ἐλάττονος· ὥστε ἀφʼ οὗ πρώτου κατὰ τὴν αἴσθησιν μὴ ἐνδέχεται, τοῦτο πάντες ποιοῦνται μέτρον καὶ ὑγρῶν καὶ ξηρῶν καὶ βάρους καὶ μεγέθους· καὶ τότʼ οἴονται εἰδέναι τὸ ποσόν, ὅταν εἰδῶσι διὰ τούτου τοῦ μέτρου. καὶ δὴ καὶ κίνησιν τῇ ἁπλῇ κινήσει καὶ τῇ ταχίστῃ (ὀλίγιστον γὰρ αὕτη ἔχει χρόνον)· διὸ ἐν τῇ ἀστρολογίᾳ τὸ τοιοῦτον ἓν ἀρχὴ καὶ μέτρον (τὴν κίνησιν γὰρ ὁμαλὴν ὑποτίθενται καὶ ταχίστην τὴν τοῦ οὐρανοῦ, πρὸς ἣν κρίνουσι τὰς ἄλλας), καὶ ἐν μουσικῇ δίεσις, ὅτι ἐλάχιστον, καὶ ἐν φωνῇ στοιχεῖον.","kind":"passage"},{"id":"RAW_DAZY_BO_2:P0007","text":"καὶ ταῦτα πάντα ἕν τι οὕτως, οὐχ ὡς κοινόν τι τὸ ἓν ἀλλʼ ὥσπερ εἴρηται.—οὐκ ἀεὶ δὲ τῷ ἀριθμῷ ἓν τὸ μέτρον ἀλλʼ ἐνίοτε πλείω, οἷον αἱ διέσεις δύο, αἱ μὴ κατὰ τὴν ἀκοὴν ἀλλʼ ἐν τοῖς λόγοις, καὶ αἱ φωναὶ πλείους αἷς μετροῦμεν, καὶ ἡ διάμετρος δυσὶ μετρεῖται καὶ ἡ πλευρά, καὶ τὰ μεγέθη πάντα. οὕτω δὴ πάντων μέτρον τὸ ἕν, ὅτι γνωρίζομεν ἐξ ὧν ἐστὶν ἡ οὐσία διαιροῦντες ἢ κατὰ τὸ ποσὸν ἢ κατὰ τὸ εἶδος. καὶ διὰ τοῦτο τὸ ἓν ἀδιαίρετον, ὅτι τὸ πρῶτον ἑκάστων ἀδιαίρετον. οὐχ ὁμοίως δὲ πᾶν ἀδιαίρετον, οἷον ποὺς καὶ μονάς, ἀλλὰ τὸ μὲν πάντῃ, τὸ δʼ εἰς ἀδιαίρετα πρὸς τὴν αἴσθησιν θετέον, ὥσπερ εἴρηται ἤδη· ἴσως γὰρ πᾶν συνεχὲς διαιρετόν. ἀεὶ δὲ συγγενὲς τὸ μέτρον· μεγεθῶν μὲν γὰρ μέγεθος, καὶ καθʼ ἕκαστον μήκους μῆκος, πλάτους πλάτος, φωνῆς φωνή, βάρους βάρος, μονάδων μονάς.","kind":"passage"},{"id":"RAW_DAZY_BO_2:P0008","text":"οὕτω γὰρ δεῖ λαμβάνειν, ἀλλʼ οὐχ ὅτι ἀριθμῶν ἀριθμός· καίτοι ἔδει, εἰ ὁμοίως· ἀλλʼ οὐχ ὁμοίως ἀξιοῖ ἀλλʼ ὥσπερ εἰ μονάδων μονάδας ἀξιώσειε μέτρον ἀλλὰ μὴ μονάδα· ὁ δʼ ἀριθμὸς πλῆθος μονάδων. καὶ τὴν ἐπιστήμην δὲ μέτρον τῶν πραγμάτων λέγομεν καὶ τὴν αἴσθησιν διὰ τὸ αὐτό, ὅτι γνωρίζομέν τι αὐταῖς, ἐπεὶ μετροῦνται μᾶλλον ἢ μετροῦσιν. ἀλλὰ συμβαίνει ἡμῖν ὥσπερ ἂν εἰ ἄλλου ἡμᾶς μετροῦντος ἐγνωρίσαμεν πηλίκοι ἐσμὲν τῷ τὸν πῆχυν ἐπὶ τοσοῦτον ἡμῶν ἐπιβάλλειν. Πρωταγόρας δʼ ἄνθρωπόν φησι πάντων εἶναι μέτρον, ὥσπερ ἂν εἰ τὸν ἐπιστήμονα εἰπὼν ἢ τὸν αἰσθανόμενον·  τούτους δʼ ὅτι ἔχουσιν ὁ μὲν αἴσθησιν ὁ δὲ ἐπιστήμην, ἅ φαμεν εἶναι μέτρα τῶν ὑποκειμένων. οὐθὲν δὴ λέγοντες περιττὸν φαίνονταί τι λέγειν.","kind":"passage"},{"id":"RAW_DAZY_BO_2:P0009","text":"ὅτι μὲν οὖν τὸ ἑνὶ εἶναι μάλιστά ἐστι κατὰ τὸ ὄνομα ἀφορίζοντι μέτρον τι, καὶ κυριώτατα τοῦ ποσοῦ, εἶτα τοῦ ποιοῦ, φανερόν· ἔσται δὲ τοιοῦτον τὸ μὲν ἂν ᾖ ἀδιαίρετον κατὰ τὸ ποσόν, τὸ δὲ ἂν κατὰ τὸ ποιόν· διόπερ ἀδιαίρετον τὸ ἓν ἢ ἁπλῶς ἢ ᾗ ἕν.","kind":"passage"},{"id":"RAW_DAZY_BO_2:P0010","text":"κατὰ δὲ τὴν οὐσίαν καὶ τὴν φύσιν ζητητέον ποτέρως ἔχει, καθάπερ ἐν τοῖς διαπορήμασιν ἐπήλθομεν τί τὸ ἕν ἐστι καὶ πῶς δεῖ περὶ αὐτοῦ λαβεῖν, πότερον ὡς οὐσίας τινὸς οὔσης αὐτοῦ τοῦ ἑνός, καθάπερ οἵ τε Πυθαγόρειοί φασι πρότερον καὶ Πλάτων ὕστερον, ἢ μᾶλλον ὑπόκειταί τις φύσις καὶ  δεῖ γνωριμωτέρως λεχθῆναι καὶ μᾶλλον ὥσπερ οἱ περὶ φύσεως· ἐκείνων γὰρ ὁ μέν τις φιλίαν εἶναί φησι τὸ ἓν ὁ δʼ ἀέρα ὁ δὲ τὸ ἄπειρον. εἰ δὴ μηδὲν τῶν καθόλου δυνατὸν οὐσίαν εἶναι, καθάπερ ἐν τοῖς περὶ οὐσίας καὶ περὶ τοῦ ὄντος εἴρηται λόγοις, οὐδʼ αὐτὸ τοῦτο οὐσίαν ὡς ἕν τι παρὰ τὰ πολλὰ δυνατὸν εἶναι (κοινὸν γάρ) ἀλλʼ ἢ κατηγόρημα μόνον, δῆλον ὡς οὐδὲ τὸ ἕν· τὸ γὰρ ὂν καὶ τὸ ἓν καθόλου κατηγορεῖται μάλιστα πάντων. ὥστε οὔτε τὰ γένη φύσεις τινὲς καὶ οὐσίαι χωρισταὶ τῶν ἄλλων εἰσίν, οὔτε τὸ ἓν γένος ἐνδέχεται εἶναι διὰ τὰς αὐτὰς αἰτίας διʼ ἅσπερ οὐδὲ τὸ ὂν οὐδὲ τὴν οὐσίαν.","kind":"passage"},{"id":"RAW_DAZY_BO_2:P0011","text":"ἔτι δʼ ὁμοίως ἐπὶ πάντων ἀναγκαῖον ἔχειν· λέγεται δʼ ἰσαχῶς τὸ ὂν καὶ τὸ ἕν· ὥστʼ ἐπείπερ ἐν τοῖς ποιοῖς ἐστί τι τὸ ἓν καί τις φύσις, ὁμοίως δὲ καὶ ἐν τοῖς ποσοῖς, δῆλον ὅτι καὶ ὅλως ζητητέον τί τὸ ἕν, ὥσπερ καὶ τί τὸ ὄν, ὡς οὐχ ἱκανὸν ὅτι τοῦτο αὐτὸ ἡ φύσις αὐτοῦ. ἀλλὰ μὴν ἔν γε χρώμασίν ἐστι τὸ ἓν χρῶμα, οἷον τὸ λευκόν, εἶτα τὰ ἄλλα ἐκ τούτου καὶ τοῦ μέλανος φαίνεται γιγνόμενα, τὸ δὲ μέλαν στέρησις λευκοῦ ὥσπερ καὶ φωτὸς σκότος · ὥστε εἰ τὰ ὄντα ἦν χρώματα, ἦν ἂν ἀριθμός τις τὰ ὄντα, ἀλλὰ τίνων; δῆλον δὴ ὅτι χρωμάτων, καὶ τὸ ἓν ἦν ἄν τι ἕν, οἷον τὸ λευκόν. ὁμοίως δὲ καὶ εἰ μέλη τὰ ὄντα ἦν, ἀριθμὸς ἂν ἦν, διέσεων μέντοι, ἀλλʼ οὐκ ἀριθμὸς ἡ οὐσία αὐτῶν· καὶ τὸ ἓν ἦν ἄν τι οὗ ἡ οὐσία οὐ τὸ ἓν ἀλλὰ δίεσις.  ὁμοίως δὲ καὶ ἐπὶ τῶν φθόγγων στοιχείων ἂν ἦν τὰ ὄντα ἀριθμός, καὶ τὸ ἓν στοιχεῖον φωνῆεν. καὶ εἰ σχήματα εὐθύγραμμα, σχημάτων ἂν ἦν ἀριθμός, καὶ τὸ ἓν τὸ τρίγωνον.","kind":"passage"},{"id":"RAW_DAZY_BO_2:P0012","text":"ὁ δʼ αὐτὸς λόγος καὶ ἐπὶ τῶν ἄλλων γενῶν, ὥστʼ εἴπερ καὶ ἐν τοῖς πάθεσι καὶ ἐν τοῖς ποιοῖς καὶ ἐν τοῖς ποσοῖς καὶ ἐν κινήσει ἀριθμῶν ὄντων καὶ ἑνός τινος ἐν ἅπασιν ὅ τε ἀριθμὸς τινῶν καὶ τὸ ἓν τὶ ἕν, ἀλλʼ οὐχὶ τοῦτο αὐτὸ ἡ οὐσία, καὶ ἐπὶ τῶν οὐσιῶν ἀνάγκη ὡσαύτως ἔχειν· ὁμοίως γὰρ ἔχει ἐπὶ πάντων.—ὅτι μὲν οὖν τὸ ἓν ἐν ἅπαντι γένει ἐστί τις φύσις, καὶ οὐδενὸς τοῦτό γʼ αὐτὸ ἡ φύσις τὸ ἕν, φανερόν, ἀλλʼ ὥσπερ ἐν χρώμασι χρῶμα ἓν ζητητέον αὐτὸ τὸ ἕν, οὕτω καὶ ἐν οὐσίᾳ οὐσίαν μίαν αὐτὸ τὸ ἕν· ὅτι δὲ ταὐτὸ σημαίνει πως τὸ ἓν καὶ τὸ ὄν, δῆλον τῷ τε παρακολουθεῖν ἰσαχῶς ταῖς κατηγορίαις καὶ μὴ εἶναι ἐν μηδεμιᾷ (οἷον οὔτʼ ἐν τῇ τί ἐστιν οὔτʼ ἐν τῇ ποῖον, ἀλλʼ ὁμοίως ἔχει ὥσπερ τὸ ὄν) καὶ τῷ μὴ προσκατηγορεῖσθαι ἕτερόν τι τὸ εἷς ἄνθρωπος τοῦ ἄνθρωπος (ὥσπερ οὐδὲ τὸ εἶναι παρὰ τὸ τί ἢ ποῖον ἢ πόσον) καὶ τῷ εἶναι τὸ ἑνὶ εἶναι τὸ ἑκάστῳ εἶναι.","kind":"passage"},{"id":"RAW_DAZY_BO_2:P0013","text":"ἀντίκειται δὲ τὸ ἓν καὶ τὰ πολλὰ κατὰ πλείους τρόπους, ὧν ἕνα τὸ ἓν καὶ τὸ πλῆθος ὡς ἀδιαίρετον καὶ διαιρετόν· τὸ μὲν γὰρ ἢ διῃρημένον ἢ διαιρετὸν πλῆθός τι λέγεται, τὸ δὲ ἀδιαίρετον ἢ μὴ διῃρημένον ἕν. ἐπεὶ οὖν αἱ ἀντιθέσεις τετραχῶς, καὶ τούτων κατὰ στέρησιν λέγεται θάτερον, ἐναντία ἂν εἴη καὶ οὔτε ὡς ἀντίφασις οὔτε ὡς τὰ πρός τι λεγόμενα. λέγεται δὲ ἐκ τοῦ ἐναντίου καὶ δηλοῦται τὸ ἕν, ἐκ τοῦ διαιρετοῦ τὸ ἀδιαίρετον, διὰ τὸ μᾶλλον αἰσθητὸν τὸ πλῆθος εἶναι καὶ τὸ διαιρετὸν ἢ τὸ ἀδιαίρετον, ὥστε τῷ λόγῳ πρότερον τὸ πλῆθος τοῦ ἀδιαιρέτου διὰ τὴν αἴσθησιν. ἔστι δὲ τοῦ μὲν ἑνός, ὥσπερ καὶ ἐν τῇ διαιρέσει τῶν ἐναντίων διεγράψαμεν, τὸ ταὐτὸ καὶ ὅμοιον καὶ ἴσον, τοῦ δὲ πλήθους τὸ ἕτερον καὶ ἀνόμοιον καὶ ἄνισον.","kind":"passage"},{"id":"RAW_DAZY_BO_2:P0014","text":"λεγομένου δὲ τοῦ ταὐτοῦ πολλαχῶς, ἕνα μὲν τρόπον κατʼ ἀριθμὸν λέγομεν ἐνίοτε αὐτό, τὸ δʼ ἐὰν καὶ λόγῳ καὶ ἀριθμῷ ἓν ᾖ, οἷον σὺ σαυτῷ καὶ τῷ εἴδει καὶ τῇ ὕλῃ ἕν· ἔτι δʼ ἐὰν ὁ λόγος ὁ τῆς πρώτης οὐσίας εἷς ᾖ,  οἷον αἱ ἴσαι γραμμαὶ εὐθεῖαι αἱ αὐταί, καὶ τὰ ἴσα καὶ ἰσογώνια τετράγωνα, καίτοι πλείω· ἀλλʼ ἐν τούτοις ἡ ἰσότης ἑνότης. ὅμοια δὲ ἐὰν μὴ ταὐτὰ ἁπλῶς ὄντα, μηδὲ κατὰ τὴν οὐσίαν ἀδιάφορα τὴν συγκειμένην, κατὰ τὸ εἶδος ταὐτὰ ᾖ, ὥσπερ τὸ μεῖζον τετράγωνον τῷ μικρῷ ὅμοιον, καὶ αἱ ἄνισοι εὐθεῖαι· αὗται γὰρ ὅμοιαι μέν, αἱ αὐταὶ δὲ ἁπλῶς οὔ. τὰ δὲ ἐὰν τὸ αὐτὸ εἶδος ἔχοντα, ἐν οἷς τὸ μᾶλλον καὶ ἧττον ἐγγίγνεται, μήτε μᾶλλον ᾖ μήτε ἧττον. τὰ δὲ ἐὰν ᾖ τὸ αὐτὸ πάθος καὶ ἓν τῷ εἴδει, οἷον τὸ λευκόν, σφόδρα καὶ ἧττον, ὅμοιά φασιν εἶναι ὅτι ἓν τὸ εἶδος αὐτῶν. τὰ δὲ ἐὰν πλείω ἔχῃ ταὐτὰ ἢ ἕτερα, ἢ ἁπλῶς ἢ τὰ πρόχειρα, οἷον καττίτερος ἀργύρῳ ᾗ λευκόν, χρυσὸς δὲ πυρὶ ᾗ ξανθὸν καὶ πυρρόν.","kind":"passage"},{"id":"RAW_DAZY_BO_2:P0015","text":"ὥστε δῆλον ὅτι καὶ τὸ ἕτερον καὶ τὸ ἀνόμοιον πολλαχῶς λέγεται. καὶ τὸ μὲν ἄλλο ἀντικειμένως καὶ τὸ ταὐτό, διὸ ἅπαν πρὸς ἅπαν ἢ ταὐτὸ ἢ ἄλλο· τὸ δʼ ἐὰν μὴ καὶ ἡ ὕλη καὶ ὁ λόγος εἷς, διὸ σὺ καὶ ὁ πλησίον ἕτερος· τὸ δὲ τρίτον ὡς τὰ ἐν τοῖς μαθηματικοῖς. τὸ μὲν οὖν ἕτερον ἢ ταὐτὸ διὰ τοῦτο πᾶν πρὸς πᾶν λέγεται, ὅσα λέγεται ἓν καὶ ὄν· οὐ γὰρ ἀντίφασίς ἐστι τοῦ ταὐτοῦ, διὸ οὐ λέγεται ἐπὶ τῶν μὴ ὄντων (τὸ δὲ μὴ ταὐτὸ λέγεται), ἐπὶ δὲ τῶν ὄντων πάντων· ἢ γὰρ ἓν ἢ οὐχ ἓν πέφυχʼ ὅσα ὂν καὶ ἕν. τὸ μὲν οὖν ἕτερον καὶ ταὐτὸν οὕτως ἀντίκειται, διαφορὰ δὲ καὶ ἑτερότης ἄλλο. τὸ μὲν γὰρ ἕτερον καὶ οὗ ἕτερον οὐκ ἀνάγκη εἶναι τινὶ ἕτερον· πᾶν γὰρ ἢ ἕτερον ἢ ταὐτὸ ὅ τι ἂν ᾖ ὄν· τὸ δὲ διάφορον τινὸς τινὶ διάφορον, ὥστε ἀνάγκη ταὐτό τι εἶναι ᾧ διαφέρουσιν.","kind":"passage"},{"id":"RAW_DAZY_BO_2:P0016","text":"τοῦτο δὲ τὸ ταὐτὸ γένος ἢ εἶδος· πᾶν γὰρ τὸ διαφέρον διαφέρει ἢ γένει ἢ εἴδει, γένει μὲν ὧν μὴ ἔστι κοινὴ ἡ ὕλη μηδὲ γένεσις εἰς ἄλληλα, οἷον ὅσων ἄλλο σχῆμα τῆς κατηγορίας, εἴδει δὲ ὧν τὸ αὐτὸ γένος (λέγεται δὲ γένος ὃ ἄμφω τὸ αὐτὸ λέγονται κατὰ τὴν οὐσίαν τὰ διάφορα). τὰ δʼ ἐναντία διάφορα, καὶ ἡ ἐναντίωσις διαφορά τις. ὅτι δὲ καλῶς τοῦτο ὑποτιθέμεθα, δῆλον ἐκ τῆς ἐπαγωγῆς· πάντα γὰρ διαφέροντα φαίνεται καὶ ταῦτα, οὐ μόνον ἕτερα ὄντα ἀλλὰ τὰ μὲν τὸ γένος ἕτερα τὰ δʼ ἐν τῇ αὐτῇ συστοιχίᾳ τῆς κατηγορίας,  ὥστʼ ἐν ταὐτῷ γένει καὶ ταὐτὰ τῷ γένει. διώρισται δʼ ἐν ἄλλοις ποῖα τῷ γένει ταὐτὰ ἢ ἕτερα.","kind":"passage"},{"id":"RAW_DAZY_BO_2:P0017","text":"ἐπεὶ δὲ διαφέρειν ἐνδέχεται ἀλλήλων τὰ διαφέροντα πλεῖον καὶ ἔλαττον, ἔστι τις καὶ μεγίστη διαφορά, καὶ ταύτην λέγω ἐναντίωσιν. ὅτι δʼ ἡ μεγίστη ἐστὶ διαφορά, δῆλον ἐκ τῆς ἐπαγωγῆς. τὰ μὲν γὰρ γένει διαφέροντα οὐκ ἔχει ὁδὸν εἰς ἄλληλα, ἀλλʼ ἀπέχει πλέον καὶ ἀσύμβλητα· τοῖς δʼ εἴδει διαφέρουσιν αἱ γενέσεις ἐκ τῶν ἐναντίων εἰσὶν ὡς ἐσχάτων, τὸ δὲ τῶν ἐσχάτων διάστημα μέγιστον, ὥστε καὶ τὸ τῶν ἐναντίων. ἀλλὰ μὴν τό γε μέγιστον ἐν ἑκάστῳ γένει τέλειον. μέγιστόν τε γὰρ οὗ μὴ ἔστιν ὑπερβολή, καὶ τέλειον οὗ μὴ ἔστιν ἔξω λαβεῖν τι δυνατόν· τέλος γὰρ ἔχει ἡ τελεία διαφορά (ὥσπερ καὶ τἆλλα τῷ τέλος ἔχειν λέγεται τέλεια), τοῦ δὲ τέλους οὐθὲν ἔξω· ἔσχατον γὰρ ἐν παντὶ καὶ περιέχει, διὸ οὐδὲν ἔξω τοῦ τέλους, οὐδὲ προσδεῖται οὐδενὸς τὸ τέλειον.","kind":"passage"},{"id":"RAW_DAZY_BO_2:P0018","text":"ὅτι μὲν οὖν ἡ ἐναντιότης ἐστὶ διαφορὰ τέλειος, ἐκ τούτων δῆλον· πολλαχῶς δὲ λεγομένων τῶν ἐναντίων, ἀκολουθήσει τὸ τελείως οὕτως ὡς ἂν καὶ τὸ ἐναντίοις εἶναι ὑπάρχῃ αὐτοῖς. τούτων δὲ ὄντων φανερὸν ὅτι οὐκ ἐνδέχεται ἑνὶ πλείω ἐναντία εἶναι (οὔτε γὰρ τοῦ ἐσχάτου ἐσχατώτερον εἴη ἄν τι, οὔτε τοῦ ἑνὸς διαστήματος πλείω δυοῖν ἔσχατα), ὅλως τε εἰ ἔστιν ἡ ἐναντιότης διαφορά, ἡ δὲ διαφορὰ δυοῖν, ὥστε καὶ ἡ τέλειος. ἀνάγκη δὲ καὶ τοὺς ἄλλους ὅρους ἀληθεῖς εἶναι τῶν ἐναντίων.","kind":"passage"},{"id":"RAW_DAZY_BO_2:P0019","text":"καὶ γὰρ πλεῖστον διαφέρει ἡ τέλειος διαφορά (τῶν τε γὰρ γένει διαφερόντων οὐκ ἔστιν ἐξωτέρω λαβεῖν καὶ τῶν εἴδει· δέδεικται γὰρ ὅτι πρὸς τὰ ἔξω τοῦ γένους οὐκ ἔστι διαφορά, τούτων δʼ αὕτη μεγίστη), καὶ τὰ ἐν ταὐτῷ γένει πλεῖστον διαφέροντα ἐναντία (μεγίστη γὰρ διαφορὰ τούτων ἡ τέλειος), καὶ τὰ ἐν τῷ αὐτῷ δεκτικῷ πλεῖστον διαφέροντα ἐναντία (ἡ γὰρ ὕλη ἡ αὐτὴ τοῖς ἐναντίοις) καὶ τὰ ὑπὸ τὴν αὐτὴν δύναμιν πλεῖστον διαφέροντα (καὶ γὰρ ἡ ἐπιστήμη περὶ ἓν γένος ἡ μία)· ἐν οἷς ἡ τελεία διαφορὰ μεγίστη.—πρώτη δὲ ἐναντίωσις ἕξις καὶ στέρησίς ἐστιν· οὐ πᾶσα δὲ στέρησις (πολλαχῶς γὰρ λέγεται ἡ στέρησις) ἀλλʼ ἥτις ἂν τελεία ᾖ. τὰ δʼ ἄλλα ἐναντία κατὰ ταῦτα λεχθήσεται, τὰ μὲν τῷ ἔχειν τὰ δὲ τῷ ποιεῖν ἢ ποιητικὰ εἶναι τὰ δὲ τῷ λήψεις εἶναι καὶ ἀποβολαὶ τούτων ἢ ἄλλων ἐναντίων.","kind":"passage"},{"id":"RAW_DAZY_BO_2:P0020","text":"εἰ δὴ ἀντίκειται μὲν ἀντίφασις καὶ στέρησις καὶ ἐναντιότης καὶ τὰ πρός τι,  τούτων δὲ πρῶτον ἀντίφασις, ἀντιφάσεως δὲ μηδέν ἐστι μεταξύ, τῶν δὲ ἐναντίων ἐνδέχεται, ὅτι μὲν οὐ ταὐτὸν ἀντίφασις καὶ τἀναντία δῆλον· ἡ δὲ στέρησις ἀντίφασίς τίς ἐστιν· ἢ γὰρ τὸ ἀδύνατον ὅλως ἔχειν, ἢ ὃ ἂν πεφυκὸς ἔχειν μὴ ἔχῃ, ἐστέρηται ἢ ὅλως ἢ πὼς ἀφορισθέν (πολλαχῶς γὰρ ἤδη τοῦτο λέγομεν, ὥσπερ διῄρηται ἡμῖν ἐν ἄλλοις), ὥστʼ ἐστὶν ἡ στέρησις ἀντίφασίς τις ἢ ἀδυναμία διορισθεῖσα ἢ συνειλημμένη τῷ δεκτικῷ· διὸ ἀντιφάσεως μὲν οὐκ ἔστι μεταξύ, στερήσεως δέ τινος ἔστιν· ἴσον μὲν γὰρ ἢ οὐκ ἴσον πᾶν, ἴσον δʼ ἢ ἄνισον οὐ πᾶν, ἀλλʼ εἴπερ, μόνον ἐν τῷ δεκτικῷ τοῦ ἴσου.","kind":"passage"},{"id":"RAW_DAZY_BO_2:P0021","text":"εἰ δὴ αἱ γενέσεις τῇ ὕλῃ ἐκ τῶν ἐναντίων, γίγνονται δὲ ἢ ἐκ τοῦ εἴδους καὶ τῆς τοῦ εἴδους ἕξεως ἢ ἐκ στερήσεώς τινος τοῦ εἴδους καὶ τῆς μορφῆς, δῆλον ὅτι ἡ μὲν ἐναντίωσις στέρησις ἂν εἴη πᾶσα, ἡ δὲ στέρησις ἴσως οὐ πᾶσα ἐναντιότης (αἴτιον δʼ ὅτι πολλαχῶς ἐνδέχεται ἐστερῆσθαι τὸ ἐστερημένον)· ἐξ ὧν γὰρ αἱ μεταβολαὶ ἐσχάτων, ἐναντία ταῦτα. φανερὸν δὲ καὶ διὰ τῆς ἐπαγωγῆς. πᾶσα γὰρ ἐναντίωσις ἔχει στέρησιν θάτερον τῶν ἐναντίων, ἀλλʼ οὐχ ὁμοίως πάντα· ἀνισότης μὲν γὰρ ἰσότητος ἀνομοιότης δὲ ὁμοιότητος κακία δὲ ἀρετῆς, διαφέρει δὲ ὥσπερ εἴρηται· τὸ μὲν γὰρ ἐὰν μόνον ᾖ ἐστερημένον, τὸ δʼ ἐὰν ἢ ποτὲ ἢ ἔν τινι, οἷον ἂν ἐν ἡλικίᾳ τινὶ ἢ τῷ κυρίῳ, ἢ πάντῃ· διὸ τῶν μὲν ἔστι μεταξύ, καὶ ἔστιν οὔτε ἀγαθὸς ἄνθρωπος οὔτε κακός, τῶν δὲ οὐκ ἔστιν, ἀλλʼ ἀνάγκη εἶναι ἢ περιττὸν ἢ ἄρτιον. ἔτι τὰ μὲν ἔχει τὸ ὑποκείμενον ὡρισμένον, τὰ δʼ οὔ.","kind":"passage"},{"id":"RAW_DAZY_BO_2:P0022","text":"ὥστε φανερὸν ὅτι ἀεὶ θάτερον τῶν ἐναντίων λέγεται κατὰ στέρησιν· ἀπόχρη δὲ κἂν τὰ πρῶτα καὶ τὰ γένη τῶν ἐναντίων, οἷον τὸ ἓν καὶ τὰ πολλά· τὰ γὰρ ἄλλα εἰς ταῦτα ἀνάγεται.","kind":"passage"},{"id":"RAW_DAZY_BO_2:P0023","text":"ἐπεὶ δὲ ἓν ἑνὶ ἐναντίον, ἀπορήσειεν ἄν τις πῶς ἀντίκειται τὸ ἓν καὶ τὰ πολλά, καὶ τὸ ἴσον τῷ μεγάλῳ καὶ τῷ μικρῷ. εἰ γὰρ τὸ πότερον ἀεὶ ἐν ἀντιθέσει λέγομεν, οἷον πότερον λευκὸν ἢ μέλαν, καὶ πότερον λευκὸν ἢ οὐ λευκόν (πότερον δὲ ἄνθρωπος ἢ λευκὸν οὐ λέγομεν, ἐὰν μὴ ἐξ ὑποθέσεως καὶ ζητοῦντες οἷον πότερον ἦλθε Κλέων ἢ Σωκράτης—ἀλλʼ οὐκ ἀνάγκη ἐν οὐδενὶ γένει τοῦτο· ἀλλὰ καὶ τοῦτο ἐκεῖθεν ἐλήλυθεν· τὰ γὰρ ἀντικείμενα μόνα οὐκ ἐνδέχεται ἅμα ὑπάρχειν, ᾧ καὶ ἐνταῦθα χρῆται ἐν τῷ πότερος ἦλθεν·  εἰ γὰρ ἅμα ἐνεδέχετο, γελοῖον τὸ ἐρώτημα· εἰ δέ, καὶ οὕτως ὁμοίως ἐμπίπτει εἰς ἀντίθεσιν, εἰς τὸ ἓν ἢ πολλά, οἷον πότερον ἀμφότεροι ἦλθον ἢ ἅτερος)·—εἰ δὴ ἐν τοῖς ἀντικειμένοις ἀεὶ τοῦ ποτέρου ἡ ζήτησις, λέγεται δὲ πότερον μεῖζον ἢ ἔλαττον ἢ ἴσον, τίς ἐστιν ἡ ἀντίθεσις πρὸς ταῦτα τοῦ ἴσου; οὔτε γὰρ θατέρῳ μόνῳ ἐναντίον οὔτʼ ἀμφοῖν· τί γὰρ μᾶλλον τῷ μείζονι ἢ τῷ ἐλάττονι; ἔτι τῷ ἀνίσῳ ἐναντίον τὸ","kind":"passage"},{"id":"RAW_DAZY_BO_2:P0024","text":"ἴσον, ὥστε πλείοσιν ἔσται ἢ ἑνί. εἰ δὲ τὸ ἄνισον σημαίνει τὸ αὐτὸ ἅμα ἀμφοῖν, εἴη μὲν ἂν ἀντικείμενον ἀμφοῖν (καὶ ἡ ἀπορία βοηθεῖ τοῖς φάσκουσι τὸ ἄνισον δυάδα εἶναι), ἀλλὰ συμβαίνει ἓν δυοῖν ἐναντίον· ὅπερ ἀδύνατον. ἔτι τὸ μὲν ἴσον μεταξὺ φαίνεται μεγάλου καὶ μικροῦ, ἐναντίωσις δὲ μεταξὺ οὐδεμία οὔτε φαίνεται οὔτε ἐκ τοῦ ὁρισμοῦ δυνατόν· οὐ γὰρ ἂν εἴη τελεία μεταξύ τινος οὖσα, ἀλλὰ μᾶλλον ἔχει ἀεὶ ἑαυτῆς τι μεταξύ. λείπεται δὴ ἢ ὡς ἀπόφασιν ἀντικεῖσθαι ἢ ὡς στέρησιν. θατέρου μὲν δὴ οὐκ ἐνδέχεται (τί γὰρ μᾶλλον τοῦ μεγάλου ἢ μικροῦ;)· ἀμφοῖν ἄρα ἀπόφασις στερητική, διὸ καὶ πρὸς ἀμφότερα τὸ πότερον λέγεται, πρὸς δὲ θάτερον οὔ (οἷον πότερον μεῖζον ἢ ἴσον, ἢ πότερον ἴσον ἢ ἔλαττον), ἀλλʼ ἀεὶ τρία.","kind":"passage"},{"id":"RAW_DAZY_BO_2:P0025","text":"οὐ στέρησις δὲ ἐξ ἀνάγκης· οὐ γὰρ πᾶν ἴσον ὃ μὴ μεῖζον ἢ ἔλαττον, ἀλλʼ ἐν οἷς πέφυκεν ἐκεῖνα.—ἔστι δὴ τὸ ἴσον τὸ μήτε μέγα μήτε μικρόν, πεφυκὸς δὲ ἢ μέγα ἢ μικρὸν εἶναι· καὶ ἀντίκειται ἀμφοῖν ὡς ἀπόφασις στερητική, διὸ καὶ μεταξύ ἐστιν. καὶ τὸ μήτε ἀγαθὸν μήτε κακὸν ἀντίκειται ἀμφοῖν, ἀλλʼ ἀνώνυμον· πολλαχῶς γὰρ λέγεται ἑκάτερον καὶ οὐκ ἔστιν ἓν τὸ δεκτικόν, ἀλλὰ μᾶλλον τὸ μήτε λευκὸν μήτε μέλαν. ἓν δὲ οὐδὲ τοῦτο λέγεται, ἀλλʼ ὡρισμένα πως ἐφʼ ὧν λέγεται στερητικῶς ἡ ἀπόφασις αὕτη· ἀνάγκη γὰρ ἢ φαιὸν ἢ ὠχρὸν εἶναι ἢ τοιοῦτόν τι ἄλλο. ὥστε οὐκ ὀρθῶς ἐπιτιμῶσιν οἱ νομίζοντες ὁμοίως λέγεσθαι πάντα, ὥστε ἔσεσθαι ὑποδήματος καὶ χειρὸς μεταξὺ τὸ μήτε ὑπόδημα μήτε χεῖρα, ἔπειπερ καὶ τὸ μήτε ἀγαθὸν μήτε κακὸν τοῦ ἀγαθοῦ καὶ τοῦ κακοῦ, ὡς πάντων ἐσομένου τινὸς μεταξύ. οὐκ ἀνάγκη δὲ τοῦτο συμβαίνειν.","kind":"passage"},{"id":"RAW_DAZY_BO_2:P0026","text":"ἡ μὲν γὰρ ἀντικειμένων συναπόφασίς ἐστιν ὧν ἔστι μεταξύ τι καὶ διάστημά τι πέφυκεν εἶναι·  τῶν δʼ οὐκ ἔστι διαφορά· ἐν ἄλλῳ γὰρ γένει ὧν αἱ συναποφάσεις, ὥστʼ οὐχ ἓν τὸ ὑποκείμενον.","kind":"passage"},{"id":"RAW_DAZY_BO_2:P0027","text":"ὁμοίως δὲ καὶ περὶ τοῦ ἑνὸς καὶ τῶν πολλῶν ἀπορήσειεν ἄν τις. εἰ γὰρ τὰ πολλὰ τῷ ἑνὶ ἁπλῶς ἀντίκειται, συμβαίνει ἔνια ἀδύνατα. τὸ γὰρ ἓν ὀλίγον ἢ ὀλίγα ἔσται· τὰ γὰρ πολλὰ καὶ τοῖς ὀλίγοις ἀντίκειται. ἔτι τὰ δύο πολλά, εἴπερ τὸ διπλάσιον πολλαπλάσιον λέγεται δὲ κατὰ τὰ δύο· ὥστε τὸ ἓν ὀλίγον· πρὸς τί γὰρ πολλὰ τὰ δύο εἰ μὴ πρὸς ἕν τε καὶ τὸ ὀλίγον; οὐθὲν γάρ ἐστιν ἔλαττον. ἔτι εἰ ὡς ἐν μήκει τὸ μακρὸν καὶ βραχύ, οὕτως ἐν πλήθει τὸ πολὺ καὶ ὀλίγον, καὶ ὃ ἂν ᾖ πολὺ καὶ πολλά, καὶ τὰ πολλὰ πολύ (εἰ μή τι ἄρα διαφέρει ἐν συνεχεῖ εὐορίστῳ), τὸ ὀλίγον πλῆθός τι ἔσται. ὥστε τὸ ἓν πλῆθός τι, εἴπερ καὶ ὀλίγον· τοῦτο δʼ ἀνάγκη, εἰ τὰ δύο πολλά. ἀλλʼ ἴσως τὰ πολλὰ λέγεται μέν πως καὶ  πολύ, ἀλλʼ ὡς διαφέρον, οἷον ὕδωρ πολύ, πολλὰ δʼ οὔ.","kind":"passage"},{"id":"RAW_DAZY_BO_2:P0028","text":"ἀλλʼ ὅσα διαιρετά, ἐν τούτοις λέγεται, ἕνα μὲν τρόπον ἐὰν ᾖ πλῆθος ἔχον ὑπεροχὴν ἢ ἁπλῶς ἢ πρός τι (καὶ τὸ ὀλίγον ὡσαύτως πλῆθος ἔχον ἔλλειψιν), τὸ δὲ ὡς ἀριθμός, ὃ καὶ ἀντίκειται τῷ ἑνὶ μόνον. οὕτως γὰρ λέγομεν ἓν ἢ πολλά, ὥσπερ εἴ τις εἴποι ἓν καὶ ἕνα ἢ λευκὸν καὶ λευκά, καὶ τὰ μεμετρημένα πρὸς τὸ μέτρον · οὕτως καὶ τὰ πολλαπλάσια λέγεται· πολλὰ γὰρ ἕκαστος ὁ ἀριθμὸς ὅτι ἕνα καὶ ὅτι μετρητὸς ἑνὶ ἕκαστος, καὶ ὡς τὸ ἀντικείμενον τῷ ἑνί, οὐ τῷ ὀλίγῳ. οὕτω μὲν οὖν ἐστὶ πολλὰ καὶ τὰ δύο, ὡς δὲ πλῆθος ἔχον ὑπεροχὴν ἢ πρός τι ἢ ἁπλῶς οὐκ ἔστιν, ἀλλὰ πρῶτον.","kind":"passage"},{"id":"RAW_DAZY_BO_2:P0029","text":"ὀλίγα δʼ ἁπλῶς τὰ δύο· πλῆθος γάρ ἐστιν ἔλλειψιν ἔχον πρῶτον (διὸ καὶ οὐκ ὀρθῶς ἀπέστη Ἀναξαγόρας εἰπὼν ὅτι ὁμοῦ πάντα χρήματα ἦν ἄπειρα καὶ πλήθει καὶ μικρότητι, ἔδει δʼ εἰπεῖν ἀντὶ τοῦ καὶ μικρότητι καὶ ὀλιγότητι· οὐ γὰρ ἄπειρα), ἐπεὶ τὸ ὀλίγον οὐ διὰ τὸ ἕν, ὥσπερ τινές φασιν, ἀλλὰ διὰ τὰ δύο.—ἀντίκειται δὴ τὸ ἓν καὶ τὰ πολλὰ τὰ ἐν ἀριθμοῖς ὡς μέτρον μετρητῷ· ταῦτα δὲ ὡς τὰ πρός τι, ὅσα μὴ καθʼ αὑτὰ τῶν πρός τι. διῄρηται δʼ ἡμῖν ἐν ἄλλοις ὅτι διχῶς λέγεται τὰ πρός τι, τὰ μὲν ὡς ἐναντία, τὰ δʼ ὡς ἐπιστήμη πρὸς ἐπιστητόν, τῷ λέγεσθαί τι ἄλλο πρὸς αὐτό.  τὸ δὲ ἓν ἔλαττον εἶναι τινός, οἷον τοῖν δυοῖν, οὐδὲν κωλύει· οὐ γάρ, εἰ ἔλαττον, καὶ ὀλίγον.","kind":"passage"},{"id":"RAW_DAZY_BO_2:P0030","text":"τὸ δὲ πλῆθος οἷον γένος ἐστὶ τοῦ ἀριθμοῦ· ἔστι γὰρ ἀριθμὸς πλῆθος ἑνὶ μετρητόν, καὶ ἀντίκειταί πως τὸ ἓν καὶ ἀριθμός, οὐχ ὡς ἐναντίον ἀλλʼ ὥσπερ εἴρηται τῶν πρός τι ἔνια· ᾗ γὰρ μέτρον τὸ δὲ μετρητόν, ταύτῃ ἀντίκειται, διὸ οὐ πᾶν ὃ ἂν ᾖ ἓν ἀριθμός ἐστιν, οἷον εἴ τι ἀδιαίρετόν ἐστιν. ὁμοίως δὲ λεγομένη ἡ ἐπιστήμη πρὸς τὸ ἐπιστητὸν οὐχ ὁμοίως ἀποδίδωσιν. δόξειε μὲν γὰρ ἂν μέτρον ἡ ἐπιστήμη εἶναι τὸ δὲ ἐπιστητὸν τὸ μετρούμενον, συμβαίνει δὲ ἐπιστήμην μὲν πᾶσαν ἐπιστητὸν εἶναι τὸ δὲ ἐπιστητὸν μὴ πᾶν ἐπιστήμην, ὅτι τρόπον τινὰ ἡ ἐπιστήμη μετρεῖται τῷ ἐπιστητῷ. τὸ δὲ πλῆθος οὔτε τῷ ὀλίγῳ ἐναντίον—ἀλλὰ τούτῳ μὲν τὸ πολὺ ὡς ὑπερέχον πλῆθος ὑπερεχομένῳ πλήθει—οὔτε τῷ ἑνὶ πάντως· ἀλλὰ τὸ μὲν ὥσπερ εἴρηται, ὅτι διαιρετὸν τὸ δʼ ἀδιαίρετον, τὸ δʼ ὡς πρός τι ὥσπερ ἡ ἐπιστήμη ἐπιστητῷ, ἐὰν ᾖ ἀριθμὸς τὸ δʼ ἓν μέτρον.","kind":"passage"},{"id":"RAW_DAZY_BO_2:P0031","text":"ἐπεὶ δὲ τῶν ἐναντίων ἐνδέχεται εἶναί τι μεταξὺ καὶ ἐνίων ἔστιν, ἀνάγκη ἐκ τῶν ἐναντίων εἶναι τὰ μεταξύ. πάντα γὰρ τὰ μεταξὺ ἐν τῷ αὐτῷ γένει ἐστὶ καὶ ὧν ἐστὶ μεταξύ. μεταξὺ μὲν γὰρ ταῦτα λέγομεν εἰς ὅσα μεταβάλλειν ἀνάγκη πρότερον τὸ μεταβάλλον (οἷον ἀπὸ τῆς ὑπάτης ἐπὶ τὴν νήτην εἰ μεταβαίνοι τῷ ὀλιγίστῳ, ἥξει πρότερον εἰς τοὺς μεταξὺ φθόγγους, καὶ ἐν χρώμασιν εἰ  ἐκ τοῦ λευκοῦ εἰς τὸ μέλαν, πρότερον ἥξει εἰς τὸ φοινικοῦν καὶ φαιὸν ἢ εἰς τὸ μέλαν· ὁμοίως δὲ καὶ ἐπὶ τῶν ἄλλων)· μεταβάλλειν δʼ ἐξ ἄλλου γένους εἰς ἄλλο γένος οὐκ ἔστιν ἀλλʼ ἢ κατὰ συμβεβηκός, οἷον ἐκ χρώματος εἰς σχῆμα. ἀνάγκη ἄρα τὰ μεταξὺ καὶ αὑτοῖς καὶ ὧν μεταξύ εἰσιν ἐν τῷ αὐτῷ γένει εἶναι. ἀλλὰ μὴν πάντα γε τὰ μεταξύ ἐστιν ἀντικειμένων τινῶν· ἐκ τούτων γὰρ μόνων καθʼ αὑτὰ ἔστι μεταβάλλειν (διὸ ἀδύνατον εἶναι μεταξὺ μὴ ἀντικειμένων· εἴη γὰρ ἂν μεταβολὴ καὶ μὴ ἐξ ἀντικειμένων).","kind":"passage"},{"id":"RAW_DAZY_BO_2:P0032","text":"τῶν δʼ ἀντικειμένων ἀντιφάσεως μὲν οὐκ ἔστι μεταξύ (τοῦτο γάρ ἐστιν ἀντίφασις, ἀντίθεσις ἧς ὁτῳοῦν θάτερον μόριον πάρεστιν, οὐκ ἐχούσης οὐθὲν μεταξύ), τῶν δὲ λοιπῶν τὰ μὲν πρός τι τὰ δὲ στέρησις τὰ δὲ ἐναντία ἐστίν. τῶν δὲ πρός τι ὅσα μὴ ἐναντία, οὐκ ἔχει μεταξύ· αἴτιον δʼ ὅτι οὐκ ἐν τῷ αὐτῷ γένει ἐστίν.  τί γὰρ ἐπιστήμης καὶ ἐπιστητοῦ μεταξύ; ἀλλὰ μεγάλου καὶ μικροῦ. εἰ δʼ ἐστὶν ἐν ταὐτῷ γένει τὰ μεταξύ, ὥσπερ δέδεικται, καὶ μεταξὺ ἐναντίων, ἀνάγκη αὐτὰ συγκεῖσθαι ἐκ τούτων τῶν ἐναντίων. ἢ γὰρ ἔσται τι γένος αὐτῶν ἢ οὐθέν.","kind":"passage"},{"id":"RAW_DAZY_BO_2:P0033","text":"καὶ εἰ μὲν γένος ἔσται οὕτως ὥστʼ εἶναι πρότερόν τι τῶν ἐναντίων, αἱ διαφοραὶ πρότεραι ἐναντίαι ἔσονται αἱ ποιήσουσαι τὰ ἐναντία εἴδη ὡς γένους· ἐκ γὰρ τοῦ γένους καὶ τῶν διαφορῶν τὰ εἴδη (οἷον εἰ τὸ λευκὸν καὶ μέλαν ἐναντία, ἔστι δὲ τὸ μὲν διακριτικὸν χρῶμα τὸ δὲ συγκριτικὸν χρῶμα, αὗται αἱ διαφοραί, τὸ διακριτικὸν καὶ συγκριτικόν, πρότεραι· ὥστε ταῦτα ἐναντία ἀλλήλοις πρότερα).","kind":"passage"},{"id":"RAW_DAZY_BO_2:P0034","text":"ἀλλὰ μὴν τά γε ἐναντίως διαφέροντα μᾶλλον ἐναντίἀ· καὶ τὰ λοιπὰ καὶ τὰ μεταξὺ ἐκ τοῦ γένους ἔσται καὶ τῶν διαφορῶν (οἷον ὅσα χρώματα τοῦ λευκοῦ καὶ μέλανός ἐστι μεταξύ, ταῦτα δεῖ ἔκ τε τοῦ γένους λέγεσθαι —ἔστι δὲ γένος τὸ χρῶμα—καὶ ἐκ διαφορῶν τινῶν· αὗται δὲ οὐκ ἔσονται τὰ πρῶτα ἐναντία· εἰ δὲ μή, ἔσται ἕκαστον ἢ λευκὸν ἢ μέλαν· ἕτεραι ἄρα· μεταξὺ ἄρα τῶν πρώτων ἐναντίων αὗται ἔσονται, αἱ πρῶται δὲ διαφοραὶ τὸ διακριτικὸν καὶ συγκριτικόν)· ὥστε ταῦτα πρῶτα ζητητέον ὅσα ἐναντία μὴ ἐν γένει, ἐκ τίνος τὰ μεταξὺ αὐτῶν (ἀνάγκη γὰρ τὰ ἐν τῷ αὐτῷ γένει ἐκ τῶν ἀσυνθέτων τῷ γένει συγκεῖσθαι ἢ ἀσύνθετα εἶναι). τὰ μὲν οὖν ἐναντία ἀσύνθετα ἐξ ἀλλήλων, ὥστε ἀρχαί· τὰ δὲ μεταξὺ ἢ πάντα ἢ οὐθέν. ἐκ δὲ τῶν ἐναντίων γίγνεταί τι, ὥστʼ ἔσται μεταβολὴ εἰς τοῦτο πρὶν ἢ εἰς αὐτά· ἑκατέρου γὰρ καὶ ἧττον ἔσται καὶ μᾶλλον. μεταξὺ ἄρα ἔσται καὶ τοῦτο τῶν ἐναντίων.","kind":"passage"},{"id":"RAW_DAZY_BO_2:P0035","text":"καὶ τἆλλα ἄρα πάντα σύνθετα τὰ μεταξύ· τὸ γὰρ τοῦ μὲν μᾶλλον τοῦ δʼ ἧττον σύνθετόν πως ἐξ ἐκείνων ὧν λέγεται εἶναι τοῦ μὲν μᾶλλον τοῦ δʼ ἧττον. ἐπεὶ δʼ οὐκ ἔστιν ἕτερα πρότερα ὁμογενῆ τῶν ἐναντίων, ἅπαντʼ ἂν ἐκ τῶν ἐναντίων εἴη τὰ μεταξύ, ὥστε καὶ τὰ κάτω πάντα, καὶ τἀναντία καὶ τὰ μεταξύ, ἐκ τῶν πρώτων ἐναντίων ἔσονται. ὅτι μὲν οὖν τὰ μεταξὺ ἔν τε ταὐτῷ γένει πάντα καὶ μεταξὺ ἐναντίων καὶ σύγκειται ἐκ τῶν ἐναντίων πάντα, δῆλον.","kind":"passage"},{"id":"RAW_DAZY_BO_2:P0036","text":"τὸ δʼ ἕτερον τῷ εἴδει τινὸς τὶ ἕτερόν ἐστι, καὶ δεῖ τοῦτο ἀμφοῖν ὑπάρχειν· οἷον εἰ ζῷον ἕτερον τῷ εἴδει, ἄμφω ζῷα. ἀνάγκη ἄρα ἐν γένει τῷ αὐτῷ εἶναι τὰ ἕτερα τῷ εἴδει· τὸ γὰρ τοιοῦτο γένος καλῶ ὃ ἄμφω ἓν ταὐτὸ λέγεται, μὴ κατὰ συμβεβηκὸς ἔχον διαφοράν,  εἴτε ὡς ὕλη ὂν εἴτε ἄλλως. οὐ μόνον γὰρ δεῖ τὸ κοινὸν ὑπάρχειν, οἷον ἄμφω ζῷα, ἀλλὰ καὶ ἕτερον ἑκατέρῳ τοῦτο αὐτὸ τὸ ζῷον, οἷον τὸ μὲν ἵππον τὸ δὲ ἄνθρωπον, διὸ τοῦτο τὸ κοινὸν ἕτερον ἀλλήλων ἐστὶ τῷ εἴδει. ἔσται δὴ καθʼ αὑτὰ τὸ μὲν τοιονδὶ ζῷον τὸ δὲ τοιονδί, οἷον τὸ μὲν ἵππος τὸ δʼ ἄνθρωπος. ἀνάγκη ἄρα τὴν διαφορὰν ταύτην ἑτερότητα τοῦ γένους εἶναι. λέγω γὰρ γένους διαφορὰν ἑτερότητα ἣ ἕτερον ποιεῖ τοῦτο αὐτό.","kind":"passage"},{"id":"RAW_DAZY_BO_2:P0037","text":"ἐναντίωσις τοίνυν ἔσται αὕτη (δῆλον δὲ καὶ ἐκ τῆς ἐπαγωγῆς)· πάντα γὰρ διαιρεῖται τοῖς ἀντικειμένοις, καὶ ὅτι τὰ ἐναντία ἐν ταὐτῷ γένει, δέδεικται· ἡ γὰρ ἐναντιότης ἦν διαφορὰ τελεία, ἡ δὲ διαφορὰ ἡ εἴδει πᾶσα τινὸς τί, ὥστε τοῦτο τὸ αὐτό τε καὶ γένος ἐπʼ ἀμφοῖν (διὸ καὶ ἐν τῇ αὐτῇ συστοιχίᾳ πάντα τὰ ἐναντία τῆς κατηγορίας ὅσα εἴδει διάφορα καὶ μὴ γένει, ἕτερά τε ἀλλήλων μάλιστα—τελεία γὰρ ἡ διαφορά—καὶ ἅμα ἀλλήλοις οὐ γίγνεται). ἡ ἄρα διαφορὰ ἐναντίωσίς ἐστιν.","kind":"passage"},{"id":"RAW_DAZY_BO_2:P0038","text":"τοῦτο ἄρα ἐστὶ τὸ ἑτέροις εἶναι τῷ εἴδει, τὸ ἐν ταὐτῷ γένει ὄντα ἐναντίωσιν ἔχειν ἄτομα ὄντα (ταὐτὰ δὲ τῷ εἴδει ὅσα μὴ ἔχει ἐναντίωσιν ἄτομα ὄντα)· ἐν γὰρ τῇ διαιρέσει καὶ ἐν τοῖς μεταξὺ γίγνονται ἐναντιώσεις πρὶν εἰς τὰ ἄτομα ἐλθεῖν· ὥστε φανερὸν ὅτι πρὸς τὸ καλούμενον γένος οὔτε ταὐτὸν οὔτε ἕτερον τῷ εἴδει οὐθέν ἐστι τῶν ὡς γένους εἰδῶν (προσηκόντως· ἡ γὰρ ὕλη ἀποφάσει δηλοῦται, τὸ δὲ γένος ὕλη οὗ λέγεται γένος—μὴ ὡς τὸ τῶν Ἡρακλειδῶν ἀλλʼ ὡς τὸ ἐν τῇ φύσει), οὐδὲ πρὸς τὰ μὴ ἐν ταὐτῷ γένει, ἀλλὰ διοίσει τῷ γένει ἐκείνων, εἴδει δὲ τῶν ἐν ταὐτῷ γένει. ἐναντίωσιν γὰρ ἀνάγκη εἶναι τὴν διαφορὰν οὗ διαφέρει εἴδει· αὕτη δὲ ὑπάρχει τοῖς ἐν ταὐτῷ γένει οὖσι μόνοις.","kind":"passage"},{"id":"RAW_DAZY_BO_2:P0039","text":"ἀπορήσειε δʼ ἄν τις διὰ τί γυνὴ ἀνδρὸς οὐκ εἴδει διαφέρει, ἐναντίου τοῦ θήλεος καὶ τοῦ ἄρρενος ὄντος τῆς δὲ διαφορᾶς ἐναντιώσεως, οὐδὲ ζῷον θῆλυ καὶ ἄρρεν ἕτερον τῷ εἴδει· καίτοι καθʼ αὑτὸ τοῦ ζῴου αὕτη ἡ διαφορὰ καὶ οὐχ ὡς λευκότης ἢ μελανία ἀλλʼ ᾗ ζῷον καὶ τὸ θῆλυ καὶ τὸ ἄρρεν ὑπάρχει. ἔστι δʼ ἡ ἀπορία αὕτη σχεδὸν ἡ αὐτὴ καὶ διὰ τί ἡ μὲν ποιεῖ τῷ εἴδει ἕτερα ἐναντίωσις ἡ δʼ οὔ, οἷον τὸ πεζὸν καὶ τὸ πτερωτόν, λευκότης δὲ καὶ μελανία οὔ. ἢ ὅτι τὰ μὲν οἰκεῖα πάθη τοῦ γένους τὰ δʼ ἧττον; καὶ ἐπειδή ἐστι τὸ μὲν λόγος τὸ δʼ ὕλη,  ὅσαι μὲν ἐν τῷ λόγῳ εἰσὶν ἐναντιότητες εἴδει ποιοῦσι διαφοράν, ὅσαι δʼ ἐν τῷ συνειλημμένῳ τῇ ὕλῃ οὐ ποιοῦσιν. διὸ ἀνθρώπου λευκότης οὐ ποιεῖ οὐδὲ μελανία, οὐδὲ τοῦ λευκοῦ ἀνθρώπου ἔστι διαφορὰ κατʼ εἶδος πρὸς μέλανα ἄνθρωπον, οὐδʼ ἂν ὄνομα ἓν τεθῇ.","kind":"passage"},{"id":"RAW_DAZY_BO_2:P0040","text":"ὡς ὕλη γὰρ ὁ ἄνθρωπος, οὐ ποιεῖ δὲ διαφορὰν ἡ ὕλη· οὐδʼ ἀνθρώπου γὰρ εἴδη εἰσὶν οἱ ἄνθρωποι διὰ τοῦτο, καίτοι ἕτεραι αἱ σάρκες καὶ τὰ ὀστᾶ ἐξ ὧν ὅδε καὶ ὅδε· ἀλλὰ τὸ σύνολον ἕτερον μέν, εἴδει δʼ οὐχ ἕτερον, ὅτι ἐν τῷ λόγῳ οὐκ ἔστιν ἐναντίωσις. τοῦτο δʼ ἐστὶ τὸ ἔσχατον ἄτομον· ὁ δὲ Καλλίας ἐστὶν ὁ λόγος μετὰ τῆς ὕλης· καὶ ὁ λευκὸς δὴ ἄνθρωπος, ὅτι Καλλίας λευκός· κατὰ συμβεβηκὸς οὖν ὁ ἄνθρωπος. οὐδὲ χαλκοῦς δὴ κύκλος καὶ ξύλινος· οὐδὲ τρίγωνον χαλκοῦν καὶ κύκλος ξύλινος, οὐ διὰ τὴν ὕλην εἴδει διαφέρουσιν ἀλλʼ ὅτι ἐν τῷ λόγῳ ἔνεστιν ἐναντίωσις. πότερον δʼ ἡ ὕλη οὐ ποιεῖ ἕτερα τῷ εἴδει, οὖσά πως ἑτέρα, ἢ ἔστιν ὡς ποιεῖ; διὰ τί γὰρ ὁδὶ ὁ ἵππος τουδὶ τοῦ ἀνθρώπου ἕτερος τῷ εἴδει; καίτοι σὺν τῇ ὕλῃ οἱ λόγοι αὐτῶν.","kind":"passage"},{"id":"RAW_DAZY_BO_2:P0041","text":"ἢ ὅτι ἔνεστιν ἐν τῷ λόγῳ ἐναντίωσις; καὶ γὰρ τοῦ λευκοῦ ἀνθρώπου καὶ μέλανος ἵππου, καὶ ἔστι γε εἴδει, ἀλλʼ οὐχ ᾗ ὁ μὲν λευκὸς ὁ δὲ μέλας, ἐπεὶ καὶ εἰ ἄμφω λευκὰ ἦν, ὅμως ἂν ἦν εἴδει ἕτερα. τὸ δὲ ἄρρεν καὶ θῆλυ τοῦ ζῴου οἰκεῖα μὲν πάθη, ἀλλʼ οὐ κατὰ τὴν οὐσίαν ἀλλʼ ἐν τῇ ὕλῃ καὶ τῷ σώματι, διὸ τὸ αὐτὸ σπέρμα θῆλυ ἢ ἄρρεν γίγνεται παθόν τι πάθος. τί μὲν οὖν ἐστὶ τὸ τῷ εἴδει ἕτερον εἶναι, καὶ διὰ τί τὰ μὲν διαφέρει εἴδει τὰ δʼ οὔ, εἴρηται.","kind":"passage"},{"id":"RAW_DAZY_BO_2:P0042","text":"ἐπειδὴ δὲ τὰ ἐναντία ἕτερα τῷ εἴδει, τὸ δὲ φθαρτὸν καὶ τὸ ἄφθαρτον ἐναντία (στέρησις γὰρ ἀδυναμία διωρισμένη), ἀνάγκη ἕτερον εἶναι τῷ γένει τὸ φθαρτὸν καὶ τὸ ἄφθαρτον. νῦν μὲν οὖν ἐπʼ αὐτῶν εἰρήκαμεν τῶν καθόλου ὀνομάτων, ὥστε δόξειεν ἂν οὐκ ἀναγκαῖον εἶναι ὁτιοῦν ἄφθαρτον καὶ φθαρτὸν ἕτερα εἶναι τῷ εἴδει, ὥσπερ οὐδὲ λευκὸν καὶ μέλαν (τὸ γὰρ αὐτὸ ἐνδέχεται εἶναι, καὶ ἅμα, ἐὰν ᾖ τῶν καθόλου, ὥσπερ ὁ ἄνθρωπος εἴη ἂν καὶ λευκὸς καὶ μέλας, καὶ τῶν καθʼ ἕκαστον· εἴη γὰρ ἄν, μὴ ἅμα, ὁ αὐτὸς λευκὸς καὶ μέλας· καίτοι ἐναντίον τὸ λευκὸν τῷ μέλανι)· ἀλλὰ τῶν ἐναντίων τὰ μὲν κατὰ συμβεβηκὸς ὑπάρχει ἐνίοις, οἷον καὶ τὰ νῦν εἰρημένα καὶ ἄλλα πολλά, τὰ δὲ ἀδύνατον, ὧν ἐστὶ καὶ τὸ φθαρτὸν καὶ τὸ ἄφθαρτον·  οὐδὲν γάρ ἐστι φθαρτὸν κατὰ συμβεβηκός· τὸ μὲν γὰρ συμβεβηκὸς ἐνδέχεται μὴ ὑπάρχειν, τὸ δὲ φθαρτὸν τῶν ἐξ ἀνάγκης ὑπαρχόντων ἐστὶν οἷς ὑπάρχει· ἢ ἔσται τὸ αὐτὸ καὶ ἓν φθαρτὸν καὶ ἄφθαρτον, εἰ","kind":"passage"},{"id":"RAW_DAZY_BO_2:P0043","text":"ἐνδέχεται μὴ ὑπάρχειν αὐτῷ τὸ φθαρτόν. ἢ τὴν οὐσίαν ἄρα ἢ ἐν τῇ οὐσίᾳ ἀνάγκη ὑπάρχειν τὸ φθαρτὸν ἑκάστῳ τῶν φθαρτῶν. ὁ δʼ αὐτὸς λόγος καὶ περὶ τοῦ ἀφθάρτου· τῶν γὰρ ἐξ ἀνάγκης ὑπαρχόντων ἄμφω. ᾗ ἄρα καὶ καθʼ ὃ πρῶτον τὸ μὲν φθαρτὸν τὸ δʼ ἄφθαρτον, ἔχει ἀντίθεσιν, ὥστε ἀνάγκη γένει ἕτερα εἶναι. φανερὸν τοίνυν ὅτι οὐκ ἐνδέχεται εἶναι εἴδη τοιαῦτα οἷα λέγουσί τινες· ἔσται γὰρ καὶ ἄνθρωπος ὁ μὲν φθαρτὸς ὁ δʼ ἄφθαρτος. καίτοι τῷ εἴδει ταὐτὰ λέγεται εἶναι τὰ εἴδη τοῖς τισὶ καὶ οὐχ ὁμώνυμα· τὰ δὲ γένει ἕτερα πλεῖον διέστηκεν ἢ τὰ εἴδει.","kind":"passage"}],"sha256":"75b2bd02fdf731239fb49e1c5fa5712ba347a401fe739c86fe777b54212563b4","language_code":"grc","language_name":"Ἑλληνική","translations":[{"id":"SOURCE_TR_20261015T173454559976Z_9e0acb5f","language_code":"en","language_name":"English","title":"Book 10","source_sha256":"75b2bd02fdf731239fb49e1c5fa5712ba347a401fe739c86fe777b54212563b4","sha256":"393eb90506cfb4bd5c673e0a8302922ffdfc85e4dd8053f461c4309358dae89f","rendition_type":"machine","review_status":"machine","model":"gpt-5.6-terra","created":"2026-10-15T17:34:54.559993+00:00","paragraphs":[{"id":"RAW_DAZY_BO_2:P0001","text":"That “one” is spoken of in many ways has been stated previously in the divisions concerning the ways in which things are spoken of. Of what is spoken of in several ways, there are four comprehensive modes of the primary and per se senses of “one,” but not the accidental senses. For the continuous, either simply, or especially that which is so by nature and not by contact or by binding, is one; and among these, that is more one and prior whose motion is more indivisible and more simple. Further, such, and more so, is the whole which has some shape and form, especially if it is such by nature and not by force, as are things held together by glue, nail, or bond, but has in itself the cause of its own being continuous. Such a thing is so because its motion is one and indivisible in place and time; so that it is clear that, if something has by nature a principle of the first motion which is itself first—for example, I mean the circular motion of locomotion—this is the first one magnitude.","kind":"passage","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_DAZY_BO_2:P0001"]},{"id":"RAW_DAZY_BO_2:P0002","text":"Things, then, are one in this way insofar as they are continuous or whole; others are one when their account is one, and such are those whose thought is one; and such are those which are indivisible, and indivisible either in form or in number. In number, the particular is indivisible; in form, that which is indivisible for knowledge and science. Hence that which is the cause of unity for substances would be primary one. Thus “one” is spoken of in so many ways: the naturally continuous, the whole, the particular, and the universal; but all these are one by being indivisible—some in motion, others in thought or in account.\n\n—But one must observe that it is not in the same way that one should take what sorts of things are called one, and what being one is, and what its account is.","kind":"passage","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_DAZY_BO_2:P0002"]},{"id":"RAW_DAZY_BO_2:P0003","text":"For “one” is spoken of in so many ways, and each of those things will be one to which one of these modes belongs. But being one will sometimes be one of these, and sometimes something else which is even nearer to the name, while those things are one in potency; just as, concerning element and cause, if one had to speak both by distinguishing among the things and by giving the definition of the name. For fire is in one way an element (and perhaps the unlimited, or something else of this sort, is also so in itself), but in another way it is not; for being fire and being an element are not the same. Rather, as a certain thing and nature, fire is an element; but the name signifies that this has happened to belong to it, namely, that something is from it as from a first constituent.","kind":"passage","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_DAZY_BO_2:P0003"]},{"id":"RAW_DAZY_BO_2:P0004","text":"So too in the case of cause and one and all such things. Hence being one is being indivisible, namely, being a this and separately distinct either in place or in form or in thought; or also being whole and indivisible. But above all it is being the first measure of each genus, and most properly of quantity; for from this it has extended to the other cases. For a measure is that by which quantity is known; and quantity qua quantity is known either by one or by number, while every number is known by one. Hence every quantity is known qua quantity by one, and that by which quantities are first known is itself one. Therefore one is the principle of number qua number.","kind":"passage","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_DAZY_BO_2:P0004"]},{"id":"RAW_DAZY_BO_2:P0005","text":"From this, too, in other cases that by which each thing is first known is called a measure, and the measure of each thing is one: in length, breadth, depth, weight, and speed. (For weight and speed are common to contraries; for each of them is twofold: weight is both whatever has any inclination and what has an excess of inclination; and speed is both whatever has any motion and what has an excess of motion. For there is a certain speed even of the slow, and weight of what is lighter.) In all these cases, then, the measure and principle is something one and indivisible, since even in lines they employ the foot as an indivisible unit. For everywhere they seek as measure something one and indivisible; and this is what is simple either in quality or in quantity.","kind":"passage","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_DAZY_BO_2:P0005"]},{"id":"RAW_DAZY_BO_2:P0006","text":"Where it seems impossible to subtract or add, there the measure is exact (and for this reason that of number is most exact; for they posit the unit as everywhere indivisible). In other cases they imitate such a measure. For from a stadium, a talent, and always from the larger thing, an addition or subtraction may escape notice more readily than from a smaller thing. Thus that from which, first, something is impossible according to perception, all make the measure both of liquids and solids, and of weight and magnitude; and then they think they know the quantity when they know it through this measure. And indeed they measure motion by simple and by swiftest motion (for this has the least time). Hence in astronomy such a one is principle and measure (for they posit the motion of heaven as uniform and swiftest, and judge the others in relation to it); and in music, the diesis, because it is least; and in voice, the element.","kind":"passage","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_DAZY_BO_2:P0006"]},{"id":"RAW_DAZY_BO_2:P0007","text":"And all these are one in this way, not as though one were something common, but as has been said.—Yet the measure is not always one in number, but sometimes several: for example, there are two dieses, not according to hearing but in the ratios; and there are several sounds by which we measure; and the diagonal and the side are measured by two, and so are all magnitudes. Thus one is the measure of all things, because we recognize from what things the substance is composed by dividing either according to quantity or according to form. And for this reason one is indivisible, because the first of each thing is indivisible. But not every indivisible is so in the same way, as foot and unit are; rather, the one is so in every respect, while the other must be posited as indivisible relative to perception, as has already been said; for perhaps every continuous thing is divisible. But the measure is always connatural: for a magnitude is the measure of magnitudes, and, in each case, length of length, breadth of breadth, sound of sound, weight of weight, unit of units.","kind":"passage","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_DAZY_BO_2:P0007"]},{"id":"RAW_DAZY_BO_2:P0008","text":"For this is how it must be taken, and not that number is the measure of numbers; though it ought to be, if it were in the same way. But it is not regarded as being in the same way, but as if one were to hold units to be the measure of units, and not a unit; whereas number is a multitude of units. We also call science and perception measures of things for the same reason, because we recognize something by them, though they are measured rather than measure. But it happens to us as if, while another was measuring us, we recognized how large we are by his applying the cubit to us so many times. Protagoras says that man is the measure of all things, as if he had said the knower or the perceiver; and these because the one has perception and the other science, which we say are measures of the underlying things. Thus, while saying nothing superfluous, they seem to say something remarkable.","kind":"passage","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_DAZY_BO_2:P0008"]},{"id":"RAW_DAZY_BO_2:P0009","text":"It is clear, then, that being one is, especially when one defines according to the name, being some measure, and most properly a measure of quantity, then of quality. Such a measure will be what is indivisible according to quantity in one case, and what is indivisible according to quality in another. Hence one is indivisible either simply or qua one.","kind":"passage","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_DAZY_BO_2:P0009"]},{"id":"RAW_DAZY_BO_2:P0010","text":"But with respect to substance and nature, one must inquire how it stands, just as in the puzzles we considered what one is and how one must take it: whether one itself is some substance, as the Pythagoreans first say and Plato later; or whether rather some nature underlies it and one must speak more intelligibly and more as the natural philosophers do. For one of them says that one is friendship, another air, another the unlimited. If, then, none of the universals can be substance, as has been said in the discussions concerning substance and being, nor can this itself be substance as some one thing apart from the many (for it is common), but only a predicate, it is clear that neither can one be substance; for being and one are predicated most universally of all. Hence neither are genera certain natures and substances separate from other things, nor can one be a genus, for the same reasons for which neither being nor substance can be.","kind":"passage","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_DAZY_BO_2:P0010"]},{"id":"RAW_DAZY_BO_2:P0011","text":"Further, it must be alike in all cases; and being and one are spoken of in equal numbers of ways. Therefore, since among qualities there is some one thing and some nature, and similarly among quantities, it is clear that one must inquire generally what one is, just as one inquires what being is, since it is not sufficient that this itself is its nature. Yet among colours there is one colour, for example white; then the other colours appear to arise from this and black, and black is a privation of white, just as darkness is of light. Hence, if beings were colours, beings would be some number—but of what? Clearly, of colours; and one would be some one thing, for example white. Similarly, if beings were musical intervals, they would be a number, though a number of dieses; but their substance would not be number, and one would be something whose substance is not one but a diesis. Similarly, in the case of tones, beings would be a number of elements, and one would be a vowel-element. And if beings were rectilinear figures, they would be a number of figures, and one would be the triangle.","kind":"passage","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_DAZY_BO_2:P0011"]},{"id":"RAW_DAZY_BO_2:P0012","text":"The same account applies also to the other genera. Thus, if in affections, qualities, quantities, and motion there are numbers and some one in all cases, and the number is a number of certain things and one is some one thing, but its substance is not this itself, it is necessary that the case should be the same with substances as well; for it is alike in all cases.—It is clear, then, that in every genus one is some nature, and that one itself is not the nature of any of them; but just as among colours one must seek one colour, one itself, so among substances one must seek one substance, one itself. That one and being signify in a way the same thing is clear both from their following the categories in equal numbers of ways and from their not being in any one of them (for example, neither in what-it-is nor in what-sort, but they stand as being does), and from “one man” not additionally predicating anything over “man” (just as neither does being over what or what-sort or how-much), and from being one being being each thing.","kind":"passage","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_DAZY_BO_2:P0012"]},{"id":"RAW_DAZY_BO_2:P0013","text":"One and many are opposed in several ways, one of which is one and plurality as indivisible and divisible. For what is either divided or divisible is called a plurality, while what is indivisible or not divided is called one. Since oppositions are fourfold, and one of these is spoken of according to privation, they would be contraries, and neither as contradiction nor as relative terms. One is spoken of and made clear from its contrary, the indivisible from the divisible, because plurality and the divisible are more perceptible than the indivisible; hence, in account, plurality is prior to the indivisible because of perception. To one belong, as we set out in the division of contraries, the same, like, and equal; to plurality belong the other, unlike, and unequal.","kind":"passage","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_DAZY_BO_2:P0013"]},{"id":"RAW_DAZY_BO_2:P0014","text":"Since “the same” is spoken of in many ways, in one way we sometimes call something the same in number; in another, when it is one both in account and in number, as you are one with yourself both in form and in matter; and further, when the account of the primary substance is one, as equal straight lines are the same, and equal and equiangular squares, though they are many; but in these cases equality is unity. Things are like when, not being the same simply, nor undifferentiated in their composite substance, they are the same in form, as a larger square is like a smaller one, and unequal straight lines are like; for these are like, but not simply the same. Things are also called like when, having the same form in which more and less arise, they are neither more nor less. And when they have the same affection, one in form, for example whiteness, in a high degree and in a lesser degree, they are said to be like because their form is one. And things are like when they have more things the same than different, either simply or the conspicuous things, as tin is like silver insofar as it is white, and gold is like fire insofar as it is yellow and reddish.","kind":"passage","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_DAZY_BO_2:P0014"]},{"id":"RAW_DAZY_BO_2:P0015","text":"Hence it is clear that “other” and “unlike” too are spoken of in many ways. “Other” is opposed to “same,” and therefore everything is either the same as or other than everything. Another sense applies when both matter and account are not one, and therefore you and your neighbour are other. A third is as in mathematical objects. Thus other or same is said of everything in relation to everything for this reason: of all things that are called one and being. For other is not the contradictory of same, and hence is not said of non-beings (though not-same is said of them), but is said of all beings; for whatever is naturally being and one is either one or not one. Thus other and same are opposed in this way; but difference and otherness are different. For the other, and that of which it is other, need not be other than something; for everything whatsoever that is a being is either other or same. But what is different is different from something in respect of something; hence there must be something the same in respect of which they differ.","kind":"passage","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_DAZY_BO_2:P0015"]},{"id":"RAW_DAZY_BO_2:P0016","text":"This same thing is genus or form; for everything that differs differs either in genus or in form. Things differ in genus when they have neither common matter nor generation into one another, for example things whose category has a different figure; they differ in form when they have the same genus (and genus is called that which both different things are said to be the same in according to substance). Contraries are different, and contrariety is a kind of difference. That we rightly posit this is clear from induction; for all things which differ appear to differ in these ways as well—not merely being other, but some being other in genus and others being in the same coordinate series of a category, so that they are in the same genus and the same in genus. What things are the same or other in genus has been determined elsewhere.","kind":"passage","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_DAZY_BO_2:P0016"]},{"id":"RAW_DAZY_BO_2:P0017","text":"Since things which differ can differ from one another more or less, there is also a greatest difference, and this I call contrariety. That it is the greatest difference is clear from induction. Things differing in genus have no way into one another, but are more distant and incomparable; whereas for things differing in form, generations are from contraries as extremes, and the interval between extremes is greatest; hence so too is that between contraries. But the greatest in each genus is complete. For greatest is that of which there is no excess, and complete is that beyond which there is nothing possible to take; for complete difference has an end (just as other things too are called complete by having an end), and outside the end there is nothing. For it is ultimate in every case and encompasses; hence there is nothing outside the end, nor does the complete need anything.","kind":"passage","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_DAZY_BO_2:P0017"]},{"id":"RAW_DAZY_BO_2:P0018","text":"That contrariety is complete difference is clear from these considerations. But since contraries are spoken of in many ways, their being complete will follow in the manner in which their being contraries belongs to them. This being so, it is clear that one thing cannot have more than one contrary (for neither could there be anything more extreme than the extreme, nor more than two extremes of one interval); and, generally, if contrariety is difference, and difference is between two things, then so too is complete difference. The other definitions of contraries must also be true.","kind":"passage","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_DAZY_BO_2:P0018"]},{"id":"RAW_DAZY_BO_2:P0019","text":"For complete difference differs most (for among things differing in genus there is nothing further beyond to take, and likewise among things differing in form; for it has been shown that there is no difference in relation to things outside the genus, and of the differences in question this is greatest); and things in the same genus which differ most are contraries (for their greatest difference is complete); and things in the same recipient which differ most are contraries (for the matter is the same for contraries); and things under the same capacity which differ most are contraries (for the one science too concerns one genus): in all these cases complete difference is greatest.—The primary contrariety is state and privation; but not every privation (for privation is spoken of in many ways), but that which is complete. The other contraries will be spoken of in accordance with these: some because they have them, some because they do or are productive of them, and some because they are acquisitions and losses of these or of other contraries.","kind":"passage","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_DAZY_BO_2:P0019"]},{"id":"RAW_DAZY_BO_2:P0020","text":"If, then, contradiction, privation, contrariety, and relative terms are opposites, and of these contradiction is primary; if there is no intermediate in contradiction, while there can be one between contraries, it is clear that contradiction and contraries are not the same. Privation is a kind of contradiction: for either what is altogether incapable of having something, or what is naturally fitted to have it but does not have it, is deprived, either absolutely or in some determinate respect (for we speak of this in many ways already, as has been divided by us elsewhere). Hence privation is a kind of contradiction, or an incapacity determined or taken together with the recipient. Therefore there is no intermediate in contradiction, but there is one in a certain privation; for everything is either equal or not equal, but not everything is equal or unequal—except, if so, only in what is receptive of equality.","kind":"passage","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_DAZY_BO_2:P0020"]},{"id":"RAW_DAZY_BO_2:P0021","text":"If, then, generations in matter are from contraries, and come to be either from the form and the possession of the form, or from some privation of the form and shape, it is clear that every contrariety would be a privation, but perhaps not every privation a contrariety (the reason being that what is deprived may be deprived in many ways). For the things from which the changes are ultimate are contraries. This is also clear by induction. For every contrariety has a privation in one of the contraries, though not all alike: inequality is a privation of equality, unlikeness of likeness, and vice of virtue; but they differ, as has been said. For in one case it is enough if it is merely deprived, in another if it is deprived either at some time or in something, for example at a certain age or in the principal subject, or altogether. Hence in some cases there is an intermediate, and a man can be neither good nor bad; but in others there is none, and it is necessary to be either odd or even. Again, some have their underlying subject determined, while others do not.","kind":"passage","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_DAZY_BO_2:P0021"]},{"id":"RAW_DAZY_BO_2:P0022","text":"Thus it is clear that one of the contraries is always spoken of according to privation; and it is sufficient to consider even the primary contraries and the genera of contraries, such as the one and the many; for the others are reduced to these.","kind":"passage","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_DAZY_BO_2:P0022"]},{"id":"RAW_DAZY_BO_2:P0023","text":"Since one thing is contrary to one thing, one might raise a difficulty as to how the one and the many are opposed, and the equal to the great and the small. For if we always use “which of the two?” in reference to things in opposition—for example, “which, white or black?” and “which, white or not-white?” (but we do not say “which, man or white?”, unless by hypothesis and in inquiry, as, “which came, Cleon or Socrates?”—but this is not necessary in any genus; rather, this too has come from that source. For only opposites cannot belong simultaneously; and this is what is used here too in “which came?” For if both could belong simultaneously, the question would be ridiculous; but if they cannot, then in this way too it similarly falls under opposition, under “one or many,” for example, “which came, both or one of the two?”)—if, then, inquiry by “which of the two?” is always concerned with opposites, and yet one says “which, greater or less or equal?”, what is the opposition of the equal to these? For it is contrary neither to one of the two alone nor to both; for why rather to the greater than to the less? Again, the equal is contrary to the unequal, so that it will be contrary to more than one thing, rather than to one. But if the unequal signifies the same thing as both together, it would indeed be opposed to both (and the difficulty supports those who say that the unequal is a dyad), but it follows that one thing is contrary to two—which is impossible. Again, the equal appears to be intermediate between great and small, but no contrariety either appears to have an intermediate or can do so from the definition; for it would not be complete if it were intermediate between something, but rather always has something intermediate between itself. It remains, then, that it is opposed either as a negation or as a privation. Now it cannot be the negation of one of the two (for why rather of great than of small?); therefore it is a privative negation of both, and hence “which of the two?” is said in relation to both, but not in relation to one of them (for example, “which, greater or equal?”, or “which, equal or less?”), but always three.","kind":"passage","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_DAZY_BO_2:P0023"]},{"id":"RAW_DAZY_BO_2:P0024","text":"equal, so that it will be contrary to more than one thing, rather than to one. But if the unequal signifies the same thing as both together, it would indeed be opposed to both (and the difficulty supports those who say that the unequal is a dyad), but it follows that one thing is contrary to two—which is impossible. Again, the equal appears to be intermediate between great and small, but no contrariety either appears to have an intermediate or can do so from the definition; for it would not be complete if it were intermediate between something, but rather always has something intermediate between itself. It remains, then, that it is opposed either as a negation or as a privation. Now it cannot be the negation of one of the two (for why rather of great than of small?); therefore it is a privative negation of both, and hence “which of the two?” is said in relation to both, but not in relation to one of them (for example, “which, greater or equal?”, or “which, equal or less?”), but always three.","kind":"passage","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_DAZY_BO_2:P0024"]},{"id":"RAW_DAZY_BO_2:P0025","text":"But it is not necessarily a privation; for not everything which is neither greater nor less is equal, but only among things naturally fitted for those predicates.—The equal, then, is that which is neither great nor small, but is naturally fitted to be either great or small; and it is opposed to both as a privative negation, and hence is intermediate. And that which is neither good nor bad is opposed to both, but is unnamed; for each is spoken of in many ways, and the receptive subject is not one; but rather it is that which is neither white nor black. Yet not even this is spoken of as one thing, but the things of which this negation is spoken privatively are in some way determined; for it must be grey or sallow or something else of that sort. Thus those are not right who think that all things are spoken of alike, so that there will be an intermediate between a shoe and a hand, namely that which is neither shoe nor hand, since that which is neither good nor bad is intermediate between good and bad, as though there were to be something intermediate in every case. But this does not necessarily follow.","kind":"passage","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_DAZY_BO_2:P0025"]},{"id":"RAW_DAZY_BO_2:P0026","text":"For the joint negation of opposites applies to things for which there is some intermediate and which are naturally such as to have some interval; but in the other cases there is no difference. For the things whose joint negations they are belong to another genus, so that the underlying subject is not one.","kind":"passage","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_DAZY_BO_2:P0026"]},{"id":"RAW_DAZY_BO_2:P0027","text":"Similarly, one might raise a difficulty concerning the one and the many. For if the many are opposed simply to the one, certain impossibilities follow. The one will be few or a few; for the many are also opposed to the few. Again, two are many, if the double is called multiple and is so called with reference to two; thus one is few. For in relation to what are two many, if not in relation to one and to the few? For there is nothing less. Again, if, as in length there are long and short, so in multitude there are much and little, and whatever is much is many, and the many are much (unless perhaps there is some difference in a determinate continuum), the few will be some multitude. Thus the one will be some multitude, if indeed it is also few; and this is necessary if two are many. But perhaps “many” is in some way also said of “much,” though differently, as there is much water but not many waters.","kind":"passage","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_DAZY_BO_2:P0027"]},{"id":"RAW_DAZY_BO_2:P0028","text":"But among things divisible it is spoken of in one way when there is a multitude having excess, either simply or relatively (and the few likewise are a multitude having deficiency); in another way as number, which alone is opposed to the one. For thus we say “one or many,” just as one might say “one and a one,” or “white and whites,” and of things measured in relation to the measure. It is in this way that multiples too are spoken of; for every number is many because it consists of ones and because each is measurable by one, and as what is opposed to the one, not to the few. In this way, then, two too are many; but as a multitude having excess, either relatively or simply, they are not, but are first.","kind":"passage","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_DAZY_BO_2:P0028"]},{"id":"RAW_DAZY_BO_2:P0029","text":"But two are few simply; for they are the first multitude having deficiency (and this is why Anaxagoras was not right when he departed by saying that all things were together, infinite both in multitude and in smallness; he should have said, instead of “and in smallness,” “and in fewness”; for they are not infinite), since the few are not due to one, as some say, but to two.—The one and the many in numbers, then, are opposed as measure to what is measured; and these are relatives in the way that those relatives are which are not relatives in themselves. We have distinguished elsewhere that relatives are spoken of in two ways: some as contraries, others as knowledge to what is knowable, by one thing being said relative to another. But nothing prevents the one from being less than something, for example less than two; for if it is less, it is not therefore few.","kind":"passage","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_DAZY_BO_2:P0029"]},{"id":"RAW_DAZY_BO_2:P0030","text":"Multitude is as it were the genus of number; for number is a multitude measurable by one, and the one and number are in some way opposed, not as contraries but, as was said, as certain relatives. For insofar as the one is measure and number measurable, they are opposed; hence not everything that is one is number, for example if something is indivisible. Knowledge, too, though spoken of relatively to what is knowable, does not yield the relation in the same way. For knowledge might seem to be the measure and what is knowable the measured; but it follows that every knowledge is knowable, while not everything knowable is knowledge, because in a certain way knowledge is measured by what is knowable. But multitude is contrary neither to the few—for to the few, as a multitude exceeded, is opposed the much, as an exceeding multitude—nor simply to the one. Rather, in one sense it is as was said, because the one is divisible and the other indivisible; in another, it is relative, as knowledge is to what is knowable, if multitude is number and the one measure.","kind":"passage","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_DAZY_BO_2:P0030"]},{"id":"RAW_DAZY_BO_2:P0031","text":"Since it is possible for there to be something intermediate between contraries, and there is in some cases, the intermediates must be from the contraries. For all intermediates are in the same genus as the things between which they are. For we call those things intermediate through which that which is changing must first pass in changing—for example, if it changed from the lowest note to the highest by the shortest route, it will first arrive at the intermediate notes; and in colours, if from white to black, it will first arrive at crimson and grey rather than at black; and similarly in the other cases. But it is impossible to change from one genus to another except accidentally, for example from colour to shape. The intermediates, therefore, must be in the same genus both as one another and as the things between which they are. But all intermediates are between certain opposites; for only from these can change occur in itself (hence it is impossible for there to be an intermediate between things not opposed; for there would then be change not from opposites).","kind":"passage","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_DAZY_BO_2:P0031"]},{"id":"RAW_DAZY_BO_2:P0032","text":"Of opposites, there is no intermediate in the case of contradiction (for this is contradiction: an opposition in which one or the other part belongs to anything whatsoever, and which has no intermediate); but of the rest, some are relatives, some privations, and some contraries. Among relatives which are not contraries, there is no intermediate; the reason is that they are not in the same genus. For what is intermediate between knowledge and what is knowable? But there is an intermediate between great and small. If intermediates are in the same genus, as has been shown, and are between contraries, they must be composed from these contraries. For either there will be some genus of them, or none.","kind":"passage","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_DAZY_BO_2:P0032"]},{"id":"RAW_DAZY_BO_2:P0033","text":"And if there is a genus in such a way as to be something prior to the contraries, there will be contrary differentiae prior to the contraries, which will make the contrary species as species of a genus; for species are from genus and differentiae. For example, if white and black are contraries, and the former is dispersive colour and the latter aggregative colour, these differentiae—dispersive and aggregative—are prior; hence these are prior contraries to one another.","kind":"passage","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_DAZY_BO_2:P0033"]},{"id":"RAW_DAZY_BO_2:P0034","text":"But surely things differing more contrarily are more contrary; and the other things, including the intermediates, will be from the genus and the differentiae. For example, all the colours intermediate between white and black must be stated from the genus—colour is the genus—and from certain differentiae; but these will not be the primary contraries, since otherwise each would be either white or black. They are therefore different; thus they will be intermediate between the primary contraries, while the primary differentiae are dispersive and aggregative. Therefore one must first inquire, in cases where contraries are not in a genus, from what their intermediates are composed (for things in the same genus must either be composed from things not compounded with the genus, or be uncompounded). Now contraries are uncompounded from one another, and thus are principles; but the intermediates are either all compounded or none. Yet something comes to be from contraries, so that there will be change into this before change into the contraries themselves; for it will be both less and more than either. Therefore this too will be intermediate between the contraries.","kind":"passage","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_DAZY_BO_2:P0034"]},{"id":"RAW_DAZY_BO_2:P0035","text":"Thus all the other intermediates too are compounded; for what is more than one thing and less than another is in some way compounded from those of which it is said to be more than one and less than another. And since there are no other prior things of the same genus as the contraries, all the intermediates will be from the contraries; hence all subordinate things, both contraries and intermediates, will be from the primary contraries. It is clear, then, that all intermediates are in the same genus, are intermediate between contraries, and are all composed from contraries.","kind":"passage","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_DAZY_BO_2:P0035"]},{"id":"RAW_DAZY_BO_2:P0036","text":"What is other in species is other than something in species, and this must belong to both; for example, if an animal is other in species, both are animals. Things other in species must therefore be in the same genus; for I call that a genus which is predicated as one and the same thing of both, not having its difference accidentally, whether as matter or otherwise. For it is not only necessary that the common term belong, for example that both are animals, but also that this animal itself be other in each—for example, horse in the one and man in the other—and thus this common term is other in species in the one from the other. Therefore, in themselves, the one will be such-and-such an animal and the other such-and-such an animal, for example the one horse and the other man. This difference, then, must be an otherness of the genus. For by a difference of genus I mean an otherness which makes this thing itself other.","kind":"passage","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_DAZY_BO_2:P0036"]},{"id":"RAW_DAZY_BO_2:P0037","text":"This, then, will be contrariety (and it is clear also from induction). For all things are divided by opposites, and it has been shown that contraries are in the same genus; for contrariety was complete difference, and every difference in species is a difference of something from something, so that this same thing belongs to both and is a genus. Hence all contraries in the same column of a category which differ in species but not in genus are most other from one another—for the difference is complete—and do not come to be simultaneously with one another. Difference, then, is contrariety.","kind":"passage","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_DAZY_BO_2:P0037"]},{"id":"RAW_DAZY_BO_2:P0038","text":"Thus to be other in species is this: for things which are in the same genus and are indivisible to have contrariety (and things the same in species are those which, being indivisible, do not have contrariety). For in division and among the intermediates contrarieties arise before one reaches indivisibles. It is therefore clear that, in relation to what is called the genus, none of the species as species of a genus is either the same in species or other in species (appropriately so; for matter is signified by negation, and the genus is the matter of that of which it is called genus—not as the genus of the Heracleidae, but as genus in nature); nor are they other in species in relation to things not in the same genus, but they differ in genus from those, and in species from things in the same genus. For the difference of things that differ in species must be contrariety; and this belongs only to things in the same genus.","kind":"passage","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_DAZY_BO_2:P0038"]},{"id":"RAW_DAZY_BO_2:P0039","text":"One might raise a difficulty as to why woman does not differ in species from man, although female and male are contrary and the difference is contrariety, nor a female animal from a male animal; and yet this difference belongs to animal in itself, not as whiteness or blackness does, but female and male belong insofar as it is animal. This difficulty is nearly the same as the question why one contrariety makes things different in species while another does not—for example, footedness and wingedness do, while whiteness and blackness do not. Or is it because the former are proper affections of the genus and the latter less so? And since one thing is account and another matter, all contrarieties which are in the account produce difference in species, whereas those which are in what is taken together with matter do not. Hence whiteness or blackness does not produce difference in species in man, nor is there a difference in species between a white man and a black man, not even if one name is assigned to them.","kind":"passage","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_DAZY_BO_2:P0039"]},{"id":"RAW_DAZY_BO_2:P0040","text":"For man is as matter, and matter does not produce difference; nor for this reason are individual men species of man, though the flesh and bones from which this man and that man are constituted are different. But the composite is different, though not different in species, because there is no contrariety in the account. This is the ultimate individual. Callias is the account together with the matter; and so too is the white man, because Callias is white; therefore man is accidental. Nor do a bronze circle and a wooden circle differ in species; nor do a bronze triangle and a wooden circle differ in species because of their matter, but because contrariety is present in the account. But does matter not make things other in species, although it is in some way other, or is there a way in which it does? For why is this horse other in species from this man? And yet their accounts are together with matter.","kind":"passage","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_DAZY_BO_2:P0040"]},{"id":"RAW_DAZY_BO_2:P0041","text":"Or is it because contrariety is present in the account? For it is present also in the case of a white man and a black horse, and they are different in species, but not insofar as the one is white and the other black; for even if both were white, they would nevertheless be different in species. But male and female are proper affections of animal, though not according to substance but in matter and body; hence the same seed becomes female or male by undergoing some affection. What it is, then, to be other in species, and why some things differ in species and others do not, has been stated.","kind":"passage","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_DAZY_BO_2:P0041"]},{"id":"RAW_DAZY_BO_2:P0042","text":"But since contraries are other in form, and the perishable and the imperishable are contraries (for privation is a determinate incapacity), it is necessary that the perishable and the imperishable be other in genus. Now, we have spoken of the universal names themselves, so that it might seem not necessary for any perishable and imperishable thing whatsoever to be other in form, just as neither are white and black; for it is possible for the same thing to be both, even simultaneously, if it is among universals—as a human being might be both white and black—and likewise among particulars; for the same thing might be white and black, though not simultaneously, although white is contrary to black. But of contraries, some belong accidentally to certain things, such as those now mentioned and many others, while others cannot; among these are the perishable and the imperishable. For nothing is perishable accidentally: for the accidental can fail to belong, whereas the perishable is among the attributes that belong necessarily to the things to which it belongs. Otherwise, the same one thing will be both perishable and imperishable, if","kind":"passage","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_DAZY_BO_2:P0042"]},{"id":"RAW_DAZY_BO_2:P0043","text":"perishability can fail to belong to it. Therefore, perishability must belong either to the substance, or in the substance, of each of the perishable things. The same argument applies also to the imperishable; for both are among the attributes that belong necessarily. Therefore, in respect of that whereby and according to which, primarily, the one is perishable and the other imperishable, they have opposition, so that they must be other in genus. It is clear, then, that it is not possible for there to be such Forms as some assert; for then there will be a human being, one perishable and another imperishable. Yet the Forms are said to be the same in form as certain things, and not homonymous with them; but things other in genus are more widely separated than things other in form.","kind":"passage","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_DAZY_BO_2:P0043"]}],"source_digest":"6c6b209ea67656a4913b188fba8327807267e116d536f68d88c62022525a3c8c","source_language_code":"grc","original_title":"Book 10"}],"passage_basis":"frozen-grounding","original_title":"Book 10","title_language_code":"en"},{"id":"RAW_DAZY_BO_3","title":"Book 11","order":11,"chapter_id":"CH_DAZY_BO_3","text":"ὅτι μὲν ἡ σοφία περὶ ἀρχὰς ἐπιστήμη τίς ἐστι, δῆλον ἐκ τῶν πρώτων ἐν οἷς διηπόρηται πρὸς τὰ ὑπὸ τῶν ἄλλων εἰρημένα περὶ τῶν ἀρχῶν· ἀπορήσειε δʼ ἄν τις πότερον μίαν ὑπολαβεῖν εἶναι δεῖ τὴν σοφίαν ἐπιστήμην ἢ πολλάς· εἰ μὲν γὰρ μίαν, μία γʼ ἐστὶν ἀεὶ τῶν ἐναντίων, αἱ δʼ ἀρχαὶ οὐκ ἐναντίαι· εἰ δὲ μὴ μία, ποίας δεῖ θεῖναι ταύτας; ἔτι τὰς ἀποδεικτικὰς ἀρχὰς θεωρῆσαι μιᾶς ἢ πλειόνων; εἰ μὲν γὰρ μιᾶς, τί μᾶλλον ταύτης ἢ ὁποιασοῦν; εἰ δὲ πλειόνων, ποίας δεῖ ταύτας τιθέναι; ἔτι πότερον πασῶν τῶν οὐσιῶν ἢ οὔ; εἰ μὲν γὰρ μὴ πασῶν, ποίων χαλεπὸν ἀποδοῦναι· εἰ δὲ πασῶν μία, ἄδηλον πῶς ἐνδέχεται πλειόνων τὴν αὐτὴν ἐπιστήμην εἶναι. ἔτι πότερον περὶ τὰς οὐσίας μόνον ἢ καὶ τὰ συμβεβηκότα ; εἰ γὰρ περί γε τὰ συμβεβηκότα ἀπόδειξίς ἐστιν, περὶ τὰς οὐσίας οὐκ ἔστιν· εἰ δʼ ἑτέρα, τίς ἑκατέρα καὶ ποτέρα σοφία; ᾗ μὲν γὰρ ἀποδεικτική, σοφία ἡ περὶ τὰ συμβεβηκότα· ᾗ δὲ περὶ τὰ πρῶτα, ἡ τῶν οὐσιῶν. ἀλλʼ οὐδὲ περὶ τὰς ἐν τοῖς φυσικοῖς εἰρημένας αἰτίας τὴν ἐπιζητουμένην ἐπιστήμην θετέον· οὔτε γὰρ περὶ τὸ οὗ ἕνεκεν (τοιοῦτον γὰρ τὸ ἀγαθόν, τοῦτο δʼ ἐν τοῖς πρακτοῖς ὑπάρχει καὶ ταῖς οὖσιν ἐν κινήσει· καὶ τοῦτο πρῶτον κινεῖ—τοιοῦτον γὰρ τὸ τέλος—τὸ δὲ πρῶτον κινῆσαν οὐκ ἔστιν ἐν τοῖς ἀκινήτοις)· ὅλως δʼ ἀπορίαν ἔχει πότερόν ποτε περὶ τὰς αἰσθητὰς οὐσίας ἐστὶν ἡ ζητουμένη νῦν ἐπιστήμη ἢ οὔ, περὶ δέ τινας ἑτέρας.  εἰ γὰρ περὶ ἄλλας, ἢ περὶ τὰ εἴδη εἴη ἂν ἢ περὶ τὰ μαθηματικά. τὰ μὲν οὖν εἴδη ὅτι οὐκ ἔστι, δῆλον (ὅμως δὲ ἀπορίαν ἔχει, κἂν εἶναί τις αὐτὰ θῇ, διὰ τί ποτʼ οὐχ ὥσπερ ἐπὶ τῶν μαθηματικῶν, οὕτως ἔχει καὶ ἐπὶ τῶν ἄλλων ὧν ἔστιν εἴδη· λέγω δʼ ὅτι τὰ μαθηματικὰ μὲν μεταξύ τε τῶν εἰδῶν τιθέασι καὶ τῶν αἰσθητῶν οἷον τρίτα τινὰ παρὰ τὰ εἴδη τε καὶ τὰ δεῦρο, τρίτος δʼ ἄνθρωπος οὐκ ἔστιν οὐδʼ ἵππος παρʼ αὐτόν τε καὶ τοὺς καθʼ ἕκαστον· εἰ δʼ αὖ μὴ ἔστιν ὡς λέγουσι, περὶ ποῖα θετέον πραγματεύεσθαι τὸν μαθηματικόν; οὐ γὰρ δὴ περὶ τὰ δεῦρο· τούτων γὰρ οὐθέν ἐστιν οἷον αἱ μαθηματικαὶ ζητοῦσι τῶν ἐπιστημῶν)· οὐδὲ μὴν περὶ τὰ μαθηματικὰ ἡ ζητουμένη νῦν ἐστὶν ἐπιστήμη (χωριστὸν γὰρ αὐτῶν οὐθέν)· ἀλλʼ οὐδὲ τῶν αἰσθητῶν οὐσιῶν· φθαρταὶ γάρ. ὅλως δʼ ἀπορήσειέ τις ἂν ποίας ἐστὶν ἐπιστήμης τὸ διαπορῆσαι περὶ τῆς τῶν μαθηματικῶν ὕλης. οὔτε γὰρ τῆς φυσικῆς, διὰ τὸ περὶ τὰ ἔχοντα ἐν αὑτοῖς ἀρχὴν κινήσεως καὶ στάσεως τὴν τοῦ φυσικοῦ πᾶσαν εἶναι πραγματείαν, οὐδὲ μὴν τῆς σκοπούσης περὶ ἀποδείξεώς τε καὶ ἐπιστήμης· περὶ γὰρ αὐτὸ τοῦτο τὸ γένος τὴν ζήτησιν ποιεῖται. λείπεται τοίνυν τὴν προκειμένην φιλοσοφίαν περὶ αὐτῶν τὴν σκέψιν ποιεῖσθαι. διαπορήσειε δʼ ἄν τις εἰ δεῖ θεῖναι τὴν ζητουμένην ἐπιστήμην περὶ τὰς ἀρχάς, τὰ καλούμενα ὑπό τινων στοιχεῖα· ταῦτα δὲ πάντες ἐνυπάρχοντα τοῖς συνθέτοις τιθέασιν. μᾶλλον δʼ ἂν δόξειε τῶν καθόλου δεῖν εἶναι τὴν ζητουμένην ἐπιστήμην· πᾶς γὰρ λόγος καὶ πᾶσα ἐπιστήμη τῶν καθόλου καὶ οὐ τῶν ἐσχάτων, ὥστʼ εἴη ἂν οὕτω τῶν πρώτων γενῶν. ταῦτα δὲ γίγνοιτʼ ἂν τό τε ὂν καὶ τὸ ἕν· ταῦτα γὰρ μάλιστʼ ἂν ὑποληφθείη περιέχειν τὰ ὄντα πάντα καὶ μάλιστα ἀρχαῖς ἐοικέναι διὰ τὸ εἶναι πρῶτα τῇ φύσει· φθαρέντων γὰρ αὐτῶν συναναιρεῖται καὶ τὰ λοιπά· πᾶν γὰρ ὂν καὶ ἕν. ᾗ δὲ τὰς διαφορὰς αὐτῶν ἀνάγκη μετέχειν εἰ θήσει τις αὐτὰ γένη, διαφορὰ δʼ οὐδεμία τοῦ γένους μετέχει, ταύτῃ δʼ οὐκ ἂν δόξειε δεῖν αὐτὰ τιθέναι γένη οὐδʼ ἀρχάς. ἔτι δʼ εἰ μᾶλλον ἀρχὴ τὸ ἁπλούστερον τοῦ ἧττον τοιούτου, τὰ δʼ ἔσχατα τῶν ἐκ τοῦ γένους ἁπλούστερα τῶν γενῶν (ἄτομα γάρ, τὰ γένη δʼ εἰς εἴδη πλείω καὶ διαφέροντα διαιρεῖται), μᾶλλον ἂν ἀρχὴ δόξειεν εἶναι τὰ εἴδη τῶν γενῶν. ᾗ δὲ συναναιρεῖται τοῖς γένεσι τὰ εἴδη, τὰ γένη ταῖς ἀρχαῖς ἔοικε μᾶλλον· ἀρχὴ γὰρ τὸ συναναιροῦν.  τὰ μὲν οὖν τὴν ἀπορίαν ἔχοντα ταῦτα καὶ τοιαῦτʼ ἐστὶν ἕτερα. \n\n\n\n \n\n \n\nἔτι πότερον δεῖ τιθέναι τι παρὰ τὰ καθʼ ἕκαστα ἢ οὔ, ἀλλὰ τούτων ἡ ζητουμένη ἐπιστήμη; ἀλλὰ ταῦτα ἄπειρα· τά γε μὴν παρὰ τὰ καθʼ ἕκαστα γένη ἢ εἴδη ἐστίν, ἀλλʼ οὐδετέρου τούτων ἡ ζητουμένη νῦν ἐπιστήμη. διότι γὰρ ἀδύνατον τοῦτο, εἴρηται. καὶ γὰρ ὅλως ἀπορίαν ἔχει πότερον δεῖ τινὰ ὑπολαβεῖν οὐσίαν εἶναι χωριστὴν παρὰ τὰς αἰσθητὰς οὐσίας καὶ τὰς δεῦρο, ἢ οὔ, ἀλλὰ ταῦτʼ εἶναι τὰ ὄντα καὶ περὶ ταῦτα τὴν σοφίαν ὑπάρχειν. ζητεῖν μὲν γὰρ ἐοίκαμεν ἄλλην τινά, καὶ τὸ προκείμενον τοῦτʼ ἔστιν ἡμῖν, λέγω δὲ τὸ ἰδεῖν εἴ τι χωριστὸν καθʼ αὑτὸ καὶ μηδενὶ τῶν αἰσθητῶν ὑπάρχον. ἔτι δʼ εἰ παρὰ τὰς αἰσθητὰς οὐσίας ἔστι τις ἑτέρα οὐσία, παρὰ ποίας τῶν αἰσθητῶν δεῖ τιθέναι ταύτην εἶναι; τί γὰρ μᾶλλον παρὰ τοὺς ἀνθρώπους ἢ τοὺς ἵππους ἢ τῶν ἄλλων ζῴων θήσει τις αὐτὴν ἢ καὶ τῶν ἀψύχων ὅλως; τό γε μὴν ἴσας ταῖς αἰσθηταῖς καὶ φθαρταῖς οὐσίαις ἀϊδίους ἑτέρας κατασκευάζειν ἐκτὸς τῶν εὐλόγων δόξειεν ἂν πίπτειν. εἰ δὲ μὴ χωριστὴ τῶν σωμάτων ἡ ζητουμένη νῦν ἀρχή, τίνα ἄν τις ἄλλην θείη μᾶλλον τῆς ὕλης; αὕτη γε μὴν ἐνεργείᾳ μὲν οὐκ ἔστι, δυνάμει δʼ ἔστιν. μᾶλλόν τʼ ἂν ἀρχὴ κυριωτέρα ταύτης δόξειεν εἶναι τὸ εἶδος καὶ ἡ μορφή· τοῦτο δὲ φθαρτόν, ὥσθʼ ὅλως οὐκ ἔστιν ἀΐδιος οὐσία χωριστὴ καὶ καθʼ αὑτήν. ἀλλʼ ἄτοπον· ἔοικε γὰρ καὶ ζητεῖται σχεδὸν ὑπὸ τῶν χαριεστάτων ὡς οὖσά τις ἀρχὴ καὶ οὐσία τοιαύτη· πῶς γὰρ ἔσται τάξις μή τινος ὄντος ἀϊδίου καὶ χωριστοῦ καὶ μένοντος; ἔτι δʼ εἴπερ ἔστι τις οὐσία καὶ ἀρχὴ τοιαύτη τὴν φύσιν οἵαν νῦν ζητοῦμεν, καὶ αὕτη μία πάντων καὶ ἡ αὐτὴ τῶν ἀϊδίων τε καὶ φθαρτῶν, ἀπορίαν ἔχει διὰ τί ποτε τῆς αὐτῆς ἀρχῆς οὔσης τὰ μέν ἐστιν ἀΐδια τῶν ὑπὸ τὴν ἀρχὴν τὰ δʼ οὐκ ἀΐδια (τοῦτο γὰρ ἄτοπον)· εἰ δʼ ἄλλη μέν ἐστιν ἀρχὴ τῶν φθαρτῶν ἄλλη δὲ τῶν ἀϊδίων, εἰ μὲν ἀΐδιος καὶ ἡ τῶν φθαρτῶν, ὁμοίως ἀπορήσομεν (διὰ τί γὰρ οὐκ ἀϊδίου τῆς ἀρχῆς οὔσης καὶ τὰ ὑπὸ τὴν ἀρχὴν ἀΐδια;)· φθαρτῆς δʼ οὔσης ἄλλη τις ἀρχὴ γίγνεται ταύτης κἀκείνης ἑτέρα, καὶ τοῦτʼ εἰς ἄπειρον πρόεισιν. εἰ δʼ αὖ τις τὰς δοκούσας μάλιστʼ ἀρχὰς ἀκινήτους εἶναι, τό τε ὂν καὶ τὸ ἕν, θήσει, πρῶτον μὲν εἰ μὴ τόδε τι καὶ οὐσίαν ἑκάτερον αὐτῶν σημαίνει,  πῶς ἔσονται χωρισταὶ καὶ καθʼ αὑτάς; τοιαύτας δὲ ζητοῦμεν τὰς ἀϊδίους τε καὶ πρώτας ἀρχάς. εἴ γε μὴν τόδε τι καὶ οὐσίαν ἑκάτερον αὐτῶν δηλοῖ, πάντʼ ἐστὶν οὐσίαι τὰ ὄντα· κατὰ πάντων γὰρ τὸ ὂν κατηγορεῖται (κατʼ ἐνίων δὲ καὶ τὸ ἕν)· οὐσίαν δʼ εἶναι πάντα τὰ ὄντα ψεῦδος. ἔτι δὲ τοῖς τὴν πρώτην ἀρχὴν τὸ ἓν λέγουσι καὶ τοῦτʼ οὐσίαν, ἐκ δὲ τοῦ ἑνὸς καὶ τῆς ὕλης τὸν ἀριθμὸν γεννῶσι πρῶτον καὶ τοῦτον οὐσίαν φάσκουσιν εἶναι, πῶς ἐνδέχεται τὸ λεγόμενον ἀληθὲς εἶναι; τὴν γὰρ δυάδα καὶ τῶν λοιπῶν ἕκαστον ἀριθμῶν τῶν συνθέτων πῶς ἓν δεῖ νοῆσαι; περὶ τούτου γὰρ οὔτε λέγουσιν οὐδὲν οὔτε ῥᾴδιον εἰπεῖν. εἴ γε μὴν γραμμὰς ἢ τὰ τούτων ἐχόμενα (λέγω δὲ ἐπιφανείας τὰς πρώτας) θήσει τις ἀρχάς, ταῦτά γʼ οὐκ εἰσὶν οὐσίαι χωρισταί, τομαὶ δὲ καὶ διαιρέσεις αἱ μὲν ἐπιφανειῶν αἱ δὲ σωμάτων (αἱ δὲ στιγμαὶ γραμμῶν), ἔτι δὲ πέρατα τῶν αὐτῶν τούτων· πάντα δὲ ταῦτα ἐν ἄλλοις ὑπάρχει καὶ χωριστὸν οὐδέν ἐστιν. ἔτι πῶς οὐσίαν ὑπολαβεῖν εἶναι δεῖ τοῦ ἑνὸς καὶ στιγμῆς; οὐσίας μὲν γὰρ πάσης γένεσις ἔστι, στιγμῆς δʼ οὐκ ἔστιν· διαίρεσις γὰρ ἡ στιγμή. παρέχει δʼ ἀπορίαν καὶ τὸ πᾶσαν μὲν ἐπιστήμην εἶναι τῶν καθόλου καὶ τοῦ τοιουδί, τὴν δʼ οὐσίαν μὴ τῶν καθόλου εἶναι, μᾶλλον δὲ τόδε τι καὶ χωριστόν, ὥστʼ εἰ περὶ τὰς ἀρχάς ἐστιν ἐπιστήμη, πῶς δεῖ τὴν ἀρχὴν ὑπολαβεῖν οὐσίαν εἶναι; ἔτι πότερον ἔστι τι παρὰ τὸ σύνολον ἢ οὔ (λέγω δὲ τὴν ὕλην καὶ τὸ μετὰ ταύτης); εἰ μὲν γὰρ μή, τά γε ἐν ὕλῃ φθαρτὰ πάντα· εἰ δʼ ἔστι τι, τὸ εἶδος ἂν εἴη καὶ ἡ μορφή· τοῦτʼ οὖν ἐπὶ τίνων ἔστι καὶ ἐπὶ τίνων οὔ, χαλεπὸν ἀφορίσαι· ἐπʼ ἐνίων γὰρ δῆλον οὐκ ὂν χωριστὸν τὸ εἶδος, οἷον οἰκίας. ἔτι πότερον αἱ ἀρχαὶ εἴδει ἢ ἀριθμῷ αἱ αὐταί; εἰ γὰρ ἀριθμῷ ἕν, πάντʼ ἔσται ταὐτά. \n\n\n\n \n\n \n\nἐπεὶ δʼ ἐστὶν ἡ τοῦ φιλοσόφου ἐπιστήμη τοῦ ὄντος ᾗ ὂν καθόλου καὶ οὐ κατὰ μέρος, τὸ δʼ ὂν πολλαχῶς καὶ οὐ καθʼ ἕνα λέγεται τρόπον· εἰ μὲν οὖν ὁμωνύμως κατὰ δὲ κοινὸν μηδέν, οὐκ ἔστιν ὑπὸ μίαν ἐπιστήμην (οὐ γὰρ ἓν γένος τῶν τοιούτων), εἰ δὲ κατά τι κοινόν, εἴη ἂν ὑπὸ μίαν ἐπιστήμην. ἔοικε δὴ τὸν εἰρημένον λέγεσθαι τρόπον καθάπερ τό τε ἰατρικὸν καὶ ὑγιεινόν· καὶ γὰρ τούτων ἑκάτερον πολλαχῶς λέγομεν.  λέγεται δὲ τοῦτον τὸν τρόπον ἕκαστον τῷ τὸ μὲν πρὸς τὴν ἰατρικὴν ἐπιστήμην ἀνάγεσθαί πως τὸ δὲ πρὸς ὑγίειαν τὸ δʼ ἄλλως, πρὸς ταὐτὸ δʼ ἕκαστον. ἰατρικὸς γὰρ λόγος καὶ μαχαίριον λέγεται τῷ τὸ μὲν ἀπὸ τῆς ἰατρικῆς ἐπιστήμης εἶναι τὸ δὲ ταύτῃ χρήσιμον. ὁμοίως δὲ καὶ ὑγιεινόν· τὸ μὲν γὰρ ὅτι σημαντικὸν ὑγιείας τὸ δʼ ὅτι ποιητικόν. ὁ δʼ αὐτὸς τρόπος καὶ ἐπὶ τῶν λοιπῶν. τὸν αὐτὸν δὴ τρόπον καὶ τὸ ὂν ἅπαν λέγεται· τῷ γὰρ τοῦ ὄντος ᾗ ὂν πάθος ἢ ἕξις ἢ διάθεσις ἢ κίνησις ἢ τῶν ἄλλων τι τῶν τοιούτων εἶναι λέγεται ἕκαστον αὐτῶν ὄν. ἐπεὶ δὲ παντὸς τοῦ ὄντος πρὸς ἕν τι καὶ κοινὸν ἡ ἀναγωγὴ γίγνεται, καὶ τῶν ἐναντιώσεων ἑκάστη πρὸς τὰς πρώτας διαφορὰς καὶ ἐναντιώσεις ἀναχθήσεται τοῦ ὄντος, εἴτε πλῆθος καὶ ἓν εἴθʼ ὁμοιότης καὶ ἀνομοιότης αἱ πρῶται τοῦ ὄντος εἰσὶ διαφοραί, εἴτʼ ἄλλαι τινές· ἔστωσαν γὰρ αὗται τεθεωρημέναι. διαφέρει δʼ οὐδὲν τὴν τοῦ ὄντος ἀναγωγὴν πρὸς τὸ ὂν ἢ πρὸς τὸ ἓν γίγνεσθαι. καὶ γὰρ εἰ μὴ ταὐτὸν ἄλλο δʼ ἐστίν, ἀντιστρέφει γε· τό τε γὰρ ἓν καὶ ὄν πως, τό τε ὂν ἕν.—ἐπεὶ δʼ ἐστὶ τὰ ἐναντία πάντα τῆς αὐτῆς καὶ μιᾶς ἐπιστήμης θεωρῆσαι, λέγεται δʼ ἕκαστον αὐτῶν κατὰ στέρησιν—καίτοι γʼ ἔνια ἀπορήσειέ τις ἂν πῶς λέγεται κατὰ στέρησιν, ὧν ἔστιν ἀνὰ μέσον τι, καθάπερ ἀδίκου καὶ δικαίου—περὶ πάντα δὴ τὰ τοιαῦτα τὴν στέρησιν δεῖ τιθέναι μὴ τοῦ ὅλου λόγου, τοῦ τελευταίου δὲ εἴδους· οἷον εἰ ἔστιν ὁ δίκαιος καθʼ ἕξιν τινὰ πειθαρχικὸς τοῖς νόμοις, οὐ πάντως ὁ ἄδικος ἔσται τοῦ ὅλου στερούμενος λόγου, περὶ δὲ τὸ πείθεσθαι τοῖς νόμοις ἐκλείπων πῃ, καὶ ταύτῃ ἡ στέρησις ὑπάρξει αὐτῷ· τὸν αὐτὸν δὲ τρόπον καὶ ἐπὶ τῶν ἄλλων.—καθάπερ δʼ ὁ μαθηματικὸς περὶ τὰ ἐξ ἀφαιρέσεως τὴν θεωρίαν ποιεῖται (περιελὼν γὰρ πάντα τὰ αἰσθητὰ θεωρεῖ, οἷον βάρος καὶ κουφότητα καὶ σκληρότητα καὶ τοὐναντίον, ἔτι δὲ καὶ θερμότητα καὶ ψυχρότητα καὶ τὰς ἄλλας αἰσθητὰς ἐναντιώσεις, μόνον δὲ καταλείπει τὸ ποσὸν καὶ συνεχές, τῶν μὲν ἐφʼ ἓν τῶν δʼ ἐπὶ δύο τῶν δʼ ἐπὶ τρία, καὶ τὰ πάθη τὰ τούτων ᾗ ποσά ἐστι καὶ συνεχῆ, καὶ οὐ καθʼ ἕτερόν τι θεωρεῖ, καὶ τῶν μὲν τὰς πρὸς ἄλληλα θέσεις σκοπεῖ καὶ τὰ ταύταις ὑπάρχοντα,  τῶν δὲ τὰς συμμετρίας καὶ ἀσυμμετρίας, τῶν δὲ τοὺς λόγους, ἀλλʼ ὅμως μίαν πάντων καὶ τὴν αὐτὴν τίθεμεν ἐπιστήμην τὴν γεωμετρικήν), τὸν αὐτὸν δὴ τρόπον ἔχει καὶ περὶ τὸ ὄν. τὰ γὰρ τούτῳ συμβεβηκότα καθʼ ὅσον ἐστὶν ὄν, καὶ τὰς ἐναντιώσεις αὐτοῦ ᾗ ὄν, οὐκ ἄλλης ἐπιστήμης ἢ φιλοσοφίας θεωρῆσαι. τῇ φυσικῇ μὲν γὰρ οὐχ ᾗ ὄντα, μᾶλλον δʼ ᾗ κινήσεως μετέχει, τὴν θεωρίαν τις ἀπονείμειεν ἄν· ἥ γε μὴν διαλεκτικὴ καὶ ἡ σοφιστικὴ τῶν συμβεβηκότων μέν εἰσι τοῖς οὖσιν, οὐχ ᾗ δʼ ὄντα οὐδὲ περὶ τὸ ὂν αὐτὸ καθʼ ὅσον ὄν ἐστιν· ὥστε λείπεται τὸν φιλοσόφον, καθʼ ὅσον ὄντʼ ἐστίν, εἶναι περὶ τὰ λεχθέντα θεωρητικόν. ἐπεὶ δὲ τό τε ὂν ἅπαν καθʼ ἕν τι καὶ κοινὸν λέγεται πολλαχῶς λεγόμενον, καὶ τἀναντία τὸν αὐτὸν τρόπον (εἰς τὰς πρώτας γὰρ ἐναντιώσεις καὶ διαφορὰς τοῦ ὄντος ἀνάγεται), τὰ δὲ τοιαῦτα δυνατὸν ὑπὸ μίαν ἐπιστήμην εἶναι, διαλύοιτʼ ἂν ἡ κατʼ ἀρχὰς ἀπορία λεχθεῖσα, λέγω δʼ ἐν ᾗ διηπορεῖτο πῶς ἔσται πολλῶν καὶ διαφόρων ὄντων τῷ γένει μία τις ἐπιστήμη. \n\n\n\n \n\n \n\nἐπεὶ δὲ καὶ ὁ μαθηματικὸς χρῆται τοῖς κοινοῖς ἰδίως, καὶ τὰς τούτων ἀρχὰς ἂν εἴη θεωρῆσαι τῆς πρώτης φιλοσοφίας. ὅτι γὰρ ἀπὸ τῶν ἴσων ἴσων ἀφαιρεθέντων ἴσα τὰ λειπόμενα, κοινὸν μέν ἐστιν ἐπὶ πάντων τῶν ποσῶν, ἡ μαθηματικὴ δʼ ἀπολαβοῦσα περί τι μέρος τῆς οἰκείας ὕλης ποιεῖται τὴν θεωρίαν, οἷον περὶ γραμμὰς ἢ γωνίας ἢ ἀριθμοὺς ἢ τῶν λοιπῶν τι ποσῶν, οὐχ ᾗ δʼ ὄντα ἀλλʼ ᾗ συνεχὲς αὐτῶν ἕκαστον ἐφʼ ἓν ἢ δύο ἢ τρία· ἡ δὲ φιλοσοφία περὶ τῶν ἐν μέρει μέν, ᾗ τούτων ἑκάστῳ τι συμβέβηκεν, οὐ σκοπεῖ, περὶ τὸ ὂν δέ, ᾗ ὂν τῶν τοιούτων ἕκαστον, θεωρεῖ. τὸν αὐτὸν δʼ ἔχει τρόπον καὶ περὶ τὴν φυσικὴν ἐπιστήμην τῇ μαθηματικῇ· τὰ συμβεβηκότα γὰρ ἡ φυσικὴ καὶ τὰς ἀρχὰς θεωρεῖ τὰς τῶν ὄντων ᾗ κινούμενα καὶ οὐχ ᾗ ὄντα (τὴν δὲ πρώτην εἰρήκαμεν ἐπιστήμην τούτων εἶναι καθʼ ὅσον ὄντα τὰ ὑποκείμενά ἐστιν, ἀλλʼ οὐχ ᾗ ἕτερόν τι)· διὸ καὶ ταύτην καὶ τὴν μαθηματικὴν ἐπιστήμην μέρη τῆς σοφίας εἶναι θετέον.\n\n\n\n \n\n \n\nἔστι δέ τις ἐν τοῖς οὖσιν ἀρχὴ περὶ ἣν οὐκ ἔστι διεψεῦσθαι, τοὐναντίον δὲ ἀναγκαῖον ἀεὶ ποιεῖν, λέγω δὲ ἀληθεύειν, οἷον ὅτι οὐκ ἐνδέχεται τὸ αὐτὸ καθʼ ἕνα καὶ τὸν αὐτὸν χρόνον εἶναι καὶ μὴ εἶναι,  καὶ τἆλλα τὰ τοῦτον αὑτοῖς ἀντικείμενα τὸν τρόπον. καὶ περὶ τῶν τοιούτων ἁπλῶς μὲν οὐκ ἔστιν ἀπόδειξις, πρὸς τόνδε δὲ ἔστιν· οὐ γὰρ ἔστιν ἐκ πιστοτέρας ἀρχῆς αὐτοῦ τούτου ποιήσασθαι συλλογισμόν, δεῖ δέ γʼ εἴπερ ἔσται τὸ ἁπλῶς ἀποδεδεῖχθαι. πρὸς δὲ τὸν λέγοντα τὰς ἀντικειμένας φάσεις τῷ δεικνύντι διότι ψεῦδος ληπτέον τι τοιοῦτον ὃ ταὐτὸ μὲν ἔσται τῷ μὴ ἐνδέχεσθαι ταὐτὸ εἶναι καὶ μὴ εἶναι καθʼ ἕνα καὶ τὸν αὐτὸν χρόνον, μὴ δόξει δʼ εἶναι ταὐτόν· οὕτω γὰρ μόνως ἂν ἀποδειχθείη πρὸς τὸν φάσκοντα ἐνδέχεσθαι τὰς ἀντικειμένας φάσεις ἀληθεύεσθαι κατὰ τοῦ αὐτοῦ. τοὺς δὴ μέλλοντας ἀλλήλοις λόγου κοινωνήσειν δεῖ τι συνιέναι αὑτῶν· μὴ γιγνομένου γὰρ τούτου πῶς ἔσται κοινωνία τούτοις πρὸς ἀλλήλους λόγου; δεῖ τοίνυν τῶν ὀνομάτων ἕκαστον εἶναι γνώριμον καὶ δηλοῦν τι, καὶ μὴ πολλά, μόνον δὲ ἕν· ἂν δὲ πλείονα σημαίνῃ, φανερὸν ποιεῖν ἐφʼ ὃ φέρει τοὔνομα τούτων. ὁ δὴ λέγων εἶναι τοῦτο καὶ μὴ εἶναι, τοῦτο ὅ φησιν οὔ φησιν, ὥσθʼ ὃ σημαίνει τοὔνομα τοῦτʼ οὔ φησι σημαίνειν· τοῦτο δʼ ἀδύνατον. ὥστʼ εἴπερ σημαίνει τι τὸ εἶναι τόδε, τὴν ἀντίφασιν ἀδύνατον ἀληθεύειν. ἔτι δʼ εἴ τι σημαίνει τοὔνομα καὶ τοῦτʼ ἀληθεύεται, δεῖ τοῦτʼ ἐξ ἀνάγκης εἶναι· τὸ δʼ ἐξ ἀνάγκης ὂν οὐκ ἐνδέχεταί ποτε μὴ εἶναι· τὰς ἀντικειμένας ἄρα οὐκ ἐνδέχεται φάσεις καὶ ἀποφάσεις ἀληθεύειν κατὰ τοῦ αὐτοῦ. ἔτι δʼ εἰ μηθὲν μᾶλλον ἡ φάσις ἢ ἡ ἀπόφασις ἀληθεύεται, ὁ λέγων ἄνθρωπον ἢ οὐκ ἄνθρωπον οὐθὲν μᾶλλον ἀληθεύσει· δόξειε δὲ κἂν οὐχ ἵππον εἶναι φάσκων τὸν ἄνθρωπον ἢ μᾶλλον ἢ οὐχ ἧττον ἀληθεύειν ἢ οὐκ ἄνθρωπον, ὥστε καὶ ἵππον φάσκων εἶναι τὸν αὐτὸν ἀληθεύσει (τὰς γὰρ ἀντικειμένας ὁμοίως ἦν ἀληθεύειν)· συμβαίνει τοίνυν τὸν αὐτὸν ἄνθρωπον εἶναι καὶ ἵππον ἢ τῶν ἄλλων τι ζῴων.—ἀπόδειξις μὲν οὖν οὐδεμία τούτων ἐστὶν ἁπλῶς, πρὸς μέντοι τὸν ταῦτα τιθέμενον ἀπόδειξις. ταχέως δʼ ἄν τις καὶ αὐτὸν τὸν Ἡράκλειτον τοῦτον ἐρωτῶν τὸν τρόπον ἠνάγκασεν ὁμολογεῖν μηδέποτε τὰς ἀντικειμένας φάσεις δυνατὸν εἶναι κατὰ τῶν αὐτῶν ἀληθεύεσθαι· νῦν δʼ οὐ συνιεὶς ἑαυτοῦ τί ποτε λέγει, ταύτην ἔλαβε τὴν δόξαν. ὅλως δʼ εἰ τὸ λεγόμενον ὑπʼ αὐτοῦ ἐστὶν ἀληθές, οὐδʼ ἂν αὐτὸ τοῦτο εἴη ἀληθές,  λέγω δὲ τὸ ἐνδέχεσθαι τὸ αὐτὸ καθʼ ἕνα καὶ τὸν αὐτὸν χρόνον εἶναί τε καὶ μὴ εἶναι· καθάπερ γὰρ καὶ διῃρημένων αὐτῶν οὐδὲν μᾶλλον ἡ κατάφασις ἢ ἡ ἀπόφασις ἀληθεύεται, τὸν αὐτὸν τρόπον καὶ τοῦ συναμφοτέρου καὶ τοῦ συμπεπλεγμένου καθάπερ μιᾶς τινὸς καταφάσεως οὔσης οὐθὲν μᾶλλον ἢ ἡ ἀπόφασις  τὸ ὅλον ὡς ἐν καταφάσει τιθέμενον ἀληθεύσεται. ἔτι δʼ εἰ μηθὲν ἔστιν ἀληθῶς καταφῆσαι, κἂν αὐτὸ τοῦτο ψεῦδος εἴη τὸ φάναι μηδεμίαν ἀληθῆ κατάφασιν ὑπάρχειν. εἰ δʼ ἔστι τι, λύοιτʼ ἂν τὸ λεγόμενον ὑπὸ τῶν τὰ τοιαῦτα ἐνισταμένων καὶ παντελῶς ἀναιρούντων τὸ διαλέγεσθαι.\n\n\n\n \n\n \n\nπαραπλήσιον δὲ τοῖς εἰρημένοις ἐστὶ καὶ τὸ λεχθὲν ὑπὸ τοῦ Πρωταγόρου· καὶ γὰρ ἐκεῖνος ἔφη πάντων εἶναι χρημάτων μέτρον ἄνθρωπον, οὐδὲν ἕτερον λέγων ἢ τὸ δοκοῦν ἑκάστῳ τοῦτο καὶ εἶναι παγίως· τούτου δὲ γιγνομένου τὸ αὐτὸ συμβαίνει καὶ εἶναι καὶ μὴ εἶναι, καὶ κακὸν καὶ ἀγαθὸν εἶναι, καὶ τἆλλα τὰ κατὰ τὰς ἀντικειμένας λεγόμενα φάσεις, διὰ τὸ πολλάκις τοισδὶ μὲν φαίνεσθαι τόδε εἶναι καλὸν τοισδὶ δὲ τοὐναντίον, μέτρον δʼ εἶναι τὸ φαινόμενον ἑκάστῳ. λύοιτο δʼ ἂν αὕτη ἡ ἀπορία θεωρήσασι πόθεν ἐλήλυθεν ἡ ἀρχὴ τῆς ὑπολήψεως ταύτης· ἔοικε γὰρ ἐνίοις μὲν ἐκ τῆς τῶν φυσιολόγων δόξης γεγενῆσθαι, τοῖς δʼ ἐκ τοῦ μὴ ταὐτὰ περὶ τῶν αὐτῶν ἅπαντας γιγνώσκειν ἀλλὰ τοῖσδε μὲν ἡδὺ τόδε φαίνεσθαι τοῖσδε δὲ τοὐναντίον. τὸ γὰρ μηδὲν ἐκ μὴ ὄντος γίγνεσθαι, πᾶν δʼ ἐξ ὄντος, σχεδὸν ἁπάντων ἐστὶ κοινὸν δόγμα τῶν περὶ φύσεως· ἐπεὶ οὖν οὐ λευκὸν γίγνεται λευκοῦ τελέως ὄντος καὶ οὐδαμῇ μὴ λευκοῦ , γίγνοιτʼ ἂν ἐκ μὴ ὄντος λευκοῦ τὸ γιγνόμενον  λευκόν· ὥστε ἐκ μὴ ὄντος γίγνοιτʼ ἂν κατʼ ἐκείνους, εἰ μὴ ὑπῆρχε λευκὸν τὸ αὐτὸ καὶ μὴ λευκόν. οὐ χαλεπὸν δὲ διαλύειν τὴν ἀπορίαν ταύτην· εἴρηται γὰρ ἐν τοῖς φυσικοῖς πῶς ἐκ τοῦ μὴ ὄντος γίγνεται τὰ γιγνόμενα καὶ πῶς ἐξ ὄντος. τό γε μὴν ὁμοίως προσέχειν ταῖς δόξαις καὶ ταῖς φαντασίαις τῶν πρὸς αὑτοὺς διαμφισβητούντων εὔηθες· δῆλον γὰρ ὅτι τοὺς ἑτέρους αὐτῶν ἀνάγκη διεψεῦσθαι. φανερὸν δὲ τοῦτʼ ἐκ τῶν γιγνομένων κατὰ τὴν αἴσθησιν· οὐδέποτε γὰρ τὸ αὐτὸ φαίνεται τοῖς μὲν γλυκὺ τοῖς δὲ τοὐναντίον,  μὴ διεφθαρμένων καὶ λελωβημένων τῶν ἑτέρων τὸ αἰσθητήριον καὶ κριτήριον τῶν λεχθέντων χυμῶν. τούτου δʼ ὄντος τοιούτου τοὺς ἑτέρους μὲν ὑποληπτέον μέτρον εἶναι τοὺς δʼ ἄλλους οὐχ ὑποληπτέον. ὁμοίως δὲ τοῦτο λέγω καὶ ἐπὶ ἀγαθοῦ καὶ κακοῦ, καὶ καλοῦ καὶ αἰσχροῦ, καὶ τῶν ἄλλων τῶν τοιούτων. οὐδὲν γὰρ διαφέρει τοῦτʼ ἀξιοῦν ἢ τὰ φαινόμενα τοῖς ὑπὸ τὴν ὄψιν ὑποβάλλουσι τὸν δάκτυλον καὶ ποιοῦσιν ἐκ τοῦ ἑνὸς φαίνεσθαι δύο, δύο δεῖν εἶναι διὰ τὸ φαίνεσθαι τοσαῦτα, καὶ πάλιν ἕν· τοῖς γὰρ μὴ κινοῦσι τὴν ὄψιν ἓν φαίνεται τὸ ἕν. ὅλως δὲ ἄτοπον ἐκ τοῦ φαίνεσθαι τὰ δεῦρο μεταβάλλοντα καὶ μηδέποτε διαμένοντα ἐν τοῖς αὐτοῖς, ἐκ τούτου περὶ τῆς ἀληθείας τὴν κρίσιν ποιεῖσθαι· δεῖ γὰρ ἐκ τῶν ἀεὶ κατὰ ταὐτὰ ἐχόντων καὶ μηδεμίαν μεταβολὴν ποιουμένων τἀληθὲς θηρεύειν, τοιαῦτα δʼ ἐστὶ τὰ κατὰ τὸν κόσμον· ταῦτα γὰρ οὐχ ὁτὲ μὲν τοιαδὶ πάλιν δʼ ἀλλοῖα φαίνεται, ταὐτὰ δʼ ἀεὶ καὶ μεταβολῆς οὐδεμιᾶς κοινωνοῦντα. ἔτι δʼ εἰ κίνησις ἔστι, καὶ κινούμενόν τι, κινεῖται δὲ πᾶν ἔκ τινος καὶ εἴς τι· δεῖ ἄρα τὸ κινούμενον εἶναι ἐν ἐκείνῳ ἐξ οὗ κινήσεται καὶ οὐκ εἶναι ἐν αὐτῷ, καὶ εἰς τοδὶ κινεῖσθαι καὶ γίγνεσθαι ἐν τούτῳ, τὸ δὲ κατὰ τὴν ἀντίφασιν μὴ συναληθεύεσθαι κατʼ αὐτούς. καὶ εἰ κατὰ τὸ ποσὸν συνεχῶς τὰ δεῦρο ῥεῖ καὶ κινεῖται, καί τις τοῦτο θείη καίπερ οὐκ ἀληθὲς ὄν, διὰ τί κατὰ τὸ ποιὸν οὐ μενεῖ; φαίνονται γὰρ οὐχ ἥκιστα τὰ κατὰ τὰς ἀντιφάσεις  ταὐτοῦ κατηγορεῖν ἐκ τοῦ τὸ ποσὸν ὑπειληφέναι μὴ μένειν ἐπὶ τῶν σωμάτων, διὸ καὶ εἶναι τετράπηχυ τὸ αὐτὸ καὶ οὐκ εἶναι. ἡ δʼ οὐσία κατὰ τὸ ποιόν, τοῦτο δὲ τῆς ὡρισμένης φύσεως, τὸ δὲ ποσὸν τῆς ἀορίστου. ἔτι διὰ τί προστάττοντος τοῦ ἰατροῦ τοδὶ τὸ σιτίον προσενέγκασθαι προσφέρονται; τί γὰρ μᾶλλον τοῦτο ἄρτος ἐστὶν ἢ οὐκ ἔστιν; ὥστʼ οὐθὲν ἂν διέχοι φαγεῖν ἢ μὴ φαγεῖν· νῦν δʼ ὡς ἀληθεύοντες περὶ αὐτὸ καὶ ὄντος τοῦ προσταχθέντος σιτίου τούτου προσφέρονται τοῦτο· καίτοι γʼ οὐκ ἔδει μὴ διαμενούσης παγίως μηδεμιᾶς φύσεως ἐν τοῖς αἰσθητοῖς ἀλλʼ ἀεὶ πασῶν κινουμένων καὶ ῥεουσῶν. ἔτι δʼ εἰ μὲν ἀλλοιούμεθα ἀεὶ καὶ μηδέποτε διαμένομεν οἱ αὐτοί, τί καὶ θαυμαστὸν εἰ μηδέποθʼ ἡμῖν ταὐτὰ φαίνεται καθάπερ τοῖς κάμνουσιν  (καὶ γὰρ τούτοις διὰ τὸ μὴ ὁμοίως διακεῖσθαι τὴν ἕξιν καὶ ὅθʼ ὑγίαινον, οὐχ ὅμοια φαίνεται τὰ κατὰ τὰς αἰσθήσεις, αὐτὰ μὲν οὐδεμιᾶς διά γε τοῦτο μεταβολῆς κοινωνοῦντα τὰ αἰσθητά, αἰσθήματα δʼ ἕτερα ποιοῦντα τοῖς κάμνουσι καὶ μὴ τὰ αὐτά· τὸν αὐτὸν δὴ τρόπον ἔχειν καὶ τῆς εἰρημένης μεταβολῆς γιγνομένης ἴσως ἀναγκαῖόν ἐστιν); εἰ δὲ μὴ μεταβάλλομεν ἀλλʼ οἱ αὐτοὶ διατελοῦμεν ὄντες, εἴη ἄν τι μένον.—πρὸς μὲν οὖν τοὺς ἐκ λόγου τὰς εἰρημένας ἀπορίας ἔχοντας οὐ ῥᾴδιον διαλῦσαι μὴ τιθέντων τι καὶ τούτου μηκέτι λόγον ἀπαιτούντων· οὕτω γὰρ πᾶς λόγος καὶ πᾶσα ἀπόδειξις γίγνεται· μηθὲν γὰρ τιθέντες ἀναιροῦσι τὸ διαλέγεσθαι καὶ ὅλως λόγον,—ὥστε πρὸς μὲν τοὺς τοιούτους οὐκ ἔστι λόγος, πρὸς δὲ τοὺς διαποροῦντας ἐκ τῶν παραδεδομένων ἀποριῶν ῥᾴδιον ἀπαντᾶν καὶ διαλύειν τὰ ποιοῦντα τὴν ἀπορίαν ἐν αὐτοῖς· δῆλον δʼ ἐκ τῶν εἰρημένων. ὥστε φανερὸν ἐκ τούτων. ὅτι οὐκ ἐνδέχεται τὰς ἀντικειμένας φάσεις περὶ τοῦ αὐτοῦ καθʼ ἕνα χρόνον ἀληθεύειν, οὐδὲ τὰ ἐναντία, διὰ τὸ λέγεσθαι κατὰ στέρησιν πᾶσαν ἐναντιότητα· δῆλον δὲ τοῦτʼ ἐπʼ ἀρχὴν τοὺς λόγους ἀναλύουσι τοὺς τῶν ἐναντίων. ὁμοίως δʼ οὐδὲ τῶν ἀνὰ μέσον οὐδὲν οἷόν τε κατηγορεῖσθαι καθʼ ἑνὸς καὶ τοῦ αὐτοῦ· λευκοῦ γὰρ ὄντος τοῦ ὑποκειμένου λέγοντες αὐτὸ εἶναι οὔτε μέλαν οὔτε λευκὸν ψευσόμεθα· συμβαίνει γὰρ εἶναι λευκὸν αὐτὸ καὶ μὴ εἶναι· θάτερον γὰρ τῶν συμπεπλεγμένων ἀληθεύσεται κατʼ αὐτοῦ, τοῦτο δʼ ἐστὶν ἀντίφασις τοῦ λευκοῦ. οὔτε δὴ καθʼ Ἡράκλειτον ἐνδέχεται λέγοντας ἀληθεύειν, οὔτε κατʼ Ἀναξαγόραν· εἰ δὲ μή, συμβήσεται τἀναντία τοῦ αὐτοῦ κατηγορεῖν· ὅταν γὰρ ἐν παντὶ φῇ παντὸς εἶναι μοῖραν, οὐδὲν μᾶλλον εἶναί φησι γλυκὺ ἢ πικρὸν ἢ τῶν λοιπῶν ὁποιανοῦν ἐναντιώσεων, εἴπερ ἐν ἅπαντι πᾶν ὑπάρχει μὴ δυνάμει μόνον ἀλλʼ ἐνεργεία ι ικαὶ ἀποκεκριμένον. ὁμοίως δὲ οὐδὲ πάσας ψευδεῖς οὐδʼ ἀληθεῖς τὰς φάσεις δυνατὸν εἶναι, διʼ ἄλλα τε πολλὰ τῶν συναχθέντων ἂν δυσχερῶν διὰ ταύτην τὴν θέσιν, καὶ διότι ψευδῶν μὲν οὐσῶν πασῶν οὐδʼ αὐτὸ τοῦτό τις φάσκων ἀληθεύσει, ἀληθῶν δὲ ψευδεῖς εἶναι πάσας λέγων οὐ ψεύσεται.\n\n\n\n \n\n \n\nπᾶσα δʼ ἐπιστήμη ζητεῖ τινὰς ἀρχὰς καὶ αἰτίας περὶ ἕκαστον τῶν ὑφʼ αὑτὴν ἐπιστητῶν,  οἷον ἰατρικὴ καὶ γυμναστικὴ καὶ τῶν λοιπῶν ἑκάστη τῶν ποιητικῶν καὶ μαθηματικῶν. ἑκάστη γὰρ τούτων περιγραψαμένη τι γένος αὑτῇ περὶ τοῦτο πραγματεύεται ὡς ὑπάρχον καὶ ὄν, οὐχ ᾗ δὲ ὄν, ἀλλʼ ἑτέρα τις αὕτη παρὰ ταύτας τὰς ἐπιστήμας ἐστὶν ἐπιστήμη. τῶν δὲ λεχθεισῶν ἐπιστημῶν ἑκάστη λαβοῦσά πως τὸ τί ἐστιν ἐν ἑκάστῳ γένει πειρᾶται δεικνύναι τὰ λοιπὰ μαλακώτερον ἢ ἀκριβέστερον. λαμβάνουσι δὲ τὸ τί ἐστιν αἱ μὲν διʼ αἰσθήσεως αἱ δʼ ὑποτιθέμεναι· διὸ καὶ δῆλον ἐκ τῆς τοιαύτης ἐπαγωγῆς ὅτι τῆς οὐσίας καὶ τοῦ τί ἐστιν οὐκ ἔστιν ἀπόδειξις. ἐπεὶ δʼ ἔστι τις ἡ περὶ φύσεως ἐπιστήμη, δῆλον ὅτι καὶ πρακτικῆς ἑτέρα καὶ ποιητικῆς ἔσται. ποιητικῆς μὲν γὰρ ἐν τῷ ποιοῦντι καὶ οὐ τῷ ποιουμένῳ τῆς κινήσεως ἡ ἀρχή, καὶ τοῦτʼ ἔστιν εἴτε τέχνη τις εἴτʼ ἄλλη τις δύναμις· ὁμοίως δὲ καὶ τῆς πρακτικῆς οὐκ ἐν τῷ πρακτῷ μᾶλλον δʼ ἐν τοῖς πράττουσιν ἡ κίνησις. ἡ δὲ τοῦ φυσικοῦ περὶ τὰ ἔχοντʼ ἐν ἑαυτοῖς κινήσεως ἀρχήν ἐστιν. ὅτι μὲν τοίνυν οὔτε πρακτικὴν οὔτε ποιητικὴν ἀλλὰ θεωρητικὴν ἀναγκαῖον εἶναι τὴν φυσικὴν ἐπιστήμην, δῆλον ἐκ τούτων (εἰς ἓν γάρ τι τούτων τῶν γενῶν ἀνάγκη πίπτειν)· ἐπεὶ δὲ τὸ τί ἐστιν ἀναγκαῖον ἑκάστῃ πως τῶν ἐπιστημῶν εἰδέναι καὶ τούτῳ χρῆσθαι ἀρχῇ, δεῖ μὴ λανθάνειν πῶς ὁριστέον τῷ φυσικῷ καὶ πῶς ὁ τῆς οὐσίας λόγος ληπτέος, πότερον ὡς τὸ σιμὸν ἢ μᾶλλον ὡς τὸ κοῖλον. τούτων γὰρ ὁ μὲν τοῦ σιμοῦ λόγος μετὰ τῆς ὕλης λέγεται τῆς τοῦ πράγματος, ὁ δὲ τοῦ κοίλου χωρὶς τῆς ὕλης· ἡ γὰρ σιμότης ἐν ῥινὶ γίγνεται, διὸ καὶ ὁ λόγος αὐτῆς μετὰ ταύτης θεωρεῖται· τὸ σιμὸν γάρ ἐστι ῥὶς κοίλη. φανερὸν οὖν ὅτι καὶ σαρκὸς καὶ ὀφθαλμοῦ καὶ τῶν λοιπῶν μορίων μετὰ τῆς ὕλης ἀεὶ τὸν λόγον ἀποδοτέον. ἐπεὶ δʼ ἔστι τις ἐπιστήμη τοῦ ὄντος ᾗ ὂν καὶ χωριστόν, σκεπτέον πότερόν ποτε τῇ φυσικῆ ι ιτὴν αὐτὴν θετέον εἶναι ταύτην ἢ μᾶλλον ἑτέραν. ἡ μὲν οὖν φυσικὴ περὶ τὰ κινήσεως ἔχοντʼ ἀρχὴν ἐν αὑτοῖς ἐστίν, ἡ δὲ μαθηματικὴ θεωρητικὴ μὲν καὶ περὶ μένοντά τις αὕτη, ἀλλʼ οὐ χωριστά. περὶ τὸ χωριστὸν ἄρα ὂν καὶ ἀκίνητον ἑτέρα τούτων ἀμφοτέρων τῶν ἐπιστημῶν ἔστι τις, εἴπερ ὑπάρχει τις οὐσία τοιαύτη, λέγω δὲ χωριστὴ καὶ ἀκίνητος, ὅπερ πειρασόμεθα δεικνύναι. καὶ εἴπερ ἔστι τις τοιαύτη φύσις ἐν τοῖς οὖσιν, ἐνταῦθʼ ἂν εἴη που καὶ τὸ θεῖον, καὶ αὕτη ἂν εἴη πρώτη καὶ κυριωτάτη ἀρχή.  δῆλον τοίνυν ὅτι τρία γένη τῶν θεωρητικῶν ἐπιστημῶν ἔστι, φυσική, μαθηματική, θεολογική. βέλτιστον μὲν οὖν τὸ τῶν θεωρητικῶν γένος, τούτων δʼ αὐτῶν ἡ τελευταία λεχθεῖσα· περὶ τὸ τιμιώτατον γάρ ἐστι τῶν ὄντων, βελτίων δὲ καὶ χείρων ἑκάστη λέγεται κατὰ τὸ οἰκεῖον ἐπιστητόν. ἀπορήσειε δʼ ἄν τις πότερόν ποτε τὴν τοῦ ὄντος ᾗ ὂν ἐπιστήμην καθόλου δεῖ θεῖναι ἢ οὔ. τῶν μὲν γὰρ μαθηματικῶν ἑκάστη περὶ ἕν τι γένος ἀφωρισμένον ἐστίν, ἡ δὲ καθόλου κοινὴ περὶ πάντων. εἰ μὲν οὖν αἱ φυσικαὶ οὐσίαι πρῶται τῶν ὄντων εἰσί, κἂν ἡ φυσικὴ πρώτη τῶν ἐπιστημῶν εἴη· εἰ δʼ ἔστιν ἑτέρα φύσις καὶ οὐσία χωριστὴ καὶ ἀκίνητος, ἑτέραν ἀνάγκη καὶ τὴν ἐπιστήμην αὐτῆς εἶναι καὶ προτέραν τῆς φυσικῆς καὶ καθόλου τῷ προτέραν.\n\n\n\n \n\n \n\nἐπεὶ δὲ τὸ ἁπλῶς ὂν κατὰ πλείους λέγεται τρόπους, ὧν εἷς ἐστὶν ὁ κατὰ συμβεβηκὸς εἶναι λεγόμενος, σκεπτέον πρῶτον περὶ τοῦ οὕτως ὄντος. ὅτι μὲν οὖν οὐδεμία τῶν παραδεδομένων ἐπιστημῶν πραγματεύεται περὶ τὸ συμβεβηκός, δῆλον (οὔτε γὰρ οἰκοδομικὴ σκοπεῖ τὸ συμβησόμενον τοῖς τῇ οἰκίᾳ χρησομένοις, οἷον εἰ λυπηρῶς ἢ τοὐναντίον οἰκήσουσιν, οὔθʼ ὑφαντικὴ οὔτε σκυτοτομικὴ οὔτε ὀψοποιική, τὸ δὲ καθʼ αὑτὴν ἴδιον ἑκάστη τούτων σκοπεῖ τῶν ἐπιστημῶν μόνον, τοῦτο δʼ ἐστὶ τὸ οἰκεῖον τέλος·  οὐδὲ τὸν ὄντα μουσικὸν ὅτι γενόμενος γραμματικὸς ἅμα ἔσται τὰ ἀμφότερα, πρότερον οὐκ ὤν, ὃ δὲ μὴ ἀεὶ ὂν ἔστιν, ἐγένετο τοῦτο, ὥσθʼ ἅμα μουσικὸς ἐγένετο καὶ γραμματικός,—τοῦτο δὲ οὐδεμία ζητεῖ τῶν ὁμολογουμένως οὐσῶν ἐπιστημῶν πλὴν ἡ σοφιστική· περὶ τὸ συμβεβηκὸς γὰρ αὕτη μόνη πραγματεύεται, διὸ Πλάτων οὐ κακῶς εἴρηκε φήσας τὸν σοφιστὴν περὶ τὸ μὴ ὂν διατρίβειν)· ὅτι δʼ οὐδʼ ἐνδεχόμενόν ἐστιν εἶναι τοῦ συμβεβηκότος ἐπιστήμην, φανερὸν ἔσται πειραθεῖσιν ἰδεῖν τί ποτʼ ἐστὶ τὸ συμβεβηκός. πᾶν δή φαμεν εἶναι τὸ μὲν ἀεὶ καὶ ἐξ ἀνάγκης (ἀνάγκης δʼ οὐ τῆς κατὰ τὸ βίαιον λεγομένης ἀλλʼ ᾗ χρώμεθα ἐν τοῖς κατὰ τὰς ἀποδείξεις), τὸ δʼ ὡς ἐπὶ τὸ πολύ, τὸ δʼ οὔθʼ ὡς ἐπὶ τὸ πολὺ οὔτʼ ἀεὶ καὶ ἐξ ἀνάγκης ἀλλʼ ὅπως ἔτυχεν· οἷον ἐπὶ κυνὶ γένοιτʼ ἂν ψῦχος, ἀλλὰ τοῦτʼ οὔτʼ  ἀεὶ καὶ ἐξ ἀνάγκης οὔθʼ ὡς ἐπὶ τὸ πολὺ γίγνεται, συμβαίη δέ ποτʼ ἄν.  ἔστι δὴ τὸ συμβεβηκὸς ὃ γίγνεται μέν, οὐκ ἀεὶ δʼ οὐδʼ ἐξ ἀνάγκης οὐδʼ ὡς ἐπὶ τὸ πολύ. τί μὲν οὖν ἐστὶ τὸ συμβεβηκός, εἴρηται, διότι δʼ οὐκ ἔστιν ἐπιστήμη τοῦ τοιούτου, δῆλον· ἐπιστήμη μὲν γὰρ πᾶσα τοῦ ἀεὶ ὄντος ἢ ὡς ἐπὶ τὸ πολύ, τὸ δὲ συμβεβηκὸς ἐν οὐδετέρῳ τούτων ἐστίν. ὅτι δὲ τοῦ κατὰ συμβεβηκὸς ὄντος οὐκ εἰσὶν αἰτίαι καὶ ἀρχαὶ τοιαῦται οἷαίπερ τοῦ καθʼ αὑτὸ ὄντος, δῆλον· ἔσται γὰρ ἅπαντʼ ἐξ ἀνάγκης. εἰ γὰρ τόδε μὲν ἔστι τοῦδε ὄντος τόδε δὲ τοῦδε, τοῦτο δὲ μὴ ὅπως ἔτυχεν ἀλλʼ ἐξ ἀνάγκης, ἐξ ἀνάγκης ἔσται καὶ οὗ τοῦτʼ ἦν αἴτιον ἕως τοῦ τελευταίου λεγομένου αἰτιατοῦ (τοῦτο δʼ ἦν κατὰ συμβεβηκός), ὥστʼ ἐξ ἀνάγκης ἅπαντʼ ἔσται, καὶ τὸ ὁποτέρως ἔτυχε καὶ τὸ ἐνδέχεσθαι καὶ γενέσθαι καὶ μὴ παντελῶς ἐκ τῶν γιγνομένων ἀναιρεῖται. κἂν μὴ ὂν δὲ ἀλλὰ γιγνόμενον τὸ αἴτιον ὑποτεθῇ, ταὐτὰ συμβήσεται· πᾶν γὰρ ἐξ ἀνάγκης γενήσεται. ἡ γὰρ αὔριον ἔκλειψις γενήσεται ἂν τόδε γένηται, τοῦτο δʼ ἐὰν ἕτερόν τι, καὶ τοῦτʼ ἂν ἄλλο· καὶ τοῦτον δὴ τὸν τρόπον ἀπὸ πεπερασμένου χρόνου τοῦ ἀπὸ τοῦ νῦν μέχρι αὔριον ἀφαιρουμένου χρόνου ἥξει ποτὲ εἰς τὸ ὑπάρχον, ὥστʼ ἐπεὶ τοῦτʼ ἔστιν, ἅπαντʼ ἐξ ἀνάγκης τὰ μετὰ τοῦτο γενήσεται, ὥστε πάντα ἐξ ἀνάγκης γίγνεσθαι. τὸ δʼ ὡς ἀληθὲς ὂν καὶ κατὰ συμβεβηκὸς τὸ μέν ἐστιν ἐν συμπλοκῇ διανοίας καὶ πάθος ἐν ταύτῃ (διὸ περὶ μὲν τὸ οὕτως ὂν οὐ ζητοῦνται αἱ ἀρχαί, περὶ δὲ τὸ ἔξω ὂν καὶ χωριστόν)· τὸ δʼ οὐκ ἀναγκαῖον ἀλλʼ ἀόριστον, λέγω δὲ τὸ κατὰ συμβεβηκός· τοῦ τοιούτου δʼ ἄτακτα καὶ ἄπειρα τὰ αἴτια.—τὸ δὲ ἕνεκά του ἐν τοῖς φύσει γιγνομένοις ἢ ἀπὸ διανοίας ἐστίν, τύχη δέ ἐστιν ὅταν τι τούτων γένηται κατὰ συμβεβηκός· ὥσπερ γὰρ καὶ ὄν ἐστι τὸ μὲν καθʼ αὑτὸ τὸ δὲ κατὰ συμβεβηκός, οὕτω καὶ αἴτιον. ἡ τύχη δʼ αἰτία κατὰ συμβεβηκὸς ἐν τοῖς κατὰ προαίρεσιν τῶν ἕνεκά του γιγνομένοις, διὸ περὶ ταὐτὰ τύχη καὶ διάνοια· προαίρεσις γὰρ οὐ χωρὶς διανοίας. τὰ δʼ αἴτια ἀόριστα ἀφʼ ὧν ἂν γένοιτο τὰ ἀπὸ τύχης, διὸ ἄδηλος ἀνθρωπίνῳ λογισμῷ καὶ αἴτιον κατὰ συμβεβηκός, ἁπλῶς δʼ οὐδενός. ἀγαθὴ δὲ τύχη καὶ κακὴ ὅταν ἀγαθὸν ἢ φαῦλον ἀποβῇ· εὐτυχία δὲ καὶ δυστυχία περὶ μέγεθος τούτων.  ἐπεὶ δʼ οὐθὲν κατὰ συμβεβηκὸς πρότερον τῶν καθʼ αὑτό, οὐδʼ ἄρʼ αἴτια· εἰ ἄρα τύχη ἢ τὸ αὐτόματον αἴτιον τοῦ οὐρανοῦ, πρότερον νοῦς αἴτιος καὶ φύσις.\n\n\n\n \n\n \n\nἔστι δὲ τὸ μὲν ἐνεργείᾳ μόνον τὸ δὲ δυνάμει τὸ δὲ δυνάμει καὶ ἐνεργείᾳ, τὸ μὲν ὂν τὸ δὲ ποσὸν τὸ δὲ τῶν λοιπῶν. οὐκ ἔστι δέ τις κίνησις παρὰ τὰ πράγματα· μεταβάλλει γὰρ ἀεὶ κατὰ τὰς τοῦ ὄντος κατηγορίας, κοινὸν δʼ ἐπὶ τούτων οὐδέν ἐστιν ὃ οὐδʼ ἐν μιᾷ κατηγορίᾳ. ἕκαστον δὲ διχῶς ὑπάρχει πᾶσιν (οἷον τὸ τόδε—τὸ μὲν γὰρ μορφὴ αὐτοῦ τὸ δὲ στέρησις—καὶ κατὰ τὸ ποιὸν τὸ μὲν λευκὸν τὸ δὲ μέλαν, καὶ κατὰ τὸ ποσὸν τὸ μὲν τέλειον τὸ δὲ ἀτελές, καὶ κατὰ φορὰν τὸ μὲν ἄνω τὸ δὲ κάτω, ἢ κοῦφον καὶ βαρύ)· ὥστε κινήσεως καὶ μεταβολῆς τοσαῦτʼ εἴδη ὅσα τοῦ ὄντος. διῃρημένου δὲ καθʼ ἕκαστον γένος τοῦ μὲν δυνάμει τοῦ δʼ ἐντελεχείᾳ, τὴν τοῦ δυνάμει ᾗ τοιοῦτόν ἐστιν ἐνέργειαν λέγω κίνησιν. ὅτι δʼ ἀληθῆ λέγομεν, ἐνθένδε δῆλον· ὅταν γὰρ τὸ οἰκοδομητόν, ᾗ τοιοῦτον αὐτὸ λέγομεν εἶναι, ἐνεργείᾳ ᾖ, οἰκοδομεῖται, καὶ ἔστι τοῦτο οἰκοδόμησις· ὁμοίως μάθησις, ἰάτρευσις, βάδισις, ἅλσις, γήρανσις, ἅδρυνσις. συμβαίνει δὲ κινεῖσθαι ὅταν ἡ ἐντελέχεια ᾖ αὐτή, καὶ οὔτε πρότερον οὔθʼ ὕστερον. ἡ δὴ τοῦ δυνάμει ὄντος, ὅταν ἐντελεχείᾳ ὂν ἐνεργῇ, οὐχ ᾗ αὐτὸ ἀλλʼ ᾗ κινητόν, κίνησίς ἐστιν. λέγω δὲ τὸ ᾗ ὧδε. ἔστι γὰρ ὁ χαλκὸς δυνάμει ἀνδριάς· ἀλλʼ ὅμως οὐχ ἡ τοῦ χαλκοῦ ἐντελέχεια, ᾗ χαλκός, κίνησίς ἐστιν. οὐ γὰρ ταὐτὸν χαλκῷ εἶναι καὶ δυνάμει τινί, ἐπεὶ εἰ ταὐτὸν ἦν ἁπλῶς κατὰ τὸν λόγον, ἦν ἂν ἡ τοῦ χαλκοῦ ἐντελέχεια κίνησίς τις. οὐκ ἔστι δὲ ταὐτό (δῆλον δʼ ἐπὶ τῶν ἐναντίων· τὸ μὲν γὰρ δύνασθαι ὑγιαίνειν καὶ δύνασθαι κάμνειν οὐ ταὐτόν—καὶ γὰρ ἂν τὸ ὑγιαίνειν καὶ τὸ κάμνειν ταὐτὸν ἦν—τὸ δʼ ὑποκείμενον καὶ ὑγιαῖνον καὶ νοσοῦν, εἴθʼ ὑγρότης εἴθʼ αἷμα, ταὐτὸ καὶ ἕν). ἐπεὶ δὲ οὐ τὸ αὐτό, ὥσπερ οὐδὲ χρῶμα ταὐτὸν καὶ ὁρατόν, ἡ τοῦ δυνατοῦ καὶ ᾗ δυνατὸν ἐντελέχεια κίνησίς ἐστιν. ὅτι μὲν οὖν ἐστιν αὕτη, καὶ ὅτι συμβαίνει τότε κινεῖσθαι ὅταν ἡ ἐντελέχεια ᾖ αὐτή, καὶ οὔτε πρότερον οὔθʼ ὕστερον, δῆλον (ἐνδέχεται γὰρ ἕκαστον ὁτὲ μὲν ἐνεργεῖν ὁτὲ δὲ μή,  οἷον τὸ οἰκοδομητὸν ᾗ οἰκοδομητόν, καὶ ἡ τοῦ οἰκοδομητοῦ ἐνέργεια ᾗ οἰκοδομητὸν οἰκοδόμησίς ἐστιν· ἢ γὰρ τοῦτό ἐστιν, ἡ οἰκοδόμησις, ἡ ἐνέργεια, ἢ οἰκία· ἀλλʼ ὅταν οἰκία ᾖ, οὐκέτι οἰκοδομητόν, οἰκοδομεῖται δὲ τὸ οἰκοδομητόν· ἀνάγκη ἄρα οἰκοδόμησιν τὴν ἐνέργειαν εἶναι, ἡ δʼ οἰκοδόμησις κίνησίς τις, ὁ δʼ αὐτὸς λόγος καὶ ἐπὶ τῶν ἄλλων κινήσεων)· ὅτι δὲ καλῶς εἴρηται, δῆλον ἐξ ὧν οἱ ἄλλοι λέγουσι περὶ αὐτῆς, καὶ ἐκ τοῦ μὴ ῥᾴδιον εἶναι διορίσαι ἄλλως αὐτήν. οὔτε γὰρ ἐν ἄλλῳ τις γένει δύναιτʼ ἂν θεῖναι αὐτήν· δῆλον δʼ ἐξ ὧν λέγουσιν· οἱ μὲν γὰρ ἑτερότητα καὶ ἀνισότητα καὶ τὸ μὴ ὄν, ὧν οὐδὲν ἀνάγκη κινεῖσθαι, ἀλλʼ οὐδʼ ἡ μεταβολὴ οὔτʼ εἰς ταῦτα οὔτʼ ἐκ τούτων μᾶλλον ἢ τῶν ἀντικειμένων. αἴτιον δὲ τοῦ εἰς ταῦτα τιθέναι ὅτι ἀόριστόν τι δοκεῖ εἶναι ἡ κίνησις, τῆς δʼ ἑτέρας συστοιχίας αἱ ἀρχαὶ διὰ τὸ στερητικαὶ εἶναι ἀόριστοι· οὔτε γὰρ τόδε οὔτε τοιόνδε οὐδεμία αὐτῶν οὔτε τῶν λοιπῶν κατηγοριῶν. τοῦ δὲ δοκεῖν ἀόριστον εἶναι τὴν κίνησιν αἴτιον ὅτι οὔτʼ εἰς δύναμιν τῶν ὄντων οὔτʼ εἰς ἐνέργειαν ἔστι θεῖναι αὐτήν· οὔτε γὰρ τὸ δυνατὸν ποσὸν εἶναι κινεῖται ἐξ ἀνάγκης, οὔτε τὸ ἐνεργείᾳ ποσόν, ἥ τε κίνησις ἐνέργεια μὲν εἶναι δοκεῖ τις, ἀτελὴς δέ· αἴτιον δʼ ὅτι ἀτελὲς τὸ δυνατὸν οὗ ἐστὶν ἐνέργεια. καὶ διὰ τοῦτο χαλεπὸν αὐτὴν λαβεῖν τί ἐστιν· ἢ γὰρ εἰς στέρησιν ἀνάγκη θεῖναι ἢ εἰς δύναμιν ἢ εἰς ἐνέργειαν ἁπλῆν, τούτων δʼ οὐδὲν φαίνεται ἐνδεχόμενον, ὥστε λείπεται τὸ λεχθὲν εἶναι, καὶ ἐνέργειαν καὶ  ἐνέργειαν τὴν εἰρημένην, ἰδεῖν μὲν χαλεπὴν ἐνδεχομένην δʼ εἶναι. καὶ ὅτι ἐστὶν ἡ κίνησις ἐν τῷ κινητῷ, δῆλον· ἐντελέχεια γάρ ἐστι τούτου ὑπὸ τοῦ κινητικοῦ. καὶ ἡ τοῦ κινητικοῦ ἐνέργεια οὐκ ἄλλη ἐστίν. δεῖ μὲν γὰρ εἶναι ἐντελέχειαν ἀμφοῖν· κινητικὸν μὲν γάρ ἐστι τῷ δύνασθαι, κινοῦν δὲ τῷ ἐνεργεῖν, ἀλλʼ ἔστιν ἐνεργητικὸν τοῦ κινητοῦ, ὥσθʼ ὁμοίως μία ἡ ἀμφοῖν ἐνέργεια ὥσπερ τὸ αὐτὸ διάστημα ἓν πρὸς δύο καὶ δύο πρὸς ἕν, καὶ τὸ ἄναντες καὶ τὸ κάταντες, ἀλλὰ τὸ εἶναι οὐχ ἕν· ὁμοίως δὲ καὶ ἐπὶ τοῦ κινοῦντος καὶ κινουμένου.\n\n\n\n \n\n \n\nτὸ δʼ ἄπειρον ἢ τὸ ἀδύνατον διελθεῖν τῷ μὴ πεφυκέναι διιέναι, καθάπερ ἡ φωνὴ ἀόρατος, ἢ τὸ διέξοδον ἔχον ἀτελεύτητον, ἢ ὃ μόλις, ἢ ὃ πεφυκὸς ἔχειν μὴ ἔχει διέξοδον ἢ πέρας· ἔτι προσθέσει ἢ ἀφαιρέσει ἢ ἄμφω.  χωριστὸν μὲν δὴ αὐτό τι ὂν οὐχ οἷόν τʼ εἶναι· εἰ γὰρ μήτε μέγεθος μήτε πλῆθος, οὐσία δʼ αὐτὸ τὸ ἄπειρον καὶ μὴ συμβεβηκός, ἀδιαίρετον ἔσται (τὸ γὰρ διαιρετὸν ἢ μέγεθος ἢ πλῆθος), εἰ δὲ ἀδιαίρετον, οὐκ ἄπειρον, εἰ μὴ καθάπερ ἡ φωνὴ ἀόρατος· ἀλλʼ οὐχ οὕτω λέγουσιν οὐδʼ ἡμεῖς ζητοῦμεν, ἀλλʼ ὡς ἀδιέξοδον. ἔτι πῶς ἐνδέχεται καθʼ αὑτὸ εἶναι ἄπειρον, εἰ μὴ καὶ ἀριθμὸς καὶ μέγεθος, ὧν πάθος τὸ ἄπειρον; ἔτι εἰ κατὰ συμβεβηκός, οὐκ ἂν εἴη στοιχεῖον τῶν ὄντων ᾗ ἄπειρον, ὥσπερ οὐδὲ τὸ ἀόρατον τῆς διαλέκτου, καίτοι ἡ φωνὴ ἀόρατος. καὶ ὅτι οὐκ ἔστιν ἐνεργείᾳ εἶναι τὸ ἄπειρον, δῆλον. ἔσται γὰρ ὁτιοῦν αὐτοῦ ἄπειρον μέρος τὸ λαμβανόμενον (τὸ γὰρ ἀπείρῳ εἶναι καὶ ἄπειρον τὸ αὐτό, εἴπερ οὐσία τὸ ἄπειρον καὶ μὴ καθʼ ὑποκειμένου), ὥστε ἢ ἀδιαίρετον, ἢ εἰς ἄπειρα διαιρετόν, εἰ μεριστόν· πολλὰ δʼ εἶναι τὸ αὐτὸ ἀδύνατον ἄπειρα (ὥσπερ γὰρ ἀέρος ἀὴρ μέρος, οὕτως ἄπειρον ἀπείρου, εἰ ἔστιν οὐσία καὶ ἀρχή)· ἀμέριστον ἄρα καὶ ἀδιαίρετον. ἀλλὰ ἀδύνατον τὸ ἐντελεχείᾳ ὂν ἄπειρον (ποσὸν γὰρ εἶναι ἀνάγκη)· κατὰ συμβεβηκὸς ἄρα ὑπάρχει. ἀλλʼ εἰ οὕτως, εἴρηται ὅτι οὐκ ἐνδέχεται εἶναι ἀρχήν, ἀλλʼ ἐκεῖνο ᾧ συμβέβηκε, τὸν ἀέρα ἢ τὸ ἄρτιον.—αὕτη μὲν οὖν ἡ ζήτησις καθόλου, ὅτι δʼ ἐν τοῖς αἰσθητοῖς οὐκ ἔστιν, ἐνθένδε δῆλον· εἰ γὰρ σώματος λόγος τὸ ἐπιπέδοις ὡρισμένον, οὐκ εἴη ἂν ἄπειρον σῶμα οὔτʼ αἰσθητὸν οὔτε νοητόν, οὐδʼ ἀριθμὸς ὡς κεχωρισμένος καὶ ἄπειρος· ἀριθμητὸν γὰρ ὁ ἀριθμὸς ἢ τὸ ἔχον ἀριθμόν. φυσικῶς δὲ ἐκ τῶνδε δῆλον· οὔτε γὰρ σύνθετον οἷόν τʼ εἶναι οὔθʼ ἁπλοῦν. σύνθετον μὲν γὰρ οὐκ ἔσται σῶμα, εἰ πεπέρανται τῷ πλήθει τὰ στοιχεῖα (δεῖ γὰρ ἰσάζειν τὰ ἐναντία καὶ μὴ εἶναι ἓν αὐτῶν ἄπειρον· εἰ γὰρ ὁτῳοῦν λείπεται ἡ θατέρου σώματος δύναμις, φθαρήσεται ὑπὸ τοῦ ἀπείρου τὸ πεπερασμένον· ἕκαστον δʼ ἄπειρον εἶναι ἀδύνατον, σῶμα γάρ ἐστι τὸ πάντῃ ἔχον διάστασιν, ἄπειρον δὲ τὸ ἀπεράντως διεστηκός, ὥστʼ εἰ τὸ ἄπειρον σῶμα, πάντῃ ἔσται ἄπειρον)· οὐδὲ ἓν δὲ καὶ ἁπλοῦν ἐνδέχεται τὸ ἄπειρον εἶναι σῶμα, οὔθʼ ὡς λέγουσί τινες, παρὰ τὰ στοιχεῖα ἐξ οὗ γεννῶσι ταῦτα (οὐκ ἔστι γὰρ τοιοῦτο σῶμα παρὰ τὰ στοιχεῖα· ἅπαν γάρ, ἐξ οὗ ἐστί, καὶ διαλύεται εἰς τοῦτο, οὐ φαίνεται δὲ τοῦτο παρὰ τὰ ἁπλᾶ σώματα),  οὐδὲ πῦρ οὐδʼ ἄλλο τῶν στοιχείων οὐθέν· χωρὶς γὰρ τοῦ ἄπειρον εἶναί τι αὐτῶν, ἀδύνατον τὸ ἅπαν, κἂν ᾖ πεπερασμένον, ἢ εἶναι ἢ γίγνεσθαι ἕν τι αὐτῶν, ὥσπερ Ἡράκλειτός φησιν ἅπαντα γίγνεσθαί ποτε πῦρ. ὁ δʼ αὐτὸς λόγος καὶ ἐπὶ τοῦ ἑνὸς ὃ ποιοῦσι παρὰ τὰ στοιχεῖα οἱ φυσικοί· πᾶν γὰρ μεταβάλλει ἐξ ἐναντίου, οἷον ἐκ θερμοῦ εἰς ψυχρόν.—ἔτι τὸ αἰσθητὸν σῶμα πού, καὶ ὁ αὐτὸς τόπος ὅλου καὶ μορίου, οἷον τῆς γῆς, ὥστʼ εἰ μὲν ὁμοειδές, ἀκίνητον ἔσται ἢ ἀεὶ οἰσθήσεται, τοῦτο δὲ ἀδύνατον (τί γὰρ μᾶλλον κάτω ἢ ἄνω ἢ ὁπουοῦν; οἷον εἰ βῶλος εἴη, ποῦ αὕτη κινήσεται ἢ μενεῖ; ὁ γὰρ τόπος τοῦ συγγενοῦς αὐτῇ σώματος ἄπειρος· καθέξει οὖν τὸν ὅλον τόπον; καὶ πῶς; τίς οὖν ἡ μονὴ καὶ ἡ κίνησις; ἢ πανταχοῦ μενεῖ—οὐ κινηθήσεται ἄρα, ἢ πανταχοῦ κινηθήσεται —οὐκ ἄρα στήσεται)· εἰ δʼ ἀνόμοιον τὸ πᾶν, ἀνόμοιοι καὶ οἱ τόποι, καὶ πρῶτον μὲν οὐχ ἓν τὸ σῶμα τοῦ παντὸς ἀλλʼ ἢ τῷ ἅπτεσθαι, εἶτα ἢ πεπερασμένα ταῦτʼ ἔσται ἢ ἄπειρα εἴδει. πεπερασμένα μὲν οὖν οὐχ οἷόν τε (ἔσται γὰρ τὰ μὲν ἄπειρα τὰ δʼ οὔ, εἰ τὸ πᾶν ἄπειρον, οἷον πῦρ ἢ ὕδωρ· φθορὰ δὲ τὸ τοιοῦτον τοῖς ἐναντίοις)· εἰ δʼ ἄπειρα καὶ ἁπλᾶ, καὶ οἱ τόποι ἄπειροι καὶ ἔσται ἄπειρα στοιχεῖα· εἰ δὲ τοῦτʼ ἀδύνατον καὶ οἱ τόποι πεπερασμένοι, καὶ τὸ πᾶν ἀνάγκη πεπεράνθαι. ὅλως δʼ ἀδύνατον ἄπειρον εἶναι σῶμα καὶ τόπον τοῖς σώμασιν, εἰ πᾶν σῶμα αἰσθητὸν ἢ βάρος ἔχει ἢ κουφότητα· ἢ γὰρ ἐπὶ τὸ μέσον ἢ ἄνω οἰσθήσεται, ἀδύνατον δὲ τὸ ἄπειρον ἢ πᾶν ἢ τὸ ἥμισυ ὁποτερονοῦν πεπονθέναι· πῶς γὰρ διελεῖς; ἢ πῶς τοῦ ἀπείρου ἔσται τὸ μὲν κάτω τὸ δʼ ἄνω, ἢ ἔσχατον καὶ μέσον; ἔτι πᾶν σῶμα αἰσθητὸν ἐν τόπῳ, τόπου δὲ εἴδη ἕξ, ἀδύνατον δʼ ἐν τῷ ἀπείρῳ σώματι ταῦτʼ εἶναι. ὅλως δʼ εἰ ἀδύνατον τόπον ἄπειρον εἶναι, καὶ σῶμα ἀδύνατον· τὸ γὰρ ἐν τόπῳ πού, τοῦτο δὲ σημαίνει ἢ ἄνω ἢ κάτω ἢ τῶν λοιπῶν τι, τούτων δʼ ἕκαστον πέρας τι. τὸ δʼ ἄπειρον οὐ ταὐτὸν ἐν μεγέθει καὶ κινήσει καὶ χρόνῳ ὡς μία τις φύσις, ἀλλὰ τὸ ὕστερον λέγεται κατὰ τὸ πρότερον, οἷον κίνησις κατὰ τὸ μέγεθος ἐφʼ οὗ κινεῖται ἢ ἀλλοιοῦται ἢ αὔξεται, χρόνος δὲ διὰ τὴν κίνησιν.\n\n\n\n \n\n \n\n μεταβάλλει δὲ τὸ μεταβάλλον τὸ μὲν κατὰ συμβεβηκός, ὡς τὸ μουσικὸν βαδίζει, τὸ δὲ τῷ τούτου τι μεταβάλλειν ἁπλῶς λέγεται μεταβάλλειν, οἷον ὅσα κατὰ μέρη (ὑγιάζεται γὰρ τὸ σῶμα, ὅτι ὁ ὀφθαλμός), ἔστι δέ τι ὃ καθʼ αὑτὸ πρῶτον κινεῖται, καὶ τοῦτʼ ἔστι τὸ καθʼ αὑτὸ κινητόν. ἔστι δέ  καὶ ἐπὶ τοῦ κινοῦντος ὡσαύτως· κινεῖ γὰρ κατὰ συμβεβηκὸς τὸ δὲ κατὰ μέρος τὸ δὲ καθʼ αὑτό· ἔστι δέ τι τὸ κινοῦν πρῶτον· ἔστι δέ τι τὸ κινούμενον, ἔτι ἐν ᾧ χρόνῳ καὶ ἐξ οὗ καὶ εἰς ὅ. τὰ δʼ εἴδη καὶ τὰ πάθη καὶ ὁ τόπος, εἰς ἃ κινοῦνται τὰ κινούμενα, ἀκίνητά ἐστιν, οἷον ἐπιστήμη καὶ θερμότης· ἔστι δʼ οὐχ ἡ θερμότης κίνησις ἀλλʼ ἡ θέρμανσις. ἡ δὲ μὴ κατὰ συμβεβηκὸς μεταβολὴ οὐκ ἐν ἅπασιν ὑπάρχει ἀλλʼ ἐν τοῖς ἐναντίοις καὶ μεταξὺ καὶ ἐν ἀντιφάσει· τούτου δὲ πίστις ἐκ τῆς ἐπαγωγῆς. μεταβάλλει δὲ τὸ μεταβάλλον ἢ ἐξ ὑποκειμένου εἰς ὑποκείμενον, ἢ οὐκ ἐξ ὑποκειμένου εἰς οὐχ ὑποκείμενον, ἢ ἐξ ὑποκειμένου εἰς οὐχ ὑποκείμενον, ἢ οὐκ ἐξ ὑποκειμένου εἰς ὑποκείμενον (λέγω δὲ ὑποκείμενον τὸ καταφάσει δηλούμενον), ὥστʼ ἀνάγκη τρεῖς εἶναι μεταβολάς· ἡ γὰρ ἐξ οὐχ ὑποκειμένου  εἰς μὴ ὑποκείμενον οὐκ ἔστι μεταβολή· οὔτε γὰρ ἐναντία οὔτε ἀντίφασίς ἐστιν, ὅτι οὐκ ἀντίθεσις. ἡ μὲν οὖν οὐκ ἐξ ὑποκειμένου εἰς ὑποκείμενον κατʼ ἀντίφασιν γένεσίς ἐστιν, ἡ μὲν ἁπλῶς ἁπλῆ, ἡ δὲ τινὸς τίς· ἡ δʼ ἐξ ὑποκειμένου εἰς μὴ ὑποκείμενον φθορά, ἡ μὲν ἁπλῶς ἁπλῆ, ἡ δὲ τινὸς τίς. εἰ δὴ τὸ μὴ ὂν λέγεται πλεοναχῶς, καὶ μήτε τὸ κατὰ σύνθεσιν ἢ διαίρεσιν ἐνδέχεται κινεῖσθαι μήτε τὸ κατὰ δύναμιν τὸ τῷ ἁπλῶς ὄντι ἀντικείμενον (τὸ γὰρ μὴ λευκὸν ἢ μὴ ἀγαθὸν ὅμως ἐνδέχεται κινεῖσθαι κατὰ συμβεβηκός, εἴη γὰρ ἂν ἄνθρωπος τὸ μὴ λευκόν· τὸ δʼ ἁπλῶς μὴ τόδε οὐδαμῶς), ἀδύνατον τὸ μὴ ὂν κινεῖσθαι (εἰ δὲ τοῦτο, καὶ τὴν γένεσιν κίνησιν εἶναι· γίγνεται γὰρ τὸ μὴ ὄν· εἰ γὰρ καὶ ὅτι μάλιστα κατὰ συμβεβηκὸς γίγνεται, ἀλλʼ ὅμως ἀληθὲς εἰπεῖν ὅτι ὑπάρχει τὸ μὴ ὂν κατὰ τοῦ γιγνομένου ἁπλῶς)· ὁμοίως δὲ καὶ τὸ ἠρεμεῖν. ταῦτά τε δὴ συμβαίνει δυσχερῆ, καὶ εἰ πᾶν τὸ κινούμενον ἐν τόπῳ, τὸ δὲ μὴ ὂν οὐκ ἔστιν ἐν τόπῳ· εἴη γὰρ ἂν πού. οὐδὲ δὴ ἡ φθορὰ κίνησις· ἐναντίον γὰρ κινήσει κίνησις ἢ ἠρεμία, φθορὰ δὲ γενέσει.  ἐπεὶ δὲ πᾶσα κίνησις μεταβολή τις, μεταβολαὶ δὲ τρεῖς αἱ εἰρημέναι, τούτων δʼ αἱ κατὰ γένεσιν καὶ φθορὰν οὐ κινήσεις, αὗται δʼ εἰσὶν αἱ κατʼ ἀντίφασιν, ἀνάγκη τὴν ἐξ ὑποκειμένου εἰς ὑποκείμενον κίνησιν εἶναι μόνην. τὰ δʼ ὑποκείμενα ἢ ἐναντία ἢ μεταξύ (καὶ γὰρ ἡ στέρησις κείσθω ἐναντίον), καὶ δηλοῦται καταφάσει, οἷον τὸ γυμνὸν καὶ νωδὸν καὶ μέλαν.\n\n\n\n \n\n \n\nεἰ οὖν αἱ κατηγορίαι διῄρηνται οὐσίᾳ, ποιότητι, τόπῳ, τῷ ποιεῖν ἢ πάσχειν, τῷ πρός τι, τῷ ποσῷ, ἀνάγκη τρεῖς εἶναι κινήσεις, ποιοῦ ποσοῦ τόπου· κατʼ οὐσίαν δʼ οὔ, διὰ τὸ μηθὲν εἶναι οὐσίᾳ ἐναντίον, οὐδὲ τοῦ πρός τι (ἔστι γὰρ θατέρου μεταβάλλοντος μὴ ἀληθεύεσθαι θάτερον μηδὲν μεταβάλλον, ὥστε κατὰ συμβεβηκὸς ἡ κίνησις αὐτῶν), οὐδὲ ποιοῦντος καὶ πάσχοντος, ἢ κινοῦντος καὶ κινουμένου, ὅτι οὐκ ἔστι κινήσεως κίνησις οὐδὲ γενέσεως γένεσις, οὐδʼ ὅλως μεταβολῆς μεταβολή. διχῶς γὰρ ἐνδέχεται κινήσεως εἶναι κίνησιν, ἢ ὡς ὑποκειμένου (οἷον ὁ ἄνθρωπος κινεῖται ὅτι ἐκ λευκοῦ εἰς μέλαν μεταβάλλει, ὥστε οὕτω καὶ ἡ κίνησις ἢ θερμαίνεται ἢ ψύχεται ἢ τόπον ἀλλάττει ἢ αὔξεται· τοῦτο δὲ ἀδύνατον· οὐ γὰρ τῶν ὑποκειμένων τι ἡ μεταβολή), ἢ τῷ ἕτερόν τι ὑποκείμενον ἐκ μεταβολῆς μεταβάλλειν εἰς ἄλλο εἶδος, οἷον ἄνθρωπον ἐκ νόσου εἰς ὑγίειαν. ἀλλʼ οὐδὲ τοῦτο δυνατὸν πλὴν κατὰ συμβεβηκός. πᾶσα γὰρ κίνησις ἐξ ἄλλου εἰς ἄλλο ἐστὶ μεταβολή, καὶ γένεσις καὶ φθορὰ ὡσαύτως· πλὴν αἱ μὲν εἰς ἀντικείμενα ὡδί, ἡ δʼ ὡδί, ἡ κίνησις. ἅμα οὖν μεταβάλλει ἐξ ὑγιείας εἰς νόσον, καὶ ἐξ αὐτῆς ταύτης τῆς μεταβολῆς εἰς ἄλλην. δῆλον δὴ ὅτι ἂν νοσήσῃ, μεταβεβληκὸς ἔσται εἰς ὁποιανοῦν (ἐνδέχεται γὰρ ἠρεμεῖν) καὶ ἔτι εἰς μὴ τὴν τυχοῦσαν ἀεί· κἀκείνη ἔκ τινος εἴς τι ἄλλο ἔσται· ὥσθʼ ἡ ἀντικειμένη ἔσται, ὑγίανσις, ἀλλὰ τῷ συμβεβηκέναι, οἷον ἐξ ἀναμνήσεως εἰς λήθην μεταβάλλει ὅτι ᾧ ὑπάρχει ἐκεῖνο μεταβάλλει, ὁτὲ μὲν εἰς ἐπιστήμην ὁτὲ δὲ εἰς ἄγνοιαν.—ἔτι εἰς ἄπειρον βαδιεῖται, εἰ ἔσται μεταβολῆς μεταβολὴ καὶ γενέσεως γένεσις. ἀνάγκη δὴ καὶ τὴν προτέραν, εἰ ἡ ὑστέρα· οἷον εἰ ἡ ἁπλῆ γένεσις ἐγίγνετό ποτε, καὶ τὸ γιγνόμενον ἐγίγνετο·  ὥστε οὔπω ἦν τὸ γιγνόμενον ἁπλῶς, ἀλλά τι γιγνόμενον  γιγνόμενον ἤδη. καὶ τοῦτʼ ἐγίγνετό ποτε, ὥστʼ οὐκ ἦν πω τότε γιγνόμενον. ἐπεὶ δὲ τῶν ἀπείρων οὐκ ἔστι τι πρῶτον, οὐκ ἔσται τὸ πρῶτον, ὥστʼ οὐδὲ τὸ ἐχόμενον. οὔτε γίγνεσθαι οὖν οὔτε κινεῖσθαι οἷόν τε οὔτε μεταβάλλειν οὐδέν. ἔτι τοῦ αὐτοῦ κίνησις ἡ ἐναντία καὶ ἠρέμησις, καὶ γένεσις καὶ φθορά, ὥστε τὸ γιγνόμενον, ὅταν γένηται γιγνόμενον, τότε φθείρεται· οὔτε γὰρ εὐθὺς γιγνόμενον οὔθʼ ὕστερον· εἶναι γὰρ δεῖ τὸ φθειρόμενον. ἔτι δεῖ ὕλην ὑπεῖναι τῷ γιγνομένῳ καὶ μεταβάλλοντι. τίς οὖν ἔσται ὥσπερ τὸ ἀλλοιωτὸν σῶμα ἢ ψυχή—οὕτω τί τὸ γιγνόμενον κίνησις ἢ γένεσις; καὶ ἔτι τί εἰς ὃ κινοῦνται; δεῖ γὰρ εἶναι τὴν τοῦδε ἐκ τοῦδε εἰς τόδε κίνησιν ἢ γένεσιν. πῶς οὖν; οὐ γὰρ ἔσται μάθησις τῆς μαθήσεως, ὥστʼ οὐδὲ γένεσις γενέσεως. ἐπεὶ δʼ οὔτʼ οὐσίας οὔτε τοῦ πρός τι οὔτε τοῦ ποιεῖν καὶ πάσχειν, λείπεται κατὰ τὸ ποιὸν καὶ ποσὸν καὶ τόπον κίνησιν εἶναι (τούτων γὰρ ἑκάστῳ ἐναντίωσις ἔστιν), λέγω δὲ τὸ ποιὸν οὐ τὸ ἐν τῇ οὐσίᾳ (καὶ γὰρ ἡ διαφορὰ ποιόν) ἀλλὰ τὸ παθητικόν, καθʼ ὃ λέγεται πάσχειν ἢ ἀπαθὲς εἶναι. τὸ δὲ ἀκίνητον τό τε ὅλως ἀδύνατον κινηθῆναι καὶ τὸ μόλις ἐν χρόνῳ πολλῷ ἢ βραδέως ἀρχόμενον, καὶ τὸ πεφυκὸς μὲν κινεῖσθαι καὶ δυνάμενον μὴ κινούμενον δὲ ὅτε πέφυκε καὶ οὗ καὶ ὥς· ὃ καλῶ ἠρεμεῖν τῶν ἀκινήτων μόνον· ἐναντίον γὰρ ἠρεμία κινήσει, ὥστε στέρησις ἂν εἴη τοῦ δεκτικοῦ. ἅμα κατὰ τόπον ὅσα ἐν ἑνὶ τόπῳ πρώτῳ, καὶ χωρὶς ὅσα ἐν ἄλλῳ· ἅπτεσθαι δὲ ὧν τὰ ἄκρα ἅμα· μεταξὺ δʼ εἰς ὃ πέφυκε πρότερον ἀφικνεῖσθαι τὸ μεταβάλλον ἢ εἰς ὃ ἔσχατον μεταβάλλει κατὰ φύσιν τὸ συνεχῶς μεταβάλλον. ἐναντίον κατὰ τόπον τὸ κατʼ εὐθεῖαν ἀπέχον πλεῖστον· ἑξῆς δὲ οὗ μετὰ τὴν ἀρχὴν ὄντος, θέσει ἢ εἴδει ἢ ἄλλως πως ἀφορισθέντος, μηθὲν μεταξύ ἐστι τῶν ἐν ταὐτῷ γένει καὶ οὗ ἐφεξῆς ἐστίν, οἷον γραμμαὶ γραμμῆς ἢ μονάδες μονάδος ἢ οἰκίας οἰκία (ἄλλο δʼ οὐθὲν κωλύει μεταξὺ εἶναι). τὸ γὰρ ἑξῆς τινὸς ἐφεξῆς καὶ ὕστερόν τι· οὐ γὰρ τὸ ἓν ἑξῆς τῶν δύο οὐδʼ ἡ νουμηνία τῆς δευτέρας.  ἐχόμενον δὲ ὃ ἂν ἑξῆς ὂν ἅπτηται. ἐπεὶ δὲ πᾶσα μεταβολὴ ἐν τοῖς ἀντικειμένοις, ταῦτα δὲ τὰ ἐναντία καὶ ἀντίφασις, ἀντιφάσεως δʼ οὐδὲν ἀνὰ μέσον, δῆλον ὡς ἐν τοῖς ἐναντίοις τὸ μεταξύ. τὸ δὲ συνεχὲς ὅπερ ἐχόμενόν τι. λέγω δὲ συνεχὲς ὅταν ταὐτὸ γένηται καὶ ἓν τὸ ἑκατέρου πέρας οἷς ἅπτονται καὶ συνέχονται, ὥστε δῆλον ὅτι τὸ συνεχὲς ἐν τούτοις ἐξ ὧν ἕν τι πέφυκε γίγνεσθαι κατὰ τὴν σύναψιν. καὶ ὅτι πρῶτον τὸ ἐφεξῆς, δῆλον (τὸ γὰρ ἐφεξῆς οὐχ ἅπτεται, τοῦτο δʼ ἐφεξῆς· καὶ εἰ συνεχές, ἅπτεται, εἰ δʼ ἅπτεται, οὔπω συνεχές· ἐν οἷς δὲ μὴ ἔστιν ἁφή, οὐκ ἔστι σύμφυσις ἐν τούτοις)· ὥστʼ οὐκ ἔστι στιγμὴ μονάδι ταὐτόν· ταῖς μὲν γὰρ ὑπάρχει τὸ ἅπτεσθαι, ταῖς δʼ οὔ, ἀλλὰ τὸ ἐφεξῆς· καὶ τῶν μὲν μεταξύ τι τῶν δʼ οὔ.","paragraphs":[{"id":"RAW_DAZY_BO_3:P0001","text":"ὅτι μὲν ἡ σοφία περὶ ἀρχὰς ἐπιστήμη τίς ἐστι, δῆλον ἐκ τῶν πρώτων ἐν οἷς διηπόρηται πρὸς τὰ ὑπὸ τῶν ἄλλων εἰρημένα περὶ τῶν ἀρχῶν· ἀπορήσειε δʼ ἄν τις πότερον μίαν ὑπολαβεῖν εἶναι δεῖ τὴν σοφίαν ἐπιστήμην ἢ πολλάς· εἰ μὲν γὰρ μίαν, μία γʼ ἐστὶν ἀεὶ τῶν ἐναντίων, αἱ δʼ ἀρχαὶ οὐκ ἐναντίαι· εἰ δὲ μὴ μία, ποίας δεῖ θεῖναι ταύτας; ἔτι τὰς ἀποδεικτικὰς ἀρχὰς θεωρῆσαι μιᾶς ἢ πλειόνων; εἰ μὲν γὰρ μιᾶς, τί μᾶλλον ταύτης ἢ ὁποιασοῦν; εἰ δὲ πλειόνων, ποίας δεῖ ταύτας τιθέναι; ἔτι πότερον πασῶν τῶν οὐσιῶν ἢ οὔ; εἰ μὲν γὰρ μὴ πασῶν, ποίων χαλεπὸν ἀποδοῦναι· εἰ δὲ πασῶν μία, ἄδηλον πῶς ἐνδέχεται πλειόνων τὴν αὐτὴν ἐπιστήμην εἶναι. ἔτι πότερον περὶ τὰς οὐσίας μόνον ἢ καὶ τὰ συμβεβηκότα ; εἰ γὰρ περί γε τὰ συμβεβηκότα ἀπόδειξίς ἐστιν, περὶ τὰς οὐσίας οὐκ ἔστιν· εἰ δʼ ἑτέρα, τίς ἑκατέρα καὶ ποτέρα σοφία; ᾗ μὲν γὰρ ἀποδεικτική, σοφία ἡ περὶ τὰ συμβεβηκότα· ᾗ δὲ περὶ τὰ πρῶτα, ἡ τῶν οὐσιῶν.","kind":"passage"},{"id":"RAW_DAZY_BO_3:P0002","text":"ἀλλʼ οὐδὲ περὶ τὰς ἐν τοῖς φυσικοῖς εἰρημένας αἰτίας τὴν ἐπιζητουμένην ἐπιστήμην θετέον· οὔτε γὰρ περὶ τὸ οὗ ἕνεκεν (τοιοῦτον γὰρ τὸ ἀγαθόν, τοῦτο δʼ ἐν τοῖς πρακτοῖς ὑπάρχει καὶ ταῖς οὖσιν ἐν κινήσει· καὶ τοῦτο πρῶτον κινεῖ—τοιοῦτον γὰρ τὸ τέλος—τὸ δὲ πρῶτον κινῆσαν οὐκ ἔστιν ἐν τοῖς ἀκινήτοις)· ὅλως δʼ ἀπορίαν ἔχει πότερόν ποτε περὶ τὰς αἰσθητὰς οὐσίας ἐστὶν ἡ ζητουμένη νῦν ἐπιστήμη ἢ οὔ, περὶ δέ τινας ἑτέρας.  εἰ γὰρ περὶ ἄλλας, ἢ περὶ τὰ εἴδη εἴη ἂν ἢ περὶ τὰ μαθηματικά.","kind":"passage"},{"id":"RAW_DAZY_BO_3:P0003","text":"τὰ μὲν οὖν εἴδη ὅτι οὐκ ἔστι, δῆλον (ὅμως δὲ ἀπορίαν ἔχει, κἂν εἶναί τις αὐτὰ θῇ, διὰ τί ποτʼ οὐχ ὥσπερ ἐπὶ τῶν μαθηματικῶν, οὕτως ἔχει καὶ ἐπὶ τῶν ἄλλων ὧν ἔστιν εἴδη· λέγω δʼ ὅτι τὰ μαθηματικὰ μὲν μεταξύ τε τῶν εἰδῶν τιθέασι καὶ τῶν αἰσθητῶν οἷον τρίτα τινὰ παρὰ τὰ εἴδη τε καὶ τὰ δεῦρο, τρίτος δʼ ἄνθρωπος οὐκ ἔστιν οὐδʼ ἵππος παρʼ αὐτόν τε καὶ τοὺς καθʼ ἕκαστον· εἰ δʼ αὖ μὴ ἔστιν ὡς λέγουσι, περὶ ποῖα θετέον πραγματεύεσθαι τὸν μαθηματικόν; οὐ γὰρ δὴ περὶ τὰ δεῦρο· τούτων γὰρ οὐθέν ἐστιν οἷον αἱ μαθηματικαὶ ζητοῦσι τῶν ἐπιστημῶν)· οὐδὲ μὴν περὶ τὰ μαθηματικὰ ἡ ζητουμένη νῦν ἐστὶν ἐπιστήμη (χωριστὸν γὰρ αὐτῶν οὐθέν)· ἀλλʼ οὐδὲ τῶν αἰσθητῶν οὐσιῶν· φθαρταὶ γάρ. ὅλως δʼ ἀπορήσειέ τις ἂν ποίας ἐστὶν ἐπιστήμης τὸ διαπορῆσαι περὶ τῆς τῶν μαθηματικῶν ὕλης.","kind":"passage"},{"id":"RAW_DAZY_BO_3:P0004","text":"οὔτε γὰρ τῆς φυσικῆς, διὰ τὸ περὶ τὰ ἔχοντα ἐν αὑτοῖς ἀρχὴν κινήσεως καὶ στάσεως τὴν τοῦ φυσικοῦ πᾶσαν εἶναι πραγματείαν, οὐδὲ μὴν τῆς σκοπούσης περὶ ἀποδείξεώς τε καὶ ἐπιστήμης· περὶ γὰρ αὐτὸ τοῦτο τὸ γένος τὴν ζήτησιν ποιεῖται. λείπεται τοίνυν τὴν προκειμένην φιλοσοφίαν περὶ αὐτῶν τὴν σκέψιν ποιεῖσθαι. διαπορήσειε δʼ ἄν τις εἰ δεῖ θεῖναι τὴν ζητουμένην ἐπιστήμην περὶ τὰς ἀρχάς, τὰ καλούμενα ὑπό τινων στοιχεῖα· ταῦτα δὲ πάντες ἐνυπάρχοντα τοῖς συνθέτοις τιθέασιν. μᾶλλον δʼ ἂν δόξειε τῶν καθόλου δεῖν εἶναι τὴν ζητουμένην ἐπιστήμην· πᾶς γὰρ λόγος καὶ πᾶσα ἐπιστήμη τῶν καθόλου καὶ οὐ τῶν ἐσχάτων, ὥστʼ εἴη ἂν οὕτω τῶν πρώτων γενῶν. ταῦτα δὲ γίγνοιτʼ ἂν τό τε ὂν καὶ τὸ ἕν· ταῦτα γὰρ μάλιστʼ ἂν ὑποληφθείη περιέχειν τὰ ὄντα πάντα καὶ μάλιστα ἀρχαῖς ἐοικέναι διὰ τὸ εἶναι πρῶτα τῇ φύσει· φθαρέντων γὰρ αὐτῶν συναναιρεῖται καὶ τὰ λοιπά· πᾶν γὰρ ὂν καὶ ἕν.","kind":"passage"},{"id":"RAW_DAZY_BO_3:P0005","text":"ᾗ δὲ τὰς διαφορὰς αὐτῶν ἀνάγκη μετέχειν εἰ θήσει τις αὐτὰ γένη, διαφορὰ δʼ οὐδεμία τοῦ γένους μετέχει, ταύτῃ δʼ οὐκ ἂν δόξειε δεῖν αὐτὰ τιθέναι γένη οὐδʼ ἀρχάς. ἔτι δʼ εἰ μᾶλλον ἀρχὴ τὸ ἁπλούστερον τοῦ ἧττον τοιούτου, τὰ δʼ ἔσχατα τῶν ἐκ τοῦ γένους ἁπλούστερα τῶν γενῶν (ἄτομα γάρ, τὰ γένη δʼ εἰς εἴδη πλείω καὶ διαφέροντα διαιρεῖται), μᾶλλον ἂν ἀρχὴ δόξειεν εἶναι τὰ εἴδη τῶν γενῶν. ᾗ δὲ συναναιρεῖται τοῖς γένεσι τὰ εἴδη, τὰ γένη ταῖς ἀρχαῖς ἔοικε μᾶλλον· ἀρχὴ γὰρ τὸ συναναιροῦν.  τὰ μὲν οὖν τὴν ἀπορίαν ἔχοντα ταῦτα καὶ τοιαῦτʼ ἐστὶν ἕτερα.","kind":"passage"},{"id":"RAW_DAZY_BO_3:P0006","text":"ἔτι πότερον δεῖ τιθέναι τι παρὰ τὰ καθʼ ἕκαστα ἢ οὔ, ἀλλὰ τούτων ἡ ζητουμένη ἐπιστήμη; ἀλλὰ ταῦτα ἄπειρα· τά γε μὴν παρὰ τὰ καθʼ ἕκαστα γένη ἢ εἴδη ἐστίν, ἀλλʼ οὐδετέρου τούτων ἡ ζητουμένη νῦν ἐπιστήμη. διότι γὰρ ἀδύνατον τοῦτο, εἴρηται. καὶ γὰρ ὅλως ἀπορίαν ἔχει πότερον δεῖ τινὰ ὑπολαβεῖν οὐσίαν εἶναι χωριστὴν παρὰ τὰς αἰσθητὰς οὐσίας καὶ τὰς δεῦρο, ἢ οὔ, ἀλλὰ ταῦτʼ εἶναι τὰ ὄντα καὶ περὶ ταῦτα τὴν σοφίαν ὑπάρχειν. ζητεῖν μὲν γὰρ ἐοίκαμεν ἄλλην τινά, καὶ τὸ προκείμενον τοῦτʼ ἔστιν ἡμῖν, λέγω δὲ τὸ ἰδεῖν εἴ τι χωριστὸν καθʼ αὑτὸ καὶ μηδενὶ τῶν αἰσθητῶν ὑπάρχον. ἔτι δʼ εἰ παρὰ τὰς αἰσθητὰς οὐσίας ἔστι τις ἑτέρα οὐσία, παρὰ ποίας τῶν αἰσθητῶν δεῖ τιθέναι ταύτην εἶναι; τί γὰρ μᾶλλον παρὰ τοὺς ἀνθρώπους ἢ τοὺς ἵππους ἢ τῶν ἄλλων ζῴων θήσει τις αὐτὴν ἢ καὶ τῶν ἀψύχων ὅλως; τό γε μὴν ἴσας ταῖς αἰσθηταῖς καὶ φθαρταῖς οὐσίαις ἀϊδίους ἑτέρας κατασκευάζειν ἐκτὸς τῶν εὐλόγων δόξειεν ἂν πίπτειν.","kind":"passage"},{"id":"RAW_DAZY_BO_3:P0007","text":"εἰ δὲ μὴ χωριστὴ τῶν σωμάτων ἡ ζητουμένη νῦν ἀρχή, τίνα ἄν τις ἄλλην θείη μᾶλλον τῆς ὕλης; αὕτη γε μὴν ἐνεργείᾳ μὲν οὐκ ἔστι, δυνάμει δʼ ἔστιν. μᾶλλόν τʼ ἂν ἀρχὴ κυριωτέρα ταύτης δόξειεν εἶναι τὸ εἶδος καὶ ἡ μορφή· τοῦτο δὲ φθαρτόν, ὥσθʼ ὅλως οὐκ ἔστιν ἀΐδιος οὐσία χωριστὴ καὶ καθʼ αὑτήν.","kind":"passage"},{"id":"RAW_DAZY_BO_3:P0008","text":"ἀλλʼ ἄτοπον· ἔοικε γὰρ καὶ ζητεῖται σχεδὸν ὑπὸ τῶν χαριεστάτων ὡς οὖσά τις ἀρχὴ καὶ οὐσία τοιαύτη· πῶς γὰρ ἔσται τάξις μή τινος ὄντος ἀϊδίου καὶ χωριστοῦ καὶ μένοντος; ἔτι δʼ εἴπερ ἔστι τις οὐσία καὶ ἀρχὴ τοιαύτη τὴν φύσιν οἵαν νῦν ζητοῦμεν, καὶ αὕτη μία πάντων καὶ ἡ αὐτὴ τῶν ἀϊδίων τε καὶ φθαρτῶν, ἀπορίαν ἔχει διὰ τί ποτε τῆς αὐτῆς ἀρχῆς οὔσης τὰ μέν ἐστιν ἀΐδια τῶν ὑπὸ τὴν ἀρχὴν τὰ δʼ οὐκ ἀΐδια (τοῦτο γὰρ ἄτοπον)· εἰ δʼ ἄλλη μέν ἐστιν ἀρχὴ τῶν φθαρτῶν ἄλλη δὲ τῶν ἀϊδίων, εἰ μὲν ἀΐδιος καὶ ἡ τῶν φθαρτῶν, ὁμοίως ἀπορήσομεν (διὰ τί γὰρ οὐκ ἀϊδίου τῆς ἀρχῆς οὔσης καὶ τὰ ὑπὸ τὴν ἀρχὴν ἀΐδια;)· φθαρτῆς δʼ οὔσης ἄλλη τις ἀρχὴ γίγνεται ταύτης κἀκείνης ἑτέρα, καὶ τοῦτʼ εἰς ἄπειρον πρόεισιν.","kind":"passage"},{"id":"RAW_DAZY_BO_3:P0009","text":"εἰ δʼ αὖ τις τὰς δοκούσας μάλιστʼ ἀρχὰς ἀκινήτους εἶναι, τό τε ὂν καὶ τὸ ἕν, θήσει, πρῶτον μὲν εἰ μὴ τόδε τι καὶ οὐσίαν ἑκάτερον αὐτῶν σημαίνει,  πῶς ἔσονται χωρισταὶ καὶ καθʼ αὑτάς; τοιαύτας δὲ ζητοῦμεν τὰς ἀϊδίους τε καὶ πρώτας ἀρχάς. εἴ γε μὴν τόδε τι καὶ οὐσίαν ἑκάτερον αὐτῶν δηλοῖ, πάντʼ ἐστὶν οὐσίαι τὰ ὄντα· κατὰ πάντων γὰρ τὸ ὂν κατηγορεῖται (κατʼ ἐνίων δὲ καὶ τὸ ἕν)· οὐσίαν δʼ εἶναι πάντα τὰ ὄντα ψεῦδος. ἔτι δὲ τοῖς τὴν πρώτην ἀρχὴν τὸ ἓν λέγουσι καὶ τοῦτʼ οὐσίαν, ἐκ δὲ τοῦ ἑνὸς καὶ τῆς ὕλης τὸν ἀριθμὸν γεννῶσι πρῶτον καὶ τοῦτον οὐσίαν φάσκουσιν εἶναι, πῶς ἐνδέχεται τὸ λεγόμενον ἀληθὲς εἶναι; τὴν γὰρ δυάδα καὶ τῶν λοιπῶν ἕκαστον ἀριθμῶν τῶν συνθέτων πῶς ἓν δεῖ νοῆσαι; περὶ τούτου γὰρ οὔτε λέγουσιν οὐδὲν οὔτε ῥᾴδιον εἰπεῖν.","kind":"passage"},{"id":"RAW_DAZY_BO_3:P0010","text":"εἴ γε μὴν γραμμὰς ἢ τὰ τούτων ἐχόμενα (λέγω δὲ ἐπιφανείας τὰς πρώτας) θήσει τις ἀρχάς, ταῦτά γʼ οὐκ εἰσὶν οὐσίαι χωρισταί, τομαὶ δὲ καὶ διαιρέσεις αἱ μὲν ἐπιφανειῶν αἱ δὲ σωμάτων (αἱ δὲ στιγμαὶ γραμμῶν), ἔτι δὲ πέρατα τῶν αὐτῶν τούτων· πάντα δὲ ταῦτα ἐν ἄλλοις ὑπάρχει καὶ χωριστὸν οὐδέν ἐστιν. ἔτι πῶς οὐσίαν ὑπολαβεῖν εἶναι δεῖ τοῦ ἑνὸς καὶ στιγμῆς; οὐσίας μὲν γὰρ πάσης γένεσις ἔστι, στιγμῆς δʼ οὐκ ἔστιν· διαίρεσις γὰρ ἡ στιγμή.","kind":"passage"},{"id":"RAW_DAZY_BO_3:P0011","text":"παρέχει δʼ ἀπορίαν καὶ τὸ πᾶσαν μὲν ἐπιστήμην εἶναι τῶν καθόλου καὶ τοῦ τοιουδί, τὴν δʼ οὐσίαν μὴ τῶν καθόλου εἶναι, μᾶλλον δὲ τόδε τι καὶ χωριστόν, ὥστʼ εἰ περὶ τὰς ἀρχάς ἐστιν ἐπιστήμη, πῶς δεῖ τὴν ἀρχὴν ὑπολαβεῖν οὐσίαν εἶναι; ἔτι πότερον ἔστι τι παρὰ τὸ σύνολον ἢ οὔ (λέγω δὲ τὴν ὕλην καὶ τὸ μετὰ ταύτης); εἰ μὲν γὰρ μή, τά γε ἐν ὕλῃ φθαρτὰ πάντα· εἰ δʼ ἔστι τι, τὸ εἶδος ἂν εἴη καὶ ἡ μορφή· τοῦτʼ οὖν ἐπὶ τίνων ἔστι καὶ ἐπὶ τίνων οὔ, χαλεπὸν ἀφορίσαι· ἐπʼ ἐνίων γὰρ δῆλον οὐκ ὂν χωριστὸν τὸ εἶδος, οἷον οἰκίας. ἔτι πότερον αἱ ἀρχαὶ εἴδει ἢ ἀριθμῷ αἱ αὐταί; εἰ γὰρ ἀριθμῷ ἕν, πάντʼ ἔσται ταὐτά.","kind":"passage"},{"id":"RAW_DAZY_BO_3:P0012","text":"ἐπεὶ δʼ ἐστὶν ἡ τοῦ φιλοσόφου ἐπιστήμη τοῦ ὄντος ᾗ ὂν καθόλου καὶ οὐ κατὰ μέρος, τὸ δʼ ὂν πολλαχῶς καὶ οὐ καθʼ ἕνα λέγεται τρόπον· εἰ μὲν οὖν ὁμωνύμως κατὰ δὲ κοινὸν μηδέν, οὐκ ἔστιν ὑπὸ μίαν ἐπιστήμην (οὐ γὰρ ἓν γένος τῶν τοιούτων), εἰ δὲ κατά τι κοινόν, εἴη ἂν ὑπὸ μίαν ἐπιστήμην. ἔοικε δὴ τὸν εἰρημένον λέγεσθαι τρόπον καθάπερ τό τε ἰατρικὸν καὶ ὑγιεινόν· καὶ γὰρ τούτων ἑκάτερον πολλαχῶς λέγομεν.  λέγεται δὲ τοῦτον τὸν τρόπον ἕκαστον τῷ τὸ μὲν πρὸς τὴν ἰατρικὴν ἐπιστήμην ἀνάγεσθαί πως τὸ δὲ πρὸς ὑγίειαν τὸ δʼ ἄλλως, πρὸς ταὐτὸ δʼ ἕκαστον. ἰατρικὸς γὰρ λόγος καὶ μαχαίριον λέγεται τῷ τὸ μὲν ἀπὸ τῆς ἰατρικῆς ἐπιστήμης εἶναι τὸ δὲ ταύτῃ χρήσιμον. ὁμοίως δὲ καὶ ὑγιεινόν· τὸ μὲν γὰρ ὅτι σημαντικὸν ὑγιείας τὸ δʼ ὅτι ποιητικόν. ὁ δʼ αὐτὸς τρόπος καὶ ἐπὶ τῶν λοιπῶν.","kind":"passage"},{"id":"RAW_DAZY_BO_3:P0013","text":"τὸν αὐτὸν δὴ τρόπον καὶ τὸ ὂν ἅπαν λέγεται· τῷ γὰρ τοῦ ὄντος ᾗ ὂν πάθος ἢ ἕξις ἢ διάθεσις ἢ κίνησις ἢ τῶν ἄλλων τι τῶν τοιούτων εἶναι λέγεται ἕκαστον αὐτῶν ὄν. ἐπεὶ δὲ παντὸς τοῦ ὄντος πρὸς ἕν τι καὶ κοινὸν ἡ ἀναγωγὴ γίγνεται, καὶ τῶν ἐναντιώσεων ἑκάστη πρὸς τὰς πρώτας διαφορὰς καὶ ἐναντιώσεις ἀναχθήσεται τοῦ ὄντος, εἴτε πλῆθος καὶ ἓν εἴθʼ ὁμοιότης καὶ ἀνομοιότης αἱ πρῶται τοῦ ὄντος εἰσὶ διαφοραί, εἴτʼ ἄλλαι τινές· ἔστωσαν γὰρ αὗται τεθεωρημέναι. διαφέρει δʼ οὐδὲν τὴν τοῦ ὄντος ἀναγωγὴν πρὸς τὸ ὂν ἢ πρὸς τὸ ἓν γίγνεσθαι.","kind":"passage"},{"id":"RAW_DAZY_BO_3:P0014","text":"καὶ γὰρ εἰ μὴ ταὐτὸν ἄλλο δʼ ἐστίν, ἀντιστρέφει γε· τό τε γὰρ ἓν καὶ ὄν πως, τό τε ὂν ἕν.—ἐπεὶ δʼ ἐστὶ τὰ ἐναντία πάντα τῆς αὐτῆς καὶ μιᾶς ἐπιστήμης θεωρῆσαι, λέγεται δʼ ἕκαστον αὐτῶν κατὰ στέρησιν—καίτοι γʼ ἔνια ἀπορήσειέ τις ἂν πῶς λέγεται κατὰ στέρησιν, ὧν ἔστιν ἀνὰ μέσον τι, καθάπερ ἀδίκου καὶ δικαίου—περὶ πάντα δὴ τὰ τοιαῦτα τὴν στέρησιν δεῖ τιθέναι μὴ τοῦ ὅλου λόγου, τοῦ τελευταίου δὲ εἴδους· οἷον εἰ ἔστιν ὁ δίκαιος καθʼ ἕξιν τινὰ πειθαρχικὸς τοῖς νόμοις, οὐ πάντως ὁ ἄδικος ἔσται τοῦ ὅλου στερούμενος λόγου, περὶ δὲ τὸ πείθεσθαι τοῖς νόμοις ἐκλείπων πῃ, καὶ ταύτῃ ἡ στέρησις ὑπάρξει αὐτῷ· τὸν αὐτὸν δὲ τρόπον καὶ ἐπὶ τῶν ἄλλων.—καθάπερ δʼ ὁ μαθηματικὸς περὶ τὰ ἐξ ἀφαιρέσεως τὴν θεωρίαν ποιεῖται (περιελὼν γὰρ πάντα τὰ αἰσθητὰ θεωρεῖ, οἷον βάρος καὶ κουφότητα καὶ σκληρότητα καὶ τοὐναντίον, ἔτι δὲ καὶ θερμότητα καὶ ψυχρότητα καὶ τὰς ἄλλας αἰσθητὰς ἐναντιώσεις, μόνον δὲ καταλείπει τὸ","kind":"passage"},{"id":"RAW_DAZY_BO_3:P0015","text":"ποσὸν καὶ συνεχές, τῶν μὲν ἐφʼ ἓν τῶν δʼ ἐπὶ δύο τῶν δʼ ἐπὶ τρία, καὶ τὰ πάθη τὰ τούτων ᾗ ποσά ἐστι καὶ συνεχῆ, καὶ οὐ καθʼ ἕτερόν τι θεωρεῖ, καὶ τῶν μὲν τὰς πρὸς ἄλληλα θέσεις σκοπεῖ καὶ τὰ ταύταις ὑπάρχοντα,  τῶν δὲ τὰς συμμετρίας καὶ ἀσυμμετρίας, τῶν δὲ τοὺς λόγους, ἀλλʼ ὅμως μίαν πάντων καὶ τὴν αὐτὴν τίθεμεν ἐπιστήμην τὴν γεωμετρικήν), τὸν αὐτὸν δὴ τρόπον ἔχει καὶ περὶ τὸ ὄν. τὰ γὰρ τούτῳ συμβεβηκότα καθʼ ὅσον ἐστὶν ὄν, καὶ τὰς ἐναντιώσεις αὐτοῦ ᾗ ὄν, οὐκ ἄλλης ἐπιστήμης ἢ φιλοσοφίας θεωρῆσαι. τῇ φυσικῇ μὲν γὰρ οὐχ ᾗ ὄντα, μᾶλλον δʼ ᾗ κινήσεως μετέχει, τὴν θεωρίαν τις ἀπονείμειεν ἄν· ἥ γε μὴν διαλεκτικὴ καὶ ἡ σοφιστικὴ τῶν συμβεβηκότων μέν εἰσι τοῖς οὖσιν, οὐχ ᾗ δʼ ὄντα οὐδὲ περὶ τὸ ὂν αὐτὸ καθʼ ὅσον ὄν ἐστιν· ὥστε λείπεται τὸν φιλοσόφον, καθʼ ὅσον ὄντʼ ἐστίν, εἶναι περὶ τὰ λεχθέντα θεωρητικόν.","kind":"passage"},{"id":"RAW_DAZY_BO_3:P0016","text":"ἐπεὶ δὲ τό τε ὂν ἅπαν καθʼ ἕν τι καὶ κοινὸν λέγεται πολλαχῶς λεγόμενον, καὶ τἀναντία τὸν αὐτὸν τρόπον (εἰς τὰς πρώτας γὰρ ἐναντιώσεις καὶ διαφορὰς τοῦ ὄντος ἀνάγεται), τὰ δὲ τοιαῦτα δυνατὸν ὑπὸ μίαν ἐπιστήμην εἶναι, διαλύοιτʼ ἂν ἡ κατʼ ἀρχὰς ἀπορία λεχθεῖσα, λέγω δʼ ἐν ᾗ διηπορεῖτο πῶς ἔσται πολλῶν καὶ διαφόρων ὄντων τῷ γένει μία τις ἐπιστήμη.","kind":"passage"},{"id":"RAW_DAZY_BO_3:P0017","text":"ἐπεὶ δὲ καὶ ὁ μαθηματικὸς χρῆται τοῖς κοινοῖς ἰδίως, καὶ τὰς τούτων ἀρχὰς ἂν εἴη θεωρῆσαι τῆς πρώτης φιλοσοφίας. ὅτι γὰρ ἀπὸ τῶν ἴσων ἴσων ἀφαιρεθέντων ἴσα τὰ λειπόμενα, κοινὸν μέν ἐστιν ἐπὶ πάντων τῶν ποσῶν, ἡ μαθηματικὴ δʼ ἀπολαβοῦσα περί τι μέρος τῆς οἰκείας ὕλης ποιεῖται τὴν θεωρίαν, οἷον περὶ γραμμὰς ἢ γωνίας ἢ ἀριθμοὺς ἢ τῶν λοιπῶν τι ποσῶν, οὐχ ᾗ δʼ ὄντα ἀλλʼ ᾗ συνεχὲς αὐτῶν ἕκαστον ἐφʼ ἓν ἢ δύο ἢ τρία· ἡ δὲ φιλοσοφία περὶ τῶν ἐν μέρει μέν, ᾗ τούτων ἑκάστῳ τι συμβέβηκεν, οὐ σκοπεῖ, περὶ τὸ ὂν δέ, ᾗ ὂν τῶν τοιούτων ἕκαστον, θεωρεῖ. τὸν αὐτὸν δʼ ἔχει τρόπον καὶ περὶ τὴν φυσικὴν ἐπιστήμην τῇ μαθηματικῇ· τὰ συμβεβηκότα γὰρ ἡ φυσικὴ καὶ τὰς ἀρχὰς θεωρεῖ τὰς τῶν ὄντων ᾗ κινούμενα καὶ οὐχ ᾗ ὄντα (τὴν δὲ πρώτην εἰρήκαμεν ἐπιστήμην τούτων εἶναι καθʼ ὅσον ὄντα τὰ ὑποκείμενά ἐστιν, ἀλλʼ οὐχ ᾗ ἕτερόν τι)· διὸ καὶ ταύτην καὶ τὴν μαθηματικὴν ἐπιστήμην μέρη τῆς σοφίας εἶναι θετέον.","kind":"passage"},{"id":"RAW_DAZY_BO_3:P0018","text":"ἔστι δέ τις ἐν τοῖς οὖσιν ἀρχὴ περὶ ἣν οὐκ ἔστι διεψεῦσθαι, τοὐναντίον δὲ ἀναγκαῖον ἀεὶ ποιεῖν, λέγω δὲ ἀληθεύειν, οἷον ὅτι οὐκ ἐνδέχεται τὸ αὐτὸ καθʼ ἕνα καὶ τὸν αὐτὸν χρόνον εἶναι καὶ μὴ εἶναι,  καὶ τἆλλα τὰ τοῦτον αὑτοῖς ἀντικείμενα τὸν τρόπον. καὶ περὶ τῶν τοιούτων ἁπλῶς μὲν οὐκ ἔστιν ἀπόδειξις, πρὸς τόνδε δὲ ἔστιν· οὐ γὰρ ἔστιν ἐκ πιστοτέρας ἀρχῆς αὐτοῦ τούτου ποιήσασθαι συλλογισμόν, δεῖ δέ γʼ εἴπερ ἔσται τὸ ἁπλῶς ἀποδεδεῖχθαι. πρὸς δὲ τὸν λέγοντα τὰς ἀντικειμένας φάσεις τῷ δεικνύντι διότι ψεῦδος ληπτέον τι τοιοῦτον ὃ ταὐτὸ μὲν ἔσται τῷ μὴ ἐνδέχεσθαι ταὐτὸ εἶναι καὶ μὴ εἶναι καθʼ ἕνα καὶ τὸν αὐτὸν χρόνον, μὴ δόξει δʼ εἶναι ταὐτόν· οὕτω γὰρ μόνως ἂν ἀποδειχθείη πρὸς τὸν φάσκοντα ἐνδέχεσθαι τὰς ἀντικειμένας φάσεις ἀληθεύεσθαι κατὰ τοῦ αὐτοῦ.","kind":"passage"},{"id":"RAW_DAZY_BO_3:P0019","text":"τοὺς δὴ μέλλοντας ἀλλήλοις λόγου κοινωνήσειν δεῖ τι συνιέναι αὑτῶν· μὴ γιγνομένου γὰρ τούτου πῶς ἔσται κοινωνία τούτοις πρὸς ἀλλήλους λόγου; δεῖ τοίνυν τῶν ὀνομάτων ἕκαστον εἶναι γνώριμον καὶ δηλοῦν τι, καὶ μὴ πολλά, μόνον δὲ ἕν· ἂν δὲ πλείονα σημαίνῃ, φανερὸν ποιεῖν ἐφʼ ὃ φέρει τοὔνομα τούτων. ὁ δὴ λέγων εἶναι τοῦτο καὶ μὴ εἶναι, τοῦτο ὅ φησιν οὔ φησιν, ὥσθʼ ὃ σημαίνει τοὔνομα τοῦτʼ οὔ φησι σημαίνειν· τοῦτο δʼ ἀδύνατον. ὥστʼ εἴπερ σημαίνει τι τὸ εἶναι τόδε, τὴν ἀντίφασιν ἀδύνατον ἀληθεύειν. ἔτι δʼ εἴ τι σημαίνει τοὔνομα καὶ τοῦτʼ ἀληθεύεται, δεῖ τοῦτʼ ἐξ ἀνάγκης εἶναι· τὸ δʼ ἐξ ἀνάγκης ὂν οὐκ ἐνδέχεταί ποτε μὴ εἶναι· τὰς ἀντικειμένας ἄρα οὐκ ἐνδέχεται φάσεις καὶ ἀποφάσεις ἀληθεύειν κατὰ τοῦ αὐτοῦ.","kind":"passage"},{"id":"RAW_DAZY_BO_3:P0020","text":"ἔτι δʼ εἰ μηθὲν μᾶλλον ἡ φάσις ἢ ἡ ἀπόφασις ἀληθεύεται, ὁ λέγων ἄνθρωπον ἢ οὐκ ἄνθρωπον οὐθὲν μᾶλλον ἀληθεύσει· δόξειε δὲ κἂν οὐχ ἵππον εἶναι φάσκων τὸν ἄνθρωπον ἢ μᾶλλον ἢ οὐχ ἧττον ἀληθεύειν ἢ οὐκ ἄνθρωπον, ὥστε καὶ ἵππον φάσκων εἶναι τὸν αὐτὸν ἀληθεύσει (τὰς γὰρ ἀντικειμένας ὁμοίως ἦν ἀληθεύειν)· συμβαίνει τοίνυν τὸν αὐτὸν ἄνθρωπον εἶναι καὶ ἵππον ἢ τῶν ἄλλων τι ζῴων.—ἀπόδειξις μὲν οὖν οὐδεμία τούτων ἐστὶν ἁπλῶς, πρὸς μέντοι τὸν ταῦτα τιθέμενον ἀπόδειξις. ταχέως δʼ ἄν τις καὶ αὐτὸν τὸν Ἡράκλειτον τοῦτον ἐρωτῶν τὸν τρόπον ἠνάγκασεν ὁμολογεῖν μηδέποτε τὰς ἀντικειμένας φάσεις δυνατὸν εἶναι κατὰ τῶν αὐτῶν ἀληθεύεσθαι· νῦν δʼ οὐ συνιεὶς ἑαυτοῦ τί ποτε λέγει, ταύτην ἔλαβε τὴν δόξαν.","kind":"passage"},{"id":"RAW_DAZY_BO_3:P0021","text":"ὅλως δʼ εἰ τὸ λεγόμενον ὑπʼ αὐτοῦ ἐστὶν ἀληθές, οὐδʼ ἂν αὐτὸ τοῦτο εἴη ἀληθές,  λέγω δὲ τὸ ἐνδέχεσθαι τὸ αὐτὸ καθʼ ἕνα καὶ τὸν αὐτὸν χρόνον εἶναί τε καὶ μὴ εἶναι· καθάπερ γὰρ καὶ διῃρημένων αὐτῶν οὐδὲν μᾶλλον ἡ κατάφασις ἢ ἡ ἀπόφασις ἀληθεύεται, τὸν αὐτὸν τρόπον καὶ τοῦ συναμφοτέρου καὶ τοῦ συμπεπλεγμένου καθάπερ μιᾶς τινὸς καταφάσεως οὔσης οὐθὲν μᾶλλον ἢ ἡ ἀπόφασις  τὸ ὅλον ὡς ἐν καταφάσει τιθέμενον ἀληθεύσεται. ἔτι δʼ εἰ μηθὲν ἔστιν ἀληθῶς καταφῆσαι, κἂν αὐτὸ τοῦτο ψεῦδος εἴη τὸ φάναι μηδεμίαν ἀληθῆ κατάφασιν ὑπάρχειν. εἰ δʼ ἔστι τι, λύοιτʼ ἂν τὸ λεγόμενον ὑπὸ τῶν τὰ τοιαῦτα ἐνισταμένων καὶ παντελῶς ἀναιρούντων τὸ διαλέγεσθαι.","kind":"passage"},{"id":"RAW_DAZY_BO_3:P0022","text":"παραπλήσιον δὲ τοῖς εἰρημένοις ἐστὶ καὶ τὸ λεχθὲν ὑπὸ τοῦ Πρωταγόρου· καὶ γὰρ ἐκεῖνος ἔφη πάντων εἶναι χρημάτων μέτρον ἄνθρωπον, οὐδὲν ἕτερον λέγων ἢ τὸ δοκοῦν ἑκάστῳ τοῦτο καὶ εἶναι παγίως· τούτου δὲ γιγνομένου τὸ αὐτὸ συμβαίνει καὶ εἶναι καὶ μὴ εἶναι, καὶ κακὸν καὶ ἀγαθὸν εἶναι, καὶ τἆλλα τὰ κατὰ τὰς ἀντικειμένας λεγόμενα φάσεις, διὰ τὸ πολλάκις τοισδὶ μὲν φαίνεσθαι τόδε εἶναι καλὸν τοισδὶ δὲ τοὐναντίον, μέτρον δʼ εἶναι τὸ φαινόμενον ἑκάστῳ. λύοιτο δʼ ἂν αὕτη ἡ ἀπορία θεωρήσασι πόθεν ἐλήλυθεν ἡ ἀρχὴ τῆς ὑπολήψεως ταύτης· ἔοικε γὰρ ἐνίοις μὲν ἐκ τῆς τῶν φυσιολόγων δόξης γεγενῆσθαι, τοῖς δʼ ἐκ τοῦ μὴ ταὐτὰ περὶ τῶν αὐτῶν ἅπαντας γιγνώσκειν ἀλλὰ τοῖσδε μὲν ἡδὺ τόδε φαίνεσθαι τοῖσδε δὲ τοὐναντίον.","kind":"passage"},{"id":"RAW_DAZY_BO_3:P0023","text":"τὸ γὰρ μηδὲν ἐκ μὴ ὄντος γίγνεσθαι, πᾶν δʼ ἐξ ὄντος, σχεδὸν ἁπάντων ἐστὶ κοινὸν δόγμα τῶν περὶ φύσεως· ἐπεὶ οὖν οὐ λευκὸν γίγνεται λευκοῦ τελέως ὄντος καὶ οὐδαμῇ μὴ λευκοῦ , γίγνοιτʼ ἂν ἐκ μὴ ὄντος λευκοῦ τὸ γιγνόμενον  λευκόν· ὥστε ἐκ μὴ ὄντος γίγνοιτʼ ἂν κατʼ ἐκείνους, εἰ μὴ ὑπῆρχε λευκὸν τὸ αὐτὸ καὶ μὴ λευκόν. οὐ χαλεπὸν δὲ διαλύειν τὴν ἀπορίαν ταύτην· εἴρηται γὰρ ἐν τοῖς φυσικοῖς πῶς ἐκ τοῦ μὴ ὄντος γίγνεται τὰ γιγνόμενα καὶ πῶς ἐξ ὄντος. τό γε μὴν ὁμοίως προσέχειν ταῖς δόξαις καὶ ταῖς φαντασίαις τῶν πρὸς αὑτοὺς διαμφισβητούντων εὔηθες· δῆλον γὰρ ὅτι τοὺς ἑτέρους αὐτῶν ἀνάγκη διεψεῦσθαι. φανερὸν δὲ τοῦτʼ ἐκ τῶν γιγνομένων κατὰ τὴν αἴσθησιν· οὐδέποτε γὰρ τὸ αὐτὸ φαίνεται τοῖς μὲν γλυκὺ τοῖς δὲ τοὐναντίον,  μὴ διεφθαρμένων καὶ λελωβημένων τῶν ἑτέρων τὸ αἰσθητήριον καὶ κριτήριον τῶν λεχθέντων χυμῶν.","kind":"passage"},{"id":"RAW_DAZY_BO_3:P0024","text":"τούτου δʼ ὄντος τοιούτου τοὺς ἑτέρους μὲν ὑποληπτέον μέτρον εἶναι τοὺς δʼ ἄλλους οὐχ ὑποληπτέον. ὁμοίως δὲ τοῦτο λέγω καὶ ἐπὶ ἀγαθοῦ καὶ κακοῦ, καὶ καλοῦ καὶ αἰσχροῦ, καὶ τῶν ἄλλων τῶν τοιούτων. οὐδὲν γὰρ διαφέρει τοῦτʼ ἀξιοῦν ἢ τὰ φαινόμενα τοῖς ὑπὸ τὴν ὄψιν ὑποβάλλουσι τὸν δάκτυλον καὶ ποιοῦσιν ἐκ τοῦ ἑνὸς φαίνεσθαι δύο, δύο δεῖν εἶναι διὰ τὸ φαίνεσθαι τοσαῦτα, καὶ πάλιν ἕν· τοῖς γὰρ μὴ κινοῦσι τὴν ὄψιν ἓν φαίνεται τὸ ἕν. ὅλως δὲ ἄτοπον ἐκ τοῦ φαίνεσθαι τὰ δεῦρο μεταβάλλοντα καὶ μηδέποτε διαμένοντα ἐν τοῖς αὐτοῖς, ἐκ τούτου περὶ τῆς ἀληθείας τὴν κρίσιν ποιεῖσθαι· δεῖ γὰρ ἐκ τῶν ἀεὶ κατὰ ταὐτὰ ἐχόντων καὶ μηδεμίαν μεταβολὴν ποιουμένων τἀληθὲς θηρεύειν, τοιαῦτα δʼ ἐστὶ τὰ κατὰ τὸν κόσμον· ταῦτα γὰρ οὐχ ὁτὲ μὲν τοιαδὶ πάλιν δʼ ἀλλοῖα φαίνεται, ταὐτὰ δʼ ἀεὶ καὶ μεταβολῆς οὐδεμιᾶς κοινωνοῦντα.","kind":"passage"},{"id":"RAW_DAZY_BO_3:P0025","text":"ἔτι δʼ εἰ κίνησις ἔστι, καὶ κινούμενόν τι, κινεῖται δὲ πᾶν ἔκ τινος καὶ εἴς τι· δεῖ ἄρα τὸ κινούμενον εἶναι ἐν ἐκείνῳ ἐξ οὗ κινήσεται καὶ οὐκ εἶναι ἐν αὐτῷ, καὶ εἰς τοδὶ κινεῖσθαι καὶ γίγνεσθαι ἐν τούτῳ, τὸ δὲ κατὰ τὴν ἀντίφασιν μὴ συναληθεύεσθαι κατʼ αὐτούς. καὶ εἰ κατὰ τὸ ποσὸν συνεχῶς τὰ δεῦρο ῥεῖ καὶ κινεῖται, καί τις τοῦτο θείη καίπερ οὐκ ἀληθὲς ὄν, διὰ τί κατὰ τὸ ποιὸν οὐ μενεῖ; φαίνονται γὰρ οὐχ ἥκιστα τὰ κατὰ τὰς ἀντιφάσεις  ταὐτοῦ κατηγορεῖν ἐκ τοῦ τὸ ποσὸν ὑπειληφέναι μὴ μένειν ἐπὶ τῶν σωμάτων, διὸ καὶ εἶναι τετράπηχυ τὸ αὐτὸ καὶ οὐκ εἶναι. ἡ δʼ οὐσία κατὰ τὸ ποιόν, τοῦτο δὲ τῆς ὡρισμένης φύσεως, τὸ δὲ ποσὸν τῆς ἀορίστου.","kind":"passage"},{"id":"RAW_DAZY_BO_3:P0026","text":"ἔτι διὰ τί προστάττοντος τοῦ ἰατροῦ τοδὶ τὸ σιτίον προσενέγκασθαι προσφέρονται; τί γὰρ μᾶλλον τοῦτο ἄρτος ἐστὶν ἢ οὐκ ἔστιν; ὥστʼ οὐθὲν ἂν διέχοι φαγεῖν ἢ μὴ φαγεῖν· νῦν δʼ ὡς ἀληθεύοντες περὶ αὐτὸ καὶ ὄντος τοῦ προσταχθέντος σιτίου τούτου προσφέρονται τοῦτο· καίτοι γʼ οὐκ ἔδει μὴ διαμενούσης παγίως μηδεμιᾶς φύσεως ἐν τοῖς αἰσθητοῖς ἀλλʼ ἀεὶ πασῶν κινουμένων καὶ ῥεουσῶν.","kind":"passage"},{"id":"RAW_DAZY_BO_3:P0027","text":"ἔτι δʼ εἰ μὲν ἀλλοιούμεθα ἀεὶ καὶ μηδέποτε διαμένομεν οἱ αὐτοί, τί καὶ θαυμαστὸν εἰ μηδέποθʼ ἡμῖν ταὐτὰ φαίνεται καθάπερ τοῖς κάμνουσιν  (καὶ γὰρ τούτοις διὰ τὸ μὴ ὁμοίως διακεῖσθαι τὴν ἕξιν καὶ ὅθʼ ὑγίαινον, οὐχ ὅμοια φαίνεται τὰ κατὰ τὰς αἰσθήσεις, αὐτὰ μὲν οὐδεμιᾶς διά γε τοῦτο μεταβολῆς κοινωνοῦντα τὰ αἰσθητά, αἰσθήματα δʼ ἕτερα ποιοῦντα τοῖς κάμνουσι καὶ μὴ τὰ αὐτά· τὸν αὐτὸν δὴ τρόπον ἔχειν καὶ τῆς εἰρημένης μεταβολῆς γιγνομένης ἴσως ἀναγκαῖόν ἐστιν); εἰ δὲ μὴ μεταβάλλομεν ἀλλʼ οἱ αὐτοὶ διατελοῦμεν ὄντες, εἴη ἄν τι μένον.—πρὸς μὲν οὖν τοὺς ἐκ λόγου τὰς εἰρημένας ἀπορίας ἔχοντας οὐ ῥᾴδιον διαλῦσαι μὴ τιθέντων τι καὶ τούτου μηκέτι λόγον ἀπαιτούντων· οὕτω γὰρ πᾶς λόγος καὶ πᾶσα ἀπόδειξις γίγνεται· μηθὲν γὰρ τιθέντες ἀναιροῦσι τὸ διαλέγεσθαι καὶ ὅλως λόγον,—ὥστε πρὸς μὲν τοὺς τοιούτους οὐκ ἔστι λόγος, πρὸς δὲ τοὺς διαποροῦντας ἐκ τῶν παραδεδομένων ἀποριῶν ῥᾴδιον ἀπαντᾶν καὶ διαλύειν","kind":"passage"},{"id":"RAW_DAZY_BO_3:P0028","text":"τὰ ποιοῦντα τὴν ἀπορίαν ἐν αὐτοῖς· δῆλον δʼ ἐκ τῶν εἰρημένων. ὥστε φανερὸν ἐκ τούτων. ὅτι οὐκ ἐνδέχεται τὰς ἀντικειμένας φάσεις περὶ τοῦ αὐτοῦ καθʼ ἕνα χρόνον ἀληθεύειν, οὐδὲ τὰ ἐναντία, διὰ τὸ λέγεσθαι κατὰ στέρησιν πᾶσαν ἐναντιότητα· δῆλον δὲ τοῦτʼ ἐπʼ ἀρχὴν τοὺς λόγους ἀναλύουσι τοὺς τῶν ἐναντίων. ὁμοίως δʼ οὐδὲ τῶν ἀνὰ μέσον οὐδὲν οἷόν τε κατηγορεῖσθαι καθʼ ἑνὸς καὶ τοῦ αὐτοῦ· λευκοῦ γὰρ ὄντος τοῦ ὑποκειμένου λέγοντες αὐτὸ εἶναι οὔτε μέλαν οὔτε λευκὸν ψευσόμεθα· συμβαίνει γὰρ εἶναι λευκὸν αὐτὸ καὶ μὴ εἶναι· θάτερον γὰρ τῶν συμπεπλεγμένων ἀληθεύσεται κατʼ αὐτοῦ, τοῦτο δʼ ἐστὶν ἀντίφασις τοῦ λευκοῦ.","kind":"passage"},{"id":"RAW_DAZY_BO_3:P0029","text":"οὔτε δὴ καθʼ Ἡράκλειτον ἐνδέχεται λέγοντας ἀληθεύειν, οὔτε κατʼ Ἀναξαγόραν· εἰ δὲ μή, συμβήσεται τἀναντία τοῦ αὐτοῦ κατηγορεῖν· ὅταν γὰρ ἐν παντὶ φῇ παντὸς εἶναι μοῖραν, οὐδὲν μᾶλλον εἶναί φησι γλυκὺ ἢ πικρὸν ἢ τῶν λοιπῶν ὁποιανοῦν ἐναντιώσεων, εἴπερ ἐν ἅπαντι πᾶν ὑπάρχει μὴ δυνάμει μόνον ἀλλʼ ἐνεργεία ι ικαὶ ἀποκεκριμένον. ὁμοίως δὲ οὐδὲ πάσας ψευδεῖς οὐδʼ ἀληθεῖς τὰς φάσεις δυνατὸν εἶναι, διʼ ἄλλα τε πολλὰ τῶν συναχθέντων ἂν δυσχερῶν διὰ ταύτην τὴν θέσιν, καὶ διότι ψευδῶν μὲν οὐσῶν πασῶν οὐδʼ αὐτὸ τοῦτό τις φάσκων ἀληθεύσει, ἀληθῶν δὲ ψευδεῖς εἶναι πάσας λέγων οὐ ψεύσεται.","kind":"passage"},{"id":"RAW_DAZY_BO_3:P0030","text":"πᾶσα δʼ ἐπιστήμη ζητεῖ τινὰς ἀρχὰς καὶ αἰτίας περὶ ἕκαστον τῶν ὑφʼ αὑτὴν ἐπιστητῶν,  οἷον ἰατρικὴ καὶ γυμναστικὴ καὶ τῶν λοιπῶν ἑκάστη τῶν ποιητικῶν καὶ μαθηματικῶν. ἑκάστη γὰρ τούτων περιγραψαμένη τι γένος αὑτῇ περὶ τοῦτο πραγματεύεται ὡς ὑπάρχον καὶ ὄν, οὐχ ᾗ δὲ ὄν, ἀλλʼ ἑτέρα τις αὕτη παρὰ ταύτας τὰς ἐπιστήμας ἐστὶν ἐπιστήμη. τῶν δὲ λεχθεισῶν ἐπιστημῶν ἑκάστη λαβοῦσά πως τὸ τί ἐστιν ἐν ἑκάστῳ γένει πειρᾶται δεικνύναι τὰ λοιπὰ μαλακώτερον ἢ ἀκριβέστερον. λαμβάνουσι δὲ τὸ τί ἐστιν αἱ μὲν διʼ αἰσθήσεως αἱ δʼ ὑποτιθέμεναι· διὸ καὶ δῆλον ἐκ τῆς τοιαύτης ἐπαγωγῆς ὅτι τῆς οὐσίας καὶ τοῦ τί ἐστιν οὐκ ἔστιν ἀπόδειξις. ἐπεὶ δʼ ἔστι τις ἡ περὶ φύσεως ἐπιστήμη, δῆλον ὅτι καὶ πρακτικῆς ἑτέρα καὶ ποιητικῆς ἔσται.","kind":"passage"},{"id":"RAW_DAZY_BO_3:P0031","text":"ποιητικῆς μὲν γὰρ ἐν τῷ ποιοῦντι καὶ οὐ τῷ ποιουμένῳ τῆς κινήσεως ἡ ἀρχή, καὶ τοῦτʼ ἔστιν εἴτε τέχνη τις εἴτʼ ἄλλη τις δύναμις· ὁμοίως δὲ καὶ τῆς πρακτικῆς οὐκ ἐν τῷ πρακτῷ μᾶλλον δʼ ἐν τοῖς πράττουσιν ἡ κίνησις. ἡ δὲ τοῦ φυσικοῦ περὶ τὰ ἔχοντʼ ἐν ἑαυτοῖς κινήσεως ἀρχήν ἐστιν. ὅτι μὲν τοίνυν οὔτε πρακτικὴν οὔτε ποιητικὴν ἀλλὰ θεωρητικὴν ἀναγκαῖον εἶναι τὴν φυσικὴν ἐπιστήμην, δῆλον ἐκ τούτων (εἰς ἓν γάρ τι τούτων τῶν γενῶν ἀνάγκη πίπτειν)· ἐπεὶ δὲ τὸ τί ἐστιν ἀναγκαῖον ἑκάστῃ πως τῶν ἐπιστημῶν εἰδέναι καὶ τούτῳ χρῆσθαι ἀρχῇ, δεῖ μὴ λανθάνειν πῶς ὁριστέον τῷ φυσικῷ καὶ πῶς ὁ τῆς οὐσίας λόγος ληπτέος, πότερον ὡς τὸ σιμὸν ἢ μᾶλλον ὡς τὸ κοῖλον. τούτων γὰρ ὁ μὲν τοῦ σιμοῦ λόγος μετὰ τῆς ὕλης λέγεται τῆς τοῦ πράγματος, ὁ δὲ τοῦ κοίλου χωρὶς τῆς ὕλης· ἡ γὰρ σιμότης ἐν ῥινὶ γίγνεται, διὸ καὶ ὁ λόγος αὐτῆς μετὰ ταύτης θεωρεῖται· τὸ σιμὸν γάρ ἐστι ῥὶς κοίλη.","kind":"passage"},{"id":"RAW_DAZY_BO_3:P0032","text":"φανερὸν οὖν ὅτι καὶ σαρκὸς καὶ ὀφθαλμοῦ καὶ τῶν λοιπῶν μορίων μετὰ τῆς ὕλης ἀεὶ τὸν λόγον ἀποδοτέον. ἐπεὶ δʼ ἔστι τις ἐπιστήμη τοῦ ὄντος ᾗ ὂν καὶ χωριστόν, σκεπτέον πότερόν ποτε τῇ φυσικῆ ι ιτὴν αὐτὴν θετέον εἶναι ταύτην ἢ μᾶλλον ἑτέραν. ἡ μὲν οὖν φυσικὴ περὶ τὰ κινήσεως ἔχοντʼ ἀρχὴν ἐν αὑτοῖς ἐστίν, ἡ δὲ μαθηματικὴ θεωρητικὴ μὲν καὶ περὶ μένοντά τις αὕτη, ἀλλʼ οὐ χωριστά. περὶ τὸ χωριστὸν ἄρα ὂν καὶ ἀκίνητον ἑτέρα τούτων ἀμφοτέρων τῶν ἐπιστημῶν ἔστι τις, εἴπερ ὑπάρχει τις οὐσία τοιαύτη, λέγω δὲ χωριστὴ καὶ ἀκίνητος, ὅπερ πειρασόμεθα δεικνύναι. καὶ εἴπερ ἔστι τις τοιαύτη φύσις ἐν τοῖς οὖσιν, ἐνταῦθʼ ἂν εἴη που καὶ τὸ θεῖον, καὶ αὕτη ἂν εἴη πρώτη καὶ κυριωτάτη ἀρχή.  δῆλον τοίνυν ὅτι τρία γένη τῶν θεωρητικῶν ἐπιστημῶν ἔστι, φυσική, μαθηματική, θεολογική.","kind":"passage"},{"id":"RAW_DAZY_BO_3:P0033","text":"βέλτιστον μὲν οὖν τὸ τῶν θεωρητικῶν γένος, τούτων δʼ αὐτῶν ἡ τελευταία λεχθεῖσα· περὶ τὸ τιμιώτατον γάρ ἐστι τῶν ὄντων, βελτίων δὲ καὶ χείρων ἑκάστη λέγεται κατὰ τὸ οἰκεῖον ἐπιστητόν. ἀπορήσειε δʼ ἄν τις πότερόν ποτε τὴν τοῦ ὄντος ᾗ ὂν ἐπιστήμην καθόλου δεῖ θεῖναι ἢ οὔ. τῶν μὲν γὰρ μαθηματικῶν ἑκάστη περὶ ἕν τι γένος ἀφωρισμένον ἐστίν, ἡ δὲ καθόλου κοινὴ περὶ πάντων. εἰ μὲν οὖν αἱ φυσικαὶ οὐσίαι πρῶται τῶν ὄντων εἰσί, κἂν ἡ φυσικὴ πρώτη τῶν ἐπιστημῶν εἴη· εἰ δʼ ἔστιν ἑτέρα φύσις καὶ οὐσία χωριστὴ καὶ ἀκίνητος, ἑτέραν ἀνάγκη καὶ τὴν ἐπιστήμην αὐτῆς εἶναι καὶ προτέραν τῆς φυσικῆς καὶ καθόλου τῷ προτέραν.","kind":"passage"},{"id":"RAW_DAZY_BO_3:P0034","text":"ἐπεὶ δὲ τὸ ἁπλῶς ὂν κατὰ πλείους λέγεται τρόπους, ὧν εἷς ἐστὶν ὁ κατὰ συμβεβηκὸς εἶναι λεγόμενος, σκεπτέον πρῶτον περὶ τοῦ οὕτως ὄντος. ὅτι μὲν οὖν οὐδεμία τῶν παραδεδομένων ἐπιστημῶν πραγματεύεται περὶ τὸ συμβεβηκός, δῆλον (οὔτε γὰρ οἰκοδομικὴ σκοπεῖ τὸ συμβησόμενον τοῖς τῇ οἰκίᾳ χρησομένοις, οἷον εἰ λυπηρῶς ἢ τοὐναντίον οἰκήσουσιν, οὔθʼ ὑφαντικὴ οὔτε σκυτοτομικὴ οὔτε ὀψοποιική, τὸ δὲ καθʼ αὑτὴν ἴδιον ἑκάστη τούτων σκοπεῖ τῶν ἐπιστημῶν μόνον, τοῦτο δʼ ἐστὶ τὸ οἰκεῖον τέλος·  οὐδὲ τὸν ὄντα μουσικὸν ὅτι γενόμενος γραμματικὸς ἅμα ἔσται τὰ ἀμφότερα, πρότερον οὐκ ὤν, ὃ δὲ μὴ ἀεὶ ὂν ἔστιν, ἐγένετο τοῦτο, ὥσθʼ ἅμα μουσικὸς ἐγένετο καὶ γραμματικός,—τοῦτο δὲ οὐδεμία ζητεῖ τῶν ὁμολογουμένως οὐσῶν ἐπιστημῶν πλὴν ἡ σοφιστική· περὶ τὸ συμβεβηκὸς γὰρ αὕτη μόνη πραγματεύεται, διὸ Πλάτων οὐ κακῶς εἴρηκε φήσας τὸν σοφιστὴν περὶ τὸ μὴ ὂν διατρίβειν)· ὅτι δʼ οὐδʼ ἐνδεχόμενόν ἐστιν εἶναι τοῦ συμβεβηκότος","kind":"passage"},{"id":"RAW_DAZY_BO_3:P0035","text":"ἐπιστήμην, φανερὸν ἔσται πειραθεῖσιν ἰδεῖν τί ποτʼ ἐστὶ τὸ συμβεβηκός. πᾶν δή φαμεν εἶναι τὸ μὲν ἀεὶ καὶ ἐξ ἀνάγκης (ἀνάγκης δʼ οὐ τῆς κατὰ τὸ βίαιον λεγομένης ἀλλʼ ᾗ χρώμεθα ἐν τοῖς κατὰ τὰς ἀποδείξεις), τὸ δʼ ὡς ἐπὶ τὸ πολύ, τὸ δʼ οὔθʼ ὡς ἐπὶ τὸ πολὺ οὔτʼ ἀεὶ καὶ ἐξ ἀνάγκης ἀλλʼ ὅπως ἔτυχεν· οἷον ἐπὶ κυνὶ γένοιτʼ ἂν ψῦχος, ἀλλὰ τοῦτʼ οὔτʼ  ἀεὶ καὶ ἐξ ἀνάγκης οὔθʼ ὡς ἐπὶ τὸ πολὺ γίγνεται, συμβαίη δέ ποτʼ ἄν.  ἔστι δὴ τὸ συμβεβηκὸς ὃ γίγνεται μέν, οὐκ ἀεὶ δʼ οὐδʼ ἐξ ἀνάγκης οὐδʼ ὡς ἐπὶ τὸ πολύ. τί μὲν οὖν ἐστὶ τὸ συμβεβηκός, εἴρηται, διότι δʼ οὐκ ἔστιν ἐπιστήμη τοῦ τοιούτου, δῆλον· ἐπιστήμη μὲν γὰρ πᾶσα τοῦ ἀεὶ ὄντος ἢ ὡς ἐπὶ τὸ πολύ, τὸ δὲ συμβεβηκὸς ἐν οὐδετέρῳ τούτων ἐστίν. ὅτι δὲ τοῦ κατὰ συμβεβηκὸς ὄντος οὐκ εἰσὶν αἰτίαι καὶ ἀρχαὶ τοιαῦται οἷαίπερ τοῦ καθʼ αὑτὸ ὄντος, δῆλον· ἔσται γὰρ ἅπαντʼ ἐξ ἀνάγκης.","kind":"passage"},{"id":"RAW_DAZY_BO_3:P0036","text":"εἰ γὰρ τόδε μὲν ἔστι τοῦδε ὄντος τόδε δὲ τοῦδε, τοῦτο δὲ μὴ ὅπως ἔτυχεν ἀλλʼ ἐξ ἀνάγκης, ἐξ ἀνάγκης ἔσται καὶ οὗ τοῦτʼ ἦν αἴτιον ἕως τοῦ τελευταίου λεγομένου αἰτιατοῦ (τοῦτο δʼ ἦν κατὰ συμβεβηκός), ὥστʼ ἐξ ἀνάγκης ἅπαντʼ ἔσται, καὶ τὸ ὁποτέρως ἔτυχε καὶ τὸ ἐνδέχεσθαι καὶ γενέσθαι καὶ μὴ παντελῶς ἐκ τῶν γιγνομένων ἀναιρεῖται. κἂν μὴ ὂν δὲ ἀλλὰ γιγνόμενον τὸ αἴτιον ὑποτεθῇ, ταὐτὰ συμβήσεται· πᾶν γὰρ ἐξ ἀνάγκης γενήσεται. ἡ γὰρ αὔριον ἔκλειψις γενήσεται ἂν τόδε γένηται, τοῦτο δʼ ἐὰν ἕτερόν τι, καὶ τοῦτʼ ἂν ἄλλο· καὶ τοῦτον δὴ τὸν τρόπον ἀπὸ πεπερασμένου χρόνου τοῦ ἀπὸ τοῦ νῦν μέχρι αὔριον ἀφαιρουμένου χρόνου ἥξει ποτὲ εἰς τὸ ὑπάρχον, ὥστʼ ἐπεὶ τοῦτʼ ἔστιν, ἅπαντʼ ἐξ ἀνάγκης τὰ μετὰ τοῦτο γενήσεται, ὥστε πάντα ἐξ ἀνάγκης γίγνεσθαι.","kind":"passage"},{"id":"RAW_DAZY_BO_3:P0037","text":"τὸ δʼ ὡς ἀληθὲς ὂν καὶ κατὰ συμβεβηκὸς τὸ μέν ἐστιν ἐν συμπλοκῇ διανοίας καὶ πάθος ἐν ταύτῃ (διὸ περὶ μὲν τὸ οὕτως ὂν οὐ ζητοῦνται αἱ ἀρχαί, περὶ δὲ τὸ ἔξω ὂν καὶ χωριστόν)· τὸ δʼ οὐκ ἀναγκαῖον ἀλλʼ ἀόριστον, λέγω δὲ τὸ κατὰ συμβεβηκός· τοῦ τοιούτου δʼ ἄτακτα καὶ ἄπειρα τὰ αἴτια.—τὸ δὲ ἕνεκά του ἐν τοῖς φύσει γιγνομένοις ἢ ἀπὸ διανοίας ἐστίν, τύχη δέ ἐστιν ὅταν τι τούτων γένηται κατὰ συμβεβηκός· ὥσπερ γὰρ καὶ ὄν ἐστι τὸ μὲν καθʼ αὑτὸ τὸ δὲ κατὰ συμβεβηκός, οὕτω καὶ αἴτιον. ἡ τύχη δʼ αἰτία κατὰ συμβεβηκὸς ἐν τοῖς κατὰ προαίρεσιν τῶν ἕνεκά του γιγνομένοις, διὸ περὶ ταὐτὰ τύχη καὶ διάνοια· προαίρεσις γὰρ οὐ χωρὶς διανοίας. τὰ δʼ αἴτια ἀόριστα ἀφʼ ὧν ἂν γένοιτο τὰ ἀπὸ τύχης, διὸ ἄδηλος ἀνθρωπίνῳ λογισμῷ καὶ αἴτιον κατὰ συμβεβηκός, ἁπλῶς δʼ οὐδενός. ἀγαθὴ δὲ τύχη καὶ κακὴ ὅταν ἀγαθὸν ἢ φαῦλον ἀποβῇ· εὐτυχία δὲ καὶ δυστυχία περὶ μέγεθος τούτων.","kind":"passage"},{"id":"RAW_DAZY_BO_3:P0038","text":"ἐπεὶ δʼ οὐθὲν κατὰ συμβεβηκὸς πρότερον τῶν καθʼ αὑτό, οὐδʼ ἄρʼ αἴτια· εἰ ἄρα τύχη ἢ τὸ αὐτόματον αἴτιον τοῦ οὐρανοῦ, πρότερον νοῦς αἴτιος καὶ φύσις.","kind":"passage"},{"id":"RAW_DAZY_BO_3:P0039","text":"ἔστι δὲ τὸ μὲν ἐνεργείᾳ μόνον τὸ δὲ δυνάμει τὸ δὲ δυνάμει καὶ ἐνεργείᾳ, τὸ μὲν ὂν τὸ δὲ ποσὸν τὸ δὲ τῶν λοιπῶν. οὐκ ἔστι δέ τις κίνησις παρὰ τὰ πράγματα· μεταβάλλει γὰρ ἀεὶ κατὰ τὰς τοῦ ὄντος κατηγορίας, κοινὸν δʼ ἐπὶ τούτων οὐδέν ἐστιν ὃ οὐδʼ ἐν μιᾷ κατηγορίᾳ. ἕκαστον δὲ διχῶς ὑπάρχει πᾶσιν (οἷον τὸ τόδε—τὸ μὲν γὰρ μορφὴ αὐτοῦ τὸ δὲ στέρησις—καὶ κατὰ τὸ ποιὸν τὸ μὲν λευκὸν τὸ δὲ μέλαν, καὶ κατὰ τὸ ποσὸν τὸ μὲν τέλειον τὸ δὲ ἀτελές, καὶ κατὰ φορὰν τὸ μὲν ἄνω τὸ δὲ κάτω, ἢ κοῦφον καὶ βαρύ)· ὥστε κινήσεως καὶ μεταβολῆς τοσαῦτʼ εἴδη ὅσα τοῦ ὄντος. διῃρημένου δὲ καθʼ ἕκαστον γένος τοῦ μὲν δυνάμει τοῦ δʼ ἐντελεχείᾳ, τὴν τοῦ δυνάμει ᾗ τοιοῦτόν ἐστιν ἐνέργειαν λέγω κίνησιν. ὅτι δʼ ἀληθῆ λέγομεν, ἐνθένδε δῆλον· ὅταν γὰρ τὸ οἰκοδομητόν, ᾗ τοιοῦτον αὐτὸ λέγομεν εἶναι, ἐνεργείᾳ ᾖ, οἰκοδομεῖται, καὶ ἔστι τοῦτο οἰκοδόμησις· ὁμοίως μάθησις, ἰάτρευσις, βάδισις, ἅλσις, γήρανσις, ἅδρυνσις.","kind":"passage"},{"id":"RAW_DAZY_BO_3:P0040","text":"συμβαίνει δὲ κινεῖσθαι ὅταν ἡ ἐντελέχεια ᾖ αὐτή, καὶ οὔτε πρότερον οὔθʼ ὕστερον. ἡ δὴ τοῦ δυνάμει ὄντος, ὅταν ἐντελεχείᾳ ὂν ἐνεργῇ, οὐχ ᾗ αὐτὸ ἀλλʼ ᾗ κινητόν, κίνησίς ἐστιν. λέγω δὲ τὸ ᾗ ὧδε. ἔστι γὰρ ὁ χαλκὸς δυνάμει ἀνδριάς· ἀλλʼ ὅμως οὐχ ἡ τοῦ χαλκοῦ ἐντελέχεια, ᾗ χαλκός, κίνησίς ἐστιν. οὐ γὰρ ταὐτὸν χαλκῷ εἶναι καὶ δυνάμει τινί, ἐπεὶ εἰ ταὐτὸν ἦν ἁπλῶς κατὰ τὸν λόγον, ἦν ἂν ἡ τοῦ χαλκοῦ ἐντελέχεια κίνησίς τις. οὐκ ἔστι δὲ ταὐτό (δῆλον δʼ ἐπὶ τῶν ἐναντίων· τὸ μὲν γὰρ δύνασθαι ὑγιαίνειν καὶ δύνασθαι κάμνειν οὐ ταὐτόν—καὶ γὰρ ἂν τὸ ὑγιαίνειν καὶ τὸ κάμνειν ταὐτὸν ἦν—τὸ δʼ ὑποκείμενον καὶ ὑγιαῖνον καὶ νοσοῦν, εἴθʼ ὑγρότης εἴθʼ αἷμα, ταὐτὸ καὶ ἕν). ἐπεὶ δὲ οὐ τὸ αὐτό, ὥσπερ οὐδὲ χρῶμα ταὐτὸν καὶ ὁρατόν, ἡ τοῦ δυνατοῦ καὶ ᾗ δυνατὸν ἐντελέχεια κίνησίς ἐστιν.","kind":"passage"},{"id":"RAW_DAZY_BO_3:P0041","text":"ὅτι μὲν οὖν ἐστιν αὕτη, καὶ ὅτι συμβαίνει τότε κινεῖσθαι ὅταν ἡ ἐντελέχεια ᾖ αὐτή, καὶ οὔτε πρότερον οὔθʼ ὕστερον, δῆλον (ἐνδέχεται γὰρ ἕκαστον ὁτὲ μὲν ἐνεργεῖν ὁτὲ δὲ μή,  οἷον τὸ οἰκοδομητὸν ᾗ οἰκοδομητόν, καὶ ἡ τοῦ οἰκοδομητοῦ ἐνέργεια ᾗ οἰκοδομητὸν οἰκοδόμησίς ἐστιν· ἢ γὰρ τοῦτό ἐστιν, ἡ οἰκοδόμησις, ἡ ἐνέργεια, ἢ οἰκία· ἀλλʼ ὅταν οἰκία ᾖ, οὐκέτι οἰκοδομητόν, οἰκοδομεῖται δὲ τὸ οἰκοδομητόν· ἀνάγκη ἄρα οἰκοδόμησιν τὴν ἐνέργειαν εἶναι, ἡ δʼ οἰκοδόμησις κίνησίς τις, ὁ δʼ αὐτὸς λόγος καὶ ἐπὶ τῶν ἄλλων κινήσεων)· ὅτι δὲ καλῶς εἴρηται, δῆλον ἐξ ὧν οἱ ἄλλοι λέγουσι περὶ αὐτῆς, καὶ ἐκ τοῦ μὴ ῥᾴδιον εἶναι διορίσαι ἄλλως αὐτήν. οὔτε γὰρ ἐν ἄλλῳ τις γένει δύναιτʼ ἂν θεῖναι αὐτήν· δῆλον δʼ ἐξ ὧν λέγουσιν· οἱ μὲν γὰρ ἑτερότητα καὶ ἀνισότητα καὶ τὸ μὴ ὄν, ὧν οὐδὲν ἀνάγκη κινεῖσθαι, ἀλλʼ οὐδʼ ἡ μεταβολὴ οὔτʼ εἰς ταῦτα οὔτʼ ἐκ τούτων μᾶλλον ἢ τῶν ἀντικειμένων.","kind":"passage"},{"id":"RAW_DAZY_BO_3:P0042","text":"αἴτιον δὲ τοῦ εἰς ταῦτα τιθέναι ὅτι ἀόριστόν τι δοκεῖ εἶναι ἡ κίνησις, τῆς δʼ ἑτέρας συστοιχίας αἱ ἀρχαὶ διὰ τὸ στερητικαὶ εἶναι ἀόριστοι· οὔτε γὰρ τόδε οὔτε τοιόνδε οὐδεμία αὐτῶν οὔτε τῶν λοιπῶν κατηγοριῶν. τοῦ δὲ δοκεῖν ἀόριστον εἶναι τὴν κίνησιν αἴτιον ὅτι οὔτʼ εἰς δύναμιν τῶν ὄντων οὔτʼ εἰς ἐνέργειαν ἔστι θεῖναι αὐτήν· οὔτε γὰρ τὸ δυνατὸν ποσὸν εἶναι κινεῖται ἐξ ἀνάγκης, οὔτε τὸ ἐνεργείᾳ ποσόν, ἥ τε κίνησις ἐνέργεια μὲν εἶναι δοκεῖ τις, ἀτελὴς δέ· αἴτιον δʼ ὅτι ἀτελὲς τὸ δυνατὸν οὗ ἐστὶν ἐνέργεια. καὶ διὰ τοῦτο χαλεπὸν αὐτὴν λαβεῖν τί ἐστιν· ἢ γὰρ εἰς στέρησιν ἀνάγκη θεῖναι ἢ εἰς δύναμιν ἢ εἰς ἐνέργειαν ἁπλῆν, τούτων δʼ οὐδὲν φαίνεται ἐνδεχόμενον, ὥστε λείπεται τὸ λεχθὲν εἶναι, καὶ ἐνέργειαν καὶ  ἐνέργειαν τὴν εἰρημένην, ἰδεῖν μὲν χαλεπὴν ἐνδεχομένην δʼ εἶναι. καὶ ὅτι ἐστὶν ἡ κίνησις ἐν τῷ κινητῷ, δῆλον· ἐντελέχεια γάρ ἐστι τούτου ὑπὸ τοῦ κινητικοῦ.","kind":"passage"},{"id":"RAW_DAZY_BO_3:P0043","text":"καὶ ἡ τοῦ κινητικοῦ ἐνέργεια οὐκ ἄλλη ἐστίν. δεῖ μὲν γὰρ εἶναι ἐντελέχειαν ἀμφοῖν· κινητικὸν μὲν γάρ ἐστι τῷ δύνασθαι, κινοῦν δὲ τῷ ἐνεργεῖν, ἀλλʼ ἔστιν ἐνεργητικὸν τοῦ κινητοῦ, ὥσθʼ ὁμοίως μία ἡ ἀμφοῖν ἐνέργεια ὥσπερ τὸ αὐτὸ διάστημα ἓν πρὸς δύο καὶ δύο πρὸς ἕν, καὶ τὸ ἄναντες καὶ τὸ κάταντες, ἀλλὰ τὸ εἶναι οὐχ ἕν· ὁμοίως δὲ καὶ ἐπὶ τοῦ κινοῦντος καὶ κινουμένου.","kind":"passage"},{"id":"RAW_DAZY_BO_3:P0044","text":"τὸ δʼ ἄπειρον ἢ τὸ ἀδύνατον διελθεῖν τῷ μὴ πεφυκέναι διιέναι, καθάπερ ἡ φωνὴ ἀόρατος, ἢ τὸ διέξοδον ἔχον ἀτελεύτητον, ἢ ὃ μόλις, ἢ ὃ πεφυκὸς ἔχειν μὴ ἔχει διέξοδον ἢ πέρας· ἔτι προσθέσει ἢ ἀφαιρέσει ἢ ἄμφω.  χωριστὸν μὲν δὴ αὐτό τι ὂν οὐχ οἷόν τʼ εἶναι· εἰ γὰρ μήτε μέγεθος μήτε πλῆθος, οὐσία δʼ αὐτὸ τὸ ἄπειρον καὶ μὴ συμβεβηκός, ἀδιαίρετον ἔσται (τὸ γὰρ διαιρετὸν ἢ μέγεθος ἢ πλῆθος), εἰ δὲ ἀδιαίρετον, οὐκ ἄπειρον, εἰ μὴ καθάπερ ἡ φωνὴ ἀόρατος· ἀλλʼ οὐχ οὕτω λέγουσιν οὐδʼ ἡμεῖς ζητοῦμεν, ἀλλʼ ὡς ἀδιέξοδον. ἔτι πῶς ἐνδέχεται καθʼ αὑτὸ εἶναι ἄπειρον, εἰ μὴ καὶ ἀριθμὸς καὶ μέγεθος, ὧν πάθος τὸ ἄπειρον; ἔτι εἰ κατὰ συμβεβηκός, οὐκ ἂν εἴη στοιχεῖον τῶν ὄντων ᾗ ἄπειρον, ὥσπερ οὐδὲ τὸ ἀόρατον τῆς διαλέκτου, καίτοι ἡ φωνὴ ἀόρατος. καὶ ὅτι οὐκ ἔστιν ἐνεργείᾳ εἶναι τὸ ἄπειρον, δῆλον.","kind":"passage"},{"id":"RAW_DAZY_BO_3:P0045","text":"ἔσται γὰρ ὁτιοῦν αὐτοῦ ἄπειρον μέρος τὸ λαμβανόμενον (τὸ γὰρ ἀπείρῳ εἶναι καὶ ἄπειρον τὸ αὐτό, εἴπερ οὐσία τὸ ἄπειρον καὶ μὴ καθʼ ὑποκειμένου), ὥστε ἢ ἀδιαίρετον, ἢ εἰς ἄπειρα διαιρετόν, εἰ μεριστόν· πολλὰ δʼ εἶναι τὸ αὐτὸ ἀδύνατον ἄπειρα (ὥσπερ γὰρ ἀέρος ἀὴρ μέρος, οὕτως ἄπειρον ἀπείρου, εἰ ἔστιν οὐσία καὶ ἀρχή)· ἀμέριστον ἄρα καὶ ἀδιαίρετον. ἀλλὰ ἀδύνατον τὸ ἐντελεχείᾳ ὂν ἄπειρον (ποσὸν γὰρ εἶναι ἀνάγκη)· κατὰ συμβεβηκὸς ἄρα ὑπάρχει. ἀλλʼ εἰ οὕτως, εἴρηται ὅτι οὐκ ἐνδέχεται εἶναι ἀρχήν, ἀλλʼ ἐκεῖνο ᾧ συμβέβηκε, τὸν ἀέρα ἢ τὸ ἄρτιον.—αὕτη μὲν οὖν ἡ ζήτησις καθόλου, ὅτι δʼ ἐν τοῖς αἰσθητοῖς οὐκ ἔστιν, ἐνθένδε δῆλον· εἰ γὰρ σώματος λόγος τὸ ἐπιπέδοις ὡρισμένον, οὐκ εἴη ἂν ἄπειρον σῶμα οὔτʼ αἰσθητὸν οὔτε νοητόν, οὐδʼ ἀριθμὸς ὡς κεχωρισμένος καὶ ἄπειρος· ἀριθμητὸν γὰρ ὁ ἀριθμὸς ἢ τὸ ἔχον ἀριθμόν. φυσικῶς δὲ ἐκ τῶνδε δῆλον· οὔτε γὰρ σύνθετον οἷόν τʼ εἶναι οὔθʼ ἁπλοῦν.","kind":"passage"},{"id":"RAW_DAZY_BO_3:P0046","text":"σύνθετον μὲν γὰρ οὐκ ἔσται σῶμα, εἰ πεπέρανται τῷ πλήθει τὰ στοιχεῖα (δεῖ γὰρ ἰσάζειν τὰ ἐναντία καὶ μὴ εἶναι ἓν αὐτῶν ἄπειρον· εἰ γὰρ ὁτῳοῦν λείπεται ἡ θατέρου σώματος δύναμις, φθαρήσεται ὑπὸ τοῦ ἀπείρου τὸ πεπερασμένον· ἕκαστον δʼ ἄπειρον εἶναι ἀδύνατον, σῶμα γάρ ἐστι τὸ πάντῃ ἔχον διάστασιν, ἄπειρον δὲ τὸ ἀπεράντως διεστηκός, ὥστʼ εἰ τὸ ἄπειρον σῶμα, πάντῃ ἔσται ἄπειρον)· οὐδὲ ἓν δὲ καὶ ἁπλοῦν ἐνδέχεται τὸ ἄπειρον εἶναι σῶμα, οὔθʼ ὡς λέγουσί τινες, παρὰ τὰ στοιχεῖα ἐξ οὗ γεννῶσι ταῦτα (οὐκ ἔστι γὰρ τοιοῦτο σῶμα παρὰ τὰ στοιχεῖα· ἅπαν γάρ, ἐξ οὗ ἐστί, καὶ διαλύεται εἰς τοῦτο, οὐ φαίνεται δὲ τοῦτο παρὰ τὰ ἁπλᾶ σώματα),  οὐδὲ πῦρ οὐδʼ ἄλλο τῶν στοιχείων οὐθέν· χωρὶς γὰρ τοῦ ἄπειρον εἶναί τι αὐτῶν, ἀδύνατον τὸ ἅπαν, κἂν ᾖ πεπερασμένον, ἢ εἶναι ἢ γίγνεσθαι ἕν τι αὐτῶν, ὥσπερ Ἡράκλειτός φησιν ἅπαντα γίγνεσθαί ποτε πῦρ.","kind":"passage"},{"id":"RAW_DAZY_BO_3:P0047","text":"ὁ δʼ αὐτὸς λόγος καὶ ἐπὶ τοῦ ἑνὸς ὃ ποιοῦσι παρὰ τὰ στοιχεῖα οἱ φυσικοί· πᾶν γὰρ μεταβάλλει ἐξ ἐναντίου, οἷον ἐκ θερμοῦ εἰς ψυχρόν.—ἔτι τὸ αἰσθητὸν σῶμα πού, καὶ ὁ αὐτὸς τόπος ὅλου καὶ μορίου, οἷον τῆς γῆς, ὥστʼ εἰ μὲν ὁμοειδές, ἀκίνητον ἔσται ἢ ἀεὶ οἰσθήσεται, τοῦτο δὲ ἀδύνατον (τί γὰρ μᾶλλον κάτω ἢ ἄνω ἢ ὁπουοῦν; οἷον εἰ βῶλος εἴη, ποῦ αὕτη κινήσεται ἢ μενεῖ; ὁ γὰρ τόπος τοῦ συγγενοῦς αὐτῇ σώματος ἄπειρος· καθέξει οὖν τὸν ὅλον τόπον; καὶ πῶς; τίς οὖν ἡ μονὴ καὶ ἡ κίνησις; ἢ πανταχοῦ μενεῖ—οὐ κινηθήσεται ἄρα, ἢ πανταχοῦ κινηθήσεται —οὐκ ἄρα στήσεται)· εἰ δʼ ἀνόμοιον τὸ πᾶν, ἀνόμοιοι καὶ οἱ τόποι, καὶ πρῶτον μὲν οὐχ ἓν τὸ σῶμα τοῦ παντὸς ἀλλʼ ἢ τῷ ἅπτεσθαι, εἶτα ἢ πεπερασμένα ταῦτʼ ἔσται ἢ ἄπειρα εἴδει.","kind":"passage"},{"id":"RAW_DAZY_BO_3:P0048","text":"πεπερασμένα μὲν οὖν οὐχ οἷόν τε (ἔσται γὰρ τὰ μὲν ἄπειρα τὰ δʼ οὔ, εἰ τὸ πᾶν ἄπειρον, οἷον πῦρ ἢ ὕδωρ· φθορὰ δὲ τὸ τοιοῦτον τοῖς ἐναντίοις)· εἰ δʼ ἄπειρα καὶ ἁπλᾶ, καὶ οἱ τόποι ἄπειροι καὶ ἔσται ἄπειρα στοιχεῖα· εἰ δὲ τοῦτʼ ἀδύνατον καὶ οἱ τόποι πεπερασμένοι, καὶ τὸ πᾶν ἀνάγκη πεπεράνθαι. ὅλως δʼ ἀδύνατον ἄπειρον εἶναι σῶμα καὶ τόπον τοῖς σώμασιν, εἰ πᾶν σῶμα αἰσθητὸν ἢ βάρος ἔχει ἢ κουφότητα· ἢ γὰρ ἐπὶ τὸ μέσον ἢ ἄνω οἰσθήσεται, ἀδύνατον δὲ τὸ ἄπειρον ἢ πᾶν ἢ τὸ ἥμισυ ὁποτερονοῦν πεπονθέναι· πῶς γὰρ διελεῖς; ἢ πῶς τοῦ ἀπείρου ἔσται τὸ μὲν κάτω τὸ δʼ ἄνω, ἢ ἔσχατον καὶ μέσον; ἔτι πᾶν σῶμα αἰσθητὸν ἐν τόπῳ, τόπου δὲ εἴδη ἕξ, ἀδύνατον δʼ ἐν τῷ ἀπείρῳ σώματι ταῦτʼ εἶναι. ὅλως δʼ εἰ ἀδύνατον τόπον ἄπειρον εἶναι, καὶ σῶμα ἀδύνατον· τὸ γὰρ ἐν τόπῳ πού, τοῦτο δὲ σημαίνει ἢ ἄνω ἢ κάτω ἢ τῶν λοιπῶν τι, τούτων δʼ ἕκαστον πέρας τι.","kind":"passage"},{"id":"RAW_DAZY_BO_3:P0049","text":"τὸ δʼ ἄπειρον οὐ ταὐτὸν ἐν μεγέθει καὶ κινήσει καὶ χρόνῳ ὡς μία τις φύσις, ἀλλὰ τὸ ὕστερον λέγεται κατὰ τὸ πρότερον, οἷον κίνησις κατὰ τὸ μέγεθος ἐφʼ οὗ κινεῖται ἢ ἀλλοιοῦται ἢ αὔξεται, χρόνος δὲ διὰ τὴν κίνησιν.","kind":"passage"},{"id":"RAW_DAZY_BO_3:P0050","text":"μεταβάλλει δὲ τὸ μεταβάλλον τὸ μὲν κατὰ συμβεβηκός, ὡς τὸ μουσικὸν βαδίζει, τὸ δὲ τῷ τούτου τι μεταβάλλειν ἁπλῶς λέγεται μεταβάλλειν, οἷον ὅσα κατὰ μέρη (ὑγιάζεται γὰρ τὸ σῶμα, ὅτι ὁ ὀφθαλμός), ἔστι δέ τι ὃ καθʼ αὑτὸ πρῶτον κινεῖται, καὶ τοῦτʼ ἔστι τὸ καθʼ αὑτὸ κινητόν. ἔστι δέ  καὶ ἐπὶ τοῦ κινοῦντος ὡσαύτως· κινεῖ γὰρ κατὰ συμβεβηκὸς τὸ δὲ κατὰ μέρος τὸ δὲ καθʼ αὑτό· ἔστι δέ τι τὸ κινοῦν πρῶτον· ἔστι δέ τι τὸ κινούμενον, ἔτι ἐν ᾧ χρόνῳ καὶ ἐξ οὗ καὶ εἰς ὅ. τὰ δʼ εἴδη καὶ τὰ πάθη καὶ ὁ τόπος, εἰς ἃ κινοῦνται τὰ κινούμενα, ἀκίνητά ἐστιν, οἷον ἐπιστήμη καὶ θερμότης· ἔστι δʼ οὐχ ἡ θερμότης κίνησις ἀλλʼ ἡ θέρμανσις. ἡ δὲ μὴ κατὰ συμβεβηκὸς μεταβολὴ οὐκ ἐν ἅπασιν ὑπάρχει ἀλλʼ ἐν τοῖς ἐναντίοις καὶ μεταξὺ καὶ ἐν ἀντιφάσει· τούτου δὲ πίστις ἐκ τῆς ἐπαγωγῆς.","kind":"passage"},{"id":"RAW_DAZY_BO_3:P0051","text":"μεταβάλλει δὲ τὸ μεταβάλλον ἢ ἐξ ὑποκειμένου εἰς ὑποκείμενον, ἢ οὐκ ἐξ ὑποκειμένου εἰς οὐχ ὑποκείμενον, ἢ ἐξ ὑποκειμένου εἰς οὐχ ὑποκείμενον, ἢ οὐκ ἐξ ὑποκειμένου εἰς ὑποκείμενον (λέγω δὲ ὑποκείμενον τὸ καταφάσει δηλούμενον), ὥστʼ ἀνάγκη τρεῖς εἶναι μεταβολάς· ἡ γὰρ ἐξ οὐχ ὑποκειμένου  εἰς μὴ ὑποκείμενον οὐκ ἔστι μεταβολή· οὔτε γὰρ ἐναντία οὔτε ἀντίφασίς ἐστιν, ὅτι οὐκ ἀντίθεσις. ἡ μὲν οὖν οὐκ ἐξ ὑποκειμένου εἰς ὑποκείμενον κατʼ ἀντίφασιν γένεσίς ἐστιν, ἡ μὲν ἁπλῶς ἁπλῆ, ἡ δὲ τινὸς τίς· ἡ δʼ ἐξ ὑποκειμένου εἰς μὴ ὑποκείμενον φθορά, ἡ μὲν ἁπλῶς ἁπλῆ, ἡ δὲ τινὸς τίς.","kind":"passage"},{"id":"RAW_DAZY_BO_3:P0052","text":"εἰ δὴ τὸ μὴ ὂν λέγεται πλεοναχῶς, καὶ μήτε τὸ κατὰ σύνθεσιν ἢ διαίρεσιν ἐνδέχεται κινεῖσθαι μήτε τὸ κατὰ δύναμιν τὸ τῷ ἁπλῶς ὄντι ἀντικείμενον (τὸ γὰρ μὴ λευκὸν ἢ μὴ ἀγαθὸν ὅμως ἐνδέχεται κινεῖσθαι κατὰ συμβεβηκός, εἴη γὰρ ἂν ἄνθρωπος τὸ μὴ λευκόν· τὸ δʼ ἁπλῶς μὴ τόδε οὐδαμῶς), ἀδύνατον τὸ μὴ ὂν κινεῖσθαι (εἰ δὲ τοῦτο, καὶ τὴν γένεσιν κίνησιν εἶναι· γίγνεται γὰρ τὸ μὴ ὄν· εἰ γὰρ καὶ ὅτι μάλιστα κατὰ συμβεβηκὸς γίγνεται, ἀλλʼ ὅμως ἀληθὲς εἰπεῖν ὅτι ὑπάρχει τὸ μὴ ὂν κατὰ τοῦ γιγνομένου ἁπλῶς)· ὁμοίως δὲ καὶ τὸ ἠρεμεῖν. ταῦτά τε δὴ συμβαίνει δυσχερῆ, καὶ εἰ πᾶν τὸ κινούμενον ἐν τόπῳ, τὸ δὲ μὴ ὂν οὐκ ἔστιν ἐν τόπῳ· εἴη γὰρ ἂν πού. οὐδὲ δὴ ἡ φθορὰ κίνησις· ἐναντίον γὰρ κινήσει κίνησις ἢ ἠρεμία, φθορὰ δὲ γενέσει.","kind":"passage"},{"id":"RAW_DAZY_BO_3:P0053","text":"ἐπεὶ δὲ πᾶσα κίνησις μεταβολή τις, μεταβολαὶ δὲ τρεῖς αἱ εἰρημέναι, τούτων δʼ αἱ κατὰ γένεσιν καὶ φθορὰν οὐ κινήσεις, αὗται δʼ εἰσὶν αἱ κατʼ ἀντίφασιν, ἀνάγκη τὴν ἐξ ὑποκειμένου εἰς ὑποκείμενον κίνησιν εἶναι μόνην. τὰ δʼ ὑποκείμενα ἢ ἐναντία ἢ μεταξύ (καὶ γὰρ ἡ στέρησις κείσθω ἐναντίον), καὶ δηλοῦται καταφάσει, οἷον τὸ γυμνὸν καὶ νωδὸν καὶ μέλαν.","kind":"passage"},{"id":"RAW_DAZY_BO_3:P0054","text":"εἰ οὖν αἱ κατηγορίαι διῄρηνται οὐσίᾳ, ποιότητι, τόπῳ, τῷ ποιεῖν ἢ πάσχειν, τῷ πρός τι, τῷ ποσῷ, ἀνάγκη τρεῖς εἶναι κινήσεις, ποιοῦ ποσοῦ τόπου· κατʼ οὐσίαν δʼ οὔ, διὰ τὸ μηθὲν εἶναι οὐσίᾳ ἐναντίον, οὐδὲ τοῦ πρός τι (ἔστι γὰρ θατέρου μεταβάλλοντος μὴ ἀληθεύεσθαι θάτερον μηδὲν μεταβάλλον, ὥστε κατὰ συμβεβηκὸς ἡ κίνησις αὐτῶν), οὐδὲ ποιοῦντος καὶ πάσχοντος, ἢ κινοῦντος καὶ κινουμένου, ὅτι οὐκ ἔστι κινήσεως κίνησις οὐδὲ γενέσεως γένεσις, οὐδʼ ὅλως μεταβολῆς μεταβολή. διχῶς γὰρ ἐνδέχεται κινήσεως εἶναι κίνησιν, ἢ ὡς ὑποκειμένου (οἷον ὁ ἄνθρωπος κινεῖται ὅτι ἐκ λευκοῦ εἰς μέλαν μεταβάλλει, ὥστε οὕτω καὶ ἡ κίνησις ἢ θερμαίνεται ἢ ψύχεται ἢ τόπον ἀλλάττει ἢ αὔξεται· τοῦτο δὲ ἀδύνατον· οὐ γὰρ τῶν ὑποκειμένων τι ἡ μεταβολή), ἢ τῷ ἕτερόν τι ὑποκείμενον ἐκ μεταβολῆς μεταβάλλειν εἰς ἄλλο εἶδος, οἷον ἄνθρωπον ἐκ νόσου εἰς ὑγίειαν. ἀλλʼ οὐδὲ τοῦτο δυνατὸν πλὴν κατὰ συμβεβηκός.","kind":"passage"},{"id":"RAW_DAZY_BO_3:P0055","text":"πᾶσα γὰρ κίνησις ἐξ ἄλλου εἰς ἄλλο ἐστὶ μεταβολή, καὶ γένεσις καὶ φθορὰ ὡσαύτως· πλὴν αἱ μὲν εἰς ἀντικείμενα ὡδί, ἡ δʼ ὡδί, ἡ κίνησις. ἅμα οὖν μεταβάλλει ἐξ ὑγιείας εἰς νόσον, καὶ ἐξ αὐτῆς ταύτης τῆς μεταβολῆς εἰς ἄλλην. δῆλον δὴ ὅτι ἂν νοσήσῃ, μεταβεβληκὸς ἔσται εἰς ὁποιανοῦν (ἐνδέχεται γὰρ ἠρεμεῖν) καὶ ἔτι εἰς μὴ τὴν τυχοῦσαν ἀεί· κἀκείνη ἔκ τινος εἴς τι ἄλλο ἔσται· ὥσθʼ ἡ ἀντικειμένη ἔσται, ὑγίανσις, ἀλλὰ τῷ συμβεβηκέναι, οἷον ἐξ ἀναμνήσεως εἰς λήθην μεταβάλλει ὅτι ᾧ ὑπάρχει ἐκεῖνο μεταβάλλει, ὁτὲ μὲν εἰς ἐπιστήμην ὁτὲ δὲ εἰς ἄγνοιαν.—ἔτι εἰς ἄπειρον βαδιεῖται, εἰ ἔσται μεταβολῆς μεταβολὴ καὶ γενέσεως γένεσις. ἀνάγκη δὴ καὶ τὴν προτέραν, εἰ ἡ ὑστέρα· οἷον εἰ ἡ ἁπλῆ γένεσις ἐγίγνετό ποτε, καὶ τὸ γιγνόμενον ἐγίγνετο·  ὥστε οὔπω ἦν τὸ γιγνόμενον ἁπλῶς, ἀλλά τι γιγνόμενον  γιγνόμενον ἤδη. καὶ τοῦτʼ ἐγίγνετό ποτε, ὥστʼ οὐκ ἦν πω τότε γιγνόμενον.","kind":"passage"},{"id":"RAW_DAZY_BO_3:P0056","text":"ἐπεὶ δὲ τῶν ἀπείρων οὐκ ἔστι τι πρῶτον, οὐκ ἔσται τὸ πρῶτον, ὥστʼ οὐδὲ τὸ ἐχόμενον. οὔτε γίγνεσθαι οὖν οὔτε κινεῖσθαι οἷόν τε οὔτε μεταβάλλειν οὐδέν. ἔτι τοῦ αὐτοῦ κίνησις ἡ ἐναντία καὶ ἠρέμησις, καὶ γένεσις καὶ φθορά, ὥστε τὸ γιγνόμενον, ὅταν γένηται γιγνόμενον, τότε φθείρεται· οὔτε γὰρ εὐθὺς γιγνόμενον οὔθʼ ὕστερον· εἶναι γὰρ δεῖ τὸ φθειρόμενον. ἔτι δεῖ ὕλην ὑπεῖναι τῷ γιγνομένῳ καὶ μεταβάλλοντι. τίς οὖν ἔσται ὥσπερ τὸ ἀλλοιωτὸν σῶμα ἢ ψυχή—οὕτω τί τὸ γιγνόμενον κίνησις ἢ γένεσις; καὶ ἔτι τί εἰς ὃ κινοῦνται; δεῖ γὰρ εἶναι τὴν τοῦδε ἐκ τοῦδε εἰς τόδε κίνησιν ἢ γένεσιν. πῶς οὖν; οὐ γὰρ ἔσται μάθησις τῆς μαθήσεως, ὥστʼ οὐδὲ γένεσις γενέσεως.","kind":"passage"},{"id":"RAW_DAZY_BO_3:P0057","text":"ἐπεὶ δʼ οὔτʼ οὐσίας οὔτε τοῦ πρός τι οὔτε τοῦ ποιεῖν καὶ πάσχειν, λείπεται κατὰ τὸ ποιὸν καὶ ποσὸν καὶ τόπον κίνησιν εἶναι (τούτων γὰρ ἑκάστῳ ἐναντίωσις ἔστιν), λέγω δὲ τὸ ποιὸν οὐ τὸ ἐν τῇ οὐσίᾳ (καὶ γὰρ ἡ διαφορὰ ποιόν) ἀλλὰ τὸ παθητικόν, καθʼ ὃ λέγεται πάσχειν ἢ ἀπαθὲς εἶναι. τὸ δὲ ἀκίνητον τό τε ὅλως ἀδύνατον κινηθῆναι καὶ τὸ μόλις ἐν χρόνῳ πολλῷ ἢ βραδέως ἀρχόμενον, καὶ τὸ πεφυκὸς μὲν κινεῖσθαι καὶ δυνάμενον μὴ κινούμενον δὲ ὅτε πέφυκε καὶ οὗ καὶ ὥς· ὃ καλῶ ἠρεμεῖν τῶν ἀκινήτων μόνον· ἐναντίον γὰρ ἠρεμία κινήσει, ὥστε στέρησις ἂν εἴη τοῦ δεκτικοῦ. ἅμα κατὰ τόπον ὅσα ἐν ἑνὶ τόπῳ πρώτῳ, καὶ χωρὶς ὅσα ἐν ἄλλῳ· ἅπτεσθαι δὲ ὧν τὰ ἄκρα ἅμα· μεταξὺ δʼ εἰς ὃ πέφυκε πρότερον ἀφικνεῖσθαι τὸ μεταβάλλον ἢ εἰς ὃ ἔσχατον μεταβάλλει κατὰ φύσιν τὸ συνεχῶς μεταβάλλον.","kind":"passage"},{"id":"RAW_DAZY_BO_3:P0058","text":"ἐναντίον κατὰ τόπον τὸ κατʼ εὐθεῖαν ἀπέχον πλεῖστον· ἑξῆς δὲ οὗ μετὰ τὴν ἀρχὴν ὄντος, θέσει ἢ εἴδει ἢ ἄλλως πως ἀφορισθέντος, μηθὲν μεταξύ ἐστι τῶν ἐν ταὐτῷ γένει καὶ οὗ ἐφεξῆς ἐστίν, οἷον γραμμαὶ γραμμῆς ἢ μονάδες μονάδος ἢ οἰκίας οἰκία (ἄλλο δʼ οὐθὲν κωλύει μεταξὺ εἶναι). τὸ γὰρ ἑξῆς τινὸς ἐφεξῆς καὶ ὕστερόν τι· οὐ γὰρ τὸ ἓν ἑξῆς τῶν δύο οὐδʼ ἡ νουμηνία τῆς δευτέρας.  ἐχόμενον δὲ ὃ ἂν ἑξῆς ὂν ἅπτηται. ἐπεὶ δὲ πᾶσα μεταβολὴ ἐν τοῖς ἀντικειμένοις, ταῦτα δὲ τὰ ἐναντία καὶ ἀντίφασις, ἀντιφάσεως δʼ οὐδὲν ἀνὰ μέσον, δῆλον ὡς ἐν τοῖς ἐναντίοις τὸ μεταξύ. τὸ δὲ συνεχὲς ὅπερ ἐχόμενόν τι. λέγω δὲ συνεχὲς ὅταν ταὐτὸ γένηται καὶ ἓν τὸ ἑκατέρου πέρας οἷς ἅπτονται καὶ συνέχονται, ὥστε δῆλον ὅτι τὸ συνεχὲς ἐν τούτοις ἐξ ὧν ἕν τι πέφυκε γίγνεσθαι κατὰ τὴν σύναψιν.","kind":"passage"},{"id":"RAW_DAZY_BO_3:P0059","text":"καὶ ὅτι πρῶτον τὸ ἐφεξῆς, δῆλον (τὸ γὰρ ἐφεξῆς οὐχ ἅπτεται, τοῦτο δʼ ἐφεξῆς· καὶ εἰ συνεχές, ἅπτεται, εἰ δʼ ἅπτεται, οὔπω συνεχές· ἐν οἷς δὲ μὴ ἔστιν ἁφή, οὐκ ἔστι σύμφυσις ἐν τούτοις)· ὥστʼ οὐκ ἔστι στιγμὴ μονάδι ταὐτόν· ταῖς μὲν γὰρ ὑπάρχει τὸ ἅπτεσθαι, ταῖς δʼ οὔ, ἀλλὰ τὸ ἐφεξῆς· καὶ τῶν μὲν μεταξύ τι τῶν δʼ οὔ.","kind":"passage"}],"sha256":"bacac38ec528f9c98b21cf4ea97a9efae727e591a0d61b82367a52ece40fe61a","language_code":"grc","language_name":"Ἑλληνική","translations":[{"id":"SOURCE_TR_20261015T173702807672Z_5a0a44ae","language_code":"en","language_name":"English","title":"Book 11","source_sha256":"bacac38ec528f9c98b21cf4ea97a9efae727e591a0d61b82367a52ece40fe61a","sha256":"34a39527e8292cd412417567c0763adfa1b208565c11b10b71059a0c5833ef36","rendition_type":"machine","review_status":"machine","model":"gpt-5.6-terra","created":"2026-10-15T17:37:02.807695+00:00","paragraphs":[{"id":"RAW_DAZY_BO_3:P0001","text":"That wisdom is a certain science concerning principles is clear from the first discussions in which difficulties were raised in relation to what others have said about principles. But one might raise the difficulty whether wisdom must be supposed to be one science or many. For if it is one, there is always one science of contraries, whereas principles are not contrary; but if it is not one, what sorts of sciences must these be supposed to be? Again, is it the task of one science or of several to study the principles of demonstration? For if of one, why of this one rather than of any other? But if of several, what sorts must these be supposed to be? Again, is it concerned with all substances or not? For if not with all, it is difficult to state with which; but if one science is concerned with all, it is unclear how the same science can be of several things. Again, is it concerned only with substances, or also with attributes? For if there is demonstration concerning attributes, there is not concerning substances; but if there is another science, what is each, and which is wisdom? For insofar as it is demonstrative, the science concerning attributes is wisdom; but insofar as it concerns the first things, that concerning substances is wisdom.","kind":"passage","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_DAZY_BO_3:P0001"]},{"id":"RAW_DAZY_BO_3:P0002","text":"But neither should the science sought be posited as concerned with the causes spoken of in the Physics. For it is not concerned with that for the sake of which (for the good is of this sort, and this belongs among things practicable and among things which are in motion; and this is the first mover—for the end is of this sort—but the first mover is not among unmoved things). In general, moreover, there is a difficulty whether the science now sought is concerned with sensible substances or not, but with certain other substances. For if it is concerned with others, it would be concerned either with the Forms or with mathematical objects.","kind":"passage","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_DAZY_BO_3:P0002"]},{"id":"RAW_DAZY_BO_3:P0003","text":"That the Forms do not exist is clear. Yet there is still a difficulty, even if one posits that they exist, why they are not situated in the same way in the case of the other things of which there are Forms as they are in the case of mathematical objects. I mean that mathematical objects are posited as intermediate between the Forms and sensible things, as certain third things besides both the Forms and the things here below; but there is no third man or horse besides the man-itself and the particular men. And if, again, things are not as they say, with what things is the mathematician to be supposed to deal? For surely not with the things here below; for none of these is such as the mathematical sciences seek.\n\nNor, indeed, is the science now sought concerned with mathematical objects, for none of them is separate; nor yet with sensible substances, for they are perishable. In general, one might raise the difficulty to what science it belongs to inquire into the matter of mathematical objects.","kind":"passage","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_DAZY_BO_3:P0003"]},{"id":"RAW_DAZY_BO_3:P0004","text":"For it belongs neither to natural science, since the whole inquiry of the natural philosopher concerns things having within themselves a principle of motion and rest; nor yet to the science which investigates demonstration and science, for it makes inquiry concerning this genus itself. It remains, then, for the philosophy before us to make the investigation concerning them. But one might raise the difficulty whether the science sought should be posited as concerning the principles called by some elements; and all men posit these as present in composite things. Yet the science sought would seem rather to have to be concerned with universals; for every account and every science is of universals and not of ultimate particulars, so that it would thus be of the primary genera. And these would be being and one; for these would most of all be supposed to encompass all beings, and most of all to resemble principles because they are first by nature. For if they are destroyed, the rest are destroyed with them; for everything is being and one.","kind":"passage","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_DAZY_BO_3:P0004"]},{"id":"RAW_DAZY_BO_3:P0005","text":"But insofar as, if one posits them as genera, it is necessary that their differentiae partake of them, and no differentia partakes of its genus, in this respect they would not seem to have to be posited as genera or as principles. Again, if the simpler is more a principle than what is less simple, and the ultimate things among those derived from a genus are simpler than the genera (for they are indivisible, whereas genera are divided into more and differing species), the species would seem to be principles more than the genera. But insofar as the species are destroyed together with the genera, the genera resemble principles more; for a principle is that whose removal removes with it.\n\nSuch, then, and other such things are those which involve difficulty.","kind":"passage","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_DAZY_BO_3:P0005"]},{"id":"RAW_DAZY_BO_3:P0006","text":"Again, must one posit something besides particulars or not, but that the science sought is concerned with these? But these are infinite. Yet the things besides particulars are genera or species, whereas the science now sought is concerned with neither of these. Why this is impossible has been stated. And in general there is a difficulty whether one ought to suppose there to be some substance separate from sensible substances and those here below, or not, but that these are the beings and that wisdom is concerned with these. For we seem to be seeking some other substance, and this is what lies before us: I mean, to see whether there is something separate in itself and belonging to none of the sensible things. Again, if there is some other substance besides sensible substances, alongside which sensible things should it be posited? For why should one posit it alongside men rather than horses or the other animals, or indeed alongside inanimate things generally? Yet to construct other eternal substances equal in number to sensible and perishable substances would seem to fall outside what is reasonable.","kind":"passage","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_DAZY_BO_3:P0006"]},{"id":"RAW_DAZY_BO_3:P0007","text":"But if the principle now sought is not separate from bodies, what other thing could one posit rather than matter? Yet this exists not in actuality but in potentiality. Form and shape would seem to be a more authoritative principle than this; but this is perishable, so that in general there is no eternal substance separate and in itself.","kind":"passage","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_DAZY_BO_3:P0007"]},{"id":"RAW_DAZY_BO_3:P0008","text":"But this is absurd. For such a principle and substance seems to exist, and is sought by almost the most discerning thinkers. For how will there be order unless there is something eternal, separate, and abiding? Again, if there is some substance and principle of such a nature as we are now seeking, and it is one and the same for all things, both eternal and perishable, there is a difficulty why, though the principle is the same, some things subject to the principle are eternal while others are not eternal—for this is absurd. But if one principle is of perishable things and another of eternal things, then, if that of perishable things too is eternal, we shall be similarly at a loss (for why, if the principle is eternal, are the things subject to it not also eternal?). But if it is perishable, another principle comes to be of it, and another of that one, and this proceeds to infinity.","kind":"passage","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_DAZY_BO_3:P0008"]},{"id":"RAW_DAZY_BO_3:P0009","text":"But if, again, someone posits as the principles which seem most of all to be unmoved being and one, first, if neither of these signifies a this-something and substance, how will they be separate and in themselves? Yet it is such eternal and primary principles that we seek. But if each of them signifies a this-something and substance, all beings are substances; for being is predicated of all things (and one of some things as well); but it is false that all beings are substances. Again, for those who say that the first principle is the one and that this is substance, and who generate number first from the one and matter and assert that it is substance, how can what is said be true? For how ought one to understand the dyad and each of the remaining composite numbers as one? For concerning this they say nothing, nor is it easy to say.","kind":"passage","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_DAZY_BO_3:P0009"]},{"id":"RAW_DAZY_BO_3:P0010","text":"But if someone posits lines, or the things consequent upon these—I mean the primary surfaces—as principles, these at any rate are not separate substances, but sections and divisions, some of surfaces and others of bodies (while points are divisions of lines); and further they are limits of these same things. But all these exist in other things, and none is separate. Again, how ought one to suppose that there is a substance of the one and of a point? For every substance has generation, but a point has none; for a point is a division.","kind":"passage","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_DAZY_BO_3:P0010"]},{"id":"RAW_DAZY_BO_3:P0011","text":"And it presents a difficulty also that every science is of universals and of what is of this kind, while substance is not among universals, but rather is a this-something and separate. Hence, if there is science concerning principles, how should one suppose the principle to be substance? Again, is there anything besides the composite whole or not—I mean, besides matter and that which is with it? For if not, all things in matter are perishable; but if there is something, it would be form and shape. It is difficult, then, to determine in what cases this exists and in what cases it does not; for in some cases it is clear that the form is not separate, as in the case of a house. Again, are the principles the same in species or in number? For if they are one in number, all things will be the same.","kind":"passage","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_DAZY_BO_3:P0011"]},{"id":"RAW_DAZY_BO_3:P0012","text":"Since the philosopher's science is universally of being qua being, and not of a part, while being is spoken of in many ways and not in one way: if, then, it is spoken of homonymously and with nothing in common, it does not fall under one science (for things of that kind do not constitute one genus); but if it is spoken of in accordance with something common, it would fall under one science. It appears, then, to be spoken of in the way stated, just as medical and healthy are. For we speak of each of these in many ways.\n\nEach is spoken of in this way because one thing is somehow referred to medical science, another to health, and another otherwise, while each is referred to the same thing. For an account and a knife are called medical, the one because it proceeds from medical science, the other because it is useful to it. Likewise also with healthy: one thing because it is indicative of health, another because it is productive of it. And the same way applies to the other cases.","kind":"passage","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_DAZY_BO_3:P0012"]},{"id":"RAW_DAZY_BO_3:P0013","text":"In the same way, then, all being is spoken of. For each of these things is said to be because it is an affection, state, disposition, motion, or some other such thing of being qua being. And since every being is referred to some one common thing, each of the oppositions too will be referred to the primary differences and oppositions of being—whether plurality and one, or likeness and unlikeness, are the primary differences of being, or whether they are some others; for let these have been considered. And it makes no difference whether the reference of being is to being or to one.","kind":"passage","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_DAZY_BO_3:P0013"]},{"id":"RAW_DAZY_BO_3:P0014","text":"For even if they are not the same but different, they are at least reciprocal: for the one is in some way being, and being is one.—And since it belongs to the same one science to study all contraries, and each of them is spoken of according to privation—though one might perhaps raise a difficulty as to how some things having an intermediate are spoken of according to privation, as unjust and just are—the privation in all such cases must be posited not of the whole account but of the ultimate species. For example, if the just man is one who by some state is obedient to the laws, the unjust man will not necessarily be deprived of the whole account, but will be deficient in some way with respect to obedience to the laws, and in this respect privation will belong to him; and the same way applies also in the other cases.—And just as the mathematician makes his inquiry concerning things by abstraction (for, having removed all sensible things, he studies, for example, weight and lightness and hardness and their contrary, and further heat and cold and the other sensible oppositions, leaving only the","kind":"passage","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_DAZY_BO_3:P0014"]},{"id":"RAW_DAZY_BO_3:P0015","text":"quantity and continuity, of some in one dimension, of others in two, and of others in three, and the affections of these insofar as they are quantities and continuous, and does not study them in respect of anything else; and in some cases he considers their positions relative to one another and what belongs to these, in others their commensurabilities and incommensurabilities, and in others their ratios, but nevertheless we posit one and the same science of all these, namely geometry), so too is it with being. For it belongs to no science other than philosophy to study the attributes which belong to it insofar as it is being, and its oppositions insofar as it is being. For one might assign inquiry to natural science not insofar as things are beings, but rather insofar as they partake of motion; while dialectic and sophistic are concerned with the attributes of beings, but not insofar as they are beings, nor with being itself insofar as it is being. It remains, therefore, for the philosopher, insofar as things are beings, to be the student of the matters mentioned.","kind":"passage","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_DAZY_BO_3:P0015"]},{"id":"RAW_DAZY_BO_3:P0016","text":"And since all being is spoken of in many ways in reference to some one common thing, and contraries likewise in the same way (for they are referred to the primary oppositions and differences of being), and since things of this kind can fall under one science, the difficulty stated at the beginning would be resolved—I mean that in which the question was raised how there will be one science of many things differing in genus.","kind":"passage","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_DAZY_BO_3:P0016"]},{"id":"RAW_DAZY_BO_3:P0017","text":"And since the mathematician too uses the common principles in a particular way, it would belong to first philosophy to study their principles. For that, when equals are subtracted from equals, the remainders are equal, is common to all quantities; but mathematics, having marked off some part of its own matter, conducts its inquiry concerning lines or angles or numbers or some other of the quantities, not insofar as they are beings but insofar as each of them is continuous in one, two, or three dimensions. Philosophy, however, does not consider what has happened to each of such things in part, but studies each of them in respect of being, insofar as it is being. And natural science stands in the same relation to mathematics; for natural science studies the attributes and principles of beings insofar as they are moved, and not insofar as they are beings (whereas we have said that the first science is concerned with them insofar as its subjects are beings, and not insofar as they are something else). Hence both this science and mathematics must be posited as parts of wisdom.","kind":"passage","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_DAZY_BO_3:P0017"]},{"id":"RAW_DAZY_BO_3:P0018","text":"There is among beings a principle concerning which it is not possible to be deceived, but concerning which it is necessary always to do the contrary—I mean, to speak truth: namely, that it is not possible for the same thing both to be and not to be in one and the same respect and at the same time, and likewise the other things which are opposed to one another in this way. Concerning such things there is no demonstration without qualification, but there is one against this man. For it is impossible to make a syllogism concerning this very thing from a more trustworthy principle, though this would be necessary if there were to be demonstration without qualification. But against one who says that opposed assertions are true, the person demonstrating that this is false must take something which will be the same as the proposition that it is not possible for the same thing both to be and not to be in one and the same respect and at the same time, but will not seem to be the same. For only thus could there be demonstration against one who asserts that opposed assertions may be true of the same thing.","kind":"passage","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_DAZY_BO_3:P0018"]},{"id":"RAW_DAZY_BO_3:P0019","text":"Those who are going to communicate with one another by discourse must understand something of one another. For if this does not occur, how will there be communication of discourse between them? Each of the names, then, must be known and signify something, not many things but only one; and if it signifies more than one, it must make clear to which of these the name is applied. The man, then, who says that this is and is not, does not say what he says; hence he says that what the name signifies does not signify this, which is impossible. Therefore, if being this signifies something, it is impossible for the contradiction to be true. Again, if the name signifies something and this is true, this must be necessarily so; but what necessarily is cannot ever not be. Therefore it is impossible for opposed assertions and negations to be true of the same thing.","kind":"passage","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_DAZY_BO_3:P0019"]},{"id":"RAW_DAZY_BO_3:P0020","text":"Again, if the assertion is no more true than the negation, one who says “man” or “not-man” will not speak truth any more in the one case than in the other. Yet he would seem, in saying that a man is not a horse, to speak truth either more or no less than in saying that he is not a man; and thus, in saying that the same man is a horse, he will also speak truth (for it was assumed that the opposed assertions are equally true). It follows, therefore, that the same thing is a man and a horse, or some other animal.—There is, then, no demonstration of these things without qualification, but there is demonstration against one who posits them. And someone questioning Heraclitus himself in this way would quickly compel him to agree that opposed assertions can never be true of the same things. But now, not understanding what he himself says, he adopted this opinion.","kind":"passage","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_DAZY_BO_3:P0020"]},{"id":"RAW_DAZY_BO_3:P0021","text":"And in general, if what is said by him is true, not even this itself would be true—I mean, that it is possible for the same thing at one and the same time both to be and not to be. For just as, when they are separated, the affirmation is no more true than the negation, so too, in the case of the combined and compounded statement, as though it were some single affirmation, the whole, being posited as an affirmation, will be no more true than the negation. Further, if it is possible to affirm nothing truly, then this very assertion too, namely that no true affirmation exists, would be false. But if there is something, the assertion made by those who raise such objections and entirely abolish discussion would be dissolved.","kind":"passage","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_DAZY_BO_3:P0021"]},{"id":"RAW_DAZY_BO_3:P0022","text":"Related to what has been said is also the statement made by Protagoras. For he too said that man is the measure of all things, meaning nothing other than that what seems to each person is also firmly so. But if this is so, it follows that the same thing both is and is not, and is both bad and good, and likewise with the other things said according to opposed assertions, because often this seems beautiful to some and the contrary to others, and what appears to each is the measure. This difficulty might be dissolved by considering whence the starting-point of this opinion has come. For it seems to some to have arisen from the doctrine of the natural philosophers, and to others from the fact that not everyone judges the same things about the same objects, but this seems pleasant to some and the contrary to others.","kind":"passage","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_DAZY_BO_3:P0022"]},{"id":"RAW_DAZY_BO_3:P0023","text":"For that nothing comes to be from what is not, but everything from what is, is a doctrine common to nearly all those concerned with nature. Since, then, what is white does not come to be from what is completely white and in no way not-white, the white thing that comes to be would come to be from what is not white; and thus, according to them, it would come to be from what is not, unless the same thing were both white and not white. But it is not difficult to resolve this difficulty. For it has been stated in the physical writings how things that come to be come to be from what is not and how from what is.\n\nYet to give equal heed to the opinions and appearances of those who dispute with one another is foolish. For it is clear that one of the two must be in error. This is evident from things that occur in perception. For the same thing never appears sweet to some and the contrary to others, unless the sense-organ and criterion of the tastes in question in one of them have been corrupted and impaired.","kind":"passage","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_DAZY_BO_3:P0023"]},{"id":"RAW_DAZY_BO_3:P0024","text":"This being so, some persons are to be supposed to be the measure, and others are not to be supposed so. And I say the same concerning good and bad, beautiful and ugly, and the other such things. For to maintain this is no different from maintaining that, because things appear two to those who put a finger beneath their sight and make one appear two, there must be two because so many appear, and again one; for to those who do not move their sight, the one appears one.\n\nIn general, it is absurd to make judgment concerning truth on the basis of things here below, which appear to change and never remain in the same condition. For one must seek the truth from things that are always in the same condition and undergo no change; and such are the things according to the cosmos. For these do not appear at one time one way and at another differently, but always the same and sharing in no change whatever.","kind":"passage","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_DAZY_BO_3:P0024"]},{"id":"RAW_DAZY_BO_3:P0025","text":"Further, if there is motion, and something that is moved, and everything moved is moved from something and toward something, then what is moved must be in that from which it will be moved and not be in it, and must move toward this and come to be in it; but, according to them, what is according to contradiction cannot be true together. And if things here below continuously flow and move in respect of quantity, and someone were to grant this, although it is not true, why will they not remain in respect of quality? For they seem especially to predicate the contradictory assertions of the same thing because they suppose that quantity does not remain in bodies, and hence that the same thing is both four cubits long and not four cubits long. But substance is according to quality, and this is of a determinate nature, whereas quantity is of an indeterminate nature.","kind":"passage","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_DAZY_BO_3:P0025"]},{"id":"RAW_DAZY_BO_3:P0026","text":"Further, why, when the doctor orders that this food be brought, do they bring it? For why is this bread rather than not bread? Thus it would make no difference to eat or not to eat. But as though they spoke truly about it, and as though this prescribed food existed, they bring it. And yet they ought not to do so, if no nature in perceptible things remains firmly fixed, but all are always in motion and flux.","kind":"passage","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_DAZY_BO_3:P0026"]},{"id":"RAW_DAZY_BO_3:P0027","text":"Further, if we are always being altered and never remain the same, what is surprising if the same things never appear the same to us, as to the sick? (For to these too, because their condition is not disposed in the same way as when they were healthy, the things of perception do not appear alike—not because the perceptible objects themselves share through this in any change, but because they produce different perceptions in the sick and not the same ones. And perhaps it is necessary that things be similarly disposed when the alteration mentioned takes place.) But if we do not change, but continue being the same, there would be something remaining.—Now, with those who hold the aforesaid difficulties on rational grounds, it is not easy to resolve them if they posit nothing and demand no further account of this; for in this way every argument and every demonstration proceeds. But by positing nothing, they abolish discussion and discourse altogether; hence there is no argument against such persons, but it is easy to answer those who are perplexed by the difficulties handed down and to resolve","kind":"passage","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_DAZY_BO_3:P0027"]},{"id":"RAW_DAZY_BO_3:P0028","text":"the things that produce the difficulty in them. This is clear from what has been said. Thus it is evident from these considerations that opposed assertions concerning the same thing cannot be true at the same time, nor can contraries, because every contrariety is spoken of according to privation; and this is clear when the accounts of contraries are analyzed back to their principle. Likewise, none of the intermediates can be predicated of one and the same thing. For if the underlying subject is white, then in saying that it is neither black nor white we shall speak falsely; for it follows that it both is white and is not white. For one member of the compound will be true of it, and this is a contradiction of “white.”","kind":"passage","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_DAZY_BO_3:P0028"]},{"id":"RAW_DAZY_BO_3:P0029","text":"Therefore it is not possible to speak truly either in accordance with Heraclitus or in accordance with Anaxagoras. Otherwise, it will follow that contraries are predicated of the same thing. For when he says that in everything there is a portion of everything, he says that a thing is no more sweet than bitter, or than any of the other contraries, if indeed everything belongs to everything not merely potentially but actually ι ι and separately. Likewise, it is not possible for all assertions to be either false or true, both because of many other difficulties that would follow from this thesis, and because, if all are false, not even one who asserts this very thing will speak truly; while, if all are true, one who says that they are all false will not speak falsely.","kind":"passage","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_DAZY_BO_3:P0029"]},{"id":"RAW_DAZY_BO_3:P0030","text":"Every science seeks certain principles and causes concerning each of the objects knowable falling under it—for example, medicine and gymnastics, and each of the other productive and mathematical sciences. For each of these, having marked off for itself some genus, concerns itself with this as existing and being, though not qua being; rather, there is another science, distinct from these sciences, which studies it qua being. And each of the sciences mentioned, having somehow grasped the what-it-is in each genus, attempts to demonstrate the rest, more loosely or more exactly. They grasp the what-it-is, some through perception and others by hypothesis; hence it is also clear from such induction that there is no demonstration of substance and of the what-it-is. And since there is a science concerning nature, it is clear that it will also be distinct from practical and productive science.","kind":"passage","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_DAZY_BO_3:P0030"]},{"id":"RAW_DAZY_BO_3:P0031","text":"For in productive science the principle of motion is in the producer and not in what is produced, and this is either some art or some other capacity. Likewise, in practical science too, the motion is not in what is done but rather in those who act. But the science of the natural philosopher concerns things that have in themselves a principle of motion. That natural science must therefore be neither practical nor productive but theoretical is clear from these considerations (for it must fall into one of these classes). But since it is necessary for each of the sciences somehow to know the what-it-is and to use it as a principle, the natural philosopher must not be unaware how he should define and how the account of substance should be taken: whether as the snub or rather as the concave. For the account of the snub is stated together with the matter of the thing, whereas that of the concave is stated apart from matter. For snubness occurs in a nose; hence its account too is considered together with this. For the snub is a concave nose.","kind":"passage","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_DAZY_BO_3:P0031"]},{"id":"RAW_DAZY_BO_3:P0032","text":"It is clear, then, that the account of flesh, eye, and the other parts too must always be given together with their matter. But since there is a science of being qua being and separate, one must consider whether this is to be posited as the same as natural science ι ι or rather as different. Natural science concerns things that have in themselves a principle of motion, while mathematics too is theoretical and concerns things that remain, but not things that are separate. Therefore there is a science distinct from both of these concerning separate and unmoved being, if indeed there is some such substance—I mean separate and unmoved—which we shall attempt to show. And if there is some such nature among beings, there too, perhaps, would be the divine, and this would be the first and most authoritative principle. It is clear, then, that there are three kinds of theoretical sciences: natural science, mathematics, theology.","kind":"passage","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_DAZY_BO_3:P0032"]},{"id":"RAW_DAZY_BO_3:P0033","text":"The class of theoretical sciences, then, is the best, and among these the one last mentioned is best; for it concerns the most honorable of beings, and each science is called better or worse according to its proper object of knowledge. One might raise the question whether the science of being qua being should be posited as universal or not. For each of the mathematical sciences concerns one determinate genus, whereas the universal science is common to all. If, then, natural substances are first among beings, natural science too would be first among the sciences. But if there is another nature and a substance that is separate and unmoved, the science of it too must be different, prior to natural science, and universal by being prior.","kind":"passage","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_DAZY_BO_3:P0033"]},{"id":"RAW_DAZY_BO_3:P0034","text":"Since being without qualification is spoken of in several ways, one of which is what is said to be according to accident, we must first consider being in this sense. That none of the established sciences concerns the accidental is clear. For neither house-building considers what will happen to those who use the house—for example, whether they will dwell in it sorrowfully or contrariwise—nor weaving nor shoemaking nor cookery; but each of these sciences considers only what belongs to it in itself, and this is its proper end. Nor does any of the sciences admittedly existing inquire whether one who is musical, having become grammatical, will simultaneously be both, though previously he was not; but what is not always, came to be this, so that he became musical and grammatical at the same time. No science admittedly existing investigates this, except sophistry; for this alone concerns the accidental. Hence Plato did not speak badly when he said that the sophist spends his time on what is not. But that it is not even possible for there to be a science of the accidental","kind":"passage","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_DAZY_BO_3:P0034"]},{"id":"RAW_DAZY_BO_3:P0035","text":"will be clear when we attempt to see what the accidental is. We say that everything is either always and of necessity (necessity not in the sense of compulsion, but in the sense in which we use it in demonstrations), or for the most part, or neither for the most part nor always and of necessity, but as it happens; for example, cold might occur during the dog-days, but this occurs neither always and of necessity nor for the most part, though it might happen at some time. The accidental, then, is what comes to be, but neither always, nor of necessity, nor for the most part. What the accidental is has thus been stated; and why there is no science of such a thing is clear. For every science is of what is always or for the most part, whereas the accidental is in neither of these classes. And that the causes and principles of accidental being are not such as those of being in itself is clear; for otherwise everything would be of necessity.","kind":"passage","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_DAZY_BO_3:P0035"]},{"id":"RAW_DAZY_BO_3:P0036","text":"For if this is when this is, and this when this is, and this not as it happens but of necessity, then that of which this was the cause will also be of necessity, down to what is called the last effect (and this was according to accident); thus everything will be of necessity, and chance, possibility, and coming to be or not coming to be will be utterly removed from things that come to be. And if the cause is posited not as existing but as coming to be, the same consequences will follow; for everything will come to be of necessity. For tomorrow’s eclipse will occur if this occurs, and this if something else occurs, and this if another thing occurs. And in this way, by subtracting time from a finite period extending from the present to tomorrow, one will at some time arrive at what is present; hence, since this is so, everything subsequent will occur of necessity, so that everything comes to be of necessity.","kind":"passage","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_DAZY_BO_3:P0036"]},{"id":"RAW_DAZY_BO_3:P0037","text":"But being as true and being according to accident—the former is in a combination of thought and is an affection in it (hence principles are not sought concerning being in this sense, but concerning being external and separate); the latter is not necessary but indeterminate, I mean the accidental. The causes of such a thing are unordered and infinite.—That for the sake of which is among things coming to be by nature or from thought; and chance is when one of these comes to be according to accident. For just as being is either in itself or according to accident, so too is cause. Chance is an accidental cause in things that come to be for the sake of something and according to choice; hence chance and thought concern the same things, for choice is not without thought. But the causes from which chance-events may occur are indeterminate; hence chance is unclear to human calculation and is an accidental cause, though simply speaking it is the cause of nothing. Chance is good or bad when the outcome is good or bad; good fortune and misfortune concern the magnitude of these.","kind":"passage","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_DAZY_BO_3:P0037"]},{"id":"RAW_DAZY_BO_3:P0038","text":"Since nothing according to accident is prior to what is in itself, neither, then, are accidental causes prior. Therefore, if chance or spontaneity is a cause of the heaven, intellect and nature are prior causes.","kind":"passage","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_DAZY_BO_3:P0038"]},{"id":"RAW_DAZY_BO_3:P0039","text":"There is what is only actually, what is potentially, and what is potentially and actually: in one case being, in another quantity, in another one of the remaining categories. There is no motion apart from things; for things always change according to the categories of being, and there is nothing common to these which is not also in one category. But each belongs to all things in two ways: for example, the this—one is its form and the other privation—and in respect of quality one is white and the other black, and in respect of quantity one is complete and the other incomplete, and in respect of locomotion one is upward and the other downward, or light and heavy. Thus there are as many kinds of motion and change as there are kinds of being. And since in each genus one thing is potentially and another in actuality, I call motion the actuality of what is potential, qua such. That we speak truly is clear from this: when what is buildable, qua we say it to be such, is actually so, it is being built, and this is building; likewise learning, healing, walking, leaping, aging, growing.","kind":"passage","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_DAZY_BO_3:P0039"]},{"id":"RAW_DAZY_BO_3:P0040","text":"It happens that a thing is moved when the actuality is itself [present], and neither before nor after. The actuality of what exists potentially, when, being actual, it is active—not insofar as it is itself, but insofar as it is movable—is motion. By “insofar as” I mean in the following way. Bronze is potentially a statue; nevertheless, the actuality of bronze, insofar as it is bronze, is not motion. For it is not the same thing to be bronze and to be something potentially; since, if it were simply the same in account, the actuality of bronze would be a certain motion. But they are not the same (this is clear in the case of contraries: for the capacity for being healthy and the capacity for being ill are not the same—for then being healthy and being ill would be the same—whereas the underlying thing, both healthy and sick, whether it be moisture or blood, is the same and one). Since, then, it is not the same thing, just as colour and visible are not the same, the actuality of what is potential, and potential insofar as it is potential, is motion.","kind":"passage","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_DAZY_BO_3:P0040"]},{"id":"RAW_DAZY_BO_3:P0041","text":"That this, then, is so, and that it happens that a thing is moved when the actuality is itself [present], and neither before nor after, is clear (for each thing may sometimes be active and sometimes not, as what is buildable insofar as it is buildable; and the activity of what is buildable insofar as it is buildable is building. For either building is this activity, or it is a house; but when there is a house, it is no longer buildable, whereas what is buildable is being built. Therefore the activity must be building; and building is a certain motion; and the same account applies also in the other motions). That it has been well stated is clear both from what others say concerning it and from the fact that it is not easy to define it otherwise. For one could not place it in another genus; this is clear from what they say. For some place otherness and inequality and non-being [there], though none of these need be moved; nor is change either into or out of these any more than into or out of their opposites.","kind":"passage","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_DAZY_BO_3:P0041"]},{"id":"RAW_DAZY_BO_3:P0042","text":"The reason for placing it among these is that motion seems to be something indeterminate, and the principles of the other column are indeterminate because they are privative; for none of them is either a this or such, nor any of the remaining predicates. The reason why motion seems to be indeterminate is that it cannot be placed either among the potentialities of beings or among their actualities: for neither what is potentially a quantity is necessarily moved, nor what is actually a quantity; and motion seems to be a certain actuality, but an incomplete one. The cause is that the potentiality whose actuality it is is incomplete. And for this reason it is difficult to grasp what it is; for it is necessary either to place it under privation, or under potentiality, or under simple actuality, yet none of these seems possible. Thus there remains what has been said: that it is an actuality, and the actuality described—one difficult to see, yet capable of being. And that motion is in the movable thing is clear; for it is the actuality of this thing caused by what moves.","kind":"passage","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_DAZY_BO_3:P0042"]},{"id":"RAW_DAZY_BO_3:P0043","text":"And the actuality of what moves is not different. For there must be an actuality of both; for it is capable of moving by its potentiality, but moving by its activity; yet it is active upon the movable thing, so that the actuality of both is alike one, just as the same interval is one in relation to two and two in relation to one, and [as] uphill and downhill; though their being is not one. Likewise also in the case of what moves and what is moved.","kind":"passage","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_DAZY_BO_3:P0043"]},{"id":"RAW_DAZY_BO_3:P0044","text":"The infinite is either what cannot be traversed because it is not naturally fitted to be traversed, as a voice is invisible; or what has an endless passage; or what is scarcely [traversable]; or what is naturally fitted to have a passage or limit but does not have one; and further, [what is infinite] by addition or subtraction or both. Now it cannot be some separate thing itself. For if it is neither magnitude nor multitude, but the infinite itself is substance and not an attribute, it will be indivisible (for what is divisible is either magnitude or multitude); but if indivisible, it is not infinite, unless it is infinite as a voice is invisible. Yet neither do they speak in this way nor do we inquire into it in this way, but [into it] as having no passage. Further, how can the infinite exist in itself, if number and magnitude do not also exist in themselves, of which the infinite is an attribute? Further, if it exists accidentally, it could not be an element of beings insofar as it is infinite, just as the invisible is not an element of speech, although voice is invisible. And it is clear that the infinite cannot exist actually.","kind":"passage","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_DAZY_BO_3:P0044"]},{"id":"RAW_DAZY_BO_3:P0045","text":"For any part taken of it will be infinite (for being infinite and the infinite are the same, if the infinite is substance and not predicated of a subject), so that it is either indivisible, or divisible into infinites, if it is divisible. But it is impossible for the same thing to be many infinites (for as air is a part of air, so the infinite is a part of the infinite, if it is substance and principle). Therefore it is partless and indivisible. But it is impossible for what exists actually to be infinite (for it must be a quantity); therefore it belongs accidentally. But if so, it has been said that it cannot be a principle, but rather that to which it belongs—the air or the even.—This inquiry, then, is universal; but that it is not among sensible things is clear from the following. For if the account of body is that which is bounded by planes, there could be no infinite body, either sensible or intelligible; nor an infinite number as separate, for number, or what has number, is countable. And this is clear also naturally from the following: for it cannot be either composite or simple.","kind":"passage","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_DAZY_BO_3:P0045"]},{"id":"RAW_DAZY_BO_3:P0046","text":"For there will not be a composite body, if the elements are limited in multitude (for the contraries must be equal, and none of them must be infinite; for if the power of either body falls short in any degree, the finite will be destroyed by the infinite; but it is impossible for each to be infinite, since body is what has extension in every direction, and the infinite is what is extended without limit, so that, if the infinite is body, it will be infinite in every direction). Nor can an infinite body be one and simple, either as some say, something besides the elements from which they generate these (for there is no such body besides the elements; for everything is dissolved into that from which it is, but this does not appear besides the simple bodies), nor fire nor any other of the elements. For, apart from one of them being infinite, it is impossible for the whole, even if finite, either to be or to become one of them, as Heraclitus says that all things at some time become fire.","kind":"passage","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_DAZY_BO_3:P0046"]},{"id":"RAW_DAZY_BO_3:P0047","text":"The same account applies also to the one which the natural philosophers posit besides the elements; for everything changes from a contrary, for example from hot to cold.—Further, sensible body is somewhere, and the place of the whole and of a part is the same, as in the case of earth. Hence, if it is homogeneous, it will either be immovable or be borne eternally; but this is impossible. For why [should it move] downward rather than upward or anywhere whatever? For example, if it were a clod, where would it move or remain? For the place of the body akin to it is infinite. Will it then occupy the whole place? And how? What, then, will its rest and motion be? Either it will remain everywhere—then it will not move—or it will move everywhere—then it will not come to rest. But if the whole is unlike in kind, the places too are unlike in kind; and first, the body of the whole is not one except by contact; then these [bodies] will be either limited or infinite in kind.","kind":"passage","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_DAZY_BO_3:P0047"]},{"id":"RAW_DAZY_BO_3:P0048","text":"Now they cannot be limited; for some will be infinite and others not, if the whole is infinite—for example, fire or water; and such a thing is destruction for the contraries. But if they are infinite and simple, the places also are infinite and the elements will be infinite. But if this is impossible and the places are limited, the whole too must be limited. In general, it is impossible for body to be infinite and for there to be a place for bodies, if every sensible body has either heaviness or lightness; for it will be borne either toward the centre or upward, but it is impossible for the infinite, either the whole or the half, to undergo either of these. For how will you divide it? Or how will one part of the infinite be below and the other above, or [how will there be] an extremity and a centre? Further, every sensible body is in a place, and there are six species of place; but these cannot exist in an infinite body. In general, if an infinite place is impossible, an infinite body is impossible too; for what is in a place is somewhere, and this means either above or below or one of the remaining [directions], each of which is a certain limit.","kind":"passage","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_DAZY_BO_3:P0048"]},{"id":"RAW_DAZY_BO_3:P0049","text":"The infinite is not the same in magnitude, motion, and time, as though it were one nature; rather, the later is spoken of in accordance with the prior—for example, motion in accordance with the magnitude over which a thing is moved or altered or increased, and time because of motion.","kind":"passage","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_DAZY_BO_3:P0049"]},{"id":"RAW_DAZY_BO_3:P0050","text":"What changes changes in one way accidentally, as the musical man walks; in another way it is said without qualification to change because something belonging to it changes, as in the case of things changing in respect of parts (for the body becomes healthy because the eye does); but there is something which is first moved in itself, and this is what is movable in itself. The same distinctions apply also in the case of what moves: for one thing moves accidentally, another in respect of a part, and another in itself; and there is something which moves first. There is also something moved, further, the time in which [it is moved], and that from which and that into which [it is moved]. But the forms, affections, and place, into which the things moved are moved, are unmoved—for example, knowledge and heat; yet it is not heat that is motion, but heating. Change not accidental does not belong to all things, but to contraries and intermediates and contradiction; induction provides evidence of this.","kind":"passage","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_DAZY_BO_3:P0050"]},{"id":"RAW_DAZY_BO_3:P0051","text":"What changes changes either from a subject into a subject, or from a non-subject into a non-subject, or from a subject into a non-subject, or from a non-subject into a subject (by “subject” I mean what is indicated by affirmation). Thus there must be three changes; for change from a non-subject into a non-subject is not change, since there are neither contraries nor contradiction, because there is no opposition. Change from a non-subject into a subject, according to contradiction, is generation—generation without qualification in one case, and generation of something in another; whereas [change] from a subject into a non-subject is destruction—destruction without qualification in one case, and destruction of something in another.","kind":"passage","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_DAZY_BO_3:P0051"]},{"id":"RAW_DAZY_BO_3:P0052","text":"Now since non-being is spoken of in several ways, and neither what is in respect of combination or division can be moved, nor what is potential and opposed to what is without qualification (for what is not white or not good can nevertheless be moved accidentally, since what is not white may be a man; but what is not this without qualification [can be moved] in no way), it is impossible for non-being to be moved. But if this is so, it is also impossible for generation to be motion; for what is not being generated is generated. For even if it is generated to the highest degree accidentally, it is nevertheless true to say that non-being belongs to what is generated without qualification. Likewise also with being at rest. These difficulties consequently arise, as also [does the difficulty] that, if everything moved is in a place, non-being is not in a place; for it would be somewhere. Nor, then, is destruction motion; for the contrary of motion is motion or rest, whereas destruction is contrary to generation.","kind":"passage","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_DAZY_BO_3:P0052"]},{"id":"RAW_DAZY_BO_3:P0053","text":"Since every motion is a certain change, and the changes mentioned are three, while those according to generation and destruction are not motions, and these are those according to contradiction, it is necessary that only change from a subject into a subject be motion. The subjects are either contraries or intermediates (for privation too is to be counted as a contrary), and they are indicated by affirmation, for example naked and toothless and black.","kind":"passage","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_DAZY_BO_3:P0053"]},{"id":"RAW_DAZY_BO_3:P0054","text":"If, then, the categories are distinguished into substance, quality, place, acting or being acted upon, relation, and quantity, there must be three motions: in respect of quality, quantity, and place. There is none in respect of substance, because there is nothing contrary to substance; nor in respect of relation (for when one of two relatives changes, it may be that the other is no longer truly predicated although it has changed in no respect, so that their motion is accidental); nor in respect of acting and being acted upon, or moving and being moved, because there is no motion of motion, nor generation of generation, nor, in general, change of change. For there can be motion of motion in two ways: either as of a subject (for example, a man is moved because he changes from white to black; thus on this account motion too would be heated or cooled or change place or increase; but this is impossible, for change is not one of the subjects); or because some other subject changes from a change into another form, as a man from sickness into health. But not even this is possible except accidentally.","kind":"passage","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_DAZY_BO_3:P0054"]},{"id":"RAW_DAZY_BO_3:P0055","text":"For every motion is a change from one thing into another, and generation and destruction likewise; except that the former are into opposites in this way, whereas motion is in that way. Therefore at the same time it changes from health into sickness, and from this very change into another. It is clear, then, that whenever it becomes sick, it will have changed into any whatever [state] (for it may come to rest), and further, not always into a chance [state]; and that [change] will be from something into something else. Thus the contrary [change] will be restoration to health, but accidentally, as [a man] changes from recollection into forgetfulness because that in which the former belongs changes, at one time into knowledge and at another into ignorance.—Further, there will be progression to infinity if there is change of change and generation of generation. For if the later is, the prior must be also. For example, if simple generation was at some time being generated, then what is generated was also being generated; thus what is simply generated was not yet, but something which is being generated was already being generated. And this too was at some time being generated, so that at that time it was not yet being generated.","kind":"passage","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_DAZY_BO_3:P0055"]},{"id":"RAW_DAZY_BO_3:P0056","text":"But since among infinites there is no first, the first will not exist, and hence neither will what follows it. Thus nothing can either come to be, or be moved, or change. Further, contrary motion and rest belong to the same thing, as do generation and destruction, so that what is generated, when it comes to be generated, is then destroyed; for it cannot be destroyed either immediately upon being generated or later, since what is destroyed must exist. Further, there must underlie the thing coming to be and changing a matter. What, then, will this be, just as body or soul is that which is alterable—what is it that is generated: motion or generation? And further, what is that into which they are moved? For there must be motion or generation of this from this into this. How then [could it be]? For there will not be learning of learning, and therefore neither generation of generation.","kind":"passage","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_DAZY_BO_3:P0056"]},{"id":"RAW_DAZY_BO_3:P0057","text":"Since there is motion neither of substance nor of relation nor of acting and being acted upon, it remains that there is motion in respect of quality, quantity, and place (for in each of these there is contrariety). By quality I mean not that in substance (for the differentia too is a quality), but the passive quality, in respect of which a thing is said to be affected or unaffected. The unmoved is both what is altogether incapable of being moved, and what begins [to move] only scarcely, after much time or slowly, and what, though naturally fitted and capable of being moved, is not moved when and where and in the way in which it is naturally fitted [to be moved]; this alone among unmoved things I call being at rest. For rest is contrary to motion, so that it would be a privation in what is receptive. Things are together in place when they are in one first place, and separate when they are in another; things touch when their extremities are together. Between is that at which what changes naturally arrives before reaching that into which it changes last, when it changes continuously according to nature.","kind":"passage","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_DAZY_BO_3:P0057"]},{"id":"RAW_DAZY_BO_3:P0058","text":"Contrary in place is what is at the greatest distance in a straight line. Successive is that of which, after something defined as a beginning by position or form or in some other way, there is nothing between it and that of which it is successive among things in the same genus—for example, lines of a line, or units of a unit, or houses of a house (though nothing prevents there being something else between). For what is successive is successive to something and is something later; for one is not successive to two, nor the new moon to the second [day]. Contiguous is what, being successive, touches. Since every change is among opposites, and these are contraries and contradiction, while there is nothing between the terms of contradiction, it is clear that the intermediate is among contraries. The continuous is a certain kind of contiguous. I call things continuous when the extremity of each, by which they touch and are held together, becomes the same and one; so that it is clear that continuity belongs to those things from which one thing is naturally fitted to come to be by their being joined.","kind":"passage","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_DAZY_BO_3:P0058"]},{"id":"RAW_DAZY_BO_3:P0059","text":"And that the successive is first is clear (for what is successive does not touch, though this is successive; and if it is continuous, it touches, but if it touches, it is not yet continuous; and in things in which there is no touching, there is no natural union). Hence a point is not the same as a unit; for touching belongs to the former, but not to the latter—only succession; and in the one case there is something between, in the other not.","kind":"passage","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_DAZY_BO_3:P0059"]}],"source_digest":"97d20853ef03c363aae296ca232713e6ab6094b9d07331fbe9273152a92cd4df","source_language_code":"grc","original_title":"Book 11"}],"passage_basis":"frozen-grounding","original_title":"Book 11","title_language_code":"en"},{"id":"RAW_DAZY_BO_4","title":"Book 12","order":12,"chapter_id":"CH_DAZY_BO_4","text":"περὶ τῆς οὐσίας ἡ θεωρία· τῶν γὰρ οὐσιῶν αἱ ἀρχαὶ καὶ τὰ αἴτια ζητοῦνται. καὶ γὰρ εἰ ὡς ὅλον τι τὸ πᾶν, ἡ οὐσία πρῶτον μέρος· καὶ εἰ τῷ ἐφεξῆς, κἂν οὕτως πρῶτον ἡ οὐσία, εἶτα τὸ ποιόν, εἶτα τὸ ποσόν. ἅμα δὲ οὐδʼ ὄντα ὡς εἰπεῖν ἁπλῶς ταῦτα, ἀλλὰ ποιότητες καὶ κινήσεις, ἢ καὶ τὸ οὐ λευκὸν καὶ τὸ οὐκ εὐθύ· λέγομεν γοῦν εἶναι καὶ ταῦτα, οἷον ἔστιν οὐ λευκόν. ἔτι οὐδὲν τῶν ἄλλων χωριστόν. μαρτυροῦσι δὲ καὶ οἱ ἀρχαῖοι ἔργῳ· τῆς γὰρ οὐσίας ἐζήτουν ἀρχὰς καὶ στοιχεῖα καὶ αἴτια. οἱ μὲν οὖν νῦν τὰ καθόλου οὐσίας μᾶλλον τιθέασιν (τὰ γὰρ γένη καθόλου, ἅ φασιν ἀρχὰς καὶ οὐσίας εἶναι μᾶλλον διὰ τὸ λογικῶς ζητεῖν)· οἱ δὲ πάλαι τὰ καθʼ ἕκαστα, οἷον πῦρ καὶ γῆν, ἀλλʼ οὐ τὸ κοινόν, σῶμα. οὐσίαι δὲ τρεῖς, μία μὲν αἰσθητή—ἧς ἡ μὲν ἀΐδιος ἡ δὲ φθαρτή, ἣν πάντες ὁμολογοῦσιν, οἷον τὰ φυτὰ καὶ τὰ ζῷα —ἧς ἀνάγκη τὰ στοιχεῖα λαβεῖν, εἴτε ἓν εἴτε πολλά· ἄλλη δὲ ἀκίνητος, καὶ ταύτην φασί τινες εἶναι χωριστήν, οἱ μὲν εἰς δύο διαιροῦντες, οἱ δὲ εἰς μίαν φύσιν τιθέντες τὰ εἴδη καὶ τὰ μαθηματικά, οἱ δὲ τὰ μαθηματικὰ μόνον τούτων. ἐκεῖναι μὲν δὴ φυσικῆς (μετὰ κινήσεως γάρ),  αὕτη δὲ ἑτέρας, εἰ μηδεμία αὐτοῖς ἀρχὴ κοινή.\n\n \n\nἡ δʼ αἰσθητὴ οὐσία μεταβλητή. εἰ δʼ ἡ μεταβολὴ ἐκ τῶν ἀντικειμένων ἢ τῶν μεταξύ, ἀντικειμένων δὲ μὴ πάντων (οὐ λευκὸν γὰρ ἡ φωνή) ἀλλʼ ἐκ τοῦ ἐναντίου, ἀνάγκη ὑπεῖναί τι τὸ μεταβάλλον εἰς τὴν ἐναντίωσιν· οὐ γὰρ τὰ ἐναντία μεταβάλλει. \n\n\n\n \n\n \n\nἔτι τὸ μὲν ὑπομένει, τὸ δʼ ἐναντίον οὐχ ὑπομένει· ἔστιν ἄρα τι τρίτον παρὰ τὰ ἐναντία, ἡ ὕλη. εἰ δὴ αἱ μεταβολαὶ τέτταρες, ἢ κατὰ τὸ τί ἢ κατὰ τὸ ποῖον ἢ πόσον ἢ ποῦ, καὶ γένεσις μὲν ἡ ἁπλῆ καὶ φθορὰ ἡ κατὰ τὸ τόδε, αὔξησις δὲ καὶ φθίσις ἡ κατὰ τὸ ποσόν, ἀλλοίωσις δὲ ἡ κατὰ τὸ πάθος, φορὰ δὲ ἡ κατὰ τόπον, εἰς ἐναντιώσεις ἂν εἶεν τὰς καθʼ ἕκαστον αἱ μεταβολαί. ἀνάγκη δὴ μεταβάλλειν τὴν ὕλην δυναμένην ἄμφω· ἐπεὶ δὲ διττὸν τὸ ὄν, μεταβάλλει πᾶν ἐκ τοῦ δυνάμει ὄντος εἰς τὸ ἐνεργείᾳ ὄν (οἷον ἐκ λευκοῦ δυνάμει εἰς τὸ ἐνεργείᾳ λευκόν, ὁμοίως δὲ καὶ ἐπʼ αὐξήσεως καὶ φθίσεως), ὥστε οὐ μόνον κατὰ συμβεβηκὸς ἐνδέχεται γίγνεσθαι ἐκ μὴ ὄντος, ἀλλὰ καὶ ἐξ ὄντος γίγνεται πάντα, δυνάμει μέντοι ὄντος, ἐκ μὴ ὄντος δὲ ἐνεργείᾳ. καὶ τοῦτʼ ἔστι τὸ Ἀναξαγόρου ἕν· βέλτιον γὰρ ἢ ὁμοῦ πάντα —καὶ Ἐμπεδοκλέους τὸ μῖγμα καὶ Ἀναξιμάνδρου, καὶ ὡς Δημόκριτός φησιν—\"ἦν ὁμοῦ πάντα δυνάμει, ἐνεργείᾳ δʼ οὔ\"· ὥστε τῆς ὕλης ἂν εἶεν ἡμμένοι· πάντα δʼ ὕλην ἔχει ὅσα μεταβάλλει, ἀλλʼ ἑτέραν· καὶ τῶν ἀϊδίων ὅσα μὴ γενητὰ κινητὰ δὲ φορᾷ, ἀλλʼ οὐ γενητὴν ἀλλὰ ποθὲν ποί. ἀπορήσειε δʼ ἄν τις ἐκ ποίου μὴ ὄντος ἡ γένεσις· τριχῶς γὰρ τὸ μὴ ὄν. εἰ δή τι ἔστι δυνάμει, ἀλλʼ ὅμως οὐ τοῦ τυχόντος ἀλλʼ ἕτερον ἐξ ἑτέρου· οὐδʼ ἱκανὸν ὅτι ὁμοῦ πάντα χρήματα· διαφέρει γὰρ τῇ ὕλῃ, ἐπεὶ διὰ τί ἄπειρα ἐγένετο ἀλλʼ οὐχ ἕν; ὁ γὰρ νοῦς εἷς, ὥστʼ εἰ καὶ ἡ ὕλη μία, ἐκεῖνο ἐγένετο ἐνεργείᾳ οὗ ἡ ὕλη ἦν δυνάμει. τρία δὴ τὰ αἴτια καὶ τρεῖς αἱ ἀρχαί, δύο μὲν ἡ ἐναντίωσις, ἧς τὸ μὲν λόγος καὶ εἶδος τὸ δὲ στέρησις, τὸ δὲ τρίτον ἡ ὕλη. \n\n\n\n \n\n \n\nμετὰ ταῦτα ὅτι οὐ γίγνεται οὔτε ἡ ὕλη οὔτε τὸ εἶδος, λέγω δὲ τὰ ἔσχατα. πᾶν γὰρ μεταβάλλει τὶ καὶ ὑπό τινος καὶ εἴς τι·  ὑφʼ οὗ μέν, τοῦ πρώτου κινοῦντος· ὃ δέ, ἡ ὕλη· εἰς ὃ δέ, τὸ εἶδος. εἰς ἄπειρον οὖν εἶσιν, εἰ μὴ μόνον ὁ χαλκὸς γίγνεται στρογγύλος ἀλλὰ καὶ τὸ στρογγύλον ἢ ὁ χαλκός· ἀνάγκη δὴ στῆναι.—μετὰ ταῦτα ὅτι ἑκάστη ἐκ συνωνύμου γίγνεται οὐσία (τὰ γὰρ φύσει οὐσίαι καὶ τὰ ἄλλα). ἢ γὰρ τέχνῃ ἢ φύσει γίγνεται ἢ τύχῃ ἢ τῷ αὐτομάτῳ. ἡ μὲν οὖν τέχνη ἀρχὴ ἐν ἄλλῳ, ἡ δὲ φύσις ἀρχὴ ἐν αὐτῷ (ἄνθρωπος γὰρ ἄνθρωπον γεννᾷ), αἱ δὲ λοιπαὶ αἰτίαι στερήσεις τούτων. οὐσίαι δὲ τρεῖς, ἡ μὲν ὕλη τόδε τι οὖσα τῷ φαίνεσθαι (ὅσα γὰρ ἁφῇ καὶ μὴ συμφύσει, ὕλη καὶ ὑποκείμενον), ἡ δὲ φύσις τόδε τι καὶ ἕξις τις εἰς ἥν· ἔτι τρίτη ἡ ἐκ τούτων ἡ καθʼ ἕκαστα, οἷον Σωκράτης ἢ Καλλίας. ἐπὶ μὲν οὖν τινῶν τὸ τόδε τι οὐκ ἔστι παρὰ τὴν συνθετὴν οὐσίαν, οἷον οἰκίας τὸ εἶδος, εἰ μὴ ἡ τέχνη (οὐδʼ ἔστι γένεσις καὶ φθορὰ τούτων, ἀλλʼ ἄλλον τρόπον εἰσὶ καὶ οὐκ εἰσὶν οἰκία τε ἡ ἄνευ ὕλης καὶ ὑγίεια καὶ πᾶν τὸ κατὰ τέχνην), ἀλλʼ εἴπερ, ἐπὶ τῶν φύσει· διὸ δὴ οὐ κακῶς Πλάτων ἔφη ὅτι εἴδη ἔστιν ὁπόσα φύσει, εἴπερ ἔστιν εἴδη ἄλλα τούτων οἷον πῦρ σὰρξ κεφαλή· ἅπαντα γὰρ ὕλη ἐστί, καὶ τῆς μάλιστʼ οὐσίας ἡ τελευταία. τὰ μὲν οὖν κινοῦντα αἴτια ὡς προγεγενημένα ὄντα, τὰ δʼ ὡς ὁ λόγος ἅμα. ὅτε γὰρ ὑγιαίνει ὁ ἄνθρωπος, τότε καὶ ἡ ὑγίεια ἔστιν, καὶ τὸ σχῆμα τῆς χαλκῆς σφαίρας ἅμα καὶ ἡ χαλκῆ σφαῖρα (εἰ δὲ καὶ ὕστερόν τι ὑπομένει, σκεπτέον· ἐπʼ ἐνίων γὰρ οὐδὲν κωλύει, οἷον εἰ ἡ ψυχὴ τοιοῦτον, μὴ πᾶσα ἀλλʼ ὁ νοῦς· πᾶσαν γὰρ ἀδύνατον ἴσως). φανερὸν δὴ ὅτι οὐδὲν δεῖ διά γε ταῦτʼ εἶναι τὰς ἰδέας· ἄνθρωπος γὰρ ἄνθρωπον γεννᾷ, ὁ καθʼ ἕκαστον τὸν τινά· ὁμοίως δὲ καὶ ἐπὶ τῶν τεχνῶν· ἡ γὰρ ἰατρικὴ τέχνη ὁ λόγος τῆς ὑγιείας ἐστίν. \n\n\n\n \n\n \n\nτὰ δʼ αἴτια καὶ αἱ ἀρχαὶ ἄλλα ἄλλων ἔστιν ὥς, ἔστι δʼ ὡς, ἂν καθόλου λέγῃ τις καὶ κατʼ ἀναλογίαν, ταὐτὰ πάντων. ἀπορήσειε γὰρ ἄν τις πότερον ἕτεραι ἢ αἱ αὐταὶ ἀρχαὶ καὶ στοιχεῖα τῶν οὐσιῶν καὶ τῶν πρός τι, καὶ καθʼ ἑκάστην δὴ τῶν κατηγοριῶν ὁμοίως. ἀλλʼ ἄτοπον εἰ ταὐτὰ πάντων· ἐκ τῶν αὐτῶν γὰρ ἔσται τὰ πρός τι καὶ αἱ οὐσίαι.  τί οὖν τοῦτʼ ἔσται; παρὰ γὰρ τὴν οὐσίαν καὶ τἆλλα τὰ κατηγορούμενα οὐδέν ἐστι κοινόν, πρότερον δὲ τὸ στοιχεῖον ἢ ὧν στοιχεῖον· ἀλλὰ μὴν οὐδʼ ἡ οὐσία στοιχεῖον τῶν πρός τι, οὐδὲ τούτων οὐδὲν τῆς οὐσίας. ἔτι πῶς ἐνδέχεται πάντων εἶναι ταὐτὰ στοιχεῖα; οὐδὲν γὰρ οἷόν τʼ εἶναι τῶν στοιχείων τῷ ἐκ στοιχείων συγκειμένῳ τὸ αὐτό, οἷον τῷ ΒΑ τὸ Β ἢ Α (οὐδὲ δὴ τῶν νοητῶν στοιχεῖόν ἐστιν, οἷον τὸ ὂν ἢ τὸ ἕν· ὑπάρχει γὰρ ταῦτα ἑκάστῳ καὶ τῶν συνθέτων). οὐδὲν ἄρʼ ἔσται αὐτῶν οὔτʼ οὐσία οὔτε πρός τι· ἀλλʼ ἀναγκαῖον. οὐκ ἔστιν ἄρα πάντων ταὐτὰ στοιχεῖα.—ἢ ὥσπερ λέγομεν, ἔστι μὲν ὥς, ἔστι δʼ ὡς οὔ, οἷον ἴσως τῶν αἰσθητῶν σωμάτων ὡς μὲν εἶδος τὸ θερμὸν καὶ ἄλλον τρόπον τὸ ψυχρὸν ἡ στέρησις, ὕλη δὲ τὸ δυνάμει ταῦτα πρῶτον καθʼ αὑτό, οὐσίαι δὲ ταῦτά τε καὶ τὰ ἐκ τούτων, ὧν ἀρχαὶ ταῦτα, ἢ εἴ τι ἐκ θερμοῦ καὶ ψυχροῦ γίγνεται ἕν, οἷον σὰρξ ἢ ὀστοῦν· ἕτερον γὰρ ἀνάγκη ἐκείνων εἶναι τὸ γενόμενον. τούτων μὲν οὖν ταὐτὰ στοιχεῖα καὶ ἀρχαί (ἄλλων δʼ ἄλλα), πάντων δὲ οὕτω μὲν εἰπεῖν οὐκ ἔστιν, τῷ ἀνάλογον δέ, ὥσπερ εἴ τις εἴποι ὅτι ἀρχαὶ εἰσὶ τρεῖς, τὸ εἶδος καὶ ἡ στέρησις καὶ ἡ ὕλη. ἀλλʼ ἕκαστον τούτων ἕτερον περὶ ἕκαστον γένος ἐστίν, οἷον ἐν χρώματι λευκὸν μέλαν ἐπιφάνεια· φῶς σκότος ἀήρ, ἐκ δὲ τούτων ἡμέρα καὶ νύξ. ἐπεὶ δὲ οὐ μόνον τὰ ἐνυπάρχοντα αἴτια, ἀλλὰ καὶ τῶν ἐκτὸς οἷον τὸ κινοῦν, δῆλον ὅτι ἕτερον ἀρχὴ καὶ στοιχεῖον, αἴτια δʼ ἄμφω, καὶ εἰς ταῦτα διαιρεῖται ἡ ἀρχή, τὸ δʼ ὡς κινοῦν ἢ ἱστὰν ἀρχή τις καὶ οὐσία, ὥστε στοιχεῖα μὲν κατʼ ἀναλογίαν τρία, αἰτίαι δὲ καὶ ἀρχαὶ τέτταρες· ἄλλο δʼ ἐν ἄλλῳ, καὶ τὸ πρῶτον αἴτιον ὡς κινοῦν ἄλλο ἄλλῳ. ὑγίεια, νόσος, σῶμα· τὸ κινοῦν ἰατρική. εἶδος, ἀταξία τοιαδί, πλίνθοι· τὸ κινοῦν οἰκοδομική . ἐπεὶ δὲ τὸ κινοῦν ἐν μὲν τοῖς φυσικοῖς ἀνθρώπῳ ἄνθρωπος, ἐν δὲ τοῖς ἀπὸ διανοίας τὸ εἶδος ἢ τὸ ἐναντίον, τρόπον τινὰ τρία αἴτια ἂν εἴη, ὡδὶ δὲ τέτταρα. ὑγίεια γάρ πως ἡ ἰατρική, καὶ οἰκίας εἶδος ἡ οἰκοδομική, καὶ ἄνθρωπος ἄνθρωπον γεννᾷ· ἔτι παρὰ ταῦτα τὸ ὡς πρῶτον πάντων κινοῦν πάντα. \n\n\n\n \n\n \n\nἐπεὶ δʼ ἐστὶ τὰ μὲν χωριστὰ τὰ δʼ οὐ χωριστά, οὐσίαι ἐκεῖνα.  καὶ διὰ τοῦτο πάντων αἴτια ταὐτά, ὅτι τῶν οὐσιῶν ἄνευ οὐκ ἔστι τὰ πάθη καὶ αἱ κινήσεις. ἔπειτα ἔσται ταῦτα ψυχὴ ἴσως καὶ σῶμα, ἢ νοῦς καὶ ὄρεξις καὶ σῶμα.—ἔτι δʼ ἄλλον τρόπον τῷ ἀνάλογον ἀρχαὶ αἱ αὐταί, οἷον ἐνέργεια καὶ δύναμις· ἀλλὰ καὶ ταῦτα ἄλλα τε ἄλλοις καὶ ἄλλως. ἐν ἐνίοις μὲν γὰρ τὸ αὐτὸ ὁτὲ μὲν ἐνεργείᾳ ἔστιν ὁτὲ δὲ δυνάμει, οἷον οἶνος ἢ σὰρξ ἢ ἄνθρωπος (πίπτει δὲ καὶ ταῦτα εἰς τὰ εἰρημένα αἴτια· ἐνεργείᾳ μὲν γὰρ τὸ εἶδος, ἐὰν ᾖ χωριστόν, καὶ τὸ ἐξ ἀμφοῖν στέρησις δέ, οἷον σκότος ἢ κάμνον, δυνάμει δὲ ἡ ὕλη· τοῦτο γάρ ἐστι τὸ δυνάμενον γίγνεσθαι ἄμφω)· ἄλλως δʼ ἐνεργείᾳ καὶ δυνάμει διαφέρει ὧν μὴ ἔστιν ἡ αὐτὴ ὕλη, ὧν ἐνίων οὐκ ἔστι τὸ αὐτὸ εἶδος ἀλλʼ ἕτερον, ὥσπερ ἀνθρώπου αἴτιον τά τε στοιχεῖα, πῦρ καὶ γῆ ὡς ὕλη καὶ τὸ ἴδιον εἶδος, καὶ ἔτι τι ἄλλο ἔξω οἷον ὁ πατήρ, καὶ παρὰ ταῦτα ὁ ἥλιος καὶ ὁ λοξὸς κύκλος, οὔτε ὕλη ὄντα οὔτʼ εἶδος οὔτε στέρησις οὔτε ὁμοειδὲς ἀλλὰ κινοῦντα. ἔτι δὲ ὁρᾶν δεῖ ὅτι τὰ μὲν καθόλου ἔστιν εἰπεῖν, τὰ δʼ οὔ. πάντων δὴ πρῶται ἀρχαὶ τὸ ἐνεργείᾳ πρῶτον τοδὶ καὶ ἄλλο ὃ δυνάμει. ἐκεῖνα μὲν οὖν τὰ καθόλου οὐκ ἔστιν· ἀρχὴ γὰρ τὸ καθʼ ἕκαστον τῶν καθʼ ἕκαστον· ἄνθρωπος μὲν γὰρ ἀνθρώπου καθόλου, ἀλλʼ οὐκ ἔστιν οὐδείς, ἀλλὰ Πηλεὺς Ἀχιλλέως σοῦ δὲ ὁ πατήρ, καὶ τοδὶ τὸ Β τουδὶ τοῦ ΒΑ, ὅλως δὲ τὸ Β τοῦ ἁπλῶς ΒΑ. ἔπειτα, εἰ δὴ τὰ τῶν οὐσιῶν, ἄλλα δὲ ἄλλων αἴτια καὶ στοιχεῖα, ὥσπερ ἐλέχθη, τῶν μὴ ἐν ταὐτῷ γένει, χρωμάτων ψόφων οὐσιῶν ποσότητος, πλὴν τῷ ἀνάλογον· καὶ τῶν ἐν ταὐτῷ εἴδει ἕτερα, οὐκ εἴδει ἀλλʼ ὅτι τῶν καθʼ ἕκαστον ἄλλο, ἥ τε σὴ ὕλη καὶ τὸ εἶδος καὶ τὸ κινῆσαν καὶ ἡ ἐμή, τῷ καθόλου δὲ λόγῳ ταὐτά. τὸ δὲ ζητεῖν τίνες ἀρχαὶ ἢ στοιχεῖα τῶν οὐσιῶν καὶ πρός τι καὶ ποιῶν, πότερον αἱ αὐταὶ ἢ ἕτεραι, δῆλον ὅτι πολλαχῶς γε λεγομένων ἔστιν ἑκάστου, διαιρεθέντων δὲ οὐ ταὐτὰ ἀλλʼ ἕτερα, πλὴν ὡδὶ καὶ πάντων, ὡδὶ μὲν ταὐτὰ ἢ τὸ ἀνάλογον, ὅτι ὕλη, εἶδος, στέρησις, τὸ κινοῦν, καὶ ὡδὶ τὰ τῶν οὐσιῶν αἴτια ὡς αἴτια πάντων, ὅτι ἀναιρεῖται ἀναιρουμένων· ἔτι τὸ πρῶτον ἐντελεχείᾳ· ὡδὶ δὲ ἕτερα πρῶτα ὅσα τὰ ἐναντία ἃ μήτε ὡς γένη λέγεται μήτε πολλαχῶς λέγεται· καὶ ἔτι αἱ ὗλαι.  τίνες μὲν οὖν αἱ ἀρχαὶ τῶν αἰσθητῶν καὶ πόσαι, καὶ πῶς αἱ αὐταὶ καὶ πῶς ἕτεραι, εἴρηται.\n\n\n\n \n\n \n\nἐπεὶ δʼ ἦσαν τρεῖς οὐσίαι, δύο μὲν αἱ φυσικαὶ μία δʼ ἡ ἀκίνητος, περὶ ταύτης λεκτέον ὅτι ἀνάγκη εἶναι ἀΐδιόν τινα οὐσίαν ἀκίνητον. αἵ τε γὰρ οὐσίαι πρῶται τῶν ὄντων, καὶ εἰ πᾶσαι φθαρταί, πάντα φθαρτά· ἀλλʼ ἀδύνατον κίνησιν ἢ γενέσθαι ἢ φθαρῆναι (ἀεὶ γὰρ ἦν), οὐδὲ χρόνον. οὐ γὰρ οἷόν τε τὸ πρότερον καὶ ὕστερον εἶναι μὴ ὄντος χρόνου· καὶ ἡ κίνησις ἄρα οὕτω συνεχὴς ὥσπερ καὶ ὁ χρόνος· ἢ γὰρ τὸ αὐτὸ ἢ κινήσεώς τι πάθος. κίνησις δʼ οὐκ ἔστι συνεχὴς ἀλλʼ ἢ ἡ κατὰ τόπον, καὶ ταύτης ἡ κύκλῳ.\n\n \n\nἀλλὰ μὴν εἰ ἔστι κινητικὸν ἢ ποιητικόν, μὴ ἐνεργοῦν δέ τι, οὐκ ἔσται κίνησις· ἐνδέχεται γὰρ τὸ δύναμιν ἔχον μὴ ἐνεργεῖν. οὐθὲν ἄρα ὄφελος οὐδʼ ἐὰν οὐσίας ποιήσωμεν ἀϊδίους, ὥσπερ οἱ τὰ εἴδη, εἰ μή τις δυναμένη ἐνέσται ἀρχὴ μεταβάλλειν· οὐ τοίνυν οὐδʼ αὕτη ἱκανή, οὐδʼ ἄλλη οὐσία παρὰ τὰ εἴδη· εἰ γὰρ μὴ ἐνεργήσει, οὐκ ἔσται κίνησις. ἔτι οὐδʼ εἰ ἐνεργήσει, ἡ δʼ οὐσία αὐτῆς δύναμις· οὐ γὰρ ἔσται κίνησις ἀΐδιος· ἐνδέχεται γὰρ τὸ δυνάμει ὂν μὴ εἶναι. δεῖ ἄρα εἶναι ἀρχὴν τοιαύτην ἧς ἡ οὐσία ἐνέργεια. ἔτι τοίνυν ταύτας δεῖ τὰς οὐσίας εἶναι ἄνευ ὕλης· ἀϊδίους γὰρ δεῖ, εἴπερ γε καὶ ἄλλο τι ἀΐδιον. ἐνέργεια ἄρα. καίτοι ἀπορία· δοκεῖ γὰρ τὸ μὲν ἐνεργοῦν πᾶν δύνασθαι τὸ δὲ δυνάμενον οὐ πᾶν ἐνεργεῖν, ὥστε πρότερον εἶναι τὴν δύναμιν. ἀλλὰ μὴν εἰ τοῦτο, οὐθὲν ἔσται τῶν ὄντων· ἐνδέχεται γὰρ δύνασθαι μὲν εἶναι μήπω δʼ εἶναι. καίτοι εἰ ὡς λέγουσιν οἱ θεολόγοι οἱ ἐκ νυκτὸς γεννῶντες, ἢ ὡς οἱ φυσικοὶ ὁμοῦ πάντα χρήματά φασι, τὸ αὐτὸ ἀδύνατον. πῶς γὰρ κινηθήσεται, εἰ μὴ ἔσται ἐνεργείᾳ τι αἴτιον; οὐ γὰρ ἥ γε ὕλη κινήσει αὐτὴ ἑαυτήν, ἀλλὰ τεκτονική, οὐδὲ τὰ ἐπιμήνια οὐδʼ ἡ γῆ, ἀλλὰ τὰ σπέρματα καὶ ἡ γονή. διὸ ἔνιοι ποιοῦσιν ἀεὶ ἐνέργειαν, οἷον Λεύκιππος καὶ Πλάτων· ἀεὶ γὰρ εἶναί φασι κίνησιν. ἀλλὰ διὰ τί καὶ τίνα οὐ λέγουσιν, οὐδʼ, εἰ ὡδὶ ἢ ὡδί, τὴν αἰτίαν. οὐδὲν γὰρ ὡς ἔτυχε κινεῖται, ἀλλὰ δεῖ τι ἀεὶ ὑπάρχειν, ἕσπερ νῦν φύσει μὲν ὡδί, βίᾳ δὲ ἢ ὑπὸ νοῦ ἢ ἄλλου ὡδί. (εἶτα ποία πρώτη; διαφέρει γὰρ ἀμήχανον ὅσον). ἀλλὰ μὴν οὐδὲ Πλάτωνί γε οἷόν τε λέγειν ἣν οἴεται ἐνίοτε ἀρχὴν εἶναι,  τὸ αὐτὸ ἑαυτὸ κινοῦν· ὕστερον γὰρ καὶ ἅμα τῷ οὐρανῷ ἡ ψυχή, ὡς φησίν. τὸ μὲν δὴ δύναμιν οἴεσθαι ἐνεργείας πρότερον ἔστι μὲν ὡς καλῶς ἔστι δʼ ὡς οὔ (εἴρηται δὲ πῶς)· ὅτι δʼ ἐνέργεια πρότερον, μαρτυρεῖ Ἀναξαγόρας (ὁ γὰρ νοῦς ἐνέργεια) καὶ Ἐμπεδοκλῆς φιλίαν καὶ τὸ νεῖκος, καὶ οἱ ἀεὶ λέγοντες κίνησιν εἶναι, ὥσπερ Λεύκιππος· ὥστʼ οὐκ ἦν ἄπειρον χρόνον χάος ἢ νύξ, ἀλλὰ ταὐτὰ ἀεὶ ἢ περιόδῳ ἢ ἄλλως, εἴπερ πρότερον ἐνέργεια δυνάμεως. εἰ δὴ τὸ αὐτὸ ἀεὶ περιόδῳ, δεῖ τι ἀεὶ μένειν ὡσαύτως ἐνεργοῦν. εἰ δὲ μέλλει γένεσις καὶ φθορὰ εἶναι, ἄλλο δεῖ εἶναι ἀεὶ ἐνεργοῦν ἄλλως καὶ ἄλλως. ἀνάγκη ἄρα ὡδὶ μὲν καθʼ αὑτὸ ἐνεργεῖν ὡδὶ δὲ κατʼ ἄλλο· ἤτοι ἄρα καθʼ ἕτερον ἢ κατὰ τὸ πρῶτον. ἀνάγκη δὴ κατὰ τοῦτο· πάλιν γὰρ ἐκεῖνο αὐτῷ τε αἴτιον κἀκείνῳ. οὐκοῦν βέλτιον τὸ πρῶτον· καὶ γὰρ αἴτιον ἦν ἐκεῖνο τοῦ ἀεὶ ὡσαύτως· τοῦ δʼ ἄλλως ἕτερον, τοῦ δʼ ἀεὶ ἄλλως ἄμφω δηλονότι. οὐκοῦν οὕτως καὶ ἔχουσιν αἱ κινήσεις. τί οὖν ἄλλας δεῖ ζητεῖν ἀρχάς; \n\n\n\n \n\n \n\nἐπεὶ δʼ οὕτω τʼ ἐνδέχεται, καὶ εἰ μὴ οὕτως, ἐκ νυκτὸς ἔσται καὶ ὁμοῦ πάντων καὶ ἐκ μὴ ὄντος, λύοιτʼ ἂν ταῦτα, καὶ ἔστι τι ἀεὶ κινούμενον κίνησιν ἄπαυστον, αὕτη δʼ ἡ κύκλῳ (καὶ τοῦτο οὐ λόγῳ μόνον ἀλλʼ ἔργῳ δῆλον), ὥστʼ ἀΐδιος ἂν εἴη ὁ πρῶτος οὐρανός. ἔστι τοίνυν τι καὶ ὃ κινεῖ. ἐπεὶ δὲ τὸ κινούμενον καὶ κινοῦν  μέσον, †τοίνυν† ἔστι τι ὃ οὐ κινούμενον κινεῖ, ἀΐδιον καὶ οὐσία καὶ ἐνέργεια οὖσα. κινεῖ δὲ ὧδε τὸ ὀρεκτὸν καὶ τὸ νοητόν· κινεῖ οὐ κινούμενα. τούτων τὰ πρῶτα τὰ αὐτά. ἐπιθυμητὸν μὲν γὰρ τὸ φαινόμενον καλόν, βουλητὸν δὲ πρῶτον τὸ ὂν καλόν· ὀρεγόμεθα δὲ διότι δοκεῖ μᾶλλον ἢ δοκεῖ διότι ὀρεγόμεθα· ἀρχὴ γὰρ ἡ νόησις. νοῦς δὲ ὑπὸ τοῦ νοητοῦ κινεῖται, νοητὴ δὲ ἡ ἑτέρα συστοιχία καθʼ αὑτήν· καὶ ταύτης ἡ οὐσία πρώτη, καὶ ταύτης ἡ ἁπλῆ καὶ κατʼ ἐνέργειαν (ἔστι δὲ τὸ ἓν καὶ τὸ ἁπλοῦν οὐ τὸ αὐτό· τὸ μὲν γὰρ ἓν μέτρον σημαίνει, τὸ δὲ ἁπλοῦν πὼς ἔχον αὐτό). ἀλλὰ μὴν καὶ τὸ καλὸν καὶ τὸ διʼ αὑτὸ αἱρετὸν ἐν τῇ αὐτῇ συστοιχίᾳ· καὶ ἔστιν ἄριστον ἀεὶ ἢ ἀνάλογον τὸ πρῶτον.  ὅτι δʼ ἔστι τὸ οὗ ἕνεκα ἐν τοῖς ἀκινήτοις, ἡ διαίρεσις δηλοῖ· ἔστι γὰρ τινὶ τὸ οὗ ἕνεκα καὶ τινός, ὧν τὸ μὲν ἔστι τὸ δʼ οὐκ ἔστι. κινεῖ δὴ ὡς ἐρώμενον, κινούμενα δὲ τἆλλα κινεῖ. εἰ μὲν οὖν τι κινεῖται, ἐνδέχεται καὶ ἄλλως ἔχειν, ὥστʼ εἰ  φορὰ πρώτη ἡ ἐνέργειά ἐστιν, ᾗ κινεῖται ταύτῃ γε ἐνδέχεται ἄλλως ἔχειν, κατὰ τόπον, καὶ εἰ μὴ κατʼ οὐσίαν· ἐπεὶ δὲ ἔστι τι κινοῦν αὐτὸ ἀκίνητον ὄν, ἐνεργείᾳ ὄν, τοῦτο οὐκ ἐνδέχεται ἄλλως ἔχειν οὐδαμῶς. φορὰ γὰρ ἡ πρώτη τῶν μεταβολῶν, ταύτης δὲ ἡ κύκλῳ· ταύτην δὲ τοῦτο κινεῖ. ἐξ ἀνάγκης ἄρα ἐστὶν ὄν· καὶ ᾗ ἀνάγκῃ, καλῶς, καὶ οὕτως ἀρχή. τὸ γὰρ ἀναγκαῖον τοσαυταχῶς, τὸ μὲν βίᾳ ὅτι παρὰ τὴν ὁρμήν, τὸ δὲ οὗ οὐκ ἄνευ τὸ εὖ, τὸ δὲ μὴ ἐνδεχόμενον ἄλλως ἀλλʼ ἁπλῶς.—ἐκ τοιαύτης ἄρα ἀρχῆς ἤρτηται ὁ οὐρανὸς καὶ ἡ φύσις. διαγωγὴ δʼ ἐστὶν οἵα ἡ ἀρίστη μικρὸν χρόνον ἡμῖν (οὕτω γὰρ ἀεὶ ἐκεῖνο· ἡμῖν μὲν γὰρ ἀδύνατον), ἐπεὶ καὶ ἡδονὴ ἡ ἐνέργεια τούτου (καὶ διὰ τοῦτο ἐγρήγορσις αἴσθησις νόησις ἥδιστον, ἐλπίδες δὲ καὶ μνῆμαι διὰ ταῦτα). ἡ δὲ νόησις ἡ καθʼ αὑτὴν τοῦ καθʼ αὑτὸ ἀρίστου, καὶ ἡ μάλιστα τοῦ μάλιστα. αὑτὸν δὲ νοεῖ ὁ νοῦς κατὰ μετάληψιν τοῦ νοητοῦ· νοητὸς γὰρ γίγνεται θιγγάνων καὶ νοῶν, ὥστε ταὐτὸν νοῦς καὶ νοητόν. τὸ γὰρ δεκτικὸν τοῦ νοητοῦ καὶ τῆς οὐσίας νοῦς, ἐνεργεῖ δὲ ἔχων, ὥστʼ ἐκείνου μᾶλλον τοῦτο ὃ δοκεῖ ὁ νοῦς θεῖον ἔχειν, καὶ ἡ θεωρία τὸ ἥδιστον καὶ ἄριστον. εἰ οὖν οὕτως εὖ ἔχει, ὡς ἡμεῖς ποτέ, ὁ θεὸς ἀεί, θαυμαστόν· εἰ δὲ μᾶλλον, ἔτι θαυμασιώτερον. ἔχει δὲ ὧδε. καὶ ζωὴ δέ γε ὑπάρχει· ἡ γὰρ νοῦ ἐνέργεια ζωή, ἐκεῖνος δὲ ἡ ἐνέργεια· ἐνέργεια δὲ ἡ καθʼ αὑτὴν ἐκείνου ζωὴ ἀρίστη καὶ ἀΐδιος. φαμὲν δὴ τὸν θεὸν εἶναι ζῷον ἀΐδιον ἄριστον, ὥστε ζωὴ καὶ αἰὼν συνεχὴς καὶ ἀΐδιος ὑπάρχει τῷ θεῷ· τοῦτο γὰρ ὁ θεός. ὅσοι δὲ ὑπολαμβάνουσιν, ὥσπερ οἱ Πυθαγόρειοι καὶ Σπεύσιππος τὸ κάλλιστον καὶ ἄριστον μὴ ἐν ἀρχῇ εἶναι, διὰ τὸ καὶ τῶν φυτῶν καὶ τῶν ζῴων τὰς ἀρχὰς αἴτια μὲν εἶναι τὸ δὲ καλὸν καὶ τέλειον ἐν τοῖς ἐκ τούτων, οὐκ ὀρθῶς οἴονται. τὸ γὰρ σπέρμα ἐξ ἑτέρων ἐστὶ προτέρων τελείων, καὶ τὸ πρῶτον οὐ σπέρμα ἐστὶν ἀλλὰ τὸ τέλειον·  οἷον πρότερον ἄνθρωπον ἂν φαίη τις εἶναι τοῦ σπέρματος, οὐ τὸν ἐκ τούτου γενόμενον ἀλλʼ ἕτερον ἐξ οὗ τὸ σπέρμα. ὅτι μὲν οὖν ἔστιν οὐσία τις ἀΐδιος καὶ ἀκίνητος καὶ κεχωρισμένη τῶν αἰσθητῶν, φανερὸν ἐκ τῶν εἰρημένων· δέδεικται δὲ καὶ ὅτι μέγεθος οὐδὲν ἔχειν ἐνδέχεται ταύτην τὴν οὐσίαν ἀλλʼ ἀμερὴς καὶ ἀδιαίρετός ἐστιν (κινεῖ γὰρ τὸν ἄπειρον χρόνον, οὐδὲν δʼ ἔχει δύναμιν ἄπειρον πεπερασμένον· ἐπεὶ δὲ πᾶν μέγεθος ἢ ἄπειρον ἢ πεπερασμένον, πεπερασμένον μὲν διὰ τοῦτο οὐκ ἂν ἔχοι μέγεθος, ἄπειρον δʼ ὅτι ὅλως οὐκ ἔστιν οὐδὲν ἄπειρον μέγεθος)· ἀλλὰ μὴν καὶ ὅτι ἀπαθὲς καὶ ἀναλλοίωτον· πᾶσαι γὰρ αἱ ἄλλαι κινήσεις ὕστεραι τῆς κατὰ τόπον. ταῦτα μὲν οὖν δῆλα διότι τοῦτον ἔχει τὸν τρόπον.\n\n\n\n \n\n \n\nπότερον δὲ μίαν θετέον τὴν τοιαύτην οὐσίαν ἢ πλείους, καὶ πόσας, δεῖ μὴ λανθάνειν, ἀλλὰ μεμνῆσθαι καὶ τὰς τῶν ἄλλων ἀποφάσεις, ὅτι περὶ πλήθους οὐθὲν εἰρήκασιν ὅ τι καὶ σαφὲς εἰπεῖν. ἡ μὲν γὰρ περὶ τὰς ἰδέας ὑπόληψις οὐδεμίαν ἔχει σκέψιν ἰδίαν (ἀριθμοὺς γὰρ λέγουσι τὰς ἰδέας οἱ λέγοντες ἰδέας, περὶ δὲ τῶν ἀριθμῶν ὁτὲ μὲν ὡς περὶ ἀπείρων λέγουσιν ὁτὲ δὲ ὡς μέχρι τῆς δεκάδος ὡρισμένων· διʼ ἣν δʼ αἰτίαν τοσοῦτον τὸ πλῆθος τῶν ἀριθμῶν, οὐδὲν λέγεται μετὰ σπουδῆς ἀποδεικτικῆς)· ἡμῖν δʼ ἐκ τῶν ὑποκειμένων καὶ διωρισμένων λεκτέον. ἡ μὲν γὰρ ἀρχὴ καὶ τὸ πρῶτον τῶν ὄντων ἀκίνητον καὶ καθʼ αὑτὸ καὶ κατὰ συμβεβηκός, κινοῦν δὲ τὴν πρώτην ἀΐδιον καὶ μίαν κίνησιν· ἐπεὶ δὲ τὸ κινούμενον ἀνάγκη ὑπό τινος κινεῖσθαι, καὶ τὸ πρῶτον κινοῦν ἀκίνητον εἶναι καθʼ αὑτό, καὶ τὴν ἀΐδιον κίνησιν ὑπὸ ἀϊδίου κινεῖσθαι καὶ τὴν μίαν ὑφʼ ἑνός, ὁρῶμεν δὲ παρὰ τὴν τοῦ παντὸς τὴν ἁπλῆν φοράν, ἣν κινεῖν φαμὲν τὴν πρώτην οὐσίαν καὶ ἀκίνητον, ἄλλας φορὰς οὔσας τὰς τῶν πλανήτων ἀϊδίους (ἀΐδιον γὰρ καὶ ἄστατον τὸ κύκλῳ σῶμα· δέδεικται δʼ ἐν τοῖς φυσικοῖς περὶ τούτων), ἀνάγκη καὶ τούτων ἑκάστην τῶν φορῶν ὑπʼ ἀκινήτου τε κινεῖσθαι καθʼ αὑτὴν καὶ ἀϊδίου οὐσίας. ἥ τε γὰρ τῶν ἄστρων φύσις ἀΐδιος οὐσία τις οὖσα, καὶ τὸ κινοῦν ἀΐδιον καὶ πρότερον τοῦ κινουμένου, καὶ τὸ πρότερον οὐσίας οὐσίαν ἀναγκαῖον εἶναι. φανερὸν τοίνυν ὅτι τοσαύτας τε οὐσίας ἀναγκαῖον εἶναι τήν τε φύσιν ἀϊδίους καὶ ἀκινήτους καθʼ αὑτάς, καὶ ἄνευ μεγέθους διὰ τὴν εἰρημένην αἰτίαν πρότερον.  —ὅτι μὲν οὖν εἰσὶν οὐσίαι, καὶ τούτων τις πρώτη καὶ δευτέρα κατὰ τὴν αὐτὴν τάξιν ταῖς φοραῖς τῶν ἄστρων, φανερόν· τὸ δὲ πλῆθος ἤδη τῶν φορῶν ἐκ τῆς οἰκειοτάτης φιλοσοφίᾳ τῶν μαθηματικῶν ἐπιστημῶν δεῖ σκοπεῖν, ἐκ τῆς ἀστρολογίας· αὕτη γὰρ περὶ οὐσίας αἰσθητῆς μὲν ἀϊδίου δὲ ποιεῖται τὴν θεωρίαν, αἱ δʼ ἄλλαι περὶ οὐδεμιᾶς οὐσίας, οἷον ἥ τε περὶ τοὺς ἀριθμοὺς καὶ τὴν γεωμετρίαν. ὅτι μὲν οὖν πλείους τῶν φερομένων αἱ φοραί, φανερὸν τοῖς καὶ μετρίως ἡμμένοις (πλείους γὰρ ἕκαστον φέρεται μιᾶς τῶν πλανωμένων ἄστρων)· πόσαι δʼ αὗται τυγχάνουσιν οὖσαι, νῦν μὲν ἡμεῖς ἃ λέγουσι τῶν μαθηματικῶν τινὲς ἐννοίας χάριν λέγομεν, ὅπως ᾖ τι τῇ διανοίᾳ πλῆθος ὡρισμένον ὑπολαβεῖν· τὸ δὲ λοιπὸν τὰ μὲν ζητοῦντας αὐτοὺς δεῖ τὰ δὲ πυνθανομένους παρὰ τῶν ζητούντων, ἄν τι φαίνηται παρὰ τὰ νῦν εἰρημένα τοῖς ταῦτα πραγματευομένοις, φιλεῖν μὲν ἀμφοτέρους, πείθεσθαι δὲ τοῖς ἀκριβεστέροις.—Εὔδοξος μὲν οὖν ἡλίου καὶ σελήνης ἑκατέρου τὴν φορὰν ἐν τρισὶν ἐτίθετʼ εἶναι σφαίραις, ὧν τὴν μὲν πρώτην τὴν τῶν ἀπλανῶν ἄστρων εἶναι, τὴν δὲ δευτέραν κατὰ τὸν διὰ μέσων τῶν ζῳδίων, τὴν δὲ τρίτην κατὰ τὸν λελοξωμένον ἐν τῷ πλάτει τῶν ζῳδίων (ἐν μείζονι δὲ πλάτει λελοξῶσθαι καθʼ ὃν ἡ σελήνη φέρεται ἢ καθʼ ὃν ὁ ἥλιος), τῶν δὲ πλανωμένων ἄστρων ἐν τέτταρσιν ἑκάστου σφαίραις, καὶ τούτων δὲ τὴν μὲν πρώτην καὶ δευτέραν τὴν αὐτὴν εἶναι ἐκείναις (τήν τε γὰρ τῶν ἀπλανῶν τὴν ἁπάσας φέρουσαν εἶναι, καὶ τὴν ὑπὸ ταύτῃ τεταγμένην καὶ κατὰ τὸν διὰ μέσων τῶν ζῳδίων τὴν φορὰν ἔχουσαν κοινὴν ἁπασῶν εἶναι), τῆς δὲ τρίτης ἁπάντων τοὺς πόλους ἐν τῷ διὰ μέσων τῶν ζῳδίων εἶναι, τῆς δὲ τετάρτης τὴν φορὰν κατὰ τὸν λελοξωμένον πρὸς τὸν μέσον ταύτης· εἶναι δὲ τῆς τρίτης σφαίρας τοὺς πόλους τῶν μὲν ἄλλων ἰδίους, τοὺς δὲ τῆς Ἀφροδίτης καὶ τοῦ Ἑρμοῦ τοὺς αὐτούς· Κάλλιππος δὲ τὴν μὲν θέσιν τῶν σφαιρῶν τὴν αὐτὴν ἐτίθετο Εὐδόξῳ , τὸ δὲ πλῆθος τῷ μὲν τοῦ Διὸς καὶ τῷ τοῦ Κρόνου τὸ αὐτὸ ἐκείνῳ ἀπεδίδου, τῷ δʼ ἡλίῳ καὶ τῇ σελήνῃ δύο ᾤετο ἔτι προσθετέας εἶναι σφαίρας, τὰ φαινόμενα εἰ μέλλει τις ἀποδώσειν, τοῖς δὲ λοιποῖς τῶν πλανήτων ἑκάστῳ μίαν. ἀναγκαῖον δέ, εἰ μέλλουσι συντεθεῖσαι πᾶσαι τὰ φαινόμενα ἀποδώσειν,  καθʼ ἕκαστον τῶν πλανωμένων ἑτέρας σφαίρας μιᾷ ἐλάττονας εἶναι τὰς ἀνελιττούσας καὶ εἰς τὸ αὐτὸ ἀποκαθιστάσας τῇ θέσει τὴν πρώτην σφαῖραν ἀεὶ τοῦ ὑποκάτω τεταγμένου ἄστρου· οὕτω γὰρ μόνως ἐνδέχεται τὴν τῶν πλανήτων φορὰν ἅπαντα ποιεῖσθαι. ἐπεὶ οὖν ἐν αἷς μὲν αὐτὰ φέρεται σφαίραις αἱ μὲν ὀκτὼ αἱ δὲ πέντε καὶ εἴκοσίν εἰσιν, τούτων δὲ μόνας οὐ δεῖ ἀνελιχθῆναι ἐν αἷς τὸ κατωτάτω τεταγμένον φέρεται, αἱ μὲν τὰς τῶν πρώτων δύο ἀνελίττουσαι ἓξ ἔσονται, αἱ δὲ τὰς τῶν ὕστερον τεττάρων ἑκκαίδεκα· ὁ δὴ ἁπασῶν ἀριθμὸς τῶν τε φερουσῶν καὶ τῶν ἀνελιττουσῶν ταύτας πεντήκοντά τε καὶ πέντε. εἰ δὲ τῇ σελήνῃ τε καὶ τῷ ἡλίῳ μὴ προστιθείη τις ἃς εἴπομεν κινήσεις, αἱ πᾶσαι σφαῖραι ἔσονται ἑπτά τε καὶ τεσσαράκοντα.—τὸ μὲν οὖν πλῆθος τῶν σφαιρῶν ἔστω τοσοῦτον, ὥστε καὶ τὰς οὐσίας καὶ τὰς ἀρχὰς τὰς ἀκινήτους  τοσαύτας εὔλογον ὑπολαβεῖν (τὸ γὰρ ἀναγκαῖον ἀφείσθω τοῖς ἰσχυροτέροις λέγειν)· εἰ δὲ μηδεμίαν οἷόν τʼ εἶναι φορὰν μὴ συντείνουσαν πρὸς ἄστρου φοράν, ἔτι δὲ πᾶσαν φύσιν καὶ πᾶσαν οὐσίαν ἀπαθῆ καὶ καθʼ αὑτὴν τοῦ ἀρίστου τετυχηκυῖαν τέλος εἶναι δεῖ νομίζειν, οὐδεμία ἂν εἴη παρὰ ταύτας ἑτέρα φύσις, ἀλλὰ τοῦτον ἀνάγκη τὸν ἀριθμὸν εἶναι τῶν οὐσιῶν. εἴτε γὰρ εἰσὶν ἕτεραι, κινοῖεν ἂν ὡς τέλος οὖσαι φορᾶς· ἀλλὰ εἶναί γε ἄλλας φορὰς ἀδύνατον παρὰ τὰς εἰρημένας. τοῦτο δὲ εὔλογον ἐκ τῶν φερομένων ὑπολαβεῖν. εἰ γὰρ πᾶν τὸ φέρον τοῦ φερομένου χάριν πέφυκε καὶ φορὰ πᾶσα φερομένου τινός ἐστιν, οὐδεμία φορὰ αὑτῆς ἂν ἕνεκα εἴη οὐδʼ ἄλλης φορᾶς, ἀλλὰ τῶν ἄστρων ἕνεκα. εἰ γὰρ ἔσται φορὰ φορᾶς ἕνεκα, καὶ ἐκείνην ἑτέρου δεήσει χάριν εἶναι· ὥστʼ ἐπειδὴ οὐχ οἷόν τε εἰς ἄπειρον,  τέλος ἔσται πάσης φορᾶς τῶν φερομένων τι θείων σωμάτων κατὰ τὸν οὐρανόν. ὅτι δὲ εἷς οὐρανός, φανερόν. εἰ γὰρ πλείους οὐρανοὶ ὥσπερ ἄνθρωποι, ἔσται εἴδει μία ἡ περὶ ἕκαστον ἀρχή, ἀριθμῷ δέ γε πολλαί. ἀλλʼ ὅσα ἀριθμῷ πολλά, ὕλην ἔχει (εἷς γὰρ λόγος καὶ ὁ αὐτὸς πολλῶν, οἷον ἀνθρώπου, Σωκράτης δὲ εἷς)· τὸ δὲ τί ἦν εἶναι οὐκ ἔχει ὕλην τὸ πρῶτον· ἐντελέχεια γάρ. ἓν ἄρα καὶ λόγῳ καὶ ἀριθμῷ τὸ πρῶτον κινοῦν ἀκίνητον ὄν· καὶ τὸ κινούμενον ἄρα ἀεὶ καὶ συνεχῶς· εἷς ἄρα οὐρανὸς μόνος.  παραδέδοται δὲ παρὰ τῶν ἀρχαίων καὶ παμπαλαίων ἐν μύθου σχήματι καταλελειμμένα τοῖς ὕστερον ὅτι θεοί τέ εἰσιν οὗτοι καὶ περιέχει τὸ θεῖον τὴν ὅλην φύσιν. τὰ δὲ λοιπὰ μυθικῶς ἤδη προσῆκται πρὸς τὴν πειθὼ τῶν πολλῶν καὶ πρὸς τὴν εἰς τοὺς νόμους καὶ τὸ συμφέρον χρῆσιν· ἀνθρωποειδεῖς τε γὰρ τούτους καὶ τῶν ἄλλων ζῴων ὁμοίους τισὶ λέγουσι, καὶ τούτοις ἕτερα ἀκόλουθα καὶ παραπλήσια τοῖς εἰρημένοις, ὧν εἴ τις χωρίσας αὐτὸ λάβοι μόνον τὸ πρῶτον, ὅτι θεοὺς ᾤοντο τὰς πρώτας οὐσίας εἶναι, θείως ἂν εἰρῆσθαι νομίσειεν, καὶ κατὰ τὸ εἰκὸς πολλάκις εὑρημένης εἰς τὸ δυνατὸν ἑκάστης καὶ τέχνης καὶ φιλοσοφίας καὶ πάλιν φθειρομένων καὶ ταύτας τὰς δόξας ἐκείνων οἷον λείψανα περισεσῶσθαι μέχρι τοῦ νῦν. ἡ μὲν οὖν πάτριος δόξα καὶ ἡ παρὰ τῶν πρώτων ἐπὶ τοσοῦτον ἡμῖν φανερὰ μόνον.\n\n\n\n \n\n \n\nτὰ δὲ περὶ τὸν νοῦν ἔχει τινὰς ἀπορίας· δοκεῖ μὲν γὰρ εἶναι τῶν φαινομένων θειότατον, πῶς δʼ ἔχων τοιοῦτος ἂν εἴη, ἔχει τινὰς δυσκολίας. εἴτε γὰρ μηδὲν νοεῖ, τί ἂν εἴη τὸ σεμνόν, ἀλλʼ ἔχει ὥσπερ ἂν εἰ ὁ καθεύδων· εἴτε νοεῖ, τούτου δʼ ἄλλο κύριον, οὐ γάρ ἐστι τοῦτο ὅ ἐστιν αὐτοῦ ἡ οὐσία νόησις, ἀλλὰ δύναμις, οὐκ ἂν ἡ ἀρίστη οὐσία εἴη· διὰ γὰρ τοῦ νοεῖν τὸ τίμιον αὐτῷ ὑπάρχει. ἔτι δὲ εἴτε νοῦς ἡ οὐσία αὐτοῦ εἴτε νόησίς ἐστι, τί νοεῖ; ἢ γὰρ αὐτὸς αὑτὸν ἢ ἕτερόν τι· καὶ εἰ ἕτερόν τι, ἢ τὸ αὐτὸ ἀεὶ ἢ ἄλλο. πότερον οὖν διαφέρει τι ἢ οὐδὲν τὸ νοεῖν τὸ καλὸν ἢ τὸ τυχόν; ἢ καὶ ἄτοπον τὸ διανοεῖσθαι περὶ ἐνίων; δῆλον τοίνυν ὅτι τὸ θειότατον καὶ τιμιώτατον νοεῖ, καὶ οὐ μεταβάλλει· εἰς χεῖρον γὰρ ἡ μεταβολή, καὶ κίνησίς τις ἤδη τὸ τοιοῦτον. πρῶτον μὲν οὖν εἰ μὴ νόησίς ἐστιν ἀλλὰ δύναμις, εὔλογον ἐπίπονον εἶναι τὸ συνεχὲς αὐτῷ τῆς νοήσεως· ἔπειτα δῆλον ὅτι ἄλλο τι ἂν εἴη τὸ τιμιώτερον ἢ ὁ νοῦς, τὸ νοούμενον. καὶ γὰρ τὸ νοεῖν καὶ ἡ νόησις ὑπάρξει καὶ τὸ χείριστον νοοῦντι, ὥστʼ εἰ φευκτὸν τοῦτο (καὶ γὰρ μὴ ὁρᾶν ἔνια κρεῖττον ἢ ὁρᾶν), οὐκ ἂν εἴη τὸ ἄριστον ἡ νόησις. αὑτὸν ἄρα νοεῖ, εἴπερ ἐστὶ τὸ κράτιστον, καὶ ἔστιν ἡ νόησις νοήσεως νόησις. φαίνεται δʼ ἀεὶ ἄλλου ἡ ἐπιστήμη καὶ ἡ αἴσθησις καὶ ἡ δόξα καὶ ἡ διάνοια, αὑτῆς δʼ ἐν παρέργῳ. ἔτι εἰ ἄλλο τὸ νοεῖν καὶ τὸ νοεῖσθαι, κατὰ πότερον αὐτῷ τὸ εὖ ὑπάρχει; οὐδὲ γὰρ ταὐτὸ τὸ εἶναι νοήσει καὶ νοουμένῳ. ἢ ἐπʼ ἐνίων ἡ ἐπιστήμη τὸ πρᾶγμα,  ἐπὶ μὲν τῶν ποιητικῶν ἄνευ ὕλης ἡ οὐσία καὶ τὸ τί ἦν εἶναι, ἐπὶ δὲ τῶν θεωρητικῶν ὁ λόγος τὸ πρᾶγμα καὶ ἡ νόησις; οὐχ ἑτέρου οὖν ὄντος τοῦ νοουμένου καὶ τοῦ νοῦ, ὅσα μὴ ὕλην ἔχει, τὸ αὐτὸ ἔσται, καὶ ἡ νόησις τῷ νοουμένῳ μία. ἔτι δὴ λείπεται ἀπορία, εἰ σύνθετον τὸ νοούμενον· μεταβάλλοι γὰρ ἂν ἐν τοῖς μέρεσι τοῦ ὅλου. ἢ ἀδιαίρετον πᾶν τὸ μὴ ἔχον ὕλην—ὥσπερ ὁ ἀνθρώπινος νοῦς ἢ ὅ γε τῶν συνθέτων ἔχει ἔν τινι χρόνῳ (οὐ γὰρ ἔχει τὸ εὖ ἐν τῳδὶ ἢ ἐν τῳδί, ἀλλʼ ἐν ὅλῳ τινὶ τὸ ἄριστον, ὂν ἄλλο τι)—οὕτως δʼ ἔχει αὐτὴ αὑτῆς ἡ νόησις τὸν ἅπαντα αἰῶνα; \n\n\n\n \n\n \n\nἐπισκεπτέον δὲ καὶ ποτέρως ἔχει ἡ τοῦ ὅλου φύσις τὸ ἀγαθὸν καὶ τὸ ἄριστον, πότερον κεχωρισμένον τι καὶ αὐτὸ καθʼ αὑτό, ἢ τὴν τάξιν. ἢ ἀμφοτέρως ὥσπερ στράτευμα; καὶ γὰρ ἐν τῇ τάξει τὸ εὖ καὶ ὁ στρατηγός, καὶ μᾶλλον οὗτος· οὐ γὰρ οὗτος διὰ τὴν τάξιν ἀλλʼ ἐκείνη διὰ τοῦτόν ἐστιν. πάντα δὲ συντέτακταί πως, ἀλλʼ οὐχ ὁμοίως, καὶ πλωτὰ καὶ πτηνὰ καὶ φυτά· καὶ οὐχ οὕτως ἔχει ὥστε μὴ εἶναι θατέρῳ πρὸς θάτερον μηδέν, ἀλλʼ ἔστι τι. πρὸς μὲν γὰρ ἓν ἅπαντα συντέτακται, ἀλλʼ ὥσπερ ἐν οἰκίᾳ τοῖς ἐλευθέροις ἥκιστα ἔξεστιν ὅ τι ἔτυχε ποιεῖν, ἀλλὰ πάντα ἢ τὰ πλεῖστα τέτακται, τοῖς δὲ ἀνδραπόδοις καὶ τοῖς θηρίοις μικρὸν τὸ εἰς τὸ κοινόν, τὸ δὲ πολὺ ὅ τι ἔτυχεν· τοιαύτη γὰρ ἑκάστου ἀρχὴ αὐτῶν ἡ φύσις ἐστίν. λέγω δʼ οἷον εἴς γε τὸ διακριθῆναι ἀνάγκη ἅπασιν ἐλθεῖν, καὶ ἄλλα οὕτως ἔστιν ὧν κοινωνεῖ ἅπαντα εἰς τὸ ὅλον.—ὅσα δὲ ἀδύνατα συμβαίνει ἢ ἄτοπα τοῖς ἄλλως λέγουσι, καὶ ποῖα οἱ χαριεστέρως λέγοντες, καὶ ἐπὶ ποίων ἐλάχισται ἀπορίαι, δεῖ μὴ λανθάνειν. πάντες γὰρ ἐξ ἐναντίων ποιοῦσι πάντα. οὔτε δὲ τὸ πάντα οὔτε τὸ ἐξ ἐναντίων ὀρθῶς, οὔτʼ ἐν ὅσοις τὰ ἐναντία ὑπάρχει, πῶς ἐκ τῶν ἐναντίων ἔσται, οὐ λέγουσιν· ἀπαθῆ γὰρ τὰ ἐναντία ὑπʼ ἀλλήλων. ἡμῖν δὲ λύεται τοῦτο εὐλόγως τῷ τρίτον τι εἶναι. οἱ δὲ τὸ ἕτερον τῶν ἐναντίων ὕλην ποιοῦσιν, ὥσπερ οἱ τὸ ἄνισον τῷ ἴσῳ ἢ τῷ ἑνὶ τὰ πολλά. λύεται δὲ καὶ τοῦτο τὸν αὐτὸν τρόπον· ἡ γὰρ ὕλη ἡ μία οὐδενὶ ἐναντίον. ἔτι ἅπαντα τοῦ φαύλου μεθέξει ἔξω τοῦ ἑνός· τὸ γὰρ κακὸν αὐτὸ θάτερον τῶν στοιχείων. οἱ δʼ ἄλλοι οὐδʼ ἀρχὰς τὸ ἀγαθὸν καὶ τὸ κακόν· καίτοι ἐν ἅπασι μάλιστα τὸ ἀγαθὸν ἀρχή. οἱ δὲ τοῦτο μὲν ὀρθῶς ὅτι ἀρχήν, ἀλλὰ πῶς τὸ ἀγαθὸν ἀρχὴ οὐ λέγουσιν, πότερον ὡς τέλος ἢ ὡς κινῆσαν ἢ ὡς εἶδος.  ἀτόπως δὲ καὶ Ἐμπεδοκλῆς· τὴν γὰρ φιλίαν ποιεῖ τὸ ἀγαθόν, αὕτη δʼ ἀρχὴ καὶ ὡς κινοῦσα (συνάγει γάρ) καὶ ὡς ὕλη· μόριον γὰρ τοῦ μίγματος. εἰ δὴ καὶ τῷ αὐτῷ συμβέβηκεν καὶ ὡς ὕλῃ ἀρχῇ εἶναι καὶ ὡς κινοῦντι, ἀλλὰ τό γʼ εἶναι οὐ ταὐτό. κατὰ πότερον οὖν φιλία; ἄτοπον δὲ καὶ τὸ ἄφθαρτον εἶναι τὸ νεῖκος· τοῦτο δʼ ἐστὶν αὐτῷ ἡ τοῦ κακοῦ φύσις. Ἀναξαγόρας δὲ ὡς κινοῦν τὸ ἀγαθὸν ἀρχήν· ὁ γὰρ νοῦς κινεῖ. ἀλλὰ κινεῖ ἕνεκά τινος, ὥστε ἕτερον, πλὴν ὡς ἡμεῖς λέγομεν· ἡ γὰρ ἰατρική ἐστί πως ἡ ὑγίεια. ἄτοπον δὲ καὶ τὸ ἐναντίον μὴ ποιῆσαι τῷ ἀγαθῷ καὶ τῷ νῷ. πάντες δʼ οἱ τἀναντία λέγοντες οὐ χρῶνται τοῖς ἐναντίοις, ἐὰν μὴ ῥυθμίσῃ τις. καὶ διὰ τί τὰ μὲν φθαρτὰ τὰ δʼ ἄφθαρτα, οὐδεὶς λέγει· πάντα γὰρ τὰ ὄντα ποιοῦσιν ἐκ τῶν αὐτῶν ἀρχῶν. ἔτι οἱ μὲν ἐκ τοῦ μὴ ὄντος ποιοῦσι τὰ ὄντα· οἱ δʼ ἵνα μὴ τοῦτο ἀναγκασθῶσιν, ἓν πάντα ποιοῦσιν.—ἔτι διὰ τί ἀεὶ ἔσται γένεσις καὶ τί αἴτιον γενέσεως, οὐδεὶς λέγει. καὶ τοῖς δύο ἀρχὰς ποιοῦσιν ἄλλην ἀνάγκη ἀρχὴν κυριωτέραν εἶναι, καὶ τοῖς τὰ εἴδη ἔτι ἄλλη ἀρχὴ κυριωτέρα· διὰ τί γὰρ μετέσχεν ἢ μετέχει; καὶ τοῖς μὲν ἄλλοις ἀνάγκη τῇ σοφίᾳ καὶ τῇ τιμιωτάτῃ ἐπιστήμῃ εἶναί τι ἐναντίον, ἡμῖν δʼ οὔ. οὐ γάρ ἐστιν ἐναντίον τῷ πρώτῳ οὐδέν· πάντα γὰρ τὰ ἐναντία ὕλην ἔχει, καὶ δυνάμει ταῦτα ἔστιν· ἡ δὲ ἐναντία ἄγνοια εἰς τὸ ἐναντίον, τῷ δὲ πρώτῳ ἐναντίον οὐδέν. εἴ τε μὴ ἔσται παρὰ τὰ αἰσθητὰ ἄλλα, οὐκ ἔσται ἀρχὴ καὶ τάξις καὶ γένεσις καὶ τὰ οὐράνια, ἀλλʼ ἀεὶ τῆς ἀρχῆς ἀρχή, ὥσπερ τοῖς θεολόγοις καὶ τοῖς φυσικοῖς πᾶσιν. εἰ δʼ ἔσται τὰ εἴδη· ἢ οἱ ἀριθμοί, οὐδενὸς αἴτια· εἰ δὲ μή, οὔτι κινήσεώς γε. ἔτι πῶς ἔσται ἐξ ἀμεγεθῶν μέγεθος καὶ συνεχές; ὁ γὰρ ἀριθμὸς οὐ ποιήσει συνεχές, οὔτε ὡς κινοῦν οὔτε ὡς εἶδος. ἀλλὰ μὴν οὐδέν γʼ ἔσται τῶν ἐναντίων ὅπερ καὶ ποιητικὸν καὶ κινητικόν; ἐνδέχοιτο γὰρ ἂν μὴ εἶναι. ἀλλὰ μὴν ὕστερόν γε τὸ ποιεῖν δυνάμεως. οὐκ ἄρα ἀΐδια τὰ ὄντα. ἀλλʼ ἔστιν· ἀναιρετέον ἄρα τούτων τι. τοῦτο δʼ εἴρηται πῶς. ἔτι τίνι οἱ ἀριθμοὶ ἓν ἢ ἡ ψυχὴ καὶ τὸ σῶμα καὶ ὅλως τὸ εἶδος καὶ τὸ πρᾶγμα, οὐδὲν λέγει οὐδείς· οὐδʼ ἐνδέχεται εἰπεῖν, ἐὰν μὴ ὡς ἡμεῖς εἴπῃ, ὡς τὸ κινοῦν ποιεῖ. οἱ δὲ λέγοντες τὸν ἀριθμὸν πρῶτον τὸν μαθηματικὸν καὶ οὕτως ἀεὶ ἄλλην ἐχομένην οὐσίαν καὶ ἀρχὰς ἑκάστης ἄλλας,  ἐπεισοδιώδη τὴν τοῦ παντὸς οὐσίαν ποιοῦσιν (οὐδὲν γὰρ ἡ ἑτέρα τῇ ἑτέρᾳ συμβάλλεται οὖσα ἢ μὴ οὖσα) καὶ ἀρχὰς πολλάς· τὰ δὲ ὄντα οὐ βούλεται πολιτεύεσθαι κακῶς. οὐκ ἀγαθὸν πολυκοιρανίη· εἷς κοίρανος ἔστω.","paragraphs":[{"id":"RAW_DAZY_BO_4:P0001","text":"περὶ τῆς οὐσίας ἡ θεωρία· τῶν γὰρ οὐσιῶν αἱ ἀρχαὶ καὶ τὰ αἴτια ζητοῦνται. καὶ γὰρ εἰ ὡς ὅλον τι τὸ πᾶν, ἡ οὐσία πρῶτον μέρος· καὶ εἰ τῷ ἐφεξῆς, κἂν οὕτως πρῶτον ἡ οὐσία, εἶτα τὸ ποιόν, εἶτα τὸ ποσόν. ἅμα δὲ οὐδʼ ὄντα ὡς εἰπεῖν ἁπλῶς ταῦτα, ἀλλὰ ποιότητες καὶ κινήσεις, ἢ καὶ τὸ οὐ λευκὸν καὶ τὸ οὐκ εὐθύ· λέγομεν γοῦν εἶναι καὶ ταῦτα, οἷον ἔστιν οὐ λευκόν. ἔτι οὐδὲν τῶν ἄλλων χωριστόν. μαρτυροῦσι δὲ καὶ οἱ ἀρχαῖοι ἔργῳ· τῆς γὰρ οὐσίας ἐζήτουν ἀρχὰς καὶ στοιχεῖα καὶ αἴτια. οἱ μὲν οὖν νῦν τὰ καθόλου οὐσίας μᾶλλον τιθέασιν (τὰ γὰρ γένη καθόλου, ἅ φασιν ἀρχὰς καὶ οὐσίας εἶναι μᾶλλον διὰ τὸ λογικῶς ζητεῖν)· οἱ δὲ πάλαι τὰ καθʼ ἕκαστα, οἷον πῦρ καὶ γῆν, ἀλλʼ οὐ τὸ κοινόν, σῶμα.","kind":"passage"},{"id":"RAW_DAZY_BO_4:P0002","text":"οὐσίαι δὲ τρεῖς, μία μὲν αἰσθητή—ἧς ἡ μὲν ἀΐδιος ἡ δὲ φθαρτή, ἣν πάντες ὁμολογοῦσιν, οἷον τὰ φυτὰ καὶ τὰ ζῷα —ἧς ἀνάγκη τὰ στοιχεῖα λαβεῖν, εἴτε ἓν εἴτε πολλά· ἄλλη δὲ ἀκίνητος, καὶ ταύτην φασί τινες εἶναι χωριστήν, οἱ μὲν εἰς δύο διαιροῦντες, οἱ δὲ εἰς μίαν φύσιν τιθέντες τὰ εἴδη καὶ τὰ μαθηματικά, οἱ δὲ τὰ μαθηματικὰ μόνον τούτων. ἐκεῖναι μὲν δὴ φυσικῆς (μετὰ κινήσεως γάρ),  αὕτη δὲ ἑτέρας, εἰ μηδεμία αὐτοῖς ἀρχὴ κοινή.","kind":"passage"},{"id":"RAW_DAZY_BO_4:P0003","text":"ἡ δʼ αἰσθητὴ οὐσία μεταβλητή. εἰ δʼ ἡ μεταβολὴ ἐκ τῶν ἀντικειμένων ἢ τῶν μεταξύ, ἀντικειμένων δὲ μὴ πάντων (οὐ λευκὸν γὰρ ἡ φωνή) ἀλλʼ ἐκ τοῦ ἐναντίου, ἀνάγκη ὑπεῖναί τι τὸ μεταβάλλον εἰς τὴν ἐναντίωσιν· οὐ γὰρ τὰ ἐναντία μεταβάλλει.","kind":"passage"},{"id":"RAW_DAZY_BO_4:P0004","text":"ἔτι τὸ μὲν ὑπομένει, τὸ δʼ ἐναντίον οὐχ ὑπομένει· ἔστιν ἄρα τι τρίτον παρὰ τὰ ἐναντία, ἡ ὕλη. εἰ δὴ αἱ μεταβολαὶ τέτταρες, ἢ κατὰ τὸ τί ἢ κατὰ τὸ ποῖον ἢ πόσον ἢ ποῦ, καὶ γένεσις μὲν ἡ ἁπλῆ καὶ φθορὰ ἡ κατὰ τὸ τόδε, αὔξησις δὲ καὶ φθίσις ἡ κατὰ τὸ ποσόν, ἀλλοίωσις δὲ ἡ κατὰ τὸ πάθος, φορὰ δὲ ἡ κατὰ τόπον, εἰς ἐναντιώσεις ἂν εἶεν τὰς καθʼ ἕκαστον αἱ μεταβολαί. ἀνάγκη δὴ μεταβάλλειν τὴν ὕλην δυναμένην ἄμφω· ἐπεὶ δὲ διττὸν τὸ ὄν, μεταβάλλει πᾶν ἐκ τοῦ δυνάμει ὄντος εἰς τὸ ἐνεργείᾳ ὄν (οἷον ἐκ λευκοῦ δυνάμει εἰς τὸ ἐνεργείᾳ λευκόν, ὁμοίως δὲ καὶ ἐπʼ αὐξήσεως καὶ φθίσεως), ὥστε οὐ μόνον κατὰ συμβεβηκὸς ἐνδέχεται γίγνεσθαι ἐκ μὴ ὄντος, ἀλλὰ καὶ ἐξ ὄντος γίγνεται πάντα, δυνάμει μέντοι ὄντος, ἐκ μὴ ὄντος δὲ ἐνεργείᾳ.","kind":"passage"},{"id":"RAW_DAZY_BO_4:P0005","text":"καὶ τοῦτʼ ἔστι τὸ Ἀναξαγόρου ἕν· βέλτιον γὰρ ἢ ὁμοῦ πάντα —καὶ Ἐμπεδοκλέους τὸ μῖγμα καὶ Ἀναξιμάνδρου, καὶ ὡς Δημόκριτός φησιν—\"ἦν ὁμοῦ πάντα δυνάμει, ἐνεργείᾳ δʼ οὔ\"· ὥστε τῆς ὕλης ἂν εἶεν ἡμμένοι· πάντα δʼ ὕλην ἔχει ὅσα μεταβάλλει, ἀλλʼ ἑτέραν· καὶ τῶν ἀϊδίων ὅσα μὴ γενητὰ κινητὰ δὲ φορᾷ, ἀλλʼ οὐ γενητὴν ἀλλὰ ποθὲν ποί. ἀπορήσειε δʼ ἄν τις ἐκ ποίου μὴ ὄντος ἡ γένεσις· τριχῶς γὰρ τὸ μὴ ὄν. εἰ δή τι ἔστι δυνάμει, ἀλλʼ ὅμως οὐ τοῦ τυχόντος ἀλλʼ ἕτερον ἐξ ἑτέρου· οὐδʼ ἱκανὸν ὅτι ὁμοῦ πάντα χρήματα· διαφέρει γὰρ τῇ ὕλῃ, ἐπεὶ διὰ τί ἄπειρα ἐγένετο ἀλλʼ οὐχ ἕν; ὁ γὰρ νοῦς εἷς, ὥστʼ εἰ καὶ ἡ ὕλη μία, ἐκεῖνο ἐγένετο ἐνεργείᾳ οὗ ἡ ὕλη ἦν δυνάμει. τρία δὴ τὰ αἴτια καὶ τρεῖς αἱ ἀρχαί, δύο μὲν ἡ ἐναντίωσις, ἧς τὸ μὲν λόγος καὶ εἶδος τὸ δὲ στέρησις, τὸ δὲ τρίτον ἡ ὕλη.","kind":"passage"},{"id":"RAW_DAZY_BO_4:P0006","text":"μετὰ ταῦτα ὅτι οὐ γίγνεται οὔτε ἡ ὕλη οὔτε τὸ εἶδος, λέγω δὲ τὰ ἔσχατα. πᾶν γὰρ μεταβάλλει τὶ καὶ ὑπό τινος καὶ εἴς τι·  ὑφʼ οὗ μέν, τοῦ πρώτου κινοῦντος· ὃ δέ, ἡ ὕλη· εἰς ὃ δέ, τὸ εἶδος. εἰς ἄπειρον οὖν εἶσιν, εἰ μὴ μόνον ὁ χαλκὸς γίγνεται στρογγύλος ἀλλὰ καὶ τὸ στρογγύλον ἢ ὁ χαλκός· ἀνάγκη δὴ στῆναι.—μετὰ ταῦτα ὅτι ἑκάστη ἐκ συνωνύμου γίγνεται οὐσία (τὰ γὰρ φύσει οὐσίαι καὶ τὰ ἄλλα). ἢ γὰρ τέχνῃ ἢ φύσει γίγνεται ἢ τύχῃ ἢ τῷ αὐτομάτῳ. ἡ μὲν οὖν τέχνη ἀρχὴ ἐν ἄλλῳ, ἡ δὲ φύσις ἀρχὴ ἐν αὐτῷ (ἄνθρωπος γὰρ ἄνθρωπον γεννᾷ), αἱ δὲ λοιπαὶ αἰτίαι στερήσεις τούτων. οὐσίαι δὲ τρεῖς, ἡ μὲν ὕλη τόδε τι οὖσα τῷ φαίνεσθαι (ὅσα γὰρ ἁφῇ καὶ μὴ συμφύσει, ὕλη καὶ ὑποκείμενον), ἡ δὲ φύσις τόδε τι καὶ ἕξις τις εἰς ἥν· ἔτι τρίτη ἡ ἐκ τούτων ἡ καθʼ ἕκαστα, οἷον Σωκράτης ἢ Καλλίας.","kind":"passage"},{"id":"RAW_DAZY_BO_4:P0007","text":"ἐπὶ μὲν οὖν τινῶν τὸ τόδε τι οὐκ ἔστι παρὰ τὴν συνθετὴν οὐσίαν, οἷον οἰκίας τὸ εἶδος, εἰ μὴ ἡ τέχνη (οὐδʼ ἔστι γένεσις καὶ φθορὰ τούτων, ἀλλʼ ἄλλον τρόπον εἰσὶ καὶ οὐκ εἰσὶν οἰκία τε ἡ ἄνευ ὕλης καὶ ὑγίεια καὶ πᾶν τὸ κατὰ τέχνην), ἀλλʼ εἴπερ, ἐπὶ τῶν φύσει· διὸ δὴ οὐ κακῶς Πλάτων ἔφη ὅτι εἴδη ἔστιν ὁπόσα φύσει, εἴπερ ἔστιν εἴδη ἄλλα τούτων οἷον πῦρ σὰρξ κεφαλή· ἅπαντα γὰρ ὕλη ἐστί, καὶ τῆς μάλιστʼ οὐσίας ἡ τελευταία. τὰ μὲν οὖν κινοῦντα αἴτια ὡς προγεγενημένα ὄντα, τὰ δʼ ὡς ὁ λόγος ἅμα. ὅτε γὰρ ὑγιαίνει ὁ ἄνθρωπος, τότε καὶ ἡ ὑγίεια ἔστιν, καὶ τὸ σχῆμα τῆς χαλκῆς σφαίρας ἅμα καὶ ἡ χαλκῆ σφαῖρα (εἰ δὲ καὶ ὕστερόν τι ὑπομένει, σκεπτέον· ἐπʼ ἐνίων γὰρ οὐδὲν κωλύει, οἷον εἰ ἡ ψυχὴ τοιοῦτον, μὴ πᾶσα ἀλλʼ ὁ νοῦς· πᾶσαν γὰρ ἀδύνατον ἴσως).","kind":"passage"},{"id":"RAW_DAZY_BO_4:P0008","text":"φανερὸν δὴ ὅτι οὐδὲν δεῖ διά γε ταῦτʼ εἶναι τὰς ἰδέας· ἄνθρωπος γὰρ ἄνθρωπον γεννᾷ, ὁ καθʼ ἕκαστον τὸν τινά· ὁμοίως δὲ καὶ ἐπὶ τῶν τεχνῶν· ἡ γὰρ ἰατρικὴ τέχνη ὁ λόγος τῆς ὑγιείας ἐστίν.","kind":"passage"},{"id":"RAW_DAZY_BO_4:P0009","text":"τὰ δʼ αἴτια καὶ αἱ ἀρχαὶ ἄλλα ἄλλων ἔστιν ὥς, ἔστι δʼ ὡς, ἂν καθόλου λέγῃ τις καὶ κατʼ ἀναλογίαν, ταὐτὰ πάντων. ἀπορήσειε γὰρ ἄν τις πότερον ἕτεραι ἢ αἱ αὐταὶ ἀρχαὶ καὶ στοιχεῖα τῶν οὐσιῶν καὶ τῶν πρός τι, καὶ καθʼ ἑκάστην δὴ τῶν κατηγοριῶν ὁμοίως. ἀλλʼ ἄτοπον εἰ ταὐτὰ πάντων· ἐκ τῶν αὐτῶν γὰρ ἔσται τὰ πρός τι καὶ αἱ οὐσίαι.  τί οὖν τοῦτʼ ἔσται; παρὰ γὰρ τὴν οὐσίαν καὶ τἆλλα τὰ κατηγορούμενα οὐδέν ἐστι κοινόν, πρότερον δὲ τὸ στοιχεῖον ἢ ὧν στοιχεῖον· ἀλλὰ μὴν οὐδʼ ἡ οὐσία στοιχεῖον τῶν πρός τι, οὐδὲ τούτων οὐδὲν τῆς οὐσίας. ἔτι πῶς ἐνδέχεται πάντων εἶναι ταὐτὰ στοιχεῖα; οὐδὲν γὰρ οἷόν τʼ εἶναι τῶν στοιχείων τῷ ἐκ στοιχείων συγκειμένῳ τὸ αὐτό, οἷον τῷ ΒΑ τὸ Β ἢ Α (οὐδὲ δὴ τῶν νοητῶν στοιχεῖόν ἐστιν, οἷον τὸ ὂν ἢ τὸ ἕν· ὑπάρχει γὰρ ταῦτα ἑκάστῳ καὶ τῶν συνθέτων). οὐδὲν ἄρʼ ἔσται αὐτῶν οὔτʼ οὐσία οὔτε πρός τι· ἀλλʼ ἀναγκαῖον.","kind":"passage"},{"id":"RAW_DAZY_BO_4:P0010","text":"οὐκ ἔστιν ἄρα πάντων ταὐτὰ στοιχεῖα.—ἢ ὥσπερ λέγομεν, ἔστι μὲν ὥς, ἔστι δʼ ὡς οὔ, οἷον ἴσως τῶν αἰσθητῶν σωμάτων ὡς μὲν εἶδος τὸ θερμὸν καὶ ἄλλον τρόπον τὸ ψυχρὸν ἡ στέρησις, ὕλη δὲ τὸ δυνάμει ταῦτα πρῶτον καθʼ αὑτό, οὐσίαι δὲ ταῦτά τε καὶ τὰ ἐκ τούτων, ὧν ἀρχαὶ ταῦτα, ἢ εἴ τι ἐκ θερμοῦ καὶ ψυχροῦ γίγνεται ἕν, οἷον σὰρξ ἢ ὀστοῦν· ἕτερον γὰρ ἀνάγκη ἐκείνων εἶναι τὸ γενόμενον. τούτων μὲν οὖν ταὐτὰ στοιχεῖα καὶ ἀρχαί (ἄλλων δʼ ἄλλα), πάντων δὲ οὕτω μὲν εἰπεῖν οὐκ ἔστιν, τῷ ἀνάλογον δέ, ὥσπερ εἴ τις εἴποι ὅτι ἀρχαὶ εἰσὶ τρεῖς, τὸ εἶδος καὶ ἡ στέρησις καὶ ἡ ὕλη. ἀλλʼ ἕκαστον τούτων ἕτερον περὶ ἕκαστον γένος ἐστίν, οἷον ἐν χρώματι λευκὸν μέλαν ἐπιφάνεια· φῶς σκότος ἀήρ, ἐκ δὲ τούτων ἡμέρα καὶ νύξ.","kind":"passage"},{"id":"RAW_DAZY_BO_4:P0011","text":"ἐπεὶ δὲ οὐ μόνον τὰ ἐνυπάρχοντα αἴτια, ἀλλὰ καὶ τῶν ἐκτὸς οἷον τὸ κινοῦν, δῆλον ὅτι ἕτερον ἀρχὴ καὶ στοιχεῖον, αἴτια δʼ ἄμφω, καὶ εἰς ταῦτα διαιρεῖται ἡ ἀρχή, τὸ δʼ ὡς κινοῦν ἢ ἱστὰν ἀρχή τις καὶ οὐσία, ὥστε στοιχεῖα μὲν κατʼ ἀναλογίαν τρία, αἰτίαι δὲ καὶ ἀρχαὶ τέτταρες· ἄλλο δʼ ἐν ἄλλῳ, καὶ τὸ πρῶτον αἴτιον ὡς κινοῦν ἄλλο ἄλλῳ. ὑγίεια, νόσος, σῶμα· τὸ κινοῦν ἰατρική. εἶδος, ἀταξία τοιαδί, πλίνθοι· τὸ κινοῦν οἰκοδομική . ἐπεὶ δὲ τὸ κινοῦν ἐν μὲν τοῖς φυσικοῖς ἀνθρώπῳ ἄνθρωπος, ἐν δὲ τοῖς ἀπὸ διανοίας τὸ εἶδος ἢ τὸ ἐναντίον, τρόπον τινὰ τρία αἴτια ἂν εἴη, ὡδὶ δὲ τέτταρα. ὑγίεια γάρ πως ἡ ἰατρική, καὶ οἰκίας εἶδος ἡ οἰκοδομική, καὶ ἄνθρωπος ἄνθρωπον γεννᾷ· ἔτι παρὰ ταῦτα τὸ ὡς πρῶτον πάντων κινοῦν πάντα.","kind":"passage"},{"id":"RAW_DAZY_BO_4:P0012","text":"ἐπεὶ δʼ ἐστὶ τὰ μὲν χωριστὰ τὰ δʼ οὐ χωριστά, οὐσίαι ἐκεῖνα.  καὶ διὰ τοῦτο πάντων αἴτια ταὐτά, ὅτι τῶν οὐσιῶν ἄνευ οὐκ ἔστι τὰ πάθη καὶ αἱ κινήσεις. ἔπειτα ἔσται ταῦτα ψυχὴ ἴσως καὶ σῶμα, ἢ νοῦς καὶ ὄρεξις καὶ σῶμα.—ἔτι δʼ ἄλλον τρόπον τῷ ἀνάλογον ἀρχαὶ αἱ αὐταί, οἷον ἐνέργεια καὶ δύναμις· ἀλλὰ καὶ ταῦτα ἄλλα τε ἄλλοις καὶ ἄλλως.","kind":"passage"},{"id":"RAW_DAZY_BO_4:P0013","text":"ἐν ἐνίοις μὲν γὰρ τὸ αὐτὸ ὁτὲ μὲν ἐνεργείᾳ ἔστιν ὁτὲ δὲ δυνάμει, οἷον οἶνος ἢ σὰρξ ἢ ἄνθρωπος (πίπτει δὲ καὶ ταῦτα εἰς τὰ εἰρημένα αἴτια· ἐνεργείᾳ μὲν γὰρ τὸ εἶδος, ἐὰν ᾖ χωριστόν, καὶ τὸ ἐξ ἀμφοῖν στέρησις δέ, οἷον σκότος ἢ κάμνον, δυνάμει δὲ ἡ ὕλη· τοῦτο γάρ ἐστι τὸ δυνάμενον γίγνεσθαι ἄμφω)· ἄλλως δʼ ἐνεργείᾳ καὶ δυνάμει διαφέρει ὧν μὴ ἔστιν ἡ αὐτὴ ὕλη, ὧν ἐνίων οὐκ ἔστι τὸ αὐτὸ εἶδος ἀλλʼ ἕτερον, ὥσπερ ἀνθρώπου αἴτιον τά τε στοιχεῖα, πῦρ καὶ γῆ ὡς ὕλη καὶ τὸ ἴδιον εἶδος, καὶ ἔτι τι ἄλλο ἔξω οἷον ὁ πατήρ, καὶ παρὰ ταῦτα ὁ ἥλιος καὶ ὁ λοξὸς κύκλος, οὔτε ὕλη ὄντα οὔτʼ εἶδος οὔτε στέρησις οὔτε ὁμοειδὲς ἀλλὰ κινοῦντα. ἔτι δὲ ὁρᾶν δεῖ ὅτι τὰ μὲν καθόλου ἔστιν εἰπεῖν, τὰ δʼ οὔ. πάντων δὴ πρῶται ἀρχαὶ τὸ ἐνεργείᾳ πρῶτον τοδὶ καὶ ἄλλο ὃ δυνάμει.","kind":"passage"},{"id":"RAW_DAZY_BO_4:P0014","text":"ἐκεῖνα μὲν οὖν τὰ καθόλου οὐκ ἔστιν· ἀρχὴ γὰρ τὸ καθʼ ἕκαστον τῶν καθʼ ἕκαστον· ἄνθρωπος μὲν γὰρ ἀνθρώπου καθόλου, ἀλλʼ οὐκ ἔστιν οὐδείς, ἀλλὰ Πηλεὺς Ἀχιλλέως σοῦ δὲ ὁ πατήρ, καὶ τοδὶ τὸ Β τουδὶ τοῦ ΒΑ, ὅλως δὲ τὸ Β τοῦ ἁπλῶς ΒΑ. ἔπειτα, εἰ δὴ τὰ τῶν οὐσιῶν, ἄλλα δὲ ἄλλων αἴτια καὶ στοιχεῖα, ὥσπερ ἐλέχθη, τῶν μὴ ἐν ταὐτῷ γένει, χρωμάτων ψόφων οὐσιῶν ποσότητος, πλὴν τῷ ἀνάλογον· καὶ τῶν ἐν ταὐτῷ εἴδει ἕτερα, οὐκ εἴδει ἀλλʼ ὅτι τῶν καθʼ ἕκαστον ἄλλο, ἥ τε σὴ ὕλη καὶ τὸ εἶδος καὶ τὸ κινῆσαν καὶ ἡ ἐμή, τῷ καθόλου δὲ λόγῳ ταὐτά.","kind":"passage"},{"id":"RAW_DAZY_BO_4:P0015","text":"τὸ δὲ ζητεῖν τίνες ἀρχαὶ ἢ στοιχεῖα τῶν οὐσιῶν καὶ πρός τι καὶ ποιῶν, πότερον αἱ αὐταὶ ἢ ἕτεραι, δῆλον ὅτι πολλαχῶς γε λεγομένων ἔστιν ἑκάστου, διαιρεθέντων δὲ οὐ ταὐτὰ ἀλλʼ ἕτερα, πλὴν ὡδὶ καὶ πάντων, ὡδὶ μὲν ταὐτὰ ἢ τὸ ἀνάλογον, ὅτι ὕλη, εἶδος, στέρησις, τὸ κινοῦν, καὶ ὡδὶ τὰ τῶν οὐσιῶν αἴτια ὡς αἴτια πάντων, ὅτι ἀναιρεῖται ἀναιρουμένων· ἔτι τὸ πρῶτον ἐντελεχείᾳ· ὡδὶ δὲ ἕτερα πρῶτα ὅσα τὰ ἐναντία ἃ μήτε ὡς γένη λέγεται μήτε πολλαχῶς λέγεται· καὶ ἔτι αἱ ὗλαι.  τίνες μὲν οὖν αἱ ἀρχαὶ τῶν αἰσθητῶν καὶ πόσαι, καὶ πῶς αἱ αὐταὶ καὶ πῶς ἕτεραι, εἴρηται.","kind":"passage"},{"id":"RAW_DAZY_BO_4:P0016","text":"ἐπεὶ δʼ ἦσαν τρεῖς οὐσίαι, δύο μὲν αἱ φυσικαὶ μία δʼ ἡ ἀκίνητος, περὶ ταύτης λεκτέον ὅτι ἀνάγκη εἶναι ἀΐδιόν τινα οὐσίαν ἀκίνητον. αἵ τε γὰρ οὐσίαι πρῶται τῶν ὄντων, καὶ εἰ πᾶσαι φθαρταί, πάντα φθαρτά· ἀλλʼ ἀδύνατον κίνησιν ἢ γενέσθαι ἢ φθαρῆναι (ἀεὶ γὰρ ἦν), οὐδὲ χρόνον. οὐ γὰρ οἷόν τε τὸ πρότερον καὶ ὕστερον εἶναι μὴ ὄντος χρόνου· καὶ ἡ κίνησις ἄρα οὕτω συνεχὴς ὥσπερ καὶ ὁ χρόνος· ἢ γὰρ τὸ αὐτὸ ἢ κινήσεώς τι πάθος. κίνησις δʼ οὐκ ἔστι συνεχὴς ἀλλʼ ἢ ἡ κατὰ τόπον, καὶ ταύτης ἡ κύκλῳ.","kind":"passage"},{"id":"RAW_DAZY_BO_4:P0017","text":"ἀλλὰ μὴν εἰ ἔστι κινητικὸν ἢ ποιητικόν, μὴ ἐνεργοῦν δέ τι, οὐκ ἔσται κίνησις· ἐνδέχεται γὰρ τὸ δύναμιν ἔχον μὴ ἐνεργεῖν. οὐθὲν ἄρα ὄφελος οὐδʼ ἐὰν οὐσίας ποιήσωμεν ἀϊδίους, ὥσπερ οἱ τὰ εἴδη, εἰ μή τις δυναμένη ἐνέσται ἀρχὴ μεταβάλλειν· οὐ τοίνυν οὐδʼ αὕτη ἱκανή, οὐδʼ ἄλλη οὐσία παρὰ τὰ εἴδη· εἰ γὰρ μὴ ἐνεργήσει, οὐκ ἔσται κίνησις. ἔτι οὐδʼ εἰ ἐνεργήσει, ἡ δʼ οὐσία αὐτῆς δύναμις· οὐ γὰρ ἔσται κίνησις ἀΐδιος· ἐνδέχεται γὰρ τὸ δυνάμει ὂν μὴ εἶναι. δεῖ ἄρα εἶναι ἀρχὴν τοιαύτην ἧς ἡ οὐσία ἐνέργεια. ἔτι τοίνυν ταύτας δεῖ τὰς οὐσίας εἶναι ἄνευ ὕλης· ἀϊδίους γὰρ δεῖ, εἴπερ γε καὶ ἄλλο τι ἀΐδιον. ἐνέργεια ἄρα. καίτοι ἀπορία· δοκεῖ γὰρ τὸ μὲν ἐνεργοῦν πᾶν δύνασθαι τὸ δὲ δυνάμενον οὐ πᾶν ἐνεργεῖν, ὥστε πρότερον εἶναι τὴν δύναμιν. ἀλλὰ μὴν εἰ τοῦτο, οὐθὲν ἔσται τῶν ὄντων· ἐνδέχεται γὰρ δύνασθαι μὲν εἶναι μήπω δʼ εἶναι.","kind":"passage"},{"id":"RAW_DAZY_BO_4:P0018","text":"καίτοι εἰ ὡς λέγουσιν οἱ θεολόγοι οἱ ἐκ νυκτὸς γεννῶντες, ἢ ὡς οἱ φυσικοὶ ὁμοῦ πάντα χρήματά φασι, τὸ αὐτὸ ἀδύνατον. πῶς γὰρ κινηθήσεται, εἰ μὴ ἔσται ἐνεργείᾳ τι αἴτιον; οὐ γὰρ ἥ γε ὕλη κινήσει αὐτὴ ἑαυτήν, ἀλλὰ τεκτονική, οὐδὲ τὰ ἐπιμήνια οὐδʼ ἡ γῆ, ἀλλὰ τὰ σπέρματα καὶ ἡ γονή. διὸ ἔνιοι ποιοῦσιν ἀεὶ ἐνέργειαν, οἷον Λεύκιππος καὶ Πλάτων· ἀεὶ γὰρ εἶναί φασι κίνησιν. ἀλλὰ διὰ τί καὶ τίνα οὐ λέγουσιν, οὐδʼ, εἰ ὡδὶ ἢ ὡδί, τὴν αἰτίαν. οὐδὲν γὰρ ὡς ἔτυχε κινεῖται, ἀλλὰ δεῖ τι ἀεὶ ὑπάρχειν, ἕσπερ νῦν φύσει μὲν ὡδί, βίᾳ δὲ ἢ ὑπὸ νοῦ ἢ ἄλλου ὡδί. (εἶτα ποία πρώτη; διαφέρει γὰρ ἀμήχανον ὅσον). ἀλλὰ μὴν οὐδὲ Πλάτωνί γε οἷόν τε λέγειν ἣν οἴεται ἐνίοτε ἀρχὴν εἶναι,  τὸ αὐτὸ ἑαυτὸ κινοῦν· ὕστερον γὰρ καὶ ἅμα τῷ οὐρανῷ ἡ ψυχή, ὡς φησίν.","kind":"passage"},{"id":"RAW_DAZY_BO_4:P0019","text":"τὸ μὲν δὴ δύναμιν οἴεσθαι ἐνεργείας πρότερον ἔστι μὲν ὡς καλῶς ἔστι δʼ ὡς οὔ (εἴρηται δὲ πῶς)· ὅτι δʼ ἐνέργεια πρότερον, μαρτυρεῖ Ἀναξαγόρας (ὁ γὰρ νοῦς ἐνέργεια) καὶ Ἐμπεδοκλῆς φιλίαν καὶ τὸ νεῖκος, καὶ οἱ ἀεὶ λέγοντες κίνησιν εἶναι, ὥσπερ Λεύκιππος· ὥστʼ οὐκ ἦν ἄπειρον χρόνον χάος ἢ νύξ, ἀλλὰ ταὐτὰ ἀεὶ ἢ περιόδῳ ἢ ἄλλως, εἴπερ πρότερον ἐνέργεια δυνάμεως. εἰ δὴ τὸ αὐτὸ ἀεὶ περιόδῳ, δεῖ τι ἀεὶ μένειν ὡσαύτως ἐνεργοῦν. εἰ δὲ μέλλει γένεσις καὶ φθορὰ εἶναι, ἄλλο δεῖ εἶναι ἀεὶ ἐνεργοῦν ἄλλως καὶ ἄλλως. ἀνάγκη ἄρα ὡδὶ μὲν καθʼ αὑτὸ ἐνεργεῖν ὡδὶ δὲ κατʼ ἄλλο· ἤτοι ἄρα καθʼ ἕτερον ἢ κατὰ τὸ πρῶτον. ἀνάγκη δὴ κατὰ τοῦτο· πάλιν γὰρ ἐκεῖνο αὐτῷ τε αἴτιον κἀκείνῳ. οὐκοῦν βέλτιον τὸ πρῶτον· καὶ γὰρ αἴτιον ἦν ἐκεῖνο τοῦ ἀεὶ ὡσαύτως· τοῦ δʼ ἄλλως ἕτερον, τοῦ δʼ ἀεὶ ἄλλως ἄμφω δηλονότι. οὐκοῦν οὕτως καὶ ἔχουσιν αἱ κινήσεις. τί οὖν ἄλλας δεῖ ζητεῖν ἀρχάς;","kind":"passage"},{"id":"RAW_DAZY_BO_4:P0020","text":"ἐπεὶ δʼ οὕτω τʼ ἐνδέχεται, καὶ εἰ μὴ οὕτως, ἐκ νυκτὸς ἔσται καὶ ὁμοῦ πάντων καὶ ἐκ μὴ ὄντος, λύοιτʼ ἂν ταῦτα, καὶ ἔστι τι ἀεὶ κινούμενον κίνησιν ἄπαυστον, αὕτη δʼ ἡ κύκλῳ (καὶ τοῦτο οὐ λόγῳ μόνον ἀλλʼ ἔργῳ δῆλον), ὥστʼ ἀΐδιος ἂν εἴη ὁ πρῶτος οὐρανός. ἔστι τοίνυν τι καὶ ὃ κινεῖ. ἐπεὶ δὲ τὸ κινούμενον καὶ κινοῦν  μέσον, †τοίνυν† ἔστι τι ὃ οὐ κινούμενον κινεῖ, ἀΐδιον καὶ οὐσία καὶ ἐνέργεια οὖσα. κινεῖ δὲ ὧδε τὸ ὀρεκτὸν καὶ τὸ νοητόν· κινεῖ οὐ κινούμενα. τούτων τὰ πρῶτα τὰ αὐτά. ἐπιθυμητὸν μὲν γὰρ τὸ φαινόμενον καλόν, βουλητὸν δὲ πρῶτον τὸ ὂν καλόν· ὀρεγόμεθα δὲ διότι δοκεῖ μᾶλλον ἢ δοκεῖ διότι ὀρεγόμεθα· ἀρχὴ γὰρ ἡ νόησις. νοῦς δὲ ὑπὸ τοῦ νοητοῦ κινεῖται, νοητὴ δὲ ἡ ἑτέρα συστοιχία καθʼ αὑτήν· καὶ ταύτης ἡ οὐσία πρώτη, καὶ ταύτης ἡ ἁπλῆ καὶ κατʼ ἐνέργειαν (ἔστι δὲ τὸ ἓν καὶ τὸ ἁπλοῦν οὐ τὸ αὐτό· τὸ μὲν γὰρ ἓν μέτρον σημαίνει, τὸ δὲ ἁπλοῦν πὼς ἔχον αὐτό).","kind":"passage"},{"id":"RAW_DAZY_BO_4:P0021","text":"ἀλλὰ μὴν καὶ τὸ καλὸν καὶ τὸ διʼ αὑτὸ αἱρετὸν ἐν τῇ αὐτῇ συστοιχίᾳ· καὶ ἔστιν ἄριστον ἀεὶ ἢ ἀνάλογον τὸ πρῶτον.  ὅτι δʼ ἔστι τὸ οὗ ἕνεκα ἐν τοῖς ἀκινήτοις, ἡ διαίρεσις δηλοῖ· ἔστι γὰρ τινὶ τὸ οὗ ἕνεκα καὶ τινός, ὧν τὸ μὲν ἔστι τὸ δʼ οὐκ ἔστι. κινεῖ δὴ ὡς ἐρώμενον, κινούμενα δὲ τἆλλα κινεῖ. εἰ μὲν οὖν τι κινεῖται, ἐνδέχεται καὶ ἄλλως ἔχειν, ὥστʼ εἰ  φορὰ πρώτη ἡ ἐνέργειά ἐστιν, ᾗ κινεῖται ταύτῃ γε ἐνδέχεται ἄλλως ἔχειν, κατὰ τόπον, καὶ εἰ μὴ κατʼ οὐσίαν· ἐπεὶ δὲ ἔστι τι κινοῦν αὐτὸ ἀκίνητον ὄν, ἐνεργείᾳ ὄν, τοῦτο οὐκ ἐνδέχεται ἄλλως ἔχειν οὐδαμῶς. φορὰ γὰρ ἡ πρώτη τῶν μεταβολῶν, ταύτης δὲ ἡ κύκλῳ· ταύτην δὲ τοῦτο κινεῖ. ἐξ ἀνάγκης ἄρα ἐστὶν ὄν· καὶ ᾗ ἀνάγκῃ, καλῶς, καὶ οὕτως ἀρχή. τὸ γὰρ ἀναγκαῖον τοσαυταχῶς, τὸ μὲν βίᾳ ὅτι παρὰ τὴν ὁρμήν, τὸ δὲ οὗ οὐκ ἄνευ τὸ εὖ, τὸ δὲ μὴ ἐνδεχόμενον ἄλλως ἀλλʼ ἁπλῶς.—ἐκ τοιαύτης ἄρα ἀρχῆς ἤρτηται ὁ οὐρανὸς καὶ ἡ φύσις.","kind":"passage"},{"id":"RAW_DAZY_BO_4:P0022","text":"διαγωγὴ δʼ ἐστὶν οἵα ἡ ἀρίστη μικρὸν χρόνον ἡμῖν (οὕτω γὰρ ἀεὶ ἐκεῖνο· ἡμῖν μὲν γὰρ ἀδύνατον), ἐπεὶ καὶ ἡδονὴ ἡ ἐνέργεια τούτου (καὶ διὰ τοῦτο ἐγρήγορσις αἴσθησις νόησις ἥδιστον, ἐλπίδες δὲ καὶ μνῆμαι διὰ ταῦτα). ἡ δὲ νόησις ἡ καθʼ αὑτὴν τοῦ καθʼ αὑτὸ ἀρίστου, καὶ ἡ μάλιστα τοῦ μάλιστα. αὑτὸν δὲ νοεῖ ὁ νοῦς κατὰ μετάληψιν τοῦ νοητοῦ· νοητὸς γὰρ γίγνεται θιγγάνων καὶ νοῶν, ὥστε ταὐτὸν νοῦς καὶ νοητόν. τὸ γὰρ δεκτικὸν τοῦ νοητοῦ καὶ τῆς οὐσίας νοῦς, ἐνεργεῖ δὲ ἔχων, ὥστʼ ἐκείνου μᾶλλον τοῦτο ὃ δοκεῖ ὁ νοῦς θεῖον ἔχειν, καὶ ἡ θεωρία τὸ ἥδιστον καὶ ἄριστον. εἰ οὖν οὕτως εὖ ἔχει, ὡς ἡμεῖς ποτέ, ὁ θεὸς ἀεί, θαυμαστόν· εἰ δὲ μᾶλλον, ἔτι θαυμασιώτερον. ἔχει δὲ ὧδε. καὶ ζωὴ δέ γε ὑπάρχει· ἡ γὰρ νοῦ ἐνέργεια ζωή, ἐκεῖνος δὲ ἡ ἐνέργεια· ἐνέργεια δὲ ἡ καθʼ αὑτὴν ἐκείνου ζωὴ ἀρίστη καὶ ἀΐδιος.","kind":"passage"},{"id":"RAW_DAZY_BO_4:P0023","text":"φαμὲν δὴ τὸν θεὸν εἶναι ζῷον ἀΐδιον ἄριστον, ὥστε ζωὴ καὶ αἰὼν συνεχὴς καὶ ἀΐδιος ὑπάρχει τῷ θεῷ· τοῦτο γὰρ ὁ θεός. ὅσοι δὲ ὑπολαμβάνουσιν, ὥσπερ οἱ Πυθαγόρειοι καὶ Σπεύσιππος τὸ κάλλιστον καὶ ἄριστον μὴ ἐν ἀρχῇ εἶναι, διὰ τὸ καὶ τῶν φυτῶν καὶ τῶν ζῴων τὰς ἀρχὰς αἴτια μὲν εἶναι τὸ δὲ καλὸν καὶ τέλειον ἐν τοῖς ἐκ τούτων, οὐκ ὀρθῶς οἴονται. τὸ γὰρ σπέρμα ἐξ ἑτέρων ἐστὶ προτέρων τελείων, καὶ τὸ πρῶτον οὐ σπέρμα ἐστὶν ἀλλὰ τὸ τέλειον·  οἷον πρότερον ἄνθρωπον ἂν φαίη τις εἶναι τοῦ σπέρματος, οὐ τὸν ἐκ τούτου γενόμενον ἀλλʼ ἕτερον ἐξ οὗ τὸ σπέρμα.","kind":"passage"},{"id":"RAW_DAZY_BO_4:P0024","text":"ὅτι μὲν οὖν ἔστιν οὐσία τις ἀΐδιος καὶ ἀκίνητος καὶ κεχωρισμένη τῶν αἰσθητῶν, φανερὸν ἐκ τῶν εἰρημένων· δέδεικται δὲ καὶ ὅτι μέγεθος οὐδὲν ἔχειν ἐνδέχεται ταύτην τὴν οὐσίαν ἀλλʼ ἀμερὴς καὶ ἀδιαίρετός ἐστιν (κινεῖ γὰρ τὸν ἄπειρον χρόνον, οὐδὲν δʼ ἔχει δύναμιν ἄπειρον πεπερασμένον· ἐπεὶ δὲ πᾶν μέγεθος ἢ ἄπειρον ἢ πεπερασμένον, πεπερασμένον μὲν διὰ τοῦτο οὐκ ἂν ἔχοι μέγεθος, ἄπειρον δʼ ὅτι ὅλως οὐκ ἔστιν οὐδὲν ἄπειρον μέγεθος)· ἀλλὰ μὴν καὶ ὅτι ἀπαθὲς καὶ ἀναλλοίωτον· πᾶσαι γὰρ αἱ ἄλλαι κινήσεις ὕστεραι τῆς κατὰ τόπον. ταῦτα μὲν οὖν δῆλα διότι τοῦτον ἔχει τὸν τρόπον.","kind":"passage"},{"id":"RAW_DAZY_BO_4:P0025","text":"πότερον δὲ μίαν θετέον τὴν τοιαύτην οὐσίαν ἢ πλείους, καὶ πόσας, δεῖ μὴ λανθάνειν, ἀλλὰ μεμνῆσθαι καὶ τὰς τῶν ἄλλων ἀποφάσεις, ὅτι περὶ πλήθους οὐθὲν εἰρήκασιν ὅ τι καὶ σαφὲς εἰπεῖν. ἡ μὲν γὰρ περὶ τὰς ἰδέας ὑπόληψις οὐδεμίαν ἔχει σκέψιν ἰδίαν (ἀριθμοὺς γὰρ λέγουσι τὰς ἰδέας οἱ λέγοντες ἰδέας, περὶ δὲ τῶν ἀριθμῶν ὁτὲ μὲν ὡς περὶ ἀπείρων λέγουσιν ὁτὲ δὲ ὡς μέχρι τῆς δεκάδος ὡρισμένων· διʼ ἣν δʼ αἰτίαν τοσοῦτον τὸ πλῆθος τῶν ἀριθμῶν, οὐδὲν λέγεται μετὰ σπουδῆς ἀποδεικτικῆς)· ἡμῖν δʼ ἐκ τῶν ὑποκειμένων καὶ διωρισμένων λεκτέον.","kind":"passage"},{"id":"RAW_DAZY_BO_4:P0026","text":"ἡ μὲν γὰρ ἀρχὴ καὶ τὸ πρῶτον τῶν ὄντων ἀκίνητον καὶ καθʼ αὑτὸ καὶ κατὰ συμβεβηκός, κινοῦν δὲ τὴν πρώτην ἀΐδιον καὶ μίαν κίνησιν· ἐπεὶ δὲ τὸ κινούμενον ἀνάγκη ὑπό τινος κινεῖσθαι, καὶ τὸ πρῶτον κινοῦν ἀκίνητον εἶναι καθʼ αὑτό, καὶ τὴν ἀΐδιον κίνησιν ὑπὸ ἀϊδίου κινεῖσθαι καὶ τὴν μίαν ὑφʼ ἑνός, ὁρῶμεν δὲ παρὰ τὴν τοῦ παντὸς τὴν ἁπλῆν φοράν, ἣν κινεῖν φαμὲν τὴν πρώτην οὐσίαν καὶ ἀκίνητον, ἄλλας φορὰς οὔσας τὰς τῶν πλανήτων ἀϊδίους (ἀΐδιον γὰρ καὶ ἄστατον τὸ κύκλῳ σῶμα· δέδεικται δʼ ἐν τοῖς φυσικοῖς περὶ τούτων), ἀνάγκη καὶ τούτων ἑκάστην τῶν φορῶν ὑπʼ ἀκινήτου τε κινεῖσθαι καθʼ αὑτὴν καὶ ἀϊδίου οὐσίας. ἥ τε γὰρ τῶν ἄστρων φύσις ἀΐδιος οὐσία τις οὖσα, καὶ τὸ κινοῦν ἀΐδιον καὶ πρότερον τοῦ κινουμένου, καὶ τὸ πρότερον οὐσίας οὐσίαν ἀναγκαῖον εἶναι.","kind":"passage"},{"id":"RAW_DAZY_BO_4:P0027","text":"φανερὸν τοίνυν ὅτι τοσαύτας τε οὐσίας ἀναγκαῖον εἶναι τήν τε φύσιν ἀϊδίους καὶ ἀκινήτους καθʼ αὑτάς, καὶ ἄνευ μεγέθους διὰ τὴν εἰρημένην αἰτίαν πρότερον.  —ὅτι μὲν οὖν εἰσὶν οὐσίαι, καὶ τούτων τις πρώτη καὶ δευτέρα κατὰ τὴν αὐτὴν τάξιν ταῖς φοραῖς τῶν ἄστρων, φανερόν· τὸ δὲ πλῆθος ἤδη τῶν φορῶν ἐκ τῆς οἰκειοτάτης φιλοσοφίᾳ τῶν μαθηματικῶν ἐπιστημῶν δεῖ σκοπεῖν, ἐκ τῆς ἀστρολογίας· αὕτη γὰρ περὶ οὐσίας αἰσθητῆς μὲν ἀϊδίου δὲ ποιεῖται τὴν θεωρίαν, αἱ δʼ ἄλλαι περὶ οὐδεμιᾶς οὐσίας, οἷον ἥ τε περὶ τοὺς ἀριθμοὺς καὶ τὴν γεωμετρίαν.","kind":"passage"},{"id":"RAW_DAZY_BO_4:P0028","text":"ὅτι μὲν οὖν πλείους τῶν φερομένων αἱ φοραί, φανερὸν τοῖς καὶ μετρίως ἡμμένοις (πλείους γὰρ ἕκαστον φέρεται μιᾶς τῶν πλανωμένων ἄστρων)· πόσαι δʼ αὗται τυγχάνουσιν οὖσαι, νῦν μὲν ἡμεῖς ἃ λέγουσι τῶν μαθηματικῶν τινὲς ἐννοίας χάριν λέγομεν, ὅπως ᾖ τι τῇ διανοίᾳ πλῆθος ὡρισμένον ὑπολαβεῖν· τὸ δὲ λοιπὸν τὰ μὲν ζητοῦντας αὐτοὺς δεῖ τὰ δὲ πυνθανομένους παρὰ τῶν ζητούντων, ἄν τι φαίνηται παρὰ τὰ νῦν εἰρημένα τοῖς ταῦτα πραγματευομένοις, φιλεῖν μὲν ἀμφοτέρους, πείθεσθαι δὲ τοῖς ἀκριβεστέροις.—Εὔδοξος μὲν οὖν ἡλίου καὶ σελήνης ἑκατέρου τὴν φορὰν ἐν τρισὶν ἐτίθετʼ εἶναι σφαίραις, ὧν τὴν μὲν πρώτην τὴν τῶν ἀπλανῶν ἄστρων εἶναι, τὴν δὲ δευτέραν κατὰ τὸν διὰ μέσων τῶν ζῳδίων, τὴν δὲ τρίτην κατὰ τὸν λελοξωμένον ἐν τῷ πλάτει τῶν ζῳδίων (ἐν μείζονι δὲ πλάτει λελοξῶσθαι καθʼ ὃν ἡ σελήνη φέρεται ἢ καθʼ ὃν ὁ ἥλιος), τῶν δὲ πλανωμένων ἄστρων ἐν τέτταρσιν ἑκάστου σφαίραις, καὶ τούτων δὲ τὴν μὲν πρώτην καὶ","kind":"passage"},{"id":"RAW_DAZY_BO_4:P0029","text":"δευτέραν τὴν αὐτὴν εἶναι ἐκείναις (τήν τε γὰρ τῶν ἀπλανῶν τὴν ἁπάσας φέρουσαν εἶναι, καὶ τὴν ὑπὸ ταύτῃ τεταγμένην καὶ κατὰ τὸν διὰ μέσων τῶν ζῳδίων τὴν φορὰν ἔχουσαν κοινὴν ἁπασῶν εἶναι), τῆς δὲ τρίτης ἁπάντων τοὺς πόλους ἐν τῷ διὰ μέσων τῶν ζῳδίων εἶναι, τῆς δὲ τετάρτης τὴν φορὰν κατὰ τὸν λελοξωμένον πρὸς τὸν μέσον ταύτης· εἶναι δὲ τῆς τρίτης σφαίρας τοὺς πόλους τῶν μὲν ἄλλων ἰδίους, τοὺς δὲ τῆς Ἀφροδίτης καὶ τοῦ Ἑρμοῦ τοὺς αὐτούς· Κάλλιππος δὲ τὴν μὲν θέσιν τῶν σφαιρῶν τὴν αὐτὴν ἐτίθετο Εὐδόξῳ , τὸ δὲ πλῆθος τῷ μὲν τοῦ Διὸς καὶ τῷ τοῦ Κρόνου τὸ αὐτὸ ἐκείνῳ ἀπεδίδου, τῷ δʼ ἡλίῳ καὶ τῇ σελήνῃ δύο ᾤετο ἔτι προσθετέας εἶναι σφαίρας, τὰ φαινόμενα εἰ μέλλει τις ἀποδώσειν, τοῖς δὲ λοιποῖς τῶν πλανήτων ἑκάστῳ μίαν.","kind":"passage"},{"id":"RAW_DAZY_BO_4:P0030","text":"ἀναγκαῖον δέ, εἰ μέλλουσι συντεθεῖσαι πᾶσαι τὰ φαινόμενα ἀποδώσειν,  καθʼ ἕκαστον τῶν πλανωμένων ἑτέρας σφαίρας μιᾷ ἐλάττονας εἶναι τὰς ἀνελιττούσας καὶ εἰς τὸ αὐτὸ ἀποκαθιστάσας τῇ θέσει τὴν πρώτην σφαῖραν ἀεὶ τοῦ ὑποκάτω τεταγμένου ἄστρου· οὕτω γὰρ μόνως ἐνδέχεται τὴν τῶν πλανήτων φορὰν ἅπαντα ποιεῖσθαι. ἐπεὶ οὖν ἐν αἷς μὲν αὐτὰ φέρεται σφαίραις αἱ μὲν ὀκτὼ αἱ δὲ πέντε καὶ εἴκοσίν εἰσιν, τούτων δὲ μόνας οὐ δεῖ ἀνελιχθῆναι ἐν αἷς τὸ κατωτάτω τεταγμένον φέρεται, αἱ μὲν τὰς τῶν πρώτων δύο ἀνελίττουσαι ἓξ ἔσονται, αἱ δὲ τὰς τῶν ὕστερον τεττάρων ἑκκαίδεκα· ὁ δὴ ἁπασῶν ἀριθμὸς τῶν τε φερουσῶν καὶ τῶν ἀνελιττουσῶν ταύτας πεντήκοντά τε καὶ πέντε.","kind":"passage"},{"id":"RAW_DAZY_BO_4:P0031","text":"εἰ δὲ τῇ σελήνῃ τε καὶ τῷ ἡλίῳ μὴ προστιθείη τις ἃς εἴπομεν κινήσεις, αἱ πᾶσαι σφαῖραι ἔσονται ἑπτά τε καὶ τεσσαράκοντα.—τὸ μὲν οὖν πλῆθος τῶν σφαιρῶν ἔστω τοσοῦτον, ὥστε καὶ τὰς οὐσίας καὶ τὰς ἀρχὰς τὰς ἀκινήτους  τοσαύτας εὔλογον ὑπολαβεῖν (τὸ γὰρ ἀναγκαῖον ἀφείσθω τοῖς ἰσχυροτέροις λέγειν)· εἰ δὲ μηδεμίαν οἷόν τʼ εἶναι φορὰν μὴ συντείνουσαν πρὸς ἄστρου φοράν, ἔτι δὲ πᾶσαν φύσιν καὶ πᾶσαν οὐσίαν ἀπαθῆ καὶ καθʼ αὑτὴν τοῦ ἀρίστου τετυχηκυῖαν τέλος εἶναι δεῖ νομίζειν, οὐδεμία ἂν εἴη παρὰ ταύτας ἑτέρα φύσις, ἀλλὰ τοῦτον ἀνάγκη τὸν ἀριθμὸν εἶναι τῶν οὐσιῶν. εἴτε γὰρ εἰσὶν ἕτεραι, κινοῖεν ἂν ὡς τέλος οὖσαι φορᾶς· ἀλλὰ εἶναί γε ἄλλας φορὰς ἀδύνατον παρὰ τὰς εἰρημένας. τοῦτο δὲ εὔλογον ἐκ τῶν φερομένων ὑπολαβεῖν. εἰ γὰρ πᾶν τὸ φέρον τοῦ φερομένου χάριν πέφυκε καὶ φορὰ πᾶσα φερομένου τινός ἐστιν, οὐδεμία φορὰ αὑτῆς ἂν ἕνεκα εἴη οὐδʼ ἄλλης φορᾶς, ἀλλὰ τῶν ἄστρων ἕνεκα.","kind":"passage"},{"id":"RAW_DAZY_BO_4:P0032","text":"εἰ γὰρ ἔσται φορὰ φορᾶς ἕνεκα, καὶ ἐκείνην ἑτέρου δεήσει χάριν εἶναι· ὥστʼ ἐπειδὴ οὐχ οἷόν τε εἰς ἄπειρον,  τέλος ἔσται πάσης φορᾶς τῶν φερομένων τι θείων σωμάτων κατὰ τὸν οὐρανόν. ὅτι δὲ εἷς οὐρανός, φανερόν. εἰ γὰρ πλείους οὐρανοὶ ὥσπερ ἄνθρωποι, ἔσται εἴδει μία ἡ περὶ ἕκαστον ἀρχή, ἀριθμῷ δέ γε πολλαί. ἀλλʼ ὅσα ἀριθμῷ πολλά, ὕλην ἔχει (εἷς γὰρ λόγος καὶ ὁ αὐτὸς πολλῶν, οἷον ἀνθρώπου, Σωκράτης δὲ εἷς)· τὸ δὲ τί ἦν εἶναι οὐκ ἔχει ὕλην τὸ πρῶτον· ἐντελέχεια γάρ. ἓν ἄρα καὶ λόγῳ καὶ ἀριθμῷ τὸ πρῶτον κινοῦν ἀκίνητον ὄν· καὶ τὸ κινούμενον ἄρα ἀεὶ καὶ συνεχῶς· εἷς ἄρα οὐρανὸς μόνος.  παραδέδοται δὲ παρὰ τῶν ἀρχαίων καὶ παμπαλαίων ἐν μύθου σχήματι καταλελειμμένα τοῖς ὕστερον ὅτι θεοί τέ εἰσιν οὗτοι καὶ περιέχει τὸ θεῖον τὴν ὅλην φύσιν.","kind":"passage"},{"id":"RAW_DAZY_BO_4:P0033","text":"τὰ δὲ λοιπὰ μυθικῶς ἤδη προσῆκται πρὸς τὴν πειθὼ τῶν πολλῶν καὶ πρὸς τὴν εἰς τοὺς νόμους καὶ τὸ συμφέρον χρῆσιν· ἀνθρωποειδεῖς τε γὰρ τούτους καὶ τῶν ἄλλων ζῴων ὁμοίους τισὶ λέγουσι, καὶ τούτοις ἕτερα ἀκόλουθα καὶ παραπλήσια τοῖς εἰρημένοις, ὧν εἴ τις χωρίσας αὐτὸ λάβοι μόνον τὸ πρῶτον, ὅτι θεοὺς ᾤοντο τὰς πρώτας οὐσίας εἶναι, θείως ἂν εἰρῆσθαι νομίσειεν, καὶ κατὰ τὸ εἰκὸς πολλάκις εὑρημένης εἰς τὸ δυνατὸν ἑκάστης καὶ τέχνης καὶ φιλοσοφίας καὶ πάλιν φθειρομένων καὶ ταύτας τὰς δόξας ἐκείνων οἷον λείψανα περισεσῶσθαι μέχρι τοῦ νῦν. ἡ μὲν οὖν πάτριος δόξα καὶ ἡ παρὰ τῶν πρώτων ἐπὶ τοσοῦτον ἡμῖν φανερὰ μόνον.","kind":"passage"},{"id":"RAW_DAZY_BO_4:P0034","text":"τὰ δὲ περὶ τὸν νοῦν ἔχει τινὰς ἀπορίας· δοκεῖ μὲν γὰρ εἶναι τῶν φαινομένων θειότατον, πῶς δʼ ἔχων τοιοῦτος ἂν εἴη, ἔχει τινὰς δυσκολίας. εἴτε γὰρ μηδὲν νοεῖ, τί ἂν εἴη τὸ σεμνόν, ἀλλʼ ἔχει ὥσπερ ἂν εἰ ὁ καθεύδων· εἴτε νοεῖ, τούτου δʼ ἄλλο κύριον, οὐ γάρ ἐστι τοῦτο ὅ ἐστιν αὐτοῦ ἡ οὐσία νόησις, ἀλλὰ δύναμις, οὐκ ἂν ἡ ἀρίστη οὐσία εἴη· διὰ γὰρ τοῦ νοεῖν τὸ τίμιον αὐτῷ ὑπάρχει. ἔτι δὲ εἴτε νοῦς ἡ οὐσία αὐτοῦ εἴτε νόησίς ἐστι, τί νοεῖ; ἢ γὰρ αὐτὸς αὑτὸν ἢ ἕτερόν τι· καὶ εἰ ἕτερόν τι, ἢ τὸ αὐτὸ ἀεὶ ἢ ἄλλο. πότερον οὖν διαφέρει τι ἢ οὐδὲν τὸ νοεῖν τὸ καλὸν ἢ τὸ τυχόν; ἢ καὶ ἄτοπον τὸ διανοεῖσθαι περὶ ἐνίων; δῆλον τοίνυν ὅτι τὸ θειότατον καὶ τιμιώτατον νοεῖ, καὶ οὐ μεταβάλλει· εἰς χεῖρον γὰρ ἡ μεταβολή, καὶ κίνησίς τις ἤδη τὸ τοιοῦτον.","kind":"passage"},{"id":"RAW_DAZY_BO_4:P0035","text":"πρῶτον μὲν οὖν εἰ μὴ νόησίς ἐστιν ἀλλὰ δύναμις, εὔλογον ἐπίπονον εἶναι τὸ συνεχὲς αὐτῷ τῆς νοήσεως· ἔπειτα δῆλον ὅτι ἄλλο τι ἂν εἴη τὸ τιμιώτερον ἢ ὁ νοῦς, τὸ νοούμενον. καὶ γὰρ τὸ νοεῖν καὶ ἡ νόησις ὑπάρξει καὶ τὸ χείριστον νοοῦντι, ὥστʼ εἰ φευκτὸν τοῦτο (καὶ γὰρ μὴ ὁρᾶν ἔνια κρεῖττον ἢ ὁρᾶν), οὐκ ἂν εἴη τὸ ἄριστον ἡ νόησις. αὑτὸν ἄρα νοεῖ, εἴπερ ἐστὶ τὸ κράτιστον, καὶ ἔστιν ἡ νόησις νοήσεως νόησις. φαίνεται δʼ ἀεὶ ἄλλου ἡ ἐπιστήμη καὶ ἡ αἴσθησις καὶ ἡ δόξα καὶ ἡ διάνοια, αὑτῆς δʼ ἐν παρέργῳ. ἔτι εἰ ἄλλο τὸ νοεῖν καὶ τὸ νοεῖσθαι, κατὰ πότερον αὐτῷ τὸ εὖ ὑπάρχει; οὐδὲ γὰρ ταὐτὸ τὸ εἶναι νοήσει καὶ νοουμένῳ. ἢ ἐπʼ ἐνίων ἡ ἐπιστήμη τὸ πρᾶγμα,  ἐπὶ μὲν τῶν ποιητικῶν ἄνευ ὕλης ἡ οὐσία καὶ τὸ τί ἦν εἶναι, ἐπὶ δὲ τῶν θεωρητικῶν ὁ λόγος τὸ πρᾶγμα καὶ ἡ νόησις; οὐχ ἑτέρου οὖν ὄντος τοῦ νοουμένου καὶ τοῦ νοῦ, ὅσα μὴ ὕλην ἔχει, τὸ αὐτὸ ἔσται, καὶ ἡ νόησις τῷ νοουμένῳ μία.","kind":"passage"},{"id":"RAW_DAZY_BO_4:P0036","text":"ἔτι δὴ λείπεται ἀπορία, εἰ σύνθετον τὸ νοούμενον· μεταβάλλοι γὰρ ἂν ἐν τοῖς μέρεσι τοῦ ὅλου. ἢ ἀδιαίρετον πᾶν τὸ μὴ ἔχον ὕλην—ὥσπερ ὁ ἀνθρώπινος νοῦς ἢ ὅ γε τῶν συνθέτων ἔχει ἔν τινι χρόνῳ (οὐ γὰρ ἔχει τὸ εὖ ἐν τῳδὶ ἢ ἐν τῳδί, ἀλλʼ ἐν ὅλῳ τινὶ τὸ ἄριστον, ὂν ἄλλο τι)—οὕτως δʼ ἔχει αὐτὴ αὑτῆς ἡ νόησις τὸν ἅπαντα αἰῶνα;","kind":"passage"},{"id":"RAW_DAZY_BO_4:P0037","text":"ἐπισκεπτέον δὲ καὶ ποτέρως ἔχει ἡ τοῦ ὅλου φύσις τὸ ἀγαθὸν καὶ τὸ ἄριστον, πότερον κεχωρισμένον τι καὶ αὐτὸ καθʼ αὑτό, ἢ τὴν τάξιν. ἢ ἀμφοτέρως ὥσπερ στράτευμα; καὶ γὰρ ἐν τῇ τάξει τὸ εὖ καὶ ὁ στρατηγός, καὶ μᾶλλον οὗτος· οὐ γὰρ οὗτος διὰ τὴν τάξιν ἀλλʼ ἐκείνη διὰ τοῦτόν ἐστιν. πάντα δὲ συντέτακταί πως, ἀλλʼ οὐχ ὁμοίως, καὶ πλωτὰ καὶ πτηνὰ καὶ φυτά· καὶ οὐχ οὕτως ἔχει ὥστε μὴ εἶναι θατέρῳ πρὸς θάτερον μηδέν, ἀλλʼ ἔστι τι. πρὸς μὲν γὰρ ἓν ἅπαντα συντέτακται, ἀλλʼ ὥσπερ ἐν οἰκίᾳ τοῖς ἐλευθέροις ἥκιστα ἔξεστιν ὅ τι ἔτυχε ποιεῖν, ἀλλὰ πάντα ἢ τὰ πλεῖστα τέτακται, τοῖς δὲ ἀνδραπόδοις καὶ τοῖς θηρίοις μικρὸν τὸ εἰς τὸ κοινόν, τὸ δὲ πολὺ ὅ τι ἔτυχεν· τοιαύτη γὰρ ἑκάστου ἀρχὴ αὐτῶν ἡ φύσις ἐστίν.","kind":"passage"},{"id":"RAW_DAZY_BO_4:P0038","text":"λέγω δʼ οἷον εἴς γε τὸ διακριθῆναι ἀνάγκη ἅπασιν ἐλθεῖν, καὶ ἄλλα οὕτως ἔστιν ὧν κοινωνεῖ ἅπαντα εἰς τὸ ὅλον.—ὅσα δὲ ἀδύνατα συμβαίνει ἢ ἄτοπα τοῖς ἄλλως λέγουσι, καὶ ποῖα οἱ χαριεστέρως λέγοντες, καὶ ἐπὶ ποίων ἐλάχισται ἀπορίαι, δεῖ μὴ λανθάνειν. πάντες γὰρ ἐξ ἐναντίων ποιοῦσι πάντα. οὔτε δὲ τὸ πάντα οὔτε τὸ ἐξ ἐναντίων ὀρθῶς, οὔτʼ ἐν ὅσοις τὰ ἐναντία ὑπάρχει, πῶς ἐκ τῶν ἐναντίων ἔσται, οὐ λέγουσιν· ἀπαθῆ γὰρ τὰ ἐναντία ὑπʼ ἀλλήλων. ἡμῖν δὲ λύεται τοῦτο εὐλόγως τῷ τρίτον τι εἶναι. οἱ δὲ τὸ ἕτερον τῶν ἐναντίων ὕλην ποιοῦσιν, ὥσπερ οἱ τὸ ἄνισον τῷ ἴσῳ ἢ τῷ ἑνὶ τὰ πολλά. λύεται δὲ καὶ τοῦτο τὸν αὐτὸν τρόπον· ἡ γὰρ ὕλη ἡ μία οὐδενὶ ἐναντίον. ἔτι ἅπαντα τοῦ φαύλου μεθέξει ἔξω τοῦ ἑνός· τὸ γὰρ κακὸν αὐτὸ θάτερον τῶν στοιχείων. οἱ δʼ ἄλλοι οὐδʼ ἀρχὰς τὸ ἀγαθὸν καὶ τὸ κακόν· καίτοι ἐν ἅπασι μάλιστα τὸ ἀγαθὸν ἀρχή.","kind":"passage"},{"id":"RAW_DAZY_BO_4:P0039","text":"οἱ δὲ τοῦτο μὲν ὀρθῶς ὅτι ἀρχήν, ἀλλὰ πῶς τὸ ἀγαθὸν ἀρχὴ οὐ λέγουσιν, πότερον ὡς τέλος ἢ ὡς κινῆσαν ἢ ὡς εἶδος.  ἀτόπως δὲ καὶ Ἐμπεδοκλῆς· τὴν γὰρ φιλίαν ποιεῖ τὸ ἀγαθόν, αὕτη δʼ ἀρχὴ καὶ ὡς κινοῦσα (συνάγει γάρ) καὶ ὡς ὕλη· μόριον γὰρ τοῦ μίγματος. εἰ δὴ καὶ τῷ αὐτῷ συμβέβηκεν καὶ ὡς ὕλῃ ἀρχῇ εἶναι καὶ ὡς κινοῦντι, ἀλλὰ τό γʼ εἶναι οὐ ταὐτό. κατὰ πότερον οὖν φιλία; ἄτοπον δὲ καὶ τὸ ἄφθαρτον εἶναι τὸ νεῖκος· τοῦτο δʼ ἐστὶν αὐτῷ ἡ τοῦ κακοῦ φύσις. Ἀναξαγόρας δὲ ὡς κινοῦν τὸ ἀγαθὸν ἀρχήν· ὁ γὰρ νοῦς κινεῖ. ἀλλὰ κινεῖ ἕνεκά τινος, ὥστε ἕτερον, πλὴν ὡς ἡμεῖς λέγομεν· ἡ γὰρ ἰατρική ἐστί πως ἡ ὑγίεια. ἄτοπον δὲ καὶ τὸ ἐναντίον μὴ ποιῆσαι τῷ ἀγαθῷ καὶ τῷ νῷ. πάντες δʼ οἱ τἀναντία λέγοντες οὐ χρῶνται τοῖς ἐναντίοις, ἐὰν μὴ ῥυθμίσῃ τις. καὶ διὰ τί τὰ μὲν φθαρτὰ τὰ δʼ ἄφθαρτα, οὐδεὶς λέγει· πάντα γὰρ τὰ ὄντα ποιοῦσιν ἐκ τῶν αὐτῶν ἀρχῶν.","kind":"passage"},{"id":"RAW_DAZY_BO_4:P0040","text":"ἔτι οἱ μὲν ἐκ τοῦ μὴ ὄντος ποιοῦσι τὰ ὄντα· οἱ δʼ ἵνα μὴ τοῦτο ἀναγκασθῶσιν, ἓν πάντα ποιοῦσιν.—ἔτι διὰ τί ἀεὶ ἔσται γένεσις καὶ τί αἴτιον γενέσεως, οὐδεὶς λέγει. καὶ τοῖς δύο ἀρχὰς ποιοῦσιν ἄλλην ἀνάγκη ἀρχὴν κυριωτέραν εἶναι, καὶ τοῖς τὰ εἴδη ἔτι ἄλλη ἀρχὴ κυριωτέρα· διὰ τί γὰρ μετέσχεν ἢ μετέχει; καὶ τοῖς μὲν ἄλλοις ἀνάγκη τῇ σοφίᾳ καὶ τῇ τιμιωτάτῃ ἐπιστήμῃ εἶναί τι ἐναντίον, ἡμῖν δʼ οὔ. οὐ γάρ ἐστιν ἐναντίον τῷ πρώτῳ οὐδέν· πάντα γὰρ τὰ ἐναντία ὕλην ἔχει, καὶ δυνάμει ταῦτα ἔστιν· ἡ δὲ ἐναντία ἄγνοια εἰς τὸ ἐναντίον, τῷ δὲ πρώτῳ ἐναντίον οὐδέν. εἴ τε μὴ ἔσται παρὰ τὰ αἰσθητὰ ἄλλα, οὐκ ἔσται ἀρχὴ καὶ τάξις καὶ γένεσις καὶ τὰ οὐράνια, ἀλλʼ ἀεὶ τῆς ἀρχῆς ἀρχή, ὥσπερ τοῖς θεολόγοις καὶ τοῖς φυσικοῖς πᾶσιν. εἰ δʼ ἔσται τὰ εἴδη· ἢ οἱ ἀριθμοί, οὐδενὸς αἴτια· εἰ δὲ μή, οὔτι κινήσεώς γε.","kind":"passage"},{"id":"RAW_DAZY_BO_4:P0041","text":"ἔτι πῶς ἔσται ἐξ ἀμεγεθῶν μέγεθος καὶ συνεχές; ὁ γὰρ ἀριθμὸς οὐ ποιήσει συνεχές, οὔτε ὡς κινοῦν οὔτε ὡς εἶδος. ἀλλὰ μὴν οὐδέν γʼ ἔσται τῶν ἐναντίων ὅπερ καὶ ποιητικὸν καὶ κινητικόν; ἐνδέχοιτο γὰρ ἂν μὴ εἶναι. ἀλλὰ μὴν ὕστερόν γε τὸ ποιεῖν δυνάμεως. οὐκ ἄρα ἀΐδια τὰ ὄντα. ἀλλʼ ἔστιν· ἀναιρετέον ἄρα τούτων τι. τοῦτο δʼ εἴρηται πῶς. ἔτι τίνι οἱ ἀριθμοὶ ἓν ἢ ἡ ψυχὴ καὶ τὸ σῶμα καὶ ὅλως τὸ εἶδος καὶ τὸ πρᾶγμα, οὐδὲν λέγει οὐδείς· οὐδʼ ἐνδέχεται εἰπεῖν, ἐὰν μὴ ὡς ἡμεῖς εἴπῃ, ὡς τὸ κινοῦν ποιεῖ. οἱ δὲ λέγοντες τὸν ἀριθμὸν πρῶτον τὸν μαθηματικὸν καὶ οὕτως ἀεὶ ἄλλην ἐχομένην οὐσίαν καὶ ἀρχὰς ἑκάστης ἄλλας,  ἐπεισοδιώδη τὴν τοῦ παντὸς οὐσίαν ποιοῦσιν (οὐδὲν γὰρ ἡ ἑτέρα τῇ ἑτέρᾳ συμβάλλεται οὖσα ἢ μὴ οὖσα) καὶ ἀρχὰς πολλάς· τὰ δὲ ὄντα οὐ βούλεται πολιτεύεσθαι κακῶς. οὐκ ἀγαθὸν πολυκοιρανίη· εἷς κοίρανος ἔστω.","kind":"passage"}],"sha256":"aa1779e5cf9f8818985c68b89f3950900e3e22ce11fa50793781710bf3efd4bd","language_code":"grc","language_name":"Ἑλληνική","translations":[{"id":"SOURCE_TR_20261015T173842945152Z_17dfa288","language_code":"en","language_name":"English","title":"Book 12","source_sha256":"aa1779e5cf9f8818985c68b89f3950900e3e22ce11fa50793781710bf3efd4bd","sha256":"2ad428053bc495e94ba14550b327b41a281fc0c471e956a8d8f49dd70643f0ff","rendition_type":"machine","review_status":"machine","model":"gpt-5.6-terra","created":"2026-10-15T17:38:42.945231+00:00","paragraphs":[{"id":"RAW_DAZY_BO_4:P0001","text":"The inquiry concerns substance; for the principles and causes of substances are sought. For if the all is as some whole, substance is its first part; and if it is by succession, then even so substance is first, then quality, then quantity. Yet at the same time these are not, so to speak, beings without qualification, but qualities and motions, or even the not-white and the not-straight; at any rate, we say that these too are, for example, “there is something not-white.” Further, none of the others is separable. And the ancients bear witness to this in their practice; for it was principles and elements and causes of substance that they sought. Now those of the present day rather posit universals as substances (for genera are universals, which they say are principles and substances, because they inquire in a logical manner); whereas those of old posited particulars, such as fire and earth, but not the common thing, body.","kind":"passage","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_DAZY_BO_4:P0001"]},{"id":"RAW_DAZY_BO_4:P0002","text":"There are three substances: one is sensible—of which one kind is eternal and another perishable, which all acknowledge, such as plants and animals; and it is necessary to grasp its elements, whether one or many. Another is unmoved, and some say that this is separable: some divide it into two, others place the Forms and mathematical objects in one nature, and others take mathematical objects alone as belonging to these. The former belong to natural science (for they involve motion), but the latter to another science, if there is no principle common to them.","kind":"passage","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_DAZY_BO_4:P0002"]},{"id":"RAW_DAZY_BO_4:P0003","text":"Sensible substance is changeable. But if change is from opposites or from intermediates, and not from all opposites (for not-white is not the voice), but from the contrary, there must underlie the thing that changes into the contrariety; for the contraries do not change.","kind":"passage","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_DAZY_BO_4:P0003"]},{"id":"RAW_DAZY_BO_4:P0004","text":"Further, one thing remains, whereas the contrary does not remain; therefore there is some third thing besides the contraries, namely matter. Now if changes are fourfold, either with respect to what-it-is, or quality, or quantity, or where; and simple coming-to-be and destruction are with respect to this particular thing, increase and decrease with respect to quantity, alteration with respect to affection, and locomotion with respect to place; then changes in each case will be into contrarieties. Therefore matter, capable of changing into both, must change. And since being is twofold, everything changes from what is potentially into what is actually, for example from what is potentially white into what is actually white, and similarly in increase and decrease; so that not only can something come to be from not-being incidentally, but everything comes to be from being—being potentially, however, but actually from not-being.","kind":"passage","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_DAZY_BO_4:P0004"]},{"id":"RAW_DAZY_BO_4:P0005","text":"And this is Anaxagoras’ one; for it is better than “all things together”—and so too Empedocles’ mixture and Anaximander’s, and, as Democritus says: “all things were together potentially, but not actually.” Thus they would seem to have touched upon matter. But everything that changes has matter, though different matter; and among eternal things, whatever are not generated but are moved in locomotion have matter—not generated matter, but matter whence to whither. One might raise a difficulty: from which not-being is generation? For not-being is threefold. If, then, something is potentially, nevertheless it is not from anything whatever, but one thing from another; nor is it sufficient that all things are together. For they differ in matter; otherwise why did infinitely many things come to be, rather than one? For intellect is one; so that if matter too is one, that came to be actually of which matter was potentially. Thus the causes are three and the principles three: two are the contrariety, of which the one is account and form, the other privation; and the third is matter.","kind":"passage","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_DAZY_BO_4:P0005"]},{"id":"RAW_DAZY_BO_4:P0006","text":"After this, [it must be shown] that neither matter nor form comes to be—I mean the ultimate ones. For everything that changes is changed by something and into something: that by which it is changed is the first mover; that which changes is matter; and that into which it changes is form. Thus there will be an infinite regress, if not only the bronze comes to be round, but also the round or the bronze; it is therefore necessary to stop.—After this, [it must be shown] that each substance comes to be from something synonymous with it (for both natural substances and the others do so). For things come to be either by art, or by nature, or by chance, or spontaneously. Art is a principle in another thing, whereas nature is a principle in the thing itself (for a human being begets a human being); the remaining causes are privations of these. There are three substances: matter, which is a this-something in appearance (for whatever are by contact and not by being grown together are matter and substrate); nature, which is a this-something and a certain state into which [matter is brought]; and, third, the particular compound from these, such as Socrates or Callias.","kind":"passage","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_DAZY_BO_4:P0006"]},{"id":"RAW_DAZY_BO_4:P0007","text":"In some cases, then, the this-something does not exist apart from the composite substance—for example, the form of a house, unless it is the art; nor is there generation and destruction of such things, but in another way the house without matter, health, and everything according to art both are and are not. But if there are such things, they are among natural things. Hence Plato was not wrong in saying that there are Forms of as many things as are by nature, if there are Forms other than these, such as fire, flesh, head; for all these are matter, and the ultimate matter of what is substance in the highest degree. The moving causes, then, are causes as having come to be previously, whereas causes as account are simultaneous. For when a human being becomes healthy, health too then exists; and the shape of the bronze sphere and the bronze sphere are simultaneous. But whether something remains afterward must be considered; for in some cases nothing prevents it—for example, if the soul is such, not the whole soul but intellect; for perhaps it is impossible that the whole soul should remain.","kind":"passage","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_DAZY_BO_4:P0007"]},{"id":"RAW_DAZY_BO_4:P0008","text":"It is clear, then, that for these reasons there is no need for the Ideas to exist; for a human being begets a human being, the particular a particular human being. Similarly also in the arts; for the medical art is the account of health.","kind":"passage","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_DAZY_BO_4:P0008"]},{"id":"RAW_DAZY_BO_4:P0009","text":"The causes and principles of different things are different in one way, but in another way, if one speaks universally and analogically, they are the same for all things. For one might raise the question whether the principles and elements of substances and relatives are different or the same, and similarly for each of the categories. But it is absurd if they are the same for all things; for relatives and substances will then be from the same things. What, then, will this be? For there is nothing common apart from substance and the other predicates, while an element is prior to that of which it is an element. Yet neither is substance an element of relatives, nor is any of these an element of substance. Further, how can the elements of all things be the same? For none of the elements can be the same as the composite made out of elements, for example B or A cannot be the same as BA; nor indeed is any intelligible thing an element, such as being or one, for these belong to each of the composites as well. Therefore none of them will be either substance or relative; yet it is necessary [that they be].","kind":"passage","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_DAZY_BO_4:P0009"]},{"id":"RAW_DAZY_BO_4:P0010","text":"Therefore the elements are not the same for all things.—Or, as we say, they are in one way, and in another way they are not; for example, perhaps in sensible bodies the hot is form, and in another way the cold is privation, while matter is what is first potentially these things in itself; and substances are both these and the things composed from them, of which these are principles—or, if something one comes to be from hot and cold, such as flesh or bone; for what has come to be must be different from those things. For these things, then, the elements and principles are the same (but for other things, others); though it is not possible to say so of all things, except analogically, as if one were to say that there are three principles: form, privation, and matter. But each of these is different in relation to each genus; for example, in colour: white, black, surface; in day and night: light, darkness, air.","kind":"passage","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_DAZY_BO_4:P0010"]},{"id":"RAW_DAZY_BO_4:P0011","text":"Since the causes are not only the immanent ones, but also the external ones, such as the mover, it is clear that principle and element are different, though both are causes; and principle is divided into these. What is a principle as moving or bringing to rest is a certain principle and substance; so that analogically the elements are three, but the causes and principles four. Yet the [fourth] is different in different cases, and the first cause as mover is different for different things. Health, disease, body; the mover is medicine. Form, such disorder, bricks; the mover is house-building. And since among natural things the mover is a human being for a human being, while among things from thought it is the form or its contrary, in one way there would be three causes, but in another four. For medicine is in some way health, and house-building is the form of a house, and a human being begets a human being; and beyond these, that which as first mover moves all things.","kind":"passage","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_DAZY_BO_4:P0011"]},{"id":"RAW_DAZY_BO_4:P0012","text":"Since some things are separable and others not separable, the former are substances. And for this reason the causes of all things are the same, because without substances there are no affections and motions. Further, these will perhaps be soul and body, or intellect and desire and body.—Again, in another way the principles are the same analogically, such as actuality and potentiality; but these too are different for different things and in different ways.","kind":"passage","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_DAZY_BO_4:P0012"]},{"id":"RAW_DAZY_BO_4:P0013","text":"For in some cases the same thing is at one time actual and at another potential, for example wine or flesh or a human being. (These too fall under the causes mentioned: for the form, if it is separable, and the compound of both, are actual; privation, such as darkness or illness, is actual; whereas matter is potential; for this is what is capable of becoming both.) But actual and potential differ otherwise in things that do not have the same matter, and in some of which there is not the same form but another; as the causes of a human being are the elements, fire and earth as matter, and the proper form, and further something else external, such as the father; and besides these the sun and the oblique circle, which are neither matter nor form nor privation nor homogeneous, but movers. Further, one must see that some things can be stated universally, while others cannot. The first principles of all things, then, are what is first actually this particular thing and another thing which is potentially [such].","kind":"passage","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_DAZY_BO_4:P0013"]},{"id":"RAW_DAZY_BO_4:P0014","text":"Those universal things, then, do not exist; for the principle of particulars is a particular. For human being universally is [the cause] of human being, but there is no [universal] human being; rather, Peleus is [the cause] of Achilles, and your father of you, and this B of this BA, while B universally is [the cause] of BA universally. Further, if [the causes] of substances, the causes and elements of different things are different, as has been said, in things not in the same genus—colours, sounds, substances, quantity—except analogically; and even among things in the same species they are different, not in species but because they are of particulars: your matter and form and mover are different from mine, though in universal account they are the same.","kind":"passage","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_DAZY_BO_4:P0014"]},{"id":"RAW_DAZY_BO_4:P0015","text":"As for asking what are the principles or elements of substances and relatives and qualities, whether the same or different, it is clear that, since each is spoken of in many ways, when they are distinguished they are not the same but different—except in this way, that they are so for all things: in one way they are the same, or analogically the same, because they are matter, form, privation, and the mover; and in this way the causes of substances are causes of all things, because when substances are removed, the others are removed. Further, [there is] what is first in complete actuality. But in another way the first principles are different: all the contraries which are neither said as genera nor said in many ways, and further the matters. What the principles of sensible things are and how many, and how they are the same and how different, has been stated.","kind":"passage","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_DAZY_BO_4:P0015"]},{"id":"RAW_DAZY_BO_4:P0016","text":"Since there were three substances, two natural and one unmoved, we must speak of the latter, namely that there must be some eternal unmoved substance. For substances are the first of beings, and if all were perishable, all things would be perishable. But it is impossible for motion either to come to be or to perish (for it always was), and likewise for time. For it is impossible for before and after to exist if time does not exist; and so motion is continuous in the same way as time, for time is either the same thing as motion or some affection of motion. But no motion is continuous except locomotion, and among locomotions circular locomotion.","kind":"passage","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_DAZY_BO_4:P0016"]},{"id":"RAW_DAZY_BO_4:P0017","text":"But indeed, if there is something capable of moving or producing, yet not active, there will not be motion; for what has a capacity may fail to exercise it. Therefore there is no benefit even if we make substances eternal, as those who posit the Forms do, unless there is some principle in them capable of bringing about change. Nor, then, is even this sufficient, nor any other substance besides the Forms; for if it will not be active, there will be no motion. Further, not even if it will be active, while its substance is potentiality; for there will not be eternal motion, since what is potentially may fail to be. Therefore there must be such a principle whose substance is actuality. Further, these substances must be without matter; for they must be eternal, if anything else too is eternal. Therefore, actuality. Yet there is a difficulty: for it seems that everything actual is capable, but not everything capable is actual, so that potentiality seems prior. But if this is so, none of beings will exist; for what is capable of being may not yet be.","kind":"passage","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_DAZY_BO_4:P0017"]},{"id":"RAW_DAZY_BO_4:P0018","text":"Yet if it is as the theologians say, who generate things from night, or as the natural philosophers say, who assert that all things were together, the same impossibility results. For how will there be motion if there is not some cause actually at work? Matter will certainly not move itself, but house-building [will move it]; nor will menstrual fluids or earth [move themselves], but seeds and semen [will]. Hence some make actuality eternal, such as Leucippus and Plato; for they say that motion always exists. But they do not say why or what [it is], nor, if it is in this way or that, the cause of it. For nothing is moved at random, but something must always be present: as now something moves in one way by nature, and in another by force or by intellect or something else. (And which is first? For the difference is immeasurably great.) Yet neither can Plato say that what he sometimes thinks to be a principle, the self-moving itself, is [the principle]; for soul is later than, and simultaneous with, the heaven, as he says.","kind":"passage","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_DAZY_BO_4:P0018"]},{"id":"RAW_DAZY_BO_4:P0019","text":"To suppose that potentiality is prior to actuality is in one way correct, but in another not (and it has been said how); but that actuality is prior is testified by Anaxagoras—for intellect is actuality—and by Empedocles, with Love and Strife, and by those who say that motion always exists, such as Leucippus. Thus chaos or night did not exist for an infinite time, but the same things always existed, either in a cycle or otherwise, if actuality is prior to potentiality. If, then, the same thing always exists cyclically, something must always remain, acting in the same way. But if there is to be generation and destruction, there must always be something else acting differently in different ways. Therefore it must act in one way through itself, and in another way through another thing—either, then, through something else or through the first. It must be through the first; for again that other will be a cause both to itself and to that thing. Therefore the first is better; for that was the cause of what is always in the same way, whereas another is cause of what is otherwise, and evidently both are causes of what is always otherwise. Thus the motions are in fact constituted in this way. Why, then, must one seek other principles?","kind":"passage","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_DAZY_BO_4:P0019"]},{"id":"RAW_DAZY_BO_4:P0020","text":"Since this is possible in this way—and if not in this way, [things] will be from night and from all things together and from not-being—these difficulties would be resolved; and there is something always moved with unceasing motion, and this is circular motion (and this is clear not only in account but also in fact), so that the first heaven would be eternal. Therefore there is also something which moves it. And since what is moved and moves is intermediate, there is something which moves without being moved, eternal and substance and actuality. It moves in this way: the object of desire and the object of thought move without being moved. The primary instances of these are the same. For the apparent beautiful is desired, but what is beautiful in being is first willed; and we desire because it appears [good], rather than it appears [good] because we desire it; for thought is the principle. Intellect is moved by the intelligible, and the other column of opposites is intelligible in itself; and substance is first in this [column], and in substance what is simple and actual is first. (But the one and the simple are not the same: the one signifies a measure, whereas the simple signifies how the thing itself is disposed.)","kind":"passage","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_DAZY_BO_4:P0020"]},{"id":"RAW_DAZY_BO_4:P0021","text":"But indeed the beautiful and that which is choiceworthy in itself are in the same column; and the first is always best, or proportionately best. That final cause exists among unmoved things is shown by division: for the final cause is for something and belongs to something, of which in one case it exists and in the other it does not. It moves, then, as an object of love, while what is moved moves the other things. If, then, something is moved, it can also be otherwise; so that if locomotion is its first actuality, then in so far as it is moved it can at least be otherwise in respect of place, even if not in respect of substance. But since there is something which moves while itself being unmoved, and is actual, this cannot be otherwise in any way. For locomotion is the first of changes, and circular locomotion is first among these; and this [unmoved mover] moves it. Therefore it exists of necessity; and insofar as it is by necessity, it exists well, and in this way it is a principle. For “necessary” is spoken of in so many ways: one kind is what is by force, because contrary to impulse; another is that without which the good is impossible; and another is what cannot be otherwise, but is simply so.—Upon such a principle, therefore, heaven and nature depend.","kind":"passage","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_DAZY_BO_4:P0021"]},{"id":"RAW_DAZY_BO_4:P0022","text":"And the way of life is such as the best [being has] for a short time for us (for that [being has it] always; for us it is impossible), since pleasure too is the actuality of this (and on account of this waking, perception, and thinking are most pleasant, while hopes and memories are so because of these). Thinking in itself is of what is best in itself, and the highest thinking is of what is highest. And intellect thinks itself by participation in the intelligible; for it becomes intelligible by touching and thinking, so that intellect and intelligible are the same. For intellect is what is receptive of the intelligible and of essence, and it is actual when it possesses [them]; hence what intellect seems to possess as divine belongs more to that [activity], and contemplation is what is most pleasant and best. If, then, God is always as well disposed as we are sometimes, that is wonderful; and if more so, still more wonderful. And he is so disposed. And life indeed belongs to him; for the actuality of intellect is life, and he is that actuality; and his actuality in itself is life best and eternal.","kind":"passage","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_DAZY_BO_4:P0022"]},{"id":"RAW_DAZY_BO_4:P0023","text":"We say, then, that God is a living being, eternal and best; so that life and continuous, eternal duration belong to God; for this is God. But those who suppose, as the Pythagoreans and Speusippus do, that the most beautiful and best is not in the principle, on the ground that the principles of plants and animals too are causes, whereas the beautiful and complete are in the things that come from these, do not think rightly. For the seed is from other things that are prior and complete, and the first is not seed but the complete thing; for example, one might say that a human being is prior to the seed—not the one generated from it, but another, from whom the seed comes.","kind":"passage","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_DAZY_BO_4:P0023"]},{"id":"RAW_DAZY_BO_4:P0024","text":"That there is, then, a certain eternal, unmoved, and separate substance, distinct from sensible things, is clear from what has been said; and it has also been demonstrated that this substance cannot have any magnitude, but is without parts and indivisible (for it moves for an infinite time, and nothing finite has infinite power; and since every magnitude is either infinite or finite, it could not have finite magnitude for this reason, nor infinite magnitude because there is in general no infinite magnitude). Further, it is also impassible and unalterable; for all the other motions are posterior to motion in place. These points, then, are clear, because it has this character.","kind":"passage","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_DAZY_BO_4:P0024"]},{"id":"RAW_DAZY_BO_4:P0025","text":"But whether one must posit one such substance or several, and how many, must not escape us; rather, we should recall also the pronouncements of the others, that they have said nothing about plurality which can even be stated clearly. For the doctrine concerning the Forms contains no inquiry peculiar to itself (for those who speak of Forms call the Forms numbers, and concerning numbers they speak sometimes as though they were infinite, sometimes as though they were determined up to ten; but no reason is seriously stated demonstratively why the plurality of numbers is of such extent). But we must speak from what has been laid down and distinguished.","kind":"passage","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_DAZY_BO_4:P0025"]},{"id":"RAW_DAZY_BO_4:P0026","text":"For the principle and the first among beings is unmoved both in itself and accidentally, but moves the first eternal and single motion. And since what is moved must be moved by something, and the first mover must be unmoved in itself, and eternal motion must be moved by an eternal thing and single motion by one thing, and since besides the simple revolution of the whole—which we say is moved by the first and unmoved substance—we observe other eternal revolutions, those of the wandering stars (for the body that moves in a circle is eternal and unceasing; this has been demonstrated in the physical works), it is necessary that each of these revolutions too be moved by an unmoved thing in itself and by an eternal substance. For the nature of the stars, being a certain eternal substance, is eternal; and what moves is eternal and prior to what is moved; and what is prior to a substance must be a substance.","kind":"passage","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_DAZY_BO_4:P0026"]},{"id":"RAW_DAZY_BO_4:P0027","text":"It is clear, accordingly, that there must be as many substances as there are eternal and unmoved natures in themselves, and [that they are] without magnitude for the reason stated before. —That there are substances, then, and that among them one is first and another second in the same order as the revolutions of the stars, is clear. But the number of the revolutions must now be investigated from that branch of the mathematical sciences most akin to philosophy, namely astronomy; for it makes its inquiry concerning sensible but eternal substance, whereas the others concern no substance at all, for example those concerned with numbers and geometry.","kind":"passage","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_DAZY_BO_4:P0027"]},{"id":"RAW_DAZY_BO_4:P0028","text":"That the revolutions are more numerous than the things moved is clear to those who have even moderately attended to these matters (for each of the wandering stars is moved by more than one [revolution]). But how many these happen to be, we now state what certain mathematical thinkers say, for the sake of forming some conception, so that the mind may suppose a determinate number. For the rest, one must both investigate some matters oneself and inquire about others from those who investigate them; and if anything appears otherwise to those who concern themselves with these matters than what has now been said, one should love both [views], but be persuaded by the more exact.—Eudoxus, then, posited that the motion of the sun and moon, of each respectively, occurs in three spheres: the first of these being that of the fixed stars, the second along the middle of the zodiacal signs, and the third along that which is inclined in breadth through the zodiacal signs (and that the path along which the moon moves is inclined through a greater breadth than that along which the sun moves); and that the wandering stars each occur in four spheres, and of these likewise the first and","kind":"passage","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_DAZY_BO_4:P0028"]},{"id":"RAW_DAZY_BO_4:P0029","text":"second are the same as those [just mentioned] (for both the sphere of the fixed stars, which carries them all, and the one arranged beneath it and having its motion along the middle of the zodiacal signs, are common to all); that the poles of the third [sphere] of all are in the middle of the zodiacal signs, and the motion of the fourth is along the inclined [circle] relative to the middle of this [third sphere]; and that the poles of the third sphere are peculiar in the case of the other [planets], but those of Aphrodite and Hermes are the same. Callippus, however, posited the arrangement of the spheres to be the same as Eudoxus', but assigned the same number as Eudoxus to Zeus and Cronus, while he thought that two further spheres must be added for the sun and moon, if one is to account for the phenomena, and one for each of the remaining wandering stars.","kind":"passage","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_DAZY_BO_4:P0029"]},{"id":"RAW_DAZY_BO_4:P0030","text":"But if, when all are combined, they are to account for the phenomena, it is necessary, for each of the wandering stars, that there be other spheres, one fewer in number, which unwind [the motions] and always restore the first sphere of the star arranged beneath to the same position; for only thus is it possible for all the motions of the wandering stars to occur. Since, then, the spheres in which they themselves are carried number eight and twenty-five, and of these only those in which the lowest-arranged [star] is carried need not be unwound, the spheres unwinding those of the first two will be six, and those unwinding the [spheres] of the latter four sixteen. Thus the total number of all the spheres, both those carrying and those unwinding these, is fifty-five.","kind":"passage","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_DAZY_BO_4:P0030"]},{"id":"RAW_DAZY_BO_4:P0031","text":"But if one does not add to the moon and the sun the motions which we mentioned, all the spheres will be forty-seven.—Let, then, the number of the spheres be so great; so that it is reasonable to suppose that the substances and the unmoved principles are also so many (let the more powerful say what is necessary). But if no motion can be such as does not contribute to the motion of a star, and if one must judge every nature and every impassible substance which has attained its end in itself to be an end, there could be no other nature besides these, but this must be the number of the substances. For if there were others, they would cause motion by being ends of motion; but it is impossible that there be other motions besides those stated. And it is reasonable to infer this from the things moved. For if every mover is naturally constituted for the sake of what is moved, and every motion is motion of some moved thing, no motion can be for its own sake or for the sake of another motion, but for the sake of the stars.","kind":"passage","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_DAZY_BO_4:P0031"]},{"id":"RAW_DAZY_BO_4:P0032","text":"For if there is a motion for the sake of motion, that motion too will have to be for the sake of something else; so that, since this cannot proceed to infinity, the end of every motion will be one of the divine bodies moved in the heaven. That there is one heaven is clear. For if there were several heavens, as there are several human beings, the principle concerned with each would be one in form but many in number. But whatever things are many in number have matter (for the account is one and the same for many, for example for human being, but Socrates is one); whereas the what-it-was-to-be for the first has no matter, for it is actuality. Therefore the first unmoved mover is one both in account and in number; and therefore that which is moved always and continuously is also one. Therefore there is only one heaven. It has been handed down from the ancients and from the very ancient, in mythical form and left to later generations, that these are gods and that the divine encompasses the whole of nature.","kind":"passage","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_DAZY_BO_4:P0032"]},{"id":"RAW_DAZY_BO_4:P0033","text":"The rest has already been added mythically for the persuasion of the multitude and for usefulness with respect to the laws and what is advantageous. For they say that these are human-shaped and like certain of the other animals, and they add other things consequent upon and similar to what has been said. But if, separating these things, one were to take only this first point—that they thought the first substances to be gods—one would judge it to have been divinely said; and [one would judge] that, since every art and philosophy has probably been discovered many times so far as possible and again perished, these opinions have been preserved until now like relics of those [discoveries]. Thus far alone is the ancestral opinion and that handed down from the first [thinkers] clear to us.","kind":"passage","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_DAZY_BO_4:P0033"]},{"id":"RAW_DAZY_BO_4:P0034","text":"But the matters concerning intellect involve certain difficulties. For it seems to be the most divine of apparent things, but how, being such as it is, it would be so involves certain difficulties. For if it thinks nothing, what would be august about it? Rather, it is in the state in which one would be if asleep. But if it thinks, while something else is sovereign over this, then this—thinking—is not what its substance is, but a capacity; it would not be the best substance. For its worth belongs to it through thinking. Further, whether its substance is intellect or thinking, what does it think? For it either thinks itself or something else; and if something else, either always the same thing or a different thing. Does it then make any difference, or none, whether it thinks what is beautiful or whatever happens [to come before it]? Or is it even absurd to think about some things? Clearly, then, it thinks what is most divine and most worthy, and it does not change; for change is for the worse, and such a thing would already be a kind of motion.","kind":"passage","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_DAZY_BO_4:P0034"]},{"id":"RAW_DAZY_BO_4:P0035","text":"First, then, if it is not thinking but a capacity, it is reasonable that the continuity of thinking should be burdensome to it. Next, it is clear that something other than intellect, namely the object thought, would be more worthy. For thinking and thought will belong even to one thinking what is worst; so that if this is to be avoided (for it is better not to see some things than to see them), thinking would not be the best thing. Therefore it thinks itself, if indeed it is the most powerful thing, and thought is thought of thought. Yet knowledge and perception and opinion and understanding always appear to be of something else, and of themselves only incidentally. Further, if thinking and being thought are different, in respect of which does well-being belong to it? For being is not the same for thought and for the object thought. Or is it that in some cases knowledge is the thing: in productive sciences, the substance and the what-it-was-to-be are without matter; in theoretical sciences, the account is the thing and the thought? Therefore, where the object thought and intellect are not different, in things that do not have matter, they will be the same, and the thought will be one with the object thought.","kind":"passage","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_DAZY_BO_4:P0035"]},{"id":"RAW_DAZY_BO_4:P0036","text":"There still remains a difficulty: is the object thought composite? For then it would change among the parts of the whole. Or is everything that has no matter indivisible—just as the human intellect, or at least [the intellect] of composite things, has [the good] in some time (for it does not have the good in this [part] or in that [part], but has its best in a certain whole, which is something else)—whereas thought itself has itself in this way throughout all eternity?","kind":"passage","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_DAZY_BO_4:P0036"]},{"id":"RAW_DAZY_BO_4:P0037","text":"One must also consider in which of two ways the nature of the whole has the good and the best: as something separate and itself by itself, or as order. Or in both ways, as an army does? For the good is both in the order and in the general, and more in the latter; for he does not exist because of the order, but it exists because of him. And all things are somehow arranged together, though not alike—things that swim, things that fly, and plants. And it is not so ordered that there is no relation at all of one to the other, but there is some. For all are arranged together in relation to one thing; but as in a household, the free have least license to do whatever happens [to occur to them], but all or most things are ordered, while for slaves and beasts there is little that is directed to the common good, and much is whatever happens [to occur]. For the nature of each of them is such a principle.","kind":"passage","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_DAZY_BO_4:P0037"]},{"id":"RAW_DAZY_BO_4:P0038","text":"I mean, for example, that all must come to separation, and there are other things of this sort which all share for the sake of the whole.—But one must not fail to notice what impossibilities or absurdities result for those who speak otherwise, what [results] for those who speak more elegantly, and in regard to which [views] the difficulties are least. For all make all things out of contraries. But neither that all things are [made] nor that [they are made] out of contraries is correct; nor do they say how things in which contraries are present will come from contraries, for contraries are unaffected by one another. But for us this is reasonably solved by there being some third thing. Others make one of the contraries matter, as those who make the unequal matter for the equal, or the many for the one. But this too is solved in the same way; for the one matter is contrary to nothing. Further, all things except the one will partake of the bad; for evil itself is the other of the elements. But the others do not even make the good and the bad principles, although the good is most of all a principle in all things.","kind":"passage","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_DAZY_BO_4:P0038"]},{"id":"RAW_DAZY_BO_4:P0039","text":"Others correctly hold that it is a principle, but do not say how the good is a principle—whether as an end, or as that which moves, or as form. Empedocles too is absurd; for he makes love the good, but this is a principle both as moving (for it brings together) and as matter, for it is a part of the mixture. Now even if it happens to the same thing to be a principle both as matter and as mover, still its being is not the same. In which respect, then, is love [a principle]? It is also absurd that strife should be imperishable; yet this is for him the nature of evil. Anaxagoras makes the good a principle as moving; for intellect moves. But it moves for the sake of something, so that [this is] something else, except in the way we state; for medicine is in some way health. It is also absurd not to make an opposite to the good and to intellect. Yet all who speak of contraries do not use the contraries, unless one makes some qualification. And why some things are perishable and others imperishable, no one says; for they make all beings from the same principles.","kind":"passage","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_DAZY_BO_4:P0039"]},{"id":"RAW_DAZY_BO_4:P0040","text":"Further, some make beings out of what is not; while others, in order not to be compelled to do this, make all things one.—Further, why generation will always exist and what the cause of generation is, no one says. And for those who make two principles, it is necessary that there be another, more sovereign principle; and for those who make the Forms [principles], there must be yet another, more sovereign principle. For why did [things] participate, or why do they participate? And for the others it is necessary that there be something contrary to wisdom and to the most honorable science, but not for us. For nothing is contrary to the first; for all contraries have matter, and are potentially such things; and the ignorance contrary [to knowledge] is directed toward the contrary, but there is nothing contrary to the first. And if there are no things besides sensibles, there will be no principle, order, generation, or heavenly things; rather, there will always be a principle of principle, as for all the theologians and natural philosophers. But if there are Forms or numbers, they are causes of nothing; and if not, certainly not of motion.","kind":"passage","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_DAZY_BO_4:P0040"]},{"id":"RAW_DAZY_BO_4:P0041","text":"Again, how will magnitude and continuity arise from things without magnitude? For number will not produce continuity, either as a mover or as a form. Yet surely there will be none of the contraries which is both productive and capable of moving? For it might then not be. Yet surely acting is posterior to capacity. Therefore the things that are are not eternal. But they are; therefore one of these positions must be rejected. And how this is to be done has been stated.\n\nAgain, by what are numbers one, or soul and body, and in general form and the thing? No one says anything; nor is it possible to say, unless one speaks as we do, namely, that the mover makes them one. But those who posit mathematical number as first, and then in this way always another substance following it, and different principles for each, make the substance of the whole episodic (for one contributes nothing to another, whether it is or is not), and make the principles many; but the things that are do not wish to be governed badly. “The rule of many is not good; let there be one ruler.”","kind":"passage","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_DAZY_BO_4:P0041"]}],"source_digest":"eecb376683ff7157d261c5d7095f5b6da7e238766f77faef354d8ea29aa330af","source_language_code":"grc","original_title":"Book 12"}],"passage_basis":"frozen-grounding","original_title":"Book 12","title_language_code":"en"},{"id":"RAW_DAZY_BO_5","title":"Book 13","order":13,"chapter_id":"CH_DAZY_BO_5","text":"περὶ μὲν οὖν τῆς τῶν αἰσθητῶν οὐσίας εἴρηται τίς ἐστιν, ἐν μὲν τῇ μεθόδῳ τῇ τῶν φυσικῶν περὶ τῆς ὕλης, ὕστερον δὲ περὶ τῆς κατʼ ἐνέργειαν· ἐπεὶ δʼ ἡ σκέψις ἐστὶ πότερον ἔστι τις παρὰ τὰς αἰσθητὰς οὐσίας ἀκίνητος καὶ ἀΐδιος ἢ οὐκ ἔστι, καὶ εἰ ἔστι τίς ἐστι, πρῶτον τὰ παρὰ τῶν ἄλλων λεγόμενα θεωρητέον, ὅπως εἴτε τι μὴ καλῶς λέγουσι, μὴ τοῖς αὐτοῖς ἔνοχοι ὦμεν, καὶ εἴ τι δόγμα κοινὸν ἡμῖν κἀκείνοις, τοῦτʼ ἰδίᾳ μὴ καθʼ ἡμῶν δυσχεραίνωμεν· ἀγαπητὸν γὰρ εἴ τις τὰ μὲν κάλλιον λέγοι τὰ δὲ μὴ χεῖρον. δύο δʼ εἰσὶ δόξαι περὶ τούτων· τά τε γὰρ μαθηματικά φασιν οὐσίας εἶναί τινες, οἷον ἀριθμοὺς καὶ γραμμὰς καὶ τὰ συγγενῆ τούτοις, καὶ πάλιν τὰς ἰδέας. ἐπεὶ δὲ οἱ μὲν δύο ταῦτα γένη ποιοῦσι, τάς τε ἰδέας καὶ τοὺς μαθηματικοὺς ἀριθμούς, οἱ δὲ μίαν φύσιν ἀμφοτέρων, ἕτεροι δέ τινες τὰς μαθηματικὰς μόνον οὐσίας εἶναί φασι, σκεπτέον πρῶτον μὲν περὶ τῶν μαθηματικῶν, μηδεμίαν προστιθέντας φύσιν ἄλλην αὐτοῖς, οἷον πότερον ἰδέαι τυγχάνουσιν οὖσαι ἢ οὔ, καὶ πότερον ἀρχαὶ καὶ οὐσίαι τῶν ὄντων ἢ οὔ, ἀλλʼ ὡς περὶ μαθηματικῶν μόνον εἴτʼ εἰσὶν εἴτε μὴ εἰσί, καὶ εἰ εἰσὶ πῶς εἰσίν· ἔπειτα μετὰ ταῦτα χωρὶς περὶ τῶν ἰδεῶν αὐτῶν ἁπλῶς καὶ ὅσον νόμου χάριν· τεθρύληται γὰρ τὰ πολλὰ καὶ ὑπὸ τῶν ἐξωτερικῶν λόγων, ἔτι δὲ πρὸς ἐκείνην δεῖ τὴν σκέψιν ἀπαντᾶν τὸν πλείω λόγον, ὅταν ἐπισκοπῶμεν εἰ αἱ οὐσίαι καὶ αἱ ἀρχαὶ τῶν ὄντων ἀριθμοὶ καὶ ἰδέαι εἰσίν· μετὰ γὰρ τὰς ἰδέας αὕτη λείπεται τρίτη σκέψις.—ἀνάγκη δʼ, εἴπερ ἔστι τὰ μαθηματικά, ἢ ἐν τοῖς αἰσθητοῖς εἶναι αὐτὰ καθάπερ λέγουσί τινες, ἢ κεχωρισμένα τῶν αἰσθητῶν (λέγουσι δὲ καὶ οὕτω τινές)· ἢ εἰ μηδετέρως, ἢ οὐκ εἰσὶν ἢ ἄλλον τρόπον εἰσίν· ὥσθʼ ἡ ἀμφισβήτησις ἡμῖν ἔσται οὐ περὶ τοῦ εἶναι ἀλλὰ περὶ τοῦ τρόπου. \n\n\n\n \n\n \n\nὅτι μὲν τοίνυν ἔν γε τοῖς αἰσθητοῖς ἀδύνατον εἶναι καὶ ἅμα πλασματίας ὁ λόγος, εἴρηται μὲν καὶ ἐν τοῖς διαπορήμασιν ὅτι δύο ἅμα στερεὰ εἶναι ἀδύνατον,  ἔτι δὲ καὶ ὅτι τοῦ αὐτοῦ λόγου καὶ τὰς ἄλλας δυνάμεις καὶ φύσεις ἐν τοῖς αἰσθητοῖς εἶναι καὶ μηδεμίαν κεχωρισμένην·—ταῦτα μὲν οὖν εἴρηται πρότερον, ἀλλὰ πρὸς τούτοις φανερὸν ὅτι ἀδύνατον διαιρεθῆναι ὁτιοῦν σῶμα· κατʼ ἐπίπεδον γὰρ διαιρεθήσεται, καὶ τοῦτο κατὰ γραμμὴν καὶ αὕτη κατὰ στιγμήν, ὥστʼ εἰ τὴν στιγμὴν διελεῖν ἀδύνατον, καὶ τὴν γραμμήν, εἰ δὲ ταύτην, καὶ τἆλλα. τί οὖν διαφέρει ἢ ταύτας εἶναι τοιαύτας φύσεις, ἢ αὐτὰς μὲν μή, εἶναι δʼ ἐν αὐταῖς τοιαύτας φύσεις; τὸ αὐτὸ γὰρ συμβήσεται· διαιρουμένων γὰρ τῶν αἰσθητῶν διαιρεθήσονται, ἢ οὐδὲ αἱ αἰσθηταί. ἀλλὰ μὴν οὐδὲ κεχωρισμένας γʼ εἶναι φύσεις τοιαύτας δυνατόν. εἰ γὰρ ἔσται στερεὰ παρὰ τὰ αἰσθητὰ κεχωρισμένα τούτων ἕτερα καὶ πρότερα τῶν αἰσθητῶν, δῆλον ὅτι καὶ παρὰ τὰ ἐπίπεδα ἕτερα ἀναγκαῖον εἶναι ἐπίπεδα κεχωρισμένα καὶ στιγμὰς καὶ γραμμάς (τοῦ γὰρ αὐτοῦ λόγου)· εἰ δὲ ταῦτα, πάλιν παρὰ τὰ τοῦ στερεοῦ τοῦ μαθηματικοῦ ἐπίπεδα καὶ γραμμὰς καὶ στιγμὰς ἕτερα κεχωρισμένα (πρότερα γὰρ τῶν συγκειμένων ἐστὶ τὰ ἀσύνθετα· καὶ εἴπερ τῶν αἰσθητῶν πρότερα σώματα μὴ αἰσθητά, τῷ αὐτῷ λόγῳ καὶ τῶν ἐπιπέδων τῶν ἐν τοῖς ἀκινήτοις στερεοῖς τὰ αὐτὰ καθʼ αὑτά, ὥστε ἕτερα ταῦτα ἐπίπεδα καὶ γραμμαὶ τῶν ἅμα τοῖς στερεοῖς τοῖς κεχωρισμένοις· τὰ μὲν γὰρ ἅμα τοῖς μαθηματικοῖς στερεοῖς τὰ δὲ πρότερα τῶν μαθηματικῶν στερεῶν). πάλιν τοίνυν τούτων τῶν ἐπιπέδων ἔσονται γραμμαί, ὧν πρότερον δεήσει ἑτέρας γραμμὰς καὶ στιγμὰς εἶναι διὰ τὸν αὐτὸν λόγον· καὶ τούτων τῶν ἐκ ταῖς προτέραις γραμμαῖς ἑτέρας προτέρας στιγμάς, ὧν οὐκέτι πρότεραι ἕτεραι. ἄτοπός τε δὴ γίγνεται ἡ σώρευσις (συμβαίνει γὰρ στερεὰ μὲν μοναχὰ παρὰ τὰ αἰσθητά, ἐπίπεδα δὲ τριττὰ παρὰ τὰ αἰσθητά—τά τε παρὰ τὰ αἰσθητὰ καὶ τὰ ἐν τοῖς μαθηματικοῖς στερεοῖς καὶ τὰ παρὰ τὰ ἐν τούτοις—γραμμαὶ δὲ τετραξαί, στιγμαὶ δὲ πενταξαί· ὥστε περὶ ποῖα αἱ ἐπιστῆμαι ἔσονται αἱ μαθηματικαὶ τούτων; οὐ γὰρ δὴ περὶ τὰ ἐν τῷ στερεῷ τῷ ἀκινήτῳ ἐπίπεδα καὶ γραμμὰς καὶ στιγμάς· ἀεὶ γὰρ περὶ τὰ πρότερα ἡ ἐπιστήμη)· ὁ δʼ αὐτὸς λόγος καὶ περὶ τῶν ἀριθμῶν· παρʼ ἑκάστας γὰρ τὰς στιγμὰς ἕτεραι ἔσονται μονάδες, καὶ παρʼ ἕκαστα τὰ ὄντα, τὰ αἰσθητά, εἶτα τὰ νοητά, ὥστʼ ἔσται γένη τῶν μαθηματικῶν ἀριθμῶν. ἔτι ἅπερ καὶ ἐν τοῖς ἀπορήμασιν ἐπήλθομεν πῶς ἐνδέχεται λύειν;  περὶ ἃ γὰρ ἡ ἀστρολογία ἐστίν, ὁμοίως ἔσται παρὰ τὰ αἰσθητὰ καὶ περὶ ἃ ἡ γεωμετρία· εἶναι δʼ οὐρανὸν καὶ τὰ μόρια αὐτοῦ πῶς δυνατόν, ἢ ἄλλο ὁτιοῦν ἔχον κίνησιν; ὁμοίως δὲ καὶ τὰ ὀπτικὰ καὶ τὰ ἁρμονικά· ἔσται γὰρ φωνή τε καὶ ὄψις παρὰ τὰ αἰσθητὰ καὶ τὰ καθʼ ἕκαστα, ὥστε δῆλον ὅτι καὶ αἱ ἄλλαι αἰσθήσεις καὶ τὰ ἄλλα αἰσθητά· τί γὰρ μᾶλλον τάδε ἢ τάδε; εἰ δὲ ταῦτα, καὶ ζῷα ἔσονται, εἴπερ καὶ αἰσθήσεις. ἔτι γράφεται ἔνια καθόλου ὑπὸ τῶν μαθηματικῶν παρὰ ταύτας τὰς οὐσίας. ἔσται οὖν καὶ αὕτη τις ἄλλη οὐσία μεταξὺ κεχωρισμένη τῶν τʼ ἰδεῶν καὶ τῶν μεταξύ, ἣ οὔτε ἀριθμός ἐστιν οὔτε στιγμαὶ οὔτε μέγεθος οὔτε χρόνος. εἰ δὲ τοῦτο ἀδύνατον, δῆλον ὅτι κἀκεῖνα ἀδύνατον εἶναι κεχωρισμένα τῶν αἰσθητῶν. ὅλως δὲ τοὐναντίον συμβαίνει καὶ τοῦ ἀληθοῦς καὶ τοῦ εἰωθότος ὑπολαμβάνεσθαι, εἴ τις θήσει οὕτως εἶναι τὰ μαθηματικὰ ὡς κεχωρισμένας τινὰς φύσεις. ἀνάγκη γὰρ διὰ τὸ μὲν οὕτως εἶναι αὐτὰς προτέρας εἶναι τῶν αἰσθητῶν μεγεθῶν, κατὰ τὸ ἀληθὲς δὲ ὑστέρας· τὸ γὰρ ἀτελὲς μέγεθος γενέσει μὲν πρότερόν ἐστι, τῇ οὐσίᾳ δʼ ὕστερον, οἷον ἄψυχον ἐμψύχου. ἔτι τίνι καὶ πότʼ ἔσται ἓν τὰ μαθηματικὰ μεγέθη; τὰ μὲν γὰρ ἐνταῦθα ψυχῇ ἢ μέρει ψυχῆς ἢ ἄλλῳ τινί, εὐλόγως (εἰ δὲ μή, πολλά, καὶ διαλύεται), ἐκείνοις δὲ διαιρετοῖς καὶ ποσοῖς οὖσι τί αἴτιον τοῦ ἓν εἶναι καὶ συμμένειν; ἔτι αἱ γενέσεις δηλοῦσιν. πρῶτον μὲν γὰρ ἐπὶ μῆκος γίγνεται, εἶτα ἐπὶ πλάτος, τελευταῖον δʼ εἰς βάθος, καὶ τέλος ἔσχεν. εἰ οὖν τὸ τῇ γενέσει ὕστερον τῇ οὐσίᾳ πρότερον, τὸ σῶμα πρότερον ἂν εἴη ἐπιπέδου καὶ μήκους· καὶ ταύτῃ καὶ τέλειον καὶ ὅλον μᾶλλον, ὅτι ἔμψυχον γίγνεται· γραμμὴ δὲ ἔμψυχος ἢ ἐπίπεδον πῶς ἂν εἴη; ὑπὲρ γὰρ τὰς αἰσθήσεις τὰς ἡμετέρας ἂν εἴη τὸ ἀξίωμα. ἔτι τὸ μὲν σῶμα οὐσία τις (ἤδη γὰρ ἔχει πως τὸ τέλειον), αἱ δὲ γραμμαὶ πῶς οὐσίαι; οὔτε γὰρ ὡς εἶδος καὶ μορφή τις, οἷον εἰ ἄρα ἡ ψυχὴ τοιοῦτον, οὔτε ὡς ἡ ὕλη, οἷον τὸ σῶμα· οὐθὲν γὰρ ἐκ γραμμῶν οὐδʼ ἐπιπέδων οὐδὲ στιγμῶν φαίνεται συνίστασθαι δυνάμενον, εἰ δʼ ἦν οὐσία τις ὑλική, τοῦτʼ ἂν ἐφαίνετο δυνάμενα πάσχειν. τῷ μὲν οὖν λόγῳ ἔστω πρότερα,  ἀλλʼ οὐ πάντα ὅσα τῷ λόγῳ πρότερα καὶ τῇ οὐσίᾳ πρότερα. τῇ μὲν γὰρ οὐσίᾳ πρότερα ὅσα χωριζόμενα τῷ εἶναι ὑπερβάλλει, τῷ λόγῳ δὲ ὅσων οἱ λόγοι ἐκ τῶν λόγων· ταῦτα δὲ οὐχ ἅμα ὑπάρχει. εἰ γὰρ μὴ ἔστι τὰ πάθη παρὰ τὰς οὐσίας, οἷον κινούμενόν τι ἢ λευκόν, τοῦ λευκοῦ ἀνθρώπου τὸ λευκὸν πρότερον κατὰ τὸν λόγον ἀλλʼ οὐ κατὰ τὴν οὐσίαν· οὐ γὰρ ἐνδέχεται εἶναι κεχωρισμένον ἀλλʼ ἀεὶ ἅμα τῷ συνόλῳ ἐστίν (σύνολον δὲ λέγω τὸν ἄνθρωπον τὸν λευκόν), ὥστε φανερὸν ὅτι οὔτε τὸ ἐξ ἀφαιρέσεως πρότερον οὔτε τὸ ἐκ προσθέσεως ὕστερον· ἐκ προσθέσεως γὰρ τῷ λευκῷ ὁ λευκὸς ἄνθρωπος λέγεται.\n\n \n\nὅτι μὲν οὖν οὔτε οὐσίαι μᾶλλον τῶν σωμάτων εἰσὶν οὔτε πρότερα τῷ εἶναι τῶν αἰσθητῶν ἀλλὰ τῷ λόγῳ μόνον, οὔτε κεχωρισμένα που εἶναι δυνατόν, εἴρηται ἱκανῶς· ἐπεὶ δʼ οὐδʼ ἐν τοῖς αἰσθητοῖς ἐνεδέχετο αὐτὰ εἶναι, φανερὸν ὅτι ἢ ὅλως οὐκ ἔστιν ἢ τρόπον τινὰ ἔστι καὶ διὰ τοῦτο οὐχ ἁπλῶς ἔστιν· πολλαχῶς γὰρ τὸ εἶναι λέγομεν.\n\n\n\n \n\n \n\nὥσπερ γὰρ καὶ τὰ καθόλου ἐν τοῖς μαθήμασιν οὐ περὶ κεχωρισμένων ἐστὶ παρὰ τὰ μεγέθη καὶ τοὺς ἀριθμοὺς ἀλλὰ περὶ τούτων μέν, οὐχ ᾗ δὲ τοιαῦτα οἷα ἔχειν μέγεθος ἢ εἶναι διαιρετά, δῆλον ὅτι ἐνδέχεται καὶ περὶ τῶν αἰσθητῶν μεγεθῶν εἶναι καὶ λόγους καὶ ἀποδείξεις, μὴ ᾗ δὲ αἰσθητὰ ἀλλʼ ᾗ τοιαδί. ὥσπερ γὰρ καὶ ᾗ κινούμενα μόνον πολλοὶ λόγοι εἰσί, χωρὶς τοῦ τί ἕκαστόν ἐστι τῶν τοιούτων καὶ τῶν συμβεβηκότων αὐτοῖς, καὶ οὐκ ἀνάγκη διὰ ταῦτα ἢ κεχωρισμένον τι εἶναι κινούμενον τῶν αἰσθητῶν ἢ ἐν τούτοις τινὰ φύσιν εἶναι ἀφωρισμένην, οὕτω καὶ ἐπὶ τῶν κινουμένων ἔσονται λόγοι καὶ ἐπιστῆμαι, οὐχ ᾗ κινούμενα δὲ ἀλλʼ ᾗ σώματα μόνον, καὶ πάλιν ᾗ ἐπίπεδα μόνον καὶ ᾗ μήκη μόνον, καὶ ᾗ διαιρετὰ καὶ ᾗ ἀδιαίρετα ἔχοντα δὲ θέσιν καὶ ᾗ ἀδιαίρετα μόνον, ὥστʼ ἐπεὶ ἁπλῶς λέγειν ἀληθὲς μὴ μόνον τὰ χωριστὰ εἶναι ἀλλὰ καὶ τὰ μὴ χωριστά (οἷον κινούμενα εἶναι), καὶ τὰ μαθηματικὰ ὅτι ἔστιν ἁπλῶς ἀληθὲς εἰπεῖν, καὶ τοιαῦτά γε οἷα λέγουσιν. καὶ ὥσπερ καὶ τὰς ἄλλας ἐπιστήμας ἁπλῶς ἀληθὲς εἰπεῖν τούτου εἶναι, οὐχὶ τοῦ συμβεβηκότος (οἷον ὅτι λευκοῦ, εἰ τὸ ὑγιεινὸν λευκόν, ἡ δʼ ἔστιν ὑγιεινοῦ) ἀλλʼ ἐκείνου οὗ ἐστὶν ἑκάστη,  εἰ ᾗ ὑγιεινὸν ὑγιεινοῦ, εἰ δʼ ᾗ ἄνθρωπος ἀνθρώπου, οὕτω καὶ τὴν γεωμετρίαν· οὐκ εἰ συμβέβηκεν αἰσθητὰ εἶναι ὧν ἐστί, μὴ ἔστι δὲ ᾗ αἰσθητά, οὐ τῶν αἰσθητῶν ἔσονται αἱ μαθηματικαὶ ἐπιστῆμαι, οὐ μέντοι οὐσὲ παρὰ ταῦτα ἄλλων κεχωρισμένων. πολλὰ δὲ συμβέβηκε καθʼ αὑτὰ τοῖς πράγμασιν ᾗ ἕκαστον ὑπάρχει τῶν τοιούτων, ἐπεὶ καὶ ᾗ θῆλυ τὸ ζῷον καὶ ᾗ ἄρρεν, ἴδια πάθη ἔστιν (καίτοι οὐκ ἔστι τι θῆλυ οὐδʼ ἄρρεν κεχωρισμένον τῶν ζῴων)· ὥστε καὶ ᾗ μήκη μόνον καὶ ᾗ ἐπίπεδα. καὶ ὅσῳ δὴ ἂν περὶ προτέρων τῷ λόγῳ καὶ ἁπλουστέρων, τοσούτῳ μᾶλλον ἔχει τὸ ἀκριβές (τοῦτο δὲ τὸ ἁπλοῦν ἐστίν), ὥστε ἄνευ τε μεγέθους μᾶλλον ἢ μετὰ μεγέθους, καὶ μάλιστα ἄνευ κινήσεως, ἐὰν δὲ κίνησιν, μάλιστα τὴν πρώτην· ἁπλουστάτη γάρ, καὶ ταύτης ἡ ὁμαλή. ὁ δʼ αὐτὸς λόγος καὶ περὶ ἁρμονικῆς καὶ ὀπτικῆς· οὐδετέρα γὰρ ᾗ ὄψις ἢ ᾗ φωνὴ θεωρεῖ, ἀλλʼ ᾗ γραμμαὶ καὶ ἀριθμοί (οἰκεῖα μέντοι ταῦτα πάθη ἐκείνων), καὶ ἡ μηχανικὴ δὲ ὡσαύτως, ὥστʼ εἴ τις θέμενος κεχωρισμένα τῶν συμβεβηκότων σκοπεῖ τι περὶ τούτων ᾗ τοιαῦτα, οὐθὲν διὰ τοῦτο ψεῦδος ψεύσεται, ὥσπερ οὐδʼ ὅταν ἐν τῇ γῇ γράφῃ καὶ ποδιαίαν φῇ τὴν μὴ ποδιαίαν· οὐ γὰρ ἐν ταῖς προτάσεσι τὸ ψεῦδος. ἄριστα δʼ ἂν οὕτω θεωρηθείη ἕκαστον, εἴ τις τὸ μὴ κεχωρισμένον θείη χωρίσας, ὅπερ ὁ ἀριθμητικὸς ποιεῖ καὶ ὁ γεωμέτρης. ἓν μὲν γὰρ καὶ ἀδιαίρετον ὁ ἄνθρωπος ᾗ ἄνθρωπος· ὁ δʼ ἔθετο ἓν ἀδιαίρετον, εἶτʼ ἐθεώρησεν εἴ τι τῷ ἀνθρώπῳ συμβέβηκεν ᾗ ἀδιαίρετος. ὁ δὲ γεωμέτρης οὔθʼ ᾗ ἄνθρωπος οὔθʼ ᾗ ἀδιαίρετος ἀλλʼ ᾗ στερεόν. ἃ γὰρ κἂν εἰ μή που ἦν ἀδιαίρετος ὑπῆρχεν αὐτῷ, δῆλον ὅτι καὶ ἄνευ τούτων ἐνδέχεται αὐτῷ ὑπάρχειν , ὥστε διὰ τοῦτο ὀρθῶς οἱ γεωμέτραι λέγουσι, καὶ περὶ ὄντων διαλέγονται, καὶ ὄντα ἐστίν· διττὸν γὰρ τὸ ὄν, τὸ μὲν ἐντελεχείᾳ τὸ δʼ ὑλικῶς. ἐπεὶ δὲ τὸ ἀγαθὸν καὶ τὸ καλὸν ἕτερον (τὸ μὲν γὰρ ἀεὶ ἐν πράξει, τὸ δὲ καλὸν καὶ ἐν τοῖς ἀκινήτοις), οἱ φάσκοντες οὐδὲν λέγειν τὰς μαθηματικὰς ἐπιστήμας περὶ καλοῦ ἢ ἀγαθοῦ ψεύδονται. λέγουσι γὰρ καὶ δεικνύουσι μάλιστα· οὐ γὰρ εἰ μὴ ὀνομάζουσι τὰ δʼ ἔργα καὶ τοὺς λόγους δεικνύουσιν, οὐ λέγουσι περὶ αὐτῶν. τοῦ δὲ καλοῦ μέγιστα εἴδη τάξις καὶ συμμετρία καὶ τὸ ὡρισμένον,  ἃ μάλιστα δεικνύουσιν αἱ μαθηματικαὶ ἐπιστῆμαι. καὶ ἐπεί γε πολλῶν αἴτια φαίνεται ταῦτα (λέγω δʼ οἷον ἡ τάξις καὶ τὸ ὡρισμένον), δῆλον ὅτι λέγοιεν ἂν καὶ τὴν τοιαύτην αἰτίαν τὴν ὡς τὸ καλὸν αἴτιον τρόπον τινά. μᾶλλον δὲ γνωρίμως ἐν ἄλλοις περὶ αὐτῶν ἐροῦμεν. \n\n\n\n \n\n \n\nπερὶ μὲν οὖν τῶν μαθηματικῶν, ὅτι τε ὄντα ἐστὶ καὶ πῶς ὄντα, καὶ πῶς πρότερα καὶ πῶς οὐ πρότερα, τοσαῦτα εἰρήσθω· περὶ δὲ τῶν ἰδεῶν πρῶτον αὐτὴν τὴν κατὰ τὴν ἰδέαν δόξαν ἐπισκεπτέον, μηθὲν συνάπτοντας πρὸς τὴν τῶν ἀριθμῶν φύσιν, ἀλλʼ ὡς ὑπέλαβον ἐξ ἀρχῆς οἱ πρῶτοι τὰς ἰδέας φήσαντες εἶναι. συνέβη δʼ ἡ περὶ τῶν εἰδῶν δόξα τοῖς εἰποῦσι διὰ τὸ πεισθῆναι περὶ τῆς ἀληθείας τοῖς Ἡρακλειτείοις λόγοις ὡς πάντων τῶν αἰσθητῶν ἀεὶ ῥεόντων, ὥστʼ εἴπερ ἐπιστήμη τινὸς ἔσται καὶ φρόνησις, ἑτέρας δεῖν τινὰς φύσεις εἶναι παρὰ τὰς αἰσθητὰς μενούσας· οὐ γὰρ εἶναι τῶν ῥεόντων ἐπιστήμην. Σωκράτους δὲ περὶ τὰς ἠθικὰς ἀρετὰς πραγματευομένου καὶ περὶ τούτων ὁρίζεσθαι καθόλου ζητοῦντος πρώτου (τῶν μὲν γὰρ φυσικῶν ἐπὶ μικρὸν Δημόκριτος ἥψατο μόνον καὶ ὡρίσατό πως τὸ θερμὸν καὶ τὸ ψυχρόν· οἱ δὲ Πυθαγόρειοι πρότερον περί τινων ὀλίγων, ὧν τοὺς λόγους εἰς τοὺς ἀριθμοὺς ἀνῆπτον, οἷον τί ἐστι καιρὸς ἢ τὸ δίκαιον ἢ γάμος· ἐκεῖνος δʼ εὐλόγως ἐζήτει τὸ τί ἐστιν· συλλογίζεσθαι γὰρ ἐζήτει, ἀρχὴ δὲ τῶν συλλογισμῶν τὸ τί ἐστιν· διαλεκτικὴ γὰρ ἰσχὺς οὔπω τότʼ ἦν ὥστε δύνασθαι καὶ χωρὶς τοῦ τί ἐστι τἀναντία ἐπισκοπεῖν, καὶ τῶν ἐναντίων εἰ ἡ αὐτὴ ἐπιστήμη· δύο γάρ ἐστιν ἅ τις ἂν ἀποδοίη Σωκράτει δικαίως, τούς τʼ ἐπακτικοὺς λόγους καὶ τὸ ὁρίζεσθαι καθόλου· ταῦτα γάρ ἐστιν ἄμφω περὶ ἀρχὴν ἐπιστήμης)·—ἀλλʼ ὁ μὲν Σωκράτης τὰ καθόλου οὐ χωριστὰ ἐποίει οὐδὲ τοὺς ὁρισμούς· οἱ δʼ ἐχώρισαν, καὶ τὰ τοιαῦτα τῶν ὄντων ἰδέας προσηγόρευσαν, ὥστε συνέβαινεν αὐτοῖς σχεδὸν τῷ αὐτῷ λόγῳ πάντων ἰδέας εἶναι τῶν καθόλου λεγομένων, καὶ παραπλήσιον ὥσπερ ἂν εἴ τις ἀριθμῆσαι βουλόμενος ἐλαττόνων μὲν ὄντων οἴοιτο μὴ δυνήσεσθαι, πλείω δὲ ποιήσας ἀριθμοίη· πλείω γάρ ἐστι τῶν καθʼ ἕκαστα αἰσθητῶν ὡς εἰπεῖν τὰ εἴδη,  περὶ ὧν ζητοῦντες τὰς αἰτίας ἐκ τούτων ἐκεῖ προῆλθον· καθʼ ἕκαστόν τε γὰρ ὁμώνυμόν τι ἔστι καὶ παρὰ τὰς οὐσίας, τῶν τε ἄλλων ἓν ἔστιν ἐπὶ πολλῶν, καὶ ἐπὶ τοῖσδε καὶ ἐπὶ τοῖς ἀϊδίοις. ἔτι καθʼ οὓς τρόπους δείκνυται ὅτι ἔστι τὰ εἴδη, κατʼ οὐθένα φαίνεται τούτων· ἐξ ἐνίων μὲν γὰρ οὐκ ἀνάγκη γίγνεσθαι συλλογισμόν, ἐξ ἐνίων δὲ καὶ οὐχ ὧν οἴονται τούτων εἴδη γίγνεται. κατά τε γὰρ τοὺς λόγους τοὺς ἐκ τῶν ἐπιστημῶν ἔσται εἴδη πάντων ὅσων ἐπιστῆμαι εἰσίν, καὶ κατὰ τὸ ἓν ἐπὶ πολλῶν καὶ τῶν ἀποφάσεων, κατὰ δὲ τὸ νοεῖν τι φθαρέντος τῶν φθαρτῶν· φάντασμα γάρ τι τούτων ἔστιν. ἔτι δὲ οἱ ἀκριβέστατοι τῶν λόγων οἱ μὲν τῶν πρός τι ποιοῦσιν ἰδέας, ὧν οὔ φασιν εἶναι καθʼ αὑτὸ γένος, οἱ δὲ τὸν τρίτον ἄνθρωπον λέγουσιν. ὅλως τε ἀναιροῦσιν οἱ περὶ τῶν εἰδῶν λόγοι ἃ μᾶλλον βούλονται εἶναι οἱ λέγοντες εἴδη τοῦ τὰς ἰδέας εἶναι· συμβαίνει γὰρ μὴ εἶναι πρῶτον τὴν δυάδα ἀλλὰ τὸν ἀριθμόν, καὶ τούτου τὸ πρός τι καὶ τοῦτο τοῦ καθʼ αὑτό, καὶ πάνθʼ ὅσα τινὲς ἀκολουθήσαντες ταῖς περὶ τῶν εἰδῶν δόξαις ἠναντιώθησαν ταῖς ἀρχαῖς. ἔτι κατὰ μὲν τὴν ὑπόληψιν καθʼ ἥν φασιν εἶναι τὰς ἰδέας οὐ μόνον τῶν οὐσιῶν ἔσονται εἴδη ἀλλὰ καὶ ἄλλων πολλῶν (τὸ γὰρ νόημα ἓν οὐ μόνον περὶ τὰς οὐσίας ἀλλὰ καὶ κατὰ μὴ οὐσιῶν ἐστί, καὶ ἐπιστῆμαι οὐ μόνον τῆς οὐσίας εἰσί· συμβαίνει δὲ καὶ ἄλλα μυρία τοιαῦτα)· κατὰ δὲ τὸ ἀναγκαῖον καὶ τὰς δόξας τὰς περὶ αὐτῶν, εἰ ἔστι μεθεκτὰ τὰ εἴδη, τῶν οὐσιῶν ἀναγκαῖον ἰδέας εἶναι μόνον· οὐ γὰρ κατὰ συμβεβηκὸς μετέχονται ἀλλὰ δεῖ ταύτῃ ἑκάστου μετέχειν ᾗ μὴ καθʼ ὑποκειμένου λέγονται (λέγω δʼ οἷον, εἴ τι αὐτοῦ διπλασίου μετέχει, τοῦτο καὶ ἀϊδίου μετέχει, ἀλλὰ κατὰ συμβεβηκός· συμβέβηκε γὰρ τῷ διπλασίῳ ἀϊδίῳ εἶναι), ὥστε ἔσται οὐσία τὰ εἴδη· ταὐτὰ δʼ ἐνταῦθα οὐσίαν σημαίνει κἀκεῖ· ἢ τί ἔσται τὸ εἶναι φάναι τι παρὰ ταῦτα, τὸ ἓν ἐπὶ πολλῶν; καὶ εἰ μὲν ταὐτὸ εἶδος τῶν ἰδεῶν καὶ τῶν μετεχόντων, ἔσται τι κοινόν (τί γὰρ μᾶλλον ἐπὶ τῶν φθαρτῶν δυάδων, καὶ τῶν δυάδων τῶν πολλῶν μὲν ἀϊδίων δέ, τὸ δυὰς ἓν καὶ ταὐτόν, ἢ ἐπʼ αὐτῆς καὶ τῆς τινός;)· εἰ δὲ μὴ τὸ αὐτὸ εἶδος,  ὁμώνυμα ἂν εἴη, καὶ ὅμοιον ὥσπερ ἂν εἴ τις καλοῖ ἄνθρωπον τόν τε Καλλίαν καὶ τὸ ξύλον, μηδεμίαν κοινωνίαν ἐπιβλέψας αὐτῶν. εἰ δὲ τὰ μὲν ἄλλα τοὺς κοινοὺς λόγους ἐφαρμόττειν θήσομεν τοῖς εἴδεσιν, οἷον ἐπʼ αὐτὸν τὸν κύκλον σχῆμα ἐπίπεδον καὶ τὰ λοιπὰ μέρη τοῦ λόγου, τὸ δʼ ὃ ἔστι προστεθήσεται, σκοπεῖν δεῖ μὴ κενὸν ᾖ τοῦτο παντελῶς. τίνι τε γὰρ προστεθήσεται; τῷ μέσῳ ἢ τῷ ἐπιπέδῳ ἢ πᾶσιν; πάντα γὰρ τὰ ἐν τῇ οὐσίᾳ ἰδέαι, οἷον τὸ ζῷον καὶ τὸ δίπουν. ἔτι δῆλον ὅτι ἀνάγκη αὐτὸ εἶναί τι, ὥσπερ τὸ ἐπίπεδον, φύσιν τινὰ ἣ πᾶσιν ἐνυπάρξει τοῖς εἴδεσιν ὡς γένος.\n\n\n\n \n\n \n\nπάντων δὲ μάλιστα διαπορήσειεν ἄν τις τί ποτε συμβάλλονται τὰ εἴδη ἢ τοῖς ἀϊδίοις τῶν αἰσθητῶν ἢ τοῖς γιγνομένοις καὶ  φθειρομένοις· οὔτε γὰρ κινήσεώς ἐστιν οὔτε μεταβολῆς οὐδεμιᾶς αἴτια αὐτοῖς. ἀλλὰ μὴν οὔτε πρὸς τὴν ἐπιστήμην οὐθὲν βοηθεῖ τὴν τῶν ἄλλων (οὐδὲ γὰρ οὐσία ἐκεῖνα τούτων· ἐν τούτοις γὰρ ἂν ἦν), οὔτʼ εἰς τὸ εἶναι, μὴ ἐνυπάρχοντά γε τοῖς μετέχουσιν· οὕτω μὲν γὰρ ἴσως αἴτια δόξειεν ἂν εἶναι ὡς τὸ λευκὸν μεμιγμένον τῷ λευκῷ, ἀλλʼ οὗτος μὲν ὁ λόγος λίαν εὐκίνητος, ὃν Ἀναξαγόρας μὲν πρότερος Εὔδοξος δὲ ὕστερος ἔλεγε διαπορῶν καὶ ἕτεροί τινες (ῥᾴδιον γὰρ πολλὰ συναγαγεῖν καὶ ἀδύνατα πρὸς τὴν τοιαύτην δόξαν)· ἀλλὰ μὴν οὐδὲ ἐκ τῶν εἰδῶν ἐστὶ τἆλλα κατʼ οὐθένα τρόπον τῶν εἰωθότων λέγεσθαι. τὸ δὲ λέγειν παραδείγματα εἶναι καὶ μετέχειν αὐτῶν τὰ ἄλλα κενολογεῖν ἐστὶ καὶ μεταφορὰς λέγειν ποιητικάς. τί γάρ ἐστι τὸ ἐργαζόμενον πρὸς τὰς ἰδέας ἀποβλέπον; ἐνδέχεταί τε καὶ εἶναι καὶ γίγνεσθαι ὁτιοῦν καὶ μὴ εἰκαζόμενον, ὥστε καὶ ὄντος Σωκράτους καὶ μὴ ὄντος γένοιτʼ ἂν οἷος Σωκράτης· ὁμοίως δὲ δῆλον ὅτι κἂν εἰ ἦν ὁ Σωκράτης ἀΐδιος. ἔσται τε πλείω παραδείγματα τοῦ αὐτοῦ, ὥστε καὶ εἴδη, οἷον τοῦ ἀνθρώπου τὸ ζῷον καὶ τὸ δίπουν, ἅμα δὲ καὶ αὐτοάνθρωπος. ἔτι οὐ μόνον τῶν αἰσθητῶν παραδείγματα τὰ εἴδη ἀλλὰ καὶ αὐτῶν, οἷον τὸ γένος τῶν ὡς γένους εἰδῶν· ὥστε τὸ αὐτὸ ἔσται παράδειγμα καὶ εἰκών. ἔτι δόξειεν ἂν ἀδύνατον χωρὶς εἶναι τὴν οὐσίαν καὶ οὗ ἡ οὐσία·  ὥστε πῶς ἂν αἱ ἰδέαι οὐσίαι τῶν πραγμάτων οὖσαι χωρὶς εἶεν; ἐν δὲ τῷ Φαίδωνι τοῦτον λέγεται τὸν τρόπον, ὡς καὶ τοῦ εἶναι καὶ τοῦ γίγνεσθαι αἴτια τὰ εἴδη ἐστίν· καίτοι τῶν εἰδῶν ὄντων ὅμως οὐ γίγνεται ἂν μὴ ᾖ τὸ κινῆσον, καὶ πολλὰ γίγνεται ἕτερα, οἷον οἰκία καὶ δακτύλιος, ὧν οὔ φασιν εἶναι εἴδη· ὥστε δῆλον ὅτι ἐνδέχεται κἀκεῖνα, ὧν φασὶν ἰδέας εἶναι, καὶ εἶναι καὶ γίγνεσθαι διὰ τοιαύτας αἰτίας οἵας καὶ τὰ ῥηθέντα νῦν, ἀλλʼ οὐ διὰ τὰ εἴδη. ἀλλὰ περὶ μὲν τῶν ἰδεῶν καὶ τοῦτον τὸν τρόπον καὶ διὰ λογικωτέρων καὶ ἀκριβεστέρων λόγων ἔστι πολλὰ συναγαγεῖν ὅμοια τοῖς τεθεωρημένοις.\n\n\n\n \n\n \n\nἐπεὶ δὲ διώρισται περὶ τούτων, καλῶς ἔχει πάλιν θεωρῆσαι τὰ περὶ τοὺς ἀριθμοὺς συμβαίνοντα τοῖς λέγουσιν οὐσίας αὐτοὺς εἶναι χωριστὰς καὶ τῶν ὄντων αἰτίας πρώτας. ἀνάγκη δʼ, εἴπερ ἐστὶν ὁ ἀριθμὸς φύσις τις καὶ μὴ ἄλλη τίς ἐστιν αὐτοῦ ἡ οὐσία ἀλλὰ τοῦτʼ αὐτό, ὥσπερ φασί τινες, ἤτοι εἶναι τὸ μὲν πρῶτόν τι αὐτοῦ τὸ δʼ ἐχόμενον, ἕτερον ὂν τῷ εἴδει ἕκαστον,—καὶ τοῦτο ἢ ἐπὶ τῶν μονάδων εὐθὺς ὑπάρχει καὶ ἔστιν ἀσύμβλητος ὁποιαοῦν μονὰς ὁποιᾳοῦν μονάδι, ἢ εὐθὺς ἐφεξῆς πᾶσαι καὶ συμβληταὶ ὁποιαιοῦν ὁποιαισοῦν, οἷον λέγουσιν εἶναι τὸν μαθηματικὸν ἀριθμόν (ἐν γὰρ τῷ μαθηματικῷ οὐδὲν διαφέρει οὐδεμία μονὰς ἑτέρα ἑτέρας)· ἢ τὰς μὲν συμβλητὰς τὰς δὲ μή (οἷον εἰ ἔστι μετὰ τὸ ἓν πρώτη ἡ δυάς, ἔπειτα ἡ τριὰς καὶ οὕτω δὴ ὁ ἄλλος ἀριθμός, εἰσὶ δὲ συμβληταὶ αἱ ἐν ἑκάστῳ ἀριθμῷ μονάδες, οἷον αἱ ἐν τῇ δυάδι τῇ πρώτῃ αὑταῖς, καὶ αἱ ἐν τῇ τριάδι τῇ πρώτῃ αὑταῖς, καὶ οὕτω δὴ ἐπὶ τῶν ἄλλων ἀριθμῶν· αἱ δʼ ἐν τῇ δυάδι αὐτῇ πρὸς τὰς ἐν τῇ τριάδι αὐτῇ ἀσύμβλητοι, ὁμοίως δὲ καὶ ἐπὶ τῶν ἄλλων τῶν ἐφεξῆς ἀριθμῶν· διὸ καὶ ὁ μὲν μαθηματικὸς ἀριθμεῖται μετὰ τὸ ἓν δύο, πρὸς τῷ ἔμπροσθεν ἑνὶ ἄλλο ἕν, καὶ τὰ τρία πρὸς τοῖς δυσὶ τούτοις ἄλλο ἕν, καὶ ὁ λοιπὸς δὲ ὡσαύτως· οὗτος δὲ μετὰ τὸ ἓν δύο ἕτερα ἄνευ τοῦ ἑνὸς τοῦ πρώτου, καὶ ἡ τριὰς ἄνευ τῆς δυάδος, ὁμοίως δὲ καὶ ὁ ἄλλος ἀριθμός)· ἢ τὸν μὲν εἶναι τῶν ἀριθμῶν οἷος ὁ πρῶτος ἐλέχθη, τὸν δʼ οἷον οἱ μαθηματικοὶ λέγουσι, τρίτον δὲ τὸν ῥηθέντα τελευταῖον· ἔτι τούτους ἢ χωριστοὺς εἶναι τοὺς ἀριθμοὺς τῶν πραγμάτων,  ἢ οὐ χωριστοὺς ἀλλʼ ἐν τοῖς αἰσθητοῖς (οὐχ οὕτως δʼ ὡς τὸ πρῶτον ἐπεσκοποῦμεν, ἀλλʼ ὡς ἐκ τῶν ἀριθμῶν ἐνυπαρχόντων ὄντα τὰ αἰσθητά) ἢ τὸν μὲν αὐτῶν εἶναι τὸν δὲ μή, ἢ πάντας εἶναι.—οἱ μὲν οὖν τρόποι καθʼ οὓς ἐνδέχεται αὐτοὺς εἶναι οὗτοί εἰσιν ἐξ ἀνάγκης μόνοι, σχεδὸν δὲ καὶ οἱ λέγοντες τὸ ἓν ἀρχὴν εἶναι καὶ οὐσίαν καὶ στοιχεῖον πάντων, καὶ ἐκ τούτου καὶ ἄλλου τινὸς εἶναι τὸν ἀριθμόν, ἕκαστος τούτων τινὰ τῶν τρόπων εἴρηκε, πλὴν τοῦ πάσας τὰς μονάδας εἶναι ἀσυμβλήτους. καὶ τοῦτο συμβέβηκεν εὐλόγως· οὐ γὰρ ἐνδέχεται ἔτι ἄλλον τρόπον εἶναι παρὰ τοὺς εἰρημένους. οἱ μὲν οὖν ἀμφοτέρους φασὶν εἶναι τοὺς ἀριθμούς, τὸν μὲν ἔχοντα τὸ πρότερον καὶ ὕστερον τὰς ἰδέας, τὸν δὲ μαθηματικὸν παρὰ τὰς ἰδέας καὶ τὰ αἰσθητά, καὶ χωριστοὺς ἀμφοτέρους τῶν αἰσθητῶν· οἱ δὲ τὸν μαθηματικὸν μόνον ἀριθμὸν εἶναι, τὸν πρῶτον τῶν ὄντων, κεχωρισμένον τῶν αἰσθητῶν. καὶ οἱ Πυθαγόρειοι δʼ ἕνα, τὸν μαθηματικόν, πλὴν οὐ κεχωρισμένον ἀλλʼ ἐκ τούτου τὰς αἰσθητὰς οὐσίας συνεστάναι φασίν· τὸν γὰρ ὅλον οὐρανὸν κατασκευάζουσιν ἐξ ἀριθμῶν, πλὴν οὐ μοναδικῶν, ἀλλὰ τὰς μονάδας  ὑπολαμβάνουσιν ἔχειν μέγεθος· ὅπως δὲ τὸ πρῶτον ἓν συνέστη ἔχον μέγεθος, ἀπορεῖν ἐοίκασιν. ἄλλος δέ τις τὸν πρῶτον ἀριθμὸν τὸν τῶν εἰδῶν ἕνα εἶναι, ἔνιοι δὲ καὶ τὸν μαθηματικὸν τὸν αὐτὸν τοῦτον εἶναι. ὁμοίως δὲ καὶ περὶ τὰ μήκη καὶ περὶ τὰ ἐπίπεδα καὶ περὶ τὰ στερεά. οἱ μὲν γὰρ ἕτερα τὰ μαθηματικὰ καὶ τὰ μετὰ τὰς ἰδέας· τῶν δὲ ἄλλως λεγόντων οἱ μὲν τὰ μαθηματικὰ καὶ μαθηματικῶς λέγουσιν, ὅσοι μὴ ποιοῦσι τὰς ἰδέας ἀριθμοὺς μηδὲ εἶναί φασιν ἰδέας, οἱ δὲ τὰ μαθηματικά, οὐ μαθηματικῶς δέ· οὐ γὰρ τέμνεσθαι οὔτε μέγεθος πᾶν εἰς μεγέθη, οὔθʼ ὁποιασοῦν μονάδας δυάδα εἶναι. μοναδικοὺς δὲ τοὺς ἀριθμοὺς εἶναι πάντες τιθέασι, πλὴν τῶν Πυθαγορείων, ὅσοι τὸ ἓν στοιχεῖον καὶ ἀρχήν φασιν εἶναι τῶν ὄντων· ἐκεῖνοι δʼ ἔχοντας μέγεθος, καθάπερ εἴηρται πρότερον. ὁσαχῶς μὲν οὖν ἐνδέχεται λεχθῆναι περὶ αὐτῶν, καὶ ὅτι πάντες εἰσὶν εἰρημένοι οἱ τρόποι, φανερὸν ἐκ τούτων· ἔστι δὲ πάντα μὲν ἀδύνατα, μᾶλλον δʼ ἴσως θάτερα τῶν ἑτέρων. \n\n\n\n \n\n \n\nπρῶτον μὲν οὖν σκεπτέον εἰ συμβληταὶ αἱ μονάδες ἢ ἀσύμβλητοι, καὶ εἰ ἀσύμβλητοι, ποτέρως ὧνπερ διείλομεν.  ἔστι μὲν γὰρ ὁποιανοῦν εἶναι ὁποιᾳοῦν μονάδι ἀσύμβλητον, ἔστι δὲ τὰς ἐν αὐτῇ τῇ δυάδι πρὸς τὰς ἐν αὐτῇ τῇ τριάδι, καὶ οὕτως δὴ ἀσυμβλήτους εἶναι τὰς ἐν ἑκάστῳ τῷ πρώτῳ ἀριθμῷ πρὸς ἀλλήλας. εἰ μὲν οὖν πᾶσαι συμβληταὶ καὶ ἀδιάφοροι αἱ μονάδες, ὁ μαθηματικὸς γίγνεται ἀριθμὸς καὶ εἷς μόνος, καὶ τὰς ἰδέας οὐκ ἐνδέχεται εἶναι τοὺς ἀριθμούς (ποῖος γὰρ ἔσται ἀριθμὸς αὐτὸ ἄνθρωπος ἢ ζῷον ἢ ἄλλο ὁτιοῦν τῶν εἰδῶν; ἰδέα μὲν γὰρ μία ἑκάστου, οἷον αὐτοῦ ἀνθρώπου μία καὶ αὐτοῦ ζῴου ἄλλη μία· οἱ δʼ ὅμοιοι καὶ ἀδιάφοροι ἄπειροι, ὥστʼ οὐθὲν μᾶλλον ἥδε ἡ τριὰς αὐτοάνθρωπος ἢ ὁποιαοῦν), εἰ δὲ μὴ εἰσὶν ἀριθμοὶ αἱ ἰδέαι, οὐδʼ ὅλως οἷόν τε αὐτὰς εἶναι (ἐκ τίνων γὰρ ἔσονται ἀρχῶν αἱ ἰδέαι; ὁ γὰρ ἀριθμός ἐστιν ἐκ τοῦ ἑνὸς καὶ τῆς δυάδος τῆς ἀορίστου, καὶ αἱ ἀρχαὶ καὶ τὰ στοιχεῖα λέγονται τοῦ ἀριθμοῦ εἶναι, τάξαι τε οὔτε προτέρας ἐνδέχεται τῶν ἀριθμῶν αὐτὰς οὔθʼ ὑστέρας)· εἰ δʼ ἀσύμβλητοι αἱ μονάδες, καὶ οὕτως ἀσύμβλητοι ὥστε ἡτισοῦν ᾑτινιοῦν, οὔτε τὸν μαθηματικὸν ἐνδέχεται εἶναι τοῦτον τὸν ἀριθμόν (ὁ μὲν γὰρ μαθηματικὸς ἐξ ἀδιαφόρων, καὶ τὰ δεικνύμενα κατʼ αὐτοῦ ὡς ἐπὶ τοιούτου ἁρμόττει) οὔτε τὸν τῶν εἰδῶν. οὐ γὰρ ἔσται ἡ δυὰς πρώτη ἐκ τοῦ ἑνὸς καὶ τῆς ἀορίστου δυάδος, ἔπειτα οἱ ἑξῆς ἀριθμοί, ὡς λέγεται δυάς, τριάς, τετράς—ἅμα γὰρ αἱ ἐν τῇ δυάδι τῇ πρώτῃ μονάδες γεννῶνται, εἴτε ὥσπερ ὁ πρῶτος εἰπὼν ἐξ ἀνίσων (ἰσασθέντων γὰρ ἐγένοντο) εἴτε ἄλλως—, ἐπεὶ εἰ ἔσται ἡ ἑτέρα μονὰς τῆς ἑτέρας προτέρα, καὶ τῆς δυάδος τῆς ἐκ τούτων ἔσται προτέρα· ὅταν γὰρ ᾖ τι τὸ μὲν πρότερον τὸ δὲ ὕστερον, καὶ τὸ ἐκ τούτων τοῦ μὲν ἔσται πρότερον τοῦ δʼ ὕστερον. ἔτι ἐπειδὴ ἔστι πρῶτον μὲν αὐτὸ τὸ ἕν, ἔπειτα τῶν ἄλλων ἔστι τι πρῶτον ἓν δεύτερον δὲ μετʼ ἐκεῖνο, καὶ πάλιν τρίτον τὸ δεύτερον μὲν μετὰ τὸ δεύτερον τρίτον δὲ μετὰ τὸ πρῶτον ἕν,—ὥστε πρότεραι ἂν εἶεν αἱ μονάδες ἢ οἱ ἀριθμοὶ ἐξ ὧν λέγονται, οἷον ἐν τῇ δυάδι τρίτη μονὰς ἔσται πρὶν τὰ τρία εἶναι, καὶ ἐν τῇ τριάδι τετάρτη καὶ  πέμπτη πρὶν τοὺς ἀριθμοὺς τούτους. οὐδεὶς μὲν οὖν τὸν τρόπον τοῦτον εἴρηκεν αὐτῶν τὰς μονάδας ἀσυμβλήτους, ἔστι δὲ κατὰ μὲν τὰς ἐκείνων ἀρχὰς εὔλογον καὶ οὕτως, κατὰ μέντοι τὴν ἀλήθειαν ἀδύνατον.  τάς τε γὰρ μονάδας προτέρας καὶ ὑστέρας εἶναι εὔλογον, εἴπερ καὶ πρώτη τις ἔστι μονὰς καὶ ἓν πρῶτον, ὁμοίως δὲ καὶ δυάδας, εἴπερ καὶ δυὰς πρώτη ἔστιν· μετὰ γὰρ τὸ πρῶτον εὔλογον καὶ ἀναγκαῖον δεύτερόν τι εἶναι, καὶ εἰ δεύτερον, τρίτον, καὶ οὕτω δὴ τὰ ἄλλα ἐφεξῆς (ἅμα δʼ ἀμφότερα λέγειν, μονάδα τε μετὰ τὸ ἓν πρώτην εἶναι καὶ δευτέραν, καὶ δυάδα πρώτην, ἀδύνατον). οἱ δὲ ποιοῦσι μονάδα μὲν καὶ ἓν πρῶτον, δεύτερον δὲ καὶ τρίτον οὐκέτι, καὶ δυάδα πρώτην, δευτέραν δὲ καὶ τρίτην οὐκέτι. φανερὸν δὲ καὶ ὅτι οὐκ ἐνδέχεται, εἰ ἀσύμβλητοι πᾶσαι αἱ μονάδες, δυάδα εἶναι αὐτὴν καὶ τριάδα καὶ οὕτω τοὺς ἄλλους ἀριθμούς. ἄν τε γὰρ ὦσιν ἀδιάφοροι αἱ μονάδες ἄν τε διαφέρουσαι ἑκάστη ἑκάστης, ἀνάγκη ἀριθμεῖσθαι τὸν ἀριθμὸν κατὰ πρόσθεσιν, οἷον τὴν δυάδα πρὸς τῷ ἑνὶ ἄλλου ἑνὸς προστεθέντος, καὶ τὴν τριάδα ἄλλου ἑνὸς πρὸς τοῖς δυσὶ προστεθέντος, καὶ τὴν τετράδα ὡσαύτως· τούτων δὲ ὄντων ἀδύνατον τὴν γένεσιν εἶναι τῶν ἀριθμῶν ὡς γεννῶσιν ἐκ τῆς δυάδος καὶ τοῦ ἑνός. μόριον γὰρ γίγνεται ἡ δυὰς τῆς τριάδος καὶ αὕτη τῆς τετράδος, τὸν αὐτὸν δὲ τρόπον συμβαίνει καὶ ἐπὶ τῶν ἐχομένων. ἀλλʼ ἐκ τῆς δυάδος τῆς πρώτης καὶ τῆς ἀορίστου δυάδος ἐγίγνετο ἡ τετράς, δύο δυάδες παρʼ αὐτὴν τὴν δυάδα· εἰ δὲ μή, μόριον ἔσται αὐτὴ ἡ δυάς, ἑτέρα δὲ προσέσται μία δυάς. καὶ ἡ δυὰς ἔσται ἐκ τοῦ ἑνὸς αὐτοῦ καὶ ἄλλου ἑνός· εἰ δὲ τοῦτο, οὐχ οἷόν τʼ εἶναι τὸ ἕτερον στοιχεῖον δυάδα ἀόριστον· μονάδα γὰρ μίαν γεννᾷ ἀλλʼ οὐ δυάδα ὡρισμένην. ἔτι παρʼ αὐτὴν τὴν τριάδα καὶ αὐτὴν τὴν δυάδα πῶς ἔσονται ἄλλαι τριάδες καὶ δυάδες; καὶ τίνα τρόπον ἐκ προτέρων μονάδων καὶ ὑστέρων σύγκεινται; πάντα γὰρ ταῦτʼ  ἄτοπά ἐστι καὶ πλασματώδη, καὶ ἀδύνατον εἶναι πρώτην δυάδα, εἶτʼ αὐτὴν τριάδα. ἀνάγκη δʼ, ἐπείπερ ἔσται τὸ ἓν καὶ ἡ ἀόριστος δυὰς στοιχεῖα. εἰ δʼ ἀδύνατα τὰ συμβαίνοντα, καὶ τὰς ἀρχὰς εἶναι ταύτας ἀδύνατον.—εἰ μὲν οὖν διάφοροι αἱ μονάδες ὁποιαιοῦν ὁποιαισοῦν, ταῦτα καὶ τοιαῦθʼ ἕτερα συμβαίνει ἐξ ἀνάγκης· εἰ δʼ αἱ μὲν ἐν ἄλλῳ διάφοροι αἱ δʼ ἐν τῷ αὐτῷ ἀριθμῷ ἀδιάφοροι ἀλλήλαις μόναι, καὶ οὕτως οὐθὲν ἐλάττω συμβαίνει τὰ δυσχερῆ.  οἷον γὰρ ἐν τῇ δεκάδι αὐτῇ ἔνεισι δέκα μονάδες, σύγκειται δὲ καὶ ἐκ τούτων καὶ ἐκ δύο πεντάδων ἡ δεκάς. ἐπεὶ δʼ οὐχ ὁ τυχὼν ἀριθμὸς αὐτὴ ἡ δεκὰς οὐδὲ σύγκειται ἐκ τῶν τυχουσῶν πεντάδων, ὥσπερ οὐδὲ μονάδων, ἀνάγκη διαφέρειν τὰς μονάδας τὰς ἐν τῇ δεκάδι ταύτῃ. ἂν γὰρ μὴ διαφέρωσιν, οὐδʼ αἱ πεντάδες διοίσουσιν ἐξ ὧν ἐστὶν ἡ δεκάς· ἐπεὶ δὲ διαφέρουσι, καὶ αἱ μονάδες διοίσουσιν. εἰ δὲ διαφέρουσι, πότερον οὐκ ἐνέσονται πεντάδες ἄλλαι ἀλλὰ μόνον αὗται αἱ δύο, ἢ ἔσονται; εἴτε δὲ μὴ ἐνέσονται, ἄτοπον· εἴτʼ ἐνέσονται, ποία ἔσται δεκὰς ἐξ ἐκείνων; οὐ γὰρ ἔστιν ἑτέρα δεκὰς ἐν τῇ δεκάδι παρʼ αὐτήν. ἀλλὰ μὴν καὶ ἀνάγκη γε μὴ ἐκ τῶν τυχουσῶν δυάδων τὴν τετράδα συγκεῖσθαι· ἡ γὰρ ἀόριστος δυάς, ὥς φασι, λαβοῦσα τὴν ὡρισμένην δυάδα δύο δυάδας ἐποίησεν· τοῦ γὰρ ληφθέντος ἦν δυοποιός.—ἔτι τὸ εἶναι παρὰ τὰς δύο μονάδας τὴν δυάδα φύσιν τινά, καὶ τὴν τριάδα παρὰ τὰς τρεῖς μονάδας, πῶς ἐνδέχεται; ἢ γὰρ μεθέξει θατέρου θατέρου, ὥσπερ λευκὸς ἄνθρωπος παρὰ λευκὸν καὶ ἄνθρωπον (μετέχει γὰρ τούτων), ἢ ὅταν ᾖ θατέρου θάτερον διαφορά τις, ὥσπερ ὁ ἄνθρωπος παρὰ ζῷον καὶ δίπουν. ἔτι τὰ μὲν ἁφῇ ἐστὶν ἓν τὰ δὲ μίξει τὰ δὲ θέσει· ὧν οὐδὲν ἐνδέχεται ὑπάρχειν ταῖς μονάσιν ἐξ ὧν ἡ δυὰς καὶ ἡ τριάς· ἀλλʼ ὥσπερ οἱ δύο ἄνθρωποι οὐχ ἕν τι παρʼ ἀμφοτέρους, οὕτως ἀνάγκη καὶ τὰς μονάδας. καὶ οὐχ ὅτι ἀδιαίρετοι, διοίσουσι διὰ τοῦτο· καὶ γὰρ αἱ στιγμαὶ ἀδιαίρετοι, ἀλλʼ ὅμως παρὰ τὰς δύο οὐθὲν ἕτερον ἡ δυὰς αὐτῶν.—ἀλλὰ μὴν οὐδὲ τοῦτο δεῖ λανθάνειν, ὅτι συμβαίνει προτέρας καὶ ὑστέρας εἶναι δυάδας, ὁμοίως δὲ καὶ τοὺς ἄλλους ἀριθμούς. αἱ μὲν γὰρ ἐν τῇ τετράδι δυάδες ἔστωσαν ἀλλήλαις ἅμα· ἀλλʼ αὗται τῶν ἐν τῇ ὀκτάδι πρότεραί εἰσι, καὶ ἐγέννησαν, ὥσπερ ἡ δυὰς ταύτας, αὗται τὰς τετράδας τὰς ἐν τῇ ὀκτάδι αὐτῇ, ὥστε εἰ καὶ ἡ πρώτη δυὰς ἰδέα, καὶ αὗται ἰδέαι τινὲς ἔσονται. ὁ δʼ αὐτὸς λόγος καὶ ἐπὶ τῶν μονάδων· αἱ γὰρ ἐν τῇ δυάδι τῇ πρώτῃ μονάδες γεννῶσι τὰς τέτταρας τὰς ἐν τῇ τετράδι, ὥστε πᾶσαι αἱ μονάδες ἰδέαι γίγνονται καὶ συγκείσεται ἰδέα ἐξ ἰδεῶν· ὥστε δῆλον ὅτι κἀκεῖνα ὧν ἰδέαι αὗται τυγχάνουσιν οὖσαι συγκείμενα ἔσται, οἷον εἰ τὰ ζῷα φαίη τις συγκεῖσθαι ἐκ ζῴων, εἰ τούτων ἰδέαι εἰσίν.  —ὅλως δὲ τὸ ποιεῖν τὰς μονάδας διαφόρους ὁπωσοῦν ἄτοπον καὶ πλασματῶδες (λέγω δὲ πλασματῶδες τὸ πρὸς ὑπόθεσιν βεβιασμένον)· οὔτε γὰρ κατὰ τὸ ποσὸν οὔτε κατὰ τὸ ποιὸν ὁρῶμεν διαφέρουσαν μονάδα μονάδος, ἀνάγκη τε ἢ ἴσον ἢ ἄνισον εἶναι ἀριθμόν, πάντα μὲν ἀλλὰ μάλιστα τὸν μοναδικόν, ὥστʼ εἰ μήτε πλείων μήτʼ ἐλάττων, ἴσος· τὰ δὲ ἴσα καὶ ὅλως ἀδιάφορα ταὐτὰ ὑπολαμβάνομεν ἐν τοῖς ἀριθμοῖς. εἰ δὲ μή, οὐδʼ αἱ ἐν αὐτῇ τῇ δεκάδι δυάδες ἀδιάφοροι ἔσονται ἴσαι οὖσαι· τίνα γὰρ αἰτίαν ἕξει λέγειν ὁ φάσκων ἀδιαφόρους εἶναι; ἔτι εἰ ἅπασα μονὰς καὶ μονὰς ἄλλη δύο, ἡ ἐκ τῆς δυάδος αὐτῆς μονὰς καὶ ἡ ἐκ τῆς τριάδος αὐτῆς δυὰς ἔσται ἐκ διαφερουσῶν τε, καὶ πότερον προτέρα τῆς τριάδος ἢ ὑστέρα; μᾶλλον γὰρ ἔοικε προτέραν ἀναγκαῖον εἶναι· ἡ μὲν γὰρ ἅμα τῇ τριάδι ἡ δʼ ἅμα τῇ δυάδι τῶν μονάδων. καὶ ἡμεῖς μὲν ὑπολαμβάνομεν ὅλως ἓν καὶ ἕν, καὶ ἐὰν ᾖ ἴσα ἢ ἄνισα, δύο εἶναι, οἷον τὸ ἀγαθὸν καὶ τὸ κακόν, καὶ ἄνθρωπον καὶ ἵππον· οἱ δʼ οὕτως λέγοντες οὐδὲ τὰς μονάδας. εἴτε δὲ μὴ ἔστι πλείων ἀριθμὸς ὁ τῆς τριάδος αὐτῆς ἢ ὁ τῆς δυάδος, θαυμαστόν· εἴτε ἐστὶ πλείων, δῆλον ὅτι καὶ ἴσος ἔνεστι τῇ δυάδι, ὥστε οὗτος ἀδιάφορος αὐτῇ τῇ δυάδι. ἀλλʼ οὐκ ἐνδέχεται, εἰ πρῶτός τις ἔστιν ἀριθμὸς καὶ δεύτερος. οὐδὲ ἔσονται αἱ ἰδέαι ἀριθμοί. τοῦτο μὲν γὰρ αὐτὸ ὀρθῶς λέγουσιν οἱ διαφόρους τὰς μονάδας ἀξιοῦντες εἶναι, εἴπερ ἰδέαι ἔσονται, ὥσπερ εἴρηται πρότερον· ἓν γὰρ τὸ εἶδος, αἱ δὲ μονάδες εἰ ἀδιάφοροι, καὶ αἱ δυάδες καὶ αἱ τριάδες ἔσονται ἀδιάφοροι. διὸ καὶ τὸ ἀριθμεῖσθαι οὕτως, ἓν δύο, μὴ προσλαμβανομένου πρὸς τῷ ὑπάρχοντι ἀναγκαῖον αὐτοῖς λέγειν (οὔτε γὰρ ἡ γένεσις ἔσται ἐκ τῆς ἀορίστου δυάδος, οὔτʼ ἰδέαν ἐνδέχεται εἶναι· ἐνυπάρξει γὰρ ἑτέρα ἰδέα ἐν ἑτέρᾳ, καὶ πάντα τὰ εἴδη ἑνὸς μέρη)· διὸ πρὸς μὲν τὴν ὑπόθεσιν ὀρθῶς λέγουσιν, ὅλως δʼ οὐκ ὀρθῶς· πολλὰ γὰρ ἀναιροῦσιν, ἐπεὶ τοῦτό γʼ αὐτὸ ἔχειν τινὰ φήσουσιν ἀπορίαν, πότερον, ὅταν ἀριθμῶμεν καὶ εἴπωμεν ἓν δύο τρία, προσλαμβάνοντες ἀριθμοῦμεν ἢ κατὰ μερίδας. ποιοῦμεν δὲ ἀμφοτέρως· διὸ γελοῖον ταύτην εἰς τηλικαύτην τῆς οὐσίας ἀνάγειν διαφοράν.\n\n\n\n \n\n \n\n  πάντων δὲ πρῶτον καλῶς ἔχει διορίσασθαι τίς ἀριθμοῦ διαφορά, καὶ μονάδος, εἰ ἔστιν. ἀνάγκη δʼ ἢ κατὰ τὸ ποσὸν ἢ κατὰ τὸ ποιὸν διαφέρειν· τούτων δʼ οὐδέτερον φαίνεται ἐνδέχεσθαι ὑπάρχειν. ἀλλʼ ᾗ ἀριθμός, κατὰ τὸ ποσόν. εἰ δὲ δὴ καὶ αἱ μονάδες τῷ ποσῷ διέφερον, κἂν ἀριθμὸς ἀριθμοῦ διέφερεν ὁ ἴσος τῷ πλήθει τῶν μονάδων. ἔτι πότερον αἱ πρῶται μείζους ἢ ἐλάττους, καὶ αἱ ὕστερον ἐπιδιδόασιν ἢ τοὐναντίον; πάντα γὰρ ταῦτα ἄλογα. ἀλλὰ μὴν οὐδὲ κατὰ τὸ ποιὸν διαφέρειν ἐνδέχεται. οὐθὲν γὰρ αὐταῖς οἷόν τε ὑπάρχειν πάθος· ὕστερον γὰρ καὶ τοῖς ἀριθμοῖς φασὶν ὑπάρχειν τὸ ποιὸν τοῦ ποσοῦ. ἔτι οὔτʼ ἂν ἀπὸ τοῦ ἑνὸς τοῦτʼ αὐταῖς γένοιτο οὔτʼ ἂν ἀπὸ τῆς δυάδος· τὸ μὲν γὰρ οὐ ποιὸν ἡ δὲ ποσοποιόν· τοῦ γὰρ πολλὰ τὰ ὄντα εἶναι αἰτία αὕτη ἡ φύσις. εἰ δʼ ἄρα ἔχει πως ἄλλως, λεκτέον ἐν ἀρχῇ μάλιστα τοῦτο καὶ διοριστέον περὶ μονάδος διαφορᾶς, μάλιστα μὲν καὶ διότι ἀνάγκη ὑπάρχειν· εἰ δὲ μή, τίνα λέγουσιν;—ὅτι μὲν οὖν, εἴπερ εἰσὶν ἀριθμοὶ αἱ ἰδέαι, οὔτε συμβλητὰς τὰς μονάδας ἁπάσας ἐνδέχεται εἶναι, φανερόν, οὔτε ἀσυμβλήτους ἀλλήλαις οὐδέτερον τῶν τρόπων· ἀλλὰ μὴν οὐδʼ ὡς ἕτεροί τινες λέγουσι περὶ τῶν ἀριθμῶν λέγεται καλῶς. εἰσὶ δʼ οὗτοι ὅσοι ἰδέας μὲν οὐκ οἴονται εἶναι οὔτε ἁπλῶς οὔτε ὡς ἀριθμούς τινας οὔσας, τὰ δὲ μαθηματικὰ εἶναι καὶ τοὺς ἀριθμοὺς πρώτους τῶν ὄντων, καὶ ἀρχὴν αὐτῶν εἶναι αὐτὸ τὸ ἕν. ἄτοπον γὰρ τὸ ἓν μὲν εἶναί τι πρῶτον τῶν ἑνῶν, ὥσπερ ἐκεῖνοί φασι, δυάδα δὲ τῶν δυάδων μή, μηδὲ τριάδα τῶν τριάδων· τοῦ γὰρ αὐτοῦ λόγου πάντα ἐστίν. εἰ μὲν οὖν οὕτως ἔχει τὰ περὶ τὸν ἀριθμὸν καὶ θήσει τις εἶναι τὸν μαθηματικὸν μόνον, οὐκ ἔστι τὸ ἓν ἀρχή (ἀνάγκη γὰρ διαφέρειν τὸ ἓν τὸ τοιοῦτο τῶν ἄλλων μονάδων· εἰ δὲ τοῦτο, καὶ δυάδα τινὰ πρώτην τῶν δυάδων, ὁμοίως δὲ καὶ τοὺς ἄλλους ἀριθμοὺς τοὺς ἐφεξῆς)· εἰ δέ ἐστι τὸ ἓν ἀρχή, ἀνάγκη μᾶλλον ὥσπερ Πλάτων ἔλεγεν ἔχειν τὰ περὶ τοὺς ἀριθμούς, καὶ εἶναι δυάδα πρώτην καὶ τριάδα, καὶ οὐ συμβλητοὺς εἶναι τοὺς ἀριθμοὺς πρὸς ἀλλήλους. ἂν δʼ αὖ πάλιν τις τιθῇ ταῦτα, εἴρηται ὅτι ἀδύνατα πολλὰ συμβαίνει. ἀλλὰ μὴν ἀνάγκη γε ἢ οὕτως ἢ ἐκείνως ἔχειν, ὥστʼ εἰ μηδετέρως, οὐκ ἂν ἐνδέχοιτο εἶναι τὸν ἀριθμὸν χωριστόν.  —φανερὸν δʼ ἐκ τούτων καὶ ὅτι χείριστα λέγεται ὁ τρίτος τρόπος, τὸ εἶναι τὸν αὐτὸν ἀριθμὸν τὸν τῶν εἰδῶν καὶ τὸν μαθηματικόν. ἀνάγκη γὰρ εἰς μίαν δόξαν συμβαίνειν δύο ἁμαρτίας· οὔτε γὰρ μαθηματικὸν ἀριθμὸν ἐνδέχεται τοῦτον εἶναι τὸν τρόπον, ἀλλʼ ἰδίας ὑποθέσεις ὑποθέμενον ἀνάγκη μηκύνειν, ὅσα τε τοῖς ὡς εἴδη τὸν ἀριθμὸν λέγουσι συμβαίνει, καὶ ταῦτα ἀναγκαῖον λέγειν.—ὁ δὲ τῶν Πυθαγορείων τρόπος τῇ μὲν ἐλάττους ἔχει δυσχερείας τῶν πρότερον εἰρημένων, τῇ δὲ ἰδίας ἑτέρας. τὸ μὲν γὰρ μὴ χωριστὸν ποιεῖν τὸν ἀριθμὸν ἀφαιρεῖται πολλὰ τῶν ἀδυνάτων· τὸ δὲ τὰ σώματα ἐξ ἀριθμῶν εἶναι συγκείμενα, καὶ τὸν ἀριθμὸν τοῦτον εἶναι μαθηματικόν, ἀδύνατόν ἐστιν. οὔτε γὰρ ἄτομα μεγέθη λέγειν ἀληθές, εἴ θʼ ὅτι μάλιστα τοῦτον ἔχει τὸν τρόπον, οὐχ αἵ γε μονάδες μέγεθος ἔχουσιν· μέγεθος δὲ ἐξ ἀδιαιρέτων συγκεῖσθαι πῶς δυνατόν; ἀλλὰ μὴν ὅ γʼ ἀριθμητικὸς ἀριθμὸς μοναδικός ἐστιν. ἐκεῖνοι δὲ τὸν ἀριθμὸν τὰ ὄντα λέγουσιν· τὰ γοῦν θεωρήματα προσάπτουσι τοῖς σώμασιν ὡς ἐξ ἐκείνων ὄντων τῶν ἀριθμῶν.—εἰ τοίνυν ἀνάγκη μέν, εἴπερ ἐστὶν ἀριθμὸς τῶν ὄντων τι καθʼ αὑτό, τούτων εἶναί τινα τῶν εἰρημένων τρόπων, οὐθένα δὲ τούτων ἐνδέχεται, φανερὸν ὡς οὐκ ἔστιν ἀριθμοῦ τις τοιαύτη φύσις οἵαν κατασκευάζουσιν οἱ χωριστὸν ποιοῦντες αὐτόν.—ἔτι πότερον ἑκάστη μονὰς ἐκ τοῦ μεγάλου καὶ μικροῦ ἰσασθέντων ἐστίν, ἢ ἡ μὲν ἐκ τοῦ μικροῦ ἡ δʼ ἐκ τοῦ μεγάλου; εἰ μὲν δὴ οὕτως, οὔτε ἐκ πάντων τῶν στοιχείων ἕκαστον οὔτε ἀδιάφοροι αἱ μονάδες (ἐν τῇ μὲν γὰρ τὸ μέγα ἐν τῇ δὲ τὸ μικρὸν ὑπάρχει, ἐναντίον τῇ φύσει ὄν)· ἔτι αἱ ἐν τῇ τριάδι αὐτῇ πῶς; μία γὰρ περιττή· ἀλλὰ διὰ τοῦτο ἴσως αὐτὸ τὸ ἓν ποιοῦσιν ἐν τῷ περιττῷ μέσον. εἰ δʼ ἑκατέρα τῶν μονάδων ἐξ ἀμφοτέρων ἐστὶν ἰσασθέντων, ἡ δυὰς πῶς ἔσται μία τις οὖσα φύσις ἐκ τοῦ μεγάλου καὶ μικροῦ; ἢ τί διοίσει τῆς μονάδος; ἔτι προτέρα ἡ μονὰς τῆς δυάδος (ἀναιρουμένης γὰρ ἀναιρεῖται ἡ δυάς)· ἰδέαν οὖν ἰδέας ἀναγκαῖον αὐτὴν εἶναι, προτέραν γʼ οὖσαν ἰδέας, καὶ γεγονέναι προτέραν. ἐκ τίνος οὖν; ἡ γὰρ ἀόριστος δυὰς δυοποιὸς ἦν.—ἔτι ἀνάγκη ἤτοι ἄπειρον τὸν ἀριθμὸν εἶναι ἢ πεπερασμένον· χωριστὸν γὰρ ποιοῦσι τὸν ἀριθμόν, ὥστε οὐχ οἷόν τε μὴ οὐχὶ τούτων θάτερον ὑπάρχειν.  ὅτι μὲν τοίνυν ἄπειρον οὐκ ἐνδέχεται, δῆλον (οὔτε γὰρ περιττὸς ὁ ἄπειρός ἐστιν οὔτʼ ἄρτιος, ἡ δὲ γένεσις τῶν ἀριθμῶν ἢ περιττοῦ ἀριθμοῦ ἢ ἀρτίου ἀεί ἐστιν· ὡδὶ μὲν τοῦ ἑνὸς εἰς τὸν ἄρτιον πίπτοντος περιττός, ὡδὶ δὲ τῆς μὲν δυάδος ἐμπιπτούσης ὁ ἀφʼ ἑνὸς διπλασιαζόμενος, ὡδὶ δὲ τῶν περιττῶν ὁ ἄλλος ἄρτιος· ἔτι εἰ πᾶσα ἰδέα τινὸς οἱ δὲ ἀριθμοὶ ἰδέαι, καὶ ὁ ἄπειρος ἔσται ἰδέα τινός, ἢ τῶν αἰσθητῶν ἢ ἄλλου τινός· καίτοι οὔτε κατὰ τὴν θέσιν ἐνδέχεται οὔτε κατὰ λόγον, τάττουσί γʼ οὕτω τὰς ἰδέας)· εἰ δὲ πεπερασμένος, μέχρι πόσου; τοῦτο γὰρ δεῖ λέγεσθαι οὐ μόνον ὅτι ἀλλὰ καὶ διότι. ἀλλὰ μὴν εἰ μέχρι τῆς δεκάδος ὁ ἀριθμός, ὥσπερ τινές φασιν, πρῶτον μὲν ταχὺ ἐπιλείψει τὰ εἴδη—οἷον εἰ ἔστιν ἡ τριὰς αὐτοάνθρωπος, τίς ἔσται ἀριθμὸς αὐτόιππος; αὐτὸ γὰρ ἕκαστος ἀριθμὸς μέχρι δεκάδος· ἀνάγκη δὴ τῶν ἐν τούτοις ἀριθμῶν τινὰ εἶναι (οὐσίαι γὰρ καὶ ἰδέαι οὗτοι)· ἀλλʼ ὅμως ἐπιλείψει (τὰ τοῦ ζῴου γὰρ εἴδη ὑπερέξει)—. ἅμα δὲ δῆλον ὅτι εἰ οὕτως ἡ τριὰς αὐτοάνθρωπος, καὶ αἱ ἄλλαι τριάδες (ὅμοιαι γὰρ αἱ ἐν τοῖς αὐτοῖς ἀριθμοῖς), ὥστʼ ἄπειροι ἔσονται ἄνθρωποι, εἰ μὲν ἰδέα ἑκάστη τριάς, αὐτὸ ἕκαστος ἄνθρωπος, εἰ δὲ μή, ἀλλʼ ἄνθρωποί γε. καὶ εἰ μέρος ὁ ἐλάττων τοῦ μείζονος, ὁ ἐκ τῶν συμβλητῶν μονάδων τῶν ἐν τῷ αὐτῷ ἀριθμῷ, εἰ δὴ ἡ τετρὰς αὐτὴ ἰδέα τινός ἐστιν, οἷον ἵππου ἢ λευκοῦ, ὁ ἄνθρωπος ἔσται μέρος ἵππου, εἰ δυὰς ὁ ἄνθρωπος. ἄτοπον δὲ καὶ τὸ τῆς μὲν δεκάδος εἶναι ἰδέαν ἑνδεκάδος δὲ μή, μηδὲ τῶν ἐχομένων ἀριθμῶν. ἔτι δὲ καὶ ἔστι καὶ γίγνεται ἔνια καὶ ὧν εἴδη οὐκ ἔστιν, ὥστε διὰ τί οὐ κἀκείνων εἴδη ἔστιν; οὐκ ἄρα αἴτια τὰ εἴδη ἐστίν. ἔτι ἄτοπον εἰ ὁ ἀριθμὸς ὁ μέχρι τῆς δεκάδος μᾶλλόν τι ὂν καὶ εἶδος αὐτῆς τῆς δεκάδος, καίτοι τοῦ μὲν οὐκ ἔστι γένεσις ὡς ἑνός, τῆς δʼ ἔστιν. πειρῶνται δʼ ὡς τοῦ μέχρι τῆς δεκάδος τελείου ὄντος ἀριθμοῦ. γεννῶσι γοῦν τὰ ἑπόμενα, οἷον τὸ κενόν, ἀναλογίαν, τὸ περιττόν, τὰ ἄλλα τὰ τοιαῦτα, ἐντὸς τῆς δεκάδος· τὰ μὲν γὰρ ταῖς ἀρχαῖς ἀποδιδόασιν, οἷον κίνησιν στάσιν, ἀγαθὸν κακόν, τὰ δʼ ἄλλα τοῖς ἀριθμοῖς· διὸ τὸ ἓν τὸ περιττόν· εἰ γὰρ ἐν τῇ τριάδι, πῶς ἡ πεντὰς περιττόν; ἔτι τὰ μεγέθη καὶ ὅσα τοιαῦτα μέχρι ποσοῦ,  οἷον ἡ πρώτη γραμμή, ἡ ἄτομος, εἶτα δυάς, εἶτα καὶ ταῦτα μέχρι δεκάδος.—ἔτι εἰ ἔστι χωριστὸς ὁ ἀριθμός, ἀπορήσειεν ἄν τις πότερον πρότερον τὸ ἓν ἢ ἡ τριὰς καὶ ἡ δυάς. ᾗ μὲν δὴ σύνθετος ὁ ἀριθμός, τὸ ἕν, ᾗ δὲ τὸ καθόλου πρότερον καὶ τὸ εἶδος, ὁ ἀριθμός· ἑκάστη γὰρ τῶν μονάδων μόριον τοῦ ἀριθμοῦ ὡς ὕλη, ὁ δʼ ὡς εἶδος. καὶ ἔστι μὲν ὡς ἡ ὀρθὴ προτέρα τῆς ὀξείας, ὅτι ὥρισται καὶ τῷ λόγῳ· ἔστι δʼ ὡς ἡ ὀξεῖα, ὅτι μέρος καὶ εἰς ταύτην διαιρεῖται. ὡς μὲν δὴ ὕλη ἡ ὀξεῖα καὶ τὸ στοιχεῖον καὶ ἡ μονὰς πρότερον, ὡς δὲ κατὰ τὸ εἶδος καὶ τὴν οὐσίαν τὴν κατὰ τὸν λόγον ἡ ὀρθὴ καὶ τὸ ὅλον τὸ ἐκ τῆς ὕλης καὶ τοῦ εἴδους· ἐγγύτερον γὰρ τοῦ εἴδους καὶ οὗ ὁ λόγος τὸ ἄμφω, γενέσει δʼ ὕστερον. πῶς οὖν ἀρχὴ τὸ ἕν; ὅτι οὐ διαιρετόν, φασίν· ἀλλʼ ἀδιαίρετον καὶ τὸ καθόλου καὶ τὸ ἐπὶ μέρους καὶ τὸ στοιχεῖον. ἀλλὰ τρόπον ἄλλον, τὸ μὲν κατὰ λόγον τὸ δὲ κατὰ χρόνον. ποτέρως οὖν τὸ ἓν ἀρχή; ὥσπερ γὰρ εἴρηται, καὶ ἡ ὀρθὴ τῆς ὀξείας καὶ αὕτη ἐκείνης δοκεῖ προτέρα εἶναι, καὶ ἑκατέρα μία. ἀμφοτέρως δὴ ποιοῦσι τὸ ἓν ἀρχήν. ἔστι δὲ ἀδύνατον· τὸ μὲν γὰρ ὡς εἶδος καὶ ἡ οὐσία τὸ δʼ ὡς μέρος καὶ ὡς ὕλη. ἔστι γάρ πως ἓν ἑκάτερον—τῇ μὲν ἀληθείᾳ δυνάμει (εἴ γε ὁ ἀριθμὸς ἕν τι καὶ μὴ ὡς σωρὸς ἀλλʼ ἕτερος ἐξ ἑτέρων μονάδων, ὥσπερ φασίν), ἐντελεχείᾳ δʼ οὔ, ἔστι μονὰς ἑκατέρα· αἴτιον δὲ τῆς συμβαινούσης ἁμαρτίας ὅτι ἅμα ἐκ τῶν μαθημάτων ἐθήρευον καὶ ἐκ τῶν λόγων τῶν καθόλου, ὥστʼ ἐξ ἐκείνων μὲν ὡς στιγμὴν τὸ ἓν καὶ τὴν ἀρχὴν ἔθηκαν (ἡ γὰρ μονὰς στιγμὴ ἄθετός ἐστιν· καθάπερ οὖν καὶ ἕτεροί τινες ἐκ τοῦ ἐλαχίστου τὰ ὄντα συνετίθεσαν, καὶ οὗτοι, ὥστε γίγνεται ἡ μονὰς ὕλη τῶν ἀριθμῶν, καὶ ἅμα προτέρα τῆς δυάδος, πάλιν δʼ ὑστέρα ὡς ὅλου τινὸς καὶ ἑνὸς καὶ εἴδους τῆς δυάδος οὔσης)· διὰ δὲ τὸ καθόλου ζητεῖν τὸ κατηγορούμενον ἓν καὶ οὕτως ὡς μέρος ἔλεγον. ταῦτα δʼ ἅμα τῷ αὐτῷ ἀδύνατον ὑπάρχειν. εἰ δὲ τὸ ἓν αὐτὸ δεῖ †μόνον ἄθετον† εἶναι (οὐθενὶ γὰρ διαφέρει ἢ ὅτι ἀρχή), καὶ ἡ μὲν δυὰς διαιρετὴ ἡ δὲ μονὰς οὔ, ὁμοιοτέρα ἂν εἴη τῷ ἑνὶ αὐτῷ ἡ μονάς. εἰ δʼ ἡ μονάς, κἀκεῖνο τῇ μονάδι ἢ τῇ δυάδι· ὥστε προτέρα ἂν εἴη ἑκατέρα ἡ μονὰς τῆς δυάδος. οὔ φασι δέ· γεννῶσι γοῦν τὴν δυάδα πρῶτον.  ἔτι εἰ ἔστιν ἡ δυὰς ἕν τι αὐτὴ καὶ ἡ τριὰς αὐτή, ἄμφω δυάς. ἐκ τίνος οὖν αὕτη ἡ δυάς;\n\n\n\n \n\n \n\nἀπορήσειε δʼ ἄν τις καὶ ἐπεὶ ἁφὴ μὲν οὐκ ἔστιν ἐν τοῖς ἀριθμοῖς, τὸ δʼ ἐφεξῆς, ὅσων μὴ ἔστι μεταξὺ μονάδων (οἷον τῶν ἐν τῇ δυάδι ἢ τῇ τριάδι), πότερον ἐφεξῆς τῷ ἑνὶ αὐτῷ ἢ οὔ, καὶ πότερον ἡ δυὰς προτέρα τῶν ἐφεξῆς ἢ τῶν μονάδων ὁποτεραοῦν.—ὁμοίως δὲ καὶ περὶ τῶν ὕστερον γενῶν τοῦ ἀριθμοῦ συμβαίνει τὰ δυσχερῆ, γραμμῆς τε καὶ ἐπιπέδου καὶ σώματος. οἱ μὲν γὰρ ἐκ τῶν εἰδῶν τοῦ μεγάλου καὶ τοῦ μικροῦ ποιοῦσιν, οἷον ἐκ μακροῦ μὲν καὶ βραχέος τὰ μήκη, πλατέος δὲ καὶ στενοῦ τὰ ἐπίπεδα, ἐκ βαθέος δὲ καὶ ταπεινοῦ τοὺς ὄγκους· ταῦτα δέ ἐστιν εἴδη τοῦ μεγάλου καὶ μικροῦ. τὴν δὲ κατὰ τὸ ἓν ἀρχὴν ἄλλοι ἄλλως τιθέασι τῶν τοιούτων. καὶ ἐν τούτοις δὲ μυρία φαίνεται τά τε ἀδύνατα καὶ τὰ πλασματώδη καὶ τὰ ὑπεναντία πᾶσι τοῖς εὐλόγοις. ἀπολελυμένα τε γὰρ ἀλλήλων συμβαίνει, εἰ μὴ συνακολουθοῦσι καὶ αἱ ἀρχαὶ ὥστʼ εἶναι τὸ πλατὺ καὶ στενὸν καὶ μακρὸν καὶ βραχύ (εἰ δὲ τοῦτο, ἔσται τὸ ἐπίπεδον γραμμὴ καὶ τὸ στερεὸν ἐπίπεδον· ἔτι δὲ γωνίαι καὶ σχήματα καὶ τὰ τοιαῦτα πῶς ἀποδοθήσεται;), ταὐτό τε συμβαίνει τοῖς περὶ τὸν ἀριθμόν· ταῦτα γὰρ πάθη μεγέθους ἐστίν, ἀλλʼ οὐκ ἐκ τούτων τὸ μέγεθος, ὥσπερ οὐδʼ ἐξ εὐθέος καὶ καμπύλου τὸ μῆκος οὐδʼ ἐκ λείου καὶ τραχέος τὰ στερεά.—πάντων δὲ κοινὸν τούτων ὅπερ ἐπὶ τῶν εἰδῶν τῶν ὡς γένους συμβαίνει διαπορεῖν, ὅταν τις θῇ τὰ καθόλου, πότερον τὸ ζῷον αὐτὸ ἐν τῷ ζῴῳ ἢ ἕτερον αὐτοῦ ζῴου. τοῦτο γὰρ μὴ χωριστοῦ μὲν ὄντος οὐδεμίαν ποιήσει ἀπορίαν· χωριστοῦ δέ, ὥσπερ οἱ ταῦτα λέγοντές φασι, τοῦ ἑνὸς καὶ τῶν ἀριθμῶν οὐ ῥᾴδιον λῦσαι, εἰ μὴ ῥᾴδιον δεῖ λέγειν τὸ ἀδύνατον. ὅταν γὰρ νοῇ τις ἐν τῇ δυάδι τὸ ἓν καὶ ὅλως ἐν ἀριθμῷ, πότερον αὐτὸ νοεῖ τι ἢ ἕτερον;—οἱ μὲν οὖν τὰ μεγέθη γεννῶσιν ἐκ τοιαύτης ὕλης, ἕτεροι δὲ ἐκ τῆς στιγμῆς (ἡ δὲ στιγμὴ αὐτοῖς δοκεῖ εἶναι οὐχ ἓν ἀλλʼ οἷον τὸ ἕν) καὶ ἄλλης ὕλης οἵας τὸ πλῆθος, ἀλλʼ οὐ πλήθους· περὶ ὧν οὐδὲν ἧττον συμβαίνει τὰ αὐτὰ ἀπορεῖν. εἰ μὲν γὰρ μία ἡ ὕλη, ταὐτὸ γραμμὴ καὶ ἐπίπεδον καὶ στερεόν (ἐκ γὰρ τῶν αὐτῶν τὸ αὐτὸ καὶ ἓν ἔσται)·  εἰ δὲ πλείους αἱ ὗλαι καὶ ἑτέρα μὲν γραμμῆς ἑτέρα δὲ τοῦ ἐπιπέδου καὶ ἄλλη τοῦ στερεοῦ, ἤτοι ἀκολουθοῦσιν ἀλλήλαις ἢ οὔ, ὥστε ταὐτὰ συμβήσεται καὶ οὕτως· ἢ γὰρ οὐχ ἕξει τὸ ἐπίπεδον γραμμὴν ἢ ἔσται γραμμή.—ἔτι πῶς μὲν ἐνδέχεται εἶναι ἐκ τοῦ ἑνὸς καὶ πλήθους τὸν ἀριθμὸν οὐθὲν ἐπιχειρεῖται· ὅπως δʼ οὖν λέγουσι ταὐτὰ συμβαίνει δυσχερῆ ἅπερ καὶ τοῖς ἐκ τοῦ ἑνὸς καὶ ἐκ τῆς δυάδος τῆς ἀορίστου. ὁ μὲν γὰρ ἐκ τοῦ κατηγορουμένου καθόλου γεννᾷ τὸν ἀριθμὸν καὶ οὐ τινὸς πλήθους, ὁ δʼ ἐκ τινὸς πλήθους, τοῦ πρώτου δέ (τὴν γὰρ δυάδα πρῶτόν τι εἶναι πλῆθος), ὥστε διαφέρει οὐθὲν ὡς εἰπεῖν, ἀλλʼ αἱ ἀπορίαι αἱ αὐταὶ ἀκολουθήσουσι, μῖξις ἢ θέσις ἢ κρᾶσις ἢ γένεσις καὶ ὅσα ἄλλα τοιαῦτα. μάλιστα δʼ ἄν τις ἐπιζητήσειεν, εἰ μία ἑκάστη μονάς, ἐκ τίνος ἐστίν· οὐ γὰρ δὴ αὐτό γε τὸ ἓν ἑκάστη. ἀνάγκη δὴ ἐκ τοῦ ἑνὸς αὐτοῦ εἶναι καὶ πλήθους ἢ μορίου τοῦ πλήθους. τὸ μὲν οὖν πλῆθός τι εἶναι φάναι τὴν μονάδα ἀδύνατον, ἀδιαίρετόν γʼ οὖσαν· τὸ δʼ ἐκ μορίου ἄλλας ἔχει πολλὰς δυσχερείας· ἀδιαίρετόν τε γὰρ ἕκαστον ἀναγκαῖον εἶναι τῶν μορίων (ἢ πλῆθος εἶναι καὶ τὴν μονάδα διαιρετήν) καὶ μὴ στοιχεῖον εἶναι τὸ ἓν καὶ τὸ πλῆθος (ἡ γὰρ μονὰς ἑκάστη οὐκ ἐκ πλήθους καὶ ἑνός)· ἔτι οὐθὲν ἄλλο ποιεῖ ὁ τοῦτο λέγων ἀλλʼ ἢ ἀριθμὸν ἕτερον· τὸ γὰρ πλῆθος ἀδιαιρέτων ἐστὶν ἀριθμός. ἔτι ζητητέον καὶ περὶ τοὺς οὕτω λέγοντας πότερον ἄπειρος ὁ ἀριθμὸς ἢ πεπερασμένος. ὑπῆρχε γάρ, ὡς ἔοικε, καὶ πεπερασμένον πλῆθος, ἐξ οὗ αἱ πεπερασμέναι μονάδες καὶ τοῦ ἑνός· ἔστι τε ἕτερον αὐτὸ πλῆθος καὶ πλῆθος ἄπειρον· ποῖον οὖν πλῆθος στοιχεῖόν ἐστι καὶ τὸ ἕν; ὁμοίως δὲ καὶ περὶ στιγμῆς ἄν τις ζητήσειε καὶ τοῦ στοιχείου ἐξ οὗ ποιοῦσι τὰ μεγέθη. οὐ γὰρ μία γε μόνον στιγμή ἐστιν αὕτη· τῶν γοῦν ἄλλων στιγμῶν ἑκάστη ἐκ τίνος; οὐ γὰρ δὴ ἔκ γε διαστήματός τινος καὶ αὐτῆς στιγμῆς. ἀλλὰ μὴν οὐδὲ μόρια ἀδιαίρετα ἐνδέχεται τοῦ διαστήματος εἶναι , ὥσπερ τοῦ πλήθους ἐξ ὧν αἱ μονάδες· ὁ μὲν γὰρ ἀριθμὸς ἐξ ἀδιαιρέτων σύγκειται τὰ δὲ μεγέθη οὔ.—πάντα δὴ ταῦτα καὶ ἄλλα τοιαῦτα φανερὸν ποιεῖ ὅτι ἀδύνατον εἶναι τὸν ἀριθμὸν καὶ τὰ μεγέθη χωριστά,  ἔτι δὲ τὸ διαφωνεῖν τοὺς τρόπους περὶ τῶν ἀριθμῶν σημεῖον ὅτι τὰ πράγματα αὐτὰ οὐκ ὄντα ἀληθῆ παρέχει τὴν ταραχὴν αὐτοῖς. οἱ μὲν γὰρ τὰ μαθηματικὰ μόνον ποιοῦντες παρὰ τὰ αἰσθητά, ὁρῶντες τὴν περὶ τὰ εἴδη δυσχέρειαν καὶ πλάσιν, ἀπέστησαν ἀπὸ τοῦ εἰδητικοῦ ἀριθμοῦ καὶ τὸν μαθηματικὸν ἐποίησαν· οἱ δὲ τὰ εἴδη βουλόμενοι ἅμα καὶ ἀριθμοὺς ποιεῖν, οὐχ ὁρῶντες δέ, εἰ τὰς ἀρχάς τις ταύτας θήσεται, πῶς ἔσται ὁ μαθηματικὸς ἀριθμὸς παρὰ τὸν εἰδητικόν, τὸν αὐτὸν εἰδητικὸν καὶ μαθηματικὸν ἐποίησαν ἀριθμὸν τῷ λόγῳ, ἐπεὶ ἔργῳ γε ἀνῄρηται ὁ μαθηματικός (ἰδίας γὰρ καὶ οὐ μαθηματικὰς ὑποθέσεις λέγουσιν)· ὁ δὲ πρῶτος θέμενος τὰ εἴδη εἶναι καὶ ἀριθμοὺς τὰ εἴδη καὶ τὰ μαθηματικὰ εἶναι εὐλόγως ἐχώρισεν· ὥστε πάντας συμβαίνει κατὰ μέν τι λέγειν ὀρθῶς, ὅλως δʼ οὐκ ὀρθῶς. καὶ αὐτοὶ δὲ ὁμολογοῦσιν οὐ ταὐτὰ λέγοντες ἀλλὰ τὰ ἐναντία. αἴτιον δʼ ὅτι αἱ ὑποθέσεις καὶ αἱ ἀρχαὶ ψευδεῖς. χαλεπὸν δʼ ἐκ μὴ καλῶς ἐχόντων λέγειν καλῶς, κατʼ Ἐπίχαρμον· ἀρτίως τε γὰρ λέλεκται, καὶ εὐθέως φαίνεται οὐ καλῶς ἔχον.—ἀλλὰ περὶ μὲν τῶν ἀριθμῶν ἱκανὰ τὰ διηπορημένα καὶ διωρισμένα (μᾶλλον γὰρ ἐκ πλειόνων ἂν ἔτι πεισθείη τις πεπεισμένος, πρὸς δὲ τὸ πεισθῆναι μὴ πεπεισμένος οὐθὲν μᾶλλον)· περὶ δὲ τῶν πρώτων ἀρχῶν καὶ τῶν πρώτων αἰτίων καὶ στοιχείων ὅσα μὲν λέγουσιν οἱ περὶ μόνης τῆς αἰσθητῆς οὐσίας διορίζοντες, τὰ μὲν ἐν τοῖς περὶ φύσεως εἴρηται, τὰ δʼ οὐκ ἔστι τῆς μεθόδου τῆς νῦν· ὅσα δὲ οἱ φάσκοντες εἶναι παρὰ τὰς αἰσθητὰς ἑτέρας οὐσίας, ἐχόμενόν ἐστι θεωρῆσαι τῶν εἰρημένων. ἐπεὶ οὖν λέγουσί τινες τοιαύτας εἶναι τὰς ἰδέας καὶ τοὺς ἀριθμούς, καὶ τὰ τούτων στοιχεῖα τῶν ὄντων εἶναι στοιχεῖα καὶ ἀρχάς, σκεπτέον περὶ τούτων τί λέγουσι καὶ πῶς λέγουσιν. οἱ μὲν οὖν ἀριθμοὺς ποιοῦντες μόνον καὶ τούτους μαθηματικοὺς ὕστερον ἐπισκεπτέοι· τῶν δὲ τὰς ἰδέας λεγόντων ἅμα τόν τε τρόπον θεάσαιτʼ ἄν τις καὶ τὴν ἀπορίαν τὴν περὶ αὐτῶν. ἅμα γὰρ καθόλου τε  ποιοῦσι τὰς ἰδέας καὶ πάλιν ὡς χωριστὰς καὶ τῶν καθʼ ἕκαστον. ταῦτα δʼ ὅτι οὐκ ἐνδέχεται διηπόρηται πρότερον. αἴτιον δὲ τοῦ συνάψαι ταῦτα εἰς ταὐτὸν τοῖς λέγουσι τὰς οὐσίας καθόλου, ὅτι τοῖς αἰσθητοῖς οὐ τὰς αὐτὰς  ἐποίουν· τὰ μὲν οὖν ἐν τοῖς αἰσθητοῖς καθʼ ἕκαστα ῥεῖν ἐνόμιζον καὶ μένειν οὐθὲν αὐτῶν,  τὸ δὲ καθόλου παρὰ ταῦτα εἶναί τε καὶ ἕτερόν τι εἶναι. τοῦτο δʼ, ὥσπερ ἐν τοῖς ἔμπροσθεν  ἐλέγομεν, ἐκίνησε μὲν Σωκράτης διὰ τοὺς ὁρισμούς, οὐ μὴν ἐχώρισέ γε τῶν καθʼ ἕκαστον· καὶ τοῦτο ὀρθῶς ἐνόησεν οὐ χωρίσας. δηλοῖ δὲ ἐκ τῶν ἔργων· ἄνευ μὲν γὰρ τοῦ καθόλου οὐκ ἔστιν ἐπιστήμην λαβεῖν, τὸ δὲ χωρίζειν αἴτιον τῶν συμβαινόντων δυσχερῶν περὶ τὰς ἰδέας ἐστίν. οἱ δʼ ὡς ἀναγκαῖον, εἴπερ ἔσονταί τινες οὐσίαι παρὰ τὰς αἰσθητὰς καὶ ῥεούσας, χωριστὰς εἶναι, ἄλλας μὲν οὐκ εἶχον ταύτας δὲ τὰς καθόλου λεγομένας ἐξέθεσαν, ὥστε συμβαίνειν σχεδὸν τὰς αὐτὰς φύσεις εἶναι τὰς καθόλου καὶ τὰς καθʼ ἕκαστον. αὕτη μὲν οὖν αὐτὴ καθʼ αὑτὴν εἴη τις ἂν δυσχέρεια τῶν εἰρημένων.\n\n\n\n \n\n \n\nὃ δὲ καὶ τοῖς λέγουσι τὰς ἰδέας ἔχει τινὰ ἀπορίαν καὶ τοῖς μὴ λέγουσιν, καὶ κατʼ ἀρχὰς ἐν τοῖς διαπορήμασιν ἐλέχθη πρότερον, λέγωμεν νῦν. εἰ μὲν γάρ τις μὴ θήσει τὰς οὐσίας εἶναι κεχωρισμένας, καὶ τὸν τρόπον τοῦτον ὡς λέγεται τὰ καθʼ ἕκαστα τῶν ὄντων, ἀναιρήσει τὴν οὐσίαν ὡς βουλόμεθα λέγειν· ἂν δέ τις θῇ τὰς οὐσίας χωριστάς, πῶς θήσει τὰ στοιχεῖα καὶ τὰς ἀρχὰς αὐτῶν; εἰ μὲν γὰρ καθʼ ἕκαστον καὶ μὴ καθόλου, τοσαῦτʼ ἔσται τὰ ὄντα ὅσαπερ τὰ στοιχεῖα, καὶ οὐκ ἐπιστητὰ τὰ στοιχεῖα (ἔστωσαν γὰρ αἱ μὲν ἐν τῇ φωνῇ συλλαβαὶ οὐσίαι τὰ δὲ στοιχεῖα αὐτῶν στοιχεῖα τῶν οὐσιῶν· ἀνάγκη δὴ τὸ ΒΑ ἓν εἶναι καὶ ἑκάστην τῶν συλλαβῶν μίαν, εἴπερ μὴ καθόλου καὶ τῷ εἴδει αἱ αὐταὶ ἀλλὰ μία ἑκάστη τῷ ἀριθμῷ καὶ τόδε τι καὶ μὴ ὁμώνυμον· ἔτι δʼ αὐτὸ ὃ ἔστιν ἓν ἕκαστον τιθέασιν· εἰ δʼ αἱ συλλαβαί, οὕτω καὶ ἐξ ὧν εἰσίν· οὐκ ἔσται ἄρα πλείω ἄλφα ἑνός, οὐδὲ τῶν ἄλλων στοιχείων οὐθὲν κατὰ τὸν αὐτὸν λόγον ὅνπερ οὐδὲ τῶν  συλλαβῶν ἡ αὐτὴ ἄλλη καὶ ἄλλη· ἀλλὰ μὴν εἰ τοῦτο, οὐκ ἔσται παρὰ τὰ στοιχεῖα ἕτερα ὄντα, ἀλλὰ μόνον τὰ στοιχεῖα· ἔτι δὲ οὐδʼ ἐπιστητὰ τὰ στοιχεῖα· οὐ γὰρ καθόλου, ἡ δʼ ἐπιστήμη τῶν καθόλου· δῆλον δʼ ἐκ τῶν ἀποδείξεων καὶ τῶν ὁρισμῶν, οὐ γὰρ γίγνεται συλλογισμὸς ὅτι τόδε τὸ τρίγωνον δύο ὀρθαῖς, εἰ μὴ πᾶν τρίγωνον δύο ὀρθαί, οὐδʼ ὅτι ὁδὶ ὁ ἄνθρωπος ζῷον, εἰ μὴ πᾶς ἄνθρωπος ζῷον)·  ἀλλὰ μὴν εἴγε καθόλου αἱ ἀρχαί, ἢ καὶ αἱ ἐκ τούτων οὐσίαι καθόλου ἢ ἔσται μὴ οὐσία πρότερον οὐσίας· τὸ μὲν γὰρ καθόλου οὐκ οὐσία, τὸ δὲ στοιχεῖον καὶ ἡ ἀρχὴ καθόλου, πρότερον δὲ τὸ στοιχεῖον καὶ ἡ ἀρχὴ ὧν ἀρχὴ καὶ στοιχεῖόν ἐστιν. ταῦτά τε δὴ πάντα συμβαίνει εὐλόγως, ὅταν ἐκ στοιχείων τε ποιῶσι τὰς ἰδέας καὶ παρὰ τὰς τὸ αὐτὸ εἶδος ἐχούσας οὐσίας  ἕν τι ἀξιῶσιν εἶναι καχωρισμένον· εἰ δὲ μηθὲν κωλύει ὥσπερ ἐπὶ τῶν τῆς φωνῆς στοιχείων πολλὰ εἶναι τὰ ἄλφα καὶ τὰ βῆτα καὶ μηθὲν εἶναι παρὰ τὰ πολλὰ αὐτὸ ἄλφα καὶ αὐτὸ βῆτα, ἔσονται ἕνεκά γε τούτου ἄπειροι αἱ ὅμοιαι συλλαβαί. τὸ δὲ τὴν ἐπιστήμην εἶναι καθόλου πᾶσαν, ὥστε ἀναγκαῖον εἶναι καὶ τὰς τῶν ὄντων ἀρχὰς καθόλου εἶναι καὶ μὴ οὐσίας κεχωρισμένας, ἔχει μὲν μάλιστʼ ἀπορίαν τῶν λεχθέντων, οὐ μὴν ἀλλὰ ἔστι μὲν ὡς ἀληθὲς τὸ λεγόμενον, ἔστι δʼ ὡς οὐκ ἀληθές. ἡ γὰρ ἐπιστήμη, ὥσπερ καὶ τὸ ἐπίστασθαι, διττόν, ὧν τὸ μὲν δυνάμει τὸ δὲ ἐνεργείᾳ. ἡ μὲν οὖν δύναμις ὡς ὕλη  καθόλου οὖσα καὶ ἀόριστος τοῦ καθόλου καὶ ἀορίστου ἐστίν, ἡ δʼ ἐνέργεια ὡρισμένη καὶ ὡρισμένου, τόδε τι οὖσα τοῦδέ τινος, ἀλλὰ κατὰ συμβεβηκὸς ἡ ὄψις τὸ καθόλου χρῶμα ὁρᾷ ὅτι τόδε τὸ χρῶμα ὃ ὁρᾷ χρῶμά ἐστιν, καὶ ὃ θεωρεῖ ὁ γραμματικός, τόδε τὸ ἄλφα ἄλφα· ἐπεὶ εἰ ἀνάγκη τὰς ἀρχὰς καθόλου εἶναι, ἀνάγκη καὶ τὰ ἐκ τούτων καθόλου, ὥσπερ ἐπὶ τῶν ἀποδείξεων· εἰ δὲ τοῦτο, οὐκ ἔσται χωριστὸν οὐθὲν οὐδʼ οὐσία. ἀλλὰ δῆλον ὅτι ἔστι μὲν ὡς ἡ ἐπιστήμη καθόλου, ἔστι δʼ ὡς οὔ.","paragraphs":[{"id":"RAW_DAZY_BO_5:P0001","text":"περὶ μὲν οὖν τῆς τῶν αἰσθητῶν οὐσίας εἴρηται τίς ἐστιν, ἐν μὲν τῇ μεθόδῳ τῇ τῶν φυσικῶν περὶ τῆς ὕλης, ὕστερον δὲ περὶ τῆς κατʼ ἐνέργειαν· ἐπεὶ δʼ ἡ σκέψις ἐστὶ πότερον ἔστι τις παρὰ τὰς αἰσθητὰς οὐσίας ἀκίνητος καὶ ἀΐδιος ἢ οὐκ ἔστι, καὶ εἰ ἔστι τίς ἐστι, πρῶτον τὰ παρὰ τῶν ἄλλων λεγόμενα θεωρητέον, ὅπως εἴτε τι μὴ καλῶς λέγουσι, μὴ τοῖς αὐτοῖς ἔνοχοι ὦμεν, καὶ εἴ τι δόγμα κοινὸν ἡμῖν κἀκείνοις, τοῦτʼ ἰδίᾳ μὴ καθʼ ἡμῶν δυσχεραίνωμεν· ἀγαπητὸν γὰρ εἴ τις τὰ μὲν κάλλιον λέγοι τὰ δὲ μὴ χεῖρον. δύο δʼ εἰσὶ δόξαι περὶ τούτων· τά τε γὰρ μαθηματικά φασιν οὐσίας εἶναί τινες, οἷον ἀριθμοὺς καὶ γραμμὰς καὶ τὰ συγγενῆ τούτοις, καὶ πάλιν τὰς ἰδέας.","kind":"passage"},{"id":"RAW_DAZY_BO_5:P0002","text":"ἐπεὶ δὲ οἱ μὲν δύο ταῦτα γένη ποιοῦσι, τάς τε ἰδέας καὶ τοὺς μαθηματικοὺς ἀριθμούς, οἱ δὲ μίαν φύσιν ἀμφοτέρων, ἕτεροι δέ τινες τὰς μαθηματικὰς μόνον οὐσίας εἶναί φασι, σκεπτέον πρῶτον μὲν περὶ τῶν μαθηματικῶν, μηδεμίαν προστιθέντας φύσιν ἄλλην αὐτοῖς, οἷον πότερον ἰδέαι τυγχάνουσιν οὖσαι ἢ οὔ, καὶ πότερον ἀρχαὶ καὶ οὐσίαι τῶν ὄντων ἢ οὔ, ἀλλʼ ὡς περὶ μαθηματικῶν μόνον εἴτʼ εἰσὶν εἴτε μὴ εἰσί, καὶ εἰ εἰσὶ πῶς εἰσίν· ἔπειτα μετὰ ταῦτα χωρὶς περὶ τῶν ἰδεῶν αὐτῶν ἁπλῶς καὶ ὅσον νόμου χάριν· τεθρύληται γὰρ τὰ πολλὰ καὶ ὑπὸ τῶν ἐξωτερικῶν λόγων, ἔτι δὲ πρὸς ἐκείνην δεῖ τὴν σκέψιν ἀπαντᾶν τὸν πλείω λόγον, ὅταν ἐπισκοπῶμεν εἰ αἱ οὐσίαι καὶ αἱ ἀρχαὶ τῶν ὄντων ἀριθμοὶ καὶ ἰδέαι εἰσίν· μετὰ γὰρ τὰς ἰδέας αὕτη λείπεται τρίτη σκέψις.—ἀνάγκη δʼ, εἴπερ ἔστι τὰ μαθηματικά, ἢ ἐν τοῖς αἰσθητοῖς εἶναι αὐτὰ καθάπερ λέγουσί τινες, ἢ κεχωρισμένα τῶν αἰσθητῶν (λέγουσι δὲ καὶ οὕτω τινές)· ἢ εἰ μηδετέρως, ἢ","kind":"passage"},{"id":"RAW_DAZY_BO_5:P0003","text":"οὐκ εἰσὶν ἢ ἄλλον τρόπον εἰσίν· ὥσθʼ ἡ ἀμφισβήτησις ἡμῖν ἔσται οὐ περὶ τοῦ εἶναι ἀλλὰ περὶ τοῦ τρόπου.","kind":"passage"},{"id":"RAW_DAZY_BO_5:P0004","text":"ὅτι μὲν τοίνυν ἔν γε τοῖς αἰσθητοῖς ἀδύνατον εἶναι καὶ ἅμα πλασματίας ὁ λόγος, εἴρηται μὲν καὶ ἐν τοῖς διαπορήμασιν ὅτι δύο ἅμα στερεὰ εἶναι ἀδύνατον,  ἔτι δὲ καὶ ὅτι τοῦ αὐτοῦ λόγου καὶ τὰς ἄλλας δυνάμεις καὶ φύσεις ἐν τοῖς αἰσθητοῖς εἶναι καὶ μηδεμίαν κεχωρισμένην·—ταῦτα μὲν οὖν εἴρηται πρότερον, ἀλλὰ πρὸς τούτοις φανερὸν ὅτι ἀδύνατον διαιρεθῆναι ὁτιοῦν σῶμα· κατʼ ἐπίπεδον γὰρ διαιρεθήσεται, καὶ τοῦτο κατὰ γραμμὴν καὶ αὕτη κατὰ στιγμήν, ὥστʼ εἰ τὴν στιγμὴν διελεῖν ἀδύνατον, καὶ τὴν γραμμήν, εἰ δὲ ταύτην, καὶ τἆλλα. τί οὖν διαφέρει ἢ ταύτας εἶναι τοιαύτας φύσεις, ἢ αὐτὰς μὲν μή, εἶναι δʼ ἐν αὐταῖς τοιαύτας φύσεις; τὸ αὐτὸ γὰρ συμβήσεται· διαιρουμένων γὰρ τῶν αἰσθητῶν διαιρεθήσονται, ἢ οὐδὲ αἱ αἰσθηταί. ἀλλὰ μὴν οὐδὲ κεχωρισμένας γʼ εἶναι φύσεις τοιαύτας δυνατόν.","kind":"passage"},{"id":"RAW_DAZY_BO_5:P0005","text":"εἰ γὰρ ἔσται στερεὰ παρὰ τὰ αἰσθητὰ κεχωρισμένα τούτων ἕτερα καὶ πρότερα τῶν αἰσθητῶν, δῆλον ὅτι καὶ παρὰ τὰ ἐπίπεδα ἕτερα ἀναγκαῖον εἶναι ἐπίπεδα κεχωρισμένα καὶ στιγμὰς καὶ γραμμάς (τοῦ γὰρ αὐτοῦ λόγου)· εἰ δὲ ταῦτα, πάλιν παρὰ τὰ τοῦ στερεοῦ τοῦ μαθηματικοῦ ἐπίπεδα καὶ γραμμὰς καὶ στιγμὰς ἕτερα κεχωρισμένα (πρότερα γὰρ τῶν συγκειμένων ἐστὶ τὰ ἀσύνθετα· καὶ εἴπερ τῶν αἰσθητῶν πρότερα σώματα μὴ αἰσθητά, τῷ αὐτῷ λόγῳ καὶ τῶν ἐπιπέδων τῶν ἐν τοῖς ἀκινήτοις στερεοῖς τὰ αὐτὰ καθʼ αὑτά, ὥστε ἕτερα ταῦτα ἐπίπεδα καὶ γραμμαὶ τῶν ἅμα τοῖς στερεοῖς τοῖς κεχωρισμένοις· τὰ μὲν γὰρ ἅμα τοῖς μαθηματικοῖς στερεοῖς τὰ δὲ πρότερα τῶν μαθηματικῶν στερεῶν). πάλιν τοίνυν τούτων τῶν ἐπιπέδων ἔσονται γραμμαί, ὧν πρότερον δεήσει ἑτέρας γραμμὰς καὶ στιγμὰς εἶναι διὰ τὸν αὐτὸν λόγον· καὶ τούτων τῶν ἐκ ταῖς προτέραις γραμμαῖς ἑτέρας προτέρας στιγμάς, ὧν οὐκέτι πρότεραι ἕτεραι.","kind":"passage"},{"id":"RAW_DAZY_BO_5:P0006","text":"ἄτοπός τε δὴ γίγνεται ἡ σώρευσις (συμβαίνει γὰρ στερεὰ μὲν μοναχὰ παρὰ τὰ αἰσθητά, ἐπίπεδα δὲ τριττὰ παρὰ τὰ αἰσθητά—τά τε παρὰ τὰ αἰσθητὰ καὶ τὰ ἐν τοῖς μαθηματικοῖς στερεοῖς καὶ τὰ παρὰ τὰ ἐν τούτοις—γραμμαὶ δὲ τετραξαί, στιγμαὶ δὲ πενταξαί· ὥστε περὶ ποῖα αἱ ἐπιστῆμαι ἔσονται αἱ μαθηματικαὶ τούτων; οὐ γὰρ δὴ περὶ τὰ ἐν τῷ στερεῷ τῷ ἀκινήτῳ ἐπίπεδα καὶ γραμμὰς καὶ στιγμάς· ἀεὶ γὰρ περὶ τὰ πρότερα ἡ ἐπιστήμη)· ὁ δʼ αὐτὸς λόγος καὶ περὶ τῶν ἀριθμῶν· παρʼ ἑκάστας γὰρ τὰς στιγμὰς ἕτεραι ἔσονται μονάδες, καὶ παρʼ ἕκαστα τὰ ὄντα, τὰ αἰσθητά, εἶτα τὰ νοητά, ὥστʼ ἔσται γένη τῶν μαθηματικῶν ἀριθμῶν.","kind":"passage"},{"id":"RAW_DAZY_BO_5:P0007","text":"ἔτι ἅπερ καὶ ἐν τοῖς ἀπορήμασιν ἐπήλθομεν πῶς ἐνδέχεται λύειν;  περὶ ἃ γὰρ ἡ ἀστρολογία ἐστίν, ὁμοίως ἔσται παρὰ τὰ αἰσθητὰ καὶ περὶ ἃ ἡ γεωμετρία· εἶναι δʼ οὐρανὸν καὶ τὰ μόρια αὐτοῦ πῶς δυνατόν, ἢ ἄλλο ὁτιοῦν ἔχον κίνησιν; ὁμοίως δὲ καὶ τὰ ὀπτικὰ καὶ τὰ ἁρμονικά· ἔσται γὰρ φωνή τε καὶ ὄψις παρὰ τὰ αἰσθητὰ καὶ τὰ καθʼ ἕκαστα, ὥστε δῆλον ὅτι καὶ αἱ ἄλλαι αἰσθήσεις καὶ τὰ ἄλλα αἰσθητά· τί γὰρ μᾶλλον τάδε ἢ τάδε; εἰ δὲ ταῦτα, καὶ ζῷα ἔσονται, εἴπερ καὶ αἰσθήσεις. ἔτι γράφεται ἔνια καθόλου ὑπὸ τῶν μαθηματικῶν παρὰ ταύτας τὰς οὐσίας. ἔσται οὖν καὶ αὕτη τις ἄλλη οὐσία μεταξὺ κεχωρισμένη τῶν τʼ ἰδεῶν καὶ τῶν μεταξύ, ἣ οὔτε ἀριθμός ἐστιν οὔτε στιγμαὶ οὔτε μέγεθος οὔτε χρόνος. εἰ δὲ τοῦτο ἀδύνατον, δῆλον ὅτι κἀκεῖνα ἀδύνατον εἶναι κεχωρισμένα τῶν αἰσθητῶν.","kind":"passage"},{"id":"RAW_DAZY_BO_5:P0008","text":"ὅλως δὲ τοὐναντίον συμβαίνει καὶ τοῦ ἀληθοῦς καὶ τοῦ εἰωθότος ὑπολαμβάνεσθαι, εἴ τις θήσει οὕτως εἶναι τὰ μαθηματικὰ ὡς κεχωρισμένας τινὰς φύσεις. ἀνάγκη γὰρ διὰ τὸ μὲν οὕτως εἶναι αὐτὰς προτέρας εἶναι τῶν αἰσθητῶν μεγεθῶν, κατὰ τὸ ἀληθὲς δὲ ὑστέρας· τὸ γὰρ ἀτελὲς μέγεθος γενέσει μὲν πρότερόν ἐστι, τῇ οὐσίᾳ δʼ ὕστερον, οἷον ἄψυχον ἐμψύχου. ἔτι τίνι καὶ πότʼ ἔσται ἓν τὰ μαθηματικὰ μεγέθη; τὰ μὲν γὰρ ἐνταῦθα ψυχῇ ἢ μέρει ψυχῆς ἢ ἄλλῳ τινί, εὐλόγως (εἰ δὲ μή, πολλά, καὶ διαλύεται), ἐκείνοις δὲ διαιρετοῖς καὶ ποσοῖς οὖσι τί αἴτιον τοῦ ἓν εἶναι καὶ συμμένειν; ἔτι αἱ γενέσεις δηλοῦσιν. πρῶτον μὲν γὰρ ἐπὶ μῆκος γίγνεται, εἶτα ἐπὶ πλάτος, τελευταῖον δʼ εἰς βάθος, καὶ τέλος ἔσχεν.","kind":"passage"},{"id":"RAW_DAZY_BO_5:P0009","text":"εἰ οὖν τὸ τῇ γενέσει ὕστερον τῇ οὐσίᾳ πρότερον, τὸ σῶμα πρότερον ἂν εἴη ἐπιπέδου καὶ μήκους· καὶ ταύτῃ καὶ τέλειον καὶ ὅλον μᾶλλον, ὅτι ἔμψυχον γίγνεται· γραμμὴ δὲ ἔμψυχος ἢ ἐπίπεδον πῶς ἂν εἴη; ὑπὲρ γὰρ τὰς αἰσθήσεις τὰς ἡμετέρας ἂν εἴη τὸ ἀξίωμα. ἔτι τὸ μὲν σῶμα οὐσία τις (ἤδη γὰρ ἔχει πως τὸ τέλειον), αἱ δὲ γραμμαὶ πῶς οὐσίαι; οὔτε γὰρ ὡς εἶδος καὶ μορφή τις, οἷον εἰ ἄρα ἡ ψυχὴ τοιοῦτον, οὔτε ὡς ἡ ὕλη, οἷον τὸ σῶμα· οὐθὲν γὰρ ἐκ γραμμῶν οὐδʼ ἐπιπέδων οὐδὲ στιγμῶν φαίνεται συνίστασθαι δυνάμενον, εἰ δʼ ἦν οὐσία τις ὑλική, τοῦτʼ ἂν ἐφαίνετο δυνάμενα πάσχειν. τῷ μὲν οὖν λόγῳ ἔστω πρότερα,  ἀλλʼ οὐ πάντα ὅσα τῷ λόγῳ πρότερα καὶ τῇ οὐσίᾳ πρότερα. τῇ μὲν γὰρ οὐσίᾳ πρότερα ὅσα χωριζόμενα τῷ εἶναι ὑπερβάλλει, τῷ λόγῳ δὲ ὅσων οἱ λόγοι ἐκ τῶν λόγων· ταῦτα δὲ οὐχ ἅμα ὑπάρχει.","kind":"passage"},{"id":"RAW_DAZY_BO_5:P0010","text":"εἰ γὰρ μὴ ἔστι τὰ πάθη παρὰ τὰς οὐσίας, οἷον κινούμενόν τι ἢ λευκόν, τοῦ λευκοῦ ἀνθρώπου τὸ λευκὸν πρότερον κατὰ τὸν λόγον ἀλλʼ οὐ κατὰ τὴν οὐσίαν· οὐ γὰρ ἐνδέχεται εἶναι κεχωρισμένον ἀλλʼ ἀεὶ ἅμα τῷ συνόλῳ ἐστίν (σύνολον δὲ λέγω τὸν ἄνθρωπον τὸν λευκόν), ὥστε φανερὸν ὅτι οὔτε τὸ ἐξ ἀφαιρέσεως πρότερον οὔτε τὸ ἐκ προσθέσεως ὕστερον· ἐκ προσθέσεως γὰρ τῷ λευκῷ ὁ λευκὸς ἄνθρωπος λέγεται.","kind":"passage"},{"id":"RAW_DAZY_BO_5:P0011","text":"ὅτι μὲν οὖν οὔτε οὐσίαι μᾶλλον τῶν σωμάτων εἰσὶν οὔτε πρότερα τῷ εἶναι τῶν αἰσθητῶν ἀλλὰ τῷ λόγῳ μόνον, οὔτε κεχωρισμένα που εἶναι δυνατόν, εἴρηται ἱκανῶς· ἐπεὶ δʼ οὐδʼ ἐν τοῖς αἰσθητοῖς ἐνεδέχετο αὐτὰ εἶναι, φανερὸν ὅτι ἢ ὅλως οὐκ ἔστιν ἢ τρόπον τινὰ ἔστι καὶ διὰ τοῦτο οὐχ ἁπλῶς ἔστιν· πολλαχῶς γὰρ τὸ εἶναι λέγομεν.","kind":"passage"},{"id":"RAW_DAZY_BO_5:P0012","text":"ὥσπερ γὰρ καὶ τὰ καθόλου ἐν τοῖς μαθήμασιν οὐ περὶ κεχωρισμένων ἐστὶ παρὰ τὰ μεγέθη καὶ τοὺς ἀριθμοὺς ἀλλὰ περὶ τούτων μέν, οὐχ ᾗ δὲ τοιαῦτα οἷα ἔχειν μέγεθος ἢ εἶναι διαιρετά, δῆλον ὅτι ἐνδέχεται καὶ περὶ τῶν αἰσθητῶν μεγεθῶν εἶναι καὶ λόγους καὶ ἀποδείξεις, μὴ ᾗ δὲ αἰσθητὰ ἀλλʼ ᾗ τοιαδί.","kind":"passage"},{"id":"RAW_DAZY_BO_5:P0013","text":"ὥσπερ γὰρ καὶ ᾗ κινούμενα μόνον πολλοὶ λόγοι εἰσί, χωρὶς τοῦ τί ἕκαστόν ἐστι τῶν τοιούτων καὶ τῶν συμβεβηκότων αὐτοῖς, καὶ οὐκ ἀνάγκη διὰ ταῦτα ἢ κεχωρισμένον τι εἶναι κινούμενον τῶν αἰσθητῶν ἢ ἐν τούτοις τινὰ φύσιν εἶναι ἀφωρισμένην, οὕτω καὶ ἐπὶ τῶν κινουμένων ἔσονται λόγοι καὶ ἐπιστῆμαι, οὐχ ᾗ κινούμενα δὲ ἀλλʼ ᾗ σώματα μόνον, καὶ πάλιν ᾗ ἐπίπεδα μόνον καὶ ᾗ μήκη μόνον, καὶ ᾗ διαιρετὰ καὶ ᾗ ἀδιαίρετα ἔχοντα δὲ θέσιν καὶ ᾗ ἀδιαίρετα μόνον, ὥστʼ ἐπεὶ ἁπλῶς λέγειν ἀληθὲς μὴ μόνον τὰ χωριστὰ εἶναι ἀλλὰ καὶ τὰ μὴ χωριστά (οἷον κινούμενα εἶναι), καὶ τὰ μαθηματικὰ ὅτι ἔστιν ἁπλῶς ἀληθὲς εἰπεῖν, καὶ τοιαῦτά γε οἷα λέγουσιν.","kind":"passage"},{"id":"RAW_DAZY_BO_5:P0014","text":"καὶ ὥσπερ καὶ τὰς ἄλλας ἐπιστήμας ἁπλῶς ἀληθὲς εἰπεῖν τούτου εἶναι, οὐχὶ τοῦ συμβεβηκότος (οἷον ὅτι λευκοῦ, εἰ τὸ ὑγιεινὸν λευκόν, ἡ δʼ ἔστιν ὑγιεινοῦ) ἀλλʼ ἐκείνου οὗ ἐστὶν ἑκάστη,  εἰ ᾗ ὑγιεινὸν ὑγιεινοῦ, εἰ δʼ ᾗ ἄνθρωπος ἀνθρώπου, οὕτω καὶ τὴν γεωμετρίαν· οὐκ εἰ συμβέβηκεν αἰσθητὰ εἶναι ὧν ἐστί, μὴ ἔστι δὲ ᾗ αἰσθητά, οὐ τῶν αἰσθητῶν ἔσονται αἱ μαθηματικαὶ ἐπιστῆμαι, οὐ μέντοι οὐσὲ παρὰ ταῦτα ἄλλων κεχωρισμένων. πολλὰ δὲ συμβέβηκε καθʼ αὑτὰ τοῖς πράγμασιν ᾗ ἕκαστον ὑπάρχει τῶν τοιούτων, ἐπεὶ καὶ ᾗ θῆλυ τὸ ζῷον καὶ ᾗ ἄρρεν, ἴδια πάθη ἔστιν (καίτοι οὐκ ἔστι τι θῆλυ οὐδʼ ἄρρεν κεχωρισμένον τῶν ζῴων)· ὥστε καὶ ᾗ μήκη μόνον καὶ ᾗ ἐπίπεδα.","kind":"passage"},{"id":"RAW_DAZY_BO_5:P0015","text":"καὶ ὅσῳ δὴ ἂν περὶ προτέρων τῷ λόγῳ καὶ ἁπλουστέρων, τοσούτῳ μᾶλλον ἔχει τὸ ἀκριβές (τοῦτο δὲ τὸ ἁπλοῦν ἐστίν), ὥστε ἄνευ τε μεγέθους μᾶλλον ἢ μετὰ μεγέθους, καὶ μάλιστα ἄνευ κινήσεως, ἐὰν δὲ κίνησιν, μάλιστα τὴν πρώτην· ἁπλουστάτη γάρ, καὶ ταύτης ἡ ὁμαλή. ὁ δʼ αὐτὸς λόγος καὶ περὶ ἁρμονικῆς καὶ ὀπτικῆς· οὐδετέρα γὰρ ᾗ ὄψις ἢ ᾗ φωνὴ θεωρεῖ, ἀλλʼ ᾗ γραμμαὶ καὶ ἀριθμοί (οἰκεῖα μέντοι ταῦτα πάθη ἐκείνων), καὶ ἡ μηχανικὴ δὲ ὡσαύτως, ὥστʼ εἴ τις θέμενος κεχωρισμένα τῶν συμβεβηκότων σκοπεῖ τι περὶ τούτων ᾗ τοιαῦτα, οὐθὲν διὰ τοῦτο ψεῦδος ψεύσεται, ὥσπερ οὐδʼ ὅταν ἐν τῇ γῇ γράφῃ καὶ ποδιαίαν φῇ τὴν μὴ ποδιαίαν· οὐ γὰρ ἐν ταῖς προτάσεσι τὸ ψεῦδος. ἄριστα δʼ ἂν οὕτω θεωρηθείη ἕκαστον, εἴ τις τὸ μὴ κεχωρισμένον θείη χωρίσας, ὅπερ ὁ ἀριθμητικὸς ποιεῖ καὶ ὁ γεωμέτρης. ἓν μὲν γὰρ καὶ ἀδιαίρετον ὁ ἄνθρωπος ᾗ ἄνθρωπος· ὁ δʼ ἔθετο ἓν ἀδιαίρετον, εἶτʼ ἐθεώρησεν εἴ τι τῷ ἀνθρώπῳ συμβέβηκεν ᾗ ἀδιαίρετος.","kind":"passage"},{"id":"RAW_DAZY_BO_5:P0016","text":"ὁ δὲ γεωμέτρης οὔθʼ ᾗ ἄνθρωπος οὔθʼ ᾗ ἀδιαίρετος ἀλλʼ ᾗ στερεόν. ἃ γὰρ κἂν εἰ μή που ἦν ἀδιαίρετος ὑπῆρχεν αὐτῷ, δῆλον ὅτι καὶ ἄνευ τούτων ἐνδέχεται αὐτῷ ὑπάρχειν , ὥστε διὰ τοῦτο ὀρθῶς οἱ γεωμέτραι λέγουσι, καὶ περὶ ὄντων διαλέγονται, καὶ ὄντα ἐστίν· διττὸν γὰρ τὸ ὄν, τὸ μὲν ἐντελεχείᾳ τὸ δʼ ὑλικῶς. ἐπεὶ δὲ τὸ ἀγαθὸν καὶ τὸ καλὸν ἕτερον (τὸ μὲν γὰρ ἀεὶ ἐν πράξει, τὸ δὲ καλὸν καὶ ἐν τοῖς ἀκινήτοις), οἱ φάσκοντες οὐδὲν λέγειν τὰς μαθηματικὰς ἐπιστήμας περὶ καλοῦ ἢ ἀγαθοῦ ψεύδονται. λέγουσι γὰρ καὶ δεικνύουσι μάλιστα· οὐ γὰρ εἰ μὴ ὀνομάζουσι τὰ δʼ ἔργα καὶ τοὺς λόγους δεικνύουσιν, οὐ λέγουσι περὶ αὐτῶν. τοῦ δὲ καλοῦ μέγιστα εἴδη τάξις καὶ συμμετρία καὶ τὸ ὡρισμένον,  ἃ μάλιστα δεικνύουσιν αἱ μαθηματικαὶ ἐπιστῆμαι. καὶ ἐπεί γε πολλῶν αἴτια φαίνεται ταῦτα (λέγω δʼ οἷον ἡ τάξις καὶ τὸ ὡρισμένον), δῆλον ὅτι λέγοιεν ἂν καὶ τὴν τοιαύτην αἰτίαν τὴν ὡς τὸ καλὸν αἴτιον τρόπον τινά.","kind":"passage"},{"id":"RAW_DAZY_BO_5:P0017","text":"μᾶλλον δὲ γνωρίμως ἐν ἄλλοις περὶ αὐτῶν ἐροῦμεν.","kind":"passage"},{"id":"RAW_DAZY_BO_5:P0018","text":"περὶ μὲν οὖν τῶν μαθηματικῶν, ὅτι τε ὄντα ἐστὶ καὶ πῶς ὄντα, καὶ πῶς πρότερα καὶ πῶς οὐ πρότερα, τοσαῦτα εἰρήσθω· περὶ δὲ τῶν ἰδεῶν πρῶτον αὐτὴν τὴν κατὰ τὴν ἰδέαν δόξαν ἐπισκεπτέον, μηθὲν συνάπτοντας πρὸς τὴν τῶν ἀριθμῶν φύσιν, ἀλλʼ ὡς ὑπέλαβον ἐξ ἀρχῆς οἱ πρῶτοι τὰς ἰδέας φήσαντες εἶναι. συνέβη δʼ ἡ περὶ τῶν εἰδῶν δόξα τοῖς εἰποῦσι διὰ τὸ πεισθῆναι περὶ τῆς ἀληθείας τοῖς Ἡρακλειτείοις λόγοις ὡς πάντων τῶν αἰσθητῶν ἀεὶ ῥεόντων, ὥστʼ εἴπερ ἐπιστήμη τινὸς ἔσται καὶ φρόνησις, ἑτέρας δεῖν τινὰς φύσεις εἶναι παρὰ τὰς αἰσθητὰς μενούσας· οὐ γὰρ εἶναι τῶν ῥεόντων ἐπιστήμην.","kind":"passage"},{"id":"RAW_DAZY_BO_5:P0019","text":"Σωκράτους δὲ περὶ τὰς ἠθικὰς ἀρετὰς πραγματευομένου καὶ περὶ τούτων ὁρίζεσθαι καθόλου ζητοῦντος πρώτου (τῶν μὲν γὰρ φυσικῶν ἐπὶ μικρὸν Δημόκριτος ἥψατο μόνον καὶ ὡρίσατό πως τὸ θερμὸν καὶ τὸ ψυχρόν· οἱ δὲ Πυθαγόρειοι πρότερον περί τινων ὀλίγων, ὧν τοὺς λόγους εἰς τοὺς ἀριθμοὺς ἀνῆπτον, οἷον τί ἐστι καιρὸς ἢ τὸ δίκαιον ἢ γάμος· ἐκεῖνος δʼ εὐλόγως ἐζήτει τὸ τί ἐστιν· συλλογίζεσθαι γὰρ ἐζήτει, ἀρχὴ δὲ τῶν συλλογισμῶν τὸ τί ἐστιν· διαλεκτικὴ γὰρ ἰσχὺς οὔπω τότʼ ἦν ὥστε δύνασθαι καὶ χωρὶς τοῦ τί ἐστι τἀναντία ἐπισκοπεῖν, καὶ τῶν ἐναντίων εἰ ἡ αὐτὴ ἐπιστήμη· δύο γάρ ἐστιν ἅ τις ἂν ἀποδοίη Σωκράτει δικαίως, τούς τʼ ἐπακτικοὺς λόγους καὶ τὸ ὁρίζεσθαι καθόλου· ταῦτα γάρ ἐστιν ἄμφω περὶ ἀρχὴν ἐπιστήμης)·—ἀλλʼ ὁ μὲν Σωκράτης τὰ καθόλου οὐ χωριστὰ ἐποίει οὐδὲ τοὺς ὁρισμούς· οἱ δʼ ἐχώρισαν, καὶ τὰ τοιαῦτα τῶν ὄντων ἰδέας προσηγόρευσαν, ὥστε συνέβαινεν αὐτοῖς σχεδὸν τῷ αὐτῷ λόγῳ πάντων ἰδέας εἶναι","kind":"passage"},{"id":"RAW_DAZY_BO_5:P0020","text":"τῶν καθόλου λεγομένων, καὶ παραπλήσιον ὥσπερ ἂν εἴ τις ἀριθμῆσαι βουλόμενος ἐλαττόνων μὲν ὄντων οἴοιτο μὴ δυνήσεσθαι, πλείω δὲ ποιήσας ἀριθμοίη· πλείω γάρ ἐστι τῶν καθʼ ἕκαστα αἰσθητῶν ὡς εἰπεῖν τὰ εἴδη,  περὶ ὧν ζητοῦντες τὰς αἰτίας ἐκ τούτων ἐκεῖ προῆλθον· καθʼ ἕκαστόν τε γὰρ ὁμώνυμόν τι ἔστι καὶ παρὰ τὰς οὐσίας, τῶν τε ἄλλων ἓν ἔστιν ἐπὶ πολλῶν, καὶ ἐπὶ τοῖσδε καὶ ἐπὶ τοῖς ἀϊδίοις. ἔτι καθʼ οὓς τρόπους δείκνυται ὅτι ἔστι τὰ εἴδη, κατʼ οὐθένα φαίνεται τούτων· ἐξ ἐνίων μὲν γὰρ οὐκ ἀνάγκη γίγνεσθαι συλλογισμόν, ἐξ ἐνίων δὲ καὶ οὐχ ὧν οἴονται τούτων εἴδη γίγνεται. κατά τε γὰρ τοὺς λόγους τοὺς ἐκ τῶν ἐπιστημῶν ἔσται εἴδη πάντων ὅσων ἐπιστῆμαι εἰσίν, καὶ κατὰ τὸ ἓν ἐπὶ πολλῶν καὶ τῶν ἀποφάσεων, κατὰ δὲ τὸ νοεῖν τι φθαρέντος τῶν φθαρτῶν· φάντασμα γάρ τι τούτων ἔστιν.","kind":"passage"},{"id":"RAW_DAZY_BO_5:P0021","text":"ἔτι δὲ οἱ ἀκριβέστατοι τῶν λόγων οἱ μὲν τῶν πρός τι ποιοῦσιν ἰδέας, ὧν οὔ φασιν εἶναι καθʼ αὑτὸ γένος, οἱ δὲ τὸν τρίτον ἄνθρωπον λέγουσιν. ὅλως τε ἀναιροῦσιν οἱ περὶ τῶν εἰδῶν λόγοι ἃ μᾶλλον βούλονται εἶναι οἱ λέγοντες εἴδη τοῦ τὰς ἰδέας εἶναι· συμβαίνει γὰρ μὴ εἶναι πρῶτον τὴν δυάδα ἀλλὰ τὸν ἀριθμόν, καὶ τούτου τὸ πρός τι καὶ τοῦτο τοῦ καθʼ αὑτό, καὶ πάνθʼ ὅσα τινὲς ἀκολουθήσαντες ταῖς περὶ τῶν εἰδῶν δόξαις ἠναντιώθησαν ταῖς ἀρχαῖς.","kind":"passage"},{"id":"RAW_DAZY_BO_5:P0022","text":"ἔτι κατὰ μὲν τὴν ὑπόληψιν καθʼ ἥν φασιν εἶναι τὰς ἰδέας οὐ μόνον τῶν οὐσιῶν ἔσονται εἴδη ἀλλὰ καὶ ἄλλων πολλῶν (τὸ γὰρ νόημα ἓν οὐ μόνον περὶ τὰς οὐσίας ἀλλὰ καὶ κατὰ μὴ οὐσιῶν ἐστί, καὶ ἐπιστῆμαι οὐ μόνον τῆς οὐσίας εἰσί· συμβαίνει δὲ καὶ ἄλλα μυρία τοιαῦτα)· κατὰ δὲ τὸ ἀναγκαῖον καὶ τὰς δόξας τὰς περὶ αὐτῶν, εἰ ἔστι μεθεκτὰ τὰ εἴδη, τῶν οὐσιῶν ἀναγκαῖον ἰδέας εἶναι μόνον· οὐ γὰρ κατὰ συμβεβηκὸς μετέχονται ἀλλὰ δεῖ ταύτῃ ἑκάστου μετέχειν ᾗ μὴ καθʼ ὑποκειμένου λέγονται (λέγω δʼ οἷον, εἴ τι αὐτοῦ διπλασίου μετέχει, τοῦτο καὶ ἀϊδίου μετέχει, ἀλλὰ κατὰ συμβεβηκός· συμβέβηκε γὰρ τῷ διπλασίῳ ἀϊδίῳ εἶναι), ὥστε ἔσται οὐσία τὰ εἴδη· ταὐτὰ δʼ ἐνταῦθα οὐσίαν σημαίνει κἀκεῖ· ἢ τί ἔσται τὸ εἶναι φάναι τι παρὰ ταῦτα, τὸ ἓν ἐπὶ πολλῶν; καὶ εἰ μὲν ταὐτὸ εἶδος τῶν ἰδεῶν καὶ τῶν μετεχόντων, ἔσται τι κοινόν (τί γὰρ μᾶλλον ἐπὶ τῶν φθαρτῶν δυάδων, καὶ τῶν δυάδων τῶν πολλῶν μὲν ἀϊδίων δέ, τὸ δυὰς ἓν καὶ","kind":"passage"},{"id":"RAW_DAZY_BO_5:P0023","text":"ταὐτόν, ἢ ἐπʼ αὐτῆς καὶ τῆς τινός;)· εἰ δὲ μὴ τὸ αὐτὸ εἶδος,  ὁμώνυμα ἂν εἴη, καὶ ὅμοιον ὥσπερ ἂν εἴ τις καλοῖ ἄνθρωπον τόν τε Καλλίαν καὶ τὸ ξύλον, μηδεμίαν κοινωνίαν ἐπιβλέψας αὐτῶν. εἰ δὲ τὰ μὲν ἄλλα τοὺς κοινοὺς λόγους ἐφαρμόττειν θήσομεν τοῖς εἴδεσιν, οἷον ἐπʼ αὐτὸν τὸν κύκλον σχῆμα ἐπίπεδον καὶ τὰ λοιπὰ μέρη τοῦ λόγου, τὸ δʼ ὃ ἔστι προστεθήσεται, σκοπεῖν δεῖ μὴ κενὸν ᾖ τοῦτο παντελῶς. τίνι τε γὰρ προστεθήσεται; τῷ μέσῳ ἢ τῷ ἐπιπέδῳ ἢ πᾶσιν; πάντα γὰρ τὰ ἐν τῇ οὐσίᾳ ἰδέαι, οἷον τὸ ζῷον καὶ τὸ δίπουν. ἔτι δῆλον ὅτι ἀνάγκη αὐτὸ εἶναί τι, ὥσπερ τὸ ἐπίπεδον, φύσιν τινὰ ἣ πᾶσιν ἐνυπάρξει τοῖς εἴδεσιν ὡς γένος.","kind":"passage"},{"id":"RAW_DAZY_BO_5:P0024","text":"πάντων δὲ μάλιστα διαπορήσειεν ἄν τις τί ποτε συμβάλλονται τὰ εἴδη ἢ τοῖς ἀϊδίοις τῶν αἰσθητῶν ἢ τοῖς γιγνομένοις καὶ  φθειρομένοις· οὔτε γὰρ κινήσεώς ἐστιν οὔτε μεταβολῆς οὐδεμιᾶς αἴτια αὐτοῖς. ἀλλὰ μὴν οὔτε πρὸς τὴν ἐπιστήμην οὐθὲν βοηθεῖ τὴν τῶν ἄλλων (οὐδὲ γὰρ οὐσία ἐκεῖνα τούτων· ἐν τούτοις γὰρ ἂν ἦν), οὔτʼ εἰς τὸ εἶναι, μὴ ἐνυπάρχοντά γε τοῖς μετέχουσιν· οὕτω μὲν γὰρ ἴσως αἴτια δόξειεν ἂν εἶναι ὡς τὸ λευκὸν μεμιγμένον τῷ λευκῷ, ἀλλʼ οὗτος μὲν ὁ λόγος λίαν εὐκίνητος, ὃν Ἀναξαγόρας μὲν πρότερος Εὔδοξος δὲ ὕστερος ἔλεγε διαπορῶν καὶ ἕτεροί τινες (ῥᾴδιον γὰρ πολλὰ συναγαγεῖν καὶ ἀδύνατα πρὸς τὴν τοιαύτην δόξαν)· ἀλλὰ μὴν οὐδὲ ἐκ τῶν εἰδῶν ἐστὶ τἆλλα κατʼ οὐθένα τρόπον τῶν εἰωθότων λέγεσθαι. τὸ δὲ λέγειν παραδείγματα εἶναι καὶ μετέχειν αὐτῶν τὰ ἄλλα κενολογεῖν ἐστὶ καὶ μεταφορὰς λέγειν ποιητικάς.","kind":"passage"},{"id":"RAW_DAZY_BO_5:P0025","text":"τί γάρ ἐστι τὸ ἐργαζόμενον πρὸς τὰς ἰδέας ἀποβλέπον; ἐνδέχεταί τε καὶ εἶναι καὶ γίγνεσθαι ὁτιοῦν καὶ μὴ εἰκαζόμενον, ὥστε καὶ ὄντος Σωκράτους καὶ μὴ ὄντος γένοιτʼ ἂν οἷος Σωκράτης· ὁμοίως δὲ δῆλον ὅτι κἂν εἰ ἦν ὁ Σωκράτης ἀΐδιος. ἔσται τε πλείω παραδείγματα τοῦ αὐτοῦ, ὥστε καὶ εἴδη, οἷον τοῦ ἀνθρώπου τὸ ζῷον καὶ τὸ δίπουν, ἅμα δὲ καὶ αὐτοάνθρωπος. ἔτι οὐ μόνον τῶν αἰσθητῶν παραδείγματα τὰ εἴδη ἀλλὰ καὶ αὐτῶν, οἷον τὸ γένος τῶν ὡς γένους εἰδῶν· ὥστε τὸ αὐτὸ ἔσται παράδειγμα καὶ εἰκών.","kind":"passage"},{"id":"RAW_DAZY_BO_5:P0026","text":"ἔτι δόξειεν ἂν ἀδύνατον χωρὶς εἶναι τὴν οὐσίαν καὶ οὗ ἡ οὐσία·  ὥστε πῶς ἂν αἱ ἰδέαι οὐσίαι τῶν πραγμάτων οὖσαι χωρὶς εἶεν; ἐν δὲ τῷ Φαίδωνι τοῦτον λέγεται τὸν τρόπον, ὡς καὶ τοῦ εἶναι καὶ τοῦ γίγνεσθαι αἴτια τὰ εἴδη ἐστίν· καίτοι τῶν εἰδῶν ὄντων ὅμως οὐ γίγνεται ἂν μὴ ᾖ τὸ κινῆσον, καὶ πολλὰ γίγνεται ἕτερα, οἷον οἰκία καὶ δακτύλιος, ὧν οὔ φασιν εἶναι εἴδη· ὥστε δῆλον ὅτι ἐνδέχεται κἀκεῖνα, ὧν φασὶν ἰδέας εἶναι, καὶ εἶναι καὶ γίγνεσθαι διὰ τοιαύτας αἰτίας οἵας καὶ τὰ ῥηθέντα νῦν, ἀλλʼ οὐ διὰ τὰ εἴδη. ἀλλὰ περὶ μὲν τῶν ἰδεῶν καὶ τοῦτον τὸν τρόπον καὶ διὰ λογικωτέρων καὶ ἀκριβεστέρων λόγων ἔστι πολλὰ συναγαγεῖν ὅμοια τοῖς τεθεωρημένοις.","kind":"passage"},{"id":"RAW_DAZY_BO_5:P0027","text":"ἐπεὶ δὲ διώρισται περὶ τούτων, καλῶς ἔχει πάλιν θεωρῆσαι τὰ περὶ τοὺς ἀριθμοὺς συμβαίνοντα τοῖς λέγουσιν οὐσίας αὐτοὺς εἶναι χωριστὰς καὶ τῶν ὄντων αἰτίας πρώτας. ἀνάγκη δʼ, εἴπερ ἐστὶν ὁ ἀριθμὸς φύσις τις καὶ μὴ ἄλλη τίς ἐστιν αὐτοῦ ἡ οὐσία ἀλλὰ τοῦτʼ αὐτό, ὥσπερ φασί τινες, ἤτοι εἶναι τὸ μὲν πρῶτόν τι αὐτοῦ τὸ δʼ ἐχόμενον, ἕτερον ὂν τῷ εἴδει ἕκαστον,—καὶ τοῦτο ἢ ἐπὶ τῶν μονάδων εὐθὺς ὑπάρχει καὶ ἔστιν ἀσύμβλητος ὁποιαοῦν μονὰς ὁποιᾳοῦν μονάδι, ἢ εὐθὺς ἐφεξῆς πᾶσαι καὶ συμβληταὶ ὁποιαιοῦν ὁποιαισοῦν, οἷον λέγουσιν εἶναι τὸν μαθηματικὸν ἀριθμόν (ἐν γὰρ τῷ μαθηματικῷ οὐδὲν διαφέρει οὐδεμία μονὰς ἑτέρα ἑτέρας)· ἢ τὰς μὲν συμβλητὰς τὰς δὲ μή (οἷον εἰ ἔστι μετὰ τὸ ἓν πρώτη ἡ δυάς, ἔπειτα ἡ τριὰς καὶ οὕτω δὴ ὁ ἄλλος ἀριθμός, εἰσὶ δὲ συμβληταὶ αἱ ἐν ἑκάστῳ ἀριθμῷ μονάδες, οἷον αἱ ἐν τῇ δυάδι τῇ πρώτῃ αὑταῖς, καὶ αἱ ἐν τῇ τριάδι τῇ πρώτῃ αὑταῖς, καὶ οὕτω δὴ ἐπὶ τῶν ἄλλων ἀριθμῶν· αἱ δʼ ἐν τῇ","kind":"passage"},{"id":"RAW_DAZY_BO_5:P0028","text":"δυάδι αὐτῇ πρὸς τὰς ἐν τῇ τριάδι αὐτῇ ἀσύμβλητοι, ὁμοίως δὲ καὶ ἐπὶ τῶν ἄλλων τῶν ἐφεξῆς ἀριθμῶν· διὸ καὶ ὁ μὲν μαθηματικὸς ἀριθμεῖται μετὰ τὸ ἓν δύο, πρὸς τῷ ἔμπροσθεν ἑνὶ ἄλλο ἕν, καὶ τὰ τρία πρὸς τοῖς δυσὶ τούτοις ἄλλο ἕν, καὶ ὁ λοιπὸς δὲ ὡσαύτως· οὗτος δὲ μετὰ τὸ ἓν δύο ἕτερα ἄνευ τοῦ ἑνὸς τοῦ πρώτου, καὶ ἡ τριὰς ἄνευ τῆς δυάδος, ὁμοίως δὲ καὶ ὁ ἄλλος ἀριθμός)· ἢ τὸν μὲν εἶναι τῶν ἀριθμῶν οἷος ὁ πρῶτος ἐλέχθη, τὸν δʼ οἷον οἱ μαθηματικοὶ λέγουσι, τρίτον δὲ τὸν ῥηθέντα τελευταῖον· ἔτι τούτους ἢ χωριστοὺς εἶναι τοὺς ἀριθμοὺς τῶν πραγμάτων,  ἢ οὐ χωριστοὺς ἀλλʼ ἐν τοῖς αἰσθητοῖς (οὐχ οὕτως δʼ ὡς τὸ πρῶτον ἐπεσκοποῦμεν, ἀλλʼ ὡς ἐκ τῶν ἀριθμῶν ἐνυπαρχόντων ὄντα τὰ αἰσθητά) ἢ τὸν μὲν αὐτῶν εἶναι τὸν δὲ μή, ἢ πάντας εἶναι.—οἱ μὲν οὖν τρόποι καθʼ οὓς ἐνδέχεται αὐτοὺς εἶναι οὗτοί εἰσιν ἐξ ἀνάγκης μόνοι, σχεδὸν δὲ καὶ οἱ λέγοντες τὸ ἓν ἀρχὴν εἶναι καὶ οὐσίαν καὶ στοιχεῖον πάντων, καὶ ἐκ τούτου","kind":"passage"},{"id":"RAW_DAZY_BO_5:P0029","text":"καὶ ἄλλου τινὸς εἶναι τὸν ἀριθμόν, ἕκαστος τούτων τινὰ τῶν τρόπων εἴρηκε, πλὴν τοῦ πάσας τὰς μονάδας εἶναι ἀσυμβλήτους. καὶ τοῦτο συμβέβηκεν εὐλόγως· οὐ γὰρ ἐνδέχεται ἔτι ἄλλον τρόπον εἶναι παρὰ τοὺς εἰρημένους. οἱ μὲν οὖν ἀμφοτέρους φασὶν εἶναι τοὺς ἀριθμούς, τὸν μὲν ἔχοντα τὸ πρότερον καὶ ὕστερον τὰς ἰδέας, τὸν δὲ μαθηματικὸν παρὰ τὰς ἰδέας καὶ τὰ αἰσθητά, καὶ χωριστοὺς ἀμφοτέρους τῶν αἰσθητῶν· οἱ δὲ τὸν μαθηματικὸν μόνον ἀριθμὸν εἶναι, τὸν πρῶτον τῶν ὄντων, κεχωρισμένον τῶν αἰσθητῶν. καὶ οἱ Πυθαγόρειοι δʼ ἕνα, τὸν μαθηματικόν, πλὴν οὐ κεχωρισμένον ἀλλʼ ἐκ τούτου τὰς αἰσθητὰς οὐσίας συνεστάναι φασίν· τὸν γὰρ ὅλον οὐρανὸν κατασκευάζουσιν ἐξ ἀριθμῶν, πλὴν οὐ μοναδικῶν, ἀλλὰ τὰς μονάδας  ὑπολαμβάνουσιν ἔχειν μέγεθος· ὅπως δὲ τὸ πρῶτον ἓν συνέστη ἔχον μέγεθος, ἀπορεῖν ἐοίκασιν. ἄλλος δέ τις τὸν πρῶτον ἀριθμὸν τὸν τῶν εἰδῶν ἕνα εἶναι, ἔνιοι δὲ καὶ τὸν μαθηματικὸν τὸν αὐτὸν τοῦτον εἶναι.","kind":"passage"},{"id":"RAW_DAZY_BO_5:P0030","text":"ὁμοίως δὲ καὶ περὶ τὰ μήκη καὶ περὶ τὰ ἐπίπεδα καὶ περὶ τὰ στερεά. οἱ μὲν γὰρ ἕτερα τὰ μαθηματικὰ καὶ τὰ μετὰ τὰς ἰδέας· τῶν δὲ ἄλλως λεγόντων οἱ μὲν τὰ μαθηματικὰ καὶ μαθηματικῶς λέγουσιν, ὅσοι μὴ ποιοῦσι τὰς ἰδέας ἀριθμοὺς μηδὲ εἶναί φασιν ἰδέας, οἱ δὲ τὰ μαθηματικά, οὐ μαθηματικῶς δέ· οὐ γὰρ τέμνεσθαι οὔτε μέγεθος πᾶν εἰς μεγέθη, οὔθʼ ὁποιασοῦν μονάδας δυάδα εἶναι. μοναδικοὺς δὲ τοὺς ἀριθμοὺς εἶναι πάντες τιθέασι, πλὴν τῶν Πυθαγορείων, ὅσοι τὸ ἓν στοιχεῖον καὶ ἀρχήν φασιν εἶναι τῶν ὄντων· ἐκεῖνοι δʼ ἔχοντας μέγεθος, καθάπερ εἴηρται πρότερον. ὁσαχῶς μὲν οὖν ἐνδέχεται λεχθῆναι περὶ αὐτῶν, καὶ ὅτι πάντες εἰσὶν εἰρημένοι οἱ τρόποι, φανερὸν ἐκ τούτων· ἔστι δὲ πάντα μὲν ἀδύνατα, μᾶλλον δʼ ἴσως θάτερα τῶν ἑτέρων.","kind":"passage"},{"id":"RAW_DAZY_BO_5:P0031","text":"πρῶτον μὲν οὖν σκεπτέον εἰ συμβληταὶ αἱ μονάδες ἢ ἀσύμβλητοι, καὶ εἰ ἀσύμβλητοι, ποτέρως ὧνπερ διείλομεν.  ἔστι μὲν γὰρ ὁποιανοῦν εἶναι ὁποιᾳοῦν μονάδι ἀσύμβλητον, ἔστι δὲ τὰς ἐν αὐτῇ τῇ δυάδι πρὸς τὰς ἐν αὐτῇ τῇ τριάδι, καὶ οὕτως δὴ ἀσυμβλήτους εἶναι τὰς ἐν ἑκάστῳ τῷ πρώτῳ ἀριθμῷ πρὸς ἀλλήλας.","kind":"passage"},{"id":"RAW_DAZY_BO_5:P0032","text":"εἰ μὲν οὖν πᾶσαι συμβληταὶ καὶ ἀδιάφοροι αἱ μονάδες, ὁ μαθηματικὸς γίγνεται ἀριθμὸς καὶ εἷς μόνος, καὶ τὰς ἰδέας οὐκ ἐνδέχεται εἶναι τοὺς ἀριθμούς (ποῖος γὰρ ἔσται ἀριθμὸς αὐτὸ ἄνθρωπος ἢ ζῷον ἢ ἄλλο ὁτιοῦν τῶν εἰδῶν; ἰδέα μὲν γὰρ μία ἑκάστου, οἷον αὐτοῦ ἀνθρώπου μία καὶ αὐτοῦ ζῴου ἄλλη μία· οἱ δʼ ὅμοιοι καὶ ἀδιάφοροι ἄπειροι, ὥστʼ οὐθὲν μᾶλλον ἥδε ἡ τριὰς αὐτοάνθρωπος ἢ ὁποιαοῦν), εἰ δὲ μὴ εἰσὶν ἀριθμοὶ αἱ ἰδέαι, οὐδʼ ὅλως οἷόν τε αὐτὰς εἶναι (ἐκ τίνων γὰρ ἔσονται ἀρχῶν αἱ ἰδέαι; ὁ γὰρ ἀριθμός ἐστιν ἐκ τοῦ ἑνὸς καὶ τῆς δυάδος τῆς ἀορίστου, καὶ αἱ ἀρχαὶ καὶ τὰ στοιχεῖα λέγονται τοῦ ἀριθμοῦ εἶναι, τάξαι τε οὔτε προτέρας ἐνδέχεται τῶν ἀριθμῶν αὐτὰς οὔθʼ ὑστέρας)· εἰ δʼ ἀσύμβλητοι αἱ μονάδες, καὶ οὕτως ἀσύμβλητοι ὥστε ἡτισοῦν ᾑτινιοῦν, οὔτε τὸν μαθηματικὸν ἐνδέχεται εἶναι τοῦτον τὸν ἀριθμόν (ὁ μὲν γὰρ μαθηματικὸς ἐξ ἀδιαφόρων, καὶ τὰ δεικνύμενα κατʼ αὐτοῦ ὡς ἐπὶ τοιούτου ἁρμόττει) οὔτε τὸν","kind":"passage"},{"id":"RAW_DAZY_BO_5:P0033","text":"τῶν εἰδῶν. οὐ γὰρ ἔσται ἡ δυὰς πρώτη ἐκ τοῦ ἑνὸς καὶ τῆς ἀορίστου δυάδος, ἔπειτα οἱ ἑξῆς ἀριθμοί, ὡς λέγεται δυάς, τριάς, τετράς—ἅμα γὰρ αἱ ἐν τῇ δυάδι τῇ πρώτῃ μονάδες γεννῶνται, εἴτε ὥσπερ ὁ πρῶτος εἰπὼν ἐξ ἀνίσων (ἰσασθέντων γὰρ ἐγένοντο) εἴτε ἄλλως—, ἐπεὶ εἰ ἔσται ἡ ἑτέρα μονὰς τῆς ἑτέρας προτέρα, καὶ τῆς δυάδος τῆς ἐκ τούτων ἔσται προτέρα· ὅταν γὰρ ᾖ τι τὸ μὲν πρότερον τὸ δὲ ὕστερον, καὶ τὸ ἐκ τούτων τοῦ μὲν ἔσται πρότερον τοῦ δʼ ὕστερον. ἔτι ἐπειδὴ ἔστι πρῶτον μὲν αὐτὸ τὸ ἕν, ἔπειτα τῶν ἄλλων ἔστι τι πρῶτον ἓν δεύτερον δὲ μετʼ ἐκεῖνο, καὶ πάλιν τρίτον τὸ δεύτερον μὲν μετὰ τὸ δεύτερον τρίτον δὲ μετὰ τὸ πρῶτον ἕν,—ὥστε πρότεραι ἂν εἶεν αἱ μονάδες ἢ οἱ ἀριθμοὶ ἐξ ὧν λέγονται, οἷον ἐν τῇ δυάδι τρίτη μονὰς ἔσται πρὶν τὰ τρία εἶναι, καὶ ἐν τῇ τριάδι τετάρτη καὶ  πέμπτη πρὶν τοὺς ἀριθμοὺς τούτους.","kind":"passage"},{"id":"RAW_DAZY_BO_5:P0034","text":"οὐδεὶς μὲν οὖν τὸν τρόπον τοῦτον εἴρηκεν αὐτῶν τὰς μονάδας ἀσυμβλήτους, ἔστι δὲ κατὰ μὲν τὰς ἐκείνων ἀρχὰς εὔλογον καὶ οὕτως, κατὰ μέντοι τὴν ἀλήθειαν ἀδύνατον.  τάς τε γὰρ μονάδας προτέρας καὶ ὑστέρας εἶναι εὔλογον, εἴπερ καὶ πρώτη τις ἔστι μονὰς καὶ ἓν πρῶτον, ὁμοίως δὲ καὶ δυάδας, εἴπερ καὶ δυὰς πρώτη ἔστιν· μετὰ γὰρ τὸ πρῶτον εὔλογον καὶ ἀναγκαῖον δεύτερόν τι εἶναι, καὶ εἰ δεύτερον, τρίτον, καὶ οὕτω δὴ τὰ ἄλλα ἐφεξῆς (ἅμα δʼ ἀμφότερα λέγειν, μονάδα τε μετὰ τὸ ἓν πρώτην εἶναι καὶ δευτέραν, καὶ δυάδα πρώτην, ἀδύνατον). οἱ δὲ ποιοῦσι μονάδα μὲν καὶ ἓν πρῶτον, δεύτερον δὲ καὶ τρίτον οὐκέτι, καὶ δυάδα πρώτην, δευτέραν δὲ καὶ τρίτην οὐκέτι. φανερὸν δὲ καὶ ὅτι οὐκ ἐνδέχεται, εἰ ἀσύμβλητοι πᾶσαι αἱ μονάδες, δυάδα εἶναι αὐτὴν καὶ τριάδα καὶ οὕτω τοὺς ἄλλους ἀριθμούς.","kind":"passage"},{"id":"RAW_DAZY_BO_5:P0035","text":"ἄν τε γὰρ ὦσιν ἀδιάφοροι αἱ μονάδες ἄν τε διαφέρουσαι ἑκάστη ἑκάστης, ἀνάγκη ἀριθμεῖσθαι τὸν ἀριθμὸν κατὰ πρόσθεσιν, οἷον τὴν δυάδα πρὸς τῷ ἑνὶ ἄλλου ἑνὸς προστεθέντος, καὶ τὴν τριάδα ἄλλου ἑνὸς πρὸς τοῖς δυσὶ προστεθέντος, καὶ τὴν τετράδα ὡσαύτως· τούτων δὲ ὄντων ἀδύνατον τὴν γένεσιν εἶναι τῶν ἀριθμῶν ὡς γεννῶσιν ἐκ τῆς δυάδος καὶ τοῦ ἑνός. μόριον γὰρ γίγνεται ἡ δυὰς τῆς τριάδος καὶ αὕτη τῆς τετράδος, τὸν αὐτὸν δὲ τρόπον συμβαίνει καὶ ἐπὶ τῶν ἐχομένων. ἀλλʼ ἐκ τῆς δυάδος τῆς πρώτης καὶ τῆς ἀορίστου δυάδος ἐγίγνετο ἡ τετράς, δύο δυάδες παρʼ αὐτὴν τὴν δυάδα· εἰ δὲ μή, μόριον ἔσται αὐτὴ ἡ δυάς, ἑτέρα δὲ προσέσται μία δυάς. καὶ ἡ δυὰς ἔσται ἐκ τοῦ ἑνὸς αὐτοῦ καὶ ἄλλου ἑνός· εἰ δὲ τοῦτο, οὐχ οἷόν τʼ εἶναι τὸ ἕτερον στοιχεῖον δυάδα ἀόριστον· μονάδα γὰρ μίαν γεννᾷ ἀλλʼ οὐ δυάδα ὡρισμένην.","kind":"passage"},{"id":"RAW_DAZY_BO_5:P0036","text":"ἔτι παρʼ αὐτὴν τὴν τριάδα καὶ αὐτὴν τὴν δυάδα πῶς ἔσονται ἄλλαι τριάδες καὶ δυάδες; καὶ τίνα τρόπον ἐκ προτέρων μονάδων καὶ ὑστέρων σύγκεινται; πάντα γὰρ ταῦτʼ  ἄτοπά ἐστι καὶ πλασματώδη, καὶ ἀδύνατον εἶναι πρώτην δυάδα, εἶτʼ αὐτὴν τριάδα. ἀνάγκη δʼ, ἐπείπερ ἔσται τὸ ἓν καὶ ἡ ἀόριστος δυὰς στοιχεῖα. εἰ δʼ ἀδύνατα τὰ συμβαίνοντα, καὶ τὰς ἀρχὰς εἶναι ταύτας ἀδύνατον.—εἰ μὲν οὖν διάφοροι αἱ μονάδες ὁποιαιοῦν ὁποιαισοῦν, ταῦτα καὶ τοιαῦθʼ ἕτερα συμβαίνει ἐξ ἀνάγκης· εἰ δʼ αἱ μὲν ἐν ἄλλῳ διάφοροι αἱ δʼ ἐν τῷ αὐτῷ ἀριθμῷ ἀδιάφοροι ἀλλήλαις μόναι, καὶ οὕτως οὐθὲν ἐλάττω συμβαίνει τὰ δυσχερῆ.  οἷον γὰρ ἐν τῇ δεκάδι αὐτῇ ἔνεισι δέκα μονάδες, σύγκειται δὲ καὶ ἐκ τούτων καὶ ἐκ δύο πεντάδων ἡ δεκάς. ἐπεὶ δʼ οὐχ ὁ τυχὼν ἀριθμὸς αὐτὴ ἡ δεκὰς οὐδὲ σύγκειται ἐκ τῶν τυχουσῶν πεντάδων, ὥσπερ οὐδὲ μονάδων, ἀνάγκη διαφέρειν τὰς μονάδας τὰς ἐν τῇ δεκάδι ταύτῃ.","kind":"passage"},{"id":"RAW_DAZY_BO_5:P0037","text":"ἂν γὰρ μὴ διαφέρωσιν, οὐδʼ αἱ πεντάδες διοίσουσιν ἐξ ὧν ἐστὶν ἡ δεκάς· ἐπεὶ δὲ διαφέρουσι, καὶ αἱ μονάδες διοίσουσιν. εἰ δὲ διαφέρουσι, πότερον οὐκ ἐνέσονται πεντάδες ἄλλαι ἀλλὰ μόνον αὗται αἱ δύο, ἢ ἔσονται; εἴτε δὲ μὴ ἐνέσονται, ἄτοπον· εἴτʼ ἐνέσονται, ποία ἔσται δεκὰς ἐξ ἐκείνων; οὐ γὰρ ἔστιν ἑτέρα δεκὰς ἐν τῇ δεκάδι παρʼ αὐτήν. ἀλλὰ μὴν καὶ ἀνάγκη γε μὴ ἐκ τῶν τυχουσῶν δυάδων τὴν τετράδα συγκεῖσθαι· ἡ γὰρ ἀόριστος δυάς, ὥς φασι, λαβοῦσα τὴν ὡρισμένην δυάδα δύο δυάδας ἐποίησεν· τοῦ γὰρ ληφθέντος ἦν δυοποιός.—ἔτι τὸ εἶναι παρὰ τὰς δύο μονάδας τὴν δυάδα φύσιν τινά, καὶ τὴν τριάδα παρὰ τὰς τρεῖς μονάδας, πῶς ἐνδέχεται; ἢ γὰρ μεθέξει θατέρου θατέρου, ὥσπερ λευκὸς ἄνθρωπος παρὰ λευκὸν καὶ ἄνθρωπον (μετέχει γὰρ τούτων), ἢ ὅταν ᾖ θατέρου θάτερον διαφορά τις, ὥσπερ ὁ ἄνθρωπος παρὰ ζῷον καὶ δίπουν.","kind":"passage"},{"id":"RAW_DAZY_BO_5:P0038","text":"ἔτι τὰ μὲν ἁφῇ ἐστὶν ἓν τὰ δὲ μίξει τὰ δὲ θέσει· ὧν οὐδὲν ἐνδέχεται ὑπάρχειν ταῖς μονάσιν ἐξ ὧν ἡ δυὰς καὶ ἡ τριάς· ἀλλʼ ὥσπερ οἱ δύο ἄνθρωποι οὐχ ἕν τι παρʼ ἀμφοτέρους, οὕτως ἀνάγκη καὶ τὰς μονάδας. καὶ οὐχ ὅτι ἀδιαίρετοι, διοίσουσι διὰ τοῦτο· καὶ γὰρ αἱ στιγμαὶ ἀδιαίρετοι, ἀλλʼ ὅμως παρὰ τὰς δύο οὐθὲν ἕτερον ἡ δυὰς αὐτῶν.—ἀλλὰ μὴν οὐδὲ τοῦτο δεῖ λανθάνειν, ὅτι συμβαίνει προτέρας καὶ ὑστέρας εἶναι δυάδας, ὁμοίως δὲ καὶ τοὺς ἄλλους ἀριθμούς. αἱ μὲν γὰρ ἐν τῇ τετράδι δυάδες ἔστωσαν ἀλλήλαις ἅμα· ἀλλʼ αὗται τῶν ἐν τῇ ὀκτάδι πρότεραί εἰσι, καὶ ἐγέννησαν, ὥσπερ ἡ δυὰς ταύτας, αὗται τὰς τετράδας τὰς ἐν τῇ ὀκτάδι αὐτῇ, ὥστε εἰ καὶ ἡ πρώτη δυὰς ἰδέα, καὶ αὗται ἰδέαι τινὲς ἔσονται.","kind":"passage"},{"id":"RAW_DAZY_BO_5:P0039","text":"ὁ δʼ αὐτὸς λόγος καὶ ἐπὶ τῶν μονάδων· αἱ γὰρ ἐν τῇ δυάδι τῇ πρώτῃ μονάδες γεννῶσι τὰς τέτταρας τὰς ἐν τῇ τετράδι, ὥστε πᾶσαι αἱ μονάδες ἰδέαι γίγνονται καὶ συγκείσεται ἰδέα ἐξ ἰδεῶν· ὥστε δῆλον ὅτι κἀκεῖνα ὧν ἰδέαι αὗται τυγχάνουσιν οὖσαι συγκείμενα ἔσται, οἷον εἰ τὰ ζῷα φαίη τις συγκεῖσθαι ἐκ ζῴων, εἰ τούτων ἰδέαι εἰσίν.  —ὅλως δὲ τὸ ποιεῖν τὰς μονάδας διαφόρους ὁπωσοῦν ἄτοπον καὶ πλασματῶδες (λέγω δὲ πλασματῶδες τὸ πρὸς ὑπόθεσιν βεβιασμένον)· οὔτε γὰρ κατὰ τὸ ποσὸν οὔτε κατὰ τὸ ποιὸν ὁρῶμεν διαφέρουσαν μονάδα μονάδος, ἀνάγκη τε ἢ ἴσον ἢ ἄνισον εἶναι ἀριθμόν, πάντα μὲν ἀλλὰ μάλιστα τὸν μοναδικόν, ὥστʼ εἰ μήτε πλείων μήτʼ ἐλάττων, ἴσος· τὰ δὲ ἴσα καὶ ὅλως ἀδιάφορα ταὐτὰ ὑπολαμβάνομεν ἐν τοῖς ἀριθμοῖς.","kind":"passage"},{"id":"RAW_DAZY_BO_5:P0040","text":"εἰ δὲ μή, οὐδʼ αἱ ἐν αὐτῇ τῇ δεκάδι δυάδες ἀδιάφοροι ἔσονται ἴσαι οὖσαι· τίνα γὰρ αἰτίαν ἕξει λέγειν ὁ φάσκων ἀδιαφόρους εἶναι; ἔτι εἰ ἅπασα μονὰς καὶ μονὰς ἄλλη δύο, ἡ ἐκ τῆς δυάδος αὐτῆς μονὰς καὶ ἡ ἐκ τῆς τριάδος αὐτῆς δυὰς ἔσται ἐκ διαφερουσῶν τε, καὶ πότερον προτέρα τῆς τριάδος ἢ ὑστέρα; μᾶλλον γὰρ ἔοικε προτέραν ἀναγκαῖον εἶναι· ἡ μὲν γὰρ ἅμα τῇ τριάδι ἡ δʼ ἅμα τῇ δυάδι τῶν μονάδων. καὶ ἡμεῖς μὲν ὑπολαμβάνομεν ὅλως ἓν καὶ ἕν, καὶ ἐὰν ᾖ ἴσα ἢ ἄνισα, δύο εἶναι, οἷον τὸ ἀγαθὸν καὶ τὸ κακόν, καὶ ἄνθρωπον καὶ ἵππον· οἱ δʼ οὕτως λέγοντες οὐδὲ τὰς μονάδας. εἴτε δὲ μὴ ἔστι πλείων ἀριθμὸς ὁ τῆς τριάδος αὐτῆς ἢ ὁ τῆς δυάδος, θαυμαστόν· εἴτε ἐστὶ πλείων, δῆλον ὅτι καὶ ἴσος ἔνεστι τῇ δυάδι, ὥστε οὗτος ἀδιάφορος αὐτῇ τῇ δυάδι. ἀλλʼ οὐκ ἐνδέχεται, εἰ πρῶτός τις ἔστιν ἀριθμὸς καὶ δεύτερος. οὐδὲ ἔσονται αἱ ἰδέαι ἀριθμοί.","kind":"passage"},{"id":"RAW_DAZY_BO_5:P0041","text":"τοῦτο μὲν γὰρ αὐτὸ ὀρθῶς λέγουσιν οἱ διαφόρους τὰς μονάδας ἀξιοῦντες εἶναι, εἴπερ ἰδέαι ἔσονται, ὥσπερ εἴρηται πρότερον· ἓν γὰρ τὸ εἶδος, αἱ δὲ μονάδες εἰ ἀδιάφοροι, καὶ αἱ δυάδες καὶ αἱ τριάδες ἔσονται ἀδιάφοροι. διὸ καὶ τὸ ἀριθμεῖσθαι οὕτως, ἓν δύο, μὴ προσλαμβανομένου πρὸς τῷ ὑπάρχοντι ἀναγκαῖον αὐτοῖς λέγειν (οὔτε γὰρ ἡ γένεσις ἔσται ἐκ τῆς ἀορίστου δυάδος, οὔτʼ ἰδέαν ἐνδέχεται εἶναι· ἐνυπάρξει γὰρ ἑτέρα ἰδέα ἐν ἑτέρᾳ, καὶ πάντα τὰ εἴδη ἑνὸς μέρη)· διὸ πρὸς μὲν τὴν ὑπόθεσιν ὀρθῶς λέγουσιν, ὅλως δʼ οὐκ ὀρθῶς· πολλὰ γὰρ ἀναιροῦσιν, ἐπεὶ τοῦτό γʼ αὐτὸ ἔχειν τινὰ φήσουσιν ἀπορίαν, πότερον, ὅταν ἀριθμῶμεν καὶ εἴπωμεν ἓν δύο τρία, προσλαμβάνοντες ἀριθμοῦμεν ἢ κατὰ μερίδας. ποιοῦμεν δὲ ἀμφοτέρως· διὸ γελοῖον ταύτην εἰς τηλικαύτην τῆς οὐσίας ἀνάγειν διαφοράν.","kind":"passage"},{"id":"RAW_DAZY_BO_5:P0042","text":"πάντων δὲ πρῶτον καλῶς ἔχει διορίσασθαι τίς ἀριθμοῦ διαφορά, καὶ μονάδος, εἰ ἔστιν. ἀνάγκη δʼ ἢ κατὰ τὸ ποσὸν ἢ κατὰ τὸ ποιὸν διαφέρειν· τούτων δʼ οὐδέτερον φαίνεται ἐνδέχεσθαι ὑπάρχειν. ἀλλʼ ᾗ ἀριθμός, κατὰ τὸ ποσόν. εἰ δὲ δὴ καὶ αἱ μονάδες τῷ ποσῷ διέφερον, κἂν ἀριθμὸς ἀριθμοῦ διέφερεν ὁ ἴσος τῷ πλήθει τῶν μονάδων. ἔτι πότερον αἱ πρῶται μείζους ἢ ἐλάττους, καὶ αἱ ὕστερον ἐπιδιδόασιν ἢ τοὐναντίον; πάντα γὰρ ταῦτα ἄλογα. ἀλλὰ μὴν οὐδὲ κατὰ τὸ ποιὸν διαφέρειν ἐνδέχεται. οὐθὲν γὰρ αὐταῖς οἷόν τε ὑπάρχειν πάθος· ὕστερον γὰρ καὶ τοῖς ἀριθμοῖς φασὶν ὑπάρχειν τὸ ποιὸν τοῦ ποσοῦ. ἔτι οὔτʼ ἂν ἀπὸ τοῦ ἑνὸς τοῦτʼ αὐταῖς γένοιτο οὔτʼ ἂν ἀπὸ τῆς δυάδος· τὸ μὲν γὰρ οὐ ποιὸν ἡ δὲ ποσοποιόν· τοῦ γὰρ πολλὰ τὰ ὄντα εἶναι αἰτία αὕτη ἡ φύσις.","kind":"passage"},{"id":"RAW_DAZY_BO_5:P0043","text":"εἰ δʼ ἄρα ἔχει πως ἄλλως, λεκτέον ἐν ἀρχῇ μάλιστα τοῦτο καὶ διοριστέον περὶ μονάδος διαφορᾶς, μάλιστα μὲν καὶ διότι ἀνάγκη ὑπάρχειν· εἰ δὲ μή, τίνα λέγουσιν;—ὅτι μὲν οὖν, εἴπερ εἰσὶν ἀριθμοὶ αἱ ἰδέαι, οὔτε συμβλητὰς τὰς μονάδας ἁπάσας ἐνδέχεται εἶναι, φανερόν, οὔτε ἀσυμβλήτους ἀλλήλαις οὐδέτερον τῶν τρόπων· ἀλλὰ μὴν οὐδʼ ὡς ἕτεροί τινες λέγουσι περὶ τῶν ἀριθμῶν λέγεται καλῶς. εἰσὶ δʼ οὗτοι ὅσοι ἰδέας μὲν οὐκ οἴονται εἶναι οὔτε ἁπλῶς οὔτε ὡς ἀριθμούς τινας οὔσας, τὰ δὲ μαθηματικὰ εἶναι καὶ τοὺς ἀριθμοὺς πρώτους τῶν ὄντων, καὶ ἀρχὴν αὐτῶν εἶναι αὐτὸ τὸ ἕν. ἄτοπον γὰρ τὸ ἓν μὲν εἶναί τι πρῶτον τῶν ἑνῶν, ὥσπερ ἐκεῖνοί φασι, δυάδα δὲ τῶν δυάδων μή, μηδὲ τριάδα τῶν τριάδων· τοῦ γὰρ αὐτοῦ λόγου πάντα ἐστίν.","kind":"passage"},{"id":"RAW_DAZY_BO_5:P0044","text":"εἰ μὲν οὖν οὕτως ἔχει τὰ περὶ τὸν ἀριθμὸν καὶ θήσει τις εἶναι τὸν μαθηματικὸν μόνον, οὐκ ἔστι τὸ ἓν ἀρχή (ἀνάγκη γὰρ διαφέρειν τὸ ἓν τὸ τοιοῦτο τῶν ἄλλων μονάδων· εἰ δὲ τοῦτο, καὶ δυάδα τινὰ πρώτην τῶν δυάδων, ὁμοίως δὲ καὶ τοὺς ἄλλους ἀριθμοὺς τοὺς ἐφεξῆς)· εἰ δέ ἐστι τὸ ἓν ἀρχή, ἀνάγκη μᾶλλον ὥσπερ Πλάτων ἔλεγεν ἔχειν τὰ περὶ τοὺς ἀριθμούς, καὶ εἶναι δυάδα πρώτην καὶ τριάδα, καὶ οὐ συμβλητοὺς εἶναι τοὺς ἀριθμοὺς πρὸς ἀλλήλους. ἂν δʼ αὖ πάλιν τις τιθῇ ταῦτα, εἴρηται ὅτι ἀδύνατα πολλὰ συμβαίνει. ἀλλὰ μὴν ἀνάγκη γε ἢ οὕτως ἢ ἐκείνως ἔχειν, ὥστʼ εἰ μηδετέρως, οὐκ ἂν ἐνδέχοιτο εἶναι τὸν ἀριθμὸν χωριστόν.  —φανερὸν δʼ ἐκ τούτων καὶ ὅτι χείριστα λέγεται ὁ τρίτος τρόπος, τὸ εἶναι τὸν αὐτὸν ἀριθμὸν τὸν τῶν εἰδῶν καὶ τὸν μαθηματικόν.","kind":"passage"},{"id":"RAW_DAZY_BO_5:P0045","text":"ἀνάγκη γὰρ εἰς μίαν δόξαν συμβαίνειν δύο ἁμαρτίας· οὔτε γὰρ μαθηματικὸν ἀριθμὸν ἐνδέχεται τοῦτον εἶναι τὸν τρόπον, ἀλλʼ ἰδίας ὑποθέσεις ὑποθέμενον ἀνάγκη μηκύνειν, ὅσα τε τοῖς ὡς εἴδη τὸν ἀριθμὸν λέγουσι συμβαίνει, καὶ ταῦτα ἀναγκαῖον λέγειν.—ὁ δὲ τῶν Πυθαγορείων τρόπος τῇ μὲν ἐλάττους ἔχει δυσχερείας τῶν πρότερον εἰρημένων, τῇ δὲ ἰδίας ἑτέρας. τὸ μὲν γὰρ μὴ χωριστὸν ποιεῖν τὸν ἀριθμὸν ἀφαιρεῖται πολλὰ τῶν ἀδυνάτων· τὸ δὲ τὰ σώματα ἐξ ἀριθμῶν εἶναι συγκείμενα, καὶ τὸν ἀριθμὸν τοῦτον εἶναι μαθηματικόν, ἀδύνατόν ἐστιν. οὔτε γὰρ ἄτομα μεγέθη λέγειν ἀληθές, εἴ θʼ ὅτι μάλιστα τοῦτον ἔχει τὸν τρόπον, οὐχ αἵ γε μονάδες μέγεθος ἔχουσιν· μέγεθος δὲ ἐξ ἀδιαιρέτων συγκεῖσθαι πῶς δυνατόν; ἀλλὰ μὴν ὅ γʼ ἀριθμητικὸς ἀριθμὸς μοναδικός ἐστιν.","kind":"passage"},{"id":"RAW_DAZY_BO_5:P0046","text":"ἐκεῖνοι δὲ τὸν ἀριθμὸν τὰ ὄντα λέγουσιν· τὰ γοῦν θεωρήματα προσάπτουσι τοῖς σώμασιν ὡς ἐξ ἐκείνων ὄντων τῶν ἀριθμῶν.—εἰ τοίνυν ἀνάγκη μέν, εἴπερ ἐστὶν ἀριθμὸς τῶν ὄντων τι καθʼ αὑτό, τούτων εἶναί τινα τῶν εἰρημένων τρόπων, οὐθένα δὲ τούτων ἐνδέχεται, φανερὸν ὡς οὐκ ἔστιν ἀριθμοῦ τις τοιαύτη φύσις οἵαν κατασκευάζουσιν οἱ χωριστὸν ποιοῦντες αὐτόν.—ἔτι πότερον ἑκάστη μονὰς ἐκ τοῦ μεγάλου καὶ μικροῦ ἰσασθέντων ἐστίν, ἢ ἡ μὲν ἐκ τοῦ μικροῦ ἡ δʼ ἐκ τοῦ μεγάλου; εἰ μὲν δὴ οὕτως, οὔτε ἐκ πάντων τῶν στοιχείων ἕκαστον οὔτε ἀδιάφοροι αἱ μονάδες (ἐν τῇ μὲν γὰρ τὸ μέγα ἐν τῇ δὲ τὸ μικρὸν ὑπάρχει, ἐναντίον τῇ φύσει ὄν)· ἔτι αἱ ἐν τῇ τριάδι αὐτῇ πῶς; μία γὰρ περιττή· ἀλλὰ διὰ τοῦτο ἴσως αὐτὸ τὸ ἓν ποιοῦσιν ἐν τῷ περιττῷ μέσον.","kind":"passage"},{"id":"RAW_DAZY_BO_5:P0047","text":"εἰ δʼ ἑκατέρα τῶν μονάδων ἐξ ἀμφοτέρων ἐστὶν ἰσασθέντων, ἡ δυὰς πῶς ἔσται μία τις οὖσα φύσις ἐκ τοῦ μεγάλου καὶ μικροῦ; ἢ τί διοίσει τῆς μονάδος; ἔτι προτέρα ἡ μονὰς τῆς δυάδος (ἀναιρουμένης γὰρ ἀναιρεῖται ἡ δυάς)· ἰδέαν οὖν ἰδέας ἀναγκαῖον αὐτὴν εἶναι, προτέραν γʼ οὖσαν ἰδέας, καὶ γεγονέναι προτέραν. ἐκ τίνος οὖν; ἡ γὰρ ἀόριστος δυὰς δυοποιὸς ἦν.—ἔτι ἀνάγκη ἤτοι ἄπειρον τὸν ἀριθμὸν εἶναι ἢ πεπερασμένον· χωριστὸν γὰρ ποιοῦσι τὸν ἀριθμόν, ὥστε οὐχ οἷόν τε μὴ οὐχὶ τούτων θάτερον ὑπάρχειν.","kind":"passage"},{"id":"RAW_DAZY_BO_5:P0048","text":"ὅτι μὲν τοίνυν ἄπειρον οὐκ ἐνδέχεται, δῆλον (οὔτε γὰρ περιττὸς ὁ ἄπειρός ἐστιν οὔτʼ ἄρτιος, ἡ δὲ γένεσις τῶν ἀριθμῶν ἢ περιττοῦ ἀριθμοῦ ἢ ἀρτίου ἀεί ἐστιν· ὡδὶ μὲν τοῦ ἑνὸς εἰς τὸν ἄρτιον πίπτοντος περιττός, ὡδὶ δὲ τῆς μὲν δυάδος ἐμπιπτούσης ὁ ἀφʼ ἑνὸς διπλασιαζόμενος, ὡδὶ δὲ τῶν περιττῶν ὁ ἄλλος ἄρτιος· ἔτι εἰ πᾶσα ἰδέα τινὸς οἱ δὲ ἀριθμοὶ ἰδέαι, καὶ ὁ ἄπειρος ἔσται ἰδέα τινός, ἢ τῶν αἰσθητῶν ἢ ἄλλου τινός· καίτοι οὔτε κατὰ τὴν θέσιν ἐνδέχεται οὔτε κατὰ λόγον, τάττουσί γʼ οὕτω τὰς ἰδέας)· εἰ δὲ πεπερασμένος, μέχρι πόσου; τοῦτο γὰρ δεῖ λέγεσθαι οὐ μόνον ὅτι ἀλλὰ καὶ διότι.","kind":"passage"},{"id":"RAW_DAZY_BO_5:P0049","text":"ἀλλὰ μὴν εἰ μέχρι τῆς δεκάδος ὁ ἀριθμός, ὥσπερ τινές φασιν, πρῶτον μὲν ταχὺ ἐπιλείψει τὰ εἴδη—οἷον εἰ ἔστιν ἡ τριὰς αὐτοάνθρωπος, τίς ἔσται ἀριθμὸς αὐτόιππος; αὐτὸ γὰρ ἕκαστος ἀριθμὸς μέχρι δεκάδος· ἀνάγκη δὴ τῶν ἐν τούτοις ἀριθμῶν τινὰ εἶναι (οὐσίαι γὰρ καὶ ἰδέαι οὗτοι)· ἀλλʼ ὅμως ἐπιλείψει (τὰ τοῦ ζῴου γὰρ εἴδη ὑπερέξει)—. ἅμα δὲ δῆλον ὅτι εἰ οὕτως ἡ τριὰς αὐτοάνθρωπος, καὶ αἱ ἄλλαι τριάδες (ὅμοιαι γὰρ αἱ ἐν τοῖς αὐτοῖς ἀριθμοῖς), ὥστʼ ἄπειροι ἔσονται ἄνθρωποι, εἰ μὲν ἰδέα ἑκάστη τριάς, αὐτὸ ἕκαστος ἄνθρωπος, εἰ δὲ μή, ἀλλʼ ἄνθρωποί γε. καὶ εἰ μέρος ὁ ἐλάττων τοῦ μείζονος, ὁ ἐκ τῶν συμβλητῶν μονάδων τῶν ἐν τῷ αὐτῷ ἀριθμῷ, εἰ δὴ ἡ τετρὰς αὐτὴ ἰδέα τινός ἐστιν, οἷον ἵππου ἢ λευκοῦ, ὁ ἄνθρωπος ἔσται μέρος ἵππου, εἰ δυὰς ὁ ἄνθρωπος. ἄτοπον δὲ καὶ τὸ τῆς μὲν δεκάδος εἶναι ἰδέαν ἑνδεκάδος δὲ μή, μηδὲ τῶν ἐχομένων ἀριθμῶν.","kind":"passage"},{"id":"RAW_DAZY_BO_5:P0050","text":"ἔτι δὲ καὶ ἔστι καὶ γίγνεται ἔνια καὶ ὧν εἴδη οὐκ ἔστιν, ὥστε διὰ τί οὐ κἀκείνων εἴδη ἔστιν; οὐκ ἄρα αἴτια τὰ εἴδη ἐστίν. ἔτι ἄτοπον εἰ ὁ ἀριθμὸς ὁ μέχρι τῆς δεκάδος μᾶλλόν τι ὂν καὶ εἶδος αὐτῆς τῆς δεκάδος, καίτοι τοῦ μὲν οὐκ ἔστι γένεσις ὡς ἑνός, τῆς δʼ ἔστιν. πειρῶνται δʼ ὡς τοῦ μέχρι τῆς δεκάδος τελείου ὄντος ἀριθμοῦ. γεννῶσι γοῦν τὰ ἑπόμενα, οἷον τὸ κενόν, ἀναλογίαν, τὸ περιττόν, τὰ ἄλλα τὰ τοιαῦτα, ἐντὸς τῆς δεκάδος· τὰ μὲν γὰρ ταῖς ἀρχαῖς ἀποδιδόασιν, οἷον κίνησιν στάσιν, ἀγαθὸν κακόν, τὰ δʼ ἄλλα τοῖς ἀριθμοῖς· διὸ τὸ ἓν τὸ περιττόν· εἰ γὰρ ἐν τῇ τριάδι, πῶς ἡ πεντὰς περιττόν; ἔτι τὰ μεγέθη καὶ ὅσα τοιαῦτα μέχρι ποσοῦ,  οἷον ἡ πρώτη γραμμή, ἡ ἄτομος, εἶτα δυάς, εἶτα καὶ ταῦτα μέχρι δεκάδος.—ἔτι εἰ ἔστι χωριστὸς ὁ ἀριθμός, ἀπορήσειεν ἄν τις πότερον πρότερον τὸ ἓν ἢ ἡ τριὰς καὶ ἡ δυάς.","kind":"passage"},{"id":"RAW_DAZY_BO_5:P0051","text":"ᾗ μὲν δὴ σύνθετος ὁ ἀριθμός, τὸ ἕν, ᾗ δὲ τὸ καθόλου πρότερον καὶ τὸ εἶδος, ὁ ἀριθμός· ἑκάστη γὰρ τῶν μονάδων μόριον τοῦ ἀριθμοῦ ὡς ὕλη, ὁ δʼ ὡς εἶδος. καὶ ἔστι μὲν ὡς ἡ ὀρθὴ προτέρα τῆς ὀξείας, ὅτι ὥρισται καὶ τῷ λόγῳ· ἔστι δʼ ὡς ἡ ὀξεῖα, ὅτι μέρος καὶ εἰς ταύτην διαιρεῖται. ὡς μὲν δὴ ὕλη ἡ ὀξεῖα καὶ τὸ στοιχεῖον καὶ ἡ μονὰς πρότερον, ὡς δὲ κατὰ τὸ εἶδος καὶ τὴν οὐσίαν τὴν κατὰ τὸν λόγον ἡ ὀρθὴ καὶ τὸ ὅλον τὸ ἐκ τῆς ὕλης καὶ τοῦ εἴδους· ἐγγύτερον γὰρ τοῦ εἴδους καὶ οὗ ὁ λόγος τὸ ἄμφω, γενέσει δʼ ὕστερον. πῶς οὖν ἀρχὴ τὸ ἕν; ὅτι οὐ διαιρετόν, φασίν· ἀλλʼ ἀδιαίρετον καὶ τὸ καθόλου καὶ τὸ ἐπὶ μέρους καὶ τὸ στοιχεῖον. ἀλλὰ τρόπον ἄλλον, τὸ μὲν κατὰ λόγον τὸ δὲ κατὰ χρόνον. ποτέρως οὖν τὸ ἓν ἀρχή; ὥσπερ γὰρ εἴρηται, καὶ ἡ ὀρθὴ τῆς ὀξείας καὶ αὕτη ἐκείνης δοκεῖ προτέρα εἶναι, καὶ ἑκατέρα μία. ἀμφοτέρως δὴ ποιοῦσι τὸ ἓν ἀρχήν.","kind":"passage"},{"id":"RAW_DAZY_BO_5:P0052","text":"ἔστι δὲ ἀδύνατον· τὸ μὲν γὰρ ὡς εἶδος καὶ ἡ οὐσία τὸ δʼ ὡς μέρος καὶ ὡς ὕλη. ἔστι γάρ πως ἓν ἑκάτερον—τῇ μὲν ἀληθείᾳ δυνάμει (εἴ γε ὁ ἀριθμὸς ἕν τι καὶ μὴ ὡς σωρὸς ἀλλʼ ἕτερος ἐξ ἑτέρων μονάδων, ὥσπερ φασίν), ἐντελεχείᾳ δʼ οὔ, ἔστι μονὰς ἑκατέρα· αἴτιον δὲ τῆς συμβαινούσης ἁμαρτίας ὅτι ἅμα ἐκ τῶν μαθημάτων ἐθήρευον καὶ ἐκ τῶν λόγων τῶν καθόλου, ὥστʼ ἐξ ἐκείνων μὲν ὡς στιγμὴν τὸ ἓν καὶ τὴν ἀρχὴν ἔθηκαν (ἡ γὰρ μονὰς στιγμὴ ἄθετός ἐστιν· καθάπερ οὖν καὶ ἕτεροί τινες ἐκ τοῦ ἐλαχίστου τὰ ὄντα συνετίθεσαν, καὶ οὗτοι, ὥστε γίγνεται ἡ μονὰς ὕλη τῶν ἀριθμῶν, καὶ ἅμα προτέρα τῆς δυάδος, πάλιν δʼ ὑστέρα ὡς ὅλου τινὸς καὶ ἑνὸς καὶ εἴδους τῆς δυάδος οὔσης)· διὰ δὲ τὸ καθόλου ζητεῖν τὸ κατηγορούμενον ἓν καὶ οὕτως ὡς μέρος ἔλεγον. ταῦτα δʼ ἅμα τῷ αὐτῷ ἀδύνατον ὑπάρχειν.","kind":"passage"},{"id":"RAW_DAZY_BO_5:P0053","text":"εἰ δὲ τὸ ἓν αὐτὸ δεῖ †μόνον ἄθετον† εἶναι (οὐθενὶ γὰρ διαφέρει ἢ ὅτι ἀρχή), καὶ ἡ μὲν δυὰς διαιρετὴ ἡ δὲ μονὰς οὔ, ὁμοιοτέρα ἂν εἴη τῷ ἑνὶ αὐτῷ ἡ μονάς. εἰ δʼ ἡ μονάς, κἀκεῖνο τῇ μονάδι ἢ τῇ δυάδι· ὥστε προτέρα ἂν εἴη ἑκατέρα ἡ μονὰς τῆς δυάδος. οὔ φασι δέ· γεννῶσι γοῦν τὴν δυάδα πρῶτον.  ἔτι εἰ ἔστιν ἡ δυὰς ἕν τι αὐτὴ καὶ ἡ τριὰς αὐτή, ἄμφω δυάς. ἐκ τίνος οὖν αὕτη ἡ δυάς;","kind":"passage"},{"id":"RAW_DAZY_BO_5:P0054","text":"ἀπορήσειε δʼ ἄν τις καὶ ἐπεὶ ἁφὴ μὲν οὐκ ἔστιν ἐν τοῖς ἀριθμοῖς, τὸ δʼ ἐφεξῆς, ὅσων μὴ ἔστι μεταξὺ μονάδων (οἷον τῶν ἐν τῇ δυάδι ἢ τῇ τριάδι), πότερον ἐφεξῆς τῷ ἑνὶ αὐτῷ ἢ οὔ, καὶ πότερον ἡ δυὰς προτέρα τῶν ἐφεξῆς ἢ τῶν μονάδων ὁποτεραοῦν.—ὁμοίως δὲ καὶ περὶ τῶν ὕστερον γενῶν τοῦ ἀριθμοῦ συμβαίνει τὰ δυσχερῆ, γραμμῆς τε καὶ ἐπιπέδου καὶ σώματος. οἱ μὲν γὰρ ἐκ τῶν εἰδῶν τοῦ μεγάλου καὶ τοῦ μικροῦ ποιοῦσιν, οἷον ἐκ μακροῦ μὲν καὶ βραχέος τὰ μήκη, πλατέος δὲ καὶ στενοῦ τὰ ἐπίπεδα, ἐκ βαθέος δὲ καὶ ταπεινοῦ τοὺς ὄγκους· ταῦτα δέ ἐστιν εἴδη τοῦ μεγάλου καὶ μικροῦ. τὴν δὲ κατὰ τὸ ἓν ἀρχὴν ἄλλοι ἄλλως τιθέασι τῶν τοιούτων. καὶ ἐν τούτοις δὲ μυρία φαίνεται τά τε ἀδύνατα καὶ τὰ πλασματώδη καὶ τὰ ὑπεναντία πᾶσι τοῖς εὐλόγοις.","kind":"passage"},{"id":"RAW_DAZY_BO_5:P0055","text":"ἀπολελυμένα τε γὰρ ἀλλήλων συμβαίνει, εἰ μὴ συνακολουθοῦσι καὶ αἱ ἀρχαὶ ὥστʼ εἶναι τὸ πλατὺ καὶ στενὸν καὶ μακρὸν καὶ βραχύ (εἰ δὲ τοῦτο, ἔσται τὸ ἐπίπεδον γραμμὴ καὶ τὸ στερεὸν ἐπίπεδον· ἔτι δὲ γωνίαι καὶ σχήματα καὶ τὰ τοιαῦτα πῶς ἀποδοθήσεται;), ταὐτό τε συμβαίνει τοῖς περὶ τὸν ἀριθμόν· ταῦτα γὰρ πάθη μεγέθους ἐστίν, ἀλλʼ οὐκ ἐκ τούτων τὸ μέγεθος, ὥσπερ οὐδʼ ἐξ εὐθέος καὶ καμπύλου τὸ μῆκος οὐδʼ ἐκ λείου καὶ τραχέος τὰ στερεά.—πάντων δὲ κοινὸν τούτων ὅπερ ἐπὶ τῶν εἰδῶν τῶν ὡς γένους συμβαίνει διαπορεῖν, ὅταν τις θῇ τὰ καθόλου, πότερον τὸ ζῷον αὐτὸ ἐν τῷ ζῴῳ ἢ ἕτερον αὐτοῦ ζῴου. τοῦτο γὰρ μὴ χωριστοῦ μὲν ὄντος οὐδεμίαν ποιήσει ἀπορίαν· χωριστοῦ δέ, ὥσπερ οἱ ταῦτα λέγοντές φασι, τοῦ ἑνὸς καὶ τῶν ἀριθμῶν οὐ ῥᾴδιον λῦσαι, εἰ μὴ ῥᾴδιον δεῖ λέγειν τὸ ἀδύνατον.","kind":"passage"},{"id":"RAW_DAZY_BO_5:P0056","text":"ὅταν γὰρ νοῇ τις ἐν τῇ δυάδι τὸ ἓν καὶ ὅλως ἐν ἀριθμῷ, πότερον αὐτὸ νοεῖ τι ἢ ἕτερον;—οἱ μὲν οὖν τὰ μεγέθη γεννῶσιν ἐκ τοιαύτης ὕλης, ἕτεροι δὲ ἐκ τῆς στιγμῆς (ἡ δὲ στιγμὴ αὐτοῖς δοκεῖ εἶναι οὐχ ἓν ἀλλʼ οἷον τὸ ἕν) καὶ ἄλλης ὕλης οἵας τὸ πλῆθος, ἀλλʼ οὐ πλήθους· περὶ ὧν οὐδὲν ἧττον συμβαίνει τὰ αὐτὰ ἀπορεῖν. εἰ μὲν γὰρ μία ἡ ὕλη, ταὐτὸ γραμμὴ καὶ ἐπίπεδον καὶ στερεόν (ἐκ γὰρ τῶν αὐτῶν τὸ αὐτὸ καὶ ἓν ἔσται)·  εἰ δὲ πλείους αἱ ὗλαι καὶ ἑτέρα μὲν γραμμῆς ἑτέρα δὲ τοῦ ἐπιπέδου καὶ ἄλλη τοῦ στερεοῦ, ἤτοι ἀκολουθοῦσιν ἀλλήλαις ἢ οὔ, ὥστε ταὐτὰ συμβήσεται καὶ οὕτως· ἢ γὰρ οὐχ ἕξει τὸ ἐπίπεδον γραμμὴν ἢ ἔσται γραμμή.—ἔτι πῶς μὲν ἐνδέχεται εἶναι ἐκ τοῦ ἑνὸς καὶ πλήθους τὸν ἀριθμὸν οὐθὲν ἐπιχειρεῖται· ὅπως δʼ οὖν λέγουσι ταὐτὰ συμβαίνει δυσχερῆ ἅπερ καὶ τοῖς ἐκ τοῦ ἑνὸς καὶ ἐκ τῆς δυάδος τῆς ἀορίστου.","kind":"passage"},{"id":"RAW_DAZY_BO_5:P0057","text":"ὁ μὲν γὰρ ἐκ τοῦ κατηγορουμένου καθόλου γεννᾷ τὸν ἀριθμὸν καὶ οὐ τινὸς πλήθους, ὁ δʼ ἐκ τινὸς πλήθους, τοῦ πρώτου δέ (τὴν γὰρ δυάδα πρῶτόν τι εἶναι πλῆθος), ὥστε διαφέρει οὐθὲν ὡς εἰπεῖν, ἀλλʼ αἱ ἀπορίαι αἱ αὐταὶ ἀκολουθήσουσι, μῖξις ἢ θέσις ἢ κρᾶσις ἢ γένεσις καὶ ὅσα ἄλλα τοιαῦτα. μάλιστα δʼ ἄν τις ἐπιζητήσειεν, εἰ μία ἑκάστη μονάς, ἐκ τίνος ἐστίν· οὐ γὰρ δὴ αὐτό γε τὸ ἓν ἑκάστη. ἀνάγκη δὴ ἐκ τοῦ ἑνὸς αὐτοῦ εἶναι καὶ πλήθους ἢ μορίου τοῦ πλήθους. τὸ μὲν οὖν πλῆθός τι εἶναι φάναι τὴν μονάδα ἀδύνατον, ἀδιαίρετόν γʼ οὖσαν· τὸ δʼ ἐκ μορίου ἄλλας ἔχει πολλὰς δυσχερείας· ἀδιαίρετόν τε γὰρ ἕκαστον ἀναγκαῖον εἶναι τῶν μορίων (ἢ πλῆθος εἶναι καὶ τὴν μονάδα διαιρετήν) καὶ μὴ στοιχεῖον εἶναι τὸ ἓν καὶ τὸ πλῆθος (ἡ γὰρ μονὰς ἑκάστη οὐκ ἐκ πλήθους καὶ ἑνός)· ἔτι οὐθὲν ἄλλο ποιεῖ ὁ τοῦτο λέγων ἀλλʼ ἢ ἀριθμὸν ἕτερον· τὸ γὰρ πλῆθος ἀδιαιρέτων ἐστὶν ἀριθμός.","kind":"passage"},{"id":"RAW_DAZY_BO_5:P0058","text":"ἔτι ζητητέον καὶ περὶ τοὺς οὕτω λέγοντας πότερον ἄπειρος ὁ ἀριθμὸς ἢ πεπερασμένος. ὑπῆρχε γάρ, ὡς ἔοικε, καὶ πεπερασμένον πλῆθος, ἐξ οὗ αἱ πεπερασμέναι μονάδες καὶ τοῦ ἑνός· ἔστι τε ἕτερον αὐτὸ πλῆθος καὶ πλῆθος ἄπειρον· ποῖον οὖν πλῆθος στοιχεῖόν ἐστι καὶ τὸ ἕν; ὁμοίως δὲ καὶ περὶ στιγμῆς ἄν τις ζητήσειε καὶ τοῦ στοιχείου ἐξ οὗ ποιοῦσι τὰ μεγέθη. οὐ γὰρ μία γε μόνον στιγμή ἐστιν αὕτη· τῶν γοῦν ἄλλων στιγμῶν ἑκάστη ἐκ τίνος; οὐ γὰρ δὴ ἔκ γε διαστήματός τινος καὶ αὐτῆς στιγμῆς. ἀλλὰ μὴν οὐδὲ μόρια ἀδιαίρετα ἐνδέχεται τοῦ διαστήματος εἶναι , ὥσπερ τοῦ πλήθους ἐξ ὧν αἱ μονάδες· ὁ μὲν γὰρ ἀριθμὸς ἐξ ἀδιαιρέτων σύγκειται τὰ δὲ μεγέθη οὔ.—πάντα δὴ ταῦτα καὶ ἄλλα τοιαῦτα φανερὸν ποιεῖ ὅτι ἀδύνατον εἶναι τὸν ἀριθμὸν καὶ τὰ μεγέθη χωριστά,  ἔτι δὲ τὸ διαφωνεῖν τοὺς τρόπους περὶ τῶν ἀριθμῶν σημεῖον ὅτι τὰ πράγματα αὐτὰ οὐκ ὄντα ἀληθῆ παρέχει τὴν ταραχὴν αὐτοῖς.","kind":"passage"},{"id":"RAW_DAZY_BO_5:P0059","text":"οἱ μὲν γὰρ τὰ μαθηματικὰ μόνον ποιοῦντες παρὰ τὰ αἰσθητά, ὁρῶντες τὴν περὶ τὰ εἴδη δυσχέρειαν καὶ πλάσιν, ἀπέστησαν ἀπὸ τοῦ εἰδητικοῦ ἀριθμοῦ καὶ τὸν μαθηματικὸν ἐποίησαν· οἱ δὲ τὰ εἴδη βουλόμενοι ἅμα καὶ ἀριθμοὺς ποιεῖν, οὐχ ὁρῶντες δέ, εἰ τὰς ἀρχάς τις ταύτας θήσεται, πῶς ἔσται ὁ μαθηματικὸς ἀριθμὸς παρὰ τὸν εἰδητικόν, τὸν αὐτὸν εἰδητικὸν καὶ μαθηματικὸν ἐποίησαν ἀριθμὸν τῷ λόγῳ, ἐπεὶ ἔργῳ γε ἀνῄρηται ὁ μαθηματικός (ἰδίας γὰρ καὶ οὐ μαθηματικὰς ὑποθέσεις λέγουσιν)· ὁ δὲ πρῶτος θέμενος τὰ εἴδη εἶναι καὶ ἀριθμοὺς τὰ εἴδη καὶ τὰ μαθηματικὰ εἶναι εὐλόγως ἐχώρισεν· ὥστε πάντας συμβαίνει κατὰ μέν τι λέγειν ὀρθῶς, ὅλως δʼ οὐκ ὀρθῶς. καὶ αὐτοὶ δὲ ὁμολογοῦσιν οὐ ταὐτὰ λέγοντες ἀλλὰ τὰ ἐναντία. αἴτιον δʼ ὅτι αἱ ὑποθέσεις καὶ αἱ ἀρχαὶ ψευδεῖς.","kind":"passage"},{"id":"RAW_DAZY_BO_5:P0060","text":"χαλεπὸν δʼ ἐκ μὴ καλῶς ἐχόντων λέγειν καλῶς, κατʼ Ἐπίχαρμον· ἀρτίως τε γὰρ λέλεκται, καὶ εὐθέως φαίνεται οὐ καλῶς ἔχον.—ἀλλὰ περὶ μὲν τῶν ἀριθμῶν ἱκανὰ τὰ διηπορημένα καὶ διωρισμένα (μᾶλλον γὰρ ἐκ πλειόνων ἂν ἔτι πεισθείη τις πεπεισμένος, πρὸς δὲ τὸ πεισθῆναι μὴ πεπεισμένος οὐθὲν μᾶλλον)· περὶ δὲ τῶν πρώτων ἀρχῶν καὶ τῶν πρώτων αἰτίων καὶ στοιχείων ὅσα μὲν λέγουσιν οἱ περὶ μόνης τῆς αἰσθητῆς οὐσίας διορίζοντες, τὰ μὲν ἐν τοῖς περὶ φύσεως εἴρηται, τὰ δʼ οὐκ ἔστι τῆς μεθόδου τῆς νῦν· ὅσα δὲ οἱ φάσκοντες εἶναι παρὰ τὰς αἰσθητὰς ἑτέρας οὐσίας, ἐχόμενόν ἐστι θεωρῆσαι τῶν εἰρημένων. ἐπεὶ οὖν λέγουσί τινες τοιαύτας εἶναι τὰς ἰδέας καὶ τοὺς ἀριθμούς, καὶ τὰ τούτων στοιχεῖα τῶν ὄντων εἶναι στοιχεῖα καὶ ἀρχάς, σκεπτέον περὶ τούτων τί λέγουσι καὶ πῶς λέγουσιν.","kind":"passage"},{"id":"RAW_DAZY_BO_5:P0061","text":"οἱ μὲν οὖν ἀριθμοὺς ποιοῦντες μόνον καὶ τούτους μαθηματικοὺς ὕστερον ἐπισκεπτέοι· τῶν δὲ τὰς ἰδέας λεγόντων ἅμα τόν τε τρόπον θεάσαιτʼ ἄν τις καὶ τὴν ἀπορίαν τὴν περὶ αὐτῶν. ἅμα γὰρ καθόλου τε  ποιοῦσι τὰς ἰδέας καὶ πάλιν ὡς χωριστὰς καὶ τῶν καθʼ ἕκαστον. ταῦτα δʼ ὅτι οὐκ ἐνδέχεται διηπόρηται πρότερον. αἴτιον δὲ τοῦ συνάψαι ταῦτα εἰς ταὐτὸν τοῖς λέγουσι τὰς οὐσίας καθόλου, ὅτι τοῖς αἰσθητοῖς οὐ τὰς αὐτὰς  ἐποίουν· τὰ μὲν οὖν ἐν τοῖς αἰσθητοῖς καθʼ ἕκαστα ῥεῖν ἐνόμιζον καὶ μένειν οὐθὲν αὐτῶν,  τὸ δὲ καθόλου παρὰ ταῦτα εἶναί τε καὶ ἕτερόν τι εἶναι. τοῦτο δʼ, ὥσπερ ἐν τοῖς ἔμπροσθεν  ἐλέγομεν, ἐκίνησε μὲν Σωκράτης διὰ τοὺς ὁρισμούς, οὐ μὴν ἐχώρισέ γε τῶν καθʼ ἕκαστον· καὶ τοῦτο ὀρθῶς ἐνόησεν οὐ χωρίσας. δηλοῖ δὲ ἐκ τῶν ἔργων· ἄνευ μὲν γὰρ τοῦ καθόλου οὐκ ἔστιν ἐπιστήμην λαβεῖν, τὸ δὲ χωρίζειν αἴτιον τῶν συμβαινόντων δυσχερῶν περὶ τὰς ἰδέας ἐστίν.","kind":"passage"},{"id":"RAW_DAZY_BO_5:P0062","text":"οἱ δʼ ὡς ἀναγκαῖον, εἴπερ ἔσονταί τινες οὐσίαι παρὰ τὰς αἰσθητὰς καὶ ῥεούσας, χωριστὰς εἶναι, ἄλλας μὲν οὐκ εἶχον ταύτας δὲ τὰς καθόλου λεγομένας ἐξέθεσαν, ὥστε συμβαίνειν σχεδὸν τὰς αὐτὰς φύσεις εἶναι τὰς καθόλου καὶ τὰς καθʼ ἕκαστον. αὕτη μὲν οὖν αὐτὴ καθʼ αὑτὴν εἴη τις ἂν δυσχέρεια τῶν εἰρημένων.","kind":"passage"},{"id":"RAW_DAZY_BO_5:P0063","text":"ὃ δὲ καὶ τοῖς λέγουσι τὰς ἰδέας ἔχει τινὰ ἀπορίαν καὶ τοῖς μὴ λέγουσιν, καὶ κατʼ ἀρχὰς ἐν τοῖς διαπορήμασιν ἐλέχθη πρότερον, λέγωμεν νῦν. εἰ μὲν γάρ τις μὴ θήσει τὰς οὐσίας εἶναι κεχωρισμένας, καὶ τὸν τρόπον τοῦτον ὡς λέγεται τὰ καθʼ ἕκαστα τῶν ὄντων, ἀναιρήσει τὴν οὐσίαν ὡς βουλόμεθα λέγειν· ἂν δέ τις θῇ τὰς οὐσίας χωριστάς, πῶς θήσει τὰ στοιχεῖα καὶ τὰς ἀρχὰς αὐτῶν; εἰ μὲν γὰρ καθʼ ἕκαστον καὶ μὴ καθόλου, τοσαῦτʼ ἔσται τὰ ὄντα ὅσαπερ τὰ στοιχεῖα, καὶ οὐκ ἐπιστητὰ τὰ στοιχεῖα (ἔστωσαν γὰρ αἱ μὲν ἐν τῇ φωνῇ συλλαβαὶ οὐσίαι τὰ δὲ στοιχεῖα αὐτῶν στοιχεῖα τῶν οὐσιῶν· ἀνάγκη δὴ τὸ ΒΑ ἓν εἶναι καὶ ἑκάστην τῶν συλλαβῶν μίαν, εἴπερ μὴ καθόλου καὶ τῷ εἴδει αἱ αὐταὶ ἀλλὰ μία ἑκάστη τῷ ἀριθμῷ καὶ τόδε τι καὶ μὴ ὁμώνυμον· ἔτι δʼ αὐτὸ ὃ ἔστιν ἓν ἕκαστον τιθέασιν· εἰ δʼ αἱ συλλαβαί, οὕτω καὶ ἐξ ὧν εἰσίν· οὐκ ἔσται ἄρα πλείω ἄλφα ἑνός, οὐδὲ τῶν ἄλλων στοιχείων οὐθὲν κατὰ τὸν αὐτὸν λόγον ὅνπερ οὐδὲ","kind":"passage"},{"id":"RAW_DAZY_BO_5:P0064","text":"τῶν  συλλαβῶν ἡ αὐτὴ ἄλλη καὶ ἄλλη· ἀλλὰ μὴν εἰ τοῦτο, οὐκ ἔσται παρὰ τὰ στοιχεῖα ἕτερα ὄντα, ἀλλὰ μόνον τὰ στοιχεῖα· ἔτι δὲ οὐδʼ ἐπιστητὰ τὰ στοιχεῖα· οὐ γὰρ καθόλου, ἡ δʼ ἐπιστήμη τῶν καθόλου· δῆλον δʼ ἐκ τῶν ἀποδείξεων καὶ τῶν ὁρισμῶν, οὐ γὰρ γίγνεται συλλογισμὸς ὅτι τόδε τὸ τρίγωνον δύο ὀρθαῖς, εἰ μὴ πᾶν τρίγωνον δύο ὀρθαί, οὐδʼ ὅτι ὁδὶ ὁ ἄνθρωπος ζῷον, εἰ μὴ πᾶς ἄνθρωπος ζῷον)·  ἀλλὰ μὴν εἴγε καθόλου αἱ ἀρχαί, ἢ καὶ αἱ ἐκ τούτων οὐσίαι καθόλου ἢ ἔσται μὴ οὐσία πρότερον οὐσίας· τὸ μὲν γὰρ καθόλου οὐκ οὐσία, τὸ δὲ στοιχεῖον καὶ ἡ ἀρχὴ καθόλου, πρότερον δὲ τὸ στοιχεῖον καὶ ἡ ἀρχὴ ὧν ἀρχὴ καὶ στοιχεῖόν ἐστιν.","kind":"passage"},{"id":"RAW_DAZY_BO_5:P0065","text":"ταῦτά τε δὴ πάντα συμβαίνει εὐλόγως, ὅταν ἐκ στοιχείων τε ποιῶσι τὰς ἰδέας καὶ παρὰ τὰς τὸ αὐτὸ εἶδος ἐχούσας οὐσίας  ἕν τι ἀξιῶσιν εἶναι καχωρισμένον· εἰ δὲ μηθὲν κωλύει ὥσπερ ἐπὶ τῶν τῆς φωνῆς στοιχείων πολλὰ εἶναι τὰ ἄλφα καὶ τὰ βῆτα καὶ μηθὲν εἶναι παρὰ τὰ πολλὰ αὐτὸ ἄλφα καὶ αὐτὸ βῆτα, ἔσονται ἕνεκά γε τούτου ἄπειροι αἱ ὅμοιαι συλλαβαί. τὸ δὲ τὴν ἐπιστήμην εἶναι καθόλου πᾶσαν, ὥστε ἀναγκαῖον εἶναι καὶ τὰς τῶν ὄντων ἀρχὰς καθόλου εἶναι καὶ μὴ οὐσίας κεχωρισμένας, ἔχει μὲν μάλιστʼ ἀπορίαν τῶν λεχθέντων, οὐ μὴν ἀλλὰ ἔστι μὲν ὡς ἀληθὲς τὸ λεγόμενον, ἔστι δʼ ὡς οὐκ ἀληθές. ἡ γὰρ ἐπιστήμη, ὥσπερ καὶ τὸ ἐπίστασθαι, διττόν, ὧν τὸ μὲν δυνάμει τὸ δὲ ἐνεργείᾳ.","kind":"passage"},{"id":"RAW_DAZY_BO_5:P0066","text":"ἡ μὲν οὖν δύναμις ὡς ὕλη  καθόλου οὖσα καὶ ἀόριστος τοῦ καθόλου καὶ ἀορίστου ἐστίν, ἡ δʼ ἐνέργεια ὡρισμένη καὶ ὡρισμένου, τόδε τι οὖσα τοῦδέ τινος, ἀλλὰ κατὰ συμβεβηκὸς ἡ ὄψις τὸ καθόλου χρῶμα ὁρᾷ ὅτι τόδε τὸ χρῶμα ὃ ὁρᾷ χρῶμά ἐστιν, καὶ ὃ θεωρεῖ ὁ γραμματικός, τόδε τὸ ἄλφα ἄλφα· ἐπεὶ εἰ ἀνάγκη τὰς ἀρχὰς καθόλου εἶναι, ἀνάγκη καὶ τὰ ἐκ τούτων καθόλου, ὥσπερ ἐπὶ τῶν ἀποδείξεων· εἰ δὲ τοῦτο, οὐκ ἔσται χωριστὸν οὐθὲν οὐδʼ οὐσία. ἀλλὰ δῆλον ὅτι ἔστι μὲν ὡς ἡ ἐπιστήμη καθόλου, ἔστι δʼ ὡς οὔ.","kind":"passage"}],"sha256":"40146869f3b5accf0c1980bd1fcd27bf11940c4ab58fca86de36a1fad46bee6b","language_code":"grc","language_name":"Ἑλληνική","translations":[{"id":"SOURCE_TR_20261015T174152394559Z_777f1dba","language_code":"en","language_name":"English","title":"Book 13","source_sha256":"40146869f3b5accf0c1980bd1fcd27bf11940c4ab58fca86de36a1fad46bee6b","sha256":"58ac05091f34e959a12e5e3eb77d869f788e2b4ffc0b19abff73b9b0d8a3fbbb","rendition_type":"machine","review_status":"machine","model":"gpt-5.6-terra","created":"2026-10-15T17:41:52.394577+00:00","paragraphs":[{"id":"RAW_DAZY_BO_5:P0001","text":"So much, then, has been said concerning the substance of sensible things—concerning matter in the inquiry of the physicists, and later concerning actuality. But since the investigation is whether there is some substance besides sensible substances, unmoved and eternal, or whether there is not, and, if there is, what it is, we must first examine what is said by others, in order that, if they say anything not rightly, we may not be liable to the same objections, and that, if there is any opinion common to us and them, we may not be aggrieved by this as peculiar to ourselves. For it is satisfactory if one should speak some things better and others no worse. There are two opinions concerning these matters: for they say that mathematical objects are certain substances, such as numbers and lines and things akin to these, and again the Ideas.","kind":"passage","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_DAZY_BO_5:P0001"]},{"id":"RAW_DAZY_BO_5:P0002","text":"And since some make these two kinds—the Ideas and mathematical numbers—while others make both one nature, and yet others say that mathematical objects alone are substances, we must first consider mathematical objects, without adding any other nature to them—for example, whether they happen to be Ideas or not, and whether they are principles and substances of beings or not—but only as mathematical objects: whether they are or are not, and, if they are, how they are. Then, after this, we must consider the Ideas themselves separately, simply and only for the sake of the customary procedure. For most of the points have been much talked about even in the exoteric discussions; moreover, most of the argument must be directed toward that inquiry when we examine whether the substances and principles of beings are numbers and Ideas. For after the Ideas this third inquiry remains.—Now, if mathematical objects exist, they must either be in sensible things, as some say, or separate from sensible things (and some also say this); or, if neither,","kind":"passage","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_DAZY_BO_5:P0002"]},{"id":"RAW_DAZY_BO_5:P0003","text":"they do not exist, or they exist in some other way. Thus our dispute will not be about whether they exist, but about the manner [of their existence].","kind":"passage","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_DAZY_BO_5:P0003"]},{"id":"RAW_DAZY_BO_5:P0004","text":"That it is impossible for them to be in sensible things, and that the account is at the same time fictitious, has been stated also among the aporiai: namely, that it is impossible for two solids to be in the same place at once, and further that by the same argument the other powers and natures too would be in sensible things, with none existing separately.—These points, then, have been stated before; but in addition to these it is clear that it is impossible for any body whatever to be divided. For it will be divided into a plane, and this into a line, and this into a point; so that, if it is impossible to divide the point, it is also impossible to divide the line, and if the latter, the other things also. What difference, then, is there between these being natures of such a kind, and their not being so themselves but having natures of such a kind in them? For the same consequence will result: when sensible things are divided, these too will be divided—or not even sensible things. Yet neither is it possible that natures of such a kind should exist separately.","kind":"passage","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_DAZY_BO_5:P0004"]},{"id":"RAW_DAZY_BO_5:P0005","text":"For if there are solids separate from sensible things, distinct from these and prior to sensible things, it is evident that there must also be, besides planes, other planes separate from them, and points and lines (for the same argument applies). But if these exist, then again, besides the planes and lines and points of the mathematical solid, there will be others separate from them. For the uncompounded are prior to the compounded; and if bodies not sensible are prior to sensible bodies, by the same argument the planes in unmoved solids will also exist in themselves, so that these planes and lines will be distinct from those existing together with the separate solids—for the latter exist together with mathematical solids, while the former are prior to mathematical solids. Again, then, these planes will have lines, prior to which there must, by the same argument, be other lines and points; and, besides the points in the prior lines, other prior points, before which there will no longer be others prior.","kind":"passage","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_DAZY_BO_5:P0005"]},{"id":"RAW_DAZY_BO_5:P0006","text":"And the accumulation becomes absurd. For it follows that there are solids in one set besides sensible things, planes in three sets besides sensible things—the planes besides sensible things, those in mathematical solids, and those besides the planes in these—lines in four sets, and points in five sets. About which of these, then, will the mathematical sciences be concerned? Surely not about the planes, lines, and points in the unmoved solid; for a science is always concerned with what is prior. The same argument applies also to numbers: for besides each set of points there will be other units, and besides each class of beings—first sensible, then intelligible—so that there will be kinds of mathematical numbers.","kind":"passage","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_DAZY_BO_5:P0006"]},{"id":"RAW_DAZY_BO_5:P0007","text":"Further, how is it possible to resolve the difficulties which we also raised among the aporiai? For the objects with which astronomy is concerned will likewise exist besides sensible things, as will those with which geometry is concerned. But how is it possible for a heaven and its parts to exist, or for anything else whatever that has motion? The same holds also of optics and harmonics: for there will be sound and sight besides sensible things and particulars; hence it is clear that there will be the other senses too and other sensible things. For why these rather than those? But if these exist, animals too will exist, if indeed senses do. Further, mathematicians also draw certain universal objects besides these substances. Thus there will be yet another substance, intermediate and separate both from the Ideas and from intermediates, which is neither number nor points nor magnitude nor time. But if this is impossible, it is evident that those things too cannot exist separately from sensible things.","kind":"passage","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_DAZY_BO_5:P0007"]},{"id":"RAW_DAZY_BO_5:P0008","text":"In general, moreover, the contrary of both what is true and what is customarily supposed follows if one posits that mathematical objects exist as certain separate natures. For, because they exist in this way, they must be prior to sensible magnitudes, whereas in truth they are posterior. For incomplete magnitude is prior in coming-to-be, but posterior in substance, as the inanimate is posterior to the animate. Further, by what and when will mathematical magnitudes be one? Things here are one through soul, or a part of soul, or something else, reasonably so—for otherwise they are many and fall apart—but in the case of those things, being divisible and quantitative, what is the cause of their being one and holding together? Further, the generations make this clear. For first there comes to be what is in length, then what is in breadth, and last what is in depth, and it has attained its end.","kind":"passage","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_DAZY_BO_5:P0008"]},{"id":"RAW_DAZY_BO_5:P0009","text":"If, then, what is posterior in coming-to-be is prior in substance, body would be prior to plane and length; and in this respect it is also more complete and whole, because it becomes animate. But how could a line or a plane be animate? For the assertion would be beyond our senses. Further, body is a certain substance—for it already in some way has completeness—but how are lines substances? For they are neither as form and some shape, as perhaps the soul is such a thing, nor as matter, as body is. For nothing seems able to be constituted out of lines or planes or points; but if they were some material substance, they would appear capable of undergoing this. Let them, then, be prior in account; but not all things prior in account are also prior in substance. For things are prior in substance when, being separate in being, they surpass; but they are prior in account when their accounts are constituents of the accounts [of other things]. These conditions do not belong together.","kind":"passage","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_DAZY_BO_5:P0009"]},{"id":"RAW_DAZY_BO_5:P0010","text":"For if attributes do not exist apart from substances, for example something moving or white, white is prior in account to the white man, but not in substance; for it cannot exist separately, but is always together with the composite whole (by composite whole I mean the white man). Hence it is clear that neither what results from abstraction is prior, nor what results from addition posterior; for it is by addition to white that the white man is called such.","kind":"passage","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_DAZY_BO_5:P0010"]},{"id":"RAW_DAZY_BO_5:P0011","text":"That mathematical objects are neither substances more than bodies nor prior in being to sensible things, but only prior in account, and that it is not possible for them to exist separately anywhere, has been sufficiently stated. But since neither could they exist in sensible things, it is clear that either they do not exist at all, or they exist in a certain way and therefore do not exist without qualification; for “to be” is spoken of in many ways.","kind":"passage","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_DAZY_BO_5:P0011"]},{"id":"RAW_DAZY_BO_5:P0012","text":"For just as universals in mathematics are not concerned with things separate besides magnitudes and numbers, but with these, though not insofar as they are such as to have magnitude or to be divisible, it is clear that there can also be accounts and demonstrations concerning sensible magnitudes, not insofar as they are sensible but insofar as they are of such a kind.","kind":"passage","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_DAZY_BO_5:P0012"]},{"id":"RAW_DAZY_BO_5:P0013","text":"For just as there are many accounts of things merely insofar as they are moved, apart from what each such thing is and from its attributes, and it is not necessary on that account either that there be some separate moving thing apart from sensibles or that there be in these some determinate nature, so too there will be accounts and sciences of moving things, not insofar as they are moving but insofar as they are bodies only, and again insofar as they are planes only, and insofar as they are lengths only, and insofar as they are divisible, and insofar as they are indivisible but have position, and insofar as they are indivisible only. Therefore, since it is simply true to say that not only separate things are, but also things not separate—for example, that moving things are—it is also simply true to say that mathematical objects are, and indeed are such as they say they are.","kind":"passage","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_DAZY_BO_5:P0013"]},{"id":"RAW_DAZY_BO_5:P0014","text":"And just as it is simply true to say that the other sciences are of that of which each is a science, not of its accident—for example, not of white, if the healthy is white, though it is the science of the healthy—but, if it is of the healthy insofar as healthy, of the healthy, and if of man insofar as man, of man; so too with geometry. If the things of which it is happen to be sensible, but it is not concerned with them insofar as sensible, the mathematical sciences will not be sciences of sensibles; yet neither will they be sciences of other separate things besides these. Many attributes belong to things in themselves insofar as each is one of such things; for there are proper attributes of an animal insofar as female and insofar as male, although there is no female or male separate from animals. Thus there are also attributes of things insofar as lengths only and insofar as planes.","kind":"passage","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_DAZY_BO_5:P0014"]},{"id":"RAW_DAZY_BO_5:P0015","text":"And the more a science is concerned with things prior in account and simpler, the more exactness it possesses (and this is simplicity). Thus it has more exactness without magnitude than with magnitude, and most of all without motion; and if with motion, most of all with the first motion, for that is simplest, and among these the uniform motion. The same argument applies to harmonics and optics: neither investigates insofar as it is sight or insofar as it is sound, but insofar as they are lines and numbers—though these are proper attributes of those. So too mechanics. Therefore, if someone, having posited things separately from their attributes, investigates something concerning them insofar as they are such, he will not on that account speak falsely, just as neither does one who draws on the ground and says that what is not a foot long is a foot long; for the falsehood is not in the premises. Each thing would best be investigated in this way, if one were to posit as separated what is not separate, as the arithmetician and geometer do. For man, insofar as man, is one and indivisible; but the arithmetician posited him as one indivisible thing, and then investigated whether anything belongs to man insofar as indivisible.","kind":"passage","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_DAZY_BO_5:P0015"]},{"id":"RAW_DAZY_BO_5:P0016","text":"The geometer, however, considers him neither insofar as man nor insofar as indivisible, but insofar as solid. For what would belong to him even if he were not in some respect indivisible, evidently can belong to him apart from these conditions too. Hence geometers speak correctly, and discourse about beings, and the things they discuss are beings. For being is twofold: in actuality and materially. Since the good and the beautiful are different—for the good is always in action, whereas the beautiful is also among unmoved things—those who say that the mathematical sciences say nothing about the beautiful or the good are mistaken. For they speak of them and demonstrate them most of all. Even if they do not name them, but demonstrate the works and accounts, they do not thereby fail to speak about them. The greatest forms of the beautiful are order, symmetry, and definiteness, which the mathematical sciences especially demonstrate. And since these—for example, order and definiteness—appear to be causes of many things, it is clear that they would in a certain way also speak of this sort of cause, namely the cause as beautiful.","kind":"passage","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_DAZY_BO_5:P0016"]},{"id":"RAW_DAZY_BO_5:P0017","text":"But we shall speak of these matters more intelligibly elsewhere.","kind":"passage","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_DAZY_BO_5:P0017"]},{"id":"RAW_DAZY_BO_5:P0018","text":"Let so much be said concerning mathematical objects: that they are beings, how they are beings, and how they are prior and how not prior. Concerning Ideas, however, we must first examine the doctrine concerning the Idea itself, connecting nothing with the nature of numbers, but considering it as those who first asserted the existence of Ideas supposed it from the beginning. The doctrine concerning Forms arose for those who stated it because they were persuaded by the Heraclitean arguments concerning truth, according to which all sensible things are always in flux; so that, if there is to be knowledge and understanding of anything, there must be certain other natures, abiding besides sensible things. For there is no knowledge of things in flux.","kind":"passage","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_DAZY_BO_5:P0018"]},{"id":"RAW_DAZY_BO_5:P0019","text":"But when Socrates was occupied with the ethical virtues and was the first to seek universal definitions concerning them (for Democritus touched only slightly upon natural things and in some way defined the hot and the cold; and the Pythagoreans before him dealt with a few things, the accounts of which they connected with numbers, such as what the opportune is, or the just, or marriage; but he reasonably sought the what-it-is. For he sought to reason syllogistically, and the what-it-is is the principle of syllogisms. For dialectical power had not yet then developed so as to be able, even without the what-it-is, to examine contraries and whether there is one and the same science of contraries. For there are two things which one might justly ascribe to Socrates: inductive arguments and universal definition; for both concern the principle of science)—Socrates himself, however, did not make universals or definitions separate; but they separated them, and called such beings Ideas, so that it followed for them, by almost the same argument, that there are Ideas of all","kind":"passage","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_DAZY_BO_5:P0019"]},{"id":"RAW_DAZY_BO_5:P0020","text":"things spoken of universally. This is like a person who, wishing to count, thought he would be unable to do so if there were fewer things, but, having made them more numerous, counted them. For the Forms are, so to speak, more numerous than the particular sensible things, from which, in seeking the causes of these, they proceeded to those. For there is something synonymous with each particular and besides substances, and there is one thing over many, both in the case of these things and in that of eternal things. Further, of the ways in which it is demonstrated that Forms exist, none appears to establish them. For from some of them no necessary syllogism results, while from others Forms result even of things of which they do not suppose there to be Forms. For according to the arguments from the sciences there will be Forms of all things of which there are sciences; and according to the one-over-many, there will be Forms also of negations; and according to the thinking of something after it has perished, there will be Forms of perishable things, since there is some phantasm of these.","kind":"passage","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_DAZY_BO_5:P0020"]},{"id":"RAW_DAZY_BO_5:P0021","text":"Further, among the more precise arguments, some make Ideas of relative things, though they deny that there is a genus of relative things in itself; while others state the Third Man. And in general the arguments concerning Forms do away with things which those who assert Forms wish more to exist than that Ideas should exist. For it follows that the dyad is not first, but number; and that the relative is prior to this, and this prior to what is in itself; and so with all the things in which some, following the opinions concerning Forms, have contradicted the principles.","kind":"passage","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_DAZY_BO_5:P0021"]},{"id":"RAW_DAZY_BO_5:P0022","text":"Further, according to the supposition on which they say that Ideas exist, there will be Forms not only of substances but also of many other things (for the concept is one not only in the case of substances but also in the case of non-substances, and there are sciences not only of substance; and there follow countless other such consequences). But according to necessity and their opinions concerning them, if Forms are participable, there must be Ideas only of substances. For things do not participate accidentally, but each must participate in that respect in which it is not predicated of a subject (I mean, for example, if something participates in the Double-itself, it also participates in the eternal, but accidentally; for it happens to the double to be eternal). Thus the Forms will be substance; and the same things signify substance here and there. Or what will it mean to say that something exists besides these, the one over many? And if the Ideas and the participants have the same Form, there will be something common; for why, more in the case of perishable dyads and of the many eternal dyads, is the Dyad one and","kind":"passage","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_DAZY_BO_5:P0022"]},{"id":"RAW_DAZY_BO_5:P0023","text":"the same, or upon itself and the particular thing?) But if it is not the same Form, they would be homonymous, and it would be like calling both Callias and a piece of wood “man,” without having observed any community between them. But if we are to posit that the other elements of the common accounts apply to the Forms—for example, “plane figure” to the circle itself, and the remaining parts of the account—while the “what it is” is added, one must consider whether this is not wholly empty. For to what is it to be added? To the middle, or to the plane, or to all? For all the things in the substance are Forms, for example animal and two-footed. Further, it is clear that it must itself be something, like plane, a certain nature which will inhere in all the Forms as a genus.","kind":"passage","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_DAZY_BO_5:P0023"]},{"id":"RAW_DAZY_BO_5:P0024","text":"But above all one might be perplexed as to what contribution the Forms make, either to the eternal sensibles or to those that come to be and perish. For they are causes neither of motion nor of any change for them. Nor indeed do they provide any help toward knowledge of the other things (for those things are not the substance of these; for then they would be in them), nor toward their being, since they do not inhere in the things which participate in them. In that case they might perhaps seem to be causes, as the white when mixed with the white; but this argument is very easily moved, which Anaxagoras first and Eudoxus later stated in raising difficulties, and certain others too (for it is easy to assemble many impossibilities against such an opinion). But neither are the other things from the Forms in any of the customary senses of “from.” And to say that they are paradigms and that the other things participate in them is empty talk and poetic metaphor.","kind":"passage","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_DAZY_BO_5:P0024"]},{"id":"RAW_DAZY_BO_5:P0025","text":"For what is it that acts while looking to the Ideas? And anything whatever can both be and come to be without being assimilated [to them], so that, whether Socrates exists or does not exist, there might come to be such a man as Socrates; and similarly, clearly, even if Socrates were eternal. There will also be several paradigms of the same thing, and hence also Forms: for example, of man, animal and two-footed, and at the same time Man-itself. Further, the Forms are paradigms not only of sensibles but also of themselves, for example the genus is a paradigm of the Forms as species of a genus; hence the same thing will be paradigm and image.","kind":"passage","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_DAZY_BO_5:P0025"]},{"id":"RAW_DAZY_BO_5:P0026","text":"Further, it might seem impossible for the substance and that of which it is the substance to be separate; how then could the Ideas, being substances of things, be separate? And in the Phaedo it is said in this way, that the Forms are causes both of being and of coming to be. Yet, although the Forms exist, things would not come to be unless there were that which moves; and many other things come to be, such as a house and a ring, of which they say there are no Forms. Hence it is clear that those things too of which they say there are Ideas can both be and come to be through causes such as those now mentioned, and not through the Forms. But concerning the Ideas, both in this manner and through arguments more logical and more exact, it is possible to collect many points similar to those considered.","kind":"passage","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_DAZY_BO_5:P0026"]},{"id":"RAW_DAZY_BO_5:P0027","text":"Now that these matters have been distinguished, it is well to consider again the consequences concerning numbers for those who say that they are separate substances and first causes of beings. It is necessary, if number is some nature and its substance is nothing other than this itself, as some say, that either one part of it is first and another consequent, each differing in Form—and this either belongs immediately to the units, so that any unit is non-combinable with any unit, or all are immediately consecutive and any are combinable with any, as they say mathematical number is (for in mathematical number no unit differs at all from another unit); or some are combinable and some are not (for example, if after the one the dyad is first, then the triad, and so the rest of number, while the units in each number are combinable with one another—for example, those in the first dyad with one another, and those in the first triad with one another, and likewise in the other numbers; but those in the","kind":"passage","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_DAZY_BO_5:P0027"]},{"id":"RAW_DAZY_BO_5:P0028","text":"dyad itself are non-combinable with those in the triad itself, and similarly in the other consecutive numbers. Hence mathematical number is counted after one as two, another one added to the preceding one, and three as another one added to these two, and the rest likewise; but this [number is counted] after one as two others, without the first one, and the triad without the dyad, and likewise the other number); or one kind of number is as was first described, another as mathematicians speak of, and a third as was mentioned last. Further, these numbers are either separate from things, or not separate but in sensibles (not in the way we first examined, but in the sense that sensibles are constituted from numbers inherent in them), or one kind of them is separate and another is not, or all are. These, then, are necessarily the only ways in which they can be; and nearly all who say that the one is principle and substance and element of all things, and that number is from this","kind":"passage","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_DAZY_BO_5:P0028"]},{"id":"RAW_DAZY_BO_5:P0029","text":"and some other thing, have stated some one of these ways, except that all units are non-combinable. And this has occurred reasonably; for it is not possible that there should be another way besides those stated. Some say that both kinds of number exist: the Ideas, which have prior and posterior, and mathematical number besides the Ideas and sensibles, both being separate from sensibles. Others say that mathematical number alone exists, the first of beings, separate from sensibles. And the Pythagoreans too [say there is] one number, the mathematical, except not separate; rather, they say that sensible substances are constituted from it. For they construct the whole heaven out of numbers, though not unit-numbers, but they suppose the units to have magnitude; but how the first one, having magnitude, was constituted, they seem unable to explain. Another says that the first number, that of the Forms, is one; and some say that mathematical number is this same one.","kind":"passage","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_DAZY_BO_5:P0029"]},{"id":"RAW_DAZY_BO_5:P0030","text":"Likewise also concerning lengths, planes, and solids. For some make mathematical objects distinct from those after the Ideas; while among those who speak otherwise, some speak of mathematical objects mathematically—namely, all who do not make the Ideas numbers or say that there are Ideas—while others speak of mathematical objects, but not mathematically. For they deny that every magnitude is divisible into magnitudes, or that any two units are a dyad. All posit numbers to be unit-numbers, except the Pythagoreans, who say that the one is an element and principle of beings; they, however, posit numbers as having magnitude, as was said before. Thus, in how many ways it is possible to speak about them, and that all the ways have been stated, is clear from these considerations. But all are impossible, though perhaps some more than others.","kind":"passage","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_DAZY_BO_5:P0030"]},{"id":"RAW_DAZY_BO_5:P0031","text":"First, then, one must examine whether units are combinable or non-combinable, and, if non-combinable, in which of the ways that we distinguished. For any unit may be non-combinable with any unit; or the units in the dyad itself may be non-combinable with those in the triad itself, and in this way the units in each first number may be non-combinable with one another.","kind":"passage","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_DAZY_BO_5:P0031"]},{"id":"RAW_DAZY_BO_5:P0032","text":"If, then, all units are combinable and undifferentiated, mathematical number results, and one number only; and it is not possible for the Ideas to be numbers (for what number will Man-itself, or Animal-itself, or any other of the Forms be? There is one Idea of each, for example one of Man-itself and another one of Animal-itself; but the similar and undifferentiated [numbers] are infinite, so that this triad is no more Man-itself than any other). But if the Ideas are not numbers, it is not possible for them to exist at all (for from what principles will the Ideas be? For number is from the one and the indefinite dyad, and the principles and elements are said to be those of number; and it is impossible to rank the Ideas either before numbers or after them). But if the units are non-combinable, and non-combinable in such a way that any is non-combinable with any, this number can be neither mathematical (for mathematical number consists of undifferentiated units, and the demonstrations concerning it apply to it as such) nor the number","kind":"passage","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_DAZY_BO_5:P0032"]},{"id":"RAW_DAZY_BO_5:P0033","text":"of the Forms. For the first dyad will not be from the one and the indefinite dyad, and then the successive numbers, as they are called dyad, triad, tetrad—for the units in the first dyad come to be simultaneously, whether, as the first speaker said, from unequals (for they came to be when these were equalized), or otherwise. For if one unit is prior to another, it will also be prior to the dyad composed of these; for whenever one thing is prior and another posterior, that which is composed of them will be prior to the one and posterior to the other. Further, since first there is the one-itself, then among the others there is some first one, second after that, and again a third, second after the second but third after the first one—thus the units would be prior to the numbers from which they are said to be [derived]; for example, in the dyad there will be a third unit before three exists, and in the triad a fourth and a fifth before these numbers [exist].","kind":"passage","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_DAZY_BO_5:P0033"]},{"id":"RAW_DAZY_BO_5:P0034","text":"None of them, then, has said that the units are non-combinable in this way; yet according to their principles it is reasonable even so, though according to the truth it is impossible. For it is reasonable that there should be prior and posterior units, if indeed there is some first unit and first one; and likewise dyads, if indeed there is a first dyad. For after the first it is reasonable and necessary that there be some second, and if second, third, and then the rest successively (but to say both at once, that a unit after the one is first and second, and that there is a first dyad, is impossible). Yet they make a first unit and one, but no longer a second and third, and a first dyad, but no longer a second and third. It is also clear that, if all units are non-combinable, it is impossible for there to be a dyad itself and a triad itself, and thus the other numbers.","kind":"passage","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_DAZY_BO_5:P0034"]},{"id":"RAW_DAZY_BO_5:P0035","text":"For whether the units are undifferentiated or each differs from each, number must be counted by addition: for example, the dyad by another one being added to the one, the triad by another one being added to the two, and the tetrad likewise. But if this is so, it is impossible for the generation of numbers to be as they generate them from the dyad and the one. For the dyad becomes a part of the triad, and this a part of the tetrad, and the same follows in the succeeding cases. But the tetrad was generated from the first dyad and the indefinite dyad, as two dyads apart from the dyad itself; otherwise the dyad itself will be a part, and one other dyad will be added. And the dyad will be from the one-itself and another one; but if this is so, the other element cannot be an indefinite dyad. For it generates one unit, not a determinate dyad.","kind":"passage","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_DAZY_BO_5:P0035"]},{"id":"RAW_DAZY_BO_5:P0036","text":"Further, besides the triad itself and the dyad itself, how will there be other triads and dyads? And in what way are they composed of prior and posterior units? For all this is absurd and fabricated, and it is impossible that there should be a first dyad and then a triad itself. Yet this must be so, if the one and the indefinite dyad are to be elements. But if the consequences are impossible, it is impossible for these to be the principles. If, then, units of any sort differ from units of any sort, these and other such consequences necessarily follow. But if those in one number differ from those in another, while those in the same number alone are undifferentiated from one another, even so the difficulties are no less.\n\nFor example, ten units are present in the decad itself, and the decad is composed both from these and from two pentads. But since the decad itself is not just any number, nor composed of just any pentads, any more than of just any units, the units in this decad must differ.","kind":"passage","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_DAZY_BO_5:P0036"]},{"id":"RAW_DAZY_BO_5:P0037","text":"For if they do not differ, neither will the pentads from which the decad consists differ; but if the pentads differ, the units will differ too. But if they differ, will there be no other pentads in it, but only these two, or will there be? If there will not be, it is absurd; but if there will be, what decad will be composed of those? For there is no other decad in the decad besides itself. But further, the tetrad must not be composed of just any dyads. For the indefinite dyad, as they say, having received the determinate dyad, made two dyads; for it was productive of two from what was received. Further, how can the dyad be a certain nature besides the two units, and the triad besides the three units? Either one will participate in the other, as a white man is besides white and man (for he participates in these), or [this will occur] whenever one is a difference of the other, as man is besides animal and two-footed.","kind":"passage","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_DAZY_BO_5:P0037"]},{"id":"RAW_DAZY_BO_5:P0038","text":"Further, some things are one by contact, others by mixture, others by position; none of these can belong to the units from which the dyad and triad consist. Rather, just as two men are not some one thing besides both, so necessarily also with the units. And they will not differ on account of being indivisible; for points too are indivisible, but nevertheless their dyad is nothing other besides the two points. But this too must not escape notice: it follows that there are prior and posterior dyads, and likewise other numbers. For let the dyads in the tetrad be simultaneous with one another; yet these are prior to those in the octad, and, as the dyad generated these, they generated the tetrads in the octad itself; so that, if the first dyad is an Idea, these too will be certain Ideas.","kind":"passage","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_DAZY_BO_5:P0038"]},{"id":"RAW_DAZY_BO_5:P0039","text":"The same argument also applies to the units. For the units in the first dyad generate the four in the tetrad, so that all units become Ideas, and an Idea will be composed of Ideas. Hence it is clear that those things of which these happen to be Ideas will also be composite, as if someone were to say that animals are composed of animals, if these are their Ideas.\n\n—In general, to make units different in any way is absurd and fabricated (by “fabricated” I mean what is forced to fit a hypothesis). For we see no unit differing from unit either in quantity or in quality; and a number, all numbers but especially a unit-number, must be either equal or unequal. Therefore, if it is neither greater nor less, it is equal; and we take equal things, and in general undifferentiated things, to be the same in the case of numbers.","kind":"passage","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_DAZY_BO_5:P0039"]},{"id":"RAW_DAZY_BO_5:P0040","text":"But if not, not even the dyads in the decad itself will be undifferentiated although equal; for what reason will he who asserts that they are undifferentiated have to say so? Further, if every unit and another unit are two, the unit from the dyad itself and the two from the triad itself will be from different units; and will they be prior to the triad or posterior? For they seem necessarily to be rather prior: the one is simultaneous with the triad, the other with the dyad of units. And we suppose that, in general, one and one, whether equal or unequal, are two—for example, good and bad, man and horse; but those who speak in this way do not [so suppose] even of units. But if the number of the triad itself is not greater than that of the dyad itself, it is strange; while if it is greater, it is clear that there is also an equal [number] in the dyad, so that this is undifferentiated from the dyad itself. But this is impossible if there is some first number and a second. Nor will the Ideas be numbers.","kind":"passage","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_DAZY_BO_5:P0040"]},{"id":"RAW_DAZY_BO_5:P0041","text":"For those who maintain that the units must be different are correct in this very point, if there are to be Ideas, as was said before; for the form is one, whereas if the units are undifferentiated, then the twos and threes too will be undifferentiated. Hence they must say that counting in this way, one, two, does not involve taking in addition to what is present (for neither will generation be from the indefinite dyad, nor can there be an Idea; for another Idea will inhere in another, and all the forms will be parts of one); hence, with reference to the hypothesis, they speak correctly, but absolutely not correctly. For they do away with many things, since they will say that this very question presents some difficulty: when we count and say one, two, three, are we counting by taking in addition or by parts? Yet we do both; and so it is absurd to reduce this to so great a difference of substance.","kind":"passage","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_DAZY_BO_5:P0041"]},{"id":"RAW_DAZY_BO_5:P0042","text":"But first of all, it is well to determine what difference there is, if any, between number and unit. They must differ either in quantity or in quality; but neither of these seems possible. Yet, qua number, they differ in quantity. But if the units too differed in quantity, then number would differ from number even when equal in the multitude of units. Again, are the first greater or smaller, and do those that come later increase, or the contrary? For all these things are irrational. But neither can they differ in quality. For no affection can belong to them; indeed, they say that quality belongs later even to numbers than quantity. Again, this could arise for them neither from the one nor from the dyad: for the former is not qualitative, while the latter makes quantitative; for this nature is the cause of beings being many.","kind":"passage","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_DAZY_BO_5:P0042"]},{"id":"RAW_DAZY_BO_5:P0043","text":"But if it is somehow otherwise, this above all must be stated at the outset and determined concerning the difference of the unit—above all, why it must belong; and if not, what do they say?—That, if the Ideas are numbers, all the units cannot be commensurable, nor can they be incommensurable with one another in either of the two ways, is clear. But neither is what certain others say concerning numbers correctly stated. These are those who think that Ideas do not exist, either simply or as certain numbers, but that mathematical objects exist and that numbers are the first of beings, and that the principle of them is the one itself. For it is absurd that the one should be some first among ones, as they say, but that there should not be a dyad among dyads, nor a triad among triads; for the same account applies to all.","kind":"passage","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_DAZY_BO_5:P0043"]},{"id":"RAW_DAZY_BO_5:P0044","text":"If, then, matters concerning number are thus, and one posits that only mathematical number exists, the one is not a principle (for such a one must differ from the other units; and if this is so, there must likewise be some first dyad among dyads, and similarly the other successive numbers). But if the one is a principle, matters concerning numbers must rather be as Plato said, and there must be a first dyad and triad, and numbers must not be commensurable with one another. But if, again, someone posits these things, it has been said that many impossibilities result. Yet necessarily matters must be either thus or thus; so that, if neither, number could not exist separately.\n\n—From these considerations it is also clear that the third way, namely that the number of Forms and mathematical number are the same number, is the worst stated.","kind":"passage","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_DAZY_BO_5:P0044"]},{"id":"RAW_DAZY_BO_5:P0045","text":"For two errors must converge in one opinion. For number cannot be mathematical in this way; rather, one must extend it by positing special hypotheses, and must say all the things that result for those who say that number consists of Forms. The Pythagorean way, on the one hand, has fewer difficulties than those previously mentioned, but on the other hand has other difficulties peculiar to itself. For not making number separate removes many impossibilities; but that bodies are composed of numbers, and that this number is mathematical, is impossible. For it is not true to speak of indivisible magnitudes; and even if this should be so in the highest degree, at least units do not have magnitude. But how can magnitude be composed of indivisibles? Yet arithmetical number consists of units.","kind":"passage","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_DAZY_BO_5:P0045"]},{"id":"RAW_DAZY_BO_5:P0046","text":"But they say that beings are number; at any rate, they attach the theorems to bodies as though they were constituted from those numbers.—If, then, it is necessary, if number is some being in itself, that it be one of the ways mentioned, but none of these is possible, it is clear that number does not have such a nature as those who make it separate construct for it.—Again, is each unit composed of the equalized great and small, or one from the small and another from the great? If so, neither is each thing from all the elements nor are the units undifferentiated (for in one the great and in the other the small is present, which are contrary in nature). Again, how are the units in the triad itself? For one is odd. But perhaps for this reason they make the one itself intermediate in the odd.","kind":"passage","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_DAZY_BO_5:P0046"]},{"id":"RAW_DAZY_BO_5:P0047","text":"But if each of the units is from both, once equalized, how will the dyad, being some one nature, be from the great and small? Or how will it differ from the unit? Again, the unit is prior to the dyad (for when it is destroyed, the dyad is destroyed); therefore it must be an Idea of an Idea, since it is prior to an Idea, and must have come to be prior to it. From what, then? For the indefinite dyad was productive of two.—Again, number must be either infinite or finite; for they make number separate, so it cannot be the case that neither alternative belongs to it.","kind":"passage","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_DAZY_BO_5:P0047"]},{"id":"RAW_DAZY_BO_5:P0048","text":"That it cannot be infinite is clear (for the infinite is neither odd nor even, whereas the generation of numbers is always either of an odd number or of an even: in one way odd, when the one falls into the even; in another, when the dyad falls in and what starts from one is doubled; and in another, the other even numbers from the odd numbers. Again, if every Idea is of something, and numbers are Ideas, then the infinite too will be an Idea of something, either of sensibles or of something else; yet this is possible neither according to their position nor according to reason, given the way in which they arrange the Ideas). But if it is finite, up to what point? For this must be stated not only that it is so, but also why.","kind":"passage","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_DAZY_BO_5:P0048"]},{"id":"RAW_DAZY_BO_5:P0049","text":"But if number extends up to the decad, as some say, first, the Forms will quickly fail—for example, if the triad is man-itself, what number will horse-itself be? For each number is itself up to the decad; it must therefore be one of the numbers in these (for these are substances and Ideas); yet they will still fail (for the Forms of animal will exceed them). At the same time, it is clear that if the triad is man-itself in this way, then so too are the other triads (for those in the same numbers are alike), so that there will be infinite men: if each triad is an Idea, each will be man-itself; if not, they will at least be men. And if the lesser is part of the greater, where it is composed of commensurable units in the same number, then if the tetrad itself is an Idea of something, such as horse or white, man will be a part of horse, if man is a dyad. It is also absurd that there should be an Idea of the decad but not of the eleven or of the subsequent numbers.","kind":"passage","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_DAZY_BO_5:P0049"]},{"id":"RAW_DAZY_BO_5:P0050","text":"Again, some things both are and come to be, though there are no Forms of them; so why are there not Forms of those things too? Thus Forms are not causes. Again, it is absurd if the number up to the decad is something more and a Form of the decad itself, although there is no generation of the former as one thing, while there is of the latter. Yet they attempt this as though the number up to the decad were complete. At any rate, they generate the consequent things, such as void, proportion, oddness, and the other such things, within the decad; for they assign some things to the principles, such as motion and rest, good and evil, and others to the numbers. Hence the one is odd; for if it were in the triad, how would the pentad be odd? Again, magnitudes and things of this sort extend up to a certain point—for example, the first line, the indivisible, then the dyad, and then these too up to the decad.—Again, if number is separate, one might raise the question whether the one or the triad and dyad is prior.","kind":"passage","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_DAZY_BO_5:P0050"]},{"id":"RAW_DAZY_BO_5:P0051","text":"Insofar as number is composite, the one is prior; but insofar as the universal and the form are prior, number is prior. For each of the units is a part of number as matter, while number is as form. And in one way the right angle is prior to the acute, because it is determinate and in account; but in another way the acute is prior, because it is a part and the right is divided into it. Thus, as matter, the acute and the element and the unit are prior; but as form and substance according to account, the right and the whole composed of matter and form are prior. For the compound is nearer to the form and to that of which there is an account, though later in generation. How then is the one a principle? Because it is indivisible, they say. But the universal, the particular, and the element are also indivisible. Yet they are so in another way, the one in account, the other in time. In which way, then, is the one a principle? For, as was said, both the right angle seems prior to the acute and this to that, and each is one. Thus they make the one a principle in both ways.","kind":"passage","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_DAZY_BO_5:P0051"]},{"id":"RAW_DAZY_BO_5:P0052","text":"But this is impossible: for the one is as form and substance, the other as part and matter. For each is in some way one—one in truth potentially (if indeed number is some one thing and not as a heap, but is different from different units, as they say), but neither is actually a unit. The cause of the error that results is that they hunted simultaneously from mathematics and from universal accounts, so that from the former they posited the one and the principle as a point (for the unit is a positionless point; just as certain others composed beings out of the least, so did these; hence the unit becomes the matter of numbers, and at the same time prior to the dyad, but again later, the dyad being some whole and one and form); while, because they sought the universal, they spoke of the one that is predicated also in this way as a part. But it is impossible for these to belong simultaneously to the same thing.","kind":"passage","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_DAZY_BO_5:P0052"]},{"id":"RAW_DAZY_BO_5:P0053","text":"But if the one itself must be †only positionless† (for it differs in nothing from being a principle), and the dyad is divisible while the unit is not, the unit would be more like the one itself. But if the unit is so, then that too is like the unit or the dyad; hence each unit would be prior to the dyad. But they do not say this; at any rate, they generate the dyad first.\n\nAgain, if the dyad itself is some one thing and the triad itself is some one thing, both are a dyad. From what, then, is this dyad?","kind":"passage","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_DAZY_BO_5:P0053"]},{"id":"RAW_DAZY_BO_5:P0054","text":"One might also raise a difficulty: since there is no contact among numbers, but succession belongs to things between whose units there is nothing intermediate (such as those in the dyad or triad), whether they are successive to the one itself or not, and whether the dyad is prior to things successive or to either sort of units.—Likewise, difficulties result concerning the genera subsequent to number, line, plane, and solid. For some make them from the forms of the great and small—for example, lengths from long and short, planes from broad and narrow, and solids from deep and shallow; and these are forms of the great and small. Others posit differently the principle corresponding to the one in such things. And in these too there appear countless impossibilities, fabrications, and things contrary to all reasonable considerations.","kind":"passage","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_DAZY_BO_5:P0054"]},{"id":"RAW_DAZY_BO_5:P0055","text":"For they result as disconnected from one another, unless the principles too follow one another, so that broad and narrow are long and short (but if this is so, the plane will be a line and the solid a plane; further, how will angles, figures, and such things be accounted for?), and the same thing results for matters concerning number. For these are affections of magnitude, but magnitude is not from these, just as length is not from straight and curved, nor solids from smooth and rough.—Common to all these is the very difficulty that arises concerning Forms as genus, when one posits universals: whether animal-itself is in animal or is different from animal itself. For if it is not separate, this will create no difficulty; but if the one and numbers are separate, as those who say these things assert, it is not easy to solve, unless one must call what is impossible easy.","kind":"passage","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_DAZY_BO_5:P0055"]},{"id":"RAW_DAZY_BO_5:P0056","text":"For when one thinks the one in the dyad, and generally in number, does one think of something itself or of something else?—Some generate magnitudes from such matter, others from the point (and the point seems to them to be not one, but rather like the one) and from another matter, such as multitude, but not a multitude; concerning whom the same difficulties arise no less. For if matter is one, line, plane, and solid are the same (for what comes from the same things will be the same and one); but if the matters are many, and one is that of line, another of plane, and another of solid, either they follow one another or not, so that the same results follow in this way too: for either the plane will not contain a line, or it will be a line.—Again, no attempt is made to show how number can be from the one and multitude; but however they speak, the same difficult consequences follow as for those who derive it from the one and the indefinite dyad.","kind":"passage","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_DAZY_BO_5:P0056"]},{"id":"RAW_DAZY_BO_5:P0057","text":"For one generates number from the universal predicated and not from any multitude, while another from some multitude, namely the first (for he makes the dyad a first multitude); hence there is, so to speak, no difference, but the same difficulties will follow: mixture, position, blending, generation, and all other such things. Above all one might inquire, if each unit is one, from what it is; for certainly each is not the one itself. It must therefore be from the one itself and multitude, or from a part of multitude. But to say that the unit is a multitude is impossible, since it is indivisible; and to say it is from a part has many other difficulties. For each of the parts must be indivisible (or the unit will be a multitude and divisible), and the one and multitude will not be elements (for each unit is not from multitude and one). Again, he who says this does nothing other than make another number; for a multitude of indivisibles is number.","kind":"passage","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_DAZY_BO_5:P0057"]},{"id":"RAW_DAZY_BO_5:P0058","text":"Again, one must inquire of those who speak in this way whether number is infinite or finite. For there existed, it seems, also a finite multitude, from which the finite units and the one are; and multitude-itself and infinite multitude are different. What sort of multitude, then, is an element, along with the one? Likewise one might inquire concerning the point and the element from which they make magnitudes. For this is surely not just one point; from what, then, is each of the other points? For surely it is not from some interval and from a point itself. But neither can there be indivisible parts of an interval, as there are parts of multitude from which the units are constituted; for number is composed of indivisibles, but magnitudes are not.—All these things, then, and others of this kind make it clear that number and magnitudes cannot be separate; and further, the disagreement of the accounts concerning numbers is a sign that the things themselves, not being true, cause them confusion.","kind":"passage","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_DAZY_BO_5:P0058"]},{"id":"RAW_DAZY_BO_5:P0059","text":"For those who make only mathematical objects exist besides sensibles, seeing the difficulty and artificiality concerning Forms, withdrew from ideal number and made mathematical number. But those who wished to make Forms also numbers, not seeing how, if one posits these principles, mathematical number will exist besides ideal number, made ideal and mathematical number the same in account, though in fact mathematical number has been done away with (for they state special hypotheses, not mathematical ones). But the first man to posit that Forms exist, and that Forms are numbers and mathematical objects exist, reasonably separated them. Thus it turns out that all speak correctly in some respect, but not correctly without qualification. And they themselves agree that they are saying not the same things but contraries. The cause is that their hypotheses and principles are false.","kind":"passage","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_DAZY_BO_5:P0059"]},{"id":"RAW_DAZY_BO_5:P0060","text":"It is difficult to speak well from things not well disposed, according to Epicharmus; for it has just been said, and it immediately appears not to be well disposed.—But concerning numbers, the matters investigated and distinguished are sufficient (for one who is convinced might perhaps still be persuaded from more [considerations], but for one who is not convinced, toward becoming convinced, [they are] no more [effective]); but concerning the first principles and the first causes and elements, whatever is said by those who define only sensible substance, some things have been stated in the works on nature, while others do not belong to the present inquiry; but whatever is said by those who assert that there are other substances besides sensible ones, it follows upon what has been said to consider. Since, then, some say that the Ideas and the numbers are such things, and that the elements of these are elements and principles of beings, we must examine what they say about these things and how they say it.","kind":"passage","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_DAZY_BO_5:P0060"]},{"id":"RAW_DAZY_BO_5:P0061","text":"Those, then, who make only numbers, and these mathematical, must be considered later; but in the case of those who speak of the Ideas, one might at the same time observe both the manner [of their doctrine] and the difficulty concerning them. For they make the Ideas both universal and, again, as separate and as particulars. That this is impossible has been investigated earlier. The cause of combining these [claims] into one for those who say that substances are universals was that they did not make them the same as sensibles; for they thought that the particulars among sensibles were in flux and that none of them remained, whereas the universal existed besides these and was something different. As we said in the earlier discussions, Socrates set this [line of thought] in motion on account of the definitions, but he did not separate [universals] from particulars; and in not separating them he thought rightly. This is clear from the results: for without the universal it is not possible to attain knowledge, but separating it is the cause of the difficulties that result concerning the Ideas.","kind":"passage","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_DAZY_BO_5:P0061"]},{"id":"RAW_DAZY_BO_5:P0062","text":"But those who [thought it] necessary, if there were to be any substances besides sensible and flowing ones, that they be separate, had no others [to posit], but set forth these things called universals, with the result that universal and particular natures are nearly the same. This in itself, then, would be one difficulty of the things stated.","kind":"passage","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_DAZY_BO_5:P0062"]},{"id":"RAW_DAZY_BO_5:P0063","text":"But let us now state what presents a certain difficulty both for those who speak of the Ideas and for those who do not, and which was previously stated at the beginning among the aporiai. For if one does not posit substances as separate, and separate in the way that individual beings are said to be, one will do away with substance in the sense in which we wish to speak of it; but if one posits substances as separate, how will one posit their elements and principles? For if [they are] particular and not universal, beings will be as numerous as the elements, and the elements will not be knowable. (For let the syllables in speech be substances, and their elements elements of the substances; then BA must be one, and each of the syllables one, if they are not universal and the same in form, but each one numerically and a this-something and not homonymous; further, they posit each to be one precisely what it is; and if this is so of syllables, it is so also of those out of which they are composed; therefore there will not be more alphas than one, nor any of the other elements, by the same argument whereby neither","kind":"passage","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_DAZY_BO_5:P0063"]},{"id":"RAW_DAZY_BO_5:P0064","text":"the same syllable is both one thing and another. But if this is so, there will not be other beings besides the elements, but only the elements; further, the elements will not even be knowable. For they are not universal, whereas knowledge is of universals; this is clear from demonstrations and definitions, for there is no syllogism that this triangle [has angles equal to] two right angles, unless every triangle [has angles equal to] two right angles, nor that this man is an animal, unless every man is an animal.) But if indeed the principles are universal, either the substances from these are also universal, or that which is not substance will be prior to substance. For the universal is not substance, whereas the element and the principle are universal; and the element and principle are prior to the things of which they are principle and element.","kind":"passage","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_DAZY_BO_5:P0064"]},{"id":"RAW_DAZY_BO_5:P0065","text":"All these consequences reasonably follow whenever they make the Ideas out of elements and demand that, besides substances having the same form, there be some one separate thing; but if nothing prevents, just as in the case of the elements of speech, there being many alphas and betas, with there being no Alpha-itself or Beta-itself besides the many, there will on this account be infinitely many similar syllables. But the claim that all knowledge is universal, so that it is necessary that the principles of beings also be universal and not separate substances, contains the greatest difficulty among the things said; nevertheless, what is said is in one way true and in another way not true. For knowledge, like knowing, is twofold, one being in potentiality and the other in actuality.","kind":"passage","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_DAZY_BO_5:P0065"]},{"id":"RAW_DAZY_BO_5:P0066","text":"Potentiality, then, being as matter universal and indefinite, is of the universal and indefinite; but actuality is determinate and of the determinate, being a this-something of a this-something. But incidentally sight sees the universal color, because this color which it sees is color, and what the grammarian contemplates, this alpha, is alpha. Since, if it is necessary that the principles be universal, it is necessary also that the things from these be universal, as in demonstrations; but if this is so, nothing will be separate, nor [will there be] substance. But it is clear that in one way knowledge is universal, and in another way it is not.","kind":"passage","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_DAZY_BO_5:P0066"]}],"source_digest":"ae878fd2fe2df0c57e7200b22f275b108514c60739d5da7c820e5c0b3cf9991f","source_language_code":"grc","original_title":"Book 13"}],"passage_basis":"frozen-grounding","original_title":"Book 13","title_language_code":"en"},{"id":"RAW_DAZY_BO_6","title":"Book 14","order":14,"chapter_id":"CH_DAZY_BO_6","text":"περὶ μὲν οὖν τῆς οὐσίας ταύτης εἰρήσθω τοσαῦτα, πάντες δὲ ποιοῦσι τὰς ἀρχὰς ἐναντίας, ὥσπερ ἐν τοῖς φυσικοῖς, καὶ περὶ τὰς ἀκινήτους οὐσίας ὁμοίως. εἰ δὲ τῆς τῶν ἁπάντων ἀρχῆς μὴ ἐνδέχεται πρότερόν τι εἶναι, ἀδύνατον ἂν εἴη τὴν ἀρχὴν ἕτερόν τι οὖσαν εἶναι ἀρχήν, οἷον εἴ τις λέγοι τὸ λευκὸν ἀρχὴν εἶναι οὐχ ᾗ ἕτερον ἀλλʼ ᾗ λευκόν, εἶναι μέντοι καθʼ ὑποκειμένου καὶ ἕτερόν τι ὂν λευκὸν εἶναι· ἐκεῖνο γὰρ πρότερον ἔσται. ἀλλὰ μὴν γίγνεται πάντα ἐξ ἐναντίων ὡς ὑποκειμένου τινός· ἀνάγκη ἄρα μάλιστα τοῖς ἐναντίοις τοῦθʼ ὑπάρχειν.  ἀεὶ ἄρα πάντα τὰ ἐναντία καθʼ ὑποκειμένου καὶ οὐθὲν χωριστόν, ἀλλʼ ὥσπερ καὶ φαίνεται οὐθὲν οὐσίᾳ ἐναντίον, καὶ ὁ λόγος μαρτυρεῖ. οὐθὲν ἄρα τῶν ἐναντίων κυρίως ἀρχὴ πάντων ἀλλʼ ἑτέρα.—οἱ δὲ τὸ ἕτερον τῶν ἐναντίων ὕλην ποιοῦσιν, οἱ μὲν τῷ ἑνὶ  τὸ ἄνισον, ὡς τοῦτο τὴν τοῦ πλήθους οὖσαν φύσιν, οἱ δὲ τῷ ἑνὶ τὸ πλῆθος (γεννῶνται γὰρ οἱ ἀριθμοὶ τοῖς μὲν ἐκ τῆς τοῦ ἀνίσου δυάδος, τοῦ μεγάλου καὶ μικροῦ, τῷ δʼ ἐκ τοῦ πλήθους, ὑπὸ τῆς τοῦ ἑνὸς δὲ οὐσίας ἀμφοῖν)· καὶ γὰρ ὁ τὸ ἄνισον καὶ ἓν λέγων τὰ στοιχεῖα, τὸ δʼ ἄνισον ἐκ μεγάλου καὶ μικροῦ δυάδα, ὡς ἓν ὄντα τὸ ἄνισον καὶ τὸ μέγα καὶ τὸ μικρὸν λέγει, καὶ οὐ διορίζει ὅτι λόγῳ ἀριθμῷ δʼ οὔ. ἀλλὰ μὴν καὶ τὰς ἀρχὰς ἃς στοιχεῖα καλοῦσιν οὐ καλῶς ἀποδιδόασιν, οἱ μὲν τὸ μέγα καὶ τὸ μικρὸν λέγοντες μετὰ τοῦ ἑνός, τρία ταῦτα στοιχεῖα τῶν ἀριθμῶν, τὰ μὲν δύο ὕλην τὸ δʼ ἓν τὴν μορφήν, οἱ δὲ τὸ πολὺ καὶ ὀλίγον, ὅτι τὸ μέγα καὶ τὸ μικρὸν μεγέθους οἰκειότερα τὴν φύσιν, οἱ δὲ τὸ καθόλου μᾶλλον ἐπὶ τούτων, τὸ ὑπερέχον καὶ τὸ ὑπερεχόμενον. διαφέρει δὲ τούτων οὐθὲν ὡς εἰπεῖν πρὸς ἔνια τῶν συμβαινόντων, ἀλλὰ πρὸς τὰς λογικὰς μόνον δυσχερείας, ἃς φυλάττονται διὰ τὸ καὶ αὐτοὶ λογικὰς φέρειν τὰς ἀποδείξεις. πλὴν τοῦ αὐτοῦ γε λόγου ἐστὶ τὸ ὑπερέχον καὶ ὑπερεχόμενον εἶναι ἀρχὰς ἀλλὰ μὴ τὸ μέγα καὶ τὸ μικρόν, καὶ τὸν ἀριθμὸν πρότερον τῆς δυάδος ἐκ τῶν στοιχείων· καθόλου γὰρ ἀμφότερα μᾶλλόν ἐστιν. νῦν δὲ τὸ μὲν λέγουσι τὸ δʼ οὐ λέγουσιν. οἱ δὲ τὸ ἕτερον καὶ τὸ ἄλλο πρὸς τὸ ἓν ἀντιτιθέασιν, οἱ δὲ πλῆθος καὶ τὸ ἕν. εἰ δέ ἐστιν, ὥσπερ βούλονται, τὰ ὄντα ἐξ ἐναντίων, τῷ δὲ ἑνὶ ἢ οὐθὲν ἐναντίον ἢ εἴπερ ἄρα μέλλει, τὸ πλῆθος, τὸ δʼ ἄνισον τῷ ἴσῳ καὶ τὸ ἕτερον τῷ ταὐτῷ καὶ τὸ ἄλλο αὐτῷ, μάλιστα μὲν οἱ τὸ ἓν τῷ πλήθει ἀντιτιθέντες ἔχονταί τινος δόξης, οὐ μὴν οὐδʼ οὗτοι ἱκανῶς· ἔσται γὰρ τὸ ἓν ὀλίγον· πλῆθος μὲν γὰρ ὀλιγότητι τὸ δὲ πολὺ τῷ ὀλίγῳ ἀντίκειται.—τὸ δʼ ἓν ὅτι μέτρον σημαίνει, φανερόν. καὶ ἐν παντὶ ἔστι τι ἕτερον ὑποκείμενον, οἷον ἐν ἁρμονίᾳ δίεσις, ἐν δὲ μεγέθει δάκτυλος ἢ ποὺς ἤ τι τοιοῦτον, ἐν δὲ ῥυθμοῖς βάσις ἢ συλλαβή· ὁμοίως δὲ καὶ ἐν βάρει σταθμός τις ὡρισμένος ἐστίν· καὶ κατὰ πάντων δὲ τὸν αὐτὸν τρόπον,  ἐν μὲν τοῖς ποιοῖς ποιόν τι, ἐν δὲ τοῖς ποσοῖς ποσόν τι, καὶ ἀδιαίρετον τὸ μέτρον, τὸ μὲν κατὰ τὸ εἶδος τὸ δὲ πρὸς τὴν αἴσθησιν, ὡς οὐκ ὄντος τινὸς τοῦ ἑνὸς καθʼ αὑτὸ οὐσίας. καὶ τοῦτο κατὰ λόγον· σημαίνει γὰρ τὸ ἓν ὅτι μέτρον πλήθους τινός, καὶ ὁ ἀριθμὸς ὅτι πλῆθος μεμετρημένον καὶ πλῆθος μέτρων (διὸ καὶ εὐλόγως οὐκ ἔστι τὸ ἓν ἀριθμός· οὐδὲ γὰρ τὸ μέτρον μέτρα, ἀλλʼ ἀρχὴ καὶ τὸ μέτρον καὶ τὸ ἕν). δεῖ δὲ ἀεὶ τὸ αὐτό τι ὑπάρχειν πᾶσι τὸ μέτρον, οἷον εἰ ἵπποι, τὸ μέτρον ἵππος, καὶ εἰ ἄνθρωποι, ἄνθρωπος. εἰ δʼ ἄνθρωπος καὶ ἵππος καὶ θεός, ζῷον ἴσως, καὶ ὁ ἀριθμὸς αὐτῶν ἔσται ζῷα. εἰ δʼ ἄνθρωπος καὶ λευκὸν καὶ βαδίζον, ἥκιστα μὲν ἀριθμὸς τούτων διὰ τὸ ταὐτῷ πάντα ὑπάρχειν καὶ ἑνὶ κατὰ ἀριθμόν, ὅμως δὲ γενῶν ἔσται ὁ ἀριθμὸς ὁ τούτων, ἤ τινος ἄλλης τοιαύτης προσηγορίας.\n\n \n\nοἱ δὲ τὸ ἄνισον ὡς ἕν τι, τὴν δυάδα δὲ ἀόριστον ποιοῦντες μεγάλου καὶ μικροῦ, πόρρω λίαν τῶν δοκούντων καὶ δυνατῶν λέγουσιν· πάθη τε γὰρ ταῦτα καὶ συμβεβηκότα μᾶλλον ἢ ὑποκείμενα τοῖς ἀριθμοῖς καὶ τοῖς μεγέθεσίν ἐστι, τὸ πολὺ καὶ ὀλίγον ἀριθμοῦ, καὶ μέγα καὶ μικρὸν μεγέθους, ὥσπερ ἄρτιον καὶ περιττόν, καὶ λεῖον καὶ τραχύ, καὶ εὐθὺ καὶ καμπύλον· ἔτι δὲ πρὸς ταύτῃ τῇ ἁμαρτίᾳ καὶ πρός τι ἀνάγκη εἶναι τὸ μέγα καὶ τὸ μικρὸν καὶ ὅσα τοιαῦτα· τὸ δὲ πρός τι πάντων ἥκιστα φύσις τις ἢ οὐσία  ἐστι, καὶ ὑστέρα τοῦ ποιοῦ καὶ ποσοῦ· καὶ πάθος τι τοῦ ποσοῦ τὸ πρός τι, ὥσπερ ἐλέχθη, ἀλλʼ οὐχ ὕλη, εἴ τι ἕτερον καὶ τῷ ὅλως κοινῷ πρός τι καὶ τοῖς μέρεσιν αὐτοῦ καὶ εἴδεσιν. οὐθὲν γάρ ἐστιν οὔτε μέγα οὔτε μικρόν, οὔτε πολὺ οὔτε ὀλίγον, οὔτε ὅλως πρός τι, ὃ οὐχ ἕτερόν τι ὂν πολὺ ἢ ὀλίγον ἢ μέγα ἢ μικρὸν ἢ πρός τί ἐστιν. σημεῖον δʼ ὅτι ἥκιστα οὐσί τις καὶ ὄν τι τὸ πρός τι τὸ μόνου μὴ εἶναι γένεσιν αὐτοῦ μηδὲ φθορὰν μηδὲ κίνησιν ὥσπερ κατὰ τὸ ποσὸν αὔξησις καὶ φθίσις, κατὰ τὸ ποιὸν ἀλλοίωσις, κατὰ τόπον φορά, κατὰ τὴν οὐσίαν ἡ ἁπλῆ γένεσις καὶ φθορά,—ἀλλʼ οὐ κατὰ τὸ πρός τι· ἄνευ γὰρ τοῦ κινηθῆναι ὁτὲ μὲν μεῖζον ὁτὲ δὲ ἔλαττον ἢ ἴσον ἔσται θατέρου κινηθέντος κατὰ τὸ ποσόν.  ἀνάγκη τε ἑκάστου ὕλην εἶναι τὸ δυνάμει τοιοῦτον, ὥστε καὶ οὐσίας· τὸ δὲ πρός τι οὔτε δυνάμει οὐσία οὔτε ἐνεργείᾳ. ἄτοπον οὖν, μᾶλλον δὲ ἀδύνατον, τὸ οὐσίας μὴ οὐσίαν ποιεῖν στοιχεῖον καὶ πρότερον· ὕστερον γὰρ πᾶσαι αἱ κατηγορίαι. ἔτι δὲ τὰ στοιχεῖα οὐ κατηγορεῖται καθʼ ὧν στοιχεῖα, τὸ δὲ πολὺ καὶ ὀλίγον καὶ χωρὶς καὶ ἅμα κατηγορεῖται ἀριθμοῦ, καὶ τὸ μακρὸν καὶ τὸ βραχὺ γραμμῆς, καὶ ἐπίπεδόν ἐστι καὶ πλατὺ καὶ στενόν. εἰ δὲ δὴ καὶ ἔστι τι πλῆθος οὗ τὸ μὲν ἀεί, τὸ ὀλίγον, οἷον ἡ δυάς (εἰ γὰρ πολύ, τὸ ἓν ἂν ὀλίγον εἴη), κἂν πολὺ ἁπλῶς εἴη, οἷον ἡ δεκὰς πολύ,  εἰ ταύτης μή ἐστι πλεῖον, ἢ τὰ μύρια. πῶς οὖν ἔσται οὕτως ἐξ ὀλίγου καὶ πολλοῦ ὁ ἀριθμός; ἢ γὰρ ἄμφω ἔδει κατηγορεῖσθαι ἢ μηδέτερον· νῦν δὲ τὸ ἕτερον μόνον κατηγορεῖται.\n\n\n\n \n\n \n\nἁπλῶς δὲ δεῖ σκοπεῖν, ἆρα δυνατὸν τὰ ἀΐδια ἐκ στοιχείων συγκεῖσθαι; ὕλην γὰρ ἕξει· σύνθετον γὰρ πᾶν τὸ ἐκ στοιχείων. εἰ τοίνυν ἀνάγκη, ἐξ οὗ ἐστιν, εἰ καὶ ἀεὶ ἔστι, κἄν, εἰ ἐγένετο, ἐκ τούτου γίγνεσθαι, γίγνεται δὲ πᾶν ἐκ τοῦ δυνάμει ὄντος τοῦτο ὃ γίγνεται (οὐ γὰρ ἂν ἐγένετο ἐκ τοῦ ἀδυνάτου οὐδὲ ἦν), τὸ δὲ δυνατὸν ἐνδέχεται καὶ ἐνεργεῖν καὶ μή, εἰ καὶ ὅτι μάλιστα ἀεὶ ἔστιν ὁ ἀριθμὸς ἢ ὁτιοῦν ἄλλο ὕλην ἔχον, ἐνδέχοιτʼ ἂν μὴ εἶναι, ὥσπερ καὶ τὸ μίαν ἡμέραν ἔχον καὶ τὸ ὁποσαοῦν ἔτη· εἰ δʼ οὕτω, καὶ τὸ τοσοῦτον χρόνον οὗ μὴ ἔστι πέρας. οὐκ ἂν τοίνυν εἴη ἀΐδια, εἴπερ μὴ ἀΐδιον τὸ ἐνδεχόμενον μὴ εἶναι, καθάπερ ἐν ἄλλοις λόγοις συνέβη πραγματευθῆναι. εἰ δέ ἐστι τὸ λεγόμενον νῦν ἀληθὲς καθόλου, ὅτι οὐδεμία ἐστὶν ἀΐδιος οὐσία ἐὰν μὴ ᾖ ἐνέργεια, τὰ δὲ στοιχεῖα ὕλη τῆς οὐσίας, οὐδεμιᾶς ἂν εἴη ἀϊδίου οὐσίας στοιχεῖα ἐξ ὧν ἐστιν ἐνυπαρχόντων. εἰσὶ δέ τινες οἳ δυάδα μὲν ἀόριστον ποιοῦσι τὸ μετὰ τοῦ ἑνὸς στοιχεῖον, τὸ δʼ ἄνισον δυσχεραίνουσιν εὐλόγως διὰ τὰ συμβαίνοντα ἀδύνατα· οἷς τοσαῦτα μόνον ἀφῄρηται τῶν δυσχερῶν ὅσα διὰ τὸ ποιεῖν τὸ ἄνισον καὶ τὸ πρός τι στοιχεῖον ἀναγκαῖα συμβαίνει τοῖς λέγουσιν· ὅσα δὲ χωρὶς ταύτης τῆς δόξης, ταῦτα κἀκείνοις ὑπάρχειν ἀναγκαῖον, ἐάν τε τὸν εἰδητικὸν ἀριθμὸν ἐξ αὐτῶν ποιῶσιν ἐάν τε τὸν μαθηματικόν.—πολλὰ μὲν οὖν τὰ αἴτια τῆς ἐπὶ ταύτας τὰς αἰτίας ἐκτροπῆς,  μάλιστα δὲ τὸ ἀπορῆσαι ἀρχαϊκῶς. ἔδοξε γὰρ αὐτοῖς πάντʼ ἔσεσθαι ἓν τὰ ὄντα, αὐτὸ τὸ ὄν, εἰ μή τις λύσει καὶ ὁμόσε βαδιεῖται τῷ Παρμενίδου λόγῳ οὐ γὰρ μήποτε τοῦτο δαμῇ, εἶναι μὴ ἐόντα, ἀλλʼ ἀνάγκη εἶναι τὸ μὴ ὂν δεῖξαι ὅτι ἔστιν· οὕτω γάρ, ἐκ τοῦ ὄντος καὶ ἄλλου τινός, τὰ ὄντα ἔσεσθαι, εἰ πολλά ἐστιν. καίτοι πρῶτον μέν, εἰ τὸ ὂν πολλαχῶς (τὸ μὲν γὰρ  οὐσίαν σημαίνει, τὸ δʼ ὅτι ποιόν, τὸ δʼ ὅτι ποσόν, καὶ τὰς ἄλλας δὴ κατηγορίας), ποῖον οὖν τὰ ὄντα πάντα ἕν, εἰ μὴ τὸ μὴ ὂν ἔσται; πότερον αἱ οὐσίαι, ἢ τὰ πάθη καὶ τὰ ἄλλα δὴ ὁμοίως, ἢ πάντα, καὶ ἔσται ἓν τὸ τόδε καὶ τὸ τοιόνδε καὶ τὸ τοσόνδε καὶ τὰ ἄλλα ὅσα ἕν τι σημαίνει; ἀλλʼ ἄτοπον, μᾶλλον δὲ ἀδύνατον, τὸ μίαν φύσιν τινὰ γενομένην αἰτίαν εἶναι τοῦ τοῦ ὄντος τὸ μὲν τόδε εἶναι τὸ δὲ τοιόνδε τὸ δὲ τοσόνδε τὸ δὲ πού. ἔπειτα ἐκ ποίου μὴ ὄντος καὶ ὄντος τὰ ὄντα; πολλαχῶς γὰρ καὶ τὸ μὴ ὄν, ἐπειδὴ καὶ τὸ ὄν· καὶ τὸ μὲν μὴ ἄνθρωπον εἶναι σημαίνει τὸ μὴ εἶναι τοδί, τὸ δὲ μὴ εὐθὺ τὸ μὴ εἶναι τοιονδί, τὸ δὲ μὴ τρίπηχυ τὸ μὴ εἶναι τοσονδί. ἐκ ποίου οὖν ὄντος καὶ μὴ ὄντος πολλὰ τὰ ὄντα; βούλεται μὲν δὴ τὸ ψεῦδος καὶ ταύτην τὴν φύσιν λέγειν τὸ οὐκ ὄν, ἐξ οὗ καὶ τοῦ ὄντος πολλὰ τὰ ὄντα, διὸ καὶ ἐλέγετο ὅτι δεῖ ψεῦδός τι ὑποθέσθαι, ὥσπερ καὶ οἱ γεωμέτραι τὸ ποδιαίαν εἶναι τὴν μὴ ποδιαίαν· ἀδύνατον δὲ ταῦθʼ οὕτως ἔχειν, οὔτε γὰρ οἱ γεωμέτραι ψεῦδος οὐθὲν ὑποτίθενται (οὐ γὰρ ἐν τῷ συλλογισμῷ ἡ πρότασις), οὔτε ἐκ τοῦ οὕτω μὴ ὄντος τὰ ὄντα γίγνεται οὐδὲ φθείρεται. ἀλλʼ ἐπειδὴ τὸ μὲν κατὰ τὰς πτώσεις μὴ ὂν ἰσαχῶς ταῖς κατηγορίαις λέγεται, παρὰ τοῦτο δὲ τὸ ὡς ψεῦδος λέγεται  μὴ ὂν καὶ τὸ κατὰ δύναμιν, ἐκ τούτου ἡ γένεσίς ἐστιν, ἐκ τοῦ μὴ ἀνθρώπου δυνάμει δὲ ἀνθρώπου ἄνθρωπος, καὶ ἐκ τοῦ μὴ λευκοῦ δυνάμει δὲ λευκοῦ λευκόν, ὁμοίως ἐάν τε ἕν τι γίγνηται ἐάν τε πολλά.—φαίνεται δὲ ἡ ζήτησις πῶς πολλὰ τὸ ὂν τὸ κατὰ τὰς οὐσίας λεγόμενον· ἀριθμοὶ γὰρ καὶ μήκη καὶ σώματα τὰ γεννώμενά ἐστιν. ἄτοπον δὴ τὸ ὅπως μὲν πολλὰ τὸ ὂν τὸ τί ἐστι ζητῆσαι, πῶς δὲ ἢ ποιὰ ἢ ποσά, μή. οὐ γὰρ δὴ ἡ δυὰς ἡ ἀόριστος αἰτία οὐδὲ τὸ μέγα καὶ τὸ μικρὸν τοῦ δύο λευκὰ ἢ πολλὰ εἶναι χρώματα ἢ χυμοὺς ἢ σχήματα·  ἀριθμοὶ γὰρ ἂν καὶ ταῦτα ἦσαν καὶ μονάδες. ἀλλὰ μὴν εἴ γε ταῦτʼ ἐπῆλθον, εἶδον ἂν τὸ αἴτιον καὶ τὸ ἐν ἐκείνοις· τὸ γὰρ αὐτὸ καὶ τὸ ἀνάλογον αἴτιον. αὕτη γὰρ ἡ παρέκβασις αἰτία καὶ τοῦ τὸ ἀντικείμενον ζητοῦντας τῷ ὄντι καὶ τῷ ἑνί, ἐξ οὗ καὶ τούτων τὰ ὄντα, τὸ πρός τι καὶ τὸ ἄνισον ὑποθεῖναι, ὃ οὔτʼ ἐναντίον οὔτʼ ἀπόφασις ἐκείνων, μία τε φύσις τῶν ὄντων ὥσπερ καὶ τὸ τί καὶ τὸ ποῖον. καὶ ζητεῖν ἔδει καὶ τοῦτο, πῶς πολλὰ τὰ πρός τι ἀλλʼ οὐχ ἕν· νῦν δὲ πῶς μὲν πολλαὶ μονάδες παρὰ τὸ πρῶτον ἓν ζητεῖται, πῶς δὲ πολλὰ ἄνισα παρὰ τὸ ἄνισον οὐκέτι. καίτοι χρῶνται καὶ λέγουσι μέγα μικρόν, πολὺ ὀλίγον, ἐξ ὧν οἱ ἀριθμοί, μακρὸν βραχύ, ἐξ ὧν τὸ μῆκος, πλατὺ στενόν, ἐξ ὧν τὸ ἐπίπεδον, βαθὺ ταπεινόν, ἐξ ὧν οἱ ὄγκοι· καὶ ἔτι δὴ πλείω εἴδη λέγουσι τοῦ πρός τι· τούτοις δὴ τί αἴτιον τοῦ πολλὰ εἶναι;—ἀνάγκη μὲν οὖν, ὥσπερ λέγομεν, ὑποθεῖναι τὸ δυνάμει ὂν ἑκάστῳ (τοῦτο δὲ προσαπεφήνατο ὁ ταῦτα λέγων, τί τὸ δυνάμει τόδε καὶ οὐσία, μὴ ὂν δὲ καθʼ αὑτό, ὅτι τὸ πρός τι, ὥσπερ εἰ εἶπε τὸ ποιόν, ὃ οὔτε δυνάμει ἐστὶ τὸ ἓν ἢ τὸ ὂν οὔτε ἀπόφασις τοῦ ἑνὸς οὐδὲ τοῦ ὄντος ἀλλʼ ἕν τι τῶν ὄντων), πολύ τε μᾶλλον, ὥσπερ ἐλέχθη, εἰ ἐζήτει πῶς πολλὰ τὰ ὄντα, μὴ τὰ ἐν τῇ αὐτῇ κατηγορίᾳ ζητεῖν, πῶς πολλαὶ οὐσίαι ἢ πολλὰ ποιά, ἀλλὰ πῶς πολλὰ τὰ ὄντα· τὰ μὲν γὰρ οὐσίαι τὰ δὲ πάθη τὰ δὲ πρός τι. ἐπὶ μὲν οὖν τῶν ἄλλων κατηγοριῶν ἔχει τινὰ καὶ ἄλλην ἐπίστασιν πῶς πολλά (διὰ γὰρ τὸ μὴ χωριστὰ εἶναι τῷ τὸ ὑποκείμενον πολλὰ γίγνεσθαι καὶ εἶναι ποιά τε πολλὰ  καὶ ποσά· καίτοι δεῖ γέ τινα εἶναι ὕλην ἑκάστῳ γένει, πλὴν χωριστὴν ἀδύνατον τῶν οὐσιῶν)· ἀλλʼ ἐπὶ τῶν τόδε τι ἔχει τινὰ λόγον πῶς πολλὰ τὸ τόδε τι, εἰ μή τι ἔσται καὶ τόδε τι καὶ φύσις τις τοιαύτη· αὕτη δέ ἐστιν ἐκεῖθεν μᾶλλον ἡ ἀπορία, πῶς πολλαὶ ἐνεργείᾳ οὐσίαι ἀλλʼ οὐ μία. ἀλλὰ μὴν καὶ εἰ μὴ ταὐτόν ἐστι τὸ τόδε καὶ τὸ ποσόν, οὐ λέγεται πῶς καὶ διὰ τί πολλὰ τὰ ὄντα, ἀλλὰ πῶς ποσὰ πολλά. ὁ γὰρ ἀριθμὸς πᾶς ποσόν τι σημαίνει, καὶ ἡ μονάς, εἰ μὴ μέτρον καὶ τὸ κατὰ τὸ ποσὸν ἀδιαίρετον. εἰ μὲν οὖν ἕτερον τὸ ποσὸν καὶ τὸ τί ἐστιν, οὐ λέγεται τὸ τί ἐστιν ἐκ τίνος οὐδὲ πῶς πολλά·  εἰ δὲ ταὐτό, πολλὰς ὑπομένει ὁ λέγων ἐναντιώσεις.—ἐπιστήσειε δʼ ἄν τις τὴν σκέψιν καὶ περὶ τῶν ἀριθμῶν πόθεν δεῖ λαβεῖν τὴν πίστιν ὡς εἰσίν. τῷ μὲν γὰρ ἰδέας τιθεμένῳ παρέχονταί τινʼ αἰτίαν τοῖς οὖσιν, εἴπερ ἕκαστος τῶν ἀριθμῶν ἰδέα τις ἡ δʼ ἰδέα τοῖς ἄλλοις αἰτία τοῦ εἶναι ὃν δή ποτε τρόπον (ἔστω γὰρ ὑποκείμενον αὐτοῖς τοῦτο)· τῷ δὲ τοῦτον μὲν τὸν τρόπον οὐκ οἰομένῳ διὰ τὸ τὰς ἐνούσας δυσχερείας ὁρᾶν περὶ τὰς ἰδέας ὥστε διά γε ταῦτα μὴ ποιεῖν ἀριθμούς, ποιοῦντι δὲ ἀριθμὸν τὸν μαθηματικόν, πόθεν τε χρὴ πιστεῦσαι ὡς ἔστι τοιοῦτος ἀριθμός, καὶ τί τοῖς ἄλλοις χρήσιμος; οὐθενὸς γὰρ οὔτε φησὶν ὁ λέγων αὐτὸν εἶναι, ἀλλʼ ὡς αὐτήν τινα λέγει καθʼ αὑτὴν φύσιν οὖσαν, οὔτε φαίνεται ὢν αἴτιος· τὰ γὰρ θεωρήματα τῶν ἀριθμητικῶν πάντα καὶ κατὰ τῶν αἰσθητῶν ὑπάρξει, καθάπερ ἐλέχθη. \n\n\n\n \n\n \n\nοἱ μὲν οὖν τιθέμενοι τὰς ἰδέας εἶναι, καὶ ἀριθμοὺς αὐτὰς εἶναι, τῷ κατὰ τὴν ἔκθεσιν ἑκάστου παρὰ τὰ πολλὰ λαμβάνειν  ἕν τι ἕκαστον πειρῶνταί γε λέγειν πως διὰ τί ἔστιν, οὐ μὴν ἀλλὰ ἐπεὶ οὔτε ἀναγκαῖα οὔτε δυνατὰ ταῦτα, οὐδὲ τὸν ἀριθμὸν διά γε ταῦτα εἶναι λεκτέον· οἱ δὲ Πυθαγόρειοι διὰ τὸ ὁρᾶν πολλὰ τῶν ἀριθμῶν πάθη ὑπάρχοντα τοῖς αἰσθητοῖς σώμασιν, εἶναι μὲν ἀριθμοὺς ἐποίησαν τὰ ὄντα, οὐ χωριστοὺς δέ, ἀλλʼ ἐξ ἀριθμῶν τὰ ὄντα· διὰ τί δέ; ὅτι τὰ πάθη τὰ τῶν ἀριθμῶν ἐν ἁρμονίᾳ ὑπάρχει καὶ ἐν τῷ οὐρανῷ καὶ ἐν πολλοῖς ἄλλοις. τοῖς δὲ τὸν μαθηματικὸν μόνον λέγουσιν εἶναι ἀριθμὸν οὐθὲν τοιοῦτον ἐνδέχεται λέγειν κατὰ τὰς ὑποθέσεις, ἀλλʼ ὅτι οὐκ ἔσονται αὐτῶν αἱ ἐπιστῆμαι ἐλέγετο. ἡμεῖς δέ φαμεν εἶναι, καθάπερ εἴπομεν πρότερον. καὶ δῆλον ὅτι οὐ κεχώρισται τὰ μαθηματικά· οὐ γὰρ ἂν κεχωρισμένων τὰ πάθη ὑπῆρχεν ἐν τοῖς σώμασιν. οἱ μὲν οὖν Πυθαγόρειοι κατὰ μὲν τὸ τοιοῦτον οὐθενὶ ἔνοχοί εἰσιν, κατὰ μέντοι τὸ ποιεῖν ἐξ ἀριθμῶν τὰ φυσικὰ σώματα, ἐκ μὴ ἐχόντων βάρος μηδὲ κουφότητα ἔχοντα κουφότητα καὶ βάρος, ἐοίκασι περὶ ἄλλου οὐρανοῦ λέγειν καὶ σωμάτων ἀλλʼ οὐ τῶν αἰσθητῶν· οἱ δὲ χωριστὸν ποιοῦντες, ὅτι ἐπὶ τῶν αἰσθητῶν οὐκ ἔσται τὰ ἀξιώματα, ἀληθῆ δὲ τὰ λεγόμενα καὶ σαίνει τὴν ψυχήν, εἶναί τε ὑπολαμβάνουσι καὶ χωριστὰ εἶναι· ὁμοίως δὲ καὶ τὰ μεγέθη τὰ μαθηματικά.  δῆλον οὖν ὅτι καὶ ὁ ἐναντιούμενος λόγος τἀναντία ἐρεῖ, καὶ ὃ ἄρτι ἠπορήθη λυτέον τοῖς οὕτω λέγουσι, διὰ τί οὐδαμῶς ἐν τοῖς αἰσθητοῖς ὑπαρχόντων τὰ πάθη ὑπάρχει αὐτῶν ἐν τοῖς αἰσθητοῖς. εἰσὶ δέ τινες οἳ ἐκ τοῦ πέρατα εἶναι καὶ ἔσχατα τὴν στιγμὴν μὲν γραμμῆς, ταύτην δʼ ἐπιπέδου, τοῦτο δὲ τοῦ στερεοῦ, οἴονται εἶναι ἀνάγκην τοιαύτας φύσεις εἶναι. δεῖ δὴ καὶ τοῦτον ὁρᾶν τὸν λόγον, μὴ λίαν ᾖ μαλακός. οὔτε γὰρ οὐσίαι εἰσὶ τὰ ἔσχατα ἀλλὰ μᾶλλον πάντα ταῦτα πέρατα (ἑπεὶ καὶ τῆς βαδίσεως καὶ ὅλως κινήσεως ἔστι τι πέρας· τοῦτʼ οὖν ἔσται τόδε τι καὶ οὐσία τις· ἀλλʼ ἄτοπον)·—οὐ μὴν ἀλλὰ εἰ καὶ εἰσί, τῶνδε τῶν αἰσθητῶν ἔσονται πάντα (ἐπὶ τούτων γὰρ ὁ λόγος εἴρηκεν)· διὰ τί οὖν χωριστὰ ἔσται;—ἔτι δὲ ἐπιζητήσειεν ἄν τις μὴ λίαν εὐχερὴς ὢν περὶ μὲν τοῦ ἀριθμοῦ παντὸς καὶ τῶν μαθηματικῶν τὸ μηθὲν συμβάλλεσθαι ἀλλήλοις τὰ πρότερα τοῖς ὕστερον (μὴ ὄντος γὰρ τοῦ ἀριθμοῦ οὐθὲν ἧττον τὰ μεγέθη ἔσται τοῖς τὰ μαθηματικὰ μόνον εἶναι φαμένοις, καὶ τούτων μὴ ὄντων ἡ ψυχὴ καὶ τὰ σώματα τὰ αἰσθητά· οὐκ ἔοικε δʼ ἡ φύσις ἐπεισοδιώδης οὖσα ἐκ τῶν φαινομένων, ὥσπερ μοχθηρὰ τραγῳδία)· τοῖς δὲ τὰς ἰδέας τιθεμένοις τοῦτο μὲν ἐκφεύγει—ποιοῦσι γὰρ τὰ μεγέθη ἐκ τῆς ὕλης καὶ ἀριθμοῦ, ἐκ μὲν τῆς δυάδος τὰ μήκη, ἐκ τριάδος δʼ ἴσως τὰ ἐπίπεδα, ἐκ δὲ τῆς τετράδος τὰ στερεὰ ἢ καὶ ἐξ ἄλλων ἀριθμῶν· διαφέρει γὰρ οὐθέν—, ἀλλὰ ταῦτά γε πότερον ἰδέαι ἔσονται, ἢ τίς ὁ τρόπος αὐτῶν, καὶ τί συμβάλλονται τοῖς οὖσιν; οὐθὲν γάρ, ὥσπερ οὐδὲ τὰ μαθηματικά, οὐδὲ ταῦτα συμβάλλεται. ἀλλὰ μὴν οὐδʼ ὑπάρχει γε κατʼ αὐτῶν οὐθὲν θεώρημα, ἐὰν μή τις βούληται κινεῖν τὰ μαθηματικὰ καὶ ποιεῖν ἰδίας τινὰς δόξας. ἔστι δʼ οὐ χαλεπὸν ὁποιασοῦν ὑποθέσεις λαμβάνοντας μακροποιεῖν καὶ συνείρειν. οὗτοι μὲν οὖν ταύτῃ προσγλιχόμενοι ταῖς ἰδέαις τὰ μαθηματικὰ διαμαρτάνουσιν· οἱ δὲ πρῶτοι δύο τοὺς ἀριθμοὺς ποιήσαντες, τόν τε τῶν εἰδῶν καὶ τὸν μαθηματικόν, οὔτʼ εἰρήκασιν οὔτʼ ἔχοιεν ἂν εἰπεῖν πῶς καὶ ἐκ τίνος ἔσται ὁ μαθηματικός. ποιοῦσι γὰρ αὐτὸν μεταξὺ τοῦ εἰδητικοῦ καὶ τοῦ αἰσθητοῦ. εἰ μὲν γὰρ ἐκ τοῦ μεγάλου καὶ μικροῦ, ὁ αὐτὸς ἐκείνῳ ἔσται τῷ τῶν ἰδεῶν (ἐξ ἄλλου δέ τινος μικροῦ καὶ μεγάλου τὰ  μεγέθη ποιεῖ)·  εἰ δʼ ἕτερόν τι ἐρεῖ, πλείω τὰ στοιχεῖα ἐρεῖ· καὶ εἰ ἕν τι ἑκατέρου ἡ ἀρχή, κοινόν τι ἐπὶ τούτων ἔσται τὸ ἕν, ζητητέον τε πῶς καὶ ταῦτα πολλὰ τὸ ἓν καὶ ἅμα τὸν ἀριθμὸν γενέσθαι ἄλλως ἢ ἐξ ἑνὸς καὶ δυάδος ἀορίστου ἀδύνατον κατʼ ἐκεῖνον. πάντα δὴ ταῦτα ἄλογα, καὶ μάχεται καὶ αὐτὰ ἑαυτοῖς καὶ τοῖς εὐλόγοις, καὶ ἔοικεν ἐν αὐτοῖς εἶναι ὁ Σιμωνίδου μακρὸς λόγος· γίγνεται γὰρ ὁ μακρὸς λόγος ὥσπερ ὁ τῶν δούλων ὅταν μηθὲν ὑγιὲς λέγωσιν. φαίνεται δὲ καὶ αὐτὰ τὰ στοιχεῖα τὸ μέγα καὶ τὸ μικρὸν βοᾶν ὡς ἑλκόμενα· οὐ δύναται γὰρ οὐδαμῶς γεννῆσαι τὸν ἀριθμὸν ἀλλʼ ἢ τὸν ἀφʼ ἑνὸς διπλασιαζόμενον.—ἄτοπον δὲ καὶ γένεσιν ποιεῖν ἀϊδίων ὄντων, μᾶλλον δʼ ἕν τι τῶν ἀδυνάτων. οἱ μὲν οὖν Πυθαγόρειοι πότερον οὐ ποιοῦσιν ἢ ποιοῦσι γένεσιν οὐδὲν δεῖ διστάζειν· φανερῶς γὰρ λέγουσιν ὡς τοῦ ἑνὸς συσταθέντος, εἴτʼ ἐξ ἐπιπέδων εἴτʼ ἐκ χροιᾶς εἴτʼ ἐκ σπέρματος εἴτʼ ἐξ ὧν ἀποροῦσιν εἰπεῖν, εὐθὺς τὸ ἔγγιστα τοῦ ἀπείρου ὅτι εἵλκετο καὶ ἐπεραίνετο ὑπὸ τοῦ πέρατος. ἀλλʼ ἐπειδὴ κοσμοποιοῦσι καὶ φυσικῶς βούλονται λέγειν, δίκαιον αὐτοὺς ἐξετάζειν τι περὶ φύσεως, ἐκ δὲ τῆς νῦν ἀφεῖναι μεθόδου· τὰς γὰρ ἐν τοῖς ἀκινήτοις ζητοῦμεν ἀρχάς, ὥστε καὶ τῶν ἀριθμῶν τῶν τοιούτων ἐπισκεπτέον τὴν γένεσιν. \n\n\n\n \n\n \n\nτοῦ μὲν οὖν περιττοῦ γένεσιν οὔ φασιν, ὡς δηλονότι τοῦ ἀρτίου οὔσης γενέσεως· τὸν δʼ ἄρτιον πρῶτον ἐξ ἀνίσων τινὲς κατασκευάζουσι τοῦ μεγάλου καὶ μικροῦ ἰσασθέντων. ἀνάγκη οὖν πρότερον ὑπάρχειν τὴν ἀνισότητα αὐτοῖς τοῦ ἰσασθῆναι· εἰ δʼ ἀεὶ ἦσαν ἰσασμένα, οὐκ ἂν ἦσαν ἄνισα πρότερον (τοῦ γὰρ ἀεὶ οὐκ ἔστι πρότερον οὐθέν), ὥστε φανερὸν ὅτι οὐ τοῦ θεωρῆσαι ἕνεκεν ποιοῦσι τὴν γένεσιν τῶν ἀριθμῶν.—ἔχει δʼ ἀπορίαν καὶ εὐπορήσαντι ἐπιτίμησιν πῶς ἔχει πρὸς τὸ ἀγαθὸν καὶ τὸ καλὸν τὰ στοιχεῖα καὶ αἱ ἀρχαί· ἀπορίαν μὲν ταύτην, πότερόν ἐστί τι ἐκείνων οἷον βουλόμεθα λέγειν αὐτὸ τὸ ἀγαθὸν καὶ τὸ ἄριστον, ἢ οὔ, ἀλλʼ ὑστερογενῆ. παρὰ μὲν γὰρ τῶν θεολόγων ἔοικεν ὁμολογεῖσθαι τῶν νῦν τισίν, οἳ οὔ φασιν, ἀλλὰ προελθούσης τῆς τῶν ὄντων φύσεως καὶ τὸ ἀγαθὸν καὶ τὸ καλὸν ἐμφαίνεσθαι (τοῦτο δὲ ποιοῦσιν εὐλαβούμενοι ἀληθινὴν δυσχέρειαν ἣ συμβαίνει τοῖς λέγουσιν, ὥσπερ ἔνιοι, τὸ ἓν ἀρχήν·  ἔστι δʼ ἡ δυσχέρεια οὐ διὰ τὸ τῇ ἀρχῇ τὸ εὖ ἀποδιδόναι ὡς ὑπάρχον, ἀλλὰ διὰ τὸ τὸ ἓν ἀρχὴν καὶ ἀρχὴν ὡς στοιχεῖον καὶ τὸν ἀριθμὸν ἐκ τοῦ ἑνός),—οἱ δὲ ποιηταὶ οἱ ἀρχαῖοι ταύτῃ ὁμοίως, ᾗ βασιλεύειν καὶ ἄρχειν φασὶν οὐ τοὺς πρώτους, οἷον νύκτα καὶ οὐρανὸν ἢ χάος ἢ ὠκεανόν, ἀλλὰ τὸν Δία· οὐ μὴν ἀλλὰ τούτοις μὲν διὰ τὸ μεταβάλλειν τοὺς ἄρχοντας τῶν ὄντων συμβαίνει τοιαῦτα λέγειν, ἐπεὶ οἵ γε μεμιγμένοι αὐτῶν  τῷ μὴ μυθικῶς πάντα λέγειν, οἷον Φερεκύδης καὶ ἕτεροί τινες, τὸ γεννῆσαν πρῶτον ἄριστον τιθέασι, καὶ οἱ Μάγοι, καὶ τῶν ὑστέρων δὲ σοφῶν οἷον Ἐμπεδοκλῆς τε καὶ Ἀναξαγόρας, ὁ μὲν τὴν φιλίαν στοιχεῖον ὁ δὲ τὸν νοῦν ἀρχὴν ποιήσας. τῶν δὲ τὰς ἀκινήτους οὐσίας εἶναι λεγόντων οἱ μέν φασιν αὐτὸ τὸ ἓν τὸ ἀγαθὸν αὐτὸ εἶναι· οὐσίαν μέντοι τὸ ἓν αὐτοῦ ᾤοντο εἶναι μάλιστα.—ἡ μὲν οὖν ἀπορία αὕτη, ποτέρως δεῖ λέγειν· θαυμαστὸν δʼ εἰ τῷ πρώτῳ καὶ ἀϊδίῳ καὶ αὐταρκεστάτῳ τοῦτʼ αὐτὸ πρῶτον οὐχ ὡς ἀγαθὸν ὑπάρχει, τὸ αὔταρκες καὶ ἡ σωτηρία. ἀλλὰ μὴν οὐ διʼ ἄλλο τι ἄφθαρτον ἢ διότι εὖ ἔχει, οὐδʼ αὔταρκες, ὥστε τὸ μὲν φάναι τὴν ἀρχὴν τοιαύτην εἶναι εὔλογον ἀληθὲς εἶναι, τὸ μέντοι ταύτην εἶναι τὸ ἕν, ἢ εἰ μὴ τοῦτο, στοιχεῖόν γε καὶ στοιχεῖον ἀριθμῶν, ἀδύνατον. συμβαίνει γὰρ πολλὴ δυσχέρεια—ἣν ἔνιοι φεύγοντες ἀπειρήκασιν, οἱ τὸ ἓν μὲν ὁμολογοῦντες ἀρχὴν εἶναι πρώτην καὶ στοιχεῖον, τοῦ ἀριθμοῦ δὲ τοῦ μαθηματικοῦ —ἅπασαι γὰρ αἱ μονάδες γίγνονται ὅπερ ἀγαθόν τι, καὶ πολλή τις εὐπορία ἀγαθῶν. ἔτι εἰ τὰ εἴδη ἀριθμοί, τὰ εἴδη πάντα ὅπερ ἀγαθόν τι· ἀλλὰ μὴν ὅτου βούλεται τιθέτω τις εἶναι ἰδέας· εἰ μὲν γὰρ τῶν ἀγαθῶν μόνον, οὐκ ἔσονται οὐσίαι αἱ ἰδέαι, εἰ δὲ καὶ τῶν οὐσιῶν, πάντα τὰ ζῷα καὶ τὰ φυτὰ ἀγαθὰ καὶ τὰ μετέχοντα. ταῦτά τε δὴ συμβαίνει ἄτοπα, καὶ τὸ ἐναντίον στοιχεῖον, εἴτε πλῆθος ὂν εἴτε τὸ ἄνισον καὶ μέγα καὶ μικρόν, τὸ κακὸν αὐτό (διόπερ ὁ μὲν ἔφευγε τὸ ἀγαθὸν προσάπτειν τῷ ἑνὶ ὡς ἀναγκαῖον ὄν, ἐπειδὴ ἐξ ἐναντίων ἡ γένεσις, τὸ κακὸν τὴν τοῦ πλήθους φύσιν εἶναι· οἱ δὲ λέγουσι τὸ ἄνισον τὴν τοῦ κακοῦ φύσιν)· συμβαίνει δὴ πάντα τὰ ὄντα μετέχειν τοῦ κακοῦ ἔξω ἑνὸς αὐτοῦ τοῦ ἑνός, καὶ μᾶλλον ἀκράτου μετέχειν τοὺς ἀριθμοὺς ἢ τὰ μεγέθη,  καὶ τὸ κακὸν τοῦ ἀγαθοῦ χώραν εἶναι, καὶ μετέχειν καὶ ὀρέγεσθαι τοῦ φθαρτικοῦ· φθαρτικὸν γὰρ τοῦ ἐναντίου τὸ ἐναντίον. καὶ εἰ ὥσπερ ἐλέγομεν ὅτι ἡ ὕλη ἐστὶ τὸ δυνάμει ἕκαστον, οἷον πυρὸς τοῦ ἐνεργείᾳ τὸ δυνάμει πῦρ, τὸ κακὸν ἔσται αὐτὸ τὸ δυνάμει ἀγαθόν. ταῦτα δὴ πάντα συμβαίνει, τὸ μὲν ὅτι ἀρχὴν πᾶσαν στοιχεῖον ποιοῦσι, τὸ δʼ ὅτι τἀναντία ἀρχάς, τὸ δʼ ὅτι τὸ ἓν ἀρχήν, τὸ δʼ ὅτι τοὺς ἀριθμοὺς τὰς πρώτας οὐσίας καὶ χωριστὰ καὶ εἴδη. \n\n\n\n \n\n \n\nεἰ οὖν καὶ τὸ μὴ τιθέναι τὸ ἀγαθὸν ἐν ταῖς ἀρχαῖς καὶ τὸ τιθέναι οὕτως ἀδύνατον, δῆλον ὅτι αἱ ἀρχαὶ οὐκ ὀρθῶς ἀποδίδονται οὐδὲ αἱ πρῶται οὐσίαι. οὐκ ὀρθῶς δʼ ὑπολαμβάνει οὐδʼ εἴ τις παρεικάζει τὰς τοῦ ὅλου ἀρχὰς τῇ τῶν ζῴων καὶ φυτῶν, ὅτι ἐξ ἀορίστων ἀτελῶν τε ἀεὶ τὰ τελειότερα, διὸ καὶ ἐπὶ τῶν πρώτων οὕτως ἔχειν φησίν, ὥστε μηδὲ ὄν τι εἶναι τὸ ἓν αὐτό. εἰσὶ γὰρ καὶ ἐνταῦθα τέλειαι αἱ ἀρχαὶ ἐξ ὧν ταῦτα· ἄνθρωπος γὰρ ἄνθρωπον γεννᾷ, καὶ οὐκ ἔστι τὸ σπέρμα πρῶτον. ἄτοπον δὲ καὶ τὸ τόπον ἅμα τοῖς στερεοῖς τοῖς μαθηματικοῖς ποιῆσαι (ὁ μὲν γὰρ τόπος τῶν καθʼ ἕκαστον ἴδιος, διὸ χωριστὰ τόπῳ, τὰ δὲ μαθηματικὰ οὐ πού), καὶ τὸ εἰπεῖν μὲν ὅτι ποὺ ἔσται, τί δέ ἐστιν ὁ τόπος μή.—ἔδει δὲ τοὺς λέγοντας ἐκ στοιχείων εἶναι τὰ ὄντα καὶ τῶν ὄντων τὰ πρῶτα τοὺς ἀριθμούς, διελομένους πῶς ἄλλο ἐξ ἄλλου ἐστίν, οὕτω λέγειν τίνα τρόπον ὁ ἀριθμός ἐστιν ἐκ τῶν ἀρχῶν. πότερον μίξει; ἀλλʼ οὔτε πᾶν μικτόν, τό τε γιγνόμενον ἕτερον, οὐκ ἔσται τε χωριστὸν τὸ ἓν οὐδʼ ἑτέρα φύσις· οἱ δὲ βούλονται. ἀλλὰ συνθέσει, ὥσπερ συλλαβή; ἀλλὰ θέσιν τε ἀνάγκη ὑπάρχειν, καὶ χωρὶς ὁ νοῶν νοήσει τὸ ἓν καὶ τὸ πλῆθος. τοῦτʼ οὖν ἔσται ὁ ἀριθμός, μονὰς καὶ πλῆθος, ἢ τὸ ἓν καὶ ἄνισον. καὶ ἐπεὶ τὸ ἐκ τινῶν εἶναι ἔστι μὲν ὡς ἐνυπαρχόντων ἔστι δὲ ὡς οὔ, ποτέρως ὁ ἀριθμός; οὕτως γὰρ ὡς ἐνυπαρχόντων οὐκ ἔστιν ἀλλʼ ἢ ὧν γένεσις ἔστιν. ἀλλʼ ὡς ἀπὸ σπέρματος; ἀλλʼ οὐχ οἷόν τε τοῦ ἀδιαιρέτου τι ἀπελθεῖν. ἀλλʼ ὡς ἐκ τοῦ ἐναντίου μὴ ὑπομένοντος; ἀλλʼ ὅσα οὕτως ἔστι, καὶ ἐξ ἄλλου τινός ἐστιν ὑπομένοντος. ἐπεὶ τοίνυν τὸ ἓν ὁ μὲν τῷ πλήθει ὡς ἐναντίον τίθησιν,  ὁ δὲ τῷ ἀνίσῳ, ὡς ἴσῳ τῷ ἑνὶ χρώμενος, ὡς ἐξ ἐναντίων εἴη ἂν ὁ ἀριθμός· ἔστιν ἄρα τι ἕτερον ἐξ οὗ ὑπομένοντος καὶ θατέρου ἐστὶν ἢ γέγονεν. ἔτι τί δή ποτε τὰ μὲν ἄλλʼ ὅσα ἐξ ἐναντίων ἢ οἷς ἔστιν ἐναντία φθείρεται κἂν ἐκ παντὸς ᾖ, ὁ δὲ ἀριθμὸς οὔ; περὶ τούτου γὰρ οὐθὲν λέγεται. καίτοι καὶ ἐνυπάρχον καὶ μὴ ἐνυπάρχον φθείρει τὸ ἐναντίον, οἷον τὸ νεῖκος τὸ μῖγμα (καίτοι γε οὐκ ἔδει· οὐ γὰρ ἐκείνῳ γε ἐναντίον).—οὐθὲν δὲ διώρισται οὐδὲ ὁποτέρως οἱ ἀριθμοὶ αἴτιοι τῶν οὐσιῶν καὶ τοῦ εἶναι, πότερον ὡς ὅροι (οἷον αἱ στιγμαὶ τῶν μεγεθῶν, καὶ ὡς Εὔρυτος ἔταττε τίς ἀριθμὸς τίνος, οἷον ὁδὶ μὲν ἀνθρώπου ὁδὶ δὲ ἵππου, ὥσπερ οἱ τοὺς ἀριθμοὺς ἄγοντες εἰς τὰ σχήματα τρίγωνον καὶ τετράγωνον, οὕτως ἀφομοιῶν ταῖς ψήφοις τὰς μορφὰς τῶν φυτῶν), ἢ ὅτι  λόγος ἡ συμφωνία ἀριθμῶν, ὁμοίως δὲ καὶ ἄνθρωπος καὶ τῶν ἄλλων ἕκαστον; τὰ δὲ δὴ πάθη πῶς ἀριθμοί, τὸ λευκὸν καὶ γλυκὺ καὶ τὸ θερμόν; ὅτι δὲ οὐχ οἱ ἀριθμοὶ οὐσία οὐδὲ τῆς μορφῆς αἴτιοι, δῆλον· ὁ γὰρ λόγος ἡ οὐσία, ὁ δʼ ἀριθμὸς ὕλη. οἷον σαρκὸς ἢ ὀστοῦ ἀριθμὸς ἡ οὐσία οὕτω, τρία πυρὸς γῆς δὲ δύο· καὶ ἀεὶ ὁ ἀριθμὸς ὃς ἂν ᾖ τινῶν ἐστιν, ἢ πύρινος ἢ γήϊνος ἢ μοναδικός, ἀλλʼ ἡ οὐσία τὸ τοσόνδʼ εἶναι πρὸς τοσόνδε κατὰ τὴν μῖξιν· τοῦτο δʼ οὐκέτι ἀριθμὸς ἀλλὰ λόγος μίξεως ἀριθμῶν σωματικῶν ἢ ὁποιωνοῦν. οὔτε οὖν τῷ ποιῆσαι αἴτιος ὁ ἀριθμός, οὔτε ὅλως ὁ ἀριθμὸς οὔτε ὁ μοναδικός, οὔτε ὕλη οὔτε λόγος καὶ εἶδος τῶν πραγμάτων. ἀλλὰ μὴν οὐδʼ ὡς τὸ οὗ ἕνεκα. \n\n\n\n \n\n \n\nἀπορήσειε δʼ ἄν τις καὶ τί τὸ εὖ ἐστὶ τὸ ἀπὸ τῶν ἀριθμῶν τῷ ἐν ἀριθμῷ εἶναι τὴν μῖξιν, ἢ ἐν εὐλογίστῳ ἢ ἐν περιττῷ. νυνὶ γὰρ οὐθὲν ὑγιεινότερον τρὶς τρία ἂν ᾖ τὸ μελίκρατον κεκραμένον, ἀλλὰ μᾶλλον ὠφελήσειεν ἂν ἐν οὐθενὶ λόγῳ ὂν ὑδαρὲς δὲ ἢ ἐν ἀριθμῷ ἄκρατον ὄν. ἔτι οἱ λόγοι ἐν προσθέσει ἀριθμῶν εἰσὶν οἱ τῶν μίξεων, οὐκ ἐν ἀριθμοῖς, οἷον τρία πρὸς δύο ἀλλʼ οὐ τρὶς δύο. τὸ γὰρ αὐτὸ δεῖ γένος εἶναι ἐν ταῖς πολλαπλασιώσεσιν, ὥστε δεῖ μετρεῖσθαι τῷ τε Α τὸν στοῖχον ἐφʼ οὗ ΑΒΓ καὶ τῷ Δ τὸν ΔΕΖ· ὥστε τῷ αὐτῷ πάντα. οὔκουν ἔσται πυρὸς ΒΕΓΖ καὶ ὕδατος ἀριθμὸς δὶς τρία.  —εἰ δʼ ἀνάγκη πάντα ἀριθμοῦ κοινωνεῖν, ἀνάγκη πολλὰ συμβαίνειν τὰ αὐτά, καὶ ἀριθμὸν τὸν αὐτὸν τῷδε καὶ ἄλλῳ. ἆρʼ οὖν τοῦτʼ αἴτιον καὶ διὰ τοῦτό ἐστι τὸ πρᾶγμα, ἢ ἄδηλον; οἷον ἔστι τις τῶν τοῦ ἡλίου φορῶν ἀριθμός, καὶ πάλιν τῶν τῆς σελήνης, καὶ τῶν ζῴων γε ἑκάστου τοῦ βίου καὶ ἡλικίας· τί οὖν κωλύει ἐνίους μὲν τούτων τετραγώνους εἶναι ἐνίους δὲ κύβους, καὶ ἴσους τοὺς δὲ διπλασίους; οὐθὲν γὰρ κωλύει, ἀλλʼ ἀνάγκη ἐν τούτοις στρέφεσθαι, εἰ ἀριθμοῦ πάντα ἐκοινώνει. ἐνεδέχετό τε τὰ διαφέροντα ὑπὸ τὸν αὐτὸν ἀριθμὸν πίπτειν· ὥστʼ εἴ τισιν ὁ αὐτὸς ἀριθμὸς συνεβεβήκει, ταὐτὰ ἂν ἦν ἀλλήλοις ἐκεῖνα τὸ αὐτὸ εἶδος ἀριθμοῦ ἔχοντα, οἷον ἥλιος καὶ σελήνη τὰ αὐτά. ἀλλὰ διὰ τί αἴτια ταῦτα; ἑπτὰ μὲν φωνήεντα, ἑπτὰ δὲ χορδαὶ ἡ ἁρμονία, ἑπτὰ δὲ αἱ πλειάδες, ἐν ἑπτὰ δὲ ὀδόντας βάλλει (ἔνιά γε, ἔνια δʼ οὔ), ἑπτὰ δὲ οἱ ἐπὶ Θήβας. ἆρʼ οὖν ὅτι τοιοσδὶ ὁ ἀριθμὸς πέφυκεν, διὰ τοῦτο ἢ ἐκεῖνοι ἐγένοντο ἑπτὰ ἢ ἡ πλειὰς ἑπτὰ ἀστέρων ἐστίν; ἢ οἱ μὲν διὰ τὰς πύλας ἢ ἄλλην τινὰ αἰτίαν, τὴν δὲ ἡμεῖς οὕτως ἀριθμοῦμεν, τὴν δὲ ἄρκτον γε δώδεκα, οἱ δὲ πλείους· ἐπεὶ καὶ τὸ ΞΨΖ συμφωνίας φασὶν εἶναι, καὶ ὅτι ἐκεῖναι τρεῖς, καὶ ταῦτα τρία· ὅτι δὲ μυρία ἂν εἴη τοιαῦτα, οὐθὲν μέλει (τῷ γὰρ Γ καὶ Ρ εἴη ἂν ἓν σημεῖον)· εἰ δʼ ὅτι διπλάσιον τῶν ἄλλων ἕκαστον, ἄλλο δʼ οὔ, αἴτιον δʼ ὅτι τριῶν ὄντων τόπων ἓν ἐφʼ ἑκάστου ἐπιφέρεται τῷ σίγμα, διὰ τοῦτο τρία μόνον ἐστὶν ἀλλʼ οὐχ ὅτι αἱ συμφωνίαι τρεῖς, ἐπεὶ πλείους γε αἱ συμφωνίαι, ἐνταῦθα δʼ οὐκέτι δύναται. ὅμοιοι δὴ καὶ οὗτοι τοῖς ἀρχαίοις Ὁμηρικοῖς, οἳ μικρὰς ὁμοιότητας ὁρῶσι μεγάλας δὲ παρορῶσιν. λέγουσι δέ τινες ὅτι πολλὰ τοιαῦτα, οἷον αἵ τε μέσαι ἡ μὲν ἐννέα ἡ δὲ ὀκτώ, καὶ τὸ ἔπος δεκαεπτά, ἰσάριθμον τούτοις, βαίνεται δʼ ἐν μὲν τῷ δεξιῷ ἐννέα συλλαβαῖς, ἐν δὲ τῷ ἀριστερῷ ὀκτώ·  καὶ ὅτι ἴσον τὸ διάστημα ἔν τε τοῖς γράμμασιν ἀπὸ τοῦ Α πρὸς τὸ Ω, καὶ ἀπὸ τοῦ βόμβυκος ἐπὶ τὴν ὀξυτάτην  ἐν αὐλοῖς, ἧς ὁ ἀριθμὸς ἴσος τῇ οὐλομελείᾳ τοῦ οὐρανοῦ. ὁρᾶν δὲ δεῖ μὴ τοιαῦτα οὐθεὶς ἂν ἀπορήσειεν οὔτε λέγειν οὔθʼ εὑρίσκειν ἐν τοῖς ἀϊδίοις, ἐπεὶ καὶ ἐν τοῖς φθαρτοῖς. ἀλλʼ αἱ ἐν τοῖς ἀριθμοῖς φύσεις αἱ ἐπαινούμεναι καὶ τὰ τούτοις ἐναντία καὶ ὅλως τὰ ἐν τοῖς μαθήμασιν, ὡς μὲν λέγουσί τινες καὶ αἴτια ποιοῦσι τῆς φύσεως, ἔοικεν οὑτωσί γε σκοπουμένοις διαφεύγειν (κατʼ οὐδένα γὰρ τρόπον τῶν διωρισμένων περὶ τὰς ἀρχὰς οὐδὲν αὐτῶν αἴτιον)· ἔστιν ὡς μέντοι ποιοῦσι φανερὸν ὅτι τὸ εὖ ὑπάρχει καὶ τῆς συστοιχίας ἐστὶ τῆς τοῦ καλοῦ τὸ περιττόν, τὸ εὐθύ, τὸ ἰσάκις ἴσον, αἱ δυνάμεις ἐνίων ἀριθμῶν· ἅμα γὰρ ὧραι καὶ ἀριθμὸς τοιοσδί· καὶ τὰ ἄλλα δὴ ὅσα συνάγουσιν ἐκ τῶν μαθηματικῶν θεωρημάτων πάντα ταύτην ἔχει τὴν δύναμιν. διὸ καὶ ἔοικε συμπτώμασιν· ἔστι γὰρ συμβεβηκότα μέν, ἀλλʼ οἰκεῖα ἀλλήλοις πάντα, ἓν δὲ τῷ ἀνάλογον· ἐν ἑκάστῃ γὰρ τοῦ ὄντος κατηγορίᾳ ἐστὶ τὸ ἀνάλογον, ὡς εὐθὺ ἐν μήκει οὕτως ἐν πλάτει τὸ ὁμαλόν, ἴσως ἐν ἀριθμῷ τὸ περιττόν, ἐν δὲ χροιᾷ τὸ λευκόν.—ἔτι οὐχ οἱ ἐν τοῖς εἴδεσιν ἀριθμοὶ αἴτιοι τῶν ἁρμονικῶν καὶ τῶν τοιούτων (διαφέρουσι γὰρ ἐκεῖνοι ἀλλήλων οἱ ἴσοι εἴδει· καὶ γὰρ αἱ μονάδες)· ὥστε διά γε ταῦτα εἴδη οὐ ποιητέον. τὰ μὲν οὖν συμβαίνοντα ταῦτά τε κἂν ἔτι πλείω συναχθείη· ἔοικε δὲ τεκμήριον εἶναι τὸ πολλὰ κακοπαθεῖν περὶ τὴν γένεσιν αὐτῶν καὶ μηδένα τρόπον δύνασθαι συνεῖραι τοῦ μὴ χωριστὰ εἶναι τὰ μαθηματικὰ τῶν αἰσθητῶν, ὡς ἔνιοι λέγουσι, μηδὲ ταύτας εἶναι τὰς ἀρχάς.","paragraphs":[{"id":"RAW_DAZY_BO_6:P0001","text":"περὶ μὲν οὖν τῆς οὐσίας ταύτης εἰρήσθω τοσαῦτα, πάντες δὲ ποιοῦσι τὰς ἀρχὰς ἐναντίας, ὥσπερ ἐν τοῖς φυσικοῖς, καὶ περὶ τὰς ἀκινήτους οὐσίας ὁμοίως. εἰ δὲ τῆς τῶν ἁπάντων ἀρχῆς μὴ ἐνδέχεται πρότερόν τι εἶναι, ἀδύνατον ἂν εἴη τὴν ἀρχὴν ἕτερόν τι οὖσαν εἶναι ἀρχήν, οἷον εἴ τις λέγοι τὸ λευκὸν ἀρχὴν εἶναι οὐχ ᾗ ἕτερον ἀλλʼ ᾗ λευκόν, εἶναι μέντοι καθʼ ὑποκειμένου καὶ ἕτερόν τι ὂν λευκὸν εἶναι· ἐκεῖνο γὰρ πρότερον ἔσται. ἀλλὰ μὴν γίγνεται πάντα ἐξ ἐναντίων ὡς ὑποκειμένου τινός· ἀνάγκη ἄρα μάλιστα τοῖς ἐναντίοις τοῦθʼ ὑπάρχειν.  ἀεὶ ἄρα πάντα τὰ ἐναντία καθʼ ὑποκειμένου καὶ οὐθὲν χωριστόν, ἀλλʼ ὥσπερ καὶ φαίνεται οὐθὲν οὐσίᾳ ἐναντίον, καὶ ὁ λόγος μαρτυρεῖ.","kind":"passage"},{"id":"RAW_DAZY_BO_6:P0002","text":"οὐθὲν ἄρα τῶν ἐναντίων κυρίως ἀρχὴ πάντων ἀλλʼ ἑτέρα.—οἱ δὲ τὸ ἕτερον τῶν ἐναντίων ὕλην ποιοῦσιν, οἱ μὲν τῷ ἑνὶ  τὸ ἄνισον, ὡς τοῦτο τὴν τοῦ πλήθους οὖσαν φύσιν, οἱ δὲ τῷ ἑνὶ τὸ πλῆθος (γεννῶνται γὰρ οἱ ἀριθμοὶ τοῖς μὲν ἐκ τῆς τοῦ ἀνίσου δυάδος, τοῦ μεγάλου καὶ μικροῦ, τῷ δʼ ἐκ τοῦ πλήθους, ὑπὸ τῆς τοῦ ἑνὸς δὲ οὐσίας ἀμφοῖν)· καὶ γὰρ ὁ τὸ ἄνισον καὶ ἓν λέγων τὰ στοιχεῖα, τὸ δʼ ἄνισον ἐκ μεγάλου καὶ μικροῦ δυάδα, ὡς ἓν ὄντα τὸ ἄνισον καὶ τὸ μέγα καὶ τὸ μικρὸν λέγει, καὶ οὐ διορίζει ὅτι λόγῳ ἀριθμῷ δʼ οὔ. ἀλλὰ μὴν καὶ τὰς ἀρχὰς ἃς στοιχεῖα καλοῦσιν οὐ καλῶς ἀποδιδόασιν, οἱ μὲν τὸ μέγα καὶ τὸ μικρὸν λέγοντες μετὰ τοῦ ἑνός, τρία ταῦτα στοιχεῖα τῶν ἀριθμῶν, τὰ μὲν δύο ὕλην τὸ δʼ ἓν τὴν μορφήν, οἱ δὲ τὸ πολὺ καὶ ὀλίγον, ὅτι τὸ μέγα καὶ τὸ μικρὸν μεγέθους οἰκειότερα τὴν φύσιν, οἱ δὲ τὸ καθόλου μᾶλλον ἐπὶ τούτων, τὸ ὑπερέχον καὶ τὸ ὑπερεχόμενον.","kind":"passage"},{"id":"RAW_DAZY_BO_6:P0003","text":"διαφέρει δὲ τούτων οὐθὲν ὡς εἰπεῖν πρὸς ἔνια τῶν συμβαινόντων, ἀλλὰ πρὸς τὰς λογικὰς μόνον δυσχερείας, ἃς φυλάττονται διὰ τὸ καὶ αὐτοὶ λογικὰς φέρειν τὰς ἀποδείξεις. πλὴν τοῦ αὐτοῦ γε λόγου ἐστὶ τὸ ὑπερέχον καὶ ὑπερεχόμενον εἶναι ἀρχὰς ἀλλὰ μὴ τὸ μέγα καὶ τὸ μικρόν, καὶ τὸν ἀριθμὸν πρότερον τῆς δυάδος ἐκ τῶν στοιχείων· καθόλου γὰρ ἀμφότερα μᾶλλόν ἐστιν. νῦν δὲ τὸ μὲν λέγουσι τὸ δʼ οὐ λέγουσιν. οἱ δὲ τὸ ἕτερον καὶ τὸ ἄλλο πρὸς τὸ ἓν ἀντιτιθέασιν, οἱ δὲ πλῆθος καὶ τὸ ἕν. εἰ δέ ἐστιν, ὥσπερ βούλονται, τὰ ὄντα ἐξ ἐναντίων, τῷ δὲ ἑνὶ ἢ οὐθὲν ἐναντίον ἢ εἴπερ ἄρα μέλλει, τὸ πλῆθος, τὸ δʼ ἄνισον τῷ ἴσῳ καὶ τὸ ἕτερον τῷ ταὐτῷ καὶ τὸ ἄλλο αὐτῷ, μάλιστα μὲν οἱ τὸ ἓν τῷ πλήθει ἀντιτιθέντες ἔχονταί τινος δόξης, οὐ μὴν οὐδʼ οὗτοι ἱκανῶς· ἔσται γὰρ τὸ ἓν ὀλίγον· πλῆθος μὲν γὰρ ὀλιγότητι τὸ δὲ πολὺ τῷ ὀλίγῳ ἀντίκειται.—τὸ δʼ ἓν ὅτι μέτρον σημαίνει, φανερόν.","kind":"passage"},{"id":"RAW_DAZY_BO_6:P0004","text":"καὶ ἐν παντὶ ἔστι τι ἕτερον ὑποκείμενον, οἷον ἐν ἁρμονίᾳ δίεσις, ἐν δὲ μεγέθει δάκτυλος ἢ ποὺς ἤ τι τοιοῦτον, ἐν δὲ ῥυθμοῖς βάσις ἢ συλλαβή· ὁμοίως δὲ καὶ ἐν βάρει σταθμός τις ὡρισμένος ἐστίν· καὶ κατὰ πάντων δὲ τὸν αὐτὸν τρόπον,  ἐν μὲν τοῖς ποιοῖς ποιόν τι, ἐν δὲ τοῖς ποσοῖς ποσόν τι, καὶ ἀδιαίρετον τὸ μέτρον, τὸ μὲν κατὰ τὸ εἶδος τὸ δὲ πρὸς τὴν αἴσθησιν, ὡς οὐκ ὄντος τινὸς τοῦ ἑνὸς καθʼ αὑτὸ οὐσίας. καὶ τοῦτο κατὰ λόγον· σημαίνει γὰρ τὸ ἓν ὅτι μέτρον πλήθους τινός, καὶ ὁ ἀριθμὸς ὅτι πλῆθος μεμετρημένον καὶ πλῆθος μέτρων (διὸ καὶ εὐλόγως οὐκ ἔστι τὸ ἓν ἀριθμός· οὐδὲ γὰρ τὸ μέτρον μέτρα, ἀλλʼ ἀρχὴ καὶ τὸ μέτρον καὶ τὸ ἕν). δεῖ δὲ ἀεὶ τὸ αὐτό τι ὑπάρχειν πᾶσι τὸ μέτρον, οἷον εἰ ἵπποι, τὸ μέτρον ἵππος, καὶ εἰ ἄνθρωποι, ἄνθρωπος. εἰ δʼ ἄνθρωπος καὶ ἵππος καὶ θεός, ζῷον ἴσως, καὶ ὁ ἀριθμὸς αὐτῶν ἔσται ζῷα.","kind":"passage"},{"id":"RAW_DAZY_BO_6:P0005","text":"εἰ δʼ ἄνθρωπος καὶ λευκὸν καὶ βαδίζον, ἥκιστα μὲν ἀριθμὸς τούτων διὰ τὸ ταὐτῷ πάντα ὑπάρχειν καὶ ἑνὶ κατὰ ἀριθμόν, ὅμως δὲ γενῶν ἔσται ὁ ἀριθμὸς ὁ τούτων, ἤ τινος ἄλλης τοιαύτης προσηγορίας.","kind":"passage"},{"id":"RAW_DAZY_BO_6:P0006","text":"οἱ δὲ τὸ ἄνισον ὡς ἕν τι, τὴν δυάδα δὲ ἀόριστον ποιοῦντες μεγάλου καὶ μικροῦ, πόρρω λίαν τῶν δοκούντων καὶ δυνατῶν λέγουσιν· πάθη τε γὰρ ταῦτα καὶ συμβεβηκότα μᾶλλον ἢ ὑποκείμενα τοῖς ἀριθμοῖς καὶ τοῖς μεγέθεσίν ἐστι, τὸ πολὺ καὶ ὀλίγον ἀριθμοῦ, καὶ μέγα καὶ μικρὸν μεγέθους, ὥσπερ ἄρτιον καὶ περιττόν, καὶ λεῖον καὶ τραχύ, καὶ εὐθὺ καὶ καμπύλον· ἔτι δὲ πρὸς ταύτῃ τῇ ἁμαρτίᾳ καὶ πρός τι ἀνάγκη εἶναι τὸ μέγα καὶ τὸ μικρὸν καὶ ὅσα τοιαῦτα· τὸ δὲ πρός τι πάντων ἥκιστα φύσις τις ἢ οὐσία  ἐστι, καὶ ὑστέρα τοῦ ποιοῦ καὶ ποσοῦ· καὶ πάθος τι τοῦ ποσοῦ τὸ πρός τι, ὥσπερ ἐλέχθη, ἀλλʼ οὐχ ὕλη, εἴ τι ἕτερον καὶ τῷ ὅλως κοινῷ πρός τι καὶ τοῖς μέρεσιν αὐτοῦ καὶ εἴδεσιν. οὐθὲν γάρ ἐστιν οὔτε μέγα οὔτε μικρόν, οὔτε πολὺ οὔτε ὀλίγον, οὔτε ὅλως πρός τι, ὃ οὐχ ἕτερόν τι ὂν πολὺ ἢ ὀλίγον ἢ μέγα ἢ μικρὸν ἢ πρός τί ἐστιν.","kind":"passage"},{"id":"RAW_DAZY_BO_6:P0007","text":"σημεῖον δʼ ὅτι ἥκιστα οὐσί τις καὶ ὄν τι τὸ πρός τι τὸ μόνου μὴ εἶναι γένεσιν αὐτοῦ μηδὲ φθορὰν μηδὲ κίνησιν ὥσπερ κατὰ τὸ ποσὸν αὔξησις καὶ φθίσις, κατὰ τὸ ποιὸν ἀλλοίωσις, κατὰ τόπον φορά, κατὰ τὴν οὐσίαν ἡ ἁπλῆ γένεσις καὶ φθορά,—ἀλλʼ οὐ κατὰ τὸ πρός τι· ἄνευ γὰρ τοῦ κινηθῆναι ὁτὲ μὲν μεῖζον ὁτὲ δὲ ἔλαττον ἢ ἴσον ἔσται θατέρου κινηθέντος κατὰ τὸ ποσόν.  ἀνάγκη τε ἑκάστου ὕλην εἶναι τὸ δυνάμει τοιοῦτον, ὥστε καὶ οὐσίας· τὸ δὲ πρός τι οὔτε δυνάμει οὐσία οὔτε ἐνεργείᾳ. ἄτοπον οὖν, μᾶλλον δὲ ἀδύνατον, τὸ οὐσίας μὴ οὐσίαν ποιεῖν στοιχεῖον καὶ πρότερον· ὕστερον γὰρ πᾶσαι αἱ κατηγορίαι. ἔτι δὲ τὰ στοιχεῖα οὐ κατηγορεῖται καθʼ ὧν στοιχεῖα, τὸ δὲ πολὺ καὶ ὀλίγον καὶ χωρὶς καὶ ἅμα κατηγορεῖται ἀριθμοῦ, καὶ τὸ μακρὸν καὶ τὸ βραχὺ γραμμῆς, καὶ ἐπίπεδόν ἐστι καὶ πλατὺ καὶ στενόν.","kind":"passage"},{"id":"RAW_DAZY_BO_6:P0008","text":"εἰ δὲ δὴ καὶ ἔστι τι πλῆθος οὗ τὸ μὲν ἀεί, τὸ ὀλίγον, οἷον ἡ δυάς (εἰ γὰρ πολύ, τὸ ἓν ἂν ὀλίγον εἴη), κἂν πολὺ ἁπλῶς εἴη, οἷον ἡ δεκὰς πολύ,  εἰ ταύτης μή ἐστι πλεῖον, ἢ τὰ μύρια. πῶς οὖν ἔσται οὕτως ἐξ ὀλίγου καὶ πολλοῦ ὁ ἀριθμός; ἢ γὰρ ἄμφω ἔδει κατηγορεῖσθαι ἢ μηδέτερον· νῦν δὲ τὸ ἕτερον μόνον κατηγορεῖται.","kind":"passage"},{"id":"RAW_DAZY_BO_6:P0009","text":"ἁπλῶς δὲ δεῖ σκοπεῖν, ἆρα δυνατὸν τὰ ἀΐδια ἐκ στοιχείων συγκεῖσθαι; ὕλην γὰρ ἕξει· σύνθετον γὰρ πᾶν τὸ ἐκ στοιχείων. εἰ τοίνυν ἀνάγκη, ἐξ οὗ ἐστιν, εἰ καὶ ἀεὶ ἔστι, κἄν, εἰ ἐγένετο, ἐκ τούτου γίγνεσθαι, γίγνεται δὲ πᾶν ἐκ τοῦ δυνάμει ὄντος τοῦτο ὃ γίγνεται (οὐ γὰρ ἂν ἐγένετο ἐκ τοῦ ἀδυνάτου οὐδὲ ἦν), τὸ δὲ δυνατὸν ἐνδέχεται καὶ ἐνεργεῖν καὶ μή, εἰ καὶ ὅτι μάλιστα ἀεὶ ἔστιν ὁ ἀριθμὸς ἢ ὁτιοῦν ἄλλο ὕλην ἔχον, ἐνδέχοιτʼ ἂν μὴ εἶναι, ὥσπερ καὶ τὸ μίαν ἡμέραν ἔχον καὶ τὸ ὁποσαοῦν ἔτη· εἰ δʼ οὕτω, καὶ τὸ τοσοῦτον χρόνον οὗ μὴ ἔστι πέρας. οὐκ ἂν τοίνυν εἴη ἀΐδια, εἴπερ μὴ ἀΐδιον τὸ ἐνδεχόμενον μὴ εἶναι, καθάπερ ἐν ἄλλοις λόγοις συνέβη πραγματευθῆναι. εἰ δέ ἐστι τὸ λεγόμενον νῦν ἀληθὲς καθόλου, ὅτι οὐδεμία ἐστὶν ἀΐδιος οὐσία ἐὰν μὴ ᾖ ἐνέργεια, τὰ δὲ στοιχεῖα ὕλη τῆς οὐσίας, οὐδεμιᾶς ἂν εἴη ἀϊδίου οὐσίας στοιχεῖα ἐξ ὧν ἐστιν ἐνυπαρχόντων.","kind":"passage"},{"id":"RAW_DAZY_BO_6:P0010","text":"εἰσὶ δέ τινες οἳ δυάδα μὲν ἀόριστον ποιοῦσι τὸ μετὰ τοῦ ἑνὸς στοιχεῖον, τὸ δʼ ἄνισον δυσχεραίνουσιν εὐλόγως διὰ τὰ συμβαίνοντα ἀδύνατα· οἷς τοσαῦτα μόνον ἀφῄρηται τῶν δυσχερῶν ὅσα διὰ τὸ ποιεῖν τὸ ἄνισον καὶ τὸ πρός τι στοιχεῖον ἀναγκαῖα συμβαίνει τοῖς λέγουσιν· ὅσα δὲ χωρὶς ταύτης τῆς δόξης, ταῦτα κἀκείνοις ὑπάρχειν ἀναγκαῖον, ἐάν τε τὸν εἰδητικὸν ἀριθμὸν ἐξ αὐτῶν ποιῶσιν ἐάν τε τὸν μαθηματικόν.—πολλὰ μὲν οὖν τὰ αἴτια τῆς ἐπὶ ταύτας τὰς αἰτίας ἐκτροπῆς,  μάλιστα δὲ τὸ ἀπορῆσαι ἀρχαϊκῶς. ἔδοξε γὰρ αὐτοῖς πάντʼ ἔσεσθαι ἓν τὰ ὄντα, αὐτὸ τὸ ὄν, εἰ μή τις λύσει καὶ ὁμόσε βαδιεῖται τῷ Παρμενίδου λόγῳ οὐ γὰρ μήποτε τοῦτο δαμῇ, εἶναι μὴ ἐόντα, ἀλλʼ ἀνάγκη εἶναι τὸ μὴ ὂν δεῖξαι ὅτι ἔστιν· οὕτω γάρ, ἐκ τοῦ ὄντος καὶ ἄλλου τινός, τὰ ὄντα ἔσεσθαι, εἰ πολλά ἐστιν.","kind":"passage"},{"id":"RAW_DAZY_BO_6:P0011","text":"καίτοι πρῶτον μέν, εἰ τὸ ὂν πολλαχῶς (τὸ μὲν γὰρ  οὐσίαν σημαίνει, τὸ δʼ ὅτι ποιόν, τὸ δʼ ὅτι ποσόν, καὶ τὰς ἄλλας δὴ κατηγορίας), ποῖον οὖν τὰ ὄντα πάντα ἕν, εἰ μὴ τὸ μὴ ὂν ἔσται; πότερον αἱ οὐσίαι, ἢ τὰ πάθη καὶ τὰ ἄλλα δὴ ὁμοίως, ἢ πάντα, καὶ ἔσται ἓν τὸ τόδε καὶ τὸ τοιόνδε καὶ τὸ τοσόνδε καὶ τὰ ἄλλα ὅσα ἕν τι σημαίνει; ἀλλʼ ἄτοπον, μᾶλλον δὲ ἀδύνατον, τὸ μίαν φύσιν τινὰ γενομένην αἰτίαν εἶναι τοῦ τοῦ ὄντος τὸ μὲν τόδε εἶναι τὸ δὲ τοιόνδε τὸ δὲ τοσόνδε τὸ δὲ πού. ἔπειτα ἐκ ποίου μὴ ὄντος καὶ ὄντος τὰ ὄντα; πολλαχῶς γὰρ καὶ τὸ μὴ ὄν, ἐπειδὴ καὶ τὸ ὄν· καὶ τὸ μὲν μὴ ἄνθρωπον εἶναι σημαίνει τὸ μὴ εἶναι τοδί, τὸ δὲ μὴ εὐθὺ τὸ μὴ εἶναι τοιονδί, τὸ δὲ μὴ τρίπηχυ τὸ μὴ εἶναι τοσονδί.","kind":"passage"},{"id":"RAW_DAZY_BO_6:P0012","text":"ἐκ ποίου οὖν ὄντος καὶ μὴ ὄντος πολλὰ τὰ ὄντα; βούλεται μὲν δὴ τὸ ψεῦδος καὶ ταύτην τὴν φύσιν λέγειν τὸ οὐκ ὄν, ἐξ οὗ καὶ τοῦ ὄντος πολλὰ τὰ ὄντα, διὸ καὶ ἐλέγετο ὅτι δεῖ ψεῦδός τι ὑποθέσθαι, ὥσπερ καὶ οἱ γεωμέτραι τὸ ποδιαίαν εἶναι τὴν μὴ ποδιαίαν· ἀδύνατον δὲ ταῦθʼ οὕτως ἔχειν, οὔτε γὰρ οἱ γεωμέτραι ψεῦδος οὐθὲν ὑποτίθενται (οὐ γὰρ ἐν τῷ συλλογισμῷ ἡ πρότασις), οὔτε ἐκ τοῦ οὕτω μὴ ὄντος τὰ ὄντα γίγνεται οὐδὲ φθείρεται. ἀλλʼ ἐπειδὴ τὸ μὲν κατὰ τὰς πτώσεις μὴ ὂν ἰσαχῶς ταῖς κατηγορίαις λέγεται, παρὰ τοῦτο δὲ τὸ ὡς ψεῦδος λέγεται  μὴ ὂν καὶ τὸ κατὰ δύναμιν, ἐκ τούτου ἡ γένεσίς ἐστιν, ἐκ τοῦ μὴ ἀνθρώπου δυνάμει δὲ ἀνθρώπου ἄνθρωπος, καὶ ἐκ τοῦ μὴ λευκοῦ δυνάμει δὲ λευκοῦ λευκόν, ὁμοίως ἐάν τε ἕν τι γίγνηται ἐάν τε πολλά.—φαίνεται δὲ ἡ ζήτησις πῶς πολλὰ τὸ ὂν τὸ κατὰ τὰς οὐσίας λεγόμενον· ἀριθμοὶ γὰρ καὶ μήκη καὶ σώματα τὰ γεννώμενά ἐστιν.","kind":"passage"},{"id":"RAW_DAZY_BO_6:P0013","text":"ἄτοπον δὴ τὸ ὅπως μὲν πολλὰ τὸ ὂν τὸ τί ἐστι ζητῆσαι, πῶς δὲ ἢ ποιὰ ἢ ποσά, μή. οὐ γὰρ δὴ ἡ δυὰς ἡ ἀόριστος αἰτία οὐδὲ τὸ μέγα καὶ τὸ μικρὸν τοῦ δύο λευκὰ ἢ πολλὰ εἶναι χρώματα ἢ χυμοὺς ἢ σχήματα·  ἀριθμοὶ γὰρ ἂν καὶ ταῦτα ἦσαν καὶ μονάδες. ἀλλὰ μὴν εἴ γε ταῦτʼ ἐπῆλθον, εἶδον ἂν τὸ αἴτιον καὶ τὸ ἐν ἐκείνοις· τὸ γὰρ αὐτὸ καὶ τὸ ἀνάλογον αἴτιον. αὕτη γὰρ ἡ παρέκβασις αἰτία καὶ τοῦ τὸ ἀντικείμενον ζητοῦντας τῷ ὄντι καὶ τῷ ἑνί, ἐξ οὗ καὶ τούτων τὰ ὄντα, τὸ πρός τι καὶ τὸ ἄνισον ὑποθεῖναι, ὃ οὔτʼ ἐναντίον οὔτʼ ἀπόφασις ἐκείνων, μία τε φύσις τῶν ὄντων ὥσπερ καὶ τὸ τί καὶ τὸ ποῖον. καὶ ζητεῖν ἔδει καὶ τοῦτο, πῶς πολλὰ τὰ πρός τι ἀλλʼ οὐχ ἕν· νῦν δὲ πῶς μὲν πολλαὶ μονάδες παρὰ τὸ πρῶτον ἓν ζητεῖται, πῶς δὲ πολλὰ ἄνισα παρὰ τὸ ἄνισον οὐκέτι.","kind":"passage"},{"id":"RAW_DAZY_BO_6:P0014","text":"καίτοι χρῶνται καὶ λέγουσι μέγα μικρόν, πολὺ ὀλίγον, ἐξ ὧν οἱ ἀριθμοί, μακρὸν βραχύ, ἐξ ὧν τὸ μῆκος, πλατὺ στενόν, ἐξ ὧν τὸ ἐπίπεδον, βαθὺ ταπεινόν, ἐξ ὧν οἱ ὄγκοι· καὶ ἔτι δὴ πλείω εἴδη λέγουσι τοῦ πρός τι· τούτοις δὴ τί αἴτιον τοῦ πολλὰ εἶναι;—ἀνάγκη μὲν οὖν, ὥσπερ λέγομεν, ὑποθεῖναι τὸ δυνάμει ὂν ἑκάστῳ (τοῦτο δὲ προσαπεφήνατο ὁ ταῦτα λέγων, τί τὸ δυνάμει τόδε καὶ οὐσία, μὴ ὂν δὲ καθʼ αὑτό, ὅτι τὸ πρός τι, ὥσπερ εἰ εἶπε τὸ ποιόν, ὃ οὔτε δυνάμει ἐστὶ τὸ ἓν ἢ τὸ ὂν οὔτε ἀπόφασις τοῦ ἑνὸς οὐδὲ τοῦ ὄντος ἀλλʼ ἕν τι τῶν ὄντων), πολύ τε μᾶλλον, ὥσπερ ἐλέχθη, εἰ ἐζήτει πῶς πολλὰ τὰ ὄντα, μὴ τὰ ἐν τῇ αὐτῇ κατηγορίᾳ ζητεῖν, πῶς πολλαὶ οὐσίαι ἢ πολλὰ ποιά, ἀλλὰ πῶς πολλὰ τὰ ὄντα· τὰ μὲν γὰρ οὐσίαι τὰ δὲ πάθη τὰ δὲ πρός τι.","kind":"passage"},{"id":"RAW_DAZY_BO_6:P0015","text":"ἐπὶ μὲν οὖν τῶν ἄλλων κατηγοριῶν ἔχει τινὰ καὶ ἄλλην ἐπίστασιν πῶς πολλά (διὰ γὰρ τὸ μὴ χωριστὰ εἶναι τῷ τὸ ὑποκείμενον πολλὰ γίγνεσθαι καὶ εἶναι ποιά τε πολλὰ  καὶ ποσά· καίτοι δεῖ γέ τινα εἶναι ὕλην ἑκάστῳ γένει, πλὴν χωριστὴν ἀδύνατον τῶν οὐσιῶν)· ἀλλʼ ἐπὶ τῶν τόδε τι ἔχει τινὰ λόγον πῶς πολλὰ τὸ τόδε τι, εἰ μή τι ἔσται καὶ τόδε τι καὶ φύσις τις τοιαύτη· αὕτη δέ ἐστιν ἐκεῖθεν μᾶλλον ἡ ἀπορία, πῶς πολλαὶ ἐνεργείᾳ οὐσίαι ἀλλʼ οὐ μία. ἀλλὰ μὴν καὶ εἰ μὴ ταὐτόν ἐστι τὸ τόδε καὶ τὸ ποσόν, οὐ λέγεται πῶς καὶ διὰ τί πολλὰ τὰ ὄντα, ἀλλὰ πῶς ποσὰ πολλά. ὁ γὰρ ἀριθμὸς πᾶς ποσόν τι σημαίνει, καὶ ἡ μονάς, εἰ μὴ μέτρον καὶ τὸ κατὰ τὸ ποσὸν ἀδιαίρετον. εἰ μὲν οὖν ἕτερον τὸ ποσὸν καὶ τὸ τί ἐστιν, οὐ λέγεται τὸ τί ἐστιν ἐκ τίνος οὐδὲ πῶς πολλά·  εἰ δὲ ταὐτό, πολλὰς ὑπομένει ὁ λέγων ἐναντιώσεις.—ἐπιστήσειε δʼ ἄν τις τὴν σκέψιν καὶ περὶ τῶν ἀριθμῶν πόθεν δεῖ λαβεῖν τὴν πίστιν ὡς εἰσίν.","kind":"passage"},{"id":"RAW_DAZY_BO_6:P0016","text":"τῷ μὲν γὰρ ἰδέας τιθεμένῳ παρέχονταί τινʼ αἰτίαν τοῖς οὖσιν, εἴπερ ἕκαστος τῶν ἀριθμῶν ἰδέα τις ἡ δʼ ἰδέα τοῖς ἄλλοις αἰτία τοῦ εἶναι ὃν δή ποτε τρόπον (ἔστω γὰρ ὑποκείμενον αὐτοῖς τοῦτο)· τῷ δὲ τοῦτον μὲν τὸν τρόπον οὐκ οἰομένῳ διὰ τὸ τὰς ἐνούσας δυσχερείας ὁρᾶν περὶ τὰς ἰδέας ὥστε διά γε ταῦτα μὴ ποιεῖν ἀριθμούς, ποιοῦντι δὲ ἀριθμὸν τὸν μαθηματικόν, πόθεν τε χρὴ πιστεῦσαι ὡς ἔστι τοιοῦτος ἀριθμός, καὶ τί τοῖς ἄλλοις χρήσιμος; οὐθενὸς γὰρ οὔτε φησὶν ὁ λέγων αὐτὸν εἶναι, ἀλλʼ ὡς αὐτήν τινα λέγει καθʼ αὑτὴν φύσιν οὖσαν, οὔτε φαίνεται ὢν αἴτιος· τὰ γὰρ θεωρήματα τῶν ἀριθμητικῶν πάντα καὶ κατὰ τῶν αἰσθητῶν ὑπάρξει, καθάπερ ἐλέχθη.","kind":"passage"},{"id":"RAW_DAZY_BO_6:P0017","text":"οἱ μὲν οὖν τιθέμενοι τὰς ἰδέας εἶναι, καὶ ἀριθμοὺς αὐτὰς εἶναι, τῷ κατὰ τὴν ἔκθεσιν ἑκάστου παρὰ τὰ πολλὰ λαμβάνειν  ἕν τι ἕκαστον πειρῶνταί γε λέγειν πως διὰ τί ἔστιν, οὐ μὴν ἀλλὰ ἐπεὶ οὔτε ἀναγκαῖα οὔτε δυνατὰ ταῦτα, οὐδὲ τὸν ἀριθμὸν διά γε ταῦτα εἶναι λεκτέον· οἱ δὲ Πυθαγόρειοι διὰ τὸ ὁρᾶν πολλὰ τῶν ἀριθμῶν πάθη ὑπάρχοντα τοῖς αἰσθητοῖς σώμασιν, εἶναι μὲν ἀριθμοὺς ἐποίησαν τὰ ὄντα, οὐ χωριστοὺς δέ, ἀλλʼ ἐξ ἀριθμῶν τὰ ὄντα· διὰ τί δέ; ὅτι τὰ πάθη τὰ τῶν ἀριθμῶν ἐν ἁρμονίᾳ ὑπάρχει καὶ ἐν τῷ οὐρανῷ καὶ ἐν πολλοῖς ἄλλοις. τοῖς δὲ τὸν μαθηματικὸν μόνον λέγουσιν εἶναι ἀριθμὸν οὐθὲν τοιοῦτον ἐνδέχεται λέγειν κατὰ τὰς ὑποθέσεις, ἀλλʼ ὅτι οὐκ ἔσονται αὐτῶν αἱ ἐπιστῆμαι ἐλέγετο. ἡμεῖς δέ φαμεν εἶναι, καθάπερ εἴπομεν πρότερον. καὶ δῆλον ὅτι οὐ κεχώρισται τὰ μαθηματικά· οὐ γὰρ ἂν κεχωρισμένων τὰ πάθη ὑπῆρχεν ἐν τοῖς σώμασιν.","kind":"passage"},{"id":"RAW_DAZY_BO_6:P0018","text":"οἱ μὲν οὖν Πυθαγόρειοι κατὰ μὲν τὸ τοιοῦτον οὐθενὶ ἔνοχοί εἰσιν, κατὰ μέντοι τὸ ποιεῖν ἐξ ἀριθμῶν τὰ φυσικὰ σώματα, ἐκ μὴ ἐχόντων βάρος μηδὲ κουφότητα ἔχοντα κουφότητα καὶ βάρος, ἐοίκασι περὶ ἄλλου οὐρανοῦ λέγειν καὶ σωμάτων ἀλλʼ οὐ τῶν αἰσθητῶν· οἱ δὲ χωριστὸν ποιοῦντες, ὅτι ἐπὶ τῶν αἰσθητῶν οὐκ ἔσται τὰ ἀξιώματα, ἀληθῆ δὲ τὰ λεγόμενα καὶ σαίνει τὴν ψυχήν, εἶναί τε ὑπολαμβάνουσι καὶ χωριστὰ εἶναι· ὁμοίως δὲ καὶ τὰ μεγέθη τὰ μαθηματικά.  δῆλον οὖν ὅτι καὶ ὁ ἐναντιούμενος λόγος τἀναντία ἐρεῖ, καὶ ὃ ἄρτι ἠπορήθη λυτέον τοῖς οὕτω λέγουσι, διὰ τί οὐδαμῶς ἐν τοῖς αἰσθητοῖς ὑπαρχόντων τὰ πάθη ὑπάρχει αὐτῶν ἐν τοῖς αἰσθητοῖς. εἰσὶ δέ τινες οἳ ἐκ τοῦ πέρατα εἶναι καὶ ἔσχατα τὴν στιγμὴν μὲν γραμμῆς, ταύτην δʼ ἐπιπέδου, τοῦτο δὲ τοῦ στερεοῦ, οἴονται εἶναι ἀνάγκην τοιαύτας φύσεις εἶναι. δεῖ δὴ καὶ τοῦτον ὁρᾶν τὸν λόγον, μὴ λίαν ᾖ μαλακός.","kind":"passage"},{"id":"RAW_DAZY_BO_6:P0019","text":"οὔτε γὰρ οὐσίαι εἰσὶ τὰ ἔσχατα ἀλλὰ μᾶλλον πάντα ταῦτα πέρατα (ἑπεὶ καὶ τῆς βαδίσεως καὶ ὅλως κινήσεως ἔστι τι πέρας· τοῦτʼ οὖν ἔσται τόδε τι καὶ οὐσία τις· ἀλλʼ ἄτοπον)·—οὐ μὴν ἀλλὰ εἰ καὶ εἰσί, τῶνδε τῶν αἰσθητῶν ἔσονται πάντα (ἐπὶ τούτων γὰρ ὁ λόγος εἴρηκεν)· διὰ τί οὖν χωριστὰ ἔσται;—ἔτι δὲ ἐπιζητήσειεν ἄν τις μὴ λίαν εὐχερὴς ὢν περὶ μὲν τοῦ ἀριθμοῦ παντὸς καὶ τῶν μαθηματικῶν τὸ μηθὲν συμβάλλεσθαι ἀλλήλοις τὰ πρότερα τοῖς ὕστερον (μὴ ὄντος γὰρ τοῦ ἀριθμοῦ οὐθὲν ἧττον τὰ μεγέθη ἔσται τοῖς τὰ μαθηματικὰ μόνον εἶναι φαμένοις, καὶ τούτων μὴ ὄντων ἡ ψυχὴ καὶ τὰ σώματα τὰ αἰσθητά· οὐκ ἔοικε δʼ ἡ φύσις ἐπεισοδιώδης οὖσα ἐκ τῶν φαινομένων, ὥσπερ μοχθηρὰ τραγῳδία)· τοῖς δὲ τὰς ἰδέας τιθεμένοις τοῦτο μὲν ἐκφεύγει—ποιοῦσι γὰρ τὰ μεγέθη ἐκ τῆς ὕλης καὶ ἀριθμοῦ, ἐκ μὲν τῆς δυάδος τὰ μήκη, ἐκ τριάδος δʼ ἴσως τὰ ἐπίπεδα, ἐκ δὲ τῆς τετράδος τὰ στερεὰ ἢ καὶ ἐξ ἄλλων ἀριθμῶν· διαφέρει γὰρ οὐθέν—,","kind":"passage"},{"id":"RAW_DAZY_BO_6:P0020","text":"ἀλλὰ ταῦτά γε πότερον ἰδέαι ἔσονται, ἢ τίς ὁ τρόπος αὐτῶν, καὶ τί συμβάλλονται τοῖς οὖσιν; οὐθὲν γάρ, ὥσπερ οὐδὲ τὰ μαθηματικά, οὐδὲ ταῦτα συμβάλλεται. ἀλλὰ μὴν οὐδʼ ὑπάρχει γε κατʼ αὐτῶν οὐθὲν θεώρημα, ἐὰν μή τις βούληται κινεῖν τὰ μαθηματικὰ καὶ ποιεῖν ἰδίας τινὰς δόξας. ἔστι δʼ οὐ χαλεπὸν ὁποιασοῦν ὑποθέσεις λαμβάνοντας μακροποιεῖν καὶ συνείρειν. οὗτοι μὲν οὖν ταύτῃ προσγλιχόμενοι ταῖς ἰδέαις τὰ μαθηματικὰ διαμαρτάνουσιν· οἱ δὲ πρῶτοι δύο τοὺς ἀριθμοὺς ποιήσαντες, τόν τε τῶν εἰδῶν καὶ τὸν μαθηματικόν, οὔτʼ εἰρήκασιν οὔτʼ ἔχοιεν ἂν εἰπεῖν πῶς καὶ ἐκ τίνος ἔσται ὁ μαθηματικός. ποιοῦσι γὰρ αὐτὸν μεταξὺ τοῦ εἰδητικοῦ καὶ τοῦ αἰσθητοῦ.","kind":"passage"},{"id":"RAW_DAZY_BO_6:P0021","text":"εἰ μὲν γὰρ ἐκ τοῦ μεγάλου καὶ μικροῦ, ὁ αὐτὸς ἐκείνῳ ἔσται τῷ τῶν ἰδεῶν (ἐξ ἄλλου δέ τινος μικροῦ καὶ μεγάλου τὰ  μεγέθη ποιεῖ)·  εἰ δʼ ἕτερόν τι ἐρεῖ, πλείω τὰ στοιχεῖα ἐρεῖ· καὶ εἰ ἕν τι ἑκατέρου ἡ ἀρχή, κοινόν τι ἐπὶ τούτων ἔσται τὸ ἕν, ζητητέον τε πῶς καὶ ταῦτα πολλὰ τὸ ἓν καὶ ἅμα τὸν ἀριθμὸν γενέσθαι ἄλλως ἢ ἐξ ἑνὸς καὶ δυάδος ἀορίστου ἀδύνατον κατʼ ἐκεῖνον. πάντα δὴ ταῦτα ἄλογα, καὶ μάχεται καὶ αὐτὰ ἑαυτοῖς καὶ τοῖς εὐλόγοις, καὶ ἔοικεν ἐν αὐτοῖς εἶναι ὁ Σιμωνίδου μακρὸς λόγος· γίγνεται γὰρ ὁ μακρὸς λόγος ὥσπερ ὁ τῶν δούλων ὅταν μηθὲν ὑγιὲς λέγωσιν. φαίνεται δὲ καὶ αὐτὰ τὰ στοιχεῖα τὸ μέγα καὶ τὸ μικρὸν βοᾶν ὡς ἑλκόμενα· οὐ δύναται γὰρ οὐδαμῶς γεννῆσαι τὸν ἀριθμὸν ἀλλʼ ἢ τὸν ἀφʼ ἑνὸς διπλασιαζόμενον.—ἄτοπον δὲ καὶ γένεσιν ποιεῖν ἀϊδίων ὄντων, μᾶλλον δʼ ἕν τι τῶν ἀδυνάτων.","kind":"passage"},{"id":"RAW_DAZY_BO_6:P0022","text":"οἱ μὲν οὖν Πυθαγόρειοι πότερον οὐ ποιοῦσιν ἢ ποιοῦσι γένεσιν οὐδὲν δεῖ διστάζειν· φανερῶς γὰρ λέγουσιν ὡς τοῦ ἑνὸς συσταθέντος, εἴτʼ ἐξ ἐπιπέδων εἴτʼ ἐκ χροιᾶς εἴτʼ ἐκ σπέρματος εἴτʼ ἐξ ὧν ἀποροῦσιν εἰπεῖν, εὐθὺς τὸ ἔγγιστα τοῦ ἀπείρου ὅτι εἵλκετο καὶ ἐπεραίνετο ὑπὸ τοῦ πέρατος. ἀλλʼ ἐπειδὴ κοσμοποιοῦσι καὶ φυσικῶς βούλονται λέγειν, δίκαιον αὐτοὺς ἐξετάζειν τι περὶ φύσεως, ἐκ δὲ τῆς νῦν ἀφεῖναι μεθόδου· τὰς γὰρ ἐν τοῖς ἀκινήτοις ζητοῦμεν ἀρχάς, ὥστε καὶ τῶν ἀριθμῶν τῶν τοιούτων ἐπισκεπτέον τὴν γένεσιν.","kind":"passage"},{"id":"RAW_DAZY_BO_6:P0023","text":"τοῦ μὲν οὖν περιττοῦ γένεσιν οὔ φασιν, ὡς δηλονότι τοῦ ἀρτίου οὔσης γενέσεως· τὸν δʼ ἄρτιον πρῶτον ἐξ ἀνίσων τινὲς κατασκευάζουσι τοῦ μεγάλου καὶ μικροῦ ἰσασθέντων. ἀνάγκη οὖν πρότερον ὑπάρχειν τὴν ἀνισότητα αὐτοῖς τοῦ ἰσασθῆναι· εἰ δʼ ἀεὶ ἦσαν ἰσασμένα, οὐκ ἂν ἦσαν ἄνισα πρότερον (τοῦ γὰρ ἀεὶ οὐκ ἔστι πρότερον οὐθέν), ὥστε φανερὸν ὅτι οὐ τοῦ θεωρῆσαι ἕνεκεν ποιοῦσι τὴν γένεσιν τῶν ἀριθμῶν.—ἔχει δʼ ἀπορίαν καὶ εὐπορήσαντι ἐπιτίμησιν πῶς ἔχει πρὸς τὸ ἀγαθὸν καὶ τὸ καλὸν τὰ στοιχεῖα καὶ αἱ ἀρχαί· ἀπορίαν μὲν ταύτην, πότερόν ἐστί τι ἐκείνων οἷον βουλόμεθα λέγειν αὐτὸ τὸ ἀγαθὸν καὶ τὸ ἄριστον, ἢ οὔ, ἀλλʼ ὑστερογενῆ.","kind":"passage"},{"id":"RAW_DAZY_BO_6:P0024","text":"παρὰ μὲν γὰρ τῶν θεολόγων ἔοικεν ὁμολογεῖσθαι τῶν νῦν τισίν, οἳ οὔ φασιν, ἀλλὰ προελθούσης τῆς τῶν ὄντων φύσεως καὶ τὸ ἀγαθὸν καὶ τὸ καλὸν ἐμφαίνεσθαι (τοῦτο δὲ ποιοῦσιν εὐλαβούμενοι ἀληθινὴν δυσχέρειαν ἣ συμβαίνει τοῖς λέγουσιν, ὥσπερ ἔνιοι, τὸ ἓν ἀρχήν·  ἔστι δʼ ἡ δυσχέρεια οὐ διὰ τὸ τῇ ἀρχῇ τὸ εὖ ἀποδιδόναι ὡς ὑπάρχον, ἀλλὰ διὰ τὸ τὸ ἓν ἀρχὴν καὶ ἀρχὴν ὡς στοιχεῖον καὶ τὸν ἀριθμὸν ἐκ τοῦ ἑνός),—οἱ δὲ ποιηταὶ οἱ ἀρχαῖοι ταύτῃ ὁμοίως, ᾗ βασιλεύειν καὶ ἄρχειν φασὶν οὐ τοὺς πρώτους, οἷον νύκτα καὶ οὐρανὸν ἢ χάος ἢ ὠκεανόν, ἀλλὰ τὸν Δία· οὐ μὴν ἀλλὰ τούτοις μὲν διὰ τὸ μεταβάλλειν τοὺς ἄρχοντας τῶν ὄντων συμβαίνει τοιαῦτα λέγειν, ἐπεὶ οἵ γε μεμιγμένοι αὐτῶν  τῷ μὴ μυθικῶς πάντα λέγειν, οἷον Φερεκύδης καὶ ἕτεροί τινες, τὸ γεννῆσαν πρῶτον ἄριστον τιθέασι, καὶ οἱ Μάγοι, καὶ τῶν ὑστέρων δὲ σοφῶν οἷον Ἐμπεδοκλῆς τε καὶ Ἀναξαγόρας, ὁ μὲν τὴν φιλίαν στοιχεῖον ὁ δὲ τὸν νοῦν ἀρχὴν ποιήσας.","kind":"passage"},{"id":"RAW_DAZY_BO_6:P0025","text":"τῶν δὲ τὰς ἀκινήτους οὐσίας εἶναι λεγόντων οἱ μέν φασιν αὐτὸ τὸ ἓν τὸ ἀγαθὸν αὐτὸ εἶναι· οὐσίαν μέντοι τὸ ἓν αὐτοῦ ᾤοντο εἶναι μάλιστα.—ἡ μὲν οὖν ἀπορία αὕτη, ποτέρως δεῖ λέγειν· θαυμαστὸν δʼ εἰ τῷ πρώτῳ καὶ ἀϊδίῳ καὶ αὐταρκεστάτῳ τοῦτʼ αὐτὸ πρῶτον οὐχ ὡς ἀγαθὸν ὑπάρχει, τὸ αὔταρκες καὶ ἡ σωτηρία. ἀλλὰ μὴν οὐ διʼ ἄλλο τι ἄφθαρτον ἢ διότι εὖ ἔχει, οὐδʼ αὔταρκες, ὥστε τὸ μὲν φάναι τὴν ἀρχὴν τοιαύτην εἶναι εὔλογον ἀληθὲς εἶναι, τὸ μέντοι ταύτην εἶναι τὸ ἕν, ἢ εἰ μὴ τοῦτο, στοιχεῖόν γε καὶ στοιχεῖον ἀριθμῶν, ἀδύνατον. συμβαίνει γὰρ πολλὴ δυσχέρεια—ἣν ἔνιοι φεύγοντες ἀπειρήκασιν, οἱ τὸ ἓν μὲν ὁμολογοῦντες ἀρχὴν εἶναι πρώτην καὶ στοιχεῖον, τοῦ ἀριθμοῦ δὲ τοῦ μαθηματικοῦ —ἅπασαι γὰρ αἱ μονάδες γίγνονται ὅπερ ἀγαθόν τι, καὶ πολλή τις εὐπορία ἀγαθῶν.","kind":"passage"},{"id":"RAW_DAZY_BO_6:P0026","text":"ἔτι εἰ τὰ εἴδη ἀριθμοί, τὰ εἴδη πάντα ὅπερ ἀγαθόν τι· ἀλλὰ μὴν ὅτου βούλεται τιθέτω τις εἶναι ἰδέας· εἰ μὲν γὰρ τῶν ἀγαθῶν μόνον, οὐκ ἔσονται οὐσίαι αἱ ἰδέαι, εἰ δὲ καὶ τῶν οὐσιῶν, πάντα τὰ ζῷα καὶ τὰ φυτὰ ἀγαθὰ καὶ τὰ μετέχοντα. ταῦτά τε δὴ συμβαίνει ἄτοπα, καὶ τὸ ἐναντίον στοιχεῖον, εἴτε πλῆθος ὂν εἴτε τὸ ἄνισον καὶ μέγα καὶ μικρόν, τὸ κακὸν αὐτό (διόπερ ὁ μὲν ἔφευγε τὸ ἀγαθὸν προσάπτειν τῷ ἑνὶ ὡς ἀναγκαῖον ὄν, ἐπειδὴ ἐξ ἐναντίων ἡ γένεσις, τὸ κακὸν τὴν τοῦ πλήθους φύσιν εἶναι· οἱ δὲ λέγουσι τὸ ἄνισον τὴν τοῦ κακοῦ φύσιν)· συμβαίνει δὴ πάντα τὰ ὄντα μετέχειν τοῦ κακοῦ ἔξω ἑνὸς αὐτοῦ τοῦ ἑνός, καὶ μᾶλλον ἀκράτου μετέχειν τοὺς ἀριθμοὺς ἢ τὰ μεγέθη,  καὶ τὸ κακὸν τοῦ ἀγαθοῦ χώραν εἶναι, καὶ μετέχειν καὶ ὀρέγεσθαι τοῦ φθαρτικοῦ· φθαρτικὸν γὰρ τοῦ ἐναντίου τὸ ἐναντίον.","kind":"passage"},{"id":"RAW_DAZY_BO_6:P0027","text":"καὶ εἰ ὥσπερ ἐλέγομεν ὅτι ἡ ὕλη ἐστὶ τὸ δυνάμει ἕκαστον, οἷον πυρὸς τοῦ ἐνεργείᾳ τὸ δυνάμει πῦρ, τὸ κακὸν ἔσται αὐτὸ τὸ δυνάμει ἀγαθόν. ταῦτα δὴ πάντα συμβαίνει, τὸ μὲν ὅτι ἀρχὴν πᾶσαν στοιχεῖον ποιοῦσι, τὸ δʼ ὅτι τἀναντία ἀρχάς, τὸ δʼ ὅτι τὸ ἓν ἀρχήν, τὸ δʼ ὅτι τοὺς ἀριθμοὺς τὰς πρώτας οὐσίας καὶ χωριστὰ καὶ εἴδη.","kind":"passage"},{"id":"RAW_DAZY_BO_6:P0028","text":"εἰ οὖν καὶ τὸ μὴ τιθέναι τὸ ἀγαθὸν ἐν ταῖς ἀρχαῖς καὶ τὸ τιθέναι οὕτως ἀδύνατον, δῆλον ὅτι αἱ ἀρχαὶ οὐκ ὀρθῶς ἀποδίδονται οὐδὲ αἱ πρῶται οὐσίαι. οὐκ ὀρθῶς δʼ ὑπολαμβάνει οὐδʼ εἴ τις παρεικάζει τὰς τοῦ ὅλου ἀρχὰς τῇ τῶν ζῴων καὶ φυτῶν, ὅτι ἐξ ἀορίστων ἀτελῶν τε ἀεὶ τὰ τελειότερα, διὸ καὶ ἐπὶ τῶν πρώτων οὕτως ἔχειν φησίν, ὥστε μηδὲ ὄν τι εἶναι τὸ ἓν αὐτό. εἰσὶ γὰρ καὶ ἐνταῦθα τέλειαι αἱ ἀρχαὶ ἐξ ὧν ταῦτα· ἄνθρωπος γὰρ ἄνθρωπον γεννᾷ, καὶ οὐκ ἔστι τὸ σπέρμα πρῶτον. ἄτοπον δὲ καὶ τὸ τόπον ἅμα τοῖς στερεοῖς τοῖς μαθηματικοῖς ποιῆσαι (ὁ μὲν γὰρ τόπος τῶν καθʼ ἕκαστον ἴδιος, διὸ χωριστὰ τόπῳ, τὰ δὲ μαθηματικὰ οὐ πού), καὶ τὸ εἰπεῖν μὲν ὅτι ποὺ ἔσται, τί δέ ἐστιν ὁ τόπος μή.—ἔδει δὲ τοὺς λέγοντας ἐκ στοιχείων εἶναι τὰ ὄντα καὶ τῶν ὄντων τὰ πρῶτα τοὺς ἀριθμούς, διελομένους πῶς ἄλλο ἐξ ἄλλου ἐστίν, οὕτω λέγειν τίνα τρόπον ὁ ἀριθμός ἐστιν ἐκ τῶν ἀρχῶν.","kind":"passage"},{"id":"RAW_DAZY_BO_6:P0029","text":"πότερον μίξει; ἀλλʼ οὔτε πᾶν μικτόν, τό τε γιγνόμενον ἕτερον, οὐκ ἔσται τε χωριστὸν τὸ ἓν οὐδʼ ἑτέρα φύσις· οἱ δὲ βούλονται. ἀλλὰ συνθέσει, ὥσπερ συλλαβή; ἀλλὰ θέσιν τε ἀνάγκη ὑπάρχειν, καὶ χωρὶς ὁ νοῶν νοήσει τὸ ἓν καὶ τὸ πλῆθος. τοῦτʼ οὖν ἔσται ὁ ἀριθμός, μονὰς καὶ πλῆθος, ἢ τὸ ἓν καὶ ἄνισον. καὶ ἐπεὶ τὸ ἐκ τινῶν εἶναι ἔστι μὲν ὡς ἐνυπαρχόντων ἔστι δὲ ὡς οὔ, ποτέρως ὁ ἀριθμός; οὕτως γὰρ ὡς ἐνυπαρχόντων οὐκ ἔστιν ἀλλʼ ἢ ὧν γένεσις ἔστιν. ἀλλʼ ὡς ἀπὸ σπέρματος; ἀλλʼ οὐχ οἷόν τε τοῦ ἀδιαιρέτου τι ἀπελθεῖν. ἀλλʼ ὡς ἐκ τοῦ ἐναντίου μὴ ὑπομένοντος; ἀλλʼ ὅσα οὕτως ἔστι, καὶ ἐξ ἄλλου τινός ἐστιν ὑπομένοντος. ἐπεὶ τοίνυν τὸ ἓν ὁ μὲν τῷ πλήθει ὡς ἐναντίον τίθησιν,  ὁ δὲ τῷ ἀνίσῳ, ὡς ἴσῳ τῷ ἑνὶ χρώμενος, ὡς ἐξ ἐναντίων εἴη ἂν ὁ ἀριθμός· ἔστιν ἄρα τι ἕτερον ἐξ οὗ ὑπομένοντος καὶ θατέρου ἐστὶν ἢ γέγονεν.","kind":"passage"},{"id":"RAW_DAZY_BO_6:P0030","text":"ἔτι τί δή ποτε τὰ μὲν ἄλλʼ ὅσα ἐξ ἐναντίων ἢ οἷς ἔστιν ἐναντία φθείρεται κἂν ἐκ παντὸς ᾖ, ὁ δὲ ἀριθμὸς οὔ; περὶ τούτου γὰρ οὐθὲν λέγεται. καίτοι καὶ ἐνυπάρχον καὶ μὴ ἐνυπάρχον φθείρει τὸ ἐναντίον, οἷον τὸ νεῖκος τὸ μῖγμα (καίτοι γε οὐκ ἔδει· οὐ γὰρ ἐκείνῳ γε ἐναντίον).—οὐθὲν δὲ διώρισται οὐδὲ ὁποτέρως οἱ ἀριθμοὶ αἴτιοι τῶν οὐσιῶν καὶ τοῦ εἶναι, πότερον ὡς ὅροι (οἷον αἱ στιγμαὶ τῶν μεγεθῶν, καὶ ὡς Εὔρυτος ἔταττε τίς ἀριθμὸς τίνος, οἷον ὁδὶ μὲν ἀνθρώπου ὁδὶ δὲ ἵππου, ὥσπερ οἱ τοὺς ἀριθμοὺς ἄγοντες εἰς τὰ σχήματα τρίγωνον καὶ τετράγωνον, οὕτως ἀφομοιῶν ταῖς ψήφοις τὰς μορφὰς τῶν φυτῶν), ἢ ὅτι  λόγος ἡ συμφωνία ἀριθμῶν, ὁμοίως δὲ καὶ ἄνθρωπος καὶ τῶν ἄλλων ἕκαστον; τὰ δὲ δὴ πάθη πῶς ἀριθμοί, τὸ λευκὸν καὶ γλυκὺ καὶ τὸ θερμόν; ὅτι δὲ οὐχ οἱ ἀριθμοὶ οὐσία οὐδὲ τῆς μορφῆς αἴτιοι, δῆλον· ὁ γὰρ λόγος ἡ οὐσία, ὁ δʼ ἀριθμὸς ὕλη.","kind":"passage"},{"id":"RAW_DAZY_BO_6:P0031","text":"οἷον σαρκὸς ἢ ὀστοῦ ἀριθμὸς ἡ οὐσία οὕτω, τρία πυρὸς γῆς δὲ δύο· καὶ ἀεὶ ὁ ἀριθμὸς ὃς ἂν ᾖ τινῶν ἐστιν, ἢ πύρινος ἢ γήϊνος ἢ μοναδικός, ἀλλʼ ἡ οὐσία τὸ τοσόνδʼ εἶναι πρὸς τοσόνδε κατὰ τὴν μῖξιν· τοῦτο δʼ οὐκέτι ἀριθμὸς ἀλλὰ λόγος μίξεως ἀριθμῶν σωματικῶν ἢ ὁποιωνοῦν. οὔτε οὖν τῷ ποιῆσαι αἴτιος ὁ ἀριθμός, οὔτε ὅλως ὁ ἀριθμὸς οὔτε ὁ μοναδικός, οὔτε ὕλη οὔτε λόγος καὶ εἶδος τῶν πραγμάτων. ἀλλὰ μὴν οὐδʼ ὡς τὸ οὗ ἕνεκα.","kind":"passage"},{"id":"RAW_DAZY_BO_6:P0032","text":"ἀπορήσειε δʼ ἄν τις καὶ τί τὸ εὖ ἐστὶ τὸ ἀπὸ τῶν ἀριθμῶν τῷ ἐν ἀριθμῷ εἶναι τὴν μῖξιν, ἢ ἐν εὐλογίστῳ ἢ ἐν περιττῷ. νυνὶ γὰρ οὐθὲν ὑγιεινότερον τρὶς τρία ἂν ᾖ τὸ μελίκρατον κεκραμένον, ἀλλὰ μᾶλλον ὠφελήσειεν ἂν ἐν οὐθενὶ λόγῳ ὂν ὑδαρὲς δὲ ἢ ἐν ἀριθμῷ ἄκρατον ὄν. ἔτι οἱ λόγοι ἐν προσθέσει ἀριθμῶν εἰσὶν οἱ τῶν μίξεων, οὐκ ἐν ἀριθμοῖς, οἷον τρία πρὸς δύο ἀλλʼ οὐ τρὶς δύο. τὸ γὰρ αὐτὸ δεῖ γένος εἶναι ἐν ταῖς πολλαπλασιώσεσιν, ὥστε δεῖ μετρεῖσθαι τῷ τε Α τὸν στοῖχον ἐφʼ οὗ ΑΒΓ καὶ τῷ Δ τὸν ΔΕΖ· ὥστε τῷ αὐτῷ πάντα. οὔκουν ἔσται πυρὸς ΒΕΓΖ καὶ ὕδατος ἀριθμὸς δὶς τρία.  —εἰ δʼ ἀνάγκη πάντα ἀριθμοῦ κοινωνεῖν, ἀνάγκη πολλὰ συμβαίνειν τὰ αὐτά, καὶ ἀριθμὸν τὸν αὐτὸν τῷδε καὶ ἄλλῳ.","kind":"passage"},{"id":"RAW_DAZY_BO_6:P0033","text":"ἆρʼ οὖν τοῦτʼ αἴτιον καὶ διὰ τοῦτό ἐστι τὸ πρᾶγμα, ἢ ἄδηλον; οἷον ἔστι τις τῶν τοῦ ἡλίου φορῶν ἀριθμός, καὶ πάλιν τῶν τῆς σελήνης, καὶ τῶν ζῴων γε ἑκάστου τοῦ βίου καὶ ἡλικίας· τί οὖν κωλύει ἐνίους μὲν τούτων τετραγώνους εἶναι ἐνίους δὲ κύβους, καὶ ἴσους τοὺς δὲ διπλασίους; οὐθὲν γὰρ κωλύει, ἀλλʼ ἀνάγκη ἐν τούτοις στρέφεσθαι, εἰ ἀριθμοῦ πάντα ἐκοινώνει. ἐνεδέχετό τε τὰ διαφέροντα ὑπὸ τὸν αὐτὸν ἀριθμὸν πίπτειν· ὥστʼ εἴ τισιν ὁ αὐτὸς ἀριθμὸς συνεβεβήκει, ταὐτὰ ἂν ἦν ἀλλήλοις ἐκεῖνα τὸ αὐτὸ εἶδος ἀριθμοῦ ἔχοντα, οἷον ἥλιος καὶ σελήνη τὰ αὐτά. ἀλλὰ διὰ τί αἴτια ταῦτα; ἑπτὰ μὲν φωνήεντα, ἑπτὰ δὲ χορδαὶ ἡ ἁρμονία, ἑπτὰ δὲ αἱ πλειάδες, ἐν ἑπτὰ δὲ ὀδόντας βάλλει (ἔνιά γε, ἔνια δʼ οὔ), ἑπτὰ δὲ οἱ ἐπὶ Θήβας.","kind":"passage"},{"id":"RAW_DAZY_BO_6:P0034","text":"ἆρʼ οὖν ὅτι τοιοσδὶ ὁ ἀριθμὸς πέφυκεν, διὰ τοῦτο ἢ ἐκεῖνοι ἐγένοντο ἑπτὰ ἢ ἡ πλειὰς ἑπτὰ ἀστέρων ἐστίν; ἢ οἱ μὲν διὰ τὰς πύλας ἢ ἄλλην τινὰ αἰτίαν, τὴν δὲ ἡμεῖς οὕτως ἀριθμοῦμεν, τὴν δὲ ἄρκτον γε δώδεκα, οἱ δὲ πλείους· ἐπεὶ καὶ τὸ ΞΨΖ συμφωνίας φασὶν εἶναι, καὶ ὅτι ἐκεῖναι τρεῖς, καὶ ταῦτα τρία· ὅτι δὲ μυρία ἂν εἴη τοιαῦτα, οὐθὲν μέλει (τῷ γὰρ Γ καὶ Ρ εἴη ἂν ἓν σημεῖον)· εἰ δʼ ὅτι διπλάσιον τῶν ἄλλων ἕκαστον, ἄλλο δʼ οὔ, αἴτιον δʼ ὅτι τριῶν ὄντων τόπων ἓν ἐφʼ ἑκάστου ἐπιφέρεται τῷ σίγμα, διὰ τοῦτο τρία μόνον ἐστὶν ἀλλʼ οὐχ ὅτι αἱ συμφωνίαι τρεῖς, ἐπεὶ πλείους γε αἱ συμφωνίαι, ἐνταῦθα δʼ οὐκέτι δύναται. ὅμοιοι δὴ καὶ οὗτοι τοῖς ἀρχαίοις Ὁμηρικοῖς, οἳ μικρὰς ὁμοιότητας ὁρῶσι μεγάλας δὲ παρορῶσιν.","kind":"passage"},{"id":"RAW_DAZY_BO_6:P0035","text":"λέγουσι δέ τινες ὅτι πολλὰ τοιαῦτα, οἷον αἵ τε μέσαι ἡ μὲν ἐννέα ἡ δὲ ὀκτώ, καὶ τὸ ἔπος δεκαεπτά, ἰσάριθμον τούτοις, βαίνεται δʼ ἐν μὲν τῷ δεξιῷ ἐννέα συλλαβαῖς, ἐν δὲ τῷ ἀριστερῷ ὀκτώ·  καὶ ὅτι ἴσον τὸ διάστημα ἔν τε τοῖς γράμμασιν ἀπὸ τοῦ Α πρὸς τὸ Ω, καὶ ἀπὸ τοῦ βόμβυκος ἐπὶ τὴν ὀξυτάτην  ἐν αὐλοῖς, ἧς ὁ ἀριθμὸς ἴσος τῇ οὐλομελείᾳ τοῦ οὐρανοῦ. ὁρᾶν δὲ δεῖ μὴ τοιαῦτα οὐθεὶς ἂν ἀπορήσειεν οὔτε λέγειν οὔθʼ εὑρίσκειν ἐν τοῖς ἀϊδίοις, ἐπεὶ καὶ ἐν τοῖς φθαρτοῖς.","kind":"passage"},{"id":"RAW_DAZY_BO_6:P0036","text":"ἀλλʼ αἱ ἐν τοῖς ἀριθμοῖς φύσεις αἱ ἐπαινούμεναι καὶ τὰ τούτοις ἐναντία καὶ ὅλως τὰ ἐν τοῖς μαθήμασιν, ὡς μὲν λέγουσί τινες καὶ αἴτια ποιοῦσι τῆς φύσεως, ἔοικεν οὑτωσί γε σκοπουμένοις διαφεύγειν (κατʼ οὐδένα γὰρ τρόπον τῶν διωρισμένων περὶ τὰς ἀρχὰς οὐδὲν αὐτῶν αἴτιον)· ἔστιν ὡς μέντοι ποιοῦσι φανερὸν ὅτι τὸ εὖ ὑπάρχει καὶ τῆς συστοιχίας ἐστὶ τῆς τοῦ καλοῦ τὸ περιττόν, τὸ εὐθύ, τὸ ἰσάκις ἴσον, αἱ δυνάμεις ἐνίων ἀριθμῶν· ἅμα γὰρ ὧραι καὶ ἀριθμὸς τοιοσδί· καὶ τὰ ἄλλα δὴ ὅσα συνάγουσιν ἐκ τῶν μαθηματικῶν θεωρημάτων πάντα ταύτην ἔχει τὴν δύναμιν.","kind":"passage"},{"id":"RAW_DAZY_BO_6:P0037","text":"διὸ καὶ ἔοικε συμπτώμασιν· ἔστι γὰρ συμβεβηκότα μέν, ἀλλʼ οἰκεῖα ἀλλήλοις πάντα, ἓν δὲ τῷ ἀνάλογον· ἐν ἑκάστῃ γὰρ τοῦ ὄντος κατηγορίᾳ ἐστὶ τὸ ἀνάλογον, ὡς εὐθὺ ἐν μήκει οὕτως ἐν πλάτει τὸ ὁμαλόν, ἴσως ἐν ἀριθμῷ τὸ περιττόν, ἐν δὲ χροιᾷ τὸ λευκόν.—ἔτι οὐχ οἱ ἐν τοῖς εἴδεσιν ἀριθμοὶ αἴτιοι τῶν ἁρμονικῶν καὶ τῶν τοιούτων (διαφέρουσι γὰρ ἐκεῖνοι ἀλλήλων οἱ ἴσοι εἴδει· καὶ γὰρ αἱ μονάδες)· ὥστε διά γε ταῦτα εἴδη οὐ ποιητέον. τὰ μὲν οὖν συμβαίνοντα ταῦτά τε κἂν ἔτι πλείω συναχθείη· ἔοικε δὲ τεκμήριον εἶναι τὸ πολλὰ κακοπαθεῖν περὶ τὴν γένεσιν αὐτῶν καὶ μηδένα τρόπον δύνασθαι συνεῖραι τοῦ μὴ χωριστὰ εἶναι τὰ μαθηματικὰ τῶν αἰσθητῶν, ὡς ἔνιοι λέγουσι, μηδὲ ταύτας εἶναι τὰς ἀρχάς.","kind":"passage"}],"sha256":"98f15c83d38533c4038a4e93231aaf5416caf52b597197fea01ee6e0b82bd626","language_code":"grc","language_name":"Ἑλληνική","translations":[{"id":"SOURCE_TR_20261015T174315947838Z_214e0c3d","language_code":"en","language_name":"English","title":"Book 14","source_sha256":"98f15c83d38533c4038a4e93231aaf5416caf52b597197fea01ee6e0b82bd626","sha256":"8dfdd64f7b0cf4c3eb1746ad85ee82918a544f1b57bf5eadd20b856f954f599f","rendition_type":"machine","review_status":"machine","model":"gpt-5.6-terra","created":"2026-10-15T17:43:15.947877+00:00","paragraphs":[{"id":"RAW_DAZY_BO_6:P0001","text":"Let so much then have been said about this substance. But all make the principles contraries, as in the case of natural things, and likewise in the case of immovable substances. Yet if it is impossible for anything to be prior to the principle of all things, it would be impossible for the principle, being something else, to be a principle; as if someone were to say that white is a principle, not insofar as it is something else but insofar as it is white, while nevertheless it belongs to a substrate and, being something else, is white; for that other thing will be prior. But all things come to be from contraries as from some substrate; therefore this must belong most of all to contraries.\n\nTherefore all contraries are always predicated of a substrate, and none is separate; but, as also appears, nothing is contrary to substance, and the argument bears witness to this.","kind":"passage","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_DAZY_BO_6:P0001"]},{"id":"RAW_DAZY_BO_6:P0002","text":"Therefore none of the contraries is, properly speaking, a principle of all things, but some other thing is.—But some make one of the contraries matter: some set the unequal against the one, as being the nature of plurality; others set plurality against the one (for numbers are generated, according to some, from the dyad of the unequal, the great and the small, and according to others from plurality, both being under the substance of the one). For he too who calls the unequal and the one the elements, and the unequal a dyad composed of great and small, says that the unequal and the great and the small are one, though he does not distinguish that they are one in account but not in number. But neither do they rightly render the principles which they call elements: some say that the great and the small together with the one are three elements of numbers, the two being matter and the one form; others say the many and the few, because the great and the small are more proper in nature to magnitude; and others still state the more universal terms in these cases, the exceeding and the exceeded.","kind":"passage","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_DAZY_BO_6:P0002"]},{"id":"RAW_DAZY_BO_6:P0003","text":"These differ, one may say, in nothing with respect to some of the consequences, but only with respect to logical difficulties, which they guard against because they themselves conduct logical demonstrations. Yet it belongs to the same argument to make the exceeding and the exceeded principles, rather than the great and the small, and to make number prior to the dyad from the elements; for both are more universal. But now they state the one and not the other. Some oppose the different and the other to the one; others oppose plurality and the one. But if beings are, as they wish, from contraries, and if nothing is contrary to the one—or, if anything is to be, plurality—while the unequal is contrary to the equal, and the different to the same, and the other to itself, those who oppose the one to plurality have most hold of some plausible view; yet not even these sufficiently. For the one will be few; for plurality is opposed to fewness, and the many to the few.—That the one signifies a measure is clear.","kind":"passage","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_DAZY_BO_6:P0003"]},{"id":"RAW_DAZY_BO_6:P0004","text":"And in every case there is some other underlying thing: for example, in harmony a diesis; in magnitude a finger or a foot or something of that sort; in rhythms a foot or a syllable. Likewise, in weight there is some determinate weight; and in all cases in the same way: among qualities, some quality; among quantities, some quantity; and the measure is indivisible, in one case according to the form, in another with respect to perception, as though the one were not some substance existing by itself. And this is reasonable; for the one signifies that it is the measure of some plurality, and number that it is a measured plurality and a plurality of measures (and therefore it is reasonably not the one that is number; for neither is the measure measures, but both the measure and the one are a principle). But the measure must always be something the same for all things: for example, if they are horses, the measure is a horse; and if men, a man. But if there are a man and a horse and a god, perhaps the measure is an animal, and their number will be animals.","kind":"passage","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_DAZY_BO_6:P0004"]},{"id":"RAW_DAZY_BO_6:P0005","text":"But if there are a man and white and walking, there is least of all a number of these, because all belong to the same thing and are one in number; nevertheless, their number will be of genera, or of some other such designation.","kind":"passage","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_DAZY_BO_6:P0005"]},{"id":"RAW_DAZY_BO_6:P0006","text":"But those who make the unequal one thing, and the indefinite dyad of great and small, speak very far from what seems and is possible. For these are affections and attributes rather than substrates of numbers and magnitudes: the many and few of number, and the great and small of magnitude, just as even and odd, smooth and rough, straight and curved. Further, besides this error, the great and small and things of this sort must be relatives; and the relative is least of all a certain nature or substance, and is posterior to quality and quantity. And the relative is, as was said, an affection of quantity, but not matter—if indeed it is something different both from the relative in general and from its parts and species. For there is nothing that is either great or small, or many or few, or in general relative, which is not, being something else, many or few or great or small or relative.","kind":"passage","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_DAZY_BO_6:P0006"]},{"id":"RAW_DAZY_BO_6:P0007","text":"A sign that the relative is least of all a substance and a being is that it alone has neither generation nor destruction nor motion, as in quantity there are increase and diminution, in quality alteration, in place locomotion, and in substance simple generation and destruction—but there is none according to relation. For without itself being moved, a thing will at one time be greater, at another lesser or equal, another thing having been moved quantitatively.\n\nAnd the matter of each thing must be that which is potentially such, and therefore also in the case of substance; but the relative is neither potentially substance nor actually. It is absurd, or rather impossible, then, to make what is not a substance an element of substance and prior to it; for all the categories are posterior. Further, the elements are not predicated of the things of which they are elements; but many and few are predicated separately and together of number, and long and short of a line, and a plane is broad and narrow.","kind":"passage","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_DAZY_BO_6:P0007"]},{"id":"RAW_DAZY_BO_6:P0008","text":"But if there is indeed some plurality of which few is always predicated, such as the dyad (for if it were many, the one would be few), and if there can be something simply many, such as ten, if there is no greater number than this, or ten thousand: how then will number thus be composed from few and many? For either both ought to be predicated or neither; but now only one of them is predicated.","kind":"passage","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_DAZY_BO_6:P0008"]},{"id":"RAW_DAZY_BO_6:P0009","text":"But one must consider in general whether eternal things can be composed of elements. For they will have matter; for everything composed of elements is composite. If, then, it is necessary that that from which a thing is—even if it always is—should be that from which it comes to be, if it comes to be, and everything comes to be from that which is potentially the thing that comes to be (for it could not come to be from the impossible, nor could it be), and what is possible admits both of being actual and of not being actual, then, even if number or anything else having matter exists always as much as possible, it would admit of not being, just as does that which lasts one day and that which lasts however many years; and if so, also that which lasts for a time without limit. They would not then be eternal, if that which admits of not being is not eternal, as it has happened to us to establish in other discussions. But if what is now said is universally true, that no substance is eternal unless it is actuality, while the elements are matter of substance, there could be no elements, inherent in it, from which any eternal substance is composed.","kind":"passage","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_DAZY_BO_6:P0009"]},{"id":"RAW_DAZY_BO_6:P0010","text":"There are some who make the indefinite dyad, together with the one, an element, but object to the unequal, reasonably, on account of the impossible consequences. They have removed from the difficulties only as many as necessarily follow for those who make the unequal and the relative an element because they do so; but whatever difficulties are apart from this opinion must necessarily belong to them too, whether they make the ideal number from these or the mathematical number.—There are many causes of their turning aside toward these causes, but most of all their having been perplexed in an archaic manner. For it seemed to them that all beings would be one, being itself, unless someone should solve and confront Parmenides' saying:\n\nfor never shall this prevail, that things which are not are;\nbut it is necessary to show that what is not is.\n\nFor thus, from being and something else, beings will exist, if they are many.","kind":"passage","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_DAZY_BO_6:P0010"]},{"id":"RAW_DAZY_BO_6:P0011","text":"And yet, first, if being is said in many ways (for it signifies substance in one way, quality in another, quantity in another, and likewise the other categories), in what sense will all beings be one if not-being is not to be? Will substances be one, or affections and likewise the other things, or all of them, so that this and such and so much and all the other things which signify some one thing will be one? But it is absurd, or rather impossible, that one nature, having come to be, should be the cause of being's being this in one case, such in another, so much in another, and somewhere in another. Further, from what not-being and being are beings many? For not-being too is said in many ways, since being is; and not being a man signifies not being this, not straight signifies not being such, and not three cubits signifies not being so much.","kind":"passage","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_DAZY_BO_6:P0011"]},{"id":"RAW_DAZY_BO_6:P0012","text":"From what being and not-being, then, are beings many? He wishes, indeed, to call not-being falsity and this nature, from which, together with being, beings are many; hence it was also said that one must posit some falsity, just as geometers posit that what is not a foot long is a foot long. But this cannot be so. For geometers posit no falsity (for the proposition is not in the syllogism), nor do beings come to be or perish from not-being in this sense. But since not-being according to the cases is spoken of in as many ways as the categories, and besides this there is the not-being that is spoken of as false and that which is according to potentiality, generation is from the latter: a man comes from what is not a man but is potentially a man, and a white thing from what is not white but is potentially white, alike whether one thing or many things come to be.—But the inquiry appears to concern how being, in the sense of substances, is many; for the things generated are numbers and lengths and bodies.","kind":"passage","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_DAZY_BO_6:P0012"]},{"id":"RAW_DAZY_BO_6:P0013","text":"It is absurd, then, to inquire how being, that which it is, is many, but not how qualities or quantities are many. For surely neither the indefinite dyad nor the great and the small is the cause of there being two white things, or many colors or flavors or figures; for these too would then be numbers and units. But if they had pursued these matters, they would have seen the cause also in those cases; for the cause is the same and analogous. For this divergence is also the cause of their, in seeking what is opposed to being and the one, from which beings arise, positing the relative and the unequal, which is neither contrary nor a negation of them, but one nature among beings, just as what-it-is and quality are. And they should have inquired also how relatives are many and not one; but now they inquire how there are many units besides the first one, but no longer how there are many unequals besides the unequal.","kind":"passage","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_DAZY_BO_6:P0013"]},{"id":"RAW_DAZY_BO_6:P0014","text":"And yet they employ and speak of great and small, from which numbers arise; long and short, from which length arises; broad and narrow, from which plane arises; deep and shallow, from which solids arise; and they speak of yet more species of the relative. What, then, is the cause in these cases of their being many?—It is necessary, then, as we say, to posit for each a being potentially (and the speaker of these things further declared what is potentially this and substance, but is not-being in itself: namely, the relative, as though he had said quality, which is neither potentially the one or being nor a negation of the one or of being, but one of the beings); and much more, as was said, if he was inquiring how beings are many, he should not inquire about those in the same category—how there are many substances or many qualities—but how beings are many; for some are substances, others affections, others relatives.","kind":"passage","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_DAZY_BO_6:P0014"]},{"id":"RAW_DAZY_BO_6:P0015","text":"In the case of the other categories, then, there is some other account of how they are many (for, because they are not separate, qualities and quantities become and are many through the substrate becoming many; though there must be some matter for each genus, except that it is impossible for it to be separate from substances). But in the case of this something there is some account of how this something is many, unless there is both this something and some such nature; rather, the difficulty is chiefly there: how there are many substances in actuality rather than one. Yet if this something and quantity are not the same, it is not stated how and why beings are many, but how quantities are many. For every number signifies some quantity, and the unit too, unless it is a measure and indivisible in respect of quantity. If, then, quantity and what-it-is are different, it is not stated from what or how what-it-is is many; but if they are the same, the speaker maintains many contradictions.—One might also pause the inquiry concerning numbers: whence must one obtain conviction that they exist?","kind":"passage","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_DAZY_BO_6:P0015"]},{"id":"RAW_DAZY_BO_6:P0016","text":"For one who posits Ideas has some cause furnished for beings, if each of the numbers is an Idea and the Idea is for the other things a cause of being in whatever way it may be (let this be assumed for them). But for one who does not think this in this way, because he sees the inherent difficulties concerning the Ideas, so that for these reasons he does not make numbers thus, but makes mathematical number, from what source should one be convinced that such a number exists, and what use is it to other things? For the person who says it exists does not say that it is the number of anything, but says that it is some nature existing by itself; nor does it appear to be a cause. For all the theorems of arithmetic will also belong to sensible things, as was said.","kind":"passage","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_DAZY_BO_6:P0016"]},{"id":"RAW_DAZY_BO_6:P0017","text":"Those, then, who posit that Ideas exist and that they are numbers, by taking each one thing besides the many according to the exposition of each, at least attempt to say somehow why it exists; yet since these things are neither necessary nor possible, one must not say that number exists on these grounds. But the Pythagoreans, because they saw many properties of numbers belonging to sensible bodies, made beings to be numbers—not separate numbers, but beings constituted from numbers. Why? Because the properties of numbers are present in harmony and in the heaven and in many other things. But those who say that only mathematical number exists can say nothing of this sort in accordance with their hypotheses; rather, it was said that their sciences would not exist. But we say that they do exist, as we said before. And it is clear that mathematical objects are not separate; for if they were separate, their attributes would not belong to bodies.","kind":"passage","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_DAZY_BO_6:P0017"]},{"id":"RAW_DAZY_BO_6:P0018","text":"The Pythagoreans, then, are not liable to criticism in this respect; but in making natural bodies from numbers—things having weight and lightness from things having neither weight nor lightness—they seem to speak about another heaven and other bodies, not the sensible ones. But those who make them separate suppose them to exist and to be separate because the axioms will not hold of sensible things, whereas the statements are true and delight the soul; and likewise with mathematical magnitudes.\n\nIt is clear, then, that the opposing argument will say the contrary, and that those who speak thus must solve the difficulty just raised: why, though the properties are in no way present in sensible things, their properties are present in sensible things. There are some who think it necessary that such natures exist because a point is the limit and extremity of a line, this of a plane, and this of a solid. One must therefore examine this argument too, lest it be excessively weak.","kind":"passage","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_DAZY_BO_6:P0018"]},{"id":"RAW_DAZY_BO_6:P0019","text":"For the extremities are not substances, but rather all these things are limits (since even walking and motion in general have some limit; that, then, will be a this-something and a substance—but that is absurd). Yet even if they are, they will all belong to these sensible things (for the argument has spoken of these); why, then, will they be separate?—Further, one who is not too readily satisfied might inquire, concerning every number and mathematical object, that the prior contributes nothing to the posterior (for even if number did not exist, magnitudes would nonetheless exist for those who say that only mathematical objects exist, and if these did not exist, soul and sensible bodies would exist; yet nature does not appear from the phenomena to be episodic, like a poor tragedy). But those who posit Ideas escape this—for they make magnitudes from matter and number: lengths from the dyad, planes perhaps from the triad, solids from the tetrad, or even from other numbers; for it makes no difference—,","kind":"passage","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_DAZY_BO_6:P0019"]},{"id":"RAW_DAZY_BO_6:P0020","text":"But are these, then, going to be Forms, or what is their mode, and what do they contribute to the things that are? For they contribute nothing, any more than mathematical objects do. Nor, indeed, does any theorem hold of them, unless one wishes to set mathematical objects in motion and make up certain peculiar doctrines. And it is not difficult, by assuming any sort of hypotheses, to spin out a long argument and string things together. These men, then, by attaching themselves in this way to the Forms, go astray concerning mathematical objects; while the first two, who made two kinds of number, that of the Forms and the mathematical, have neither said nor could say how and from what the mathematical number will be. For they place it between the ideal and the sensible.","kind":"passage","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_DAZY_BO_6:P0020"]},{"id":"RAW_DAZY_BO_6:P0021","text":"For if it is from the Great and the Small, it will be the same as that number of the Forms (but he makes magnitudes from some other Great and Small); whereas if he names something else, he will name more elements. And if the principle of each kind is some one thing, the One will be something common to these, and it must be investigated how these too are many; yet, according to that doctrine, it is impossible for number to come to be otherwise than from the One and the indefinite dyad. All these things, then, are irrational, and conflict both with themselves and with reasonable accounts; and Simonides' “long discourse” seems to be present in them. For the long discourse comes about, like that of slaves, when they say nothing sound. And the elements themselves, the Great and the Small, seem to cry out as though being dragged; for they can in no way generate number except that which is doubled from one.—It is also absurd to make a coming-to-be of things that are eternal, or rather it is one of the impossibilities.","kind":"passage","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_DAZY_BO_6:P0021"]},{"id":"RAW_DAZY_BO_6:P0022","text":"As for the Pythagoreans, whether they do not make a coming-to-be or do make one, there is no need to hesitate; for they clearly say that, when the One had been constituted, whether from planes or from colour or from seed or from things which they are at a loss to state, immediately the nearest part of the unlimited was drawn in and limited by the limit. But since they are making a cosmos and wish to speak physically, it is just to examine them concerning nature, but to leave them aside from the present inquiry. For we are seeking the principles among immovable things, so that we must examine also the coming-to-be of numbers of this sort.","kind":"passage","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_DAZY_BO_6:P0022"]},{"id":"RAW_DAZY_BO_6:P0023","text":"They do not, then, assert a coming-to-be of the odd, evidently because there is a coming-to-be of the even; and some construct the first even out of unequal Great and Small when these have been equalized. Therefore inequality must belong to them prior to their being equalized; but if they had always been equalized, they would not have been unequal before (for of that which always is there is nothing prior), so that it is clear that they do not make the coming-to-be of numbers for the sake of theoretical inquiry.—There is also a difficulty, and a reproach even for one who has found an answer, as to how the elements and principles stand in relation to the good and the beautiful: namely, whether any one of them is such as we mean when we speak of the Good Itself and the Best, or not, but whether these are later-born.","kind":"passage","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_DAZY_BO_6:P0023"]},{"id":"RAW_DAZY_BO_6:P0024","text":"For among the theologians there seems to be agreement with certain of the present thinkers, who say that they are not so, but that, after the nature of beings had advanced, both the good and the beautiful are manifested. They do this from caution before a genuine difficulty which confronts those who, like some thinkers, say that the One is a principle; and the difficulty is not due to their attributing good to the principle as belonging to it, but to their making the One a principle, and a principle as an element, and number out of the One.—The ancient poets likewise agree in this respect, in that they say that not the first things, such as Night and Heaven or Chaos or Ocean, reign and rule, but Zeus. Yet these poets come to say such things because the rulers of beings change; whereas those among them who are mixed in so as not to say everything mythically, such as Pherecydes and certain others, posit that which first generated as best; so too do the Magi, and among the later wise men Empedocles and Anaxagoras, the former making Love an element, the latter Intellect a principle.","kind":"passage","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_DAZY_BO_6:P0024"]},{"id":"RAW_DAZY_BO_6:P0025","text":"Of those who say that there are immovable substances, some say that the One Itself is the Good Itself; yet they supposed that its substance was pre-eminently the One.—This, then, is the difficulty: how one ought to speak. Yet it would be strange if the first, eternal, and most self-sufficient thing did not possess this very thing primarily as a good, namely self-sufficiency and preservation. But surely it is not imperishable, nor self-sufficient, for any other reason than because it is well. Thus to say that the principle is such is reasonably true; but that it is the One, or, failing that, an element, and an element of numbers, is impossible. For a great difficulty follows—one which some, seeking to avoid, have renounced: those who grant that the One is the first principle and element, but only of mathematical number—for all units become what is some good, and there is a great abundance of goods.","kind":"passage","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_DAZY_BO_6:P0025"]},{"id":"RAW_DAZY_BO_6:P0026","text":"Further, if the Forms are numbers, all the Forms are what is some good. Yet let one posit Forms of whatever things he wishes. For if they are Forms of goods only, the Forms will not be substances; but if they are also Forms of substances, all animals and plants and all things participating in them will be good. Both these absurdities follow, and also that the contrary element—whether it is plurality or the unequal, Great and Small—is Evil Itself. Hence one man avoided attaching the good to the One, on the ground that, since coming-to-be is from contraries, the nature of plurality must be evil; while others say that the unequal is the nature of evil. It follows, then, that all beings participate in evil except the One Itself alone, and that numbers participate in a more unmixed evil than magnitudes do; that evil is the place of the good; and that it participates in and desires what is destructive. For the contrary is destructive of its contrary.","kind":"passage","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_DAZY_BO_6:P0026"]},{"id":"RAW_DAZY_BO_6:P0027","text":"And if, as we said, matter is each thing potentially—for example, potentially fire is the matter of fire actually—evil will be the potentially good itself. All these consequences follow: one because they make every principle an element; another because they make contraries principles; another because they make the One a principle; another because they make numbers the primary substances, separate and Forms.","kind":"passage","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_DAZY_BO_6:P0027"]},{"id":"RAW_DAZY_BO_6:P0028","text":"If, then, both not placing the good among the principles and placing it there in this way are impossible, it is clear that the principles and the primary substances are not correctly assigned. Nor does one suppose correctly if one compares the principles of the whole to those of animals and plants, on the ground that the more complete things always come from indefinite and incomplete things, and therefore says that the same holds also of the first things, so that the One Itself is not even something that is. For here too the principles from which these things come are complete: a human being generates a human being, and seed is not first. It is also absurd to make place exist simultaneously with mathematical solids (for place is peculiar to individual things, which is why they are separate in place, whereas mathematical objects are not somewhere), and to say that they will be somewhere, but not to say what place is.—Those who say that beings are from elements, and that numbers are the first among beings, ought, after distinguishing how one thing is from another, thus to say in what way number is from the principles.","kind":"passage","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_DAZY_BO_6:P0028"]},{"id":"RAW_DAZY_BO_6:P0029","text":"Is it by mixture? But not everything is mixed, and what comes to be is different; nor will the One be separate, nor have another nature—which, however, they wish. Or is it by composition, as a syllable? But position must then belong to them, and the thinker will think the One and the plurality separately. Number, then, will be this: a unit and plurality, or the One and the unequal. And since being from certain things is in one way from things present in it, and in another way from things not present in it, in which way is number from them? For in the way of things present in it, only things that have coming-to-be are so. But is it as from seed? Yet it is not possible for anything to depart from the indivisible. Or as from a contrary that does not remain? But all things that are in this way are also from some other thing which remains. Since, therefore, one man posits the One as contrary to plurality, another as contrary to the unequal, treating the One as equal, number would be from contraries. Therefore there is some other thing from which, while it remains, number is or has come to be, together with the other contrary.","kind":"passage","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_DAZY_BO_6:P0029"]},{"id":"RAW_DAZY_BO_6:P0030","text":"Further, why ever is it that all other things that are from contraries, or have contraries, perish even if they are from all of them, whereas number does not? For nothing is said about this. Yet a contrary destroys both what is present in a thing and what is not present, as Strife destroys the mixture—though it ought not to do so, since it is not contrary to that.—Nor has it been determined in what way numbers are causes of substances and of being: whether as limits (as points are limits of magnitudes, and as Eurytus assigned what number belonged to what thing—for example, this number to a human being, that to a horse—likening the forms of plants to pebbles, just as those who make numbers into figures make a triangle and a square); or because a concord is a ratio of numbers, and likewise a human being and each of the other things? But how are the attributes numbers—white, sweet, and hot? That numbers are neither substance nor causes of form is clear; for the ratio is the substance, while number is matter.","kind":"passage","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_DAZY_BO_6:P0030"]},{"id":"RAW_DAZY_BO_6:P0031","text":"For example, the substance of flesh or bone is number in this sense: three parts fire and two earth; and the number, whatever it may be, is always a number of certain things, either fiery or earthy or of units; but the substance is being so much to so much according to the mixture. And this is no longer number, but a ratio of a mixture of bodily numbers, or of numbers of whatever sort. Therefore number, neither number in general nor unit-number, is not a cause by making, nor matter, nor the ratio and form of things. Nor, indeed, is it a cause as that for the sake of which.","kind":"passage","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_DAZY_BO_6:P0031"]},{"id":"RAW_DAZY_BO_6:P0032","text":"One might also inquire what the benefit is that comes from mixtures being in a number, whether in a readily calculable number or in an odd number. For at present a honey-and-water mixture would be no more healthful if mixed three times three; rather, if watery, it would be more beneficial with no ratio at all than if it were unmixed in a number. Further, the ratios of mixtures are in the addition of numbers, not in numbers—for example, three to two, not three times two. For in multiplications the genus must be the same, so that the line on which A B C is must be measured by A, and the line D E Z by D; hence all must be measured by the same thing. Therefore B E G Z will not be a number of fire and twice three a number of water. —But if everything must participate in number, it is necessary that many things be the same, and that the same number belong to this thing and to another.","kind":"passage","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_DAZY_BO_6:P0032"]},{"id":"RAW_DAZY_BO_6:P0033","text":"Is this, then, the cause, and is the thing so because of this, or is it unclear? For example, there is some number of the courses of the sun, and again of those of the moon, and of the life and age of each animal. What, then, prevents some of these from being squares, others cubes, some equal, and others double? Nothing prevents it; indeed, it is necessary to move among such things if all things participate in number. And different things could fall under the same number; so that, if the same number happened to belong to certain things, those things having the same species of number would be the same as one another—for example, the sun and moon would be the same. But why are these causes? There are seven vowels, seven strings in the scale, seven Pleiades, at seven months some animals cast teeth (though some do not), and seven against Thebes.","kind":"passage","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_DAZY_BO_6:P0033"]},{"id":"RAW_DAZY_BO_6:P0034","text":"Is it then because number is naturally of such a kind that those men were seven, or that the Pleiad is seven stars? Or were the former seven because of the gates or some other cause, while we count the Pleiad in this way, whereas we count the Bear as twelve, and others count more? For they even say that ΞΨΖ are concords, and because those are three, these too are three; but they do not care that there could be ten thousand such cases—for Γ and Ρ could be one sign. And if the cause is that each of the others is double while this one is not, and that, because there are three places, one sigma is added in each case, this is why there are only three and not because the concords are three—since there are in fact more concords, but here they can no longer be so. These men, then, resemble the ancient Homeric scholars, who see small likenesses but overlook great ones.","kind":"passage","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_DAZY_BO_6:P0034"]},{"id":"RAW_DAZY_BO_6:P0035","text":"Some say that there are many such facts: for example, the two middle strings, one having nine and the other eight, and the epic verse, having seventeen, equal in number to these; and that it is scanned in nine syllables on the right and eight on the left. And they say that the interval is equal both in the letters from Α to Ω and from the lowest note to the highest on flutes, whose number is equal to the complete melody of the heaven. But one should see that no one would be at a loss either to state or to discover such things among eternal things, since they occur even among perishable things.","kind":"passage","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_DAZY_BO_6:P0035"]},{"id":"RAW_DAZY_BO_6:P0036","text":"But the much-praised natures in numbers, and their contraries, and in general the things in mathematical studies, which, as some say, they make causes of nature, appear to escape anyone who examines them in this way—for none of them is a cause in any of the ways distinguished concerning principles. Yet in one sense they evidently make clear that the good belongs to things, and that the odd, the straight, the square, and the powers of certain numbers belong to the column of the beautiful; for seasons and number of such a kind occur together. And all the other things which they collect from mathematical theorems have this same force.","kind":"passage","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_DAZY_BO_6:P0036"]},{"id":"RAW_DAZY_BO_6:P0037","text":"Therefore they seem to be coincidences. For they are accidental, but all are akin to one another and are one by analogy. For in each category of being there is the analogous: as the straight is in length, so the level is in breadth; perhaps the odd is in number, and white in colour.—Further, the numbers in the Forms are not causes of harmonies and things of that sort (for those numbers equal in species differ from one another; the units too differ). Hence Forms should not be posited on these grounds. Such, then, are the consequences, and still more could be collected. But the fact that they suffer many difficulties concerning their generation, and are unable in any way to connect things so as to show that mathematical objects are not separate from sensible things, as some say, nor that these are the principles, seems to be evidence.","kind":"passage","source_paragraph_ids":["RAW_DAZY_BO_6:P0037"]}],"source_digest":"2b51c75e3939b1f437d8610b2cd425043f3e43f9163f532b5518ee7cfd969f2c","source_language_code":"grc","original_title":"Book 14"}],"passage_basis":"frozen-grounding","original_title":"Book 14","title_language_code":"en"}],"units":[{"id":"CH_DAZY_BOO1","layer":"CH","title":"Book 1","order":1,"parents":["RAW_DAZY_BOO1"],"revision":"CH_DAZY_BOO1@R001","sha256":"5a1ea952134154148204e0b171a6a70d05e63d524323d29858d395e3727990c5","sections":[{"number":"1","name":"Argumental Spine","items":[{"id":"CH_DAZY_BOO1:AS1","text":"Ζητεῖται ποία τῶν αἰτιῶν καὶ ἀρχῶν θεωρία σοφία ἐστὶ καὶ τίνα σκοπὸν ἡ περὶ αὐτὴν μέθοδος διώκει.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_DAZY_BOO1:P0007","trace_type":"Composite","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"},{"id":"RAW_DAZY_BOO1:P0012","trace_type":"Composite","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_DAZY_BOO1:AS2","text":"Ἡ σοφία τίθεται θεωρητικὴ ἐπιστήμη τῶν πρώτων ἀρχῶν καὶ αἰτιῶν, αὑτῆς ἕνεκα αἱρετὴ καὶ τῶν ἄλλων ἐπιστημῶν ἀρχική.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_DAZY_BOO1:P0007","trace_type":"Composite","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"},{"id":"RAW_DAZY_BOO1:P0009","trace_type":"Composite","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_DAZY_BOO1:AS3","text":"Ἡ θέσις γεννᾶται διὰ τῆς ἀνόδου ἀπὸ αἰσθήσεως εἰς μνήμην, ἐμπειρίαν, καθόλου ὑπόληψιν καὶ αἰτιώδη ἐπιστήμην.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_DAZY_BOO1:P0001","trace_type":"Composite","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"},{"id":"RAW_DAZY_BOO1:P0002","trace_type":"Composite","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"},{"id":"RAW_DAZY_BOO1:P0004","trace_type":"Composite","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_DAZY_BOO1:AS4","text":"Ὁ συλλογισμὸς διαγνωστικῶς μὲν ἐκ τῶν περὶ τοῦ σοφοῦ ὑπολήψεων, γενεαλογικῶς δὲ διὰ τῆς τῶν προτέρων φιλοσοφούντων ἐξετάσεως προβαίνει.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_DAZY_BOO1:P0007","trace_type":"Composite","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"},{"id":"RAW_DAZY_BOO1:P0013","trace_type":"Composite","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_DAZY_BOO1:AS5","text":"Ἡ τετραχὴ διαίρεσις τῶν αἰτίων γίνεται κανὼν πρὸς τὸ τάττειν, συγκρίνειν καὶ ἐλέγχειν τὰς προτέρας θεωρίας.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_DAZY_BOO1:P0013","trace_type":"Composite","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"},{"id":"RAW_DAZY_BOO1:P0044","trace_type":"Composite","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"},{"id":"RAW_DAZY_BOO1:P0047","trace_type":"Composite","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_DAZY_BOO1:AS6","text":"Ἡ κίνησις ἀπὸ τῆς φυσικῆς ὀρέξεως τοῦ εἰδέναι διὰ τοῦ διορισμοῦ τῆς σοφίας εἰς τὴν τῶν αἰτίων κατάταξιν καὶ ἐκεῖθεν εἰς τὰς ὑπολειπομένας ἀπορίας καταλήγει.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_DAZY_BOO1:P0001","trace_type":"Structural","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"},{"id":"RAW_DAZY_BOO1:P0009","trace_type":"Structural","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"},{"id":"RAW_DAZY_BOO1:P0013","trace_type":"Structural","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"},{"id":"RAW_DAZY_BOO1:P0072","trace_type":"Structural","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]}],"original_name":"Argumental Spine"},{"number":"2","name":"Load-Bearing Axioms","items":[{"id":"CH_DAZY_BOO1:A1","text":"Πάντες ἄνθρωποι τοῦ εἰδέναι ὀρέγονται φύσει.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_DAZY_BOO1:P0001","trace_type":"Direct","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_DAZY_BOO1:A2","text":"Ἡ μετὰ τῆς αἰσθήσεως μνήμη τὴν μάθησιν καὶ φρόνησιν δυνατωτέραν ποιεῖ.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_DAZY_BOO1:P0001","trace_type":"Direct","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_DAZY_BOO1:A3","text":"Αἱ πολλαὶ μνῆμαι τοῦ αὐτοῦ πράγματος μιᾶς ἐμπειρίας δύναμιν ἀποτελοῦσιν.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_DAZY_BOO1:P0002","trace_type":"Direct","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_DAZY_BOO1:A4","text":"Τέχνη γίνεται ὅταν ἐκ πολλῶν ἐμπείρων ἐννοημάτων μία καθόλου περὶ τῶν ὁμοίων ὑπόληψις γένηται.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_DAZY_BOO1:P0002","trace_type":"Direct","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_DAZY_BOO1:A5","text":"Αἱ πράξεις καὶ αἱ γενέσεις περὶ τὸ καθ᾽ ἕκαστον εἰσίν, διὸ ἡ ἐμπειρία πολλάκις πρακτικῶς τοῦ ἄνευ ἐμπειρίας λόγου μᾶλλον ἐπιτυγχάνει.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_DAZY_BOO1:P0003","trace_type":"Direct","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_DAZY_BOO1:A6","text":"Ἡ τοῦ διότι καὶ τῆς αἰτίας γνῶσις μᾶλλον εἰδέναι ποιεῖ ἢ ἡ τοῦ ὅτι μόνον γνῶσις.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_DAZY_BOO1:P0004","trace_type":"Direct","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_DAZY_BOO1:A7","text":"Τὸ δύνασθαι διδάσκειν σημεῖόν ἐστι τῆς κατὰ τὰς αἰτίας γνώσεως.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_DAZY_BOO1:P0005","trace_type":"Direct","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_DAZY_BOO1:A8","text":"Ὁ μάλιστα τὴν καθόλου ἐπιστήμην ἔχων οἶδέ πως πάντα τὰ ὑποκείμενα, καίπερ μὴ καθ᾽ ἕκαστον ἐπιστάμενος.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_DAZY_BOO1:P0008","trace_type":"Direct","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_DAZY_BOO1:A9","text":"Αἱ τῶν πρώτων καὶ ἐλαττόνων ἀρχῶν ἐπιστῆμαι ἀκριβέστεραι καὶ διδασκαλικώτεραι τῶν ἐκ προσθέσεως γιγνομένων εἰσίν.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_DAZY_BOO1:P0008","trace_type":"Direct","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_DAZY_BOO1:A10","text":"Ἡ δι᾽ αὑτὴν καὶ τοῦ εἰδέναι χάριν αἱρετὴ ἐπιστήμη μᾶλλον σοφία ἐστὶ τῆς διὰ τὰ ἀποβαίνοντα αἱρετῆς.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_DAZY_BOO1:P0007","trace_type":"Direct","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_DAZY_BOO1:A11","text":"Ἡ τὸ οὗ ἕνεκα καὶ τἀγαθὸν γνωρίζουσα ἐπιστήμη ἀρχικωτέρα τῆς ὑπηρετούσης ἐστίν.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_DAZY_BOO1:P0009","trace_type":"Direct","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_DAZY_BOO1:A12","text":"Αἱ κοινὰς περὶ τοῦ σοφοῦ ὑπολήψεις ἐπὶ τὴν αὐτὴν τῶν πρώτων ἀρχῶν καὶ αἰτιῶν ἐπιστήμην συμπίπτουσιν.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_DAZY_BOO1:P0007","trace_type":"Composite","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"},{"id":"RAW_DAZY_BOO1:P0009","trace_type":"Composite","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_DAZY_BOO1:A13","text":"Ἡ φιλοσοφία ἐκ τοῦ θαυμάζειν καὶ τῆς ἰδίας ἀγνοίας αἰσθήσεως ἄρχεται, διὸ τοῦ εἰδέναι καὶ οὐ χρήσεώς τινος ἕνεκα ζητεῖται.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_DAZY_BOO1:P0010","trace_type":"Direct","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_DAZY_BOO1:A14","text":"Τότε ἕκαστον εἰδέναι λεγόμεθα, ὅταν τὴν πρώτην αὐτοῦ αἰτίαν γνωρίζειν οἰώμεθα.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_DAZY_BOO1:P0013","trace_type":"Direct","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_DAZY_BOO1:A15","text":"Τὰ αἴτια τετραχῶς λέγεται, ὡς οὐσία καὶ τί ἦν εἶναι, ὡς ὕλη καὶ ὑποκείμενον, ὡς ὅθεν ἡ κίνησις, καὶ ὡς οὗ ἕνεκα καὶ τἀγαθόν.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_DAZY_BOO1:P0013","trace_type":"Direct","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_DAZY_BOO1:A16","text":"Κατὰ τὴν ἐπαγωγὴν τοῦ λόγου οὐδεὶς τῶν προτέρων ἔξω τῶν τεττάρων αἰτίων ἀρχὴν ἔθετο, πάντες δὲ ἀμυδρῶς καὶ μερικῶς αὐτῶν ἥψαντο.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_DAZY_BOO1:P0044","trace_type":"Direct","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_DAZY_BOO1:A17","text":"Αἰτία ὀνομαζομένη ἀλλὰ μὴ πρὸς τὴν γένεσιν, τὴν κίνησιν, τὴν οὐσίαν ἢ τὸ τέλος ἱκανῶς καὶ ὁμολογουμένως χρησιμοποιουμένη οὐ πλήρης ἐξήγησίς ἐστιν.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_DAZY_BOO1:P0029","trace_type":"Structural","confidence":"medium","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"},{"id":"RAW_DAZY_BOO1:P0045","trace_type":"Structural","confidence":"medium","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"},{"id":"RAW_DAZY_BOO1:P0046","trace_type":"Structural","confidence":"medium","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]}],"original_name":"Load-Bearing Axioms"},{"number":"3","name":"Apophatic Structure","items":[{"id":"CH_DAZY_BOO1:NX1","text":"Οὐδεμία αἴσθησις σοφία ἐστίν, ἐπειδὴ τὸ ὅτι δηλοῖ, τὸ δὲ διὰ τί οὐ λέγει.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_DAZY_BOO1:P0005","trace_type":"Negative","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_DAZY_BOO1:NX2","text":"Ἡ ἐμπειρία οὐκ ἔχει καθ᾽ αὑτὴν τὴν τοῦ καθόλου καὶ τῆς αἰτίας γνῶσιν.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_DAZY_BOO1:P0003","trace_type":"Negative","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"},{"id":"RAW_DAZY_BOO1:P0004","trace_type":"Negative","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_DAZY_BOO1:NX3","text":"Ἡ περὶ τὰ καθ᾽ ἕκαστα πρακτικὴ ἐπιτυχία οὐκ ἀποτελεῖ μόνη τὸ εἰδέναι ἢ τὴν σοφίαν.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_DAZY_BOO1:P0003","trace_type":"Negative","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"},{"id":"RAW_DAZY_BOO1:P0004","trace_type":"Negative","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_DAZY_BOO1:NX4","text":"Ἡ ζητουμένη σοφία οὐ ποιητικὴ οὐδὲ πρὸς ἑτέραν χρείαν τεταγμένη ἐστίν.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_DAZY_BOO1:P0010","trace_type":"Negative","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_DAZY_BOO1:NX5","text":"Τὸ θεῖον οὐκ ἐνδέχεται φθονερὸν εἶναι, ὥστε ἡ θεία ἐπιστήμη οὐκ ἀπαγορευτέα τῷ ἀνθρώπῳ ἐστίν.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_DAZY_BOO1:P0011","trace_type":"Negative","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_DAZY_BOO1:NX6","text":"Τὸ ὑποκείμενον καὶ ἡ ὕλη οὐ ποιεῖ καθ᾽ αὑτὸ ἑαυτὸ μεταβάλλειν.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_DAZY_BOO1:P0017","trace_type":"Negative","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_DAZY_BOO1:NX7","text":"Τὸ αὐτόματον καὶ ἡ τύχη οὐχ ἱκανῶς αἰτιῶνται τὴν τάξιν καὶ τὸ καλῶς ἔχειν τῶν ὄντων.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_DAZY_BOO1:P0019","trace_type":"Negative","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_DAZY_BOO1:NX8","text":"Μία σωματικὴ ὕλη οὐ δύναται τῶν ἀσωμάτων, τῆς κινήσεως καὶ τοῦ τί ἐστιν ἅμα αἰτία εἶναι.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_DAZY_BOO1:P0048","trace_type":"Direct","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_DAZY_BOO1:NX9","text":"Ἀριθμοὶ καὶ ἐναντιώσεις ἄνευ λόγου κινήσεως οὐ γεννῶσι τὴν τῶν αἰσθητῶν φύσιν.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_DAZY_BOO1:P0054","trace_type":"Negative","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_DAZY_BOO1:NX10","text":"Ἡ μέθεξις καὶ ἡ μίμησις, ἐὰν μόνα ὀνόματα μένωσιν, οὐ δηλοῦσι πῶς τὰ εἴδη αἴτια τῶν αἰσθητῶν ἐστίν.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_DAZY_BOO1:P0041","trace_type":"Negative","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"},{"id":"RAW_DAZY_BOO1:P0060","trace_type":"Negative","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_DAZY_BOO1:NX11","text":"Τὰ χωριστὰ εἴδη οὔτε κινήσεως οὔτε γενέσεως οὔτε τοῦ εἶναι τῶν μετεχόντων ἱκανῶς αἴτια δείκνυται.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_DAZY_BOO1:P0059","trace_type":"Negative","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"},{"id":"RAW_DAZY_BOO1:P0060","trace_type":"Negative","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"},{"id":"RAW_DAZY_BOO1:P0062","trace_type":"Negative","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_DAZY_BOO1:NX12","text":"Τὰ στοιχεῖα πάντων τῶν ὄντων ἀδιακρίτως ζητεῖν οὐ δυνατόν ἐστιν, ἐπειδὴ τὸ ὂν πολλαχῶς λέγεται.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_DAZY_BOO1:P0068","trace_type":"Negative","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]}],"original_name":"Apophatic Structure"},{"number":"4","name":"Derivative Propositions","items":[{"id":"CH_DAZY_BOO1:D1","text":"Ἡ ἀπὸ τῆς αἰσθήσεως εἰς τὴν τέχνην μετάβασις γίνεται διὰ διατηρήσεως, πολλαπλασιασμοῦ καὶ καθολικῆς συνάψεως τῶν καθ᾽ ἕκαστα γνωσμάτων.","refs":["CH_DAZY_BOO1:A2","CH_DAZY_BOO1:A3","CH_DAZY_BOO1:A4"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"From","passages":[{"id":"RAW_DAZY_BOO1:P0001","trace_type":"Composite","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"},{"id":"RAW_DAZY_BOO1:P0002","trace_type":"Composite","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_DAZY_BOO1:D2","text":"Ἡ πρακτικὴ ἐπιτυχία καὶ ἡ αἰτιώδης ἐπιστήμη δύνανται χωρίζεσθαι, ἐπειδὴ ἡ μὲν τῶν καθ᾽ ἕκαστα ἅπτεται, ἡ δὲ τὸ διότι γνωρίζει.","refs":["CH_DAZY_BOO1:A5","CH_DAZY_BOO1:A6"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"From","passages":[{"id":"RAW_DAZY_BOO1:P0003","trace_type":"Composite","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"},{"id":"RAW_DAZY_BOO1:P0004","trace_type":"Composite","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_DAZY_BOO1:D3","text":"Ἡ διδασκαλία τὴν καθόλου αἰτίαν ἀπαιτεῖ, διότι τὸ καθ᾽ ἕκαστον ὡς τοιοῦτον οὔτε κοινὸν λόγον οὔτε μεταδοτὴν ἐπιστήμην παρέχει.","refs":["CH_DAZY_BOO1:A4","CH_DAZY_BOO1:A6","CH_DAZY_BOO1:A7"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"From","passages":[{"id":"RAW_DAZY_BOO1:P0002","trace_type":"Composite","confidence":"medium","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"},{"id":"RAW_DAZY_BOO1:P0004","trace_type":"Composite","confidence":"medium","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"},{"id":"RAW_DAZY_BOO1:P0005","trace_type":"Composite","confidence":"medium","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_DAZY_BOO1:D4","text":"Ἡ ζητουμένη σοφία ἐστὶν ἡ τῶν πρώτων καὶ καθόλου αἰτίων ἀκριβεστάτη, διδασκαλικωτάτη καὶ ἀρχικωτάτη θεωρία.","refs":["CH_DAZY_BOO1:A8","CH_DAZY_BOO1:A9","CH_DAZY_BOO1:A11","CH_DAZY_BOO1:A12"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"From","passages":[{"id":"RAW_DAZY_BOO1:P0008","trace_type":"Composite","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"},{"id":"RAW_DAZY_BOO1:P0009","trace_type":"Composite","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_DAZY_BOO1:D5","text":"Ἡ σοφία ἐλευθέρα καλεῖται, ἐπειδὴ αὑτῆς ἕνεκα ὑπάρχει καὶ οὐχ ὡς ὄργανον ἑτέρας χρείας.","refs":["CH_DAZY_BOO1:A10","CH_DAZY_BOO1:A13"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"From","passages":[{"id":"RAW_DAZY_BOO1:P0010","trace_type":"Composite","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"},{"id":"RAW_DAZY_BOO1:P0011","trace_type":"Composite","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_DAZY_BOO1:D6","text":"Ἡ πρώτη φιλοσοφία δεῖ πᾶν αἴτιον ἐξετάζειν κατὰ τὸ εἶδος ᾧ τὴν πρώτη τοῦ πράγματος ἐξήγησιν παρέχει.","refs":["CH_DAZY_BOO1:A14","CH_DAZY_BOO1:A15"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"From","passages":[{"id":"RAW_DAZY_BOO1:P0013","trace_type":"Composite","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"},{"id":"RAW_DAZY_BOO1:P0047","trace_type":"Composite","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_DAZY_BOO1:D7","text":"Ἡ ὑλικὴ ἀρχὴ ἀναγκάζει ζητεῖν ἑτέραν ἀρχὴν κινήσεως, ἐπειδὴ τὸ ὑποκείμενον οὐχ ἱκανῶς τὴν ἑαυτοῦ μεταβολὴν αἰτιᾶται.","refs":["CH_DAZY_BOO1:A15","CH_DAZY_BOO1:A17"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"From","passages":[{"id":"RAW_DAZY_BOO1:P0017","trace_type":"Direct","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_DAZY_BOO1:D8","text":"Ἡ τῶν προτέρων ἐξέτασις ἅμα βεβαιοῖ τὴν τετραχὴν διαίρεσιν καὶ φανερὰς ποιεῖ τὰς ἀσυνεπείας τῆς ἐλλιποῦς χρήσεως τῶν αἰτίων.","refs":["CH_DAZY_BOO1:A15","CH_DAZY_BOO1:A16","CH_DAZY_BOO1:A17"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"From","passages":[{"id":"RAW_DAZY_BOO1:P0029","trace_type":"Composite","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"},{"id":"RAW_DAZY_BOO1:P0044","trace_type":"Composite","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"},{"id":"RAW_DAZY_BOO1:P0046","trace_type":"Composite","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]}],"original_name":"Derivative Propositions"},{"number":"5","name":"Super-Derivatives","items":[{"id":"CH_DAZY_BOO1:SD1","text":"Ἡ γνωστικὴ ἄνοδος οὐχ ἁπλῆ πρόσθεσις γνωσμάτων ἐστίν, ἀλλὰ μετάθεσις ἀπὸ τοῦ ὅτι καὶ τοῦ καθ᾽ ἕκαστον εἰς τὸ διότι καὶ τὸ καθόλου.","refs":["CH_DAZY_BOO1:A3","CH_DAZY_BOO1:A4","CH_DAZY_BOO1:A6","CH_DAZY_BOO1:A8"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"From","passages":[{"id":"RAW_DAZY_BOO1:P0002","trace_type":"Structural","confidence":"medium","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"},{"id":"RAW_DAZY_BOO1:P0004","trace_type":"Structural","confidence":"medium","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"},{"id":"RAW_DAZY_BOO1:P0008","trace_type":"Structural","confidence":"medium","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_DAZY_BOO1:SD2","text":"Ἡ ἀξία τῆς σοφίας οὐ μετρεῖται τῇ ἀμέσῳ πρακτικῇ δυνάμει, ἀλλὰ τῷ βαθμῷ καθ᾽ ὃν τὰς αἰτίας καὶ τὸ τέλος γνωρίζει.","refs":["CH_DAZY_BOO1:A5","CH_DAZY_BOO1:A6","CH_DAZY_BOO1:A10","CH_DAZY_BOO1:A11"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"From","passages":[{"id":"RAW_DAZY_BOO1:P0003","trace_type":"Composite","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"},{"id":"RAW_DAZY_BOO1:P0004","trace_type":"Composite","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"},{"id":"RAW_DAZY_BOO1:P0009","trace_type":"Composite","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_DAZY_BOO1:SD3","text":"Ἡ ἐλευθερωτάτη ἐπιστήμη γίνεται ἅμα ἀρχικωτάτη, διότι ἡ μὴ ὑπηρετοῦσα ἑτέρῳ τὸ οὗ ἕνεκα τῶν ἄλλων δύναται γνωρίζειν.","refs":["CH_DAZY_BOO1:A10","CH_DAZY_BOO1:A11","CH_DAZY_BOO1:A12"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"From","passages":[{"id":"RAW_DAZY_BOO1:P0007","trace_type":"Composite","confidence":"medium","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"},{"id":"RAW_DAZY_BOO1:P0009","trace_type":"Composite","confidence":"medium","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_DAZY_BOO1:SD4","text":"Ἡ ἱστορία τῶν προτέρων φιλοσόφων μετατίθεται εἰς ἐνδογενῆ γένεσιν τῶν αἰτίων, καθ᾽ ἣν τὰ ἀνεξήγητα φαινόμενα ἀναγκάζει τὴν διάνοιαν νέαν ἀρχὴν ζητεῖν.","refs":["CH_DAZY_BOO1:A15","CH_DAZY_BOO1:A16","CH_DAZY_BOO1:A17"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"From","passages":[{"id":"RAW_DAZY_BOO1:P0013","trace_type":"Structural","confidence":"medium","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"},{"id":"RAW_DAZY_BOO1:P0017","trace_type":"Structural","confidence":"medium","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"},{"id":"RAW_DAZY_BOO1:P0019","trace_type":"Structural","confidence":"medium","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_DAZY_BOO1:SD5","text":"Ἡ τετραχὴ αἰτιότης ἐντὸς τοῦ λόγου λειτουργεῖ ὡς ὅρος πληρότητος, ἐπειδὴ πᾶσα προτέρα θεωρία ἢ εἰς ταύτην ἀνάγεται ἢ διὰ παραλείψεώς τινος αὐτῆς ἐλέγχεται.","refs":["CH_DAZY_BOO1:A15","CH_DAZY_BOO1:A16","CH_DAZY_BOO1:A17"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"From","passages":[{"id":"RAW_DAZY_BOO1:P0044","trace_type":"Structural","confidence":"medium","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"},{"id":"RAW_DAZY_BOO1:P0045","trace_type":"Structural","confidence":"medium","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"},{"id":"RAW_DAZY_BOO1:P0046","trace_type":"Structural","confidence":"medium","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_DAZY_BOO1:SD6","text":"Ἡ χωριστὴ οὐσία, ἐὰν μήτε ἐνυπάρχῃ μήτε κινῇ μήτε τέλος παρέχῃ, τὸ αἰτιώδης εἶναι μόνον ὀνόματι διασῴζει.","refs":["CH_DAZY_BOO1:A14","CH_DAZY_BOO1:A15","CH_DAZY_BOO1:A17"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"From","passages":[{"id":"RAW_DAZY_BOO1:P0059","trace_type":"Composite","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"},{"id":"RAW_DAZY_BOO1:P0060","trace_type":"Composite","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"},{"id":"RAW_DAZY_BOO1:P0066","trace_type":"Composite","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]}],"original_name":"Super-Derivatives"},{"number":"6","name":"Deep Apophatics","items":[{"id":"CH_DAZY_BOO1:DNX1","text":"Ἡ φυσικὴ ὄρεξις τοῦ εἰδέναι οὐκ ἀνάγεται ὅλη εἰς τὴν τῶν χρησίμων ζήτησιν.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_DAZY_BOO1:P0001","trace_type":"Negative","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"},{"id":"RAW_DAZY_BOO1:P0010","trace_type":"Negative","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_DAZY_BOO1:DNX2","text":"Ἡ σώρευσις τῶν ὅτι οὐ μεταβάλλεται αὐτομάτως εἰς ἐπιστήμην, ἐὰν μὴ μία καθόλου αἰτία συναγάγῃ αὐτά.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_DAZY_BOO1:P0002","trace_type":"Negative","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"},{"id":"RAW_DAZY_BOO1:P0004","trace_type":"Negative","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_DAZY_BOO1:DNX3","text":"Τὸ καθόλου οὐ καθ᾽ αὑτὸ σοφία ἐστίν, ἐὰν μὴ τὴν αἰτίαν τῶν καθ᾽ ἕκαστα δηλοῖ.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_DAZY_BOO1:P0004","trace_type":"Structural","confidence":"medium","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"},{"id":"RAW_DAZY_BOO1:P0008","trace_type":"Structural","confidence":"medium","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_DAZY_BOO1:DNX4","text":"Ἀρχὴ λεγομένη ἀλλὰ σπανίως ἢ ἀντιφατικῶς χρησιμοποιουμένη οὐκ ἔχει πλήρη αἰτιώδη δύναμιν.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_DAZY_BOO1:P0029","trace_type":"Negative","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_DAZY_BOO1:DNX5","text":"Ἡ παράθεσις πλειόνων οὐσιῶν οὐ λύει τὸ ζήτημα τῆς αἰτίας, ἐὰν ἡ πρὸς τὰ φαινόμενα σχέσις αὐτῶν μένῃ ἄδηλος.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_DAZY_BOO1:P0056","trace_type":"Negative","confidence":"medium","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"},{"id":"RAW_DAZY_BOO1:P0059","trace_type":"Negative","confidence":"medium","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"},{"id":"RAW_DAZY_BOO1:P0060","trace_type":"Negative","confidence":"medium","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_DAZY_BOO1:DNX6","text":"Τὸ αἰσθητὸν οὐκ ἐξαντλεῖ τὸ γένος τοῦ ὄντος, ἐπειδὴ ἡ περὶ τῶν ἀρχῶν ζήτησις καὶ ἀσώματα ὄντα προϋπολαμβάνει.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_DAZY_BOO1:P0048","trace_type":"Negative","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"},{"id":"RAW_DAZY_BOO1:P0052","trace_type":"Negative","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_DAZY_BOO1:DNX7","text":"Ἡ τῶν πάντων ἐπιστήμη οὐ σημαίνει χωριστὴν καθ᾽ ἕκαστον ἐπιστήμην πάντων, ἀλλὰ τὴν διὰ τῶν καθόλου ἀρχῶν γνώσιν.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_DAZY_BOO1:P0007","trace_type":"Composite","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"},{"id":"RAW_DAZY_BOO1:P0008","trace_type":"Composite","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_DAZY_BOO1:DNX8","text":"Ἡ κριτικὴ τῶν προτέρων οὐ δεῖ προσέχειν τοῖς ψελλιζομένοις μόνον, ἀλλὰ τῇ διανοίᾳ ἣν ἀναγκαῖον ἦν ἐκ τῶν λεγομένων ἀκολουθεῖν.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_DAZY_BOO1:P0028","trace_type":"Direct","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]}],"original_name":"Deep Apophatics"},{"number":"7","name":"Super-Apophatics","items":[{"id":"CH_DAZY_BOO1:SNX1","text":"Ἡ ἀρχὴ ὅτι φύσει ὀρεγόμεθα τοῦ εἰδέναι προαποκλείει τὴν παντελῆ ἀδυναμίαν τῆς γνώσεως.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[]},{"id":"CH_DAZY_BOO1:SNX2","text":"Τὸ σύστημα οὔτε τὴν αἴσθησιν ἄνευ καθόλου λόγου οὔτε τὸ χωριστὸν καθόλου ἄνευ σχέσεως πρὸς τὰ αἰσθητὰ δέχεται ὡς αὐτάρκη ἐπιστήμην.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_DAZY_BOO1:P0005","trace_type":"Composite","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"},{"id":"RAW_DAZY_BOO1:P0059","trace_type":"Composite","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"},{"id":"RAW_DAZY_BOO1:P0060","trace_type":"Composite","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_DAZY_BOO1:SNX3","text":"Ἡ ἐξήγησις τῆς τάξεως ἀπαγορεύεται νὰ καταλήγῃ εἰς ψιλὴν τύχην, ἀλλ᾽ οὐ διὰ τοῦτο ἡ ἀταξία καὶ τὸ κακὸν ἐκβάλλεται ἐκ τῆς φύσεως.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_DAZY_BOO1:P0019","trace_type":"Composite","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"},{"id":"RAW_DAZY_BOO1:P0027","trace_type":"Composite","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_DAZY_BOO1:SNX4","text":"Οὐδεμία ὑπηρετικὴ ἐπιστήμη δύναται τὴν ἀνωτάτην σοφίαν ἐπιτάττειν, ἐπειδὴ ἐκείνη τὸ τέλος καὶ τἀγαθὸν γνωρίζει.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_DAZY_BOO1:P0007","trace_type":"Composite","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"},{"id":"RAW_DAZY_BOO1:P0009","trace_type":"Composite","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_DAZY_BOO1:SNX5","text":"Τὸ σύστημα προαποκλείει πέμπτον γένος αἰτίας, οὐ δι᾽ ἰδίαν ἀπόδειξιν ἀδυνατότητος, ἀλλὰ διὰ τῆς ἀναγωγῆς πασῶν τῶν προτέρων θεωριῶν εἰς τὰ τέτταρα γένη.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_DAZY_BOO1:P0044","trace_type":"Negative","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"},{"id":"RAW_DAZY_BOO1:P0047","trace_type":"Negative","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_DAZY_BOO1:SNX6","text":"Ἡ ζητουμένη σοφία οὐ παραδίδοται ἤδη ὡς τετελεσμένη θεωρία, ἀλλὰ μόνον τὸ γένος, ὁ σκοπὸς καὶ αἱ ἀπορίαι αὐτῆς προορίζονται.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_DAZY_BOO1:P0012","trace_type":"Structural","confidence":"medium","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"},{"id":"RAW_DAZY_BOO1:P0047","trace_type":"Structural","confidence":"medium","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"},{"id":"RAW_DAZY_BOO1:P0072","trace_type":"Structural","confidence":"medium","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]}],"original_name":"Super-Apophatics"},{"number":"8","name":"Formal Generative Schema","items":[{"id":"CH_DAZY_BOO1:FGS1","text":"Ἡ δι᾽ αὑτὰς αἵρεσις τῶν αἰσθήσεων τεκμηριοῖ τὴν φυσικὴν ὄρεξιν τοῦ εἰδέναι.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_DAZY_BOO1:P0001","trace_type":"Direct","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_DAZY_BOO1:FGS2","text":"Ἡ αἴσθησις διὰ μνήμης πολλαπλασιαζομένη μίαν ἐμπειρίαν περὶ τῶν καθ᾽ ἕκαστα γεννᾷ.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_DAZY_BOO1:P0001","trace_type":"Composite","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"},{"id":"RAW_DAZY_BOO1:P0002","trace_type":"Composite","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_DAZY_BOO1:FGS3","text":"Ἡ ἐμπειρία διὰ συναγωγῆς τῶν ὁμοίων μίαν καθόλου ὑπόληψιν καὶ τέχνην γεννᾷ.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_DAZY_BOO1:P0002","trace_type":"Direct","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_DAZY_BOO1:FGS4","text":"Ἡ σύγκρισις τοῦ ὅτι πρὸς τὸ διότι μεταθέτει τὸ κριτήριον τοῦ εἰδέναι ἀπὸ τῆς ἐπιτυχίας εἰς τὴν αἰτίαν καὶ τὴν διδασκαλίαν.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_DAZY_BOO1:P0003","trace_type":"Composite","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"},{"id":"RAW_DAZY_BOO1:P0004","trace_type":"Composite","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"},{"id":"RAW_DAZY_BOO1:P0005","trace_type":"Composite","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_DAZY_BOO1:FGS5","text":"Αἱ κοινὰς ὑπολήψεις περὶ τοῦ σοφοῦ συνάγονται εἰς μίαν ἐπιστήμην τῶν πρώτων καὶ καθόλου αἰτίων.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_DAZY_BOO1:P0007","trace_type":"Composite","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"},{"id":"RAW_DAZY_BOO1:P0009","trace_type":"Composite","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_DAZY_BOO1:FGS6","text":"Ἡ γένεσις τῆς φιλοσοφίας ἐκ θαύματος ἀποδεικνύει ὅτι ἡ ζητουμένη ἐπιστήμη θεωρητικὴ καὶ αὑτῆς ἕνεκεν ἐστίν.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_DAZY_BOO1:P0010","trace_type":"Direct","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_DAZY_BOO1:FGS7","text":"Ἡ πρώτη αἰτιώδης γνώσις διαιρεῖται εἰς οὐσίαν, ὕλην, ἀρχὴν κινήσεως καὶ τέλος.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_DAZY_BOO1:P0013","trace_type":"Direct","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_DAZY_BOO1:FGS8","text":"Αἱ προγενέστεραι θεωρίαι εἰς ταῦτα τὰ γένη ἀναγόμεναι ἢ ἁρμόττουσι μερικῶς ἢ διὰ παραλείψεων καὶ ἀντιφάσεων νέας ἀπορίας γεννῶσιν.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_DAZY_BOO1:P0029","trace_type":"Structural","confidence":"medium","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"},{"id":"RAW_DAZY_BOO1:P0044","trace_type":"Structural","confidence":"medium","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"},{"id":"RAW_DAZY_BOO1:P0072","trace_type":"Structural","confidence":"medium","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]}],"original_name":"Formal Generative Schema"},{"number":"9","name":"Argument Flow Map","items":[{"id":"CH_DAZY_BOO1:AF1","text":"Ἡ φυσικὴ ὄρεξις τοῦ εἰδέναι διὰ τῆς αἰσθήσεως εἰς μνήμην καὶ ἐμπειρίαν προβαίνει.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_DAZY_BOO1:P0001","trace_type":"Composite","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"},{"id":"RAW_DAZY_BOO1:P0002","trace_type":"Composite","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_DAZY_BOO1:AF2","text":"Ἡ ἐμπειρία διὰ καθολικῆς συναγωγῆς τέχνην καὶ αἰτιώδη λόγον γεννᾷ.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_DAZY_BOO1:P0002","trace_type":"Composite","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"},{"id":"RAW_DAZY_BOO1:P0004","trace_type":"Composite","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_DAZY_BOO1:AF3","text":"Ἡ διάκρισις τοῦ ὅτι καὶ τοῦ διότι τὴν σοφίαν ὑπὲρ τὴν ψιλὴν πρακτικὴν ἐπιτυχίαν τάττει.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_DAZY_BOO1:P0003","trace_type":"Composite","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"},{"id":"RAW_DAZY_BOO1:P0004","trace_type":"Composite","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_DAZY_BOO1:AF4","text":"Αἱ περὶ τοῦ σοφοῦ ὑπολήψεις τὸ ζήτημα εἰς τὴν θεωρίαν τῶν πρώτων ἀρχῶν καὶ αἰτιῶν μετατιθέασιν.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_DAZY_BOO1:P0007","trace_type":"Composite","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"},{"id":"RAW_DAZY_BOO1:P0009","trace_type":"Composite","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_DAZY_BOO1:AF5","text":"Ἡ τετραχὴ διαίρεσις τῶν αἰτίων τὰς προτέρας φιλοσοφίας εἰς ὕλην, κίνησιν, οὐσίαν καὶ τέλος κατατάσσει.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_DAZY_BOO1:P0013","trace_type":"Composite","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"},{"id":"RAW_DAZY_BOO1:P0045","trace_type":"Composite","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_DAZY_BOO1:AF6","text":"Ἡ ἀσυνεπὴς ἢ ἐλλιπὴς χρῆσις τῶν αἰτίων τὴν ἱστορικὴν κατάταξιν εἰς προπαρασκευὴν τῶν ὑστέρων ἀποριῶν μεταβάλλει.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_DAZY_BOO1:P0029","trace_type":"Structural","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"},{"id":"RAW_DAZY_BOO1:P0047","trace_type":"Structural","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"},{"id":"RAW_DAZY_BOO1:P0072","trace_type":"Structural","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]}],"original_name":"Argument Flow Map"},{"number":"10","name":"Map-Effects","items":[{"id":"CH_DAZY_BOO1:ME1","text":"Ἡ χώρα τῆς γνώσεως διαιρεῖται οὐ κατὰ πλῆθος γνωσμάτων μόνον, ἀλλὰ κατὰ βαθμοὺς αἰτιώδους καθολικότητος.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_DAZY_BOO1:P0003","trace_type":"Structural","confidence":"medium","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"},{"id":"RAW_DAZY_BOO1:P0004","trace_type":"Structural","confidence":"medium","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"},{"id":"RAW_DAZY_BOO1:P0008","trace_type":"Structural","confidence":"medium","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_DAZY_BOO1:ME2","text":"Ἡ πρακτικὴ δύναμις καὶ ἡ σοφία παύονται ταὐτὸν εἶναι, ἐπειδὴ ἡ μὲν τὸ καθ᾽ ἕκαστον, ἡ δὲ τὸ διότι δύναται ἔχειν.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_DAZY_BOO1:P0003","trace_type":"Composite","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"},{"id":"RAW_DAZY_BOO1:P0004","trace_type":"Composite","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_DAZY_BOO1:ME3","text":"Αἱ ἐπιστῆμαι κατατάσσονται ἐκ νέου κατὰ τὸ εἶναι ὑπηρετικαί, ποιητικαί, θεωρητικαὶ ἢ ἀρχικαί.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_DAZY_BOO1:P0006","trace_type":"Composite","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"},{"id":"RAW_DAZY_BOO1:P0007","trace_type":"Composite","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_DAZY_BOO1:ME4","text":"Ἡ τῶν φιλοσόφων διαδοχὴ παύει ψιλὴ χρονολογικὴ παράθεσις εἶναι καὶ γίνεται χάρτης τῶν μερικῶς ἀνευρισκομένων αἰτίων.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_DAZY_BOO1:P0013","trace_type":"Composite","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"},{"id":"RAW_DAZY_BOO1:P0044","trace_type":"Composite","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_DAZY_BOO1:ME5","text":"Τὸ ὂν καθίσταται θεωρητὸν διὰ τεσσάρων ἀλλήλοις μὴ ἀντικαθισταμένων ἐρωτήσεων, τί ἐστιν, ἐξ οὗ ἐστιν, ὑφ᾽ οὗ κινεῖται καὶ οὗ ἕνεκα ἐστίν.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_DAZY_BOO1:P0013","trace_type":"Direct","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_DAZY_BOO1:ME6","text":"Τὰ χωριστὰ εἴδη μετατίθενται ἀπὸ φερομένης καθολικῆς λύσεως εἰς μερικὴν θεωρίαν τοῦ τί ἐστιν, ἧς ἡ πρὸς τὰ αἰσθητὰ αἰτιότης μένει ἀνεξήγητος.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_DAZY_BOO1:P0045","trace_type":"Composite","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"},{"id":"RAW_DAZY_BOO1:P0059","trace_type":"Composite","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"},{"id":"RAW_DAZY_BOO1:P0060","trace_type":"Composite","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_DAZY_BOO1:ME7","text":"Τὸ θαυμάζειν μετατίθεται ἀπὸ μόνιμον πάθος εἰς ἀφετηρίαν, ἧς τέλος ἐστὶν ἡ διὰ τῆς αἰτίας ἀναίρεσις τοῦ θαύματος.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_DAZY_BOO1:P0010","trace_type":"Composite","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"},{"id":"RAW_DAZY_BOO1:P0012","trace_type":"Composite","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]}],"original_name":"Map-Effects"},{"number":"11","name":"Altitude Levels","items":[{"id":"CH_DAZY_BOO1:ALT1","text":"Κατὰ τὸν ἐπιφανειακὸν βαθμὸν ἐξετάζονται αἱ γνωστικαὶ δυνάμεις, αἱ κοινὰς ὑπολήψεις περὶ σοφίας καὶ αἱ δόξαι τῶν προτέρων φιλοσόφων.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_DAZY_BOO1:P0001","trace_type":"Composite","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"},{"id":"RAW_DAZY_BOO1:P0007","trace_type":"Composite","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"},{"id":"RAW_DAZY_BOO1:P0013","trace_type":"Composite","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_DAZY_BOO1:ALT2","text":"Κατὰ τὸν τῆς διατάξεως βαθμὸν ἐνεργοῦσιν ἡ ἀπὸ αἰσθήσεως εἰς αἰτίαν τάξις καὶ ἡ τετραχὴ διαίρεσις τῆς αἰτιότητος.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_DAZY_BOO1:P0001","trace_type":"Structural","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"},{"id":"RAW_DAZY_BOO1:P0002","trace_type":"Structural","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"},{"id":"RAW_DAZY_BOO1:P0004","trace_type":"Structural","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"},{"id":"RAW_DAZY_BOO1:P0013","trace_type":"Structural","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_DAZY_BOO1:ALT3","text":"Κατὰ τὸν ὑπερστρατηγικὸν βαθμὸν ἡ ἱστορία τῶν δοξῶν γίνεται ὄργανον βεβαιώσεως τοῦ αἰτιώδους σχήματος καὶ παραγωγῆς ἀποριῶν.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_DAZY_BOO1:P0013","trace_type":"Structural","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"},{"id":"RAW_DAZY_BOO1:P0044","trace_type":"Structural","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"},{"id":"RAW_DAZY_BOO1:P0047","trace_type":"Structural","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_DAZY_BOO1:ALT4","text":"Κατὰ τὸν περὶ ὄντος καὶ χρόνου βαθμὸν τὸ γιγνόμενον νοεῖται διὰ προτέρων τῇ φύσει αἰτίων, ἡ δὲ ἱστορικῶς ὑστέρα θεωρία ἐπανέρχεται ἐπὶ τὰ ὀντολογικῶς πρῶτα.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_DAZY_BOO1:P0013","trace_type":"Structural","confidence":"medium","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"},{"id":"RAW_DAZY_BOO1:P0050","trace_type":"Structural","confidence":"medium","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]}],"original_name":"Altitude Levels"},{"number":"12","name":"Minimal Reconstruction Test","items":[{"id":"CH_DAZY_BOO1:MRT1","text":"Τὰ ἐξῃρημένα ἀξιώματα ἱκανὰ ἐστὶ πρὸς ἀνασύστασιν τῆς ἀνόδου ἀπὸ τῆς αἰσθήσεως εἰς τὴν τῶν πρώτων αἰτίων σοφίαν.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_DAZY_BOO1:P0001","trace_type":"Structural","confidence":"medium","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"},{"id":"RAW_DAZY_BOO1:P0002","trace_type":"Structural","confidence":"medium","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"},{"id":"RAW_DAZY_BOO1:P0004","trace_type":"Structural","confidence":"medium","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"},{"id":"RAW_DAZY_BOO1:P0009","trace_type":"Structural","confidence":"medium","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_DAZY_BOO1:MRT2","text":"Ἰσχυροτάτη ἡ διάταξις ἐστὶν ὅπου ἡ διάκρισις τοῦ ὅτι καὶ τοῦ διότι συνδέεται πρὸς τὴν αἰτίαν, τὴν διδασκαλίαν καὶ τὴν καθολικότητα.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_DAZY_BOO1:P0004","trace_type":"Structural","confidence":"medium","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"},{"id":"RAW_DAZY_BOO1:P0005","trace_type":"Structural","confidence":"medium","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"},{"id":"RAW_DAZY_BOO1:P0008","trace_type":"Structural","confidence":"medium","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_DAZY_BOO1:MRT3","text":"Ἡ φαινομένη ἀντίφασις, καθ᾽ ἣν ὁ ἔμπειρος πράττει βέλτιον ὁ δὲ τεχνίτης σοφώτερός ἐστι, λύεται διὰ τῆς διακρίσεως πρακτικῆς ἐπιτυχίας καὶ αἰτιώδους γνώσεως.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_DAZY_BOO1:P0003","trace_type":"Tension","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"},{"id":"RAW_DAZY_BOO1:P0004","trace_type":"Tension","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_DAZY_BOO1:MRT4","text":"Ἀδιοριστότερον μένει πῶς ἡ καθόλου ἐπιστήμη οἶδέ πως πάντα ἄνευ τῆς καθ᾽ ἕκαστον γνώσεως καὶ πῶς αὕτη συμβιβάζεται πρὸς τὴν ἀνάγκην προγιγνωσκομένων.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_DAZY_BOO1:P0007","trace_type":"Tension","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"},{"id":"RAW_DAZY_BOO1:P0008","trace_type":"Tension","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"},{"id":"RAW_DAZY_BOO1:P0069","trace_type":"Tension","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_DAZY_BOO1:MRT5","text":"Ἡ πληρότης τῆς τετραχῆς διαιρέσεως στηρίζεται ἐπὶ προτέρᾳ φυσικῇ θεωρίᾳ καὶ ἐπὶ τῇ ἐπαγωγῇ ὅτι οὐδεὶς τῶν προτέρων ἄλλης αἰτίας ἥψατο.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_DAZY_BOO1:P0013","trace_type":"Composite","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"},{"id":"RAW_DAZY_BOO1:P0044","trace_type":"Composite","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_DAZY_BOO1:MRT6","text":"Ἡ ἐλευθερία καὶ θειότης τῆς σοφίας στηρίζεται ἐπὶ τῇ ἀναλογίᾳ τοῦ ἐλευθέρου ἀνθρώπου καὶ ἐπὶ ταῖς κοινὰς περὶ τοῦ σοφοῦ ὑπολήψεσιν.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_DAZY_BOO1:P0007","trace_type":"Composite","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"},{"id":"RAW_DAZY_BOO1:P0011","trace_type":"Composite","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_DAZY_BOO1:MRT7","text":"Ἡ τελικὴ αἰτία ἀναγκαίως μὲν εἰσάγεται διὰ τοῦ ἀγαθοῦ καὶ τῆς τάξεως, ἀμυδρότερον δὲ ἢ ἡ ὕλη καὶ ἡ κίνησις ἐκ τῆς τῶν προτέρων ἐξετάσεως βεβαιοῦται.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_DAZY_BOO1:P0019","trace_type":"Composite","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"},{"id":"RAW_DAZY_BOO1:P0045","trace_type":"Composite","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"},{"id":"RAW_DAZY_BOO1:P0046","trace_type":"Composite","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_DAZY_BOO1:MRT8","text":"Ὁ τῶν εἰδῶν ἔλεγχος δείκνυσιν ὅτι ἡ χωριστὴ οὐσία οὐχ ἱκανῶς αἰτιᾶται τὰ αἰσθητά, οὐ μέντοι ἐνταῦθα πλήρως ἀποδίδωσι πῶς ἡ οὐσία ἐνυπάρχει τοῖς πράγμασιν.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_DAZY_BOO1:P0059","trace_type":"Composite","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"},{"id":"RAW_DAZY_BOO1:P0060","trace_type":"Composite","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"},{"id":"RAW_DAZY_BOO1:P0062","trace_type":"Composite","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_DAZY_BOO1:MRT9","text":"Αἱ περὶ τῶν παλαιοτάτων, τῶν ποιητῶν καὶ τῆς προτεραιότητος ἑκάστου ἀποδόσεις διατηροῦνται ὡς εἰκότα ἢ λεγόμενα καὶ οὐχ ὡς ἀναγκαῖαι βεβαιώσεις.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_DAZY_BOO1:P0015","trace_type":"Direct","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"},{"id":"RAW_DAZY_BOO1:P0016","trace_type":"Direct","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"},{"id":"RAW_DAZY_BOO1:P0021","trace_type":"Direct","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"},{"id":"RAW_DAZY_BOO1:P0027","trace_type":"Direct","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_DAZY_BOO1:MRT10","text":"Ἡ ἀνασύστασις σῴζει τὴν ἀληθῆ κίνησιν τοῦ λόγου, ὅτι ἀπὸ τῆς τοῦ εἰδέναι ὀρέξεως εἰς τὸν διορισμὸν τῆς σοφίας, ἐκεῖθεν δὲ εἰς τὴν τετραχὴν αἰτιότητα, τὸν ἔλεγχον τῶν προτέρων καὶ τὰς ὑστέρους ἀπορίας προβαίνει.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_DAZY_BOO1:P0001","trace_type":"Structural","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"},{"id":"RAW_DAZY_BOO1:P0007","trace_type":"Structural","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"},{"id":"RAW_DAZY_BOO1:P0009","trace_type":"Structural","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"},{"id":"RAW_DAZY_BOO1:P0013","trace_type":"Structural","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"},{"id":"RAW_DAZY_BOO1:P0044","trace_type":"Structural","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"},{"id":"RAW_DAZY_BOO1:P0072","trace_type":"Structural","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]}],"original_name":"Minimal Reconstruction Test"}],"language_code":"grc","language_name":"Ἑλληνική","translations":[{"id":"CAREVAR_20261015T174351657240Z_28dba379","language_code":"en","language_name":"English","title":"Book 1","source_sha256":"5a1ea952134154148204e0b171a6a70d05e63d524323d29858d395e3727990c5","sha256":"c051575c1292ffcf8c8b8cf1c1ac6f781283aa97835ad35278dccbb38aa23229","rendition_type":"machine","review_status":"machine","model":"gpt-5.6-terra","created":"2026-10-15T17:43:51.659139+00:00","sections":[{"number":"1","name":"Argumental Spine","items":[{"id":"CH_DAZY_BOO1:AS1","text":"The question is which study of causes and principles is wisdom and what aim the method concerning it pursues.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_DAZY_BOO1:P0007","trace_type":"Composite","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"},{"id":"RAW_DAZY_BOO1:P0012","trace_type":"Composite","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_DAZY_BOO1:AS2","text":"Wisdom is posited as a theoretical science of first principles and causes, choiceworthy for its own sake and governing over the other sciences.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_DAZY_BOO1:P0007","trace_type":"Composite","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"},{"id":"RAW_DAZY_BOO1:P0009","trace_type":"Composite","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_DAZY_BOO1:AS3","text":"The position arises through the ascent from perception to memory, experience, universal conception, and causal science.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_DAZY_BOO1:P0001","trace_type":"Composite","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"},{"id":"RAW_DAZY_BOO1:P0002","trace_type":"Composite","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"},{"id":"RAW_DAZY_BOO1:P0004","trace_type":"Composite","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_DAZY_BOO1:AS4","text":"The argument proceeds diagnostically from the opinions concerning the wise person, and genealogically through examination of earlier philosophers.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_DAZY_BOO1:P0007","trace_type":"Composite","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"},{"id":"RAW_DAZY_BOO1:P0013","trace_type":"Composite","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_DAZY_BOO1:AS5","text":"The fourfold division of causes becomes a rule for arranging, comparing, and testing the earlier theories.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_DAZY_BOO1:P0013","trace_type":"Composite","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"},{"id":"RAW_DAZY_BOO1:P0044","trace_type":"Composite","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"},{"id":"RAW_DAZY_BOO1:P0047","trace_type":"Composite","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_DAZY_BOO1:AS6","text":"The movement concludes from the natural desire to know, through the definition of wisdom, in the classification of causes and thence in the remaining aporiai.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_DAZY_BOO1:P0001","trace_type":"Structural","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"},{"id":"RAW_DAZY_BOO1:P0009","trace_type":"Structural","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"},{"id":"RAW_DAZY_BOO1:P0013","trace_type":"Structural","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"},{"id":"RAW_DAZY_BOO1:P0072","trace_type":"Structural","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]}],"original_name":"Argumental Spine"},{"number":"2","name":"Load-Bearing Axioms","items":[{"id":"CH_DAZY_BOO1:A1","text":"All human beings by nature desire to know.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_DAZY_BOO1:P0001","trace_type":"Direct","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_DAZY_BOO1:A2","text":"Memory together with perception makes learning and practical wisdom more powerful.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_DAZY_BOO1:P0001","trace_type":"Direct","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_DAZY_BOO1:A3","text":"Many memories of the same thing constitute the capacity of a single experience.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_DAZY_BOO1:P0002","trace_type":"Direct","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_DAZY_BOO1:A4","text":"Art arises when, from many experiential conceptions, one universal conception concerning similar things comes to be.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_DAZY_BOO1:P0002","trace_type":"Direct","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_DAZY_BOO1:A5","text":"Actions and generations concern particulars; hence experience often succeeds more in practice than discourse without experience.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_DAZY_BOO1:P0003","trace_type":"Direct","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_DAZY_BOO1:A6","text":"Knowledge of the why and of the cause makes one know more than knowledge of the that alone.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_DAZY_BOO1:P0004","trace_type":"Direct","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_DAZY_BOO1:A7","text":"The ability to teach is a sign of knowledge according to causes.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_DAZY_BOO1:P0005","trace_type":"Direct","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_DAZY_BOO1:A8","text":"One who has universal science to the greatest degree knows somehow all the things subordinate to it, although not knowing each individually.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_DAZY_BOO1:P0008","trace_type":"Direct","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_DAZY_BOO1:A9","text":"The sciences of first and fewer principles are more exact and more teachable than those produced by addition.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_DAZY_BOO1:P0008","trace_type":"Direct","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_DAZY_BOO1:A10","text":"The science choiceworthy for its own sake and for the sake of knowing is more wisdom than that choiceworthy for its outcomes.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_DAZY_BOO1:P0007","trace_type":"Direct","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_DAZY_BOO1:A11","text":"The science that knows the final cause and the good is more governing than the serving science.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_DAZY_BOO1:P0009","trace_type":"Direct","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_DAZY_BOO1:A12","text":"Common opinions concerning the wise person converge upon the same science of first principles and causes.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_DAZY_BOO1:P0007","trace_type":"Composite","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"},{"id":"RAW_DAZY_BOO1:P0009","trace_type":"Composite","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_DAZY_BOO1:A13","text":"Philosophy begins from wonder and awareness of one's own ignorance; hence it is sought for the sake of knowing and not for the sake of any use.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_DAZY_BOO1:P0010","trace_type":"Direct","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_DAZY_BOO1:A14","text":"Then we say that we know each thing when we suppose that we know its first cause.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_DAZY_BOO1:P0013","trace_type":"Direct","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_DAZY_BOO1:A15","text":"Causes are spoken of in four ways: as substance and what-it-was-to-be, as matter and substrate, as that from which motion comes, and as that for the sake of which and the good.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_DAZY_BOO1:P0013","trace_type":"Direct","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_DAZY_BOO1:A16","text":"According to the induction of the argument, none of the earlier thinkers posited a principle outside the four causes, but all touched upon them dimly and partially.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_DAZY_BOO1:P0044","trace_type":"Direct","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_DAZY_BOO1:A17","text":"What is called a cause but is not adequately and consistently employed in relation to generation, motion, substance, or end is not a complete explanation.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_DAZY_BOO1:P0029","trace_type":"Structural","confidence":"medium","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"},{"id":"RAW_DAZY_BOO1:P0045","trace_type":"Structural","confidence":"medium","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"},{"id":"RAW_DAZY_BOO1:P0046","trace_type":"Structural","confidence":"medium","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]}],"original_name":"Load-Bearing Axioms"},{"number":"3","name":"Apophatic Structure","items":[{"id":"CH_DAZY_BOO1:NX1","text":"No perception is wisdom, because it indicates the that but does not state the why.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_DAZY_BOO1:P0005","trace_type":"Negative","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_DAZY_BOO1:NX2","text":"Experience does not possess by itself knowledge of the universal and of the cause.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_DAZY_BOO1:P0003","trace_type":"Negative","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"},{"id":"RAW_DAZY_BOO1:P0004","trace_type":"Negative","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_DAZY_BOO1:NX3","text":"Practical success concerning particulars does not by itself constitute knowing or wisdom.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_DAZY_BOO1:P0003","trace_type":"Negative","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"},{"id":"RAW_DAZY_BOO1:P0004","trace_type":"Negative","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_DAZY_BOO1:NX4","text":"The wisdom sought is neither productive nor ordered toward another use.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_DAZY_BOO1:P0010","trace_type":"Negative","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_DAZY_BOO1:NX5","text":"The divine cannot be envious, so that divine science is not to be forbidden to a human being.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_DAZY_BOO1:P0011","trace_type":"Negative","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_DAZY_BOO1:NX6","text":"The substrate and matter do not by themselves make themselves change.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_DAZY_BOO1:P0017","trace_type":"Negative","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_DAZY_BOO1:NX7","text":"The spontaneous and chance do not adequately account for the order and good condition of beings.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_DAZY_BOO1:P0019","trace_type":"Negative","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_DAZY_BOO1:NX8","text":"One bodily matter cannot simultaneously be the cause of incorporeal things, motion, and what something is.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_DAZY_BOO1:P0048","trace_type":"Direct","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_DAZY_BOO1:NX9","text":"Numbers and oppositions without an account of motion do not generate the nature of sensibles.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_DAZY_BOO1:P0054","trace_type":"Negative","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_DAZY_BOO1:NX10","text":"Participation and imitation, if they remain mere names, do not show how forms are causes of sensibles.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_DAZY_BOO1:P0041","trace_type":"Negative","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"},{"id":"RAW_DAZY_BOO1:P0060","trace_type":"Negative","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_DAZY_BOO1:NX11","text":"Separate forms are shown to be adequate causes neither of motion nor of generation nor of the being of participants.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_DAZY_BOO1:P0059","trace_type":"Negative","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"},{"id":"RAW_DAZY_BOO1:P0060","trace_type":"Negative","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"},{"id":"RAW_DAZY_BOO1:P0062","trace_type":"Negative","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_DAZY_BOO1:NX12","text":"It is not possible to seek the elements of all beings indiscriminately, because being is spoken of in many ways.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_DAZY_BOO1:P0068","trace_type":"Negative","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]}],"original_name":"Apophatic Structure"},{"number":"4","name":"Derivative Propositions","items":[{"id":"CH_DAZY_BOO1:D1","text":"The transition from perception to art occurs through the preservation, multiplication, and universal connection of particular cognitions.","refs":["CH_DAZY_BOO1:A2","CH_DAZY_BOO1:A3","CH_DAZY_BOO1:A4"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"From","passages":[{"id":"RAW_DAZY_BOO1:P0001","trace_type":"Composite","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"},{"id":"RAW_DAZY_BOO1:P0002","trace_type":"Composite","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_DAZY_BOO1:D2","text":"Practical success and causal science can be separated, because the former concerns particulars whereas the latter knows the why.","refs":["CH_DAZY_BOO1:A5","CH_DAZY_BOO1:A6"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"From","passages":[{"id":"RAW_DAZY_BOO1:P0003","trace_type":"Composite","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"},{"id":"RAW_DAZY_BOO1:P0004","trace_type":"Composite","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_DAZY_BOO1:D3","text":"Teaching requires the universal cause, because the particular as such supplies neither a common account nor communicable science.","refs":["CH_DAZY_BOO1:A4","CH_DAZY_BOO1:A6","CH_DAZY_BOO1:A7"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"From","passages":[{"id":"RAW_DAZY_BOO1:P0002","trace_type":"Composite","confidence":"medium","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"},{"id":"RAW_DAZY_BOO1:P0004","trace_type":"Composite","confidence":"medium","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"},{"id":"RAW_DAZY_BOO1:P0005","trace_type":"Composite","confidence":"medium","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_DAZY_BOO1:D4","text":"The wisdom sought is the most exact, most teachable, and most governing theory of first and universal causes.","refs":["CH_DAZY_BOO1:A8","CH_DAZY_BOO1:A9","CH_DAZY_BOO1:A11","CH_DAZY_BOO1:A12"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"From","passages":[{"id":"RAW_DAZY_BOO1:P0008","trace_type":"Composite","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"},{"id":"RAW_DAZY_BOO1:P0009","trace_type":"Composite","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_DAZY_BOO1:D5","text":"Wisdom is called free because it exists for its own sake and not as an instrument of another need.","refs":["CH_DAZY_BOO1:A10","CH_DAZY_BOO1:A13"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"From","passages":[{"id":"RAW_DAZY_BOO1:P0010","trace_type":"Composite","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"},{"id":"RAW_DAZY_BOO1:P0011","trace_type":"Composite","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_DAZY_BOO1:D6","text":"First philosophy must examine every cause according to the form in which it provides the first explanation of the thing.","refs":["CH_DAZY_BOO1:A14","CH_DAZY_BOO1:A15"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"From","passages":[{"id":"RAW_DAZY_BOO1:P0013","trace_type":"Composite","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"},{"id":"RAW_DAZY_BOO1:P0047","trace_type":"Composite","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_DAZY_BOO1:D7","text":"The material principle compels the seeking of another principle of motion, because the substrate does not adequately account for its own change.","refs":["CH_DAZY_BOO1:A15","CH_DAZY_BOO1:A17"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"From","passages":[{"id":"RAW_DAZY_BOO1:P0017","trace_type":"Direct","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_DAZY_BOO1:D8","text":"Examination of the earlier thinkers at once confirms the fourfold division and makes manifest the inconsistencies of deficient use of causes.","refs":["CH_DAZY_BOO1:A15","CH_DAZY_BOO1:A16","CH_DAZY_BOO1:A17"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"From","passages":[{"id":"RAW_DAZY_BOO1:P0029","trace_type":"Composite","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"},{"id":"RAW_DAZY_BOO1:P0044","trace_type":"Composite","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"},{"id":"RAW_DAZY_BOO1:P0046","trace_type":"Composite","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]}],"original_name":"Derivative Propositions"},{"number":"5","name":"Super-Derivatives","items":[{"id":"CH_DAZY_BOO1:SD1","text":"The cognitive ascent is not a simple addition of cognitions, but a transition from the that and the particular to the why and the universal.","refs":["CH_DAZY_BOO1:A3","CH_DAZY_BOO1:A4","CH_DAZY_BOO1:A6","CH_DAZY_BOO1:A8"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"From","passages":[{"id":"RAW_DAZY_BOO1:P0002","trace_type":"Structural","confidence":"medium","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"},{"id":"RAW_DAZY_BOO1:P0004","trace_type":"Structural","confidence":"medium","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"},{"id":"RAW_DAZY_BOO1:P0008","trace_type":"Structural","confidence":"medium","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_DAZY_BOO1:SD2","text":"The worth of wisdom is not measured by immediate practical power, but by the degree to which it knows causes and the end.","refs":["CH_DAZY_BOO1:A5","CH_DAZY_BOO1:A6","CH_DAZY_BOO1:A10","CH_DAZY_BOO1:A11"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"From","passages":[{"id":"RAW_DAZY_BOO1:P0003","trace_type":"Composite","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"},{"id":"RAW_DAZY_BOO1:P0004","trace_type":"Composite","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"},{"id":"RAW_DAZY_BOO1:P0009","trace_type":"Composite","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_DAZY_BOO1:SD3","text":"The freest science becomes at the same time the most governing, because what does not serve another can know the final cause of the others.","refs":["CH_DAZY_BOO1:A10","CH_DAZY_BOO1:A11","CH_DAZY_BOO1:A12"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"From","passages":[{"id":"RAW_DAZY_BOO1:P0007","trace_type":"Composite","confidence":"medium","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"},{"id":"RAW_DAZY_BOO1:P0009","trace_type":"Composite","confidence":"medium","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_DAZY_BOO1:SD4","text":"The history of earlier philosophers is transformed into an endogenous generation of causes, in which unexplained phenomena compel the intellect to seek a new principle.","refs":["CH_DAZY_BOO1:A15","CH_DAZY_BOO1:A16","CH_DAZY_BOO1:A17"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"From","passages":[{"id":"RAW_DAZY_BOO1:P0013","trace_type":"Structural","confidence":"medium","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"},{"id":"RAW_DAZY_BOO1:P0017","trace_type":"Structural","confidence":"medium","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"},{"id":"RAW_DAZY_BOO1:P0019","trace_type":"Structural","confidence":"medium","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_DAZY_BOO1:SD5","text":"Fourfold causality functions within the account as a condition of completeness, because every earlier theory is either reduced to it or tested through some omission of it.","refs":["CH_DAZY_BOO1:A15","CH_DAZY_BOO1:A16","CH_DAZY_BOO1:A17"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"From","passages":[{"id":"RAW_DAZY_BOO1:P0044","trace_type":"Structural","confidence":"medium","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"},{"id":"RAW_DAZY_BOO1:P0045","trace_type":"Structural","confidence":"medium","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"},{"id":"RAW_DAZY_BOO1:P0046","trace_type":"Structural","confidence":"medium","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_DAZY_BOO1:SD6","text":"Separate substance, if it neither inheres nor moves nor supplies an end, preserves being causal in name alone.","refs":["CH_DAZY_BOO1:A14","CH_DAZY_BOO1:A15","CH_DAZY_BOO1:A17"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"From","passages":[{"id":"RAW_DAZY_BOO1:P0059","trace_type":"Composite","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"},{"id":"RAW_DAZY_BOO1:P0060","trace_type":"Composite","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"},{"id":"RAW_DAZY_BOO1:P0066","trace_type":"Composite","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]}],"original_name":"Super-Derivatives"},{"number":"6","name":"Deep Apophatics","items":[{"id":"CH_DAZY_BOO1:DNX1","text":"The natural desire to know is not wholly reducible to the pursuit of useful things.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_DAZY_BOO1:P0001","trace_type":"Negative","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"},{"id":"RAW_DAZY_BOO1:P0010","trace_type":"Negative","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_DAZY_BOO1:DNX2","text":"The accumulation of thats does not automatically turn into science unless one universal cause gathers them together.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_DAZY_BOO1:P0002","trace_type":"Negative","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"},{"id":"RAW_DAZY_BOO1:P0004","trace_type":"Negative","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_DAZY_BOO1:DNX3","text":"The universal is not by itself wisdom unless it discloses the cause of particulars.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_DAZY_BOO1:P0004","trace_type":"Structural","confidence":"medium","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"},{"id":"RAW_DAZY_BOO1:P0008","trace_type":"Structural","confidence":"medium","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_DAZY_BOO1:DNX4","text":"What is called a principle but used rarely or contradictorily does not possess complete causal power.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_DAZY_BOO1:P0029","trace_type":"Negative","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_DAZY_BOO1:DNX5","text":"The positing of several substances does not solve the question of cause if their relation to phenomena remains unclear.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_DAZY_BOO1:P0056","trace_type":"Negative","confidence":"medium","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"},{"id":"RAW_DAZY_BOO1:P0059","trace_type":"Negative","confidence":"medium","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"},{"id":"RAW_DAZY_BOO1:P0060","trace_type":"Negative","confidence":"medium","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_DAZY_BOO1:DNX6","text":"The sensible does not exhaust the genus of being, because inquiry concerning principles presupposes incorporeal beings as well.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_DAZY_BOO1:P0048","trace_type":"Negative","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"},{"id":"RAW_DAZY_BOO1:P0052","trace_type":"Negative","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_DAZY_BOO1:DNX7","text":"Knowledge of all things does not signify separate particular knowledge of all things, but knowledge through universal principles.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_DAZY_BOO1:P0007","trace_type":"Composite","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"},{"id":"RAW_DAZY_BOO1:P0008","trace_type":"Composite","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_DAZY_BOO1:DNX8","text":"Criticism of earlier thinkers should not attend only to what is stammered out, but to the thought that it was necessary to follow from what was said.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_DAZY_BOO1:P0028","trace_type":"Direct","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]}],"original_name":"Deep Apophatics"},{"number":"7","name":"Super-Apophatics","items":[{"id":"CH_DAZY_BOO1:SNX1","text":"The principle that we naturally desire to know precludes in advance the complete impossibility of knowledge.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[]},{"id":"CH_DAZY_BOO1:SNX2","text":"The system accepts neither perception without a universal account nor the separate universal without relation to sensibles as self-sufficient science.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_DAZY_BOO1:P0005","trace_type":"Composite","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"},{"id":"RAW_DAZY_BOO1:P0059","trace_type":"Composite","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"},{"id":"RAW_DAZY_BOO1:P0060","trace_type":"Composite","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_DAZY_BOO1:SNX3","text":"The explanation of order is forbidden to end in bare chance, yet disorder and evil are not thereby expelled from nature.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_DAZY_BOO1:P0019","trace_type":"Composite","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"},{"id":"RAW_DAZY_BOO1:P0027","trace_type":"Composite","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_DAZY_BOO1:SNX4","text":"No serving science can command the highest wisdom, because that wisdom knows the end and the good.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_DAZY_BOO1:P0007","trace_type":"Composite","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"},{"id":"RAW_DAZY_BOO1:P0009","trace_type":"Composite","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_DAZY_BOO1:SNX5","text":"The system precludes in advance a fifth kind of cause, not through a separate proof of impossibility, but through the reduction of all earlier theories to the four kinds.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_DAZY_BOO1:P0044","trace_type":"Negative","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"},{"id":"RAW_DAZY_BOO1:P0047","trace_type":"Negative","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_DAZY_BOO1:SNX6","text":"The wisdom sought is not yet delivered as a completed theory, but only its genus, aim, and aporiai are set forth in advance.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_DAZY_BOO1:P0012","trace_type":"Structural","confidence":"medium","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"},{"id":"RAW_DAZY_BOO1:P0047","trace_type":"Structural","confidence":"medium","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"},{"id":"RAW_DAZY_BOO1:P0072","trace_type":"Structural","confidence":"medium","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]}],"original_name":"Super-Apophatics"},{"number":"8","name":"Formal Generative Schema","items":[{"id":"CH_DAZY_BOO1:FGS1","text":"The choice of perceptions for their own sake furnishes evidence for the natural desire to know.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_DAZY_BOO1:P0001","trace_type":"Direct","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_DAZY_BOO1:FGS2","text":"Perception multiplied through memory generates one experience concerning particulars.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_DAZY_BOO1:P0001","trace_type":"Composite","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"},{"id":"RAW_DAZY_BOO1:P0002","trace_type":"Composite","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_DAZY_BOO1:FGS3","text":"Experience, through collection of similar things, generates one universal conception and art.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_DAZY_BOO1:P0002","trace_type":"Direct","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_DAZY_BOO1:FGS4","text":"Comparison of the that with the why shifts the criterion of knowing from success to cause and teaching.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_DAZY_BOO1:P0003","trace_type":"Composite","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"},{"id":"RAW_DAZY_BOO1:P0004","trace_type":"Composite","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"},{"id":"RAW_DAZY_BOO1:P0005","trace_type":"Composite","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_DAZY_BOO1:FGS5","text":"Common opinions concerning the wise person are gathered into one science of first and universal causes.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_DAZY_BOO1:P0007","trace_type":"Composite","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"},{"id":"RAW_DAZY_BOO1:P0009","trace_type":"Composite","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_DAZY_BOO1:FGS6","text":"The genesis of philosophy from wonder proves that the science sought is theoretical and for its own sake.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_DAZY_BOO1:P0010","trace_type":"Direct","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_DAZY_BOO1:FGS7","text":"First causal knowledge is divided into substance, matter, principle of motion, and end.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_DAZY_BOO1:P0013","trace_type":"Direct","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_DAZY_BOO1:FGS8","text":"Earlier theories, reduced to these kinds, either fit partially or generate new aporiai through omissions and contradictions.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_DAZY_BOO1:P0029","trace_type":"Structural","confidence":"medium","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"},{"id":"RAW_DAZY_BOO1:P0044","trace_type":"Structural","confidence":"medium","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"},{"id":"RAW_DAZY_BOO1:P0072","trace_type":"Structural","confidence":"medium","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]}],"original_name":"Formal Generative Schema"},{"number":"9","name":"Argument Flow Map","items":[{"id":"CH_DAZY_BOO1:AF1","text":"The natural desire to know proceeds through perception to memory and experience.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_DAZY_BOO1:P0001","trace_type":"Composite","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"},{"id":"RAW_DAZY_BOO1:P0002","trace_type":"Composite","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_DAZY_BOO1:AF2","text":"Experience through universal collection generates art and causal account.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_DAZY_BOO1:P0002","trace_type":"Composite","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"},{"id":"RAW_DAZY_BOO1:P0004","trace_type":"Composite","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_DAZY_BOO1:AF3","text":"The distinction between the that and the why places wisdom above bare practical success.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_DAZY_BOO1:P0003","trace_type":"Composite","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"},{"id":"RAW_DAZY_BOO1:P0004","trace_type":"Composite","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_DAZY_BOO1:AF4","text":"Opinions concerning the wise person transfer the inquiry to the theory of first principles and causes.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_DAZY_BOO1:P0007","trace_type":"Composite","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"},{"id":"RAW_DAZY_BOO1:P0009","trace_type":"Composite","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_DAZY_BOO1:AF5","text":"The fourfold division of causes classifies earlier philosophies under matter, motion, substance, and end.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_DAZY_BOO1:P0013","trace_type":"Composite","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"},{"id":"RAW_DAZY_BOO1:P0045","trace_type":"Composite","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_DAZY_BOO1:AF6","text":"Inconsistent or deficient use of causes transforms historical classification into preparation for later aporiai.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_DAZY_BOO1:P0029","trace_type":"Structural","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"},{"id":"RAW_DAZY_BOO1:P0047","trace_type":"Structural","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"},{"id":"RAW_DAZY_BOO1:P0072","trace_type":"Structural","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]}],"original_name":"Argument Flow Map"},{"number":"10","name":"Map-Effects","items":[{"id":"CH_DAZY_BOO1:ME1","text":"The domain of knowledge is divided not according to the quantity of cognitions alone, but according to degrees of causal universality.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_DAZY_BOO1:P0003","trace_type":"Structural","confidence":"medium","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"},{"id":"RAW_DAZY_BOO1:P0004","trace_type":"Structural","confidence":"medium","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"},{"id":"RAW_DAZY_BOO1:P0008","trace_type":"Structural","confidence":"medium","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_DAZY_BOO1:ME2","text":"Practical capacity and wisdom cease to be the same, because the former can possess the particular while the latter can possess the why.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_DAZY_BOO1:P0003","trace_type":"Composite","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"},{"id":"RAW_DAZY_BOO1:P0004","trace_type":"Composite","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_DAZY_BOO1:ME3","text":"The sciences are classified anew according to whether they are serving, productive, theoretical, or governing.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_DAZY_BOO1:P0006","trace_type":"Composite","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"},{"id":"RAW_DAZY_BOO1:P0007","trace_type":"Composite","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_DAZY_BOO1:ME4","text":"The succession of philosophers ceases to be a bare chronological juxtaposition and becomes a map of partially discovered causes.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_DAZY_BOO1:P0013","trace_type":"Composite","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"},{"id":"RAW_DAZY_BOO1:P0044","trace_type":"Composite","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_DAZY_BOO1:ME5","text":"Being becomes an object of theory through four non-interchangeable questions: what it is, from what it is, by what it is moved, and for the sake of what it is.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_DAZY_BOO1:P0013","trace_type":"Direct","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_DAZY_BOO1:ME6","text":"Separate forms are shifted from an alleged universal solution to a partial theory of what something is, whose causality with respect to sensibles remains unexplained.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_DAZY_BOO1:P0045","trace_type":"Composite","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"},{"id":"RAW_DAZY_BOO1:P0059","trace_type":"Composite","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"},{"id":"RAW_DAZY_BOO1:P0060","trace_type":"Composite","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_DAZY_BOO1:ME7","text":"Wonder is shifted from a permanent affection to a starting-point, whose end is the removal of wonder through the cause.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_DAZY_BOO1:P0010","trace_type":"Composite","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"},{"id":"RAW_DAZY_BOO1:P0012","trace_type":"Composite","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]}],"original_name":"Map-Effects"},{"number":"11","name":"Altitude Levels","items":[{"id":"CH_DAZY_BOO1:ALT1","text":"At the surface level, cognitive capacities, common opinions concerning wisdom, and the beliefs of earlier philosophers are examined.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_DAZY_BOO1:P0001","trace_type":"Composite","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"},{"id":"RAW_DAZY_BOO1:P0007","trace_type":"Composite","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"},{"id":"RAW_DAZY_BOO1:P0013","trace_type":"Composite","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_DAZY_BOO1:ALT2","text":"At the level of arrangement, the order from perception to cause and the fourfold division of causality operate.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_DAZY_BOO1:P0001","trace_type":"Structural","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"},{"id":"RAW_DAZY_BOO1:P0002","trace_type":"Structural","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"},{"id":"RAW_DAZY_BOO1:P0004","trace_type":"Structural","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"},{"id":"RAW_DAZY_BOO1:P0013","trace_type":"Structural","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_DAZY_BOO1:ALT3","text":"At the hyper-strategic level, the history of opinions becomes an instrument for confirming the causal schema and producing aporiai.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_DAZY_BOO1:P0013","trace_type":"Structural","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"},{"id":"RAW_DAZY_BOO1:P0044","trace_type":"Structural","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"},{"id":"RAW_DAZY_BOO1:P0047","trace_type":"Structural","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_DAZY_BOO1:ALT4","text":"At the level concerning being and time, what comes to be is understood through causes prior by nature, while the historically later theory returns to what is ontologically first.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_DAZY_BOO1:P0013","trace_type":"Structural","confidence":"medium","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"},{"id":"RAW_DAZY_BOO1:P0050","trace_type":"Structural","confidence":"medium","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]}],"original_name":"Altitude Levels"},{"number":"12","name":"Minimal Reconstruction Test","items":[{"id":"CH_DAZY_BOO1:MRT1","text":"The extracted axioms are sufficient for reconstructing the ascent from perception to the wisdom of first causes.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_DAZY_BOO1:P0001","trace_type":"Structural","confidence":"medium","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"},{"id":"RAW_DAZY_BOO1:P0002","trace_type":"Structural","confidence":"medium","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"},{"id":"RAW_DAZY_BOO1:P0004","trace_type":"Structural","confidence":"medium","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"},{"id":"RAW_DAZY_BOO1:P0009","trace_type":"Structural","confidence":"medium","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_DAZY_BOO1:MRT2","text":"The arrangement is strongest where the distinction between the that and the why is connected with cause, teaching, and universality.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_DAZY_BOO1:P0004","trace_type":"Structural","confidence":"medium","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"},{"id":"RAW_DAZY_BOO1:P0005","trace_type":"Structural","confidence":"medium","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"},{"id":"RAW_DAZY_BOO1:P0008","trace_type":"Structural","confidence":"medium","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_DAZY_BOO1:MRT3","text":"The apparent contradiction whereby the experienced person acts better but the artisan is wiser is resolved through the distinction between practical success and causal knowledge.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_DAZY_BOO1:P0003","trace_type":"Tension","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"},{"id":"RAW_DAZY_BOO1:P0004","trace_type":"Tension","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_DAZY_BOO1:MRT4","text":"It remains more indeterminate how universal science somehow knows all things without particular knowledge and how this is reconciled with the necessity of what is known beforehand.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_DAZY_BOO1:P0007","trace_type":"Tension","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"},{"id":"RAW_DAZY_BOO1:P0008","trace_type":"Tension","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"},{"id":"RAW_DAZY_BOO1:P0069","trace_type":"Tension","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_DAZY_BOO1:MRT5","text":"The completeness of the fourfold division rests upon prior natural theory and upon the induction that none of the earlier thinkers touched upon another cause.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_DAZY_BOO1:P0013","trace_type":"Composite","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"},{"id":"RAW_DAZY_BOO1:P0044","trace_type":"Composite","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_DAZY_BOO1:MRT6","text":"The freedom and divinity of wisdom rest upon the analogy of the free human being and upon common opinions concerning the wise person.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_DAZY_BOO1:P0007","trace_type":"Composite","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"},{"id":"RAW_DAZY_BOO1:P0011","trace_type":"Composite","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_DAZY_BOO1:MRT7","text":"The final cause is necessarily introduced through the good and order, but is confirmed more dimly from examination of earlier thinkers than matter and motion are.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_DAZY_BOO1:P0019","trace_type":"Composite","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"},{"id":"RAW_DAZY_BOO1:P0045","trace_type":"Composite","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"},{"id":"RAW_DAZY_BOO1:P0046","trace_type":"Composite","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_DAZY_BOO1:MRT8","text":"The critique of forms shows that separate substance does not adequately account for sensibles, but it does not here fully explain how substance inheres in things.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_DAZY_BOO1:P0059","trace_type":"Composite","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"},{"id":"RAW_DAZY_BOO1:P0060","trace_type":"Composite","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"},{"id":"RAW_DAZY_BOO1:P0062","trace_type":"Composite","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_DAZY_BOO1:MRT9","text":"Attributions concerning the most ancient thinkers, poets, and the priority of each are retained as plausible or reported claims and not as necessary confirmations.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_DAZY_BOO1:P0015","trace_type":"Direct","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"},{"id":"RAW_DAZY_BOO1:P0016","trace_type":"Direct","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"},{"id":"RAW_DAZY_BOO1:P0021","trace_type":"Direct","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"},{"id":"RAW_DAZY_BOO1:P0027","trace_type":"Direct","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_DAZY_BOO1:MRT10","text":"The reconstruction preserves the true movement of the argument: it proceeds from the desire to know to the definition of wisdom, thence to fourfold causality, the examination of earlier thinkers, and the later aporiai.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_DAZY_BOO1:P0001","trace_type":"Structural","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"},{"id":"RAW_DAZY_BOO1:P0007","trace_type":"Structural","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"},{"id":"RAW_DAZY_BOO1:P0009","trace_type":"Structural","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"},{"id":"RAW_DAZY_BOO1:P0013","trace_type":"Structural","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"},{"id":"RAW_DAZY_BOO1:P0044","trace_type":"Structural","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"},{"id":"RAW_DAZY_BOO1:P0072","trace_type":"Structural","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]}],"original_name":"Minimal Reconstruction Test"}],"revision":"CH_DAZY_BOO1@R001","source_variant_id":"canonical:CH_DAZY_BOO1:v1","source_language_code":"grc","original_title":"Book 1"}],"identity":{"layer":"CH","kicker":"Chapter 01","title":"Book 1","context":"Chapter 01","original_title":"Book 1","original_context":"Chapter 01"},"original_title":"Book 1","title_language_code":"en"},{"id":"CH_DAZY_BOO2","layer":"CH","title":"Book 2","order":2,"parents":["RAW_DAZY_BOO2"],"revision":"CH_DAZY_BOO2@R001","sha256":"42bf1c088fed3ec65dec5570d62dbff586daadfb4d28b0c03a3103ca212b02b7","sections":[{"number":"1","name":"Argumental Spine","items":[{"id":"CH_DAZY_BOO2:AS1","text":"Ἡ περὶ ἀληθείας ζήτησις χαλεπὴ φαίνεται, ὅτι τὸ ὅλον οὐχ ὑπὸ ἑνὸς καταλαμβάνεται, ῥᾳδία δέ, ὅτι οὐδεὶς παντελῶς ἀποτυγχάνει τῆς φύσεως.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_DAZY_BOO2:P0001","trace_type":"Direct","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_DAZY_BOO2:AS2","text":"Ἡ μερικὴ τῶν πολλῶν συμβολὴ ἀθροιζομένη μέγεθός τι ἀληθείας ποιεῖ, ἀλλὰ ἡ ὅλη αἰτιακὴ τάξις ζητεῖται.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_DAZY_BOO2:P0001","trace_type":"Direct","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_DAZY_BOO2:AS3","text":"Τὸ ἀληθὲς οὐκ ἄνευ αἰτίας γιγνώσκεται, καὶ τὸ μάλιστα ὂν καὶ αἴτιον τῶν ἄλλων μάλιστα ἀληθές ἐστιν.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_DAZY_BOO2:P0003","trace_type":"Direct","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_DAZY_BOO2:AS4","text":"Ἡ ἀπείρως προϊοῦσα αἰτιότης ἀναιρεῖ καὶ πρῶτον αἴτιον καὶ τέλος καὶ ὁρισμὸν καὶ γνῶσιν.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_DAZY_BOO2:P0004","trace_type":"Composite","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"},{"id":"RAW_DAZY_BOO2:P0007","trace_type":"Composite","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"},{"id":"RAW_DAZY_BOO2:P0008","trace_type":"Composite","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_DAZY_BOO2:AS5","text":"Δεῖ οὖν πεπερασμένας εἶναι τὰς ἀρχὰς κατὰ ὕλην, κίνησιν, οὗ ἕνεκα καὶ τί ἦν εἶναι.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_DAZY_BOO2:P0004","trace_type":"Direct","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_DAZY_BOO2:AS6","text":"Ἡ ζήτησις περὶ τῶν ἀρχῶν μεταπίπτει εἰς ζήτησιν τοῦ προσήκοντος τρόπου ἀκροάσεως, ἐπειδὴ τὸ ἔθος τὸν τρόπον τῆς παραδοχῆς προδιατάσσει.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_DAZY_BOO2:P0009","trace_type":"Direct","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_DAZY_BOO2:AS7","text":"Ἡ ἀκριβολογία οὐ καθόλου ἐφαρμοστέα, ἀλλὰ πρὸς τὴν ὕλην καὶ τὴν φύσιν τοῦ ὑποκειμένου μετρητέα.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_DAZY_BOO2:P0010","trace_type":"Direct","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]}],"original_name":"Argumental Spine"},{"number":"2","name":"Load-Bearing Axioms","items":[{"id":"CH_DAZY_BOO2:A1","text":"Ἕκαστος τῶν ζητούντων λέγει τι περὶ τῆς φύσεως, κἂν μικρὸν ᾖ τὸ συμβαλλόμενον.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_DAZY_BOO2:P0001","trace_type":"Direct","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_DAZY_BOO2:A2","text":"Ἡ καθόλου ἀλήθεια οὐκ ἐκ μιᾶς ἰδίας ἐπιβολῆς, ἀλλ᾽ ἐκ συναθροίσεως μερικῶν ἐπιβολῶν προσεγγίζεται.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_DAZY_BOO2:P0001","trace_type":"Direct","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_DAZY_BOO2:A3","text":"Ἡ δυσχέρεια τῆς ἀληθείας οὐκ ἐν τοῖς πράγμασιν μόνον, ἀλλὰ καὶ ἐν τῇ πρὸς τὰ φανερώτατα ἀσθενείᾳ τοῦ νοῦ κεῖται.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_DAZY_BOO2:P0001","trace_type":"Direct","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_DAZY_BOO2:A4","text":"Ἡ θεωρητικὴ ἐπιστήμη τὴν ἀλήθειαν τέλος ἔχει, ἡ δὲ πρακτικὴ τὸ ἔργον.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_DAZY_BOO2:P0002","trace_type":"Direct","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_DAZY_BOO2:A5","text":"Τὸ ἀληθὲς οὐ γιγνώσκεται ἄνευ τοῦ αἰτίου.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_DAZY_BOO2:P0003","trace_type":"Direct","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_DAZY_BOO2:A6","text":"Τὸ αἴτιον τοῖς ἄλλοις ὑπάρχειν ποιεῖ ὃ αὐτὸ μάλιστα κατέχει.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_DAZY_BOO2:P0003","trace_type":"Direct","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_DAZY_BOO2:A7","text":"Αἱ τῶν ἀεὶ ὄντων ἀρχαὶ ἀεὶ καὶ μάλιστα ἀληθεῖς εἰσιν, ἐπειδὴ αὐταὶ τοῖς ἄλλοις τοῦ εἶναι αἴτιαι εἰσίν.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_DAZY_BOO2:P0003","trace_type":"Direct","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_DAZY_BOO2:A8","text":"Εἰ μηδὲν πρῶτον ἐστίν, οὐδὲν αἴτιον ἐστίν.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_DAZY_BOO2:P0005","trace_type":"Direct","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_DAZY_BOO2:A9","text":"Τὸ οὗ ἕνεκα τέλος ἐστίν, καὶ τὸ τέλος οὐκ ἄλλου ἕνεκα ὑπάρχει.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_DAZY_BOO2:P0007","trace_type":"Direct","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_DAZY_BOO2:A10","text":"Ἄνευ πεπερασμένου ὁρισμοῦ οὔτε ἐπίστασθαι οὔτε νοεῖν δυνατὸν ἐστίν.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_DAZY_BOO2:P0008","trace_type":"Direct","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_DAZY_BOO2:A11","text":"Τὰ αἴτια, ἐὰν ἀπείρως πλήθει προΐῃ, οὐκ ἐν πεπερασμένῳ χρόνῳ γνωσθῆναι δύναται.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_DAZY_BOO2:P0008","trace_type":"Direct","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_DAZY_BOO2:A12","text":"Ὁ προσήκων τρόπος ἀποδείξεως ἐκ τῆς τοῦ ὑποκειμένου φύσεως λαμβάνεται, οὐκ ἐκ τῆς συνήθειας τοῦ ἀκροατοῦ μόνον.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_DAZY_BOO2:P0009","trace_type":"Composite","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"},{"id":"RAW_DAZY_BOO2:P0010","trace_type":"Composite","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]}],"original_name":"Load-Bearing Axioms"},{"number":"3","name":"Apophatic Structure","items":[{"id":"CH_DAZY_BOO2:NX1","text":"Ἡ ἀλήθεια οὐκ ἀνέφικτος ἐστίν, ἐπειδὴ πάντες ἄνθρωποι παντελῶς ἀποτυγχάνουσιν αὐτῆς.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_DAZY_BOO2:P0001","trace_type":"Negative","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_DAZY_BOO2:NX2","text":"Ἡ ἀλήθεια οὐκ ἰδίᾳ καὶ τελείᾳ κτῆσις ἑνὸς ζητητοῦ ἐστίν.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_DAZY_BOO2:P0001","trace_type":"Negative","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_DAZY_BOO2:NX3","text":"Ἡ ἀλήθεια οὐκ ἀναιτίως ἔχει, οὐδὲ ἡ γνῶσις ἀνεξέταστον παράθεσιν δοξῶν ἐστίν.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_DAZY_BOO2:P0003","trace_type":"Negative","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_DAZY_BOO2:NX4","text":"Ἡ ὑλικὴ, κινητικὴ, τελικὴ καὶ ὁριστικὴ αἰτιότης οὐκ εἰς ἄπειρον ἐκτείνεται.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_DAZY_BOO2:P0004","trace_type":"Negative","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"},{"id":"RAW_DAZY_BOO2:P0007","trace_type":"Negative","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_DAZY_BOO2:NX5","text":"Τὸ οὗ ἕνεκα οὐκ ἀναιρεῖται ἄνευ τοῦ συναφανίζειν τὴν τοῦ ἀγαθοῦ φύσιν.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_DAZY_BOO2:P0007","trace_type":"Negative","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_DAZY_BOO2:NX6","text":"Ὁ ὁρισμὸς οὐκ ἀναλύεται εἰς ἀεὶ πλεονάζοντα ὁρισμὸν ἄνευ πρώτου λόγου.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_DAZY_BOO2:P0008","trace_type":"Negative","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_DAZY_BOO2:NX7","text":"Ἡ μαθηματικὴ ἀκρίβεια οὐκ ἀπαιτητέα ἐν παντὶ γένει λόγου.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_DAZY_BOO2:P0009","trace_type":"Negative","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"},{"id":"RAW_DAZY_BOO2:P0010","trace_type":"Negative","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_DAZY_BOO2:NX8","text":"Τὸ σύνηθες οὐκ αὐτόθεν κριτήριον τοῦ ἀληθοῦς οὐδὲ τοῦ προσήκοντος τρόπου ἐστίν.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_DAZY_BOO2:P0009","trace_type":"Negative","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]}],"original_name":"Apophatic Structure"},{"number":"4","name":"Derivative Propositions","items":[{"id":"CH_DAZY_BOO2:D1","text":"Ἡ προγενεστέρα δόξα, κἂν ἐπιπολαία ᾖ, ὄργανον γίνεται τῆς ἑξῆς ζητήσεως, ἐπειδὴ τὴν ἕξιν τοῦ ζητεῖν προασκεῖ.","refs":["CH_DAZY_BOO2:A1","CH_DAZY_BOO2:A2","CH_DAZY_BOO2:A3"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"From","passages":[{"id":"RAW_DAZY_BOO2:P0001","trace_type":"Composite","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"},{"id":"RAW_DAZY_BOO2:P0002","trace_type":"Composite","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_DAZY_BOO2:D2","text":"Ἡ φιλοσοφία δικαίως ἐπιστήμη τῆς ἀληθείας καλεῖται, ἐπειδὴ θεωρητικῶς πρὸς τὸ ἀληθὲς τελευτᾷ.","refs":["CH_DAZY_BOO2:A4","CH_DAZY_BOO2:A5"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"From","passages":[{"id":"RAW_DAZY_BOO2:P0002","trace_type":"Composite","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"},{"id":"RAW_DAZY_BOO2:P0003","trace_type":"Composite","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_DAZY_BOO2:D3","text":"Τὰ ἀεὶ ὄντα μᾶλλον ἀληθῆ λέγεται, καθόσον μᾶλλον τοῦ εἶναι αἴτια τοῖς ὑστεροῖς ἐστίν.","refs":["CH_DAZY_BOO2:A5","CH_DAZY_BOO2:A6","CH_DAZY_BOO2:A7"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"From","passages":[{"id":"RAW_DAZY_BOO2:P0003","trace_type":"Direct","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_DAZY_BOO2:D4","text":"Ἡ ἀναίρεσις τοῦ πρώτου αἰτίου ἀναιρεῖ τὴν αἰτιακὴν ἐξήγησιν καθόλου, ἐπειδὴ πάντα τὰ ἐν ἀπείρῳ μέσοις ἔσται.","refs":["CH_DAZY_BOO2:A8"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"From","passages":[{"id":"RAW_DAZY_BOO2:P0005","trace_type":"Direct","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_DAZY_BOO2:D5","text":"Ἡ ἀπειρία τῶν τελικῶν αἰτίων καθιστᾷ πᾶσαν πρᾶξιν ἄνευ πέρατος καὶ διὰ τοῦτο ἀνόητον.","refs":["CH_DAZY_BOO2:A9"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"From","passages":[{"id":"RAW_DAZY_BOO2:P0007","trace_type":"Direct","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_DAZY_BOO2:D6","text":"Ἡ ἀπειρία τῶν ὁρισμῶν ἀναιρεῖ τὴν εἴδησιν, ἐπειδὴ ἡ διάνοια οὐκ ἥκει εἰς ἄτομα νοητά.","refs":["CH_DAZY_BOO2:A10","CH_DAZY_BOO2:A11"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"From","passages":[{"id":"RAW_DAZY_BOO2:P0008","trace_type":"Direct","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_DAZY_BOO2:D7","text":"Ἡ φυσικὴ ζήτησις οὐ μαθηματικῇ ἀκριβείας ὑποτάσσεται, ἐπειδὴ ἡ φύσις ὕλην ἔχει.","refs":["CH_DAZY_BOO2:A12"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"From","passages":[{"id":"RAW_DAZY_BOO2:P0009","trace_type":"Composite","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"},{"id":"RAW_DAZY_BOO2:P0010","trace_type":"Composite","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]}],"original_name":"Derivative Propositions"},{"number":"5","name":"Super-Derivatives","items":[{"id":"CH_DAZY_BOO2:SD1","text":"Ἡ ἀλήθεια τάξις αἰτιακὴ γίνεται, ἐν ᾗ ὁ βαθμὸς τοῦ εἶναι καὶ ὁ βαθμὸς τοῦ ἀληθεύειν ἀλλήλοις ἀκολουθοῦσιν.","refs":["CH_DAZY_BOO2:A5","CH_DAZY_BOO2:A6","CH_DAZY_BOO2:A7"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"From","passages":[{"id":"RAW_DAZY_BOO2:P0003","trace_type":"Direct","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_DAZY_BOO2:SD2","text":"Ἡ μεταφυσικὴ ζήτησις ἀπαιτεῖ πεπερασμένην ἀρχὴν, ἵνα καὶ αἰτία καὶ τέλος καὶ ἐπιστήμη καὶ νοῦς σώζωνται.","refs":["CH_DAZY_BOO2:A8","CH_DAZY_BOO2:A9","CH_DAZY_BOO2:A10","CH_DAZY_BOO2:A11"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"From","passages":[{"id":"RAW_DAZY_BOO2:P0005","trace_type":"Structural","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"},{"id":"RAW_DAZY_BOO2:P0007","trace_type":"Structural","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"},{"id":"RAW_DAZY_BOO2:P0008","trace_type":"Structural","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_DAZY_BOO2:SD3","text":"Ἡ ὀρθὴ μέθοδος οὐκ ἑνιαία τέχνη τοῦ λέγειν ἐστίν, ἀλλὰ συμμετρία λόγου πρὸς τὸ εἶδος τοῦ πράγματος.","refs":["CH_DAZY_BOO2:A3","CH_DAZY_BOO2:A12"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"From","passages":[{"id":"RAW_DAZY_BOO2:P0009","trace_type":"Composite","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"},{"id":"RAW_DAZY_BOO2:P0010","trace_type":"Composite","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_DAZY_BOO2:SD4","text":"Ἡ παράδοσις τῶν δοξῶν οὐκ αὐτάρκης πηγὴ ἀληθείας, ἀλλὰ γενεαλογικὸς ὅρος δι᾽ οὗ ἡ νῦν ζήτησις δυνατὴ γίνεται.","refs":["CH_DAZY_BOO2:A1","CH_DAZY_BOO2:A2","CH_DAZY_BOO2:A3"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"From","passages":[{"id":"RAW_DAZY_BOO2:P0001","trace_type":"Structural","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"},{"id":"RAW_DAZY_BOO2:P0002","trace_type":"Structural","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]}],"original_name":"Super-Derivatives"},{"number":"6","name":"Deep Apophatics","items":[{"id":"CH_DAZY_BOO2:DNX1","text":"Οὐδεμία ἀλήθεια καθόλου νοητὴ μένει, εἰ τὸ αἴτιον οὐκ ἔχει πρὸς τὸ αἰτιατὸν τάξιν προτέρου καὶ ὑστέρου.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_DAZY_BOO2:P0003","trace_type":"Composite","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"},{"id":"RAW_DAZY_BOO2:P0004","trace_type":"Composite","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_DAZY_BOO2:DNX2","text":"Οὐδεμία πράξις ἔμφρων μένει, εἰ τὸ πρακτὸν οὐκ ἔχει πέρας πρὸς ὃ κινείται.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_DAZY_BOO2:P0007","trace_type":"Direct","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_DAZY_BOO2:DNX3","text":"Οὐδεμία ἐπιστήμη μένει, εἰ ἡ ἀνάλυσις τῶν αἰτίων καὶ ὁρισμῶν οὐδέποτε παύεται.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_DAZY_BOO2:P0008","trace_type":"Direct","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_DAZY_BOO2:DNX4","text":"Οὐδεμία μέθοδος ὀρθὴ μένει, εἰ ὁ τρόπος τοῦ λέγειν κρίνεται μόνῃ τῇ ἡδονῇ ἢ συνήθειᾳ τοῦ ἀκούοντος.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_DAZY_BOO2:P0009","trace_type":"Direct","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]}],"original_name":"Deep Apophatics"},{"number":"7","name":"Super-Apophatics","items":[{"id":"CH_DAZY_BOO2:SNX1","text":"Ἀποκλείεται κόσμος ἐν ᾧ τὰ ὄντα μόνον ἀλλήλων συνέπειαι εἰσίν, μηδεμιᾶς ἀρχῆς τὴν τάξιν θεμελιούσης.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_DAZY_BOO2:P0004","trace_type":"Negative","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"},{"id":"RAW_DAZY_BOO2:P0005","trace_type":"Negative","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_DAZY_BOO2:SNX2","text":"Ἀποκλείεται νοῦς ὃς ἐνεργεῖ ἄνευ τέλους, ἐπειδὴ τὸ νοεῖν καὶ πράττειν διὰ τι ἀεὶ κινείται.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_DAZY_BOO2:P0007","trace_type":"Negative","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_DAZY_BOO2:SNX3","text":"Ἀποκλείεται γνῶσις ἡ μὴ διακρίνουσα τὸ πεπερασμένον ἀπὸ τοῦ ἀπείρου καὶ τὸ προσήκον μέτρον ἀκριβείας ἀπὸ τῆς ἀμετρίας.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_DAZY_BOO2:P0008","trace_type":"Composite","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"},{"id":"RAW_DAZY_BOO2:P0009","trace_type":"Composite","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"},{"id":"RAW_DAZY_BOO2:P0010","trace_type":"Composite","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]}],"original_name":"Super-Apophatics"},{"number":"8","name":"Formal Generative Schema","items":[{"id":"CH_DAZY_BOO2:FGS1","text":"Μερικαὶ δόξαι περὶ φύσεως συναθροιζόμεναι → μερικὴ πρόσβασις πρὸς ἀλήθειαν → ἀνάγκη ζητήσεως τοῦ ὅλου.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_DAZY_BOO2:P0001","trace_type":"Direct","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_DAZY_BOO2:FGS2","text":"Ζήτησις τοῦ ὅλου → ζήτησις αἰτίας → ἀνάβασις πρὸς τὸ μάλιστα αἴτιον καὶ μάλιστα ἀληθές.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_DAZY_BOO2:P0001","trace_type":"Structural","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"},{"id":"RAW_DAZY_BOO2:P0003","trace_type":"Structural","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_DAZY_BOO2:FGS3","text":"Ἀναίρεσις ἀπείρου προόδου → θέσις πρώτης ἀρχῆς → σωτηρία αἰτιότητος, τέλους, ὁρισμοῦ καὶ γνώσεως.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_DAZY_BOO2:P0004","trace_type":"Structural","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"},{"id":"RAW_DAZY_BOO2:P0007","trace_type":"Structural","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"},{"id":"RAW_DAZY_BOO2:P0008","trace_type":"Structural","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_DAZY_BOO2:FGS4","text":"Διάκρισις τῶν γενῶν τοῦ ὄντος → διάκρισις τῶν προσήκοντων τρόπων λόγου → περιορισμὸς τῆς μαθηματικῆς ἀκριβείας.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_DAZY_BOO2:P0009","trace_type":"Composite","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"},{"id":"RAW_DAZY_BOO2:P0010","trace_type":"Composite","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_DAZY_BOO2:FGS5","text":"Ἔθος τῶν ἀκροατῶν → πλάνη περὶ τοῦ δέοντος τρόπου → παιδεία πρὸς κρίσιν τοῦ καθ᾽ ἕκαστον μέτρου.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_DAZY_BOO2:P0009","trace_type":"Direct","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]}],"original_name":"Formal Generative Schema"},{"number":"9","name":"Argument Flow Map","items":[{"id":"CH_DAZY_BOO2:AF1","text":"Μερικὴ ἐπιτυχία τῶν πολλῶν → δυσχέρεια τοῦ ὅλου → ζήτησις τῆς αἰτίας.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_DAZY_BOO2:P0001","trace_type":"Composite","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"},{"id":"RAW_DAZY_BOO2:P0003","trace_type":"Composite","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_DAZY_BOO2:AF2","text":"Αἰτία τοῦ ἀληθεύειν → βαθμὸς τοῦ εἶναι → πρωτεῖον τῶν ἀϊδίων ἀρχῶν.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_DAZY_BOO2:P0003","trace_type":"Direct","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_DAZY_BOO2:AF3","text":"Ἀπειρία αἰτίων → ἀναίρεσις πρώτου καὶ τέλους → ἀναίρεσις γνώσεως καὶ πράξεως.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_DAZY_BOO2:P0004","trace_type":"Structural","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"},{"id":"RAW_DAZY_BOO2:P0007","trace_type":"Structural","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"},{"id":"RAW_DAZY_BOO2:P0008","trace_type":"Structural","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_DAZY_BOO2:AF4","text":"Ἀδυναμία ἀπείρου → περατότης τῶν ἀρχῶν → δυνατότης ἐπιστήμης.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_DAZY_BOO2:P0004","trace_type":"Composite","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"},{"id":"RAW_DAZY_BOO2:P0008","trace_type":"Composite","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_DAZY_BOO2:AF5","text":"Ἔθος ἀκροάσεως → ποικιλία ψευδῶν ἀπαιτήσεων → μέτρησις τῆς ἀκριβείας κατὰ τὸ ὑποκείμενον.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_DAZY_BOO2:P0009","trace_type":"Composite","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"},{"id":"RAW_DAZY_BOO2:P0010","trace_type":"Composite","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]}],"original_name":"Argument Flow Map"},{"number":"10","name":"Map-Effects","items":[{"id":"CH_DAZY_BOO2:ME1","text":"Ἡ ἀλήθεια ἐκ μεμονωμένου κτήματος μετατίθεται εἰς κοινὴν καὶ διαχρονικὴν συναγωγὴν μερικῶν θεωριῶν.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_DAZY_BOO2:P0001","trace_type":"Structural","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"},{"id":"RAW_DAZY_BOO2:P0002","trace_type":"Structural","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_DAZY_BOO2:ME2","text":"Ἡ ἀλήθεια οὐκέτι ψιλὴ συμφωνία λόγου γίνεται, ἀλλὰ ἰδιότης ἐξαρτωμένη ἀπὸ τοῦ βαθμοῦ τῆς αἰτιακῆς ἀρχῆς.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_DAZY_BOO2:P0003","trace_type":"Direct","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_DAZY_BOO2:ME3","text":"Τὸ ἄπειρον μετατίθεται ἐκ ἀορίστου μεγέθους εἰς ἀπειλὴν κατὰ τῆς αἰτιότητος, τῆς τελεολογίας καὶ τῆς γνωσιμότητος.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_DAZY_BOO2:P0004","trace_type":"Structural","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"},{"id":"RAW_DAZY_BOO2:P0007","trace_type":"Structural","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"},{"id":"RAW_DAZY_BOO2:P0008","trace_type":"Structural","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_DAZY_BOO2:ME4","text":"Ἡ διάκρισις θεωρητικοῦ καὶ πρακτικοῦ μεταβάλλει τὸ τέλος τῆς ζητήσεως, ἀληθείας μὲν τῷ θεωρητικῷ, ἔργου δὲ τῷ πρακτικῷ.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_DAZY_BOO2:P0002","trace_type":"Direct","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_DAZY_BOO2:ME5","text":"Ἡ ἀκρίβεια μετατίθεται ἐκ καθολικῆς ἀξιώσεως εἰς σχετικὸν μέτρον πρὸς τὴν ὕλην τοῦ ζητουμένου.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_DAZY_BOO2:P0010","trace_type":"Direct","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]}],"original_name":"Map-Effects"},{"number":"11","name":"Altitude Levels","items":[{"id":"CH_DAZY_BOO2:ALT1","text":"Ἐπιφανειακῶς ὁ λόγος περὶ ἀληθείας, αἰτίων, ἀπείρου καὶ τρόπων ἀκροάσεως διαλέγεται.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_DAZY_BOO2:P0001","trace_type":"Structural","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"},{"id":"RAW_DAZY_BOO2:P0004","trace_type":"Structural","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"},{"id":"RAW_DAZY_BOO2:P0009","trace_type":"Structural","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_DAZY_BOO2:ALT2","text":"Κατὰ διάταξιν ὁ λόγος τὴν ἐπιστήμην ἐπὶ τάξει προτέρου καὶ ὑστέρου, ἀρχῆς καὶ ἐπακολουθήματος θεμελιοῖ.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_DAZY_BOO2:P0004","trace_type":"Structural","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"},{"id":"RAW_DAZY_BOO2:P0005","trace_type":"Structural","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_DAZY_BOO2:ALT3","text":"Ὑπερστρατηγικῶς ὁ λόγος τὴν διαφωνίαν τῶν δοξῶν οὐκ ὡς κώλυμα, ἀλλ᾽ ὡς ὕλην καὶ προπαίδευσιν τῆς ζητήσεως τίθησιν.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_DAZY_BOO2:P0001","trace_type":"Structural","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"},{"id":"RAW_DAZY_BOO2:P0002","trace_type":"Structural","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_DAZY_BOO2:ALT4","text":"Περὶ ὄντος ὁ λόγος τὸ εἶναι, τὸ ἀληθεύειν καὶ τὸ αἰτιᾶσθαι κατὰ τὸν αὐτὸν βαθμὸν συντάττει.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_DAZY_BOO2:P0003","trace_type":"Direct","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_DAZY_BOO2:ALT5","text":"Περὶ χρόνου ὁ λόγος τὸ ἀΐδιον οὐχ ὡς μακρὸν χρόνον μόνον, ἀλλ᾽ ὡς ἀναίτιον καὶ αἴτιον τῶν ἄλλων νοεῖ.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_DAZY_BOO2:P0003","trace_type":"Composite","confidence":"medium","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]}],"original_name":"Altitude Levels"},{"number":"12","name":"Minimal Reconstruction Test","items":[{"id":"CH_DAZY_BOO2:MRT1","text":"Τὰ ἀξιώματα ἀρκεῖ πρὸς ἀνασύστασιν τοῦ πυρήνος, ἐπειδὴ ἐξ αὐτῶν ἡ μετάβασις ἀπὸ τῆς ἀληθείας εἰς τὰς πεπερασμένας ἀρχὰς ἀκολουθεῖ.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_DAZY_BOO2:P0001","trace_type":"Structural","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"},{"id":"RAW_DAZY_BOO2:P0003","trace_type":"Structural","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"},{"id":"RAW_DAZY_BOO2:P0004","trace_type":"Structural","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"},{"id":"RAW_DAZY_BOO2:P0010","trace_type":"Structural","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_DAZY_BOO2:MRT2","text":"Ἡ ἰσχυροτάτη σύναψις τοῦ συστήματος κεῖται ἐν τῷ δεσμῷ αἰτίας, ἀληθείας, εἶναι καὶ γνώσεως.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_DAZY_BOO2:P0003","trace_type":"Structural","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"},{"id":"RAW_DAZY_BOO2:P0008","trace_type":"Structural","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_DAZY_BOO2:MRT3","text":"Ἡ ἀναίρεσις τοῦ ἀπείρου ἐνεργεῖ ὡς ἀναγκαῖος ὅρος τοῦ συστήματος, ἀλλὰ ἡ ἀπόδειξις προϋποτίθεται τὴν ἀξίαν τοῦ πρώτου μᾶλλον ἢ ἐκ παντὸς ἀναγκαστικῶς ἐκφέρει αὐτήν.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_DAZY_BOO2:P0004","trace_type":"Tension","confidence":"medium","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"},{"id":"RAW_DAZY_BOO2:P0005","trace_type":"Tension","confidence":"medium","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_DAZY_BOO2:MRT4","text":"Ἡ σχέσις μεταξὺ τῆς συλλογικῆς προόδου τῶν δοξῶν καὶ τῆς ἱεραρχίας τῶν ἀληθεστέρων ἀρχῶν μένει οὐ παντελῶς διηρθρωμένη.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_DAZY_BOO2:P0001","trace_type":"Tension","confidence":"medium","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"},{"id":"RAW_DAZY_BOO2:P0002","trace_type":"Tension","confidence":"medium","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"},{"id":"RAW_DAZY_BOO2:P0003","trace_type":"Tension","confidence":"medium","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_DAZY_BOO2:MRT5","text":"Ἡ ἀνασύστασις σῴζει τὴν κίνησιν τοῦ λόγου, ἐὰν τὸ τελευταῖον μέρος περὶ ἀκροάσεως νοηθῇ ὡς μεθοδικὸς περιορισμὸς τῆς προτέρας αἰτιολογικῆς ζητήσεως.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_DAZY_BOO2:P0009","trace_type":"Structural","confidence":"medium","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"},{"id":"RAW_DAZY_BOO2:P0010","trace_type":"Structural","confidence":"medium","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]}],"original_name":"Minimal Reconstruction Test"}],"language_code":"grc","language_name":"Ἑλληνική","translations":[{"id":"CAREVAR_20261015T174414936780Z_03bae38f","language_code":"en","language_name":"English","title":"Book 2","source_sha256":"42bf1c088fed3ec65dec5570d62dbff586daadfb4d28b0c03a3103ca212b02b7","sha256":"5ed415e55c17df4861c49e44ef40d912d32d54c504575d946fdad6428cfd071f","rendition_type":"machine","review_status":"machine","model":"gpt-5.6-terra","created":"2026-10-15T17:44:14.939339+00:00","sections":[{"number":"1","name":"Argumental Spine","items":[{"id":"CH_DAZY_BOO2:AS1","text":"The inquiry concerning truth appears difficult, because the whole is not grasped by one person, but easy, because no one fails utterly to attain nature.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_DAZY_BOO2:P0001","trace_type":"Direct","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_DAZY_BOO2:AS2","text":"The partial contribution of the many, when accumulated, produces some magnitude of truth, but the whole causal order is sought.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_DAZY_BOO2:P0001","trace_type":"Direct","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_DAZY_BOO2:AS3","text":"The true is not known without a cause, and that which is most fully is and is the cause of the others is most true.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_DAZY_BOO2:P0003","trace_type":"Direct","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_DAZY_BOO2:AS4","text":"Causality proceeding to infinity removes both a first cause and an end and a definition and knowledge.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_DAZY_BOO2:P0004","trace_type":"Composite","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"},{"id":"RAW_DAZY_BOO2:P0007","trace_type":"Composite","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"},{"id":"RAW_DAZY_BOO2:P0008","trace_type":"Composite","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_DAZY_BOO2:AS5","text":"Therefore the principles must be finite with respect to matter, motion, that for the sake of which, and what-it-was-to-be.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_DAZY_BOO2:P0004","trace_type":"Direct","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_DAZY_BOO2:AS6","text":"The inquiry concerning principles shifts into an inquiry concerning the appropriate manner of hearing, since habit predisposes the manner of reception.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_DAZY_BOO2:P0009","trace_type":"Direct","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_DAZY_BOO2:AS7","text":"Exactness is not to be applied universally, but is to be measured according to the matter and the nature of the subject.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_DAZY_BOO2:P0010","trace_type":"Direct","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]}],"original_name":"Argumental Spine"},{"number":"2","name":"Load-Bearing Axioms","items":[{"id":"CH_DAZY_BOO2:A1","text":"Each of those who inquire says something concerning nature, even if what is contributed is small.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_DAZY_BOO2:P0001","trace_type":"Direct","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_DAZY_BOO2:A2","text":"Universal truth is approached not from one private apprehension but from the gathering together of partial apprehensions.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_DAZY_BOO2:P0001","trace_type":"Direct","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_DAZY_BOO2:A3","text":"The difficulty of truth lies not only in things, but also in the weakness of intellect in relation to the things most evident.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_DAZY_BOO2:P0001","trace_type":"Direct","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_DAZY_BOO2:A4","text":"Theoretical science has truth as its end, whereas practical science has the work.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_DAZY_BOO2:P0002","trace_type":"Direct","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_DAZY_BOO2:A5","text":"The true is not known without the cause.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_DAZY_BOO2:P0003","trace_type":"Direct","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_DAZY_BOO2:A6","text":"The cause makes the others possess that which it itself possesses most fully.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_DAZY_BOO2:P0003","trace_type":"Direct","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_DAZY_BOO2:A7","text":"The principles of things that always are are always and most truly true, since they are themselves causes of being for the others.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_DAZY_BOO2:P0003","trace_type":"Direct","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_DAZY_BOO2:A8","text":"If there is nothing first, there is nothing causal.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_DAZY_BOO2:P0005","trace_type":"Direct","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_DAZY_BOO2:A9","text":"That for the sake of which is an end, and the end does not exist for the sake of another.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_DAZY_BOO2:P0007","trace_type":"Direct","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_DAZY_BOO2:A10","text":"Without a finite definition, neither knowing nor thinking is possible.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_DAZY_BOO2:P0008","trace_type":"Direct","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_DAZY_BOO2:A11","text":"The causes, if they proceed infinitely in multitude, cannot be known in finite time.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_DAZY_BOO2:P0008","trace_type":"Direct","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_DAZY_BOO2:A12","text":"The appropriate mode of demonstration is taken from the nature of the subject, not merely from the listener's habit.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_DAZY_BOO2:P0009","trace_type":"Composite","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"},{"id":"RAW_DAZY_BOO2:P0010","trace_type":"Composite","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]}],"original_name":"Load-Bearing Axioms"},{"number":"3","name":"Apophatic Structure","items":[{"id":"CH_DAZY_BOO2:NX1","text":"Truth is not unattainable, because all human beings do not fail utterly to attain it.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_DAZY_BOO2:P0001","trace_type":"Negative","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_DAZY_BOO2:NX2","text":"Truth is not the private and complete possession of a single inquirer.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_DAZY_BOO2:P0001","trace_type":"Negative","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_DAZY_BOO2:NX3","text":"Truth does not obtain without cause, nor is knowledge an unexamined setting forth of opinions.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_DAZY_BOO2:P0003","trace_type":"Negative","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_DAZY_BOO2:NX4","text":"Material, motive, final, and definitional causality does not extend to infinity.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_DAZY_BOO2:P0004","trace_type":"Negative","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"},{"id":"RAW_DAZY_BOO2:P0007","trace_type":"Negative","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_DAZY_BOO2:NX5","text":"That for the sake of which is not removed without simultaneously obliterating the nature of the good.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_DAZY_BOO2:P0007","trace_type":"Negative","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_DAZY_BOO2:NX6","text":"The definition is not analyzed into an ever-increasing definition without a first account.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_DAZY_BOO2:P0008","trace_type":"Negative","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_DAZY_BOO2:NX7","text":"Mathematical exactness is not to be demanded in every kind of discourse.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_DAZY_BOO2:P0009","trace_type":"Negative","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"},{"id":"RAW_DAZY_BOO2:P0010","trace_type":"Negative","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_DAZY_BOO2:NX8","text":"What is customary is not of itself a criterion either of the true or of the appropriate mode.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_DAZY_BOO2:P0009","trace_type":"Negative","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]}],"original_name":"Apophatic Structure"},{"number":"4","name":"Derivative Propositions","items":[{"id":"CH_DAZY_BOO2:D1","text":"A prior opinion, even if superficial, becomes an instrument for the subsequent inquiry, because it pre-trains the disposition to inquire.","refs":["CH_DAZY_BOO2:A1","CH_DAZY_BOO2:A2","CH_DAZY_BOO2:A3"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"From","passages":[{"id":"RAW_DAZY_BOO2:P0001","trace_type":"Composite","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"},{"id":"RAW_DAZY_BOO2:P0002","trace_type":"Composite","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_DAZY_BOO2:D2","text":"Philosophy is rightly called the science of truth, because it ends theoretically in the true.","refs":["CH_DAZY_BOO2:A4","CH_DAZY_BOO2:A5"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"From","passages":[{"id":"RAW_DAZY_BOO2:P0002","trace_type":"Composite","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"},{"id":"RAW_DAZY_BOO2:P0003","trace_type":"Composite","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_DAZY_BOO2:D3","text":"Things that always are are said to be more true, insofar as they are more causes of being for later things.","refs":["CH_DAZY_BOO2:A5","CH_DAZY_BOO2:A6","CH_DAZY_BOO2:A7"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"From","passages":[{"id":"RAW_DAZY_BOO2:P0003","trace_type":"Direct","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_DAZY_BOO2:D4","text":"The removal of the first cause removes causal explanation universally, since all things will be among infinite intermediates.","refs":["CH_DAZY_BOO2:A8"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"From","passages":[{"id":"RAW_DAZY_BOO2:P0005","trace_type":"Direct","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_DAZY_BOO2:D5","text":"The infinity of final causes renders every action without an end and therefore unintelligible.","refs":["CH_DAZY_BOO2:A9"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"From","passages":[{"id":"RAW_DAZY_BOO2:P0007","trace_type":"Direct","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_DAZY_BOO2:D6","text":"The infinity of definitions removes knowledge, because thought does not arrive at indivisible intelligibles.","refs":["CH_DAZY_BOO2:A10","CH_DAZY_BOO2:A11"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"From","passages":[{"id":"RAW_DAZY_BOO2:P0008","trace_type":"Direct","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_DAZY_BOO2:D7","text":"Natural inquiry is not subject to mathematical exactness, because nature has matter.","refs":["CH_DAZY_BOO2:A12"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"From","passages":[{"id":"RAW_DAZY_BOO2:P0009","trace_type":"Composite","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"},{"id":"RAW_DAZY_BOO2:P0010","trace_type":"Composite","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]}],"original_name":"Derivative Propositions"},{"number":"5","name":"Super-Derivatives","items":[{"id":"CH_DAZY_BOO2:SD1","text":"Truth becomes a causal order, in which the degree of being and the degree of being true follow one another.","refs":["CH_DAZY_BOO2:A5","CH_DAZY_BOO2:A6","CH_DAZY_BOO2:A7"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"From","passages":[{"id":"RAW_DAZY_BOO2:P0003","trace_type":"Direct","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_DAZY_BOO2:SD2","text":"Metaphysical inquiry requires a finite principle, so that cause, end, science, and intellect may be preserved.","refs":["CH_DAZY_BOO2:A8","CH_DAZY_BOO2:A9","CH_DAZY_BOO2:A10","CH_DAZY_BOO2:A11"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"From","passages":[{"id":"RAW_DAZY_BOO2:P0005","trace_type":"Structural","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"},{"id":"RAW_DAZY_BOO2:P0007","trace_type":"Structural","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"},{"id":"RAW_DAZY_BOO2:P0008","trace_type":"Structural","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_DAZY_BOO2:SD3","text":"Correct method is not a uniform art of speaking, but a commensurateness of discourse to the form of the thing.","refs":["CH_DAZY_BOO2:A3","CH_DAZY_BOO2:A12"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"From","passages":[{"id":"RAW_DAZY_BOO2:P0009","trace_type":"Composite","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"},{"id":"RAW_DAZY_BOO2:P0010","trace_type":"Composite","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_DAZY_BOO2:SD4","text":"The tradition of opinions is not a self-sufficient source of truth, but a genealogical condition through which present inquiry becomes possible.","refs":["CH_DAZY_BOO2:A1","CH_DAZY_BOO2:A2","CH_DAZY_BOO2:A3"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"From","passages":[{"id":"RAW_DAZY_BOO2:P0001","trace_type":"Structural","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"},{"id":"RAW_DAZY_BOO2:P0002","trace_type":"Structural","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]}],"original_name":"Super-Derivatives"},{"number":"6","name":"Deep Apophatics","items":[{"id":"CH_DAZY_BOO2:DNX1","text":"No truth whatsoever remains intelligible if the cause does not have, in relation to the caused, an order of prior and posterior.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_DAZY_BOO2:P0003","trace_type":"Composite","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"},{"id":"RAW_DAZY_BOO2:P0004","trace_type":"Composite","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_DAZY_BOO2:DNX2","text":"No prudent action remains if the practicable has no end toward which it moves.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_DAZY_BOO2:P0007","trace_type":"Direct","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_DAZY_BOO2:DNX3","text":"No science remains if the analysis of causes and definitions never ceases.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_DAZY_BOO2:P0008","trace_type":"Direct","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_DAZY_BOO2:DNX4","text":"No correct method remains if the mode of speaking is judged by the pleasure or habit of the hearer alone.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_DAZY_BOO2:P0009","trace_type":"Direct","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]}],"original_name":"Deep Apophatics"},{"number":"7","name":"Super-Apophatics","items":[{"id":"CH_DAZY_BOO2:SNX1","text":"A world is excluded in which beings are only consequences of one another, with no principle founding the order.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_DAZY_BOO2:P0004","trace_type":"Negative","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"},{"id":"RAW_DAZY_BOO2:P0005","trace_type":"Negative","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_DAZY_BOO2:SNX2","text":"An intellect that acts without an end is excluded, since thinking and acting always move for the sake of something.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_DAZY_BOO2:P0007","trace_type":"Negative","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_DAZY_BOO2:SNX3","text":"Knowledge is excluded that does not distinguish the finite from the infinite and the appropriate measure of exactness from immoderateness.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_DAZY_BOO2:P0008","trace_type":"Composite","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"},{"id":"RAW_DAZY_BOO2:P0009","trace_type":"Composite","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"},{"id":"RAW_DAZY_BOO2:P0010","trace_type":"Composite","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]}],"original_name":"Super-Apophatics"},{"number":"8","name":"Formal Generative Schema","items":[{"id":"CH_DAZY_BOO2:FGS1","text":"Partial opinions concerning nature gathered together → partial access to truth → necessity of inquiry into the whole.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_DAZY_BOO2:P0001","trace_type":"Direct","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_DAZY_BOO2:FGS2","text":"Inquiry into the whole → inquiry into cause → ascent to that which is most causal and most true.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_DAZY_BOO2:P0001","trace_type":"Structural","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"},{"id":"RAW_DAZY_BOO2:P0003","trace_type":"Structural","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_DAZY_BOO2:FGS3","text":"Rejection of infinite progression → positing of a first principle → preservation of causality, end, definition, and knowledge.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_DAZY_BOO2:P0004","trace_type":"Structural","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"},{"id":"RAW_DAZY_BOO2:P0007","trace_type":"Structural","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"},{"id":"RAW_DAZY_BOO2:P0008","trace_type":"Structural","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_DAZY_BOO2:FGS4","text":"Distinction of the kinds of being → distinction of the appropriate modes of discourse → limitation of mathematical exactness.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_DAZY_BOO2:P0009","trace_type":"Composite","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"},{"id":"RAW_DAZY_BOO2:P0010","trace_type":"Composite","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_DAZY_BOO2:FGS5","text":"Habit of listeners → error concerning the requisite mode → education toward judgment of the measure appropriate to each case.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_DAZY_BOO2:P0009","trace_type":"Direct","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]}],"original_name":"Formal Generative Schema"},{"number":"9","name":"Argument Flow Map","items":[{"id":"CH_DAZY_BOO2:AF1","text":"Partial success of the many → difficulty of the whole → inquiry into the cause.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_DAZY_BOO2:P0001","trace_type":"Composite","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"},{"id":"RAW_DAZY_BOO2:P0003","trace_type":"Composite","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_DAZY_BOO2:AF2","text":"Cause of being true → degree of being → primacy of eternal principles.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_DAZY_BOO2:P0003","trace_type":"Direct","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_DAZY_BOO2:AF3","text":"Infinity of causes → removal of the first and the end → removal of knowledge and action.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_DAZY_BOO2:P0004","trace_type":"Structural","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"},{"id":"RAW_DAZY_BOO2:P0007","trace_type":"Structural","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"},{"id":"RAW_DAZY_BOO2:P0008","trace_type":"Structural","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_DAZY_BOO2:AF4","text":"Impossibility of infinity → finitude of principles → possibility of science.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_DAZY_BOO2:P0004","trace_type":"Composite","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"},{"id":"RAW_DAZY_BOO2:P0008","trace_type":"Composite","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_DAZY_BOO2:AF5","text":"Habit of hearing → variety of false demands → measuring exactness according to the subject.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_DAZY_BOO2:P0009","trace_type":"Composite","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"},{"id":"RAW_DAZY_BOO2:P0010","trace_type":"Composite","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]}],"original_name":"Argument Flow Map"},{"number":"10","name":"Map-Effects","items":[{"id":"CH_DAZY_BOO2:ME1","text":"Truth is shifted from an isolated possession to a common and diachronic collection of partial theories.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_DAZY_BOO2:P0001","trace_type":"Structural","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"},{"id":"RAW_DAZY_BOO2:P0002","trace_type":"Structural","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_DAZY_BOO2:ME2","text":"Truth no longer becomes mere agreement of discourse, but a property dependent on the degree of the causal principle.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_DAZY_BOO2:P0003","trace_type":"Direct","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_DAZY_BOO2:ME3","text":"The infinite is shifted from indeterminate magnitude to a threat against causality, teleology, and knowability.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_DAZY_BOO2:P0004","trace_type":"Structural","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"},{"id":"RAW_DAZY_BOO2:P0007","trace_type":"Structural","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"},{"id":"RAW_DAZY_BOO2:P0008","trace_type":"Structural","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_DAZY_BOO2:ME4","text":"The distinction between theoretical and practical changes the end of inquiry: truth for the theoretical, work for the practical.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_DAZY_BOO2:P0002","trace_type":"Direct","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_DAZY_BOO2:ME5","text":"Exactness is shifted from a universal demand to a relative measure in relation to the matter sought.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_DAZY_BOO2:P0010","trace_type":"Direct","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]}],"original_name":"Map-Effects"},{"number":"11","name":"Altitude Levels","items":[{"id":"CH_DAZY_BOO2:ALT1","text":"At the surface level, the discourse treats of truth, causes, the infinite, and modes of hearing.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_DAZY_BOO2:P0001","trace_type":"Structural","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"},{"id":"RAW_DAZY_BOO2:P0004","trace_type":"Structural","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"},{"id":"RAW_DAZY_BOO2:P0009","trace_type":"Structural","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_DAZY_BOO2:ALT2","text":"In its arrangement, the discourse grounds science upon an order of prior and posterior, principle and consequence.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_DAZY_BOO2:P0004","trace_type":"Structural","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"},{"id":"RAW_DAZY_BOO2:P0005","trace_type":"Structural","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_DAZY_BOO2:ALT3","text":"At a super-strategic level, the discourse posits disagreement among opinions not as an obstacle but as matter and preliminary training for inquiry.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_DAZY_BOO2:P0001","trace_type":"Structural","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"},{"id":"RAW_DAZY_BOO2:P0002","trace_type":"Structural","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_DAZY_BOO2:ALT4","text":"Concerning being, the discourse orders being, being true, and being caused according to the same degree.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_DAZY_BOO2:P0003","trace_type":"Direct","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_DAZY_BOO2:ALT5","text":"Concerning time, the discourse conceives the eternal not merely as long duration, but as uncaused and as the cause of the others.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_DAZY_BOO2:P0003","trace_type":"Composite","confidence":"medium","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]}],"original_name":"Altitude Levels"},{"number":"12","name":"Minimal Reconstruction Test","items":[{"id":"CH_DAZY_BOO2:MRT1","text":"The axioms suffice for reconstruction of the core, since from them follows the transition from truth to finite principles.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_DAZY_BOO2:P0001","trace_type":"Structural","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"},{"id":"RAW_DAZY_BOO2:P0003","trace_type":"Structural","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"},{"id":"RAW_DAZY_BOO2:P0004","trace_type":"Structural","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"},{"id":"RAW_DAZY_BOO2:P0010","trace_type":"Structural","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_DAZY_BOO2:MRT2","text":"The system's strongest connection lies in the bond of cause, truth, being, and knowledge.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_DAZY_BOO2:P0003","trace_type":"Structural","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"},{"id":"RAW_DAZY_BOO2:P0008","trace_type":"Structural","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_DAZY_BOO2:MRT3","text":"The rejection of the infinite functions as a necessary condition of the system, but the proof presupposes the value of the first rather than compellingly deriving it from everything.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_DAZY_BOO2:P0004","trace_type":"Tension","confidence":"medium","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"},{"id":"RAW_DAZY_BOO2:P0005","trace_type":"Tension","confidence":"medium","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_DAZY_BOO2:MRT4","text":"The relation between the collective progress of opinions and the hierarchy of truer principles remains not wholly articulated.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_DAZY_BOO2:P0001","trace_type":"Tension","confidence":"medium","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"},{"id":"RAW_DAZY_BOO2:P0002","trace_type":"Tension","confidence":"medium","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"},{"id":"RAW_DAZY_BOO2:P0003","trace_type":"Tension","confidence":"medium","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_DAZY_BOO2:MRT5","text":"The reconstruction preserves the movement of the argument if the final part concerning hearing is understood as a methodological limitation of the preceding causal inquiry.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_DAZY_BOO2:P0009","trace_type":"Structural","confidence":"medium","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"},{"id":"RAW_DAZY_BOO2:P0010","trace_type":"Structural","confidence":"medium","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]}],"original_name":"Minimal Reconstruction Test"}],"revision":"CH_DAZY_BOO2@R001","source_variant_id":"canonical:CH_DAZY_BOO2:v1","source_language_code":"grc","original_title":"Book 2"}],"identity":{"layer":"CH","kicker":"Chapter 02","title":"Book 2","context":"Chapter 02","original_title":"Book 2","original_context":"Chapter 02"},"original_title":"Book 2","title_language_code":"en"},{"id":"CH_DAZY_BOO3","layer":"CH","title":"Book 3","order":3,"parents":["RAW_DAZY_BOO3"],"revision":"CH_DAZY_BOO3@R001","sha256":"e28313d937663e274ee3a016cde013aed0c1d1eb6015f16521ae69a688b933ef","sections":[{"number":"1","name":"Argumental Spine","items":[{"id":"CH_DAZY_BOO3:AS1","text":"Τὸ κύριον ἀπόρημα ἐστὶ πῶς ἡ ζητουμένη πρώτη ἐπιστήμη δύναται γνῶναι τὰς ἀρχὰς καὶ τὴν οὐσίαν, ὅταν ἡ ἑνότης τοῦ ἀντικειμένου, ἡ φύσις τῶν ἀρχῶν καὶ ὁ τρόπος τῆς γνώσεως ἀμφισβητῶνται.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_DAZY_BOO3:P0002","trace_type":"Composite","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"},{"id":"RAW_DAZY_BOO3:P0005","trace_type":"Composite","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_DAZY_BOO3:AS2","text":"Ἡ κυρία θέσις ἐστὶν ὅτι λύσις οὐκ ἔστιν ἄνευ προτέρας καὶ πλήρους διαπορήσεως, ἐπειδὴ ἡ εὐπορία λύσις ἐγνωσμένου δεσμοῦ ἐστιν.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_DAZY_BOO3:P0001","trace_type":"Direct","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_DAZY_BOO3:AS3","text":"Ὁ λόγος οὐ λύσεις εὐθὺς τίθεται, ἀλλὰ τοὺς ὅρους ἀποκαλύπτει οὓς πᾶσα δυνατὴ λύσις ὀφείλει σῴζειν.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_DAZY_BOO3:P0001","trace_type":"Structural","confidence":"medium","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_DAZY_BOO3:AS4","text":"Ὁ συλλογισμὸς διαπορητικὸς καὶ διαλεκτικός ἐστιν, ἐπεὶ ἑκάστην θέσιν διὰ τῶν ἀναγκαίων ἐπακολούθων καὶ τῶν ἀντικειμένων λόγων δοκιμάζει.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_DAZY_BOO3:P0012","trace_type":"Structural","confidence":"medium","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"},{"id":"RAW_DAZY_BOO3:P0014","trace_type":"Structural","confidence":"medium","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"},{"id":"RAW_DAZY_BOO3:P0045","trace_type":"Structural","confidence":"medium","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_DAZY_BOO3:AS5","text":"Ἡ κίνησις φέρεται ἀπὸ τῶν παραδεδομένων δοξῶν καὶ τῶν παρεωραμένων ζητημάτων πρὸς διχοτομίας, ἐξ αὐτῶν δὲ πρὸς ἀδυνάτους ἢ ἀλόγους συνεπείας καὶ τέλος πρὸς ἀκριβέστερον δεσμόν.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_DAZY_BOO3:P0001","trace_type":"Structural","confidence":"medium","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"},{"id":"RAW_DAZY_BOO3:P0014","trace_type":"Structural","confidence":"medium","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"},{"id":"RAW_DAZY_BOO3:P0045","trace_type":"Structural","confidence":"medium","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_DAZY_BOO3:AS6","text":"Ἡ ὅλη διαπόρησις συνάγει τὰς ἀντιθέσεις καθόλου καὶ καθ᾽ ἕκαστον, ἑνὸς καὶ πολλῶν, αἰσθητοῦ καὶ χωριστοῦ, οὐσίας καὶ κατηγορουμένου, φθαρτοῦ καὶ ἀφθάρτου.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_DAZY_BOO3:P0002","trace_type":"Structural","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"},{"id":"RAW_DAZY_BOO3:P0003","trace_type":"Structural","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"},{"id":"RAW_DAZY_BOO3:P0005","trace_type":"Structural","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"},{"id":"RAW_DAZY_BOO3:P0006","trace_type":"Structural","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]}],"original_name":"Argumental Spine"},{"number":"2","name":"Load-Bearing Axioms","items":[{"id":"CH_DAZY_BOO3:A1","text":"Ἡ ὕστερον εὐπορία λύσις τῶν πρότερον ἀπορουμένων ἐστίν, καὶ λύειν οὐκ ἔστιν ἀγνοοῦντα τὸν δεσμόν.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_DAZY_BOO3:P0001","trace_type":"Direct","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_DAZY_BOO3:A2","text":"Ὁ πάντων τῶν ἀμφισβητούντων λόγων ἀκηκοὼς βέλτιον δύναται κρίνειν.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_DAZY_BOO3:P0002","trace_type":"Direct","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_DAZY_BOO3:A3","text":"Τὰ πράγματα γνωρίζεται ᾗ ἕν τι καὶ ταὐτὸν καὶ καθόλου αὐτοῖς ὑπάρχει.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_DAZY_BOO3:P0025","trace_type":"Direct","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_DAZY_BOO3:A4","text":"Μία ἐπιστήμη ἓν ὑποκείμενον γένος καὶ τὰ καθ᾽ αὑτὸ συμβεβηκότα αὐτῷ θεωρεῖ.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_DAZY_BOO3:P0013","trace_type":"Direct","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_DAZY_BOO3:A5","text":"Πᾶσα ἀπόδειξις ἔκ τινων, περί τι καὶ τινῶν ἐστίν, ὥστε περὶ πάντων ἀπόδειξις εἶναι ἀδύνατον.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_DAZY_BOO3:P0012","trace_type":"Direct","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_DAZY_BOO3:A6","text":"Τοῦ τί ἐστιν οὐκ ἔστιν ἀπόδειξις κατὰ τὸν τρόπον καθ᾽ ὃν τὰ πάθη ἀποδείκνυται.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_DAZY_BOO3:P0014","trace_type":"Direct","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_DAZY_BOO3:A7","text":"Ἡ οὐσία τόδε τι σημαίνει, τὸ δὲ κοινῇ κατηγορούμενον τοιόνδε σημαίνει.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_DAZY_BOO3:P0058","trace_type":"Direct","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_DAZY_BOO3:A8","text":"Τὰ πάθη, αἱ κινήσεις, τὰ πρός τι καὶ αἱ διαθέσεις καθ᾽ ὑποκειμένου λέγεται καὶ οὐ τόδε τι σημαίνει.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_DAZY_BOO3:P0052","trace_type":"Direct","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_DAZY_BOO3:A9","text":"Τὰ γένη τῶν αἰτίων οὔτε ἐναντία ἀλλήλοις ἐστὶν οὔτε πᾶσι τοῖς οὖσιν ἅπαντα ὑπάρχει.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_DAZY_BOO3:P0007","trace_type":"Direct","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_DAZY_BOO3:A10","text":"Ἀρχὴ ἢ αἰτία δεῖ τὸ ἐξ αὐτῆς ἀποδιδόμενον διαφέρειν καὶ αἰτιᾶσθαι.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_DAZY_BOO3:P0024","trace_type":"Direct","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_DAZY_BOO3:A11","text":"Τὰ αἰσθητὰ πάντα ἐν κινήσει ἐστὶ καὶ φθορᾷ ὑπόκειται.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_DAZY_BOO3:P0026","trace_type":"Direct","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_DAZY_BOO3:A12","text":"Ἡ γένεσις οὔτε ἐκ μὴ ὄντος γίνεται οὔτε εἰς ἄπειρον προέρχεται, ἀλλ᾽ ἀγένητόν τι καὶ πέρας προϋποτίθεται.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_DAZY_BOO3:P0026","trace_type":"Direct","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_DAZY_BOO3:A13","text":"Τὸ ὂν καὶ τὸ ἓν γένη εἶναι οὐ δύναται, ἐπειδὴ αἱ διαφοραὶ τοῦ γένους δεῖ καὶ εἶναι καὶ μίαν ἑκάστην εἶναι.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_DAZY_BOO3:P0021","trace_type":"Direct","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_DAZY_BOO3:A14","text":"Ὁ διὰ τῶν γενῶν ὁρισμὸς καὶ ὁ ἐκ τῶν ἐνυπαρχόντων στοιχείων λόγος δύο διαφόρους ἀποδόσεις τῆς οὐσίας παρέχει.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_DAZY_BOO3:P0021","trace_type":"Direct","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_DAZY_BOO3:A15","text":"Ἡ κατ᾽ ἀριθμὸν ἑνότης τῶν ἀρχῶν τὸ πλῆθος τῶν ἐξ αὐτῶν ἀναιρεῖ, ἡ δὲ κατ᾽ εἶδος ἑνότης τὸν ἀριθμὸν αὐτῶν οὐχ ὁρίζει.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_DAZY_BOO3:P0028","trace_type":"Direct","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_DAZY_BOO3:A16","text":"Αἱ στιγμαί, αἱ γραμμαί, αἱ ἐπιφάνειαι καὶ τὸ νῦν πέρατα ἢ διαιρέσεις εἰσίν, οὐχ οὐσίαι κατὰ γένεσιν καὶ φθοράν.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_DAZY_BOO3:P0054","trace_type":"Composite","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"},{"id":"RAW_DAZY_BOO3:P0055","trace_type":"Composite","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_DAZY_BOO3:A17","text":"Τὸ δυνάμει ὂν ἐνδέχεται μὴ ἐνεργείᾳ εἶναι.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_DAZY_BOO3:P0057","trace_type":"Direct","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_DAZY_BOO3:A18","text":"Πᾶσα προτεινομένη ἀρχὴ κρίνεται ἐκ τῶν ἀναγκαίων ἐπακολούθων καὶ ἐκ τῆς δυνάμεως αὐτῆς αἰτιᾶσθαι τὰ φαινόμενα.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_DAZY_BOO3:P0007","trace_type":"Structural","confidence":"medium","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"},{"id":"RAW_DAZY_BOO3:P0012","trace_type":"Structural","confidence":"medium","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"},{"id":"RAW_DAZY_BOO3:P0045","trace_type":"Structural","confidence":"medium","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]}],"original_name":"Load-Bearing Axioms"},{"number":"3","name":"Apophatic Structure","items":[{"id":"CH_DAZY_BOO3:NX1","text":"Ἡ ζήτησις ἄνευ προτέρας διαπορήσεως οὐκ ἔχει φανερὸν τέλος οὐδὲ κριτήριον τοῦ εὑρηκέναι.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_DAZY_BOO3:P0001","trace_type":"Negative","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_DAZY_BOO3:NX2","text":"Ἡ τοῦ κοινοῦ ὀνόματος «αἰτία» ἑνότης οὐχ ἱκανή ἐστι πρὸς σύστασιν μιᾶς ἐπιστήμης πασῶν τῶν αἰτίων.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_DAZY_BOO3:P0007","trace_type":"Direct","confidence":"medium","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_DAZY_BOO3:NX3","text":"Τὰ κοινὰ ἀποδεικτικὰ ἀξιώματα οὔτε ἰδίᾳ γεωμετρίας οὔτε ἄλλης τινὸς μερικῆς ἐπιστήμης ἐστίν.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_DAZY_BOO3:P0011","trace_type":"Negative","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_DAZY_BOO3:NX4","text":"Τὰ ἀξιώματα οὐ δύναται πάντα δι᾽ ἀποδείξεως γνωρίζεσθαι, ἐπεὶ πᾶσα ἀπόδειξις προτέροις χρῆται.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_DAZY_BOO3:P0012","trace_type":"Negative","confidence":"medium","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_DAZY_BOO3:NX5","text":"Ἡ οὐσία οὐκ ἐξαντλεῖται ἐν τοῖς συμβεβηκόσιν οὐδὲ τὸ τί ἐστιν ὡς πάθος ἀποδείκνυται.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_DAZY_BOO3:P0014","trace_type":"Composite","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"},{"id":"RAW_DAZY_BOO3:P0052","trace_type":"Composite","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_DAZY_BOO3:NX6","text":"Αἱ αἰσθηταὶ οὐσίαι μόνον οὐκ ἱκαναὶ εἰσὶ πρὸς θεμελίωσιν ἐπιστήμης, ἀϊδιότητος καὶ γενέσεως.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_DAZY_BOO3:P0025","trace_type":"Composite","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"},{"id":"RAW_DAZY_BOO3:P0026","trace_type":"Composite","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_DAZY_BOO3:NX7","text":"Τὰ εἴδη οὐκ ἐξηγεῖται ἱκανῶς ὡς αἰσθητὰ πράγματα μόνον ἀΐδια πεποιημένα.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_DAZY_BOO3:P0014","trace_type":"Composite","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"},{"id":"RAW_DAZY_BOO3:P0015","trace_type":"Composite","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_DAZY_BOO3:NX8","text":"Τὰ μαθηματικὰ μεταξύ οὔτε χωρὶς τῶν αἰσθητῶν ῥᾳδίως τίθεται οὔτε ἐν αὐτοῖς ἄνευ διπλασιασμοῦ καὶ τοπικῆς ἀδυναμίας.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_DAZY_BOO3:P0015","trace_type":"Composite","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"},{"id":"RAW_DAZY_BOO3:P0018","trace_type":"Composite","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_DAZY_BOO3:NX9","text":"Τὰ γένη καὶ τὰ ἐνυπάρχοντα στοιχεῖα οὐ δύναται ὁμοίως καὶ κατὰ τὸ αὐτὸ ἀρχαὶ τῆς οὐσίας εἶναι.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_DAZY_BOO3:P0020","trace_type":"Composite","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"},{"id":"RAW_DAZY_BOO3:P0021","trace_type":"Composite","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_DAZY_BOO3:NX10","text":"Τὸ ὂν καὶ τὸ ἓν οὐ δύναται ὡς πρῶτα γένη κατηγορεῖσθαι.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_DAZY_BOO3:P0021","trace_type":"Negative","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_DAZY_BOO3:NX11","text":"Ὁ μυθικὸς λόγος περὶ νέκταρος καὶ ἀμβροσίας οὐκ αἰτιᾶται τὴν διαφορὰν θνητοῦ καὶ ἀθανάτου.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_DAZY_BOO3:P0029","trace_type":"Negative","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_DAZY_BOO3:NX12","text":"Αἱ αὐταὶ ἀρχαὶ φθαρτῶν καὶ ἀφθάρτων οὐκ ἀρκοῦσιν, ἐὰν μηδεμία διαφοροποιὸς αἰτία ἀποδίδωται.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_DAZY_BOO3:P0029","trace_type":"Composite","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"},{"id":"RAW_DAZY_BOO3:P0030","trace_type":"Composite","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_DAZY_BOO3:NX13","text":"Τὸ νεῖκος οὐ καθαρῶς μόνον φθορᾶς αἴτιόν ἐστιν, οὐδὲ ἡ φιλότης μόνον τοῦ εἶναι, ἐπεὶ ἑκάτερον καὶ γεννᾷ καὶ φθείρει κατὰ διάφορον σύνθεσιν.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_DAZY_BOO3:P0041","trace_type":"Negative","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_DAZY_BOO3:NX14","text":"Αἱ στιγμαί, αἱ γραμμαὶ καὶ αἱ ἐπιφάνειαι οὐ καθίστανται οὐσίαι μόνον διότι τὰ σώματα δι᾽ αὐτῶν ὁρίζεται.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_DAZY_BOO3:P0054","trace_type":"Negative","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"},{"id":"RAW_DAZY_BOO3:P0055","trace_type":"Negative","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]}],"original_name":"Apophatic Structure"},{"number":"4","name":"Derivative Propositions","items":[{"id":"CH_DAZY_BOO3:D1","text":"Ἡ πλήρης προαπορία ἅμα τὸν ζητούμενον δεσμὸν καὶ τὸ κριτήριον τῆς μελλούσης λύσεως φανερὸν ποιεῖ.","refs":["CH_DAZY_BOO3:A1","CH_DAZY_BOO3:A2"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"From","passages":[{"id":"RAW_DAZY_BOO3:P0001","trace_type":"Direct","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_DAZY_BOO3:D2","text":"Ἡ ἑνότης τῆς περὶ τὰς αἰτίας ἐπιστήμης οὐκ ἕπεται ἐκ μόνης τῆς κοινῆς προσηγορίας, ἐπειδὴ τὰ εἴδη τῶν αἰτίων μήτε ὑφ᾽ ἓν γένος ῥᾳδίως πίπτει μήτε πᾶσιν ὑπάρχει.","refs":["CH_DAZY_BOO3:A4","CH_DAZY_BOO3:A9"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"From","passages":[{"id":"RAW_DAZY_BOO3:P0007","trace_type":"Direct","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_DAZY_BOO3:D3","text":"Εἰ τὰ κοινὰ ἀξιώματα ἀποδεικτὰ εἴη, ἓν γένος πάντων τῶν δεικνυμένων ὑποκείσθαι ἔδει· τοῦτο δὲ τὴν καθολικότητα αὐτῶν ἀναιρεῖ.","refs":["CH_DAZY_BOO3:A4","CH_DAZY_BOO3:A5"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"From","passages":[{"id":"RAW_DAZY_BOO3:P0011","trace_type":"Composite","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"},{"id":"RAW_DAZY_BOO3:P0012","trace_type":"Composite","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_DAZY_BOO3:D4","text":"Ἡ αὐτὴ θεωρία οὐ ῥᾳδίως ἀποδίδωσι καὶ τὴν οὐσίαν καὶ τὰ καθ᾽ αὑτὴν συμβεβηκότα, ἐπειδὴ τὰ μὲν ἀποδείκνυται, τοῦ δὲ τί ἐστιν οὐκ ἔστιν ὁμοία ἀπόδειξις.","refs":["CH_DAZY_BOO3:A4","CH_DAZY_BOO3:A6","CH_DAZY_BOO3:A8"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"From","passages":[{"id":"RAW_DAZY_BOO3:P0013","trace_type":"Composite","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"},{"id":"RAW_DAZY_BOO3:P0014","trace_type":"Composite","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_DAZY_BOO3:D5","text":"Εἰ μηδὲν παρὰ τὰ αἰσθητὰ καθ᾽ ἕκαστα ἐστίν, οὔτε καθόλου ἐπιστήμη οὔτε ἀΐδιον οὔτε ἀκίνητον ὑπολείπεται.","refs":["CH_DAZY_BOO3:A3","CH_DAZY_BOO3:A11"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"From","passages":[{"id":"RAW_DAZY_BOO3:P0025","trace_type":"Composite","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"},{"id":"RAW_DAZY_BOO3:P0026","trace_type":"Composite","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_DAZY_BOO3:D6","text":"Αἱ ἀρχαὶ καθόλου μὲν οὐσίαι οὐκ εἰσίν, καθ᾽ ἕκαστα δὲ οὖσαι καθόλου ἐπιστηταὶ οὐκ εἰσίν.","refs":["CH_DAZY_BOO3:A3","CH_DAZY_BOO3:A7"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"From","passages":[{"id":"RAW_DAZY_BOO3:P0058","trace_type":"Direct","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_DAZY_BOO3:D7","text":"Εἰ τὸ ἓν καὶ τὸ ὂν χωρισταὶ οὐσίαι εἰσίν, ἡ τῶν ὄντων πολλότης ἄπορος γίνεται· εἰ δὲ μὴ οὐσίαι, ἡ χωριστὴ φύσις τοῦ ἀριθμοῦ στερεῖται θεμελίου.","refs":["CH_DAZY_BOO3:A7","CH_DAZY_BOO3:A13","CH_DAZY_BOO3:A15"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"From","passages":[{"id":"RAW_DAZY_BOO3:P0048","trace_type":"Direct","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_DAZY_BOO3:D8","text":"Τὰ γένη καὶ τὰ ἐνυπάρχοντα στοιχεῖα οὐ παρέχει τὸν αὐτὸν λόγον τῆς οὐσίας, ὥστε ἡ ταυτόχρονος ἀρχικότης αὐτῶν διπλοῦν ὁρισμὸν γεννᾷ.","refs":["CH_DAZY_BOO3:A6","CH_DAZY_BOO3:A14"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"From","passages":[{"id":"RAW_DAZY_BOO3:P0020","trace_type":"Composite","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"},{"id":"RAW_DAZY_BOO3:P0021","trace_type":"Composite","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_DAZY_BOO3:D9","text":"Τὰ μαθηματικὰ πέρατα πρότερα μὲν φαίνεται τῶν σωμάτων κατὰ τὸν ὁρισμόν, οὐκ ἔχει δὲ τὴν αὐτοτελῆ γένεσιν, φθορὰν καὶ ὑπόστασιν οὐσίας.","refs":["CH_DAZY_BOO3:A7","CH_DAZY_BOO3:A16"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"From","passages":[{"id":"RAW_DAZY_BOO3:P0052","trace_type":"Composite","confidence":"medium","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"},{"id":"RAW_DAZY_BOO3:P0054","trace_type":"Composite","confidence":"medium","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"},{"id":"RAW_DAZY_BOO3:P0055","trace_type":"Composite","confidence":"medium","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_DAZY_BOO3:D10","text":"Αἱ αὐταὶ ἀρχαὶ οὐκ ἐξηγοῦσιν αὐταὶ καθ᾽ αὑτὰς διὰ τί τὰ μὲν ἐξ αὐτῶν φθείρεται τὰ δὲ μένει ἄφθαρτα.","refs":["CH_DAZY_BOO3:A10","CH_DAZY_BOO3:A18"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"From","passages":[{"id":"RAW_DAZY_BOO3:P0029","trace_type":"Composite","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"},{"id":"RAW_DAZY_BOO3:P0030","trace_type":"Composite","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_DAZY_BOO3:D11","text":"Ἡ πρόσθεσις χωριστῶν εἰδῶν ἢ μεταξύ οὐ λύει τὴν ἀπορίαν, ἐὰν τὰ πρόσθετα μόνον διπλασιάζῃ τὰ αἰσθητὰ καὶ μηδεμίαν ἰδίαν αἰτίαν παρέχῃ.","refs":["CH_DAZY_BOO3:A10","CH_DAZY_BOO3:A18"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"From","passages":[{"id":"RAW_DAZY_BOO3:P0014","trace_type":"Composite","confidence":"medium","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"},{"id":"RAW_DAZY_BOO3:P0015","trace_type":"Composite","confidence":"medium","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"},{"id":"RAW_DAZY_BOO3:P0016","trace_type":"Composite","confidence":"medium","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_DAZY_BOO3:D12","text":"Εἰ τὰ στοιχεῖα μόνον δυνάμει ἐστίν, ἐνδέχεται μηδὲν ἐνεργείᾳ εἶναι· εἰ δὲ ἄλλον τρόπον ἐστίν, προτέρα δύναμις ἢ αἰτία ζητεῖται.","refs":["CH_DAZY_BOO3:A17","CH_DAZY_BOO3:A18"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"From","passages":[{"id":"RAW_DAZY_BOO3:P0057","trace_type":"Direct","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]}],"original_name":"Derivative Propositions"},{"number":"5","name":"Super-Derivatives","items":[{"id":"CH_DAZY_BOO3:SD1","text":"Ἡ καθόλου ἐπιστήμη τοῦ ὄντος οὐ δύναται τὴν ἑνότητά της ἐκ γένους λαβεῖν, ἐπειδὴ τὸ ὂν γένος οὐκ ἔστιν· ἄλλος οὖν τρόπος ἑνώσεως τοῦ ἀντικειμένου ζητητέος ἐστίν.","refs":["CH_DAZY_BOO3:A3","CH_DAZY_BOO3:A4","CH_DAZY_BOO3:A13"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"From","passages":[{"id":"RAW_DAZY_BOO3:P0013","trace_type":"Structural","confidence":"medium","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"},{"id":"RAW_DAZY_BOO3:P0021","trace_type":"Structural","confidence":"medium","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_DAZY_BOO3:SD2","text":"Ἡ διάταξις τῆς γνώσεως καὶ ἡ διάταξις τῆς οὐσίας ἀντιτείνεται· ἡ μὲν γνώσις τὸ καθόλου ἀπαιτεῖ, ἡ δὲ οὐσία τόδε τι εἶναι δεῖ.","refs":["CH_DAZY_BOO3:A3","CH_DAZY_BOO3:A7","CH_DAZY_BOO3:D6"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"From","passages":[{"id":"RAW_DAZY_BOO3:P0025","trace_type":"Composite","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"},{"id":"RAW_DAZY_BOO3:P0058","trace_type":"Composite","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_DAZY_BOO3:SD3","text":"Ἡ χωριστότης οὐκ ἀποτελεῖ καθ᾽ αὑτὴν ἐξηγητικὴν προτεραιότητα, ἐὰν ἡ σχέσις τοῦ χωριστοῦ πρὸς τὰ αἰσθητὰ καὶ τὴν γένεσιν ἀνεξήγητος μένῃ.","refs":["CH_DAZY_BOO3:A10","CH_DAZY_BOO3:D11"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"From","passages":[{"id":"RAW_DAZY_BOO3:P0014","trace_type":"Structural","confidence":"medium","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"},{"id":"RAW_DAZY_BOO3:P0018","trace_type":"Structural","confidence":"medium","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_DAZY_BOO3:SD4","text":"Πᾶσα θεωρία τῶν πρώτων ἀρχῶν ὀφείλει ἅμα τὴν ἑνότητα, τὴν πολλότητα, τὴν μεταβολήν, τὴν φθορὰν καὶ τὴν ἀϊδιότητα αἰτιᾶσθαι.","refs":["CH_DAZY_BOO3:A10","CH_DAZY_BOO3:A11","CH_DAZY_BOO3:A12","CH_DAZY_BOO3:D10"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"From","passages":[{"id":"RAW_DAZY_BOO3:P0026","trace_type":"Structural","confidence":"medium","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"},{"id":"RAW_DAZY_BOO3:P0028","trace_type":"Structural","confidence":"medium","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"},{"id":"RAW_DAZY_BOO3:P0029","trace_type":"Structural","confidence":"medium","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"},{"id":"RAW_DAZY_BOO3:P0045","trace_type":"Structural","confidence":"medium","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_DAZY_BOO3:SD5","text":"Ἡ διαπόρησις μεταβάλλει τὴν πρώτη φιλοσοφίαν ἐξ ἑτοίμου δόγματος εἰς σύστημα ὅρων καὶ ἀποκλεισμῶν, οὓς ἡ μελλούσα λύσις δεῖ συντηρεῖν.","refs":["CH_DAZY_BOO3:A1","CH_DAZY_BOO3:A18","CH_DAZY_BOO3:D1"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"From","passages":[{"id":"RAW_DAZY_BOO3:P0001","trace_type":"Structural","confidence":"medium","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"},{"id":"RAW_DAZY_BOO3:P0045","trace_type":"Structural","confidence":"medium","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]}],"original_name":"Super-Derivatives"},{"number":"6","name":"Deep Apophatics","items":[{"id":"CH_DAZY_BOO3:DNX1","text":"Ἡ καθολικότης τῆς γνώσεως καὶ ἡ καθ᾽ ἕκαστον ὑπόστασις οὐ δύναται ἁπλῶς ταὐτίζεσθαι.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_DAZY_BOO3:P0058","trace_type":"Direct","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_DAZY_BOO3:DNX2","text":"Ἡ λογικὴ προτεραιότης οὐ συνεπάγεται ἀναγκαίως χωριστὴν οὐσίαν.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_DAZY_BOO3:P0052","trace_type":"Structural","confidence":"medium","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"},{"id":"RAW_DAZY_BOO3:P0055","trace_type":"Structural","confidence":"medium","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_DAZY_BOO3:DNX3","text":"Ὁ πολλαπλασιασμὸς τῶν γενῶν τοῦ ὄντος οὐ λύνει ἀπορίαν, ἐὰν μόνον ἀντίγραφα τῶν αἰσθητῶν παράγῃ.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_DAZY_BOO3:P0014","trace_type":"Negative","confidence":"medium","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"},{"id":"RAW_DAZY_BOO3:P0015","trace_type":"Negative","confidence":"medium","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_DAZY_BOO3:DNX4","text":"Τὸ ὂν οὐκ ἀνάγεται εἰς ἓν ἀνώτατον γένος ἄνευ ἀναιρέσεως τῶν διαφορῶν αὐτοῦ.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_DAZY_BOO3:P0021","trace_type":"Negative","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_DAZY_BOO3:DNX5","text":"Οὔτε ἡ ἐξ οὐκ ὄντος γένεσις οὔτε ἡ εἰς ἄπειρον ἀναγωγὴ τῶν ἀρχῶν ἐπιτρέπεται.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_DAZY_BOO3:P0026","trace_type":"Negative","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_DAZY_BOO3:DNX6","text":"Μία ἀδιαφοροποίητος αἰτιακὴ διάταξις οὐ δύναται ἐξηγεῖν ἐναντίας ἐκβάσεις, οἷον φθορὰν καὶ ἀφθαρσίαν.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_DAZY_BOO3:P0029","trace_type":"Composite","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"},{"id":"RAW_DAZY_BOO3:P0030","trace_type":"Composite","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_DAZY_BOO3:DNX7","text":"Ἡ ψιλὴ δύναμις οὐκ ἐγγυᾶται ὅτι ἔσται τι ἐνεργείᾳ.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_DAZY_BOO3:P0057","trace_type":"Negative","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]}],"original_name":"Deep Apophatics"},{"number":"7","name":"Super-Apophatics","items":[{"id":"CH_DAZY_BOO3:SNX1","text":"Ἐκκλείεται ἡ δυνατότης πάντα τὰ πρῶτα ἀξιώματα ἀποδεικνύναι, ἐπειδὴ ἡ ἀπόδειξις ἀναγκαίως ἐξ ἀναποδείκτων τινῶν ἄρχεται.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_DAZY_BOO3:P0012","trace_type":"Negative","confidence":"medium","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_DAZY_BOO3:SNX2","text":"Ἐκκλείεται μία ἀδιάκριτος φύσις ἅμα καθόλου καὶ τόδε τι, μία καὶ πολλά, χωριστὴ καὶ ἐνυπάρχουσα, ἀκίνητος καὶ γεννητική.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_DAZY_BOO3:P0004","trace_type":"Structural","confidence":"low","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"},{"id":"RAW_DAZY_BOO3:P0005","trace_type":"Structural","confidence":"low","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"},{"id":"RAW_DAZY_BOO3:P0026","trace_type":"Structural","confidence":"low","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"},{"id":"RAW_DAZY_BOO3:P0058","trace_type":"Structural","confidence":"low","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_DAZY_BOO3:SNX3","text":"Ἀπουσιάζει ἐκ τοῦ βιβλίου τελικὸν κριτήριον ᾧ τὸ ἕτερον κέρας ἑκάστης ἀπορίας ἁπλῶς αἱρεῖται· ἡ ἀπουσία αὕτη τὴν μελλούσαν θεωρίαν ἀναγκαίαν ποιεῖ.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_DAZY_BOO3:P0001","trace_type":"Structural","confidence":"low","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"},{"id":"RAW_DAZY_BOO3:P0058","trace_type":"Structural","confidence":"low","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_DAZY_BOO3:SNX4","text":"Ἡ ὑπόθεσις δευτέρου χωριστοῦ κόσμου οὐ δύναται ἀντὶ αἰτιώδους λόγου χρησιμεύειν.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_DAZY_BOO3:P0015","trace_type":"Negative","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"},{"id":"RAW_DAZY_BOO3:P0016","trace_type":"Negative","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]}],"original_name":"Super-Apophatics"},{"number":"8","name":"Formal Generative Schema","items":[{"id":"CH_DAZY_BOO3:FGS1","text":"Ἡ πρώτη βαθμὶς συλλέγει τάς τε παραδεδομένας ἀμφισβητήσεις καὶ τὰ παρεωραμένα ζητήματα, ἵνα ὁ δεσμὸς ὅλος φανερὸς γένηται.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_DAZY_BOO3:P0001","trace_type":"Direct","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_DAZY_BOO3:FGS2","text":"Ἡ δευτέρα βαθμὶς μετατίθησιν ἕκαστον ζήτημα εἰς ἀντίθεσιν δύο κεράτων, οἷον μία ἢ πολλαὶ ἐπιστῆμαι, καθόλου ἢ καθ᾽ ἕκαστον, χωριστὸν ἢ ἐνυπάρχον.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_DAZY_BOO3:P0002","trace_type":"Composite","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"},{"id":"RAW_DAZY_BOO3:P0005","trace_type":"Composite","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_DAZY_BOO3:FGS3","text":"Ἡ τρίτη βαθμὶς συνάπτει ἕκαστον κέρας πρὸς τοὺς κοινοὺς ὅρους τῆς ἐπιστήμης, τῆς οὐσίας, τῆς αἰτίας καὶ τῆς ἀποδείξεως.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_DAZY_BOO3:P0007","trace_type":"Structural","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"},{"id":"RAW_DAZY_BOO3:P0012","trace_type":"Structural","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"},{"id":"RAW_DAZY_BOO3:P0014","trace_type":"Structural","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_DAZY_BOO3:FGS4","text":"Ἡ τετάρτη βαθμὶς παράγει ἐξ ἑκατέρου κέρατος ἀδυναμίαν, ἄλογον, ἀναίρεσιν τοῦ ζητουμένου ἢ ἄπειρον ἀναγωγήν.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_DAZY_BOO3:P0014","trace_type":"Structural","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"},{"id":"RAW_DAZY_BOO3:P0022","trace_type":"Structural","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"},{"id":"RAW_DAZY_BOO3:P0045","trace_type":"Structural","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_DAZY_BOO3:FGS5","text":"Ἡ πέμπτη βαθμὶς οὐκ ἀναιρεῖ τὸ ζήτημα, ἀλλὰ διακρίνει ποῖος ὅρος ἐν ἑκατέρῳ κέρατι σώζεται καὶ ποῖος ἀπόλλυται.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_DAZY_BOO3:P0048","trace_type":"Structural","confidence":"medium","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"},{"id":"RAW_DAZY_BOO3:P0058","trace_type":"Structural","confidence":"medium","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_DAZY_BOO3:FGS6","text":"Ἡ ἕκτη βαθμὶς μεταβάλλει τὴν ἀποτυχίαν τῶν κεράτων εἰς ἀπαίτησιν νέας διακρίσεως, ἣ δύναται τὰ ἀληθῆ ἑκατέρου σῴζειν ἄνευ τῶν ἀδυνάτων.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[]},{"id":"CH_DAZY_BOO3:FGS7","text":"Ἡ γεννητικὴ ἀκολουθία κατὰ τύπον ἐστίν· προαπορία → διχοτομία → δοκιμὴ ἑκατέρου κέρατος → ἄλογον ἢ ἀδύνατον → κοινὸς ὅρος → ἀπαίτησις μελλούσης λύσεως.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_DAZY_BOO3:P0001","trace_type":"Structural","confidence":"medium","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"},{"id":"RAW_DAZY_BOO3:P0012","trace_type":"Structural","confidence":"medium","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"},{"id":"RAW_DAZY_BOO3:P0045","trace_type":"Structural","confidence":"medium","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]}],"original_name":"Formal Generative Schema"},{"number":"9","name":"Argument Flow Map","items":[{"id":"CH_DAZY_BOO3:AF1","text":"Ἡ πρώτη φορὰ κινείται οὕτως· ἄδηλον ζητούμενον → προαπορία → γνώσις τοῦ δεσμοῦ → φανερὸν τέλος τῆς ζητήσεως.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_DAZY_BOO3:P0001","trace_type":"Direct","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_DAZY_BOO3:AF2","text":"Ἡ περὶ τῆς ζητουμένης ἐπιστήμης ἀμφιβολία μεταβαίνει εἰς τὴν ἑνότητα τῶν αἰτίων, τὰ κοινὰ ἀξιώματα, τὰς οὐσίας καὶ τὰ καθ᾽ αὑτὰ συμβεβηκότα.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_DAZY_BOO3:P0002","trace_type":"Composite","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"},{"id":"RAW_DAZY_BOO3:P0003","trace_type":"Composite","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"},{"id":"RAW_DAZY_BOO3:P0010","trace_type":"Composite","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_DAZY_BOO3:AF3","text":"Ἡ περὶ τῆς οὐσίας ἀμφιβολία μεταβαίνει ἀπὸ τὰ αἰσθητὰ πρὸς τὰ εἴδη καὶ τὰ μεταξύ, εἶτα πρὸς τὰ γένη, τὰ στοιχεῖα καὶ τὸ παρὰ τὸ σύνολον εἶδος.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_DAZY_BOO3:P0002","trace_type":"Composite","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"},{"id":"RAW_DAZY_BOO3:P0003","trace_type":"Composite","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"},{"id":"RAW_DAZY_BOO3:P0004","trace_type":"Composite","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_DAZY_BOO3:AF4","text":"Ἡ περὶ τῶν ἀρχῶν ἔρευνα διακλαδοῦται κατὰ τὸ ἀριθμῷ ἢ εἴδει, ταὐτὸν ἢ ἕτερον, καθόλου ἢ καθ᾽ ἕκαστον, δυνάμει ἢ ἐνεργείᾳ.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_DAZY_BOO3:P0005","trace_type":"Composite","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"},{"id":"RAW_DAZY_BOO3:P0029","trace_type":"Composite","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"},{"id":"RAW_DAZY_BOO3:P0057","trace_type":"Composite","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_DAZY_BOO3:AF5","text":"Ἡ τελικὴ φορὰ κινείται οὕτως· ἀντίρροπα ἀδύνατα → ἀνεπίλυτος ἀπορία → ἀκριβέστεροι ὅροι τῆς οὐσίας καὶ τῆς ἀρχῆς → ἀναβολὴ τῆς θετικῆς λύσεως.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_DAZY_BOO3:P0001","trace_type":"Structural","confidence":"medium","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"},{"id":"RAW_DAZY_BOO3:P0045","trace_type":"Structural","confidence":"medium","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"},{"id":"RAW_DAZY_BOO3:P0058","trace_type":"Structural","confidence":"medium","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]}],"original_name":"Argument Flow Map"},{"number":"10","name":"Map-Effects","items":[{"id":"CH_DAZY_BOO3:ME1","text":"Ἡ πρώτη φιλοσοφία παύει νοεῖσθαι ὡς ἤδη ἡνωμένη ἐπιστήμη, καὶ αὐτὴ ἡ ἑνότης τοῦ ἀντικειμένου αὐτῆς γίνεται ζητούμενον.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_DAZY_BOO3:P0002","trace_type":"Structural","confidence":"medium","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"},{"id":"RAW_DAZY_BOO3:P0012","trace_type":"Structural","confidence":"medium","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_DAZY_BOO3:ME2","text":"Ἡ ἀντίθεσις αἰσθητοῦ καὶ νοητοῦ μετατάσσεται, ἐπειδὴ τὰ μαθηματικὰ μεταξύ καὶ τὰ εἴδη κρίνονται κατὰ τὴν αἰτιώδη σχέσιν αὐτῶν πρὸς τὰ αἰσθητά.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_DAZY_BOO3:P0014","trace_type":"Structural","confidence":"medium","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"},{"id":"RAW_DAZY_BOO3:P0015","trace_type":"Structural","confidence":"medium","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"},{"id":"RAW_DAZY_BOO3:P0018","trace_type":"Structural","confidence":"medium","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_DAZY_BOO3:ME3","text":"Τὸ καθόλου καὶ τὸ καθ᾽ ἕκαστον παύει εἶναι ψιλὴ λογικὴ διάκρισις καὶ γίνεται ἀντίθεσις ἐπιστητότητος καὶ οὐσιώδους ὑποστάσεως.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_DAZY_BOO3:P0025","trace_type":"Composite","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"},{"id":"RAW_DAZY_BOO3:P0058","trace_type":"Composite","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_DAZY_BOO3:ME4","text":"Αἱ ἀρχαὶ διαγράφονται ἐπὶ πλειόνων ἀξόνων· γένος ἢ στοιχεῖον, πρῶτον ἢ ἔσχατον, ἀριθμῷ ἓν ἢ εἴδει ἕν, αἱ αὐταὶ ἢ ἕτεραι, δυνάμει ἢ ἐνεργείᾳ.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_DAZY_BOO3:P0003","trace_type":"Composite","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"},{"id":"RAW_DAZY_BOO3:P0005","trace_type":"Composite","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"},{"id":"RAW_DAZY_BOO3:P0029","trace_type":"Composite","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"},{"id":"RAW_DAZY_BOO3:P0057","trace_type":"Composite","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_DAZY_BOO3:ME5","text":"Τὰ μαθηματικὰ ἀντικείμενα μετατίθεται ἀπὸ τὴν τάξιν τῶν αὐτονοήτως χωριστῶν οὐσιῶν εἰς τὴν τάξιν τῶν περάτων καὶ διαιρέσεων, ὧν ἡ ὑπόστασις ζητεῖται.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_DAZY_BOO3:P0006","trace_type":"Composite","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"},{"id":"RAW_DAZY_BOO3:P0054","trace_type":"Composite","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"},{"id":"RAW_DAZY_BOO3:P0055","trace_type":"Composite","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_DAZY_BOO3:ME6","text":"Ἡ γένεσις καὶ ἡ φθορὰ καθίστανται κριτήρια τῶν ἀρχῶν, ἐπειδὴ ἡ θεωρία ὀφείλει αἰτιᾶσθαι οὐ μόνον τί ἐστιν ἀλλὰ καὶ διὰ τί τὰ μὲν μεταβάλλει τὰ δὲ μένει.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_DAZY_BOO3:P0029","trace_type":"Composite","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"},{"id":"RAW_DAZY_BOO3:P0045","trace_type":"Composite","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]}],"original_name":"Map-Effects"},{"number":"11","name":"Altitude Levels","items":[{"id":"CH_DAZY_BOO3:ALT1","text":"Ἐπιφανειακῶς ὁ λόγος παρατίθησι καὶ ἀναπτύσσει τὰς ἀπορίας περὶ τῆς ζητουμένης ἐπιστήμης, τῶν οὐσιῶν καὶ τῶν ἀρχῶν.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_DAZY_BOO3:P0002","trace_type":"Composite","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"},{"id":"RAW_DAZY_BOO3:P0003","trace_type":"Composite","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"},{"id":"RAW_DAZY_BOO3:P0005","trace_type":"Composite","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"},{"id":"RAW_DAZY_BOO3:P0006","trace_type":"Composite","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_DAZY_BOO3:ALT2","text":"Κατὰ τὴν διάταξιν ἐξετάζεται πῶς ἡ ἑνότης τῆς ἐπιστήμης ἐξαρτᾶται ἀπὸ τὸ ὑποκείμενον γένος, τὰ ἀξιώματα, τὴν οὐσίαν καὶ τὰ καθ᾽ αὑτὰ πάθη.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_DAZY_BOO3:P0012","trace_type":"Composite","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"},{"id":"RAW_DAZY_BOO3:P0013","trace_type":"Composite","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"},{"id":"RAW_DAZY_BOO3:P0014","trace_type":"Composite","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_DAZY_BOO3:ALT3","text":"Ὑπερστρατηγικῶς ὁ λόγος κωλύει τὴν πρόωρον αἵρεσιν δόγματος καὶ μεταβάλλει ἑκάστην ἀντίθεσιν εἰς δοκιμὴν τῶν ὅρων τῆς μελλούσης θεωρίας.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_DAZY_BOO3:P0001","trace_type":"Structural","confidence":"medium","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"},{"id":"RAW_DAZY_BOO3:P0012","trace_type":"Structural","confidence":"medium","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"},{"id":"RAW_DAZY_BOO3:P0045","trace_type":"Structural","confidence":"medium","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_DAZY_BOO3:ALT4","text":"Περὶ ὄντος καὶ χρόνου ὁ λόγος ἀπαιτεῖ φύσιν δυναμένην σῴζειν ἅμα τὸ τόδε τι, τὸ καθόλου γνωστόν, τὴν πολλότητα, τὴν κίνησιν, τὴν γένεσιν καὶ τὸ ἀΐδιον.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_DAZY_BOO3:P0025","trace_type":"Structural","confidence":"low","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"},{"id":"RAW_DAZY_BOO3:P0026","trace_type":"Structural","confidence":"low","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"},{"id":"RAW_DAZY_BOO3:P0048","trace_type":"Structural","confidence":"low","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"},{"id":"RAW_DAZY_BOO3:P0058","trace_type":"Structural","confidence":"low","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]}],"original_name":"Altitude Levels"},{"number":"12","name":"Minimal Reconstruction Test","items":[{"id":"CH_DAZY_BOO3:MRT1","text":"Τὰ ἐξαιρεθέντα ἀξιώματα ἱκανά ἐστι πρὸς ἀνασύστασιν τῆς διαπορητικῆς μηχανῆς καὶ τῶν κυρίων ἀντιθέσεων αὐτῆς.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[]},{"id":"CH_DAZY_BOO3:MRT2","text":"Ἀδιορίστως μένει ὁ θετικὸς τρόπος καθ᾽ ὃν ἡ τοῦ ὄντος ἐπιστήμη μία ἐστίν, ἐπειδὴ τὸ ὂν γένος οὐκ ἔστιν.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_DAZY_BOO3:P0012","trace_type":"Tension","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"},{"id":"RAW_DAZY_BOO3:P0021","trace_type":"Tension","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_DAZY_BOO3:MRT3","text":"Ἡ διάταξις συμφωνεῖ πρὸς ἑαυτὴν ὡς διαπόρησις, οὐχ ὡς τετελεσμένη θεωρία, ἐπεὶ ἡ ἀναβολὴ τῆς λύσεως μέρος τῆς μεθόδου ἐστίν.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_DAZY_BOO3:P0001","trace_type":"Structural","confidence":"medium","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"},{"id":"RAW_DAZY_BOO3:P0058","trace_type":"Structural","confidence":"medium","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_DAZY_BOO3:MRT4","text":"Ἰσχυροτάτη ἐστὶν ἡ διάταξις ὅπου ἡ καθολικότης τῆς γνώσεως ἀντιτίθεται πρὸς τὸ τόδε τι τῆς οὐσίας.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_DAZY_BOO3:P0025","trace_type":"Composite","confidence":"medium","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"},{"id":"RAW_DAZY_BOO3:P0058","trace_type":"Composite","confidence":"medium","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_DAZY_BOO3:MRT5","text":"Ῥωγμὴ μένει μεταξὺ τῆς ἀπαιτήσεως ἑνὸς ὑποκειμένου γένους καὶ τῆς ἀρνήσεως ὅτι τὸ ὂν δύναται γένος εἶναι.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_DAZY_BOO3:P0013","trace_type":"Tension","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"},{"id":"RAW_DAZY_BOO3:P0021","trace_type":"Tension","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_DAZY_BOO3:MRT6","text":"Ἡ συναγωγὴ ὅτι φθαρτὰ καὶ ἄφθαρτα ἑτέρας ἀρχὰς ἔχει ἰσχυρότερον συμπεραίνει ἢ αἱ προκείμεναι ἀναγκάζουσιν, ἐπειδὴ αἱ αὐταὶ ἀρχαὶ ἴσως δι᾽ ἑτέρας σχέσεις ἢ διαθέσεις ἑτέρας ἐκβάσεις ἂν παρέχοιεν.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_DAZY_BOO3:P0029","trace_type":"Tension","confidence":"medium","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"},{"id":"RAW_DAZY_BOO3:P0030","trace_type":"Tension","confidence":"medium","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"},{"id":"RAW_DAZY_BOO3:P0041","trace_type":"Tension","confidence":"medium","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_DAZY_BOO3:MRT7","text":"Αἱ εἰκόνες τοῦ δεσμοῦ καὶ τῆς ὁδοῦ καὶ ἡ ἀπόρριψις τῶν θεολόγων ῥητορικῶς ἐνισχύουσι τὴν μέθοδον, οὐ γεννῶσι δὲ τὰ κύρια οντολογικὰ συμπεράσματα.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[]},{"id":"CH_DAZY_BOO3:MRT8","text":"Ἡ κριτικὴ τῶν εἰδῶν ἀποκλείει μάλιστα τὰ εἴδη ὡς ἀΐδια ἀντίγραφα τῶν αἰσθητῶν, οὐ πᾶσαν δυνατὴν θεωρίαν νοητῆς οὐσίας.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_DAZY_BOO3:P0014","trace_type":"Negative","confidence":"medium","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"},{"id":"RAW_DAZY_BOO3:P0015","trace_type":"Negative","confidence":"medium","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_DAZY_BOO3:MRT9","text":"Ἡ ἀνασύστασις σῴζει τὴν ἀληθῆ κίνησιν τοῦ λόγου, ἐπειδὴ ἀπὸ τῆς ἀνάγκης τοῦ διαπορῆσαι διὰ τῶν ἀντιρρόπων ἀδυνάτων εἰς τοὺς ὅρους μελλούσης λύσεως προάγει.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_DAZY_BOO3:P0001","trace_type":"Structural","confidence":"medium","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"},{"id":"RAW_DAZY_BOO3:P0014","trace_type":"Structural","confidence":"medium","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"},{"id":"RAW_DAZY_BOO3:P0045","trace_type":"Structural","confidence":"medium","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"},{"id":"RAW_DAZY_BOO3:P0058","trace_type":"Structural","confidence":"medium","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]}],"original_name":"Minimal Reconstruction Test"}],"language_code":"grc","language_name":"Ἑλληνική","translations":[{"id":"CAREVAR_20261015T174450512006Z_769080ef","language_code":"en","language_name":"English","title":"Book 3","source_sha256":"e28313d937663e274ee3a016cde013aed0c1d1eb6015f16521ae69a688b933ef","sha256":"0d9a180cf4dda704e39c86e8314873af971363f61b15bba6b6f2804109e80ac2","rendition_type":"machine","review_status":"machine","model":"gpt-5.6-terra","created":"2026-10-15T17:44:50.512980+00:00","sections":[{"number":"1","name":"Argumental Spine","items":[{"id":"CH_DAZY_BOO3:AS1","text":"The principal aporia is how the sought first science can know the principles and substance, when the unity of its object, the nature of the principles, and the mode of knowledge are disputed.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_DAZY_BOO3:P0002","trace_type":"Composite","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"},{"id":"RAW_DAZY_BOO3:P0005","trace_type":"Composite","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_DAZY_BOO3:AS2","text":"The principal thesis is that there is no solution without prior and complete aporia, since a solution is an untying of a known knot.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_DAZY_BOO3:P0001","trace_type":"Direct","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_DAZY_BOO3:AS3","text":"The discourse does not immediately set forth solutions, but reveals the terms which every possible solution must preserve.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_DAZY_BOO3:P0001","trace_type":"Structural","confidence":"medium","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_DAZY_BOO3:AS4","text":"The reasoning is aporetic and dialectical, since it tests each position through its necessary consequences and opposing arguments.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_DAZY_BOO3:P0012","trace_type":"Structural","confidence":"medium","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"},{"id":"RAW_DAZY_BOO3:P0014","trace_type":"Structural","confidence":"medium","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"},{"id":"RAW_DAZY_BOO3:P0045","trace_type":"Structural","confidence":"medium","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_DAZY_BOO3:AS5","text":"The movement proceeds from received opinions and overlooked questions to dichotomies, and from these to impossible or irrational consequences, and finally to a more precise knot.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_DAZY_BOO3:P0001","trace_type":"Structural","confidence":"medium","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"},{"id":"RAW_DAZY_BOO3:P0014","trace_type":"Structural","confidence":"medium","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"},{"id":"RAW_DAZY_BOO3:P0045","trace_type":"Structural","confidence":"medium","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_DAZY_BOO3:AS6","text":"The whole aporia brings together the oppositions of universal and particular, one and many, sensible and separate, substance and predicate, perishable and imperishable.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_DAZY_BOO3:P0002","trace_type":"Structural","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"},{"id":"RAW_DAZY_BOO3:P0003","trace_type":"Structural","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"},{"id":"RAW_DAZY_BOO3:P0005","trace_type":"Structural","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"},{"id":"RAW_DAZY_BOO3:P0006","trace_type":"Structural","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]}],"original_name":"Argumental Spine"},{"number":"2","name":"Load-Bearing Axioms","items":[{"id":"CH_DAZY_BOO3:A1","text":"The subsequent resolution is a solution of the matters previously in aporia, and it is not possible to untie without knowing the knot.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_DAZY_BOO3:P0001","trace_type":"Direct","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_DAZY_BOO3:A2","text":"One who has heard all the disputing arguments is better able to judge.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_DAZY_BOO3:P0002","trace_type":"Direct","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_DAZY_BOO3:A3","text":"Things are known insofar as something one and the same and universal belongs to them.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_DAZY_BOO3:P0025","trace_type":"Direct","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_DAZY_BOO3:A4","text":"One science studies one underlying genus and the attributes belonging to it in itself.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_DAZY_BOO3:P0013","trace_type":"Direct","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_DAZY_BOO3:A5","text":"Every demonstration is from certain things, about something, and of certain things, so that demonstration concerning all things is impossible.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_DAZY_BOO3:P0012","trace_type":"Direct","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_DAZY_BOO3:A6","text":"There is no demonstration of what a thing is in the manner in which attributes are demonstrated.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_DAZY_BOO3:P0014","trace_type":"Direct","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_DAZY_BOO3:A7","text":"Substance signifies a this-something, whereas what is commonly predicated signifies a such-like.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_DAZY_BOO3:P0058","trace_type":"Direct","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_DAZY_BOO3:A8","text":"Attributes, motions, relatives, and dispositions are said of a subject and do not signify a this-something.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_DAZY_BOO3:P0052","trace_type":"Direct","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_DAZY_BOO3:A9","text":"The genera of causes are neither contrary to one another nor do they all belong to all beings.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_DAZY_BOO3:P0007","trace_type":"Direct","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_DAZY_BOO3:A10","text":"A principle or cause must differ from, and be causally responsible for, what is attributed to it.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_DAZY_BOO3:P0024","trace_type":"Direct","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_DAZY_BOO3:A11","text":"All sensible things are in motion and subject to destruction.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_DAZY_BOO3:P0026","trace_type":"Direct","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_DAZY_BOO3:A12","text":"Generation occurs neither from non-being nor proceeds to infinity, but presupposes something ungenerated and a limit.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_DAZY_BOO3:P0026","trace_type":"Direct","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_DAZY_BOO3:A13","text":"Being and one cannot be genera, since the differentiae of a genus must both be and each be one.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_DAZY_BOO3:P0021","trace_type":"Direct","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_DAZY_BOO3:A14","text":"Definition through genera and the account from inherent elements provide two different accounts of substance.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_DAZY_BOO3:P0021","trace_type":"Direct","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_DAZY_BOO3:A15","text":"Numerical unity of principles eliminates the plurality of things from them, whereas specific unity does not determine their number.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_DAZY_BOO3:P0028","trace_type":"Direct","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_DAZY_BOO3:A16","text":"Points, lines, surfaces, and the now are limits or divisions, not substances subject to generation and destruction.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_DAZY_BOO3:P0054","trace_type":"Composite","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"},{"id":"RAW_DAZY_BOO3:P0055","trace_type":"Composite","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_DAZY_BOO3:A17","text":"What is potentially being can fail to be actually.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_DAZY_BOO3:P0057","trace_type":"Direct","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_DAZY_BOO3:A18","text":"Every proposed principle is judged from its necessary consequences and from its capacity to cause the phenomena.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_DAZY_BOO3:P0007","trace_type":"Structural","confidence":"medium","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"},{"id":"RAW_DAZY_BOO3:P0012","trace_type":"Structural","confidence":"medium","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"},{"id":"RAW_DAZY_BOO3:P0045","trace_type":"Structural","confidence":"medium","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]}],"original_name":"Load-Bearing Axioms"},{"number":"3","name":"Apophatic Structure","items":[{"id":"CH_DAZY_BOO3:NX1","text":"Inquiry without prior aporia has neither a manifest end nor a criterion for having found.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_DAZY_BOO3:P0001","trace_type":"Negative","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_DAZY_BOO3:NX2","text":"The unity of the common name “cause” is not sufficient for constituting one science of all causes.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_DAZY_BOO3:P0007","trace_type":"Direct","confidence":"medium","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_DAZY_BOO3:NX3","text":"The common demonstrative axioms belong neither specifically to geometry nor to any other particular science.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_DAZY_BOO3:P0011","trace_type":"Negative","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_DAZY_BOO3:NX4","text":"The axioms cannot all be known through demonstration, since every demonstration employs prior things.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_DAZY_BOO3:P0012","trace_type":"Negative","confidence":"medium","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_DAZY_BOO3:NX5","text":"Substance is not exhausted in accidents, nor is what a thing is demonstrated as an attribute.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_DAZY_BOO3:P0014","trace_type":"Composite","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"},{"id":"RAW_DAZY_BOO3:P0052","trace_type":"Composite","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_DAZY_BOO3:NX6","text":"Sensible substances alone are not sufficient to ground science, eternity, and generation.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_DAZY_BOO3:P0025","trace_type":"Composite","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"},{"id":"RAW_DAZY_BOO3:P0026","trace_type":"Composite","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_DAZY_BOO3:NX7","text":"Forms are not adequately explained as sensible things merely made eternal.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_DAZY_BOO3:P0014","trace_type":"Composite","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"},{"id":"RAW_DAZY_BOO3:P0015","trace_type":"Composite","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_DAZY_BOO3:NX8","text":"Intermediate mathematical objects are neither readily posited separately from sensible things nor in them without duplication and local impossibility.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_DAZY_BOO3:P0015","trace_type":"Composite","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"},{"id":"RAW_DAZY_BOO3:P0018","trace_type":"Composite","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_DAZY_BOO3:NX9","text":"Genera and inherent elements cannot in the same way and in the same respect be principles of substance.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_DAZY_BOO3:P0020","trace_type":"Composite","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"},{"id":"RAW_DAZY_BOO3:P0021","trace_type":"Composite","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_DAZY_BOO3:NX10","text":"Being and one cannot be predicated as first genera.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_DAZY_BOO3:P0021","trace_type":"Negative","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_DAZY_BOO3:NX11","text":"The mythical account concerning nectar and ambrosia does not explain the difference between mortal and immortal.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_DAZY_BOO3:P0029","trace_type":"Negative","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_DAZY_BOO3:NX12","text":"The same principles of perishable and imperishable things are not sufficient if no differentiating cause is assigned.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_DAZY_BOO3:P0029","trace_type":"Composite","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"},{"id":"RAW_DAZY_BOO3:P0030","trace_type":"Composite","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_DAZY_BOO3:NX13","text":"Strife is not solely a cause of destruction, nor is Love solely a cause of being, since each both generates and destroys according to a different composition.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_DAZY_BOO3:P0041","trace_type":"Negative","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_DAZY_BOO3:NX14","text":"Points, lines, and surfaces do not become substances merely because bodies are delimited through them.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_DAZY_BOO3:P0054","trace_type":"Negative","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"},{"id":"RAW_DAZY_BOO3:P0055","trace_type":"Negative","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]}],"original_name":"Apophatic Structure"},{"number":"4","name":"Derivative Propositions","items":[{"id":"CH_DAZY_BOO3:D1","text":"Complete prior aporia makes manifest both the sought knot and the criterion of the future solution.","refs":["CH_DAZY_BOO3:A1","CH_DAZY_BOO3:A2"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"From","passages":[{"id":"RAW_DAZY_BOO3:P0001","trace_type":"Direct","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_DAZY_BOO3:D2","text":"The unity of the science concerning causes does not follow from common appellation alone, since the kinds of causes neither readily fall under one genus nor belong to all things.","refs":["CH_DAZY_BOO3:A4","CH_DAZY_BOO3:A9"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"From","passages":[{"id":"RAW_DAZY_BOO3:P0007","trace_type":"Direct","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_DAZY_BOO3:D3","text":"If the common axioms were demonstrable, one underlying genus of all demonstrated things would have to be posited; but this eliminates their universality.","refs":["CH_DAZY_BOO3:A4","CH_DAZY_BOO3:A5"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"From","passages":[{"id":"RAW_DAZY_BOO3:P0011","trace_type":"Composite","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"},{"id":"RAW_DAZY_BOO3:P0012","trace_type":"Composite","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_DAZY_BOO3:D4","text":"The same theory does not readily account for both substance and its attributes in themselves, since the latter are demonstrated, whereas there is no similar demonstration of what a thing is.","refs":["CH_DAZY_BOO3:A4","CH_DAZY_BOO3:A6","CH_DAZY_BOO3:A8"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"From","passages":[{"id":"RAW_DAZY_BOO3:P0013","trace_type":"Composite","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"},{"id":"RAW_DAZY_BOO3:P0014","trace_type":"Composite","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_DAZY_BOO3:D5","text":"If nothing exists besides particular sensible things, neither universal science nor anything eternal or unmoved remains.","refs":["CH_DAZY_BOO3:A3","CH_DAZY_BOO3:A11"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"From","passages":[{"id":"RAW_DAZY_BOO3:P0025","trace_type":"Composite","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"},{"id":"RAW_DAZY_BOO3:P0026","trace_type":"Composite","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_DAZY_BOO3:D6","text":"Principles, if universal, are not substances; but if they are particulars, they are not universally knowable.","refs":["CH_DAZY_BOO3:A3","CH_DAZY_BOO3:A7"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"From","passages":[{"id":"RAW_DAZY_BOO3:P0058","trace_type":"Direct","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_DAZY_BOO3:D7","text":"If one and being are separate substances, the plurality of beings becomes aporetic; but if they are not substances, the separate nature of number lacks a foundation.","refs":["CH_DAZY_BOO3:A7","CH_DAZY_BOO3:A13","CH_DAZY_BOO3:A15"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"From","passages":[{"id":"RAW_DAZY_BOO3:P0048","trace_type":"Direct","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_DAZY_BOO3:D8","text":"Genera and inherent elements do not provide the same account of substance, so that their simultaneous status as principles generates a double definition.","refs":["CH_DAZY_BOO3:A6","CH_DAZY_BOO3:A14"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"From","passages":[{"id":"RAW_DAZY_BOO3:P0020","trace_type":"Composite","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"},{"id":"RAW_DAZY_BOO3:P0021","trace_type":"Composite","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_DAZY_BOO3:D9","text":"Mathematical limits appear prior to bodies in definition, but do not possess the self-subsisting generation, destruction, and subsistence of substance.","refs":["CH_DAZY_BOO3:A7","CH_DAZY_BOO3:A16"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"From","passages":[{"id":"RAW_DAZY_BOO3:P0052","trace_type":"Composite","confidence":"medium","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"},{"id":"RAW_DAZY_BOO3:P0054","trace_type":"Composite","confidence":"medium","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"},{"id":"RAW_DAZY_BOO3:P0055","trace_type":"Composite","confidence":"medium","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_DAZY_BOO3:D10","text":"The same principles do not by themselves explain why some things from them perish while others remain imperishable.","refs":["CH_DAZY_BOO3:A10","CH_DAZY_BOO3:A18"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"From","passages":[{"id":"RAW_DAZY_BOO3:P0029","trace_type":"Composite","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"},{"id":"RAW_DAZY_BOO3:P0030","trace_type":"Composite","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_DAZY_BOO3:D11","text":"The addition of separate Forms or intermediates does not solve the aporia if the additions merely duplicate sensible things and provide no proper cause.","refs":["CH_DAZY_BOO3:A10","CH_DAZY_BOO3:A18"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"From","passages":[{"id":"RAW_DAZY_BOO3:P0014","trace_type":"Composite","confidence":"medium","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"},{"id":"RAW_DAZY_BOO3:P0015","trace_type":"Composite","confidence":"medium","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"},{"id":"RAW_DAZY_BOO3:P0016","trace_type":"Composite","confidence":"medium","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_DAZY_BOO3:D12","text":"If elements are only potentially, it is possible that nothing is actually; but if they are in another way, a prior potentiality or cause is sought.","refs":["CH_DAZY_BOO3:A17","CH_DAZY_BOO3:A18"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"From","passages":[{"id":"RAW_DAZY_BOO3:P0057","trace_type":"Direct","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]}],"original_name":"Derivative Propositions"},{"number":"5","name":"Super-Derivatives","items":[{"id":"CH_DAZY_BOO3:SD1","text":"The universal science of being cannot derive its unity from a genus, since being is not a genus; therefore another mode of unifying its object must be sought.","refs":["CH_DAZY_BOO3:A3","CH_DAZY_BOO3:A4","CH_DAZY_BOO3:A13"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"From","passages":[{"id":"RAW_DAZY_BOO3:P0013","trace_type":"Structural","confidence":"medium","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"},{"id":"RAW_DAZY_BOO3:P0021","trace_type":"Structural","confidence":"medium","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_DAZY_BOO3:SD2","text":"The order of knowledge and the order of substance stand in opposition: knowledge requires the universal, whereas substance must be a this-something.","refs":["CH_DAZY_BOO3:A3","CH_DAZY_BOO3:A7","CH_DAZY_BOO3:D6"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"From","passages":[{"id":"RAW_DAZY_BOO3:P0025","trace_type":"Composite","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"},{"id":"RAW_DAZY_BOO3:P0058","trace_type":"Composite","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_DAZY_BOO3:SD3","text":"Separateness does not by itself constitute explanatory priority if the relation of the separate to sensible things and generation remains unexplained.","refs":["CH_DAZY_BOO3:A10","CH_DAZY_BOO3:D11"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"From","passages":[{"id":"RAW_DAZY_BOO3:P0014","trace_type":"Structural","confidence":"medium","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"},{"id":"RAW_DAZY_BOO3:P0018","trace_type":"Structural","confidence":"medium","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_DAZY_BOO3:SD4","text":"Every theory of first principles must account simultaneously for unity, plurality, change, destruction, and eternity.","refs":["CH_DAZY_BOO3:A10","CH_DAZY_BOO3:A11","CH_DAZY_BOO3:A12","CH_DAZY_BOO3:D10"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"From","passages":[{"id":"RAW_DAZY_BOO3:P0026","trace_type":"Structural","confidence":"medium","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"},{"id":"RAW_DAZY_BOO3:P0028","trace_type":"Structural","confidence":"medium","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"},{"id":"RAW_DAZY_BOO3:P0029","trace_type":"Structural","confidence":"medium","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"},{"id":"RAW_DAZY_BOO3:P0045","trace_type":"Structural","confidence":"medium","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_DAZY_BOO3:SD5","text":"Aporia transforms first philosophy from a ready-made doctrine into a system of terms and exclusions which the future solution must preserve.","refs":["CH_DAZY_BOO3:A1","CH_DAZY_BOO3:A18","CH_DAZY_BOO3:D1"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"From","passages":[{"id":"RAW_DAZY_BOO3:P0001","trace_type":"Structural","confidence":"medium","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"},{"id":"RAW_DAZY_BOO3:P0045","trace_type":"Structural","confidence":"medium","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]}],"original_name":"Super-Derivatives"},{"number":"6","name":"Deep Apophatics","items":[{"id":"CH_DAZY_BOO3:DNX1","text":"The universality of knowledge and particular subsistence cannot simply be identified.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_DAZY_BOO3:P0058","trace_type":"Direct","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_DAZY_BOO3:DNX2","text":"Logical priority does not necessarily entail separate substance.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_DAZY_BOO3:P0052","trace_type":"Structural","confidence":"medium","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"},{"id":"RAW_DAZY_BOO3:P0055","trace_type":"Structural","confidence":"medium","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_DAZY_BOO3:DNX3","text":"The multiplication of the genera of being does not solve an aporia if it produces only copies of sensible things.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_DAZY_BOO3:P0014","trace_type":"Negative","confidence":"medium","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"},{"id":"RAW_DAZY_BOO3:P0015","trace_type":"Negative","confidence":"medium","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_DAZY_BOO3:DNX4","text":"Being cannot be reduced to one highest genus without eliminating its differences.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_DAZY_BOO3:P0021","trace_type":"Negative","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_DAZY_BOO3:DNX5","text":"Neither generation from non-being nor an infinite regress of principles is permitted.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_DAZY_BOO3:P0026","trace_type":"Negative","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_DAZY_BOO3:DNX6","text":"One undifferentiated causal order cannot explain contrary outcomes, such as destruction and imperishability.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_DAZY_BOO3:P0029","trace_type":"Composite","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"},{"id":"RAW_DAZY_BOO3:P0030","trace_type":"Composite","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_DAZY_BOO3:DNX7","text":"Mere potentiality does not guarantee that anything will be actual.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_DAZY_BOO3:P0057","trace_type":"Negative","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]}],"original_name":"Deep Apophatics"},{"number":"7","name":"Super-Apophatics","items":[{"id":"CH_DAZY_BOO3:SNX1","text":"The possibility of demonstrating all first axioms is excluded, since demonstration necessarily begins from certain indemonstrables.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_DAZY_BOO3:P0012","trace_type":"Negative","confidence":"medium","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_DAZY_BOO3:SNX2","text":"One undifferentiated nature is excluded from being at once universal and a this-something, one and many, separate and inherent, unmoved and generative.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_DAZY_BOO3:P0004","trace_type":"Structural","confidence":"low","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"},{"id":"RAW_DAZY_BOO3:P0005","trace_type":"Structural","confidence":"low","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"},{"id":"RAW_DAZY_BOO3:P0026","trace_type":"Structural","confidence":"low","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"},{"id":"RAW_DAZY_BOO3:P0058","trace_type":"Structural","confidence":"low","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_DAZY_BOO3:SNX3","text":"The book lacks a final criterion by which one horn of each aporia is simply chosen; this absence makes the future theory necessary.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_DAZY_BOO3:P0001","trace_type":"Structural","confidence":"low","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"},{"id":"RAW_DAZY_BOO3:P0058","trace_type":"Structural","confidence":"low","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_DAZY_BOO3:SNX4","text":"The hypothesis of a second separate world cannot serve in place of a causal account.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_DAZY_BOO3:P0015","trace_type":"Negative","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"},{"id":"RAW_DAZY_BOO3:P0016","trace_type":"Negative","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]}],"original_name":"Super-Apophatics"},{"number":"8","name":"Formal Generative Schema","items":[{"id":"CH_DAZY_BOO3:FGS1","text":"The first stage gathers both received disputes and overlooked questions, so that the whole knot may become manifest.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_DAZY_BOO3:P0001","trace_type":"Direct","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_DAZY_BOO3:FGS2","text":"The second stage recasts each question as an opposition of two horns, such as one or many sciences, universal or particular, separate or inherent.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_DAZY_BOO3:P0002","trace_type":"Composite","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"},{"id":"RAW_DAZY_BOO3:P0005","trace_type":"Composite","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_DAZY_BOO3:FGS3","text":"The third stage connects each horn to the common terms of science, substance, cause, and demonstration.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_DAZY_BOO3:P0007","trace_type":"Structural","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"},{"id":"RAW_DAZY_BOO3:P0012","trace_type":"Structural","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"},{"id":"RAW_DAZY_BOO3:P0014","trace_type":"Structural","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_DAZY_BOO3:FGS4","text":"The fourth stage derives from each horn an impossibility, an irrationality, a negation of what is sought, or an infinite regress.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_DAZY_BOO3:P0014","trace_type":"Structural","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"},{"id":"RAW_DAZY_BOO3:P0022","trace_type":"Structural","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"},{"id":"RAW_DAZY_BOO3:P0045","trace_type":"Structural","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_DAZY_BOO3:FGS5","text":"The fifth stage does not eliminate the question, but distinguishes which term is preserved in each horn and which is lost.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_DAZY_BOO3:P0048","trace_type":"Structural","confidence":"medium","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"},{"id":"RAW_DAZY_BOO3:P0058","trace_type":"Structural","confidence":"medium","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_DAZY_BOO3:FGS6","text":"The sixth stage transforms the failure of the horns into a demand for a new distinction, capable of preserving the truths of each without the impossibilities.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[]},{"id":"CH_DAZY_BOO3:FGS7","text":"The generative sequence in schema is: prior aporia → dichotomy → testing of each horn → irrationality or impossibility → common term → demand for a future solution.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_DAZY_BOO3:P0001","trace_type":"Structural","confidence":"medium","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"},{"id":"RAW_DAZY_BOO3:P0012","trace_type":"Structural","confidence":"medium","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"},{"id":"RAW_DAZY_BOO3:P0045","trace_type":"Structural","confidence":"medium","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]}],"original_name":"Formal Generative Schema"},{"number":"9","name":"Argument Flow Map","items":[{"id":"CH_DAZY_BOO3:AF1","text":"The first movement proceeds thus: unclear object of inquiry → prior aporia → knowledge of the knot → manifest end of inquiry.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_DAZY_BOO3:P0001","trace_type":"Direct","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_DAZY_BOO3:AF2","text":"The doubt concerning the sought science passes into the unity of causes, the common axioms, substances, and attributes in themselves.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_DAZY_BOO3:P0002","trace_type":"Composite","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"},{"id":"RAW_DAZY_BOO3:P0003","trace_type":"Composite","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"},{"id":"RAW_DAZY_BOO3:P0010","trace_type":"Composite","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_DAZY_BOO3:AF3","text":"The doubt concerning substance passes from sensible things to Forms and intermediates, then to genera, elements, and the Form apart from the composite.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_DAZY_BOO3:P0002","trace_type":"Composite","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"},{"id":"RAW_DAZY_BOO3:P0003","trace_type":"Composite","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"},{"id":"RAW_DAZY_BOO3:P0004","trace_type":"Composite","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_DAZY_BOO3:AF4","text":"The investigation concerning principles branches according to numerical or specific unity, same or different, universal or particular, potentiality or actuality.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_DAZY_BOO3:P0005","trace_type":"Composite","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"},{"id":"RAW_DAZY_BOO3:P0029","trace_type":"Composite","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"},{"id":"RAW_DAZY_BOO3:P0057","trace_type":"Composite","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_DAZY_BOO3:AF5","text":"The final movement proceeds thus: opposing impossibilities → unresolved aporia → more precise terms of substance and principle → postponement of the positive solution.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_DAZY_BOO3:P0001","trace_type":"Structural","confidence":"medium","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"},{"id":"RAW_DAZY_BOO3:P0045","trace_type":"Structural","confidence":"medium","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"},{"id":"RAW_DAZY_BOO3:P0058","trace_type":"Structural","confidence":"medium","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]}],"original_name":"Argument Flow Map"},{"number":"10","name":"Map-Effects","items":[{"id":"CH_DAZY_BOO3:ME1","text":"First philosophy ceases to be conceived as an already unified science, and the unity of its object itself becomes a matter of inquiry.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_DAZY_BOO3:P0002","trace_type":"Structural","confidence":"medium","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"},{"id":"RAW_DAZY_BOO3:P0012","trace_type":"Structural","confidence":"medium","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_DAZY_BOO3:ME2","text":"The opposition between sensible and intelligible is displaced, since mathematical intermediates and Forms are judged according to their causal relation to sensible things.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_DAZY_BOO3:P0014","trace_type":"Structural","confidence":"medium","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"},{"id":"RAW_DAZY_BOO3:P0015","trace_type":"Structural","confidence":"medium","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"},{"id":"RAW_DAZY_BOO3:P0018","trace_type":"Structural","confidence":"medium","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_DAZY_BOO3:ME3","text":"The universal and the particular cease to be a mere logical distinction and become an opposition between knowability and substantial subsistence.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_DAZY_BOO3:P0025","trace_type":"Composite","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"},{"id":"RAW_DAZY_BOO3:P0058","trace_type":"Composite","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_DAZY_BOO3:ME4","text":"Principles are mapped across several axes: genus or element, first or last, numerically one or specifically one, the same or different, potentially or actually.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_DAZY_BOO3:P0003","trace_type":"Composite","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"},{"id":"RAW_DAZY_BOO3:P0005","trace_type":"Composite","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"},{"id":"RAW_DAZY_BOO3:P0029","trace_type":"Composite","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"},{"id":"RAW_DAZY_BOO3:P0057","trace_type":"Composite","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_DAZY_BOO3:ME5","text":"Mathematical objects are displaced from the class of self-evidently separate substances into the class of limits and divisions whose subsistence is sought.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_DAZY_BOO3:P0006","trace_type":"Composite","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"},{"id":"RAW_DAZY_BOO3:P0054","trace_type":"Composite","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"},{"id":"RAW_DAZY_BOO3:P0055","trace_type":"Composite","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_DAZY_BOO3:ME6","text":"Generation and destruction become criteria of principles, since theory must explain not only what a thing is but also why some things change while others remain.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_DAZY_BOO3:P0029","trace_type":"Composite","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"},{"id":"RAW_DAZY_BOO3:P0045","trace_type":"Composite","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]}],"original_name":"Map-Effects"},{"number":"11","name":"Altitude Levels","items":[{"id":"CH_DAZY_BOO3:ALT1","text":"At the surface level, the discourse sets forth and develops the aporiai concerning the sought science, substances, and principles.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_DAZY_BOO3:P0002","trace_type":"Composite","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"},{"id":"RAW_DAZY_BOO3:P0003","trace_type":"Composite","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"},{"id":"RAW_DAZY_BOO3:P0005","trace_type":"Composite","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"},{"id":"RAW_DAZY_BOO3:P0006","trace_type":"Composite","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_DAZY_BOO3:ALT2","text":"At the structural level, it examines how the unity of science depends on the underlying genus, axioms, substance, and attributes in themselves.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_DAZY_BOO3:P0012","trace_type":"Composite","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"},{"id":"RAW_DAZY_BOO3:P0013","trace_type":"Composite","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"},{"id":"RAW_DAZY_BOO3:P0014","trace_type":"Composite","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_DAZY_BOO3:ALT3","text":"At the super-strategic level, the discourse prevents the premature choice of a doctrine and transforms each opposition into a test of the terms of future theory.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_DAZY_BOO3:P0001","trace_type":"Structural","confidence":"medium","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"},{"id":"RAW_DAZY_BOO3:P0012","trace_type":"Structural","confidence":"medium","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"},{"id":"RAW_DAZY_BOO3:P0045","trace_type":"Structural","confidence":"medium","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_DAZY_BOO3:ALT4","text":"Concerning being and time, the discourse requires a nature capable of preserving at once the this-something, the universally knowable, plurality, motion, generation, and the eternal.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_DAZY_BOO3:P0025","trace_type":"Structural","confidence":"low","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"},{"id":"RAW_DAZY_BOO3:P0026","trace_type":"Structural","confidence":"low","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"},{"id":"RAW_DAZY_BOO3:P0048","trace_type":"Structural","confidence":"low","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"},{"id":"RAW_DAZY_BOO3:P0058","trace_type":"Structural","confidence":"low","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]}],"original_name":"Altitude Levels"},{"number":"12","name":"Minimal Reconstruction Test","items":[{"id":"CH_DAZY_BOO3:MRT1","text":"The selected axioms are sufficient for reconstructing the aporetic mechanism and its principal oppositions.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[]},{"id":"CH_DAZY_BOO3:MRT2","text":"The positive manner in which the science of being is one remains indeterminate, since being is not a genus.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_DAZY_BOO3:P0012","trace_type":"Tension","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"},{"id":"RAW_DAZY_BOO3:P0021","trace_type":"Tension","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_DAZY_BOO3:MRT3","text":"The arrangement is consistent with itself as aporia, not as a completed theory, since postponement of the solution is part of the method.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_DAZY_BOO3:P0001","trace_type":"Structural","confidence":"medium","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"},{"id":"RAW_DAZY_BOO3:P0058","trace_type":"Structural","confidence":"medium","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_DAZY_BOO3:MRT4","text":"The arrangement is strongest where the universality of knowledge is opposed to the this-something of substance.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_DAZY_BOO3:P0025","trace_type":"Composite","confidence":"medium","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"},{"id":"RAW_DAZY_BOO3:P0058","trace_type":"Composite","confidence":"medium","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_DAZY_BOO3:MRT5","text":"A fissure remains between the requirement of one underlying genus and the denial that being can be a genus.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_DAZY_BOO3:P0013","trace_type":"Tension","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"},{"id":"RAW_DAZY_BOO3:P0021","trace_type":"Tension","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_DAZY_BOO3:MRT6","text":"The inference that perishable and imperishable things have different principles concludes more strongly than the premises compel, since the same principles might perhaps provide different outcomes through different relations or dispositions.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_DAZY_BOO3:P0029","trace_type":"Tension","confidence":"medium","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"},{"id":"RAW_DAZY_BOO3:P0030","trace_type":"Tension","confidence":"medium","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"},{"id":"RAW_DAZY_BOO3:P0041","trace_type":"Tension","confidence":"medium","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_DAZY_BOO3:MRT7","text":"The images of the knot and the road, and the rejection of the theologians, rhetorically reinforce the method but do not generate the principal ontological conclusions.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[]},{"id":"CH_DAZY_BOO3:MRT8","text":"The criticism of Forms excludes especially Forms as eternal copies of sensible things, not every possible theory of intelligible substance.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_DAZY_BOO3:P0014","trace_type":"Negative","confidence":"medium","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"},{"id":"RAW_DAZY_BOO3:P0015","trace_type":"Negative","confidence":"medium","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_DAZY_BOO3:MRT9","text":"The reconstruction preserves the true movement of the discourse, since it advances from the necessity of being in aporia through opposing impossibilities to the terms of a future solution.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_DAZY_BOO3:P0001","trace_type":"Structural","confidence":"medium","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"},{"id":"RAW_DAZY_BOO3:P0014","trace_type":"Structural","confidence":"medium","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"},{"id":"RAW_DAZY_BOO3:P0045","trace_type":"Structural","confidence":"medium","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"},{"id":"RAW_DAZY_BOO3:P0058","trace_type":"Structural","confidence":"medium","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]}],"original_name":"Minimal Reconstruction Test"}],"revision":"CH_DAZY_BOO3@R001","source_variant_id":"canonical:CH_DAZY_BOO3:v1","source_language_code":"grc","original_title":"Book 3"}],"identity":{"layer":"CH","kicker":"Chapter 03","title":"Book 3","context":"Chapter 03","original_title":"Book 3","original_context":"Chapter 03"},"original_title":"Book 3","title_language_code":"en"},{"id":"CH_DAZY_BOO4","layer":"CH","title":"Book 4","order":4,"parents":["RAW_DAZY_BOO4"],"revision":"CH_DAZY_BOO4@R001","sha256":"7e538e0ac77cd2680c523edfa10f174f4524baa454b3bd7c0cb76ea45be8eb44","sections":[{"number":"1","name":"Argumental Spine","items":[{"id":"CH_DAZY_BOO4:AS1","text":"Ζητεῖται πῶς τῶν πολλαχῶς λεγομένων ὄντων μία δύναται ἐπιστήμη εἶναι καὶ τίνος ἕνεκα αὕτη τὰς πρώτας ἀρχὰς ἐπισκοπεῖ.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_DAZY_BOO4:P0001","trace_type":"Composite","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"},{"id":"RAW_DAZY_BOO4:P0002","trace_type":"Composite","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"},{"id":"RAW_DAZY_BOO4:P0003","trace_type":"Composite","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_DAZY_BOO4:AS2","text":"Ἡ ἑνότης τῆς ἐπιστήμης θεμελιοῦται οὐκ ἐπὶ μονοσημάντῳ γένει, ἀλλ᾽ ἐπὶ τῇ πάντων τῶν τοῦ ὄντος σημασιῶν πρὸς μίαν ἀρχὴν ἀναφορᾷ.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_DAZY_BOO4:P0002","trace_type":"Direct","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_DAZY_BOO4:AS3","text":"Ἡ οὐσία τίθεται πρώτη φύσις, ἐξ ἧς τὰ λοιπὰ ὄντα ἤρτηται καὶ δι᾽ ἣν ὄντα λέγεται.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_DAZY_BOO4:P0003","trace_type":"Direct","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_DAZY_BOO4:AS4","text":"Ἡ θεωρία τοῦ ὄντος ἐκτείνεται εἰς τὸ ἕν, τὸ πλῆθος, τὰ ἀντικείμενα, τὰ καθ᾽ αὑτὰ πάθη καὶ τὰ κοινὰ πάσης ἀποδείξεως ἀξιώματα.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_DAZY_BOO4:P0005","trace_type":"Composite","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"},{"id":"RAW_DAZY_BOO4:P0007","trace_type":"Composite","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"},{"id":"RAW_DAZY_BOO4:P0011","trace_type":"Composite","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_DAZY_BOO4:AS5","text":"Ἡ ἀρχὴ τῆς μὴ ἀντιφάσεως καθίσταται ἡ βεβαιοτάτη ἀρχή, ἣν οὐκ ἀποδεικνύει ὁ λόγος ἀλλ᾽ ἐλεγκτικῶς ἀνακτᾶται ἐκ τῆς ἀνάγκης τοῦ σημαίνειν τι.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_DAZY_BOO4:P0013","trace_type":"Composite","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"},{"id":"RAW_DAZY_BOO4:P0015","trace_type":"Composite","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"},{"id":"RAW_DAZY_BOO4:P0016","trace_type":"Composite","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_DAZY_BOO4:AS6","text":"Ὁ συλλογισμὸς μεταφυσικὸς, διαγνωστικὸς καὶ ἐλεγκτικὸς ὢν κινεῖται ἀπὸ τῆς πολλαχῆς λέξεως διὰ τῆς πρὸς ἓν ἀναγωγῆς εἰς μίαν ἐπιστήμην, εἶτα δὲ διὰ τῆς πρώτης ἀρχῆς εἰς ἔλεγχον τῆς ἀντιφάσεως, τοῦ σχετικισμοῦ καὶ τῆς καθολικῆς μεταβολῆς.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_DAZY_BOO4:P0001","trace_type":"Structural","confidence":"medium","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"},{"id":"RAW_DAZY_BOO4:P0002","trace_type":"Structural","confidence":"medium","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"},{"id":"RAW_DAZY_BOO4:P0003","trace_type":"Structural","confidence":"medium","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"},{"id":"RAW_DAZY_BOO4:P0013","trace_type":"Structural","confidence":"medium","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"},{"id":"RAW_DAZY_BOO4:P0016","trace_type":"Structural","confidence":"medium","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"},{"id":"RAW_DAZY_BOO4:P0030","trace_type":"Structural","confidence":"medium","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"},{"id":"RAW_DAZY_BOO4:P0045","trace_type":"Structural","confidence":"medium","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"},{"id":"RAW_DAZY_BOO4:P0060","trace_type":"Structural","confidence":"medium","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]}],"original_name":"Argumental Spine"},{"number":"2","name":"Load-Bearing Axioms","items":[{"id":"CH_DAZY_BOO4:A1","text":"Αἱ ἀκρόταται αἰτίαι τοῦ ὄντος ὑπάρχουσιν αὐτῷ ᾗ ὄντι καὶ οὐ κατὰ συμβεβηκός.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_DAZY_BOO4:P0001","trace_type":"Direct","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_DAZY_BOO4:A2","text":"Τὸ ὂν πολλαχῶς μὲν λέγεται, πᾶσαι δὲ αἱ σημασίαι αὐτοῦ πρὸς μίαν ἀρχὴν ἀναφέρονται.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_DAZY_BOO4:P0002","trace_type":"Direct","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_DAZY_BOO4:A3","text":"Ἡ οὐσία ἐστὶν ἡ πρώτη φύσις πρὸς ἣν τὰ λοιπὰ τοῦ ὄντος λεγόμενα ἀναφέρεται.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_DAZY_BOO4:P0003","trace_type":"Direct","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_DAZY_BOO4:A4","text":"Μία ἐπιστήμη θεωρεῖ καὶ τὰ καθ᾽ ἓν καὶ τὰ πρὸς μίαν φύσιν λεγόμενα.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_DAZY_BOO4:P0003","trace_type":"Direct","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_DAZY_BOO4:A5","text":"Τὸ ὂν καὶ τὸ ἓν μία φύσις ἐστὶ τῷ συνακολουθεῖν, οὐχ εἷς δὲ λόγος τῷ σημαίνειν.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_DAZY_BOO4:P0004","trace_type":"Direct","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_DAZY_BOO4:A6","text":"Μιᾶς ἐπιστήμης ἐστὶ τὸ ἕν, τὸ πλῆθος, ἡ ἀπόφασις, ἡ στέρησις καὶ τὰ ἀντικείμενα αὐτῶν θεωρεῖν.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_DAZY_BOO4:P0005","trace_type":"Composite","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"},{"id":"RAW_DAZY_BOO4:P0006","trace_type":"Composite","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_DAZY_BOO4:A7","text":"Τὰ κοινὰ συλλογιστικὰ ἀξιώματα πᾶσι τοῖς οὖσιν ὑπάρχει ᾗ ὄντα.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_DAZY_BOO4:P0011","trace_type":"Direct","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_DAZY_BOO4:A8","text":"Οὐκ ἐνδέχεται πάντων εἶναι ἀπόδειξιν, ἐπεὶ ἡ ἀπόδειξις εἰς ἄπειρον ἀναχωροῦσα ἀναιρεῖται.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_DAZY_BOO4:P0015","trace_type":"Direct","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_DAZY_BOO4:A9","text":"Ἀδύνατον τὸ αὐτὸ ἅμα ὑπάρχειν τε καὶ μὴ ὑπάρχειν τῷ αὐτῷ καὶ κατὰ τὸ αὐτό.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_DAZY_BOO4:P0013","trace_type":"Direct","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_DAZY_BOO4:A10","text":"Ὁ ἀμφισβητῶν ἐλέγχεται μόνον ἐὰν σημαίνῃ τι αὑτῷ καὶ ἄλλῳ.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_DAZY_BOO4:P0016","trace_type":"Direct","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_DAZY_BOO4:A11","text":"Τὸ σημαίνειν ἀπαιτεῖ μίαν ἢ πεπερασμένας ὡρισμένας σημασίας, ἐπεὶ τὸ ἄπειρα σημαίνειν ταὐτόν ἐστι τῷ μηδὲν σημαίνειν.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_DAZY_BOO4:P0017","trace_type":"Direct","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_DAZY_BOO4:A12","text":"Ἡ οὐσία σημαίνει ὡρισμένον τί ἐστιν, ὃ οὐκ ἔστιν ἅμα τὸ ἐναντίον αὑτοῦ.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_DAZY_BOO4:P0016","trace_type":"Composite","confidence":"medium","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"},{"id":"RAW_DAZY_BOO4:P0019","trace_type":"Composite","confidence":"medium","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"},{"id":"RAW_DAZY_BOO4:P0020","trace_type":"Composite","confidence":"medium","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_DAZY_BOO4:A13","text":"Τὸ συμβεβηκὸς καθ᾽ ὑποκειμένου κατηγορεῖται καὶ οὐκ ἀναβαίνει εἰς ἄπειρον διὰ συμβεβηκότων.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_DAZY_BOO4:P0021","trace_type":"Direct","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_DAZY_BOO4:A14","text":"Τὸ μὲν λέγειν τὸ ὂν εἶναι καὶ τὸ μὴ ὂν μὴ εἶναι ἀληθές, τὸ δὲ ἐναντίως λέγειν ψεῦδός ἐστιν.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_DAZY_BOO4:P0055","trace_type":"Direct","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_DAZY_BOO4:A15","text":"Τὸ φαινόμενον ἀεὶ τινὶ καὶ ὅτε καὶ ᾗ καὶ ὡς φαίνεται.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_DAZY_BOO4:P0052","trace_type":"Direct","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_DAZY_BOO4:A16","text":"Τὸ κινοῦν τὴν αἴσθησιν ὑποκείμενον φύσει πρότερόν ἐστι τῆς αἰσθήσεως.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_DAZY_BOO4:P0050","trace_type":"Direct","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_DAZY_BOO4:A17","text":"Δυνάμει μὲν ἐνδέχεται τὸ αὐτὸ εἶναι τὰ ἐναντία, ἐντελεχείᾳ δὲ καὶ κατὰ ταὐτὸ οὔ.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_DAZY_BOO4:P0032","trace_type":"Direct","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_DAZY_BOO4:A18","text":"Τὸ πρῶτον κινοῦν τὰ κινούμενα ἀκίνητόν ἐστιν.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_DAZY_BOO4:P0060","trace_type":"Direct","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]}],"original_name":"Load-Bearing Axioms"},{"number":"3","name":"Apophatic Structure","items":[{"id":"CH_DAZY_BOO4:NX1","text":"Οὐδεμία τῶν ἐν μέρει ἐπιστημῶν θεωρεῖ καθόλου τὸ ὂν ᾗ ὄν.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_DAZY_BOO4:P0001","trace_type":"Direct","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_DAZY_BOO4:NX2","text":"Τὸ ὂν οὐχ ὁμωνύμως καὶ ἀσυνδέτως λέγεται, οὐδὲ γένος ἓν μονοσήμαντον ἢ χωριστὸν ἐπὶ πάντων ἐστίν.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_DAZY_BOO4:P0002","trace_type":"Composite","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"},{"id":"RAW_DAZY_BOO4:P0010","trace_type":"Composite","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_DAZY_BOO4:NX3","text":"Οὔτε ὁ γεωμέτρης οὔτε ὁ ἀριθμητικὸς κρίνει κυρίως περὶ τῶν κοινῶν ἀξιωμάτων, ἀλλὰ χρῆται αὐτοῖς μέχρι τοῦ οἰκείου γένους.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_DAZY_BOO4:P0011","trace_type":"Direct","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_DAZY_BOO4:NX4","text":"Ἡ διαλεκτικὴ καὶ ἡ σοφιστικὴ οὐ ταὐτὸν τῇ φιλοσοφίᾳ εἰσίν, κἂν περὶ τοῦ αὐτοῦ καθολικοῦ πεδίου στρέφωνται.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_DAZY_BOO4:P0008","trace_type":"Direct","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_DAZY_BOO4:NX5","text":"Ἡ ἀρχὴ τῆς μὴ ἀντιφάσεως οὐ λαμβάνεται δι᾽ ἀποδείξεως ἀπὸ προτέρας ἀρχῆς.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_DAZY_BOO4:P0015","trace_type":"Direct","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_DAZY_BOO4:NX6","text":"Οὐκ ἐνδέχεται τὰς ἀντικειμένας φάσεις ἅμα ἀληθεύειν κατὰ τοῦ αὐτοῦ καὶ κατὰ ταὐτό.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_DAZY_BOO4:P0013","trace_type":"Direct","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_DAZY_BOO4:NX7","text":"Ὄνομα ἄπειρα σημαῖνον οὔτε λόγον οὔτε διάνοιαν ἱκανὴν συνίστησιν.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_DAZY_BOO4:P0017","trace_type":"Direct","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_DAZY_BOO4:NX8","text":"Οὐ πάντα κατὰ συμβεβηκὸς λέγεται, ἐπεὶ ἄνευ πρώτου ὑποκειμένου ἡ κατηγορία εἰς ἄπειρον πίπτει.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_DAZY_BOO4:P0021","trace_type":"Direct","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_DAZY_BOO4:NX9","text":"Ἡ καθολικὴ συναλήθεια τῶν ἀντιφάσεων οὐ διασῴζει διαφορὰν πραγμάτων, ἀλλὰ πάντα ἓν ποιεῖ.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_DAZY_BOO4:P0022","trace_type":"Direct","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_DAZY_BOO4:NX10","text":"Οὐ πᾶν τὸ φαινόμενον ἁπλῶς ἀληθές ἐστιν, οὐδὲ τὸ ἀληθὲς πλήθει τῶν δοξαζόντων κρίνεται.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_DAZY_BOO4:P0033","trace_type":"Composite","confidence":"medium","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"},{"id":"RAW_DAZY_BOO4:P0052","trace_type":"Composite","confidence":"medium","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_DAZY_BOO4:NX11","text":"Τὰ ὄντα οὐκ εἰσὶ τὰ αἰσθητὰ μόνον, οὐδὲ ἡ φρόνησις ταὐτὸν ἁπλῶς τῇ αἰσθήσει.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_DAZY_BOO4:P0033","trace_type":"Composite","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"},{"id":"RAW_DAZY_BOO4:P0045","trace_type":"Composite","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"},{"id":"RAW_DAZY_BOO4:P0047","trace_type":"Composite","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_DAZY_BOO4:NX12","text":"Οὔτε πάντα ἀεὶ κινεῖται οὔτε πάντα ἀεὶ ἠρεμεῖ.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_DAZY_BOO4:P0060","trace_type":"Direct","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_DAZY_BOO4:NX13","text":"Οὐδὲν μεταξὺ καταφάσεως καὶ ἀποφάσεως τῆς αὐτῆς ἀντιφάσεως ἐνδέχεται εἶναι.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_DAZY_BOO4:P0055","trace_type":"Direct","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_DAZY_BOO4:NX14","text":"Οὔτε πάντα ἀληθῆ οὔτε πάντα ψευδῆ δύναται εἶναι, ἐπεὶ ἑκάτερος λόγος ἑαυτὸν ἀναιρεῖ.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_DAZY_BOO4:P0059","trace_type":"Direct","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]}],"original_name":"Apophatic Structure"},{"number":"4","name":"Derivative Propositions","items":[{"id":"CH_DAZY_BOO4:D1","text":"Μία καθόλου ἐπιστήμη θεωρεῖ τὸ ὂν ᾗ ὂν καὶ τὰς πρώτας αἰτίας αὐτοῦ.","refs":["CH_DAZY_BOO4:A1","CH_DAZY_BOO4:A2","CH_DAZY_BOO4:A4"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"From","passages":[{"id":"RAW_DAZY_BOO4:P0001","trace_type":"Direct","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_DAZY_BOO4:D2","text":"Ἡ θεωρία τοῦ ὄντος κυρίως περὶ τὴν οὐσίαν στρέφεται, ἐπειδὴ τὰ λοιπὰ δι᾽ ἐκείνην ὄντα λέγεται.","refs":["CH_DAZY_BOO4:A2","CH_DAZY_BOO4:A3","CH_DAZY_BOO4:A4"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"From","passages":[{"id":"RAW_DAZY_BOO4:P0002","trace_type":"Composite","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"},{"id":"RAW_DAZY_BOO4:P0003","trace_type":"Composite","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_DAZY_BOO4:D3","text":"Ἡ αὐτὴ ἐπιστήμη θεωρεῖ ταὐτόν, ὅμοιον, ἕτερον, ἀνόμοιον, πρότερον, ὕστερον, γένος, εἶδος, ὅλον καὶ μέρος.","refs":["CH_DAZY_BOO4:A5","CH_DAZY_BOO4:A6"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"From","passages":[{"id":"RAW_DAZY_BOO4:P0004","trace_type":"Composite","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"},{"id":"RAW_DAZY_BOO4:P0006","trace_type":"Composite","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"},{"id":"RAW_DAZY_BOO4:P0010","trace_type":"Composite","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_DAZY_BOO4:D4","text":"Ὁ φιλόσοφος κρίνει περὶ τῶν κοινῶν ἀξιωμάτων, καὶ ἡ ἀρχὴ τῆς μὴ ἀντιφάσεως ἐστὶν ἡ βεβαιοτάτη αὐτῶν.","refs":["CH_DAZY_BOO4:A7","CH_DAZY_BOO4:A8","CH_DAZY_BOO4:A9"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"From","passages":[{"id":"RAW_DAZY_BOO4:P0011","trace_type":"Composite","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"},{"id":"RAW_DAZY_BOO4:P0013","trace_type":"Composite","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_DAZY_BOO4:D5","text":"Ἡ πρώτη ἀρχὴ ἐλέγχεται οὐ δι᾽ ἀποδείξεως, ἀλλὰ διὰ τοῦ τὸν ἀρνούμενον ἀναγκάζεσθαι ὡρισμένως σημαίνειν.","refs":["CH_DAZY_BOO4:A8","CH_DAZY_BOO4:A10","CH_DAZY_BOO4:A11"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"From","passages":[{"id":"RAW_DAZY_BOO4:P0015","trace_type":"Composite","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"},{"id":"RAW_DAZY_BOO4:P0016","trace_type":"Composite","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"},{"id":"RAW_DAZY_BOO4:P0017","trace_type":"Composite","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_DAZY_BOO4:D6","text":"Ὁ τὴν ἀντίφασιν συγχωρῶν ἀναιρεῖ τὸ τί ἦν εἶναι, τὴν οὐσίαν καὶ τὴν ὡρισμένην κατηγορίαν.","refs":["CH_DAZY_BOO4:A9","CH_DAZY_BOO4:A11","CH_DAZY_BOO4:A12","CH_DAZY_BOO4:A13"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"From","passages":[{"id":"RAW_DAZY_BOO4:P0017","trace_type":"Composite","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"},{"id":"RAW_DAZY_BOO4:P0020","trace_type":"Composite","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"},{"id":"RAW_DAZY_BOO4:P0021","trace_type":"Composite","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_DAZY_BOO4:D7","text":"Εἰ πᾶσαι αἱ ἀντιφάσεις ἅμα ἀληθεύουσιν, οὐδεμία διαφορὰ πραγμάτων μένει καὶ πάντα γίνεται ἕν.","refs":["CH_DAZY_BOO4:A9","CH_DAZY_BOO4:A12","CH_DAZY_BOO4:A14"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"From","passages":[{"id":"RAW_DAZY_BOO4:P0022","trace_type":"Direct","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_DAZY_BOO4:D8","text":"Καθ᾽ ἑκάστην ἀντίφασιν ἀνάγκη θάτερον μόριον ἀληθὲς εἶναι, καὶ οὐδὲν τρίτον μεταξύ ἐστιν.","refs":["CH_DAZY_BOO4:A11","CH_DAZY_BOO4:A14"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"From","passages":[{"id":"RAW_DAZY_BOO4:P0055","trace_type":"Composite","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"},{"id":"RAW_DAZY_BOO4:P0059","trace_type":"Composite","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_DAZY_BOO4:D9","text":"Ἡ διαφορὰ τῶν φαινομένων δηλοῖ διαφορὰν αἰσθανομένου, χρόνου, τρόπου ἢ αἰσθήσεως, οὐ καθολικὴν ἀοριστίαν τοῦ ὄντος.","refs":["CH_DAZY_BOO4:A15","CH_DAZY_BOO4:A16"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"From","passages":[{"id":"RAW_DAZY_BOO4:P0050","trace_type":"Composite","confidence":"medium","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"},{"id":"RAW_DAZY_BOO4:P0052","trace_type":"Composite","confidence":"medium","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"},{"id":"RAW_DAZY_BOO4:P0053","trace_type":"Composite","confidence":"medium","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_DAZY_BOO4:D10","text":"Ἡ γένεσις καὶ ἡ μεταβολὴ οὐκ ἀπαιτοῦσιν ἐνεργείᾳ συνύπαρξιν ἐναντίων, ἐπειδὴ ἡ συνύπαρξις δύναται δυνάμει εἶναι.","refs":["CH_DAZY_BOO4:A9","CH_DAZY_BOO4:A17"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"From","passages":[{"id":"RAW_DAZY_BOO4:P0032","trace_type":"Direct","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]}],"original_name":"Derivative Propositions"},{"number":"5","name":"Super-Derivatives","items":[{"id":"CH_DAZY_BOO4:SD1","text":"Ἡ πρώτη φιλοσοφία καθόλου ἐστὶν οὐχ ὡς ἐπιστήμη ὑπεργένους μονοσήμαντου, ἀλλ᾽ ὡς θεωρία δικτύου σημασιῶν πρὸς οὐσίαν ἠρτημένων.","refs":["CH_DAZY_BOO4:D1","CH_DAZY_BOO4:D2"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"From","passages":[{"id":"RAW_DAZY_BOO4:P0002","trace_type":"Structural","confidence":"medium","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"},{"id":"RAW_DAZY_BOO4:P0003","trace_type":"Structural","confidence":"medium","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"},{"id":"RAW_DAZY_BOO4:P0010","trace_type":"Structural","confidence":"medium","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_DAZY_BOO4:SD2","text":"Ἡ οὐσία ἅμα ὀντολογικὸν καὶ σημασιολογικὸν θεμέλιόν ἐστιν, ἐπειδὴ ἄνευ ὡρισμένου τί ἐστιν οὔτε κατηγορία οὔτε ἀντίφασις οὔτε λόγος συνίσταται.","refs":["CH_DAZY_BOO4:A3","CH_DAZY_BOO4:A11","CH_DAZY_BOO4:A12","CH_DAZY_BOO4:D6"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"From","passages":[{"id":"RAW_DAZY_BOO4:P0017","trace_type":"Structural","confidence":"medium","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"},{"id":"RAW_DAZY_BOO4:P0020","trace_type":"Structural","confidence":"medium","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"},{"id":"RAW_DAZY_BOO4:P0021","trace_type":"Structural","confidence":"medium","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_DAZY_BOO4:SD3","text":"Πᾶσα καθολικὴ ἄρνησις ἀληθείας ἢ ὁρισμοῦ παρασιτεῖ τῷ ὡρισμένῳ λόγῳ ᾧ χρωμένη τὸν ὡρισμένον λόγον ἀναιρεῖ.","refs":["CH_DAZY_BOO4:A10","CH_DAZY_BOO4:A11","CH_DAZY_BOO4:D7","CH_DAZY_BOO4:D8"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"From","passages":[{"id":"RAW_DAZY_BOO4:P0016","trace_type":"Composite","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"},{"id":"RAW_DAZY_BOO4:P0017","trace_type":"Composite","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"},{"id":"RAW_DAZY_BOO4:P0059","trace_type":"Composite","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_DAZY_BOO4:SD4","text":"Ἡ ἀρχὴ τῆς μὴ ἀντιφάσεως οὐκ ἀναιρεῖ τὴν πολλαπλότητα καὶ τὴν μεταβολήν, ἀλλὰ τοὺς ὅρους ὑφ᾽ οὓς αὗται νοηταὶ γίγνονται παρέχει.","refs":["CH_DAZY_BOO4:A9","CH_DAZY_BOO4:A17","CH_DAZY_BOO4:D10"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"From","passages":[{"id":"RAW_DAZY_BOO4:P0032","trace_type":"Structural","confidence":"medium","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"},{"id":"RAW_DAZY_BOO4:P0054","trace_type":"Structural","confidence":"medium","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_DAZY_BOO4:SD5","text":"Ἡ κρίσις τῆς ἀληθείας ἀπαιτεῖ ἱεράρχησιν αἰσθήσεων, καταστάσεων καὶ κριτῶν κατὰ τὸ οἰκεῖον ἀντικείμενον, οὐκ ἰσοδυναμίαν πασῶν τῶν δοξῶν.","refs":["CH_DAZY_BOO4:A15","CH_DAZY_BOO4:A16","CH_DAZY_BOO4:D9"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"From","passages":[{"id":"RAW_DAZY_BOO4:P0048","trace_type":"Composite","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"},{"id":"RAW_DAZY_BOO4:P0052","trace_type":"Composite","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_DAZY_BOO4:SD6","text":"Αἱ ἀντιφατικαὶ δόξαι γεννῶνται ἐκ συγχύσεως αἰσθήσεως καὶ φαντασίας, δυνάμεως καὶ ἐντελεχείας, τοῦ ἁπλῶς καὶ τοῦ πῇ, καὶ τοῦ αἰσθητοῦ μέρους πρὸς τὸ ὅλον τοῦ ὄντος.","refs":["CH_DAZY_BOO4:D5","CH_DAZY_BOO4:D9","CH_DAZY_BOO4:D10"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"From","passages":[{"id":"RAW_DAZY_BOO4:P0031","trace_type":"Structural","confidence":"medium","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"},{"id":"RAW_DAZY_BOO4:P0032","trace_type":"Structural","confidence":"medium","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"},{"id":"RAW_DAZY_BOO4:P0045","trace_type":"Structural","confidence":"medium","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"},{"id":"RAW_DAZY_BOO4:P0047","trace_type":"Structural","confidence":"medium","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"},{"id":"RAW_DAZY_BOO4:P0054","trace_type":"Structural","confidence":"medium","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_DAZY_BOO4:SD7","text":"Ἡ ἀπόρριψις τῆς καθολικῆς κινήσεως καὶ τῆς καθολικῆς στάσεως περαίνεται εἰς τάξιν μεταβολῆς ἐξαρτωμένην ἀπὸ πρώτου ἀκινήτου κινοῦντος.","refs":["CH_DAZY_BOO4:A1","CH_DAZY_BOO4:A18","CH_DAZY_BOO4:D10"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"From","passages":[{"id":"RAW_DAZY_BOO4:P0059","trace_type":"Composite","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"},{"id":"RAW_DAZY_BOO4:P0060","trace_type":"Composite","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]}],"original_name":"Super-Derivatives"},{"number":"6","name":"Deep Apophatics","items":[{"id":"CH_DAZY_BOO4:DNX1","text":"Τὸ σύστημα σιγῇ ἀποκλείει τὴν παντελῆ διάσπασιν τῶν σημασιῶν τοῦ ὄντος, ἐπεὶ ἄνευ πρὸς ἓν ἀναφορᾶς μία ἐπιστήμη ἀδύνατος γίνεται.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_DAZY_BOO4:P0002","trace_type":"Structural","confidence":"medium","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"},{"id":"RAW_DAZY_BOO4:P0003","trace_type":"Structural","confidence":"medium","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_DAZY_BOO4:DNX2","text":"Ἀποκλείεται ἡ ἀόριστος μεταβολὴ τῶν σημασιῶν, ἐπεὶ λόγος ἄνευ πεπερασμένου σημασιολογικοῦ ὅρου οὐκ ἔστι λόγος.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_DAZY_BOO4:P0017","trace_type":"Direct","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_DAZY_BOO4:DNX3","text":"Ἀποκλείεται κόσμος συγκείμενος μόνον ἐκ συμβεβηκότων, ἐπεὶ τὸ συμβεβηκὸς ὑποκείμενον πρῶτον ἀπαιτεῖ.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_DAZY_BOO4:P0021","trace_type":"Direct","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_DAZY_BOO4:DNX4","text":"Ἀποκλείεται ὁ καθολικὸς σχετικισμός, ἐπεὶ τὸ πρός τι ἀπαιτεῖ ὡρισμένον τι πρὸς ὃ ἀναφέρεται.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_DAZY_BOO4:P0052","trace_type":"Composite","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"},{"id":"RAW_DAZY_BOO4:P0054","trace_type":"Composite","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_DAZY_BOO4:DNX5","text":"Ἀποκλείεται ἡ ταύτισις παντὸς τοῦ ὄντος πρὸς τὸ αἰσθητόν, ἐπεὶ τὸ αἰσθητὸν ὡς πάθος ἐξαρτᾶται ἀπὸ προτέρου κινοῦντος.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_DAZY_BOO4:P0045","trace_type":"Composite","confidence":"medium","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"},{"id":"RAW_DAZY_BOO4:P0049","trace_type":"Composite","confidence":"medium","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"},{"id":"RAW_DAZY_BOO4:P0050","trace_type":"Composite","confidence":"medium","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_DAZY_BOO4:DNX6","text":"Ἀποκλείεται ἡ ἀπαίτησις ἀποδείξεως περὶ πάσης ἀρχῆς, ἐπεὶ αὕτη ἀναιρεῖ τὴν ἀπόδειξιν διὰ τῆς εἰς ἄπειρον ἀναγωγῆς.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_DAZY_BOO4:P0015","trace_type":"Direct","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_DAZY_BOO4:DNX7","text":"Ἀποκλείεται ἡ ἐνεργείᾳ συνύπαρξις ἐναντίων ὡς ἐξήγησις τῆς μεταβολῆς, ἐπεὶ ἡ δύναμις ἱκανὴν παρέχει τὴν διάκρισιν.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_DAZY_BOO4:P0032","trace_type":"Direct","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]}],"original_name":"Deep Apophatics"},{"number":"7","name":"Super-Apophatics","items":[{"id":"CH_DAZY_BOO4:SNX1","text":"Ἡ παντελὴς ἀοριστία ἀδύνατός ἐστιν, ἐπειδὴ καὶ ὁ λέγων τὰ πάντα ἀόριστα εἶναι ὡρισμένως τι σημαίνει.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_DAZY_BOO4:P0016","trace_type":"Composite","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"},{"id":"RAW_DAZY_BOO4:P0017","trace_type":"Composite","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_DAZY_BOO4:SNX2","text":"Ὁ ῥιζικῶς ἀρνούμενος πᾶσαν σημασίαν δύναται μένειν ἀνέλεγκτος μόνον ἐὰν παύσηται λέγων, ὁπότε οὐκέτι ἀντίπαλος λόγος ἀλλ᾽ ἀπουσία λόγου γίνεται.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_DAZY_BOO4:P0015","trace_type":"Composite","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"},{"id":"RAW_DAZY_BOO4:P0016","trace_type":"Composite","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_DAZY_BOO4:SNX3","text":"Οὐκ ἐνδέχεται τάξιν ὄντων εἶναι ἄνευ προτέρου θεμελίου, ἐπεὶ ἡ αἴσθησις ἀπαιτεῖ κινοῦν, τὸ συμβεβηκὸς ὑποκείμενον, ἡ κατηγορία οὐσίαν καὶ ἡ κίνησις ἀκίνητον ἀρχήν.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_DAZY_BOO4:P0021","trace_type":"Composite","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"},{"id":"RAW_DAZY_BOO4:P0050","trace_type":"Composite","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"},{"id":"RAW_DAZY_BOO4:P0060","trace_type":"Composite","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_DAZY_BOO4:SNX4","text":"Ἀλήθεια ἄνευ ἀποκλεισμοῦ τοῦ ψεύδους καὶ διαφορὰ ἄνευ ἀποκλεισμοῦ ταὐτότητος οὐ συνίσταται.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_DAZY_BOO4:P0022","trace_type":"Structural","confidence":"medium","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"},{"id":"RAW_DAZY_BOO4:P0055","trace_type":"Structural","confidence":"medium","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_DAZY_BOO4:SNX5","text":"Μεταβολὴ ἄνευ τινὸς ὑπομένοντος ἢ ὡρισμένων ὅρων ἐξ οὗ καὶ εἰς ὃ μεταβάλλει καταπίπτει εἰς ἀνόριστον μὴ ὄν.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_DAZY_BOO4:P0023","trace_type":"Structural","confidence":"medium","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"},{"id":"RAW_DAZY_BOO4:P0046","trace_type":"Structural","confidence":"medium","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"},{"id":"RAW_DAZY_BOO4:P0055","trace_type":"Structural","confidence":"medium","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]}],"original_name":"Super-Apophatics"},{"number":"8","name":"Formal Generative Schema","items":[{"id":"CH_DAZY_BOO4:FGS1","text":"Ἡ ζήτησις τῶν ἀκροτάτων αἰτίων μεταβαίνει ἀναγκαίως ἀπὸ τὰ κατὰ μέρος ὄντα εἰς τὸ ὂν ᾗ ὄν.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_DAZY_BOO4:P0001","trace_type":"Direct","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_DAZY_BOO4:FGS2","text":"Ἡ πολλαχῆ λέξις τοῦ ὄντος ἀνάγεται πρὸς μίαν φύσιν, ἡ δὲ μία φύσις πρὸς οὐσίαν, ἡ δὲ οὐσία θεμελιοῖ μίαν ἐπιστήμην.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_DAZY_BOO4:P0002","trace_type":"Composite","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"},{"id":"RAW_DAZY_BOO4:P0003","trace_type":"Composite","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_DAZY_BOO4:FGS3","text":"Ἡ συνακολουθία τοῦ ὄντος καὶ τοῦ ἑνὸς ἐπεκτείνει τὴν ἐπιστήμην εἰς πλῆθος, ταὐτότητα, ἑτερότητα, στέρησιν καὶ ἐναντιότητα.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_DAZY_BOO4:P0004","trace_type":"Composite","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"},{"id":"RAW_DAZY_BOO4:P0005","trace_type":"Composite","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"},{"id":"RAW_DAZY_BOO4:P0006","trace_type":"Composite","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_DAZY_BOO4:FGS4","text":"Ἡ καθολικότης τοῦ ὄντος ἀνάγει τὰ κοινὰ ἀξιώματα εἰς τὴν δικαιοδοσίαν τοῦ φιλοσόφου.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_DAZY_BOO4:P0011","trace_type":"Direct","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_DAZY_BOO4:FGS5","text":"Ἡ ἀνάγκη πρώτης καὶ ἀνυποθέτου ἀρχῆς καταλήγει εἰς τὸ ἀδύνατον τῆς ἅμα καταφάσεως καὶ ἀποφάσεως κατὰ ταὐτό.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_DAZY_BOO4:P0013","trace_type":"Direct","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_DAZY_BOO4:FGS6","text":"Ἡ ἀδυναμία ἀποδείξεως τῆς πρώτης ἀρχῆς μετατίθησι τὸν λόγον ἀπὸ τὴν ἀπόδειξιν εἰς τὸν ἔλεγχον τοῦ σημαίνοντος.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_DAZY_BOO4:P0015","trace_type":"Composite","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"},{"id":"RAW_DAZY_BOO4:P0016","trace_type":"Composite","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_DAZY_BOO4:FGS7","text":"Τὸ σημαίνειν ἕν γεννᾷ ὡρισμένον λόγον, ὁ ὡρισμένος λόγος οὐσίαν, καὶ ἡ οὐσία ἀποκλείει τὴν συναλήθειαν τῆς οἰκείας ἀποφάσεως.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_DAZY_BOO4:P0017","trace_type":"Structural","confidence":"medium","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"},{"id":"RAW_DAZY_BOO4:P0019","trace_type":"Structural","confidence":"medium","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"},{"id":"RAW_DAZY_BOO4:P0020","trace_type":"Structural","confidence":"medium","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_DAZY_BOO4:FGS8","text":"Ἡ ἄρνησις τῆς πρώτης ἀρχῆς διαλύεται διὰ τῆς ἀναγωγῆς αὐτῆς εἰς ἀσημαντίαν, κατάλυσιν οὐσίας, ταύτισιν πάντων καὶ αὐτοαναίρεσιν.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_DAZY_BOO4:P0017","trace_type":"Structural","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"},{"id":"RAW_DAZY_BOO4:P0020","trace_type":"Structural","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"},{"id":"RAW_DAZY_BOO4:P0022","trace_type":"Structural","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"},{"id":"RAW_DAZY_BOO4:P0025","trace_type":"Structural","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_DAZY_BOO4:FGS9","text":"Ἡ διάγνωσις τῶν πλανῶν ἀποκαθιστᾷ τὰς διακρίσεις δυνάμεως καὶ ἐντελεχείας, αἰσθήσεως καὶ φαντασίας, ἁπλῶς καὶ πῇ, κινήσεως καὶ ἀκινήτου ἀρχῆς.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_DAZY_BOO4:P0032","trace_type":"Structural","confidence":"medium","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"},{"id":"RAW_DAZY_BOO4:P0047","trace_type":"Structural","confidence":"medium","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"},{"id":"RAW_DAZY_BOO4:P0054","trace_type":"Structural","confidence":"medium","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"},{"id":"RAW_DAZY_BOO4:P0060","trace_type":"Structural","confidence":"medium","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]}],"original_name":"Formal Generative Schema"},{"number":"9","name":"Argument Flow Map","items":[{"id":"CH_DAZY_BOO4:AF1","text":"Ἡ κατασκευαστικὴ φορά ἐστίν· ζήτησις πρώτων αἰτίων → τὸ ὂν ᾗ ὄν → πρὸς ἓν ἀναφορὰ → οὐσία → μία ἐπιστήμη → κοινὰ ἀξιώματα → πρώτη ἀρχή.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_DAZY_BOO4:P0001","trace_type":"Structural","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"},{"id":"RAW_DAZY_BOO4:P0002","trace_type":"Structural","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"},{"id":"RAW_DAZY_BOO4:P0003","trace_type":"Structural","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"},{"id":"RAW_DAZY_BOO4:P0011","trace_type":"Structural","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"},{"id":"RAW_DAZY_BOO4:P0013","trace_type":"Structural","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_DAZY_BOO4:AF2","text":"Ἡ ἐλεγκτικὴ φορά ἐστίν· ἄρνησις μὴ ἀντιφάσεως → ἀπαίτησις σημαίνειν τι → ὡρισμένος λόγος → οὐσία → ἀποκλεισμὸς τῆς ἀντιφάσεως → αὐτοαναίρεσις τοῦ ἀρνουμένου.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_DAZY_BOO4:P0015","trace_type":"Structural","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"},{"id":"RAW_DAZY_BOO4:P0016","trace_type":"Structural","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"},{"id":"RAW_DAZY_BOO4:P0017","trace_type":"Structural","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"},{"id":"RAW_DAZY_BOO4:P0020","trace_type":"Structural","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"},{"id":"RAW_DAZY_BOO4:P0025","trace_type":"Structural","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_DAZY_BOO4:AF3","text":"Ἡ διαγνωστικὴ φορά ἐστίν· μεταβολὴ καὶ διαφορὰ φαινομένων → σύγχυσις αἰσθητοῦ καὶ ὄντος → σχετικισμὸς ἢ καθολικὴ ῥοή → διάκρισις πῇ καὶ ἁπλῶς, δυνάμει καὶ ἐνεργείᾳ → ἀποκατάστασις ὡρισμένης ἀληθείας.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_DAZY_BOO4:P0032","trace_type":"Structural","confidence":"medium","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"},{"id":"RAW_DAZY_BOO4:P0033","trace_type":"Structural","confidence":"medium","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"},{"id":"RAW_DAZY_BOO4:P0045","trace_type":"Structural","confidence":"medium","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"},{"id":"RAW_DAZY_BOO4:P0048","trace_type":"Structural","confidence":"medium","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"},{"id":"RAW_DAZY_BOO4:P0054","trace_type":"Structural","confidence":"medium","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]}],"original_name":"Argument Flow Map"},{"number":"10","name":"Map-Effects","items":[{"id":"CH_DAZY_BOO4:ME1","text":"Τὸ ὂν μετατίθεται ἀπὸ ἀσύνδετον πλῆθος σημασιῶν εἰς τεταγμένον πεδίον πρὸς οὐσίαν ἀναφερόμενον.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_DAZY_BOO4:P0002","trace_type":"Composite","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"},{"id":"RAW_DAZY_BOO4:P0003","trace_type":"Composite","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_DAZY_BOO4:ME2","text":"Αἱ ἐπιστῆμαι διακρίνονται εἰς τὰς μέρος τοῦ ὄντος ἀποτεμνομένας καὶ εἰς τὴν πρώτη φιλοσοφίαν τὸ κοινὸν αὐτοῦ θεμελιοῦσαν.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_DAZY_BOO4:P0001","trace_type":"Composite","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"},{"id":"RAW_DAZY_BOO4:P0012","trace_type":"Composite","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_DAZY_BOO4:ME3","text":"Τὸ ἕν, τὸ πλῆθος καὶ τὰ ἐναντία παύεται εἶναι χωριστὰ ζητήματα καὶ γίνεται πάθη τοῦ ὄντος ᾗ ὄν.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_DAZY_BOO4:P0005","trace_type":"Composite","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"},{"id":"RAW_DAZY_BOO4:P0007","trace_type":"Composite","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_DAZY_BOO4:ME4","text":"Τὰ λογικὰ ἀξιώματα μετατίθεται ἀπὸ ψιλὰ ὄργανα συλλογισμοῦ εἰς καθολικὰς ἀρχὰς τοῦ ὄντος καὶ τοῦ νοεῖν.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_DAZY_BOO4:P0011","trace_type":"Structural","confidence":"medium","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"},{"id":"RAW_DAZY_BOO4:P0012","trace_type":"Structural","confidence":"medium","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"},{"id":"RAW_DAZY_BOO4:P0013","trace_type":"Structural","confidence":"medium","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_DAZY_BOO4:ME5","text":"Ὁ ἀντιλέγων μετατίθεται ἀπὸ ἐξωτερικὸν ἀντίπαλον εἰς λόγον ἐξαρτώμενον ἀπὸ τὴν ἀρχὴν ἣν ἀρνεῖται.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_DAZY_BOO4:P0016","trace_type":"Direct","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_DAZY_BOO4:ME6","text":"Τὸ φαινόμενον μετατίθεται ἀπὸ ἀπόλυτον μέτρον ἀληθείας εἰς ὡρισμένην σχέσιν αἰσθανομένου, αἰσθητοῦ, χρόνου καὶ τρόπου.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_DAZY_BOO4:P0052","trace_type":"Direct","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_DAZY_BOO4:ME7","text":"Ἡ μεταβολὴ μετατίθεται ἀπὸ συναλήθειαν ἐναντίων εἰς μετάβασιν τεταγμένην διὰ δυνάμεως, ἐντελεχείας καὶ ὡρισμένων ὅρων.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_DAZY_BOO4:P0032","trace_type":"Composite","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"},{"id":"RAW_DAZY_BOO4:P0046","trace_type":"Composite","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"},{"id":"RAW_DAZY_BOO4:P0055","trace_type":"Composite","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_DAZY_BOO4:ME8","text":"Ἡ ἀλήθεια μετατίθεται ἀπὸ ἰσοδυναμίαν δοξῶν εἰς διάταξιν ἐν ᾗ τὸ ὂν καὶ τὸ μὴ ὂν ὁρίζει τὴν κατάφασιν καὶ τὴν ἀπόφασιν.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_DAZY_BOO4:P0048","trace_type":"Composite","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"},{"id":"RAW_DAZY_BOO4:P0055","trace_type":"Composite","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]}],"original_name":"Map-Effects"},{"number":"11","name":"Altitude Levels","items":[{"id":"CH_DAZY_BOO4:ALT1","text":"Ἐπιφανειακὸς βαθμός· μία ἐπιστήμη θεωρεῖ τὸ ὂν ᾗ ὂν, καὶ ἡ μὴ ἀντίφασις ἐστὶν ἡ βεβαιοτάτη ἀρχή.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_DAZY_BOO4:P0001","trace_type":"Composite","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"},{"id":"RAW_DAZY_BOO4:P0013","trace_type":"Composite","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_DAZY_BOO4:ALT2","text":"Τῆς διατάξεως βαθμός· αἱ πολλαὶ σημασίαι, αἱ ἐπιστῆμαι καὶ αἱ ἀντιθέσεις ἱεραρχοῦνται διὰ τῆς πρὸς οὐσίαν ἀναφορᾶς.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_DAZY_BOO4:P0001","trace_type":"Structural","confidence":"medium","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"},{"id":"RAW_DAZY_BOO4:P0002","trace_type":"Structural","confidence":"medium","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"},{"id":"RAW_DAZY_BOO4:P0003","trace_type":"Structural","confidence":"medium","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"},{"id":"RAW_DAZY_BOO4:P0005","trace_type":"Structural","confidence":"medium","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_DAZY_BOO4:ALT3","text":"Ὑπερστρατηγικὸς βαθμός· ὁ ἀγὼν μετατίθεται ἀπὸ τὴν ἀπόδειξιν τῆς πρώτης ἀρχῆς εἰς τὴν φανέρωσιν τῶν προϋποθέσεων παντὸς ἀντιλέγειν.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_DAZY_BOO4:P0015","trace_type":"Composite","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"},{"id":"RAW_DAZY_BOO4:P0016","trace_type":"Composite","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_DAZY_BOO4:ALT4","text":"Περὶ ὄντος καὶ χρόνου βαθμός· τὸ ὂν προϋποτίθεται ὡρισμένον κατ᾽ οὐσίαν, δεκτικὸν μεταβολῆς κατὰ δύναμιν καὶ ἐντελέχειαν, καὶ τελικῶς ἐξαρτώμενον ἀπὸ ἀκίνητον ἀρχήν.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_DAZY_BOO4:P0017","trace_type":"Structural","confidence":"medium","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"},{"id":"RAW_DAZY_BOO4:P0021","trace_type":"Structural","confidence":"medium","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"},{"id":"RAW_DAZY_BOO4:P0032","trace_type":"Structural","confidence":"medium","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"},{"id":"RAW_DAZY_BOO4:P0060","trace_type":"Structural","confidence":"medium","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]}],"original_name":"Altitude Levels"},{"number":"12","name":"Minimal Reconstruction Test","items":[{"id":"CH_DAZY_BOO4:MRT1","text":"Τὰ ἐξαιρεθέντα ἀξιώματα ἱκανά ἐστι πρὸς ἀνασύστασιν τῆς κινήσεως ἀπὸ τὴν ἐπιστήμην τοῦ ὄντος μέχρι τὸν ἔλεγχον τῶν ἀντιφατικῶν δοξῶν.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[]},{"id":"CH_DAZY_BOO4:MRT2","text":"Ἡ διάταξις ἰσχυροτάτη ἐστὶν ὅπου ἡ πρὸς ἓν ἀναφορὰ συνδέει τὴν πολλαχῆ λέξιν τοῦ ὄντος πρὸς τὴν πρωτείαν τῆς οὐσίας.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[]},{"id":"CH_DAZY_BOO4:MRT3","text":"Ὁ ἐλεγκτικὸς λόγος ἀναγκαῖος γίνεται ἐφ᾽ ὅσον ὁ ἀμφισβητῶν σημαίνει τι, οὐ δύναται δὲ λαβεῖν ὡς ἀντίπαλον τὸν μηδὲν λόγον ὑπομένοντα.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_DAZY_BOO4:P0015","trace_type":"Composite","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"},{"id":"RAW_DAZY_BOO4:P0016","trace_type":"Composite","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_DAZY_BOO4:MRT4","text":"Ἡ ἀναγωγὴ πάντων σχεδὸν τῶν ἐναντίων εἰς ἓν καὶ πλῆθος προϋποτίθεται ἐκ προτέρας ἐκλογῆς καὶ οὐκ ἀναπτύσσεται πλήρως ἐνταῦθα.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_DAZY_BOO4:P0004","trace_type":"Direct","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_DAZY_BOO4:MRT5","text":"Ἡ ἀκίνητος φύσις καὶ τὸ πρῶτον ἀκίνητον κινοῦν εἰσάγεται ὡς ἀναγκαῖον πέρας, ἀλλ᾽ ἡ πλήρης ἀπόδειξις αὐτοῦ ἔξω τῆς παρούσης διατάξεως μένει.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[]},{"id":"CH_DAZY_BOO4:MRT6","text":"Ἡ πρᾶξις τῶν ἀντιλεγόντων φανερώνει ὅτι οὐ βιῶσι κατὰ τὴν καθολικὴν ἀντίφασιν, ἀλλ᾽ αὕτη ἐπικουρεῖ τῷ ἐλέγχῳ καὶ οὐκ ἀντικαθίστησι τὴν ὀντολογικὴν θεμελίωσιν.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_DAZY_BOO4:P0015","trace_type":"Structural","confidence":"medium","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"},{"id":"RAW_DAZY_BOO4:P0020","trace_type":"Structural","confidence":"medium","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"},{"id":"RAW_DAZY_BOO4:P0027","trace_type":"Structural","confidence":"medium","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_DAZY_BOO4:MRT7","text":"Ἡ διάκρισις ὑγιαίνοντος καὶ κάμνοντος, οἰκείας καὶ ἀλλοτρίας αἰσθήσεως, ἐπιστήμονος καὶ ἀγνοοῦντος περιορίζει τὸν σχετικισμόν, ἀλλ᾽ οὐκ ἀνάγεται ἐνταῦθα εἰς ἓν πλήρως ἐκτεθειμένον κριτήριον.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[]},{"id":"CH_DAZY_BOO4:MRT8","text":"Ἡ ἐντὸς συμφωνία τοῦ συστήματος ἐξαρτᾶται ἀπὸ τὴν ἀκριβῆ τήρησιν τοῦ αὐτοῦ, τοῦ ἅμα, τοῦ κατὰ ταὐτό, καὶ τῆς διακρίσεως δυνάμεως καὶ ἐντελεχείας.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_DAZY_BOO4:P0013","trace_type":"Composite","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"},{"id":"RAW_DAZY_BOO4:P0032","trace_type":"Composite","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_DAZY_BOO4:MRT9","text":"Ἡ ἀνασύστασις σῴζει τὴν ἀληθῆ κίνησιν τοῦ λόγου, ἐπειδὴ συνδέει τὴν ἑνότητα τῆς ἐπιστήμης, τὴν πρωτείαν τῆς οὐσίας, τὴν ἀνάγκην τῆς μὴ ἀντιφάσεως καὶ τὴν διάγνωσιν τῶν ἀντιπάλων δοξῶν.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[]}],"original_name":"Minimal Reconstruction Test"}],"language_code":"grc","language_name":"Ἑλληνική","translations":[{"id":"CAREVAR_20261015T174524652451Z_d47469ca","language_code":"en","language_name":"English","title":"Book 4","source_sha256":"7e538e0ac77cd2680c523edfa10f174f4524baa454b3bd7c0cb76ea45be8eb44","sha256":"edd28e84a2016d126c765d5218e3e7806a108e30d25c80c486582100969b980c","rendition_type":"machine","review_status":"machine","model":"gpt-5.6-terra","created":"2026-10-15T17:45:24.654250+00:00","sections":[{"number":"1","name":"Argumental Spine","items":[{"id":"CH_DAZY_BOO4:AS1","text":"It is sought how there can be one science of beings spoken of in many ways, and why this science examines the first principles.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_DAZY_BOO4:P0001","trace_type":"Composite","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"},{"id":"RAW_DAZY_BOO4:P0002","trace_type":"Composite","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"},{"id":"RAW_DAZY_BOO4:P0003","trace_type":"Composite","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_DAZY_BOO4:AS2","text":"The unity of the science is grounded not in a univocal genus, but in the reference of all the meanings of being to one principle.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_DAZY_BOO4:P0002","trace_type":"Direct","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_DAZY_BOO4:AS3","text":"Substance is posited as the primary nature, on which the remaining beings depend and through which they are called beings.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_DAZY_BOO4:P0003","trace_type":"Direct","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_DAZY_BOO4:AS4","text":"The study of being extends to the one, the many, opposites, per se affections, and the axioms common to every demonstration.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_DAZY_BOO4:P0005","trace_type":"Composite","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"},{"id":"RAW_DAZY_BOO4:P0007","trace_type":"Composite","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"},{"id":"RAW_DAZY_BOO4:P0011","trace_type":"Composite","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_DAZY_BOO4:AS5","text":"The principle of non-contradiction is established as the most certain principle, which reason does not demonstrate but recovers refutatively from the necessity of signifying something.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_DAZY_BOO4:P0013","trace_type":"Composite","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"},{"id":"RAW_DAZY_BOO4:P0015","trace_type":"Composite","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"},{"id":"RAW_DAZY_BOO4:P0016","trace_type":"Composite","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_DAZY_BOO4:AS6","text":"The reasoning, being metaphysical, diagnostic, and refutative, proceeds from the manifold expression through reduction to one toward one science, and then through the first principle toward a refutation of contradiction, relativism, and universal change.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_DAZY_BOO4:P0001","trace_type":"Structural","confidence":"medium","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"},{"id":"RAW_DAZY_BOO4:P0002","trace_type":"Structural","confidence":"medium","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"},{"id":"RAW_DAZY_BOO4:P0003","trace_type":"Structural","confidence":"medium","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"},{"id":"RAW_DAZY_BOO4:P0013","trace_type":"Structural","confidence":"medium","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"},{"id":"RAW_DAZY_BOO4:P0016","trace_type":"Structural","confidence":"medium","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"},{"id":"RAW_DAZY_BOO4:P0030","trace_type":"Structural","confidence":"medium","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"},{"id":"RAW_DAZY_BOO4:P0045","trace_type":"Structural","confidence":"medium","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"},{"id":"RAW_DAZY_BOO4:P0060","trace_type":"Structural","confidence":"medium","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]}],"original_name":"Argumental Spine"},{"number":"2","name":"Load-Bearing Axioms","items":[{"id":"CH_DAZY_BOO4:A1","text":"The highest causes of being belong to it insofar as it is being, and not accidentally.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_DAZY_BOO4:P0001","trace_type":"Direct","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_DAZY_BOO4:A2","text":"Being is spoken of in many ways, but all its meanings are referred to one principle.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_DAZY_BOO4:P0002","trace_type":"Direct","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_DAZY_BOO4:A3","text":"Substance is the primary nature to which the remaining things called beings are referred.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_DAZY_BOO4:P0003","trace_type":"Direct","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_DAZY_BOO4:A4","text":"One science studies both things said according to one and things said in relation to one nature.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_DAZY_BOO4:P0003","trace_type":"Direct","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_DAZY_BOO4:A5","text":"Being and one are one nature by concomitance, though not one account in signification.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_DAZY_BOO4:P0004","trace_type":"Direct","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_DAZY_BOO4:A6","text":"It belongs to one science to study the one, the many, negation, privation, and their opposites.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_DAZY_BOO4:P0005","trace_type":"Composite","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"},{"id":"RAW_DAZY_BOO4:P0006","trace_type":"Composite","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_DAZY_BOO4:A7","text":"The common syllogistic axioms belong to all beings insofar as they are beings.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_DAZY_BOO4:P0011","trace_type":"Direct","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_DAZY_BOO4:A8","text":"It is not possible for there to be demonstration of everything, since demonstration, proceeding to infinity, is destroyed.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_DAZY_BOO4:P0015","trace_type":"Direct","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_DAZY_BOO4:A9","text":"It is impossible for the same thing both to belong and not to belong at the same time to the same thing and in the same respect.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_DAZY_BOO4:P0013","trace_type":"Direct","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_DAZY_BOO4:A10","text":"The disputant is refuted only if he signifies something to himself and to another.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_DAZY_BOO4:P0016","trace_type":"Direct","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_DAZY_BOO4:A11","text":"Signifying requires one or finitely many determinate meanings, since signifying infinitely many things is the same as signifying nothing.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_DAZY_BOO4:P0017","trace_type":"Direct","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_DAZY_BOO4:A12","text":"Substance signifies a determinate what-it-is, which is not at the same time its own contrary.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_DAZY_BOO4:P0016","trace_type":"Composite","confidence":"medium","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"},{"id":"RAW_DAZY_BOO4:P0019","trace_type":"Composite","confidence":"medium","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"},{"id":"RAW_DAZY_BOO4:P0020","trace_type":"Composite","confidence":"medium","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_DAZY_BOO4:A13","text":"An accident is predicated of a subject and does not ascend to infinity through accidents.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_DAZY_BOO4:P0021","trace_type":"Direct","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_DAZY_BOO4:A14","text":"To say that what is is, and that what is not is not, is true; to say the contrary is false.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_DAZY_BOO4:P0055","trace_type":"Direct","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_DAZY_BOO4:A15","text":"What appears always appears to someone, at some time, in some respect, and in some manner.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_DAZY_BOO4:P0052","trace_type":"Direct","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_DAZY_BOO4:A16","text":"The underlying thing that moves sensation is by nature prior to sensation.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_DAZY_BOO4:P0050","trace_type":"Direct","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_DAZY_BOO4:A17","text":"Potentially, the same thing can be contraries; but actually and in the same respect, it cannot.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_DAZY_BOO4:P0032","trace_type":"Direct","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_DAZY_BOO4:A18","text":"The first mover of things moved is unmoved.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_DAZY_BOO4:P0060","trace_type":"Direct","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]}],"original_name":"Load-Bearing Axioms"},{"number":"3","name":"Apophatic Structure","items":[{"id":"CH_DAZY_BOO4:NX1","text":"None of the particular sciences studies being universally insofar as it is being.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_DAZY_BOO4:P0001","trace_type":"Direct","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_DAZY_BOO4:NX2","text":"Being is not spoken of homonymously and without connection, nor is there one univocal or separate genus over all things.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_DAZY_BOO4:P0002","trace_type":"Composite","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"},{"id":"RAW_DAZY_BOO4:P0010","trace_type":"Composite","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_DAZY_BOO4:NX3","text":"Neither the geometer nor the arithmetician properly judges concerning the common axioms, but uses them as far as his proper genus extends.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_DAZY_BOO4:P0011","trace_type":"Direct","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_DAZY_BOO4:NX4","text":"Dialectic and sophistry are not the same as philosophy, even if they concern the same universal field.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_DAZY_BOO4:P0008","trace_type":"Direct","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_DAZY_BOO4:NX5","text":"The principle of non-contradiction is not obtained by demonstration from a prior principle.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_DAZY_BOO4:P0015","trace_type":"Direct","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_DAZY_BOO4:NX6","text":"It is not possible for opposed affirmations to be true at the same time of the same thing and in the same respect.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_DAZY_BOO4:P0013","trace_type":"Direct","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_DAZY_BOO4:NX7","text":"A name signifying infinitely many things establishes neither an adequate account nor thought.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_DAZY_BOO4:P0017","trace_type":"Direct","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_DAZY_BOO4:NX8","text":"Not everything is said accidentally, since without a first subject predication falls into infinity.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_DAZY_BOO4:P0021","trace_type":"Direct","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_DAZY_BOO4:NX9","text":"The universal co-truth of contradictions preserves no difference among things, but makes all things one.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_DAZY_BOO4:P0022","trace_type":"Direct","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_DAZY_BOO4:NX10","text":"Not everything that appears is simply true, nor is truth judged by the multitude of those who hold an opinion.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_DAZY_BOO4:P0033","trace_type":"Composite","confidence":"medium","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"},{"id":"RAW_DAZY_BOO4:P0052","trace_type":"Composite","confidence":"medium","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_DAZY_BOO4:NX11","text":"Beings are not merely perceptible things, nor is thought simply identical with sensation.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_DAZY_BOO4:P0033","trace_type":"Composite","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"},{"id":"RAW_DAZY_BOO4:P0045","trace_type":"Composite","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"},{"id":"RAW_DAZY_BOO4:P0047","trace_type":"Composite","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_DAZY_BOO4:NX12","text":"Neither are all things always in motion nor are all things always at rest.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_DAZY_BOO4:P0060","trace_type":"Direct","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_DAZY_BOO4:NX13","text":"Nothing intermediate between affirmation and negation of the same contradiction can exist.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_DAZY_BOO4:P0055","trace_type":"Direct","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_DAZY_BOO4:NX14","text":"Neither can all things be true nor all things false, since either account destroys itself.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_DAZY_BOO4:P0059","trace_type":"Direct","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]}],"original_name":"Apophatic Structure"},{"number":"4","name":"Derivative Propositions","items":[{"id":"CH_DAZY_BOO4:D1","text":"One universal science studies being insofar as it is being and its first causes.","refs":["CH_DAZY_BOO4:A1","CH_DAZY_BOO4:A2","CH_DAZY_BOO4:A4"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"From","passages":[{"id":"RAW_DAZY_BOO4:P0001","trace_type":"Direct","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_DAZY_BOO4:D2","text":"The study of being is principally concerned with substance, since the remaining things are called beings through it.","refs":["CH_DAZY_BOO4:A2","CH_DAZY_BOO4:A3","CH_DAZY_BOO4:A4"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"From","passages":[{"id":"RAW_DAZY_BOO4:P0002","trace_type":"Composite","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"},{"id":"RAW_DAZY_BOO4:P0003","trace_type":"Composite","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_DAZY_BOO4:D3","text":"The same science studies same, like, other, unlike, prior, posterior, genus, species, whole, and part.","refs":["CH_DAZY_BOO4:A5","CH_DAZY_BOO4:A6"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"From","passages":[{"id":"RAW_DAZY_BOO4:P0004","trace_type":"Composite","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"},{"id":"RAW_DAZY_BOO4:P0006","trace_type":"Composite","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"},{"id":"RAW_DAZY_BOO4:P0010","trace_type":"Composite","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_DAZY_BOO4:D4","text":"The philosopher judges concerning the common axioms, and the principle of non-contradiction is the most certain of them.","refs":["CH_DAZY_BOO4:A7","CH_DAZY_BOO4:A8","CH_DAZY_BOO4:A9"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"From","passages":[{"id":"RAW_DAZY_BOO4:P0011","trace_type":"Composite","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"},{"id":"RAW_DAZY_BOO4:P0013","trace_type":"Composite","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_DAZY_BOO4:D5","text":"The first principle is refuted not through demonstration, but by the denier's being compelled to signify determinately.","refs":["CH_DAZY_BOO4:A8","CH_DAZY_BOO4:A10","CH_DAZY_BOO4:A11"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"From","passages":[{"id":"RAW_DAZY_BOO4:P0015","trace_type":"Composite","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"},{"id":"RAW_DAZY_BOO4:P0016","trace_type":"Composite","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"},{"id":"RAW_DAZY_BOO4:P0017","trace_type":"Composite","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_DAZY_BOO4:D6","text":"He who admits contradiction destroys the what-it-was-to-be, substance, and determinate predication.","refs":["CH_DAZY_BOO4:A9","CH_DAZY_BOO4:A11","CH_DAZY_BOO4:A12","CH_DAZY_BOO4:A13"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"From","passages":[{"id":"RAW_DAZY_BOO4:P0017","trace_type":"Composite","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"},{"id":"RAW_DAZY_BOO4:P0020","trace_type":"Composite","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"},{"id":"RAW_DAZY_BOO4:P0021","trace_type":"Composite","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_DAZY_BOO4:D7","text":"If all contradictions are true at the same time, no difference among things remains and all things become one.","refs":["CH_DAZY_BOO4:A9","CH_DAZY_BOO4:A12","CH_DAZY_BOO4:A14"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"From","passages":[{"id":"RAW_DAZY_BOO4:P0022","trace_type":"Direct","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_DAZY_BOO4:D8","text":"For each contradiction, one of the two parts must be true, and there is no third between them.","refs":["CH_DAZY_BOO4:A11","CH_DAZY_BOO4:A14"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"From","passages":[{"id":"RAW_DAZY_BOO4:P0055","trace_type":"Composite","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"},{"id":"RAW_DAZY_BOO4:P0059","trace_type":"Composite","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_DAZY_BOO4:D9","text":"The difference of appearances indicates a difference of perceiver, time, manner, or sense, not a universal indeterminacy of being.","refs":["CH_DAZY_BOO4:A15","CH_DAZY_BOO4:A16"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"From","passages":[{"id":"RAW_DAZY_BOO4:P0050","trace_type":"Composite","confidence":"medium","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"},{"id":"RAW_DAZY_BOO4:P0052","trace_type":"Composite","confidence":"medium","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"},{"id":"RAW_DAZY_BOO4:P0053","trace_type":"Composite","confidence":"medium","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_DAZY_BOO4:D10","text":"Generation and change do not require the actual coexistence of contraries, since coexistence can be potential.","refs":["CH_DAZY_BOO4:A9","CH_DAZY_BOO4:A17"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"From","passages":[{"id":"RAW_DAZY_BOO4:P0032","trace_type":"Direct","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]}],"original_name":"Derivative Propositions"},{"number":"5","name":"Super-Derivatives","items":[{"id":"CH_DAZY_BOO4:SD1","text":"First philosophy is universal not as a science of a univocal highest genus, but as a study of a network of meanings dependent upon substance.","refs":["CH_DAZY_BOO4:D1","CH_DAZY_BOO4:D2"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"From","passages":[{"id":"RAW_DAZY_BOO4:P0002","trace_type":"Structural","confidence":"medium","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"},{"id":"RAW_DAZY_BOO4:P0003","trace_type":"Structural","confidence":"medium","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"},{"id":"RAW_DAZY_BOO4:P0010","trace_type":"Structural","confidence":"medium","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_DAZY_BOO4:SD2","text":"Substance is at once an ontological and semantic foundation, since without a determinate what-it-is neither predication nor contradiction nor account is constituted.","refs":["CH_DAZY_BOO4:A3","CH_DAZY_BOO4:A11","CH_DAZY_BOO4:A12","CH_DAZY_BOO4:D6"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"From","passages":[{"id":"RAW_DAZY_BOO4:P0017","trace_type":"Structural","confidence":"medium","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"},{"id":"RAW_DAZY_BOO4:P0020","trace_type":"Structural","confidence":"medium","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"},{"id":"RAW_DAZY_BOO4:P0021","trace_type":"Structural","confidence":"medium","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_DAZY_BOO4:SD3","text":"Every universal denial of truth or definition parasitically depends upon the determinate account by which it destroys determinate account.","refs":["CH_DAZY_BOO4:A10","CH_DAZY_BOO4:A11","CH_DAZY_BOO4:D7","CH_DAZY_BOO4:D8"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"From","passages":[{"id":"RAW_DAZY_BOO4:P0016","trace_type":"Composite","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"},{"id":"RAW_DAZY_BOO4:P0017","trace_type":"Composite","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"},{"id":"RAW_DAZY_BOO4:P0059","trace_type":"Composite","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_DAZY_BOO4:SD4","text":"The principle of non-contradiction does not destroy plurality and change, but supplies the conditions under which they become intelligible.","refs":["CH_DAZY_BOO4:A9","CH_DAZY_BOO4:A17","CH_DAZY_BOO4:D10"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"From","passages":[{"id":"RAW_DAZY_BOO4:P0032","trace_type":"Structural","confidence":"medium","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"},{"id":"RAW_DAZY_BOO4:P0054","trace_type":"Structural","confidence":"medium","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_DAZY_BOO4:SD5","text":"The judgment of truth requires a hierarchy of sensations, states, and judges according to the proper object, not an equivalence of all opinions.","refs":["CH_DAZY_BOO4:A15","CH_DAZY_BOO4:A16","CH_DAZY_BOO4:D9"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"From","passages":[{"id":"RAW_DAZY_BOO4:P0048","trace_type":"Composite","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"},{"id":"RAW_DAZY_BOO4:P0052","trace_type":"Composite","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_DAZY_BOO4:SD6","text":"Contradictory opinions arise from confusion of sensation and imagination, potentiality and actuality, the unqualified and the qualified, and the perceptible part with the whole of being.","refs":["CH_DAZY_BOO4:D5","CH_DAZY_BOO4:D9","CH_DAZY_BOO4:D10"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"From","passages":[{"id":"RAW_DAZY_BOO4:P0031","trace_type":"Structural","confidence":"medium","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"},{"id":"RAW_DAZY_BOO4:P0032","trace_type":"Structural","confidence":"medium","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"},{"id":"RAW_DAZY_BOO4:P0045","trace_type":"Structural","confidence":"medium","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"},{"id":"RAW_DAZY_BOO4:P0047","trace_type":"Structural","confidence":"medium","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"},{"id":"RAW_DAZY_BOO4:P0054","trace_type":"Structural","confidence":"medium","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_DAZY_BOO4:SD7","text":"The rejection of universal motion and universal rest concludes in an order of change dependent on a first unmoved mover.","refs":["CH_DAZY_BOO4:A1","CH_DAZY_BOO4:A18","CH_DAZY_BOO4:D10"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"From","passages":[{"id":"RAW_DAZY_BOO4:P0059","trace_type":"Composite","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"},{"id":"RAW_DAZY_BOO4:P0060","trace_type":"Composite","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]}],"original_name":"Super-Derivatives"},{"number":"6","name":"Deep Apophatics","items":[{"id":"CH_DAZY_BOO4:DNX1","text":"The system silently excludes the complete disjunction of the meanings of being, since without reference to one, one science becomes impossible.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_DAZY_BOO4:P0002","trace_type":"Structural","confidence":"medium","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"},{"id":"RAW_DAZY_BOO4:P0003","trace_type":"Structural","confidence":"medium","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_DAZY_BOO4:DNX2","text":"The indeterminate alteration of meanings is excluded, since an account without a finite semantic limit is not an account.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_DAZY_BOO4:P0017","trace_type":"Direct","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_DAZY_BOO4:DNX3","text":"A world composed only of accidents is excluded, since an accident requires a first subject.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_DAZY_BOO4:P0021","trace_type":"Direct","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_DAZY_BOO4:DNX4","text":"Universal relativism is excluded, since the relative requires something determinate to which it is referred.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_DAZY_BOO4:P0052","trace_type":"Composite","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"},{"id":"RAW_DAZY_BOO4:P0054","trace_type":"Composite","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_DAZY_BOO4:DNX5","text":"The identification of all being with the perceptible is excluded, since the perceptible, as an affection, depends upon a prior mover.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_DAZY_BOO4:P0045","trace_type":"Composite","confidence":"medium","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"},{"id":"RAW_DAZY_BOO4:P0049","trace_type":"Composite","confidence":"medium","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"},{"id":"RAW_DAZY_BOO4:P0050","trace_type":"Composite","confidence":"medium","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_DAZY_BOO4:DNX6","text":"The demand for demonstration concerning every principle is excluded, since it destroys demonstration through regress to infinity.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_DAZY_BOO4:P0015","trace_type":"Direct","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_DAZY_BOO4:DNX7","text":"The actual coexistence of contraries as an explanation of change is excluded, since potentiality supplies a sufficient distinction.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_DAZY_BOO4:P0032","trace_type":"Direct","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]}],"original_name":"Deep Apophatics"},{"number":"7","name":"Super-Apophatics","items":[{"id":"CH_DAZY_BOO4:SNX1","text":"Complete indeterminacy is impossible, since even he who says that all things are indeterminate signifies something determinately.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_DAZY_BOO4:P0016","trace_type":"Composite","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"},{"id":"RAW_DAZY_BOO4:P0017","trace_type":"Composite","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_DAZY_BOO4:SNX2","text":"He who radically denies every meaning can remain unrefuted only if he ceases speaking, at which point there is no longer an opposing account but an absence of account.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_DAZY_BOO4:P0015","trace_type":"Composite","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"},{"id":"RAW_DAZY_BOO4:P0016","trace_type":"Composite","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_DAZY_BOO4:SNX3","text":"An order of beings cannot exist without a prior foundation, since sensation requires a mover, accident a subject, predication substance, and motion an unmoved principle.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_DAZY_BOO4:P0021","trace_type":"Composite","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"},{"id":"RAW_DAZY_BOO4:P0050","trace_type":"Composite","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"},{"id":"RAW_DAZY_BOO4:P0060","trace_type":"Composite","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_DAZY_BOO4:SNX4","text":"Truth without exclusion of falsity, and difference without exclusion of identity, are not constituted.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_DAZY_BOO4:P0022","trace_type":"Structural","confidence":"medium","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"},{"id":"RAW_DAZY_BOO4:P0055","trace_type":"Structural","confidence":"medium","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_DAZY_BOO4:SNX5","text":"Change without something persisting, or without determinate limits from which and into which it changes, collapses into indeterminate non-being.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_DAZY_BOO4:P0023","trace_type":"Structural","confidence":"medium","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"},{"id":"RAW_DAZY_BOO4:P0046","trace_type":"Structural","confidence":"medium","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"},{"id":"RAW_DAZY_BOO4:P0055","trace_type":"Structural","confidence":"medium","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]}],"original_name":"Super-Apophatics"},{"number":"8","name":"Formal Generative Schema","items":[{"id":"CH_DAZY_BOO4:FGS1","text":"The inquiry into the highest causes necessarily passes from particular beings to being insofar as it is being.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_DAZY_BOO4:P0001","trace_type":"Direct","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_DAZY_BOO4:FGS2","text":"The manifold expression of being is reduced to one nature, the one nature to substance, and substance grounds one science.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_DAZY_BOO4:P0002","trace_type":"Composite","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"},{"id":"RAW_DAZY_BOO4:P0003","trace_type":"Composite","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_DAZY_BOO4:FGS3","text":"The concomitance of being and one extends the science to plurality, identity, otherness, privation, and contrariety.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_DAZY_BOO4:P0004","trace_type":"Composite","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"},{"id":"RAW_DAZY_BOO4:P0005","trace_type":"Composite","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"},{"id":"RAW_DAZY_BOO4:P0006","trace_type":"Composite","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_DAZY_BOO4:FGS4","text":"The universality of being brings the common axioms within the philosopher's jurisdiction.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_DAZY_BOO4:P0011","trace_type":"Direct","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_DAZY_BOO4:FGS5","text":"The necessity of a first and indemonstrable principle concludes in the impossibility of simultaneous affirmation and negation in the same respect.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_DAZY_BOO4:P0013","trace_type":"Direct","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_DAZY_BOO4:FGS6","text":"The impossibility of demonstrating the first principle transfers the account from demonstration to refutation of the one who signifies.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_DAZY_BOO4:P0015","trace_type":"Composite","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"},{"id":"RAW_DAZY_BOO4:P0016","trace_type":"Composite","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_DAZY_BOO4:FGS7","text":"Signifying one thing generates a determinate account, the determinate account substance, and substance excludes the co-truth of its proper negation.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_DAZY_BOO4:P0017","trace_type":"Structural","confidence":"medium","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"},{"id":"RAW_DAZY_BOO4:P0019","trace_type":"Structural","confidence":"medium","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"},{"id":"RAW_DAZY_BOO4:P0020","trace_type":"Structural","confidence":"medium","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_DAZY_BOO4:FGS8","text":"The denial of the first principle is dissolved by reducing it to meaninglessness, destruction of substance, identification of all things, and self-refutation.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_DAZY_BOO4:P0017","trace_type":"Structural","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"},{"id":"RAW_DAZY_BOO4:P0020","trace_type":"Structural","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"},{"id":"RAW_DAZY_BOO4:P0022","trace_type":"Structural","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"},{"id":"RAW_DAZY_BOO4:P0025","trace_type":"Structural","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_DAZY_BOO4:FGS9","text":"The diagnosis of errors restores the distinctions of potentiality and actuality, sensation and imagination, the unqualified and the qualified, motion and an unmoved principle.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_DAZY_BOO4:P0032","trace_type":"Structural","confidence":"medium","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"},{"id":"RAW_DAZY_BOO4:P0047","trace_type":"Structural","confidence":"medium","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"},{"id":"RAW_DAZY_BOO4:P0054","trace_type":"Structural","confidence":"medium","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"},{"id":"RAW_DAZY_BOO4:P0060","trace_type":"Structural","confidence":"medium","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]}],"original_name":"Formal Generative Schema"},{"number":"9","name":"Argument Flow Map","items":[{"id":"CH_DAZY_BOO4:AF1","text":"The constructive direction is: inquiry into first causes → being insofar as it is being → reference to one → substance → one science → common axioms → first principle.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_DAZY_BOO4:P0001","trace_type":"Structural","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"},{"id":"RAW_DAZY_BOO4:P0002","trace_type":"Structural","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"},{"id":"RAW_DAZY_BOO4:P0003","trace_type":"Structural","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"},{"id":"RAW_DAZY_BOO4:P0011","trace_type":"Structural","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"},{"id":"RAW_DAZY_BOO4:P0013","trace_type":"Structural","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_DAZY_BOO4:AF2","text":"The refutative direction is: denial of non-contradiction → requirement to signify something → determinate account → substance → exclusion of contradiction → self-refutation of the denier.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_DAZY_BOO4:P0015","trace_type":"Structural","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"},{"id":"RAW_DAZY_BOO4:P0016","trace_type":"Structural","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"},{"id":"RAW_DAZY_BOO4:P0017","trace_type":"Structural","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"},{"id":"RAW_DAZY_BOO4:P0020","trace_type":"Structural","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"},{"id":"RAW_DAZY_BOO4:P0025","trace_type":"Structural","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_DAZY_BOO4:AF3","text":"The diagnostic direction is: change and difference of appearances → confusion of the perceptible and being → relativism or universal flux → distinction of qualified and unqualified, potentiality and actuality → restoration of determinate truth.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_DAZY_BOO4:P0032","trace_type":"Structural","confidence":"medium","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"},{"id":"RAW_DAZY_BOO4:P0033","trace_type":"Structural","confidence":"medium","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"},{"id":"RAW_DAZY_BOO4:P0045","trace_type":"Structural","confidence":"medium","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"},{"id":"RAW_DAZY_BOO4:P0048","trace_type":"Structural","confidence":"medium","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"},{"id":"RAW_DAZY_BOO4:P0054","trace_type":"Structural","confidence":"medium","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]}],"original_name":"Argument Flow Map"},{"number":"10","name":"Map-Effects","items":[{"id":"CH_DAZY_BOO4:ME1","text":"Being is transformed from an unconnected multiplicity of meanings into an ordered field referred to substance.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_DAZY_BOO4:P0002","trace_type":"Composite","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"},{"id":"RAW_DAZY_BOO4:P0003","trace_type":"Composite","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_DAZY_BOO4:ME2","text":"The sciences are distinguished into those cutting off parts of being and first philosophy, which grounds what is common to it.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_DAZY_BOO4:P0001","trace_type":"Composite","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"},{"id":"RAW_DAZY_BOO4:P0012","trace_type":"Composite","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_DAZY_BOO4:ME3","text":"The one, the many, and contraries cease to be separate inquiries and become affections of being insofar as it is being.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_DAZY_BOO4:P0005","trace_type":"Composite","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"},{"id":"RAW_DAZY_BOO4:P0007","trace_type":"Composite","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_DAZY_BOO4:ME4","text":"Logical axioms are transformed from bare instruments of syllogism into universal principles of being and thinking.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_DAZY_BOO4:P0011","trace_type":"Structural","confidence":"medium","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"},{"id":"RAW_DAZY_BOO4:P0012","trace_type":"Structural","confidence":"medium","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"},{"id":"RAW_DAZY_BOO4:P0013","trace_type":"Structural","confidence":"medium","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_DAZY_BOO4:ME5","text":"The contradictor is transformed from an external opponent into an account dependent on the principle that he denies.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_DAZY_BOO4:P0016","trace_type":"Direct","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_DAZY_BOO4:ME6","text":"What appears is transformed from an absolute measure of truth into a determinate relation of perceiver, perceptible object, time, and manner.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_DAZY_BOO4:P0052","trace_type":"Direct","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_DAZY_BOO4:ME7","text":"Change is transformed from the co-truth of contraries into a transition ordered through potentiality, actuality, and determinate limits.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_DAZY_BOO4:P0032","trace_type":"Composite","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"},{"id":"RAW_DAZY_BOO4:P0046","trace_type":"Composite","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"},{"id":"RAW_DAZY_BOO4:P0055","trace_type":"Composite","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_DAZY_BOO4:ME8","text":"Truth is transformed from an equivalence of opinions into an order in which being and non-being determine affirmation and negation.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_DAZY_BOO4:P0048","trace_type":"Composite","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"},{"id":"RAW_DAZY_BOO4:P0055","trace_type":"Composite","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]}],"original_name":"Map-Effects"},{"number":"11","name":"Altitude Levels","items":[{"id":"CH_DAZY_BOO4:ALT1","text":"Surface level: one science studies being insofar as it is being, and non-contradiction is the most certain principle.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_DAZY_BOO4:P0001","trace_type":"Composite","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"},{"id":"RAW_DAZY_BOO4:P0013","trace_type":"Composite","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_DAZY_BOO4:ALT2","text":"Level of arrangement: the many meanings, sciences, and oppositions are hierarchized through reference to substance.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_DAZY_BOO4:P0001","trace_type":"Structural","confidence":"medium","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"},{"id":"RAW_DAZY_BOO4:P0002","trace_type":"Structural","confidence":"medium","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"},{"id":"RAW_DAZY_BOO4:P0003","trace_type":"Structural","confidence":"medium","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"},{"id":"RAW_DAZY_BOO4:P0005","trace_type":"Structural","confidence":"medium","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_DAZY_BOO4:ALT3","text":"Hyper-strategic level: the contest is transferred from demonstration of the first principle to disclosure of the presuppositions of every act of contradiction.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_DAZY_BOO4:P0015","trace_type":"Composite","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"},{"id":"RAW_DAZY_BOO4:P0016","trace_type":"Composite","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_DAZY_BOO4:ALT4","text":"Level concerning being and time: being is presupposed as determinate in respect of substance, receptive of change in respect of potentiality and actuality, and ultimately dependent upon an unmoved principle.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_DAZY_BOO4:P0017","trace_type":"Structural","confidence":"medium","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"},{"id":"RAW_DAZY_BOO4:P0021","trace_type":"Structural","confidence":"medium","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"},{"id":"RAW_DAZY_BOO4:P0032","trace_type":"Structural","confidence":"medium","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"},{"id":"RAW_DAZY_BOO4:P0060","trace_type":"Structural","confidence":"medium","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]}],"original_name":"Altitude Levels"},{"number":"12","name":"Minimal Reconstruction Test","items":[{"id":"CH_DAZY_BOO4:MRT1","text":"The selected axioms are sufficient to reconstruct the progression from the science of being to the refutation of contradictory opinions.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[]},{"id":"CH_DAZY_BOO4:MRT2","text":"The arrangement is strongest where reference to one connects the manifold expression of being to the primacy of substance.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[]},{"id":"CH_DAZY_BOO4:MRT3","text":"The refutative argument becomes necessary insofar as the disputant signifies something, but it cannot take as an opponent one who maintains no account at all.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_DAZY_BOO4:P0015","trace_type":"Composite","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"},{"id":"RAW_DAZY_BOO4:P0016","trace_type":"Composite","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_DAZY_BOO4:MRT4","text":"The reduction of nearly all contraries to one and many presupposes a prior selection and is not fully developed here.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_DAZY_BOO4:P0004","trace_type":"Direct","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_DAZY_BOO4:MRT5","text":"The unmoved nature and the first unmoved mover are introduced as a necessary limit, but its full demonstration remains outside the present arrangement.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[]},{"id":"CH_DAZY_BOO4:MRT6","text":"The practice of the contradictors reveals that they do not live according to universal contradiction, but this assists the refutation and does not replace the ontological grounding.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_DAZY_BOO4:P0015","trace_type":"Structural","confidence":"medium","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"},{"id":"RAW_DAZY_BOO4:P0020","trace_type":"Structural","confidence":"medium","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"},{"id":"RAW_DAZY_BOO4:P0027","trace_type":"Structural","confidence":"medium","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_DAZY_BOO4:MRT7","text":"The distinction between healthy and sick, proper and alien sensation, knowledgeable and ignorant, limits relativism, but is not here reduced to one fully expounded criterion.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[]},{"id":"CH_DAZY_BOO4:MRT8","text":"The internal coherence of the system depends upon precise observance of the same, the simultaneous, the in-the-same-respect, and the distinction between potentiality and actuality.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_DAZY_BOO4:P0013","trace_type":"Composite","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"},{"id":"RAW_DAZY_BOO4:P0032","trace_type":"Composite","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_DAZY_BOO4:MRT9","text":"The reconstruction preserves the true movement of the argument, because it connects the unity of science, the primacy of substance, the necessity of non-contradiction, and the diagnosis of opposing opinions.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[]}],"original_name":"Minimal Reconstruction Test"}],"revision":"CH_DAZY_BOO4@R001","source_variant_id":"canonical:CH_DAZY_BOO4:v1","source_language_code":"grc","original_title":"Book 4"}],"identity":{"layer":"CH","kicker":"Chapter 04","title":"Book 4","context":"Chapter 04","original_title":"Book 4","original_context":"Chapter 04"},"original_title":"Book 4","title_language_code":"en"},{"id":"CH_DAZY_BOO5","layer":"CH","title":"Book 5","order":5,"parents":["RAW_DAZY_BOO5"],"revision":"CH_DAZY_BOO5@R001","sha256":"2d20a9ab15b6740ed0647f53ab5a143d1f032f166a52eecc336c3838e1e3660c","sections":[{"number":"1","name":"Argumental Spine","items":[{"id":"CH_DAZY_BOO5:AS1","text":"Ζητεῖται πῶς τὰ θεμελιώδη ὀνόματα, πολλαχῶς λεγόμενα, διορισθήσεται ἄνευ συγχύσεως τῶν τρόπων τοῦ εἶναι, τοῦ γίγνεσθαι καὶ τοῦ γιγνώσκεσθαι.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_DAZY_BOO5:P0002","trace_type":"Structural","confidence":"medium","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"},{"id":"RAW_DAZY_BOO5:P0027","trace_type":"Structural","confidence":"medium","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"},{"id":"RAW_DAZY_BOO5:P0028","trace_type":"Structural","confidence":"medium","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"},{"id":"RAW_DAZY_BOO5:P0029","trace_type":"Structural","confidence":"medium","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_DAZY_BOO5:AS2","text":"Οἱ πολλοὶ τρόποι ἑκάστου ὀνόματος εἰς κοινὸν λόγον ἢ πρὸς κυριώτατον σημαινόμενον τάττονται, ὥστε ἡ πολλαχῶς λέξις μὴ ἀτάκτως ὁμώνυμος εἶναι.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_DAZY_BOO5:P0015","trace_type":"Structural","confidence":"medium","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"},{"id":"RAW_DAZY_BOO5:P0045","trace_type":"Structural","confidence":"medium","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_DAZY_BOO5:AS3","text":"Ὁ συλλογισμὸς διαγνωστικός τε καὶ ἀνασυστατικός ἐστιν, ἐπειδὴ τὰς χρήσεις διαιρεῖ, τὸ κοινὸν αὐτῶν ἐξαιρεῖ καὶ τὴν πρώτην σημασίαν ἀποκαθίστησιν.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_DAZY_BOO5:P0015","trace_type":"Structural","confidence":"medium","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"},{"id":"RAW_DAZY_BOO5:P0045","trace_type":"Structural","confidence":"medium","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_DAZY_BOO5:AS4","text":"Τὸ ὂν καὶ τὸ ἓν παρέχει τὸ γενικώτατον πρότυπον, ἐπειδὴ αἱ κατηγορίαι, ἡ δύναμις καὶ ἡ ἐντελέχεια, τὸ καθ᾽ αὑτὸ καὶ τὸ συμβεβηκὸς τοὺς τρόπους αὐτῶν διαιροῦσιν.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_DAZY_BOO5:P0027","trace_type":"Composite","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"},{"id":"RAW_DAZY_BOO5:P0028","trace_type":"Composite","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"},{"id":"RAW_DAZY_BOO5:P0029","trace_type":"Composite","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"},{"id":"RAW_DAZY_BOO5:P0023","trace_type":"Composite","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"},{"id":"RAW_DAZY_BOO5:P0024","trace_type":"Composite","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_DAZY_BOO5:AS5","text":"Ἡ ἀπόδειξις διὰ σταθερῶν τομῶν κινεῖται, οἷον διὰ τοῦ ὑποκειμένου καὶ τοῦ εἴδους, τοῦ αἰτίου καὶ τοῦ τέλους, τῆς δυνάμεως καὶ τῆς ἐνεργείας, τῆς ἑνότητος καὶ τῆς διαιρέσεως.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_DAZY_BOO5:P0003","trace_type":"Structural","confidence":"medium","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"},{"id":"RAW_DAZY_BOO5:P0031","trace_type":"Structural","confidence":"medium","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"},{"id":"RAW_DAZY_BOO5:P0039","trace_type":"Structural","confidence":"medium","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"},{"id":"RAW_DAZY_BOO5:P0025","trace_type":"Structural","confidence":"medium","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_DAZY_BOO5:AS6","text":"Ἡ γεννητικὴ κίνησις ἔχει τόδε τὸ εἶδος· πολλαχῶς λεγόμενον ὄνομα → διαίρεσις τρόπων → κοινὸς ὅρος → κυρία σημασία → συναγωγὴ τῶν συγγενῶν καὶ ἀντικειμένων ἐννοιῶν.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_DAZY_BOO5:P0015","trace_type":"Structural","confidence":"medium","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"},{"id":"RAW_DAZY_BOO5:P0045","trace_type":"Structural","confidence":"medium","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"},{"id":"RAW_DAZY_BOO5:P0035","trace_type":"Structural","confidence":"medium","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]}],"original_name":"Argumental Spine"},{"number":"2","name":"Load-Bearing Axioms","items":[{"id":"CH_DAZY_BOO5:A1","text":"Τὰ θεμελιώδη ὀνόματα τοῦ ὄντος πολλαχῶς λέγεται.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_DAZY_BOO5:P0027","trace_type":"Direct","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"},{"id":"RAW_DAZY_BOO5:P0028","trace_type":"Direct","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"},{"id":"RAW_DAZY_BOO5:P0029","trace_type":"Direct","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_DAZY_BOO5:A2","text":"Ὅπου πολλὰ σημαινόμενα ὑπὸ τὸ αὐτὸ ὄνομα συνάγεται, ἡ τάξις αὐτῶν ἢ κοινῷ λόγῳ ἢ πρὸς κυριώτατον τρόπον ὁρίζεται.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_DAZY_BOO5:P0015","trace_type":"Structural","confidence":"medium","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"},{"id":"RAW_DAZY_BOO5:P0049","trace_type":"Structural","confidence":"medium","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_DAZY_BOO5:A3","text":"Τὸ καθ᾽ αὑτὸ καὶ τὸ κατὰ συμβεβηκὸς διαφόρους τρόπους τοῦ εἶναι καὶ κατηγορεῖσθαι σημαίνει.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_DAZY_BOO5:P0027","trace_type":"Direct","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"},{"id":"RAW_DAZY_BOO5:P0058","trace_type":"Direct","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_DAZY_BOO5:A4","text":"Τὸ πρότερον καὶ τὸ ὕστερον ὁρίζεται πρὸς ἀρχήν τινα, κατὰ φύσιν δὲ πρότερον ἐστὶν ὃ δύναται ἄνευ τοῦ ὑστέρου εἶναι.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_DAZY_BOO5:P0037","trace_type":"Direct","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"},{"id":"RAW_DAZY_BOO5:P0039","trace_type":"Direct","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_DAZY_BOO5:A5","text":"Τὸ καθ᾽ αὑτὸ ὂν τοσαυταχῶς λέγεται ὁσαχῶς τὰ σχήματα τῆς κατηγορίας.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_DAZY_BOO5:P0027","trace_type":"Direct","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"},{"id":"RAW_DAZY_BOO5:P0028","trace_type":"Direct","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_DAZY_BOO5:A6","text":"Τὸ εἶναι σημαίνει καὶ τὸ ἀληθές, τὸ δὲ μὴ εἶναι καὶ τὸ ψεῦδος.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_DAZY_BOO5:P0028","trace_type":"Direct","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_DAZY_BOO5:A7","text":"Τῶν κατηγορουμένων ἕκαστον δύναται λέγεσθαι δυνάμει ἢ ἐντελεχείᾳ.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_DAZY_BOO5:P0029","trace_type":"Direct","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_DAZY_BOO5:A8","text":"Ἡ οὐσία λέγεται κατὰ δύο κυρίους τρόπους, ὡς ἔσχατον ὑποκείμενον καὶ ὡς τόδε τι χωριστὸν διὰ μορφῆς καὶ εἴδους.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_DAZY_BOO5:P0031","trace_type":"Direct","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_DAZY_BOO5:A9","text":"Πᾶν αἴτιον κατ᾽ εἶδος πίπτει εἰς ὕλην, εἶδος, ἀρχὴν κινήσεως ἢ τέλος.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_DAZY_BOO5:P0003","trace_type":"Direct","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_DAZY_BOO5:A10","text":"Τὸ ἓν πρώτως λέγεται καθ᾽ ἀδιαιρεσίαν συνεχείας, εἴδους ἢ λόγου, καὶ γίνεται πρῶτον μέτρον ἑκάστου γένους.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_DAZY_BOO5:P0023","trace_type":"Composite","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"},{"id":"RAW_DAZY_BOO5:P0024","trace_type":"Composite","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_DAZY_BOO5:A11","text":"Τὸ ποσὸν διαιρετόν ἐστιν εἰς ἐνυπάρχοντα ὧν ἕκαστον ἕν τι πέφυκεν εἶναι, πλῆθος μὲν ἐὰν ἀριθμητόν, μέγεθος δὲ ἐὰν μετρητόν.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_DAZY_BOO5:P0046","trace_type":"Direct","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_DAZY_BOO5:A12","text":"Ἡ πρώτη ποιότης διαφορὰ οὐσίας ἐστίν, αἱ δὲ λοιπαὶ ποιότητες πάθη καὶ διαφοραὶ κινήσεως ἢ ἐνεργείας εἰσίν.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_DAZY_BOO5:P0048","trace_type":"Composite","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"},{"id":"RAW_DAZY_BOO5:P0049","trace_type":"Composite","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_DAZY_BOO5:A13","text":"Τὰ πρός τι ἢ τῷ ὅπερ ἐστὶν ἄλλου λέγεσθαι ἢ τῷ ἄλλο πρὸς αὐτὰ λέγεσθαι ὁρίζεται.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_DAZY_BOO5:P0053","trace_type":"Direct","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_DAZY_BOO5:A14","text":"Τὸ πρώτως ἀναγκαῖον οὐκ ἐνδέχεται ἄλλως ἔχειν, οἱ δὲ λοιποὶ τρόποι τῆς ἀνάγκης πρὸς τοῦτον ἀνάγονται.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_DAZY_BOO5:P0017","trace_type":"Direct","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_DAZY_BOO5:A15","text":"Στέρησις ἐστὶ μὴ ἔχειν ὃ πέφυκεν ἔχειν τὸ ὑποκείμενον ἢ τὸ γένος αὐτοῦ, ὅτε, ᾗ καὶ ὡς πέφυκεν ἔχειν.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_DAZY_BOO5:P0063","trace_type":"Direct","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_DAZY_BOO5:A16","text":"Ἡ ἀντίθεσις διαιρεῖται εἰς ἀντίφασιν, ἐναντίωσιν, τὰ πρός τι, καὶ στέρησιν πρὸς ἕξιν.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_DAZY_BOO5:P0035","trace_type":"Direct","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_DAZY_BOO5:A17","text":"Τὸ οὗ ἕνεκα ὡς τέλος ὁρίζει τὴν τελείωσιν, τὸ ἔσχατον καὶ τὸ πέρας τῆς γενέσεως ἢ πράξεως.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_DAZY_BOO5:P0055","trace_type":"Composite","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"},{"id":"RAW_DAZY_BOO5:P0056","trace_type":"Composite","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"},{"id":"RAW_DAZY_BOO5:P0057","trace_type":"Composite","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_DAZY_BOO5:A18","text":"Τὸ συμβεβηκὸς διχῶς λέγεται, ἢ ὡς μὴ ἐξ ἀνάγκης μηδ᾽ ὡς ἐπὶ τὸ πολὺ ὑπάρχον, ἢ ὡς καθ᾽ αὑτὸ μὲν ὑπάρχον ἔξω δὲ τοῦ λόγου τῆς οὐσίας.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_DAZY_BOO5:P0079","trace_type":"Composite","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"},{"id":"RAW_DAZY_BOO5:P0080","trace_type":"Composite","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]}],"original_name":"Load-Bearing Axioms"},{"number":"3","name":"Apophatic Structure","items":[{"id":"CH_DAZY_BOO5:NX1","text":"Τὰ πολλαχῶς λεγόμενα οὐχ ἑνὶ ἀδιαφόρῳ λόγῳ κατὰ πάντων λέγεται.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_DAZY_BOO5:P0077","trace_type":"Negative","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_DAZY_BOO5:NX2","text":"Ἡ κατὰ συμβεβηκὸς ταυτότης οὐ δύναται καθόλου καὶ καθ᾽ αὑτὸ κατηγορεῖσθαι.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_DAZY_BOO5:P0032","trace_type":"Negative","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_DAZY_BOO5:NX3","text":"Τὰ πολλὰ αἴτια τοῦ αὐτοῦ οὐ τὸν αὐτὸν τρόπον αἴτια εἰσίν.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_DAZY_BOO5:P0004","trace_type":"Direct","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_DAZY_BOO5:NX4","text":"Ἡ σύμφυσις καὶ ἡ φύσει συνέχεια οὐ ταὐτὸν τῇ ψιλῇ ἁφῇ ἐστίν.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_DAZY_BOO5:P0010","trace_type":"Negative","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"},{"id":"RAW_DAZY_BOO5:P0021","trace_type":"Negative","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_DAZY_BOO5:NX5","text":"Ἡ κυρίως φύσις οὔτε γένεσις μόνον οὔτε πρώτη ὕλη μόνον ἐστίν.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_DAZY_BOO5:P0015","trace_type":"Negative","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_DAZY_BOO5:NX6","text":"Ἡ ἀνάγκη οὐ ταὐτὸν τῇ βίᾳ ἐστίν, ἀλλ᾽ ἡ βία παράγωγός τις τρόπος τοῦ μὴ ἐνδέχεσθαι ἄλλως ἐστίν.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_DAZY_BOO5:P0016","trace_type":"Composite","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"},{"id":"RAW_DAZY_BOO5:P0017","trace_type":"Composite","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_DAZY_BOO5:NX7","text":"Τὸ λογικῶς δυνατὸν οὐ πάντως κατὰ δύναμιν μεταβλητικὴν λέγεται.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_DAZY_BOO5:P0044","trace_type":"Direct","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_DAZY_BOO5:NX8","text":"Τὰ πρός τι οὐ πάντα ἀντιστρέφει τὸν ὁρισμὸν κατὰ τὸν αὐτὸν τρόπον.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_DAZY_BOO5:P0053","trace_type":"Direct","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_DAZY_BOO5:NX9","text":"Οὐ πᾶσα ἀπουσία στέρησίς ἐστιν, ἀλλὰ μόνον ἡ ἀπουσία τοῦ πεφυκότος ὑπάρχειν ἐν ὡρισμένῳ χρόνῳ καὶ ὑποκειμένῳ.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_DAZY_BOO5:P0063","trace_type":"Negative","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_DAZY_BOO5:NX10","text":"Οὔτε πᾶν συνεχὲς ὅλον οὔτε πᾶν ἔσχατον τέλειόν ἐστιν.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_DAZY_BOO5:P0024","trace_type":"Composite","confidence":"medium","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"},{"id":"RAW_DAZY_BOO5:P0055","trace_type":"Composite","confidence":"medium","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_DAZY_BOO5:NX11","text":"Τὸ ψεῦδος πρᾶγμα οὐ πάντως μηδαμῇ ὄν ἐστιν, ἐπειδὴ δύναται ὑπάρχειν ἄλλως ἢ ὡς φαίνεται.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_DAZY_BOO5:P0076","trace_type":"Direct","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_DAZY_BOO5:NX12","text":"Τὸ κατὰ τύχην συμβεβηκὸς ᾗ συμβεβηκὸς οὐκ ἔχει αἴτιον ὡρισμένον.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_DAZY_BOO5:P0079","trace_type":"Direct","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_DAZY_BOO5:NX13","text":"Ἡ ἀξίωσις ἓν ἐφ᾽ ἑνὸς τῷ οἰκείῳ μόνῳ λόγῳ λέγεσθαι ἀποβάλλεται, ἐπειδὴ ἀδύνατον ποιεῖ τό τε ἀντιλέγειν καὶ τὸ ψεύδεσθαι.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_DAZY_BOO5:P0077","trace_type":"Negative","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]}],"original_name":"Apophatic Structure"},{"number":"4","name":"Derivative Propositions","items":[{"id":"CH_DAZY_BOO5:D1","text":"Ἀρχή ἐστι τὸ πρῶτον ὅθεν ἢ ἔστιν ἢ γίγνεται ἢ γιγνώσκεται τι, ἐνυπάρχον ἢ ἐκτός, διὸ πάντα τὰ αἴτια ἀρχαί εἰσιν.","refs":["CH_DAZY_BOO5:A2","CH_DAZY_BOO5:A4","CH_DAZY_BOO5:A9"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"From","passages":[{"id":"RAW_DAZY_BOO5:P0002","trace_type":"Direct","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_DAZY_BOO5:D2","text":"Τὸ αὐτὸ δύναται πλείονα αἴτια ἔχειν καὶ δύο πράγματα δύναται ἀλλήλων αἴτια εἶναι, ἀλλ᾽ οὐ κατὰ τὸ αὐτὸ εἶδος αἰτίας.","refs":["CH_DAZY_BOO5:A7","CH_DAZY_BOO5:A9","CH_DAZY_BOO5:A17"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"From","passages":[{"id":"RAW_DAZY_BOO5:P0004","trace_type":"Direct","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_DAZY_BOO5:D3","text":"Στοιχεῖόν ἐστι τὸ πρῶτον ἐνυπάρχον καὶ εἰδοποιῶς ἀδιαίρετον, ὃ διὰ τὴν ἁπλότητα ἐν πολλοῖς δύναται ὑπάρχειν.","refs":["CH_DAZY_BOO5:A2","CH_DAZY_BOO5:A4","CH_DAZY_BOO5:A8","CH_DAZY_BOO5:A10"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"From","passages":[{"id":"RAW_DAZY_BOO5:P0008","trace_type":"Composite","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"},{"id":"RAW_DAZY_BOO5:P0009","trace_type":"Composite","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_DAZY_BOO5:D4","text":"Ἡ κυρίως φύσις ἐστὶν ἡ οὐσία τῶν ἐχόντων ἐν αὑτοῖς ἀρχὴν κινήσεως ᾗ αὐτά, ἡ δὲ ὕλη καὶ ἡ γένεσις φύσεις λέγονται πρὸς ταύτην.","refs":["CH_DAZY_BOO5:A2","CH_DAZY_BOO5:A7","CH_DAZY_BOO5:A8","CH_DAZY_BOO5:A9"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"From","passages":[{"id":"RAW_DAZY_BOO5:P0015","trace_type":"Direct","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_DAZY_BOO5:D5","text":"Ἡ ταυτότης ἑνότης τοῦ εἶναι ἐστίν, ἡ ἰσότης ἑνότης ποσοῦ, ἡ ὁμοιότης ἑνότης ποιότητος, ἡ δὲ διαφορὰ κοινόν τι ὑποκείμενον προϋποτίθεται.","refs":["CH_DAZY_BOO5:A10","CH_DAZY_BOO5:A11","CH_DAZY_BOO5:A12","CH_DAZY_BOO5:A13","CH_DAZY_BOO5:A16"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"From","passages":[{"id":"RAW_DAZY_BOO5:P0032","trace_type":"Composite","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"},{"id":"RAW_DAZY_BOO5:P0034","trace_type":"Composite","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"},{"id":"RAW_DAZY_BOO5:P0051","trace_type":"Composite","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_DAZY_BOO5:D6","text":"Τὰ πολλὰ ἀντικειμένως τῷ ἑνὶ λέγεται κατὰ ἀσυνέχειαν, εἰδοποιὸν διαίρεσιν ἢ πλῆθος λόγων, αἱ δὲ ἑνότητες ἀριθμοῦ, εἴδους, γένους καὶ ἀναλογίας ἱεραρχικῶς ἀκολουθοῦσιν.","refs":["CH_DAZY_BOO5:A2","CH_DAZY_BOO5:A10","CH_DAZY_BOO5:A11"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"From","passages":[{"id":"RAW_DAZY_BOO5:P0025","trace_type":"Composite","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"},{"id":"RAW_DAZY_BOO5:P0026","trace_type":"Composite","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_DAZY_BOO5:D7","text":"Τὸ πρότερον μεταβάλλει τὸν τρόπον κατὰ τόπον, χρόνον, κίνησιν, δύναμιν, γνῶσιν καὶ οὐσίαν, ἡ δὲ κατὰ δύναμιν προτεραιότης δύναται τῇ κατ᾽ ἐντελέχειαν ἀντιστρέφεσθαι.","refs":["CH_DAZY_BOO5:A4","CH_DAZY_BOO5:A7","CH_DAZY_BOO5:A8"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"From","passages":[{"id":"RAW_DAZY_BOO5:P0037","trace_type":"Structural","confidence":"medium","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"},{"id":"RAW_DAZY_BOO5:P0038","trace_type":"Structural","confidence":"medium","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"},{"id":"RAW_DAZY_BOO5:P0039","trace_type":"Structural","confidence":"medium","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_DAZY_BOO5:D8","text":"Ἡ ἀπόδειξις ἀναγκαία γίνεται ὅταν τὰ πρῶτα ἐξ ὧν ὁ συλλογισμὸς σύγκειται μὴ ἐνδέχηται ἄλλως ἔχειν.","refs":["CH_DAZY_BOO5:A4","CH_DAZY_BOO5:A14"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"From","passages":[{"id":"RAW_DAZY_BOO5:P0017","trace_type":"Direct","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_DAZY_BOO5:D9","text":"Ἡ διάθεσις τάξις μερῶν ἐστιν, ἡ ἕξις σταθερὰ διάθεσις τοῦ εὖ ἢ κακῶς ἔχειν, τὸ δὲ πάθος ποιότης ἢ ἐνέργεια ἀλλοιώσεως.","refs":["CH_DAZY_BOO5:A7","CH_DAZY_BOO5:A12","CH_DAZY_BOO5:A13","CH_DAZY_BOO5:A16"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"From","passages":[{"id":"RAW_DAZY_BOO5:P0060","trace_type":"Composite","confidence":"medium","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"},{"id":"RAW_DAZY_BOO5:P0061","trace_type":"Composite","confidence":"medium","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"},{"id":"RAW_DAZY_BOO5:P0062","trace_type":"Composite","confidence":"medium","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_DAZY_BOO5:D10","text":"Τὸ ἔχειν σημαίνει ἄγειν, δέχεσθαι, περιέχειν ἢ κωλύειν τὸ κατὰ τὴν οἰκείαν ὁρμὴν κινεῖσθαι, ὥστε σχέσιν δυνάμεως καὶ περιεχομένου δηλοῖ.","refs":["CH_DAZY_BOO5:A7","CH_DAZY_BOO5:A10","CH_DAZY_BOO5:A13"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"From","passages":[{"id":"RAW_DAZY_BOO5:P0065","trace_type":"Direct","confidence":"medium","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_DAZY_BOO5:D11","text":"Τὸ ἔκ τινος εἶναι διαιρεῖται κατὰ ὕλην, πρώτην κίνησιν, σύνθετον ὅλον, μέρος τοῦ εἴδους καὶ χρονικὴν διαδοχήν.","refs":["CH_DAZY_BOO5:A4","CH_DAZY_BOO5:A8","CH_DAZY_BOO5:A9","CH_DAZY_BOO5:A17"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"From","passages":[{"id":"RAW_DAZY_BOO5:P0067","trace_type":"Composite","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"},{"id":"RAW_DAZY_BOO5:P0068","trace_type":"Composite","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_DAZY_BOO5:D12","text":"Ὅλον ἐστὶ τὸ μηδενὸς φυσικοῦ μέρους ἐλλεῖπον καὶ τὰ μέρη εἰς ἓν περιέχον, κολοβὸν δὲ γίνεται μόνον τὸ συνεχὲς καὶ θέσιν ἔχον ὅλον ὅταν ἀναγκαῖον ἀκρωτήριον ἀποβάλῃ μένον τῇ οὐσίᾳ.","refs":["CH_DAZY_BOO5:A10","CH_DAZY_BOO5:A11","CH_DAZY_BOO5:A15","CH_DAZY_BOO5:A17"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"From","passages":[{"id":"RAW_DAZY_BOO5:P0070","trace_type":"Composite","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"},{"id":"RAW_DAZY_BOO5:P0072","trace_type":"Composite","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"},{"id":"RAW_DAZY_BOO5:P0073","trace_type":"Composite","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_DAZY_BOO5:D13","text":"Γένος λέγεται ἡ συνεχὴς γένεσις ὁμοειδῶν, ὁ πρῶτος γεννήσας, τὸ ὑποκείμενον τῶν διαφορών, καὶ τὸ πρῶτον ἐνυπάρχον ἐν τῷ λόγῳ τοῦ τί ἐστιν.","refs":["CH_DAZY_BOO5:A4","CH_DAZY_BOO5:A5","CH_DAZY_BOO5:A8","CH_DAZY_BOO5:A9","CH_DAZY_BOO5:A12"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"From","passages":[{"id":"RAW_DAZY_BOO5:P0074","trace_type":"Direct","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_DAZY_BOO5:D14","text":"Ψευδὲς πρᾶγμα ἢ μὴ σύγκειται ὡς λέγεται ἢ φαντασίαν ἐμποιεῖ ἄλλου ἢ ὅ ἐστι, ψευδὴς δὲ λόγος ἄλλου ἐστὶν ἢ οὗ ἀληθεύει.","refs":["CH_DAZY_BOO5:A3","CH_DAZY_BOO5:A6","CH_DAZY_BOO5:A16","CH_DAZY_BOO5:A18"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"From","passages":[{"id":"RAW_DAZY_BOO5:P0076","trace_type":"Direct","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]}],"original_name":"Derivative Propositions"},{"number":"5","name":"Super-Derivatives","items":[{"id":"CH_DAZY_BOO5:SD1","text":"Ἡ τῶν λέξεων διαίρεσις γίνεται διαγραφὴ τῶν τρόπων τοῦ ὄντος, ἐπειδὴ αἱ σημασίαι ἀκολουθοῦσι κατηγορίαις, αἰτίαις, τρόποις ὑπάρξεως καὶ βαθμοῖς προτεραιότητος.","refs":["CH_DAZY_BOO5:A1","CH_DAZY_BOO5:A2","CH_DAZY_BOO5:A4","CH_DAZY_BOO5:A5","CH_DAZY_BOO5:A9"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"From","passages":[{"id":"RAW_DAZY_BOO5:P0002","trace_type":"Structural","confidence":"medium","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"},{"id":"RAW_DAZY_BOO5:P0003","trace_type":"Structural","confidence":"medium","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"},{"id":"RAW_DAZY_BOO5:P0027","trace_type":"Structural","confidence":"medium","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"},{"id":"RAW_DAZY_BOO5:P0028","trace_type":"Structural","confidence":"medium","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"},{"id":"RAW_DAZY_BOO5:P0037","trace_type":"Structural","confidence":"medium","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_DAZY_BOO5:SD2","text":"Αἱ ἔννοιαι οὐχ ὡς αὐτόνομα λήμματα ἀλλ᾽ ὡς πλέγμα ἐνεργοῦσιν, ἐπειδὴ ἡ ἀρχή, τὸ αἴτιον, ἡ οὐσία, τὸ ἓν καὶ τὸ τέλος τὰς λοιπὰς διακρίσεις γεννῶσιν.","refs":["CH_DAZY_BOO5:A8","CH_DAZY_BOO5:A9","CH_DAZY_BOO5:A10","CH_DAZY_BOO5:A17","CH_DAZY_BOO5:D1"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"From","passages":[{"id":"RAW_DAZY_BOO5:P0002","trace_type":"Structural","confidence":"low","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"},{"id":"RAW_DAZY_BOO5:P0003","trace_type":"Structural","confidence":"low","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"},{"id":"RAW_DAZY_BOO5:P0031","trace_type":"Structural","confidence":"low","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"},{"id":"RAW_DAZY_BOO5:P0024","trace_type":"Structural","confidence":"low","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"},{"id":"RAW_DAZY_BOO5:P0057","trace_type":"Structural","confidence":"low","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_DAZY_BOO5:SD3","text":"Οὐδεμία προτεραιότης ἁπλῶς ἐπὶ πάντων κρατεῖ, ἐπειδὴ ἄλλο πρότερον κατὰ γένεσιν, ἄλλο κατὰ γνῶσιν, ἄλλο κατὰ δύναμιν καὶ ἄλλο κατ᾽ ἐντελέχειαν ἐστίν.","refs":["CH_DAZY_BOO5:A4","CH_DAZY_BOO5:A7","CH_DAZY_BOO5:D7"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"From","passages":[{"id":"RAW_DAZY_BOO5:P0037","trace_type":"Structural","confidence":"medium","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"},{"id":"RAW_DAZY_BOO5:P0038","trace_type":"Structural","confidence":"medium","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"},{"id":"RAW_DAZY_BOO5:P0039","trace_type":"Structural","confidence":"medium","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_DAZY_BOO5:SD4","text":"Ἡ αἰτιότης περιλαμβάνει ὕλην, μορφήν, κίνησιν, τέλος, παρουσίαν καὶ στέρησιν, ἄνευ τοῦ ταῦτα πάντα εἰς ἕνα τρόπον αἰτίας συγχέειν.","refs":["CH_DAZY_BOO5:A9","CH_DAZY_BOO5:A15","CH_DAZY_BOO5:A16","CH_DAZY_BOO5:A17","CH_DAZY_BOO5:D2"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"From","passages":[{"id":"RAW_DAZY_BOO5:P0003","trace_type":"Composite","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"},{"id":"RAW_DAZY_BOO5:P0004","trace_type":"Composite","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"},{"id":"RAW_DAZY_BOO5:P0005","trace_type":"Composite","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_DAZY_BOO5:SD5","text":"Ἡ ἐπιστημονικὴ ἀπόδειξις προϋποτίθεται τὸ καθ᾽ αὑτό, τὸ ἀναγκαῖον καὶ τὰ πρῶτα, ἐκβάλλει δὲ τὸ κατὰ τύχην συμβεβηκὸς ἀπὸ τῆς ὡρισμένης αἰτιολογίας.","refs":["CH_DAZY_BOO5:A3","CH_DAZY_BOO5:A4","CH_DAZY_BOO5:A14","CH_DAZY_BOO5:A18","CH_DAZY_BOO5:D8"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"From","passages":[{"id":"RAW_DAZY_BOO5:P0017","trace_type":"Composite","confidence":"medium","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"},{"id":"RAW_DAZY_BOO5:P0058","trace_type":"Composite","confidence":"medium","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"},{"id":"RAW_DAZY_BOO5:P0079","trace_type":"Composite","confidence":"medium","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_DAZY_BOO5:SD6","text":"Ἡ ὀντολογικὴ τάξις ἀσύμμετρός ἐστιν, ἐπειδὴ ἡ οὐσία δύναται χωρὶς τῶν συμβεβηκότων νοεῖσθαι, τὰ δὲ συμβεβηκότα οὔτε ἄνευ ὑποκειμένου οὔτε ὡς πρῶται αἰτίαι.","refs":["CH_DAZY_BOO5:A3","CH_DAZY_BOO5:A8","CH_DAZY_BOO5:A18"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"From","passages":[{"id":"RAW_DAZY_BOO5:P0027","trace_type":"Structural","confidence":"medium","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"},{"id":"RAW_DAZY_BOO5:P0030","trace_type":"Structural","confidence":"medium","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"},{"id":"RAW_DAZY_BOO5:P0079","trace_type":"Structural","confidence":"medium","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_DAZY_BOO5:SD7","text":"Τὸ τέλος συνδέει τὴν αἰτιότητα, τὴν φύσιν, τὴν τελειότητα καὶ τὸ πέρας, ὥστε ἡ ὁλοκλήρωσις εἶναι εἰδοποιὸς καὶ οὐ μόνον χρονικὴ παῦλα.","refs":["CH_DAZY_BOO5:A9","CH_DAZY_BOO5:A17","CH_DAZY_BOO5:D4","CH_DAZY_BOO5:D12"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"From","passages":[{"id":"RAW_DAZY_BOO5:P0003","trace_type":"Composite","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"},{"id":"RAW_DAZY_BOO5:P0015","trace_type":"Composite","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"},{"id":"RAW_DAZY_BOO5:P0055","trace_type":"Composite","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"},{"id":"RAW_DAZY_BOO5:P0057","trace_type":"Composite","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]}],"original_name":"Super-Derivatives"},{"number":"6","name":"Deep Apophatics","items":[{"id":"CH_DAZY_BOO5:DNX1","text":"Ἡ πολλαχῶς λέξις οὐ δύναται ἀτάκτῳ ὁμωνυμίᾳ ἐξαντλεῖσθαι, ἐπεὶ τότε οὐδεμία κοινὴ ἀναγωγὴ τῶν χρήσεων δυνατὴ ἂν εἴη.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_DAZY_BOO5:P0015","trace_type":"Structural","confidence":"low","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"},{"id":"RAW_DAZY_BOO5:P0049","trace_type":"Structural","confidence":"low","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"},{"id":"RAW_DAZY_BOO5:P0077","trace_type":"Structural","confidence":"low","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_DAZY_BOO5:DNX2","text":"Τὸ κατὰ τύχην συμβεβηκὸς οὐ δύναται θεμέλιον ἀποδείξεως ἢ ὡρισμένης αἰτιολογίας εἶναι.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_DAZY_BOO5:P0017","trace_type":"Composite","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"},{"id":"RAW_DAZY_BOO5:P0079","trace_type":"Composite","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_DAZY_BOO5:DNX3","text":"Τὰ σχήματα τῆς κατηγορίας οὐ δύναται εἰς ἄλληλα ἀναλύεσθαι ὡς εἰς κοινὸν γένος.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_DAZY_BOO5:P0075","trace_type":"Direct","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_DAZY_BOO5:DNX4","text":"Τὸ δυνάμει ὂν οὐκ ἀναιρεῖται διὰ τὸ μὴ ἤδη ἐνεργείᾳ εἶναι.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_DAZY_BOO5:P0029","trace_type":"Direct","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_DAZY_BOO5:DNX5","text":"Ἡ ἀπουσία οὐ πάντως ἀνενέργητος ἐστίν, ἐπειδὴ ἡ στέρησις δύναται αἰτία τοῦ ἐναντίου ἀποτελέσματος λογίζεσθαι.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_DAZY_BOO5:P0004","trace_type":"Direct","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_DAZY_BOO5:DNX6","text":"Ἡ σχέσις οὐ προϋποτίθεται ἀεὶ συμμετρικὴν ἀντιστροφὴν τοῦ λόγου τῶν σχετιζομένων.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_DAZY_BOO5:P0053","trace_type":"Direct","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_DAZY_BOO5:DNX7","text":"Τὸ πρότερον οὐκ ἔχει νόημα χωρὶς ὡρισμένης ἀρχῆς, τάξεως ἢ τρόπου ἐξαρτήσεως.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_DAZY_BOO5:P0037","trace_type":"Composite","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"},{"id":"RAW_DAZY_BOO5:P0038","trace_type":"Composite","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"},{"id":"RAW_DAZY_BOO5:P0039","trace_type":"Composite","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_DAZY_BOO5:DNX8","text":"Οὔτε ἡ ὕλη μόνη οὔτε τὸ εἶδος ἄνευ παντὸς ὑποκειμένου ἱκανόν ἐστι πρὸς ἐξήγησιν τῆς συνθέτου οὐσίας.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_DAZY_BOO5:P0015","trace_type":"Negative","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"},{"id":"RAW_DAZY_BOO5:P0031","trace_type":"Negative","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]}],"original_name":"Deep Apophatics"},{"number":"7","name":"Super-Apophatics","items":[{"id":"CH_DAZY_BOO5:SNX1","text":"Τὸ ὂν οὐ δύναται ἓν ὁμοίως κατηγορούμενον γένος εἶναι, ἐπειδὴ τὰ σχήματα τῆς κατηγορίας εἰς ἄλληλα οὐκ ἀναλύεται.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_DAZY_BOO5:P0028","trace_type":"Composite","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"},{"id":"RAW_DAZY_BOO5:P0075","trace_type":"Composite","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_DAZY_BOO5:SNX2","text":"Οὐδεμία μονομερὴς ἀναγωγὴ εἰς ὕλην μόνον, εἶδος μόνον, κίνησιν μόνον ἢ τέλος μόνον δύναται τὸ ὂν ἀποχρώντως ἐξηγεῖν.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_DAZY_BOO5:P0003","trace_type":"Structural","confidence":"medium","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"},{"id":"RAW_DAZY_BOO5:P0015","trace_type":"Structural","confidence":"medium","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"},{"id":"RAW_DAZY_BOO5:P0028","trace_type":"Structural","confidence":"medium","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_DAZY_BOO5:SNX3","text":"Ἡ χρονικὴ διαδοχὴ μόνη οὐ ποιεῖ τὸ πρότερον αἴτιον τοῦ ὑστέρου.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_DAZY_BOO5:P0037","trace_type":"Structural","confidence":"medium","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"},{"id":"RAW_DAZY_BOO5:P0068","trace_type":"Structural","confidence":"medium","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_DAZY_BOO5:SNX4","text":"Ἡ ἀντίθεσις οὐ πάντως διαιρεῖ τὸ πεδίον εἰς δύο ἐξαντλητικὰ μέρη, ἐπειδὴ μεταξὺ ἕξεως καὶ πλήρους στερήσεως δύναται μέσον εἶναι.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_DAZY_BOO5:P0064","trace_type":"Direct","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_DAZY_BOO5:SNX5","text":"Ἡ διάταξις οὐ συγχωρεῖ τὰ σημαινόμενα εἶναι ψιλὰς λεκτικὰς συμβάσεις, ἐπειδὴ τὰ κυριώτατα αὐτῶν πρὸς οὐσίαν, αἰτίαν, δύναμιν καὶ τρόπον ὑπάρξεως ἀνάγεται.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_DAZY_BOO5:P0015","trace_type":"Structural","confidence":"low","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"},{"id":"RAW_DAZY_BOO5:P0028","trace_type":"Structural","confidence":"low","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"},{"id":"RAW_DAZY_BOO5:P0045","trace_type":"Structural","confidence":"low","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]}],"original_name":"Super-Apophatics"},{"number":"8","name":"Formal Generative Schema","items":[{"id":"CH_DAZY_BOO5:FGS1","text":"Ἡ μηχανὴ ἀπὸ φωνῆς πολλαχῶς λεγομένης ἐπὶ τὴν συλλογὴν τῶν διακεκριμένων χρήσεων χωρεῖ.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_DAZY_BOO5:P0001","trace_type":"Structural","confidence":"medium","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"},{"id":"RAW_DAZY_BOO5:P0002","trace_type":"Structural","confidence":"medium","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_DAZY_BOO5:FGS2","text":"Αἱ χρήσεις τέμνονται κατὰ τὸ καθ᾽ αὑτό καὶ τὸ συμβεβηκός, τὸ πρῶτον καὶ τὸ ὕστερον, τὸ κύριον καὶ τὸ μεταφορικόν.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_DAZY_BOO5:P0015","trace_type":"Composite","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"},{"id":"RAW_DAZY_BOO5:P0027","trace_type":"Composite","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"},{"id":"RAW_DAZY_BOO5:P0037","trace_type":"Composite","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"},{"id":"RAW_DAZY_BOO5:P0056","trace_type":"Composite","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_DAZY_BOO5:FGS3","text":"Ἕκαστος τρόπος τίθεται ὑπὸ ὡρισμένον ἄξονα, οἷον κατηγορίαν, αἰτίαν, ὑποκείμενον, δύναμιν, ἐντελέχειαν, ἑνότητα ἢ τέλος.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_DAZY_BOO5:P0003","trace_type":"Structural","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"},{"id":"RAW_DAZY_BOO5:P0028","trace_type":"Structural","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"},{"id":"RAW_DAZY_BOO5:P0029","trace_type":"Structural","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"},{"id":"RAW_DAZY_BOO5:P0031","trace_type":"Structural","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"},{"id":"RAW_DAZY_BOO5:P0024","trace_type":"Structural","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"},{"id":"RAW_DAZY_BOO5:P0057","trace_type":"Structural","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_DAZY_BOO5:FGS4","text":"Ἐκ τῶν διῃρημένων τρόπων ἐξαιρεῖται κοινὸς λόγος ἢ κυριώτατον σημαινόμενον πρὸς ὃ τὰ λοιπὰ λέγεται.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_DAZY_BOO5:P0015","trace_type":"Structural","confidence":"medium","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"},{"id":"RAW_DAZY_BOO5:P0045","trace_type":"Structural","confidence":"medium","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_DAZY_BOO5:FGS5","text":"Ὁ κοινὸς λόγος γεννᾷ τὰ συγγενῆ, τὰ ἀντικείμενα καὶ τὰ παράγωγα σημαινόμενα, ὥσπερ τὸ ἓν τὰ πολλὰ καὶ ἡ δύναμις τὴν ἀδυναμίαν.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_DAZY_BOO5:P0025","trace_type":"Structural","confidence":"medium","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"},{"id":"RAW_DAZY_BOO5:P0026","trace_type":"Structural","confidence":"medium","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"},{"id":"RAW_DAZY_BOO5:P0042","trace_type":"Structural","confidence":"medium","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"},{"id":"RAW_DAZY_BOO5:P0043","trace_type":"Structural","confidence":"medium","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_DAZY_BOO5:FGS6","text":"Τὰ ὅρια ἑκάστης ἐννοίας δοκιμάζεται διὰ ἀποκλεισμοῦ τῶν ἐγγὺς ἀλλ᾽ οὐ ταὐτῶν περιπτώσεων, οἷον ἁφῆς, ψιλῆς ἀπουσίας, χρονικῆς διαδοχῆς καὶ τυχαίας συνυπάρξεως.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_DAZY_BOO5:P0010","trace_type":"Composite","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"},{"id":"RAW_DAZY_BOO5:P0021","trace_type":"Composite","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"},{"id":"RAW_DAZY_BOO5:P0063","trace_type":"Composite","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"},{"id":"RAW_DAZY_BOO5:P0068","trace_type":"Composite","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"},{"id":"RAW_DAZY_BOO5:P0079","trace_type":"Composite","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_DAZY_BOO5:FGS7","text":"Ἡ πλήρης γένεσις ἔχει τόδε τὸ σχῆμα· χρήσις → διαίρεσις → ὀντολογικὸς ἄξων → κοινὸς ὅρος → κυρία σημασία → παράγωγα καὶ ἀποκλεισμοί.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_DAZY_BOO5:P0015","trace_type":"Structural","confidence":"medium","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"},{"id":"RAW_DAZY_BOO5:P0028","trace_type":"Structural","confidence":"medium","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"},{"id":"RAW_DAZY_BOO5:P0063","trace_type":"Structural","confidence":"medium","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"},{"id":"RAW_DAZY_BOO5:P0077","trace_type":"Structural","confidence":"medium","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]}],"original_name":"Formal Generative Schema"},{"number":"9","name":"Argument Flow Map","items":[{"id":"CH_DAZY_BOO5:AF1","text":"Ἡ τοπικὴ κίνησις ἑκάστου λήμματος ἐστίν· πολλαχῶς λεγόμενον → διάκρισις κυρίων καὶ παραγώγων τρόπων → κοινὸν στοιχεῖον → κυρίως λεγόμενον.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_DAZY_BOO5:P0015","trace_type":"Structural","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"},{"id":"RAW_DAZY_BOO5:P0017","trace_type":"Structural","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"},{"id":"RAW_DAZY_BOO5:P0045","trace_type":"Structural","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_DAZY_BOO5:AF2","text":"Ἡ διακεφαλαιωτικὴ κίνησις ἐστίν· ἀρχὴ → αἴτιον → στοιχεῖον καὶ φύσις → οὐσία καὶ ὄν → ἓν καὶ ἀντίθεσις → δύναμις, τέλος καὶ συμβεβηκός.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_DAZY_BOO5:P0002","trace_type":"Structural","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"},{"id":"RAW_DAZY_BOO5:P0003","trace_type":"Structural","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"},{"id":"RAW_DAZY_BOO5:P0008","trace_type":"Structural","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"},{"id":"RAW_DAZY_BOO5:P0015","trace_type":"Structural","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"},{"id":"RAW_DAZY_BOO5:P0019","trace_type":"Structural","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"},{"id":"RAW_DAZY_BOO5:P0027","trace_type":"Structural","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"},{"id":"RAW_DAZY_BOO5:P0031","trace_type":"Structural","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"},{"id":"RAW_DAZY_BOO5:P0035","trace_type":"Structural","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"},{"id":"RAW_DAZY_BOO5:P0040","trace_type":"Structural","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"},{"id":"RAW_DAZY_BOO5:P0055","trace_type":"Structural","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"},{"id":"RAW_DAZY_BOO5:P0079","trace_type":"Structural","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_DAZY_BOO5:AF3","text":"Ἡ τελικὴ φορά ἐστίν· λεκτικὴ πολλαπλότης → ὀντολογικὴ διάκρισις → τάξις προτεραιοτήτων → ὅροι ἀποδείξεως καὶ αἰτιώδους γνώσεως.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_DAZY_BOO5:P0002","trace_type":"Structural","confidence":"medium","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"},{"id":"RAW_DAZY_BOO5:P0003","trace_type":"Structural","confidence":"medium","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"},{"id":"RAW_DAZY_BOO5:P0017","trace_type":"Structural","confidence":"medium","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"},{"id":"RAW_DAZY_BOO5:P0027","trace_type":"Structural","confidence":"medium","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"},{"id":"RAW_DAZY_BOO5:P0037","trace_type":"Structural","confidence":"medium","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]}],"original_name":"Argument Flow Map"},{"number":"10","name":"Map-Effects","items":[{"id":"CH_DAZY_BOO5:ME1","text":"Τὰ φαινόμενα ὡς ἀμφίβολα ὀνόματα μετατίθεται εἰς τεταγμένους τρόπους τοῦ εἶναι, τοῦ ποιεῖν, τοῦ πάσχειν καὶ τοῦ γιγνώσκεσθαι.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_DAZY_BOO5:P0002","trace_type":"Structural","confidence":"medium","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"},{"id":"RAW_DAZY_BOO5:P0028","trace_type":"Structural","confidence":"medium","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_DAZY_BOO5:ME2","text":"Τὸ ὂν παύει νοεῖσθαι ὡς ὁμοιογενὲς πεδίον καὶ διαγράφεται κατὰ κατηγορίαν, ἀλήθειαν, δύναμιν καὶ ἐντελέχειαν.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_DAZY_BOO5:P0027","trace_type":"Composite","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"},{"id":"RAW_DAZY_BOO5:P0028","trace_type":"Composite","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"},{"id":"RAW_DAZY_BOO5:P0029","trace_type":"Composite","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_DAZY_BOO5:ME3","text":"Ἡ αἰτιότης παύει ταὐτίζεσθαι πρὸς μόνην τὴν κίνησιν καὶ διανέμεται εἰς ὕλην, εἶδος, κινοῦν καὶ τέλος.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_DAZY_BOO5:P0003","trace_type":"Direct","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"},{"id":"RAW_DAZY_BOO5:P0005","trace_type":"Direct","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_DAZY_BOO5:ME4","text":"Ἡ ταυτότης, ἡ ἰσότης, ἡ ὁμοιότης καὶ ἡ διαφορὰ ἐπανατάσσονται ὡς εἰδικαὶ μορφαὶ ἑνότητος καὶ διαιρέσεως.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_DAZY_BOO5:P0032","trace_type":"Composite","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"},{"id":"RAW_DAZY_BOO5:P0034","trace_type":"Composite","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"},{"id":"RAW_DAZY_BOO5:P0051","trace_type":"Composite","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"},{"id":"RAW_DAZY_BOO5:P0026","trace_type":"Composite","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_DAZY_BOO5:ME5","text":"Ὁ χρόνος παύει παρέχειν μόνος τὸ μέτρον τοῦ προτέρου, ἐπειδὴ προτεραιότης ἀναγνωρίζεται κατὰ γνώσιν, οὐσίαν, δύναμιν καὶ ἐντελέχειαν.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_DAZY_BOO5:P0037","trace_type":"Composite","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"},{"id":"RAW_DAZY_BOO5:P0038","trace_type":"Composite","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"},{"id":"RAW_DAZY_BOO5:P0039","trace_type":"Composite","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_DAZY_BOO5:ME6","text":"Ἡ ἀπουσία παύει εἶναι ἁπλοῦν μηδέν, ἐπειδὴ ὡς στέρησις δύναται ἀντίθεσιν, αἰτιότητα καὶ ἀδυναμίαν ὁρίζειν.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_DAZY_BOO5:P0004","trace_type":"Composite","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"},{"id":"RAW_DAZY_BOO5:P0035","trace_type":"Composite","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"},{"id":"RAW_DAZY_BOO5:P0043","trace_type":"Composite","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_DAZY_BOO5:ME7","text":"Τὸ συμβεβηκὸς χωρίζεται ἀπὸ τοῦ ἀναγκαίου καὶ τοῦ ὡς ἐπὶ τὸ πολύ, ὥστε τὰ ὅρια τῆς ἀποδεικτικῆς γνώσεως φανερὰ γίγνεσθαι.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_DAZY_BOO5:P0017","trace_type":"Composite","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"},{"id":"RAW_DAZY_BOO5:P0079","trace_type":"Composite","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]}],"original_name":"Map-Effects"},{"number":"11","name":"Altitude Levels","items":[{"id":"CH_DAZY_BOO5:ALT1","text":"Ἐπιφανειακῶς ὁ λόγος διορίζει τί σημαίνει ἕκαστον τῶν θεμελιωδῶν φιλοσοφικῶν ὀνομάτων.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_DAZY_BOO5:P0002","trace_type":"Structural","confidence":"medium","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"},{"id":"RAW_DAZY_BOO5:P0027","trace_type":"Structural","confidence":"medium","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"},{"id":"RAW_DAZY_BOO5:P0030","trace_type":"Structural","confidence":"medium","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"},{"id":"RAW_DAZY_BOO5:P0079","trace_type":"Structural","confidence":"medium","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_DAZY_BOO5:ALT2","text":"Κατὰ τὴν διάταξιν ὁ λόγος ἀνασυνθέτει πλέγμα κατηγοριῶν, αἰτίων, ὑποκειμένων, τρόπων ἑνότητος καὶ ἀντιθέσεων.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_DAZY_BOO5:P0003","trace_type":"Structural","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"},{"id":"RAW_DAZY_BOO5:P0023","trace_type":"Structural","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"},{"id":"RAW_DAZY_BOO5:P0028","trace_type":"Structural","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"},{"id":"RAW_DAZY_BOO5:P0031","trace_type":"Structural","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"},{"id":"RAW_DAZY_BOO5:P0035","trace_type":"Structural","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_DAZY_BOO5:ALT3","text":"Ὑπερστρατηγικῶς ὁ λόγος μεταθέτει τὴν φιλοσοφικὴν ἔριν ἀπὸ τῆς ἀμέσου χρήσεως τῶν ὀνομάτων εἰς τὸν πρότερον διορισμὸν τοῦ τρόπου καθ᾽ ὃ λέγεται.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_DAZY_BOO5:P0015","trace_type":"Structural","confidence":"medium","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"},{"id":"RAW_DAZY_BOO5:P0077","trace_type":"Structural","confidence":"medium","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_DAZY_BOO5:ALT4","text":"Περὶ ὄντος καὶ χρόνου ὁ λόγος ὑποτίθεται πραγματικότητα πολυτρόπως τεταγμένην, ἐν ᾗ τὸ δυνάμει καὶ τὸ ἐνεργείᾳ, τὸ πρότερον καὶ τὸ ὕστερον, τὸ ἐνυπάρχον καὶ τὸ ἐκτὸς οὐ συμπίπτει.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_DAZY_BOO5:P0002","trace_type":"Structural","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"},{"id":"RAW_DAZY_BOO5:P0029","trace_type":"Structural","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"},{"id":"RAW_DAZY_BOO5:P0037","trace_type":"Structural","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"},{"id":"RAW_DAZY_BOO5:P0039","trace_type":"Structural","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]}],"original_name":"Altitude Levels"},{"number":"12","name":"Minimal Reconstruction Test","items":[{"id":"CH_DAZY_BOO5:MRT1","text":"Τὰ δεκαοκτὼ ἀξιώματα ἱκανά ἐστι πρὸς ἀνασύστασιν τῆς κυρίας μηχανῆς, ἐπειδὴ σῴζει τὴν πολλαχῶς λέξιν, τὰς βασικὰς τομὰς καὶ τὰς ἀρχὰς ἀναγωγῆς.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[]},{"id":"CH_DAZY_BOO5:MRT2","text":"Ἀδιοριστότερον τοῦ δέοντος μένει πότε κοινὸς λόγος καὶ πότε μόνον κυριώτατον σημαινόμενον ἀρκεῖ πρὸς ἕνωσιν τῶν πολλῶν χρήσεων.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[]},{"id":"CH_DAZY_BOO5:MRT3","text":"Ἡ οὐσία ὡς ἔσχατον ὑποκείμενον καὶ ὡς χωριστὸν εἶδος δίδωσι δύο ἀρχὰς ὧν ἡ μεταξύ σχέσις ἐνταῦθα οὐ πλήρως λύεται.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_DAZY_BOO5:P0030","trace_type":"Tension","confidence":"low","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"},{"id":"RAW_DAZY_BOO5:P0031","trace_type":"Tension","confidence":"low","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_DAZY_BOO5:MRT4","text":"Τὸ συμβεβηκὸς ὡς τυχαῖον καὶ τὸ συμβεβηκὸς ὡς καθ᾽ αὑτὸ μὲν ἀλλ᾽ ἔξω τῆς οὐσίας ὑπάρχον μένει διττὸν ἄνευ πλήρους συνδέσεως.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_DAZY_BOO5:P0079","trace_type":"Structural","confidence":"medium","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"},{"id":"RAW_DAZY_BOO5:P0080","trace_type":"Structural","confidence":"medium","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_DAZY_BOO5:MRT5","text":"Ἡ εἰς τέτταρα εἴδη ἀναγωγὴ τῶν αἰτίων βεβαιοῦται διὰ καταλόγου καὶ παραδειγμάτων μᾶλλον ἢ διὰ ῥητῆς ἀποδείξεως τῆς ἐξαντλήσεως.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_DAZY_BOO5:P0003","trace_type":"Structural","confidence":"medium","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"},{"id":"RAW_DAZY_BOO5:P0004","trace_type":"Structural","confidence":"medium","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"},{"id":"RAW_DAZY_BOO5:P0005","trace_type":"Structural","confidence":"medium","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_DAZY_BOO5:MRT6","text":"Ἡ διάταξις ἰσχυροτάτη ἐστὶν ὅπου αἱ αὐταὶ τομαὶ ἐπανέρχονται, οἷον τὸ καθ᾽ αὑτὸ καὶ τὸ συμβεβηκός, ἡ δύναμις καὶ ἡ ἐντελέχεια, ἡ ὕλη καὶ τὸ εἶδος.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_DAZY_BOO5:P0015","trace_type":"Structural","confidence":"low","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"},{"id":"RAW_DAZY_BOO5:P0027","trace_type":"Structural","confidence":"low","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"},{"id":"RAW_DAZY_BOO5:P0029","trace_type":"Structural","confidence":"low","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"},{"id":"RAW_DAZY_BOO5:P0031","trace_type":"Structural","confidence":"low","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"},{"id":"RAW_DAZY_BOO5:P0079","trace_type":"Structural","confidence":"low","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_DAZY_BOO5:MRT7","text":"Ἡ σύνδεσις τῶν κατηγοριῶν πρὸς τὴν ἀλήθειαν, τὴν δύναμιν καὶ τὴν οὐσίαν πυκνώσει μᾶλλον ἢ πλήρει ἱεραρχικῇ ἀποδείξει στηρίζεται.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_DAZY_BOO5:P0028","trace_type":"Structural","confidence":"low","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"},{"id":"RAW_DAZY_BOO5:P0029","trace_type":"Structural","confidence":"low","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"},{"id":"RAW_DAZY_BOO5:P0030","trace_type":"Structural","confidence":"low","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"},{"id":"RAW_DAZY_BOO5:P0031","trace_type":"Structural","confidence":"low","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_DAZY_BOO5:MRT8","text":"Ἡ ἀνασύστασις σῴζει τὴν ἀληθῆ κίνησιν τοῦ λόγου, ἐπειδὴ ἀπὸ τῆς πολλαπλότητος τῶν χρήσεων οὐκ εἰς σύνοψιν ἀλλ᾽ εἰς τεταγμένην ὀντολογικὴν γραμματικὴν καταλήγει.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_DAZY_BOO5:P0027","trace_type":"Structural","confidence":"low","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"},{"id":"RAW_DAZY_BOO5:P0028","trace_type":"Structural","confidence":"low","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"},{"id":"RAW_DAZY_BOO5:P0029","trace_type":"Structural","confidence":"low","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"},{"id":"RAW_DAZY_BOO5:P0037","trace_type":"Structural","confidence":"low","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"},{"id":"RAW_DAZY_BOO5:P0039","trace_type":"Structural","confidence":"low","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]}],"original_name":"Minimal Reconstruction Test"}],"language_code":"grc","language_name":"Ἑλληνική","translations":[{"id":"CAREVAR_20261015T174600251810Z_5a8c4cf7","language_code":"en","language_name":"English","title":"Book 5","source_sha256":"2d20a9ab15b6740ed0647f53ab5a143d1f032f166a52eecc336c3838e1e3660c","sha256":"a518e95533bc2c8b41a1d5da9517c7c2846f94ac0fa74182b0ed831bc0084f06","rendition_type":"machine","review_status":"machine","model":"gpt-5.6-terra","created":"2026-10-15T17:46:00.253041+00:00","sections":[{"number":"1","name":"Argumental Spine","items":[{"id":"CH_DAZY_BOO5:AS1","text":"It is sought how the fundamental names, said in many ways, are to be distinguished without confusion of the modes of being, becoming, and being known.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_DAZY_BOO5:P0002","trace_type":"Structural","confidence":"medium","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"},{"id":"RAW_DAZY_BOO5:P0027","trace_type":"Structural","confidence":"medium","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"},{"id":"RAW_DAZY_BOO5:P0028","trace_type":"Structural","confidence":"medium","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"},{"id":"RAW_DAZY_BOO5:P0029","trace_type":"Structural","confidence":"medium","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_DAZY_BOO5:AS2","text":"The many modes of each name are ordered under a common account or in relation to a primary signification, so that the expression said in many ways is not disorderly homonymous.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_DAZY_BOO5:P0015","trace_type":"Structural","confidence":"medium","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"},{"id":"RAW_DAZY_BOO5:P0045","trace_type":"Structural","confidence":"medium","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_DAZY_BOO5:AS3","text":"The reasoning is both diagnostic and reconstructive, since it divides the uses, extracts what is common to them, and restores the primary meaning.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_DAZY_BOO5:P0015","trace_type":"Structural","confidence":"medium","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"},{"id":"RAW_DAZY_BOO5:P0045","trace_type":"Structural","confidence":"medium","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_DAZY_BOO5:AS4","text":"Being and one provide the most general model, since the categories, potentiality and actuality, per se and accident divide their modes.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_DAZY_BOO5:P0027","trace_type":"Composite","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"},{"id":"RAW_DAZY_BOO5:P0028","trace_type":"Composite","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"},{"id":"RAW_DAZY_BOO5:P0029","trace_type":"Composite","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"},{"id":"RAW_DAZY_BOO5:P0023","trace_type":"Composite","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"},{"id":"RAW_DAZY_BOO5:P0024","trace_type":"Composite","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_DAZY_BOO5:AS5","text":"The demonstration proceeds through stable cuts, such as subject and form, cause and end, potentiality and actuality, unity and division.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_DAZY_BOO5:P0003","trace_type":"Structural","confidence":"medium","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"},{"id":"RAW_DAZY_BOO5:P0031","trace_type":"Structural","confidence":"medium","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"},{"id":"RAW_DAZY_BOO5:P0039","trace_type":"Structural","confidence":"medium","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"},{"id":"RAW_DAZY_BOO5:P0025","trace_type":"Structural","confidence":"medium","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_DAZY_BOO5:AS6","text":"The generative movement has this form: a name said in many ways → division of modes → common definition → primary meaning → derivation of cognate and opposed concepts.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_DAZY_BOO5:P0015","trace_type":"Structural","confidence":"medium","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"},{"id":"RAW_DAZY_BOO5:P0045","trace_type":"Structural","confidence":"medium","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"},{"id":"RAW_DAZY_BOO5:P0035","trace_type":"Structural","confidence":"medium","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]}],"original_name":"Argumental Spine"},{"number":"2","name":"Load-Bearing Axioms","items":[{"id":"CH_DAZY_BOO5:A1","text":"The fundamental names of being are said in many ways.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_DAZY_BOO5:P0027","trace_type":"Direct","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"},{"id":"RAW_DAZY_BOO5:P0028","trace_type":"Direct","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"},{"id":"RAW_DAZY_BOO5:P0029","trace_type":"Direct","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_DAZY_BOO5:A2","text":"Where many significations are gathered under the same name, their order is determined either by a common account or in relation to a primary mode.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_DAZY_BOO5:P0015","trace_type":"Structural","confidence":"medium","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"},{"id":"RAW_DAZY_BOO5:P0049","trace_type":"Structural","confidence":"medium","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_DAZY_BOO5:A3","text":"Per se and accidentally signify different modes of being and being predicated.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_DAZY_BOO5:P0027","trace_type":"Direct","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"},{"id":"RAW_DAZY_BOO5:P0058","trace_type":"Direct","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_DAZY_BOO5:A4","text":"Prior and posterior are determined in relation to some principle, but what can be without the posterior is prior by nature.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_DAZY_BOO5:P0037","trace_type":"Direct","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"},{"id":"RAW_DAZY_BOO5:P0039","trace_type":"Direct","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_DAZY_BOO5:A5","text":"That which is per se is said in as many ways as the figures of predication.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_DAZY_BOO5:P0027","trace_type":"Direct","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"},{"id":"RAW_DAZY_BOO5:P0028","trace_type":"Direct","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_DAZY_BOO5:A6","text":"To be signifies also the true, and not to be also the false.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_DAZY_BOO5:P0028","trace_type":"Direct","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_DAZY_BOO5:A7","text":"Each of the predicates may be spoken of potentially or actually.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_DAZY_BOO5:P0029","trace_type":"Direct","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_DAZY_BOO5:A8","text":"Substance is said in two primary ways: as the ultimate subject and as a separate this-something through form and species.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_DAZY_BOO5:P0031","trace_type":"Direct","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_DAZY_BOO5:A9","text":"Every cause falls by kind into matter, form, principle of motion, or end.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_DAZY_BOO5:P0003","trace_type":"Direct","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_DAZY_BOO5:A10","text":"One is primarily said according to indivisibility of continuity, form, or account, and becomes the first measure of each genus.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_DAZY_BOO5:P0023","trace_type":"Composite","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"},{"id":"RAW_DAZY_BOO5:P0024","trace_type":"Composite","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_DAZY_BOO5:A11","text":"Quantity is divisible into inherent parts, each of which is naturally some one thing: multitude if numerable, magnitude if measurable.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_DAZY_BOO5:P0046","trace_type":"Direct","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_DAZY_BOO5:A12","text":"The first quality is a difference of substance, while the remaining qualities are affections and differences of motion or actuality.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_DAZY_BOO5:P0048","trace_type":"Composite","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"},{"id":"RAW_DAZY_BOO5:P0049","trace_type":"Composite","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_DAZY_BOO5:A13","text":"Relatives are defined either by being said to be what they are of another, or by another's being said in relation to them.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_DAZY_BOO5:P0053","trace_type":"Direct","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_DAZY_BOO5:A14","text":"What is primarily necessary cannot be otherwise, and the remaining modes of necessity are referred to this.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_DAZY_BOO5:P0017","trace_type":"Direct","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_DAZY_BOO5:A15","text":"Privation is not having what the subject or its genus is naturally fitted to have, when, in respect of what, and as it is naturally fitted to have it.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_DAZY_BOO5:P0063","trace_type":"Direct","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_DAZY_BOO5:A16","text":"Opposition is divided into contradiction, contrariety, relatives, and privation in relation to possession.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_DAZY_BOO5:P0035","trace_type":"Direct","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_DAZY_BOO5:A17","text":"That for the sake of which, as end, defines completion, the last thing and limit of generation or action.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_DAZY_BOO5:P0055","trace_type":"Composite","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"},{"id":"RAW_DAZY_BOO5:P0056","trace_type":"Composite","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"},{"id":"RAW_DAZY_BOO5:P0057","trace_type":"Composite","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_DAZY_BOO5:A18","text":"Accident is said in two ways: either as belonging neither necessarily nor for the most part, or as belonging per se but outside the account of substance.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_DAZY_BOO5:P0079","trace_type":"Composite","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"},{"id":"RAW_DAZY_BOO5:P0080","trace_type":"Composite","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]}],"original_name":"Load-Bearing Axioms"},{"number":"3","name":"Apophatic Structure","items":[{"id":"CH_DAZY_BOO5:NX1","text":"Things said in many ways are not said of all according to one undifferentiated account.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_DAZY_BOO5:P0077","trace_type":"Negative","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_DAZY_BOO5:NX2","text":"Accidental identity cannot be predicated universally and per se.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_DAZY_BOO5:P0032","trace_type":"Negative","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_DAZY_BOO5:NX3","text":"The many causes of the same thing are not causes in the same way.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_DAZY_BOO5:P0004","trace_type":"Direct","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_DAZY_BOO5:NX4","text":"Coalescence and natural continuity are not the same as mere contact.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_DAZY_BOO5:P0010","trace_type":"Negative","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"},{"id":"RAW_DAZY_BOO5:P0021","trace_type":"Negative","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_DAZY_BOO5:NX5","text":"Nature in the primary sense is neither generation alone nor first matter alone.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_DAZY_BOO5:P0015","trace_type":"Negative","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_DAZY_BOO5:NX6","text":"Necessity is not the same as compulsion, but compulsion is a derivative mode of not being able to be otherwise.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_DAZY_BOO5:P0016","trace_type":"Composite","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"},{"id":"RAW_DAZY_BOO5:P0017","trace_type":"Composite","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_DAZY_BOO5:NX7","text":"What is logically possible is not in every case said according to changeable potentiality.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_DAZY_BOO5:P0044","trace_type":"Direct","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_DAZY_BOO5:NX8","text":"Not all relatives reciprocate the definition in the same manner.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_DAZY_BOO5:P0053","trace_type":"Direct","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_DAZY_BOO5:NX9","text":"Not every absence is privation, but only the absence of what is naturally fitted to belong at a determinate time and in a determinate subject.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_DAZY_BOO5:P0063","trace_type":"Negative","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_DAZY_BOO5:NX10","text":"Neither is every continuous thing a whole, nor is every last thing complete.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_DAZY_BOO5:P0024","trace_type":"Composite","confidence":"medium","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"},{"id":"RAW_DAZY_BOO5:P0055","trace_type":"Composite","confidence":"medium","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_DAZY_BOO5:NX11","text":"A false thing is not in every respect nowhere being, since it may exist otherwise than as it appears.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_DAZY_BOO5:P0076","trace_type":"Direct","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_DAZY_BOO5:NX12","text":"The accidental as accidental has no determinate cause.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_DAZY_BOO5:P0079","trace_type":"Direct","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_DAZY_BOO5:NX13","text":"The demand that one thing be said of one thing according to its own account alone is rejected, since it makes both contradiction and falsehood impossible.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_DAZY_BOO5:P0077","trace_type":"Negative","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]}],"original_name":"Apophatic Structure"},{"number":"4","name":"Derivative Propositions","items":[{"id":"CH_DAZY_BOO5:D1","text":"A principle is the first thing from which something either is, comes to be, or is known, whether inherent or external; hence all causes are principles.","refs":["CH_DAZY_BOO5:A2","CH_DAZY_BOO5:A4","CH_DAZY_BOO5:A9"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"From","passages":[{"id":"RAW_DAZY_BOO5:P0002","trace_type":"Direct","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_DAZY_BOO5:D2","text":"The same thing may have several causes, and two things may be causes of one another, but not according to the same kind of cause.","refs":["CH_DAZY_BOO5:A7","CH_DAZY_BOO5:A9","CH_DAZY_BOO5:A17"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"From","passages":[{"id":"RAW_DAZY_BOO5:P0004","trace_type":"Direct","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_DAZY_BOO5:D3","text":"An element is what is first, inherent, and indivisible in species, which by reason of its simplicity can belong in many things.","refs":["CH_DAZY_BOO5:A2","CH_DAZY_BOO5:A4","CH_DAZY_BOO5:A8","CH_DAZY_BOO5:A10"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"From","passages":[{"id":"RAW_DAZY_BOO5:P0008","trace_type":"Composite","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"},{"id":"RAW_DAZY_BOO5:P0009","trace_type":"Composite","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_DAZY_BOO5:D4","text":"Nature in the primary sense is the substance of things having in themselves a principle of motion insofar as they are themselves; matter and generation are called natures in relation to this.","refs":["CH_DAZY_BOO5:A2","CH_DAZY_BOO5:A7","CH_DAZY_BOO5:A8","CH_DAZY_BOO5:A9"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"From","passages":[{"id":"RAW_DAZY_BOO5:P0015","trace_type":"Direct","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_DAZY_BOO5:D5","text":"Identity is unity of being, equality unity of quantity, similarity unity of quality, while difference presupposes some common subject.","refs":["CH_DAZY_BOO5:A10","CH_DAZY_BOO5:A11","CH_DAZY_BOO5:A12","CH_DAZY_BOO5:A13","CH_DAZY_BOO5:A16"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"From","passages":[{"id":"RAW_DAZY_BOO5:P0032","trace_type":"Composite","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"},{"id":"RAW_DAZY_BOO5:P0034","trace_type":"Composite","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"},{"id":"RAW_DAZY_BOO5:P0051","trace_type":"Composite","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_DAZY_BOO5:D6","text":"Many is said in opposition to one according to discontinuity, specific division, or plurality of accounts, while unities of number, species, genus, and analogy follow hierarchically.","refs":["CH_DAZY_BOO5:A2","CH_DAZY_BOO5:A10","CH_DAZY_BOO5:A11"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"From","passages":[{"id":"RAW_DAZY_BOO5:P0025","trace_type":"Composite","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"},{"id":"RAW_DAZY_BOO5:P0026","trace_type":"Composite","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_DAZY_BOO5:D7","text":"Prior changes its mode according to place, time, motion, potentiality, knowledge, and substance; priority according to potentiality can be reversed with priority according to actuality.","refs":["CH_DAZY_BOO5:A4","CH_DAZY_BOO5:A7","CH_DAZY_BOO5:A8"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"From","passages":[{"id":"RAW_DAZY_BOO5:P0037","trace_type":"Structural","confidence":"medium","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"},{"id":"RAW_DAZY_BOO5:P0038","trace_type":"Structural","confidence":"medium","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"},{"id":"RAW_DAZY_BOO5:P0039","trace_type":"Structural","confidence":"medium","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_DAZY_BOO5:D8","text":"Demonstration becomes necessary when the first things from which the syllogism is composed cannot be otherwise.","refs":["CH_DAZY_BOO5:A4","CH_DAZY_BOO5:A14"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"From","passages":[{"id":"RAW_DAZY_BOO5:P0017","trace_type":"Direct","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_DAZY_BOO5:D9","text":"Disposition is an arrangement of parts; habit is a stable disposition of being well or badly disposed; and affection is a quality or actuality of alteration.","refs":["CH_DAZY_BOO5:A7","CH_DAZY_BOO5:A12","CH_DAZY_BOO5:A13","CH_DAZY_BOO5:A16"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"From","passages":[{"id":"RAW_DAZY_BOO5:P0060","trace_type":"Composite","confidence":"medium","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"},{"id":"RAW_DAZY_BOO5:P0061","trace_type":"Composite","confidence":"medium","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"},{"id":"RAW_DAZY_BOO5:P0062","trace_type":"Composite","confidence":"medium","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_DAZY_BOO5:D10","text":"Having signifies leading, receiving, containing, or hindering something from being moved according to its own impulse, and thus indicates a relation of potentiality and what is contained.","refs":["CH_DAZY_BOO5:A7","CH_DAZY_BOO5:A10","CH_DAZY_BOO5:A13"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"From","passages":[{"id":"RAW_DAZY_BOO5:P0065","trace_type":"Direct","confidence":"medium","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_DAZY_BOO5:D11","text":"To be from something is divided according to matter, first motion, composite whole, part of the form, and temporal succession.","refs":["CH_DAZY_BOO5:A4","CH_DAZY_BOO5:A8","CH_DAZY_BOO5:A9","CH_DAZY_BOO5:A17"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"From","passages":[{"id":"RAW_DAZY_BOO5:P0067","trace_type":"Composite","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"},{"id":"RAW_DAZY_BOO5:P0068","trace_type":"Composite","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_DAZY_BOO5:D12","text":"A whole is what lacks none of its natural parts and contains its parts in one; a mutilated whole arises only when a continuous whole having position loses a necessary extremity while remaining in substance.","refs":["CH_DAZY_BOO5:A10","CH_DAZY_BOO5:A11","CH_DAZY_BOO5:A15","CH_DAZY_BOO5:A17"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"From","passages":[{"id":"RAW_DAZY_BOO5:P0070","trace_type":"Composite","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"},{"id":"RAW_DAZY_BOO5:P0072","trace_type":"Composite","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"},{"id":"RAW_DAZY_BOO5:P0073","trace_type":"Composite","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_DAZY_BOO5:D13","text":"Genus is said of the continuous generation of homogeneous things, the first progenitor, the subject of differences, and the first inherent thing in the account of what-it-is.","refs":["CH_DAZY_BOO5:A4","CH_DAZY_BOO5:A5","CH_DAZY_BOO5:A8","CH_DAZY_BOO5:A9","CH_DAZY_BOO5:A12"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"From","passages":[{"id":"RAW_DAZY_BOO5:P0074","trace_type":"Direct","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_DAZY_BOO5:D14","text":"A false thing either is not compounded as it is said to be, or produces an appearance of being other than it is; a false account is of something other than that of which it is true.","refs":["CH_DAZY_BOO5:A3","CH_DAZY_BOO5:A6","CH_DAZY_BOO5:A16","CH_DAZY_BOO5:A18"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"From","passages":[{"id":"RAW_DAZY_BOO5:P0076","trace_type":"Direct","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]}],"original_name":"Derivative Propositions"},{"number":"5","name":"Super-Derivatives","items":[{"id":"CH_DAZY_BOO5:SD1","text":"The division of words becomes a mapping of the modes of being, since significations follow categories, causes, modes of existence, and degrees of priority.","refs":["CH_DAZY_BOO5:A1","CH_DAZY_BOO5:A2","CH_DAZY_BOO5:A4","CH_DAZY_BOO5:A5","CH_DAZY_BOO5:A9"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"From","passages":[{"id":"RAW_DAZY_BOO5:P0002","trace_type":"Structural","confidence":"medium","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"},{"id":"RAW_DAZY_BOO5:P0003","trace_type":"Structural","confidence":"medium","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"},{"id":"RAW_DAZY_BOO5:P0027","trace_type":"Structural","confidence":"medium","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"},{"id":"RAW_DAZY_BOO5:P0028","trace_type":"Structural","confidence":"medium","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"},{"id":"RAW_DAZY_BOO5:P0037","trace_type":"Structural","confidence":"medium","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_DAZY_BOO5:SD2","text":"Concepts operate not as autonomous entries but as a web, since principle, cause, substance, one, and end generate the remaining distinctions.","refs":["CH_DAZY_BOO5:A8","CH_DAZY_BOO5:A9","CH_DAZY_BOO5:A10","CH_DAZY_BOO5:A17","CH_DAZY_BOO5:D1"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"From","passages":[{"id":"RAW_DAZY_BOO5:P0002","trace_type":"Structural","confidence":"low","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"},{"id":"RAW_DAZY_BOO5:P0003","trace_type":"Structural","confidence":"low","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"},{"id":"RAW_DAZY_BOO5:P0031","trace_type":"Structural","confidence":"low","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"},{"id":"RAW_DAZY_BOO5:P0024","trace_type":"Structural","confidence":"low","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"},{"id":"RAW_DAZY_BOO5:P0057","trace_type":"Structural","confidence":"low","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_DAZY_BOO5:SD3","text":"No priority prevails simply in all respects, since one thing is prior in generation, another in knowledge, another in potentiality, and another in actuality.","refs":["CH_DAZY_BOO5:A4","CH_DAZY_BOO5:A7","CH_DAZY_BOO5:D7"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"From","passages":[{"id":"RAW_DAZY_BOO5:P0037","trace_type":"Structural","confidence":"medium","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"},{"id":"RAW_DAZY_BOO5:P0038","trace_type":"Structural","confidence":"medium","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"},{"id":"RAW_DAZY_BOO5:P0039","trace_type":"Structural","confidence":"medium","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_DAZY_BOO5:SD4","text":"Causality includes matter, form, motion, end, presence, and privation, without confusing all these into one mode of cause.","refs":["CH_DAZY_BOO5:A9","CH_DAZY_BOO5:A15","CH_DAZY_BOO5:A16","CH_DAZY_BOO5:A17","CH_DAZY_BOO5:D2"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"From","passages":[{"id":"RAW_DAZY_BOO5:P0003","trace_type":"Composite","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"},{"id":"RAW_DAZY_BOO5:P0004","trace_type":"Composite","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"},{"id":"RAW_DAZY_BOO5:P0005","trace_type":"Composite","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_DAZY_BOO5:SD5","text":"Scientific demonstration presupposes the per se, the necessary, and first things, but excludes the accidental from determinate explanation.","refs":["CH_DAZY_BOO5:A3","CH_DAZY_BOO5:A4","CH_DAZY_BOO5:A14","CH_DAZY_BOO5:A18","CH_DAZY_BOO5:D8"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"From","passages":[{"id":"RAW_DAZY_BOO5:P0017","trace_type":"Composite","confidence":"medium","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"},{"id":"RAW_DAZY_BOO5:P0058","trace_type":"Composite","confidence":"medium","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"},{"id":"RAW_DAZY_BOO5:P0079","trace_type":"Composite","confidence":"medium","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_DAZY_BOO5:SD6","text":"The ontological order is asymmetrical, since substance can be conceived apart from accidents, whereas accidents are neither without a subject nor as first causes.","refs":["CH_DAZY_BOO5:A3","CH_DAZY_BOO5:A8","CH_DAZY_BOO5:A18"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"From","passages":[{"id":"RAW_DAZY_BOO5:P0027","trace_type":"Structural","confidence":"medium","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"},{"id":"RAW_DAZY_BOO5:P0030","trace_type":"Structural","confidence":"medium","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"},{"id":"RAW_DAZY_BOO5:P0079","trace_type":"Structural","confidence":"medium","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_DAZY_BOO5:SD7","text":"The end connects causality, nature, perfection, and limit, so that completion is specific and not merely a temporal cessation.","refs":["CH_DAZY_BOO5:A9","CH_DAZY_BOO5:A17","CH_DAZY_BOO5:D4","CH_DAZY_BOO5:D12"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"From","passages":[{"id":"RAW_DAZY_BOO5:P0003","trace_type":"Composite","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"},{"id":"RAW_DAZY_BOO5:P0015","trace_type":"Composite","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"},{"id":"RAW_DAZY_BOO5:P0055","trace_type":"Composite","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"},{"id":"RAW_DAZY_BOO5:P0057","trace_type":"Composite","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]}],"original_name":"Super-Derivatives"},{"number":"6","name":"Deep Apophatics","items":[{"id":"CH_DAZY_BOO5:DNX1","text":"An expression said in many ways cannot be exhausted by disorderly homonymy, since then no common reduction of its uses would be possible.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_DAZY_BOO5:P0015","trace_type":"Structural","confidence":"low","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"},{"id":"RAW_DAZY_BOO5:P0049","trace_type":"Structural","confidence":"low","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"},{"id":"RAW_DAZY_BOO5:P0077","trace_type":"Structural","confidence":"low","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_DAZY_BOO5:DNX2","text":"The accidental cannot be a foundation of demonstration or determinate explanation.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_DAZY_BOO5:P0017","trace_type":"Composite","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"},{"id":"RAW_DAZY_BOO5:P0079","trace_type":"Composite","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_DAZY_BOO5:DNX3","text":"The figures of predication cannot be analyzed into one another as into a common genus.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_DAZY_BOO5:P0075","trace_type":"Direct","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_DAZY_BOO5:DNX4","text":"What is potentially cannot be negated on account of not already being actually.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_DAZY_BOO5:P0029","trace_type":"Direct","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_DAZY_BOO5:DNX5","text":"Absence is not in every case inactive, since privation can be counted as a cause of the contrary result.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_DAZY_BOO5:P0004","trace_type":"Direct","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_DAZY_BOO5:DNX6","text":"Relation does not always presuppose a symmetrical reciprocal reversal of the account of the related things.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_DAZY_BOO5:P0053","trace_type":"Direct","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_DAZY_BOO5:DNX7","text":"Prior has no meaning apart from a determinate principle, order, or mode of dependence.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_DAZY_BOO5:P0037","trace_type":"Composite","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"},{"id":"RAW_DAZY_BOO5:P0038","trace_type":"Composite","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"},{"id":"RAW_DAZY_BOO5:P0039","trace_type":"Composite","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_DAZY_BOO5:DNX8","text":"Neither matter alone nor form without every subject is sufficient for explaining composite substance.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_DAZY_BOO5:P0015","trace_type":"Negative","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"},{"id":"RAW_DAZY_BOO5:P0031","trace_type":"Negative","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]}],"original_name":"Deep Apophatics"},{"number":"7","name":"Super-Apophatics","items":[{"id":"CH_DAZY_BOO5:SNX1","text":"Being cannot be one genus predicated uniformly, since the figures of predication are not analyzed into one another.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_DAZY_BOO5:P0028","trace_type":"Composite","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"},{"id":"RAW_DAZY_BOO5:P0075","trace_type":"Composite","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_DAZY_BOO5:SNX2","text":"No one-sided reduction to matter alone, form alone, motion alone, or end alone can sufficiently explain being.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_DAZY_BOO5:P0003","trace_type":"Structural","confidence":"medium","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"},{"id":"RAW_DAZY_BOO5:P0015","trace_type":"Structural","confidence":"medium","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"},{"id":"RAW_DAZY_BOO5:P0028","trace_type":"Structural","confidence":"medium","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_DAZY_BOO5:SNX3","text":"Temporal succession alone does not make the prior a cause of the posterior.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_DAZY_BOO5:P0037","trace_type":"Structural","confidence":"medium","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"},{"id":"RAW_DAZY_BOO5:P0068","trace_type":"Structural","confidence":"medium","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_DAZY_BOO5:SNX4","text":"Opposition does not in every case divide the field into two exhaustive parts, since there can be an intermediate between possession and complete privation.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_DAZY_BOO5:P0064","trace_type":"Direct","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_DAZY_BOO5:SNX5","text":"The ordering does not allow significations to be mere verbal conventions, since their primary senses are referred to substance, cause, potentiality, and mode of existence.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_DAZY_BOO5:P0015","trace_type":"Structural","confidence":"low","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"},{"id":"RAW_DAZY_BOO5:P0028","trace_type":"Structural","confidence":"low","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"},{"id":"RAW_DAZY_BOO5:P0045","trace_type":"Structural","confidence":"low","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]}],"original_name":"Super-Apophatics"},{"number":"8","name":"Formal Generative Schema","items":[{"id":"CH_DAZY_BOO5:FGS1","text":"The mechanism proceeds from a word said in many ways to the collection of its distinguished uses.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_DAZY_BOO5:P0001","trace_type":"Structural","confidence":"medium","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"},{"id":"RAW_DAZY_BOO5:P0002","trace_type":"Structural","confidence":"medium","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_DAZY_BOO5:FGS2","text":"Uses are cut according to per se and accident, prior and posterior, primary and metaphorical.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_DAZY_BOO5:P0015","trace_type":"Composite","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"},{"id":"RAW_DAZY_BOO5:P0027","trace_type":"Composite","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"},{"id":"RAW_DAZY_BOO5:P0037","trace_type":"Composite","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"},{"id":"RAW_DAZY_BOO5:P0056","trace_type":"Composite","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_DAZY_BOO5:FGS3","text":"Each mode is placed under a determinate axis, such as category, cause, subject, potentiality, actuality, unity, or end.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_DAZY_BOO5:P0003","trace_type":"Structural","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"},{"id":"RAW_DAZY_BOO5:P0028","trace_type":"Structural","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"},{"id":"RAW_DAZY_BOO5:P0029","trace_type":"Structural","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"},{"id":"RAW_DAZY_BOO5:P0031","trace_type":"Structural","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"},{"id":"RAW_DAZY_BOO5:P0024","trace_type":"Structural","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"},{"id":"RAW_DAZY_BOO5:P0057","trace_type":"Structural","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_DAZY_BOO5:FGS4","text":"From the divided modes a common account or primary signification is extracted, in relation to which the remaining modes are spoken.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_DAZY_BOO5:P0015","trace_type":"Structural","confidence":"medium","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"},{"id":"RAW_DAZY_BOO5:P0045","trace_type":"Structural","confidence":"medium","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_DAZY_BOO5:FGS5","text":"The common account generates cognate, opposed, and derivative significations, as one generates many and potentiality impotence.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_DAZY_BOO5:P0025","trace_type":"Structural","confidence":"medium","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"},{"id":"RAW_DAZY_BOO5:P0026","trace_type":"Structural","confidence":"medium","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"},{"id":"RAW_DAZY_BOO5:P0042","trace_type":"Structural","confidence":"medium","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"},{"id":"RAW_DAZY_BOO5:P0043","trace_type":"Structural","confidence":"medium","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_DAZY_BOO5:FGS6","text":"The boundaries of each concept are tested by excluding cases that are near but not identical, such as contact, mere absence, temporal succession, and accidental coexistence.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_DAZY_BOO5:P0010","trace_type":"Composite","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"},{"id":"RAW_DAZY_BOO5:P0021","trace_type":"Composite","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"},{"id":"RAW_DAZY_BOO5:P0063","trace_type":"Composite","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"},{"id":"RAW_DAZY_BOO5:P0068","trace_type":"Composite","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"},{"id":"RAW_DAZY_BOO5:P0079","trace_type":"Composite","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_DAZY_BOO5:FGS7","text":"Complete generation has this schema: use → division → ontological axis → common definition → primary meaning → derivatives and exclusions.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_DAZY_BOO5:P0015","trace_type":"Structural","confidence":"medium","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"},{"id":"RAW_DAZY_BOO5:P0028","trace_type":"Structural","confidence":"medium","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"},{"id":"RAW_DAZY_BOO5:P0063","trace_type":"Structural","confidence":"medium","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"},{"id":"RAW_DAZY_BOO5:P0077","trace_type":"Structural","confidence":"medium","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]}],"original_name":"Formal Generative Schema"},{"number":"9","name":"Argument Flow Map","items":[{"id":"CH_DAZY_BOO5:AF1","text":"The local movement of each entry is: what is said in many ways → distinction of primary and derivative modes → common element → what is said primarily.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_DAZY_BOO5:P0015","trace_type":"Structural","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"},{"id":"RAW_DAZY_BOO5:P0017","trace_type":"Structural","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"},{"id":"RAW_DAZY_BOO5:P0045","trace_type":"Structural","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_DAZY_BOO5:AF2","text":"The recapitulative movement is: principle → cause → element and nature → substance and being → one and opposition → potentiality, end, and accident.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_DAZY_BOO5:P0002","trace_type":"Structural","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"},{"id":"RAW_DAZY_BOO5:P0003","trace_type":"Structural","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"},{"id":"RAW_DAZY_BOO5:P0008","trace_type":"Structural","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"},{"id":"RAW_DAZY_BOO5:P0015","trace_type":"Structural","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"},{"id":"RAW_DAZY_BOO5:P0019","trace_type":"Structural","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"},{"id":"RAW_DAZY_BOO5:P0027","trace_type":"Structural","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"},{"id":"RAW_DAZY_BOO5:P0031","trace_type":"Structural","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"},{"id":"RAW_DAZY_BOO5:P0035","trace_type":"Structural","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"},{"id":"RAW_DAZY_BOO5:P0040","trace_type":"Structural","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"},{"id":"RAW_DAZY_BOO5:P0055","trace_type":"Structural","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"},{"id":"RAW_DAZY_BOO5:P0079","trace_type":"Structural","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_DAZY_BOO5:AF3","text":"The final direction is: verbal multiplicity → ontological distinction → order of priorities → conditions of demonstration and causal knowledge.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_DAZY_BOO5:P0002","trace_type":"Structural","confidence":"medium","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"},{"id":"RAW_DAZY_BOO5:P0003","trace_type":"Structural","confidence":"medium","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"},{"id":"RAW_DAZY_BOO5:P0017","trace_type":"Structural","confidence":"medium","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"},{"id":"RAW_DAZY_BOO5:P0027","trace_type":"Structural","confidence":"medium","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"},{"id":"RAW_DAZY_BOO5:P0037","trace_type":"Structural","confidence":"medium","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]}],"original_name":"Argument Flow Map"},{"number":"10","name":"Map-Effects","items":[{"id":"CH_DAZY_BOO5:ME1","text":"Phenomena as ambiguous names are transformed into ordered modes of being, acting, being affected, and being known.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_DAZY_BOO5:P0002","trace_type":"Structural","confidence":"medium","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"},{"id":"RAW_DAZY_BOO5:P0028","trace_type":"Structural","confidence":"medium","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_DAZY_BOO5:ME2","text":"Being ceases to be conceived as a homogeneous field and is mapped according to category, truth, potentiality, and actuality.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_DAZY_BOO5:P0027","trace_type":"Composite","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"},{"id":"RAW_DAZY_BOO5:P0028","trace_type":"Composite","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"},{"id":"RAW_DAZY_BOO5:P0029","trace_type":"Composite","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_DAZY_BOO5:ME3","text":"Causality ceases to be identified with motion alone and is distributed into matter, form, mover, and end.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_DAZY_BOO5:P0003","trace_type":"Direct","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"},{"id":"RAW_DAZY_BOO5:P0005","trace_type":"Direct","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_DAZY_BOO5:ME4","text":"Identity, equality, similarity, and difference are reordered as specific forms of unity and division.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_DAZY_BOO5:P0032","trace_type":"Composite","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"},{"id":"RAW_DAZY_BOO5:P0034","trace_type":"Composite","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"},{"id":"RAW_DAZY_BOO5:P0051","trace_type":"Composite","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"},{"id":"RAW_DAZY_BOO5:P0026","trace_type":"Composite","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_DAZY_BOO5:ME5","text":"Time ceases alone to provide the measure of the prior, since priority is recognized according to knowledge, substance, potentiality, and actuality.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_DAZY_BOO5:P0037","trace_type":"Composite","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"},{"id":"RAW_DAZY_BOO5:P0038","trace_type":"Composite","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"},{"id":"RAW_DAZY_BOO5:P0039","trace_type":"Composite","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_DAZY_BOO5:ME6","text":"Absence ceases to be a simple nothing, since as privation it can define opposition, causality, and incapacity.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_DAZY_BOO5:P0004","trace_type":"Composite","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"},{"id":"RAW_DAZY_BOO5:P0035","trace_type":"Composite","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"},{"id":"RAW_DAZY_BOO5:P0043","trace_type":"Composite","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_DAZY_BOO5:ME7","text":"Accident is separated from the necessary and from what holds for the most part, so that the limits of demonstrative knowledge become clear.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_DAZY_BOO5:P0017","trace_type":"Composite","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"},{"id":"RAW_DAZY_BOO5:P0079","trace_type":"Composite","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]}],"original_name":"Map-Effects"},{"number":"11","name":"Altitude Levels","items":[{"id":"CH_DAZY_BOO5:ALT1","text":"At the surface level, the account distinguishes what each of the fundamental philosophical names signifies.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_DAZY_BOO5:P0002","trace_type":"Structural","confidence":"medium","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"},{"id":"RAW_DAZY_BOO5:P0027","trace_type":"Structural","confidence":"medium","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"},{"id":"RAW_DAZY_BOO5:P0030","trace_type":"Structural","confidence":"medium","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"},{"id":"RAW_DAZY_BOO5:P0079","trace_type":"Structural","confidence":"medium","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_DAZY_BOO5:ALT2","text":"In its ordering, the account reconstructs a web of categories, causes, subjects, modes of unity, and oppositions.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_DAZY_BOO5:P0003","trace_type":"Structural","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"},{"id":"RAW_DAZY_BOO5:P0023","trace_type":"Structural","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"},{"id":"RAW_DAZY_BOO5:P0028","trace_type":"Structural","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"},{"id":"RAW_DAZY_BOO5:P0031","trace_type":"Structural","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"},{"id":"RAW_DAZY_BOO5:P0035","trace_type":"Structural","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_DAZY_BOO5:ALT3","text":"At the hyper-strategic level, the account shifts philosophical dispute from the immediate use of names to the prior determination of the way in which each is said.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_DAZY_BOO5:P0015","trace_type":"Structural","confidence":"medium","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"},{"id":"RAW_DAZY_BOO5:P0077","trace_type":"Structural","confidence":"medium","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_DAZY_BOO5:ALT4","text":"Concerning being and time, the account posits a reality ordered in many ways, in which the potential and the actual, the prior and the posterior, the inherent and the external do not coincide.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_DAZY_BOO5:P0002","trace_type":"Structural","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"},{"id":"RAW_DAZY_BOO5:P0029","trace_type":"Structural","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"},{"id":"RAW_DAZY_BOO5:P0037","trace_type":"Structural","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"},{"id":"RAW_DAZY_BOO5:P0039","trace_type":"Structural","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]}],"original_name":"Altitude Levels"},{"number":"12","name":"Minimal Reconstruction Test","items":[{"id":"CH_DAZY_BOO5:MRT1","text":"The eighteen axioms are sufficient for reconstructing the principal mechanism, since they preserve the expression said in many ways, the basic cuts, and the principles of reduction.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[]},{"id":"CH_DAZY_BOO5:MRT2","text":"It remains more indeterminate than is necessary when a common account, and when a primary signification alone, suffices for unifying the many uses.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[]},{"id":"CH_DAZY_BOO5:MRT3","text":"Substance as ultimate subject and as separate form yields two principles whose mutual relation is not fully resolved here.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_DAZY_BOO5:P0030","trace_type":"Tension","confidence":"low","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"},{"id":"RAW_DAZY_BOO5:P0031","trace_type":"Tension","confidence":"low","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_DAZY_BOO5:MRT4","text":"Accident as chance and accident as what belongs per se but exists outside substance remain twofold without a complete connection.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_DAZY_BOO5:P0079","trace_type":"Structural","confidence":"medium","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"},{"id":"RAW_DAZY_BOO5:P0080","trace_type":"Structural","confidence":"medium","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_DAZY_BOO5:MRT5","text":"The reduction of causes to four kinds is confirmed by enumeration and examples rather than by an explicit demonstration of exhaustiveness.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_DAZY_BOO5:P0003","trace_type":"Structural","confidence":"medium","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"},{"id":"RAW_DAZY_BOO5:P0004","trace_type":"Structural","confidence":"medium","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"},{"id":"RAW_DAZY_BOO5:P0005","trace_type":"Structural","confidence":"medium","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_DAZY_BOO5:MRT6","text":"The ordering is strongest where the same cuts recur, such as per se and accident, potentiality and actuality, matter and form.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_DAZY_BOO5:P0015","trace_type":"Structural","confidence":"low","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"},{"id":"RAW_DAZY_BOO5:P0027","trace_type":"Structural","confidence":"low","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"},{"id":"RAW_DAZY_BOO5:P0029","trace_type":"Structural","confidence":"low","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"},{"id":"RAW_DAZY_BOO5:P0031","trace_type":"Structural","confidence":"low","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"},{"id":"RAW_DAZY_BOO5:P0079","trace_type":"Structural","confidence":"low","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_DAZY_BOO5:MRT7","text":"The connection of the categories with truth, potentiality, and substance rests more on density than on a complete hierarchical demonstration.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_DAZY_BOO5:P0028","trace_type":"Structural","confidence":"low","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"},{"id":"RAW_DAZY_BOO5:P0029","trace_type":"Structural","confidence":"low","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"},{"id":"RAW_DAZY_BOO5:P0030","trace_type":"Structural","confidence":"low","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"},{"id":"RAW_DAZY_BOO5:P0031","trace_type":"Structural","confidence":"low","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_DAZY_BOO5:MRT8","text":"The reconstruction preserves the true movement of the account, since from the multiplicity of uses it arrives not at a synopsis but at an ordered ontological grammar.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_DAZY_BOO5:P0027","trace_type":"Structural","confidence":"low","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"},{"id":"RAW_DAZY_BOO5:P0028","trace_type":"Structural","confidence":"low","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"},{"id":"RAW_DAZY_BOO5:P0029","trace_type":"Structural","confidence":"low","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"},{"id":"RAW_DAZY_BOO5:P0037","trace_type":"Structural","confidence":"low","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"},{"id":"RAW_DAZY_BOO5:P0039","trace_type":"Structural","confidence":"low","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]}],"original_name":"Minimal Reconstruction Test"}],"revision":"CH_DAZY_BOO5@R001","source_variant_id":"canonical:CH_DAZY_BOO5:v1","source_language_code":"grc","original_title":"Book 5"}],"identity":{"layer":"CH","kicker":"Chapter 05","title":"Book 5","context":"Chapter 05","original_title":"Book 5","original_context":"Chapter 05"},"original_title":"Book 5","title_language_code":"en"},{"id":"CH_DAZY_BOO6","layer":"CH","title":"Book 6","order":6,"parents":["RAW_DAZY_BOO6"],"revision":"CH_DAZY_BOO6@R001","sha256":"d66cf9ef24bb21608eac3069bc34d03efee86b40ecbbe920cd8c41b2d562e990","sections":[{"number":"1","name":"Argumental Spine","items":[{"id":"CH_DAZY_BOO6:AS1","text":"Ἡ ἀπορία ζητεῖ πῶς ἐπιστήμη τις τὰς ἀρχὰς τοῦ ὄντος ᾗ ὂν θεωρήσει, ἐπειδὴ αἱ ἄλλαι ἐπιστῆμαι γένος τι περιγράψασαι μόνον πραγματεύονται.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_DAZY_BOO6:P0001","trace_type":"Direct","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_DAZY_BOO6:AS2","text":"Ἡ λύσις διακρίνει τὴν καθόλου ζήτησιν τοῦ ὄντος ἀπὸ τῶν μερικῶν ἐπιστημῶν, αἳ τὸ τί ἐστι καὶ τὸ εἰ ἔστιν ὑποτίθενται μᾶλλον ἢ ἀποδεικνύουσιν.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_DAZY_BOO6:P0001","trace_type":"Composite","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"},{"id":"RAW_DAZY_BOO6:P0002","trace_type":"Composite","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_DAZY_BOO6:AS3","text":"Ἡ διαίρεσις τῶν θεωρητικῶν ἐπιστημῶν κατὰ κίνησιν καὶ χωρισμὸν τῆς οὐσίας ἐκφέρει τὴν πρώτην φιλοσοφίαν, εἰ οὐσία ἀκίνητος καὶ χωριστὴ ὑπάρχει.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_DAZY_BOO6:P0002","trace_type":"Composite","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"},{"id":"RAW_DAZY_BOO6:P0004","trace_type":"Composite","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"},{"id":"RAW_DAZY_BOO6:P0005","trace_type":"Composite","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_DAZY_BOO6:AS4","text":"Ἡ πολλαχότης τοῦ ὄντος ἀναγκάζει τὸν λόγον ἀφεῖναι τὸ κατὰ συμβεβηκὸς καὶ τὸ ὡς ἀληθὲς ὄν, ἵνα τὸ κυρίως ὂν ζητηθῇ.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_DAZY_BOO6:P0006","trace_type":"Composite","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"},{"id":"RAW_DAZY_BOO6:P0014","trace_type":"Composite","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_DAZY_BOO6:AS5","text":"Ἡ ἀδυναμία ἐπιστήμης τοῦ συμβεβηκότος δείκνυται ἐκ τοῦ ἀορίστου αἰτίου αὐτοῦ καὶ ἐκ τοῦ μήτε ἀεὶ μήτε ὡς ἐπὶ τὸ πολὺ συμβαίνειν.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_DAZY_BOO6:P0008","trace_type":"Composite","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"},{"id":"RAW_DAZY_BOO6:P0009","trace_type":"Composite","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"},{"id":"RAW_DAZY_BOO6:P0010","trace_type":"Composite","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_DAZY_BOO6:AS6","text":"Ἡ ἀναγωγὴ τῶν γενητῶν εἰς ἀρχὰς οὐκ ἐφίεται εἰς ἄπειρον, ἀλλὰ καταλήγει εἰς ἀρχὴν τοῦ ὁποτέρ᾽ ἔτυχε, ἧς τὸ περαιτέρω αἴτιον ζητεῖται.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_DAZY_BOO6:P0011","trace_type":"Composite","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"},{"id":"RAW_DAZY_BOO6:P0012","trace_type":"Composite","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_DAZY_BOO6:AS7","text":"Ἡ τελικὴ κίνησις ἀφαιρεῖ τὸ συμβεβηκὸς καὶ τὸ ἀληθὲς ὡς πάθη διανοίας, καὶ ἐπανέρχεται εἰς τὰς ἀρχὰς τοῦ ὄντος αὐτοῦ ᾗ ὄν.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_DAZY_BOO6:P0013","trace_type":"Composite","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"},{"id":"RAW_DAZY_BOO6:P0014","trace_type":"Composite","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]}],"original_name":"Argumental Spine"},{"number":"2","name":"Load-Bearing Axioms","items":[{"id":"CH_DAZY_BOO6:A1","text":"Ἡ πρώτη ζήτησις τὰς ἀρχὰς καὶ τὰ αἴτια ζητεῖ τῶν ὄντων ᾗ ὄντα.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_DAZY_BOO6:P0001","trace_type":"Direct","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_DAZY_BOO6:A2","text":"Πᾶσα μερικὴ ἐπιστήμη γένος τι τοῦ ὄντος περιγράφει καὶ περὶ τούτου μόνον πραγματεύεται.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_DAZY_BOO6:P0001","trace_type":"Direct","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_DAZY_BOO6:A3","text":"Αἱ μερικαὶ ἐπιστῆμαι οὔτε τὸ τί ἐστιν οὔτε τὸ εἰ ἔστι τὸ γένος αὐτῶν ἀποδεικνύουσιν, ἀλλὰ τοῦτο προϋποτίθενται ἢ δι᾽ αἰσθήσεως λαμβάνουσιν.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_DAZY_BOO6:P0001","trace_type":"Direct","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"},{"id":"RAW_DAZY_BOO6:P0002","trace_type":"Direct","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_DAZY_BOO6:A4","text":"Ἡ φυσικὴ θεωρητική ἐστιν, ὅτι περὶ οὐσίαν κινεῖσθαι δυναμένην καὶ μετὰ ὕλης θεωρεῖ.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_DAZY_BOO6:P0002","trace_type":"Direct","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_DAZY_BOO6:A5","text":"Ἡ οὐσία ἡ ἀΐδιος, ἀκίνητος καὶ χωριστὴ ἄλλης θεωρητικῆς ἐπιστήμης ἐστὶν ἀντικείμενον παρὰ φυσικὴν καὶ μαθηματικήν.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_DAZY_BOO6:P0004","trace_type":"Direct","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_DAZY_BOO6:A6","text":"Εἰ οὐσία ἀκίνητος καὶ χωριστὴ ἔστιν, ἡ περὶ αὐτὴν ἐπιστήμη πρώτη ἐστὶ καὶ καθόλου περὶ τὸ ὂν ᾗ ὂν θεωρεῖ.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_DAZY_BOO6:P0005","trace_type":"Direct","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_DAZY_BOO6:A7","text":"Τὸ ὂν λέγεται πολλαχῶς, κατὰ συμβεβηκός, ὡς ἀληθές, κατὰ τὰ σχήματα τῶν κατηγοριῶν, κατὰ δύναμιν καὶ κατ᾽ ἐνέργειαν.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_DAZY_BOO6:P0006","trace_type":"Direct","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_DAZY_BOO6:A8","text":"Τὸ κατὰ συμβεβηκὸς οὔτε ἀεὶ οὔτε ὡς ἐπὶ τὸ πολὺ ὑπάρχει.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_DAZY_BOO6:P0008","trace_type":"Direct","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_DAZY_BOO6:A9","text":"Ἐπιστήμη πᾶσα τοῦ ἀεὶ ἢ τοῦ ὡς ἐπὶ τὸ πολὺ ἐστίν.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_DAZY_BOO6:P0010","trace_type":"Direct","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_DAZY_BOO6:A10","text":"Ἡ ὕλη ἡ ἐνδεχομένη ἄλλως ἔχειν αἰτία τοῦ κατὰ συμβεβηκὸς ἐστίν.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_DAZY_BOO6:P0009","trace_type":"Direct","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_DAZY_BOO6:A11","text":"Ἡ τῶν γενητῶν αἰτίων ἀναγωγὴ δεῖ εἰς ἀρχὴν τινὰ καταλήγειν, εἰ μὴ πάντα ἐξ ἀνάγκης ἔσται.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_DAZY_BOO6:P0011","trace_type":"Direct","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_DAZY_BOO6:A12","text":"Τὸ ἀληθὲς καὶ τὸ ψεῦδος ἐν συνθέσει καὶ διαιρέσει τῆς διανοίας ἐστίν, οὐκ ἐν τοῖς πράγμασιν αὐτοῖς.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_DAZY_BOO6:P0013","trace_type":"Direct","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_DAZY_BOO6:A13","text":"Τὸ κατὰ συμβεβηκὸς καὶ τὸ ὡς ἀληθὲς ὂν οὐκ ἔξωθεν δηλοῖ φύσιν τινὰ κυρίως οὖσαν τοῦ ὄντος.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_DAZY_BOO6:P0014","trace_type":"Direct","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]}],"original_name":"Load-Bearing Axioms"},{"number":"3","name":"Apophatic Structure","items":[{"id":"CH_DAZY_BOO6:NX1","text":"Οὐδεμία τῶν μερικῶν ἐπιστημῶν τὸ ὂν ἁπλῶς ἢ τὸ ὂν ᾗ ὂν θεωρεῖ.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_DAZY_BOO6:P0001","trace_type":"Negative","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_DAZY_BOO6:NX2","text":"Ἡ φυσικὴ οὔτε πρακτικὴ οὔτε ποιητικὴ ἐστίν, ἀλλὰ θεωρητική.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_DAZY_BOO6:P0002","trace_type":"Negative","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_DAZY_BOO6:NX3","text":"Ἡ φυσικὴ οὐκ ἂν γιγνώσκοι τὴν ἀκίνητον καὶ χωριστὴν οὐσίαν, ἐπειδὴ περὶ κινητῶν πραγματεύεται.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_DAZY_BOO6:P0004","trace_type":"Negative","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_DAZY_BOO6:NX4","text":"Ἡ μαθηματικὴ οὐκ ἂν εἴη ἡ πρώτη φιλοσοφία, ἐπειδὴ τὰ ἀκίνητα ἃ θεωρεῖ οὐ δῆλον ὅτι χωριστὰ θεωρεῖ.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_DAZY_BOO6:P0003","trace_type":"Negative","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"},{"id":"RAW_DAZY_BOO6:P0004","trace_type":"Negative","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_DAZY_BOO6:NX5","text":"Οὐδεμία ἐπιστήμη περὶ τὸ κατὰ συμβεβηκὸς ἐστίν, ἐπειδὴ οὐδεμία δύναμις ἢ τέχνη ὡρισμένη τοῦ τοιούτου ὑπάρχει.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_DAZY_BOO6:P0006","trace_type":"Negative","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"},{"id":"RAW_DAZY_BOO6:P0009","trace_type":"Negative","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"},{"id":"RAW_DAZY_BOO6:P0010","trace_type":"Negative","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_DAZY_BOO6:NX6","text":"Τὸ κατὰ συμβεβηκὸς οὐκ ἔχει γένεσιν καὶ φθορὰν καθ᾽ αὑτό, ἀλλὰ μόνον κατὰ τὴν σύμπτωσιν ἑτέρων ὄντων.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_DAZY_BOO6:P0008","trace_type":"Negative","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_DAZY_BOO6:NX7","text":"Τὸ ἀληθὲς καὶ τὸ ψεῦδος οὐκ εἰσὶν ἐν τοῖς πράγμασιν ὡς αὐτῶν οὐσίαι ἢ πάθη.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_DAZY_BOO6:P0013","trace_type":"Negative","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_DAZY_BOO6:NX8","text":"Ἡ ἀναγωγὴ τῶν αἰτίων οὐκ ἐπιτρέπεται ἀπείρως εἰς προτέραν αἰτίαν προχωρεῖν.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_DAZY_BOO6:P0012","trace_type":"Negative","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]}],"original_name":"Apophatic Structure"},{"number":"4","name":"Derivative Propositions","items":[{"id":"CH_DAZY_BOO6:D1","text":"Ἡ τῶν θεωρητικῶν ἐπιστημῶν τάξις ἀπὸ τοῦ τρόπου τῆς οὐσίας, κινουμένης ἢ ἀκινήτου καὶ χωριστῆς ἢ ἀχωρίστου, ὁρίζεται.","refs":["CH_DAZY_BOO6:A4","CH_DAZY_BOO6:A5"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"From","passages":[{"id":"RAW_DAZY_BOO6:P0002","trace_type":"Composite","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"},{"id":"RAW_DAZY_BOO6:P0004","trace_type":"Composite","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_DAZY_BOO6:D2","text":"Ἡ πρώτη φιλοσοφία καθόλου γίνεται οὐχ ὡς γένος κοινὸν πάντων, ἀλλ᾽ ὡς ἡ προτέρα περὶ τὴν προτέραν οὐσίαν θεωρία.","refs":["CH_DAZY_BOO6:A5","CH_DAZY_BOO6:A6"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"From","passages":[{"id":"RAW_DAZY_BOO6:P0004","trace_type":"Composite","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"},{"id":"RAW_DAZY_BOO6:P0005","trace_type":"Composite","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_DAZY_BOO6:D3","text":"Ἡ ἀπουσία ἐπιστήμης τοῦ συμβεβηκότος ἐκ τῆς ἀοριστίας καὶ τῆς μὴ κανονικῆς αὐτοῦ ὑπάρξεως ἀκολουθεῖ.","refs":["CH_DAZY_BOO6:A8","CH_DAZY_BOO6:A9","CH_DAZY_BOO6:A10"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"From","passages":[{"id":"RAW_DAZY_BOO6:P0008","trace_type":"Composite","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"},{"id":"RAW_DAZY_BOO6:P0009","trace_type":"Composite","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"},{"id":"RAW_DAZY_BOO6:P0010","trace_type":"Composite","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_DAZY_BOO6:D4","text":"Ἡ ὕλη τὴν ἐνδεχομένην ἄλλως ἔχειν ἀρχὴν παρέχει, δι᾽ ἧς τὸ συμβεβηκὸς δυνατὸν ἐστὶν ἄνευ τοῦ πάντα κατ᾽ ἀνάγκην γίγνεσθαι.","refs":["CH_DAZY_BOO6:A8","CH_DAZY_BOO6:A10","CH_DAZY_BOO6:A11"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"From","passages":[{"id":"RAW_DAZY_BOO6:P0009","trace_type":"Composite","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"},{"id":"RAW_DAZY_BOO6:P0011","trace_type":"Composite","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"},{"id":"RAW_DAZY_BOO6:P0012","trace_type":"Composite","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_DAZY_BOO6:D5","text":"Ἡ τοῦ ἀληθοῦς καὶ ψεύδους ἐξέτασις μετατίθεται ἀπὸ τῆς ὀντολογίας εἰς τὴν περὶ τῆς διανοίας θεωρίαν.","refs":["CH_DAZY_BOO6:A12","CH_DAZY_BOO6:A13"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"From","passages":[{"id":"RAW_DAZY_BOO6:P0013","trace_type":"Composite","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"},{"id":"RAW_DAZY_BOO6:P0014","trace_type":"Composite","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_DAZY_BOO6:D6","text":"Ἡ ζήτησις τοῦ κυρίως ὄντος ἐνεργεῖ δι᾽ ἀφαιρέσεως τῶν ἀορίστων καὶ διανοητικῶν τρόπων τοῦ λέγεσθαι τὸ ὄν.","refs":["CH_DAZY_BOO6:A7","CH_DAZY_BOO6:A13"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"From","passages":[{"id":"RAW_DAZY_BOO6:P0006","trace_type":"Structural","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"},{"id":"RAW_DAZY_BOO6:P0013","trace_type":"Structural","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"},{"id":"RAW_DAZY_BOO6:P0014","trace_type":"Structural","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]}],"original_name":"Derivative Propositions"},{"number":"5","name":"Super-Derivatives","items":[{"id":"CH_DAZY_BOO6:SD1","text":"Ἡ καθόλου ὀντολογία οὐκ ἀρχίζει ἀπὸ γενικῆς ἀφαιρέσεως μόνον, ἀλλὰ ἐκ τῆς προτεραιότητος τῆς ἀκινήτου καὶ χωριστῆς οὐσίας λαμβάνει τὸ δικαίωμα τῆς καθολικότητος.","refs":["CH_DAZY_BOO6:A1","CH_DAZY_BOO6:A5","CH_DAZY_BOO6:A6"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"From","passages":[{"id":"RAW_DAZY_BOO6:P0001","trace_type":"Structural","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"},{"id":"RAW_DAZY_BOO6:P0004","trace_type":"Structural","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"},{"id":"RAW_DAZY_BOO6:P0005","trace_type":"Structural","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_DAZY_BOO6:SD2","text":"Ἡ ἐπιστημονικὴ γνωσιμότης τοῦ ὄντος προϋποτίθεται σταθερότητα ἢ κανονικότητα, διὸ τὸ συμβεβηκὸς ὡς ἀόριστον ὑπολείπεται ἐκτὸς ἐπιστήμης.","refs":["CH_DAZY_BOO6:A8","CH_DAZY_BOO6:A9","CH_DAZY_BOO6:A10"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"From","passages":[{"id":"RAW_DAZY_BOO6:P0008","trace_type":"Composite","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"},{"id":"RAW_DAZY_BOO6:P0010","trace_type":"Composite","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_DAZY_BOO6:SD3","text":"Ἡ διάκρισις τοῦ ὄντος ἀπὸ τοῦ ἀληθοῦς ἀποδεσμεύει τὴν περὶ οὐσίας ζήτησιν ἀπὸ τῆς λογικῆς συνθέσεως καὶ διαιρέσεως.","refs":["CH_DAZY_BOO6:A12","CH_DAZY_BOO6:A13"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"From","passages":[{"id":"RAW_DAZY_BOO6:P0013","trace_type":"Composite","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"},{"id":"RAW_DAZY_BOO6:P0014","trace_type":"Composite","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_DAZY_BOO6:SD4","text":"Ἡ ἀπόρριψις τοῦ ἀπείρου αἰτιακοῦ δρόμου διασῴζει τὸ ἐνδεχόμενον ἀπὸ τοῦ καθολικοῦ ἀναγκαίου καὶ θέτει τὴν ὕλην ὡς ὅρον τῆς ἀταξίας.","refs":["CH_DAZY_BOO6:A10","CH_DAZY_BOO6:A11"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"From","passages":[{"id":"RAW_DAZY_BOO6:P0009","trace_type":"Structural","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"},{"id":"RAW_DAZY_BOO6:P0011","trace_type":"Structural","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"},{"id":"RAW_DAZY_BOO6:P0012","trace_type":"Structural","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]}],"original_name":"Super-Derivatives"},{"number":"6","name":"Deep Apophatics","items":[{"id":"CH_DAZY_BOO6:DNX1","text":"Οὐ πᾶσα κατηγόρησις περὶ τινὸς ὄντος δηλοῖ φύσιν ἢ οὐσίαν τοῦ ὑποκειμένου.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_DAZY_BOO6:P0014","trace_type":"Negative","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_DAZY_BOO6:DNX2","text":"Οὐ πᾶν ὃ γίνεται δύναται κατ᾽ ἐπιστήμην ἀνάγεσθαι, ἐπειδὴ τὸ ἀόριστον καὶ τὸ ὁποτέρ᾽ ἔτυχεν οὐχ ὑπομένει ὡρισμένην αἰτίαν.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_DAZY_BOO6:P0011","trace_type":"Negative","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"},{"id":"RAW_DAZY_BOO6:P0012","trace_type":"Negative","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_DAZY_BOO6:DNX3","text":"Οὐχὶ πᾶσα αἰτιακὴ ἀκολουθία ἀναγκαίαν τάξιν φέρει, ἐπειδὴ ἡ ἐνδεχομένη ὕλη παρεκβάσεις παρὰ τὸ ὡς ἐπὶ τὸ πολὺ ποιεῖ.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_DAZY_BOO6:P0009","trace_type":"Negative","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"},{"id":"RAW_DAZY_BOO6:P0011","trace_type":"Negative","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_DAZY_BOO6:DNX4","text":"Οὐκ ἔστι τὸ ψεῦδος ἐν τῷ πράγματι, ἀλλὰ ἐν τῷ ἐσφαλμένῳ χωρισμῷ ἢ συνδέσμῳ τῆς διανοίας.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_DAZY_BOO6:P0013","trace_type":"Negative","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_DAZY_BOO6:DNX5","text":"Οὐκ ἔστι καθόλου ἐπιστήμη τοῦ ὄντος ἄνευ προτερᾶς οὐσίας, ἐπειδὴ ἡ καθολικότης αὐτῆς ἀπὸ τῆς πρώτης φύσεως ἐξαρτᾶται.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_DAZY_BOO6:P0005","trace_type":"Negative","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]}],"original_name":"Deep Apophatics"},{"number":"7","name":"Super-Apophatics","items":[{"id":"CH_DAZY_BOO6:SNX1","text":"Τὸ ὂν οὐκ ἀποτελεῖ ἓν μονοσήμαντον γένος, ἐπειδὴ πολλαχῶς λέγεται καὶ οἱ τρόποι αὐτοῦ οὐχ ὁμοίως ἐπιστητοί εἰσιν.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_DAZY_BOO6:P0006","trace_type":"Negative","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_DAZY_BOO6:SNX2","text":"Ἡ οὐσία οὐκ ἐξάγεται ἐκ τῶν ὑποθέσεων τῶν μερικῶν ἐπιστημῶν, ἐπειδὴ αὗται τὸ τί ἐστιν ἤδη λαβοῦσαι ἐνεργοῦσιν.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_DAZY_BOO6:P0001","trace_type":"Negative","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_DAZY_BOO6:SNX3","text":"Ἡ ὅλη γένεσις οὐκ ἐπιτρέπεται εἰς πλήρη εἱμαρμένην ἀνάγκην ἀναχθῆναι, ἐπειδὴ τότε τὸ κατὰ συμβεβηκὸς καὶ τὸ ἐνδεχόμενον ἀναιρεῖται.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_DAZY_BOO6:P0009","trace_type":"Negative","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"},{"id":"RAW_DAZY_BOO6:P0011","trace_type":"Negative","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_DAZY_BOO6:SNX4","text":"Ἡ ὀντολογία οὐκ ἐξισοῦται τῇ λογικῇ ἀληθείᾳ, ἐπειδὴ ἡ μὲν περὶ τὰ πράγματα καὶ τὰς ἀρχὰς στρέφεται, ἡ δὲ περὶ πάθος τι τῆς διανοίας.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_DAZY_BOO6:P0013","trace_type":"Negative","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"},{"id":"RAW_DAZY_BOO6:P0014","trace_type":"Negative","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]}],"original_name":"Super-Apophatics"},{"number":"8","name":"Formal Generative Schema","items":[{"id":"CH_DAZY_BOO6:FGS1","text":"Ὁ περιορισμὸς τῶν μερικῶν ἐπιστημῶν εἰς γένη ὑπολειπομένην ποιεῖ τὴν ζήτησιν τοῦ ὄντος ᾗ ὄν.","refs":["CH_DAZY_BOO6:A1","CH_DAZY_BOO6:A2","CH_DAZY_BOO6:A3"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"From","passages":[{"id":"RAW_DAZY_BOO6:P0001","trace_type":"Structural","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_DAZY_BOO6:FGS2","text":"Ἡ διαίρεσις οὐσίας κατὰ κίνησιν καὶ χωρισμὸν → ἡ ὑπόθεσις ἀκινήτου καὶ χωριστῆς οὐσίας → ἡ προτεραιότης τῆς πρώτης φιλοσοφίας.","refs":["CH_DAZY_BOO6:A4","CH_DAZY_BOO6:A5","CH_DAZY_BOO6:A6"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"From","passages":[{"id":"RAW_DAZY_BOO6:P0002","trace_type":"Structural","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"},{"id":"RAW_DAZY_BOO6:P0004","trace_type":"Structural","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"},{"id":"RAW_DAZY_BOO6:P0005","trace_type":"Structural","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_DAZY_BOO6:FGS3","text":"Ἡ πολλαχότης τοῦ ὄντος → ἡ διάκρισις τῶν τρόπων τοῦ λέγεσθαι → ἡ ἀφαίρεσις τοῦ συμβεβηκότος καὶ τοῦ ὡς ἀληθοῦς.","refs":["CH_DAZY_BOO6:A7","CH_DAZY_BOO6:A12","CH_DAZY_BOO6:A13"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"From","passages":[{"id":"RAW_DAZY_BOO6:P0006","trace_type":"Structural","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"},{"id":"RAW_DAZY_BOO6:P0013","trace_type":"Structural","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"},{"id":"RAW_DAZY_BOO6:P0014","trace_type":"Structural","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_DAZY_BOO6:FGS4","text":"Ἡ μὴ κανονικὴ ὕπαρξις τοῦ συμβεβηκότος → ἡ ἀοριστία τοῦ αἰτίου → ἡ ἀδυναμία ἐπιστήμης περὶ αὐτό.","refs":["CH_DAZY_BOO6:A8","CH_DAZY_BOO6:A9","CH_DAZY_BOO6:A10"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"From","passages":[{"id":"RAW_DAZY_BOO6:P0008","trace_type":"Structural","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"},{"id":"RAW_DAZY_BOO6:P0009","trace_type":"Structural","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"},{"id":"RAW_DAZY_BOO6:P0010","trace_type":"Structural","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_DAZY_BOO6:FGS5","text":"Ἡ ἀπειλητικὴ ἀναγωγὴ τῶν αἰτίων εἰς ἀνάγκην → ἡ στάσις εἰς ἀρχὴν τοῦ ὁποτέρ᾽ ἔτυχε → ἡ νέα ζήτησις περὶ ὕλης, τέλους καὶ κινοῦντος.","refs":["CH_DAZY_BOO6:A10","CH_DAZY_BOO6:A11"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"From","passages":[{"id":"RAW_DAZY_BOO6:P0009","trace_type":"Structural","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"},{"id":"RAW_DAZY_BOO6:P0011","trace_type":"Structural","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"},{"id":"RAW_DAZY_BOO6:P0012","trace_type":"Structural","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_DAZY_BOO6:FGS6","text":"Ἡ ἀφαίρεσις τῶν μὴ κυρίως ὄντων τρόπων → ἡ ἐπανόρθωσις τῆς ζητήσεως πρὸς τὰ αἴτια τοῦ ὄντος αὐτοῦ ᾗ ὄν.","refs":["CH_DAZY_BOO6:A1","CH_DAZY_BOO6:A7","CH_DAZY_BOO6:A13"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"From","passages":[{"id":"RAW_DAZY_BOO6:P0001","trace_type":"Structural","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"},{"id":"RAW_DAZY_BOO6:P0006","trace_type":"Structural","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"},{"id":"RAW_DAZY_BOO6:P0014","trace_type":"Structural","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]}],"original_name":"Formal Generative Schema"},{"number":"9","name":"Argument Flow Map","items":[{"id":"CH_DAZY_BOO6:AF1","text":"Αἱ μερικαὶ ἐπιστῆμαι προϋποτίθενται τὰ ἴδια γένη → ἡ ἀπορία περὶ τῆς ἐπιστήμης τοῦ ὄντος ᾗ ὄν.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_DAZY_BOO6:P0001","trace_type":"Composite","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_DAZY_BOO6:AF2","text":"Ἡ φυσικὴ ὡς θεωρία κινουμένης καὶ ἐνύλου οὐσίας → ἡ διάκρισις φυσικῆς, μαθηματικῆς καὶ θεολογικῆς.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_DAZY_BOO6:P0002","trace_type":"Composite","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"},{"id":"RAW_DAZY_BOO6:P0003","trace_type":"Composite","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"},{"id":"RAW_DAZY_BOO6:P0004","trace_type":"Composite","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_DAZY_BOO6:AF3","text":"Ἡ ὕπαρξις ἀκινήτου καὶ χωριστῆς οὐσίας → ἡ πρώτη φιλοσοφία → ἡ καθόλου θεωρία τοῦ ὄντος.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_DAZY_BOO6:P0004","trace_type":"Composite","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"},{"id":"RAW_DAZY_BOO6:P0005","trace_type":"Composite","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_DAZY_BOO6:AF4","text":"Ἡ πολλαχότης τοῦ ὄντος → ἡ ἐξαίρεσις τοῦ συμβεβηκότος καὶ τοῦ ὡς ἀληθοῦς.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_DAZY_BOO6:P0006","trace_type":"Composite","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"},{"id":"RAW_DAZY_BOO6:P0014","trace_type":"Composite","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_DAZY_BOO6:AF5","text":"Τὸ συμβεβηκὸς ὡς μὴ ἀεὶ μηδὲ ὡς ἐπὶ τὸ πολύ → ἡ ὕλη κ